<h2>Introduction to Matrices</h2>

<p><b>One concept:</b> A matrix is a rectangular arrangement of numbers in rows and columns.</p>

<h3>Understand the arrangement</h3>

<p>Consider the matrix:</p>

<pre>
       [ 4   7   2 ]
A  =   [ 9   5   6 ]
</pre>

<p>A <b>row</b> runs horizontally, from left to right.</p>

<pre>
Row 1: [ 4   7   2 ]
Row 2: [ 9   5   6 ]
</pre>

<p>There are two horizontal lines, so the matrix has <b>2 rows</b>.</p>

<p>A <b>column</b> runs vertically, from top to bottom.</p>

<pre>
Column 1    Column 2    Column 3
    4           7           2
    9           5           6
</pre>

<p>The first column contains 4 and 9. The second contains 7 and 5. The third contains 2 and 6.</p>

<p>Therefore, the matrix has <b>3 columns</b>.</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1: Identify the rows</h3>

<p>Given:</p>

<pre>
B = [ 3   8 ]
    [ 1   6 ]
    [ 5   9 ]
</pre>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Read the first horizontal line: [3, 8]. This is row 1.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Read the second horizontal line: [1, 6]. This is row 2.</p>
<p><b>Step 3:</b> Read the third horizontal line: [5, 9]. This is row 3.</p>

<p>There are three horizontal lines.</p>
<p><b>Answer: 3 rows.</b></p>

<h3>Worked Example 2: Identify the columns</h3>

<p>Given:</p>

<pre>
C = [ 2   4   6 ]
    [ 7   9   1 ]
</pre>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Read down the first position: 2, 7. That is column 1.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Read down the second position: 4, 9. That is column 2.</p>
<p><b>Step 3:</b> Read down the third position: 6, 1. That is column 3.</p>

<p>There are three vertical positions.</p>
<p><b>Answer: 3 columns.</b></p>

<h3>Worked Example 3: Count rows and columns</h3>

<p>Given:</p>

<pre>
D = [ 5   2   8   4 ]
    [ 1   7   3   9 ]
</pre>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Count the horizontal lines. There are 2, so there are 2 rows.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Count the entries across the first row. There are 4 entries, so there are 4 columns.</p>

<p><b>Answer: 2 rows and 4 columns.</b></p>

<h3>Common mistake</h3>

<p>Do not confuse rows with columns. Rows go across; columns go down. Count the horizontal lines for rows and the vertical positions for columns.</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Introduction to Matrices', 15),
  (288, 288, '
<h2>Order of a Matrix</h2>

<p><b>One concept:</b> The order of a matrix is the number of rows followed by the number of columns.</p>

<p><b>Rule:</b> Order = rows × columns.</p>

<p>The multiplication sign here separates the two dimensions. It does not mean that we multiply the dimensions when writing the order.</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1: A matrix with 2 rows and 3 columns</h3>

<pre>
A = [ 3   5   7 ]
    [ 2   4   6 ]
</pre>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Count the horizontal lines. There are 2 rows.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Count the entries across one row. There are 3 columns.</p>
<p><b>Step 3:</b> Write rows first and columns second.</p>

<p><b>Order of A = 2 × 3.</b></p>

<h3>Worked Example 2: A matrix with 4 rows and 2 columns</h3>

<pre>
B = [ 1   4 ]
    [ 2   5 ]
    [ 3   6 ]
    [ 7   8 ]
</pre>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Count the horizontal lines. There are 4 rows.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Each row has 2 entries, so there are 2 columns.</p>
<p><b>Step 3:</b> Write rows before columns.</p>

<p><b>Order of B = 4 × 2.</b></p>

<h3>Worked Example 3: Avoid reversing the order</h3>

<pre>
C = [ 8   1   5   3 ]
    [ 2   6   4   9 ]
    [ 7   0   3   1 ]
</pre>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Count the horizontal lines: 3 rows.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Count the entries across a row: 4 columns.</p>
<p><b>Step 3:</b> The order must begin with 3 because there are 3 rows.</p>

<p><b>Order of C = 3 × 4, not 4 × 3.</b></p>

<h3>Worked Example 4: Find the total number of entries</h3>

<p>A matrix has order 3 × 5. How many entries does it contain?</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> The first number tells us there are 3 rows.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> The second number tells us there are 5 columns.</p>
<p><b>Step 3:</b> Each of the 3 rows has 5 entries. Add the entries row by row:</p>

<pre>
5 + 5 + 5 = 15
</pre>

<p>Equivalently, calculate 3 × 5 = 15.</p>
<p><b>Answer: 15 entries.</b></p>

<h3>Common mistake</h3>

<p>Writing 4 × 2 for a matrix with 2 rows and 4 columns reverses the order. Always count rows first.</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Order of a Matrix', 15),
  (289, 289, '
<h2>Elements of a Matrix</h2>

<p><b>One concept:</b> Locate an element using its row number first and column number second.</p>

<p>The numbers inside a matrix are called <b>elements</b> or <b>entries</b>.</p>

<p>For a matrix A, the symbol a<sub>ij</sub> identifies the entry in row i, column j.</p>

<ul>
<li>The first subscript tells you the row.</li>
<li>The second subscript tells you the column.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Worked Example 1: Find a₂₃</h3>

<p>Given:</p>

<pre>
A = [ 4   7   2 ]
    [ 9   5   6 ]
    [ 1   8   3 ]
</pre>

<p>Find a₂₃.</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Read the first subscript, 2. This means go to row 2.</p>

<pre>
Row 2: [ 9   5   6 ]
</pre>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Read the second subscript, 3. Count across to column 3.</p>

<pre>
Column 1 = 9
Column 2 = 5
Column 3 = 6
</pre>

<p><b>Answer: a₂₃ = 6.</b></p>

<p>The answer is 6 because the entry at the intersection of row 2 and column 3 is 6.</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2: Find a₃₁</h3>

<p>Use the same matrix A and find a₃₁.</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> The first subscript is 3, so select row 3.</p>

<pre>
Row 3: [ 1   8   3 ]
</pre>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> The second subscript is 1, so select the first entry in that row.</p>

<p>The first entry in row 3 is 1.</p>

<p><b>Answer: a₃₁ = 1.</b></p>

<h3>Worked Example 3: Find a₁₂</h3>

<p>Use the same matrix A and find a₁₂.</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> The first subscript is 1. Select row 1.</p>

<pre>
Row 1: [ 4   7   2 ]
</pre>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> The second subscript is 2. Select the second entry in row 1.</p>

<p>The second entry is 7.</p>

<p><b>Answer: a₁₂ = 7.</b></p>

<h3>Worked Example 4: Locate a negative element</h3>

<p>Given:</p>

<pre>
B = [ 2  -1   5 ]
    [ 4   3   7 ]
</pre>

<p>Find b₁₂.</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> The first subscript is 1, so select row 1: [2, -1, 5].</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> The second subscript is 2, so select the second entry.</p>
<p><b>Step 3:</b> The second entry is -1. Keep the negative sign because it is part of the element.</p>

<p><b>Answer: b₁₂ = -1.</b></p>

<h3>Common mistake</h3>

<p>a₂₃ does not mean column 2, row 3. It means <b>row 2, column 3</b>. Select the row first, then count across to the column.</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Elements of a Matrix', 15),
  (290, 290, '
<h2>Row Matrix</h2>

<p><b>One concept:</b> A row matrix has exactly one row.</p>

<p>A row matrix can contain any positive number of entries, provided all entries are arranged in one horizontal line.</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1: Identify a row matrix</h3>

<pre>
A = [ 3   5   7 ]
</pre>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Count the horizontal lines. There is only one.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> The three entries are arranged side by side on that single line.</p>
<p><b>Conclusion:</b> A is a row matrix.</p>

<p>Its order is 1 × 3 because it has 1 row and 3 columns.</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2: A row matrix with four entries</h3>

<pre>
B = [ 2  -4   6   8 ]
</pre>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> There is one horizontal line, so there is 1 row.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Count the entries: 2, -4, 6 and 8. There are 4 entries, so there are 4 columns.</p>
<p><b>Step 3:</b> Since there is exactly one row, B is a row matrix.</p>

<p><b>Order = 1 × 4.</b></p>

<h3>Worked Example 3: Distinguish a row matrix from other shapes</h3>

<p>Consider:</p>

<pre>
C = [ 1   2 ]
    [ 3   4 ]
</pre>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Count the horizontal lines. There are 2 rows.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> A row matrix must have exactly 1 row.</p>
<p><b>Conclusion:</b> C is not a row matrix because it has 2 rows.</p>

<p>Having numbers in the first row does not make the entire matrix a row matrix. The whole matrix must have exactly one row.</p>

<h3>Worked Example 4: Write a row matrix from given entries</h3>

<p>Write a row matrix containing the entries 5, -2 and 9.</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> A row matrix requires one horizontal line.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Place the three entries side by side, keeping their values and signs unchanged.</p>

<pre>
D = [ 5  -2   9 ]
</pre>

<p><b>Check:</b> There is 1 row and 3 columns. Therefore, D is a row matrix of order 1 × 3.</p>

<h3>Common mistake</h3>

<p>A matrix is not a row matrix just because its first line is horizontal. Check the number of rows in the entire matrix: it must be exactly one.</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Row Matrix', 15),
  (291, 291, '
<h2>Column Matrix</h2>

<p><b>One concept:</b> A column matrix has exactly one column.</p>

<p>Its entries are arranged vertically, one below another.</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1: Identify a column matrix</h3>

<pre>
A = [ 3 ]
    [ 5 ]
    [ 7 ]
</pre>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Count the vertical positions. There is only 1 column.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Count the horizontal lines. There are 3 rows.</p>
<p><b>Conclusion:</b> A is a column matrix.</p>

<p>Its order is 3 × 1 because it has 3 rows and 1 column.</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2: A column matrix with four entries</h3>

<pre>
B = [ 2  ]
    [ -4 ]
    [ 6  ]
    [ 8  ]
</pre>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Read the entries from top to bottom: 2, -4, 6 and 8.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> There are 4 horizontal positions, so there are 4 rows.</p>
<p><b>Step 3:</b> All entries occupy one vertical position, so there is 1 column.</p>

<p><b>Order = 4 × 1.</b></p>

<h3>Worked Example 3: Distinguish a column matrix from other shapes</h3>

<p>Consider:</p>

<pre>
C = [ 1   2 ]
    [ 3   4 ]
    [ 5   6 ]
</pre>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Count the vertical positions. There are 2 columns.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> A column matrix must have exactly 1 column.</p>
<p><b>Conclusion:</b> C is not a column matrix because it has 2 columns.</p>

<h3>Worked Example 4: Write a column matrix from given entries</h3>

<p>Write a column matrix containing -2, 4 and 9 in that order.</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> A column matrix needs one vertical column.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Place -2 at the top, 4 below it and 9 at the bottom.</p>

<pre>
D = [ -2 ]
    [  4 ]
    [  9 ]
</pre>

<p><b>Check:</b> There are 3 rows and 1 column. Therefore, D is a column matrix of order 3 × 1.</p>

<h3>Common mistake</h3>

<p>Do not call a matrix a column matrix merely because its entries appear in columns. The entire matrix must have exactly one column.</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Column Matrix', 15),
  (292, 292, '
<h2>Square Matrix</h2>

<p><b>One concept:</b> A square matrix has the same number of rows as columns.</p>

<p>That means its order has the form:</p>

<pre>
n × n
</pre>

<p>For example, a 2 × 2 matrix is square, a 3 × 3 matrix is square, and a 4 × 4 matrix is square.</p>

<p>The important test is simple:</p>

<p><b>Number of rows = number of columns.</b></p>

<h3>Worked Example 1: Is the matrix square?</h3>

<p>Consider:</p>

<pre>
        [ 4   7 ]
A   =   [ 2   5 ]
</pre>

<p><b>Step 1: Count the rows.</b></p>

<p>There are two horizontal rows:</p>

<pre>
Row 1: [ 4   7 ]
Row 2: [ 2   5 ]
</pre>

<p>So there are <b>2 rows</b>.</p>

<p><b>Step 2: Count the columns.</b></p>

<pre>
Column 1: 4, 2
Column 2: 7, 5
</pre>

<p>There are <b>2 columns</b>.</p>

<p><b>Step 3: Compare.</b></p>

<pre>
Rows    = 2
Columns = 2
</pre>

<p>The numbers are equal.</p>

<p>Therefore, A is a <b>square matrix</b>.</p>

<p>Its order is:</p>

<pre>
2 × 2
</pre>

<h3>Worked Example 2: A matrix that is not square</h3>

<p>Consider:</p>

<pre>
        [ 3   8   1 ]
B   =   [ 5   2   7 ]
</pre>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Count the rows.</p>

<p>There are 2 rows.</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Count the columns.</p>

<p>There are 3 columns.</p>

<p>Therefore:</p>

<pre>
Rows    = 2
Columns = 3
</pre>

<p>Since 2 is not equal to 3, B is <b>not</b> a square matrix.</p>

<p>Its order is 2 × 3.</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3: A larger square matrix</h3>

<p>Determine whether the following matrix is square:</p>

<pre>
        [ 1   4   6 ]
C   =   [ 2   7   3 ]
        [ 5   8   9 ]
</pre>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Count the rows.</p>

<p>There are 3 rows.</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Count the columns.</p>

<p>There are 3 columns.</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> Compare the two numbers.</p>

<pre>
3 rows = 3 columns
</pre>

<p>Therefore C is a <b>3 × 3 square matrix</b>.</p>

<h3>Worked Example 4: Construct a square matrix</h3>

<p>A student is asked to write a square matrix of order 3 × 3.</p>

<p>What must the student produce?</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> The first number, 3, means there must be 3 rows.</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> The second number, 3, means there must be 3 columns.</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> Therefore the matrix must contain 3 entries in each of its 3 rows.</p>

<pre>
        [ a   b   c ]
A   =   [ d   e   f ]
        [ g   h   i ]
</pre>

<p>The actual values do not determine whether the matrix is square. Its <b>shape</b> does.</p>

<h3>Important distinction</h3>

<p>A square matrix is defined by its dimensions, not by the values inside it.</p>

<p>For example:</p>

<pre>
[ 1   1 ]       [ 8  -4 ]
[ 1   1 ]       [ 0   3 ]
</pre>

<p>Both are square because both have 2 rows and 2 columns.</p>

<h3>Common mistake</h3>

<p>Do not look at the numbers and decide whether a matrix is square. Count its rows and columns.</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Square Matrix', 15),
  (293, 293, '
<h2>Zero Matrix</h2>

<p><b>One concept:</b> A zero matrix is a matrix in which every element is zero.</p>

<p>The matrix can have different dimensions. What makes it a zero matrix is not its shape, but the value of <b>every entry</b>.</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1: Recognising a zero matrix</h3>

<p>Consider:</p>

<pre>
        [ 0   0 ]
A   =   [ 0   0 ]
</pre>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Inspect every entry.</p>

<p>The first row contains:</p>

<pre>
0   0
</pre>

<p>The second row contains:</p>

<pre>
0   0
</pre>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Check whether any entry is different from zero.</p>

<p>There is no non-zero entry.</p>

<p>Therefore A is a <b>zero matrix</b>.</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2: A rectangular zero matrix</h3>

<p>Consider:</p>

<pre>
        [ 0   0   0 ]
B   =   [ 0   0   0 ]
</pre>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> There are 2 rows and 3 columns.</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Look at every position:</p>

<pre>
Row 1: 0, 0, 0
Row 2: 0, 0, 0
</pre>

<p>Every element is zero.</p>

<p>Therefore B is a <b>zero matrix of order 2 × 3</b>.</p>

<p>Notice that a zero matrix does <b>not</b> have to be square.</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3: Is this a zero matrix?</h3>

<p>Consider:</p>

<pre>
        [ 0   0   0 ]
C   =   [ 0   5   0 ]
        [ 0   0   0 ]
</pre>

<p>At first glance, most entries are zero.</p>

<p>But we must inspect <b>every</b> entry.</p>

<p>The middle entry is:</p>

<pre>
5
</pre>

<p>Since 5 is not zero, not every element is zero.</p>

<p>Therefore C is <b>not</b> a zero matrix.</p>

<h3>Worked Example 4: Write a zero matrix</h3>

<p>Write a zero matrix of order 3 × 2.</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> We need 3 rows.</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> We need 2 columns.</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> Every position must contain zero.</p>

<pre>
        [ 0   0 ]
A   =   [ 0   0 ]
        [ 0   0 ]
</pre>

<p>There are 3 rows, 2 columns, and every element is zero.</p>

<p>Therefore this is a <b>3 × 2 zero matrix</b>.</p>

<h3>The key test</h3>

<p>When deciding whether a matrix is a zero matrix, ask:</p>

<p><b>"Is every single entry equal to zero?"</b></p>

<p>If even one entry is non-zero, the matrix is not a zero matrix.</p>

<h3>Common mistake</h3>

<p>Do not decide based on the majority of entries. One non-zero element is enough to make the matrix non-zero.</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Zero Matrix', 15),
  (294, 294, '
<h2>Diagonal Matrix</h2>

<p><b>One concept:</b> A diagonal matrix is a square matrix in which every element outside the main diagonal is zero.</p>

<p>The <b>main diagonal</b> runs from the top-left corner to the bottom-right corner.</p>

<pre>
        [ a   0   0 ]
A   =   [ 0   b   0 ]
        [ 0   0   c ]

          ↑   ↑   ↑
       main diagonal
</pre>

<p>The entries a, b and c may be any numbers, including zero. What matters is that every entry <b>outside</b> the main diagonal is zero.</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1: Identify the main diagonal</h3>

<p>Consider:</p>

<pre>
        [ 4   0   0 ]
A   =   [ 0   7   0 ]
        [ 0   0   9 ]
</pre>

<p>Start at the top-left corner.</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> The first diagonal entry is 4.</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Move one row down and one column right. The next diagonal entry is 7.</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> Move one row down and one column right again. The final diagonal entry is 9.</p>

<p>Therefore the main diagonal is:</p>

<pre>
4, 7, 9
</pre>

<p>Every entry outside this diagonal is zero.</p>

<p>Therefore A is a <b>diagonal matrix</b>.</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2: A diagonal matrix does not need zeros on the diagonal</h3>

<p>Consider:</p>

<pre>
        [ 5    0    0 ]
B   =   [ 0   -2    0 ]
        [ 0    0    8 ]
</pre>

<p>The main diagonal contains:</p>

<pre>
5, -2, 8
</pre>

<p>These numbers are allowed to be non-zero.</p>

<p>Now inspect everything outside the main diagonal:</p>

<pre>
0   0
0   0
0   0
</pre>

<p>All are zero.</p>

<p>Therefore B is diagonal.</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3: Find the mistake</h3>

<p>Consider:</p>

<pre>
        [ 3   0   0 ]
C   =   [ 0   4   2 ]
        [ 0   0   7 ]
</pre>

<p>Is C diagonal?</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify the main diagonal:</p>

<pre>
3, 4, 7
</pre>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Inspect entries outside the diagonal.</p>

<p>The entry in row 2, column 3 is:</p>

<pre>
2
</pre>

<p>This entry is outside the main diagonal.</p>

<p>For a diagonal matrix, it must be zero.</p>

<p>But 2 ≠ 0.</p>

<p>Therefore C is <b>not</b> diagonal.</p>

<h3>Worked Example 4: A diagonal matrix can contain zero on its diagonal</h3>

<p>Consider:</p>

<pre>
        [ 4   0   0 ]
D   =   [ 0   0   0 ]
        [ 0   0   6 ]
</pre>

<p>The main diagonal is:</p>

<pre>
4, 0, 6
</pre>

<p>The zero in the middle does not cause a problem.</p>

<p>Now inspect all entries outside the diagonal. They are all zero.</p>

<p>Therefore D is a <b>diagonal matrix</b>.</p>

<h3>The key test</h3>

<p>Do not ask:</p>

<p>"Are all the numbers on the diagonal non-zero?"</p>

<p>Instead ask:</p>

<p><b>"Are all entries outside the main diagonal zero?"</b></p>

<h3>Common mistake</h3>

<p>A diagonal matrix does not require non-zero diagonal entries. Only the entries <b>outside</b> the main diagonal must be zero.</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Diagonal Matrix', 15),
  (295, 295, '
<h2>Identity Matrix</h2>

<p><b>One concept:</b> An identity matrix is a square matrix with 1s on the main diagonal and 0s everywhere else.</p>

<p>The identity matrix of order 2 is:</p>

<pre>
        [ 1   0 ]
I₂  =   [ 0   1 ]
</pre>

<p>The identity matrix of order 3 is:</p>

<pre>
        [ 1   0   0 ]
I₃  =   [ 0   1   0 ]
        [ 0   0   1 ]
</pre>

<p>Notice the two conditions:</p>

<ol>
<li>Every entry on the main diagonal is <b>1</b>.</li>
<li>Every entry outside the main diagonal is <b>0</b>.</li>
</ol>

<p>Both conditions must be satisfied.</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1: Recognise I₂</h3>

<p>Consider:</p>

<pre>
        [ 1   0 ]
A   =   [ 0   1 ]
</pre>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Check the main diagonal.</p>

<pre>
1
     1
</pre>

<p>Both diagonal entries are 1.</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Check outside the diagonal.</p>

<p>The other two entries are both 0.</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> Check that the matrix is square.</p>

<p>It has 2 rows and 2 columns.</p>

<p>Therefore A is the <b>2 × 2 identity matrix</b>, written I₂.</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2: Recognise I₃</h3>

<p>Consider:</p>

<pre>
        [ 1   0   0 ]
B   =   [ 0   1   0 ]
        [ 0   0   1 ]
</pre>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> The matrix has 3 rows and 3 columns, so it is square.</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> The main diagonal is:</p>

<pre>
1, 1, 1
</pre>

<p>Every diagonal entry is 1.</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> Every entry outside the diagonal is 0.</p>

<p>Therefore B is the identity matrix I₃.</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3: Why this is NOT an identity matrix</h3>

<p>Consider:</p>

<pre>
        [ 1   0 ]
C   =   [ 0   2 ]
</pre>

<p>It looks almost like I₂.</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Check the main diagonal.</p>

<pre>
1, 2
</pre>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> The second diagonal entry is 2.</p>

<p>For an identity matrix, it must be 1.</p>

<p>Therefore C is <b>not</b> an identity matrix.</p>

<p>It is a diagonal matrix, but it is not an identity matrix.</p>

<h3>Worked Example 4: Why this is NOT an identity matrix</h3>

<p>Consider:</p>

<pre>
        [ 1   0 ]
D   =   [ 1   1 ]
</pre>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> The main diagonal contains 1 and 1.</p>

<p>So the diagonal condition is satisfied.</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Inspect the entry below the main diagonal.</p>

<p>That entry is 1.</p>

<p>For an identity matrix, every off-diagonal entry must be zero.</p>

<p>But:</p>

<pre>
1 ≠ 0
</pre>

<p>Therefore D is <b>not</b> an identity matrix.</p>

<h3>Identity versus diagonal</h3>

<p>Every identity matrix is a diagonal matrix, because its off-diagonal entries are zero.</p>

<p>But not every diagonal matrix is an identity matrix.</p>

<p>For example:</p>

<pre>
[ 4   0 ]
[ 0   7 ]
</pre>

<p>is diagonal, but it is not an identity matrix because the diagonal contains 4 and 7 instead of 1 and 1.</p>

<h3>The key test</h3>

<p>To identify an identity matrix, check:</p>

<pre>
Main diagonal → ALL 1
Outside diagonal → ALL 0
</pre>

<p>Both conditions must be true.</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Identity Matrix', 15),
  (296, 296, '
<h2>Equality of Matrices</h2>

<p><b>One concept:</b> Two matrices are equal only when they have the same order and corresponding elements are equal.</p>

<p>There are therefore <b>two conditions</b> to check:</p>

<ol>
<li>The matrices must have the same number of rows and columns.</li>
<li>Every corresponding pair of elements must have the same value.</li>
</ol>

<p>Both conditions are necessary.</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1: Two equal matrices</h3>

<p>Consider:</p>

<pre>
        [ 2   5 ]             [ 2   5 ]
A   =   [ 7   9 ]     and B = [ 7   9 ]
</pre>

<p><b>Step 1: Compare the orders.</b></p>

<p>A has 2 rows and 2 columns.</p>

<p>B has 2 rows and 2 columns.</p>

<p>Therefore both are 2 × 2.</p>

<p><b>Step 2: Compare corresponding elements.</b></p>

<pre>
A₁₁ = 2    B₁₁ = 2
A₁₂ = 5    B₁₂ = 5
A₂₁ = 7    B₂₁ = 7
A₂₂ = 9    B₂₂ = 9
</pre>

<p>Every corresponding pair is equal.</p>

<p>Therefore:</p>

<pre>
A = B
</pre>

<h3>Worked Example 2: Same order but not equal</h3>

<p>Consider:</p>

<pre>
        [ 2   5 ]             [ 2   5 ]
A   =   [ 7   9 ]     and B = [ 7   4 ]
</pre>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Both matrices have order 2 × 2.</p>

<p>So the first condition is satisfied.</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Compare corresponding elements.</p>

<p>The first three corresponding pairs agree:</p>

<pre>
2 = 2
5 = 5
7 = 7
</pre>

<p>But compare the bottom-right entries:</p>

<pre>
9 ≠ 4
</pre>

<p>Therefore the matrices are <b>not equal</b>.</p>

<p>One unequal corresponding element is enough to make the matrices unequal.</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3: Same entries but different arrangement</h3>

<p>Consider:</p>

<pre>
        [ 1   2 ]             [ 2   1 ]
A   =   [ 3   4 ]     and B = [ 4   3 ]
</pre>

<p>Both matrices have order 2 × 2.</p>

<p>However, equality requires the entries to occupy the same corresponding positions.</p>

<p>Compare the top-left entries:</p>

<pre>
A₁₁ = 1
B₁₁ = 2
</pre>

<p>Since:</p>

<pre>
1 ≠ 2
</pre>

<p>the matrices are not equal.</p>

<p>Having the same numbers somewhere in the matrices is not enough. They must be in the same corresponding positions.</p>

<h3>Worked Example 4: Can we find an unknown?</h3>

<p>Given:</p>

<pre>
        [ x   4 ]             [ 7   4 ]
A   =   [ 2   9 ]     and B = [ 2   9 ]
</pre>

<p>If A = B, find x.</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Because A and B are equal, corresponding elements must be equal.</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Compare the top-left entries.</p>

<pre>
A₁₁ = x
B₁₁ = 7
</pre>

<p>Therefore:</p>

<pre>
x = 7
</pre>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> Check the other corresponding entries:</p>

<pre>
4 = 4
2 = 2
9 = 9
</pre>

<p>Everything agrees.</p>

<p>Therefore:</p>

<pre>
x = 7
</pre>

<h3>Worked Example 5: Different orders cannot be equal</h3>

<p>Consider:</p>

<pre>
        [ 1   2   3 ]
A   =   [ 4   5   6 ]

        [ 1   2 ]
B   =   [ 4   5 ]
        [ 6   3 ]
</pre>

<p>A has order:</p>

<pre>
2 × 3
</pre>

<p>B has order:</p>

<pre>
3 × 2
</pre>

<p>The orders are different.</p>

<p>Therefore the matrices cannot be equal, regardless of the numbers inside them.</p>

<h3>The equality test</h3>

<p>When asked whether A = B, use this exact process:</p>

<pre>
1. Compare the orders.
        ↓
2. If the orders differ → NOT equal.
        ↓
3. If the orders agree → compare corresponding entries.
        ↓
4. Every pair must be equal.
</pre>

<p>Do not compare numbers randomly. Compare entries occupying the same position.</p>

<h3>Common mistakes</h3>

<ul>
<li>Checking only whether the matrices have the same order.</li>
<li>Checking whether they contain the same numbers without checking positions.</li>
<li>Ignoring one unequal entry.</li>
<li>Comparing entries from different positions.</li>
</ul>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Equality of Matrices', 15),
  (297, 297, '
<h2>Matrix Addition</h2>

<p><b>One concept:</b> To add two matrices, add corresponding elements — entries occupying the same position.</p>

<p>Before adding, the two matrices must have the <b>same order</b>.</p>

<h3>Why do we add corresponding elements?</h3>

<p>Suppose:</p>

<pre>
        [ 2   5 ]
A   =   [ 7   3 ]

        [ 4   1 ]
B   =   [ 6   8 ]
</pre>

<p>The top-left entry of A is 2 and the top-left entry of B is 4.</p>

<p>Those two entries occupy the same position, so they are corresponding elements.</p>

<p>Therefore:</p>

<pre>
2 + 4 = 6
</pre>

<p>We do the same thing at every position.</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1: Add two 2 × 2 matrices</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> Given</p>

<pre>
        [ 2   5 ]             [ 4   1 ]
A   =   [ 7   3 ]     and B = [ 6   8 ]

find A + B.
</pre>

<p><b>Step 1: Check the orders.</b></p>

<p>A has 2 rows and 2 columns.</p>

<p>B also has 2 rows and 2 columns.</p>

<p>Therefore addition is possible.</p>

<p><b>Step 2: Add the top-left elements.</b></p>

<pre>
2 + 4 = 6
</pre>

<p><b>Step 3: Add the top-right elements.</b></p>

<pre>
5 + 1 = 6
</pre>

<p><b>Step 4: Add the bottom-left elements.</b></p>

<pre>
7 + 6 = 13
</pre>

<p><b>Step 5: Add the bottom-right elements.</b></p>

<pre>
3 + 8 = 11
</pre>

<p>Put each answer back into the position from which it came:</p>

<pre>
A + B = [ 2+4   5+1 ]
        [ 7+6   3+8 ]

      = [  6    6  ]
        [ 13   11  ]
</pre>

<p><b>Answer:</b></p>

<pre>
[ 6    6  ]
[ 13  11  ]
</pre>

<h3>Worked Example 2: Addition involving negative numbers</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> Find A + B if</p>

<pre>
        [ 5   -3 ]             [ -2   7 ]
A   =   [ 4    6 ]     and B = [  8  -1 ]
</pre>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Check the orders.</p>

<p>Both matrices are 2 × 2, so addition is possible.</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Add corresponding elements.</p>

<pre>
Top-left:
5 + (-2) = 3

Top-right:
-3 + 7 = 4

Bottom-left:
4 + 8 = 12

Bottom-right:
6 + (-1) = 5
</pre>

<p>Therefore:</p>

<pre>
A + B = [  3   4 ]
        [ 12   5 ]
</pre>

<h3>Worked Example 3: Adding 3 × 2 matrices</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> Find A + B:</p>

<pre>
        [ 1   4 ]             [ 5   2 ]
A   =   [ 3   0 ]     B   =   [ 1   7 ]
        [ 6   2 ]             [ 4   3 ]
</pre>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Both matrices are 3 × 2.</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Add each corresponding pair.</p>

<pre>
1 + 5 = 6
4 + 2 = 6

3 + 1 = 4
0 + 7 = 7

6 + 4 = 10
2 + 3 = 5
</pre>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> Preserve the original positions.</p>

<pre>
A + B = [  6   6 ]
        [  4   7 ]
        [ 10   5 ]
</pre>

<h3>Worked Example 4: When addition is impossible</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> Can we calculate A + B?</p>

<pre>
        [ 1   2   3 ]
A   =   [ 4   5   6 ]

        [ 7   8 ]
B   =   [ 9   10 ]
        [ 11  12 ]
</pre>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Determine the order of A.</p>

<p>A is 2 × 3.</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Determine the order of B.</p>

<p>B is 3 × 2.</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> Compare them.</p>

<pre>
A → 2 × 3
B → 3 × 2
</pre>

<p>The orders are different.</p>

<p>Therefore corresponding positions cannot be matched throughout the matrices.</p>

<p><b>A + B is not defined.</b></p>

<h3>How to check your addition</h3>

<p>After adding, check every position against the original matrices.</p>

<p>For example, if the top-right answer is 6, verify that:</p>

<pre>
original top-right of A = 5
original top-right of B = 1

5 + 1 = 6
</pre>

<p>This catches errors where an answer is accidentally placed in the wrong position.</p>

<h3>Common mistakes</h3>

<ul>
<li>Adding numbers from different positions.</li>
<li>Adding matrices with different orders.</li>
<li>Changing the position of an answer after calculating it.</li>
<li>Making errors with negative numbers.</li>
</ul>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Matrix Addition', 15),
  (298, 298, '
<h2>Matrix Subtraction</h2>

<p><b>One concept:</b> To subtract matrices, subtract corresponding elements in the same positions.</p>

<p>The order of the matrices must be the same.</p>

<p>Unlike addition, <b>order matters</b>:</p>

<pre>
A - B ≠ B - A
</pre>

<p>because the first matrix supplies the first number in every subtraction.</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1: Basic subtraction</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> Given</p>

<pre>
        [ 9   7 ]             [ 4   2 ]
A   =   [ 6   5 ]     and B = [ 1   3 ]

find A - B.
</pre>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Both matrices have order 2 × 2, so subtraction is possible.</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Subtract the top-left elements:</p>

<pre>
9 - 4 = 5
</pre>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> Subtract the top-right elements:</p>

<pre>
7 - 2 = 5
</pre>

<p><b>Step 4:</b> Subtract the bottom-left elements:</p>

<pre>
6 - 1 = 5
</pre>

<p><b>Step 5:</b> Subtract the bottom-right elements:</p>

<pre>
5 - 3 = 2
</pre>

<p>Therefore:</p>

<pre>
A - B = [ 5   5 ]
        [ 5   2 ]
</pre>

<h3>Worked Example 2: Subtracting negative numbers</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> Find A - B:</p>

<pre>
        [ 4   -2 ]             [ 7   -5 ]
A   =   [ 3    8 ]     B   =   [ 1    4 ]
</pre>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Top-left:</p>

<pre>
4 - 7 = -3
</pre>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Top-right:</p>

<pre>
-2 - (-5)

= -2 + 5

= 3
</pre>

<p>Subtracting a negative number changes the operation to addition.</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> Bottom-left:</p>

<pre>
3 - 1 = 2
</pre>

<p><b>Step 4:</b> Bottom-right:</p>

<pre>
8 - 4 = 4
</pre>

<p>Therefore:</p>

<pre>
A - B = [ -3   3 ]
        [  2   4 ]
</pre>

<h3>Worked Example 3: Why order matters</h3>

<p>Consider:</p>

<pre>
        [ 8   5 ]             [ 3   2 ]
A   =   [ 6   4 ]     and B = [ 1   7 ]
</pre>

<p>Calculate A - B.</p>

<pre>
A - B = [ 8-3   5-2 ]
        [ 6-1   4-7 ]

      = [ 5   3 ]
        [ 5  -3 ]
</pre>

<p>Now calculate B - A.</p>

<pre>
B - A = [ 3-8   2-5 ]
        [ 1-6   7-4 ]

      = [ -5  -3 ]
        [ -5   3 ]
</pre>

<p>The results are different.</p>

<p>This demonstrates:</p>

<pre>
A - B ≠ B - A
</pre>

<h3>Worked Example 4: When subtraction is impossible</h3>

<p>Consider:</p>

<pre>
A = [ 1   2 ]
    [ 3   4 ]

B = [ 5   6   7 ]
    [ 8   9   1 ]
</pre>

<p>A has order 2 × 2.</p>

<p>B has order 2 × 3.</p>

<p>The number of columns is different.</p>

<p>Therefore corresponding positions cannot be matched.</p>

<p><b>A - B is not defined.</b></p>

<h3>How to check subtraction</h3>

<p>Take one result and reverse the subtraction.</p>

<p>For example:</p>

<pre>
9 - 4 = 5
</pre>

<p>Check:</p>

<pre>
5 + 4 = 9
</pre>

<p>If this works for every position, your subtraction is consistent.</p>

<h3>Common mistakes</h3>

<ul>
<li>Reversing A - B into B - A.</li>
<li>Ignoring negative signs.</li>
<li>Subtracting numbers from different positions.</li>
<li>Subtracting matrices with different orders.</li>
</ul>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Matrix Subtraction', 15),
  (299, 299, '
<h2>Scalar Multiplication</h2>

<p><b>One concept:</b> To multiply a matrix by a scalar, multiply every element of the matrix by that number.</p>

<p>A <b>scalar</b> is simply a single number.</p>

<p>For example, in:</p>

<pre>
3A
</pre>

<p>the number 3 is the scalar.</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1: Multiply by a positive scalar</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> Given</p>

<pre>
        [ 2   4 ]
A   =   [ 3   5 ]
</pre>

<p>find 3A.</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> The scalar is 3.</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Multiply the top-left element by 3:</p>

<pre>
3 × 2 = 6
</pre>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> Multiply the top-right element:</p>

<pre>
3 × 4 = 12
</pre>

<p><b>Step 4:</b> Multiply the bottom-left element:</p>

<pre>
3 × 3 = 9
</pre>

<p><b>Step 5:</b> Multiply the bottom-right element:</p>

<pre>
3 × 5 = 15
</pre>

<p>Therefore:</p>

<pre>
3A = [  6   12 ]
     [  9   15 ]
</pre>

<h3>Worked Example 2: Multiply by a negative scalar</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> Find -2B if</p>

<pre>
        [ 3   -4 ]
B   =   [ 5    1 ]
</pre>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Multiply 3 by -2:</p>

<pre>
-2 × 3 = -6
</pre>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Multiply -4 by -2:</p>

<pre>
-2 × (-4) = 8
</pre>

<p>Two negative signs produce a positive result.</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> Multiply 5 by -2:</p>

<pre>
-2 × 5 = -10
</pre>

<p><b>Step 4:</b> Multiply 1 by -2:</p>

<pre>
-2 × 1 = -2
</pre>

<p>Therefore:</p>

<pre>
-2B = [ -6    8 ]
      [ -10  -2 ]
</pre>

<h3>Worked Example 3: Scalar multiplication does not change the order</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> If</p>

<pre>
        [ 1   3   5 ]
C   =   [ 2   4   6 ]
</pre>

<p>find 4C.</p>

<p>C has order 2 × 3.</p>

<p>Multiplication by 4 changes the values, but it does not add or remove rows or columns.</p>

<p>Calculate:</p>

<pre>
4 × 1 = 4
4 × 3 = 12
4 × 5 = 20

4 × 2 = 8
4 × 4 = 16
4 × 6 = 24
</pre>

<p>Therefore:</p>

<pre>
4C = [  4   12   20 ]
     [  8   16   24 ]
</pre>

<p>The result is still 2 × 3.</p>

<h3>Worked Example 4: Multiplication by zero</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> Find 0A if</p>

<pre>
        [ 7   -2 ]
A   =   [ 4    9 ]
</pre>

<p>Multiply every element by zero:</p>

<pre>
0 × 7  = 0
0 × -2 = 0
0 × 4  = 0
0 × 9  = 0
</pre>

<p>Therefore:</p>

<pre>
0A = [ 0   0 ]
     [ 0   0 ]
</pre>

<p>The result is the zero matrix of the same order.</p>

<h3>The key rule</h3>

<p>If:</p>

<pre>
A = [ a   b ]
    [ c   d ]
</pre>

<p>then:</p>

<pre>
kA = [ ka   kb ]
     [ kc   kd ]
</pre>

<p>The scalar multiplies <b>every entry</b>.</p>

<h3>Common mistakes</h3>

<ul>
<li>Multiplying only some of the entries.</li>
<li>Multiplying rows instead of individual entries.</li>
<li>Forgetting the negative sign of the scalar.</li>
<li>Changing the order of the matrix.</li>
</ul>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Scalar Multiplication', 15),
  (300, 300, '
<h2>Matrix Multiplication</h2>

<p><b>One concept:</b> To multiply matrices, each entry in the answer is found by multiplying corresponding entries from a row of the first matrix and a column of the second matrix, then adding those products.</p>

<p>This is different from matrix addition.</p>

<p>We do <b>not</b> multiply entries in the same position.</p>

<h3>First: when is multiplication possible?</h3>

<p>Suppose:</p>

<pre>
A has order m × n
B has order n × p
</pre>

<p>The number of columns in A must equal the number of rows in B.</p>

<p>For example:</p>

<pre>
A → 2 × 3
B → 3 × 2
</pre>

<p>These can be multiplied because:</p>

<pre>
A columns = 3
B rows    = 3
</pre>

<p>The answer will have order:</p>

<pre>
2 × 2
</pre>

<p>The outside numbers determine the answer''s order.</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1: 2 × 2 multiplication</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> Given</p>

<pre>
        [ 2   3 ]             [ 4   1 ]
A   =   [ 1   5 ]     and B = [ 2   3 ]

find AB.
</pre>

<p><b>Step 1: Check whether multiplication is possible.</b></p>

<p>A is 2 × 2.</p>

<p>B is 2 × 2.</p>

<p>The number of columns in A is 2, and the number of rows in B is 2.</p>

<p>Therefore multiplication is possible.</p>

<p>The answer will be 2 × 2.</p>

<h3>Finding the top-left entry</h3>

<p>To find the top-left entry, use:</p>

<p><b>Row 1 of A × Column 1 of B.</b></p>

<pre>
Row 1 of A = [ 2   3 ]

Column 1 of B =
[ 4 ]
[ 2 ]
</pre>

<p>Multiply matching positions and add:</p>

<pre>
(2 × 4) + (3 × 2)

= 8 + 6

= 14
</pre>

<p>Therefore the top-left entry is 14.</p>

<h3>Finding the top-right entry</h3>

<p>Use row 1 of A and column 2 of B:</p>

<pre>
Row 1 of A = [ 2   3 ]

Column 2 of B =
[ 1 ]
[ 3 ]
</pre>

<p>Calculate:</p>

<pre>
(2 × 1) + (3 × 3)

= 2 + 9

= 11
</pre>

<p>So the top-right entry is 11.</p>

<h3>Finding the bottom-left entry</h3>

<p>Use row 2 of A and column 1 of B:</p>

<pre>
Row 2 of A = [ 1   5 ]

Column 1 of B =
[ 4 ]
[ 2 ]
</pre>

<p>Calculate:</p>

<pre>
(1 × 4) + (5 × 2)

= 4 + 10

= 14
</pre>

<p>So the bottom-left entry is 14.</p>

<h3>Finding the bottom-right entry</h3>

<p>Use row 2 of A and column 2 of B:</p>

<pre>
Row 2 of A = [ 1   5 ]

Column 2 of B =
[ 1 ]
[ 3 ]
</pre>

<p>Calculate:</p>

<pre>
(1 × 1) + (5 × 3)

= 1 + 15

= 16
</pre>

<p>Therefore:</p>

<pre>
AB = [ 14   11 ]
     [ 14   16 ]
</pre>

<h3>Worked Example 2: 2 × 3 multiplied by 3 × 2</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> Find AB if</p>

<pre>
        [ 1   2   3 ]             [ 2   1 ]
A   =   [ 4   5   6 ]     B   =   [ 0   3 ]
                                  [ 1   2 ]
</pre>

<p><b>Step 1: Check the orders.</b></p>

<pre>
A → 2 × 3
B → 3 × 2
</pre>

<p>The inner dimensions are both 3, so multiplication is possible.</p>

<p>The result will have order:</p>

<pre>
2 × 2
</pre>

<h3>Top-left entry</h3>

<p>Use row 1 of A and column 1 of B:</p>

<pre>
(1 × 2) + (2 × 0) + (3 × 1)

= 2 + 0 + 3

= 5
</pre>

<h3>Top-right entry</h3>

<p>Use row 1 of A and column 2 of B:</p>

<pre>
(1 × 1) + (2 × 3) + (3 × 2)

= 1 + 6 + 6

= 13
</pre>

<h3>Bottom-left entry</h3>

<p>Use row 2 of A and column 1 of B:</p>

<pre>
(4 × 2) + (5 × 0) + (6 × 1)

= 8 + 0 + 6

= 14
</pre>

<h3>Bottom-right entry</h3>

<p>Use row 2 of A and column 2 of B:</p>

<pre>
(4 × 1) + (5 × 3) + (6 × 2)

= 4 + 15 + 12

= 31
</pre>

<p>Therefore:</p>

<pre>
AB = [  5   13 ]
     [ 14   31 ]
</pre>

<h3>Worked Example 3: Multiplication that is impossible</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> Can AB be calculated if:</p>

<pre>
A → 2 × 3
B → 2 × 2
</pre>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Look at the number of columns in A:</p>

<pre>
3 columns
</pre>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Look at the number of rows in B:</p>

<pre>
2 rows
</pre>

<p>These numbers are different.</p>

<pre>
3 ≠ 2
</pre>

<p>Therefore the inner dimensions do not match.</p>

<p><b>AB is not defined.</b></p>

<h3>Worked Example 4: Why AB and BA may be different</h3>

<p>Using:</p>

<pre>
A = [ 2   3 ]
    [ 1   5 ]

B = [ 4   1 ]
    [ 2   3 ]
</pre>

<p>We already found:</p>

<pre>
AB = [ 14   11 ]
     [ 14   16 ]
</pre>

<p>Now calculate BA.</p>

<p>Top-left:</p>

<pre>
(4 × 2) + (1 × 1)
= 8 + 1
= 9
</pre>

<p>Top-right:</p>

<pre>
(4 × 3) + (1 × 5)
= 12 + 5
= 17
</pre>

<p>Bottom-left:</p>

<pre>
(2 × 2) + (3 × 1)
= 4 + 3
= 7
</pre>

<p>Bottom-right:</p>

<pre>
(2 × 3) + (3 × 5)
= 6 + 15
= 21
</pre>

<p>Therefore:</p>

<pre>
BA = [ 9   17 ]
     [ 7   21 ]
</pre>

<p>Compare:</p>

<pre>
AB ≠ BA
</pre>

<p>Therefore matrix multiplication is generally <b>not commutative</b>.</p>

<h3>The safest multiplication method</h3>

<p>For every entry, ask yourself:</p>

<pre>
Which row am I using?
        +
Which column am I using?
        ↓
Multiply corresponding pairs.
        ↓
Add the products.
</pre>

<p>Never multiply entries simply because they occupy the same position.</p>

<h3>Common mistakes</h3>

<ul>
<li>Multiplying corresponding entries as if this were scalar multiplication.</li>
<li>Using a column from the first matrix instead of a row.</li>
<li>Using the wrong column from the second matrix.</li>
<li>Forgetting to add the products.</li>
<li>Ignoring the inner-dimension rule.</li>
<li>Assuming AB = BA.</li>
</ul>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Matrix Multiplication', 15),
  (301, 301, '
<h2>Transpose of a Matrix</h2>

<p>
The <b>transpose</b> of a matrix is formed by changing its
<b>rows into columns</b>.
</p>

<p>
The transpose of matrix <b>A</b> is written as <b>A<sup>T</sup></b>.
The superscript T means "transpose".
</p>

<h3>THE ONE ACTION TO LEARN</h3>

<p>
To transpose a matrix:
</p>

<ol>
  <li>Take the first row and make it the first column.</li>
  <li>Take the second row and make it the second column.</li>
  <li>Continue until all rows have become columns.</li>
</ol>

<p>
<b>Important:</b> The numbers themselves do not change.
Only their positions change.
</p>

<h3>Example 1: Transpose a 2 × 3 Matrix</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> Find the transpose of</p>

<pre>
A = [ 2  5  7
      1  4  6 ]
</pre>

<h4>Step 1: Look at the first row</h4>

<p>
The first row is:
</p>

<pre>
2   5   7
</pre>

<p>
This row becomes the <b>first column</b> of the transpose.
</p>

<pre>
[ 2
  5
  7 ]
</pre>

<h4>Step 2: Look at the second row</h4>

<p>
The second row is:
</p>

<pre>
1   4   6
</pre>

<p>
This row becomes the <b>second column</b>.
</p>

<pre>
[ 2  1
  5  4
  7  6 ]
</pre>

<h4>Therefore:</h4>

<pre>
Aᵀ = [ 2  1
       5  4
       7  6 ]
</pre>

<p>
Check the movement:
</p>

<pre>
Original row 1:  2  5  7
                 ↓  ↓  ↓

Transpose:       2
                 5
                 7
</pre>

<pre>
Original row 2:  1  4  6
                 ↓  ↓  ↓

Transpose:       1
                 4
                 6
</pre>

<p>
So:
</p>

<pre>
Row 1 → Column 1
Row 2 → Column 2
</pre>

<h3>Example 2: Transpose a 3 × 2 Matrix</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> Find the transpose of</p>

<pre>
B = [ 3  8
      5  1
      7  4 ]
</pre>

<h4>Step 1: Take row 1</h4>

<pre>
3   8
</pre>

<p>
This becomes column 1:
</p>

<pre>
[ 3
  8 ]
</pre>

<h4>Step 2: Take row 2</h4>

<pre>
5   1
</pre>

<p>
This becomes column 2:
</p>

<pre>
[ 5
  1 ]
</pre>

<h4>Step 3: Take row 3</h4>

<pre>
7   4
</pre>

<p>
This becomes column 3.
</p>

<pre>
[ 7
  4 ]
</pre>

<h4>Put the columns together</h4>

<pre>
Bᵀ = [ 3  5  7
       8  1  4 ]
</pre>

<p>
Notice what happened:
</p>

<pre>
Original B        Transpose Bᵀ

3  8              3  5  7
5  1       →      8  1  4
7  4
</pre>

<p>
The first row became the first column,
the second row became the second column,
and the third row became the third column.
</p>

<h3>Example 3: Transpose a Matrix Containing Negative Numbers</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> Find the transpose of</p>

<pre>
C = [ -2   4
       6  -5 ]
</pre>

<h4>Step 1: First row</h4>

<pre>
-2   4
</pre>

<p>
becomes the first column:
</p>

<pre>
[ -2
   4 ]
</pre>

<h4>Step 2: Second row</h4>

<pre>
6   -5
</pre>

<p>
becomes the second column:
</p>

<pre>
[  6
  -5 ]
</pre>

<h4>Therefore:</h4>

<pre>
Cᵀ = [ -2   6
        4  -5 ]
</pre>

<p>
Notice that <b>-5 remains -5</b>.
The sign does not change.
It only moves to a new position.
</p>

<h3>Example 4: Transpose a 3 × 3 Matrix</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> Find the transpose of</p>

<pre>
D = [ 1  2  3
      4  5  6
      7  8  9 ]
</pre>

<h4>Step 1: Turn row 1 into column 1</h4>

<pre>
1  2  3

↓

[ 1
  2
  3 ]
</pre>

<h4>Step 2: Turn row 2 into column 2</h4>

<pre>
4  5  6

↓

[ 4
  5
  6 ]
</pre>

<h4>Step 3: Turn row 3 into column 3</h4>

<pre>
7  8  9

↓

[ 7
  8
  9 ]
</pre>

<h4>Final answer</h4>

<pre>
Dᵀ = [ 1  4  7
       2  5  8
       3  6  9 ]
</pre>

<h3>THE PATTERN</h3>

<p>
Look carefully at the positions:
</p>

<pre>
Original:          Transpose:

1  2  3             1  4  7
4  5  6      →      2  5  8
7  8  9             3  6  9
</pre>

<p>
The first row <b>1, 2, 3</b> became the first column.
</p>

<p>
The second row <b>4, 5, 6</b> became the second column.
</p>

<p>
The third row <b>7, 8, 9</b> became the third column.
</p>

<h3>A SIMPLE WAY TO REMEMBER IT</h3>

<p>
<b>Read across → write down.</b>
</p>

<p>
Read each row from left to right, then write that row vertically as a column.
</p>

<h3>COMMON MISTAKE</h3>

<p>
A student may think that transposing means changing the numbers themselves.
That is wrong.
</p>

<p>
For example:
</p>

<pre>
A = [ 2  5
      7  9 ]
</pre>

<p>
The transpose is:
</p>

<pre>
Aᵀ = [ 2  7
        5  9 ]
</pre>

<p>
The numbers <b>2, 5, 7, 9</b> are exactly the same.
Only their positions have changed.
</p>

<h3>SELF-CHECK</h3>

<p>
After transposing, ask yourself:
</p>

<ul>
  <li>Did every original row become a column?</li>
  <li>Did I keep every number?</li>
  <li>Did I accidentally change any sign or value?</li>
  <li>Did I put the first row into the first column?</li>
  <li>Did I put the second row into the second column?</li>
</ul>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Transpose of a Matrix', 15),
  (302, 302, '
<h2>Addition of Matrices</h2>

<p>
<b>Matrix addition</b> means adding corresponding entries of two matrices.
Corresponding entries are entries that occupy the same position.
</p>

<p>
Before adding two matrices, they must have the <b>same size</b>.
For example, a 2 × 2 matrix can be added to another 2 × 2 matrix.
</p>

<h3>THE ONE ACTION</h3>

<p>
To add two matrices:
</p>

<ol>
  <li>Take the entry in the first position of the first matrix.</li>
  <li>Add it to the entry in the same position of the second matrix.</li>
  <li>Repeat this for every position.</li>
</ol>

<h3>Example 1: Adding Two 2 × 2 Matrices</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> Find A + B if</p>

<pre>
A = [ 2  5
      7  1 ]

B = [ 4  3
      6  8 ]
</pre>

<h4>Step 1: Add the top-left entries</h4>

<p>
The top-left entry of A is 2.
The top-left entry of B is 4.
</p>

<pre>
2 + 4 = 6
</pre>

<h4>Step 2: Add the top-right entries</h4>

<pre>
5 + 3 = 8
</pre>

<h4>Step 3: Add the bottom-left entries</h4>

<pre>
7 + 6 = 13
</pre>

<h4>Step 4: Add the bottom-right entries</h4>

<pre>
1 + 8 = 9
</pre>

<h4>Put the answers in their original positions</h4>

<pre>
A + B = [ 6   8
         13   9 ]
</pre>

<p>
Nothing moves during addition.
Each position is calculated independently.
</p>

<h3>Example 2: Addition with Negative Numbers</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> Find P + Q if</p>

<pre>
P = [  6  -3
      -2   5 ]

Q = [ -4   7
       8  -1 ]
</pre>

<h4>Top-left</h4>

<pre>
6 + (-4) = 2
</pre>

<h4>Top-right</h4>

<pre>
-3 + 7 = 4
</pre>

<h4>Bottom-left</h4>

<pre>
-2 + 8 = 6
</pre>

<h4>Bottom-right</h4>

<pre>
5 + (-1) = 4
</pre>

<p><b>Therefore:</b></p>

<pre>
P + Q = [ 2  4
          6  4 ]
</pre>

<h3>Example 3: Adding 2 × 3 Matrices</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> Find X + Y if</p>

<pre>
X = [ 1  4  7
      2  5  8 ]

Y = [ 3  2  1
      6  4  2 ]
</pre>

<p>
The matrices have the same size, so corresponding entries can be added.
</p>

<pre>
Top row:

1 + 3 = 4
4 + 2 = 6
7 + 1 = 8
</pre>

<pre>
Bottom row:

2 + 6 = 8
5 + 4 = 9
8 + 2 = 10
</pre>

<p><b>Therefore:</b></p>

<pre>
X + Y = [ 4   6   8
           8   9  10 ]
</pre>

<h3>Example 4: Why Different-Sized Matrices Cannot Be Added</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> Can the following matrices be added?</p>

<pre>
A = [ 1  2
      3  4 ]

B = [ 5  6  7
      8  9  10 ]
</pre>

<p>
A has 2 columns, while B has 3 columns.
Therefore, there is no corresponding entry for every position.
</p>

<p>
So:
</p>

<pre>
A + B = NOT DEFINED
</pre>

<p>
The important rule is:
<b>same size is required before addition.</b>
</p>

<h3>COMMON MISTAKE</h3>

<p>
Do not add entries diagonally or randomly.
For example, do not calculate 2 + 8 just because both numbers appear in the matrices.
</p>

<p>
You must add entries occupying the <b>same position</b>.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Addition of Matrices', 15),
  (303, 303, '
<h2>Subtraction of Matrices</h2>

<p>
<b>Matrix subtraction</b> means subtracting corresponding entries of one matrix
from the corresponding entries of another matrix.
</p>

<p>
The two matrices must have the <b>same size</b>.
</p>

<h3>THE ONE ACTION</h3>

<p>
For A − B:
</p>

<p>
Take each entry of A and subtract the entry in the <b>same position</b> in B.
</p>

<h3>Example 1: Basic Subtraction</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> Find A − B if</p>

<pre>
A = [ 9  7
      5  3 ]

B = [ 4  2
      1  6 ]
</pre>

<h4>Top-left</h4>

<pre>
9 - 4 = 5
</pre>

<h4>Top-right</h4>

<pre>
7 - 2 = 5
</pre>

<h4>Bottom-left</h4>

<pre>
5 - 1 = 4
</pre>

<h4>Bottom-right</h4>

<pre>
3 - 6 = -3
</pre>

<p><b>Therefore:</b></p>

<pre>
A - B = [ 5   5
          4  -3 ]
</pre>

<h3>Example 2: Subtracting Negative Numbers</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> Find P − Q if</p>

<pre>
P = [  6  -2
      -4   7 ]

Q = [ -3   5
       2  -1 ]
</pre>

<h4>Top-left</h4>

<pre>
6 - (-3)
= 6 + 3
= 9
</pre>

<h4>Top-right</h4>

<pre>
-2 - 5 = -7
</pre>

<h4>Bottom-left</h4>

<pre>
-4 - 2 = -6
</pre>

<h4>Bottom-right</h4>

<pre>
7 - (-1)
= 7 + 1
= 8
</pre>

<p><b>Therefore:</b></p>

<pre>
P - Q = [ 9  -7
         -6   8 ]
</pre>

<h3>Example 3: The Order Matters</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> Given</p>

<pre>
A = [ 8  5
      3  1 ]

B = [ 2  4
      6  7 ]
</pre>

<p>Find both A − B and B − A.</p>

<h4>First calculate A − B</h4>

<pre>
8 - 2 = 6
5 - 4 = 1
3 - 6 = -3
1 - 7 = -6
</pre>

<pre>
A - B = [ 6   1
         -3  -6 ]
</pre>

<h4>Now calculate B − A</h4>

<pre>
2 - 8 = -6
4 - 5 = -1
6 - 3 = 3
7 - 1 = 6
</pre>

<pre>
B - A = [ -6  -1
           3   6 ]
</pre>

<p>
The answers are different.
Therefore, reversing the order changes the result.
</p>

<h3>Example 4: Different Sizes</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> Can these matrices be subtracted?</p>

<pre>
A = [ 1  2
      3  4 ]

B = [ 5  6  7
      8  9  10 ]
</pre>

<p>
A has 2 columns.
B has 3 columns.
Their sizes are different.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<pre>
A - B = NOT DEFINED
</pre>

<h3>COMMON MISTAKE</h3>

<p>
In A − B, do not reverse the subtraction.
The operation is:
</p>

<pre>
entry from A - corresponding entry from B
</pre>

<p>
It is not:
</p>

<pre>
entry from B - corresponding entry from A
</pre>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Subtraction of Matrices', 15),
  (304, 304, '
<h2>Scalar Multiplication of a Matrix</h2>

<p>
A <b>scalar</b> is an ordinary number.
Scalar multiplication means multiplying <b>every entry</b> of a matrix by that number.
</p>

<p>
If a number k multiplies matrix A, we write:
</p>

<pre>
kA
</pre>

<p>
The number k must multiply every entry.
</p>

<h3>THE ONE ACTION</h3>

<p>
To calculate kA:
</p>

<ol>
  <li>Take the scalar k.</li>
  <li>Multiply it by the first entry.</li>
  <li>Multiply it by the second entry.</li>
  <li>Continue until every entry has been multiplied.</li>
</ol>

<h3>Example 1: Multiplying by 3</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> Find 3A if</p>

<pre>
A = [ 2  4
      5  1 ]
</pre>

<p>
Multiply every entry by 3.
</p>

<pre>
3(2) = 6
3(4) = 12
3(5) = 15
3(1) = 3
</pre>

<p><b>Therefore:</b></p>

<pre>
3A = [  6  12
        15   3 ]
</pre>

<h3>Example 2: Multiplying by a Negative Scalar</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> Find -2B if</p>

<pre>
B = [  3  -4
      -5   2 ]
</pre>

<h4>Multiply each entry</h4>

<pre>
-2(3)  = -6
-2(-4) = 8
-2(-5) = 10
-2(2)  = -4
</pre>

<p><b>Therefore:</b></p>

<pre>
-2B = [ -6   8
         10  -4 ]
</pre>

<p>
Notice that multiplying a negative number by a negative entry produces a positive result.
</p>

<h3>Example 3: Multiplying by a Fraction</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> Find 1/2 C if</p>

<pre>
C = [ 6  10
      4   8 ]
</pre>

<p>
Multiply every entry by 1/2.
</p>

<pre>
1/2(6)  = 3
1/2(10) = 5
1/2(4)  = 2
1/2(8)  = 4
</pre>

<p><b>Therefore:</b></p>

<pre>
1/2 C = [ 3  5
          2  4 ]
</pre>

<h3>Example 4: Multiplying a 2 × 3 Matrix</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> Find 4D if</p>

<pre>
D = [ 1  -2   3
      0   5  -1 ]
</pre>

<p>
Multiply all six entries by 4.
</p>

<pre>
4(1)  = 4
4(-2) = -8
4(3)  = 12

4(0)  = 0
4(5)  = 20
4(-1) = -4
</pre>

<p><b>Therefore:</b></p>

<pre>
4D = [ 4  -8  12
        0  20  -4 ]
</pre>

<h3>COMMON MISTAKE</h3>

<p>
Do not multiply only one row or one column.
The scalar must multiply <b>every entry</b>.
</p>

<p>
For example, 3A does not mean multiply only the 3rd entry.
It means multiply the entire matrix by 3.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Scalar Multiplication of a Matrix', 15),
  (305, 305, '
<h2>Multiplication of Matrices</h2>

<p>
Matrix multiplication is different from matrix addition.
You do <b>not</b> simply multiply entries in the same positions.
</p>

<p>
To multiply matrices, we combine a <b>row from the first matrix</b>
with a <b>column from the second matrix</b>.
</p>

<h3>THE ONE ACTION</h3>

<p>
For each entry in the answer:
</p>

<ol>
  <li>Take one row from the first matrix.</li>
  <li>Take one column from the second matrix.</li>
  <li>Multiply matching numbers.</li>
  <li>Add those products.</li>
</ol>

<h3>Example 1: 2 × 2 Matrix Multiplication</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> Find AB if</p>

<pre>
A = [ 2  3
      1  4 ]

B = [ 5  2
      6  1 ]
</pre>

<h4>Find the top-left entry</h4>

<p>
Use row 1 of A and column 1 of B:
</p>

<pre>
[2  3]    [5
           6]
</pre>

<p>
Multiply matching numbers and add:
</p>

<pre>
(2 × 5) + (3 × 6)
= 10 + 18
= 28
</pre>

<p>
So the top-left entry is 28.
</p>

<h4>Find the top-right entry</h4>

<p>
Use row 1 of A and column 2 of B:
</p>

<pre>
(2 × 2) + (3 × 1)
= 4 + 3
= 7
</pre>

<h4>Find the bottom-left entry</h4>

<p>
Use row 2 of A and column 1 of B:
</p>

<pre>
(1 × 5) + (4 × 6)
= 5 + 24
= 29
</pre>

<h4>Find the bottom-right entry</h4>

<pre>
(1 × 2) + (4 × 1)
= 2 + 4
= 6
</pre>

<p><b>Therefore:</b></p>

<pre>
AB = [ 28   7
        29   6 ]
</pre>

<h3>Example 2: Understanding Where One Answer Comes From</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> Find the top-left entry of AB where</p>

<pre>
A = [ 4  1
      2  3 ]

B = [ 7  5
      6  8 ]
</pre>

<p>
The top-left position requires:
</p>

<ul>
  <li>Row 1 from A</li>
  <li>Column 1 from B</li>
</ul>

<pre>
[4  1]    [7
           6]
</pre>

<p>
Now multiply matching positions:
</p>

<pre>
4 × 7 = 28
1 × 6 = 6
</pre>

<p>
Add the products:
</p>

<pre>
28 + 6 = 34
</pre>

<p>
Therefore, the top-left entry of AB is <b>34</b>.
</p>

<h3>Example 3: Multiplying a 2 × 2 Matrix by a 2 × 1 Matrix</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> Find AB if</p>

<pre>
A = [ 3  2
      1  4 ]

B = [ 5
      6 ]
</pre>

<h4>First answer entry</h4>

<p>
Use row 1 of A and the only column of B:
</p>

<pre>
(3 × 5) + (2 × 6)
= 15 + 12
= 27
</pre>

<h4>Second answer entry</h4>

<p>
Use row 2 of A:
</p>

<pre>
(1 × 5) + (4 × 6)
= 5 + 24
= 29
</pre>

<p><b>Therefore:</b></p>

<pre>
AB = [ 27
       29 ]
</pre>

<h3>Example 4: When Multiplication Is Not Defined</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> Can these matrices be multiplied in the order AB?</p>

<pre>
A = [ 1  2  3
      4  5  6 ]

B = [ 7  8
      9  10 ]
</pre>

<p>
A has <b>3 columns</b>.
B has <b>2 rows</b>.
</p>

<p>
For AB to be possible, the number of columns of A must equal
the number of rows of B.
</p>

<pre>
3 ≠ 2
</pre>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<pre>
AB is NOT DEFINED.
</pre>

<h3>THE MOST IMPORTANT RULE</h3>

<p>
If:
</p>

<pre>
A is m × n
B is n × p
</pre>

<p>
then:
</p>

<pre>
AB is m × p
</pre>

<p>
The <b>inside numbers must match</b>.
</p>

<h3>COMMON MISTAKE</h3>

<p>
Do not calculate matrix multiplication by multiplying corresponding
entries like matrix addition.
</p>

<p>
For example, the top-left entry is not:
</p>

<pre>
2 × 5
</pre>

<p>
It is:
</p>

<pre>
(2 × 5) + (3 × 6)
</pre>

<p>
The entire row must interact with the entire column.
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Multiplication of Matrices', 15),
  (306, 306, '
<h2>Determinant of a 2 × 2 Matrix</h2>

<p>
The <b>determinant</b> is a single number calculated from a square matrix.
For a 2 × 2 matrix, there is a specific calculation pattern.
</p>

<p>
If:
</p>

<pre>
A = [ a  b
      c  d ]
</pre>

<p>
then:
</p>

<pre>
det(A) = ad - bc
</pre>

<p>
This means:
</p>

<ol>
  <li>Multiply the top-left entry by the bottom-right entry.</li>
  <li>Multiply the top-right entry by the bottom-left entry.</li>
  <li>Subtract the second product from the first.</li>
</ol>

<h3>THE ONE ACTION</h3>

<p>
Think:
</p>

<pre>
main diagonal product
MINUS
other diagonal product
</pre>

<h3>Example 1: Basic Determinant</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> Find the determinant of</p>

<pre>
A = [ 3  2
      5  4 ]
</pre>

<p>
First multiply the main diagonal entries:
</p>

<pre>
3 × 4 = 12
</pre>

<p>
Now multiply the other diagonal entries:
</p>

<pre>
2 × 5 = 10
</pre>

<p>
Subtract:
</p>

<pre>
det(A) = 12 - 10
       = 2
</pre>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<pre>
det(A) = 2
</pre>

<h3>Example 2: A Negative Determinant</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> Find the determinant of</p>

<pre>
B = [ 2  7
      5  3 ]
</pre>

<p>
First product:
</p>

<pre>
2 × 3 = 6
</pre>

<p>
Second product:
</p>

<pre>
7 × 5 = 35
</pre>

<p>
Subtract:
</p>

<pre>
det(B) = 6 - 35
       = -29
</pre>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<pre>
det(B) = -29
</pre>

<h3>Example 3: Determinant with Negative Entries</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> Find the determinant of</p>

<pre>
C = [ -2   4
       3  -5 ]
</pre>

<p>
Multiply the main diagonal:
</p>

<pre>
(-2)(-5) = 10
</pre>

<p>
Multiply the other diagonal:
</p>

<pre>
4 × 3 = 12
</pre>

<p>
Subtract:
</p>

<pre>
det(C) = 10 - 12
       = -2
</pre>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<pre>
det(C) = -2
</pre>

<h3>Example 4: Determinant Equal to Zero</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> Find the determinant of</p>

<pre>
D = [ 2  4
      1  2 ]
</pre>

<p>
First product:
</p>

<pre>
2 × 2 = 4
</pre>

<p>
Second product:
</p>

<pre>
4 × 1 = 4
</pre>

<p>
Subtract:
</p>

<pre>
det(D) = 4 - 4
       = 0
</pre>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<pre>
det(D) = 0
</pre>

<h3>COMMON MISTAKE</h3>

<p>
The formula is:
</p>

<pre>
ad - bc
</pre>

<p>
not:
</p>

<pre>
ad + bc
</pre>

<p>
The subtraction is essential.
</p>

<p>
For:
</p>

<pre>
[ a  b
  c  d ]
</pre>

<p>
always identify the four positions before calculating.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Determinant of a 2 × 2 Matrix', 15),
  (307, 307, '
<h2>Determinant of a 3 × 3 Matrix</h2>

<p>
A determinant of a 3 × 3 matrix can be found by expanding along a row.
In this lesson, we will use the <b>first row only</b>.
</p>

<p>
Consider:
</p>

<pre>
A = [ a  b  c
      d  e  f
      g  h  i ]
</pre>

<p>
The determinant is:
</p>

<pre>
det(A) = a(ei - fh) - b(di - fg) + c(dh - eg)
</pre>

<h3>The Method</h3>

<p>
Look at the first row:
</p>

<pre>
a    b    c
</pre>

<p>
The signs are:
</p>

<pre>
+    -    +
</pre>

<p>
For each first-row number:
</p>

<ol>
  <li>Remove its row and column.</li>
  <li>Use the remaining 2 × 2 matrix.</li>
  <li>Find that 2 × 2 determinant.</li>
  <li>Multiply by the original first-row number.</li>
  <li>Apply the correct sign.</li>
</ol>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> Find the determinant of:</p>

<pre>
A = [ 1  2  3
      0  4  5
      1  0  6 ]
</pre>

<h4>Step 1: Start with the first number, 1</h4>

<p>
The number 1 is in the first position of the first row.
Remove its row and its column.
</p>

<pre>
[ 1  2  3
  X  X  X
  X  X  X ]

Remove the row and column containing 1.

Remaining matrix:

[ 4  5
  0  6 ]
</pre>

<p>
Find its determinant:
</p>

<pre>
(4 × 6) - (5 × 0)
= 24 - 0
= 24
</pre>

<p>
The contribution from 1 is:
</p>

<pre>
+1(24) = 24
</pre>

<h4>Step 2: Use the second number, 2</h4>

<p>
The sign for the second position is negative.
Remove the row and column containing 2.
</p>

<pre>
Remaining matrix:

[ 0  5
  1  6 ]
</pre>

<p>
Find its determinant:
</p>

<pre>
(0 × 6) - (5 × 1)
= 0 - 5
= -5
</pre>

<p>
Now include the negative sign:
</p>

<pre>
-2(-5)
= +10
</pre>

<h4>Step 3: Use the third number, 3</h4>

<p>
The sign for the third position is positive.
Remove the row and column containing 3.
</p>

<pre>
Remaining matrix:

[ 0  4
  1  0 ]
</pre>

<p>
Find its determinant:
</p>

<pre>
(0 × 0) - (4 × 1)
= 0 - 4
= -4
</pre>

<p>
The contribution is:
</p>

<pre>
+3(-4)
= -12
</pre>

<h4>Step 4: Add the three contributions</h4>

<pre>
det(A)
= 24 + 10 - 12
= 22
</pre>

<p><b>Answer:</b></p>

<pre>
det(A) = 22
</pre>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> Find the determinant of:</p>

<pre>
B = [ 2  0  1
      3  4  5
      1  2  6 ]
</pre>

<p>
Expand along the first row.
The signs are:
</p>

<pre>
+    -    +
</pre>

<h4>First term</h4>

<p>
The first number is 2.
Remove its row and column.
</p>

<pre>
[ 4  5
  2  6 ]
</pre>

<p>
Calculate:
</p>

<pre>
(4 × 6) - (5 × 2)
= 24 - 10
= 14
</pre>

<p>
Multiply by 2:
</p>

<pre>
2 × 14 = 28
</pre>

<h4>Second term</h4>

<p>
The second number is 0.
Anything multiplied by zero is zero.
Therefore this whole term is:
</p>

<pre>
-0( ... ) = 0
</pre>

<h4>Third term</h4>

<p>
The third number is 1.
Remove its row and column.
</p>

<pre>
[ 3  4
  1  2 ]
</pre>

<p>
Calculate:
</p>

<pre>
(3 × 2) - (4 × 1)
= 6 - 4
= 2
</pre>

<p>
Multiply by 1:
</p>

<pre>
1 × 2 = 2
</pre>

<h4>Final calculation</h4>

<pre>
det(B)
= 28 + 0 + 2
= 30
</pre>

<p><b>Answer:</b></p>

<pre>
det(B) = 30
</pre>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> Find the determinant of:</p>

<pre>
C = [ 1  2  0
      3  4  5
      6  7  8 ]
</pre>

<h4>First term</h4>

<pre>
1 × [(4 × 8) - (5 × 7)]

= 1 × (32 - 35)

= 1 × (-3)

= -3
</pre>

<h4>Second term</h4>

<p>
Remember that the second term has a negative sign.
</p>

<pre>
-2 × [(3 × 8) - (5 × 6)]

= -2 × (24 - 30)

= -2 × (-6)

= +12
</pre>

<h4>Third term</h4>

<p>
The third number is 0, so its contribution is zero.
</p>

<pre>
+0( ... ) = 0
</pre>

<h4>Final calculation</h4>

<pre>
det(C)
= -3 + 12 + 0
= 9
</pre>

<p><b>Answer:</b></p>

<pre>
det(C) = 9
</pre>

<h3>Common Mistake</h3>

<p>
The most common mistake is forgetting the signs:
</p>

<pre>
+    -    +
</pre>

<p>
The second term must be subtracted.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Determinant of a 3 × 3 Matrix', 15),
  (308, 308, '
<h2>Singular and Non-Singular Matrices</h2>

<p>
A square matrix can be classified as either <b>singular</b> or
<b>non-singular</b>.
</p>

<h3>The One Rule</h3>

<p>
First calculate the determinant.
Then use this rule:
</p>

<pre>
det(A) = 0
→ Singular

det(A) ≠ 0
→ Non-singular
</pre>

<p>
So the determinant is what decides the classification.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> Determine whether the matrix is singular or non-singular.</p>

<pre>
A = [ 2  4
      1  2 ]
</pre>

<h4>Step 1: Find the determinant</h4>

<pre>
det(A)
= (2 × 2) - (4 × 1)
= 4 - 4
= 0
</pre>

<h4>Step 2: Apply the rule</h4>

<pre>
det(A) = 0
<p> Therefore A is <b>singular</b>. </p> <h3>Worked Example 2</h3> <p><b>Question:</b> Determine whether the matrix is singular or non-singular.</p> <pre> B = [ 3 2 5 4 ] </pre> <h4>Find the determinant</h4> <pre> det(B) = (3 × 4) - (2 × 5) = 12 - 10 = 2 </pre> <p> Since: </p> <pre> 2 ≠ 0 </pre> <p> B is <b>non-singular</b>. </p> <h3>Worked Example 3</h3> <p><b>Question:</b> Find x if the matrix below is singular.</p> <pre> A = [ x 2 3 6 ] </pre> <p> A singular matrix has determinant zero. </p> <pre> det(A) = 0 </pre> <p> Calculate the determinant: </p> <pre> (x × 6) - (2 × 3) = 0 6x - 6 = 0 </pre> <p> Add 6 to both sides: </p> <pre> 6x = 6 </pre> <p> Divide both sides by 6: </p> <pre> x = 1 </pre> <h3>Worked Example 4</h3> <p><b>Question:</b> Find x if the matrix below is singular.</p> <pre> B = [ x 1 2 3 ] </pre> <p> Because the matrix is singular: </p> <pre> det(B) = 0 </pre> <p> Therefore: </p> <pre> (x × 3) - (1 × 2) = 0 3x - 2 = 0 </pre> <p> Add 2: </p> <pre> 3x = 2 </pre> <p> Divide by 3: </p> <pre> x = 2/3 </pre> <h3>Common Mistake</h3> <p> Do not decide that a matrix is singular just because its entries look similar. Always calculate its determinant. </p> ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Singular and Non-Singular Matrices', 15),
  (309, 309, '

<h2>Inverse of a 2 × 2 Matrix</h2> <p> The inverse of a matrix A is written as: </p> <pre> A⁻¹ </pre> <p> For: </p> <pre> A = [ a b c d ] </pre> <p> the inverse is: </p> <pre> A⁻¹ = 1/(ad - bc) [ d -b -c a ] </pre> <p> The inverse exists only when: </p> <pre> ad - bc ≠ 0 </pre> <h3>The Four Actions</h3> <ol> <li>Find the determinant.</li> <li>Swap the top-left and bottom-right entries.</li> <li>Change the signs of the other two entries.</li> <li>Multiply by the reciprocal of the determinant.</li> </ol> <h3>Worked Example 1</h3> <p><b>Question:</b> Find the inverse of:</p> <pre> A = [ 3 2 1 4 ] </pre> <h4>Step 1: Find the determinant</h4> <pre> det(A) = (3 × 4) - (2 × 1) = 12 - 2 = 10 </pre> <p> The determinant is not zero, so the inverse exists. </p> <h4>Step 2: Swap the diagonal entries</h4> <pre> [ 4 2 1 3 ] </pre> <h4>Step 3: Change the signs of the other entries</h4> <pre> [ 4 -2 -1 3 ] </pre> <h4>Step 4: Multiply by 1/10</h4> <pre> A⁻¹ = 1/10 [ 4 -2 -1 3 ] </pre> <p> Therefore: </p> <pre> A⁻¹ = [ 2/5 -1/5 -1/10 3/10 ] </pre> <h3>Worked Example 2</h3> <p><b>Question:</b> Find the inverse of:</p> <pre> B = [ 2 1 5 3 ] </pre> <h4>Find the determinant</h4> <pre> det(B) = (2 × 3) - (1 × 5) = 6 - 5 = 1 </pre> <h4>Rearrange the entries</h4> <pre> [ 3 -1 -5 2 ] </pre> <p> Because the determinant is 1: </p> <pre> 1/1 = 1 </pre> <p> Therefore: </p> <pre> B⁻¹ = [ 3 -1 -5 2 ] </pre> <h3>Worked Example 3: An Inverse Does Not Exist</h3> <p><b>Question:</b> Does the following matrix have an inverse?</p> <pre> C = [ 2 4 1 2 ] </pre> <h4>Find the determinant</h4> <pre> det(C) = (2 × 2) - (4 × 1) = 4 - 4 = 0 </pre> <p> The determinant is zero. The inverse formula would require division by zero. </p> <p> Therefore C has <b>no inverse</b>. </p> <h3>Worked Example 4</h3> <p><b>Question:</b> Find the inverse of:</p> <pre> D = [ 4 1 2 1 ] </pre> <h4>Find the determinant</h4> <pre> det(D) = (4 × 1) - (1 × 2) = 4 - 2 = 2 </pre> <h4>Swap the diagonal entries</h4> <pre> [ 1 1 2 4 ] </pre> <h4>Change the signs of the other entries</h4> <pre> [ 1 -1 -2 4 ] </pre> <h4>Multiply by 1/2</h4> <pre> D⁻¹ = 1/2 [ 1 -1 -2 4 ] </pre> <p> Therefore: </p> <pre> D⁻¹ = [ 1/2 -1/2 -1 2 ] </pre> <h3>Common Mistake</h3> <p> Do not swap all four entries. Only the top-left and bottom-right entries are exchanged. The other two entries change signs. </p> ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Inverse of a 2 × 2 Matrix', 15),
  (310, 310, '

<h2>Solving Matrix Equations</h2> <p> A matrix equation can contain an unknown matrix. The aim is to isolate the unknown matrix. </p> <h3>Worked Example 1</h3> <p><b>Question:</b> Find X if:</p> <pre> X + [ 2 3 4 5 ] = [ 7 9 10 12 ] </pre> <p> We need X by itself. The matrix added to X must therefore be removed by subtraction. </p> <pre> X = [ 7 9 10 12 ] - [ 2 3 4 5 ] </pre> <p> Subtract corresponding entries: </p> <pre> 7 - 2 = 5 9 - 3 = 6 10 - 4 = 6 12 - 5 = 7 </pre> <p> Therefore: </p> <pre> X = [ 5 6 6 7 ] </pre> <h3>Worked Example 2</h3> <p><b>Question:</b> Find X if:</p> <pre> 3X = [ 12 15 9 18 ] </pre> <p> Every entry of X has been multiplied by 3. Therefore divide every entry by 3. </p> <pre> 12 ÷ 3 = 4 15 ÷ 3 = 5 9 ÷ 3 = 3 18 ÷ 3 = 6 </pre> <p> Therefore: </p> <pre> X = [ 4 5 3 6 ] </pre> <h3>Worked Example 3</h3> <p><b>Question:</b> Find X if:</p> <pre> X - [ 3 1 2 4 ] = [ 5 7 8 6 ] </pre> <p> The matrix is being subtracted from X. To undo subtraction, add the same matrix to both sides. </p> <pre> X = [ 5 7 8 6 ] + [ 3 1 2 4 ] </pre> <p> Calculate each position: </p> <pre> 5 + 3 = 8 7 + 1 = 8 8 + 2 = 10 6 + 4 = 10 </pre> <p> Therefore: </p> <pre> X = [ 8 8 10 10 ] </pre> <h3>Worked Example 4</h3> <p><b>Question:</b> Find x and y if:</p> <pre> [ x 4 7 y ] + [ 3 2 1 5 ] = [ 8 6 8 12 ] </pre> <h4>Find x</h4> <p> Look at the top-left position. </p> <pre> x + 3 = 8 </pre> <p> Subtract 3: </p> <pre> x = 5 </pre> <h4>Find y</h4> <p> Look at the bottom-right position. </p> <pre> y + 5 = 12 </pre> <p> Subtract 5: </p> <pre> y = 7 </pre> <p> Therefore: </p> <pre> x = 5 y = 7 </pre> <h3>Common Mistake</h3> <p> Always compare entries occupying the same position. Do not compare numbers simply because they appear in the same row or column. </p> ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Solving Matrix Equations', 15),
  (311, 311, '
<h2>Vector From One Point to Another</h2>

<p>
A vector can describe the movement from one point to another.
</p>

<p>
If a point moves from <b>A</b> to <b>B</b>, the vector is written:
</p>

<p><b>\vec{AB}</b></p>

<p>
The important idea is:
</p>

<p><b>Vector = final position − initial position</b></p>

<h3>1. THE MAIN RULE</h3>

<p>
Suppose:
</p>

<pre>
A(x₁,y₁)
B(x₂,y₂)
</pre>

<p>
Then the vector from A to B is:
</p>

<pre>
→       (x₂ - x₁)
AB  =
        (y₂ - y₁)
</pre>

<p>
So we subtract the coordinates of the starting point
from the coordinates of the finishing point.
</p>

<h3>2. WORKED EXAMPLE 1</h3>

<p>
Find the vector <b>AB</b> if:
</p>

<pre>
A(2,3)
B(7,5)
</pre>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Subtract the x-coordinates.</p>

<pre>
7 - 2 = 5
</pre>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Subtract the y-coordinates.</p>

<pre>
5 - 3 = 2
</pre>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> Combine the components.</p>

<pre>
→
AB = (5,2)
</pre>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p><b>AB = 5i + 2j</b></p>

<h3>3. WORKED EXAMPLE 2</h3>

<p>
Find the vector <b>PQ</b> if:
</p>

<pre>
P(-3,4)
Q(2,-1)
</pre>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Subtract the x-coordinates.</p>

<pre>
2 - (-3) = 5
</pre>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Subtract the y-coordinates.</p>

<pre>
-1 - 4 = -5
</pre>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> Write the vector.</p>

<pre>
→
PQ = (5,-5)
</pre>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p><b>PQ = 5i - 5j</b></p>

<h3>4. WORKED EXAMPLE 3</h3>

<p>
Find the vector <b>CD</b> if:
</p>

<pre>
C(6,-2)
D(1,4)
</pre>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Subtract the x-coordinates.</p>

<pre>
1 - 6 = -5
</pre>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Subtract the y-coordinates.</p>

<pre>
4 - (-2) = 6
</pre>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> Write the vector.</p>

<pre>
→
CD = (-5,6)
</pre>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p><b>CD = -5i + 6j</b></p>

<h3>5. THE DIRECTION MATTERS</h3>

<p>
The order of the letters tells us the direction.
</p>

<p>
For example, if:
</p>

<pre>
A(2,3)
B(7,5)
</pre>

<p>
then:
</p>

<pre>
→
AB = (5,2)
</pre>

<p>
But if we move in the opposite direction:
</p>

<pre>
→
BA = (2-7, 3-5)
   = (-5,-2)
</pre>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p><b>BA = -AB</b></p>

<p>
Changing the direction changes the signs of both components.
</p>

<h3>6. THE RULE TO REMEMBER</h3>

<pre>
       FINAL POINT
            -
      STARTING POINT
            =
          VECTOR
</pre>

<p>
Therefore, if:
</p>

<pre>
A(x₁,y₁)
B(x₂,y₂)
</pre>

<p>
then:
</p>

<p><b>AB = (x₂-x₁, y₂-y₁)</b></p>

<p>
The first point is always the <b>starting point</b>.
The second point is always the <b>finishing point</b>.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Vector From One Point to Another', 15),
  (312, 312, '
<h2>A Vector as a Directed Line Segment</h2>

<h3>ONE CONCEPT</h3>

<p>
A vector can be represented by a <b>directed line segment</b>.
It is a line segment with an <b>arrowhead</b> showing its direction.
</p>

<p>
For example:
</p>

<p>
<b>A ─────────→ B</b>
</p>

<p>
The arrow tells us that the vector points from <b>A to B</b>.
</p>

<p>
The two important parts of the drawing are:
</p>

<ul>
  <li><b>Length of the arrow:</b> represents the magnitude.</li>
  <li><b>Arrowhead:</b> represents the direction.</li>
</ul>

<p>
So the drawing contains the same two pieces of information we
learned earlier:
</p>

<p>
<b>Vector = magnitude + direction</b>
</p>

<h3>START AND END</h3>

<p>
In:
</p>

<p>
<b>A ───────→ B</b>
</p>

<p>
A is the <b>initial point</b> (starting point).
</p>

<p>
B is the <b>terminal point</b> (ending point).
</p>

<p>
The vector therefore describes movement from A to B.
</p>

<h3>WHY THE ARROW MATTERS</h3>

<p>
Consider:
</p>

<p>
<b>A ───────→ B</b>
</p>

<p>
and:
</p>

<p>
<b>A ←─────── B</b>
</p>

<p>
The line segment may have the same length, but the directions
are different.
</p>

<p>
The first points from A to B.
The second points from B to A.
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE 1</h3>

<p>
Consider the vector:
</p>

<p>
<b>P ───────→ Q</b>
</p>

<p>
Identify its starting point and ending point.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Look at where the arrow begins.</p>
<p>
The vector starts at P.
</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Look at where the arrowhead points.</p>
<p>
The arrowhead points toward Q.
</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b></p>
<p>
Starting point = P<br>
Ending point = Q
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE 2</h3>

<p>
A vector is drawn as:
</p>

<p>
<b>X ←────── Y</b>
</p>

<p>
Which point is the starting point?
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Find the arrowhead.</p>
<p>
The arrowhead points toward X.
</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> The vector moves toward X.</p>

<p>
Therefore it starts at Y and ends at X.
</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b> Starting point = Y.</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE 3</h3>

<p>
Two arrows have the same length. The first points east and the
second points west. Are they the same vector?
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Same length means they have the same magnitude.</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Their arrowheads point in opposite directions.</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> A vector requires both magnitude and direction.</p>

<p><b>Conclusion:</b></p>
<p>
They are <b>not the same vector</b>.
</p>

<h3>KEY IDEA</h3>

<p>
The <b>length</b> of a vector shows its magnitude.
The <b>arrowhead</b> shows its direction.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for A Vector as a Directed Line Segment', 15),
  (313, 313, '
<h2>Vector Notation</h2>

<h3>ONE CONCEPT</h3>

<p>
A vector can be named using a letter with an arrow above it,
such as:
</p>

<p>
<b>⃗a</b>
</p>

<p>
It can also be named using two points:
</p>

<p>
<b>⃗AB</b>
</p>

<p>
The notation <b>⃗AB</b> means the vector that starts at A
and ends at B.
</p>

<p>
The order of the letters matters.
</p>

<p>
<b>⃗AB</b> means A → B.
</p>

<p>
<b>⃗BA</b> means B → A.
</p>

<p>
These point in opposite directions.
</p>

<h3>WHY THE ORDER MATTERS</h3>

<p>
Suppose:
</p>

<p>
<b>A ─────────→ B</b>
</p>

<p>
The correct notation is:
</p>

<p>
<b>⃗AB</b>
</p>

<p>
If we write:
</p>

<p>
<b>⃗BA</b>
</p>

<p>
we have reversed the direction.
</p>

<h3>LETTER NOTATION</h3>

<p>
Sometimes a vector is given a single letter, for example:
</p>

<p>
<b>⃗a</b>
</p>

<p>
This is useful when performing operations such as:
</p>

<p>
<b>⃗a + ⃗b</b>
</p>

<p>
The letter is simply a name for the vector.
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE 1</h3>

<p>
A vector moves from P to Q.
Write its vector notation.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify the starting point.</p>
<p>
The vector starts at P.
</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Identify the ending point.</p>
<p>
The vector ends at Q.
</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> Write the letters in start-to-end order.</p>

<p>
<b>Answer: ⃗PQ</b>
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE 2</h3>

<p>
A vector is represented by:
</p>

<p>
<b>R ←────── S</b>
</p>

<p>
Write its vector notation.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> The arrow points toward R.</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Therefore the movement is from S to R.</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> Write start point first.</p>

<p>
<b>Answer: ⃗SR</b>
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE 3</h3>

<p>
If a vector is called <b>⃗a</b>, can we tell its direction simply
from the letter a?
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> The letter a is only a name.</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> The actual direction must come from its diagram,
description, or other information.</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b></p>

<p>
No. The letter <b>⃗a</b> names the vector but does not by itself
tell us which way it points.
</p>

<h3>KEY IDEA</h3>

<p>
For two-point notation:
</p>

<p>
<b>First letter = starting point.</b>
</p>

<p>
<b>Second letter = ending point.</b>
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Vector Notation', 15),
  (314, 314, '
<h2>Naming a Vector from Its Initial and Terminal Points</h2>

<h3>ONE CONCEPT</h3>

<p>
When a vector is drawn between two labelled points, we can name
the vector by writing the <b>initial point first</b> and the
<b>terminal point second</b>.
</p>

<p>
The initial point is where the vector starts.
</p>

<p>
The terminal point is where the arrowhead ends.
</p>

<p>
For:
</p>

<p>
<b>A ─────────→ B</b>
</p>

<p>
A is the initial point and B is the terminal point.
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>⃗AB</b>
</p>

<h3>DO NOT NAME IT BY POSITION</h3>

<p>
The correct name does not depend on which point is on the left
or right of the page.
</p>

<p>
What matters is the <b>direction of the arrow</b>.
</p>

<p>
For example:
</p>

<p>
<b>B ←──────── A</b>
</p>

<p>
The vector still moves from A to B, so it is:
</p>

<p>
<b>⃗AB</b>
</p>

<h3>WHY THIS MATTERS</h3>

<p>
If you reverse the points, you reverse the vector.
</p>

<p>
<b>⃗AB ≠ ⃗BA</b>
</p>

<p>
They have the same line and the same length, but opposite directions.
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE 1</h3>

<p>
Consider:
</p>

<p>
<b>C ─────────→ D</b>
</p>

<p>
Name the vector.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Find where the arrow starts.</p>
<p>
It starts at C.
</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Find where the arrow ends.</p>
<p>
It ends at D.
</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> Write start then end.</p>

<p><b>Answer: ⃗CD</b></p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE 2</h3>

<p>
Consider:
</p>

<p>
<b>E ←──────── F</b>
</p>

<p>
Name the vector.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> The arrowhead points toward E.</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Therefore the vector moves from F to E.</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> Write F first and E second.</p>

<p><b>Answer: ⃗FE</b></p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE 3</h3>

<p>
A vector starts at M and ends at N.
A student writes ⃗NM.
Is the student''s notation correct?
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> The vector starts at M.</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Therefore M must be written first.</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> The correct notation is ⃗MN.</p>

<p>
<b>Answer:</b> No. ⃗NM describes the opposite direction.
</p>

<h3>KEY IDEA</h3>

<p>
<b>Always follow the arrow.</b>
</p>

<p>
Start point → end point → vector name.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Naming a Vector from Its Initial and Terminal Points', 15),
  (315, 315, '
<h2>Equivalent Vectors</h2>

<h3>ONE CONCEPT</h3>

<p>
Two vectors are <b>equivalent</b> if they have the same
<b>magnitude</b> and the same <b>direction</b>.
</p>

<p>
They do not have to start at the same point.
</p>

<p>
For example:
</p>

<p>
<b>A ─────→ B</b>
</p>

<p>
and
</p>

<p>
<b>C ─────→ D</b>
</p>

<p>
If the arrows have equal length and point in the same direction,
then:
</p>

<p>
<b>⃗AB = ⃗CD</b>
</p>

<h3>THE TWO CONDITIONS</h3>

<p>
For vectors to be equivalent:
</p>

<p><b>Condition 1:</b> Same magnitude.</p>

<p>
Their sizes must be equal.
</p>

<p><b>Condition 2:</b> Same direction.</p>

<p>
They must point the same way.
</p>

<p>
Both conditions must be satisfied.
</p>

<h3>POSITION DOES NOT MATTER</h3>

<p>
An equivalent vector can be drawn somewhere else on the page.
</p>

<p>
Moving the arrow without changing its length or direction does
not change the vector it represents.
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE 1</h3>

<p>
Vector A has magnitude 6 cm and points east.
Vector B has magnitude 6 cm and points east.
Are they equivalent?
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Compare magnitudes.</p>
<p>
Both have magnitude 6 cm.
</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Compare directions.</p>
<p>
Both point east.
</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> Both conditions are satisfied.</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b></p>
<p>
Yes, the vectors are equivalent.
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE 2</h3>

<p>
Vector P has magnitude 8 m and points north.
Vector Q has magnitude 8 m and points south.
Are they equivalent?
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Compare magnitudes.</p>
<p>
Both have magnitude 8 m.
</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Compare directions.</p>
<p>
One points north and the other points south.
</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> The directions are different.</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b></p>
<p>
No. They are not equivalent.
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE 3</h3>

<p>
Vector X has magnitude 5 units and points east.
Vector Y has magnitude 7 units and points east.
Are they equivalent?
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Compare directions.</p>
<p>
Both point east.
</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Compare magnitudes.</p>
<p>
5 units ≠ 7 units.
</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> The magnitudes are different.</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b></p>
<p>
No. They are not equivalent.
</p>

<h3>KEY IDEA</h3>

<p>
<b>Equivalent vectors = same magnitude + same direction.</b>
</p>

<p>
Their starting positions can be different.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Equivalent Vectors', 15),
  (316, 316, '
<h2>Opposite Vectors</h2>

<h3>ONE CONCEPT</h3>

<p>
Two vectors are <b>opposite vectors</b> when they have the
<b>same magnitude but opposite directions</b>.
</p>

<p>
For example:
</p>

<p>
<b>⃗a = 5 m east</b>
</p>

<p>
The opposite vector is:
</p>

<p>
<b>-⃗a = 5 m west</b>
</p>

<p>
Notice what changed:
</p>

<ul>
  <li>The magnitude stayed 5 m.</li>
  <li>The direction changed to the opposite direction.</li>
</ul>

<h3>THE NEGATIVE SIGN</h3>

<p>
The negative sign in:
</p>

<p>
<b>-⃗a</b>
</p>

<p>
does not mean that the magnitude has become negative.
</p>

<p>
It means the vector has been reversed in direction.
</p>

<p>
If:
</p>

<p>
<b>⃗a = 4 units east</b>
</p>

<p>
then:
</p>

<p>
<b>-⃗a = 4 units west</b>
</p>

<h3>VECTOR NOTATION</h3>

<p>
If:
</p>

<p>
<b>⃗AB</b>
</p>

<p>
represents movement from A to B, then the opposite direction
is represented by:
</p>

<p>
<b>⃗BA</b>
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>⃗BA = -⃗AB</b>
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE 1</h3>

<p>
A vector is 7 m east. State its opposite vector.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Keep the magnitude.</p>

<p>
Magnitude remains 7 m.
</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Reverse the direction.</p>

<p>
The opposite of east is west.
</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b></p>

<p>
7 m west.
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE 2</h3>

<p>
If:
</p>

<p>
<b>⃗a = 10 N upward</b>
</p>

<p>
find <b>-⃗a</b>.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Keep the magnitude.</p>

<p>
Magnitude = 10 N.
</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Reverse the direction.</p>

<p>
The opposite of upward is downward.
</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b></p>

<p>
<b>-⃗a = 10 N downward</b>
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE 3</h3>

<p>
If:
</p>

<p>
<b>⃗PQ</b>
</p>

<p>
represents movement from P to Q, what vector represents the
opposite direction?
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> The original vector moves P → Q.</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> The opposite vector must move Q → P.</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> Write the new vector using start point then end point.</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b></p>

<p>
<b>⃗QP</b>
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>⃗QP = -⃗PQ</b>
</p>

<h3>KEY IDEA</h3>

<p>
<b>Opposite vector = same magnitude + reversed direction.</b>
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Opposite Vectors', 15),
  (317, 317, '
<h2>Scalar Quantities</h2>

<h3>ONE CONCEPT</h3>
<p>
A <b>scalar quantity</b> is a quantity that is described by
<b>magnitude only</b>.
</p>

<p>
<b>Magnitude</b> simply means the amount or size of something.
A scalar tells us <b>how much</b>, but it does not need to tell us
a direction.
</p>

<p>
For example, if we say:
</p>

<p><b>5 kg</b></p>

<p>
we know the amount of mass is 5 kilograms. There is no direction
attached to mass, so mass is a scalar quantity.
</p>

<h3>COMMON SCALAR QUANTITIES</h3>

<ul>
  <li>Mass — measured in kilograms (kg)</li>
  <li>Time — measured in seconds (s)</li>
  <li>Temperature — measured in degrees Celsius (°C)</li>
  <li>Distance — measured in metres (m)</li>
  <li>Speed — measured in metres per second (m/s)</li>
  <li>Volume — measured in cubic metres (m³)</li>
</ul>

<h3>HOW TO RECOGNISE A SCALAR</h3>

<p>
Ask one simple question:
</p>

<p>
<b>"Does this quantity need a direction to be completely described?"</b>
</p>

<p>
If the answer is <b>no</b>, the quantity is scalar.
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE 1</h3>

<p>
A bag has a mass of 8 kg. Is mass a scalar quantity?
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify the quantity.</p>
<p>The quantity is mass.</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Ask whether mass needs a direction.</p>
<p>
An object can have a mass of 8 kg regardless of whether it is
moving north, south, up, or down.
</p>

<p><b>Conclusion:</b></p>
<p>
Mass is a <b>scalar quantity</b>.
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE 2</h3>

<p>
A journey takes 45 minutes. Is time a scalar quantity?
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify the quantity.</p>
<p>The quantity is time.</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Check whether time requires direction.</p>
<p>
We only need to know that the journey lasted 45 minutes.
A direction is not required.
</p>

<p><b>Conclusion:</b></p>
<p>
Time is a <b>scalar quantity</b>.
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE 3</h3>

<p>
A car travels at 60 km/h. Is speed a scalar quantity?
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify the quantity.</p>
<p>The quantity is speed.</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Check what information speed gives us.</p>
<p>
Speed tells us how fast the car is moving. It does not specify
which direction the car is travelling.
</p>

<p><b>Conclusion:</b></p>
<p>
Speed is a <b>scalar quantity</b>.
</p>

<h3>KEY IDEA</h3>
<p>
<b>Scalar = magnitude only.</b>
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Scalar Quantities', 15),
  (318, 318, '
<h2>Vector Quantities</h2>

<h3>ONE CONCEPT</h3>

<p>
A <b>vector quantity</b> is a quantity that has both
<b>magnitude and direction</b>.
</p>

<p>
Magnitude tells us <b>how much</b>.
Direction tells us <b>which way</b>.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>Vector = magnitude + direction</b>
</p>

<p>
For example:
</p>

<p>
<b>20 m east</b>
</p>

<p>
The <b>20 m</b> gives the magnitude, while <b>east</b> gives
the direction.
</p>

<p>
If we only said <b>20 m</b>, the direction would be missing.
</p>

<h3>COMMON VECTOR QUANTITIES</h3>

<ul>
  <li>Displacement</li>
  <li>Velocity</li>
  <li>Force</li>
  <li>Acceleration</li>
</ul>

<h3>WHY DIRECTION MATTERS</h3>

<p>
Suppose a person moves 5 m.
</p>

<p>
That statement does not tell us which way the person moved.
They could have moved north, south, east, or west.
</p>

<p>
If we say:
</p>

<p><b>5 m east</b></p>

<p>
the movement is now completely described.
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE 1</h3>

<p>
A student walks 10 m north. Identify the magnitude and direction.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Find the magnitude.</p>
<p>
The number with the unit is <b>10 m</b>.
</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Find the direction.</p>
<p>
The direction is <b>north</b>.
</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b></p>
<p>
Magnitude = 10 m<br>
Direction = north
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE 2</h3>

<p>
A force of 15 N acts to the right. Identify its magnitude and direction.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Magnitude = 15 N.</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Direction = right.</p>

<p>
Therefore the vector quantity is described as:
</p>

<p><b>15 N to the right</b></p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE 3</h3>

<p>
A car has a velocity of 25 m/s east. What information makes
this a vector quantity?
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> The magnitude is 25 m/s.</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> The direction is east.</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> Both magnitude and direction are given.</p>

<p>
Therefore velocity is being described as a vector quantity.
</p>

<h3>KEY IDEA</h3>

<p>
A vector must answer two questions:
</p>

<p>
<b>How much?</b> → magnitude
</p>

<p>
<b>Which way?</b> → direction
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Vector Quantities', 15),
  (319, 319, '
<h2>Scalar vs Vector Quantities</h2>

<h3>ONE CONCEPT</h3>

<p>
The key difference between a scalar and a vector is whether
<b>direction is required</b>.
</p>

<p>
<b>Scalar:</b> magnitude only.
</p>

<p>
<b>Vector:</b> magnitude and direction.
</p>

<table>
<tr>
  <th>Quantity</th>
  <th>Type</th>
  <th>Why?</th>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td>Mass</td>
  <td>Scalar</td>
  <td>No direction is needed.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td>Time</td>
  <td>Scalar</td>
  <td>No direction is needed.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td>Distance</td>
  <td>Scalar</td>
  <td>It tells how much ground was covered.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td>Speed</td>
  <td>Scalar</td>
  <td>It tells how fast something moves.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td>Displacement</td>
  <td>Vector</td>
  <td>It requires magnitude and direction.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td>Velocity</td>
  <td>Vector</td>
  <td>It requires magnitude and direction.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td>Force</td>
  <td>Vector</td>
  <td>A force acts in a particular direction.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td>Acceleration</td>
  <td>Vector</td>
  <td>It has magnitude and direction.</td>
</tr>
</table>

<h3>THE MOST IMPORTANT TEST</h3>

<p>
When you are unsure whether a quantity is scalar or vector,
do not guess from the name.
</p>

<p>
Ask:
</p>

<p>
<b>
"If I give only the numerical size, have I completely described
the quantity?"
</b>
</p>

<p>
If yes → scalar.
</p>

<p>
If no, because direction is also needed → vector.
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE 1</h3>

<p>
Classify <b>50 km</b> of road travelled as scalar or vector.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify the quantity.</p>
<p>
The statement describes distance travelled.
</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Ask whether direction is required.</p>
<p>
Distance tells us how much ground was covered.
It does not require a direction.
</p>

<p><b>Conclusion:</b></p>
<p>
Distance is a <b>scalar</b>.
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE 2</h3>

<p>
Classify <b>50 km east</b> as scalar or vector if it describes
a displacement.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify the magnitude.</p>
<p>
Magnitude = 50 km.
</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Identify the direction.</p>
<p>
Direction = east.
</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> Both magnitude and direction are present.</p>

<p><b>Conclusion:</b></p>
<p>
Displacement is a <b>vector</b>.
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE 3</h3>

<p>
A car travels at 80 km/h north. If we are describing its velocity,
is it scalar or vector?
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Magnitude = 80 km/h.</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Direction = north.</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> Both magnitude and direction are given.</p>

<p><b>Conclusion:</b></p>
<p>
Velocity is a <b>vector quantity</b>.
</p>

<h3>KEY IDEA</h3>

<p>
<b>Direction is the deciding factor.</b>
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Scalar vs Vector Quantities', 15),
  (320, 320, '
<h2>Magnitude of a Vector</h2>

<h3>ONE CONCEPT</h3>

<p>
The <b>magnitude</b> of a vector is the <b>size of the vector</b>.
It tells us how large the vector is, without considering its direction.
</p>

<p>
For example, consider:
</p>

<p><b>8 m east</b></p>

<p>
The magnitude is:
</p>

<p><b>8 m</b></p>

<p>
The word <b>east</b> gives the direction, not the magnitude.
</p>

<h3>IMPORTANT DISTINCTION</h3>

<p>
A vector contains:
</p>

<p>
<b>magnitude + direction</b>
</p>

<p>
But the magnitude is only the numerical size of the vector
with its unit.
</p>

<h3>READING MAGNITUDE FROM A VECTOR</h3>

<p>
If a vector is described as:
</p>

<p>
<b>15 N upward</b>
</p>

<p>
then:
</p>

<p>
Magnitude = 15 N
</p>

<p>
Direction = upward
</p>

<p>
The direction does not become part of the magnitude.
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE 1</h3>

<p>
A displacement is <b>25 m south</b>. Find its magnitude.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify the numerical size.</p>
<p>
The numerical size is 25 m.
</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Ignore the direction when finding magnitude.</p>
<p>
South tells us the direction.
</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b></p>
<p>
Magnitude = <b>25 m</b>.
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE 2</h3>

<p>
A force of <b>40 N to the left</b> acts on a box.
What is the magnitude of the force?
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> The force is 40 N.</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> "To the left" describes direction.</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b></p>
<p>
Magnitude = <b>40 N</b>.
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE 3</h3>

<p>
A velocity is given as <b>18 m/s west</b>.
State its magnitude.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify the numerical value.</p>
<p>
18 m/s is the numerical size.
</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> West gives direction.</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b></p>
<p>
Magnitude = <b>18 m/s</b>.
</p>

<h3>KEY IDEA</h3>

<p>
<b>Magnitude tells how much.</b>
</p>

<p>
If a vector is written as:
</p>

<p><b>number + unit + direction</b></p>

<p>
the <b>number + unit</b> gives its magnitude.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Magnitude of a Vector', 15),
  (321, 321, '
<h2>Direction of a Vector</h2>

<h3>ONE CONCEPT</h3>

<p>
The <b>direction of a vector</b> tells us <b>which way the vector
acts or points</b>.
</p>

<p>
For example:
</p>

<p><b>12 m east</b></p>

<p>
Here:
</p>

<p>
Magnitude = 12 m
</p>

<p>
Direction = east
</p>

<p>
The direction is essential because two vectors can have the same
magnitude but point in different directions.
</p>

<h3>SAME MAGNITUDE, DIFFERENT DIRECTION</h3>

<p>
Consider:
</p>

<p>
<b>10 m east</b>
</p>

<p>
and
</p>

<p>
<b>10 m west</b>
</p>

<p>
Both have magnitude 10 m.
However, they point in opposite directions.
Therefore, they are not the same vector.
</p>

<h3>COMMON WAYS OF DESCRIBING DIRECTION</h3>

<p>
At this stage, direction can be described using simple terms such as:
</p>

<ul>
  <li>right</li>
  <li>left</li>
  <li>up</li>
  <li>down</li>
  <li>north</li>
  <li>south</li>
  <li>east</li>
  <li>west</li>
</ul>

<p>
Later, vectors can also be represented using arrows and coordinate
directions. For now, the important idea is simply that the direction
tells us which way the vector points.
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE 1</h3>

<p>
A displacement is <b>20 m north</b>. State its direction.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify the part that tells which way the movement occurred.</p>

<p>
The word <b>north</b> tells us the direction.
</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b></p>

<p>
Direction = <b>north</b>.
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE 2</h3>

<p>
A force of <b>35 N to the right</b> acts on an object.
State its direction.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Separate magnitude from direction.</p>

<p>
35 N is the magnitude.
</p>

<p>
"To the right" tells us which way the force acts.
</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b></p>

<p>
Direction = <b>right</b>.
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE 3</h3>

<p>
Two displacements are:
</p>

<p>
A = 15 m east
</p>

<p>
B = 15 m west
</p>

<p>
Do they have the same direction?
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify the direction of A.</p>
<p>
A points east.
</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Identify the direction of B.</p>
<p>
B points west.
</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> Compare the directions.</p>
<p>
East and west are opposite directions.
</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b></p>

<p>
No. They have opposite directions.
</p>

<h3>KEY IDEA</h3>

<p>
<b>Direction tells which way a vector points.</b>
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Direction of a Vector', 15),
  (322, 322, '
<h2>Column Vector Notation</h2>

<h3>ONE CONCEPT</h3>

<p>
A vector can be written using a <b>column vector</b>.
A column vector records the horizontal and vertical movement
of a vector.
</p>

<p>
For example:
</p>

<p>
\[
\begin{pmatrix}
4 \\
2
\end{pmatrix}
\]
</p>

<p>
The top number represents the <b>horizontal movement</b>.
</p>

<p>
The bottom number represents the <b>vertical movement</b>.
</p>

<p>
So:
</p>

<p>
\[
\begin{pmatrix}
4 \\
2
\end{pmatrix}
\]
</p>

<p>
means:
</p>

<p>
<b>4 units horizontally and 2 units vertically.</b>
</p>

<p>
The two numbers belong together because they describe one vector.
</p>

<h3>THE ORDER MATTERS</h3>

<p>
In:
</p>

<p>
\[
\begin{pmatrix}
4 \\
2
\end{pmatrix}
\]
</p>

<p>
4 is horizontal and 2 is vertical.
</p>

<p>
We do not swap them.
</p>

<p>
\[
\begin{pmatrix}
2 \\
4
\end{pmatrix}
\]
</p>

<p>
would describe a different movement.
</p>

<h3>POSITIVE AND NEGATIVE NUMBERS</h3>

<p>
The sign of a component tells us its direction.
For the usual coordinate system:
</p>

<ul>
  <li>Positive horizontal → right</li>
  <li>Negative horizontal → left</li>
  <li>Positive vertical → up</li>
  <li>Negative vertical → down</li>
</ul>

<p>
For example:
</p>

<p>
\[
\begin{pmatrix}
-3 \\
2
\end{pmatrix}
\]
</p>

<p>
means 3 units left and 2 units up.
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE 1</h3>

<p>
Interpret:
</p>

<p>
\[
\begin{pmatrix}
5 \\
3
\end{pmatrix}
\]
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Read the top number.</p>
<p>
5 means 5 units horizontally to the right.
</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Read the bottom number.</p>
<p>
3 means 3 units vertically upward.
</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b></p>
<p>
5 units right and 3 units up.
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE 2</h3>

<p>
Interpret:
</p>

<p>
\[
\begin{pmatrix}
-4 \\
1
\end{pmatrix}
\]
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> The top number is -4.</p>
<p>
This means 4 units left.
</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> The bottom number is 1.</p>
<p>
This means 1 unit up.
</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b></p>
<p>
4 units left and 1 unit up.
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE 3</h3>

<p>
Interpret:
</p>

<p>
\[
\begin{pmatrix}
2 \\
-6
\end{pmatrix}
\]
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> The top number is 2.</p>
<p>
Move 2 units right.
</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> The bottom number is -6.</p>
<p>
Move 6 units down.
</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b></p>
<p>
2 units right and 6 units down.
</p>

<h3>KEY IDEA</h3>

<p>
In a column vector:
</p>

<p>
\[
\begin{pmatrix}
\text{horizontal} \\
\text{vertical}
\end{pmatrix}
\]
</p>

<p>
<b>Top = horizontal.</b><br>
<b>Bottom = vertical.</b>
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Column Vector Notation', 15),
  (323, 323, '
<h2>Reading a Column Vector as Movement</h2>

<h3>ONE CONCEPT</h3>

<p>
A column vector can be read as a precise instruction for moving
from one point to another.
</p>

<p>
For example:
</p>

<p>
\[
\begin{pmatrix}
4 \\
-2
\end{pmatrix}
\]
</p>

<p>
means:
</p>

<p>
<b>4 units right, then 2 units down.</b>
</p>

<p>
The vector does not mean "4 and -2" as two unrelated numbers.
Together they describe one movement.
</p>

<h3>READING THE SIGN</h3>

<p>
For the coordinate plane:
</p>

<table>
<tr>
  <th>Component</th>
  <th>Movement</th>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td>+ horizontal</td>
  <td>Right</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td>- horizontal</td>
  <td>Left</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td>+ vertical</td>
  <td>Up</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td>- vertical</td>
  <td>Down</td>
</tr>
</table>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE 1</h3>

<p>
Interpret:
</p>

<p>
\[
\begin{pmatrix}
6 \\
1
\end{pmatrix}
\]
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Horizontal component = 6.</p>
<p>
Positive means right.
</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Vertical component = 1.</p>
<p>
Positive means up.
</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b></p>
<p>
6 units right and 1 unit up.
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE 2</h3>

<p>
Interpret:
</p>

<p>
\[
\begin{pmatrix}
-5 \\
-2
\end{pmatrix}
\]
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Horizontal component = -5.</p>
<p>
Therefore move 5 units left.
</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Vertical component = -2.</p>
<p>
Therefore move 2 units down.
</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b></p>
<p>
5 units left and 2 units down.
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE 3</h3>

<p>
A vector tells a learner to move 3 units left and 7 units up.
Write the movement as a column vector.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Horizontal movement is left.</p>

<p>
Left means negative horizontal movement:
</p>

<p>
-3
</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Vertical movement is up.</p>

<p>
Up means positive vertical movement:
</p>

<p>
+7
</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> Put horizontal first and vertical second.</p>

<p>
\[
\boxed{
\begin{pmatrix}
-3 \\
7
\end{pmatrix}}
\]
</p>

<h3>KEY IDEA</h3>

<p>
A column vector is a movement instruction:
</p>

<p>
<b>horizontal first → vertical second.</b>
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Reading a Column Vector as Movement', 15),
  (324, 324, '
<h2>Horizontal and Vertical Components</h2>

<h3>ONE CONCEPT</h3>

<p>
A column vector has two <b>components</b>.
Each component describes movement in one direction.
</p>

<p>
For:
</p>

<p>
\[
\begin{pmatrix}
-4 \\
6
\end{pmatrix}
\]
</p>

<p>
the two components are:
</p>

<p>
<b>Horizontal component = -4</b>
</p>

<p>
<b>Vertical component = 6</b>
</p>

<p>
The word <b>component</b> simply means one part of the vector
that describes a particular direction.
</p>

<h3>WHY TWO COMPONENTS?</h3>

<p>
Movement on a two-dimensional coordinate plane can change in two
directions:
</p>

<ul>
  <li>left/right</li>
  <li>up/down</li>
</ul>

<p>
Therefore we need two components to describe the movement.
</p>

<p>
The first component handles horizontal movement.
The second handles vertical movement.
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE 1</h3>

<p>
For:
</p>

<p>
\[
\begin{pmatrix}
7 \\
3
\end{pmatrix}
\]
</p>

<p>
identify the two components.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Read the top entry.</p>
<p>
Horizontal component = 7.
</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Read the bottom entry.</p>
<p>
Vertical component = 3.
</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b></p>

<p>
Horizontal component = 7<br>
Vertical component = 3
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE 2</h3>

<p>
For:
</p>

<p>
\[
\begin{pmatrix}
-8 \\
5
\end{pmatrix}
\]
</p>

<p>
identify the horizontal and vertical components.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> The first entry is -8.</p>

<p>
Therefore horizontal component = -8.
</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> The second entry is 5.</p>

<p>
Therefore vertical component = 5.
</p>

<p>
The vector therefore describes 8 units left and 5 units up.
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE 3</h3>

<p>
A vector moves 9 units left and 4 units down.
Identify its horizontal and vertical components.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Left means negative horizontal movement.</p>

<p>
Horizontal component = -9.
</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Down means negative vertical movement.</p>

<p>
Vertical component = -4.
</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b></p>

<p>
Horizontal component = -9<br>
Vertical component = -4
</p>

<h3>KEY IDEA</h3>

<p>
\[
\begin{pmatrix}
\text{horizontal component} \\
\text{vertical component}
\end{pmatrix}
\]
</p>

<p>
The first component tells us what happens left/right.
The second tells us what happens up/down.
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Horizontal and Vertical Components', 15),
  (325, 325, '
<h2>Representing a Geometric Vector as a Column Vector</h2>

<h3>ONE CONCEPT</h3>

<p>
A vector drawn as an arrow can be converted into a column vector
by identifying its <b>horizontal movement</b> and
<b>vertical movement</b>.
</p>

<p>
For example, suppose an arrow moves:
</p>

<p>
<b>4 units right and 3 units up.</b>
</p>

<p>
The horizontal component is:
</p>

<p><b>+4</b></p>

<p>
The vertical component is:
</p>

<p><b>+3</b></p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
\[
\boxed{
\begin{pmatrix}
4 \\
3
\end{pmatrix}}
\]
</p>

<h3>THE PROCESS</h3>

<p>
Whenever you convert a geometric vector into column form:
</p>

<ol>
  <li>Find the horizontal movement.</li>
  <li>Give it the correct sign.</li>
  <li>Find the vertical movement.</li>
  <li>Give it the correct sign.</li>
  <li>Put horizontal on top and vertical underneath.</li>
</ol>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE 1</h3>

<p>
A vector moves 6 units right and 2 units up.
Write it as a column vector.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Horizontal movement = 6 units right.</p>

<p>
So horizontal component = +6.
</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Vertical movement = 2 units up.</p>

<p>
So vertical component = +2.
</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> Write horizontal first.</p>

<p>
\[
\boxed{
\begin{pmatrix}
6 \\
2
\end{pmatrix}}
\]
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE 2</h3>

<p>
A vector moves 5 units left and 4 units down.
Write it as a column vector.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Left means negative horizontal movement.</p>

<p>
Horizontal component = -5.
</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Down means negative vertical movement.</p>

<p>
Vertical component = -4.
</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> Place the components in order.</p>

<p>
\[
\boxed{
\begin{pmatrix}
-5 \\
-4
\end{pmatrix}}
\]
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE 3</h3>

<p>
A vector moves 3 units left and 7 units up.
Represent it as a column vector.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Horizontal movement = 3 units left.</p>

<p>
Horizontal component = -3.
</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Vertical movement = 7 units up.</p>

<p>
Vertical component = +7.
</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> Put the horizontal component first.</p>

<p>
\[
\boxed{
\begin{pmatrix}
-3 \\
7
\end{pmatrix}}
\]
</p>

<h3>KEY IDEA</h3>

<p>
Do not guess the signs.
</p>

<p>
<b>Right = +</b><br>
<b>Left = −</b><br>
<b>Up = +</b><br>
<b>Down = −</b>
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Representing a Geometric Vector as a Column Vector', 15),
  (326, 326, '
<h2>Finding the Column Vector Between Two Points</h2>

<h3>ONE CONCEPT</h3>

<p>
When two points are given by their coordinates, we can find the
vector from one point to the other by calculating the change in
the horizontal and vertical coordinates.
</p>

<p>
Suppose:
</p>

<p>
A = (2, 3)
</p>

<p>
B = (7, 5)
</p>

<p>
We want the vector from A to B.
</p>

<p>
Ask:
</p>

<p>
<b>How much did x change?</b>
</p>

<p>
<b>How much did y change?</b>
</p>

<p>
From A to B:
</p>

<p>
Horizontal change:
</p>

<p>
7 - 2 = 5
</p>

<p>
Vertical change:
</p>

<p>
5 - 3 = 2
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
\[
\vec{AB}
=
\begin{pmatrix}
5 \\
2
\end{pmatrix}
\]
</p>

<h3>THE RULE</h3>

<p>
For a vector from:
</p>

<p>
A = (x₁, y₁)
</p>

<p>
to:
</p>

<p>
B = (x₂, y₂)
</p>

<p>
calculate:
</p>

<p>
\[
\vec{AB}
=
\begin{pmatrix}
x_2-x_1 \\
y_2-y_1
\end{pmatrix}
\]
</p>

<p>
The key idea is:
</p>

<p>
<b>destination − starting point</b>
</p>

<p>
We subtract the coordinates of where we start from the coordinates
of where we finish.
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE 1</h3>

<p>
Find the column vector from A(1, 2) to B(6, 5).
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Calculate the horizontal change.</p>

<p>
6 - 1 = 5
</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Calculate the vertical change.</p>

<p>
5 - 2 = 3
</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> Write horizontal change above vertical change.</p>

<p>
\[
\boxed{
\vec{AB}
=
\begin{pmatrix}
5 \\
3
\end{pmatrix}}
\]
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE 2</h3>

<p>
Find the vector from P(4, 7) to Q(1, 3).
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Horizontal change:</p>

<p>
1 - 4 = -3
</p>

<p>
So the movement is 3 units left.
</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Vertical change:</p>

<p>
3 - 7 = -4
</p>

<p>
So the movement is 4 units down.
</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> Write the vector.</p>

<p>
\[
\boxed{
\vec{PQ}
=
\begin{pmatrix}
-3 \\
-4
\end{pmatrix}}
\]
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE 3</h3>

<p>
Find the vector from C(-2, 4) to D(3, 1).
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Find the horizontal change.</p>

<p>
3 - (-2) = 3 + 2 = 5
</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Find the vertical change.</p>

<p>
1 - 4 = -3
</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> Put the changes into column form.</p>

<p>
\[
\boxed{
\vec{CD}
=
\begin{pmatrix}
5 \\
-3
\end{pmatrix}}
\]
</p>

<p>
So the vector represents 5 units right and 3 units down.
</p>

<h3>THE SAFEST METHOD</h3>

<p>
Never try to guess the direction from the diagram.
Use:
</p>

<p>
<b>final coordinate − starting coordinate</b>
</p>

<p>
Do it separately for x and y.
</p>

<h3>KEY IDEA</h3>

<p>
\[
\boxed{
\text{Vector}=
\begin{pmatrix}
\text{final x - starting x} \\
\text{final y - starting y}
\end{pmatrix}}
\]
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Finding the Column Vector Between Two Points', 15),
  (327, 327, '
<h2>Meaning of a Position Vector</h2>

<p>
A <b>position vector</b> describes the position of a point relative to the origin.
</p>

<p>
The origin is written as:
</p>

<p>
<b>O = (0, 0)</b>
</p>

<p>
To describe the position of point A using a vector, we draw a vector
from the origin O to A.
</p>

<p>
This vector is called the <b>position vector of A</b>.
</p>

<p>
It is written as:
</p>

<p>
<b>OA</b>
</p>

<h3>Why does it start at the origin?</h3>

<p>
The purpose of a position vector is to tell us where a point is
measured from a fixed reference point.
</p>

<p>
In coordinate geometry, that fixed reference point is the origin.
</p>

<p>
So:
</p>

<p>
<b>Position vector of A = vector from O to A</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
Point A is at (4, 3).
</p>

<p>
The position vector must start at O = (0, 0)
and finish at A = (4, 3).
</p>

<p>
Therefore the vector is:
</p>

<p>
<b>OA</b>
</p>

<p>
It represents the movement from the origin to A:
</p>

<p>
4 units right and 3 units up.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
Point B is at (-3, 5).
</p>

<p>
The position vector is the vector from O to B.
</p>

<p>
Starting at (0, 0), we move:
</p>

<p>
3 units left and 5 units up.
</p>

<p>
Therefore the position vector is:
</p>

<p>
<b>OB</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
Point C is at (2, -4).
</p>

<p>
The vector from O to C moves:
</p>

<p>
2 units right and 4 units down.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>OC</b>
</p>

<h3>Key idea</h3>

<p>
A position vector is not just any vector.
It specifically describes the position of a point by starting at the origin.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Meaning of a Position Vector', 15),
  (328, 328, '
<h2>Finding a Position Vector from Coordinates</h2>

<p>
If the coordinates of a point are known, its position vector can be written
directly from those coordinates.
</p>

<p>
If:
</p>

<p>
<b>A = (x, y)</b>
</p>

<p>
then:
</p>

<p>
<b>OA = (x, y)</b>
</p>

<p>
The first coordinate becomes the horizontal component.
</p>

<p>
The second coordinate becomes the vertical component.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
A = (3, 5)
</p>

<p>
The x-coordinate is 3.
</p>

<p>
The y-coordinate is 5.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>OA = (3, 5)</b>
</p>

<p>
This means 3 units right and 5 units up from the origin.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
B = (-4, 2)
</p>

<p>
The x-coordinate is -4.
</p>

<p>
So the horizontal movement is 4 units left.
</p>

<p>
The y-coordinate is 2.
</p>

<p>
So the vertical movement is 2 units up.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>OB = (-4, 2)</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
C = (-3, -6)
</p>

<p>
The x-coordinate is -3.
</p>

<p>
The y-coordinate is -6.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>OC = (-3, -6)</b>
</p>

<p>
The two negative signs tell us that C is left of and below the origin.
</p>

<h3>Rule</h3>

<p>
For A = (x, y):
</p>

<p>
<b>OA = (x, y)</b>
</p>

<p>
Do not change the signs.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Finding a Position Vector from Coordinates', 15),
  (329, 329, '
<h2>Finding Coordinates from a Position Vector</h2>

<p>
We can reverse the previous process.
</p>

<p>
If we are given the position vector of a point,
the two components give the coordinates of that point.
</p>

<p>
If:
</p>

<p>
<b>OA = (x, y)</b>
</p>

<p>
then:
</p>

<p>
<b>A = (x, y)</b>
</p>

<p>
The first component is the x-coordinate.
</p>

<p>
The second component is the y-coordinate.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
Given:
</p>

<p>
<b>OA = (4, 7)</b>
</p>

<p>
First component = 4.
</p>

<p>
Second component = 7.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>A = (4, 7)</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
Given:
</p>

<p>
<b>OB = (-6, 2)</b>
</p>

<p>
The first component is -6.
</p>

<p>
The second component is 2.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>B = (-6, 2)</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
Given:
</p>

<p>
<b>OC = (3, -5)</b>
</p>

<p>
The first component is 3.
</p>

<p>
The second component is -5.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>C = (3, -5)</b>
</p>

<h3>Common mistake</h3>

<p>
Do not change a negative component into a positive number.
</p>

<p>
For example:
</p>

<p>
OA = (-4, 6)
</p>

<p>
means:
</p>

<p>
<b>A = (-4, 6)</b>
</p>

<p>
not (4, 6).
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Finding Coordinates from a Position Vector', 15),
  (330, 330, '
<h2>Finding a Vector Between Two Points Using Position Vectors</h2>

<p>
Suppose A and B are two points.
</p>

<p>
We may know their position vectors:
</p>

<p>
<b>OA</b> and <b>OB</b>
</p>

<p>
We can use them to find the vector from A to B.
</p>

<p>
The rule is:
</p>

<p>
<b>AB = OB - OA</b>
</p>

<p>
In words:
</p>

<p>
<b>Vector to the destination - vector to the starting point</b>
</p>

<p>
The order matters.
For AB, we start at A and finish at B.
So we subtract A from B.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
A = (2, 3)
</p>

<p>
B = (7, 6)
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
OA = (2, 3)
</p>

<p>
OB = (7, 6)
</p>

<p>
Now:
</p>

<p>
AB = OB - OA
</p>

<p>
= (7, 6) - (2, 3)
</p>

<p>
Subtract the horizontal components:
</p>

<p>
7 - 2 = 5
</p>

<p>
Subtract the vertical components:
</p>

<p>
6 - 3 = 3
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>AB = (5, 3)</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
A = (-4, 2)
</p>

<p>
B = (3, 8)
</p>

<p>
AB = OB - OA
</p>

<p>
= (3, 8) - (-4, 2)
</p>

<p>
Horizontal:
</p>

<p>
3 - (-4) = 3 + 4 = 7
</p>

<p>
Vertical:
</p>

<p>
8 - 2 = 6
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>AB = (7, 6)</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
A = (6, 5)
</p>

<p>
B = (1, -2)
</p>

<p>
AB = OB - OA
</p>

<p>
= (1, -2) - (6, 5)
</p>

<p>
Horizontal:
</p>

<p>
1 - 6 = -5
</p>

<p>
Vertical:
</p>

<p>
-2 - 5 = -7
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>AB = (-5, -7)</b>
</p>

<h3>Check the direction</h3>

<p>
The result (-5, -7) means:
</p>

<p>
5 units left and 7 units down.
</p>

<p>
That makes sense because B is left of and below A.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Finding a Vector Between Two Points Using Position Vectors', 15),
  (331, 331, '
<h2>Finding an Unknown Point Using a Vector and a Position Vector</h2>

<p>
Sometimes a question gives us one point and the vector connecting it
to another point, but the second point is unknown.
</p>

<p>
We use the position-vector relationship:
</p>

<p>
<b>AB = OB - OA</b>
</p>

<p>
If OB is unknown, rearrange:
</p>

<p>
<b>OB = OA + AB</b>
</p>

<p>
So we can find the position vector of B by combining:
</p>

<p>
<b>position of A + movement from A to B</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
A = (2, 3)
</p>

<p>
AB = (4, 5)
</p>

<p>
Find B.
</p>

<p>
First write the position vector of A:
</p>

<p>
<b>OA = (2, 3)</b>
</p>

<p>
We know:
</p>

<p>
<b>OB = OA + AB</b>
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
OB = (2, 3) + (4, 5)
</p>

<p>
Horizontal:
</p>

<p>
2 + 4 = 6
</p>

<p>
Vertical:
</p>

<p>
3 + 5 = 8
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>OB = (6, 8)</b>
</p>

<p>
So:
</p>

<p>
<b>B = (6, 8)</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
A = (-3, 6)
</p>

<p>
AB = (5, -2)
</p>

<p>
Find B.
</p>

<p>
OA = (-3, 6)
</p>

<p>
OB = OA + AB
</p>

<p>
= (-3, 6) + (5, -2)
</p>

<p>
Horizontal:
</p>

<p>
-3 + 5 = 2
</p>

<p>
Vertical:
</p>

<p>
6 + (-2) = 4
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>B = (2, 4)</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
A = (7, 2)
</p>

<p>
AB = (-4, 6)
</p>

<p>
Find B.
</p>

<p>
OB = OA + AB
</p>

<p>
= (7, 2) + (-4, 6)
</p>

<p>
Horizontal:
</p>

<p>
7 + (-4) = 3
</p>

<p>
Vertical:
</p>

<p>
2 + 6 = 8
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>B = (3, 8)</b>
</p>

<h3>Why this works</h3>

<p>
The vector AB tells us exactly how to move from A to B.
</p>

<p>
So we start at A and apply that movement.
The result is B.
</p>

<p>
This is the same idea as:
</p>

<p>
<b>starting position + movement = destination</b>
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Finding an Unknown Point Using a Vector and a Position Vector', 15),
  (332, 332, '
<h2>Meaning of Vector Addition</h2>

<p>
<b>Vector addition</b> means combining two or more movements to find
the single movement that has the same overall effect.
</p>

<p>
The answer obtained after adding vectors is called the
<b>resultant vector</b>.
</p>

<h3>Think about movement</h3>

<p>
Suppose you move 3 units to the right and then 2 units to the right.
</p>

<p>
The total movement is:
</p>

<p>
3 + 2 = 5 units to the right.
</p>

<p>
So two movements can be replaced by one movement of 5 units to the right.
</p>

<p>
That single movement is the <b>resultant</b>.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
A vector represents a movement of 4 units to the right.
A second vector represents a movement of 3 units to the right.
</p>

<p>
The two movements are in the same direction.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
4 + 3 = 7
</p>

<p>
The resultant movement is:
</p>

<p>
<b>7 units to the right.</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
A vector represents 5 units upward.
Another vector represents 2 units upward.
</p>

<p>
Both movements are upward.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
5 + 2 = 7
</p>

<p>
The resultant movement is:
</p>

<p>
<b>7 units upward.</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
A vector represents 6 units to the right.
Another vector represents 4 units upward.
</p>

<p>
The movements are in different directions.
</p>

<p>
We cannot simply say the resultant is 10 units in one direction.
Instead, the two movements must be combined while keeping
both the horizontal and vertical parts.
</p>

<p>
The resultant movement is therefore represented by a new vector
containing the combined horizontal and vertical movement.
</p>

<h3>Key idea</h3>

<p>
Vector addition combines movements.
</p>

<p>
The result is one vector that represents the overall movement.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Meaning of Vector Addition', 15),
  (333, 333, '
<h2>Geometric Addition of Vectors</h2>

<p>
Vectors can be added by joining them head-to-tail.
</p>

<p>
The <b>head</b> of a vector is the end with the arrowhead.
The <b>tail</b> is the starting end.
</p>

<p>
To add vector <b>a</b> and vector <b>b</b>:
</p>

<ol>
<li>Draw vector a.</li>
<li>Move vector b so that its tail starts at the head of vector a.</li>
<li>Draw the resultant from the tail of a to the head of b.</li>
</ol>

<p>
The resultant is written:
</p>

<p>
<b>a + b</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
Vector a moves 3 units to the right.
Vector b moves 2 units to the right.
</p>

<p>
Place b after a.
</p>

<p>
The total movement is:
</p>

<p>
3 + 2 = 5
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>a + b = 5 units right.</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
Vector a moves 4 units upward.
Vector b moves 3 units upward.
</p>

<p>
Place b at the head of a.
</p>

<p>
The total vertical movement is:
</p>

<p>
4 + 3 = 7
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>a + b = 7 units upward.</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
Vector a moves 4 units right.
Vector b moves 3 units up.
</p>

<p>
Place the tail of b at the head of a.
</p>

<p>
The combined movement goes:
</p>

<p>
4 units right and 3 units up.
</p>

<p>
Therefore the resultant vector has:
</p>

<p>
<b>horizontal component = 4</b>
</p>

<p>
<b>vertical component = 3</b>
</p>

<p>
So its column-vector form is:
</p>

<p>
<b>(4, 3)</b>
</p>

<h3>Important</h3>

<p>
The order in which vectors are joined head-to-tail does not change
the final resultant for ordinary vector addition.
</p>

<p>
The important thing is that the vectors are connected correctly
and the resultant is drawn from the starting point to the final point.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Geometric Addition of Vectors', 15),
  (334, 334, '
<h2>Column Vector Addition</h2>

<p>
When vectors are written as column vectors, we add corresponding
components.
</p>

<p>
For example:
</p>

<p>
(2, 3) + (4, 5)
</p>

<p>
The horizontal components are added together.
</p>

<p>
2 + 4 = 6
</p>

<p>
The vertical components are added together.
</p>

<p>
3 + 5 = 8
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>(2, 3) + (4, 5) = (6, 8)</b>
</p>

<h3>Why do we add corresponding components?</h3>

<p>
The first component describes horizontal movement.
</p>

<p>
The second component describes vertical movement.
</p>

<p>
So horizontal movement must be combined with horizontal movement,
and vertical movement must be combined with vertical movement.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
Add:
</p>

<p>
<b>(3, 2) + (4, 5)</b>
</p>

<p>
Horizontal:
</p>

<p>
3 + 4 = 7
</p>

<p>
Vertical:
</p>

<p>
2 + 5 = 7
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>(3, 2) + (4, 5) = (7, 7)</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
Add:
</p>

<p>
<b>(-2, 4) + (5, 3)</b>
</p>

<p>
Horizontal:
</p>

<p>
-2 + 5 = 3
</p>

<p>
Vertical:
</p>

<p>
4 + 3 = 7
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>(-2, 4) + (5, 3) = (3, 7)</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
Add:
</p>

<p>
<b>(6, -3) + (-2, 7)</b>
</p>

<p>
Horizontal:
</p>

<p>
6 + (-2) = 4
</p>

<p>
Vertical:
</p>

<p>
-3 + 7 = 4
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>(6, -3) + (-2, 7) = (4, 4)</b>
</p>

<h3>Rule</h3>

<p>
<b>Add first components together and second components together.</b>
</p>

<p>
Never add the first component of one vector to the second component
of another vector.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Column Vector Addition', 15),
  (335, 335, '
<h2>Finding a Resultant Vector</h2>

<p>
The <b>resultant vector</b> is the single vector that represents
the combined effect of two or more vectors.
</p>

<p>
If:
</p>

<p>
<b>a = (2, 3)</b>
</p>

<p>
and:
</p>

<p>
<b>b = (4, 1)</b>
</p>

<p>
then:
</p>

<p>
<b>resultant = a + b</b>
</p>

<p>
So:
</p>

<p>
(2, 3) + (4, 1)
</p>

<p>
= (2 + 4, 3 + 1)
</p>

<p>
= <b>(6, 4)</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
A = (3, 4)
</p>

<p>
B = (2, 1)
</p>

<p>
Find the resultant.
</p>

<p>
Resultant = A + B
</p>

<p>
= (3, 4) + (2, 1)
</p>

<p>
= (5, 5)
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>Resultant = (5, 5)</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
a = (-4, 6)
</p>

<p>
b = (7, -2)
</p>

<p>
Resultant:
</p>

<p>
= (-4, 6) + (7, -2)
</p>

<p>
Horizontal:
</p>

<p>
-4 + 7 = 3
</p>

<p>
Vertical:
</p>

<p>
6 + (-2) = 4
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>Resultant = (3, 4)</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
Three vectors are:
</p>

<p>
a = (2, 1)
</p>

<p>
b = (3, -2)
</p>

<p>
c = (-1, 4)
</p>

<p>
Find the resultant.
</p>

<p>
First combine a and b:
</p>

<p>
(2, 1) + (3, -2)
</p>

<p>
= (5, -1)
</p>

<p>
Now add c:
</p>

<p>
(5, -1) + (-1, 4)
</p>

<p>
= (4, 3)
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>Resultant = (4, 3)</b>
</p>

<h3>Key idea</h3>

<p>
The resultant is not another unrelated vector.
It is the vector produced by combining all the given movements.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Finding a Resultant Vector', 15),
  (336, 336, '
<h2>Vector Addition in Geometrical Problems</h2>

<p>
Vector addition can be used to describe movement through points in a
geometrical figure.
</p>

<p>
The key idea is simple:
</p>

<p>
<b>follow the first movement, then follow the second movement.</b>
</p>

<p>
The resultant joins the starting point directly to the final point.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
A point P moves 4 units right from A to B.
It then moves 3 units up from B to C.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>AB = (4, 0)</b>
</p>

<p>
and:
</p>

<p>
<b>BC = (0, 3)</b>
</p>

<p>
The movement from A to C is:
</p>

<p>
AC = AB + BC
</p>

<p>
= (4, 0) + (0, 3)
</p>

<p>
= <b>(4, 3)</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
A moves to B by:
</p>

<p>
<b>AB = (5, 2)</b>
</p>

<p>
Then B moves to C by:
</p>

<p>
<b>BC = (-1, 4)</b>
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
AC = AB + BC
</p>

<p>
= (5, 2) + (-1, 4)
</p>

<p>
Horizontal:
</p>

<p>
5 + (-1) = 4
</p>

<p>
Vertical:
</p>

<p>
2 + 4 = 6
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>AC = (4, 6)</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
In a triangle ABC:
</p>

<p>
<b>AB = (3, 1)</b>
</p>

<p>
and:
</p>

<p>
<b>BC = (2, 5)</b>
</p>

<p>
Find AC.
</p>

<p>
To travel from A to C, we first travel from A to B
and then from B to C.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
AC = AB + BC
</p>

<p>
= (3, 1) + (2, 5)
</p>

<p>
= <b>(5, 6)</b>
</p>

<h3>Important idea</h3>

<p>
When vectors represent consecutive parts of a journey,
we can add them to find the vector from the starting point
to the final point.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Vector Addition in Geometrical Problems', 15),
  (337, 337, '
<h2>Meaning of Vector Subtraction</h2>

<p>
<b>Vector subtraction</b> means finding the movement that remains when one
vector is taken away from another vector.
</p>

<p>
For vectors, subtraction is closely connected to direction.
</p>

<p>
Suppose:
</p>

<p>
<b>a = 7 units right</b>
</p>

<p>
and:
</p>

<p>
<b>b = 3 units right</b>
</p>

<p>
Then:
</p>

<p>
<b>a - b = 4 units right</b>
</p>

<p>
because 3 units of the 7-unit movement are removed.
</p>

<h3>Why direction matters</h3>

<p>
Vectors are not just numbers.
They have direction.
</p>

<p>
Therefore, when subtracting vectors, we must subtract their corresponding
components while keeping their signs.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
A vector represents 8 units to the right.
Another vector represents 3 units to the right.
</p>

<p>
Subtract the second from the first:
</p>

<p>
8 - 3 = 5
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>Result = 5 units right.</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
A vector represents 9 units upward.
Another represents 4 units upward.
</p>

<p>
9 - 4 = 5
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>Result = 5 units upward.</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
Consider the vectors:
</p>

<p>
<b>a = (7, 5)</b>
</p>

<p>
<b>b = (2, 3)</b>
</p>

<p>
Subtract corresponding components:
</p>

<p>
Horizontal:
</p>

<p>
7 - 2 = 5
</p>

<p>
Vertical:
</p>

<p>
5 - 3 = 2
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>a - b = (5, 2)</b>
</p>

<h3>Key idea</h3>

<p>
Vector subtraction is performed component by component.
</p>

<p>
The first component is subtracted from the first component,
and the second component from the second component.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Meaning of Vector Subtraction', 15),
  (338, 338, '
<h2>Negative Vectors</h2>

<p>
The <b>negative of a vector</b> has the same magnitude as the original
vector but points in the opposite direction.
</p>

<p>
If:
</p>

<p>
<b>a = (3, 5)</b>
</p>

<p>
then:
</p>

<p>
<b>-a = (-3, -5)</b>
</p>

<p>
Both components change sign.
</p>

<h3>Why do both signs change?</h3>

<p>
The first component controls horizontal direction.
The second component controls vertical direction.
</p>

<p>
Changing both signs reverses both directions.
</p>

<p>
For example:
</p>

<p>
(3, 5)
</p>

<p>
means 3 units right and 5 units up.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
(-3, -5)
</p>

<p>
means 3 units left and 5 units down.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
Given:
</p>

<p>
<b>a = (4, 2)</b>
</p>

<p>
Change the sign of each component:
</p>

<p>
4 becomes -4.
</p>

<p>
2 becomes -2.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>-a = (-4, -2)</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
Given:
</p>

<p>
<b>b = (-3, 6)</b>
</p>

<p>
Change both signs:
</p>

<p>
-3 becomes 3.
</p>

<p>
6 becomes -6.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>-b = (3, -6)</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
Given:
</p>

<p>
<b>c = (-5, -7)</b>
</p>

<p>
Reverse both directions:
</p>

<p>
-5 becomes 5.
</p>

<p>
-7 becomes 7.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>-c = (5, 7)</b>
</p>

<h3>Connection with subtraction</h3>

<p>
A vector can be subtracted by adding its negative.
</p>

<p>
For example:
</p>

<p>
<b>a - b = a + (-b)</b>
</p>

<p>
This idea will help us understand geometric subtraction.
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Negative Vectors', 15),
  (339, 339, '
<h2>Geometric Vector Subtraction</h2>

<p>
We can understand vector subtraction by thinking about the direction
of the second vector.
</p>

<p>
The key rule is:
</p>

<p>
<b>a - b = a + (-b)</b>
</p>

<p>
This means:
</p>

<ol>
<li>Keep vector a.</li>
<li>Reverse the direction of vector b to get -b.</li>
<li>Add a and -b.</li>
</ol>

<p>
So subtraction can be viewed as adding a vector pointing in the
opposite direction.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
Suppose:
</p>

<p>
<b>a = 6 units right</b>
</p>

<p>
and:
</p>

<p>
<b>b = 2 units right</b>
</p>

<p>
The opposite of b is 2 units left.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
a - b
</p>

<p>
means:
</p>

<p>
6 units right + 2 units left.
</p>

<p>
The result is:
</p>

<p>
<b>4 units right.</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
Let:
</p>

<p>
<b>a = (5, 3)</b>
</p>

<p>
<b>b = (2, 1)</b>
</p>

<p>
The negative of b is:
</p>

<p>
<b>-b = (-2, -1)</b>
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
a - b = a + (-b)
</p>

<p>
= (5, 3) + (-2, -1)
</p>

<p>
= <b>(3, 2)</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
Let:
</p>

<p>
<b>a = (4, 6)</b>
</p>

<p>
<b>b = (-2, 3)</b>
</p>

<p>
First reverse b:
</p>

<p>
<b>-b = (2, -3)</b>
</p>

<p>
Now:
</p>

<p>
a - b
</p>

<p>
= (4, 6) + (2, -3)
</p>

<p>
= <b>(6, 3)</b>
</p>

<h3>Why this works</h3>

<p>
Subtracting a vector means moving in the opposite direction of that
vector.
</p>

<p>
That is why:
</p>

<p>
<b>a - b = a + (-b)</b>
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Geometric Vector Subtraction', 15),
  (340, 340, '
<h2>Column Vector Subtraction</h2>

<p>
When vectors are written as column vectors, subtract corresponding
components.
</p>

<p>
The horizontal components are subtracted from each other.
</p>

<p>
The vertical components are also subtracted from each other.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
Find:
</p>

<p>
<b>(7, 5) - (3, 2)</b>
</p>

<p>
Horizontal:
</p>

<p>
7 - 3 = 4
</p>

<p>
Vertical:
</p>

<p>
5 - 2 = 3
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>(7, 5) - (3, 2) = (4, 3)</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
Find:
</p>

<p>
<b>(2, 6) - (-4, 3)</b>
</p>

<p>
Horizontal:
</p>

<p>
2 - (-4) = 2 + 4 = 6
</p>

<p>
Vertical:
</p>

<p>
6 - 3 = 3
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>(2, 6) - (-4, 3) = (6, 3)</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
Find:
</p>

<p>
<b>(-3, 4) - (5, -2)</b>
</p>

<p>
Horizontal:
</p>

<p>
-3 - 5 = -8
</p>

<p>
Vertical:
</p>

<p>
4 - (-2) = 6
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>(-3, 4) - (5, -2) = (-8, 6)</b>
</p>

<h3>Common mistake</h3>

<p>
Do not subtract across components.
</p>

<p>
For example, do NOT calculate:
</p>

<p>
7 - 2 and 5 - 3.
</p>

<p>
The correct order is:
</p>

<p>
first with first,
second with second.
</p>

<p>
So:
</p>

<p>
<b>(7, 5) - (3, 2) = (7 - 3, 5 - 2)</b>
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Column Vector Subtraction', 15),
  (341, 341, '
<h2>Finding the Difference Between Two Vectors</h2>

<p>
In examination questions, you may be given two vectors and asked to
find their <b>difference</b>.
</p>

<p>
The word "difference" means subtraction.
</p>

<p>
If vectors a and b are given, then:
</p>

<p>
<b>Difference = a - b</b>
</p>

<p>
The order must be followed exactly.
</p>

<p>
<b>a - b</b> is generally not the same as <b>b - a</b>.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
Given:
</p>

<p>
<b>a = (8, 6)</b>
</p>

<p>
<b>b = (3, 2)</b>
</p>

<p>
Find a - b.
</p>

<p>
a - b
</p>

<p>
= (8, 6) - (3, 2)
</p>

<p>
= (8 - 3, 6 - 2)
</p>

<p>
= <b>(5, 4)</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
Given:
</p>

<p>
<b>a = (-2, 7)</b>
</p>

<p>
<b>b = (4, 3)</b>
</p>

<p>
Find a - b.
</p>

<p>
a - b
</p>

<p>
= (-2, 7) - (4, 3)
</p>

<p>
Horizontal:
</p>

<p>
-2 - 4 = -6
</p>

<p>
Vertical:
</p>

<p>
7 - 3 = 4
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>a - b = (-6, 4)</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
Given:
</p>

<p>
<b>a = (5, -3)</b>
</p>

<p>
<b>b = (-2, 4)</b>
</p>

<p>
Find a - b.
</p>

<p>
a - b
</p>

<p>
= (5, -3) - (-2, 4)
</p>

<p>
Horizontal:
</p>

<p>
5 - (-2) = 7
</p>

<p>
Vertical:
</p>

<p>
-3 - 4 = -7
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>a - b = (7, -7)</b>
</p>

<h3>Important examination rule</h3>

<p>
Do not reverse the vectors.
</p>

<p>
If the question says:
</p>

<p>
<b>a - b</b>
</p>

<p>
start with a and subtract b.
</p>

<p>
If it says:
</p>

<p>
<b>b - a</b>
</p>

<p>
start with b and subtract a.
</p>

<p>
Changing the order changes the answer.
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Finding the Difference Between Two Vectors', 15),
  (342, 342, '
<h2>Meaning of Scalar Multiplication</h2>

<h3>What does it mean to multiply a vector by a scalar?</h3>

<p>
A <b>scalar</b> is an ordinary number such as 2, 3, -1 or 5.
</p>

<p>
When we multiply a vector by a scalar, we are changing the vector according to that number.
The scalar tells us <b>how much to scale the vector</b>.
</p>

<p>
For example, if
<b>a = (3, 2)</b>,
then
<b>2a</b>
means that every component of the vector is multiplied by 2.
</p>

<p>
So:
</p>

<p>
<b>2a = 2(3, 2) = (6, 4)</b>
</p>

<h3>The important rule</h3>

<p>
If
<b>a = (x, y)</b>,
then
<b>ka = (kx, ky)</b>.
</p>

<p>
The scalar is applied to <b>every component</b> of the vector.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
Given
<b>a = (4, 3)</b>,
find
<b>2a</b>.
</p>

<p>
Step 1: Write the vector.
<br>
<b>a = (4, 3)</b>
</p>

<p>
Step 2: Multiply every component by 2.
<br>
<b>2a = (2 × 4, 2 × 3)</b>
</p>

<p>
Step 3: Calculate.
<br>
<b>2a = (8, 6)</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
Given
<b>b = (-2, 5)</b>,
find
<b>3b</b>.
</p>

<p>
Multiply both components by 3:
</p>

<p>
<b>3b = (3 × -2, 3 × 5)</b>
</p>

<p>
<b>3b = (-6, 15)</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
Given
<b>c = (7, -4)</b>,
find
<b>2c</b>.
</p>

<p>
Multiply each component by 2:
</p>

<p>
<b>2c = (2 × 7, 2 × -4)</b>
</p>

<p>
<b>2c = (14, -8)</b>
</p>

<h3>Key idea</h3>

<p>
Scalar multiplication does not mean multiplying the two components together.
It means multiplying <b>each component</b> by the scalar.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Meaning of Scalar Multiplication', 15),
  (343, 343, '
<h2>Recognising a Scalar Multiple</h2>

<h3>What is a scalar multiple?</h3>

<p>
A vector is a <b>scalar multiple</b> of another vector if it can be obtained by multiplying the whole vector by one number.
</p>

<p>
For example:
</p>

<p>
<b>a = (2, 3)</b>
</p>

<p>
Multiplying a by 2 gives:
</p>

<p>
<b>2a = (4, 6)</b>
</p>

<p>
Therefore <b>(4, 6)</b> is a scalar multiple of <b>(2, 3)</b>.
</p>

<h3>What must happen?</h3>

<p>
The <b>same scalar</b> must multiply every component.
</p>

<p>
For example:
</p>

<p>
<b>(2, 3) → (6, 9)</b>
</p>

<p>
Both components were multiplied by 3:
</p>

<p>
<b>2 × 3 = 6</b>
<br>
<b>3 × 3 = 9</b>
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>(6, 9) = 3(2, 3)</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
Is <b>(8, 12)</b> a scalar multiple of <b>(2, 3)</b>?
</p>

<p>
Compare the corresponding components:
</p>

<p>
<b>8 ÷ 2 = 4</b>
</p>

<p>
<b>12 ÷ 3 = 4</b>
</p>

<p>
The same number, 4, was used for both components.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>(8, 12) = 4(2, 3)</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
Is <b>(-6, 10)</b> a scalar multiple of <b>(-3, 5)</b>?
</p>

<p>
<b>-6 ÷ -3 = 2</b>
</p>

<p>
<b>10 ÷ 5 = 2</b>
</p>

<p>
The same scalar is obtained.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>(-6, 10) = 2(-3, 5)</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
Is <b>(6, 8)</b> a scalar multiple of <b>(2, 3)</b>?
</p>

<p>
<b>6 ÷ 2 = 3</b>
</p>

<p>
But:
</p>

<p>
<b>8 ÷ 3 ≠ 3</b>
</p>

<p>
The same scalar does not work for both components.
</p>

<p>
Therefore <b>(6, 8)</b> is <b>not</b> a scalar multiple of <b>(2, 3)</b>.
</p>

<h3>Key rule</h3>

<p>
To recognise a scalar multiple, the <b>same number must multiply every component</b>.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Recognising a Scalar Multiple', 15),
  (344, 344, '
<h2>Adding Like Vector Terms</h2>

<h3>What is a like vector term?</h3>

<p>
Two vector terms are <b>like terms</b> when they contain the same vector.
</p>

<p>
For example:
</p>

<p>
<b>2a</b> and <b>5a</b>
</p>

<p>
are like terms because both contain vector <b>a</b>.
</p>

<p>
Just as:
</p>

<p>
<b>2x + 5x = 7x</b>
</p>

<p>
we can write:
</p>

<p>
<b>2a + 5a = 7a</b>
</p>

<h3>Why can we combine them?</h3>

<p>
If
<b>a = (3, 2)</b>,
then:
</p>

<p>
<b>2a = (6, 4)</b>
</p>

<p>
and:
</p>

<p>
<b>5a = (15, 10)</b>
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>2a + 5a = (6,4) + (15,10)</b>
</p>

<p>
<b>= (21,14)</b>
</p>

<p>
And:
</p>

<p>
<b>7a = 7(3,2) = (21,14)</b>
</p>

<p>
So:
</p>

<p>
<b>2a + 5a = 7a</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
Simplify:
</p>

<p>
<b>3a + 4a</b>
</p>

<p>
Both terms contain <b>a</b>.
</p>

<p>
Add their scalar coefficients:
</p>

<p>
<b>3 + 4 = 7</b>
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>3a + 4a = 7a</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
Simplify:
</p>

<p>
<b>6b + 2b</b>
</p>

<p>
Both terms contain <b>b</b>.
</p>

<p>
<b>6 + 2 = 8</b>
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>6b + 2b = 8b</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
Simplify:
</p>

<p>
<b>5c + 7c</b>
</p>

<p>
Add the coefficients:
</p>

<p>
<b>5 + 7 = 12</b>
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>5c + 7c = 12c</b>
</p>

<h3>Key rule</h3>

<p>
When adding like vector terms, <b>add the scalar coefficients and keep the vector</b>.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Adding Like Vector Terms', 15),
  (345, 345, '
<h2>Magnitude from a Column Vector</h2>

<h3>What is magnitude?</h3>

<p>
The <b>magnitude</b> of a vector is its length.
</p>

<p>
If a vector moves horizontally by <b>x</b> units and vertically by <b>y</b> units, its magnitude is found using Pythagoras'' theorem.
</p>

<p>
For a vector
<b>a = (x, y)</b>:
</p>

<p>
<b>|a| = √(x² + y²)</b>
</p>

<h3>Why does this formula work?</h3>

<p>
The horizontal component, vertical component and vector itself form a right-angled triangle.
</p>

<p>
Pythagoras'' theorem says:
</p>

<p>
<b>hypotenuse² = horizontal² + vertical²</b>
</p>

<p>
The vector is the hypotenuse, so:
</p>

<p>
<b>|a|² = x² + y²</b>
</p>

<p>
Taking the square root:
</p>

<p>
<b>|a| = √(x² + y²)</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
Find the magnitude of:
</p>

<p>
<b>a = (3, 4)</b>
</p>

<p>
Use:
</p>

<p>
<b>|a| = √(3² + 4²)</b>
</p>

<p>
<b>= √(9 + 16)</b>
</p>

<p>
<b>= √25</b>
</p>

<p>
<b>= 5</b>
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>|a| = 5</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
Find the magnitude of:
</p>

<p>
<b>b = (-6, 8)</b>
</p>

<p>
<b>|b| = √((-6)² + 8²)</b>
</p>

<p>
<b>= √(36 + 64)</b>
</p>

<p>
<b>= √100</b>
</p>

<p>
<b>= 10</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
Find the magnitude of:
</p>

<p>
<b>c = (5, 12)</b>
</p>

<p>
<b>|c| = √(5² + 12²)</b>
</p>

<p>
<b>= √(25 + 144)</b>
</p>

<p>
<b>= √169</b>
</p>

<p>
<b>= 13</b>
</p>

<h3>Important</h3>

<p>
Negative components are squared.
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>(-6)² = 36</b>
</p>

<p>
not -36.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Magnitude from a Column Vector', 15),
  (346, 346, '
<h2> Gradient of a Curve</h2>

<h3> DEEP NOTES</h3>
<p>
The gradient of a curve shows how steep the curve is at a specific point. It is calculated using differentiation and represents the instantaneous rate of change.
</p>

<pre>
dy/dx = gradient at a point
</pre>

 It represents the slope of the tangent at that point.

---

<h3> EXAMPLES</h3>

<p><b>Example 1:</b> y = x² → gradient at x = 2 is 4</p>
<p><b>Example 2:</b> y = x³ → gradient at x = 1 is 3</p>
<p><b>Example 3:</b> y = 2x + 5 → gradient is constant = 2</p>

---

<h3> WORKED EXAMPLES</h3>

<p><b>Example 1</b></p>
<p><b>Question:</b> Find gradient of y = x² at x = 3</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Differentiate</p>
<p>dy/dx = 2x</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Substitute x = 3</p>
<p>dy/dx = 6</p>
<p><b>Final Answer:</b> gradient = 6</p>

<br>

<p><b>Example 2</b></p>
<p><b>Question:</b> Find gradient of y = 5x at any point</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Differentiate</p>
<p>dy/dx = 5</p>
<p><b>Final Answer:</b> gradient is constant = 5</p>

---

<h3> DIAGRAM</h3>

<pre>
      /
     /  ← tangent line (gradient here)
    /
---•---------- curve point
</pre>

---

<h3> REAL WORLD APPLICATION</h3>
<ul>
<li>Road slope measurement</li>
<li>Mountain incline calculation</li>
<li>Engineering design of ramps</li>
</ul>

---
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Gradient of a Curve', 15),
  (347, 347, '
<h2> Rate of Change</h2>

<h3> DEEP NOTES</h3>
<p>
Rate of change describes how one quantity changes with respect to another. It is the foundation of differentiation.
</p>

<pre>
dy/dx = rate of change
</pre>

---

<h3> EXAMPLES</h3>

<p><b>Example 1:</b> distance vs time = speed</p>
<p><b>Example 2:</b> y = x² → rate = 2x</p>
<p><b>Example 3:</b> population growth curve</p>

---

<h3> WORKED EXAMPLES</h3>

<p><b>Example 1</b></p>
<p><b>Question:</b> If s = t², find rate of change of distance.</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Differentiate</p>
<p>ds/dt = 2t</p>
<p><b>Final Answer:</b> rate = 2t</p>

<br>

<p><b>Example 2</b></p>
<p><b>Question:</b> Find rate of change of y = x² at x = 3</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Differentiate</p>
<p>dy/dx = 2x</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Substitute x = 3</p>
<p>dy/dx = 6</p>
<p><b>Final Answer:</b> 6</p>

---

<h3> DIAGRAM</h3>

<pre>
Time →
Distance curve rising ↑
Slope shows speed
</pre>

---

<h3> REAL WORLD APPLICATION</h3>
<ul>
<li>Speed of cars (physics)</li>
<li>Stock market growth rate</li>
<li>Population increase models</li>
</ul>

---
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Rate of Change', 15),
  (348, 348, '
<h2> Maxima and Minima</h2>

<h3> DEEP NOTES</h3>
<p>
Maxima are highest points and minima are lowest points of a curve.  
They occur where the derivative equals zero.
</p>

<pre>
dy/dx = 0 → critical point
</pre>

---

<h3> EXAMPLES</h3>

<p><b>Example 1:</b> y = x² has minimum at x = 0</p>
<p><b>Example 2:</b> y = −x² has maximum at x = 0</p>
<p><b>Example 3:</b> profit optimization in business models</p>

---

<h3> WORKED EXAMPLES</h3>

<p><b>Example 1</b></p>
<p><b>Question:</b> Find stationary point of y = x²</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> dy/dx = 2x</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> 2x = 0 → x = 0</p>
<p><b>Final Answer:</b> minimum at x = 0</p>

<br>

<p><b>Example 2</b></p>
<p><b>Question:</b> Find maxima of y = −x²</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> dy/dx = −2x</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> −2x = 0 → x = 0</p>
<p><b>Final Answer:</b> maximum at x = 0</p>

---
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Maxima and Minima', 15),
  (349, 349, '
<h2> Tangents and Normals</h2>

<h3> DEEP NOTES</h3>
<p>
A tangent is a straight line that touches a curve at exactly one point without crossing it locally.
A normal is a line perpendicular to the tangent at the same point.
</p>

<pre>
Slope of tangent = dy/dx  
Slope of normal = -1 / (dy/dx)
</pre>

---

<h3> EXAMPLES</h3>

<p><b>Example 1:</b> slope of tangent = dy/dx</p>
<p><b>Example 2:</b> slope of normal = -1/(dy/dx)</p>
<p><b>Example 3:</b> curve intersection point analysis</p>

---

<h3> WORKED EXAMPLES (3 EXAM-STYLE)</h3>

<p><b>Example 1</b></p>
<p><b>Question:</b> Find slope of tangent to y = x² at x = 2</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Differentiate</p>
<p>dy/dx = 2x</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Substitute x = 2</p>
<p>dy/dx = 4</p>
<p><b>Final Answer:</b> slope of tangent = 4</p>

<br>

<p><b>Example 2</b></p>
<p><b>Question:</b> Find slope of normal when slope of tangent is 3</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Use formula</p>
<p>slope(normal) = -1/3</p>
<p><b>Final Answer:</b> -1/3</p>

<br>

<p><b>Example 3</b></p>
<p><b>Question:</b> Why are tangent and normal perpendicular?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> They intersect at 90°</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Product of slopes = -1</p>
<p><b>Final Answer:</b> Because perpendicular lines satisfy m₁·m₂ = -1</p>

---

<h3> DIAGRAM</h3>

<pre>
      tangent /
             /
   curve •---
                           normal ⟂
</pre>

---

<h3> REAL WORLD APPLICATION</h3>
<ul>
<li>Road design angles</li>
<li>Reflection of light in physics</li>
<li>Engineering stress directions</li>
</ul>

---
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Tangents and Normals', 15),
  (350, 350, '
<h2> Applications of Differentiation</h2>


<h3> DEEP NOTES</h3>
<p>
Differentiation is used to model change in real systems. It tells how fast one quantity changes with respect to another.
</p>

<p>
Geometrically, the derivative represents the slope of a curve at a point.
</p>

<pre>
f''(x) = rate of change
</pre>

---

<h3> EXAMPLES</h3>

<p><b>Example 1:</b> speed of moving object</p>
<p><b>Example 2:</b> maximizing profit function</p>
<p><b>Example 3:</b> minimizing cost of production</p>

---

<h3> WORKED EXAMPLES (3 EXAM-STYLE)</h3>

<p><b>Example 1</b></p>
<p><b>Question:</b> A position is given by s(t) = t². Find velocity.</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Differentiate position</p>
<p>v(t) = ds/dt = 2t</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Interpret result</p>
<p>Velocity increases with time</p>
<p><b>Final Answer:</b> v(t) = 2t</p>

<br>

<p><b>Example 2</b></p>
<p><b>Question:</b> Find stationary points of f(x) = x² - 4x</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Differentiate</p>
<p>f''(x) = 2x - 4</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Set derivative to zero</p>
<p>2x - 4 = 0 → x = 2</p>
<p><b>Final Answer:</b> x = 2 is a stationary point</p>

<br>

<p><b>Example 3</b></p>
<p><b>Question:</b> Why is differentiation used in optimization?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify maximum/minimum points</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> These occur when slope = 0</p>
<p><b>Final Answer:</b> Because derivatives help locate maxima and minima</p>

---

<h3> DIAGRAM</h3>

<pre>
Curve → slope at each point = change rate
Peak point → slope = 0
</pre>

---

<h3> REAL WORLD APPLICATION</h3>
<ul>
<li>Physics motion equations</li>
<li>AI gradient descent learning</li>
<li>Economics optimization models</li>
</ul>

---
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Applications of Differentiation', 15),
  (351, 351, '
<h2> Area Under a Curve</h2>

<h3> DEEP NOTES</h3>
<p>
Integration is used to calculate the area between a curve and the x-axis over a given interval.
For positive functions, this area is directly given by a definite integral.
</p>

<pre>
∫ f(x) dx = area under curve
</pre>

 It is the reverse process of differentiation and accumulates infinitely small slices into a total area.

---

<h3> EXAMPLES</h3>

<p><b>Example 1:</b> ∫ x dx = x²/2</p>
<p><b>Example 2:</b> ∫ x² dx = x³/3</p>
<p><b>Example 3:</b> ∫ 2x dx = x²</p>

---

<h3> WORKED EXAMPLES (3 EXAM-STYLE)</h3>

<p><b>Example 1</b></p>
<p><b>Question:</b> Find ∫₀¹ x dx</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Antiderivative of x</p>
<p>x²/2</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Apply limits</p>
<p>(1²/2) - (0²/2)</p>
<p><b>Final Answer:</b> 1/2 (area under curve)</p>

<br>

<p><b>Example 2</b></p>
<p><b>Question:</b> Find ∫₀² x² dx</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Antiderivative</p>
<p>x³/3</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Apply limits</p>
<p>(2³/3) - (0³/3)</p>
<p><b>Final Answer:</b> 8/3</p>

<br>

<p><b>Example 3</b></p>
<p><b>Question:</b> What does a definite integral represent geometrically?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> It sums infinitely small rectangles under curve</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Total gives enclosed region</p>
<p><b>Final Answer:</b> The area under a curve over an interval</p>

