<h2> Reverse of Differentiation</h2>

<h3> DEEP NOTES</h3>
<p>
Integration reverses differentiation.
If dy/dx = f(x), then ∫f(x) dx = original function.
</p>

<h3> EXAMPLES</h3>

<p><b>Example 1:</b> derivative x² → integral gives x³/3</p>
<p><b>Example 2:</b> derivative 2x → integral gives x²</p>
<p><b>Example 3:</b> checking correctness of solutions</p>

---

<h3> WORKED EXAMPLES (3 EXAM-STYLE)</h3>

<p><b>Example 1</b></p>
<p><b>Question:</b> Find ∫2x dx</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Apply power rule in reverse</p>
<p>∫2x dx = x² + C</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Add constant of integration</p>
<p><b>Final Answer:</b> x² + C</p>

<br>

<p><b>Example 2</b></p>
<p><b>Question:</b> Find ∫3x² dx</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Increase power by 1</p>
<p>3x² → x³</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Divide by new power</p>
<p>∫3x² dx = x³ + C</p>
<p><b>Final Answer:</b> x³ + C</p>

<br>

<p><b>Example 3</b></p>
<p><b>Question:</b> If dy/dx = 4x³, find y</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Integrate both sides</p>
<p>y = ∫4x³ dx</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Apply rule</p>
<p>y = x⁴ + C</p>
<p><b>Final Answer:</b> y = x⁴ + C</p>

---

<h3> DIAGRAM</h3>

<pre>
Differentiation ↓
Integration ↑ (reverse process)
</pre>

---

<h3> REAL WORLD APPLICATION</h3>
<ul>
<li>Recovering position from velocity</li>
<li>Signal reconstruction in electronics</li>
<li>AI model inversion problems</li>
</ul>

---
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Integration as Reverse of Differentiation', 15),
  (355, 355, '
<h2> Applications of Integration</h2>

<h3> DEEP NOTES</h3>
<p>
Integration is used to accumulate small changes into a total result. It is essentially the reverse process of differentiation.
</p>

<pre>
∫ f(x) dx → total accumulation
</pre>
<h3> WORKED EXAMPLES (MATHEMATICAL CALCULATION STYLE)</h3>

<p><b>Example 1</b></p>
<p><b>Question:</b> Show why ∫ v(t) dt gives displacement</p>
<p><b>Hint:</b> velocity = rate of change of displacement</p>
<p><b>Steps:</b></p>
<p>Step 1: Let v(t) = ds/dt</p>
<p>Step 2: Multiply both sides by dt → ds = v(t)dt</p>
<p>Step 3: Integrate both sides → ∫ ds = ∫ v(t)dt</p>
<p>Step 4: Left side becomes displacement s(t)</p>
<p>Step 5: Final result → s(t) = ∫ v(t)dt</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> Integration of velocity gives displacement</p>
<p><b>Explanation:</b> Integration reverses differentiation and accumulates total change</p>

<br>

<p><b>Example 2</b></p>
<p><b>Question:</b> Find area under curve using integration idea</p>
<p><b>Hint:</b> sum of rectangles</p>
<p><b>Steps:</b></p>
<p>Step 1: Divide area into small width Δx</p>
<p>Step 2: Height of each rectangle = f(x)</p>
<p>Step 3: Area of one strip = f(x)Δx</p>
<p>Step 4: Sum all strips → Σ f(x)Δx</p>
<p>Step 5: Take limit as Δx → 0</p>
<p><b>Final expression:</b> ∫ f(x) dx</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> Integration gives total area</p>

<br>

<p><b>Example 3</b></p>
<p><b>Question:</b> Show why ∫ F dx gives work done in physics</p>
<p><b>Hint:</b> force × distance</p>
<p><b>Steps:</b></p>
<p>Step 1: Small work done dW = F dx</p>
<p>Step 2: Add all small work contributions</p>
<p>Step 3: ∫ dW = ∫ F dx</p>
<p>Step 4: Total work W = ∫ F dx</p>
<p>Step 5: Result gives accumulated energy transfer</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> W = ∫ F dx</p>
<p><b>Explanation:</b> Integration sums continuous force over distance</p>

<h3> REAL WORLD APPLICATION</h3>
<ul>
<li>Engineering design (volume of irregular objects)</li>
<li>Physics motion tracking</li>
<li>Economics cumulative profit analysis</li>
</ul>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Applications of Integration', 15),
  (356, 356, '
  <h2>Meaning of Force</h2>

  <h3>Foundation Understanding</h3>
  <p>A <b>force</b> is a push or pull arising from an interaction between objects. A force can change an object''s state of motion, its direction of movement, or its shape.</p>

  <div class="keyfact">
    KEY IDEA: A force can change motion or deform an object. An object does not have to move whenever a force acts on it.
  </div>

  <h3>What Can a Force Do?</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Start an object moving.</li>
    <li>Stop a moving object.</li>
    <li>Increase or decrease its speed.</li>
    <li>Change its direction of motion.</li>
    <li>Change its shape or size.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Force as a Vector Quantity</h3>
  <p>Force is a <b>vector quantity</b> because both its magnitude and direction matter. A force of 10 N east is different from a force of 10 N west.</p>

  <h3>Unit and Measurement</h3>
  <p>The SI unit of force is the <b>newton (N)</b>. A force can be measured using a calibrated spring balance or force meter.</p>

  <div class="formula">
    1 N = 1 kg m/s²
  </div>
  <p>This means that a net force of 1 N produces an acceleration of 1 m/s² in a mass of 1 kg.</p>

  <h3>Representing a Force</h3>
  <p>A force can be represented by an arrow. The arrow points in the direction of the force, while its length can represent the force''s magnitude using a chosen scale.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Changing Motion</h3>
  <div class="example-box">
    A stationary football is kicked and begins moving.

    <br/><br/><b>Step 1:</b> Identify the initial state. The football is stationary.

    <br/><br/><b>Step 2:</b> Identify the interaction. The player''s foot exerts a force on the football.

    <br/><br/><b>Step 3:</b> Identify the effect. The football changes from rest to motion.

    <br/><br/><b>Answer:</b> The force changes the football''s state of motion.
  </div>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Changing Shape</h3>
  <div class="example-box">
    A student squeezes a soft sponge.

    <br/><br/><b>Step 1:</b> The student''s hands exert forces on the sponge.

    <br/><br/><b>Step 2:</b> The sponge changes shape under the applied forces.

    <br/><br/><b>Answer:</b> The force deforms the sponge.
  </div>

  <h3>Important Distinction</h3>
  <p>A force can act without producing a change in motion. For example, a book resting on a table experiences gravity downward and an upward supporting force from the table. These forces balance, so the book remains at rest.</p>

  <h3>Common Misconceptions</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Assuming every force must make an object move.</li>
    <li>Thinking force has magnitude but no direction.</li>
    <li>Confusing the unit of force (newton) with the unit of mass (kilogram).</li>
  </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Meaning of Force', 15),
  (357, 357, '
  <h2>Types of Forces</h2>

  <h3>Foundation Understanding</h3>
  <p>Different interactions produce different forces. Recognising the type of force helps explain why an object falls, moves through water, stretches a spring, or slows down.</p>

  <div class="keyfact">
    KEY IDEA: Identify a force by the physical interaction or effect that produces it, not merely by the object experiencing it.
  </div>

  <h3>Important Types of Force</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Gravitational force:</b> attraction between objects with mass. Near Earth, it pulls objects toward Earth''s centre.</li>
    <li><b>Friction:</b> a force that opposes relative motion or attempted motion between surfaces in contact.</li>
    <li><b>Air resistance:</b> a force exerted by air that opposes an object''s motion relative to the air.</li>
    <li><b>Tension:</b> a pulling force transmitted through a taut string, rope or cable.</li>
    <li><b>Normal contact force:</b> the supporting force exerted by a surface, perpendicular to that surface.</li>
    <li><b>Upthrust:</b> the upward force exerted by a fluid on an immersed or floating object.</li>
    <li><b>Elastic or spring force:</b> a restoring force produced when an elastic object is stretched or compressed.</li>
    <li><b>Magnetic force:</b> a force associated with magnets and magnetic fields.</li>
    <li><b>Electrostatic force:</b> attraction or repulsion between electrically charged objects.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: A Hanging Bucket</h3>
  <div class="example-box">
    A bucket hangs motionless from a rope.

    <br/><br/><b>Step 1:</b> Earth attracts the bucket downward. This is gravitational force.

    <br/><br/><b>Step 2:</b> The taut rope pulls the bucket upward. This is tension.

    <br/><br/><b>Step 3:</b> Since the bucket remains at rest, the upward and downward forces balance.

    <br/><br/><b>Answer:</b> The forces are gravity and tension.
  </div>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: A Moving Bicycle</h3>
  <div class="example-box">
    A bicycle slows down when its rider stops pedalling.

    <br/><br/><b>Step 1:</b> Identify the change: the bicycle''s speed decreases.

    <br/><br/><b>Step 2:</b> Identify forces opposing its motion: friction and air resistance.

    <br/><br/><b>Answer:</b> Friction and air resistance contribute to slowing the bicycle.
  </div>

  <h3>How to Identify a Force</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Ask what interaction produces it.</li>
    <li>Determine the direction in which it acts.</li>
    <li>Distinguish the force from the motion it may cause.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Common Misconceptions</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Calling every upward force upthrust, even when it is a solid surface supporting an object.</li>
    <li>Confusing tension in a rope with the force of gravity.</li>
    <li>Assuming friction always opposes an object''s overall direction of travel; it opposes relative slipping or its tendency.</li>
  </ul>
  
<hr>

  <h2>Types of Forces</h2>
  <p>A force is a push or pull caused by an interaction between objects. Different forces arise from different physical interactions.</p>

  <h3>Important forces</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Gravitational force:</b> attraction between objects with mass. Earth''s gravity pulls objects towards its centre.</li>
    <li><b>Friction:</b> a force that opposes relative motion or attempted motion between surfaces in contact.</li>
    <li><b>Normal reaction:</b> a support force exerted perpendicular to a surface.</li>
    <li><b>Tension:</b> a pulling force transmitted through a taut string, rope or cable.</li>
    <li><b>Air resistance:</b> a force that opposes an object''s motion through air.</li>
    <li><b>Upthrust:</b> an upward force exerted by a fluid on an immersed object.</li>
    <li><b>Elastic or spring force:</b> a restoring force produced when an elastic object is stretched or compressed.</li>
    <li><b>Magnetic force:</b> attraction or repulsion involving magnets and magnetic materials.</li>
    <li><b>Electrostatic force:</b> attraction or repulsion between electric charges.</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="keyfact"><b>Key idea:</b> Identify a force by asking what interaction produces it and what object exerts it.</div>

  <h3>Worked examples</h3>
  <div class="example-box">
    <b>Example 1: A book resting on a table</b><br>
    The Earth exerts gravitational force downward. The table exerts a normal reaction upward.
  </div>
  <div class="example-box">
    <b>Example 2: A bucket lifted by a rope</b><br>
    The rope exerts tension upward. Gravity acts downward.
  </div>
  <div class="example-box">
    <b>Example 3: A moving bicycle slowing down</b><br>
    Friction and air resistance oppose the bicycle''s motion.
  </div>

  <h3>Common mistakes</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Calling every force friction.</li>
    <li>Confusing the object experiencing a force with the object exerting it.</li>
    <li>Assuming gravity acts only when an object is falling.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Types of Forces', 15),
  (358, 358, '
  <h2>Contact and Non-contact Forces</h2>

  <h3>Foundation Understanding</h3>
  <p>Forces can be classified according to whether the interacting objects must physically touch to exert the force.</p>

  <div class="keyfact">
    KEY IDEA: Contact forces require physical contact between interacting objects. Non-contact forces can act across a gap.
  </div>

  <h3>1. Contact Forces</h3>
  <p>A contact force occurs when interacting objects physically touch. Examples include:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Friction:</b> acts between contacting surfaces.</li>
    <li><b>Normal force:</b> a surface supports an object touching it.</li>
    <li><b>Tension:</b> a taut rope or string pulls an attached object.</li>
    <li><b>Air resistance:</b> air interacts with an object moving relative to it.</li>
    <li><b>Upthrust:</b> a liquid or gas exerts a force on an object in contact with the fluid.</li>
    <li><b>Spring force:</b> an elastic spring exerts a force through contact with an attached object.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>2. Non-contact Forces</h3>
  <p>A non-contact force acts without direct physical contact between the objects producing and experiencing the force.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Gravitational force:</b> Earth attracts a falling stone.</li>
    <li><b>Magnetic force:</b> a magnet attracts a suitable magnetic material across a gap.</li>
    <li><b>Electrostatic force:</b> charged objects attract or repel one another across a gap.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: A Falling Stone</h3>
  <div class="example-box">
    A stone falls toward the ground.

    <br/><br/><b>Step 1:</b> Identify the force pulling the stone toward Earth: gravity.

    <br/><br/><b>Step 2:</b> The stone does not need to touch Earth for gravity to act.

    <br/><br/><b>Answer:</b> Gravity is a non-contact force.
  </div>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: A Box on a Floor</h3>
  <div class="example-box">
    A box rests on a floor.

    <br/><br/><b>Step 1:</b> The floor physically touches the box.

    <br/><br/><b>Step 2:</b> The floor exerts a supporting force on the box.

    <br/><br/><b>Answer:</b> The normal contact force is a contact force.
  </div>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: A Charged Comb</h3>
  <div class="example-box">
    A charged comb attracts small pieces of paper without touching them.

    <br/><br/><b>Step 1:</b> There is a gap between the comb and paper.

    <br/><br/><b>Step 2:</b> The attraction is electrostatic.

    <br/><br/><b>Answer:</b> The electrostatic force is non-contact.
  </div>

  <h3>Common Misconceptions</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Thinking gravity requires an object to touch Earth.</li>
    <li>Classifying a force by whether the object is moving rather than whether contact is required.</li>
    <li>Assuming all forces are contact forces because they produce observable effects.</li>
  </ul>
  
<hr>

  <h2>Contact and Non-contact Forces</h2>
  <p>Forces can be classified according to whether the interacting objects must touch one another.</p>

  <h3>Contact forces</h3>
  <p>A contact force acts when objects are physically touching, or when an intervening material transmits the force.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Friction between a tyre and a road.</li>
    <li>Normal reaction from a table supporting a book.</li>
    <li>Tension transmitted through a taut rope.</li>
    <li>Air resistance acting on a moving object.</li>
    <li>Upthrust exerted by water.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Non-contact forces</h3>
  <p>A non-contact force acts without the objects physically touching.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Gravitational force between Earth and a falling object.</li>
    <li>Magnetic force between magnets.</li>
    <li>Electrostatic force between electric charges.</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="keyfact"><b>Classification test:</b> Ask whether physical contact or a contacting medium is required for the force to act.</div>

  <h3>Worked examples</h3>
  <div class="example-box">
    <b>Example 1: A magnet attracts an iron nail.</b><br>
    The magnet and nail need not touch for the magnetic force to act. It is a non-contact force.
  </div>
  <div class="example-box">
    <b>Example 2: A hand pushes a trolley.</b><br>
    The hand touches the trolley. The applied push is a contact force.
  </div>
  <div class="example-box">
    <b>Example 3: A parachutist falls through air.</b><br>
    Air resistance is a contact force because air particles interact with the parachutist and parachute.
  </div>

  <h3>Common mistakes</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Assuming gravity requires objects to touch.</li>
    <li>Calling air resistance a non-contact force simply because air is difficult to see.</li>
    <li>Assuming a force stops acting whenever objects are not visibly touching.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Contact and Non-contact Forces', 15),
  (359, 359, '
  <h2>Effects of Force</h2>

  <h3>Foundation Understanding</h3>
  <p>A force can change how an object moves or change its shape. The effect depends on the forces acting, the object''s properties, and the circumstances of the interaction.</p>

  <div class="keyfact">
    KEY IDEA: Look for a change in speed, direction, or shape. A force may also help maintain a constrained situation without changing the object''s motion.
  </div>

  <h3>Effect 1: Starting or Stopping Motion</h3>
  <p>A force can set a stationary object in motion or bring a moving object to rest. For example, kicking a stationary ball starts it moving.</p>

  <h3>Effect 2: Changing Speed</h3>
  <p>A force in the direction of motion can increase speed when it produces a net force in that direction. A net force opposing motion can reduce speed.</p>

  <h3>Effect 3: Changing Direction</h3>
  <p>A force can change an object''s direction even when its speed remains constant. For example, a force toward the centre of a circular path continually changes the direction of an object moving around that path.</p>

  <h3>Effect 4: Deformation</h3>
  <p><b>Deformation</b> is a change in shape or size. Stretching a spring, compressing a sponge, and bending a ruler are examples.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Elastic deformation:</b> the object returns to its original shape when the deforming force is removed, provided its elastic limit has not been exceeded.</li>
    <li><b>Plastic deformation:</b> some permanent change remains after the force is removed.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: A Moving Ball</h3>
  <div class="example-box">
    A player kicks a moving ball, causing it to travel in a different direction.

    <br/><br/><b>Step 1:</b> Compare the direction before and after the kick.

    <br/><br/><b>Step 2:</b> The direction changes because the kick exerts a force.

    <br/><br/><b>Answer:</b> The force changes the ball''s direction of motion.
  </div>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Stretching a Spring</h3>
  <div class="example-box">
    A spring extends when a load is attached.

    <br/><br/><b>Step 1:</b> The load exerts a pulling force on the spring.

    <br/><br/><b>Step 2:</b> The spring''s length increases.

    <br/><br/><b>Step 3:</b> If it returns to its original length after unloading, the deformation was elastic.

    <br/><br/><b>Answer:</b> The force deforms the spring.
  </div>

  <h3>Important Distinction</h3>
  <p>Do not confuse a force with its effect. A force is an interaction; a change in motion or shape is a possible consequence. An object''s motion changes when the forces acting on it do not balance.</p>

  <h3>Common Misconceptions</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Assuming a force can change speed but cannot change direction.</li>
    <li>Assuming all deformation is permanent.</li>
    <li>Assuming an object''s motion must change whenever any single force acts.</li>
  </ul>
  
<hr>

  <h2>Effects of Force</h2>
  <p>A force can change an object''s motion or deform it. The result depends on the object''s existing motion, the magnitude and direction of the force, and the other forces acting on it.</p>

  <h3>Main effects</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Start motion:</b> a stationary trolley can begin moving when pushed.</li>
    <li><b>Stop motion:</b> braking can bring a moving bicycle to rest.</li>
    <li><b>Change speed:</b> a force may speed an object up or slow it down.</li>
    <li><b>Change direction:</b> a force can turn a moving ball.</li>
    <li><b>Change shape or size:</b> squeezing a sponge compresses it; stretching a spring lengthens it.</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="keyfact"><b>Important:</b> A force does not always change an object''s motion. If the forces balance, the object may remain at rest or continue at constant velocity.</div>

  <h3>Worked examples</h3>
  <div class="example-box">
    <b>Example 1: Kicking a stationary football</b><br>
    The applied force changes the ball from rest to motion.
  </div>
  <div class="example-box">
    <b>Example 2: A moving car brakes</b><br>
    The braking force opposes the car''s motion, reducing its speed.
  </div>
  <div class="example-box">
    <b>Example 3: Squeezing modelling clay</b><br>
    The applied force changes the clay''s shape.
  </div>

  <h3>Common mistakes</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Claiming that force always makes an object move faster.</li>
    <li>Ignoring changes in direction.</li>
    <li>Assuming a stationary object has no forces acting on it.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Effects of Force', 15),
  (360, 360, '
  <h2>Resultant Force</h2>

  <h3>Foundation Understanding</h3>
  <p>An object may experience several forces at the same time. The <b>resultant force</b> is the single force that has the same overall effect on the object''s motion as all the forces acting together.</p>

  <div class="keyfact">
    KEY IDEA: Forces in the same direction add. Forces in opposite directions subtract, and the resultant points in the direction of the larger force.
  </div>

  <h3>Forces in the Same Direction</h3>
  <p>When forces act along the same straight line in the same direction, their magnitudes add.</p>
  <div class="formula">
    Resultant force = F₁ + F₂
  </div>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Same Direction</h3>
  <div class="example-box">
    Two students push a trolley to the right with forces of 12 N and 8 N.

    <br/><br/><b>Step 1:</b> Both forces act to the right.

    <br/><br/><b>Step 2:</b> Add their magnitudes: 12 + 8 = 20 N.

    <br/><br/><b>Answer:</b> Resultant force = 20 N to the right.
  </div>

  <h3>Forces in Opposite Directions</h3>
  <p>When two forces act along the same straight line in opposite directions, subtract the smaller magnitude from the larger magnitude.</p>
  <div class="formula">
    Resultant magnitude = Larger force − Smaller force
  </div>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Opposite Directions</h3>
  <div class="example-box">
    A box is pulled rightward with 18 N and leftward with 11 N.

    <br/><br/><b>Step 1:</b> The forces oppose one another.

    <br/><br/><b>Step 2:</b> Calculate the difference: 18 − 11 = 7 N.

    <br/><br/><b>Step 3:</b> The larger force acts to the right.

    <br/><br/><b>Answer:</b> Resultant force = 7 N to the right.
  </div>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: More Than Two Forces</h3>
  <div class="example-box">
    Three horizontal forces act on a trolley: 15 N right, 9 N left and 4 N left.

    <br/><br/><b>Step 1:</b> Add rightward forces: 15 N.

    <br/><br/><b>Step 2:</b> Add leftward forces: 9 + 4 = 13 N.

    <br/><br/><b>Step 3:</b> Find the difference: 15 − 13 = 2 N.

    <br/><br/><b>Step 4:</b> The larger total is rightward.

    <br/><br/><b>Answer:</b> Resultant force = 2 N to the right.
  </div>

  <h3>Choosing a Positive Direction</h3>
  <p>You can choose right as positive and left as negative. Then add the signed forces algebraically. A positive result means rightward; a negative result means leftward. The choice of positive direction is arbitrary, but it must remain consistent.</p>

  <h3>Common Misconceptions</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Adding forces that act in opposite directions.</li>
    <li>Giving a magnitude without identifying the resultant''s direction when direction is required.</li>
    <li>Confusing the resultant force with just one of the forces acting.</li>
  </ul>
  
<hr>

  <h2>Resultant Force</h2>
  <p>The <b>resultant force</b> is the single force that has the same overall effect on an object''s motion as all the forces acting together.</p>

  <h3>Forces in the same direction</h3>
  <p>Add their magnitudes when they act along the same straight line in the same direction.</p>
  <div class="formula">Resultant force = F₁ + F₂</div>

  <h3>Forces in opposite directions</h3>
  <p>Subtract the smaller magnitude from the larger. The resultant acts in the direction of the larger force.</p>
  <div class="formula">Resultant force magnitude = larger force − smaller force</div>

  <h3>Worked examples</h3>
  <div class="example-box">
    <b>Example 1: Same direction</b><br>
    Two people push a trolley to the right with 12 N and 8 N.<br>
    Resultant = 12 + 8 = <b>20 N to the right</b>.
  </div>
  <div class="example-box">
    <b>Example 2: Opposite directions</b><br>
    A box experiences 25 N to the right and 9 N to the left.<br>
    Resultant magnitude = 25 − 9 = 16 N.<br>
    Direction = right, because 25 N is larger.<br>
    <b>Resultant = 16 N to the right</b>.
  </div>
  <div class="example-box">
    <b>Example 3: Equal opposing forces</b><br>
    A box experiences 10 N to the left and 10 N to the right.<br>
    Resultant = 10 − 10 = <b>0 N</b>.
  </div>

  <h3>Sign convention</h3>
  <p>For one-dimensional calculations, choose one direction as positive. For example, right may be positive and left negative. Add the signed forces algebraically.</p>

  <h3>Common mistakes</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Adding forces that act in opposite directions without considering direction.</li>
    <li>Giving a direction opposite to the larger force.</li>
    <li>Writing a resultant force without its unit, newtons (N).</li>
  </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Resultant Force', 15),
  (361, 361, '
  <h2>Balanced and Unbalanced Forces</h2>

  <h3>Foundation Understanding</h3>
  <p>Whether forces are balanced depends on their combined effect, not simply on how many forces act on an object.</p>

  <div class="keyfact">
    KEY IDEA: Balanced forces have a zero resultant. Unbalanced forces have a non-zero resultant.
  </div>

  <h3>Balanced Forces</h3>
  <p>Forces are balanced when their vector sum is zero. The object''s velocity does not change because of those forces.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>A stationary book can remain at rest.</li>
    <li>A moving object can continue in a straight line at constant speed.</li>
    <li>Forces can be balanced even when several forces act simultaneously.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Unbalanced Forces</h3>
  <p>Forces are unbalanced when their resultant is not zero. The object''s velocity changes: it accelerates, which may mean speeding up, slowing down or changing direction.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: A Book on a Table</h3>
  <div class="example-box">
    A book experiences 5 N downward due to gravity and 5 N upward from the table.

    <br/><br/><b>Step 1:</b> Identify opposite forces: 5 N up and 5 N down.

    <br/><br/><b>Step 2:</b> Find the resultant: 5 − 5 = 0 N.

    <br/><br/><b>Answer:</b> The vertical forces are balanced. The book remains at rest.
  </div>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: A Pulled Trolley</h3>
  <div class="example-box">
    A trolley experiences 16 N forward and 10 N backward.

    <br/><br/><b>Step 1:</b> Find the resultant: 16 − 10 = 6 N.

    <br/><br/><b>Step 2:</b> The resultant is forward and is not zero.

    <br/><br/><b>Answer:</b> The forces are unbalanced. The trolley accelerates forward.
  </div>

  <h3>Rest Is Not the Same as Balanced</h3>
  <p>An object at rest is not necessarily free of forces. A table supports a resting book while gravity pulls it downward. Likewise, a moving object does not automatically have unbalanced forces: constant-velocity motion is possible with zero resultant force.</p>

  <h3>Common Misconceptions</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Thinking balanced forces mean no forces act.</li>
    <li>Thinking a moving object must have an unbalanced force in its direction of motion.</li>
    <li>Thinking an unbalanced force always makes an object speed up; it can also slow or redirect it.</li>
  </ul>
  
<hr>

  <h2>Balanced and Unbalanced Forces</h2>
  <p>Whether forces are balanced depends on their resultant, not on whether forces are present.</p>

  <h3>Balanced forces</h3>
  <p>Forces are balanced when their vector sum is zero. The resultant force is zero.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>An object at rest remains at rest if the resultant stays zero.</li>
    <li>An object already moving continues at constant velocity if the resultant stays zero.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Unbalanced forces</h3>
  <p>Forces are unbalanced when their resultant is not zero. The object''s velocity changes: its speed, direction, or both may change.</p>

  <div class="keyfact"><b>Critical distinction:</b> Zero resultant force means zero acceleration, not necessarily zero velocity.</div>

  <h3>Worked examples</h3>
  <div class="example-box">
    <b>Example 1: A hanging lamp</b><br>
    Tension acts upward with 40 N and weight acts downward with 40 N.<br>
    Resultant = 40 − 40 = 0 N.<br>
    The forces are balanced.
  </div>
  <div class="example-box">
    <b>Example 2: A trolley starts accelerating</b><br>
    A 15 N push acts right and friction acts left with 5 N.<br>
    Resultant = 15 − 5 = 10 N right.<br>
    The forces are unbalanced.
  </div>
  <div class="example-box">
    <b>Example 3: A car moving at constant velocity</b><br>
    If the forward driving force equals the total opposing force, the resultant is zero and the car''s velocity remains constant.
  </div>

  <h3>Common mistakes</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Assuming balanced forces mean an object must be stationary.</li>
    <li>Assuming a moving object must have a resultant force in its direction of motion.</li>
    <li>Confusing the presence of forces with a non-zero resultant.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Balanced and Unbalanced Forces', 15),
  (362, 362, '
  <h2>Meaning of Weight</h2>

  <h3>Learning objective</h3>
  <p>Explain what weight is and identify the force responsible for it.</p>

  <h3>1. What is weight?</h3>
  <p>
    <b>Weight</b> is the gravitational force acting on an object.
    Near the Earth''s surface, the Earth pulls objects towards its centre.
    This gravitational pull is the object''s weight.
  </p>

  <p>
    Weight is a <b>force</b>, so its SI unit is the newton (N).
    A weighing instrument calibrated in newtons measures weight.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Worked Example 1: Identifying weight</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A book rests on a table. What is the weight of the book?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify the gravitational force acting on the book.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> The Earth attracts the book towards its centre.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> The weight is the gravitational force exerted by the Earth on the book.</p>

  <h3>3. Worked Example 2: Identifying the direction</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> In which direction does the Earth''s gravitational force act on a ball held above the ground?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Weight is caused by the Earth''s gravitational attraction.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Near the Earth''s surface, this attraction acts towards the Earth''s centre.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Vertically downwards, towards the Earth''s centre.</p>

  <h3>4. Worked Example 3: Identifying the correct unit</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A learner says a person''s weight is 500 kg. Explain the error.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Kilogram (kg) is the SI unit of mass.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Weight is a force.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Force is measured in newtons (N).</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> The unit is incorrect. Weight should be expressed in newtons, not kilograms.</p>

  <h3>Remember</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Weight is gravitational force.</li>
    <li>Weight is measured in newtons (N).</li>
    <li>Near Earth, weight acts towards Earth''s centre.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Meaning of Weight', 15),
  (363, 363, '
  <h2>Mass and Weight</h2>

  <h3>Learning objective</h3>
  <p>Distinguish mass from weight using their meanings, units and behaviour in different gravitational fields.</p>

  <h3>1. Mass versus weight</h3>
  <p>
    <b>Mass</b> measures the amount of matter in an object. Its SI unit
    is the kilogram (kg).
  </p>
  <p>
    <b>Weight</b> is the gravitational force acting on an object.
    Its SI unit is the newton (N).
  </p>

  <p>
    An object''s mass remains the same when it moves from Earth to the
    Moon, but its weight changes because the Moon''s gravitational field
    is weaker.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Worked Example 1: Classifying quantities</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A bag has a mass of 4 kg. Is 4 kg its mass or weight?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify the given unit.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Kilograms measure mass.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> 4 kg is the mass of the bag.</p>

  <h3>3. Worked Example 2: Comparing units</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A force meter reads 30 N when an object is suspended. Is this reading mass or weight?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify the unit, newtons.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Newtons measure force.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> The reading is the object''s weight.</p>

  <h3>4. Worked Example 3: Earth and Moon</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> An object has a mass of 6 kg on Earth. What happens to its mass when taken to the Moon?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Mass measures the amount of matter in the object.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Moving the object does not change the amount of matter it contains.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Its mass remains 6 kg.</p>
  <p>
    Its weight would be smaller on the Moon because the Moon''s
    gravitational field is weaker.
  </p>

  <h3>Remember</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Mass: kilogram (kg), amount of matter.</li>
    <li>Weight: newton (N), gravitational force.</li>
    <li>Mass remains constant when location changes; weight can change.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Mass and Weight', 15),
  (364, 364, '
  <h2>Calculating Weight</h2>

  <h3>Learning objective</h3>
  <p>Calculate an object''s weight using its mass and gravitational field strength.</p>

  <h3>1. The formula</h3>
  <p>
    Weight depends on an object''s mass and the gravitational field
    strength where it is located.
  </p>

  <p style="text-align:center;"><b>W = mg</b></p>

  <ul>
    <li><b>W</b> = weight in newtons (N)</li>
    <li><b>m</b> = mass in kilograms (kg)</li>
    <li><b>g</b> = gravitational field strength in N/kg</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Unless a question specifies otherwise, use the value of g given
    in the question or required by the examination.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Worked Example 1: A simple calculation</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> Calculate the weight of a 5 kg object where g = 10 N/kg.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Write the formula: W = mg.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Substitute: W = 5 × 10.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Calculate: W = 50 N.</p>
  <p><b>Answer: 50 N</b></p>

  <h3>3. Worked Example 2: Using a different field strength</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> Find the weight of a 12 kg object where g = 9.8 N/kg.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> W = mg.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> W = 12 × 9.8.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> W = 117.6 N.</p>
  <p><b>Answer: 117.6 N</b></p>

  <h3>4. Worked Example 3: Checking the units</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A 3 kg object is in a gravitational field of 10 N/kg. A learner calculates its weight as 30 kg. Identify and correct the error.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Apply W = mg.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> W = 3 × 10 = 30.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> The unit is kg × N/kg = N.</p>
  <p><b>Answer: 30 N.</b> The numerical calculation was correct, but the unit was wrong.</p>

  <h3>Remember</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Multiply mass by gravitational field strength.</li>
    <li>Use mass in kilograms.</li>
    <li>Express weight in newtons.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Calculating Weight', 15),
  (365, 365, '
  <h2>Calculating Mass from Weight</h2>

  <h3>Learning objective</h3>
  <p>Calculate mass when weight and gravitational field strength are known.</p>

  <h3>1. Rearranging the weight formula</h3>
  <p>
    The starting relationship is:
  </p>

  <p style="text-align:center;"><b>W = mg</b></p>

  <p>
    To make mass the subject, divide both sides by g:
  </p>

  <p style="text-align:center;"><b>m = W/g</b></p>

  <ul>
    <li>Weight W is measured in newtons (N).</li>
    <li>Gravitational field strength g is measured in N/kg.</li>
    <li>Mass m is measured in kilograms (kg).</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>2. Worked Example 1: Finding mass</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> An object weighs 60 N where g = 10 N/kg. Find its mass.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Use m = W/g.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Substitute: m = 60/10.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Calculate: m = 6 kg.</p>
  <p><b>Answer: 6 kg</b></p>

  <h3>3. Worked Example 2: A non-integer result</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> An object weighs 49 N in a field of 9.8 N/kg. Calculate its mass.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> m = W/g.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> m = 49/9.8.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> m = 5 kg.</p>
  <p><b>Answer: 5 kg</b></p>

  <h3>4. Worked Example 3: Checking a student''s rearrangement</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A learner writes m = W × g. Explain the error and find the mass of an object weighing 80 N where g = 10 N/kg.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Start with W = mg.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Divide by g to isolate m: m = W/g.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Substitute: m = 80/10 = 8 kg.</p>
  <p><b>Answer: 8 kg.</b> Divide weight by field strength; do not multiply them.</p>

  <h3>Remember</h3>
  <p><b>Mass = Weight ÷ Gravitational field strength</b></p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Calculating Mass from Weight', 15),
  (366, 366, '
  <h2>Weight in Different Gravitational Fields</h2>

  <h3>Learning objective</h3>
  <p>Explain and calculate how an object''s weight changes when gravitational field strength changes.</p>

  <h3>1. Why weight changes</h3>
  <p>
    Weight is calculated using:
  </p>

  <p style="text-align:center;"><b>W = mg</b></p>

  <p>
    For the same object, mass m stays constant. If gravitational field
    strength g changes, weight W changes in direct proportion to g.
  </p>

  <p>
    For example, the Moon''s gravitational field is weaker than Earth''s,
    so an object weighs less on the Moon. Its mass does not change.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Worked Example 1: Comparing Earth and Moon</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> An object has a mass of 6 kg. Take Earth''s field strength as 10 N/kg and the Moon''s as 1.6 N/kg. Find its weight in each location.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1: Earth.</b></p>
  <p>W = mg = 6 × 10 = 60 N.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2: Moon.</b></p>
  <p>W = mg = 6 × 1.6 = 9.6 N.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Earth: 60 N; Moon: 9.6 N.</p>

  <h3>3. Worked Example 2: Field strength doubles</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> An object weighs 40 N in a field of 5 N/kg. What will it weigh in a field of 10 N/kg?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Find the mass: m = W/g = 40/5 = 8 kg.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Calculate the new weight: W = 8 × 10.</p>
  <p><b>Answer: 80 N.</b> Doubling the field strength doubles the weight of the same object.</p>

  <h3>4. Worked Example 3: Field strength is halved</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> An object weighs 100 N where g = 10 N/kg. Calculate its weight where g = 5 N/kg.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Find the mass: m = 100/10 = 10 kg.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Calculate the new weight: W = 10 × 5 = 50 N.</p>
  <p><b>Answer: 50 N.</b> Halving the field strength halves the weight.</p>

  <h3>Remember</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Mass remains constant when location changes.</li>
    <li>Weight depends on gravitational field strength.</li>
    <li>For the same mass, doubling g doubles W; halving g halves W.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Weight in Different Gravitational Fields', 15),
  (367, 367, '
  <h2>Meaning and Direction of Friction</h2>

  <h3>Core idea</h3>
  <p>
    Friction is a force that opposes relative motion, or the tendency
    for relative motion, between surfaces in contact.
  </p>
  <p>
    Friction acts along the surfaces in contact. Its direction is
    opposite to the sliding motion or the tendency to slide.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: A box sliding to the right</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A box slides to the right across a rough floor.
  In which direction does friction act on the box?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify the direction of sliding: right.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Friction opposes the sliding motion.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Friction acts to the left.</p>
  <p><b>Check:</b> The friction force is parallel to the floor and
  opposite to the sliding direction.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Pulling a box left</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A box is pulled to the left and slides across
  a rough floor. What is the direction of friction?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> The box slides left.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Friction opposes that sliding.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Friction acts to the right.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: A stationary box</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A stationary box is pushed to the right but
  does not move. Which direction does static friction act?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> The push tends to move the box right.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Static friction opposes the tendency to slide.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Static friction acts to the left, balancing the
  push if no other horizontal force acts.</p>

  <h3>Remember</h3>
  <p>Do not automatically say friction acts opposite to the applied
  force. Determine the direction of actual or impending relative
  motion first.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Meaning and Direction of Friction', 15),
  (368, 368, '
  <h2>Static and Sliding Friction</h2>

  <h3>Core idea</h3>
  <p>
    <b>Static friction</b> acts between surfaces that are not sliding
    relative to each other. It adjusts as needed to resist an applied
    force, up to a maximum value.
  </p>
  <p>
    <b>Sliding friction</b> acts when surfaces slide across each other.
    In many ordinary situations, the maximum static friction is greater
    than the sliding friction.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: A small push</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A box is pushed horizontally with a force of
  8 N, but it remains stationary. What is the friction force if no
  other horizontal force acts?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> The box remains at rest, so the resultant horizontal
  force is zero.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Static friction balances the 8 N push.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> 8 N opposite to the push.</p>
  <p><b>Check:</b> The horizontal forces balance: 8 N − 8 N = 0 N.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Increasing the push</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A stationary box experiences a 12 N push and
  remains at rest. What is the static friction force?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> The box is still at rest.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Static friction balances the push, provided its
  maximum value has not been exceeded.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> 12 N opposite to the push.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Sliding box</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A box slides right while the friction force is
  6 N. What type of friction acts, and in which direction?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> The surfaces are sliding relative to one another.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Sliding friction acts opposite to the sliding.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Sliding friction acts to the left with magnitude 6 N.</p>

  <h3>Remember</h3>
  <p>Static friction does not always equal its maximum value. It
  adjusts to the applied force until slipping begins.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Static and Sliding Friction', 15),
  (369, 369, '
  <h2>Resultant Force with Friction</h2>

  <h3>Core idea</h3>
  <p>
    The resultant force is the vector sum of all forces acting on
    an object. For forces along one straight line, choose a positive
    direction and add forces with their signs.
  </p>
  <p>
    If two horizontal forces act in opposite directions, subtract
    the smaller magnitude from the larger. The resultant acts in
    the direction of the larger force.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Applied force greater than friction</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A 20 N force pulls a box right. Friction is
  7 N left. Find the resultant horizontal force.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Take right as positive.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Resultant force = 20 − 7.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> 13 N to the right.</p>
  <p><b>Check:</b> The rightward force is larger, so the resultant
  must point right.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Forces balance</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A box is pulled right by 15 N and experiences
  15 N friction left. Find the resultant force.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> The forces have equal magnitudes.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Resultant = 15 − 15.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> 0 N.</p>
  <p><b>Meaning:</b> The box has no acceleration. It may remain at
  rest or continue at constant velocity.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Friction greater than the applied force</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> An object has a 9 N force right and a 14 N
  opposing force left. Find the resultant.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Compare the forces: 14 N is larger than 9 N.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Resultant magnitude = 14 − 9 = 5 N.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> 5 N to the left.</p>

  <h3>Remember</h3>
  <p>Zero resultant force means zero acceleration, not necessarily
  zero velocity.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Resultant Force with Friction', 15),
  (370, 370, '
  <h2>Newton''s First Law and Inertia</h2>

  <h3>Core idea</h3>
  <p>
    Newton''s first law states that an object remains at rest or
    continues moving with constant velocity in a straight line
    unless acted on by a non-zero resultant external force.
  </p>
  <p>
    <b>Inertia</b> is the tendency of an object to resist changes
    in its state of rest or motion. Greater mass means greater inertia.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: A book on a table</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A book rests on a horizontal table. What does
  its unchanged state of motion tell us about the resultant force?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> The book remains at rest.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Its acceleration is zero.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> The resultant force on the book is zero.</p>
  <p><b>Check:</b> The upward support force balances the book''s weight,
  assuming no other vertical forces act.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: A moving trolley</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A trolley moves in a straight line at constant
  velocity. What is its acceleration?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Its velocity is unchanged.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Acceleration is the rate of change of velocity.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> 0 m/s².</p>
  <p><b>Conclusion:</b> Its resultant force is zero.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Two objects of different masses</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A loaded trolley and an empty trolley are at rest.
  Which has greater inertia?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Inertia depends on mass.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> The loaded trolley has greater mass.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> The loaded trolley has greater inertia.</p>

  <h3>Remember</h3>
  <p>A force is not required to maintain constant velocity. A
  resultant force is required to change velocity.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Newton''s First Law and Inertia', 15),
  (371, 371, '
  <h2>Newton''s Second Law: Force, Mass and Acceleration</h2>

  <h3>Core idea</h3>
  <p>
    Newton''s second law relates the resultant force on an object
    to its mass and acceleration:
  </p>
  <p><b>Resultant force = mass × acceleration</b></p>
  <p><b>F = ma</b></p>
  <p>
    Force is measured in newtons (N), mass in kilograms (kg), and
    acceleration in metres per second squared (m/s²).
  </p>
  <p>
    For a fixed mass, a greater resultant force produces greater
    acceleration. For a fixed resultant force, a greater mass
    produces smaller acceleration.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Calculating force</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A 4 kg object accelerates at 3 m/s². Find
  the resultant force.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Write the formula: F = ma.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Substitute: F = 4 × 3.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> F = 12 N.</p>
  <p><b>Check:</b> kg × m/s² is equivalent to a newton.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Calculating acceleration</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A resultant force of 20 N acts on a 5 kg
  trolley. Calculate its acceleration.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> F = ma, so a = F/m.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Substitute: a = 20/5.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> a = 4 m/s².</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Calculating mass</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A resultant force of 18 N produces an
  acceleration of 6 m/s². Find the mass.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> F = ma, so m = F/a.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Substitute: m = 18/6.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> m = 3 kg.</p>

  <h3>Remember</h3>
  <p>Use the <b>resultant force</b>, not automatically the applied
  force. If friction or other forces act, combine all forces first.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Newton''s Second Law: Force, Mass and Acceleration', 15),
  (372, 372, '
  <h2>Meaning of Circular Motion</h2>

  <h3>Core idea</h3>
  <p>
    Circular motion occurs when an object moves along a circular
    path around a fixed centre or axis.
  </p>
  <p>
    Even if an object moves around the circle at constant speed,
    its velocity changes because velocity includes both speed
    and direction. A change in velocity means the object is
    accelerating.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: A stone on a string</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A stone is whirled in a horizontal circle
  at constant speed. Is its velocity constant?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Its speed remains constant.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Its direction changes continuously as it
  moves around the circle.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Its velocity is not constant.</p>
  <p><b>Check:</b> Constant speed does not mean constant velocity.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: A car on a circular track</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A car travels around a circular track at
  constant speed. Does its velocity change?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> At one point, the car moves in one direction.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> At another point, its direction is different.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Yes. Its velocity changes because its
  direction changes.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Straight-line motion</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A trolley moves in a straight line at
  constant speed and does not change direction. Is it accelerating?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Its speed is constant.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Its direction is also constant.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Its velocity therefore remains constant.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> No. Its acceleration is zero.</p>

  <h3>Remember</h3>
  <p>Circular motion at constant speed still involves acceleration
  because the direction of velocity changes.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Meaning of Circular Motion', 15),
  (373, 373, '
  <h2>Centripetal Acceleration</h2>

  <h3>Core idea</h3>
  <p>
    In circular motion, acceleration is directed towards the
    centre of the circle. This is called centripetal acceleration.
  </p>
  <p>
    For an object moving at speed v in a circle of radius r:
  </p>
  <p><b>a = v²/r</b></p>
  <p>
    Here, a is centripetal acceleration in m/s², v is speed in m/s,
    and r is radius in metres.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Calculating acceleration</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A car moves at 4 m/s around a circular path
  of radius 8 m. Calculate its centripetal acceleration.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Write the formula: a = v²/r.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Substitute: a = 4²/8.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Calculate: a = 16/8.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> 2 m/s² towards the centre.</p>
  <p><b>Check:</b> The units are m²/s² divided by m, giving m/s².</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Higher speed</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> An object moves at 6 m/s in a circle of
  radius 3 m. Find its centripetal acceleration.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> a = v²/r.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> a = 6²/3 = 36/3.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> 12 m/s² towards the centre.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Larger radius</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A vehicle moves at 5 m/s around a circular
  path of radius 10 m. Find its centripetal acceleration.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> a = v²/r.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> a = 5²/10 = 25/10.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> 2.5 m/s² towards the centre.</p>

  <h3>Remember</h3>
  <p>Speed is squared in the formula. If speed doubles while the
  radius stays constant, centripetal acceleration becomes four times
  as large.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Centripetal Acceleration', 15),
  (374, 374, '
  <h2>Centripetal Force</h2>

  <h3>Core idea</h3>
  <p>
    Centripetal force is the resultant force directed towards the
    centre of a circular path. It is not a separate new type of force.
    It describes the role played by the resultant inward force.
  </p>
  <p>
    The required centripetal force is:
  </p>
  <p><b>F = mv²/r</b></p>
  <p>
    Here, m is mass in kilograms, v is speed in metres per second,
    and r is radius in metres.
  </p>
  <p>
    Depending on the situation, tension, friction, gravity or a
    normal contact force can provide the inward resultant force.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Calculating centripetal force</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A 2 kg object moves at 3 m/s in a circle of
  radius 6 m. Calculate the required centripetal force.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> F = mv²/r.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> F = (2 × 3²)/6.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> F = 18/6.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> 3 N towards the centre.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: A faster object</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A 4 kg object moves at 5 m/s in a circle of
  radius 10 m. Find the required centripetal force.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> F = mv²/r.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> F = (4 × 5²)/10.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> F = 100/10.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> 10 N towards the centre.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Identifying the source of force</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A stone moves in a horizontal circle on a
  string. Which force provides the inward force in the idealised
  horizontal-circle model?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> The stone needs an inward resultant force to
  follow the circular path.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> The string pulls the stone towards the centre.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> The string''s tension provides the inward force
  in this model.</p>

  <h3>Remember</h3>
  <p>Always identify the actual physical force supplying the
  centripetal force. Do not add an extra “centripetal force” to
  the force diagram if the inward force is already represented.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Centripetal Force', 15),
  (375, 375, '
  <h2>Effect of Speed and Radius on Centripetal Force</h2>

  <h3>Core idea</h3>
  <p>
    The centripetal force needed for circular motion is:
  </p>
  <p><b>F = mv²/r</b></p>
  <p>
    For constant mass and radius, force is proportional to the
    square of speed. For constant mass and speed, force is inversely
    proportional to radius.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Doubling speed</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A car requires 100 N of inward force at a
  particular speed on a bend. If its speed doubles and the radius
  stays the same, what force is required?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Force is proportional to speed squared.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Doubling speed multiplies force by 2² = 4.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> New force = 4 × 100 N.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> 400 N.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Doubling radius</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> An object needs 60 N of centripetal force.
  Its speed and mass stay constant, but its radius doubles. Find
  the new required force.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Force is inversely proportional to radius.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Doubling radius halves the force.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> 30 N.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Tripling speed</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A body requires 8 N of centripetal force.
  If its speed triples while mass and radius remain constant,
  what force is required?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Force is proportional to v².</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Tripling speed multiplies force by 3² = 9.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> New force = 9 × 8 N.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> 72 N.</p>

  <h3>Remember</h3>
  <p>Speed changes have a squared effect. Radius changes have an
  inverse effect when mass and speed remain fixed.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Effect of Speed and Radius on Centripetal Force', 15),
  (376, 376, '
  <h2>Gravitation and Gravitational Force</h2>

  <h3>Core idea</h3>
  <p>
    Gravitation is the attractive force between objects that have
    mass. The Earth attracts objects towards its centre, which is
    why unsupported objects fall towards the ground.
  </p>
  <p>
    Gravitational attraction depends on the masses of the objects
    and the distance between their centres. In Newton''s law of
    gravitation, the force increases with either mass and decreases
    with the square of the separation.
  </p>
  <p>
    Near Earth''s surface, the weight of an object can usually be
    calculated using <b>W = mg</b>, where g is gravitational field
    strength.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Calculating weight</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> Calculate the weight of a 5 kg object where
  g = 10 N/kg.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Use W = mg.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> W = 5 × 10.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> 50 N.</p>
  <p><b>Check:</b> Weight is a force and is measured in newtons.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Comparing masses</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> Two objects are at the same place. Object A
  has mass 2 kg and object B has mass 6 kg. Which experiences the
  greater gravitational force from Earth?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> At the same location, g is approximately the same.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Weight = mg.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Object B has three times the mass of object A.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Object B experiences three times the weight.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Weight on a different world</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> An object has mass 4 kg. Its local gravitational
  field strength is 2 N/kg. Calculate its weight there.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> W = mg.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> W = 4 × 2.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> 8 N.</p>
  <p><b>Check:</b> The mass remains 4 kg, but the weight depends on
  the local gravitational field strength.</p>

  <h3>Remember</h3>
  <p>Mass measures the amount of matter and is measured in kilograms.
  Weight is gravitational force and is measured in newtons.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Gravitation and Gravitational Force', 15),
  (377, 377, '
  <h2>Describing Position and Motion</h2>

  <h3>Foundation Understanding</h3>
  <p>To describe where an object is and whether it moves, we compare its position with a chosen reference point or reference frame.</p>

  <div class="keyfact">
    KEY IDEA: Motion is a change in position relative to a reference point over time.
  </div>

  <h3>Position and Reference Points</h3>
  <p><b>Position</b> describes an object''s location relative to a reference. For example, saying that a student is 4 m east of a classroom door gives a location and a direction relative to the door.</p>

  <h3>Describing Position in One Dimension</h3>
  <p>Choose an origin and a positive direction. If the door is at position 0 m and east is positive, a student 4 m east is at +4 m. A student 3 m west is at −3 m.</p>

  <div class="formula">
    Position = signed location relative to a chosen origin
  </div>

  <h3>Motion and Rest</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>An object is in motion relative to a reference when its position changes over time.</li>
    <li>An object is at rest relative to a reference when its position remains unchanged over time.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Motion Is Relative</h3>
  <p>A passenger sitting in a moving bus is at rest relative to the bus but moving relative to a person standing beside the road. Neither description is necessarily wrong: the reference frames differ.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Position on a Straight Road</h3>
  <div class="example-box">
    A signpost is the origin. A cyclist is 7 m west of it. East is positive.

    <br/><br/><b>Step 1:</b> Identify the origin: the signpost at 0 m.

    <br/><br/><b>Step 2:</b> West is opposite to the chosen positive direction.

    <br/><br/><b>Step 3:</b> Assign a negative position.

    <br/><br/><b>Answer:</b> The cyclist''s position is −7 m.
  </div>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Relative Motion</h3>
  <div class="example-box">
    A passenger sits in a bus travelling along a straight road.

    <br/><br/><b>Step 1:</b> Relative to the seat, the passenger''s position does not change.

    <br/><br/><b>Step 2:</b> Relative to a stationary roadside tree, the passenger''s position changes.

    <br/><br/><b>Answer:</b> The passenger is at rest relative to the bus and moving relative to the tree.
  </div>

  <h3>Common Misconceptions</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Describing position without stating or implying a reference point.</li>
    <li>Assuming position must always be positive.</li>
    <li>Assuming motion or rest is identical for every observer.</li>
  </ul>
  
<hr>

  <h2>Describing Position and Motion</h2>
  <p>To describe where an object is or whether it is moving, we compare its position with a chosen reference point or observer.</p>

  <h3>Position</h3>
  <p>Position specifies an object''s location relative to a reference point. In a straight-line problem, a position can be described using a distance and a direction from the origin.</p>

  <h3>Rest and motion</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>At rest:</b> the object''s position relative to the chosen reference point does not change with time.</li>
    <li><b>In motion:</b> the object''s position relative to that reference point changes with time.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Motion is relative</h3>
  <p>An object can be at rest relative to one observer and moving relative to another. Always identify the reference point before deciding whether an object is moving.</p>

  <h3>Worked examples</h3>
  <div class="example-box">
    <b>Example 1: A passenger on a bus</b><br>
    The passenger is at rest relative to the seat but moving relative to a person standing beside the road.
  </div>
  <div class="example-box">
    <b>Example 2: A student walks east from a school gate</b><br>
    If the gate is the reference point, the student''s position changes as the student walks east.
  </div>
  <div class="example-box">
    <b>Example 3: A parked car</b><br>
    The car is at rest relative to the road if its position relative to the road does not change.
  </div>

  <h3>Common mistakes</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Describing motion without specifying what it is relative to.</li>
    <li>Confusing position with the total distance travelled.</li>
    <li>Assuming that a passenger sitting inside a moving vehicle is at rest relative to every observer.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Describing Position and Motion', 15),
  (378, 378, '
  <h2>Distance and Displacement</h2>

  <h3>Foundation Understanding</h3>
  <p>Distance and displacement both describe movement, but they answer different questions. Distance describes the total path travelled; displacement describes the net change in position.</p>

  <div class="keyfact">
    KEY IDEA: Distance depends on the entire route. Displacement depends only on the initial and final positions and includes direction.
  </div>

  <h3>Clear Definitions</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Distance:</b> the total length of the path travelled.</li>
    <li><b>Displacement:</b> the change in position from the starting point to the ending point, expressed with direction.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Key Differences</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Distance is a scalar; displacement is a vector.</li>
    <li>Distance is never negative.</li>
    <li>Displacement can be positive, negative or zero in a one-dimensional coordinate system, depending on the chosen positive direction.</li>
    <li>The magnitude of displacement cannot exceed the distance travelled.</li>
    <li>Distance and displacement are equal in magnitude when the object moves along a straight line without reversing direction.</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="formula">
    Distance = total path length
    <br/>
    Displacement = final position − initial position
  </div>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Same Direction</h3>
  <div class="example-box">
    A learner walks 5 m east and then 3 m east.

    <br/><br/><b>Step 1 — Distance:</b> Add the path lengths: 5 + 3 = 8 m.

    <br/><br/><b>Step 2 — Displacement:</b> Both movements are eastward: 5 + 3 = 8 m east.

    <br/><br/><b>Answer:</b> Distance = 8 m; displacement = 8 m east.
  </div>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Opposite Directions</h3>
  <div class="example-box">
    A person walks 10 m east, then 6 m west.

    <br/><br/><b>Step 1 — Distance:</b> 10 + 6 = 16 m.

    <br/><br/><b>Step 2 — Displacement:</b> Take east as positive. Net change = +10 − 6 = +4 m.

    <br/><br/><b>Answer:</b> Distance = 16 m; displacement = 4 m east.
  </div>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Returning to the Start</h3>
  <div class="example-box">
    A runner completes one full lap of a 400 m track and finishes at the starting point.

    <br/><br/><b>Step 1 — Distance:</b> One complete lap is 400 m.

    <br/><br/><b>Step 2 — Displacement:</b> Final position equals initial position, so the change in position is zero.

    <br/><br/><b>Answer:</b> Distance = 400 m; displacement = 0 m.
  </div>

  <h3>Important Insights</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Returning to the start does not erase the distance travelled.</li>
    <li>Different paths can have the same displacement but different distances.</li>
    <li>Always include direction when a displacement answer requires it.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Common Misconceptions</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Adding every movement to calculate displacement.</li>
    <li>Subtracting movements when calculating distance.</li>
    <li>Thinking zero displacement means no movement occurred.</li>
  </ul>
  
<hr>

  <h2>Distance and Displacement</h2>
  <p>Distance and displacement describe different aspects of a journey.</p>

  <h3>Definitions</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Distance:</b> the total length of the path travelled. It is a scalar and has magnitude only.</li>
    <li><b>Displacement:</b> the change from initial position to final position. It is a vector and includes direction.</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="keyfact"><b>Key idea:</b> Distance depends on the path travelled. Displacement depends on the initial and final positions.</div>

  <h3>One-dimensional calculations</h3>
  <p>Choose a positive direction. Add the lengths of all journey segments to find distance. For displacement, add signed movements: movements in the positive direction are positive and movements in the opposite direction are negative.</p>
  <div class="formula">Displacement = final position − initial position</div>

  <h3>Worked examples</h3>
  <div class="example-box">
    <b>Example 1: Same direction</b><br>
    A learner walks 5 m east, then 3 m east.<br>
    Distance = 5 + 3 = 8 m.<br>
    Displacement = 8 m east.
  </div>
  <div class="example-box">
    <b>Example 2: Opposite directions</b><br>
    A learner walks 10 m east, then 6 m west.<br>
    Distance = 10 + 6 = 16 m.<br>
    Displacement = 10 − 6 = 4 m east.
  </div>
  <div class="example-box">
    <b>Example 3: Returning to the start</b><br>
    A runner completes one 400 m lap and returns to the starting point.<br>
    Distance = 400 m.<br>
    Displacement = 0 m.
  </div>

  <h3>Common mistakes</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Subtracting journey segments when calculating distance.</li>
    <li>Adding opposite-direction movements when calculating displacement.</li>
    <li>Giving displacement without a direction when direction is required.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Distance and Displacement', 15),
  (379, 379, '
  <h2>Speed</h2>

  <h3>Foundation Understanding</h3>
  <p><b>Speed</b> describes how quickly an object covers distance. It tells us the distance travelled per unit of time, without specifying direction.</p>

  <div class="keyfact">
    KEY IDEA: Speed tells how fast an object moves, not which direction it moves.
  </div>

  <h3>Formula for Average Speed</h3>
  <div class="formula">
    Average speed = total distance travelled ÷ total time taken
    <br/><br/>
    v = d / t
  </div>
  <ul>
    <li><b>v</b> = average speed</li>
    <li><b>d</b> = total distance travelled</li>
    <li><b>t</b> = total elapsed time</li>
  </ul>

  <p>In SI units, distance is measured in metres (m), time in seconds (s), and speed in metres per second (m/s).</p>

  <h3>Rearranging the Formula</h3>
  <div class="formula">
    Speed = distance / time
    <br/>
    Distance = speed × time
    <br/>
    Time = distance / speed
  </div>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Calculating Speed</h3>
  <div class="example-box">
    A runner covers 100 m in 20 s. Calculate the average speed.

    <br/><br/><b>Step 1:</b> Identify the known values: distance = 100 m; time = 20 s.

    <br/><br/><b>Step 2:</b> Use average speed = distance ÷ time.

    <br/><br/><b>Step 3:</b> Speed = 100 ÷ 20 = 5 m/s.

    <br/><br/><b>Answer:</b> Average speed = 5 m/s.
  </div>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Calculating Distance</h3>
  <div class="example-box">
    A car travels at an average speed of 12 m/s for 8 s. Find the distance travelled.

    <br/><br/><b>Step 1:</b> Speed = 12 m/s; time = 8 s.

    <br/><br/><b>Step 2:</b> Distance = speed × time.

    <br/><br/><b>Step 3:</b> Distance = 12 × 8 = 96 m.

    <br/><br/><b>Answer:</b> Distance = 96 m.
  </div>

  <h3>Average Speed Is Not Always the Usual Speed</h3>
  <p>Average speed uses the entire journey''s distance and elapsed time, including stops. It is not generally found by taking the simple average of speeds unless the conditions justify that method.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: A Journey with a Stop</h3>
  <div class="example-box">
    A learner walks 60 m in 30 s, rests for 10 s, and then walks another 40 m in 20 s.

    <br/><br/><b>Step 1:</b> Total distance = 60 + 40 = 100 m.

    <br/><br/><b>Step 2:</b> Total elapsed time = 30 + 10 + 20 = 60 s.

    <br/><br/><b>Step 3:</b> Average speed = 100 ÷ 60 ≈ 1.67 m/s.

    <br/><br/><b>Answer:</b> Average speed ≈ 1.67 m/s.
  </div>

  <h3>Unit Conversion</h3>
  <p>To convert km/h to m/s, multiply by 5/18. To convert m/s to km/h, multiply by 18/5.</p>

  <h3>Common Misconceptions</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Using displacement instead of total distance when calculating average speed.</li>
    <li>Forgetting to include stops in the total elapsed time.</li>
    <li>Writing speed in m instead of m/s.</li>
  </ul>
  
<hr>

  <h2>Speed</h2>
  <p>Speed describes how quickly an object covers distance. It tells us the distance travelled per unit time, without specifying direction.</p>

  <h3>Formula</h3>
  <div class="formula">Average speed = total distance travelled ÷ total time taken</div>

  <p>The SI unit of speed is metres per second (m/s). Kilometres per hour (km/h) is also commonly used.</p>

  <h3>Unit conversion</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>To convert km/h to m/s, divide by 3.6.</li>
    <li>To convert m/s to km/h, multiply by 3.6.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Worked examples</h3>
  <div class="example-box">
    <b>Example 1: Calculating average speed</b><br>
    A cyclist travels 120 m in 20 s.<br>
    Speed = distance ÷ time = 120 ÷ 20 = <b>6 m/s</b>.
  </div>
  <div class="example-box">
    <b>Example 2: Finding distance</b><br>
    A car travels at 15 m/s for 8 s.<br>
    Distance = speed × time = 15 × 8 = <b>120 m</b>.
  </div>
  <div class="example-box">
    <b>Example 3: Converting units</b><br>
    Convert 72 km/h to m/s.<br>
    72 ÷ 3.6 = <b>20 m/s</b>.
  </div>

  <h3>Important distinction</h3>
  <p>Average speed uses the total distance and total elapsed time. It is not necessarily the arithmetic mean of the speeds at different moments.</p>

  <h3>Common mistakes</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Dividing time by distance instead of distance by time.</li>
    <li>Using minutes with a distance in metres without converting time to seconds when m/s is required.</li>
    <li>Giving a speed without a unit.</li>
  </ul>
  
<hr>

   <h2>Speed</h2>
<h3> FOUNDATION UNDERSTANDING</h3>
<p>
Speed tells us how quickly an object is moving from one place to another.
It describes the rate at which <b>distance is covered over time</b>.
</p>

<div class="keyfact"> KEY IDEA: Speed measures “how fast”, not “in what direction”.</div>

<h3> CLEAR DEFINITION</h3>
<p>
<b>Speed</b> is defined as the distance travelled per unit time.
</p>

<h3> KEY POINTS</h3>
<ul>
<li>Speed = distance ÷ time</li>
<li>Speed is a <b>scalar quantity</b> (has magnitude only, no direction)</li>
<li>SI unit: <b>m/s (metres per second)</b></li>
<li>Other common unit: <b>km/h (kilometres per hour)</b></li>
<li>A higher speed means an object covers more distance in less time</li>
</ul>

<div class="formula">
Speed = Distance / Time
</div>

<h3> IMPORTANT REARRANGED FORMULAS</h3>
<div class="formula">
Distance = Speed × Time<br>
Time = Distance ÷ Speed
</div>

<h3> STEP-BY-STEP WORKED EXAMPLES</h3>

<div class="example-box">
<strong> Example 1: Basic Speed Calculation</strong><br><br>

A car travels <b>100 m</b> in <b>10 s</b>.<br><br>

<b>Step 1: Write formula</b><br>
Speed = Distance ÷ Time<br><br>

<b>Step 2: Substitute values</b><br>
Speed = 100 ÷ 10<br><br>

<b>Step 3: Calculate</b><br>
Speed = <b>10 m/s</b><br><br>

<b> Final Answer:</b> 10 m/s
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<strong> Example 2: Medium Distance Motion</strong><br><br>

A runner covers <b>200 m</b> in <b>20 s</b>.<br><br>

<b>Step 1: Formula</b><br>
Speed = Distance ÷ Time<br><br>

<b>Step 2: Substitute</b><br>
Speed = 200 ÷ 20<br><br>

<b>Step 3: Calculate</b><br>
Speed = <b>10 m/s</b><br><br>

<b> Final Answer:</b> 10 m/s<br><br>

<b> Insight:</b> Even different motions can have the same speed.
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<strong> Example 3: Real-Life Unit (km/h)</strong><br><br>

A cyclist travels <b>60 km</b> in <b>2 hours</b>.<br><br>

<b>Step 1: Formula</b><br>
Speed = Distance ÷ Time<br><br>

<b>Step 2: Substitute</b><br>
Speed = 60 ÷ 2<br><br>

<b>Step 3: Calculate</b><br>
Speed = <b>30 km/h</b><br><br>

<b> Final Answer:</b> 30 km/h<br><br>

<b> Insight:</b> km/h is commonly used for vehicles because it suits long distances.
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<strong> Example 4: Finding Distance</strong><br><br>

A train moves at <b>20 m/s</b> for <b>5 seconds</b>. Find distance travelled.<br><br>

<b>Step 1: Use rearranged formula</b><br>
Distance = Speed × Time<br><br>

<b>Step 2: Substitute values</b><br>
Distance = 20 × 5<br><br>

<b>Step 3: Calculate</b><br>
Distance = <b>100 m</b><br><br>

<b> Final Answer:</b> 100 m
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<strong> Example 5: Finding Time</strong><br><br>

A car travels <b>150 m</b> at a speed of <b>10 m/s</b>. Find time taken.<br><br>

<b>Step 1: Formula</b><br>
Time = Distance ÷ Speed<br><br>

<b>Step 2: Substitute</b><br>
Time = 150 ÷ 10<br><br>

<b>Step 3: Calculate</b><br>
Time = <b>15 s</b><br><br>

<b> Final Answer:</b> 15 seconds
</div>

<h3> KEY INSIGHTS</h3>
<div class="keyfact"> Speed only describes magnitude, not direction.</div>
<div class="keyfact"> Two objects can have the same speed but move differently.</div>
<div class="keyfact"> Speed can change if distance or time changes.</div>

<h3> COMMON MISTAKES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Confusing speed with velocity (velocity includes direction).</li>
<li>Mixing units (m/s vs km/h without conversion).</li>
<li>Using total distance incorrectly in multi-step motion problems.</li>
</ul>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Speed', 15),
  (380, 380, '
  <h2>Velocity</h2>

  <h3>Foundation Understanding</h3>
  <p><b>Velocity</b> describes the rate of change of position. Unlike speed, velocity includes direction. Two objects can have the same speed but different velocities if they travel in different directions.</p>

  <div class="keyfact">
    KEY IDEA: Speed describes how fast. Velocity describes how fast and in which direction.
  </div>

  <h3>Average Velocity</h3>
  <div class="formula">
    Average velocity = displacement ÷ elapsed time
    <br/><br/>
    v = Δx / Δt
  </div>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Δx</b> = final position minus initial position.</li>
    <li><b>Δt</b> = elapsed time.</li>
    <li>The SI unit is metres per second (m/s).</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Velocity in One Dimension</h3>
  <p>Choose a positive direction and use it consistently. If east is positive, eastward velocity is positive and westward velocity is negative.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Eastward Motion</h3>
  <div class="example-box">
    A student moves 60 m east in 12 s without reversing direction.

    <br/><br/><b>Step 1:</b> Displacement = 60 m east.

    <br/><br/><b>Step 2:</b> Average velocity = displacement ÷ time = 60 ÷ 12 = 5 m/s east.

    <br/><br/><b>Answer:</b> Average velocity = 5 m/s east.
  </div>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Returning Partway</h3>
  <div class="example-box">
    A person walks 20 m east and then 8 m west in a total of 14 s.

    <br/><br/><b>Step 1:</b> Take east as positive.

    <br/><br/><b>Step 2:</b> Displacement = +20 − 8 = +12 m.

    <br/><br/><b>Step 3:</b> Average velocity = 12 ÷ 14 ≈ 0.86 m/s.

    <br/><br/><b>Answer:</b> Average velocity ≈ 0.86 m/s east.
  </div>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: A Round Trip</h3>
  <div class="example-box">
    A runner completes a lap and returns to the starting point in 80 s.

    <br/><br/><b>Step 1:</b> The runner has travelled a non-zero distance.

    <br/><br/><b>Step 2:</b> Initial and final positions are the same, so displacement = 0 m.

    <br/><br/><b>Step 3:</b> Average velocity = 0 ÷ 80 = 0 m/s.

    <br/><br/><b>Answer:</b> Average velocity = 0 m/s.
  </div>

  <h3>Speed and Velocity Compared</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Speed is a scalar; velocity is a vector.</li>
    <li>Average speed uses total distance; average velocity uses displacement.</li>
    <li>Average speed is non-negative; signed velocity can be positive, negative or zero in one dimension.</li>
    <li>Average speed and the magnitude of average velocity are equal only when the total distance equals the magnitude of displacement.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Important Insight</h3>
  <p>An object can move throughout a journey yet have zero average velocity if it finishes at its starting position. This does not mean its speed was zero throughout the journey.</p>

  <h3>Common Misconceptions</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Using total distance instead of displacement to calculate average velocity.</li>
    <li>Giving velocity without direction or a consistent sign convention.</li>
    <li>Thinking zero average velocity means that no movement occurred.</li>
  </ul>
  
<hr>

  <h2>Velocity</h2>
  <p>Velocity describes the rate at which displacement changes with time. Unlike speed, velocity includes direction.</p>

  <h3>Average velocity</h3>
  <div class="formula">Average velocity = displacement ÷ elapsed time</div>
  <p>The SI unit is metres per second (m/s). In straight-line motion, a chosen positive direction gives positive velocity; motion in the opposite direction gives negative velocity.</p>

  <h3>Speed and velocity</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Speed is based on distance and has no direction.</li>
    <li>Velocity is based on displacement and includes direction.</li>
    <li>Average speed is total distance divided by total time.</li>
    <li>Average velocity is total displacement divided by total time.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Worked examples</h3>
  <div class="example-box">
    <b>Example 1: Straight-line motion</b><br>
    A student moves 60 m east in 10 s.<br>
    Average velocity = 60 ÷ 10 = <b>6 m/s east</b>.
  </div>
  <div class="example-box">
    <b>Example 2: Opposite direction</b><br>
    Take east as positive. A student moves 20 m west in 5 s.<br>
    Displacement = −20 m.<br>
    Average velocity = −20 ÷ 5 = <b>−4 m/s</b>, meaning 4 m/s west.
  </div>
  <div class="example-box">
    <b>Example 3: Returning to the start</b><br>
    A runner completes a lap in 80 s and finishes where they started.<br>
    Displacement = 0 m.<br>
    Average velocity = 0 ÷ 80 = <b>0 m/s</b>, even though the runner travelled a non-zero distance.
  </div>

  <h3>Common mistakes</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Using distance instead of displacement to calculate average velocity.</li>
    <li>Leaving out direction or the sign convention in a one-dimensional problem.</li>
    <li>Assuming zero average velocity means the object never moved.</li>
  </ul>
  
<hr>

<h2>Velocity</h2> 
<h3> FOUNDATION UNDERSTANDING</h3>
<p>
Velocity describes how fast an object’s <b>displacement changes with time</b>.
It tells both <b>how fast</b> something is moving and <b>in which direction</b>.
</p>

<div class="keyfact"> KEY IDEA: Velocity = speed + direction (it depends on displacement, not distance).</div>

<h3> CLEAR DEFINITION</h3>
<p>
<b>Velocity</b> is the rate of change of displacement with time.
It is a <b>vector quantity</b> (has magnitude and direction).
</p>

<div class="formula">
v = s / t
</div>

<h3> KEY CONCEPTS</h3>
<ul>
<li>Velocity depends on <b>displacement</b>, not distance</li>
<li>Direction is essential (east, west, north, south)</li>
<li>SI unit: <b>m/s</b></li>
<li>Can be positive, negative, or zero</li>
<li>Zero velocity means no change in position</li>
</ul>

<h3> IMPORTANT TYPES OF VELOCITY</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Average velocity</b> = total displacement ÷ total time</li>
<li><b>Instantaneous velocity</b> = velocity at a specific moment</li>
</ul>

<div class="formula">
Average velocity = Total displacement / Total time
</div>

<h3> MOTION GRAPHS</h3>

<p><b>Displacement–Time Graph</b></p>
<pre>
s
|      /
|     /
|    /
|   /
|  /
+------------ t
Slope = Velocity
</pre>

<p><b>Velocity–Time Graph</b></p>
<pre>
v
|      /
|     /
|    /
|   /
|  /
+------------ t

Slope = Acceleration  
Area = Displacement
</pre>

<h3> STEP-BY-STEP WORKED EXAMPLES</h3>

<div class="example-box">
<strong> Example 1: Basic Velocity</strong><br><br>

A student walks <b>240 m north</b> in <b>12 s</b>.<br><br>

<b>Step 1: Identify values</b><br>
s = 240 m, t = 12 s<br><br>

<b>Step 2: Formula</b><br>
v = s ÷ t<br><br>

<b>Step 3: Substitute</b><br>
v = 240 ÷ 12<br><br>

<b>Step 4: Calculate</b><br>
v = <b>20 m/s north</b><br><br>

<b> Final Answer:</b> 20 m/s north
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<strong> Example 2: Opposite Directions</strong><br><br>

A person moves <b>150 m east</b> then <b>50 m west</b> in <b>10 s</b>.<br><br>

<b>Step 1: Find displacement</b><br>
Net displacement = 150 − 50 = <b>100 m east</b><br><br>

<b>Step 2: Formula</b><br>
v = s ÷ t<br><br>

<b>Step 3: Substitute</b><br>
v = 100 ÷ 10<br><br>

<b>Step 4: Calculate</b><br>
v = <b>10 m/s east</b><br><br>

<b> Final Answer:</b> 10 m/s east
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<strong> Example 3: Zero Velocity Concept</strong><br><br>

A runner completes a <b>400 m track</b> and returns to the starting point in <b>50 s</b>.<br><br>

<b>Step 1: Understand displacement</b><br>
Start position = End position → displacement = 0 m<br><br>

<b>Step 2: Formula</b><br>
v = s ÷ t<br><br>

<b>Step 3: Substitute</b><br>
v = 0 ÷ 50 = <b>0 m/s</b><br><br>

<b> Final Answer:</b> 0 m/s<br><br>

<b> Insight:</b> You can move a long distance but still have zero velocity.
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<strong> Example 4: Circular Motion Concept</strong><br><br>

A car moves in a circle at constant speed of <b>15 m/s</b>.<br><br>

<b>Step 1: Understand motion</b><br>
Speed remains constant, but direction keeps changing.<br><br>

<b>Step 2: Effect on velocity</b><br>
Since velocity depends on direction, velocity is changing continuously.<br><br>

<b> Final Conclusion:</b> Velocity changes even if speed is constant.
</div>

<h3> KEY INSIGHTS</h3>
<div class="keyfact"> Velocity depends on displacement, not distance.</div>
<div class="keyfact"> Direction is essential in velocity.</div>
<div class="keyfact"> Velocity can be zero even when motion occurs.</div>
<div class="keyfact"> Changing direction means changing velocity.</div>

<h3> COMMON MISTAKES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Confusing velocity with speed</li>
<li>Ignoring direction signs (+ / -)</li>
<li>Using distance instead of displacement</li>
</ul>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Velocity', 15),
  (381, 381, '
  <h2>Acceleration</h2>
  <p>Acceleration is the rate of change of velocity with time. An object accelerates whenever its velocity changes in magnitude, direction, or both.</p>

  <h3>Average acceleration</h3>
  <div class="formula">a = (final velocity − initial velocity) ÷ time taken</div>
  <p>In symbols: a = (v − u) / t, where u is initial velocity, v is final velocity and t is elapsed time.</p>

  <p>The SI unit is metres per second squared (m/s²). Acceleration has direction, so signs depend on the chosen positive direction.</p>

  <h3>Interpreting acceleration</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Positive acceleration means velocity changes in the positive direction under the chosen sign convention.</li>
    <li>Negative acceleration means acceleration points in the negative direction.</li>
    <li>Negative acceleration does not always mean slowing down. The object may be moving in the negative direction and speeding up.</li>
    <li>An object moving at constant velocity has zero acceleration.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Worked examples</h3>
  <div class="example-box">
    <b>Example 1: Speeding up in the positive direction</b><br>
    A car''s velocity increases from 5 m/s to 17 m/s in 4 s.<br>
    a = (17 − 5) ÷ 4 = 12 ÷ 4 = <b>3 m/s²</b>.
  </div>
  <div class="example-box">
    <b>Example 2: Slowing down while moving forward</b><br>
    A car moving east at 20 m/s slows to 8 m/s in 3 s. Take east as positive.<br>
    a = (8 − 20) ÷ 3 = −12 ÷ 3 = <b>−4 m/s²</b>.<br>
    The negative sign shows acceleration westward, opposite to the car''s motion.
  </div>
  <div class="example-box">
    <b>Example 3: Constant velocity</b><br>
    A train''s velocity remains 12 m/s east for 10 s.<br>
    Change in velocity = 12 − 12 = 0 m/s.<br>
    Acceleration = 0 ÷ 10 = <b>0 m/s²</b>.
  </div>

  <h3>Common mistakes</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Dividing velocity by time without subtracting the initial velocity.</li>
    <li>Using distance instead of change in velocity.</li>
    <li>Assuming a negative acceleration always means the object is slowing down.</li>
    <li>Using m/s instead of m/s² for acceleration.</li>
  </ul>
  
<hr>

    <h2>Acceleration</h2>
<h3> FOUNDATION UNDERSTANDING</h3>
<p>
Acceleration describes how quickly an object''s velocity changes.
This means an object is accelerating if it is:
<ul>
<li>Speeding up</li>
<li>Slowing down</li>
<li>Changing direction</li>
</ul>
</p>

<div class="keyfact"> KEY IDEA: Acceleration is about CHANGE in velocity, not just motion.</div>

<h3> CLEAR DEFINITION</h3>
<p>
<b>Acceleration</b> is the rate of change of velocity with respect to time.
</p>

<h3> KEY POINTS</h3>
<ul>
<li>Acceleration = change in velocity ÷ time taken</li>
<li>It is a <b>vector quantity</b> (has magnitude and direction)</li>
<li>SI unit: <b>m/s² (metres per second squared)</b></li>
<li>Positive acceleration → speeding up</li>
<li>Negative acceleration → slowing down (deceleration / retardation)</li>
</ul>

<div class="formula">
Acceleration = (Final velocity − Initial velocity) / Time
</div>

<h3> IMPORTANT REARRANGED FORMULAS</h3>
<div class="formula">
Final velocity = Initial velocity + (Acceleration × Time)<br>
Initial velocity = Final velocity − (Acceleration × Time)<br>
Time = (Final velocity − Initial velocity) ÷ Acceleration
</div>

<h3> STEP-BY-STEP WORKED EXAMPLES</h3>

<div class="example-box">
<strong> Example 1: Basic Acceleration</strong><br><br>

A car increases its speed from <b>0 m/s</b> to <b>20 m/s</b> in <b>5 s</b>.<br><br>

<b>Step 1: Write formula</b><br>
Acceleration = (Final velocity − Initial velocity) ÷ Time<br><br>

<b>Step 2: Substitute values</b><br>
Acceleration = (20 − 0) ÷ 5<br><br>

<b>Step 3: Calculate</b><br>
Acceleration = 20 ÷ 5 = <b>4 m/s²</b><br><br>

<b> Final Answer:</b> 4 m/s²
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<strong> Example 2: Increasing Speed</strong><br><br>

A cyclist speeds up from <b>5 m/s</b> to <b>25 m/s</b> in <b>10 s</b>.<br><br>

<b>Step 1: Formula</b><br>
Acceleration = (v − u) ÷ t<br><br>

<b>Step 2: Substitute</b><br>
Acceleration = (25 − 5) ÷ 10<br><br>

<b>Step 3: Calculate</b><br>
Acceleration = 20 ÷ 10 = <b>2 m/s²</b><br><br>

<b> Final Answer:</b> 2 m/s²
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<strong> Example 3: Deceleration (Slowing Down)</strong><br><br>

A car slows from <b>30 m/s</b> to <b>10 m/s</b> in <b>5 s</b>.<br><br>

<b>Step 1: Formula</b><br>
Acceleration = (v − u) ÷ t<br><br>

<b>Step 2: Substitute</b><br>
Acceleration = (10 − 30) ÷ 5<br><br>

<b>Step 3: Calculate</b><br>
Acceleration = −20 ÷ 5 = <b>−4 m/s²</b><br><br>

<b> Final Answer:</b> −4 m/s²<br><br>

<b> Insight:</b> Negative sign means the object is slowing down.
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<strong> Example 4: Finding Final Velocity</strong><br><br>

A bus starts at <b>10 m/s</b> and accelerates at <b>3 m/s²</b> for <b>6 s</b>.<br><br>

<b>Step 1: Use formula</b><br>
v = u + at<br><br>

<b>Step 2: Substitute</b><br>
v = 10 + (3 × 6)<br><br>

<b>Step 3: Calculate</b><br>
v = 10 + 18 = <b>28 m/s</b><br><br>

<b> Final Answer:</b> 28 m/s
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<strong> Example 5: Finding Time</strong><br><br>

A car increases speed from <b>15 m/s</b> to <b>35 m/s</b> with an acceleration of <b>5 m/s²</b>.<br><br>

<b>Step 1: Formula</b><br>
t = (v − u) ÷ a<br><br>

<b>Step 2: Substitute</b><br>
t = (35 − 15) ÷ 5<br><br>

<b>Step 3: Calculate</b><br>
t = 20 ÷ 5 = <b>4 s</b><br><br>

<b> Final Answer:</b> 4 seconds
</div>

<h3> KEY INSIGHTS</h3>
<div class="keyfact"> Acceleration depends on change in velocity, not just speed.</div>
<div class="keyfact"> Zero acceleration means constant velocity.</div>
<div class="keyfact"> Negative acceleration means slowing down.</div>
<div class="keyfact"> Even direction change means acceleration.</div>

<h3> COMMON MISTAKES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Confusing acceleration with speed.</li>
<li>Ignoring the negative sign in deceleration.</li>
<li>Using speed instead of velocity (direction matters).</li>
</ul>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Acceleration', 15),
  (382, 382, '
    <h2>Equations of Motion</h2>
<h3> FOUNDATION UNDERSTANDING</h3>
<p>
The equations of motion describe how objects move when they are accelerating uniformly.
They connect <b>velocity, acceleration, time, and displacement</b>.
</p>

<div class="keyfact"> KEY IDEA: These equations only work when acceleration is constant (uniform acceleration).</div>

<h3> IMPORTANT SYMBOLS</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>u</b> = initial velocity (m/s)</li>
<li><b>v</b> = final velocity (m/s)</li>
<li><b>a</b> = acceleration (m/s²)</li>
<li><b>t</b> = time (s)</li>
<li><b>s</b> = displacement (m)</li>
</ul>

<h3> THE 3 EQUATIONS OF MOTION</h3>

<div class="formula">
1) v = u + at
</div>

<div class="formula">
2) s = ut + ½at²
</div>

<div class="formula">
3) v² = u² + 2as
</div>

<h3> WHY WE NEED THESE EQUATIONS</h3>
<ul>
<li>To find unknown velocity, time, displacement, or acceleration</li>
<li>To solve real-world motion problems (cars, falling objects, rockets)</li>
<li>To analyze motion under constant acceleration</li>
</ul>

<h3> STEP-BY-STEP WORKED EXAMPLES</h3>

<div class="example-box">
<strong> Example 1: Final Velocity</strong><br><br>

A car starts at <b>u = 5 m/s</b> and accelerates at <b>a = 3 m/s²</b> for <b>t = 4 s</b>.<br><br>

<b>Step 1: Use equation</b><br>
v = u + at<br><br>

<b>Step 2: Substitute values</b><br>
v = 5 + (3 × 4)<br><br>

<b>Step 3: Calculate</b><br>
v = 5 + 12 = <b>17 m/s</b><br><br>

<b> Final Answer:</b> 17 m/s
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<strong> Example 2: Displacement (Acceleration Motion)</strong><br><br>

A body starts from rest (u = 0), accelerates at <b>2 m/s²</b> for <b>6 s</b>.<br><br>

<b>Step 1: Use equation</b><br>
s = ut + ½at²<br><br>

<b>Step 2: Substitute</b><br>
s = (0 × 6) + ½(2 × 6²)<br><br>

<b>Step 3: Calculate</b><br>
s = 0 + 1 × 36 = <b>36 m</b><br><br>

<b> Final Answer:</b> 36 m
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<strong> Example 3: Final Velocity (No Time Needed)</strong><br><br>

A car has initial velocity <b>u = 10 m/s</b>, acceleration <b>a = 4 m/s²</b>, and displacement <b>s = 20 m</b>.<br><br>

<b>Step 1: Use equation</b><br>
v² = u² + 2as<br><br>

<b>Step 2: Substitute</b><br>
v² = 10² + 2(4)(20)<br><br>

<b>Step 3: Calculate</b><br>
v² = 100 + 160 = 260<br>
v = √260 ≈ <b>16.1 m/s</b><br><br>

<b> Final Answer:</b> ≈ 16.1 m/s
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<strong> Example 4: Finding Acceleration</strong><br><br>

A train increases speed from <b>u = 15 m/s</b> to <b>v = 35 m/s</b> in <b>5 s</b>.<br><br>

<b>Step 1: Use equation</b><br>
v = u + at<br><br>

<b>Step 2: Rearrange</b><br>
a = (v − u) ÷ t<br><br>

<b>Step 3: Substitute</b><br>
a = (35 − 15) ÷ 5<br><br>

<b>Step 4: Calculate</b><br>
a = 20 ÷ 5 = <b>4 m/s²</b><br><br>

<b> Final Answer:</b> 4 m/s²
</div>

<h3> KEY INSIGHTS</h3>
<div class="keyfact"> Use the correct equation based on known values.</div>
<div class="keyfact"> All equations assume constant acceleration.</div>
<div class="keyfact"> Displacement is NOT the same as distance in these equations.</div>
<div class="keyfact"> Always check units before solving.</div>

<h3> COMMON MISTAKES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Using equations when acceleration is not constant.</li>
<li>Mixing up u and v.</li>
<li>Forgetting to square root in v² equation.</li>
<li>Using distance instead of displacement.</li>
</ul>

<h3> MEMORY TRICK</h3>
<div class="keyfact">
Think: “SUVAT system” <br>
S = displacement<br>
U = initial velocity<br>
V = final velocity<br>
A = acceleration<br>
T = time
</div>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Equations of Motion', 15),
  (383, 383, '
  <h2>Solving Motion Problems</h2>

  <p>Motion problems often provide several quantities and ask you to calculate one missing quantity. The reliable method is to identify the known values, choose an equation that connects them, substitute carefully, and check the answer.</p>

  <h3>The problem-solving method</h3>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Read:</strong> Identify what the question asks you to find.</li>
    <li><strong>List:</strong> Write down the known quantities with their symbols and units.</li>
    <li><strong>Convert:</strong> Change units when necessary.</li>
    <li><strong>Choose:</strong> Select an equation that connects the known quantities to the unknown.</li>
    <li><strong>Calculate:</strong> Substitute values and solve.</li>
    <li><strong>Check:</strong> Confirm the units and whether the result is physically reasonable.</li>
  </ol>

  <p>For motion with constant acceleration, the common symbols are:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>u:</strong> initial velocity, in m/s</li>
    <li><strong>v:</strong> final velocity, in m/s</li>
    <li><strong>a:</strong> acceleration, in m/s²</li>
    <li><strong>t:</strong> time, in seconds</li>
    <li><strong>s:</strong> displacement, in metres</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Finding final velocity</h3>
  <p>A cyclist travelling at 4 m/s accelerates uniformly at 2 m/s² for 3 seconds. Find the final velocity.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 1 — Identify the values:</strong> u = 4 m/s, a = 2 m/s², t = 3 s.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2 — Choose the equation:</strong> v = u + at.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3 — Substitute:</strong> v = 4 + (2 × 3).</p>
  <p><strong>Step 4 — Calculate:</strong> v = 4 + 6 = 10 m/s.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The final velocity is 10 m/s.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Finding displacement</h3>
  <p>A vehicle starts from rest and accelerates uniformly at 3 m/s² for 4 seconds. Find its displacement.</p>
  <p>Known values: u = 0 m/s, a = 3 m/s², t = 4 s.</p>
  <p>Use s = ut + ½at².</p>
  <p>s = (0 × 4) + ½ × 3 × 4².</p>
  <p>s = 0 + 1.5 × 16 = 24 m.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The displacement is 24 m.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Converting units first</h3>
  <p>A car travels at a constant speed of 72 km/h for 10 seconds. Find the distance travelled.</p>
  <p>Convert the speed: 72 ÷ 3.6 = 20 m/s.</p>
  <p>Use distance = speed × time.</p>
  <p>Distance = 20 × 10 = 200 m.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The car travels 200 m.</p>

  <h3>Common mistakes</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Using a constant-acceleration equation when acceleration is not constant.</li>
    <li>Mixing km/h with m/s without converting.</li>
    <li>Using final velocity where initial velocity is required.</li>
    <li>Forgetting to square the time in an equation containing t².</li>
    <li>Giving a numerical answer without a unit.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Solving Motion Problems', 15),
  (384, 384, '
  <h2>Uniform Motion</h2>

  <p>Uniform motion occurs when an object moves with constant velocity. Its speed and direction remain unchanged, so its acceleration is zero.</p>

  <p>If an object moves in a straight line at constant speed, the distance travelled is:</p>
  <p><strong>distance = speed × time</strong></p>

  <p>For constant velocity, displacement is:</p>
  <p><strong>displacement = velocity × time</strong></p>

  <p>Distance is a scalar quantity and cannot be negative. Displacement includes direction and may be positive, negative or zero.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Distance at constant speed</h3>
  <p>A bus travels at a constant speed of 15 m/s for 12 s. Find the distance travelled.</p>
  <p>Distance = speed × time.</p>
  <p>Distance = 15 × 12 = 180 m.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 180 m.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Finding time</h3>
  <p>A student walks 600 m at a constant speed of 2 m/s. Find the time taken.</p>
  <p>Time = distance ÷ speed.</p>
  <p>Time = 600 ÷ 2 = 300 s.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 300 s, or 5 minutes.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Constant velocity with direction</h3>
  <p>A trolley moves at a constant velocity of −3 m/s for 4 s. Find its displacement.</p>
  <p>Displacement = velocity × time.</p>
  <p>Displacement = −3 × 4 = −12 m.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> −12 m.</p>

  <h3>Recognising uniform motion</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>A straight distance–time graph with constant positive gradient indicates constant speed.</li>
    <li>A straight displacement–time graph with constant gradient indicates constant velocity.</li>
    <li>A velocity–time graph that is horizontal indicates constant velocity and zero acceleration.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Common mistakes</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Assuming constant speed always means constant velocity; direction must also stay the same.</li>
    <li>Using distance when the question asks for displacement.</li>
    <li>Mixing hours with seconds without converting units.</li>
    <li>Assuming uniform motion means the object is stationary.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Uniform Motion', 15),
  (385, 385, '
  <h2>Uniform Acceleration</h2>

  <p>Uniform acceleration means velocity changes by equal amounts in equal time intervals. Acceleration is constant in magnitude and direction throughout the interval.</p>

  <p>Acceleration is calculated using:</p>
  <p><strong>a = (v − u) ÷ t</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>u:</strong> initial velocity</li>
    <li><strong>v:</strong> final velocity</li>
    <li><strong>t:</strong> elapsed time</li>
    <li><strong>a:</strong> constant acceleration</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Rearranging gives the first equation of linear motion:</p>
  <p><strong>v = u + at</strong></p>

  <p>Positive acceleration means velocity changes in the positive direction under the chosen sign convention. Negative acceleration means acceleration points in the negative direction. Whether an object speeds up or slows down depends on the relationship between velocity and acceleration.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Speeding up</h3>
  <p>A car increases its velocity from 6 m/s to 18 m/s in 4 s. Find its acceleration.</p>
  <p>a = (v − u) ÷ t.</p>
  <p>a = (18 − 6) ÷ 4 = 12 ÷ 4 = 3 m/s².</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> +3 m/s².</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Slowing down</h3>
  <p>A bicycle moving at 12 m/s slows uniformly to 4 m/s in 2 s.</p>
  <p>a = (4 − 12) ÷ 2 = −8 ÷ 2 = −4 m/s².</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> −4 m/s².</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Finding final velocity</h3>
  <p>A trolley starts at 3 m/s and accelerates uniformly at 2 m/s² for 5 s.</p>
  <p>v = u + at.</p>
  <p>v = 3 + (2 × 5) = 13 m/s.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 13 m/s.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 4: Velocity in the negative direction</h3>
  <p>An object has initial velocity −10 m/s and acceleration +2 m/s² for 3 s.</p>
  <p>v = −10 + (2 × 3) = −4 m/s.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> −4 m/s. The object is still moving in the negative direction, but its velocity is becoming less negative.</p>

  <h3>Common mistakes</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Using u − v instead of v − u.</li>
    <li>Assuming every negative acceleration means slowing down.</li>
    <li>Using the equations of linear motion when acceleration is changing.</li>
    <li>Confusing acceleration in m/s² with velocity in m/s.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Uniform Acceleration', 15),
  (386, 386, '
  <h2>Selecting the Correct Motion Equation</h2>

  <p>The SUVAT equations describe straight-line motion with constant acceleration. The most efficient way to choose an equation is to identify the known quantities, the unknown quantity, and the quantity that is missing.</p>

  <h3>The five equations</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>v = u + at</strong></li>
    <li><strong>s = ½(u + v)t</strong></li>
    <li><strong>s = ut + ½at²</strong></li>
    <li><strong>v² = u² + 2as</strong></li>
    <li><strong>s = vt − ½at²</strong></li>
  </ul>

  <h3>What the symbols mean</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>u:</strong> initial velocity (m/s)</li>
    <li><strong>v:</strong> final velocity (m/s)</li>
    <li><strong>a:</strong> constant acceleration (m/s²)</li>
    <li><strong>t:</strong> time (s)</li>
    <li><strong>s:</strong> displacement (m)</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Choosing by the missing quantity</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Missing v, with u, a and t known: use v = u + at.</li>
    <li>Missing s, with u, a and t known: use s = ut + ½at².</li>
    <li>Missing s, with u, v and t known: use s = ½(u + v)t.</li>
    <li>Missing s, with u, v and a known but no time: use v² = u² + 2as.</li>
    <li>Missing s, with v, a and t known: use s = vt − ½at².</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Do not select an equation just because it looks familiar. First confirm that the question describes constant acceleration and that the equation contains the known quantities and the unknown.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Time is given</h3>
  <p>A body starts at 5 m/s and accelerates uniformly at 2 m/s² for 4 s. Find its final velocity.</p>
  <p>Known: u, a and t. Unknown: v.</p>
  <p>Choose v = u + at.</p>
  <p>v = 5 + (2 × 4) = 13 m/s.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Time is not given</h3>
  <p>A vehicle changes velocity from 4 m/s to 12 m/s at a constant acceleration of 2 m/s². Find its displacement.</p>
  <p>Known: u, v and a. Unknown: s. Time is not given.</p>
  <p>Choose v² = u² + 2as.</p>
  <p>12² = 4² + 2 × 2 × s.</p>
  <p>144 = 16 + 4s.</p>
  <p>128 = 4s, so s = 32 m.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Initial and final velocities are known</h3>
  <p>A vehicle accelerates uniformly from 6 m/s to 14 m/s over 5 s. Find the displacement.</p>
  <p>Known: u, v and t. Unknown: s.</p>
  <p>Choose s = ½(u + v)t.</p>
  <p>s = ½(6 + 14) × 5.</p>
  <p>s = 10 × 5 = 50 m.</p>

  <h3>Common mistakes</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Using SUVAT when acceleration is not constant.</li>
    <li>Confusing displacement s with speed or distance.</li>
    <li>Choosing an equation that contains another unknown quantity.</li>
    <li>Forgetting to square velocities in v² = u² + 2as.</li>
    <li>Ignoring the sign of velocity or acceleration.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Selecting the Correct Motion Equation', 15),
  (387, 387, '
  <h2>Free Fall</h2>

  <p>Free fall is motion in which gravity is the only force acting on an object. Near Earth''s surface, the acceleration due to gravity is approximately 9.8 m/s² downward. Some school problems use 10 m/s² when instructed to do so.</p>

  <p>When air resistance is negligible, all objects in the same gravitational field have the same acceleration due to gravity, regardless of their masses.</p>

  <h3>Important ideas</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>An object released from rest has initial velocity u = 0.</li>
    <li>Gravity accelerates a falling object downward.</li>
    <li>If upward is chosen as positive, gravitational acceleration is negative: a = −g.</li>
    <li>If downward is chosen as positive, gravitational acceleration is positive: a = +g.</li>
    <li>Use equations of linear motion because acceleration is approximately constant near Earth''s surface.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Speed after falling</h3>
  <p>A stone is released from rest and falls for 3 s. Take g = 10 m/s² and ignore air resistance. Find its velocity after 3 s, taking downward as positive.</p>
  <p>u = 0, a = +10 m/s², t = 3 s.</p>
  <p>v = u + at.</p>
  <p>v = 0 + (10 × 3) = +30 m/s.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 30 m/s downward.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Distance fallen</h3>
  <p>A ball is released from rest and falls for 2 s. Take g = 10 m/s².</p>
  <p>s = ut + ½at².</p>
  <p>s = (0 × 2) + ½ × 10 × 2².</p>
  <p>s = 5 × 4 = 20 m.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 20 m downward.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Object thrown vertically upward</h3>
  <p>A ball is thrown vertically upward at 20 m/s. Take upward as positive and g = 10 m/s². Find the time taken to reach its highest point.</p>
  <p>At the highest point, the instantaneous velocity is v = 0 m/s.</p>
  <p>u = +20 m/s, a = −10 m/s².</p>
  <p>v = u + at.</p>
  <p>0 = 20 − 10t.</p>
  <p>10t = 20, so t = 2 s.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 2 s.</p>

  <h3>Common mistakes</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Using the wrong sign for gravitational acceleration.</li>
    <li>Assuming an object at its highest point has zero acceleration. Its velocity is momentarily zero, but gravity still acts.</li>
    <li>Claiming heavier objects fall faster in a vacuum.</li>
    <li>Forgetting that air resistance can change the motion of real falling objects.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Free Fall', 15),
  (388, 388, '
  <h2>Distance–Time Graphs</h2>

  <p>A distance–time graph shows how the total distance travelled changes with time. Time is normally placed on the horizontal axis, and distance on the vertical axis.</p>

  <h3>Reading the graph</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Horizontal axis:</strong> time, usually in seconds.</li>
    <li><strong>Vertical axis:</strong> distance, usually in metres.</li>
    <li><strong>Gradient:</strong> speed.</li>
    <li><strong>Horizontal line:</strong> the object is stationary because its distance from the starting point is not increasing.</li>
    <li><strong>Straight rising line:</strong> constant speed.</li>
    <li><strong>Steeper rising line:</strong> greater speed.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>The gradient is calculated using two points on the line:</p>
  <p><strong>Speed = change in distance ÷ change in time</strong></p>
  <p>In symbols: speed = (d₂ − d₁) ÷ (t₂ − t₁).</p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Finding speed from a graph</h3>
  <p>A straight section of a distance–time graph goes from (0 s, 0 m) to (5 s, 20 m). Find the speed.</p>
  <p>Change in distance = 20 − 0 = 20 m.</p>
  <p>Change in time = 5 − 0 = 5 s.</p>
  <p>Speed = 20 ÷ 5 = 4 m/s.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 4 m/s.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Recognising rest</h3>
  <p>Between 6 s and 10 s, a distance–time graph remains horizontal at 30 m.</p>
  <p>The distance does not change during this interval.</p>
  <p>Speed = (30 − 30) ÷ (10 − 6) = 0 ÷ 4 = 0 m/s.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The object is stationary for 4 seconds.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Comparing two objects</h3>
  <p>Object A travels 12 m in 3 s. Object B travels 20 m in 5 s. Compare their speeds.</p>
  <p>Speed of A = 12 ÷ 3 = 4 m/s.</p>
  <p>Speed of B = 20 ÷ 5 = 4 m/s.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> They have equal speeds, so their straight graph sections have equal gradients.</p>

  <h3>Common mistakes</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Reading the height of the graph as the speed.</li>
    <li>Calculating distance divided by time using the wrong interval.</li>
    <li>Assuming a horizontal distance–time line means constant motion rather than rest.</li>
    <li>Confusing a steeper gradient with a greater distance at that instant.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Distance–Time Graphs', 15),
  (389, 389, '
  <h2>Displacement–Time Graphs</h2>

  <p>A displacement–time graph shows an object''s displacement from a chosen origin at different times. Unlike distance, displacement includes direction through its positive or negative sign.</p>

  <h3>What the gradient tells you</h3>
  <p><strong>Gradient of a displacement–time graph = velocity.</strong></p>
  <p>Average velocity = change in displacement ÷ change in time.</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Positive gradient:</strong> positive velocity in the chosen direction.</li>
    <li><strong>Negative gradient:</strong> motion in the opposite direction.</li>
    <li><strong>Zero gradient:</strong> the object is stationary during that interval.</li>
    <li><strong>Straight sloping line:</strong> constant velocity.</li>
    <li><strong>Changing gradient:</strong> changing velocity.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>The graph''s vertical position gives displacement, not velocity. Velocity is obtained from its gradient.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Positive velocity</h3>
  <p>An object''s displacement changes from 2 m at 1 s to 14 m at 4 s.</p>
  <p>Change in displacement = 14 − 2 = 12 m.</p>
  <p>Change in time = 4 − 1 = 3 s.</p>
  <p>Velocity = 12 ÷ 3 = +4 m/s.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> +4 m/s.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Negative velocity</h3>
  <p>An object moves from displacement +12 m at 2 s to +3 m at 5 s.</p>
  <p>Change in displacement = 3 − 12 = −9 m.</p>
  <p>Change in time = 5 − 2 = 3 s.</p>
  <p>Velocity = −9 ÷ 3 = −3 m/s.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> −3 m/s. The object moves in the negative direction.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Stationary interval</h3>
  <p>The displacement remains −4 m from 3 s to 8 s.</p>
  <p>Velocity = [−4 − (−4)] ÷ (8 − 3) = 0 ÷ 5 = 0 m/s.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The object is stationary at a displacement of −4 m during this interval.</p>

  <h3>Common mistakes</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Using the vertical displacement value as velocity.</li>
    <li>Ignoring the negative sign when displacement decreases.</li>
    <li>Confusing negative displacement with negative speed.</li>
    <li>Assuming a negative gradient means the object is slowing down. It indicates negative velocity; speed depends on the magnitude of velocity.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Displacement–Time Graphs', 15),
  (390, 390, '
  <h2>Speed–Time Graphs</h2>

  <p>A speed–time graph shows how an object''s speed changes with time. Time is placed on the horizontal axis and speed on the vertical axis.</p>

  <h3>Two important features</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Gradient:</strong> the rate of change of speed. For motion along a fixed direction without reversal, this gives the magnitude of acceleration.</li>
    <li><strong>Area under the graph:</strong> distance travelled, because speed is never negative.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>For a straight graph section, gradient = change in speed ÷ change in time.</p>
  <p>For a rectangular area, distance = base × height = time × speed.</p>
  <p>For a triangular area, distance = ½ × base × height.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Distance at constant speed</h3>
  <p>A vehicle travels at 6 m/s for 5 s. The speed–time graph is a horizontal line at 6 m/s.</p>
  <p>Area = 5 × 6 = 30 m.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 30 m travelled.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Distance while accelerating from rest</h3>
  <p>A body increases speed uniformly from 0 to 8 m/s over 4 s.</p>
  <p>The area under the graph is a triangle.</p>
  <p>Distance = ½ × base × height.</p>
  <p>Distance = ½ × 4 × 8 = 16 m.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 16 m.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Finding the rate of change of speed</h3>
  <p>Speed increases from 3 m/s to 15 m/s in 4 s.</p>
  <p>Gradient = (15 − 3) ÷ 4 = 12 ÷ 4 = 3 m/s².</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The speed increases at 3 m/s².</p>

  <h3>Common mistakes</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Using the gradient to calculate distance.</li>
    <li>Using the area under the graph to calculate acceleration.</li>
    <li>Forgetting the factor ½ for a triangular area.</li>
    <li>Using the final speed alone as the distance travelled.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Speed–Time Graphs', 15),
  (391, 391, '
  <h2>Velocity–Time Graphs</h2>

  <p>A velocity–time graph shows how velocity changes with time. Velocity includes direction, so it can be positive, zero or negative.</p>

  <h3>Gradient gives acceleration</h3>
  <p><strong>Acceleration = change in velocity ÷ change in time.</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li>Positive gradient: positive acceleration.</li>
    <li>Negative gradient: negative acceleration.</li>
    <li>Zero gradient: constant velocity.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Signed area gives displacement</h3>
  <p>The area between the velocity–time graph and the time axis represents displacement. Area above the time axis is positive; area below it is negative. Add the signed areas to find total displacement.</p>
  <p>Total distance is different: add the magnitudes of the areas above and below the axis.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Acceleration</h3>
  <p>Velocity changes from 2 m/s to 14 m/s over 4 s.</p>
  <p>Acceleration = (14 − 2) ÷ 4 = 12 ÷ 4 = 3 m/s².</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> +3 m/s².</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Displacement during constant velocity</h3>
  <p>An object has a constant velocity of 5 m/s for 6 s.</p>
  <p>Displacement = area = 5 × 6 = 30 m.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> +30 m.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Negative displacement</h3>
  <p>An object moves at −4 m/s for 3 s.</p>
  <p>Displacement = velocity × time = −4 × 3 = −12 m.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> −12 m. The distance travelled is 12 m.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 4: Changing velocity</h3>
  <p>Velocity rises uniformly from 0 to 8 m/s over 4 s.</p>
  <p>Displacement = triangular area = ½ × 4 × 8 = 16 m.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> +16 m.</p>

  <h3>Common mistakes</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Using the graph''s height to calculate acceleration.</li>
    <li>Ignoring negative areas when calculating displacement.</li>
    <li>Confusing total distance with displacement.</li>
    <li>Assuming a negative velocity automatically means negative acceleration.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Velocity–Time Graphs', 15),
  (392, 392, '
  <h2>Interpreting Motion Graphs</h2>

  <p>Interpreting a motion graph means identifying the motion represented by each section, calculating quantities from the correct graph feature, and distinguishing what the graph shows from what it does not show.</p>

  <h3>Identify the graph first</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Distance–time:</strong> gradient gives speed; horizontal sections show rest.</li>
    <li><strong>Displacement–time:</strong> gradient gives velocity; negative gradients show motion in the negative direction.</li>
    <li><strong>Speed–time:</strong> gradient gives the rate of change of speed; area gives distance.</li>
    <li><strong>Velocity–time:</strong> gradient gives acceleration; signed area gives displacement.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Read sections in order</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Check the axis labels and units.</li>
    <li>Identify the time interval being discussed.</li>
    <li>Determine whether the graph is rising, falling, horizontal or curved.</li>
    <li>Choose the relevant feature: gradient, area or vertical value.</li>
    <li>Calculate using the correct interval and state the unit.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Distance–time interpretation</h3>
  <p>A distance–time graph rises from 0 m to 12 m during the first 3 s, then remains at 12 m until 5 s.</p>
  <p>First section: speed = 12 ÷ 3 = 4 m/s.</p>
  <p>Second section: distance is unchanged, so speed = 0 m/s.</p>
  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> The object moves at constant speed for 3 s and then rests for 2 s.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Velocity–time interpretation</h3>
  <p>A velocity–time graph shows +4 m/s from 0 to 3 s and −2 m/s from 3 to 5 s.</p>
  <p>First displacement = +4 × 3 = +12 m.</p>
  <p>Second displacement = −2 × 2 = −4 m.</p>
  <p>Total displacement = 12 − 4 = +8 m.</p>
  <p>Total distance = 12 + 4 = 16 m.</p>
  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> The object travels 16 m, but its net displacement is +8 m.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Identifying acceleration</h3>
  <p>A velocity–time graph rises in a straight line from 2 m/s at 1 s to 10 m/s at 5 s.</p>
  <p>Acceleration = (10 − 2) ÷ (5 − 1) = 8 ÷ 4 = 2 m/s².</p>
  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> The object has constant positive acceleration of 2 m/s² during this section.</p>

  <h3>Common mistakes</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Using the same calculation for every type of motion graph.</li>
    <li>Confusing a falling graph with a stationary object.</li>
    <li>Ignoring axis labels and units.</li>
    <li>Calling total distance and displacement the same quantity.</li>
    <li>Assuming a curved line always means the object is speeding up; inspect the gradient over time.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Interpreting Motion Graphs', 15),
  (393, 393, '
  <h2>Newton’s First Law</h2>

  <p>Newton''s first law states that an object remains at rest or continues moving with constant velocity in a straight line unless acted on by a non-zero resultant external force.</p>

  <p>This law describes inertia: objects resist changes to their state of motion.</p>

  <h3>Resultant force and motion</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>If the resultant force is zero, acceleration is zero.</li>
    <li>An object at rest remains at rest if the resultant force stays zero.</li>
    <li>An object already moving continues at constant velocity if the resultant force stays zero.</li>
    <li>A non-zero resultant force causes acceleration, changing velocity.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Zero resultant force does not mean that no forces act. Several forces may act and balance one another.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Balanced forces</h3>
  <p>A box is pushed to the right with 20 N while friction acts to the left with 20 N.</p>
  <p>Resultant force = 20 − 20 = 0 N.</p>
  <p>Therefore, acceleration is zero. The box may remain at rest or continue at constant velocity, depending on its initial state.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: A moving vehicle</h3>
  <p>A vehicle moves in a straight line at constant velocity. Its driving force is 1,500 N forward.</p>
  <p>For the velocity to remain constant, the total opposing force must also be 1,500 N.</p>
  <p>Resultant force = 1,500 − 1,500 = 0 N.</p>
  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> Constant velocity is consistent with zero resultant force.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Why passengers move forward</h3>
  <p>A moving bus brakes suddenly. Passengers tend to move forward relative to the bus because their bodies tend to maintain their previous motion. A seat belt supplies a force that changes the passengers'' motion with the bus.</p>

  <h3>Common mistakes</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Thinking a moving object must always have a forward resultant force.</li>
    <li>Thinking zero resultant force means zero velocity.</li>
    <li>Confusing balanced forces with the absence of forces.</li>
    <li>Forgetting that a change in direction is also a change in velocity.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Newton’s First Law', 15),
  (394, 394, '
  <h2>Newton’s Second Law</h2>

  <p>Newton''s second law relates the resultant force on an object to its mass and acceleration. For constant mass, the resultant force equals mass multiplied by acceleration.</p>

  <p><strong>F = ma</strong></p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>F:</strong> resultant force, in newtons (N)</li>
    <li><strong>m:</strong> mass, in kilograms (kg)</li>
    <li><strong>a:</strong> acceleration, in metres per second squared (m/s²)</li>
  </ul>

  <p>One newton is the resultant force that gives a mass of 1 kg an acceleration of 1 m/s².</p>

  <h3>Rearranging the equation</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Force: F = ma</li>
    <li>Mass: m = F ÷ a</li>
    <li>Acceleration: a = F ÷ m</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Finding force</h3>
  <p>A 4 kg trolley accelerates at 3 m/s². Find the resultant force.</p>
  <p>F = ma = 4 × 3 = 12 N.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 12 N in the direction of acceleration.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Finding acceleration</h3>
  <p>A resultant force of 20 N acts on a 5 kg object.</p>
  <p>a = F ÷ m = 20 ÷ 5 = 4 m/s².</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 4 m/s².</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Finding mass</h3>
  <p>A resultant force of 18 N gives an object an acceleration of 6 m/s².</p>
  <p>m = F ÷ a = 18 ÷ 6 = 3 kg.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 3 kg.</p>

  <h3>Common mistakes</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Using mass in grams without converting to kilograms.</li>
    <li>Using one force instead of the resultant force.</li>
    <li>Multiplying when the required rearrangement involves division.</li>
    <li>Writing the force unit as kg or m/s².</li>
  </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Newton’s Second Law', 15),
  (395, 395, '
  <h2>Newton’s Third Law</h2>

  <p>Newton''s third law states that when one object exerts a force on another object, the second object simultaneously exerts a force equal in magnitude and opposite in direction on the first object.</p>

  <p>These forces are often described as an action–reaction pair, but neither force happens first. They act on different objects.</p>

  <h3>Recognising a force pair</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>The two forces are equal in magnitude.</li>
    <li>They act in opposite directions.</li>
    <li>They act on different objects.</li>
    <li>They belong to the same interaction.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Pushing a wall</h3>
  <p>A person pushes a wall with a force of 50 N to the right.</p>
  <p>The wall exerts a force of 50 N to the left on the person.</p>
  <p><strong>Important:</strong> The forces do not cancel on one object because one acts on the wall and the other acts on the person.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Walking</h3>
  <p>A person''s foot pushes the ground backward. The ground exerts a forward frictional force on the foot. This forward force helps the person move forward.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: A swimmer</h3>
  <p>A swimmer pushes water backward. The water pushes the swimmer forward with an equal and opposite force.</p>

  <h3>Why force pairs do not cancel each other</h3>
  <p>To calculate the resultant force on an object, include only the forces acting on that object. The reaction force acts on the other object, so it is not included in the first object''s force total.</p>

  <h3>Common mistakes</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Claiming that action and reaction forces act on the same object.</li>
    <li>Claiming one force occurs before the other.</li>
    <li>Thinking the forces must always produce equal accelerations. Different masses can have different accelerations.</li>
    <li>Confusing a third-law pair with two balanced forces acting on one object.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Newton’s Third Law', 15),
  (396, 396, '
  <h2>Mass and Inertia</h2>

  <p>Mass is a measure of the amount of matter in an object and, in mechanics, of its resistance to acceleration. Its SI unit is the kilogram (kg).</p>

  <p><strong>Inertia</strong> is the tendency of an object to resist changes in its state of rest or uniform straight-line motion. Greater mass means greater inertia.</p>

  <h3>How mass affects motion</h3>
  <p>Newton''s second law is F = ma. Rearranging gives a = F/m.</p>
  <p>For the same resultant force, an object with greater mass has less acceleration. An object with smaller mass has greater acceleration.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Comparing acceleration</h3>
  <p>A 2 kg object and an 8 kg object each experience a resultant force of 16 N.</p>
  <p>For the 2 kg object: a = 16 ÷ 2 = 8 m/s².</p>
  <p>For the 8 kg object: a = 16 ÷ 8 = 2 m/s².</p>
  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> The 2 kg object accelerates four times as much as the 8 kg object.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Inertia in a bus</h3>
  <p>When a bus starts moving suddenly, passengers may appear to lean backward relative to the bus. Their bodies tend to remain in their previous state of rest while the bus accelerates forward.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Increasing mass</h3>
  <p>If the same net force acts on an object whose mass doubles, its acceleration halves because a = F/m.</p>

  <h3>Mass is not weight</h3>
  <p>Mass is measured in kilograms and does not depend on location. Weight is the gravitational force on an object and is measured in newtons.</p>

  <h3>Common mistakes</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Using kilograms as a unit of weight.</li>
    <li>Thinking that a large mass must always move slowly; mass alone does not specify velocity.</li>
    <li>Confusing inertia with a force.</li>
    <li>Assuming mass changes simply because an object moves to the Moon.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Mass and Inertia', 15),
  (397, 397, '
  <h2>Force, Mass and Acceleration Calculations</h2>

  <p>Problems involving force, mass and acceleration are solved using Newton''s second law. The force in the equation must be the <strong>resultant force</strong>, not automatically the largest force or the applied force alone.</p>

  <p><strong>F = ma</strong></p>

  <h3>When forces act in opposite directions</h3>
  <p>Choose a positive direction. Add forces acting in that direction and subtract forces acting in the opposite direction. The result is the signed resultant force.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Opposing forces</h3>
  <p>A 5 kg trolley is pulled rightward with 30 N. Friction acts leftward with 10 N. Find the acceleration.</p>
  <p>Resultant force = 30 − 10 = 20 N rightward.</p>
  <p>a = F/m = 20/5 = 4 m/s².</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 4 m/s² rightward.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Equal opposing forces</h3>
  <p>A 10 kg box is pushed rightward with 40 N. Friction acts leftward with 40 N.</p>
  <p>Resultant force = 40 − 40 = 0 N.</p>
  <p>a = 0/10 = 0 m/s².</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Zero acceleration.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Finding the applied force</h3>
  <p>A 4 kg object accelerates rightward at 3 m/s² while friction acts leftward with 5 N. Find the applied rightward force.</p>
  <p>Required resultant force = ma = 4 × 3 = 12 N rightward.</p>
  <p>Applied force − 5 = 12.</p>
  <p>Applied force = 17 N.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 17 N rightward.</p>

  <h3>Common mistakes</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Using the applied force directly when opposing forces are present.</li>
    <li>Adding forces that act in opposite directions.</li>
    <li>Forgetting that acceleration points in the direction of the resultant force.</li>
    <li>Using grams instead of kilograms.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Force, Mass and Acceleration Calculations', 15),
  (398, 398, '
  <h2>Mass and Weight</h2>

  <p>Mass is the amount of matter in an object and a measure of its resistance to acceleration. Its SI unit is the kilogram (kg).</p>

  <p>Weight is the gravitational force acting on an object. Its SI unit is the newton (N).</p>

  <p><strong>Weight = mass × gravitational field strength</strong></p>
  <p><strong>W = mg</strong></p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>W:</strong> weight in newtons (N)</li>
    <li><strong>m:</strong> mass in kilograms (kg)</li>
    <li><strong>g:</strong> gravitational field strength in N/kg</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Near Earth''s surface, g is approximately 9.8 N/kg, although some school calculations use 10 N/kg when specified.</p>

  <h3>Key differences</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Mass is measured in kilograms; weight is measured in newtons.</li>
    <li>Mass remains the same when an object moves between locations, provided no material is lost or gained.</li>
    <li>Weight changes when gravitational field strength changes.</li>
    <li>Weight acts toward the centre of the attracting body.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Finding weight</h3>
  <p>A student has a mass of 50 kg. Take g = 10 N/kg. Find the student''s weight.</p>
  <p>W = mg = 50 × 10 = 500 N.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 500 N.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Finding mass from weight</h3>
  <p>An object weighs 120 N where g = 10 N/kg. Find its mass.</p>
  <p>m = W/g = 120 ÷ 10 = 12 kg.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 12 kg.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Weight on the Moon</h3>
  <p>An object has a mass of 6 kg on Earth. If the Moon''s gravitational field strength is 1.6 N/kg, find its weight on the Moon.</p>
  <p>W = mg = 6 × 1.6 = 9.6 N.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 9.6 N. Its mass remains 6 kg.</p>

  <h3>Common mistakes</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Writing weight in kilograms.</li>
    <li>Using W = m/g instead of W = mg.</li>
    <li>Assuming mass changes on the Moon.</li>
    <li>Using a value of g without checking the question''s instructions.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Mass and Weight', 15),
  (399, 399, '
  <h2>Gravitational Field Strength</h2>

  <p>Gravitational field strength at a location is the gravitational force acting per unit mass on an object placed there.</p>

  <p><strong>g = W ÷ m</strong></p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>g:</strong> gravitational field strength, in N/kg</li>
    <li><strong>W:</strong> weight, in N</li>
    <li><strong>m:</strong> mass, in kg</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Near Earth''s surface, gravitational field strength is approximately 9.8 N/kg. Its value varies with location and is smaller at the Moon''s surface.</p>

  <p>Gravitational field strength in N/kg has the same numerical value as gravitational acceleration in m/s² at the same location, because 1 N = 1 kg·m/s².</p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Finding field strength</h3>
  <p>An object of mass 5 kg has a weight of 49 N. Calculate the gravitational field strength.</p>
  <p>g = W/m = 49 ÷ 5 = 9.8 N/kg.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 9.8 N/kg.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Comparing weight on two planets</h3>
  <p>A 10 kg object is on a planet where g = 4 N/kg.</p>
  <p>W = mg = 10 × 4 = 40 N.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 40 N.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Finding mass</h3>
  <p>An object weighs 80 N in a gravitational field of 8 N/kg.</p>
  <p>m = W/g = 80 ÷ 8 = 10 kg.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 10 kg.</p>

  <h3>Common mistakes</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Using mass divided by weight instead of weight divided by mass.</li>
    <li>Writing N/kg as the unit of mass.</li>
    <li>Assuming gravitational field strength is identical everywhere.</li>
    <li>Confusing gravitational field strength with the object''s mass.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Gravitational Field Strength', 15),
  (400, 400, '
  <h2>Calculating Weight</h2>

  <p>Weight is the gravitational force acting on an object. Calculate it by multiplying the object''s mass by the gravitational field strength at its location.</p>

  <p><strong>W = mg</strong></p>

  <p>Before calculating:</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Identify the object''s mass in kilograms.</li>
    <li>Identify the gravitational field strength in N/kg.</li>
    <li>Multiply the values.</li>
    <li>State the answer in newtons.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Standard calculation</h3>
  <p>Find the weight of a 3.5 kg object where g = 10 N/kg.</p>
  <p>W = 3.5 × 10 = 35 N.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 35 N.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Decimal field strength</h3>
  <p>Calculate the weight of a 7.5 kg object where g = 9.8 N/kg.</p>
  <p>W = 7.5 × 9.8 = 73.5 N.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 73.5 N.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Mass given in grams</h3>
  <p>A sample has a mass of 800 g. Find its weight where g = 10 N/kg.</p>
  <p>Convert mass: 800 ÷ 1,000 = 0.8 kg.</p>
  <p>W = 0.8 × 10 = 8 N.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 8 N.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 4: Comparing locations</h3>
  <p>A 5 kg object is on Earth where g = 9.8 N/kg and then on a planet where g = 3 N/kg.</p>
  <p>Earth weight = 5 × 9.8 = 49 N.</p>
  <p>Other planet''s weight = 5 × 3 = 15 N.</p>
  <p>The mass remains 5 kg at both locations.</p>

  <h3>Common mistakes</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Using the wrong value of g.</li>
    <li>Multiplying a mass in grams directly by g in N/kg.</li>
    <li>Reporting weight in kilograms.</li>
    <li>Assuming weight stays constant at every location.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Calculating Weight', 15),
  (401, 401, '
  <h2>Density</h2>

  <p>Density is the mass per unit volume of a substance. It describes how much mass is contained in a given volume.</p>

  <p><strong>Density = mass ÷ volume</strong></p>
  <p><strong>ρ = m/V</strong></p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>ρ:</strong> density</li>
    <li><strong>m:</strong> mass</li>
    <li><strong>V:</strong> volume</li>
  </ul>

  <p>The SI unit of density is kg/m³. Another commonly used unit is g/cm³.</p>

  <p>Density is a property of the material under specified conditions. For a uniform sample of the same material, increasing the amount increases both mass and volume in the same proportion, so density remains unchanged.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Calculating density</h3>
  <p>A metal block has a mass of 600 kg and a volume of 0.2 m³. Calculate its density.</p>
  <p>ρ = m/V = 600 ÷ 0.2 = 3,000 kg/m³.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 3,000 kg/m³.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Using grams and cubic centimetres</h3>
  <p>A sample has a mass of 180 g and a volume of 60 cm³.</p>
  <p>Density = 180 ÷ 60 = 3 g/cm³.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 3 g/cm³.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Comparing equal volumes</h3>
  <p>Two blocks each have a volume of 2 m³. Block A has a mass of 10 kg, while Block B has a mass of 18 kg.</p>
  <p>Density of A = 10 ÷ 2 = 5 kg/m³.</p>
  <p>Density of B = 18 ÷ 2 = 9 kg/m³.</p>
  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> Block B has the greater density.</p>

  <h3>Common mistakes</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Multiplying mass by volume instead of dividing.</li>
    <li>Mixing units such as kg and cm³ without conversion.</li>
    <li>Writing density in kg or m³ instead of kg/m³.</li>
    <li>Assuming a larger object must have a greater density.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Density', 15),
  (402, 402, '
  <h2>Calculating Density</h2>
  <p>Density tells us how much mass is contained in a given volume. To calculate density, divide mass by volume.</p>

  <h3>Formula</h3>
  <p><strong>Density = Mass ÷ Volume</strong></p>
  <p>\(\rho = \frac{m}{V}\)</p>
  <ul>
    <li>\(\rho\) = density</li>
    <li>\(m\) = mass</li>
    <li>\(V\) = volume</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The SI unit of density is kg/m³. Another common unit is g/cm³.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Find density</h3>
  <p>A block has a mass of 600 g and a volume of 200 cm³. Find its density.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Write the formula: density = mass ÷ volume.</li>
    <li>Substitute: density = 600 ÷ 200.</li>
    <li>Calculate: density = 3 g/cm³.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Find mass</h3>
  <p>A liquid has a density of 800 kg/m³ and a volume of 0.02 m³.</p>
  <p>Rearrange the formula: mass = density × volume.</p>
  <p>Mass = 800 × 0.02 = <strong>16 kg</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Find volume</h3>
  <p>An object has a mass of 2 kg and a density of 500 kg/m³.</p>
  <p>Volume = mass ÷ density = 2 ÷ 500 = <strong>0.004 m³</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Unit conversion</h3>
  <p>Before substituting values, make sure the units are compatible. Remember:</p>
  <p><strong>1 g/cm³ = 1000 kg/m³</strong></p>
  <p>For example, 2.7 g/cm³ = 2700 kg/m³.</p>

  <h3>Common mistake</h3>
  <p>Do not multiply mass by volume when calculating density. Divide mass by volume, and include the correct unit in your answer.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Calculating Density', 15),
  (403, 403, '
  <h2>Relative Density</h2>
  <p>Relative density compares the density of a substance with the density of water. It tells us how dense the substance is compared with water.</p>

  <h3>Formula</h3>
  <p><strong>Relative density = Density of substance ÷ Density of water</strong></p>
  <p>\(\text{Relative density} = \frac{\rho_s}{\rho_w}\)</p>
  <p>For school calculations, the density of water is commonly taken as 1000 kg/m³ or 1 g/cm³.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 1</h3>
  <p>A liquid has a density of 800 kg/m³. Calculate its relative density.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Relative density = 800 ÷ 1000.</li>
    <li>Relative density = <strong>0.8</strong>.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Worked example 2</h3>
  <p>A solid has a density of 2.7 g/cm³.</p>
  <p>Relative density = 2.7 ÷ 1 = <strong>2.7</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Important properties</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Relative density has no unit because it is a ratio of two densities.</li>
    <li>Convert densities into compatible units before dividing.</li>
    <li>A substance less dense than water may float in water; one more dense than water may sink, depending on the object''s overall density and conditions.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Common mistake</h3>
  <p>Do not attach kg/m³ or g/cm³ to relative density. Those are units of density, not relative density.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Relative Density', 15),
  (404, 404, '
  <h2>Causes of Friction</h2>
  <p>Friction is a force that opposes relative sliding, or the tendency of relative sliding, between surfaces in contact.</p>

  <h3>Why friction occurs</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Surface irregularities:</strong> even surfaces that look smooth have tiny irregularities. These can interlock when pressed together.</li>
    <li><strong>Adhesion:</strong> microscopic attractions between contacting surfaces can resist sliding.</li>
    <li><strong>Force pressing surfaces together:</strong> pressing surfaces together more strongly often increases the frictional force, depending on the materials and conditions.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Worked example 1</h3>
  <p>A box is pushed across a rough floor. The floor exerts a frictional force on the box opposite to its sliding relative to the floor.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2</h3>
  <p>A heavy crate is harder to slide than a similar lighter crate on the same floor. One reason is that the heavier crate presses the surfaces together more strongly.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3</h3>
  <p>A polished surface may offer less friction than a rough surface under comparable conditions. However, friction also depends on the materials and the condition of the surfaces; roughness alone does not determine every case.</p>

  <h3>Common mistake</h3>
  <p>Friction does not always point opposite to an object''s overall motion. It opposes relative slipping at the contact surface. For example, friction between the ground and a walking foot can push the person forward.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Causes of Friction', 15),
  (405, 405, '
  <h2>Effects of Friction</h2>
  <p>Friction affects movement whenever contacting surfaces resist relative sliding. Its effects can be useful or unwanted.</p>

  <h3>Effect 1: It opposes sliding</h3>
  <p>A box sliding across a floor slows down if the frictional force is not balanced by another forward force.</p>

  <h3>Effect 2: It produces heating</h3>
  <p>When surfaces rub together, some mechanical energy is transferred into internal energy, warming the surfaces.</p>

  <h3>Effect 3: It causes wear</h3>
  <p>Repeated rubbing can gradually remove material from tyres, shoe soles, machine parts and other surfaces.</p>

  <h3>Effect 4: It provides grip</h3>
  <p>Friction between shoes and the ground helps walking. Friction between tyres and the road helps vehicles accelerate, turn and brake.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 1</h3>
  <p>A bicycle brake presses a pad against the wheel''s braking surface. Friction transfers kinetic energy mainly into thermal energy, slowing the bicycle.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2</h3>
  <p>Rubbing your hands together warms them because friction converts some mechanical energy into internal energy.</p>

  <h3>Common mistake</h3>
  <p>Do not conclude that friction is always harmful. Without sufficient friction, walking, gripping and effective braking would be difficult or impossible.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Effects of Friction', 15),
  (406, 406, '
  <h2>Advantages and Disadvantages of Friction</h2>
  <p>Friction is useful when grip or controlled slowing is needed. It is disadvantageous when it causes unwanted energy transfer, wear or resistance.</p>

  <h3>Advantages</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Walking:</strong> friction between shoes and the ground helps prevent slipping.</li>
    <li><strong>Braking:</strong> friction helps vehicles and bicycles slow down.</li>
    <li><strong>Grip:</strong> friction helps hands hold objects and tools.</li>
    <li><strong>Writing:</strong> friction between a pencil and paper allows graphite to mark the paper.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Disadvantages</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Wear:</strong> rubbing can damage surfaces and machine components.</li>
    <li><strong>Heating:</strong> some mechanical energy is transferred into internal energy.</li>
    <li><strong>Reduced efficiency:</strong> machines may require extra energy to overcome unwanted friction.</li>
    <li><strong>Resistance:</strong> friction can oppose movement where sliding is intended.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Worked example 1</h3>
  <p>For a car''s brakes, friction is an advantage because it helps the car stop.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2</h3>
  <p>Inside a poorly lubricated engine, unwanted friction can cause heating and wear. Lubrication can reduce this problem.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3</h3>
  <p>When walking on an icy surface, available friction may be too small for a secure grip, increasing the risk of slipping.</p>

  <h3>Common mistake</h3>
  <p>Whether friction is an advantage depends on the task. The same force that helps brakes work can waste energy inside a machine.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Advantages and Disadvantages of Friction', 15),
  (407, 407, '
  <h2>Reducing and Increasing Friction</h2>
  <p>Friction is reduced when less resistance is needed, and increased when more grip is required. The correct method depends on the task and the surfaces involved.</p>

  <h3>Methods of reducing friction</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Lubrication:</strong> oil or grease separates contacting surfaces and reduces direct rubbing.</li>
    <li><strong>Ball bearings:</strong> rolling elements allow parts to move with less sliding friction.</li>
    <li><strong>Rollers or wheels:</strong> rolling can replace sliding and often reduces resistance.</li>
    <li><strong>Streamlining:</strong> a suitable shape reduces fluid resistance when moving through air or water.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Methods of increasing friction</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Treads:</strong> tyre treads help maintain grip and channel water away under suitable conditions.</li>
    <li><strong>Rough or high-grip surfaces:</strong> suitable surface materials can improve traction.</li>
    <li><strong>Brake pads:</strong> materials are selected to provide useful friction during braking.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Worked example 1</h3>
  <p>Oil is placed between moving machine parts to reduce direct contact and unwanted friction.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2</h3>
  <p>Ball bearings in a wheel reduce resistance by allowing rolling rather than extensive sliding at the bearing surfaces.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3</h3>
  <p>Tyres are designed to provide grip on roads. Tread design is especially important when road conditions involve water or loose material.</p>

  <h3>Common mistake</h3>
  <p>Streamlining reduces fluid resistance, not every type of friction. Lubricants are also not suitable for every contact, especially where a high-grip surface is needed.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Reducing and Increasing Friction', 15),
  (408, 408, '
  <h2>Air Resistance and Terminal Velocity</h2>
  <p>Air resistance, also called drag, is a force exerted by air on an object moving through it. It acts opposite to the object''s motion relative to the air.</p>

  <h3>How air resistance changes</h3>
  <p>For many ordinary objects, air resistance increases as speed through the air increases. The exact relationship depends on the object''s shape, size and flow conditions.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: A falling object</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>At the start of a fall, the object''s weight acts downward.</li>
    <li>If air resistance is initially small, the object accelerates downward.</li>
    <li>As its speed increases, air resistance increases upward.</li>
    <li>The resultant downward force becomes smaller, so the acceleration decreases.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Terminal velocity</h3>
  <p>Terminal velocity is reached when the upward forces of air resistance and buoyancy balance the object''s weight downward. The resultant force is then zero, so the object continues falling at a constant velocity.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2</h3>
  <p>A falling object has weight 20 N downward and air resistance 20 N upward. If buoyancy is negligible, the resultant force is zero. The object has no acceleration at that instant and, if these forces remain balanced, falls at constant velocity.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: A parachute</h3>
  <p>Opening a parachute greatly increases the area interacting with the air. Drag increases, slowing the fall until a new, lower terminal speed can be reached.</p>

  <h3>Common mistake</h3>
  <p>At terminal velocity, the object''s velocity is not zero. Its acceleration is zero because the resultant force is zero.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Air Resistance and Terminal Velocity', 15),
  (409, 409, '
  <h2>Elasticity and Plasticity</h2>
  <p>When a force deforms a material, the material may return to its original shape when the force is removed, or it may remain permanently deformed.</p>

  <h3>Elastic deformation</h3>
  <p>A material undergoes elastic deformation when it returns to its original shape and dimensions after the deforming force is removed, provided it has not been damaged or taken beyond its elastic limit.</p>

  <h3>Plastic deformation</h3>
  <p>A material undergoes plastic deformation when some deformation remains after the force is removed.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Spring</h3>
  <p>A spring is stretched slightly and returns to its original length when the load is removed. This is elastic behaviour.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Bent wire</h3>
  <p>A wire is bent and stays bent after the force is removed. It has undergone plastic deformation.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Elastic limit</h3>
  <p>If a spring is stretched too far, it may not return to its original length. The force has taken it beyond its elastic limit.</p>

  <h3>Common mistake</h3>
  <p>Elasticity does not mean that a material must be soft or stretch easily. It describes recovery after the deforming force is removed.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Elasticity and Plasticity', 15),
  (410, 410, '
  <h2>Hooke''s Law</h2>
  <p>For a suitable elastic spring, extension is directly proportional to the applied force, provided the elastic limit is not exceeded. This relationship is known as Hooke''s law.</p>

  <h3>Formula</h3>
  <p>\(F = ke\)</p>
  <ul>
    <li>\(F\) = applied force in newtons (N)</li>
    <li>\(k\) = spring constant in N/m</li>
    <li>\(e\) = extension in metres (m)</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Calculate force</h3>
  <p>A spring has a spring constant of 200 N/m and an extension of 0.05 m.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Write F = ke.</li>
    <li>Substitute F = 200 × 0.05.</li>
    <li>Force = <strong>10 N</strong>.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Calculate extension</h3>
  <p>A force of 12 N acts on a spring with k = 300 N/m.</p>
  <p>Extension = F/k = 12/300 = <strong>0.04 m</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Calculate spring constant</h3>
  <p>A force of 15 N produces an extension of 0.03 m.</p>
  <p>k = F/e = 15/0.03 = <strong>500 N/m</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Common mistake</h3>
  <p>Extension is the increase in length, not the spring''s total length. Convert centimetres to metres when using a spring constant in N/m.</p>
  
<hr>
<h2>Hooke''s Law</h2>
  <p>Hooke''s law states that, for a spring or suitable elastic material, extension is directly proportional to the applied force, provided the limit of proportionality has not been exceeded.</p>
  <p><strong>F ∝ x</strong></p>
  <p>For a spring obeying Hooke''s law:</p>
  <p><strong>F = kx</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li>F = applied force in newtons (N)</li>
    <li>k = spring constant in newtons per metre (N/m)</li>
    <li>x = extension in metres (m)</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>A spring extends by 0.04 m when a force of 8 N is applied. Calculate its spring constant.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>k = F/x.</li>
    <li>k = 8/0.04.</li>
    <li>k = 200 N/m.</li>
  </ol>
  <p><strong>Answer: 200 N/m.</strong></p>
  <p>Hooke''s law does not apply indefinitely. Beyond the limit of proportionality, extension is no longer directly proportional to force.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Hooke''s Law', 15),
  (411, 411, '
  <h2>Stress and Strain</h2>
  <p>Stress and strain describe how a material responds when forces deform it.</p>

  <h3>Stress</h3>
  <p>Stress is the applied force per unit cross-sectional area.</p>
  <p>\(\text{Stress} = \frac{F}{A}\)</p>
  <p>Its SI unit is the pascal (Pa), equivalent to N/m².</p>

  <h3>Strain</h3>
  <p>For a stretched wire or rod, tensile strain is extension divided by original length.</p>
  <p>\(\text{Strain} = \frac{\text{extension}}{\text{original length}}\)</p>
  <p>Strain is a ratio and has no unit.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Stress</h3>
  <p>A force of 100 N acts on a cross-sectional area of 0.002 m².</p>
  <p>Stress = 100 ÷ 0.002 = <strong>50,000 Pa</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Strain</h3>
  <p>A wire originally 2 m long extends by 0.004 m.</p>
  <p>Strain = 0.004 ÷ 2 = <strong>0.002</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Interpret strain</h3>
  <p>A strain of 0.01 means the extension is 0.01 times the original length, or 1% of that length.</p>

  <h3>Common mistake</h3>
  <p>Stress has units, but strain does not. Always use the original length in the strain formula, not the stretched length.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Stress and Strain', 15),
  (412, 412, '
  <h2>Young''s Modulus</h2>
  <p>Young''s modulus describes the stiffness of a material under tensile or compressive loading within its elastic range. It is the ratio of stress to strain.</p>

  <h3>Formula</h3>
  <p>\(E = \frac{\text{stress}}{\text{strain}}\)</p>
  <p>Since strain has no unit, Young''s modulus has the same unit as stress: pascals (Pa).</p>

  <h3>Worked example 1</h3>
  <p>A wire experiences a stress of 4 × 10⁷ Pa and a strain of 0.002.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Write E = stress ÷ strain.</li>
    <li>Substitute E = (4 × 10⁷) ÷ 0.002.</li>
    <li>E = <strong>2 × 10¹⁰ Pa</strong>.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Compare materials</h3>
  <p>For the same tensile stress, a material with a greater Young''s modulus has a smaller strain in the linear elastic range. It is stiffer under those conditions.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Find stress</h3>
  <p>If E = 2 × 10¹¹ Pa and strain = 0.001, stress = E × strain = 2 × 10⁸ Pa.</p>

  <h3>Common mistake</h3>
  <p>Young''s modulus is not the same as force or extension. It is a material property determined from stress and strain, and the simple relationship is used in the linear elastic range.</p>
  
<hr>
<h2>Young''s Modulus</h2>
  <p>Young''s modulus measures the stiffness of a material under tensile or compressive stress, within the range where stress is directly proportional to strain.</p>
  <p><strong>Young''s modulus = stress ÷ strain</strong></p>
  <p><strong>E = σ/ε</strong></p>
  <p>Stress is measured in pascals (Pa), while strain has no unit. Therefore, Young''s modulus is measured in pascals (Pa).</p>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>A material experiences stress of 4 × 10⁶ Pa and strain of 0.002.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>E = stress ÷ strain.</li>
    <li>E = (4 × 10⁶)/0.002.</li>
    <li>E = 2 × 10⁹ Pa.</li>
  </ol>
  <p><strong>Answer: 2 × 10⁹ Pa.</strong></p>
  <p>A material with a larger Young''s modulus is generally stiffer under comparable conditions: more stress is required to produce the same strain in the proportional region.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Young''s Modulus', 15),
  (413, 413, '<h2>Elasticity</h2>
  <p>Elasticity is the ability of a material to return to its original shape and dimensions after the deforming force is removed, provided the material has not been stretched beyond its elastic limit.</p>
  <p>A deforming force can stretch, compress, bend or twist an object.</p>
  <h3>Examples</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>A spring returns towards its original length after a small stretching force is removed.</li>
    <li>A rubber band returns towards its original shape when stretched within its elastic range.</li>
    <li>A metal wire can behave elastically under sufficiently small loads.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Elasticity does not mean a material cannot change shape. It means the material can recover its original shape when the force is removed, within the elastic range.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Elasticity', 15),
  (414, 414, '<h2>Extension and Compression</h2>
  <p>Extension is the increase in length of an object when a stretching force acts on it.</p>
  <p><strong>Extension = stretched length − original length</strong></p>
  <p>Compression is a decrease in length or a reduction in size caused by a compressive force.</p>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>A spring has an original length of 12 cm. Its stretched length is 17 cm.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Extension = stretched length − original length.</li>
    <li>Extension = 17 − 12.</li>
    <li>Extension = 5 cm = 0.05 m.</li>
  </ol>
  <p><strong>Answer: 5 cm or 0.05 m.</strong></p>
  <p>When using SI units in calculations, convert centimetres to metres where required. One centimetre equals 0.01 m.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Extension and Compression', 15),
  (415, 415, '<h2>Spring Constant</h2>
  <p>The spring constant, k, measures the force needed per unit extension of a spring while it obeys Hooke''s law.</p>
  <p><strong>k = F/x</strong></p>
  <p>Its SI unit is the newton per metre (N/m).</p>
  <p>A larger spring constant means a stiffer spring: more force is required to produce the same extension.</p>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>A force of 15 N produces an extension of 0.05 m.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>k = F/x.</li>
    <li>k = 15/0.05.</li>
    <li>k = 300 N/m.</li>
  </ol>
  <p><strong>Answer: 300 N/m.</strong></p>
  <p>Compare spring constants using the same units and within the range where the springs obey Hooke''s law.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Spring Constant', 15),
  (416, 416, '<h2>Force–Extension Graphs</h2>
  <p>A force–extension graph shows how the extension of a material changes as the applied force increases.</p>
  <p>For a spring obeying Hooke''s law, a graph of force F on the vertical axis against extension x on the horizontal axis is a straight line through the origin.</p>
  <p><strong>Gradient = change in force ÷ change in extension</strong></p>
  <p>For a Hookean spring, this gradient equals the spring constant k.</p>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>Force increases from 2 N to 10 N while extension increases from 0.01 m to 0.05 m.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Change in force = 10 − 2 = 8 N.</li>
    <li>Change in extension = 0.05 − 0.01 = 0.04 m.</li>
    <li>Gradient = 8/0.04 = 200 N/m.</li>
  </ol>
  <p><strong>Answer: 200 N/m.</strong></p>
  <p>Always check which quantity is plotted on each axis before calculating the gradient.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Force–Extension Graphs', 15),
  (417, 417, '<h2>Limit of Proportionality</h2>
  <p>The limit of proportionality is the point beyond which extension is no longer directly proportional to the applied force.</p>
  <p>Before this limit, a force–extension graph for a Hookean spring is a straight line through the origin. Beyond it, the graph no longer follows that straight-line relationship.</p>
  <p>The limit of proportionality is not necessarily the same as the elastic limit.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Limit of proportionality:</strong> direct proportionality between force and extension ends.</li>
    <li><strong>Elastic limit:</strong> the material can no longer fully recover its original dimensions after the force is removed.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>A material may stop obeying Hooke''s law but still recover its original shape when unloaded.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Limit of Proportionality', 15),
  (418, 418, '<h2>Elastic and Plastic Deformation</h2>
  <p>Deformation is a change in an object''s shape or dimensions caused by a force.</p>
  <p><strong>Elastic deformation:</strong> the object returns to its original dimensions when the force is removed.</p>
  <p><strong>Plastic deformation:</strong> some change remains after the force is removed.</p>
  <h3>Examples</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>A spring stretched within its elastic range shows elastic deformation.</li>
    <li>A metal wire that remains longer after being stretched beyond its elastic limit shows plastic deformation.</li>
    <li>If a material is overloaded, it may eventually fracture.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Plastic deformation is not the same as breaking. A material can undergo permanent deformation without separating into pieces.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Elastic and Plastic Deformation', 15),
  (419, 419, '<h2>Stress</h2>
  <p>Stress is the force acting per unit cross-sectional area inside a material under load. For a uniform tensile or compressive force acting normally on a cross-section:</p>
  <p><strong>Stress = force ÷ cross-sectional area</strong></p>
  <p><strong>σ = F/A</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li>σ = stress in pascals (Pa)</li>
    <li>F = force in newtons (N)</li>
    <li>A = cross-sectional area in square metres (m²)</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Stress describes how concentrated the force is within the material. One pascal equals one newton per square metre.</p>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>A wire carries a tensile force of 200 N and has a cross-sectional area of 0.001 m².</p>
  <ol>
    <li>σ = F/A.</li>
    <li>σ = 200/0.001.</li>
    <li>σ = 200,000 Pa.</li>
  </ol>
  <p><strong>Answer: 200,000 Pa.</strong></p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Stress', 15),
  (420, 420, '<h2>Strain</h2>
  <p>Strain measures the fractional change in length of a material when a force deforms it.</p>
  <p><strong>Strain = extension ÷ original length</strong></p>
  <p><strong>ε = x/L</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li>ε = strain</li>
    <li>x = extension in metres</li>
    <li>L = original length in metres</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Strain has no unit because it is a ratio of two lengths measured in the same unit.</p>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>A wire of original length 2 m extends by 0.004 m.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Strain = extension ÷ original length.</li>
    <li>Strain = 0.004/2.</li>
    <li>Strain = 0.002.</li>
  </ol>
  <p><strong>Answer: 0.002, with no unit.</strong></p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Strain', 15),
  (421, 421, '
  <h2>Moment of a Force</h2>
  <p>The moment of a force is its turning effect about a pivot or fixed point. It depends on the size of the force and the perpendicular distance from the pivot to the force''s line of action.</p>

  <h3>Formula</h3>
  <p><strong>Moment = Force × perpendicular distance</strong></p>
  <p>\(M = Fd\)</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Moment, M, is measured in newton metres (N m).</li>
    <li>Force, F, is measured in newtons (N).</li>
    <li>Perpendicular distance, d, is measured in metres (m).</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Worked example 1</h3>
  <p>A force of 20 N acts perpendicularly at a distance of 0.5 m from a pivot.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Write M = Fd.</li>
    <li>Substitute M = 20 × 0.5.</li>
    <li>Moment = <strong>10 N m</strong>.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Worked example 2</h3>
  <p>A 30 N force acts 0.2 m from a pivot.</p>
  <p>Moment = 30 × 0.2 = <strong>6 N m</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Find the force</h3>
  <p>A moment of 12 N m is produced at a perpendicular distance of 0.4 m.</p>
  <p>Force = moment ÷ distance = 12 ÷ 0.4 = <strong>30 N</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Common mistake</h3>
  <p>Use the perpendicular distance from the pivot to the force''s line of action, not necessarily the full length of the object.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Moment of a Force', 15),
  (422, 422, '
  <h2>Clockwise and Anticlockwise Moments</h2>
  <p>A force can turn an object clockwise or anticlockwise about a pivot. The turning direction depends on where the force acts and the direction in which it pushes or pulls.</p>

  <h3>Identifying turning direction</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Clockwise:</strong> the object tends to rotate in the direction of clock hands.</li>
    <li><strong>Anticlockwise:</strong> the object tends to rotate opposite to clock hands.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Worked example 1: A seesaw</h3>
  <p>A child sitting to the left of a central pivot pushes the left end downward. The seesaw tends to turn anticlockwise.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: A door</h3>
  <p>Pushing a door away from its hinges makes it turn about the hinge line. The hinges act as the pivot axis.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Reversing the force</h3>
  <p>At the same point on a lever, reversing the direction of the force reverses the turning tendency.</p>

  <h3>Common mistake</h3>
  <p>Do not determine the turning direction from the force''s position alone. You must consider both the position relative to the pivot and the direction of the force.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Clockwise and Anticlockwise Moments', 15),
  (423, 423, '
  <h2>Principle of Moments</h2>
  <p>For a body in rotational equilibrium, the total clockwise moment about a pivot equals the total anticlockwise moment about that pivot.</p>

  <h3>Relationship</h3>
  <p><strong>Sum of clockwise moments = Sum of anticlockwise moments</strong></p>

  <h3>Worked example 1</h3>
  <p>A 20 N force acts 0.5 m to the left of a pivot. What force acting 0.25 m to the right would balance it, assuming the forces produce opposite turning directions?</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Left moment = 20 × 0.5 = 10 N m.</li>
    <li>For balance, the right moment must be 10 N m.</li>
    <li>Force × 0.25 = 10.</li>
    <li>Force = <strong>40 N</strong>.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Worked example 2</h3>
  <p>A clockwise moment is 12 N m and the anticlockwise moment is 12 N m. The net moment is zero, so there is no resultant turning effect.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3</h3>
  <p>If the clockwise moment is 15 N m and the anticlockwise moment is 9 N m, the resultant moment is 6 N m clockwise.</p>

  <h3>Common mistake</h3>
  <p>Equal moments establish rotational balance about the chosen pivot. Full mechanical equilibrium also requires the resultant force to be zero.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Principle of Moments', 15),
  (424, 424, '
  <h2>Centre of Gravity</h2>
  <p>The centre of gravity of a body is the point through which the resultant gravitational force on the body acts. In a uniform gravitational field, it coincides with the centre of mass.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: A uniform ruler</h3>
  <p>For a uniform ruler of the same material and cross-section throughout, the centre of gravity is at its midpoint when the gravitational field is uniform.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: An irregular flat object</h3>
  <p>The centre of gravity of a freely suspended irregular lamina can be located by suspending it from different points and drawing vertical lines using a plumb line. The lines intersect at the centre of gravity.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: A loaded object</h3>
  <p>Adding a load to one side of an object can shift its centre of gravity toward the added mass.</p>

  <h3>Why it matters</h3>
  <p>The position of the centre of gravity affects balance and stability. An object is less likely to topple when the vertical line through its centre of gravity remains within its base of support.</p>

  <h3>Common mistake</h3>
  <p>The centre of gravity does not have to be inside the material itself. For example, a ring''s centre of gravity can lie in its empty central space.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Centre of Gravity', 15),
  (425, 425, '
  <h2>Stability of Objects</h2>
  <p>Stability describes how well an object resists toppling when disturbed. Two important factors are the height of its centre of gravity and the width of its base of support.</p>

  <h3>Factors that increase stability</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>A lower centre of gravity.</li>
    <li>A wider base of support.</li>
    <li>A mass distribution that keeps the vertical line through the centre of gravity within the base when slightly tilted.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Worked example 1: A wide-based vehicle</h3>
  <p>A vehicle with a wider base of support is generally more resistant to toppling than an otherwise similar vehicle with a narrow base.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Lowering a load</h3>
  <p>Carrying a heavy load lower can lower the combined centre of gravity, often improving stability.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Toppling condition</h3>
  <p>When an object tilts enough that the vertical line through its centre of gravity falls outside its base of support, its weight can create a moment that makes it topple.</p>

  <h3>Common mistake</h3>
  <p>A low centre of gravity improves stability, but it does not guarantee that an object can never topple. Base width, tilt and external forces also matter.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Stability of Objects', 15),
  (426, 426, '<h2>Finding the distance from a pivot</h2>
  <p>The moment of a force is calculated using:</p>
  <p><b>Moment = force × perpendicular distance from pivot</b></p>
  <p>For a balanced object, clockwise moments equal anticlockwise moments.</p>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>A 40 N force acts 0.5 m to the left of a pivot. A 20 N force acts to the right at an unknown distance d. The beam is balanced.</p>
  <p>40 × 0.5 = 20 × d</p>
  <p>20 = 20d</p>
  <p>d = 20/20 = <b>1 m</b>.</p>
  <p><b>Check:</b> both moments must be equal for rotational equilibrium.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Calculating an Unknown Distance Using Moments', 15),
  (427, 427, '<h2>Finding a balancing force</h2>
  <p>If a beam is balanced, the total clockwise moment equals the total anticlockwise moment.</p>
  <p><b>Clockwise moment = anticlockwise moment</b></p>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>A 60 N load acts 0.4 m from a pivot. An effort acts 1.2 m from the pivot on the opposite side.</p>
  <p>60 × 0.4 = F × 1.2</p>
  <p>24 = 1.2F</p>
  <p>F = 24/1.2 = <b>20 N</b>.</p>
  <p>The effort is smaller because it acts farther from the pivot.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Finding an Unknown Force Using Moments', 15),
  (428, 428, '<h2>More than one force on each side</h2>
  <p>When several forces act on a beam, calculate each moment about the same pivot.</p>
  <p>For equilibrium:</p>
  <p><b>Total clockwise moments = total anticlockwise moments</b></p>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>Two downward forces of 10 N and 20 N act on the left of a pivot at distances 1 m and 2 m respectively. A force F acts downward on the right, 2 m from the pivot.</p>
  <p>Clockwise moment = F × 2</p>
  <p>Anticlockwise moment = (10 × 1) + (20 × 2) = 50 N m</p>
  <p>For balance: 2F = 50, so <b>F = 25 N</b>.</p>
  <p>The direction assigned as clockwise or anticlockwise depends on the force''s position and direction relative to the pivot.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Moments from Several Forces', 15),
  (429, 429, '<h2>When a beam remains balanced</h2>
  <p>For a beam to remain in static equilibrium, its forces must balance and its total turning effect must be zero.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>The resultant force must be zero.</li>
    <li>The resultant moment about a pivot must be zero.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>A beam can have zero resultant moment but still accelerate in a straight line if its forces do not balance.</p>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>A beam is at rest on a support. Its total clockwise moment is 24 N m and its total anticlockwise moment is 24 N m.</p>
  <p>Net moment = 24 − 24 = <b>0 N m</b>.</p>
  <p>If its forces also balance, it remains in static equilibrium.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Equilibrium of a Beam', 15),
  (430, 430, '<h2>Where an object''s weight acts</h2>
  <p>The centre of gravity is the point through which the resultant gravitational force on an object acts.</p>
  <p>For many ordinary objects in a uniform gravitational field, the centre of gravity is at or near the centre of mass.</p>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>Consider a uniform metre rule supported at its midpoint. Its weight acts through its centre of gravity, near the 50 cm mark.</p>
  <p>When the support is directly below this point and no other turning effects act, the rule can balance.</p>
  <p><b>Remember:</b> the centre of gravity depends on how mass is distributed, not just on an object''s outer shape.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Centre of Gravity and Balance', 15),
  (431, 431, '<h2>Using the centre of gravity to predict balance</h2>
  <p>The line of action of weight is the vertical line passing through an object''s centre of gravity.</p>
  <p>For an object resting on a level base to remain stable, the vertical line through its centre of gravity must fall within its base of support.</p>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>A rectangular block stands on a broad, level base. Its centre of gravity is above the middle of the base.</p>
  <p>The vertical line through the centre of gravity falls within the base, so the weight does not create a toppling moment about the edge.</p>
  <p>If the block tilts so that this line falls outside the base, the weight can create a turning effect that topples it.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for The Line of Action of Weight', 15),
  (432, 432, '<h2>Making objects harder to topple</h2>
  <p>Two common ways to increase an object''s stability are:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Lower its centre of gravity.</li>
    <li>Increase the width of its base of support.</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>A tall cabinet is more likely to topple than a shorter, broader cabinet with a lower centre of gravity and a wider base.</p>
  <p>Lowering the centre of gravity makes the vertical line through it less likely to move outside the base when the cabinet tilts slightly.</p>
  <p>Widening the base gives the line of action more room to move before reaching an edge.</p>
  <p><b>Application:</b> wide wheelbases and low centres of gravity help vehicles resist toppling.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Increasing the Stability of an Object', 15),
  (433, 433, '<h2>Why an object topples</h2>
  <p>An object may topple when it tilts far enough that the vertical line through its centre of gravity falls outside its base of support.</p>
  <p>At that point, the weight produces a moment about the edge of the base that tends to increase the tilt.</p>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>A box is slowly tilted about its lower edge.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>While the weight''s line of action falls within the base, the box can remain supported.</li>
    <li>When the line reaches the edge, the box is at the limiting condition for toppling.</li>
    <li>When the line falls outside the base, the weight creates a toppling moment.</li>
  </ul>
  <p><b>Key distinction:</b> toppling is a rotational effect; sliding is motion along the surface and involves different conditions.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Toppling and the Turning Effect of Weight', 15),
  (434, 434, '<h2>Turning effects in tools</h2>
  <p>Tools such as spanners, door handles and bottle openers use turning effects to make tasks easier.</p>
  <p>The moment of a force is:</p>
  <p><b>Moment = force × perpendicular distance from pivot</b></p>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>A spanner applies a turning force of 40 N at a perpendicular distance of 0.25 m from a nut.</p>
  <p>Moment = 40 × 0.25 = <b>10 N m</b>.</p>
  <p>A longer spanner can produce a larger moment for the same applied force, provided the force acts in the same effective direction.</p>
  <p><b>Important:</b> the pivot is the point or axis about which the object turns.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Applying Moments to Everyday Tools', 15),
  (435, 435, '
  <h2>Work Done by a Force</h2>
  <p>In physics, work is done when a force causes displacement. For a constant force acting in the same direction as the displacement, work done is the force multiplied by the distance moved.</p>

  <h3>Formula</h3>
  <p>\(W = Fs\)</p>
  <ul>
    <li>W = work done, measured in joules (J)</li>
    <li>F = force in newtons (N)</li>
    <li>s = displacement in metres (m), along the force''s direction</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Worked example 1</h3>
  <p>A force of 10 N moves a box 3 m in the direction of the force.</p>
  <p>Work done = 10 × 3 = <strong>30 J</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2</h3>
  <p>A force of 50 N moves an object 0.2 m in the force''s direction.</p>
  <p>Work done = 50 × 0.2 = <strong>10 J</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: No displacement</h3>
  <p>A person holds a stationary bag. Although the person applies an upward force, the bag has no displacement. The mechanical work done by that force on the stationary bag is zero.</p>

  <h3>Common mistake</h3>
  <p>In physics, feeling tired does not by itself mean that mechanical work has been done on an object. There must be displacement in the direction of the force, or a force component along the displacement.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Work Done by a Force', 15),
  (436, 436, '
  <h2>Gravitational Potential Energy</h2>
  <p>Gravitational potential energy is energy associated with an object''s position in a gravitational field. Near Earth''s surface, the change in gravitational potential energy can be calculated using mass, gravitational field strength and vertical height change.</p>

  <h3>Formula</h3>
  <p>\(\Delta E_p = mg\Delta h\)</p>
  <ul>
    <li>m = mass in kilograms (kg)</li>
    <li>g = gravitational field strength in N/kg</li>
    <li>Δh = vertical height change in metres (m)</li>
    <li>ΔEp = change in gravitational potential energy in joules (J)</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Worked example 1</h3>
  <p>A 2 kg object is lifted vertically by 3 m. Take g = 10 N/kg.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Write ΔEp = mgΔh.</li>
    <li>Substitute ΔEp = 2 × 10 × 3.</li>
    <li>Change in potential energy = <strong>60 J</strong>.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Worked example 2</h3>
  <p>A 5 kg object is raised by 2 m where g = 9.8 N/kg.</p>
  <p>ΔEp = 5 × 9.8 × 2 = <strong>98 J</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Find height change</h3>
  <p>A 4 kg object gains 80 J of gravitational potential energy. If g = 10 N/kg, Δh = 80 ÷ (4 × 10) = <strong>2 m</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Common mistake</h3>
  <p>Use vertical height change, not the distance travelled along a sloping path. The formula gives the change in gravitational potential energy near Earth''s surface.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Gravitational Potential Energy', 15),
  (437, 437, '<h2>Kinetic Energy</h2>
  <p>Kinetic energy is the energy an object possesses because it is moving.</p>
  <p><strong>Formula:</strong> KE = 1/2 × m × v²</p>
  <ul>
    <li>KE = kinetic energy in joules (J)</li>
    <li>m = mass in kilograms (kg)</li>
    <li>v = speed in metres per second (m/s)</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>A 4 kg object moves at 3 m/s. Calculate its kinetic energy.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Write the formula: KE = 1/2 × m × v².</li>
    <li>Substitute: KE = 1/2 × 4 × 3².</li>
    <li>Square the speed: 3² = 9.</li>
    <li>Calculate: KE = 2 × 9 = 18 J.</li>
  </ol>
  <p><strong>Answer: 18 J.</strong></p>
  <h3>Important relationship</h3>
  <p>At constant mass, doubling speed makes kinetic energy four times greater because speed is squared. Doubling mass at constant speed doubles kinetic energy.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Kinetic Energy', 15),
  (438, 438, '<h2>Conservation of Energy</h2>
  <p>The principle of conservation of energy states that energy cannot be created or destroyed. It can only be transferred or transformed from one form to another.</p>
  <h3>Example: a falling object</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>At a height, the object has gravitational potential energy.</li>
    <li>As it falls, gravitational potential energy decreases.</li>
    <li>Its kinetic energy increases as its speed increases.</li>
    <li>Ignoring air resistance, the decrease in potential energy equals the increase in kinetic energy.</li>
  </ol>
  <h3>Energy in real systems</h3>
  <p>When friction or air resistance acts, some mechanical energy is transferred to the surroundings, often as thermal energy and sound. The total energy is still conserved.</p>
  <p><strong>Key idea:</strong> Energy is transferred or transformed, not destroyed.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Conservation of Energy', 15),
  (439, 439, '<h2>Power</h2>
  <p>Power is the rate at which work is done or energy is transferred.</p>
  <p><strong>Formula:</strong> P = W/t, where W is work done in joules and t is time in seconds.</p>
  <p>Power can also be calculated using P = E/t when E is the energy transferred.</p>
  <p><strong>Unit:</strong> watt (W). One watt equals one joule per second.</p>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>A motor does 600 J of work in 20 s.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Write P = W/t.</li>
    <li>Substitute: P = 600/20.</li>
    <li>Calculate: P = 30 W.</li>
  </ol>
  <p><strong>Answer: 30 W.</strong></p>
  <p>Doing the same amount of work in less time requires greater power.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Power', 15),
  (440, 440, '<h2>Efficiency</h2>
  <p>Efficiency measures the fraction of the input energy or power that becomes useful output.</p>
  <p><strong>Efficiency (%) = (useful output energy ÷ total input energy) × 100%</strong></p>
  <p>The same relationship applies to useful output power divided by input power.</p>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>A device receives 500 J and provides 350 J of useful energy.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Efficiency = (useful output ÷ input) × 100%.</li>
    <li>Efficiency = (350 ÷ 500) × 100%.</li>
    <li>Efficiency = 70%.</li>
  </ol>
  <p><strong>Answer: 70%.</strong></p>
  <p>For an ordinary energy-converting device, efficiency cannot exceed 100%. Some input energy is often transferred to the surroundings in less useful forms.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Efficiency', 15),
  (441, 441, '
  <h2>Meaning of Work</h2>

  <h3>Core idea</h3>
  <p>
    In physics, work is done when a force causes displacement.
    When a constant force acts in the direction of displacement:
  </p>
  <p><b>Work done = force × displacement</b></p>
  <p><b>W = Fd</b></p>
  <p>
    Work is measured in joules (J), force in newtons (N), and
    displacement in metres (m).
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Pushing a box</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A force of 10 N moves a box 3 m in the
  direction of the force. Calculate the work done.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> W = Fd.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> W = 10 × 3.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> 30 J.</p>
  <p><b>Check:</b> N × m = J.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Pulling a trolley</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A student pulls a trolley with a constant
  force of 15 N through 4 m in the direction of the force. Find
  the work done by the pulling force.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify F = 15 N and d = 4 m.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> W = Fd = 15 × 4.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> 60 J.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: A smaller force</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A force of 6 N moves an object 5 m in the
  direction of the force. Calculate the work done.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> W = Fd.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> W = 6 × 5.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> 30 J.</p>

  <h3>Remember</h3>
  <p>Work in physics is not simply the effort someone feels they
  are making. The force must cause displacement in the relevant
  direction.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Meaning of Work', 15),
  (442, 442, '
  <h2>Calculating Force or Displacement from Work</h2>

  <h3>Core idea</h3>
  <p>
    When a constant force acts in the direction of displacement:
  </p>
  <p><b>W = Fd</b></p>
  <p>Rearrange the formula to find either unknown:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Force:</b> F = W/d</li>
    <li><b>Displacement:</b> d = W/F</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Finding force</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A force does 80 J of work while moving an
  object 4 m in the force''s direction. Find the force.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> W = Fd, so F = W/d.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> F = 80/4.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> 20 N.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Finding displacement</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A 12 N force does 72 J of work. Find the
  displacement in the direction of the force.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> d = W/F.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> d = 72/12.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> 6 m.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: A heavier load</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A constant force does 150 J of work while
  moving an object 5 m in its direction. Calculate the force.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> F = W/d.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> F = 150/5.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> 30 N.</p>

  <h3>Remember</h3>
  <p>Choose the rearranged formula that matches the unknown.
  Convert displacement to metres before substituting if necessary.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Calculating Force or Displacement from Work', 15),
  (443, 443, '
  <h2>When Work Done Is Zero</h2>

  <h3>Core idea</h3>
  <p>
    For a constant force, work depends on the displacement in the
    direction of the force. If there is no displacement, that force
    does no work on the object.
  </p>
  <p>
    If the force is perpendicular to the displacement, the work
    done by that force is also zero.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Pushing a wall</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A student pushes a wall with a force of 100 N,
  but the wall does not move. How much work does the push do on the wall?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> The wall has zero displacement.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> W = Fd = 100 × 0.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> 0 J of work is done on the wall by the push.</p>
  <p><b>Check:</b> The student may use energy physiologically, but
  the force does no mechanical work on the wall because it does not move.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Carrying a bag horizontally</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A student carries a bag horizontally at
  constant height. The upward supporting force is 40 N. What work
  does this upward force do during a horizontal displacement of 5 m?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> The support force acts upward.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> The displacement is horizontal, so it is
  perpendicular to the support force.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> The upward supporting force does 0 J of work.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Lifting a book</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A book is lifted vertically by 2 m. Its
  weight is 10 N downward. Find the work done by gravity.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Gravity acts downward, opposite to the upward displacement.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Work done by gravity = − weight × displacement.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> W = −10 × 2.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> −20 J.</p>
  <p><b>Meaning:</b> The negative sign shows that gravity opposes
  the displacement.</p>

  <h3>Remember</h3>
  <p>Distinguish between work done by an individual force and the
  total work done by all forces. Different forces can do different
  amounts of work on the same object.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for When Work Done Is Zero', 15),
  (444, 444, '
  <h2>Purpose and Parts of Simple Machines</h2>

  <h3>Core idea</h3>
  <p>
    A machine helps us perform a task by changing the size or
    direction of a force, or by changing the distance over which
    the force acts.
  </p>
  <p>Common machine terms:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Load:</b> the resistance or object being moved.</li>
    <li><b>Effort:</b> the force applied to operate the machine.</li>
    <li><b>Fulcrum:</b> the pivot about which a lever turns.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    A machine does not create energy. In real machines, some energy
    is transferred to the surroundings, often through friction.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: A lever lifting a stone</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A lever is used to lift a heavy stone. Identify
  the load and the effort.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> The stone is the object being moved.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> The force applied by the person operates the lever.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> The stone is the load; the person''s applied
  force is the effort.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: A seesaw</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> In a seesaw, what is the fulcrum?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> A lever rotates around a pivot.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> The seesaw turns around its central support.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> The central support is the fulcrum.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: A ramp</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A worker uses a ramp to push a load into a
  truck. How does the ramp help?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> The load gains height over a longer distance.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> For an ideal ramp, the required effort can be
  smaller than lifting the load vertically, but it acts over a
  longer distance.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> The ramp reduces the effort needed by increasing
  the distance over which it is applied, ideally without changing
  the total work required.</p>

  <h3>Remember</h3>
  <p>Machines make tasks easier by changing force, direction or
  distance. They do not produce energy from nothing.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Purpose and Parts of Simple Machines', 15),
  (445, 445, '
  <h2>Mechanical Advantage</h2>

  <h3>Core idea</h3>
  <p>
    Mechanical advantage (M.A.) compares the load force produced
    by a machine with the effort force applied to it.
  </p>
  <p><b>M.A. = Load / Effort</b></p>
  <p>
    Load and effort must be expressed in the same force units.
    Mechanical advantage is a ratio and has no unit.
  </p>
  <p>
    If M.A. is greater than 1, the load force is greater than the
    effort force. If M.A. is less than 1, the effort force is
    greater than the load force.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Finding mechanical advantage</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A machine lifts a load of 200 N using an
  effort of 50 N. Calculate its mechanical advantage.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> M.A. = Load/Effort.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> M.A. = 200/50.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> M.A. = 4.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Finding effort</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A machine has a mechanical advantage of 5
  and lifts a load of 300 N. Find the effort.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> M.A. = Load/Effort.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Effort = Load/M.A.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Effort = 300/5.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> 60 N.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Finding the load</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A machine has a mechanical advantage of 3.
  An effort of 40 N is applied. Calculate the load.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Load = M.A. × Effort.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Load = 3 × 40.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> 120 N.</p>

  <h3>Remember</h3>
  <p>Mechanical advantage tells you how the load force compares
  with the applied effort. It does not by itself tell you how
  efficient the machine is.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Mechanical Advantage', 15),
  (446, 446, '
  <h2>Meaning and Unit of Power</h2>

  <h3>Core idea</h3>
  <p>
    Power is the rate at which work is done or energy is transferred.
    It tells us how quickly work is done, not simply how much work
    is done.
  </p>
  <p><b>Power = Work done / Time taken</b></p>
  <p><b>P = W/t</b></p>
  <p>
    Power is measured in watts (W). One watt is equal to one joule
    of work done per second.
  </p>
  <p><b>1 W = 1 J/s</b></p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Work done in one second</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A machine does 100 J of work in 1 s.
  What is its average power?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> P = W/t.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> P = 100/1.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> 100 W.</p>
  <p><b>Check:</b> 100 J/s = 100 W.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: A longer time</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A motor transfers 600 J of energy in 3 s.
  Calculate its average power.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> P = W/t.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> P = 600/3.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> 200 W.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Same work, different time</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> Two machines each do 400 J of work.
  Machine A takes 2 s while machine B takes 4 s. Calculate
  the average power of each machine.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> For A, P = 400/2 = 200 W.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> For B, P = 400/4 = 100 W.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> A has average power 200 W; B has average
  power 100 W.</p>
  <p><b>Conclusion:</b> For the same work, the machine that takes
  less time has greater average power.</p>

  <h3>Remember</h3>
  <p>Power measures how quickly work is done. The SI unit is
  the watt, equivalent to one joule per second.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Meaning and Unit of Power', 15),
  (447, 447, '
  <h2>Calculating Power from Work and Time</h2>

  <h3>Core idea</h3>
  <p>
    When the work done and the time taken are known, calculate
    average power using:
  </p>
  <p><b>P = W/t</b></p>
  <p>
    Use joules for work and seconds for time to obtain power
    in watts.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Lifting a load</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A worker does 1,200 J of work lifting a load
  in 6 s. Find the average power.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify W = 1,200 J and t = 6 s.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> P = W/t = 1,200/6.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> 200 W.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: A pump</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A pump does 9,000 J of work in 30 s.
  Calculate its average power.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> P = W/t.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> P = 9,000/30.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> 300 W.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Converting minutes to seconds</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A machine does 12,000 J of work in 2 minutes.
  Calculate its average power in watts.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Convert time to seconds: 2 × 60 = 120 s.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> P = W/t = 12,000/120.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> 100 W.</p>
  <p><b>Check:</b> Always convert minutes to seconds when using
  joules and watts.</p>

  <h3>Remember</h3>
  <p>Power is the rate of work. Convert all quantities to
  compatible SI units before calculating.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Calculating Power from Work and Time', 15),
  (448, 448, '
  <h2>Calculating Work or Time from Power</h2>

  <h3>Core idea</h3>
  <p>
    The power formula can be rearranged to calculate work or time:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Power:</b> P = W/t</li>
    <li><b>Work:</b> W = Pt</li>
    <li><b>Time:</b> t = W/P</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Remember that 1 W = 1 J/s, so power multiplied by time
    gives work in joules.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Finding work</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A motor operates at 250 W for 8 s.
  How much work does it do at that average power?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> W = Pt.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> W = 250 × 8.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> 2,000 J.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Finding time</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A machine does 3,600 J of work at an
  average power of 600 W. Find the time taken.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> t = W/P.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> t = 3,600/600.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> 6 s.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: A stronger motor</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A motor operating at 400 W transfers energy
  for 15 s. How much work is done at that power?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> W = Pt.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> W = 400 × 15.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> 6,000 J.</p>

  <h3>Remember</h3>
  <p>Choose the version of the formula that isolates the unknown.
  Keep work in joules, power in watts and time in seconds.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Calculating Work or Time from Power', 15),
  (449, 449, '
  <h2>Comparing Power</h2>

  <h3>Core idea</h3>
  <p>
    Power compares how quickly work is done or energy is transferred.
    When two machines do the same work, the one taking less time
    has greater average power.
  </p>
  <p>
    When two machines operate for the same time, the one doing
    more work has greater average power.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Same work, different times</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> Machine A does 600 J in 3 s. Machine B does
  the same 600 J in 6 s. Compare their average powers.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> P(A) = 600/3 = 200 W.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> P(B) = 600/6 = 100 W.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Machine A has twice the average power of B.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Same time, different work</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> Machine A does 900 J in 3 s. Machine B does
  1,500 J in the same 3 s. Which has greater average power?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> P(A) = 900/3 = 300 W.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> P(B) = 1,500/3 = 500 W.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Machine B has greater average power.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Comparing workers</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> Worker A does 2,400 J of work in 12 s.
  Worker B does 1,800 J in 6 s. Compare their average powers.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> P(A) = 2,400/12 = 200 W.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> P(B) = 1,800/6 = 300 W.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Worker B has the greater average power, even
  though worker A does more total work.</p>

  <h3>Remember</h3>
  <p>Do not compare total work alone. Calculate work per unit time
  when the question asks about power.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Comparing Power', 15),
  (450, 450, '
  <h2>Power, Force and Speed</h2>

  <h3>Core idea</h3>
  <p>
    When a force acts in the direction of an object''s motion,
    the instantaneous power delivered by that force is:
  </p>
  <p><b>P = Fv</b></p>
  <p>
    Here, P is power in watts, F is force in newtons and v is
    speed in metres per second.
  </p>
  <p>
    This relation applies to the component of force in the direction
    of motion. If force and velocity are not aligned, only the
    force component along the motion contributes to the power.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: A moving vehicle</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A vehicle experiences a driving force of
  500 N while moving at 10 m/s. Calculate the power delivered
  by that force.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> P = Fv.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> P = 500 × 10.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> 5,000 W, or 5 kW.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: A faster vehicle</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A motor supplies a force of 200 N in the
  direction of motion of a vehicle travelling at 15 m/s. Find
  the power delivered by the force.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> P = Fv.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> P = 200 × 15.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> 3,000 W, or 3 kW.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Finding force</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A motor delivers 6,000 W of power to an
  object moving at 12 m/s. Assuming the force is in the direction
  of motion, calculate the force.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> P = Fv, so F = P/v.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> F = 6,000/12.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> 500 N.</p>

  <h3>Remember</h3>
  <p>Use P = Fv when the force and speed are known. Use P = W/t
  when work and time are known. Make sure the force component
  along the motion is used.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Power, Force and Speed', 15),
  (451, 451, '
  <h2>Meaning of Kinetic Energy</h2>

  <h3>1. What is kinetic energy?</h3>
  <p>
    <b>Kinetic energy</b> is the energy an object possesses because it is moving.
    A moving car, a rolling ball and a running student all have kinetic energy.
  </p>

  <p>
    An object at rest has zero kinetic energy because its speed is zero.
    The faster a moving object travels, the greater its kinetic energy,
    provided its mass remains constant.
  </p>

  <h3>2. The formula</h3>
  <p>
    <b>KE = ½mv²</b>
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>KE = kinetic energy, measured in joules (J)</li>
    <li>m = mass, measured in kilograms (kg)</li>
    <li>v = speed, measured in metres per second (m/s)</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    The speed is squared before multiplying by the mass and one-half.
    This means that doubling speed does not merely double kinetic energy.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: A moving ball</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> Explain why a moving ball possesses kinetic energy.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Kinetic energy is energy due to motion.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> The ball is moving.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> The ball possesses kinetic energy because it is in motion.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: An object at rest</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> Find the kinetic energy of a 4 kg object at rest.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Write the formula: KE = ½mv².</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Substitute m = 4 kg and v = 0 m/s.</p>
  <p>KE = ½ × 4 × 0²</p>
  <p>KE = 0 J</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> The kinetic energy is 0 J.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Identifying the quantities</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A trolley has a mass of 3 kg and moves at 2 m/s. Identify the values needed to calculate its kinetic energy.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Mass, m = 3 kg.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Speed, v = 2 m/s.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> The required formula is KE = ½mv².</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Use m = 3 kg and v = 2 m/s.</p>

  <h3>Remember</h3>
  <p>
    Kinetic energy depends on motion. Always identify the mass and speed
    before using KE = ½mv².
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Meaning of Kinetic Energy', 15),
  (452, 452, '
  <h2>Calculating Kinetic Energy</h2>

  <h3>Formula</h3>
  <p><b>KE = ½mv²</b></p>

  <p>
    To calculate kinetic energy:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>Write down the mass and speed.</li>
    <li>Square the speed.</li>
    <li>Multiply the squared speed by the mass.</li>
    <li>Multiply the result by one-half.</li>
    <li>Give the answer in joules.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Worked example 1</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> Calculate the kinetic energy of a 2 kg object moving at 3 m/s.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> m = 2 kg, v = 3 m/s.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> KE = ½mv².</p>
  <p>KE = ½ × 2 × 3²</p>
  <p>KE = 1 × 9</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> KE = 9 J.</p>
  <p><b>Check:</b> The speed was squared before multiplication.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A 5 kg trolley moves at 4 m/s. Find its kinetic energy.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> m = 5 kg, v = 4 m/s.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> KE = ½ × 5 × 4².</p>
  <p>KE = 2.5 × 16</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> KE = 40 J.</p>
  <p><b>Check:</b> 4² = 16, not 8.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> Calculate the kinetic energy of a 1,200 kg car moving at 10 m/s.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> m = 1,200 kg, v = 10 m/s.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> KE = ½ × 1,200 × 10².</p>
  <p>KE = 600 × 100</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> KE = 60,000 J.</p>
  <p><b>Check:</b> The answer is in joules, not newtons.</p>

  <h3>Common mistake</h3>
  <p>
    Do not calculate ½ × mass × speed and stop there.
    The formula requires speed squared: v × v.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Calculating Kinetic Energy', 15),
  (453, 453, '
  <h2>Mass and Speed in Kinetic Energy</h2>

  <h3>1. Effect of mass</h3>
  <p>
    From KE = ½mv², kinetic energy is directly proportional to mass
    when speed is constant.
  </p>
  <p>
    If mass doubles while speed stays the same, kinetic energy doubles.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Effect of speed</h3>
  <p>
    Kinetic energy is proportional to the square of speed.
    This makes speed especially important.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Double the speed: kinetic energy becomes 4 times greater.</li>
    <li>Triple the speed: kinetic energy becomes 9 times greater.</li>
    <li>Halve the speed: kinetic energy becomes one-quarter.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Doubling mass</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A 2 kg object moving at 4 m/s has a certain kinetic energy. What happens to its kinetic energy if its mass doubles and its speed remains unchanged?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Original KE = ½ × 2 × 4² = 16 J.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> The new mass is 4 kg.</p>
  <p>New KE = ½ × 4 × 4² = 32 J.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Kinetic energy doubles from 16 J to 32 J.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Doubling speed</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> An object''s speed increases from 3 m/s to 6 m/s while its mass remains constant. By what factor does its kinetic energy change?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Speed factor = 6 ÷ 3 = 2.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Square the factor: 2² = 4.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Its kinetic energy becomes four times greater.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Tripling speed</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> The speed of a moving object triples. Its mass does not change. Find the factor by which its kinetic energy changes.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Speed factor = 3.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Kinetic energy factor = 3².</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Kinetic energy becomes 9 times greater.</p>

  <h3>Remember</h3>
  <p>
    When comparing two situations, keep track of what changes.
    Mass affects kinetic energy directly; speed affects it through its square.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Mass and Speed in Kinetic Energy', 15),
  (454, 454, '
  <h2>Meaning of Gravitational Potential Energy</h2>

  <h3>1. What is gravitational potential energy?</h3>
  <p>
    <b>Gravitational potential energy (GPE)</b> is energy an object has
    because of its position or height in a gravitational field.
  </p>
  <p>
    For example, a book held above the floor has gravitational potential
    energy relative to the floor. When it falls, this energy can be
    transferred into kinetic energy.
  </p>

  <h3>2. The formula near Earth''s surface</h3>
  <p><b>GPE = mgh</b></p>
  <ul>
    <li>GPE = gravitational potential energy in joules (J)</li>
    <li>m = mass in kilograms (kg)</li>
    <li>g = gravitational field strength in N/kg</li>
    <li>h = vertical height above the chosen reference level in metres (m)</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    In many school questions, use g = 10 N/kg when the question instructs
    you to do so. If a question gives another value, use the supplied value.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Identifying GPE</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> Why does a book resting on a high shelf have gravitational potential energy relative to the floor?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> GPE is associated with position in a gravitational field.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> The shelf is above the floor.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> The book has GPE because it is elevated above the chosen reference level.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Identifying the quantities</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A 3 kg object is held 2 m above the ground where g = 10 N/kg. Identify the quantities needed to calculate its GPE.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Mass, m = 3 kg.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Gravitational field strength, g = 10 N/kg.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Height, h = 2 m.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Use GPE = mgh with these three values.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Comparing heights</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> Two identical objects are held at different heights above the same floor. Which has more GPE?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Both objects have the same mass.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> The higher object has the greater height h.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> The higher object has more GPE relative to the floor.</p>

  <h3>Remember</h3>
  <p>
    Gravitational potential energy depends on mass, gravitational field
    strength and vertical height relative to a reference level.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Meaning of Gravitational Potential Energy', 15),
  (455, 455, '
  <h2>Calculating Gravitational Potential Energy</h2>

  <h3>Formula</h3>
  <p><b>GPE = mgh</b></p>

  <p>
    To calculate GPE:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>Identify the mass, m.</li>
    <li>Identify gravitational field strength, g.</li>
    <li>Identify the vertical height, h.</li>
    <li>Multiply the three values.</li>
    <li>Give the answer in joules.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Worked example 1</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> Calculate the GPE of a 2 kg object held 5 m above the ground. Take g = 10 N/kg.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> m = 2 kg, g = 10 N/kg, h = 5 m.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> GPE = mgh.</p>
  <p>GPE = 2 × 10 × 5</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> GPE = 100 J.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A 4 kg bag is lifted to a height of 3 m. Calculate the gain in GPE using g = 10 N/kg.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> m = 4 kg, g = 10 N/kg, h = 3 m.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Gain in GPE = mgh.</p>
  <p>Gain in GPE = 4 × 10 × 3</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> The gain in GPE is 120 J.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A 6 kg object is raised vertically by 2 m where g = 9.8 N/kg. Calculate its gain in GPE.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> m = 6 kg, g = 9.8 N/kg, h = 2 m.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Gain in GPE = mgh.</p>
  <p>Gain in GPE = 6 × 9.8 × 2</p>
  <p>Gain in GPE = 117.6 J</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> 117.6 J.</p>

  <h3>Common mistakes</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Do not use speed in the GPE formula.</li>
    <li>Use mass in kilograms, not grams.</li>
    <li>Use vertical height, not the distance travelled along a slope.</li>
    <li>Use the value of g given in the question.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Calculating Gravitational Potential Energy', 15),
  (456, 456, '
  <h2>Principle of Conservation of Energy</h2>

  <h3>1. The principle</h3>
  <p>
    The <b>principle of conservation of energy</b> states that energy
    cannot be created or destroyed. It can only be transferred from
    one object or place to another, or transformed from one form to another.
  </p>

  <p>
    In a closed system, the total energy remains constant when all forms
    of energy are included.
  </p>

  <h3>2. What happens in real situations?</h3>
  <p>
    When a moving object slows down because of friction, its kinetic energy
    decreases. Energy is transferred mainly to the thermal energy of the
    object and its surroundings, with some possibly transferred as sound.
    The energy has not disappeared.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: A falling stone</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A stone falls from a height. Describe the energy change, ignoring air resistance.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Before falling, the stone has gravitational potential energy.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> As it falls, its height decreases and its speed increases.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Gravitational potential energy is transformed into kinetic energy. The total mechanical energy remains constant if air resistance is ignored.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: A braking bicycle</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A bicycle slows down when its brakes are applied. Has its kinetic energy been destroyed?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> The bicycle''s speed decreases.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Its kinetic energy decreases.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Friction in the brakes and between surfaces transfers energy to thermal energy and the surroundings.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> No. The energy is transferred and transformed; it is not destroyed.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: An electric lamp</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A lamp receives 100 J of electrical energy. The energy is eventually transferred as 15 J of light and 85 J of thermal energy. Verify conservation of energy.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Total energy supplied = 100 J.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Total energy transferred out = 15 J + 85 J.</p>
  <p>Total = 100 J.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> The energy is conserved because 100 J supplied equals 100 J transferred out.</p>

  <h3>Remember</h3>
  <p>
    Always account for all energy forms. A decrease in one form does not
    mean that energy has been destroyed.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Principle of Conservation of Energy', 15),
  (457, 457, '
  <h2>Energy Transfer and Transformation</h2>

  <h3>1. Energy transfer</h3>
  <p>
    An <b>energy transfer</b> occurs when energy moves from one object
    or place to another. The energy may remain in the same form during
    the transfer.
  </p>
  <p>
    Example: thermal energy moves from hot water to a cooler metal spoon.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Energy transformation</h3>
  <p>
    An <b>energy transformation</b> occurs when energy changes from one
    form to another.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Electrical energy to light and thermal energy in a lamp.</li>
    <li>Chemical energy to kinetic energy in a moving person.</li>
    <li>Gravitational potential energy to kinetic energy in a falling object.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    A single process can involve both transfer and transformation.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: A lamp</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> State the main energy transformation in an electric lamp.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> The lamp receives electrical energy.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> It produces light and thermal energy.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Electrical energy is transformed into light and thermal energy.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: A hot spoon</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A metal spoon is placed in hot tea. Thermal energy moves from the tea into the spoon. Is this a transfer or transformation?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> The energy moves from the tea to the spoon.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> The energy remains thermal energy during this transfer.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> This is an energy transfer.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: A falling object</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> Identify the main energy transformation as a stone falls, ignoring air resistance.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> The stone initially has gravitational potential energy.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Its speed increases as it falls.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Gravitational potential energy is transformed into kinetic energy.</p>

  <h3>Remember</h3>
  <p>
    Ask two separate questions: Has energy moved between objects or places?
    Has energy changed form? Some processes involve both.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Energy Transfer and Transformation', 15),
  (458, 458, '
  <h2>Energy Changes During Falling</h2>

  <h3>1. Before the object falls</h3>
  <p>
    An object held above the ground has gravitational potential energy
    relative to the ground. If it is at rest, its kinetic energy is zero.
  </p>

  <h3>2. As the object falls</h3>
  <p>
    Its height decreases, so its gravitational potential energy decreases.
    Its speed increases, so its kinetic energy increases.
  </p>

  <p>
    If air resistance is ignored, the decrease in GPE equals the increase
    in KE. If air resistance is significant, some mechanical energy is
    transferred to the surroundings, mainly as thermal energy.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Identifying the energy change</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A ball is released from rest above the ground. Describe its energy changes as it falls, ignoring air resistance.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Initially, the ball has GPE and zero KE.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> As it falls, GPE decreases and KE increases.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> GPE is transformed into KE. The total mechanical energy remains constant.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Comparing the top and bottom</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A ball falls from a height. Compare its GPE and KE at the top and just before reaching the ground. Ignore air resistance.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> At the top, the ball has maximum GPE relative to the ground and zero KE if released from rest.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Just before reaching the ground, its GPE relative to the ground is approximately zero and its KE is greatest.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> GPE decreases while KE increases. Their total remains constant.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Effect of air resistance</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A paper sheet falls through air. Why might its kinetic energy not equal the GPE it has lost?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Air resistance acts against the sheet''s motion.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Some energy is transferred to the surroundings through the interaction with air.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Some of the lost GPE is transferred to the surroundings, so not all of it becomes the sheet''s kinetic energy.</p>

  <h3>Remember</h3>
  <p>
    For a falling object, always state whether air resistance is ignored.
    This determines whether the lost GPE equals the gain in KE.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Energy Changes During Falling', 15),
  (459, 459, '
  <h2>Calculating Speed from Lost Gravitational Potential Energy</h2>

  <h3>1. The energy relationship</h3>
  <p>
    When an object falls from rest and air resistance is ignored,
    its loss of gravitational potential energy equals its gain in
    kinetic energy.
  </p>

  <p><b>Loss in GPE = Gain in KE</b></p>
  <p><b>mgh = ½mv²</b></p>

  <p>
    Cancelling the common mass gives:
  </p>
  <p><b>v² = 2gh</b></p>
  <p><b>v = √(2gh)</b></p>

  <p>
    This simplified formula applies when the object starts from rest,
    falls vertically through height h, and air resistance is ignored.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Falling through 5 m</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> An object falls from rest through 5 m. Calculate its speed just before reaching the ground. Take g = 10 m/s² and ignore air resistance.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> v = √(2gh).</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> v = √(2 × 10 × 5).</p>
  <p>v = √100</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> v = 10 m/s.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Falling through 20 m</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A stone falls from rest through 20 m. Calculate its speed just before impact, taking g = 10 m/s².</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> v = √(2gh).</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> v = √(2 × 10 × 20).</p>
  <p>v = √400</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> v = 20 m/s.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: A smaller drop</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A ball falls from rest through 1.8 m. Calculate its speed just before reaching the ground. Take g = 10 m/s².</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> v = √(2gh).</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> v = √(2 × 10 × 1.8).</p>
  <p>v = √36</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> v = 6 m/s.</p>

  <h3>Common mistakes</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Do not forget the square root when finding v.</li>
    <li>Use height lost, not total path length along a curved route.</li>
    <li>Do not use this simplified formula if the stated assumptions do not apply.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Calculating Speed from Lost Gravitational Potential Energy', 15),
  (460, 460, '
  <h2>Useful and Wasted Energy</h2>

  <h3>1. Useful energy</h3>
  <p>
    <b>Useful energy</b> is the energy transferred in the form needed
    to achieve a device''s intended purpose.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Wasted energy</h3>
  <p>
    <b>Wasted energy</b> is energy transferred into forms or places that
    are not useful for the intended purpose. It is often transferred
    to the surroundings as thermal energy or sound.
  </p>

  <p>
    Wasted energy has not been destroyed. It is usually less useful
    because it spreads into the surroundings and becomes harder to use.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Energy efficiency</h3>
  <p>
    Efficiency compares the useful energy output with the total energy input.
  </p>
  <p><b>Efficiency = (Useful energy output ÷ Total energy input) × 100%</b></p>

  <p>
    When all energy outputs are accounted for:
    total energy input = useful energy output + wasted energy output.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: An electric lamp</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> An electric lamp receives 100 J of electrical energy. It produces 20 J of useful light energy. Calculate its efficiency.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Useful output = 20 J; total input = 100 J.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Efficiency = (20 ÷ 100) × 100%.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Efficiency = 20%.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Finding wasted energy</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A motor receives 500 J of energy and transfers 350 J usefully into mechanical energy. Calculate the wasted energy if these are the only two output categories.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Wasted energy = total input − useful output.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Wasted energy = 500 J − 350 J.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Wasted energy = 150 J.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Comparing efficiencies</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> Device A receives 200 J and produces 100 J of useful energy. Device B receives 200 J and produces 150 J of useful energy. Calculate both efficiencies and identify the more efficient device.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Efficiency of A = (100 ÷ 200) × 100% = 50%.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Efficiency of B = (150 ÷ 200) × 100% = 75%.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Device B is more efficient because its efficiency is 75%, compared with 50% for A.</p>

  <h3>Remember</h3>
  <p>
    Efficiency is a percentage of the energy input. For ordinary devices,
    it cannot exceed 100% when all energy inputs and outputs are correctly
    accounted for.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Useful and Wasted Energy', 15),
  (461, 461, '<h2>Simple Machines</h2>
  <p>A simple machine makes a task easier by changing the size or direction of a force, or the distance over which the force acts.</p>
  <p>Common simple machines include levers, pulleys, inclined planes, and wheel-and-axle systems.</p>
  <h3>Examples</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Lever:</strong> a crowbar can help lift a heavy load.</li>
    <li><strong>Pulley:</strong> a fixed pulley can change the direction in which a person pulls.</li>
    <li><strong>Inclined plane:</strong> a ramp allows a load to be raised using a smaller force over a longer distance.</li>
    <li><strong>Wheel and axle:</strong> a handle attached to an axle can help turn a mechanism.</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Work and machines</h3>
  <p>A machine does not create energy. In an ideal machine, input work equals output work. In a real machine, the input work is greater than the useful output because of friction and other energy transfers.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Simple Machines', 15),
  (462, 462, '<h2>Mechanical Advantage</h2>
  <p>Mechanical advantage (MA) compares the load force a machine moves with the effort force applied to it.</p>
  <p><strong>MA = Load ÷ Effort</strong></p>
  <p>Load and effort must be measured in the same units, usually newtons (N). Mechanical advantage has no unit because it is a ratio.</p>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>A lever lifts a load of 600 N using an effort of 150 N.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>MA = Load ÷ Effort.</li>
    <li>MA = 600 ÷ 150.</li>
    <li>MA = 4.</li>
  </ol>
  <p><strong>Answer: MA = 4.</strong> The load force is four times the effort force.</p>
  <p>Mechanical advantage does not tell us by itself whether a machine is efficient.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Mechanical Advantage', 15),
  (463, 463, '<h2>Velocity Ratio</h2>
  <p>Velocity ratio (VR) compares the distance moved by the effort with the distance moved by the load during the same operation.</p>
  <p><strong>VR = Distance moved by effort ÷ Distance moved by load</strong></p>
  <p>Use distances measured in the same units. Velocity ratio has no unit.</p>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>The effort moves 2 m while the load rises 0.5 m.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>VR = distance moved by effort ÷ distance moved by load.</li>
    <li>VR = 2 ÷ 0.5.</li>
    <li>VR = 4.</li>
  </ol>
  <p><strong>Answer: VR = 4.</strong></p>
  <p>A high velocity ratio means the effort moves a greater distance than the load.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Velocity Ratio', 15),
  (464, 464, '<h2>Mechanical Advantage and Velocity Ratio</h2>
  <p>Mechanical advantage compares forces; velocity ratio compares distances moved.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>MA = Load ÷ Effort</strong></li>
    <li><strong>VR = Distance moved by effort ÷ Distance moved by load</strong></li>
  </ul>
  <p>For an ideal machine, MA = VR because input work equals output work.</p>
  <p>For an ordinary real machine, friction and other losses mean that MA is generally less than VR.</p>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>A machine has MA = 3 and VR = 5.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Compare MA with VR.</li>
    <li>MA is smaller than VR.</li>
    <li>This is consistent with a real machine that has energy losses.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>Do not confuse the two ratios: MA uses forces; VR uses distances.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Mechanical Advantage and Velocity Ratio', 15),
  (465, 465, '<h2>Efficiency of Machines</h2>
  <p>Machine efficiency is the percentage of input work converted into useful output work.</p>
  <p><strong>Efficiency (%) = (MA ÷ VR) × 100%</strong></p>
  <p>This formula is equivalent to useful output work divided by input work, multiplied by 100%, when the relevant forces and distances are used consistently.</p>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>A machine has MA = 3 and VR = 4.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Efficiency = (MA ÷ VR) × 100%.</li>
    <li>Efficiency = (3 ÷ 4) × 100%.</li>
    <li>Efficiency = 75%.</li>
  </ol>
  <p><strong>Answer: 75%.</strong></p>
  <p>For an ordinary machine, efficiency is below 100% because some input energy is transferred to the surroundings rather than becoming useful output.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Efficiency of Machines', 15),
  (466, 466, '<h2>Work Input and Work Output</h2>
  <p>Work input is the work done by the effort on a machine. Work output is the useful work done by the machine on the load.</p>
  <p>When force acts in the same direction as movement:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Work input = effort × distance moved by effort</strong></li>
    <li><strong>Work output = load × distance moved by load</strong></li>
  </ul>
  <p>Use force in newtons (N) and distance in metres (m). Work is measured in joules (J).</p>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>An effort of 50 N moves 3 m while lifting a load of 200 N through 0.5 m.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Input work = 50 × 3 = 150 J.</li>
    <li>Output work = 200 × 0.5 = 100 J.</li>
    <li>The difference is 50 J, transferred into other forms.</li>
  </ol>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> input work = 150 J; useful output work = 100 J.</p>
  <p>In an ideal machine, input work equals output work. In a real machine, input work is usually greater than useful output work.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Work Input and Work Output', 15),
  (467, 467, '
  <h2>Pressure in Solids</h2>
  <p>Pressure is the normal force acting per unit area of a surface. For a force distributed uniformly over a flat area, pressure is calculated by dividing the perpendicular force by the area.</p>

  <h3>Formula</h3>
  <p><strong>Pressure = Force ÷ Area</strong></p>
  <p>\(P = \frac{F}{A}\)</p>
  <ul>
    <li>\(P\) = pressure, measured in pascals (Pa)</li>
    <li>\(F\) = force perpendicular to the surface, measured in newtons (N)</li>
    <li>\(A\) = area, measured in square metres (m²)</li>
  </ul>
  <p><strong>1 Pa = 1 N/m²</strong></p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Calculate pressure</h3>
  <p>A force of 100 N acts perpendicularly on an area of 2 m².</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Write P = F/A.</li>
    <li>Substitute P = 100/2.</li>
    <li>Pressure = <strong>50 Pa</strong>.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Smaller area</h3>
  <p>The same 100 N force acts over 0.5 m².</p>
  <p>Pressure = 100/0.5 = <strong>200 Pa</strong>.</p>
  <p>The smaller area produces a greater pressure when the force is unchanged.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Find force</h3>
  <p>If pressure is 400 Pa and area is 0.2 m², then F = PA = 400 × 0.2 = <strong>80 N</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Common mistake</h3>
  <p>Convert the area to m² before calculating pressure in pascals. Also, use the force component perpendicular to the surface.</p>
  
<hr>
<h2>Pressure in Solids</h2>
  <p>A solid object exerts pressure on a surface when it applies a force perpendicular to the surface.</p>
  <p><strong>Pressure = perpendicular force ÷ contact area.</strong></p>
  <p>For an object resting on a horizontal surface, the force may be its weight, provided no other vertical forces change the normal force.</p>
  <h3>Everyday applications</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>A sharp knife has a small cutting area, producing high pressure for a given force.</li>
    <li>Wide tyres spread a vehicle''s weight over a larger area, reducing pressure on soft ground.</li>
    <li>Wide foundations spread a building''s load over a larger area.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Changing the contact area changes pressure even if the force stays the same.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Pressure in Solids', 15),
  (468, 468, '
  <h2>Pressure in Liquids</h2>
  <p>A liquid exerts pressure because of the weight of the liquid above a point. In a liquid at rest, pressure increases with depth and with the liquid''s density.</p>

  <h3>Pressure due to a liquid column</h3>
  <p>For a liquid of uniform density, the gauge pressure at depth h is:</p>
  <p>\(p = \rho gh\)</p>
  <ul>
    <li>\(p\) = gauge pressure in pascals (Pa)</li>
    <li>\(\rho\) = liquid density in kg/m³</li>
    <li>\(g\) = gravitational field strength in N/kg</li>
    <li>\(h\) = depth below the liquid surface in metres</li>
  </ul>
  <p>In school calculations, use the value of g given in the question. If none is given, 10 N/kg is often used for convenient approximations.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 1</h3>
  <p>Find the gauge pressure 2 m below the surface of water. Take density = 1000 kg/m³ and g = 10 N/kg.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Write p = ρgh.</li>
    <li>Substitute p = 1000 × 10 × 2.</li>
    <li>Gauge pressure = <strong>20,000 Pa</strong>.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Greater depth</h3>
  <p>At 4 m depth in the same water, p = 1000 × 10 × 4 = 40,000 Pa. Doubling the depth doubles the gauge pressure.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Different density</h3>
  <p>At the same depth and gravitational field strength, a denser liquid produces a greater gauge pressure because p is proportional to density.</p>

  <h3>Important distinction</h3>
  <p>The formula p = ρgh gives gauge pressure due to the liquid column. Absolute pressure also includes atmospheric pressure at the surface.</p>

  <h3>Common mistake</h3>
  <p>Use depth below the liquid surface, not the container''s total height unless the point is at the bottom and the liquid fills that height.</p>
  
<hr>
<h2>Pressure in Liquids</h2>
  <p>A liquid exerts pressure because of the weight of the liquid above a point. In a stationary liquid of uniform density, pressure increases with depth.</p>
  <p><strong>Liquid pressure:</strong> p = ρgh</p>
  <ul>
    <li>ρ = liquid density in kg/m³</li>
    <li>g = gravitational field strength in N/kg</li>
    <li>h = depth below the liquid surface in metres</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The formula gives the pressure due to the liquid alone. It does not include atmospheric pressure.</p>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>Water has density 1,000 kg/m³. Take g = 10 N/kg. Find the liquid pressure at a depth of 2 m.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>p = ρgh.</li>
    <li>p = 1,000 × 10 × 2.</li>
    <li>p = 20,000 Pa.</li>
  </ol>
  <p><strong>Answer: 20,000 Pa.</strong></p>
<hr>
<h2>Pressure beneath a liquid surface</h2>
  <p>A liquid exerts pressure on the bottom and sides of its container. Liquid pressure acts in all directions and increases with depth.</p>
  <p>At the same depth in the same stationary liquid, pressure is equal at different points.</p>
  <h3>Why does pressure increase with depth?</h3>
  <p>A deeper point has a taller column of liquid above it. The weight of that liquid produces greater pressure.</p>
  <h3>Example</h3>
  <p>Water emerging from a lower hole in a container generally travels farther than water from a higher hole because the pressure is greater at the lower hole.</p>
  <p><b>Important:</b> Liquid pressure depends on depth and liquid density, not simply on the total volume of liquid in the container.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Pressure in Liquids', 15),
  (469, 469, '
  <h2>Atmospheric Pressure</h2>
  <p>Atmospheric pressure is the pressure exerted by the air around Earth. Air has mass, and gravity gives the atmosphere weight. Air molecules also collide with surfaces, contributing to pressure.</p>

  <h3>What causes atmospheric pressure?</h3>
  <p>The air above a surface contributes to the pressure at that location. Atmospheric pressure acts in all directions, not only downward.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: A drinking straw</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>When you suck air from the straw, the pressure inside your mouth and the straw becomes lower.</li>
    <li>Atmospheric pressure on the drink''s surface is then greater than the pressure in the straw.</li>
    <li>The pressure difference pushes the liquid up the straw.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Worked example 2: A suction cup</h3>
  <p>Pressing a suction cup against a smooth surface removes some air from the space beneath it. The higher outside atmospheric pressure can then press the cup against the surface. A good seal is important.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Altitude</h3>
  <p>Atmospheric pressure generally decreases as altitude increases because less air lies above a higher location.</p>

  <h3>Measuring atmospheric pressure</h3>
  <p>A barometer is an instrument used to measure atmospheric pressure. A mercury barometer balances atmospheric pressure against the pressure due to a column of mercury.</p>

  <h3>Common mistake</h3>
  <p>Atmospheric pressure is not caused only by wind. It exists even when the air appears still, and it acts in all directions.</p>
  
<hr>
<h2>Atmospheric Pressure</h2>
  <p>Atmospheric pressure is the pressure exerted by the weight and motion of air in the atmosphere. Air pressure acts in all directions.</p>
  <p>Near sea level, standard atmospheric pressure is approximately 101,325 Pa, often rounded to 101,000 Pa or 1.01 × 10⁵ Pa.</p>
  <h3>How atmospheric pressure changes</h3>
  <p>Atmospheric pressure generally decreases with increasing altitude because less air lies above a point at higher altitude.</p>
  <h3>Everyday examples</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>A drinking straw works because sucking lowers the pressure inside the straw, allowing atmospheric pressure on the drink''s surface to push liquid up.</li>
    <li>A suction cup holds when the pressure difference presses it against a surface.</li>
    <li>A barometer is used to measure atmospheric pressure.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Atmospheric pressure is real even though we do not normally feel it as a net force, because pressures around our bodies are largely balanced.</p>
<hr>
<h2>Pressure from the atmosphere</h2>
  <p>The atmosphere is the layer of gases surrounding Earth. Air has mass, and its weight produces <b>atmospheric pressure</b>.</p>
  <p>Air molecules also collide with surfaces, exerting pressure. Atmospheric pressure acts in all directions.</p>
  <p>Near sea level, standard atmospheric pressure is approximately <b>101,325 Pa</b>, often rounded to 101,000 Pa.</p>
  <h3>Evidence for atmospheric pressure</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>A suction cup can stick to a smooth surface when pressure beneath it is reduced.</li>
    <li>A drinking straw works because pressure differences move the liquid.</li>
    <li>Atmospheric pressure generally decreases as altitude increases.</li>
  </ul>
  <p><b>Important:</b> Atmospheric pressure acts on us from all directions; we do not get crushed because internal pressures in our bodies largely balance the external pressure.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Atmospheric Pressure', 15),
  (470, 470, '
  <h2>Applications of Liquid Pressure</h2>
  <p>Liquid pressure increases with depth. This principle explains why dams are designed with thicker bases, why water can emerge forcefully from a low hole in a tank, and why divers experience greater pressure at greater depths.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Holes in a water tank</h3>
  <p>Imagine a tank with holes at different heights. Water from a lower hole generally emerges faster because the pressure due to the water above that hole is greater.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Dam walls</h3>
  <p>The bottom of a dam experiences greater water pressure than the top. A dam is therefore often made thicker near its base to withstand the greater pressure and resulting forces.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Divers</h3>
  <p>As a diver moves deeper into water, the gauge pressure increases according to p = ρgh. This is why depth matters when planning safe dives.</p>

  <h3>Key relationship</h3>
  <p>For a liquid of uniform density at rest, pressure at a point depends on the liquid''s density, gravitational field strength and depth below the surface. It does not directly depend on the shape of the container.</p>

  <h3>Common mistake</h3>
  <p>Do not confuse pressure with total force. Pressure is force per unit area. The total force on a surface depends on pressure and the area over which it acts.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Applications of Liquid Pressure', 15),
  (471, 471, '
  <h2>Pressure Acts in All Directions</h2>
  <p>A liquid at rest exerts pressure in all directions. At a given point in a stationary liquid, the pressure has the same magnitude in every direction.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: A hole in the side of a container</h3>
  <p>Water flows sideways from a hole in a tank because the water exerts pressure on the side wall. Liquid pressure is not limited to downward forces.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: A submerged object</h3>
  <p>Water presses on the top, bottom and sides of an immersed object. The pressure is greater at the bottom than at the top when those surfaces are at different depths.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Pressure differences</h3>
  <p>Although pressure acts in all directions at a point, pressure can differ between two points at different depths. The difference can create a resultant force.</p>

  <h3>Important distinction</h3>
  <p>Pressure is a scalar quantity: it has magnitude but no single direction of its own. The force exerted by pressure on a surface acts perpendicular to that surface.</p>

  <h3>Common mistake</h3>
  <p>Do not say that liquid pressure acts only downward. The weight of the liquid contributes to the pressure, but the liquid also exerts pressure sideways and upward.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Pressure Acts in All Directions', 15),
  (472, 472, '
  <h2>Pressure in Gases</h2>
  <p>A gas consists of particles moving randomly. Gas pressure is caused by these particles colliding with the walls of their container and exerting forces on them.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Air in a tyre</h3>
  <p>Air particles collide with the inside walls of a tyre. These collisions produce pressure on the tyre''s inner surface.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Compressing a gas</h3>
  <p>If a gas is compressed into a smaller volume while its temperature remains constant and the amount of gas stays fixed, its pressure increases. The particles have less space and collide with the walls more frequently.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Heating a gas in a sealed container</h3>
  <p>When a gas in a rigid sealed container is heated, its particles have greater average kinetic energy. Their collisions with the walls become more forceful, so the pressure increases if the volume remains constant.</p>

  <h3>Key conditions</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Compression effects depend on temperature and the amount of gas.</li>
    <li>Heating increases pressure when the gas is in a fixed-volume container.</li>
    <li>If a container can expand, its volume may change instead of pressure rising as much.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Common mistake</h3>
  <p>Gas particles do not need to be pushed continuously in one direction to produce pressure. Their random motion and collisions create pressure in all directions.</p>
  
<hr>
<h2>Pressure in Gases</h2>
  <p>A gas consists of particles moving randomly. Gas pressure arises from collisions of these particles with the walls of their container.</p>
  <p>When particles collide with a wall, they exert force on it. The combined effect of many collisions produces measurable pressure.</p>
  <h3>Changing gas pressure</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Increasing temperature in a sealed rigid container increases particle speeds and usually increases pressure.</li>
    <li>Reducing the volume of a fixed amount of gas at constant temperature increases pressure.</li>
    <li>Adding more gas particles to a fixed volume at constant temperature generally increases pressure.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>These comparisons assume the other stated conditions remain constant. Gas pressure acts in all directions.</p>
<hr>
<h2>How gases exert pressure</h2>
  <p>Gas particles move randomly and collide with the walls of their container. These collisions exert forces on the walls. The force per unit area is the gas pressure.</p>
  <p>For a gas confined in a container, pressure can change when the gas is compressed, heated or cooled, depending on which other conditions are kept constant.</p>
  <h3>Example: compressing air</h3>
  <p>If air in a sealed syringe is compressed by pushing the plunger inward, its volume decreases and its pressure generally increases, provided the gas temperature is approximately constant.</p>
  <p><b>Do not confuse:</b> pressure in a gas is caused by particle collisions with surfaces. Atmospheric pressure is the pressure exerted by the air around us, including the effect of the weight of the atmosphere.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Pressure in Gases', 15),
  (473, 473, '
  <h2>Pressure and Volume of a Gas</h2>
  <p>For a fixed amount of gas at constant temperature, pressure is inversely proportional to volume. This relationship is known as Boyle''s law.</p>

  <h3>Formula</h3>
  <p>\(P_1V_1 = P_2V_2\)</p>
  <p>Here, P₁ and V₁ are the initial pressure and volume, while P₂ and V₂ are the final pressure and volume.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 1</h3>
  <p>A gas occupies 600 cm³ at a pressure of 100 kPa. It is compressed to 300 cm³ at constant temperature. Find the final pressure.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Write P₁V₁ = P₂V₂.</li>
    <li>Substitute: 100 × 600 = P₂ × 300.</li>
    <li>P₂ = (100 × 600) ÷ 300 = <strong>200 kPa</strong>.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Worked example 2</h3>
  <p>A gas at 150 kPa occupies 400 cm³. Its pressure changes to 100 kPa at constant temperature.</p>
  <p>V₂ = P₁V₁ ÷ P₂ = (150 × 400) ÷ 100 = <strong>600 cm³</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Interpretation</h3>
  <p>Halving the volume doubles the pressure, provided temperature and amount of gas remain constant.</p>

  <h3>Common mistake</h3>
  <p>Do not use Boyle''s law without checking the conditions. Pressure units must be consistent, and volume units must be consistent across the two states.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Pressure and Volume of a Gas', 15),
  (474, 474, '<h2>Meaning of Pressure</h2>
  <p>Pressure is the normal force acting per unit area of a surface.</p>
  <p><strong>Formula:</strong> P = F/A</p>
  <ul>
    <li>P = pressure in pascals (Pa)</li>
    <li>F = perpendicular force in newtons (N)</li>
    <li>A = area in square metres (m²)</li>
  </ul>
  <p>One pascal is one newton per square metre: 1 Pa = 1 N/m².</p>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>A force of 100 N acts normally on an area of 5 m².</p>
  <ol>
    <li>P = F/A.</li>
    <li>P = 100/5.</li>
    <li>P = 20 Pa.</li>
  </ol>
  <p><strong>Answer: 20 Pa.</strong></p>
  <p>For the same force, a smaller contact area produces greater pressure.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Meaning of Pressure', 15),
  (475, 475, '<h2>Factors Affecting Liquid Pressure</h2>
  <p>For a stationary liquid, the pressure due to the liquid is given by p = ρgh.</p>
  <p>Therefore, liquid pressure depends on:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Depth (h):</strong> greater depth gives greater pressure.</li>
    <li><strong>Density (ρ):</strong> a denser liquid gives greater pressure at the same depth.</li>
    <li><strong>Gravitational field strength (g):</strong> greater g gives greater pressure for the same liquid and depth.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>At the same depth in the same stationary liquid, pressure is the same regardless of the container''s shape. The formula gives gauge pressure due to the liquid, excluding atmospheric pressure.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Factors Affecting Liquid Pressure', 15),
  (476, 476, '<h2>Pressure Differences</h2>
  <p>A pressure difference is the difference between pressure at two locations. A fluid tends to move from a region of higher pressure towards a region of lower pressure when a path is available.</p>
  <p><strong>Pressure difference:</strong> Δp = p₁ − p₂, where p₁ is the higher pressure and p₂ is the lower pressure.</p>
  <p>A pressure difference can produce a net force on an object or drive fluid flow.</p>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>Pressure on one side of a surface is 120,000 Pa and pressure on the other side is 100,000 Pa.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Δp = 120,000 − 100,000.</li>
    <li>Δp = 20,000 Pa.</li>
  </ol>
  <p><strong>Answer: 20,000 Pa.</strong></p>
  <p>When pressure is equal on both sides, the pressures do not produce a net force across the surface.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Pressure Differences', 15),
  (477, 477, '<h2>Upthrust</h2>
  <p>Upthrust is the upward force a fluid exerts on an object partly or fully immersed in it.</p>
  <p>In a liquid, pressure increases with depth. The bottom of an immersed object is generally at a greater depth than its top, so the upward pressure force on the bottom can exceed the downward pressure force on the top. The result is an upward force.</p>
  <h3>Archimedes'' principle</h3>
  <p>The upthrust on an immersed object equals the weight of the fluid displaced by the object.</p>
  <p><strong>Upthrust = weight of displaced fluid</strong></p>
  <p>For a uniform gravitational field, the weight of displaced fluid is its mass multiplied by g.</p>
  <p>Upthrust acts upward, opposing the object''s weight, but it does not always equal the object''s weight.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Upthrust', 15),
  (478, 478, '<h2>Floating and Sinking</h2>
  <p>Whether an object rises, sinks or floats depends on its weight and the upthrust acting on it.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Upthrust greater than weight:</strong> the object accelerates upward.</li>
    <li><strong>Weight greater than upthrust:</strong> the object accelerates downward.</li>
    <li><strong>Upthrust equal to weight:</strong> the vertical forces balance; the object can remain at rest or move at constant vertical velocity.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>A floating object at rest has upthrust equal to its weight. It displaces enough fluid for the weight of that displaced fluid to equal the object''s weight.</p>
  <p>Average density helps explain flotation: an object whose average density is less than the fluid''s density can float, depending on its shape and whether it can take in the fluid.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Floating and Sinking', 15),
  (479, 479, '<h2>Applications of Upthrust</h2>
  <p>Upthrust explains why ships can float, why balloons filled with a suitable gas rise in air, and how hydrometers can be used to compare liquid densities.</p>
  <h3>Ships</h3>
  <p>A ship''s hollow hull makes its overall average density lower than that of the water it displaces. It sinks until the upthrust balances its weight.</p>
  <h3>Hydrometers</h3>
  <p>A hydrometer floats at a depth determined by the balance between its weight and the upthrust. In a denser liquid, it generally needs to displace less liquid volume, so it floats higher.</p>
  <h3>Balloons</h3>
  <p>A balloon rises when the upthrust from the surrounding air exceeds the total downward weight of the balloon and its contents.</p>
  <p>In every case, the key principle is a balance between weight and upthrust, along with the density of the surrounding fluid.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Applications of Upthrust', 15),
  (480, 480, '<h2>What is pressure?</h2>
  <p><b>Pressure</b> is the normal force acting per unit area of a surface.</p>
  <p>Formula: <b>P = F/A</b></p>
  <ul>
    <li>P = pressure in pascals (Pa)</li>
    <li>F = perpendicular force in newtons (N)</li>
    <li>A = area in square metres (m²)</li>
  </ul>
  <p>One pascal is one newton per square metre: 1 Pa = 1 N/m².</p>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>A force of 60 N acts normally on an area of 3 m².</p>
  <p>P = F/A = 60/3 = <b>20 Pa</b>.</p>
  <p><b>Key idea:</b> Pressure depends on both the force and the area over which it acts.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Pressure and Force', 15),
  (481, 481, '<h2>How area affects pressure</h2>
  <p>For a solid surface, pressure is calculated using:</p>
  <p><b>P = F/A</b></p>
  <p>For the same force, a smaller contact area produces greater pressure. A larger contact area produces lower pressure.</p>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>A block exerts a force of 120 N over an area of 0.04 m².</p>
  <p>P = 120/0.04 = <b>3,000 Pa</b>.</p>
  <p><b>Watch the units:</b> area must be in m². Convert cm² to m² before substituting.</p>
  <p>Example: 20 cm² = 20 ÷ 10,000 = 0.002 m².</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Calculating Pressure in Solids', 15),
  (482, 482, '<h2>Three factors</h2>
  <p>For a liquid at rest, the pressure due to the liquid depends on:</p>
  <ol>
    <li><b>Depth (h):</b> greater depth gives greater pressure.</li>
    <li><b>Density (ρ):</b> a denser liquid gives greater pressure at the same depth.</li>
    <li><b>Gravitational field strength (g):</b> stronger gravity gives greater pressure.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>The relationship is <b>P = ρgh</b>.</p>
  <h3>Compare two situations</h3>
  <p>If the liquid and gravity remain the same, doubling the depth doubles the liquid pressure.</p>
  <p>If the depth and gravity remain the same, doubling the liquid density doubles the liquid pressure.</p>
  <p><b>Remember:</b> this equation gives pressure due to the liquid, not the total pressure including atmospheric pressure.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Factors Affecting Pressure in Liquids', 15),
  (483, 483, '<h2>Using P = ρgh</h2>
  <p>Use this equation to calculate pressure due to a liquid:</p>
  <p><b>P = ρgh</b></p>
  <ul>
    <li>P = pressure in pascals (Pa)</li>
    <li>ρ = density in kg/m³</li>
    <li>g = gravitational field strength in N/kg</li>
    <li>h = depth in metres (m)</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>Calculate the pressure 2 m below the surface of water. Take ρ = 1,000 kg/m³ and g = 10 N/kg.</p>
  <p>P = 1,000 × 10 × 2</p>
  <p><b>P = 20,000 Pa</b></p>
  <p><b>Common error:</b> using centimetres instead of metres, or using density in g/cm³ without converting it to kg/m³.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Calculating Pressure in Liquids', 15),
  (484, 484, '<h2>Pressure transmission</h2>
  <p>When pressure is applied to an enclosed fluid, the pressure change is transmitted throughout the fluid and to the walls of its container. This is the principle associated with <b>Pascal''s law</b>.</p>
  <p>Liquids are commonly used in hydraulic systems because they are difficult to compress.</p>
  <h3>Example: an enclosed liquid</h3>
  <p>Pressing a piston into a sealed liquid increases the pressure. That pressure change is transmitted through the liquid to another piston.</p>
  <p><b>Do not confuse:</b> transmission of an applied pressure change with the natural increase in liquid pressure at greater depth. They are related to pressure but describe different effects.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Transmission of Pressure in Fluids', 15),
  (485, 485, '<h2>How hydraulic machines work</h2>
  <p>Hydraulic machines use pressure transmission in an enclosed liquid. Examples include hydraulic brakes and hydraulic lifts.</p>
  <p>For ideal pistons at the same transmitted pressure:</p>
  <p><b>P = F₁/A₁ = F₂/A₂</b></p>
  <p>Therefore, <b>F₂ = F₁ × A₂/A₁</b>.</p>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>A small piston has an area of 0.01 m² and receives a force of 50 N. The larger piston has an area of 0.1 m².</p>
  <p>P = 50 ÷ 0.01 = 5,000 Pa.</p>
  <p>F₂ = 5,000 × 0.1 = <b>500 N</b>.</p>
  <p>The larger piston can produce a larger force, but in an ideal system it moves a shorter distance. Energy is not created.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Hydraulic Machines', 15),
  (486, 486, '<h2>The barometer</h2>
  <p>A <b>barometer</b> measures atmospheric pressure. A mercury barometer uses a column of mercury supported by atmospheric pressure acting on mercury in a reservoir.</p>
  <p>At standard atmospheric pressure, the mercury column is approximately 760 mm high.</p>
  <p>The pressure represented by a liquid column can be calculated using:</p>
  <p><b>P = ρgh</b></p>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>For mercury, take ρ = 13,600 kg/m³, g = 10 N/kg and h = 0.76 m.</p>
  <p>P = 13,600 × 10 × 0.76</p>
  <p>P = <b>103,360 Pa</b>.</p>
  <p>This is close to standard atmospheric pressure; using g = 9.81 N/kg gives a value closer to 101,000 Pa.</p>
  <p><b>Remember:</b> a barometer measures atmospheric pressure. A pressure gauge commonly measures pressure relative to the surrounding atmosphere.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Measuring Atmospheric Pressure with a Barometer', 15),
  (487, 487, '<h2>Pressure gauges</h2>
  <p>A pressure gauge measures the pressure of a gas or liquid in a system. Many common gauges indicate pressure relative to atmospheric pressure.</p>
  <p>A Bourdon gauge uses a curved metal tube that changes shape slightly when the pressure inside it changes. A mechanism moves a pointer over a scale.</p>
  <h2>Siphons</h2>
  <p>A siphon is a tube that transfers liquid from a higher reservoir to a lower level, provided the tube is primed and the arrangement is suitable.</p>
  <p>Gravity and pressure differences maintain the flow. Atmospheric pressure can be important in ordinary siphon operation, but the explanation is not simply that air ''sucks'' the liquid through.</p>
  <p><b>Keep the devices distinct:</b> a gauge measures pressure; a siphon transfers liquid between levels.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Pressure Gauges and Siphons', 15),
  (488, 488, '<h2>Measuring pressure difference</h2>
  <p>A U-tube manometer contains a liquid in a U-shaped tube. One side may be connected to a gas container while the other is open to the atmosphere.</p>
  <p>A difference in pressure causes the liquid levels in the two arms to differ.</p>
  <p>For a simple manometer containing the same liquid in both arms, the pressure difference is:</p>
  <p><b>ΔP = ρgh</b></p>
  <ul>
    <li>ρ = density of the manometer liquid in kg/m³</li>
    <li>g = gravitational field strength in N/kg</li>
    <li>h = vertical difference between the liquid levels in metres</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>Mercury has density 13,600 kg/m³. The level difference is 0.10 m. Take g = 10 N/kg.</p>
  <p>ΔP = 13,600 × 10 × 0.10 = <b>13,600 Pa</b>.</p>
  <p><b>Important:</b> use the vertical height difference, not the length of the tube.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for The U-Tube Manometer', 15),
  (489, 489, '<h2>Which pressure is greater?</h2>
  <p>In a simple U-tube manometer containing the same liquid in both arms, the side with the lower liquid level is generally the side with the greater applied pressure.</p>
  <p>The greater pressure pushes the manometer liquid down on its side and raises the liquid on the other side.</p>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>A gas container is connected to one arm of a mercury manometer. The mercury is lower on the gas side by 0.05 m.</p>
  <p>This indicates that the gas pressure exceeds atmospheric pressure by:</p>
  <p>ΔP = ρgh = 13,600 × 10 × 0.05 = <b>6,800 Pa</b>.</p>
  <p>If atmospheric pressure is 100,000 Pa, then the gas''s absolute pressure is 100,000 + 6,800 = <b>106,800 Pa</b>.</p>
  <p><b>Distinguish:</b> gauge pressure is measured relative to atmospheric pressure; absolute pressure is measured relative to a vacuum.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Interpreting Manometer Readings', 15),
  (490, 490, '<h2>How hydraulic brakes work</h2>
  <p>Hydraulic brakes use pressure transmission through an enclosed liquid to apply braking force at the wheels.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>The driver presses the brake pedal.</li>
    <li>The pedal operates a master-cylinder piston.</li>
    <li>The piston increases pressure in the brake fluid.</li>
    <li>The pressure is transmitted through the fluid to the wheel mechanisms.</li>
    <li>The brake pads or shoes press against rotating parts, producing friction that slows the vehicle.</li>
  </ol>
  <h3>Properties of brake fluid</h3>
  <p>Brake fluid should transmit pressure effectively, have a suitable boiling point and be compatible with the system''s seals and materials.</p>
  <p>Air in the brake lines is undesirable because it is compressible and can make the pedal feel spongy.</p>
  <p><b>Key distinction:</b> the fluid transmits pressure; friction at the braking surfaces slows the vehicle.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Hydraulic Braking Systems', 15),
  (491, 491, '<h2>How a bicycle pump works</h2>
  <p>A simple hand pump compresses air and forces it into a tyre through a valve.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>When the piston is pulled back, the pump chamber expands and its pressure falls.</li>
    <li>Air enters the chamber through an inlet valve.</li>
    <li>When the piston is pushed down, the air is compressed.</li>
    <li>When the chamber pressure exceeds the tyre pressure sufficiently, the outlet valve allows air into the tyre.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>The valves control the direction of air flow.</p>
  <p><b>Important:</b> the pump raises the air pressure by compressing air and forcing more air into the tyre. The pump does not work by making the air disappear.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for The Bicycle Pump', 15),
  (492, 492, '<h2>Using pressure differences</h2>
  <p>A syringe can draw liquid in or expel liquid by changing the pressure inside its barrel.</p>
  <h3>Drawing liquid in</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Place the nozzle in the liquid.</li>
    <li>Pull the plunger back.</li>
    <li>The volume inside the syringe increases and its pressure falls.</li>
    <li>The pressure on the liquid outside is greater, so liquid enters through the nozzle.</li>
  </ol>
  <h3>Expelling liquid</h3>
  <p>Pushing the plunger decreases the volume and increases the pressure inside the syringe. If the nozzle is open, the liquid can be forced out.</p>
  <p><b>Key idea:</b> the pressure difference causes the flow. The plunger changes the pressure inside the syringe.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for The Syringe', 15),
  (493, 493, '<h2>Why liquid rises in a straw</h2>
  <p>A drinking straw works through a pressure difference.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Before drinking, the pressure on the liquid surface in the cup is approximately atmospheric pressure.</li>
    <li>When a person sucks, the pressure inside the mouth and straw is reduced.</li>
    <li>The pressure acting on the liquid surface in the cup is now greater than the pressure inside the straw.</li>
    <li>This pressure difference pushes liquid up the straw.</li>
  </ol>
  <p><b>Important:</b> sucking lowers the pressure inside the straw; atmospheric pressure helps push the liquid upwards.</p>
  <p>If the straw has a hole that allows air to enter, the pressure difference may not be maintained, making it difficult to draw up the liquid.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for The Drinking Straw', 15),
  (494, 494, '<h2>How a force pump works</h2>
  <p>A force pump uses a piston and valves to move liquid, often water, against a pressure difference or to a higher level.</p>
  <p>In a common reciprocating force pump:</p>
  <ol>
    <li>During the intake stroke, the piston moves to draw liquid into the cylinder through an inlet valve.</li>
    <li>During the delivery stroke, the piston pushes the liquid and increases its pressure.</li>
    <li>The inlet valve closes to prevent backward flow.</li>
    <li>The outlet valve opens when the liquid pressure is sufficient to push liquid into the delivery pipe.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>The valves help maintain one-way flow.</p>
  <p><b>Remember:</b> the exact arrangement varies by pump design, but the basic principle is that a moving piston changes pressure and valves control flow direction.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for The Force Pump', 15),
  (495, 495, '<h2>How a lift pump raises water</h2>
  <p>A conventional lift pump raises water from a well by creating a region of lower pressure above the water. Atmospheric pressure acting on the water surface then pushes water up the pump pipe.</p>
  <p>In a typical hand-operated lift pump:</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Moving the handle raises the piston and changes the pressure in the cylinder.</li>
    <li>The valves open or close to control the flow of water.</li>
    <li>Atmospheric pressure helps push water up the pipe into the pump.</li>
    <li>Repeated strokes move water upward and out through the spout.</li>
  </ol>
  <p><b>Important limit:</b> a simple suction lift pump cannot raise water from an arbitrarily great depth. Atmospheric pressure limits the theoretical lift, and real pumps have a lower practical limit.</p>
  <p>Do not confuse a lift pump with a force pump: the typical lift pump relies on atmospheric pressure to push water into a low-pressure region, while a force pump can deliver liquid under increased pressure.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for The Lift Pump', 15),
  (496, 496, '<h2>Evidence that atmospheric pressure exists</h2>
  <p>Atmospheric pressure is not visible directly, but its effects can be demonstrated.</p>
  <h3>Experiment: inverted water-filled container</h3>
  <p>A simple demonstration uses a rigid cup or container, water, and a flat sheet of stiff card.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Fill the container with water.</li>
    <li>Place the card over its opening and hold it firmly.</li>
    <li>Carefully invert the container.</li>
    <li>Release the card while keeping the setup steady.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>Under suitable conditions, the card may remain in place because the pressure difference helps support it. Surface tension and the seal at the rim also matter.</p>
  <p>Other demonstrations include suction cups and the collapse of a container when its internal pressure is reduced.</p>
  <p><b>Safety:</b> perform demonstrations over a basin and use a lightweight container. Do not use hot or fragile vessels.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Demonstrating Atmospheric Pressure', 15),
  (497, 497, '<h2>Temperature</h2>
  <p>Temperature is a measure of how hot or cold an object is. At the particle level, the Kelvin temperature of a substance is related to the average kinetic energy of its particles in random motion.</p>
  <p>Temperature is measured in degrees Celsius (°C) or kelvin (K).</p>
  <p><strong>Temperature conversion:</strong> T(K) = t(°C) + 273.15</p>
  <p>For many school calculations, 273 is used as an approximation.</p>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>Convert 27°C to kelvin.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>T = 27 + 273.15.</li>
    <li>T = 300.15 K.</li>
  </ol>
  <p><strong>Answer: 300.15 K, approximately 300 K.</strong></p>
  <p>Temperature is different from heat: temperature describes thermal state, while heat is energy transferred because of a temperature difference.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Temperature', 15),
  (498, 498, '<h2>Thermal Expansion</h2>
  <p>Thermal expansion is the increase in a material''s dimensions when its temperature rises, usually because its particles vibrate more strongly or move farther apart on average.</p>
  <p>Solids can expand in length, area and volume. Liquids and gases also expand when heated under suitable conditions.</p>
  <h3>Examples</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Bridges use expansion joints to accommodate temperature changes.</li>
    <li>Gaps are left between some railway rails or track sections to manage expansion.</li>
    <li>Overhead wires may sag more in hot weather because their length increases.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>When a material cools, it generally contracts. Different materials expand by different amounts for the same temperature rise.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Thermal Expansion', 15),
  (499, 499, '<h2>Linear Expansion of Solids</h2>
  <p>Linear expansion is the change in length of a solid when its temperature changes.</p>
  <p>For a solid that expands uniformly over a suitable temperature range:</p>
  <p><strong>ΔL = αL₀ΔT</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li>ΔL = change in length, in metres (m)</li>
    <li>α = coefficient of linear expansion, in K⁻¹ or °C⁻¹</li>
    <li>L₀ = original length, in metres (m)</li>
    <li>ΔT = temperature change, in K or °C</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>A 2 m rod has α = 1.2 × 10⁻⁵ K⁻¹ and is heated by 50 K.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>ΔL = αL₀ΔT.</li>
    <li>ΔL = (1.2 × 10⁻⁵) × 2 × 50.</li>
    <li>ΔL = 0.0012 m = 1.2 mm.</li>
  </ol>
  <p><strong>Answer: 1.2 mm.</strong></p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Linear Expansion of Solids', 15),
  (500, 500, '<h2>Heat Capacity</h2>
  <p>Heat capacity is the energy required to raise the temperature of an entire object by one kelvin.</p>
  <p><strong>C = Q/ΔT</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li>C = heat capacity, in J/K</li>
    <li>Q = energy transferred as heat, in joules (J)</li>
    <li>ΔT = temperature change, in K</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Heat capacity depends on the object''s material and its mass. A large object made from a given material generally has a greater heat capacity than a smaller object made from the same material.</p>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>An object absorbs 6,000 J and its temperature rises by 20 K.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>C = Q/ΔT.</li>
    <li>C = 6,000/20.</li>
    <li>C = 300 J/K.</li>
  </ol>
  <p><strong>Answer: 300 J/K.</strong></p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Heat Capacity', 15);
INSERT INTO public.lessons (id, topic_id, content, summary, estimated_minutes) VALUES
  (501, 501, '<h2>Specific Heat Capacity</h2>
  <p>Specific heat capacity is the energy required to raise the temperature of 1 kg of a substance by 1 K, without a change of state.</p>
  <p><strong>Q = mcΔT</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li>Q = energy transferred, in J</li>
    <li>m = mass, in kg</li>
    <li>c = specific heat capacity, in J/(kg·K)</li>
    <li>ΔT = temperature change, in K or °C</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>Calculate the energy needed to heat 2 kg of water by 10 K, using c = 4,200 J/(kg·K).</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Q = mcΔT.</li>
    <li>Q = 2 × 4,200 × 10.</li>
    <li>Q = 84,000 J.</li>
  </ol>
  <p><strong>Answer: 84,000 J.</strong></p>
  <p>Specific heat capacity is a property of the substance. It is not the same as the heat capacity of an entire object.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Specific Heat Capacity', 15),
  (502, 502, '<h2>Heat Transfer by Conduction</h2>
  <p>Conduction is the transfer of thermal energy through a material without the material as a whole moving from place to place.</p>
  <p>In solids, vibrating particles transfer energy to neighbouring particles. In metals, mobile electrons also transfer energy efficiently.</p>
  <h3>Examples</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>A metal spoon becomes warm when one end is placed in hot water.</li>
    <li>A metal cooking pot transfers energy from its hot base to its contents.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Metals are generally good thermal conductors. Materials such as wood, plastic and trapped air are comparatively poor conductors and are often used as insulators.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Heat Transfer by Conduction', 15),
  (503, 503, '<h2>Heat Transfer by Convection</h2>
  <p>Convection is the transfer of thermal energy by the bulk movement of a fluid, such as a liquid or gas.</p>
  <p>When a region of fluid is heated, it often expands and becomes less dense than the surrounding cooler fluid. The warmer fluid rises while cooler, denser fluid moves in to replace it. This circulation can form a convection current.</p>
  <h3>Example: heating water</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Water near the heat source warms.</li>
    <li>It expands and becomes less dense than nearby cooler water.</li>
    <li>The warmer water rises and cooler water moves down towards the heat source.</li>
    <li>The circulating water transfers thermal energy around the container.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>Convection occurs in fluids, not through bulk circulation of a solid.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Heat Transfer by Convection', 15),
  (504, 504, '<h2>Heat Transfer by Radiation</h2>
  <p>Thermal radiation is the transfer of energy by electromagnetic waves, mainly infrared radiation at everyday temperatures.</p>
  <p>Radiation does not require a material medium, so energy from the Sun can travel through the vacuum of space to Earth.</p>
  <h3>Surface properties</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Dull, dark surfaces are generally good absorbers and emitters of thermal radiation.</li>
    <li>Shiny, light-coloured surfaces are generally poorer absorbers and emitters, and good reflectors.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>All objects above absolute zero emit thermal radiation. The net energy transfer by radiation depends on the object''s temperature relative to its surroundings.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Heat Transfer by Radiation', 15),
  (505, 505, '<h2>Comparing Conduction, Convection and Radiation</h2>
  <p>Thermal energy can be transferred by conduction, convection and radiation. Different situations may involve more than one method at the same time.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Conduction:</strong> energy transfer through interactions within matter; mobile electrons also help in metals.</li>
    <li><strong>Convection:</strong> energy transfer by bulk movement of a liquid or gas.</li>
    <li><strong>Radiation:</strong> energy transfer by electromagnetic waves; a material medium is not required.</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Worked examples</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>A metal spoon warming from its immersed end: conduction.</li>
    <li>Water circulating as it heats in a pot: convection.</li>
    <li>Energy travelling from the Sun to Earth: radiation.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>In a hot cooking pot, conduction through the metal and convection in the liquid can occur together, while radiation may also transfer energy from hot surfaces.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Comparing Conduction, Convection and Radiation', 15),
  (506, 506, '<h2>Change of State</h2>
  <p>A change of state occurs when a substance changes between solid, liquid and gas.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Melting:</strong> solid to liquid.</li>
    <li><strong>Freezing:</strong> liquid to solid.</li>
    <li><strong>Vaporisation:</strong> liquid to gas, including boiling and evaporation.</li>
    <li><strong>Condensation:</strong> gas to liquid.</li>
    <li><strong>Sublimation:</strong> solid directly to gas.</li>
    <li><strong>Deposition:</strong> gas directly to solid.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>For a pure substance undergoing a change of state at a fixed pressure, the temperature remains constant during the phase change. Energy supplied or removed changes the arrangement and separation of particles rather than increasing or decreasing the temperature during that stage.</p>
  <p>Evaporation can occur at temperatures below the boiling point; boiling occurs throughout the liquid at its boiling point for the given pressure.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Change of State', 15),
  (507, 507, '<h2>Latent Heat</h2>
  <p>Latent heat is the energy transferred to or from a substance during a change of state without a change in temperature, provided the change occurs at constant pressure for a pure substance.</p>
  <p>During melting, energy supplied helps particles overcome some of the attractive forces holding them in fixed positions. During boiling, energy supplied helps particles separate sufficiently to form a gas.</p>
  <p>During freezing and condensation, energy is released to the surroundings as particles form a more condensed state.</p>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>Ice at its melting point receives energy and melts while remaining at the same temperature. The energy is used for the change of state rather than raising the temperature during melting.</p>
  <p><strong>Key distinction:</strong> sensible heating changes temperature; latent heating changes state without changing temperature during the phase change.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Latent Heat', 15),
  (508, 508, '<h2>Specific Latent Heat</h2>
  <p>Specific latent heat is the energy required to change the state of 1 kg of a substance without changing its temperature.</p>
  <p><strong>Q = mL</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li>Q = energy transferred, in joules (J)</li>
    <li>m = mass, in kilograms (kg)</li>
    <li>L = specific latent heat, in J/kg</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Specific latent heat depends on the substance and the type of change of state. The specific latent heat of fusion concerns melting or freezing; the specific latent heat of vaporisation concerns boiling or condensation.</p>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>A 0.4 kg sample has a specific latent heat of 200,000 J/kg for a particular phase change.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Q = mL.</li>
    <li>Q = 0.4 × 200,000.</li>
    <li>Q = 80,000 J.</li>
  </ol>
  <p><strong>Answer: 80,000 J.</strong></p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Specific Latent Heat', 15),
  (509, 509, '<h2>Specific Latent Heat of Fusion</h2>
  <p>The specific latent heat of fusion is the energy required to change 1 kg of a solid into a liquid at its melting point without a temperature change.</p>
  <p>The same magnitude of energy per kilogram is released when that substance freezes at the same pressure.</p>
  <p><strong>Q = mLᶠ</strong></p>
  <p>Here, Lᶠ represents the specific latent heat of fusion.</p>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>Use Lᶠ = 334,000 J/kg for ice near its normal melting point. Calculate the energy needed to melt 0.2 kg of ice already at 0°C.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Q = mLᶠ.</li>
    <li>Q = 0.2 × 334,000.</li>
    <li>Q = 66,800 J.</li>
  </ol>
  <p><strong>Answer: 66,800 J.</strong></p>
  <p>This calculation assumes the ice is already at its melting point. If the ice starts colder, energy is also needed to warm it to that point.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Specific Latent Heat of Fusion', 15),
  (510, 510, '<h2>Specific Latent Heat of Vaporisation</h2>
  <p>The specific latent heat of vaporisation is the energy required to change 1 kg of a liquid into gas at its boiling point without changing its temperature.</p>
  <p>For the reverse process, condensation, the same magnitude of energy per kilogram is released at the corresponding saturation temperature and pressure.</p>
  <p><strong>Q = mLᵥ</strong></p>
  <p>Here, Lᵥ is the specific latent heat of vaporisation.</p>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>Use Lᵥ = 2.26 × 10⁶ J/kg for water at its normal boiling point. Find the energy needed to vaporise 0.1 kg of water already at 100°C.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Q = mLᵥ.</li>
    <li>Q = 0.1 × 2.26 × 10⁶.</li>
    <li>Q = 226,000 J.</li>
  </ol>
  <p><strong>Answer: 226,000 J.</strong></p>
  <p>The calculation assumes the water is already at its boiling point and does not include heating it to 100°C.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Specific Latent Heat of Vaporisation', 15),
  (511, 511, '<h2>Heating Curves</h2>
  <p>A heating curve shows how a substance''s temperature changes as energy is supplied over time or as cumulative energy input increases.</p>
  <p>For a pure substance heated at constant pressure, a typical heating curve has:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Sloping sections:</strong> the substance remains in one state and its temperature rises.</li>
    <li><strong>Flat sections:</strong> the substance changes state at constant temperature; supplied energy provides latent heat.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>For ice initially below 0°C, a typical curve shows ice warming, melting at about 0°C, liquid water warming, boiling at about 100°C at normal atmospheric pressure, and steam warming if heating continues.</p>
  <h3>Worked interpretation</h3>
  <p>If a graph is flat while energy continues to enter a pure substance, the substance may be undergoing a phase change. The plateau''s length depends on the energy needed for that change and the rate of energy supply.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Heating Curves', 15),
  (512, 512, '<h2>Cooling Curves</h2>
  <p>A cooling curve shows how the temperature of a substance changes as it loses energy to its surroundings.</p>
  <p>For a pure substance at constant pressure, a typical curve has:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Sloping sections:</strong> the substance cools within a single state.</li>
    <li><strong>Flat sections:</strong> a phase change occurs at constant temperature, such as freezing or condensation.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>During freezing, a pure substance releases latent heat while the liquid changes into a solid. During condensation, gas releases latent heat as it changes into liquid.</p>
  <h3>Worked interpretation</h3>
  <p>If liquid water cools to its freezing point, its temperature falls. While it freezes at constant pressure, the temperature stays approximately constant until the phase change is complete. After freezing, the solid can cool further.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Cooling Curves', 15),
  (513, 513, '<h2>Evaporation</h2>
  <p>Evaporation is the change from liquid to gas that occurs at the surface of a liquid. It can occur at temperatures below the boiling point.</p>
  <p>Some particles at the liquid surface have enough energy to escape into the surrounding gas. The particles that escape carry energy away, so the remaining liquid may cool if energy is not replaced from its surroundings.</p>
  <h3>Examples</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Wet clothes dry as water evaporates.</li>
    <li>Sweat evaporates from skin and can cool the body.</li>
    <li>Water in an open container gradually disappears over time.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Evaporation differs from boiling: evaporation occurs at the surface and can happen below the boiling point, while boiling occurs throughout the liquid at its boiling point for the given pressure.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Evaporation', 15),
  (514, 514, '<h2>Factors Affecting Evaporation</h2>
  <p>The rate of evaporation depends on conditions at the liquid surface and in the surrounding air.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Temperature:</strong> a higher liquid temperature generally increases evaporation rate because more particles can escape.</li>
    <li><strong>Surface area:</strong> a larger exposed surface generally allows more particles to escape at once.</li>
    <li><strong>Air movement:</strong> moving air carries away vapour near the surface, often increasing evaporation.</li>
    <li><strong>Humidity:</strong> lower relative humidity generally increases evaporation because the air is less saturated with water vapour.</li>
    <li><strong>Nature of the liquid:</strong> different liquids evaporate at different rates because their particles experience different intermolecular attractions and have different volatilities.</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Worked comparison</h3>
  <p>Water spread over a wide tray usually evaporates faster than the same amount of water in a narrow container under the same temperature, airflow and humidity, because more surface is exposed.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Factors Affecting Evaporation', 15),
  (515, 515, '<h2>Boiling Point and Pressure</h2>
  <p>A liquid boils when bubbles of vapour can form and grow throughout the liquid. This occurs when the liquid''s vapour pressure reaches the external pressure acting on it.</p>
  <p>For water, the normal boiling point is approximately 100°C at standard atmospheric pressure.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Lower external pressure:</strong> the boiling point generally decreases.</li>
    <li><strong>Higher external pressure:</strong> the boiling point generally increases.</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Applications</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>At high altitude, atmospheric pressure is lower, so water boils below 100°C.</li>
    <li>A pressure cooker raises the pressure inside, allowing water and steam to reach a temperature above 100°C.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Boiling point depends on pressure, so 100°C is not the boiling point of water under every condition.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Boiling Point and Pressure', 15),
  (516, 516, '<h2>Thermal Insulation</h2>
  <p>Thermal insulation reduces the rate at which thermal energy transfers between a system and its surroundings. It does not necessarily stop all energy transfer.</p>
  <p>Good insulation may reduce conduction, convection, radiation or a combination of these mechanisms.</p>
  <h3>Examples</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Trapped air in foam or fibreglass reduces conduction and suppresses convection when the air is held in small spaces.</li>
    <li>Shiny surfaces reduce radiative heat transfer by reflecting much of the incident thermal radiation.</li>
    <li>Double-glazed windows trap a layer of air or gas between panes, reducing heat transfer.</li>
    <li>Vacuum flasks use a vacuum between walls to greatly reduce conduction and convection through that gap.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Insulation can keep a hot object warm or a cold object cool by slowing energy transfer. It does not create heat or cold.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Thermal Insulation', 15),
  (517, 517, '<h2>Motion Calculations</h2>
<p>Speed describes how much distance an object covers per unit time.</p>
<p><strong>Speed = Distance ÷ Time</strong></p>
<p>Rearranging gives:</p>
<ul>
<li>Distance = Speed × Time</li>
<li>Time = Distance ÷ Speed</li>
</ul>
<p>Use consistent units. Convert minutes to seconds when speed is in m/s. Convert kilometres to metres when required.</p>
<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p>A cyclist travels 150 m in 30 s.</p>
<p>Speed = 150 ÷ 30 = 5 m/s.</p>
<p><strong>Check:</strong> 5 m/s × 30 s = 150 m.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Motion Calculations', 15),
  (518, 518, '<h2>Distance–Time Graphs</h2>
<p>A distance–time graph shows how the distance travelled changes with time.</p>
<ul>
<li>Time is normally on the horizontal axis.</li>
<li>Distance is normally on the vertical axis.</li>
<li>A horizontal line means the object is stationary.</li>
<li>A straight rising line means constant speed.</li>
<li>A steeper rising line represents greater speed.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p>An object''s distance increases from 0 m to 60 m in 10 s.</p>
<p>Speed = gradient = change in distance ÷ change in time.</p>
<p>Speed = 60 ÷ 10 = 6 m/s.</p>
<p>Always read the axis scale before calculating a gradient.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Distance–Time Graphs', 15),
  (519, 519, '<h2>Displacement–Time Graphs</h2>
<p>A displacement–time graph shows displacement from a reference point as time passes. Displacement includes direction.</p>
<ul>
<li>The gradient gives velocity.</li>
<li>A positive gradient indicates positive velocity.</li>
<li>A negative gradient indicates motion in the negative direction.</li>
<li>A horizontal line indicates zero velocity.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p>Displacement changes from 2 m to 18 m in 4 s.</p>
<p>Velocity = change in displacement ÷ time taken.</p>
<p>Velocity = (18 − 2) ÷ 4 = 4 m/s.</p>
<p>The sign of velocity depends on the chosen positive direction.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Displacement–Time Graphs', 15),
  (520, 520, '<h2>Speed–Time Graphs</h2>
<p>A speed–time graph shows how an object''s speed changes with time.</p>
<ul>
<li>The gradient gives the rate of change of speed.</li>
<li>A horizontal line means constant speed.</li>
<li>A rising line means increasing speed.</li>
<li>The area under the graph represents distance travelled.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p>Speed rises uniformly from 0 m/s to 10 m/s in 5 s.</p>
<p>Rate of change of speed = (10 − 0) ÷ 5 = 2 m/s².</p>
<p>Distance = area of triangle = ½ × 5 × 10 = 25 m.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Speed–Time Graphs', 15),
  (521, 521, '<h2>Velocity–Time Graphs</h2>
<p>A velocity–time graph shows how velocity changes with time, including direction.</p>
<ul>
<li>Gradient = acceleration.</li>
<li>Area between the graph and the time axis gives displacement, using signed area.</li>
<li>A graph above the axis represents positive velocity.</li>
<li>A graph below the axis represents negative velocity.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p>Velocity increases from 4 m/s to 16 m/s in 6 s.</p>
<p>Acceleration = (16 − 4) ÷ 6 = 2 m/s².</p>
<p>For uniform velocity of 5 m/s over 4 s, displacement = 5 × 4 = 20 m.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Velocity–Time Graphs', 15),
  (522, 522, '<h2>Interpreting Motion Graphs</h2>
<p>First identify the graph type and read its axes. Then interpret its gradient or area.</p>
<ul>
<li>Distance–time gradient: speed.</li>
<li>Displacement–time gradient: velocity.</li>
<li>Velocity–time gradient: acceleration.</li>
<li>Area under a velocity–time graph: displacement.</li>
<li>Area under a speed–time graph: distance.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p>A velocity–time graph shows velocity rising uniformly from 0 to 8 m/s over 4 s.</p>
<p>Acceleration = 8 ÷ 4 = 2 m/s².</p>
<p>Displacement = ½ × 4 × 8 = 16 m.</p>
<p>Do not confuse the gradient with the area: they answer different questions.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Interpreting Motion Graphs', 15),
  (523, 523, '<h2>Uniform Motion</h2>
<p>Uniform motion means an object covers equal distances in equal time intervals along a straight line. Its velocity is constant.</p>
<p>For uniform motion:</p>
<ul>
<li>Acceleration is zero.</li>
<li>Distance = speed × time when speed is constant.</li>
<li>A distance–time graph is a straight line with constant gradient.</li>
<li>A velocity–time graph is horizontal.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p>A train travels at a constant 20 m/s for 12 s.</p>
<p>Distance = 20 × 12 = 240 m.</p>
<p>Because velocity is constant, acceleration is 0 m/s².</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Uniform Motion', 15),
  (524, 524, '<h2>Uniform Acceleration</h2>
<p>Uniform acceleration means velocity changes by equal amounts in equal time intervals.</p>
<p>For straight-line motion with constant acceleration:</p>
<p><strong>v = u + at</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>u</strong>: initial velocity (m/s)</li>
<li><strong>v</strong>: final velocity (m/s)</li>
<li><strong>a</strong>: acceleration (m/s²)</li>
<li><strong>t</strong>: time (s)</li>
</ul>
<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p>An object starts at 2 m/s and accelerates at 3 m/s² for 4 s.</p>
<p>v = u + at = 2 + (3 × 4) = 14 m/s.</p>
<p>Choose a positive direction and use signs consistently.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Uniform Acceleration', 15),
  (525, 525, '<h2>Selecting the Correct Motion Equation</h2>
<p>For straight-line motion with constant acceleration, use the equation that contains the quantities given and the quantity required.</p>
<ul>
<li>v = u + at</li>
<li>s = ut + ½at²</li>
<li>v² = u² + 2as</li>
<li>s = ½(u + v)t</li>
</ul>
<p>Here, s is displacement, u is initial velocity, v is final velocity, a is acceleration, and t is time.</p>
<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p>A body starts from rest and accelerates at 2 m/s² for 5 s. Find displacement.</p>
<p>Known: u = 0, a = 2, t = 5. Unknown: s.</p>
<p>Choose s = ut + ½at² because it uses u, a and t.</p>
<p>s = 0 × 5 + ½ × 2 × 25 = 25 m.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Selecting the Correct Motion Equation', 15),
  (526, 526, '<h2>Solving Motion Problems</h2>
<p>Use a consistent process for motion calculations:</p>
<ol>
<li>Write down the known quantities and the unknown.</li>
<li>Convert units where necessary.</li>
<li>Choose a suitable equation for the motion.</li>
<li>Substitute values with their signs and units.</li>
<li>Calculate and check whether the answer is physically reasonable.</li>
</ol>
<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p>A car starts at 4 m/s and accelerates uniformly at 2 m/s² for 3 s. Find its displacement.</p>
<p>Known: u = 4 m/s, a = 2 m/s², t = 3 s.</p>
<p>s = ut + ½at² = (4 × 3) + (½ × 2 × 9) = 12 + 9 = 21 m.</p>
<p>Check: the average velocity is (4 + 10) ÷ 2 = 7 m/s; 7 × 3 = 21 m.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Solving Motion Problems', 15),
  (527, 527, '<h2>Free Fall</h2>
<p>Free fall is motion in which gravity is the only force acting on an object. Air resistance is neglected.</p>
<p>Near Earth''s surface, gravitational acceleration is approximately <strong>9.8 m/s²</strong>, often approximated as <strong>10 m/s²</strong> in school calculations when instructed.</p>
<ul>
<li>An object released from rest has initial velocity u = 0.</li>
<li>When upward is positive, gravitational acceleration is negative.</li>
<li>When downward is positive, gravitational acceleration is positive.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p>A stone is released from rest and falls for 2 s. Take g = 10 m/s².</p>
<p>Using v = u + at:</p>
<p>v = 0 + (10 × 2) = 20 m/s downward.</p>
<p>Using s = ut + ½at²:</p>
<p>s = 0 + ½ × 10 × 2² = 20 m downward.</p>
<hr>
<h2>What is free fall?</h2>
  <p><b>Free fall</b> is motion in which gravity is the only force acting on an object.</p>
  <p>Near Earth''s surface, an object in free fall accelerates downward because of gravity.</p>
  <p>When air resistance is negligible, objects of different masses fall with the same acceleration from the same height.</p>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>A stone is dropped from rest. Ignore air resistance. What happens to its speed as it falls?</p>
  <p>Gravity accelerates the stone downward, so its speed increases as it falls.</p>
  <p><b>Important distinction:</b> an object moving downward is not necessarily in free fall. If air resistance or another force acts significantly, gravity is not the only force acting.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Free Fall', 15),
  (528, 528, '<h2>Newton''s First Law of Motion</h2>
<p>An object remains at rest or continues moving with constant velocity in a straight line unless acted on by a resultant external force.</p>
<p>This is also called the law of inertia.</p>
<ul>
<li>Zero resultant force means zero acceleration.</li>
<li>An object at rest does not need a continuous resultant force to remain at rest.</li>
<li>An object moving at constant velocity does not need a resultant force to keep it moving at that velocity.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p>A trolley moves in a straight line at a constant 4 m/s. If the resultant force remains zero, its velocity stays at 4 m/s.</p>
<p>There is no acceleration because the resultant force is zero.</p>
<hr>
<h2>The law of inertia</h2>
  <p>Newton''s first law states that an object remains at rest or continues moving with constant velocity in a straight line unless acted on by a resultant external force.</p>
  <p>The key phrase is <b>resultant force</b>. Several forces can act on an object and still balance one another.</p>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>A book rests on a table. Its weight acts downward, while the table''s support force acts upward. If these forces balance, the resultant force is zero and the book remains at rest.</p>
  <p>An object does not need a continuous resultant force to maintain constant velocity. A resultant force is needed to change its velocity.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Newton''s First Law of Motion', 15),
  (529, 529, '<h2>Newton''s Second Law of Motion</h2>
<p>The resultant force on an object equals the rate of change of its momentum. For constant mass, this gives:</p>
<p><strong>F = ma</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>F = resultant force in newtons (N)</li>
<li>m = mass in kilograms (kg)</li>
<li>a = acceleration in m/s²</li>
</ul>
<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p>A 4 kg object accelerates at 3 m/s².</p>
<p>F = ma = 4 × 3 = 12 N.</p>
<p>Use the resultant force, not just one force acting on the object.</p>
<hr>
<h2>Force, mass and acceleration</h2>
  <p>Newton''s second law relates the resultant force on an object to its mass and acceleration.</p>
  <p>For a constant mass:</p>
  <p><b>F = ma</b></p>
  <ul>
    <li>F = resultant force in newtons (N)</li>
    <li>m = mass in kilograms (kg)</li>
    <li>a = acceleration in m/s²</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>A 4 kg trolley accelerates at 3 m/s².</p>
  <p>F = ma = 4 × 3 = <b>12 N</b>.</p>
  <p><b>Remember:</b> use the resultant force, not automatically one of the individual forces acting on the object.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Newton''s Second Law of Motion', 15),
  (530, 530, '<h2>Newton''s Third Law of Motion</h2>
<p>When object A exerts a force on object B, object B simultaneously exerts a force of equal magnitude and opposite direction on object A.</p>
<p>The action and reaction forces:</p>
<ul>
<li>Have equal magnitudes and opposite directions.</li>
<li>Act on different objects.</li>
<li>Are the same type of force.</li>
<li>Exist simultaneously.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p>A swimmer pushes water backward. The water exerts a forward force on the swimmer.</p>
<p>The forces are equal in magnitude and opposite in direction, but act on different objects.</p>
<hr>
<h2>Action and reaction</h2>
  <p>Newton''s third law states that when one object exerts a force on another object, the second object simultaneously exerts an equal-sized force in the opposite direction on the first object.</p>
  <p>The two forces:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>have equal magnitudes;</li>
    <li>act in opposite directions;</li>
    <li>act on different objects;</li>
    <li>occur at the same time.</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>When you push a wall, you exert a force on the wall. The wall exerts an equal and opposite force on you.</p>
  <p><b>Key idea:</b> action and reaction forces do not cancel each other on a single object because they act on different objects.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Newton''s Third Law of Motion', 15),
  (531, 531, '<h2>Mass and Inertia</h2>
<p>Inertia is the tendency of an object to resist a change in its state of rest or motion.</p>
<p>Mass is a measure of an object''s inertia.</p>
<ul>
<li>Mass is measured in kilograms (kg).</li>
<li>Greater mass means greater inertia.</li>
<li>Mass does not depend on location.</li>
<li>Weight is a gravitational force and can change with gravitational field strength.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p>A 10 kg trolley has more inertia than a 2 kg trolley. A greater resultant force is required to give the 10 kg trolley the same acceleration.</p>
<p>From F = ma, for the same acceleration, force increases with mass.</p>
<hr>
<h2>What is inertia?</h2>
  <p><b>Inertia</b> is the tendency of an object to resist a change in its state of rest or motion.</p>
  <p>Mass is a measure of an object''s inertia. An object with greater mass is harder to accelerate or decelerate by the same resultant force.</p>
  <h3>Worked comparison</h3>
  <p>A 2 kg trolley and a 10 kg trolley experience the same resultant force of 20 N.</p>
  <p>For the 2 kg trolley: a = 20/2 = 10 m/s².</p>
  <p>For the 10 kg trolley: a = 20/10 = 2 m/s².</p>
  <p>The more massive trolley has less acceleration because it has greater inertia.</p>
  <p><b>Do not confuse:</b> mass is measured in kilograms; weight is a force measured in newtons.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Mass and Inertia', 15),
  (532, 532, '<h2>Force, Mass and Acceleration Calculations</h2>
<p>For an object of constant mass:</p>
<p><strong>F = ma</strong></p>
<p>Rearrange depending on the unknown:</p>
<ul>
<li>Force: F = ma</li>
<li>Mass: m = F ÷ a</li>
<li>Acceleration: a = F ÷ m</li>
</ul>
<p>F must be the resultant force acting on the object.</p>
<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p>A resultant force of 18 N gives an object an acceleration of 3 m/s².</p>
<p>m = F ÷ a = 18 ÷ 3 = 6 kg.</p>
<p>Check: 6 × 3 = 18 N.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Force, Mass and Acceleration Calculations', 15),
  (533, 533, '<h2>Mass and Weight</h2>
<p>Mass measures inertia and is measured in kilograms (kg). Weight is the gravitational force acting on an object and is measured in newtons (N).</p>
<p><strong>Weight: W = mg</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>W = weight in N</li>
<li>m = mass in kg</li>
<li>g = gravitational field strength in N/kg (numerically equal to m/s²)</li>
</ul>
<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p>A 6 kg object is in a gravitational field of 10 N/kg.</p>
<p>W = mg = 6 × 10 = 60 N.</p>
<p>Mass stays the same when location changes, but weight changes with gravitational field strength.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Mass and Weight', 15),
  (534, 534, '<h2>Gravitational Field Strength</h2>
<p>Gravitational field strength at a location is the gravitational force acting per unit mass on an object placed there.</p>
<p><strong>g = W ÷ m</strong></p>
<p>Its SI unit is N/kg. Near Earth''s surface, g is approximately 9.8 N/kg, often rounded to 10 N/kg when a question instructs you to use that value.</p>
<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p>A 4 kg object has a weight of 39.2 N.</p>
<p>g = W ÷ m = 39.2 ÷ 4 = 9.8 N/kg.</p>
<p>Do not confuse gravitational field strength with the object''s mass.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Gravitational Field Strength', 15),
  (535, 535, '<h2>Calculating Weight</h2>
<p>Weight is the gravitational force acting on an object.</p>
<p><strong>W = mg</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>W = weight in newtons (N)</li>
<li>m = mass in kilograms (kg)</li>
<li>g = gravitational field strength in N/kg</li>
</ul>
<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p>Calculate the weight of a 7 kg object where g = 10 N/kg.</p>
<p>W = 7 × 10 = 70 N.</p>
<p>Always use mass in kilograms and the gravitational field strength specified in the question.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Calculating Weight', 15),
  (536, 536, '<h2>Density</h2>
<p>Density is mass per unit volume.</p>
<p><strong>Density = Mass ÷ Volume</strong></p>
<p>Common units are kg/m³ and g/cm³. The mass and volume units must be compatible.</p>
<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p>A block has mass 600 g and volume 200 cm³.</p>
<p>Density = 600 ÷ 200 = 3 g/cm³.</p>
<p>Density describes how much mass is contained in a given volume.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Density', 15),
  (537, 537, '<h2>Calculating Density</h2>
<p>Use <strong>ρ = m/V</strong>, where ρ is density, m is mass and V is volume.</p>
<p>Rearrange the formula according to the unknown:</p>
<ul>
<li>Density: ρ = m/V</li>
<li>Mass: m = ρV</li>
<li>Volume: V = m/ρ</li>
</ul>
<p>For a regularly shaped solid, volume can be calculated from its dimensions. For an irregular solid, volume can be found by liquid displacement when appropriate.</p>
<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p>A metal block has mass 540 g and volume 60 cm³.</p>
<p>ρ = 540 ÷ 60 = 9 g/cm³.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Calculating Density', 15),
  (538, 538, '<h2>Relative Density</h2>
<p>Relative density compares the density of a substance with the density of water at the same reference conditions.</p>
<p><strong>Relative density = Density of substance ÷ Density of water</strong></p>
<p>Relative density has no unit because it is a ratio of two densities expressed in compatible units.</p>
<p>For many school calculations, the density of water is taken as 1 g/cm³ or 1000 kg/m³.</p>
<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p>A liquid has density 0.8 g/cm³.</p>
<p>Relative density = 0.8 ÷ 1 = 0.8.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Relative Density', 15),
  (539, 539, '<h2>Causes of Friction</h2>
<p>Friction is a force that opposes relative motion, or the tendency of relative motion, between surfaces in contact.</p>
<p>Friction occurs because contacting surfaces have microscopic irregularities and because the surfaces can interact at their contact points.</p>
<ul>
<li>Roughness can increase resistance to sliding.</li>
<li>The force pressing surfaces together can affect friction.</li>
<li>Lubricants can reduce direct contact between surfaces.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p>A box is pushed across a rough floor. The floor exerts a frictional force opposite the box''s sliding direction.</p>
<p>If the box is pushed to the right, kinetic friction acts to the left.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Causes of Friction', 15),
  (540, 540, '<h2>Effects of Friction</h2>
<p>Friction can be useful or undesirable depending on the situation.</p>
<h3>Useful effects</h3>
<ul>
<li>It allows shoes to grip the ground when walking.</li>
<li>It helps vehicle tyres grip the road.</li>
<li>It enables brakes to slow vehicles.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Undesirable effects</h3>
<ul>
<li>It causes wear of contacting surfaces.</li>
<li>It can produce unwanted heating.</li>
<li>It wastes some useful mechanical energy through dissipation.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p>Car brakes use friction to reduce the car''s kinetic energy. Some of that energy is transferred to thermal energy in the brakes and surrounding materials.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Effects of Friction', 15),
  (541, 541, '<h2>Advantages and Disadvantages of Friction</h2>
<p>Whether friction is helpful depends on the task.</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Advantages</th><th>Disadvantages</th></tr>
<tr><td>Enables walking and gripping</td><td>Causes wear</td></tr>
<tr><td>Allows braking</td><td>Produces unwanted heat</td></tr>
<tr><td>Helps writing with a pencil</td><td>Can reduce machine efficiency</td></tr>
</table>
<p>Friction should be increased where grip is needed and reduced where unwanted resistance causes problems.</p>
<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p>Tyre tread helps provide grip on the road, while lubricating oil reduces friction between moving engine parts.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Advantages and Disadvantages of Friction', 15),
  (542, 542, '<h2>Reducing and Increasing Friction</h2>
<p>Friction can be controlled according to the purpose of the device.</p>
<h3>Ways to reduce friction</h3>
<ul>
<li>Lubricate surfaces with oil or grease.</li>
<li>Use rollers or ball bearings to replace sliding with rolling.</li>
<li>Smooth suitable contacting surfaces.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Ways to increase friction</h3>
<ul>
<li>Use rough or patterned surfaces where grip is needed.</li>
<li>Use tyre tread and suitable shoe soles.</li>
<li>Apply brakes to create frictional resistance.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p>Ball bearings in a wheel reduce resistance by allowing rolling between surfaces instead of direct sliding.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Reducing and Increasing Friction', 15),
  (543, 543, '<h2>Air Resistance and Terminal Velocity</h2>
<p>Air resistance is a force that opposes an object''s motion relative to air. It depends on factors including speed, shape and exposed area.</p>
<p>When an object falls:</p>
<ol>
<li>At first, its weight acts downward and air resistance is small.</li>
<li>As its speed increases, air resistance generally increases.</li>
<li>The resultant downward force decreases.</li>
<li>When air resistance equals weight, the resultant force becomes zero.</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>Terminal velocity</strong> is the constant velocity reached when the resultant force is zero.</p>
<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p>A falling object has weight 40 N downward and air resistance 40 N upward.</p>
<p>Resultant force = 40 − 40 = 0 N.</p>
<p>Its acceleration is zero. If it is already moving, it continues at constant velocity: terminal velocity.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Air Resistance and Terminal Velocity', 15),
  (544, 544, '<h2>The value of g</h2>
  <p>Near Earth''s surface, freely falling objects accelerate downward at approximately <b>9.8 m/s²</b>. In many school calculations, use <b>g = 10 m/s²</b> when instructed.</p>
  <p>Acceleration describes the change in velocity per unit time.</p>
  <p><b>a = (v − u)/t</b></p>
  <ul>
    <li>u = initial velocity</li>
    <li>v = final velocity</li>
    <li>t = time taken</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>An object starts from rest and falls freely for 3 s. Take g = 10 m/s².</p>
  <p>v = u + gt</p>
  <p>v = 0 + 10 × 3 = <b>30 m/s downward</b>.</p>
  <p><b>Watch the units:</b> acceleration is measured in m/s², not m/s.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Acceleration Due to Gravity', 15),
  (545, 545, '<h2>Find the net force first</h2>
  <p>When forces act in opposite directions, subtract the smaller force from the larger force to find the resultant force.</p>
  <p>Then use <b>a = F/m</b>.</p>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>A 4 kg trolley is pulled to the right by 20 N. Friction acts to the left with a force of 8 N.</p>
  <p>Resultant force = 20 − 8 = 12 N to the right.</p>
  <p>Acceleration = 12 ÷ 4 = <b>3 m/s² to the right</b>.</p>
  <p><b>Common error:</b> using 20 N as the resultant force while ignoring friction.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Calculating Acceleration from Resultant Force', 15),
  (546, 546, '<h2>Rearranging Newton''s second law</h2>
  <p>From <b>F = ma</b>, two useful forms are:</p>
  <p><b>m = F/a</b></p>
  <p><b>a = F/m</b></p>
  <p>Use these relationships when the force, mass or acceleration is unknown.</p>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>A resultant force of 36 N produces an acceleration of 4 m/s².</p>
  <p>m = F/a = 36/4 = <b>9 kg</b>.</p>
  <p>Check the units: N divided by m/s² gives kg.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Calculating Force and Mass', 15),
  (547, 547, '<h2>Recognising a force pair</h2>
  <p>To identify a Newton''s third-law pair, name both interacting objects and the force each exerts on the other.</p>
  <h3>Example: a book on a table</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>The book pushes downward on the table.</li>
    <li>The table pushes upward on the book.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>These are an action-reaction pair. However, the upward support force and the book''s downward weight are <b>not</b> a third-law pair: both act on the book.</p>
  <p>The reaction to Earth''s gravitational force on the book is the book''s gravitational force on Earth.</p>
  <p><b>Diagnostic rule:</b> if both forces act on the same object, they are not a Newton''s third-law pair.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Action-Reaction Force Pairs', 15),
  (548, 548, '<h2>Inertia in everyday life</h2>
  <p>Inertia helps explain everyday events involving sudden changes in motion.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Braking:</b> when a vehicle stops suddenly, passengers tend to continue moving forward.</li>
    <li><b>Starting:</b> when a bus accelerates forward, passengers may appear to lean backward relative to the bus.</li>
    <li><b>Seat belts:</b> they help restrain occupants during rapid deceleration.</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Worked explanation</h3>
  <p>A moving car brakes suddenly. The car''s speed decreases, but a passenger''s body tends to continue moving forward. A seat belt exerts a force that helps slow the passenger with the car.</p>
  <p><b>Key idea:</b> inertia is not an extra forward force. It is the tendency of the body to resist the change in its motion.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Inertia and Safety', 15),
  (549, 549, '<h2>Forces acting in the same direction</h2>
  <p>When forces act on the same object in the same direction, their magnitudes are added to find the resultant force.</p>
  <p><b>R = F₁ + F₂</b></p>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>Two people push a trolley forward with forces of 30 N and 20 N.</p>
  <p>R = 30 + 20 = <b>50 N forward</b>.</p>
  <p>If the object has mass 10 kg, its acceleration is:</p>
  <p>a = R/m = 50/10 = <b>5 m/s² forward</b>.</p>
  <p><b>Remember:</b> add forces only after confirming that they act in the same direction.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Resultant Force in the Same Direction', 15),
  (550, 550, '<h2>Opposing forces</h2>
  <p>When two forces act in opposite directions along the same straight line, subtract the smaller magnitude from the larger.</p>
  <p><b>Resultant magnitude = larger force − smaller force</b></p>
  <p>The resultant acts in the direction of the larger force.</p>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>A rope is pulled eastward with 70 N and westward with 45 N.</p>
  <p>R = 70 − 45 = <b>25 N eastward</b>.</p>
  <p>If both forces were equal, the resultant would be zero.</p>
  <p><b>Check:</b> state both the magnitude and direction of the resultant.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Resultant Force in Opposite Directions', 15),
  (551, 551, '<h2>Support from a surface</h2>
  <p>The <b>normal reaction</b> is the contact force a surface exerts on an object. It acts perpendicular to the contact surface.</p>
  <p>For a stationary object on a horizontal surface with no other vertical forces, the normal reaction balances the object''s weight.</p>
  <p><b>R = mg</b> in that specific situation.</p>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>A 4 kg box rests on a horizontal floor. Take g = 10 N/kg.</p>
  <p>Weight = mg = 4 × 10 = 40 N downward.</p>
  <p>Since the box is vertically balanced, the normal reaction is <b>40 N upward</b>.</p>
  <p><b>Important:</b> the normal reaction is not always equal to weight. Other forces or acceleration can change its value.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for The Normal Reaction Force', 15),
  (552, 552, '<h2>What is tension?</h2>
  <p><b>Tension</b> is the pulling force transmitted through a taut string, rope or cable.</p>
  <p>Tension acts along the string and pulls on the objects attached to its ends.</p>
  <h3>Worked example: a hanging mass</h3>
  <p>A 2 kg mass hangs at rest from a light vertical string. Take g = 10 N/kg.</p>
  <p>Weight = mg = 2 × 10 = 20 N downward.</p>
  <p>Because the mass is at rest, the upward tension balances the weight:</p>
  <p><b>T = 20 N</b>.</p>
  <p><b>Important:</b> tension does not always equal weight. That equality applies here because the mass is stationary and the only forces are tension and weight.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Tension in a String', 15),
  (553, 553, '<h2>Representing forces</h2>
  <p>A force diagram represents the forces acting on one chosen object using arrows.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>The arrow direction shows the force direction.</li>
    <li>The arrow length can represent the force magnitude when drawn to scale.</li>
    <li>Label each arrow with the force name or value.</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Worked example: a box on a floor</h3>
  <p>A box rests on a horizontal floor.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Weight acts downward.</li>
    <li>Normal reaction acts upward.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>If the box is stationary and no other vertical forces act, the two forces are equal in magnitude.</p>
  <p><b>Rule:</b> draw only forces acting on the chosen object. Do not draw the force it exerts on another object as if that force acted on the chosen object.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Drawing Force Diagrams', 15),
  (554, 554, '<h2>Apparent weight in a lift</h2>
  <p>A person standing on a scale in an elevator experiences weight downward and a normal reaction upward. The scale reading is the normal reaction.</p>
  <p>When the elevator is stationary or moving at constant velocity, the forces balance:</p>
  <p><b>R = mg</b></p>
  <p>If the elevator accelerates upward, the normal reaction is greater than weight. If it accelerates downward, the normal reaction is less than weight.</p>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>A 50 kg person stands in an elevator accelerating upward at 2 m/s². Take g = 10 m/s².</p>
  <p>R − mg = ma</p>
  <p>R = m(g + a) = 50(10 + 2) = <b>600 N</b>.</p>
  <p>This is an application of Newton''s second law, not a change in the person''s mass.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Forces on an Object in an Elevator', 15),
  (555, 555, '<h2>Force during braking</h2>
  <p>When brakes slow a moving vehicle, the resultant force acts opposite its direction of motion. The vehicle therefore decelerates.</p>
  <p>Using Newton''s second law, the magnitude of the resultant force is:</p>
  <p><b>F = ma</b></p>
  <p>Here, use the magnitude of the deceleration when calculating the magnitude of the braking force.</p>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>A 1,000 kg car slows at 3 m/s² on a level road. Assume the stated deceleration is caused by the resultant braking force.</p>
  <p>F = ma = 1,000 × 3 = <b>3,000 N</b>, opposite the motion.</p>
  <p><b>Distinguish:</b> deceleration is acceleration opposite the current velocity; it is not a separate kind of force.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Braking Force and Deceleration', 15),
  (556, 556, '<h2>Momentum</h2>
  <p>Momentum is the product of an object''s mass and velocity.</p>
  <p><b>p = mv</b></p>
  <ul>
    <li>p = momentum in kg m/s</li>
    <li>m = mass in kg</li>
    <li>v = velocity in m/s</li>
  </ul>
  <p>A resultant force changes an object''s momentum. For constant mass and a constant force, Newton''s second law can be expressed as:</p>
  <p><b>F = (change in momentum)/(time taken)</b></p>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>A 2 kg trolley moves at 3 m/s eastward.</p>
  <p>p = mv = 2 × 3 = <b>6 kg m/s eastward</b>.</p>
  <p><b>Note:</b> velocity has direction, so momentum also has direction.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Force and Change in Momentum', 15),
  (557, 557, '<h2>What is impulse?</h2>
  <p>Impulse is the change in momentum produced by a force acting over a time interval.</p>
  <p>For a constant resultant force:</p>
  <p><b>Impulse = F × t = change in momentum</b></p>
  <p>The SI unit is N s, equivalent to kg m/s.</p>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>A resultant force of 20 N acts on a ball for 0.5 s.</p>
  <p>Impulse = 20 × 0.5 = <b>10 N s</b>.</p>
  <p>This means the ball''s momentum changes by 10 kg m/s in the direction of the resultant force.</p>
  <p><b>Safety application:</b> airbags and padded surfaces increase the time over which a person''s momentum changes, reducing the average force for the same change in momentum.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Impulse and Impact Forces', 15),
  (558, 558, '<h2>Momentum in an isolated system</h2>
  <p>The principle of conservation of momentum states that the total momentum of a system remains constant when the resultant external force on the system is zero.</p>
  <p>For two objects moving along one straight line:</p>
  <p><b>Total momentum before interaction = total momentum after interaction</b></p>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>A 2 kg trolley moves at 4 m/s and collides with a stationary 2 kg trolley. They stick together.</p>
  <p>Initial momentum = (2 × 4) + (2 × 0) = 8 kg m/s.</p>
  <p>Combined mass = 2 + 2 = 4 kg.</p>
  <p>Final velocity = 8/4 = <b>2 m/s</b> in the original direction.</p>
  <p><b>Important:</b> momentum is conserved for the system when external forces have negligible resultant impulse during the interaction.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Conservation of Momentum', 15),
  (559, 559, '<h2>Energy of a moving object</h2>
  <p>Kinetic energy is the energy an object possesses because it is moving.</p>
  <p><b>KE = ½mv²</b></p>
  <ul>
    <li>KE = kinetic energy in joules (J)</li>
    <li>m = mass in kilograms (kg)</li>
    <li>v = speed in metres per second (m/s)</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>A 4 kg object moves at 3 m/s.</p>
  <p>KE = ½ × 4 × 3²</p>
  <p>KE = 2 × 9 = <b>18 J</b>.</p>
  <p><b>Common mistake:</b> square the speed before multiplying by half the mass.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Kinetic Energy', 15),
  (560, 560, '<h2>Energy stored by deformation</h2>
  <p>An elastic object can store elastic potential energy when it is stretched or compressed, provided it behaves elastically.</p>
  <p>For a spring obeying Hooke''s law:</p>
  <p><b>E = ½Fx = ½kx²</b></p>
  <ul>
    <li>E = elastic potential energy in joules</li>
    <li>F = final force in newtons</li>
    <li>x = extension or compression in metres</li>
    <li>k = spring constant in N/m</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>A spring has a spring constant of 200 N/m and is extended by 0.1 m.</p>
  <p>E = ½ × 200 × 0.1²</p>
  <p>E = 100 × 0.01 = <b>1 J</b>.</p>
  <p>This formula applies while the spring follows Hooke''s law.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Elastic Potential Energy', 15),
  (561, 561, '<h2>Energy cannot be created or destroyed</h2>
  <p>The principle of conservation of energy states that energy cannot be created or destroyed; it can only be transferred or transformed from one form to another.</p>
  <p>In an isolated system, the total energy remains constant.</p>
  <h3>Worked example: a falling object</h3>
  <p>A ball is held above the ground. It has gravitational potential energy because of its position.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Before falling: most of the relevant mechanical energy is gravitational potential energy.</li>
    <li>During the fall: gravitational potential energy decreases while kinetic energy increases.</li>
    <li>If air resistance is negligible: the loss in gravitational potential energy equals the gain in kinetic energy.</li>
  </ul>
  <p><b>Important:</b> when friction or air resistance acts, some mechanical energy is transferred to thermal energy and sound. Total energy is still conserved.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Conservation of Energy', 15),
  (562, 562, '<h2>Changing from one form to another</h2>
  <p>An energy transformation occurs when energy changes from one form to another.</p>
  <h3>Examples</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Electric lamp: electrical energy → light and thermal energy.</li>
    <li>Electric motor: electrical energy → kinetic energy and thermal energy.</li>
    <li>Battery-powered torch: chemical energy → electrical energy → light and thermal energy.</li>
    <li>Falling object: gravitational potential energy → kinetic energy.</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>In an electric fan, electrical energy is transferred mainly into the kinetic energy of the rotating blades, with some energy becoming sound and thermal energy.</p>
  <p><b>Remember:</b> identify the input energy and the useful output, then account for other outputs where relevant.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Energy Transformations', 15),
  (563, 563, '<h2>Rate of doing work</h2>
  <p>Power is the rate at which work is done or energy is transferred.</p>
  <p><b>P = W/t</b></p>
  <p>Power can also be calculated from energy transferred:</p>
  <p><b>P = E/t</b></p>
  <ul>
    <li>P = power in watts (W)</li>
    <li>W = work done in joules (J)</li>
    <li>E = energy transferred in joules (J)</li>
    <li>t = time in seconds (s)</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>A machine does 600 J of work in 20 s.</p>
  <p>P = W/t = 600/20 = <b>30 W</b>.</p>
  <p><b>Distinguish:</b> work tells you how much energy is transferred; power tells you how quickly it is transferred.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Power', 15),
  (564, 564, '<h2>Useful output compared with input</h2>
  <p>Efficiency describes what fraction of the input energy or work becomes useful output energy or work.</p>
  <p><b>Efficiency = (useful output energy ÷ input energy) × 100%</b></p>
  <p>The same ratio can be used with useful output work and input work when comparing work.</p>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>A machine receives 500 J of energy and transfers 350 J as useful output energy.</p>
  <p>Efficiency = (350/500) × 100%</p>
  <p>Efficiency = <b>70%</b>.</p>
  <p>The remaining 150 J is transferred into other forms, such as thermal energy or sound.</p>
  <p><b>Important:</b> for a real machine, efficiency is less than 100% when some input energy is not converted into the defined useful output.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Efficiency', 15),
  (565, 565, '<h2>Force multiplication</h2>
  <p>Mechanical advantage (MA) compares the load moved by a machine with the effort applied.</p>
  <p><b>MA = load force ÷ effort force</b></p>
  <p>Mechanical advantage has no unit because it is a ratio of two forces.</p>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>A machine lifts a load of 600 N using an effort of 150 N.</p>
  <p>MA = 600/150 = <b>4</b>.</p>
  <p>The machine''s mechanical advantage is 4: the load force is four times the effort force.</p>
  <p><b>Important:</b> mechanical advantage does not by itself tell you the machine''s efficiency.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Mechanical Advantage', 15),
  (566, 566, '<h2>Distance moved by effort and load</h2>
  <p>Velocity ratio (VR) compares the distance moved by the effort with the distance moved by the load in the same time interval.</p>
  <p><b>VR = distance moved by effort ÷ distance moved by load</b></p>
  <p>For a simple ideal machine, this ratio describes the relationship between the input and output distances. It has no unit.</p>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>The effort moves 2 m while the load rises 0.5 m.</p>
  <p>VR = 2/0.5 = <b>4</b>.</p>
  <p>The effort travels four times as far as the load.</p>
  <p><b>Do not confuse:</b> velocity ratio compares distances; mechanical advantage compares forces.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Velocity Ratio', 15),
  (567, 567, '<h2>Connecting mechanical advantage and velocity ratio</h2>
  <p>For a machine, efficiency can be calculated using mechanical advantage and velocity ratio:</p>
  <p><b>Efficiency = (MA/VR) × 100%</b></p>
  <p>Here:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>MA = load force ÷ effort force</li>
    <li>VR = effort distance ÷ load distance</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>A machine has MA = 3 and VR = 4.</p>
  <p>Efficiency = (3/4) × 100% = <b>75%</b>.</p>
  <p>For an ordinary real machine, MA is typically less than VR because some input energy is dissipated into unwanted forms.</p>
  <p><b>Check:</b> calculate MA and VR from the correct quantities before applying the efficiency formula.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Efficiency of a Machine Using MA and VR', 15),
  (568, 568, '<h2>How a lever works</h2>
  <p>A lever is a rigid bar that turns about a fixed point called the fulcrum or pivot.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Fulcrum: the pivot about which the lever turns.</li>
    <li>Effort: the force applied to the lever.</li>
    <li>Load: the force or resistance the lever moves.</li>
    <li>Effort arm: perpendicular distance from the pivot to the effort''s line of action.</li>
    <li>Load arm: perpendicular distance from the pivot to the load''s line of action.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>For a lever in equilibrium, the principle of moments gives:</p>
  <p><b>Effort × effort arm = load × load arm</b></p>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>A 200 N load acts 0.2 m from the pivot. The effort acts 0.8 m from the pivot.</p>
  <p>Effort × 0.8 = 200 × 0.2</p>
  <p>Effort = 40/0.8 = <b>50 N</b>.</p>
  <p>This calculation assumes the lever is in equilibrium and the distances are measured perpendicular to the forces.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Levers and Their Turning Effect', 15),
  (569, 569, '<h2>Meaning of a Wave</h2>

<h3>Core idea</h3>
<p>
A <b>wave</b> is a disturbance that transfers energy
from one place to another.
</p>

<p>
When a stone falls into still water, ripples spread
out from the point where the stone enters. The
disturbance travels across the water, carrying energy
away from the point of impact.
</p>

<h3>Worked example 1</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> A ripple moves across a pond after
a stone falls into it. What is travelling across the pond?</p>
<p><b>Reasoning:</b> The ripple is the travelling
disturbance. It transfers energy away from the point
where the stone entered the water.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> A wave disturbance carrying energy.</p>

<h3>Worked example 2</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> A loudspeaker produces sound that
reaches a learner across a classroom. What does the
sound wave transfer?</p>
<p><b>Reasoning:</b> The sound wave travels through
the air and transfers energy from the vibrating
loudspeaker to the learner''s ears.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> Energy.</p>

<h3>Check your understanding</h3>
<p>A disturbance travels along a rope after one end
is moved. What is the disturbance an example of?</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Meaning of a Wave', 15),
  (570, 570, '<h2>Energy Transfer by Waves</h2>

<h3>Core idea</h3>
<p>
A wave transfers <b>energy</b> from one place to
another. In a material medium, particles generally
oscillate about their equilibrium positions rather
than travelling permanently with the wave.
</p>

<p>
Imagine a floating cork on water. As ripples pass,
the cork moves up and down, but it does not travel
all the way across the pond with each ripple.
</p>

<h3>Worked example 1</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> Ripples pass a floating cork. The
cork bobs up and down. What does this show?</p>
<p><b>Reasoning:</b> The cork responds to the passing
disturbance, but it is not carried across the pond
by the ripple.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> The wave transfers energy while the
cork oscillates near its position.</p>

<h3>Worked example 2</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> A rope pulse reaches a loose ribbon
tied to the rope. Has the ribbon travelled along the
rope with the pulse?</p>
<p><b>Reasoning:</b> The ribbon moves as the pulse
passes. The pulse travels along the rope, while the
ribbon does not travel with it from end to end.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> No. The pulse transfers energy along
the rope while the ribbon moves locally.</p>

<h3>Check your understanding</h3>
<p>A water wave reaches a floating leaf. Which
statement best describes the transfer?</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Energy Transfer by Waves', 15),
  (571, 571, '<h2>Transverse Waves</h2>

<h3>Core idea</h3>
<p>
In a <b>transverse wave</b>, the oscillation of the
medium is perpendicular to the direction in which
the wave travels.
</p>

<p>
Perpendicular means that the two directions are
at right angles to each other.
</p>

<p>
For example, if a pulse travels horizontally along
a rope while a point on the rope moves up and down,
the motion is transverse.
</p>

<h3>Worked example 1</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> A wave travels from left to right
along a rope. A point on the rope moves up and down.
Classify the wave.</p>
<p><b>Reasoning:</b> The wave travels horizontally
while the point moves vertically. These directions
are perpendicular.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> A transverse wave.</p>

<h3>Worked example 2</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> A learner moves one end of a rope
up and down, producing a pulse that travels along
the rope. How does the rope move relative to the
direction of wave travel?</p>
<p><b>Reasoning:</b> The rope moves vertically while
the pulse travels horizontally.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> The rope moves perpendicular to
the direction of wave travel.</p>

<h3>Check your understanding</h3>
<p>A wave travels horizontally while the medium
oscillates vertically. What type of wave is it?</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Transverse Waves', 15),
  (572, 572, '<h2>Longitudinal Waves</h2>

<h3>Core idea</h3>
<p>
In a <b>longitudinal wave</b>, the particles of the
medium oscillate parallel to the direction in which
the wave travels.
</p>

<p>
Parallel means that the directions lie along the
same line. The particles move backwards and forwards
as the disturbance travels through the medium.
</p>

<p>
Sound travelling through air is a common example
of a longitudinal wave.
</p>

<h3>Worked example 1</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> Sound travels through air from a
loudspeaker to a listener. The air particles vibrate
backwards and forwards along the direction of travel.
Classify the wave.</p>
<p><b>Reasoning:</b> The particle oscillation is
parallel to the direction of wave travel.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> A longitudinal wave.</p>

<h3>Worked example 2</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> A slinky is pushed and pulled
along its length, producing a travelling pulse.
What type of wave is produced?</p>
<p><b>Reasoning:</b> The coils move back and forth
along the same direction in which the pulse travels.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> A longitudinal wave.</p>

<h3>Check your understanding</h3>
<p>A disturbance travels along a spring while its
coils oscillate back and forth along the spring.
Classify the wave.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Longitudinal Waves', 15),
  (573, 573, '<h2>Particle Motion in a Medium</h2>

<h3>Core idea</h3>
<p>
When a mechanical wave passes through a medium,
its particles oscillate about their
<b>equilibrium positions</b>. An equilibrium
position is the particle''s undisturbed position.
</p>

<p>
The particles interact with neighbouring particles,
allowing the disturbance and energy to travel through
the medium. The particles do not generally travel
all the way from the source to the receiver with
the wave.
</p>

<h3>Worked example 1</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> A sound wave travels through a
classroom. Does an air particle near the door travel
all the way to a listener''s ear?</p>
<p><b>Reasoning:</b> The air particle oscillates around
its equilibrium position as the sound disturbance
passes. The disturbance travels across the classroom.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> No. The particle oscillates locally;
the sound wave transfers energy across the room.</p>

<h3>Worked example 2</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> A pulse travels along a rope.
A marked point moves away from and then returns
towards its original position. What does this
illustrate?</p>
<p><b>Reasoning:</b> The point moves temporarily as
the pulse passes, rather than travelling permanently
along the rope.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> Local oscillation about an
equilibrium position.</p>

<h3>Check your understanding</h3>
<p>As a sound wave passes through air, what do the
air particles do?</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Particle Motion in a Medium', 15),
  (574, 574, '<h2>Amplitude</h2>

  <h3>Meaning</h3>
  <p>Amplitude is the <b>maximum displacement of a particle from its equilibrium position</b>.</p>

  <p>On a transverse wave, measure amplitude vertically from the equilibrium line to a crest or to a trough. Do not measure from crest to trough.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 1</h3>
  <p>A wave has a crest 4 cm above its equilibrium position. Find its amplitude.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify the maximum displacement from equilibrium.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> The displacement is 4 cm.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Amplitude = 4 cm.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2</h3>
  <p>The vertical distance from a crest to the adjacent trough is 12 cm. Find the amplitude of the wave.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Crest-to-trough distance = 2 × amplitude.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> 12 = 2A</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> A = 12 ÷ 2 = 6 cm.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Amplitude = 6 cm.</p>

  <h3>Remember</h3>
  <p>Amplitude is measured from equilibrium to a crest or trough, not from crest to trough.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Amplitude', 15),
  (575, 575, '<h2>Wavelength</h2>

  <h3>Meaning</h3>
  <p>Wavelength is the distance between two successive points on a wave that are in the same phase.</p>

  <p>Examples include:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>One crest to the next crest.</li>
    <li>One trough to the next trough.</li>
    <li>One compression to the next compression in a longitudinal wave.</li>
    <li>One rarefaction to the next rarefaction in a longitudinal wave.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>The symbol for wavelength is <b>λ</b> (lambda). Its SI unit is the metre (m).</p>

  <h3>Worked example 1</h3>
  <p>The distance between two successive crests is 0.8 m. Find the wavelength.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Successive crests are one wavelength apart.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> λ = 0.8 m.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2</h3>
  <p>Six complete wavelengths occupy a distance of 12 m. Find the wavelength.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Total distance = number of wavelengths × wavelength.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> 12 = 6λ.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> λ = 12 ÷ 6 = 2 m.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Wavelength = 2 m.</p>

  <h3>Remember</h3>
  <p>Count complete wavelengths, not the number of crests alone. Two successive crests are separated by one wavelength.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Wavelength', 15),
  (576, 576, '<h2>Frequency</h2>

  <h3>Meaning</h3>
  <p>Frequency is the <b>number of complete oscillations or wave cycles produced per second</b>.</p>

  <p>Its symbol is <b>f</b>. Its SI unit is the <b>hertz (Hz)</b>.</p>
  <p>1 Hz means one complete oscillation per second.</p>

  <h3>Formula</h3>
  <p><b>Frequency = Number of complete oscillations ÷ Time taken</b></p>
  <p>f = N/t</p>
  <p>Where N is the number of complete oscillations and t is the time in seconds.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 1</h3>
  <p>A source makes 20 complete oscillations in 5 seconds. Find its frequency.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> N = 20, t = 5 s.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> f = N/t.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> f = 20/5 = 4 Hz.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Frequency = 4 Hz.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2</h3>
  <p>A tuning fork vibrates at 50 Hz for 4 seconds. How many complete oscillations does it make?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> f = N/t, so N = ft.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> N = 50 × 4.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> 200 complete oscillations.</p>

  <h3>Remember</h3>
  <p>Frequency tells you how many cycles occur each second. It is not the total number of cycles unless the time is one second.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Frequency', 15),
  (577, 577, '<h2>Period of a Wave</h2>

  <h3>Meaning</h3>
  <p>The period is the <b>time taken to complete one full oscillation</b>.</p>

  <p>Its symbol is <b>T</b>, and its SI unit is the second (s).</p>

  <h3>Formula</h3>
  <p><b>Period = Total time ÷ Number of complete oscillations</b></p>
  <p>T = t/N</p>

  <p>Frequency and period are related by:</p>
  <p><b>T = 1/f</b></p>
  <p><b>f = 1/T</b></p>

  <h3>Worked example 1</h3>
  <p>A source makes 10 complete oscillations in 5 seconds. Find its period.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Total time = 5 s; number of oscillations = 10.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> T = t/N = 5/10.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> T = 0.5 s.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2</h3>
  <p>A wave has a frequency of 20 Hz. Calculate its period.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> T = 1/f.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> T = 1/20.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> T = 0.05 s.</p>

  <h3>Remember</h3>
  <p>Frequency and period are inversely proportional. A higher frequency means a shorter period.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Period of a Wave', 15),
  (578, 578, '<h2>Wave Speed</h2>

  <h3>Meaning</h3>
  <p>Wave speed is the distance travelled by a wave per unit time.</p>

  <p>Its SI unit is metres per second (m/s).</p>

  <h3>Formula</h3>
  <p><b>Speed = Distance ÷ Time</b></p>
  <p>For a periodic wave:</p>
  <p><b>v = fλ</b></p>

  <p>Where:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>v = wave speed in m/s</li>
    <li>f = frequency in Hz</li>
    <li>λ = wavelength in m</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Worked example 1</h3>
  <p>A wave has a frequency of 5 Hz and a wavelength of 2 m. Calculate its speed.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> v = fλ.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> v = 5 × 2.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> v = 10 m/s.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2</h3>
  <p>A wave travels 24 m in 6 seconds. Calculate its speed.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> v = distance ÷ time.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> v = 24/6.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> v = 4 m/s.</p>

  <h3>Remember</h3>
  <p>Use v = fλ when frequency and wavelength are known. Use distance ÷ time when the distance travelled and time taken are known.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Wave Speed', 15),
  (579, 579, '<h2>Reflection of Waves</h2>

<h3>Core idea</h3>
<p>
Reflection occurs when a wave reaches a boundary and
returns into the medium from which it came.
</p>

<p>
For example, a pulse travelling along a rope can
reach a fixed end and return along the rope. The
returning pulse is a reflected wave.
</p>

<h3>Worked example 1</h3>
<p>
A water wave reaches a wall and travels back across
the water. Identify the phenomenon.
</p>
<p>
<b>Reasoning:</b> The wave returns after reaching
the boundary.
</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> Reflection.</p>

<h3>Worked example 2</h3>
<p>
A sound wave strikes a distant cliff and returns
to the listener. What has happened to the sound?
</p>
<p>
The sound wave has been reflected by the cliff.
The returning sound may be heard as an echo.
</p>

<h3>Remember</h3>
<p>
Reflection means a wave returns from a boundary.
It does not mean the wave has necessarily been
completely absorbed or destroyed.
</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Reflection of Waves', 15),
  (580, 580, '<h2>Direction of a Reflected Wave</h2>

<h3>Core idea</h3>
<p>
For reflection at a plane boundary, angles are
measured from the <b>normal</b>, an imaginary line
perpendicular to the boundary at the point of
incidence.
</p>

<p>
The law of reflection states that the angle of
incidence equals the angle of reflection.
</p>

<p><b>i = r</b></p>

<p>
The angle of incidence is measured between the
incident wave''s direction and the normal. The angle
of reflection is measured between the reflected
wave''s direction and the normal.
</p>

<h3>Worked example 1</h3>
<p>
A wave strikes a reflecting boundary at an angle
of incidence of 35° to the normal. Find the angle
of reflection.
</p>
<p>
<b>Step 1:</b> Apply the law of reflection: i = r.
</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> r = 35°.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> 35°.</p>

<h3>Worked example 2</h3>
<p>
A ray makes an angle of 20° with the reflecting
surface. Find its angle of incidence.
</p>
<p>
The normal is perpendicular to the surface.
Therefore, the angle with the normal is
90° − 20° = 70°.
</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> Angle of incidence = 70°.</p>

<h3>Remember</h3>
<p>
Measure the angle from the normal, not from the
surface. This law applies to specular reflection
at a plane boundary.
</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Direction of a Reflected Wave', 15),
  (581, 581, '<h2>Refraction of Waves</h2>

<h3>Core idea</h3>
<p>
Refraction is the change in direction of a wave
when it enters a region where its speed changes
and it meets the boundary at an angle.
</p>

<p>
When a water wave enters shallow water at an angle,
its speed changes. One part of the wavefront changes
speed before another part, causing the wavefront
to turn.
</p>

<p>
A wave entering a new region straight on may change
speed without changing direction.
</p>

<h3>Worked example 1</h3>
<p>
Water waves enter a shallow region at an angle and
change direction. Identify the phenomenon.
</p>
<p>
<b>Reasoning:</b> Their speed changes and their
direction changes at the boundary.
</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> Refraction.</p>

<h3>Worked example 2</h3>
<p>
A wave enters a new region perpendicular to the
boundary. Its speed changes, but it continues in
the same direction. Has its speed changed?
</p>
<p>
Yes. A change in speed does not always produce
a change in direction. The wave can still undergo
a change of propagation speed without bending.
</p>

<h3>Remember</h3>
<p>
Refraction does not mean reflection. During
refraction, a wave enters a different region.
For a periodic wave crossing a stationary boundary,
its frequency remains unchanged while its speed
and wavelength may change.
</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Refraction of Waves', 15),
  (582, 582, '<h2>Diffraction of Waves</h2>

<h3>Core idea</h3>
<p>
Diffraction is the spreading of waves as they pass
through an opening or around an obstacle.
</p>

<p>
Diffraction can explain why sound may be heard
around a doorway even when the source is not
directly visible.
</p>

<h3>Worked example 1</h3>
<p>
Sound from a classroom is heard in a corridor
around an open doorway. Which wave behaviour helps
explain this?
</p>
<p>
<b>Reasoning:</b> Sound spreads through the opening
and into the region beyond it.
</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> Diffraction.</p>

<h3>Worked example 2</h3>
<p>
Water waves pass through a narrow opening in a
barrier and spread into the region beyond it.
Identify the phenomenon.
</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> Diffraction.</p>

<h3>Remember</h3>
<p>
Diffraction does not require a wave to bounce back
from a boundary. It describes the spreading of a
wave through an opening or around an obstacle.
</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Diffraction of Waves', 15),
  (583, 583, '<h2>Diffraction and Gap Size</h2>

<h3>Core idea</h3>
<p>
Diffraction is most noticeable when the width of an
opening is comparable to the wavelength.
</p>

<p>
When the gap is much wider than the wavelength,
spreading is generally less noticeable. When the
gap is similar to the wavelength, spreading is
more pronounced.
</p>

<h3>Worked example 1</h3>
<p>
Water waves have a wavelength of 5 cm and pass
through a gap 5 cm wide. Will diffraction be
noticeable?
</p>
<p>
The gap width is comparable to the wavelength,
so significant spreading is expected.
</p>

<h3>Worked example 2</h3>
<p>
Waves with a wavelength of 2 cm pass through a
gap 50 cm wide. Compare the gap width with the
wavelength.
</p>
<p>
The gap is much wider than the wavelength, so
spreading is generally less pronounced.
</p>

<h3>Remember</h3>
<p>
Compare the gap width with the wavelength. Do not
judge diffraction from gap width alone when the
wavelength is unknown.
</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Diffraction and Gap Size', 15),
  (584, 584, '<h2>Superposition of Waves</h2>

<h3>Core idea</h3>
<p>
When two or more waves overlap, the resultant
displacement at a point is the algebraic sum of
the individual displacements at that point.
This is the principle of superposition.
</p>

<p>
For displacements in the same direction, add their
values. For displacements in opposite directions,
use their signs when adding.
</p>

<h3>Worked example 1</h3>
<p>
Two pulses produce upward displacements of 2 cm
and 3 cm at the same point at the same time.
Find the resultant displacement.
</p>
<p>
<b>Step 1:</b> Both displacements are upward.
</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Resultant = 2 + 3 = 5 cm upward.</p>

<h3>Worked example 2</h3>
<p>
One pulse produces an upward displacement of 5 cm
and another produces a downward displacement of
2 cm at the same point. Find the resultant.
</p>
<p>
Take upward as positive and downward as negative.
Resultant = +5 + (−2) = +3 cm.
</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> 3 cm upward.</p>

<h3>Remember</h3>
<p>
Add signed displacements, not just their magnitudes.
The result depends on the direction of each
displacement at the point of overlap.
</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Superposition of Waves', 15),
  (585, 585, '<h2>Constructive Interference</h2>

<h3>Core idea</h3>
<p>
Constructive interference occurs when overlapping
waves reinforce one another, producing a resultant
displacement greater than either individual
displacement when both are in the same direction.
</p>

<p>
For two waves of equal frequency arriving in phase,
their crests align with crests and their troughs
align with troughs. Their amplitudes add at points
of complete constructive interference.
</p>

<h3>Worked example 1</h3>
<p>
Two upward pulses each have a displacement of 3 cm
at the same point. Find the resultant displacement.
</p>
<p>
Resultant = 3 + 3 = 6 cm upward.
</p>

<h3>Worked example 2</h3>
<p>
Two equal-amplitude waves arrive in phase at a
point. What happens to the resultant amplitude?
</p>
<p>
Their displacements reinforce each other. For
equal amplitudes A, the resultant amplitude at
complete constructive interference is 2A.
</p>

<h3>Remember</h3>
<p>
Constructive interference is reinforcement caused
by overlap. It does not create energy from nothing;
energy is redistributed through the combined wave
pattern.
</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Constructive Interference', 15),
  (586, 586, '<h2>Destructive Interference</h2>

<h3>Core idea</h3>
<p>
Destructive interference occurs when overlapping
waves oppose one another, reducing the resultant
displacement compared with the displacement of
either wave alone.
</p>

<p>
For two equal-amplitude waves in opposite phase,
a crest of one aligns with a trough of the other.
At complete destructive interference, their
displacements cancel at that point.
</p>

<h3>Worked example 1</h3>
<p>
One pulse produces a displacement of 4 cm upward.
Another produces a displacement of 4 cm downward
at the same point. Find the resultant.
</p>
<p>
Resultant = +4 + (−4) = 0 cm.
</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> Zero resultant displacement.</p>

<h3>Worked example 2</h3>
<p>
An upward pulse of 6 cm overlaps a downward pulse
of 2 cm. Find the resultant displacement.
</p>
<p>
Resultant = +6 + (−2) = +4 cm.
</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> 4 cm upward.</p>

<h3>Remember</h3>
<p>
Destructive interference does not always mean
complete cancellation. Complete cancellation
requires equal and opposite displacements at the
point and instant considered.
</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Destructive Interference', 15),
  (587, 587, '<h2>Production of Sound</h2>
<p>Sound is produced when an object vibrates. A vibration is a repeated movement to and fro about a position of rest.</p>

<h3>How sound is produced</h3>
<p>When a drum is struck, its stretched skin vibrates. These vibrations disturb the surrounding air, producing a sound wave.</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> Why does a guitar string produce sound when plucked?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Plucking the string makes it vibrate.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> The vibrating string disturbs the surrounding air.</p>
<p><b>Conclusion:</b> The disturbance travels through the air as a sound wave.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Production of Sound', 15),
  (588, 588, '<h2>Sound as a Mechanical Wave</h2>
<p>Sound is a <b>mechanical wave</b> because it requires a material medium, such as air, water or a solid, to travel.</p>

<p>Sound transfers energy from a vibrating source to a listener. The particles of the medium vibrate about their equilibrium positions; they do not travel all the way from the source to the listener.</p>

<h3>Sound cannot travel through a vacuum</h3>
<p>A vacuum has no particles to transmit the sound disturbance. Therefore, sound cannot travel through a vacuum.</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> An alarm rings inside a sealed bell jar. The air is gradually removed. Why does the sound become fainter?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Sound needs particles to transmit its disturbance.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Removing air reduces the number of particles available to transmit sound.</p>
<p><b>Conclusion:</b> Less sound reaches the listener. In a perfect vacuum, sound cannot travel.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Sound as a Mechanical Wave', 15),
  (589, 589, '<h2>Compressions in Sound Waves</h2>
<p>Sound in air normally travels as a longitudinal wave. Air particles vibrate parallel to the direction in which the sound wave travels.</p>

<p>A <b>compression</b> is a region where particles are closer together than normal. It has higher pressure and density than the surrounding undisturbed air.</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> What happens to the air particles in a compression?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> The sound source pushes nearby air particles.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Particles become closer together in that region.</p>
<p><b>Conclusion:</b> A compression is a high-pressure region.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Compressions in Sound Waves', 15),
  (590, 590, '<h2>Rarefactions in Sound Waves</h2>
<p>A <b>rarefaction</b> is a region in a longitudinal sound wave where particles are farther apart than normal.</p>

<p>Rarefactions have lower pressure and density than the undisturbed medium. They alternate with compressions as the sound wave travels.</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> A region of air has particles farther apart than normal as a sound wave passes. Identify the region.</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> The particles are spread farther apart.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Such a region has lower pressure and density.</p>
<p><b>Conclusion:</b> It is a rarefaction.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Rarefactions in Sound Waves', 15),
  (591, 591, '<h2>Transmission of Sound Through a Medium</h2>
<p>When a vibrating source produces sound, it causes nearby particles in a medium to vibrate. These particles exert forces on neighbouring particles, passing the disturbance along.</p>

<p>The particles oscillate about their equilibrium positions while the sound wave transfers energy through the medium.</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> How does sound from a loudspeaker reach a listener through air?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> The loudspeaker cone vibrates.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> It creates changing pressure regions in nearby air.</p>
<p><b>Step 3:</b> The disturbance is passed from particle to particle.</p>
<p><b>Conclusion:</b> Sound energy travels through the air to the listener.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Transmission of Sound Through a Medium', 15),
  (592, 592, '<h2>Speed of Sound</h2>
<p>The speed of sound is the distance travelled by a sound wave per unit time.</p>

<p><b>Formula:</b></p>
<p>Speed = Distance ÷ Time</p>
<p>In symbols: <b>v = d/t</b></p>
<p>Speed is measured in metres per second (m/s).</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> Sound travels 680 m in 2 s. Calculate its speed.</p>
<p><b>Step 1: Write the formula.</b></p>
<p>v = d/t</p>
<p><b>Step 2: Substitute the values.</b></p>
<p>v = 680/2</p>
<p><b>Step 3: Calculate.</b></p>
<p>v = 340 m/s</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> 340 m/s.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Speed of Sound', 15),
  (593, 593, '<h2>Pitch of Sound</h2>
<p><b>Pitch</b> describes how high or low a sound seems to a listener. Pitch depends mainly on the frequency of the sound wave.</p>

<ul>
<li>Higher frequency produces a higher-pitched sound.</li>
<li>Lower frequency produces a lower-pitched sound.</li>
</ul>

<p>Frequency is the number of complete vibrations or wave cycles per second. Its unit is the hertz (Hz).</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> Two tuning forks produce sounds of 256 Hz and 512 Hz. Which produces the higher pitch?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Compare their frequencies.</p>
<p>512 Hz is greater than 256 Hz.</p>
<p><b>Conclusion:</b> The 512 Hz tuning fork produces the higher-pitched sound.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Pitch of Sound', 15),
  (594, 594, '<h2>Loudness of Sound</h2>
<p>Loudness describes how loud or quiet a sound seems to a listener. For the same general conditions, a sound wave with greater amplitude is usually heard as louder.</p>

<p><b>Amplitude</b> is the maximum displacement of a vibrating particle from its equilibrium position.</p>

<ul>
<li>Greater amplitude generally means a louder sound.</li>
<li>Smaller amplitude generally means a quieter sound.</li>
</ul>

<p>Do not confuse loudness with pitch: pitch is related to frequency, while loudness is related to amplitude and also depends on factors such as distance and the listener''s hearing.</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> Two sounds have the same frequency, but one has a greater amplitude. Which is generally heard as louder?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Their frequencies are equal, so their pitches are the same.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> One sound has a greater amplitude.</p>
<p><b>Conclusion:</b> The sound with greater amplitude is generally heard as louder.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Loudness of Sound', 15),
  (595, 595, '<h2>Echoes and Reflection of Sound</h2>
<p>An <b>echo</b> is a sound heard again after the original sound has reflected from a surface and returned to the listener.</p>

<p>Large, hard surfaces such as cliffs and walls can reflect sound. A distinct echo is heard when the reflected sound reaches the listener sufficiently later than the original sound.</p>

<h3>Calculating distance using an echo</h3>
<p>The sound travels from the listener to the reflecting surface and then back again. Therefore, the total distance travelled by sound is twice the distance to the surface.</p>

<p><b>Distance to surface:</b></p>
<p>d = vt/2</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> A person hears an echo 2 seconds after shouting. Take the speed of sound as 340 m/s. How far away is the reflecting surface?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Total distance travelled by sound = vt.</p>
<p>= 340 × 2 = 680 m</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> The sound travels to the surface and back.</p>
<p>Distance to surface = 680/2 = 340 m</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> 340 m.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Echoes and Reflection of Sound', 15),
  (596, 596, '<h2>Nature of Light</h2>
<p>Light is a form of energy that enables us to see objects. In the wave model, light is a <b>transverse electromagnetic wave</b>.</p>

<p>Unlike sound, light does not require particles of a material medium to travel. It can travel through a vacuum.</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> Why can sunlight reach Earth through space?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Space is nearly a vacuum.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Light is an electromagnetic wave and does not require a material medium.</p>
<p><b>Conclusion:</b> Sunlight can travel through space and reach Earth.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Nature of Light', 15),
  (597, 597, '<h2>Transverse Nature of Light</h2>
<p>Light is a transverse wave. In a transverse wave, the oscillations are perpendicular to the direction in which the wave travels.</p>

<p>For electromagnetic waves, the electric and magnetic fields oscillate perpendicular to the direction of propagation. At secondary-school level, remember the defining relationship: <b>oscillation is at right angles to wave travel</b>.</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> Light travels horizontally. In a transverse wave, how are its oscillations oriented relative to its direction of travel?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify the direction of propagation: horizontal.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Transverse oscillations are perpendicular to propagation.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> At right angles to the direction of travel.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Transverse Nature of Light', 15),
  (598, 598, '<h2>Propagation of Light Through a Vacuum</h2>
<p>A vacuum is a region containing no material particles. Light can travel through a vacuum because it is an electromagnetic wave rather than a mechanical wave.</p>

<p>The speed of light in a vacuum is approximately:</p>
<p><b>c = 3.0 × 10<sup>8</sup> m/s</b></p>

<p>Light travels more slowly in transparent materials such as glass and water than it does in a vacuum.</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> Light from a distant star crosses space before reaching Earth. Does it need air to make this journey?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Space is nearly a vacuum.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Light does not need air or another material medium.</p>
<p><b>Conclusion:</b> No, light can cross space without air.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Propagation of Light Through a Vacuum', 15),
  (599, 599, '<h2>Wavelength of Light</h2>
<p>The <b>wavelength</b> of light is the distance between successive points that are in the same phase of the wave. For example, it is the distance from one crest to the next crest.</p>

<p>Wavelength is represented by the Greek letter lambda (λ). Its SI unit is the metre (m), although very small wavelengths are often expressed in nanometres (nm).</p>

<p><b>1 nm = 1 × 10<sup>-9</sup> m</b></p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> A light wave has a wavelength of 600 nm. Express this wavelength in metres.</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Write the conversion.</p>
<p>1 nm = 10<sup>-9</sup> m</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Multiply by 600.</p>
<p>600 nm = 600 × 10<sup>-9</sup> m</p>
<p><b>Step 3:</b> Write in standard form.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> 6.0 × 10<sup>-7</sup> m.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Wavelength of Light', 15),
  (600, 600, '<h2>Frequency of Light</h2>
<p>The <b>frequency</b> of light is the number of complete wave cycles passing a fixed point each second.</p>

<p>Frequency is represented by f and measured in hertz (Hz).</p>

<p>Light with a higher frequency has a higher frequency value in hertz. In the visible spectrum, violet light has a higher frequency than red light.</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> A light wave completes 6 × 10<sup>14</sup> cycles each second. What is its frequency?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Frequency is the number of cycles per second.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> The wave completes 6 × 10<sup>14</sup> cycles per second.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> 6 × 10<sup>14</sup> Hz.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Frequency of Light', 15),
  (601, 601, '<h2>Light Wave Equation</h2>
<p>Wave speed, frequency and wavelength are related by:</p>

<p><b>v = fλ</b></p>

<ul>
<li>v = wave speed in metres per second (m/s)</li>
<li>f = frequency in hertz (Hz)</li>
<li>λ = wavelength in metres (m)</li>
</ul>

<p>For light in a vacuum, use <b>c = fλ</b>, where c is approximately 3.0 × 10<sup>8</sup> m/s.</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> Light travels in a vacuum at 3.0 × 10<sup>8</sup> m/s and has a frequency of 6.0 × 10<sup>14</sup> Hz. Find its wavelength.</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Start with c = fλ.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Rearrange to make wavelength the subject.</p>
<p>λ = c/f</p>
<p><b>Step 3:</b> Substitute the values.</p>
<p>λ = (3.0 × 10<sup>8</sup>)/(6.0 × 10<sup>14</sup>)</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> λ = 5.0 × 10<sup>-7</sup> m.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Light Wave Equation', 15),
  (602, 602, '<h2>Visible Light Spectrum</h2>
<p>Visible light is the part of the electromagnetic spectrum that the human eye can detect.</p>

<p>The visible colours are commonly listed in this order:</p>
<p><b>Red, Orange, Yellow, Green, Blue, Indigo, Violet</b></p>

<p>From red towards violet:</p>
<ul>
<li>Wavelength decreases.</li>
<li>Frequency increases.</li>
</ul>

<p>Red light has a longer wavelength and lower frequency than violet light.</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> Which has a longer wavelength, red light or violet light?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Recall the order of the visible spectrum.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Wavelength decreases from red towards violet.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> Red light has the longer wavelength.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Visible Light Spectrum', 15),
  (603, 603, '<h2>Reflection of Light</h2>
<p><b>Reflection</b> is the bouncing back of light into the original medium after it strikes a surface.</p>

<p>When light strikes a mirror, some of the incident light is reflected. The incoming ray is called the <b>incident ray</b>, and the ray that leaves the surface is called the <b>reflected ray</b>.</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> A ray of light strikes a plane mirror and bounces back into the air. What process has occurred?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Light travels towards the mirror.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> It returns to the original medium after striking the surface.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> Reflection of light.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Reflection of Light', 15),
  (604, 604, '<h2>The Normal and Angles of Reflection</h2>
<p>The <b>normal</b> is an imaginary line drawn perpendicular to a reflecting surface at the point where the incident ray strikes it.</p>

<p>The angle of incidence (i) is the angle between the incident ray and the normal. The angle of reflection (r) is the angle between the reflected ray and the normal.</p>

<p><b>Important:</b> Both angles are measured from the normal, not from the mirror surface.</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> A ray makes an angle of 30° with the mirror surface. Find its angle of incidence.</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> The normal is perpendicular to the mirror, so it makes an angle of 90° with the surface.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> The ray''s angle with the normal is 90° − 30°.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> 60°.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for The Normal and Angles of Reflection', 15),
  (605, 605, '<h2>Laws of Reflection</h2>
<p>Reflection follows two laws:</p>

<ol>
<li>The angle of incidence equals the angle of reflection: <b>i = r</b>.</li>
<li>The incident ray, the reflected ray and the normal at the point of incidence lie in the same plane.</li>
</ol>

<p>Remember that both angles are measured from the normal.</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> A ray strikes a plane mirror at an angle of incidence of 35°. Find the angle of reflection.</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Apply the first law of reflection.</p>
<p>i = r</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Substitute i = 35°.</p>
<p>r = 35°</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> 35°.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Laws of Reflection', 15),
  (606, 606, '<h2>Angle Between Incident and Reflected Rays</h2>
<p>For reflection at a plane mirror, the angle of incidence equals the angle of reflection.</p>

<p>If the angle of incidence is i, the angle between the incident and reflected rays on the side of the incoming light is:</p>

<p><b>Angle between rays = 2i</b></p>

<p>This relationship applies when i is measured from the normal.</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> A ray strikes a plane mirror at an angle of incidence of 30°. Find the angle between the incident and reflected rays.</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Angle of reflection = 30°.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Angle between the rays = 30° + 30°.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> 60°.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Angle Between Incident and Reflected Rays', 15),
  (607, 607, '<h2>Image Formation in a Plane Mirror</h2>
<p>A plane mirror forms an image when light rays from an object reflect from the mirror and appear to come from behind it.</p>

<p>The reflected rays do not actually meet behind the mirror. Their backward extensions appear to meet there, forming a <b>virtual image</b>.</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> Why can a person see an image behind a plane mirror even though light does not actually travel from the image to the eye?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Light from the person reflects from the mirror into the eye.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> The brain traces the reflected rays backwards in straight lines.</p>
<p><b>Conclusion:</b> The rays appear to originate behind the mirror, producing a virtual image.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Image Formation in a Plane Mirror', 15),
  (608, 608, '<h2>Properties of a Plane-Mirror Image</h2>
<p>The image formed by a plane mirror has these properties:</p>

<ul>
<li><b>Virtual:</b> it appears behind the mirror.</li>
<li><b>Upright:</b> it has the same vertical orientation as the object.</li>
<li><b>Same size:</b> the image height equals the object height.</li>
<li><b>Same distance:</b> the image is as far behind the mirror as the object is in front.</li>
<li><b>Laterally inverted:</b> left and right appear reversed.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> An object is 2 m in front of a plane mirror. How far behind the mirror is its image?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> A plane-mirror image is as far behind the mirror as the object is in front.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Object distance = 2 m.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> Image distance = 2 m behind the mirror.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Properties of a Plane-Mirror Image', 15),
  (609, 609, '<h2>Lateral Inversion</h2>
<p><b>Lateral inversion</b> is the apparent reversal of left and right in the image formed by a plane mirror.</p>

<p>For example, a person''s right hand appears on the image''s left-hand side when the person faces a mirror. The image is not upside down; it remains upright.</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> A person raises their right hand while facing a plane mirror. Which hand appears raised on the corresponding side of the image?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> A plane mirror produces lateral inversion.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> The person''s right side appears reversed in the image.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> The image appears to raise its left hand.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Lateral Inversion in a Plane Mirror', 15),
  (610, 610, '<h2>Refraction of Light</h2>
<p><b>Refraction</b> is the change in direction of light as it passes obliquely from one transparent medium into another because its speed changes.</p>

<p>For example, light changes speed when it passes from air into glass. If it enters at an angle to the normal, its direction also changes.</p>

<p><b>Important:</b> Refraction is caused by a change in wave speed. Light does not always bend: when it enters along the normal, its speed changes but its direction remains unchanged.</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> A ray of light passes obliquely from air into glass and changes direction. What is the phenomenon called?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> The ray crosses from one medium into another.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Its speed changes and, because it enters obliquely, its direction changes.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> Refraction.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Refraction of Light', 15),
  (611, 611, '<h2>Refraction Towards the Normal</h2>
<p>When light passes obliquely from a less optically dense medium, such as air, into a more optically dense medium, such as glass, it slows down and bends <b>towards the normal</b>.</p>

<p>The angle of refraction is smaller than the angle of incidence:</p>
<p><b>r < i</b></p>

<p>Optical density describes how a medium affects light''s speed. It does not simply mean how physically dense the material is.</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> A ray enters glass obliquely from air. Its angle of incidence is 50°. Which way does it bend?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Light enters glass from air.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> It slows down in the glass.</p>
<p><b>Conclusion:</b> It bends towards the normal, so the refracted angle is less than 50°.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Refraction Towards the Normal', 15),
  (612, 612, '<h2>Refraction Away from the Normal</h2>
<p>When light passes obliquely from a more optically dense medium, such as glass, into a less optically dense medium, such as air, it speeds up and bends <b>away from the normal</b>.</p>

<p>The angle of refraction is greater than the angle of incidence:</p>
<p><b>r > i</b></p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> A ray travels obliquely from glass into air with an angle of incidence of 30°. Explain how it bends.</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Light moves from glass into air.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Its speed increases.</p>
<p><b>Conclusion:</b> It bends away from the normal, so the refracted angle is greater than 30° if the ray emerges into air.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Refraction Away from the Normal', 15),
  (613, 613, '<h2>Refraction at Normal Incidence</h2>
<p><b>Normal incidence</b> occurs when light strikes a boundary along the normal, so the angle of incidence is 0°.</p>

<p>At normal incidence, light changes speed when it enters a different medium, but it does not change direction. Its angle in the new medium remains 0° relative to the normal.</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> A light ray enters a glass block perpendicularly to its surface. What is its angle of refraction?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> The ray enters along the normal.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Its angle of incidence is 0°.</p>
<p><b>Step 3:</b> It continues along the normal.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> 0°.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Refraction at Normal Incidence', 15),
  (614, 614, '<h2>Refractive Index</h2>
<p>The absolute refractive index of a medium compares the speed of light in a vacuum with its speed in that medium.</p>

<p><b>Formula:</b></p>
<p><b>n = c/v</b></p>

<ul>
<li>n = absolute refractive index (no unit)</li>
<li>c = speed of light in a vacuum</li>
<li>v = speed of light in the medium</li>
</ul>

<p>A higher refractive index means that light travels more slowly in that medium.</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> Light travels through a material at 2.0 × 10<sup>8</sup> m/s. Calculate its absolute refractive index. Take c = 3.0 × 10<sup>8</sup> m/s.</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Use n = c/v.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Substitute the values.</p>
<p>n = (3.0 × 10<sup>8</sup>)/(2.0 × 10<sup>8</sup>)</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> n = 1.5.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Refractive Index', 15),
  (615, 615, '<h2>Snell''s Law</h2>
<p>Snell''s law relates the angles of incidence and refraction to the refractive indices of two media.</p>

<p><b>n₁ sin i = n₂ sin r</b></p>

<ul>
<li>n₁ = refractive index of the first medium</li>
<li>n₂ = refractive index of the second medium</li>
<li>i = angle of incidence</li>
<li>r = angle of refraction</li>
</ul>

<p>Angles are measured from the normal. When light travels from air into a material, the refractive index of air is often approximated as 1.0 for school calculations.</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> Light travels from air into glass. The angle of incidence is 30° and the angle of refraction is 20°. Calculate the refractive index of the glass, taking the refractive index of air as 1.0.</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Use n₁ sin i = n₂ sin r.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Rearrange: n₂ = n₁ sin i / sin r.</p>
<p><b>Step 3:</b> Substitute: n₂ = (1.0 × sin 30°) / sin 20°.</p>
<p>n₂ ≈ 0.5 / 0.342</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> n₂ ≈ 1.46.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Snell''s Law', 15),
  (616, 616, '<h2>Apparent Depth</h2>
<p>When an object under water is viewed from air, light refracts as it leaves the water. The object can appear closer to the surface than it really is.</p>

<p>For viewing nearly perpendicular to a flat water surface, the apparent depth is less than the real depth.</p>

<p>For an object in water viewed from air, the approximate relationship is:</p>
<p><b>Refractive index ≈ real depth / apparent depth</b></p>

<p>This approximation assumes near-normal viewing and a flat boundary.</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> A pool has a real depth of 2.0 m and appears to be 1.5 m deep when viewed from above. Estimate the refractive index of the water.</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Use n ≈ real depth / apparent depth.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> n ≈ 2.0 / 1.5.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> n ≈ 1.33.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Apparent Depth Due to Refraction', 15),
  (617, 617, '<h2>Effect of Wavelength on Diffraction</h2>
<p><b>Diffraction</b> is the spreading of waves as they pass through an opening or around an obstacle.</p>

<p>For the same gap width, waves with longer wavelengths generally diffract more noticeably than waves with shorter wavelengths.</p>

<p>Diffraction is especially noticeable when the wavelength is comparable to the gap width.</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> Two sets of waves pass through the same opening. One has a wavelength of 2 cm and the other 8 cm. Which is expected to spread out more?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> The opening is the same for both waves.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Compare their wavelengths.</p>
<p>8 cm is the longer wavelength.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> The waves with wavelength 8 cm generally diffract more noticeably.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Effect of Wavelength on Diffraction', 15),
  (618, 618, '<h2>Effect of Gap Width on Diffraction</h2>
<p>The width of an opening affects how much a wave spreads after passing through it.</p>

<ul>
<li>A narrow gap, comparable to the wavelength, produces noticeable spreading.</li>
<li>A gap much wider than the wavelength produces less noticeable spreading.</li>
</ul>

<p>When comparing two openings, keep the wavelength the same so that the effect of gap width can be identified.</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> Water waves of the same wavelength pass through two gaps. One gap is 5 cm wide and the other is 50 cm wide. Which gap produces more noticeable diffraction?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> The wavelength is unchanged.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Compare the gap widths with that wavelength.</p>
<p><b>Conclusion:</b> The narrower gap generally produces more noticeable spreading, particularly when its width is comparable to the wavelength.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Effect of Gap Width on Diffraction', 15),
  (619, 619, '<h2>Diffraction of Sound Around Obstacles</h2>
<p>Sound can spread around obstacles and through doorways. This is why a person can often hear someone speaking from another room without seeing the speaker directly.</p>

<p>Diffraction is more noticeable when the wavelength is not very small compared with the size of the opening or obstacle.</p>

<p>Sound waves often have wavelengths that allow noticeable spreading around everyday obstacles. Light has much shorter wavelengths, so its diffraction around ordinary objects is usually less noticeable.</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> A student hears a conversation from around a doorway without seeing the speakers. Which wave behaviour helps explain this?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> The sound reaches the student despite the doorway blocking a direct line of sight.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Sound waves spread through openings and around obstacles.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> Diffraction of sound.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Diffraction of Sound Around Obstacles', 15),
  (620, 620, '<h2>Wavefronts After Diffraction</h2>
<p>A <b>wavefront</b> is a line joining points on a wave that are in the same phase.</p>

<p>When straight wavefronts pass through a narrow opening, the wavefronts beyond the opening spread out. For a small opening compared with the wavelength, the emerging wavefronts are approximately semicircular in a two-dimensional water-wave diagram.</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> Straight water-wave fronts approach a narrow gap. What shape do the wavefronts approximately take after passing through the gap?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> The waves encounter a narrow opening.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Diffraction causes the waves to spread from the opening.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> Approximately semicircular wavefronts in a two-dimensional diagram.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Wavefronts After Diffraction', 15),
  (621, 621, '<h2>Coherent Sources</h2>

  <h3>1. Meaning of coherent sources</h3>
  <p>Two wave sources are <b>coherent</b> when they produce waves of the same frequency and maintain a constant phase difference.</p>

  <h3>2. Why coherence matters</h3>
  <p>Coherent sources produce a stable interference pattern. The positions of reinforcement and cancellation remain fixed because the phase relationship between the waves does not continually change.</p>

  <h3>3. Same frequency is not enough</h3>
  <p>Two sources may have the same frequency but fail to maintain a constant phase difference. For a stable interference pattern, both conditions are required:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Same frequency.</li>
    <li>Constant phase difference.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>Two sources produce waves at 20 Hz and maintain a constant phase difference of 90°. Are they coherent?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Their frequencies are equal: both are 20 Hz.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Their phase difference is constant.</p>
  <p><b>Conclusion:</b> Yes, they are coherent.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Coherent Sources', 15),
  (622, 622, '<h2>Path Difference</h2>

  <h3>1. Meaning of path difference</h3>
  <p><b>Path difference</b> is the difference between the distances travelled by two waves from their sources to the same point.</p>

  <p>If the two distances are d₁ and d₂, the magnitude of the path difference is:</p>
  <p><b>Path difference = |d₁ − d₂|</b></p>

  <h3>2. Why path difference matters</h3>
  <p>The path difference helps determine whether two coherent waves arrive in phase or out of phase.</p>

  <ul>
    <li>A path difference of one wavelength corresponds to waves arriving in phase, if they started in phase.</li>
    <li>A path difference of half a wavelength corresponds to waves arriving in opposite phase, if they started in phase.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>Wave A travels 8 m to a point. Wave B travels 11 m to the same point.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify the distances: d₁ = 8 m and d₂ = 11 m.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Find their difference: |11 − 8| = 3 m.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> The path difference is 3 m.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Path Difference', 15),
  (623, 623, '<h2>Constructive Interference Condition</h2>

  <h3>1. When does reinforcement occur?</h3>
  <p>Constructive interference occurs when two waves meet in phase: crests meet crests and troughs meet troughs. Their displacements reinforce each other.</p>

  <h3>2. Path-difference condition</h3>
  <p>For two coherent sources that emit waves in phase, constructive interference occurs when:</p>
  <p><b>Path difference = nλ</b></p>

  <p>Here:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><b>n</b> is a whole number: 0, 1, 2, 3, …</li>
    <li><b>λ</b> is the wavelength.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>3. Worked example</h3>
  <p>Two in-phase sources produce waves of wavelength 0.5 m. A point has a path difference of 1.0 m. Determine whether constructive interference occurs.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Calculate the number of wavelengths: 1.0 ÷ 0.5 = 2.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> The path difference is 2λ.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Constructive interference occurs because 2 is a whole number.</p>

  <p><b>Important:</b> This path-difference rule assumes the two sources emit waves in phase.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Constructive Interference Condition', 15),
  (624, 624, '<h2>Destructive Interference Condition</h2>

  <h3>1. When does cancellation occur?</h3>
  <p>Destructive interference occurs when two waves meet in opposite phase. A crest from one wave meets a trough from the other, so their displacements oppose each other.</p>

  <h3>2. Path-difference condition</h3>
  <p>For two coherent sources that emit waves in phase, destructive interference occurs when:</p>
  <p><b>Path difference = (n + ½)λ</b></p>

  <p>Here n is a whole number: 0, 1, 2, 3, …</p>

  <p>The possible path differences include:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>½λ</li>
    <li>1½λ</li>
    <li>2½λ</li>
    <li>3½λ</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>3. Worked example</h3>
  <p>The wavelength is 0.6 m and the path difference is 0.3 m.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Calculate the path difference in wavelengths: 0.3 ÷ 0.6 = 0.5.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> The path difference is ½λ.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Destructive interference occurs.</p>

  <p><b>Important:</b> Complete cancellation occurs only when the opposing displacements have equal magnitudes.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Destructive Interference Condition', 15),
  (625, 625, '<h2>Formation of Stationary Waves</h2>

  <h3>1. How stationary waves form</h3>
  <p>A <b>stationary wave</b>, also called a standing wave, forms when two waves of the same frequency and wavelength travel in opposite directions and superpose under suitable conditions.</p>

  <p>One common example is a wave travelling along a stretched string and reflecting from a fixed end. The incoming and reflected waves travel in opposite directions.</p>

  <h3>2. Why it is called stationary</h3>
  <p>The resulting pattern has fixed positions where the displacement is always zero and fixed positions where the displacement is greatest. The pattern does not travel along the medium as a whole.</p>

  <h3>3. Stationary versus progressive waves</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Progressive wave:</b> the wave pattern travels through the medium and transfers energy along it.</li>
    <li><b>Stationary wave:</b> the pattern has fixed nodes and antinodes; there is no net energy transfer along the medium by the ideal stationary pattern.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>A wave on a string reflects from a fixed end and overlaps with the incoming wave. The two waves have the same frequency and travel in opposite directions.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify the two waves: incoming and reflected.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> They have the same frequency and travel in opposite directions.</p>
  <p><b>Conclusion:</b> Their superposition can produce a stationary-wave pattern.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Formation of Stationary Waves', 15),
  (626, 626, '<h2>Nodes in Stationary Waves</h2>

  <h3>1. Meaning of a node</h3>
  <p>A <b>node</b> is a point on a stationary wave where the resultant displacement is always zero.</p>

  <p>At a node, the two overlapping waves have equal and opposite displacements at corresponding instants, so they cancel.</p>

  <h3>2. Distance between adjacent nodes</h3>
  <p>For a stationary wave formed from waves of wavelength λ:</p>
  <p><b>Distance between adjacent nodes = λ/2</b></p>

  <h3>3. Finding the wavelength</h3>
  <p>If the distance between adjacent nodes is d, then:</p>
  <p><b>λ = 2d</b></p>

  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>Adjacent nodes are separated by 0.15 m.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Use d = λ/2.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Rearrange: λ = 2d.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> λ = 2 × 0.15 = 0.30 m.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> The wavelength is 0.30 m.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Nodes in Stationary Waves', 15),
  (627, 627, '
  <h2>Antinodes in Stationary Waves</h2>

  <h3>Learning objective</h3>
  <p>
    By the end of this lesson, you should be able to identify an
    antinode and calculate distances involving nodes and antinodes.
  </p>

  <h3>1. What is an antinode?</h3>

  <p>
    An <b>antinode</b> is a point on a stationary wave where the
    oscillation amplitude is greatest.
  </p>

  <p>
    A <b>node</b> is a point where the resultant displacement remains
    zero. An antinode is a point where the particles oscillate with
    maximum amplitude.
  </p>

  <p>
    Remember:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><b>Node:</b> zero oscillation amplitude.</li>
    <li><b>Antinode:</b> maximum oscillation amplitude.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>2. Distance relationships</h3>

  <p>
    The positions of nodes and antinodes follow fixed wavelength
    relationships.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Node to nearest antinode:</b>
  </p>

  <p style="text-align:center;">
    <b>d = λ/4</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Distance between adjacent nodes:</b>
  </p>

  <p style="text-align:center;">
    <b>d = λ/2</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Distance between adjacent antinodes:</b>
  </p>

  <p style="text-align:center;">
    <b>d = λ/2</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    Here, <b>d</b> is the distance and <b>λ</b> (lambda) is the
    wavelength.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Worked Example 1: Distance from a node to an antinode</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Question:</b> A stationary wave has a wavelength of 0.8 m.
    Calculate the distance from a node to its nearest antinode.
  </p>

  <p><b>Step 1: Identify the relationship.</b></p>

  <p>
    A node and its nearest antinode are separated by one-quarter
    of a wavelength.
  </p>

  <p><b>d = λ/4</b></p>

  <p><b>Step 2: Substitute the wavelength.</b></p>

  <p>
    d = 0.8/4
  </p>

  <p><b>Step 3: Calculate.</b></p>

  <p>
    d = 0.2 m
  </p>

  <p><b>Answer: 0.2 m</b></p>

  <p>
    <b>Check:</b> One-quarter of 0.8 m is 0.2 m.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Worked Example 2: Finding the wavelength</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Question:</b> Two adjacent antinodes are separated by 0.35 m.
    Calculate the wavelength of the stationary wave.
  </p>

  <p><b>Step 1: Identify the relationship.</b></p>

  <p>
    Adjacent antinodes are separated by half a wavelength.
  </p>

  <p>
    d = λ/2
  </p>

  <p><b>Step 2: Rearrange the formula.</b></p>

  <p>
    Multiply both sides by 2:
  </p>

  <p>
    λ = 2d
  </p>

  <p><b>Step 3: Substitute the distance.</b></p>

  <p>
    λ = 2 × 0.35
  </p>

  <p>
    λ = 0.70 m
  </p>

  <p><b>Answer: 0.70 m</b></p>

  <p>
    <b>Check:</b> Half of 0.70 m is 0.35 m, as given in the question.
  </p>

  <h3>5. Worked Example 3: Finding the wavelength from a node-antinode distance</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Question:</b> The distance between a node and its nearest
    antinode is 0.15 m. Calculate the wavelength.
  </p>

  <p><b>Step 1: Write the relationship.</b></p>

  <p>
    d = λ/4
  </p>

  <p><b>Step 2: Make wavelength the subject.</b></p>

  <p>
    Multiply both sides by 4:
  </p>

  <p>
    λ = 4d
  </p>

  <p><b>Step 3: Substitute and calculate.</b></p>

  <p>
    λ = 4 × 0.15
  </p>

  <p>
    λ = 0.60 m
  </p>

  <p><b>Answer: 0.60 m</b></p>

  <p>
    <b>Check:</b> One-quarter of 0.60 m is 0.15 m.
  </p>

  <h3>6. Common mistake</h3>

  <p>
    Do not confuse the distance from a node to its nearest antinode
    with the distance between adjacent antinodes.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>Node to nearest antinode: λ/4.</li>
    <li>Adjacent antinodes: λ/2.</li>
    <li>Adjacent nodes: λ/2.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Before calculating, identify which two points the question
    describes.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Antinodes in Stationary Waves', 15),
  (628, 628, '
  <h2>Meaning of Electric Charge</h2>

  <h3>1. What is electric charge?</h3>
  <p>
    <b>Electric charge</b> is a property of matter responsible for
    electrical interactions. There are two types of charge:
    positive and negative.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>Protons have positive charge.</li>
    <li>Electrons have negative charge.</li>
    <li>Neutrons have no net electric charge.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    In a metal wire, some electrons can move through the material.
    Their movement makes electric current possible.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Unit of charge</h3>
  <p>
    Electric charge is measured in <b>coulombs (C)</b>.
    The symbol for charge is <b>Q</b>.
  </p>

  <p>
    The magnitude of the charge of one electron is approximately
    1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ C. An electron''s charge is negative, while a proton''s
    charge is positive with the same magnitude.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Identifying charge</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> What type of charge does an electron carry?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> An electron is a subatomic particle.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Electrons carry negative electric charge.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Negative charge.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Choosing the unit</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A body carries 8 C of charge. What does C represent?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> The quantity is electric charge.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Its SI unit is the coulomb.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> C represents coulombs.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Positive and negative particles</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A body gains some electrons. Explain the change in its net charge.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Electrons have negative charge.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Gaining electrons adds negative charge to the body.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> The body becomes negatively charged, assuming no other charge transfer occurs.</p>

  <h3>Remember</h3>
  <p>
    Charge is measured in coulombs. Do not confuse charge, Q,
    with current, I: charge is a quantity, while current describes
    the rate at which charge flows.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Meaning of Electric Charge', 15),
  (629, 629, '
  <h2>Meaning of Electric Current</h2>

  <h3>1. What is electric current?</h3>
  <p>
    <b>Electric current</b> is the rate of flow of electric charge
    through a conductor.
  </p>

  <p><b>I = Q/t</b></p>

  <ul>
    <li>I = current in amperes (A)</li>
    <li>Q = charge passing a point in coulombs (C)</li>
    <li>t = time in seconds (s)</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    One ampere means that one coulomb of charge passes a point
    in the circuit every second.
  </p>

  <h3>2. What makes current flow?</h3>
  <p>
    In a metal wire, electrons can move through the material.
    A potential difference across the wire can drive their movement
    around a complete circuit.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Conventional current</b> is defined as flowing from the positive
    terminal towards the negative terminal through the external circuit.
    In a metal wire, electrons drift in the opposite direction.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Interpreting one ampere</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> What does a current of 1 A mean?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Current is charge flowing per unit time.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> For 1 A, one coulomb flows each second.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> 1 A = 1 C/s.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Interpreting a larger current</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A wire carries a current of 3 A. How much charge passes a point each second?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> A = C/s.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> A current of 3 A means 3 C/s.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> 3 coulombs pass each second.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Conventional current and electrons</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> In a metal wire connected to a battery, which way do electrons move compared with conventional current?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Conventional current is defined from the positive terminal towards the negative terminal in the external circuit.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Electrons are negatively charged and drift in the opposite direction.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Electron drift is opposite to conventional current in the metal wire.</p>

  <h3>Remember</h3>
  <p>
    Current is not the same as charge. Current tells you how much charge
    passes a point per second.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Meaning of Electric Current', 15),
  (630, 630, '
  <h2>Calculating Electric Current</h2>

  <h3>Formula</h3>
  <p><b>I = Q/t</b></p>

  <p>
    Divide the charge passing a point by the time taken.
    Charge must be in coulombs and time in seconds to obtain current in amperes.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked example 1</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A charge of 12 C passes a point in 3 s. Calculate the current.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Q = 12 C, t = 3 s.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> I = Q/t = 12/3.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> I = 4 A.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A charge of 45 C passes through a conductor in 15 s. Find the current.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Q = 45 C, t = 15 s.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> I = 45/15.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> I = 3 A.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A charge of 600 C passes through a device in 2 minutes. Calculate the current.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Convert time to seconds: 2 × 60 = 120 s.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> I = Q/t = 600/120.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> I = 5 A.</p>

  <h3>Common mistake</h3>
  <p>
    Convert minutes to seconds before calculating current.
    For example, 2 minutes is 120 seconds, not 2 seconds.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Calculating Electric Current', 15),
  (631, 631, '
  <h2>Calculating Electric Charge</h2>

  <h3>Formula</h3>
  <p><b>Q = It</b></p>

  <p>
    Since current is charge divided by time, multiplying current
    by time gives the total charge that passes a point.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>Q is charge in coulombs (C).</li>
    <li>I is current in amperes (A).</li>
    <li>t is time in seconds (s).</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Worked example 1</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A current of 2 A flows for 10 s. Calculate the charge transferred.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> I = 2 A, t = 10 s.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Q = It = 2 × 10.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Q = 20 C.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A current of 0.5 A flows for 40 s. Find the charge transferred.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> I = 0.5 A, t = 40 s.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Q = 0.5 × 40.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Q = 20 C.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A current of 3 A flows for 2 minutes. Calculate the charge transferred.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Convert time: 2 minutes = 120 s.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Q = It = 3 × 120.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Q = 360 C.</p>

  <h3>Common mistake</h3>
  <p>
    Do not divide current by time when calculating charge.
    Use Q = It, and convert time to seconds first.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Calculating Electric Charge', 15),
  (632, 632, '
  <h2>Meaning and Calculation of Potential Difference</h2>

  <h3>1. What is potential difference?</h3>
  <p>
    <b>Potential difference (voltage)</b> between two points is the
    energy transferred per unit charge as charge moves between those points.
  </p>

  <p><b>V = E/Q</b></p>

  <ul>
    <li>V = potential difference in volts (V)</li>
    <li>E = energy transferred in joules (J)</li>
    <li>Q = charge in coulombs (C)</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    A potential difference of 1 V means that 1 J of energy is transferred
    per coulomb of charge.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Interpreting voltage</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> What does a potential difference of 6 V mean?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> One volt equals one joule per coulomb.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> At 6 V, each coulomb transfers 6 J of energy between the points.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> 6 J of energy is transferred per coulomb.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Calculating potential difference</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A device transfers 24 J of energy to 4 C of charge. Calculate the potential difference across it.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> E = 24 J, Q = 4 C.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> V = E/Q = 24/4.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> V = 6 V.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Finding energy transferred</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A charge of 3 C passes through a component with a potential difference of 12 V. Calculate the energy transferred.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Start with V = E/Q.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Rearrange to E = VQ.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> E = 12 × 3.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> E = 36 J.</p>

  <h3>Distinguish current from potential difference</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Current tells you the rate of charge flow.</li>
    <li>Potential difference tells you the energy transferred per unit charge.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Remember</h3>
  <p>
    Use V = E/Q when energy and charge are given.
    Use E = VQ when voltage and charge are given.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Meaning and Calculation of Potential Difference', 15),
  (633, 633, '
  <h2>Meaning of Electrical Resistance</h2>

  <h3>1. What is resistance?</h3>
  <p>
    <b>Electrical resistance</b> is the opposition a material or component
    offers to the flow of electric current.
  </p>

  <p>
    Resistance is represented by <b>R</b> and measured in <b>ohms (Ω)</b>.
    A component with greater resistance carries less current than a
    component with lower resistance when the potential difference across
    each is the same and both obey Ohm''s law.
  </p>

  <h3>2. The relationship between voltage, current and resistance</h3>
  <p><b>V = IR</b></p>
  <ul>
    <li>V = potential difference in volts (V)</li>
    <li>I = current in amperes (A)</li>
    <li>R = resistance in ohms (Ω)</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Identifying the unit</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A component has a resistance of 8 Ω. What does Ω represent?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> R represents electrical resistance.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> The SI unit of resistance is the ohm.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Ω represents ohms.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Comparing two resistors</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> Two resistors have resistances of 2 Ω and 10 Ω. If the same potential difference is applied across each, which carries the smaller current, assuming both obey Ohm''s law?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Use I = V/R.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> At the same voltage, current decreases as resistance increases.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> The 10 Ω resistor carries the smaller current.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Identifying the quantities</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A resistor carries 3 A when a potential difference of 12 V is applied. Identify the quantities needed to calculate its resistance.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> V = 12 V.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> I = 3 A.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Use R = V/I.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Divide the potential difference by the current.</p>

  <h3>Remember</h3>
  <p>
    Resistance describes opposition to current. Do not confuse resistance
    in ohms with current in amperes or potential difference in volts.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Meaning of Electrical Resistance', 15),
  (634, 634, '
  <h2>Calculating Electrical Resistance</h2>

  <h3>Formula</h3>
  <p><b>R = V/I</b></p>

  <p>
    Resistance equals the potential difference across a component
    divided by the current through it.
  </p>

  <p>
    Use volts for potential difference and amperes for current.
    The resulting resistance is in ohms.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked example 1</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A resistor has a potential difference of 12 V across it and carries a current of 3 A. Calculate its resistance.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> V = 12 V, I = 3 A.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> R = V/I.</p>
  <p>R = 12/3</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> R = 4 Ω.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A lamp operates at 6 V and carries a current of 0.5 A. Calculate its resistance at that operating condition.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> V = 6 V, I = 0.5 A.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> R = 6/0.5.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> R = 12 Ω.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A component has 24 V across it and carries 0.2 A. Find its resistance at that operating condition.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> V = 24 V, I = 0.2 A.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> R = 24/0.2.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> R = 120 Ω.</p>

  <h3>Common mistake</h3>
  <p>
    Do not calculate I/V when finding resistance.
    The correct formula is R = V/I.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Calculating Electrical Resistance', 15),
  (635, 635, '
  <h2>Calculating Current Using Ohm''s Law</h2>

  <h3>Formula</h3>
  <p><b>I = V/R</b></p>

  <p>
    Rearrange V = IR by dividing both sides by R.
    Current equals potential difference divided by resistance.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked example 1</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A 6 Ω resistor is connected across a 12 V supply. Calculate the current.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> V = 12 V, R = 6 Ω.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> I = V/R.</p>
  <p>I = 12/6</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> I = 2 A.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A 20 Ω resistor is connected across 10 V. Calculate the current.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> V = 10 V, R = 20 Ω.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> I = 10/20.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> I = 0.5 A.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A 4 Ω resistor is connected across 18 V. Find the current.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> V = 18 V, R = 4 Ω.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> I = 18/4.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> I = 4.5 A.</p>

  <h3>Check your answer</h3>
  <p>
    For a fixed resistance, increasing the potential difference increases
    the current in the same proportion, provided the component''s resistance
    remains constant.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Calculating Current Using Ohm''s Law', 15),
  (636, 636, '
  <h2>Calculating Potential Difference Using Ohm''s Law</h2>

  <h3>Formula</h3>
  <p><b>V = IR</b></p>

  <p>
    Multiply current by resistance to calculate the potential difference
    across a component that follows Ohm''s law.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked example 1</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A 4 Ω resistor carries a current of 2 A. Calculate the potential difference across it.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> I = 2 A, R = 4 Ω.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> V = IR = 2 × 4.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> V = 8 V.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A 15 Ω resistor carries 0.4 A. Find the potential difference across it.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> I = 0.4 A, R = 15 Ω.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> V = 0.4 × 15.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> V = 6 V.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A 2.5 Ω resistor carries a current of 6 A. Calculate the potential difference.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> I = 6 A, R = 2.5 Ω.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> V = 6 × 2.5.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> V = 15 V.</p>

  <h3>Check your answer</h3>
  <p>
    The unit is volts. Do not divide current by resistance when calculating
    potential difference; use V = IR.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Calculating Potential Difference Using Ohm''s Law', 15),
  (637, 637, '
  <h2>Ohm''s Law and Voltage-Current Graphs</h2>

  <h3>1. Statement of Ohm''s law</h3>
  <p>
    Ohm''s law states that, for a conductor at constant temperature,
    the current through it is directly proportional to the potential
    difference across it.
  </p>

  <p>
    This means that doubling the potential difference doubles the current
    if the conductor''s resistance remains constant.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Ohmic conductors</h3>
  <p>
    An <b>ohmic conductor</b> obeys Ohm''s law under the stated conditions.
    Its resistance remains constant as the potential difference changes,
    provided its temperature and other relevant conditions remain constant.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Interpreting a graph</h3>
  <p>
    On a graph with potential difference V on the vertical axis and
    current I on the horizontal axis:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>A straight line through the origin indicates constant resistance.</li>
    <li>The gradient equals V/I, which is the resistance R.</li>
    <li>A steeper line means greater resistance when the axes are V against I.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Always check which quantity is on each axis before interpreting
    the gradient.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Identifying an ohmic conductor</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A conductor''s current doubles whenever the potential difference doubles, while its temperature stays constant. Does it obey Ohm''s law?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Ohm''s law requires current to be directly proportional to potential difference at constant temperature.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> The current doubles when the voltage doubles.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Yes, this behaviour is consistent with Ohm''s law.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Calculating graph gradient</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> On a V-I graph, a straight line passes through (I = 2 A, V = 6 V). Calculate its resistance if the line passes through the origin.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Resistance = gradient = change in V ÷ change in I.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> R = 6/2.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> R = 3 Ω.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Comparing graph gradients</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> Two ohmic resistors are represented on V-I graphs with voltage on the vertical axis. Resistor A has a gradient of 2 V/A, and resistor B has a gradient of 5 V/A. Which has the greater resistance?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> For this graph, gradient = V/I = R.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> A has resistance 2 Ω and B has resistance 5 Ω.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Resistor B has the greater resistance.</p>

  <h3>Remember</h3>
  <p>
    A straight-line V-I graph through the origin indicates constant resistance.
    The gradient represents resistance only when voltage is plotted vertically
    against current horizontally.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Ohm''s Law and Voltage-Current Graphs', 15),
  (638, 638, '<h2>Identifying a Series Circuit</h2>

<h3>Learning objective</h3>
<p>Identify a series circuit by recognising that its components are connected along one continuous path for electric current.</p>

<h3>1. What is a series circuit?</h3>
<p>A <b>series circuit</b> is a circuit in which components are connected one after another along a single path.</p>

<p>For example, imagine a cell connected to a switch, then a lamp, and finally back to the cell. The current has only one path to follow.</p>

<p><b>Key features:</b></p>
<ul>
<li>There is only one path for current through the components.</li>
<li>The components are connected one after another.</li>
<li>If a break occurs anywhere along the only path, the circuit becomes incomplete and current stops throughout it.</li>
</ul>

<h3>2. Worked example 1: Recognising a series circuit</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> A cell, switch and two lamps are connected one after another in one closed loop. What type of circuit is this?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify the number of paths available to the current.</p>
<p>There is only one path.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Identify how the components are connected.</p>
<p>They are connected one after another.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> It is a series circuit.</p>

<h3>3. Worked example 2: Finding the number of paths</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> Three resistors are connected in series. How many separate paths are available for current through those resistors?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Series components share one continuous route.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> No branch provides an alternative route.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> One path.</p>

<h3>4. Worked example 3: Predicting the effect of a break</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> Two lamps are connected in series. One lamp is removed, leaving a gap. What happens to the other lamp?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Removing the lamp creates a break in the only path.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> The circuit is now incomplete, so current cannot flow around the loop.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> The other lamp goes out.</p>

<h3>Remember</h3>
<p><b>Series = one path.</b> A break anywhere in that path stops the current through the entire series circuit.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Identifying a Series Circuit', 15),
  (639, 639, '<h2>Current in a Series Circuit</h2>

<h3>Learning objective</h3>
<p>Use the rule that current is the same at every point in a series circuit to determine unknown current values.</p>

<h3>1. The current rule</h3>
<p>In a series circuit, the current is the same through every component.</p>

<p>If an ammeter measures 0.4 A before the first resistor, the current through the second resistor is also 0.4 A, provided the circuit is a single series path.</p>

<p>This is because charge cannot split between branches in a series circuit. The same rate of charge flow passes through every component.</p>

<p><b>Rule:</b></p>
<p>I₁ = I₂ = I₃</p>
<p>Here, I₁, I₂ and I₃ represent current at different points in the series path.</p>

<h3>2. Worked example 1: Finding current after a resistor</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> A series circuit carries a current of 0.6 A through the first resistor. Find the current through the second resistor.</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Recall that current is the same throughout a series circuit.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> The current through the second resistor equals the current through the first resistor.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> 0.6 A.</p>

<h3>3. Worked example 2: Reading an ammeter</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> An ammeter reads 250 mA at one point in a series circuit. What current should another ideal ammeter measure elsewhere in the same series path?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Current is the same at every point in a series circuit.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Keep the same unit: 250 mA.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> 250 mA.</p>

<h3>4. Worked example 3: Finding an unknown reading</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> Three ammeters are placed at different points in a series circuit. The readings are 0.8 A, I and 0.8 A. Find I.</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> In series, all current readings are equal.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Therefore, I = 0.8 A.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> 0.8 A.</p>

<h3>Remember</h3>
<p>In a series circuit, current is the same everywhere. Do not add the current readings together.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Current in a Series Circuit', 15),
  (640, 640, '<h2>Potential Difference in a Series Circuit</h2>

<h3>Learning objective</h3>
<p>Calculate the supply potential difference or an unknown voltage drop using the rule that the supply voltage equals the sum of the voltage drops across series components.</p>

<h3>1. How voltage is shared</h3>
<p>Potential difference is the energy transferred per unit charge between two points.</p>

<p>In a series circuit, the supply potential difference is shared between the components. The voltage drops across all components add up to the supply voltage.</p>

<p><b>Rule:</b></p>
<p>V<sub>supply</sub> = V₁ + V₂ + V₃</p>

<p>The voltage drops do not have to be equal. They depend on the resistance of each component and the current flowing through it.</p>

<h3>2. Worked example 1: Finding the supply voltage</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> Two series resistors have voltage drops of 3 V and 5 V. Find the supply voltage.</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Write the rule: supply voltage = sum of voltage drops.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Substitute the values: V = 3 + 5.</p>
<p><b>Step 3:</b> Calculate: V = 8 V.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> 8 V.</p>

<h3>3. Worked example 2: Finding an unknown voltage drop</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> A 12 V supply is connected to two series resistors. The first resistor has a voltage drop of 7 V. Find the voltage drop across the second resistor.</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> The two voltage drops must add to 12 V.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Let the unknown voltage be V₂.</p>
<p>12 = 7 + V₂</p>
<p><b>Step 3:</b> Rearrange: V₂ = 12 − 7 = 5 V.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> 5 V.</p>

<h3>4. Worked example 3: Three components</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> Three series components have voltage drops of 2 V, 4 V and 6 V. Find the supply voltage.</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Add the voltage drops.</p>
<p>V = 2 + 4 + 6</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Calculate: V = 12 V.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> 12 V.</p>

<h3>Remember</h3>
<p><b>Supply voltage = total of the voltage drops.</b> A component with a larger resistance generally has a larger voltage drop when the same current flows through the series components.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Potential Difference in a Series Circuit', 15),
  (641, 641, '<h2>Total Resistance in a Series Circuit</h2>

<h3>Learning objective</h3>
<p>Calculate the combined resistance of resistors connected in series.</p>

<h3>1. The series resistance rule</h3>
<p>When resistors are connected in series, the total resistance is the sum of their individual resistances.</p>

<p><b>Rule:</b></p>
<p>R<sub>T</sub> = R₁ + R₂ + R₃</p>

<p>Adding a resistor in series increases the total resistance because it adds more opposition to current along the single path.</p>

<h3>2. Worked example 1: Two resistors</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> Resistors of 2 Ω and 5 Ω are connected in series. Find the total resistance.</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Use R<sub>T</sub> = R₁ + R₂.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Substitute: R<sub>T</sub> = 2 + 5.</p>
<p><b>Step 3:</b> Calculate: R<sub>T</sub> = 7 Ω.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> 7 Ω.</p>

<h3>3. Worked example 2: Three resistors</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> Three resistors of 4 Ω, 6 Ω and 10 Ω are connected in series. Find the total resistance.</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Add all three resistances.</p>
<p>R<sub>T</sub> = 4 + 6 + 10</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Calculate: R<sub>T</sub> = 20 Ω.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> 20 Ω.</p>

<h3>4. Worked example 3: Finding an unknown resistance</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> Two resistors in series have a total resistance of 18 Ω. One resistor is 11 Ω. Find the resistance of the other resistor.</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Write: R<sub>T</sub> = R₁ + R₂.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Substitute: 18 = 11 + R₂.</p>
<p><b>Step 3:</b> Rearrange: R₂ = 18 − 11 = 7 Ω.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> 7 Ω.</p>

<h3>Remember</h3>
<p>For resistors in series, <b>add the resistances</b>. The total resistance is greater than any one positive individual resistance.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Total Resistance in a Series Circuit', 15),
  (642, 642, '<h2>Solving Series Circuit Problems</h2>

<h3>Learning objective</h3>
<p>Solve a series-circuit calculation by finding total resistance first, then current, then voltage drops where required.</p>

<h3>1. The solving sequence</h3>
<p>For a series circuit with a known supply voltage and resistances:</p>
<ol>
<li>Add the resistances to find total resistance.</li>
<li>Use Ohm''s law to calculate current: I = V/R.</li>
<li>Use V = IR to calculate the voltage drop across each resistor if required.</li>
</ol>

<p>Remember: current is the same through every resistor in series, and the voltage drops add up to the supply voltage.</p>

<h3>2. Worked example 1: Finding total resistance and current</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> A 12 V battery is connected to 2 Ω and 4 Ω resistors in series. Find the total resistance and current.</p>
<p><b>Step 1: Find total resistance.</b></p>
<p>R<sub>T</sub> = 2 + 4 = 6 Ω.</p>
<p><b>Step 2: Apply Ohm''s law.</b></p>
<p>I = V/R<sub>T</sub> = 12/6.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> Total resistance = 6 Ω; current = 2 A.</p>

<h3>3. Worked example 2: Finding voltage drops</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> A 10 V supply is connected to 2 Ω and 3 Ω resistors in series. Find the current and the voltage drop across each resistor.</p>
<p><b>Step 1: Total resistance.</b></p>
<p>R<sub>T</sub> = 2 + 3 = 5 Ω.</p>
<p><b>Step 2: Calculate current.</b></p>
<p>I = 10/5 = 2 A.</p>
<p><b>Step 3: Voltage across the 2 Ω resistor.</b></p>
<p>V₁ = IR₁ = 2 × 2 = 4 V.</p>
<p><b>Step 4: Voltage across the 3 Ω resistor.</b></p>
<p>V₂ = IR₂ = 2 × 3 = 6 V.</p>
<p><b>Check:</b> 4 + 6 = 10 V, which equals the supply.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> Current = 2 A; voltage drops = 4 V and 6 V.</p>

<h3>4. Worked example 3: Finding an unknown resistor</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> A 12 V supply drives a current of 2 A through two resistors in series. One resistor is 4 Ω. Find the other resistance.</p>
<p><b>Step 1: Find total resistance using Ohm''s law.</b></p>
<p>R<sub>T</sub> = V/I = 12/2 = 6 Ω.</p>
<p><b>Step 2: Subtract the known resistance.</b></p>
<p>R₂ = R<sub>T</sub> − R₁ = 6 − 4 = 2 Ω.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> The unknown resistor is 2 Ω.</p>

<h3>Remember</h3>
<p>Follow the order: <b>total resistance → current → individual voltage drops.</b> Check that all voltage drops add up to the supply voltage.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Solving Series Circuit Problems', 15),
  (643, 643, '<h2>Identifying a Parallel Circuit</h2>

<h3>Learning objective</h3>
<p>Identify a parallel circuit by recognising components connected on separate branches between the same two junctions.</p>

<h3>1. What is a parallel circuit?</h3>
<p>A <b>parallel circuit</b> has two or more branches through which electric current can flow.</p>

<p>At a junction, current can divide between the available branches. The branches join again at another junction.</p>

<p><b>Key features:</b></p>
<ul>
<li>There is more than one path for current.</li>
<li>Each branch connects across the same two junctions.</li>
<li>If one branch breaks, current may still flow through the other complete branches.</li>
</ul>

<h3>2. Worked example 1: Identifying the arrangement</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> Two lamps are connected on separate branches between the same two junctions. What type of circuit is this?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Observe whether the lamps share one path or occupy separate branches.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Each lamp has its own branch between the junctions.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> It is a parallel circuit.</p>

<h3>3. Worked example 2: Counting branches</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> Three resistors are connected on three separate branches across a battery. How many branches contain resistors?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Each resistor occupies a separate branch.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Count the branches: one, two, three.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> Three branches.</p>

<h3>4. Worked example 3: One branch breaks</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> Two lamps are connected in parallel. One lamp is removed, leaving its branch open. What happens to the other lamp, assuming its branch remains complete?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Removing one lamp breaks only its branch.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> The other lamp still has a complete path through the supply.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> The other lamp can remain lit.</p>

<h3>Remember</h3>
<p><b>Parallel = separate branches.</b> A break in one branch does not necessarily stop current in the other branches.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Identifying a Parallel Circuit', 15),
  (644, 644, '<h2>Potential Difference in a Parallel Circuit</h2>

<h3>Learning objective</h3>
<p>Determine the potential difference across each branch of a parallel circuit.</p>

<h3>1. The voltage rule</h3>
<p>In a parallel circuit, each branch is connected across the same two junctions. Therefore, the potential difference across each branch is the same as the supply potential difference for an ideal circuit with connecting wires of negligible resistance.</p>

<p><b>Rule:</b></p>
<p>V<sub>supply</sub> = V₁ = V₂ = V₃</p>

<p>This does not mean that the current in every branch is the same. Branch currents depend on the resistance of each branch.</p>

<h3>2. Worked example 1: Finding branch voltage</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> Two resistors are connected in parallel across a 12 V supply. Find the voltage across the second resistor.</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Each branch is connected across the supply.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> The voltage across every branch equals 12 V.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> 12 V.</p>

<h3>3. Worked example 2: Three branches</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> Three lamps are connected in parallel across a 9 V battery. Find the potential difference across each lamp.</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> The three lamps occupy separate branches.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Each branch has the same potential difference as the supply.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> Each lamp has a potential difference of 9 V.</p>

<h3>4. Worked example 3: Finding an unknown supply voltage</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> A resistor in one branch of a parallel circuit has a potential difference of 6 V. The circuit is connected ideally across a battery. Find the supply voltage.</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> In parallel, branch voltage equals supply voltage.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> V<sub>supply</sub> = V<sub>branch</sub>.</p>
<p><b>Step 3:</b> V<sub>supply</sub> = 6 V.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> 6 V.</p>

<h3>Remember</h3>
<p><b>Parallel voltage is the same across each branch.</b> Do not add the branch voltages together to find the supply voltage.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Potential Difference in a Parallel Circuit', 15),
  (645, 645, '<h2>Total Current in a Parallel Circuit</h2>

<h3>Learning objective</h3>
<p>Calculate the total current supplied to parallel branches by adding the currents in the individual branches.</p>

<h3>1. The current rule</h3>
<p>At a junction, current divides between the available branches. When the branch currents join again, their total equals the current entering the junction.</p>

<p><b>Rule:</b></p>
<p>I<sub>total</sub> = I₁ + I₂ + I₃</p>

<p>The current in each branch depends on the branch resistance and the voltage across it. A branch with lower resistance carries more current than a branch with higher resistance when the branch voltages are equal.</p>

<h3>2. Worked example 1: Two branches</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> Two parallel branches carry currents of 2 A and 3 A. Find the total current supplied by the battery.</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Add the branch currents.</p>
<p>I<sub>total</sub> = 2 + 3.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Calculate: I<sub>total</sub> = 5 A.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> 5 A.</p>

<h3>3. Worked example 2: Three branches</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> Three branches carry currents of 0.5 A, 0.8 A and 0.7 A. Find the total current.</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Add all branch currents.</p>
<p>I<sub>total</sub> = 0.5 + 0.8 + 0.7.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Calculate: I<sub>total</sub> = 2.0 A.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> 2.0 A.</p>

<h3>4. Worked example 3: Finding an unknown branch current</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> A parallel circuit draws a total current of 4 A. One branch carries 1.5 A. Find the current in the other branch.</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Write I<sub>total</sub> = I₁ + I₂.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Substitute: 4 = 1.5 + I₂.</p>
<p><b>Step 3:</b> Rearrange: I₂ = 4 − 1.5 = 2.5 A.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> 2.5 A.</p>

<h3>Remember</h3>
<p><b>Total current = sum of branch currents.</b> Add currents to find the total; subtract a known branch current from the total to find an unknown branch current.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Total Current in a Parallel Circuit', 15),
  (646, 646, '<h2>Equivalent Resistance in a Parallel Circuit</h2>

<h3>Learning objective</h3>
<p>Calculate the equivalent resistance of two or more resistors connected in parallel.</p>

<h3>1. The parallel resistance rule</h3>
<p>For resistors in parallel, the reciprocal of the equivalent resistance equals the sum of the reciprocals of the individual resistances.</p>

<p><b>Rule:</b></p>
<p>1/R<sub>T</sub> = 1/R₁ + 1/R₂</p>

<p>For two resistors, this can also be written as:</p>
<p>R<sub>T</sub> = (R₁ × R₂)/(R₁ + R₂)</p>

<p>For positive resistances, the equivalent resistance of a parallel combination is always less than the smallest individual resistance. This happens because parallel branches provide additional paths for current.</p>

<h3>2. Worked example 1: Two equal resistors</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> Two 6 Ω resistors are connected in parallel. Find the equivalent resistance.</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Use the two-resistor formula.</p>
<p>R<sub>T</sub> = (R₁ × R₂)/(R₁ + R₂).</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Substitute the values.</p>
<p>R<sub>T</sub> = (6 × 6)/(6 + 6) = 36/12.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> 3 Ω.</p>

<h3>3. Worked example 2: Different resistances</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> Resistors of 3 Ω and 6 Ω are connected in parallel. Find the equivalent resistance.</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Apply the formula.</p>
<p>R<sub>T</sub> = (3 × 6)/(3 + 6).</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Calculate.</p>
<p>R<sub>T</sub> = 18/9 = 2 Ω.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> 2 Ω.</p>

<h3>4. Worked example 3: Three equal resistors</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> Three 6 Ω resistors are connected in parallel. Find the equivalent resistance.</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> For n equal resistors in parallel, R<sub>T</sub> = R/n.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Substitute: R<sub>T</sub> = 6/3.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> 2 Ω.</p>

<h3>Remember</h3>
<p>Do not add parallel resistances directly. Use the reciprocal rule or the two-resistor product-over-sum formula. Check that the answer is smaller than the smallest individual resistance.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Equivalent Resistance in a Parallel Circuit', 15),
  (647, 647, '<h2>Solving Parallel Circuit Problems</h2>

<h3>Learning objective</h3>
<p>Use the voltage rule, Ohm''s law and the current rule to solve numerical problems involving parallel branches.</p>

<h3>1. The solving sequence</h3>
<p>When the supply voltage and branch resistances are known:</p>
<ol>
<li>Recognise that the voltage across each branch equals the supply voltage.</li>
<li>Calculate each branch current using I = V/R.</li>
<li>Add the branch currents to find the total current.</li>
</ol>

<p>If the question asks for the equivalent resistance, you can also use R<sub>T</sub> = V/I<sub>T</sub>, or calculate it using the parallel resistance formula.</p>

<h3>2. Worked example 1: Finding branch currents and total current</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> A 12 V supply is connected across 6 Ω and 3 Ω resistors in parallel. Find the current in each branch and the total current.</p>
<p><b>Step 1: Voltage across each branch.</b></p>
<p>V₁ = V₂ = 12 V.</p>
<p><b>Step 2: Current through the 6 Ω resistor.</b></p>
<p>I₁ = V/R₁ = 12/6 = 2 A.</p>
<p><b>Step 3: Current through the 3 Ω resistor.</b></p>
<p>I₂ = V/R₂ = 12/3 = 4 A.</p>
<p><b>Step 4: Total current.</b></p>
<p>I<sub>T</sub> = I₁ + I₂ = 2 + 4 = 6 A.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> Branch currents are 2 A and 4 A; total current is 6 A.</p>

<h3>3. Worked example 2: Finding total current and equivalent resistance</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> A 10 V supply is connected across 5 Ω and 10 Ω resistors in parallel. Find the total current and equivalent resistance.</p>
<p><b>Step 1: Calculate each branch current.</b></p>
<p>I₁ = 10/5 = 2 A.</p>
<p>I₂ = 10/10 = 1 A.</p>
<p><b>Step 2: Add the branch currents.</b></p>
<p>I<sub>T</sub> = 2 + 1 = 3 A.</p>
<p><b>Step 3: Find equivalent resistance.</b></p>
<p>R<sub>T</sub> = V/I<sub>T</sub> = 10/3 ≈ 3.33 Ω.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> Total current = 3 A; equivalent resistance ≈ 3.33 Ω.</p>

<h3>4. Worked example 3: Finding an unknown branch resistance</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> A 12 V supply is connected across two parallel branches. The total current is 5 A, and one branch has a resistance of 4 Ω. Find the resistance of the other branch.</p>
<p><b>Step 1: Find the current in the known branch.</b></p>
<p>I₁ = V/R₁ = 12/4 = 3 A.</p>
<p><b>Step 2: Find the current in the unknown branch.</b></p>
<p>I₂ = I<sub>T</sub> − I₁ = 5 − 3 = 2 A.</p>
<p><b>Step 3: Use Ohm''s law to find the unknown resistance.</b></p>
<p>R₂ = V/I₂ = 12/2 = 6 Ω.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> The other branch has a resistance of 6 Ω.</p>

<h3>Remember</h3>
<p>Follow the sequence: <b>branch voltage → branch currents → total current.</b> Check that the branch currents add up to the total current and that every branch has the supply voltage across it.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Solving Parallel Circuit Problems', 15),
  (648, 648, '<h2>Magnetic Field Patterns</h2>

<h3>Learning objective</h3>
<p>Interpret magnetic field lines to determine the direction and relative strength of a magnetic field.</p>

<h3>1. What is a magnetic field?</h3>
<p>A <b>magnetic field</b> is the region around a magnet or current-carrying conductor where magnetic forces can be experienced.</p>

<p>We represent a magnetic field using magnetic field lines. These lines help us show the field''s direction and relative strength.</p>

<h3>2. Direction of magnetic field lines</h3>
<p>Outside a bar magnet, magnetic field lines run from the <b>north pole to the south pole</b>. Inside the magnet, they return from south to north, forming closed loops.</p>

<p>The arrows on a field-line diagram show the direction in which the north pole of a small test magnet would point.</p>

<h3>3. Strength of a magnetic field</h3>
<p>Where field lines are close together, the field is stronger. Where they are farther apart, the field is weaker.</p>

<p>Magnetic field lines do not cross. If they crossed, the field would have two directions at the same point.</p>

<h3>4. Worked example 1: Direction outside a bar magnet</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> Outside a bar magnet, in which direction do magnetic field lines run?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify the poles: north and south.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Recall the convention for the field outside a magnet.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> From north to south.</p>

<h3>5. Worked example 2: Comparing field strength</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> A diagram shows field lines crowded near one pole and spread farther apart near the other region. Where is the field stronger?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Compare the spacing between the field lines.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> The lines are closer together near the pole.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> The field is stronger near the pole where the lines are closer together.</p>

<h3>6. Worked example 3: Understanding field lines</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> Why must magnetic field lines not cross each other?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> A field line indicates the direction of the magnetic field at a point.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Crossing lines would assign two directions to the field at the same point.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> Field lines cannot cross because the magnetic field has one direction at any given point.</p>

<h3>Remember</h3>
<ul>
<li>Outside a bar magnet: north to south.</li>
<li>Closer field lines indicate a stronger field.</li>
<li>Magnetic field lines never cross.</li>
</ul>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Magnetic Field Patterns', 15),
  (649, 649, '<h2>Magnetic Field Around a Current-Carrying Wire</h2>

<h3>Learning objective</h3>
<p>Describe the shape and direction of the magnetic field around a straight current-carrying conductor.</p>

<h3>1. Current produces a magnetic field</h3>
<p>When electric current flows through a conductor, a magnetic field forms around the conductor.</p>

<p>A straight wire produces circular magnetic field lines centred on the wire. The circles lie in planes perpendicular to the wire.</p>

<p>This effect is called the <b>magnetic effect of electric current</b>.</p>

<h3>2. Direction of the field</h3>
<p>The direction depends on the direction of the current.</p>

<p>Use the <b>right-hand grip rule</b> for a straight wire:</p>
<ul>
<li>Point your right thumb in the direction of conventional current.</li>
<li>Your curled fingers show the direction of the magnetic field around the wire.</li>
</ul>

<p>Conventional current is taken to flow from the positive terminal towards the negative terminal through the external circuit.</p>

<h3>3. Strength of the magnetic field</h3>
<p>For the same straight conductor, increasing the current increases the magnetic field strength. The field becomes weaker as you move farther from the wire.</p>

<h3>4. Worked example 1: Shape of the field</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> What is the shape of the magnetic field around a long, straight wire carrying current?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify the conductor as a straight wire.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Recall the field pattern for a straight current-carrying wire.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> Concentric circles centred on the wire.</p>

<h3>5. Worked example 2: Increasing current</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> The current through a straight wire is increased while the observation point remains the same distance from the wire. What happens to the magnetic field strength?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> A current-carrying wire produces a magnetic field.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> The field strength increases when the current increases.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> The magnetic field becomes stronger.</p>

<h3>6. Worked example 3: Reversing current</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> The current in a straight wire is reversed. What happens to the direction of the magnetic field?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> The right-hand grip rule links current direction to field direction.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Reversing the current reverses the direction of the circular field.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> The magnetic field direction reverses.</p>

<h3>Remember</h3>
<p>A straight current-carrying wire produces circular magnetic field lines. More current produces a stronger field, and reversing the current reverses the field direction.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Magnetic Field Around a Current-Carrying Wire', 15),
  (650, 650, '<h2>Magnetic Field of a Solenoid</h2>

<h3>Learning objective</h3>
<p>Describe the magnetic field of a solenoid and explain why a current-carrying solenoid behaves like a bar magnet.</p>

<h3>1. What is a solenoid?</h3>
<p>A <b>solenoid</b> is a long coil of insulated wire wound into many turns. When electric current flows through the coil, it produces a magnetic field.</p>

<p>The field inside a sufficiently long solenoid is approximately uniform: the field lines are nearly parallel and evenly spaced. Outside, the field resembles that of a bar magnet.</p>

<h3>2. Magnetic poles of a solenoid</h3>
<p>A current-carrying solenoid has a north pole and a south pole. The poles depend on the direction of current around the coil.</p>

<p>Reverse the current, and the north and south poles exchange positions.</p>

<h3>3. Solenoid and bar magnet comparison</h3>
<ul>
<li>Both have north and south poles.</li>
<li>Both produce magnetic fields outside them that run from north to south.</li>
<li>The solenoid''s magnetic field can be switched off by stopping the current.</li>
<li>Its field can be strengthened by increasing current or adding more turns per unit length.</li>
</ul>

<h3>4. Worked example 1: Identifying a solenoid</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> A wire is wound into many close turns in a cylindrical shape. What is this coil called?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify the many turns of wire.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> A long coil of wire is called a solenoid.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> A solenoid.</p>

<h3>5. Worked example 2: Predicting the field inside</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> Describe the magnetic field inside a long, current-carrying solenoid.</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Consider the field pattern inside the coil.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Inside a sufficiently long solenoid, the field lines are nearly parallel and evenly spaced.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> The field is approximately uniform and directed along the solenoid''s axis.</p>

<h3>6. Worked example 3: Reversing the current</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> The current through a solenoid is reversed. What happens to its magnetic poles?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> The poles depend on current direction.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Reversing the current reverses the magnetic field direction.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> The north and south poles swap positions.</p>

<h3>Remember</h3>
<p>A solenoid is a coil of wire that produces a bar-magnet-like field when current flows. Its field can be controlled by changing the current or the coil''s construction.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Magnetic Field of a Solenoid', 15),
  (651, 651, '<h2>Factors Affecting Electromagnet Strength</h2>

<h3>Learning objective</h3>
<p>Identify how current, the number of coil turns and the core material affect the strength of an electromagnet.</p>

<h3>1. What is an electromagnet?</h3>
<p>An <b>electromagnet</b> is a magnet whose magnetic field is produced mainly by electric current. It is commonly made by winding insulated wire around a soft iron core.</p>

<p>Three important factors can increase its strength:</p>
<ol>
<li><b>Current:</b> Increasing current generally strengthens the magnetic field.</li>
<li><b>Number of turns:</b> Increasing the number of turns, with other conditions unchanged, generally strengthens the field.</li>
<li><b>Core material:</b> A suitable magnetic core, such as soft iron, strengthens the field compared with an air core.</li>
</ol>

<h3>2. Worked example 1: Increasing current</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> Two otherwise identical electromagnets have the same coil and core. One carries a larger current. Which is generally stronger?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Compare the currents while keeping the other factors the same.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> The larger current produces a stronger magnetic field.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> The electromagnet carrying the larger current is generally stronger.</p>

<h3>3. Worked example 2: Increasing the number of turns</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> Two coils have the same core and current. Coil A has 100 turns, while Coil B has 200 turns wound over a similar length. Which is generally stronger?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Compare the number of turns per unit length.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Coil B has more turns over a similar length.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> Coil B is generally stronger.</p>

<h3>4. Worked example 3: Choosing a core</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> A coil is tested first with an air core and then with a suitable soft iron core. Which arrangement generally produces the stronger electromagnet?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> The coil and current are kept the same.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Soft iron concentrates and strengthens the magnetic field.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> The coil with the soft iron core generally produces the stronger electromagnet.</p>

<h3>5. A fair test</h3>
<p>To investigate one factor properly, change only that factor and keep the others constant. For example, to test the effect of current, keep the coil''s turns and core the same.</p>

<h3>Remember</h3>
<p>To strengthen an electromagnet, increase current, increase turns per unit length, or use a suitable magnetic core. A controlled experiment changes one factor at a time.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Factors Affecting Electromagnet Strength', 15),
  (652, 652, '<h2>Applications of Electromagnets</h2>

<h3>Learning objective</h3>
<p>Explain how electromagnets are used to control movement, attract magnetic materials and operate electrical devices.</p>

<h3>1. Why use an electromagnet?</h3>
<p>An electromagnet produces a magnetic field when current flows through its coil. This makes it useful when a magnetic force needs to be controlled electrically.</p>

<p>Its field can be switched on or off by controlling the current. Its strength can also be changed by changing the current or the coil.</p>

<h3>2. Electric bells</h3>
<p>In a typical electric bell, current through a coil magnetises its iron core. The electromagnet attracts an iron armature, causing the hammer to strike the bell. The movement interrupts the circuit, the electromagnet releases the armature, and the circuit can reconnect. This repeated action produces the ringing sound.</p>

<h3>3. Magnetic relays</h3>
<p>A relay uses an electromagnet to move a switch. A small current in the coil can control a separate circuit, allowing a low-power control circuit to operate another circuit.</p>

<h3>4. Electromagnetic cranes</h3>
<p>Industrial cranes can use powerful electromagnets to lift iron or steel objects. Switching off the current reduces the magnetic attraction, allowing the load to be released, although residual magnetism may remain.</p>

<h3>5. Worked example 1: Lifting scrap metal</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> Why can an electromagnetic crane pick up steel objects and then release them?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> When current flows, the crane''s coil produces a magnetic field.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> The field attracts suitable ferromagnetic materials such as iron and steel.</p>
<p><b>Step 3:</b> When current is switched off, the electromagnet loses most of its field.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> It can lift metal when energised and release it when the current is switched off.</p>

<h3>6. Worked example 2: Operating a relay</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> What is the purpose of the electromagnet in a relay?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Current flows through the relay coil.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> The coil attracts an armature or moves a magnetic switch mechanism.</p>
<p><b>Step 3:</b> The movement opens or closes contacts in another circuit.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> The electromagnet allows one circuit to control a switch in another circuit.</p>

<h3>7. Worked example 3: The electric bell</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> Why does the hammer in a typical electric bell move repeatedly while the bell is operating?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Current magnetises the electromagnet and attracts the armature.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> The movement interrupts the current through the coil.</p>
<p><b>Step 3:</b> The electromagnet releases the armature, allowing the circuit to reconnect.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> The make-and-break action repeatedly energises and releases the electromagnet, causing the hammer to vibrate and strike the bell.</p>

<h3>Remember</h3>
<p>Electromagnets are useful because their magnetic effect can be controlled by electric current. Common applications include electric bells, relays and electromagnetic cranes.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Applications of Electromagnets', 15),
  (653, 653, '<h2>Density</h2>

<h3>Learning objective</h3>
<p>Calculate density, mass or volume using the relationship between these three quantities.</p>

<h3>1. What is density?</h3>
<p><b>Density</b> is the mass of a substance per unit volume. It tells us how much mass is contained in a given volume.</p>

<p><b>Formula:</b></p>
<p>Density = Mass ÷ Volume</p>
<p>ρ = m/V</p>

<p>Where:</p>
<ul>
<li>ρ is density.</li>
<li>m is mass.</li>
<li>V is volume.</li>
</ul>

<p>The SI unit of density is kg/m³. Another commonly used unit is g/cm³.</p>

<p><b>Useful conversion:</b> 1 g/cm³ = 1000 kg/m³.</p>

<h3>2. Worked example 1: Calculating density</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> A metal block has a mass of 600 g and a volume of 200 cm³. Calculate its density.</p>
<p><b>Step 1: Write the formula.</b></p>
<p>ρ = m/V</p>
<p><b>Step 2: Substitute the values.</b></p>
<p>ρ = 600/200</p>
<p><b>Step 3: Calculate.</b></p>
<p>ρ = 3 g/cm³.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> 3 g/cm³.</p>

<h3>3. Worked example 2: Calculating mass</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> A liquid has a density of 800 kg/m³ and a volume of 0.5 m³. Find its mass.</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Rearrange ρ = m/V to obtain m = ρV.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Substitute: m = 800 × 0.5.</p>
<p><b>Step 3:</b> Calculate: m = 400 kg.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> 400 kg.</p>

<h3>4. Worked example 3: Calculating volume</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> An object has a mass of 2 kg and a density of 500 kg/m³. Find its volume.</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Rearrange ρ = m/V to obtain V = m/ρ.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Substitute: V = 2/500.</p>
<p><b>Step 3:</b> Calculate: V = 0.004 m³.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> 0.004 m³.</p>

<h3>Remember</h3>
<p>Use ρ = m/V. Rearrange it to m = ρV when finding mass and V = m/ρ when finding volume. Always check that the units are consistent.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Density', 15),
  (654, 654, '<h2>Pressure</h2>

<h3>Learning objective</h3>
<p>Calculate pressure, force or area using the relationship between pressure and the area over which a force acts.</p>

<h3>1. What is pressure?</h3>
<p><b>Pressure</b> is the force acting normally per unit area of a surface.</p>

<p><b>Formula:</b></p>
<p>P = F/A</p>

<p>Where:</p>
<ul>
<li>P is pressure, measured in pascals (Pa).</li>
<li>F is the force acting perpendicular to the surface, measured in newtons (N).</li>
<li>A is the area, measured in square metres (m²).</li>
</ul>

<p>One pascal is one newton per square metre:</p>
<p>1 Pa = 1 N/m².</p>

<p>For the same force, a smaller contact area produces greater pressure. A larger contact area produces lower pressure.</p>

<h3>2. Worked example 1: Calculating pressure</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> A force of 200 N acts normally on an area of 4 m². Calculate the pressure.</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Use P = F/A.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Substitute: P = 200/4.</p>
<p><b>Step 3:</b> Calculate: P = 50 Pa.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> 50 Pa.</p>

<h3>3. Worked example 2: Calculating force</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> A pressure of 3000 Pa acts over an area of 0.2 m². Find the normal force.</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Rearrange P = F/A to obtain F = PA.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Substitute: F = 3000 × 0.2.</p>
<p><b>Step 3:</b> Calculate: F = 600 N.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> 600 N.</p>

<h3>4. Worked example 3: Calculating area</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> A force of 120 N produces a pressure of 600 Pa. Calculate the area.</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Rearrange P = F/A to obtain A = F/P.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Substitute: A = 120/600.</p>
<p><b>Step 3:</b> Calculate: A = 0.2 m².</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> 0.2 m².</p>

<h3>Remember</h3>
<p>Pressure depends on both force and area. For a fixed force, reducing the area increases pressure. Convert areas to m² before calculating pressure in pascals.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Pressure', 15),
  (655, 655, '<h2>Pressure in Liquids</h2>

<h3>Learning objective</h3>
<p>Explain how liquid pressure changes with depth and calculate the pressure due to a column of liquid.</p>

<h3>1. Why do liquids exert pressure?</h3>
<p>A liquid has weight. The liquid above a point exerts pressure on the liquid below it and on the container walls.</p>

<p>For a liquid of uniform density, the pressure due to the liquid at depth h is:</p>

<p><b>p = ρgh</b></p>

<p>Where:</p>
<ul>
<li>p is the pressure due to the liquid, in pascals (Pa).</li>
<li>ρ is the liquid density, in kg/m³.</li>
<li>g is gravitational field strength, in N/kg.</li>
<li>h is the vertical depth below the liquid surface, in metres (m).</li>
</ul>

<p>This formula gives the <b>gauge pressure</b> due to the liquid. If the liquid surface is exposed to the atmosphere, the absolute pressure also includes atmospheric pressure.</p>

<h3>2. What affects liquid pressure?</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Depth:</b> Pressure increases with depth.</li>
<li><b>Density:</b> At the same depth, a denser liquid produces greater pressure.</li>
<li><b>Gravity:</b> Greater gravitational field strength produces greater liquid pressure.</li>
</ul>

<p>At the same depth in the same stationary liquid, the pressure is the same. The shape of the container does not by itself determine the pressure at that depth.</p>

<h3>3. Worked example 1: Calculating liquid pressure</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> Calculate the pressure due to water at a depth of 2 m. Take water density as 1000 kg/m³ and g = 10 N/kg.</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Use p = ρgh.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Substitute: p = 1000 × 10 × 2.</p>
<p><b>Step 3:</b> Calculate: p = 20,000 Pa.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> 20,000 Pa.</p>

<h3>4. Worked example 2: Comparing depths</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> The pressure due to water at 1 m depth is 10,000 Pa when g = 10 N/kg. Find the pressure at 3 m depth.</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> The liquid and gravitational field are unchanged.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Pressure is directly proportional to depth.</p>
<p><b>Step 3:</b> Tripling the depth triples the pressure: p = 3 × 10,000.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> 30,000 Pa.</p>

<h3>5. Worked example 3: Comparing two liquids</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> At a depth of 0.5 m, a liquid has a density of 800 kg/m³. Calculate its pressure due to the liquid. Take g = 10 N/kg.</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Use p = ρgh.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Substitute: p = 800 × 10 × 0.5.</p>
<p><b>Step 3:</b> Calculate: p = 4000 Pa.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> 4000 Pa.</p>

<h3>Remember</h3>
<p>Use p = ρgh for pressure due to a liquid. Measure depth vertically from the surface and use density in kg/m³, depth in metres and g in N/kg.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Pressure in Liquids', 15),
  (656, 656, '<h2>Upthrust and Flotation</h2>

<h3>Learning objective</h3>
<p>Explain upthrust using pressure differences and apply the principle of flotation to determine whether an object sinks, floats or remains suspended.</p>

<h3>1. What is upthrust?</h3>
<p><b>Upthrust</b> is the upward force exerted by a fluid on an object that is partly or completely immersed in it.</p>

<p>In a liquid, pressure increases with depth. The bottom of an immersed object experiences greater pressure than the top, producing a resultant upward force.</p>

<h3>2. Archimedes'' principle</h3>
<p>Archimedes'' principle states that an immersed object experiences an upthrust equal to the weight of the fluid it displaces.</p>

<p><b>Upthrust = weight of displaced fluid</b></p>

<p>U = ρVg</p>

<p>Here, ρ is the fluid density and V is the volume of fluid displaced.</p>

<h3>3. When does an object float?</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Floats at rest:</b> Upthrust balances the object''s weight.</li>
<li><b>Sinks:</b> Its weight exceeds the available upthrust while it descends.</li>
<li><b>Suspended in a fluid:</b> Upthrust equals weight, with the object fully immersed and not rising or sinking.</li>
</ul>

<p>An object can float even if its material is denser than water. A hollow ship displaces a large volume of water, allowing the upthrust to balance its total weight.</p>

<h3>4. Worked example 1: Calculating upthrust</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> An object displaces 0.002 m³ of water. Calculate the upthrust. Take water density as 1000 kg/m³ and g = 10 N/kg.</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Use U = ρVg.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Substitute: U = 1000 × 0.002 × 10.</p>
<p><b>Step 3:</b> Calculate: U = 20 N.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> 20 N.</p>

<h3>5. Worked example 2: Determining whether an object floats at rest</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> An object weighs 15 N and experiences an upthrust of 15 N while floating. What is the resultant vertical force?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Upthrust acts upwards and weight acts downwards.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Resultant force = upthrust − weight.</p>
<p><b>Step 3:</b> Resultant force = 15 − 15 = 0 N.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> The resultant vertical force is zero, so the object can remain at rest.</p>

<h3>6. Worked example 3: Floating object</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> A floating object has a weight of 80 N. What is its upthrust when it is stationary?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> A stationary floating object is in vertical equilibrium.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> The upward upthrust balances the downward weight.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> Upthrust = 80 N.</p>

<h3>Remember</h3>
<p>Upthrust equals the weight of displaced fluid. A floating object at rest has upthrust equal to its weight.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Upthrust and Flotation', 15),
  (657, 657, '<h2>Viscosity</h2>

<h3>Learning objective</h3>
<p>Explain viscosity as resistance to flow and compare the flow behaviour of different fluids.</p>

<h3>1. What is viscosity?</h3>
<p><b>Viscosity</b> is a measure of a fluid''s resistance to flow. It describes how strongly a fluid resists layers of the fluid moving past one another.</p>

<p>A fluid with high viscosity flows less readily than a fluid with low viscosity under comparable conditions.</p>

<p>For example, honey generally flows more slowly than water because honey has a higher viscosity under ordinary room-temperature conditions.</p>

<h3>2. Comparing viscosity</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>High viscosity:</b> Greater resistance to flow.</li>
<li><b>Low viscosity:</b> Less resistance to flow.</li>
</ul>

<p>When comparing fluids, use similar conditions, especially temperature, because viscosity can change with temperature.</p>

<h3>3. Worked example 1: Comparing two liquids</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> Under the same conditions, honey flows more slowly than water. Which liquid has the higher viscosity?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify the liquid that resists flow more strongly.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Honey flows less readily.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> Honey has the higher viscosity.</p>

<h3>4. Worked example 2: Choosing a low-viscosity fluid</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> Two suitable liquids are available for a process that requires easy flow. Liquid A has lower viscosity than Liquid B. Which liquid should be selected if other relevant properties are equal?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Low viscosity means less resistance to flow.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Liquid A has the lower viscosity.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> Liquid A should flow more readily and is the appropriate choice.</p>

<h3>5. Worked example 3: Interpreting a flow test</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> Equal volumes of two liquids are released through the same suitable apparatus at the same temperature. Liquid X takes longer to flow through than Liquid Y. Which liquid appears more viscous in this test?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Both liquids are tested under the same conditions.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Liquid X flows less readily because it takes longer.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> Liquid X has the higher apparent viscosity in this comparison.</p>

<h3>Remember</h3>
<p>Viscosity measures resistance to flow. Higher viscosity means a fluid flows less readily under comparable conditions; lower viscosity means it flows more readily.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Viscosity', 15),
  (658, 658, '<h2>Structure of the Atom</h2>

<h3>1. The three subatomic particles</h3>
<p>An atom contains a small central nucleus surrounded by electrons.</p>

<ul>
  <li><b>Proton:</b> has a positive charge (+1) and is found in the nucleus.</li>
  <li><b>Neutron:</b> has no charge and is found in the nucleus.</li>
  <li><b>Electron:</b> has a negative charge (-1) and occupies the space around the nucleus.</li>
</ul>

<p>Nearly all the mass of an atom is concentrated in its nucleus because protons and neutrons are much heavier than electrons.</p>

<h3>2. Atomic number</h3>
<p>The atomic number, Z, is the number of protons in the nucleus.</p>
<p>In a neutral atom, the number of electrons equals the number of protons.</p>

<h3>3. Mass number</h3>
<p>The mass number, A, is the total number of protons and neutrons.</p>

<p><b>Mass number = number of protons + number of neutrons</b></p>
<p><b>Number of neutrons = mass number - atomic number</b></p>

<p>An atom can be represented as:</p>
<p><b>ᴬZX</b></p>
<p>X is the chemical symbol, A is the mass number, and Z is the atomic number.</p>

<h3>Worked example 1: Finding the number of neutrons</h3>
<p>An atom has atomic number 11 and mass number 23. Find its number of neutrons.</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Write the relationship.</p>
<p>Neutrons = mass number - atomic number</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Substitute.</p>
<p>Neutrons = 23 - 11</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> 12 neutrons.</p>

<h3>Worked example 2: Counting particles</h3>
<p>A neutral atom has atomic number 8 and mass number 16. Find its protons, electrons and neutrons.</p>
<p>Protons = atomic number = 8.</p>
<p>Electrons = protons because the atom is neutral = 8.</p>
<p>Neutrons = 16 - 8 = 8.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> 8 protons, 8 electrons and 8 neutrons.</p>

<h3>Worked example 3: Interpreting nuclear notation</h3>
<p>An atom is written as ²⁷₁₃Al. Determine its atomic number, mass number and number of neutrons.</p>
<p>Atomic number = 13.</p>
<p>Mass number = 27.</p>
<p>Neutrons = 27 - 13 = 14.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> Atomic number 13, mass number 27, and 14 neutrons.</p>

<h3>Remember</h3>
<p>The atomic number counts protons. The mass number counts protons and neutrons together. For a neutral atom, electrons equal protons.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Structure of the Atom', 15),
  (659, 659, '<h2>Radioactivity and Radioactive Decay</h2>

<h3>1. What is radioactivity?</h3>
<p>Radioactivity is the spontaneous and random emission of radiation from an unstable atomic nucleus.</p>

<p>The nucleus of a radioactive atom is unstable. It changes by emitting radiation and may become a different, more stable nucleus.</p>

<h3>2. Important properties</h3>
<ul>
  <li><b>Spontaneous:</b> decay occurs without an external trigger.</li>
  <li><b>Random:</b> it is impossible to predict exactly when a particular unstable nucleus will decay.</li>
  <li><b>Nuclear:</b> the change takes place in the nucleus, not in the electron arrangement.</li>
</ul>

<p>Radioactive decay is not the same as ordinary chemical change. Chemical reactions mainly involve electrons, while radioactive decay changes the nucleus.</p>

<h3>3. Activity and count rate</h3>
<p><b>Activity</b> is the number of nuclear decays per second. Its SI unit is the becquerel (Bq).</p>
<p>1 Bq = 1 decay per second.</p>
<p><b>Count rate</b> is the number of radiation counts recorded by a detector per unit time. It may be lower than the activity because not every emitted particle or photon is detected.</p>

<h3>Worked example 1: Interpreting activity</h3>
<p>A source has an activity of 250 Bq. What does this mean?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Remember that 1 Bq means one decay per second.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Apply the definition.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> The source undergoes an average of 250 nuclear decays per second.</p>

<h3>Worked example 2: Calculating total decays</h3>
<p>A source has a constant activity of 40 Bq over a short interval of 5 seconds. Estimate the number of decays in that interval.</p>
<p>Number of decays = activity × time.</p>
<p>Number of decays = 40 × 5 = 200.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> Approximately 200 decays.</p>
<p>This estimate assumes the activity remains approximately constant during the interval.</p>

<h3>Worked example 3: Correcting a detector reading</h3>
<p>A detector records 90 counts per minute when measuring a source and 10 counts per minute from background radiation. Find the source''s net count rate.</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify the total measured count rate: 90 counts/min.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Subtract background count rate: 10 counts/min.</p>
<p>Net count rate = 90 - 10 = 80 counts/min.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> 80 counts per minute.</p>

<h3>Remember</h3>
<p>Radioactive decay is spontaneous, random and nuclear. Activity measures decays per second; a detector''s count rate measures recorded counts.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Radioactivity and Radioactive Decay', 15),
  (660, 660, '<h2>Alpha, Beta and Gamma Radiation</h2>

<h3>1. Alpha radiation</h3>
<p>An alpha particle consists of two protons and two neutrons. It is the same as a helium nucleus.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Charge: +2</li>
  <li>Relative mass: 4</li>
  <li>Ionising ability: very high</li>
  <li>Penetrating ability: low; it can be stopped by paper or the outer layer of skin.</li>
</ul>
<p>Alpha radiation is particularly dangerous if an alpha-emitting substance enters the body through inhalation, ingestion or a wound.</p>

<h3>2. Beta radiation</h3>
<p>In the usual school-level example of beta-minus decay, a neutron changes into a proton and an electron is emitted. The emitted electron is called a beta-minus particle.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Charge: -1</li>
  <li>Relative mass: approximately 1/1840</li>
  <li>Ionising ability: moderate</li>
  <li>Penetrating ability: greater than alpha; it can be reduced by a thin sheet of aluminium.</li>
</ul>

<h3>3. Gamma radiation</h3>
<p>Gamma radiation is high-energy electromagnetic radiation emitted by an excited nucleus.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Charge: 0</li>
  <li>Rest mass: 0</li>
  <li>Ionising ability: lower than alpha and beta</li>
  <li>Penetrating ability: very high; thick lead or concrete can reduce its intensity.</li>
</ul>

<h3>4. Comparing the three</h3>
<p>Ionising ability: alpha &gt; beta &gt; gamma.</p>
<p>Penetrating ability: gamma &gt; beta &gt; alpha.</p>
<p>All three are ionising radiation, but they interact with matter differently.</p>

<h3>Worked example 1: Choosing a suitable absorber</h3>
<p>A school laboratory needs to reduce a beam of beta radiation. Which material is commonly suitable: paper, thin aluminium, or thick lead?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify the radiation as beta.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Recall that a thin sheet of aluminium can absorb much of the beta radiation.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> Thin aluminium is commonly used.</p>

<h3>Worked example 2: Comparing ionisation</h3>
<p>Arrange alpha, beta and gamma radiation from strongest to weakest ionising ability.</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Alpha particles are relatively massive and carry a +2 charge.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Beta particles carry a charge but have much smaller mass.</p>
<p><b>Step 3:</b> Gamma rays have no charge.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> Alpha, beta, gamma.</p>

<h3>Worked example 3: Identifying a neutral radiation</h3>
<p>Radiation from a source is unaffected by electric and magnetic fields and is highly penetrating. Which type is most likely?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Charged alpha and beta particles can be deflected by electric and magnetic fields.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Gamma radiation has no charge.</p>
<p><b>Step 3:</b> Gamma radiation is also highly penetrating.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> Gamma radiation.</p>

<h3>Remember</h3>
<p>Alpha is strongly ionising but weakly penetrating. Gamma is weakly ionising compared with alpha but highly penetrating. Beta lies between them for these properties.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Alpha, Beta and Gamma Radiation', 15),
  (661, 661, '<h2>Half-Life</h2>

<h3>1. Meaning of half-life</h3>
<p>The half-life of a radioactive isotope is the time taken for half the radioactive nuclei in a sample to decay.</p>
<p>For a sample with a constant half-life, the number of undecayed nuclei halves after each half-life.</p>

<p>The same halving pattern applies to activity, provided the measurement is corrected for background radiation.</p>

<h3>2. The halving pattern</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Initially: 100% remains.</li>
  <li>After one half-life: 50% remains.</li>
  <li>After two half-lives: 25% remains.</li>
  <li>After three half-lives: 12.5% remains.</li>
  <li>After four half-lives: 6.25% remains.</li>
</ul>

<p><b>Number of half-lives = elapsed time ÷ half-life</b></p>

<p>For simple questions:</p>
<p><b>Remaining amount = initial amount × (1/2)<sup>number of half-lives</sup></b></p>

<h3>Worked example 1: Finding the remaining mass</h3>
<p>A radioactive sample has a mass of 80 g. Its half-life is 5 hours. Find the mass of the radioactive isotope remaining after 10 hours.</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Number of half-lives = 10 ÷ 5 = 2.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> After 5 hours: 80 ÷ 2 = 40 g.</p>
<p><b>Step 3:</b> After 10 hours: 40 ÷ 2 = 20 g.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> 20 g remains.</p>

<h3>Worked example 2: Finding elapsed time</h3>
<p>A sample has an initial activity of 1600 Bq. Its activity falls to 200 Bq. If its half-life is 3 days, find the elapsed time.</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> 1600 Bq → 800 Bq after 3 days.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> 800 Bq → 400 Bq after 6 days.</p>
<p><b>Step 3:</b> 400 Bq → 200 Bq after 9 days.</p>
<p>Three half-lives have passed.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> 9 days.</p>

<h3>Worked example 3: Finding the half-life</h3>
<p>A sample''s activity falls from 600 counts per minute to 300 counts per minute in 8 hours. Assume background has been corrected. Find its half-life.</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> The activity has halved from 600 to 300.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> One half-life has therefore passed.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> 8 hours.</p>

<h3>Common mistake</h3>
<p>After two half-lives, 25% remains, not 0%. Radioactive decay reduces the amount in stages; it does not mean the entire sample disappears after one half-life.</p>

<h3>Remember</h3>
<p>Count how many times the amount halves. Then multiply that number of half-lives by the duration of one half-life to find elapsed time.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Half-Life', 15),
  (662, 662, '<h2>Uses and Safety of Radioactivity</h2>

<h3>1. Medical applications</h3>
<ul>
  <li><b>Medical tracers:</b> suitable radioactive isotopes help doctors investigate how organs function.</li>
  <li><b>Cancer treatment:</b> carefully controlled radiation can damage cancer cells.</li>
  <li><b>Sterilisation:</b> radiation can kill microorganisms on suitable medical equipment.</li>
</ul>

<h3>2. Industrial and scientific applications</h3>
<ul>
  <li><b>Thickness monitoring:</b> radiation detectors help monitor the thickness of paper or metal sheets.</li>
  <li><b>Industrial radiography:</b> penetrating radiation can reveal internal defects in some materials.</li>
  <li><b>Dating:</b> suitable radioactive isotopes can help estimate the age of archaeological or geological samples.</li>
</ul>

<h3>3. Radiation exposure and contamination</h3>
<p><b>Irradiation</b> means being exposed to radiation from a source. The radioactive material does not necessarily enter or remain on the person or object.</p>
<p><b>Contamination</b> means unwanted radioactive material gets onto or into a person or object. The material may continue emitting radiation until it is removed or decays.</p>

<h3>4. Basic radiation protection</h3>
<ul>
  <li><b>Reduce time:</b> spend as little time as reasonably possible near a source.</li>
  <li><b>Increase distance:</b> stay farther away where practical.</li>
  <li><b>Use shielding:</b> select shielding appropriate for the type and energy of radiation.</li>
  <li><b>Prevent contamination:</b> use proper containment, protective equipment and handling procedures.</li>
</ul>

<p>Never handle an unknown radioactive source. Laboratory sources must be controlled by trained personnel and according to safety procedures.</p>

<h3>Worked example 1: Choosing a use</h3>
<p>A factory needs to monitor whether a sheet of paper is becoming too thick during production. Which application is suitable?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> The task is to monitor material thickness.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> A suitable radiation source and detector can measure changes in the radiation reaching the detector.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> Radiation thickness monitoring.</p>

<h3>Worked example 2: Irradiation or contamination?</h3>
<p>A sealed source is used to irradiate a medical instrument. The source is removed, and no radioactive material is transferred to the instrument. Is this irradiation or contamination?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> The instrument was exposed to radiation.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> No radioactive material was transferred onto or into it.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> Irradiation, not contamination.</p>

<h3>Worked example 3: Applying radiation protection</h3>
<p>A trained worker must take a brief reading near a properly shielded radioactive source. State three general ways to reduce exposure.</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Reduce the time spent near the source.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Increase distance where practical.</p>
<p><b>Step 3:</b> Use appropriate shielding and follow the approved procedure.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> Reduce time, increase distance and use suitable shielding.</p>

<h3>Remember</h3>
<p>Radioactivity has useful medical, industrial and scientific applications. Safe use depends on controlling exposure, preventing contamination and following trained procedures.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Uses and Safety of Radioactivity', 15),
  (663, 663, '
  <h2>Meaning of Cathode Rays</h2>

  <p>
    Cathode rays are streams of electrons moving through
    a vacuum in a discharge tube.
  </p>

  <h3>Understanding the concept</h3>

  <p>
    Electrons are tiny particles that carry negative electric
    charge. When they are emitted from the cathode and move
    through the tube, the moving electrons form cathode rays.
  </p>

  <p>
    Cathode rays are therefore not ordinary light rays.
    They consist of moving particles.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> What are cathode rays?</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify the particles involved.</p>
  <p>They are electrons.</p>

  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Describe their movement.</p>
  <p>They move through a vacuum in a discharge tube.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> Cathode rays are streams of electrons
  moving through a vacuum in a discharge tube.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Are cathode rays made of light or
  particles?</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Recall what forms cathode rays.</p>
  <p>They are streams of electrons.</p>

  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Identify the nature of electrons.</p>
  <p>Electrons are particles.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> Cathode rays consist of particles,
  specifically electrons.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> A student says cathode rays are
  streams of protons. Identify the error.</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Recall the particles forming cathode rays.</p>
  <p>They are electrons, not protons.</p>

  <p><b>Step 2:</b> State the correction.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Cathode rays are streams of electrons.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Meaning of Cathode Rays', 15),
  (664, 664, '
  <h2>Negative Charge of Cathode Rays</h2>

  <p>
    Cathode rays carry negative electric charge because
    they consist of electrons.
  </p>

  <h3>Understanding the concept</h3>

  <p>
    Each electron carries a negative charge. A stream of
    electrons therefore carries negative charge.
  </p>

  <p>
    The charge of one electron is approximately
    -1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ C.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> What is the charge of cathode rays?</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify their constituent particles.</p>
  <p>Cathode rays consist of electrons.</p>

  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Recall the charge of an electron.</p>
  <p>An electron has a negative charge.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> Cathode rays carry negative charge.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Explain why cathode rays are negatively
  charged.</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Recall that cathode rays are electrons.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Recall that electrons carry negative charge.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> Cathode rays are negatively charged
  because they consist of negatively charged electrons.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> A particle beam is positively charged.
  Can it be a cathode ray beam as defined in this topic?</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Recall the identity of cathode rays.</p>
  <p>They are streams of electrons.</p>

  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Compare the charges.</p>
  <p>Electrons carry negative charge, not positive charge.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> No. Cathode rays consist of negatively
  charged electrons.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Negative Charge of Cathode Rays', 15),
  (665, 665, '
  <h2>Straight-Line Travel of Cathode Rays</h2>

  <p>
    Cathode rays travel in straight lines when they move
    through a uniform vacuum without deflecting fields.
  </p>

  <h3>Understanding the concept</h3>

  <p>
    An object placed in the path of cathode rays can block
    the beam. The region behind the object receives no direct
    rays, producing a shadow on a suitable fluorescent screen.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> State the direction of travel of
  cathode rays in the absence of deflecting fields.</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Recall the observed path of cathode rays.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> State the path.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> They travel in straight lines.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Why can an object produce a shadow
  in the path of cathode rays?</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Cathode rays travel in straight lines.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> The object blocks the rays.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> The region behind the object receives
  no direct rays.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> The object blocks the straight-moving
  cathode rays, producing a shadow.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> A solid object is placed between a
  cathode ray source and a fluorescent screen. What observation
  supports straight-line travel?</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> The object blocks the beam.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> The screen does not glow in the blocked
  region in the same way as the exposed region.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> A shadow forms behind the object.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Straight-Line Travel of Cathode Rays', 15),
  (666, 666, '
  <h2>Cathode Rays as Particles</h2>

  <p>
    Cathode rays can transfer momentum to objects they strike.
    This provides evidence that they consist of particles.
  </p>

  <h3>Understanding the concept</h3>

  <p>
    In a traditional discharge-tube experiment, cathode rays
    can cause a light paddle wheel to move. The moving
    electrons transfer momentum when they interact with the wheel.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> What does the movement of a light
  paddle wheel in a cathode ray tube demonstrate?</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Observe that the wheel moves when struck.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Recognize that momentum is transferred.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Relate momentum transfer to particle behaviour.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> The observation provides evidence that
  cathode rays consist of particles.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> What physical quantity is transferred
  when cathode rays push a paddle wheel?</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify the interaction between the
  electron beam and the wheel.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Identify the quantity responsible for
  the wheel''s motion.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> Momentum is transferred to the wheel.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> A paddle wheel does not move when
  exposed to a cathode ray beam. Does this single observation
  prove that cathode rays are not particles?</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Recall the purpose of the experiment.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Recognize that a lack of visible motion
  does not by itself establish the nature of the beam.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> No. The result alone does not prove that
  cathode rays are not particles.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Cathode Rays as Particles', 15),
  (667, 667, '
  <h2>Function of the Cathode</h2>

  <p>
    The cathode is the negative electrode in a discharge
    tube. In a cathode ray tube, it provides the electrons
    that form the cathode ray beam.
  </p>

  <h3>How it works</h3>

  <p>
    When the electron source is operated under suitable
    conditions, electrons are released from the cathode.
    These electrons can then be accelerated through the tube.
  </p>

  <p>
    The cathode is therefore the source of the electrons
    used to produce cathode rays.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> State the function of the cathode
  in a cathode ray tube.</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify the particles needed to form
  cathode rays.</p>
  <p>They are electrons.</p>

  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Identify the electrode that supplies them.</p>
  <p>The cathode supplies the electrons.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> The cathode provides electrons for
  the formation of cathode rays.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Is the cathode positive or negative?</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Recall the electrode arrangement in
  a cathode ray tube.</p>

  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Identify the cathode''s polarity.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> The cathode is the negative electrode.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> A student says that the cathode
  supplies protons to form cathode rays. Identify the error.</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Recall the particles forming cathode rays.</p>
  <p>They are electrons.</p>

  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Correct the student''s statement.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> The cathode supplies electrons, not
  protons, for the cathode ray beam.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Function of the Cathode', 15),
  (668, 668, '
  <h2>Electron Emission in a Discharge Tube</h2>

  <p>
    Electron emission is the release of electrons from
    a material. In a cathode ray tube, the electron source
    releases electrons that form the beam.
  </p>

  <h3>Understanding electron emission</h3>

  <p>
    Electrons are normally held within the material.
    Under suitable conditions, some electrons can escape
    from the electron source.
  </p>

  <p>
    In a traditional cathode ray tube, the cathode is
    commonly heated so that it releases electrons. This
    process is called thermionic emission.
  </p>

  <p>
    A potential difference then accelerates the emitted
    electrons through the tube.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> What is electron emission?</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify the particle involved.</p>
  <p>The particle is an electron.</p>

  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Describe what happens to the particle.</p>
  <p>It leaves the material from which it was held.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> Electron emission is the release of
  electrons from a material.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> What is thermionic emission?</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify the energy supplied to the cathode.</p>
  <p>The cathode is heated.</p>

  <p><b>Step 2:</b> State the result of heating.</p>
  <p>Electrons gain enough energy to escape from the material.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> Thermionic emission is the release of
  electrons from a material due to heating.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Why must electrons be released from
  the cathode before they can form a cathode ray beam?</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify what forms the beam.</p>
  <p>The beam consists of moving electrons.</p>

  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Determine where the electrons come from.</p>
  <p>They must first leave the electron source.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> Electrons must be released from the
  cathode so that they can be accelerated to form the beam.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Electron Emission in a Discharge Tube', 15),
  (669, 669, '
  <h2>Function of the Anode</h2>

  <p>
    The anode is the positive electrode in a cathode ray
    tube. It helps attract and accelerate electrons emitted
    by the cathode.
  </p>

  <h3>Understanding the concept</h3>

  <p>
    Electrons have negative charge. An electric field
    between the cathode and the positively charged anode
    exerts a force on the electrons.
  </p>

  <p>
    The electrons gain kinetic energy as they accelerate
    through the potential difference.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> State the function of the anode in
  a cathode ray tube.</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Recall the charge of the anode.</p>
  <p>It is positive.</p>

  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Identify the effect on electrons.</p>
  <p>The anode attracts electrons and helps accelerate them.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> The anode attracts and helps accelerate
  electrons emitted by the cathode.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Why are electrons attracted towards
  a positively charged anode?</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Electrons carry negative charge.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> The anode carries positive charge.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Opposite electric charges attract.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> Electrons are attracted towards the
  positive anode because opposite charges attract.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> What would happen to the acceleration
  of electrons if the accelerating potential difference
  were increased?</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Recall that a potential difference
  establishes an electric field.</p>

  <p><b>Step 2:</b> A larger accelerating potential
  difference provides a greater energy gain.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> The electrons gain more kinetic energy
  and leave the accelerating region at a higher speed,
  assuming the same initial conditions.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Function of the Anode', 15),
  (670, 670, '
  <h2>Acceleration of Electrons by Potential Difference</h2>

  <p>
    A potential difference between electrodes transfers
    electrical energy to electrons. The electrons gain
    kinetic energy as they accelerate through the electric field.
  </p>

  <h3>Energy relationship</h3>

  <p>
    For an electron accelerated from rest through a potential
    difference V, the kinetic energy gained is:
  </p>

  <p><b>KE = eV</b></p>

  <p>Where:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>KE is the kinetic energy gained, in joules.</li>
    <li>e is the magnitude of the electron charge,
    1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ C.</li>
    <li>V is the accelerating potential difference, in volts.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    If the electron starts from rest and its speed is much
    less than the speed of light, its kinetic energy is:
  </p>

  <p><b>KE = ½mv²</b></p>

  <p>
    Equating the expressions gives:
  </p>

  <p><b>eV = ½mv²</b></p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Energy gained</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Question:</b> An electron is accelerated from rest
    through a potential difference of 200 V. Calculate
    its kinetic energy gained.
  </p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Write the formula.</p>
  <p>KE = eV</p>

  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Substitute the values.</p>
  <p>KE = (1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹) × 200</p>

  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Calculate.</p>
  <p>KE = 3.2 × 10⁻¹⁷ J</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> 3.2 × 10⁻¹⁷ J</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Comparing energy gains</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Question:</b> Two electrons start from rest. One
    passes through 100 V and the other through 300 V.
    Which gains more kinetic energy?
  </p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Use KE = eV.</p>

  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Calculate the first energy gain.</p>
  <p>KE₁ = (1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹) × 100</p>
  <p>KE₁ = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁷ J</p>

  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Calculate the second energy gain.</p>
  <p>KE₂ = (1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹) × 300</p>
  <p>KE₂ = 4.8 × 10⁻¹⁷ J</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> The electron accelerated through
  300 V gains more kinetic energy.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Finding electron speed</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Question:</b> An electron starts from rest and is
    accelerated through 100 V. Calculate its final speed.
  </p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Equate the energy expressions.</p>
  <p>eV = ½mv²</p>

  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Rearrange to make v the subject.</p>
  <p>v = √(2eV/m)</p>

  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Substitute the values.</p>
  <p>
    v = √[(2 × 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ × 100) /
    (9.11 × 10⁻³¹)]
  </p>

  <p><b>Step 4:</b> Calculate.</p>
  <p>v ≈ 5.9 × 10⁶ m/s</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> Approximately 5.9 × 10⁶ m/s.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Acceleration of Electrons by Potential Difference', 15),
  (671, 671, '
  <h2>Fluorescence Caused by Cathode Rays</h2>

  <p>
    Cathode rays cause certain materials to emit visible
    light when the rays strike them. This effect is called
    fluorescence.
  </p>

  <h3>Understanding the concept</h3>

  <p>
    When fast-moving electrons strike a suitable fluorescent
    material, they transfer energy to it. The material then
    emits visible light.
  </p>

  <p>
    In a cathode ray tube, a fluorescent coating on the screen
    glows where the electron beam strikes it.
  </p>

  <p>
    Example: Zinc sulphide is a material that can fluoresce
    when struck by cathode rays.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> What is fluorescence?</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Consider what happens when cathode rays
  strike a suitable material.</p>

  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Identify the visible effect.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> Fluorescence is the emission of light
  by a material after it absorbs energy from incident
  radiation or particles.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Why does the screen of a cathode
  ray tube glow when struck by electrons?</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Electrons strike the fluorescent coating.</p>

  <p><b>Step 2:</b> They transfer energy to the coating.</p>

  <p><b>Step 3:</b> The coating emits visible light.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> The screen glows because the electrons
  transfer energy to its fluorescent coating, causing it
  to emit visible light.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Name one material that can fluoresce
  when struck by cathode rays.</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Recall examples of fluorescent materials.</p>

  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Identify a suitable material.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> Zinc sulphide.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Fluorescence Caused by Cathode Rays', 15),
  (672, 672, '
  <h2>Mechanical Effect of Cathode Rays</h2>

  <p>
    Cathode rays can exert a force on an object when the
    electrons transfer momentum to it.
  </p>

  <h3>Understanding the concept</h3>

  <p>
    A traditional experiment uses a light paddle wheel
    placed in the path of cathode rays. The beam can cause
    the wheel to move when momentum is transferred to it.
  </p>

  <p>
    This observation provides evidence that cathode rays
    consist of moving particles with momentum.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> What is meant by the mechanical
  effect of cathode rays?</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Recall that cathode rays consist of
  moving electrons.</p>

  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Consider what happens when the electrons
  strike an object.</p>

  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Identify the transfer of momentum.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> The mechanical effect is the ability
  of cathode rays to exert a force on an object through
  momentum transfer.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> What does the movement of a paddle
  wheel in a cathode ray experiment demonstrate?</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> The electron beam strikes the wheel.</p>

  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Momentum is transferred to the wheel.</p>

  <p><b>Step 3:</b> The wheel begins to move.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> The observation demonstrates momentum
  transfer and provides evidence of the particle nature
  of cathode rays.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Which physical quantity explains
  how cathode rays can change the motion of a paddle wheel?</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify the moving electrons.</p>

  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Identify the quantity transferred
  during their interaction with the wheel.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> Momentum.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Mechanical Effect of Cathode Rays', 15),
  (673, 673, '
  <h2>Deflection of Cathode Rays by an Electric Field</h2>

  <p>
    Cathode rays are deflected by an electric field because
    they consist of negatively charged electrons.
  </p>

  <h3>Understanding the concept</h3>

  <p>
    When an electron beam passes between oppositely charged
    parallel plates, the electric field exerts a force on
    the electrons.
  </p>

  <p>
    The electrons are attracted towards the positive plate
    and repelled by the negative plate.
  </p>

  <p>
    Consequently, the beam bends towards the positive plate.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Why are cathode rays deflected by
  an electric field?</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Cathode rays consist of electrons.</p>

  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Electrons carry negative charge.</p>

  <p><b>Step 3:</b> An electric field exerts a force on
  charged particles.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> Cathode rays are deflected because
  negatively charged electrons experience a force in
  an electric field.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Between which plates does a cathode
  ray beam bend when placed in an electric field?</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify the charge of the electrons.</p>

  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Apply the rule that opposite charges
  attract.</p>

  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Identify the direction of deflection.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> The beam bends towards the positive plate.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> An electron beam passes between two
  parallel plates. The upper plate is positive and the
  lower plate is negative. In which direction is the beam
  deflected?</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify the positive plate.</p>
  <p>It is the upper plate.</p>

  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Recall that electrons are negatively charged.</p>

  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Determine the direction of attraction.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> The beam is deflected upwards towards
  the positive plate.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Deflection of Cathode Rays by an Electric Field', 15),
  (674, 674, '
  <h2>Deflection of Cathode Rays by a Magnetic Field</h2>

  <p>
    Cathode rays can be deflected by a magnetic field
    because they consist of moving charged particles.
  </p>

  <h3>Understanding the concept</h3>

  <p>
    A moving charged particle experiences a magnetic force
    when its velocity has a component perpendicular to
    the magnetic field.
  </p>

  <p>
    The direction of the force depends on the direction
    of motion, the magnetic field, and the negative charge
    of the electrons.
  </p>

  <p>
    Reversing the magnetic field reverses the direction
    of the magnetic force, provided the electron velocity
    remains unchanged.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Why can a magnetic field deflect
  cathode rays?</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify cathode rays as moving electrons.</p>

  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Recall that electrons carry electric charge.</p>

  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Apply the effect of a magnetic field
  on moving charges.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> A magnetic field can exert a force
  on moving electrons, causing the beam to deflect.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> What happens to the direction of
  magnetic deflection if the magnetic field is reversed
  while the electron beam continues in the same direction?</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Recall that the magnetic force depends
  on the field direction.</p>

  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Reverse the field direction.</p>

  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Determine the effect on the force.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> The magnetic force reverses direction,
  so the beam deflects in the opposite direction.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Does a magnetic field always deflect
  a moving electron?</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Recall that magnetic force depends on
  the angle between velocity and magnetic field.</p>

  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Consider an electron moving parallel
  to the magnetic field.</p>

  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Determine the magnetic force.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> No. When the electron moves parallel
  or antiparallel to the magnetic field, the magnetic
  force is zero.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Deflection of Cathode Rays by a Magnetic Field', 15),
  (675, 675, '
  <h2>Energy and Momentum of Cathode Rays</h2>

  <p>
    Cathode rays possess kinetic energy and momentum
    because they consist of moving electrons.
  </p>

  <h3>Understanding the concept</h3>

  <p>
    A moving electron has kinetic energy due to its motion.
    It also has momentum, which depends on its mass and
    velocity.
  </p>

  <p>
    For speeds much lower than the speed of light, kinetic
    energy is given by:
  </p>

  <p><b>KE = 1/2 mv²</b></p>

  <p>Momentum is given by:</p>

  <p><b>p = mv</b></p>

  <p>Where:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>m is the mass of the electron.</li>
    <li>v is its speed.</li>
    <li>KE is its kinetic energy.</li>
    <li>p is its momentum.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Why do cathode rays possess kinetic energy?</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Recall that cathode rays consist of electrons.</p>

  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Recognize that the electrons are moving.</p>

  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Apply the definition of kinetic energy.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> Cathode rays possess kinetic energy
  because their electrons are moving.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Question:</b> An electron has mass
    9.11 × 10⁻³¹ kg and speed 2.0 × 10⁶ m/s.
    Calculate its momentum.
  </p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Write the formula.</p>
  <p>p = mv</p>

  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Substitute the values.</p>
  <p>p = (9.11 × 10⁻³¹)(2.0 × 10⁶)</p>

  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Multiply the numbers and combine powers.</p>
  <p>p = 18.22 × 10⁻²⁵</p>
  <p>p = 1.822 × 10⁻²⁴ kg m/s</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> 1.822 × 10⁻²⁴ kg m/s.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Question:</b> An electron has mass
    9.11 × 10⁻³¹ kg and speed 2.0 × 10⁶ m/s.
    Calculate its kinetic energy.
  </p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Write the formula.</p>
  <p>KE = 1/2 mv²</p>

  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Substitute the values.</p>
  <p>KE = 1/2 × 9.11 × 10⁻³¹ × (2.0 × 10⁶)²</p>

  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Square the speed.</p>
  <p>v² = 4.0 × 10¹²</p>

  <p><b>Step 4:</b> Calculate the energy.</p>
  <p>KE = 1.822 × 10⁻¹⁸ J</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> 1.822 × 10⁻¹⁸ J.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Energy and Momentum of Cathode Rays', 15),
  (676, 676, '
<h3>Main Parts of a Cathode Ray Tube</h3>

A cathode ray tube (CRT) is an evacuated glass tube in which a beam of electrons produces a visible spot on a fluorescent screen.

The main parts are:

1. <b>Electron gun:</b> Produces and directs a beam of electrons.
2. <b>Control grid:</b> Controls the number of electrons in the beam.
3. <b>Focusing system:</b> Makes the electron beam narrow.
4. <b>Accelerating anodes:</b> Increase the speed of the electrons.
5. <b>Deflection plates:</b> Change the direction of the beam.
6. <b>Fluorescent screen:</b> Produces a visible spot when struck by the beam.

The tube is evacuated so that electrons can travel with very few collisions with gas particles.

<h3>Worked Examples</h3>

<b>Example 1: Which part produces the electron beam?</b>
Step 1: The beam consists of electrons.
Step 2: The electron gun produces the beam.
Answer: Electron gun.

<b>Example 2: Which part changes the direction of the beam?</b>
Step 1: The beam must be moved to a different position.
Step 2: The deflection plates change its direction.
Answer: Deflection plates.

<b>Example 3: Which part displays the beam as a visible spot?</b>
Step 1: The beam strikes the end of the tube.
Step 2: The fluorescent screen emits light where the beam hits.
Answer: Fluorescent screen.
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Main Parts of a Cathode Ray Tube', 15),
  (677, 677, '
<h3>Function of the Electron Gun</h3>

The electron gun produces a narrow beam of electrons inside a cathode ray tube.

Its operation involves several stages:

1. The heated cathode emits electrons.
2. The control grid regulates the number of electrons passing through.
3. The focusing system concentrates the electrons into a narrow beam.
4. Accelerating anodes increase the speed of the electrons.

The electron gun prepares the beam before it reaches the deflection plates.

<h3>Worked Examples</h3>

<b>Example 1: Why is the cathode heated?</b>
Step 1: The cathode must release electrons.
Step 2: Heating provides energy for electron emission.
Answer: To enable the cathode to emit electrons.

<b>Example 2: Why does the electron gun contain a focusing system?</b>
Step 1: An unfocused beam spreads out.
Step 2: The focusing system concentrates the beam.
Answer: To produce a narrow beam.

<b>Example 3: What is the purpose of the accelerating anodes?</b>
Step 1: Electrons leave the cathode with relatively low speeds.
Step 2: The accelerating anodes increase their kinetic energy.
Answer: To accelerate the electrons.
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Function of the Electron Gun', 15),
  (678, 678, '
<h3>Function of the X-Deflection Plates</h3>

The X-deflection plates control the horizontal position of the electron beam on the fluorescent screen.

They produce a horizontal electric field when a potential difference is applied across them.

The electric field exerts a force on the negatively charged electrons, changing the direction of the beam.

Reversing the polarity of the plates reverses the direction of the horizontal deflection.

<h3>Worked Examples</h3>

<b>Example 1: Which plates control horizontal movement?</b>
Step 1: Horizontal movement is movement from left to right.
Step 2: The X-deflection plates control this movement.
Answer: X-deflection plates.

<b>Example 2: What happens when the polarity of the X-plates is reversed?</b>
Step 1: Reversing the polarity reverses the electric field.
Step 2: The force on the electrons reverses.
Answer: The beam deflects in the opposite horizontal direction.

<b>Example 3: What happens if no horizontal potential difference is applied?</b>
Step 1: There is no horizontal electric field due to the X-plates.
Step 2: There is no horizontal deflection caused by these plates.
Answer: The beam is not deflected horizontally by the X-plates.
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Function of the X-Deflection Plates', 15),
  (679, 679, '
<h3>Function of the Y-Deflection Plates</h3>

The Y-deflection plates control the vertical position of the electron beam on the fluorescent screen.

They produce a vertical electric field when a potential difference is applied across them.

The electric field exerts a force on the negatively charged electrons, changing the vertical direction of the beam.

Reversing the polarity of the Y-plates reverses the direction of the vertical deflection.

<h3>Worked Examples</h3>

<b>Example 1: Which plates control vertical movement?</b>
Step 1: Vertical movement is movement up or down.
Step 2: The Y-deflection plates control this movement.
Answer: Y-deflection plates.

<b>Example 2: What happens when the polarity of the Y-plates is reversed?</b>
Step 1: The electric field reverses.
Step 2: The force on the electrons reverses.
Answer: The beam deflects in the opposite vertical direction.

<b>Example 3: What happens if no vertical potential difference is applied?</b>
Step 1: No vertical electric field is produced by the Y-plates.
Step 2: The Y-plates cause no vertical deflection.
Answer: The beam is not deflected vertically by the Y-plates.
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Function of the Y-Deflection Plates', 15),
  (680, 680, '
<h3>Function of the Fluorescent Screen</h3>

The fluorescent screen converts the position of the invisible electron beam into a visible spot.

When the electron beam strikes the screen, the screen emits light at the point of impact.

The spot shows where the beam reaches the screen. If the beam changes direction, the spot moves to another position.

<h3>Worked Examples</h3>

<b>Example 1: Why is a fluorescent screen used in a CRT?</b>
Step 1: The electron beam cannot normally be seen directly.
Step 2: The screen emits visible light where the beam strikes it.
Answer: To make the position of the beam visible.

<b>Example 2: What does the position of the spot show?</b>
Step 1: The spot forms where the beam strikes the screen.
Step 2: Its position indicates where the beam reaches the screen.
Answer: The position of the electron beam.

<b>Example 3: What happens to the spot when the beam is deflected?</b>
Step 1: Deflection changes the direction of the beam.
Step 2: The beam reaches a different point on the screen.
Answer: The spot moves.
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Function of the Fluorescent Screen', 15),
  (681, 681, '
<h3>Formation of a Visible Spot on the Screen</h3>

A visible spot forms through this sequence:

1. The heated cathode emits electrons.
2. The electron gun forms the electrons into a beam.
3. The accelerating anodes increase the electrons'' speed.
4. The deflection plates direct the beam towards a position on the screen.
5. The beam strikes the fluorescent screen.
6. The screen emits light at the point of impact, producing a visible spot.

The spot changes position when the direction of the electron beam changes.

<h3>Worked Examples</h3>

<b>Example 1: What happens immediately before a visible spot is produced?</b>
Step 1: The electron beam reaches the screen.
Step 2: The screen emits light where the beam strikes it.
Answer: The electron beam strikes the fluorescent screen.

<b>Example 2: Why does a CRT need an electron gun before the deflection plates can control the beam?</b>
Step 1: The deflection plates act on an electron beam.
Step 2: The electron gun produces that beam.
Answer: The electron gun supplies the beam that the plates deflect.

<b>Example 3: Why does the spot move when the voltage across the deflection plates changes?</b>
Step 1: Changing the voltage changes the electric field.
Step 2: The field changes the force and direction of the electrons.
Step 3: The beam strikes a different point on the screen.
Answer: The spot moves because the beam is deflected to a new position.
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Formation of a Visible Spot on the Screen', 15),
  (682, 682, '
<h3>Energy Gained by an Electron</h3>

An electron gains kinetic energy when it is accelerated through a potential difference.

The energy gained is:

<b>E = eV</b>

Where:
<ul>
<li>E = energy gained in joules (J)</li>
<li>e = magnitude of the electron charge = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ C</li>
<li>V = potential difference in volts (V)</li>
</ul>

This equation gives the energy transferred to the electron. If the electron starts from rest and all the gained energy becomes kinetic energy, then:

<b>KE = eV</b>

<h3>Worked Examples</h3>

<b>Example 1: An electron moves through 10 V. Calculate the energy gained.</b>

Step 1: Use E = eV.

Step 2: Substitute:
E = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ × 10

Step 3: Calculate:
E = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁸ J

Answer: 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁸ J.

<b>Example 2: An electron moves through 250 V. Calculate the energy gained.</b>

E = eV
E = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ × 250
E = 4.0 × 10⁻¹⁷ J

Answer: 4.0 × 10⁻¹⁷ J.

<b>Example 3: An electron gains 8.0 × 10⁻¹⁷ J. Calculate the potential difference.</b>

Step 1: Rearrange E = eV:
V = E/e

Step 2: Substitute:
V = (8.0 × 10⁻¹⁷)/(1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹)

Step 3: Calculate:
V = 500 V

Answer: 500 V.
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Energy Gained by an Electron', 15),
  (683, 683, '
<h3>The Electron-Volt</h3>

The electron-volt (eV) is a unit of energy commonly used for particles such as electrons.

<b>One electron-volt</b> is the energy gained by an electron when it moves through a potential difference of one volt.

Since E = eV:

1 eV = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ J

To convert electron-volts to joules, multiply by 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹.

To convert joules to electron-volts, divide by 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹.

<h3>Worked Examples</h3>

<b>Example 1: Convert 5 eV to joules.</b>

E = 5 × 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹
E = 8.0 × 10⁻¹⁹ J

Answer: 8.0 × 10⁻¹⁹ J.

<b>Example 2: Convert 2.4 × 10⁻¹⁸ J to eV.</b>

E = (2.4 × 10⁻¹⁸)/(1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹)
E = 15 eV

Answer: 15 eV.

<b>Example 3: An electron gains 750 eV. Express its energy in joules.</b>

E = 750 × 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹
E = 1.2 × 10⁻¹⁶ J

Answer: 1.2 × 10⁻¹⁶ J.
', 'Canonical lesson notes for The Electron-Volt', 15),
  (684, 684, '
<h3>Speed of an Accelerated Electron</h3>

For an electron moving at a speed much less than the speed of light, its kinetic energy is:

<b>KE = ½mv²</b>

Where:
<ul>
<li>KE = kinetic energy in joules (J)</li>
<li>m = electron mass = 9.11 × 10⁻³¹ kg</li>
<li>v = electron speed in metres per second (m/s)</li>
</ul>

To calculate speed, rearrange:

<b>v = √(2KE/m)</b>

If the electron starts from rest and is accelerated through a potential difference V, then KE = eV. Therefore:

<b>v = √(2eV/m)</b>

This is a non-relativistic formula. It is suitable when the electron''s speed is much less than the speed of light.

<h3>Worked Examples</h3>

<b>Example 1: An electron has kinetic energy 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁸ J. Calculate its speed.</b>

Step 1:
v = √(2KE/m)

Step 2:
v = √[(2 × 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁸)/(9.11 × 10⁻³¹)]

Step 3:
v ≈ 1.87 × 10⁶ m/s

Answer: 1.87 × 10⁶ m/s.

<b>Example 2: An electron has kinetic energy 4.0 × 10⁻¹⁷ J. Calculate its speed.</b>

v = √[(2 × 4.0 × 10⁻¹⁷)/(9.11 × 10⁻³¹)]

v ≈ 9.37 × 10⁶ m/s

Answer: 9.37 × 10⁶ m/s.

<b>Example 3: An electron starts from rest and is accelerated through 100 V. Calculate its speed.</b>

Step 1: KE = eV
KE = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ × 100
KE = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁷ J

Step 2:
v = √(2KE/m)

Step 3:
v = √[(2 × 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁷)/(9.11 × 10⁻³¹)]

v ≈ 5.93 × 10⁶ m/s

Answer: 5.93 × 10⁶ m/s.
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Speed of an Accelerated Electron', 15),
  (685, 685, '
<h3>Potential Difference from Electron Speed</h3>

An electron accelerated from rest gains kinetic energy from the electrical energy supplied.

Therefore:

eV = ½mv²

Rearranging to make V the subject:

<b>V = mv²/(2e)</b>

Where:
<ul>
<li>m = 9.11 × 10⁻³¹ kg</li>
<li>v = electron speed in m/s</li>
<li>e = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ C</li>
<li>V = accelerating potential difference in volts</li>
</ul>

Use this relationship when the speed is known but the accelerating potential difference is not.

<h3>Worked Examples</h3>

<b>Example 1: An electron travels at 2.0 × 10⁶ m/s after acceleration from rest. Calculate the potential difference.</b>

Step 1:
V = mv²/(2e)

Step 2:
V = [(9.11 × 10⁻³¹)(2.0 × 10⁶)²]/(2 × 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹)

Step 3:
V ≈ 11.4 V

Answer: 11.4 V.

<b>Example 2: An electron travels at 4.0 × 10⁶ m/s. Calculate the potential difference.</b>

V = [(9.11 × 10⁻³¹)(4.0 × 10⁶)²]/(3.2 × 10⁻¹⁹)

V ≈ 45.6 V

Answer: 45.6 V.

<b>Example 3: An electron travels at 6.0 × 10⁶ m/s. Calculate the potential difference.</b>

V = [(9.11 × 10⁻³¹)(6.0 × 10⁶)²]/(3.2 × 10⁻¹⁹)

V ≈ 102.5 V

Answer: 102.5 V.
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Potential Difference from Electron Speed', 15),
  (686, 686, '
<h3>The Cathode-Ray Oscilloscope (CRO)</h3>

A cathode-ray oscilloscope is an instrument that displays electrical signals as a trace on a screen.

In a CRO:

1. The electron gun produces a beam of electrons.
2. The fluorescent screen makes the beam visible.
3. The input signal controls the vertical deflection of the beam.
4. A time-base voltage moves the beam horizontally with time.

The resulting trace can show how a voltage changes with time.

A CRO can be used to investigate the shape, amplitude and time period of a repeating signal.

<h3>Worked Examples</h3>

<b>Example 1: What is the main purpose of a CRO?</b>

Step 1: A CRO receives an electrical signal.
Step 2: It displays the signal as a trace on a screen.

Answer: To display electrical signals as traces.

<b>Example 2: What does the vertical movement of the trace represent?</b>

Step 1: The input signal controls vertical deflection.
Step 2: The vertical position represents the signal voltage, according to the instrument''s settings.

Answer: Changes in the input voltage.

<b>Example 3: What is the purpose of the time-base voltage?</b>

Step 1: The signal must be displayed as it changes over time.
Step 2: The time-base voltage moves the beam horizontally.

Answer: To move the beam horizontally with time.

<h3>Important distinction</h3>

The vertical deflection represents the input voltage, while the horizontal sweep represents time when the time-base is operating normally.
', 'Canonical lesson notes for The Cathode-Ray Oscilloscope', 15),
  (687, 687, '
<h3>Cathode Rays in Older Television Displays</h3>

Older television sets used cathode-ray tubes to form pictures on a screen.

The electron beam was directed across the screen in a repeated scanning pattern.

The intensity of the beam was varied according to the picture signal. This changed the brightness of different parts of the screen.

The fluorescent screen emitted light where the beam struck it. By controlling the beam''s position and intensity, the television formed a picture.

Colour CRT televisions used separate electron beams and coloured phosphor elements to produce coloured images.

<h3>Worked Examples</h3>

<b>Example 1: How did an older CRT television produce a picture?</b>

Step 1: An electron beam scanned across the screen.
Step 2: The beam''s intensity varied with the picture signal.
Step 3: The screen emitted different amounts of light at different positions.

Answer: By scanning the screen with a controlled electron beam.

<b>Example 2: Why was the beam scanned across the screen?</b>

Step 1: A picture contains information at many positions.
Step 2: The beam had to reach different positions to reproduce that information.

Answer: To form the picture across the screen.

<b>Example 3: What controlled the brightness of different parts of a CRT television picture?</b>

Step 1: The picture signal controlled the beam''s intensity.
Step 2: The beam caused the screen to emit light.
Step 3: Changes in beam intensity changed the brightness.

Answer: The intensity of the electron beam.

<h3>Remember</h3>

A CRT television forms an image by controlling the position and intensity of an electron beam on a fluorescent screen.
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Cathode Rays in Older Television Displays', 15),
  (688, 688, '
<h3>Meaning of Photoelectric Emission</h3>

Photoelectric emission is the release of electrons from the surface of a material when light of sufficiently high frequency falls on it.

The material is commonly a metal.

The emitted electrons are called <b>photoelectrons</b>.

Photoelectric emission occurs only when the incident light has a frequency at or above the material''s threshold frequency.

<h3>Worked Examples</h3>

<b>Example 1: Define photoelectric emission.</b>

Step 1: Light falls on a material''s surface.
Step 2: Electrons are released from the surface.

Answer: Photoelectric emission is the release of electrons from a material''s surface due to incident light of sufficiently high frequency.

<b>Example 2: Light of sufficient frequency falls on a metal and electrons leave its surface. What phenomenon has occurred?</b>

Step 1: Incident light strikes the metal.
Step 2: Electrons are released.
Step 3: This is photoelectric emission.

Answer: Photoelectric emission.

<b>Example 3: A student says photoelectric emission is the reflection of light from a metal. Is the student correct?</b>

Step 1: Reflection means light bounces off a surface.
Step 2: Photoelectric emission involves electrons leaving a surface.

Answer: No. Photoelectric emission is the release of electrons, not the reflection of light.
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Meaning of Photoelectric Emission', 15),
  (689, 689, '
<h3>Meaning of Photoelectrons</h3>

Photoelectrons are electrons emitted from a material''s surface during photoelectric emission.

They are electrons that originally belonged to the material but gained enough energy from incident light to escape its surface.

Photoelectrons have a negative electric charge because they are electrons.

<h3>Worked Examples</h3>

<b>Example 1: What are photoelectrons?</b>

Step 1: Suitable light falls on a material.
Step 2: Electrons are released from its surface.

Answer: The released electrons are called photoelectrons.

<b>Example 2: Are photoelectrons positively or negatively charged?</b>

Step 1: A photoelectron is an electron.
Step 2: Electrons carry negative charge.

Answer: Negatively charged.

<b>Example 3: A metal emits electrons when illuminated by suitable light. What name is given to the emitted electrons?</b>

Step 1: The electrons leave the metal due to incident light.
Step 2: Electrons emitted in this process are called photoelectrons.

Answer: Photoelectrons.
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Meaning of Photoelectrons', 15),
  (690, 690, '
<h3>Role of Incident Light in Photoelectric Emission</h3>

Incident light supplies energy to electrons in a material.

However, electrons are emitted only if the light frequency is high enough for the material.

Each material has a minimum required frequency called its <b>threshold frequency</b>.

- If the incident frequency is below the threshold frequency, photoelectric emission does not occur.
- If the incident frequency is at or above the threshold frequency, emission can occur.

Increasing the brightness of light does not make below-threshold-frequency light cause photoelectric emission.

<h3>Worked Examples</h3>

<b>Example 1: Why must incident light have a sufficiently high frequency?</b>

Step 1: Electrons need enough energy to escape the material.
Step 2: The frequency determines the energy of each photon.
Step 3: The photon energy must be sufficient for emission.

Answer: The light must provide photons with enough energy for electrons to escape.

<b>Example 2: A metal has a threshold frequency of 6.0 × 10¹⁴ Hz. Light of frequency 5.0 × 10¹⁴ Hz falls on it. Will photoelectric emission occur?</b>

Step 1: Compare the incident frequency with the threshold frequency.
Step 2: 5.0 × 10¹⁴ Hz is below 6.0 × 10¹⁴ Hz.

Answer: No, photoelectric emission will not occur.

<b>Example 3: Light below a metal''s threshold frequency is made brighter. Will this alone cause photoelectric emission?</b>

Step 1: Brightness changes the intensity of the light.
Step 2: The frequency remains below the threshold.
Step 3: The photons still lack the required energy.

Answer: No. Increasing intensity alone does not cause emission if the frequency remains below threshold.
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Role of Incident Light in Photoelectric Emission', 15),
  (691, 691, '
<h3>Meaning of a Photon</h3>

A photon is a discrete packet, or quantum, of electromagnetic radiation energy.

Light transfers energy through photons.

Each photon carries an amount of energy determined by the frequency of the radiation.

A photon has no electric charge and has zero rest mass.

<h3>Worked Examples</h3>

<b>Example 1: What is a photon?</b>

Step 1: Light transfers energy in discrete packets.
Step 2: Each packet is called a photon.

Answer: A photon is a discrete packet of electromagnetic radiation energy.

<b>Example 2: Does a photon have an electric charge?</b>

Step 1: A photon is not an electron or proton.
Step 2: A photon carries no electric charge.

Answer: No. A photon is electrically neutral.

<b>Example 3: What determines the energy carried by a photon?</b>

Step 1: Photons carry electromagnetic radiation energy.
Step 2: Photon energy depends on radiation frequency.

Answer: The frequency of the radiation.
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Meaning of a Photon', 15),
  (692, 692, '
<h3>Relationship Between Photon Energy and Frequency</h3>

The energy of a photon is given by Planck''s equation:

<b>E = hf</b>

Where:

- E = photon energy in joules (J)
- h = Planck''s constant = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J s
- f = frequency in hertz (Hz)

The equation shows that photon energy is directly proportional to frequency.

Therefore:

- Higher frequency means greater energy per photon.
- Lower frequency means less energy per photon.
- Doubling the frequency doubles the photon energy.

<h3>Worked Examples</h3>

<b>Example 1: What happens to photon energy when frequency doubles?</b>

Step 1: E = hf.
Step 2: If f doubles while h stays constant, E doubles.

Answer: The photon energy doubles.

<b>Example 2: Light A has twice the frequency of light B. Compare their photon energies.</b>

Step 1: E = hf.
Step 2: The energy ratio equals the frequency ratio.

Answer: Each photon of light A has twice the energy of each photon of light B.

<b>Example 3: Light has a frequency of 5.0 × 10¹⁴ Hz. Write the equation needed to calculate the photon energy.</b>

Step 1: Identify the given frequency.
f = 5.0 × 10¹⁴ Hz.

Step 2: Use Planck''s equation.
E = hf.

Step 3: Substitute h.
E = (6.63 × 10⁻³⁴)(5.0 × 10¹⁴) J.

Answer: E = hf = (6.63 × 10⁻³⁴)(5.0 × 10¹⁴) J.
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Relationship Between Photon Energy and Frequency', 15),
  (693, 693, '
<h3>Calculating the Energy of a Photon</h3>

Use Planck''s equation:

<b>E = hf</b>

Planck''s constant:

<b>h = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J s</b>

The frequency must be in hertz (Hz), and the energy obtained is in joules (J).

<h3>Worked Examples</h3>

<b>Example 1: Calculate the energy of a photon with frequency 4.0 × 10¹⁴ Hz.</b>

Step 1: E = hf.

Step 2: Substitute:
E = (6.63 × 10⁻³⁴)(4.0 × 10¹⁴)

Step 3: Multiply:
E = 26.52 × 10⁻²⁰ J
E = 2.652 × 10⁻¹⁹ J

Answer: 2.65 × 10⁻¹⁹ J.

<b>Example 2: Calculate the energy of a photon with frequency 6.0 × 10¹⁴ Hz.</b>

E = hf
E = (6.63 × 10⁻³⁴)(6.0 × 10¹⁴)
E = 3.978 × 10⁻¹⁹ J

Answer: 3.98 × 10⁻¹⁹ J.

<b>Example 3: Calculate the energy of a photon with frequency 9.0 × 10¹⁴ Hz.</b>

E = hf
E = (6.63 × 10⁻³⁴)(9.0 × 10¹⁴)
E = 5.967 × 10⁻¹⁹ J

Answer: 5.97 × 10⁻¹⁹ J.
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Calculating the Energy of a Photon', 15),
  (694, 694, '
<h3>Calculating Frequency from Photon Energy</h3>

Planck''s equation is:

<b>E = hf</b>

To calculate frequency, rearrange:

<b>f = E/h</b>

Where:

- f = frequency in hertz (Hz)
- E = photon energy in joules (J)
- h = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J s

Make sure energy is expressed in joules before using this equation.

<h3>Worked Examples</h3>

<b>Example 1: A photon has energy 3.315 × 10⁻¹⁹ J. Calculate its frequency.</b>

Step 1:
f = E/h

Step 2:
f = (3.315 × 10⁻¹⁹)/(6.63 × 10⁻³⁴)

Step 3:
f = 5.0 × 10¹⁴ Hz

Answer: 5.0 × 10¹⁴ Hz.

<b>Example 2: A photon has energy 1.326 × 10⁻¹⁹ J. Calculate its frequency.</b>

f = (1.326 × 10⁻¹⁹)/(6.63 × 10⁻³⁴)

f = 2.0 × 10¹⁴ Hz

Answer: 2.0 × 10¹⁴ Hz.

<b>Example 3: A photon has energy 5.304 × 10⁻¹⁹ J. Calculate its frequency.</b>

f = (5.304 × 10⁻¹⁹)/(6.63 × 10⁻³⁴)

f = 8.0 × 10¹⁴ Hz

Answer: 8.0 × 10¹⁴ Hz.
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Calculating Frequency from Photon Energy', 15),
  (695, 695, '
<h3>Meaning of Work Function</h3>

The work function is the minimum energy required to remove an electron from the surface of a material.

It is usually represented by the symbol φ (phi).

The work function depends on the material''s surface.

Its SI unit is the joule (J). It may also be expressed in electron-volts (eV).

For photoelectric emission to occur, the energy supplied by an individual photon must be at least equal to the work function.

<h3>Worked Examples</h3>

<b>Example 1: Define work function.</b>

Step 1: An electron is held within a material.
Step 2: Energy must be supplied to release it from the surface.

Answer: The work function is the minimum energy required to remove an electron from a material''s surface.

<b>Example 2: What symbol is commonly used for work function?</b>

Step 1: Identify the standard symbol used in photoelectric equations.
Step 2: Work function is represented by φ.

Answer: φ (phi).

<b>Example 3: A metal has a work function of 2.0 eV. What does this value represent?</b>

Step 1: Work function is the minimum energy required for electron emission.
Step 2: The value is 2.0 eV.

Answer: An electron requires at least 2.0 eV of energy to escape the metal''s surface.
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Meaning of Work Function', 15),
  (696, 696, '
<h3>Photon Energy and the Work Function</h3>

A photon can release an electron only if its energy is at least equal to the material''s work function.

The condition for photoelectric emission is:

<b>E ≥ φ</b>

Where:
- E = energy of an incident photon
- φ = work function of the material

Since E = hf, the condition can also be written as:

<b>hf ≥ φ</b>

Three cases are possible:

1. If E < φ, photoelectric emission does not occur.
2. If E = φ, emission can occur, with no excess kinetic energy in the ideal case.
3. If E > φ, emission can occur and the emitted electron can have kinetic energy.

<h3>Worked Examples</h3>

<b>Example 1: A photon has energy 2.5 eV. A metal has a work function of 2.0 eV. Can emission occur?</b>

Step 1: Compare the values.
2.5 eV > 2.0 eV.

Step 2: The photon energy exceeds the work function.

Answer: Yes, photoelectric emission can occur.

<b>Example 2: A photon has energy 1.5 eV. A metal has a work function of 2.0 eV. Can emission occur?</b>

Step 1: Compare the values.
1.5 eV < 2.0 eV.

Step 2: The photon does not have enough energy.

Answer: No, photoelectric emission does not occur.

<b>Example 3: A photon has energy 3.0 eV and the metal''s work function is 3.0 eV. What is the maximum kinetic energy of the emitted electron?</b>

Step 1: Use KEmax = E − φ.
Step 2: KEmax = 3.0 − 3.0 eV.

Answer: 0 eV in the ideal limiting case.
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Photon Energy and the Work Function', 15),
  (697, 697, '
<h3>Work Function from Threshold Frequency</h3>

The threshold frequency, f₀, is the minimum frequency of incident light that can cause photoelectric emission from a material.

At the threshold frequency, the photon has exactly the minimum energy needed to release an electron.

Therefore:

<b>φ = hf₀</b>

Where:
- φ = work function in joules (J)
- h = Planck''s constant = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J s
- f₀ = threshold frequency in hertz (Hz)

<h3>Worked Examples</h3>

<b>Example 1: A metal has a threshold frequency of 5.0 × 10¹⁴ Hz. Calculate its work function in joules.</b>

Step 1: φ = hf₀.

Step 2: Substitute:
φ = (6.63 × 10⁻³⁴)(5.0 × 10¹⁴).

Step 3: Calculate:
φ = 3.315 × 10⁻¹⁹ J.

Answer: 3.32 × 10⁻¹⁹ J.

<b>Example 2: A metal has a threshold frequency of 4.0 × 10¹⁴ Hz. Calculate its work function.</b>

φ = (6.63 × 10⁻³⁴)(4.0 × 10¹⁴)
φ = 2.652 × 10⁻¹⁹ J.

Answer: 2.65 × 10⁻¹⁹ J.

<b>Example 3: A material has a work function of 4.0 × 10⁻¹⁹ J. Calculate its threshold frequency.</b>

Step 1: Rearrange φ = hf₀.
f₀ = φ/h.

Step 2: Substitute:
f₀ = (4.0 × 10⁻¹⁹)/(6.63 × 10⁻³⁴).

Step 3: Calculate:
f₀ ≈ 6.03 × 10¹⁴ Hz.

Answer: 6.03 × 10¹⁴ Hz.
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Work Function from Threshold Frequency', 15),
  (698, 698, '
<h3>Meaning of Threshold Frequency</h3>

The threshold frequency is the minimum frequency of incident radiation required to cause photoelectric emission from a particular material.

It is represented by:

<b>f₀</b>

The threshold frequency depends on the material because different materials require different minimum energies to release electrons.

At the threshold frequency, the energy of each photon is exactly equal to the work function:

<b>hf₀ = φ</b>

Where:
- h = Planck''s constant
- f₀ = threshold frequency
- φ = work function

At this frequency, the emitted electron has zero maximum kinetic energy in the ideal model.

<h3>Worked Examples</h3>

<b>Example 1: Define threshold frequency.</b>

Step 1: Electrons require a minimum energy to escape a material.
Step 2: Photon energy depends on frequency.
Step 3: The minimum frequency that supplies sufficient photon energy is the threshold frequency.

Answer: The minimum frequency of incident radiation required to cause photoelectric emission from a material.

<b>Example 2: A metal has a threshold frequency of 5.0 × 10¹⁴ Hz. What does this value mean?</b>

Step 1: Identify the minimum frequency.
Step 2: The threshold frequency is 5.0 × 10¹⁴ Hz.

Answer: Light must have a frequency of at least 5.0 × 10¹⁴ Hz to cause photoelectric emission from the metal.

<b>Example 3: Why can two different metals have different threshold frequencies?</b>

Step 1: Different materials have different work functions.
Step 2: At threshold, φ = hf₀.
Step 3: Different work functions therefore correspond to different threshold frequencies.

Answer: Their work functions differ, so the minimum photon energies required for emission differ.

<h3>Key relationship</h3>

A greater work function corresponds to a greater threshold frequency because φ = hf₀.
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Meaning of Threshold Frequency', 15),
  (699, 699, '
<h3>Photoelectric Emission Below Threshold Frequency</h3>

When incident light has a frequency below a material''s threshold frequency, photoelectric emission does not occur.

The energy of each photon is:

<b>E = hf</b>

Below the threshold frequency:

<b>hf < φ</b>

Where:
- h = Planck''s constant
- f = incident light frequency
- φ = work function

Each photon has less energy than the minimum required to release an electron.

Increasing the intensity of the same below-threshold-frequency light does not solve this problem. It increases the number of photons arriving per second, but it does not increase the energy of each photon.

<h3>Worked Examples</h3>

<b>Example 1: A metal has a threshold frequency of 6.0 × 10¹⁴ Hz. Light of frequency 4.0 × 10¹⁴ Hz shines on it. Will electrons be emitted?</b>

Step 1: Threshold frequency = 6.0 × 10¹⁴ Hz.
Step 2: Incident frequency = 4.0 × 10¹⁴ Hz.
Step 3: The incident frequency is below the threshold.

Answer: No photoelectric emission occurs.

<b>Example 2: The intensity of the light in Example 1 is increased substantially, without changing its frequency. Will emission begin?</b>

Step 1: The frequency remains 4.0 × 10¹⁴ Hz.
Step 2: It remains below the threshold of 6.0 × 10¹⁴ Hz.
Step 3: Each photon still has insufficient energy.

Answer: No. Increasing intensity alone will not cause emission.

<b>Example 3: A metal has a threshold frequency of 5.0 × 10¹⁴ Hz. Which light can cause emission: 3.0 × 10¹⁴ Hz or 7.0 × 10¹⁴ Hz?</b>

Step 1: Compare each frequency with the threshold.
Step 2: 3.0 × 10¹⁴ Hz is below the threshold.
Step 3: 7.0 × 10¹⁴ Hz is above the threshold.

Answer: Light of frequency 7.0 × 10¹⁴ Hz can cause photoelectric emission.

<h3>Important exam point</h3>

For a given material, increasing intensity cannot cause photoelectric emission if the frequency remains below the threshold frequency.
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Photoelectric Emission Below Threshold Frequency', 15),
  (700, 700, '
<h3>Frequency and Intensity of Incident Light</h3>

Frequency and intensity describe different properties of light.

<b>Frequency</b> determines the energy carried by each photon:

<b>E = hf</b>

<b>Intensity</b> describes the rate at which energy is incident on a unit area. For light of a given frequency, increasing intensity generally means more photons arrive per second per unit area.

In the photoelectric effect, these properties have different effects.

- Increasing frequency increases the energy of each photon.
- Increasing intensity at a fixed frequency increases the photon arrival rate.
- If frequency is below threshold, increasing intensity alone does not cause emission.
- If frequency is above threshold, increasing intensity generally increases the photoelectric current, provided other conditions remain unchanged.

<h3>Worked Examples</h3>

<b>Example 1: Light above threshold frequency is made more intense. What happens to the energy of each photon?</b>

Step 1: Photon energy is E = hf.
Step 2: The frequency remains constant.
Step 3: The energy of each photon therefore remains constant.

Answer: The energy of each photon does not change.

<b>Example 2: Light above threshold frequency is made more intense. What generally happens to the photoelectric current?</b>

Step 1: The photon energy remains sufficient for emission.
Step 2: Greater intensity means more photons arrive per second per unit area.
Step 3: More photoelectrons can be emitted per second.

Answer: The photoelectric current generally increases.

<b>Example 3: Two beams have the same intensity, but beam A has a higher frequency than beam B. Compare the energy of their individual photons.</b>

Step 1: Photon energy is E = hf.
Step 2: Beam A has the higher frequency.
Step 3: Its individual photons have greater energy.

Answer: Photons in beam A have greater energy.

<h3>Do not confuse these effects</h3>

Frequency controls energy per photon. Intensity relates to the amount of radiation arriving per unit area per second. In the usual school-level photoelectric model, frequency determines whether the threshold condition is met.
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Frequency and Intensity of Incident Light', 15),
  (701, 701, '
<h3>Einstein''s Photoelectric Equation</h3>

When a photon strikes a suitable material, it can transfer its energy to an electron.

Part of this energy is used to release the electron from the material. Any remaining energy becomes the kinetic energy of the emitted electron.

The energy balance is:

<b>hf = φ + KEmax</b>

Rearranging:

<b>KEmax = hf − φ</b>

Where:
- h = Planck''s constant = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J s
- f = frequency of incident light in Hz
- φ = work function of the material in J
- KEmax = maximum kinetic energy of emitted electrons in J

The equation applies when photoelectric emission occurs.

If the photon energy is exactly equal to the work function, the maximum kinetic energy is zero in the ideal model.

If the photon energy exceeds the work function, the excess energy becomes kinetic energy.

<h3>Worked Examples</h3>

<b>Example 1: State Einstein''s photoelectric equation.</b>

Step 1: The incident photon supplies energy hf.
Step 2: Energy is used to overcome the work function φ.
Step 3: The remaining energy becomes maximum kinetic energy.

Answer: hf = φ + KEmax.

<b>Example 2: A photon has energy 5.0 eV and the work function is 2.0 eV. Explain how the energy is distributed.</b>

Step 1: Energy supplied = 5.0 eV.
Step 2: Energy used to release the electron = 2.0 eV.
Step 3: Remaining energy = 5.0 − 2.0 = 3.0 eV.

Answer: 2.0 eV overcomes the work function, and the maximum kinetic energy is 3.0 eV.

<b>Example 3: A photon has energy equal to the work function. What is the maximum kinetic energy?</b>

Step 1: KEmax = E − φ.
Step 2: E = φ.
Step 3: KEmax = φ − φ = 0.

Answer: 0 J in the ideal limiting case.

<h3>Important</h3>

Use consistent units. If photon energy and work function are in eV, kinetic energy will be in eV. If they are in joules, kinetic energy will be in joules.
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Einstein''s Photoelectric Equation', 15),
  (702, 702, '
<h3>Calculating Maximum Kinetic Energy</h3>

The maximum kinetic energy of an emitted photoelectron is calculated using:

<b>KEmax = hf − φ</b>

Alternatively, if photon energy E is already known:

<b>KEmax = E − φ</b>

Where:
- KEmax = maximum kinetic energy of the emitted electron
- E = energy of one incident photon
- φ = work function

Subtract the work function from the photon energy.

The photon energy must be at least equal to the work function for emission to occur.

<h3>Worked Examples</h3>

<b>Example 1: A photon has energy 4.5 eV and the work function is 2.0 eV. Calculate the maximum kinetic energy.</b>

Step 1:
KEmax = E − φ

Step 2:
KEmax = 4.5 − 2.0

Step 3:
KEmax = 2.5 eV

Answer: 2.5 eV.

<b>Example 2: A photon has energy 8.0 × 10⁻¹⁹ J and the work function is 3.0 × 10⁻¹⁹ J. Calculate the maximum kinetic energy.</b>

Step 1:
KEmax = E − φ

Step 2:
KEmax = 8.0 × 10⁻¹⁹ − 3.0 × 10⁻¹⁹

Step 3:
KEmax = 5.0 × 10⁻¹⁹ J

Answer: 5.0 × 10⁻¹⁹ J.

<b>Example 3: Light of frequency 8.0 × 10¹⁴ Hz falls on a material with a work function of 3.0 × 10⁻¹⁹ J. Calculate the maximum kinetic energy. Take h = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J s.</b>

Step 1:
KEmax = hf − φ

Step 2:
KEmax = (6.63 × 10⁻³⁴)(8.0 × 10¹⁴) − 3.0 × 10⁻¹⁹

Step 3:
KEmax = 5.304 × 10⁻¹⁹ − 3.0 × 10⁻¹⁹

Step 4:
KEmax = 2.304 × 10⁻¹⁹ J

Answer: 2.30 × 10⁻¹⁹ J.

<h3>Calculation check</h3>

If your calculated kinetic energy is negative, the photon energy is below the work function. In that case, photoelectric emission does not occur; do not report a negative kinetic energy for an emitted electron.
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Calculating Maximum Kinetic Energy', 15),
  (703, 703, '
<h3>Photon Frequency from Maximum Kinetic Energy</h3>

Einstein''s photoelectric equation is:

<b>hf = φ + KEmax</b>

To calculate the incident frequency, rearrange:

<b>f = (φ + KEmax)/h</b>

Where:
- f = incident frequency in Hz
- φ = work function in J
- KEmax = maximum kinetic energy in J
- h = Planck''s constant = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J s

Before substituting, make sure the work function and kinetic energy use the same energy unit.

If the values are given in eV, convert their sum to joules before dividing by h.

<h3>Worked Examples</h3>

<b>Example 1: A metal has a work function of 2.0 × 10⁻¹⁹ J. The emitted electrons have maximum kinetic energy 1.315 × 10⁻¹⁹ J. Calculate the incident frequency.</b>

Step 1:
f = (φ + KEmax)/h

Step 2:
f = [(2.0 × 10⁻¹⁹) + (1.315 × 10⁻¹⁹)]/(6.63 × 10⁻³⁴)

Step 3:
f = (3.315 × 10⁻¹⁹)/(6.63 × 10⁻³⁴)

Step 4:
f = 5.0 × 10¹⁴ Hz

Answer: 5.0 × 10¹⁴ Hz.

<b>Example 2: A material has a work function of 3.0 × 10⁻¹⁹ J and the maximum kinetic energy is 2.304 × 10⁻¹⁹ J. Calculate the incident frequency.</b>

f = (φ + KEmax)/h

f = [(3.0 × 10⁻¹⁹) + (2.304 × 10⁻¹⁹)]/(6.63 × 10⁻³⁴)

f = (5.304 × 10⁻¹⁹)/(6.63 × 10⁻³⁴)

f = 8.0 × 10¹⁴ Hz

Answer: 8.0 × 10¹⁴ Hz.

<b>Example 3: A metal has a work function of 2.5 eV and the maximum kinetic energy of emitted electrons is 1.5 eV. Calculate the incident frequency. Take 1 eV = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ J and h = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J s.</b>

Step 1: Add the energies.
E = 2.5 + 1.5 = 4.0 eV.

Step 2: Convert to joules.
E = 4.0 × 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹
E = 6.4 × 10⁻¹⁹ J.

Step 3: Calculate frequency.
f = E/h
f = (6.4 × 10⁻¹⁹)/(6.63 × 10⁻³⁴)
f ≈ 9.65 × 10¹⁴ Hz.

Answer: 9.65 × 10¹⁴ Hz.
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Photon Frequency from Maximum Kinetic Energy', 15),
  (704, 704, '[object Object]', 'Canonical lesson notes for Meaning of Stopping Potential', 15),
  (705, 705, '[object Object]', 'Canonical lesson notes for Stopping Potential and Maximum Kinetic Energy', 15),
  (706, 706, '[object Object]', 'Canonical lesson notes for Maximum Kinetic Energy from Stopping Potential', 15),
  (707, 707, '[object Object]', 'Canonical lesson notes for Photoelectric Cells', 15),
  (708, 708, '[object Object]', 'Canonical lesson notes for Automatic Light-Control Systems', 15),
  (709, 709, '[object Object]', 'Canonical lesson notes for Light Beam Detection', 15),
  (710, 710, '
  <h2>Meaning of Chemistry</h2>

  <p><strong>Chemistry</strong> is the branch of science that studies matter,
  its composition, structure, properties and the changes it undergoes.</p>

  <h3>What Chemistry Studies</h3>

  <p>Chemistry mainly deals with:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>What substances are made of.</li>
    <li>How particles are arranged.</li>
    <li>The properties of substances.</li>
    <li>How substances react with one another.</li>
    <li>The energy changes involved in chemical reactions.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Example</h3>

  <p>When magnesium burns in oxygen, a new substance called magnesium oxide
  is formed.</p>

  <p>Therefore chemistry can be used to investigate:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>the substances involved,</li>
    <li>what happens during the reaction,</li>
    <li>the new substance formed, and</li>
    <li>the energy released.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Importance of Chemistry</h3>

  <p>Chemistry is used in areas such as medicine, agriculture, manufacturing,
  food production, environmental protection and energy production.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Meaning of Chemistry', 15),
  (711, 711, '
  <h2>Laboratory Safety Rules</h2>

  <p>Laboratory safety rules are procedures followed to prevent accidents,
  injuries and damage to equipment during practical work.</p>

  <h3>Important Rules</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Wear appropriate protective equipment such as a laboratory coat and safety goggles.</li>
    <li>Do not eat or drink in the laboratory.</li>
    <li>Do not taste chemicals.</li>
    <li>Do not smell chemicals directly.</li>
    <li>Read labels before using chemicals.</li>
    <li>Keep the working area clean.</li>
    <li>Report accidents and spills immediately.</li>
    <li>Handle glassware carefully.</li>
    <li>Follow the teacher''s or laboratory instructions.</li>
    <li>Wash hands after practical work.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Why Safety Matters</h3>

  <p>Chemicals may be corrosive, poisonous, flammable or harmful if handled
  incorrectly. Laboratory equipment may also cause burns, cuts or other
  injuries.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Laboratory Safety Rules', 15),
  (712, 712, '
  <h2>Chemical Hazard Symbols</h2>

  <p>Hazard symbols are standard symbols placed on chemical containers to
  warn users about dangerous properties of the substance.</p>

  <h3>Common Hazards</h3>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Flammable:</strong> the substance can easily catch fire.</li>
    <li><strong>Corrosive:</strong> the substance can damage living tissue or corrode materials.</li>
    <li><strong>Toxic:</strong> exposure can cause serious harm or death.</li>
    <li><strong>Harmful/Irritant:</strong> the substance may cause harm or irritation.</li>
    <li><strong>Oxidising:</strong> the substance can cause or intensify combustion.</li>
    <li><strong>Explosive:</strong> the substance may explode under certain conditions.</li>
    <li><strong>Environmental hazard:</strong> the substance can harm the environment.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Using Hazard Information</h3>

  <p>A hazard symbol tells you what danger to expect. The information should
  be considered before handling the chemical.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Chemical Hazard Symbols', 15),
  (713, 713, '
  <h2>Safe Handling of Chemicals</h2>

  <p>Chemicals must be handled according to their properties and the
  instructions provided on their labels.</p>

  <h3>Important Practices</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Read the label before use.</li>
    <li>Use the correct chemical for the experiment.</li>
    <li>Use the smallest practical quantity.</li>
    <li>Wear suitable protective equipment.</li>
    <li>Keep chemical containers closed when not in use.</li>
    <li>Never return unused chemicals to the original container unless instructed.</li>
    <li>Use a fume cupboard when instructed for substances that release harmful vapours.</li>
    <li>Wash hands after handling chemicals.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Smelling a Chemical</h3>

  <p>Never place your nose directly over a chemical container. If instructed
  to detect an odour, use the wafting method: gently move vapour towards
  your nose from a distance.</p>

  <h3>Handling Acids</h3>

  <p>Concentrated acids can be corrosive. They must be handled carefully and
  according to laboratory instructions.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Safe Handling of Chemicals', 15),
  (714, 714, '
  <h2>Measuring Apparatus</h2>

  <p>Different apparatus are designed to measure different physical
  quantities accurately.</p>

  <h3>Common Measuring Apparatus</h3>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Measuring cylinder:</strong> measures liquid volume.</li>
    <li><strong>Burette:</strong> delivers accurately measured variable volumes of liquid, especially during titration.</li>
    <li><strong>Pipette:</strong> transfers a fixed, accurately measured volume of liquid.</li>
    <li><strong>Balance:</strong> measures mass.</li>
    <li><strong>Thermometer:</strong> measures temperature.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Choosing Apparatus</h3>

  <p>The apparatus selected depends on the quantity being measured and the
  required accuracy.</p>

  <p>For example, a measuring cylinder is suitable for ordinary volume
  measurements, while a burette or pipette is used when greater accuracy is
  required in analytical work.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Measuring Apparatus', 15),
  (715, 715, '
  <h2>Heating Apparatus</h2>

  <p>Heating apparatus is used to provide heat during laboratory experiments.</p>

  <h3>Common Heating Apparatus</h3>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Bunsen burner:</strong> provides a flame for heating.</li>
    <li><strong>Tripod:</strong> supports apparatus above a flame.</li>
    <li><strong>Wire gauze:</strong> supports glassware and distributes heat.</li>
    <li><strong>Evaporating dish:</strong> holds a solution during evaporation.</li>
    <li><strong>Crucible:</strong> used for strongly heating small amounts of solid substances.</li>
    <li><strong>Test tube:</strong> can hold small quantities of substances for heating.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Using a Bunsen Burner</h3>

  <p>The air hole affects the flame. A properly adjusted burner produces a
  suitable flame for laboratory heating.</p>

  <p>Glassware should be heated carefully to avoid thermal shock and breakage.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Heating Apparatus', 15),
  (716, 716, '
  <h2>Separation Apparatus</h2>

  <p>Different mixtures require different apparatus depending on the
  physical properties of their components.</p>

  <h3>Common Apparatus</h3>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Filter funnel:</strong> supports filter paper during filtration.</li>
    <li><strong>Filter paper:</strong> separates an insoluble solid from a liquid.</li>
    <li><strong>Evaporating dish:</strong> allows a solvent to evaporate from a solution.</li>
    <li><strong>Distillation flask:</strong> holds the mixture being distilled.</li>
    <li><strong>Condenser:</strong> cools vapour so that it changes back into a liquid.</li>
    <li><strong>Separating funnel:</strong> separates immiscible liquids.</li>
    <li><strong>Chromatography paper:</strong> provides a stationary surface for chromatography.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Choosing Apparatus</h3>

  <p>The separation method depends on differences in properties such as
  particle size, boiling point, solubility and density.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Separation Apparatus', 15),
  (717, 717, '
  <h2>Apparatus Used in Titration</h2>

  <p>Titration is a laboratory method used to determine the volume of one
  solution required to react completely with a measured volume of another
  solution.</p>

  <h3>Main Apparatus</h3>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Burette:</strong> delivers and measures a variable volume of solution accurately.</li>
    <li><strong>Pipette:</strong> transfers a fixed measured volume of solution.</li>
    <li><strong>Conical flask:</strong> contains the solution being titrated.</li>
    <li><strong>Funnel:</strong> helps fill the burette, but should normally be removed before taking the final burette reading.</li>
    <li><strong>White tile:</strong> makes the colour change easier to observe.</li>
    <li><strong>Indicator:</strong> shows the end point by a colour change.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Basic Arrangement</h3>

  <p>The burette is supported vertically above the conical flask. A measured
  volume of solution is placed in the flask, usually using a pipette.</p>

  <p>The burette tap controls the solution delivered into the flask.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Apparatus Used in Titration', 15),
  (718, 718, '
  <h2>Measuring Mass</h2>

  <p>Mass is the amount of matter in a substance. In the laboratory, mass is
  measured using a balance.</p>

  <h3>Common Units</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>gram (g)</li>
    <li>kilogram (kg)</li>
    <li>milligram (mg)</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Useful Conversions</h3>

  <p>1 kg = 1000 g</p>
  <p>1 g = 1000 mg</p>

  <h3>Measuring a Solid</h3>

  <p>If a container is used, its mass may be measured first. The container
  can then be tared or its mass subtracted from the combined mass.</p>

  <h3>Example</h3>

  <p>Mass of container = 25.0 g</p>
  <p>Mass of container + chemical = 43.5 g</p>

  <p>Mass of chemical:</p>

  <p><strong>43.5 − 25.0 = 18.5 g</strong></p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Measuring Mass', 15),
  (719, 719, '
  <h2>Measuring Volume</h2>

  <p>Volume is the amount of space occupied by a substance.</p>

  <h3>Common Units</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>litre (L)</li>
    <li>millilitre (mL)</li>
    <li>cubic centimetre (cm³)</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Important Conversions</h3>

  <p>1 L = 1000 mL</p>
  <p>1 mL = 1 cm³</p>
  <p>Therefore, 1 L = 1000 cm³.</p>

  <h3>Reading Liquid Volume</h3>

  <p>For many aqueous liquids in a transparent measuring vessel, the reading
  is taken at the bottom of the meniscus when the liquid is viewed at eye
  level.</p>

  <h3>Example</h3>

  <p>If a measuring cylinder contains 35 mL of water, its volume is:</p>

  <p><strong>35 mL = 35 cm³</strong></p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Measuring Volume', 15),
  (720, 720, '
  <h2>Measuring Temperature</h2>

  <p>Temperature indicates how hot or cold a substance is. In the laboratory,
  temperature is commonly measured using a thermometer or temperature
  sensor.</p>

  <h3>Common Unit</h3>

  <p>The degree Celsius (°C) is commonly used in school laboratory work.</p>

  <h3>Using a Thermometer</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Place the sensing part in the substance being measured.</li>
    <li>Do not allow the bulb to touch the bottom or sides of the container when this would affect the measurement.</li>
    <li>Allow the reading to become steady.</li>
    <li>Read the scale at eye level.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Temperature Change</h3>

  <p>Temperature change is calculated using:</p>

  <p><strong>Temperature change = final temperature − initial temperature</strong></p>

  <h3>Example</h3>

  <p>Initial temperature = 22°C</p>
  <p>Final temperature = 35°C</p>

  <p>Temperature change:</p>

  <p><strong>35 − 22 = 13°C</strong></p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Measuring Temperature', 15),
  (721, 721, '
  <h2>Reading Measuring Instruments</h2>

  <p>Accurate measurements depend on reading an instrument correctly.</p>

  <h3>Important Rules</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Identify the scale and units before reading.</li>
    <li>Determine the value of the smallest scale division.</li>
    <li>Place your eye at the correct level where appropriate.</li>
    <li>Read the correct part of the scale.</li>
    <li>Record the measurement with suitable precision.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Finding the Scale Division</h3>

  <p>Suppose a scale increases from 10 to 20 and there are 10 equal divisions.</p>

  <p>The value of one division is:</p>

  <p><strong>(20 − 10) ÷ 10 = 1 unit</strong></p>

  <h3>Meniscus</h3>

  <p>When reading many liquid volumes, the bottom of the meniscus is used.
  The eye should be level with the meniscus to reduce parallax error.</p>

  <h3>Recording Measurements</h3>

  <p>A measurement should include its numerical value and unit, for example
  <strong>25.0 cm³</strong> rather than simply writing <strong>25</strong>.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Reading Measuring Instruments', 15),
  (722, 722, '
  <h2>Meaning of Matter</h2>

  <h3>Core Concept</h3>

  <p><strong>Matter is anything that has mass and occupies space.</strong></p>

  <p>Every object or substance that is matter has two essential characteristics:</p>

  <ol>
    <li><strong>Mass:</strong> Matter has mass. Mass tells us how much matter an object contains. It is commonly measured in grams (g) or kilograms (kg).</li>
    <li><strong>Occupies space:</strong> Matter takes up space. The space occupied by an object or substance is called its volume. Volume may be measured in cubic centimetres (cm³), cubic metres (m³), or litres (L), depending on the quantity and context.</li>
  </ol>

  <p>Both characteristics are necessary when identifying matter. Something that has mass but does not occupy space, or occupies space but has no mass, would not fit the scientific definition of matter.</p>

  <h3>How to Identify Matter</h3>

  <p>When deciding whether something is matter, ask two questions:</p>

  <ol>
    <li>Does it have mass?</li>
    <li>Does it occupy space?</li>
  </ol>

  <p>If both answers are yes, it satisfies the definition of matter.</p>

  <h3>Examples of Matter</h3>

  <p><strong>Water:</strong> Water has mass and occupies space in a container.</p>

  <p><strong>Air:</strong> Air is matter even though it is usually invisible. Air has mass and occupies space. An inflated balloon expands because the air inside it occupies space.</p>

  <p><strong>A stone:</strong> A stone has mass and occupies space. Its mass can be measured using a balance, and its volume can be determined using an appropriate method.</p>

  <p><strong>Cooking oil:</strong> Cooking oil has mass and occupies space in a bottle or measuring container.</p>

  <h3>Things That Are Not Matter</h3>

  <p>Light, heat and sound are forms of energy, not matter. They do not satisfy the definition of matter as something that has mass and occupies space.</p>

  <p>Be careful not to confuse an object with the energy it produces. A glowing bulb is matter, but the light it emits is energy. A ringing bell is matter, but the sound it produces is energy.</p>

  <h3>Worked Examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: Is air matter?</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Check whether air has mass. Air has mass, even though it is difficult to notice without measuring it.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Check whether air occupies space. Air fills the inside of a balloon or container.</p>

  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> Air is matter because it has mass and occupies space.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: A stone has a mass of 250 g. Does this information alone establish that it is matter?</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> The measured mass of 250 g establishes that the stone has mass.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> A stone is a physical object that occupies space.</p>

  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> The stone is matter because it has mass and occupies space. The mass measurement alone demonstrates only one of the two defining characteristics.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: A learner says that light is matter because it comes from a physical bulb. Is the learner correct?</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Separate the source from what it produces. The bulb is a physical object, while the light emitted by it is energy.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Apply the definition of matter to the light itself, not to the bulb.</p>

  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> The learner is incorrect. The bulb is matter, but the light it emits is not matter.</p>

  <h3>Common Errors</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Assuming that invisible substances cannot be matter.</li>
    <li>Thinking that only solids are matter.</li>
    <li>Confusing energy produced by an object with the object itself.</li>
    <li>Using mass alone as the complete definition instead of including occupied space.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Meaning of Matter', 15),
  (723, 723, '
  <h2>Physical Properties</h2>

  <h3>Core Concept</h3>

  <p><strong>A physical property is a characteristic of a substance that can be observed or measured without changing the substance into a different substance.</strong></p>

  <p>Physical properties help us describe, compare and identify substances. Observing or measuring a physical property does not, by itself, involve the formation of a new substance.</p>

  <h3>Common Physical Properties</h3>

  <p><strong>1. Colour:</strong> The colour of a substance, such as the white colour of clean salt crystals.</p>

  <p><strong>2. State:</strong> Whether a substance is solid, liquid or gas under the stated conditions.</p>

  <p><strong>3. Density:</strong> Mass per unit volume. It describes how much mass is contained in a given volume.</p>

  <p><strong>4. Solubility:</strong> The ability of a substance to dissolve in a particular solvent under specified conditions.</p>

  <p><strong>5. Melting point:</strong> The temperature at which a substance changes from solid to liquid under specified pressure.</p>

  <p><strong>6. Boiling point:</strong> The temperature at which a liquid boils under specified pressure.</p>

  <p><strong>7. Electrical conductivity:</strong> The ability of a material to conduct electric current.</p>

  <p><strong>8. Hardness:</strong> A measure of a material''s resistance to scratching or indentation, depending on the test used.</p>

  <p><strong>9. Mass and volume:</strong> These can be measured to describe a sample of matter. They depend on the amount of sample, unlike many characteristic properties used to identify substances.</p>

  <h3>Observing a Property Versus Changing a Substance</h3>

  <p>Observing the colour of copper is a physical observation. Measuring the volume of water is a physical measurement. Dissolving sugar in water is also a physical process because the sugar can be recovered by removing the water under suitable conditions.</p>

  <p>By contrast, a property such as flammability describes how a substance can react chemically. The distinction is whether the characteristic can be observed or measured without changing the substance''s chemical identity.</p>

  <h3>Worked Examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: A learner measures 20 cm³ of water. Is volume a physical property?</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify what is measured. The measurement is the space occupied by the water.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Consider whether measuring the volume creates a new substance. It does not.</p>

  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> Volume is a physical property because it can be measured without changing water into another substance.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: A sample has a mass of 60 g and a volume of 20 cm³. Calculate its density.</strong></p>

  <p>Density = mass ÷ volume</p>

  <p>Density = 60 g ÷ 20 cm³</p>

  <p>Density = 3 g/cm³</p>

  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> The density of the sample is 3 g/cm³. Density is a physical property because it can be determined without changing the sample into a different substance.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: A learner records that a substance is blue and dissolves in water. Identify the physical properties reported.</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Blue describes the substance''s colour.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Dissolves in water describes its solubility in that solvent.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Neither observation requires the substance to become a different substance.</p>

  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> The two physical properties are colour and solubility in water.</p>

  <h3>Common Errors</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Calling a property physical merely because it can be seen. Flammability is a chemical property even though a flame can be seen.</li>
    <li>Confusing solubility with a chemical reaction. Dissolving does not always produce a new substance.</li>
    <li>Giving a numerical measurement without its unit.</li>
    <li>Confusing mass with density. Mass measures the amount of matter in a sample; density compares mass with volume.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Physical Properties', 15),
  (724, 724, '
  <h2>Chemical Properties</h2>

  <h3>Core Concept</h3>

  <p><strong>A chemical property describes a substance''s ability to undergo a chemical change that produces one or more different substances.</strong></p>

  <p>A chemical property concerns how a substance behaves when it reacts or undergoes another chemical change. Unlike a physical property, demonstrating a chemical property through a reaction changes the chemical identity of the reacting substance.</p>

  <h3>Common Chemical Properties</h3>

  <p><strong>1. Flammability:</strong> The ability of a substance to burn in a suitable environment. Burning involves a chemical reaction and produces new substances.</p>

  <p><strong>2. Reactivity with oxygen:</strong> The tendency of a substance to react with oxygen. For example, iron can react with oxygen in the presence of water to form rust.</p>

  <p><strong>3. Reactivity with water:</strong> The ability of a substance to react chemically with water. Different substances behave differently when exposed to water.</p>

  <p><strong>4. Reactivity with acids:</strong> The ability of a substance to react with an acid. The products depend on the substances and reaction conditions involved.</p>

  <p><strong>5. Corrosiveness:</strong> The ability of a substance to chemically damage or react with materials it contacts.</p>

  <h3>How to Recognise a Chemical Property</h3>

  <p>Ask whether the property describes a substance''s ability to react and form different substances.</p>

  <p>Flammability is a chemical property because burning produces new substances. The ability of iron to rust is a chemical property because rusting forms substances different from the original iron.</p>

  <p>Colour, density and melting point are physical properties. They can be observed or measured without changing a substance into a different substance.</p>

  <h3>Property Versus Evidence of a Reaction</h3>

  <p><strong>Property:</strong> A substance is flammable. This describes its ability to burn.</p>

  <p><strong>Change:</strong> The substance burns. This is a chemical change.</p>

  <p><strong>Possible evidence:</strong> Heat or light may be released, and new substances may form. However, no single visible sign should be treated as conclusive proof of a chemical reaction in every situation.</p>

  <p>Similarly, the ability of iron to rust is a chemical property. The actual formation of rust is a chemical change.</p>

  <h3>Worked Examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: A learner says that flammability is a physical property because a flame can be observed. Is this correct?</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify what flammability describes: the ability of a substance to burn.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Consider what happens during burning. The reacting substance undergoes a chemical reaction and forms different substances.</p>

  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> Flammability is a chemical property. The visibility of a flame does not make the property physical.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: Iron can react with oxygen and form rust. Identify the chemical property and the chemical change.</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> The ability of iron to react with oxygen describes its reactivity with oxygen.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> The actual formation of rust is the chemical change.</p>

  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> Reactivity with oxygen is the chemical property; rusting is the chemical change.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: A substance has a density of 5 g/cm³ and is flammable. Classify each property and explain why.</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Density can be measured without changing the substance into a different substance.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Flammability describes the substance''s ability to undergo a burning reaction that produces new substances.</p>

  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> Density is a physical property; flammability is a chemical property.</p>

  <h3>Common Errors</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Classifying flammability as physical because a flame is visible.</li>
    <li>Confusing a chemical property with the chemical change that demonstrates it.</li>
    <li>Assuming that every chemical reaction must produce an obvious colour change or bubbles.</li>
    <li>Calling rusting a physical change. Rusting involves a chemical reaction that forms new substances.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Chemical Properties', 15),
  (725, 725, '
  <h2>Solids</h2>

  <h3>Core Concept</h3>

  <p>A <strong>solid</strong> is a state of matter that has a definite shape and a definite volume.</p>

  <p>A solid maintains its own shape and volume under ordinary conditions. It does not take the shape of its container, although it may be cut, bent, compressed slightly or broken by applying a suitable force.</p>

  <h3>Properties of Solids</h3>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Definite shape:</strong> A solid retains its own shape instead of taking the shape of its container.</li>
    <li><strong>Definite volume:</strong> A solid occupies a particular amount of space.</li>
    <li><strong>Usually difficult to compress:</strong> Applying pressure produces little change in the volume of most solids.</li>
    <li><strong>Particles are closely packed:</strong> The particles are generally close together, with little space between neighbouring particles.</li>
    <li><strong>Particles vibrate about fixed positions:</strong> The particles are not completely motionless. They vibrate around their positions rather than moving freely from place to place.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Why Solids Retain Their Shape</h3>

  <p>The particles in a solid are held in relatively fixed positions by attractive forces between them. They can vibrate, but they do not normally move freely past one another. Consequently, the solid retains its shape.</p>

  <p>The close packing of particles also explains why most solids are difficult to compress: there is little empty space available to reduce.</p>

  <h3>Examples of Solids</h3>

  <p>A stone, a metal spoon, a wooden ruler, a piece of chalk and an ice cube are examples of solids under ordinary conditions.</p>

  <p>Powdered sugar and sand are made of solid particles. A pile of these materials may change shape when poured, but the individual grains remain solid.</p>

  <h3>Worked Examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: A metal block is placed in a rectangular box. Explain why the block does not take the shape of the box.</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the state of the metal block: it is a solid.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Recall that solid particles vibrate about relatively fixed positions.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> These particles do not freely move past one another to fit the shape of the box.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The block retains its own shape because its particles remain in relatively fixed positions.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: A learner presses a solid stone with their hands, but its volume hardly changes. Explain why.</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Most solids have particles packed closely together.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> There is little empty space between the particles.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Pressing the stone cannot reduce that space very much under ordinary conditions.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The stone is difficult to compress because its particles are closely packed.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: A student says that particles in a solid do not move. Correct the statement.</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Particles in a solid occupy relatively fixed positions.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Being in fixed positions does not mean being completely motionless.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> The particles vibrate about their positions.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Particles in a solid vibrate about relatively fixed positions; they are not completely motionless.</p>

  <h3>Common Errors</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Claiming that particles in a solid do not move at all.</li>
    <li>Confusing a pile of solid grains with a liquid merely because the pile changes shape.</li>
    <li>Assuming that every solid is perfectly rigid or impossible to deform.</li>
    <li>Claiming that solids have a definite shape but no definite volume.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Solids', 15),
  (726, 726, '
  <h2>Liquids</h2>

  <h3>Core Concept</h3>

  <p>A <strong>liquid</strong> is a state of matter that has a definite volume but does not have a definite shape.</p>

  <p>A liquid takes the shape of the part of its container that it occupies. Its volume remains approximately constant under ordinary conditions, even when it is transferred between containers of different shapes.</p>

  <h3>Properties of Liquids</h3>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Definite volume:</strong> A fixed amount of liquid occupies a definite volume under specified conditions.</li>
    <li><strong>No definite shape:</strong> A liquid takes the shape of its container.</li>
    <li><strong>Ability to flow:</strong> A liquid can flow because its particles can move past one another.</li>
    <li><strong>Particles are close together:</strong> Liquid particles remain relatively close together.</li>
    <li><strong>Relatively difficult to compress:</strong> There is little empty space between the particles compared with a gas.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Why Liquids Flow</h3>

  <p>The particles in a liquid are close together but are not held in fixed positions in the same way as particles in a solid. They can move past one another.</p>

  <p>This movement allows a liquid to flow and change shape. However, the particles remain close together, so the liquid maintains a definite volume under ordinary conditions.</p>

  <h3>Examples of Liquids</h3>

  <p>Water, cooking oil, milk and liquid ethanol are examples of liquids under ordinary conditions.</p>

  <h3>Worked Examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: A learner pours 100 cm³ of water from a tall measuring cylinder into a wider container. Explain what happens to its shape and volume.</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Water is a liquid.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> A liquid takes the shape of its container, so the water changes shape.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Transferring the water does not normally change the amount of water. Its volume remains approximately 100 cm³, assuming none is lost and conditions remain unchanged.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The water takes the shape of the wider container while retaining approximately the same volume.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: Explain why cooking oil flows when a container is tilted.</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Cooking oil is a liquid.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Its particles can move past one another.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> This movement allows the oil to flow under gravity.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Cooking oil flows because its particles can move past one another.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: A learner claims that a liquid has no definite volume because it has no definite shape. Explain the error.</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Shape and volume describe different characteristics.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> A liquid changes shape to fit its container.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> The same amount of liquid retains approximately the same volume under ordinary conditions.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The claim is incorrect. A liquid has no definite shape but does have a definite volume.</p>

  <h3>Common Errors</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Claiming that liquids have neither a definite shape nor a definite volume.</li>
    <li>Assuming that liquids flow because their particles are very far apart.</li>
    <li>Confusing the volume of a liquid with the shape of its container.</li>
    <li>Assuming that transferring a liquid automatically changes its volume.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Liquids', 15),
  (727, 727, '
  <h2>Gases</h2>

  <h3>Core Concept</h3>

  <p>A <strong>gas</strong> is a state of matter that has neither a definite shape nor a definite volume.</p>

  <p>A gas spreads to fill the available space in a container. Its volume depends on the space available and on conditions such as temperature and pressure.</p>

  <h3>Properties of Gases</h3>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>No definite shape:</strong> A gas takes the shape of its container.</li>
    <li><strong>No definite volume:</strong> A gas expands to fill the available space.</li>
    <li><strong>Compressibility:</strong> A gas can be compressed considerably because its particles are widely spaced compared with particles in solids and liquids.</li>
    <li><strong>Ability to spread:</strong> Gas particles move freely and spread throughout the available space.</li>
    <li><strong>Low density compared with many liquids and solids:</strong> For a given substance under suitable conditions, the gaseous state is generally much less dense than its liquid or solid state.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Why Gases Fill Containers</h3>

  <p>Gas particles are widely separated and move freely in different directions. They spread throughout the space available to them rather than remaining in one fixed region.</p>

  <p>Because there is substantial space between gas particles, a gas can be compressed into a smaller volume by applying pressure. Its volume is not fixed independently of the container.</p>

  <h3>Examples of Gases</h3>

  <p>Air, oxygen, nitrogen and carbon dioxide are gases under ordinary conditions.</p>

  <h3>Worked Examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: Air is released into an empty, sealed container. Explain why it spreads throughout the container.</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Air is a mixture of gases.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Gas particles move freely in different directions.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Their movement spreads the gas through the available space.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Air spreads throughout the container because its particles move freely and occupy the available space.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: A gas occupies 600 cm³ in a syringe. The nozzle is sealed, and the piston is pushed so that the gas occupies 300 cm³. Calculate the reduction in volume.</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Initial volume = 600 cm³.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Final volume = 300 cm³.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Reduction = initial volume − final volume.</p>

  <p>Reduction = 600 − 300 = 300 cm³.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The volume decreases by 300 cm³. The gas has been compressed to half its original volume.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: A learner claims that a gas has a fixed volume because it is inside a bottle. Explain the mistake.</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> The bottle limits the space available to the gas.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> The gas occupies that available space.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> If the gas is transferred to a larger suitable container, it can expand to occupy more space.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The gas does not have a definite volume of its own. Its volume depends on the available space and the conditions.</p>

  <h3>Common Errors</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Claiming that a gas has a definite volume because it is contained in a bottle.</li>
    <li>Assuming that gas particles are motionless.</li>
    <li>Thinking that compressing a gas destroys its particles.</li>
    <li>Confusing the volume of a container with the volume a gas would occupy if more space were available.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Gases', 15),
  (728, 728, '
  <h2>Particle Arrangement</h2>

  <h3>Core Concept</h3>

  <p><strong>Particle arrangement</strong> describes how the particles of a substance are positioned relative to one another.</p>

  <p>The arrangement differs among solids, liquids and gases. These differences help explain the different properties of the three states of matter.</p>

  <h3>Arrangement in a Solid</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Particles are closely packed.</li>
    <li>They occupy relatively fixed positions.</li>
    <li>In a crystalline solid, particles often form a regular, repeating arrangement.</li>
    <li>In other solids, the arrangement may not be regular, but the particles remain closely packed.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>The close arrangement leaves little space between particles and helps a solid maintain its shape and volume.</p>

  <h3>Arrangement in a Liquid</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Particles remain relatively close together.</li>
    <li>They are not held in the same fixed positions as particles in a solid.</li>
    <li>Their arrangement can change as particles move past one another.</li>
    <li>They do not form one rigid arrangement that maintains a definite shape.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>The close spacing helps a liquid retain a definite volume, while the changeable arrangement allows it to take the shape of its container.</p>

  <h3>Arrangement in a Gas</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Particles are widely separated compared with particles in solids and liquids.</li>
    <li>There is substantial space between neighbouring particles.</li>
    <li>Particles are distributed throughout the available space.</li>
    <li>They do not form a closely packed structure.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>The widely spaced arrangement helps explain why gases can be compressed and spread throughout a container.</p>

  <h3>Comparing the Arrangements</h3>

  <p><strong>Solid:</strong> Closely packed, relatively fixed arrangement.</p>

  <p><strong>Liquid:</strong> Closely packed but changeable arrangement.</p>

  <p><strong>Gas:</strong> Widely spaced arrangement with particles distributed throughout the available space.</p>

  <p>These are general particle-model descriptions. Actual arrangements vary among substances, especially among different types of solids.</p>

  <h3>Worked Examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: A particle diagram shows particles arranged in regular rows with very little space between them. Which state is most likely represented?</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Observe that the particles are closely packed.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Observe that the arrangement is regular.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Compare these features with the particle arrangement in a solid.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> A solid is most likely represented.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: A diagram shows particles close together but arranged irregularly, with no fixed repeating pattern. Which state could it represent?</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> The particles are close together.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Their arrangement is not a rigid, repeating pattern.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> This is consistent with a liquid, whose particles remain close together but can move past one another.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The diagram could represent a liquid. Particle arrangement should be interpreted together with other evidence because not every solid has a regular arrangement.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: A diagram shows particles spread far apart throughout a box. Explain what this suggests about the state represented.</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the large spaces between the particles.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Compare this spacing with the closely packed particles in solids and liquids.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Recognise that widely spaced particles are characteristic of a gas.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The diagram represents a gas because its particles are widely separated and spread throughout the available space.</p>

  <h3>Common Errors</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Drawing particles in a solid with large gaps between them.</li>
    <li>Drawing liquid particles as widely separated as gas particles.</li>
    <li>Assuming that every solid must have perfectly regular particle rows.</li>
    <li>Confusing particle arrangement with particle movement. They are related but describe different features.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Particle Arrangement', 15),
  (729, 729, '
  <h2>Particle Movement</h2>

  <h3>Core Concept</h3>

  <p><strong>Particle movement</strong> describes how the particles of a substance move. In the particle model, particles are not generally treated as completely motionless.</p>

  <p>The type and freedom of movement differ among solids, liquids and gases. These differences help explain why the states behave differently.</p>

  <h3>Movement in Solids</h3>

  <p>Particles in a solid vibrate about relatively fixed positions. They do not normally move freely from one position to another.</p>

  <p>This restricted movement helps the solid retain its shape. The particles can vibrate even when the solid appears completely still to an observer.</p>

  <h3>Movement in Liquids</h3>

  <p>Particles in a liquid move around and can slide past one another. They remain relatively close together but are not restricted to the same fixed positions as particles in a solid.</p>

  <p>This movement allows a liquid to flow and change shape while maintaining a definite volume under ordinary conditions.</p>

  <h3>Movement in Gases</h3>

  <p>Gas particles move freely and rapidly in different directions. They travel through the available space and collide with other particles and the walls of their container.</p>

  <p>Their movement helps explain why a gas spreads throughout a container and why it exerts pressure on the container walls.</p>

  <h3>Comparing Particle Movement</h3>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Solid:</strong> Particles vibrate about relatively fixed positions.</li>
    <li><strong>Liquid:</strong> Particles move past one another while remaining relatively close together.</li>
    <li><strong>Gas:</strong> Particles move freely in different directions through the available space.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Do not confuse particle movement with particle arrangement. Arrangement describes where particles are positioned relative to one another; movement describes how they move.</p>

  <h3>Worked Examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: A metal spoon remains still on a table. Does this mean its particles have stopped moving?</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Distinguish the movement of the spoon as a whole from the movement of its particles.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> A solid''s particles vibrate about relatively fixed positions.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> The spoon can appear still even though its particles continue vibrating.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> No. The spoon can remain still as an object while its particles vibrate about relatively fixed positions.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: Explain why water flows when poured from a jug.</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Water is a liquid.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Its particles can move past one another.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> This movement allows the water to change shape and flow out of the jug.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Water flows because its particles can move past one another.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: A drop of perfume released in a room can eventually be detected farther away. Explain the particle movement involved.</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Some perfume particles enter the air as a gas.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Gas particles move freely in different directions.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> The perfume particles spread through the air, allowing the smell to reach other parts of the room.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The perfume particles spread through the air because gas particles move freely in different directions. This spreading is called diffusion.</p>

  <h3>Common Errors</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Assuming that solid particles do not move.</li>
    <li>Claiming that liquid particles remain fixed in position.</li>
    <li>Claiming that gas particles all travel in one direction continuously.</li>
    <li>Confusing the movement of an entire object with the movement of its particles.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Particle Movement', 15),
  (730, 730, '
  <h2>Effect of Temperature on Particles</h2>

  <h3>Core Concept</h3>

  <p><strong>Temperature is related to the average kinetic energy of the particles in a substance.</strong> Kinetic energy is the energy a particle has because of its motion.</p>

  <p>For the same substance in the same state, increasing its temperature generally increases the average kinetic energy of its particles. Decreasing its temperature generally decreases their average kinetic energy.</p>

  <h3>When Temperature Increases</h3>

  <p>When a substance is heated without undergoing a change of state, its particles generally gain average kinetic energy and move more vigorously.</p>

  <ul>
    <li>In a solid, particles vibrate more vigorously about their positions.</li>
    <li>In a liquid, particles generally move around more vigorously.</li>
    <li>In a gas, particles generally move faster on average.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Do not assume that heating always makes particles move from one place to another. Solid particles generally vibrate more vigorously while remaining around their positions.</p>

  <h3>When Temperature Decreases</h3>

  <p>When a substance is cooled without undergoing a change of state, its particles generally lose average kinetic energy and move less vigorously.</p>

  <ul>
    <li>In a solid, particles vibrate less vigorously.</li>
    <li>In a liquid, particles generally move around less vigorously.</li>
    <li>In a gas, particles generally move more slowly on average.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Temperature and Average Kinetic Energy</h3>

  <p>Temperature is related to the average kinetic energy of particles. For gases, the average translational kinetic energy is directly proportional to absolute temperature measured in kelvin.</p>

  <p>For a gas whose particles have only the usual three translational degrees of freedom, the relationship is:</p>

  <p>Average translational kinetic energy = (3/2)kT</p>

  <p>Here, k is the Boltzmann constant and T is the absolute temperature in kelvin. This formula applies to the average translational kinetic energy of an ideal-gas particle; it is not a formula for the total energy of every substance in every state.</p>

  <h3>Important Distinction</h3>

  <p>Temperature and heat are not the same thing. Temperature describes the thermal state of a substance and is related to average particle kinetic energy. Heating is a process in which energy is transferred because of a temperature difference.</p>

  <p>For this screen, focus on how temperature affects particle motion. The detailed processes in which substances change state are covered in the later changes-of-state batches.</p>

  <h3>Worked Examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: A solid is heated, but it remains a solid. Explain how its particles are affected.</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> The temperature of the solid increases.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Its particles generally gain average kinetic energy.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> The particles vibrate more vigorously about their relatively fixed positions.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The particles generally gain average kinetic energy and vibrate more vigorously.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: A gas is cooled while remaining gaseous. Explain the expected effect on its particles.</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> The temperature decreases.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> The average kinetic energy of the gas particles decreases.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> The particles move more slowly on average.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The gas particles have lower average kinetic energy and move more slowly on average.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: The absolute temperature of an ideal gas increases from 200 K to 400 K. How does its average translational kinetic energy change?</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> For an ideal gas, average translational kinetic energy is proportional to absolute temperature.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Calculate the temperature ratio: 400 K ÷ 200 K = 2.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> The absolute temperature doubles, so the average translational kinetic energy also doubles, provided the ideal-gas model applies.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The average translational kinetic energy doubles.</p>

  <h3>Common Errors</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Claiming that heating always causes particles to move freely from place to place, including in solids.</li>
    <li>Confusing temperature with the total amount of energy in a sample.</li>
    <li>Assuming that every particle has exactly the same kinetic energy.</li>
    <li>Using Celsius temperature directly in the ideal-gas kinetic-energy proportionality, which requires absolute temperature in kelvin.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Effect of Temperature on Particles', 15),
  (731, 731, '
  <h2>Melting</h2>

  <h3>Core Concept</h3>

  <p><strong>Melting is the change of state in which a solid changes into a liquid when it gains sufficient thermal energy.</strong></p>

  <p>For a pure substance at a given pressure, melting occurs at a characteristic temperature called its melting point.</p>

  <h3>What Happens During Melting?</h3>

  <ol>
    <li>The solid absorbs thermal energy from its surroundings.</li>
    <li>Before melting begins, the particles generally gain kinetic energy and vibrate more vigorously as the solid warms.</li>
    <li>At the melting point, the energy supplied helps overcome some of the forces holding the particles in their fixed arrangement.</li>
    <li>The particles become able to move past one another as the substance becomes liquid.</li>
  </ol>

  <p>During the melting of a pure crystalline substance at constant pressure, the temperature remains constant while the solid and liquid coexist, provided the substance is melting under equilibrium conditions. The energy supplied during this period is used for the change of state rather than increasing the temperature.</p>

  <h3>Melting Point</h3>

  <p>The <strong>melting point</strong> is the temperature at which a solid changes into a liquid under specified pressure.</p>

  <p>For example, pure ice melts at approximately 0°C at standard atmospheric pressure.</p>

  <p>Different substances have different melting points. This makes melting point useful when identifying and checking the purity of substances, although the measurement must be interpreted under appropriate conditions.</p>

  <h3>Worked Examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: Ice at 0°C is supplied with energy and changes into liquid water. Name the process and explain what happens.</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the initial state: ice is solid water.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Identify the final state: liquid water.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> A change from solid to liquid is melting.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Melting occurs. The ice absorbs energy and changes from a solid into liquid water.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: A pure substance melts at 80°C under a specified pressure. What does this tell us?</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the measured temperature at which the solid becomes liquid.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Compare it with the definition of melting point.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The melting point of the substance under the stated pressure is 80°C. This value may help identify the substance when compared with reliable reference data.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: A pure crystalline solid is melting at a constant pressure. Energy continues to be supplied, but its temperature remains constant. Explain why.</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Recognise that the substance is undergoing a change of state.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> The supplied energy is used to overcome forces maintaining the solid arrangement.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Under equilibrium melting conditions, the energy does not raise the temperature while both solid and liquid coexist.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The energy supplied is used for the change from solid to liquid rather than increasing the temperature during the melting stage.</p>

  <h3>Common Errors</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Confusing melting with boiling. Melting changes a solid into a liquid.</li>
    <li>Assuming every substance melts at 0°C. That temperature is the melting point of pure ice at standard atmospheric pressure.</li>
    <li>Claiming that particles stop moving during melting.</li>
    <li>Assuming the temperature of a pure crystalline substance must rise continuously throughout melting.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Melting', 15),
  (732, 732, '
  <h2>Freezing</h2>

  <h3>Core Concept</h3>

  <p><strong>Freezing is the change of state in which a liquid changes into a solid when it loses sufficient thermal energy.</strong></p>

  <p>For a pure substance at a given pressure, freezing occurs at its characteristic freezing point. For a pure substance under the same pressure conditions, the freezing point is the same temperature as its melting point.</p>

  <h3>What Happens During Freezing?</h3>

  <ol>
    <li>The liquid transfers thermal energy to cooler surroundings.</li>
    <li>Before freezing begins, the particles generally lose kinetic energy as the liquid cools.</li>
    <li>At the freezing point, the particles form a solid arrangement as the substance changes state.</li>
    <li>The particles in the solid occupy relatively fixed positions and vibrate about those positions.</li>
  </ol>

  <p>During the freezing of a pure substance at constant pressure, the temperature remains constant while the liquid and solid coexist under equilibrium conditions. Energy is released to the surroundings during the change of state.</p>

  <h3>Freezing Point</h3>

  <p>The <strong>freezing point</strong> is the temperature at which a liquid changes into a solid under specified pressure.</p>

  <p>Pure water freezes at approximately 0°C at standard atmospheric pressure. Other substances have different freezing points.</p>

  <p>Freezing is a physical change: the substance changes state without necessarily changing its chemical identity. When liquid water freezes, it forms solid ice, and both consist of water molecules.</p>

  <h3>Worked Examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: Liquid water is cooled and becomes ice. Name the process and explain why it occurs.</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the initial state: liquid water.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Identify the final state: solid ice.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> A change from liquid to solid is freezing.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Freezing occurs because water loses thermal energy and changes from a liquid into a solid.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: A pure liquid freezes at 35°C under a specified pressure. What is its freezing point under those conditions?</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the temperature at which the liquid changes into a solid.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Apply the definition of freezing point.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Its freezing point is 35°C under the stated pressure.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: A pure substance is freezing at constant pressure. Its temperature remains constant even though it continues to lose energy. Explain why.</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Recognise that the substance is changing from liquid to solid.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> During the change of state, particles form the solid arrangement.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Energy is released to the surroundings while the substance freezes.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The substance releases energy during freezing. Under equilibrium conditions, its temperature remains constant while liquid and solid coexist.</p>

  <h3>Common Errors</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Confusing freezing with melting. Freezing changes a liquid into a solid.</li>
    <li>Assuming all substances freeze at 0°C.</li>
    <li>Claiming that particles stop moving completely when a liquid freezes.</li>
    <li>Assuming that freezing changes water into a different chemical substance.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Freezing', 15),
  (733, 733, '
  <h2>Evaporation</h2>

  <h3>Core Concept</h3>

  <p><strong>Evaporation is the process in which particles escape from the surface of a liquid and enter the gaseous state.</strong></p>

  <p>Evaporation occurs at the surface of a liquid and can take place at temperatures below the liquid''s boiling point.</p>

  <h3>How Evaporation Occurs</h3>

  <ol>
    <li>Liquid particles have a range of kinetic energies.</li>
    <li>Some particles at the surface have enough energy to overcome the attractive forces holding them in the liquid.</li>
    <li>These particles escape from the surface and become gas particles.</li>
    <li>The liquid gradually loses particles to the surrounding gas.</li>
  </ol>

  <p>Evaporation does not require the whole liquid to reach its boiling point. It can occur from an exposed liquid surface at ordinary temperatures.</p>

  <h3>Factors Affecting the Rate of Evaporation</h3>

  <p><strong>Temperature:</strong> Increasing the temperature generally increases the rate of evaporation because a greater proportion of liquid particles can escape from the surface.</p>

  <p><strong>Surface area:</strong> A larger exposed surface generally allows more particles to escape at the same time, increasing the rate of evaporation.</p>

  <p><strong>Air movement:</strong> Moving air generally increases evaporation by carrying away vapour near the liquid''s surface.</p>

  <p><strong>Humidity:</strong> Lower humidity generally favours faster evaporation because the surrounding air contains less water vapour. Higher humidity generally slows the net evaporation of water under otherwise similar conditions.</p>

  <h3>Evaporation and Cooling</h3>

  <p>When the higher-energy particles escape from a liquid, the particles left behind have lower average kinetic energy. As a result, evaporation can cool the remaining liquid and its surroundings.</p>

  <p>For example, sweat evaporating from the skin removes energy and helps cool the body.</p>

  <h3>Worked Examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: Wet clothes dry in the sun even though the water does not boil. Explain why.</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Water is present as a liquid on the clothes.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Some surface water particles gain enough energy to escape into the air.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> This process occurs below the boiling point and is called evaporation.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The clothes dry because surface water evaporates into the air. Boiling is not necessary.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: Equal amounts of water are placed in a wide, shallow dish and a narrow, deep container. Under otherwise similar conditions, which is likely to evaporate faster?</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> The wide, shallow dish exposes a larger liquid surface.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> More surface particles are exposed to the air at the same time.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> A larger exposed surface generally increases the evaporation rate.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The water in the wide, shallow dish is likely to evaporate faster, assuming other conditions are similar.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: Explain why sweat evaporating from the skin can cool the body.</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Some higher-energy water particles escape from the sweat into the air.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Energy is carried away with the escaping particles.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> The skin and remaining liquid lose thermal energy.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Evaporation removes energy from the skin and sweat, helping cool the body.</p>

  <h3>Common Errors</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Claiming that evaporation occurs only at the boiling point.</li>
    <li>Claiming that evaporation occurs throughout the entire liquid rather than at its surface.</li>
    <li>Assuming that every liquid particle has enough energy to escape at the same time.</li>
    <li>Confusing evaporation with boiling, which involves vapour formation throughout the liquid.</li>
  </ul>
  
<hr>

  <h2>Evaporation</h2>

  <h3>Meaning of Evaporation</h3>

  <p>
    <strong>Evaporation</strong> is the process by which particles escape
    from the surface of a liquid and enter the gaseous state.
  </p>

  <p>
    In separation, evaporation can be used to recover a dissolved solid
    from a solution when the liquid solvent is allowed to escape as vapour.
  </p>

  <h3>Using Evaporation to Separate a Dissolved Solid</h3>

  <p>
    Consider salt dissolved in water. The salt is distributed throughout
    the solution. When the solution is heated in a suitable evaporating
    dish, water escapes as vapour and the solution becomes more concentrated.
    If sufficient water is removed, solid salt remains.
  </p>

  <ol>
    <li>Place the solution in an evaporating dish.</li>
    <li>Heat gently when appropriate, using suitable laboratory precautions.</li>
    <li>Allow the solvent to escape as vapour.</li>
    <li>Stop heating at an appropriate point and allow the dish to cool safely.</li>
    <li>Observe the solid remaining in the dish.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>What Determines Whether Evaporation Is Suitable?</h3>

  <p>
    Evaporation is useful when the aim is to recover a dissolved solid
    and the solvent can be allowed to escape. The method is unsuitable
    when the dissolved substance would be damaged by the heating conditions
    or when recovering the solvent is also required.
  </p>

  <p>
    Evaporation does not mean that the solvent disappears. The solvent
    changes into vapour and enters the surrounding air unless it is
    collected by suitable equipment.
  </p>

  <h3>Factors Affecting the Rate of Evaporation</h3>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Temperature:</strong> higher temperature generally increases the rate of evaporation.</li>
    <li><strong>Surface area:</strong> a larger exposed surface generally increases the rate.</li>
    <li><strong>Air movement:</strong> moving air generally increases the rate by carrying vapour away.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Worked Examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: Recovering salt from salt solution</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> How can solid salt be obtained from a solution of salt in water if collecting the water is not necessary?</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Place the salt solution in an evaporating dish.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Heat gently so that water escapes as vapour.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Continue under suitable conditions until the water has been removed sufficiently to leave solid salt.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Use evaporation to remove the water and recover the salt.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: Comparing two exposed dishes</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Equal amounts of water are placed in a wide dish and a narrow dish under the same conditions. Which is expected to evaporate faster?</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> The wide dish exposes a larger surface area of water.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> More liquid particles are exposed at the surface at a given time.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> The wider dish is therefore expected to lose water faster, if the other conditions are comparable.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The wide dish is expected to evaporate faster because it has a larger exposed surface area.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: Choosing a method when only the solid is needed</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> A learner needs solid salt from a salt solution but does not need to collect the water. Explain why evaporation is suitable.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Salt is dissolved in the water, so ordinary filtration will not retain it.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Water can escape as vapour when the solution evaporates.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Removing the water leaves the dissolved salt as a solid.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Evaporation is suitable because it removes the solvent and leaves the dissolved salt behind.</p>

  <h3>Evaporation and Recovery of the Solvent</h3>

  <p>
    During ordinary evaporation, the solvent is not normally collected.
    If both the dissolved solid and the liquid solvent must be recovered,
    evaporation alone is not sufficient because the escaping vapour is
    not collected in the usual open-dish arrangement.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Evaporation', 15),
  (734, 734, '
  <h2>Boiling</h2>

  <h3>Core Concept</h3>

  <p><strong>Boiling is the rapid vaporisation of a liquid that occurs throughout the liquid when its vapour pressure equals the external pressure.</strong></p>

  <p>At a given external pressure, a pure liquid has a characteristic boiling point. At the boiling point, bubbles of vapour can form within the liquid and rise to the surface.</p>

  <h3>What Happens During Boiling?</h3>

  <ol>
    <li>The liquid receives thermal energy from its surroundings.</li>
    <li>As the liquid warms, its temperature generally increases.</li>
    <li>At the boiling point for the external pressure, vapour bubbles can form throughout the liquid.</li>
    <li>The bubbles rise and escape at the surface, converting liquid into gas.</li>
  </ol>

  <p>During the boiling of a pure liquid at constant external pressure, its temperature remains constant while the liquid and vapour coexist under equilibrium boiling conditions. The energy supplied is used for vaporisation during this period.</p>

  <h3>Boiling Point</h3>

  <p>The <strong>boiling point</strong> is the temperature at which a liquid boils under a specified external pressure.</p>

  <p>Pure water boils at approximately 100°C at standard atmospheric pressure. At lower external pressure, water boils at a lower temperature; at higher external pressure, it boils at a higher temperature.</p>

  <h3>Boiling Compared with Evaporation</h3>

  <p><strong>Boiling:</strong> Vapour bubbles form throughout the liquid, and the process occurs at the boiling point for the external pressure.</p>

  <p><strong>Evaporation:</strong> Particles escape from the liquid''s surface and evaporation can occur below the boiling point.</p>

  <p>Both processes convert a liquid into gas, but they differ in where vaporisation occurs and the conditions under which it takes place.</p>

  <h3>Worked Examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: Water is heated at standard atmospheric pressure until bubbles form throughout the liquid. The temperature is approximately 100°C. Identify the process and explain the observation.</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the starting state as liquid water.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Observe that vapour bubbles form throughout the liquid.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> At standard atmospheric pressure, pure water boils at approximately 100°C.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The process is boiling. Vapour bubbles form throughout the water as it changes into gas.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: A pure liquid boils at 78°C under a specified pressure. What is its boiling point under those conditions?</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the temperature at which the liquid boils.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Apply the definition of boiling point.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Its boiling point under the stated pressure is 78°C.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: A pure liquid is boiling at constant external pressure. Energy is supplied, but its temperature remains constant. Explain why.</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Recognise that the liquid is undergoing vaporisation.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> The energy supplied is used to convert liquid into vapour.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Under equilibrium boiling conditions, the temperature remains constant while liquid and vapour coexist.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The supplied energy is used for vaporisation rather than raising the temperature during the boiling stage.</p>

  <h3>Common Errors</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Claiming that boiling occurs only at the liquid''s surface.</li>
    <li>Assuming every liquid boils at 100°C.</li>
    <li>Claiming that evaporation and boiling are identical processes.</li>
    <li>Ignoring the effect of external pressure on boiling point.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Boiling', 15),
  (735, 735, '
  <h2>Condensation</h2>

  <h3>Meaning of Condensation</h3>

  <p>
    <strong>Condensation</strong> is the change of state in which a gas
    changes into a liquid. It occurs when the gas loses thermal energy
    to its surroundings under suitable conditions.
  </p>

  <p>
    During condensation, the particles lose kinetic energy on average.
    Their movement becomes less vigorous, allowing attractive forces
    between the particles to bring them closer together in the liquid state.
  </p>

  <p>
    The substance remains the same during condensation. Only its physical
    state changes. For example, water vapour condenses to form liquid water.
  </p>

  <h3>How Condensation Occurs</h3>

  <ol>
    <li>A substance is present as a gas.</li>
    <li>The gas transfers thermal energy to its surroundings.</li>
    <li>The particles lose kinetic energy on average.</li>
    <li>Under suitable conditions, the particles come closer together.</li>
    <li>The gas changes into a liquid.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Water Droplets on a Cold Glass</h3>

  <p><strong>Observation:</strong> A cold glass of water develops droplets
  on its outside, even though no water has been poured onto the outside.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the source of the water.</p>
  <p>
    Water vapour is present in the air surrounding the glass.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Identify what happens to the water vapour.</p>
  <p>
    The water vapour transfers thermal energy to the cold glass and
    the nearby surroundings.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Identify the state change.</p>
  <p>
    The water vapour changes from a gas into liquid water droplets.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> The droplets form through condensation
  of water vapour from the air, not because water passes through the glass.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Droplets on a Bathroom Mirror</h3>

  <p><strong>Observation:</strong> A mirror becomes covered with tiny
  water droplets after a hot shower.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Warm air in the bathroom contains water vapour.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> The mirror is cooler than the warm, moist air.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Water vapour loses thermal energy near the
  cooler surface and changes into liquid water.</p>

  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> The droplets on the mirror are formed
  by condensation.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Formation of Clouds</h3>

  <p><strong>Observation:</strong> Tiny water droplets form in parts of
  the atmosphere.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Water is present in the atmosphere as water vapour.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> When moist air cools sufficiently, water vapour
  can condense.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> The vapour changes into tiny liquid water
  droplets that can form clouds.</p>

  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> Condensation changes atmospheric water
  vapour into liquid droplets. Cloud formation can involve additional
  atmospheric processes, but the state change described here is condensation.</p>

  <h3>Important Points</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Condensation is a change from gas to liquid.</li>
    <li>The gas loses thermal energy to its surroundings.</li>
    <li>The particles lose kinetic energy on average and move closer together.</li>
    <li>Condensation does not produce a new substance.</li>
    <li>A cool surface can encourage condensation when water vapour
    comes into contact with it.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Diagnostic Questions</h3>

  <p>Answer each question in your own words. Explain your reasoning,
  not just the name of the process.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Condensation', 15),
  (736, 736, '
  <h2>Sublimation</h2>

  <h3>Meaning of Sublimation</h3>

  <p>
    <strong>Sublimation</strong> is the change of state in which a solid
    changes directly into a gas without first becoming a liquid.
  </p>

  <p>
    During sublimation, particles in the solid gain enough energy to
    escape directly into the gas state under suitable conditions.
    The solid-to-gas change does not pass through the liquid state.
  </p>

  <p>
    Sublimation is a physical change. The substance itself does not
    become a different chemical substance merely because it changes state.
  </p>

  <h3>How Sublimation Occurs</h3>

  <ol>
    <li>The substance begins in the solid state.</li>
    <li>The solid gains thermal energy or is exposed to conditions
    that allow direct transfer into the gas state.</li>
    <li>Some particles escape from the solid directly into the gas state.</li>
    <li>The substance changes from solid to gas without becoming liquid first.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Dry Ice</h3>

  <p><strong>Observation:</strong> Solid dry ice appears to shrink while
  producing a visible mist in the surrounding air.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the substance.</p>
  <p>
    Dry ice is solid carbon dioxide.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Identify the state change.</p>
  <p>
    Under ordinary atmospheric pressure, solid carbon dioxide changes
    directly into gaseous carbon dioxide rather than melting into a liquid.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Explain the observation.</p>
  <p>
    The solid becomes smaller as carbon dioxide gas forms and disperses.
    The visible white mist is mainly tiny liquid water droplets formed
    from water vapour in the surrounding air, not the carbon dioxide gas itself.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> Dry ice undergoes sublimation under
  ordinary atmospheric pressure.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Iodine</h3>

  <p><strong>Observation:</strong> When solid iodine is heated carefully
  in a suitable laboratory setup, a purple vapour can appear.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> The starting substance is solid iodine.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Heating supplies thermal energy.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Iodine can pass directly from the solid
  state into the gas state, producing iodine vapour.</p>

  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> The direct solid-to-gas change is
  sublimation. Heating iodine requires proper laboratory safety precautions.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Naphthalene</h3>

  <p><strong>Observation:</strong> A naphthalene solid can gradually
  become smaller when left exposed to air under suitable conditions.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the initial state.</p>
  <p>
    Naphthalene begins as a solid.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Consider what leaves the solid.</p>
  <p>
    Some particles enter the gas state directly.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Check whether a liquid stage is required.</p>
  <p>
    In the process described, the solid becomes vapour without first
    forming a liquid.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> The direct solid-to-gas change is sublimation.</p>

  <h3>Important Points</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Sublimation is a change from solid directly to gas.</li>
    <li>The substance does not pass through the liquid state during this change.</li>
    <li>Thermal energy can enable particles to escape from the solid.</li>
    <li>Sublimation is a physical change, not necessarily a chemical reaction.</li>
    <li>Examples include dry ice at ordinary atmospheric pressure,
    iodine under suitable conditions, and naphthalene.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Diagnostic Questions</h3>

  <p>For every answer, identify the starting state, final state, and
  whether a liquid state occurs during the process.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Sublimation', 15),
  (737, 737, '
  <h2>Deposition</h2>

  <h3>Meaning of Deposition</h3>

  <p>
    <strong>Deposition</strong> is the change of state in which a gas
    changes directly into a solid without first becoming a liquid.
  </p>

  <p>
    During deposition, particles in the gas lose thermal energy.
    Under suitable conditions, they become part of a solid structure
    without passing through the liquid state.
  </p>

  <p>
    Deposition is a physical change. The substance remains chemically
    the same even though its physical state changes.
  </p>

  <h3>How Deposition Occurs</h3>

  <ol>
    <li>The substance begins in the gas state.</li>
    <li>The gas transfers thermal energy to its surroundings.</li>
    <li>Under suitable conditions, the particles lose enough energy
    to become part of a solid.</li>
    <li>The substance changes directly from gas to solid without
    becoming liquid first.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Formation of Frost</h3>

  <p><strong>Observation:</strong> Ice crystals form on a very cold
  surface on a frosty morning.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the substance in the air.</p>
  <p>
    The air contains water vapour, which is water in the gas state.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Consider the cold surface.</p>
  <p>
    When the surface is sufficiently cold and conditions are suitable,
    water vapour can lose thermal energy.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Identify the state change.</p>
  <p>
    Water vapour changes directly into solid ice crystals without first
    becoming liquid water on that surface.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> Frost can form by deposition of
  water vapour into ice.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Ice Crystals in a Freezer</h3>

  <p><strong>Observation:</strong> Ice crystals gradually appear on
  a very cold freezer surface.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Water vapour is present in the air
  entering or inside the freezer.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> The cold surface provides conditions
  under which the vapour loses thermal energy.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> The water vapour changes directly into
  solid ice crystals.</p>

  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> When the ice forms directly from
  water vapour, the process is deposition.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Formation of Solid Crystals from a Vapour</h3>

  <p><strong>Observation:</strong> A vapour forms a solid deposit on
  a sufficiently cool surface in a suitable controlled setup.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the starting state as gas.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Identify the final state as solid.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Determine whether the vapour first
  becomes liquid.</p>

  <p>
    If the vapour changes directly into a solid without an intermediate
    liquid state, the process is deposition.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> A direct gas-to-solid change is deposition.</p>

  <h3>Important Points</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Deposition is a change from gas directly to solid.</li>
    <li>The substance does not pass through the liquid state during this change.</li>
    <li>The gas loses thermal energy under suitable conditions.</li>
    <li>Deposition is a physical change, not necessarily a chemical reaction.</li>
    <li>Frost can form when water vapour changes directly into ice crystals.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Diagnostic Questions</h3>

  <p>In every answer, identify the starting state, final state, and
  whether the substance passes through the liquid state.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Deposition', 15),
  (738, 738, '
  <h2>Elements</h2>

  <h3>Meaning of an Element</h3>

  <p>
    An <strong>element</strong> is a pure substance that contains only
    one type of atom. It cannot be broken down into simpler substances
    by ordinary chemical methods.
  </p>

  <p>
    Every element has a unique name and chemical symbol. For example,
    hydrogen has the symbol H, oxygen has the symbol O, and iron has
    the symbol Fe.
  </p>

  <p>
    An element may exist as individual atoms or as molecules containing
    atoms of the same element. Therefore, an element does not always
    consist of single, separate atoms.
  </p>

  <h3>Atoms and Elements</h3>

  <p>
    An atom is the smallest unit of an element that retains the chemical
    identity of that element. All atoms of a given element have the same
    number of protons in their nuclei.
  </p>

  <p>
    For example, every carbon atom has 6 protons. Every oxygen atom has
    8 protons. The number of protons identifies the element.
  </p>

  <h3>Elements That Exist as Molecules</h3>

  <p>
    Some elements exist naturally as molecules made of two or more atoms
    of the same element.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>Hydrogen gas: H₂ — each molecule contains two hydrogen atoms.</li>
    <li>Oxygen gas: O₂ — each molecule contains two oxygen atoms.</li>
    <li>Nitrogen gas: N₂ — each molecule contains two nitrogen atoms.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Hydrogen gas is still an element because the molecules contain only
    hydrogen atoms. The same reasoning applies to oxygen and nitrogen.
  </p>

  <h3>Chemical Symbols</h3>

  <p>
    A chemical symbol is the shortened representation of an element.
    It usually consists of one or two letters. The first letter is
    always capitalised, while the second letter, if present, is lowercase.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>H — hydrogen</li>
    <li>O — oxygen</li>
    <li>C — carbon</li>
    <li>N — nitrogen</li>
    <li>Na — sodium</li>
    <li>Cl — chlorine</li>
    <li>Fe — iron</li>
    <li>Cu — copper</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Capitalisation matters. For example, Co is the symbol for cobalt,
    while CO represents one carbon atom chemically combined with one
    oxygen atom in a carbon monoxide molecule.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Classifying Oxygen Gas</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Oxygen gas is represented by O₂.
  Explain why it is classified as an element.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Read the formula.</p>
  <p>
    The symbol O represents oxygen, and the subscript 2 indicates that
    each molecule contains two oxygen atoms.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Identify the types of atoms present.</p>
  <p>
    Both atoms are oxygen atoms. There is only one type of atom.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Apply the definition.</p>
  <p>
    A substance containing only one type of atom is an element.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> O₂ is an element because every molecule
  contains only oxygen atoms.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Classifying Iron</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> A sample contains only iron atoms.
  Explain why the sample is an element.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the atoms.</p>
  <p>
    The sample contains iron atoms, represented by the symbol Fe.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Count the types of atoms.</p>
  <p>
    Only one type of atom is present.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Apply the definition of an element.</p>
  <p>
    The sample is made from only one type of atom and is not a combination
    of different elements.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The sample is an element because it
  contains only iron atoms.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Interpreting a Chemical Symbol</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> What does the symbol Na represent,
  and why is sodium classified as an element?</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the symbol.</p>
  <p>
    Na is the chemical symbol for sodium.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Identify the type of substance.</p>
  <p>
    Sodium is a chemical element, so its atoms all have the same
    number of protons.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Connect this to the definition.</p>
  <p>
    Sodium is not made by chemically combining different elements.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Na represents sodium. Sodium is an
  element because it consists of one type of atom.</p>

  <h3>Important Points</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>An element contains only one type of atom.</li>
    <li>An element cannot be broken down into simpler substances by
    ordinary chemical methods.</li>
    <li>Elements are represented by chemical symbols.</li>
    <li>An element may exist as single atoms or as molecules containing
    atoms of the same element.</li>
    <li>The number of protons in an atom identifies its element.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Diagnostic Questions</h3>

  <p>
    Answer in complete sentences. When classifying a substance, explain
    your decision using the types of atoms present.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Elements', 15),
  (739, 739, '
  <h2>Compounds</h2>

  <h3>Meaning of a Compound</h3>

  <p>
    A <strong>compound</strong> is a pure substance formed when atoms
    of two or more different elements are chemically combined in fixed
    proportions.
  </p>

  <p>
    The elements in a compound are joined by chemical bonds. A compound
    has its own chemical properties, which may differ significantly
    from the properties of the elements that form it.
  </p>

  <p>
    A compound cannot be separated into its constituent elements by
    ordinary physical methods such as filtration. Chemical reactions
    are needed to decompose it into simpler substances.
  </p>

  <h3>Fixed Proportions</h3>

  <p>
    A particular compound has a definite composition. Its formula shows
    which elements are present and the relative numbers of their atoms
    in the substance.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>Water: H₂O — two hydrogen atoms for every oxygen atom in each molecule.</li>
    <li>Carbon dioxide: CO₂ — one carbon atom for every two oxygen atoms.</li>
    <li>Sodium chloride: NaCl — sodium and chlorine in a 1:1 ratio
    in its formula unit.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    The formula H₂O does not mean that water is a mixture of hydrogen
    and oxygen gases. It represents a compound whose hydrogen and oxygen
    atoms are chemically bonded.
  </p>

  <h3>How Compounds Form</h3>

  <ol>
    <li>Atoms of different elements take part in a chemical reaction.</li>
    <li>Chemical bonds form between atoms or ions, depending on the compound.</li>
    <li>The resulting substance has a definite chemical composition.</li>
    <li>The compound has properties that are not simply identical to
    those of its constituent elements.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Water</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Explain why water, H₂O, is a compound.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Read the formula.</p>
  <p>
    H₂O contains hydrogen and oxygen.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Identify the types of elements.</p>
  <p>
    Two different elements are present: hydrogen and oxygen.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Consider how the atoms are joined.</p>
  <p>
    The hydrogen and oxygen atoms are chemically bonded in a definite
    proportion.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Water is a compound because hydrogen and
  oxygen are chemically combined in a fixed proportion.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Carbon Dioxide</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Use the formula CO₂ to explain the
  composition of carbon dioxide and why it is a compound.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the symbols.</p>
  <p>
    C represents carbon and O represents oxygen.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Interpret the subscript.</p>
  <p>
    Each carbon dioxide molecule contains one carbon atom and two oxygen atoms.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Apply the definition.</p>
  <p>
    The two different elements are chemically combined in a definite ratio.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Carbon dioxide is a compound because
  carbon and oxygen are chemically combined in a 1:2 atomic ratio.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Sodium Chloride</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Explain why sodium chloride, NaCl,
  is a compound rather than an element.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the elements.</p>
  <p>
    Na represents sodium and Cl represents chlorine.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Determine the number of elements.</p>
  <p>
    Two different elements are present.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Identify their chemical relationship.</p>
  <p>
    Sodium and chlorine form an ionic compound with a 1:1 ratio of
    sodium ions to chloride ions in the formula unit.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Sodium chloride is a compound because
  it consists of sodium and chlorine chemically combined in a fixed ratio.</p>

  <h3>Properties of Compounds</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>They contain two or more different elements chemically combined.</li>
    <li>They have a definite composition.</li>
    <li>They have their own chemical properties.</li>
    <li>They cannot be separated into their constituent elements by
    ordinary physical separation methods.</li>
    <li>Their chemical formulas represent their composition.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Diagnostic Questions</h3>

  <p>
    Use chemical formulas where appropriate. Explain how the elements
    are combined rather than merely naming the substance.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Compounds', 15),
  (740, 740, '
  <h2>Mixtures</h2>

  <h3>Meaning of a Mixture</h3>

  <p>
    A <strong>mixture</strong> is formed when two or more substances
    are combined physically without being chemically combined into
    one new compound.
  </p>

  <p>
    The substances in a mixture are called its components. These
    components can be elements, compounds, or other substances.
  </p>

  <p>
    Unlike a compound, a mixture does not have one fixed chemical
    formula that describes the whole mixture. The proportions of its
    components can vary.
  </p>

  <h3>How Mixtures Differ from Compounds</h3>

  <p>
    In a mixture, the components retain their chemical identities.
    Combining them does not necessarily produce a new substance through
    a chemical reaction.
  </p>

  <p>
    Many mixtures can be separated using physical methods. The method
    chosen depends on differences in the components'' physical properties,
    such as particle size, solubility, boiling point, or magnetic behaviour.
  </p>

  <h3>Examples of Mixtures</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Air — a mixture of gases, including nitrogen, oxygen, and argon,
    with variable amounts of water vapour and other gases.</li>
    <li>Salt water — water containing dissolved sodium chloride.</li>
    <li>Brass — an alloy made mainly of copper and zinc.</li>
    <li>Soil — a mixture of mineral particles, organic matter, water,
    air, and other components.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    These mixtures differ in composition. Some appear uniform throughout,
    while others have visibly different components.
  </p>

  <h3>Composition of Mixtures</h3>

  <p>
    The amounts of components in a mixture can change without changing
    the identity of the components themselves.
  </p>

  <p>
    For example, a small amount of salt can be dissolved in water, or
    a larger amount can be dissolved if conditions permit. Both samples
    are mixtures of salt and water, but their compositions differ.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Salt and Sand</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Salt and sand are placed together in
  a container. Explain why the resulting material is a mixture.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the components.</p>
  <p>
    The components are salt and sand.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Consider whether they have chemically
  combined into one new compound.</p>
  <p>
    Simply placing them together does not chemically combine them into
    one new compound.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Apply the definition.</p>
  <p>
    The substances are physically combined and retain their identities.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Salt and sand form a mixture because
  they are combined physically rather than chemically forming a new compound.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Salt Water</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Explain why dissolving salt in water
  produces a mixture.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the substances.</p>
  <p>
    The components are sodium chloride and water.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Consider what happens during dissolving.</p>
  <p>
    The salt separates into hydrated ions dispersed throughout the water.
    Dissolving under these conditions does not turn the salt and water
    into a single new compound.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Apply the definition.</p>
  <p>
    The salt and water form a physical combination whose composition
    can vary.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Salt water is a mixture because it contains
  dissolved sodium chloride and water without forming one new compound.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Air</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Explain why air is classified as a mixture.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the components.</p>
  <p>
    Air contains several gases, mainly nitrogen and oxygen, along with
    argon, carbon dioxide, and variable amounts of water vapour.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Consider their proportions.</p>
  <p>
    The proportions of some components, particularly water vapour,
    can vary from place to place and over time.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Apply the definition.</p>
  <p>
    The gases are physically mixed rather than chemically combined
    into one compound.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Air is a mixture because it contains
  several gases whose proportions can vary.</p>

  <h3>Separation of Mixtures</h3>

  <p>
    Physical separation methods take advantage of differences in the
    properties of the components. Examples include:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>Filtration — can separate an insoluble solid from a liquid.</li>
    <li>Evaporation — can recover a dissolved solid from a solution.</li>
    <li>Distillation — can separate substances using differences in
    volatility and boiling behaviour.</li>
    <li>Magnetic separation — can separate a magnetic component from
    non-magnetic material when their magnetic properties differ.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    The suitability of a method depends on the actual components in
    the mixture and their properties.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 4: Choosing a Separation Method</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> A mixture contains sand and water.
  Which physical method can separate the sand from the water, and why?</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the physical properties.</p>
  <p>
    Sand is insoluble in water, and its particles can be retained by
    a suitable filter.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Choose a method.</p>
  <p>
    Filtration is suitable for separating the insoluble sand from water.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Explain the result.</p>
  <p>
    The sand remains as residue on the filter while water passes through
    as filtrate.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Filtration separates the sand from the
  water because the sand is insoluble and can be retained by the filter.</p>

  <h3>Important Points</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>A mixture contains two or more substances physically combined.</li>
    <li>The components retain their chemical identities.</li>
    <li>The proportions of components can vary.</li>
    <li>A mixture does not have one fixed chemical formula describing
    the entire mixture.</li>
    <li>Many mixtures can be separated by suitable physical methods.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Diagnostic Questions</h3>

  <p>
    Explain your reasoning for every classification. Where a separation
    method is requested, connect your choice to a relevant physical property.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Mixtures', 15),
  (741, 741, '
  <h2>Homogeneous Mixtures</h2>

  <h3>Meaning of a Homogeneous Mixture</h3>

  <p>
    A <strong>homogeneous mixture</strong> is a mixture that has a
    uniform composition throughout the sample at the scale being
    considered.
  </p>

  <p>
    Its components are distributed evenly enough that different portions
    of the mixture have the same composition, provided the mixture
    remains uniform.
  </p>

  <p>
    A homogeneous mixture contains more than one substance, but the
    components are not separated into visibly distinct portions
    throughout the mixture.
  </p>

  <h3>Solutions</h3>

  <p>
    A solution is a homogeneous mixture. It contains a solvent and
    one or more solutes.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Solvent:</strong> the substance that dissolves the solute.
    In a solution of salt in water, water is the solvent.</li>
    <li><strong>Solute:</strong> the substance dissolved in the solvent.
    In salt water, sodium chloride is the solute.</li>
    <li><strong>Solution:</strong> the homogeneous mixture formed when
    the solute is distributed throughout the solvent.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    In a solution of salt in water, the salt dissolves and its ions
    become distributed throughout the water. A properly prepared solution
    has the same composition in equal portions taken from different
    places in the solution.
  </p>

  <h3>Examples of Homogeneous Mixtures</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Salt dissolved in water to form a uniform solution.</li>
    <li>Sugar dissolved in water to form a uniform solution.</li>
    <li>Air that is uniformly mixed at the scale being considered.</li>
    <li>Brass, an alloy consisting mainly of copper and zinc, when
    considered as a uniform alloy.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    These examples contain multiple substances. Their uniform appearance
    does not make them pure substances or compounds.
  </p>

  <h3>Composition Throughout the Sample</h3>

  <p>
    The key property is uniform composition. If equal portions are
    taken from different places in a uniform salt solution, each portion
    has the same salt-to-water ratio, within ordinary measurement limits.
  </p>

  <p>
    If additional water is poured into a salt solution and mixed
    thoroughly, the result can remain a homogeneous mixture, but its
    concentration changes.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Salt Dissolved in Water</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Salt is dissolved completely in water
  and stirred until the solution is uniform. Explain why it is a
  homogeneous mixture.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the substances.</p>
  <p>
    The solution contains water and dissolved sodium chloride.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Consider the distribution of the components.</p>
  <p>
    When the salt has dissolved completely and the solution is uniform,
    the dissolved components are distributed throughout the liquid.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Apply the definition.</p>
  <p>
    The composition is uniform throughout the sample.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The salt solution is a homogeneous
  mixture because it contains water and dissolved salt distributed
  uniformly throughout the solution.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Sugar Solution</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> A student dissolves sugar in water
  until no sugar crystals remain visible. Explain how the student can
  determine whether the resulting sample is homogeneous.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Inspect the sample.</p>
  <p>
    Check that no separate sugar crystals remain and that the solution
    appears uniform.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Consider the composition at different positions.</p>
  <p>
    If the sugar is fully dissolved and the solution is mixed uniformly,
    equal portions taken from different positions have the same composition.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Classify the sample.</p>
  <p>
    A uniform sugar solution is a homogeneous mixture of sugar and water.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The sample is homogeneous if the sugar
  is fully dissolved and the solution has uniform composition throughout.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Diluting a Salt Solution</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Water is added to a uniform salt solution
  and the mixture is stirred thoroughly. Explain what happens to its
  concentration and whether it can remain homogeneous.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify what is added.</p>
  <p>
    Additional water increases the amount of solvent.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Consider the amount of dissolved salt.</p>
  <p>
    If no salt is added or removed, the amount of dissolved salt remains
    the same while the total solution volume increases.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Determine the effect on concentration.</p>
  <p>
    The salt becomes less concentrated because it is distributed through
    a larger amount of solution.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Determine whether the mixture remains uniform.</p>
  <p>
    If the salt remains dissolved and stirring produces a uniform
    solution, the diluted sample is still homogeneous.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The solution becomes less concentrated
  and can remain homogeneous if the dissolved salt is distributed
  uniformly throughout the water.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 4: Air as a Homogeneous Mixture</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Explain why a well-mixed sample of air
  can be classified as a homogeneous mixture.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the components.</p>
  <p>
    Air contains nitrogen, oxygen, argon, carbon dioxide, and other gases.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Consider their distribution.</p>
  <p>
    In a well-mixed sample, the gases are distributed uniformly at
    the scale being considered.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Apply the definition.</p>
  <p>
    The sample has a uniform composition even though it contains
    different gases.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Well-mixed air is a homogeneous mixture
  because several gases are uniformly distributed throughout the sample.</p>

  <h3>Important Points</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>A homogeneous mixture has uniform composition throughout the sample.</li>
    <li>A solution is a homogeneous mixture.</li>
    <li>The solvent dissolves the solute.</li>
    <li>Homogeneous mixtures contain multiple substances and are not
    automatically pure substances or compounds.</li>
    <li>Dilution changes concentration but can leave the mixture homogeneous.</li>
    <li>Uniform appearance alone does not make a mixture a pure substance.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Diagnostic Questions</h3>

  <p>
    Explain the uniformity of each sample. Distinguish uniform composition
    from the idea that a sample contains only one substance.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Homogeneous Mixtures', 15),
  (742, 742, '
  <h2>Heterogeneous Mixtures</h2>

  <h3>Meaning of a Heterogeneous Mixture</h3>

  <p>
    A <strong>heterogeneous mixture</strong> is a mixture whose
    composition is not uniform throughout the sample.
  </p>

  <p>
    Different portions of a heterogeneous mixture can contain different
    amounts or proportions of its components. The components may be
    visibly distinguishable, but visible differences are not required
    in every heterogeneous mixture.
  </p>

  <p>
    The substances in a heterogeneous mixture are physically combined.
    They do not have to react chemically to form a new compound.
  </p>

  <h3>Recognising Heterogeneous Mixtures</h3>

  <p>
    A useful way to identify a heterogeneous mixture is to consider
    whether different parts of the sample have different compositions.
    For example, a sample of soil may contain mineral particles,
    organic matter, and other components distributed unevenly.
  </p>

  <p>
    In some mixtures, different components are easy to see. In others,
    the mixture may look uniform to the naked eye, even though its
    composition differs between microscopic regions.
  </p>

  <h3>Examples</h3>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Sand and water:</strong> sand particles remain separate
    from the water and may settle.</li>
    <li><strong>Oil and water:</strong> under ordinary conditions,
    the liquids form separate layers when allowed to settle.</li>
    <li><strong>Soil:</strong> different particles and materials are
    distributed unevenly.</li>
    <li><strong>Granite:</strong> different mineral grains can often
    be seen in the rock.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Sand and Water</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> A container holds sand and water.
  Explain why the contents form a heterogeneous mixture.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1: Identify the components.</strong></p>
  <p>
    The sample contains sand and water.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2: Examine their distribution.</strong></p>
  <p>
    Sand does not dissolve in water under ordinary conditions. Its
    particles remain separate from the water and may settle at the bottom.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3: Apply the definition.</strong></p>
  <p>
    Different portions can contain different proportions of sand and
    water, so the composition is not uniform throughout the sample.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Sand and water form a heterogeneous
  mixture because the sand remains separate and is not distributed
  uniformly throughout the water.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Oil and Water</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Oil and water are poured into a
  transparent container. After standing, two layers appear. Explain
  why this is a heterogeneous mixture.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1: Identify the components.</strong></p>
  <p>
    The components are oil and water.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2: Examine their behaviour.</strong></p>
  <p>
    Under ordinary conditions, oil and water do not mix into one
    uniform liquid. They separate into distinct layers.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3: Compare the composition of the layers.</strong></p>
  <p>
    One layer is mainly oil, while the other is mainly water.
    Their compositions are different.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Oil and water form a heterogeneous
  mixture because the two components form separate layers with
  different compositions.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Granite</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> A piece of granite contains visible
  grains of different minerals. Explain why it is a heterogeneous mixture.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1: Observe the sample.</strong></p>
  <p>
    The granite contains visibly different mineral grains.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2: Consider the composition of different regions.</strong></p>
  <p>
    Different parts of the rock can contain different minerals and
    different proportions of those minerals.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3: Classify the material.</strong></p>
  <p>
    The sample is not uniform in composition throughout.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Granite is a heterogeneous mixture of
  minerals because its different regions can have different compositions.</p>

  <h3>Important Points</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>A heterogeneous mixture has a non-uniform composition.</li>
    <li>Different portions of the sample can contain different
    proportions of components.</li>
    <li>Its components may be visibly distinct, but visibility alone
    is not the definition.</li>
    <li>The components are physically combined rather than necessarily
    chemically combined into a new compound.</li>
    <li>Examples include sand and water, oil and water, soil, and granite.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Diagnostic Questions</h3>

  <p>
    For each answer, identify the components and explain how their
    distribution determines whether the mixture is heterogeneous.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Heterogeneous Mixtures', 15),
  (743, 743, '
  <h2>Identifying Pure Substances</h2>

  <h3>Meaning of a Pure Substance</h3>

  <p>
    In chemistry, a <strong>pure substance</strong> is material made
    up of only one chemical substance, with a definite composition
    and characteristic properties.
  </p>

  <p>
    A pure substance may be an element or a compound. An element contains
    one type of atom, while a compound contains different elements
    chemically combined in fixed proportions.
  </p>

  <p>
    A pure substance is not necessarily an element. Pure water is a
    compound, while pure copper is an element.
  </p>

  <h3>Composition and Identity</h3>

  <p>
    A pure substance has a definite chemical identity. A sample of
    pure water consists of water, H₂O, rather than water mixed with
    dissolved salt or other substances.
  </p>

  <p>
    If two samples are both pure samples of the same substance, their
    chemical composition is the same, even if the samples have different
    masses or volumes.
  </p>

  <h3>Using Melting Point and Boiling Point</h3>

  <p>
    At a specified pressure, a pure substance generally melts and boils
    at characteristic temperatures. For a pure crystalline substance
    at a given pressure, melting commonly occurs over a very narrow
    temperature range.
  </p>

  <p>
    Many mixtures melt or boil over a range of temperatures because
    their components have different physical properties. However, this
    is evidence rather than a universal test: some mixtures have
    characteristic phase-change behaviour, and pressure must be
    considered when comparing temperatures.
  </p>

  <p>
    A melting-point or boiling-point measurement should therefore be
    interpreted alongside other evidence about the sample''s composition.
  </p>

  <h3>Purity and Appearance</h3>

  <p>
    A material that looks clear, colourless, or uniform is not
    automatically pure. Dissolved substances may be invisible, and
    some mixtures look uniform even though they contain more than one
    substance.
  </p>

  <p>
    For example, a clear salt solution is a mixture of salt and water.
    Its appearance alone does not establish that it is a pure substance.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Pure Copper</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> A sample is confirmed to contain only
  copper. Explain whether it is a pure substance and whether it is
  an element or a compound.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1: Identify its composition.</strong></p>
  <p>
    The sample contains only copper atoms.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2: Apply the definition of purity.</strong></p>
  <p>
    It contains only one chemical substance, copper.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3: Classify the substance.</strong></p>
  <p>
    Copper is an element, not a compound, because it contains only
    one type of atom.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The sample is a pure substance and an
  element because it contains only copper.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Pure Water</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> A sample contains only H₂O molecules.
  Explain why it is a pure substance but not an element.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1: Examine the chemical identity.</strong></p>
  <p>
    All the molecules in the sample are water molecules.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2: Determine whether the sample is pure.</strong></p>
  <p>
    Since only one chemical substance is present, the sample is pure water.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3: Determine whether it is an element.</strong></p>
  <p>
    Each water molecule contains hydrogen and oxygen, which are
    different elements chemically combined.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Pure water is a pure substance, but it
  is a compound rather than an element.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Interpreting Melting-Point Evidence</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> At the same specified pressure, Sample A
  melts over a very narrow temperature range, while Sample B melts
  over a broad range. What can this evidence suggest about the samples?</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1: Compare the observations.</strong></p>
  <p>
    Sample A has a narrow melting range, while Sample B has a broad range.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2: Apply the usual behaviour of pure substances.</strong></p>
  <p>
    A pure crystalline substance commonly melts at a characteristic
    temperature under specified conditions.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3: Draw a cautious conclusion.</strong></p>
  <p>
    Sample A may be purer than Sample B, while Sample B may contain
    impurities or be a mixture.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The observations suggest that Sample A
  may be purer and Sample B may contain impurities or multiple components.
  The melting data alone do not prove the identity or purity of either sample.</p>

  <h3>Important Points</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>A pure substance contains only one chemical substance.</li>
    <li>Pure substances include elements and compounds.</li>
    <li>A pure substance has a definite chemical composition.</li>
    <li>A clear or uniform appearance does not prove purity.</li>
    <li>Melting-point and boiling-point behaviour can provide evidence
    about purity when pressure and measurement conditions are controlled.</li>
    <li>Experimental evidence should be interpreted carefully rather
    than treated as an infallible test.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Diagnostic Questions</h3>

  <p>
    For each sample, explain what is present and distinguish purity
    from whether the substance is an element or a compound.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Identifying Pure Substances', 15),
  (744, 744, '
  <h2>Identifying Mixtures</h2>

  <h3>How to Identify a Mixture</h3>

  <p>
    A <strong>mixture</strong> contains two or more substances physically
    combined. The components retain their chemical identities and
    are not necessarily chemically combined into one new compound.
  </p>

  <p>
    To identify a mixture, investigate what substances are present,
    whether their proportions can vary, and whether they can be
    separated by suitable physical methods.
  </p>

  <p>
    No single observation is guaranteed to identify every mixture.
    A reliable conclusion should be based on the available evidence
    about the sample''s composition and behaviour.
  </p>

  <h3>Clues That a Sample May Be a Mixture</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Two or more substances are known to be present.</li>
    <li>The proportions of the components can vary from one sample
    to another.</li>
    <li>Different components can be separated by a suitable physical method.</li>
    <li>The sample contains visibly different materials or layers,
    although a mixture can also look uniform.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Physical Separation as Evidence</h3>

  <p>
    Many mixtures can be separated because their components have
    different physical properties. For example, filtration can separate
    an insoluble solid from a liquid, and a magnet can remove a magnetic
    component from non-magnetic material.
  </p>

  <p>
    Separating a mixture does not require turning its components into
    different chemical substances. The method separates substances
    that were already present.
  </p>

  <h3>Composition Can Vary</h3>

  <p>
    The component proportions in a mixture are not fixed by a single
    chemical formula. Two salt solutions can contain different
    concentrations of salt and still be mixtures of salt and water.
  </p>

  <p>
    A mixture can be homogeneous, with uniform composition, or
    heterogeneous, with non-uniform composition. Therefore, a uniform
    appearance does not rule out a mixture.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Identifying Salt Water</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Two samples of salt water contain
  different amounts of dissolved salt relative to the amount of water.
  Explain why both samples are mixtures.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1: Identify the components.</strong></p>
  <p>
    Both samples contain sodium chloride and water.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2: Compare their compositions.</strong></p>
  <p>
    The relative amounts of salt and water differ between the samples.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3: Apply the definition.</strong></p>
  <p>
    The salt and water are physically combined, and their proportions
    can vary.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Both samples are mixtures because they
  contain salt and water in variable proportions rather than one
  chemically combined substance with a fixed formula.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Identifying Iron and Sand</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> A sample contains iron filings and
  sand. A magnet removes the iron filings while the sand remains.
  Explain what this evidence tells you.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1: Identify the components.</strong></p>
  <p>
    The sample contains iron and sand.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2: Interpret the separation.</strong></p>
  <p>
    A magnet attracts the iron filings but not the sand under ordinary
    conditions.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3: Draw a conclusion.</strong></p>
  <p>
    The components were present as separate substances and could be
    separated using their different magnetic properties.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The sample is a mixture of iron and sand.
  Magnetic separation removes the iron without requiring the formation
  of new substances.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Identifying a Uniform Liquid</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> A clear liquid looks uniform. Testing
  establishes that it contains water and a dissolved substance. Explain
  whether it can be a mixture despite its uniform appearance.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1: Consider the appearance.</strong></p>
  <p>
    The liquid appears uniform, but appearance alone does not establish purity.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2: Use the composition evidence.</strong></p>
  <p>
    The sample contains water and another dissolved substance.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3: Classify the sample.</strong></p>
  <p>
    Since more than one substance is physically combined in a uniform
    composition, the liquid is a homogeneous mixture.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Yes. The sample can be a homogeneous
  mixture because it contains multiple substances even though it looks uniform.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 4: Distinguishing a Mixture from a Compound</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> A sample contains two substances
  physically combined, and each can be recovered using a suitable
  physical separation method. Explain why the evidence supports
  classifying the sample as a mixture.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the substances already present.</p>
  <p>
    Two components are present in the original sample.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Consider the separation.</p>
  <p>
    A suitable physical method separates the components without
    chemically decomposing one compound into its elements.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Apply the definition.</p>
  <p>
    The sample is a physical combination of substances.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The evidence supports classifying the
  sample as a mixture because its components were physically combined
  and can be separated using a suitable physical method.</p>

  <h3>Important Points</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>A mixture contains at least two substances physically combined.</li>
    <li>Its components retain their chemical identities.</li>
    <li>The proportions of the components can vary.</li>
    <li>Mixtures may be homogeneous or heterogeneous.</li>
    <li>Some mixtures can be separated using physical properties such
    as solubility, particle size, boiling behaviour, or magnetism.</li>
    <li>A uniform appearance does not prove that a sample is pure.</li>
    <li>Use composition and suitable experimental evidence to classify
    an unknown sample.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Diagnostic Questions</h3>

  <p>
    Use the evidence given in each question. Explain why the evidence
    supports the classification instead of relying on appearance alone.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Identifying Mixtures', 15),
  (745, 745, '
  <h2>Filtration</h2>

  <h3>Meaning of Filtration</h3>

  <p>
    <strong>Filtration</strong> is a method of separating an insoluble solid
    from a liquid by passing the mixture through a filter.
  </p>

  <p>
    An <strong>insoluble solid</strong> is a solid that does not dissolve
    in the liquid under the conditions used. For example, sand is insoluble
    in water.
  </p>

  <h3>How Filtration Works</h3>

  <p>
    Filter paper contains tiny pores. When a mixture of an insoluble solid
    and a liquid passes through the paper, the liquid passes through the
    pores while the solid particles are retained, provided the particles
    are larger than the effective pores.
  </p>

  <p>The two products have specific names:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Residue:</strong> the solid left on the filter paper.</li>
    <li><strong>Filtrate:</strong> the liquid that passes through the filter paper.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Apparatus and Arrangement</h3>

  <p>
    The usual apparatus includes a filter funnel, filter paper, a container
    for the mixture and a receiving container for the filtrate.
  </p>

  <ol>
    <li>Fold the filter paper and place it inside the funnel.</li>
    <li>Position the funnel above a clean receiving container.</li>
    <li>Pour the mixture carefully into the filter paper.</li>
    <li>Allow the liquid to pass through.</li>
    <li>Identify the residue and the filtrate.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>What Filtration Can and Cannot Do</h3>

  <p>
    Filtration separates an insoluble solid from a liquid. It does not
    normally separate a dissolved substance from its solution because
    dissolved particles pass through the filter paper with the liquid.
  </p>

  <p>
    For example, filtration can separate sand from water, but it cannot
    remove dissolved salt from salt solution using ordinary filter paper.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: Separating sand from water</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> A learner pours a mixture of sand and water through filter paper. Identify the residue and the filtrate.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Sand does not dissolve in water, so it remains as solid particles.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> The sand particles are retained by the filter paper.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Water passes through the filter paper into the receiving container.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Residue — sand. Filtrate — water.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: Separating chalk powder from water</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Chalk powder is mixed with water and filtered. Where is the chalk powder found?</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Chalk powder is insoluble in water.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Its solid particles are retained on the filter paper.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> The water passes through into the receiving container.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The chalk powder is the residue on the filter paper.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: Filtering a salt solution</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> A learner filters a clear salt solution. Will the filter paper collect the dissolved salt?</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> The salt has dissolved and its particles are distributed throughout the water.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> The dissolved particles pass through ordinary filter paper with the water.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> No salt residue is normally left behind if the solution contains no undissolved material.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> No. The dissolved salt passes through with the water, so filtration does not separate it from the solution.</p>

  <h3>Important Distinction</h3>

  <p>
    A mixture may look cloudy because it contains suspended solid particles.
    A dissolved substance, however, is distributed throughout the liquid
    and is not removed by ordinary filtration. The key question is whether
    the solid is insoluble or dissolved.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Filtration', 15),
  (746, 746, '
  <h2>Crystallisation</h2>

  <h3>Meaning of Crystallisation</h3>

  <p>
    <strong>Crystallisation</strong> is a method of obtaining a dissolved
    solid from a solution in the form of crystals.
  </p>

  <p>
    It is useful when the aim is to obtain solid crystals rather than
    simply leave any solid residue after removing the liquid. The process
    depends on the solubility of the substance in the solvent.
  </p>

  <h3>How Crystallisation Works</h3>

  <p>
    Many solids dissolve more readily in a hot solvent than in a cold
    solvent. A hot, concentrated solution can therefore be cooled so that
    less of the dissolved substance remains dissolved. The excess substance
    forms crystals.
  </p>

  <p>A common procedure is:</p>

  <ol>
    <li>Prepare a solution containing the substance to be crystallised.</li>
    <li>Warm the solution, where appropriate, to dissolve the substance.</li>
    <li>Evaporate some solvent carefully to make the solution concentrated.</li>
    <li>Allow the concentrated solution to cool undisturbed.</li>
    <li>Allow crystals to form.</li>
    <li>Separate the crystals from the remaining liquid and dry them appropriately.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Why the Solution Is Concentrated First</h3>

  <p>
    If the solution is too dilute, cooling may not produce many crystals
    because the solvent can still keep most or all of the dissolved
    substance in solution. Removing some solvent helps make the solution
    concentrated enough for crystals to form when conditions change.
  </p>

  <h3>Crystallisation Versus Simply Evaporating to Dryness</h3>

  <p>
    In crystallisation, the solution is usually concentrated and then
    allowed to cool so that crystals form. The aim is to obtain crystals
    under controlled conditions.
  </p>

  <p>
    Evaporating a solution completely to dryness instead aims to remove
    the solvent as fully as practical and leave the solid behind. It may
    not produce crystals of the desired quality, and heating some
    substances strongly may damage them.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: Obtaining crystals from a solution</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> A learner wants to obtain crystals of a soluble solid from its aqueous solution. Describe the main steps.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Warm the solution if needed to help the solid dissolve.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Evaporate some water to produce a concentrated solution.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Allow the concentrated solution to cool undisturbed.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Collect the crystals that form and dry them appropriately.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Concentrate the solution, allow it to cool so crystals form, then collect and dry the crystals.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: Why cooling produces crystals</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> A hot concentrated solution forms crystals when it cools. Explain why.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> The solid is dissolved in the solvent while the solution is hot.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> As the solution cools, the solubility of many solids decreases.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> The solution can no longer keep as much of the solid dissolved.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Some dissolved particles form solid crystals.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Cooling can reduce solubility, causing excess dissolved substance to form crystals.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: A solution is too dilute</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> A learner cools a dilute solution but obtains very few crystals. Give a reason and a suitable adjustment.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> A dilute solution contains relatively little dissolved substance compared with the amount of solvent.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Cooling may not make enough substance come out of solution to form many crystals.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Carefully evaporating some solvent first can make the solution more concentrated.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 4:</strong> The concentrated solution can then be cooled to encourage crystal formation.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The solution may be too dilute. Evaporate some solvent carefully, then allow the concentrated solution to cool.</p>

  <h3>Conditions and Care</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Concentrate the solution carefully rather than overheating it.</li>
    <li>Allow sufficient time for crystals to form during cooling.</li>
    <li>Do not assume every dissolved solid has the same solubility behaviour.</li>
    <li>Some substances require different temperatures or procedures because their solubility changes differently.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Crystallisation', 15),
  (747, 747, '
  <h2>Simple Distillation</h2>

  <h3>Meaning of Simple Distillation</h3>

  <p>
    <strong>Simple distillation</strong> is a method of separating a
    liquid from a solution by vaporising the liquid and then cooling
    its vapour so that it condenses back into a liquid.
  </p>

  <p>
    It is commonly used to obtain a solvent from a solution containing
    a dissolved solid, such as obtaining water from salt solution.
  </p>

  <h3>Principle of Simple Distillation</h3>

  <p>
    When a salt solution is heated, water vaporises while the dissolved
    salt remains in the distillation flask under normal conditions.
    The water vapour passes into a condenser, where cooling changes it
    back into liquid water.
  </p>

  <p>
    The liquid collected after condensation is called the <strong>distillate</strong>.
  </p>

  <h3>Main Apparatus</h3>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Distillation flask:</strong> holds the solution being heated.</li>
    <li><strong>Heat source:</strong> supplies energy to vaporise the liquid.</li>
    <li><strong>Thermometer:</strong> measures the temperature of the vapour when positioned correctly.</li>
    <li><strong>Condenser:</strong> cools the vapour so that it condenses.</li>
    <li><strong>Receiving container:</strong> collects the distillate.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Procedure</h3>

  <ol>
    <li>Place the solution in the distillation flask.</li>
    <li>Arrange the flask, thermometer and condenser correctly.</li>
    <li>Heat the solution carefully so that the more volatile liquid vaporises.</li>
    <li>Pass the vapour through the cooled condenser.</li>
    <li>Collect the condensed liquid in the receiving container.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Worked Examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: Obtaining water from salt solution</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> A learner wants to obtain water from salt solution and collect it as a liquid. Which method is suitable, and how does it work?</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Heat the salt solution in a distillation flask.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Water vaporises and passes into the condenser.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> The condenser cools the water vapour.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 4:</strong> The vapour condenses to liquid water and is collected.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Simple distillation. The collected liquid water is the distillate.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: Identifying the distillate</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> During the simple distillation of salt solution, a liquid collects in the receiving container. What is this liquid expected to be, and why?</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Water vaporises from the heated solution.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> The dissolved salt does not normally vaporise with the water under these conditions.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Water vapour condenses in the condenser and flows into the receiver.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The distillate is water because the water vapour condenses and is collected as a liquid.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: The condenser is not cooled</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Explain what problem may occur if the condenser is not cooled properly during simple distillation.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> The liquid vapour must lose heat to condense.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> If the condenser is not cooled adequately, some vapour may not condense efficiently.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Less liquid distillate may be collected, and vapour may escape from the apparatus.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Condensation may be incomplete, reducing the amount of liquid collected and allowing vapour to escape.</p>

  <h3>Why Simple Distillation Is Useful</h3>

  <p>
    Unlike ordinary evaporation in an open dish, simple distillation
    collects the vapour after it has condensed. This makes it useful
    when recovering the liquid solvent is important.
  </p>

  <p>
    Simple distillation is not the best method for separating every
    mixture of liquids. If two miscible liquids have boiling points
    that are too close for effective separation by simple distillation,
    another method may be needed.
  </p>

  <h3>Laboratory Safety</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Use suitable laboratory supervision and protective equipment.</li>
    <li>Do not seal a heated distillation system in a way that allows pressure to build up dangerously.</li>
    <li>Secure the apparatus and handle hot glassware carefully.</li>
    <li>Never heat a distillation flask to dryness unless the procedure specifically permits it.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Simple Distillation', 15),
  (748, 748, '
  <h2>Fractional Distillation</h2>

  <h3>Meaning of Fractional Distillation</h3>

  <p>
    <strong>Fractional distillation</strong> is a method of separating
    a mixture of miscible liquids that have different boiling points.
    It is particularly useful when the boiling points are relatively
    close together.
  </p>

  <p>
    Miscible liquids are liquids that mix completely to form one uniform
    liquid mixture. Fractional distillation separates them by using
    differences in volatility and boiling point.
  </p>

  <h3>Principle of Fractional Distillation</h3>

  <p>
    When a mixture of liquids is heated, the vapour formed is generally
    richer in the more volatile component. The more volatile liquid
    usually has the lower boiling point.
  </p>

  <p>
    In the fractionating column, rising vapour repeatedly cools and
    condenses while vapour below it rises again. These repeated
    vaporisation and condensation processes enrich the rising vapour
    in the more volatile component.
  </p>

  <p>
    The vapour that reaches the condenser is cooled into a liquid and
    collected. As the temperature and composition change, different
    fractions may be collected over the course of the separation.
  </p>

  <h3>Main Apparatus</h3>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Distillation flask:</strong> holds the liquid mixture.</li>
    <li><strong>Heat source:</strong> heats the mixture.</li>
    <li><strong>Fractionating column:</strong> provides a surface for repeated condensation and vaporisation.</li>
    <li><strong>Thermometer:</strong> monitors the temperature of the vapour near the column outlet.</li>
    <li><strong>Condenser:</strong> cools vapour into liquid.</li>
    <li><strong>Receiving container:</strong> collects the liquid fraction.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Procedure</h3>

  <ol>
    <li>Place the mixture of miscible liquids in the distillation flask.</li>
    <li>Fit the fractionating column between the flask and the condenser.</li>
    <li>Heat the mixture carefully.</li>
    <li>Allow repeated vaporisation and condensation to occur in the column.</li>
    <li>Pass the vapour into the condenser.</li>
    <li>Collect the condensed liquid fraction.</li>
    <li>Monitor the temperature and collect fractions as appropriate.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Worked Examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: Choosing a method for two miscible liquids</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Two miscible liquids have boiling points of 78°C and 82°C. Which distillation method is generally more suitable for separating them, and why?</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> The liquids mix completely, so they form one liquid mixture.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Their boiling points are relatively close: the difference is 82 − 78 = 4°C.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> A fractionating column improves separation through repeated vaporisation and condensation.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Fractional distillation is generally more suitable because the liquids are miscible and have relatively close boiling points.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: Identifying the more volatile component</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> A mixture contains two miscible liquids with boiling points of 65°C and 100°C. Which component is more volatile, and which is generally enriched in the early vapour?</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Compare the boiling points: 65°C is lower than 100°C.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> The liquid with the lower boiling point is generally more volatile.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> The early vapour is generally richer in that more volatile component.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The liquid with a boiling point of 65°C is more volatile and is generally enriched in the early vapour.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: Understanding the fractionating column</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Explain why a fractionating column improves the separation of two miscible liquids whose boiling points are close together.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Vapour rises through the column and encounters cooler surfaces.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Some vapour condenses, and liquid may vaporise again as heat moves through the column.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> These repeated processes enrich the rising vapour in the more volatile component.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 4:</strong> The improved vapour composition makes separation more effective.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The column provides repeated vaporisation and condensation, helping separate liquids with close boiling points.</p>

  <h3>Important Distinction</h3>

  <p>
    Simple distillation is often suitable for recovering a liquid from a
    solution containing a dissolved solid, or for separating liquids
    when their boiling points are sufficiently different. Fractional
    distillation is particularly useful for separating miscible liquids
    with relatively close boiling points.
  </p>

  <p>
    Distillation does not always produce perfectly pure fractions.
    The effectiveness depends on the substances, their boiling points,
    the apparatus and the operating conditions.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Fractional Distillation', 15),
  (749, 749, '
  <h2>Chromatography</h2>

  <h3>Meaning of Chromatography</h3>

  <p>
    <strong>Chromatography</strong> is a separation technique used to
    separate the components of a mixture because the components move
    differently through a stationary material when carried by a mobile
    phase.
  </p>

  <p>
    In paper chromatography, the paper is the stationary phase and a
    suitable solvent is the mobile phase. The solvent moves through
    the paper and carries components of the mixture with it.
  </p>

  <h3>Principle of Paper Chromatography</h3>

  <p>
    Different substances interact differently with the paper and the
    solvent. Some components travel farther with the solvent, while
    others move more slowly or remain closer to the starting point.
    This causes the mixture to separate into distinct spots.
  </p>

  <h3>Procedure for Paper Chromatography</h3>

  <ol>
    <li>Draw a pencil baseline near the bottom of a strip of chromatography paper.</li>
    <li>Place a small spot of the mixture on the baseline.</li>
    <li>Allow the spot to dry if necessary.</li>
    <li>Place the lower end of the paper in a suitable solvent, keeping the baseline above the solvent level.</li>
    <li>Allow the solvent to travel up the paper.</li>
    <li>Remove the paper before the solvent reaches the top.</li>
    <li>Mark the solvent front promptly and allow the paper to dry.</li>
    <li>Observe the separated spots.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Why the Baseline Must Be Above the Solvent</h3>

  <p>
    If the sample spot is submerged in the solvent at the start, the
    sample may dissolve directly into the solvent reservoir instead of
    travelling up the paper as intended. Keeping the baseline above the
    solvent level allows the solvent to rise and carry the sample through
    the paper.
  </p>

  <h3>Interpreting a Chromatogram</h3>

  <p>
    A sample that separates into several distinct spots contains several
    components detectable under the conditions used. A sample that forms
    one spot may contain one detectable component, but one spot alone
    does not prove absolute purity because different substances can
    sometimes travel to the same position.
  </p>

  <p>
    When reference samples are run under the same conditions, the
    positions of their spots can help identify components in an unknown
    mixture. Matching positions alone are useful evidence, but may not
    always establish identity conclusively.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: Separating ink colours</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> A black ink sample produces blue, red and yellow spots on chromatography paper. What does this show?</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> The original ink appeared black.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> The chromatogram shows three distinct spots.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> The spots indicate components that travel differently through the paper under the chosen conditions.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The ink contains at least three detectable components under these conditions: those producing the blue, red and yellow spots.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: Why the baseline is drawn in pencil</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Explain why pencil is normally used to draw the baseline in paper chromatography instead of ordinary ink.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> The solvent moves up the paper and can dissolve substances that it contacts.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Ordinary ink may dissolve and separate into its own components.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Those components could create extra spots and interfere with the results.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Pencil is used because its graphite generally does not dissolve in the solvent, unlike many inks that could interfere with the chromatogram.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: Correcting an incorrect setup</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> A learner places the sample spot below the solvent level. Explain the problem and state how to correct it.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> The sample spot is submerged in the solvent reservoir.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> The sample may dissolve into the reservoir instead of being carried up the paper.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> The learner should raise the paper or lower the solvent so that the baseline is above the solvent level.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The sample may wash directly into the solvent. Keep the baseline above the solvent level before starting the separation.</p>

  <h3>What Affects the Separation?</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>The solvent selected.</li>
    <li>The type of paper or stationary material.</li>
    <li>The solubility of each component in the mobile phase.</li>
    <li>How strongly each component interacts with the stationary phase.</li>
    <li>The amount of sample placed on the baseline.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    The solvent must be suitable for the substances being separated.
    A solvent that does not carry the components effectively may produce
    little or no useful separation.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Chromatography', 15),
  (750, 750, '
  <h2>Decantation</h2>

  <h3>Meaning of Decantation</h3>

  <p>
    <strong>Decantation</strong> is a separation method in which a liquid
    is carefully poured away from a settled solid or from another liquid
    that does not mix with it.
  </p>

  <p>
    In solid-liquid decantation, the solid is allowed to settle at the
    bottom of the container before the liquid above it is poured away.
  </p>

  <h3>Decantation of a Solid-Liquid Mixture</h3>

  <p>
    Consider a mixture of sand and water. If the mixture is left
    undisturbed, the sand particles may settle at the bottom. The clearer
    water above the sand can then be poured carefully into another
    container without disturbing the settled solid.
  </p>

  <ol>
    <li>Allow the solid particles to settle where settling is possible.</li>
    <li>Keep the container still so the settled solid is not disturbed.</li>
    <li>Tilt the container slowly.</li>
    <li>Pour the liquid into a second container, leaving the settled solid behind.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Decantation of Immiscible Liquids</h3>

  <p>
    Decantation can also be used to separate two immiscible liquids,
    which form separate layers. For example, oil and water form distinct
    layers because they do not mix completely.
  </p>

  <p>
    The upper layer may be poured off carefully when the layers are
    clearly separated. For more controlled separation, a separating
    funnel can be used.
  </p>

  <h3>Limitations of Decantation</h3>

  <p>
    Decantation is simple, but it may not produce a completely pure
    liquid. Fine particles can remain suspended in the liquid, and
    some of the liquid may remain mixed with or trapped among the
    settled solid.
  </p>

  <p>
    If the solid does not settle well, or if a clearer separation is
    required, decantation alone may be insufficient.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: Separating sand and water</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> A learner mixes sand and water and leaves the container undisturbed until the sand settles. Explain how the learner can separate most of the water.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Allow the sand particles to settle at the bottom.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Keep the container steady so the sand remains settled.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Tilt the container carefully and pour the water into another container.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Use decantation by carefully pouring off the water after the sand has settled.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: Separating oil and water</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Oil and water form two distinct layers in a container. Explain how careful decantation can separate them.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Allow the mixture to stand until the two layers are clearly separated.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Identify the upper and lower layers.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Carefully pour off one layer without mixing it with the other.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Allow the liquids to form separate layers and carefully pour off the desired layer. A separating funnel may provide more controlled separation.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: Why decantation may leave impurities</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> After decanting water from a mixture containing fine solid particles, the water remains cloudy. Explain why.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Not all fine particles may settle during the waiting period.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Some particles remain suspended in the liquid.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> When the liquid is poured off, those suspended particles move with it.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The water remains cloudy because some fine solid particles have not settled and are carried into the receiving container.</p>

  <h3>Choosing Decantation</h3>

  <p>
    Decantation is most useful when the components can separate into
    distinct layers or when a solid settles sufficiently for the liquid
    above it to be poured away. It is less reliable when the solid
    remains suspended or when very complete separation is required.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Decantation', 15);
INSERT INTO public.lessons (id, topic_id, content, summary, estimated_minutes) VALUES
  (751, 751, '
  <h2>Magnetic Separation</h2>

  <h3>Meaning of Magnetic Separation</h3>

  <p>
    <strong>Magnetic separation</strong> is a method of separating
    magnetic material from non-magnetic material by using a magnet.
  </p>

  <p>
    The method works because some materials, such as iron, are attracted
    strongly to a magnet, while other materials, such as sulfur and
    ordinary sand, are not attracted in the same way.
  </p>

  <h3>Principle of Magnetic Separation</h3>

  <p>
    When a magnet is brought near a mixture containing magnetic and
    non-magnetic solids, the magnetic material is attracted to the magnet.
    The non-magnetic material remains behind, provided the arrangement
    allows the materials to be separated.
  </p>

  <h3>Procedure</h3>

  <ol>
    <li>Identify a mixture containing a magnetic material and a non-magnetic material.</li>
    <li>Bring a magnet close to the mixture.</li>
    <li>Allow the magnetic material to be attracted to the magnet.</li>
    <li>Remove the attracted material from the magnet safely.</li>
    <li>Repeat if necessary to improve the separation.</li>
  </ol>

  <p>
    Wrapping the magnet in suitable paper or a thin covering can make
    it easier to remove the collected magnetic material, provided the
    covering does not prevent effective attraction.
  </p>

  <h3>Examples of Suitable Mixtures</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Iron filings mixed with sulfur powder.</li>
    <li>Iron filings mixed with sand.</li>
    <li>Magnetic iron-containing material mixed with non-magnetic solids, where the magnetic component can be attracted effectively.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Limitations</h3>

  <p>
    Magnetic separation only works effectively when the mixture contains
    a component that responds sufficiently to the magnet. It cannot
    separate two non-magnetic substances from each other using an
    ordinary magnet alone.
  </p>

  <p>
    Not every metal is attracted strongly to an ordinary magnet. For
    example, aluminium and copper are not attracted in the same way as
    iron. Therefore, a learner must identify the magnetic behaviour of
    the materials rather than assume that every metal is magnetic.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: Separating iron filings from sulfur</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> A mixture contains iron filings and sulfur powder. Describe how to separate the iron filings.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Iron is attracted to a magnet.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Sulfur is not attracted to an ordinary magnet in the same way.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Bring a magnet close to the mixture so that the iron filings are attracted.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Remove the iron filings from the magnet using a suitable method.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Use a magnet to attract and collect the iron filings, leaving the sulfur behind.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: Separating iron from sand</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> A learner mixes iron filings with sand. Explain why a magnet can separate the two solids.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Iron is magnetic and is attracted to the magnet.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Ordinary sand is not attracted to the magnet in the same way.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> The iron can be collected while most of the sand remains behind.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Magnetic separation works because the iron is attracted to the magnet while the sand is not.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: A mixture of two non-magnetic solids</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> A mixture contains sulfur powder and ordinary sand. Explain why an ordinary magnet cannot separate these substances effectively.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Ordinary sulfur is not attracted to an ordinary magnet in the same way as iron.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Ordinary sand is also not attracted to the magnet in the same way.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> The magnet therefore does not selectively collect one of these components.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> An ordinary magnet cannot effectively separate sulfur from sand because neither component is attracted strongly enough for selective collection.</p>

  <h3>Choosing Magnetic Separation</h3>

  <p>
    Before choosing this method, ask whether one component is magnetic
    and the other is not. The method depends on that difference in
    magnetic behaviour, not simply on whether one component is a metal.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Magnetic Separation', 15),
  (752, 752, '
  <h2>Structure of the Atom</h2>

  <h3>1. What is an atom?</h3>

  <p>
    An <b>atom</b> is the smallest particle of an element that
    retains the chemical identity of that element.
    All ordinary matter is made from atoms or combinations of atoms.
  </p>

  <p>
    An atom contains a tiny, dense central region called the
    <b>nucleus</b>, surrounded by a region occupied by electrons.
    The nucleus contains protons and, in most atoms, neutrons.
    Electrons occupy specific energy levels around the nucleus.
  </p>

  <h3>2. The three main subatomic particles</h3>

  <table>
    <tr>
      <th>Particle</th>
      <th>Relative charge</th>
      <th>Relative mass</th>
      <th>Location</th>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Proton</td>
      <td>+1</td>
      <td>1</td>
      <td>Nucleus</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Neutron</td>
      <td>0</td>
      <td>1</td>
      <td>Nucleus</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Electron</td>
      <td>-1</td>
      <td>Approximately 1/1840</td>
      <td>Energy levels around the nucleus</td>
    </tr>
  </table>

  <p>
    Protons and neutrons account for almost all the mass of an atom.
    Electrons have a much smaller mass, although they are essential
    in determining how atoms form chemical bonds.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Why is an atom electrically neutral?</h3>

  <p>
    A proton carries one unit of positive relative charge.
    An electron carries one unit of negative relative charge.
    A neutron carries no charge.
  </p>

  <p>
    For a neutral atom, the number of protons equals the number
    of electrons. The positive and negative charges therefore cancel.
  </p>

  <p><b>Net relative charge = number of protons - number of electrons</b></p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Finding the overall charge</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> An atom contains 11 protons, 12 neutrons
  and 11 electrons. Calculate its overall relative charge.</p>

  <p><b>Step 1: Identify the charged particles.</b></p>
  <p>Protons contribute positive charge; electrons contribute negative charge.
  Neutrons contribute zero charge.</p>

  <p><b>Step 2: Calculate the total positive charge.</b></p>
  <p>Positive charge = 11 x (+1) = +11.</p>

  <p><b>Step 3: Calculate the total negative charge.</b></p>
  <p>Negative charge = 11 x (-1) = -11.</p>

  <p><b>Step 4: Add the charges.</b></p>
  <p>Net charge = +11 + (-11) = 0.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> The atom is electrically neutral.</p>

  <p><b>Check:</b> The 12 neutrons do not affect the charge
  because each neutron has relative charge zero.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Recognising a charged particle</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> A particle contains 13 protons and 10 electrons.
  Determine its net relative charge.</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Total positive charge = 13 x (+1) = +13.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Total negative charge = 10 x (-1) = -10.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Net charge = +13 + (-10) = +3.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> The particle has a relative charge of +3.</p>

  <p>
    It is positively charged because it has three more protons
    than electrons. If it is an aluminium particle, its symbol
    is Al3+.
  </p>

  <h3>Common mistake</h3>
  <p>
    Do not count neutrons when calculating electrical charge.
    Neutrons contribute to mass number, not net electrical charge.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Structure of the Atom', 15),
  (753, 753, '
  <h2>Protons</h2>

  <h3>Definition and properties</h3>

  <p>
    A <b>proton</b> is a positively charged subatomic particle
    found in the nucleus of an atom.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>Relative charge: +1.</li>
    <li>Relative mass: approximately 1.</li>
    <li>Location: nucleus.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    The number of protons determines the identity of an element.
    This number is called the <b>atomic number</b>, represented by Z.
  </p>

  <p>
    Hydrogen has 1 proton, carbon has 6, oxygen has 8,
    sodium has 11 and chlorine has 17.
  </p>

  <p>
    An atom cannot become a different element simply by gaining
    or losing electrons. Its proton number must change for its
    elemental identity to change.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Identifying an element</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> An atom contains 17 protons. Identify the element.</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Atomic number = number of protons.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Atomic number = 17.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> The element with atomic number 17 is chlorine.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> Chlorine (Cl).</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Comparing two particles</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Particle A contains 11 protons and particle B
  contains 12 protons. Can both particles be atoms of the same element?</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Particle A has atomic number 11, so it is sodium.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Particle B has atomic number 12, so it is magnesium.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Their proton numbers are different.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> No. They are different elements.</p>

  <p>
    Their electron or neutron numbers may vary, but their proton
    numbers establish their elemental identities.
  </p>

  <h3>Common mistake</h3>
  <p>
    Do not identify an element from its neutron or electron number
    alone. Use the proton number, or atomic number.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Protons', 15),
  (754, 754, '
  <h2>Neutrons</h2>

  <h3>Definition and properties</h3>

  <p>
    A <b>neutron</b> is a subatomic particle found in the nucleus
    of an atom. It has no electrical charge and a relative mass
    approximately equal to 1.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>Relative charge: 0.</li>
    <li>Relative mass: approximately 1.</li>
    <li>Location: nucleus.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Neutrons contribute to the mass of an atom but do not contribute
    to its net electrical charge.
  </p>

  <h3>Calculating the number of neutrons</h3>

  <p>
    The <b>mass number</b>, A, is the total number of protons
    and neutrons in the nucleus.
  </p>

  <p><b>A = Z + n</b></p>

  <p>Rearranging gives:</p>

  <p><b>Number of neutrons, n = A - Z</b></p>

  <p>
    Here, A represents mass number and Z represents atomic number,
    which is the number of protons.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Finding neutrons</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Sodium-23 has atomic number 11.
  Calculate the number of neutrons.</p>

  <p><b>Step 1: Write the known values.</b></p>
  <p>Mass number A = 23; atomic number Z = 11.</p>

  <p><b>Step 2: Choose the correct formula.</b></p>
  <p>n = A - Z.</p>

  <p><b>Step 3: Substitute the values.</b></p>
  <p>n = 23 - 11 = 12.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> 12 neutrons.</p>

  <p><b>Verification:</b> Protons + neutrons = 11 + 12 = 23.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Finding mass number</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> An atom contains 19 protons and 20 neutrons.
  Calculate its mass number.</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> A = protons + neutrons.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> A = 19 + 20.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> A = 39.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> Mass number = 39.</p>

  <h3>Common mistake</h3>
  <p>
    Do not subtract the mass number from the atomic number.
    Neutron number = mass number - atomic number, because the
    mass number includes both protons and neutrons.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Neutrons', 15),
  (755, 755, '
  <h2>Electrons</h2>

  <h3>Definition and properties</h3>

  <p>
    An <b>electron</b> is a negatively charged subatomic particle
    found in energy levels around an atom''s nucleus.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>Relative charge: -1.</li>
    <li>Relative mass: approximately 1/1840.</li>
    <li>Location: energy levels around the nucleus.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    The electron''s relative mass is much smaller than that of a proton
    or neutron. Consequently, the nucleus contains almost all the
    atom''s mass.
  </p>

  <h3>Electron number in a neutral atom</h3>

  <p>
    A neutral atom contains equal numbers of protons and electrons.
    Since the atomic number gives the number of protons:
  </p>

  <p><b>Electrons in a neutral atom = atomic number</b></p>

  <h3>What happens when electrons change?</h3>

  <p>
    Gaining or losing electrons changes the net electrical charge
    of an atom. It does not change the element''s proton number.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>Losing electrons produces a positive ion.</li>
    <li>Gaining electrons produces a negative ion.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Finding electrons</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> A neutral magnesium atom has atomic number 12.
  Calculate its number of electrons.</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Atomic number = proton number = 12.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> A neutral atom has equal numbers of protons and electrons.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Electron number = 12.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> 12 electrons.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Losing electrons</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> A neutral aluminium atom has 13 electrons.
  It loses three electrons. Calculate its new electron number and charge.</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Initial electron number = 13.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> New electron number = 13 - 3 = 10.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Aluminium still has 13 protons.</p>
  <p><b>Step 4:</b> Charge = protons - electrons = 13 - 10 = +3.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> 10 electrons; charge +3; ion Al3+.</p>

  <h3>Common mistake</h3>
  <p>
    Do not assume an ion has the same number of electrons as protons.
    That equality applies to neutral atoms, not to charged ions.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Electrons', 15),
  (756, 756, '
  <h2>Atomic Number</h2>

  <h3>Definition</h3>

  <p>
    The <b>atomic number</b>, represented by Z, is the number
    of protons in the nucleus of an atom.
  </p>

  <p><b>Z = number of protons</b></p>

  <p>
    Atomic number uniquely identifies an element. For example,
    atomic number 6 identifies carbon, 8 identifies oxygen,
    11 identifies sodium and 17 identifies chlorine.
  </p>

  <p>
    The proton number remains the same when an atom forms an ion
    or when isotopes of an element are compared.
  </p>

  <h3>Relationship to electron number</h3>

  <p>
    For a neutral atom, the number of electrons equals the atomic number.
    For an ion, the electron number differs because electrons have
    been gained or lost.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Atomic number and electron number</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> A neutral atom has atomic number 12.
  State its proton number and electron number, and identify the element.</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Proton number = Z = 12.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Because the atom is neutral, electron number = 12.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Atomic number 12 identifies magnesium.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> 12 protons, 12 electrons, magnesium (Mg).</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Atomic number of an ion</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> An ion contains 13 protons and 10 electrons.
  Determine its atomic number, elemental identity and charge.</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Atomic number = proton number = 13.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Element 13 is aluminium.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Charge = 13 - 10 = +3.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> Atomic number 13; aluminium; Al3+.</p>

  <h3>Common mistake</h3>
  <p>
    Atomic number is not the mass number. Atomic number counts protons;
    mass number counts protons plus neutrons.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Atomic Number', 15),
  (757, 757, '
  <h2>Mass Number</h2>

  <h3>1. Meaning of mass number</h3>

  <p>
    The <b>mass number</b>, represented by A, is the total number
    of protons and neutrons in the nucleus of an atom.
    Protons and neutrons are called nucleons.
  </p>

  <p><b>Mass number = number of protons + number of neutrons</b></p>
  <p><b>A = Z + n</b></p>

  <p>
    In this equation, A is the mass number, Z is the atomic number
    and n is the number of neutrons.
  </p>

  <p>
    Rearranging the equation gives:
  </p>

  <p><b>Number of neutrons = A - Z</b></p>
  <p><b>Number of protons = A - n</b></p>

  <h3>2. Why are electrons excluded?</h3>

  <p>
    Protons and neutrons each have a relative mass approximately
    equal to 1. An electron has a much smaller relative mass,
    approximately 1/1840.
  </p>

  <p>
    Mass number is a count of nuclear particles, not a measurement
    of the atom''s total mass in grams. Electrons are excluded
    from the mass-number calculation.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Calculating mass number</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> An atom contains 13 protons and 14 neutrons.
  Calculate its mass number.</p>

  <p><b>Step 1: Write the relationship.</b></p>
  <p>A = Z + n</p>

  <p><b>Step 2: Substitute the values.</b></p>
  <p>A = 13 + 14</p>

  <p><b>Step 3: Calculate.</b></p>
  <p>A = 27</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> The mass number is 27.</p>

  <p><b>Verification:</b> The nucleus contains 27 nucleons:
  13 protons + 14 neutrons.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Calculating neutrons</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Aluminium-27 has atomic number 13.
  Calculate its number of neutrons.</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Mass number A = 27.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Atomic number Z = 13.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> n = A - Z = 27 - 13 = 14.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> 14 neutrons.</p>

  <p><b>Verification:</b> 13 + 14 = 27.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Nuclear notation</h3>

  <p>
    An atom can be represented as <sup>27</sup><sub>13</sub>Al.
    The upper number is the mass number, 27. The lower number
    is the atomic number, 13.
  </p>

  <p>
    Protons = 13; neutrons = 27 - 13 = 14.
    If the atom is neutral, electrons = 13.
  </p>

  <h3>Common mistake</h3>

  <p>
    Do not add electrons to the mass number. Also, do not confuse
    atomic number with mass number: atomic number counts protons;
    mass number counts protons and neutrons.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Mass Number', 15),
  (758, 758, '
  <h2>Electron Arrangement</h2>

  <h3>1. Meaning of electron arrangement</h3>

  <p>
    Electrons occupy energy levels around the nucleus.
    The distribution of electrons among these energy levels
    is called the <b>electron arrangement</b>.
  </p>

  <p>
    For the first 20 elements, the introductory shell model
    commonly uses the arrangement 2, 8, 8, 2.
    This is a simplified model for these elements, not a
    universal rule for all elements.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>First shell: maximum 2 electrons.</li>
    <li>Second shell: maximum 8 electrons.</li>
    <li>Third shell: up to 8 electrons in the simplified arrangement for the first 20 elements.</li>
    <li>Fourth shell: begins to fill for potassium and calcium.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>2. Finding the number of electrons</h3>

  <p>
    For a neutral atom, the total number of electrons equals
    its atomic number. Add the numbers in the shell arrangement
    to check the total.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Sodium</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Write the electron arrangement of sodium,
  atomic number 11.</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> A neutral sodium atom has 11 electrons.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Place 2 electrons in the first shell.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Place 8 electrons in the second shell.</p>
  <p><b>Step 4:</b> Electrons placed = 2 + 8 = 10.</p>
  <p><b>Step 5:</b> Remaining electrons = 11 - 10 = 1.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> 2,8,1.</p>

  <p><b>Check:</b> 2 + 8 + 1 = 11.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Calcium</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Write the electron arrangement of calcium,
  atomic number 20.</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Calcium has 20 electrons if neutral.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> The first shell receives 2 electrons.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> The second shell receives 8 electrons.</p>
  <p><b>Step 4:</b> The third shell receives 8 electrons in the simplified arrangement.</p>
  <p><b>Step 5:</b> Electrons placed = 2 + 8 + 8 = 18.</p>
  <p><b>Step 6:</b> Remaining electrons = 20 - 18 = 2.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> 2,8,8,2.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Finding atomic number</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> A neutral atom has electron arrangement 2,8,7.
  Find its atomic number and identify the element.</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Add the electrons: 2 + 8 + 7 = 17.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> A neutral atom has 17 protons.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Atomic number = 17.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Chlorine.</p>

  <h3>Common mistake</h3>

  <p>
    Do not distribute electrons randomly. Fill the lower energy
    levels according to the model being used, then check that
    the total equals the correct electron number.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Electron Arrangement', 15),
  (759, 759, '
  <h2>Isotopes</h2>

  <h3>1. Definition</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Isotopes</b> are atoms of the same element that have the
    same number of protons but different numbers of neutrons.
  </p>

  <p>
    Since the proton number is unchanged, isotopes have the same
    atomic number and the same elemental identity. Their different
    neutron numbers give them different mass numbers.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Example: Carbon isotopes</h3>

  <p><b>Carbon-12:</b></p>
  <p>Protons = 6; neutrons = 12 - 6 = 6.</p>

  <p><b>Carbon-14:</b></p>
  <p>Protons = 6; neutrons = 14 - 6 = 8.</p>

  <p>
    Both atoms are carbon because each has six protons.
    They differ in neutron number: carbon-12 has six neutrons,
    while carbon-14 has eight.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Calculating isotope particles</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Chlorine has atomic number 17.
  Calculate the neutron numbers of chlorine-35 and chlorine-37.</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Both isotopes have 17 protons.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Chlorine-35: neutrons = 35 - 17 = 18.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Chlorine-37: neutrons = 37 - 17 = 20.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> 18 and 20 neutrons, respectively.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Comparing isotope composition</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Two atoms each contain 8 protons.
  Atom A contains 8 neutrons, while atom B contains 10 neutrons.
  Determine their mass numbers and explain their relationship.</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Atom A: mass number = 8 + 8 = 16.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Atom B: mass number = 8 + 10 = 18.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Both have atomic number 8, which is oxygen.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> Oxygen-16 and oxygen-18; they are isotopes.</p>

  <h3>Common mistake</h3>

  <p>
    Isotopes do not have different proton numbers. If their proton
    numbers differ, they are different elements.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Isotopes', 15),
  (760, 760, '
  <h2>Identifying Isotopes</h2>

  <h3>1. The identification rule</h3>

  <p>
    To determine whether two atoms are isotopes, compare their
    proton numbers first and their neutron numbers second.
  </p>

  <p><b>Same proton number + different neutron numbers = isotopes.</b></p>

  <p>
    If the proton numbers differ, the atoms are different elements
    and cannot be isotopes of one another.
  </p>

  <h3>2. A systematic comparison method</h3>

  <ol>
    <li>Identify or calculate the proton number of each atom.</li>
    <li>If proton numbers differ, they are not isotopes.</li>
    <li>If proton numbers match, compare their neutron numbers.</li>
    <li>If neutron numbers differ, they are isotopes.</li>
    <li>If both proton and neutron numbers match, they are the same nuclide, not different isotopes.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Same element, different neutrons</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Atom A has 8 protons and 8 neutrons.
  Atom B has 8 protons and 10 neutrons. Determine whether they
  are isotopes.</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Compare proton numbers: 8 and 8.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> The proton numbers are equal.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Compare neutron numbers: 8 and 10.</p>
  <p><b>Step 4:</b> The neutron numbers differ.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> They are isotopes of oxygen.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Using nuclear notation</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Determine whether ³⁵₁₇Cl and ³⁷₁₇Cl
  are isotopes.</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Both have atomic number 17.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Both contain 17 protons.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Chlorine-35 has 35 - 17 = 18 neutrons.</p>
  <p><b>Step 4:</b> Chlorine-37 has 37 - 17 = 20 neutrons.</p>
  <p><b>Step 5:</b> The proton numbers match, but neutron numbers differ.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> Yes, they are isotopes of chlorine.</p>

  <h3>Common mistake</h3>

  <p>
    Do not compare mass numbers alone. Equal mass numbers do not
    prove that atoms are isotopes.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Identifying Isotopes', 15),
  (761, 761, '
  <h2>Uses of Isotopes</h2>

  <h3>1. Stable isotopes and radioisotopes</h3>

  <p>
    Isotopes of an element have the same proton number but
    different neutron numbers. Some isotopes have unstable nuclei
    and emit radiation as their nuclei change. These are called
    <b>radioisotopes</b>.
  </p>

  <p>
    Their uses depend on properties such as the radiation they emit,
    their half-life and whether the radiation can be detected safely
    for a particular application.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Medical applications</h3>

  <p>
    Some radioisotopes are used as tracers to investigate processes
    inside the body. Radiation from a tracer can be detected to
    obtain diagnostic information.
  </p>

  <p>
    Other radioisotopes are used in treatment, where suitable
    radiation can damage targeted abnormal cells.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Industrial applications</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Thickness monitoring: radiation measurements help monitor the thickness of sheet materials during manufacture.</li>
    <li>Leak detection and tracing: suitable radioactive tracers help investigate the movement of substances.</li>
    <li>Industrial measurement: radiation detection can provide information about materials and processes.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>4. Archaeology and age determination</h3>

  <p>
    Carbon-14 dating can estimate the age of suitable once-living
    materials. Living organisms exchange carbon with their
    environment. After death, this exchange stops and the carbon-14
    content decreases through radioactive decay.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked example: Radioactive decay calculation</h3>

  <p>
    The half-life of a radioisotope is the time required for half
    the radioactive nuclei in a sample to decay.
  </p>

  <p><b>Question:</b> A sample initially contains 80 mg of a
  radioisotope. Its half-life is 5 years. Calculate the mass
  of the radioactive isotope remaining after 15 years.</p>

  <p><b>Step 1: Calculate the number of half-lives.</b></p>
  <p>Number of half-lives = elapsed time / half-life.</p>
  <p>= 15 / 5 = 3.</p>

  <p><b>Step 2: Halve the mass after each half-life.</b></p>
  <p>Initially: 80 mg.</p>
  <p>After 5 years: 80 / 2 = 40 mg.</p>
  <p>After 10 years: 40 / 2 = 20 mg.</p>
  <p>After 15 years: 20 / 2 = 10 mg.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> 10 mg of the radioactive isotope remains.</p>

  <p>
    Alternatively, remaining mass = initial mass x (1/2)^n,
    where n is the number of half-lives.
  </p>

  <p>
    Remaining mass = 80 x (1/2)^3 = 80 / 8 = 10 mg.
  </p>

  <h3>5. Radiation safety</h3>

  <p>
    Ionising radiation can damage living cells. Radioactive
    materials therefore require appropriate controls, including
    reducing exposure time, maintaining distance where suitable
    and using suitable shielding.
  </p>

  <h3>Common mistake</h3>

  <p>
    Do not assume all isotopes are radioactive. Isotopes can be
    stable or radioactive, and the application depends on the
    specific isotope and its properties.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Uses of Isotopes', 15),
  (762, 762, '
  <h2>Ion Formation</h2>

  <h3>1. What is an ion?</h3>

  <p>
    An <b>ion</b> is an atom or group of atoms with a net
    electrical charge. An atom forms an ion by gaining or losing
    electrons.
  </p>

  <p>
    Protons are positively charged and remain in the nucleus
    during ordinary chemical ion formation. Electrons are
    transferred, changing the balance between positive and
    negative charge.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Formation of positive ions</h3>

  <p>
    A positive ion is called a <b>cation</b>. It forms when an
    atom loses one or more electrons.
  </p>

  <p><b>Na → Na+ + e-</b></p>

  <p>
    A neutral sodium atom loses one electron to form Na+.
    The proton number remains 11, but the electron number falls
    from 11 to 10.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Formation of negative ions</h3>

  <p>
    A negative ion is called an <b>anion</b>. It forms when an
    atom gains one or more electrons.
  </p>

  <p><b>Cl + e- → Cl-</b></p>

  <p>
    A neutral chlorine atom gains one electron. It still has
    17 protons but now has 18 electrons, giving a charge of -1.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Magnesium ion</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> A magnesium atom has atomic number 12.
  It loses two electrons. Determine its ion symbol and charge.</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Proton number = 12.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Initial electron number = 12.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Final electron number = 12 - 2 = 10.</p>
  <p><b>Step 4:</b> Charge = 12 - 10 = +2.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> Mg2+.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Oxide ion</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Oxygen has atomic number 8 and gains two electrons.
  Determine the number of electrons and the charge of the ion.</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Oxygen initially has 8 electrons when neutral.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> New electron number = 8 + 2 = 10.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Charge = 8 - 10 = -2.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> O2-, containing 10 electrons.</p>

  <h3>Common mistake</h3>

  <p>
    Losing electrons produces a positive ion, not a negative one.
    Gaining electrons produces a negative ion, not a positive one.
    The protons do not disappear during ordinary ion formation.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Ion Formation', 15),
  (763, 763, '
  <h2>Writing Ion Charges</h2>

  <h3>1. What does an ion charge represent?</h3>

  <p>
    An ion''s charge represents the difference between the number
    of protons and electrons in the particle.
  </p>

  <p><b>Relative charge = number of protons - number of electrons</b></p>

  <p>
    A positive result means a cation; a negative result means
    an anion; zero means the particle is neutral.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Writing ion symbols</h3>

  <p>
    The charge is written as a superscript to the upper right
    of the element symbol.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>Na+ means a charge of +1.</li>
    <li>Mg2+ means a charge of +2.</li>
    <li>Al3+ means a charge of +3.</li>
    <li>Cl- means a charge of -1.</li>
    <li>O2- means a charge of -2.</li>
    <li>N3- means a charge of -3.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    For charges of magnitude one, the numeral 1 is normally omitted.
    The sign must not be omitted because it indicates whether the
    ion is positive or negative.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Charge from particle numbers</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> An ion has 12 protons and 10 electrons.
  Calculate its charge and write its symbol.</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Charge = protons - electrons.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Charge = 12 - 10 = +2.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Atomic number 12 identifies magnesium.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> Mg2+.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Charge of a negative ion</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> A particle contains 17 protons and 18 electrons.
  Determine its ion symbol.</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Charge = 17 - 18 = -1.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Proton number 17 identifies chlorine.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Write the element symbol with its charge.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> Cl-.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Finding the electron number</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> An aluminium ion has charge +3.
  Aluminium has atomic number 13. Calculate the electron number.</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Protons = 13.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Charge = protons - electrons.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> +3 = 13 - electrons.</p>
  <p><b>Step 4:</b> Electrons = 13 - 3 = 10.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> 10 electrons; Al3+.</p>

  <h3>Common mistake</h3>

  <p>
    The charge is not the total number of electrons or protons.
    It is their difference. Always preserve the sign when writing
    the ion symbol.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Writing Ion Charges', 15),
  (764, 764, '
  <h2>Arrangement of the Periodic Table</h2>

  <h3>1. What is the periodic table?</h3>

  <p>
    The <strong>periodic table</strong> is a systematic arrangement
    of chemical elements in which elements are placed in order of
    increasing atomic number and elements with related chemical
    properties occur in the same groups.
  </p>

  <p>
    An <strong>element</strong> is a pure substance consisting of
    atoms with the same number of protons. Examples include hydrogen,
    oxygen, sodium and iron.
  </p>

  <p>
    Each element has a unique atomic number. The atomic number is the
    number of protons in the nucleus of an atom. For a neutral atom,
    it is also equal to the number of electrons.
  </p>

  <p>
    The periodic table is not simply a list of element names.
    Its arrangement allows us to connect an element''s position
    with its electronic structure and chemical behaviour.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Why are elements arranged by atomic number?</h3>

  <p>
    The atomic number identifies an element. Hydrogen has atomic
    number 1, helium has atomic number 2, lithium has atomic number 3,
    and so on.
  </p>

  <p>
    Each successive element has one more proton than the preceding
    element. For neutral atoms, it also has one more electron.
    This changes the electronic structure in a systematic way.
  </p>

  <p>
    As electrons occupy energy levels, recurring outer-electron
    arrangements appear. Since outer electrons strongly influence
    chemical reactions, elements with related arrangements often
    have similar properties.
  </p>

  <p>
    This recurring pattern is the basis of the periodic arrangement.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Atomic number, symbol and element name</h3>

  <p>
    Every element has a name and a chemical symbol. Symbols are
    one or two letters long in the elements considered here.
    The first letter is capitalised; the second, if present, is
    lowercase.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>H represents hydrogen.</li>
    <li>He represents helium.</li>
    <li>Na represents sodium.</li>
    <li>Cl represents chlorine.</li>
    <li>Ca represents calcium.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Notice that sodium is represented by Na, not S. The symbol
    must be learnt accurately because S represents sulfur.
  </p>

  <h3>4. The structure of the table</h3>

  <p>
    The main structure consists of horizontal rows called
    <strong>periods</strong> and vertical columns called
    <strong>groups</strong>.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Periods:</strong> horizontal rows. For the elements
      covered at this level, the period number tells us the number
      of occupied main electron energy levels.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Groups:</strong> vertical columns. Elements in the
      same main-group column generally have related outer-electron
      arrangements and similar chemical properties.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    These two directions tell us different things. A period is
    associated with the number of occupied energy levels, while
    a group helps us understand the outer electrons and chemical
    behaviour.
  </p>

  <h3>5. The first 20 elements</h3>

  <p>
    Learn to recognise the names, symbols and atomic numbers of
    the first 20 elements. Their sequence is especially useful
    when studying electronic structure and periodic position.
  </p>

  <table>
    <tr>
      <th>Atomic number</th>
      <th>Symbol</th>
      <th>Element</th>
    </tr>
    <tr><td>1</td><td>H</td><td>Hydrogen</td></tr>
    <tr><td>2</td><td>He</td><td>Helium</td></tr>
    <tr><td>3</td><td>Li</td><td>Lithium</td></tr>
    <tr><td>4</td><td>Be</td><td>Beryllium</td></tr>
    <tr><td>5</td><td>B</td><td>Boron</td></tr>
    <tr><td>6</td><td>C</td><td>Carbon</td></tr>
    <tr><td>7</td><td>N</td><td>Nitrogen</td></tr>
    <tr><td>8</td><td>O</td><td>Oxygen</td></tr>
    <tr><td>9</td><td>F</td><td>Fluorine</td></tr>
    <tr><td>10</td><td>Ne</td><td>Neon</td></tr>
    <tr><td>11</td><td>Na</td><td>Sodium</td></tr>
    <tr><td>12</td><td>Mg</td><td>Magnesium</td></tr>
    <tr><td>13</td><td>Al</td><td>Aluminium</td></tr>
    <tr><td>14</td><td>Si</td><td>Silicon</td></tr>
    <tr><td>15</td><td>P</td><td>Phosphorus</td></tr>
    <tr><td>16</td><td>S</td><td>Sulfur</td></tr>
    <tr><td>17</td><td>Cl</td><td>Chlorine</td></tr>
    <tr><td>18</td><td>Ar</td><td>Argon</td></tr>
    <tr><td>19</td><td>K</td><td>Potassium</td></tr>
    <tr><td>20</td><td>Ca</td><td>Calcium</td></tr>
  </table>

  <h3>6. How to read an element''s position</h3>

  <p>
    Suppose an element has atomic number 12. A neutral atom has
    12 protons and 12 electrons. Its electron arrangement is 2,8,2.
    This places it in Period 3 and Group 2.
  </p>

  <p>
    Its position is useful because it allows us to predict that
    magnesium has two outer electrons and commonly forms Mg²⁺ ions
    in its compounds.
  </p>

  <p>
    Therefore, the periodic table connects three important ideas:
    atomic number, electronic structure and chemical properties.
  </p>

  <h3>Common mistakes to avoid</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>
      Do not confuse atomic number with mass number. Atomic number
      counts protons; mass number counts protons plus neutrons.
    </li>
    <li>
      Do not confuse a period with a group. Periods run horizontally;
      groups run vertically.
    </li>
    <li>
      Do not assume that neighbouring elements always have similar
      chemical properties. Similarity is especially useful when
      comparing elements in the same group.
    </li>
    <li>
      Do not use the element''s mass to determine its position.
      Modern periodic order is based on atomic number.
    </li>
  </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Arrangement of the Periodic Table', 15),
  (765, 765, '
  <h2>Groups in the Periodic Table</h2>

  <h3>1. Definition of a group</h3>

  <p>
    A <strong>group</strong> is a vertical column in the periodic
    table. Elements in the same main group generally have the same
    number of outer-shell electrons, except for special cases such
    as helium, and consequently often show similar chemical
    properties.
  </p>

  <p>
    The electrons in the outermost occupied energy level are called
    <strong>outer-shell electrons</strong> or, in this context,
    valence electrons. They are particularly important because they
    participate in many chemical reactions and chemical bonds.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Why does group membership matter?</h3>

  <p>
    Consider lithium, sodium and potassium. Their electron
    arrangements are:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>Lithium: 2,1</li>
    <li>Sodium: 2,8,1</li>
    <li>Potassium: 2,8,8,1</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    The total number of electrons differs, but each atom has one
    electron in its outermost occupied energy level.
  </p>

  <p>
    Each can lose that outer electron to form an ion with a charge
    of +1. Their similar outer-electron arrangements explain why
    they belong to the same chemical family and have related
    chemical behaviour.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Important groups and their names</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Group 1 — alkali metals:</strong> lithium, sodium
      and potassium are examples. They have one outer electron
      and commonly form +1 ions.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Group 2 — alkaline earth metals:</strong> magnesium
      and calcium are examples. They have two outer electrons and
      commonly form +2 ions.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Group 7 in the older school notation — halogens:</strong>
      fluorine and chlorine are examples. They have seven outer
      electrons and commonly form −1 ions.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Group 0 in the older school notation — noble gases:</strong>
      helium, neon and argon are examples. Their outer energy levels
      are full, so they are generally unreactive.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    <strong>Notation note:</strong> Many Kenyan school resources
    use Groups 1, 2, 7 and 0 for these familiar families. In modern
    IUPAC numbering, the halogens are Group 17 and the noble gases
    are Group 18. The chemistry is the same; the numbering system
    differs.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Finding a group from an electron arrangement</h3>

  <p>
    For the main-group elements covered in the first 20 elements,
    the number of outer electrons is a useful guide to group
    membership.
  </p>

  <p>
    For example, an atom with arrangement 2,8,6 has three occupied
    energy levels and six outer electrons. It is sulfur, a Period 3
    element in Group 6 under the older school notation.
  </p>

  <p>
    This simple rule should not be applied indiscriminately to every
    element in the periodic table. Transition elements have more
    complicated electron arrangements and require additional rules.
  </p>

  <h3>5. Groups and chemical reactivity</h3>

  <p>
    Group membership can help predict how atoms form ions and
    compounds. For example, sodium tends to lose one electron,
    while chlorine tends to gain one electron.
  </p>

  <p>
    Sodium forms Na⁺ and chlorine forms Cl⁻. Oppositely charged
    ions attract, producing the ionic compound sodium chloride,
    NaCl.
  </p>

  <p>
    The same group does not mean identical properties. Sodium and
    potassium both belong to Group 1, but potassium is more
    reactive. Related outer-electron arrangements produce
    similarities, while differences in atomic size and shielding
    affect the details.
  </p>

  <h3>Common mistakes to avoid</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Do not confuse a group with a period.</li>
    <li>
      Do not assume that elements in one group have the same total
      number of electrons.
    </li>
    <li>
      Do not confuse outer electrons with total electrons.
    </li>
    <li>
      Do not assume every group follows the simple outer-electron
      rule without exceptions.
    </li>
  </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Groups', 15),
  (766, 766, '
  <h2>Periods in the Periodic Table</h2>

  <h3>1. What is a period?</h3>

  <p>
    A <strong>period</strong> is a horizontal row in the periodic
    table. Elements in the same period have the same number of
    occupied main electron energy levels.
  </p>

  <p>
    An energy level is a region associated with electrons having
    particular allowed energies around the nucleus. At this level,
    electron arrangements such as 2,8,1 provide a simplified way
    of showing how electrons are distributed among occupied levels.
  </p>

  <h3>2. The connection between energy levels and periods</h3>

  <p>
    For the first 20 elements, count the numbers in an electron
    arrangement to find the number of occupied energy levels.
    The number of occupied levels gives the period number.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      Hydrogen: 1. There is one occupied energy level, so hydrogen
      is in Period 1.
    </li>
    <li>
      Carbon: 2,4. There are two occupied energy levels, so carbon
      is in Period 2.
    </li>
    <li>
      Sodium: 2,8,1. There are three occupied energy levels, so
      sodium is in Period 3.
    </li>
    <li>
      Calcium: 2,8,8,2. There are four occupied energy levels,
      so calcium is in Period 4.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>3. Why do energy levels matter?</h3>

  <p>
    Each occupied energy level contributes to the overall size
    and electron structure of an atom. Moving down a group
    generally introduces an additional occupied energy level.
  </p>

  <p>
    The outer electrons are then, on average, farther from the
    nucleus. This affects atomic size, shielding and the attraction
    between the nucleus and electrons.
  </p>

  <p>
    These effects help explain why properties change down a group.
    The period number therefore provides more than a position
    label: it gives information about the atom''s electron structure.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Periods are not the same as groups</h3>

  <p>
    Consider magnesium, with arrangement 2,8,2, and chlorine,
    with arrangement 2,8,7. Both have three occupied energy levels,
    so both belong to Period 3.
  </p>

  <p>
    However, magnesium has two outer electrons and chlorine has
    seven. Their chemical properties differ significantly.
  </p>

  <p>
    This shows why period number alone does not tell us everything
    about an element. We also need its group and outer-electron
    arrangement.
  </p>

  <h3>5. Determining the period from a diagram or arrangement</h3>

  <p>
    If an electron arrangement is provided, count the occupied
    levels rather than adding all the electrons.
  </p>

  <p>
    For example, 2,8,5 contains 15 electrons distributed across
    three occupied energy levels. The period is 3, not 15 and
    not 5.
  </p>

  <p>
    This arrangement belongs to phosphorus. The final number
    indicates five outer electrons, but the number of occupied
    levels determines the period.
  </p>

  <h3>Common mistakes to avoid</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>
      Do not use the total number of electrons as the period number.
    </li>
    <li>
      Do not use the number of outer electrons to determine the period.
    </li>
    <li>
      Do not confuse the number of occupied levels with the maximum
      capacity of an energy level.
    </li>
    <li>
      When using simplified shell arrangements, remember that the
      method is being applied to the elements for which that
      notation is appropriate.
    </li>
  </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Periods', 15),
  (767, 767, '
  <h2>Electronic Structure and Periodic Position</h2>

  <h3>1. What is electronic structure?</h3>

  <p>
    The <strong>electronic structure</strong> of an atom describes
    how its electrons are distributed among energy levels around
    the nucleus.
  </p>

  <p>
    The nucleus contains protons and neutrons. Electrons occupy
    allowed energy levels outside the nucleus. In the simplified
    shell notation used for the first 20 elements, we represent
    electron arrangements using numbers separated by commas.
  </p>

  <p>Examples:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>Hydrogen: 1</li>
    <li>Carbon: 2,4</li>
    <li>Sodium: 2,8,1</li>
    <li>Calcium: 2,8,8,2</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    The arrangement 2,8,1 means that the atom has two electrons
    in the first occupied energy level, eight in the second and
    one in the third.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Connecting atomic number to electrons</h3>

  <p>
    The <strong>atomic number</strong> is the number of protons
    in an atom''s nucleus. In a neutral atom, the number of
    electrons equals the number of protons.
  </p>

  <p>
    Therefore, the atomic number gives us the total number of
    electrons to distribute when writing the electronic structure
    of a neutral atom.
  </p>

  <p>
    For example, magnesium has atomic number 12. A neutral
    magnesium atom has 12 protons and 12 electrons. Its
    electronic structure is 2,8,2.
  </p>

  <h3>3. How to write an electron arrangement</h3>

  <p>
    For the first 20 elements, use the simplified shell capacities
    appropriate to this level: the first shell holds up to 2
    electrons, the second up to 8, and the third up to 8 for
    these elements.
  </p>

  <p>
    To write an arrangement:
  </p>

  <ol>
    <li>Find the atomic number.</li>
    <li>For a neutral atom, use that number as the total number of electrons.</li>
    <li>Place up to 2 electrons in the first shell.</li>
    <li>Place up to 8 in the second shell.</li>
    <li>For these first 20 elements, place up to 8 in the third shell before starting the fourth.</li>
    <li>Check that the numbers add up to the total number of electrons.</li>
  </ol>

  <p>
    This is a school-level shell model. Electron arrangements
    become more complicated for elements beyond the range
    covered by this simplified method.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Finding the period</h3>

  <p>
    For the elements covered here, the number of occupied
    main energy levels determines the period.
  </p>

  <p>
    An atom with arrangement 2,8,3 has three occupied levels.
    It is therefore in Period 3.
  </p>

  <h3>5. Finding the group</h3>

  <p>
    For main-group elements, the number of outer-shell electrons
    helps determine group membership.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>One outer electron: Group 1.</li>
    <li>Two outer electrons: Group 2.</li>
    <li>Six outer electrons: Group 6 in the older school notation.</li>
    <li>Seven outer electrons: Group 7 in the older school notation.</li>
    <li>A full outer shell: Group 0 in the older school notation, with helium as a special case.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    In modern IUPAC numbering, these familiar groups include
    Group 1, Group 2, Group 16 for the chalcogens, Group 17
    for the halogens and Group 18 for the noble gases.
  </p>

  <p>
    Do not apply the simple outer-electron rule to transition
    elements without considering their more complicated
    electronic structures.
  </p>

  <h3>6. Predicting chemical behaviour</h3>

  <p>
    Outer electrons are especially important in chemical reactions.
    Atoms may lose, gain or share electrons when forming ions
    or chemical bonds.
  </p>

  <p>
    Sodium has the arrangement 2,8,1. It commonly loses one
    electron to form Na⁺, giving it the electron arrangement
    2,8.
  </p>

  <p>
    Chlorine has the arrangement 2,8,7. It can gain one electron
    to form Cl⁻, giving it the arrangement 2,8,8.
  </p>

  <p>
    The resulting ions have stable outer-shell arrangements.
    Sodium chloride contains Na⁺ and Cl⁻ ions held together
    by electrostatic attraction.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Electronic structure from atomic number</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Write the electronic structure of aluminium, atomic number 13.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1: Find the total electrons.</strong></p>

  <p>
    Aluminium has atomic number 13. A neutral aluminium atom
    therefore contains 13 electrons.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2: Fill the first shell.</strong></p>

  <p>
    The first shell holds 2 electrons. Remaining electrons:
    13 − 2 = 11.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3: Fill the second shell.</strong></p>

  <p>
    The second shell holds 8 electrons. Remaining electrons:
    11 − 8 = 3.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 4: Place the remaining electrons.</strong></p>

  <p>
    The third shell receives the remaining 3 electrons.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 2,8,3.</p>

  <p>
    <strong>Check:</strong> 2 + 8 + 3 = 13. The arrangement
    accounts for every electron.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Finding group and period</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Determine the group and period of an element with arrangement 2,8,6.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1: Count the occupied levels.</strong></p>

  <p>
    There are three occupied levels, so the element is in Period 3.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2: Count the outer electrons.</strong></p>

  <p>
    The outermost level contains 6 electrons.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3: Determine the group.</strong></p>

  <p>
    Six outer electrons place this main-group element in Group 6
    using the older school notation.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Group 6, Period 3. The element is sulfur.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Inferring an ion''s electronic structure</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> A neutral sodium atom has 11 electrons. What is the electronic structure of Na⁺?</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1: Write the neutral arrangement.</strong></p>

  <p>
    Sodium has 11 electrons arranged as 2,8,1.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2: Interpret the charge.</strong></p>

  <p>
    The +1 charge means sodium has lost one electron.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3: Recalculate the number of electrons.</strong></p>

  <p>
    11 − 1 = 10 electrons remain.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 4: Distribute the electrons.</strong></p>

  <p>
    Ten electrons are arranged as 2,8.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Na⁺ has electronic structure 2,8.</p>

  <p>
    <strong>Why it matters:</strong> When forming a positive ion,
    an atom loses electrons; it does not lose protons from its
    nucleus.
  </p>

  <h3>Common mistakes</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Confusing atomic number with mass number.</li>
    <li>Adding the shell numbers incorrectly.</li>
    <li>Using total electrons instead of outer electrons to identify the main group.</li>
    <li>Forgetting that a positive ion has fewer electrons than its neutral atom.</li>
    <li>Assuming that an ion''s charge changes its atomic number.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Electronic Structure and Periodic Position', 15),
  (768, 768, '
  <h2>Group 1 Elements: The Alkali Metals</h2>

  <h3>1. What are Group 1 elements?</h3>

  <p>
    Group 1 elements are called the <strong>alkali metals</strong>.
    Important examples are lithium (Li), sodium (Na) and
    potassium (K).
  </p>

  <p>
    Hydrogen is positioned above Group 1 in the periodic table
    but is a non-metal and is not classified as an alkali metal.
    Its position should not lead us to treat it as chemically
    identical to lithium, sodium or potassium.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Electronic structure</h3>

  <p>
    Group 1 alkali metals have one electron in their outermost
    occupied energy level.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>Lithium: 2,1</li>
    <li>Sodium: 2,8,1</li>
    <li>Potassium: 2,8,8,1</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Their inner electron arrangements differ, but each has one
    outer electron. This explains their similar reactions.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Why do Group 1 metals form +1 ions?</h3>

  <p>
    A neutral sodium atom has 11 protons and 11 electrons.
    Its electron arrangement is 2,8,1.
  </p>

  <p>
    If sodium loses its single outer electron, it has 11 protons
    but only 10 electrons. The positive charge of its protons
    exceeds the negative charge of its electrons by one.
  </p>

  <p>
    The resulting ion is Na⁺, with arrangement 2,8.
  </p>

  <p>
    Losing one electron also produces a full outer shell in this
    example. Similar reasoning explains why lithium forms Li⁺
    and potassium forms K⁺.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Physical properties</h3>

  <p>
    Alkali metals are generally soft, silvery metals when freshly
    cut and are good conductors of heat and electricity. They
    have relatively low melting points compared with many other
    metals.
  </p>

  <p>
    They are stored carefully because they react with substances
    in their surroundings. Freshly cut surfaces quickly lose
    their shine as they react with air.
  </p>

  <h3>5. Reaction with water</h3>

  <p>
    Group 1 metals react with water to form a metal hydroxide
    and hydrogen gas.
  </p>

  <p><strong>General word equation:</strong></p>

  <p>alkali metal + water → metal hydroxide + hydrogen</p>

  <p>
    For sodium, the balanced symbol equation is:
  </p>

  <p>2Na + 2H₂O → 2NaOH + H₂</p>

  <p>
    The products are sodium hydroxide, which dissolves in water,
    and hydrogen gas.
  </p>

  <p>
    Hydrogen is flammable. Group 1 reactions can be dangerous,
    particularly for the more reactive metals. These reactions
    must only be demonstrated by trained professionals using
    appropriate safety controls; learners should not attempt
    them at home.
  </p>

  <h3>6. Why does reactivity increase down Group 1?</h3>

  <p>
    Potassium is generally more reactive than sodium, and sodium
    is generally more reactive than lithium.
  </p>

  <p>
    Moving down the group adds occupied energy levels. The
    outer electron is farther from the nucleus and is shielded
    by more inner electrons.
  </p>

  <p>
    Although the nucleus has more protons, the combined effects
    of increased distance and shielding reduce the attraction
    holding the outer electron. It can therefore be removed
    more easily.
  </p>

  <p>
    Since Group 1 reactions depend strongly on losing this
    electron, reactivity generally increases down the group.
  </p>

  <h3>7. Worked Example 1: Identifying a Group 1 element</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> An element has electron arrangement 2,8,1. Explain why it belongs to Group 1.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the outermost energy level.</p>

  <p>
    The last number in 2,8,1 is 1.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Interpret the number.</p>

  <p>
    The atom has one outer electron.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Connect the arrangement to group membership.</p>

  <p>
    One outer electron identifies Group 1 among the main-group
    elements covered here.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> It belongs to Group 1 because it has one outer electron.</p>

  <h3>8. Worked Example 2: Predicting the ion charge</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Predict the charge of the ion formed when potassium loses its outer electron.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Potassium has arrangement 2,8,8,1.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> It loses one electron.</p>

  <p>
    The number of protons is now one greater than the number
    of electrons.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Determine the net charge.</p>

  <p>
    Losing one negative charge gives a net charge of +1.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> K⁺.</p>

  <h3>9. Worked Example 3: Balancing the reaction with water</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Balance the equation for sodium reacting with water:</p>

  <p>Na + H₂O → NaOH + H₂</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Count atoms on both sides.</p>

  <p>
    Initially, the left side contains one Na, two H and one O.
    The right side contains one Na, three H and one O in total.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Balance sodium and the hydroxide product.</p>

  <p>
    Put 2 before Na and NaOH:
    2Na + H₂O → 2NaOH + H₂.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Balance oxygen and hydrogen.</p>

  <p>
    There are two oxygen atoms in 2NaOH, so put 2 before H₂O.
    The equation becomes:
  </p>

  <p>2Na + 2H₂O → 2NaOH + H₂</p>

  <p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Check the atoms.</p>

  <ul>
    <li>Na: 2 on each side.</li>
    <li>O: 2 on each side.</li>
    <li>H: 4 on each side.</li>
  </ul>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 2Na + 2H₂O → 2NaOH + H₂.</p>

  <p>
    <strong>Key principle:</strong> Balancing changes the
    coefficients in front of formulae, not the chemical formulae
    themselves.
  </p>

  <h3>Common mistakes</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Confusing the formation of a positive ion with gaining electrons.</li>
    <li>Forgetting that hydrogen is not an alkali metal.</li>
    <li>Changing subscripts to balance an equation.</li>
    <li>Explaining increased reactivity merely by saying that atoms get bigger without connecting this to electron loss.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Group 1 Elements', 15),
  (769, 769, '
  <h2>Group 2 Elements: The Alkaline Earth Metals</h2>

  <h3>1. Identity and position</h3>

  <p>
    Group 2 elements are called the <strong>alkaline earth metals</strong>.
    Examples include beryllium (Be), magnesium (Mg) and calcium (Ca).
  </p>

  <p>
    They are metals and occupy the second column of the main-group
    region of the periodic table.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Electronic structure</h3>

  <p>
    Group 2 elements have two electrons in their outermost
    occupied energy level.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>Beryllium: 2,2</li>
    <li>Magnesium: 2,8,2</li>
    <li>Calcium: 2,8,8,2</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Their inner shells differ, but the outermost shell contains
    two electrons in each case.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Formation of 2+ ions</h3>

  <p>
    Magnesium has 12 protons and 12 electrons. Its neutral
    electronic structure is 2,8,2.
  </p>

  <p>
    Magnesium can lose its two outer electrons. The resulting
    Mg²⁺ ion has 12 protons and 10 electrons, giving it a
    net charge of +2.
  </p>

  <p>
    Its electronic structure becomes 2,8.
  </p>

  <p>
    The general process can be represented as:
  </p>

  <p>Mg → Mg²⁺ + 2e⁻</p>

  <p>
    This equation shows that a magnesium atom loses two electrons.
    It does not mean that magnesium loses two protons.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Physical and chemical properties</h3>

  <p>
    Group 2 elements are metals and generally conduct heat and
    electricity. Their properties vary down the group.
  </p>

  <p>
    Magnesium burns in oxygen to produce magnesium oxide:
  </p>

  <p>2Mg + O₂ → 2MgO</p>

  <p>
    Magnesium oxide is a basic oxide. It reacts with acids to
    form a salt and water.
  </p>

  <p>
    Calcium reacts with water to produce calcium hydroxide
    and hydrogen:
  </p>

  <p>Ca + 2H₂O → Ca(OH)₂ + H₂</p>

  <p>
    This reaction is less vigorous than the reactions of
    potassium with water under comparable conditions.
    Beryllium does not react readily with water, while magnesium
    reacts only slowly with cold water.
  </p>

  <h3>5. Reactivity down Group 2</h3>

  <p>
    For the Group 2 metals, reactivity generally increases
    down the group.
  </p>

  <p>
    Moving down the group adds occupied energy levels. The
    outer electrons are farther from the nucleus and are more
    shielded by inner electrons.
  </p>

  <p>
    These effects reduce the attraction between the nucleus
    and the outer electrons. Losing two electrons becomes
    easier, so the metals generally become more reactive.
  </p>

  <p>
    The trend is related to the energy required to remove
    electrons, not simply to the number of protons in the nucleus.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Finding the group</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> An atom has arrangement 2,8,2. Explain why it belongs to Group 2.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Examine the final number.</p>

  <p>
    The final number is 2.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Interpret the arrangement.</p>

  <p>
    The atom has two outer electrons.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Determine the group.</p>

  <p>
    Two outer electrons indicate Group 2 for this main-group element.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Group 2; the element is magnesium.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Formation of magnesium ions</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Write the equation showing magnesium forming a magnesium ion.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Write the neutral atom.</p>

  <p>Mg</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Identify the electrons lost.</p>

  <p>
    Magnesium loses two electrons, represented by 2e⁻.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Write the product and balance charge.</p>

  <p>Mg → Mg²⁺ + 2e⁻</p>

  <p>
    The total charge on the right is +2 − 2 = 0, matching
    the neutral atom on the left.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Mg → Mg²⁺ + 2e⁻.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Predicting the period and group of calcium</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Calcium has atomic number 20. Determine its electronic structure, group and period.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Count its electrons.</p>

  <p>
    A neutral calcium atom has 20 electrons.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Distribute the electrons.</p>

  <p>
    Put 2 in the first shell, 8 in the second and 8 in the
    third. The remaining 2 occupy the fourth shell.
  </p>

  <p>
    Electronic structure = 2,8,8,2.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Determine the period.</p>

  <p>
    Four shells are occupied, so calcium is in Period 4.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Determine the group.</p>

  <p>
    The outermost shell contains two electrons, so calcium
    belongs to Group 2.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 2,8,8,2; Group 2, Period 4.</p>

  <h3>Common mistakes</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Writing Mg⁺ instead of Mg²⁺ when magnesium loses two electrons.</li>
    <li>Confusing a 2+ charge with two additional electrons.</li>
    <li>Assuming every Group 2 metal reacts with cold water equally vigorously.</li>
    <li>Forgetting that an ion''s proton number stays unchanged when electrons are lost.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Group 2 Elements', 15),
  (770, 770, '
  <h2>Group 7 Elements: The Halogens</h2>

  <h3>1. What are halogens?</h3>

  <p>
    The <strong>halogens</strong> are the elements in Group 17
    of the modern IUPAC periodic table. They are often called
    Group 7 elements in the older school notation.
  </p>

  <p>
    Important examples include fluorine (F), chlorine (Cl),
    bromine (Br) and iodine (I).
  </p>

  <h3>2. Electronic structure</h3>

  <p>
    Halogens have seven electrons in their outermost occupied
    energy level.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>Fluorine: 2,7</li>
    <li>Chlorine: 2,8,7</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    These arrangements show that each atom has seven outer
    electrons. An atom with seven outer electrons can gain one
    electron to reach eight in its outer shell.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Why do halogens form −1 ions?</h3>

  <p>
    A neutral chlorine atom has 17 protons and 17 electrons.
    Its arrangement is 2,8,7.
  </p>

  <p>
    If chlorine gains one electron, it has 17 protons and
    18 electrons. It now has one more electron than protons,
    so its net charge is −1.
  </p>

  <p>
    The resulting chloride ion is Cl⁻, with arrangement 2,8,8.
  </p>

  <p>Cl + e⁻ → Cl⁻</p>

  <p>
    This represents electron gain. Unlike Group 1 metals,
    halogens tend to gain rather than lose an electron when
    forming simple ions.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Physical properties</h3>

  <p>
    Halogens are non-metals and exist as diatomic molecules
    under ordinary conditions. A diatomic molecule contains
    two atoms joined together.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>Fluorine: F₂, a pale yellow gas.</li>
    <li>Chlorine: Cl₂, a greenish-yellow gas.</li>
    <li>Bromine: Br₂, a red-brown liquid.</li>
    <li>Iodine: I₂, a grey-black solid that gives purple vapour when heated.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Chlorine and other halogens can be toxic or corrosive.
    They must not be handled or inhaled outside an appropriately
    controlled laboratory.
  </p>

  <h3>5. Reactions with metals</h3>

  <p>
    Halogens react with metals to form metal halides. The metal
    generally loses electrons, while the halogen gains electrons.
  </p>

  <p>
    For example, sodium reacts with chlorine to form sodium chloride:
  </p>

  <p>2Na + Cl₂ → 2NaCl</p>

  <p>
    Each sodium atom forms Na⁺ by losing one electron. Each
    chlorine atom forms Cl⁻ by gaining one electron. Oppositely
    charged ions attract to form the ionic compound.
  </p>

  <h3>6. Displacement reactions</h3>

  <p>
    A more reactive halogen can displace a less reactive halogen
    from a solution containing its halide ions.
  </p>

  <p>
    For example, chlorine displaces bromine from potassium
    bromide solution:
  </p>

  <p>Cl₂ + 2KBr → 2KCl + Br₂</p>

  <p>
    Chlorine is more reactive than bromine, so chlorine gains
    electrons from bromide ions and produces chloride ions.
    Bromide ions lose electrons and form bromine.
  </p>

  <h3>7. Why does halogen reactivity decrease down the group?</h3>

  <p>
    Halogens react by gaining an electron. Moving down the group
    adds occupied energy levels, making the atom larger and
    increasing the shielding of the nucleus by inner electrons.
  </p>

  <p>
    An incoming electron experiences competing effects: the
    nucleus has more protons down the group, but the electron
    is entering a larger atom with greater shielding and a
    greater distance from the nucleus.
  </p>

  <p>
    The overall attraction for an incoming electron generally
    becomes less effective down the group. Therefore, halogen
    reactivity generally decreases down the group.
  </p>

  <p>
    Fluorine is more reactive than chlorine, which is more
    reactive than bromine, which is more reactive than iodine.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Formation of a chloride ion</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Explain how a chlorine atom forms Cl⁻.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Write the initial arrangement.</p>

  <p>
    Chlorine has 17 electrons arranged as 2,8,7.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Determine the electron change.</p>

  <p>
    Chlorine gains one electron.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Calculate the new number of electrons.</p>

  <p>
    17 + 1 = 18 electrons.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Write the new arrangement and charge.</p>

  <p>
    The arrangement becomes 2,8,8. One additional electron
    gives the ion a charge of −1.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Cl + e⁻ → Cl⁻.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Predicting reactivity</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Which is more reactive, chlorine or iodine? Explain why.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Locate the elements.</p>

  <p>
    Both are halogens. Chlorine is above iodine in the group.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Recall the trend.</p>

  <p>
    Halogen reactivity generally decreases down the group.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Explain the cause.</p>

  <p>
    Chlorine has fewer occupied energy levels and less shielding
    than iodine, so it attracts an incoming electron more
    effectively under comparable conditions.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Chlorine is more reactive than iodine.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Predicting a displacement reaction</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Predict what happens when chlorine is added to potassium bromide solution.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the halogens.</p>

  <p>
    Chlorine is the free halogen. Potassium bromide contains
    bromide ions.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Compare their reactivity.</p>

  <p>
    Chlorine is more reactive than bromine.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Predict the displacement.</p>

  <p>
    Chlorine displaces bromine from the bromide solution.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Write the balanced equation.</p>

  <p>Cl₂ + 2KBr → 2KCl + Br₂</p>

  <p>
    <strong>Answer:</strong> Potassium chloride and bromine form.
  </p>

  <h3>Common mistakes</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Confusing electron gain with electron loss.</li>
    <li>Writing a chloride ion as Cl⁺ rather than Cl⁻.</li>
    <li>Forgetting that elemental chlorine exists as Cl₂.</li>
    <li>Assuming a less reactive halogen can displace a more reactive one.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Group 7 Elements', 15),
  (771, 771, '
  <h2>Group 0 Elements: The Noble Gases</h2>

  <h3>1. What are noble gases?</h3>

  <p>
    The <strong>noble gases</strong> occupy Group 18 in modern
    IUPAC numbering. They are often called Group 0 elements
    in older school notation.
  </p>

  <p>
    Important examples include helium (He), neon (Ne) and
    argon (Ar).
  </p>

  <h3>2. Electronic structure</h3>

  <p>
    Noble gases have full outer electron shells. For helium,
    the first shell is full with two electrons. For neon and
    argon, the outer shell contains eight electrons.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>Helium: 2</li>
    <li>Neon: 2,8</li>
    <li>Argon: 2,8,8</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    A full outer shell makes it energetically unfavourable for
    these atoms to gain, lose or share electrons in many ordinary
    chemical reactions.
  </p>

  <p>
    This is why noble gases are generally described as
    <strong>unreactive</strong> or <strong>inert</strong>.
    Their reactivity is very low, although it is not scientifically
    correct to claim that no noble gas compound can ever exist.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Why helium is special</h3>

  <p>
    The first energy level can hold a maximum of two electrons.
    Helium has exactly two electrons, so its only occupied
    energy level is full.
  </p>

  <p>
    Helium does not need eight outer electrons. The first shell
    is complete with two. This explains why helium is a noble
    gas even though it has fewer electrons than neon or argon.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Physical properties</h3>

  <p>
    Noble gases are colourless, monatomic gases at room
    temperature. <strong>Monatomic</strong> means that each
    gas particle is a single atom rather than a molecule
    containing two or more atoms.
  </p>

  <p>
    Their atoms have weak attractions between them. As a result,
    noble gases have low boiling points. Their boiling points
    generally increase down the group as atoms become larger
    and the attractions between their electron clouds increase.
  </p>

  <h3>5. Uses of noble gases</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Helium:</strong> used in balloons and in some
      specialised cooling applications.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Neon:</strong> used in illuminated signs.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Argon:</strong> used in electric bulbs and as
      a protective atmosphere in some welding processes.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    These applications make use of the gases'' low reactivity
    or other physical properties.
  </p>

  <h3>6. Why noble gases rarely form compounds</h3>

  <p>
    Chemical bonding often involves changes in electron
    distribution. An atom with a full outer shell generally
    has little tendency to gain or lose electrons.
  </p>

  <p>
    Noble gases also have relatively high ionisation energies,
    meaning that considerable energy is required to remove
    an electron. Their full outer shells therefore help explain
    their low chemical reactivity.
  </p>

  <p>
    Heavier noble gases, especially xenon, can form compounds
    under suitable conditions. This is why "very unreactive"
    is more accurate than "absolutely incapable of reacting."
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Explaining helium''s group</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Explain why helium is a noble gas despite having only two electrons.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify its electron arrangement.</p>

  <p>
    Helium has two electrons, arranged as 2.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Consider the first shell''s capacity.</p>

  <p>
    The first shell holds a maximum of two electrons.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Determine whether the shell is full.</p>

  <p>
    Both available positions are occupied.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Helium is a noble gas because its only shell is full.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Predicting reactivity from electron structure</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Explain why neon is generally unreactive.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Write its electron arrangement.</p>

  <p>
    Neon has arrangement 2,8.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Examine its outer shell.</p>

  <p>
    The outer shell contains eight electrons and is full.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Connect this to electron transfer.</p>

  <p>
    Neon has little tendency to gain or lose electrons in
    ordinary reactions.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Neon is generally unreactive because its outer shell is full.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Identifying a noble gas</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> An atom has electronic structure 2,8,8. Identify the element and explain its low reactivity.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Count the electrons.</p>

  <p>
    2 + 8 + 8 = 18 electrons.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Identify the atomic number.</p>

  <p>
    For a neutral atom, 18 electrons correspond to 18 protons.
    Atomic number 18 identifies argon.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Examine the outer shell.</p>

  <p>
    The outermost shell contains eight electrons and is full.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Argon (Ar), a noble gas with low chemical reactivity.</p>

  <h3>Common mistakes</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Assuming every full shell must contain eight electrons.</li>
    <li>Forgetting that helium''s first shell is full with two electrons.</li>
    <li>Claiming noble gases can never form compounds.</li>
    <li>Confusing monatomic atoms with diatomic molecules.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Group 0 Elements', 15),
  (772, 772, '<h2>Metallic Bonding</h2>

  <h3>1. What is metallic bonding?</h3>

  <p>Metallic bonding is the strong electrostatic attraction between a lattice of positive metal ions and delocalised electrons.</p>

  <p>To understand this definition, identify the three important ideas:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Positive metal ions:</strong> metal particles that have lost some of their outer electrons.</li>
    <li><strong>Delocalised electrons:</strong> outer electrons that are not confined to one particular atom and can move throughout the metal structure.</li>
    <li><strong>Electrostatic attraction:</strong> the force of attraction between particles with opposite electrical charges.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Metallic bonding is not simply an attraction between neutral metal atoms. It is the attraction between positive ions and delocalised electrons.</p>

  <h3>2. How metallic bonding forms</h3>

  <p>Consider a metal such as sodium. An isolated sodium atom has the electron arrangement 2,8,1. Its outer electron can become delocalised when sodium forms a metallic structure.</p>

  <ol>
    <li>Metal atoms contribute some of their outer electrons to a shared pool of delocalised electrons.</li>
    <li>The atoms become positive metal ions because they have lost electrons.</li>
    <li>The positive ions arrange themselves in a regular, closely packed structure.</li>
    <li>The delocalised electrons move throughout the structure.</li>
    <li>The electrostatic attraction between the positive ions and negative electrons holds the structure together.</li>
  </ol>

  <p><strong>Important:</strong> the positive ions do not normally move freely through the solid metal. They occupy positions in the lattice, while the delocalised electrons can move through it.</p>

  <h3>3. Understanding the structure</h3>

  <pre>
Positive metal ions in a lattice

   M⁺       M⁺       M⁺
      e⁻  e⁻  e⁻
   M⁺       M⁺       M⁺
    e⁻  e⁻  e⁻  e⁻
   M⁺       M⁺       M⁺

M⁺ = positive metal ion
e⁻ = delocalised electron
  </pre>

  <p>This is a simplified model. The actual structure is three-dimensional, and the electrons are not confined to the spaces shown in the diagram.</p>

  <h3>4. Why the structure remains together</h3>

  <p>Each positive ion attracts the surrounding negative delocalised electrons. The electrons are spread throughout the structure, so their attraction helps hold the entire metallic lattice together.</p>

  <p>There is no requirement for each positive ion to be paired with one particular electron. The metallic bond is a collective attraction throughout the structure.</p>

  <h3>5. Worked examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: Define metallic bonding.</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the positively charged particles: positive metal ions.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Identify the negatively charged particles: delocalised electrons.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> State the force between them: strong electrostatic attraction.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Metallic bonding is the strong electrostatic attraction between positive metal ions and delocalised electrons.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: Explain how a sodium atom contributes to metallic bonding.</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Sodium has electron arrangement 2,8,1.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> It contributes its one outer electron to the delocalised electron system.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> The sodium particle becomes Na⁺ because it has lost one electron.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Na⁺ is attracted to the delocalised electrons.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Each sodium atom contributes one outer electron to the delocalised electron system and forms a positive sodium ion. The attraction between the ions and electrons helps hold the metal together.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: Correct the statement: “Metallic bonding is the attraction between negative metal ions and positive electrons.”</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Check the charge of the metal ions. They are positive, not negative.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Check the charge of electrons. Electrons are negative, not positive.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> State the attraction using the correct charges.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Metallic bonding is the strong electrostatic attraction between positive metal ions and negatively charged delocalised electrons.</p>

  <h3>6. Common misconception</h3>

  <p><strong>Incorrect:</strong> Metallic bonding occurs because metal atoms share electrons in the same way as non-metal atoms in a covalent molecule.</p>

  <p><strong>Correct:</strong> In the metallic model, outer electrons become delocalised throughout the metal. The positive ions and the delocalised electrons are held together by electrostatic attraction.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Metallic Bonding', 15),
  (773, 773, '<h2>Delocalised Electrons in Metals</h2>

  <h3>1. Meaning of delocalised electrons</h3>

  <p>Delocalised electrons are electrons that are not confined to one particular atom or bond. In a metal, they can move throughout the metallic structure.</p>

  <p>In the metallic bonding model, these electrons usually originate from the outer energy levels of metal atoms.</p>

  <h3>2. How delocalisation occurs</h3>

  <p>When many metal atoms form a metallic structure, their outer electrons can become part of a shared electron system.</p>

  <ol>
    <li>Metal atoms contribute outer electrons.</li>
    <li>The atoms become positive metal ions.</li>
    <li>The ions form an ordered lattice in the solid metal.</li>
    <li>The delocalised electrons extend throughout the structure and can move through it.</li>
  </ol>

  <p>Delocalisation does not mean that electrons have no attraction to the metal. The electrons remain attracted to the positive ions throughout the structure.</p>

  <h3>3. Ions and electrons behave differently</h3>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Positive metal ions:</strong> occupy positions in the solid lattice and vibrate about those positions.</li>
    <li><strong>Delocalised electrons:</strong> are mobile and can move through the metal.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>This difference explains why electrons, rather than the fixed metal ions, carry electrical charge through an ordinary solid metal.</p>

  <h3>4. Electrical conductivity</h3>

  <p>Electrical current requires charged particles that can move. When a potential difference is applied across a metal, its delocalised electrons can drift through the structure and carry charge.</p>

  <p>The electrons already exist in the metal; the power supply does not need to create them. It establishes an electric field that drives their net movement.</p>

  <h3>5. Thermal conductivity</h3>

  <p>Metals also transfer thermal energy efficiently. Mobile electrons can carry energy through the structure and transfer it through interactions with other electrons and the lattice.</p>

  <p>Vibrations of the lattice also transfer thermal energy. Therefore, it is more accurate to say that mobile electrons make an important contribution to heat conduction than to say they are the only mechanism.</p>

  <h3>6. Worked examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: Explain why delocalised electrons are different from electrons confined to an individual atom.</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> An electron confined to an individual atom belongs to that atom''s electron arrangement.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> A delocalised electron is not restricted to one particular atom.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> In a metal, delocalised electrons can move throughout the structure.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Delocalised electrons are not confined to individual metal atoms and can move throughout the metallic structure.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: Explain how delocalised electrons enable a metal to conduct electricity.</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Electrical current is the flow of electric charge.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Delocalised electrons carry negative charge.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> When a potential difference is applied, the electrons acquire a net drift through the metal.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Their movement carries charge from one region to another.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Delocalised electrons are mobile charge carriers. When a potential difference is applied, their net movement through the metal produces an electric current.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: Explain why the positive ions in a solid metal do not carry the current in the same way as the electrons.</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> The metal ions are positively charged.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> In the solid lattice, they occupy fixed average positions and mainly vibrate about them.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> They do not flow freely through the solid metal.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 4:</strong> The delocalised electrons are mobile and carry the current.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The positive ions are held at lattice positions, whereas the delocalised electrons can move through the metal and carry electric charge.</p>

  <h3>7. Common misconception</h3>

  <p><strong>Incorrect:</strong> The positive ions flow through a solid metal when it conducts electricity.</p>

  <p><strong>Correct:</strong> In an ordinary solid metal, the positive ions remain around their lattice positions. The delocalised electrons provide the mobile charge carriers.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Delocalised Electrons in Metals', 15),
  (774, 774, '<h2>Metallic Bonding and Metal Properties</h2>

  <h3>1. How structure determines properties</h3>

  <p>The properties of a metal are related to its structure: positive metal ions occupy lattice positions, while delocalised electrons move throughout the structure.</p>

  <p>Different properties arise from different features of this structure. Electrical conductivity depends on mobile electrons; malleability depends on how the lattice can change shape; and melting requires the metallic structure to be disrupted.</p>

  <h3>2. Electrical conductivity</h3>

  <p>Metals conduct electricity because they contain mobile delocalised electrons.</p>

  <ol>
    <li>A metal contains delocalised electrons that carry negative charge.</li>
    <li>A potential difference establishes an electric field through the metal.</li>
    <li>The electrons acquire a net drift through the structure.</li>
    <li>The movement of charge produces an electric current.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>3. Thermal conductivity</h3>

  <p>Mobile electrons transfer thermal energy through the metal. Lattice vibrations also contribute to heat transfer.</p>

  <p>In many metals, mobile electrons are an important reason that heat spreads quickly through the material.</p>

  <h3>4. Malleability</h3>

  <p><strong>Malleability</strong> is the ability of a material to be hammered or pressed into sheets without breaking.</p>

  <p>When a force is applied to a metal, layers of ions can shift relative to one another. The delocalised electrons continue to attract the positive ions, allowing metallic bonding to persist as the structure changes shape.</p>

  <p>This does not mean that metals can be deformed without limit. A sufficiently large force can damage or fracture a metal.</p>

  <h3>5. Ductility</h3>

  <p><strong>Ductility</strong> is the ability of a material to be drawn into wires.</p>

  <p>Many metals are ductile because their metallic structure can undergo substantial deformation while the metallic bonding remains effective. The layers and lattice positions can rearrange instead of the material immediately separating into individual particles.</p>

  <h3>6. Melting and boiling points</h3>

  <p>Metallic bonding involves electrostatic attraction between positive metal ions and delocalised electrons. Energy must be supplied to disrupt the metallic structure during melting.</p>

  <p>Many metals have high melting points, but the values vary widely. Metallic bond strength and structure differ between metals, so it is not correct to claim that every metal has a high melting point.</p>

  <p>Boiling a metal requires much more extensive separation of its particles than melting it. Many metals therefore also have high boiling points.</p>

  <h3>7. Worked examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: Explain why copper conducts electricity.</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Copper has a metallic structure containing delocalised electrons.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> These electrons carry negative charge and are mobile.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> A potential difference causes a net drift of electrons.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Copper conducts electricity because its delocalised electrons can move through the metal and carry electric charge.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: Explain why a metal can be hammered into a sheet.</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Hammering applies a force to the metallic lattice.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Layers of metal ions can shift relative to one another.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> The delocalised electrons continue to attract the positive ions as the structure changes shape.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 4:</strong> The metal can flatten instead of immediately breaking apart.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Metals are malleable because their layers can shift while metallic bonding continues to hold the structure together.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: Explain why many metals have high melting points.</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Positive metal ions and delocalised electrons are held together by electrostatic attraction.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Melting requires energy to disrupt the ordered metallic structure.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Where the metallic attraction is strong, substantial energy is required.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Many metals have high melting points because considerable energy is needed to disrupt their metallic structures. The exact melting point depends on the metal.</p>

  <h3>8. Common misconception</h3>

  <p><strong>Incorrect:</strong> Metals are malleable because metallic bonds are weak.</p>

  <p><strong>Correct:</strong> Metallic bonding can be strong. Malleability arises because the metallic structure can change shape as layers shift, while the attraction between the positive ions and delocalised electrons continues.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Metallic Bonding and Metal Properties', 15),
  (775, 775, '
  <h2>Why Atoms Bond</h2>

  <h3>1. What is chemical bonding?</h3>
  <p>
    A <b>chemical bond</b> is an attractive force that holds atoms or ions
    together in a substance. Chemical bonds form when interactions between
    particles produce a more stable arrangement.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Why do atoms form bonds?</h3>
  <p>
    Atoms can combine because the bonded arrangement may have lower energy
    and greater stability than the separate particles. For many elements
    in the main groups of the periodic table, this stability is associated
    with a filled outer electron shell.
  </p>

  <p>
    A filled outer shell is a useful model for understanding many familiar
    compounds. However, not every stable substance follows a simple
    eight-electron rule. The important idea is that bonding depends on
    the attractions and energy changes involved.
  </p>

  <h3>3. The role of outer-shell electrons</h3>
  <p>
    Electrons in the outermost occupied shell are called
    <b>valence electrons</b>. They are especially important in chemical
    bonding because they participate in electron transfer or sharing.
  </p>

  <p>
    For example, sodium has the electron arrangement 2,8,1. It can lose
    one outer electron to form Na⁺, leaving the arrangement 2,8.
    Chlorine has the arrangement 2,8,7. It can gain one electron to form
    Cl⁻, giving the arrangement 2,8,8. The resulting oppositely charged
    ions attract one another.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Bonding does not mean that atoms simply want electrons</h3>
  <p>
    Atoms do not make conscious choices. A bond forms when the
    interactions between particles make the bonded arrangement more
    energetically favourable. The octet model helps predict many
    introductory examples, but it is a model rather than a universal
    rule for every substance.
  </p>

  <h3>5. Main types of chemical bonding</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Ionic bonding:</b> attraction between oppositely charged ions.</li>
    <li><b>Covalent bonding:</b> attraction involving shared pairs of electrons and the nuclei of the bonded atoms.</li>
    <li><b>Metallic bonding:</b> attraction between positive metal ions and delocalised electrons.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Why can sodium form a positive ion?</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> Sodium has the electron arrangement 2,8,1. Explain why it can form Na⁺.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify its outer-shell electrons. Sodium has one electron in its outer shell.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Consider what happens if it loses that electron. Its arrangement becomes 2,8.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Determine the resulting charge. It has 11 protons but only 10 electrons, so its charge is +1.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Sodium can lose one outer electron to form Na⁺. The resulting ion has the electron arrangement 2,8.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Why can chlorine form a negative ion?</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> Chlorine has the electron arrangement 2,8,7. Explain how it can form Cl⁻.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Count the outer-shell electrons. Chlorine has seven.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Determine how many electrons are needed to reach eight in the outer shell. It needs one.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Calculate the charge after gaining one electron. Chlorine has 17 protons and now 18 electrons, giving a charge of −1.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Chlorine gains one electron to form Cl⁻, with electron arrangement 2,8,8.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Explain the formation of sodium chloride</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> Explain how sodium and chlorine form sodium chloride using their electron arrangements.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Sodium has arrangement 2,8,1 and can lose one electron.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Chlorine has arrangement 2,8,7 and can gain one electron.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> The electron transfer produces Na⁺ and Cl⁻.</p>
  <p><b>Step 4:</b> The oppositely charged ions attract by electrostatic force.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Sodium chloride forms when sodium transfers one electron to chlorine. The resulting Na⁺ and Cl⁻ ions attract and form an ionic compound.</p>

  <h3>Key idea</h3>
  <p>
    Chemical bonding produces stable arrangements of particles through
    attractive forces and favourable energy changes. Outer-shell
    electrons help explain many bonding patterns.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Why Atoms Bond', 15),
  (776, 776, '
  <h2>Valency</h2>

  <h3>1. Meaning of valency</h3>
  <p>
    <b>Valency</b> is the combining capacity of an atom or group of atoms.
    In simple introductory examples, it is often determined by the number
    of electrons an atom loses, gains, or shares when forming bonds.
  </p>

  <p>
    Valency is not always identical to ionic charge or oxidation state.
    In this screen, use the simple combining-capacity model commonly
    applied to introductory chemical formulae.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Finding valency from electron arrangements</h3>
  <p>
    For many main-group elements, the outer-shell electron count helps
    predict common valencies.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>One outer electron: commonly valency 1, as in sodium.</li>
    <li>Two outer electrons: commonly valency 2, as in magnesium.</li>
    <li>Three outer electrons: commonly valency 3, as in aluminium.</li>
    <li>Five outer electrons: commonly valency 3 in simple covalent examples, as in nitrogen.</li>
    <li>Six outer electrons: commonly valency 2 in simple covalent examples, as in oxygen.</li>
    <li>Seven outer electrons: commonly valency 1 in simple examples, as in chlorine.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    For elements with five, six, or seven outer electrons, the simple
    model often estimates how many electrons are needed to complete an
    outer shell. Some elements show variable valency, so these patterns
    must not be treated as universal.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Valency and the formation of ions</h3>
  <p>
    In common ionic examples, sodium forms Na⁺ and has valency 1;
    magnesium forms Mg²⁺ and has valency 2; aluminium forms Al³⁺ and
    has valency 3. The valency gives the magnitude of the combining
    capacity in these examples, while the ion''s charge includes a sign.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Using valency to write formulae</h3>
  <p>
    A compound''s formula must represent an electrically neutral
    combination of ions. The total positive charge must equal the
    total negative charge.
  </p>

  <p>
    For magnesium chloride, magnesium forms Mg²⁺ and chlorine forms Cl⁻.
    One Mg²⁺ ion requires two Cl⁻ ions to balance the charge, so the
    formula is MgCl₂.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Find the common valency of magnesium</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> Magnesium has electron arrangement 2,8,2. Determine its common valency in simple ionic compounds.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Count the outer-shell electrons. Magnesium has two.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Consider the common ion it forms. Magnesium can lose these two electrons to form Mg²⁺.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Determine the combining capacity from the number of electrons lost.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Magnesium commonly has valency 2 in simple ionic compounds.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Estimate the valency of oxygen in water</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> Oxygen has six outer-shell electrons. In a simple covalent model, how many bonds does it commonly form?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Oxygen has six electrons in its outer shell.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> It needs two more electrons to reach eight in the simple shell model.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Oxygen can share one pair of electrons with each of two hydrogen atoms.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Oxygen commonly has valency 2 in water, H₂O.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Use valency to write magnesium chloride''s formula</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> Write the formula of a compound formed from Mg²⁺ and Cl⁻ ions.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Magnesium has charge +2.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Each chloride ion has charge −1.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Two chloride ions give a total charge of 2 × (−1) = −2.</p>
  <p><b>Step 4:</b> The charges balance: +2 + (−2) = 0.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> MgCl₂.</p>

  <h3>Key idea</h3>
  <p>
    Valency describes combining capacity. Use outer-shell electrons to
    predict common valencies, then check that any ionic formula has
    balanced total charge.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Valency', 15),
  (777, 777, '
  <h2>Ionic Bonding</h2>

  <h3>1. What is an ion?</h3>
  <p>
    An <b>ion</b> is an atom or group of atoms with an overall electric
    charge because the number of electrons differs from the number
    of protons.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>A positive ion, or cation, has fewer electrons than protons.</li>
    <li>A negative ion, or anion, has more electrons than protons.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>2. Definition of ionic bonding</h3>
  <p>
    <b>Ionic bonding</b> is the strong electrostatic attraction between
    oppositely charged ions. Electron transfer often produces the ions,
    but the bond itself is the attraction between the charges.
  </p>

  <h3>3. How ionic bonding forms</h3>
  <p>
    Consider sodium chloride. A sodium atom loses one electron and
    becomes Na⁺. A chlorine atom gains that electron and becomes Cl⁻.
    The opposite charges attract.
  </p>

  <p>
    The electron-transfer equations are:
  </p>
  <p><b>Sodium:</b> Na → Na⁺ + e⁻</p>
  <p><b>Chlorine:</b> Cl + e⁻ → Cl⁻</p>

  <p>
    One electron is lost by sodium and gained by chlorine. Electrons
    are transferred; they are not created or destroyed in the process.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Ionic compounds form giant lattices</h3>
  <p>
    Solid sodium chloride is not made of separate NaCl molecules.
    Its ions are arranged in a repeating three-dimensional ionic lattice.
    Each ion is surrounded by ions of the opposite charge. The
    electrostatic attractions extend throughout the structure.
  </p>

  <h3>5. Formulae and charge balance</h3>
  <p>
    An ionic compound must have an overall charge of zero. For example,
    Mg²⁺ combines with two Cl⁻ ions to form MgCl₂. The ratio is one
    magnesium ion to two chloride ions because +2 + 2(−1) = 0.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Explain the ionic bond in sodium chloride</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> Describe how sodium chloride forms from sodium and chlorine atoms.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Sodium loses one electron: Na → Na⁺ + e⁻.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Chlorine gains that electron: Cl + e⁻ → Cl⁻.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> The ions have opposite charges: Na⁺ is positive and Cl⁻ is negative.</p>
  <p><b>Step 4:</b> Electrostatic attraction holds the ions together in the ionic lattice.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Sodium transfers one electron to chlorine, forming Na⁺ and Cl⁻. Their electrostatic attraction produces ionic bonding.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Determine the formula of calcium chloride</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> Calcium forms Ca²⁺ and chlorine forms Cl⁻. Determine the formula of calcium chloride.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> One calcium ion contributes +2.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Each chloride ion contributes −1.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Two chloride ions contribute a total of −2.</p>
  <p><b>Step 4:</b> The charges balance: +2 + 2(−1) = 0.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> CaCl₂.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Explain the formula MgO</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> Magnesium forms Mg²⁺ and oxygen forms O²⁻. Explain why the formula is MgO.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Magnesium contributes a charge of +2.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Oxide contributes a charge of −2.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> One of each ion gives +2 + (−2) = 0.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> MgO, because the ions combine in a 1:1 ratio to produce a neutral compound.</p>

  <h3>Key idea</h3>
  <p>
    Electron transfer forms ions in many ionic compounds. Ionic bonding
    is the electrostatic attraction between oppositely charged ions,
    which are arranged in a giant lattice in the solid.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Ionic Bonding', 15),
  (778, 778, '
  <h2>Electron Transfer</h2>

  <h3>1. Meaning of electron transfer</h3>
  <p>
    <b>Electron transfer</b> is the movement of one or more electrons
    from one particle to another. In many introductory examples of
    ionic bonding, a metal atom loses electrons and a non-metal atom
    gains them.
  </p>

  <p>
    Losing electrons forms a positive ion. Gaining electrons forms a
    negative ion. The number of protons in the nucleus does not change
    during ordinary ion formation.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Electron loss</h3>
  <p>
    A neutral sodium atom contains 11 protons and 11 electrons.
    When it loses one electron, it still has 11 protons but only
    10 electrons. Its net charge becomes +1.
  </p>
  <p><b>Equation:</b> Na → Na⁺ + e⁻</p>

  <h3>3. Electron gain</h3>
  <p>
    A neutral chlorine atom contains 17 protons and 17 electrons.
    When it gains one electron, it has 17 protons and 18 electrons.
    Its net charge becomes −1.
  </p>
  <p><b>Equation:</b> Cl + e⁻ → Cl⁻</p>

  <h3>4. Electrons are conserved</h3>
  <p>
    In a transfer between sodium and chlorine, the electron lost by
    sodium is the electron gained by chlorine. The transfer must be
    accounted for on both sides. It does not mean that the electron
    disappears or that a new electron is created.
  </p>

  <h3>5. Predicting the number of electrons transferred</h3>
  <p>
    The common ion formed by an atom helps identify the number of
    electrons transferred. Magnesium commonly loses two electrons
    to form Mg²⁺. Oxygen commonly gains two electrons to form O²⁻.
    Their charges balance in magnesium oxide, MgO.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Write the electron-loss equation for sodium</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> Write an equation showing a sodium atom forming a sodium ion.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Sodium loses one electron.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Write Na on the left.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Write Na⁺ and the released electron e⁻ on the right.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Na → Na⁺ + e⁻.</p>
  <p><b>Check:</b> The total charge on the right is +1 − 1 = 0, matching the neutral atom on the left.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Write the electron-gain equation for oxygen</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> Write the equation for a neutral oxygen atom gaining two electrons to form an oxide ion.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Oxygen gains two electrons.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Place O and two electrons on the left.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Place O²⁻ on the right.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> O + 2e⁻ → O²⁻.</p>
  <p><b>Check:</b> The left side has total charge −2 and the right side has charge −2.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Balance electron transfer between magnesium and chlorine</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> Magnesium forms Mg²⁺ and chlorine forms Cl⁻. How many chlorine atoms are needed to accept the electrons lost by one magnesium atom?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Magnesium loses two electrons: Mg → Mg²⁺ + 2e⁻.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Each chlorine atom gains one electron: Cl + e⁻ → Cl⁻.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Two chlorine atoms are needed to accept the two electrons.</p>
  <p><b>Step 4:</b> The overall transfer is Mg + 2Cl → Mg²⁺ + 2Cl⁻.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Two chlorine atoms accept the two electrons lost by one magnesium atom. The resulting ion ratio is 1:2, giving MgCl₂.</p>

  <h3>Key idea</h3>
  <p>
    Track electrons explicitly. Electron loss produces positive ions,
    electron gain produces negative ions, and the number of electrons
    lost must equal the number gained in the transfer.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Electron Transfer', 15),
  (779, 779, '
  <h2>Formation of Positive Ions</h2>

  <h3>1. What is a positive ion?</h3>
  <p>
    A <b>positive ion</b>, also called a <b>cation</b>, is an atom or
    group of atoms with an overall positive charge. A positive ion forms
    when the particle loses one or more electrons.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Why does electron loss produce a positive charge?</h3>
  <p>
    Protons have a charge of +1 and electrons have a charge of −1.
    In a neutral atom, the number of protons equals the number of
    electrons. If electrons are removed, there are more protons than
    electrons, so the remaining particle is positively charged.
  </p>

  <p>
    The charge of an ion can be found using:
  </p>
  <p><b>Ion charge = number of protons − number of electrons</b></p>

  <h3>3. Formation of sodium ions</h3>
  <p>
    Sodium has 11 protons and 11 electrons. It loses one electron
    to form Na⁺. The ion has 11 protons and 10 electrons.
  </p>
  <p><b>Equation:</b> Na → Na⁺ + e⁻</p>

  <h3>4. Formation of magnesium ions</h3>
  <p>
    Magnesium has 12 protons and 12 electrons. It commonly loses two
    electrons to form Mg²⁺. The ion has 12 protons and 10 electrons.
  </p>
  <p><b>Equation:</b> Mg → Mg²⁺ + 2e⁻</p>

  <h3>5. Formation of aluminium ions</h3>
  <p>
    Aluminium has 13 protons and 13 electrons. It commonly loses three
    electrons to form Al³⁺. The ion has 13 protons and 10 electrons.
  </p>
  <p><b>Equation:</b> Al → Al³⁺ + 3e⁻</p>

  <h3>6. Do positive ions lose protons?</h3>
  <p>
    No. Ordinary chemical ion formation involves electrons, not changes
    to the nucleus. Sodium remains sodium after losing an electron
    because it still has 11 protons. If the number of protons changed,
    the atom would become a different element.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Find the charge of a sodium ion</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> An ion contains 11 protons and 10 electrons. Calculate its charge.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Each proton contributes +1, so the total proton charge is +11.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Each electron contributes −1, so the total electron charge is −10.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Net charge = 11 − 10 = +1.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> The ion has charge +1, written Na⁺ if it is a sodium ion.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Determine the electrons in Mg²⁺</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> Magnesium has atomic number 12. How many electrons are in Mg²⁺?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Atomic number 12 means magnesium has 12 protons.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> A neutral magnesium atom has 12 electrons.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> The 2+ charge means it has lost two electrons.</p>
  <p><b>Step 4:</b> Electrons remaining = 12 − 2 = 10.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Mg²⁺ has 10 electrons and 12 protons.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Write the equation for aluminium ion formation</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> Write the equation showing aluminium forming Al³⁺.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Aluminium loses three electrons.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Put the neutral aluminium atom on the left.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Put Al³⁺ and three electrons on the right.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Al → Al³⁺ + 3e⁻.</p>
  <p><b>Check:</b> The right-side charge is +3 − 3 = 0, equal to the charge of the neutral atom on the left.</p>

  <h3>Key idea</h3>
  <p>
    Positive ions form when electrons are lost. The proton number remains
    unchanged, and the positive charge equals the number of electrons
    lost by a neutral atom.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Formation of Positive Ions', 15),
  (780, 780, '
  <h2>Formation of Negative Ions</h2>

  <h3>1. What is a negative ion?</h3>
  <p>
    A <b>negative ion</b>, also called an <b>anion</b>, is an atom or
    group of atoms with an overall negative electrical charge. A negative
    ion forms when a particle gains one or more electrons.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Why does gaining electrons produce a negative charge?</h3>
  <p>
    Each proton has a charge of +1, while each electron has a charge
    of −1. A neutral atom has equal numbers of protons and electrons.
    When it gains electrons, it has more electrons than protons and
    therefore has a net negative charge.
  </p>

  <p><b>Ion charge = number of protons − number of electrons</b></p>

  <h3>3. Formation of a chloride ion</h3>
  <p>
    A chlorine atom has 17 protons and 17 electrons. It can gain one
    electron to form Cl⁻. The resulting ion has 17 protons and
    18 electrons.
  </p>
  <p><b>Equation:</b> Cl + e⁻ → Cl⁻</p>

  <h3>4. Formation of an oxide ion</h3>
  <p>
    An oxygen atom has 8 protons and 8 electrons. It commonly gains
    two electrons to form O²⁻. The resulting ion has 8 protons and
    10 electrons.
  </p>
  <p><b>Equation:</b> O + 2e⁻ → O²⁻</p>

  <h3>5. Formation of a sulfide ion</h3>
  <p>
    A sulfur atom has 16 protons and 16 electrons. In many simple
    ionic compounds, it gains two electrons to form S²⁻.
  </p>
  <p><b>Equation:</b> S + 2e⁻ → S²⁻</p>

  <h3>6. What changes when an anion forms?</h3>
  <p>
    The number of electrons increases, but the number of protons stays
    unchanged. The element therefore remains the same element.
    For example, a chlorine atom becomes a chloride ion; it does not
    become a different element.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Determine the charge of a chloride ion</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A particle has 17 protons and 18 electrons. Calculate its net charge.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Each proton contributes +1, giving a total proton charge of +17.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Each electron contributes −1, giving a total electron charge of −18.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Net charge = 17 − 18 = −1.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> The particle has charge −1. If it is chlorine, it is Cl⁻.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Determine the electrons in an oxide ion</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> Oxygen has atomic number 8. How many electrons are present in O²⁻?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Atomic number 8 means oxygen has 8 protons.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> A neutral oxygen atom has 8 electrons.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> A 2− charge means the atom has gained two electrons.</p>
  <p><b>Step 4:</b> Total electrons = 8 + 2 = 10.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> O²⁻ has 10 electrons and 8 protons.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Write the formation equation for a sulfide ion</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> Write the equation showing sulfur gaining two electrons to form S²⁻.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Write the neutral sulfur atom as S.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Add two electrons to the reactant side because sulfur gains them.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Write the sulfide ion on the product side.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> S + 2e⁻ → S²⁻.</p>
  <p><b>Check:</b> The total charge is −2 on both sides.</p>

  <h3>Key idea</h3>
  <p>
    Negative ions form by gaining electrons. The magnitude of the
    negative charge equals the number of electrons gained by a neutral
    atom, provided no other charge-bearing particles are involved.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Formation of Negative Ions', 15),
  (781, 781, '
  <h2>Formation of Ionic Compounds</h2>

  <h3>1. How an ionic compound forms</h3>
  <p>
    An ionic compound forms when oppositely charged ions are held
    together by electrostatic attraction. In many simple examples,
    electrons transfer from metal atoms to non-metal atoms, producing
    positive and negative ions.
  </p>

  <p>
    The electron transfer explains how the ions form. The ionic bond
    is the attraction between the resulting opposite charges.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Formation of sodium chloride</h3>
  <p>
    Sodium loses one electron to form Na⁺. Chlorine gains one electron
    to form Cl⁻. The ions attract, and the solid forms a giant ionic
    lattice with a 1:1 ratio of sodium ions to chloride ions.
  </p>
  <p><b>Sodium:</b> Na → Na⁺ + e⁻</p>
  <p><b>Chlorine:</b> Cl + e⁻ → Cl⁻</p>
  <p><b>Formula:</b> NaCl</p>

  <h3>3. Ionic formulae must be electrically neutral</h3>
  <p>
    The total positive charge in an ionic formula must balance the
    total negative charge. The formula gives the simplest whole-number
    ratio of ions.
  </p>

  <p>
    For example, magnesium forms Mg²⁺ and chlorine forms Cl⁻.
    One magnesium ion needs two chloride ions:
  </p>
  <p>+2 + 2(−1) = 0</p>
  <p>Therefore, the formula is MgCl₂.</p>

  <h3>4. Formation of magnesium oxide</h3>
  <p>
    Magnesium loses two electrons to form Mg²⁺. Oxygen gains two
    electrons to form O²⁻. The ions combine in a 1:1 ratio because
    +2 and −2 cancel.
  </p>
  <p><b>Magnesium:</b> Mg → Mg²⁺ + 2e⁻</p>
  <p><b>Oxygen:</b> O + 2e⁻ → O²⁻</p>
  <p><b>Formula:</b> MgO</p>

  <h3>5. Formation of aluminium oxide</h3>
  <p>
    Aluminium forms Al³⁺, while oxygen forms O²⁻ in aluminium oxide.
    Two Al³⁺ ions give a total charge of +6. Three O²⁻ ions give
    a total charge of −6. The charges balance in Al₂O₃.
  </p>

  <h3>6. Ionic compounds are not usually separate molecules</h3>
  <p>
    In a solid ionic compound, many positive and negative ions are
    arranged in a repeating three-dimensional lattice. A formula such
    as MgO represents the simplest ratio of ions in the structure,
    not an isolated MgO molecule.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Write the formula of sodium oxide</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> Sodium forms Na⁺ and oxygen forms O²⁻. Determine the formula of sodium oxide.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Each Na⁺ ion contributes +1.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Each O²⁻ ion contributes −2.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Two Na⁺ ions contribute +2, balancing one O²⁻ ion.</p>
  <p><b>Step 4:</b> The simplest ratio is two sodium ions to one oxide ion.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Na₂O.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Write the formula of calcium fluoride</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> Calcium forms Ca²⁺ and fluorine forms F⁻. Determine the formula of calcium fluoride.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Calcium contributes +2.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Each fluoride ion contributes −1.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Two fluoride ions contribute −2.</p>
  <p><b>Step 4:</b> The charges cancel: +2 + 2(−1) = 0.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> CaF₂.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Derive the formula of aluminium oxide</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> Aluminium forms Al³⁺ and oxygen forms O²⁻. Determine the neutral formula.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> The smallest common total charge magnitude for 3 and 2 is 6.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Two Al³⁺ ions give 2 × (+3) = +6.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Three O²⁻ ions give 3 × (−2) = −6.</p>
  <p><b>Step 4:</b> The total charge is +6 − 6 = 0.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Al₂O₃.</p>

  <h3>Key idea</h3>
  <p>
    To derive an ionic formula, identify the ions and their charges,
    choose the smallest whole-number ratio that balances the charges,
    and write the formula without the ion charges.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Formation of Ionic Compounds', 15),
  (782, 782, '
  <h2>Properties of Ionic Compounds</h2>

  <h3>1. Structure explains properties</h3>
  <p>
    Many ionic compounds have high melting and boiling points, are
    brittle solids at room temperature, and conduct electricity when
    molten or dissolved in water if their ions are free to move.
    These properties are explained by their giant ionic lattice.
  </p>

  <h3>2. High melting and boiling points</h3>
  <p>
    In a solid ionic lattice, positive and negative ions are held
    together by strong electrostatic attractions. A large amount of
    energy is required to overcome enough of these attractions for
    the solid to melt or the liquid to boil.
  </p>

  <p>
    The melting point is the temperature at which a solid changes
    into a liquid. The boiling point is the temperature at which
    a liquid changes into a gas.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Electrical conductivity in the solid state</h3>
  <p>
    In a solid ionic compound, the ions occupy fixed positions in
    the lattice. Although the ions are charged, they cannot move
    freely through the solid. Therefore, solid sodium chloride
    does not conduct electricity under ordinary conditions.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Conductivity when molten</h3>
  <p>
    When an ionic compound melts, its lattice structure breaks down
    enough for the ions to move. The mobile charged ions carry
    electrical current through the liquid.
  </p>

  <h3>5. Conductivity in aqueous solution</h3>
  <p>
    When a soluble ionic compound dissolves in water, it separates
    into mobile ions. These ions can move towards oppositely charged
    electrodes, allowing the solution to conduct electricity.
  </p>

  <p>
    Not every ionic compound is soluble in water. Conductivity in
    aqueous solution depends on whether the substance dissolves
    sufficiently to produce mobile ions.
  </p>

  <h3>6. Why ionic compounds are brittle</h3>
  <p>
    If layers of ions shift when a force is applied, ions of the
    same charge may be brought next to one another. Their repulsion
    can cause the crystal to split. This is why ionic crystals tend
    to be brittle rather than malleable.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Explain why solid sodium chloride does not conduct electricity</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> Explain why solid sodium chloride is a poor conductor of electricity.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Sodium chloride contains positive Na⁺ and negative Cl⁻ ions.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> In the solid, the ions are held in fixed positions in the lattice.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Electrical conduction requires charged particles that can move through the material.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Solid sodium chloride does not conduct because its ions cannot move freely through the fixed lattice.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Explain why molten sodium chloride conducts electricity</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> Why does molten sodium chloride conduct electricity while solid sodium chloride does not?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> In the solid, the ions are fixed in their lattice positions.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> On melting, the lattice no longer holds the ions in fixed positions.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> The ions can move through the liquid and carry charge.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Molten sodium chloride conducts because its ions are mobile and carry electrical charge.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Explain the high melting point of an ionic compound</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> Explain why many ionic compounds have high melting points.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> The ions form a giant lattice.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Oppositely charged ions attract strongly throughout the lattice.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> A large amount of energy is needed to overcome enough of these attractions for melting to occur.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Ionic compounds often have high melting points because strong electrostatic attractions between ions require substantial energy to overcome.</p>

  <h3>Key idea</h3>
  <p>
    Always explain an ionic compound''s property using its structure:
    strong attractions explain high melting points, fixed ions explain
    poor solid conductivity, and mobile ions explain conductivity
    when molten or in solution.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Properties of Ionic Compounds', 15),
  (783, 783, '
  <h2>Covalent Bonding</h2>

  <h3>1. What is a covalent bond?</h3>
  <p>
    A <b>covalent bond</b> is a chemical bond formed when atoms share
    one or more pairs of electrons. The shared electrons are attracted
    to the nuclei of both bonded atoms, helping hold the atoms together.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Why do atoms share electrons?</h3>
  <p>
    Sharing electrons can produce a more stable electron arrangement.
    In many simple examples, hydrogen shares electrons to fill its
    first shell with two electrons, while carbon, nitrogen, oxygen,
    and chlorine often share electrons to achieve eight outer-shell
    electrons in the introductory model.
  </p>

  <p>
    These are useful models for common covalent compounds. Not all
    covalent substances obey a strict octet rule.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Covalent bonding in hydrogen</h3>
  <p>
    Each hydrogen atom has one electron. Two hydrogen atoms share a
    pair of electrons. Each hydrogen nucleus attracts the shared pair,
    and each hydrogen atom is associated with two electrons in the
    shared arrangement.
  </p>
  <p><b>Formula:</b> H₂</p>
  <p><b>Displayed formula:</b> H—H</p>

  <h3>4. Covalent bonding in chlorine</h3>
  <p>
    Each chlorine atom has seven outer-shell electrons. Two chlorine
    atoms share one pair of electrons. Each atom contributes one
    electron to the shared pair, and each chlorine atom is associated
    with eight outer-shell electrons.
  </p>
  <p><b>Formula:</b> Cl₂</p>
  <p><b>Displayed formula:</b> Cl—Cl</p>

  <h3>5. Covalent bonding in water</h3>
  <p>
    Oxygen has six outer-shell electrons, while each hydrogen has one
    electron. Oxygen shares one pair with each of two hydrogen atoms.
    Water therefore contains two O—H single covalent bonds.
  </p>
  <p><b>Formula:</b> H₂O</p>
  <p><b>Displayed formula:</b> H—O—H</p>

  <h3>6. Shared electrons are not completely transferred</h3>
  <p>
    In ionic bonding, electrons are transferred to form ions. In
    covalent bonding, the atoms share electron pairs. The shared
    electrons are attracted to both bonded nuclei.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Explain the covalent bond in H₂</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> Explain how two hydrogen atoms form a covalent bond.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Each hydrogen atom has one electron.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Each atom contributes one electron to a shared pair.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> The shared pair is attracted to both hydrogen nuclei.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Two hydrogen atoms share one pair of electrons, forming a single covalent bond in H₂.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Predict the number of covalent bonds in H₂O</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> Oxygen has six outer-shell electrons and hydrogen has one. Explain why water contains two O—H bonds.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Oxygen has six outer-shell electrons and needs two more to reach eight in the simple shell model.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Each hydrogen can share one electron with oxygen.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Oxygen shares one pair with each of two hydrogen atoms.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Water contains two single covalent bonds, one between oxygen and each hydrogen atom.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Explain the bond in Cl₂</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> Each chlorine atom has seven outer-shell electrons. Explain how Cl₂ forms.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Each chlorine atom needs one more electron to reach eight in the simple shell model.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Each chlorine atom contributes one electron to a shared pair.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Both chlorine atoms are associated with the shared pair.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Two chlorine atoms share one pair of electrons to form Cl₂, with one single covalent bond.</p>

  <h3>Key idea</h3>
  <p>
    Covalent bonding involves shared electron pairs. A single
    covalent bond consists of one shared pair of electrons attracted
    to both bonded nuclei.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Covalent Bonding', 15),
  (784, 784, '
  <h2>Single Covalent Bonds</h2>

  <h3>1. Definition of a single covalent bond</h3>
  <p>
    A <b>single covalent bond</b> consists of one shared pair of
    electrons between two atoms. One line in a displayed formula
    represents one shared pair.
  </p>

  <p>
    For example, H—H represents one single covalent bond between
    the two hydrogen atoms in H₂.
  </p>

  <h3>2. How a single bond forms</h3>
  <p>
    In a simple single covalent bond, each bonded atom contributes
    one electron to the shared pair. The shared electrons are attracted
    to the nuclei of both atoms. The atoms are held together by this
    attraction.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Hydrogen molecule, H₂</h3>
  <p>
    Each hydrogen atom has one electron. Sharing one pair gives each
    hydrogen access to two electrons in the shared arrangement.
  </p>
  <p><b>Displayed formula:</b> H—H</p>
  <p><b>Shared pairs between the atoms:</b> 1</p>

  <h3>4. Chlorine molecule, Cl₂</h3>
  <p>
    Each chlorine atom has seven outer-shell electrons. The atoms share
    one pair of electrons, producing one single bond. Each chlorine
    atom is then associated with eight outer-shell electrons in the
    simple shell model.
  </p>
  <p><b>Displayed formula:</b> Cl—Cl</p>

  <h3>5. Methane, CH₄</h3>
  <p>
    Carbon has four outer-shell electrons. In methane, carbon shares
    one pair of electrons with each of four hydrogen atoms. There are
    four C—H single covalent bonds in one methane molecule.
  </p>
  <p><b>Formula:</b> CH₄</p>

  <h3>6. Ammonia, NH₃</h3>
  <p>
    Nitrogen has five outer-shell electrons. In ammonia, nitrogen forms
    three single covalent bonds by sharing one pair with each of three
    hydrogen atoms. The nitrogen atom also has one lone pair that is
    not shared in bonding.
  </p>
  <p><b>Formula:</b> NH₃</p>

  <h3>7. A bond line and a shared pair</h3>
  <p>
    Each single bond line represents two electrons, not one electron.
    Lone pairs are pairs of outer-shell electrons that are not shared
    between atoms in a covalent bond.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Count bonds in methane</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> Methane has the formula CH₄. How many single covalent bonds are present in one molecule?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify the central carbon atom and the four hydrogen atoms.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Each hydrogen forms one single bond with carbon.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Count the four C—H connections.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Four single covalent bonds.</p>
  <p><b>Check:</b> Four bonds contain four shared pairs, or eight electrons in total across the four bonding pairs.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Count bonding electrons in H₂</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A hydrogen molecule contains one single covalent bond. How many electrons are in the shared pair?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> One single covalent bond represents one shared pair.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> A pair contains two electrons.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Two shared electrons.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Count the bonds and lone pairs in ammonia</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> Ammonia, NH₃, has three N—H bonds. How many shared pairs and lone pairs are around the nitrogen atom in the simple electron-pair model?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Each N—H single bond is one shared pair.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Three bonds mean three shared pairs around nitrogen.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Nitrogen has five outer-shell electrons and forms three bonds, leaving one lone pair in the simple model.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Three shared pairs and one lone pair around nitrogen.</p>

  <h3>Key idea</h3>
  <p>
    One single covalent bond equals one shared pair of electrons.
    Count bond lines to determine the number of single bonds, then
    distinguish bonding pairs from lone pairs.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Single Covalent Bonds', 15),
  (785, 785, '
  <h2>Double Covalent Bonds</h2>

  <h3>1. What is a double covalent bond?</h3>
  <p>
    A <b>double covalent bond</b> consists of two shared pairs of
    electrons between the same two atoms. It contains four shared
    electrons in total.
  </p>
  <p>
    In a displayed formula, a double covalent bond is represented
    by two lines, for example O=O.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Formation of oxygen, O₂</h3>
  <p>
    Each oxygen atom has six outer-shell electrons. In the simple
    octet model, each oxygen atom needs two more electrons to reach
    eight. Two oxygen atoms share two pairs of electrons, forming
    a double bond.
  </p>
  <p><b>Displayed formula:</b> O=O</p>
  <p>
    Each oxygen atom is associated with eight outer-shell electrons
    when the two shared pairs are counted.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Formation of carbon dioxide, CO₂</h3>
  <p>
    Carbon has four outer-shell electrons, while oxygen has six.
    In carbon dioxide, carbon shares two pairs of electrons with
    each oxygen atom. Each C=O connection is a double bond.
  </p>
  <p><b>Displayed formula:</b> O=C=O</p>
  <p>
    The molecule contains two double bonds. Each double bond contains
    two shared pairs, so there are four shared pairs across the
    two C=O bonds.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Counting electrons in a double bond</h3>
  <p>
    One shared pair contains two electrons. A double bond has two
    shared pairs:
  </p>
  <p>2 pairs × 2 electrons per pair = 4 shared electrons.</p>

  <h3>5. Single and double bonds</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Single bond:</b> one shared pair, or two shared electrons.</li>
    <li><b>Double bond:</b> two shared pairs, or four shared electrons.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    In general, a double bond is stronger and shorter than a single
    bond between the same two elements, although bond properties
    depend on the atoms and their chemical environment.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Count the shared electrons in O₂</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> An oxygen molecule contains one double bond. How many electrons are shared between its two atoms?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> A double bond contains two shared pairs.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Each pair contains two electrons.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Total shared electrons = 2 × 2 = 4.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Four shared electrons.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Count the double bonds in carbon dioxide</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> Carbon dioxide has the displayed formula O=C=O. How many double bonds and shared electron pairs does it contain?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify the two C=O connections.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Each connection is a double bond, so there are two double bonds.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Each double bond contains two shared pairs.</p>
  <p><b>Step 4:</b> Total shared pairs = 2 × 2 = 4.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Two double bonds and four shared pairs, equivalent to eight shared electrons.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Compare single and double bonds</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> Calculate the number of shared electrons in one single bond and one double bond combined.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> A single bond contains two shared electrons.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> A double bond contains four shared electrons.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Total = 2 + 4 = 6 shared electrons.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Six shared electrons.</p>

  <h3>Key idea</h3>
  <p>
    A double covalent bond consists of two shared electron pairs.
    Use the displayed formula to count bonds, then multiply the
    number of shared pairs by two to find the number of shared electrons.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Double Covalent Bonds', 15),
  (786, 786, '
  <h2>Triple Covalent Bonds</h2>

  <h3>1. What is a triple covalent bond?</h3>
  <p>
    A <b>triple covalent bond</b> consists of three shared pairs
    of electrons between two atoms. It contains six shared electrons.
    In a displayed formula, three lines represent the bond.
  </p>
  <p><b>Example:</b> N≡N</p>

  <h3>2. Formation of nitrogen, N₂</h3>
  <p>
    Each nitrogen atom has five outer-shell electrons. In the simple
    octet model, each nitrogen atom needs three additional electrons
    to reach eight. Two nitrogen atoms share three pairs of electrons,
    forming a triple covalent bond.
  </p>
  <p><b>Displayed formula:</b> N≡N</p>
  <p>
    Each nitrogen atom is associated with eight outer-shell electrons
    when the three shared pairs are counted.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Counting electrons in a triple bond</h3>
  <p>
    One shared pair contains two electrons. Therefore:
  </p>
  <p>3 shared pairs × 2 electrons per pair = 6 shared electrons.</p>

  <h3>4. Comparing single, double, and triple bonds</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Single bond:</b> one shared pair = two electrons.</li>
    <li><b>Double bond:</b> two shared pairs = four electrons.</li>
    <li><b>Triple bond:</b> three shared pairs = six electrons.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Between the same two elements, a triple bond is generally shorter
    and stronger than a double bond, which is generally shorter and
    stronger than a single bond. These are general trends for comparable
    bonds, not a rule that predicts every bond in every molecule.
  </p>

  <h3>5. Nitrogen and its triple bond</h3>
  <p>
    Nitrogen gas consists of N₂ molecules. The triple bond holds the
    two nitrogen atoms together strongly. Breaking this bond requires
    substantial energy, which helps explain why nitrogen gas is
    relatively unreactive under ordinary conditions.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Count electrons in N≡N</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> How many shared electrons are present in one nitrogen molecule''s triple bond?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> A triple bond contains three shared pairs.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Each pair contains two electrons.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Total shared electrons = 3 × 2 = 6.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Six shared electrons.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Compare the electron counts of three bond types</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> Calculate the total number of shared electrons represented by one single bond, one double bond, and one triple bond.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Single bond: 1 × 2 = 2 electrons.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Double bond: 2 × 2 = 4 electrons.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Triple bond: 3 × 2 = 6 electrons.</p>
  <p><b>Step 4:</b> Total = 2 + 4 + 6 = 12 electrons.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Twelve shared electrons in total.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Explain nitrogen''s triple bond</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> Explain why two nitrogen atoms form a triple bond in N₂ using the simple octet model.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Each nitrogen atom has five outer-shell electrons.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Each needs three more electrons to reach eight in the model.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> The atoms share three pairs of electrons.</p>
  <p><b>Step 4:</b> The shared pairs are attracted to both nitrogen nuclei.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> The nitrogen atoms share three pairs of electrons, forming N≡N and completing the octet for each atom in the simple model.</p>

  <h3>Key idea</h3>
  <p>
    A triple covalent bond consists of three shared pairs, or six
    electrons. Nitrogen gas is a familiar example represented by N≡N.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Triple Covalent Bonds', 15),
  (787, 787, '
  <h2>Dot-and-Cross Diagrams</h2>

  <h3>1. What is a dot-and-cross diagram?</h3>
  <p>
    A <b>dot-and-cross diagram</b> represents the outer-shell electrons
    of atoms using dots and crosses. The two symbols distinguish
    electrons that came from different atoms before bonding.
  </p>

  <p>
    Dots and crosses do not represent different kinds of electrons.
    They are simply a drawing convention used to show which atom
    contributed each electron.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Drawing a covalent bond</h3>
  <p>
    In a covalent bond, the shared pair is placed between the two
    bonded atoms. In a dot-and-cross diagram, one electron may be
    shown as a dot and the other as a cross.
  </p>

  <p>
    For H₂, each hydrogen contributes one electron. The shared pair
    contains one dot and one cross. Each hydrogen is associated with
    two electrons in the shared arrangement.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Drawing a chlorine molecule</h3>
  <p>
    Each chlorine atom has seven outer-shell electrons. When two
    chlorine atoms bond, each contributes one electron to the shared
    pair. Each chlorine has one shared pair and three lone pairs
    in the simple diagram.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Drawing an oxygen molecule</h3>
  <p>
    Each oxygen atom has six outer-shell electrons. In O₂, the atoms
    share two pairs of electrons. The shared region therefore contains
    four electrons: two dots from one oxygen and two crosses from
    the other, if that symbol convention is used.
  </p>

  <h3>5. Drawing an ionic compound</h3>
  <p>
    For an ionic compound such as sodium chloride, the diagram shows
    an electron transferred from sodium to chlorine. The ions are
    then placed in square brackets with their charges. The chloride
    ion is shown with eight outer-shell electrons, including the
    transferred electron.
  </p>

  <p>
    The electron symbols should show the source of the transferred
    electron, but the charge notation shows the ion''s final charge.
  </p>

  <h3>6. A reliable method for drawing the diagrams</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Write each atom''s symbol.</li>
    <li>Determine its number of outer-shell electrons.</li>
    <li>Choose dots for one atom and crosses for the other.</li>
    <li>For covalent bonding, place shared pairs between the atoms.</li>
    <li>For ionic bonding, show electron transfer, then bracket each ion and write its charge.</li>
    <li>Count the electrons around each atom or ion to check the diagram.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Draw the electron-sharing arrangement for H₂</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> Describe the dot-and-cross arrangement for a hydrogen molecule.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Each hydrogen atom has one electron.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Represent the electron from the first hydrogen as a dot and the electron from the second as a cross.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Place the dot and cross together between the two hydrogen symbols to show the shared pair.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> H atoms share one pair containing one dot and one cross; this represents one single covalent bond.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Determine the electron pairs in Cl₂</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> How many shared pairs and lone pairs are present in a dot-and-cross diagram of Cl₂?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Each chlorine has seven outer-shell electrons.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Each chlorine contributes one electron to the shared pair.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> The shared region contains one pair.</p>
  <p><b>Step 4:</b> Each chlorine has six other outer-shell electrons arranged as three lone pairs.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> One shared pair and three lone pairs on each chlorine atom.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Check the outer shell of a chloride ion</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A chlorine atom gains one electron. How many outer-shell electrons should be shown around Cl⁻ in an ionic dot-and-cross diagram?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> A neutral chlorine atom has seven outer-shell electrons.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> The atom gains one electron.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Outer-shell electrons = 7 + 1 = 8.</p>
  <p><b>Step 4:</b> Draw the ion in square brackets with a − charge.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Show eight outer-shell electrons around [Cl]⁻, including the transferred electron.</p>

  <h3>Key idea</h3>
  <p>
    Dot-and-cross diagrams are electron-accounting diagrams. Show
    the correct outer-shell electrons, distinguish their origins,
    and check that sharing or transfer is represented accurately.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Dot-and-Cross Diagrams', 15),
  (788, 788, '
  <h2>Properties of Simple Covalent Substances</h2>

  <h3>1. What is a simple covalent substance?</h3>
  <p>
    A <b>simple covalent substance</b> consists of separate molecules
    in which atoms are joined by covalent bonds. Examples include
    hydrogen, oxygen, chlorine, water, methane, and carbon dioxide.
  </p>

  <p>
    The covalent bonds within each molecule are strong. However,
    the forces between separate molecules are often much weaker than
    the covalent bonds inside the molecules.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Why many simple covalent substances have low melting points</h3>
  <p>
    When a simple molecular substance melts, the molecules can move
    past one another. The process generally involves overcoming
    intermolecular attractions rather than breaking all the covalent
    bonds within the molecules.
  </p>

  <p>
    Many simple molecular substances therefore have relatively low
    melting and boiling points. The exact values depend on the strength
    of the intermolecular forces and the molecular structure.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Why many simple covalent substances do not conduct electricity</h3>
  <p>
    Simple molecular substances generally do not contain mobile ions
    or a sea of delocalised electrons. Their electrons are involved
    in covalent bonds or remain in lone pairs, so there are usually
    no freely moving charged particles to carry current.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Solubility depends on the substance and solvent</h3>
  <p>
    Simple covalent substances do not all have the same solubility.
    Some dissolve in water, while others dissolve more readily in
    non-polar solvents. Solubility depends on the interactions between
    the solute particles and the solvent molecules.
  </p>

  <p>
    For example, ammonia is very soluble in water, while methane is
    only very slightly soluble. It is incorrect to say that all
    covalent substances are insoluble in water.
  </p>

  <h3>5. Do not confuse simple molecular and giant covalent structures</h3>
  <p>
    Diamond and silicon dioxide have giant covalent structures rather
    than separate small molecules throughout the solid. Many giant
    covalent substances have very high melting points because melting
    requires disrupting many strong covalent bonds.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Explain the low boiling point of methane</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> Methane is a simple molecular substance. Explain why it has a relatively low boiling point compared with many giant covalent substances.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Methane consists of separate CH₄ molecules.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> The molecules attract one another through weak intermolecular forces.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Boiling requires enough energy to overcome these attractions so the molecules can separate into the gas.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Methane has a relatively low boiling point because the attractions between its molecules are weak compared with the covalent bonds within molecules.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Explain why pure water does not conduct electricity well</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> Explain why pure water is a poor electrical conductor compared with a solution of sodium chloride.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Pure water contains very few ions compared with an ionic solution.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Sodium chloride dissolves to produce mobile Na⁺ and Cl⁻ ions.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> These mobile ions carry electrical current through the solution.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Pure water conducts poorly because it has very few mobile ions, while sodium chloride solution contains mobile ions that carry charge.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Compare methane and diamond</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> Explain why methane and diamond have very different melting behaviour despite both involving covalent bonds.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Methane consists of separate small molecules.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> When methane changes state, the main attractions overcome are between molecules.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Diamond has a giant three-dimensional covalent network.</p>
  <p><b>Step 4:</b> Disrupting diamond''s structure requires breaking many strong covalent bonds.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Methane is molecular and has weak intermolecular attractions, whereas diamond has a giant covalent network requiring substantial energy to disrupt.</p>

  <h3>Key idea</h3>
  <p>
    For simple covalent substances, distinguish the strong covalent
    bonds within molecules from the often weaker attractions between
    molecules. Do not apply the properties of simple molecules to
    giant covalent structures.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Properties of Simple Covalent Substances', 15),
  (789, 789, '
  <h2>Giant Covalent Structures</h2>

  <h3>1. What is a giant covalent structure?</h3>
  <p>
    A <b>giant covalent structure</b> is a large continuous arrangement
    of atoms joined by covalent bonds. Instead of consisting of separate
    small molecules, the atoms form an extensive network.
  </p>

  <p>
    Examples include diamond, graphite, and silicon dioxide. Their
    properties depend on how the atoms are connected and how the
    electrons are arranged.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Why many giant covalent structures have high melting points</h3>
  <p>
    Many giant covalent structures contain numerous strong covalent
    bonds extending throughout the network. A large amount of energy
    is required to disrupt the structure because many bonds must be
    broken.
  </p>

  <p>
    This differs from melting a simple molecular substance, where
    the main attractions overcome are generally between separate
    molecules.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Diamond and graphite are different structures of carbon</h3>
  <p>
    Diamond and graphite are both forms of carbon, but their atoms
    are arranged differently.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Diamond:</b> each carbon atom forms four covalent bonds in a rigid three-dimensional network.</li>
    <li><b>Graphite:</b> each carbon atom forms three covalent bonds in layers; one electron per carbon atom is delocalised in the structure.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Diamond is very hard and does not conduct electricity under
    ordinary conditions. Graphite is soft between its layers and
    conducts electricity because it has delocalised electrons that
    can move along the layers.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Silicon dioxide</h3>
  <p>
    Silicon dioxide, SiO₂, has a giant covalent network. Each silicon
    atom is bonded to oxygen atoms, and the oxygen atoms link the
    network together. It is not made of separate SiO₂ molecules in
    the solid structure.
  </p>

  <h3>5. Why structure matters</h3>
  <p>
    The label "covalent" alone does not tell you every physical
    property. You must identify whether the substance contains
    separate molecules or a giant network, and then consider how
    its atoms and electrons are arranged.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Explain the high melting point of diamond</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> Explain why diamond has a very high melting point.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Diamond contains a giant three-dimensional network of carbon atoms.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Each carbon atom is joined to four other carbon atoms by strong covalent bonds.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Melting requires disrupting many strong covalent bonds throughout the network.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Diamond has a very high melting point because many strong covalent bonds must be broken to disrupt its giant network.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Explain why graphite conducts electricity</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> Explain why graphite conducts electricity while diamond does not conduct under ordinary conditions.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> In graphite, each carbon atom forms three covalent bonds.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> One outer electron per carbon atom is delocalised in the structure.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> These delocalised electrons can move along the layers and carry charge.</p>
  <p><b>Step 4:</b> In diamond, each carbon atom uses its four outer electrons in four covalent bonds, so there are no comparable mobile delocalised electrons.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Graphite conducts because it has mobile delocalised electrons; diamond does not conduct under ordinary conditions because its outer electrons are involved in covalent bonding.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Distinguish a giant covalent structure from a simple molecular substance</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> Explain the structural difference between silicon dioxide and carbon dioxide.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Solid silicon dioxide forms a giant covalent network.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Carbon dioxide consists of separate CO₂ molecules under ordinary conditions.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> The formula SiO₂ represents the ratio of atoms in the silicon dioxide network, whereas CO₂ represents the composition of each carbon dioxide molecule.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Silicon dioxide has a giant covalent network, while carbon dioxide consists of separate molecules. Their formulae do not indicate the same type of structure.</p>

  <h3>Key idea</h3>
  <p>
    Giant covalent structures contain extensive networks of covalent
    bonds. Their properties depend on the network''s arrangement and
    the availability of mobile electrons.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Giant Covalent Structures', 15),
  (790, 790, '
  <h2>Diamond: Structure and Properties</h2>

  <h3>1. What type of structure does diamond have?</h3>

  <p>
    Diamond is a form of carbon. It has a <b>giant covalent structure</b>,
    meaning that many carbon atoms are joined together by strong covalent
    bonds in a continuous three-dimensional network.
  </p>

  <p>
    Diamond is not made of separate carbon molecules. The covalent network
    extends throughout the crystal.
  </p>

  <h3>2. How are the carbon atoms bonded?</h3>

  <p>
    Each carbon atom has four electrons in its outer shell. In diamond,
    each carbon atom shares one electron with each of four neighbouring
    carbon atoms.
  </p>

  <p>
    Each shared pair of electrons forms a covalent bond. Therefore,
    <b>each carbon atom forms four covalent bonds</b>.
  </p>

  <p>
    The carbon atoms are arranged in a three-dimensional tetrahedral
    network. The bonds extend in different directions, so the structure
    is rigid and difficult to deform.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Why is diamond extremely hard?</h3>

  <p>
    Diamond is extremely hard because each carbon atom is connected to
    four other carbon atoms by strong covalent bonds. These bonds form
    a continuous network throughout the structure.
  </p>

  <p>
    Scratching or cutting diamond requires disrupting strong covalent
    bonds. This requires considerable energy. The hardness is therefore
    a consequence of the bonding throughout the network, not simply
    because carbon is a hard element.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Why does diamond have a high melting point?</h3>

  <p>
    A large amount of energy is needed to break enough of the strong
    covalent bonds in the giant structure for the solid to melt.
    Diamond consequently has an extremely high melting temperature
    under appropriate pressure conditions.
  </p>

  <p>
    Do not confuse the energy needed to overcome attractions between
    separate molecules with the energy needed to disrupt a giant
    covalent network. Diamond does not consist of separate molecules
    held together by weak intermolecular forces.
  </p>

  <h3>5. Why does diamond not conduct electricity?</h3>

  <p>
    Each carbon atom uses all four of its outer-shell electrons in
    covalent bonding. There are no freely moving electrons available
    to carry electrical charge through the structure.
  </p>

  <p>
    Diamond is therefore an electrical insulator under ordinary
    conditions.
  </p>

  <h3>6. Uses of diamond</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <b>Cutting and drilling tools:</b> its extreme hardness makes it
      suitable for cutting or grinding hard materials.
    </li>
    <li>
      <b>Jewellery:</b> its brilliance, transparency and ability to
      withstand scratching make it valuable in jewellery.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Explain the hardness of diamond</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Explain why diamond is extremely hard.</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify its structure.</p>
  <p>
    Diamond has a giant three-dimensional covalent structure.
  </p>

  <p><b>Step 2:</b> State the bonding arrangement.</p>
  <p>
    Each carbon atom forms four strong covalent bonds with neighbouring
    carbon atoms.
  </p>

  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Connect the bonding to the property.</p>
  <p>
    The continuous network cannot be easily deformed without disrupting
    strong covalent bonds.
  </p>

  <p><b>Conclusion:</b> Diamond is extremely hard because its carbon
  atoms are joined by strong covalent bonds throughout a rigid
  three-dimensional network.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Explain the high melting point</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Why does diamond have a very high melting point?</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Diamond is a giant covalent structure, not a collection
  of separate molecules.</p>

  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Strong covalent bonds connect the carbon atoms
  throughout the structure.</p>

  <p><b>Step 3:</b> A large amount of energy is required to disrupt
  enough of these bonds for the structure to melt.</p>

  <p><b>Conclusion:</b> Diamond has a very high melting point because
  its giant covalent network contains many strong covalent bonds.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Explain electrical insulation</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Diamond and graphite are both made of carbon.
  Why does diamond not conduct electricity?</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> In diamond, each carbon atom forms four covalent bonds.</p>

  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Its four outer-shell electrons are involved in these
  bonds.</p>

  <p><b>Step 3:</b> There are no delocalised electrons free to move
  through the structure and carry charge.</p>

  <p><b>Conclusion:</b> Diamond does not conduct electricity because
  it has no freely moving charge carriers.</p>

  <h3>Common mistake</h3>

  <p>
    Do not write that diamond is hard because its atoms are closely
    packed. The essential explanation is the strong covalent bonding
    throughout its three-dimensional network.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Diamond', 15),
  (791, 791, '
  <h2>Graphite: Structure and Properties</h2>

  <h3>1. Graphite is another form of carbon</h3>

  <p>
    Graphite and diamond are both forms of the element carbon, but
    their atoms are arranged differently. This difference in structure
    gives them different physical properties.
  </p>

  <p>
    Graphite has a <b>giant covalent structure made of layers</b>.
    Each layer contains carbon atoms bonded together in a repeating
    network of hexagonal rings.
  </p>

  <h3>2. How are carbon atoms bonded in graphite?</h3>

  <p>
    Each carbon atom forms three strong covalent bonds with three
    neighbouring carbon atoms in the same layer.
  </p>

  <p>
    Carbon has four outer-shell electrons. Three are used in the
    three covalent bonds. The remaining electron contributes to a
    system of <b>delocalised electrons</b> within the layer.
  </p>

  <p>
    Delocalised electrons are not confined to one particular
    covalent bond. They can move through the graphite layers and
    carry electrical charge.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Why does graphite conduct electricity?</h3>

  <p>
    Graphite conducts electricity because it contains delocalised
    electrons that can move along its layers.
  </p>

  <p>
    When a potential difference is applied, these mobile electrons
    can carry charge through the material. This makes graphite
    different from diamond, in which all four outer-shell electrons
    of each carbon atom are used in covalent bonding.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Why is graphite soft and slippery?</h3>

  <p>
    The carbon atoms within each layer are connected by strong
    covalent bonds. However, the attractions between neighbouring
    layers are much weaker than the covalent bonds within a layer.
  </p>

  <p>
    The layers can slide over one another relatively easily.
    This makes graphite soft and allows it to act as a lubricant
    in suitable applications.
  </p>

  <p>
    Do not say that the covalent bonds inside each layer are weak.
    The weak attractions are <b>between the layers</b>, not within them.
  </p>

  <h3>5. Why does graphite have a high melting point?</h3>

  <p>
    Graphite is a giant covalent structure. Strong covalent bonds
    connect the carbon atoms throughout each layer. A very large
    amount of energy is needed to disrupt this network.
  </p>

  <p>
    Therefore, graphite has a very high melting or sublimation
    temperature under appropriate conditions. The weak attractions
    between layers help explain its softness, but they do not mean
    the entire structure melts at a low temperature.
  </p>

  <h3>6. Uses of graphite</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <b>Electrodes:</b> graphite conducts electricity and can be
      used as an electrode in suitable electrochemical processes.
    </li>
    <li>
      <b>Lubricants:</b> its layers slide over one another, reducing
      friction in suitable conditions.
    </li>
    <li>
      <b>Pencil cores:</b> layers of graphite rub off onto paper
      during writing.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Explain why graphite conducts electricity</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Explain why graphite conducts electricity but
  diamond does not.</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> In graphite, each carbon atom forms three covalent
  bonds with neighbouring atoms.</p>

  <p><b>Step 2:</b> The remaining outer-shell electron contributes to
  the delocalised electron system.</p>

  <p><b>Step 3:</b> These electrons can move along the layers and carry
  electrical charge. In diamond, each carbon uses all four outer-shell
  electrons in covalent bonds.</p>

  <p><b>Conclusion:</b> Graphite conducts electricity because it has
  mobile delocalised electrons, whereas diamond has no freely moving
  electrons under ordinary conditions.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Explain why graphite is soft</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Explain why graphite can be used as a lubricant.</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Graphite is made of layers of carbon atoms.</p>

  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Strong covalent bonds hold the atoms together
  within each layer.</p>

  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Much weaker attractions act between neighbouring
  layers, allowing them to slide over one another.</p>

  <p><b>Conclusion:</b> Graphite can act as a lubricant because its
  layers slide over one another relatively easily.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Explain the high melting point of graphite</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> If the layers in graphite slide easily, why does
  graphite still have a very high melting temperature?</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Sliding layers does not require breaking all the
  covalent bonds within those layers.</p>

  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Each layer contains a continuous network of carbon
  atoms joined by strong covalent bonds.</p>

  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Disrupting this giant covalent network requires a
  large amount of energy.</p>

  <p><b>Conclusion:</b> Graphite has a very high melting or sublimation
  temperature because of its strong covalent bonding within the layers,
  even though the layers can slide over one another.</p>

  <h3>Common mistake</h3>

  <p>
    Graphite is not soft because the bonds between carbon atoms
    within a layer are weak. Those covalent bonds are strong.
    Its softness results from the relatively weak attractions
    between the layers.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Graphite', 15),
  (792, 792, '
  <h2>Ionic and Covalent Structures</h2>

  <h3>1. Why compare ionic and covalent substances?</h3>

  <p>
    Ionic and covalent substances have different bonding arrangements.
    Understanding those arrangements helps explain their melting points,
    electrical conductivity and other physical properties.
  </p>

  <p>
    The important rule is to identify the structure first. Do not assume
    that every covalent substance has the same properties: a simple
    molecular substance behaves differently from a giant covalent
    structure.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Ionic bonding and ionic lattices</h3>

  <p>
    Ionic bonding involves electrostatic attraction between oppositely
    charged ions. A typical ionic compound contains positive ions
    (cations) and negative ions (anions).
  </p>

  <p>
    In a solid ionic compound, the ions are arranged in a regular,
    repeating three-dimensional lattice. Each ion is attracted to
    oppositely charged ions around it.
  </p>

  <p>
    The strong electrostatic attractions extend throughout the lattice.
    A large amount of energy is generally needed to separate the ions,
    so ionic compounds usually have high melting points.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Electrical conductivity of ionic compounds</h3>

  <p>
    In a solid ionic compound, the ions are held in fixed positions
    in the lattice. They cannot move freely through the solid, so the
    solid does not normally conduct electricity.
  </p>

  <p>
    When the compound is molten, the lattice has broken down and the
    ions can move. If the compound dissolves in water to produce mobile
    ions, the resulting solution can also conduct electricity.
  </p>

  <p>
    In molten ionic compounds and suitable aqueous solutions, the
    mobile ions carry electrical charge.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Simple molecular covalent substances</h3>

  <p>
    In a simple molecular substance, atoms within each molecule are
    held together by strong covalent bonds. Separate molecules are
    attracted to one another by intermolecular forces.
  </p>

  <p>
    When many simple molecular substances melt or boil, energy is
    mainly needed to overcome the intermolecular attractions between
    molecules. The covalent bonds within the molecules generally remain
    intact.
  </p>

  <p>
    Many simple molecular substances therefore have relatively low
    melting and boiling points compared with ionic compounds and giant
    covalent substances.
  </p>

  <p>
    Their low melting points should not be explained by claiming that
    the covalent bonds inside each molecule are weak. The important
    distinction is between covalent bonds within molecules and
    intermolecular forces between molecules.
  </p>

  <h3>5. Giant covalent structures</h3>

  <p>
    In a giant covalent structure, many atoms are connected by strong
    covalent bonds in a continuous network. There are no separate
    small molecules making up the network.
  </p>

  <p>
    Diamond and graphite are examples of giant covalent forms of carbon.
    Silicon dioxide is another example of a giant covalent substance.
  </p>

  <p>
    Giant covalent substances generally have high melting temperatures
    because large amounts of energy are needed to disrupt their
    extensive covalent networks.
  </p>

  <p>
    Their electrical properties depend on their structures. Diamond
    does not conduct electricity under ordinary conditions because it
    lacks mobile electrons, while graphite conducts because it has
    delocalised electrons.
  </p>

  <h3>6. Compare the structures</h3>

  <table>
    <tr>
      <th>Feature</th>
      <th>Ionic lattice</th>
      <th>Simple molecular</th>
      <th>Giant covalent</th>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Structure</td>
      <td>Positive and negative ions in a lattice</td>
      <td>Separate molecules</td>
      <td>Continuous network of atoms</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Main attraction or bond</td>
      <td>Electrostatic attraction between oppositely charged ions</td>
      <td>Covalent bonds within molecules; intermolecular forces between molecules</td>
      <td>Strong covalent bonds throughout the network</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Melting point</td>
      <td>Usually high</td>
      <td>Often relatively low</td>
      <td>Usually high</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Electrical conduction</td>
      <td>When molten or in a solution with mobile ions; not normally when solid</td>
      <td>Usually does not conduct</td>
      <td>Depends on the structure; graphite conducts, diamond does not</td>
    </tr>
  </table>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Explain the melting point of sodium chloride</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Sodium chloride has a high melting point.
  Explain this using its structure.</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Sodium chloride is an ionic compound containing
  positive sodium ions and negative chloride ions.</p>

  <p><b>Step 2:</b> The ions are arranged in a giant ionic lattice.
  Strong electrostatic attractions act between oppositely charged ions
  throughout the lattice.</p>

  <p><b>Step 3:</b> A large amount of energy is required to overcome
  enough of these attractions for the lattice to melt.</p>

  <p><b>Conclusion:</b> Sodium chloride has a high melting point
  because strong electrostatic attractions hold its oppositely
  charged ions together in a giant lattice.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Explain why solid sodium chloride does not conduct</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Solid sodium chloride does not conduct electricity,
  but molten sodium chloride does. Explain the difference.</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> In solid sodium chloride, positive and negative ions
  occupy fixed positions in the lattice.</p>

  <p><b>Step 2:</b> The ions cannot move freely through the solid to
  carry electrical charge.</p>

  <p><b>Step 3:</b> When sodium chloride melts, the lattice breaks down
  and the ions become mobile.</p>

  <p><b>Conclusion:</b> Solid sodium chloride does not normally conduct
  because its ions are fixed, while molten sodium chloride conducts
  because its mobile ions carry charge.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Compare a simple molecular substance with diamond</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Explain why a simple molecular substance may have
  a low melting point while diamond has an extremely high melting point.</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> A simple molecular substance contains separate
  molecules. When it melts, the energy supplied mainly overcomes
  intermolecular attractions between those molecules.</p>

  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Diamond is a giant covalent network. Its carbon
  atoms are joined by strong covalent bonds throughout the structure.</p>

  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Melting diamond requires disrupting the giant
  covalent network, which requires far more energy than overcoming
  the intermolecular attractions in many simple molecular substances.</p>

  <p><b>Conclusion:</b> Many simple molecular substances have relatively
  low melting points because their intermolecular forces are overcome
  during melting, whereas diamond has an extremely high melting point
  because of its giant covalent network.</p>

  <h3>Common mistakes</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>
      Do not say that solid ionic compounds conduct electricity because
      they contain ions. The ions must be mobile to carry charge.
    </li>
    <li>
      Do not say that the covalent bonds inside simple molecules are
      broken whenever the substance melts. Melting usually overcomes
      intermolecular attractions.
    </li>
    <li>
      Do not claim that all covalent substances have low melting points.
      Giant covalent structures such as diamond have very high melting
      temperatures.
    </li>
    <li>
      Do not claim that all giant covalent substances are electrical
      insulators. Graphite conducts electricity because of its
      delocalised electrons.
    </li>
  </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Ionic and Covalent Structures', 15),
  (793, 793, '
  <h2>Valency</h2>

  <h3>1. Meaning of Valency</h3>

  <p>
    <strong>Valency</strong> is the combining capacity of an atom or
    group of atoms. It tells us the number of bonds an atom can form
    or the number of electrons involved in combining, depending on
    the type of chemical bonding.
  </p>

  <p>
    Valency is useful when determining how atoms combine to form
    compounds. Different elements have different common valencies.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Common Valencies</h3>

  <table>
    <tr>
      <th>Element</th>
      <th>Symbol</th>
      <th>Common valency</th>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Hydrogen</td>
      <td>H</td>
      <td>1</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Sodium</td>
      <td>Na</td>
      <td>1</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Chlorine</td>
      <td>Cl</td>
      <td>1</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Magnesium</td>
      <td>Mg</td>
      <td>2</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Oxygen</td>
      <td>O</td>
      <td>2</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Aluminium</td>
      <td>Al</td>
      <td>3</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Nitrogen</td>
      <td>N</td>
      <td>3</td>
    </tr>
  </table>

  <p>
    These are common valencies used in the examples on this screen.
    Some elements can have more than one valency in different
    compounds, so a listed value should not be assumed to apply
    in every possible compound.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Using Valency to Understand Combining Ratios</h3>

  <p>
    When two elements combine, their atoms combine in a ratio that
    satisfies their combining capacities. For example, an atom with
    valency 1 can combine with an atom of valency 2 in a ratio of
    two atoms to one atom.
  </p>

  <p>
    This relationship helps us determine how many atoms of each
    element are needed when writing a chemical formula.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Identify the valency of magnesium</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Magnesium has a common valency of 2. What does this tell us?</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the given value.</p>
  <p>Valency of magnesium = 2.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Interpret the value.</p>
  <p>
    Magnesium has a combining capacity of 2 in the compounds
    considered here.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Magnesium has valency 2.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Compare sodium and oxygen</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Sodium has valency 1 and oxygen has valency 2. Which has the greater combining capacity?</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Write the two valencies.</p>
  <p>Sodium = 1; oxygen = 2.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Compare the numbers.</p>
  <p>Since 2 is greater than 1, oxygen has the greater numerical valency.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Oxygen has the greater numerical valency.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Determine a combining ratio</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> An element A has valency 2 and element B has valency 1. What is the simplest atom ratio in which they can combine?</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Write the valencies.</p>
  <p>A = 2; B = 1.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Balance the combining capacities.</p>
  <p>
    One atom of A has combining capacity 2. Each atom of B has
    combining capacity 1, so two atoms of B are needed to match
    one atom of A.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> State the ratio.</p>
  <p>A : B = 1 : 2.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The simplest atom ratio is 1 : 2.</p>

  <h3>Remember</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Valency means combining capacity.</li>
    <li>Different elements can have different common valencies.</li>
    <li>Valency helps determine the ratio of atoms in a compound.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Valency', 15),
  (794, 794, '
  <h2>Writing Chemical Formulae</h2>

  <h3>1. What Is a Chemical Formula?</h3>

  <p>
    A <strong>chemical formula</strong> represents a substance using
    chemical symbols and, where necessary, subscripts. It shows which
    elements are present and the relative numbers of their atoms
    represented by the formula.
  </p>

  <p>
    For example, H<sub>2</sub>O contains hydrogen and oxygen. The
    subscript 2 means that two hydrogen atoms are represented for
    every one oxygen atom in a water molecule.
  </p>

  <h3>2. The Basic Method</h3>

  <ol>
    <li>Write the correct chemical symbols for the elements.</li>
    <li>Write their known valencies above or beside the symbols.</li>
    <li>Find the smallest whole-number ratio that balances their combining capacities.</li>
    <li>Write that ratio as subscripts in the formula.</li>
    <li>Do not write subscript 1.</li>
  </ol>

  <p>
    For simple binary compounds in which the valency method applies,
    the lowest common multiple can help determine the atom ratio.
    The final ratio must be expressed using the smallest whole
    numbers.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Worked Example 1: Write the formula formed by hydrogen and oxygen using their common valencies</h3>

  <p><strong>Given:</strong> Hydrogen has valency 1 and oxygen has valency 2.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Write the symbols.</p>
  <p>H and O.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Write the valencies.</p>
  <p>H = 1; O = 2.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Find a balanced ratio.</p>
  <p>
    The lowest common multiple of 1 and 2 is 2. Two hydrogen atoms
    provide a total combining capacity of 2, matching one oxygen
    atom''s combining capacity of 2.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Write the formula.</p>
  <p>Two hydrogen atoms and one oxygen atom give H<sub>2</sub>O.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> H<sub>2</sub>O.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Write the formula formed by magnesium and chlorine using their common valencies</h3>

  <p><strong>Given:</strong> Magnesium has valency 2 and chlorine has valency 1.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Write the symbols.</p>
  <p>Mg and Cl.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Balance the combining capacities.</p>
  <p>
    One magnesium atom has combining capacity 2. Each chlorine atom
    has combining capacity 1, so two chlorine atoms are needed.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Write the formula.</p>
  <p>One Mg and two Cl atoms give MgCl<sub>2</sub>.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> MgCl<sub>2</sub>.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Write the formula formed by aluminium and oxygen using their common valencies</h3>

  <p><strong>Given:</strong> Aluminium has valency 3 and oxygen has valency 2.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Write the symbols and valencies.</p>
  <p>Al = 3; O = 2.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Find a common total.</p>
  <p>
    The lowest common multiple of 3 and 2 is 6.
    Two aluminium atoms provide 2 × 3 = 6.
    Three oxygen atoms provide 3 × 2 = 6.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Write the smallest whole-number ratio.</p>
  <p>Al : O = 2 : 3.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Write the formula.</p>
  <p>Al<sub>2</sub>O<sub>3</sub>.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Al<sub>2</sub>O<sub>3</sub>.</p>

  <h3>4. Important Formula-Writing Rules</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Use the correct element symbols.</li>
    <li>Write subscripts as small numbers after the symbols.</li>
    <li>Do not write subscript 1.</li>
    <li>Use the simplest whole-number ratio.</li>
    <li>
      The valency method is a useful starting method for simple
      compounds, but it must not be applied blindly to every substance.
    </li>
  </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Writing Chemical Formulae', 15),
  (795, 795, '
  <h2>Formulae of Ionic Compounds</h2>

  <h3>1. What Is an Ionic Compound?</h3>

  <p>
    An <strong>ionic compound</strong> consists of positively charged
    ions and negatively charged ions held together by electrostatic
    attraction. Its chemical formula represents the simplest
    whole-number ratio of the ions in the compound.
  </p>

  <p>
    A positive ion is called a cation. A negative ion is called an
    anion. In an ionic compound, the total positive charge and the
    total negative charge balance, so the compound has no overall
    electrical charge.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Writing an Ionic Formula</h3>

  <ol>
    <li>Write the positive ion first and the negative ion second.</li>
    <li>Write each ion''s charge.</li>
    <li>Choose the smallest number of each ion that makes the total charge zero.</li>
    <li>Write those numbers as subscripts.</li>
    <li>Omit a subscript of 1.</li>
  </ol>

  <p>
    The charges help determine the ratio. They are not written as
    subscripts in the final formula.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Sodium chloride</h3>

  <p><strong>Given ions:</strong> Na<sup>+</sup> and Cl<sup>-</sup>.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the charges.</p>
  <p>Sodium ion = +1; chloride ion = -1.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Balance the charges.</p>
  <p>
    One positive charge balances one negative charge:
    (+1) + (-1) = 0.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Write the formula.</p>
  <p>One Na ion and one Cl ion give NaCl.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> NaCl.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Magnesium oxide</h3>

  <p><strong>Given ions:</strong> Mg<sup>2+</sup> and O<sup>2-</sup>.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the charges.</p>
  <p>Magnesium ion = +2; oxide ion = -2.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Balance the charges.</p>
  <p>
    One Mg<sup>2+</sup> ion and one O<sup>2-</sup> ion give
    (+2) + (-2) = 0.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Write the formula.</p>
  <p>MgO.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> MgO, not Mg<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub>.</p>

  <p>
    Although two of each ion would also give a total charge of zero,
    Mg<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> is not the simplest ratio. Ionic
    formulae use the lowest whole-number ratio.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Aluminium oxide</h3>

  <p><strong>Given ions:</strong> Al<sup>3+</sup> and O<sup>2-</sup>.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the charges.</p>
  <p>Aluminium ion = +3; oxide ion = -2.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Find a total charge that can balance.</p>
  <p>
    The smallest common total is 6. Two aluminium ions give
    2 × (+3) = +6. Three oxide ions give 3 × (-2) = -6.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Check the overall charge.</p>
  <p>(+6) + (-6) = 0.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Write the formula.</p>
  <p>Al<sub>2</sub>O<sub>3</sub>.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Al<sub>2</sub>O<sub>3</sub>.</p>

  <h3>3. Why Charge Balance Matters</h3>

  <p>
    An ionic formula must represent an electrically neutral compound.
    If the positive and negative charges do not add to zero, the
    proposed ratio is incorrect.
  </p>

  <p>
    Always check both the charge balance and the simplest whole-number
    ratio before accepting a formula.
  </p>

  <h3>Remember</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Write the positive ion first.</li>
    <li>Balance total positive and negative charges.</li>
    <li>Use the simplest whole-number ratio.</li>
    <li>Do not include ion charges as subscripts in the final formula.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Formulae of Ionic Compounds', 15),
  (796, 796, '
  <h2>Formulae of Covalent Compounds</h2>

  <h3>1. Meaning of a Covalent Formula</h3>

  <p>
    A <strong>covalent compound</strong> contains atoms joined by
    covalent bonds, which form when atoms share pairs of electrons.
    Its chemical formula uses element symbols and subscripts to show
    the types and numbers of atoms in a molecule.
  </p>

  <p>
    Unlike the formula of an ionic compound, which represents the
    simplest ratio of ions, a molecular formula for a covalent
    substance shows the actual number of each type of atom in
    one molecule.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Reading Subscripts in Covalent Formulae</h3>

  <p>
    A subscript is the small number written after an element symbol.
    It tells you how many atoms of that element are represented
    in the molecule.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      H<sub>2</sub>O contains 2 hydrogen atoms and 1 oxygen atom.
    </li>
    <li>
      CO<sub>2</sub> contains 1 carbon atom and 2 oxygen atoms.
    </li>
    <li>
      NH<sub>3</sub> contains 1 nitrogen atom and 3 hydrogen atoms.
    </li>
    <li>
      If an element has no subscript, its number of atoms is 1.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Read each subscript as applying only to the element symbol
    immediately before it. Do not treat a subscript as the total
    number of atoms in the entire molecule.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Writing a Covalent Formula from the Given Atom Numbers</h3>

  <p>
    When the numbers of atoms in a molecule are given, write the
    correct element symbols and place each atom count as a subscript.
    Omit a subscript of 1.
  </p>

  <p>
    Do not automatically use the valency-crossing method to produce
    every covalent molecular formula. The formula must represent the
    actual molecule or the atom numbers specified in the question.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Interpret H2O</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> How many atoms of each element are present in H<sub>2</sub>O?</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the element symbols.</p>
  <p>H represents hydrogen; O represents oxygen.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Read the subscripts.</p>
  <p>
    H has subscript 2, so there are two hydrogen atoms.
    O has no written subscript, so there is one oxygen atom.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Two hydrogen atoms and one oxygen atom, giving 3 atoms in total.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Interpret CO2</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> How many atoms of each element are present in CO<sub>2</sub>?</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the symbols.</p>
  <p>C represents carbon; O represents oxygen.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Count each type of atom.</p>
  <p>
    C has no subscript, so there is one carbon atom.
    O has subscript 2, so there are two oxygen atoms.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Find the total number of atoms.</p>
  <p>1 + 2 = 3 atoms.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> One carbon atom and two oxygen atoms, 3 atoms in total.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Write a formula from atom counts</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> A molecule contains two nitrogen atoms and three hydrogen atoms. Write its formula.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Write the symbols.</p>
  <p>N represents nitrogen; H represents hydrogen.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Add the atom counts as subscripts.</p>
  <p>
    Two nitrogen atoms give N<sub>2</sub>.
    Three hydrogen atoms give H<sub>3</sub>.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Combine the symbols in the stated order.</p>
  <p>N<sub>2</sub>H<sub>3</sub>.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> N<sub>2</sub>H<sub>3</sub> represents the atom counts specified in the question. This exercise constructs a formula from given counts; it does not establish that the species is a stable, ordinary compound.</p>

  <h3>4. Avoiding Common Errors</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>
      Do not omit a subscript when more than one atom of that element
      is present.
    </li>
    <li>
      Do not write a subscript 1. One atom is understood when no
      subscript appears.
    </li>
    <li>
      Do not confuse the number of atoms of one element with the
      total number of atoms in a molecule.
    </li>
    <li>
      Do not change the atom counts in a given molecular formula
      by simplifying its subscripts.
    </li>
    <li>
      Use the actual molecular formula supplied or established for
      the substance, rather than assuming all covalent formulae can
      be generated by crossing valencies.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Remember</h3>

  <p>
    A covalent molecular formula identifies the elements in a molecule
    and shows how many atoms of each element it contains. Read every
    subscript carefully, and remember that a missing subscript means 1.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Formulae of Covalent Compounds', 15),
  (797, 797, '
  <h2>Naming Ionic Compounds</h2>

  <h3>1. The Basic Naming Rule</h3>

  <p>
    An ionic compound consists of positive ions and negative ions.
    To name a simple ionic compound, name the positive ion first,
    followed by the negative ion.
  </p>

  <p>
    For many simple compounds made from a metal and a non-metal,
    the metal keeps its element name, while the non-metal''s name
    changes to end in <strong>-ide</strong>.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>NaCl: sodium chloride</li>
    <li>MgO: magnesium oxide</li>
    <li>CaCl<sub>2</sub>: calcium chloride</li>
    <li>Al<sub>2</sub>O<sub>3</sub>: aluminium oxide</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>2. How to Name a Formula</h3>

  <ol>
    <li>Identify the positive ion from the first element or ion shown.</li>
    <li>Name the positive ion.</li>
    <li>Identify the negative ion.</li>
    <li>Use the appropriate negative-ion name, often ending in -ide for simple monatomic ions.</li>
    <li>Write the positive-ion name first, then the negative-ion name.</li>
  </ol>

  <p>
    The subscripts tell you the ratio of ions in the formula. They
    do not normally change the basic name of a simple ionic compound.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Name NaCl</h3>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the elements.</p>
  <p>Na is sodium; Cl is chlorine.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Name the positive ion.</p>
  <p>Na<sup>+</sup> is the sodium ion, so the first part is sodium.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Name the negative ion.</p>
  <p>Cl<sup>-</sup> is the chloride ion, so the second part is chloride.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Sodium chloride.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Name MgO</h3>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the elements.</p>
  <p>Mg is magnesium; O is oxygen.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Name the positive ion.</p>
  <p>Mg<sup>2+</sup> is the magnesium ion.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Name the negative ion.</p>
  <p>O<sup>2-</sup> is the oxide ion.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Magnesium oxide.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Name CaCl2</h3>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the elements.</p>
  <p>Ca is calcium; Cl is chlorine.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Name the positive ion.</p>
  <p>The positive ion is calcium.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Name the negative ion.</p>
  <p>The negative ion is chloride.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Consider the subscript.</p>
  <p>
    The subscript 2 means that the formula contains two chloride
    ions for each calcium ion. It does not require the word
    "dichloride" in the name of this simple ionic compound.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Calcium chloride.</p>

  <h3>3. Naming and Formulae</h3>

  <p>
    Naming a compound and writing its formula are related but
    different tasks. When naming, identify the ions and use their
    correct names. Do not read the subscripts as prefixes such as
    di- or tri- when naming a simple ionic compound.
  </p>

  <h3>Remember</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Name the positive ion first.</li>
    <li>For simple monatomic negative ions, the name commonly ends in -ide.</li>
    <li>Do not use covalent prefixes to name simple ionic compounds.</li>
    <li>Subscripts describe the ion ratio; they do not automatically become name prefixes.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Naming Ionic Compounds', 15),
  (798, 798, '
  <h2>Naming Covalent Compounds</h2>

  <h3>1. Why Covalent Compounds Use Prefixes</h3>

  <p>
    Many simple binary covalent compounds are named using prefixes
    that indicate how many atoms of each element are represented
    in the molecular formula.
  </p>

  <p>
    A binary compound contains two different elements. For simple
    binary covalent compounds, the prefix system helps distinguish
    formulae that contain different numbers of atoms.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Common Prefixes</h3>

  <table>
    <tr>
      <th>Number of atoms</th>
      <th>Prefix</th>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>1</td>
      <td>mono-</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>2</td>
      <td>di-</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>3</td>
      <td>tri-</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>4</td>
      <td>tetra-</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>5</td>
      <td>penta-</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>6</td>
      <td>hexa-</td>
    </tr>
  </table>

  <h3>3. Basic Naming Rules</h3>

  <ol>
    <li>Name the first element using its element name.</li>
    <li>For the first element, the prefix mono- is usually omitted when there is only one atom.</li>
    <li>Name the second element using its modified form, commonly ending in -ide.</li>
    <li>Use a prefix for the number of atoms of the second element, including mono- when required.</li>
    <li>Use the appropriate prefixes to distinguish different atom counts.</li>
  </ol>

  <p>
    These rules apply to simple binary covalent compounds. Some
    familiar compounds have established names that do not follow
    this prefix pattern, so the prefix method should not be applied
    indiscriminately to every covalent substance.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Name CO2</h3>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the elements.</p>
  <p>C is carbon; O is oxygen.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Count the atoms.</p>
  <p>There is one carbon atom and two oxygen atoms.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Name the first element.</p>
  <p>One carbon atom is named carbon; mono- is omitted for the first element.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Name the second element.</p>
  <p>Two oxygen atoms use the prefix di-, and oxygen becomes oxide.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Carbon dioxide.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Name CO</h3>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the elements.</p>
  <p>C is carbon; O is oxygen.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Count the atoms.</p>
  <p>There is one carbon atom and one oxygen atom.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Name the first element.</p>
  <p>Use carbon; omit mono- for the first element.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Name the second element.</p>
  <p>One oxygen atom uses mono- with oxide, giving monoxide.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Carbon monoxide.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Name N2O3</h3>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the elements.</p>
  <p>N is nitrogen; O is oxygen.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Count the atoms.</p>
  <p>There are two nitrogen atoms and three oxygen atoms.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Name the first element.</p>
  <p>Two nitrogen atoms use di-, giving dinitrogen.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Name the second element.</p>
  <p>Three oxygen atoms use tri- with oxide, giving trioxide.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Dinitrogen trioxide.</p>

  <h3>4. Avoiding Naming Errors</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Read the subscripts to count atoms of each element.</li>
    <li>Do not confuse ionic naming with covalent prefix naming.</li>
    <li>Usually omit mono- for the first element when there is one atom.</li>
    <li>Use the modified name of the second element, such as oxide.</li>
    <li>Check that the prefixes match the formula''s subscripts.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Remember</h3>

  <p>
    Prefixes communicate atom counts in the names of many simple
    binary covalent compounds. The name should match the molecular
    formula, not an assumed ratio.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Naming Covalent Compounds', 15),
  (799, 799, '
  <h2>State Symbols</h2>

  <h3>1. What Are State Symbols?</h3>

  <p>
    <strong>State symbols</strong> are letters written in brackets
    after a chemical formula in an equation. They show the physical
    state of a substance under the stated conditions.
  </p>

  <p>
    State symbols do not change the identity of the substance. They
    provide extra information about whether the substance is solid,
    liquid, gas or dissolved in water.
  </p>

  <h3>2. The Four Common State Symbols</h3>

  <table>
    <tr>
      <th>Symbol</th>
      <th>Meaning</th>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>(s)</td>
      <td>Solid</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>(l)</td>
      <td>Liquid</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>(g)</td>
      <td>Gas</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>(aq)</td>
      <td>Aqueous: dissolved in water</td>
    </tr>
  </table>

  <h3>3. Understanding Each Symbol</h3>

  <p>
    <strong>(s)</strong> means the substance is in the solid state.
    For example, NaCl(s) represents solid sodium chloride.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>(l)</strong> means the substance is in the liquid state.
    For example, H2O(l) represents liquid water.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>(g)</strong> means the substance is in the gaseous state.
    For example, CO2(g) represents carbon dioxide gas.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>(aq)</strong> means the substance is dissolved in water
    to form an aqueous solution. For example, NaCl(aq) represents
    sodium chloride dissolved in water.
  </p>

  <p>
    Aqueous does not simply mean liquid. A liquid substance is itself
    in the liquid state, while an aqueous substance is a substance
    dissolved in water.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Interpret NaCl(s)</h3>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the formula.</p>
  <p>NaCl represents sodium chloride.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Read the state symbol.</p>
  <p>The symbol (s) means solid.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> NaCl(s) means solid sodium chloride.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Interpret H2O(l)</h3>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the formula.</p>
  <p>H2O represents water.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Read the state symbol.</p>
  <p>The symbol (l) means liquid.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> H2O(l) means liquid water.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Distinguish NaCl(l) from NaCl(aq)</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> What is the difference between NaCl(l) and NaCl(aq)?</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Interpret (l).</p>
  <p>NaCl(l) represents sodium chloride in the liquid state.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Interpret (aq).</p>
  <p>NaCl(aq) represents sodium chloride dissolved in water.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> State the difference.</p>
  <p>
    The first indicates the physical state of the substance itself.
    The second indicates that it is dissolved in water.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> (l) means liquid; (aq) means dissolved in water.</p>

  <h3>4. How State Symbols Are Written</h3>

  <p>
    Write a state symbol in brackets immediately after the chemical
    formula, for example H2O(l) or CO2(g). The formula identifies
    the substance, while the state symbol describes its physical
    state under the stated conditions.
  </p>

  <p>
    A substance''s state can depend on conditions such as temperature
    and pressure. Use the state given in a question or equation
    rather than assuming a state without sufficient information.
  </p>

  <h3>Remember</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>(s) = solid.</li>
    <li>(l) = liquid.</li>
    <li>(g) = gas.</li>
    <li>(aq) = dissolved in water.</li>
    <li>Aqueous and liquid do not mean the same thing.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for State Symbols', 15),
  (800, 800, '<h2>Meaning of Mole</h2>

  <h3>1. Why chemists need the mole</h3>

  <p>Chemical reactions involve particles that are far too small and numerous to count individually. A tiny sample of a substance can contain billions of billions of atoms or molecules.</p>

  <p>For example, when a chemist measures 18 g of water, the sample contains an enormous number of water molecules. Counting every molecule separately would be impractical. Chemists therefore use a counting unit called the <strong>mole</strong>.</p>

  <p>The mole works in chemistry in the same way that a dozen works in ordinary counting. A dozen means 12 items, regardless of whether the items are eggs, pencils or books. Similarly, one mole means a fixed number of specified particles, regardless of the substance.</p>

  <h3>2. Definition of a mole</h3>

  <p>One mole of a substance contains exactly <strong>6.02214076 × 10²³ specified elementary entities</strong>. In many school calculations, this is rounded to <strong>6.02 × 10²³</strong>.</p>

  <p>This number is called the <strong>Avogadro constant</strong>, represented by Nₐ.</p>

  <p>Nₐ = 6.02 × 10²³ mol⁻¹ (school approximation)</p>

  <p>The symbol mol is the unit for amount of substance. The number of particles in one mole is fixed; the mass of one mole depends on which substance is being considered.</p>

  <h3>3. What counts as a particle?</h3>

  <p>The word particle must be interpreted using the substance and the question.</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Atoms:</strong> one mole of helium atoms contains 6.02 × 10²³ helium atoms.</li>
    <li><strong>Molecules:</strong> one mole of water molecules contains 6.02 × 10²³ water molecules.</li>
    <li><strong>Ions:</strong> one mole of sodium ions contains 6.02 × 10²³ sodium ions.</li>
    <li><strong>Formula units:</strong> one mole of sodium chloride contains 6.02 × 10²³ formula units of NaCl.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>For ionic compounds such as sodium chloride, it is more accurate to say <em>formula units</em> rather than molecules. Solid sodium chloride is made of an extended arrangement of Na⁺ and Cl⁻ ions, not separate NaCl molecules.</p>

  <h3>4. One mole does not mean one gram</h3>

  <p>A mole measures the <strong>amount of substance</strong>; a gram measures <strong>mass</strong>. They are different quantities.</p>

  <p>One mole of carbon atoms has a mass of approximately 12 g, while one mole of water molecules has a mass of approximately 18 g. Both samples contain the same number of specified particles, but their masses differ because their particles have different masses.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Identifying the particles</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> What does 1 mol of oxygen molecules, O₂, contain?</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1: Identify the entity.</strong> The formula O₂ represents a molecule made of two oxygen atoms. The question asks for oxygen molecules, not individual oxygen atoms.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2: Recall the definition.</strong> One mole contains 6.02 × 10²³ specified entities.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3: State the correct entity.</strong> There are 6.02 × 10²³ oxygen molecules.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 6.02 × 10²³ O₂ molecules.</p>

  <p><strong>Important distinction:</strong> Since every O₂ molecule contains two oxygen atoms, this sample contains 2 × 6.02 × 10²³ = 1.204 × 10²⁴ oxygen atoms.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Comparing two substances</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Which contains more particles: 1 mol of helium atoms or 1 mol of water molecules?</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> One mole of helium contains 6.02 × 10²³ helium atoms.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> One mole of water contains 6.02 × 10²³ water molecules.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Compare the counts. Both have the same number of specified entities.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> They contain equal numbers of specified particles, 6.02 × 10²³ each. This does not mean they have equal masses.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Distinguishing amount from mass</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> A learner says, “One mole of every substance has a mass of one gram.” Explain why this is incorrect.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> A mole is a unit of amount of substance, not a unit of mass.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> One mole of carbon atoms has a mass of approximately 12 g.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> One mole of water molecules has a mass of approximately 18 g.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 4:</strong> The masses differ because carbon atoms and water molecules have different masses.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> One mole always contains the same number of specified entities, but its mass depends on the substance.</p>

  <h3>Common mistakes to avoid</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Do not define a mole as a mass of 1 g.</li>
    <li>Do not confuse the number of molecules with the number of atoms inside those molecules.</li>
    <li>Always state whether you are counting atoms, molecules, ions or formula units.</li>
    <li>Use 6.02 × 10²³ for school calculations unless the question supplies a different value or requires greater precision.</li>
  </ul>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Meaning of Mole', 15),
  (801, 801, '<h2>Avogadro Constant</h2>

  <h3>1. Meaning of the Avogadro constant</h3>

  <p>The <strong>Avogadro constant</strong>, represented by Nₐ, is the number of specified elementary entities in one mole.</p>

  <p>For school calculations, use:</p>

  <p><strong>Nₐ = 6.02 × 10²³ mol⁻¹</strong></p>

  <p>The unit mol⁻¹ means “per mole”. The constant connects the amount of substance, measured in moles, to the number of entities present.</p>

  <h3>2. Calculating particles from moles</h3>

  <p>If one mole contains 6.02 × 10²³ particles, then several moles contain the corresponding multiple of that number.</p>

  <p><strong>N = n × Nₐ</strong></p>

  <ul>
    <li>N = number of specified particles</li>
    <li>n = amount of substance in mol</li>
    <li>Nₐ = Avogadro constant</li>
  </ul>

  <p><strong>Example of the reasoning:</strong> 2 mol contains twice the number of particles in 1 mol, so the number of particles is 2 × 6.02 × 10²³ = 1.204 × 10²⁴.</p>

  <h3>3. Calculating moles from particles</h3>

  <p>If the number of particles is known, divide it by the number of particles in one mole.</p>

  <p><strong>n = N ÷ Nₐ</strong></p>

  <p>For example, 3.01 × 10²³ particles represent half a mole because this number is half of 6.02 × 10²³.</p>

  <h3>4. Handling standard form correctly</h3>

  <p>Avogadro calculations often use numbers written in standard form, such as 3.01 × 10²³.</p>

  <p>When multiplying 0.5 by 6.02 × 10²³, calculate the ordinary numbers first:</p>

  <p>0.5 × 6.02 = 3.01</p>

  <p>The power of ten remains unchanged, giving 3.01 × 10²³.</p>

  <p>When dividing numbers with the same power of ten, the powers cancel:</p>

  <p>(3.01 × 10²³) ÷ (6.02 × 10²³) = 3.01 ÷ 6.02 = 0.5 mol.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Finding particles from moles</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Calculate the number of molecules in 2.5 mol of carbon dioxide.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1: Identify what is required.</strong> The question asks for the number of CO₂ molecules, so the final unit is molecules.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2: Write the relationship.</strong></p>

  <p>N = n × Nₐ</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3: Substitute the values.</strong></p>

  <p>N = 2.5 × 6.02 × 10²³</p>

  <p><strong>Step 4: Multiply.</strong></p>

  <p>2.5 × 6.02 = 15.05</p>

  <p>N = 15.05 × 10²³</p>

  <p><strong>Step 5: Express in standard form.</strong></p>

  <p>15.05 × 10²³ = 1.505 × 10²⁴</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 1.505 × 10²⁴ CO₂ molecules.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Finding moles from particles</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Calculate the amount of substance represented by 9.03 × 10²³ atoms of an element.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Use n = N ÷ Nₐ.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Substitute the values.</p>

  <p>n = (9.03 × 10²³) ÷ (6.02 × 10²³)</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Cancel the equal powers of ten.</p>

  <p>n = 9.03 ÷ 6.02</p>

  <p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Divide.</p>

  <p>n = 1.5 mol</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 1.5 mol of atoms.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Finding particles from a small amount</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Calculate the number of molecules in 0.025 mol of a gas.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Use N = n × Nₐ.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Substitute n = 0.025 mol.</p>

  <p>N = 0.025 × 6.02 × 10²³</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Multiply.</p>

  <p>0.025 × 6.02 = 0.1505</p>

  <p>N = 0.1505 × 10²³</p>

  <p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Convert to standard form.</p>

  <p>0.1505 × 10²³ = 1.505 × 10²²</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 1.505 × 10²² molecules.</p>

  <h3>How to choose the correct formula</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>If moles are given and particles are required, multiply: N = n × Nₐ.</li>
    <li>If particles are given and moles are required, divide: n = N ÷ Nₐ.</li>
    <li>Write the particle type in the final answer.</li>
    <li>Check whether the result is reasonable: more moles must mean more particles.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Common mistakes</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Dividing when you should multiply.</li>
    <li>Multiplying when you should divide.</li>
    <li>Dropping the power of ten.</li>
    <li>Writing “particles” when the question specifically asks for molecules, atoms or ions.</li>
    <li>Giving an amount in mol when the question asks for a number of particles.</li>
  </ul>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Avogadro Constant', 15),
  (802, 802, '<h2>Relative Atomic Mass</h2>

  <h3>1. Why relative atomic mass is needed</h3>

  <p>Atoms are extremely small, so their actual masses are inconvenient to use in ordinary chemical calculations. Chemists therefore compare atomic masses using a standard reference and use relative values.</p>

  <p><strong>Relative atomic mass</strong>, represented by Aᵣ, is the weighted mean mass of the atoms of an element compared with one-twelfth of the mass of a carbon-12 atom.</p>

  <p>Relative atomic mass has no unit because it is a ratio of masses.</p>

  <h3>2. Common relative atomic masses</h3>

  <p>Use the values provided by the question or the periodic table. Common school values include:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>H = 1</li>
    <li>C = 12</li>
    <li>N = 14</li>
    <li>O = 16</li>
    <li>Na = 23</li>
    <li>Mg = 24</li>
    <li>S = 32</li>
    <li>Cl = 35.5</li>
    <li>Ca = 40</li>
    <li>Fe = 56</li>
  </ul>

  <p>These are commonly rounded school values. If a question gives a value, use that value consistently rather than silently substituting another.</p>

  <h3>3. Why chlorine has a relative atomic mass of about 35.5</h3>

  <p>Many elements exist naturally as mixtures of isotopes. Isotopes are atoms of the same element with the same number of protons but different numbers of neutrons.</p>

  <p>Chlorine mainly contains chlorine-35 and chlorine-37. Its relative atomic mass is a weighted average based on the natural abundances of these isotopes. Consequently, the value is approximately 35.5 rather than a whole number.</p>

  <p>Relative atomic mass is therefore not always the mass number of one particular atom.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Selecting the correct value</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> What relative atomic mass should be used for oxygen when calculating the relative formula mass of water, H₂O?</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the element: oxygen, O.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Use the supplied periodic-table value or the common school value Aᵣ(O) = 16.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 16, with no unit.</p>

  <p><strong>Why this matters:</strong> The value will later be added to the contribution from the two hydrogen atoms when calculating the relative formula mass of water.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Using relative atomic mass in a formula</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Find the total relative mass contribution from the chlorine atoms in CaCl₂. Use Aᵣ(Cl) = 35.5.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Read the formula. CaCl₂ contains two chlorine atoms.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Each chlorine atom contributes 35.5.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Multiply the value by the number of chlorine atoms.</p>

  <p>Chlorine contribution = 2 × 35.5 = 71.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 71 relative mass units in the formula-mass sum. The complete relative formula mass of CaCl₂ also includes calcium.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Understanding a fractional value</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> A learner argues that chlorine cannot have a relative atomic mass of 35.5 because an individual atom cannot have half a neutron. Explain the mistake.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> A single chlorine atom has a particular isotope and a whole-number mass number, such as 35 or 37.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> A natural sample contains a mixture of chlorine isotopes.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> The relative atomic mass represents a weighted average of the isotopes in the sample.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 4:</strong> An average can be fractional even when the individual mass numbers are whole numbers.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Chlorine''s relative atomic mass is a weighted average of naturally occurring isotopes, not the neutron count of a single atom.</p>

  <h3>4. Relative atomic mass versus mass number</h3>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Mass number:</strong> the total number of protons and neutrons in one particular atom; it is a whole number.</li>
    <li><strong>Relative atomic mass:</strong> the weighted mean atomic mass relative to the carbon-12 standard; it may be fractional.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Common mistakes</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Do not attach grams or g mol⁻¹ to a relative atomic mass; it has no unit.</li>
    <li>Do not confuse relative atomic mass with mass number.</li>
    <li>Do not forget that a subscript tells you how many atoms contribute to a formula-mass calculation.</li>
    <li>Use the values supplied in the question when they differ from rounded reference values.</li>
  </ul>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Relative Atomic Mass', 15),
  (803, 803, '<h2>Relative Formula Mass</h2>

  <h3>1. What is relative formula mass?</h3>

  <p>The <strong>relative formula mass</strong>, represented by Mᵣ, is the sum of the relative atomic masses of all the atoms represented in a chemical formula.</p>

  <p>A chemical formula tells us which elements are present and how many atoms of each element must be counted. The small number written after an element symbol is called a subscript. It tells us how many atoms of that element occur in the formula.</p>

  <p>For example, H₂O contains two hydrogen atoms and one oxygen atom. Its relative formula mass is therefore calculated by adding the contribution of two hydrogen atoms to the contribution of one oxygen atom.</p>

  <p><strong>General rule:</strong></p>

  <p>Mᵣ = sum of (number of atoms of each element × its Aᵣ)</p>

  <p>Relative formula mass has no unit because relative atomic masses are dimensionless.</p>

  <h3>2. Read the formula before calculating</h3>

  <p>Before doing any arithmetic, count the atoms correctly.</p>

  <ul>
    <li>NaCl contains 1 Na atom and 1 Cl atom.</li>
    <li>H₂O contains 2 H atoms and 1 O atom.</li>
    <li>CO₂ contains 1 C atom and 2 O atoms.</li>
    <li>Ca(OH)₂ contains 1 Ca atom, 2 O atoms and 2 H atoms.</li>
    <li>Al₂(SO₄)₃ contains 2 Al atoms, 3 S atoms and 12 O atoms.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>A subscript outside brackets multiplies every atom inside those brackets. This is a frequent source of mistakes in formula-mass calculations.</p>

  <h3>3. Relative atomic masses used in these examples</h3>

  <p>Unless a question gives different values, use:</p>

  <p>H = 1, C = 12, N = 14, O = 16, Na = 23, Mg = 24, Al = 27, S = 32, Cl = 35.5, Ca = 40.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Water, H₂O</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Calculate the relative formula mass of water. Use H = 1 and O = 16.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1: Count the atoms.</strong> H₂O contains two hydrogen atoms and one oxygen atom.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2: Calculate each contribution.</strong></p>

  <p>Hydrogen contribution = 2 × 1 = 2.</p>

  <p>Oxygen contribution = 1 × 16 = 16.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3: Add the contributions.</strong></p>

  <p>Mᵣ(H₂O) = 2 + 16 = 18.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Mᵣ(H₂O) = 18.</p>

  <p><strong>Check:</strong> The hydrogen contribution must be counted twice because the formula contains H₂, not H.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Calcium hydroxide, Ca(OH)₂</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Calculate Mᵣ of Ca(OH)₂. Use Ca = 40, O = 16 and H = 1.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1: Identify the bracket.</strong> The subscript 2 outside (OH) means there are two O atoms and two H atoms.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2: Calculate calcium.</strong> Calcium contribution = 1 × 40 = 40.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3: Calculate oxygen.</strong> Oxygen contribution = 2 × 16 = 32.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 4: Calculate hydrogen.</strong> Hydrogen contribution = 2 × 1 = 2.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 5: Add all contributions.</strong></p>

  <p>Mᵣ = 40 + 32 + 2 = 74.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Mᵣ[Ca(OH)₂] = 74.</p>

  <p><strong>Check:</strong> Do not calculate Ca + O + H only once. The bracket subscript doubles both O and H.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Aluminium sulfate, Al₂(SO₄)₃</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Calculate Mᵣ of Al₂(SO₄)₃. Use Al = 27, S = 32 and O = 16.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1: Count the atoms.</strong> There are 2 Al atoms, 3 S atoms and 4 × 3 = 12 O atoms.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2: Calculate aluminium.</strong></p>

  <p>2 × 27 = 54.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3: Calculate sulfur.</strong></p>

  <p>3 × 32 = 96.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 4: Calculate oxygen.</strong></p>

  <p>12 × 16 = 192.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 5: Add the contributions.</strong></p>

  <p>Mᵣ = 54 + 96 + 192 = 342.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Mᵣ[Al₂(SO₄)₃] = 342.</p>

  <h3>4. A reliable method for every formula</h3>

  <ol>
    <li>Write the formula clearly.</li>
    <li>Count each type of atom, including atoms inside brackets.</li>
    <li>Multiply each atom count by its relative atomic mass.</li>
    <li>Add the contributions.</li>
    <li>Check that no subscript or bracket multiplier was ignored.</li>
    <li>Give Mᵣ without a unit.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Common mistakes</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Ignoring a subscript such as the 2 in CO₂.</li>
    <li>Applying a bracket subscript to only the first element inside the brackets.</li>
    <li>Writing g mol⁻¹ for relative formula mass. That unit belongs to molar mass.</li>
    <li>Adding the atomic masses without multiplying them by the number of atoms.</li>
  </ul>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Relative Formula Mass', 15),
  (804, 804, '<h2>Molar Mass</h2>

  <h3>1. Meaning of molar mass</h3>

  <p><strong>Molar mass</strong> is the mass of one mole of a substance. It is represented by M and is commonly expressed in grams per mole, g mol⁻¹.</p>

  <p>Relative formula mass and molar mass are closely related, but they are not identical quantities:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Relative formula mass, Mᵣ:</strong> a dimensionless relative value obtained by adding relative atomic masses.</li>
    <li><strong>Molar mass, M:</strong> the mass of one mole of a substance, expressed in g mol⁻¹.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>For a substance with a formula mass of 18, its molar mass is 18 g mol⁻¹. This means one mole of that substance has a mass of 18 g.</p>

  <h3>2. From formula to molar mass</h3>

  <p>To calculate molar mass:</p>

  <ol>
    <li>Write the chemical formula.</li>
    <li>Calculate the relative formula mass using the supplied relative atomic masses.</li>
    <li>Use the same numerical value as the molar mass in g mol⁻¹.</li>
  </ol>

  <p><strong>Example:</strong> For H₂O, Mᵣ = (2 × 1) + 16 = 18. Therefore, M = 18 g mol⁻¹.</p>

  <h3>3. Why molar mass matters</h3>

  <p>A balance measures mass, but chemical equations and mole calculations use amounts in moles. Molar mass connects these two quantities.</p>

  <p>Once the molar mass is known, you can calculate the number of moles in a measured mass, or calculate the mass needed for a required amount of substance.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Molar mass of water</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Calculate the molar mass of H₂O. Use H = 1 and O = 16.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Count the atoms: two H atoms and one O atom.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Calculate the relative formula mass.</p>

  <p>Mᵣ = (2 × 1) + 16 = 18.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Express the same numerical value as molar mass.</p>

  <p>M = 18 g mol⁻¹.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 18 g mol⁻¹. One mole of water has a mass of 18 g.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Molar mass of calcium carbonate</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Calculate the molar mass of CaCO₃. Use Ca = 40, C = 12 and O = 16.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> CaCO₃ contains one Ca, one C and three O atoms.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Calculate the contributions.</p>

  <p>Ca = 1 × 40 = 40.</p>

  <p>C = 1 × 12 = 12.</p>

  <p>O = 3 × 16 = 48.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Add the contributions.</p>

  <p>Mᵣ = 40 + 12 + 48 = 100.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Give the molar mass with the correct unit.</p>

  <p>M = 100 g mol⁻¹.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 100 g mol⁻¹.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Molar mass of aluminium sulfate</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Calculate the molar mass of Al₂(SO₄)₃. Use Al = 27, S = 32 and O = 16.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Count the atoms: 2 Al, 3 S and 12 O.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Aluminium contribution = 2 × 27 = 54.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Sulfur contribution = 3 × 32 = 96.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Oxygen contribution = 12 × 16 = 192.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 5:</strong> Add all contributions.</p>

  <p>Mᵣ = 54 + 96 + 192 = 342.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 6:</strong> Add the molar-mass unit.</p>

  <p>M = 342 g mol⁻¹.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 342 g mol⁻¹.</p>

  <h3>4. Understanding the units</h3>

  <p>The unit g mol⁻¹ means grams per mole. If a substance has a molar mass of 44 g mol⁻¹, each mole of the substance has a mass of 44 g.</p>

  <p>This does not mean every sample has a mass of 44 g. A sample containing half a mole has half that mass, while a sample containing two moles has twice that mass.</p>

  <h3>Common mistakes</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Giving relative formula mass a unit.</li>
    <li>Giving molar mass without a unit.</li>
    <li>Confusing the mass of one mole with the mass of any arbitrary sample.</li>
    <li>Failing to multiply atomic masses by the subscripts in a formula.</li>
  </ul>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Molar Mass', 15),
  (805, 805, '<h2>Calculating Moles from Mass</h2>

  <h3>1. The relationship between mass and moles</h3>

  <p>In a laboratory, we often measure a sample using a balance. However, chemical equations describe reacting amounts in moles. We therefore need a method for converting a measured mass into an amount of substance.</p>

  <p>The relationship is:</p>

  <p><strong>n = m ÷ M</strong></p>

  <ul>
    <li>n = amount of substance in mol</li>
    <li>m = mass of the sample in g</li>
    <li>M = molar mass in g mol⁻¹</li>
  </ul>

  <p><strong>In words:</strong> number of moles = mass of the sample divided by the mass of one mole.</p>

  <h3>2. Why division works</h3>

  <p>Suppose one mole of a substance has a mass of 18 g. A sample of 36 g contains two groups of 18 g. Each group represents one mole, so the sample contains 2 mol.</p>

  <p>If the sample has a mass of 9 g, it contains half of 18 g, so it represents 0.5 mol.</p>

  <p>Dividing the sample mass by the molar mass tells us how many one-mole portions are present.</p>

  <h3>3. Choose the correct molar mass first</h3>

  <p>Before using n = m ÷ M, calculate or identify the molar mass of the substance. If the substance is H₂O, use 18 g mol⁻¹. If it is CO₂, use 44 g mol⁻¹. Do not use the mass of another compound.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Moles in 36 g of water</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Calculate the amount of substance in 36 g of H₂O. Use H = 1 and O = 16.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1: Find the molar mass.</strong></p>

  <p>M(H₂O) = (2 × 1) + 16 = 18 g mol⁻¹.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2: Write the formula.</strong></p>

  <p>n = m ÷ M</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3: Substitute the values.</strong></p>

  <p>n = 36 g ÷ 18 g mol⁻¹</p>

  <p><strong>Step 4: Calculate.</strong></p>

  <p>n = 2 mol.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 2 mol of water.</p>

  <p><strong>Check:</strong> Since 36 g is twice 18 g, the sample must contain twice one mole.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Moles in 11 g of carbon dioxide</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Calculate the amount of CO₂ in 11 g. Use C = 12 and O = 16.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1: Calculate molar mass.</strong></p>

  <p>M(CO₂) = 12 + (2 × 16) = 44 g mol⁻¹.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2: Use n = m ÷ M.</strong></p>

  <p>n = 11 ÷ 44</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3: Divide.</strong></p>

  <p>n = 0.25 mol.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 0.25 mol of CO₂.</p>

  <p><strong>Check:</strong> 11 g is one-quarter of 44 g, so the amount is one-quarter of a mole.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Moles in 12.5 g of calcium carbonate</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Calculate the amount of CaCO₃ in 12.5 g. Use Ca = 40, C = 12 and O = 16.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1: Calculate the molar mass.</strong></p>

  <p>M(CaCO₃) = 40 + 12 + (3 × 16).</p>

  <p>M(CaCO₃) = 40 + 12 + 48 = 100 g mol⁻¹.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2: Use the mole formula.</strong></p>

  <p>n = m ÷ M</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3: Substitute.</strong></p>

  <p>n = 12.5 ÷ 100</p>

  <p><strong>Step 4: Calculate.</strong></p>

  <p>n = 0.125 mol.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 0.125 mol of CaCO₃.</p>

  <h3>4. Unit check</h3>

  <p>Mass is measured in grams, and molar mass is measured in grams per mole:</p>

  <p>n = g ÷ (g mol⁻¹) = mol.</p>

  <p>The grams cancel, leaving moles. This confirms that the formula produces the correct unit.</p>

  <h3>Common mistakes</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Multiplying mass by molar mass instead of dividing.</li>
    <li>Using Mᵣ without first expressing the molar mass in g mol⁻¹.</li>
    <li>Forgetting to calculate the molar mass from the chemical formula.</li>
    <li>Reporting the answer in grams when the question asks for moles.</li>
    <li>Ignoring the units or using a molar mass belonging to a different substance.</li>
  </ul>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Calculating Moles from Mass', 15),
  (806, 806, '<h2>Calculating Mass from Moles</h2>

  <h3>1. The relationship between mass, moles and molar mass</h3>

  <p>Sometimes the amount of substance is known, but the mass is required. For example, a laboratory procedure may require 0.25 mol of a substance, and the learner must calculate how many grams to weigh.</p>

  <p>The relationship is:</p>

  <p><strong>m = n × M</strong></p>

  <ul>
    <li>m = mass in g</li>
    <li>n = amount of substance in mol</li>
    <li>M = molar mass in g mol⁻¹</li>
  </ul>

  <p><strong>In words:</strong> mass = amount in moles × mass per mole.</p>

  <h3>2. Why multiplication works</h3>

  <p>If one mole of a substance has a mass of 18 g, then two moles have a mass of 2 × 18 = 36 g.</p>

  <p>Likewise, half a mole has a mass of 0.5 × 18 = 9 g.</p>

  <p>The amount in moles tells us how many one-mole portions we have. Multiplying that amount by the mass of each mole gives the total mass.</p>

  <h3>3. Choose the correct molar mass</h3>

  <p>Always identify the substance and calculate its molar mass before substituting into the equation. Do not use the molar mass of a different substance just because it appears in a previous question.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Mass of 3 mol of water</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Calculate the mass of 3 mol of H₂O. Use H = 1 and O = 16.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1: Calculate molar mass.</strong></p>

  <p>M(H₂O) = (2 × 1) + 16 = 18 g mol⁻¹.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2: Write the formula.</strong></p>

  <p>m = n × M</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3: Substitute.</strong></p>

  <p>m = 3 × 18</p>

  <p><strong>Step 4: Calculate.</strong></p>

  <p>m = 54 g.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 54 g of water.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Mass of 0.25 mol of carbon dioxide</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Calculate the mass of 0.25 mol of CO₂. Use C = 12 and O = 16.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Calculate M(CO₂) = 12 + (2 × 16) = 44 g mol⁻¹.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Use m = n × M.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Substitute m = 0.25 × 44.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Calculate m = 11 g.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 11 g of CO₂.</p>

  <p><strong>Check:</strong> One-quarter of 44 g is 11 g, so the result is reasonable.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Mass of 1.5 mol of calcium carbonate</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Calculate the mass of 1.5 mol of CaCO₃. Use Ca = 40, C = 12 and O = 16.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> M(CaCO₃) = 40 + 12 + (3 × 16) = 100 g mol⁻¹.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Use m = n × M.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Substitute m = 1.5 × 100.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Calculate m = 150 g.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 150 g of CaCO₃.</p>

  <h3>4. Checking the units</h3>

  <p>Mass = mol × g mol⁻¹.</p>

  <p>The mol units cancel, leaving grams. Therefore, the final answer must be a mass in grams when the molar mass is given in g mol⁻¹.</p>

  <h3>5. Connecting both mass–mole formulas</h3>

  <p>The two formulas describe the same relationship:</p>

  <p><strong>n = m ÷ M</strong></p>

  <p><strong>m = n × M</strong></p>

  <p>Use division when finding moles from mass. Use multiplication when finding mass from moles.</p>

  <h3>Common mistakes</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Dividing by molar mass when the mass is required.</li>
    <li>Using relative formula mass without the molar-mass unit.</li>
    <li>Using the wrong chemical formula to calculate molar mass.</li>
    <li>Reporting a mass in moles or an amount in grams.</li>
  </ul>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Calculating Mass from Moles', 15),
  (807, 807, '<h2>Moles and Particles</h2>

  <h3>1. Connecting amount of substance to particle count</h3>

  <p>A mole is a counting unit. One mole contains the Avogadro constant number of specified entities:</p>

  <p><strong>Nₐ = 6.02 × 10²³ mol⁻¹</strong></p>

  <p>This allows us to convert between the amount of substance and the number of particles.</p>

  <p><strong>Number of particles:</strong></p>

  <p>N = n × Nₐ</p>

  <p><strong>Amount in moles:</strong></p>

  <p>n = N ÷ Nₐ</p>

  <ul>
    <li>N = number of specified particles</li>
    <li>n = amount of substance in mol</li>
    <li>Nₐ = Avogadro constant</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>2. Always identify the particle type</h3>

  <p>Before calculating, read the formula and question carefully.</p>

  <ul>
    <li>For He, the entities are helium atoms.</li>
    <li>For H₂, the entities are hydrogen molecules.</li>
    <li>For H₂O, the entities are water molecules.</li>
    <li>For Na⁺, the entities are sodium ions.</li>
    <li>For NaCl, the entities are formula units in the ionic solid.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Counting molecules is not always the same as counting atoms. A molecule may contain several atoms, so the total atom count can be greater than the molecule count.</p>

  <h3>3. Atoms inside molecules</h3>

  <p>Consider one mole of O₂ molecules. It contains 6.02 × 10²³ O₂ molecules. Since every O₂ molecule contains two oxygen atoms, the total number of oxygen atoms is:</p>

  <p>2 × 6.02 × 10²³ = 1.204 × 10²⁴ oxygen atoms.</p>

  <p>For one mole of H₂O molecules, each molecule contains three atoms in total: two H atoms and one O atom. Therefore, one mole of water molecules contains 2 mol of hydrogen atoms and 1 mol of oxygen atoms.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Molecules in 2 mol of CO₂</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Calculate the number of molecules in 2 mol of carbon dioxide.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> The specified entities are CO₂ molecules.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Use N = n × Nₐ.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Substitute N = 2 × 6.02 × 10²³.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Calculate N = 12.04 × 10²³.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 5:</strong> Convert to standard form: N = 1.204 × 10²⁴.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 1.204 × 10²⁴ CO₂ molecules.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Atoms in 0.5 mol of oxygen gas</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Calculate the number of oxygen atoms in 0.5 mol of O₂ molecules.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> First calculate the number of O₂ molecules.</p>

  <p>N(molecules) = 0.5 × 6.02 × 10²³.</p>

  <p>N(molecules) = 3.01 × 10²³.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Each O₂ molecule contains two oxygen atoms.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Multiply the number of molecules by two.</p>

  <p>N(atoms) = 2 × 3.01 × 10²³.</p>

  <p>N(atoms) = 6.02 × 10²³.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 6.02 × 10²³ oxygen atoms.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Finding moles from a particle count</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> A sample contains 1.505 × 10²³ molecules of a substance. Calculate the amount in moles.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Use n = N ÷ Nₐ.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Substitute n = (1.505 × 10²³) ÷ (6.02 × 10²³).</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Cancel the equal powers of ten.</p>

  <p>n = 1.505 ÷ 6.02.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Calculate n = 0.25 mol.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 0.25 mol.</p>

  <h3>4. A reliable method</h3>

  <ol>
    <li>Identify the entity being counted.</li>
    <li>Identify whether the question gives moles or a particle count.</li>
    <li>Use N = n × Nₐ when converting moles to particles.</li>
    <li>Use n = N ÷ Nₐ when converting particles to moles.</li>
    <li>If the question asks for atoms within molecules, use the formula''s subscripts to count the atoms per molecule.</li>
    <li>Include the correct particle type in the final answer.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Common mistakes</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Reporting molecules when the question asks for atoms.</li>
    <li>Forgetting that O₂ has two oxygen atoms per molecule.</li>
    <li>Dividing by Avogadro''s constant when the number of particles is required from moles.</li>
    <li>Multiplying by Avogadro''s constant when the amount in moles is required from particles.</li>
    <li>Ignoring powers of ten when dividing or multiplying standard-form numbers.</li>
  </ul>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Moles and Particles', 15),
  (808, 808, '
  <h2>Chemical Reactions</h2>

  <h3>What Is a Chemical Reaction?</h3>

  <p>A <strong>chemical reaction</strong> is a process in which one or more substances change into new substances with different chemical compositions and properties.</p>

  <p>During a chemical reaction, the atoms in the starting substances are rearranged to form new substances. In an ordinary chemical reaction, atoms are not created or destroyed; chemical bonds break and new bonds form.</p>

  <h3>Physical Changes and Chemical Changes</h3>

  <p>A <strong>physical change</strong> changes a substance''s form, size or state without producing a new substance. For example, ice melting produces liquid water.</p>

  <p>A <strong>chemical change</strong> produces one or more new substances. For example, burning magnesium produces magnesium oxide.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Burning Magnesium</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Magnesium burns in oxygen to form magnesium oxide. Explain why this is a chemical reaction.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the starting substances: magnesium and oxygen.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Identify the substance formed: magnesium oxide.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Compare the substances. Magnesium oxide has a different chemical composition and different properties from magnesium and oxygen.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> A new substance, magnesium oxide, forms when magnesium reacts with oxygen. Therefore, the process is a chemical reaction.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Melting Ice</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Is melting ice a chemical reaction?</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the substance before the change: solid water (ice).</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Identify the substance after the change: liquid water.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Both forms are water, H₂O. Only the physical state changes; no new substance forms.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> No. Melting ice is a physical change, not a chemical reaction.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Formation of Water</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Hydrogen reacts with oxygen to form water. Explain what happens to the atoms.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Hydrogen and oxygen are the starting substances.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Their atoms rearrange as chemical bonds break and new bonds form.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> The rearranged atoms form water molecules. The atoms themselves are conserved.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Hydrogen and oxygen atoms rearrange to form water molecules; they are not created or destroyed.</p>

  <h3>Evidence of a Chemical Reaction</h3>

  <p>Possible signs include a colour change, gas production, formation of a solid, a temperature change, or light emission. These signs provide evidence, but no single sign automatically proves that a chemical reaction has occurred. The underlying question is whether a new substance has formed.</p>

  <h3>Remember</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>A chemical reaction forms new substances.</li>
    <li>Atoms are rearranged during the reaction.</li>
    <li>A physical change does not produce a new substance.</li>
    <li>Observe the evidence and determine whether a new substance has formed.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Chemical Reactions', 15),
  (809, 809, '
  <h2>Reactants</h2>

  <h3>Meaning of Reactants</h3>

  <p><strong>Reactants</strong> are the substances that are present at the start of a chemical reaction and take part in the process of forming products.</p>

  <p>In a chemical equation, reactants are written on the <strong>left-hand side</strong> of the reaction arrow. The arrow points from the reactants towards the products.</p>

  <p>For example:</p>

  <p><strong>2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O</strong></p>

  <p>In this equation, hydrogen (H₂) and oxygen (O₂) are the reactants. Water (H₂O) is the product.</p>

  <h3>How to Identify Reactants</h3>

  <ol>
    <li>Locate the reaction arrow (→).</li>
    <li>Read the substances on the left-hand side of the arrow.</li>
    <li>Identify those substances as the reactants.</li>
  </ol>

  <p>A plus sign between substances on the left means that more than one reactant is involved. It does not mean that the substances have already combined into a single product.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Formation of Water</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Identify the reactants in the equation 2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Locate the arrow.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Read the left-hand side: 2H₂ + O₂.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> The substances represented are hydrogen and oxygen. The coefficient 2 indicates two units of hydrogen molecules for every one unit of oxygen molecules in the balanced equation.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Hydrogen (H₂) and oxygen (O₂) are the reactants.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Formation of Iron Sulfide</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Identify the reactants in Fe + S → FeS.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Find the arrow in the equation.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Inspect the left-hand side: Fe + S.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Fe represents iron, and S represents sulfur.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Iron and sulfur are the reactants. FeS, iron sulfide, is the product.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Thermal Decomposition of Calcium Carbonate</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Identify the reactant in CaCO₃ → CaO + CO₂.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Locate the reaction arrow.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Read the left-hand side, which contains CaCO₃ only.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> CaCO₃ is calcium carbonate. The substances on the right, CaO and CO₂, are the products.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Calcium carbonate (CaCO₃) is the reactant.</p>

  <h3>Reactants and Coefficients</h3>

  <p>A coefficient is the number written before a chemical formula. For example, in 2Mg + O₂ → 2MgO, magnesium and oxygen are the reactants. The coefficient 2 tells us that two magnesium atoms are represented for every oxygen molecule in the equation.</p>

  <p>A coefficient does not change the identity of a substance. The formula H₂ represents hydrogen molecules, while 2H₂ represents two hydrogen molecules.</p>

  <h3>Remember</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Reactants are the starting substances in a chemical reaction.</li>
    <li>In a chemical equation, reactants are on the left of the arrow.</li>
    <li>The substances on the right are the products.</li>
    <li>Read the chemical formula carefully; a coefficient changes the quantity represented, not the substance''s identity.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Reactants', 15),
  (810, 810, '
  <h2>Products</h2>

  <h3>Meaning of Products</h3>

  <p><strong>Products</strong> are the new substances formed during a chemical reaction. They result when the reactant particles rearrange and form new chemical bonds.</p>

  <p>In a chemical equation, products are written on the <strong>right-hand side</strong> of the reaction arrow.</p>

  <p>For example:</p>

  <p><strong>2Mg + O₂ → 2MgO</strong></p>

  <ul>
    <li>Reactants: magnesium (Mg) and oxygen (O₂).</li>
    <li>Product: magnesium oxide (MgO).</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>How to Identify Products</h3>

  <ol>
    <li>Locate the reaction arrow (→).</li>
    <li>Read the substances on the right-hand side.</li>
    <li>Name each substance using its chemical formula.</li>
  </ol>

  <p>A plus sign on the right separates different product substances. One reaction can produce one product or several products.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Burning Magnesium</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Identify the product in 2Mg + O₂ → 2MgO.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Locate the arrow.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Read the right-hand side: 2MgO.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Identify MgO as magnesium oxide. The coefficient 2 indicates two formula units of magnesium oxide in the balanced equation.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Magnesium oxide (MgO) is the product.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Thermal Decomposition</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Identify the products in CaCO₃ → CaO + CO₂.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Find the arrow.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Read the right-hand side: CaO + CO₂.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Identify CaO as calcium oxide and CO₂ as carbon dioxide.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Calcium oxide and carbon dioxide are the products.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Formation of Ammonia</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Identify the product in N₂ + 3H₂ → 2NH₃.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Locate the arrow.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Read the right-hand side: 2NH₃.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Identify NH₃ as ammonia. The coefficient 2 indicates two ammonia molecules.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Ammonia (NH₃) is the product.</p>

  <h3>One Reactant Can Form Several Products</h3>

  <p>In a decomposition reaction, one reactant can break down into two or more products. For example, calcium carbonate decomposes on heating to form calcium oxide and carbon dioxide:</p>

  <p><strong>CaCO₃ → CaO + CO₂</strong></p>

  <p>Conversely, two or more reactants can combine to form one product, as in the formation of magnesium oxide.</p>

  <h3>Do Not Confuse Products with Reactants</h3>

  <p>The arrow shows the direction from the reactants towards the products in the equation. The substances on the left are the starting substances; those on the right are the substances formed.</p>

  <h3>Remember</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Products are formed during a chemical reaction.</li>
    <li>Products are written on the right-hand side of the arrow.</li>
    <li>A reaction may form one or several products.</li>
    <li>Coefficients indicate quantities; they do not change a substance''s identity.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Products', 15),
  (811, 811, '
  <h2>Signs of Chemical Reactions</h2>

  <h3>How Can a Chemical Reaction Be Recognised?</h3>

  <p>A chemical reaction forms new substances. During an experiment, several observable changes may provide evidence that a reaction has occurred. However, a visible change alone does not always prove that a new substance formed.</p>

  <h3>1. Colour Change</h3>

  <p>A substance may change colour when it reacts chemically. For example, iron reacts with sulfur on heating to form iron sulfide, a black solid. The colour change is evidence of a new substance.</p>

  <h3>2. Gas Production</h3>

  <p>A reaction may produce a gas, seen as bubbles or effervescence. For example, dilute hydrochloric acid reacts with calcium carbonate to produce carbon dioxide gas.</p>

  <p><strong>CaCO₃ + 2HCl → CaCl₂ + H₂O + CO₂</strong></p>

  <p>However, bubbles can also form when a liquid boils. Therefore, bubbles alone do not prove that a chemical reaction occurred.</p>

  <h3>3. Formation of a Precipitate</h3>

  <p>A <strong>precipitate</strong> is an insoluble solid formed when suitable solutions react. For example, mixing aqueous lead(II) nitrate and potassium iodide produces a yellow precipitate of lead(II) iodide.</p>

  <p>The appearance of a new solid from two solutions can provide strong evidence of a chemical reaction.</p>

  <h3>4. Temperature Change</h3>

  <p>Some reactions release heat, causing the surroundings to become warmer. These are <strong>exothermic reactions</strong>. Other reactions absorb heat from their surroundings and are called <strong>endothermic reactions</strong>.</p>

  <p>A temperature change can support the conclusion that a reaction occurred, but temperature can also change for physical reasons, such as heating or cooling a substance.</p>

  <h3>5. Light or Sound</h3>

  <p>Some reactions release energy as light or produce a noticeable sound. Burning magnesium produces a bright white light. These observations can be evidence of a chemical reaction when interpreted alongside the substances involved.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Acid and Calcium Carbonate</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Dilute hydrochloric acid is added to calcium carbonate. Bubbles form, and the gas turns limewater milky. What evidence supports a chemical reaction?</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Bubbles suggest that a gas is being produced.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> The gas turns limewater milky, which identifies it as carbon dioxide under the test conditions.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Carbon dioxide is a new substance produced by the reaction.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Gas production and the positive carbon dioxide test support the conclusion that a chemical reaction occurred.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Formation of a Precipitate</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Two colourless solutions are mixed, and a yellow solid appears. Explain the observation.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Observe that the starting solutions were colourless.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> A yellow solid forms after mixing.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> If the solid is an insoluble product, its formation indicates that a new substance has formed.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The yellow solid is evidence of a chemical reaction because an insoluble product has formed.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Melting Ice</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Ice melts, and the temperature rises as it absorbs heat. Does the temperature change prove that a chemical reaction occurred?</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the process as melting.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Compare the substance before and after the change: both are water, H₂O.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> No new substance forms, so the temperature change is not evidence of a chemical reaction in this case.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> No. The process is a physical change because the substance remains water.</p>

  <h3>How to Evaluate the Evidence</h3>

  <ol>
    <li>Describe exactly what was observed.</li>
    <li>Consider whether the observation could result from a physical change.</li>
    <li>Use a suitable test or other evidence to determine whether a new substance formed.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Remember</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Possible signs include colour change, gas production, precipitate formation, temperature change, light or sound.</li>
    <li>One observation may have more than one explanation.</li>
    <li>Stronger conclusions come from identifying the new substance formed.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Signs of Chemical Reactions', 15),
  (812, 812, '
  <h2>Conservation of Mass</h2>

  <h3>The Law of Conservation of Mass</h3>

  <p>The <strong>law of conservation of mass</strong> states that mass is neither created nor destroyed during an ordinary chemical reaction. In a closed system, the total mass of the reactants equals the total mass of the products.</p>

  <p>During a chemical reaction, atoms are rearranged to form new substances. The same atoms are present before and after the reaction, so the total mass remains constant.</p>

  <p><strong>Total mass of reactants = Total mass of products</strong></p>

  <h3>Why Does Mass Remain Constant?</h3>

  <p>Atoms have mass. When chemical bonds break and new bonds form, the atoms are rearranged rather than created or destroyed. Therefore, if all substances are accounted for, the total mass is conserved.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Finding the Mass of a Product</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Magnesium reacts completely with oxygen. If 12 g of magnesium reacts with 8 g of oxygen, calculate the mass of magnesium oxide formed.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Write the conservation relationship:</p>
  <p>Total mass of reactants = Total mass of products.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Add the masses of the reactants:</p>
  <p>12 g + 8 g = 20 g.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Since magnesium oxide is the only product, its mass equals the total mass of the reactants.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 20 g of magnesium oxide.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Finding an Unknown Reactant Mass</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> A reaction produces 35 g of product from two reactants. One reactant has a mass of 15 g. Calculate the mass of the other reactant, assuming all reactants and products are accounted for.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Total mass of reactants = 35 g.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Let the unknown mass be x.</p>
  <p>15 g + x = 35 g.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Subtract 15 g from both sides:</p>
  <p>x = 35 g − 15 g = 20 g.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 20 g.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Gas Escaping from an Open System</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> A student reacts 10 g of calcium carbonate with dilute hydrochloric acid in an open flask. The measured mass of the flask and contents decreases as gas escapes. Does this mean mass was destroyed?</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Recognise that the reaction produces carbon dioxide gas.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> The gas escapes from the open flask and is no longer included in the measurement.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> If the escaping gas is collected and included, the total mass of all reactants and products is conserved.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> No. The measured mass decreases because carbon dioxide escapes from the open system, not because mass is destroyed.</p>

  <h3>Closed and Open Systems</h3>

  <p>A <strong>closed system</strong> prevents matter from entering or leaving during the experiment. When all substances remain accounted for, the total mass remains constant.</p>

  <p>An <strong>open system</strong> allows matter to enter or leave. A measured mass may appear to increase or decrease if a gas enters from the air or escapes from the container.</p>

  <h3>Using Conservation of Mass in Calculations</h3>

  <ol>
    <li>Identify all reactants and products included in the problem.</li>
    <li>Write total mass of reactants = total mass of products.</li>
    <li>Substitute the known masses.</li>
    <li>Rearrange the equation to calculate the unknown mass.</li>
    <li>Include the correct mass unit in the answer.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Remember</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Mass is conserved in ordinary chemical reactions.</li>
    <li>In a closed system, total reactant mass equals total product mass.</li>
    <li>Escaping gas can cause an apparent mass loss in an open system.</li>
    <li>Always account for every substance when comparing masses.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Conservation of Mass', 15),
  (813, 813, '
  <h2>Chemical Equations</h2>

  <h3>Meaning of a Chemical Equation</h3>

  <p>A <strong>chemical equation</strong> is a symbolic representation of a chemical reaction using chemical formulae and a reaction arrow.</p>

  <p>Instead of describing a reaction only in words, a chemical equation identifies the substances involved and shows how the reactants change into products.</p>

  <p>For example:</p>

  <p><strong>Mg + O₂ → MgO</strong></p>

  <p>This equation represents magnesium reacting with oxygen to form magnesium oxide. It identifies the substances, but it is not balanced as written.</p>

  <h3>The Main Parts of a Chemical Equation</h3>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Reactants:</strong> Starting substances, written on the left of the arrow.</li>
    <li><strong>Reaction arrow (→):</strong> Means that the reactants form the products under the stated conditions.</li>
    <li><strong>Products:</strong> Substances formed, written on the right of the arrow.</li>
    <li><strong>Plus sign (+):</strong> Separates different substances on the same side.</li>
    <li><strong>Chemical formulae:</strong> Represent substances using element symbols and, where necessary, subscripts.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Interpreting a Simple Equation</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Interpret Fe + S → FeS.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Read the left-hand side: Fe + S. These represent iron and sulfur.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Read the arrow as meaning that the reactants form a product.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Read the right-hand side: FeS, which represents iron sulfide.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Iron reacts with sulfur to form iron sulfide.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Reading an Equation with Two Products</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Interpret CaCO₃ → CaO + CO₂.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify CaCO₃ on the left as calcium carbonate.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Identify CaO and CO₂ on the right as calcium oxide and carbon dioxide.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Recognise that one reactant forms two product substances.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Calcium carbonate decomposes to form calcium oxide and carbon dioxide.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Interpreting Coefficients</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Interpret 2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify hydrogen and oxygen as the reactants.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Read the coefficients: 2H₂ represents two hydrogen molecules, and O₂ represents one oxygen molecule.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> On the right, 2H₂O represents two water molecules.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Two hydrogen molecules react with one oxygen molecule to form two water molecules, as represented by the balanced equation.</p>

  <h3>What Does a Chemical Equation Tell Us?</h3>

  <p>A chemical equation can show:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>The identities of the reactants and products.</li>
    <li>The relative quantities represented by coefficients in a balanced equation.</li>
    <li>Sometimes, physical states and reaction conditions, when these are included.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>A chemical equation does not automatically show every experimental detail, such as how quickly a reaction occurs or the full procedure for carrying it out.</p>

  <h3>Balanced and Unbalanced Equations</h3>

  <p>A balanced chemical equation has the same number of atoms of each element on both sides. For example, Mg + O₂ → MgO is unbalanced because it has two oxygen atoms on the left but only one on the right.</p>

  <p>The balanced form is:</p>

  <p><strong>2Mg + O₂ → 2MgO</strong></p>

  <p>Balancing equations is a separate skill. The key skill on this screen is reading what a chemical equation represents.</p>

  <h3>Remember</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>A chemical equation represents a reaction using formulae and symbols.</li>
    <li>Reactants appear on the left; products appear on the right.</li>
    <li>The arrow connects reactants to products.</li>
    <li>Coefficients show relative quantities; subscripts form part of a chemical formula.</li>
    <li>A chemical equation must be balanced to represent conservation of atoms correctly.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Chemical Equations', 15),
  (814, 814, '
  <h2>Writing Chemical Equations</h2>

  <h3>From Word Equations to Chemical Equations</h3>

  <p>A <strong>word equation</strong> describes a chemical reaction using the names of substances. A <strong>chemical equation</strong> represents the same reaction using chemical formulae.</p>

  <p>For example:</p>

  <p><strong>Word equation:</strong> magnesium + oxygen → magnesium oxide</p>
  <p><strong>Chemical equation:</strong> Mg + O₂ → MgO</p>

  <p>The chemical equation identifies the substances using symbols and formulae. It must then be checked and balanced so that each element has the same number of atoms on both sides.</p>

  <h3>A Reliable Method</h3>

  <ol>
    <li><strong>Identify the reactants:</strong> Determine which substances are present at the start.</li>
    <li><strong>Identify the products:</strong> Determine which substances are formed.</li>
    <li><strong>Write the correct formula for each substance:</strong> Use chemical symbols and formulae, not guessed abbreviations.</li>
    <li><strong>Place the reactants on the left and products on the right:</strong> Separate different substances on the same side with a plus sign.</li>
    <li><strong>Check the equation:</strong> Count the atoms of each element on both sides and balance the equation using coefficients.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Magnesium Reacting with Oxygen</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Write a balanced chemical equation for magnesium reacting with oxygen to form magnesium oxide.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the reactants: magnesium and oxygen.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Write their formulae: Mg and O₂.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Identify the product and its formula: magnesium oxide, MgO.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Write the unbalanced equation: Mg + O₂ → MgO.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 5:</strong> Oxygen has two atoms on the left but one on the right. Put 2 before MgO, then put 2 before Mg to balance magnesium.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 2Mg + O₂ → 2MgO</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Zinc Reacting with Hydrochloric Acid</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Write a balanced chemical equation for zinc reacting with hydrochloric acid to form zinc chloride and hydrogen gas.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the reactants: zinc and hydrochloric acid.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Write the formulae: Zn and HCl.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Identify the products: zinc chloride (ZnCl₂) and hydrogen gas (H₂).</p>
  <p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Write the unbalanced equation: Zn + HCl → ZnCl₂ + H₂.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 5:</strong> The product contains two chlorine atoms and two hydrogen atoms. Put 2 before HCl.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Zn + 2HCl → ZnCl₂ + H₂</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Calcium Carbonate Decomposing</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Write the chemical equation for calcium carbonate decomposing into calcium oxide and carbon dioxide when heated.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the reactant: calcium carbonate, CaCO₃.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Identify the products: calcium oxide, CaO, and carbon dioxide, CO₂.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Write the equation: CaCO₃ → CaO + CO₂.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Count the atoms. Both sides contain one calcium atom, one carbon atom, and three oxygen atoms.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> CaCO₃ → CaO + CO₂. The equation is already balanced.</p>

  <h3>Do Not Guess Chemical Formulae</h3>

  <p>Use the correct formula for each substance. For example, elemental oxygen normally exists as O₂, while water is H₂O and carbon dioxide is CO₂. A wrong formula represents a different composition and can make an otherwise correct equation inaccurate.</p>

  <h3>Coefficients and Subscripts</h3>

  <p>When balancing an equation, change coefficients placed before formulae. Do not change subscripts inside formulae. For example, 2H₂O represents two water molecules; H₂O₂ represents hydrogen peroxide, a different substance.</p>

  <h3>Remember</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Translate the names of substances into correct chemical formulae.</li>
    <li>Put reactants on the left and products on the right.</li>
    <li>Use plus signs to separate different substances on the same side.</li>
    <li>Check and balance atom counts using coefficients.</li>
    <li>Never change a formula''s subscripts merely to balance an equation.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Writing Chemical Equations', 15),
  (815, 815, '
  <h2>State Symbols</h2>

  <h3>What Are State Symbols?</h3>

  <p><strong>State symbols</strong> are letters written in brackets after chemical formulae to show the physical state of each substance in a chemical equation under the stated conditions.</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>(s)</strong> — solid</li>
    <li><strong>(l)</strong> — liquid</li>
    <li><strong>(g)</strong> — gas</li>
    <li><strong>(aq)</strong> — aqueous: a substance dissolved in water</li>
  </ul>

  <p>For example, H₂O(l) represents liquid water, while H₂O(g) represents water vapour. Both are water, but they are in different physical states.</p>

  <h3>Why State Symbols Matter</h3>

  <p>State symbols give additional information about the physical state of reactants and products. They can help distinguish a gas from a dissolved substance or an insoluble solid.</p>

  <p>Compare:</p>

  <p><strong>NaCl(s)</strong> — solid sodium chloride.</p>
  <p><strong>NaCl(aq)</strong> — sodium chloride dissolved in water.</p>

  <p>The substance is sodium chloride in both cases, but its physical state and surroundings differ.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Burning Magnesium</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Interpret the state symbols in 2Mg(s) + O₂(g) → 2MgO(s).</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Mg(s) means solid magnesium.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> O₂(g) means oxygen gas.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> MgO(s) means solid magnesium oxide.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Solid magnesium reacts with oxygen gas to form solid magnesium oxide.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Aqueous Solutions Forming a Precipitate</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Interpret AgNO₃(aq) + NaCl(aq) → AgCl(s) + NaNO₃(aq).</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> AgNO₃(aq) and NaCl(aq) are dissolved in water.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> AgCl(s) is solid silver chloride. It forms as an insoluble precipitate.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> NaNO₃(aq) is sodium nitrate remaining dissolved in water.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Two aqueous solutions react to form solid silver chloride and aqueous sodium nitrate.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Formation of Water</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Explain the state symbols in 2H₂(g) + O₂(g) → 2H₂O(l).</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> H₂(g) represents hydrogen gas.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> O₂(g) represents oxygen gas.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> H₂O(l) represents liquid water.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Hydrogen gas reacts with oxygen gas to form liquid water.</p>

  <h3>How to Choose a State Symbol</h3>

  <ol>
    <li>Identify the substance in the reaction.</li>
    <li>Determine its physical state under the stated conditions.</li>
    <li>Write the correct state symbol in brackets immediately after its formula.</li>
  </ol>

  <p>Do not assume that every substance containing water is automatically aqueous. The symbol (aq) specifically means that a substance is dissolved in water. Pure liquid water is written H₂O(l).</p>

  <h3>Remember</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>(s) means solid.</li>
    <li>(l) means liquid.</li>
    <li>(g) means gas.</li>
    <li>(aq) means dissolved in water.</li>
    <li>State symbols describe physical states; they do not change the chemical identity of the formula.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for State Symbols', 15),
  (816, 816, '
  <h2>Balancing Chemical Equations</h2>

  <h3>What Is a Balanced Chemical Equation?</h3>

  <p>A <strong>balanced chemical equation</strong> has the same number of atoms of each element on both sides of the reaction arrow.</p>

  <p>This follows the law of conservation of mass: atoms are rearranged during an ordinary chemical reaction, but they are not created or destroyed.</p>

  <p>For example, the equation H₂ + O₂ → H₂O is not balanced. There are two oxygen atoms on the left but only one on the right.</p>

  <h3>Coefficients and Subscripts</h3>

  <p>A <strong>coefficient</strong> is the number placed before a chemical formula. It multiplies every atom in that formula.</p>

  <ul>
    <li>H₂O contains 2 hydrogen atoms and 1 oxygen atom per molecule.</li>
    <li>2H₂O represents 2 water molecules, containing 4 hydrogen atoms and 2 oxygen atoms in total.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>A <strong>subscript</strong> is the small number within a formula. It forms part of the identity of the substance. When balancing equations, change coefficients, not subscripts.</p>

  <h3>A Reliable Balancing Method</h3>

  <ol>
    <li>Write the correct chemical formulae for all reactants and products.</li>
    <li>Count the atoms of each element on both sides.</li>
    <li>Choose a coefficient for a formula to balance an element.</li>
    <li>Recount all affected atoms and continue until every element balances.</li>
    <li>Reduce the coefficients to the smallest whole-number ratio, if possible.</li>
    <li>Check the final atom counts on both sides.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Formation of Water</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Balance H₂ + O₂ → H₂O.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Count the atoms.</p>
  <p>Left: H = 2, O = 2.</p>
  <p>Right: H = 2, O = 1.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Balance oxygen by placing 2 before H₂O.</p>
  <p>H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Recount hydrogen on the right. There are now 4 hydrogen atoms, so place 2 before H₂.</p>
  <p>2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O</p>

  <p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Check both sides: H = 4 and O = 2.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Formation of Magnesium Oxide</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Balance Mg + O₂ → MgO.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Oxygen has 2 atoms on the left and 1 on the right.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Put 2 before MgO: Mg + O₂ → 2MgO.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> There are now 2 magnesium atoms on the right, so put 2 before Mg.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Check: Mg = 2 and O = 2 on each side.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 2Mg + O₂ → 2MgO</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Formation of Ammonia</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Balance N₂ + H₂ → NH₃.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> There are 2 nitrogen atoms on the left and 1 on the right. Put 2 before NH₃.</p>
  <p>N₂ + H₂ → 2NH₃</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> The right side now contains 6 hydrogen atoms because each NH₃ has 3 hydrogen atoms.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Put 3 before H₂ to give 6 hydrogen atoms on the left.</p>
  <p>N₂ + 3H₂ → 2NH₃</p>

  <p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Check: N = 2 and H = 6 on both sides.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> N₂ + 3H₂ → 2NH₃</p>

  <h3>Common Mistakes</h3>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Changing subscripts:</strong> Never turn H₂O into H₂O₂ to balance an equation. These are different substances.</li>
    <li><strong>Balancing only one element:</strong> Recheck every element after changing a coefficient.</li>
    <li><strong>Forgetting to multiply the whole formula:</strong> In 3H₂O, there are 6 hydrogen atoms and 3 oxygen atoms.</li>
    <li><strong>Leaving unnecessary large coefficients:</strong> Use the smallest whole-number ratio.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Remember</h3>

  <p>Balance an equation by changing coefficients only. Count atoms carefully, and verify every element before accepting the final equation.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Balancing Chemical Equations', 15),
  (817, 817, '
  <h2>Balancing Equations with Multiple Elements</h2>

  <h3>When an Equation Contains Several Elements</h3>

  <p>Some equations contain three or more elements. Balancing them requires careful atom counting because changing one coefficient may affect several elements at once.</p>

  <p>The rule remains the same: every element must have the same total number of atoms on both sides. Change coefficients only, never subscripts.</p>

  <h3>Count Every Atom in a Formula</h3>

  <p>For example, one Ca(OH)₂ formula unit contains:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>1 calcium atom (Ca)</li>
    <li>2 oxygen atoms (O)</li>
    <li>2 hydrogen atoms (H)</li>
  </ul>

  <p>The subscript outside the brackets multiplies everything inside the brackets. Thus, in Ca(OH)₂, the group OH occurs twice.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Aluminium Oxide Formation</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Balance Al + O₂ → Al₂O₃.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Aluminium oxide contains 2 Al atoms and 3 O atoms per formula unit.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Use 2Al₂O₃ to give 4 Al atoms and 6 O atoms on the right.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Put 3 before O₂ to give 6 O atoms on the left.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Put 4 before Al to balance aluminium.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 4Al + 3O₂ → 2Al₂O₃</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Calcium Hydroxide and Hydrochloric Acid</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Balance Ca(OH)₂ + HCl → CaCl₂ + H₂O.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Calcium is already balanced: 1 atom on each side.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> CaCl₂ contains 2 chlorine atoms, so put 2 before HCl.</p>
  <p>Ca(OH)₂ + 2HCl → CaCl₂ + H₂O</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Count hydrogen on the left: 2 from Ca(OH)₂ and 2 from 2HCl, giving 4 in total. There are also 2 oxygen atoms on the left.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Put 2 before H₂O, giving 4 hydrogen atoms and 2 oxygen atoms on the right.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Ca(OH)₂ + 2HCl → CaCl₂ + 2H₂O</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Combustion of Propane</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Balance C₃H₈ + O₂ → CO₂ + H₂O.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Balance carbon. There are 3 carbon atoms in propane, so put 3 before CO₂.</p>
  <p>C₃H₈ + O₂ → 3CO₂ + H₂O</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Balance hydrogen. Propane has 8 hydrogen atoms, so put 4 before H₂O.</p>
  <p>C₃H₈ + O₂ → 3CO₂ + 4H₂O</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Count oxygen on the right: 3CO₂ contains 6 oxygen atoms, and 4H₂O contains 4. Total = 10 oxygen atoms.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Put 5 before O₂ to supply 10 oxygen atoms.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> C₃H₈ + 5O₂ → 3CO₂ + 4H₂O</p>

  <h3>Using an Unchanged Polyatomic Group</h3>

  <p>A <strong>polyatomic ion</strong> is a group of bonded atoms carrying an overall charge. In some equations, a group such as SO₄ remains unchanged on both sides. When the group remains intact, it can be counted as a unit to make balancing easier.</p>

  <p>However, this shortcut is only valid when the same group appears on both sides unchanged. If its atoms are rearranged into different substances, count the individual atoms instead.</p>

  <h3>A Reliable Order for Complex Equations</h3>

  <ol>
    <li>Write correct chemical formulae.</li>
    <li>Balance elements that appear in only one formula on each side first, where practical.</li>
    <li>Balance unchanged polyatomic groups as units when appropriate.</li>
    <li>Balance hydrogen and oxygen later when that makes the counting simpler.</li>
    <li>Check every element and reduce coefficients to the smallest whole-number ratio.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Remember</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Subscripts inside brackets multiply the atoms in the bracketed group.</li>
    <li>A coefficient multiplies the entire formula that follows it.</li>
    <li>Balance each element, even when several elements are present.</li>
    <li>Only treat a polyatomic group as a unit when it remains unchanged on both sides.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Balancing Equations with Multiple Elements', 15),
  (818, 818, '
  <h2>Combination Reactions</h2>

  <h3>Meaning</h3>
  <p>A <strong>combination reaction</strong> is a chemical reaction in which two or more reactants combine to form one product.</p>

  <p>The general pattern is:</p>
  <p><strong>A + B → AB</strong></p>

  <p>The letters represent substances, not necessarily individual atoms. The important feature is that several reactants form one product.</p>

  <h3>How to Recognise a Combination Reaction</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Count the reactants on the left-hand side of the equation.</li>
    <li>Count the products on the right-hand side.</li>
    <li>If two or more reactants form one product, the reaction is a combination reaction.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Magnesium Reacting with Oxygen</h3>
  <p>Magnesium burns in oxygen to form magnesium oxide.</p>
  <p>Unbalanced equation:</p>
  <p>Mg + O₂ → MgO</p>
  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> The reactants are magnesium and oxygen.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> The only product is magnesium oxide.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Balance the oxygen atoms by placing 2 before MgO. Then balance magnesium by placing 2 before Mg.</p>
  <p><strong>Balanced equation:</strong> 2Mg + O₂ → 2MgO</p>
  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> Two reactants form one product, so this is a combination reaction.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Iron Reacting with Sulphur</h3>
  <p>Iron reacts with sulphur when heated to form iron(II) sulphide.</p>
  <p>Fe + S → FeS</p>
  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> The reactants are iron and sulphur.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> They form one product, iron(II) sulphide.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> There is one iron atom and one sulphur atom on each side, so the equation is already balanced.</p>
  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> This is a combination reaction because two reactants form one product.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Calcium Oxide Reacting with Water</h3>
  <p>Calcium oxide reacts with water to form calcium hydroxide.</p>
  <p>CaO + H₂O → Ca(OH)₂</p>
  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> The reactants are calcium oxide and water.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> The only product is calcium hydroxide.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Count the atoms: both sides have 1 Ca, 2 H and 2 O atoms. The equation is balanced.</p>
  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> This is a combination reaction because two reactants form one product.</p>

  <h3>Important Distinction</h3>
  <p>A combination reaction is classified by its reaction pattern, not simply by whether heat is supplied. The defining feature is that two or more reactants produce one product.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Combination Reactions', 15),
  (819, 819, '
  <h2>Decomposition Reactions</h2>

  <h3>Meaning</h3>
  <p>A <strong>decomposition reaction</strong> is a chemical reaction in which one reactant breaks down into two or more products.</p>

  <p>The general pattern is:</p>
  <p><strong>AB → A + B</strong></p>

  <p>This is the opposite pattern to a combination reaction: one starting substance produces several products.</p>

  <h3>Energy in Decomposition</h3>
  <p>Many decomposition reactions require an energy supply, such as heat, light or electricity.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Thermal decomposition:</strong> breakdown caused by heating.</li>
    <li><strong>Electrolysis:</strong> breakdown caused by passing an electric current through a suitable substance.</li>
    <li><strong>Photochemical decomposition:</strong> breakdown caused by light.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>These are ways decomposition can occur. Do not assume every decomposition reaction uses the same energy source.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Thermal Decomposition of Calcium Carbonate</h3>
  <p>When calcium carbonate is strongly heated, it breaks down into calcium oxide and carbon dioxide.</p>
  <p>CaCO₃ → CaO + CO₂</p>
  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> There is one reactant: calcium carbonate.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> There are two products: calcium oxide and carbon dioxide.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Count atoms. Both sides contain 1 Ca, 1 C and 3 O atoms.</p>
  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> This is a decomposition reaction because one reactant forms two products.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Decomposition of Potassium Chlorate</h3>
  <p>On heating, potassium chlorate decomposes to form potassium chloride and oxygen. Manganese(IV) oxide may be used as a catalyst.</p>
  <p>Unbalanced equation:</p>
  <p>KClO₃ → KCl + O₂</p>
  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Balance oxygen by using 2KClO₃, giving 6 oxygen atoms.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Place 3 before O₂ to give 6 oxygen atoms on the right.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Balance potassium and chlorine by placing 2 before KCl.</p>
  <p><strong>Balanced equation:</strong> 2KClO₃ → 2KCl + 3O₂</p>
  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> One reactant forms two products, so this is a decomposition reaction. A catalyst speeds up the reaction but is not consumed as a reactant in the overall equation.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Electrolysis of Water</h3>
  <p>Electricity can decompose water into hydrogen and oxygen gases.</p>
  <p>Unbalanced equation:</p>
  <p>H₂O → H₂ + O₂</p>
  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Oxygen occurs as O₂ on the right, so place 2 before H₂O.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> The left now contains 4 hydrogen atoms, so place 2 before H₂.</p>
  <p><strong>Balanced equation:</strong> 2H₂O → 2H₂ + O₂</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Check the atoms: H = 4 and O = 2 on each side.</p>
  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> One reactant forms two products, making this a decomposition reaction.</p>

  <h3>How to Recognise Decomposition</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Identify the reactant on the left-hand side.</li>
    <li>Count the products on the right-hand side.</li>
    <li>If one reactant forms two or more products, classify it as decomposition.</li>
  </ol>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Decomposition Reactions', 15),
  (820, 820, '
  <h2>Displacement Reactions</h2>

  <h3>Meaning</h3>
  <p>A <strong>displacement reaction</strong> occurs when a more reactive element replaces a less reactive element from its compound.</p>

  <p>For displacement of a metal from its compound, the general pattern is:</p>
  <p><strong>A + BC → AC + B</strong></p>
  <p>This pattern applies when element A is more reactive than element B and can replace it in compound BC.</p>

  <h3>The Reactivity Series</h3>
  <p>The reactivity series ranks metals from more reactive to less reactive. A more reactive metal can displace a less reactive metal from a suitable solution of its compound. A less reactive metal cannot displace a more reactive metal from its compound.</p>

  <p>A useful simplified order is:</p>
  <p><strong>K, Na, Ca, Mg, Al, Zn, Fe, Pb, (H), Cu, Ag, Au</strong></p>
  <p>Metals higher in this series are generally more reactive than those below them. Hydrogen is included as a reference point for reactions of metals with acids; it is not a metal.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Zinc and Copper(II) Sulphate</h3>
  <p>Zinc is more reactive than copper. When zinc is placed in copper(II) sulphate solution, it displaces copper.</p>
  <p><strong>Equation:</strong> Zn + CuSO₄ → ZnSO₄ + Cu</p>
  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the free metal: zinc.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Compare reactivity. Zinc is above copper in the reactivity series.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Zinc replaces copper in copper(II) sulphate, forming zinc sulphate and copper.</p>
  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> This is a displacement reaction.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Iron and Copper(II) Sulphate</h3>
  <p>Iron is more reactive than copper, so it displaces copper from copper(II) sulphate solution.</p>
  <p><strong>Equation:</strong> Fe + CuSO₄ → FeSO₄ + Cu</p>
  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> The free element is iron.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Iron is more reactive than copper.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Iron replaces copper, forming iron(II) sulphate and copper.</p>
  <p><strong>Observation:</strong> The blue solution may turn pale green, and a reddish-brown copper deposit may form.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Copper and Zinc Sulphate</h3>
  <p>Consider copper placed in zinc sulphate solution.</p>
  <p><strong>Proposed equation:</strong> Cu + ZnSO₄ → ?</p>
  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> The free metal is copper.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Zinc is more reactive than copper.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Copper cannot displace zinc from zinc sulphate.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> No displacement reaction occurs under these conditions.</p>

  <h3>Important Rule</h3>
  <p>Always compare the reactivity of the free element with the element it might replace. A reaction is not guaranteed simply because a metal is placed in a salt solution.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Displacement Reactions', 15),
  (821, 821, '
  <h2>Neutralisation Reactions</h2>

  <h3>Meaning</h3>
  <p><strong>Neutralisation</strong> is a reaction between an acid and a base in which salt and water are formed.</p>

  <p>For a typical acid reacting with a metal oxide or metal hydroxide, the general word equation is:</p>
  <p><strong>Acid + Base → Salt + Water</strong></p>

  <p>A base that dissolves in water is called an <strong>alkali</strong>. Therefore, acid–alkali reactions are examples of neutralisation.</p>

  <h3>How Salts Are Named</h3>
  <p>The acid helps determine the name of the salt:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Hydrochloric acid forms chloride salts.</li>
    <li>Sulphuric acid forms sulphate salts.</li>
    <li>Nitric acid forms nitrate salts.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Hydrochloric Acid and Sodium Hydroxide</h3>
  <p>Hydrochloric acid reacts with sodium hydroxide to form sodium chloride and water.</p>
  <p><strong>Word equation:</strong> Hydrochloric acid + sodium hydroxide → sodium chloride + water</p>
  <p><strong>Formula equation:</strong> HCl + NaOH → NaCl + H₂O</p>
  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the acid, HCl, and the base, NaOH.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> The salt is sodium chloride, NaCl, because hydrochloric acid forms chloride salts.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Water is the other product. Count the atoms: H = 2, Na = 1, Cl = 1 and O = 1 on each side.</p>
  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> The equation is balanced and represents neutralisation.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Sulphuric Acid and Potassium Hydroxide</h3>
  <p>Sulphuric acid reacts with potassium hydroxide to form potassium sulphate and water.</p>
  <p><strong>Unbalanced equation:</strong> H₂SO₄ + KOH → K₂SO₄ + H₂O</p>
  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> The salt is potassium sulphate, K₂SO₄.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Place 2 before KOH to supply the two potassium atoms required.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Balance hydrogen and oxygen by placing 2 before H₂O.</p>
  <p><strong>Balanced equation:</strong> H₂SO₄ + 2KOH → K₂SO₄ + 2H₂O</p>
  <p><strong>Check:</strong> K = 2, H = 4, S = 1 and O = 6 on each side.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Hydrochloric Acid and Magnesium Oxide</h3>
  <p>Magnesium oxide is a basic oxide. It reacts with hydrochloric acid to form magnesium chloride and water.</p>
  <p><strong>Unbalanced equation:</strong> HCl + MgO → MgCl₂ + H₂O</p>
  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Magnesium chloride contains two chlorine atoms.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Place 2 before HCl to balance chlorine.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Check hydrogen, magnesium and oxygen. Each is now balanced.</p>
  <p><strong>Balanced equation:</strong> 2HCl + MgO → MgCl₂ + H₂O</p>
  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> An acid reacts with this basic oxide to form a salt and water.</p>

  <h3>Understanding Neutralisation</h3>
  <p>In aqueous solution, acids provide hydrogen ions, H⁺, while alkalis provide hydroxide ions, OH⁻. These ions react to form water:</p>
  <p><strong>H⁺(aq) + OH⁻(aq) → H₂O(l)</strong></p>
  <p>This ionic equation describes the essential neutralisation process for an acid reacting with an alkali. Neutralisation does not mean every resulting solution must have pH 7 under all conditions; the final pH depends on the substances and quantities involved.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Neutralisation Reactions', 15),
  (822, 822, '
  <h2>Combustion Reactions</h2>

  <h3>Meaning</h3>
  <p><strong>Combustion</strong> is a chemical reaction in which a substance reacts with an oxidising agent, commonly oxygen in air, releasing energy as heat and often light.</p>

  <p>In ordinary burning, oxygen supports the reaction. Many fuels contain carbon and hydrogen, so their combustion can produce carbon dioxide and water when oxygen is sufficient.</p>

  <h3>Complete and Incomplete Combustion</h3>

  <p><strong>Complete combustion:</strong> A carbon-containing fuel burns with sufficient oxygen. Carbon is converted mainly to carbon dioxide, while hydrogen is converted to water.</p>

  <p><strong>Incomplete combustion:</strong> When oxygen is insufficient, carbon monoxide and/or solid carbon (soot) may form instead of all the carbon becoming carbon dioxide. Water can still form from the hydrogen in the fuel.</p>

  <p>Carbon monoxide is poisonous because it reduces the blood''s ability to transport oxygen. Combustion must therefore take place with adequate ventilation.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Complete Combustion of Carbon</h3>
  <p>Carbon burns in sufficient oxygen to form carbon dioxide.</p>
  <p><strong>Equation:</strong> C + O₂ → CO₂</p>
  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Carbon is the fuel and oxygen is the reactant supporting combustion.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Under sufficient oxygen, the carbon forms carbon dioxide.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Count the atoms: one carbon and two oxygen atoms occur on each side.</p>
  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> The equation is balanced and represents complete combustion of carbon.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Complete Combustion of Methane</h3>
  <p>Methane, CH₄, burns in sufficient oxygen to form carbon dioxide and water.</p>
  <p><strong>Unbalanced equation:</strong> CH₄ + O₂ → CO₂ + H₂O</p>
  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Carbon is already balanced: one atom on each side.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Methane has four hydrogen atoms, so place 2 before H₂O.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> The products now contain four oxygen atoms in total. Place 2 before O₂.</p>
  <p><strong>Balanced equation:</strong> CH₄ + 2O₂ → CO₂ + 2H₂O</p>
  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> One carbon atom, four hydrogen atoms and four oxygen atoms occur on each side.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Complete Combustion of Propane</h3>
  <p>Propane, C₃H₈, burns in sufficient oxygen to form carbon dioxide and water.</p>
  <p><strong>Unbalanced equation:</strong> C₃H₈ + O₂ → CO₂ + H₂O</p>
  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Balance carbon by placing 3 before CO₂.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Balance hydrogen by placing 4 before H₂O.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> The products contain 6 + 4 = 10 oxygen atoms. Place 5 before O₂.</p>
  <p><strong>Balanced equation:</strong> C₃H₈ + 5O₂ → 3CO₂ + 4H₂O</p>
  <p><strong>Check:</strong> C = 3, H = 8 and O = 10 on each side.</p>

  <h3>Recognising Combustion</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Look for a substance reacting with oxygen.</li>
    <li>Combustion releases energy to the surroundings.</li>
    <li>For complete combustion of a hydrocarbon, the products are carbon dioxide and water.</li>
    <li>Do not assume every combustion reaction produces carbon dioxide and water; the products depend on the substance and conditions.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Combustion Reactions', 15),
  (823, 823, '
  <h2>Precipitation Reactions</h2>

  <h3>Meaning</h3>
  <p>A <strong>precipitation reaction</strong> occurs when two solutions react to form an insoluble solid called a <strong>precipitate</strong>.</p>

  <p>The solutions contain dissolved ions. When the solutions are mixed, some ions combine to form a substance that does not remain dissolved under the reaction conditions. The solid separates from the solution.</p>

  <h3>How to Recognise a Precipitation Reaction</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Start with two suitable aqueous solutions.</li>
    <li>Consider which positive and negative ions can exchange partners.</li>
    <li>Use solubility rules to predict whether a product is insoluble.</li>
    <li>If an insoluble solid forms, that solid is the precipitate.</li>
  </ol>

  <p>A cloudy mixture or visible solid may indicate precipitation, but the products and their solubilities determine whether the reaction is a precipitation reaction.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Silver Nitrate and Sodium Chloride</h3>
  <p>Mixing aqueous silver nitrate and sodium chloride forms a white precipitate of silver chloride.</p>
  <p><strong>Word equation:</strong> Silver nitrate + sodium chloride → silver chloride + sodium nitrate</p>
  <p><strong>Formula equation:</strong> AgNO₃(aq) + NaCl(aq) → AgCl(s) + NaNO₃(aq)</p>
  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> The reactants are aqueous solutions containing Ag⁺, NO₃⁻, Na⁺ and Cl⁻ ions.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Silver ions combine with chloride ions to form AgCl, which is insoluble in water under these conditions.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> AgCl forms a white solid, while sodium nitrate remains dissolved.</p>
  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> This is a precipitation reaction because an insoluble solid forms from aqueous solutions.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Lead(II) Nitrate and Potassium Iodide</h3>
  <p>When aqueous lead(II) nitrate is mixed with aqueous potassium iodide, a yellow precipitate of lead(II) iodide forms.</p>
  <p><strong>Formula equation:</strong> Pb(NO₃)₂(aq) + 2KI(aq) → PbI₂(s) + 2KNO₃(aq)</p>
  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> The lead(II) ion is Pb²⁺, while the iodide ion is I⁻.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Two iodide ions are needed to balance the +2 charge on one lead(II) ion, giving PbI₂.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Place 2 before KI and 2 before KNO₃ to balance potassium and nitrate.</p>
  <p><strong>Observation:</strong> A yellow solid forms because lead(II) iodide is poorly soluble in water.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Barium Chloride and Sodium Sulphate</h3>
  <p>Mixing aqueous barium chloride and sodium sulphate forms a white precipitate of barium sulphate.</p>
  <p><strong>Formula equation:</strong> BaCl₂(aq) + Na₂SO₄(aq) → BaSO₄(s) + 2NaCl(aq)</p>
  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Barium ions combine with sulphate ions to form BaSO₄.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Barium sulphate is insoluble in water and forms a white precipitate.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Place 2 before NaCl to balance the two sodium and two chlorine atoms.</p>
  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> The equation is balanced, and BaSO₄ is the precipitate.</p>

  <h3>Net Ionic Equations</h3>
  <p>A net ionic equation shows only the ions that actually form the precipitate. Ions that remain unchanged in solution are called spectator ions.</p>
  <p>For silver chloride formation:</p>
  <p><strong>Ag⁺(aq) + Cl⁻(aq) → AgCl(s)</strong></p>
  <p>This equation shows the silver and chloride ions combining to form the solid.</p>

  <h3>State Symbols</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>(aq)</strong> means dissolved in water.</li>
    <li><strong>(s)</strong> means solid.</li>
    <li><strong>(l)</strong> means liquid.</li>
    <li><strong>(g)</strong> means gas.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>In a precipitation equation, the precipitate is labelled (s), while the dissolved substances are labelled (aq).</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Precipitation Reactions', 15),
  (824, 824, '
  <h2>Properties of Acids</h2>

  <h3>1. What is an acid?</h3>

  <p>
    An <strong>acid</strong> is a substance that produces hydrogen ions,
    H<sup>+</sup>, when dissolved in water. These ions are responsible
    for the characteristic acidic properties of aqueous solutions.
  </p>

  <p>
    For example, when hydrogen chloride dissolves in water, it forms
    hydrochloric acid:
  </p>

  <p>
    HCl(aq) → H<sup>+</sup>(aq) + Cl<sup>−</sup>(aq)
  </p>

  <p>
    This equation is a simplified school-level representation of
    ionisation in water. Hydrogen ions associate with water molecules
    to form hydronium ions, H<sub>3</sub>O<sup>+</sup>.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Characteristic properties</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Effect on indicators:</strong> acids turn blue litmus paper red.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>pH:</strong> an acidic aqueous solution has a pH below 7
      under the usual school-level conditions.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Electrical conductivity:</strong> aqueous acids conduct
      electricity because they contain mobile ions.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Reactions:</strong> acids react with certain metals,
      carbonates, hydrogen carbonates and basic metal oxides.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    These properties are observed in aqueous solutions. Do not assume
    that every acid has the same concentration, reacts at the same rate,
    or produces identical observations in every reaction.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Worked examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: Identifying an acid using litmus</strong></p>

  <p>
    A colourless solution turns blue litmus paper red. What does this
    observation suggest?
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Recall the characteristic litmus test.</p>
  <p>
    Acids turn blue litmus red, whereas alkalis turn red litmus blue.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Match the observation.</p>
  <p>
    Since blue litmus turns red, the observation is consistent with an
    acidic solution.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The solution is acidic, subject to confirming
  the test conditions and identity of the sample.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: Interpreting pH</strong></p>

  <p>
    A solution has a pH of 3. Is it acidic, neutral or alkaline?
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Use the usual school-level pH classification.</p>
  <p>
    pH below 7 is acidic, pH 7 is neutral, and pH above 7 is alkaline.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Compare the given value with 7.</p>
  <p>
    Since 3 is less than 7, the solution is acidic.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Acidic.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: Explaining conductivity</strong></p>

  <p>
    Why can aqueous hydrochloric acid conduct electricity?
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the particles in solution.</p>
  <p>
    Hydrochloric acid forms mobile ions in water, including H<sup>+</sup>
    in the simplified school-level representation and Cl<sup>−</sup>.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Connect ions to electrical conduction.</p>
  <p>
    These charged particles move through the solution and carry electric
    charge.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Aqueous hydrochloric acid conducts electricity
  because it contains mobile ions.</p>

  <h3>4. Safety</h3>

  <p>
    Never taste an acid to identify it. Some acids are corrosive.
    Avoid skin and eye contact, use appropriate protective equipment,
    and follow laboratory instructions.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Properties of Acids', 15),
  (825, 825, '
  <h2>Reactions of Acids with Metals</h2>

  <h3>1. The general reaction</h3>

  <p>
    A sufficiently reactive metal reacts with a suitable dilute acid to
    form a <strong>salt and hydrogen gas</strong>.
  </p>

  <p><strong>General word equation:</strong></p>

  <p>Metal + Acid → Salt + Hydrogen</p>

  <p>
    The salt formed depends on the metal and the acid used. For example,
    hydrochloric acid forms chloride salts, while dilute sulfuric acid
    forms sulfate salts.
  </p>

  <p>
    Not every metal reacts with dilute acids in the same way. Metals
    below hydrogen in the reactivity series, such as copper, do not
    normally displace hydrogen from dilute hydrochloric acid or dilute
    sulfuric acid.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Recognising hydrogen gas</h3>

  <p>
    A reacting metal may produce bubbles of gas. To test whether the
    gas is hydrogen, collect a small sample safely and bring a lighted
    splint to the mouth of the test tube as instructed by the teacher.
    Hydrogen burns with a characteristic squeaky pop.
  </p>

  <p>
    Bubbles alone do not prove that the gas is hydrogen. The gas must
    be tested appropriately.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Worked examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: Magnesium and hydrochloric acid</strong></p>

  <p>
    Write the balanced equation for magnesium reacting with dilute
    hydrochloric acid.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1: Predict the products.</strong></p>
  <p>
    Magnesium forms magnesium chloride, and hydrogen gas is released.
  </p>

  <p>
    Mg + HCl → MgCl<sub>2</sub> + H<sub>2</sub>
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2: Balance chlorine and hydrogen.</strong></p>
  <p>
    Magnesium chloride contains two chlorine atoms, so place 2 before HCl.
    This also gives two hydrogen atoms, which form one H<sub>2</sub> molecule.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Balanced equation:</strong>
    Mg(s) + 2HCl(aq) → MgCl<sub>2</sub>(aq) + H<sub>2</sub>(g)
  </p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: Zinc and sulfuric acid</strong></p>

  <p>
    Predict the products when zinc reacts with dilute sulfuric acid.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> The salt is zinc sulfate.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Hydrogen gas is the other product.</p>

  <p>
    <strong>Balanced equation:</strong>
    Zn(s) + H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub>(aq) →
    ZnSO<sub>4</sub>(aq) + H<sub>2</sub>(g)
  </p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: Identifying the gas</strong></p>

  <p>
    Magnesium reacts with dilute hydrochloric acid. A gas is collected
    and burns with a squeaky pop when tested with a lighted splint.
    Identify the gas and explain your answer.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Recall the reaction pattern.</p>
  <p>
    A reactive metal reacting with a suitable dilute acid produces a
    salt and hydrogen.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Interpret the test.</p>
  <p>
    The squeaky-pop test is the characteristic test for hydrogen.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The gas is hydrogen.</p>

  <h3>4. Observations</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Bubbles may form as gas is released.</li>
    <li>The metal may gradually dissolve.</li>
    <li>The reaction may warm the mixture.</li>
    <li>The rate depends on the metal, acid concentration, temperature and surface area.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Carry out gas tests only with small quantities under teacher
    supervision, away from flames during gas collection.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Reactions of Acids with Metals', 15),
  (826, 826, '
  <h2>Reactions of Acids with Carbonates</h2>

  <h3>1. The reaction pattern</h3>

  <p>
    Acids react with metal carbonates to produce a <strong>salt, water
    and carbon dioxide gas</strong>.
  </p>

  <p><strong>General word equation:</strong></p>

  <p>Acid + Metal Carbonate → Salt + Water + Carbon Dioxide</p>

  <p>
    The salt depends on the acid and the metal in the carbonate.
    Hydrochloric acid forms chloride salts, while sulfuric acid forms
    sulfate salts.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Testing the gas</h3>

  <p>
    Carbon dioxide can be tested by passing it through clear limewater.
    The limewater turns milky because insoluble calcium carbonate forms.
  </p>

  <p>
    Carbon dioxide + Calcium hydroxide → Calcium carbonate + Water
  </p>

  <p>
    CO<sub>2</sub>(g) + Ca(OH)<sub>2</sub>(aq) →
    CaCO<sub>3</sub>(s) + H<sub>2</sub>O(l)
  </p>

  <h3>3. Worked examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: Calcium carbonate and hydrochloric acid</strong></p>

  <p>
    Write the balanced equation for calcium carbonate reacting with
    hydrochloric acid.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1: Identify the salt.</strong></p>
  <p>
    Calcium carbonate contains calcium, and hydrochloric acid supplies
    chloride ions. The salt is calcium chloride.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2: Write all the products.</strong></p>

  <p>
    CaCO<sub>3</sub> + HCl →
    CaCl<sub>2</sub> + H<sub>2</sub>O + CO<sub>2</sub>
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3: Balance the equation.</strong></p>
  <p>
    Place 2 before HCl to supply two chlorine atoms and two hydrogen atoms.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Balanced equation:</strong>
    CaCO<sub>3</sub>(s) + 2HCl(aq) →
    CaCl<sub>2</sub>(aq) + H<sub>2</sub>O(l) + CO<sub>2</sub>(g)
  </p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: Sodium carbonate and nitric acid</strong></p>

  <p>
    Predict the salt and write the balanced equation.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Sodium carbonate contains sodium, so the salt is sodium nitrate.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> The other products are water and carbon dioxide.</p>

  <p>
    <strong>Balanced equation:</strong>
    Na<sub>2</sub>CO<sub>3</sub>(s) + 2HNO<sub>3</sub>(aq) →
    2NaNO<sub>3</sub>(aq) + H<sub>2</sub>O(l) + CO<sub>2</sub>(g)
  </p>

  <p>
    Check: both sides contain two sodium atoms, two nitrogen atoms,
    two hydrogen atoms, one carbon atom and six oxygen atoms.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: Interpreting an observation</strong></p>

  <p>
    A carbonate is added to dilute acid. Bubbles form, and the gas turns
    limewater milky. Identify the gas and explain the evidence.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Carbonates reacting with acids release carbon dioxide.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Carbon dioxide turns limewater milky.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The gas is carbon dioxide.</p>

  <h3>4. What the observations mean</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Effervescence indicates that gas is being released.</li>
    <li>Milky limewater supports the identification of carbon dioxide.</li>
    <li>The carbonate may gradually dissolve as the reaction proceeds.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Bubbles alone are not enough to identify the gas; use the appropriate
    test to support the conclusion.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Reactions of Acids with Carbonates', 15),
  (827, 827, '
  <h2>Reactions of Acids with Hydrogen Carbonates</h2>

  <h3>1. What is a hydrogen carbonate?</h3>

  <p>
    A hydrogen carbonate contains the hydrogen carbonate ion,
    HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>−</sup>. Sodium hydrogen carbonate,
    NaHCO<sub>3</sub>, is one example.
  </p>

  <p>
    Hydrogen carbonates react with acids to form a <strong>salt, water
    and carbon dioxide</strong>.
  </p>

  <p><strong>General word equation:</strong></p>

  <p>
    Acid + Hydrogen Carbonate → Salt + Water + Carbon Dioxide
  </p>

  <p>
    The products resemble those of acid-carbonate reactions. However,
    a hydrogen carbonate contains HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>−</sup>, whereas
    a carbonate contains CO<sub>3</sub><sup>2−</sup>.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Worked examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: Sodium hydrogen carbonate and hydrochloric acid</strong></p>

  <p>
    Write the balanced equation for sodium hydrogen carbonate reacting
    with hydrochloric acid.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1: Identify the salt.</strong></p>
  <p>
    Sodium hydrogen carbonate supplies sodium, and hydrochloric acid
    supplies chloride. The salt is sodium chloride.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2: Identify the other products.</strong></p>
  <p>
    The reaction also forms water and carbon dioxide.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Balanced equation:</strong>
    NaHCO<sub>3</sub>(s) + HCl(aq) →
    NaCl(aq) + H<sub>2</sub>O(l) + CO<sub>2</sub>(g)
  </p>

  <p>
    Check: Na = 1, H = 2, C = 1, O = 3 and Cl = 1 on both sides.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: Potassium hydrogen carbonate and nitric acid</strong></p>

  <p>
    Predict the products when potassium hydrogen carbonate reacts with
    nitric acid.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> The salt is potassium nitrate, KNO<sub>3</sub>.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Water and carbon dioxide are also formed.</p>

  <p>
    <strong>Balanced equation:</strong>
    KHCO<sub>3</sub> + HNO<sub>3</sub> →
    KNO<sub>3</sub> + H<sub>2</sub>O + CO<sub>2</sub>
  </p>

  <p>
    All atoms balance with a coefficient of 1 for each substance.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: Distinguishing the gas from the salt</strong></p>

  <p>
    Sodium hydrogen carbonate reacts with dilute hydrochloric acid.
    Name the gas and the salt formed.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Use the reaction pattern to identify the gas.</p>
  <p>
    Acid-hydrogen carbonate reactions produce carbon dioxide.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Identify the salt from the reactants.</p>
  <p>
    Sodium hydrogen carbonate reacting with hydrochloric acid forms sodium chloride.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Carbon dioxide gas and sodium chloride.</p>

  <h3>3. Observations and interpretation</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Effervescence occurs as carbon dioxide is released.</li>
    <li>The hydrogen carbonate may gradually be used up.</li>
    <li>The gas turns limewater milky in the standard carbon dioxide test.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Remember the complete product pattern: the salt alone is not the
    only product. Water and carbon dioxide are also formed.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Reactions of Acids with Hydrogen Carbonates', 15),
  (828, 828, '
  <h2>Reactions of Acids with Metal Oxides</h2>

  <h3>1. The reaction pattern</h3>

  <p>
    Many basic metal oxides react with acids to form a <strong>salt
    and water</strong>.
  </p>

  <p><strong>General word equation:</strong></p>

  <p>Metal Oxide + Acid → Salt + Water</p>

  <p>
    The salt formed depends on the metal oxide and the acid used.
    For example, copper(II) oxide reacting with sulfuric acid forms
    copper(II) sulfate and water.
  </p>

  <p>
    This screen focuses on basic metal oxides. Not all metal oxides
    have identical chemical behaviour, so do not apply this reaction
    pattern indiscriminately to every oxide.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Worked examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: Copper(II) oxide and sulfuric acid</strong></p>

  <p>
    Write the balanced equation for copper(II) oxide reacting with
    sulfuric acid.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1: Identify the salt.</strong></p>
  <p>
    Copper(II) oxide contains copper, and sulfuric acid supplies the
    sulfate ion. The salt is copper(II) sulfate.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2: Predict the other product.</strong></p>
  <p>
    Water is formed when the basic oxide reacts with the acid.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Balanced equation:</strong>
    CuO(s) + H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub>(aq) →
    CuSO<sub>4</sub>(aq) + H<sub>2</sub>O(l)
  </p>

  <p>
    Check: Cu = 1, S = 1, H = 2 and O = 5 on both sides.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: Magnesium oxide and hydrochloric acid</strong></p>

  <p>
    Predict the products and balance the equation.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Magnesium chloride is the salt.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Water is the other product.</p>

  <p>
    Start with:
    MgO + HCl → MgCl<sub>2</sub> + H<sub>2</sub>O
  </p>

  <p>
    There are two chlorine atoms in MgCl<sub>2</sub>, so place 2 before HCl.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Balanced equation:</strong>
    MgO(s) + 2HCl(aq) →
    MgCl<sub>2</sub>(aq) + H<sub>2</sub>O(l)
  </p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: Identifying products</strong></p>

  <p>
    A learner reacts zinc oxide with dilute sulfuric acid. Identify the
    salt and write the balanced equation.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Zinc oxide provides zinc.</p>
  <p>
    Sulfuric acid supplies the sulfate ion, so the salt is zinc sulfate.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Add water as the other product.</p>

  <p>
    <strong>Balanced equation:</strong>
    ZnO(s) + H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub>(aq) →
    ZnSO<sub>4</sub>(aq) + H<sub>2</sub>O(l)
  </p>

  <p>
    Check: one zinc atom, one sulfur atom, two hydrogen atoms and five
    oxygen atoms appear on each side.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Understanding the observations</h3>

  <p>
    A metal oxide may gradually disappear as it reacts with the acid.
    The salt may remain dissolved if it is soluble. If excess solid
    remains after the reaction, it may need to be separated from the
    solution when preparing a salt.
  </p>

  <p>
    The key distinction for this screen is the product pattern:
    a basic metal oxide reacts with an acid to form a salt and water,
    not hydrogen gas or carbon dioxide.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Reactions of Acids with Metal Oxides', 15),
  (829, 829, '
  <h2>Bases</h2>

  <h3>1. What is a base?</h3>

  <p>
    A <strong>base</strong> is a substance that neutralises an acid.
    Many common bases are metal oxides or metal hydroxides. Some bases
    are soluble in water, while others are insoluble.
  </p>

  <p>
    For example, copper(II) oxide is a base that reacts with sulfuric
    acid to form copper(II) sulfate and water.
  </p>

  <p>
    CuO(s) + H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub>(aq) →
    CuSO<sub>4</sub>(aq) + H<sub>2</sub>O(l)
  </p>

  <h3>2. Important properties</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Bases neutralise acids.</li>
    <li>Many bases are metal oxides or metal hydroxides.</li>
    <li>Some bases dissolve in water, while others do not.</li>
    <li>Soluble bases form alkaline solutions in water.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    A base should not automatically be called an alkali. The term
    alkali specifically refers to a base that dissolves in water and
    produces an alkaline solution.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Worked examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: Identifying a base</strong></p>

  <p>
    Copper(II) oxide reacts with hydrochloric acid to form copper(II)
    chloride and water. Explain why copper(II) oxide is classified
    as a base.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Recall the defining property of a base.</p>
  <p>A base neutralises an acid.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Examine the reaction.</p>
  <p>
    Copper(II) oxide reacts with hydrochloric acid, producing a salt
    and water.
  </p>

  <p>
    CuO + 2HCl → CuCl<sub>2</sub> + H<sub>2</sub>O
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Copper(II) oxide is a base because it
  neutralises hydrochloric acid to form a salt and water.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: Identifying the products</strong></p>

  <p>
    Predict the products when magnesium oxide reacts with sulfuric acid.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the metal in the oxide: magnesium.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Identify the acid''s salt-forming ion: sulfate.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> The products are magnesium sulfate and water.</p>

  <p>
    <strong>Equation:</strong>
    MgO + H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub> →
    MgSO<sub>4</sub> + H<sub>2</sub>O
  </p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: Base or alkali?</strong></p>

  <p>
    A student has a sample of insoluble copper(II) oxide. Should the
    student describe it as an alkali?
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Recall that an alkali is a base soluble in water.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Copper(II) oxide is insoluble in water.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> It is a base, but it is not an alkali.</p>

  <h3>4. Key distinction</h3>

  <p>
    Every alkali is a base, but not every base is an alkali.
    Solubility in water distinguishes an alkali from an insoluble base.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Bases', 15),
  (830, 830, '
  <h2>Alkalis</h2>

  <h3>1. What is an alkali?</h3>

  <p>
    An <strong>alkali</strong> is a base that dissolves in water and
    produces an alkaline solution containing hydroxide ions,
    OH<sup>−</sup>.
  </p>

  <p>
    Examples include sodium hydroxide, potassium hydroxide and aqueous
    ammonia. Sodium hydroxide and potassium hydroxide are soluble
    metal hydroxides.
  </p>

  <p>
    Sodium hydroxide dissolves in water and separates into ions:
  </p>

  <p>
    NaOH(s) → Na<sup>+</sup>(aq) + OH<sup>−</sup>(aq)
  </p>

  <p>
    The hydroxide ions are responsible for the alkaline properties of
    the solution.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Properties of alkalis</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>They neutralise acids.</li>
    <li>Their aqueous solutions have a pH above 7 under the usual school-level conditions.</li>
    <li>They turn red litmus paper blue.</li>
    <li>Their aqueous solutions conduct electricity because they contain mobile ions.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Strong alkalis can be corrosive. Never taste chemicals to identify
    them, and avoid direct skin contact.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Worked examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: Identifying an alkali</strong></p>

  <p>
    Sodium hydroxide dissolves in water. Explain why the resulting
    solution is alkaline.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Sodium hydroxide separates into ions in water.</p>
  <p>
    NaOH → Na<sup>+</sup> + OH<sup>−</sup>
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Identify the important ion.</p>
  <p>
    The solution contains hydroxide ions, OH<sup>−</sup>.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> It is alkaline because dissolved sodium
  hydroxide produces hydroxide ions in water.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: Classifying a solution by pH</strong></p>

  <p>
    A solution has a pH of 11. Is it acidic, neutral or alkaline?
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Compare the pH with 7.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Since 11 is greater than 7, the solution is alkaline.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Alkaline.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: Electrical conductivity</strong></p>

  <p>
    Explain why aqueous potassium hydroxide conducts electricity.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Potassium hydroxide forms K<sup>+</sup> and OH<sup>−</sup> ions in water.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> These ions can move through the solution.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The mobile ions carry electric charge through the solution.</p>

  <h3>4. Remember</h3>

  <p>
    An alkali is not simply any substance that feels slippery or has
    a high pH. Its chemical classification depends on its behaviour
    in water. Never touch or taste an unknown chemical.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Alkalis', 15),
  (831, 831, '
  <h2>Litmus</h2>

  <h3>1. What is litmus?</h3>

  <p>
    Litmus is an acid-base indicator. It changes colour depending on
    whether it is exposed to an acidic or alkaline solution.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Acid:</strong> blue litmus turns red.</li>
    <li><strong>Alkali:</strong> red litmus turns blue.</li>
    <li><strong>Neutral solution:</strong> litmus shows no acid-or-alkali colour change; red litmus remains red and blue litmus remains blue.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Litmus helps classify a solution as acidic or alkaline, but it does
    not provide an exact pH value.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Using litmus correctly</h3>

  <p>
    Use clean litmus paper and avoid contaminating the sample. Test a
    small amount of solution according to laboratory instructions.
    Record the original colour and the final colour before drawing
    a conclusion.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Worked examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: Blue litmus turns red</strong></p>

  <p>
    A solution changes blue litmus paper to red. Classify the solution.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Recall the colour change caused by acids.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Compare the observed change with the rule.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The solution is acidic.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: Red litmus turns blue</strong></p>

  <p>
    Red litmus paper turns blue when placed in a solution. What does
    the result indicate?
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Red litmus turns blue in an alkaline solution.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Match the observation.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The solution is alkaline.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: No colour change</strong></p>

  <p>
    Red litmus remains red and blue litmus remains blue in a sample.
    Can you conclude the exact pH?
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Recognise that litmus does not measure pH numerically.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> No colour change with both papers is consistent with a neutral solution, but it does not establish an exact pH.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> No. Litmus cannot provide an exact pH value.</p>

  <h3>4. Limitation of litmus</h3>

  <p>
    Litmus gives a broad classification, not a numerical measurement.
    For more detailed colour-based pH estimation, universal indicator
    is more useful.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Litmus', 15),
  (832, 832, '
  <h2>Universal Indicator</h2>

  <h3>1. What is universal indicator?</h3>

  <p>
    Universal indicator is a mixture of indicators that produces
    different colours across a range of pH values. It helps estimate
    how acidic or alkaline a solution is.
  </p>

  <p>
    Unlike litmus, which mainly distinguishes acidic from alkaline
    solutions, universal indicator gives a range of colours that can
    be compared with a reference colour chart.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Interpreting its colours</h3>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Strongly acidic:</strong> typically red.</li>
    <li><strong>Weakly acidic:</strong> typically orange or yellow.</li>
    <li><strong>Near neutral:</strong> typically green around pH 7.</li>
    <li><strong>Alkaline:</strong> typically blue to purple as pH increases.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    The exact colour depends on the indicator formulation and the
    reference chart. Always compare the result with the chart supplied
    for the indicator being used.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Worked examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: A red universal-indicator result</strong></p>

  <p>
    Universal indicator turns red in a sample. What does the colour
    suggest?
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Compare red with the universal-indicator chart.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Red corresponds to a strongly acidic region.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The sample is strongly acidic, subject to the chart used.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: A green result</strong></p>

  <p>
    Universal indicator turns green. What pH region does this usually
    suggest?
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Green is the typical neutral colour.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Neutral aqueous solutions are represented around pH 7 on the usual school-level chart.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The solution is approximately neutral, around pH 7.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: Comparing two solutions</strong></p>

  <p>
    Sample A produces a yellow colour, while Sample B produces a blue
    colour. Which sample is more alkaline?
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Yellow typically indicates an acidic region.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Blue typically indicates an alkaline region.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Sample B is more alkaline.</p>

  <h3>4. Limitations</h3>

  <p>
    Universal indicator estimates pH by colour comparison. It does not
    normally give the precision of a properly calibrated pH meter.
    A contaminated sample or poor lighting can make the colour harder
    to interpret.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Universal Indicator', 15),
  (833, 833, '
  <h2>pH Scale</h2>

  <h3>1. What is pH?</h3>

  <p>
    The <strong>pH scale</strong> is used to express how acidic or
    alkaline an aqueous solution is. On the usual school-level scale,
    values range from 0 to 14, with 7 representing neutrality.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>pH below 7:</strong> acidic.</li>
    <li><strong>pH 7:</strong> neutral.</li>
    <li><strong>pH above 7:</strong> alkaline.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    These classifications apply to the usual school-level treatment
    of aqueous solutions. Values outside 0–14 can occur under some
    conditions, so the range is not an absolute limit for every
    possible solution.
  </p>

  <h3>2. What do pH numbers mean?</h3>

  <p>
    A lower pH indicates greater acidity, while a higher pH indicates
    greater alkalinity in the usual comparisons made at school.
  </p>

  <p>
    The pH scale is logarithmic. A change of one pH unit corresponds
    to a tenfold change in hydrogen-ion activity; in dilute solutions,
    this is commonly introduced as an approximately tenfold change
    in hydrogen-ion concentration.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Worked examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: Classifying pH 2</strong></p>

  <p>
    A solution has pH 2. Classify it.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Compare 2 with 7.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Since 2 is below 7, the solution is acidic.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Acidic.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: Classifying pH 7</strong></p>

  <p>
    A solution has pH 7 under the usual school-level conditions.
    Classify it.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify 7 as the neutral reference point.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Neutral.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: Comparing pH 3 and pH 5</strong></p>

  <p>
    Which solution is more acidic: one with pH 3 or one with pH 5?
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Compare the two values.</p>
  <p>
    Since 3 is lower than 5, the pH 3 solution is more acidic.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Find the difference.</p>
  <p>5 − 3 = 2 pH units.</p>

  <p>
    <strong>Answer:</strong> The pH 3 solution is more acidic. The
    difference is two pH units, corresponding to a hundredfold
    difference in hydrogen-ion activity.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Important caution</h3>

  <p>
    pH does not directly tell you the total amount of acid present.
    Concentration, volume and the identity of the acid matter when
    comparing how much acid two samples contain.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for pH Scale', 15),
  (834, 834, '
  <h2>Interpreting pH</h2>

  <h3>1. Reading a pH value</h3>

  <p>
    Interpreting pH means using the numerical value to classify a
    solution and compare its acidity or alkalinity.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>A lower pH indicates greater acidity.</li>
    <li>A pH near 7 is near neutral under the usual school-level conditions.</li>
    <li>A higher pH indicates greater alkalinity.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Remember that pH is logarithmic. A difference of one unit represents
    a tenfold difference in hydrogen-ion activity, not a simple increase
    or decrease by one equal-sized amount of acidity.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Worked examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: Ordering acidic solutions</strong></p>

  <p>
    Arrange solutions with pH 2, pH 4 and pH 6 from most acidic to
    least acidic.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Lower pH means greater acidity.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Order the numbers from lowest to highest.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> pH 2, pH 4, pH 6.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: Comparing pH 4 and pH 6</strong></p>

  <p>
    How many pH units separate the two solutions, and what is the
    approximate hydrogen-ion activity ratio?
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Calculate the difference.</p>
  <p>6 − 4 = 2 pH units.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Each pH unit corresponds to a tenfold change.</p>
  <p>
    Two units correspond to 10 × 10 = 100.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Answer:</strong> Two pH units; the pH 4 solution has
    approximately 100 times the hydrogen-ion activity of the pH 6
    solution.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: Interpreting a pH change</strong></p>

  <p>
    A solution changes from pH 5 to pH 3. Has its acidity increased
    or decreased?
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Compare the starting and final pH.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> The pH has fallen by two units.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> A lower pH indicates greater acidity.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Acidity has increased. The hydrogen-ion
  activity has increased by a factor of about 100.</p>

  <h3>3. Avoid these mistakes</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Do not say pH 6 is more acidic than pH 3; pH 3 is more acidic.</li>
    <li>Do not treat pH differences as ordinary linear differences in acidity.</li>
    <li>Do not infer the total amount of acid from pH alone.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Interpreting pH', 15),
  (835, 835, '
  <h2>Strong and Weak Acids</h2>

  <h3>1. What determines acid strength?</h3>

  <p>
    The strength of an acid depends on how completely it ionises in
    water, not simply on how concentrated its solution is.
  </p>

  <p>
    A <strong>strong acid</strong> ionises essentially completely in
    water. A <strong>weak acid</strong> ionises only partially, so an
    equilibrium is established between the un-ionised acid and its ions.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Strong acids</h3>

  <p>
    Hydrochloric acid is an example of a strong acid. In water, it
    ionises essentially completely:
  </p>

  <p>
    HCl(aq) → H<sup>+</sup>(aq) + Cl<sup>−</sup>(aq)
  </p>

  <p>
    This is a simplified school-level representation. In water,
    hydrogen ions are associated with water molecules as hydronium ions.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Weak acids</h3>

  <p>
    Ethanoic acid is an example of a weak acid. It ionises only
    partially in water:
  </p>

  <p>
    CH<sub>3</sub>COOH(aq) ⇌
    H<sup>+</sup>(aq) + CH<sub>3</sub>COO<sup>−</sup>(aq)
  </p>

  <p>
    The double arrow indicates that the reaction is reversible and
    that an equilibrium is established. Most ethanoic acid molecules
    remain un-ionised in a typical dilute aqueous solution.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Strength is not concentration</h3>

  <p>
    <strong>Strength</strong> describes the extent of ionisation.
    <strong>Concentration</strong> describes the amount of dissolved
    substance per unit volume of solution.
  </p>

  <p>
    A concentrated weak acid can contain more acid per unit volume
    than a very dilute strong acid. Therefore, the words strong and
    concentrated do not mean the same thing.
  </p>

  <h3>5. Worked examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: Classifying hydrochloric acid</strong></p>

  <p>
    Hydrochloric acid ionises essentially completely in water.
    Classify it as strong or weak.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Recall the definition of a strong acid.</p>
  <p>
    A strong acid ionises essentially completely in water.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Compare the given information with the definition.</p>
  <p>
    Hydrochloric acid ionises essentially completely.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Hydrochloric acid is a strong acid.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: Interpreting ethanoic acid ionisation</strong></p>

  <p>
    Ethanoic acid is represented by the equation:
  </p>

  <p>
    CH<sub>3</sub>COOH ⇌
    H<sup>+</sup> + CH<sub>3</sub>COO<sup>−</sup>
  </p>

  <p>
    Explain why the double arrow is appropriate.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Recall that a weak acid ionises partially.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Some acid molecules ionise, while some ions recombine.</p>

  <p>
    <strong>Answer:</strong> The double arrow represents reversible,
    partial ionisation and the establishment of equilibrium.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: Strength versus concentration</strong></p>

  <p>
    Solution A is a concentrated weak acid. Solution B is a very dilute
    strong acid. Can you determine which has the greater acid
    concentration from strength alone?
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify what strength measures.</p>
  <p>
    Strength describes the extent of ionisation.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Identify what concentration measures.</p>
  <p>
    Concentration describes the amount of dissolved acid per unit volume.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> No. The descriptions of strength alone
  are insufficient to determine which solution has the greater
  concentration.
  </p>

  <h3>6. Remember</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Strong acid: essentially complete ionisation.</li>
    <li>Weak acid: partial ionisation.</li>
    <li>Strength and concentration describe different properties.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Strong and Weak Acids', 15),
  (836, 836, '
  <h2>Strong and Weak Bases</h2>

  <h3>1. What determines base strength?</h3>

  <p>
    The strength of a base describes how it behaves in water and how
    effectively it produces or accepts ions involved in basic
    behaviour. For common school-level examples, strong bases produce
    hydroxide ions extensively in aqueous solution, while weak bases
    react with water only partially.
  </p>

  <p>
    Base strength is different from concentration. Concentration
    describes how much substance is dissolved per unit volume.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Strong bases</h3>

  <p>
    Sodium hydroxide is a strong base. When it dissolves in water,
    it dissociates essentially completely into sodium ions and
    hydroxide ions:
  </p>

  <p>
    NaOH(s) → Na<sup>+</sup>(aq) + OH<sup>−</sup>(aq)
  </p>

  <p>
    Potassium hydroxide behaves similarly:
  </p>

  <p>
    KOH(s) → K<sup>+</sup>(aq) + OH<sup>−</sup>(aq)
  </p>

  <h3>3. Weak bases</h3>

  <p>
    Aqueous ammonia is a common example of a weak base. It reacts
    partially with water to form ammonium ions and hydroxide ions:
  </p>

  <p>
    NH<sub>3</sub>(aq) + H<sub>2</sub>O(l) ⇌
    NH<sub>4</sub><sup>+</sup>(aq) + OH<sup>−</sup>(aq)
  </p>

  <p>
    The double arrow indicates an equilibrium. Most dissolved ammonia
    remains as NH<sub>3</sub> rather than forming ions through this
    reaction.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Strength versus concentration</h3>

  <p>
    A dilute solution of a strong base can have a lower hydroxide-ion
    concentration than a more concentrated solution of a weak base.
    The exact comparison depends on the concentrations and chemical
    equilibria involved.
  </p>

  <p>
    Therefore, the word strong does not automatically mean concentrated,
    and the word weak does not mean dilute.
  </p>

  <h3>5. Worked examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: Sodium hydroxide</strong></p>

  <p>
    Sodium hydroxide dissociates essentially completely in water.
    Explain what this means.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the ions formed.</p>
  <p>NaOH → Na<sup>+</sup> + OH<sup>−</sup></p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Interpret the arrow.</p>
  <p>
    The single arrow represents essentially complete dissociation
    of dissolved sodium hydroxide into its ions.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The dissolved sodium hydroxide produces
  sodium and hydroxide ions essentially completely.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: Aqueous ammonia</strong></p>

  <p>
    Explain why aqueous ammonia is classified as a weak base.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Examine its reaction with water.</p>

  <p>
    NH<sub>3</sub> + H<sub>2</sub>O ⇌
    NH<sub>4</sub><sup>+</sup> + OH<sup>−</sup>
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Interpret the double arrow.</p>
  <p>
    The reaction is reversible, and only a proportion of the ammonia
    reacts with water to produce ions.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Aqueous ammonia is a weak base because
  its reaction with water is partial and establishes an equilibrium.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: Strength and concentration</strong></p>

  <p>
    A learner calls sodium hydroxide weak because its solution is
    dilute. Explain the error.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Strength describes the extent of the
  relevant ion formation or reaction.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Dilute describes the amount of substance
  per unit volume.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The conclusion is incorrect. Sodium
  hydroxide is a strong base even when its solution is dilute.
  </p>

  <h3>6. Remember</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Sodium hydroxide is a strong base.</li>
    <li>Aqueous ammonia is a weak base.</li>
    <li>Strong or weak describes base behaviour, not solution concentration.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Strong and Weak Bases', 15),
  (837, 837, '
  <h2>Neutralisation</h2>

  <h3>1. What is neutralisation?</h3>

  <p>
    <strong>Neutralisation</strong> is a reaction in which an acid
    reacts with a base. In the usual school-level reaction between
    an acid and a base such as a metal hydroxide, the products are
    a salt and water.
  </p>

  <p><strong>General word equation:</strong></p>

  <p>Acid + Base → Salt + Water</p>

  <p>
    The salt formed depends on the acid and the base used. For example,
    hydrochloric acid forms chloride salts, while sulfuric acid forms
    sulfate salts.
  </p>

  <h3>2. The ionic explanation</h3>

  <p>
    For the reaction between an aqueous acid and a soluble hydroxide,
    hydrogen ions react with hydroxide ions to form water:
  </p>

  <p>
    H<sup>+</sup>(aq) + OH<sup>−</sup>(aq) →
    H<sub>2</sub>O(l)
  </p>

  <p>
    This is the simplified net ionic equation used at school level.
    In water, hydrogen ions are associated with water molecules.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Worked examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: Hydrochloric acid and sodium hydroxide</strong></p>

  <p>
    Write the balanced equation for hydrochloric acid reacting with
    sodium hydroxide.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1: Identify the salt.</strong></p>
  <p>
    Hydrochloric acid supplies chloride, and sodium hydroxide supplies
    sodium. The salt is sodium chloride.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2: Add water as the other product.</strong></p>

  <p>
    HCl + NaOH → NaCl + H<sub>2</sub>O
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3: Check the equation.</strong></p>
  <p>
    There is one sodium, one chlorine, two hydrogen and one oxygen
    atom on each side.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong>
    HCl(aq) + NaOH(aq) → NaCl(aq) + H<sub>2</sub>O(l)
  </p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: Sulfuric acid and potassium hydroxide</strong></p>

  <p>
    Write the balanced equation for sulfuric acid reacting completely
    with potassium hydroxide.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> The salt is potassium sulfate, K<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub>.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Write the unbalanced equation.</p>

  <p>
    H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub> + KOH →
    K<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub> + H<sub>2</sub>O
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Balance potassium.</p>
  <p>
    Place 2 before KOH to supply two potassium atoms.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Balance hydrogen and oxygen.</p>
  <p>
    Two KOH units and one sulfuric acid molecule contain four hydrogen
    atoms in total, so place 2 before water.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Balanced equation:</strong></p>

  <p>
    H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub> + 2KOH →
    K<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub> + 2H<sub>2</sub>O
  </p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: Net ionic equation</strong></p>

  <p>
    Write the net ionic equation for hydrogen ions reacting with
    hydroxide ions during neutralisation.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the reacting ions: H<sup>+</sup> and OH<sup>−</sup>.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Their product is water.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong>
    H<sup>+</sup>(aq) + OH<sup>−</sup>(aq) →
    H<sub>2</sub>O(l)
  </p>

  <h3>4. Understanding neutralisation</h3>

  <p>
    Neutralisation does not mean that every mixture of acid and base
    automatically ends at pH 7. The final pH depends on the quantities
    and strengths of the substances and the reaction conditions.
  </p>

  <p>
    A neutral solution is one with the appropriate balance of acidic
    and basic character; at ordinary school-level conditions, pure
    water is represented as pH 7.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Neutralisation', 15),
  (838, 838, '
  <h2>Soluble Salts</h2>

  <h3>Meaning of a Salt</h3>
  <p>A <strong>salt</strong> is a compound formed when the hydrogen ions of an acid are replaced by metal ions or ammonium ions. Salts contain positive ions and negative ions.</p>

  <h3>Meaning of Solubility</h3>
  <p>A salt is described as <strong>soluble</strong> if it dissolves in water to form a solution. A salt described as insoluble dissolves only to a very small extent in water under the stated conditions.</p>
  <p>Solubility is not the same as whether a substance is a solid. A soluble salt may be a solid before it is added to water.</p>

  <h3>Common Soluble Salts</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Sodium chloride — NaCl</li>
    <li>Potassium nitrate — KNO₃</li>
    <li>Copper(II) sulfate — CuSO₄</li>
    <li>Magnesium sulfate — MgSO₄</li>
    <li>Ammonium chloride — NH₄Cl</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>How the Acid Helps Name the Salt</h3>
  <p>The acid used determines the negative ion, which helps determine the salt''s name:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Hydrochloric acid forms <strong>chloride</strong> salts.</li>
    <li>Sulfuric acid forms <strong>sulfate</strong> salts.</li>
    <li>Nitric acid forms <strong>nitrate</strong> salts.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The positive ion identifies the other part of the salt''s name. For example, copper ions combined with sulfate ions form copper(II) sulfate.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Identify a Soluble Salt</h3>
  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Name the salt NaCl.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Na represents sodium.</li>
    <li>Cl in this compound represents chloride.</li>
    <li>Combine the ion names: sodium chloride.</li>
  </ol>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Sodium chloride.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Name the Salt from Its Acid</h3>
  <p><strong>Question:</strong> What salt can be formed when sulfuric acid reacts with copper(II) oxide?</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Sulfuric acid forms sulfate salts.</li>
    <li>The copper(II) oxide supplies copper(II) ions to the salt.</li>
    <li>Combine the names copper(II) and sulfate.</li>
  </ol>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Copper(II) sulfate, CuSO₄.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Identify the Acid from the Salt</h3>
  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Which acid provides the nitrate part of potassium nitrate?</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Identify the negative ion in potassium nitrate: nitrate, NO₃⁻.</li>
    <li>Nitrate salts are associated with nitric acid.</li>
  </ol>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Nitric acid, HNO₃.</p>

  <h3>Check Your Understanding</h3>
  <p>Answer each question in writing. Explain your reasoning where requested.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Soluble Salts', 15),
  (839, 839, '
  <h2>Insoluble Salts</h2>

  <h3>Meaning of an Insoluble Salt</h3>
  <p>An <strong>insoluble salt</strong> is a salt that dissolves only to a very small extent in water under the stated conditions. When it forms as a solid from aqueous solutions, the solid is called a <strong>precipitate</strong>.</p>
  <p>Insoluble does not mean that absolutely no particles dissolve. It means that the amount that dissolves is very small.</p>

  <h3>Examples of Insoluble Salts</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Silver chloride — AgCl</li>
    <li>Barium sulfate — BaSO₄</li>
    <li>Lead(II) iodide — PbI₂</li>
  </ul>
  <p>These are examples of salts that are very sparingly soluble in water. Lead compounds are toxic and must not be handled casually.</p>

  <h3>Recognising an Insoluble Salt</h3>
  <p>If solutions containing suitable ions are mixed, an insoluble salt may form as a solid. The appearance of the solid indicates that a new substance has formed, rather than the product remaining dissolved.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Name an Insoluble Salt</h3>
  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Name AgCl.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Ag represents silver.</li>
    <li>Cl represents chloride in this compound.</li>
    <li>Combine the names.</li>
  </ol>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Silver chloride.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Identify the Solid</h3>
  <p><strong>Question:</strong> A white solid of silver chloride forms when suitable aqueous solutions are mixed. What is the solid called in this reaction?</p>
  <ol>
    <li>The product is a salt.</li>
    <li>It forms as a solid from solutions.</li>
    <li>A solid formed in this way is called a precipitate.</li>
  </ol>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Silver chloride precipitate.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Interpret a Formula</h3>
  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Name BaSO₄ and identify the two ions.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Ba²⁺ is the barium ion.</li>
    <li>SO₄²⁻ is the sulfate ion.</li>
    <li>The compound is named barium sulfate.</li>
  </ol>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Barium sulfate, made from barium ions and sulfate ions.</p>

  <h3>Check Your Understanding</h3>
  <p>Answer each question in writing.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Insoluble Salts', 15),
  (840, 840, '
  <h2>Preparing Soluble Salts from Acids and Metals</h2>

  <h3>When This Method Is Suitable</h3>
  <p>This method is suitable when a suitable reactive metal reacts with a dilute acid to form the required soluble salt. Not every metal reacts with every dilute acid. For example, copper does not react with dilute hydrochloric acid under ordinary laboratory conditions.</p>

  <h3>General Reaction</h3>
  <p><strong>Reactive metal + dilute acid → salt + hydrogen gas</strong></p>
  <p>The acid determines the negative ion of the salt. For example, sulfuric acid forms sulfate salts, while hydrochloric acid forms chloride salts.</p>

  <h3>Laboratory Preparation</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Place a measured amount of a suitable dilute acid in a suitable container and warm it gently if instructed.</li>
    <li>Add small portions of the reactive metal carefully.</li>
    <li>Continue until the reaction stops and a little metal remains in excess. This helps ensure that the acid has been used up.</li>
    <li>Filter the mixture to remove the excess unreacted metal.</li>
    <li>Gently heat the filtrate to concentrate the salt solution until it reaches the crystallisation point.</li>
    <li>Allow the solution to cool so crystals can form.</li>
    <li>Separate the crystals and dry them appropriately.</li>
  </ol>
  <p><strong>Safety:</strong> Hydrogen is flammable. Keep flames away from the reaction, use appropriate eye protection, and follow laboratory supervision. Never seal a reacting mixture in a closed container.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Magnesium and Hydrochloric Acid</h3>
  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Write the balanced equation for magnesium reacting with dilute hydrochloric acid.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>The products are magnesium chloride and hydrogen.</li>
    <li>Write the formula equation: Mg + HCl → MgCl₂ + H₂.</li>
    <li>MgCl₂ contains two chlorine atoms, so place 2 before HCl.</li>
    <li>Two hydrogen atoms then form one H₂ molecule.</li>
  </ol>
  <p><strong>Balanced equation:</strong> Mg(s) + 2HCl(aq) → MgCl₂(aq) + H₂(g).</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Zinc and Sulfuric Acid</h3>
  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Write the balanced equation for zinc reacting with dilute sulfuric acid.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>The salt formed is zinc sulfate.</li>
    <li>Hydrogen gas is the other product.</li>
    <li>The formula equation is already balanced in a 1:1:1:1 ratio.</li>
  </ol>
  <p><strong>Balanced equation:</strong> Zn(s) + H₂SO₄(aq) → ZnSO₄(aq) + H₂(g).</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Choosing the Correct Preparation Steps</h3>
  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Why is excess magnesium filtered off before the salt solution is concentrated?</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Magnesium is added in excess to help use up the acid.</li>
    <li>Any remaining magnesium is an unreacted solid.</li>
    <li>Filtration separates that solid from the salt solution.</li>
  </ol>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> To remove the unreacted magnesium before crystallising the dissolved salt.</p>

  <h3>Check Your Understanding</h3>
  <p>Answer each question in writing.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Preparing Soluble Salts from Acids and Metals', 15),
  (841, 841, '
  <h2>Preparing Soluble Salts from Insoluble Bases</h2>

  <h3>Principle</h3>
  <p>An insoluble base, such as copper(II) oxide, can react with a suitable dilute acid to form a salt and water. Because the base is insoluble, excess solid can be filtered off after the reaction.</p>

  <h3>General Reaction</h3>
  <p><strong>Insoluble base + acid → salt + water</strong></p>
  <p>This method does not produce hydrogen gas. The base neutralises the acid.</p>

  <h3>Laboratory Preparation</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Place a suitable dilute acid in a container and warm it gently if instructed.</li>
    <li>Add the insoluble base in small portions while stirring.</li>
    <li>Continue until a little unreacted base remains, indicating that the acid has been used up.</li>
    <li>Filter the mixture to remove the excess solid base.</li>
    <li>Gently heat the filtrate to concentrate the salt solution to the crystallisation point.</li>
    <li>Allow it to cool so crystals form.</li>
    <li>Separate and dry the crystals.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Copper(II) Oxide and Sulfuric Acid</h3>
  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Write the balanced equation for the reaction.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Copper(II) oxide reacts with sulfuric acid.</li>
    <li>The products are copper(II) sulfate and water.</li>
    <li>Write CuO + H₂SO₄ → CuSO₄ + H₂O.</li>
    <li>Count the atoms on both sides; the equation is balanced.</li>
  </ol>
  <p><strong>Balanced equation:</strong> CuO(s) + H₂SO₄(aq) → CuSO₄(aq) + H₂O(l).</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Magnesium Oxide and Hydrochloric Acid</h3>
  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Write the balanced equation for magnesium oxide reacting with hydrochloric acid.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>The salt formed is magnesium chloride.</li>
    <li>Water is the other product.</li>
    <li>Write MgO + HCl → MgCl₂ + H₂O.</li>
    <li>Place 2 before HCl to balance chlorine and hydrogen.</li>
  </ol>
  <p><strong>Balanced equation:</strong> MgO(s) + 2HCl(aq) → MgCl₂(aq) + H₂O(l).</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Why Filter the Mixture?</h3>
  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Copper(II) oxide was added until some remained unreacted. Why is the mixture filtered?</p>
  <ol>
    <li>The extra copper(II) oxide is an insoluble solid.</li>
    <li>The formed copper(II) sulfate is dissolved in the solution.</li>
    <li>Filtration removes the undissolved solid while the solution passes through.</li>
  </ol>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> To remove excess copper(II) oxide from the copper(II) sulfate solution.</p>

  <h3>Check Your Understanding</h3>
  <p>Answer each question in writing.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Preparing Soluble Salts from Insoluble Bases', 15),
  (842, 842, '
  <h2>Preparing Soluble Salts from Acids and Carbonates</h2>

  <h3>Principle</h3>
  <p>A suitable carbonate reacts with an acid to produce a salt, water and carbon dioxide. An insoluble carbonate can be added in excess, then the unreacted solid can be removed by filtration.</p>

  <h3>General Reaction</h3>
  <p><strong>Carbonate + acid → salt + water + carbon dioxide</strong></p>
  <p>Effervescence, or fizzing, occurs because carbon dioxide gas is released.</p>

  <h3>Laboratory Preparation</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Place a suitable dilute acid in a container and warm it gently if instructed.</li>
    <li>Add small portions of a suitable insoluble carbonate while stirring.</li>
    <li>Continue until the reaction stops and a little carbonate remains in excess.</li>
    <li>Filter to remove the unreacted carbonate.</li>
    <li>Gently heat the filtrate to concentrate the salt solution to the crystallisation point.</li>
    <li>Allow it to cool so crystals form.</li>
    <li>Separate and dry the crystals.</li>
  </ol>
  <p><strong>Safety:</strong> Avoid inhaling gases directly. Use appropriate laboratory supervision and do not seal a reacting mixture in a closed container.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Copper(II) Carbonate and Sulfuric Acid</h3>
  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Write the balanced equation for the reaction.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>The salt formed is copper(II) sulfate.</li>
    <li>Water and carbon dioxide are also formed.</li>
    <li>Write CuCO₃ + H₂SO₄ → CuSO₄ + H₂O + CO₂.</li>
    <li>Count the atoms on both sides; the equation is balanced.</li>
  </ol>
  <p><strong>Balanced equation:</strong> CuCO₃(s) + H₂SO₄(aq) → CuSO₄(aq) + H₂O(l) + CO₂(g).</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Zinc Carbonate and Hydrochloric Acid</h3>
  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Write the balanced equation for zinc carbonate reacting with hydrochloric acid.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>The salt formed is zinc chloride.</li>
    <li>Water and carbon dioxide are the other products.</li>
    <li>Write ZnCO₃ + HCl → ZnCl₂ + H₂O + CO₂.</li>
    <li>Place 2 before HCl to balance hydrogen and chlorine.</li>
  </ol>
  <p><strong>Balanced equation:</strong> ZnCO₃(s) + 2HCl(aq) → ZnCl₂(aq) + H₂O(l) + CO₂(g).</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Identifying the Gas</h3>
  <p><strong>Question:</strong> A carbonate reacts with a dilute acid and releases a gas that turns limewater milky. Identify the gas.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>The reaction of an acid with a carbonate produces a salt, water and a gas.</li>
    <li>The gas that turns limewater milky is carbon dioxide.</li>
  </ol>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Carbon dioxide, CO₂.</p>

  <h3>Check Your Understanding</h3>
  <p>Answer each question in writing.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Preparing Soluble Salts from Acids and Carbonates', 15),
