  <h2>Titration</h2>

  <h3>What Is Titration?</h3>
  <p><strong>Titration</strong> is a laboratory method used to determine the volume of one solution required to react completely with a measured volume of another solution.</p>

  <p>In an acid-base titration, an acid reacts with a base. An indicator helps show when the reaction has reached its endpoint, which is used to identify the appropriate volume of solution required.</p>

  <h3>Apparatus Used</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Burette:</strong> delivers a measured, variable volume of solution.</li>
    <li><strong>Pipette:</strong> measures and transfers a fixed volume of solution.</li>
    <li><strong>Conical flask:</strong> holds the measured solution being titrated.</li>
    <li><strong>Indicator:</strong> changes colour over a suitable pH range to help identify the endpoint.</li>
    <li><strong>White tile:</strong> makes the indicator colour change easier to observe.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Basic Titration Procedure</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Rinse the pipette with the solution it will measure and use it to transfer a known volume into a conical flask.</li>
    <li>Add a few drops of a suitable indicator to the solution in the flask.</li>
    <li>Rinse the burette with the solution it will deliver, fill it, and record the initial burette reading.</li>
    <li>Add the burette solution to the flask while swirling the flask gently.</li>
    <li>As the endpoint approaches, add the solution drop by drop until the indicator shows a permanent endpoint colour change.</li>
    <li>Record the final burette reading.</li>
    <li>Calculate the volume delivered by subtracting the initial reading from the final reading.</li>
    <li>Repeat the titration to obtain closely agreeing results where required.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Calculating the Titre</h3>
  <p>The <strong>titre</strong> is the volume delivered from the burette during a titration.</p>

  <p><strong>Titre = final burette reading − initial burette reading</strong></p>

  <p>Burette readings are normally recorded in cm³. The titre is also expressed in cm³.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Calculating a Titre</h3>
  <p><strong>Question:</strong> The initial burette reading is 1.20 cm³ and the final reading is 24.60 cm³. Calculate the titre.</p>

  <ol>
    <li>Write the formula: titre = final reading − initial reading.</li>
    <li>Substitute the readings: titre = 24.60 − 1.20.</li>
    <li>Subtract: 24.60 − 1.20 = 23.40.</li>
  </ol>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The titre is 23.40 cm³.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Finding a Missing Final Reading</h3>
  <p><strong>Question:</strong> A titration has a titre of 22.75 cm³ and an initial burette reading of 0.50 cm³. Find the final reading.</p>

  <ol>
    <li>Start with titre = final reading − initial reading.</li>
    <li>Rearrange: final reading = titre + initial reading.</li>
    <li>Substitute: final reading = 22.75 + 0.50.</li>
    <li>Add: 22.75 + 0.50 = 23.25.</li>
  </ol>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The final burette reading is 23.25 cm³.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Comparing Two Titrations</h3>
  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Two titrations give titres of 24.20 cm³ and 24.30 cm³. Calculate their mean titre.</p>

  <ol>
    <li>Add the two titres: 24.20 + 24.30 = 48.50 cm³.</li>
    <li>Divide the total by the number of titres: 48.50 ÷ 2 = 24.25 cm³.</li>
  </ol>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The mean titre is 24.25 cm³.</p>
  <p>In an actual experiment, use the closely agreeing titres specified by the practical instructions. Do not automatically average results that differ substantially.</p>

  <h3>Understanding the Endpoint</h3>
  <p>The endpoint is the point at which the indicator changes colour according to the chosen indicator''s transition range. A suitable indicator should be selected for the acid-base reaction being investigated.</p>

  <p>The endpoint is an experimental observation. It is used to estimate the equivalence point, where the reacting amounts are in the stoichiometric proportions required by the balanced chemical equation.</p>

  <h3>Check Your Understanding</h3>
  <p>Answer each question in writing. Show your calculations where necessary.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Titration', 15),
  (844, 844, '
  <h2>Exothermic Reactions</h2>

  <h3>1. Meaning of an Exothermic Reaction</h3>

  <p>
    An <strong>exothermic reaction</strong> is a chemical reaction that transfers energy
    from the reacting chemicals to the surroundings. The energy transferred is commonly
    released as heat, although some reactions also release energy as light or sound.
  </p>

  <p>
    The <strong>surroundings</strong> are everything outside the reacting system.
    For example, if chemicals react inside a test tube, the chemicals are the system,
    while the test tube, thermometer and surrounding air form part of the surroundings.
  </p>

  <p>
    When an exothermic reaction transfers heat to its surroundings, the surroundings
    usually become warmer. If the reaction takes place in a solution, a thermometer
    will often show an increase in temperature, provided that the temperature change
    caused by the reaction is greater than any heat lost to the environment.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Where Does the Released Energy Come From?</h3>

  <p>
    Chemical substances contain energy associated with the arrangement of their particles
    and the bonds between their atoms. During a chemical reaction, bonds in the reactants
    are broken and new bonds form in the products.
  </p>

  <p>
    Breaking chemical bonds requires energy. Forming chemical bonds releases energy.
    In an exothermic reaction, the energy released when new bonds form is greater than
    the energy required to break the bonds in the reactants. The difference is transferred
    to the surroundings.
  </p>

  <p>
    This is why an exothermic reaction releases energy overall, even though energy must
    first be supplied to break bonds.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Energy Change of an Exothermic Reaction</h3>

  <p>
    The products of an exothermic reaction have less chemical energy than the reactants.
    The difference in energy is released to the surroundings.
  </p>

  <p>
    The energy change can be represented as:
  </p>

  <p><strong>Energy change = Energy of products − Energy of reactants</strong></p>

  <p>
    For an exothermic reaction, the energy of the products is lower than the energy of
    the reactants. Therefore, the energy change is negative.
  </p>

  <p>
    Using the symbol ΔH for enthalpy change:
    <strong>ΔH is negative (ΔH &lt; 0)</strong> for an exothermic reaction.
    The negative sign indicates that energy has been released by the reacting system.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Examples of Exothermic Reactions</h3>

  <p><strong>Combustion:</strong> Burning a fuel releases energy to the surroundings.</p>

  <p><strong>Neutralisation:</strong> When a suitable acid reacts with a suitable alkali,
  energy is released and the temperature of the mixture commonly rises.</p>

  <p><strong>Respiration:</strong> Cells release energy through chemical reactions that
  break down glucose. The released energy supports cellular activities.</p>

  <h3>5. Worked Examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: Identifying a reaction</strong></p>

  <p>
    A fuel burns and releases heat. Explain why the combustion reaction is classified
    as exothermic.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the direction of energy transfer. Heat moves from
  the reacting system to the surroundings.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Apply the definition. A reaction that transfers energy
  to the surroundings is exothermic.</p>

  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> The combustion reaction is exothermic because it
  releases energy to the surroundings.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: Interpreting a temperature change</strong></p>

  <p>
    Two solutions are mixed in an insulated container. Their temperature rises from
    22°C to 29°C. What does this observation suggest about the reaction?
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Calculate the temperature change.</p>

  <p>Temperature change = Final temperature − Initial temperature</p>

  <p>= 29°C − 22°C = 7°C</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Interpret the positive temperature change. The mixture
  became warmer, indicating that energy was transferred to the surroundings.</p>

  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> The result supports the conclusion that the reaction
  is exothermic, assuming the rise was caused by the reaction.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: Calculating an energy change</strong></p>

  <p>
    The reactants in a reaction have an energy of 250 kJ, while the products have
    an energy of 180 kJ. Calculate the energy change and classify the reaction.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Use the formula:</p>

  <p>Energy change = Energy of products − Energy of reactants</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Substitute the values.</p>

  <p>Energy change = 180 kJ − 250 kJ = −70 kJ</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Interpret the sign. The energy change is negative,
  meaning that the products have less energy than the reactants.</p>

  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> The reaction is exothermic and releases 70 kJ
  of energy per stated reaction event or amount of reaction represented by the data.</p>

  <h3>6. Common Misunderstandings</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>An exothermic reaction releases energy; it does not absorb energy overall.</li>
    <li>The reaction mixture often warms up, but heat loss to the environment can affect the measured temperature.</li>
    <li>Energy is needed to break bonds. The reaction is exothermic because bond formation releases more energy than bond breaking requires.</li>
    <li>A negative energy change indicates that the products have less energy than the reactants.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Exothermic Reactions', 15),
  (845, 845, '
  <h2>Endothermic Reactions</h2>

  <h3>1. Meaning of an Endothermic Reaction</h3>

  <p>
    An <strong>endothermic reaction</strong> is a chemical reaction that absorbs energy
    from its surroundings. The absorbed energy is commonly transferred as heat.
  </p>

  <p>
    The reacting chemicals are called the system. Everything outside the reacting system
    is part of the surroundings. In an endothermic reaction, energy moves from the
    surroundings into the reacting system.
  </p>

  <p>
    When the reaction absorbs heat from a solution or its surroundings, the measured
    temperature often falls. The surroundings lose energy as the reacting system takes
    it in. However, the observed temperature change depends on the experimental setup
    and whether energy enters from other sources.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Why Does an Endothermic Reaction Absorb Energy?</h3>

  <p>
    During a chemical reaction, bonds in the reactants break and new bonds form in
    the products. Breaking bonds requires energy, while forming bonds releases energy.
  </p>

  <p>
    In an endothermic reaction, the energy required to break bonds is greater than the
    energy released when new bonds form. The difference is absorbed from the surroundings.
  </p>

  <p>
    This does not mean that no energy is released during the reaction. Some energy is
    released when product bonds form, but it is less than the energy required overall.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Energy Change in an Endothermic Reaction</h3>

  <p>
    The products of an endothermic reaction have more energy than the reactants.
    The reacting system absorbs the difference from its surroundings.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Energy change = Energy of products − Energy of reactants</strong></p>

  <p>
    Since the products have more energy, the energy change is positive.
    In enthalpy notation, <strong>ΔH &gt; 0</strong> for an endothermic reaction.
  </p>

  <p>
    A positive energy change indicates that energy has been absorbed by the reacting
    system. It does not mean that the reaction necessarily occurs quickly or slowly.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Examples of Endothermic Reactions</h3>

  <p>
    <strong>Thermal decomposition:</strong> Some compounds absorb heat as they break down
    into simpler substances. For example, calcium carbonate decomposes when heated:
  </p>

  <p>CaCO₃(s) → CaO(s) + CO₂(g)</p>

  <p>
    Heat must be supplied to maintain this decomposition under suitable conditions.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Photosynthesis:</strong> Plants absorb light energy and use it to drive
    the chemical process of making glucose from carbon dioxide and water.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Some dissolving processes:</strong> Certain substances absorb heat from their
    surroundings when they dissolve in water. This is an endothermic dissolving process;
    not every substance dissolves endothermically.
  </p>

  <h3>5. Worked Examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: Identifying energy transfer</strong></p>

  <p>
    A reaction absorbs 85 kJ of energy from its surroundings. Classify the reaction
    and explain your decision.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the direction of transfer. Energy moves from
  the surroundings into the reacting system.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Apply the definition. A reaction that absorbs energy from
  the surroundings is endothermic.</p>

  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> The reaction is endothermic because it absorbs
  85 kJ of energy.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: Interpreting a temperature decrease</strong></p>

  <p>
    A mixture cools from 27°C to 19°C during a reaction. Calculate the temperature
    change and explain what it suggests.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Use temperature change = final temperature − initial temperature.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Substitute the values.</p>

  <p>Temperature change = 19°C − 27°C = −8°C</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Interpret the result. The mixture''s temperature decreased
  by 8°C, suggesting that energy was absorbed from the surroundings.</p>

  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> The observation supports an endothermic classification,
  assuming the reaction caused the cooling.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: Calculating energy change</strong></p>

  <p>
    The reactants have an energy of 150 kJ and the products have an energy of 225 kJ.
    Calculate the energy change and classify the reaction.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Write the formula.</p>

  <p>Energy change = Energy of products − Energy of reactants</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Substitute the values.</p>

  <p>Energy change = 225 kJ − 150 kJ = +75 kJ</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Interpret the positive sign. The products contain 75 kJ
  more energy than the reactants.</p>

  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> The reaction is endothermic and absorbs 75 kJ of
  energy per stated reaction event or amount of reaction represented by the data.</p>

  <h3>6. Common Misunderstandings</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>An endothermic reaction absorbs energy from the surroundings.</li>
    <li>A temperature decrease can indicate an endothermic process, but other causes of cooling must be ruled out.</li>
    <li>Energy is still released when product bonds form. The reaction is endothermic because the energy required to break bonds is greater overall.</li>
    <li>A positive energy change means the products have more energy than the reactants.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Endothermic Reactions', 15),
  (846, 846, '
  <h2>Energy Profile Diagrams</h2>

  <h3>1. What Is an Energy Profile Diagram?</h3>

  <p>
    An <strong>energy profile diagram</strong> is a graph showing how the energy of a
    reacting system changes as a reaction progresses from reactants to products.
    It helps us compare the energy levels of the reactants and products and identify
    the energy barrier that must be overcome during the reaction.
  </p>

  <p>
    The horizontal axis represents the <strong>progress of the reaction</strong>.
    It does not usually represent time. The vertical axis represents the energy of
    the reacting system.
  </p>

  <p>
    The curve usually rises from the reactants to a maximum and then falls towards
    the products. The maximum represents the high-energy state associated with the
    reaction pathway. The energy difference between the reactants and this maximum
    is the activation energy for the forward reaction.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Energy Profile for an Exothermic Reaction</h3>

  <p>
    In an exothermic reaction, the products have less energy than the reactants.
    The curve therefore ends below the starting energy level.
  </p>

  <p>
    The vertical difference between the reactants and products represents the overall
    energy change. Since the products have less energy, the energy change is negative.
  </p>

  <p>
    For example, if the reactants have an energy of 300 kJ and the products have
    an energy of 180 kJ:
  </p>

  <p>Energy change = 180 kJ − 300 kJ = −120 kJ</p>

  <p>
    The reaction releases 120 kJ per stated reaction event or amount of reaction
    represented by the data.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Energy Profile for an Endothermic Reaction</h3>

  <p>
    In an endothermic reaction, the products have more energy than the reactants.
    The curve therefore ends above the starting energy level.
  </p>

  <p>
    For example, if the reactants have an energy of 140 kJ and the products have
    an energy of 215 kJ:
  </p>

  <p>Energy change = 215 kJ − 140 kJ = +75 kJ</p>

  <p>
    The reaction absorbs 75 kJ per stated reaction event or amount of reaction
    represented by the data.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Reading the Diagram Correctly</h3>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Reactants:</strong> the starting energy level.</li>
    <li><strong>Products:</strong> the ending energy level.</li>
    <li><strong>Peak:</strong> the highest point on the reaction pathway.</li>
    <li><strong>Activation energy:</strong> the energy difference from the reactants'' level to the peak.</li>
    <li><strong>Overall energy change:</strong> the energy of the products minus the energy of the reactants.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Do not confuse the activation energy with the overall energy change. Activation
    energy measures the energy barrier from the reactants to the peak. The overall
    energy change compares the products directly with the reactants.
  </p>

  <h3>5. Worked Examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: Classifying a diagram from energy levels</strong></p>

  <p>
    An energy profile begins at 260 kJ and ends at 170 kJ. Determine the type of
    reaction and calculate the overall energy change.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the reactants'' energy: 260 kJ.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Identify the products'' energy: 170 kJ.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Calculate the change.</p>

  <p>Energy change = Products − Reactants</p>

  <p>= 170 kJ − 260 kJ = −90 kJ</p>

  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> The products are lower in energy, so the reaction
  is exothermic and the energy change is −90 kJ.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: Interpreting an endothermic diagram</strong></p>

  <p>
    A diagram begins at 125 kJ and ends at 205 kJ. Explain the type of reaction
    and calculate the energy change.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Subtract the reactants'' energy from the products'' energy.</p>

  <p>Energy change = 205 kJ − 125 kJ = +80 kJ</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Interpret the positive sign. The products have more
  energy than the reactants.</p>

  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> The reaction is endothermic and absorbs 80 kJ.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: Finding activation energy from a profile</strong></p>

  <p>
    The reactants have an energy of 90 kJ and the peak of the energy profile is
    245 kJ. Calculate the activation energy for the forward reaction.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the peak energy: 245 kJ.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Identify the reactants'' energy: 90 kJ.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Subtract the reactants'' energy from the peak energy.</p>

  <p>Activation energy = Peak energy − Reactants'' energy</p>

  <p>= 245 kJ − 90 kJ = 155 kJ</p>

  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> The activation energy for the forward reaction is
  155 kJ on the energy scale provided.</p>

  <h3>6. Common Misunderstandings</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>The horizontal axis usually shows reaction progress, not elapsed time.</li>
    <li>Exothermic profiles end below the reactants'' energy level.</li>
    <li>Endothermic profiles end above the reactants'' energy level.</li>
    <li>Activation energy is measured from the reactants'' energy level to the peak.</li>
    <li>The overall energy change is measured between the reactants and products, not between the reactants and the peak.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Energy Profile Diagrams', 15),
  (847, 847, '
  <h2>Activation Energy</h2>

  <h3>1. Meaning of Activation Energy</h3>

  <p>
    <strong>Activation energy</strong> is the minimum energy that reacting particles
    must have for a successful reaction to occur through a particular reaction pathway.
    It represents an energy barrier that must be overcome before the reaction can proceed.
  </p>

  <p>
    Even when a reaction can release energy overall, the reacting particles may still
    need to overcome this energy barrier. This is why some reactions do not begin
    noticeably until an appropriate condition, such as heating or the presence of
    a suitable catalyst, helps the reaction proceed.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Why Is Activation Energy Needed?</h3>

  <p>
    Reacting particles must interact in a way that allows old bonds to break and new
    bonds to form. Their energy and arrangement during a collision affect whether
    that collision can lead to a reaction.
  </p>

  <p>
    A collision is not automatically successful simply because particles meet.
    The particles need sufficient energy to overcome the energy barrier and must
    interact in a suitable way.
  </p>

  <p>
    Activation energy describes the energy requirement associated with overcoming
    that barrier. It does not represent the total energy released or absorbed by the
    reaction.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Activation Energy and Energy Profiles</h3>

  <p>
    On an energy profile diagram, activation energy for the forward reaction is the
    energy difference between the reactants and the peak of the curve.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Activation energy = Energy at the peak − Energy of the reactants</strong></p>

  <p>
    For example, if the reactants are at 75 kJ and the peak is at 210 kJ:
  </p>

  <p>Activation energy = 210 kJ − 75 kJ = 135 kJ</p>

  <p>
    The energy difference is 135 kJ on the scale used in the diagram.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Activation Energy Is Not the Overall Energy Change</h3>

  <p>
    These two quantities answer different questions:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Activation energy:</strong> How much energy separates the reactants from the peak?</li>
    <li><strong>Overall energy change:</strong> How much higher or lower in energy are the products compared with the reactants?</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    An exothermic reaction can have a substantial activation energy. Releasing energy
    overall does not remove the need to overcome the initial energy barrier.
  </p>

  <h3>5. What a Catalyst Does to Activation Energy</h3>

  <p>
    A <strong>catalyst</strong> provides an alternative reaction pathway with a lower
    activation energy. As a result, a greater proportion of particles can have enough
    energy to react successfully under the same conditions.
  </p>

  <p>
    A catalyst is not the energy released by a reaction, and it does not supply a
    permanent store of energy to the products. It changes the pathway available for
    the reaction.
  </p>

  <p>
    A catalyst does not change the energy levels of the reactants and products for
    the same overall reaction. Therefore, it lowers the activation-energy barrier
    without changing the overall energy change.
  </p>

  <h3>6. Worked Examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: Calculating activation energy</strong></p>

  <p>
    The reactants are at 65 kJ on an energy profile. The peak is at 205 kJ.
    Calculate the activation energy for the forward reaction.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Write the formula.</p>

  <p>Activation energy = Peak energy − Reactants'' energy</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Substitute the values.</p>

  <p>Activation energy = 205 kJ − 65 kJ</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Calculate.</p>

  <p>Activation energy = 140 kJ</p>

  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> The activation energy is 140 kJ on the given scale.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: Comparing two reaction pathways</strong></p>

  <p>
    Pathway A has an activation energy of 95 kJ, while pathway B has an activation
    energy of 60 kJ. Which pathway has the lower energy barrier, and by how much?
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Compare the two values: 95 kJ and 60 kJ.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Identify the smaller value. Pathway B requires 60 kJ.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Calculate the difference.</p>

  <p>Difference = 95 kJ − 60 kJ = 35 kJ</p>

  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> Pathway B has the lower activation energy by 35 kJ.
  If the pathways describe the same reaction under comparable conditions, the lower
  barrier can allow the reaction to proceed faster.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: Separating activation energy from overall energy change</strong></p>

  <p>
    The reactants have an energy of 100 kJ, the peak is at 240 kJ, and the products
    have an energy of 70 kJ. Calculate the activation energy and the overall energy
    change, then classify the reaction.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Calculate activation energy.</p>

  <p>Activation energy = 240 kJ − 100 kJ = 140 kJ</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Calculate the overall energy change.</p>

  <p>Energy change = Products − Reactants</p>

  <p>= 70 kJ − 100 kJ = −30 kJ</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Interpret the two results separately.</p>

  <p>
    The activation energy is 140 kJ, which is the barrier from the reactants to the
    peak. The overall energy change is −30 kJ, which means the products have less
    energy than the reactants.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> The reaction is exothermic even though it has an
  activation-energy barrier of 140 kJ.</p>

  <h3>7. Common Misunderstandings</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Activation energy is the energy barrier, not the total energy released by a reaction.</li>
    <li>Particles must have sufficient energy for a successful reaction; not every collision produces a reaction.</li>
    <li>A catalyst lowers the activation energy by providing an alternative pathway.</li>
    <li>A lower activation energy does not mean the products must have less energy than the reactants.</li>
    <li>An exothermic reaction can still require an initial energy input to overcome its activation barrier.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Activation Energy', 15),
  (848, 848, '
  <h2>Meaning of Rate of Reaction</h2>

  <h3>1. Definition of Rate of Reaction</h3>

  <p>
    The <strong>rate of reaction</strong> is the change in the amount of a reactant
    used up or product formed per unit time during a chemical reaction.
  </p>

  <p>
    A reaction with a high rate produces the observable change quickly. A reaction
    with a low rate produces the change more slowly. Rate describes how quickly a
    reaction proceeds, not how much product can eventually be formed.
  </p>

  <p>
    For example, magnesium reacting with dilute hydrochloric acid produces hydrogen
    gas. If the gas is produced rapidly, the reaction has a high rate. If the same
    amount of gas takes much longer to form, the reaction has a lower average rate.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Measuring the Rate of Reaction</h3>

  <p>
    The rate can be investigated by measuring a quantity that changes as the reaction
    proceeds. The measurement chosen depends on the reaction.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Volume of gas:</strong> measure the volume of gas produced over time.</li>
    <li><strong>Mass:</strong> measure the decrease in mass when a gas escapes from the reaction vessel.</li>
    <li><strong>Concentration:</strong> measure how the concentration of a reactant or product changes with time.</li>
    <li><strong>Time taken:</strong> measure how long a reaction takes to reach a clearly defined endpoint.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    For a reaction producing gas, a gas syringe can measure the volume collected.
    For a reaction that releases a gas from an open vessel, a balance can measure
    the decrease in mass.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Calculating Average Rate</h3>

  <p>
    If a measurable quantity increases as a product forms, the average rate can be
    calculated using:
  </p>

  <p><strong>Average rate = Change in quantity ÷ Time taken</strong></p>

  <p>
    For gas production:
  </p>

  <p><strong>Average rate of gas production = Volume of gas produced ÷ Time</strong></p>

  <p>
    If 60 cm³ of gas is produced in 20 seconds:
  </p>

  <p>Average rate = 60 cm³ ÷ 20 s = 3 cm³/s</p>

  <p>
    The unit depends on the quantity measured. Gas volume divided by time may be
    expressed in cm³/s. Mass divided by time may be expressed in g/s.
    Concentration divided by time may be expressed in mol dm⁻³ s⁻¹.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Average Rate and Instantaneous Rate</h3>

  <p>
    <strong>Average rate</strong> describes the change over a stated time interval.
    It is calculated by dividing the total change in the measured quantity by the
    time interval.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Instantaneous rate</strong> describes the rate at a particular moment.
    On a graph of quantity against time, it is represented by the gradient of the
    tangent to the curve at that moment.
  </p>

  <p>
    The rate often decreases as a reaction proceeds because reactants are used up.
    In many reactions, there are fewer effective collisions per second later in the
    reaction. Eventually, the measured quantity may stop changing because the reaction
    has finished or because the limiting reactant has been consumed.
  </p>

  <h3>5. Worked Examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: Calculating average gas-production rate</strong></p>

  <p>
    A reaction produces 90 cm³ of hydrogen gas in 30 seconds. Calculate its average
    rate of gas production.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Write the formula.</p>

  <p>Average rate = Volume of gas produced ÷ Time</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Substitute the values.</p>

  <p>Average rate = 90 cm³ ÷ 30 s</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Calculate.</p>

  <p>Average rate = 3 cm³/s</p>

  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> The average rate of gas production is 3 cm³/s.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: Comparing average rates</strong></p>

  <p>
    Reaction A produces 40 cm³ of gas in 10 seconds. Reaction B produces 72 cm³
    in 24 seconds. Calculate both average rates and identify which reaction has
    the higher average rate.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Calculate the rate for reaction A.</p>

  <p>Rate A = 40 cm³ ÷ 10 s = 4 cm³/s</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Calculate the rate for reaction B.</p>

  <p>Rate B = 72 cm³ ÷ 24 s = 3 cm³/s</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Compare the results.</p>

  <p>
    Reaction A produces an average of 4 cm³ each second, while reaction B produces
    an average of 3 cm³ each second.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> Reaction A has the higher average rate by 1 cm³/s.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: Calculating a rate from mass loss</strong></p>

  <p>
    A reaction vessel has a mass of 150.0 g before a reaction begins and 147.6 g
    after 40 seconds. A gas escapes during the reaction. Calculate the average
    rate of mass loss.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Calculate the decrease in mass.</p>

  <p>Mass lost = Initial mass − Final mass</p>

  <p>= 150.0 g − 147.6 g = 2.4 g</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Divide the mass lost by the time.</p>

  <p>Average rate of mass loss = 2.4 g ÷ 40 s</p>

  <p>= 0.06 g/s</p>

  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> The average rate of mass loss is 0.06 g/s.</p>

  <h3>6. Common Misunderstandings</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>A faster reaction does not necessarily produce a greater final amount of product.</li>
    <li>Always state the quantity measured and the time unit when reporting a rate.</li>
    <li>For average rate, divide the change in the measured quantity by the elapsed time.</li>
    <li>Mass loss can indicate that a gas has escaped, but it is not a direct measurement of gas volume.</li>
    <li>A rate calculated over an entire interval is an average rate, not necessarily the rate at every moment.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Meaning of Rate of Reaction', 15),
  (849, 849, '
  <h2>Effect of Temperature on Rate</h2>

  <h3>1. How Temperature Affects Reaction Rate</h3>

  <p>
    Increasing the temperature usually increases the rate of a chemical reaction.
    Decreasing the temperature usually reduces the rate, provided the reaction
    conditions remain comparable.
  </p>

  <p>
    Temperature affects the average kinetic energy of particles. At a higher
    temperature, reacting particles move faster on average and have greater kinetic
    energy. This changes both how frequently particles collide and how many collisions
    have enough energy to lead to a reaction.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Collision Frequency</h3>

  <p>
    When particles move faster, they can collide more frequently under suitable
    conditions. This can increase the number of opportunities for a reaction to occur.
  </p>

  <p>
    However, the increased collision frequency is not the only explanation for the
    effect of temperature. The more important factor is the increase in the proportion
    of collisions with sufficient energy to overcome the activation-energy barrier.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Energy and Successful Collisions</h3>

  <p>
    Not every collision between reacting particles produces a reaction. For a collision
    to be successful, the particles must have sufficient energy to overcome the
    activation energy and must interact in a suitable way.
  </p>

  <p>
    When temperature increases, the distribution of particle energies changes.
    A greater proportion of particles have energy equal to or greater than the
    activation energy. Consequently, more collisions can result in a reaction per
    second, increasing the reaction rate.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Example: Magnesium and Hydrochloric Acid</h3>

  <p>
    Magnesium reacts with dilute hydrochloric acid to produce magnesium chloride
    and hydrogen gas:
  </p>

  <p>Mg(s) + 2HCl(aq) → MgCl₂(aq) + H₂(g)</p>

  <p>
    If the acid is warmer, the reaction will generally produce hydrogen faster.
    A fair comparison requires the same acid concentration and volume, the same
    magnesium mass and exposed surface area, and the same measuring method.
  </p>

  <h3>5. Investigating Temperature Fairly</h3>

  <p>
    To investigate temperature, use the same quantities and concentrations of
    reactants while changing only the starting temperature.
  </p>

  <ol>
    <li>Measure a fixed volume of acid at a known concentration.</li>
    <li>Bring it to the selected starting temperature.</li>
    <li>Add the same mass and form of magnesium.</li>
    <li>Measure the gas volume at regular time intervals.</li>
    <li>Repeat at another temperature while keeping the other conditions the same.</li>
  </ol>

  <p>
    Compare the gas volume produced in the same time interval, or calculate the
    average rate for each experiment. Use suitable eye protection and follow
    laboratory safety procedures when handling acids and flammable hydrogen.
  </p>

  <h3>6. Worked Examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: Explaining the effect of temperature</strong></p>

  <p>
    Explain why magnesium generally reacts faster with dilute hydrochloric acid
    at 40°C than at 20°C.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the particle change. At 40°C, particles have
  greater average kinetic energy than at 20°C.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Consider collisions. The particles move faster and may
  collide more frequently.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Consider activation energy. A greater proportion of
  collisions have enough energy to overcome the activation-energy barrier.</p>

  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> More successful collisions occur per second, so
  the reaction is generally faster at 40°C.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: Comparing measured rates</strong></p>

  <p>
    At 20°C, a reaction produces 24 cm³ of gas in 30 seconds. At 40°C, it produces
    54 cm³ in the same time. Calculate both average rates and compare them.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Calculate the rate at 20°C.</p>

  <p>Rate = 24 cm³ ÷ 30 s = 0.8 cm³/s</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Calculate the rate at 40°C.</p>

  <p>Rate = 54 cm³ ÷ 30 s = 1.8 cm³/s</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Compare the rates.</p>

  <p>Difference = 1.8 − 0.8 = 1.0 cm³/s</p>

  <p>Rate ratio = 1.8 ÷ 0.8 = 2.25</p>

  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> The average rate at 40°C is 2.25 times the
  average rate at 20°C for these measurements.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: Designing a fair test</strong></p>

  <p>
    A learner compares the rate of magnesium reacting with acid at 20°C and 40°C.
    In the second experiment, the learner also uses more concentrated acid and
    smaller magnesium pieces. Explain why the results cannot establish the effect
    of temperature alone.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the intended independent variable: temperature.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Identify the additional changes: acid concentration and
  magnesium surface area.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Explain the problem. Both changes can affect the rate
  independently of temperature.</p>

  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> The test is not fair. Keep acid concentration and
  magnesium surface area constant, changing only the temperature.</p>

  <h3>7. Common Misunderstandings</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Higher temperature generally increases reaction rate; it does not automatically increase the final amount of product if the reactants and limiting reactant remain unchanged.</li>
    <li>The effect is not explained only by more frequent collisions. The greater proportion of particles with sufficient energy is crucial.</li>
    <li>Temperature comparisons require other rate-affecting variables to be controlled.</li>
    <li>A reaction being twice as fast in one experiment does not establish a universal rule that every 20°C increase doubles every reaction rate.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Effect of Temperature on Rate', 15),
  (850, 850, '
  <h2>Effect of Concentration on Rate</h2>

  <h3>1. Meaning of Concentration</h3>

  <p>
    <strong>Concentration</strong> describes how much dissolved substance is present
    in a given volume of solution. A more concentrated solution contains more
    dissolved particles per unit volume than a more dilute solution of the same
    substance.
  </p>

  <p>
    Concentration is commonly expressed in mol dm⁻³. For example, a solution with
    a concentration of 2.0 mol dm⁻³ contains twice as many moles of dissolved
    solute per cubic decimetre as a solution with a concentration of 1.0 mol dm⁻³.
  </p>

  <p>
    Concentration is not the same as total amount. A large volume of dilute solution
    can contain more total solute than a small volume of concentrated solution.
    Concentration compares amount with volume.
  </p>

  <h3>2. How Concentration Affects Reaction Rate</h3>

  <p>
    Increasing the concentration of a reactant in solution usually increases the
    rate of reaction, provided other relevant conditions remain unchanged.
  </p>

  <p>
    A more concentrated solution has more reactant particles in the same volume.
    The particles therefore have more opportunities to collide with other reacting
    particles. This generally increases the number of effective collisions per
    second and raises the reaction rate.
  </p>

  <p>
    Diluting a solution reduces the number of reactant particles per unit volume.
    Collisions between reacting particles become less frequent, so the rate
    generally decreases.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Example: Magnesium and Hydrochloric Acid</h3>

  <p>
    Magnesium reacts with hydrochloric acid to form magnesium chloride and hydrogen:
  </p>

  <p>Mg(s) + 2HCl(aq) → MgCl₂(aq) + H₂(g)</p>

  <p>
    If the concentration of hydrochloric acid increases while the magnesium mass,
    surface area, temperature and acid volume are controlled appropriately, hydrogen
    will generally be produced at a faster rate.
  </p>

  <p>
    A fair comparison should keep the other conditions the same. The acid volume
    and concentration also determine the total amount of acid available, so the
    final quantity of hydrogen may differ if the amount of limiting reactant differs.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Dilution Calculations</h3>

  <p>
    When a solution is diluted by adding water, the amount of dissolved solute
    remains constant, but the solution volume increases. The concentration decreases.
  </p>

  <p>
    For dilution calculations:
  </p>

  <p><strong>C₁V₁ = C₂V₂</strong></p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>C₁:</strong> initial concentration.</li>
    <li><strong>V₁:</strong> initial volume of solution taken.</li>
    <li><strong>C₂:</strong> final concentration after dilution.</li>
    <li><strong>V₂:</strong> final total solution volume.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Use consistent volume units on both sides. This equation calculates concentration
    after dilution; it does not directly calculate the reaction rate.
  </p>

  <h3>5. Worked Examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: Explaining the concentration effect</strong></p>

  <p>
    Explain why magnesium generally reacts faster with 2.0 mol dm⁻³ hydrochloric
    acid than with 0.5 mol dm⁻³ hydrochloric acid at the same temperature.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Compare the concentrations. The 2.0 mol dm⁻³ solution
  contains more acid particles per unit volume.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Consider collisions. More acid particles are available
  to collide with the magnesium surface in a given time.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Connect to rate. More effective collisions occur per
  second, so the reaction is generally faster.</p>

  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> The more concentrated acid generally reacts faster
  when other relevant conditions are controlled.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: Calculating concentration after dilution</strong></p>

  <p>
    A learner takes 25 cm³ of hydrochloric acid at 2.0 mol dm⁻³ and adds water
    until the total volume is 100 cm³. Calculate the final concentration.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Write the dilution formula.</p>

  <p>C₁V₁ = C₂V₂</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Substitute the values.</p>

  <p>2.0 × 25 = C₂ × 100</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Rearrange to find C₂.</p>

  <p>C₂ = (2.0 × 25) ÷ 100</p>

  <p>C₂ = 50 ÷ 100 = 0.50 mol dm⁻³</p>

  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> The final acid concentration is 0.50 mol dm⁻³.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: Comparing measured reaction rates</strong></p>

  <p>
    With 1.0 mol dm⁻³ acid, a reaction produces 30 cm³ of gas in 20 seconds.
    With 2.0 mol dm⁻³ acid, it produces 48 cm³ in the same time. Calculate both
    average rates and compare them.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Calculate the rate at 1.0 mol dm⁻³.</p>

  <p>Rate = 30 cm³ ÷ 20 s = 1.5 cm³/s</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Calculate the rate at 2.0 mol dm⁻³.</p>

  <p>Rate = 48 cm³ ÷ 20 s = 2.4 cm³/s</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Compare the rates.</p>

  <p>Rate difference = 2.4 − 1.5 = 0.9 cm³/s</p>

  <p>Rate ratio = 2.4 ÷ 1.5 = 1.6</p>

  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> The measured average rate at 2.0 mol dm⁻³ is
  1.6 times the rate at 1.0 mol dm⁻³. These results do not establish that rate is
  always directly proportional to concentration.</p>

  <h3>6. Common Misunderstandings</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Concentration is the amount of dissolved substance per unit volume, not the total amount in the container.</li>
    <li>Dilution increases solution volume while the amount of solute remains constant.</li>
    <li>A more concentrated solution generally reacts faster under comparable conditions.</li>
    <li>Doubling concentration does not necessarily double reaction rate; the relationship depends on the reaction.</li>
    <li>To investigate concentration fairly, control temperature, reactant surface area and other relevant variables.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Effect of Concentration on Rate', 15),
  (851, 851, '
  <h2>Effect of Surface Area on Rate</h2>

  <h3>1. Meaning of Surface Area</h3>

  <p>
    <strong>Surface area</strong> is the total area of a substance''s exposed surface.
    In a reaction involving a solid and a liquid or gas, the exposed surface is the
    part of the solid that the other reactant can contact.
  </p>

  <p>
    If the same mass of a solid is broken into smaller pieces, its total exposed
    surface area generally increases. The smaller pieces expose more of the solid
    to the other reactant.
  </p>

  <p>
    Powdering a solid can produce a particularly large total exposed surface area.
    A single large piece and a powder made from the same mass contain the same
    amount of solid, but they expose different amounts of surface to the surroundings.
  </p>

  <h3>2. How Surface Area Affects Reaction Rate</h3>

  <p>
    Increasing the exposed surface area of a solid reactant generally increases the
    rate of a reaction involving that solid, provided other conditions are unchanged.
  </p>

  <p>
    When a solid is broken into smaller pieces, more of its particles are exposed
    at the surface and can come into contact with the other reactant. This allows
    more collisions to occur at the solid''s surface per second.
  </p>

  <p>
    More effective collisions can occur in a given time, so the reaction rate
    generally increases. A larger exposed surface area does not mean the solid has
    more mass; it means more of its surface is available for reaction.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Example: Calcium Carbonate and Hydrochloric Acid</h3>

  <p>
    Calcium carbonate reacts with hydrochloric acid to produce calcium chloride,
    water and carbon dioxide:
  </p>

  <p>CaCO₃(s) + 2HCl(aq) → CaCl₂(aq) + H₂O(l) + CO₂(g)</p>

  <p>
    Large calcium carbonate chips react more slowly than an equal mass of smaller
    chips or powder under otherwise comparable conditions. The powder exposes
    more surface area to the acid.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Conducting a Fair Comparison</h3>

  <p>
    To investigate surface area, compare different particle sizes of the same
    solid while keeping the other important conditions constant.
  </p>

  <ol>
    <li>Measure the same mass of the solid in each experiment.</li>
    <li>Use the same volume and concentration of acid.</li>
    <li>Maintain the same starting temperature.</li>
    <li>Measure the gas volume at regular time intervals.</li>
    <li>Compare the average rates or the time taken to reach the same endpoint.</li>
  </ol>

  <p>
    If powdered calcium carbonate is used, take care to add it safely because fine
    powders can be dispersed into the air. Keep the reaction apparatus suitable
    for gas collection and follow laboratory safety procedures.
  </p>

  <h3>5. Surface Area and Total Product</h3>

  <p>
    If the same mass of the same solid is reacted with the same amount of acid,
    changing particle size usually changes how quickly the reaction proceeds,
    not the theoretical amount of product determined by the limiting reactant.
  </p>

  <p>
    A faster reaction can reach its endpoint sooner without producing a greater
    final amount of product. The final amount can differ if the amounts of reactants
    or the limiting reactant differ.
  </p>

  <h3>6. Worked Examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: Explaining the effect of particle size</strong></p>

  <p>
    Equal masses of calcium carbonate are reacted with equal volumes of hydrochloric
    acid. One sample consists of large chips and the other is powder. Explain why
    the powder generally reacts faster.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Compare the solid forms. The powder consists of many
  smaller particles.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Compare exposed surface area. The smaller particles
  provide a larger total exposed surface area.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Connect surface area to collisions. More acid particles
  can contact the solid surface, increasing the opportunities for effective collisions.</p>

  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> The powdered calcium carbonate generally reacts
  faster because its greater exposed surface area allows more reaction at the surface
  per unit time.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: Comparing measured rates</strong></p>

  <p>
    Calcium carbonate chips produce 24 cm³ of carbon dioxide in 40 seconds. An equal
    mass of powdered calcium carbonate produces 48 cm³ in the same time. Calculate
    both average rates and compare them.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Calculate the rate for the chips.</p>

  <p>Rate = 24 cm³ ÷ 40 s = 0.6 cm³/s</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Calculate the rate for the powder.</p>

  <p>Rate = 48 cm³ ÷ 40 s = 1.2 cm³/s</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Compare the rates.</p>

  <p>Rate ratio = 1.2 ÷ 0.6 = 2</p>

  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> The powder has twice the average gas-production
  rate in these measurements.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: Designing a fair test</strong></p>

  <p>
    A learner reacts 5 g of calcium carbonate powder with concentrated acid and
    compares it with 10 g of large chips reacted with dilute acid. Explain why
    this comparison cannot establish the effect of surface area alone.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the intended variable: surface area.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Identify the other changes: mass and acid concentration.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Explain why these matter. The mass affects the amount
  of solid available, while acid concentration affects collision frequency and rate.</p>

  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> The experiment is not a fair test. Use the same
  mass of solid and the same acid volume, concentration and temperature, changing
  only the particle size.</p>

  <h3>7. Common Misunderstandings</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Breaking a solid into smaller pieces increases its total exposed surface area; it does not create more solid mass.</li>
    <li>A greater exposed surface area generally increases reaction rate for a solid reactant.</li>
    <li>The surface-area effect is explained by greater contact between the solid and the other reactant.</li>
    <li>Faster reaction does not necessarily mean a greater final yield when the amounts of reactants are unchanged.</li>
    <li>A fair test must control mass, acid concentration, acid volume and temperature.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Effect of Surface Area on Rate', 15),
  (852, 852, '
  <h2>Catalysts</h2>

  <h3>1. Meaning of a Catalyst</h3>

  <p>
    A <strong>catalyst</strong> is a substance that increases the rate of a chemical
    reaction without being permanently consumed by the overall reaction.
  </p>

  <p>
    A catalyst may take part in intermediate steps of the reaction, but it is
    regenerated by the end of the catalytic process. It can therefore continue
    helping the reaction proceed, provided it remains active and suitable conditions
    are maintained.
  </p>

  <p>
    A catalyst does not have to be present in the same physical state as every
    reactant. Some catalysts are solids that interact with gaseous or dissolved
    reactants at their surfaces, while others operate in solution.
  </p>

  <h3>2. How a Catalyst Increases Reaction Rate</h3>

  <p>
    A catalyst provides an alternative reaction pathway with a lower activation
    energy than the uncatalysed pathway.
  </p>

  <p>
    At the same temperature, a greater proportion of reacting particles can overcome
    the lower activation-energy barrier. This increases the number of successful
    reactions per second and usually increases the reaction rate.
  </p>

  <p>
    A catalyst does not simply supply energy to every reacting particle. It changes
    the pathway by which the reaction can occur, reducing the energy barrier that
    must be overcome.
  </p>

  <h3>3. What a Catalyst Does Not Change</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>A catalyst does not change the chemical equation for the overall reaction.</li>
    <li>A catalyst does not change the overall energy difference between the reactants and products for the same reaction.</li>
    <li>A catalyst does not change the equilibrium position of a reversible reaction; it speeds up the forward and reverse reactions so equilibrium is reached sooner.</li>
    <li>A catalyst is not normally used up permanently by the overall reaction, although it can become inactive or be lost through side processes.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    For an irreversible reaction under fixed conditions, a catalyst generally helps
    the reaction reach its attainable final amount more quickly. It does not create
    additional reactant or automatically increase the theoretical yield.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Examples of Catalysts</h3>

  <p>
    <strong>Manganese(IV) oxide:</strong> Manganese(IV) oxide, MnO₂, catalyses the
    decomposition of hydrogen peroxide. Hydrogen peroxide decomposes into water
    and oxygen:
  </p>

  <p>2H₂O₂(aq) → 2H₂O(l) + O₂(g)</p>

  <p>
    <strong>Iron:</strong> Finely divided iron is used as a catalyst in the Haber
    process for making ammonia from nitrogen and hydrogen.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Biological enzymes:</strong> Enzymes act as biological catalysts. They
    speed up particular reactions in living organisms. Their activity depends on
    conditions such as temperature and pH.
  </p>

  <h3>5. Comparing Catalysed and Uncatalysed Reactions</h3>

  <p>
    When comparing two pathways for the same reaction, the catalysed pathway has
    a lower activation energy. If temperature and other conditions are comparable,
    the lower barrier allows more particles to react successfully per second.
  </p>

  <p>
    A fair experiment comparing a reaction with and without a catalyst should use
    the same quantities and concentrations of reactants, the same temperature and
    the same measurement method. The presence or absence of the catalyst should
    be the main changed variable.
  </p>

  <h3>6. Worked Examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: Explaining the action of a catalyst</strong></p>

  <p>
    A reaction proceeds faster when a suitable catalyst is added. Explain the
    effect of the catalyst in terms of activation energy.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the change. The catalyst provides an alternative
  reaction pathway.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Compare activation energies. The catalysed pathway has
  a lower activation energy.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Connect to successful collisions. At the same temperature,
  a greater proportion of particles can overcome the lower barrier.</p>

  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> The catalyst increases the reaction rate by providing
  a pathway with lower activation energy.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: Calculating activation-energy reduction</strong></p>

  <p>
    An uncatalysed pathway has an activation energy of 150 kJ, while the catalysed
    pathway has an activation energy of 95 kJ. Calculate the reduction in activation
    energy.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the original activation energy: 150 kJ.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Identify the catalysed activation energy: 95 kJ.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Subtract.</p>

  <p>Reduction = 150 kJ − 95 kJ = 55 kJ</p>

  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> The catalyst lowers the activation energy by 55 kJ.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: Understanding what a catalyst changes</strong></p>

  <p>
    A reaction has reactants at 200 kJ and products at 130 kJ. A catalyst lowers
    the activation energy from 110 kJ to 65 kJ. Calculate the overall energy change
    and explain whether the catalyst changes it.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Calculate the overall energy change.</p>

  <p>Energy change = Products − Reactants</p>

  <p>= 130 kJ − 200 kJ = −70 kJ</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Interpret the sign. The reaction is exothermic.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Consider the catalyst. It lowers the activation-energy
  barrier but does not change the energy levels of the reactants and products.</p>

  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> The overall energy change remains −70 kJ. The
  catalyst changes the reaction pathway, not the overall energy difference.</p>

  <h3>7. Common Misunderstandings</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>A catalyst increases reaction rate by providing an alternative pathway with lower activation energy.</li>
    <li>A catalyst is not permanently consumed by the overall reaction, although it can become inactive.</li>
    <li>A catalyst does not change the overall energy change between the same reactants and products.</li>
    <li>A catalyst does not change the equilibrium position of a reversible reaction.</li>
    <li>A catalyst usually helps a reaction reach its final state sooner; it does not automatically increase the theoretical amount of product.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Catalysts', 15),
  (853, 853, '
  <h2>Specific Heat Capacity</h2>

  <h3>1. Meaning of Specific Heat Capacity</h3>

  <p>
    <strong>Specific heat capacity</strong> is the amount of thermal energy required
    to raise the temperature of 1 kg of a substance by 1°C or 1 K, without a change
    of state.
  </p>

  <p>
    Different substances require different amounts of energy to produce the same
    temperature increase in equal masses. For example, water has a relatively high
    specific heat capacity, so a considerable amount of energy is required to raise
    the temperature of a given mass of water.
  </p>

  <p>
    Specific heat capacity is represented by the symbol <strong>c</strong>.
    Its SI unit is <strong>J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹</strong>. It can also be expressed as
    J kg⁻¹ °C⁻¹ when calculating temperature changes in degrees Celsius.
  </p>

  <h3>2. The Specific Heat Capacity Formula</h3>

  <p><strong>Q = mcΔT</strong></p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Q</strong> = thermal energy transferred, in joules (J).</li>
    <li><strong>m</strong> = mass of the substance, in kilograms (kg).</li>
    <li><strong>c</strong> = specific heat capacity, in J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹.</li>
    <li><strong>ΔT</strong> = temperature change, in °C or K.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    The temperature change is calculated using:
  </p>

  <p><strong>ΔT = Final temperature − Initial temperature</strong></p>

  <p>
    A temperature difference of 1°C has the same numerical size as a temperature
    difference of 1 K. Therefore, either can be used for ΔT, provided the units
    are handled consistently.
  </p>

  <h3>3. What the Formula Tells Us</h3>

  <p>
    For the same substance and the same temperature change, doubling the mass
    doubles the energy required. For the same mass and temperature change, a
    substance with a higher specific heat capacity requires more energy.
  </p>

  <p>
    If energy is supplied to a substance and no change of state occurs, its
    temperature generally rises. If thermal energy is removed, its temperature
    generally falls.
  </p>

  <p>
    The formula Q = mcΔT calculates the energy associated with the temperature
    change. It does not calculate latent heat during a change of state.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Worked Examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: Calculating thermal energy</strong></p>

  <p>
    Calculate the energy needed to raise the temperature of 2 kg of water by 5°C.
    Use c = 4,200 J kg⁻¹ °C⁻¹.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Write the formula.</p>
  <p>Q = mcΔT</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Identify the values.</p>
  <p>m = 2 kg, c = 4,200 J kg⁻¹ °C⁻¹, ΔT = 5°C.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Substitute.</p>
  <p>Q = 2 × 4,200 × 5</p>

  <p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Calculate.</p>
  <p>Q = 42,000 J = 42 kJ</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 42,000 J of thermal energy is required.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: Calculating specific heat capacity</strong></p>

  <p>
    A 0.5 kg metal block absorbs 3,000 J of energy and its temperature rises by
    20°C. Calculate its specific heat capacity.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Start with Q = mcΔT.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Rearrange to make c the subject.</p>
  <p>c = Q ÷ (mΔT)</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Substitute.</p>
  <p>c = 3,000 ÷ (0.5 × 20)</p>

  <p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Calculate.</p>
  <p>c = 3,000 ÷ 10 = 300 J kg⁻¹ °C⁻¹</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The metal''s specific heat capacity is
  300 J kg⁻¹ °C⁻¹.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: Finding the final temperature</strong></p>

  <p>
    A 2 kg substance with a specific heat capacity of 500 J kg⁻¹ °C⁻¹ absorbs
    10,000 J. Its initial temperature is 18°C. Calculate its final temperature,
    assuming no change of state or heat loss.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Rearrange Q = mcΔT.</p>
  <p>ΔT = Q ÷ (mc)</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Substitute.</p>
  <p>ΔT = 10,000 ÷ (2 × 500)</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Calculate the temperature increase.</p>
  <p>ΔT = 10,000 ÷ 1,000 = 10°C</p>

  <p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Add the temperature increase to the initial temperature.</p>
  <p>Final temperature = 18°C + 10°C = 28°C</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The final temperature is 28°C.</p>

  <h3>5. Common Errors</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Convert mass from grams to kilograms before using a specific heat capacity expressed per kilogram.</li>
    <li>Use the temperature difference, not just the final temperature.</li>
    <li>Rearrange the formula carefully when finding c, m or ΔT.</li>
    <li>Use Q = mcΔT for temperature changes without a change of state.</li>
    <li>In real experiments, some supplied energy may be lost to the surroundings or absorbed by the container.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Specific Heat Capacity', 15),
  (854, 854, '
  <h2>Heat Energy Calculations</h2>

  <h3>1. What Is Heat Energy?</h3>

  <p>
    In these calculations, <strong>heat</strong> refers to thermal energy transferred
    because of a temperature difference. Energy moves from a hotter object or region
    to a colder one unless other processes supply or remove energy.
  </p>

  <p>
    The amount of energy transferred can depend on the mass being heated, the
    substance''s specific heat capacity and the temperature change.
  </p>

  <h3>2. The Heat Energy Formula</h3>

  <p><strong>Q = mcΔT</strong></p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Q:</strong> thermal energy transferred, in joules (J).</li>
    <li><strong>m:</strong> mass, in kilograms (kg).</li>
    <li><strong>c:</strong> specific heat capacity, in J kg⁻¹ °C⁻¹ or J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹.</li>
    <li><strong>ΔT:</strong> final temperature minus initial temperature.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    This formula applies when the substance changes temperature without changing
    state, and when the specific heat capacity can be treated as constant over
    the temperature interval.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Units and Conversions</h3>

  <p>
    If the specific heat capacity is given per kilogram, mass must be in kilograms.
    Convert grams to kilograms by dividing by 1,000.
  </p>

  <p>For example: 250 g = 250 ÷ 1,000 = 0.250 kg.</p>

  <p>
    Convert joules to kilojoules by dividing by 1,000. Convert kilojoules to joules
    by multiplying by 1,000.
  </p>

  <p>
    The temperature change is the difference between final and initial temperatures.
    A rise from 20°C to 70°C is a change of 50°C, not 70°C.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Heating and Cooling</h3>

  <p>
    When energy is absorbed and no state change occurs, the temperature usually rises.
    When energy is removed, the temperature usually falls.
  </p>

  <p>
    The magnitude of the energy transferred can be calculated using the magnitude
    of the temperature change. If the direction of transfer is required, state
    whether energy was absorbed or released by the object.
  </p>

  <p>
    In practical experiments, not all energy supplied by a heater necessarily enters
    the substance. Some may heat the container or escape to the surroundings.
    A calculation using Q = mcΔT for the substance assumes the stated energy transfer
    is the energy absorbed by that substance.
  </p>

  <h3>5. Worked Examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: Converting mass before calculating heat energy</strong></p>

  <p>
    Calculate the energy required to heat 250 g of water from 20°C to 60°C.
    Use c = 4,200 J kg⁻¹ °C⁻¹.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Convert mass to kilograms.</p>
  <p>m = 250 ÷ 1,000 = 0.250 kg</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Calculate the temperature change.</p>
  <p>ΔT = 60°C − 20°C = 40°C</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Substitute into Q = mcΔT.</p>
  <p>Q = 0.250 × 4,200 × 40</p>

  <p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Calculate.</p>
  <p>Q = 42,000 J = 42 kJ</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The water absorbs 42,000 J of energy.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: Finding mass from heat energy</strong></p>

  <p>
    A substance with c = 500 J kg⁻¹ °C⁻¹ absorbs 15,000 J as its temperature
    rises by 30°C. Calculate its mass.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Write Q = mcΔT.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Rearrange to make m the subject.</p>
  <p>m = Q ÷ (cΔT)</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Substitute.</p>
  <p>m = 15,000 ÷ (500 × 30)</p>

  <p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Calculate.</p>
  <p>m = 15,000 ÷ 15,000 = 1 kg</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The mass is 1 kg.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: Calculating energy removed during cooling</strong></p>

  <p>
    A 2 kg metal block with c = 450 J kg⁻¹ °C⁻¹ cools from 100°C to 40°C.
    Calculate the magnitude of the thermal energy released.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Calculate the temperature decrease.</p>
  <p>Temperature decrease = 100°C − 40°C = 60°C</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Use the magnitude of the temperature change.</p>
  <p>Q = mcΔT = 2 × 450 × 60</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Calculate.</p>
  <p>Q = 54,000 J = 54 kJ</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The block releases 54,000 J of thermal energy,
  assuming the calculated energy loss is attributed to the block''s cooling.</p>

  <h3>6. Common Errors</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Convert grams to kilograms when using a specific heat capacity per kilogram.</li>
    <li>Use the difference between final and initial temperatures.</li>
    <li>Do not use Q = mcΔT for the energy absorbed during a change of state at constant temperature.</li>
    <li>Distinguish energy absorbed by an object from energy supplied by a heater when heat losses are present.</li>
    <li>Include the correct unit in every final answer.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Heat Energy Calculations', 15),
  (855, 855, '
  <h2>Latent Heat of Fusion</h2>

  <h3>1. Meaning of Fusion</h3>

  <p>
    <strong>Fusion</strong> is the change of state from solid to liquid. In everyday
    language, it is called melting. For example, ice changes into liquid water
    when it melts.
  </p>

  <p>
    During melting at the melting point, a pure substance can absorb thermal energy
    without its temperature increasing. The energy is used for the change of state
    rather than producing a temperature rise.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Meaning of Specific Latent Heat of Fusion</h3>

  <p>
    The <strong>specific latent heat of fusion</strong> is the energy required to
    change 1 kg of a substance from solid to liquid at its melting point without
    changing its temperature.
  </p>

  <p>
    The symbol for specific latent heat is <strong>L</strong>, and for fusion it
    is commonly written as Lf. Its SI unit is <strong>J kg⁻¹</strong>.
  </p>

  <h3>3. The Latent Heat Formula</h3>

  <p><strong>Q = mLf</strong></p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Q:</strong> thermal energy transferred, in joules (J).</li>
    <li><strong>m:</strong> mass changing state, in kilograms (kg).</li>
    <li><strong>Lf:</strong> specific latent heat of fusion, in J kg⁻¹.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    To find the energy required to melt a mass, multiply the mass by the specific
    latent heat of fusion. To find the mass, divide the energy by the specific
    latent heat. To find the specific latent heat, divide the energy by the mass.
  </p>

  <h3>4. What Happens During Melting?</h3>

  <p>
    As a solid absorbs energy and reaches its melting point, the energy supplied
    during the change of state is used to overcome interactions holding its
    particles in their solid arrangement. The particles become able to move past
    one another as a liquid.
  </p>

  <p>
    For a pure substance melting at a constant pressure, the temperature remains
    constant while the solid and liquid coexist during the phase change. After all
    the solid has melted, further heating can raise the temperature of the liquid.
  </p>

  <p>
    During freezing, the reverse process occurs: a liquid becomes a solid and
    releases energy to its surroundings.
  </p>

  <h3>5. Worked Examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: Calculating energy to melt ice</strong></p>

  <p>
    Calculate the energy required to melt 2 kg of ice at its melting point.
    Use the specific latent heat of fusion of ice as 334,000 J kg⁻¹.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Write the formula.</p>
  <p>Q = mLf</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Substitute.</p>
  <p>Q = 2 × 334,000</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Calculate.</p>
  <p>Q = 668,000 J = 668 kJ</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 668,000 J of energy is required to melt the ice.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: Calculating the mass melted</strong></p>

  <p>
    A sample of ice at its melting point absorbs 167,000 J and melts completely.
    Calculate the mass of ice melted. Use Lf = 334,000 J kg⁻¹.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Rearrange Q = mLf.</p>
  <p>m = Q ÷ Lf</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Substitute.</p>
  <p>m = 167,000 ÷ 334,000</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Calculate.</p>
  <p>m = 0.5 kg</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The mass of ice melted is 0.5 kg.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: Finding specific latent heat of fusion</strong></p>

  <p>
    A 0.25 kg solid melts at its melting point after absorbing 80,000 J.
    Calculate its specific latent heat of fusion.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Rearrange Q = mLf.</p>
  <p>Lf = Q ÷ m</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Substitute.</p>
  <p>Lf = 80,000 ÷ 0.25</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Calculate.</p>
  <p>Lf = 320,000 J kg⁻¹</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The specific latent heat of fusion is 320,000 J kg⁻¹.</p>

  <h3>6. Common Misunderstandings</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Fusion means melting: solid to liquid.</li>
    <li>During melting at the melting point, energy is absorbed without a temperature increase.</li>
    <li>Use Q = mLf for the phase change, not Q = mcΔT.</li>
    <li>Specific latent heat is measured in J kg⁻¹, not J kg⁻¹ °C⁻¹.</li>
    <li>Once melting is complete, further heating can increase the liquid''s temperature.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Latent Heat of Fusion', 15),
  (856, 856, '
  <h2>Latent Heat of Vaporisation</h2>

  <h3>1. Meaning of Vaporisation</h3>

  <p>
    <strong>Vaporisation</strong> is the change of state from liquid to gas.
    It includes boiling, which occurs throughout a liquid at its boiling point,
    and evaporation, which can occur at the surface of a liquid below its boiling point.
  </p>

  <p>
    During boiling at a constant pressure, a pure liquid can absorb energy without
    its temperature increasing while it changes into gas. The supplied energy is
    used for the phase change.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Meaning of Specific Latent Heat of Vaporisation</h3>

  <p>
    The <strong>specific latent heat of vaporisation</strong> is the energy required
    to change 1 kg of a liquid into gas at its boiling point without changing its
    temperature.
  </p>

  <p>
    It is represented by Lv and measured in <strong>J kg⁻¹</strong>.
    Different substances have different specific latent heats of vaporisation.
  </p>

  <h3>3. The Latent Heat Formula</h3>

  <p><strong>Q = mLv</strong></p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Q:</strong> energy transferred, in joules (J).</li>
    <li><strong>m:</strong> mass vaporised, in kilograms (kg).</li>
    <li><strong>Lv:</strong> specific latent heat of vaporisation, in J kg⁻¹.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    To calculate the energy required to vaporise a mass, multiply the mass by
    the specific latent heat of vaporisation. To calculate the mass vaporised,
    divide the energy by Lv.
  </p>

  <h3>4. What Happens During Boiling?</h3>

  <p>
    As a liquid absorbs energy and reaches its boiling point, the energy supplied
    during boiling is used to separate particles sufficiently to form a gas.
    The temperature remains constant while the liquid and gas coexist during
    the phase change of a pure substance at constant pressure.
  </p>

  <p>
    Once all the liquid has vaporised, further heating can increase the temperature
    of the gas. If a gas condenses back into a liquid, energy is released to the
    surroundings.
  </p>

  <h3>5. Vaporisation Compared with Fusion</h3>

  <p>
    Fusion is the change from solid to liquid and uses Q = mLf. Vaporisation is
    the change from liquid to gas and uses Q = mLv.
  </p>

  <p>
    Both are phase changes. During each change, the temperature of a pure substance
    remains constant under the stated transition conditions, while energy is
    transferred to change the state.
  </p>

  <h3>6. Worked Examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: Calculating energy to vaporise water</strong></p>

  <p>
    Calculate the energy required to vaporise 0.5 kg of water at its boiling point.
    Use Lv = 2,260,000 J kg⁻¹.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Write the formula.</p>
  <p>Q = mLv</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Substitute.</p>
  <p>Q = 0.5 × 2,260,000</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Calculate.</p>
  <p>Q = 1,130,000 J = 1,130 kJ</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 1,130,000 J is required to vaporise the water.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: Calculating the mass vaporised</strong></p>

  <p>
    A quantity of water at its boiling point absorbs 678,000 J during vaporisation.
    Calculate the mass vaporised. Use Lv = 2,260,000 J kg⁻¹.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Rearrange Q = mLv.</p>
  <p>m = Q ÷ Lv</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Substitute.</p>
  <p>m = 678,000 ÷ 2,260,000</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Calculate.</p>
  <p>m = 0.30 kg</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The mass vaporised is 0.30 kg.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: Calculating specific latent heat of vaporisation</strong></p>

  <p>
    A 0.2 kg liquid absorbs 460,000 J to change completely into gas at its boiling
    point. Calculate its specific latent heat of vaporisation.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Start with Q = mLv.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Rearrange.</p>
  <p>Lv = Q ÷ m</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Substitute and calculate.</p>
  <p>Lv = 460,000 ÷ 0.2 = 2,300,000 J kg⁻¹</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The specific latent heat of vaporisation is
  2,300,000 J kg⁻¹.</p>

  <h3>7. Common Misunderstandings</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Vaporisation means liquid to gas; boiling is one form of vaporisation.</li>
    <li>Use Q = mLv for energy transferred during vaporisation at the boiling point.</li>
    <li>Specific latent heat is measured in J kg⁻¹, not J kg⁻¹ °C⁻¹.</li>
    <li>During boiling, energy is absorbed while the temperature of a pure substance remains constant under constant-pressure conditions.</li>
    <li>Evaporation can occur below the boiling point and does not require the entire liquid to reach its boiling point.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Latent Heat of Vaporisation', 15),
  (857, 857, '
  <h2>Electrolytes</h2>

  <h3>Meaning of an Electrolyte</h3>

  <p>An <strong>electrolyte</strong> is a substance that conducts electricity when molten or dissolved in water because it contains mobile ions.</p>

  <p>Ions are charged particles. Positive ions carry positive charge, while negative ions carry negative charge. When these ions move, they transport electrical charge through the electrolyte.</p>

  <p>Examples of electrolytes include molten sodium chloride, aqueous sodium chloride, aqueous copper(II) sulphate and dilute hydrochloric acid.</p>

  <h3>Why the Physical State Matters</h3>

  <p>In a solid ionic compound, positive and negative ions occupy fixed positions in a crystal lattice. The ions cannot move freely through the solid, so the solid does not conduct electricity effectively.</p>

  <p>When the ionic compound melts, the lattice breaks down and its ions become mobile. When an ionic compound dissolves in water, its ions separate and can move through the solution.</p>

  <p>Therefore, the important distinction is not simply whether ions are present. The ions must be free to move.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Solid Sodium Chloride</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Explain why solid sodium chloride does not conduct electricity effectively.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Sodium chloride contains Na⁺ and Cl⁻ ions.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> In the solid, these ions are held in fixed positions in a crystal lattice.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> The ions cannot move freely through the solid.</p>

  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> Solid sodium chloride does not conduct electricity effectively because its ions are not mobile.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Molten Sodium Chloride</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Explain why molten sodium chloride conducts electricity.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Sodium chloride contains Na⁺ and Cl⁻ ions.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Melting removes the fixed arrangement of the solid lattice.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> The Na⁺ and Cl⁻ ions can now move through the molten substance.</p>

  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> Molten sodium chloride conducts electricity because its mobile ions carry electrical charge.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Comparing Two Samples</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Sample A is solid NaCl and Sample B is molten NaCl. Which sample conducts electricity, and why?</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Both samples contain Na⁺ and Cl⁻ ions.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> In Sample A, the ions are fixed in the lattice.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> In Sample B, the ions are free to move.</p>

  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> Sample B conducts electricity effectively because its ions are mobile. Sample A does not conduct effectively because its ions cannot move freely.</p>

  <h3>Key Principle</h3>

  <p><strong>Electrical conduction through an electrolyte depends on the movement of ions, not merely on the presence of ions.</strong></p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Electrolytes', 15),
  (858, 858, '
  <h2>Meaning of Electrolysis</h2>

  <h3>Definition</h3>

  <p><strong>Electrolysis</strong> is the chemical decomposition of an ionic substance in the molten state or in aqueous solution by passing an electric current through it.</p>

  <p>The ionic substance must contain mobile ions so that current can pass through the electrolyte. An external power supply provides the electrical energy required to drive the chemical change.</p>

  <h3>What Happens During Electrolysis?</h3>

  <p>When an electric current passes through an electrolyte:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>Positive ions move towards the negative electrode.</li>
    <li>Negative ions move towards the positive electrode.</li>
    <li>At the electrodes, ions may gain or lose electrons and form products.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Electrolysis converts electrical energy into chemical change. It is different from a simple chemical cell, which converts chemical energy into electrical energy.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Identifying Electrolysis</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> An electric current is passed through molten lead(II) bromide, producing lead and bromine. Explain why this process is electrolysis.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Lead(II) bromide is an ionic compound.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> In the molten state, its ions can move.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> An electric current causes the compound to decompose into lead and bromine.</p>

  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> The process is electrolysis because electrical energy causes the chemical decomposition of a molten ionic compound.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Electrolysis of Molten Sodium Chloride</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Write the overall equation for the electrolysis of molten sodium chloride.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Sodium chloride contains Na⁺ and Cl⁻ ions.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Sodium ions gain electrons to form sodium atoms.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Chloride ions lose electrons to form chlorine molecules.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Balance the number of atoms on both sides.</p>

  <p><strong>Equation:</strong></p>

  <p>2NaCl(l) → 2Na(l) + Cl₂(g)</p>

  <p>Two formula units of sodium chloride produce two sodium atoms and one chlorine molecule.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Why the Solid Cannot Be Used in the Same Way</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Explain why molten sodium chloride can undergo electrolysis while solid sodium chloride cannot normally be electrolysed under the same conditions.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Electrolysis requires ions to move through the electrolyte.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> In solid sodium chloride, the ions are fixed in a lattice.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> In molten sodium chloride, the ions are mobile.</p>

  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> Molten sodium chloride can carry current through its mobile ions and undergo electrolysis; the solid cannot do so effectively.</p>

  <h3>Key Principle</h3>

  <p>Electrolysis requires a suitable electrolyte, mobile ions and an external source of electrical energy.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Meaning of Electrolysis', 15),
  (859, 859, '
  <h2>Electrodes</h2>

  <h3>Meaning of an Electrode</h3>

  <p>An <strong>electrode</strong> is an electrical conductor through which current enters or leaves an electrolyte.</p>

  <p>Electrodes connect the external circuit to the electrolyte. They provide surfaces where ions may gain or lose electrons during electrolysis.</p>

  <h3>The Two Electrodes</h3>

  <p><strong>Cathode:</strong> The negative electrode in an electrolytic cell. Positive ions move towards it and may gain electrons.</p>

  <p><strong>Anode:</strong> The positive electrode in an electrolytic cell. Negative ions move towards it and may lose electrons.</p>

  <p>The power supply determines the electrode charges during electrolysis. Do not assume that electrode charges are identical in every kind of electrochemical cell.</p>

  <h3>Movement of Ions</h3>

  <p>Positive ions are called <strong>cations</strong>. They move towards the negative cathode during electrolysis.</p>

  <p>Negative ions are called <strong>anions</strong>. They move towards the positive anode during electrolysis.</p>

  <p>Ions move through the electrolyte, while electrons move through the external conducting circuit. These are different parts of the electrical pathway.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Identifying an Electrode</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> In an electrolytic cell, which electrode is connected to the negative terminal of the power supply?</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> The power supply establishes the electrode charges.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> The electrode connected to the negative terminal is negative.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> The negative electrode in an electrolytic cell is the cathode.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The cathode.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Predicting Ion Movement</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Where do Mg²⁺ ions move during electrolysis?</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Mg²⁺ carries a positive charge.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Positive ions are attracted towards the negative electrode.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> The negative electrode is the cathode.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Mg²⁺ ions move towards the cathode.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Predicting Anion Movement</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Where do oxide ions, O²⁻, move during electrolysis?</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> O²⁻ carries a negative charge.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Negative ions are attracted towards the positive electrode.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> The positive electrode is the anode in an electrolytic cell.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Oxide ions move towards the anode.</p>

  <h3>Key Principle</h3>

  <p>During electrolysis, cations move towards the cathode and anions move towards the anode.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Electrodes', 15),
  (860, 860, '
  <h2>Anode</h2>

  <h3>Meaning and Charge</h3>

  <p>The <strong>anode</strong> is the electrode at which oxidation occurs.</p>

  <p>In an electrolytic cell, the anode is connected to the positive terminal of the external power supply and is therefore positive.</p>

  <p>Negative ions, called anions, move towards the positive anode because opposite charges attract.</p>

  <h3>Oxidation at the Anode</h3>

  <p>Oxidation is the loss of electrons. At the anode, anions can lose electrons and form neutral atoms or molecules.</p>

  <p>A useful memory aid is <strong>AN OX</strong>: oxidation occurs at the anode.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Chloride Ions</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Write the half-equation for the formation of chlorine from chloride ions at the anode.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Chloride ions have the formula Cl⁻.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Chlorine exists as diatomic molecules, Cl₂, so two chloride ions are needed.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Each chloride ion loses one electron, giving two electrons in total.</p>

  <p><strong>Half-equation:</strong></p>

  <p>2Cl⁻ → Cl₂ + 2e⁻</p>

  <p>The atoms and total charge are balanced: both sides have a total charge of −2.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Bromide Ions</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Write the half-equation for the formation of bromine from bromide ions.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Bromide ions are Br⁻.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Bromine forms diatomic molecules, Br₂.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Two bromide ions lose two electrons.</p>

  <p><strong>Half-equation:</strong></p>

  <p>2Br⁻ → Br₂ + 2e⁻</p>

  <p>The electrons appear on the product side because they are lost during oxidation.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Identifying Oxidation</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> In the equation 2I⁻ → I₂ + 2e⁻, identify the process and the electrode where it occurs during electrolysis.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Electrons appear on the right-hand side.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> The iodide ions lose electrons.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Loss of electrons is oxidation.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Oxidation occurs at the anode.</p>

  <h3>Key Principle</h3>

  <p>During electrolysis, the anode is positive and oxidation occurs there. Anions move towards it and may lose electrons.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Anode', 15),
  (861, 861, '
  <h2>Cathode</h2>

  <h3>Meaning and Charge</h3>

  <p>The <strong>cathode</strong> is the electrode at which reduction occurs.</p>

  <p>In an electrolytic cell, the cathode is connected to the negative terminal of the external power supply and is therefore negative.</p>

  <p>Positive ions, called cations, move towards the cathode because opposite charges attract.</p>

  <h3>Reduction at the Cathode</h3>

  <p>Reduction is the gain of electrons. At the cathode, positive ions can gain electrons and form neutral atoms or other reduced products.</p>

  <p>A useful memory aid is <strong>RED CAT</strong>: reduction occurs at the cathode.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Sodium Ions</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Write the half-equation for the formation of sodium from sodium ions.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> A sodium ion is represented by Na⁺ and has a charge of +1.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> It must gain one electron to become a neutral sodium atom.</p>

  <p><strong>Half-equation:</strong></p>

  <p>Na⁺ + e⁻ → Na</p>

  <p>The total charge on each side is zero after the electron is included.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Copper(II) Ions</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Write the half-equation for the formation of copper from Cu²⁺ ions.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> A copper(II) ion has a charge of +2.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> It needs two electrons to become a neutral copper atom.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Place two electrons on the left-hand side.</p>

  <p><strong>Half-equation:</strong></p>

  <p>Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu</p>

  <p>The charge on the left is +2 − 2 = 0, matching the neutral atom on the right.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Identifying Reduction</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> In the equation Ag⁺ + e⁻ → Ag, identify the process and the electrode where it occurs during electrolysis.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> An electron appears on the left-hand side.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> The silver ion gains an electron.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Gain of electrons is reduction.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Reduction occurs at the cathode.</p>

  <h3>Key Principle</h3>

  <p>During electrolysis, the cathode is negative and reduction occurs there. Cations move towards it and may gain electrons.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Cathode', 15),
  (862, 862, '
  <h2>Electrolysis of Molten Compounds</h2>

  <h3>Why Molten Ionic Compounds Can Be Electrolysed</h3>

  <p>A molten ionic compound contains positive and negative ions that are free to move. When a direct electric current passes through the molten compound, the ions migrate towards oppositely charged electrodes and may gain or lose electrons.</p>

  <p>Positive ions move towards the negative cathode, where reduction occurs. Negative ions move towards the positive anode, where oxidation occurs.</p>

  <p>When a pure molten binary ionic compound is electrolysed using suitable inert electrodes, its positive ion is usually reduced to the corresponding element, while its negative ion is oxidised to the corresponding non-metal element.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Electrolysis of Molten Sodium Chloride</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> State the products and write the electrode half-equations for electrolysis of molten sodium chloride.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1: Identify the ions.</strong></p>

  <p>Molten sodium chloride contains Na⁺ and Cl⁻ ions.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2: Find the cathode product.</strong></p>

  <p>Na⁺ ions gain electrons:</p>

  <p>Na⁺ + e⁻ → Na</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3: Find the anode product.</strong></p>

  <p>Cl⁻ ions lose electrons:</p>

  <p>2Cl⁻ → Cl₂ + 2e⁻</p>

  <p><strong>Step 4: Balance the overall equation.</strong></p>

  <p>2NaCl(l) → 2Na(l) + Cl₂(g)</p>

  <p><strong>Products:</strong> Sodium metal at the cathode and chlorine gas at the anode.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Electrolysis of Molten Lead(II) Bromide</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Predict the products and write the half-equations for molten PbBr₂.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1: Identify the ions.</strong></p>

  <p>PbBr₂ contains Pb²⁺ and Br⁻ ions.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2: Reduction at the cathode.</strong></p>

  <p>Pb²⁺ ions need two electrons to form lead atoms:</p>

  <p>Pb²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Pb</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3: Oxidation at the anode.</strong></p>

  <p>Two bromide ions form one bromine molecule:</p>

  <p>2Br⁻ → Br₂ + 2e⁻</p>

  <p><strong>Step 4: Write the overall equation.</strong></p>

  <p>PbBr₂(l) → Pb(l) + Br₂(g)</p>

  <p><strong>Products:</strong> Lead at the cathode and bromine at the anode.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Electrolysis of Molten Magnesium Chloride</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Write the electrode half-equations and overall equation for molten MgCl₂.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1: Identify the ions.</strong></p>

  <p>MgCl₂ contains Mg²⁺ and Cl⁻ ions.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2: Reduction at the cathode.</strong></p>

  <p>Mg²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Mg</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3: Oxidation at the anode.</strong></p>

  <p>2Cl⁻ → Cl₂ + 2e⁻</p>

  <p><strong>Step 4: Combine the half-equations.</strong></p>

  <p>MgCl₂(l) → Mg(l) + Cl₂(g)</p>

  <p>The two half-equations involve two electrons each, so the electrons cancel when the equations are added.</p>

  <h3>Rules for Solving These Questions</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Identify the positive and negative ions in the molten compound.</li>
    <li>Send positive ions to the cathode and reduce them.</li>
    <li>Send negative ions to the anode and oxidise them.</li>
    <li>Balance both atoms and charge in each half-equation.</li>
    <li>Cancel electrons when combining the half-equations.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Electrolysis of Molten Compounds', 15),
  (863, 863, '
  <h2>Electrolysis of Aqueous Solutions</h2>

  <h3>Why Aqueous Electrolysis Is Different</h3>

  <p>An aqueous solution is a substance dissolved in water. During electrolysis, the solution may contain ions from the dissolved substance as well as ions produced by the ionisation of water.</p>

  <p>For example, aqueous sodium chloride contains Na⁺ and Cl⁻ ions from the salt. Water also participates in the electrode reactions, commonly represented using H⁺ and OH⁻ ions in introductory electrolysis analysis.</p>

  <p>These species can compete for discharge at the electrodes. Therefore, the products of electrolysis of an aqueous solution cannot always be predicted by considering only the ions in the dissolved salt.</p>

  <h3>Factors That Affect the Products</h3>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Position in the reactivity series:</strong> At the cathode, a less reactive metal ion may be discharged instead of hydrogen from water, depending on the conditions.</li>
    <li><strong>Concentration:</strong> Concentrated halide solutions may produce the corresponding halogen at the anode more readily than dilute solutions.</li>
    <li><strong>Nature of the electrodes:</strong> Inert electrodes generally do not take part in the reaction, whereas active electrodes may dissolve or react.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>The exact products depend on the electrolyte, its concentration, the electrode material and the operating conditions.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Identifying the Ions in Aqueous Sodium Chloride</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> List the main ions considered when analysing the electrolysis of aqueous sodium chloride.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Sodium chloride separates into Na⁺ and Cl⁻ ions in water.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Water contributes hydrogen-containing and hydroxide species.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> In the usual introductory analysis, these are represented as H⁺ and OH⁻.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Na⁺, Cl⁻, H⁺ and OH⁻.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Predicting Cathode Products in Aqueous Solutions</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Explain why hydrogen may form at the cathode during electrolysis of an aqueous solution containing a very reactive metal ion.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Positive ions move towards the negative cathode.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> The metal ion and hydrogen-containing species may compete for reduction.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> For ions of very reactive metals, such as Na⁺, reduction of water is favoured under ordinary aqueous electrolysis conditions.</p>

  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> Hydrogen gas is produced instead of sodium metal when aqueous sodium chloride is electrolysed under ordinary conditions.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Concentration and Anode Products</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Explain why the concentration of a chloride solution can affect whether chlorine or oxygen is produced at the anode.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Chloride ions and species from water may compete for oxidation.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> In sufficiently concentrated chloride solutions, chloride ions are more likely to be discharged at the anode.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> In sufficiently dilute solutions, oxygen formation from water may be favoured.</p>

  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> The concentration of the electrolyte can influence the anode product.</p>

  <h3>Important Exam Rule</h3>

  <p>Before predicting products, identify the electrolyte, determine whether it is molten or aqueous, note the electrode materials and consider the relevant discharge conditions.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Electrolysis of Aqueous Solutions', 15),
  (864, 864, '
  <h2>Electrolysis of Copper(II) Sulphate</h2>

  <h3>Aqueous Copper(II) Sulphate with Inert Electrodes</h3>

  <p>Aqueous copper(II) sulphate contains Cu²⁺ and SO₄²⁻ ions, as well as species from water. When inert electrodes, such as suitable graphite electrodes, are used, the electrodes are not intended to take part in the chemical reaction.</p>

  <p>Under the usual school-laboratory conditions, copper is deposited at the cathode and oxygen is produced at the anode.</p>

  <h3>Reaction at the Cathode</h3>

  <p>Copper(II) ions move towards the negative cathode and gain two electrons:</p>

  <p><strong>Cu²⁺(aq) + 2e⁻ → Cu(s)</strong></p>

  <p>A reddish-brown deposit of copper forms on the cathode.</p>

  <h3>Reaction at the Anode</h3>

  <p>Hydroxide ions from the aqueous system can be oxidised to produce oxygen:</p>

  <p><strong>4OH⁻(aq) → O₂(g) + 2H₂O(l) + 4e⁻</strong></p>

  <p>Oxygen gas is released at the anode under these conditions. Sulphate ions generally remain in solution rather than being discharged.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Identifying the Cathode Product</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> What forms at the cathode when aqueous copper(II) sulphate is electrolysed using inert electrodes?</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Copper(II) sulphate provides Cu²⁺ ions.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Cu²⁺ ions move towards the negative cathode.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Each Cu²⁺ ion gains two electrons.</p>

  <p><strong>Half-equation:</strong></p>

  <p>Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Copper metal is deposited at the cathode.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Identifying the Anode Product</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> What gas is produced at the anode when aqueous copper(II) sulphate is electrolysed using inert electrodes?</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> The aqueous solution contains species from water.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Under the usual school-laboratory conditions, water-derived hydroxide ions are oxidised.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> The oxidation produces oxygen gas and electrons.</p>

  <p><strong>Half-equation:</strong></p>

  <p>4OH⁻ → O₂ + 2H₂O + 4e⁻</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Oxygen gas is produced at the anode.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Calculating the Change in Cathode Mass</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> During electrolysis, 0.10 mol of Cu²⁺ ions are completely reduced to copper. Calculate the mass of copper deposited. Use Cu = 63.5 g mol⁻¹.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1: Write the half-equation.</strong></p>

  <p>Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2: Find the mole ratio.</strong></p>

  <p>One mole of Cu²⁺ produces one mole of Cu.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3: Calculate the moles of copper formed.</strong></p>

  <p>Moles of Cu = 0.10 mol</p>

  <p><strong>Step 4: Calculate the mass.</strong></p>

  <p>Mass = moles × molar mass</p>

  <p>Mass = 0.10 × 63.5 = 6.35 g</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 6.35 g of copper is deposited, assuming complete reduction and no loss of the deposit.</p>

  <h3>Important Comparison</h3>

  <p>The products described above apply to aqueous copper(II) sulphate with inert electrodes. If copper electrodes are used instead, the copper anode can dissolve and supply Cu²⁺ ions. The anode reaction is then different.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Electrolysis of Copper(II) Sulphate', 15),
  (865, 865, '
  <h2>Simple Cells</h2>

  <h3>1. Meaning of a Simple Cell</h3>

  <p>
    A <strong>simple cell</strong> is a device that converts chemical energy
    into electrical energy through chemical reactions.
  </p>

  <p>
    A basic cell consists of two different electrodes placed in an electrolyte.
    The electrodes are connected externally by a conducting wire. When the
    circuit is complete, chemical reactions can cause electrons to flow
    through the external wire.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Main Components</h3>

  <p><strong>Electrodes:</strong> Conducting materials through which electrons
  enter or leave the cell. Different metals, such as zinc and copper, may be
  used.</p>

  <p><strong>Electrolyte:</strong> A liquid or solution containing mobile ions
  that carry charge inside the cell.</p>

  <p><strong>External circuit:</strong> The wire and any connected device
  through which electrons flow outside the cell.</p>

  <h3>3. How a Simple Cell Works</h3>

  <p>
    The two electrodes have different tendencies to undergo chemical
    reactions. When suitable electrodes and an electrolyte are connected
    in a complete circuit, one electrode may release electrons while a
    reaction at the other electrode accepts electrons.
  </p>

  <p>
    For a typical zinc-copper cell, zinc is oxidised and releases electrons:
  </p>

  <p><strong>Zn(s) → Zn²⁺(aq) + 2e⁻</strong></p>

  <p>
    The electrons travel through the external wire from the zinc electrode
    towards the copper electrode. At the copper electrode, copper ions may
    accept electrons:
  </p>

  <p><strong>Cu²⁺(aq) + 2e⁻ → Cu(s)</strong></p>

  <p>
    These half-equations illustrate a zinc-copper cell containing suitable
    zinc and copper ion solutions. The actual reactions depend on the
    materials and electrolyte used.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Electron Flow and Conventional Current</h3>

  <p>
    In the external circuit of the zinc-copper cell described above,
    electrons flow from zinc to copper. Conventional current is defined
    in the opposite direction, from the positive terminal towards the
    negative terminal through the external circuit.
  </p>

  <p>
    Do not confuse electron flow with conventional current: they point in
    opposite directions in a metallic wire.
  </p>

  <h3>5. Worked Examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: Identifying the components</strong></p>

  <p>
    A cell contains zinc and copper electrodes immersed in suitable
    electrolyte solutions. Identify three essential components.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the conducting materials.</p>
  <p>The zinc and copper strips are the two electrodes.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Identify the ion-containing medium.</p>
  <p>The electrolyte solutions provide mobile ions.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Identify the external path.</p>
  <p>A conducting wire connects the electrodes and allows electrons to flow.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Two electrodes, an electrolyte, and an external
  conducting circuit.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: Writing the oxidation equation</strong></p>

  <p>
    Zinc atoms form zinc ions by losing two electrons. Write the half-equation.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Write the change in the chemical species.</p>
  <p>Zn → Zn²⁺</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Balance the electrical charge.</p>
  <p>The zinc ion has a charge of +2, so two electrons must appear on the
  product side.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Zn(s) → Zn²⁺(aq) + 2e⁻</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: Determining electron direction</strong></p>

  <p>
    In the zinc-copper cell described in these notes, which electrode supplies
    electrons to the external wire?
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the oxidation reaction.</p>
  <p>Zn → Zn²⁺ + 2e⁻</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Identify where electrons are released.</p>
  <p>Zinc releases the electrons.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Follow their path.</p>
  <p>The electrons travel through the external wire towards the copper
  electrode.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The zinc electrode supplies electrons to the
  external circuit.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Simple Cells', 15),
  (866, 866, '
  <h2>Chemical Cell Voltage</h2>

  <h3>1. Meaning of Cell Voltage</h3>

  <p>
    <strong>Cell voltage</strong> is the electrical potential difference
    between the terminals of a cell. It describes the energy transferred
    per unit charge between two points.
  </p>

  <p>
    Voltage is measured in volts (V). A volt is equivalent to one joule
    of energy transferred per coulomb of charge.
  </p>

  <p><strong>1 V = 1 J/C</strong></p>

  <h3>2. Formula Linking Voltage, Energy and Charge</h3>

  <p>
    When a quantity of charge transfers an amount of energy, the average
    energy transferred per unit charge is:
  </p>

  <p><strong>V = E / Q</strong></p>

  <p>Where:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>V = potential difference in volts (V)</li>
    <li>E = energy transferred in joules (J)</li>
    <li>Q = charge transferred in coulombs (C)</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Rearranging the formula gives:
  </p>

  <p><strong>E = V × Q</strong></p>
  <p><strong>Q = E / V</strong></p>

  <h3>3. Worked Example: Calculating Voltage</h3>

  <p>
    A cell transfers 12 J of energy when 3 C of charge passes through it.
    Calculate its voltage.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Write the formula.</p>
  <p>V = E / Q</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Substitute the values.</p>
  <p>V = 12 / 3</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Calculate.</p>
  <p>V = 4 V</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 4 V</p>

  <h3>4. Worked Example: Calculating Energy</h3>

  <p>
    A 2.0 V cell transfers 5 C of charge. Calculate the energy transferred.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Use E = V × Q.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Substitute the values.</p>
  <p>E = 2.0 × 5</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Calculate.</p>
  <p>E = 10 J</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 10 J</p>

  <h3>5. Worked Example: Calculating Charge</h3>

  <p>
    A cell transfers 18 J of energy at a potential difference of 3 V.
    Calculate the charge transferred.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Rearrange the formula.</p>
  <p>Q = E / V</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Substitute the values.</p>
  <p>Q = 18 / 3</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Calculate.</p>
  <p>Q = 6 C</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 6 C</p>

  <h3>6. Combining Cell Voltages</h3>

  <p>
    For ideal cells connected in series in the same direction, their
    voltages add. If cells are connected in opposition, their voltages
    subtract, assuming the opposing voltages are unequal.
  </p>

  <p>
    For example, two 1.5 V cells connected in series in the same direction
    provide a total voltage of:
  </p>

  <p><strong>V = 1.5 + 1.5 = 3.0 V</strong></p>

  <p>
    Two cells of 1.5 V and 1.0 V connected in opposition provide a net
    voltage of:
  </p>

  <p><strong>V = 1.5 − 1.0 = 0.5 V</strong></p>

  <p>
    These calculations describe ideal voltage combinations. The terminal
    voltage of a real cell under load can differ because of internal
    resistance and other losses.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Chemical Cell Voltage', 15),
  (867, 867, '
  <h2>Batteries</h2>

  <h3>1. Meaning of a Battery</h3>

  <p>
    A <strong>battery</strong> is a source of electrical energy made from
    one or more electrochemical cells. A battery converts stored chemical
    energy into electrical energy while it operates as a source.
  </p>

  <p>
    In everyday language, a single electrochemical cell is often called
    a battery, but technically a battery can contain several cells.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Primary Batteries</h3>

  <p>
    A <strong>primary battery</strong> uses chemical reactions that are
    generally not designed to be reversed by ordinary charging. Once its
    useful reactants are depleted, the battery is replaced.
  </p>

  <p>
    Examples include many disposable alkaline cells used in clocks,
    remote controls and simple portable devices.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Secondary Batteries</h3>

  <p>
    A <strong>secondary battery</strong> is designed to be recharged.
    During discharge, chemical reactions provide electrical energy.
    When a suitable external power source drives the reverse reactions,
    energy is stored again in chemical form.
  </p>

  <p>
    Examples include rechargeable lithium-ion batteries used in phones
    and laptops, and lead-acid batteries used in many vehicles.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Comparing Primary and Secondary Batteries</h3>

  <p>
    The key distinction is whether the cell chemistry is designed to be
    restored by charging. Primary batteries are generally single-use;
    secondary batteries are designed for repeated charge-discharge cycles.
  </p>

  <p>
    Rechargeable does not mean that a battery lasts forever. Repeated
    cycling, heat, age and unsuitable charging can reduce its capacity.
  </p>

  <h3>5. Batteries Connected in Series</h3>

  <p>
    When compatible cells are connected in series in the same direction,
    their voltages add. This arrangement is used when a device requires
    a higher voltage than one cell can provide.
  </p>

  <p>
    For example, three ideal 1.5 V cells connected in series provide:
  </p>

  <p><strong>V = 1.5 + 1.5 + 1.5 = 4.5 V</strong></p>

  <h3>6. Batteries Connected in Parallel</h3>

  <p>
    When compatible cells with the same voltage are connected in parallel
    with matching polarity, the voltage remains approximately the same
    as that of one cell. The arrangement may provide greater capacity
    or longer operating time, depending on the cells and circuit.
  </p>

  <p>
    Cells should not be connected in parallel indiscriminately. Their
    chemistry, voltage, condition and protection requirements must be
    compatible.
  </p>

  <h3>7. Worked Examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: Classifying a battery</strong></p>

  <p>
    A device uses a cell designed to be recharged after its energy has
    been used. Classify the cell.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify whether charging is intended to
  restore its chemical state.</p>
  <p>Charging is designed to reverse suitable reactions.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Match the description to the battery type.</p>
  <p>A battery designed for repeated recharging is secondary.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> A secondary battery.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: Calculating the voltage of cells in series</strong></p>

  <p>
    Three ideal 1.5 V cells are connected in series in the same direction.
    Calculate the total voltage.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Write the series rule.</p>
  <p>Total voltage = sum of the individual voltages.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Substitute the values.</p>
  <p>V = 1.5 + 1.5 + 1.5</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Calculate.</p>
  <p>V = 4.5 V</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 4.5 V.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: Choosing a battery arrangement</strong></p>

  <p>
    A circuit requires approximately 3.0 V. You have two compatible ideal
    1.5 V cells. How should you connect them to obtain the required voltage?
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the voltage required.</p>
  <p>The circuit requires 3.0 V.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Consider a series connection.</p>
  <p>Series voltages add when the cells face the same direction.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Calculate the total.</p>
  <p>V = 1.5 + 1.5 = 3.0 V</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Connect the cells in series in the same
  direction.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Batteries', 15),
  (868, 868, '
  <h2>Strong Electrolytes</h2>

  <h3>1. Meaning of a Strong Electrolyte</h3>

  <p>
    A <strong>strong electrolyte</strong> is a substance that produces
    ions almost completely when it dissolves in water or when it is
    molten, allowing the substance to conduct electricity effectively.
  </p>

  <p>
    Electrical conduction in an electrolyte occurs because charged
    particles called <strong>ions</strong> move through the liquid or
    solution. Electrons do not normally travel through the electrolyte
    in the same way they travel through a metal wire.
  </p>

  <h3>2. How Strong Electrolytes Produce Ions</h3>

  <p>
    Ionic compounds contain positive and negative ions. When a soluble
    ionic compound dissolves in water, its ions separate and become
    mobile.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Example: Sodium chloride</strong></p>

  <p>
    Sodium chloride separates into sodium ions and chloride ions in water:
  </p>

  <p><strong>NaCl(s) → Na⁺(aq) + Cl⁻(aq)</strong></p>

  <p>
    The aqueous symbols, (aq), indicate that the ions are dissolved
    in water.
  </p>

  <p>
    Some strong electrolytes are molecular substances that ionise in
    water. For example, hydrochloric acid forms hydrogen ions and
    chloride ions in aqueous solution:
  </p>

  <p><strong>HCl(aq) → H⁺(aq) + Cl⁻(aq)</strong></p>

  <p>
    Here, H⁺ is the conventional simplified notation used at this level;
    in water, hydrogen ions are associated with water molecules.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Examples of Strong Electrolytes</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Sodium chloride solution.</li>
    <li>Hydrochloric acid solution.</li>
    <li>Sodium hydroxide solution.</li>
    <li>Potassium nitrate solution.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    These examples produce mobile ions extensively under suitable
    aqueous conditions.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Electrical Conductivity</h3>

  <p>
    A solution containing many mobile ions can conduct electricity.
    When a potential difference is applied, positive ions move towards
    the negative electrode and negative ions move towards the positive
    electrode.
  </p>

  <p>
    However, the conductivity of a solution also depends on factors such
    as concentration, temperature, ion charge and ion mobility.
    A strong electrolyte can conduct poorly if its solution is extremely
    dilute compared with a more concentrated solution of another
    electrolyte.
  </p>

  <h3>5. Strength Is Not the Same as Concentration</h3>

  <p>
    <strong>Strength</strong> describes the extent to which a substance
    forms ions in solution. <strong>Concentration</strong> describes
    the amount of dissolved substance in a given volume of solution.
  </p>

  <p>
    Therefore, a dilute solution of a strong electrolyte can contain
    fewer ions per unit volume than a concentrated solution of a weak
    electrolyte. Do not use the words strong and concentrated as if
    they mean the same thing.
  </p>

  <h3>6. Worked Examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: Identifying a strong electrolyte</strong></p>

  <p>
    Sodium chloride dissolves in water and separates into Na⁺ and Cl⁻
    ions. Explain why the solution conducts electricity.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the particles formed.</p>
  <p>Sodium chloride produces Na⁺ and Cl⁻ ions.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Identify whether they can move.</p>
  <p>The ions are mobile in the aqueous solution.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Link ion movement to conduction.</p>
  <p>The ions carry charge through the solution when a potential difference
  is applied.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The solution conducts because it contains
  mobile positive and negative ions.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: Writing an ionisation equation</strong></p>

  <p>
    Write the equation for hydrochloric acid forming ions in water using
    the simplified school-level notation.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Write the formula of hydrochloric acid.</p>
  <p>HCl</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Identify the ions produced.</p>
  <p>H⁺ and Cl⁻</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Check atom and charge balance.</p>
  <p>There is one hydrogen atom, one chlorine atom, and a total charge of
  zero on both sides.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> HCl(aq) → H⁺(aq) + Cl⁻(aq)</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: Distinguishing strength from concentration</strong></p>

  <p>
    A student says that a concentrated weak electrolyte must be a stronger
    electrolyte than a dilute strong electrolyte. Explain the error.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Define strength.</p>
  <p>Strength concerns the extent of ionisation or dissociation.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Define concentration.</p>
  <p>Concentration concerns the amount of dissolved substance per volume.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Compare the two ideas.</p>
  <p>A concentrated solution does not automatically have a greater extent
  of ionisation.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The claim is incorrect because strength and
  concentration describe different properties.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Strong Electrolytes', 15),
  (869, 869, '
  <h2>Weak Electrolytes</h2>

  <h3>1. Meaning of a Weak Electrolyte</h3>

  <p>
    A <strong>weak electrolyte</strong> is a substance that forms ions
    only partially when dissolved in water. At equilibrium, the solution
    contains both ions and un-ionised molecules.
  </p>

  <p>
    Because only a fraction of the dissolved substance forms ions, a weak
    electrolyte generally conducts electricity less effectively than a
    comparable solution of a strong electrolyte at the same concentration
    and under similar conditions.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Example: Ethanoic Acid</h3>

  <p>
    Ethanoic acid, CH₃COOH, is a weak acid. It ionises partially in water:
  </p>

  <p><strong>CH₃COOH(aq) ⇌ H⁺(aq) + CH₃COO⁻(aq)</strong></p>

  <p>
    The double arrow, ⇌, indicates a reversible process. The forward
    reaction forms ions, while the reverse reaction allows ions to
    combine again.
  </p>

  <p>
    At equilibrium, many ethanoic acid particles remain as CH₃COOH
    molecules, while some exist as ions.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Example: Ammonia in Water</h3>

  <p>
    Ammonia reacts partially with water to form ammonium and hydroxide ions:
  </p>

  <p><strong>NH₃(aq) + H₂O(l) ⇌ NH₄⁺(aq) + OH⁻(aq)</strong></p>

  <p>
    This reversible reaction produces ions, but it does not convert all
    dissolved ammonia particles into ions.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Why Weak Electrolytes Conduct Electricity</h3>

  <p>
    A weak electrolyte still conducts electricity because some ions are
    present and can move through the solution. Its conductivity is often
    lower than that of a strong electrolyte of the same concentration
    because fewer ions are formed.
  </p>

  <p>
    Conductivity also depends on concentration, temperature and ion
    mobility. Therefore, do not conclude that a weak electrolyte produces
    no ions or cannot conduct at all.
  </p>

  <h3>5. Weak Does Not Mean Dilute</h3>

  <p>
    The word <strong>weak</strong> describes partial ionisation.
    The word <strong>dilute</strong> describes a low amount of dissolved
    substance per unit volume.
  </p>

  <p>
    A concentrated solution of a weak electrolyte is possible. Likewise,
    a dilute solution of a strong electrolyte is possible.
  </p>

  <h3>6. Worked Examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: Identifying partial ionisation</strong></p>

  <p>
    Ethanoic acid is dissolved in water. Explain what makes it a weak
    electrolyte.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the ionisation process.</p>
  <p>CH₃COOH ⇌ H⁺ + CH₃COO⁻</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Interpret the reversible arrow.</p>
  <p>The reaction proceeds in both directions.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Identify the particles at equilibrium.</p>
  <p>Both ions and un-ionised ethanoic acid molecules remain present.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Ethanoic acid is weak because it ionises
  only partially in water.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: Writing the equation for ammonia</strong></p>

  <p>
    Write the equation for ammonia reacting with water to produce
    ammonium and hydroxide ions.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Write the reactants.</p>
  <p>NH₃ + H₂O</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Write the products.</p>
  <p>NH₄⁺ + OH⁻</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Check atom and charge balance.</p>
  <p>Atoms are balanced, and the product charges +1 and −1 sum to zero.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> NH₃(aq) + H₂O(l) ⇌ NH₄⁺(aq) + OH⁻(aq)</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: Comparing two solutions</strong></p>

  <p>
    Two solutions have the same concentration and are tested under similar
    conditions. One contains a strong electrolyte and the other a weak
    electrolyte. Predict which generally conducts better and explain why.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Compare the extent of ion formation.</p>
  <p>The strong electrolyte forms ions extensively; the weak electrolyte
  forms ions partially.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Compare the mobile charge carriers.</p>
  <p>Under comparable conditions, the strong electrolyte generally provides
  more mobile ions.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The strong electrolyte generally conducts
  better because it produces more mobile ions at the same concentration.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Weak Electrolytes', 15),
  (870, 870, '
  <h2>Non-electrolytes</h2>

  <h3>1. Meaning of a Non-electrolyte</h3>

  <p>
    A <strong>non-electrolyte</strong> is a substance that does not
    produce a significant concentration of mobile ions when dissolved
    in water. Its solution therefore does not conduct electricity
    effectively through ions.
  </p>

  <p>
    Many non-electrolytes are molecular substances. When they dissolve,
    their molecules remain largely as neutral particles instead of
    separating into positive and negative ions.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Example: Sugar in Water</h3>

  <p>
    Ordinary table sugar, sucrose, dissolves in water. Its molecules
    spread throughout the water, but they do not normally split into
    ions simply because the sugar dissolves.
  </p>

  <p><strong>C₁₂H₂₂O₁₁(s) → C₁₂H₂₂O₁₁(aq)</strong></p>

  <p>
    This equation represents sucrose molecules dissolving. It does not
    show the formation of ions.
  </p>

  <p>
    A sugar solution therefore contains mostly neutral sugar molecules
    and water molecules. It has very few ions from the sugar itself and
    conducts electricity very poorly compared with an electrolyte solution.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Example: Ethanol in Water</h3>

  <p>
    Ethanol mixes with water, but its molecules remain largely neutral.
    Dissolving ethanol does not normally produce a substantial
    concentration of ions.
  </p>

  <p>
    Consequently, ethanol solution is a poor conductor of electricity
    compared with solutions containing mobile ions.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Why Dissolving Does Not Always Produce Ions</h3>

  <p>
    Dissolving means that a substance mixes with a solvent to form a
    solution. Ion formation is a separate process.
  </p>

  <p>
    An ionic substance may dissociate into ions when it dissolves.
    Some molecular substances ionise in water. Other molecular
    substances, such as sucrose, dissolve without forming significant
    numbers of ions.
  </p>

  <p>
    Therefore, a substance can dissolve readily in water and still be
    a non-electrolyte.
  </p>

  <h3>5. Comparing the Three Types</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Strong electrolyte:</strong> Forms ions extensively.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Weak electrolyte:</strong> Forms ions partially.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Non-electrolyte:</strong> Does not form a significant
      concentration of ions under the conditions considered.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    These categories describe ion formation, not simply whether a
    substance dissolves or whether it is present in a large amount.
  </p>

  <h3>6. Worked Examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: Explaining sugar solution</strong></p>

  <p>
    Sugar dissolves in water, but the resulting solution conducts
    electricity very poorly. Explain why.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the particles in the solution.</p>
  <p>Sugar molecules and water molecules are present.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Determine whether sugar forms ions.</p>
  <p>Sucrose dissolves largely as neutral molecules.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Relate the particles to conduction.</p>
  <p>There are very few mobile ions supplied by the sugar.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Sugar is a non-electrolyte because it dissolves
  without producing a significant concentration of mobile ions.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: Distinguishing dissolving from ionisation</strong></p>

  <p>
    Explain why the statement “every substance that dissolves in water
    conducts electricity” is incorrect.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Define dissolving.</p>
  <p>Dissolving distributes particles of a substance throughout a solvent.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Consider the type of particles formed.</p>
  <p>Some substances form mobile ions; others remain as neutral molecules.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Link mobile ions to conductivity.</p>
  <p>A solution needs mobile charge carriers for effective ionic conduction.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> A substance may dissolve as neutral molecules
  without forming enough ions to conduct electricity effectively.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: Classifying ethanol solution</strong></p>

  <p>
    Ethanol mixes with water but remains largely in molecular form.
    Classify the solution in terms of electrolyte behaviour.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the particles formed.</p>
  <p>Ethanol molecules remain largely neutral in water.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Check whether significant ion formation occurs.</p>
  <p>Ethanol does not produce a significant concentration of ions under
  ordinary aqueous conditions.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Ethanol is treated as a non-electrolyte.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Non-electrolytes', 15),
  (871, 871, '
  <h2>Introduction to Organic Chemistry</h2>

  <h3>1. Meaning of Organic Chemistry</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Organic chemistry</b> is the branch of chemistry that studies
    carbon compounds, especially hydrocarbons and compounds derived
    from hydrocarbons.
  </p>

  <p>
    Carbon forms a very large number of compounds because its atoms
    can form four covalent bonds and join to other carbon atoms to
    produce chains, branches and rings.
  </p>

  <h3>2. What Is an Organic Compound?</h3>

  <p>
    An organic compound is a carbon compound belonging to the broad
    group studied in organic chemistry. Many organic compounds contain
    hydrogen, and some also contain oxygen, nitrogen, sulfur or
    halogens such as chlorine.
  </p>

  <p>Examples include:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>Methane, CH<sub>4</sub> — a simple hydrocarbon.</li>
    <li>Ethanol, C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub>OH — contains carbon, hydrogen and oxygen.</li>
    <li>Ethanoic acid, CH<sub>3</sub>COOH — contains carbon, hydrogen and oxygen.</li>
    <li>Glucose, C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>12</sub>O<sub>6</sub> — contains carbon, hydrogen and oxygen.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>3. What Is a Hydrocarbon?</h3>

  <p>
    A <b>hydrocarbon</b> is a compound containing only carbon and
    hydrogen atoms. Methane, ethane and ethene are hydrocarbons.
    Ethanol is not a hydrocarbon because it also contains oxygen.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Important Exceptions at School Level</h3>

  <p>
    Although organic chemistry focuses on carbon compounds, not every
    carbon-containing substance is normally classified as organic.
    Carbon dioxide, CO<sub>2</sub>, carbon monoxide, CO, and carbonates
    such as calcium carbonate, CaCO<sub>3</sub>, are commonly studied
    as inorganic substances at this level.
  </p>

  <p>
    Therefore, the rule is not simply that every compound containing
    carbon must be organic. The type of compound matters.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Identify an Organic Compound</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Which of these is an organic compound: methane,
  CH<sub>4</sub>, or carbon dioxide, CO<sub>2</sub>?</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Recognise that methane is a simple carbon-hydrogen compound.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Recognise that carbon dioxide is conventionally classified as inorganic.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Methane is the organic compound.</p>
  <p><b>Reason:</b> Methane belongs to the hydrocarbons, while carbon dioxide is treated as inorganic at school level.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Distinguish a Hydrocarbon from an Organic Compound</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Ethanol has the formula C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub>OH.
  Is it a hydrocarbon?</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> A hydrocarbon must contain only carbon and hydrogen.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Ethanol contains oxygen, shown by the O in its formula.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Ethanol is not a hydrocarbon.</p>
  <p><b>Reason:</b> It is an organic compound, but it contains an element other than carbon and hydrogen.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Classify Glucose</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Glucose has the formula C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>12</sub>O<sub>6</sub>.
  Is it a hydrocarbon?</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Examine the elements present: carbon, hydrogen and oxygen.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Compare them with the definition of a hydrocarbon.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Glucose is not a hydrocarbon.</p>
  <p><b>Reason:</b> Its formula contains oxygen. Glucose is an organic compound containing more than two elements.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Introduction to Organic Chemistry', 15),
  (872, 872, '
  <h2>Tetravalency of Carbon</h2>

  <h3>1. Meaning of Tetravalency</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Tetravalency</b> is the ability of an atom to form four covalent
    bonds. Carbon has four electrons in its outer shell. It commonly
    forms four covalent bonds by sharing electrons with other atoms.
  </p>

  <p>
    A covalent bond forms when atoms share a pair of electrons. In
    simple organic compounds, each shared pair forms one covalent bond.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Tetravalency in Methane</h3>

  <p>
    Methane has the molecular formula CH<sub>4</sub>. One carbon atom
    forms four single covalent bonds with four hydrogen atoms.
  </p>

  <p>
    Each hydrogen forms one covalent bond, while carbon forms four.
    This satisfies the usual bonding capacity of both elements in methane.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Tetravalency in Other Compounds</h3>

  <p>
    Carbon''s four bonds do not have to be four separate single bonds
    to four different atoms. For example, carbon dioxide contains two
    carbon-oxygen double bonds. Each double bond counts as two covalent
    bonds around carbon, giving carbon four bonds in total.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Count Bonds in Methane</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> How many covalent bonds are present in one methane molecule, CH4?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Methane contains one carbon atom and four hydrogen atoms.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Each hydrogen forms one single covalent bond with carbon.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Four C–H bonds are formed.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Four single covalent bonds.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Identify the Bonding Capacity</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> A carbon atom forms four single bonds with four hydrogen atoms. Name the property demonstrated.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Count the bonds formed by carbon: four.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> The ability to form four bonds is called tetravalency.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Tetravalency.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Count Bonds in Carbon Dioxide</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Carbon dioxide has the structure O=C=O. How many covalent bonds does carbon form?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Carbon forms one double bond with the first oxygen.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> It forms another double bond with the second oxygen.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Each double bond contains two shared pairs, so carbon forms four bonds in total.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Four covalent bonds.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Tetravalency of Carbon', 15),
  (873, 873, '
  <h2>Catenation</h2>

  <h3>1. Meaning of Catenation</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Catenation</b> is the ability of atoms of the same element to
    bond to one another, forming chains, branches or rings.
  </p>

  <p>
    Carbon shows extensive catenation because carbon-carbon covalent
    bonds are sufficiently strong to allow many stable structures.
    Carbon can form long chains, branched chains and rings.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Types of Carbon Chains</h3>

  <ul>
    <li><b>Straight chain:</b> carbon atoms are connected in an unbranched sequence.</li>
    <li><b>Branched chain:</b> the main carbon chain has one or more side branches.</li>
    <li><b>Ring:</b> carbon atoms connect to form a closed chain.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>3. Why Catenation Matters</h3>

  <p>
    Carbon can form compounds containing two, three, four or many
    more carbon atoms. Different arrangements of the same number of
    carbon atoms can also produce different compounds.
  </p>

  <p>
    Catenation is not exclusive to carbon; some other elements also
    form bonds with themselves. Carbon is particularly important
    because of the extensive variety and stability of its structures.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Identify Catenation</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> A molecule contains a chain of five carbon atoms joined by C–C bonds. Which property allows this chain to form?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify the repeated atoms: carbon atoms.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> They are bonded to one another.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Catenation.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Compare a Chain and a Ring</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> A carbon structure is a closed loop rather than an open chain. Does this demonstrate catenation?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Check whether carbon atoms bond to other carbon atoms.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> In a carbon ring, the carbon atoms form a connected closed chain.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Yes. A carbon ring is an example of catenation.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Explain Carbon''s Variety</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Explain how catenation helps carbon form many different compounds.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Carbon atoms can bond to one another.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> They can form chains of different lengths, branches and rings.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> These structures can combine with different atoms and bonds.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Catenation allows carbon to form many different molecular structures, increasing the variety of carbon compounds.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Catenation', 15),
  (874, 874, '
  <h2>Hydrocarbons</h2>

  <h3>1. Definition</h3>

  <p>
    A <b>hydrocarbon</b> is a compound made up of carbon and hydrogen
    atoms only. If a compound contains oxygen, nitrogen or another
    element, it is not a hydrocarbon.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Main Groups of Hydrocarbons</h3>

  <p>
    In introductory organic chemistry, hydrocarbons are commonly
    classified by the types of carbon-carbon bonds present.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><b>Alkanes:</b> open-chain saturated hydrocarbons containing only C–C single bonds.</li>
    <li><b>Alkenes:</b> hydrocarbons containing at least one C=C double bond; the basic family studied here has one double bond in an open chain.</li>
    <li><b>Alkynes:</b> hydrocarbons containing at least one C≡C triple bond; the basic family studied here has one triple bond in an open chain.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>3. Saturated and Unsaturated Hydrocarbons</h3>

  <p>
    A saturated open-chain hydrocarbon has only carbon-carbon single
    bonds. Alkanes are saturated hydrocarbons.
  </p>

  <p>
    An unsaturated hydrocarbon contains a carbon-carbon double or
    triple bond. Alkenes and alkynes are unsaturated.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Classify Methane</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Methane has formula CH4. Is it a hydrocarbon?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify the elements: carbon and hydrogen.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> No other element appears in the formula.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Yes, methane is a hydrocarbon.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Classify Ethanol</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Ethanol has formula C2H5OH. Is it a hydrocarbon?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Check the formula for elements other than carbon and hydrogen.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Ethanol contains oxygen.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> No, ethanol is not a hydrocarbon.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Identify Unsaturation</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> A hydrocarbon contains a C=C bond. Which main group does it belong to in this introductory classification?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify the carbon-carbon double bond.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Hydrocarbons with a C=C bond belong to the alkene group.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> An alkene.</p>

  <p>
    <b>Important:</b> A molecular formula alone does not always prove
    whether a compound is an alkene or a cycloalkane. Structural
    information may be needed.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Hydrocarbons', 15),
  (875, 875, '
  <h2>Alkanes</h2>

  <h3>1. Definition</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Alkanes</b> are saturated open-chain hydrocarbons containing
    only single bonds between carbon atoms.
  </p>

  <p>
    Because their carbon-carbon bonds are single bonds, alkanes contain
    the maximum number of hydrogen atoms possible for an open-chain
    hydrocarbon with that number of carbon atoms.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Names of the First Five Alkanes</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Methane — CH<sub>4</sub></li>
    <li>Ethane — C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>6</sub></li>
    <li>Propane — C<sub>3</sub>H<sub>8</sub></li>
    <li>Butane — C<sub>4</sub>H<sub>10</sub></li>
    <li>Pentane — C<sub>5</sub>H<sub>12</sub></li>
  </ul>

  <h3>3. Recognising an Alkane</h3>

  <p>
    To identify an alkane, check that the substance is a hydrocarbon
    and that its carbon framework is an open chain containing only
    single carbon-carbon bonds.
  </p>

  <p>
    The general formula for open-chain alkanes is
    C<sub>n</sub>H<sub>2n+2</sub>, where n is the number of carbon atoms.
    The formula is explored in detail on the dedicated formula screen.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Identify an Alkane</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Ethane contains two carbon atoms joined by a single bond. Which hydrocarbon family does it belong to?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Ethane contains carbon and hydrogen only.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Its carbon atoms are joined by a single bond.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Ethane belongs to the alkane family.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Name C3H8</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Name the alkane containing three carbon atoms.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> The prefix for three carbon atoms is prop-.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> The suffix for an alkane is -ane.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Propane, C3H8.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Compare Ethane and Ethene</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Explain why ethane is an alkane but ethene is an alkene.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Ethane has a single C–C bond.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Ethene has a C=C double bond.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Ethane is saturated and belongs to the alkanes; ethene is unsaturated and belongs to the alkenes.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Alkanes', 15),
  (876, 876, '
  <h2>Alkenes</h2>

  <h3>1. Definition</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Alkenes</b> are unsaturated hydrocarbons containing at least
    one carbon-carbon double bond, C=C. In this introductory section,
    the main examples are open-chain alkenes containing one double bond.
  </p>

  <h3>2. The Double Bond</h3>

  <p>
    In a carbon-carbon double bond, two pairs of electrons are shared
    between the carbon atoms. The double bond affects the number of
    hydrogen atoms that can be attached to the carbon framework.
  </p>

  <p>
    The simplest alkene is ethene, C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>4</sub>.
    It contains two carbon atoms joined by a double bond.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Names of Common Alkenes</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Ethene — C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>4</sub></li>
    <li>Propene — C<sub>3</sub>H<sub>6</sub></li>
    <li>Butene — C<sub>4</sub>H<sub>8</sub></li>
    <li>Pentene — C<sub>5</sub>H<sub>10</sub></li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    For compounds with four or more carbon atoms, the position of the
    double bond may need to be stated. For example, but-1-ene and
    but-2-ene have different double-bond positions.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Recognise an Alkene</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> A hydrocarbon contains a C=C bond. Which family does it belong to?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify the double bond.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> A hydrocarbon containing a carbon-carbon double bond belongs to the alkene family in this classification.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Alkene.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Name C2H4</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Name the two-carbon alkene.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Two carbon atoms use the prefix eth-.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> The suffix -ene indicates a carbon-carbon double bond.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Ethene, C2H4.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Explain Unsaturation</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Why is ethene unsaturated?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Ethene contains a C=C double bond.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> A double bond means the carbon framework does not have the maximum hydrogen count of the corresponding open-chain alkane.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Ethene is unsaturated because it contains a carbon-carbon double bond.</p>

  <p>
    <b>Scope note:</b> The formula CnH2n applies to open-chain alkenes
    with one double bond. A cyclic hydrocarbon may share that formula,
    so structure must be considered when identifying an unknown compound.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Alkenes', 15),
  (877, 877, '
  <h2>Alkynes</h2>

  <h3>1. Definition</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Alkynes</b> are unsaturated hydrocarbons containing at least
    one carbon-carbon triple bond, C≡C. The introductory examples
    here are open-chain alkynes containing one triple bond.
  </p>

  <h3>2. The Triple Bond</h3>

  <p>
    A carbon-carbon triple bond contains three shared pairs of electrons.
    In an alkyne, the triple bond changes how many hydrogen atoms can
    attach to the carbon framework compared with an alkane.
  </p>

  <p>
    The simplest alkyne is ethyne, C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>2</sub>.
    It contains two carbon atoms joined by a triple bond.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Names of Common Alkynes</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Ethyne — C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>2</sub></li>
    <li>Propyne — C<sub>3</sub>H<sub>4</sub></li>
    <li>Butyne — C<sub>4</sub>H<sub>6</sub></li>
    <li>Pentyne — C<sub>5</sub>H<sub>8</sub></li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    For longer chains, the triple-bond position may need to be stated,
    for example but-1-yne and but-2-yne.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Identify an Alkyne</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> A hydrocarbon contains a carbon-carbon triple bond. Identify its family.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify the C≡C bond.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Hydrocarbons with this bond belong to the alkyne family.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Alkyne.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Name C2H2</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Name the simplest alkyne.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> The molecule contains two carbon atoms, so use eth-.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> The suffix -yne identifies the triple-bond family.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Ethyne, C2H2.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Compare Ethene and Ethyne</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Distinguish between ethene and ethyne by their carbon-carbon bonds.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Ethene contains a C=C double bond.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Ethyne contains a C≡C triple bond.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Ethene is an alkene; ethyne is an alkyne.</p>

  <p>
    <b>Scope note:</b> CnH2n−2 applies to open-chain alkynes with one
    triple bond. A molecular formula alone may not identify a unique
    structure in every case.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Alkynes', 15),
  (878, 878, '
  <h2>Homologous Series</h2>

  <h3>1. Definition</h3>

  <p>
    A <b>homologous series</b> is a family of organic compounds that
    share the same functional group, have the same general formula
    for the family, and show a regular pattern in their molecular
    structures.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Main Characteristics</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Members have the same functional group, where applicable.</li>
    <li>Members follow the same general formula.</li>
    <li>Successive members differ by one CH<sub>2</sub> unit.</li>
    <li>Members have similar chemical properties.</li>
    <li>Physical properties generally change gradually as molecular size increases.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>3. Example: The Alkane Homologous Series</h3>

  <p>
    Methane, CH<sub>4</sub>; ethane, C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>6</sub>;
    propane, C<sub>3</sub>H<sub>8</sub>; and butane, C<sub>4</sub>H<sub>10</sub>
    belong to the alkane homologous series.
  </p>

  <p>
    Compare methane with ethane: moving from CH<sub>4</sub> to
    C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>6</sub> adds one carbon and two hydrogen atoms,
    equivalent to adding CH<sub>2</sub>. The same pattern occurs
    between successive members.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Identify Successive Members</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Are ethane, C2H6, and propane, C3H8, successive members of the alkane series?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Compare the carbon counts: 2 and 3.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Compare the hydrogen counts: 6 and 8.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> The difference is C1H2, or CH2.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Yes, they are successive members.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Find the Next Member</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> What is the next alkane after propane, C3H8?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Add one carbon atom.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Add two hydrogen atoms.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> The new formula is C4H10.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Butane, C4H10.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Explain Similar Chemical Properties</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Why do members of the alkane series have similar chemical properties?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> All members belong to the same family.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> They have the same general type of carbon-carbon and carbon-hydrogen bonding.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Their structures and bonding patterns follow the same family pattern, so their chemical properties are similar, though not necessarily identical in every reaction condition.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Homologous Series', 15),
  (879, 879, '
  <h2>General Formula of Alkanes</h2>

  <h3>1. The Formula</h3>

  <p>
    The general formula for an open-chain alkane is:
  </p>

  <p><b>C<sub>n</sub>H<sub>2n+2</sub></b></p>

  <p>
    In this formula, <b>n</b> is the number of carbon atoms in the
    molecule. The expression 2n + 2 gives the number of hydrogen atoms.
  </p>

  <h3>2. How to Use the Formula</h3>

  <p>
    If the number of carbon atoms is known, substitute that value for
    n and calculate the number of hydrogen atoms.
  </p>

  <p>
    For example, if n = 3:
  </p>

  <p>H = 2n + 2</p>
  <p>H = 2(3) + 2</p>
  <p>H = 6 + 2 = 8</p>
  <p>The formula is C<sub>3</sub>H<sub>8</sub>.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Find the Formula for n = 4</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Find the molecular formula of an open-chain alkane containing four carbon atoms.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Write the general formula: CnH2n+2.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Substitute n = 4.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Calculate hydrogen: 2(4) + 2 = 10.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> C4H10, butane.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Find the Formula for n = 6</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Find the formula of an open-chain alkane containing six carbon atoms.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Substitute n = 6 into H = 2n + 2.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> H = 2(6) + 2.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> H = 12 + 2 = 14.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> C6H14, hexane.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Determine the Number of Carbon Atoms</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> An alkane has the formula C7H16. Verify that it fits the general formula.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify n = 7.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Calculate 2n + 2 = 2(7) + 2 = 16.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Compare the calculated hydrogen count with the given count.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> C7H16 fits the alkane formula because the calculated hydrogen count is 16.</p>

  <h3>3. Important Limitation</h3>

  <p>
    This formula applies to open-chain alkanes. Cyclic saturated
    hydrocarbons have a different general formula because forming a
    ring changes the hydrogen count.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for General Formula of Alkanes', 15),
  (880, 880, '
  <h2>General Formulae of Alkenes and Alkynes</h2>

  <h3>1. Formula for Open-Chain Alkenes</h3>

  <p>
    An open-chain alkene containing one carbon-carbon double bond
    follows the general formula:
  </p>

  <p><b>C<sub>n</sub>H<sub>2n</sub></b></p>

  <p>
    Here, n is the number of carbon atoms. The formula applies to
    open-chain alkenes with one double bond, not every possible
    compound with the same atom counts.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Formula for Open-Chain Alkynes</h3>

  <p>
    An open-chain alkyne containing one carbon-carbon triple bond
    follows the general formula:
  </p>

  <p><b>C<sub>n</sub>H<sub>2n-2</sub></b></p>

  <p>
    The minimum value of n for either an open-chain alkene or alkyne
    with a carbon-carbon multiple bond is 2, because the bond requires
    two carbon atoms.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Why the Formulae Differ</h3>

  <p>
    For the same number of carbon atoms, an open-chain alkane has
    formula CnH2n+2. Introducing one double bond reduces the hydrogen
    count by two, giving CnH2n. Introducing one triple bond reduces
    it by four relative to the corresponding alkane, giving CnH2n-2.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Find the Formula of an Alkene with Four Carbons</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Calculate the formula of an open-chain alkene with one double bond and four carbon atoms.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Use CnH2n.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Substitute n = 4.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> H = 2(4) = 8.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> C4H8.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Find the Formula of an Alkyne with Four Carbons</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Calculate the formula of an open-chain alkyne with one triple bond and four carbon atoms.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Use CnH2n-2.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Substitute n = 4.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> H = 2(4) − 2 = 6.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> C4H6.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Compare the Two Families</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Compare the formulae of an open-chain alkene and an open-chain alkyne, each containing five carbon atoms and one multiple bond.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> For the alkene, H = 2(5) = 10.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> For the alkyne, H = 2(5) − 2 = 8.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> The alkene formula is C5H10; the alkyne formula is C5H8.</p>
  <p><b>Reason:</b> With the same number of carbon atoms, the alkyne has two fewer hydrogen atoms than the corresponding one-double-bond alkene.</p>

  <h3>4. Important Limitation</h3>

  <p>
    A general formula describes a family of structures. It does not
    always identify one unique compound. For example, C4H8 may
    represent an open-chain alkene or a cycloalkane. Structural
    information is needed to distinguish them.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for General Formulae of Alkenes and Alkynes', 15),
  (881, 881, '<h2>Structural Formulae</h2>

  <h3>1. What is a structural formula?</h3>
  <p>A <strong>structural formula</strong> shows how atoms are connected in a molecule. It tells us which atoms are joined by covalent bonds and whether carbon atoms are connected by single, double or triple bonds.</p>

  <p>A molecular formula tells us the number of atoms of each element, but it does not necessarily show how those atoms are arranged.</p>

  <p>For example, the molecular formula C₂H₆ tells us that a molecule contains two carbon atoms and six hydrogen atoms. Its structural formula is CH₃–CH₃.</p>

  <h3>2. Applying carbon''s valency</h3>
  <p>Carbon normally forms four covalent bonds. Hydrogen forms one covalent bond. When drawing a structural formula, ensure that each carbon has four bonds in total, counting a double bond as two bonds and a triple bond as three.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Ethane</h3>
  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Write the structural formula of ethane, C₂H₆.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Place the two carbon atoms together: C–C.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Each carbon already has one bond to the other carbon.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Each carbon therefore needs three more bonds. Attach three hydrogen atoms to each carbon.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> CH₃–CH₃.</p>
  <p><strong>Check:</strong> There are two carbon atoms and six hydrogen atoms. Each carbon forms four bonds.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Propane</h3>
  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Write the structural formula of propane, C₃H₈.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Arrange three carbon atoms in a chain: C–C–C.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Each end carbon has one carbon-carbon bond, so each needs three hydrogen atoms.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> The middle carbon has two carbon-carbon bonds, so it needs two hydrogen atoms.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> CH₃–CH₂–CH₃.</p>
  <p><strong>Check:</strong> The hydrogen total is 3 + 2 + 3 = 8.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Propene</h3>
  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Write a structural formula for propene, C₃H₆, with the double bond at the end of the chain.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Arrange three carbon atoms in a chain.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Place a double bond between the first and second carbon atoms: C=C–C.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Complete each carbon''s four bonds with hydrogen atoms.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> CH₂=CH–CH₃.</p>
  <p><strong>Check:</strong> The hydrogen total is 2 + 1 + 3 = 6. The double bond must be shown because it is part of the structure.</p>

  <h3>Common mistake</h3>
  <p>Do not assume that a molecular formula alone always identifies a unique structure. Different structures can have the same molecular formula; these are called structural isomers.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Structural Formulae', 15),
  (882, 882, '<h2>Condensed Structural Formulae</h2>

  <h3>1. Meaning</h3>
  <p>A <strong>condensed structural formula</strong> represents a molecule by grouping hydrogen atoms with the carbon atoms to which they are attached. It avoids drawing every individual C–H bond but keeps the order of the groups visible.</p>

  <p>For example, CH₃–CH₂–CH₂–CH₃ may be written as CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₃. Both represent straight-chain butane.</p>

  <h3>2. Understanding the groups</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>CH₃:</strong> one carbon attached to three hydrogen atoms.</li>
    <li><strong>CH₂:</strong> one carbon attached to two hydrogen atoms.</li>
    <li><strong>CH:</strong> one carbon attached to one hydrogen atom.</li>
    <li><strong>C:</strong> a carbon written without hydrogen in that group; its other bonds must be considered.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>The number of hydrogens in each group depends on the bonds that carbon makes to other atoms. Never assume that every carbon must be written as CH₃.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Propane</h3>
  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Write the condensed structural formula of propane.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Propane has three carbon atoms.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> The terminal carbons each have three hydrogen atoms.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> The middle carbon has two hydrogen atoms.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> CH₃CH₂CH₃.</p>
  <p><strong>Check:</strong> There are three carbons and eight hydrogens.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Counting atoms</h3>
  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Determine the molecular formula represented by CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₃.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Count the four carbon groups: four carbon atoms.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Add the hydrogen counts: 3 + 2 + 2 + 3 = 10.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> C₄H₁₀.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Pentane</h3>
  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Write the condensed structural formula of straight-chain pentane.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Arrange five carbon atoms in a chain.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Write CH₃ at both ends.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Write CH₂ for each of the three internal carbons.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂CH₃.</p>
  <p><strong>Check:</strong> The formula contains C₅H₁₂.</p>

  <h3>Important distinction</h3>
  <p>Brackets can show branches. For example, CH₃CH(CH₃)CH₃ represents a branched molecule: the CH₃ inside brackets is attached to the carbon immediately before the brackets.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Condensed Structural Formulae', 15),
  (883, 883, '<h2>Naming Straight-Chain Alkanes</h2>

  <h3>1. What is a straight-chain alkane?</h3>
  <p>An alkane is a saturated hydrocarbon containing carbon-carbon single bonds. A straight-chain alkane has its carbon atoms connected in one continuous, unbranched chain.</p>

  <p>In basic nomenclature, its name combines a prefix indicating the number of carbon atoms with the ending <strong>-ane</strong>.</p>

  <h3>2. Carbon prefixes</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>1 carbon: meth-</li>
    <li>2 carbons: eth-</li>
    <li>3 carbons: prop-</li>
    <li>4 carbons: but-</li>
    <li>5 carbons: pent-</li>
    <li>6 carbons: hex-</li>
    <li>7 carbons: hept-</li>
    <li>8 carbons: oct-</li>
    <li>9 carbons: non-</li>
    <li>10 carbons: dec-</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Method</h3>
  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Count the carbon atoms in the continuous chain.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Select the corresponding prefix.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Add -ane because the compound is an alkane.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: CH₃CH₂CH₃</h3>
  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Count three carbon atoms.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Three carbons use the prefix prop-.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Add -ane.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Propane.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂CH₃</h3>
  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Count five carbon atoms.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Five carbons use pent-.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Add -ane.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Pentane.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂CH₂CH₃</h3>
  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Count six carbon atoms.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Six carbons use hex-.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Add -ane.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Hexane.</p>

  <h3>Check before naming</h3>
  <p>Make sure the structure is unbranched and contains only carbon-carbon single bonds. If the chain contains a double or triple bond, it belongs to a different naming pattern.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Naming Straight-Chain Alkanes', 15),
  (884, 884, '<h2>Naming Branched-Chain Alkanes</h2>

  <h3>1. What is a branched-chain alkane?</h3>
  <p>A branched-chain alkane contains a main carbon chain with one or more carbon-containing groups attached to it. These attached groups are called <strong>alkyl substituents</strong>.</p>

  <p>A one-carbon substituent is methyl, –CH₃. A two-carbon substituent is ethyl, –CH₂CH₃.</p>

  <h3>2. Naming procedure</h3>
  <p><strong>Step 1: Find the longest continuous carbon chain.</strong> This determines the parent alkane name.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2: Number the parent chain.</strong> Start from the end that gives the substituent or substituents the lowest possible locants.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3: Identify each substituent.</strong> For example, –CH₃ is methyl.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 4: Record each substituent''s position.</strong> The number identifies the parent-chain carbon to which it is attached.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 5: Combine the name.</strong> Write the position number, substituent name and parent alkane name.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: CH₃CH(CH₃)CH₃</h3>
  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> The longest continuous chain has three carbons: propane.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> The additional CH₃ group is a methyl substituent.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> The methyl group is attached to carbon 2.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 2-methylpropane.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: CH₃CH(CH₃)CH₂CH₃</h3>
  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> The longest chain has four carbons: butane.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> There is one methyl substituent.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Number from the end nearest the branch. The branch is on carbon 2, not carbon 3.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 2-methylbutane.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: CH₃CH₂CH(CH₃)CH₂CH₃</h3>
  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> The longest chain contains five carbons: pentane.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> The branch is a methyl group.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Numbering from either end places the branch on carbon 3.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 3-methylpentane.</p>

  <h3>Common mistake</h3>
  <p>Do not choose the parent chain merely because it looks horizontal or straight on the page. Follow the longest continuous route through the carbon skeleton. Also, do not confuse the position of a branch with the total number of carbon atoms in the molecule.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Naming Branched-Chain Alkanes', 15),
  (885, 885, '<h2>Naming Alkenes</h2>

  <h3>1. What is an alkene?</h3>
  <p>An alkene is an unsaturated hydrocarbon containing a carbon-carbon double bond. In naming an alkene, the parent chain must include the double bond.</p>

  <p>The ending <strong>-ene</strong> indicates a carbon-carbon double bond. The position number identifies the first carbon of that double bond in the numbered parent chain.</p>

  <h3>2. Naming procedure</h3>
  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Find the longest continuous carbon chain that contains the double bond.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Number the chain from the end nearest the double bond.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Identify the position where the double bond begins.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Use the carbon prefix and the ending -ene.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: CH₂=CH₂</h3>
  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> There are two carbon atoms, so use eth-.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> There is a carbon-carbon double bond, so use -ene.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Ethene. A position number is unnecessary because only one position is possible.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: CH₂=CHCH₂CH₃</h3>
  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> The chain has four carbon atoms, so use but-.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Number from the end nearest the double bond.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> The double bond begins at carbon 1.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> But-1-ene.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: CH₃CH=CHCH₃</h3>
  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> The chain contains four carbon atoms.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> The double bond lies between carbons 2 and 3.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Numbering from either end gives locant 2.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> But-2-ene.</p>

  <h3>Important check</h3>
  <p>The position number refers to the first carbon involved in the double bond. A four-carbon chain with a double bond beginning at carbon 1 is but-1-ene; one beginning at carbon 2 is but-2-ene.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Naming Alkenes', 15),
  (886, 886, '<h2>Naming Alkynes</h2>

  <h3>1. What is an alkyne?</h3>
  <p>An alkyne is an unsaturated hydrocarbon containing a carbon-carbon triple bond. The ending <strong>-yne</strong> indicates the presence of this bond.</p>

  <p>As with alkenes, the parent chain must contain the triple bond. The chain is numbered from the end nearest the triple bond so that the bond receives the lowest possible locant.</p>

  <h3>2. Naming procedure</h3>
  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Find the longest continuous carbon chain containing the triple bond.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Number from the end nearest the triple bond.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Identify the first carbon in the triple bond.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Use the carbon prefix and the ending -yne.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: HC≡CH</h3>
  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> The molecule has two carbon atoms, so use eth-.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> It contains a triple bond, so use -yne.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Ethyne.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: HC≡CCH₂CH₃</h3>
  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Count four carbon atoms.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Number from the end nearest the triple bond.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> The triple bond begins at carbon 1.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> But-1-yne.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: CH₃C≡CCH₃</h3>
  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> The chain contains four carbon atoms.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> The triple bond lies between carbons 2 and 3.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Its locant is 2.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> But-2-yne.</p>

  <h3>Check the bond type</h3>
  <p>Do not confuse -ene with -yne. An alkene has a C=C double bond; an alkyne has a C≡C triple bond. The locant is based on the first carbon involved in the multiple bond.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Naming Alkynes', 15),
  (887, 887, '<h2>Structural Isomerism</h2>

  <h3>1. Definition</h3>
  <p><strong>Structural isomers</strong> are compounds with the same molecular formula but different arrangements of bonds between their atoms. This means the atoms are connected differently, even though the total number of each type of atom is unchanged.</p>

  <h3>2. Example: C₄H₁₀</h3>
  <p>Butane has the straight-chain structure:</p>
  <p><strong>CH₃–CH₂–CH₂–CH₃</strong></p>
  <p>2-methylpropane has a branched structure:</p>
  <p><strong>CH₃–CH(CH₃)–CH₃</strong></p>
  <p>Both have the molecular formula C₄H₁₀. However, their carbon skeletons differ, so they are structural isomers.</p>

  <h3>3. How to check for structural isomerism</h3>
  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Compare the molecular formulae. They must be the same.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Compare the structures. Check whether the atoms are connected differently.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> If the formulae match and the connectivity differs, the compounds are structural isomers.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Identifying isomers</h3>
  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Are butane and 2-methylpropane structural isomers?</p>
  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Butane has formula C₄H₁₀.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> 2-methylpropane also has formula C₄H₁₀.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Butane has an unbranched chain, while 2-methylpropane has a branched carbon skeleton.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Yes, they are structural isomers.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Counting C₅H₁₂ isomers</h3>
  <p><strong>Question:</strong> How many structural isomers does C₅H₁₂ have?</p>
  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Draw the straight chain: pentane.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Draw a four-carbon chain with one methyl branch: 2-methylbutane. A branch at carbon 3 is equivalent after reversing the chain.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Draw a three-carbon chain with two methyl groups on carbon 2: 2,2-dimethylpropane.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Three structural isomers.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Rejecting a false pair</h3>
  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Are C₃H₈ and C₄H₁₀ structural isomers?</p>
  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Compare the molecular formulae.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> C₃H₈ has three carbon atoms; C₄H₁₀ has four.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> No. Their molecular formulae are different.</p>

  <h3>Important distinction</h3>
  <p>Structural isomerism is not the same as geometric isomerism. Structural isomers have different atom connectivity; geometric isomers have the same connectivity but different spatial arrangements.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Structural Isomerism', 15),
  (888, 888, '<h2>Geometric Isomerism</h2>

  <h3>1. Meaning</h3>
  <p><strong>Geometric isomerism</strong> occurs when compounds have the same molecular formula and the same atom connectivity but different spatial arrangements of groups around a restricted bond.</p>

  <p>In the common school-level examples involving alkenes, the carbon-carbon double bond restricts rotation. This can allow two different arrangements to exist.</p>

  <h3>2. Conditions for cis-trans isomerism</h3>
  <p>For an alkene to show the common cis-trans form of geometric isomerism:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>There must be a carbon-carbon double bond that restricts rotation.</li>
    <li>Each carbon atom in the double bond must have two different groups attached.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>If either double-bonded carbon has two identical groups attached, the usual cis-trans distinction does not apply.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: But-2-ene</h3>
  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Why can but-2-ene show geometric isomerism?</p>
  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> But-2-ene contains a C=C double bond.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Rotation around this double bond is restricted.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Each double-bonded carbon is attached to a hydrogen atom and a methyl group.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 4:</strong> The groups can be arranged with the methyl groups on the same side or on opposite sides.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> But-2-ene can exist as cis-but-2-ene and trans-but-2-ene.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Cis and trans</h3>
  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Distinguish cis-but-2-ene from trans-but-2-ene.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the C=C bond.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> In cis-but-2-ene, the two methyl groups are on the same side of the double bond.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> In trans-but-2-ene, the two methyl groups are on opposite sides.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Cis means same side; trans means opposite sides, for this pair of isomers.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Butane</h3>
  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Does butane show cis-trans geometric isomerism?</p>
  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Butane contains only carbon-carbon single bonds.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Single bonds can rotate, so butane does not have the restricted C=C bond required for the usual cis-trans alkene isomerism.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> No, butane does not show cis-trans geometric isomerism.</p>

  <h3>Structural versus geometric isomerism</h3>
  <p>Structural isomers have different atom connectivity. Geometric isomers have the same connectivity but different spatial arrangements. In both cases, the molecular formula can be the same.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Geometric Isomerism', 15),
  (889, 889, '
<h2>Alcohols</h2>

<h3>1. Meaning of an alcohol</h3>
<p>An <strong>alcohol</strong> is an organic compound containing a hydroxyl group, <strong>–OH</strong>, bonded to a saturated carbon atom. The –OH group is called the <strong>hydroxyl functional group</strong>.</p>

<p>A functional group is an atom or group of atoms responsible for characteristic chemical reactions of an organic compound. Identifying the functional group helps us classify a compound and predict some of its properties.</p>

<h3>2. General structure</h3>
<p>A simple alcohol can be represented as <strong>R–OH</strong>. Here, R represents the carbon-containing part of the molecule, while –OH is the hydroxyl group.</p>

<p>For example, in CH₃CH₂OH, the group CH₃CH₂– is the carbon-containing part and –OH is the functional group.</p>

<h3>3. Recognising alcohols</h3>
<ul>
<li>Methanol: CH₃OH — one carbon atom.</li>
<li>Ethanol: CH₃CH₂OH — two carbon atoms.</li>
<li>Propan-1-ol: CH₃CH₂CH₂OH — three carbon atoms.</li>
</ul>

<p>These compounds belong to the same homologous series because they contain the same functional group and have related chemical properties.</p>

<h3>4. Do not confuse alcohols with other compounds</h3>
<p>Not every compound containing oxygen is an alcohol. For example, ethanoic acid, CH₃COOH, contains a –COOH group and belongs to the carboxylic acid family. Its –OH is part of the carboxyl group, not an alcohol functional group.</p>

<h3>Worked example 1: Identify the functional group</h3>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which functional group identifies CH₃CH₂OH as an alcohol?</p>
<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Examine the formula CH₃CH₂OH.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Locate the oxygen and hydrogen written together as –OH.</p>
<p><strong>Step 3:</strong> This is the hydroxyl group.</p>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> The hydroxyl group, –OH.</p>

<h3>Worked example 2: Classify a compound</h3>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> Is CH₃CH₂CH₂OH an alcohol? Explain.</p>
<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Inspect the formula for a hydroxyl group.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> The formula ends in –OH.</p>
<p><strong>Step 3:</strong> The –OH group is attached to the three-carbon chain.</p>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Yes. It is propan-1-ol, an alcohol containing three carbon atoms.</p>

<h3>Worked example 3: Distinguish an alcohol from an acid</h3>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which is an alcohol: CH₃CH₂OH or CH₃COOH?</p>
<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> CH₃CH₂OH contains the hydroxyl group –OH attached to a carbon chain.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> CH₃COOH contains the carboxyl group –COOH.</p>
<p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Compounds containing –OH as the alcohol functional group are classified as alcohols, while compounds containing –COOH are carboxylic acids.</p>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> CH₃CH₂OH is the alcohol.</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Alcohols', 15),
  (890, 890, '
<h2>Ethanol</h2>

<h3>1. What is ethanol?</h3>
<p><strong>Ethanol</strong> is an alcohol containing two carbon atoms. The prefix <strong>eth-</strong> indicates two carbon atoms, while the ending <strong>-ol</strong> identifies the alcohol family.</p>

<p>Its molecular formula is <strong>C₂H₆O</strong>. It is also commonly written as <strong>C₂H₅OH</strong>, which makes the hydroxyl group easier to recognise.</p>

<h3>2. Understanding the formula</h3>
<p>The condensed structural formula of ethanol is <strong>CH₃CH₂OH</strong>.</p>
<ul>
<li>CH₃ represents the first carbon group.</li>
<li>CH₂ represents the second carbon group.</li>
<li>OH is the hydroxyl functional group.</li>
</ul>

<p>The two carbon atoms are joined by a single covalent bond. The atoms share electrons, and each carbon forms four covalent bonds in total.</p>

<h3>3. Counting atoms</h3>
<p>In C₂H₅OH, there are two carbon atoms, five hydrogen atoms in C₂H₅, one additional hydrogen in OH, and one oxygen atom. Therefore, the total is C₂H₆O.</p>

<h3>Worked example 1: Convert the formula</h3>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> Write the molecular formula of ethanol from C₂H₅OH.</p>
<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Count carbon atoms: C₂ means 2.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Count hydrogen atoms: H₅ plus the H in OH gives 5 + 1 = 6.</p>
<p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Count oxygen atoms: there is 1 O.</p>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> C₂H₆O.</p>

<h3>Worked example 2: Calculate relative molecular mass</h3>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> Calculate the relative molecular mass of ethanol, C₂H₆O. Use C = 12, H = 1 and O = 16.</p>
<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Carbon contribution = 2 × 12 = 24.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Hydrogen contribution = 6 × 1 = 6.</p>
<p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Oxygen contribution = 1 × 16 = 16.</p>
<p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Add the contributions: 24 + 6 + 16 = 46.</p>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Relative molecular mass = 46.</p>

<h3>Worked example 3: Find the percentage by mass of oxygen</h3>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> Calculate the percentage by mass of oxygen in ethanol, C₂H₆O.</p>
<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Relative molecular mass = 46.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Mass contributed by oxygen = 16.</p>
<p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Percentage of oxygen = (16 ÷ 46) × 100.</p>
<p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Percentage of oxygen ≈ 34.8%.</p>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Approximately 34.8% oxygen by mass.</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Ethanol', 15),
  (891, 891, '
<h2>Properties of Ethanol</h2>

<h3>1. Physical properties</h3>
<p>Ethanol is a colourless liquid at room temperature. It has a characteristic smell, evaporates relatively readily, mixes completely with water, and dissolves many substances.</p>

<p>A <strong>physical property</strong> can be observed or measured without changing the substance into a different chemical substance. Examples include colour, state, boiling point and miscibility.</p>

<h3>2. Volatility and miscibility</h3>
<p><strong>Volatility</strong> describes how readily a liquid changes into vapour. Ethanol is volatile, so it evaporates more readily than many less volatile liquids.</p>
<p><strong>Miscible</strong> means that two liquids mix completely to form one uniform liquid. Ethanol and water are miscible.</p>

<h3>3. Flammability and combustion</h3>
<p>Ethanol is flammable. When it burns completely in sufficient oxygen, it forms carbon dioxide and water and releases energy.</p>
<p><strong>C₂H₅OH + 3O₂ → 2CO₂ + 3H₂O</strong></p>

<p>To balance the equation, balance carbon first, then hydrogen, and finally oxygen. The equation has 2 carbon atoms and 6 hydrogen atoms on each side. It also has 7 oxygen atoms on each side.</p>

<h3>Worked example 1: Balance complete combustion</h3>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> Balance C₂H₅OH + O₂ → CO₂ + H₂O.</p>
<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> There are 2 carbon atoms in ethanol, so write 2CO₂.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> There are 6 hydrogen atoms, so write 3H₂O.</p>
<p><strong>Step 3:</strong> The products contain 4 + 3 = 7 oxygen atoms.</p>
<p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Ethanol supplies 1 oxygen atom, leaving 6 to be supplied by O₂.</p>
<p><strong>Step 5:</strong> Six oxygen atoms require 3O₂.</p>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> C₂H₅OH + 3O₂ → 2CO₂ + 3H₂O.</p>

<h3>Worked example 2: Distinguish physical and chemical properties</h3>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> Classify the statements “ethanol is colourless” and “ethanol burns in oxygen”.</p>
<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Colour can be observed without changing ethanol into another substance.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Burning causes ethanol to react chemically with oxygen.</p>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Colourlessness is a physical property; flammability is a chemical property.</p>

<h3>Worked example 3: Interpret the combustion equation</h3>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> What is the mole ratio of ethanol to carbon dioxide in complete combustion?</p>
<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Read the balanced equation: C₂H₅OH + 3O₂ → 2CO₂ + 3H₂O.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> The coefficient of ethanol is 1.</p>
<p><strong>Step 3:</strong> The coefficient of carbon dioxide is 2.</p>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> 1 mole of ethanol produces 2 moles of carbon dioxide under complete combustion.</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Properties of Ethanol', 15),
  (892, 892, '
<h2>Fermentation</h2>

<h3>1. Meaning of fermentation</h3>
<p><strong>Alcoholic fermentation</strong> is the conversion of sugars such as glucose into ethanol and carbon dioxide by yeast under anaerobic conditions. Anaerobic means that oxygen is absent or very limited.</p>

<h3>2. Reactant and products</h3>
<p>The reactant is glucose. The main products are ethanol and carbon dioxide.</p>
<p><strong>Word equation:</strong> glucose → ethanol + carbon dioxide</p>
<p><strong>Balanced symbol equation:</strong> C₆H₁₂O₆ → 2C₂H₅OH + 2CO₂</p>

<h3>3. Conditions required</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Yeast:</strong> supplies enzymes that catalyse the conversion.</li>
<li><strong>Glucose:</strong> supplies the sugar used in the process.</li>
<li><strong>Suitable temperature:</strong> allows the enzymes to work effectively. Excessive heat can denature enzymes and kill yeast.</li>
<li><strong>Little or no oxygen:</strong> provides the anaerobic conditions required for alcoholic fermentation.</li>
</ul>

<h3>4. Balancing the fermentation equation</h3>
<p>One glucose molecule contains 6 carbon atoms, 12 hydrogen atoms and 6 oxygen atoms. Two ethanol molecules contain 4 carbon atoms, while two carbon dioxide molecules contain 2. The total remains 6 carbon atoms.</p>

<h3>Worked example 1: Write the word equation</h3>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> State the word equation for alcoholic fermentation.</p>
<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the sugar: glucose.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Identify the products: ethanol and carbon dioxide.</p>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Glucose → ethanol + carbon dioxide.</p>

<h3>Worked example 2: Balance the symbol equation</h3>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> Balance C₆H₁₂O₆ → C₂H₅OH + CO₂.</p>
<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Place 2 before ethanol, giving 4 carbon atoms and 12 hydrogen atoms.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Place 2 before CO₂ to supply the remaining 2 carbon atoms.</p>
<p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Check oxygen: the products contain 2 oxygen atoms in ethanol and 4 in carbon dioxide, making 6.</p>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> C₆H₁₂O₆ → 2C₂H₅OH + 2CO₂.</p>

<h3>Worked example 3: Calculate the mole ratio</h3>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> According to the balanced equation, how many moles of ethanol can form from 3 moles of glucose if conversion is complete?</p>
<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> The equation shows 1 mole of glucose produces 2 moles of ethanol.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Multiply both sides of the ratio by 3.</p>
<p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Ethanol formed = 3 × 2 = 6 moles.</p>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> 6 moles of ethanol.</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Fermentation', 15),
  (893, 893, '
<h2>Oxidation of Ethanol</h2>

<h3>1. Meaning of oxidation in this reaction</h3>
<p>Ethanol can be oxidised to form <strong>ethanoic acid</strong>. Oxidation can involve the addition of oxygen, removal of hydrogen, or an increase in oxidation state. At this level, the important change is the conversion of an alcohol into a carboxylic acid.</p>

<h3>2. Overall change</h3>
<p><strong>Ethanol → ethanoic acid</strong></p>
<p>When oxygen is the oxidising reactant, the overall equation is:</p>
<p><strong>C₂H₅OH + O₂ → CH₃COOH + H₂O</strong></p>

<p>Check the atoms: both sides contain 2 carbon atoms, 6 hydrogen atoms and 3 oxygen atoms. The equation is balanced as written.</p>

<h3>3. Oxidation using an oxidising agent</h3>
<p>In a laboratory, ethanol can be oxidised using a suitable oxidising agent, such as acidified potassium dichromate(VI). Under appropriate conditions, ethanol is oxidised to ethanoic acid. If oxidation is incomplete or conditions are controlled differently, other products may form, so the conditions matter.</p>

<h3>4. Oxidation by microorganisms</h3>
<p>Ethanol can also be converted into ethanoic acid by microorganisms in the presence of oxygen. This is associated with the formation of vinegar from alcoholic liquids.</p>

<h3>Worked example 1: Identify the product</h3>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> What carboxylic acid forms when ethanol is oxidised fully under suitable conditions?</p>
<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Ethanol contains two carbon atoms.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Oxidation of ethanol can produce the corresponding two-carbon carboxylic acid.</p>
<p><strong>Step 3:</strong> The two-carbon carboxylic acid is ethanoic acid.</p>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Ethanoic acid, CH₃COOH.</p>

<h3>Worked example 2: Balance the equation</h3>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> Check whether C₂H₅OH + O₂ → CH₃COOH + H₂O is balanced.</p>
<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Carbon: 2 atoms on each side.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Hydrogen: 6 on the left; 4 in CH₃COOH plus 2 in H₂O gives 6 on the right.</p>
<p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Oxygen: 1 + 2 = 3 on the left and 2 + 1 = 3 on the right.</p>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> The equation is balanced as written.</p>

<h3>Worked example 3: Recognise the product family</h3>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> Classify CH₃COOH by its functional group.</p>
<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Locate the –COOH group.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> The –COOH group is the carboxyl group.</p>
<p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Compounds containing this group are carboxylic acids.</p>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> CH₃COOH is a carboxylic acid.</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Oxidation of Ethanol', 15),
  (894, 894, '
<h2>Carboxylic Acids</h2>

<h3>1. Meaning of carboxylic acids</h3>
<p><strong>Carboxylic acids</strong> are organic compounds containing the <strong>carboxyl functional group, –COOH</strong>. The carboxyl group is responsible for their characteristic acidic behaviour.</p>

<h3>2. Structure of the carboxyl group</h3>
<p>The carboxyl group contains a carbon atom double-bonded to oxygen and single-bonded to a hydroxyl group:</p>
<p><strong>–C(=O)–OH</strong></p>
<p>The complete group is written as –COOH. Although it contains an –OH part, the entire –COOH group is treated as one functional group.</p>

<h3>3. General formula</h3>
<p>A simple carboxylic acid can be represented as <strong>R–COOH</strong>, where R is the carbon-containing part attached to the carboxyl group.</p>

<h3>4. Examples and naming</h3>
<ul>
<li>Methanoic acid: HCOOH — one carbon atom.</li>
<li>Ethanoic acid: CH₃COOH — two carbon atoms.</li>
<li>Propanoic acid: CH₃CH₂COOH — three carbon atoms.</li>
</ul>

<p>When counting the carbon atoms in a carboxylic acid, the carbon in the –COOH group is included in the total carbon count.</p>

<h3>5. Why are they acidic?</h3>
<p>In water, carboxylic acids can donate a hydrogen ion, H⁺, to water molecules. For example:</p>
<p><strong>CH₃COOH + H₂O ⇌ CH₃COO⁻ + H₃O⁺</strong></p>
<p>This is an equilibrium reaction. Ethanoic acid is a weak acid because it ionises only partially in water.</p>

<h3>Worked example 1: Identify the functional group</h3>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> Identify the functional group in CH₃COOH.</p>
<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Locate the COOH part of the formula.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Write the group as –COOH.</p>
<p><strong>Step 3:</strong> This is the carboxyl group.</p>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Carboxyl group, –COOH.</p>

<h3>Worked example 2: Name a carboxylic acid</h3>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> Name HCOOH.</p>
<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Count the carbon atoms, including the carbon in –COOH: there is 1.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> The one-carbon prefix is meth-.</p>
<p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Carboxylic acid names end in -oic acid.</p>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Methanoic acid.</p>

<h3>Worked example 3: Write the formula of propanoic acid</h3>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> Write the condensed structural formula of propanoic acid.</p>
<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> The prefix prop- means the molecule has three carbon atoms in total.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> One of the three carbons belongs to the –COOH group.</p>
<p><strong>Step 3:</strong> The remaining two carbons form CH₃CH₂–.</p>
<p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Attach –COOH.</p>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> CH₃CH₂COOH.</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Carboxylic Acids', 15),
  (895, 895, '
<h2>Ethanoic Acid</h2>

<h3>1. Formula and structure</h3>
<p><strong>Ethanoic acid</strong> is a carboxylic acid containing two carbon atoms. Its molecular formula is <strong>C₂H₄O₂</strong>, and its condensed structural formula is <strong>CH₃COOH</strong>.</p>

<p>The formula CH₃COOH shows a methyl group, CH₃–, attached to a carboxyl group, –COOH. The carbon atom inside –COOH is included in the total of two carbon atoms.</p>

<h3>2. Physical properties</h3>
<ul>
<li>Pure ethanoic acid is a colourless liquid at ordinary room temperature.</li>
<li>It has a sharp, characteristic smell.</li>
<li>It mixes with water.</li>
<li>It is acidic and reacts with suitable bases, carbonates and reactive metals.</li>
</ul>

<p>Vinegar is a dilute aqueous solution of ethanoic acid. It is not pure ethanoic acid.</p>

<h3>3. Counting atoms in the molecular formula</h3>
<p>C₂H₄O₂ contains 2 carbon atoms, 4 hydrogen atoms and 2 oxygen atoms.</p>

<h3>Worked example 1: Calculate relative molecular mass</h3>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> Calculate the relative molecular mass of C₂H₄O₂. Use C = 12, H = 1 and O = 16.</p>
<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Carbon contribution = 2 × 12 = 24.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Hydrogen contribution = 4 × 1 = 4.</p>
<p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Oxygen contribution = 2 × 16 = 32.</p>
<p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Total = 24 + 4 + 32 = 60.</p>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Relative molecular mass = 60.</p>

<h3>Worked example 2: Find the percentage by mass of hydrogen</h3>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> Calculate the percentage by mass of hydrogen in ethanoic acid, C₂H₄O₂.</p>
<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Relative molecular mass = 60.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Hydrogen contributes 4 × 1 = 4.</p>
<p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Percentage of hydrogen = (4 ÷ 60) × 100.</p>
<p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Percentage of hydrogen ≈ 6.67%.</p>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Approximately 6.67% hydrogen by mass.</p>

<h3>Worked example 3: Identify the functional group</h3>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which functional group makes CH₃COOH a carboxylic acid?</p>
<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Examine the formula and identify the COOH part.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Write it as –COOH.</p>
<p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Name the group as the carboxyl group.</p>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> The carboxyl group, –COOH.</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Ethanoic Acid', 15),
  (896, 896, '
<h2>Reactions of Ethanoic Acid</h2>

<h3>1. General behaviour</h3>
<p>Ethanoic acid shows the characteristic reactions of an acid. It reacts with sufficiently reactive metals, carbonates and alkalis. The products depend on the substance reacting with the acid.</p>

<h3>2. Reaction with reactive metals</h3>
<p>An acid reacts with a sufficiently reactive metal to produce a salt and hydrogen gas.</p>
<p><strong>2CH₃COOH + Mg → (CH₃COO)₂Mg + H₂</strong></p>
<p>The salt is magnesium ethanoate. Hydrogen gas may be identified by bringing a lighted splint near the collected gas; hydrogen burns with a squeaky pop. The test must be carried out using suitable laboratory safety procedures.</p>

<h3>3. Reaction with carbonates</h3>
<p>An acid reacts with a carbonate to form a salt, water and carbon dioxide.</p>
<p><strong>2CH₃COOH + Na₂CO₃ → 2CH₃COONa + H₂O + CO₂</strong></p>
<p>Carbon dioxide can be tested by bubbling it through limewater. The limewater turns milky because calcium carbonate forms.</p>

<h3>4. Reaction with alkalis</h3>
<p>Ethanoic acid reacts with sodium hydroxide in a neutralisation reaction. The products are sodium ethanoate and water.</p>
<p><strong>CH₃COOH + NaOH → CH₃COONa + H₂O</strong></p>

<h3>5. How to balance these equations</h3>
<p>Write the correct formulas first, then count each type of atom on both sides. Change coefficients in front of formulas, not the subscripts inside formulas. Changing a subscript changes the identity of a compound.</p>

<h3>Worked example 1: Reaction with sodium hydroxide</h3>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> Complete and balance CH₃COOH + NaOH →</p>
<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> An acid reacts with an alkali to form a salt and water.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> The salt formed from ethanoic acid and sodium hydroxide is sodium ethanoate, CH₃COONa.</p>
<p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Add water, H₂O, as the other product.</p>
<p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Check the atoms. They are equal on both sides.</p>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> CH₃COOH + NaOH → CH₃COONa + H₂O.</p>

<h3>Worked example 2: Balance reaction with sodium carbonate</h3>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> Balance CH₃COOH + Na₂CO₃ → CH₃COONa + H₂O + CO₂.</p>
<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Na₂CO₃ contains two sodium atoms, so put 2 before CH₃COONa.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Two sodium ethanoate units require two ethanoate groups, so put 2 before CH₃COOH.</p>
<p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Keep H₂O and CO₂ with coefficients of 1.</p>
<p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Check the atoms on both sides; all are balanced.</p>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> 2CH₃COOH + Na₂CO₃ → 2CH₃COONa + H₂O + CO₂.</p>

<h3>Worked example 3: Predict the products with magnesium</h3>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> Predict the products when ethanoic acid reacts with magnesium.</p>
<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> An acid reacting with a reactive metal produces a salt and hydrogen gas.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> The metal determines the metal ion in the salt. Magnesium forms Mg²⁺.</p>
<p><strong>Step 3:</strong> The ethanoate ion is CH₃COO⁻, so two ethanoate ions are required to balance one Mg²⁺ ion.</p>
<p><strong>Step 4:</strong> The salt formula is Mg(CH₃COO)₂.</p>
<p><strong>Step 5:</strong> Balance the equation.</p>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> 2CH₃COOH + Mg → Mg(CH₃COO)₂ + H₂.</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Reactions of Ethanoic Acid', 15),
  (897, 897, '<h2>Addition Reactions of Alkenes</h2>

<h3>1. What is an addition reaction?</h3>
<p>An addition reaction is a chemical reaction in which atoms or groups of atoms combine with an unsaturated molecule to form one main product.</p>

<p>Alkenes undergo addition reactions because they contain a carbon-carbon double bond, C=C. The double bond allows atoms to add across the two carbon atoms.</p>

<p>During an addition reaction, the carbon-carbon double bond becomes a single bond. Each of the two carbon atoms forms a new bond with an added atom or group.</p>

<h3>2. Hydrogenation</h3>
<p>Hydrogenation is the addition of hydrogen to an unsaturated compound. When an alkene reacts with hydrogen, the alkene is converted into an alkane.</p>

<p>The reaction normally uses a nickel catalyst and heating.</p>

<p><strong>Ethene + hydrogen → ethane</strong></p>
<p><strong>C₂H₄ + H₂ → C₂H₆</strong></p>

<p>Before the reaction, ethene contains a C=C double bond. After hydrogenation, ethane contains a C–C single bond. The product is saturated because it no longer contains the carbon-carbon double bond.</p>

<h3>3. Addition of bromine</h3>
<p>Bromine also adds across the double bond of an alkene. Each carbon atom in the original double bond becomes bonded to one bromine atom.</p>

<p><strong>Ethene + bromine → 1,2-dibromoethane</strong></p>
<p><strong>C₂H₄ + Br₂ → CH₂BrCH₂Br</strong></p>

<p>The product is called 1,2-dibromoethane because it has two carbon atoms and bromine atoms attached to carbon numbers 1 and 2.</p>

<h3>4. Testing for unsaturation</h3>
<p>Bromine water is orange-brown. When shaken with an alkene, it is decolourised as bromine reacts with the carbon-carbon double bond. This is a test for unsaturation.</p>

<p>An alkane does not normally decolourise bromine water under these test conditions because it does not have a carbon-carbon double bond.</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1: Hydrogenation</h3>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> Write the equation for the reaction between propene and hydrogen.</p>
<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Propene is an alkene with formula C₃H₆.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Hydrogen is H₂.</p>
<p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Hydrogen adds across the double bond, producing propane, C₃H₈.</p>
<p><strong>Equation:</strong> C₃H₆ + H₂ → C₃H₈.</p>
<p><strong>Check:</strong> There are 3 carbon atoms and 8 hydrogen atoms on each side.</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2: Bromine addition</h3>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> What is formed when propene reacts with bromine?</p>
<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Propene has the structure CH₂=CHCH₃.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> The double bond is between carbon atoms 1 and 2.</p>
<p><strong>Step 3:</strong> One bromine atom adds to each of these carbon atoms.</p>
<p><strong>Product:</strong> CH₂Br–CHBr–CH₃, called 1,2-dibromopropane.</p>
<p><strong>Equation:</strong> C₃H₆ + Br₂ → C₃H₆Br₂.</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3: Identifying an alkene</h3>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> A gas turns bromine water from orange-brown to colourless. What does this observation suggest?</p>
<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Bromine reacts by addition with carbon-carbon double bonds.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> The loss of the orange-brown colour shows that bromine has reacted.</p>
<p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> The gas may contain an alkene and is therefore unsaturated.</p>
<p><strong>Important:</strong> This test is evidence of unsaturation under the test conditions; it does not by itself identify the exact compound.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Addition Reactions', 15),
  (898, 898, '<h2>Ester Formation</h2>

<h3>1. What is an ester?</h3>
<p>An ester is an organic compound containing the functional group –COO– between carbon-containing groups. Many esters have distinctive, often fruity smells and are used in flavourings, fragrances and industrial products.</p>

<h3>2. What is esterification?</h3>
<p>Esterification is the reaction between an alcohol and a carboxylic acid to form an ester and water.</p>

<p><strong>Alcohol + carboxylic acid ⇌ ester + water</strong></p>

<p>The reaction is commonly carried out by heating the reactants with a few drops of concentrated sulfuric acid. The acid provides an acidic catalyst and can also help remove water.</p>

<h3>3. Naming an ester</h3>
<p>The first part of an ester name comes from the alcohol. The second part comes from the carboxylic acid.</p>

<ul>
<li>Ethanol gives the group name ethyl.</li>
<li>Ethanoic acid gives the name ethanoate.</li>
<li>The ester formed from ethanol and ethanoic acid is ethyl ethanoate.</li>
</ul>

<h3>4. Example: Ethyl ethanoate</h3>
<p><strong>Ethanoic acid + ethanol ⇌ ethyl ethanoate + water</strong></p>
<p><strong>CH₃COOH + C₂H₅OH ⇌ CH₃COOC₂H₅ + H₂O</strong></p>

<p>During the reaction, the –OH part of the carboxylic acid and a hydrogen from the alcohol combine to form water. The remaining parts join to form the ester. This is a useful simplified way to understand the overall change.</p>

<h3>5. Why is the reaction reversible?</h3>
<p>The products can react under suitable conditions to reform the alcohol and carboxylic acid. The double arrow indicates that the reaction can proceed in both directions.</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1: Predicting an ester</h3>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> What ester forms when methanol reacts with ethanoic acid?</p>
<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Methanol supplies the methyl part of the ester name.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Ethanoic acid supplies the ethanoate part.</p>
<p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Combine the names.</p>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Methyl ethanoate.</p>
<p><strong>Equation:</strong> CH₃COOH + CH₃OH ⇌ CH₃COOCH₃ + H₂O.</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2: Writing an esterification equation</h3>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> Write the equation for propanoic acid reacting with ethanol.</p>
<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Propanoic acid is CH₃CH₂COOH.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Ethanol is C₂H₅OH.</p>
<p><strong>Step 3:</strong> The ester is ethyl propanoate, CH₃CH₂COOC₂H₅.</p>
<p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Water is also formed.</p>
<p><strong>Equation:</strong> CH₃CH₂COOH + C₂H₅OH ⇌ CH₃CH₂COOC₂H₅ + H₂O.</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3: Identifying reactants</h3>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> An ester is called propyl methanoate. Which alcohol and carboxylic acid are needed to prepare it?</p>
<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> The first name, propyl, identifies the alcohol-derived group.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> The alcohol is propanol.</p>
<p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Methanoate identifies the acid-derived part.</p>
<p><strong>Step 4:</strong> The carboxylic acid is methanoic acid.</p>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Propanol and methanoic acid.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Ester Formation', 15),
  (899, 899, '<h2>Addition Polymerisation</h2>

<h3>1. Monomers and polymers</h3>
<p>A <strong>monomer</strong> is a small molecule that can join with many similar molecules to form a larger molecule.</p>
<p>A <strong>polymer</strong> is a very large molecule made from many repeating units joined by covalent bonds.</p>
<p>In addition polymerisation, many unsaturated monomers, usually alkenes, join together without eliminating a small molecule such as water.</p>

<h3>2. How addition polymerisation occurs</h3>
<p>An alkene contains a carbon-carbon double bond. During polymerisation, the double bond opens sufficiently for each monomer to form new carbon-carbon single bonds with neighbouring units.</p>
<p>Repeated joining creates a long chain. The atoms of the monomer are incorporated into the polymer chain.</p>

<h3>3. Poly(ethene)</h3>
<p>Ethene, CH₂=CH₂, is the monomer used to make poly(ethene).</p>

<p><strong>nCH₂=CH₂ → [–CH₂–CH₂–]ₙ</strong></p>

<p>The symbol <strong>n</strong> represents a large number of ethene molecules. The section [–CH₂–CH₂–] is the repeating unit.</p>

<p>Ethene is the monomer. Poly(ethene) is the polymer. The repeating unit contains two carbon atoms and four hydrogen atoms.</p>

<h3>4. Poly(propene)</h3>
<p>Propene, CH₂=CHCH₃, also undergoes addition polymerisation.</p>
<p><strong>nCH₂=CHCH₃ → [–CH₂–CH(CH₃)–]ₙ</strong></p>
<p>The methyl group, –CH₃, remains attached to every other carbon in the polymer backbone as shown by the repeating unit.</p>

<h3>5. Why addition polymerisation is important</h3>
<p>Polymers are used to make packaging, containers, pipes, household products, fibres and many other materials. Their properties depend on the monomer, the polymer structure and the manufacturing process.</p>

<p>Many common addition polymers are chemically resistant and durable. However, some plastic waste persists in the environment, so reuse, appropriate recycling and responsible disposal are important.</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1: Identifying a monomer</h3>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which monomer forms poly(ethene)?</p>
<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> The polymer name contains the name ethene.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> The monomer is ethene, CH₂=CH₂.</p>
<p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Its double bond opens during addition polymerisation.</p>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Ethene.</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2: Finding a repeating unit</h3>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> Write the repeating unit of poly(propene).</p>
<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Propene is CH₂=CHCH₃.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> The two double-bonded carbon atoms form the polymer backbone.</p>
<p><strong>Step 3:</strong> The methyl group remains attached to one of those backbone carbon atoms.</p>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> [–CH₂–CH(CH₃)–]ₙ.</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3: Counting monomer units</h3>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> A poly(ethene) chain contains 40 repeating units. How many carbon atoms are represented in those units?</p>
<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Each –CH₂–CH₂– repeating unit contains two carbon atoms.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> There are 40 repeating units.</p>
<p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Number of carbon atoms = 40 × 2 = 80.</p>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> 80 carbon atoms in the 40 repeating units.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Addition Polymerisation', 15),
  (900, 900, '<h2>Crude Oil</h2>

<h3>1. What is crude oil?</h3>
<p>Crude oil is a naturally occurring mixture of hydrocarbons found in underground rock formations and beneath the seabed.</p>

<p>A hydrocarbon is a compound containing only carbon and hydrogen. Crude oil contains many different hydrocarbons, with molecules of different sizes and structures.</p>

<p>Crude oil is a mixture rather than a single compound because its components are not chemically joined together in one fixed formula. Its composition varies from one source to another.</p>

<h3>2. How crude oil forms</h3>
<p>Crude oil formed over millions of years from the remains of ancient organisms. Burial beneath layers of sediment, together with heat, pressure and geological conditions, transformed organic material into petroleum.</p>

<p>Crude oil is a non-renewable resource on human timescales because it takes millions of years to form and is consumed much faster than new supplies develop.</p>

<h3>3. Why crude oil is separated</h3>
<p>Crude oil contains hydrocarbons with different boiling points. Smaller molecules generally have lower boiling points than larger molecules from similar hydrocarbon series.</p>

<p>Different hydrocarbons have different uses. Refineries separate crude oil into useful groups called <strong>fractions</strong>.</p>

<h3>4. Fractional distillation</h3>
<p>Fractional distillation separates the components of a mixture according to differences in their boiling points. It is a physical separation process; it does not normally change the hydrocarbons into different substances.</p>

<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Crude oil is heated so that much of it vaporises.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> The vapours enter a fractionating column, which is hot near the bottom and cooler near the top.</p>
<p><strong>Step 3:</strong> As vapours rise and cool, different hydrocarbons condense at different heights according to their boiling-point ranges.</p>
<p><strong>Step 4:</strong> The fractions are collected separately.</p>

<p>Fractions are groups of many hydrocarbons with similar boiling-point ranges, not usually pure individual compounds.</p>

<h3>5. Properties of fractions</h3>
<p>For many fractions, moving from lighter to heavier fractions generally means:</p>
<ul>
<li>increasing average molecular size;</li>
<li>increasing boiling-point range;</li>
<li>increasing viscosity;</li>
<li>decreasing volatility;</li>
<li>decreasing ease of ignition.</li>
</ul>

<p>These are general trends. A fraction contains a range of compounds, so its properties are not described by one exact molecular formula.</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1: Classifying crude oil</h3>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> Is crude oil an element, compound or mixture?</p>
<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> An element contains one type of atom.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> A pure compound contains elements chemically combined in a fixed ratio.</p>
<p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Crude oil contains many different hydrocarbons.</p>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Crude oil is a mixture.</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2: Explaining separation</h3>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> Why can fractional distillation separate hydrocarbons in crude oil?</p>
<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> The hydrocarbons have different boiling points.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> The column has a temperature gradient.</p>
<p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Different hydrocarbons condense at different temperatures and heights.</p>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> The hydrocarbons are separated because they have different boiling-point ranges.</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3: Comparing two fractions</h3>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> Fraction A contains generally smaller hydrocarbons than fraction B. Which is likely to be more volatile?</p>
<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Smaller hydrocarbons generally have lower boiling points than larger ones in comparable series.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> A substance with a lower boiling point evaporates more readily.</p>
<p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Therefore, fraction A is generally more volatile.</p>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Fraction A.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Crude Oil', 15),
  (901, 901, '<h2>Cracking</h2>

<h3>1. What is cracking?</h3>
<p>Cracking is the process of breaking large hydrocarbon molecules into smaller molecules by heating them under suitable conditions. A catalyst may be used in catalytic cracking.</p>

<p>Cracking is useful because some heavy fractions contain large hydrocarbons that are less useful or less demanded than smaller hydrocarbons.</p>

<h3>2. Why cracking is important</h3>
<p>Cracking helps meet demand for smaller hydrocarbons used as fuels. It also produces alkenes, which are important raw materials for making polymers and other chemicals.</p>

<p>Cracking commonly produces a mixture of smaller alkanes and alkenes. The exact products depend on the starting hydrocarbon and the cracking conditions.</p>

<h3>3. Conditions for cracking</h3>
<p>Cracking requires high temperatures. Catalytic cracking uses a suitable catalyst, such as a zeolite catalyst, to help the process occur efficiently. The exact conditions depend on the industrial process.</p>

<h3>4. Understanding cracking equations</h3>
<p>A large hydrocarbon breaks into two or more smaller molecules. The total number of carbon atoms and hydrogen atoms must be the same before and after the reaction.</p>

<p><strong>Example:</strong></p>
<p><strong>C₁₀H₂₂ → C₈H₁₈ + C₂H₄</strong></p>

<p>Decane breaks into octane and ethene. Octane is an alkane and ethene is an alkene.</p>

<p><strong>Carbon check:</strong> 10 on the left; 8 + 2 = 10 on the right.</p>
<p><strong>Hydrogen check:</strong> 22 on the left; 18 + 4 = 22 on the right.</p>
<p>The equation is balanced.</p>

<h3>5. Recognising products</h3>
<p>Alkanes have the general formula CₙH₂ₙ₊₂ for open-chain molecules containing only single carbon-carbon bonds.</p>
<p>Alkenes with one carbon-carbon double bond and an open chain have the general formula CₙH₂ₙ.</p>
<p>These formulas help identify likely products, but the structural formula or reaction context may be needed to distinguish compounds.</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1: Identifying cracking</h3>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> A large hydrocarbon is heated and breaks into smaller hydrocarbons. Name the process.</p>
<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> The starting material is a large hydrocarbon.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> It breaks down into smaller molecules.</p>
<p><strong>Step 3:</strong> This process is called cracking.</p>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Cracking.</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2: Balancing a cracking equation</h3>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> Check whether C₁₂H₂₆ → C₈H₁₈ + C₄H₈ is balanced.</p>
<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Count carbon atoms on the right: 8 + 4 = 12.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Count hydrogen atoms on the right: 18 + 8 = 26.</p>
<p><strong>Step 3:</strong> The left side also has 12 carbon atoms and 26 hydrogen atoms.</p>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> The equation is balanced.</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3: Identifying an alkene product</h3>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> Cracking produces a hydrocarbon with formula C₃H₆. If it is an open-chain hydrocarbon with one double bond, identify its homologous series.</p>
<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> For an open-chain alkene with one double bond, the general formula is CₙH₂ₙ.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> For n = 3, the formula is C₃H₆.</p>
<p><strong>Step 3:</strong> The formula matches the alkene general formula.</p>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> It is an alkene, such as propene.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Cracking', 15),
  (902, 902, '
  <h2>Properties of Sulphur</h2>

  <h3>1. Identity of sulphur</h3>

  <p>Sulphur is a chemical element with the symbol <strong>S</strong>
  and atomic number 16. It is classified as a non-metal and occurs
  naturally in the Earth''s crust and in some minerals.</p>

  <p>At room temperature, ordinary sulphur is a yellow solid.
  Its physical properties help distinguish it from metals and
  other non-metals.</p>

  <h3>2. Physical properties</h3>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Colour:</strong> Sulphur is yellow.</li>
    <li><strong>Physical state:</strong> It is a solid at room temperature.</li>
    <li><strong>Brittleness:</strong> Solid sulphur breaks into pieces
    when struck rather than being hammered into sheets.</li>
    <li><strong>Electrical conductivity:</strong> It is a poor conductor
    of electricity.</li>
    <li><strong>Solubility:</strong> It is insoluble in water.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>These properties are physical because they can be observed
  without changing sulphur into a different chemical substance.</p>

  <h3>3. Burning sulphur in oxygen</h3>

  <p>Sulphur undergoes a chemical reaction when heated in oxygen.
  It burns to form sulphur dioxide, a colourless gas.</p>

  <p><strong>Word equation:</strong></p>
  <p>Sulphur + Oxygen → Sulphur dioxide</p>

  <p><strong>Symbol equation:</strong></p>
  <p><strong>S(s) + O₂(g) → SO₂(g)</strong></p>

  <p>The equation is balanced. There is one sulphur atom and
  two oxygen atoms on each side.</p>

  <p>Burning sulphur dioxide-producing materials can release
  harmful gases. Sulphur dioxide must not be deliberately inhaled.</p>

  <h3>4. Physical change versus chemical change</h3>

  <p>Breaking a piece of sulphur into smaller pieces is a physical
  change: the substance remains sulphur.</p>

  <p>Burning sulphur is a chemical change because sulphur reacts
  with oxygen to produce a new substance, sulphur dioxide.</p>

  <h3>Worked examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: Classifying properties</strong></p>

  <p>A student observes that sulphur is yellow, brittle and a poor
  conductor of electricity. Classify each observation.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Yellow describes its colour.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Brittle describes how easily the solid breaks.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Poor conductivity describes its ability to
  conduct electric current.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> All three are physical properties.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: Writing the combustion equation</strong></p>

  <p>Write the symbol equation for the complete reaction of sulphur
  with oxygen.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Write the reactants: S and O₂.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Identify the product: SO₂.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Check the atoms on both sides.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> S + O₂ → SO₂.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: Identifying the type of change</strong></p>

  <p>Explain why burning sulphur is a chemical change but breaking
  sulphur into powder is a physical change.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Breaking sulphur changes its size only.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> The material remains sulphur.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Burning produces sulphur dioxide, a new substance.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Breaking is a physical change; burning is
  a chemical change.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Properties of Sulphur', 15),
  (903, 903, '
  <h2>Sulphur Dioxide</h2>

  <h3>1. Formula and formation</h3>

  <p>Sulphur dioxide is a compound made from sulphur and oxygen.
  Its chemical formula is <strong>SO₂</strong>.</p>

  <p>Each molecule contains one sulphur atom and two oxygen atoms.
  It can form when sulphur burns in oxygen.</p>

  <p><strong>S(s) + O₂(g) → SO₂(g)</strong></p>

  <h3>2. Physical properties</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>It is a colourless gas at room temperature.</li>
    <li>It has a pungent, irritating smell, but it must never be
    deliberately smelled directly.</li>
    <li>It dissolves in water.</li>
    <li>It is denser than air under comparable conditions.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Sulphur dioxide is toxic and can irritate the respiratory system.
  Its colourlessness means it cannot be identified reliably by sight.</p>

  <h3>3. Acidic nature</h3>

  <p>When sulphur dioxide dissolves in water, the resulting solution
  is acidic. At school level, this is commonly represented as:</p>

  <p><strong>SO₂ + H₂O ⇌ H₂SO₃</strong></p>

  <p>H₂SO₃ is called sulphurous acid. The notation represents the
  aqueous chemistry of dissolved sulphur dioxide; sulphurous acid
  is not normally isolated as a stable pure substance.</p>

  <p>The acidic solution turns blue litmus red. This is an indicator
  of acidity, not a method for identifying the gas by itself.</p>

  <h3>4. Sulphur dioxide as an air pollutant</h3>

  <p>Sulphur dioxide may be released when sulphur-containing fuels
  burn or when some sulphide ores are processed.</p>

  <p>Its environmental and health effects include:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>Irritation of the eyes and respiratory system.</li>
    <li>Worsening of breathing problems, especially in susceptible people.</li>
    <li>Contribution to acid deposition after reactions in the atmosphere.</li>
    <li>Damage to vegetation and some building materials through acidic pollution.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Appropriate industrial gas treatment and emission controls reduce
  the amount released into the atmosphere.</p>

  <h3>Worked examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: Interpreting SO₂</strong></p>

  <p>How many sulphur and oxygen atoms are present in one molecule
  of sulphur dioxide?</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> S has no subscript, so it represents one
  sulphur atom.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> O₂ represents two oxygen atoms.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> One sulphur atom and two oxygen atoms.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: Preparing the equation</strong></p>

  <p>Write the balanced equation for sulphur reacting with oxygen.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Write S + O₂ as the reactants.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Write SO₂ as the product.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Count each atom on both sides.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> S + O₂ → SO₂.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: Explaining an indicator result</strong></p>

  <p>Moist blue litmus turns red in an environment containing dissolved
  sulphur dioxide. Explain the observation.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Sulphur dioxide dissolves in the moisture.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> The dissolved gas produces an acidic solution.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Acids turn blue litmus red.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The litmus turns red because the moist
  surface becomes acidic.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Sulphur Dioxide', 15),
  (904, 904, '
  <h2>Reactions of Sulphur Dioxide</h2>

  <h3>1. Reaction with water</h3>

  <p>Sulphur dioxide dissolves in water and produces an acidic
  solution. The reaction is commonly represented at school level by:</p>

  <p><strong>SO₂ + H₂O ⇌ H₂SO₃</strong></p>

  <p>H₂SO₃ is called sulphurous acid. The double arrow indicates
  that the aqueous reaction is reversible. The notation is a
  simplified representation of dissolved sulphur dioxide chemistry.</p>

  <p>Because the solution is acidic, it turns blue litmus red.</p>

  <h3>2. Oxidation to sulphur trioxide</h3>

  <p>Sulphur dioxide can react with oxygen to form sulphur trioxide,
  SO₃. Oxygen is added to the sulphur dioxide during oxidation.</p>

  <p><strong>2SO₂(g) + O₂(g) ⇌ 2SO₃(g)</strong></p>

  <p>The reaction is reversible under the conditions used in the
  Contact Process, which manufactures sulphuric acid. Vanadium(V)
  oxide, V₂O₅, is commonly used as the catalyst, at about 450°C
  in industrial operation.</p>

  <p>A catalyst increases the rate at which equilibrium is reached.
  It does not change the equilibrium position.</p>

  <h3>3. Balancing the oxidation equation</h3>

  <p>Begin with the unbalanced equation:</p>

  <p>SO₂ + O₂ ⇌ SO₃</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Balance sulphur by putting 2 before SO₂
  and SO₃.</p>

  <p>2SO₂ + O₂ ⇌ 2SO₃</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Count oxygen atoms on the left:
  2 × 2 + 2 = 6.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Count oxygen atoms on the right:
  2 × 3 = 6.</p>

  <p>The equation is balanced because both sides contain two
  sulphur atoms and six oxygen atoms.</p>

  <h3>4. Why these reactions matter</h3>

  <p>The reaction with water explains why dissolved sulphur dioxide
  produces an acidic solution.</p>

  <p>The oxidation of sulphur dioxide produces sulphur trioxide,
  an important intermediate in the industrial manufacture of
  sulphuric acid.</p>

  <p>These are different reactions: one involves water and produces
  an acidic solution; the other involves oxygen and produces SO₃.</p>

  <h3>Worked examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: Reaction with water</strong></p>

  <p>State the product represented when sulphur dioxide dissolves
  in water at school level.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Write the reactants, SO₂ and H₂O.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Identify the represented product, H₂SO₃.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Name the product.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Sulphurous acid, H₂SO₃.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: Balancing the oxidation reaction</strong></p>

  <p>Balance SO₂ + O₂ ⇌ SO₃.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Put 2 before SO₂ to provide two sulphur atoms.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Put 2 before SO₃ to match the sulphur atoms.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> The left now has six oxygen atoms and the
  right has six.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 2SO₂ + O₂ ⇌ 2SO₃.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: Distinguishing the reactions</strong></p>

  <p>Identify the reactant that reacts with sulphur dioxide in each case:
  (a) formation of an acidic aqueous solution; (b) formation of SO₃.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Water reacts with SO₂ in the first case.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Oxygen reacts with SO₂ in the second case.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> (a) H₂O; (b) O₂.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Reactions of Sulphur Dioxide', 15),
  (905, 905, '
  <h2>Sulphur Trioxide</h2>

  <h3>1. Formula and composition</h3>

  <p>Sulphur trioxide is a compound containing sulphur and oxygen.
  Its chemical formula is <strong>SO₃</strong>.</p>

  <p>One molecule of SO₃ contains one sulphur atom and three oxygen
  atoms. The prefix <strong>tri-</strong> indicates three oxygen
  atoms in the name sulphur trioxide.</p>

  <h3>2. Formation from sulphur dioxide</h3>

  <p>Sulphur trioxide is formed when sulphur dioxide reacts with
  oxygen. This reaction is an oxidation reaction because oxygen
  is added to sulphur dioxide.</p>

  <p><strong>2SO₂(g) + O₂(g) ⇌ 2SO₃(g)</strong></p>

  <p>The reaction is reversible under the conditions used in the
  Contact Process. In industry, a vanadium(V) oxide catalyst,
  V₂O₅, is commonly used at about 450°C.</p>

  <p>A catalyst speeds up the reaction, allowing equilibrium to be
  reached faster. It does not change the equilibrium position.</p>

  <h3>3. Reaction with water</h3>

  <p>Sulphur trioxide reacts vigorously with water to form
  sulphuric acid.</p>

  <p><strong>SO₃ + H₂O → H₂SO₄</strong></p>

  <p>This reaction releases a large amount of heat. The formation
  of fine acid mist can make direct absorption of SO₃ into water
  difficult to control.</p>

  <p>For this reason, industrial manufacture normally absorbs SO₃
  into concentrated sulphuric acid to form oleum. The oleum is
  then carefully diluted with water to obtain sulphuric acid.</p>

  <h3>4. Importance of sulphur trioxide</h3>

  <p>Sulphur trioxide is an important intermediate in the
  manufacture of sulphuric acid. Its reaction with water gives
  the overall chemical relationship, while the Contact Process
  uses controlled absorption to manage the reaction safely
  and efficiently.</p>

  <h3>Worked examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: Counting atoms in SO₃</strong></p>

  <p>Determine the total number of atoms in one molecule of SO₃.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> S represents one sulphur atom.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> O₃ represents three oxygen atoms.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Add the atoms: 1 + 3 = 4.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Four atoms: one sulphur and three oxygen atoms.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: Balancing the oxidation equation</strong></p>

  <p>Balance SO₂ + O₂ ⇌ SO₃.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Put 2 before SO₂ and 2 before SO₃.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> The left side has 2 sulphur atoms and
  2(2) + 2 = 6 oxygen atoms.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> The right side has 2 sulphur atoms
  and 2(3) = 6 oxygen atoms.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 2SO₂ + O₂ ⇌ 2SO₃.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: Reaction with water</strong></p>

  <p>Write the equation for the reaction of 1 mole of SO₃ with
  water and identify the acid formed.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Write the reactants SO₃ and H₂O.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Write H₂SO₄ as the product.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Check that S, H and O atoms are balanced.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> SO₃ + H₂O → H₂SO₄; sulphuric acid is formed.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Sulphur Trioxide', 15),
  (906, 906, '
  <h2>Sulphuric Acid</h2>

  <h3>1. Formula and composition</h3>

  <p>Sulphuric acid is an important mineral acid with the formula
  <strong>H₂SO₄</strong>.</p>

  <p>One formula unit contains two hydrogen atoms, one sulphur atom
  and four oxygen atoms.</p>

  <h3>2. Acidic properties</h3>

  <p>Sulphuric acid is a strong acid in its first ionisation in
  water. An aqueous solution contains hydrogen ions, represented
  more accurately in water as hydronium ions, and hydrogen
  sulphate and sulphate species.</p>

  <p>Common school-level indicators of an acidic solution include:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>Blue litmus turns red.</li>
    <li>Universal indicator shows a colour associated with low pH,
    depending on the solution concentration.</li>
    <li>Aqueous acid solutions conduct electricity because they
    contain mobile ions.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>3. Reaction with bases</h3>

  <p>Sulphuric acid reacts with suitable bases to form a sulphate
  salt and water. This is a neutralisation reaction.</p>

  <p><strong>H₂SO₄ + 2NaOH → Na₂SO₄ + 2H₂O</strong></p>

  <p>In this equation, sulphuric acid reacts with sodium hydroxide
  to form sodium sulphate and water.</p>

  <h3>4. Concentrated sulphuric acid</h3>

  <p>Concentrated sulphuric acid is highly corrosive. It can also
  remove water from some substances through its dehydrating action.</p>

  <p>For example, concentrated sulphuric acid can remove water
  from sugar, leaving a carbon-rich black mass. This is a
  chemical demonstration that must only be carried out by a
  properly equipped teacher in a controlled laboratory.</p>

  <p>Concentrated acid must be handled with appropriate protective
  equipment. Dilution releases substantial heat, so acid should
  be added slowly to water while stirring, never water poured
  into concentrated acid.</p>

  <h3>5. Distinguishing strength from concentration</h3>

  <p><strong>Acid strength</strong> describes the extent to which
  an acid ionises in water.</p>

  <p><strong>Concentration</strong> describes the amount of acid
  dissolved in a given volume of solution.</p>

  <p>These terms are not interchangeable. A strong acid can be
  dilute, and a weak acid can be relatively concentrated.</p>

  <h3>Worked examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: Counting atoms in H₂SO₄</strong></p>

  <p>Find the total number of atoms represented by one formula
  unit of H₂SO₄.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> H₂ represents 2 hydrogen atoms.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> S represents 1 sulphur atom.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> O₄ represents 4 oxygen atoms.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Add 2 + 1 + 4 = 7.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Seven atoms in total.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: Neutralisation</strong></p>

  <p>Write the balanced equation for sulphuric acid reacting
  completely with sodium hydroxide.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Write H₂SO₄ + NaOH.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> The salt is Na₂SO₄ and water is H₂O.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Put 2 before NaOH to supply two sodium atoms.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Put 2 before H₂O to balance hydrogen and oxygen.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> H₂SO₄ + 2NaOH → Na₂SO₄ + 2H₂O.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: Choosing the safe dilution method</strong></p>

  <p>A learner needs to dilute concentrated sulphuric acid. State
  the correct order of mixing and explain why.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Place the required amount of water
  in a suitable container.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Add the acid slowly to the water
  while stirring, using appropriate protective equipment.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Allow heat to disperse safely.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Add acid to water slowly, never water
  to concentrated acid, because dilution releases considerable heat.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Sulphuric Acid', 15),
  (907, 907, '
  <h2>Manufacture of Sulphuric Acid: The Contact Process</h2>

  <h3>1. Overview</h3>

  <p>Sulphuric acid is manufactured industrially by the
  <strong>Contact Process</strong>. The process converts a
  sulphur-containing raw material into sulphur dioxide, then
  sulphur trioxide, and finally sulphuric acid.</p>

  <p>The main stages are:</p>

  <ol>
    <li>Produce sulphur dioxide.</li>
    <li>Purify and dry the gases.</li>
    <li>Oxidise sulphur dioxide to sulphur trioxide.</li>
    <li>Absorb sulphur trioxide into concentrated sulphuric acid
    to form oleum.</li>
    <li>Dilute oleum carefully to obtain sulphuric acid of the
    required concentration.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>2. Stage 1: Production of sulphur dioxide</h3>

  <p>Sulphur can be burned in oxygen to form sulphur dioxide.</p>

  <p><strong>S(s) + O₂(g) → SO₂(g)</strong></p>

  <p>Sulphur dioxide may also be obtained by roasting certain
  sulphide ores in air. The gas produced must be suitable for
  subsequent catalytic processing.</p>

  <h3>3. Stage 2: Purification and drying</h3>

  <p>Impurities must be removed from the gas stream because they
  can interfere with the catalyst or damage the equipment.
  Moisture is also removed before the gas enters the conversion
  stage.</p>

  <h3>4. Stage 3: Conversion to sulphur trioxide</h3>

  <p>Sulphur dioxide reacts reversibly with oxygen:</p>

  <p><strong>2SO₂(g) + O₂(g) ⇌ 2SO₃(g)</strong></p>

  <p>Typical industrial conditions include:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>A vanadium(V) oxide catalyst, V₂O₅.</li>
    <li>A temperature of approximately 450°C.</li>
    <li>A suitable pressure, commonly around atmospheric pressure
    or only slightly above it.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>The reaction is exothermic. The chosen temperature balances
  a favourable equilibrium yield against a sufficiently fast
  reaction rate.</p>

  <p>The catalyst increases the rate of reaching equilibrium.
  It does not increase the equilibrium yield by itself.</p>

  <h3>5. Stage 4: Absorption of sulphur trioxide</h3>

  <p>Sulphur trioxide is not normally absorbed directly into
  water on an industrial scale because the reaction releases
  considerable heat and produces a fine acid mist.</p>

  <p>Instead, SO₃ is absorbed in concentrated sulphuric acid
  to form oleum. A simplified equation is:</p>

  <p><strong>SO₃ + H₂SO₄ → H₂S₂O₇</strong></p>

  <p>H₂S₂O₇ represents oleum in this simplified equation.</p>

  <h3>6. Stage 5: Formation of sulphuric acid</h3>

  <p>Oleum reacts with a controlled amount of water to produce
  sulphuric acid:</p>

  <p><strong>H₂S₂O₇ + H₂O → 2H₂SO₄</strong></p>

  <p>This is the industrial route to the final acid product.
  The amount of water is controlled to obtain the required
  concentration safely.</p>

  <h3>7. Overall process and its importance</h3>

  <p>The simplified overall chemical relationship can be written:</p>

  <p><strong>2SO₂ + O₂ + 2H₂O → 2H₂SO₄</strong></p>

  <p>This overall equation summarises the conversion of sulphur
  dioxide into sulphuric acid. It does not show the intermediate
  oleum stage used in the industrial process.</p>

  <p>Sulphuric acid is used in the manufacture of fertilisers,
  chemicals and other industrial products. Efficient conversion,
  gas purification and emission control are important parts of
  industrial operation.</p>

  <h3>Worked examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: Identifying the Contact Process stages</strong></p>

  <p>Arrange these transformations in the correct order:
  SO₃ formation, SO₂ production, oleum formation, and acid formation.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Produce SO₂ first.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Oxidise SO₂ to SO₃.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Absorb SO₃ into concentrated sulphuric acid
  to form oleum.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 4:</strong> React oleum with controlled water to
  form sulphuric acid.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> SO₂ production → SO₃ formation →
  oleum formation → sulphuric acid formation.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: Balancing the conversion stage</strong></p>

  <p>Balance SO₂ + O₂ ⇌ SO₃.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Put 2 before SO₂ and SO₃.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Count oxygen on the left: 4 + 2 = 6.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Count oxygen on the right: 6.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 2SO₂ + O₂ ⇌ 2SO₃.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: Explaining the catalyst</strong></p>

  <p>Explain why V₂O₅ is used during the oxidation of sulphur
  dioxide in the Contact Process.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the reaction as reversible.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Recall that a catalyst increases reaction rate.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Explain that it helps the system reach
  equilibrium faster without changing the equilibrium position.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> V₂O₅ speeds up the conversion of SO₂
  to SO₃, allowing equilibrium to be reached faster.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Manufacture of Sulphuric Acid', 15),
  (908, 908, '
  <h2>Uses of Sulphuric Acid</h2>

  <h3>1. Introduction</h3>

  <p>
    Sulphuric acid, H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub>, is an important industrial
    chemical. It is used in the manufacture of fertilisers, detergents,
    other chemicals and in lead-acid batteries.
  </p>

  <p>
    Its usefulness comes from its acidic properties and its ability to
    react with many substances, including metals, bases and metal oxides.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Manufacture of Fertilisers</h3>

  <p>
    Sulphuric acid is used to manufacture phosphate fertilisers and
    ammonium sulphate. These fertilisers supply essential nutrients
    required for plant growth.
  </p>

  <p>
    For example, sulphuric acid reacts with ammonia to form ammonium
    sulphate:
  </p>

  <p>
    H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub> + 2NH<sub>3</sub>
    → (NH<sub>4</sub>)<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub>
  </p>

  <h3>3. Manufacture of Chemicals</h3>

  <p>
    Sulphuric acid is used in making other acids, sulphate salts,
    detergents, dyes and many industrial chemicals. It can also act
    as a dehydrating agent in suitable chemical processes.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Lead-Acid Batteries</h3>

  <p>
    Dilute sulphuric acid acts as the electrolyte in a lead-acid battery.
    It allows ions to move between the battery''s electrodes during
    charging and discharging.
  </p>

  <p>
    Such batteries are commonly used in vehicles and backup power
    systems. The acid is corrosive and must be handled carefully.
  </p>

  <h3>5. Petroleum Refining and Industrial Processing</h3>

  <p>
    Sulphuric acid has been used in certain petroleum-refining processes
    and in the treatment or manufacture of industrial materials.
    The exact process depends on the product being made.
  </p>

  <h3>6. Preparation of Sulphate Salts</h3>

  <p>
    Sulphuric acid reacts with suitable bases and metal oxides to form
    sulphates and water.
  </p>

  <p>
    Example with copper(II) oxide:
  </p>

  <p>
    CuO + H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub>
    → CuSO<sub>4</sub> + H<sub>2</sub>O
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Examples</h3>

  <p><b>Example 1: Fertiliser manufacture</b></p>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Write an equation for the reaction between
  sulphuric acid and ammonia to form ammonium sulphate.</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Write the reactants and product:</p>
  <p>H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub> + NH<sub>3</sub>
  → (NH<sub>4</sub>)<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub></p>

  <p><b>Step 2:</b> The product contains two ammonium ions, so use
  two ammonia molecules.</p>

  <p><b>Balanced equation:</b></p>
  <p>
    H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub> + 2NH<sub>3</sub>
    → (NH<sub>4</sub>)<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub>
  </p>

  <p><b>Example 2: Preparing a sulphate salt</b></p>

  <p><b>Question:</b> What happens when copper(II) oxide reacts
  with dilute sulphuric acid?</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify the reactants: a metal oxide and an acid.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> An acid reacts with a metal oxide to form a salt
  and water.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> The salt formed is copper(II) sulphate.</p>

  <p><b>Equation:</b></p>
  <p>
    CuO + H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub>
    → CuSO<sub>4</sub> + H<sub>2</sub>O
  </p>

  <p><b>Example 3: Identifying the battery application</b></p>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Explain the role of dilute sulphuric acid
  in a lead-acid battery.</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify the type of substance used: an electrolyte.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> An electrolyte permits ions to move within the battery.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Dilute sulphuric acid acts as the electrolyte,
  allowing ionic movement during charging and discharging.</p>

  <h3>Safety</h3>

  <p>
    Sulphuric acid is corrosive and can cause severe burns. When diluting
    concentrated acid, add the acid slowly to water while stirring.
    Never pour water into concentrated acid because the heat produced
    may cause dangerous splashing.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Uses of Sulphuric Acid', 15),
  (909, 909, '
  <h2>Properties of Carbon</h2>

  <h3>1. Position and Electronic Structure</h3>

  <p>
    Carbon is a non-metallic element with the chemical symbol C and
    atomic number 6. A neutral carbon atom has six protons and six
    electrons.
  </p>

  <p>
    Its electron arrangement is 2,4. Carbon therefore has four electrons
    in its outer shell and commonly forms four covalent bonds.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Carbon Is a Non-Metal</h3>

  <p>
    Carbon occurs in different solid forms. Many forms of carbon do not
    show the typical metallic properties of metals. Its physical
    properties depend on its structure and allotrope.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Tetravalency</h3>

  <p>
    Carbon is tetravalent because one carbon atom can form four covalent
    bonds. These bonds involve sharing electrons with other atoms.
  </p>

  <p>
    For example, methane has the formula CH<sub>4</sub>. One carbon atom
    forms four single covalent bonds with four hydrogen atoms.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Catenation</h3>

  <p>
    Catenation is the ability of an element to form bonds with atoms of
    the same element, producing chains or rings. Carbon shows extensive
    catenation because carbon-carbon covalent bonds are strong.
  </p>

  <p>
    This property allows carbon to form straight chains, branched chains
    and rings. It is one reason why a very large number of carbon
    compounds exist.
  </p>

  <h3>5. Combustion of Carbon</h3>

  <p>
    In a plentiful supply of oxygen, carbon burns to form carbon dioxide:
  </p>

  <p>
    C + O<sub>2</sub> → CO<sub>2</sub>
  </p>

  <p>
    When oxygen is insufficient, incomplete combustion can produce
    carbon monoxide:
  </p>

  <p>
    2C + O<sub>2</sub> → 2CO
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Examples</h3>

  <p><b>Example 1: Electron arrangement</b></p>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Give the electron arrangement of carbon and state
  the number of electrons in its outer shell.</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Carbon has atomic number 6, so a neutral atom has
  six electrons.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> The first shell holds two electrons.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> The remaining four occupy the second shell.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> Electron arrangement 2,4; four outer-shell electrons.</p>

  <p><b>Example 2: Tetravalency</b></p>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Explain why carbon is described as tetravalent.</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Carbon has four electrons in its outer shell.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> It can share electrons with other atoms to form
  four covalent bonds.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Carbon is tetravalent because it commonly forms
  four covalent bonds.</p>

  <p><b>Example 3: Combustion equation</b></p>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Write the balanced equation for complete combustion
  of carbon.</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify the reactants: carbon and oxygen.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Complete combustion produces carbon dioxide.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Check that there is one carbon atom and two oxygen
  atoms on each side.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> C + O<sub>2</sub> → CO<sub>2</sub></p>

  <h3>Key Distinction</h3>

  <p>
    Tetravalency refers to carbon forming four covalent bonds.
    Catenation refers to carbon bonding to other carbon atoms to form
    chains or rings. These are related but different properties.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Properties of Carbon', 15),
  (910, 910, '
  <h2>Carbon Dioxide</h2>

  <h3>1. Formula and Preparation</h3>

  <p>
    Carbon dioxide has the chemical formula CO<sub>2</sub>. Each molecule
    contains one carbon atom and two oxygen atoms.
  </p>

  <p>
    In the laboratory, carbon dioxide can be prepared by reacting calcium
    carbonate, such as marble chips, with dilute hydrochloric acid.
  </p>

  <p>
    CaCO<sub>3</sub> + 2HCl
    → CaCl<sub>2</sub> + H<sub>2</sub>O + CO<sub>2</sub>
  </p>

  <p>
    The gas can be collected by downward delivery because it is denser
    than air. It is not normally collected over water when a dry sample
    is required, because some carbon dioxide dissolves in water.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Physical Properties</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Carbon dioxide is a colourless gas.</li>
    <li>It is denser than air.</li>
    <li>It dissolves slightly in water.</li>
    <li>It does not support ordinary combustion.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>3. Chemical Properties</h3>

  <p><b>Reaction with water:</b></p>

  <p>
    Carbon dioxide dissolves in water and forms a weak acidic solution:
  </p>

  <p>
    CO<sub>2</sub> + H<sub>2</sub>O
    ⇌ H<sub>2</sub>CO<sub>3</sub>
  </p>

  <p><b>Reaction with limewater:</b></p>

  <p>
    Carbon dioxide turns limewater milky because insoluble calcium
    carbonate forms:
  </p>

  <p>
    CO<sub>2</sub> + Ca(OH)<sub>2</sub>
    → CaCO<sub>3</sub> + H<sub>2</sub>O
  </p>

  <p>
    If excess carbon dioxide is passed through the mixture, the milkiness
    can disappear because soluble calcium hydrogencarbonate forms:
  </p>

  <p>
    CaCO<sub>3</sub> + CO<sub>2</sub> + H<sub>2</sub>O
    → Ca(HCO<sub>3</sub>)<sub>2</sub>
  </p>

  <h3>4. Reaction with Burning Substances</h3>

  <p>
    Carbon dioxide does not support ordinary combustion, so it is used
    in some fire extinguishers. It helps smother a fire by displacing
    oxygen around the burning material.
  </p>

  <p>
    However, it is not suitable for every type of fire, and specialised
    fire-safety guidance must be followed.
  </p>

  <h3>5. Environmental Importance</h3>

  <p>
    Carbon dioxide is produced during respiration, combustion and the
    decomposition of organic matter. Plants use it during photosynthesis.
  </p>

  <p>
    It is also a greenhouse gas. Increasing its concentration in the
    atmosphere contributes to global warming by trapping heat.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Examples</h3>

  <p><b>Example 1: Laboratory preparation</b></p>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Write the balanced equation for preparing carbon
  dioxide using calcium carbonate and dilute hydrochloric acid.</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Write the reactants: CaCO<sub>3</sub> and HCl.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> The products are calcium chloride, water and carbon
  dioxide.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Place 2 before HCl to balance hydrogen and chlorine.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b></p>
  <p>
    CaCO<sub>3</sub> + 2HCl
    → CaCl<sub>2</sub> + H<sub>2</sub>O + CO<sub>2</sub>
  </p>

  <p><b>Example 2: Limewater test</b></p>

  <p><b>Question:</b> A colourless gas turns limewater milky. Explain
  the observation when the gas is carbon dioxide.</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Carbon dioxide reacts with calcium hydroxide in limewater.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Insoluble calcium carbonate forms.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> The limewater turns milky because a white precipitate
  of calcium carbonate forms.</p>

  <p><b>Equation:</b></p>
  <p>
    CO<sub>2</sub> + Ca(OH)<sub>2</sub>
    → CaCO<sub>3</sub> + H<sub>2</sub>O
  </p>

  <p><b>Example 3: Excess carbon dioxide</b></p>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Why can limewater become clear again when excess
  carbon dioxide is passed through it?</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Initially, insoluble calcium carbonate forms and
  makes the solution milky.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Excess carbon dioxide reacts with the calcium
  carbonate in water.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Soluble calcium hydrogencarbonate forms.</p>

  <p><b>Equation:</b></p>
  <p>
    CaCO<sub>3</sub> + CO<sub>2</sub> + H<sub>2</sub>O
    → Ca(HCO<sub>3</sub>)<sub>2</sub>
  </p>

  <h3>Key Test</h3>

  <p>
    The standard test for carbon dioxide is to bubble the gas through
    limewater. A positive result is that the limewater turns milky.
    Do not identify the gas from its appearance alone.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Carbon Dioxide', 15),
  (911, 911, '
  <h2>Ammonia</h2>

  <h3>1. Formula and Composition</h3>

  <p>
    Ammonia is a compound of nitrogen and hydrogen with the chemical
    formula NH<sub>3</sub>. Each molecule contains one nitrogen atom
    bonded to three hydrogen atoms.
  </p>

  <p>
    Ammonia is a covalent compound. It is an important raw material
    in the manufacture of fertilisers, nitric acid and other nitrogen
    compounds.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Laboratory Preparation of Ammonia</h3>

  <p>
    In the laboratory, ammonia can be prepared by heating an ammonium
    salt, such as ammonium chloride, with a suitable alkali, such as
    calcium hydroxide.
  </p>

  <p><b>Word equation:</b></p>

  <p>
    Ammonium chloride + Calcium hydroxide
    → Calcium chloride + Water + Ammonia
  </p>

  <p><b>Balanced chemical equation:</b></p>

  <p>
    2NH<sub>4</sub>Cl + Ca(OH)<sub>2</sub>
    → CaCl<sub>2</sub> + 2H<sub>2</sub>O + 2NH<sub>3</sub>
  </p>

  <h3>3. Drying and Collection</h3>

  <p>
    Ammonia is dried using quicklime, calcium oxide (CaO). It cannot
    be dried using concentrated sulphuric acid because ammonia reacts
    with the acid. Anhydrous calcium chloride is also unsuitable
    because it absorbs ammonia.
  </p>

  <p>
    Ammonia is collected by downward displacement of air, also called
    upward delivery. It is less dense than air and extremely soluble
    in water, so it cannot be collected over water.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Physical Properties</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>It is a colourless gas.</li>
    <li>It has a sharp, pungent smell.</li>
    <li>It is less dense than air.</li>
    <li>It is highly soluble in water.</li>
    <li>It can be liquefied by cooling and applying pressure.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Ammonia gas must never be identified by deliberately smelling it
    directly. Laboratory gases should be handled using approved
    safety procedures and adequate ventilation.
  </p>

  <h3>5. Chemical Properties</h3>

  <p><b>A. Ammonia is alkaline in water</b></p>

  <p>
    When ammonia dissolves in water, a small proportion reacts with
    water to form ammonium ions and hydroxide ions. This makes the
    solution alkaline.
  </p>

  <p>
    NH<sub>3</sub> + H<sub>2</sub>O
    ⇌ NH<sub>4</sub><sup>+</sup> + OH<sup>−</sup>
  </p>

  <p>
    Ammonia turns damp red litmus paper blue. Dry ammonia does not
    produce this colour change because water is needed for the
    alkaline effect.
  </p>

  <p><b>B. Reaction with acids</b></p>

  <p>
    Ammonia reacts with hydrogen chloride gas to produce dense white
    fumes of ammonium chloride.
  </p>

  <p>
    NH<sub>3</sub> + HCl → NH<sub>4</sub>Cl
  </p>

  <p>
    Ammonia also reacts with nitric acid to form ammonium nitrate
    and with sulphuric acid to form ammonium sulphate.
  </p>

  <p>
    NH<sub>3</sub> + HNO<sub>3</sub> → NH<sub>4</sub>NO<sub>3</sub>
  </p>

  <p>
    2NH<sub>3</sub> + H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub>
    → (NH<sub>4</sub>)<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub>
  </p>

  <p><b>C. Catalytic oxidation</b></p>

  <p>
    Ammonia reacts with oxygen in the presence of a platinum catalyst
    at a high temperature to form nitrogen monoxide and water.
    This reaction is an important first stage in the manufacture
    of nitric acid.
  </p>

  <p>
    4NH<sub>3</sub> + 5O<sub>2</sub>
    → 4NO + 6H<sub>2</sub>O
  </p>

  <h3>6. Industrial Manufacture: The Haber Process</h3>

  <p>
    Industrial ammonia is manufactured by reacting nitrogen with
    hydrogen in the Haber process. Nitrogen is obtained mainly from
    air, while hydrogen is commonly produced from natural gas or
    other suitable feedstocks.
  </p>

  <p>
    N<sub>2</sub> + 3H<sub>2</sub>
    ⇌ 2NH<sub>3</sub>
  </p>

  <p>
    The process uses an iron catalyst, elevated temperature and high
    pressure. The reaction is reversible and exothermic.
  </p>

  <h3>7. Uses of Ammonia</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Manufacture of nitrogen fertilisers such as ammonium sulphate and ammonium nitrate.</li>
    <li>Manufacture of nitric acid.</li>
    <li>Use as a refrigerant in suitable industrial refrigeration systems.</li>
    <li>Manufacture of other chemicals.</li>
    <li>Production of certain cleaning products and industrial solutions.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Worked Examples</h3>

  <p><b>Example 1: Balancing the laboratory preparation equation</b></p>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Balance the equation for the preparation of
  ammonia from ammonium chloride and calcium hydroxide.</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Write the unbalanced equation:</p>

  <p>
    NH<sub>4</sub>Cl + Ca(OH)<sub>2</sub>
    → CaCl<sub>2</sub> + H<sub>2</sub>O + NH<sub>3</sub>
  </p>

  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Calcium chloride contains two chlorine atoms.
  Place 2 before NH<sub>4</sub>Cl.</p>

  <p><b>Step 3:</b> This gives two nitrogen atoms, so place 2 before
  NH<sub>3</sub>. Balance the hydrogen and oxygen atoms by placing
  2 before H<sub>2</sub>O.</p>

  <p><b>Balanced equation:</b></p>

  <p>
    2NH<sub>4</sub>Cl + Ca(OH)<sub>2</sub>
    → CaCl<sub>2</sub> + 2H<sub>2</sub>O + 2NH<sub>3</sub>
  </p>

  <p><b>Example 2: Identifying ammonia</b></p>

  <p><b>Question:</b> A gas turns damp red litmus paper blue. Explain
  the observation if the gas is ammonia.</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Ammonia dissolves in the moisture on the paper.</p>

  <p><b>Step 2:</b> It reacts reversibly with water to produce
  hydroxide ions.</p>

  <p><b>Step 3:</b> The hydroxide ions make the solution alkaline.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> The paper turns blue because dissolved ammonia
  produces hydroxide ions.</p>

  <p><b>Example 3: Formation of ammonium sulphate</b></p>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Write the balanced equation for the reaction
  between ammonia and sulphuric acid.</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify the reactants: NH<sub>3</sub> and
  H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub>.</p>

  <p><b>Step 2:</b> The salt formed is ammonium sulphate,
  (NH<sub>4</sub>)<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub>.</p>

  <p><b>Step 3:</b> The product contains two nitrogen atoms, so
  two ammonia molecules are needed.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b></p>

  <p>
    2NH<sub>3</sub> + H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub>
    → (NH<sub>4</sub>)<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub>
  </p>

  <h3>Key Distinctions</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Ammonia is NH<sub>3</sub>; the ammonium ion is NH<sub>4</sub><sup>+</sup>.</li>
    <li>Ammonia is a gas under ordinary laboratory conditions; aqueous ammonia is a solution containing dissolved ammonia.</li>
    <li>Quicklime is used to dry ammonia; concentrated sulphuric acid is unsuitable because it reacts with ammonia.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Ammonia', 15),
  (912, 912, '
  <h2>Nitrogen Compounds</h2>

  <h3>1. Introduction</h3>

  <p>
    Nitrogen forms many compounds because it can combine with hydrogen,
    oxygen and other elements. Important nitrogen compounds include
    ammonia, ammonium salts, nitrates, nitric acid and nitrogen oxides.
  </p>

  <p>
    Many nitrogen compounds are important in agriculture, industry
    and biological processes. Their properties depend on their
    chemical composition and structure.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Ammonium Ions and Ammonium Salts</h3>

  <p>
    The ammonium ion has the formula NH<sub>4</sub><sup>+</sup>.
    It is formed when ammonia accepts a hydrogen ion (H<sup>+</sup>).
  </p>

  <p>
    NH<sub>3</sub> + H<sup>+</sup> → NH<sub>4</sub><sup>+</sup>
  </p>

  <p>
    An ammonium salt contains ammonium ions and negative ions from
    an acid. Examples include:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>Ammonium chloride: NH<sub>4</sub>Cl.</li>
    <li>Ammonium sulphate: (NH<sub>4</sub>)<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub>.</li>
    <li>Ammonium nitrate: NH<sub>4</sub>NO<sub>3</sub>.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Ammonium salts can be prepared by reacting ammonia with acids.
    For example, ammonia reacts with nitric acid to form ammonium nitrate.
  </p>

  <p>
    NH<sub>3</sub> + HNO<sub>3</sub> → NH<sub>4</sub>NO<sub>3</sub>
  </p>

  <h3>3. Nitrates</h3>

  <p>
    A nitrate contains the nitrate ion, NO<sub>3</sub><sup>−</sup>.
    Nitrates are salts of nitric acid, HNO<sub>3</sub>.
  </p>

  <p>Examples include:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>Sodium nitrate: NaNO<sub>3</sub>.</li>
    <li>Potassium nitrate: KNO<sub>3</sub>.</li>
    <li>Ammonium nitrate: NH<sub>4</sub>NO<sub>3</sub>.</li>
    <li>Calcium nitrate: Ca(NO<sub>3</sub>)<sub>2</sub>.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Many nitrates are soluble in water. Nitrate salts are important
    sources of nitrogen in fertilisers. Plants absorb nitrogen mainly
    as nitrate ions or ammonium ions, depending on soil conditions.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Nitric Acid</h3>

  <p>
    Nitric acid has the formula HNO<sub>3</sub>. It is a strong acid
    in aqueous solution and reacts with suitable bases and carbonates
    to form nitrate salts.
  </p>

  <p><b>Reaction with sodium hydroxide:</b></p>

  <p>
    HNO<sub>3</sub> + NaOH → NaNO<sub>3</sub> + H<sub>2</sub>O
  </p>

  <p><b>Reaction with calcium carbonate:</b></p>

  <p>
    CaCO<sub>3</sub> + 2HNO<sub>3</sub>
    → Ca(NO<sub>3</sub>)<sub>2</sub> + H<sub>2</sub>O + CO<sub>2</sub>
  </p>

  <p>
    Nitric acid is used in the manufacture of fertilisers, dyes and
    other chemicals. It is corrosive and can cause serious burns.
  </p>

  <h3>5. Nitrogen Oxides</h3>

  <p>
    Nitrogen forms several oxides, including nitrogen monoxide,
    NO, and nitrogen dioxide, NO<sub>2</sub>.
  </p>

  <p>
    Nitrogen monoxide is a colourless gas that reacts with oxygen
    in air to form brown nitrogen dioxide:
  </p>

  <p>
    2NO + O<sub>2</sub> → 2NO<sub>2</sub>
  </p>

  <p>
    Nitrogen dioxide is a toxic, reddish-brown gas. Nitrogen oxides
    can be produced by high-temperature combustion in engines and
    industrial processes. They contribute to air pollution and the
    formation of acid deposition.
  </p>

  <h3>6. Tests for Ammonium Ions</h3>

  <p>
    To test for ammonium ions, add aqueous sodium hydroxide to the
    sample and warm it gently. If ammonium ions are present, ammonia
    gas is released.
  </p>

  <p>
    NH<sub>4</sub><sup>+</sup> + OH<sup>−</sup>
    → NH<sub>3</sub> + H<sub>2</sub>O
  </p>

  <p>
    The released ammonia turns damp red litmus paper blue. This test
    must be carried out with appropriate laboratory precautions.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Examples</h3>

  <p><b>Example 1: Writing a nitrate formula</b></p>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Write the formula of calcium nitrate.</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Calcium forms Ca<sup>2+</sup>.</p>

  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Nitrate is NO<sub>3</sub><sup>−</sup>.</p>

  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Two nitrate ions are needed to balance one
  calcium ion''s +2 charge.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> Ca(NO<sub>3</sub>)<sub>2</sub>.</p>

  <p><b>Example 2: Preparing a nitrate</b></p>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Write the balanced equation for the reaction
  between nitric acid and sodium hydroxide.</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> The reactants are HNO<sub>3</sub> and NaOH.</p>

  <p><b>Step 2:</b> The salt formed is sodium nitrate.</p>

  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Acid-base neutralisation also produces water.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b></p>

  <p>
    HNO<sub>3</sub> + NaOH → NaNO<sub>3</sub> + H<sub>2</sub>O
  </p>

  <p><b>Example 3: Testing an ammonium salt</b></p>

  <p><b>Question:</b> A salt is warmed with aqueous sodium hydroxide.
  The gas produced turns damp red litmus paper blue. What ion may
  be present in the salt?</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Sodium hydroxide provides hydroxide ions.</p>

  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Hydroxide ions react with ammonium ions to release ammonia.</p>

  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Ammonia produces an alkaline solution in moisture.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> The ammonium ion, NH<sub>4</sub><sup>+</sup>,
  may be present.</p>

  <h3>Key Distinctions</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Ammonium ion: NH<sub>4</sub><sup>+</sup>.</li>
    <li>Nitrate ion: NO<sub>3</sub><sup>−</sup>.</li>
    <li>Ammonium nitrate: NH<sub>4</sub>NO<sub>3</sub>, a salt containing both ions.</li>
    <li>Nitric acid: HNO<sub>3</sub>, an acid rather than a nitrate salt.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Nitrogen Compounds', 15),
  (913, 913, '
  <h2>Nitrogen Cycle</h2>

  <h3>1. Why Nitrogen Must Be Recycled</h3>

  <p>
    Nitrogen is essential for living organisms. Plants and animals
    need nitrogen to make amino acids, proteins and nucleic acids
    such as DNA.
  </p>

  <p>
    Although the atmosphere contains approximately 78% nitrogen gas,
    N<sub>2</sub>, most plants cannot use atmospheric nitrogen directly.
    The strong triple bond in nitrogen molecules makes the gas relatively
    unreactive under ordinary conditions.
  </p>

  <p>
    The nitrogen cycle describes how nitrogen moves between the
    atmosphere, soil, plants, animals and decomposing organic matter.
    Microorganisms carry out many of its essential transformations.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Nitrogen Fixation</h3>

  <p>
    Nitrogen fixation converts atmospheric nitrogen gas into compounds
    that can enter biological and soil processes.
  </p>

  <p><b>A. Biological fixation</b></p>

  <p>
    Certain bacteria convert atmospheric nitrogen into ammonia or
    related nitrogen compounds. Some live freely in the soil, while
    others live in root nodules of leguminous plants such as beans,
    peas and clover.
  </p>

  <p>
    The plants provide the bacteria with carbohydrates and a suitable
    environment. In return, the bacteria supply fixed nitrogen that
    supports plant growth.
  </p>

  <p><b>B. Atmospheric fixation</b></p>

  <p>
    Lightning provides enough energy for nitrogen and oxygen in the
    atmosphere to react and form nitrogen oxides. These eventually
    produce nitrates that rain can carry into the soil.
  </p>

  <p><b>C. Industrial fixation</b></p>

  <p>
    The Haber process converts atmospheric nitrogen and hydrogen into
    ammonia. Industrially produced ammonia is used to make nitrogen
    fertilisers.
  </p>

  <p>
    N<sub>2</sub> + 3H<sub>2</sub>
    ⇌ 2NH<sub>3</sub>
  </p>

  <h3>3. Nitrification</h3>

  <p>
    Nitrification is the microbial conversion of ammonium compounds
    into nitrites and then nitrates under suitable aerobic conditions.
  </p>

  <p>
    In simplified form:
  </p>

  <p>
    Ammonium compounds → Nitrites (NO<sub>2</sub><sup>−</sup>)
    → Nitrates (NO<sub>3</sub><sup>−</sup>)
  </p>

  <p>
    Different groups of nitrifying microorganisms carry out the stages.
    Nitrates are readily absorbed by many plants through their roots.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Assimilation</h3>

  <p>
    Assimilation occurs when plants absorb nitrate or ammonium ions
    from the soil and use the nitrogen to manufacture amino acids,
    proteins and other nitrogen-containing substances.
  </p>

  <p>
    Animals obtain nitrogen by eating plants or other animals.
    Nitrogen therefore passes through food chains from producers
    to consumers.
  </p>

  <h3>5. Ammonification</h3>

  <p>
    Ammonification is the breakdown of nitrogen-containing organic
    matter into ammonia or ammonium compounds by decomposers such
    as certain bacteria and fungi.
  </p>

  <p>
    The process occurs when organisms die or release nitrogen-containing
    waste. Decomposers break down proteins and other organic compounds,
    returning nitrogen to the soil.
  </p>

  <h3>6. Denitrification</h3>

  <p>
    Denitrification is the conversion of nitrates into gaseous nitrogen
    compounds, including nitrogen gas, by certain microorganisms.
    It commonly occurs in oxygen-poor or waterlogged soils.
  </p>

  <p>
    Denitrification returns nitrogen to the atmosphere and completes
    an important part of the cycle.
  </p>

  <p>
    Nitrification generally requires oxygen, whereas denitrification
    is favoured by low-oxygen conditions. They are different processes
    with different effects on soil nitrogen.
  </p>

  <h3>7. The Main Stages in Sequence</h3>

  <ol>
    <li><b>Fixation:</b> Atmospheric nitrogen is converted into biologically useful nitrogen compounds.</li>
    <li><b>Nitrification:</b> Ammonium compounds are converted into nitrites and nitrates.</li>
    <li><b>Assimilation:</b> Plants absorb nitrogen compounds; animals obtain nitrogen through feeding.</li>
    <li><b>Ammonification:</b> Decomposers return nitrogen from organic matter to ammonia or ammonium compounds.</li>
    <li><b>Denitrification:</b> Nitrates are converted into gaseous nitrogen compounds and returned to the atmosphere.</li>
  </ol>

  <p>
    These processes interact rather than occurring as one simple,
    uninterrupted sequence. For example, ammonium compounds released
    during decomposition may be nitrified, absorbed directly by
    plants or undergo other soil transformations.
  </p>

  <h3>8. Importance of the Nitrogen Cycle</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Recycles nitrogen between the atmosphere, soil and living organisms.</li>
    <li>Maintains a supply of usable nitrogen for plant growth.</li>
    <li>Supports the formation of proteins and nucleic acids.</li>
    <li>Connects soil microorganisms with plants and animals in ecosystems.</li>
    <li>Helps explain why nitrogen availability affects agricultural productivity.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Worked Examples</h3>

  <p><b>Example 1: Identifying a nitrogen-cycle process</b></p>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Soil bacteria convert ammonium compounds into
  nitrates. Name the process.</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify the starting substance: ammonium compounds.</p>

  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Identify the product: nitrate ions.</p>

  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Match the conversion to the correct process.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> Nitrification.</p>

  <p><b>Example 2: Nitrogen entering a food chain</b></p>

  <p><b>Question:</b> A bean plant absorbs nitrate ions and a cow eats
  the plant. Explain how nitrogen moves between them.</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> The plant absorbs nitrate ions from the soil.</p>

  <p><b>Step 2:</b> The plant uses the nitrogen to make amino acids
  and proteins.</p>

  <p><b>Step 3:</b> The cow obtains nitrogen by eating the plant''s
  nitrogen-containing tissues.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> Nitrogen is assimilated by the plant and then
  transferred to the cow through feeding.</p>

  <p><b>Example 3: Explaining nitrogen loss from soil</b></p>

  <p><b>Question:</b> A waterlogged soil loses nitrate nitrogen as
  nitrogen gas. Identify the process and explain why it matters.</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify the starting substance: nitrate ions.</p>

  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Identify the product: gaseous nitrogen compounds.</p>

  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Link the conversion to the soil conditions.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> Denitrification. It returns nitrogen to the
  atmosphere and can reduce the amount of nitrate available to plants.</p>

  <h3>Key Distinctions</h3>

  <ul>
    <li><b>Fixation:</b> Makes atmospheric nitrogen available in compounds.</li>
    <li><b>Nitrification:</b> Converts ammonium compounds into nitrites and nitrates.</li>
    <li><b>Assimilation:</b> Incorporates nitrogen into living organisms.</li>
    <li><b>Ammonification:</b> Returns organic nitrogen to ammonia or ammonium compounds.</li>
    <li><b>Denitrification:</b> Returns nitrogen from nitrates to the atmosphere as gaseous compounds.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Nitrogen Cycle', 15),
  (914, 914, '<h2>Composition of Air</h2>

  <p>Air is a mixture of gases. The major components of dry air are:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>Nitrogen — about 78%</li>
    <li>Oxygen — about 21%</li>
    <li>Argon and other noble gases — about 0.9%</li>
    <li>Carbon dioxide — about 0.04%</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Air also contains variable amounts of water vapour and small quantities of other gases.</p>

  <h3>Important point</h3>
  <p>Air is a <strong>mixture</strong>, not a compound, because its components are not chemically bonded and can be separated by physical methods.</p>

  <h3>Example</h3>
  <p>In 100 dm³ of dry air, approximately 78 dm³ is nitrogen and 21 dm³ is oxygen.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Composition of Air', 15),
  (915, 915, '<h2>Properties of Oxygen</h2>

  <p>Oxygen is a colourless, odourless and tasteless gas at room temperature.</p>

  <ul>
    <li>It is slightly soluble in water.</li>
    <li>It is slightly denser than air.</li>
    <li>It does not burn itself.</li>
    <li>It supports combustion.</li>
    <li>It is essential for respiration.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Test for oxygen</h3>
  <p>A glowing splint is inserted into the gas. If the splint relights, oxygen is present.</p>

  <h3>Combustion</h3>
  <p>Carbon burns in oxygen:</p>

  <p><strong>C + O₂ → CO₂</strong></p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Properties of Oxygen', 15),
  (916, 916, '<h2>Uses of Oxygen</h2>

  <p>Oxygen is important because it supports combustion and respiration.</p>

  <h3>Main uses</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Respiration in hospitals.</li>
    <li>Welding and cutting metals.</li>
    <li>Steel manufacture.</li>
    <li>Supporting combustion in industrial processes.</li>
    <li>Life-support systems in aircraft and spacecraft.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Medical oxygen</h3>
  <p>Patients who cannot obtain enough oxygen from normal air may be supplied with oxygen.</p>

  <h3>Welding</h3>
  <p>Oxygen is used with suitable fuel gases to produce a very hot flame for cutting and welding metals.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Uses of Oxygen', 15),
  (917, 917, '<h2>Properties of Water</h2>

  <p>Pure water is a colourless, odourless and tasteless liquid at room temperature.</p>

  <ul>
    <li>It freezes at 0°C under standard atmospheric pressure.</li>
    <li>It boils at 100°C under standard atmospheric pressure.</li>
    <li>It is a good solvent for many substances.</li>
    <li>It is essential for living organisms.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Water as a compound</h3>
  <p>Water is a compound because hydrogen and oxygen are chemically bonded in a fixed ratio.</p>

  <p><strong>Formula: H₂O</strong></p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Properties of Water', 15),
  (918, 918, '<h2>Water as a Solvent</h2>

  <p>A <strong>solvent</strong> is a substance that dissolves a solute to form a solution.</p>

  <p>Water dissolves many ionic and polar substances and is therefore often called a good solvent.</p>

  <h3>Examples</h3>

  <p>When sodium chloride dissolves in water:</p>

  <p><strong>NaCl(s) → Na⁺(aq) + Cl⁻(aq)</strong></p>

  <p>Water is important in living organisms because many substances are transported in aqueous solution.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Water as a Solvent', 15),
  (919, 919, '<h2>Hard Water</h2>

  <p>Hard water contains dissolved calcium and magnesium ions.</p>

  <p>These ions react with soap to form an insoluble substance called <strong>scum</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Effects of hard water</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Soap does not lather easily.</li>
    <li>Scum is formed.</li>
    <li>More soap is required for washing.</li>
    <li>Some types of hard water can form scale in pipes and boilers.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Important distinction</h3>
  <p>Hard water is not necessarily unsafe to drink. The main problem considered here is its behaviour with soap and heating systems.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Hard Water', 15),
  (920, 920, '<h2>Soft Water</h2>

  <p>Soft water contains very small amounts of calcium and magnesium ions compared with hard water.</p>

  <h3>Behaviour with soap</h3>

  <p>Soft water reacts less with soap, so soap produces lather readily.</p>

  <h3>Comparison</h3>

  <p><strong>Soft water → easy lathering → little or no scum.</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Hard water → poor lathering → scum formation.</strong></p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Soft Water', 15),
  (921, 921, '<h2>Water Purification</h2>

  <p>Water purification removes unwanted substances and harmful microorganisms so that water becomes suitable for its intended use.</p>

  <h3>Common purification processes</h3>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Filtration:</strong> removes insoluble solid particles.</li>
    <li><strong>Boiling:</strong> kills many microorganisms.</li>
    <li><strong>Chlorination:</strong> uses chlorine to disinfect water.</li>
    <li><strong>Distillation:</strong> separates water from many dissolved substances by evaporation followed by condensation.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>The purification method depends on the type of impurity present.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Water Purification', 15),
  (922, 922, '<h2>Carbon Monoxide as an Air Pollutant</h2>

  <p>Carbon monoxide, CO, is a poisonous gas produced mainly by incomplete combustion of carbon-containing fuels.</p>

  <p>For example:</p>

  <p><strong>2C + O₂ → 2CO</strong></p>

  <h3>Why CO is dangerous</h3>

  <p>Carbon monoxide combines strongly with haemoglobin in blood. This reduces the ability of blood to transport oxygen around the body.</p>

  <h3>Sources</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Incomplete combustion in vehicle engines.</li>
    <li>Burning charcoal in poorly ventilated rooms.</li>
    <li>Faulty fuel-burning appliances.</li>
  </ul>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Carbon Monoxide as an Air Pollutant', 15),
  (923, 923, '<h2>Sulphur Dioxide as an Air Pollutant</h2>

  <p>Sulphur dioxide, SO₂, is produced when sulphur-containing fuels are burned.</p>

  <p>A simplified equation is:</p>

  <p><strong>S + O₂ → SO₂</strong></p>

  <h3>Effects</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Irritates the respiratory system.</li>
    <li>Contributes to acid rain.</li>
    <li>Can damage vegetation.</li>
    <li>Can contribute to corrosion of materials.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>SO₂ can react with water in the atmosphere to form acidic substances.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Sulphur Dioxide as an Air Pollutant', 15),
  (924, 924, '<h2>Nitrogen Oxides as Air Pollutants</h2>

  <p>Nitrogen oxides, commonly represented as NOₓ, include gases such as nitrogen monoxide, NO, and nitrogen dioxide, NO₂.</p>

  <p>They can form at high temperatures, especially in vehicle engines and industrial combustion processes.</p>

  <h3>Effects</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Contribute to acid rain.</li>
    <li>Can irritate the respiratory system.</li>
    <li>Contribute to photochemical smog.</li>
    <li>Can damage vegetation.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Nitrogen dioxide is a reddish-brown gas with a characteristic irritating smell.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Nitrogen Oxides as Air Pollutants', 15),
  (925, 925, '<h2>Particulate Matter</h2>

  <p>Particulate matter consists of very small solid particles or liquid droplets suspended in air.</p>

  <h3>Sources</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Vehicle exhaust.</li>
    <li>Industrial emissions.</li>
    <li>Burning biomass and waste.</li>
    <li>Dust from roads and construction.</li>
    <li>Smoke from incomplete combustion.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Effects</h3>

  <p>Very small particles can enter the respiratory system and cause or worsen respiratory problems.</p>

  <p>Reducing smoke, dust and uncontrolled combustion reduces particulate pollution.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Particulate Matter', 15),
  (926, 926, '<h2>Acid Rain</h2>

  <p>Acid rain is precipitation that is more acidic than normal because acidic substances formed in the atmosphere dissolve in rainwater.</p>

  <h3>Main gases involved</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Sulphur dioxide, SO₂</li>
    <li>Nitrogen oxides, NOₓ</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Effects of acid rain</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Damages vegetation.</li>
    <li>Acidifies lakes and rivers.</li>
    <li>Can harm aquatic organisms.</li>
    <li>Corrodes metals.</li>
    <li>Can damage limestone and marble buildings.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Control</h3>
  <p>Reducing emissions of sulphur dioxide and nitrogen oxides reduces acid rain.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Acid Rain', 15),
  (927, 927, '<h2>Greenhouse Effect</h2>

  <p>The greenhouse effect is the warming of Earth''s lower atmosphere and surface caused by gases that absorb and re-emit infrared radiation.</p>

  <h3>Greenhouse gases</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Carbon dioxide, CO₂</li>
    <li>Methane, CH₄</li>
    <li>Nitrous oxide, N₂O</li>
    <li>Water vapour</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>How it works</h3>

  <p>Earth receives energy from the Sun. The Earth''s surface emits infrared radiation. Greenhouse gases absorb some of this radiation and re-emit it, reducing the rate at which heat escapes to space.</p>

  <h3>Important distinction</h3>

  <p>The natural greenhouse effect is necessary for life. The concern is the increase in greenhouse gases caused by human activities, which enhances warming.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Greenhouse Effect', 15),
  (928, 928, '<h2>Pollution Control</h2>

  <p>Pollution control involves reducing the release of harmful substances into the environment and reducing their effects.</p>

  <h3>Air pollution control</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Use cleaner fuels.</li>
    <li>Maintain vehicle engines properly.</li>
    <li>Use emission-control systems.</li>
    <li>Reduce open burning of waste.</li>
    <li>Use renewable energy where practical.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Water pollution control</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Treat sewage before discharge.</li>
    <li>Treat industrial wastewater.</li>
    <li>Prevent harmful chemicals from entering water bodies.</li>
    <li>Control excessive use of fertilisers and pesticides.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Sustainable use of resources</h3>

  <p>Sustainable use means using natural resources in ways that meet present needs while reducing unnecessary damage and preserving resources for the future.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Pollution Control', 15),
  (929, 929, '<h2>Test for Ammonium Ions</h2>

  <p>Ammonium ions are represented by <strong>NH₄⁺</strong>.</p>

  <p>They can be identified by adding a suitable alkali, such as sodium hydroxide solution, and warming the mixture.</p>

  <h3>Test</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Add sodium hydroxide solution to the sample.</li>
    <li>Warm the mixture gently.</li>
    <li>Test any gas produced with damp red litmus paper.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Positive result</h3>
  <p>A pungent-smelling gas is produced which turns damp red litmus paper blue.</p>

  <p>The gas is <strong>ammonia, NH₃</strong>.</p>

  <p><strong>NH₄⁺ + OH⁻ → NH₃ + H₂O</strong></p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Test for Ammonium Ions', 15),
  (930, 930, '<h2>Test for Copper(II) Ions</h2>

  <p>Copper(II) ions are represented by <strong>Cu²⁺</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Test using sodium hydroxide</h3>

  <p>Add sodium hydroxide solution to the sample.</p>

  <h3>Positive result</h3>

  <p>A <strong>blue precipitate</strong> of copper(II) hydroxide forms.</p>

  <p><strong>Cu²⁺ + 2OH⁻ → Cu(OH)₂(s)</strong></p>

  <p>The precipitate is insoluble in excess sodium hydroxide under the usual school laboratory test.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Test for Copper(II) Ions', 15),
  (931, 931, '<h2>Test for Iron(II) Ions</h2>

  <p>Iron(II) ions are represented by <strong>Fe²⁺</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Test</h3>

  <p>Add sodium hydroxide solution to the sample.</p>

  <h3>Positive result</h3>

  <p>A <strong>green precipitate</strong> of iron(II) hydroxide forms.</p>

  <p><strong>Fe²⁺ + 2OH⁻ → Fe(OH)₂(s)</strong></p>

  <p>The precipitate may change colour on exposure to air because iron(II) compounds can be oxidised.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Test for Iron(II) Ions', 15),
  (932, 932, '<h2>Test for Iron(III) Ions</h2>

  <p>Iron(III) ions are represented by <strong>Fe³⁺</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Test</h3>

  <p>Add sodium hydroxide solution to the sample.</p>

  <h3>Positive result</h3>

  <p>A <strong>red-brown precipitate</strong> of iron(III) hydroxide forms.</p>

  <p><strong>Fe³⁺ + 3OH⁻ → Fe(OH)₃(s)</strong></p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Test for Iron(III) Ions', 15),
  (933, 933, '<h2>Test for Chloride Ions</h2>

  <p>Chloride ions are represented by <strong>Cl⁻</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Test</h3>

  <ol>
    <li>Acidify the sample with dilute nitric acid.</li>
    <li>Add aqueous silver nitrate.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Positive result</h3>

  <p>A <strong>white precipitate</strong> of silver chloride forms.</p>

  <p><strong>Ag⁺ + Cl⁻ → AgCl(s)</strong></p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Test for Chloride Ions', 15),
  (934, 934, '<h2>Test for Sulphate Ions</h2>

  <p>Sulphate ions are represented by <strong>SO₄²⁻</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Test</h3>

  <ol>
    <li>Acidify the sample using dilute hydrochloric acid.</li>
    <li>Add barium nitrate solution or another suitable soluble barium salt.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Positive result</h3>

  <p>A <strong>white precipitate</strong> of barium sulphate forms.</p>

  <p><strong>Ba²⁺ + SO₄²⁻ → BaSO₄(s)</strong></p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Test for Sulphate Ions', 15),
  (935, 935, '<h2>Test for Carbonate Ions</h2>

  <p>Carbonate ions are represented by <strong>CO₃²⁻</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Test</h3>

  <p>Add a dilute acid to the sample.</p>

  <h3>Positive result</h3>

  <p>Effervescence occurs because carbon dioxide gas is produced.</p>

  <p>The gas can be passed through limewater.</p>

  <p>Limewater turns <strong>milky</strong> if carbon dioxide is present.</p>

  <p><strong>CO₃²⁻ + 2H⁺ → CO₂ + H₂O</strong></p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Test for Carbonate Ions', 15),
  (936, 936, '<h2>Test for Nitrate Ions</h2>

  <p>Nitrate ions are represented by <strong>NO₃⁻</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Test</h3>

  <p>A common school laboratory test involves adding sodium hydroxide and aluminium foil, followed by gentle warming.</p>

  <p>If nitrate ions are present, ammonia gas may be produced.</p>

  <h3>Positive result</h3>

  <p>The gas turns damp red litmus paper blue.</p>

  <p>The ammonia produced indicates nitrate ions in the original sample.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Test for Nitrate Ions', 15),
  (937, 937, '<h2>Flame Test for Sodium Ions</h2>

  <p>Sodium ions are represented by <strong>Na⁺</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Procedure</h3>

  <ol>
    <li>Clean a suitable wire loop.</li>
    <li>Place a small amount of the sample on the loop.</li>
    <li>Place it in a non-luminous flame.</li>
    <li>Observe the flame colour.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Positive result</h3>

  <p>Sodium compounds produce a <strong>yellow flame</strong>.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Flame Test for Sodium Ions', 15),
  (938, 938, '<h2>Flame Test for Potassium Ions</h2>

  <p>Potassium ions are represented by <strong>K⁺</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Flame colour</h3>

  <p>Potassium compounds produce a <strong>lilac flame</strong>.</p>

  <p>A sodium impurity can produce a strong yellow colour that may mask the lilac colour, so careful observation is important.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Flame Test for Potassium Ions', 15),
  (939, 939, '<h2>Flame Test for Calcium Ions</h2>

  <p>Calcium ions are represented by <strong>Ca²⁺</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Flame colour</h3>

  <p>Calcium compounds produce a <strong>brick-red or orange-red flame</strong>.</p>

  <p>The exact appearance can depend on the conditions and sample purity.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Flame Test for Calcium Ions', 15),
  (940, 940, '<h2>Flame Test for Copper(II) Ions</h2>

  <p>Copper(II) ions are represented by <strong>Cu²⁺</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Flame colour</h3>

  <p>Copper compounds can produce a <strong>blue-green or green flame</strong>.</p>

  <p>This provides another qualitative observation that can support identification of copper compounds.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Flame Test for Copper(II) Ions', 15),
  (941, 941, '<h2>Test for Hydrogen</h2>

  <p>Hydrogen gas is represented by <strong>H₂</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Test</h3>

  <p>Collect a sample of the gas and bring a lighted splint close to the gas.</p>

  <h3>Positive result</h3>

  <p>Hydrogen burns with a characteristic <strong>squeaky pop</strong>.</p>

  <p>The reaction is:</p>

  <p><strong>2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O</strong></p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Test for Hydrogen', 15),
  (942, 942, '<h2>Test for Oxygen</h2>

  <p>Oxygen gas is represented by <strong>O₂</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Test</h3>

  <p>Insert a glowing splint into the gas.</p>

  <h3>Positive result</h3>

  <p>The glowing splint <strong>relights</strong>.</p>

  <p>Oxygen supports combustion but does not itself burn.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Test for Oxygen', 15),
  (943, 943, '<h2>Test for Carbon Dioxide</h2>

  <p>Carbon dioxide is represented by <strong>CO₂</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Test</h3>

  <p>Bubble the gas through limewater.</p>

  <h3>Positive result</h3>

  <p>Limewater turns <strong>milky</strong>.</p>

  <p>The reaction is:</p>

  <p><strong>CO₂ + Ca(OH)₂ → CaCO₃ + H₂O</strong></p>

  <p>The calcium carbonate formed is insoluble and causes the milky appearance.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Test for Carbon Dioxide', 15),
  (944, 944, '<h2>Test for Ammonia</h2>

  <p>Ammonia is represented by <strong>NH₃</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Properties useful for identification</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>It is a colourless gas.</li>
    <li>It has a pungent smell.</li>
    <li>It is alkaline in water.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Test</h3>

  <p>Expose damp red litmus paper to the gas.</p>

  <h3>Positive result</h3>

  <p>Damp red litmus paper turns <strong>blue</strong>.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Test for Ammonia', 15),
  (945, 945, '<h2>Test for Chlorine</h2>

  <p>Chlorine gas is represented by <strong>Cl₂</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Properties useful for identification</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Chlorine is a greenish-yellow gas.</li>
    <li>It has a choking smell.</li>
    <li>It is toxic.</li>
    <li>It has bleaching properties when moisture is present.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Test</h3>

  <p>Expose damp blue litmus paper to the gas.</p>

  <h3>Positive result</h3>

  <p>The blue litmus paper is first turned red and then <strong>bleached</strong>.</p>

  <p>The bleaching occurs because chlorine reacts with water to form substances that can oxidise coloured materials.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Test for Chlorine', 15),
  (946, 946, '
  <h2>Reversible Reactions</h2>

  <h3>1. What Is a Chemical Reaction?</h3>

  <p>
    A <strong>chemical reaction</strong> is a process in which one or more
    substances change into different substances. The starting substances
    are called <strong>reactants</strong>, while the substances formed are
    called <strong>products</strong>.
  </p>

  <p>
    For example, when hydrogen reacts with oxygen, water is formed:
  </p>

  <pre>2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O</pre>

  <p>
    The arrow points from the reactants towards the products. In this
    representation, the reaction is written in one direction: hydrogen
    and oxygen form water.
  </p>

  <p>
    However, not every chemical reaction is restricted to proceeding
    in only one direction under the relevant conditions. Some reactions
    can proceed in both directions.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Meaning of a Reversible Reaction</h3>

  <p>
    A <strong>reversible reaction</strong> is a chemical reaction in which
    the products can react to reform the original reactants under suitable
    conditions.
  </p>

  <p>
    This means that the chemical change can proceed in two directions:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Forward direction:</strong> reactants form products.</li>
    <li><strong>Reverse direction:</strong> products reform reactants.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    A reversible reaction is represented by a double arrow:
  </p>

  <pre>A + B ⇌ C + D</pre>

  <p>
    The symbol <strong>⇌</strong> indicates that the reaction can proceed
    from left to right and from right to left. It does not mean that both
    directions must occur at equal rates at every moment.
  </p>

  <p>
    The substances on the left and right represent the two sides of the
    same reversible chemical change. The actual direction and extent of
    reaction depend on the substances present and the conditions.
  </p>

  <h3>3. How Can a Reaction Be Reversible?</h3>

  <p>
    In a reversible reaction, the products formed in one direction can
    undergo a chemical change that produces the original reactants.
    Therefore, the original chemical change is not necessarily permanent
    under every possible set of conditions.
  </p>

  <p>
    Some reversible changes are demonstrated by changing temperature.
    Other reversible reactions occur in a mixture containing both
    reactants and products. Reversibility is a property of the chemical
    process, not simply a matter of whether a visible change can be undone
    physically.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Recognising Reversible Reactions</h3>

  <p>
    Look for evidence that the products can chemically react to regenerate
    the original reactants. A double arrow in a correctly written chemical
    equation is the standard symbol used to represent a reversible reaction.
  </p>

  <p>
    Do not assume that every reaction is reversible merely because its
    products can be changed into something else through a different
    chemical reaction. The reverse direction must regenerate the original
    reactants.
  </p>

  <h3>WORKED EXAMPLE 1 — Understanding the Double Arrow</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> What does the equation below tell us?</p>

  <pre>A + B ⇌ C + D</pre>

  <p><strong>Step 1: Identify the left-hand side.</strong></p>

  <p>
    A and B are the reactants for the left-to-right direction. They
    chemically combine or react to form C and D.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2: Interpret the arrow.</strong></p>

  <p>
    The double arrow indicates that the chemical change can also proceed
    from right to left.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3: Identify the reverse change.</strong></p>

  <p>
    C and D can react to reform A and B under suitable conditions.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Final answer:</strong> The equation represents a reversible
  reaction because the chemical change can proceed in both directions.</p>

  <h3>WORKED EXAMPLE 2 — Recognising Reversibility in a Chemical Change</h3>

  <p>
    <strong>Question:</strong> Blue hydrated copper(II) sulfate crystals
    can be heated to form white anhydrous copper(II) sulfate. When water
    is added to the anhydrous compound under suitable conditions, the blue
    hydrated compound can form again. Explain why this demonstrates a
    reversible chemical change.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1: Identify the forward chemical change.</strong></p>

  <p>
    Heating removes water of crystallisation from hydrated copper(II)
    sulfate, producing anhydrous copper(II) sulfate.
  </p>

  <pre>CuSO₄·5H₂O ⇌ CuSO₄ + 5H₂O</pre>

  <p><strong>Step 2: Identify the reverse chemical change.</strong></p>

  <p>
    The anhydrous copper(II) sulfate can combine with water to reform
    hydrated copper(II) sulfate.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3: Connect the two directions.</strong></p>

  <p>
    The original hydrated compound is regenerated. The chemical change
    can therefore proceed in both directions.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Final answer:</strong> It is a reversible chemical change
  because dehydration and rehydration connect the same two chemical
  states in opposite directions.</p>

  <p>
    <strong>Important:</strong> The colour change helps you observe the
    process, but the evidence of reversibility is the regeneration of the
    original compound, not simply the appearance of a different colour.
  </p>

  <h3>WORKED EXAMPLE 3 — Reversible or Effectively Irreversible?</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Compare these two changes:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>Change A: A reversible reaction represented by
      <code>A + B ⇌ C + D</code>.</li>
    <li>Change B: Magnesium burns in oxygen to form magnesium oxide.</li>
  </ul>

  <p><strong>Step 1: Examine Change A.</strong></p>

  <p>
    The double arrow represents a reaction that can proceed in both
    directions. The products can reform the original reactants.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2: Examine Change B.</strong></p>

  <p>
    Magnesium burns in oxygen to form magnesium oxide:
  </p>

  <pre>2Mg + O₂ → 2MgO</pre>

  <p>
    Under ordinary conditions, magnesium oxide does not simply change
    back into magnesium and oxygen. Reversing this chemical change
    requires a different process and substantial energy.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3: Make the distinction.</strong></p>

  <p>
    Change A is represented as reversible. Change B is treated as
    effectively irreversible under ordinary classroom conditions.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Final answer:</strong> Reversibility means that the original
  reactants can be regenerated through the reverse chemical reaction
  under suitable conditions. It does not mean that every product can
  be converted back by any method whatsoever.</p>

  <h3>5. Common Misconceptions</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Misconception:</strong> A reversible reaction is one that
      can be undone by any method.
      <br/>
      <strong>Correction:</strong> Its reverse direction must regenerate
      the original reactants through the corresponding chemical change.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Misconception:</strong> The double arrow means the two
      directions are equally fast.
      <br/>
      <strong>Correction:</strong> It indicates two possible reaction
      directions, not equal reaction rates.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Misconception:</strong> Every reaction is reversible because
      products can react to form other substances.
      <br/>
      <strong>Correction:</strong> Those reactions must regenerate the
      original reactants to constitute the reverse of the original change.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Misconception:</strong> A colour change alone proves that a
      reaction is reversible.
      <br/>
      <strong>Correction:</strong> The original chemical substances must
      be regenerated.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>6. What You Should Be Able to Do</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Define a reversible reaction.</li>
    <li>Interpret the double arrow, ⇌.</li>
    <li>Identify the forward and reverse possibilities in an equation.</li>
    <li>Distinguish reversibility from simply undergoing another reaction.</li>
    <li>Explain why a chemical change can regenerate its original reactants.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Reversible Reactions', 15),
  (947, 947, '
  <h2>Forward and Reverse Reactions</h2>

  <h3>1. Two Directions of One Reversible Reaction</h3>

  <p>
    A reversible reaction has two possible directions. These directions
    describe how the reactants and products change into one another.
  </p>

  <p>Consider the general equation:</p>

  <pre>A + B ⇌ C + D</pre>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Forward reaction:</strong> A and B react to form C and D.
      It proceeds from left to right.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Reverse reaction:</strong> C and D react to reform A and B.
      It proceeds from right to left.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    The forward and reverse reactions are not two unrelated chemical
    reactions. They are the opposing directions of the same reversible
    chemical system.
  </p>

  <h3>2. How to Identify Each Direction</h3>

  <p>
    Use the double arrow as your reference point. For the forward
    direction, read the equation from left to right. For the reverse
    direction, read it from right to left.
  </p>

  <p>
    The names <strong>reactants</strong> and <strong>products</strong>
    depend on the direction being described. A substance that is a
    product in the forward reaction can be a reactant in the reverse
    reaction.
  </p>

  <p>
    This is one of the most important ideas in reversible reactions:
    the substances do not change their chemical identities simply
    because you describe the reaction in the opposite direction.
    Their roles as reactants or products change with the direction.
  </p>

  <h3>WORKED EXAMPLE 1 — Identifying Both Directions</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> For the equation below, write the forward
  and reverse reactions in words.</p>

  <pre>A + B ⇌ C + D</pre>

  <p><strong>Step 1: Read from left to right.</strong></p>

  <p>
    A reacts with B to form C and D. This is the forward reaction.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2: Read from right to left.</strong></p>

  <p>
    C and D react to reform A and B. This is the reverse reaction.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3: Identify the changing roles.</strong></p>

  <p>
    A and B are reactants in the forward direction and products in the
    reverse direction. C and D are products in the forward direction
    and reactants in the reverse direction.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Final answer:</strong></p>

  <ul>
    <li>Forward: A + B → C + D</li>
    <li>Reverse: C + D → A + B</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>WORKED EXAMPLE 2 — Using a Balanced Chemical Equation</h3>

  <p>
    <strong>Question:</strong> Hydrogen reacts with iodine to form
    hydrogen iodide:
  </p>

  <pre>H₂ + I₂ ⇌ 2HI</pre>

  <p>Identify the forward and reverse reactions.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1: Identify the forward direction.</strong></p>

  <p>
    Hydrogen and iodine react to form hydrogen iodide:
  </p>

  <pre>H₂ + I₂ → 2HI</pre>

  <p><strong>Step 2: Reverse the direction.</strong></p>

  <p>
    Hydrogen iodide decomposes to form hydrogen and iodine:
  </p>

  <pre>2HI → H₂ + I₂</pre>

  <p><strong>Step 3: Preserve the atom balance.</strong></p>

  <p>
    The forward equation contains two hydrogen atoms and two iodine
    atoms on each side. The reverse equation must preserve the same
    atom counts. Reversing the reaction does not change the chemical
    formulae or remove the coefficient 2.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Final answer:</strong></p>

  <ul>
    <li>Forward: H₂ + I₂ → 2HI</li>
    <li>Reverse: 2HI → H₂ + I₂</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    <strong>Key lesson:</strong> To write the reverse reaction, exchange
    the two sides of the equation while preserving the chemical formulae
    and coefficients.
  </p>

  <h3>WORKED EXAMPLE 3 — Correcting a Reversed Equation</h3>

  <p>
    <strong>Question:</strong> A learner is given the equation:
  </p>

  <pre>N₂ + 3H₂ ⇌ 2NH₃</pre>

  <p>
    The learner writes the reverse reaction as
    <code>2NH₃ → N₂ + H₂</code>. Identify and correct the error.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1: Identify the original forward reaction.</strong></p>

  <p>
    One nitrogen molecule reacts with three hydrogen molecules to form
    two ammonia molecules.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2: Reverse the sides.</strong></p>

  <p>
    The reverse reaction must start with two ammonia molecules and form
    nitrogen and hydrogen.
  </p>

  <pre>2NH₃ → N₂ + 3H₂</pre>

  <p><strong>Step 3: Check the atoms.</strong></p>

  <ul>
    <li>Left: 2 nitrogen atoms and 6 hydrogen atoms.</li>
    <li>Right: 2 nitrogen atoms and 6 hydrogen atoms.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    The learner omitted the coefficient 3 before hydrogen, leaving the
    equation unbalanced.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Final answer:</strong></p>

  <pre>2NH₃ → N₂ + 3H₂</pre>

  <p>
    To reverse a reaction correctly, reverse its direction while
    preserving the balanced relationship between the atoms.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Reactants and Products Depend on Direction</h3>

  <p>
    Consider again:
  </p>

  <pre>A + B ⇌ C + D</pre>

  <p>
    It is correct to say that A and B are the reactants when describing
    the forward reaction. It is also correct to say that C and D are
    the reactants when describing the reverse reaction.
  </p>

  <p>
    Therefore, avoid saying that a substance is always a reactant or
    always a product in a reversible system without specifying the
    direction being discussed.
  </p>

  <h3>4. The Difference Between Direction and Rate</h3>

  <p>
    The forward and reverse directions tell you <strong>which way
    substances are changing</strong>. Reaction rate tells you
    <strong>how quickly a reaction is occurring</strong>.
  </p>

  <p>
    These are different ideas. Identifying a reaction as forward or
    reverse does not, by itself, tell you how fast it is proceeding.
    The rates can change as the conditions and amounts of reactants
    and products change.
  </p>

  <h3>5. Common Misconceptions</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Misconception:</strong> Reactants always remain on the
      left-hand side.
      <br/>
      <strong>Correction:</strong> That convention applies to the
      direction in which the equation is written. In the reverse
      direction, the original products become reactants.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Misconception:</strong> Reversing a reaction means changing
      every chemical formula.
      <br/>
      <strong>Correction:</strong> Reverse the direction; retain the
      correct chemical formulae and atom balance.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Misconception:</strong> The reverse reaction has different
      substances from those shown in the original equation.
      <br/>
      <strong>Correction:</strong> It uses the same substances, with
      reactant and product roles exchanged.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Misconception:</strong> Forward means fast and reverse
      means slow.
      <br/>
      <strong>Correction:</strong> Forward and reverse describe direction,
      not speed.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>6. What You Should Be Able to Do</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Identify the forward reaction in a reversible equation.</li>
    <li>Write the reverse reaction correctly.</li>
    <li>Identify reactants and products in either direction.</li>
    <li>Preserve chemical formulae and atom balance when reversing an equation.</li>
    <li>Distinguish reaction direction from reaction rate.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Forward and Reverse Reactions', 15),
  (948, 948, '
  <h2>Dynamic Equilibrium</h2>

  <h3>1. What Is Chemical Equilibrium?</h3>

  <p>
    In a reversible reaction, reactants form products through the forward
    reaction, while products can react to reform the original reactants
    through the reverse reaction.
  </p>

  <p>Consider the general reversible reaction:</p>

  <pre>A + B ⇌ C + D</pre>

  <p>
    If the reaction takes place under suitable conditions in a closed
    system, it can reach a state called <strong>dynamic chemical
    equilibrium</strong>.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Dynamic equilibrium</strong> is the state in a closed system
    where the forward and reverse reactions continue at equal rates,
    causing the concentrations of reactants and products to remain
    constant at a given temperature.
  </p>

  <p>
    This definition contains four essential ideas:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Closed system:</strong> substances cannot freely enter or
      escape from the reacting system.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Forward reaction continues:</strong> reactants continue
      forming products.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Reverse reaction continues:</strong> products continue
      reforming reactants.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Equal rates:</strong> the two reactions occur at the same
      rate, so there is no net change in the amounts of the substances.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>2. Why Is Equilibrium Called Dynamic?</h3>

  <p>
    The word <strong>dynamic</strong> means that activity continues.
    Equilibrium does not mean that the chemical reactions have stopped.
    Particles continue to react, bonds continue to break and form, and
    substances continue to change into one another.
  </p>

  <p>
    What changes is the overall result that can be observed. At
    equilibrium, the forward reaction produces substances at exactly
    the rate at which the reverse reaction consumes them. Consequently,
    the overall concentrations remain constant.
  </p>

  <p>
    Imagine that the forward reaction converts 12 units of reactant into
    products every second, while the reverse reaction converts 12 units
    of products back into reactants every second. Both reactions are
    active, but neither direction produces a net change in the amounts
    of the substances.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Equal Reaction Rates Do Not Mean Equal Concentrations</h3>

  <p>
    This is one of the most important distinctions in chemical
    equilibrium.
  </p>

  <p>
    The forward and reverse rates must be equal at equilibrium, but the
    concentrations of the reactants and products do not have to be equal.
    A mixture may contain much more product than reactant, or much more
    reactant than product, while still being at equilibrium.
  </p>

  <p>
    The rates describe how quickly reactions occur. Concentrations
    describe how much of each substance is present per unit volume.
    These are different quantities.
  </p>

  <h3>WORKED EXAMPLE 1 — Identifying Dynamic Equilibrium</h3>

  <p>
    <strong>Question:</strong> A reversible reaction takes place in a
    sealed container. The forward reaction occurs at 8 units per second,
    and the reverse reaction also occurs at 8 units per second. Are the
    reactions at dynamic equilibrium?
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1: Check the system.</strong></p>

  <p>
    The container is sealed, so substances cannot freely enter or leave.
    This satisfies the closed-system condition.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2: Compare the reaction rates.</strong></p>

  <ul>
    <li>Forward rate = 8 units per second.</li>
    <li>Reverse rate = 8 units per second.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    The two rates are equal.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3: Determine the net change.</strong></p>

  <p>
    Products form at the same rate as they are consumed by the reverse
    reaction. Similarly, reactants are consumed at the same rate as
    they are regenerated.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Final answer:</strong> Yes. The system is at dynamic
  equilibrium because it is closed and the forward and reverse rates
  are equal. Both reactions continue.</p>

  <h3>WORKED EXAMPLE 2 — Unequal Concentrations at Equilibrium</h3>

  <p>
    <strong>Question:</strong> At equilibrium, a reaction mixture contains
    0.80 mol/dm³ of reactant A and 0.20 mol/dm³ of product B. The forward
    and reverse rates are equal. A learner concludes that the mixture
    cannot be at equilibrium because the concentrations are different.
    Evaluate the conclusion.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1: Identify what equilibrium requires.</strong></p>

  <p>
    Dynamic equilibrium requires equal forward and reverse reaction
    rates, constant concentrations and a closed system under the
    relevant conditions.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2: Examine the concentrations.</strong></p>

  <p>
    The concentration of A is 0.80 mol/dm³, while that of B is
    0.20 mol/dm³. They are different, but this does not contradict
    equilibrium.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3: Examine the rates.</strong></p>

  <p>
    The question states that the forward and reverse rates are equal.
    If the system is closed and the conditions remain constant, the
    mixture can be at dynamic equilibrium.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Final answer:</strong> The learner''s conclusion is wrong.
  Different concentrations can exist at equilibrium because equilibrium
  requires equal reaction rates, not equal concentrations.</p>

  <h3>WORKED EXAMPLE 3 — Constant Concentration or Stopped Reaction?</h3>

  <p>
    <strong>Question:</strong> In a closed container, the measured
    concentrations of reactants and products remain unchanged for a
    period of time. A student claims that no chemical reactions are
    occurring. Explain why this conclusion is not justified.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1: Interpret the observation.</strong></p>

  <p>
    Constant concentrations mean that the overall amounts of the
    substances are not changing measurably.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2: Consider a reversible reaction.</strong></p>

  <p>
    The forward reaction may continue consuming reactants and producing
    products. At the same time, the reverse reaction may consume products
    and regenerate reactants.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3: Compare the opposing effects.</strong></p>

  <p>
    If both reactions occur at equal rates, each direction balances the
    overall change caused by the other. The concentrations remain
    constant even though particles continue reacting.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Final answer:</strong> Constant concentrations do not mean
  that the reactions have stopped. In a closed reversible system, they
  are consistent with dynamic equilibrium when the forward and reverse
  rates are equal.</p>

  <h3>4. How to Recognise Dynamic Equilibrium</h3>

  <p>
    When assessing a chemical system, ask:
  </p>

  <ol>
    <li>Is the system closed?</li>
    <li>Can the forward and reverse reactions both occur?</li>
    <li>Are the forward and reverse reaction rates equal?</li>
    <li>Do the concentrations remain constant under unchanged conditions?</li>
  </ol>

  <p>
    These ideas work together. Constant concentration alone does not
    establish every condition of dynamic equilibrium: for example, a
    reaction that has stopped because a necessary reactant is exhausted
    is not thereby a dynamic equilibrium.
  </p>

  <h3>5. What Equilibrium Does Not Mean</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>It does not mean equal concentrations.</strong> The
      concentrations of reactants and products may differ considerably.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>It does not mean zero reaction rate.</strong> Both
      opposing reactions continue.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>It does not mean equal amounts of reactants and products.</strong>
      The final composition depends on the reaction and conditions.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>It does not mean the system can be open without restriction.</strong>
      Ordinary chemical equilibrium is established in a closed system.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>It does not mean concentrations can never change.</strong>
      If conditions change, the equilibrium state can be disturbed.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>6. Common Misconceptions</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Wrong:</strong> At equilibrium, the forward reaction stops.
      <br/>
      <strong>Correct:</strong> The forward reaction continues at the
      same rate as the reverse reaction.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Wrong:</strong> The amounts of reactants and products must
      be equal.
      <br/>
      <strong>Correct:</strong> Their concentrations remain constant,
      but they need not be equal.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Wrong:</strong> Constant concentration proves that no
      particles are reacting.
      <br/>
      <strong>Correct:</strong> Opposing reactions can balance one
      another while particles continue reacting.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Wrong:</strong> Equal reaction rates mean equal numbers of
      reactant and product particles.
      <br/>
      <strong>Correct:</strong> Equal rates describe the speed of the
      opposing reactions, not the quantities present.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>7. What You Should Be Able to Do</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Define dynamic chemical equilibrium.</li>
    <li>Explain why equilibrium is dynamic rather than static.</li>
    <li>Distinguish reaction rate from concentration.</li>
    <li>Explain why concentrations can differ at equilibrium.</li>
    <li>Identify why a closed system is important.</li>
    <li>Evaluate claims about equilibrium using the correct conditions.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Dynamic Equilibrium', 15),
  (949, 949, '
  <h2>Establishing Equilibrium</h2>

  <h3>1. How Does a Reversible Reaction Reach Equilibrium?</h3>

  <p>
    Dynamic equilibrium does not normally exist from the instant a
    reversible reaction begins. If a mixture initially contains only
    reactants, the forward and reverse reactions usually begin at
    different rates.
  </p>

  <p>Consider the general reaction:</p>

  <pre>A + B ⇌ C + D</pre>

  <p>
    Suppose the reaction begins with A and B present, but little or no
    C and D. The forward reaction can produce C and D. As these products
    accumulate, the reverse reaction becomes increasingly able to occur.
  </p>

  <p>
    Eventually, under suitable constant conditions in a closed system,
    the forward and reverse reaction rates become equal. Dynamic
    equilibrium has then been established.
  </p>

  <h3>2. The Three Main Stages</h3>

  <h3>Stage 1 — Beginning of the Reaction</h3>

  <p>
    If the system initially contains reactants but no products, the
    forward reaction can begin immediately. The reverse reaction rate
    is initially zero if none of the products required for that reverse
    reaction is present.
  </p>

  <p>
    The forward rate is often greatest at the beginning because the
    reactants are most abundant then. However, the precise initial rate
    depends on the reaction mechanism and conditions.
  </p>

  <h3>Stage 2 — Products Accumulate</h3>

  <p>
    As the forward reaction consumes reactants, their concentrations
    usually decrease. For many reactions, this causes the forward rate
    to decrease.
  </p>

  <p>
    At the same time, the products accumulate. Their increased
    availability allows the reverse reaction to proceed more rapidly.
  </p>

  <p>
    The forward rate therefore commonly decreases while the reverse
    rate increases. These changes continue until the two rates become
    equal.
  </p>

  <h3>Stage 3 — Dynamic Equilibrium</h3>

  <p>
    At equilibrium, the forward and reverse reactions occur at equal
    rates. The reactions do not stop. Instead, each direction balances
    the overall change caused by the other.
  </p>

  <p>
    Under constant conditions, the concentrations of reactants and
    products remain constant. They do not have to be equal.
  </p>

  <h3>WORKED EXAMPLE 1 — Following the Changes in Reaction Rates</h3>

  <p>
    <strong>Question:</strong> A reversible reaction begins with
    reactants only. Describe what happens to the forward and reverse
    reaction rates as the system approaches equilibrium.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1: At the beginning.</strong></p>

  <p>
    The reactants are present, so the forward reaction can occur. If
    no products are present, the reverse rate is initially zero.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2: As the reaction proceeds.</strong></p>

  <p>
    Reactants are consumed and products form. The forward rate commonly
    decreases as reactant concentrations fall, while the reverse rate
    increases as products accumulate.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3: At equilibrium.</strong></p>

  <p>
    The two reaction rates become equal. Both reactions continue,
    and the concentrations remain constant under unchanged conditions.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Final answer:</strong> The forward rate commonly decreases
  and the reverse rate increases until they become equal. Equilibrium
  is the state where the opposing rates balance, not the point at which
  the reactions stop.</p>

  <h3>WORKED EXAMPLE 2 — Interpreting Rate Data</h3>

  <p>
    <strong>Question:</strong> The following data are recorded for a
    reversible reaction in a closed vessel at constant temperature.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>At time 1: forward rate = 10 units/s; reverse rate = 2 units/s.</li>
    <li>At time 2: forward rate = 7 units/s; reverse rate = 5 units/s.</li>
    <li>At time 3: forward rate = 4 units/s; reverse rate = 4 units/s.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>At which recorded time has dynamic equilibrium been reached?</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1: State the equilibrium condition.</strong></p>

  <p>
    Dynamic equilibrium occurs when the forward and reverse rates are
    equal in the closed system.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2: Compare the rates at each time.</strong></p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      Time 1: 10 units/s is not equal to 2 units/s.
    </li>
    <li>
      Time 2: 7 units/s is not equal to 5 units/s.
    </li>
    <li>
      Time 3: 4 units/s equals 4 units/s.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p><strong>Step 3: Identify the equilibrium time.</strong></p>

  <p>
    Only the rates recorded at time 3 are equal.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Final answer:</strong> Dynamic equilibrium has been reached
  by time 3, when both rates are 4 units/s.</p>

  <p>
    <strong>Important:</strong> The numerical rates in this example are
    illustrative. The defining condition is equality of the rates, not
    any particular numerical value.
  </p>

  <h3>WORKED EXAMPLE 3 — Why the Reaction Does Not Stop at Equilibrium</h3>

  <p>
    <strong>Question:</strong> A student argues that once the forward
    and reverse rates become equal, no further chemical change occurs.
    Use a numerical example to correct this claim.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1: Consider the rates at equilibrium.</strong></p>

  <p>
    Suppose the forward reaction forms 30 units of product per minute
    and the reverse reaction consumes 30 units of product per minute.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2: Calculate the net change in product.</strong></p>

  <pre>Net change = amount formed − amount consumed
            = 30 − 30
            = 0 units per minute</pre>

  <p><strong>Step 3: Interpret the result.</strong></p>

  <p>
    The net change is zero, but both reactions are occurring at
    30 units per minute. Product molecules are being formed and
    consumed continuously.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Final answer:</strong> The student is wrong. The reactions
  continue at equal rates. The overall concentrations remain constant
  because the production and consumption balance each other.</p>

  <h3>3. How Concentrations Change During Establishment</h3>

  <p>
    When a reaction begins with reactants only, the reactant
    concentrations commonly decrease while product concentrations
    increase.
  </p>

  <p>
    Once equilibrium is established, the concentrations become constant
    under unchanged conditions. They do not necessarily become equal,
    and they do not necessarily reach a point where all reactants have
    been consumed.
  </p>

  <p>
    The exact equilibrium concentrations depend on the chemical system,
    the initial mixture and the conditions. They cannot be determined
    from the definition of equilibrium alone.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Reading a Rate-versus-Time Graph</h3>

  <p>
    A graph of reaction rate against time can help show how equilibrium
    develops.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      The forward-rate curve may begin at a high value and decrease.
    </li>
    <li>
      The reverse-rate curve may begin at zero if no products are
      initially present, then increase as products form.
    </li>
    <li>
      The curves meet when the forward and reverse rates become equal.
    </li>
    <li>
      After equilibrium is established, the rates remain equal while
      conditions remain unchanged.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    These are common trends for a system initially containing reactants
    only. The exact shapes of the curves depend on the reaction and
    should not be assumed to be perfectly straight lines.
  </p>

  <h3>5. Common Misconceptions</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Wrong:</strong> Equilibrium exists immediately when
      reactants are mixed.
      <br/>
      <strong>Correct:</strong> The rates must develop until the forward
      and reverse rates are equal.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Wrong:</strong> The reverse reaction always begins at
      its maximum rate.
      <br/>
      <strong>Correct:</strong> If no products are present initially,
      the reverse rate is initially zero.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Wrong:</strong> The forward rate must decrease linearly.
      <br/>
      <strong>Correct:</strong> The exact rate changes depend on the
      reaction; a straight-line decrease is not guaranteed.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Wrong:</strong> Equal rates mean that both rates are zero.
      <br/>
      <strong>Correct:</strong> The rates can be equal and non-zero.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Wrong:</strong> Reactants must be completely used up
      before equilibrium can occur.
      <br/>
      <strong>Correct:</strong> Equilibrium can be established while
      both reactants and products remain in the mixture.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>6. What You Should Be Able to Do</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Describe the stages involved in establishing equilibrium.</li>
    <li>Explain why the forward rate commonly decreases.</li>
    <li>Explain why the reverse rate commonly increases.</li>
    <li>Identify equilibrium from forward and reverse rate data.</li>
    <li>Interpret common rate-versus-time graph features.</li>
    <li>Explain why equal reaction rates do not mean the reactions stop.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Establishing Equilibrium', 15),
  (950, 950, '
  <h2>Effect of Concentration on Equilibrium</h2>

  <h3>1. What Is Concentration?</h3>

  <p>
    <strong>Concentration</strong> describes how much of a substance is
    present in a given volume of a mixture. For a solution, concentration
    is often expressed in mol/dm³.
  </p>

  <p>
    For example, a solution containing 2 mol of dissolved substance in
    1 dm³ has a concentration of 2 mol/dm³. If the same amount is
    distributed through a larger volume, its concentration is lower.
  </p>

  <p>
    In a reversible reaction, changing the concentration of a reactant
    or product can disturb an existing equilibrium. The reaction then
    responds by changing the relative rates of the forward and reverse
    reactions until a new equilibrium is established, provided the
    conditions allow it.
  </p>

  <h3>2. The Principle Behind an Equilibrium Shift</h3>

  <p>
    When a system at equilibrium experiences a change in concentration,
    the equilibrium position shifts in the direction that partly opposes
    that change. This is an application of <strong>Le Châtelier''s
    principle</strong>.
  </p>

  <p>Consider the general reaction:</p>

  <pre>A + B ⇌ C + D</pre>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Add A or B:</strong> the system tends to consume some of
      the added reactant by shifting to the right.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Remove A or B:</strong> the system tends to replace some
      of the removed reactant by shifting to the left.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Add C or D:</strong> the system tends to consume some of
      the added product by shifting to the left.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Remove C or D:</strong> the system tends to replace some
      of the removed product by shifting to the right.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    A shift to the right means a net movement towards products. A shift
    to the left means a net movement towards reactants.
  </p>

  <p>
    The shift does not necessarily remove all of an added substance or
    replace everything removed. It is a response that helps establish
    a new equilibrium.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Why Does the System Shift?</h3>

  <p>
    At the original equilibrium, the forward and reverse reaction rates
    are equal. Changing a concentration changes the reacting mixture
    and can disturb that balance.
  </p>

  <p>
    Immediately after a concentration change, the forward and reverse
    rates are generally no longer equal. A net reaction then occurs in
    one direction. As the mixture changes, the rates adjust until they
    become equal again at the new equilibrium.
  </p>

  <p>
    The direction of the shift is a statement about the <strong>net
    reaction</strong>. It does not mean that the reaction in the
    opposite direction has stopped.
  </p>

  <h3>WORKED EXAMPLE 1 — Adding a Reactant</h3>

  <p>
    <strong>Question:</strong> Consider the equilibrium:
  </p>

  <pre>H₂(g) + I₂(g) ⇌ 2HI(g)</pre>

  <p>
    Hydrogen is added to a mixture that is already at equilibrium.
    Predict the direction of the shift and explain the result.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1: Identify what has changed.</strong></p>

  <p>
    Hydrogen, H₂, is a reactant. Its concentration has increased.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2: Identify the response that opposes the change.</strong></p>

  <p>
    The system tends to consume some of the additional hydrogen. The
    forward reaction uses hydrogen and iodine to produce hydrogen iodide.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3: Predict the direction.</strong></p>

  <p>
    The equilibrium shifts to the right, towards hydrogen iodide.
    The net forward reaction consumes some of the added hydrogen and
    some iodine.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Final answer:</strong> Adding hydrogen shifts the
  equilibrium to the right, favouring the formation of HI. The shift
  does not mean that all the added hydrogen must be consumed.</p>

  <h3>WORKED EXAMPLE 2 — Removing a Product</h3>

  <p>
    <strong>Question:</strong> For the same equilibrium,
  </p>

  <pre>H₂(g) + I₂(g) ⇌ 2HI(g)</pre>

  <p>
    some hydrogen iodide is removed. Explain the direction of the
    resulting shift.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1: Identify the substance removed.</strong></p>

  <p>
    Hydrogen iodide, HI, is a product in the forward direction.
    Its concentration has decreased.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2: Identify the response that opposes the change.</strong></p>

  <p>
    The system tends to produce more HI to replace some of what was
    removed.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3: Identify the reaction direction.</strong></p>

  <p>
    Producing more HI is the forward reaction, so the equilibrium
    shifts to the right.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Final answer:</strong> Removing HI shifts the equilibrium
  to the right, favouring the forward reaction and the formation of
  additional hydrogen iodide.</p>

  <h3>WORKED EXAMPLE 3 — Removing a Reactant</h3>

  <p>
    <strong>Question:</strong> Consider the equilibrium:
  </p>

  <pre>N₂(g) + 3H₂(g) ⇌ 2NH₃(g)</pre>

  <p>
    Nitrogen is removed from the equilibrium mixture. Predict the
    direction of the shift and explain why.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1: Identify the role of nitrogen.</strong></p>

  <p>
    Nitrogen is a reactant in the forward reaction.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2: Identify the disturbance.</strong></p>

  <p>
    Removing nitrogen lowers its concentration. The system responds
    in a way that tends to replace some of the removed nitrogen.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3: Determine which direction produces nitrogen.</strong></p>

  <p>
    The reverse reaction decomposes ammonia to form nitrogen and
    hydrogen.
  </p>

  <pre>2NH₃(g) → N₂(g) + 3H₂(g)</pre>

  <p>
    The net reaction therefore shifts to the left.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Final answer:</strong> Removing nitrogen shifts the
  equilibrium to the left, towards the reactants, because the reverse
  reaction tends to regenerate some nitrogen.</p>

  <h3>4. A Reliable Method for Predicting a Shift</h3>

  <ol>
    <li>Write or examine the balanced reversible equation.</li>
    <li>Identify the substance whose concentration changes.</li>
    <li>Decide whether that substance was added or removed.</li>
    <li>Ask which direction would consume an added substance or replace a removed substance.</li>
    <li>State the direction of the shift and explain it using the equation.</li>
  </ol>

  <p>
    Do not choose a direction simply because the word concentration
    appears in the question. You must identify the exact substance and
    whether its concentration increases or decreases.
  </p>

  <h3>5. What Happens to Concentrations After a Shift?</h3>

  <p>
    Suppose a reactant is added. Its concentration rises immediately
    because it has been introduced into the mixture. The subsequent
    shift consumes some of that reactant, while changing the
    concentrations of the other substances.
  </p>

  <p>
    Similarly, removing a product causes its concentration to fall
    immediately. The shift towards products then tends to regenerate
    some of it.
  </p>

  <p>
    It is important to distinguish the immediate effect of the
    disturbance from the changes that occur as the system moves
    towards its new equilibrium. The concentration of the substance
    added or removed need not return to its original value.
  </p>

  <h3>6. Does a Concentration Change Alter the Equilibrium Constant?</h3>

  <p>
    For a given reaction, the equilibrium constant depends on
    temperature. Changing a reactant or product concentration at
    constant temperature changes the equilibrium position, but it
    does not change the equilibrium constant.
  </p>

  <p>
    The mixture responds by changing its composition until the
    equilibrium condition is satisfied again at that temperature.
    This distinction matters: <strong>an equilibrium shift is not
    the same as a change in the equilibrium constant.</strong>
  </p>

  <h3>7. Common Misconceptions</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Wrong:</strong> Adding a reactant shifts equilibrium
      towards reactants.
      <br/>
      <strong>Correct:</strong> Adding a reactant generally shifts
      the equilibrium towards products, consuming some of the added
      reactant.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Wrong:</strong> Removing a product shifts equilibrium
      towards reactants.
      <br/>
      <strong>Correct:</strong> Removing a product generally shifts
      equilibrium towards products, tending to replace some of it.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Wrong:</strong> The system completely removes any
      substance that is added.
      <br/>
      <strong>Correct:</strong> The shift opposes the disturbance;
      it does not guarantee complete removal.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Wrong:</strong> Once the system shifts right, the
      reverse reaction stops.
      <br/>
      <strong>Correct:</strong> Both directions can continue; the
      net reaction is towards products until the rates become equal
      again.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Wrong:</strong> Changing concentration changes the
      equilibrium constant at constant temperature.
      <br/>
      <strong>Correct:</strong> Concentration changes can shift the
      equilibrium position, while the equilibrium constant remains
      unchanged at the same temperature.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>8. What You Should Be Able to Do</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>State how adding a reactant affects equilibrium.</li>
    <li>State how removing a reactant affects equilibrium.</li>
    <li>Predict the effect of adding or removing a product.</li>
    <li>Use a balanced equation to justify a shift.</li>
    <li>Distinguish an immediate concentration change from the later response.</li>
    <li>Distinguish a change in equilibrium position from a change in the equilibrium constant.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Effect of Concentration on Equilibrium', 15),
  (951, 951, '
  <h2>Effect of Temperature on Equilibrium</h2>

  <h3>1. Why Does Temperature Affect Equilibrium?</h3>

  <p>
    Chemical reactions involve energy changes. Some reactions release
    heat to their surroundings, while others absorb heat from their
    surroundings.
  </p>

  <p>
    A change in temperature can change the relative favourability of
    the forward and reverse reactions. Consequently, a system at
    equilibrium can shift towards reactants or products when its
    temperature changes.
  </p>

  <p>
    To predict the direction correctly, you must first know whether
    the forward reaction is <strong>exothermic</strong> or
    <strong>endothermic</strong>.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Exothermic and Endothermic Reactions</h3>

  <p>
    An <strong>exothermic reaction</strong> releases heat to the
    surroundings. Its enthalpy change is negative:
  </p>

  <pre>ΔH &lt; 0</pre>

  <p>
    An <strong>endothermic reaction</strong> absorbs heat from the
    surroundings. Its enthalpy change is positive:
  </p>

  <pre>ΔH &gt; 0</pre>

  <p>
    In a reversible reaction, the reverse direction has the opposite
    heat effect to the forward direction. If the forward reaction
    releases heat, the reverse reaction absorbs heat. If the forward
    reaction absorbs heat, the reverse reaction releases heat.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Representing Heat in an Equation</h3>

  <p>
    Heat can be represented as a product in an exothermic forward
    reaction:
  </p>

  <pre>A + B ⇌ C + D + heat</pre>

  <p>
    Here, the forward reaction releases heat. The reverse reaction
    consumes heat.
  </p>

  <p>
    For an endothermic forward reaction, heat can be represented as
    a reactant:
  </p>

  <pre>A + B + heat ⇌ C + D</pre>

  <p>
    Here, the forward reaction absorbs heat, while the reverse reaction
    releases heat.
  </p>

  <p>
    Treating heat this way is a useful model for predicting temperature
    effects. It does not mean that heat is a chemical substance like
    A or B.
  </p>

  <h3>4. The Temperature Rule</h3>

  <p>
    Increasing temperature favours the direction that absorbs heat.
    Decreasing temperature favours the direction that releases heat.
  </p>

  <p>
    Apply this rule to the <strong>forward and reverse directions</strong>,
    not simply to the words reactants and products.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Exothermic forward reaction:</strong> increasing temperature
      shifts equilibrium to the left; decreasing temperature shifts it
      to the right.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Endothermic forward reaction:</strong> increasing temperature
      shifts equilibrium to the right; decreasing temperature shifts it
      to the left.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>WORKED EXAMPLE 1 — Increasing Temperature in an Exothermic Reaction</h3>

  <p>
    <strong>Question:</strong> Consider the exothermic equilibrium:
  </p>

  <pre>A + B ⇌ C + D + heat</pre>

  <p>
    Predict the direction of the shift when the temperature is increased.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1: Identify the heat effect.</strong></p>

  <p>
    Heat appears on the product side. The forward reaction releases heat
    and is exothermic.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2: Identify what the temperature change favours.</strong></p>

  <p>
    Increasing temperature favours the direction that absorbs heat.
    In this system, that is the reverse reaction.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3: State the direction.</strong></p>

  <p>
    The equilibrium shifts to the left, towards A and B.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Final answer:</strong> Increasing the temperature shifts
  the equilibrium to the left because the reverse reaction absorbs heat.</p>

  <h3>WORKED EXAMPLE 2 — Decreasing Temperature in an Endothermic Reaction</h3>

  <p>
    <strong>Question:</strong> The forward reaction in the equilibrium
    below is endothermic:
  </p>

  <pre>A + B + heat ⇌ C + D</pre>

  <p>
    Predict the direction of the shift when the temperature is decreased.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1: Identify the forward reaction.</strong></p>

  <p>
    Heat is on the reactant side, so the forward reaction absorbs heat.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2: Identify the effect of cooling.</strong></p>

  <p>
    Decreasing temperature favours the direction that releases heat.
    In this example, the reverse reaction releases heat.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3: Identify the direction of the shift.</strong></p>

  <p>
    The equilibrium shifts to the left, towards A and B.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Final answer:</strong> Decreasing the temperature shifts
  the equilibrium to the left because the reverse reaction releases heat.</p>

  <h3>WORKED EXAMPLE 3 — Applying the Rule to the Haber Process</h3>

  <p>
    <strong>Question:</strong> The Haber process is represented by:
  </p>

  <pre>N₂(g) + 3H₂(g) ⇌ 2NH₃(g)     ΔH &lt; 0</pre>

  <p>
    The forward reaction is exothermic. Explain what happens to the
    equilibrium position when the temperature is increased and when
    the temperature is decreased.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1: Identify the forward heat effect.</strong></p>

  <p>
    Since ΔH is negative, the forward reaction releases heat.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2: Increase the temperature.</strong></p>

  <p>
    The system favours the endothermic direction, which is the reverse
    reaction. Equilibrium shifts to the left, favouring nitrogen and
    hydrogen and reducing the equilibrium yield of ammonia.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3: Decrease the temperature.</strong></p>

  <p>
    The system favours the exothermic direction, which is the forward
    reaction. Equilibrium shifts to the right, favouring ammonia.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Final answer:</strong></p>

  <ul>
    <li>Higher temperature: shift left; lower equilibrium yield of NH₃.</li>
    <li>Lower temperature: shift right; higher equilibrium yield of NH₃.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    <strong>Industrial qualification:</strong> A lower temperature
    favours ammonia formation at equilibrium, but it also slows the
    reaction. Industry therefore uses a practical compromise between
    equilibrium yield and reaction rate rather than simply choosing the
    lowest possible temperature.
  </p>

  <h3>5. Temperature and Reaction Rate Are Different Effects</h3>

  <p>
    Increasing temperature generally increases reaction rates because
    particles have greater average kinetic energy and a larger fraction
    of collisions can overcome the activation energy.
  </p>

  <p>
    However, the direction of the <strong>equilibrium shift</strong>
    is determined by the heat effect of the reaction. A higher
    temperature can speed up both directions while shifting the
    equilibrium position towards the endothermic direction.
  </p>

  <p>
    Do not conclude that an equilibrium shifts towards products merely
    because the reaction becomes faster. Reaction rate and equilibrium
    position are separate ideas.
  </p>

  <h3>6. Does Temperature Change the Equilibrium Constant?</h3>

  <p>
    Yes. For a given reaction, changing temperature changes the
    equilibrium constant, unlike a simple change in concentration at
    constant temperature.
  </p>

  <p>
    For an exothermic forward reaction, increasing temperature generally
    decreases the equilibrium constant, while decreasing temperature
    generally increases it. For an endothermic forward reaction, the
    opposite trend applies.
  </p>

  <p>
    A change in the equilibrium constant reflects a change in the
    equilibrium composition at the new temperature. It does not mean
    that equilibrium cannot be established.
  </p>

  <h3>7. Common Misconceptions</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Wrong:</strong> Increasing temperature always shifts
      equilibrium towards products.
      <br/>
      <strong>Correct:</strong> Increasing temperature favours the
      endothermic direction, which may be forward or reverse.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Wrong:</strong> Cooling always shifts equilibrium towards
      reactants.
      <br/>
      <strong>Correct:</strong> Cooling favours the exothermic direction.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Wrong:</strong> An exothermic reaction absorbs heat.
      <br/>
      <strong>Correct:</strong> An exothermic reaction releases heat;
      an endothermic reaction absorbs heat.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Wrong:</strong> A higher temperature always increases the
      equilibrium yield of a product.
      <br/>
      <strong>Correct:</strong> The yield depends on whether forming
      that product is favoured by the endothermic or exothermic direction.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Wrong:</strong> Faster reactions necessarily mean a
      greater equilibrium yield.
      <br/>
      <strong>Correct:</strong> Rate describes how quickly reaction
      occurs; equilibrium position describes the final composition
      under specified conditions.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>8. What You Should Be Able to Do</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Distinguish exothermic and endothermic reactions.</li>
    <li>Use the heat effect of a reaction to predict temperature shifts.</li>
    <li>Predict shifts for both increasing and decreasing temperature.</li>
    <li>Explain the temperature effect using the heat-absorption direction.</li>
    <li>Distinguish reaction rate from equilibrium position.</li>
    <li>Explain why temperature changes the equilibrium constant.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Effect of Temperature on Equilibrium', 15),
  (952, 952, '
  <h2>Effect of Pressure on Equilibrium</h2>

  <h3>1. What pressure means in gaseous equilibrium</h3>

  <p>
    Gas particles move continuously and collide with the walls of their container.
    These collisions produce pressure. When the volume of a container containing
    gases is reduced at constant temperature, the gas particles occupy less space,
    so their pressure increases.
  </p>

  <p>
    In a reversible reaction involving gases, changing pressure can change the
    position of equilibrium. The system responds in accordance with
    <strong>Le Chatelier''s principle</strong>: when a system at equilibrium is
    disturbed, it responds in a way that opposes the disturbance.
  </p>

  <p>
    To predict the effect of pressure, compare the total number of moles of
    gaseous particles represented by the balanced chemical equation on each side.
    This is a comparison of the gaseous coefficients, not the number of atoms.
  </p>

  <h3>2. The rules for predicting pressure changes</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Increasing pressure:</strong> equilibrium shifts towards the side
      with fewer moles of gaseous particles.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Decreasing pressure:</strong> equilibrium shifts towards the side
      with more moles of gaseous particles.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Equal gaseous mole totals:</strong> changing pressure does not shift
      the equilibrium position if the total gaseous coefficients are equal on
      both sides.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    In school-level equilibrium questions, pressure is commonly increased by
    reducing the container''s volume and decreased by increasing its volume.
    Apply these rules while assuming the temperature remains constant.
  </p>

  <h3>3. How to count gaseous moles correctly</h3>

  <p>
    Use the following method every time:
  </p>

  <ol>
    <li>Write the balanced reversible equation.</li>
    <li>Identify the substances labelled (g).</li>
    <li>Add their coefficients on the left-hand side.</li>
    <li>Add their coefficients on the right-hand side.</li>
    <li>Compare the totals and apply the appropriate pressure rule.</li>
  </ol>

  <p>
    A coefficient represents the relative number of moles of a substance in
    the balanced equation. A subscript tells you the number of atoms in one
    formula unit or molecule. Do not confuse the two.
  </p>

  <p>
    Pure solids, labelled (s), and pure liquids, labelled (l), are not counted
    when comparing the total moles of gaseous particles. Their coefficients
    do not contribute to the gaseous mole totals.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Increasing pressure in the Haber equilibrium</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Predict the effect of increasing pressure on the equilibrium below.</p>

  <p>
    N₂(g) + 3H₂(g) ⇌ 2NH₃(g)
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1: Count gaseous moles on the left.</strong></p>

  <p>
    Nitrogen contributes 1 mole and hydrogen contributes 3 moles.
  </p>

  <p>
    Total on the left = 1 + 3 = 4 moles of gas.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2: Count gaseous moles on the right.</strong></p>

  <p>
    Two moles of ammonia are represented.
  </p>

  <p>
    Total on the right = 2 moles of gas.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3: Compare the totals.</strong></p>

  <p>
    The left has 4 gaseous moles, while the right has 2. The right-hand side
    has fewer gaseous moles.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> Increasing pressure shifts equilibrium to the right, favouring ammonia formation.</p>

  <p>
    Conversely, decreasing pressure favours the left-hand side and reduces the
    equilibrium tendency towards ammonia formation.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: When pressure does not shift equilibrium</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> What happens when pressure increases in this equilibrium?</p>

  <p>
    H₂(g) + I₂(g) ⇌ 2HI(g)
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1: Count gaseous moles on the left.</strong></p>

  <p>
    H₂ has a coefficient of 1 and I₂ has a coefficient of 1.
    Total = 1 + 1 = 2 gaseous moles.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2: Count gaseous moles on the right.</strong></p>

  <p>
    HI has a coefficient of 2. Total = 2 gaseous moles.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3: Compare the totals.</strong></p>

  <p>
    Both sides have the same total number of gaseous moles.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> Changing pressure does not shift the equilibrium position.</p>

  <p>
    Do not conclude that pressure has no physical effect on the gases. A pressure
    change can affect gas concentrations and reaction rates. The important point
    is that neither direction is favoured by the gaseous mole-count rule in this
    example.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Counting gases when solids are present</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Predict the effect of increasing pressure on this equilibrium.</p>

  <p>
    CaCO₃(s) ⇌ CaO(s) + CO₂(g)
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1: Count gaseous moles on the left.</strong></p>

  <p>
    Calcium carbonate is a solid. There are no gaseous particles on the left.
    Total gaseous moles = 0.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2: Count gaseous moles on the right.</strong></p>

  <p>
    Calcium oxide is a solid and is ignored. Carbon dioxide is a gas with
    coefficient 1. Total gaseous moles = 1.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3: Apply the pressure rule.</strong></p>

  <p>
    The left side has fewer gaseous moles than the right side.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> Increasing pressure shifts equilibrium to the left, favouring calcium carbonate. Decreasing pressure favours the right-hand side.</p>

  <h3>4. Pressure, equilibrium position and reaction rate</h3>

  <p>
    These three ideas must be distinguished:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Pressure change:</strong> a physical disturbance to the gaseous
      system, commonly produced by changing volume.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Equilibrium position:</strong> the relative composition of the
      system when equilibrium is re-established.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Reaction rate:</strong> how quickly the forward or reverse reaction
      proceeds.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    If volume is suddenly reduced at constant temperature, the partial pressures
    of the gases increase immediately. The forward and reverse rates may no
    longer be equal. The system then responds, and the equilibrium composition
    changes if the two sides have different gaseous mole totals.
  </p>

  <p>
    The shift is not a rule that the reaction always moves towards the side with
    a higher coefficient or a more complicated formula. It depends on the total
    gaseous mole count on each side.
  </p>

  <h3>5. Important limitations and misconceptions</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>
      Count coefficients of gaseous substances only; do not count solid or
      liquid substances.
    </li>
    <li>
      Use the balanced equation. An unbalanced equation gives unreliable mole
      totals.
    </li>
    <li>
      Keep temperature constant when applying the simple pressure comparison
      rule. Changing temperature can independently shift equilibrium.
    </li>
    <li>
      If gaseous mole totals are equal, pressure does not shift the equilibrium
      position under the stated conditions.
    </li>
    <li>
      A catalyst does not shift the equilibrium position. It speeds up the
      attainment of equilibrium by increasing the rates of both directions.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>6. A reliable exam method</h3>

  <p>
    When asked how pressure affects a gaseous equilibrium, do not guess from
    the formula or memorise the reaction alone. Write the balanced equation,
    count gaseous moles on both sides, state which side has fewer or more, and
    then give the direction of the shift.
  </p>

  <p>
    A complete answer should connect the disturbance to the predicted shift
    and, where relevant, identify the substance whose formation is favoured.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Effect of Pressure on Equilibrium', 15),
  (953, 953, '
  <h2>Industrial Equilibrium</h2>

  <h3>1. Why equilibrium matters in industry</h3>

  <p>
    Some industrial chemicals are manufactured using reversible reactions.
    These reactions may not convert all the reactants into products because
    the forward and reverse reactions eventually reach dynamic equilibrium
    under fixed conditions.
  </p>

  <p>
    Industrial chemists choose conditions that balance several competing
    requirements:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Equilibrium yield:</strong> the amount of product favoured at
      equilibrium.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Reaction rate:</strong> how quickly the product can be made.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Cost:</strong> the expense of heating, cooling, compressing gases,
      building equipment, and operating the plant.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Safety and practical operation:</strong> whether the chosen
      temperature and pressure can be maintained reliably.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    The conditions that give the highest possible equilibrium yield are not
    always the most economical conditions for manufacturing a product quickly
    on a large scale.
  </p>

  <h3>2. The Haber process: manufacturing ammonia</h3>

  <p>
    The Haber process manufactures ammonia, NH₃, from nitrogen and hydrogen.
    The balanced reversible equation is:
  </p>

  <p>
    N₂(g) + 3H₂(g) ⇌ 2NH₃(g)     ΔH &lt; 0
  </p>

  <p>
    The negative enthalpy change means the forward reaction is exothermic:
    it releases heat.
  </p>

  <h3>Effect of pressure in the Haber process</h3>

  <p>
    There are 4 gaseous moles on the left (1 N₂ + 3 H₂) and 2 gaseous moles
    on the right (2 NH₃). Increasing pressure favours the side with fewer
    gaseous moles, so it shifts equilibrium towards ammonia.
  </p>

  <p>
    High pressure improves the equilibrium yield of ammonia. However, very
    high pressure requires more expensive, stronger equipment and increases
    operating demands. Industry therefore selects a high but practical pressure
    rather than increasing pressure without limit.
  </p>

  <h3>Effect of temperature in the Haber process</h3>

  <p>
    The forward reaction is exothermic. Lowering the temperature favours the
    forward direction and improves the equilibrium yield of ammonia. However,
    low temperatures make the reaction too slow for efficient industrial
    production.
  </p>

  <p>
    A temperature around 450°C is commonly taught as the industrial compromise
    for the Haber process. It provides a useful reaction rate while retaining
    a reasonable equilibrium yield. The precise operating conditions can vary
    with plant design and operating requirements.
  </p>

  <h3>Why an iron catalyst is used</h3>

  <p>
    An iron catalyst increases the rates of the forward and reverse reactions.
    Equilibrium is reached more quickly, allowing more efficient production
    over time.
  </p>

  <p>
    The catalyst does <strong>not</strong> change the equilibrium position,
    the equilibrium constant, or the theoretical equilibrium yield at a fixed
    temperature. It helps the system reach equilibrium faster.
  </p>

  <h3>Removing and recycling materials</h3>

  <p>
    Ammonia can be separated from the reaction mixture as it forms. Removing
    ammonia reduces the amount of product in the reacting system, so the
    equilibrium responds by favouring further ammonia formation.
  </p>

  <p>
    Unreacted nitrogen and hydrogen can be recycled into the reactor. Recycling
    reduces waste and helps make the process more efficient.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Choosing Haber process conditions</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Explain the expected effects of increasing pressure, lowering temperature, and adding an iron catalyst to the Haber process.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1: Increasing pressure.</strong></p>

  <p>
    The reactant side has 4 gaseous moles and the product side has 2.
    Increasing pressure favours the product side, increasing the equilibrium
    tendency towards ammonia.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2: Lowering temperature.</strong></p>

  <p>
    The forward reaction is exothermic. Lowering temperature favours the
    heat-producing direction, so it favours ammonia at equilibrium. However,
    the reaction rate decreases at lower temperature.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3: Adding an iron catalyst.</strong></p>

  <p>
    The catalyst speeds up both forward and reverse reactions and allows
    equilibrium to be reached more quickly. It does not shift equilibrium.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> High pressure and lower temperature favour the equilibrium yield of ammonia, but practical operation uses a compromise temperature and pressure. The catalyst improves the rate of reaching equilibrium.</p>

  <h3>3. The Contact process: manufacturing sulfuric acid</h3>

  <p>
    In the Contact process, sulfur dioxide reacts with oxygen to form sulfur
    trioxide. The balanced reversible equation is:
  </p>

  <p>
    2SO₂(g) + O₂(g) ⇌ 2SO₃(g)     ΔH &lt; 0
  </p>

  <p>
    The forward reaction is exothermic because it releases heat.
  </p>

  <h3>Effect of pressure in the Contact process</h3>

  <p>
    The left side contains 3 gaseous moles (2 SO₂ + 1 O₂), while the right
    side contains 2 gaseous moles (2 SO₃). Increasing pressure therefore
    favours the formation of sulfur trioxide.
  </p>

  <p>
    However, the gain from using substantially higher pressure may not justify
    the extra cost and complexity of pressure-resistant equipment. The Contact
    process is commonly operated at a moderate temperature and near atmospheric
    or slightly elevated pressure, depending on plant design.
  </p>

  <h3>Effect of temperature and the catalyst</h3>

  <p>
    Because the forward reaction is exothermic, a lower temperature favours
    sulfur trioxide at equilibrium. However, the reaction would proceed too
    slowly at a very low temperature.
  </p>

  <p>
    A temperature around 450°C is commonly taught as a compromise between
    equilibrium yield and reaction rate. Vanadium(V) oxide, V₂O₅, is used as
    a catalyst to speed up the reaction.
  </p>

  <p>
    The catalyst helps the system reach equilibrium faster; it does not increase
    the equilibrium yield at a fixed temperature.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Evaluating conditions for the Contact process</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Explain why the Contact process uses a catalyst and a compromise temperature rather than simply operating at the lowest possible temperature.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1: Consider equilibrium yield.</strong></p>

  <p>
    The forward reaction is exothermic. Lower temperature favours the formation
    of SO₃ at equilibrium.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2: Consider reaction rate.</strong></p>

  <p>
    Lowering the temperature too far makes the reaction slow, reducing the
    rate of production.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3: Consider the catalyst.</strong></p>

  <p>
    V₂O₅ speeds up the reaction so equilibrium can be reached more quickly.
    It does not change the equilibrium position.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> A compromise temperature gives a useful rate and equilibrium yield, while the catalyst speeds up the reaction without changing the equilibrium yield.</p>

  <h3>4. Comparing the Haber and Contact processes</h3>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Feature</th>
        <th>Haber process</th>
        <th>Contact process</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>Product</td>
        <td>Ammonia, NH₃</td>
        <td>Sulfur trioxide, SO₃</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Reversible equation</td>
        <td>N₂ + 3H₂ ⇌ 2NH₃</td>
        <td>2SO₂ + O₂ ⇌ 2SO₃</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Forward reaction</td>
        <td>Exothermic</td>
        <td>Exothermic</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Gaseous moles: left → right</td>
        <td>4 → 2</td>
        <td>3 → 2</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Pressure effect</td>
        <td>Higher pressure favours NH₃</td>
        <td>Higher pressure favours SO₃</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Commonly taught catalyst</td>
        <td>Iron</td>
        <td>Vanadium(V) oxide, V₂O₅</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Temperature consideration</td>
        <td>Lower temperature favours yield but slows rate</td>
        <td>Lower temperature favours yield but slows rate</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h3>5. Why industrial conditions are compromises</h3>

  <p>
    Industrial equilibrium is not about maximising one factor while ignoring
    everything else. A condition that improves equilibrium yield may make
    production too slow or expensive.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Temperature:</strong> choose a compromise between equilibrium
      yield and reaction rate.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Pressure:</strong> consider the yield benefit alongside equipment
      costs and practical operating limits.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Catalyst:</strong> use it to increase reaction rates, not to shift
      equilibrium.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Product removal:</strong> where practical, remove product to
      encourage further formation.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Recycling:</strong> return suitable unreacted reactants to the
      reactor to reduce waste.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Correcting an industrial chemistry misconception</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> A learner claims, "Adding more catalyst and lowering the temperature always increases the equilibrium yield of an exothermic industrial reaction." Evaluate the statement.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1: Evaluate the temperature claim.</strong></p>

  <p>
    Lowering temperature favours the exothermic forward direction, provided
    the reaction is considered at equilibrium. It can therefore increase the
    equilibrium yield of the forward product.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2: Evaluate the rate consequence.</strong></p>

  <p>
    Lowering temperature generally reduces reaction rate. The yield advantage
    may be outweighed by slow production.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3: Evaluate the catalyst claim.</strong></p>

  <p>
    A catalyst increases the rates of both directions. It does not change
    equilibrium composition at a fixed temperature.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> The statement is incorrect. Lower temperature may favour the exothermic product at equilibrium, but can slow production. Adding a catalyst speeds up the attainment of equilibrium without increasing the equilibrium yield.</p>

  <h3>6. How to answer industrial equilibrium exam questions</h3>

  <p>
    For a full explanation, identify the reversible equation and whether the
    forward reaction is exothermic or endothermic. Count gaseous moles if
    pressure is involved. Then distinguish equilibrium yield from reaction
    rate and explain why the industrial conditions are practical compromises.
  </p>

  <p>
    Never write that a catalyst shifts equilibrium or increases the equilibrium
    yield. State that it speeds up the forward and reverse reactions and helps
    equilibrium be reached faster.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Industrial Equilibrium', 15),
  (954, 954, '<h2>Collision Theory</h2>

  <p>Collision theory explains how chemical reactions occur.</p>

  <p>For particles to react, they must:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>collide with each other,</li>
    <li>have enough energy, and</li>
    <li>collide in a suitable orientation.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>A collision that produces a reaction is called an <strong>effective collision</strong>.</p>

  <p>Simply colliding is not enough. If the particles do not have sufficient energy or the correct orientation, no reaction occurs.</p>

  <h3>Example</h3>
  <p>When hydrogen and oxygen react to form water, their particles must collide with sufficient energy and suitable orientation for bonds to break and new bonds to form.</p>

  <p><strong>Key idea:</strong> More effective collisions per second produce a faster reaction.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Collision Theory', 15),
  (955, 955, '<h2>Activation Energy</h2>

  <p><strong>Activation energy</strong> is the minimum energy that reacting particles must have for a successful reaction to occur.</p>

  <p>It is represented by <strong>E<sub>a</sub></strong>.</p>

  <p>If particles collide with energy below the activation energy, the collision does not produce a reaction.</p>

  <p>If particles collide with energy equal to or greater than the activation energy and have suitable orientation, the collision can be effective.</p>

  <h3>Important relationship</h3>
  <p>Higher activation energy means fewer particles have enough energy to react under the same conditions.</p>

  <p>A catalyst provides an alternative reaction pathway with a lower activation energy.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Activation Energy', 15),
  (956, 956, '<h2>Effective Collisions</h2>

  <p>An <strong>effective collision</strong> is a collision between reacting particles that results in a chemical reaction.</p>

  <p>For a collision to be effective:</p>
  <ol>
    <li>The particles must collide.</li>
    <li>They must have energy equal to or greater than the activation energy.</li>
    <li>They must have a suitable orientation.</li>
  </ol>

  <p>Increasing the number of effective collisions per second increases the rate of reaction.</p>

  <h3>Effect of Temperature</h3>
  <p>When temperature increases, particles move faster and have more kinetic energy. A greater fraction of particles can therefore reach or exceed the activation energy.</p>

  <h3>Effect of a Catalyst</h3>
  <p>A catalyst provides an alternative pathway with lower activation energy. This allows a greater proportion of collisions to become effective.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Effective Collisions', 15),
  (957, 957, '
  <h2>Breaking Bonds</h2>

  <h3>1. What is a chemical bond?</h3>

  <p>
    A chemical bond is an attractive interaction that holds atoms or ions
    together in a substance. In a covalent molecule such as hydrogen, H₂,
    the two hydrogen atoms are held together by a covalent bond formed from
    a shared pair of electrons.
  </p>

  <p>
    Chemical reactions involve changes in the bonds between atoms. Some
    existing bonds must be broken, and new bonds may form as products are
    produced.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Why does breaking a bond require energy?</h3>

  <p>
    Atoms in a chemical bond attract one another. To separate the bonded
    atoms, energy must be supplied to overcome the attractive interaction
    that holds them together.
  </p>

  <p>
    Therefore, <strong>breaking a chemical bond always absorbs energy</strong>.
    The energy enters the particles being separated. Bond breaking is
    consequently an endothermic process when considered on its own.
  </p>

  <p>
    Consider a hydrogen molecule:
  </p>

  <p><strong>H₂(g) → 2H(g)</strong></p>

  <p>
    One hydrogen molecule contains one H–H bond. Energy must be supplied
    to separate its two bonded hydrogen atoms. The atoms on the right are
    separate gaseous atoms, not hydrogen molecules.
  </p>

  <h3>3. What is bond energy?</h3>

  <p>
    Bond energy is the energy required to break a specified type of chemical
    bond. In school-level bond-energy calculations, values are commonly
    expressed in kilojoules per mole (kJ/mol).
  </p>

  <p>
    For gaseous covalent substances, a bond enthalpy is commonly defined as
    the enthalpy change required to break one mole of a specified type of
    bond in gaseous molecules. Tabulated values are often average bond
    enthalpies because the energy needed to break a particular type of bond
    can depend on the molecule in which it occurs.
  </p>

  <p>
    For example, a bond energy of 436 kJ/mol for H–H means that approximately
    436 kJ is required to break one mole of H–H bonds in gaseous hydrogen
    molecules under the stated convention.
  </p>

  <p>
    The unit is important: kJ/mol describes energy per mole of bonds, not
    the energy required to break just one bond or an entire mole of an
    unspecified substance.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Bond energy and the strength of a bond</h3>

  <p>
    Bond energy provides a way to compare how much energy is required to
    break bonds. Under comparable conditions, a bond with a higher bond
    energy generally requires more energy to break than a bond with a
    lower bond energy.
  </p>

  <p>
    For example, if bond A has a bond energy of 350 kJ/mol and bond B has
    a bond energy of 600 kJ/mol, breaking one mole of bond B requires
    250 kJ more energy than breaking one mole of bond A.
  </p>

  <p>
    Do not assume that every bond between two different atoms has the same
    energy. Bond energies depend on the types of atoms, the bonding
    environment and the particular molecule.
  </p>

  <h3>5. Calculating the energy needed to break bonds</h3>

  <p>
    When the energy needed to break one mole of a specified bond is known,
    the energy required for a given amount of those bonds can be calculated
    using:
  </p>

  <p><strong>Total energy absorbed = number of moles of bonds × bond energy</strong></p>

  <p>
    In symbols:
  </p>

  <p><strong>E = n × BE</strong></p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>E</strong> = total energy absorbed, in kJ.</li>
    <li><strong>n</strong> = amount of bonds broken, in mol.</li>
    <li><strong>BE</strong> = bond energy, in kJ/mol.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    The number of moles in this formula refers to moles of the particular
    bonds being broken. If a molecule contains several bonds of the same
    type, every bond must be counted.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Understanding bond breaking</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Explain why energy is absorbed when a hydrogen molecule breaks down into hydrogen atoms.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1: Identify the bond.</strong></p>

  <p>
    A hydrogen molecule, H₂, contains one covalent H–H bond.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2: Identify what must happen.</strong></p>

  <p>
    The two bonded hydrogen atoms must be separated. Energy must be supplied
    to overcome the attraction associated with the bond.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3: State the energy change.</strong></p>

  <p>
    The process is:
  </p>

  <p><strong>H₂(g) → 2H(g)</strong></p>

  <p>
    Energy is absorbed, so breaking the H–H bond is endothermic.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Energy is absorbed because energy must be supplied to separate the bonded hydrogen atoms.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Calculating energy for several moles of bonds</h3>

  <p>
    <strong>Question:</strong> An H–H bond has a bond energy of 436 kJ/mol.
    Calculate the energy required to break 3 mol of H–H bonds.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1: Write the known values.</strong></p>

  <ul>
    <li>Bond energy = 436 kJ/mol</li>
    <li>Amount of bonds = 3 mol</li>
  </ul>

  <p><strong>Step 2: Write the formula.</strong></p>

  <p><strong>E = n × BE</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Step 3: Substitute the values.</strong></p>

  <p><strong>E = 3 × 436</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Step 4: Calculate and include the unit.</strong></p>

  <p><strong>E = 1308 kJ</strong></p>

  <p>
    <strong>Answer:</strong> 1308 kJ of energy is absorbed to break 3 mol
    of H–H bonds.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Correcting a bond-energy misconception</h3>

  <p>
    <strong>Question:</strong> A learner claims that a bond with an energy
    of 600 kJ/mol releases 600 kJ when it is broken. Explain the error.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1: Interpret the unit.</strong></p>

  <p>
    The value 600 kJ/mol represents the energy required to break one mole
    of the specified bonds.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2: Identify the direction of energy transfer.</strong></p>

  <p>
    Breaking bonds requires an energy input. Therefore, energy is absorbed,
    not released.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3: State the correct interpretation.</strong></p>

  <p>
    Breaking one mole of these bonds absorbs 600 kJ. Forming the corresponding
    bonds releases approximately the same amount under the same bond-energy
    convention.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Answer:</strong> The learner has reversed the direction of energy
    transfer. Bond breaking absorbs energy; bond formation releases energy.
  </p>

  <h3>6. Common mistakes to avoid</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Wrong:</strong> Breaking a bond releases energy.
      <strong>Correct:</strong> Breaking a bond absorbs energy.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Wrong:</strong> 436 kJ/mol means breaking any amount of H–H
      bonds always requires 436 kJ.
      <strong>Correct:</strong> 436 kJ is required per mole of the specified
      bonds; scale the value according to the amount broken.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Wrong:</strong> Bond energy is the energy change of the whole
      chemical reaction.
      <strong>Correct:</strong> Bond energy describes a specified bond.
      A reaction''s overall energy change depends on both bonds broken and
      bonds formed.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Wrong:</strong> The coefficient of a molecule automatically
      gives the number of every type of bond without inspecting its formula.
      <strong>Correct:</strong> Count the actual bonds present in each
      molecule before calculating total energy.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>7. The essential rule</h3>

  <p>
    <strong>Breaking bonds absorbs energy.</strong> The stronger the bond
    in terms of bond energy, the more energy is generally required to break
    one mole of those bonds. This rule is essential for understanding how
    to calculate the energy changes of chemical reactions.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Breaking Bonds', 15),
  (958, 958, '
  <h2>Making Bonds</h2>

  <h3>1. What happens when a chemical bond forms?</h3>

  <p>
    A chemical bond is an attractive interaction that holds atoms or ions
    together. When separate atoms form a stable chemical bond, the resulting
    bonded arrangement has a lower energy than the separated atoms, under
    the same conditions.
  </p>

  <p>
    The excess energy is transferred to the surroundings. Therefore,
    <strong>forming a chemical bond releases energy</strong>.
  </p>

  <p>
    Consider the formation of a hydrogen molecule:
  </p>

  <p><strong>2H(g) → H₂(g)</strong></p>

  <p>
    Two separate gaseous hydrogen atoms combine to form one hydrogen molecule.
    As the H–H bond forms, energy is released.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Why does bond formation release energy?</h3>

  <p>
    When atoms interact, attractive and repulsive forces act between their
    nuclei and electrons. At a stable bond length, the bonded arrangement
    has a lower energy than the separated atoms.
  </p>

  <p>
    The difference in energy is released when the bond forms. Conversely,
    energy must be supplied to separate the atoms again. This is why bond
    formation and bond breaking have opposite energy changes.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Breaking a bond:</strong> energy is absorbed.</li>
    <li><strong>Forming a bond:</strong> energy is released.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>3. Understanding bond energy during formation</h3>

  <p>
    Bond energy is conventionally described as the energy required to break
    a specified bond. The corresponding bond-formation process releases
    approximately the same magnitude of energy when the same gaseous species
    and bond-energy convention are considered.
  </p>

  <p>
    For example, if the H–H bond energy is taken as 436 kJ/mol, forming
    one mole of H–H bonds from gaseous hydrogen atoms releases approximately
    436 kJ.
  </p>

  <p>
    The direction matters. The magnitude is 436 kJ/mol, but the energy
    transfer is in the opposite direction:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>Breaking H–H bonds absorbs energy.</li>
    <li>Forming H–H bonds releases energy.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>4. Calculating energy released when bonds form</h3>

  <p>
    If the energy released when one mole of a specified bond forms is known,
    calculate the total energy released using:
  </p>

  <p><strong>Total energy released = number of moles of bonds formed × bond energy</strong></p>

  <p><strong>E = n × BE</strong></p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>E</strong> = total energy released, in kJ.</li>
    <li><strong>n</strong> = amount of bonds formed, in mol.</li>
    <li><strong>BE</strong> = bond energy, in kJ/mol.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    This calculation assumes the specified bond-energy value applies to the
    bond being formed. In a chemical reaction, the total energy released
    must be found by counting all the bonds formed.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Understanding bond formation</h3>

  <p>
    <strong>Question:</strong> Explain what happens to energy when two
    gaseous hydrogen atoms form a hydrogen molecule.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1: Identify the particles.</strong></p>

  <p>
    The reactants are two separate hydrogen atoms, represented as 2H(g).
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2: Identify the change.</strong></p>

  <p>
    The atoms come together and form an H–H covalent bond:
  </p>

  <p><strong>2H(g) → H₂(g)</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Step 3: Determine the direction of energy transfer.</strong></p>

  <p>
    The bonded molecule has a lower energy than the separated atoms.
    The energy difference is released to the surroundings.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Energy is released as the H–H bond forms.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Calculating energy released</h3>

  <p>
    <strong>Question:</strong> Forming one mole of a specified type of bond
    releases 250 kJ. Calculate the energy released when 3 mol of these bonds
    form.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1: Write the known values.</strong></p>

  <ul>
    <li>Energy released per mole = 250 kJ/mol</li>
    <li>Amount of bonds formed = 3 mol</li>
  </ul>

  <p><strong>Step 2: Write the formula.</strong></p>

  <p><strong>E = n × BE</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Step 3: Substitute the values.</strong></p>

  <p><strong>E = 3 × 250</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Step 4: Calculate.</strong></p>

  <p><strong>E = 750 kJ</strong></p>

  <p>
    <strong>Answer:</strong> 750 kJ is released when 3 mol of these bonds form.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Correcting a misconception about bond energy</h3>

  <p>
    <strong>Question:</strong> A learner states that because breaking one
    mole of H–H bonds absorbs 436 kJ, forming one mole of H–H bonds must
    also absorb 436 kJ. Explain the error.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1: Recall what bond energy represents.</strong></p>

  <p>
    The value 436 kJ/mol describes the energy required to break one mole
    of H–H bonds in gaseous hydrogen molecules.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2: Reverse the process.</strong></p>

  <p>
    Formation is the reverse of breaking for the same specified bond and
    corresponding gaseous states.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3: Reverse the direction of energy transfer.</strong></p>

  <p>
    If breaking the bonds absorbs approximately 436 kJ/mol, forming those
    bonds releases approximately 436 kJ/mol.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Answer:</strong> The learner has the direction of energy transfer
    wrong. Bond formation releases energy rather than absorbing it.
  </p>

  <h3>5. Strong bonds and energy released</h3>

  <p>
    Under comparable conditions, forming a bond with a higher bond energy
    releases more energy per mole than forming a bond with a lower bond
    energy, when the corresponding bond-energy values are applicable.
  </p>

  <p>
    For example, forming one mole of bonds with a bond energy of 600 kJ/mol
    releases approximately 250 kJ more than forming one mole of bonds with
    a bond energy of 350 kJ/mol.
  </p>

  <p>
    Remember that this comparison is about the energy change associated
    with the specified bonds. The overall energy change of a chemical
    reaction depends on both the bonds broken and the bonds formed.
  </p>

  <h3>6. Why bond formation matters in chemical reactions</h3>

  <p>
    Most chemical reactions involve breaking bonds in reactants and forming
    bonds in products. Breaking the reactant bonds absorbs energy, while
    forming the product bonds releases energy.
  </p>

  <p>
    If bond formation releases more energy than bond breaking absorbs, the
    reaction has a net release of energy and is exothermic. If bond breaking
    absorbs more energy than bond formation releases, the reaction has a
    net absorption of energy and is endothermic.
  </p>

  <p>
    The energy released by forming bonds must therefore be considered
    alongside the energy absorbed by breaking bonds. Looking at bond
    formation alone does not determine the energy change of the whole
    reaction.
  </p>

  <h3>7. Common mistakes to avoid</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Wrong:</strong> Forming bonds absorbs energy.
      <strong>Correct:</strong> Forming bonds releases energy.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Wrong:</strong> A bond energy is always energy released.
      <strong>Correct:</strong> Bond energy is conventionally the energy
      required to break a specified bond.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Wrong:</strong> If bonds form in a reaction, the whole reaction
      must be exothermic.
      <strong>Correct:</strong> The reaction''s overall energy change depends
      on the energy absorbed breaking bonds and released forming bonds.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Wrong:</strong> The number of moles of molecules always equals
      the number of moles of bonds formed.
      <strong>Correct:</strong> Count the number of relevant bonds in each
      molecule and multiply by the amount of molecules.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>8. The essential rule</h3>

  <p>
    <strong>Making bonds releases energy.</strong> The released energy is
    associated with the formation of a lower-energy bonded arrangement.
    To determine whether an entire reaction releases or absorbs energy,
    compare the energy released by all bonds formed with the energy absorbed
    by all bonds broken.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Making Bonds', 15),
  (959, 959, '
  <h2>Bond Energy Calculations</h2>

  <h3>1. What is the energy change of a chemical reaction?</h3>

  <p>
    During a chemical reaction, bonds in the reactants are broken and new
    bonds form in the products. Breaking bonds absorbs energy, while forming
    bonds releases energy.
  </p>

  <p>
    The overall enthalpy change, ΔH, depends on the difference between
    the energy absorbed during bond breaking and the energy released
    during bond formation.
  </p>

  <p><strong>ΔH = total bond energy of bonds broken − total bond energy of bonds formed</strong></p>

  <p>
    Using the summation symbol:
  </p>

  <p><strong>ΔH ≈ ΣBE(bonds broken) − ΣBE(bonds formed)</strong></p>

  <p>
    This method gives an estimate when average bond enthalpies are used.
    The approximation sign is important because tabulated average bond
    enthalpies may not exactly represent the bonds in every molecule.
  </p>

  <h3>2. What does each part of the formula mean?</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>ΣBE(bonds broken):</strong> the total energy absorbed to break
      all the relevant bonds in the reactants.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>ΣBE(bonds formed):</strong> the total energy released when all
      the relevant bonds in the products form.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>ΔH:</strong> the estimated enthalpy change for the reaction
      as written, using the specified bond-energy data.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Bond energies are usually expressed in kJ/mol. When calculating the
    energy change for a balanced reaction as written, count the bonds
    represented by the equation''s coefficients and molecular formulae.
    The resulting ΔH is in kJ per mole of reaction as written.
  </p>

  <h3>3. How to count bonds correctly</h3>

  <p>
    A molecular formula tells you how many atoms of each element are
    present in one molecule. A structural formula shows which atoms are
    connected and therefore helps you identify the bonds.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>H₂ contains one H–H bond per molecule.</li>
    <li>Cl₂ contains one Cl–Cl bond per molecule.</li>
    <li>H₂O contains two O–H bonds per molecule.</li>
    <li>NH₃ contains three N–H bonds per molecule.</li>
    <li>CH₄ contains four C–H bonds per molecule.</li>
    <li>O₂ contains one O=O double bond per molecule.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    If an equation contains 2H₂O, there are two water molecules. Together,
    they contain 2 × 2 = 4 O–H bonds. Counting only one water molecule
    would underestimate the total energy released during bond formation.
  </p>

  <h3>4. The correct calculation procedure</h3>

  <ol>
    <li>Write the balanced chemical equation.</li>
    <li>Identify every type of bond in the reactants.</li>
    <li>Count each bond, including the effect of coefficients.</li>
    <li>Multiply each bond count by its bond energy and add the results.</li>
    <li>Repeat the process for the product bonds.</li>
    <li>Subtract total energy of bonds formed from total energy of bonds broken.</li>
    <li>Use the sign of ΔH to classify the reaction.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>5. Interpreting the sign of ΔH</h3>

  <p>
    <strong>Negative ΔH:</strong> more energy is released forming bonds than
    is absorbed breaking bonds. The reaction is exothermic and releases
    energy overall.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Positive ΔH:</strong> more energy is absorbed breaking bonds than
    is released forming bonds. The reaction is endothermic and absorbs
    energy overall.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Zero ΔH:</strong> the calculated energy absorbed and released
    are equal, giving no net enthalpy change in the calculation.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Calculating ΔH for hydrogen and chlorine</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Estimate ΔH for the reaction:</p>

  <p><strong>H₂(g) + Cl₂(g) → 2HCl(g)</strong></p>

  <p>Use these average bond energies:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>H–H = 436 kJ/mol</li>
    <li>Cl–Cl = 243 kJ/mol</li>
    <li>H–Cl = 431 kJ/mol</li>
  </ul>

  <p><strong>Step 1: Count bonds broken.</strong></p>

  <p>
    One H₂ molecule contains one H–H bond. One Cl₂ molecule contains
    one Cl–Cl bond.
  </p>

  <p>
    Energy absorbed = 436 + 243 = 679 kJ.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2: Count bonds formed.</strong></p>

  <p>
    The equation produces 2HCl. Each HCl molecule contains one H–Cl bond,
    so two H–Cl bonds form.
  </p>

  <p>
    Energy released = 2 × 431 = 862 kJ.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3: Apply the formula.</strong></p>

  <p>
    ΔH ≈ energy of bonds broken − energy of bonds formed
  </p>

  <p>
    ΔH ≈ 679 − 862
  </p>

  <p><strong>ΔH ≈ −183 kJ/mol of reaction</strong></p>

  <p>
    <strong>Conclusion:</strong> The reaction is exothermic because more
    energy is released forming H–Cl bonds than is absorbed breaking H–H
    and Cl–Cl bonds.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Counting multiple bonds in ammonia formation</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Estimate ΔH for:</p>

  <p><strong>N₂(g) + 3H₂(g) → 2NH₃(g)</strong></p>

  <p>Use these average bond energies:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>N≡N = 945 kJ/mol</li>
    <li>H–H = 436 kJ/mol</li>
    <li>N–H = 391 kJ/mol</li>
  </ul>

  <p><strong>Step 1: Count bonds broken.</strong></p>

  <p>
    One N₂ molecule contains one N≡N triple bond.
    Three H₂ molecules contain three H–H bonds.
  </p>

  <p>
    Energy absorbed = 945 + (3 × 436)
  </p>

  <p>
    Energy absorbed = 945 + 1308 = 2253 kJ.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2: Count bonds formed.</strong></p>

  <p>
    Each NH₃ molecule contains three N–H bonds. Two NH₃ molecules contain
    2 × 3 = 6 N–H bonds.
  </p>

  <p>
    Energy released = 6 × 391 = 2346 kJ.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3: Calculate ΔH.</strong></p>

  <p>
    ΔH ≈ 2253 − 2346
  </p>

  <p><strong>ΔH ≈ −93 kJ/mol of reaction</strong></p>

  <p>
    <strong>Conclusion:</strong> The estimated reaction is exothermic.
    Counting all six N–H bonds is essential; counting only one NH₃ molecule
    would give the wrong total.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Diagnosing a sign error</h3>

  <p>
    <strong>Question:</strong> A learner calculates that breaking reactant
    bonds absorbs 1200 kJ and forming product bonds releases 900 kJ.
    The learner reports ΔH = −300 kJ. Find the error and classify the reaction.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1: Identify the two totals.</strong></p>

  <ul>
    <li>Energy absorbed breaking bonds = 1200 kJ.</li>
    <li>Energy released forming bonds = 900 kJ.</li>
  </ul>

  <p><strong>Step 2: Apply the correct subtraction order.</strong></p>

  <p>
    ΔH = energy absorbed − energy released
  </p>

  <p>
    ΔH = 1200 − 900 = +300 kJ.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3: Interpret the sign.</strong></p>

  <p>
    The result is positive. More energy is absorbed breaking bonds than
    is released forming bonds.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Conclusion:</strong> ΔH = +300 kJ. The reaction is endothermic.
    The learner reversed the subtraction order.
  </p>

  <h3>6. Common errors in bond-energy calculations</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Reversing the formula:</strong> always subtract bonds formed
      from bonds broken.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Ignoring coefficients:</strong> a coefficient multiplies the
      number of molecules and therefore the number of their bonds.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Counting atoms instead of bonds:</strong> identify the actual
      connections between atoms.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Forgetting multiple bonds:</strong> a triple bond counts as
      one N≡N bond, using the triple-bond energy supplied.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Misreading the sign:</strong> negative ΔH means exothermic;
      positive ΔH means endothermic.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Reporting false precision:</strong> bond-energy calculations
      give estimates when average bond enthalpies are used.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>7. Final method to remember</h3>

  <p>
    <strong>Count → Multiply → Add → Subtract → Interpret.</strong>
  </p>

  <p>
    Count all bonds broken and formed. Multiply each count by its bond
    energy. Add the totals for each side. Subtract the energy of bonds
    formed from the energy of bonds broken. Finally, interpret the sign
    of ΔH.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Bond Energy Calculations', 15),
