    <h2>Nouns</h2>

    <p>A <strong>noun</strong> names a person, place, animal, object,
    substance, activity, or idea.</p>

    <h3>1. Nouns in sentences</h3>

    <p>Consider this sentence:</p>

    <blockquote>
      The teacher carried a book into the classroom.
    </blockquote>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>teacher</strong> names a person.</li>
      <li><strong>book</strong> names an object.</li>
      <li><strong>classroom</strong> names a place.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Nouns can perform different jobs in a sentence. A noun may
    identify who performs an action, what receives an action, or
    what something is about.</p>

    <h3>2. Nouns as subjects and objects</h3>

    <p>In the sentence below, identify the nouns by their roles:</p>

    <blockquote>
      Amina opened the window.
    </blockquote>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>Amina</strong> is the subject because she performs
      the action.</li>
      <li><strong>window</strong> is the object because it receives
      the action.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>A noun is not identified only by its position. Its function
    depends on how it is used in the sentence.</p>

    <h3>3. Nouns that name ideas and qualities</h3>

    <p>Not every noun is something that can be touched.</p>

    <blockquote>
      Honesty earns respect.
    </blockquote>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>Honesty</strong> names an idea or quality.</li>
      <li><strong>respect</strong> names a feeling or attitude.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Words such as <em>honesty</em>, <em>freedom</em>,
    <em>courage</em> and <em>knowledge</em> are nouns even though
    they cannot be physically held.</p>

    <h3>4. How to identify a noun</h3>

    <p>When examining a sentence:</p>

    <ol>
      <li>Ask what person, place, thing, substance or idea is being named.</li>
      <li>Check how the word functions in the sentence.</li>
      <li>Do not assume that every word after <em>a</em>, <em>an</em>
      or <em>the</em> is automatically a noun. For example, in
      <em>the tall building</em>, <em>tall</em> describes the noun
      <em>building</em>.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Worked example</h3>

    <p><strong>Sentence:</strong> The students discussed pollution
    after the lesson.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the people involved:
    <em>students</em>.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Identify what they discussed:
    <em>pollution</em>.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Identify the event or period after
    which they talked: <em>lesson</em>.</p>

    <p><strong>Answer:</strong> <em>students</em>, <em>pollution</em>
    and <em>lesson</em> are nouns in this sentence.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Nouns', 15),
  (1737, 1737, '
    <h2>Pronouns</h2>

    <p>A <strong>pronoun</strong> is a word used in place of a noun
    or noun phrase, or to refer to someone or something identified
    by the context.</p>

    <h3>1. Why pronouns are used</h3>

    <p>Compare these sentences:</p>

    <blockquote>
      Wanjiku collected Wanjiku''s books because Wanjiku needed the books.
    </blockquote>

    <blockquote>
      Wanjiku collected her books because she needed them.
    </blockquote>

    <p>The second version uses <em>her</em>, <em>she</em> and
    <em>them</em> to avoid unnecessary repetition.</p>

    <h3>2. Personal pronouns</h3>

    <p>Personal pronouns refer to people or things. Their form
    changes according to their grammatical role.</p>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>Subject forms:</strong> I, you, he, she, it, we, they.</li>
      <li><strong>Object forms:</strong> me, you, him, her, it, us, them.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Compare:</p>

    <ul>
      <li><em>She</em> called the teacher. — <em>She</em> is the subject.</li>
      <li>The teacher called <em>her</em>. — <em>Her</em> is the object.</li>
      <li><em>They</em> invited <em>us</em>. — <em>They</em> is the subject;
      <em>us</em> is the object.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>3. Possessive forms</h3>

    <p>Possessive forms show ownership or a relationship.</p>

    <ul>
      <li>This is <em>my</em> book. — <em>My</em> comes before the noun.</li>
      <li>This book is <em>mine</em>. — <em>Mine</em> stands independently.</li>
      <li>That is <em>their</em> classroom.</li>
      <li>That classroom is <em>theirs</em>.</li>
    </ul>

    <p><strong>Important distinction:</strong> Words such as
    <em>my</em>, <em>your</em> and <em>their</em> are possessive
    determiners when they accompany nouns. Words such as
    <em>mine</em>, <em>yours</em> and <em>theirs</em> can stand
    independently as possessive pronouns.</p>

    <h3>4. Pronouns must refer clearly</h3>

    <p>The noun or noun phrase a pronoun refers to is called its
    <strong>antecedent</strong>.</p>

    <blockquote>
      Brian told Kevin that he had won the race.
    </blockquote>

    <p>Who won the race: Brian or Kevin? The pronoun <em>he</em>
    could refer to either person. The sentence is ambiguous.</p>

    <p>A clearer version is:</p>

    <blockquote>
      Brian told Kevin, “I won the race.”
    </blockquote>

    <p>Use a pronoun only when its intended reference is clear from
    the sentence or surrounding context.</p>

    <h3>Worked example</h3>

    <p><strong>Sentence:</strong> The players thanked the coach
    because she had trained them.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Find the pronouns:
    <em>she</em> and <em>them</em>.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Determine their references.
    <em>She</em> refers to the coach; <em>them</em> refers to the players.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Check the grammatical roles.
    <em>She</em> is the subject of <em>had trained</em>;
    <em>them</em> is its object.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Pronouns', 15),
  (1738, 1738, '
    <h2>Verbs</h2>

    <p>A <strong>verb</strong> expresses an action, a state or a
    condition. A verb or verb phrase forms the central part of
    what a clause says about its subject.</p>

    <h3>1. Action verbs</h3>

    <p>Action verbs express what someone or something does.</p>

    <ul>
      <li>The mechanic <em>repaired</em> the engine.</li>
      <li>The children <em>ran</em> across the field.</li>
      <li>The committee <em>discussed</em> the proposal.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>An action does not have to be physically visible. In
    “She considered the evidence,” <em>considered</em> expresses
    a mental action.</p>

    <h3>2. Linking verbs</h3>

    <p>A linking verb connects the subject to information describing
    or identifying it. It does not express an action performed on
    an object.</p>

    <ul>
      <li>The soup <em>tastes</em> salty.</li>
      <li>The learners <em>became</em> confident.</li>
      <li>Her explanation <em>seems</em> reasonable.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>In these sentences, <em>salty</em>, <em>confident</em> and
    <em>reasonable</em> describe the subjects.</p>

    <p>Some verbs can function differently depending on their use:</p>

    <ul>
      <li>The chef <em>tasted</em> the soup. — <em>Tasted</em> expresses
      an action and takes an object.</li>
      <li>The soup <em>tasted</em> salty. — <em>Tasted</em> links the
      subject to a description.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>3. Main verbs and auxiliary verbs</h3>

    <p>A <strong>main verb</strong> carries the central meaning.
    An <strong>auxiliary verb</strong> helps form tense, questions,
    negatives, aspect or voice.</p>

    <ul>
      <li>She <em>is reading</em> a novel. — <em>Reading</em> is
      the main verb; <em>is</em> is an auxiliary.</li>
      <li>They <em>have finished</em> the work. — <em>Finished</em>
      is the main verb; <em>have</em> is an auxiliary.</li>
      <li><em>Do</em> you understand the question? — <em>Do</em>
      is an auxiliary.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Some verb phrases contain more than one auxiliary:
    “The report <em>might have been completed</em>.” The main verb
    is <em>completed</em>.</p>

    <h3>4. Verbs and their objects</h3>

    <p>Some verbs take an object; others do not.</p>

    <ul>
      <li>The farmer planted <em>maize</em>. — <em>Planted</em>
      takes the object <em>maize</em>.</li>
      <li>The baby slept. — <em>Slept</em> has no direct object here.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Do not decide whether a verb takes an object from the verb
    alone. Check how it is used in the sentence.</p>

    <h3>Worked example</h3>

    <p><strong>Sentence:</strong> The students have been preparing
    for their examinations.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Find the complete verb phrase:
    <em>have been preparing</em>.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Identify the main verb:
    <em>preparing</em>.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Identify the auxiliaries:
    <em>have</em> and <em>been</em>.</p>

    <p><strong>Answer:</strong> <em>Preparing</em> carries the main
    meaning, while <em>have</em> and <em>been</em> help form the verb
    phrase.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Verbs', 15),
  (1739, 1739, '
    <h2>Adjectives</h2>

    <p>An <strong>adjective</strong> describes a noun or pronoun,
    or helps identify or distinguish the person or thing being
    referred to.</p>

    <h3>1. Describing nouns</h3>

    <p>Compare these sentences:</p>

    <blockquote>
      The student submitted an assignment.
    </blockquote>

    <blockquote>
      The diligent student submitted a difficult assignment.
    </blockquote>

    <p><em>Diligent</em> describes the student, while
    <em>difficult</em> describes the assignment. Both are adjectives.</p>

    <h3>2. Adjectives before and after nouns</h3>

    <p>An adjective often appears before the noun it describes:</p>

    <blockquote>
      The <em>narrow</em> road was flooded.
    </blockquote>

    <p>An adjective can also appear after a linking verb:</p>

    <blockquote>
      The road was <em>narrow</em>.
    </blockquote>

    <p>In both sentences, <em>narrow</em> describes the road.
    In the second sentence, it follows the linking verb <em>was</em>.</p>

    <h3>3. Adjectives that identify or distinguish</h3>

    <p>Some adjectives help specify which person or thing is meant.</p>

    <ul>
      <li><em>Those</em> books belong to the library.</li>
      <li>We need <em>three</em> volunteers.</li>
      <li>She has <em>enough</em> time.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>In traditional school grammar, words such as <em>those</em>,
    <em>three</em> and <em>enough</em> are often taught as adjectives.
    In modern grammatical analysis, many of these are classified
    as determiners or quantifiers. Their exact label depends on the
    grammar framework being used.</p>

    <h3>4. Adjectives and adverbs are different</h3>

    <p>Adjectives describe nouns or pronouns. Adverbs can describe
    verbs, adjectives or other adverbs.</p>

    <ul>
      <li>The learner gave a <em>careful</em> answer.
      — <em>Careful</em> describes the noun <em>answer</em>.</li>
      <li>The learner answered <em>carefully</em>.
      — <em>Carefully</em> describes how the learner answered.</li>
      <li>The question was <em>extremely difficult</em>.
      — <em>Extremely</em> modifies the adjective <em>difficult</em>.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Do not assume every word ending in <em>-ly</em> is an adverb.
    Words such as <em>friendly</em> and <em>lovely</em> are usually
    adjectives.</p>

    <h3>5. Choosing adjectives precisely</h3>

    <p>Choose an adjective that communicates the intended meaning.
    For example, <em>narrow</em>, <em>steep</em> and <em>slippery</em>
    describe different features of a road.</p>

    <p>A more precise description helps a reader understand the
    situation without needing unnecessary words.</p>

    <h3>Worked example</h3>

    <p><strong>Sentence:</strong> The exhausted goalkeeper made
    a remarkable save.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Find the nouns:
    <em>goalkeeper</em> and <em>save</em>.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Find the words describing those nouns.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> <em>Exhausted</em> describes the
    goalkeeper; <em>remarkable</em> describes the save.</p>

    <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The adjectives are
    <em>exhausted</em> and <em>remarkable</em>.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Adjectives', 15),
  (1740, 1740, '
    <h2>Adverbs</h2>

    <p>An <strong>adverb</strong> modifies a verb, an adjective,
    another adverb or, in some cases, a whole clause. It can tell
    us how, when, where or how often something happens, or to what
    degree something is true.</p>

    <h3>1. Adverbs of manner</h3>

    <p>Adverbs of manner describe how an action is performed.</p>

    <ul>
      <li>The athlete ran <em>quickly</em>.</li>
      <li>The nurse spoke <em>gently</em> to the patient.</li>
      <li>The learner answered <em>confidently</em>.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Many adverbs of manner end in <em>-ly</em>, but not all do.
    In “The child ran fast,” <em>fast</em> is an adverb.</p>

    <h3>2. Adverbs of time and place</h3>

    <p>Adverbs of time indicate when something happens.</p>

    <ul>
      <li>We will leave <em>tomorrow</em>.</li>
      <li>The visitors arrived <em>yesterday</em>.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Adverbs of place indicate where something happens.</p>

    <ul>
      <li>Please wait <em>outside</em>.</li>
      <li>The children looked <em>everywhere</em>.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>3. Adverbs of frequency</h3>

    <p>Adverbs of frequency tell us how often something happens.</p>

    <ul>
      <li>She <em>always</em> completes her assignments.</li>
      <li>We <em>sometimes</em> study in the library.</li>
      <li>He <em>rarely</em> misses a lesson.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Frequency adverbs usually appear before a main verb:
    “She <em>often visits</em> her grandmother.” With the verb
    <em>be</em>, they commonly follow it: “She <em>is often</em>
    late.”</p>

    <h3>4. Adverbs of degree</h3>

    <p>Adverbs of degree indicate the strength or extent of
    something.</p>

    <ul>
      <li>The water is <em>very</em> cold.</li>
      <li>The team played <em>extremely</em> well.</li>
      <li>The task was <em>almost</em> complete.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>In “extremely well,” <em>extremely</em> modifies the adverb
    <em>well</em>. In “very cold,” <em>very</em> modifies the
    adjective <em>cold</em>.</p>

    <h3>5. Adverb or adjective?</h3>

    <p>Identify what a word modifies before deciding whether it
    is an adjective or an adverb.</p>

    <ul>
      <li>The driver was <em>careful</em>. — <em>Careful</em>
      describes the driver.</li>
      <li>The driver drove <em>carefully</em>. — <em>Carefully</em>
      describes how the driver drove.</li>
      <li>The explanation was <em>clear</em>. — <em>Clear</em>
      describes the explanation.</li>
      <li>The teacher explained the rule <em>clearly</em>. —
      <em>Clearly</em> describes how the teacher explained it.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Some words can function as either adjectives or adverbs.
    In “a fast runner,” <em>fast</em> is an adjective. In “runs
    fast,” <em>fast</em> is an adverb.</p>

    <h3>6. Position can affect meaning</h3>

    <p>Adverb placement can change which part of a sentence is
    emphasised.</p>

    <ul>
      <li><em>Only</em> Amina answered the question. — Nobody else answered.</li>
      <li>Amina <em>only</em> answered the question. — She answered
      but did not do anything beyond answering.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>For precise writing, place an adverb close to the word or
    idea it is intended to modify.</p>

    <h3>Worked example</h3>

    <p><strong>Sentence:</strong> The team performed remarkably
    well during yesterday''s match.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Find the adverbs:
    <em>remarkably</em>, <em>well</em> and <em>yesterday</em>.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Identify what each modifies.
    <em>Well</em> describes how the team performed.
    <em>Remarkably</em> modifies the adverb <em>well</em>.
    <em>Yesterday</em> identifies when the match took place.</p>

    <p><strong>Answer:</strong> <em>Remarkably</em> is an adverb
    of degree, <em>well</em> is an adverb of manner, and
    <em>yesterday</em> is an adverb of time.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Adverbs', 15),
  (1741, 1741, '
    <h2>Determiners</h2>

    <p>A <strong>determiner</strong> is a word placed before a noun
    to identify it, specify which one is meant, indicate quantity,
    or show ownership.</p>

    <h3>1. Articles</h3>

    <p>The articles are <em>a</em>, <em>an</em> and <em>the</em>.</p>

    <ul>
      <li>I saw <em>a</em> bird. — The bird is not yet identified.</li>
      <li>She ate <em>an</em> orange. — Use <em>an</em> before a vowel
      sound, not simply before a vowel letter.</li>
      <li><em>The</em> bird flew away. — The bird is identifiable
      from the context.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Use <em>a</em> before a consonant sound and <em>an</em>
    before a vowel sound: <em>a university</em> begins with a
    /y/ sound, while <em>an hour</em> begins with a vowel sound.</p>

    <h3>2. Demonstrative determiners</h3>

    <p><em>This</em>, <em>that</em>, <em>these</em> and <em>those</em>
    indicate which person or thing is meant.</p>

    <ul>
      <li><em>This book</em> is mine.</li>
      <li><em>That building</em> is the library.</li>
      <li><em>These shoes</em> are new.</li>
      <li><em>Those trees</em> need water.</li>
    </ul>

    <p><em>This</em> and <em>that</em> accompany singular nouns;
    <em>these</em> and <em>those</em> accompany plural nouns.</p>

    <h3>3. Possessive determiners</h3>

    <p>Possessive determiners show who something belongs to or
    is associated with.</p>

    <ul>
      <li><em>My</em> notebook is missing.</li>
      <li><em>Your</em> answer is correct.</li>
      <li><em>Her</em> bicycle needs repair.</li>
      <li><em>Their</em> classroom is clean.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>They appear before nouns. Compare <em>my bag</em> with
    <em>mine</em>: in the second case, the possessive pronoun
    stands without a noun immediately after it.</p>

    <h3>4. Quantifiers</h3>

    <p>Quantifiers indicate amount or number.</p>

    <ul>
      <li><em>Many</em> students attended the meeting.</li>
      <li><em>Much</em> water was wasted.</li>
      <li><em>Some</em> learners arrived early.</li>
      <li><em>Few</em> seats remained.</li>
      <li><em>Enough</em> food was prepared.</li>
    </ul>

    <p><em>Many</em> and <em>few</em> normally accompany plural
    countable nouns. <em>Much</em> normally accompanies uncountable
    nouns. <em>Some</em> and <em>enough</em> can be used with both,
    depending on the noun and meaning.</p>

    <h3>5. Choosing the correct determiner</h3>

    <p>Check the noun''s number, whether it is countable, and whether
    the reader can identify the thing being discussed.</p>

    <p>For example:</p>

    <blockquote>
      There are few books on the shelf, but there is little water
      in the bottle.
    </blockquote>

    <p><em>Few</em> refers to countable books; <em>little</em>
    refers to the uncountable noun <em>water</em>.</p>

    <h3>Worked example</h3>

    <p><strong>Sentence:</strong> Those three learners brought
    their notebooks.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Find the words specifying the nouns.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> <em>Those</em> identifies which
    learners; <em>three</em> gives their number.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> <em>Their</em> shows whose notebooks
    they brought.</p>

    <p><strong>Answer:</strong> <em>Those</em>, <em>three</em>
    and <em>their</em> are determiners in this sentence.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Determiners', 15),
  (1742, 1742, '
    <h2>Prepositions</h2>

    <p>A <strong>preposition</strong> expresses a relationship
    between a noun phrase or pronoun and another part of a sentence.
    The relationship may involve time, place, direction, cause
    or other connections.</p>

    <h3>1. Prepositions of place</h3>

    <p>These indicate where something is located.</p>

    <ul>
      <li>The keys are <em>on</em> the table.</li>
      <li>The pupils waited <em>inside</em> the classroom.</li>
      <li>The shop is <em>between</em> the bank and the pharmacy.</li>
      <li>The cat hid <em>under</em> the bed.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Choose the preposition that expresses the intended spatial
    relationship. <em>Under</em> does not mean the same thing as
    <em>beside</em> or <em>behind</em>.</p>

    <h3>2. Prepositions of time</h3>

    <p><em>At</em>, <em>on</em> and <em>in</em> are commonly used
    for different kinds of time expressions.</p>

    <ul>
      <li><em>At</em> six o''clock; <em>at</em> noon.</li>
      <li><em>On</em> Monday; <em>on</em> 12 June.</li>
      <li><em>In</em> October; <em>in</em> 2026; <em>in</em> the morning.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>These are common patterns, not rules that cover every
    expression. For example, English commonly uses
    <em>at night</em> but <em>in the evening</em>.</p>

    <h3>3. Prepositions of direction and movement</h3>

    <ul>
      <li>The students walked <em>towards</em> the gate.</li>
      <li>The ball rolled <em>into</em> the room.</li>
      <li>The athlete jumped <em>over</em> the hurdle.</li>
      <li>We travelled <em>from</em> Embu <em>to</em> Nairobi.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Notice the difference between <em>in</em> and <em>into</em>.
    <em>In</em> can describe a location; <em>into</em> commonly
    describes movement from outside to inside.</p>

    <h3>4. Prepositional phrases</h3>

    <p>A prepositional phrase begins with a preposition and includes
    its complement, usually a noun phrase or pronoun.</p>

    <ul>
      <li><em>under the wooden desk</em></li>
      <li><em>after the final lesson</em></li>
      <li><em>with her classmates</em></li>
    </ul>

    <p>In “The bag under the desk belongs to Ali,” the phrase
    <em>under the desk</em> helps identify which bag is meant.</p>

    <h3>5. Prepositions in context</h3>

    <p>Some prepositions form common patterns with particular
    verbs and adjectives.</p>

    <ul>
      <li>interested <em>in</em> science</li>
      <li>depend <em>on</em> someone</li>
      <li>apologise <em>for</em> a mistake</li>
      <li>good <em>at</em> mathematics</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Learn these expressions in meaningful phrases rather than
    selecting a preposition by translating directly from another
    language.</p>

    <h3>Worked example</h3>

    <p><strong>Sentence:</strong> The visitors arrived at the
    station in the morning and travelled to Meru.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the prepositions:
    <em>at</em>, <em>in</em> and <em>to</em>.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> <em>At</em> connects the arrival
    to a location; <em>in</em> introduces a time expression.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> <em>To</em> expresses the destination
    of the journey.</p>

    <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Each preposition expresses a
    different relationship in the sentence.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Prepositions', 15),
  (1743, 1743, '
    <h2>Conjunctions</h2>

    <p>A <strong>conjunction</strong> connects words, phrases,
    clauses or sentences. The relationship it expresses may be
    addition, contrast, choice, cause, time or condition.</p>

    <h3>1. Coordinating conjunctions</h3>

    <p>The coordinating conjunctions are commonly remembered as
    <em>for, and, nor, but, or, yet, so</em>.</p>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>And:</strong> Asha bought bread <em>and</em> milk.</li>
      <li><strong>But:</strong> He studied hard, <em>but</em> he
      did not pass.</li>
      <li><strong>Or:</strong> You can walk <em>or</em> take a bus.</li>
      <li><strong>So:</strong> It was raining, <em>so</em> we stayed inside.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Coordinating conjunctions can connect elements of equal
    grammatical status, such as two nouns or two independent clauses.</p>

    <h3>2. Subordinating conjunctions</h3>

    <p>Subordinating conjunctions introduce a dependent clause
    and show how it relates to a main clause.</p>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>Cause:</strong> We stayed indoors <em>because</em>
      it was raining.</li>
      <li><strong>Time:</strong> Call me <em>when</em> you arrive.</li>
      <li><strong>Condition:</strong> <em>If</em> you practise,
      you will improve.</li>
      <li><strong>Contrast:</strong> <em>Although</em> he was tired,
      he continued working.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>A dependent clause cannot normally stand alone as a complete
    sentence in the intended construction.</p>

    <h3>3. Correlative conjunctions</h3>

    <p>Correlative conjunctions work in pairs.</p>

    <ul>
      <li><em>Either</em> Musa <em>or</em> Akinyi will lead the group.</li>
      <li><em>Neither</em> the teacher <em>nor</em> the students
      were ready.</li>
      <li><em>Both</em> the report <em>and</em> the presentation
      were completed.</li>
      <li><em>Not only</em> did she apologise, <em>but also</em>
      she corrected the mistake.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Keep the connected elements grammatically parallel where
    possible. For example, “She enjoys both reading and writing”
    connects two -ing forms.</p>

    <h3>4. Choose a conjunction that expresses the intended meaning</h3>

    <p>The conjunction determines the relationship between ideas.</p>

    <ul>
      <li>“He was ill, <em>so</em> he stayed home.” — result.</li>
      <li>“He stayed home <em>because</em> he was ill.” — reason.</li>
      <li>“He was ill, <em>but</em> he attended class.” — contrast.</li>
      <li>“He will attend <em>if</em> he feels better.” — condition.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Do not choose a conjunction merely because the sentence
    sounds familiar. Check the logical relationship between the ideas.</p>

    <h3>5. Punctuation with conjunctions</h3>

    <p>When a coordinating conjunction joins two independent
    clauses, a comma is generally placed before it.</p>

    <blockquote>
      The road was flooded, so the buses used another route.
    </blockquote>

    <p>When a dependent clause comes first, it is commonly followed
    by a comma:</p>

    <blockquote>
      Although the road was flooded, the buses continued operating.
    </blockquote>

    <h3>Worked example</h3>

    <p><strong>Sentence:</strong> Although the team was exhausted,
    it continued practising because the final match was approaching.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the conjunctions:
    <em>although</em> and <em>because</em>.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> <em>Although</em> introduces a
    contrast between exhaustion and continued practice.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> <em>Because</em> introduces the
    reason the team continued practising.</p>

    <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The conjunctions connect the ideas
    by expressing contrast and cause.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Conjunctions', 15),
  (1744, 1744, '
    <h2>Interjections</h2>

    <p>An <strong>interjection</strong> is a word or short
    expression that communicates a reaction, feeling, hesitation
    or conversational response.</p>

    <h3>1. Expressing feelings and reactions</h3>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>Ouch!</strong> That thorn hurt my foot.</li>
      <li><strong>Hurray!</strong> Our team has won.</li>
      <li><strong>Oh!</strong> I did not realise you were here.</li>
      <li><strong>Alas,</strong> the old building was destroyed.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>The interjection communicates a reaction, but the rest of
    the sentence supplies the information about what happened.</p>

    <h3>2. Interjections in conversation</h3>

    <p>Some interjections help a speaker respond or manage a
    conversation.</p>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>Well,</strong> we should examine the evidence first.</li>
      <li><strong>Hmm,</strong> that answer needs another look.</li>
      <li><strong>Ah,</strong> now I understand the problem.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>The same expression may communicate different reactions
    depending on tone and context. For example, <em>Oh!</em>
    can express surprise, recognition or disappointment.</p>

    <h3>3. Punctuation with interjections</h3>

    <p>An interjection can be followed by an exclamation mark
    when the reaction is strong.</p>

    <blockquote>
      Wow! That was an excellent performance.
    </blockquote>

    <p>A comma is often suitable when the expression is more
    conversational or integrated into the sentence.</p>

    <blockquote>
      Well, I think we should begin now.
    </blockquote>

    <p>Choose punctuation according to the force of the expression
    and the structure of the sentence. Do not use exclamation marks
    automatically after every interjection.</p>

    <h3>4. Interjections and formal writing</h3>

    <p>Interjections are common in dialogue, informal messages,
    stories and speeches. In formal reports and academic explanations,
    they are usually less frequent because the writer normally
    communicates reactions through carefully chosen statements.</p>

    <p>For example, instead of writing “Wow! The results were
    unexpected,” a formal report might state, “The results were
    unexpected.”</p>

    <h3>Worked example</h3>

    <p><strong>Sentence:</strong> “Ah, I see why the experiment failed!”</p>

    <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the short expression
    communicating a reaction.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> <em>Ah</em> signals recognition
    or understanding.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> The remaining clause explains what
    the speaker has understood.</p>

    <p><strong>Answer:</strong> <em>Ah</em> is the interjection.
    The comma separates it from the rest of the sentence.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Interjections', 15),
  (1745, 1745, '
    <h2>Common and Proper Nouns</h2>

    <p>Nouns can be classified according to whether they name
    a general category or a particular person, place, organisation
    or other named entity.</p>

    <h3>1. Common nouns</h3>

    <p>A <strong>common noun</strong> names a general class of
    people, places, animals, objects or things.</p>

    <ul>
      <li>teacher</li>
      <li>county</li>
      <li>river</li>
      <li>school</li>
      <li>mountain</li>
    </ul>

    <p>These words do not identify one particular named entity
    on their own.</p>

    <h3>2. Proper nouns</h3>

    <p>A <strong>proper noun</strong> identifies a particular
    person, place, organisation, institution or other named entity.</p>

    <ul>
      <li>Wangari Maathai</li>
      <li>Kenya</li>
      <li>Tana River</li>
      <li>Kenyatta University</li>
      <li>Mount Kenya</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Proper nouns normally begin with capital letters. The
    capitalisation of a multiword name follows the conventional
    spelling of that name.</p>

    <h3>3. Compare the general category with the specific name</h3>

    <ul>
      <li>We crossed a <strong>river</strong>. — common noun.</li>
      <li>We crossed the <strong>Tana River</strong>. — proper noun
      naming a particular river.</li>
      <li>She studies at a <strong>university</strong>. — common noun.</li>
      <li>She studies at <strong>Kenyatta University</strong>. —
      proper noun naming a particular institution.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>A word does not become a proper noun simply because it
    appears important in a sentence. Its role depends on whether
    it is part of a particular name.</p>

    <h3>4. Capitalisation in context</h3>

    <p>Compare:</p>

    <blockquote>
      The principal visited the school.
    </blockquote>

    <blockquote>
      Principal Wambui visited the school.
    </blockquote>

    <p>In the first sentence, <em>principal</em> is a common noun
    used as a job title. In the second, it forms part of a title
    attached to a person''s name and is capitalised.</p>

    <p>Capitalisation can depend on position and naming convention.
    The first word of a sentence is capitalised even when it is
    not a proper noun.</p>

    <h3>5. Avoid confusing names with categories</h3>

    <p>In “Lake Victoria is a large lake,” <em>Lake Victoria</em>
    is the proper name, while the later word <em>lake</em> is a
    common noun identifying the type of geographical feature.</p>

    <h3>Worked example</h3>

    <p><strong>Sentence:</strong> Our class visited Nairobi
    National Museum during the school holiday.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Find the general category:
    <em>class</em>, <em>museum</em> and <em>holiday</em> are common
    nouns in this sentence.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Identify the particular named
    institution: <em>Nairobi National Museum</em>.</p>

    <p><strong>Answer:</strong> <em>Nairobi National Museum</em>
    is a proper noun; the other identified nouns are common nouns.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Common and Proper Nouns', 15),
  (1746, 1746, '
    <h2>Concrete and Abstract Nouns</h2>

    <p>Nouns can name things experienced through the senses or
    ideas, qualities, states and feelings that are not physical
    objects.</p>

    <h3>1. Concrete nouns</h3>

    <p>A <strong>concrete noun</strong> names something physical
    that can be experienced through one or more senses.</p>

    <ul>
      <li><em>Drum</em> — can be heard and touched.</li>
      <li><em>Perfume</em> — can be smelled.</li>
      <li><em>Ice</em> — can be seen and felt.</li>
      <li><em>Bell</em> — can be heard.</li>
      <li><em>Orange</em> — can be seen, touched, smelled and tasted.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>A concrete noun does not have to be visible at every moment.
    Something can still be physical even when it cannot currently
    be seen.</p>

    <h3>2. Abstract nouns</h3>

    <p>An <strong>abstract noun</strong> names an idea, quality,
    state, feeling or concept rather than a physical object.</p>

    <ul>
      <li><em>Honesty</em> — a quality.</li>
      <li><em>Fear</em> — a feeling.</li>
      <li><em>Childhood</em> — a stage of life.</li>
      <li><em>Freedom</em> — a concept or state.</li>
      <li><em>Knowledge</em> — what someone knows or understands.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Abstract nouns can describe real and important things.
    They are called abstract because they name concepts, qualities
    or states rather than physical objects.</p>

    <h3>3. Recognising nouns in context</h3>

    <p>Consider:</p>

    <blockquote>
      Her kindness brought comfort to the frightened child.
    </blockquote>

    <ul>
      <li><em>Kindness</em> is an abstract noun naming a quality.</li>
      <li><em>Comfort</em> is an abstract noun naming a state or feeling.</li>
      <li><em>Child</em> is a concrete noun naming a person.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>The classification depends on the meaning of the word
    in its context.</p>

    <h3>4. Abstract nouns formed from other words</h3>

    <p>Some abstract nouns are formed from adjectives or verbs.</p>

    <ul>
      <li>kind → kindness</li>
      <li>brave → bravery</li>
      <li>happy → happiness</li>
      <li>decide → decision</li>
      <li>perform → performance</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Not every noun formed this way is abstract in every possible
    use, so check the meaning of the word in the sentence.</p>

    <h3>5. Avoid classifying by appearance alone</h3>

    <p>A noun can refer to something that is physically represented
    but still name an abstract concept.</p>

    <p>For example, in “The judge valued justice,” <em>justice</em>
    names an abstract principle, even though a court may use
    physical documents and procedures to pursue it.</p>

    <h3>Worked example</h3>

    <p><strong>Sentence:</strong> The athlete''s determination
    helped her carry the trophy home.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the nouns:
    <em>athlete</em>, <em>determination</em>, <em>trophy</em> and
    <em>home</em> as used here in the expression.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> <em>Athlete</em> and <em>trophy</em>
    name physical entities.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> <em>Determination</em> names a
    quality or mental resolve.</p>

    <p><strong>Answer:</strong> <em>Athlete</em> and <em>trophy</em>
    are concrete nouns; <em>determination</em> is an abstract noun.
    Here, <em>home</em> functions as an adverbial expression of
    destination rather than a noun.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Concrete and Abstract Nouns', 15),
  (1747, 1747, '
    <h2>Countable and Uncountable Nouns</h2>

    <p>Nouns differ in whether they can normally be counted as
    separate units. This distinction affects singular and plural
    forms, articles and quantifiers.</p>

    <h3>1. Countable nouns</h3>

    <p><strong>Countable nouns</strong> name things that can be
    counted individually.</p>

    <ul>
      <li>one book, two books</li>
      <li>one student, five students</li>
      <li>one chair, several chairs</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Countable nouns have singular and plural forms in ordinary
    uses. A singular countable noun generally needs a determiner
    when used as a complete noun phrase.</p>

    <ul>
      <li>She borrowed <em>a book</em>.</li>
      <li>She borrowed <em>the book</em>.</li>
      <li>She borrowed <em>three books</em>.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>2. Uncountable nouns</h3>

    <p><strong>Uncountable nouns</strong> usually name substances,
    materials, activities or concepts that are not counted as
    separate units in the relevant meaning.</p>

    <ul>
      <li>water</li>
      <li>rice</li>
      <li>information</li>
      <li>furniture</li>
      <li>advice</li>
      <li>equipment</li>
    </ul>

    <p>In their usual uncountable senses, these nouns do not take
    ordinary plural forms and are not used directly with
    <em>a</em> or <em>an</em>.</p>

    <p>Say:</p>

    <ul>
      <li>some information, not normally <em>an information</em></li>
      <li>a piece of advice, not normally <em>an advice</em></li>
      <li>some furniture, not normally <em>furnitures</em></li>
    </ul>

    <h3>3. Measuring uncountable nouns</h3>

    <p>Use a suitable unit or container to count an amount of
    an uncountable substance.</p>

    <ul>
      <li>a glass of water</li>
      <li>two bags of rice</li>
      <li>three pieces of information</li>
      <li>four items of furniture</li>
    </ul>

    <p>The unit is countable even when the substance or concept
    named by the main noun is not.</p>

    <h3>4. Quantifiers with countable and uncountable nouns</h3>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>Many / few:</strong> many books, few students.</li>
      <li><strong>Much / little:</strong> much water, little time.</li>
      <li><strong>Some / enough:</strong> some books, some water;
      enough chairs, enough water.</li>
      <li><strong>A number of:</strong> a number of questions.</li>
      <li><strong>An amount of:</strong> an amount of money.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>5. Nouns that can be countable or uncountable</h3>

    <p>Some nouns change meaning depending on how they are used.</p>

    <ul>
      <li>There is <em>chicken</em> in the pot. — meat, uncountable.</li>
      <li>There are three <em>chickens</em> in the yard. — animals,
      countable.</li>
      <li>She drank <em>some coffee</em>. — a drink, uncountable.</li>
      <li>We ordered two <em>coffees</em>. — two servings, countable.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Always classify a noun according to the meaning intended
    in its sentence.</p>

    <h3>Worked example</h3>

    <p><strong>Sentence:</strong> The shopkeeper sold three bags
    of rice and some sugar.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the quantities:
    <em>three bags</em> is countable.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> <em>Rice</em> and <em>sugar</em>
    are uncountable in this context.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> The bags provide countable units
    for measuring rice; <em>some</em> quantifies sugar without
    specifying an exact number of units.</p>

    <p><strong>Answer:</strong> <em>Bags</em> is countable;
    <em>rice</em> and <em>sugar</em> are uncountable in this sentence.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Countable and Uncountable Nouns', 15),
  (1748, 1748, '
    <h2>Collective Nouns</h2>

    <p>A <strong>collective noun</strong> names a group of people,
    animals or things considered as a unit.</p>

    <h3>1. Common collective nouns</h3>

    <ul>
      <li>a <em>team</em> of players</li>
      <li>a <em>class</em> of learners</li>
      <li>a <em>flock</em> of sheep</li>
      <li>a <em>herd</em> of cattle</li>
      <li>a <em>committee</em> of members</li>
      <li>a <em>crowd</em> of people</li>
    </ul>

    <p>The collective noun names the group. The words following
    <em>of</em> identify the individuals or things belonging to it.</p>

    <h3>2. Collective nouns as singular subjects</h3>

    <p>When the group is treated as one unit, a collective noun
    usually takes a singular verb in standard British English.</p>

    <ul>
      <li>The team <em>is</em> ready for the match.</li>
      <li>The committee <em>has</em> reached a decision.</li>
      <li>The class <em>was</em> quiet during the examination.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>The sentence presents each group as a single unit.</p>

    <h3>3. When the members are emphasised</h3>

    <p>In British English, some collective nouns can take a plural
    verb when the writer emphasises the individuals acting
    separately.</p>

    <ul>
      <li>The team <em>are</em> arguing among themselves.</li>
      <li>The committee <em>are</em> divided in their opinions.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>This usage is particularly common in British English.
    American English more often uses singular agreement with
    collective nouns. In formal school writing, follow the
    expected variety of English and maintain agreement consistently.</p>

    <h3>4. Collective nouns are not always interchangeable</h3>

    <p>Choose a collective noun that suits the group.</p>

    <ul>
      <li>a flock of sheep</li>
      <li>a herd of cattle</li>
      <li>a swarm of bees</li>
      <li>a fleet of ships</li>
    </ul>

    <p>These expressions are conventional. A collective noun
    describes the group, but the correct expression depends on
    the kind of members involved.</p>

    <h3>5. Do not confuse a collective noun with a plural noun</h3>

    <p>Compare:</p>

    <ul>
      <li>The <em>team</em> is training. — one collective group.</li>
      <li>The <em>players</em> are training. — a plural noun
      naming individual members.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>The meaning may be similar, but the grammatical subject
    is different. The verb must agree with the subject used.</p>

    <h3>Worked example</h3>

    <p><strong>Sentence:</strong> The committee has published
    its report.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the subject:
    <em>committee</em>.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> The committee is presented as
    one decision-making body.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Use the singular verb <em>has</em>
    and the singular possessive determiner <em>its</em>.</p>

    <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The sentence uses singular
    agreement because the committee is treated as one unit.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Collective Nouns', 15),
  (1749, 1749, '
    <h2>Singular and Plural Nouns</h2>

    <p>A singular noun normally refers to one person, thing or
    unit. A plural noun normally refers to more than one.</p>

    <h3>1. Regular plural nouns</h3>

    <p>Most countable nouns form the plural by adding <em>-s</em>.</p>

    <ul>
      <li>book → books</li>
      <li>desk → desks</li>
      <li>student → students</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Nouns ending in certain sounds or letter patterns take
    <em>-es</em>.</p>

    <ul>
      <li>bus → buses</li>
      <li>box → boxes</li>
      <li>watch → watches</li>
      <li>dish → dishes</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>2. Nouns ending in -y</h3>

    <p>When a consonant comes before the final <em>-y</em>,
    the usual plural changes <em>-y</em> to <em>-ies</em>.</p>

    <ul>
      <li>city → cities</li>
      <li>baby → babies</li>
      <li>country → countries</li>
    </ul>

    <p>When a vowel comes before the final <em>-y</em>, add
    <em>-s</em>.</p>

    <ul>
      <li>boy → boys</li>
      <li>key → keys</li>
      <li>day → days</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>3. Irregular plural nouns</h3>

    <p>Some nouns form their plurals in ways that do not follow
    the regular <em>-s</em> pattern.</p>

    <ul>
      <li>child → children</li>
      <li>man → men</li>
      <li>woman → women</li>
      <li>foot → feet</li>
      <li>tooth → teeth</li>
      <li>mouse → mice</li>
    </ul>

    <p>These forms must be learnt individually because their
    spelling changes cannot be predicted reliably from the
    regular plural rules.</p>

    <h3>4. Nouns with the same singular and plural form</h3>

    <p>Some nouns have the same form in both singular and plural.</p>

    <ul>
      <li>one sheep → five sheep</li>
      <li>one deer → several deer</li>
      <li>one aircraft → three aircraft</li>
    </ul>

    <p>The surrounding words and the meaning show whether one
    or more than one is intended.</p>

    <h3>5. Nouns that are usually uncountable</h3>

    <p>Some nouns are not normally made plural when used in their
    ordinary uncountable meanings.</p>

    <ul>
      <li>information</li>
      <li>advice</li>
      <li>furniture</li>
      <li>equipment</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Use expressions such as <em>pieces of information</em>
    or <em>items of equipment</em> when counting individual units.</p>

    <h3>6. Plural nouns and verb agreement</h3>

    <p>A plural noun generally takes a plural verb in the present
    simple tense.</p>

    <ul>
      <li>The learner <em>writes</em> neatly.</li>
      <li>The learners <em>write</em> neatly.</li>
      <li>The child <em>is</em> outside.</li>
      <li>The children <em>are</em> outside.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Be careful with irregular nouns. <em>Children</em> is plural,
    even though it does not end in <em>-s</em>.</p>

    <h3>Worked example</h3>

    <p><strong>Sentence:</strong> Three children carried two boxes
    of books into the classroom.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> <em>Children</em> is the irregular
    plural of <em>child</em>.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> <em>Boxes</em> is the plural of
    <em>box</em>, formed by adding <em>-es</em>.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> <em>Books</em> is the regular
    plural of <em>book</em>, formed by adding <em>-s</em>.</p>

    <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The sentence correctly uses three
    plural noun forms: <em>children</em>, <em>boxes</em> and
    <em>books</em>.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Singular and Plural Nouns', 15),
  (1750, 1750, '
    <h2>Possessive Nouns</h2>

    <p>A <strong>possessive noun</strong> shows ownership,
    association or a relationship. Apostrophes help mark
    possession in many noun forms.</p>

    <h3>1. Singular nouns</h3>

    <p>For most singular nouns, add an apostrophe and <em>s</em>
    to form the possessive.</p>

    <ul>
      <li>the girl''s uniform</li>
      <li>the teacher''s desk</li>
      <li>the dog''s collar</li>
      <li>James''s notebook</li>
    </ul>

    <p>The possessive form of a singular noun generally takes
    <em>''s</em>, including many names that already end in
    <em>s</em>. Some style guides allow alternatives for certain
    names ending in <em>s</em>; follow the convention required
    by your school or publication.</p>

    <h3>2. Regular plural nouns ending in -s</h3>

    <p>When a plural noun already ends in <em>-s</em>, normally
    add only an apostrophe.</p>

    <ul>
      <li>the girls'' uniforms</li>
      <li>the teachers'' meeting</li>
      <li>the players'' changing room</li>
    </ul>

    <p>The apostrophe comes after the final <em>s</em> because
    the noun is already plural.</p>

    <h3>3. Irregular plural nouns</h3>

    <p>Plural nouns that do not end in <em>-s</em> usually form
    the possessive by adding <em>''s</em>.</p>

    <ul>
      <li>children → children''s books</li>
      <li>men → men''s shoes</li>
      <li>women → women''s team</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>4. Distinguishing singular and plural possession</h3>

    <p>Compare:</p>

    <ul>
      <li><em>The student''s books</em> — books belonging to one student.</li>
      <li><em>The students'' books</em> — books belonging to multiple students.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>The position of the apostrophe changes the meaning.</p>

    <h3>5. Joint and separate possession</h3>

    <p>When two people share one thing, the possessive marker
    usually appears on the last name.</p>

    <blockquote>
      Amina and Brian''s project won the competition.
    </blockquote>

    <p>This normally means they worked on one shared project.</p>

    <p>When each person owns a separate item, each name can take
    its own possessive marker.</p>

    <blockquote>
      Amina''s and Brian''s projects were displayed.
    </blockquote>

    <p>This normally refers to separate projects belonging to
    Amina and Brian respectively.</p>

    <h3>6. Possessive nouns and contractions</h3>

    <p>Do not confuse possession with a contraction.</p>

    <ul>
      <li><em>The school''s gate</em> — the gate belonging to or
      associated with the school.</li>
      <li><em>The school is closed</em> → <em>The school''s closed</em>
      — here <em>school''s</em> means <em>school is</em>.</li>
      <li><em>It''s raining</em> — <em>it''s</em> means <em>it is</em>.</li>
      <li><em>The dog wagged its tail</em> — <em>its</em> shows
      possession and has no apostrophe.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Use the meaning and grammar of the sentence to decide
    whether the apostrophe marks possession or missing letters.</p>

    <h3>Worked example</h3>

    <p><strong>Sentence:</strong> The children''s uniforms were
    placed beside the teachers'' bags.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> <em>Children</em> is an irregular
    plural that does not end in <em>-s</em>, so its possessive
    form is <em>children''s</em>.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> <em>Teachers</em> is a regular
    plural ending in <em>-s</em>, so its possessive form is
    <em>teachers''</em>.</p>

    <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Both forms are correct because
    their plural nouns follow different spelling patterns.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Possessive Nouns', 15);
INSERT INTO public.lessons (id, topic_id, content, summary, estimated_minutes) VALUES
  (1751, 1751, '
    <h2>Articles: A, An and The</h2>

    <p>Articles are determiners that help show whether a noun
    refers to something identifiable or to one member of a general
    class. English has the indefinite articles <em>a</em> and
    <em>an</em>, and the definite article <em>the</em>.</p>

    <h3>1. Using a and an</h3>

    <p>Use <em>a</em> or <em>an</em> with a singular countable noun
    when referring to one person or thing that is not yet
    specifically identified.</p>

    <ul>
      <li>She borrowed <em>a book</em>.</li>
      <li>He ate <em>an orange</em>.</li>
      <li>We met <em>a teacher</em> at the gate.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>The choice between <em>a</em> and <em>an</em> depends on
    the first sound of the following word, not merely its first
    written letter.</p>

    <ul>
      <li><em>a university</em> — begins with a /y/ consonant sound.</li>
      <li><em>an umbrella</em> — begins with a vowel sound.</li>
      <li><em>a European country</em> — begins with a /y/ sound.</li>
      <li><em>an honest person</em> — the initial h is silent.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>2. Using the</h3>

    <p>Use <em>the</em> when the reader or listener can identify
    the person or thing being discussed.</p>

    <ul>
      <li>I saw a dog. <em>The dog</em> was wearing a collar.</li>
      <li>Please close <em>the door</em>. — The door is identifiable
      from the situation.</li>
      <li><em>The sun</em> provides light to Earth.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>The definite article can also identify something through
    a phrase that follows it:</p>

    <blockquote>
      The student who won the competition received a certificate.
    </blockquote>

    <p>The phrase <em>who won the competition</em> identifies
    which student is meant.</p>

    <h3>3. When no article is needed</h3>

    <p>English often uses no article when referring generally to
    plural countable nouns or uncountable nouns.</p>

    <ul>
      <li><em>Books</em> can provide useful knowledge.</li>
      <li><em>Water</em> is essential for life.</li>
      <li><em>Honesty</em> is important.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Compare the general meaning with a specific reference:</p>

    <ul>
      <li><em>Water</em> is necessary for survival. — water in general.</li>
      <li><em>The water</em> in this bottle is cold. — specific water.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>4. Articles with jobs and identities</h3>

    <p>Use <em>a</em> or <em>an</em> before a singular countable
    noun naming someone''s profession or role.</p>

    <ul>
      <li>She is <em>a nurse</em>.</li>
      <li>He became <em>an engineer</em>.</li>
      <li>My aunt is <em>a university lecturer</em>.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Do not normally omit the article before a singular countable
    profession noun in these constructions.</p>

    <h3>5. Common article errors</h3>

    <ul>
      <li>Incorrect: She bought <em>an uniform</em>.</li>
      <li>Correct: She bought <em>a uniform</em>.</li>
      <li>Incorrect: He is <em>teacher</em>.</li>
      <li>Correct: He is <em>a teacher</em>.</li>
      <li>Incorrect: <em>The honesty</em> is important. — when
      referring to honesty in general.</li>
      <li>Correct: <em>Honesty</em> is important.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Article use depends on countability, meaning and context.
    Avoid choosing an article by memorising only the first letter
    of the next word.</p>

    <h3>Worked example</h3>

    <p><strong>Sentence:</strong> Wanjiku found a wallet near
    the gate. The wallet contained an identification card.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Use <em>a wallet</em> because the
    wallet is introduced for the first time.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Use <em>the wallet</em> in the next
    sentence because the reader now knows which wallet is meant.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Use <em>an identification card</em>
    because it is a singular countable noun introduced with a vowel
    sound at the start of <em>identification</em>.</p>

    <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The articles guide the reader from
    a new reference to an identifiable one.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Articles: A, An and The', 15),
  (1752, 1752, '
    <h2>Quantifiers</h2>

    <p>A <strong>quantifier</strong> expresses how much or how
    many of something is being referred to. Choosing the correct
    quantifier depends partly on whether the noun is countable
    or uncountable.</p>

    <h3>1. Many and much</h3>

    <p>Use <em>many</em> with plural countable nouns and
    <em>much</em> with uncountable nouns.</p>

    <ul>
      <li>Many learners attended the lesson.</li>
      <li>Much water was wasted.</li>
      <li>How many books did you borrow?</li>
      <li>How much money do you need?</li>
    </ul>

    <p>In affirmative everyday statements, <em>a lot of</em> and
    <em>lots of</em> are often more natural than <em>much</em>
    with uncountable nouns.</p>

    <ul>
      <li>We have a lot of work to do.</li>
      <li>She has lots of friends.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>2. Few, a few, little and a little</h3>

    <p>These expressions differ in meaning.</p>

    <ul>
      <li><em>Few</em> means not many and often suggests that the
      number is insufficient.</li>
      <li><em>A few</em> means some, though not many.</li>
      <li><em>Little</em> means not much and often suggests an
      insufficient amount.</li>
      <li><em>A little</em> means some amount, though not much.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Compare:</p>

    <blockquote>
      Few students understood the explanation, so the teacher
      repeated it.
    </blockquote>

    <blockquote>
      A few students understood the explanation and helped others.
    </blockquote>

    <p>The first sentence emphasises how small the number was.
    The second presents the number as a small but useful group.</p>

    <h3>3. Some and any</h3>

    <p><em>Some</em> is common in affirmative statements.
    <em>Any</em> is common in questions and negative statements.</p>

    <ul>
      <li>We have <em>some</em> sugar.</li>
      <li>Do you have <em>any</em> sugar?</li>
      <li>We do not have <em>any</em> sugar.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>However, <em>some</em> is also natural in offers and requests
    when a positive answer is expected or when something is being
    offered.</p>

    <ul>
      <li>Would you like <em>some</em> tea?</li>
      <li>Could I have <em>some</em> water, please?</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>4. Enough, several and plenty of</h3>

    <ul>
      <li><em>Enough</em> means as much or as many as needed.</li>
      <li><em>Several</em> refers to more than two but not a large
      or precisely stated number.</li>
      <li><em>Plenty of</em> indicates an amount or number that
      is more than sufficient.</li>
    </ul>

    <blockquote>
      We have enough chairs for everyone, and several extra chairs
      are available.
    </blockquote>

    <h3>5. Quantifiers and noun agreement</h3>

    <p>Some quantifier expressions affect the form of the verb.</p>

    <ul>
      <li><em>Many students are</em> absent.</li>
      <li><em>Much of the water is</em> clean.</li>
      <li><em>Each student has</em> a notebook.</li>
      <li><em>A number of students are</em> waiting.</li>
      <li><em>The number of students is</em> increasing.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Do not confuse <em>a number of</em> with <em>the number of</em>.
    The first normally takes a plural verb; the second normally
    takes a singular verb.</p>

    <h3>Worked example</h3>

    <p><strong>Sentence:</strong> Only a few learners had enough
    time to complete all the questions.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> <em>Learners</em> is a plural
    countable noun, so <em>a few</em> is appropriate.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> <em>Time</em> is uncountable in
    this meaning.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> <em>Enough</em> indicates that the
    available time was sufficient for the task.</p>

    <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The quantifiers correctly describe
    a small number of learners and the sufficiency of their time.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Quantifiers', 15),
  (1753, 1753, '
    <h2>Formation of Noun Phrases</h2>

    <p>A <strong>noun phrase</strong> is a group of words organised
    around a noun or another word functioning as the head of the
    phrase. It can name a person, place, thing or idea and can
    perform roles such as subject, object or complement.</p>

    <h3>1. The head noun</h3>

    <p>The head noun is the central noun that determines what
    the phrase refers to.</p>

    <ul>
      <li><em>the student</em></li>
      <li><em>a wooden desk</em></li>
      <li><em>those three new books</em></li>
    </ul>

    <p>In these examples, the head nouns are <em>student</em>,
    <em>desk</em> and <em>books</em>.</p>

    <h3>2. Determiners before the noun</h3>

    <p>A noun phrase may begin with a determiner.</p>

    <ul>
      <li><em>the</em> classroom</li>
      <li><em>my</em> notebook</li>
      <li><em>several</em> learners</li>
      <li><em>those</em> houses</li>
    </ul>

    <p>The determiner helps identify the noun, indicate ownership
    or express quantity.</p>

    <h3>3. Adjectives before the head noun</h3>

    <p>Adjectives can add descriptive information.</p>

    <ul>
      <li>a <em>careful</em> driver</li>
      <li>the <em>old wooden</em> bridge</li>
      <li>three <em>challenging mathematics</em> questions</li>
    </ul>

    <p>A noun phrase can contain more than one adjective, but
    unnecessary descriptions can make writing less clear.</p>

    <h3>4. Phrases that follow the noun</h3>

    <p>Information can also follow the head noun.</p>

    <ul>
      <li>the girl <em>in the red dress</em></li>
      <li>the book <em>on the desk</em></li>
      <li>the learner <em>who answered correctly</em></li>
    </ul>

    <p>The additional phrase or clause helps identify or describe
    the head noun.</p>

    <h3>5. Noun phrases in sentences</h3>

    <p>A noun phrase can occupy different positions in a sentence.</p>

    <blockquote>
      The tall student in the front row answered the question.
    </blockquote>

    <ul>
      <li><em>The tall student in the front row</em> is the subject
      noun phrase.</li>
      <li><em>the question</em> is the object noun phrase.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Both phrases are organised around a head noun:
    <em>student</em> and <em>question</em>.</p>

    <h3>6. Expand a noun phrase precisely</h3>

    <p>Start with a head noun, then add only the information
    needed to identify or describe it.</p>

    <ol>
      <li><em>book</em></li>
      <li><em>the book</em></li>
      <li><em>the old book</em></li>
      <li><em>the old book on the shelf</em></li>
    </ol>

    <p>Each expansion provides more information. The best phrase
    is the one that communicates the intended meaning clearly,
    not necessarily the longest one.</p>

    <h3>Worked example</h3>

    <p><strong>Sentence:</strong> Three hardworking learners
    from our school won the regional competition.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the subject noun phrase:
    <em>Three hardworking learners from our school</em>.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Find its head noun:
    <em>learners</em>.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> <em>Three</em> gives the number,
    <em>hardworking</em> describes the learners, and
    <em>from our school</em> provides additional information.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Identify the object noun phrase:
    <em>the regional competition</em>, with <em>competition</em>
    as its head noun.</p>

    <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The sentence contains a detailed
    subject noun phrase and an object noun phrase, each organised
    around a head noun.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Formation of Noun Phrases', 15),
  (1754, 1754, '
    <h2>Personal Pronouns</h2>

    <p><strong>Personal pronouns</strong> refer to people, animals
    or things. Their forms depend on grammatical person, number
    and their role in the sentence.</p>

    <h3>1. Subject pronouns</h3>

    <p>Subject pronouns commonly perform the subject role.</p>

    <ul>
      <li>I study English every evening.</li>
      <li>You understand the instructions.</li>
      <li>He repaired the bicycle.</li>
      <li>She won the competition.</li>
      <li>It fell from the shelf.</li>
      <li>We completed the assignment.</li>
      <li>They travelled by bus.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>The subject pronoun identifies who or what performs the
    action or is being described.</p>

    <h3>2. Object pronouns</h3>

    <p>Object pronouns commonly appear after verbs or prepositions.</p>

    <ul>
      <li>The teacher praised <em>me</em>.</li>
      <li>I called <em>him</em>.</li>
      <li>We invited <em>them</em>.</li>
      <li>The gift was for <em>her</em>.</li>
      <li>The coach spoke to <em>us</em>.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Use <em>me</em>, not <em>I</em>, as the object in
    “The teacher called me.” Use <em>I</em> as the subject in
    “I called the teacher.”</p>

    <h3>3. Pronouns in compound subjects and objects</h3>

    <p>When a pronoun is joined to another noun phrase, its
    grammatical role does not change.</p>

    <ul>
      <li><em>Wanjiku and I</em> prepared the presentation.</li>
      <li>The teacher praised <em>Wanjiku and me</em>.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>A useful test is to remove the other person temporarily:</p>

    <ul>
      <li>“I prepared the presentation” is correct.</li>
      <li>“The teacher praised me” is correct.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>4. Pronouns and clear reference</h3>

    <p>A pronoun should make it clear who or what is being
    discussed.</p>

    <blockquote>
      After Amina spoke to Wanjiku, she left the room.
    </blockquote>

    <p>The pronoun <em>she</em> could refer to either Amina or
    Wanjiku. Rewrite the sentence if the intended person is not
    clear.</p>

    <blockquote>
      After Amina spoke to Wanjiku, Amina left the room.
    </blockquote>

    <p>Repeating a noun is sometimes better than using an ambiguous
    pronoun.</p>

    <h3>5. Formal and informal usage</h3>

    <p>In standard formal English, use the pronoun that matches
    its grammatical role.</p>

    <ul>
      <li>Formal standard usage: It was <em>I</em> who called.</li>
      <li>Common conversational usage: It was <em>me</em> who called.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>The best form can depend on the sentence structure and
    register. For school grammar exercises, follow the standard
    construction specified in the question.</p>

    <h3>Worked example</h3>

    <p><strong>Sentence:</strong> My brother and I sent the
    invitation to her.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> <em>My brother and I</em> form
    the subject.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> <em>I</em> is a subject pronoun.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> <em>Her</em> follows the
    preposition <em>to</em>, so it is an object pronoun.</p>

    <p><strong>Answer:</strong> <em>I</em> is the subject form;
    <em>her</em> is the object form.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Personal Pronouns', 15),
  (1755, 1755, '
    <h2>Possessive Pronouns</h2>

    <p><strong>Possessive pronouns</strong> show ownership or
    association and can stand in place of a noun phrase.</p>

    <h3>1. The possessive forms</h3>

    <ul>
      <li>mine</li>
      <li>yours</li>
      <li>his</li>
      <li>hers</li>
      <li>ours</li>
      <li>theirs</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Examples:</p>

    <ul>
      <li>This notebook is <em>mine</em>.</li>
      <li>That seat is <em>yours</em>.</li>
      <li>The blue bag is <em>hers</em>.</li>
      <li>The responsibility is <em>ours</em>.</li>
      <li>The footballs are <em>theirs</em>.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>2. Possessive pronouns and possessive determiners</h3>

    <p>Possessive determiners come before nouns; possessive
    pronouns can stand independently.</p>

    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Possessive determiner</th>
          <th>Possessive pronoun</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>my book</td><td>the book is mine</td></tr>
        <tr><td>your pen</td><td>the pen is yours</td></tr>
        <tr><td>her bag</td><td>the bag is hers</td></tr>
        <tr><td>our classroom</td><td>the classroom is ours</td></tr>
        <tr><td>their house</td><td>the house is theirs</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <p>In “my book,” <em>my</em> determines the noun <em>book</em>.
    In “the book is mine,” <em>mine</em> stands independently.</p>

    <h3>3. Possessive pronouns do not use apostrophes</h3>

    <p>Possessive pronouns such as <em>hers</em>, <em>ours</em>
    and <em>theirs</em> are written without apostrophes.</p>

    <ul>
      <li>Correct: The final decision was <em>theirs</em>.</li>
      <li>Incorrect: The final decision was <em>their''s</em>.</li>
      <li>Correct: The red bag is <em>hers</em>.</li>
      <li>Incorrect: The red bag is <em>her''s</em>.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Remember the distinction between <em>its</em> and
    <em>it''s</em>: <em>its</em> is possessive, while <em>it''s</em>
    normally means <em>it is</em> or <em>it has</em>.</p>

    <h3>4. Avoid unnecessary repetition</h3>

    <p>Possessive pronouns can make writing more concise.</p>

    <blockquote>
      My answer is correct, but your answer needs a correction.
    </blockquote>

    <p>This can be shortened to:</p>

    <blockquote>
      My answer is correct, but yours needs a correction.
    </blockquote>

    <p>The word <em>yours</em> replaces the phrase <em>your answer</em>.</p>

    <h3>5. Make the ownership clear</h3>

    <p>A possessive pronoun should refer to an identifiable owner
    or group of owners.</p>

    <blockquote>
      Peter compared his results with those of his classmates.
    </blockquote>

    <p>The phrase <em>his results</em> identifies Peter''s results.
    The phrase <em>those of his classmates</em> avoids repeating
    the noun <em>results</em>.</p>

    <h3>Worked example</h3>

    <p><strong>Sentence:</strong> This is our classroom, and
    that one is theirs.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> <em>Our</em> comes before the
    noun <em>classroom</em>, so it is a possessive determiner.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> <em>Theirs</em> stands independently
    and replaces a phrase such as <em>their classroom</em>.</p>

    <p><strong>Answer:</strong> <em>Our</em> is a possessive
    determiner; <em>theirs</em> is a possessive pronoun.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Possessive Pronouns', 15),
  (1756, 1756, '
    <h2>Reflexive and Emphatic Pronouns</h2>

    <p>Reflexive and emphatic pronouns have the same forms, such
    as <em>myself</em>, <em>yourself</em>, <em>herself</em>,
    <em>himself</em>, <em>itself</em>, <em>ourselves</em>,
    <em>yourselves</em> and <em>themselves</em>. Their function
    depends on how they are used.</p>

    <h3>1. Reflexive pronouns</h3>

    <p>A reflexive pronoun is used when the object refers back
    to the subject of the same clause.</p>

    <ul>
      <li>She blamed <em>herself</em>.</li>
      <li>The boy injured <em>himself</em>.</li>
      <li>We prepared <em>ourselves</em> for the examination.</li>
      <li>The cat cleaned <em>itself</em>.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>In “She blamed herself,” the person doing the blaming
    and the person receiving the blame are the same.</p>

    <h3>2. Emphatic pronouns</h3>

    <p>An emphatic pronoun adds emphasis to a noun or pronoun.
    It is not required as the object of the verb.</p>

    <ul>
      <li>The principal <em>herself</em> welcomed the visitors.</li>
      <li>I <em>myself</em> checked the figures.</li>
      <li>The students <em>themselves</em> organised the event.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>In “The principal herself welcomed the visitors,” removing
    <em>herself</em> leaves a grammatically complete sentence:
    “The principal welcomed the visitors.”</p>

    <h3>3. How to distinguish the two functions</h3>

    <p>Ask what role the pronoun plays.</p>

    <ul>
      <li><em>He taught himself to swim.</em> — reflexive; the
      pronoun is the object of <em>taught</em>.</li>
      <li><em>He himself taught the class.</em> — emphatic; it
      emphasises who taught the class.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>A useful test for emphatic use is whether the pronoun can
    be removed without changing the basic grammatical structure.
    The emphasis will disappear, but the sentence will remain complete.</p>

    <h3>4. Reflexive pronouns must fit the subject</h3>

    <ul>
      <li>I → myself</li>
      <li>you, singular → yourself</li>
      <li>he → himself</li>
      <li>she → herself</li>
      <li>it → itself</li>
      <li>we → ourselves</li>
      <li>you, plural → yourselves</li>
      <li>they → themselves</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Choose the correct form according to the subject and the
    meaning of the sentence.</p>

    <h3>5. Avoid using reflexive pronouns unnecessarily</h3>

    <p>Reflexive forms should not replace ordinary subject or
    object pronouns without a grammatical reason.</p>

    <ul>
      <li>Standard: The teacher spoke to <em>me</em>.</li>
      <li>Unnecessary in ordinary standard usage: The teacher
      spoke to <em>myself</em>.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Use <em>myself</em> when the meaning is reflexive or emphatic,
    not simply to make a sentence sound more formal.</p>

    <h3>Worked example</h3>

    <p><strong>Sentence A:</strong> Akinyi taught herself to code.</p>

    <p><strong>Sentence B:</strong> Akinyi herself designed the programme.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> In sentence A, <em>herself</em>
    receives the action of <em>taught</em> and refers back to Akinyi.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> In sentence B, <em>herself</em>
    emphasises Akinyi''s role.</p>

    <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Sentence A uses a reflexive pronoun;
    sentence B uses an emphatic pronoun.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Reflexive and Emphatic Pronouns', 15),
  (1757, 1757, '
    <h2>Demonstrative Pronouns</h2>

    <p><strong>Demonstrative pronouns</strong> point to particular
    people or things. The main forms are <em>this</em>,
    <em>that</em>, <em>these</em> and <em>those</em>.</p>

    <h3>1. This and these</h3>

    <p><em>This</em> usually refers to one person or thing near
    the speaker or closely connected to the current situation.
    <em>These</em> refers to more than one.</p>

    <ul>
      <li><em>This</em> is my notebook.</li>
      <li><em>These</em> are my notes.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>2. That and those</h3>

    <p><em>That</em> usually refers to one person or thing farther
    away or less immediate. <em>Those</em> refers to more than one.</p>

    <ul>
      <li><em>That</em> belongs to the librarian.</li>
      <li><em>Those</em> are the books we ordered.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Distance can be physical, but demonstratives can also
    refer to time, ideas or information in a conversation.</p>

    <h3>3. Pronouns and determiners</h3>

    <p>The same four words can function as pronouns or determiners.</p>

    <ul>
      <li><em>This is expensive.</em> — <em>This</em> stands alone
      and functions as a pronoun.</li>
      <li><em>This phone is expensive.</em> — <em>This</em> accompanies
      the noun <em>phone</em> and functions as a determiner.</li>
      <li><em>Those are broken.</em> — <em>Those</em> functions
      as a pronoun.</li>
      <li><em>Those chairs are broken.</em> — <em>Those</em>
      accompanies the noun <em>chairs</em>.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>4. Agreement with demonstratives</h3>

    <p>Use the singular forms with singular nouns and the plural
    forms with plural nouns.</p>

    <ul>
      <li>this chair</li>
      <li>that chair</li>
      <li>these chairs</li>
      <li>those chairs</li>
    </ul>

    <p>A common error is using <em>this</em> with a plural noun
    or <em>these</em> with a singular noun.</p>

    <h3>5. Referring to ideas and events</h3>

    <p>Demonstratives can refer to something already mentioned.</p>

    <blockquote>
      The bus arrived forty minutes late. That caused several
      learners to miss the opening announcement.
    </blockquote>

    <p>Here, <em>that</em> refers to the late arrival, not to
    a physical object.</p>

    <p>When the reference is unclear, repeat the relevant noun
    phrase instead of relying on an ambiguous demonstrative.</p>

    <h3>Worked example</h3>

    <p><strong>Sentence:</strong> These are the reports we
    discussed, but those belong to another department.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> <em>These</em> stands alone and
    points to the reports being discussed.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> <em>Those</em> also stands alone
    and points to a different group of reports.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Both words refer to plural items.</p>

    <p><strong>Answer:</strong> <em>These</em> and <em>those</em>
    are demonstrative pronouns in this sentence.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Demonstrative Pronouns', 15),
  (1758, 1758, '
    <h2>Relative Pronouns</h2>

    <p><strong>Relative pronouns</strong> introduce relative
    clauses that give additional information about a noun or
    noun phrase. Common forms include <em>who</em>, <em>whom</em>,
    <em>whose</em>, <em>which</em> and <em>that</em>.</p>

    <h3>1. Who</h3>

    <p>Use <em>who</em> when referring to people and when the
    relative pronoun functions as the subject of its clause.</p>

    <blockquote>
      The learner who answered first received a certificate.
    </blockquote>

    <p>Within the relative clause, <em>who</em> is the subject
    of <em>answered</em>.</p>

    <h3>2. Whom</h3>

    <p><em>Whom</em> is the object form used for people in formal
    English.</p>

    <blockquote>
      The candidate whom the panel selected accepted the offer.
    </blockquote>

    <p>Within the relative clause, the panel selected the candidate.
    The relative pronoun is the object of <em>selected</em>.</p>

    <p>In ordinary speech, many speakers use <em>who</em> instead
    of <em>whom</em> in object positions. For formal school grammar,
    learn the distinction and follow the register required.</p>

    <h3>3. Which</h3>

    <p><em>Which</em> commonly introduces relative clauses referring
    to things or animals.</p>

    <blockquote>
      The machine which broke down has been repaired.
    </blockquote>

    <p>The clause <em>which broke down</em> identifies the machine.</p>

    <h3>4. Whose</h3>

    <p><em>Whose</em> indicates possession or association.</p>

    <blockquote>
      The girl whose bicycle was stolen reported the incident.
    </blockquote>

    <p>The bicycle belongs to or is associated with the girl.
    <em>Whose</em> can refer to people and, in some contexts,
    things or organisations.</p>

    <h3>5. That</h3>

    <p><em>That</em> can introduce a defining relative clause
    identifying a person or thing.</p>

    <blockquote>
      The phone that I bought yesterday is already damaged.
    </blockquote>

    <p>The clause <em>that I bought yesterday</em> identifies
    which phone is meant.</p>

    <p>In standard formal usage, <em>that</em> is generally not
    used to introduce a non-defining relative clause set off
    by commas.</p>

    <h3>6. Defining and non-defining relative clauses</h3>

    <p>A defining relative clause identifies which person or
    thing is meant. It is not separated by commas.</p>

    <blockquote>
      Learners who submit their work on time will receive feedback.
    </blockquote>

    <p>A non-defining relative clause adds extra information
    about an already identified person or thing and is separated
    by commas.</p>

    <blockquote>
      My brother, who lives in Embu, is visiting next week.
    </blockquote>

    <p>The commas indicate that the clause adds extra information
    rather than identifying which brother is meant.</p>

    <h3>Worked example</h3>

    <p><strong>Sentence:</strong> The engineer whose design won
    the competition thanked the team that supported her.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> <em>Whose design won the competition</em>
    identifies the engineer and expresses possession.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> <em>That supported her</em>
    identifies which team is meant.</p>

    <p><strong>Answer:</strong> <em>Whose</em> expresses possession;
    <em>that</em> introduces a defining relative clause about the team.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Relative Pronouns', 15),
  (1759, 1759, '
    <h2>Indefinite Pronouns</h2>

    <p><strong>Indefinite pronouns</strong> refer to people or
    things without identifying exactly who or what they are.</p>

    <h3>1. Indefinite pronouns for people</h3>

    <ul>
      <li><em>Someone</em> left a bag near the door.</li>
      <li><em>Everyone</em> must follow the instructions.</li>
      <li><em>Nobody</em> answered the telephone.</li>
      <li><em>Anyone</em> can apply for the position.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>These pronouns refer to people without naming each person
    individually.</p>

    <h3>2. Indefinite pronouns for things</h3>

    <ul>
      <li><em>Something</em> is blocking the pipe.</li>
      <li><em>Everything</em> is ready.</li>
      <li><em>Nothing</em> was damaged.</li>
      <li><em>Anything</em> is possible with enough time and effort.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Pronouns ending in <em>-thing</em> commonly refer to things
    or matters rather than people.</p>

    <h3>3. Singular indefinite pronouns</h3>

    <p>Pronouns such as <em>everyone</em>, <em>everybody</em>,
    <em>someone</em>, <em>anyone</em>, <em>nobody</em> and
    <em>each</em> normally take singular verbs in standard English.</p>

    <ul>
      <li>Everyone <em>is</em> ready.</li>
      <li>Each <em>has</em> a copy.</li>
      <li>Nobody <em>knows</em> the answer.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Although <em>everyone</em> refers to multiple people
    collectively, it is grammatically singular for verb agreement.</p>

    <h3>4. Indefinite pronouns with singular or plural meanings</h3>

    <p>Some forms can take singular or plural agreement depending
    on what they refer to.</p>

    <ul>
      <li><em>All of the water is</em> clean.</li>
      <li><em>All of the students are</em> present.</li>
      <li><em>Some of the money is</em> missing.</li>
      <li><em>Some of the books are</em> missing.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Check the noun or quantity being referred to instead of
    assuming that every indefinite expression takes the same verb.</p>

    <h3>5. They as a singular pronoun</h3>

    <p>In modern standard English, singular <em>they</em> can
    refer to a person whose gender is unknown, irrelevant or
    intentionally unspecified.</p>

    <blockquote>
      Someone left their umbrella in the classroom.
    </blockquote>

    <p>The pronoun <em>their</em> refers to the unknown person.
    This use is widely accepted in contemporary English.</p>

    <h3>6. Avoid ambiguity</h3>

    <p>Indefinite pronouns can make sentences unclear when the
    intended reference is not obvious.</p>

    <blockquote>
      Everyone told someone that the meeting had changed.
    </blockquote>

    <p>The sentence does not identify who told whom. Add names
    or further context when the identity matters.</p>

    <h3>Worked example</h3>

    <p><strong>Sentence:</strong> Everybody has submitted their
    work, but some are waiting for feedback.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> <em>Everybody</em> is grammatically
    singular, so it takes <em>has</em>.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> <em>Their</em> can refer back to
    <em>everybody</em> as a singular, gender-neutral form.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> <em>Some</em> refers to some of
    the people and takes the plural verb <em>are</em> here.</p>

    <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The sentence uses singular
    agreement with <em>everybody</em> and plural agreement with
    <em>some</em> referring to people.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Indefinite Pronouns', 15),
  (1760, 1760, '
    <h2>Pronoun–Antecedent Agreement</h2>

    <p>A pronoun''s <strong>antecedent</strong> is the noun or
    noun phrase it refers to. Pronoun–antecedent agreement means
    that the pronoun fits its antecedent in number and, where
    relevant, person and reference.</p>

    <h3>1. Singular antecedents</h3>

    <p>A singular antecedent normally takes a pronoun that refers
    to one person or thing.</p>

    <ul>
      <li>The boy forgot <em>his</em> bag.</li>
      <li>The girl forgot <em>her</em> bag.</li>
      <li>The machine stopped because <em>it</em> overheated.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>The pronoun should refer to the correct antecedent rather
    than simply matching the nearest noun.</p>

    <h3>2. Plural antecedents</h3>

    <p>A plural antecedent normally takes a plural pronoun.</p>

    <ul>
      <li>The players collected <em>their</em> uniforms.</li>
      <li>The teachers submitted <em>their</em> reports.</li>
      <li>The books were returned to <em>their</em> shelf.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Check that the pronoun refers to the intended noun phrase.</p>

    <h3>3. Indefinite antecedents</h3>

    <p>Indefinite pronouns such as <em>everyone</em>,
    <em>someone</em> and <em>each</em> are grammatically singular
    for agreement with verbs.</p>

    <p>Modern standard English commonly uses singular <em>they</em>
    and its forms to refer to a person whose gender is unknown
    or unspecified.</p>

    <blockquote>
      Each learner should bring their notebook.
    </blockquote>

    <p>Here, <em>each learner</em> is grammatically singular,
    while <em>their</em> is a widely accepted gender-neutral
    reference to the individual learner.</p>

    <h3>4. Compound antecedents</h3>

    <p>When two antecedents are joined by <em>and</em>, they
    normally form a plural reference.</p>

    <blockquote>
      Amina and Brian completed their assignments.
    </blockquote>

    <p>The pronoun <em>their</em> refers to both Amina and Brian.</p>

    <p>When alternatives are joined by <em>or</em> or <em>nor</em>,
    agreement can depend on the construction. In many school
    grammar exercises, the pronoun is chosen to fit the intended
    referent clearly. Rewriting a sentence may be the best way
    to avoid an awkward or ambiguous result.</p>

    <h3>5. Pronoun reference must be clear</h3>

    <p>A sentence can have a grammatically possible pronoun but
    still be unclear because more than one antecedent is possible.</p>

    <blockquote>
      When Peter met Joseph, he was carrying a suitcase.
    </blockquote>

    <p>The pronoun <em>he</em> could refer to Peter or Joseph.</p>

    <p>Rewrite the sentence to remove the ambiguity:</p>

    <blockquote>
      Peter was carrying a suitcase when he met Joseph.
    </blockquote>

    <p>Now the sentence clearly identifies Peter as the person
    carrying the suitcase.</p>

    <h3>6. Avoid mismatched number</h3>

    <p>In ordinary standard English, do not use a plural pronoun
    to refer to one specific inanimate object unless the intended
    antecedent is plural.</p>

    <ul>
      <li>Incorrect: The machine lost their power supply.</li>
      <li>Correct: The machine lost its power supply.</li>
      <li>Incorrect: The students forgot his books.</li>
      <li>Correct: The students forgot their books.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Worked example</h3>

    <p><strong>Sentence:</strong> The committee announced its
    decision after the members had reviewed their notes.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> <em>Its</em> refers to the
    committee treated as one unit.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> <em>Their</em> refers to the
    plural noun <em>members</em>.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Each pronoun agrees with the
    intended reference in this context.</p>

    <p><strong>Answer:</strong> <em>Its</em> refers to the
    collective body; <em>their</em> refers to the individual members.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Pronoun–Antecedent Agreement', 15),
  (1761, 1761, '
    <h2>Main and Auxiliary Verbs</h2>

    <p>A <strong>main verb</strong> expresses the central action, event or state in a clause. An <strong>auxiliary verb</strong> helps the main verb express tense, aspect, voice, questions, negatives or other meanings.</p>

    <h3>1. Main verbs</h3>
    <p>In each sentence, the main verb carries the central meaning.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Amina <strong>reads</strong> every evening.</li>
      <li>The learners <strong>completed</strong> the assignment.</li>
      <li>We <strong>understand</strong> the instructions.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>2. Primary auxiliary verbs</h3>
    <p>The primary auxiliaries are <strong>be, have</strong> and <strong>do</strong>. They can function as main verbs or as auxiliaries.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Be:</strong> The students <em>are preparing</em> for the examination. Here, <em>are</em> helps the main verb <em>preparing</em>.</li>
      <li><strong>Have:</strong> She <em>has finished</em> her work. Here, <em>has</em> helps the main verb <em>finished</em>.</li>
      <li><strong>Do:</strong> <em>Do</em> you understand? Here, <em>do</em> helps form a question.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>3. The same word can have different functions</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><em>They are happy.</em> — <strong>are</strong> is the main verb linking the subject to a description.</li>
      <li><em>They are working.</em> — <strong>are</strong> is an auxiliary; <strong>working</strong> is the main verb.</li>
      <li><em>She has a pen.</em> — <strong>has</strong> is the main verb meaning possesses.</li>
      <li><em>She has written a letter.</em> — <strong>has</strong> is an auxiliary helping <strong>written</strong>.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>4. Modal auxiliaries</h3>
    <p>Words such as <em>can, could, may, might, must, shall, should, will</em> and <em>would</em> are modal auxiliaries. They express meanings such as ability, possibility, obligation and intention.</p>
    <p>Example: <em>You must submit the assignment.</em> — <em>must</em> is the auxiliary and <em>submit</em> is the main verb.</p>

    <h3>How to identify the verbs</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Find the action, event or state being expressed.</li>
      <li>Identify the verb carrying that central meaning.</li>
      <li>Check whether another verb helps it form a tense, question, negative or passive construction.</li>
    </ol>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Main and Auxiliary Verbs', 15),
  (1762, 1762, '
    <h2>Action and Linking Verbs</h2>

    <p>Verbs can express actions or connect a subject to information describing or identifying it.</p>

    <h3>1. Action verbs</h3>
    <p>An action verb expresses something a person, animal or thing does. The action may be physical or mental.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>The mechanic <strong>repaired</strong> the bicycle. — physical action</li>
      <li>Wanjiku <strong>considered</strong> the proposal. — mental action</li>
      <li>The team <strong>won</strong> the match. — event or action</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>2. Linking verbs</h3>
    <p>A linking verb connects the subject to a word or phrase that describes or identifies the subject. It does not express an action performed on an object.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>The classroom <strong>is</strong> quiet.</li>
      <li>The soup <strong>smells</strong> delicious.</li>
      <li>The learners <strong>became</strong> confident.</li>
      <li>Her explanation <strong>seems</strong> correct.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>The words <em>quiet, delicious, confident</em> and <em>correct</em> describe the subjects. They are subject complements.</p>

    <h3>3. Common linking verbs</h3>
    <p>These include forms of <em>be</em> and verbs such as <em>become, seem, appear, remain, feel, look, smell, taste</em> and <em>sound</em> when they link a subject to a description.</p>

    <h3>4. A verb can serve different functions</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><em>The chef tasted the soup.</em> — <strong>tasted</strong> expresses an action.</li>
      <li><em>The soup tasted salty.</em> — <strong>tasted</strong> links the soup to the description <em>salty</em>.</li>
      <li><em>The child looked at the picture.</em> — <strong>looked</strong> expresses an action.</li>
      <li><em>The child looked tired.</em> — <strong>looked</strong> links the child to the description <em>tired</em>.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>How to distinguish them</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Identify the subject and verb.</li>
      <li>Ask whether the verb expresses an action or connects the subject to a description or identity.</li>
      <li>Use the meaning of the whole sentence. Do not classify a verb by its spelling alone.</li>
    </ol>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Action and Linking Verbs', 15),
  (1763, 1763, '
    <h2>Regular and Irregular Verbs</h2>

    <p>Verbs change form to express grammatical information. The way a verb forms its past simple and past participle distinguishes regular verbs from irregular verbs.</p>

    <h3>1. Regular verbs</h3>
    <p>Regular verbs generally form both the past simple and past participle by adding <strong>-ed</strong> or <strong>-d</strong>.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>work → worked → worked</li>
      <li>clean → cleaned → cleaned</li>
      <li>live → lived → lived</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>2. Spelling changes in regular verbs</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>For many verbs ending in <strong>-e</strong>, add <strong>-d</strong>: hope → hoped.</li>
      <li>For a consonant followed by <strong>-y</strong>, change <strong>y</strong> to <strong>i</strong> before adding <strong>-ed</strong>: study → studied.</li>
      <li>Some short verbs double the final consonant: stop → stopped.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Doubling depends on the word''s spelling and stress pattern; it does not apply to every verb ending in a consonant.</p>

    <h3>3. Irregular verbs</h3>
    <p>Irregular verbs do not follow the usual -ed pattern. Their forms must be learned as word forms.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>go → went → gone</li>
      <li>write → wrote → written</li>
      <li>see → saw → seen</li>
      <li>take → took → taken</li>
      <li>cut → cut → cut</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>4. Past simple versus past participle</h3>
    <p>The past simple can stand alone to describe a past event: <em>She wrote a letter yesterday.</em></p>
    <p>The past participle usually works with an auxiliary in perfect tenses or passive constructions: <em>She has written a letter.</em> / <em>The letter was written yesterday.</em></p>

    <h3>5. Avoid common errors</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Incorrect: <em>She has went home.</em></li>
      <li>Correct: <em>She has gone home.</em></li>
      <li>Incorrect: <em>He buyed a book.</em></li>
      <li>Correct: <em>He bought a book.</em></li>
    </ul>

    <h3>How to choose the correct form</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Determine whether the sentence needs the past simple or past participle.</li>
      <li>Check whether the verb is regular or irregular.</li>
      <li>Use the correct form with any auxiliary verb present.</li>
    </ol>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Regular and Irregular Verbs', 15),
  (1764, 1764, '
    <h2>Transitive and Intransitive Verbs</h2>

    <p>A transitive verb takes a direct object in the sentence. An intransitive verb does not take a direct object in that use.</p>

    <h3>1. Transitive verbs</h3>
    <p>A direct object receives or is affected by the action expressed by the verb.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Peter <strong>opened</strong> the gate. — What did Peter open? The gate.</li>
      <li>The class <strong>solved</strong> the problem. — What did the class solve? The problem.</li>
      <li>She <strong>sent</strong> her friend a message. — She sent a message; <em>her friend</em> is the indirect object.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>2. Intransitive verbs</h3>
    <p>An intransitive verb does not take a direct object.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>The baby <strong>slept</strong>.</li>
      <li>The visitors <strong>arrived</strong> early.</li>
      <li>The athlete <strong>ran</strong> quickly.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Words such as <em>early</em> and <em>quickly</em> provide additional information, but they are not direct objects.</p>

    <h3>3. Some verbs can be both</h3>
    <p>The same verb may be transitive in one sentence and intransitive in another.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><em>She rang the bell.</em> — Transitive: <em>the bell</em> is the object.</li>
      <li><em>The bell rang.</em> — Intransitive: there is no direct object.</li>
      <li><em>They ate supper.</em> — Transitive.</li>
      <li><em>They ate at six.</em> — Intransitive use; no direct object is stated.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>4. A reliable identification method</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Find the verb.</li>
      <li>Ask whether the sentence contains a direct object of that verb.</li>
      <li>Do not mistake an adverb or a prepositional phrase for a direct object.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>For example, in <em>The children played in the field</em>, the phrase <em>in the field</em> tells where they played. It is not a direct object.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Transitive and Intransitive Verbs', 15),
  (1765, 1765, '
    <h2>Finite and Non-finite Verbs</h2>

    <p>A <strong>finite verb</strong> is marked for tense or agrees with a subject. A <strong>non-finite verb form</strong> does not independently show tense or subject agreement in the same way.</p>

    <h3>1. Finite verbs</h3>
    <p>A finite verb anchors a clause in tense or grammatical agreement.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>She <strong>works</strong> in Nairobi.</li>
      <li>They <strong>work</strong> in Nairobi.</li>
      <li>She <strong>worked</strong> yesterday.</li>
      <li>The learners <strong>are</strong> ready.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>In the present simple, <em>works</em> and <em>work</em> differ according to the subject. <em>Worked</em> marks past tense.</p>

    <h3>2. Non-finite forms</h3>
    <p>Three important non-finite forms are the infinitive, the -ing form used as a gerund, and participles.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Infinitive:</strong> I hope <em>to succeed</em>.</li>
      <li><strong>Gerund:</strong> <em>Reading</em> improves vocabulary.</li>
      <li><strong>Participle:</strong> The <em>broken</em> window was repaired.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>3. Verbs in a sentence may form a group</h3>
    <p>In <em>She has been working all morning</em>, <em>has</em> is the finite auxiliary. <em>Been</em> and <em>working</em> are non-finite forms within the verb group.</p>
    <p>In <em>They will leave soon</em>, <em>will</em> is the finite modal auxiliary, while <em>leave</em> is a bare infinitive.</p>

    <h3>4. Do not confuse non-finite forms with tense</h3>
    <p>An -ing form does not automatically make a clause present tense. Compare:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><em>She is cooking.</em> — finite auxiliary <em>is</em> anchors the clause in the present.</li>
      <li><em>She was cooking.</em> — finite auxiliary <em>was</em> anchors the clause in the past.</li>
      <li><em>Cooking takes patience.</em> — <em>Cooking</em> functions as a non-finite form in the subject position.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>How to identify the form</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Find the verb or verb group.</li>
      <li>Identify the form that marks tense or subject agreement.</li>
      <li>Check whether another verb form depends on an auxiliary or functions inside a phrase.</li>
    </ol>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Finite and Non-finite Verbs', 15),
  (1766, 1766, '
    <h2>Infinitives</h2>

    <p>An infinitive is a non-finite form of a verb. The common form is <strong>to + base verb</strong>, as in <em>to read</em>, <em>to learn</em> and <em>to solve</em>. English also uses the <strong>bare infinitive</strong>, which has no <em>to</em>.</p>

    <h3>1. To-infinitives</h3>
    <p>A to-infinitive can perform several functions.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Purpose:</strong> She went to the library <em>to study</em>.</li>
      <li><strong>After certain verbs:</strong> They decided <em>to leave</em>.</li>
      <li><strong>After adjectives:</strong> I am happy <em>to help</em>.</li>
      <li><strong>As a subject:</strong> <em>To forgive</em> can be difficult.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>2. Bare infinitives</h3>
    <p>The base form without <em>to</em> commonly follows modal auxiliaries and certain other verbs.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>You must <em>finish</em> the work.</li>
      <li>We can <em>solve</em> the problem.</li>
      <li>The teacher made us <em>rewrite</em> the paragraph.</li>
      <li>Let him <em>answer</em>.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>After <em>make</em> in an active construction and after <em>let</em>, the bare infinitive is used. In a passive construction, <em>make</em> is followed by a to-infinitive: <em>We were made to wait.</em></p>

    <h3>3. Choosing between a gerund and an infinitive</h3>
    <p>Some verbs are followed by a to-infinitive, others by an -ing form, and some can take both with a change in meaning.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>She hopes <em>to pass</em>.</li>
      <li>He avoids <em>wasting</em> time.</li>
      <li>They stopped <em>to rest</em>. — They paused in order to rest.</li>
      <li>They stopped <em>resting</em>. — They no longer rested.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>4. Common errors</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Incorrect: <em>She can to swim.</em></li>
      <li>Correct: <em>She can swim.</em></li>
      <li>Incorrect: <em>He made me to apologise.</em></li>
      <li>Correct: <em>He made me apologise.</em></li>
    </ul>

    <h3>How to choose the correct infinitive</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Identify the verb or expression before the infinitive.</li>
      <li>Check whether it requires a to-infinitive or bare infinitive.</li>
      <li>Use the meaning of the sentence to distinguish similar constructions.</li>
    </ol>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Infinitives', 15),
  (1767, 1767, '
    <h2>Gerunds</h2>

    <p>A <strong>gerund</strong> is an -ing form of a verb that functions like a noun. It can name an activity and occupy positions such as subject, object or complement.</p>

    <h3>1. Gerunds as subjects</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><em>Reading</em> improves vocabulary.</li>
      <li><em>Swimming</em> requires practice.</li>
      <li><em>Keeping accurate records</em> saves time.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The gerund phrase names the activity being discussed.</p>

    <h3>2. Gerunds as objects</h3>
    <p>Some verbs are commonly followed by a gerund.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>She enjoys <em>drawing</em>.</li>
      <li>They avoided <em>making</em> unnecessary noise.</li>
      <li>He suggested <em>leaving</em> early.</li>
      <li>We finished <em>cleaning the classroom</em>.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>3. Gerunds after prepositions</h3>
    <p>When a verb follows a preposition, the -ing form is normally used.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>She is interested in <em>learning</em> French.</li>
      <li>He left without <em>saying</em> goodbye.</li>
      <li>They succeeded by <em>working</em> together.</li>
      <li>I look forward to <em>meeting</em> you.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>In <em>look forward to meeting</em>, the word <em>to</em> is a preposition, not an infinitive marker.</p>

    <h3>4. Gerunds and present participles</h3>
    <p>Both forms end in -ing, but their functions differ.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><em>Swimming is enjoyable.</em> — <em>Swimming</em> is a gerund functioning as the subject.</li>
      <li><em>The children are swimming.</em> — <em>Swimming</em> is a present participle in the continuous verb group.</li>
      <li><em>The swimming children waved.</em> — <em>Swimming</em> modifies <em>children</em>.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>5. Common errors</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Incorrect: <em>He is interested in learn English.</em></li>
      <li>Correct: <em>He is interested in learning English.</em></li>
      <li>Incorrect: <em>She enjoys to dance.</em></li>
      <li>Correct: <em>She enjoys dancing.</em></li>
    </ul>

    <h3>How to identify a gerund</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Find the -ing form.</li>
      <li>Check what job it performs in the sentence.</li>
      <li>If it functions as a noun, it is a gerund; if it forms a continuous verb group or modifies a noun, it is not functioning as a gerund there.</li>
    </ol>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Gerunds', 15),
  (1768, 1768, '
    <h2>Participles</h2>

    <p>Participles are verb forms used in verb groups and in phrases that modify nouns. The two major forms are the <strong>present participle</strong>, usually ending in -ing, and the <strong>past participle</strong>, which may end in -ed for regular verbs or take an irregular form.</p>

    <h3>1. Present participles</h3>
    <p>The present participle is used with forms of <em>be</em> to form continuous verb constructions.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>She <strong>is reading</strong> a novel.</li>
      <li>They <strong>were playing</strong> outside.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>It can also modify a noun:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>The <em>shining</em> stars filled the sky.</li>
      <li>We heard a <em>crying</em> child.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>2. Past participles</h3>
    <p>Past participles are used in perfect tenses, passive constructions and participial phrases.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Perfect tense:</strong> She has <em>finished</em> the work.</li>
      <li><strong>Passive voice:</strong> The window was <em>broken</em> by the ball.</li>
      <li><strong>As a modifier:</strong> The <em>damaged</em> books were replaced.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Irregular past participles include <em>written, eaten, taken, seen</em> and <em>gone</em>.</p>

    <h3>3. Participles in phrases</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><em>Exhausted by the journey</em>, the travellers rested.</li>
      <li><em>Walking along the river</em>, we saw a kingfisher.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>A participial phrase should clearly relate to the person or thing it describes. Avoid dangling participles.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Unclear: <em>Walking along the river, the birds caught our attention.</em></li>
      <li>Clear: <em>Walking along the river, we noticed the birds.</em></li>
    </ul>

    <h3>4. Participles versus gerunds</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><em>Swimming is good exercise.</em> — <em>Swimming</em> functions as a gerund.</li>
      <li><em>The swimming athlete won.</em> — <em>Swimming</em> modifies <em>athlete</em>.</li>
      <li><em>The athlete is swimming.</em> — <em>Swimming</em> is part of a continuous verb group.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The form alone does not determine the grammatical function; its role in the sentence does.</p>

    <h3>How to identify a participle</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Locate the verb form.</li>
      <li>Check whether it forms a verb group or modifies a noun.</li>
      <li>Distinguish it from a gerund when the -ing form functions as a noun.</li>
    </ol>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Participles', 15),
  (1769, 1769, '
    <h2>Present Simple</h2>

    <p>The present simple is used for habits, routines, general truths, states and situations presented as facts. It is not the default tense for every action happening at this moment.</p>

    <h3>1. Positive statements</h3>
    <p>Use the base form with <em>I, you, we</em> and <em>they</em>. With <em>he, she</em> and <em>it</em>, normally add -s or -es.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>I <strong>study</strong> English.</li>
      <li>They <strong>study</strong> English.</li>
      <li>She <strong>studies</strong> English.</li>
      <li>He <strong>watches</strong> the news.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>2. Spelling changes</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Most verbs: add -s — read → reads.</li>
      <li>Verbs ending in -s, -sh, -ch, -x or -o commonly add -es — wash → washes; go → goes.</li>
      <li>Consonant + y: change y to i and add -es — carry → carries.</li>
      <li>Vowel + y: add -s — play → plays.</li>
      <li><em>Have</em> changes to <em>has</em>; <em>be</em> has the present forms <em>am, is, are</em>.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>3. Negative statements</h3>
    <p>Use <strong>do not (don''t)</strong> or <strong>does not (doesn''t)</strong> + base verb.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>We <strong>do not walk</strong> to school.</li>
      <li>He <strong>does not walk</strong> to school.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>After <em>does</em>, use the base form: <em>does not walk</em>, not <em>does not walks</em>.</p>

    <h3>4. Questions</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Do</strong> you understand the instructions?</li>
      <li><strong>Does</strong> she understand the instructions?</li>
      <li>Where <strong>do</strong> they live?</li>
      <li>Where <strong>does</strong> he live?</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>5. Common uses</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Routine:</strong> Musa revises every evening.</li>
      <li><strong>General truth:</strong> Water boils at about 100°C at standard atmospheric pressure.</li>
      <li><strong>State:</strong> I understand the question.</li>
      <li><strong>Timetabled event:</strong> The examination begins at eight tomorrow.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>How to form it correctly</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Decide whether the subject is third-person singular.</li>
      <li>Choose the correct positive, negative or question pattern.</li>
      <li>In negatives and questions with do/does, keep the main verb in its base form.</li>
    </ol>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Present Simple', 15),
  (1770, 1770, '
    <h2>Present Continuous</h2>

    <p>The present continuous describes actions in progress around the present time, temporary situations and certain changing situations. It is formed with a present form of <strong>be + verb-ing</strong>.</p>

    <h3>1. Positive statements</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>I <strong>am revising</strong> for the examination.</li>
      <li>She <strong>is reading</strong> a novel.</li>
      <li>They <strong>are repairing</strong> the classroom roof.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Choose <em>am</em> with <em>I</em>, <em>is</em> with singular third-person subjects, and <em>are</em> with <em>you, we</em> and <em>they</em>.</p>

    <h3>2. Spelling the -ing form</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Most verbs: add -ing — read → reading.</li>
      <li>Verbs ending in silent -e: usually drop the -e — write → writing.</li>
      <li>Some short verbs double the final consonant — run → running.</li>
      <li>Verbs ending in -ie change -ie to -y — lie → lying.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Consonant doubling depends on the verb''s spelling and stress pattern; it is not automatic for every final consonant.</p>

    <h3>3. Negative statements</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>He <strong>is not sleeping</strong>.</li>
      <li>We <strong>are not waiting</strong> outside.</li>
      <li>I <strong>am not using</strong> the computer.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>4. Questions</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Are</strong> you listening?</li>
      <li><strong>Is</strong> the teacher explaining the question?</li>
      <li>What <strong>are</strong> they doing?</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>5. Common uses</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Action in progress:</strong> The learners are writing their answers now.</li>
      <li><strong>Temporary situation:</strong> I am staying with my aunt this week.</li>
      <li><strong>Change or development:</strong> The town is growing quickly.</li>
      <li><strong>Arranged future event:</strong> We are meeting the principal tomorrow.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>6. Present simple or present continuous?</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><em>She walks to school every day.</em> — routine.</li>
      <li><em>She is walking to school now.</em> — action in progress.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Some verbs that describe states, such as <em>know, believe, understand</em> and <em>own</em>, are not normally used in the continuous form when they express those states.</p>

    <h3>How to form it correctly</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Choose the correct form of <em>be</em>.</li>
      <li>Use the -ing form of the main verb.</li>
      <li>For negatives, add <em>not</em> after <em>be</em>; for questions, place <em>be</em> before the subject.</li>
    </ol>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Present Continuous', 15),
  (1771, 1771, '
    <h2>Past Simple</h2>

    <p>The past simple describes completed events, actions or situations in the past. The time may be stated directly or understood from the context.</p>

    <h3>1. Positive statements</h3>
    <p>Regular verbs usually take -ed or -d. Irregular verbs have their own forms.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>We <strong>visited</strong> our grandparents yesterday.</li>
      <li>She <strong>carried</strong> the books to class.</li>
      <li>They <strong>went</strong> home early.</li>
      <li>He <strong>bought</strong> a new exercise book.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>2. The past form of be</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>I <strong>was</strong> tired.</li>
      <li>She <strong>was</strong> absent.</li>
      <li>You <strong>were</strong> helpful.</li>
      <li>They <strong>were</strong> ready.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>3. Negative statements</h3>
    <p>Use <strong>did not (didn''t) + base verb</strong> for most verbs.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>She <strong>did not attend</strong> the meeting.</li>
      <li>They <strong>didn''t finish</strong> the task.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Do not use the past form after <em>did</em>: say <em>did not go</em>, not <em>did not went</em>.</p>
    <p>For <em>be</em>, use <em>was not</em> or <em>were not</em>: <em>He was not late.</em></p>

    <h3>4. Questions</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Did</strong> you complete the assignment?</li>
      <li>Where <strong>did</strong> she put the keys?</li>
      <li><strong>Was</strong> he at school yesterday?</li>
      <li><strong>Were</strong> they satisfied with the result?</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>5. Common uses</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Completed event:</strong> The team won the match on Saturday.</li>
      <li><strong>Sequence of events:</strong> I opened the book, read the question and wrote my answer.</li>
      <li><strong>Past habit:</strong> We walked to school when we lived nearby.</li>
      <li><strong>Past state:</strong> She knew the answer.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>How to form it correctly</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Identify whether the sentence is positive, negative or a question.</li>
      <li>Use the past form in positive statements.</li>
      <li>With <em>did</em>, return the main verb to its base form.</li>
      <li>Use <em>was</em> or <em>were</em> when the main verb is a form of <em>be</em>.</li>
    </ol>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Past Simple', 15),
  (1772, 1772, '
    <h2>Past Continuous</h2>

    <p>The past continuous describes an action that was in progress at a particular time in the past. It is formed with <strong>was/were + verb-ing</strong>.</p>

    <h3>1. Positive statements</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>I <strong>was studying</strong> at eight last night.</li>
      <li>She <strong>was cooking</strong> when the lights went out.</li>
      <li>They <strong>were playing</strong> football after school.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>2. Choosing was or were</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>I, he, she, it → <strong>was</strong></li>
      <li>You, we, they → <strong>were</strong></li>
    </ul>

    <h3>3. Negative statements and questions</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>He <strong>was not sleeping</strong>.</li>
      <li>They <strong>were not listening</strong>.</li>
      <li><strong>Was</strong> she reading?</li>
      <li>What <strong>were</strong> you doing?</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>4. An ongoing action interrupted by another event</h3>
    <p>The past continuous often describes the background action, while the past simple describes the event that happened during it.</p>
    <p><em>I was walking home when it started raining.</em></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>was walking</strong> — action already in progress.</li>
      <li><strong>started</strong> — event that occurred during that action.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>5. Two actions in progress at the same time</h3>
    <p>The past continuous can describe simultaneous activities.</p>
    <p><em>While the teacher was explaining the problem, the learners were taking notes.</em></p>

    <h3>6. Past simple or past continuous?</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><em>The phone rang at nine.</em> — reports an event.</li>
      <li><em>The phone was ringing when I entered.</em> — describes an ongoing action at that time.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Do not assume that every sentence containing <em>when</em> requires the past simple. Choose the tense according to the meaning.</p>

    <h3>How to form it correctly</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Use <em>was</em> or <em>were</em> according to the subject.</li>
      <li>Add the -ing form of the main verb.</li>
      <li>Use the past continuous for the ongoing action and the past simple when reporting a completed event, where that distinction fits the meaning.</li>
    </ol>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Past Continuous', 15),
  (1773, 1773, '
    <h2>Present Perfect</h2>

    <p>The present perfect is formed with <strong>has/have + past participle</strong>. It connects a past event or situation to the present. The event may be completed, but its present relevance matters.</p>

    <h3>1. Forming the present perfect</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>I <strong>have completed</strong> the assignment.</li>
      <li>She <strong>has written</strong> the report.</li>
      <li>They <strong>have gone</strong> home.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Use <em>has</em> with he, she and it; use <em>have</em> with I, you, we and they.</p>

    <h3>2. Experiences up to the present</h3>
    <p>Use the present perfect to describe experiences without specifying a finished past time.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>I <strong>have visited</strong> Mombasa.</li>
      <li>She <strong>has never flown</strong> in an aeroplane.</li>
      <li><strong>Have</strong> you ever <strong>read</strong> this novel?</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>3. Recent events and present results</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>He <strong>has lost</strong> his key. — He may not be able to open the door now.</li>
      <li>The rain <strong>has stopped</strong>. — It is no longer raining.</li>
      <li>We <strong>have finished</strong> the work. — The work is complete now.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>4. Situations continuing until now</h3>
    <p>The present perfect can describe a situation that began in the past and still holds true.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>She <strong>has lived</strong> here for five years.</li>
      <li>I <strong>have known</strong> him since childhood.</li>
    </ul>
    <p><em>For</em> indicates a duration; <em>since</em> indicates a starting point.</p>

    <h3>5. Present perfect or past simple?</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><em>I visited Kisumu last year.</em> — a finished past time is specified.</li>
      <li><em>I have visited Kisumu.</em> — the experience is relevant, and no finished past time is specified.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>In standard usage, do not normally combine the present perfect with a specific finished time expression such as <em>yesterday</em> or <em>last year</em>.</p>

    <h3>6. Negative statements and questions</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>She <strong>has not finished</strong>.</li>
      <li>They <strong>have not arrived</strong>.</li>
      <li><strong>Have</strong> you <strong>seen</strong> my book?</li>
      <li><strong>Has</strong> he <strong>completed</strong> the task?</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>How to form it correctly</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Choose <em>has</em> or <em>have</em> according to the subject.</li>
      <li>Use the past participle, not the past simple form.</li>
      <li>Check whether the sentence connects the past to the present or instead refers to a finished past time.</li>
    </ol>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Present Perfect', 15),
  (1774, 1774, '
    <h2>Present Perfect Continuous</h2>

    <p>The present perfect continuous is formed with <strong>has/have + been + verb-ing</strong>. It emphasises the duration or ongoing nature of an activity that began in the past and continues now, or has recently stopped with evidence in the present.</p>

    <h3>1. Forming the tense</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>I <strong>have been studying</strong> for two hours.</li>
      <li>She <strong>has been working</strong> since morning.</li>
      <li>They <strong>have been waiting</strong> for the bus.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>2. An activity continuing until now</h3>
    <p>Use this tense when the activity started earlier and is still in progress.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>It <strong>has been raining</strong> for three hours.</li>
      <li>We <strong>have been learning</strong> English since January.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>3. A recent activity with a present result</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>He is sweating because he <strong>has been running</strong>.</li>
      <li>The ground is wet because it <strong>has been raining</strong>.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The activity may have stopped, but its evidence or effect is still noticeable.</p>

    <h3>4. For and since</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>For</strong> + duration: for twenty minutes, for two years.</li>
      <li><strong>Since</strong> + starting point: since Monday, since 2022.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Example: <em>They have been practising for an hour.</em> / <em>They have been practising since 3 p.m.</em></p>

    <h3>5. Present perfect simple or continuous?</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><em>I have written three paragraphs.</em> — emphasis on the completed result or amount.</li>
      <li><em>I have been writing for an hour.</em> — emphasis on the activity and its duration.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Some verbs describing states, such as <em>know</em> and <em>believe</em>, are not normally used in the continuous form when they express those states. Say <em>I have known her for years</em>, not normally <em>I have been knowing her for years</em>.</p>

    <h3>6. Negative statements and questions</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>She <strong>has not been sleeping</strong> well.</li>
      <li>We <strong>have not been wasting</strong> time.</li>
      <li><strong>Have</strong> you <strong>been studying</strong>?</li>
      <li>How long <strong>has</strong> he <strong>been waiting</strong>?</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>How to form it correctly</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Choose <em>has</em> or <em>have</em>.</li>
      <li>Add <em>been</em>.</li>
      <li>Use the -ing form of the main verb.</li>
      <li>Check whether the focus is on the activity and its duration rather than only the completed result.</li>
    </ol>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Present Perfect Continuous', 15),
  (1775, 1775, '
    <h2>Past Perfect</h2>

    <p>The past perfect is formed with <strong>had + past participle</strong>. It can show that an event or situation happened before another past time or event.</p>

    <h3>1. Forming the past perfect</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>She <strong>had completed</strong> the assignment.</li>
      <li>They <strong>had left</strong> before the rain began.</li>
      <li>I <strong>had seen</strong> the notice earlier.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Use <em>had</em> with all subjects.</p>

    <h3>2. An earlier event and a later past event</h3>
    <p>Compare the order of events in this sentence:</p>
    <p><em>When we arrived at the station, the train had left.</em></p>
    <ul>
      <li>First: the train left.</li>
      <li>Later: we arrived.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The past perfect makes the earlier event clear.</p>

    <h3>3. With before and after</h3>
    <p>Words such as <em>before</em> and <em>after</em> can already make the sequence clear. The past perfect may be used to emphasise which event occurred first.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><em>She had locked the door before she left.</em></li>
      <li><em>After they had eaten, they returned to class.</em></li>
    </ul>

    <h3>4. Negative statements and questions</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>He <strong>had not finished</strong> when the bell rang.</li>
      <li>They <strong>had not met</strong> before the conference.</li>
      <li><strong>Had</strong> you <strong>read</strong> the instructions before you started?</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>5. Past perfect or past simple?</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><em>The teacher entered and the learners stood.</em> — a sequence of past events.</li>
      <li><em>The learners had stood before the teacher entered.</em> — the standing happened earlier than the entry.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Do not use the past perfect automatically whenever two past events appear. Use it when the earlier-than-past relationship needs to be shown or emphasised.</p>

    <h3>How to form it correctly</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Identify the past time or event used as a reference point.</li>
      <li>Determine whether another event occurred before that reference point.</li>
      <li>Use <em>had + past participle</em> for the earlier event when needed.</li>
    </ol>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Past Perfect', 15),
  (1776, 1776, '
    <h2>Past Perfect Continuous</h2>

    <p>The past perfect continuous is formed with <strong>had been + verb-ing</strong>. It emphasises an activity that continued for some time before a particular point or event in the past.</p>

    <h3>1. Forming the tense</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>I <strong>had been reading</strong> for an hour when the lights went out.</li>
      <li>They <strong>had been travelling</strong> all night before they reached the town.</li>
      <li>She <strong>had been practising</strong> before the competition began.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The form <em>had been</em> stays the same for every subject.</p>

    <h3>2. Duration before a past point</h3>
    <p>Use this tense when the length or ongoing nature of an earlier activity matters.</p>
    <p><em>By noon, the workers had been repairing the road for five hours.</em></p>
    <p>The repair activity began before noon and had continued for five hours by that point.</p>

    <h3>3. Explaining a past result</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>His clothes were wet because he <strong>had been walking</strong> in the rain.</li>
      <li>The ground was muddy because it <strong>had been raining</strong>.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The tense helps explain the circumstances or evidence present at a later past time.</p>

    <h3>4. Past perfect simple or continuous?</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><em>She had written four pages by lunchtime.</em> — emphasises the completed result or quantity.</li>
      <li><em>She had been writing for two hours by lunchtime.</em> — emphasises duration and activity.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>5. Negative statements and questions</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>They <strong>had not been sleeping</strong> well.</li>
      <li>He <strong>had not been attending</strong> practice regularly.</li>
      <li><strong>Had</strong> you <strong>been waiting</strong> long before the doctor arrived?</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>How to form it correctly</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Identify the later reference point in the past.</li>
      <li>Use <em>had been</em>.</li>
      <li>Add the -ing form of the main verb.</li>
      <li>Use the tense when the earlier activity''s duration or ongoing nature is important.</li>
    </ol>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Past Perfect Continuous', 15),
  (1777, 1777, '
    <h2>Future Forms</h2>

    <p>English uses several structures to refer to future events. The best choice depends on whether the speaker is expressing a prediction, an intention, a plan, an arrangement or a timetable.</p>

    <h3>1. Will + base verb</h3>
    <p>Use <strong>will + base verb</strong> for predictions, decisions made at the time of speaking, offers and promises.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>I think our team <strong>will win</strong>.</li>
      <li>The phone is ringing. I <strong>will answer</strong> it.</li>
      <li>I <strong>will help</strong> you carry those books.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>2. Be going to + base verb</h3>
    <p>Use <strong>am/is/are going to + base verb</strong> for prior intentions and predictions based on present evidence.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>We <strong>are going to revise</strong> together this evening.</li>
      <li>Look at those dark clouds. It <strong>is going to rain</strong>.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>3. Present continuous for arrangements</h3>
    <p>Use the present continuous for planned personal arrangements, especially when the time or details have been agreed.</p>
    <p><em>I am meeting the principal at ten tomorrow.</em></p>

    <h3>4. Present simple for timetables</h3>
    <p>The present simple often describes scheduled events.</p>
    <p><em>The examination begins at eight tomorrow morning.</em></p>

    <h3>5. Future continuous</h3>
    <p>Use <strong>will be + verb-ing</strong> for an activity that will be in progress at a future time.</p>
    <p><em>At this time tomorrow, we will be sitting our examination.</em></p>

    <h3>6. Future perfect</h3>
    <p>Use <strong>will have + past participle</strong> for something expected to be completed before a future point.</p>
    <p><em>By Friday, she will have completed the project.</em></p>

    <h3>7. Common errors</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Incorrect: <em>She will goes home.</em></li>
      <li>Correct: <em>She will go home.</em></li>
      <li>Incorrect: <em>They are going to studying.</em></li>
      <li>Correct: <em>They are going to study.</em></li>
    </ul>

    <h3>How to choose a future form</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Identify the intended meaning: prediction, intention, arrangement, schedule or activity in progress.</li>
      <li>Choose the structure that expresses that meaning.</li>
      <li>Use the correct verb form after the auxiliary or phrase.</li>
    </ol>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Future Forms', 15),
  (1778, 1778, '
    <h2>Choosing the Correct Tense</h2>

    <p>Choosing a tense is not just about spotting a time word. Consider when the event happens, whether it is complete or ongoing, and how it relates to another event or to the present.</p>

    <h3>1. Identify the time reference</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Habit or general truth:</strong> The earth revolves around the Sun.</li>
      <li><strong>Action in progress now:</strong> The learners are writing.</li>
      <li><strong>Completed past event:</strong> The learners wrote yesterday.</li>
      <li><strong>Ongoing past action:</strong> They were writing when the bell rang.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>2. Decide what the sentence emphasises</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Present perfect simple:</strong> She has completed three exercises. — completed result.</li>
      <li><strong>Present perfect continuous:</strong> She has been practising for an hour. — activity and duration.</li>
      <li><strong>Past perfect:</strong> She had left before I arrived. — an earlier past event.</li>
      <li><strong>Past perfect continuous:</strong> She had been waiting for an hour when I arrived. — duration before a past point.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>3. Check the time expression</h3>
    <p>Time expressions provide clues, but context determines the best tense.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><em>every day</em> often signals a routine.</li>
      <li><em>right now</em> often signals an action in progress.</li>
      <li><em>yesterday</em> normally points to the past simple for a completed event.</li>
      <li><em>since Monday</em> or <em>for two hours</em> can accompany perfect forms when the situation connects to a reference point.</li>
      <li><em>by Friday</em> may indicate completion before a future deadline.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>4. Keep the time relationship clear</h3>
    <p>Compare:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><em>When I arrived, the lesson started.</em> — the arrival and start are presented as past events in sequence.</li>
      <li><em>When I arrived, the lesson had started.</em> — the lesson started before my arrival.</li>
      <li><em>When I arrived, the lesson was starting.</em> — the lesson was in the process of starting.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>5. Maintain consistency without making tense changes mechanical</h3>
    <p>In a narrative, the tense may change when the time reference changes. A change is not automatically an error.</p>
    <p><em>Yesterday, we visited the museum. Today, we are writing a report about it.</em></p>

    <h3>A practical decision process</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Locate the time reference.</li>
      <li>Decide whether the action is habitual, ongoing, completed or earlier than another event.</li>
      <li>Choose the tense that expresses that relationship.</li>
      <li>Check subject agreement and the verb form required by the tense.</li>
    </ol>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Choosing the Correct Tense', 15),
  (1779, 1779, '
    <h2>Distinguishing the Subject from Nearby Nouns</h2>

    <p>A verb agrees with its grammatical subject, not necessarily the noun closest to it. Extra phrases can place a singular or plural noun near the verb and make agreement confusing.</p>

    <h3>1. Ignore nouns inside prepositional phrases</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><em>The colour of the uniforms <strong>is</strong> blue.</em></li>
      <li><em>The uniforms of the team <strong>are</strong> blue.</em></li>
      <li><em>The teacher with several learners <strong>is</strong> outside.</em></li>
    </ul>
    <p>Identify the head noun of the subject phrase before selecting the verb.</p>

    <h3>2. Do not let a nearby noun control agreement</h3>
    <p>Compare these sentences:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><em>The box of old books <strong>belongs</strong> to Amina.</em> — subject head: <strong>box</strong>.</li>
      <li><em>The old books in the box <strong>belong</strong> to Amina.</em> — subject head: <strong>books</strong>.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>3. Separate the subject from an inserted phrase</h3>
    <p>An expression between the subject and verb may add information without changing the subject''s number.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><em>The principal, along with the teachers, <strong>is</strong> attending.</em></li>
      <li><em>The learners, as well as their class teacher, <strong>are</strong> attending.</em></li>
    </ul>
    <p>Phrases such as <em>along with</em>, <em>as well as</em> and <em>together with</em> do not normally create a compound subject.</p>

    <h3>4. Find the subject before checking the verb</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Locate the verb that needs checking.</li>
      <li>Identify the clause''s subject.</li>
      <li>Find the head noun or pronoun.</li>
      <li>Ignore nearby nouns that belong to descriptive or inserted phrases.</li>
      <li>Choose the verb that agrees with the actual subject.</li>
    </ol>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Distinguishing the Subject from Nearby Nouns', 15),
  (1780, 1780, '
    <h2>Singular Subjects in Present Simple</h2>

    <p>In the present simple, most third-person singular subjects take a main verb ending in <strong>-s</strong> or <strong>-es</strong>. The subject may be a singular noun, a singular pronoun such as <em>he</em> or <em>she</em>, or a singular noun phrase.</p>

    <h3>1. Basic agreement</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>The learner <strong>reads</strong> every evening.</li>
      <li>My sister <strong>works</strong> in town.</li>
      <li>He <strong>plays</strong> basketball.</li>
      <li>The machine <strong>makes</strong> a loud noise.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>2. Common spelling patterns</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Most verbs add -s: learn → learns.</li>
      <li>Many verbs ending in -sh, -ch, -ss, -x or -o add -es: wash → washes; watch → watches; go → goes.</li>
      <li>Consonant + y changes to -ies: carry → carries.</li>
      <li>Vowel + y takes -s: play → plays.</li>
      <li>Have → has; do → does; be → is.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>3. Singular subjects in longer phrases</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><em>The captain of the teams <strong>speaks</strong> first.</em></li>
      <li><em>The water in these bottles <strong>looks</strong> clean.</li>
      <li><em>Each learner in the class <strong>receives</strong> a booklet.</em></li>
    </ul>
    <p>Look at the head noun or pronoun. A plural noun inside the subject phrase does not automatically make the subject plural.</p>

    <h3>4. Negative statements and questions</h3>
    <p>Use <strong>does not + base verb</strong> for negatives and <strong>does + subject + base verb</strong> for questions.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>She <strong>does not understand</strong> the instruction.</li>
      <li><strong>Does</strong> she <strong>understand</strong> the instruction?</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The -s marking belongs to <em>does</em>; do not add it to the main verb in these constructions.</p>

    <h3>5. Common errors</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Incorrect: <em>He walk to school.</em></li>
      <li>Correct: <em>He walks to school.</em></li>
      <li>Incorrect: <em>Does she likes music?</em></li>
      <li>Correct: <em>Does she like music?</em></li>
    </ul>

    <h3>How to check agreement</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Confirm that the sentence is in the present simple.</li>
      <li>Identify whether the subject is third-person singular.</li>
      <li>Use the correct verb form, remembering that questions and negatives with <em>does</em> take the base form of the main verb.</li>
    </ol>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Singular Subjects in Present Simple', 15),
  (1781, 1781, '
    <h2>Singular Subjects with Auxiliary Verbs</h2>

    <p>When a sentence contains an auxiliary verb, agreement depends on the auxiliary or verb form that carries it. The main verb may not take the usual third-person singular -s ending.</p>

    <h3>1. Be: am, is and are</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>I <strong>am</strong> ready.</li>
      <li>She <strong>is</strong> reading.</li>
      <li>The learner <strong>is</strong> prepared.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>For a singular third-person subject, use <em>is</em> in the present tense.</p>

    <h3>2. Have and has</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>He <strong>has</strong> completed the task.</li>
      <li>The school <strong>has</strong> introduced a new timetable.</li>
      <li>They <strong>have</strong> completed the task.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>3. Do and does</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>She <strong>does</strong> not play chess.</li>
      <li><strong>Does</strong> he understand the question?</li>
    </ul>
    <p>After <em>does</em>, the main verb uses its base form: <em>does not play</em>, not <em>does not plays</em>.</p>

    <h3>4. Modal auxiliaries</h3>
    <p>Modal auxiliaries such as <em>can, will, should, must</em> and <em>may</em> do not change form for a singular subject.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>She <strong>can solve</strong> the problem.</li>
      <li>He <strong>will arrive</strong> soon.</li>
      <li>The learner <strong>must revise</strong>.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>5. Auxiliary chains</h3>
    <p>In a longer verb group, check the first finite auxiliary for agreement.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>She <strong>has been studying</strong>.</li>
      <li>He <strong>is being interviewed</strong>.</li>
      <li>The learner <strong>will have finished</strong> by noon.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Common errors</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Incorrect: <em>She have finished.</em></li>
      <li>Correct: <em>She has finished.</em></li>
      <li>Incorrect: <em>He can solves it.</em></li>
      <li>Correct: <em>He can solve it.</em></li>
    </ul>

    <h3>How to check</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Find the subject.</li>
      <li>Identify the auxiliary or verb group.</li>
      <li>Check whether the auxiliary changes with the subject.</li>
      <li>Do not add a third-person singular ending to a main verb already following a modal or <em>does</em>.</li>
    </ol>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Singular Subjects with Auxiliary Verbs', 15),
  (1782, 1782, '
    <h2>Agreement with Plural Subjects</h2>

    <p>Plural subjects generally take plural verb forms. In the present simple, most main verbs use the base form without the third-person singular -s ending.</p>

    <h3>1. Basic present simple agreement</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>The learner <strong>reads</strong> daily.</li>
      <li>The learners <strong>read</strong> daily.</li>
      <li>She <strong>plays</strong> volleyball.</li>
      <li>They <strong>play</strong> volleyball.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>2. Plural subjects with be</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>The players <strong>are</strong> ready.</li>
      <li>The books <strong>are</strong> on the shelf.</li>
      <li>The teachers <strong>were</strong> present yesterday.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>3. Plural subjects with have and do</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>The learners <strong>have</strong> completed the work.</li>
      <li>They <strong>do</strong> not understand the question.</li>
      <li><strong>Do</strong> the students need help?</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>4. Do not rely on the nearest noun</h3>
    <p>The subject''s head noun determines agreement.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><em>The boxes of chalk <strong>are</strong> in the cupboard.</em></li>
      <li><em>The box of chalk <strong>is</strong> in the cupboard.</em></li>
    </ul>

    <h3>5. Plural subjects with modal auxiliaries</h3>
    <p>Modal auxiliaries keep the same form with singular and plural subjects.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>The learner <strong>will attend</strong>.</li>
      <li>The learners <strong>will attend</strong>.</li>
      <li>The learners <strong>can solve</strong> the problem.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>How to check agreement</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Identify the grammatical subject.</li>
      <li>Confirm that its head noun or pronoun is plural.</li>
      <li>Choose the matching verb form.</li>
      <li>Remember that plural subjects normally take the base form of a main verb in the present simple.</li>
    </ol>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Agreement with Plural Subjects', 15),
  (1783, 1783, '
    <h2>Plural Subjects with Auxiliary Verbs</h2>

    <p>Plural subjects use the appropriate plural forms of auxiliaries such as <em>be, have</em> and <em>do</em>. Modal auxiliaries remain unchanged.</p>

    <h3>1. Be with plural subjects</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>The learners <strong>are studying</strong>.</li>
      <li>The learners <strong>were studying</strong> when the bell rang.</li>
      <li>The assignments <strong>are being marked</strong>.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>2. Have with plural subjects</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>The pupils <strong>have completed</strong> the task.</li>
      <li>The players <strong>have been practising</strong> since morning.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>3. Do with plural subjects</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>They <strong>do not understand</strong> the instruction.</li>
      <li><strong>Do</strong> the learners <strong>need</strong> extra time?</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>4. Modal auxiliaries do not change</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>The learner <strong>can answer</strong>.</li>
      <li>The learners <strong>can answer</strong>.</li>
      <li>The learner <strong>will arrive</strong>.</li>
      <li>The learners <strong>will arrive</strong>.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>5. Check the complete verb group</h3>
    <p>In a sentence such as <em>The learners have been working</em>, the plural subject takes <em>have</em>. The forms <em>been</em> and <em>working</em> do not change according to the subject.</p>

    <h3>Common errors</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Incorrect: <em>The students is working.</em></li>
      <li>Correct: <em>The students are working.</em></li>
      <li>Incorrect: <em>The players has arrived.</em></li>
      <li>Correct: <em>The players have arrived.</em></li>
      <li>Incorrect: <em>The students can solves it.</em></li>
      <li>Correct: <em>The students can solve it.</em></li>
    </ul>

    <h3>How to check</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Find the subject and confirm it is plural.</li>
      <li>Identify the auxiliary that carries tense or agreement.</li>
      <li>Use <em>are/were</em>, <em>have</em> or <em>do</em> where required.</li>
      <li>Keep the main verb in its correct form after the auxiliary.</li>
    </ol>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Plural Subjects with Auxiliary Verbs', 15),
  (1784, 1784, '
    <h2>Compound Subjects</h2>

    <p>A compound subject contains two or more subjects linked within the same clause. The verb form depends on how the subjects are joined and whether they refer to separate things or a single combined idea.</p>

    <h3>1. Subjects joined by and</h3>
    <p>Two separate subjects joined by <strong>and</strong> usually take a plural verb.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><em>Grace and Peter <strong>are</strong> classmates.</em></li>
      <li><em>The teacher and the learners <strong>have</strong> arrived.</em></li>
      <li><em>Rice and beans <strong>are</strong> available today.</em></li>
    </ul>

    <h3>2. A single idea or unit</h3>
    <p>Some expressions joined by <em>and</em> are treated as one unit and can take a singular verb.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><em>Fish and chips <strong>is</strong> today''s special.</em> — the dish is treated as one item on the menu.</li>
      <li><em>Research and development <strong>is</strong> a major department here.</em> — the phrase names one department.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Meaning and context determine whether the expression is treated as one unit or as separate items.</p>

    <h3>3. Each subject can have its own modifiers</h3>
    <p>Do not mistake a long compound subject for a single subject merely because its words are close together.</p>
    <p><em>The head teacher of the school and the chairperson of the board <strong>are</strong> attending.</em></p>
    <p>There are two distinct people, so the compound subject is plural.</p>

    <h3>4. Subjects joined by and versus descriptive phrases</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><em>The teacher and the learner <strong>are</strong> present.</em> — two subjects joined by <em>and</em>.</li>
      <li><em>The teacher, together with the learner, <strong>is</strong> present.</em> — one subject plus an additional phrase.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>How to choose the verb</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Identify each subject in the compound.</li>
      <li>Check how the subjects are connected.</li>
      <li>For two separate subjects joined by <em>and</em>, normally use a plural verb.</li>
      <li>Check whether the expression names one recognised unit or combined idea.</li>
    </ol>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Compound Subjects', 15),
  (1785, 1785, '
    <h2>Subjects Joined by And</h2>

    <p>When two or more separate subjects are joined by <strong>and</strong>, they usually form a plural subject and take a plural verb.</p>

    <h3>1. Two subjects</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><em>Mary and Joseph <strong>are</strong> studying.</em></li>
      <li><em>The dog and the cat <strong>sleep</strong> outside.</em></li>
      <li><em>Mathematics and English <strong>are</strong> compulsory subjects in this timetable.</em></li>
    </ul>

    <h3>2. More than two subjects</h3>
    <p>A list of separate subjects joined by <em>and</em> also normally takes a plural verb.</p>
    <p><em>The head teacher, the deputy and the class teachers <strong>have</strong> signed the report.</em></p>

    <h3>3. A singular verb for one combined unit</h3>
    <p>When the compound expression is understood as one unit, one dish, or one combined idea, a singular verb may be appropriate.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><em>Slow and steady <strong>wins</strong> the race.</em> — the phrase expresses a single idea.</li>
      <li><em>Peanut butter and jam <strong>is</strong> my usual sandwich filling.</em> — the expression names one combined filling.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>4. Each and every before compound subjects</h3>
    <p>When <em>each</em> or <em>every</em> is used before singular subjects joined by <em>and</em>, a singular verb is often used.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><em>Every teacher and learner <strong>is</strong> expected to attend.</em></li>
      <li><em>Each boy and girl <strong>has</strong> received a form.</em></li>
    </ul>

    <h3>5. Do not confuse and with an inserted phrase</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><em>The chairperson and the secretary <strong>are</strong> here.</em> — two subjects joined by <em>and</em>.</li>
      <li><em>The chairperson, as well as the secretary, <strong>is</strong> here.</em> — singular main subject plus an additional phrase.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>How to decide</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Find every subject joined by <em>and</em>.</li>
      <li>Determine whether they refer to separate entities or one unit.</li>
      <li>Use a plural verb for separate subjects, unless a specific construction calls for singular agreement.</li>
      <li>Check for <em>each</em> or <em>every</em>, which can affect the agreement pattern.</li>
    </ol>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Subjects Joined by And', 15),
  (1786, 1786, '
    <h2>Subjects Joined by Or and Nor</h2>
    <p>When two subjects are joined by <strong>or</strong> or <strong>nor</strong>, the verb usually agrees with the subject nearest to it.</p>

    <h3>1. When both subjects are singular</h3>
    <p>Use a singular verb when the nearer subject is singular.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> Either the teacher or the learner <em>is</em> responsible.</p>

    <h3>2. When both subjects are plural</h3>
    <p>Use a plural verb when the nearer subject is plural.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> Neither the teachers nor the learners <em>are</em> ready.</p>

    <h3>3. When one subject is singular and the other is plural</h3>
    <p>The verb generally agrees with the subject closest to it.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> Either the captain or the players <em>are</em> attending.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> Either the players or the captain <em>is</em> attending.</p>

    <h3>How to check</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Find the subjects joined by <em>or</em> or <em>nor</em>.</li>
      <li>Identify the subject nearest to the verb.</li>
      <li>Choose the verb form that agrees with that subject.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>When the sentence sounds awkward because the subjects differ in number, rewrite it to place subjects of the same number together or repeat the subject in a clearer sentence.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Subjects Joined by Or and Nor', 15),
  (1787, 1787, '
    <h2>Special Subject Forms</h2>
    <p>Some subjects require extra attention because their grammatical form does not always make their number obvious.</p>

    <h3>1. Indefinite pronouns</h3>
    <p>Words such as <em>everyone, somebody, each</em> and <em>neither</em> are usually grammatically singular.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> Everyone <em>has</em> submitted the assignment.</p>

    <h3>2. Collective nouns</h3>
    <p>A collective noun names a group, such as a team, committee or family. In many formal school contexts, it takes a singular verb when the group acts as one unit.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> The committee <em>has</em> reached a decision.</p>
    <p>Plural agreement can occur in varieties of English, particularly British English, when members are considered individually. Follow the intended meaning and the variety required by the task.</p>

    <h3>3. Each, every, either and neither</h3>
    <p>When these words form the subject, they generally take singular verbs.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> Each of the candidates <em>is</em> prepared.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> Every desk and every chair <em>has</em> been cleaned.</p>

    <h3>How to check</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Find the actual subject, not a noun inside a phrase after it.</li>
      <li>Check whether the subject is an indefinite pronoun, collective noun or special expression.</li>
      <li>Choose the verb according to the subject''s grammatical number and intended meaning.</li>
    </ol>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Special Subject Forms', 15),
  (1788, 1788, '
    <h2>Indefinite Pronoun Agreement</h2>
    <p>An indefinite pronoun refers to a person, thing or amount without identifying it precisely. The verb depends on the pronoun''s grammatical number.</p>

    <h3>1. Singular indefinite pronouns</h3>
    <p>Use a singular verb with <em>everyone, everybody, someone, somebody, anyone, anybody, no one, nobody, each, either</em> and <em>neither</em>.</p>
    <p><strong>Examples:</strong></p>
    <ul>
      <li>Someone <em>has</em> left a notebook here.</li>
      <li>Neither <em>was</em> available.</li>
      <li>Everybody <em>understands</em> the instructions.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>2. Plural indefinite pronouns</h3>
    <p>Use a plural verb with <em>both, few, many</em> and <em>several</em>.</p>
    <p><strong>Examples:</strong></p>
    <ul>
      <li>Both <em>are</em> ready.</li>
      <li>Several <em>have</em> arrived.</li>
      <li>Few <em>know</em> the answer.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>3. Pronouns whose number depends on context</h3>
    <p><em>All, any, most, none</em> and <em>some</em> can take singular or plural verbs depending on what they refer to.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Some of the water <em>is</em> missing. (uncountable water)</li>
      <li>Some of the books <em>are</em> missing. (plural books)</li>
      <li>All of the food <em>is</em> ready.</li>
      <li>All of the students <em>are</em> ready.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>How to decide</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Identify the indefinite pronoun.</li>
      <li>Determine whether it is singular, plural or dependent on the noun it refers to.</li>
      <li>Check the verb against that grammatical number.</li>
    </ol>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Indefinite Pronoun Agreement', 15),
  (1789, 1789, '
    <h2>Collective Noun Agreement</h2>
    <p>A collective noun names a group of people, animals or things as one unit. Examples include <em>team, family, committee, class, audience</em> and <em>flock</em>.</p>

    <h3>1. The group acting as one unit</h3>
    <p>Use a singular verb when the group is considered as a single body.</p>
    <p><strong>Examples:</strong></p>
    <ul>
      <li>The class <em>is</em> taking an examination.</li>
      <li>The committee <em>has</em> approved the plan.</li>
      <li>The flock <em>moves</em> across the field.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>2. Members acting individually</h3>
    <p>In British English, a collective noun can take a plural verb when attention is on the individual members.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> The team <em>are</em> arguing among themselves.</p>
    <p>In American English, singular agreement is more common with collective nouns. In school assessments, use the variety and convention expected by the question.</p>

    <h3>3. Do not confuse a collective noun with a plural noun</h3>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> The team <em>is</em> ready. (one team)</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> The players <em>are</em> ready. (individual players, plural noun)</p>

    <h3>How to check</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Identify the collective noun.</li>
      <li>Ask whether the sentence treats the group as one unit or emphasises its members.</li>
      <li>Use the agreement convention appropriate to the sentence and required variety of English.</li>
    </ol>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Collective Noun Agreement', 15),
  (1790, 1790, '
    <h2>Each, Every, Either and Neither</h2>
    <p><em>Each, every, either</em> and <em>neither</em> commonly take singular verbs when they form the subject or determine the subject.</p>

    <h3>1. Each</h3>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> Each of the pupils <em>has</em> a textbook.</p>
    <p>The noun after <em>of</em> does not change the singular subject <em>each</em>.</p>

    <h3>2. Every</h3>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> Every participant <em>receives</em> a certificate.</p>
    <p>When two nouns are joined by <em>and</em> but each is preceded by <em>every</em>, the subject is generally treated as singular.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> Every boy and every girl <em>is</em> expected to attend.</p>

    <h3>3. Either and neither</h3>
    <p>When used alone to refer to one of two choices, these pronouns generally take singular verbs.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Either <em>is</em> acceptable.</li>
      <li>Neither <em>was</em> available.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>4. Either ... or and neither ... nor</h3>
    <p>When these expressions join two subjects, the verb generally agrees with the subject nearest to it.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> Neither the pupils nor the teacher <em>is</em> absent.</p>

    <h3>How to check</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Determine whether the word is the subject itself or part of a paired construction.</li>
      <li>Use singular agreement for each, every, either and neither when they independently form the subject.</li>
      <li>For either ... or and neither ... nor, check the nearer subject.</li>
    </ol>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Each, Every, Either and Neither', 15),
  (1791, 1791, '
    <h2>Agreement with Intervening Phrases</h2>
    <p>An intervening phrase comes between the subject and the verb. It may contain a noun that distracts you from the real subject.</p>

    <h3>1. Find the main subject</h3>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> The box of exercise books <em>is</em> on the table.</p>
    <p>The subject is <em>box</em>, not <em>exercise books</em>. Since <em>box</em> is singular, use <em>is</em>.</p>

    <h3>2. Ignore phrases beginning with of, with, in or along with</h3>
    <p><strong>Examples:</strong></p>
    <ul>
      <li>The captain, along with the players, <em>is</em> attending.</li>
      <li>The quality of the answers <em>is</em> improving.</li>
      <li>The books in the cupboard <em>belong</em> to the library.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The intervening phrase adds information but does not usually change the grammatical number of the main subject.</p>

    <h3>3. Check the subject, not the nearest noun</h3>
    <p><strong>Incorrect:</strong> The list of names <em>are</em> missing.</p>
    <p><strong>Correct:</strong> The list of names <em>is</em> missing.</p>

    <h3>How to solve it</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Locate the main verb.</li>
      <li>Ask who or what performs the action or is described.</li>
      <li>Remove the intervening phrase mentally.</li>
      <li>Make the verb agree with the main subject.</li>
    </ol>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Agreement with Intervening Phrases', 15),
  (1792, 1792, '
    <h2>Correcting Subject–Verb Agreement Errors</h2>
    <p>To correct subject–verb agreement, identify the real subject, determine its grammatical number and choose the matching verb. Do not change a sentence by guessing from the nearest noun.</p>

    <h3>1. Find the subject</h3>
    <p><strong>Incorrect:</strong> The list of topics <em>are</em> on the noticeboard.</p>
    <p><strong>Correct:</strong> The list of topics <em>is</em> on the noticeboard.</p>
    <p>The subject is <em>list</em>, which is singular.</p>

    <h3>2. Check the subject''s number</h3>
    <p><strong>Incorrect:</strong> The learners <em>was</em> late.</p>
    <p><strong>Correct:</strong> The learners <em>were</em> late.</p>

    <h3>3. Check compound subjects</h3>
    <p><strong>Incorrect:</strong> A pen and a ruler <em>is</em> required.</p>
    <p><strong>Correct:</strong> A pen and a ruler <em>are</em> required.</p>
    <p>Two subjects joined by <em>and</em> generally take a plural verb.</p>

    <h3>4. Check special subjects</h3>
    <p><strong>Incorrect:</strong> Everyone <em>know</em> the rules.</p>
    <p><strong>Correct:</strong> Everyone <em>knows</em> the rules.</p>

    <h3>5. Use a repeatable correction method</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Find the main verb.</li>
      <li>Identify its subject.</li>
      <li>Ignore distracting phrases.</li>
      <li>Check whether the subject is singular or plural.</li>
      <li>Correct the verb and reread the full sentence.</li>
    </ol>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Correcting Subject–Verb Agreement Errors', 15),
  (1793, 1793, '
    <h2>Sentence Parts</h2>
    <p>A sentence can contain a subject, a predicate, objects and complements. Each part performs a different grammatical function.</p>

    <h3>1. Subject</h3>
    <p>The subject identifies who or what the sentence is about, or who or what performs the action.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> <em>The learners</em> completed the task.</p>

    <h3>2. Predicate</h3>
    <p>The predicate tells us something about the subject. It contains the verb and may include objects or other information.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> The learners <em>completed the task before lunch</em>.</p>

    <h3>3. Object</h3>
    <p>An object receives the action of a verb or identifies the person or thing affected by it.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> Amina opened <em>the window</em>.</p>

    <h3>4. Complement</h3>
    <p>A complement completes the meaning of a verb by identifying or describing the subject or object.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Subject complement: The room is <em>quiet</em>.</li>
      <li>Object complement: They elected Amina <em>chairperson</em>.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>How to analyse a sentence</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Find the verb.</li>
      <li>Identify the subject.</li>
      <li>Check whether the verb has an object.</li>
      <li>Check whether a complement identifies or describes the subject or object.</li>
    </ol>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Sentence Parts', 15),
  (1794, 1794, '
    <h2>Subjects and Predicates</h2>
    <p>A complete sentence normally contains a subject and a predicate. The subject tells us who or what the sentence is about; the predicate tells us what the subject does, is or experiences.</p>

    <h3>1. Finding the subject</h3>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> <em>The new student</em> answered the question.</p>
    <p>The subject is the person who answered.</p>

    <h3>2. Finding the predicate</h3>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> The new student <em>answered the question confidently</em>.</p>
    <p>The predicate contains the verb and the information given about the subject.</p>

    <h3>3. Simple and complete subjects</h3>
    <p>The <strong>simple subject</strong> is the main noun or pronoun. The <strong>complete subject</strong> includes the words that describe or specify it.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> <em>The tall boy near the gate</em> waved.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Simple subject: <em>boy</em></li>
      <li>Complete subject: <em>The tall boy near the gate</em></li>
    </ul>

    <h3>4. Simple and complete predicates</h3>
    <p>The <strong>simple predicate</strong> is the main verb or verb phrase. The <strong>complete predicate</strong> includes the verb and the words that complete the information.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> The tall boy near the gate <em>waved to his friend</em>.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Simple predicate: <em>waved</em></li>
      <li>Complete predicate: <em>waved to his friend</em></li>
    </ul>

    <h3>How to find them</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Locate the main verb.</li>
      <li>Ask who or what the sentence is about.</li>
      <li>Mark the complete subject.</li>
      <li>Identify the predicate that tells us about the subject.</li>
    </ol>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Subjects and Predicates', 15),
  (1795, 1795, '
    <h2>Direct and Indirect Objects</h2>
    <p>Some verbs can take two objects. The direct object identifies what is given, sent, shown or done. The indirect object identifies the person or recipient who receives it.</p>

    <h3>1. Direct object</h3>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> The teacher marked <em>the exercise</em>.</p>
    <p>Ask, “Marked what?” The answer, <em>the exercise</em>, is the direct object.</p>

    <h3>2. Indirect object</h3>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> The teacher gave <em>the learner</em> a book.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Gave what? <em>a book</em> — direct object.</li>
      <li>Gave a book to whom? <em>the learner</em> — indirect object.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>3. Two common sentence patterns</h3>
    <p><strong>Pattern A:</strong> Subject + verb + indirect object + direct object.</p>
    <p>Example: Musa sent <em>his friend</em> <em>a message</em>.</p>
    <p><strong>Pattern B:</strong> Subject + verb + direct object + to/for + recipient.</p>
    <p>Example: Musa sent <em>a message</em> to <em>his friend</em>.</p>

    <h3>4. Do not confuse an object with a subject complement</h3>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> They elected Amina captain.</p>
    <p><em>Amina</em> is the object, and <em>captain</em> is an object complement that identifies Amina''s new role.</p>

    <h3>How to identify objects</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Find the action verb.</li>
      <li>Ask what or whom the action affects to locate the direct object.</li>
      <li>If someone receives something, identify the recipient as the indirect object.</li>
      <li>Check whether the recipient is introduced by a preposition such as <em>to</em> or <em>for</em>.</li>
    </ol>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Direct and Indirect Objects', 15),
  (1796, 1796, '
    <h2>Subject and Object Complements</h2>
    <p>A complement completes the meaning of a sentence by identifying or describing a subject or an object.</p>

    <h3>1. Subject complements</h3>
    <p>A subject complement follows a linking verb such as <em>be, seem, become, appear</em> or <em>feel</em>. It describes or identifies the subject.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>The classroom is <em>quiet</em>. (adjective describing the subject)</li>
      <li>Wanjiku became <em>the captain</em>. (noun phrase identifying the subject)</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>2. Object complements</h3>
    <p>An object complement follows an object and describes or identifies it.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>The class elected Wanjiku <em>captain</em>.</li>
      <li>The news made the learners <em>happy</em>.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>In the first example, <em>captain</em> identifies Wanjiku''s role. In the second, <em>happy</em> describes the learners.</p>

    <h3>3. Complements are not the same as objects</h3>
    <p>In “The learner became confident,” <em>confident</em> is a subject complement, not an object. The linking verb describes the subject rather than transferring an action to an object.</p>

    <h3>How to identify a complement</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Find the verb.</li>
      <li>Check whether it links the subject to a description or identity.</li>
      <li>If a word describes or renames the subject after a linking verb, it is a subject complement.</li>
      <li>If a word describes or renames the object, it is an object complement.</li>
    </ol>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Subject and Object Complements', 15),
  (1797, 1797, '
    <h2>Recognising Phrases</h2>
    <p>A phrase is a group of related words that functions as a unit in a sentence. A phrase does not contain a complete subject–predicate relationship of its own.</p>

    <h3>1. Noun phrases</h3>
    <p>A noun phrase functions like a noun.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> <em>The tall girl near the window</em> answered.</p>

    <h3>2. Verb phrases</h3>
    <p>A verb phrase contains a main verb and may include auxiliary verbs.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> The learners <em>have been revising</em>.</p>

    <h3>3. Adjective phrases</h3>
    <p>An adjective phrase describes a noun or pronoun.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> The learner <em>proud of her progress</em> smiled.</p>

    <h3>4. Adverbial phrases</h3>
    <p>An adverbial phrase gives information such as when, where, how or why.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> The bus arrived <em>before sunrise</em>.</p>

    <h3>5. Prepositional phrases</h3>
    <p>A prepositional phrase begins with a preposition and includes its object.</p>
    <p><strong>Examples:</strong> <em>under the table</em>, <em>after the lesson</em>, <em>with great care</em>.</p>

    <h3>Phrase or clause?</h3>
    <p><em>After the examination</em> is a phrase. <em>After the examination ended</em> is a clause because it contains a subject and a verb.</p>

    <h3>How to identify a phrase</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Look for a group of words working together.</li>
      <li>Determine the group''s function in the sentence.</li>
      <li>Check whether it contains a subject and verb expressing a clause relationship.</li>
    </ol>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Recognising Phrases', 15),
  (1798, 1798, '
    <h2>Main Clauses</h2>
    <p>A main clause, also called an independent clause, contains a subject and a predicate and can stand alone as a complete sentence.</p>

    <h3>1. A complete thought</h3>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> The students completed the experiment.</p>
    <p>The clause has a subject, <em>the students</em>, and a predicate, <em>completed the experiment</em>. It can stand alone.</p>

    <h3>2. Main clauses with extra information</h3>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> The students completed the experiment before lunch.</p>
    <p>The main clause remains complete even though an additional phrase gives information about time.</p>

    <h3>3. Two main clauses in one sentence</h3>
    <p>Two main clauses can be joined with a coordinating conjunction such as <em>and, but</em> or <em>so</em>.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> The rain stopped, and the match resumed.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Main clause 1: The rain stopped.</li>
      <li>Main clause 2: The match resumed.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>4. Main clause or subordinate clause?</h3>
    <p><strong>Main clause:</strong> We returned home.</p>
    <p><strong>Subordinate clause:</strong> because the meeting ended.</p>
    <p>The subordinate clause begins with a word that makes it dependent on another clause.</p>

    <h3>How to identify a main clause</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Find the subject and verb.</li>
      <li>Check whether the clause expresses a complete thought.</li>
      <li>Ask whether it can stand alone as a sentence in the given context.</li>
    </ol>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Main Clauses', 15),
  (1799, 1799, '
    <h2>Subordinate Clauses</h2>
    <p>A subordinate clause contains a subject and a verb but depends on a main clause to complete its meaning in the sentence.</p>

    <h3>1. Adverbial clauses</h3>
    <p>These clauses explain when, where, why, how, under what condition or despite what circumstance something happens.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><em>When the lesson ended</em>, the learners left.</li>
      <li>We stayed indoors <em>because it was raining</em>.</li>
      <li><em>If you practise regularly</em>, you will improve.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>2. Relative clauses</h3>
    <p>A relative clause describes a noun and often begins with <em>who, whom, whose, which</em> or <em>that</em>.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> The learner <em>who won the competition</em> received a certificate.</p>

    <h3>3. Noun clauses</h3>
    <p>A noun clause performs a function usually performed by a noun phrase.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> <em>What she decided</em> surprised us.</p>
    <p>The clause <em>What she decided</em> functions as the subject of the sentence.</p>

    <h3>4. Punctuation with introductory subordinate clauses</h3>
    <p>When an introductory subordinate clause comes before the main clause, it is normally followed by a comma.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> Although the task was difficult, the learners completed it.</p>
    <p>When the subordinate clause comes after the main clause, a comma is often unnecessary for an ordinary adverbial clause.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> The learners completed the task although it was difficult.</p>

    <h3>How to identify one</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Find the subject and verb in the clause.</li>
      <li>Check whether it depends on another clause.</li>
      <li>Identify its function: adverbial, relative or noun clause.</li>
    </ol>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Subordinate Clauses', 15),
  (1800, 1800, '
    <h2>Simple Sentences</h2>
    <p>A simple sentence contains one independent clause. It may have a short subject and predicate or a long subject and predicate with extra details.</p>

    <h3>1. One independent clause</h3>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> The baby slept.</p>
    <p>This sentence contains one main clause.</p>

    <h3>2. A simple sentence can be long</h3>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> The hardworking learners in the science club carefully prepared their project after school.</p>
    <p>Although the sentence contains many words, it has one independent clause and no additional subordinate clause.</p>

    <h3>3. Compound subjects and predicates</h3>
    <p>A simple sentence may have two subjects sharing a predicate or one subject performing more than one action.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Compound subject:</strong> Amina and Brian revised together.</li>
      <li><strong>Compound predicate:</strong> Amina read the notes and answered the questions.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>These can still be simple sentences because each has one independent clause.</p>

    <h3>4. Do not count verbs alone</h3>
    <p>Having two verbs does not automatically make a sentence compound. Look for the number of independent clauses.</p>
    <p><strong>Simple:</strong> The teacher entered and greeted the class.</p>
    <p><strong>Compound:</strong> The teacher entered, and the class stood up.</p>

    <h3>How to identify a simple sentence</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Locate the subject–verb relationships.</li>
      <li>Check whether the sentence contains only one independent clause.</li>
      <li>Do not classify it by length or by counting verbs alone.</li>
    </ol>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Simple Sentences', 15),
  (1801, 1801, '
    <h2>Compound Sentences</h2>
    <p>A compound sentence contains at least two independent clauses. Each clause can stand alone as a sentence.</p>

    <h3>1. Joining clauses with coordinating conjunctions</h3>
    <p>Common coordinating conjunctions include <em>for, and, nor, but, or, yet</em> and <em>so</em>.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> The road was flooded, so the bus took another route.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Clause 1: The road was flooded.</li>
      <li>Clause 2: The bus took another route.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>2. Use a comma before the conjunction</h3>
    <p>When a coordinating conjunction joins two independent clauses, a comma normally comes before it.</p>
    <p><strong>Correct:</strong> The team practised hard, but it lost the match.</p>

    <h3>3. Joining clauses with a semicolon</h3>
    <p>A semicolon can join two closely related independent clauses.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> The library was closed; we studied at home.</p>

    <h3>4. Avoid comma splices</h3>
    <p><strong>Incorrect:</strong> The bell rang, the pupils left.</p>
    <p><strong>Correct:</strong> The bell rang, and the pupils left.</p>
    <p>A comma alone is not normally enough to join two independent clauses.</p>

    <h3>How to identify a compound sentence</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Identify the clauses.</li>
      <li>Check that each clause can stand alone.</li>
      <li>Look for a correct joining method, such as a coordinating conjunction or semicolon.</li>
    </ol>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Compound Sentences', 15),
  (1802, 1802, '
    <h2>Complex Sentences</h2>
    <p>A complex sentence contains one independent clause and at least one subordinate clause.</p>

    <h3>1. A subordinate clause of time</h3>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> When the bell rang, the learners left.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Subordinate clause: When the bell rang.</li>
      <li>Independent clause: the learners left.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>2. A subordinate clause of reason</h3>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> We stayed indoors because it was raining.</p>
    <p>The main clause expresses the main statement. The clause beginning with <em>because</em> gives the reason.</p>

    <h3>3. A relative clause</h3>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> The book that you borrowed is on the desk.</p>
    <p>The clause <em>that you borrowed</em> describes the book.</p>

    <h3>4. Punctuation</h3>
    <p>When the subordinate clause comes first, it is normally followed by a comma.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> Although the path was muddy, we continued walking.</p>
    <p>When the main clause comes first, a comma is often unnecessary for an ordinary subordinate clause.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> We continued walking although the path was muddy.</p>

    <h3>How to identify a complex sentence</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Find the independent clause.</li>
      <li>Find the subordinate clause.</li>
      <li>Confirm that the subordinate clause depends on the independent clause.</li>
    </ol>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Complex Sentences', 15),
  (1803, 1803, '
    <h2>Compound-Complex Sentences</h2>
    <p>A compound-complex sentence contains at least two independent clauses and at least one subordinate clause.</p>

    <h3>1. Identify the independent clauses</h3>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> The rain stopped, and the players returned to the field.</p>
    <p>This is a compound sentence because both clauses can stand alone.</p>

    <h3>2. Add a subordinate clause</h3>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> When the rain stopped, the players returned to the field, and the spectators cheered.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Subordinate clause: When the rain stopped.</li>
      <li>Independent clause 1: the players returned to the field.</li>
      <li>Independent clause 2: the spectators cheered.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>3. Check the clause count, not sentence length</h3>
    <p>A long sentence is not automatically compound-complex. It must contain at least two independent clauses and at least one subordinate clause.</p>

    <h3>4. Punctuation</h3>
    <p>Use punctuation to show the relationship between clauses. An introductory subordinate clause is normally followed by a comma. A comma is normally placed before a coordinating conjunction joining two independent clauses.</p>

    <h3>How to identify a compound-complex sentence</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Find at least two independent clauses.</li>
      <li>Find at least one subordinate clause.</li>
      <li>Check that the clauses are connected grammatically and punctuated clearly.</li>
    </ol>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Compound-Complex Sentences', 15),
  (1804, 1804, '
    <h2>Sentence Fragments</h2>
    <p>A sentence fragment is a group of words punctuated as a sentence but lacking a required part of a complete sentence or expressing an incomplete thought in context.</p>

    <h3>1. A missing main clause</h3>
    <p><strong>Fragment:</strong> Because the road was flooded.</p>
    <p>This subordinate clause gives a reason but leaves the reader waiting for the main statement.</p>
    <p><strong>Correction:</strong> Because the road was flooded, the bus took another route.</p>

    <h3>2. A missing subject</h3>
    <p><strong>Fragment:</strong> Went to the library after class.</p>
    <p><strong>Correction:</strong> The learners went to the library after class.</p>

    <h3>3. A missing verb or incomplete verb group</h3>
    <p><strong>Fragment:</strong> The students in the computer laboratory.</p>
    <p><strong>Correction:</strong> The students are in the computer laboratory.</p>

    <h3>4. Do not confuse a fragment with a short sentence</h3>
    <p><strong>Complete:</strong> Stop!</p>
    <p>This is a complete imperative sentence because the subject <em>you</em> is understood.</p>

    <h3>How to correct a fragment</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Check whether a subject and verb are present or understood.</li>
      <li>Check whether a subordinate clause is left without a main clause.</li>
      <li>Add the missing information or connect the fragment to a suitable complete sentence.</li>
    </ol>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Sentence Fragments', 15),
  (1805, 1805, '
    <h2>Run-on Sentences</h2>
    <p>A run-on sentence occurs when two or more independent clauses are joined incorrectly. The problem is not necessarily sentence length; it is the missing or incorrect boundary between clauses.</p>

    <h3>1. Fused sentences</h3>
    <p>A fused sentence joins independent clauses without appropriate punctuation or a conjunction.</p>
    <p><strong>Incorrect:</strong> The bell rang the learners left.</p>

    <h3>2. Correct with a full stop</h3>
    <p><strong>Correct:</strong> The bell rang. The learners left.</p>

    <h3>3. Correct with a comma and coordinating conjunction</h3>
    <p><strong>Correct:</strong> The bell rang, and the learners left.</p>

    <h3>4. Correct with a semicolon</h3>
    <p><strong>Correct:</strong> The bell rang; the learners left.</p>
    <p>A semicolon works when the two independent clauses are closely related.</p>

    <h3>5. Do not use a comma alone</h3>
    <p><strong>Incorrect:</strong> The bell rang, the learners left.</p>
    <p>This is a comma splice, a type of run-on error. A comma alone is generally insufficient to join two independent clauses.</p>

    <h3>How to repair a run-on</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Locate the independent clauses.</li>
      <li>Separate them with a full stop, join them with a suitable conjunction, or use a semicolon where appropriate.</li>
      <li>Reread the sentence to check that the relationship between ideas is clear.</li>
    </ol>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Run-on Sentences', 15),
  (1806, 1806, '
    <h2>Comma Splices</h2>
    <p>A comma splice occurs when two independent clauses are joined using only a comma.</p>

    <h3>1. Recognising a comma splice</h3>
    <p><strong>Incorrect:</strong> The library was closed, we studied at home.</p>
    <p>Both parts can stand alone as complete sentences. A comma alone does not correctly join them.</p>

    <h3>2. Use a full stop</h3>
    <p><strong>Correct:</strong> The library was closed. We studied at home.</p>
    <p>Use a full stop when the ideas should be separate sentences.</p>

    <h3>3. Use a comma and coordinating conjunction</h3>
    <p><strong>Correct:</strong> The library was closed, so we studied at home.</p>
    <p>Common coordinating conjunctions include <em>and, but, or, nor, for, so</em> and <em>yet</em>.</p>

    <h3>4. Use a semicolon</h3>
    <p><strong>Correct:</strong> The library was closed; we studied at home.</p>
    <p>A semicolon can join closely related independent clauses.</p>

    <h3>5. Do not remove every comma</h3>
    <p>Commas are correct when used for other purposes, such as separating an introductory subordinate clause from a main clause.</p>
    <p><strong>Correct:</strong> Although the library was closed, we studied at home.</p>

    <h3>How to correct a comma splice</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Check whether the words on both sides of the comma are independent clauses.</li>
      <li>If both can stand alone, choose a full stop, a suitable conjunction, or a semicolon.</li>
      <li>Keep the relationship between the ideas clear.</li>
    </ol>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Comma Splices', 15),
  (1807, 1807, '
    <h2>Statements</h2>
    <p>A statement, also called a declarative sentence, gives information, describes something, or expresses an opinion or belief.</p>

    <h3>1. Positive statements</h3>
    <p>A positive statement affirms something.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> The learners completed the assignment.</p>

    <h3>2. Negative statements</h3>
    <p>A negative statement denies something. It commonly uses words such as <em>not, never</em> or <em>no</em>.</p>
    <p><strong>Examples:</strong></p>
    <ul>
      <li>The learners did not complete the assignment.</li>
      <li>She has never visited Mombasa.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>3. Statements with auxiliary verbs</h3>
    <p>Negative statements often use an auxiliary verb followed by <em>not</em>.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>He is ready. → He is not ready.</li>
      <li>They have arrived. → They have not arrived.</li>
      <li>She works here. → She does not work here.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>With the present simple or past simple, use <em>do, does</em> or <em>did</em> when there is no existing auxiliary verb. The main verb then uses its base form.</p>

    <h3>4. Punctuation</h3>
    <p>Statements normally end with a full stop.</p>
    <p><strong>Correct:</strong> The examination begins at eight o''clock.</p>

    <h3>How to identify a statement</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Check whether the sentence primarily gives information or expresses an idea.</li>
      <li>Identify whether it affirms or denies something.</li>
      <li>Use a full stop unless the sentence has another purpose or punctuation requirement.</li>
    </ol>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Statements', 15),
  (1808, 1808, '
    <h2>Questions</h2>
    <p>An interrogative sentence asks for information, confirmation or clarification. It normally ends with a question mark.</p>

    <h3>1. Yes-or-no questions</h3>
    <p>These questions can often be answered with yes or no.</p>
    <p><strong>Examples:</strong></p>
    <ul>
      <li>Are you ready?</li>
      <li>Have they completed the task?</li>
      <li>Does the bus stop here?</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>2. Wh-questions</h3>
    <p>These questions commonly begin with <em>who, what, where, when, why</em> or <em>how</em>.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Where is the laboratory?</li>
      <li>Why did the meeting end early?</li>
      <li>How does this machine work?</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>3. Questions with do, does and did</h3>
    <p>In present simple and past simple questions, use <em>do, does</em> or <em>did</em> when another auxiliary is not already present.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>You understand. → Do you understand?</li>
      <li>She understands. → Does she understand?</li>
      <li>They arrived. → Did they arrive?</li>
    </ul>
    <p>After <em>does</em> or <em>did</em>, use the base form of the main verb.</p>

    <h3>4. Subject questions</h3>
    <p>When <em>who</em> or <em>what</em> is the subject, the question may not need <em>do</em> or <em>does</em>.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> Who opened the window?</p>

    <h3>How to form a question</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Decide what information is needed.</li>
      <li>Use the appropriate question word or auxiliary verb.</li>
      <li>Check word order and the form of the main verb.</li>
      <li>End with a question mark.</li>
    </ol>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Questions', 15),
  (1809, 1809, '
    <h2>Commands and Requests</h2>
    <p>Imperative sentences give instructions, commands, directions, warnings or requests. Their subject, usually <em>you</em>, is commonly understood rather than written.</p>

    <h3>1. Direct commands</h3>
    <p>Use the base form of the verb to give a direct instruction.</p>
    <p><strong>Examples:</strong></p>
    <ul>
      <li>Close the door.</li>
      <li>Write your name on the paper.</li>
      <li>Wait outside.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>2. Negative commands</h3>
    <p>Use <em>do not</em> or <em>don''t</em> followed by the base verb.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Do not touch the equipment.</li>
      <li>Don''t leave your books behind.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>3. Polite requests</h3>
    <p>Commands can be softened with expressions such as <em>please</em> or changed into questions using <em>could</em> or <em>would</em>.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Please pass the exercise books.</li>
      <li>Could you explain the last step, please?</li>
    </ul>
    <p>A polite request phrased as a question normally ends with a question mark.</p>

    <h3>4. Instructions with sequencing</h3>
    <p>Use clear action verbs and sequence markers when several steps must be followed.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> First, switch off the computer. Next, unplug the power cable.</p>

    <h3>How to recognise the sentence type</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Check whether the sentence directs someone to do or avoid an action.</li>
      <li>Look for the base verb, often at the beginning.</li>
      <li>Distinguish a direct imperative from a polite question-form request.</li>
    </ol>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Commands and Requests', 15),
  (1810, 1810, '
    <h2>Exclamations</h2>
    <p>An exclamatory sentence expresses strong feeling, such as surprise, excitement, anger, admiration or disappointment. It commonly ends with an exclamation mark.</p>

    <h3>1. Exclamations beginning with what</h3>
    <p><strong>Examples:</strong></p>
    <ul>
      <li>What a wonderful performance!</li>
      <li>What an interesting discovery!</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>2. Exclamations beginning with how</h3>
    <p><strong>Examples:</strong></p>
    <ul>
      <li>How quickly the time passed!</li>
      <li>How beautiful the garden looks!</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>3. Exclamatory statements</h3>
    <p>A normal statement can be made emphatic through wording and punctuation.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> That was an incredible goal!</p>

    <h3>4. Do not confuse emotion with sentence structure</h3>
    <p>An exclamation mark can sometimes appear after a command or a short expression. The punctuation alone does not always determine the sentence''s grammatical purpose.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Stop! (a forceful command)</li>
      <li>What an amazing result! (an exclamatory sentence)</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>How to recognise an exclamation</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Identify the feeling or emphasis expressed.</li>
      <li>Check whether the sentence uses an exclamatory construction.</li>
      <li>Use an exclamation mark when strong feeling is intended, without overusing it in formal writing.</li>
    </ol>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Exclamations', 15),
  (1811, 1811, '
    <h2>Modal Verbs</h2>
    <p>Modal verbs are auxiliary verbs that express meanings such as ability, permission, obligation, advice, possibility or prediction.</p>

    <h3>1. Common modal verbs</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><em>can, could</em> — ability, permission or possibility</li>
      <li><em>may, might</em> — permission or possibility</li>
      <li><em>must</em> — strong obligation or deduction</li>
      <li><em>should, ought to</em> — advice or expectation</li>
      <li><em>will, would</em> — prediction, willingness or hypothetical meaning</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>2. Use the base verb after a modal</h3>
    <p><strong>Correct:</strong> She can solve the problem.</p>
    <p><strong>Incorrect:</strong> She can solves the problem.</p>
    <p>Modal verbs do not normally take <em>-s</em> for the third-person singular subject.</p>

    <h3>3. Forming negatives</h3>
    <p>Place <em>not</em> after the modal.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>can → cannot / can''t</li>
      <li>should → should not / shouldn''t</li>
      <li>must → must not / mustn''t</li>
    </ul>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> You should not ignore the safety instructions.</p>

    <h3>4. Forming questions</h3>
    <p>Place the modal before the subject.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> Could you explain the method?</p>

    <h3>Important distinction</h3>
    <p><em>Must not</em> expresses prohibition. <em>Do not have to</em> expresses lack of necessity.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>You must not enter. (Entering is prohibited.)</li>
      <li>You do not have to enter. (Entering is not necessary.)</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>How to choose a modal</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Identify the intended meaning: ability, permission, obligation, advice or possibility.</li>
      <li>Select a modal that expresses that meaning in context.</li>
      <li>Use the base form of the main verb after the modal.</li>
    </ol>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Modal Verbs', 15),
  (1812, 1812, '
    <h2>Ability and Permission</h2>
    <p>Modal verbs and related expressions can communicate what someone is able to do and what someone is allowed to do. These meanings are related but not interchangeable.</p>

    <h3>1. Can for present ability</h3>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> Njeri can type quickly.</p>
    <p>This says Njeri has the ability to type quickly.</p>

    <h3>2. Could for past ability</h3>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> When he was younger, he could run long distances.</p>
    <p><em>Could</em> commonly describes general ability in the past.</p>

    <h3>3. Can, could and may for permission</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><em>Can I use your calculator?</em> — common and conversational.</li>
      <li><em>Could I leave a little earlier?</em> — a polite request for permission.</li>
      <li><em>May I come in?</em> — a more formal request for permission.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>4. Be able to</h3>
    <p><em>Be able to</em> can express ability in verb forms where <em>can</em> is unavailable or less suitable.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> She will be able to attend tomorrow.</p>

    <h3>5. Do not confuse ability with permission</h3>
    <p>“I can swim” usually describes ability. “You can leave now” may grant permission. Context determines the meaning.</p>

    <h3>How to choose</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Decide whether the sentence refers to ability or permission.</li>
      <li>Check whether the time is present, past or future.</li>
      <li>Choose a modal or expression that fits the meaning and level of formality.</li>
    </ol>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Ability and Permission', 15),
  (1813, 1813, '
    <h2>Obligation and Necessity</h2>
    <p>Modal verbs and related expressions communicate duties, requirements, prohibitions and the absence of necessity. Choosing the correct expression depends on the strength and source of the obligation.</p>

    <h3>1. Must</h3>
    <p><em>Must</em> expresses strong obligation, a rule or a conclusion that something is necessary.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> Candidates must bring their admission cards.</p>

    <h3>2. Have to</h3>
    <p><em>Have to</em> expresses necessity or an obligation, often one imposed by circumstances or an external rule.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> Learners have to wear the required school uniform.</p>
    <p>In many situations, <em>must</em> and <em>have to</em> can express similar meanings.</p>

    <h3>3. Must not</h3>
    <p><em>Must not</em> expresses prohibition.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> You must not copy another candidate''s answers.</p>

    <h3>4. Do not have to</h3>
    <p><em>Do not have to</em> means that something is not necessary. It does not prohibit the action.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> You do not have to print the document; an electronic copy is acceptable.</p>

    <h3>5. Had to</h3>
    <p>Use <em>had to</em> to express an obligation in the past.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> We had to leave early because the bus was departing.</p>

    <h3>How to choose</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Decide whether the sentence expresses obligation, prohibition or lack of necessity.</li>
      <li>Check whether the obligation is present, past or future.</li>
      <li>Choose the expression that matches the intended meaning.</li>
    </ol>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Obligation and Necessity', 15),
  (1814, 1814, '
    <h2>Advice and Recommendations</h2>
    <p>English uses modal verbs and related expressions to recommend actions, warn people about possible consequences and suggest better choices.</p>

    <h3>1. Should</h3>
    <p><em>Should</em> is commonly used to give advice or make a recommendation.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> You should revise the topics you found difficult.</p>

    <h3>2. Ought to</h3>
    <p><em>Ought to</em> also expresses advice, expectation or what is considered the right thing to do.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> We ought to respect other people''s opinions.</p>

    <h3>3. Had better</h3>
    <p><em>Had better</em> gives strong advice, often suggesting that an undesirable consequence may follow if the advice is ignored.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> You had better save your work before closing the computer.</p>
    <p>Although it contains the word <em>had</em>, this expression often refers to a present or future situation.</p>

    <h3>4. Should not and ought not to</h3>
    <p>These forms advise against an action.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>You should not ignore the instructions.</li>
      <li>You ought not to spread unverified information.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>5. Giving reasons for advice</h3>
    <p>Advice becomes clearer when the reason is explained.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> You should check your calculations because a small arithmetic error can change the final answer.</p>

    <h3>How to choose</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Identify the action being recommended or discouraged.</li>
      <li>Decide whether the advice is mild, ordinary or strongly urgent.</li>
      <li>Choose a suitable expression and check its verb pattern.</li>
    </ol>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Advice and Recommendations', 15),
  (1815, 1815, '
    <h2>Possibility, Probability and Deduction</h2>
    <p>Modal verbs help speakers express how possible something is or how confidently they draw a conclusion from available evidence.</p>

    <h3>1. May and might</h3>
    <p><em>May</em> and <em>might</em> can express possibility.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>The weather may improve later.</li>
      <li>The missing file might be in the downloads folder.</li>
    </ul>
    <p><em>Might</em> can sound more tentative, but the difference is not always a precise measure of probability.</p>

    <h3>2. Could</h3>
    <p><em>Could</em> can describe a possible explanation or outcome.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> The delay could be caused by heavy traffic.</p>

    <h3>3. Must for strong deduction</h3>
    <p><em>Must</em> can express a confident conclusion based on evidence rather than an obligation.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> The lights are on and voices are coming from inside. Someone must be home.</p>

    <h3>4. Cannot for a strong negative deduction</h3>
    <p><em>Cannot</em> can express the conclusion that something is impossible or very unlikely given the evidence.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> That cannot be the correct answer; it contradicts the information in the question.</p>

    <h3>5. Past deductions</h3>
    <p>Use a modal followed by <em>have</em> and a past participle to make deductions about the past.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>She must have forgotten the meeting.</li>
      <li>They might have taken the wrong bus.</li>
      <li>He cannot have completed the task in one minute.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>How to choose</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Decide whether you are describing a possibility or drawing a conclusion from evidence.</li>
      <li>Choose a modal that communicates the intended level of certainty.</li>
      <li>For a past deduction, use modal + have + past participle.</li>
    </ol>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Possibility, Probability and Deduction', 15),
  (1816, 1816, '
    <h2>Active Voice</h2>
    <p>In an active sentence, the subject performs the action expressed by the verb.</p>

    <h3>1. Subject performs the action</h3>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> The technician repaired the computer.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Subject: the technician</li>
      <li>Verb: repaired</li>
      <li>Object: the computer</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The sentence directly identifies who carried out the repair.</p>

    <h3>2. Active voice across tenses</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Present simple: The teacher marks the books.</li>
      <li>Past simple: The teacher marked the books.</li>
      <li>Present continuous: The teacher is marking the books.</li>
      <li>Present perfect: The teacher has marked the books.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>3. Active voice with two objects</h3>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> The coach gave the team new instructions.</p>
    <p>The subject, <em>the coach</em>, performs the action. The sentence also contains an indirect object, <em>the team</em>, and a direct object, <em>new instructions</em>.</p>

    <h3>4. Why use active voice?</h3>
    <p>Active voice often makes writing direct and clear, particularly when the person or thing performing an action matters.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> The committee approved the proposal.</p>

    <h3>How to identify active voice</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Find the subject and the main verb.</li>
      <li>Ask whether the subject performs the action.</li>
      <li>If it does, the sentence is in active voice.</li>
    </ol>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Active Voice', 15),
  (1817, 1817, '
    <h2>Passive Voice</h2>
    <p>In a passive sentence, the grammatical subject receives or is affected by the action rather than performing it.</p>

    <h3>1. Basic passive structure</h3>
    <p>The basic pattern is:</p>
    <p><strong>Subject receiving the action + be + past participle</strong></p>
    <p><strong>Active:</strong> The mechanic repaired the car.</p>
    <p><strong>Passive:</strong> The car was repaired by the mechanic.</p>

    <h3>2. The verb be changes with tense</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Present simple: The room is cleaned daily.</li>
      <li>Past simple: The room was cleaned yesterday.</li>
      <li>Present continuous: The room is being cleaned.</li>
      <li>Present perfect: The room has been cleaned.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>3. The agent may be omitted</h3>
    <p>Use <em>by</em> plus the agent when identifying the performer is useful.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> The bridge was designed by the engineer.</p>
    <p>If the performer is unknown, obvious or unimportant, omit the agent.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> The road was repaired last month.</p>

    <h3>4. Only certain verbs form ordinary passives</h3>
    <p>Passive constructions are most common with verbs that take a direct object.</p>
    <p><strong>Active:</strong> The pupils opened the door.</p>
    <p><strong>Passive:</strong> The door was opened.</p>
    <p>A sentence such as “The baby slept” has no direct object to promote into the usual passive subject position.</p>

    <h3>How to recognise passive voice</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Find the grammatical subject.</li>
      <li>Check whether the subject receives the action.</li>
      <li>Look for the appropriate form of <em>be</em> followed by a past participle.</li>
      <li>Do not assume every sentence containing a form of <em>be</em> is passive.</li>
    </ol>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Passive Voice', 15),
  (1818, 1818, '
    <h2>Changing Active Sentences to Passive</h2>
    <p>To change an active sentence into passive voice, make the active sentence''s direct object the passive subject, use the correct form of <em>be</em> with the past participle, and retain the original tense.</p>

    <h3>1. Identify the sentence parts</h3>
    <p><strong>Active:</strong> The chef prepared the meal.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Subject: the chef</li>
      <li>Verb: prepared</li>
      <li>Object: the meal</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>2. Move the object into subject position</h3>
    <p><strong>Passive:</strong> The meal was prepared by the chef.</p>
    <p>The object <em>the meal</em> becomes the new grammatical subject.</p>

    <h3>3. Keep the tense</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Present simple: They clean the room. → The room is cleaned.</li>
      <li>Past simple: They cleaned the room. → The room was cleaned.</li>
      <li>Present continuous: They are cleaning the room. → The room is being cleaned.</li>
      <li>Present perfect: They have cleaned the room. → The room has been cleaned.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>4. Change pronouns where necessary</h3>
    <p><strong>Active:</strong> She helped him.</p>
    <p><strong>Passive:</strong> He was helped by her.</p>
    <p>The object pronoun <em>him</em> becomes the subject pronoun <em>he</em>. After <em>by</em>, use the object form <em>her</em>.</p>

    <h3>5. Use the past participle</h3>
    <p>Do not confuse the past simple with the past participle.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>write → wrote → written</li>
      <li>take → took → taken</li>
      <li>choose → chose → chosen</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>How to transform a sentence</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Confirm that the active verb has a direct object.</li>
      <li>Move the object to subject position.</li>
      <li>Use the correct form of <em>be</em> for the original tense.</li>
      <li>Use the past participle of the main verb.</li>
      <li>Add the agent with <em>by</em> only if useful.</li>
    </ol>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Changing Active Sentences to Passive', 15),
  (1819, 1819, '
    <h2>Direct Speech</h2>
    <p>Direct speech reports a person''s exact words. Quotation marks show which words were spoken or written.</p>

    <h3>1. Basic punctuation</h3>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> Amina said, “I have finished my assignment.”</p>
    <p>The reporting clause identifies the speaker, and the quotation contains the exact words.</p>

    <h3>2. Punctuation inside the quotation</h3>
    <p>In common British-style school punctuation, a full stop or comma associated with the quoted words is usually placed inside the closing quotation mark.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> “We are ready,” the learners said.</p>
    <p>Punctuation conventions vary somewhat between style systems. Follow the convention required by the assessment or publication.</p>

    <h3>3. Questions in direct speech</h3>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> The teacher asked, “Have you completed the task?”</p>
    <p>The question mark belongs to the quoted question.</p>

    <h3>4. Different positions for the reporting clause</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Reporting clause first: The teacher said, “Open your books.”</li>
      <li>Reporting clause last: “Open your books,” the teacher said.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>5. Capitalisation</h3>
    <p>The first word of a complete quoted sentence begins with a capital letter.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> Brian said, “The examination begins tomorrow.”</p>

    <h3>How to punctuate direct speech</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Identify the exact words being quoted.</li>
      <li>Place quotation marks around those words.</li>
      <li>Separate the reporting clause from the quotation appropriately.</li>
      <li>Keep the original question mark, exclamation mark or full stop appropriate to the quoted words.</li>
    </ol>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Direct Speech', 15),
  (1820, 1820, '
    <h2>Punctuation in Direct Speech</h2>
    <p>Correct punctuation helps readers distinguish the speaker''s exact words from the reporting clause and recognise whether the speaker is making a statement, asking a question or giving a command.</p>

    <h3>1. Statements</h3>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> “The bus is late,” Peter said.</p>
    <p>When the reporting clause follows a statement, a comma commonly separates the quotation from the reporting clause.</p>

    <h3>2. Questions</h3>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> “Where is the bus?” Peter asked.</p>
    <p>The question mark stays with the quoted question.</p>

    <h3>3. Exclamations</h3>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> “What a wonderful result!” Amina exclaimed.</p>
    <p>The exclamation mark indicates the feeling expressed in the quoted words.</p>

    <h3>4. A new speaker, a new paragraph</h3>
    <p>In conventional dialogue writing, start a new paragraph when the speaker changes.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong></p>
    <blockquote>
      “Have you finished the work?” the teacher asked.<br/>
      “Yes, I have,” the learner replied.
    </blockquote>

    <h3>5. Interrupted or divided speech</h3>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> “If you finish early,” the teacher said, “check your answers.”</p>
    <p>The reporting clause interrupts the quotation. Punctuation must make clear how the quoted words continue.</p>

    <h3>How to check</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Identify the quoted words and reporting clause.</li>
      <li>Choose punctuation that matches the quoted sentence type.</li>
      <li>Use quotation marks consistently.</li>
      <li>Check that the speaker and the quoted words remain easy to follow.</li>
    </ol>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Punctuation in Direct Speech', 15),
  (1821, 1821, '
    <h2>Reporting Statements</h2>
    <p>Reported speech communicates what someone said without repeating the exact words. When reporting a statement, the speaker''s words are usually integrated into a reporting clause.</p>

    <h3>1. Use a reporting verb</h3>
    <p><strong>Direct:</strong> Amina said, “I am tired.”</p>
    <p><strong>Reported:</strong> Amina said that she was tired.</p>
    <p>The word <em>that</em> is often optional after <em>said</em>.</p>

    <h3>2. Change pronouns when necessary</h3>
    <p><strong>Direct:</strong> Brian said, “I have lost my book.”</p>
    <p><strong>Reported:</strong> Brian said that he had lost his book.</p>
    <p>Pronouns change according to who originally spoke and who is reporting the statement.</p>

    <h3>3. Backshift tense when appropriate</h3>
    <p>When the reporting verb is in the past, the reported verb often shifts back one tense.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Present simple → past simple</li>
      <li>Present continuous → past continuous</li>
      <li>Present perfect → past perfect</li>
      <li>Will → would</li>
      <li>Can → could</li>
    </ul>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> “I work here,” she said. → She said that she worked there.</p>

    <h3>4. When backshift may not be necessary</h3>
    <p>If a statement remains true or is still relevant, the present tense may be retained, depending on context.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> The teacher said that water boils at 100°C at standard atmospheric pressure.</p>

    <h3>5. Say and tell</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Say: She said that she was ready.</li>
      <li>Tell + object: She told us that she was ready.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Do not normally write “She told that she was ready” without an object after <em>told</em>.</p>

    <h3>How to report a statement</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Remove quotation marks and the direct-speech punctuation.</li>
      <li>Adjust pronouns to match the reporting perspective.</li>
      <li>Backshift tense where appropriate.</li>
      <li>Adjust time and place expressions if the context has changed.</li>
      <li>Use the correct pattern with <em>said</em> or <em>told</em>.</li>
    </ol>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Reporting Statements', 15),
  (1822, 1822, '
    <h2>Reporting Questions</h2>
    <p>Reported questions communicate what someone asked without repeating the exact question. Their word order usually changes to that of a statement.</p>

    <h3>1. Yes-or-no questions</h3>
    <p>Use <em>if</em> or <em>whether</em> to introduce a reported yes-or-no question.</p>
    <p><strong>Direct:</strong> The teacher asked, “Are you ready?”</p>
    <p><strong>Reported:</strong> The teacher asked whether we were ready.</p>

    <h3>2. Wh-questions</h3>
    <p>Keep the question word, such as <em>where, when, why, who</em> or <em>how</em>.</p>
    <p><strong>Direct:</strong> He asked, “Where do you live?”</p>
    <p><strong>Reported:</strong> He asked where I lived.</p>

    <h3>3. Use statement word order</h3>
    <p>Do not keep the question''s subject–auxiliary inversion in a reported question.</p>
    <p><strong>Incorrect:</strong> She asked where was I going.</p>
    <p><strong>Correct:</strong> She asked where I was going.</p>

    <h3>4. Remove do, does and did when appropriate</h3>
    <p><strong>Direct:</strong> He asked, “What do you want?”</p>
    <p><strong>Reported:</strong> He asked what I wanted.</p>
    <p>The auxiliary <em>do</em> is not retained in this reported-question structure.</p>

    <h3>5. Punctuation</h3>
    <p>A reported question is normally part of a statement and usually ends with a full stop.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> She asked whether the office was open.</p>

    <h3>How to report a question</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Identify whether the original question is yes-or-no or wh-.</li>
      <li>Use <em>if/whether</em> for yes-or-no questions or retain the question word.</li>
      <li>Change the word order to subject + verb.</li>
      <li>Adjust pronouns and tense as required.</li>
      <li>Use a full stop for an ordinary reported question.</li>
    </ol>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Reporting Questions', 15),
  (1823, 1823, '
    <h2>Reporting Commands and Requests</h2>
    <p>Reported commands and requests communicate what someone instructed or asked another person to do. The original words are changed into an appropriate reporting structure.</p>

    <h3>1. Reporting commands</h3>
    <p>Use <strong>told + object + to + base verb</strong> to report a command.</p>
    <p><strong>Direct:</strong> The teacher said to Brian, "Close the door."</p>
    <p><strong>Reported:</strong> The teacher told Brian to close the door.</p>

    <h3>2. Reporting requests</h3>
    <p>Use verbs such as <em>asked</em> or <em>requested</em> with an object and an infinitive.</p>
    <p><strong>Direct:</strong> Amina said to me, "Please help me."</p>
    <p><strong>Reported:</strong> Amina asked me to help her.</p>

    <h3>3. Reporting negative commands</h3>
    <p>Use <strong>not to + base verb</strong> after the reporting object.</p>
    <p><strong>Direct:</strong> The officer said, "Do not enter the building."</p>
    <p><strong>Reported:</strong> The officer told us not to enter the building.</p>

    <h3>4. Choose a suitable reporting verb</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><em>ordered</em> — a strong command</li>
      <li><em>asked</em> — a request or question, depending on the structure</li>
      <li><em>advised</em> — advice</li>
      <li><em>warned</em> — a warning</li>
      <li><em>reminded</em> — a reminder</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>5. Change pronouns where necessary</h3>
    <p><strong>Direct:</strong> Jane said to Peter, "Please return my book."</p>
    <p><strong>Reported:</strong> Jane asked Peter to return her book.</p>

    <h3>How to report a command or request</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Identify the action being requested or commanded.</li>
      <li>Choose a reporting verb that matches the speaker''s intention.</li>
      <li>Include the person addressed when needed.</li>
      <li>Use to + base verb, or not to + base verb for a negative instruction.</li>
      <li>Adjust pronouns to match the reporting perspective.</li>
    </ol>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Reporting Commands and Requests', 15),
  (1824, 1824, '
    <h2>Pronoun Changes in Reported Speech</h2>
    <p>When speech is reported, pronouns may need to change because the original speaker, listener and reporter may be different people.</p>

    <h3>1. First-person pronouns</h3>
    <p>First-person pronouns usually refer to the original speaker.</p>
    <p><strong>Direct:</strong> Amina said, "I am ready."</p>
    <p><strong>Reported:</strong> Amina said that she was ready.</p>
    <p>''I'' changes to ''she'' because Amina is the person being discussed.</p>

    <h3>2. Second-person pronouns</h3>
    <p>Second-person pronouns refer to the original listener. Their reported form depends on who was addressed.</p>
    <p><strong>Direct:</strong> Peter said to Mary, "You are early."</p>
    <p><strong>Reported:</strong> Peter told Mary that she was early.</p>

    <h3>3. Possessive pronouns and determiners</h3>
    <p><strong>Direct:</strong> John said, "My bag is missing."</p>
    <p><strong>Reported:</strong> John said that his bag was missing.</p>
    <p>The possessive determiner changes from ''my'' to ''his''.</p>

    <h3>4. Object pronouns</h3>
    <p><strong>Direct:</strong> Jane said to Tom, "I will help you."</p>
    <p><strong>Reported:</strong> Jane told Tom that she would help him.</p>
    <p>''I'' becomes ''she'', while ''you'' becomes ''him'' because Tom is being addressed.</p>

    <h3>5. Do not change pronouns mechanically</h3>
    <p>The correct pronoun depends on the real situation. A pronoun may remain unchanged if the reporting perspective is the same or the reference has not changed.</p>

    <h3>How to change pronouns</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Identify the original speaker.</li>
      <li>Identify the person addressed.</li>
      <li>Determine who each pronoun refers to in the reported sentence.</li>
      <li>Choose the correct subject, object or possessive form.</li>
      <li>Check that the meaning remains the same.</li>
    </ol>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Pronoun Changes in Reported Speech', 15),
  (1825, 1825, '
    <h2>Tense Changes in Reported Speech</h2>
    <p>When the reporting verb is in the past, the tense in the reported clause often shifts back. This is called backshift. It is common, but not compulsory in every context.</p>

    <h3>1. Present simple to past simple</h3>
    <p><strong>Direct:</strong> She said, "I work in Nairobi."</p>
    <p><strong>Reported:</strong> She said that she worked in Nairobi.</p>

    <h3>2. Present continuous to past continuous</h3>
    <p><strong>Direct:</strong> He said, "I am reading."</p>
    <p><strong>Reported:</strong> He said that he was reading.</p>

    <h3>3. Present perfect to past perfect</h3>
    <p><strong>Direct:</strong> They said, "We have completed the work."</p>
    <p><strong>Reported:</strong> They said that they had completed the work.</p>

    <h3>4. Past simple to past perfect</h3>
    <p><strong>Direct:</strong> She said, "I lost my keys."</p>
    <p><strong>Reported:</strong> She said that she had lost her keys.</p>
    <p>In some contexts, the past simple may be retained if the sequence of events is clear.</p>

    <h3>5. Future and modal changes</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>will → would</li>
      <li>can → could</li>
      <li>may → might</li>
      <li>must → often had to when reporting past obligation</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>6. When backshift is unnecessary</h3>
    <p>Backshift may be unnecessary when the reporting verb is present, when a fact remains true, or when the speaker wishes to emphasise that something is still valid.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> The teacher explained that the Earth revolves around the Sun.</p>

    <h3>How to choose the tense</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Identify the tense of the original statement.</li>
      <li>Check the tense of the reporting verb.</li>
      <li>Apply the usual backshift where appropriate.</li>
      <li>Consider whether the information remains true or the context justifies retaining the original tense.</li>
    </ol>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Tense Changes in Reported Speech', 15),
  (1826, 1826, '
    <h2>Time and Place Expressions in Reported Speech</h2>
    <p>Words such as <em>today</em>, <em>tomorrow</em>, <em>here</em> and <em>this</em> may change when speech is reported from a different time or place. The change depends on the reporting context.</p>

    <h3>1. Time expressions</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>today → that day</li>
      <li>yesterday → the previous day or the day before</li>
      <li>tomorrow → the following day or the next day</li>
      <li>now → then</li>
      <li>last week → the previous week or the week before</li>
      <li>next week → the following week or the week after</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>2. Place expressions</h3>
    <p><em>Here</em> may become <em>there</em> when the reporting location differs.</p>
    <p><strong>Direct:</strong> She said, "I live here."</p>
    <p><strong>Reported:</strong> She said that she lived there.</p>

    <h3>3. Demonstratives</h3>
    <p><em>This</em> and <em>these</em> may become <em>that</em> and <em>those</em> when the original reference is no longer near the speaker or relevant to the current situation.</p>
    <p><strong>Direct:</strong> He said, "These books are mine."</p>
    <p><strong>Reported:</strong> He said that those books were his.</p>

    <h3>4. Changes depend on context</h3>
    <p>Expressions do not have to change when the reporting takes place at the same time and place or when the original reference is still appropriate.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> If a speaker says "I will come tomorrow" and the statement is reported on the same day, <em>tomorrow</em> may remain unchanged.</p>

    <h3>How to choose</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Identify the original time and place reference.</li>
      <li>Determine when and where the report is being made.</li>
      <li>Change the expression if the original reference no longer fits.</li>
      <li>Check that the report preserves the intended meaning.</li>
    </ol>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Time and Place Expressions in Reported Speech', 15),
  (1827, 1827, '
    <h2>Zero Conditional</h2>
    <p>The zero conditional describes general truths, scientific facts, rules and situations in which one event regularly follows another.</p>

    <h3>1. Basic structure</h3>
    <p><strong>If + present simple, present simple</strong></p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> If water reaches its boiling point under suitable conditions, it boils.</p>

    <h3>2. General truths</h3>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> If plants do not receive enough light, their growth may be limited.</p>
    <p>The sentence describes a general relationship rather than one specific future event.</p>

    <h3>3. Rules and instructions</h3>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> If a learner submits the form late, the office records the submission date.</p>
    <p>The result describes the rule or procedure that applies whenever the condition is met.</p>

    <h3>4. When to use a comma</h3>
    <p>Use a comma when the if-clause comes first.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>If you heat ice, it melts.</li>
      <li>Ice melts if you heat it.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>5. Unless</h3>
    <p><em>Unless</em> means ''if not''.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> Unless plants receive water, they wilt.</p>
    <p>Avoid adding an unnecessary negative to the same condition.</p>

    <h3>How to identify the zero conditional</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Check whether the sentence expresses a general truth, rule or regular result.</li>
      <li>Use the present simple in both clauses for the usual zero-conditional pattern.</li>
      <li>Ensure the result logically follows the condition.</li>
    </ol>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Zero Conditional', 15),
  (1828, 1828, '
    <h2>First Conditional</h2>
    <p>The first conditional describes a real or possible future situation and its likely result.</p>

    <h3>1. Basic structure</h3>
    <p><strong>If + present simple, will + base verb</strong></p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> If the weather improves, we will play the match.</p>

    <h3>2. The condition clause</h3>
    <p>Use the present simple after <em>if</em> for the usual first-conditional pattern.</p>
    <p><strong>Correct:</strong> If she studies consistently, she will improve.</p>
    <p><strong>Incorrect:</strong> If she will study consistently, she will improve.</p>

    <h3>3. Other result forms</h3>
    <p>The main clause can use a modal or an imperative, depending on the meaning.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>If you finish early, you can help your partner.</li>
      <li>If the alarm rings, leave the building calmly.</li>
      <li>If you feel unwell, you should tell the teacher.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>4. Changing clause order</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>If it rains, the match will be postponed.</li>
      <li>The match will be postponed if it rains.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Use a comma when the if-clause comes first. A comma is not normally needed when it comes second.</p>

    <h3>5. Unless</h3>
    <p><em>Unless</em> can replace <em>if ... not</em>.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> Unless you hurry, you will miss the bus.</p>

    <h3>How to use the first conditional</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Identify a realistic future condition.</li>
      <li>Use the present simple in the if-clause.</li>
      <li>Choose a suitable future result, often ''will + base verb''.</li>
      <li>Check that the condition and result make logical sense.</li>
    </ol>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for First Conditional', 15),
  (1829, 1829, '
    <h2>Second Conditional</h2>
    <p>The second conditional describes an unreal, imagined or relatively unlikely present or future situation and its possible result.</p>

    <h3>1. Basic structure</h3>
    <p><strong>If + past simple, would + base verb</strong></p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> If I had more time, I would learn another language.</p>

    <h3>2. The past form does not necessarily mean past time</h3>
    <p>In a second conditional, the past simple marks distance from reality rather than necessarily describing an event in the past.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> If she lived nearer, she would walk to school.</p>
    <p>This describes an imagined present or future situation.</p>

    <h3>3. Were in hypothetical statements</h3>
    <p><em>Were</em> is commonly used for all subjects in formal hypothetical statements.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> If I were the class representative, I would organise a meeting.</p>
    <p>''If I was'' is also used in some informal contexts, but many formal grammar exercises expect ''were'' in this hypothetical pattern.</p>

    <h3>4. Other modal verbs</h3>
    <p>The result clause may use <em>could</em> or <em>might</em> when the meaning is ability or possibility.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>If we had a larger room, we could hold more learners.</li>
      <li>If he practised more, he might win the competition.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>5. Avoid mixing the standard patterns</h3>
    <p><strong>Correct:</strong> If I knew the answer, I would explain it.</p>
    <p><strong>Incorrect:</strong> If I would know the answer, I would explain it.</p>

    <h3>How to use the second conditional</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Decide whether the situation is imagined or unlikely.</li>
      <li>Use the past simple in the if-clause.</li>
      <li>Use would, could or might with the base verb in the result clause.</li>
      <li>Check that the meaning refers to an imagined present or future situation.</li>
    </ol>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Second Conditional', 15),
  (1830, 1830, '
    <h2>Third Conditional</h2>
    <p>The third conditional describes an imagined alternative to a past event. It often expresses regret, criticism or a different result that did not happen.</p>

    <h3>1. Basic structure</h3>
    <p><strong>If + past perfect, would have + past participle</strong></p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> If I had left earlier, I would have caught the bus.</p>
    <p>The sentence implies that I did not leave early enough and did not catch the bus.</p>

    <h3>2. The if-clause</h3>
    <p>Use the past perfect: <em>had + past participle</em>.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>If she had revised, ...</li>
      <li>If they had arrived earlier, ...</li>
      <li>If he had checked the figures, ...</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>3. The result clause</h3>
    <p>Use <em>would have + past participle</em> for an imagined result.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> If the team had trained regularly, it would have performed better.</p>

    <h3>4. Could have and might have</h3>
    <p>Use <em>could have</em> to express an unrealised possibility or ability, and <em>might have</em> to express an uncertain alternative result.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>If we had left earlier, we could have arrived on time.</li>
      <li>If he had applied, he might have received a place.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>5. Do not use would have in the if-clause in the standard pattern</h3>
    <p><strong>Correct:</strong> If she had studied, she would have passed.</p>
    <p><strong>Incorrect:</strong> If she would have studied, she would have passed.</p>

    <h3>How to use the third conditional</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Identify a past condition that did not happen as imagined.</li>
      <li>Use the past perfect in the if-clause.</li>
      <li>Use would have, could have or might have with a past participle in the result clause.</li>
      <li>Check that the sentence describes an alternative past, not a general truth or future possibility.</li>
    </ol>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Third Conditional', 15),
  (1831, 1831, '
    <h2>Adjectives</h2>
    <p>An adjective describes a noun or pronoun. It can tell us what something is like, which one is meant, or how many or how much is involved.</p>

    <h3>1. Adjectives before nouns</h3>
    <p>An adjective often appears before the noun it describes.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> The <em>careful</em> learner checked the answer.</p>
    <p>''Careful'' describes the noun ''learner''.</p>

    <h3>2. Adjectives after linking verbs</h3>
    <p>An adjective can follow a linking verb such as <em>be, seem, become, feel</em> or <em>look</em>.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> The learner seemed <em>confident</em>.</p>
    <p>''Confident'' describes the learner, not the action of seeming.</p>

    <h3>3. Adjectives describing different qualities</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Size:</strong> a large classroom</li>
      <li><strong>Colour:</strong> a blue folder</li>
      <li><strong>Condition:</strong> a broken chair</li>
      <li><strong>Opinion:</strong> an interesting story</li>
      <li><strong>Origin:</strong> Kenyan tea</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>4. Adjective or adverb?</h3>
    <p>An adjective describes a noun or pronoun: <em>The driver was careful.</em></p>
    <p>An adverb can describe a verb: <em>The driver drove carefully.</em></p>
    <p>Do not assume every word ending in <em>-ly</em> is an adverb, or that every adverb ends in <em>-ly</em>.</p>

    <h3>5. Find the word being described</h3>
    <p>To identify an adjective, ask which noun or pronoun the word describes, or whether it describes the subject after a linking verb.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Adjectives', 15),
  (1832, 1832, '
    <h2>Descriptive Adjectives</h2>
    <p>Descriptive adjectives express qualities or characteristics of people, places, objects and experiences.</p>

    <h3>1. Physical qualities</h3>
    <p>These describe observable characteristics.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>a tall building</li>
      <li>a smooth surface</li>
      <li>a narrow road</li>
      <li>a bright light</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>2. Conditions and states</h3>
    <p>These describe the state or condition of something.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>a damaged roof</li>
      <li>a clean laboratory</li>
      <li>a crowded bus</li>
      <li>a frozen lake</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>3. Opinions and impressions</h3>
    <p>These express a judgement or impression.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>an impressive performance</li>
      <li>a difficult question</li>
      <li>a useful explanation</li>
      <li>a disappointing result</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>4. Describing the same noun with more than one adjective</h3>
    <p>Several adjectives can describe one noun. Their order often follows a natural pattern, such as opinion, size, age, colour, origin, material and purpose.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> a beautiful small wooden box.</p>
    <p>In ordinary writing, use only the adjectives that help the reader understand the noun clearly.</p>

    <h3>5. Choose precise descriptions</h3>
    <p>Compare ''a good meal'' with ''a nutritious meal''. The second description gives more specific information.</p>
    <p>Choose an adjective that matches the intended meaning rather than one that merely sounds impressive.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Descriptive Adjectives', 15),
  (1833, 1833, '
    <h2>Adjectives of Quantity and Number</h2>
    <p>Words that express quantity or number help specify how much or how many of something is meant. In modern grammar, many of these words are also classified as determiners.</p>

    <h3>1. Quantity: how much?</h3>
    <p>Words such as <em>much, little, enough</em> and <em>some</em> can express quantity.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>There is little water left.</li>
      <li>We have enough time.</li>
      <li>She added some sugar.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>2. Number: how many?</h3>
    <p>Words such as <em>many, few, several</em> and numbers express how many items are meant.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Many learners attended.</li>
      <li>Few seats remained.</li>
      <li>Three books were missing.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>3. Countable and uncountable nouns</h3>
    <p>Use <em>many</em> with plural countable nouns and <em>much</em> with uncountable nouns.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>many questions</li>
      <li>much information</li>
      <li>few mistakes</li>
      <li>little rainfall</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>4. A few and few</h3>
    <p><em>A few</em> means some, enough to refer to a small number. <em>Few</em> emphasises that the number is small or insufficient.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> We have a few minutes, so we can finish the task.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> We have few minutes left, so we must hurry.</p>

    <h3>5. A little and little</h3>
    <p><em>A little</em> means some amount; <em>little</em> emphasises that the amount is small or insufficient.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> There is a little milk left, enough for tea.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> There is little milk left, so it may not be enough.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Adjectives of Quantity and Number', 15),
  (1834, 1834, '
    <h2>Demonstrative Adjectives and Determiners</h2>
    <p><em>This, that, these</em> and <em>those</em> point to particular people or things. When they come before a noun, they function as demonstrative determiners.</p>

    <h3>1. This and these</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>This</strong> — singular, usually near the speaker: this book.</li>
      <li><strong>These</strong> — plural, usually near the speaker: these books.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>2. That and those</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>That</strong> — singular, usually farther away: that building.</li>
      <li><strong>Those</strong> — plural, usually farther away: those buildings.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>3. Match the determiner to the noun</h3>
    <p>Use a singular demonstrative with a singular noun and a plural demonstrative with a plural noun.</p>
    <p><strong>Correct:</strong> This chair is broken.</p>
    <p><strong>Correct:</strong> Those chairs are broken.</p>
    <p><strong>Incorrect:</strong> These chair is broken.</p>

    <h3>4. Demonstrative determiner or pronoun?</h3>
    <p>Before a noun, the word is a determiner:</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> Those shoes are new.</p>
    <p>When it stands in place of a noun phrase, it is a demonstrative pronoun:</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> Those are new.</p>

    <h3>5. Distance can be physical or conversational</h3>
    <p>Distance can refer to physical location, time or the speaker''s way of presenting an idea.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>This week has been busy.</li>
      <li>Those days were difficult.</li>
    </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Demonstrative Adjectives and Determiners', 15),
  (1835, 1835, '
    <h2>Order of Adjectives</h2>
    <p>When several adjectives describe the same noun, English often follows a conventional order. The order helps the description sound natural and clear.</p>

    <h3>1. A useful general order</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Opinion: beautiful, useful, unpleasant</li>
      <li>Size: large, tiny, tall</li>
      <li>Age: old, young, modern</li>
      <li>Shape: round, square, narrow</li>
      <li>Colour: blue, black, green</li>
      <li>Origin: Kenyan, Japanese, African</li>
      <li>Material: wooden, metal, cotton</li>
      <li>Purpose or type: cooking, school, running</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>2. Examples</h3>
    <p><strong>Natural:</strong> a beautiful old wooden house.</p>
    <p><strong>Natural:</strong> a small round metal container.</p>
    <p><strong>Natural:</strong> a new Kenyan school uniform.</p>

    <h3>3. Do not force every category into every phrase</h3>
    <p>Most noun phrases use only one or two adjectives. The full sequence is a guide, not a requirement to include every type.</p>

    <h3>4. Adjectives joined by and</h3>
    <p>Adjectives of equal importance can sometimes be joined by <em>and</em>.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> a calm and patient teacher.</p>
    <p>Adjectives that describe different categories usually do not need <em>and</em>: <em>a small wooden table</em>.</p>

    <h3>5. Check the meaning</h3>
    <p>Choose an order that makes the relationship between the adjectives and the noun clear. If the phrase becomes awkward or crowded, remove unnecessary adjectives.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Order of Adjectives', 15),
  (1836, 1836, '
    <h2>Positive Forms of Adjectives</h2>
    <p>The positive form is the basic form of an adjective, without marking a comparison using <em>-er</em> or <em>more</em>.</p>

    <h3>1. Describe without comparing</h3>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> The room is quiet.</p>
    <p>The adjective ''quiet'' describes the room without comparing it with another room.</p>

    <h3>2. Equality: as ... as</h3>
    <p>Use <strong>as + adjective + as</strong> to express equal degree.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> This road is as wide as the main road.</p>

    <h3>3. Negative comparison: not as ... as</h3>
    <p>Use <strong>not as + adjective + as</strong> to say that something does not reach the same degree.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> The second road is not as wide as the main road.</p>

    <h3>4. So ... as</h3>
    <p><em>So + adjective + as</em> can be used in some negative comparisons and formal contexts.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> The path is not so narrow as it first appeared.</p>
    <p>In everyday English, <em>not as ... as</em> is generally more common.</p>

    <h3>5. Avoid double comparison</h3>
    <p>Do not combine the positive-form equality pattern with a comparative form.</p>
    <p><strong>Correct:</strong> as tall as</p>
    <p><strong>Incorrect:</strong> as taller as</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Positive Forms of Adjectives', 15),
  (1837, 1837, '
    <h2>Comparative Adjectives</h2>
    <p>Comparative adjectives compare two people, places, objects or situations.</p>

    <h3>1. Short adjectives: add -er</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>tall → taller</li>
      <li>short → shorter</li>
      <li>fast → faster</li>
    </ul>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> The second bus is faster than the first.</p>

    <h3>2. Adjectives ending in -e</h3>
    <p>Add <em>-r</em>.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>large → larger</li>
      <li>wide → wider</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>3. Consonant changes</h3>
    <p>Some short adjectives double the final consonant.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>big → bigger</li>
      <li>hot → hotter</li>
    </ul>
    <p>For adjectives ending in consonant + y, change <em>y</em> to <em>i</em> and add <em>-er</em>: <em>happy → happier</em>.</p>

    <h3>4. Longer adjectives: use more</h3>
    <p>Many longer adjectives form the comparative with <em>more</em>.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>careful → more careful</li>
      <li>expensive → more expensive</li>
      <li>interesting → more interesting</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>5. Use than when naming the comparison</h3>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> This explanation is clearer than the previous one.</p>
    <p>Avoid double comparison forms such as <em>more easier</em>.</p>

    <h3>6. Irregular comparatives</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>good → better</li>
      <li>bad → worse</li>
      <li>far → farther or further, depending on meaning and variety</li>
    </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Comparative Adjectives', 15),
  (1838, 1838, '
    <h2>Superlative Adjectives</h2>
    <p>Superlative adjectives identify the highest or lowest degree of a quality within a group of three or more, or within a defined set.</p>

    <h3>1. Short adjectives: add -est</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>tall → tallest</li>
      <li>short → shortest</li>
      <li>fast → fastest</li>
    </ul>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> Wanjiku is the tallest learner in the group.</p>

    <h3>2. Spelling changes</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>large → largest</li>
      <li>big → biggest</li>
      <li>happy → happiest</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>3. Longer adjectives: use most</h3>
    <p>Many longer adjectives form the superlative with <em>most</em>.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>careful → most careful</li>
      <li>expensive → most expensive</li>
      <li>interesting → most interesting</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>4. Use the when appropriate</h3>
    <p>A superlative adjective commonly follows <em>the</em> when identifying one item as the highest or lowest in a group.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> That was the most difficult question in the test.</p>

    <h3>5. Irregular superlatives</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>good → best</li>
      <li>bad → worst</li>
      <li>far → farthest or furthest, depending on meaning and variety</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>6. Do not mix forms</h3>
    <p><strong>Correct:</strong> the most useful tool</p>
    <p><strong>Incorrect:</strong> the most usefulest tool</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Superlative Adjectives', 15),
  (1839, 1839, '
    <h2>Adverbs of Manner</h2>
    <p>Adverbs of manner describe how an action is performed. They commonly modify verbs.</p>

    <h3>1. Forming adverbs with -ly</h3>
    <p>Many adverbs of manner are formed by adding <em>-ly</em> to an adjective.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>careful → carefully</li>
      <li>quiet → quietly</li>
      <li>quick → quickly</li>
    </ul>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> The learner carefully checked each calculation.</p>

    <h3>2. Spelling changes</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>happy → happily</li>
      <li>easy → easily</li>
      <li>gentle → gently</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>3. Irregular forms</h3>
    <p>Some common adverbs do not follow the usual <em>-ly</em> pattern.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>good (adjective) → well (adverb)</li>
      <li>fast (adjective and adverb) → fast</li>
      <li>hard (adjective and adverb) → hard</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>4. Adjective or adverb?</h3>
    <p><strong>Adjective:</strong> The driver was careful.</p>
    <p><strong>Adverb:</strong> The driver drove carefully.</p>
    <p>Use an adjective to describe a person or thing. Use an adverb when describing how an action is carried out.</p>

    <h3>5. Position</h3>
    <p>Adverbs of manner often appear after the verb or after the verb''s object.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>The child laughed loudly.</li>
      <li>The mechanic repaired the engine carefully.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Some adverbs can appear before the verb for emphasis or style, but the sentence must remain natural.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Adverbs of Manner', 15),
  (1840, 1840, '
    <h2>Adverbs of Time and Place</h2>
    <p>Adverbs of time tell us when, how long or how often something happens in a time-related sense. Adverbs of place tell us where an action happens or in which direction movement occurs.</p>

    <h3>1. Adverbs of time</h3>
    <p>Examples include <em>today, yesterday, soon, already, recently, later</em> and <em>now</em>.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>The bus arrived yesterday.</li>
      <li>We will discuss the results later.</li>
      <li>She has already completed the assignment.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>2. Duration</h3>
    <p>Some time expressions indicate how long an activity continues.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>They waited briefly.</li>
      <li>He stayed temporarily.</li>
      <li>We worked all day.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Some duration expressions are adverbial phrases rather than single adverbs.</p>

    <h3>3. Adverbs of place</h3>
    <p>Examples include <em>here, there, outside, nearby, everywhere</em> and <em>away</em>.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Please wait outside.</li>
      <li>The school is nearby.</li>
      <li>The children searched everywhere.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>4. Direction and movement</h3>
    <p>Words such as <em>upstairs, downstairs, forward</em> and <em>back</em> can indicate direction.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> The learners walked upstairs.</p>

    <h3>5. Position in a sentence</h3>
    <p>Adverbs of time often appear at the beginning or end of a clause. Adverbs of place commonly follow the verb or its object.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Tomorrow, we will visit the laboratory.</li>
      <li>We will visit the laboratory tomorrow.</li>
      <li>The students waited outside.</li>
    </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Adverbs of Time and Place', 15),
  (1841, 1841, '
    <h2>Adverbs of Frequency</h2>
    <p>Adverbs of frequency describe how often an action or situation occurs.</p>

    <h3>1. Common frequency adverbs</h3>
    <p>From high frequency to low frequency, a useful approximate sequence is:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>always</li>
      <li>usually</li>
      <li>often</li>
      <li>sometimes</li>
      <li>rarely or seldom</li>
      <li>never</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The exact frequency depends on context; these words do not represent fixed percentages.</p>

    <h3>2. Before most main verbs</h3>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> She usually revises after class.</p>
    <p>The adverb comes before the main verb ''revises''.</p>

    <h3>3. After the verb be</h3>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> He is often late.</p>
    <p>Frequency adverbs commonly follow forms of the verb <em>be</em>.</p>

    <h3>4. With auxiliary verbs</h3>
    <p>Place the adverb after the first auxiliary verb in many ordinary sentences.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> They have never missed a lesson.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> We can usually solve the problem.</p>

    <h3>5. Expressions of frequency</h3>
    <p>Phrases such as <em>every day, once a week</em> and <em>twice a month</em> also express frequency. They commonly appear at the end of the clause.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> Our class meets twice a week.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Adverbs of Frequency', 15),
  (1842, 1842, '
    <h2>Adverbs of Degree</h2>
    <p>Adverbs of degree express the intensity, extent or completeness of an adjective, adverb or action.</p>

    <h3>1. Common adverbs of degree</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><em>very</em> — to a high degree</li>
      <li><em>extremely</em> — to a very high degree</li>
      <li><em>quite</em> — to a degree that depends on context</li>
      <li><em>rather</em> — to a noticeable or sometimes unexpected degree</li>
      <li><em>almost</em> — nearly, but not completely</li>
      <li><em>too</em> — more than is desirable or acceptable in context</li>
      <li><em>enough</em> — to the required degree</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>2. Before adjectives</h3>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> The examination was extremely difficult.</p>
    <p>''Extremely'' modifies the adjective ''difficult''.</p>

    <h3>3. Before adverbs</h3>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> The athlete ran very quickly.</p>
    <p>''Very'' modifies the adverb ''quickly''.</p>

    <h3>4. Too and enough</h3>
    <p><strong>Too + adjective/adverb:</strong> The box is too heavy to lift safely.</p>
    <p><strong>Adjective/adverb + enough:</strong> The box is light enough to carry.</p>
    <p>In this pattern, <em>too</em> suggests an excessive degree, while <em>enough</em> indicates that a requirement is met.</p>

    <h3>5. Almost and completely</h3>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> The learners had almost finished the task.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> The learners had completely finished the task.</p>
    <p>''Almost'' means nearly completed; ''completely'' means fully completed.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Adverbs of Degree', 15),
  (1843, 1843, '
    <h2>Comparative Adverbs</h2>
    <p>Comparative adverbs compare how two actions are performed.</p>

    <h3>1. Short adverbs</h3>
    <p>Some short adverbs form the comparative with <em>-er</em>.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>fast → faster</li>
      <li>hard → harder</li>
      <li>late → later</li>
    </ul>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> The second runner finished faster than the first.</p>

    <h3>2. Adverbs ending in -ly</h3>
    <p>Many adverbs ending in <em>-ly</em> use <em>more</em>.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>carefully → more carefully</li>
      <li>slowly → more slowly</li>
      <li>efficiently → more efficiently</li>
    </ul>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> The second learner explained the method more clearly than the first.</p>

    <h3>3. Irregular forms</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>well → better</li>
      <li>badly → worse</li>
      <li>far → farther or further in relevant senses</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>4. Use than for a direct comparison</h3>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> The new computer processes data more quickly than the old one.</p>

    <h3>5. Do not confuse adjectives and adverbs</h3>
    <p><strong>Adjective:</strong> The second runner is faster.</p>
    <p><strong>Adverb:</strong> The second runner ran faster.</p>
    <p>The adjective describes the runner; the adverb describes how the runner ran.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Comparative Adverbs', 15),
  (1844, 1844, '
    <h2>Superlative Adverbs</h2>
    <p>Superlative adverbs identify the highest or lowest degree of the way an action is performed within a group or defined set.</p>

    <h3>1. Short adverbs</h3>
    <p>Some short adverbs form the superlative with <em>-est</em>.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>fast → fastest</li>
      <li>hard → hardest</li>
      <li>early → earliest</li>
    </ul>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> Of all the runners, Kamau ran fastest.</p>

    <h3>2. Adverbs ending in -ly</h3>
    <p>Many adverbs ending in <em>-ly</em> form the superlative with <em>most</em>.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>carefully → most carefully</li>
      <li>slowly → most slowly</li>
      <li>efficiently → most efficiently</li>
    </ul>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> Of all the candidates, Wanjiku answered the questions most accurately.</p>

    <h3>3. Irregular forms</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>well → best</li>
      <li>badly → worst</li>
    </ul>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> Of the whole team, Otieno performed best.</p>

    <h3>4. Identify the group</h3>
    <p>A superlative makes sense when a comparison set is understood or stated.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> Of the four teams, this team played best.</p>

    <h3>5. Avoid double forms</h3>
    <p><strong>Correct:</strong> She worked most efficiently.</p>
    <p><strong>Incorrect:</strong> She worked most efficientliest.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Superlative Adverbs', 15),
  (1845, 1845, '
    <h2>Distinguishing Adjectives from Adverbs</h2>
    <p>Adjectives and adverbs perform different grammatical jobs. Choosing the correct form depends on the word being described, not simply on whether the word ends in <em>-ly</em>.</p>

    <h3>1. Adjectives describe nouns and pronouns</h3>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> The careful learner checked the answer.</p>
    <p>''Careful'' describes the noun ''learner''.</p>

    <h3>2. Adverbs can describe actions</h3>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> The learner checked the answer carefully.</p>
    <p>''Carefully'' describes how the learner checked the answer.</p>

    <h3>3. After linking verbs, use adjectives</h3>
    <p>Linking verbs connect the subject with a description or state.</p>
    <p><strong>Examples:</strong></p>
    <ul>
      <li>The soup tastes delicious.</li>
      <li>The cloth feels soft.</li>
      <li>The learners seem confident.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>These sentences describe the subject, rather than the manner of an action.</p>

    <h3>4. Some words have the same adjective and adverb form</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>a fast runner / runs fast</li>
      <li>a hard question / works hard</li>
      <li>an early train / arrived early</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>5. Watch for meaning changes</h3>
    <p><em>Hard</em> means with effort; <em>hardly</em> means almost not.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> She worked hard. (She made a great effort.)</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> She hardly worked. (She did very little work.)</p>
    <p>Adding <em>-ly</em> does not always preserve the meaning.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Distinguishing Adjectives from Adverbs', 15),
  (1846, 1846, '
    <h2>Position and Meaning of Adverbs</h2>
    <p>An adverb''s position can affect clarity, emphasis and sometimes the meaning of a sentence. Place the adverb where its intended target is clear.</p>

    <h3>1. Adverbs of manner</h3>
    <p>These often appear after the verb or after its object.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>The learner wrote neatly.</li>
      <li>The learner copied the notes neatly.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>2. Adverbs of frequency</h3>
    <p>These commonly appear before the main verb but after forms of <em>be</em>.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>She often revises at night.</li>
      <li>She is often prepared.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>3. Adverbs of time and place</h3>
    <p>Time adverbs often appear at the beginning or end of a clause. Place adverbs commonly appear after the verb or object.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>We will meet tomorrow.</li>
      <li>The children played outside.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>4. Adverbs can change the intended meaning</h3>
    <p>Compare:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Only Amina answered the question.</strong> Amina was the only person who answered.</li>
      <li><strong>Amina only answered the question.</strong> She answered, but did little else.</li>
      <li><strong>Amina answered only the question.</strong> She answered the question and nothing else.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The scope of <em>only</em> depends on its position and context.</p>

    <h3>5. Keep the sentence unambiguous</h3>
    <p>Position an adverb near the word or phrase it is intended to modify, especially when words such as <em>only, nearly, almost</em> and <em>just</em> are used.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Position and Meaning of Adverbs', 15),
  (1847, 1847, '
    <h2>Prepositions of Time</h2>
    <p>Prepositions of time show when an event happens or how long it lasts. The most common are <strong>at</strong>, <strong>on</strong> and <strong>in</strong>.</p>

    <h3>1. At</h3>
    <p>Use <strong>at</strong> for precise times and certain fixed expressions.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>The lesson begins <strong>at</strong> eight o''clock.</li>
      <li>We arrived <strong>at</strong> noon.</li>
      <li>She studies <strong>at</strong> night.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>2. On</h3>
    <p>Use <strong>on</strong> for days and specific dates.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>The examination is <strong>on</strong> Monday.</li>
      <li>Kenya gained independence <strong>on</strong> 12 December 1963.</li>
      <li>We met <strong>on</strong> my birthday.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>3. In</h3>
    <p>Use <strong>in</strong> for months, years, seasons and longer periods.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Schools reopened <strong>in</strong> January.</li>
      <li>She was born <strong>in</strong> 2007.</li>
      <li>The days are shorter <strong>in</strong> the dry season.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Other time prepositions</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Since:</strong> the starting point — He has studied here <em>since</em> 2024.</li>
      <li><strong>For:</strong> the duration — He has studied here <em>for</em> two years.</li>
      <li><strong>By:</strong> no later than a deadline — Submit the assignment <em>by</em> Friday.</li>
      <li><strong>Until:</strong> continuing up to a point — The library remains open <em>until</em> six.</li>
      <li><strong>During:</strong> within a period or event — Nobody spoke <em>during</em> the examination.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Common error</h3>
    <p>Do not normally add <em>at</em>, <em>on</em> or <em>in</em> before <em>last</em>, <em>next</em>, <em>this</em> or <em>every</em> when these words refer directly to time.</p>
    <p>Correct: We travelled <strong>last Friday</strong>.</p>
    <p>Incorrect: We travelled <strong>on last Friday</strong>.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Prepositions of Time', 15),
  (1848, 1848, '
    <h2>Prepositions of Place</h2>
    <p>Prepositions of place explain where a person, object or event is located. The correct choice depends on the relationship between the objects.</p>

    <h3>At</h3>
    <p>Use <strong>at</strong> to identify a point, location or place viewed as a destination or activity point.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>She is <strong>at</strong> the bus stop.</li>
      <li>My brother is <strong>at</strong> school.</li>
      <li>We met <strong>at</strong> the entrance.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>In</h3>
    <p>Use <strong>in</strong> when something is inside an enclosed space, within an area, or in a geographical region.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>The keys are <strong>in</strong> the drawer.</li>
      <li>They live <strong>in</strong> Kenya.</li>
      <li>The learners are <strong>in</strong> the classroom.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>On</h3>
    <p>Use <strong>on</strong> when something touches or rests on a surface.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>The book is <strong>on</strong> the desk.</li>
      <li>A notice is displayed <strong>on</strong> the wall.</li>
      <li>There is mud <strong>on</strong> his shoes.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Other useful prepositions</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Above:</strong> at a higher level — The clock hangs above the door.</li>
      <li><strong>Below:</strong> at a lower level — The valley lies below the hill.</li>
      <li><strong>Under:</strong> lower than something, often directly beneath it — The cat hid under the table.</li>
      <li><strong>Between:</strong> separating two people or things, or distinct items considered individually — The shop is between the bank and the clinic.</li>
      <li><strong>Among:</strong> within a group — She stood among the other learners.</li>
      <li><strong>Behind:</strong> at the back of — The bicycle is behind the house.</li>
      <li><strong>In front of:</strong> ahead of — The bus stopped in front of the gate.</li>
      <li><strong>Beside:</strong> next to — Sit beside your partner.</li>
      <li><strong>Opposite:</strong> facing or across from — The clinic is opposite the market.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Choose by meaning</h3>
    <p>Do not select a preposition simply because it is commonly used with a particular noun. Picture the relationship being described: inside, on a surface, at a point, higher, lower or next to something.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Prepositions of Place', 15),
  (1849, 1849, '
    <h2>Prepositions of Direction and Movement</h2>
    <p>These prepositions show where movement begins, the path it follows, or the destination it reaches.</p>

    <h3>To and towards</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>To:</strong> movement to a destination — We walked <em>to</em> the library.</li>
      <li><strong>Towards:</strong> movement in the direction of something, without necessarily reaching it — The child ran <em>towards</em> the gate.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Into and out of</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Into:</strong> movement from outside to inside — The learners entered <em>into</em> the hall.</li>
      <li><strong>Out of:</strong> movement from inside to outside — She stepped <em>out of</em> the room.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>In ordinary English, <em>entered the hall</em> is also natural; the verb <em>enter</em> usually takes a direct object without an additional preposition.</p>

    <h3>Onto and off</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Onto:</strong> movement to a surface — The cat jumped <em>onto</em> the roof.</li>
      <li><strong>Off:</strong> movement away from a surface — The cup fell <em>off</em> the table.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Across, through and along</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Across:</strong> from one side to another — We crossed <em>across</em> the field.</li>
      <li><strong>Through:</strong> movement within a space or from one side to another through it — The path passes <em>through</em> the forest.</li>
      <li><strong>Along:</strong> movement following a line or length — They walked <em>along</em> the river.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Past, over and under</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Past:</strong> beyond a point — Walk <em>past</em> the post office.</li>
      <li><strong>Over:</strong> across or above an obstacle — The athlete jumped <em>over</em> the barrier.</li>
      <li><strong>Under:</strong> beneath or below an obstacle — The dog crawled <em>under</em> the fence.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Common error</h3>
    <p>Distinguish a destination from a direction. If someone walks <em>towards</em> the station, they are moving in its direction. If they walk <em>to</em> the station, the station is their destination.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Prepositions of Direction and Movement', 15),
  (1850, 1850, '
    <h2>Prepositional Phrases</h2>
    <p>A <strong>prepositional phrase</strong> begins with a preposition and includes its object, together with any words describing that object.</p>

    <h3>Structure</h3>
    <p><strong>Preposition + object of the preposition</strong></p>
    <ul>
      <li><em>under the table</em> — under is the preposition; table is its object.</li>
      <li><em>after the final examination</em> — after is the preposition; examination is its object.</li>
      <li><em>beside the old classroom</em> — beside is the preposition; classroom is its object, described by old.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>What prepositional phrases do</h3>
    <p>A prepositional phrase can provide information about a noun or a verb.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Describing a noun:</strong> The books <em>on the shelf</em> belong to the teacher. The phrase identifies which books.</li>
      <li><strong>Modifying a verb:</strong> The learners revised <em>after lunch</em>. The phrase tells when they revised.</li>
      <li><strong>Describing place:</strong> The ball rolled <em>under the desk</em>. The phrase tells where it rolled.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Finding the complete phrase</h3>
    <p>Do not identify only the preposition when asked for the phrase. Include the preposition and its object, along with any modifiers that belong to the object.</p>

    <h3>Avoid confusing the phrase with the subject</h3>
    <p>In <em>The box of old books is heavy</em>, the subject is <em>The box of old books</em>. Within it, <em>of old books</em> is a prepositional phrase. The noun <em>books</em> does not become the main subject merely because it is close to the verb.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Prepositional Phrases', 15),
  (1851, 1851, '
    <h2>Common Verb and Adjective Preposition Patterns</h2>
    <p>Some verbs and adjectives are conventionally followed by particular prepositions. The choice often depends on the expression rather than a general rule.</p>

    <h3>Common verb patterns</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>depend on:</strong> Success depends <em>on</em> consistent effort.</li>
      <li><strong>believe in:</strong> She believes <em>in</em> honest work.</li>
      <li><strong>listen to:</strong> Please listen <em>to</em> the instructions.</li>
      <li><strong>belong to:</strong> This bag belongs <em>to</em> Amina.</li>
      <li><strong>apologise for:</strong> He apologised <em>for</em> the mistake.</li>
      <li><strong>agree with:</strong> I agree <em>with</em> your explanation.</li>
      <li><strong>apply for:</strong> She applied <em>for</em> the scholarship.</li>
      <li><strong>insist on:</strong> They insisted <em>on</em> receiving a receipt.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Common adjective patterns</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>interested in:</strong> The learners are interested <em>in</em> science.</li>
      <li><strong>good at:</strong> He is good <em>at</em> mathematics.</li>
      <li><strong>afraid of:</strong> The child is afraid <em>of</em> snakes.</li>
      <li><strong>proud of:</strong> We are proud <em>of</em> our achievement.</li>
      <li><strong>responsible for:</strong> She is responsible <em>for</em> the equipment.</li>
      <li><strong>similar to:</strong> This answer is similar <em>to</em> mine.</li>
      <li><strong>different from:</strong> Your method is different <em>from</em> mine.</li>
      <li><strong>pleased with:</strong> The teacher is pleased <em>with</em> the progress.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Meaning can change the pattern</h3>
    <p>Learn the complete expression in context. For example, <em>think of</em> can mean to imagine or consider, while <em>think about</em> often means to give something careful consideration.</p>

    <h3>How to correct an error</h3>
    <p>First identify the verb or adjective. Then recall the preposition conventionally used with that expression. Finally, read the full sentence to confirm that the meaning fits.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Common Verb and Adjective Preposition Patterns', 15),
  (1852, 1852, '
    <h2>Coordinating Conjunctions</h2>
    <p>Coordinating conjunctions connect words, phrases or clauses that have equal grammatical status.</p>

    <h3>The main coordinating conjunctions</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>For:</strong> gives a reason — We returned home, for the road was flooded.</li>
      <li><strong>And:</strong> adds information — Amina revised and completed the exercise.</li>
      <li><strong>Nor:</strong> adds a negative idea — He did not call, nor did he send a message.</li>
      <li><strong>But:</strong> shows contrast — She was tired, but she continued studying.</li>
      <li><strong>Or:</strong> presents a choice — You may write in pen or pencil.</li>
      <li><strong>Yet:</strong> shows an unexpected contrast — The task was difficult, yet he completed it.</li>
      <li><strong>So:</strong> shows a result — The road was flooded, so we returned home.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Joining words and phrases</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Words: We bought <em>pens and pencils</em>.</li>
      <li>Phrases: The learner spoke <em>clearly but briefly</em>.</li>
      <li>Clauses: The rain stopped, <em>and the match resumed</em>.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Punctuation when joining clauses</h3>
    <p>When a coordinating conjunction joins two independent clauses, a comma is normally placed before the conjunction.</p>
    <p>Correct: The bell rang, and the learners left the classroom.</p>
    <p>When joining only two short words or phrases, a comma is generally unnecessary.</p>
    <p>Correct: We bought bread and milk.</p>

    <h3>Choose the relationship, not just the word</h3>
    <p>Use <em>and</em> for addition, <em>but</em> or <em>yet</em> for contrast, <em>or</em> for alternatives, and <em>so</em> for a result. The relationship between the ideas determines the appropriate conjunction.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Coordinating Conjunctions', 15),
  (1853, 1853, '
    <h2>Joining Equal Words and Clauses</h2>
    <p>Coordinating conjunctions allow a writer to connect grammatical units of equal status. The units being joined should fit together logically and grammatically.</p>

    <h3>Joining individual words</h3>
    <p>Use a coordinating conjunction to connect words performing the same grammatical role.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>She bought <em>rice and beans</em>.</li>
      <li>The question was <em>difficult but fair</em>.</li>
      <li>You can choose <em>tea or juice</em>.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Joining phrases</h3>
    <p>Parallel phrases make the relationship between ideas clear.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>He enjoys <em>reading novels and solving puzzles</em>.</li>
      <li>The team travelled <em>by bus but returned by train</em>.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Joining independent clauses</h3>
    <p>An independent clause expresses a complete thought and can stand as a sentence. Two independent clauses can be joined with a coordinating conjunction.</p>
    <p><strong>Pattern:</strong> Independent clause + comma + coordinating conjunction + independent clause.</p>
    <p>Example: The team trained every afternoon, but it lost the final match.</p>

    <h3>Avoid faulty joining</h3>
    <p>Do not join two complete sentences with only a comma. This creates a comma splice.</p>
    <p>Incorrect: The rain stopped, the players returned.</p>
    <p>Correct: The rain stopped, and the players returned.</p>
    <p>Another option is to use a full stop: The rain stopped. The players returned.</p>

    <h3>Keep the joined elements parallel</h3>
    <p>Parallel elements have matching grammatical forms.</p>
    <p>Less balanced: She likes <em>reading, swimming and to cycle</em>.</p>
    <p>Better: She likes <em>reading, swimming and cycling</em>.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Joining Equal Words and Clauses', 15),
  (1854, 1854, '
    <h2>Subordinating Conjunctions</h2>
    <p>A subordinating conjunction introduces a subordinate clause and shows how that clause relates to a main clause.</p>

    <h3>Cause and reason</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Because:</strong> We stayed indoors because it was raining.</li>
      <li><strong>Since:</strong> Since the road was blocked, we used another route.</li>
      <li><strong>As:</strong> As the bell had rung, the learners entered the classroom.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Time</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>When:</strong> Call me when you arrive.</li>
      <li><strong>While:</strong> She listened while the teacher explained.</li>
      <li><strong>Before:</strong> Wash your hands before you eat.</li>
      <li><strong>After:</strong> We reviewed our answers after we finished.</li>
      <li><strong>Until:</strong> Wait here until the bus arrives.</li>
      <li><strong>As soon as:</strong> Tell me as soon as you know.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Condition</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>If:</strong> If you practise regularly, your accuracy may improve.</li>
      <li><strong>Unless:</strong> Unless you leave now, you will miss the bus.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Contrast and concession</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Although:</strong> Although she was tired, she completed the task.</li>
      <li><strong>Even though:</strong> Even though it was late, they continued working.</li>
      <li><strong>Whereas:</strong> Amina prefers reading, whereas Otieno prefers experiments.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Purpose and result</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>So that:</strong> He spoke slowly so that everyone could understand.</li>
      <li><strong>So ... that:</strong> The rain was so heavy that the match was postponed.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Punctuation</h3>
    <p>When a subordinate clause comes first, it is normally followed by a comma.</p>
    <p>Correct: <em>Although the task was difficult, she completed it.</em></p>
    <p>When the main clause comes first, a comma is often unnecessary.</p>
    <p>Correct: <em>She completed it although the task was difficult.</em></p>

    <h3>Do not create an incomplete sentence</h3>
    <p>A subordinate clause cannot normally stand alone as a complete sentence in formal writing.</p>
    <p>Incomplete: Because the bus arrived late.</p>
    <p>Complete: Because the bus arrived late, we missed the first lesson.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Subordinating Conjunctions', 15),
  (1855, 1855, '
    <h2>Cause, Time, Condition and Contrast</h2>
    <p>Conjunctions help readers understand the logical relationship between clauses. Choosing the wrong one can change the meaning of a sentence.</p>

    <h3>Cause</h3>
    <p>Cause conjunctions explain why something happens.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Because:</strong> We postponed the match because the field was wet.</li>
      <li><strong>Since:</strong> Since the shop was closed, we returned home.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Ask: <em>Why did it happen?</em></p>

    <h3>Time</h3>
    <p>Time conjunctions show when events occur or how they relate in sequence.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Before:</strong> Lock the door before you leave.</li>
      <li><strong>After:</strong> We ate after the meeting ended.</li>
      <li><strong>While:</strong> He took notes while the speaker talked.</li>
      <li><strong>Until:</strong> Stay here until the teacher returns.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Ask: <em>When did it happen?</em></p>

    <h3>Condition</h3>
    <p>Condition conjunctions state what must happen or be true for another event to occur.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>If:</strong> If you submit the form today, it can be processed tomorrow.</li>
      <li><strong>Unless:</strong> Unless you revise, you may forget the procedure.</li>
    </ul>
    <p><em>Unless</em> generally means <em>if not</em>.</p>
    <p>Example: Unless you hurry, you will miss the bus. = If you do not hurry, you will miss the bus.</p>

    <h3>Contrast</h3>
    <p>Contrast conjunctions show a difference between ideas or an unexpected outcome.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Although:</strong> Although it was raining, the game continued.</li>
      <li><strong>Whereas:</strong> Kamau prefers tea, whereas Njeri prefers coffee.</li>
      <li><strong>While:</strong> While one team defended well, the other attacked effectively.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Choose the logical relationship</h3>
    <p>Do not choose a conjunction merely because it sounds familiar. Decide whether the second clause gives a reason, a time relationship, a condition or a contrast.</p>

    <h3>Avoid double conjunction errors</h3>
    <p>Do not normally use <em>although</em> and <em>but</em> together to connect the same contrast.</p>
    <p>Incorrect: Although it was raining, but the game continued.</p>
    <p>Correct: Although it was raining, the game continued.</p>
    <p>Also correct: It was raining, but the game continued.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Cause, Time, Condition and Contrast', 15),
  (1856, 1856, '
    <h2>Correlative Conjunctions</h2>
    <p>Correlative conjunctions work in pairs to connect words, phrases or clauses. Each part of the pair signals a relationship between the elements being joined.</p>

    <h3>Both ... and</h3>
    <p>Use this pair to include two things.</p>
    <p>Example: <em>Both Amina and Wanjiku passed the examination.</em></p>

    <h3>Either ... or</h3>
    <p>Use this pair to present alternatives.</p>
    <p>Example: <em>You can either submit the assignment today or request an extension.</em></p>

    <h3>Neither ... nor</h3>
    <p>Use this pair to exclude both alternatives.</p>
    <p>Example: <em>Neither the teacher nor the learners were ready.</em></p>
    <p>With <em>neither ... nor</em>, verb agreement can depend on the subjects and the variety of English used. In formal writing, a verb agreeing with the nearer subject is common, but rewriting the sentence can avoid awkward agreement.</p>

    <h3>Not only ... but also</h3>
    <p>Use this pair to add emphasis to two related ideas.</p>
    <p>Example: <em>The candidate was not only accurate but also clear.</em></p>
    <p>When <em>not only</em> begins a clause, inversion is normally used in formal English:</p>
    <p><em>Not only did she complete the task, but she also explained her method.</em></p>

    <h3>Whether ... or</h3>
    <p>Use this pair when considering alternatives or possibilities.</p>
    <p>Example: <em>We must decide whether to travel by bus or to walk.</em></p>

    <h3>Keep the joined structures parallel</h3>
    <p>The words or phrases joined by a correlative pair should have matching grammatical forms whenever possible.</p>
    <p>Less balanced: She enjoys both <em>reading and to write</em>.</p>
    <p>Better: She enjoys both <em>reading and writing</em>.</p>

    <h3>Check the pair</h3>
    <p>After writing the sentence, check that both parts of the conjunction are present and that the connected elements fit grammatically.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Correlative Conjunctions', 15),
  (1857, 1857, '
    <h2>Correlative Pairs</h2>
    <p>Correlative conjunctions work together as pairs. Each pair connects related grammatical elements and communicates a specific relationship.</p>

    <h3>Both ... and</h3>
    <p>Use this pair to include two people, things or ideas.</p>
    <p>Example: Both the teacher and the learners agreed.</p>

    <h3>Either ... or</h3>
    <p>Use this pair to present alternatives.</p>
    <p>Example: You can either revise now or study after supper.</p>

    <h3>Neither ... nor</h3>
    <p>Use this pair to exclude both alternatives.</p>
    <p>Example: Neither the keys nor the wallet was missing.</p>

    <h3>Not only ... but also</h3>
    <p>Use this pair to add emphasis to two related points.</p>
    <p>Example: The learner was not only accurate but also thorough.</p>

    <h3>Whether ... or</h3>
    <p>Use this pair to present alternatives being considered.</p>
    <p>Example: We must decide whether to walk or take a bus.</p>

    <h3>Maintain parallel structure</h3>
    <p>The elements connected by the pair should normally have matching grammatical forms.</p>
    <p>Incorrect: She enjoys both reading and to write.</p>
    <p>Correct: She enjoys both reading and writing.</p>

    <h3>Watch the subject–verb relationship</h3>
    <p>With <em>both ... and</em>, two singular subjects generally take a plural verb.</p>
    <p>Example: Both the teacher and the learner <strong>are</strong> present.</p>
    <p>With <em>either ... or</em> and <em>neither ... nor</em>, verb agreement can depend on the subjects'' number and proximity. When the result sounds awkward, rewrite the sentence to make the subject–verb relationship clear.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Correlative Pairs', 15),
  (1858, 1858, '
    <h2>Addition and Contrast</h2>
    <p>Connecting expressions show whether a writer is adding information or presenting a difference between ideas.</p>

    <h3>Expressing addition</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>And:</strong> joins related information — The library is quiet, and it has enough study space.</li>
      <li><strong>Also:</strong> adds another point — The library also provides reference books.</li>
      <li><strong>Furthermore:</strong> adds a supporting point — Furthermore, learners can borrow books.</li>
      <li><strong>Moreover:</strong> adds emphasis to a related point — Moreover, the service is free.</li>
      <li><strong>In addition:</strong> introduces another point — In addition, the school provides revision materials.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Expressing contrast</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>But:</strong> joins contrasting ideas — The task was difficult, but she completed it.</li>
      <li><strong>However:</strong> introduces a contrasting statement — The task was difficult. However, she completed it.</li>
      <li><strong>Although:</strong> introduces an unexpected situation — Although she was tired, she continued.</li>
      <li><strong>Nevertheless:</strong> signals that the next point remains true despite the previous one — The road was rough. Nevertheless, we arrived safely.</li>
      <li><strong>On the other hand:</strong> introduces an alternative perspective — Private transport is convenient. On the other hand, it can be expensive.</li>
      <li><strong>Whereas:</strong> contrasts two situations in one sentence — Amina prefers novels, whereas Musa prefers biographies.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Choose a connector that fits the logic</h3>
    <p>Use addition when the second point supports or extends the first. Use contrast when the points differ or when the second point is unexpected.</p>

    <h3>Punctuation matters</h3>
    <p>When <em>however</em> connects two independent sentences, it is commonly preceded by a full stop or semicolon and followed by a comma.</p>
    <p>Correct: The plan was expensive. However, it was effective.</p>
    <p>Correct: The plan was expensive; however, it was effective.</p>
    <p>Incorrect: The plan was expensive, however, it was effective.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Addition and Contrast', 15),
  (1859, 1859, '
    <h2>Cause and Result</h2>
    <p>Cause-and-result expressions explain why something happens and what follows from it. The choice of connector depends on which part of the relationship you want to emphasise.</p>

    <h3>Because and since</h3>
    <p>These expressions introduce a reason.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>The match was postponed because the field was flooded.</li>
      <li>Since the water supply was interrupted, the school closed early.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Therefore and consequently</h3>
    <p>These expressions introduce a result.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>The field was flooded. Therefore, the match was postponed.</li>
      <li>The water supply was interrupted; consequently, the school closed early.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>As a result</h3>
    <p>This expression makes the outcome explicit.</p>
    <p>Example: The learner practised each day. As a result, her accuracy improved.</p>

    <h3>So</h3>
    <p><em>So</em> can connect a cause to a result in one sentence.</p>
    <p>Example: The electricity went off, so the lesson ended early.</p>

    <h3>Because versus therefore</h3>
    <p><strong>Because</strong> introduces the reason; <strong>therefore</strong> introduces the conclusion or result.</p>
    <p>Because the road was blocked, we took another route.</p>
    <p>The road was blocked; therefore, we took another route.</p>

    <h3>Avoid double marking</h3>
    <p>Do not normally use <em>because</em> and <em>therefore</em> together in a simple construction where they redundantly mark the same cause and result.</p>
    <p>Incorrect: Because the road was blocked, therefore we took another route.</p>
    <p>Correct: Because the road was blocked, we took another route.</p>
    <p>Also correct: The road was blocked; therefore, we took another route.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Cause and Result', 15),
  (1860, 1860, '
    <h2>Sequence and Condition</h2>
    <p>Sequence connectors show the order in which events happen. Condition connectors show what must be true or happen for another event to occur.</p>

    <h3>Sequence</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>First:</strong> First, read the instructions.</li>
      <li><strong>Next:</strong> Next, identify the information provided.</li>
      <li><strong>Then:</strong> Then, choose the correct method.</li>
      <li><strong>Afterwards:</strong> Afterwards, check your answer.</li>
      <li><strong>Finally:</strong> Finally, submit your work.</li>
      <li><strong>Meanwhile:</strong> The teacher checked the register. Meanwhile, the learners prepared their books.</li>
      <li><strong>Before:</strong> Check your work before you submit it.</li>
      <li><strong>After:</strong> Wash the equipment after you finish the experiment.</li>
      <li><strong>As soon as:</strong> Inform the teacher as soon as the problem occurs.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Conditions</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>If:</strong> If you revise, you may improve your results.</li>
      <li><strong>Unless:</strong> Unless you save the file, you may lose your work.</li>
      <li><strong>Provided that:</strong> You may borrow the equipment provided that you return it on time.</li>
      <li><strong>As long as:</strong> You can use the room as long as you keep it clean.</li>
      <li><strong>In case:</strong> Take an umbrella in case it rains.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Do not confuse in case with if</h3>
    <p><em>If it rains, we will stay indoors</em> describes what we will do on the condition that rain occurs.</p>
    <p><em>Take an umbrella in case it rains</em> means to prepare for the possibility of rain.</p>

    <h3>Condition and future time</h3>
    <p>In many future conditions introduced by <em>if</em>, use the present simple in the condition clause and <em>will</em> in the result clause.</p>
    <p>Correct: If she studies, she will improve.</p>
    <p>Incorrect: If she will study, she will improve.</p>
    <p>This is a common pattern, although other conditional structures are used for general truths, hypothetical situations and past unreal conditions.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Sequence and Condition', 15),
  (1861, 1861, '
    <h2>Synonyms</h2>
    <p>Synonyms are words with the same or similar meanings. They are useful for precise expression, avoiding unnecessary repetition and understanding vocabulary in context.</p>

    <h3>Examples</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Begin:</strong> start</li>
      <li><strong>Rapid:</strong> fast</li>
      <li><strong>Assist:</strong> help</li>
      <li><strong>Purchase:</strong> buy</li>
      <li><strong>Silent:</strong> quiet</li>
      <li><strong>Accurate:</strong> correct or exact</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Synonyms are not always interchangeable</h3>
    <p>Words with similar meanings can differ in formality, strength, emotional tone or grammatical use.</p>
    <p>Example: <em>child</em> and <em>kid</em> refer to a young person, but <em>kid</em> is more informal.</p>
    <p>Example: <em>thin</em> and <em>slender</em> can describe a narrow body shape, but <em>slender</em> often carries a more positive tone.</p>

    <h3>Use the sentence as evidence</h3>
    <p>When asked for a synonym in a passage, first determine how the word is used in that sentence. Then choose a replacement that preserves the intended meaning.</p>

    <h3>Check grammar as well as meaning</h3>
    <p>A replacement must fit the sentence grammatically.</p>
    <p>Example: In ''The athlete ran rapidly'', <em>quickly</em> can replace <em>rapidly</em> because both are adverbs describing how the athlete ran.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Synonyms', 15),
  (1862, 1862, '
    <h2>Antonyms</h2>
    <p>Antonyms are words with opposite or contrasting meanings. Recognising them helps with vocabulary, comprehension and precise expression.</p>

    <h3>Common examples</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Ancient — modern</li>
      <li>Expand — contract</li>
      <li>Generous — selfish</li>
      <li>Victory — defeat</li>
      <li>Scarce — abundant</li>
      <li>Accept — reject</li>
      <li>Temporary — permanent</li>
      <li>Increase — decrease</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Different kinds of opposites</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Gradable opposites:</strong> hot and cold; something may be warm or cool between them.</li>
      <li><strong>Complementary opposites:</strong> alive and dead, in their usual literal meanings.</li>
      <li><strong>Relational opposites:</strong> teacher and student in a teaching relationship; buy and sell describe opposite sides of a transaction.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Context determines the answer</h3>
    <p>A word can have more than one meaning, so its opposite depends on how it is used.</p>
    <p>Example: The opposite of <em>light</em> can be <em>heavy</em> when describing weight, or <em>dark</em> when describing brightness.</p>

    <h3>Prefixes can reverse meaning</h3>
    <p>Some antonyms are formed with prefixes such as <em>un-</em>, <em>in-</em>, <em>im-</em>, <em>dis-</em> or <em>ir-</em>.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Fair — unfair</li>
      <li>Correct — incorrect</li>
      <li>Possible — impossible</li>
      <li>Agree — disagree</li>
      <li>Regular — irregular</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Not every word accepts every prefix. Learn established word forms rather than adding a prefix at random.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Antonyms', 15),
  (1863, 1863, '
    <h2>Homophones</h2>
    <p>Homophones are words that sound alike but have different meanings and often different spellings. Correct usage depends on understanding each word''s meaning.</p>

    <h3>Common homophones</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>There:</strong> a place or position — Put the books there.</li>
      <li><strong>Their:</strong> belonging to them — Their books are on the desk.</li>
      <li><strong>They''re:</strong> contraction of they are — They''re ready for the lesson.</li>
      <li><strong>To:</strong> direction or part of an infinitive — She went to school.</li>
      <li><strong>Too:</strong> also or excessively — I want to go too; the bag is too heavy.</li>
      <li><strong>Two:</strong> the number 2 — Two learners arrived early.</li>
      <li><strong>Hear:</strong> perceive sound — I can hear the bell.</li>
      <li><strong>Here:</strong> this place — Come here.</li>
      <li><strong>Right:</strong> correct or a direction — Your answer is right.</li>
      <li><strong>Write:</strong> put words on paper or a screen — Write your name.</li>
      <li><strong>Peace:</strong> absence of conflict — The community wants peace.</li>
      <li><strong>Piece:</strong> a part of something — Take a piece of bread.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>How to choose correctly</h3>
    <p>Do not choose a homophone by sound alone. Ask what meaning the sentence requires and check the word''s grammatical role.</p>

    <h3>Use grammar to distinguish similar forms</h3>
    <p><em>They''re</em> can be expanded to <em>they are</em>. If the sentence still makes sense after that substitution, the contraction may be correct.</p>
    <p>Example: <em>They''re waiting outside.</em> → <em>They are waiting outside.</em></p>
    <p><em>Their</em> shows possession and cannot be replaced by <em>they are</em>.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Homophones', 15),
  (1864, 1864, '
    <h2>Homonyms</h2>
    <p>Homonyms are words that share the same form or pronunciation but have different meanings. In common school usage, the term often covers words that are spelled and pronounced alike but have unrelated meanings.</p>

    <h3>Examples in context</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Bat:</strong> a flying mammal — A bat emerged from the cave.</li>
      <li><strong>Bat:</strong> equipment used in a game — She bought a new cricket bat.</li>
      <li><strong>Bank:</strong> a financial institution — He deposited money at the bank.</li>
      <li><strong>Bank:</strong> the land beside a river — They sat on the river bank.</li>
      <li><strong>Match:</strong> a contest — The match begins at three.</li>
      <li><strong>Match:</strong> a small stick used to produce a flame — He lit a match.</li>
      <li><strong>Ring:</strong> jewellery worn on a finger — She wore a gold ring.</li>
      <li><strong>Ring:</strong> the sound of a bell or telephone — I heard the phone ring.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Use context to identify meaning</h3>
    <p>When a word has several meanings, examine the other words in the sentence. These clues reveal which meaning the writer intends.</p>

    <h3>Distinguish homonyms from homophones</h3>
    <p>Homophones sound alike but can be spelled differently, such as <em>flour</em> and <em>flower</em>. Homonyms, in the narrower school sense, share their written form and pronunciation but have different meanings, such as <em>bat</em> for an animal and a piece of sports equipment.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Homonyms', 15),
  (1865, 1865, '
    <h2>Homographs</h2>
    <p>Homographs are words spelled the same way but with different meanings. Depending on the words involved, their pronunciation may be the same or different.</p>

    <h3>Examples with different pronunciations</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Lead</strong> /leed/: to guide — She will lead the group.</li>
      <li><strong>Lead</strong> /led/: a metal — The pipe contained lead.</li>
      <li><strong>Wind</strong> /wind/: moving air — The wind damaged the roof.</li>
      <li><strong>Wind</strong> /wynd/: to twist or turn — Wind the clock carefully.</li>
      <li><strong>Present</strong> /PREZ-ent/: a gift — He received a present.</li>
      <li><strong>Present</strong> /pri-ZENT/: to show or deliver — They will present their findings.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Examples with the same pronunciation</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Bat:</strong> a flying mammal or sports equipment.</li>
      <li><strong>Ring:</strong> a piece of jewellery or the sound of a bell.</li>
      <li><strong>Park:</strong> a public green space or the action of leaving a vehicle in a place.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>How to interpret a homograph</h3>
    <p>Use the sentence''s meaning and grammatical structure. A noun and a verb may have the same spelling but different pronunciations or stress patterns.</p>
    <p>Example: In <em>They will present the report</em>, <em>present</em> is a verb. In <em>She received a present</em>, it is a noun.</p>

    <h3>Why homographs matter</h3>
    <p>Recognising homographs improves reading comprehension because the same written form may represent different meanings and, sometimes, different spoken forms.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Homographs', 15),
  (1866, 1866, '
    <h2>Prefixes</h2>
    <p>A prefix is a group of letters added to the beginning of a word to change or refine its meaning.</p>

    <h3>Prefixes that express negation or the opposite</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>un-:</strong> unhappy, unfair, unsafe</li>
      <li><strong>in-:</strong> inaccurate, incomplete, inexpensive</li>
      <li><strong>im-:</strong> impossible, impatient, improper</li>
      <li><strong>dis-:</strong> disagree, dishonest, disconnect</li>
      <li><strong>ir-:</strong> irregular, irresponsible</li>
      <li><strong>il-:</strong> illegal, illogical</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Prefixes that express time or order</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>pre-:</strong> before — preview, prepay</li>
      <li><strong>re-:</strong> again — rewrite, reread</li>
      <li><strong>post-:</strong> after — postgraduate, postwar</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Prefixes that express number or amount</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>bi-:</strong> two — bicycle, bilingual</li>
      <li><strong>tri-:</strong> three — triangle, tricycle</li>
      <li><strong>multi-:</strong> many — multicultural, multimedia</li>
      <li><strong>semi-:</strong> half or partly — semicircle, semiconscious</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Prefixes that express relationships or positions</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>sub-:</strong> under or below — submarine, substandard</li>
      <li><strong>inter-:</strong> between or among — international, interact</li>
      <li><strong>trans-:</strong> across or beyond — transport, transfer</li>
      <li><strong>mis-:</strong> wrongly or badly — misunderstand, misplace</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Do not attach prefixes randomly</h3>
    <p>Although some prefixes have predictable meanings, not every prefix can be added to every word. Use established words and check spelling when necessary.</p>
    <p>Example: <em>possible → impossible</em>, not <em>unpossible</em>.</p>

    <h3>Work out an unfamiliar word</h3>
    <p>Identify the prefix, determine its likely meaning, examine the root word, and then check whether the combined meaning fits the sentence.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Prefixes', 15),
  (1867, 1867, '
    <h2>Suffixes</h2>
    <p>A suffix is a group of letters added to the end of a word. It can change the word''s meaning or grammatical class.</p>

    <h3>Suffixes that form nouns</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>-ness:</strong> kind → kindness; dark → darkness.</li>
      <li><strong>-ment:</strong> develop → development; improve → improvement.</li>
      <li><strong>-tion / -sion:</strong> educate → education; decide → decision.</li>
      <li><strong>-er / -or:</strong> teach → teacher; act → actor.</li>
      <li><strong>-ity:</strong> active → activity; possible → possibility.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Suffixes that form adjectives</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>-ful:</strong> care → careful; hope → hopeful.</li>
      <li><strong>-less:</strong> care → careless; hope → hopeless.</li>
      <li><strong>-able:</strong> enjoy → enjoyable; read → readable.</li>
      <li><strong>-ous:</strong> danger → dangerous; fame → famous.</li>
      <li><strong>-ive:</strong> attract → attractive; act → active.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Suffixes that form adverbs</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>-ly:</strong> careful → carefully; quick → quickly.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Suffixes that form verbs</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>-ise / -ize:</strong> modern → modernise; organise → organize.</li>
      <li><strong>-en:</strong> strength → strengthen; short → shorten.</li>
      <li><strong>-ify:</strong> simple → simplify; pure → purify.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Spelling changes</h3>
    <p>Adding a suffix sometimes changes the spelling of the base word.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>happy → happiness: change <em>y</em> to <em>i</em>.</li>
      <li>make → making: usually drop the final silent <em>e</em> before <em>-ing</em>.</li>
      <li>run → running: double the final consonant in this short-vowel pattern.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Use the sentence to choose the form</h3>
    <p>Choose a suffix according to the grammatical role needed. A noun may name an idea, an adjective may describe it, and an adverb may explain how an action happens.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Suffixes', 15),
  (1868, 1868, '
    <h2>Roots and Word Families</h2>
    <p>A root is the central part of a word that carries its core meaning. A word family contains related words formed from the same base or root.</p>

    <h3>Example: act</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Act:</strong> to do something.</li>
      <li><strong>Action:</strong> something done.</li>
      <li><strong>Actor:</strong> a person who performs a role.</li>
      <li><strong>Active:</strong> doing things or being energetic.</li>
      <li><strong>Activity:</strong> an action or task.</li>
      <li><strong>Activate:</strong> make something active or operational.</li>
      <li><strong>Actively:</strong> in an active manner.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Example: educate</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Educate:</strong> teach or develop knowledge.</li>
      <li><strong>Education:</strong> the process of learning and teaching.</li>
      <li><strong>Educator:</strong> a person who teaches.</li>
      <li><strong>Educational:</strong> relating to education.</li>
      <li><strong>Educated:</strong> having received education.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Example: decide</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Decide:</strong> choose after consideration.</li>
      <li><strong>Decision:</strong> a choice made.</li>
      <li><strong>Decisive:</strong> able to make decisions firmly, or producing a clear result.</li>
      <li><strong>Indecisive:</strong> unable or unwilling to decide readily.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Recognise the grammatical class</h3>
    <p>Words in a family can share a core meaning but serve different grammatical functions.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Noun: The <em>decision</em> was difficult.</li>
      <li>Verb: They must <em>decide</em> today.</li>
      <li>Adjective: She was <em>decisive</em>.</li>
      <li>Adverb: She acted <em>decisively</em>.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Do not assume every similar-looking word belongs to the same family</h3>
    <p>Check the meaning and spelling. A shared sequence of letters alone does not prove that two words have the same root or a related meaning.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Roots and Word Families', 15),
  (1869, 1869, '
    <h2>Compound Words</h2>
    <p>A compound word is formed when two or more words combine to create a word or expression with a particular meaning.</p>

    <h3>Closed compounds</h3>
    <p>These are written as one word.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>classroom</li>
      <li>football</li>
      <li>sunlight</li>
      <li>toothbrush</li>
      <li>notebook</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Open compounds</h3>
    <p>These are written as separate words but function together as one expression.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>post office</li>
      <li>swimming pool</li>
      <li>bus stop</li>
      <li>school uniform</li>
      <li>living room</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Hyphenated compounds</h3>
    <p>These use a hyphen between their parts.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>well-known</li>
      <li>mother-in-law</li>
      <li>check-in</li>
      <li>part-time</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Meaning of a compound</h3>
    <p>The meaning may be predictable from its parts, or it may be more specialised.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Toothbrush:</strong> a brush used to clean teeth.</li>
      <li><strong>Bus stop:</strong> a place where buses stop for passengers.</li>
      <li><strong>Deadline:</strong> the final time or date for completing something; its meaning is not simply the literal meanings of ''dead'' and ''line''.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Compound nouns and compound modifiers</h3>
    <p>Some compound modifiers use a hyphen before a noun to avoid confusion.</p>
    <p>Example: She submitted a <em>well-written report</em>.</p>
    <p>However, spelling conventions vary by word and context. Use a reliable dictionary when unsure rather than adding or removing hyphens at random.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Compound Words', 15),
  (1870, 1870, '
    <h2>Noun Forms</h2>
    <p>Nouns name people, places, things, qualities, states and ideas. Many nouns are formed from verbs or adjectives, and the correct form depends on the sentence.</p>

    <h3>Nouns formed from verbs</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>inform → information</li>
      <li>educate → education</li>
      <li>arrive → arrival</li>
      <li>perform → performance</li>
      <li>decide → decision</li>
      <li>fail → failure</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Nouns formed from adjectives</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>kind → kindness</li>
      <li>dark → darkness</li>
      <li>able → ability</li>
      <li>accurate → accuracy</li>
      <li>different → difference</li>
      <li>strong → strength</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Use the noun form in the correct position</h3>
    <p>Nouns commonly appear after determiners such as <em>a</em>, <em>an</em>, <em>the</em>, and possessives such as <em>her</em> or <em>their</em>. They can also follow adjectives.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>The learner gave an accurate <strong>answer</strong>.</li>
      <li>Her <strong>performance</strong> improved.</li>
      <li>We appreciated their <strong>kindness</strong>.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Irregular forms</h3>
    <p>Some noun forms cannot be predicted reliably from a single suffix.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>choose → choice</li>
      <li>grow → growth</li>
      <li>know → knowledge</li>
      <li>strong → strength</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Check the whole sentence</h3>
    <p>Do not choose a noun simply because the question asks for a word form. Check whether the sentence requires a noun and whether the chosen form has the intended meaning.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Noun Forms', 15),
  (1871, 1871, '
    <h2>Verb Forms</h2>
    <p>Verbs express actions, events and states. English uses different verb forms to express tense, agreement, aspect and grammatical patterns.</p>

    <h3>Common forms</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Base form:</strong> write, speak, learn.</li>
      <li><strong>Third-person singular present:</strong> writes, speaks, learns.</li>
      <li><strong>Past simple:</strong> wrote, spoke, learned or learnt.</li>
      <li><strong>Past participle:</strong> written, spoken, learned or learnt.</li>
      <li><strong>-ing form:</strong> writing, speaking, learning.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Regular verbs</h3>
    <p>Regular verbs usually form the past simple and past participle by adding <em>-ed</em>, with spelling adjustments where necessary.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>work → worked → worked</li>
      <li>study → studied → studied</li>
      <li>stop → stopped → stopped</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Irregular verbs</h3>
    <p>Irregular verbs do not follow the regular <em>-ed</em> pattern consistently.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>go → went → gone</li>
      <li>see → saw → seen</li>
      <li>write → wrote → written</li>
      <li>take → took → taken</li>
      <li>choose → chose → chosen</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Choose the form required by the structure</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>After <em>did</em>, use the base form: Did she <strong>write</strong> the letter?</li>
      <li>After <em>has</em> or <em>have</em> in the present perfect, use the past participle: She has <strong>written</strong> the letter.</li>
      <li>After <em>is</em> in the present continuous, use the -ing form: She is <strong>writing</strong> the letter.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Common error</h3>
    <p>Do not use the past form after <em>did</em>.</p>
    <p>Incorrect: Did he went home?</p>
    <p>Correct: Did he go home?</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Verb Forms', 15),
  (1872, 1872, '
    <h2>Adjective Forms</h2>
    <p>Adjectives describe nouns and pronouns. Many adjective forms are related to nouns or verbs, and the correct form depends on the meaning and grammatical position.</p>

    <h3>Adjectives formed from nouns</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>danger → dangerous</li>
      <li>care → careful or careless</li>
      <li>beauty → beautiful</li>
      <li>hope → hopeful or hopeless</li>
      <li>culture → cultural</li>
      <li>music → musical</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Adjectives formed from verbs</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>interest → interesting or interested</li>
      <li>confuse → confusing or confused</li>
      <li>bore → boring or bored</li>
      <li>tire → tiring or tired</li>
      <li>excite → exciting or excited</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>-ed and -ing adjectives</h3>
    <p>Adjectives ending in <em>-ed</em> often describe how a person feels. Adjectives ending in <em>-ing</em> often describe what causes that feeling.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>The lesson was <strong>interesting</strong>. It caused interest.</li>
      <li>The learners were <strong>interested</strong>. They felt interest.</li>
      <li>The instructions were <strong>confusing</strong>. They caused confusion.</li>
      <li>The learner was <strong>confused</strong>. The learner experienced confusion.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Use the adjective where it belongs</h3>
    <p>An adjective can appear before a noun or after a linking verb.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>A <strong>dangerous</strong> road.</li>
      <li>The road is <strong>dangerous</strong>.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Do not confuse adjectives with adverbs</h3>
    <p>Use an adjective to describe a noun; use an adverb when describing how an action happens.</p>
    <p>Correct: She gave a <strong>clear</strong> explanation.</p>
    <p>Correct: She explained the method <strong>clearly</strong>.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Adjective Forms', 15),
  (1873, 1873, '
    <h2>Adverb Forms</h2>
    <p>Adverbs can modify verbs, adjectives, other adverbs or entire clauses. Many are formed by adding <em>-ly</em> to an adjective, but not all adverbs follow this pattern.</p>

    <h3>Adverbs formed with -ly</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>quick → quickly</li>
      <li>careful → carefully</li>
      <li>easy → easily</li>
      <li>happy → happily</li>
      <li>gentle → gently</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Irregular or unchanged forms</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Good → well:</strong> She sings well.</li>
      <li><strong>Fast → fast:</strong> The vehicle moved fast.</li>
      <li><strong>Hard → hard:</strong> They worked hard.</li>
      <li><strong>Late → late:</strong> The bus arrived late.</li>
      <li><strong>Early → early:</strong> She arrived early.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Beware of words ending in -ly that are adjectives</h3>
    <p>Not every word ending in <em>-ly</em> is an adverb.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Friendly:</strong> a friendly neighbour.</li>
      <li><strong>Lovely:</strong> a lovely day.</li>
      <li><strong>Lonely:</strong> a lonely traveller.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Adjective or adverb?</h3>
    <p>Use an adjective to describe a noun and an adverb to describe an action or another modifier.</p>
    <p>Adjective: The learner gave a <strong>careful</strong> answer.</p>
    <p>Adverb: The learner answered <strong>carefully</strong>.</p>

    <h3>Meaning can change</h3>
    <p><em>Hard</em> means with great effort, while <em>hardly</em> means almost not.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>She worked <strong>hard</strong>. (She made a great effort.)</li>
      <li>She <strong>hardly</strong> worked. (She did almost no work.)</li>
    </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Adverb Forms', 15),
  (1874, 1874, '
    <h2>Collocations</h2>
    <p>Collocations are combinations of words that commonly occur together. They sound natural to fluent speakers because the words conventionally fit together.</p>

    <h3>Verb + noun</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>make a decision</li>
      <li>do homework</li>
      <li>take a photograph</li>
      <li>pay attention</li>
      <li>keep a promise</li>
      <li>break a record</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Adjective + noun</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>heavy rain</li>
      <li>strong wind</li>
      <li>deep sleep</li>
      <li>high expectations</li>
      <li>valuable experience</li>
      <li>serious problem</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Adverb + adjective</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>highly effective</li>
      <li>deeply concerned</li>
      <li>perfectly clear</li>
      <li>completely different</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Verb + preposition</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>depend on</li>
      <li>believe in</li>
      <li>apologise for</li>
      <li>listen to</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Why collocations matter</h3>
    <p>A sentence can be grammatically understandable but still sound unnatural if the word combination is unusual.</p>
    <p>Less natural: <em>do a decision</em>.</p>
    <p>Natural: <em>make a decision</em>.</p>
    <p>Less natural: <em>powerful rain</em>.</p>
    <p>Natural: <em>heavy rain</em>.</p>

    <h3>Learn expressions in context</h3>
    <p>Do not memorise a collocation as an isolated pair only. Read it in a sentence and learn the situation in which it is normally used.</p>
    <p>Example: The committee will <strong>make a decision</strong> after reviewing the evidence.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Collocations', 15),
  (1875, 1875, '
    <h2>Phrasal Verbs in Context</h2>
    <p>A phrasal verb combines a verb with one or more particles, usually an adverb or preposition. The complete expression often has a meaning that cannot be predicted from the verb alone.</p>

    <h3>Common phrasal verbs</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Give up:</strong> stop trying or stop doing something — He refused to give up.</li>
      <li><strong>Find out:</strong> discover information — We need to find out the examination date.</li>
      <li><strong>Carry on:</strong> continue — Carry on with your revision.</li>
      <li><strong>Put off:</strong> postpone — They put off the meeting until Friday.</li>
      <li><strong>Look after:</strong> take care of — She looks after her younger brother.</li>
      <li><strong>Run out of:</strong> have none left — We ran out of paper.</li>
      <li><strong>Set up:</strong> establish or arrange — The school set up a science club.</li>
      <li><strong>Turn down:</strong> reject or reduce — He turned down the offer.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Separable and inseparable phrasal verbs</h3>
    <p>Some phrasal verbs allow an object between the verb and particle.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>She turned <strong>down the offer</strong>.</li>
      <li>She turned <strong>the offer down</strong>.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>With many separable phrasal verbs, a pronoun object must go between the verb and particle.</p>
    <p>Correct: She turned <strong>it down</strong>.</p>
    <p>Incorrect: She turned down it.</p>

    <p>Other phrasal verbs are inseparable.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Correct: He looks after his brother.</li>
      <li>Incorrect: He looks his brother after.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Use context to find the meaning</h3>
    <p>The surrounding sentence often reveals which meaning is intended. For example, <em>turn down</em> can mean reject an offer or reduce a setting such as volume.</p>
    <p>Example: Please turn down the radio; I cannot concentrate.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Phrasal Verbs in Context', 15),
  (1876, 1876, '
    <h2>Idioms and Expressions</h2>
    <p>An idiom is an established expression whose overall meaning cannot always be understood by interpreting each word literally.</p>

    <h3>Common idioms</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Break the ice:</strong> make people feel more comfortable at the beginning of an interaction.</li>
      <li><strong>Once in a blue moon:</strong> very rarely.</li>
      <li><strong>Hit the nail on the head:</strong> identify the exact point or give the correct explanation.</li>
      <li><strong>Under the weather:</strong> feeling unwell.</li>
      <li><strong>A piece of cake:</strong> something very easy to do.</li>
      <li><strong>Spill the beans:</strong> reveal information, often a secret.</li>
      <li><strong>Cost an arm and a leg:</strong> be very expensive.</li>
      <li><strong>Burn the midnight oil:</strong> work or study late into the night.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Interpret an idiom in context</h3>
    <p>The surrounding situation helps you infer the intended meaning.</p>
    <p>Example: The new student was nervous, so the class monitor told a joke to <em>break the ice</em>.</p>
    <p>Here, the expression means making the situation less awkward, not physically breaking ice.</p>

    <h3>Idioms and tone</h3>
    <p>Idioms can make conversation lively, but too many idioms may make formal writing unclear. Choose expressions suitable for the audience and purpose.</p>

    <h3>Do not interpret every expression literally</h3>
    <p>When the literal meaning does not make sense in context, consider whether the phrase has an established figurative meaning.</p>
    <p>Example: If someone says, ''The test was a piece of cake,'' they mean it was easy, not that it was food.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Idioms and Expressions', 15),
  (1877, 1877, '
    <h2>Similar Spellings</h2>
    <p>Some English words look or sound similar but have different meanings. To choose correctly, examine the meaning required by the sentence.</p>

    <h3>Commonly confused words</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Accept:</strong> receive or agree to something. Example: She accepted the invitation.</li>
      <li><strong>Except:</strong> excluding. Example: Everyone attended except Brian.</li>
      <li><strong>Affect:</strong> usually a verb meaning to influence. Example: Lack of sleep can affect concentration.</li>
      <li><strong>Effect:</strong> usually a noun meaning a result. Example: Lack of sleep had an effect on concentration.</li>
      <li><strong>Advice:</strong> a noun meaning a recommendation. Example: The teacher gave useful advice.</li>
      <li><strong>Advise:</strong> a verb meaning to recommend. Example: The teacher advised us to revise.</li>
      <li><strong>Principal:</strong> the head of a school, or most important. Example: The principal addressed the learners.</li>
      <li><strong>Principle:</strong> a rule, belief or fundamental idea. Example: Honesty is an important principle.</li>
      <li><strong>Stationary:</strong> not moving. Example: The vehicle remained stationary.</li>
      <li><strong>Stationery:</strong> writing materials. Example: We bought stationery for school.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>A reliable method</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Read the entire sentence.</li>
      <li>Identify the meaning the sentence needs.</li>
      <li>Check the grammatical role, such as noun or verb.</li>
      <li>Choose the spelling that matches both the meaning and the grammar.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Compare the words in context</h3>
    <p>Incorrect: The teacher gave me a good advise.</p>
    <p>Correct: The teacher gave me good advice.</p>
    <p>Correct: The teacher advised me to practise.</p>

    <p>Remember: Similar spelling does not mean identical meaning. The surrounding words provide clues, but you must still know what each word means.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Similar Spellings', 15),
  (1878, 1878, '
    <h2>Similar Meanings</h2>
    <p>Some words have similar meanings but are not interchangeable in every sentence. The correct choice depends on context, intensity, grammatical patterns and tone.</p>

    <h3>Words with related meanings</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Big / large:</strong> both describe considerable size. We commonly say ''a big mistake'' and ''a large building''.</li>
      <li><strong>Begin / commence:</strong> both mean start, but ''commence'' is generally more formal.</li>
      <li><strong>Ask / enquire:</strong> both can involve seeking information; ''enquire'' often sounds more formal.</li>
      <li><strong>Help / assist:</strong> both mean provide support; ''assist'' is often more formal.</li>
      <li><strong>Childish / childlike:</strong> ''childish'' often suggests immaturity; ''childlike'' often suggests positive qualities such as innocence.</li>
      <li><strong>Thin / slim:</strong> both refer to a small body width, but ''slim'' often sounds more positive when describing a person.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Intensity matters</h3>
    <p>Words with related meanings may differ in strength.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Annoyed:</strong> slightly angry or irritated.</li>
      <li><strong>Angry:</strong> feeling strong displeasure.</li>
      <li><strong>Furious:</strong> extremely angry.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Choose according to context</h3>
    <p>In a formal report, ''The committee commenced the meeting at 9 a.m.'' may suit the tone better than ''The committee kicked off the meeting at 9 a.m.''</p>
    <p>In everyday conversation, ''Let''s begin'' may sound more natural than ''Let us commence'' in some situations.</p>

    <h3>How to choose precisely</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Identify the basic meaning required.</li>
      <li>Check the intensity or emotional tone.</li>
      <li>Consider the audience and formality.</li>
      <li>Check which words naturally occur together.</li>
    </ol>

    <p>A synonym is not automatically a perfect substitute. Choose the word that communicates the intended meaning most accurately.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Similar Meanings', 15),
  (1879, 1879, '
    <h2>Contextual Word Meaning</h2>
    <p>A word''s meaning in a passage is determined by how it is used in that particular situation. This is called contextual meaning.</p>

    <h3>Use clues around the word</h3>
    <p>Consider the word <strong>reluctant</strong>:</p>
    <p>''Although the learners were reluctant to speak at first, they gradually gained confidence and began sharing their ideas.''</p>
    <p>The contrast between being reluctant and later speaking confidently suggests that ''reluctant'' means unwilling or hesitant.</p>

    <h3>Types of context clues</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Definition:</strong> The writer explains the word directly. ''An arid region, meaning an extremely dry area, receives little rainfall.''</li>
      <li><strong>Example:</strong> Examples reveal the category. ''Nocturnal animals, such as owls and bats, are active at night.''</li>
      <li><strong>Contrast:</strong> Opposing information helps reveal meaning. ''Unlike his talkative brother, Kamau was reserved and spoke very little.''</li>
      <li><strong>Cause and effect:</strong> The result helps reveal the meaning. ''The path was slippery, so the hiker proceeded cautiously.''</li>
      <li><strong>Restatement:</strong> The writer expresses a similar idea using different words.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>A step-by-step method</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Read the whole sentence and, if necessary, the sentences around it.</li>
      <li>Identify clues such as examples, contrasts, definitions or consequences.</li>
      <li>Suggest a meaning that fits the context.</li>
      <li>Replace the unfamiliar word with your suggested meaning.</li>
      <li>Check that the sentence still makes sense.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Do not rely on one clue alone</h3>
    <p>A familiar word may have an unfamiliar meaning in a particular passage. Use the evidence in the passage rather than automatically choosing its most common meaning.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Contextual Word Meaning', 15),
  (1880, 1880, '
    <h2>Multiple Word Meanings</h2>
    <p>Many English words have more than one meaning. The surrounding words and situation determine which meaning is intended.</p>

    <h3>Examples of words with several meanings</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Bank:</strong> a financial institution — ''She deposited money at the bank.'' It can also mean land beside a river — ''They sat on the river bank.''</li>
      <li><strong>Bright:</strong> giving out much light — ''a bright lamp''; intelligent — ''a bright learner''; vivid — ''a bright colour''.</li>
      <li><strong>Run:</strong> move quickly on foot — ''He can run fast''; manage — ''She runs a business''; flow — ''The tap began to run''.</li>
      <li><strong>Head:</strong> a body part — ''He nodded his head''; a leader — ''the head of the school''; the top or front — ''the head of the table''.</li>
      <li><strong>Match:</strong> a contest — ''a football match''; something that corresponds — ''The colours match''; a small stick used to produce a flame — ''She lit a match''.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Use grammar and context together</h3>
    <p>The grammatical role may help, but it does not always settle the meaning.</p>
    <p>''They run every morning'' uses <em>run</em> to describe movement.</p>
    <p>''They run a small shop'' uses <em>run</em> to mean manage.</p>

    <h3>Distinguish literal meaning from the intended sense</h3>
    <p>In ''The news spread quickly'', the news did not physically move like a person. The verb ''spread'' means that information reached more people.</p>

    <h3>How to identify the correct meaning</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Read the complete sentence.</li>
      <li>Look at the subject, verb, object and surrounding details.</li>
      <li>List the possible meanings of the word.</li>
      <li>Select the meaning that fits the situation.</li>
    </ol>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Multiple Word Meanings', 15),
  (1881, 1881, '
    <h2>Literal Meaning</h2>
    <p>Literal meaning is the direct, ordinary meaning of words, interpreted without treating them as a figure of speech.</p>

    <h3>Examples</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>''The glass broke when it fell.'' The glass physically broke.</li>
      <li>''The child opened the door.'' The child physically opened a door.</li>
      <li>''The water is cold.'' The water has a low temperature.</li>
      <li>''The farmer planted maize.'' The farmer put maize seeds into the soil.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Literal and non-literal statements</h3>
    <p>Compare these sentences:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Literal:</strong> ''The boy ran out of the classroom.'' He physically ran outside.</li>
      <li><strong>Non-literal:</strong> ''The boy ran out of patience.'' He became unable to tolerate the situation any longer.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>How to recognise literal meaning</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Consider the ordinary meaning of each word.</li>
      <li>Check whether the statement describes something that can reasonably happen as stated.</li>
      <li>Look for clues that indicate exaggeration, comparison or another figure of speech.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Literal does not mean unimportant</h3>
    <p>Literal language can communicate precise information, instructions and facts. For example, ''Keep the medicine out of children''s reach'' is a direct instruction.</p>
    <p>Do not assume a sentence is figurative merely because it sounds descriptive. Interpret it according to its actual context.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Literal Meaning', 15),
  (1882, 1882, '
    <h2>Figurative Meaning</h2>
    <p>Figurative language uses words in a non-literal way to communicate an image, feeling, comparison or idea more vividly.</p>

    <h3>Examples</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>''The classroom was a zoo.''</strong> The classroom was noisy or disorderly, not an actual zoo.</li>
      <li><strong>''She has a heart of stone.''</strong> She seems unkind or emotionally unresponsive.</li>
      <li><strong>''Time flew.''</strong> Time seemed to pass quickly.</li>
      <li><strong>''He is drowning in work.''</strong> He has an overwhelming amount of work.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Why writers use figurative language</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>To create vivid mental pictures.</li>
      <li>To communicate emotions strongly.</li>
      <li>To make descriptions memorable.</li>
      <li>To express complex experiences in a compact way.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Interpret the expression in context</h3>
    <p>Consider: ''When the examination timetable was announced, the learners'' excitement exploded.''</p>
    <p>Their excitement did not literally explode. The expression emphasises how suddenly and strongly they reacted.</p>

    <h3>Explain both meaning and effect</h3>
    <p>When asked to explain figurative language, do more than name the expression.</p>
    <ol>
      <li>State the intended meaning.</li>
      <li>Identify the words that create the image.</li>
      <li>Explain the effect on the reader or listener.</li>
    </ol>

    <p>Example: ''The news spread like wildfire'' means the news travelled very quickly. The comparison suggests speed and rapid spread.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Figurative Meaning', 15),
  (1883, 1883, '
    <h2>Denotation</h2>
    <p>Denotation is the direct or dictionary meaning of a word, rather than the feelings or associations it may create.</p>

    <h3>Examples</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Home:</strong> the place where someone lives.</li>
      <li><strong>Snake:</strong> a long, legless reptile.</li>
      <li><strong>Child:</strong> a young human being.</li>
      <li><strong>Rose:</strong> a flowering plant or its flower.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Denotation and connotation</h3>
    <p>Two words can refer to similar things while creating different feelings.</p>
    <p><strong>Home</strong> and <strong>house</strong> can refer to a place where someone lives. The denotation of ''house'' usually focuses on the building, while ''home'' often emphasises the place where a person lives and belongs.</p>

    <p><strong>Snake</strong> denotes a type of reptile. In some contexts, however, calling a person a snake suggests that the person is deceitful. That negative association is connotative, not the literal denotation.</p>

    <h3>Why denotation matters</h3>
    <p>Precise denotations are especially important in definitions, scientific explanations, formal instructions and factual reports.</p>

    <h3>How to identify denotation</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Identify the word being used.</li>
      <li>State its direct meaning.</li>
      <li>Separate the direct meaning from the feelings or cultural associations attached to it.</li>
    </ol>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Denotation', 15),
  (1884, 1884, '
    <h2>Connotation</h2>
    <p>Connotation refers to the feelings, associations or attitudes that a word suggests beyond its direct meaning.</p>

    <h3>Positive, negative and neutral associations</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Confident:</strong> generally positive; suggests belief in one''s ability.</li>
      <li><strong>Arrogant:</strong> generally negative; suggests an exaggerated sense of one''s importance.</li>
      <li><strong>Determined:</strong> often positive; suggests persistence towards a goal.</li>
      <li><strong>Stubborn:</strong> often negative; suggests unwillingness to change one''s position.</li>
      <li><strong>Economical:</strong> often positive; suggests avoiding waste.</li>
      <li><strong>Stingy:</strong> negative; suggests unwillingness to spend or share.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Words with similar denotations can create different impressions</h3>
    <p>Consider the words <strong>slim</strong> and <strong>skinny</strong>. Both can describe a person with little body fat, but ''slim'' may sound complimentary while ''skinny'' can sound critical.</p>

    <p>Compare <strong>youthful</strong> and <strong>childish</strong>. ''Youthful'' often suggests energy or freshness; ''childish'' often criticises immature behaviour.</p>

    <h3>Writers choose words for their effect</h3>
    <p>In a persuasive speech, a speaker might call a plan ''bold'' to suggest courage, or ''reckless'' to suggest dangerous carelessness. The words influence how the audience evaluates the same action.</p>

    <h3>How to explain connotation in a passage</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Identify the word.</li>
      <li>State its direct meaning if needed.</li>
      <li>Describe the feeling or association it creates in context.</li>
      <li>Explain how that association affects the reader''s view.</li>
    </ol>

    <p>Connotations can vary across cultures and situations. Use the passage as your main evidence rather than assuming every reader will react identically.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Connotation', 15),
  (1885, 1885, '
    <h2>Similes</h2>
    <p>A simile is a figure of speech that compares two different things using words such as <em>like</em> or <em>as</em>.</p>

    <h3>Examples</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>As busy as a bee:</strong> very busy.</li>
      <li><strong>As light as a feather:</strong> very light.</li>
      <li><strong>He ran like the wind:</strong> he ran very quickly.</li>
      <li><strong>Her voice was like music:</strong> her voice was pleasant or harmonious.</li>
      <li><strong>The child clung to his mother like a shadow:</strong> the child stayed very close to her.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>How a simile works</h3>
    <p>A simile connects two things by highlighting a shared quality.</p>
    <p>In ''The classroom was as quiet as a library'', the comparison emphasises silence.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Subject:</strong> the classroom.</li>
      <li><strong>Comparison:</strong> a library.</li>
      <li><strong>Shared quality:</strong> quietness.</li>
      <li><strong>Effect:</strong> helps the reader imagine the degree of silence.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Identify the quality being compared</h3>
    <p>Do not stop at identifying the two things. Explain the shared quality and the effect of the comparison.</p>
    <p>Example: ''The road twisted like a snake.'' The road is compared with a snake to emphasise its winding shape.</p>

    <h3>Simile or literal comparison?</h3>
    <p>The word ''like'' does not automatically make a sentence a simile. ''She looks like her mother'' may simply state a real resemblance. A figurative simile usually creates an illustrative comparison to emphasise a quality.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Similes', 15),
  (1886, 1886, '
    <h2>Metaphors</h2>
    <p>A metaphor describes one thing as another to suggest a shared quality. Unlike a typical simile, it does not explicitly use ''like'' or ''as'' to make the comparison.</p>

    <h3>Examples</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>''Time is money.''</strong> Time is valuable and should not be wasted.</li>
      <li><strong>''She is the backbone of the team.''</strong> She provides essential support.</li>
      <li><strong>''The classroom was a furnace.''</strong> The classroom was extremely hot.</li>
      <li><strong>''His words were a knife.''</strong> His words were deeply hurtful.</li>
      <li><strong>''Amina is a shining star in mathematics.''</strong> Amina performs exceptionally well or stands out.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>How to interpret a metaphor</h3>
    <p>Consider: ''The rumour was a seed that grew into a crisis.''</p>
    <ol>
      <li>Identify the comparison: a rumour is described as a seed.</li>
      <li>Find the shared idea: a small beginning can develop into something much larger.</li>
      <li>Interpret the meaning: the rumour gradually caused a serious problem.</li>
      <li>Explain the effect: the image emphasises how a small event can grow into a major issue.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Metaphor and simile</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Simile:</strong> Her voice was like music.</li>
      <li><strong>Metaphor:</strong> Her voice was music.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Do not interpret a metaphor literally</h3>
    <p>When a person is called ''the backbone of a team'', the statement usually does not refer to a physical bone. It describes the person''s essential role.</p>

    <h3>Answering examination questions</h3>
    <p>When asked to explain a metaphor, state what it suggests in the passage and identify the quality or idea being emphasised. Naming it as a metaphor alone is not a complete explanation.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Metaphors', 15),
  (1887, 1887, '
    <h2>Personification</h2>
    <p>Personification gives human qualities, feelings or actions to animals, objects, ideas or forces of nature.</p>

    <h3>Examples</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>"The wind whispered through the trees."</strong> The wind is described as whispering, a human action, to suggest a soft sound.</li>
      <li><strong>"The sun smiled down on the village."</strong> The sun is given the human action of smiling, creating a warm and pleasant impression.</li>
      <li><strong>"Opportunity knocked at his door."</strong> Opportunity is presented as a person who can knock, suggesting that a chance became available.</li>
      <li><strong>"The old house groaned in the storm."</strong> The house is given a human-like action to suggest creaking sounds.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>How to explain personification</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Identify the non-human subject.</li>
      <li>Identify the human quality or action given to it.</li>
      <li>Explain the idea or image created in context.</li>
    </ol>

    <p>For example, in "The angry clouds marched across the sky", clouds are described as angry and marching. This makes the approaching storm seem powerful and threatening.</p>

    <h3>Do not confuse it with ordinary description</h3>
    <p>"The wind blew strongly" describes a natural action. "The wind angrily attacked the village" gives the wind human emotion and a human-like action.</p>

    <p>Personification helps readers imagine scenes, feel the atmosphere and connect with descriptions.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Personification', 15),
  (1888, 1888, '
    <h2>Hyperbole</h2>
    <p>Hyperbole is deliberate exaggeration used for emphasis, humour or emotional effect. It is not normally intended to be understood as an exact factual statement.</p>

    <h3>Examples</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>"I have told you a thousand times."</strong> The speaker exaggerates the number of times to express frustration.</li>
      <li><strong>"This bag weighs a tonne."</strong> The speaker exaggerates the weight to show that the bag feels very heavy.</li>
      <li><strong>"We waited forever."</strong> The expression emphasises a long or frustrating wait.</li>
      <li><strong>"She cried a river of tears."</strong> The exaggeration emphasises intense sadness.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Recognising hyperbole</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Identify the unusually extreme statement.</li>
      <li>Consider whether it is intended literally.</li>
      <li>Determine the real feeling or situation being emphasised.</li>
      <li>Explain the effect on the reader or listener.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Hyperbole and ordinary emphasis</h3>
    <p>"The room was very hot" may be a literal description. "The room was hot enough to melt the shoes off my feet" exaggerates the heat for effect.</p>

    <h3>Why writers use it</h3>
    <p>Hyperbole can express frustration, excitement, fear, humour or the intensity of an experience. In formal factual writing, exaggerated statements should not be mistaken for measured evidence.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Hyperbole', 15),
  (1889, 1889, '
    <h2>Tone and Word Choice</h2>
    <p>Tone is the writer''s or speaker''s attitude towards a subject, audience or situation. Word choice helps communicate that attitude.</p>

    <h3>Common tones</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Optimistic:</strong> expects or emphasises positive possibilities.</li>
      <li><strong>Pessimistic:</strong> expects or emphasises negative outcomes.</li>
      <li><strong>Critical:</strong> expresses disapproval or identifies faults.</li>
      <li><strong>Appreciative:</strong> expresses approval or gratitude.</li>
      <li><strong>Sarcastic:</strong> uses cutting or mocking language, sometimes saying the opposite of what is meant.</li>
      <li><strong>Concerned:</strong> communicates worry or serious attention.</li>
      <li><strong>Humorous:</strong> aims to amuse.</li>
      <li><strong>Formal:</strong> uses language suited to official or professional situations.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Word choice reveals attitude</h3>
    <p>Compare these descriptions of the same decision:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>"The committee made a <strong>courageous</strong> decision." The word suggests approval and bravery.</li>
      <li>"The committee made a <strong>reckless</strong> decision." The word suggests criticism and lack of care.</li>
      <li>"The committee made a <strong>controversial</strong> decision." The word suggests disagreement or debate without necessarily expressing approval or disapproval.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>How to identify tone</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Read enough of the passage to understand the situation.</li>
      <li>Identify words that express feelings, judgement or attitude.</li>
      <li>Consider whether the writer praises, criticises, worries, mocks or simply informs.</li>
      <li>Support your answer with a relevant word or phrase from the passage.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Answer with evidence</h3>
    <p>If asked for the tone, name it and explain the evidence. For example: "The tone is critical because the writer calls the decision ''careless'' and describes its consequences as ''avoidable''."</p>

    <p>Do not identify tone from a single word without checking the surrounding passage. Context may change how a word is intended.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Tone and Word Choice', 15),
  (1890, 1890, '
    <h2>Ambiguity and Double Meaning</h2>
    <p>A statement is ambiguous when it can reasonably be understood in more than one way. The uncertainty may arise from word meaning, sentence structure or missing context.</p>

    <h3>Ambiguity caused by word meaning</h3>
    <p>"I went to the bank."</p>
    <p>Without further context, ''bank'' could refer to a financial institution or the land beside a river.</p>

    <h3>Ambiguity caused by sentence structure</h3>
    <p>"I saw the man with the telescope."</p>
    <p>This could mean that I used a telescope to see the man, or that I saw a man who had a telescope.</p>

    <h3>Ambiguity caused by unclear reference</h3>
    <p>"Mary told Jane that she had won."</p>
    <p>The pronoun ''she'' could refer to Mary or Jane unless the surrounding context clarifies who won.</p>

    <h3>Double meaning and wordplay</h3>
    <p>Some writers deliberately create two meanings to produce humour or a clever effect. For example, a headline may use a word that refers both to a literal event and to a familiar expression.</p>

    <h3>How to resolve ambiguity</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Identify the word, phrase or sentence that allows more than one interpretation.</li>
      <li>State the possible meanings clearly.</li>
      <li>Use surrounding context to decide whether one interpretation is intended.</li>
      <li>If both meanings remain possible, explain why the statement is ambiguous.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Make writing precise</h3>
    <p>Instead of "I saw the man with the telescope", write "Using my telescope, I saw the man" if the observer used the telescope.</p>
    <p>Clear writing places words in an order that communicates the intended meaning without unnecessary confusion.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Ambiguity and Double Meaning', 15),
  (1891, 1891, '
    <h2>Sentence-Initial Capital Letters</h2>
    <p>In standard written English, the first word of a sentence begins with a capital letter. A new sentence after a full stop, question mark or exclamation mark also begins with a capital letter.</p>

    <h3>Examples</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Incorrect: "the learners entered the classroom."</li>
      <li>Correct: "The learners entered the classroom."</li>
      <li>Incorrect: "Where is the library? it is near the gate."</li>
      <li>Correct: "Where is the library? It is near the gate."</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>After a colon or semicolon</h3>
    <p>A colon or semicolon does not normally require the next word to begin with a capital letter, unless the word independently requires one, such as a proper noun.</p>
    <p>Example: "Bring three items: a pen, a ruler and a notebook."</p>

    <h3>Check every sentence boundary</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Locate full stops, question marks and exclamation marks.</li>
      <li>Check the first word of each sentence that follows.</li>
      <li>Correct lowercase sentence beginnings.</li>
      <li>Keep proper nouns capitalised wherever they occur.</li>
    </ol>

    <p>Capitalisation helps readers recognise sentence boundaries and makes writing easier to follow.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Sentence Initial Capitals', 15),
  (1892, 1892, '
    <h2>Proper Nouns and Names</h2>
    <p>A proper noun names a particular person, place, organisation or other specific entity. In English, proper nouns begin with capital letters.</p>

    <h3>Common nouns and proper nouns</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Common noun:</strong> a country. <strong>Proper noun:</strong> Kenya.</li>
      <li><strong>Common noun:</strong> a school. <strong>Proper noun:</strong> Kenyatta High School, when referring to that specific school.</li>
      <li><strong>Common noun:</strong> a river. <strong>Proper noun:</strong> Tana River.</li>
      <li><strong>Common noun:</strong> a person. <strong>Proper noun:</strong> Amina.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Names of organisations and institutions</h3>
    <p>Capitalise the official names of institutions, companies and organisations.</p>
    <p>Example: "The learners visited the Kenya National Library Service."</p>
    <p>But use lowercase for a general reference: "The learners visited a library."</p>

    <h3>Names within sentences</h3>
    <p>Proper nouns remain capitalised even when they do not begin a sentence.</p>
    <p>Example: "Our class travelled from Machakos to Nairobi."</p>

    <h3>Common errors</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Incorrect: "My cousin lives in kenya."</li>
      <li>Correct: "My cousin lives in Kenya."</li>
      <li>Incorrect: "We visited nairobi on Friday."</li>
      <li>Correct: "We visited Nairobi on Friday."</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Remember: capitalise the specific name, not every noun that refers to a person, place or organisation in general.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Proper Nouns and Names', 15),
  (1893, 1893, '
    <h2>Capitalising Titles, Days, Months and Places</h2>
    <p>English capitalisation rules apply to days of the week, months, geographical names and many titles. These words are not capitalised in the same way in every language, so learners should follow standard English conventions.</p>

    <h3>Days and months</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Correct: Monday, Tuesday, Friday.</li>
      <li>Correct: January, April, December.</li>
      <li>Incorrect: "Our examination begins on monday in october."</li>
      <li>Correct: "Our examination begins on Monday in October."</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Geographical names</h3>
    <p>Capitalise the names of specific countries, counties, cities, rivers, mountains and other named places.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Kenya, Machakos County, Nairobi, Mount Kenya, Lake Victoria.</li>
      <li>Use lowercase for general references such as "a county", "a mountain" or "a lake" when they are not part of a proper name.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Titles of people</h3>
    <p>Capitalize a title when it forms part of a specific name or is used directly as a title before a name, depending on the style followed.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>"President Wangari" would require capitalisation of the title if used as part of a name, though the historical example here is illustrative rather than a real office held by Wangari Maathai.</li>
      <li>"The president addressed the nation." The title is a general reference and is lowercase in this sentence.</li>
      <li>"Professor Otieno spoke to the class." The title before the name is capitalised.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Titles of works</h3>
    <p>Capitalisation of book, film and other work titles depends partly on the style guide. In title case, important words are capitalised; in sentence case, usually only the first word and proper nouns are capitalised. Follow the required style consistently.</p>

    <p>Always distinguish a specific name from a general description.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Titles, Days, Months and Places', 15),
  (1894, 1894, '
    <h2>Common Spelling Patterns</h2>
    <p>English spelling follows useful patterns, although there are exceptions. Recognising common patterns helps learners spell unfamiliar words more accurately.</p>

    <h3>Changing y to i</h3>
    <p>When a word ends in a consonant followed by <strong>y</strong>, the y often changes to i before an ending such as -es or -ed.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>carry → carried</li>
      <li>try → tries</li>
      <li>But when a vowel comes before y, the y usually remains: play → played; enjoy → enjoys.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Adding -ing</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Most verbs add -ing: read → reading.</li>
      <li>Silent final e is usually dropped: make → making; write → writing.</li>
      <li>Some short verbs double the final consonant: run → running; sit → sitting.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Adding -ed</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Most regular verbs add -ed: work → worked.</li>
      <li>Verbs ending in consonant + y often change y to i: carry → carried.</li>
      <li>Some verbs double the final consonant: stop → stopped.</li>
      <li>Irregular verbs may change completely: go → went; write → wrote.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Common letter combinations</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>tion:</strong> education, information, attention.</li>
      <li><strong>ment:</strong> development, agreement, improvement.</li>
      <li><strong>ough:</strong> though, through, thought. This combination has different pronunciations in different words.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Use patterns, then verify</h3>
    <p>Patterns are guides, not guarantees. When a spelling looks unfamiliar, consider the word family, check the rule and learn irregular forms separately.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Common Spelling Patterns', 15),
  (1895, 1895, '
    <h2>Silent Letters</h2>
    <p>A silent letter is a letter that appears in a word''s spelling but is not normally pronounced in that word.</p>

    <h3>Common examples</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>k:</strong> knife, knee, know. The k is silent at the beginning of these words.</li>
      <li><strong>w:</strong> write, wrong, wrist. The w is silent at the beginning.</li>
      <li><strong>b:</strong> lamb, thumb, debt. The b is silent in these words.</li>
      <li><strong>l:</strong> calm, half, could. The l is silent in these examples for many standard English pronunciations.</li>
      <li><strong>gh:</strong> light, night, though. The gh is not pronounced as a separate consonant in these words.</li>
      <li><strong>t:</strong> listen, castle, often. Pronunciation of ''often'' varies; some speakers pronounce the t.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Why silent letters matter</h3>
    <p>English spelling preserves historical forms and patterns that do not always match modern pronunciation. A learner cannot reliably infer every spelling from sound alone.</p>

    <h3>How to learn them</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Notice the silent letter when learning a new word.</li>
      <li>Say the word naturally and identify which letter is not pronounced.</li>
      <li>Practise spelling the word from memory.</li>
      <li>Use the word in a sentence to strengthen recall.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Do not remove the letter</h3>
    <p>Even though the k is silent in ''knife'', it must still be written. The correct spelling is ''knife'', not ''nife''.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Silent Letters', 15),
  (1896, 1896, '
    <h2>Adding Endings to Words</h2>
    <p>When suffixes such as -s, -es, -ed, -ing, -er and -est are added to words, spelling may change. The correct form depends on the ending and the word''s final letters.</p>

    <h3>Words ending in consonant + y</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>baby → babies</li>
      <li>study → studied</li>
      <li>happy → happier</li>
    </ul>
    <p>When a consonant comes before y, y usually changes to i before endings such as -es, -ed and -er.</p>

    <h3>Words ending in vowel + y</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>boy → boys</li>
      <li>play → played</li>
      <li>enjoy → enjoying</li>
    </ul>
    <p>When a vowel comes before y, the y normally remains.</p>

    <h3>Words ending in silent e</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>hope → hoping</li>
      <li>write → writing</li>
      <li>large → larger</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The final silent e is usually dropped before a vowel-initial ending such as -ing. However, some endings retain it: hope → hopeful.</p>

    <h3>Doubling the final consonant</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>stop → stopped; stopping.</li>
      <li>plan → planned; planning.</li>
      <li>big → bigger; biggest.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Some short words double the final consonant before a vowel-initial ending when the word ends in a consonant-vowel-consonant pattern and the stress rules allow it. Not every word ending in these letters doubles its final consonant.</p>

    <h3>Check before writing</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Identify the base word.</li>
      <li>Identify the suffix being added.</li>
      <li>Check the word''s final letters and stress where relevant.</li>
      <li>Apply the appropriate pattern and verify irregular forms.</li>
    </ol>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Adding Endings to Words', 15),
  (1897, 1897, '
    <h2>Commonly Confused Spellings</h2>
    <p>Some English words sound alike or look similar but have different meanings. Choose the correct word by checking the meaning required by the sentence.</p>

    <h3>Common examples</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>their:</strong> belonging to them. Example: The learners collected their books.</li>
      <li><strong>there:</strong> refers to a place or introduces a statement. Example: Put the bag there.</li>
      <li><strong>they''re:</strong> means "they are". Example: They''re preparing for the examination.</li>
      <li><strong>your:</strong> belonging to you. Example: Bring your notebook.</li>
      <li><strong>you''re:</strong> means "you are". Example: You''re responsible for the equipment.</li>
      <li><strong>whose:</strong> asks who something belongs to or introduces possession. Example: Whose pen is this?</li>
      <li><strong>who''s:</strong> means "who is" or "who has". Example: Who''s at the door?</li>
      <li><strong>its:</strong> indicates possession. Example: The school changed its timetable.</li>
      <li><strong>it''s:</strong> means "it is" or "it has". Example: It''s raining.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>A reliable checking method</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Read the whole sentence.</li>
      <li>Identify the meaning needed.</li>
      <li>For contractions, expand the word: "it''s" becomes "it is" or "it has".</li>
      <li>Check that the sentence still makes sense.</li>
    </ol>

    <p>Do not choose a word merely because it sounds right. The sentence''s meaning determines the spelling.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Commonly Confused Spellings', 15),
  (1898, 1898, '
    <h2>Full Stops</h2>
    <p>A full stop (.) marks the end of a complete statement. It tells the reader that the sentence has finished.</p>

    <h3>Correct use</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>"The examination begins at nine o''clock."</li>
      <li>"Our class visited the library. We returned before lunch."</li>
      <li>"The learners completed the assignment and submitted it."</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Where a full stop is needed</h3>
    <p>Use a full stop at the end of a statement. A new sentence should begin with a capital letter.</p>
    <p>Incorrect: "The bus arrived late the learners missed the assembly"</p>
    <p>Correct: "The bus arrived late. The learners missed the assembly."</p>

    <h3>Full stops and abbreviations</h3>
    <p>Some styles use full stops in abbreviations, while others omit them. Follow the convention required by the writing task and use it consistently.</p>

    <h3>Avoid unnecessary full stops</h3>
    <p>Do not place a full stop in the middle of a sentence when the ideas belong together grammatically. A full stop should separate complete sentences, not split a subject from its verb.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Full Stops', 15),
  (1899, 1899, '
    <h2>Question Marks</h2>
    <p>A question mark (?) is placed at the end of a direct question. It signals that the speaker or writer is asking something.</p>

    <h3>Direct questions</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>"Where is the library?"</li>
      <li>"Have you completed the assignment?"</li>
      <li>"Why did the electricity go off?"</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Indirect questions</h3>
    <p>An indirect question reports or describes a question without asking it directly. It normally ends with a full stop.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Direct: "Where is the office?"</li>
      <li>Indirect: "She asked where the office was."</li>
      <li>Direct: "Have you paid the fee?"</li>
      <li>Indirect: "He wanted to know whether I had paid the fee."</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Questions within longer sentences</h3>
    <p>Use a question mark when the whole sentence is a direct question.</p>
    <p>Example: "Can you explain why the machine stopped?"</p>
    <p>Although the sentence contains an embedded question, the entire sentence is a direct question, so it ends with a question mark.</p>

    <h3>Common error</h3>
    <p>Incorrect: "The teacher asked, where is your book?"</p>
    <p>Correct in reported speech: "The teacher asked where my book was."</p>
    <p>Correct as direct speech: "The teacher asked, ''Where is your book?''"</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Question Marks', 15),
  (1900, 1900, '
    <h2>Exclamation Marks</h2>
    <p>An exclamation mark (!) expresses strong feeling or marks an emphatic exclamation or command. It may communicate surprise, excitement, alarm, anger or urgency.</p>

    <h3>Examples</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Surprise: "What an unexpected result!"</li>
      <li>Excitement: "We have won the competition!"</li>
      <li>Alarm: "Look out!"</li>
      <li>Urgent command: "Stop the vehicle!"</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Exclamation or question?</h3>
    <p>"How beautiful the garden is!" expresses admiration.</p>
    <p>"How beautiful is the garden?" asks a question, though a more natural question might be "How beautiful is the garden?" in a specific context.</p>

    <h3>Use exclamation marks carefully</h3>
    <p>Too many exclamation marks can make writing seem exaggerated or unprofessional. In formal reports and academic writing, use them only when the context genuinely requires them.</p>

    <h3>Direct speech</h3>
    <p>"''Get out of the way!'' the driver shouted."</p>
    <p>The exclamation mark belongs to the spoken words. It appears inside the quotation marks in this example.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Exclamation Marks', 15),
  (1901, 1901, '
    <h2>Commas in Lists</h2>
    <p>Commas separate items in a series, making lists easier to read.</p>

    <h3>Lists of words</h3>
    <p>"We bought rice, beans, flour and cooking oil."</p>
    <p>The commas separate the listed items. In many styles, a comma before the final ''and'' is optional unless it improves clarity or is required by the chosen style.</p>

    <h3>Lists of phrases</h3>
    <p>"The learners cleaned the classroom, arranged the desks and swept the corridor."</p>
    <p>Each activity is presented as part of the same series.</p>

    <h3>When clarity matters</h3>
    <p>Consider: "The guests were my cousins, a doctor and a teacher."</p>
    <p>This may suggest that the cousins were a doctor and a teacher. If the intended meaning is that these are separate guests, rewrite it clearly: "The guests were my cousins, a doctor, and a teacher."</p>

    <h3>Do not separate a subject from its verb</h3>
    <p>Incorrect: "The books, pens and rulers, were on the table."</p>
    <p>Correct: "The books, pens and rulers were on the table."</p>

    <p>Use commas to separate list items, but do not insert a comma merely because you pause while speaking.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Commas in Lists', 15),
  (1902, 1902, '
    <h2>Commas in Sentences</h2>
    <p>Commas separate parts of sentences to make meaning clearer. Their use depends on sentence structure, not simply on where a reader pauses.</p>

    <h3>After introductory words and phrases</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>"However, the results were different."</li>
      <li>"After the meeting, the learners returned to class."</li>
      <li>"Fortunately, nobody was injured."</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Separating extra information</h3>
    <p>"The principal, who arrived early, welcomed the visitors."</p>
    <p>The clause between the commas adds information but is not essential to identify which principal is meant in this sentence.</p>

    <h3>Joining independent clauses</h3>
    <p>A comma can be used before a coordinating conjunction such as ''and'', ''but'' or ''so'' when it joins two independent clauses.</p>
    <p>"The rain stopped, and the match resumed."</p>
    <p>Both sides can stand as complete sentences.</p>

    <h3>Avoid comma splices</h3>
    <p>Incorrect: "The rain stopped, the match resumed."</p>
    <p>Correct: "The rain stopped, and the match resumed."</p>
    <p>Another correction: "The rain stopped. The match resumed."</p>

    <h3>Do not separate essential information</h3>
    <p>"Learners who submit their work early may leave first."</p>
    <p>The clause ''who submit their work early'' identifies which learners may leave first, so it is not set off with commas in this sentence.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Commas in Sentences', 15),
  (1903, 1903, '
    <h2>Introductory Commas</h2>
    <p>An introductory comma separates an opening word, phrase or dependent clause from the main part of a sentence. It helps readers recognise how the sentence is organised.</p>

    <h3>Introductory words</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>"Unfortunately, the bus had already left."</li>
      <li>"Meanwhile, the learners prepared their notes."</li>
      <li>"First, read the question carefully."</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Introductory phrases</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>"At the end of the lesson, the teacher collected the books."</li>
      <li>"Without any warning, the lights went out."</li>
      <li>"During the school holiday, we visited our grandparents."</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Introductory dependent clauses</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>"Although the road was muddy, the bus continued."</li>
      <li>"When the bell rang, the learners left the classroom."</li>
      <li>"If you finish early, check your answers."</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>When a comma may be unnecessary</h3>
    <p>Short introductory phrases do not always require a comma, depending on clarity and style. For example, "In 2025 the school opened a new library" is sometimes written without a comma after the year.</p>

    <h3>Common mistake</h3>
    <p>Incorrect: "After the lesson, the teacher, explained the assignment."</p>
    <p>Correct: "After the lesson, the teacher explained the assignment."</p>
    <p>The comma follows the introductory phrase, not the subject.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Introductory Commas', 15),
  (1904, 1904, '
    <h2>Apostrophes for Possession</h2>
    <p>An apostrophe can show that something belongs to someone or something. The placement depends on whether the owner is singular, plural or an irregular plural.</p>

    <h3>Singular owners</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>the girl''s book — one girl owns the book.</li>
      <li>the teacher''s desk — one teacher''s desk.</li>
      <li>the school''s gate — a gate belonging to the school.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Plural owners ending in s</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>the girls'' books — books belonging to several girls.</li>
      <li>the teachers'' office — an office belonging to several teachers.</li>
      <li>the players'' uniforms — uniforms belonging to several players.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Irregular plural owners</h3>
    <p>Irregular plural nouns that do not end in s usually take ''s.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>the children''s playground</li>
      <li>the men''s team</li>
      <li>the women''s meeting</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Singular nouns ending in s</h3>
    <p>Style guides differ on some singular names ending in s. A common approach is to add ''s, as in "James''s book"; some styles use only an apostrophe. Follow the required style consistently.</p>

    <h3>Possession or plural?</h3>
    <p>"The student''s books" means books belonging to one student.</p>
    <p>"The students'' books" means books belonging to multiple students.</p>
    <p>"The students are ready" is a plural noun with no apostrophe because no possession is shown.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Apostrophes for Possession', 15),
  (1905, 1905, '
    <h2>Apostrophes in Contractions</h2>
    <p>A contraction combines words by leaving out one or more letters. An apostrophe marks the missing letters.</p>

    <h3>Common contractions</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>do not → don''t</li>
      <li>cannot → can''t</li>
      <li>is not → isn''t</li>
      <li>they are → they''re</li>
      <li>we have → we''ve</li>
      <li>she will → she''ll</li>
      <li>it is → it''s</li>
      <li>who is → who''s</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Do not confuse contractions with possession</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>It''s</strong> means "it is" or "it has".</li>
      <li><strong>Its</strong> shows possession: "The dog wagged its tail."</li>
      <li><strong>Who''s</strong> means "who is" or "who has".</li>
      <li><strong>Whose</strong> asks about ownership: "Whose bag is this?"</li>
      <li><strong>They''re</strong> means "they are".</li>
      <li><strong>Their</strong> shows possession: "Their bags are here."</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Formal writing</h3>
    <p>Contractions are common in conversation, informal letters and many everyday messages. In formal reports or academic writing, the full forms are often preferred unless the task or style allows contractions.</p>

    <h3>Check a contraction</h3>
    <p>Expand the contraction to its full form. If the sentence still makes sense, the contraction is likely correct.</p>
    <p>Example: "They''re ready" → "They are ready."</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Apostrophes in Contractions', 15),
  (1906, 1906, '
    <h2>Colons</h2>
    <p>A colon (:) introduces information that explains, illustrates or expands on what comes before it. It is also used to introduce lists in suitable sentence structures.</p>

    <h3>Introducing a list</h3>
    <p>"Bring the following items: a notebook, a pen and a ruler."</p>
    <p>The words before the colon form a complete introduction to the list.</p>

    <h3>Introducing an explanation</h3>
    <p>"The team had one major problem: nobody had checked the equipment."</p>
    <p>The clause after the colon explains the problem.</p>

    <h3>Introducing an example</h3>
    <p>"One habit improves revision: reviewing difficult topics regularly."</p>
    <p>The second part explains the habit.</p>

    <h3>Colons in formal writing</h3>
    <p>Colons can introduce quotations, examples or details, but they should not separate a verb from its object or a preposition from its complement.</p>

    <p>Incorrect: "The equipment included: a camera and a microphone."</p>
    <p>Better: "The equipment included a camera and a microphone."</p>
    <p>Also correct: "The equipment included two items: a camera and a microphone."</p>

    <h3>Capitalisation after a colon</h3>
    <p>Whether the next word is capitalised depends on the sentence structure and style guide. In ordinary lists and explanations, lowercase is common unless a proper noun or another rule requires a capital.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Colons', 15),
  (1907, 1907, '
    <h2>Semicolons</h2>
    <p>A semicolon (;) connects closely related independent clauses or separates complex items in a list.</p>

    <h3>Joining related independent clauses</h3>
    <p>Each side of the semicolon must be able to stand as a complete sentence.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>"The road was flooded; the bus could not pass."</li>
      <li>"Amina studied consistently; she performed well in the examination."</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Semicolon or comma?</h3>
    <p>A comma alone cannot normally join two independent clauses.</p>
    <p>Incorrect: "The road was flooded, the bus could not pass."</p>
    <p>Correct: "The road was flooded; the bus could not pass."</p>
    <p>Another correct option: "The road was flooded, so the bus could not pass."</p>

    <h3>Separating complex list items</h3>
    <p>Semicolons help distinguish list items when the items themselves contain commas.</p>
    <p>"The visitors came from Embu, Kenya; Kampala, Uganda; and Kigali, Rwanda."</p>

    <h3>Important rule</h3>
    <p>A semicolon is not a decorative replacement for every comma or full stop. Use it when the clauses are closely related or when a complicated list needs clearer separation.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Semicolons', 15),
  (1908, 1908, '
    <h2>Quotation Marks</h2>
    <p>Quotation marks show the exact words spoken or written by someone. They can also mark titles or indicate that a word is being discussed in a particular way, depending on the style used.</p>

    <h3>Direct speech</h3>
    <p>"The teacher said, ''Open your books.''"</p>
    <p>The quoted words reproduce the speaker''s actual words.</p>

    <h3>Speech followed by a reporting clause</h3>
    <p>"''I have finished my work,'' Amina said."</p>
    <p>The reporting clause identifies the speaker.</p>

    <h3>Questions and exclamations in quotations</h3>
    <p>"Peter asked, ''Where is the laboratory?''"</p>
    <p>"''Stop!'' the driver shouted."</p>
    <p>The punctuation belongs with the quoted words in these examples.</p>

    <h3>Quoting exact words</h3>
    <p>If you quote a sentence from a source, reproduce its wording accurately and make it clear which words are quoted.</p>

    <h3>Quotation mark styles</h3>
    <p>British English often uses single quotation marks for direct speech, while American English commonly uses double quotation marks. Both systems are used; follow the required style consistently.</p>

    <h3>Common errors</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Incorrect: Amina said, "I am ready.</li>
      <li>Correct: Amina said, "I am ready."</li>
      <li>Incorrect: "Where are you? asked Peter."</li>
      <li>Correct: "Where are you?" Peter asked.</li>
    </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Quotation Marks', 15),
  (1909, 1909, '
    <h2>Hyphens and Dashes</h2>
    <p>A hyphen (-) joins words or word parts. A dash (— or –) marks a break, interruption or additional information. They have different functions.</p>

    <h3>Hyphens in compound modifiers</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>"a well-known author"</li>
      <li>"a ten-year-old building"</li>
      <li>"a part-time job"</li>
    </ul>
    <p>A hyphen can help show that two or more words work together as one description before a noun.</p>

    <h3>When the hyphen may not be needed</h3>
    <p>Compare "a well-known author" with "the author is well known". Hyphenation often depends on whether the compound expression comes before or after the noun, although established expressions and style conventions vary.</p>

    <h3>Dashes for interruption or emphasis</h3>
    <p>"The results — which surprised everyone — were announced on Friday."</p>
    <p>The dashes set off additional information.</p>

    <h3>Dashes for a sudden break</h3>
    <p>"I was about to explain — but the bell rang."</p>
    <p>The dash marks a pause or shift in thought.</p>

    <h3>Do not confuse the marks</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Hyphen: "a three-day visit".</li>
      <li>Dash: "The visit — unexpectedly — lasted a week."</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Use punctuation consistently and avoid adding hyphens or dashes when they make the sentence harder to read.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Hyphens and Dashes', 15),
  (1910, 1910, '
    <h2>Brackets and Parentheses</h2>
    <p>Parentheses ( ) enclose additional information that is not essential to the main sentence. Square brackets [ ] are often used to add clarification inside a quotation or to enclose material in specialised writing.</p>

    <h3>Parentheses for extra information</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>"The meeting (originally scheduled for Tuesday) was postponed."</li>
      <li>"Nairobi (the capital of Kenya) is a major commercial centre."</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The sentence remains grammatically complete if the parenthetical information is removed.</p>

    <h3>Parentheses for examples or details</h3>
    <p>"Bring writing materials (a pen, pencil and ruler) to the examination."</p>

    <h3>Square brackets in quotations</h3>
    <p>Square brackets can show words added by a writer to clarify a quotation.</p>
    <p>Example: The report stated, "They [the inspectors] arrived at noon."</p>
    <p>The words in brackets clarify who "they" refers to; they were not necessarily part of the original wording.</p>

    <h3>Punctuation with parentheses</h3>
    <p>If the parenthetical material occurs within a sentence, the surrounding sentence punctuation usually remains outside the closing parenthesis.</p>
    <p>Example: "The team arrived early (before the gates opened)."</p>

    <h3>Avoid overuse</h3>
    <p>Too many parenthetical comments can interrupt the flow of a paragraph. If the information is central to the meaning, state it directly instead of hiding it inside brackets.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Brackets and Parentheses', 15),
  (1911, 1911, '
    <h2>Skimming</h2>
    <p>Skimming is reading quickly to understand the general subject, main idea and overall organisation of a text. It is not intended to capture every detail.</p>

    <h3>What to look at</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Read the title and headings.</li>
      <li>Read the opening paragraph to identify the topic.</li>
      <li>Notice the first sentence of each paragraph.</li>
      <li>Look at repeated words, summaries and concluding statements.</li>
      <li>Use diagrams, captions and highlighted terms to understand the structure.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Example</h3>
    <p><strong>Title: How School Gardens Support Learning</strong></p>
    <p>A school garden can provide more than food. Learners can observe plant growth, practise measuring, study soil and water, and work together to maintain the garden. Some schools also use harvested vegetables in meals or sell surplus produce to support school projects.</p>

    <p>After skimming, you should recognise that the passage is mainly about the educational and practical benefits of school gardens. You do not need to remember every example yet.</p>

    <h3>When to skim</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>When deciding whether a text is relevant to your task.</li>
      <li>When previewing a long passage before answering questions.</li>
      <li>When looking for the overall argument or main topic.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Skimming is not scanning</h3>
    <p>Skimming searches for the overall message. Scanning searches for a specific detail, such as a date, name or figure.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Skimming', 15),
  (1912, 1912, '
    <h2>Scanning</h2>
    <p>Scanning is reading quickly to locate a specific piece of information. Unlike skimming, it does not aim to establish the overall meaning of a passage.</p>

    <h3>What can you scan for?</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>A date or time.</li>
      <li>A person''s name.</li>
      <li>A price, percentage or other number.</li>
      <li>A place or named organisation.</li>
      <li>A particular word or phrase in a timetable or notice.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Example: School notice</h3>
    <p><strong>SCIENCE CLUB MEETING</strong></p>
    <p>Date: Friday, 16 October</p>
    <p>Time: 3:30 p.m.</p>
    <p>Venue: Laboratory 2</p>
    <p>Bring: Your notebook and a pen</p>

    <p>Question: Where will the meeting take place?</p>
    <p>Scan for the label ''Venue''. The answer is Laboratory 2. You do not need to read the notice repeatedly from beginning to end.</p>

    <h3>A practical scanning method</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Identify exactly what the question asks for.</li>
      <li>Predict the form of the answer: a date, name, number or place.</li>
      <li>Move your eyes quickly through the text to locate matching information.</li>
      <li>Read the relevant words carefully to confirm the answer.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Avoid careless matching</h3>
    <p>A number or name may appear more than once. Read the words around it to confirm that it answers the exact question.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Scanning', 15),
  (1913, 1913, '
    <h2>Intensive Reading</h2>
    <p>Intensive reading means reading a short text or selected section carefully to understand precise information, vocabulary, relationships and implied meaning.</p>

    <h3>What intensive reading involves</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Read the passage carefully, sentence by sentence.</li>
      <li>Identify unfamiliar words and use context to interpret them.</li>
      <li>Distinguish the main idea from supporting details.</li>
      <li>Notice linking words that show cause, contrast or sequence.</li>
      <li>Use evidence from the passage to answer questions.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Example passage</h3>
    <p>Although the village received heavy rainfall, the reservoir remained low. Most of the water flowed across the dry ground before reaching the reservoir because the surrounding slopes had lost much of their vegetation. The community therefore began planting grass and trees to reduce runoff.</p>

    <h3>Read the relationships carefully</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Contrast:</strong> ''Although'' introduces a contrast between heavy rainfall and the low reservoir level.</li>
      <li><strong>Cause:</strong> Water flowed across the ground because the slopes had lost vegetation.</li>
      <li><strong>Response:</strong> The community planted grass and trees to reduce runoff.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Support your answers</h3>
    <p>If asked why the reservoir remained low, use the passage''s explanation about runoff and reduced vegetation. Do not invent a cause that the text does not support.</p>

    <p>Intensive reading is useful for comprehension questions that require exact details, careful interpretation or evidence-based explanations.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Intensive Reading', 15),
  (1914, 1914, '
    <h2>Identifying the Main Idea</h2>
    <p>The main idea is the central point a paragraph or passage communicates. Supporting details explain, develop or provide evidence for that point.</p>

    <h3>Example passage</h3>
    <p>School libraries support learning in several ways. They give learners access to books beyond their classroom notes, provide a quiet place for reading and research, and help learners develop independent study habits. A well-organised library can therefore support learning across many subjects.</p>

    <h3>Find the central point</h3>
    <p>The passage is mainly about how school libraries support learning.</p>
    <p>The details about access to books, quiet study and independent learning explain that central point. They are not separate main ideas of the whole paragraph.</p>

    <h3>How to identify the main idea</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Ask what the whole paragraph is mainly about.</li>
      <li>Look for an idea that connects most of the sentences.</li>
      <li>Distinguish the central message from one example or detail.</li>
      <li>Express the main idea in your own words without making it too broad or too narrow.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Main idea and topic</h3>
    <p>The topic may be ''school libraries''. The main idea states what the passage says about that topic: school libraries support learning in several ways.</p>

    <h3>Avoid common traps</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>A detail may be true but too narrow to represent the whole passage.</li>
      <li>An option may introduce information that the passage never mentions.</li>
      <li>An overly broad answer may be related to the topic but not supported by the passage.</li>
    </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Identifying Main Idea', 15),
  (1915, 1915, '
    <h2>Identifying Supporting Details</h2>
    <p>Supporting details explain, illustrate or provide evidence for a main idea. They may include examples, reasons, facts, descriptions or results.</p>

    <h3>Example passage</h3>
    <p>Regular physical activity benefits learners. It can improve fitness, help reduce stress and support concentration. For example, a short walk after a long study session can give a learner a break from sitting and help them return to work refreshed.</p>

    <h3>Main idea and supporting details</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Main idea:</strong> Regular physical activity benefits learners.</li>
      <li><strong>Supporting detail 1:</strong> It can improve fitness.</li>
      <li><strong>Supporting detail 2:</strong> It can help reduce stress.</li>
      <li><strong>Supporting detail 3:</strong> It can support concentration.</li>
      <li><strong>Example:</strong> Taking a short walk after studying.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>How to locate a supporting detail</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Identify the claim or main idea in the question.</li>
      <li>Return to the relevant section of the passage.</li>
      <li>Find the sentence that explains or illustrates the claim.</li>
      <li>Check that the detail directly supports the point being asked about.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Do not confuse a detail with an inference</h3>
    <p>A supporting detail is stated in the passage. An inference is a conclusion you draw from the stated information. Both require evidence, but they are not identical.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Identifying Supporting Details', 15),
  (1916, 1916, '
    <h2>Explicit Information</h2>
    <p>Explicit information is stated directly in a text. A reader answers questions about it by locating and understanding what the passage actually says, rather than guessing what might be true.</p>

    <h3>Example passage</h3>
    <p>The school science exhibition will take place on Thursday, 22 October, in the main hall. Visitors may enter from 10:00 a.m. Learners must register their projects with the science teacher by Monday.</p>

    <h3>Questions answered directly</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>When is the exhibition?</strong> Thursday, 22 October.</li>
      <li><strong>Where will it take place?</strong> In the main hall.</li>
      <li><strong>When may visitors enter?</strong> From 10:00 a.m.</li>
      <li><strong>When must projects be registered?</strong> By Monday.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>A reliable method</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Identify the key words in the question: who, what, when, where or how many.</li>
      <li>Locate the relevant sentence or section.</li>
      <li>Extract the stated information accurately.</li>
      <li>Answer only what the question asks.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Do not add assumptions</h3>
    <p>The passage does not say that visitors must register. It says learners must register their projects. Do not transfer a requirement from one group to another.</p>

    <p>When the question asks for explicit information, your answer should be supported directly by the passage.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Explicit Information', 15),
  (1917, 1917, '
    <h2>Implied Meaning</h2>
    <p>Implied meaning is information a writer suggests without stating it directly. Readers work it out by combining clues in the text with reasonable background knowledge.</p>

    <h3>Example</h3>
    <p>Kamau looked at the dark clouds, packed his books into a plastic bag and hurried home.</p>
    <p>The passage does not say that Kamau expected rain. However, the dark clouds and his decision to protect his books suggest that he thought it might rain.</p>

    <h3>Another example</h3>
    <p>When the teacher announced the results, Amina smiled, held her paper close and immediately called her mother.</p>
    <p>These actions may suggest that Amina received good news and wanted to share it. The passage does not explicitly state her marks.</p>

    <h3>How to identify implied meaning</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Identify the details the writer actually gives.</li>
      <li>Consider what those details suggest when read together.</li>
      <li>Choose an interpretation supported by the text.</li>
      <li>Avoid treating a possibility as a proven fact.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Explicit and implied meaning</h3>
    <p>Explicit: "Kamau carried an umbrella."</p>
    <p>Implied: "Kamau saw dark clouds and hurried home." This may suggest that he expected rain.</p>

    <p>A strong answer explains which clues support the implied meaning.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Implied Meaning', 15),
  (1918, 1918, '
    <h2>Context Clues</h2>
    <p>Context clues are words, phrases or ideas around an unfamiliar word that help a reader work out its meaning.</p>

    <h3>1. Definition or explanation</h3>
    <p>"Arid conditions, meaning extremely dry conditions, make farming difficult."</p>
    <p>The explanation directly gives the meaning of ''arid''.</p>

    <h3>2. Contrast</h3>
    <p>"Unlike his talkative brother, Musa was reserved and said very little."</p>
    <p>The contrast suggests that ''reserved'' means quiet or not inclined to speak much.</p>

    <h3>3. Examples</h3>
    <p>"Nocturnal animals, such as owls and bats, are active mainly at night."</p>
    <p>The examples and explanation reveal the meaning of ''nocturnal''.</p>

    <h3>4. Cause and effect</h3>
    <p>"The path was slippery, so the hikers proceeded cautiously."</p>
    <p>The consequence suggests that ''cautiously'' means carefully to avoid danger.</p>

    <h3>5. Restatement</h3>
    <p>"The instructions were ambiguous; nobody could agree on what they meant."</p>
    <p>The second part suggests that ''ambiguous'' means unclear or open to more than one interpretation.</p>

    <h3>A careful method</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Read the whole sentence and, if necessary, the surrounding paragraph.</li>
      <li>Look for a definition, contrast, example or consequence.</li>
      <li>Substitute a possible meaning for the unfamiliar word.</li>
      <li>Check whether the sentence still makes sense.</li>
    </ol>

    <p>Context clues help, but they do not always prove one exact definition. Use a dictionary when the context is insufficient.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Context Clues', 15),
  (1919, 1919, '
    <h2>Making Inferences</h2>
    <p>An inference is a conclusion drawn from evidence in a text and relevant background knowledge. It goes beyond what is directly stated, but it must remain supported by the evidence.</p>

    <h3>Evidence plus reasoning</h3>
    <p>Example: "The classroom windows were closed, learners were fanning themselves with exercise books, and the afternoon sun fell directly on the room."</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Evidence:</strong> Closed windows, fanning and direct sunlight.</li>
      <li><strong>Reasoning:</strong> The learners are trying to cool themselves.</li>
      <li><strong>Inference:</strong> The classroom is probably hot.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Inference is not guessing</h3>
    <p>A guess may have little evidence. An inference connects specific clues to a reasonable conclusion.</p>

    <h3>Three steps</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Collect the relevant clues.</li>
      <li>Explain what the clues suggest together.</li>
      <li>Choose the conclusion that requires the fewest unsupported assumptions.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Consider alternative explanations</h3>
    <p>A person who is quiet may be tired, worried, concentrating or simply naturally quiet. Without additional evidence, it is unsafe to claim a particular emotional state as a certainty.</p>

    <p>In comprehension questions, support an inference by referring to the words or actions that led you to it.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Making Inferences', 15),
  (1920, 1920, '
    <h2>Author''s Purpose</h2>
    <p>An author''s purpose is the main reason for writing a text. Common purposes include informing, persuading, entertaining, explaining and instructing. A text may have more than one purpose.</p>

    <h3>Inform</h3>
    <p>A text provides facts or information.</p>
    <p>Example: "The county receives most of its annual rainfall between March and May."</p>

    <h3>Persuade</h3>
    <p>A text tries to influence beliefs, opinions or actions.</p>
    <p>Example: "Every household should plant trees to help protect local water sources."</p>

    <h3>Entertain</h3>
    <p>A text aims to amuse, engage or interest the reader.</p>
    <p>Example: A humorous story about a student who accidentally attends the wrong class.</p>

    <h3>Explain</h3>
    <p>A text describes how or why something happens.</p>
    <p>Example: A passage explaining how water moves through the water cycle.</p>

    <h3>Instruct</h3>
    <p>A text gives directions or steps to follow.</p>
    <p>Example: "First, rinse the container. Next, fill it with clean water."</p>

    <h3>How to identify purpose</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Identify what the text mainly does.</li>
      <li>Look for its language, structure and supporting details.</li>
      <li>Ask what response the writer wants from the reader, if any.</li>
      <li>Choose the purpose best supported by the entire text.</li>
    </ol>

    <p>Do not assume that every text containing facts is written only to inform. Facts may also be used to support persuasion.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Author''s Purpose', 15),
  (1921, 1921, '
    <h2>Identifying Audience</h2>
    <p>The audience is the person or group a text is intended for. Writers adjust their vocabulary, tone, details and format to suit their readers.</p>

    <h3>Clues to the intended audience</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Vocabulary:</strong> technical terms may indicate readers with specialised knowledge.</li>
      <li><strong>Content:</strong> information about revision may target learners preparing for examinations.</li>
      <li><strong>Format:</strong> a notice addressed to parents suggests a school community audience.</li>
      <li><strong>Tone:</strong> a formal announcement may target an official or professional audience.</li>
      <li><strong>Direct address:</strong> words such as "Dear parents" identify the intended readers explicitly.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Example 1: School notice</h3>
    <p>"Dear parents and guardians, the school will hold a meeting on Friday at 2:00 p.m. to discuss learner progress."</p>
    <p>The intended audience is parents and guardians.</p>

    <h3>Example 2: Technical guide</h3>
    <p>"Before replacing the component, disconnect the power supply and verify that the circuit is no longer energised."</p>
    <p>The intended audience is likely people carrying out technical work, although the text could also be written for learners studying the procedure.</p>

    <h3>How to answer audience questions</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Look for direct references to readers.</li>
      <li>Identify the subject and level of detail.</li>
      <li>Consider where the text would normally appear.</li>
      <li>State the most likely audience and support it with evidence.</li>
    </ol>

    <p>A text''s topic alone does not always identify its audience. Consider the way the information is presented.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Identifying Audience', 15),
  (1922, 1922, '
    <h2>Recognising Tone</h2>
    <p>Tone is the attitude communicated by a writer or speaker towards a subject, person or situation. It is conveyed through word choice, sentence structure, emphasis and the overall context.</p>

    <h3>Common tones</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Optimistic:</strong> emphasises hope or positive possibilities.</li>
      <li><strong>Pessimistic:</strong> emphasises negative expectations.</li>
      <li><strong>Critical:</strong> points out faults or expresses disapproval.</li>
      <li><strong>Appreciative:</strong> expresses gratitude or admiration.</li>
      <li><strong>Concerned:</strong> shows worry or serious attention.</li>
      <li><strong>Sarcastic:</strong> uses mock praise or cutting language to criticise.</li>
      <li><strong>Humorous:</strong> aims to amuse.</li>
      <li><strong>Neutral:</strong> presents information without a clear emotional judgement.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Example</h3>
    <p>"The volunteers worked tirelessly to restore the damaged bridge, and their efforts gave the village renewed hope."</p>
    <p>The words "tirelessly" and "renewed hope" create an appreciative and optimistic tone.</p>

    <h3>Tone and mood are different</h3>
    <p>Tone refers to the writer''s or speaker''s attitude. Mood refers to the feeling or atmosphere experienced by the reader.</p>

    <h3>How to identify tone</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Read the passage, not just a single word.</li>
      <li>Find words that express judgement or emotion.</li>
      <li>Consider the writer''s treatment of the subject.</li>
      <li>Name the tone and cite the words that support your answer.</li>
    </ol>

    <p>Two tones may coexist, but select the one that best describes the passage as a whole when the question asks for the main tone.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Recognising Tone', 15),
  (1923, 1923, '
    <h2>Fact and Opinion</h2>
    <p>A fact is a statement that can be checked against evidence. An opinion expresses a belief, judgement, preference or evaluation. A statement can contain both factual information and opinion.</p>

    <h3>Facts</h3>
    <p>"The meeting began at 10:00 a.m."</p>
    <p>This is a factual claim that can be checked against the meeting record.</p>

    <h3>Opinions</h3>
    <p>"The meeting was unnecessarily long."</p>
    <p>The word ''unnecessarily'' expresses a judgement about the length of the meeting.</p>

    <h3>Mixed statements</h3>
    <p>"The school opened a new library last year, and it is the best library in the county."</p>
    <ul>
      <li>"The school opened a new library last year" is verifiable.</li>
      <li>"It is the best library in the county" is an evaluative claim that requires criteria and evidence.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>How to distinguish them</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Ask whether the claim can be checked using reliable evidence.</li>
      <li>Look for judgement words such as ''best'', ''terrible'', ''beautiful'' or ''unfair''.</li>
      <li>Remember that an opinion may be supported by evidence, but it remains an evaluation.</li>
      <li>Do not assume a statement is factual merely because it includes numbers.</li>
    </ol>

    <p>Critical reading involves checking evidence and separating what is known from what the writer believes or evaluates.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Fact and Opinion', 15),
  (1924, 1924, '
    <h2>Bias and Persuasive Language</h2>
    <p>Bias occurs when a text presents a person, group, issue or viewpoint unfairly or disproportionately. Persuasive language is language used to influence a reader''s beliefs, feelings or actions.</p>

    <h3>Loaded language</h3>
    <p>Words such as ''disastrous'', ''heroic'', ''lazy'' and ''brilliant'' can encourage a particular judgement.</p>
    <p>Compare:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>"The committee changed the timetable."</li>
      <li>"The committee recklessly disrupted everyone''s plans."</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The second version uses more emotionally charged language and frames the change negatively.</p>

    <h3>Other persuasive techniques</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Emotive language:</strong> appeals to feelings such as fear, pride or sympathy.</li>
      <li><strong>Rhetorical questions:</strong> encourage reflection or imply a preferred answer.</li>
      <li><strong>Appeal to popularity:</strong> suggests that something must be right because many people support it.</li>
      <li><strong>Selective evidence:</strong> highlights favourable information while leaving out important opposing evidence.</li>
      <li><strong>Exaggeration:</strong> overstates a benefit, danger or problem.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>How to evaluate a text</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Identify the claim the writer wants readers to accept.</li>
      <li>Notice emotionally charged words and persuasive techniques.</li>
      <li>Check whether evidence is provided.</li>
      <li>Ask whether important alternative explanations or opposing evidence are missing.</li>
    </ol>

    <p>Persuasion is not automatically dishonest. The important question is whether the presentation is fair and the claims are supported.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Bias and Persuasive Language', 15),
  (1925, 1925, '
    <h2>Claims and Evidence</h2>
    <p>A claim is a statement that someone wants readers to accept. Evidence is information used to support or challenge that claim. Evidence may include verified facts, measurements, records, examples or credible research.</p>

    <h3>Example</h3>
    <p><strong>Claim:</strong> Providing clean drinking water at school improves learner attendance.</p>
    <p><strong>Possible evidence:</strong> Reliable attendance records before and after a water programme, combined with information about other factors that may have affected attendance.</p>

    <h3>Strong and weak evidence</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Relevant:</strong> directly relates to the claim.</li>
      <li><strong>Reliable:</strong> comes from a source or method that deserves confidence.</li>
      <li><strong>Sufficient:</strong> provides enough support for the strength of the claim.</li>
      <li><strong>Accurate:</strong> represents the information correctly.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Examples are not always proof</h3>
    <p>"My friend started exercising and became healthier, so exercise always prevents illness."</p>
    <p>One person''s experience may illustrate a possibility, but it cannot establish that exercise always prevents illness.</p>

    <h3>Evaluate a claim</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>State exactly what the writer claims.</li>
      <li>Identify the evidence offered.</li>
      <li>Check whether the evidence is relevant and trustworthy.</li>
      <li>Decide whether the evidence supports the claim as strongly as the writer suggests.</li>
    </ol>

    <p>Evidence can support a conclusion without proving it beyond all doubt. The strength of the conclusion should match the strength of the evidence.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Claims and Evidence', 15),
  (1926, 1926, '
    <h2>Evaluating Information Sources</h2>
    <p>Evaluating an information source means judging whether it is suitable and trustworthy for the question being investigated. A source can be useful for one purpose but unreliable for another.</p>

    <h3>Check authorship and expertise</h3>
    <p>Who created the information? Does the author have relevant knowledge or experience? Can the author or organisation be identified?</p>

    <h3>Check evidence</h3>
    <p>Does the source explain how its claims were established? Does it link to records, data, research or other checkable evidence?</p>

    <h3>Check date and relevance</h3>
    <p>Some information changes quickly. Current public-health guidance, transport schedules and prices may require recent sources. Historical information may need an original record from the relevant period.</p>

    <h3>Consider purpose and possible bias</h3>
    <p>Is the source trying to inform, sell, entertain or persuade? Commercial or personal interests do not automatically make information false, but they can affect how it is presented.</p>

    <h3>Compare independent sources</h3>
    <p>Look for agreement between credible sources that rely on different evidence. Several websites repeating the same unsupported claim do not necessarily provide independent confirmation.</p>

    <h3>Practical checklist</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Who is responsible for the information?</li>
      <li>What evidence supports the claims?</li>
      <li>Is the information appropriate to the question and time period?</li>
      <li>What is the source''s purpose?</li>
      <li>Can the key claims be confirmed independently?</li>
    </ol>

    <p>Do not judge reliability solely by professional design, confident language or the number of people sharing a post.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Evaluating Information Sources', 15),
  (1927, 1927, '
    <h2>Comparing Sources</h2>
    <p>Comparing sources means examining how two or more texts present the same subject. The sources may agree, disagree, emphasise different details or serve different purposes.</p>

    <h3>What to compare</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Main claims:</strong> What does each source say?</li>
      <li><strong>Evidence:</strong> What facts, examples or data support each claim?</li>
      <li><strong>Purpose:</strong> Is each source informing, persuading, explaining or entertaining?</li>
      <li><strong>Perspective:</strong> Does the writer have a particular interest or viewpoint?</li>
      <li><strong>Date and context:</strong> Were the sources produced at different times or under different circumstances?</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Example</h3>
    <p><strong>Source A:</strong> "The new bus route has reduced travel time for many commuters."</p>
    <p><strong>Source B:</strong> "Although the new bus route saves time, fares have increased for some passengers."</p>
    <p>Both sources discuss the new route. Source A emphasises the benefit of saving time, while Source B presents both a benefit and a cost concern.</p>

    <h3>How to compare accurately</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Identify the shared subject.</li>
      <li>State the main point made by each source.</li>
      <li>Identify specific similarities and differences.</li>
      <li>Support the comparison with evidence from both texts.</li>
    </ol>

    <p>Do not assume that two sources disagree simply because they emphasise different aspects of an issue.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Comparing Sources', 15),
  (1928, 1928, '
    <h2>Recognising Misleading Claims</h2>
    <p>A misleading claim gives readers an inaccurate impression. It may contain a false statement, but it can also use true information selectively, omit important context or exaggerate what the evidence shows.</p>

    <h3>Common warning signs</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Exaggeration:</strong> claiming that something always works when evidence shows it works only sometimes.</li>
      <li><strong>Missing context:</strong> reporting a number without explaining what it measures.</li>
      <li><strong>Selective comparison:</strong> comparing unlike groups or different time periods.</li>
      <li><strong>Unsupported certainty:</strong> using words such as "guaranteed" or "proven" without adequate evidence.</li>
      <li><strong>Misleading headlines:</strong> making a stronger claim than the article itself supports.</li>
      <li><strong>False implication:</strong> suggesting that one event caused another merely because it happened first.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Example</h3>
    <p>A school reports that test scores increased after learners began using a revision app. An advertisement claims that the app alone caused the improvement.</p>
    <p>The increase may be genuine, but other factors could have contributed, including additional lessons, changes in attendance or differences between tests.</p>

    <h3>How to assess a claim</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Identify exactly what the claim says.</li>
      <li>Check the evidence and the context in which it was collected.</li>
      <li>Look for omitted information or alternative explanations.</li>
      <li>Decide whether the wording goes beyond what the evidence supports.</li>
    </ol>

    <p>A claim is not necessarily false just because it is persuasive. Judge whether the overall impression is justified by the available evidence.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Recognising Misleading Claims', 15),
  (1929, 1929, '
    <h2>Evidence vs Unsupported Claims</h2>
    <p>A supported claim is backed by relevant evidence. An unsupported claim is presented without enough evidence to justify it. A claim may be plausible and still be unsupported.</p>

    <h3>Supported claim</h3>
    <p>"The attendance register shows that 45 learners attended on Monday and 52 attended on Tuesday."</p>
    <p>If the register is accurate, it supports the claim about the recorded attendance on those days.</p>

    <h3>Unsupported claim</h3>
    <p>"The new school timetable is the best timetable in the country."</p>
    <p>Without a clear definition of "best" and suitable comparative evidence, the statement is unsupported.</p>

    <h3>Evidence must match the claim</h3>
    <p>A photograph of a healthy plant may support the claim that the plant is healthy. It does not, by itself, prove that a particular fertiliser will make every plant healthy.</p>

    <h3>Ask these questions</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>What exactly is being claimed?</li>
      <li>What evidence is supplied?</li>
      <li>Is that evidence relevant to the claim?</li>
      <li>Is it reliable and sufficient?</li>
      <li>Does the conclusion go further than the evidence allows?</li>
    </ol>

    <p>When evidence is insufficient, the careful conclusion is that the claim has not been established—not necessarily that it is false.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Evidence vs Unsupported Claims', 15),
  (1930, 1930, '
    <h2>Identifying Key Information</h2>
    <p>Key information is the information necessary to understand a text''s main message or answer a particular question. It includes central ideas and the details needed to explain or support them.</p>

    <h3>Main idea and supporting details</h3>
    <p><strong>Main idea:</strong> the central point of a passage or paragraph.</p>
    <p><strong>Supporting details:</strong> explanations, examples, facts or reasons that develop the main idea.</p>

    <h3>Example</h3>
    <p>"Many learners struggle to revise consistently. A weekly timetable can help them divide work into manageable sessions. Short review sessions also make it easier to revisit difficult concepts before an examination."</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Key idea:</strong> A planned revision routine can help learners revise consistently.</li>
      <li><strong>Supporting detail:</strong> A timetable divides work into manageable sessions.</li>
      <li><strong>Supporting detail:</strong> Short reviews help learners revisit difficult concepts.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>How to select key information</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Identify the question or purpose for reading.</li>
      <li>Find the main point of the passage.</li>
      <li>Mark details that explain, support or qualify that point.</li>
      <li>Remove repetition and unrelated examples unless they are needed.</li>
    </ol>

    <p>What counts as key information depends partly on the task. A date may be a minor detail in a general summary but essential when the question asks for a deadline.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Identifying Key Information', 15),
  (1931, 1931, '
    <h2>Main Points and Supporting Details</h2>
    <p>A main point expresses an important idea in a passage. Supporting details explain, justify or illustrate that idea.</p>

    <h3>Recognising the relationship</h3>
    <p><strong>Main point:</strong> School libraries support learning.</p>
    <p><strong>Supporting details:</strong></p>
    <ul>
      <li>They provide access to reference books.</li>
      <li>They offer a quiet place to read.</li>
      <li>They allow learners to practise independent research.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Details can serve different purposes</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Example:</strong> illustrates an idea.</li>
      <li><strong>Reason:</strong> explains why a claim may be true.</li>
      <li><strong>Evidence:</strong> provides information that supports or challenges a claim.</li>
      <li><strong>Qualification:</strong> limits or clarifies a statement.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>How to separate main points from details</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Ask what the paragraph is mainly about.</li>
      <li>Express the central message in one clear sentence.</li>
      <li>Identify which sentences explain or support that message.</li>
      <li>Check whether a detail is necessary for the task.</li>
    </ol>

    <p>A long sentence is not automatically a main point, and a short sentence is not automatically a minor detail. Importance depends on the role each sentence plays.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Main Points and Supporting Details', 15),
  (1932, 1932, '
    <h2>Selecting Relevant Information</h2>
    <p>Relevant information directly helps answer a question or achieve the purpose of a task. Information may be true but irrelevant to the question being asked.</p>

    <h3>Example</h3>
    <p>Question: What steps should learners take to prepare for an examination?</p>
    <p>Relevant information might include making a revision timetable, practising questions and reviewing mistakes. The colour of the examination room is unlikely to be relevant.</p>

    <h3>Relevance depends on purpose</h3>
    <p>If the question asks for the examination date, the date is essential. If it asks how learners can prepare, the date may be secondary or unnecessary.</p>

    <h3>How to select relevant details</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Read the task carefully and identify what it requires.</li>
      <li>Turn the task into a focused question.</li>
      <li>Select details that answer that question directly.</li>
      <li>Keep essential qualifications, conditions or exceptions.</li>
      <li>Remove unrelated information and unnecessary repetition.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Avoid two mistakes</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Including too much:</strong> adding background details that do not answer the task.</li>
      <li><strong>Removing too much:</strong> leaving out a condition that changes the meaning of the answer.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Good selection preserves the information needed for an accurate answer without carrying over every detail in the original text.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Selecting Relevant Information', 15),
  (1933, 1933, '
    <h2>Removing Unnecessary Details</h2>
    <p>When summarising or making notes, remove information that does not contribute to the main point or the task. Keep essential meaning, evidence and qualifications.</p>

    <h3>What can often be removed?</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Repeated statements of the same idea.</li>
      <li>Decorative descriptions that do not affect the message.</li>
      <li>Examples that add no new information.</li>
      <li>Side comments unrelated to the task.</li>
      <li>Unnecessary introductory phrases.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Example</h3>
    <p><strong>Original:</strong> "On Monday morning, at exactly eight o''clock, the school''s hardworking environmental club, which has many enthusiastic members, planted twenty trees around the playing field."</p>
    <p><strong>Concise version:</strong> "The environmental club planted twenty trees around the playing field."</p>
    <p>The shorter version preserves the central action and important quantity while removing details that may be unnecessary for a general summary.</p>

    <h3>Do not remove essential information</h3>
    <p>If the question asks when the trees were planted, "on Monday morning" becomes relevant. If it asks how many trees were planted, "twenty" must remain.</p>

    <h3>Editing method</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Identify the task and central message.</li>
      <li>Underline essential facts and conditions.</li>
      <li>Remove repeated or irrelevant material.</li>
      <li>Read the shorter version to ensure that the meaning remains accurate.</li>
    </ol>

    <p>Concise writing is not simply short writing. It is writing that preserves what matters and removes what does not.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Removing Unnecessary Details', 15),
  (1934, 1934, '
    <h2>Paraphrasing</h2>
    <p>Paraphrasing means expressing an idea from a source in your own words while preserving its original meaning. It is useful for summaries, notes and explanations.</p>

    <h3>Example</h3>
    <p><strong>Original:</strong> "Heavy rainfall caused the river to overflow, forcing nearby families to leave their homes."</p>
    <p><strong>Paraphrase:</strong> "The river flooded after intense rain, so families living nearby had to evacuate."</p>
    <p>The wording changes, but the key relationship remains: heavy rain caused flooding, which led families to leave.</p>

    <h3>How to paraphrase</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Read the original until you understand the complete idea.</li>
      <li>Identify the essential facts and relationships.</li>
      <li>Look away from the original and explain the idea in your own words.</li>
      <li>Change sentence structure where appropriate, not just individual words.</li>
      <li>Compare your version with the source to check accuracy.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Common mistakes</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Replacing only a few words with synonyms while copying the same structure.</li>
      <li>Changing the meaning or the strength of the original claim.</li>
      <li>Leaving out an essential cause, condition or qualification.</li>
      <li>Adding your own opinion as though it appeared in the source.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Paraphrasing another person''s ideas does not make them your original ideas. In academic work, acknowledge the source when required.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Paraphrasing', 15),
  (1935, 1935, '
    <h2>Combining Related Ideas</h2>
    <p>Combining related ideas means bringing connected details together into a clear statement without losing important information. It helps make summaries shorter and notes easier to revise.</p>

    <h3>Example</h3>
    <p><strong>Original details:</strong></p>
    <ul>
      <li>Regular exercise strengthens muscles.</li>
      <li>Exercise can improve cardiovascular fitness.</li>
      <li>Physical activity can support general well-being.</li>
    </ul>
    <p><strong>Combined idea:</strong> "Regular exercise supports physical health by strengthening muscles, improving cardiovascular fitness and contributing to well-being."</p>

    <h3>Use accurate grouping</h3>
    <p>Details should be combined only when they genuinely belong together. Do not group unrelated facts merely to shorten a passage.</p>

    <h3>Useful connecting words</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>And / as well as:</strong> adds related information.</li>
      <li><strong>Because / since:</strong> connects a reason to a result.</li>
      <li><strong>However / although:</strong> shows contrast.</li>
      <li><strong>Therefore / consequently:</strong> signals a conclusion or result.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Steps</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Identify the main idea shared by the details.</li>
      <li>Group the details that support that idea.</li>
      <li>Choose a suitable connecting structure.</li>
      <li>Write one clear statement that preserves the relationships.</li>
      <li>Check that no important detail has been distorted.</li>
    </ol>

    <p>Combining ideas is more than joining sentences with ''and''. It requires showing how the information fits together.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Combining Related Ideas', 15),
  (1936, 1936, '
    <h2>Concise Summaries</h2>
    <p>A summary presents the main ideas of a longer text in fewer words. It preserves the important meaning while leaving out unnecessary details, repetition and minor examples.</p>

    <h3>Example</h3>
    <p><strong>Original:</strong> "Learners sometimes find it difficult to remember information studied many weeks earlier. Revisiting the material at intervals helps them retrieve it repeatedly. Practising questions also reveals which concepts need more attention."</p>
    <p><strong>Summary:</strong> "Revisiting material over time strengthens recall, while practice questions reveal concepts that need further attention."</p>

    <h3>Steps for writing a summary</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Read the entire passage to understand its central message.</li>
      <li>Identify the main points and essential supporting information.</li>
      <li>Remove repetition, minor examples and irrelevant details.</li>
      <li>Combine related ideas and paraphrase where appropriate.</li>
      <li>Write in a logical order using clear, economical language.</li>
      <li>Check accuracy, grammar and any required word limit.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>What a summary should avoid</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Adding opinions that the source does not express.</li>
      <li>Copying large portions unnecessarily.</li>
      <li>Changing the author''s meaning or certainty.</li>
      <li>Leaving out a central point because a minor detail seems interesting.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>A summary is not simply the first sentence of every paragraph joined together. It should represent the important ideas across the whole text.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Concise Summaries', 15),
  (1937, 1937, '
    <h2>Meaning and Accuracy</h2>
    <p>A summary must preserve the meaning of the original passage. It should shorten the text without changing its facts, relationships, conditions or degree of certainty.</p>

    <h3>Preserve the original meaning</h3>
    <p><strong>Original:</strong> "Some learners improved after attending the extra lessons."</p>
    <p><strong>Inaccurate summary:</strong> "All learners improved because of the extra lessons."</p>
    <p>The summary changes "some" to "all" and claims a cause that the original does not establish.</p>

    <h3>Preserve important qualifications</h3>
    <p><strong>Original:</strong> "The treatment may reduce symptoms in some patients."</p>
    <p><strong>Accurate summary:</strong> "The treatment may ease symptoms for some patients."</p>
    <p><strong>Inaccurate summary:</strong> "The treatment cures everyone."</p>

    <h3>Common accuracy errors</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Changing some people into everyone.</li>
      <li>Turning a possibility into a certainty.</li>
      <li>Confusing a correlation with a proven cause.</li>
      <li>Reversing cause and effect.</li>
      <li>Leaving out a condition that limits a claim.</li>
      <li>Adding personal opinions that were not in the source.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Accuracy check</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Compare each main point with the original passage.</li>
      <li>Check quantities, names, dates and conditions.</li>
      <li>Check words expressing uncertainty, contrast or cause.</li>
      <li>Remove any conclusion that the source does not support.</li>
    </ol>

    <p>A concise summary is useful only if it remains faithful to the original message.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Meaning and Accuracy', 15),
  (1938, 1938, '
    <h2>Word Limits</h2>
    <p>A word limit sets the maximum or required range for a written response. In summary tasks, it tests whether you can select and express important information economically.</p>

    <h3>How to work within a limit</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Read the instruction and identify the exact word limit.</li>
      <li>Extract the points required by the question.</li>
      <li>Combine related points into clear sentences.</li>
      <li>Remove repetition and unnecessary phrases.</li>
      <li>Count the words using the counting method required by the task.</li>
      <li>Check that the summary remains accurate and complete.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Example</h3>
    <p><strong>Wordy:</strong> "Due to the fact that the road was flooded, the bus was unable to continue with its journey."</p>
    <p><strong>Concise:</strong> "The flooded road stopped the bus."</p>
    <p>The shorter version retains the main cause and result.</p>

    <h3>Do not cut essential meaning</h3>
    <p>Removing words is not enough if the result becomes unclear or inaccurate. Preserve important conditions, contrasts, quantities and reasons.</p>

    <h3>Counting words</h3>
    <p>Follow the instructions provided by the examiner or teacher. If no special rule is stated, count the words consistently and leave a small margin below the maximum where possible.</p>

    <p>Never invent a new fact or use an unclear abbreviation simply to reduce the word count.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Word Limits', 15),
  (1939, 1939, '
    <h2>Recording Notes</h2>
    <p>Note-making is the process of recording important information in a short, organised form for later use. Good notes preserve the main ideas without copying every sentence.</p>

    <h3>What to record</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Main ideas and essential supporting details.</li>
      <li>Important definitions, dates, figures and names.</li>
      <li>Causes, effects, comparisons and sequences.</li>
      <li>Questions or points that need clarification.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Use concise wording</h3>
    <p><strong>Full sentence:</strong> "Plants need light to carry out photosynthesis, the process by which they make food."</p>
    <p><strong>Note:</strong> "Photosynthesis: plants use light to make food."</p>
    <p>The note retains the central idea in a shorter form.</p>

    <h3>Useful note-making methods</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Keywords:</strong> record the essential terms.</li>
      <li><strong>Abbreviations:</strong> use familiar short forms consistently.</li>
      <li><strong>Numbered steps:</strong> record a process in sequence.</li>
      <li><strong>Bullets:</strong> separate distinct facts or ideas.</li>
      <li><strong>Symbols:</strong> use simple signs such as arrows to show relationships.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Notes must remain understandable</h3>
    <p>A page full of isolated words may be short but difficult to use. Include enough information to understand how the points connect.</p>

    <ol>
      <li>Identify the topic.</li>
      <li>Record the central points.</li>
      <li>Add essential supporting information.</li>
      <li>Arrange the points in a logical order.</li>
      <li>Review the notes for accuracy and clarity.</li>
    </ol>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Recording Notes', 15),
  (1940, 1940, '
    <h2>Headings and Subheadings</h2>
    <p>Headings and subheadings organise information into sections. A heading names the main topic, while a subheading identifies a smaller part of that topic.</p>

    <h3>Example: Water Conservation</h3>
    <p><strong>Heading:</strong> Water Conservation</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Subheading: Reducing Waste</strong>
        <ul>
          <li>Repair leaking taps.</li>
          <li>Turn off taps when not needed.</li>
        </ul>
      </li>
      <li><strong>Subheading: Collecting Water</strong>
        <ul>
          <li>Collect rainwater for suitable uses.</li>
          <li>Store water safely in covered containers.</li>
        </ul>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Qualities of effective headings</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Specific:</strong> they tell the reader what the section covers.</li>
      <li><strong>Concise:</strong> they avoid unnecessary wording.</li>
      <li><strong>Accurate:</strong> they match the information underneath.</li>
      <li><strong>Consistent:</strong> headings at the same level follow a similar style.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Choosing the right level</h3>
    <p>A broad subject should not be used as a subheading for an unrelated detail. Keep the hierarchy logical: main topic, section, then specific supporting point.</p>

    <h3>How to organise notes</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Identify the overall topic.</li>
      <li>Group related details.</li>
      <li>Give each group a clear subheading.</li>
      <li>Place supporting notes under the appropriate heading.</li>
      <li>Check that the structure reflects the meaning of the information.</li>
    </ol>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Headings and Subheadings', 15),
  (1941, 1941, '
    <h2>Organising Revision Notes</h2>
    <p>Organising revision notes means arranging information so that it is easy to find, understand, practise and review later.</p>

    <h3>Organise by topic</h3>
    <p>Group information under clear subject and topic headings. Keep closely related concepts together while separating ideas that require different explanations.</p>

    <h3>Use a logical structure</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Sequence:</strong> useful for processes and historical events.</li>
      <li><strong>Cause and effect:</strong> useful for explaining why something happens and its consequences.</li>
      <li><strong>Comparison:</strong> useful for similarities and differences.</li>
      <li><strong>Problem and solution:</strong> useful for describing an issue and ways to address it.</li>
      <li><strong>Definition and example:</strong> useful for learning concepts and applying them.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Make notes usable</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Use clear headings and consistent formatting.</li>
      <li>Keep definitions and examples close to the concepts they explain.</li>
      <li>Mark unclear points for later checking.</li>
      <li>Include practice questions that test the exact concepts.</li>
      <li>Correct mistakes rather than preserving inaccurate notes.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Review and update</h3>
    <p>After practising a topic, add missing explanations, correct errors and identify the concepts that still need work. Avoid copying the same material repeatedly without improving its usefulness.</p>

    <p>The goal is not to produce the largest notebook. It is to create reliable notes that make revision more focused and effective.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Organising Revision Notes', 15),
  (1942, 1942, '
    <h2>Analysing a Writing Task</h2>
    <p>Analysing a writing task means identifying exactly what the question requires before planning or writing. It prevents a writer from producing a well-written response that answers the wrong question.</p>

    <h3>Identify the instruction word</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Describe:</strong> give relevant details about a person, place, event or situation.</li>
      <li><strong>Explain:</strong> make an idea clear by showing how or why it happens.</li>
      <li><strong>Discuss:</strong> examine important aspects of an issue, often including different views.</li>
      <li><strong>Compare:</strong> show similarities and, where appropriate, differences.</li>
      <li><strong>Argue:</strong> present a reasoned position supported by evidence.</li>
      <li><strong>Narrate:</strong> tell a connected sequence of events.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Identify the subject and boundaries</h3>
    <p>Ask what the writing must be about, who will read it, what format is required and whether there are length or content restrictions.</p>

    <h3>Example task</h3>
    <p>"Write a composition explaining three ways learners can reduce examination stress."</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Instruction:</strong> explain.</li>
      <li><strong>Topic:</strong> reducing examination stress.</li>
      <li><strong>Required content:</strong> three ways, with explanations.</li>
      <li><strong>Likely structure:</strong> introduction, three developed points and conclusion.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Before you begin</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Underline the instruction word.</li>
      <li>Identify the precise topic.</li>
      <li>List the required points or format.</li>
      <li>Check the audience, purpose and length.</li>
      <li>Plan a response that meets every requirement.</li>
    </ol>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Analysing a Writing Task', 15),
  (1943, 1943, '
    <h2>Brainstorming and Selecting Ideas</h2>
    <p>Brainstorming means generating possible ideas for a writing task before deciding which ones to use. Selecting ideas means choosing those that best answer the task and can be developed clearly.</p>

    <h3>Brainstorming methods</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Quick lists:</strong> write down relevant points as they occur.</li>
      <li><strong>Mind maps:</strong> place the topic at the centre and branch into related ideas.</li>
      <li><strong>Question prompts:</strong> ask who, what, when, where, why and how.</li>
      <li><strong>Pros and cons:</strong> generate arguments on both sides of an issue.</li>
      <li><strong>Personal experience:</strong> identify relevant events or observations when the task allows them.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Example task</h3>
    <p>"Explain how a school can improve its learning environment."</p>
    <p>Possible ideas: improve classroom lighting, provide clean water, maintain sanitation, create quiet study spaces, repair desks and support respectful behaviour.</p>

    <h3>Select the strongest ideas</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Keep ideas that directly answer the task.</li>
      <li>Choose points that can be explained or supported.</li>
      <li>Remove repetition and unrelated ideas.</li>
      <li>Group points that belong together.</li>
      <li>Choose a sensible order for the final composition.</li>
    </ol>

    <p>Generating many ideas is useful only if you later select and organise the most relevant ones.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Brainstorming and Selecting Ideas', 15),
  (1944, 1944, '
    <h2>Organising Ideas into an Outline</h2>
    <p>An outline is a brief plan showing the order and relationship of the main ideas in a piece of writing. It provides a structure before full paragraphs are drafted.</p>

    <h3>A basic composition outline</h3>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Introduction:</strong> introduce the topic and establish the main direction.</li>
      <li><strong>Body point 1:</strong> develop the first major idea.</li>
      <li><strong>Body point 2:</strong> develop the second major idea.</li>
      <li><strong>Body point 3:</strong> develop the third major idea, where appropriate.</li>
      <li><strong>Conclusion:</strong> bring the discussion together without introducing an unrelated major point.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Example outline</h3>
    <p><strong>Task:</strong> Explain why school libraries are valuable.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Introduction: the role of libraries in learning.</li>
      <li>Point 1: access to books and reference materials.</li>
      <li>Point 2: quiet space for reading and revision.</li>
      <li>Point 3: development of independent research skills.</li>
      <li>Conclusion: libraries support learning in several ways.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Check the sequence</h3>
    <p>Arrange ideas so that readers can follow the argument or explanation. Use chronological order for events, logical order for explanations, or the strongest argument at a suitable point in a persuasive composition.</p>

    <h3>Revise the outline before drafting</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Check that every point answers the task.</li>
      <li>Remove duplicates.</li>
      <li>Ensure that each body point can be developed.</li>
      <li>Check that the conclusion follows from the discussion.</li>
    </ol>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Organising Ideas into an Outline', 15),
  (1945, 1945, '
    <h2>Topic Sentences</h2>
    <p>A topic sentence states the main idea of a paragraph. It helps readers understand what the paragraph will develop.</p>

    <h3>Example</h3>
    <p><strong>Topic sentence:</strong> "Regular reading improves learners'' language skills in several ways."</p>
    <p><strong>Supporting details:</strong> Reading exposes learners to new vocabulary, demonstrates correct sentence patterns and develops understanding of different writing styles.</p>
    <p>Each supporting detail explains the main idea introduced by the topic sentence.</p>

    <h3>Qualities of a strong topic sentence</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Clear:</strong> readers can understand the point.</li>
      <li><strong>Focused:</strong> it limits the paragraph to a manageable idea.</li>
      <li><strong>Relevant:</strong> it contributes to the composition''s overall purpose.</li>
      <li><strong>Developable:</strong> it can be explained with reasons, facts or examples.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Too broad or too narrow</h3>
    <p><strong>Too broad:</strong> "Education is important."</p>
    <p>This may cover too many different ideas.</p>
    <p><strong>More focused:</strong> "School libraries help learners develop independent research skills."</p>
    <p>This gives the paragraph a clear direction.</p>

    <h3>Where should it appear?</h3>
    <p>A topic sentence often appears at the beginning of a paragraph, but it may appear later when the paragraph builds towards its main point. The key requirement is that the paragraph has a clear, coherent focus.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Topic Sentences', 15),
  (1946, 1946, '
    <h2>Supporting Sentences</h2>
    <p>Supporting sentences develop a paragraph''s main idea. They may explain a reason, provide evidence, give an example or clarify how something works.</p>

    <h3>Example paragraph</h3>
    <p><strong>Topic sentence:</strong> "A regular revision timetable helps learners manage their study time."</p>
    <p><strong>Supporting sentence 1:</strong> "It allocates time to different subjects before examination dates approach."</p>
    <p><strong>Supporting sentence 2:</strong> "It also helps learners identify when a difficult topic needs additional practice."</p>
    <p><strong>Supporting sentence 3:</strong> "For example, a learner who struggles with algebra can reserve extra sessions for solving equations."</p>
    <p>The supporting sentences explain the benefit and provide a specific illustration.</p>

    <h3>Ways to develop a point</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Explanation:</strong> show why or how the point is true.</li>
      <li><strong>Example:</strong> provide a specific illustration.</li>
      <li><strong>Evidence:</strong> use relevant facts or reliable information.</li>
      <li><strong>Reasoning:</strong> connect the supporting detail to the main claim.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Check each sentence</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Does it develop the topic sentence?</li>
      <li>Does it add useful information rather than repeat the same point?</li>
      <li>Is the example or evidence accurate?</li>
      <li>Does it connect logically to the surrounding sentences?</li>
    </ol>

    <p>A paragraph becomes convincing when its supporting sentences explain the main point clearly instead of merely repeating it.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Supporting Sentences', 15),
  (1947, 1947, '
    <h2>Paragraph Unity</h2>

    <p>A paragraph has unity when every sentence supports one central idea. A sentence that introduces an unrelated idea weakens the paragraph, even when that sentence is grammatically correct.</p>

    <h3>Example: A paragraph with unity</h3>

    <p><strong>Topic: Why students should prepare for examinations early</strong></p>

    <p>Preparing for examinations early helps students learn more effectively. They have enough time to understand difficult concepts instead of memorising answers at the last minute. Early preparation also allows them to identify their weaknesses and seek assistance before the examination. Furthermore, regular revision reduces the pressure that often accompanies last-minute studying. For these reasons, beginning revision early gives students a better opportunity to perform well.</p>

    <p>Every sentence supports the central idea: the benefits of early examination preparation.</p>

    <h3>Example: A sentence that breaks unity</h3>

    <p>Preparing for examinations early helps students understand difficult concepts. They also have enough time to ask teachers for assistance. Many football clubs recruit talented players every year. Regular revision helps students identify topics they have not mastered.</p>

    <p>The sentence about football clubs does not support the paragraph''s main idea. It should be removed or developed in a separate paragraph about football.</p>

    <h3>How to check unity</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Identify the paragraph''s central idea.</li>
      <li>Ask what each sentence contributes to that idea.</li>
      <li>Remove or relocate sentences that do not contribute.</li>
      <li>Check that the remaining sentences develop the same point.</li>
    </ol>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Paragraph Unity', 15),
  (1948, 1948, '
    <h2>Coherence and Cohesion</h2>

    <p><strong>Coherence</strong> means that ideas follow a logical order and make sense together. <strong>Cohesion</strong> means that sentences are connected through language, including linking words, pronouns, and repeated key terms.</p>

    <h3>Example: A coherent and cohesive paragraph</h3>

    <p>Mary wanted to improve her mathematics results, so she created a revision timetable. First, she identified the topics she found difficult. Next, she practised questions on those topics and checked her solutions. Whenever she made a mistake, she studied the relevant method before trying a similar question. As a result, she gradually became more confident in solving problems.</p>

    <p>The ideas follow a logical sequence. Words such as <em>so</em>, <em>first</em>, <em>next</em>, and <em>as a result</em> show the relationships between the ideas.</p>

    <h3>Useful linking expressions</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Addition:</strong> furthermore, moreover, in addition.</li>
      <li><strong>Contrast:</strong> however, although, on the other hand.</li>
      <li><strong>Cause and result:</strong> because, therefore, consequently.</li>
      <li><strong>Sequence:</strong> first, next, afterwards, finally.</li>
      <li><strong>Examples:</strong> for example, for instance.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Repairing a poorly connected paragraph</h3>

    <p><strong>Weak version:</strong> Peter missed the bus. He woke up late. He walked to school. He arrived after the bell.</p>

    <p><strong>Improved version:</strong> Peter woke up late and missed the bus. Consequently, he had to walk to school, so he arrived after the bell.</p>

    <p>Linking expressions should clarify relationships between ideas. Adding words such as <em>however</em> or <em>therefore</em> without checking their meanings can make a paragraph less logical.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Coherence and Cohesion', 15),
  (1949, 1949, '
    <h2>Effective Introductions</h2>

    <p>An introduction opens a composition and prepares the reader for its main subject. A strong introduction attracts interest, establishes the topic, and leads naturally into the main discussion or story.</p>

    <h3>Method 1: Begin with a situation</h3>

    <p><strong>Composition topic:</strong> Describe a day you will never forget.</p>

    <p><strong>Example:</strong> The sky was still dark when my father knocked on my bedroom door. I thought he was waking me for school, but his trembling voice told me that something was wrong. Within minutes, our quiet home had become a place of confusion and fear.</p>

    <p>This opening creates a scene and raises a question in the reader''s mind.</p>

    <h3>Method 2: Introduce a problem</h3>

    <p><strong>Composition topic:</strong> Discuss the effects of examination anxiety on learners.</p>

    <p><strong>Example:</strong> For many learners, examinations are an opportunity to demonstrate what they have learnt. However, excessive anxiety can make it difficult to concentrate, recall information, and answer questions confidently. Understanding the causes and effects of examination anxiety is therefore important for both learners and teachers.</p>

    <p>This introduction identifies the issue and indicates what the composition will discuss.</p>

    <h3>Method 3: Begin with a relevant observation</h3>

    <p><strong>Composition topic:</strong> The importance of reading.</p>

    <p><strong>Example:</strong> A learner who reads widely encounters ideas, experiences, and vocabulary beyond the classroom. Reading can strengthen language skills, broaden knowledge, and encourage independent thinking. These benefits make regular reading an important part of education.</p>

    <h3>Avoid weak openings</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Avoid beginning every composition with “Today I am going to write about...”</li>
      <li>Avoid unrelated quotations or dramatic claims that do not fit the topic.</li>
      <li>Do not explain every argument in the introduction.</li>
      <li>Keep the opening relevant to the assigned task.</li>
    </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Effective Introductions', 15),
  (1950, 1950, '
    <h2>Effective Conclusions</h2>

    <p>A conclusion brings a composition to a satisfying close. It should follow from the ideas already discussed, reinforce the main message, and avoid introducing an entirely new argument.</p>

    <h3>Example: Conclusion for an argumentative composition</h3>

    <p><strong>Topic:</strong> Should learners have a daily reading period?</p>

    <p><strong>Example conclusion:</strong> A daily reading period would help learners develop vocabulary, improve comprehension, and build the habit of independent learning. Although schools must organise the time carefully, the long-term educational benefits make regular reading worth protecting in the timetable.</p>

    <p>The conclusion brings together the main reasons and reinforces the writer''s position.</p>

    <h3>Example: Conclusion for a narrative</h3>

    <p><strong>Topic:</strong> A day I will never forget.</p>

    <p><strong>Example conclusion:</strong> By the time we returned home, the fear of that morning had given way to relief. I had learnt how quickly an ordinary day could change and how important it was to remain calm when trouble arose. Even now, I remember that day whenever I face an unexpected challenge.</p>

    <p>The ending reflects on the experience instead of abruptly stopping the story.</p>

    <h3>Example: Conclusion for an informative composition</h3>

    <p><strong>Topic:</strong> The value of proper waste management.</p>

    <p><strong>Example conclusion:</strong> Proper waste management protects public health, keeps surroundings clean, and reduces environmental damage. When households, schools, and local authorities handle waste responsibly, everyone contributes to a cleaner and safer community.</p>

    <h3>Common conclusion mistakes</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Introducing a new major argument at the very end.</li>
      <li>Repeating the introduction word for word.</li>
      <li>Ending suddenly without completing the discussion or story.</li>
      <li>Using “That is all” in a formal composition.</li>
    </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Effective Conclusions', 15),
  (1951, 1951, '
    <h2>Narrative Composition</h2>

    <p>A narrative composition tells a story about real or imagined events. It may describe a personal experience, an unexpected event, a difficult decision, or a lesson learnt. Strong narratives combine a clear sequence of events with believable characters, setting, conflict, and resolution.</p>

    <h3>A practical narrative structure</h3>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Opening:</strong> Establish the situation, characters, and setting.</li>
      <li><strong>Development:</strong> Show how events unfold and the problem develops.</li>
      <li><strong>Climax:</strong> Present the most tense or significant moment.</li>
      <li><strong>Resolution:</strong> Show how the problem ends and what changes.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Complete model narrative</h3>

    <p><strong>The Stranger at the Bus Stop</strong></p>

    <p>The rain had just begun when I reached the bus stop after school. Most people were hurrying home, protecting their bags beneath their jackets. Near the shelter, an elderly man sat beside a small suitcase, staring anxiously at the road.</p>

    <p>At first, I assumed he was waiting for a bus. Then I noticed that he kept looking at a folded piece of paper and asking passers-by questions. When I approached him, he explained that he had travelled to the area for a hospital appointment but could no longer remember which bus would take him home.</p>

    <p>I read the address on his paper and asked a nearby shopkeeper for directions. She recognised the neighbourhood and explained which route he needed. Unfortunately, the next bus was already full. I decided to wait with him until another one arrived, even though I knew my family would be wondering where I was.</p>

    <p>When the bus finally stopped, I helped him confirm the destination with the conductor. Before boarding, he thanked me and said that he had been afraid of getting stranded in the rain. I watched the bus disappear around the corner before calling home to explain my delay.</p>

    <p>That evening, I realised that helping someone does not always require money or special skills. Sometimes, it simply requires noticing another person''s difficulty and choosing not to walk away.</p>

    <h3>Why the narrative works</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>The rainy bus stop establishes a clear setting.</li>
      <li>The elderly man''s confusion creates a problem.</li>
      <li>The narrator takes practical action, moving the story forward.</li>
      <li>The ending resolves the problem and reflects on the experience.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Writing reminder</h3>
    <p>Keep events in a logical order. Use dialogue when it adds meaning, and choose details that develop the characters or the situation rather than filling the story with unrelated description.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Narrative Composition', 15),
  (1952, 1952, '
    <h2>Descriptive Composition</h2>

    <p>A descriptive composition helps readers imagine a person, place, object, or experience. It uses precise details and sensory language rather than relying on repeated general words such as <em>nice</em>, <em>beautiful</em>, or <em>bad</em>.</p>

    <h3>Using sensory details</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Sight:</strong> colour, shape, movement, light, and distance.</li>
      <li><strong>Sound:</strong> whispers, footsteps, whistles, or rumbling engines.</li>
      <li><strong>Smell:</strong> fresh bread, damp earth, smoke, or flowers.</li>
      <li><strong>Touch:</strong> rough bark, cool stone, or warm sunlight.</li>
      <li><strong>Taste:</strong> use only when relevant to the scene.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Complete model description</h3>

    <p><strong>Market Day in Our Town</strong></p>

    <p>By seven o''clock, the town market was already awake. Traders arranged tomatoes in bright red piles while green vegetables filled baskets along the narrow paths. Yellow bananas hung from wooden stalls, and sacks of beans stood in neat rows beside heaps of onions.</p>

    <p>The air carried a mixture of ripe fruit, fresh coriander, dust, and smoke from a nearby food stall. Traders called out their prices, customers bargained over the cost of vegetables, and metal weighing scales clicked against their trays. Every few moments, someone squeezed through the crowd carrying a heavy basket or pushing a handcart.</p>

    <p>At the centre of the market, an old woman carefully selected mangoes, turning each fruit in her hands before placing it in her bag. Nearby, a young boy wiped rainwater from a plastic table while his mother served steaming mandazi. The smell made several passers-by stop and look towards the stall.</p>

    <p>Although the market was crowded and noisy, it had an organised rhythm. Each trader seemed to know what needed to be done, and every path carried its own small activity. By the time the sun climbed higher, the market had become a lively picture of work, conversation, and community life.</p>

    <h3>Why the description works</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Specific colours and objects help readers picture the scene.</li>
      <li>Sounds and smells make the setting more vivid.</li>
      <li>People''s actions make the market feel active rather than static.</li>
      <li>The details remain focused on the market and its atmosphere.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>A useful revision test</h3>
    <p>Replace vague statements with observable details. Instead of writing “The market was nice,” describe what a visitor could see, hear, smell, or notice there.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Descriptive Composition', 15),
  (1953, 1953, '
    <h2>Expository Composition</h2>

    <p>An expository composition explains a topic clearly. It may describe a process, explain causes and effects, compare ideas, or provide information. Its main purpose is to help readers understand rather than simply entertain them or persuade them to accept an opinion.</p>

    <h3>How to organise an expository composition</h3>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Introduction:</strong> Introduce the topic and establish what will be explained.</li>
      <li><strong>Body paragraphs:</strong> Explain one main point at a time, using examples or relevant facts.</li>
      <li><strong>Conclusion:</strong> Bring together the explanation''s key points.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Complete model composition</h3>

    <p><strong>How Regular Revision Improves Learning</strong></p>

    <p>Regular revision helps learners strengthen their understanding of subjects they study in class. Rather than waiting until examinations approach, learners who review material consistently have more opportunities to discover what they understand and what still needs attention.</p>

    <p>One important benefit of revision is that it helps learners identify gaps in their knowledge. For example, a learner may understand how to solve a simple equation but struggle when the equation contains brackets. By attempting questions and checking each step, the learner can identify the exact difficulty instead of assuming that the entire topic is confusing.</p>

    <p>Revision also provides opportunities to practise applying knowledge. Reading notes may help a learner recognise a method, but solving a question independently shows whether the learner can use that method. When an answer is incorrect, studying the solution and attempting a similar question can help correct the misunderstanding.</p>

    <p>Another benefit is improved organisation. A realistic timetable spreads revision across several days and helps learners avoid relying entirely on last-minute study. Short, focused sessions can also make it easier to return to difficult material regularly.</p>

    <p>In conclusion, regular revision supports understanding, reveals weaknesses, and provides opportunities for independent practice. When learners combine reviewing their notes with answering questions and correcting errors, revision becomes a more purposeful part of learning.</p>

    <h3>Why this composition is expository</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>It explains a topic instead of telling a personal story.</li>
      <li>Each body paragraph develops a distinct reason or benefit.</li>
      <li>Examples clarify the explanation.</li>
      <li>The conclusion summarises the key points.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Use reliable information and relevant examples. Do not invent statistics or present an unsupported claim as an established fact.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Expository Composition', 15),
  (1954, 1954, '
    <h2>Argumentative Composition</h2>

    <p>An argumentative composition presents a clear position on a debatable issue and supports it with reasons, examples, and relevant evidence. Strong arguments explain why the evidence supports the position instead of merely repeating an opinion.</p>

    <h3>Building a strong argument</h3>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Identify the issue:</strong> Understand exactly what the question asks you to debate.</li>
      <li><strong>State your position:</strong> Make your main claim clear.</li>
      <li><strong>Develop reasons:</strong> Explain the main reasons supporting your position.</li>
      <li><strong>Support your reasons:</strong> Use relevant examples, evidence, and explanations.</li>
      <li><strong>Consider an opposing view:</strong> Represent it fairly and explain your response.</li>
      <li><strong>Conclude:</strong> Reinforce your position based on the argument developed.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Complete model composition</h3>

    <p><strong>Should Schools Provide Time for Daily Reading?</strong></p>

    <p>Schools should provide a regular period for independent reading because strong reading skills support learning across many subjects. Although classroom lessons introduce important knowledge, learners also need opportunities to read independently, develop vocabulary, and understand different kinds of texts.</p>

    <p>First, regular reading gives learners repeated exposure to words and sentence structures. A learner who reads a variety of well-written texts can encounter unfamiliar vocabulary in context and observe how ideas are organised. These experiences can support comprehension and improve the learner''s own writing.</p>

    <p>Second, independent reading encourages learners to take responsibility for their learning. When learners choose suitable books and read consistently, they practise concentrating on a text, following an argument, and identifying its main ideas. These skills are useful beyond English lessons.</p>

    <p>Some people may argue that the school timetable is already crowded and that an additional reading period would reduce time for examinable subjects. This concern deserves attention. However, a carefully planned reading period need not replace essential lessons; schools can organise it within existing library or reading programmes and select materials that support the curriculum.</p>

    <p>In conclusion, a regular reading period can strengthen comprehension, expand vocabulary, and encourage independent learning. If schools organise it sensibly and provide suitable reading materials, the benefits can justify making time for this activity.</p>

    <h3>Why this argument works</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>The writer states a clear position.</li>
      <li>Each body paragraph develops a reason.</li>
      <li>The opposing view is acknowledged rather than ignored.</li>
      <li>The response addresses the concern without insulting those who disagree.</li>
      <li>The conclusion follows from the reasons already discussed.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Evidence matters</h3>
    <p>Use relevant facts, credible sources, examples, or sound reasoning. Do not invent survey results or statistics to make an argument appear stronger. Distinguish clearly between evidence, assumptions, and personal opinions.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Argumentative Composition', 15),
  (1955, 1955, '
    <h2>Revising Ideas and Structure</h2>

    <p>Revision means improving the content, organisation, and effectiveness of a draft. It is different from proofreading: revision asks whether the writing works, while proofreading checks smaller errors such as spelling and punctuation.</p>

    <h3>Example: Revising a weak paragraph</h3>

    <p><strong>First draft:</strong></p>

    <p>Our school should have more trees. Trees are nice. We have a football field. Trees give shade. Some learners like football. Trees can make the school look better.</p>

    <p><strong>Revised version:</strong></p>

    <p>Our school should plant more trees because they would make the compound more comfortable and attractive. During hot weather, trees would provide shade for learners who need a place to rest between lessons. They could also improve the appearance of the school and create a more pleasant environment. With careful planning, trees could be planted along suitable paths and open areas without interfering with the football field.</p>

    <h3>What changed?</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Focus:</strong> The revised paragraph consistently supports planting trees.</li>
      <li><strong>Development:</strong> Each benefit is explained instead of merely named.</li>
      <li><strong>Organisation:</strong> Ideas follow a sensible order.</li>
      <li><strong>Specificity:</strong> The paragraph explains where trees might be planted.</li>
      <li><strong>Relevance:</strong> The football field is included only where it relates to planning.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>A revision sequence</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Check whether the draft answers the writing task.</li>
      <li>Check whether the main position, purpose, or story is clear.</li>
      <li>Reorder ideas and paragraphs where necessary.</li>
      <li>Remove repetition and irrelevant details.</li>
      <li>Add explanations or examples where ideas are underdeveloped.</li>
      <li>Read the revised draft again before editing individual sentences.</li>
    </ol>

    <p>Revision can involve moving, adding, removing, or rewriting whole sentences and paragraphs. Do not assume that a draft is good simply because its grammar is correct.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Revising Ideas and Structure', 15),
  (1956, 1956, '
    <h2>Editing Grammar and Word Choice</h2>

    <p>Editing improves sentence-level accuracy and clarity. It involves checking grammar, verb forms, agreement, punctuation, spelling, word choice, and sentence construction while preserving the intended meaning.</p>

    <h3>Example: Editing a draft</h3>

    <p><strong>Unedited version:</strong></p>

    <p>Last week, our class visit a farm near town. The farmer explain how crops grows and why farmers needs to protect the soil. We was surprised by the number of activities that take place before food reaches the market.</p>

    <p><strong>Edited version:</strong></p>

    <p>Last week, our class visited a farm near town. The farmer explained how crops grow and why farmers need to protect the soil. We were surprised by the number of activities that take place before food reaches the market.</p>

    <h3>What was corrected?</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>visit → visited:</strong> The visit happened last week, so the past tense is required.</li>
      <li><strong>explain → explained:</strong> The verb must agree with the past-time context.</li>
      <li><strong>crops grows → crops grow:</strong> The plural subject <em>crops</em> takes <em>grow</em>.</li>
      <li><strong>farmers needs → farmers need:</strong> The plural subject <em>farmers</em> takes <em>need</em>.</li>
      <li><strong>We was → We were:</strong> The subject <em>we</em> takes <em>were</em> in standard English.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Choose precise words</h3>

    <p><strong>Weak:</strong> The speaker said many good things about protecting the environment.</p>

    <p><strong>More precise:</strong> The speaker explained how reducing plastic waste can protect rivers and wildlife.</p>

    <p>The second sentence communicates more clearly because it identifies the speaker''s message instead of relying on vague words such as <em>good things</em>.</p>

    <h3>Final editing checklist</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Are the verb tenses consistent with the intended time?</li>
      <li>Do subjects and verbs agree?</li>
      <li>Are pronouns clear and correctly matched to their referents?</li>
      <li>Are words used accurately and appropriately?</li>
      <li>Are spelling, capitalisation, and punctuation correct?</li>
      <li>Can any sentence be made clearer without changing its meaning?</li>
    </ol>

    <p>After editing, proofread the complete piece once more. Editing improves the writing; it should not introduce a new meaning that the writer did not intend.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Editing Grammar and Word Choice', 15),
  (1957, 1957, '
    <h2>Proofreading</h2>

    <p>Proofreading is the final check of a piece of writing before it is submitted or shared. It focuses on errors in spelling, punctuation, grammar, missing words, and formatting.</p>

    <h3>Example: Proofread a paragraph</h3>

    <p><strong>Before proofreading:</strong></p>
    <p>last monday our class visited the national museum in nairobi we learnt about kenya''s history and saw many interesting exibits our teacher said the visit would help us understand what we read in class</p>

    <p><strong>After proofreading:</strong></p>
    <p>Last Monday, our class visited the National Museum in Nairobi. We learnt about Kenya''s history and saw many interesting exhibits. Our teacher said the visit would help us understand what we read in class.</p>

    <h3>Corrections made</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Capital letters:</strong> Capitalised the beginning of the paragraph and proper names.</li>
      <li><strong>Punctuation:</strong> Divided the run-on text into complete sentences.</li>
      <li><strong>Spelling:</strong> Corrected <em>exibits</em> to <em>exhibits</em>.</li>
      <li><strong>Meaning:</strong> Preserved the writer''s original message.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>A reliable proofreading method</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Read slowly, sentence by sentence.</li>
      <li>Check capital letters, full stops, commas, and other punctuation.</li>
      <li>Check spelling, verb forms, and subject–verb agreement.</li>
      <li>Look for missing or repeated words.</li>
      <li>Read the complete piece once more to confirm that it makes sense.</li>
    </ol>

    <p>Proofreading is not the same as rewriting the entire composition. Make corrections that improve accuracy while preserving the intended meaning.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Proofreading', 15),
  (1958, 1958, '
    <h2>Informal Letters</h2>

    <p>An informal letter is written to someone with whom the writer has a personal relationship, such as a friend, cousin, or relative. Its language is warm and natural, but it should still be clear, organised, and correctly punctuated.</p>

    <h3>Common parts of an informal letter</h3>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Writer''s address:</strong> Usually placed at the top right.</li>
      <li><strong>Date:</strong> Written below the address.</li>
      <li><strong>Salutation:</strong> For example, Dear Amina,</li>
      <li><strong>Opening:</strong> Greet the recipient and establish the purpose.</li>
      <li><strong>Body:</strong> Develop the message in clear paragraphs.</li>
      <li><strong>Closing phrase:</strong> For example, Best wishes, or Your friend,</li>
      <li><strong>Name:</strong> The writer signs with their first name or familiar name.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Complete model letter</h3>

    <p><strong>14 Green Road<br/>Embu<br/>10 October 2026</strong></p>

    <p>Dear Amina,</p>

    <p>How are you? I hope you and your family are doing well. It has been a while since we last spoke, so I thought I would write and tell you about life at my new school.</p>

    <p>At first, I found it difficult to adjust because I did not know many people. Fortunately, two classmates helped me find my way around the school and understand the timetable. I have also joined the science club, where we carry out simple experiments and discuss how scientific ideas apply to everyday life.</p>

    <p>My studies are going well, although mathematics still requires extra practice. I have started setting aside thirty minutes each evening to review difficult questions. If you have any revision methods that work for you, please share them with me.</p>

    <p>I would love to hear how you are getting on. Please greet your family for me, and write back when you have time.</p>

    <p>Best wishes,</p>

    <p>Mary</p>

    <h3>Why this letter works</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>It follows a recognisable letter format.</li>
      <li>The tone is friendly and suitable for a friend.</li>
      <li>Each paragraph has a clear purpose.</li>
      <li>The writer invites a reply naturally.</li>
    </ul>

    <p><strong>Important:</strong> In an examination, use the address, date, and sign-off format required by the question or the format taught in class. The example above is a model, not the only acceptable wording.</p>
  
<hr>

    <h2>Informal Letters</h2>

    <p>An informal letter is written to someone you know personally, such as a friend, relative or classmate. Its language is friendly and natural, but the message should remain clear and organised.</p>

    <h3>Structure</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Writer''s address</li>
      <li>Date</li>
      <li>Greeting</li>
      <li>Opening paragraph</li>
      <li>Main message</li>
      <li>Closing paragraph</li>
      <li>Friendly sign-off and name</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Complete example</h3>

    <div class="writing-example">
      <p>P.O. Box 124,</p>
      <p>Embu.</p>
      <p>10 October 2026</p>

      <p>Dear Brian,</p>

      <p>How are you? I hope you and your family are doing well. It has been a while since we last met, so I decided to write and tell you about my new school.</p>

      <p>The school has a well-equipped library and a science laboratory. I have also joined the debate club, where we practise speaking confidently and defending our opinions. Although the workload is sometimes demanding, I am learning how to organise my time better.</p>

      <p>I would love you to visit during the December holidays. We could catch up and visit some of the interesting places around town. Please write back and tell me how things are going at your end.</p>

      <p>Your friend,</p>
      <p>Kevin</p>
    </div>

    <h3>What makes it effective?</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>The greeting establishes a friendly relationship.</li>
      <li>The opening reconnects with the reader.</li>
      <li>The body develops the main message with specific details.</li>
      <li>The closing gives the reader a reason to respond.</li>
    </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Informal Letters', 15),
  (1959, 1959, '
    <h2>Formal Letters</h2>

    <p>A formal letter is written for an official purpose, such as making a complaint, requesting information, applying for permission, or communicating with an organisation. It uses respectful, direct language and a clear structure.</p>

    <h3>Common parts of a formal letter</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Writer''s address and date.</li>
      <li>Recipient''s name or title and address, where known.</li>
      <li>A suitable reference or subject line, when appropriate.</li>
      <li>Formal salutation, such as Dear Sir or Madam.</li>
      <li>An opening paragraph stating the purpose.</li>
      <li>Body paragraphs explaining the details and requested action.</li>
      <li>A courteous closing and the writer''s name.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Complete model letter: Complaint</h3>

    <p><strong>P.O. Box 214<br/>Embu<br/>10 October 2026</strong></p>

    <p><strong>The Customer Service Manager<br/>Eastern Water Services<br/>Embu</strong></p>

    <p><strong>Dear Sir or Madam,</strong></p>

    <p><strong>RE: REQUEST FOR INVESTIGATION INTO INTERRUPTED WATER SUPPLY</strong></p>

    <p>I am writing to report the frequent interruption of water supply in our neighbourhood over the past two weeks. The supply has been unavailable on several days, making it difficult for residents to carry out essential household activities.</p>

    <p>Residents have had to obtain water from alternative sources, which can be inconvenient and costly. We would appreciate an explanation of the cause of these interruptions and information about when a reliable supply is expected to resume.</p>

    <p>Please investigate the matter and advise residents of any planned maintenance or other measures being taken to address the problem. I would be grateful for a response at your earliest convenience.</p>

    <p>Thank you for your attention to this matter.</p>

    <p>Yours faithfully,</p>

    <p>Mary Wanjiku</p>

    <h3>Why this letter works</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>The subject line identifies the issue.</li>
      <li>The opening explains why the writer is contacting the organisation.</li>
      <li>The body provides relevant details and asks for specific action.</li>
      <li>The tone remains respectful, even though the writer is complaining.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Use <em>Yours faithfully</em> when addressing an unknown recipient as Dear Sir or Madam. If you address a named recipient as Dear Mr Otieno or Dear Ms Kamau, <em>Yours sincerely</em> is the conventional closing in British-style formal correspondence.</p>
  
<hr>

    <h2>Formal Letters</h2>

    <p>A formal letter communicates with an organisation, official or person in a professional capacity. It should be polite, direct and organised. Avoid slang and unnecessary personal details.</p>

    <h3>Structure</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Writer''s address</li>
      <li>Date</li>
      <li>Recipient''s name or title and address</li>
      <li>Salutation</li>
      <li>Clear subject line</li>
      <li>Opening paragraph stating the purpose</li>
      <li>Body paragraphs explaining relevant details</li>
      <li>Polite closing and signature</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Complete example</h3>

    <div class="writing-example">
      <p>P.O. Box 124,</p>
      <p>Embu.</p>
      <p>10 October 2026</p>

      <p>The Manager,</p>
      <p>Embu Community Library,</p>
      <p>P.O. Box 450,</p>
      <p>Embu.</p>

      <p>Dear Sir/Madam,</p>

      <p><strong>RE: REQUEST TO USE THE COMMUNITY HALL</strong></p>

      <p>I am writing on behalf of the students'' reading club at our school to request permission to use your community hall on 24 October 2026 from 9.00 a.m. to 12.00 noon.</p>

      <p>We intend to hold a reading and public-speaking workshop for approximately forty students. The activity will help participants develop their reading habits and communication skills. Our teacher will supervise the event, and we will ensure that the hall is left clean and orderly.</p>

      <p>We would appreciate confirmation of the hall''s availability and any applicable conditions or charges. Thank you for considering our request.</p>

      <p>Yours faithfully,</p>
      <p><em>[Signature]</em></p>
      <p>Kevin Mwangi</p>
      <p>Reading Club Secretary</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Important distinction</h3>
    <p>Use <strong>Yours faithfully</strong> when the letter begins with a general greeting such as Dear Sir/Madam. In traditional British-style formal correspondence, <strong>Yours sincerely</strong> is used when the recipient is addressed by name.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Formal Letters', 15),
  (1960, 1960, '
    <h2>Application Letters</h2>

    <p>An application letter is a formal letter written to request consideration for an opportunity, such as a job, internship, scholarship, or school position. It explains what the applicant is applying for and presents relevant qualifications honestly.</p>

    <h3>What to include</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>The position or opportunity being requested.</li>
      <li>Where the applicant learnt about the opportunity, if relevant.</li>
      <li>Relevant education, skills, experience, or achievements.</li>
      <li>A clear explanation of suitability.</li>
      <li>A polite closing and reference to any required attachments.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Complete model application letter</h3>

    <p><strong>P.O. Box 118<br/>Embu<br/>10 October 2026</strong></p>

    <p><strong>The Manager<br/>Green Valley Bookshop<br/>Embu</strong></p>

    <p><strong>Dear Sir or Madam,</strong></p>

    <p><strong>RE: APPLICATION FOR A PART-TIME SHOP ASSISTANT POSITION</strong></p>

    <p>I am writing to apply for the part-time shop assistant position advertised on your noticeboard. I am currently a secondary-school leaver seeking an opportunity to develop my customer service and organisational skills while contributing to your business.</p>

    <p>During my time at school, I developed good communication skills through group assignments and school activities. I am comfortable organising materials, following instructions, working carefully with numbers, and cooperating with others. I am also willing to learn your procedures for assisting customers and arranging stock.</p>

    <p>I would approach the role with punctuality, honesty, and a willingness to learn. I understand that assisting customers requires patience, clear communication, and respect for different needs.</p>

    <p>I would welcome an opportunity to discuss my application. I have attached my curriculum vitae for your consideration and would be grateful if you considered me for an interview.</p>

    <p>Yours faithfully,</p>

    <p>Daniel Mwangi</p>

    <h3>Why this application is effective</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>It identifies the specific position.</li>
      <li>It connects the applicant''s real skills to the role.</li>
      <li>It avoids unsupported claims and invented work experience.</li>
      <li>It requests a next step politely.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Never claim qualifications or experience you do not have. Use the recipient''s name and details when they are provided, and include only attachments requested or relevant to the opportunity.</p>
  
<hr>

    <h2>Application Letters</h2>

    <p>An application letter is a formal letter in which a person applies for a job, attachment, scholarship or another opportunity. It should identify the opportunity, explain relevant qualifications and request consideration.</p>

    <h3>Complete example</h3>

    <div class="writing-example">
      <p>P.O. Box 124,</p>
      <p>Embu.</p>
      <p>10 October 2026</p>

      <p>The Human Resource Manager,</p>
      <p>Greenfields Bookshop,</p>
      <p>P.O. Box 610,</p>
      <p>Embu.</p>

      <p>Dear Sir/Madam,</p>

      <p><strong>RE: APPLICATION FOR THE POSITION OF SHOP ASSISTANT</strong></p>

      <p>I am writing to apply for the advertised position of shop assistant at your bookshop. I am a responsible and organised student who is interested in gaining practical experience in customer service and stock management.</p>

      <p>Through school activities, I have developed communication, teamwork and record-keeping skills. I can follow instructions carefully, handle assigned tasks responsibly and work cooperatively with others. I am also willing to learn your procedures for serving customers and arranging stock.</p>

      <p>I would welcome an opportunity to discuss my suitability for the position. I am available for an interview at your convenience and can provide further information if required.</p>

      <p>Thank you for considering my application.</p>

      <p>Yours faithfully,</p>
      <p><em>[Signature]</em></p>
      <p>Kevin Mwangi</p>
      <p>Telephone: 07XX XXX XXX</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Why this application works</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>The subject identifies the position.</li>
      <li>The applicant gives relevant skills rather than unsupported claims.</li>
      <li>The final paragraph requests a next step politely.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Only include qualifications, experience and personal details that are true. Follow the employer''s instructions about attachments and required documents.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Application Letters', 15),
  (1961, 1961, '
    <h2>Emails and Digital Messages</h2>

    <p>Emails and digital messages communicate information electronically. Their tone, structure, and level of detail depend on the recipient and purpose. A message to a teacher about an assignment should normally be more formal than a message to a close friend.</p>

    <h3>Parts of a formal email</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>To:</strong> The recipient''s email address.</li>
      <li><strong>Subject:</strong> A short description of the purpose.</li>
      <li><strong>Greeting:</strong> For example, Dear Mr Otieno,</li>
      <li><strong>Message:</strong> A clear explanation or request.</li>
      <li><strong>Closing:</strong> For example, Kind regards, followed by the sender''s name.</li>
      <li><strong>Attachment:</strong> Include relevant files when needed and mention them in the message.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Complete model formal email</h3>

    <p><strong>To:</strong> teacher@example.com</p>
    <p><strong>Subject:</strong> Request for Clarification on Mathematics Assignment</p>

    <p>Dear Mr Otieno,</p>

    <p>I hope you are well. I am writing to ask for clarification about Question 4 of the mathematics assignment issued yesterday.</p>

    <p>I understand the first two steps of the solution, but I am unsure why the sign changes when the expression is rearranged. I have reviewed my notes, but I would appreciate it if you could explain that step during our next lesson.</p>

    <p>Thank you for your time and assistance.</p>

    <p>Kind regards,<br/>Brian Kamau</p>

    <h3>Complete model informal digital message</h3>

    <p><strong>To:</strong> Amina</p>

    <p>Hi Amina, are we still meeting at the library at 3 p.m. to revise chemistry? I have finished the notes on acids and bases and can bring a few practice questions. Let me know if the time has changed.</p>

    <h3>Choosing the right style</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Use a meaningful subject line for important emails.</li>
      <li>Keep the message focused on its purpose.</li>
      <li>Use respectful language when contacting teachers, employers, or officials.</li>
      <li>Check the recipient''s address before sending.</li>
      <li>Do not share private information or forward other people''s messages without appropriate permission.</li>
    </ul>
  
<hr>

    <h2>Emails and Digital Messages</h2>

    <p>A professional email communicates a message electronically. It should have a useful subject, an appropriate greeting, a clear body and a suitable sign-off. Digital messages can be shorter, but their tone should match the recipient and purpose.</p>

    <h3>Complete email example</h3>

    <div class="writing-example">
      <p><strong>To:</strong> english.teacher@example.com</p>
      <p><strong>From:</strong> kevin.mwangi@example.com</p>
      <p><strong>Date:</strong> 10 October 2026</p>
      <p><strong>Subject:</strong> Request for Feedback on the Composition</p>

      <p>Dear Mr Otieno,</p>

      <p>I hope you are well. I am writing to ask whether you could provide feedback on the composition I submitted on Thursday.</p>

      <p>I would particularly appreciate advice on paragraph organisation and the use of supporting evidence. I would like to understand the areas I should improve before the next assignment.</p>

      <p>Please let me know if I should visit you during a particular time. Thank you for your guidance.</p>

      <p>Kind regards,</p>
      <p>Kevin Mwangi</p>
      <p>Form Three</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Before sending</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Check the recipient''s address.</li>
      <li>Use a subject that states the purpose.</li>
      <li>Keep paragraphs focused.</li>
      <li>Check spelling, attachments and privacy.</li>
      <li>Use a professional sign-off when writing to a teacher or employer.</li>
    </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Emails and Digital Messages', 15),
  (1962, 1962, '
    <h2>Curriculum Vitae (CV)</h2>

    <p>A curriculum vitae (CV) summarises a person''s education, relevant skills, experience, and achievements for an application. A good CV is accurate, easy to scan, and tailored to the opportunity. A school leaver can prepare a useful CV without inventing employment experience.</p>

    <h3>Common sections</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Contact details:</strong> Name, phone number, professional email address, and location as appropriate.</li>
      <li><strong>Personal profile:</strong> A short, relevant summary.</li>
      <li><strong>Education:</strong> Schools or institutions and dates, where relevant.</li>
      <li><strong>Skills:</strong> Abilities connected to the opportunity.</li>
      <li><strong>Experience:</strong> Employment, volunteering, projects, or relevant responsibilities.</li>
      <li><strong>Achievements and interests:</strong> Include only relevant, truthful information.</li>
      <li><strong>Referees:</strong> Include them when requested, with their permission.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Complete model CV for a school leaver</h3>

    <p><strong>BRIAN KAMAU</strong><br/>
    Embu, Kenya<br/>
    Phone: 07XX XXX XXX<br/>
    Email: briankamau@example.com</p>

    <h3>Personal Profile</h3>

    <p>A reliable and motivated school leaver with good communication, organisation, and basic computer skills. Willing to learn, follow instructions, and contribute positively to a team.</p>

    <h3>Education</h3>

    <p><strong>Embu Day Secondary School</strong><br/>
    Secondary education, completed 2025</p>

    <h3>Skills</h3>

    <ul>
      <li>Clear written and verbal communication.</li>
      <li>Basic computer use, including document preparation.</li>
      <li>Organisation of materials and tasks.</li>
      <li>Teamwork, punctuality, and willingness to learn.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Relevant Experience</h3>

    <p><strong>School Project Team Member</strong><br/>
    Worked with classmates to organise project materials, share responsibilities, and prepare a group presentation.</p>

    <h3>Interests</h3>

    <p>Reading, learning digital skills, and participating in community activities.</p>

    <h3>Referees</h3>

    <p>Available upon request.</p>

    <p><strong>Note:</strong> This is a fictional model. Replace the sample details with truthful information. Do not use another person''s identity, claim qualifications you do not hold, or include a referee''s contact details without permission. In many applications, a CV should be adapted to the job rather than sent unchanged every time.</p>
  
<hr>

    <h2>Curriculum Vitae (CV)</h2>

    <p>A CV summarises a person''s education, skills, experience and relevant achievements. It should be truthful, easy to scan and tailored to the opportunity. A student with little employment history can include school responsibilities, volunteering and relevant projects.</p>

    <h3>Complete example</h3>

    <div class="writing-example">
      <h3>KEVIN MWANGI</h3>
      <p>Embu, Kenya</p>
      <p>Telephone: 07XX XXX XXX</p>
      <p>Email: kevin.mwangi@example.com</p>

      <h4>PERSONAL PROFILE</h4>
      <p>A reliable and organised student seeking a part-time shop assistant opportunity. Interested in customer service, teamwork and learning practical workplace skills.</p>

      <h4>EDUCATION</h4>
      <p><strong>Embu Secondary School</strong></p>
      <p>Currently studying: Form Three</p>

      <h4>KEY SKILLS</h4>
      <ul>
        <li>Clear verbal and written communication</li>
        <li>Basic record keeping and organisation</li>
        <li>Teamwork and time management</li>
        <li>Willingness to learn new procedures</li>
      </ul>

      <h4>RELEVANT EXPERIENCE</h4>
      <p><strong>School Reading Club — Student Volunteer</strong></p>
      <ul>
        <li>Helped organise reading materials for club sessions.</li>
        <li>Worked with other members to prepare activities.</li>
        <li>Assisted with keeping shared materials in order.</li>
      </ul>

      <h4>ACHIEVEMENTS AND ACTIVITIES</h4>
      <p>Participates in school reading club activities and group assignments.</p>

      <h4>REFEREES</h4>
      <p>Available on request, if appropriate for the application.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>CV quality checks</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Use clear headings and consistent formatting.</li>
      <li>Place the most relevant information where it is easy to find.</li>
      <li>Do not invent qualifications, jobs or achievements.</li>
      <li>Include referees or other personal details when required, rather than automatically adding unnecessary information.</li>
    </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Curriculum Vitae (CV)', 15),
  (1963, 1963, '
    <h2>Application Forms</h2>

    <p>An application form collects information in a structured format. It may be used for a job, school admission, a scholarship, a club, or another service. Unlike a letter, a form normally provides labelled spaces for specific answers.</p>

    <h3>How to complete a form accurately</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Read all instructions before filling in any section.</li>
      <li>Enter names and contact details exactly as required.</li>
      <li>Use the requested date format and units.</li>
      <li>Answer every required question truthfully.</li>
      <li>Write N/A only when the instructions permit it and the question genuinely does not apply.</li>
      <li>Check spelling, figures, attachments, signatures, and required declarations before submitting.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Worked example: Fictional club application form</h3>

    <p><strong>Riverside School Science Club — Membership Application</strong></p>

    <p><strong>Full name:</strong> Brian Kamau</p>
    <p><strong>Class:</strong> Form 3</p>
    <p><strong>Preferred contact:</strong> School email</p>
    <p><strong>Reason for joining:</strong> To develop practical science skills and participate in experiments.</p>
    <p><strong>Relevant experience:</strong> Participated in a classroom science project.</p>
    <p><strong>Availability:</strong> Thursday afternoons, subject to the school timetable.</p>
    <p><strong>Applicant''s declaration:</strong> I confirm that the information provided is accurate.</p>
    <p><strong>Signature:</strong> Brian Kamau</p>
    <p><strong>Date:</strong> 10 October 2026</p>

    <h3>Handling difficult fields</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Experience:</strong> Describe real activities, even if they occurred at school or in a community group.</li>
      <li><strong>Dates:</strong> Follow the form''s specified format.</li>
      <li><strong>Required fields:</strong> Do not leave them blank without checking the instructions.</li>
      <li><strong>Declarations:</strong> Read them carefully before signing.</li>
      <li><strong>Privacy:</strong> Provide sensitive personal information only when it is necessary and the recipient is legitimate.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>The form above is a fictional teaching example. Real forms may use different fields and requirements, so always follow the instructions on the actual form.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Application Forms', 15),
  (1964, 1964, '
    <h2>Report Structure</h2>

    <p>A report presents information about an event, investigation, activity or situation in a clear, organised and factual way.</p>

    <h3>Main parts of a report</h3>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Title:</strong> States what the report is about.</li>
      <li><strong>Introduction:</strong> Explains the purpose and scope of the report.</li>
      <li><strong>Findings:</strong> Presents the information discovered, preferably under clear headings.</li>
      <li><strong>Conclusion:</strong> Summarises what the findings show.</li>
      <li><strong>Recommendations:</strong> Suggests practical action where appropriate.</li>
      <li><strong>Name and date:</strong> Identifies the writer and when the report was prepared, when required.</li>
    </ol>

    <p>The structure depends on the assignment. A short report may combine sections, while a formal investigation may require all of them. Reports should distinguish observed facts from opinions and recommendations.</p>

    <h3>Model report</h3>

    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:14px;">
      <h3 style="text-align:center;">REPORT ON THE CONDITION OF THE SCHOOL LIBRARY</h3>
      <p><strong>Prepared by:</strong> Library Prefect</p>
      <p><strong>Date:</strong> 12 September 2026</p>

      <h4>1. Introduction</h4>
      <p>This report presents the findings of an inspection of the school library conducted on 10 September 2026. The inspection focused on the condition of books, furniture and reading space.</p>

      <h4>2. Findings</h4>
      <p>Several textbooks were damaged, and some books were not arranged according to subject. Two reading tables had loose legs. The room was generally clean, although the number of chairs was insufficient during busy study periods.</p>

      <h4>3. Conclusion</h4>
      <p>The library provides a useful study space, but damaged materials and inadequate seating limit its effectiveness.</p>

      <h4>4. Recommendations</h4>
      <p>The school should repair the tables, replace severely damaged books, label the shelves and provide additional chairs.</p>

      <p><strong>Signed:</strong> Library Prefect</p>
    </div>

    <h3>What makes this report effective?</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>The title identifies the subject.</li>
      <li>The introduction explains why the report was written.</li>
      <li>The findings give specific observations rather than unsupported complaints.</li>
      <li>The conclusion follows from the findings.</li>
      <li>The recommendations suggest actions related to the problems identified.</li>
    </ul>
  
<hr>

    <h2>Report Structure</h2>

    <p>A report presents information about an event, investigation or situation in an organised and objective way. The structure depends on the task, but a school report often includes a title, purpose, method or background, findings, conclusion and recommendations.</p>

    <h3>Complete example: Report on Classroom Cleanliness</h3>

    <div class="writing-example">
      <h3>REPORT ON CLASSROOM CLEANLINESS</h3>
      <p><strong>Prepared by:</strong> Kevin Mwangi, Class Representative</p>
      <p><strong>Date:</strong> 10 October 2026</p>

      <h4>1. Purpose</h4>
      <p>This report examines classroom cleanliness and identifies practical ways to improve it.</p>

      <h4>2. Method</h4>
      <p>The classroom was observed over five school days. The class representative also discussed cleaning routines with students on duty.</p>

      <h4>3. Findings</h4>
      <p>Paper was sometimes left under desks after lessons. The waste bin was occasionally full before the end of the day. The cleaning rota was displayed, but some students were unsure who was responsible for emptying the bin.</p>

      <h4>4. Conclusion</h4>
      <p>The classroom can be kept cleaner if waste is disposed of promptly and daily responsibilities are made clearer.</p>

      <h4>5. Recommendations</h4>
      <ol>
        <li>Display the cleaning rota where everyone can see it.</li>
        <li>Assign a student to check the bin at the end of each day.</li>
        <li>Remind students to collect paper and other waste before leaving.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>

    <h3>Keep findings separate from recommendations</h3>
    <p><strong>Findings</strong> describe what was observed. <strong>Recommendations</strong> propose actions. Do not present an untested assumption as a proven fact.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Report Structure', 15),
  (1965, 1965, '
    <h2>Informational Reports</h2>

    <p>An informational report presents verified information about a subject. It helps readers understand a situation without relying on unsupported claims or emotional language.</p>

    <h3>How to write one</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Use a specific title.</li>
      <li>State the purpose of the report.</li>
      <li>Arrange information under logical headings.</li>
      <li>Use accurate details, examples or figures when available.</li>
      <li>End with a brief conclusion if the assignment requires one.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Model informational report</h3>

    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:14px;">
      <h3 style="text-align:center;">REPORT ON WATER USE AT GREENFIELD SECONDARY SCHOOL</h3>
      <p><strong>Prepared by:</strong> Environmental Club Secretary</p>
      <p><strong>Date:</strong> 18 September 2026</p>

      <h4>Introduction</h4>
      <p>This report describes how water is used at Greenfield Secondary School and identifies practices that could help reduce wastage.</p>

      <h4>Areas of Water Use</h4>
      <p>Water is used in the kitchen, laboratories, toilets, cleaning activities and school gardens. Learners also use water for drinking and handwashing.</p>

      <h4>Observations</h4>
      <p>During a school inspection, two taps were found dripping after use. Some learners also left taps running while washing containers. Water collection containers were available near the kitchen, but they were not consistently covered.</p>

      <h4>Suggested Improvements</h4>
      <p>The school could repair leaking taps, remind learners to close taps after use and keep stored water covered. A weekly inspection could help identify problems early.</p>

      <h4>Conclusion</h4>
      <p>Regular maintenance and responsible water use could reduce avoidable wastage and improve water management at the school.</p>
    </div>

    <p><strong>Remember:</strong> Do not invent statistics or claim that a survey was conducted if it was not. When figures are supplied by a question, report them accurately.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Informational Reports', 15),
  (1966, 1966, '
    <h2>Investigative Reports</h2>

    <p>An investigative report explains what was examined, what evidence was collected, what the evidence shows and what conclusions can reasonably be drawn.</p>

    <h3>Typical structure</h3>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Title and purpose:</strong> Identify the issue being investigated.</li>
      <li><strong>Method:</strong> Explain how information was collected.</li>
      <li><strong>Findings:</strong> Present observations and evidence.</li>
      <li><strong>Conclusion:</strong> State what the evidence supports.</li>
      <li><strong>Recommendations:</strong> Propose appropriate action.</li>
    </ol>

    <p>Only describe methods that were actually used. Do not accuse a person of wrongdoing without sufficient evidence, and distinguish confirmed facts from possibilities.</p>

    <h3>Model investigative report</h3>

    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:14px;">
      <h3 style="text-align:center;">INVESTIGATIVE REPORT ON MISSING LIBRARY BOOKS</h3>
      <p><strong>Prepared by:</strong> Library Committee</p>
      <p><strong>Date:</strong> 20 September 2026</p>

      <h4>1. Purpose</h4>
      <p>The investigation was conducted to establish why five library books listed in the borrowing register could not be located during the weekly stock check.</p>

      <h4>2. Method</h4>
      <p>The committee checked the borrowing register, inspected the shelves and asked the librarian to verify the books'' return records.</p>

      <h4>3. Findings</h4>
      <p>Three books were recorded as borrowed and had no return entries. Two other books were listed in the register but were found on a shelf different from their assigned location. The available records did not establish who had last handled the three unreturned books.</p>

      <h4>4. Conclusion</h4>
      <p>The investigation established that two books had been misplaced. The records indicated that three books remained unaccounted for, but the available evidence did not establish why they were missing or who was responsible.</p>

      <h4>5. Recommendations</h4>
      <p>The library should follow up on the three outstanding loans, record returns immediately, and conduct regular shelf checks. A clear procedure for reporting misplaced books should also be introduced.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Important distinction</h3>
    <p>An investigation should not claim more than the evidence supports. If records show that a book was not returned, that does not automatically prove that a particular learner stole it.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Investigative Reports', 15),
  (1967, 1967, '
    <h2>Notices and Announcements</h2>

    <p>A notice gives readers important information about an event, change, meeting or instruction. An announcement communicates information to a group, either in writing or orally.</p>

    <h3>What to include in a written notice</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Name of the organisation or group, where relevant.</li>
      <li>A clear heading stating the subject.</li>
      <li>Date of the notice.</li>
      <li>Essential details: what, who, when, where and any action required.</li>
      <li>Name or position of the person responsible, when appropriate.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Model notice</h3>

    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:14px;">
      <h3 style="text-align:center;">GREENFIELD SECONDARY SCHOOL</h3>
      <h3 style="text-align:center;">NOTICE: ENVIRONMENTAL CLEAN-UP</h3>
      <p><strong>Date:</strong> 14 September 2026</p>

      <p>All members of the Environmental Club are informed that a school clean-up exercise will take place as follows:</p>

      <p><strong>Date of activity:</strong> Saturday, 19 September 2026</p>
      <p><strong>Time:</strong> 8:00 a.m. to 11:00 a.m.</p>
      <p><strong>Meeting point:</strong> School assembly ground</p>

      <p>Members should arrive on time and bring gloves and reusable water bottles. Waste bags will be provided by the club.</p>

      <p>For further information, contact the club secretary.</p>

      <p><strong>Signed:</strong> Club Patron</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Notice or announcement?</h3>
    <p>A written notice can be read later and should contain the necessary details. An oral announcement is delivered to listeners and may be shorter, but it must still communicate the essential information clearly.</p>
  
<hr>

    <h2>Notices and Announcements</h2>

    <p>A notice communicates important information to a specific audience. It should make the purpose, event, date, time, venue and required action easy to identify.</p>

    <h3>Complete example</h3>

    <div class="writing-example">
      <h3>EMBU SECONDARY SCHOOL</h3>
      <h3>NOTICE: INTER-CLASS DEBATE</h3>

      <p><strong>Date issued:</strong> 10 October 2026</p>

      <p>All Form Three students are informed that an inter-class debate will take place as follows:</p>

      <p><strong>Date:</strong> Friday, 16 October 2026</p>
      <p><strong>Time:</strong> 2.00 p.m.</p>
      <p><strong>Venue:</strong> School assembly hall</p>
      <p><strong>Motion:</strong> School uniforms should be compulsory in all schools.</p>

      <p>Students who wish to participate should register with the English teacher by Wednesday, 14 October. Each class should select two speakers and one timekeeper.</p>

      <p>All interested students are encouraged to attend and support their classmates.</p>

      <p><strong>Signed:</strong> Amina Hassan</p>
      <p>Debate Club Secretary</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Why this notice works</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>The heading identifies the event.</li>
      <li>The date, time and venue are easy to locate.</li>
      <li>The notice explains what interested students must do.</li>
      <li>The issuing person is identified.</li>
    </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Notices and Announcements', 15),
  (1968, 1968, '
    <h2>Invitations</h2>

    <p>An invitation asks a person or group to attend an event. Its language and layout depend on the event and the relationship between the sender and the recipient.</p>

    <h3>Essential details</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Who is inviting the reader.</li>
      <li>The event or occasion.</li>
      <li>Date and time.</li>
      <li>Venue.</li>
      <li>Any special instructions, dress code or response deadline.</li>
      <li>Contact details where a reply is required.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Model formal invitation</h3>

    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:18px;text-align:center;">
      <h3>GREENFIELD SECONDARY SCHOOL</h3>
      <p><em>cordially invites you to attend</em></p>
      <h2>THE ANNUAL PRIZE-GIVING CEREMONY</h2>
      <p>to celebrate learners'' achievements and recognise their effort throughout the academic year.</p>
      <p><strong>Date:</strong> Friday, 23 October 2026</p>
      <p><strong>Time:</strong> 10:00 a.m.</p>
      <p><strong>Venue:</strong> School Assembly Hall</p>
      <p>Kindly confirm your attendance with the school office by 16 October 2026.</p>
      <p><em>We look forward to celebrating this occasion with you.</em></p>
      <p><strong>The Principal</strong></p>
    </div>

    <h3>Model informal invitation</h3>
    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:14px;">
      <p>Hi Amina,</p>
      <p>I am having a small birthday celebration at my home on Saturday, 24 October, from 2:00 p.m. I would be very happy if you could come. We will have lunch, play games and spend the afternoon together.</p>
      <p>Please let me know by Thursday if you can make it.</p>
      <p>Hope to see you!</p>
      <p>Your friend,<br />Wanjiku</p>
    </div>

    <p>A formal invitation uses a respectful, polished tone. An informal invitation can sound more personal, but both should communicate the event details clearly.</p>
  
<hr>

    <h2>Invitations</h2>

    <p>An invitation asks someone to attend an event. It should clearly state the occasion, host, date, time and venue. It may also explain how the recipient should respond.</p>

    <h3>Complete example: Formal Invitation</h3>

    <div class="writing-example">
      <p><strong>EMBU SECONDARY SCHOOL</strong></p>

      <p>The Principal, staff and students of Embu Secondary School request the pleasure of your company at the school''s Annual Prize-Giving Ceremony.</p>

      <p><strong>Date:</strong> Friday, 23 October 2026</p>
      <p><strong>Time:</strong> 10.00 a.m.</p>
      <p><strong>Venue:</strong> School assembly hall</p>

      <p>The ceremony will recognise students'' academic achievements and contributions to school life.</p>

      <p>Kindly confirm your attendance with the school office by 19 October 2026.</p>

      <p>We look forward to welcoming you.</p>

      <p><strong>By order of the Principal</strong></p>
      <p>Embu Secondary School</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Informal invitation example</h3>

    <div class="writing-example">
      <p>Hi Brian,</p>

      <p>I am having a small birthday gathering at my home on Saturday, 24 October, starting at 3.00 p.m. I would love you to come so we can spend time together and catch up.</p>

      <p>Please let me know if you can make it.</p>

      <p>See you soon,</p>
      <p>Kevin</p>
    </div>

    <p>The formality of an invitation depends on the occasion and the relationship with the recipient.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Invitations', 15),
  (1969, 1969, '
    <h2>Advertisements</h2>

    <p>An advertisement is designed to attract attention and persuade a particular audience to take action, such as buying a product, attending an event or using a service.</p>

    <h3>Features of an effective advertisement</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Headline:</strong> Captures attention and identifies the offer.</li>
      <li><strong>Audience:</strong> Addresses the people most likely to be interested.</li>
      <li><strong>Benefits:</strong> Explains what the audience gains.</li>
      <li><strong>Details:</strong> Provides accurate prices, dates, location or conditions where relevant.</li>
      <li><strong>Call to action:</strong> Tells readers what to do next.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Model advertisement</h3>

    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:16px;text-align:center;">
      <h2>READY FOR YOUR NEXT EXAM?</h2>
      <h3>GREENFIELD BOOK FAIR</h3>
      <p>Find revision guides, dictionaries, exercise books and other learning materials in one place.</p>
      <p><strong>Date:</strong> Saturday, 17 October 2026</p>
      <p><strong>Time:</strong> 9:00 a.m. to 3:00 p.m.</p>
      <p><strong>Venue:</strong> Greenfield Secondary School Hall</p>
      <p>Entry is free. Prices of books and supplies will be displayed at the stalls.</p>
      <h3>Visit the school hall and find the materials you need.</h3>
      <p>For enquiries, contact the school office.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Truthful persuasion</h3>
    <p>An advertisement may use persuasive language, but claims must not mislead the audience. Do not invent awards, discounts, guarantees or product results.</p>
  
<hr>

    <h2>Advertisements</h2>

    <p>An advertisement promotes a product, service, event or opportunity. Effective advertisements identify the audience, communicate a clear benefit and provide accurate details about how to respond.</p>

    <h3>Complete example</h3>

    <div class="writing-example">
      <h3>MAKE YOUR REVISION COUNT</h3>
      <h4>JOIN THE SATURDAY STUDY CLINIC</h4>

      <p>Are you preparing for your end-of-term examinations?</p>

      <p>Join the Saturday Study Clinic for focused Mathematics and English revision in a supportive learning environment.</p>

      <p><strong>What is included?</strong></p>
      <ul>
        <li>Guided practice on challenging questions</li>
        <li>Step-by-step explanations of common errors</li>
        <li>Small-group discussion and feedback</li>
      </ul>

      <p><strong>Date:</strong> Saturday, 17 October 2026</p>
      <p><strong>Time:</strong> 9.00 a.m. to 12.00 noon</p>
      <p><strong>Venue:</strong> School library</p>
      <p><strong>Fee:</strong> Free for registered students</p>

      <p>Register with the English or Mathematics teacher by Thursday, 15 October.</p>

      <p><strong>Come prepared. Practise with purpose. Learn from your mistakes.</strong></p>
    </div>

    <h3>Check the claims</h3>
    <p>An advertisement may use persuasive language, but its claims must be truthful. Do not invent guaranteed results, fake testimonials or false scarcity.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Advertisements', 15),
  (1970, 1970, '
    <h2>Meeting Agendas</h2>

    <p>An agenda is an ordered list of matters to be discussed at a meeting. It helps participants prepare and keeps the meeting focused.</p>

    <h3>What an agenda should contain</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Name of the organisation or committee.</li>
      <li>Type of meeting.</li>
      <li>Date, time and venue.</li>
      <li>Items in a logical order.</li>
      <li>Any preparation participants need to complete.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Common agenda items include opening the meeting, apologies, confirmation of previous minutes, matters arising, new business and the date of the next meeting. Use only the items relevant to the meeting.</p>

    <h3>Model agenda</h3>

    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:14px;">
      <h3 style="text-align:center;">GREENFIELD ENVIRONMENTAL CLUB</h3>
      <h3 style="text-align:center;">AGENDA FOR THE MONTHLY MEETING</h3>

      <p><strong>Date:</strong> 16 October 2026</p>
      <p><strong>Time:</strong> 3:30 p.m.</p>
      <p><strong>Venue:</strong> Science Laboratory</p>

      <ol>
        <li>Opening and welcome</li>
        <li>Apologies for absence</li>
        <li>Confirmation of the previous meeting''s minutes</li>
        <li>Matters arising from the previous minutes</li>
        <li>Planning the school clean-up exercise</li>
        <li>Allocation of duties and materials</li>
        <li>Any other business</li>
        <li>Date of the next meeting</li>
        <li>Adjournment</li>
      </ol>

      <p><strong>Prepared by:</strong> Club Secretary</p>
    </div>

    <p>An agenda lists matters for discussion; it does not record decisions that have already been made. Those belong in the minutes.</p>
  
<hr>

    <h2>Meeting Agendas</h2>

    <p>An agenda is a planned list of matters to be discussed at a meeting. It is usually prepared before the meeting and helps participants prepare and use their time effectively.</p>

    <h3>Complete example</h3>

    <div class="writing-example">
      <h3>EMBU SECONDARY SCHOOL READING CLUB</h3>
      <h3>AGENDA FOR THE MEETING ON 15 OCTOBER 2026</h3>

      <p><strong>Time:</strong> 3.30 p.m.</p>
      <p><strong>Venue:</strong> School library</p>
      <p><strong>Chairperson:</strong> Kevin Mwangi</p>

      <ol>
        <li>Opening and apologies</li>
        <li>Confirmation of the previous meeting''s minutes</li>
        <li>Matters arising from the previous minutes</li>
        <li>Preparation for the reading day</li>
        <li>Allocation of duties and resources</li>
        <li>Any other business</li>
        <li>Date of the next meeting</li>
        <li>Adjournment</li>
      </ol>

      <p><strong>Prepared by:</strong> Amina Hassan, Secretary</p>
      <p><strong>Date prepared:</strong> 10 October 2026</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Agenda versus minutes</h3>
    <p>An agenda lists matters to be discussed before or at the start of a meeting. Minutes record what was discussed and decided after the meeting.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Meeting Agendas', 15),
  (1971, 1971, '
    <h2>Meeting Minutes</h2>

    <p>Minutes are the official written record of a meeting. They summarise important discussions, decisions, actions assigned and other necessary proceedings.</p>

    <h3>Typical format</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Name of the organisation and meeting.</li>
      <li>Date, time and venue.</li>
      <li>Members present and apologies, as required.</li>
      <li>Numbered agenda items.</li>
      <li>Important decisions and action points.</li>
      <li>Time of adjournment and details of the next meeting, if agreed.</li>
      <li>Name or signature of the responsible officer, according to the organisation''s procedure.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Minutes are concise and factual. They do not normally reproduce every sentence spoken or include the secretary''s personal opinions.</p>

    <h3>Model minutes</h3>

    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:14px;">
      <h3 style="text-align:center;">GREENFIELD ENVIRONMENTAL CLUB</h3>
      <h3 style="text-align:center;">MINUTES OF THE MEETING HELD ON 16 OCTOBER 2026</h3>

      <p><strong>Venue:</strong> Science Laboratory</p>
      <p><strong>Time:</strong> 3:30 p.m.</p>

      <p><strong>Present:</strong> Chairperson, Secretary and eight club members.</p>
      <p><strong>Apologies:</strong> Two members sent apologies.</p>

      <p><strong>Minute 1: Opening</strong><br />The chairperson opened the meeting and welcomed members.</p>

      <p><strong>Minute 2: Confirmation of Previous Minutes</strong><br />The previous minutes were read and confirmed as an accurate record.</p>

      <p><strong>Minute 3: School Clean-up Exercise</strong><br />Members agreed to conduct the clean-up on 24 October 2026, from 8:00 a.m. to 11:00 a.m.</p>

      <p><strong>Minute 4: Allocation of Duties</strong><br />Members were assigned to collect litter, sort recyclable materials and arrange the equipment. The secretary will prepare the duty list by 20 October.</p>

      <p><strong>Minute 5: Any Other Business</strong><br />Members requested additional waste bags from the school administration.</p>

      <p><strong>Minute 6: Adjournment</strong><br />The meeting ended at 4:20 p.m. The next meeting was scheduled for 30 October 2026.</p>

      <p><strong>Prepared by:</strong> Club Secretary</p>
    </div>
  
<hr>

    <h2>Meeting Minutes</h2>

    <p>Minutes are an official record of the main matters discussed, decisions made and actions agreed during a meeting. They should be factual and concise, not a word-for-word transcript.</p>

    <h3>Complete example</h3>

    <div class="writing-example">
      <h3>MINUTES OF THE SCHOOL READING CLUB MEETING</h3>
      <p><strong>Date:</strong> 8 October 2026</p>
      <p><strong>Time:</strong> 3.30 p.m.</p>
      <p><strong>Venue:</strong> School library</p>

      <h4>1. Attendance</h4>
      <p>Present: Kevin Mwangi (Chairperson), Amina Hassan (Secretary), Peter Njoroge and Mary Wanjiku.</p>
      <p>Apologies: None recorded.</p>

      <h4>2. Agenda</h4>
      <ol>
        <li>Opening and confirmation of the previous minutes</li>
        <li>Preparation for the reading event</li>
        <li>Allocation of duties</li>
        <li>Any other business</li>
      </ol>

      <h4>3. Confirmation of Previous Minutes</h4>
      <p>The previous minutes were read and confirmed as an accurate record.</p>

      <h4>4. Preparation for the Reading Event</h4>
      <p>Members agreed to hold the event on 24 October 2026 in the school library. The programme will include silent reading and student book reviews.</p>

      <h4>5. Allocation of Duties</h4>
      <ul>
        <li>Amina will prepare the attendance list by 20 October.</li>
        <li>Peter will arrange the books by 22 October.</li>
        <li>Mary will prepare the event notice by 18 October.</li>
      </ul>

      <h4>6. Any Other Business</h4>
      <p>Members proposed inviting another class to attend, subject to the teacher''s approval.</p>

      <h4>7. Adjournment</h4>
      <p>The meeting ended at 4.15 p.m. The next meeting will be held on 15 October 2026.</p>

      <p><strong>Prepared by:</strong> Amina Hassan, Secretary</p>
      <p><strong>Confirmed by:</strong> Kevin Mwangi, Chairperson</p>
      <p><strong>Signature:</strong> ____________________</p>
      <p><strong>Date confirmed:</strong> ____________________</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Minutes must be accurate</h3>
    <p>Record decisions and assigned actions faithfully. Do not invent attendance, agreements or quotations. Follow the organisation''s required format where one exists.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Meeting Minutes', 15),
  (1972, 1972, '
    <h2>Clear Instructions</h2>

    <p>Instructions explain how to complete a task safely and correctly. They should be arranged in the order in which actions need to happen.</p>

    <h3>Rules for effective instructions</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>State the task or purpose clearly.</li>
      <li>Use direct action verbs such as open, place, check and submit.</li>
      <li>Number steps when order matters.</li>
      <li>Include necessary materials, conditions or warnings.</li>
      <li>Make each step specific enough to follow.</li>
      <li>Use simple, unambiguous language.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Model instructions</h3>

    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:14px;">
      <h3>HOW TO SUBMIT AN ASSIGNMENT THROUGH THE SCHOOL PORTAL</h3>

      <p><strong>Before you begin:</strong> Make sure your assignment file is saved in an accepted format and that you can access your school account.</p>

      <ol>
        <li>Open the school''s learning portal in your browser.</li>
        <li>Sign in using your school account.</li>
        <li>Open the subject and select the relevant assignment.</li>
        <li>Read the submission instructions and check the deadline.</li>
        <li>Select the upload option and choose the correct assignment file.</li>
        <li>Wait for the upload to finish.</li>
        <li>Select Submit, if the portal requires a separate submission step.</li>
        <li>Check the confirmation message or submission status before leaving the page.</li>
      </ol>

      <p><strong>Important:</strong> Uploading a file does not always mean it has been submitted. Follow the portal''s final submission procedure.</p>
    </div>

    <p>Good instructions do not assume the reader already knows a critical step. Include warnings where a mistake could cause data loss, safety problems or an unsuccessful submission.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Clear Instructions', 15),
  (1973, 1973, '
    <h2>Rules and Procedures</h2>

    <p>Rules state what is required, allowed or prohibited. Procedures explain the steps people must follow to complete an activity or handle a situation consistently.</p>

    <h3>Rules and procedures are different</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Rule:</strong> Keep library books clean and return them by the due date.</li>
      <li><strong>Procedure:</strong> Check the due date, take the book to the library desk and allow the librarian to record the return.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Writing effective rules</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Use clear, direct wording.</li>
      <li>State the required or prohibited behaviour.</li>
      <li>Make each rule specific and understandable.</li>
      <li>Keep rules relevant to the place or activity.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Writing effective procedures</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Arrange steps in the correct order.</li>
      <li>Use numbered steps when sequence matters.</li>
      <li>Identify who is responsible for each action when necessary.</li>
      <li>Include safety warnings or exceptions where relevant.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Model: School Library Rules and Book-Return Procedure</h3>

    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:14px;">
      <h3>GREENFIELD SECONDARY SCHOOL LIBRARY</h3>

      <h4>Library Rules</h4>
      <ol>
        <li>Keep noise to a minimum so that others can study.</li>
        <li>Handle books and other library materials carefully.</li>
        <li>Do not remove library materials without completing the borrowing procedure.</li>
        <li>Return borrowed books by the recorded due date.</li>
        <li>Leave the reading area clean and return chairs to their proper places.</li>
      </ol>

      <h4>Procedure for Returning a Book</h4>
      <ol>
        <li>Check the book''s due date in your borrowing record.</li>
        <li>Take the book to the library return desk.</li>
        <li>Give the book to the librarian or follow the library''s designated return process.</li>
        <li>Wait for the return to be recorded.</li>
        <li>Check that the book has been marked as returned if a record is available to you.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>

    <p>Rules guide behaviour. Procedures explain how to carry out an action. A well-written document may contain both, but they should be clearly separated.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Rules and Procedures', 15),
  (1974, 1974, '
    <h2>Preparing a Speech</h2>

    <p>A speech is a message prepared for delivery to an audience. Before writing it, identify the audience, purpose, occasion and time available.</p>

    <h3>Steps in preparation</h3>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Identify the purpose:</strong> Are you informing, persuading, advising or entertaining?</li>
      <li><strong>Understand the audience:</strong> Consider their age, interests and prior knowledge.</li>
      <li><strong>Select relevant points:</strong> Choose information that supports the purpose.</li>
      <li><strong>Organise the points:</strong> Arrange them into an introduction, body and conclusion.</li>
      <li><strong>Plan examples:</strong> Use relevant evidence, illustrations or experiences.</li>
      <li><strong>Practise delivery:</strong> Read aloud, check timing and improve unclear wording.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Model preparation plan</h3>
    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:14px;">
      <p><strong>Occasion:</strong> School assembly</p>
      <p><strong>Topic:</strong> Keeping Our School Clean</p>
      <p><strong>Audience:</strong> Learners and teachers</p>
      <p><strong>Purpose:</strong> Persuade learners to take responsibility for the school environment.</p>

      <h4>Opening</h4>
      <p>Greet the audience and explain why a clean school matters.</p>

      <h4>Main points</h4>
      <ol>
        <li>Litter creates an unpleasant and potentially unhealthy environment.</li>
        <li>Using bins and sorting waste makes the school easier to maintain.</li>
        <li>Every learner can help by disposing of waste properly and reporting overflowing bins.</li>
      </ol>

      <h4>Conclusion</h4>
      <p>Summarise the message and ask every learner to take one practical action today.</p>
    </div>

    <p>A preparation plan is not the same as the final speech. It organises the ideas before they are developed into connected spoken language.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Preparing a Speech', 15),
  (1975, 1975, '
    <h2>Writing and Organising a Speech</h2>

    <p>A well-organised speech has an opening, a connected body and a conclusion. It uses language suitable for speaking, addresses the audience directly and develops its points in a logical order.</p>

    <h3>Useful techniques</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Greet and acknowledge the audience appropriately.</li>
      <li>Introduce the topic clearly.</li>
      <li>Develop each main point with an explanation or example.</li>
      <li>Use transitions to connect ideas.</li>
      <li>Use repetition or rhetorical questions selectively.</li>
      <li>End with a clear summary, recommendation or call to action.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Complete model speech</h3>
    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:14px;">
      <h3 style="text-align:center;">KEEPING OUR SCHOOL CLEAN</h3>

      <p>Good morning, respected teachers and fellow learners.</p>

      <p>Today, I would like us to think about a simple question: what kind of school do we want to learn in? We all want classrooms, paths and playing fields that are clean, safe and welcoming. Achieving that environment is a responsibility we share.</p>

      <p>First, litter makes our surroundings unpleasant and can block drainage when waste is carelessly discarded. A wrapper thrown beside a classroom may seem unimportant, but many small acts of carelessness create a much larger problem.</p>

      <p>Second, we can make a difference through simple habits. We should use the available bins, avoid dropping waste on the ground and report overflowing bins. We should also handle school property carefully and take part in organised clean-up activities.</p>

      <p>Finally, keeping our school clean should not depend on the presence of a teacher. It should become a habit we practise because we respect our school and the people who use it.</p>

      <p>Fellow learners, let us begin today. Before leaving a classroom or the playing field, look around and make sure we leave the area clean. If each of us takes responsibility, our combined effort will make a visible difference.</p>

      <p>Thank you for listening.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Why this speech works</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>The greeting suits the school assembly.</li>
      <li>The opening introduces the topic and engages the audience.</li>
      <li>The body develops connected arguments.</li>
      <li>The conclusion gives the audience a practical action.</li>
      <li>The language is natural to hear aloud.</li>
    </ul>
  
<hr>

    <h2>Writing and Organising a Speech</h2>

    <p>A speech is written to be delivered aloud to an audience. It should have a suitable greeting, a clear opening, organised points, examples that support the message and a memorable conclusion.</p>

    <h3>Complete example: The Value of Reading</h3>

    <div class="writing-example">
      <p>Good morning, Principal, teachers and fellow students.</p>

      <p>I am grateful for the opportunity to speak about a habit that can change the way we learn: reading.</p>

      <p>First, reading helps us build knowledge. A textbook can explain a scientific principle, while a biography can show how someone overcame a challenge. Each text gives us a chance to learn beyond our immediate experience.</p>

      <p>Second, reading improves communication. Students who regularly read different kinds of texts encounter new vocabulary and see how ideas can be organised. This can help them express their own thoughts more clearly.</p>

      <p>Third, reading teaches us to question information. A thoughtful reader asks whether a claim is supported by evidence, whether the writer is biased and whether another source gives a different account.</p>

      <p>However, reading is most useful when we do more than turn pages. We should pause to explain an idea in our own words, ask questions and check what we have understood.</p>

      <p>My challenge to each of us is simple: set aside a little time each day to read something worthwhile. Share what you learn and encourage a classmate to join you.</p>

      <p>Let us build a school where curiosity is encouraged and knowledge is shared.</p>

      <p>Thank you for listening.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Structure to notice</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Greeting:</strong> recognises the audience.</li>
      <li><strong>Opening:</strong> introduces the topic.</li>
      <li><strong>Body:</strong> develops distinct points with explanations.</li>
      <li><strong>Conclusion:</strong> leaves the audience with a clear message or action.</li>
      <li><strong>Closing:</strong> acknowledges the audience.</li>
    </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Writing and Organising a Speech', 15),
  (1976, 1976, '
    <h2>Memoranda</h2>

    <p>A memorandum, commonly called a memo, is a short internal message used within an organisation to communicate information, instructions or decisions.</p>

    <h3>Typical features</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>The organisation''s name, where appropriate.</li>
      <li><strong>To:</strong> The recipient or group.</li>
      <li><strong>From:</strong> The sender and position.</li>
      <li><strong>Date:</strong> When it was written.</li>
      <li><strong>Subject:</strong> A concise description of the purpose.</li>
      <li>A clear message stating the information or action required.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Complete model memorandum</h3>
    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:14px;">
      <h3 style="text-align:center;">GREENFIELD SECONDARY SCHOOL</h3>
      <h2 style="text-align:center;">MEMORANDUM</h2>

      <p><strong>To:</strong> All Class Representatives</p>
      <p><strong>From:</strong> Deputy Principal</p>
      <p><strong>Date:</strong> 19 October 2026</p>
      <p><strong>Subject:</strong> Collection of Classroom Inventory Forms</p>

      <p>All class representatives are required to submit their completed classroom inventory forms to the Deputy Principal''s office by 3:00 p.m. on Thursday, 22 October 2026.</p>

      <p>Before submission, please confirm that the form records the available desks, chairs, noticeboards and other classroom equipment. Report any damaged items clearly.</p>

      <p>Class representatives who need clarification should visit the office before the deadline.</p>

      <p>Thank you for your cooperation.</p>

      <p><strong>Deputy Principal</strong></p>
    </div>

    <p>A memo is generally more direct than a formal letter because it is intended for communication within an organisation. Follow the format requested in the assignment.</p>
  
<hr>

    <h2>Memoranda</h2>

    <p>A memorandum, commonly called a memo, is a short internal message used within an organisation. It communicates information, instructions or a request to a defined group. Use a clear heading and a direct, professional message.</p>

    <h3>Complete example</h3>

    <div class="writing-example">
      <h3>MEMORANDUM</h3>

      <p><strong>To:</strong> All Form Three Class Representatives</p>
      <p><strong>From:</strong> Deputy Principal</p>
      <p><strong>Date:</strong> 10 October 2026</p>
      <p><strong>Subject:</strong> Submission of Club Activity Reports</p>

      <p>All Form Three class representatives are requested to submit their club activity reports to the deputy principal''s office by 3.00 p.m. on 16 October 2026.</p>

      <p>Each report should state the club''s activities during the term, the number of participating students, achievements and any challenges encountered. Please ensure that the information is accurate and that the report is clearly labelled with the club''s name.</p>

      <p>Class representatives who anticipate difficulty meeting the deadline should inform the office before 15 October.</p>

      <p>Thank you for your cooperation.</p>

      <p>Deputy Principal</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Why the format matters</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>To:</strong> identifies the intended readers.</li>
      <li><strong>From:</strong> identifies the sender or office.</li>
      <li><strong>Date:</strong> records when the memo was issued.</li>
      <li><strong>Subject:</strong> states the topic quickly.</li>
      <li>The body gives the instruction, deadline and requirements.</li>
    </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Memoranda', 15),
  (1977, 1977, '
    <h2>Proposals</h2>

    <p>A proposal presents a plan and asks a person or organisation to approve, support or fund it. A strong proposal explains the need, recommends a practical solution and shows how the plan could be carried out.</p>

    <h3>Common sections</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Title</li>
      <li>Background or statement of the problem</li>
      <li>Objectives or intended results</li>
      <li>Proposed activities</li>
      <li>Resources and estimated budget, when required</li>
      <li>Expected benefits</li>
      <li>Conclusion or request for approval</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Complete model proposal</h3>
    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:14px;">
      <h3 style="text-align:center;">PROPOSAL TO ESTABLISH A SCHOOL READING CORNER</h3>
      <p><strong>Submitted to:</strong> The Principal, Greenfield Secondary School</p>
      <p><strong>Submitted by:</strong> English Club</p>
      <p><strong>Date:</strong> 19 October 2026</p>

      <h4>1. Background</h4>
      <p>Some learners need a quiet space where they can read supplementary books during free periods. The English Club proposes establishing a small reading corner in an unused section of the library.</p>

      <h4>2. Objectives</h4>
      <ul>
        <li>Encourage regular independent reading.</li>
        <li>Make suitable supplementary reading materials easier to access.</li>
        <li>Promote responsible sharing and care of books.</li>
      </ul>

      <h4>3. Proposed Activities</h4>
      <p>The club will identify suitable books, arrange donated materials by category, prepare a borrowing record and create a rota for keeping the area tidy.</p>

      <h4>4. Resources Required</h4>
      <p>The project will need two small shelves, labels, a register and donated books. The school will be asked to provide the space and approve any necessary purchases. Costs will be confirmed after obtaining quotations.</p>

      <h4>5. Expected Benefits</h4>
      <p>The reading corner is expected to give learners easier access to supplementary reading and encourage a stronger reading culture.</p>

      <h4>6. Conclusion</h4>
      <p>The English Club requests approval to establish the reading corner and permission to collect suitable book donations. The club will report on its progress after the first term of operation.</p>

      <p><strong>Signed:</strong> English Club Chairperson</p>
    </div>

    <p>A proposal should not present estimated results as guaranteed outcomes. Any budget should use realistic figures supported by quotations or clearly labelled estimates.</p>
  
<hr>

    <h2>Proposals</h2>

    <p>A proposal presents a suggested project or course of action for consideration. It explains the need, the proposed activities, the resources required and how success could be evaluated. Follow the format requested by the organisation.</p>

    <h3>Complete example: School Reading Day Proposal</h3>

    <div class="writing-example">
      <h3>PROPOSAL FOR A SCHOOL READING DAY</h3>
      <p><strong>Prepared by:</strong> School Reading Club</p>
      <p><strong>Date:</strong> 10 October 2026</p>

      <h4>1. Background</h4>
      <p>The reading club proposes a school reading day to encourage regular reading and give students opportunities to discuss books they enjoy.</p>

      <h4>2. Purpose</h4>
      <p>The event is intended to increase student participation in reading activities and provide a platform for students to share book recommendations.</p>

      <h4>3. Proposed Activities</h4>
      <ol>
        <li>A thirty-minute individual reading session.</li>
        <li>Small-group discussions about selected books.</li>
        <li>Short student presentations recommending a book.</li>
        <li>A display of books available in the school library.</li>
      </ol>

      <h4>4. Resources Required</h4>
      <ul>
        <li>Access to the school library or an approved classroom.</li>
        <li>Books and writing materials.</li>
        <li>Teacher supervision.</li>
        <li>Noticeboard space for the programme.</li>
      </ul>

      <h4>5. Proposed Date and Schedule</h4>
      <p>The club proposes 24 October 2026, from 9.00 a.m. to 12.00 noon, subject to approval and venue availability.</p>

      <h4>6. Estimated Budget</h4>
      <p>Printing notices: KSh 300.</p>
      <p>Stationery: KSh 500.</p>
      <p><strong>Estimated total: KSh 800.</strong></p>
      <p>These figures are planning estimates and should be confirmed before approval.</p>

      <h4>7. Evaluation</h4>
      <p>The club will record attendance, collect brief participant feedback and review the number of students who volunteer to join future reading activities.</p>

      <h4>8. Request for Approval</h4>
      <p>We request permission to hold the event and to use the proposed venue. We welcome any adjustments the school administration considers necessary.</p>

      <p>Prepared by:</p>
      <p>Kevin Mwangi</p>
      <p>Secretary, School Reading Club</p>
    </div>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Proposals', 15),
  (1978, 1978, '
    <h2>Project Reports</h2>

    <p>A project report documents a project''s purpose, activities, results, challenges and lessons. It allows readers to compare what was planned with what actually happened.</p>

    <h3>Typical structure</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Project title and reporting period</li>
      <li>Background and objectives</li>
      <li>Activities completed</li>
      <li>Results and evidence</li>
      <li>Challenges encountered</li>
      <li>Lessons learned and recommendations</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Complete model project report</h3>
    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:14px;">
      <h3 style="text-align:center;">REPORT ON THE SCHOOL TREE-PLANTING PROJECT</h3>
      <p><strong>Prepared by:</strong> Environmental Club</p>
      <p><strong>Reporting period:</strong> September–October 2026</p>
      <p><strong>Date:</strong> 30 October 2026</p>

      <h4>1. Background and Objectives</h4>
      <p>The Environmental Club organised a tree-planting project to increase shade around the school and encourage learners to care for the environment.</p>

      <h4>2. Activities Completed</h4>
      <p>Members identified planting locations, prepared the ground, planted seedlings and organised a watering rota. The club also labelled the planted areas for monitoring.</p>

      <h4>3. Results</h4>
      <p>The club planted 30 seedlings during the project. A follow-up check found that 27 were alive at the time of inspection. The remaining three required assessment to determine whether they could recover or needed replacement.</p>

      <h4>4. Challenges</h4>
      <p>Some seedlings received insufficient water during a dry period. The watering rota was not followed consistently in the first week.</p>

      <h4>5. Recommendations</h4>
      <p>The club recommends assigning named learners to each section, recording watering activities and checking seedling survival every two weeks.</p>

      <h4>6. Conclusion</h4>
      <p>The project established 30 seedlings, and the first inspection found 27 alive. Continued care and regular monitoring will be needed to improve their chances of survival.</p>
    </div>

    <p>A project report must distinguish activities from results. Planting a seedling does not prove that it survived, and an early result should not be presented as a long-term outcome.</p>
  
<hr>

    <h2>Project Reports</h2>

    <p>A project report records what a project set out to do, how it was carried out, what happened and what can be learned. Unlike a proposal, which requests approval for a plan, a project report describes work already undertaken.</p>

    <h3>Complete example: School Book Collection Project</h3>

    <div class="writing-example">
      <h3>PROJECT REPORT: SCHOOL BOOK COLLECTION</h3>
      <p><strong>Prepared by:</strong> School Reading Club</p>
      <p><strong>Reporting period:</strong> 1–30 September 2026</p>

      <h4>1. Introduction</h4>
      <p>The reading club organised a book collection project to increase the number of reading materials available to students.</p>

      <h4>2. Objectives</h4>
      <p>The project aimed to collect suitable books, encourage responsible book sharing and make additional reading materials available in the school library.</p>

      <h4>3. Activities Completed</h4>
      <p>The club announced the collection, received donated books and sorted them by subject and reading level. The teacher in charge checked the donations before the books were placed in the library.</p>

      <h4>4. Results</h4>
      <p>The club received 48 books. After inspection, 42 were accepted for the library and six were set aside because they were damaged or unsuitable. The accepted books were labelled and recorded.</p>

      <h4>5. Challenges</h4>
      <p>Some donations arrived without clear titles or authors, which increased the time needed for sorting. Storage space was also limited.</p>

      <h4>6. Lessons Learned</h4>
      <p>Future collections should provide clear donation guidelines and arrange storage before books begin arriving.</p>

      <h4>7. Recommendations</h4>
      <ol>
        <li>Provide donors with a list of acceptable books.</li>
        <li>Prepare labelled storage boxes before the next collection.</li>
        <li>Schedule regular checks of the donated books.</li>
      </ol>

      <h4>8. Conclusion</h4>
      <p>The project added 42 suitable books to the school library. Better donation guidelines and preparation could make the next collection more efficient.</p>
    </div>

    <p><strong>Key distinction:</strong> A proposal describes what is planned; a project report records what was done and the results actually obtained.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Project Reports', 15),
  (1979, 1979, '
    <h2>Reviews</h2>

    <p>A review evaluates a book, film, performance, product or event. It describes the subject briefly, gives a reasoned judgement and helps readers decide whether the subject suits their interests.</p>

    <h3>Features of a useful review</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>A title that identifies the subject or gives the review a clear focus.</li>
      <li>A brief introduction identifying the work or event.</li>
      <li>A concise description without revealing unnecessary plot details.</li>
      <li>Specific strengths and weaknesses.</li>
      <li>Reasons or examples that support the judgement.</li>
      <li>A conclusion or recommendation for the intended audience.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Complete model book review</h3>
    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:14px;">
      <h3>BOOK REVIEW: THE RIVER BETWEEN</h3>
      <p><strong>Author:</strong> Ngũgĩ wa Thiong''o</p>
      <p><strong>Genre:</strong> Novel</p>

      <p><em>The River Between</em> explores conflict, cultural identity, education and the difficulties faced by a community divided by differing beliefs. The story centres on people whose personal relationships are affected by wider social and cultural tensions.</p>

      <p>One strength of the novel is its treatment of conflicting viewpoints. Rather than presenting every character as entirely right or wrong, the story encourages readers to consider how beliefs and traditions influence decisions. Its setting also helps readers understand the community in which the conflict develops.</p>

      <p>Some readers may find the cultural and historical context challenging at first. Reading slowly and paying attention to the relationships between characters can make the events easier to follow.</p>

      <p>Overall, the novel is worth reading for learners interested in Kenyan literature, cultural conflict and the relationship between individuals and their communities. It offers useful material for discussion and literary analysis.</p>

      <p><strong>Recommendation:</strong> Suitable for readers who enjoy novels that explore social issues and complex choices.</p>
    </div>

    <p>A review is not merely a plot summary. The writer must explain what is effective or ineffective and support that judgement with reasons.</p>
  
<hr>

    <h2>Reviews</h2>

    <p>A review evaluates a book, film, performance, product or event for a particular audience. It briefly identifies the subject, discusses specific strengths and weaknesses, supports opinions with examples and gives an overall judgement.</p>

    <h3>Complete example: Book Review</h3>

    <div class="writing-example">
      <h3>BOOK REVIEW: THE RIVER BETWEEN</h3>
      <p><strong>Title:</strong> The River Between</p>
      <p><strong>Author:</strong> Ngũgĩ wa Thiong''o</p>
      <p><strong>Genre:</strong> Novel</p>

      <h4>Overview</h4>
      <p><em>The River Between</em> explores tensions between tradition and change in two neighbouring communities. Through the conflict surrounding cultural identity and education, the novel examines how deeply held beliefs can divide people.</p>

      <h4>Strengths</h4>
      <p>One strength of the novel is its exploration of competing values. The river provides a useful symbol of both separation and connection. The conflict also encourages readers to consider the effects of rigid positions on families and communities.</p>

      <h4>Limitations</h4>
      <p>Some readers may find the cultural and historical context unfamiliar at first. Paying attention to the relationships between the characters helps make the conflict easier to follow.</p>

      <h4>Overall Judgement</h4>
      <p>The novel is worth reading for students interested in culture, identity and social change. It provides material for thoughtful discussion, although readers may need to pause and consider the context behind some of the characters'' decisions.</p>

      <p><strong>Recommendation:</strong> Recommended for readers who enjoy novels that examine social and cultural questions.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>What makes this a review rather than a summary?</h3>
    <p>A summary mainly reports what a text contains. A review goes further by judging its strengths and limitations and explaining whether it is worth reading.</p>

    <p>When reviewing a real work, check the title, author and details. Support evaluations with evidence and make clear which statements are opinions.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Reviews', 15),
  (1980, 1980, '
    <h2>Writing Clear Instructions</h2>

    <p>Instructions tell a reader how to complete a task. Good instructions use a logical order, clear action verbs and enough detail to prevent mistakes.</p>

    <h3>Complete example: How to Submit a School Assignment</h3>

    <div class="writing-example">
      <h3>PROCEDURE: SUBMITTING A WRITTEN ASSIGNMENT</h3>

      <p><strong>Materials needed:</strong> Completed assignment, pen, assignment cover sheet and school file.</p>

      <h4>Steps</h4>
      <ol>
        <li>Write your full name, class and admission number on the cover sheet.</li>
        <li>Check that every question has been answered.</li>
        <li>Review the assignment for spelling, punctuation and calculation errors.</li>
        <li>Arrange the pages in the correct order.</li>
        <li>Attach the cover sheet to the front of the assignment.</li>
        <li>Place the assignment in the designated submission tray before the stated deadline.</li>
        <li>If you cannot submit it on time, contact the teacher before the deadline and explain the problem.</li>
      </ol>

      <h4>Final check</h4>
      <p>Confirm that your name is visible, all pages are present and the assignment has been submitted to the correct place.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Writing instructions well</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Put steps in the order they must be performed.</li>
      <li>Begin each step with a clear action.</li>
      <li>Include warnings or conditions where they matter.</li>
      <li>Avoid vague directions such as ''do it properly''.</li>
    </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Writing Clear Instructions', 15),
  (1981, 1981, '
    <h2>Biographies</h2>

    <p>A biography is an account of another person''s life written by someone else. It presents important events in a meaningful order and uses reliable information rather than invented personal details.</p>

    <h3>Complete example: Short Biography</h3>

    <div class="writing-example">
      <h3>A SHORT BIOGRAPHY OF WANGARI MAATHAI</h3>

      <p>Wangari Maathai was a Kenyan environmentalist, academic and public leader whose work connected environmental protection with community development. She was born in 1940 in Nyeri, Kenya.</p>

      <p>Maathai pursued higher education in the United States before continuing her academic career in Kenya. She became known for advocating the protection of forests and for encouraging communities, especially women, to take part in tree planting and environmental conservation.</p>

      <p>In 1977, she founded the Green Belt Movement. The organisation supported community tree planting and drew attention to the links between environmental health, livelihoods and public participation.</p>

      <p>Her environmental activism also brought her into public debates about governance, land and human rights. In 2004, she received the Nobel Peace Prize in recognition of her contribution to sustainable development, democracy and peace.</p>

      <p>Wangari Maathai died in 2011. Her legacy continues through environmental organisations and people who work to protect natural resources and strengthen communities.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>How the biography is organised</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>It introduces the person and gives relevant background.</li>
      <li>It presents significant events in chronological order.</li>
      <li>It explains contributions and their wider significance.</li>
      <li>It ends by describing the person''s legacy.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>For a biography of a living person or a less widely documented individual, verify dates, achievements and quotations before including them.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Biographies', 15),
  (1982, 1982, '
    <h2>Press Releases</h2>

    <p>A press release is an announcement prepared by an organisation for journalists or the public. It presents the most important information first and provides a contact for further enquiries. It should distinguish confirmed facts from plans or expectations.</p>

    <h3>Complete example</h3>

    <div class="writing-example">
      <h3>FOR IMMEDIATE RELEASE</h3>

      <p><strong>Embu Secondary School Announces Annual Reading Day</strong></p>

      <p><strong>EMBU, 10 October 2026</strong> — Embu Secondary School has announced that its annual Reading Day will take place on 24 October 2026 at the school library.</p>

      <p>The event will bring students together for individual reading, small-group book discussions and short presentations recommending books to other learners. The school reading club is organising the programme with the support of teachers.</p>

      <p>“We want students to discover books they enjoy and feel confident sharing what they learn,” said Kevin Mwangi, secretary of the reading club.</p>

      <p>The programme is scheduled to begin at 9.00 a.m. Students interested in participating should contact the English teacher by 20 October. Attendance by visitors will be subject to school approval.</p>

      <p><strong>Media and Information Contact:</strong></p>
      <p>Amina Hassan</p>
      <p>Reading Club Secretary</p>
      <p>Email: readingclub@example.com</p>

      <p><strong>About Embu Secondary School</strong></p>
      <p>Embu Secondary School provides secondary education and supports student participation in academic and co-curricular activities.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Key conventions</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Put the main announcement near the beginning.</li>
      <li>Include a dateline and the essential event details.</li>
      <li>Use quotations only when they are real and approved.</li>
      <li>Provide a contact for follow-up questions.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>The sample quotation above is illustrative. In a real press release, do not attribute invented words to a real person.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Press Releases', 15),
  (1983, 1983, '
    <h2>Personal Statements</h2>

    <p>A personal statement explains why a person is applying for a course, programme or opportunity. It connects interests, experience, skills and future plans to the specific opportunity. It should sound personal without making unsupported claims.</p>

    <h3>Complete example: Application for a Student Coding Programme</h3>

    <div class="writing-example">
      <h3>PERSONAL STATEMENT</h3>

      <p>I am applying for the Student Coding Programme because I want to learn how software can solve practical problems in my community. My interest began when I tried to understand how educational applications help learners practise difficult topics and track their progress.</p>

      <p>At school, I have developed patience through solving Mathematics problems and persistence when a solution does not work the first time. I also enjoy working with classmates because explaining an idea often reveals whether I truly understand it.</p>

      <p>I am particularly interested in learning the fundamentals of programming, testing and user-centred design. I would like to build small projects, receive feedback and improve them through repeated practice.</p>

      <p>I understand that software development requires more than writing code. It also requires listening to users, checking assumptions and taking responsibility for errors. I am ready to learn from mistakes and work consistently with other participants.</p>

      <p>By joining the programme, I hope to strengthen my technical foundations and develop the skills needed to create useful and reliable software. I would contribute curiosity, persistence and a willingness to collaborate.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>How to strengthen a personal statement</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Explain why this particular opportunity matters to you.</li>
      <li>Use specific experiences to support claims about your skills.</li>
      <li>Connect your interests to what the programme offers.</li>
      <li>End with a realistic account of what you hope to contribute and learn.</li>
    </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Personal Statements', 15),
  (1984, 1984, '
    <h2>Evaluation Reports</h2>

    <p>An evaluation report judges how well a project, event or programme met its aims. It uses evidence to identify achievements, weaknesses and improvements. It should not confuse what was planned with what actually happened.</p>

    <h3>Complete example: Evaluation of a School Reading Day</h3>

    <div class="writing-example">
      <h3>EVALUATION REPORT: SCHOOL READING DAY</h3>
      <p><strong>Prepared by:</strong> School Reading Club</p>
      <p><strong>Date:</strong> 30 October 2026</p>

      <h4>1. Purpose of the Evaluation</h4>
      <p>This report evaluates the school Reading Day held on 24 October 2026 and identifies improvements for future events.</p>

      <h4>2. Intended Outcomes</h4>
      <p>The organisers aimed to encourage reading, involve students in book discussions and give participants an opportunity to recommend books.</p>

      <h4>3. Evidence Collected</h4>
      <p>The organisers recorded attendance, reviewed the event schedule and collected feedback forms from participants. The figures below are illustrative for this model report.</p>

      <h4>4. Results</h4>
      <p>Forty students attended. Thirty-two feedback forms were returned. Twenty-six respondents said that the group discussion was useful, while six asked for more time to read before discussing their books.</p>

      <h4>5. What Worked Well</h4>
      <p>The group discussions gave students opportunities to explain their opinions. The book-recommendation session also introduced participants to titles they had not previously considered.</p>

      <h4>6. Challenges</h4>
      <p>The schedule allowed limited time for individual reading. Some groups also needed clearer instructions about how long each presentation should last.</p>

      <h4>7. Recommendations</h4>
      <ol>
        <li>Increase the time allocated to individual reading.</li>
        <li>Provide presentation guidelines before the event.</li>
        <li>Continue collecting feedback to compare future events.</li>
      </ol>

      <h4>8. Conclusion</h4>
      <p>The event supported student discussion and book sharing. More preparation time and clearer presentation guidelines could improve the next Reading Day.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Evaluation requires evidence</h3>
    <p>A good evaluation explains what evidence was collected and how it supports the judgement. If the report uses estimates or sample figures, label them clearly rather than presenting them as verified results.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Evaluation Reports', 15),
  (1985, 1985, '
    <h2>Listening for Main Ideas</h2>

    <p>The main idea is the central message a speaker wants the audience to understand. It is broader than a single fact or example.</p>

    <h3>How to identify the main idea</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Listen for the topic being discussed.</li>
      <li>Notice ideas the speaker repeats or emphasises.</li>
      <li>Separate the central message from supporting examples.</li>
      <li>Summarise the message in one sentence.</li>
      <li>Check that the sentence represents the whole message, not just one detail.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Listening passage</h3>
    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:14px;">
      <p><strong>Teacher''s announcement:</strong></p>
      <p>Our school has introduced a reading period every Wednesday afternoon. During this time, learners may read novels, biographies, newspapers or other suitable materials. Teachers will help learners choose texts appropriate to their interests and reading levels. The purpose is to develop regular reading habits and improve learners'' ability to understand different kinds of texts. Learners should bring their reading materials and use the period quietly.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Identifying the main idea</h3>
    <p>The announcement explains a new weekly reading period intended to develop regular reading habits and reading comprehension.</p>

    <p>The types of reading materials are supporting details. They help explain the programme, but they are not the central message by themselves.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Main Ideas', 15),
  (1986, 1986, '
    <h2>Listening for Specific Details</h2>

    <p>Listening for specific details means identifying exact information such as times, places, quantities, names, instructions and deadlines.</p>

    <h3>Effective strategy</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Identify what information the question asks for.</li>
      <li>Listen for key words and related expressions.</li>
      <li>Record exact figures and names carefully.</li>
      <li>Distinguish similar details, such as a meeting time and a submission deadline.</li>
      <li>Check your answer against the information actually spoken.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Listening passage</h3>
    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:14px;">
      <p><strong>Club secretary''s announcement:</strong></p>
      <p>The Environmental Club will meet on Thursday, 22 October, at 3:40 p.m. in the science laboratory. Members should bring their notebooks. The clean-up exercise will take place on Saturday, 24 October, beginning at 8:00 a.m. Anyone who cannot attend the meeting should send an apology to the club secretary before Thursday afternoon.</p>
    </div>

    <p>When answering questions, use the exact information supplied. Do not confuse the Thursday meeting with the Saturday clean-up exercise.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Specific Details', 15),
  (1987, 1987, '
    <h2>Listening for Names, Dates and Numbers</h2>

    <p>Names, dates, times, telephone numbers and quantities are easy to confuse when listening. Accurate note-taking helps preserve this information.</p>

    <h3>What to do</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Listen carefully for the complete name, not just its first part.</li>
      <li>Distinguish similar-sounding numbers.</li>
      <li>Record dates with the month and day when necessary.</li>
      <li>Write times with a.m. or p.m. when the distinction matters.</li>
      <li>Ask for repetition or clarification when it is appropriate and possible.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Listening passage</h3>
    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:14px;">
      <p><strong>School office announcement:</strong></p>
      <p>Students selected for the science exhibition should report to the laboratory on Monday, 26 October, at 9:15 a.m. The registration list will be held by Ms Njeri. Each group must submit three display labels and one project summary. The final submission deadline is Wednesday, 28 October, at 4:00 p.m.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Model listening notes</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Activity:</strong> Science exhibition</li>
      <li><strong>Report to:</strong> Laboratory</li>
      <li><strong>Meeting date and time:</strong> Monday, 26 October, 9:15 a.m.</li>
      <li><strong>Contact person:</strong> Ms Njeri</li>
      <li><strong>Materials per group:</strong> Three display labels and one project summary</li>
      <li><strong>Deadline:</strong> Wednesday, 28 October, 4:00 p.m.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Organised notes make it easier to find the correct detail later. They also reduce the risk of confusing a meeting time with a submission deadline.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Names, Dates and Numbers', 15),
  (1988, 1988, '
    <h2>Following Multi-step Instructions</h2>

    <p>Multi-step instructions contain two or more actions that must be completed in a particular order. Missing or rearranging a step can lead to an incorrect result.</p>

    <h3>How to follow spoken instructions</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Listen to the full instruction before beginning, unless immediate action is required for safety.</li>
      <li>Identify the order of actions.</li>
      <li>Notice sequence words such as first, next, after that and finally.</li>
      <li>Remember conditions, warnings and deadlines.</li>
      <li>Repeat or write down the steps if needed.</li>
      <li>Check that the final task has been completed.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Listening passage</h3>
    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:14px;">
      <p><strong>Teacher''s instructions:</strong></p>
      <p>Before beginning the experiment, collect a clean beaker and place it on the laboratory bench. Next, measure 50 millilitres of water and pour it into the beaker. Record the initial volume in your notebook. Then add the supplied material and observe what happens. Record your observations before cleaning the equipment and returning it to the designated storage area. Do not taste or touch any laboratory material unless your teacher has explicitly confirmed that it is safe to do so.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Correct order of actions</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Collect a clean beaker and place it on the bench.</li>
      <li>Measure 50 millilitres of water and pour it into the beaker.</li>
      <li>Record the initial volume.</li>
      <li>Add the supplied material and observe.</li>
      <li>Record observations.</li>
      <li>Clean and return the equipment as instructed.</li>
    </ol>

    <p>Do not skip a recording step simply because the next action seems more interesting. In a practical activity, the order may be necessary for accurate results and safe work.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Following Multi-step Instructions', 15),
  (1989, 1989, '
    <h2>Speaker Purpose</h2>

    <p>A speaker''s purpose is the main reason for speaking. A speaker may want to inform, persuade, entertain, warn, instruct or request something.</p>

    <h3>How to identify purpose</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Identify the subject of the message.</li>
      <li>Notice what the speaker wants listeners to know, believe, feel or do.</li>
      <li>Look for instructions, reasons, warnings or attempts to influence the audience.</li>
      <li>Choose the purpose that best explains the whole message.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Listening passage A: Informing</h3>
    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:12px;">
      <p><strong>School announcement:</strong></p>
      <p>The school library will open at 8:00 a.m. from Monday to Friday beginning next week. Learners may borrow two books at a time. All borrowed books must be returned by the date recorded in the borrowing register.</p>
    </div>
    <p><strong>Purpose:</strong> To inform learners about library opening hours and borrowing rules.</p>

    <h3>Listening passage B: Persuading</h3>
    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:12px;">
      <p><strong>Environmental Club representative:</strong></p>
      <p>Fellow learners, we can reduce litter around our school if each of us uses the bins provided. Instead of leaving wrappers on the ground, carry them to a bin. Let us make our school cleaner through small actions that we practise every day.</p>
    </div>
    <p><strong>Purpose:</strong> To persuade learners to dispose of waste responsibly.</p>

    <h3>Listening passage C: Warning</h3>
    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:12px;">
      <p><strong>Laboratory teacher:</strong></p>
      <p>Do not begin the experiment until I have explained the safety procedure. Some materials can cause injury if they are handled incorrectly. Keep all materials away from your mouth and follow the instructions given during the demonstration.</p>
    </div>
    <p><strong>Purpose:</strong> To warn learners about hazards and prevent unsafe behaviour.</p>

    <p>Do not identify purpose using one word alone. Consider the complete message and the action or response the speaker wants from the audience.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Speaker Purpose', 15),
  (1990, 1990, '
    <h2>Attitude and Emotion</h2>

    <p>A speaker''s attitude is their viewpoint or feeling towards a subject. Emotion is the feeling conveyed through words, voice, pace and emphasis.</p>

    <h3>Common attitudes and emotions</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Concern:</strong> The speaker believes a problem needs attention.</li>
      <li><strong>Enthusiasm:</strong> The speaker expresses excitement or strong interest.</li>
      <li><strong>Disappointment:</strong> The speaker expresses dissatisfaction about an outcome.</li>
      <li><strong>Uncertainty:</strong> The speaker is not sure what is true or what will happen.</li>
      <li><strong>Confidence:</strong> The speaker presents a view with assurance.</li>
      <li><strong>Sarcasm:</strong> The speaker uses words to mock or criticise, often meaning the opposite of their literal meaning.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Example 1: Concern</h3>
    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:12px;">
      <p><strong>Teacher:</strong> I am concerned that several learners have missed the revision sessions. The examination is approaching, and we need to make sure everyone has access to the material.</p>
    </div>
    <p>The words "I am concerned" directly express worry about learners missing revision.</p>

    <h3>Example 2: Enthusiasm</h3>
    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:12px;">
      <p><strong>Learner:</strong> I cannot wait for the science exhibition! Our group has worked hard on the model, and I am excited to show everyone how it works.</p>
    </div>
    <p>The phrases "cannot wait" and "I am excited" signal enthusiasm.</p>

    <h3>Example 3: Disappointment</h3>
    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:12px;">
      <p><strong>Coach:</strong> We practised carefully, but we did not follow our plan during the match. I expected a more disciplined performance.</p>
    </div>
    <p>The contrast between preparation and the result, together with the final sentence, suggests disappointment.</p>

    <p>In real listening tasks, attitude may be implied rather than directly named. Consider the words and the way they are delivered; do not assume that loudness alone means anger or that quiet speech means sadness.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Attitude and Emotion', 15),
  (1991, 1991, '
    <h2>Inferring Spoken Meaning</h2>

    <p>An inference is a reasonable conclusion drawn from what a speaker says and the context in which it is said. Speakers do not always state every idea directly.</p>

    <h3>How to make a sound inference</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Identify what the speaker explicitly says.</li>
      <li>Consider the situation and relevant background details.</li>
      <li>Connect the clues logically.</li>
      <li>Choose a conclusion supported by those clues.</li>
      <li>Avoid conclusions that require facts not provided.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Listening passage</h3>
    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:12px;">
      <p><strong>Conversation:</strong></p>
      <p><strong>Brian:</strong> Have you finished the science assignment?</p>
      <p><strong>Wanjiku:</strong> I have completed the diagrams and the first two questions, but I have not understood the final calculation. I am going to ask the teacher for help before submitting it tomorrow.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>What can we infer?</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Supported inference:</strong> Wanjiku has not completed the entire assignment.</li>
      <li><strong>Evidence:</strong> She says that she has not understood the final calculation and plans to seek help.</li>
      <li><strong>Unsupported inference:</strong> Wanjiku never studies science.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>The passage gives information about one assignment. It does not establish Wanjiku''s overall ability or study habits.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Inferring Spoken Meaning', 15),
  (1992, 1992, '
    <h2>Note-taking While Listening</h2>

    <p>Listening notes capture the most important information from a spoken message. Good notes are brief, organised and accurate enough to be understood later.</p>

    <h3>How to take useful notes</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Listen for the topic and main points first.</li>
      <li>Record important facts, names, dates and quantities.</li>
      <li>Use short phrases instead of copying every sentence.</li>
      <li>Use headings and bullets to group related ideas.</li>
      <li>Use abbreviations only when you can understand them later.</li>
      <li>Review the notes soon after listening and clarify unclear details.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Listening passage</h3>
    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:12px;">
      <p><strong>Teacher''s talk: Preparing for examinations</strong></p>
      <p>Effective examination preparation begins with a realistic timetable. Learners should divide large topics into manageable sections and practise answering questions without looking at their notes. After marking their work, they should identify repeated mistakes and revisit the exact concepts they do not understand. Regular sleep and short breaks can also help learners maintain concentration. Finally, learners should prepare their materials the night before an examination to avoid unnecessary morning stress.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Example of effective notes</h3>
    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:12px;">
      <p><strong>Topic:</strong> Examination preparation</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Create a realistic timetable.</li>
        <li>Break large topics into manageable sections.</li>
        <li>Practise questions without notes.</li>
        <li>Mark work; identify repeated errors.</li>
        <li>Revisit concepts not understood.</li>
        <li>Maintain sleep and take short breaks.</li>
        <li>Prepare examination materials the night before.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <p>The notes preserve the main actions without copying the entire talk. They should still be specific enough to support revision later.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Note-taking', 15),
  (1993, 1993, '
    <h2>Summarising Spoken Information</h2>

    <p>A spoken summary presents the central message and most important supporting points in fewer words than the original talk. It should preserve the meaning without adding personal opinions.</p>

    <h3>Steps for summarising</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Identify the main idea.</li>
      <li>Select the essential supporting points.</li>
      <li>Remove examples and repetition that are not necessary.</li>
      <li>Combine related ideas in your own words.</li>
      <li>Check that the summary preserves the speaker''s meaning.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Listening passage</h3>
    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:12px;">
      <p><strong>School counsellor:</strong></p>
      <p>Healthy study habits depend on planning and consistency. Learners should set realistic goals, study difficult topics in manageable sessions and test themselves to discover what they have not understood. When they make mistakes, they should review the steps that caused the error rather than merely memorising the correct answer. Learners should also take breaks and get adequate sleep so that they can maintain concentration. A timetable is useful only when it is realistic and followed regularly.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Model summary</h3>
    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:12px;">
      <p>Effective study requires a realistic timetable, manageable study sessions and regular self-testing. Learners should analyse mistakes, review misunderstood concepts, take breaks and get enough sleep. Consistent use of a practical plan is more useful than creating an unrealistic timetable.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Why this summary works</h3>
    <p>It preserves the central advice and its main supporting points, combines related ideas and avoids adding claims that the speaker did not make.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Summarising Spoken Information', 15),
  (1994, 1994, '
    <h2>Organising Spoken Ideas</h2>

    <p>Organising spoken ideas means arranging what you say so that listeners can follow your message. A speaker should establish the topic, develop connected points and finish with a clear conclusion.</p>

    <h3>A practical structure</h3>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Opening:</strong> State the topic or purpose.</li>
      <li><strong>Point one:</strong> Present the first important idea and explain it.</li>
      <li><strong>Point two:</strong> Add another connected idea or example.</li>
      <li><strong>Point three:</strong> Develop the next relevant idea, if needed.</li>
      <li><strong>Conclusion:</strong> Summarise the message or state the next action.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Example of disorganised speaking</h3>
    <p>"We need more books. The library is quiet. Last week it rained. Some learners borrow novels. We should have a reading club."</p>

    <p>The ideas are related only loosely and are not connected into a clear message.</p>

    <h3>Improved spoken contribution</h3>
    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:12px;">
      <p>"I would like to propose that our school establish a reading club. First, the club could help learners develop a regular reading habit. Second, members could recommend books and discuss what they have read. Finally, the club could organise a shared reading session each month. I believe this would make reading more engaging and encourage learners to explore different texts."</p>
    </div>

    <p>The improved version introduces a clear proposal, develops three connected points and concludes by explaining the expected benefit.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Organising Spoken Ideas', 15),
  (1995, 1995, '
    <h2>Clear Explanations</h2>

    <p>A clear explanation helps another person understand how something works, why something happens or how to complete a task.</p>

    <h3>How to explain clearly</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Begin with the main idea.</li>
      <li>Use familiar words where possible.</li>
      <li>Break complicated information into manageable steps.</li>
      <li>Explain technical terms that the listener may not know.</li>
      <li>Use a relevant example or comparison.</li>
      <li>Check whether the listener understands.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Example: Explaining evaporation</h3>
    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:12px;">
      <p><strong>Unclear explanation:</strong> "Evaporation is when water does its thing and disappears because it gets hot."</p>

      <p><strong>Clear explanation:</strong> "Evaporation occurs when particles at the surface of a liquid gain enough energy to enter the air as a gas. For example, a puddle gradually becomes smaller after a sunny day because some of its water changes into water vapour. The water has not simply vanished; it has changed state and entered the air."</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Example: Explaining a mathematical procedure</h3>
    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:12px;">
      <p><strong>Question:</strong> Solve 3x + 5 = 20.</p>
      <p><strong>Explanation:</strong></p>
      <ol>
        <li>Subtract 5 from both sides: 3x = 15.</li>
        <li>Divide both sides by 3: x = 5.</li>
        <li>Check the result: 3(5) + 5 = 20, so the answer is correct.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>

    <p>A clear explanation does not skip a necessary step or use a term in a way that changes the meaning.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Clear Explanations', 15),
  (1996, 1996, '
    <h2>Forming Questions</h2>

    <p>Clear questions help speakers obtain information, check understanding and guide a discussion. A good question makes it clear what information is needed.</p>

    <h3>Common question types</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Yes/no questions:</strong> Often begin with an auxiliary verb, such as "Did you finish the assignment?"</li>
      <li><strong>Wh-questions:</strong> Ask for information using who, what, when, where, why or how.</li>
      <li><strong>Clarification questions:</strong> Ask someone to explain a point more clearly.</li>
      <li><strong>Follow-up questions:</strong> Ask for more information about an answer.</li>
      <li><strong>Open questions:</strong> Encourage a developed response rather than a simple yes or no.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Model conversation</h3>
    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:12px;">
      <p><strong>Brian:</strong> I did not understand the final step in the mathematics example.</p>
      <p><strong>Amina:</strong> Which part was confusing?</p>
      <p><strong>Brian:</strong> Why did the teacher divide both sides by three?</p>
      <p><strong>Amina:</strong> Because the equation had 3x = 15. Dividing both sides by three leaves x on its own and gives x = 5.</p>
      <p><strong>Brian:</strong> Could you show me how the answer is checked?</p>
      <p><strong>Amina:</strong> Substitute 5 for x in the original equation: 3(5) + 5 = 20. The left side equals the right side, so the solution is correct.</p>
    </div>

    <p>The questions identify the precise difficulty, ask for the reason behind a step and request a way to verify the answer.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Forming Questions', 15),
  (1997, 1997, '
    <h2>Relevant Answers</h2>

    <p>A relevant answer addresses the question that was asked. It gives enough information to respond clearly without drifting into unrelated details.</p>

    <h3>How to answer well</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Listen to the entire question.</li>
      <li>Identify the information being requested.</li>
      <li>Answer that point directly.</li>
      <li>Add a reason or example when useful.</li>
      <li>Stop when the question has been answered, unless more detail is requested.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Model interview conversation</h3>
    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:12px;">
      <p><strong>Interviewer:</strong> Why would you like to join the Environmental Club?</p>
      <p><strong>Applicant:</strong> I would like to join because I want to help keep the school clean and learn more about environmental conservation. I can also help organise clean-up activities and encourage other learners to dispose of waste responsibly.</p>

      <p><strong>Interviewer:</strong> How would you respond if a clean-up activity had very few volunteers?</p>
      <p><strong>Applicant:</strong> I would first ask learners why participation was low. Then I would explain the purpose of the activity, agree on a convenient time with the club and invite volunteers to take manageable roles.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Compare an irrelevant answer</h3>
    <p><strong>Question:</strong> Why would you like to join the Environmental Club?</p>
    <p><strong>Irrelevant answer:</strong> My favourite subject is mathematics, and I travelled to my grandmother''s home last holiday.</p>

    <p>The answer may contain true statements, but it does not explain the applicant''s reason for joining the club.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Relevant Answers', 15),
  (1998, 1998, '
    <h2>Turn-taking</h2>

    <p>Turn-taking is the process of sharing speaking time in a conversation. Effective speakers recognise when to speak, when to listen and how to enter or leave a discussion politely.</p>

    <h3>Useful turn-taking expressions</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Entering:</strong> "May I add something?"</li>
      <li><strong>Giving an opinion:</strong> "I think we should consider another option."</li>
      <li><strong>Agreeing:</strong> "I agree with that point because..."</li>
      <li><strong>Disagreeing politely:</strong> "I understand your view, but I see it differently."</li>
      <li><strong>Inviting others:</strong> "What do you think about this suggestion?"</li>
      <li><strong>Clarifying:</strong> "Could you explain what you mean by that?"</li>
      <li><strong>Yielding the turn:</strong> "That is my view. I would like to hear what others think."</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Model group discussion</h3>
    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:12px;">
      <p><strong>Chairperson:</strong> We need to decide how to improve waste disposal around the school. Brian, would you like to begin?</p>

      <p><strong>Brian:</strong> I think we should place more bins near the playing field because that area often has litter.</p>

      <p><strong>Amina:</strong> I agree that bins would help. May I add that we should also label them so learners know where different kinds of waste belong?</p>

      <p><strong>Wanjiku:</strong> I understand both suggestions, but I think we should first check whether the existing bins are emptied regularly. Additional bins will not solve the problem if they overflow.</p>

      <p><strong>Chairperson:</strong> That is an important point. Could we agree to inspect the existing bins this week and then decide where additional bins are needed?</p>

      <p><strong>Brian:</strong> I support that plan. We can record which areas need more bins.</p>

      <p><strong>Chairperson:</strong> Thank you. We will inspect the bins on Friday and discuss the findings at our next meeting.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>What the discussion demonstrates</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Participants contribute one at a time.</li>
      <li>They respond to the previous speaker''s ideas.</li>
      <li>They disagree without insulting one another.</li>
      <li>The chairperson invites contributions and guides the group towards a decision.</li>
    </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Turn-taking', 15),
  (1999, 1999, '
    <h2>Maintaining Conversations</h2>

    <p>Maintaining a conversation means keeping an exchange going through relevant responses, follow-up questions and contributions connected to what another person has said.</p>

    <h3>Ways to maintain a conversation</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Listen carefully before responding.</li>
      <li>Answer the question or comment directly.</li>
      <li>Ask a relevant follow-up question.</li>
      <li>Share a related experience or idea.</li>
      <li>Allow the other person an opportunity to speak.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Example: A conversation about reading</h3>
    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:12px;">
      <p><strong>Amina:</strong> I have started reading novels during the weekend.</p>
      <p><strong>Brian:</strong> That sounds interesting. What kind of novels do you enjoy?</p>
      <p><strong>Amina:</strong> I enjoy mystery stories because I like trying to solve the problem before the characters do.</p>
      <p><strong>Brian:</strong> I can understand that. Have you read any good mystery novels recently?</p>
      <p><strong>Amina:</strong> Yes. I borrowed one from the school library last week. The ending surprised me.</p>
      <p><strong>Brian:</strong> What made the ending surprising?</p>
      <p><strong>Amina:</strong> The character I suspected was innocent. The real culprit had been leaving clues throughout the story.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Why the conversation works</h3>
    <p>Each response connects to the previous contribution. Brian answers naturally, asks relevant follow-up questions and gives Amina room to develop her ideas.</p>

    <h3>Example of a conversation that stops</h3>
    <p><strong>Amina:</strong> I have started reading novels during the weekend.</p>
    <p><strong>Brian:</strong> Okay.</p>
    <p>"Okay" is not always incorrect, but by itself it gives the other speaker little opportunity to continue.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Maintaining Conversations', 15),
  (2000, 2000, '
    <h2>Checking Understanding</h2>

    <p>Checking understanding means confirming that a message has been heard and interpreted correctly. It is especially useful when instructions, unfamiliar terms or important decisions are involved.</p>

    <h3>Useful expressions</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>"Do you mean that we should submit the report on Friday?"</li>
      <li>"Could you explain the second step again?"</li>
      <li>"Let me check that I have understood correctly."</li>
      <li>"So, we should complete the experiment first and record the results afterwards. Is that right?"</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Example: Clarifying an assignment</h3>
    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:12px;">
      <p><strong>Teacher:</strong> Work in groups of four. Prepare a report on water conservation and submit it next Friday. Each learner must contribute to the research.</p>
      <p><strong>Kevin:</strong> Excuse me, teacher. Should the report be handwritten or typed?</p>
      <p><strong>Teacher:</strong> It should be typed.</p>
      <p><strong>Kevin:</strong> Let me confirm: we work in groups of four, everyone contributes, and we submit one typed report next Friday. Is that correct?</p>
      <p><strong>Teacher:</strong> Yes, that is correct.</p>
    </div>

    <p>Kevin checks the format and restates the essential instructions. This reduces the chance of completing the task incorrectly.</p>

    <h3>When you do not understand</h3>
    <p>Identify the specific point that is unclear. Instead of saying only "I don''t understand anything," ask about the step, term or instruction that needs clarification.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Checking Understanding', 15);
INSERT INTO public.lessons (id, topic_id, content, summary, estimated_minutes) VALUES
  (2001, 2001, '
    <h2>Expressing Opinions with Reasons</h2>

    <p>An opinion expresses what a person thinks or believes. A well-supported opinion states the position clearly and gives relevant reasons or evidence to explain it.</p>

    <h3>Useful expressions</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>"In my view..."</li>
      <li>"I believe this because..."</li>
      <li>"One reason for my opinion is..."</li>
      <li>"For example..."</li>
      <li>"This suggests that..."</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Model oral contribution</h3>
    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:12px;">
      <p><strong>Topic: Should schools establish quiet study periods?</strong></p>
      <p>"In my view, schools should establish quiet study periods during the week. First, these periods would give learners time to review difficult topics without unnecessary interruptions. Second, learners could identify questions to ask their teachers before examinations. For example, a learner who struggles with algebra could use the period to practise equations and identify the exact step causing difficulty. However, schools should ensure that the periods are supervised and that learners have suitable materials. For these reasons, I believe quiet study periods would be useful."</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Opinion versus unsupported assertion</h3>
    <p><strong>Unsupported:</strong> "Quiet study periods are the best idea because I said so."</p>
    <p><strong>Supported:</strong> "Quiet study periods may help learners concentrate because they provide dedicated time for independent practice."</p>

    <p>A reason strengthens an opinion, but it does not automatically prove that the opinion is true. Evidence and careful reasoning still matter.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Opinions with Reasons', 15),
  (2002, 2002, '
    <h2>Respectful Agreement and Disagreement</h2>

    <p>Respectful agreement acknowledges another person''s view. Respectful disagreement presents a different view without insulting the person who expressed the original idea.</p>

    <h3>Expressions of agreement</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>"I agree with your point because..."</li>
      <li>"That is a good observation."</li>
      <li>"I share that view, particularly because..."</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Expressions of disagreement</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>"I understand your point, but I see it differently."</li>
      <li>"I agree with part of your argument; however..."</li>
      <li>"Could we also consider another possibility?"</li>
      <li>"The evidence may suggest a different conclusion."</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Model conversation</h3>
    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:12px;">
      <p><strong>Peter:</strong> I think our class should use all its activity funds to buy more textbooks.</p>
      <p><strong>Mary:</strong> I agree that textbooks are important. However, I think we should first check which subjects lack enough copies before deciding how to spend all the money.</p>
      <p><strong>Peter:</strong> That is reasonable. I was mainly concerned that some learners do not have access to the required books.</p>
      <p><strong>Mary:</strong> I understand. We could count the available books, identify the biggest shortages and then prioritise purchases.</p>
      <p><strong>Peter:</strong> I support that approach because it would help us direct the money where it is most needed.</p>
    </div>

    <p>The speakers challenge and refine the proposal without attacking each other. They identify common ground and develop a practical solution.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Respectful Agreement and Disagreement', 15),
  (2003, 2003, '
    <h2>Discussions and Debates</h2>

    <p>A discussion allows participants to explore an issue and exchange views. A debate is more structured: speakers argue for or against a stated motion, using reasons and evidence.</p>

    <h3>Elements of a good debate</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Understand the exact motion.</li>
      <li>State whether you support or oppose it.</li>
      <li>Develop clear arguments.</li>
      <li>Support claims with examples or evidence.</li>
      <li>Address opposing arguments fairly.</li>
      <li>Conclude by explaining why your case is convincing.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Model debate excerpt</h3>
    <p><strong>Motion:</strong> This house believes that secondary schools should provide a daily supervised study period.</p>

    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:12px;">
      <p><strong>Proposition speaker:</strong></p>
      <p>"Chairperson, adjudicators and fellow learners, I support the motion that secondary schools should provide a daily supervised study period. First, it would give learners dedicated time to practise difficult topics and seek clarification. Second, it could help learners establish consistent study habits. For example, a learner who repeatedly struggles with equations could use the period to practise and identify the step causing the difficulty. I recognise that schools have limited time, so the period should be short and carefully scheduled. I therefore urge the house to support the motion."</p>

      <p><strong>Opposition speaker:</strong></p>
      <p>"Chairperson, I oppose the motion as a requirement for every school. Although supervised study can be useful, schools have different timetables and learner needs. Some learners may already have supervised preparation periods, while others may need time for clubs, sports or travel home. Instead of requiring the same arrangement everywhere, schools should be allowed to choose a suitable approach based on their circumstances. For these reasons, I oppose making a daily supervised study period compulsory for all schools."</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Evaluating the arguments</h3>
    <p>The proposition argues that regular supervised practice can support learning. The opposition argues that a compulsory arrangement may not suit every school. Both speakers address the motion and give reasons rather than relying on personal attacks.</p>

    <p>A strong debater responds to the opposing argument, not to an exaggerated version of it. Claims should be supported with evidence where available.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Discussions and Debates', 15),
  (2004, 2004, '
    <h2>Oral Presentations</h2>

    <p>An oral presentation is a planned talk delivered to an audience. It should have a clear purpose, an organised structure and language appropriate to the audience.</p>

    <h3>Planning the presentation</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Identify the topic and audience.</li>
      <li>Choose a small number of main points.</li>
      <li>Arrange the points in a logical order.</li>
      <li>Prepare examples or evidence.</li>
      <li>Practise speaking clearly and within the available time.</li>
      <li>Prepare for likely questions.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Complete model presentation</h3>
    <p><strong>Topic: Reducing Plastic Waste at School</strong></p>

    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:12px;">
      <p><strong>Opening</strong></p>
      <p>"Good morning, teachers and fellow learners. Today, I would like to discuss three practical ways our school can reduce plastic waste."</p>

      <p><strong>First point: Reduce unnecessary plastic</strong></p>
      <p>"First, we can reduce unnecessary plastic by carrying reusable water bottles and lunch containers. This would reduce the number of disposable bottles and wrappers thrown away each day."</p>

      <p><strong>Second point: Improve waste collection</strong></p>
      <p>"Second, we should place clearly labelled waste bins in suitable locations. Learners should use them properly, and the school should ensure that collected waste is handled responsibly."</p>

      <p><strong>Third point: Build shared responsibility</strong></p>
      <p>"Third, environmental clubs and class representatives can organise awareness activities. These activities should explain practical actions rather than simply telling people not to litter."</p>

      <p><strong>Conclusion</strong></p>
      <p>"In conclusion, reducing plastic waste requires prevention, proper collection and shared responsibility. If we begin with reusable containers and use the available bins correctly, we can make our school cleaner. Thank you for listening."</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Delivery</h3>
    <p>Speak at a pace the audience can follow. Look up regularly rather than reading every word from a page. Use pauses between major points and avoid adding information that distracts from the central message.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Oral Presentations', 15),
  (2005, 2005, '
    <h2>Interviews</h2>

    <p>An interview is a structured conversation in which one person asks questions to obtain information, assess suitability or understand another person''s experiences and views.</p>

    <h3>How to respond in an interview</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Listen to the complete question.</li>
      <li>Answer the question directly.</li>
      <li>Give a relevant example when appropriate.</li>
      <li>Be truthful about your experience and abilities.</li>
      <li>Use polite language and an appropriate tone.</li>
      <li>Ask for clarification if a question is unclear.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Complete model interview</h3>
    <p><strong>Situation:</strong> A learner is being interviewed for a student library assistant position.</p>

    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:12px;">
      <p><strong>Interviewer:</strong> Good morning. Please introduce yourself.</p>
      <p><strong>Applicant:</strong> Good morning. My name is Daniel, and I am a learner at this school. I enjoy reading and helping classmates find useful learning materials.</p>

      <p><strong>Interviewer:</strong> Why would you like to become a library assistant?</p>
      <p><strong>Applicant:</strong> I would like to help learners access books and keep the library organised. I also want to develop my responsibility and communication skills.</p>

      <p><strong>Interviewer:</strong> What would you do if a learner returned a book late?</p>
      <p><strong>Applicant:</strong> I would follow the library rules, record the return correctly and explain the procedure politely. If I were unsure what to do, I would ask the teacher in charge.</p>

      <p><strong>Interviewer:</strong> What would you do if you did not know where a particular book was kept?</p>
      <p><strong>Applicant:</strong> I would check the catalogue or ask the librarian for help instead of giving the learner incorrect information.</p>

      <p><strong>Interviewer:</strong> Do you have any questions for us?</p>
      <p><strong>Applicant:</strong> Yes, please. What training would student library assistants receive before beginning their duties?</p>

      <p><strong>Interviewer:</strong> We provide an orientation on borrowing procedures and how to organise the books.</p>
      <p><strong>Applicant:</strong> Thank you for explaining that. I would be happy to learn the procedures and follow them carefully.</p>
    </div>

    <p>The applicant gives direct, relevant answers, describes sensible actions and asks a question connected to the role.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Interviews', 15),
  (2006, 2006, '
    <h2>Facial Expressions and Gestures</h2>

    <p>Non-verbal communication conveys information through facial expressions, gestures, posture, eye contact and physical movement. It can support spoken words, but its meaning depends on context and culture.</p>

    <h3>Examples of non-verbal communication</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Nodding:</strong> May signal agreement or that a listener is following the message.</li>
      <li><strong>Raised hand:</strong> May signal a wish to speak or ask a question.</li>
      <li><strong>Open posture:</strong> May help communicate attentiveness.</li>
      <li><strong>Frowning:</strong> May indicate concern, confusion or displeasure, depending on the situation.</li>
      <li><strong>Eye contact:</strong> Can support engagement, but expectations vary by culture and context.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Example: A classroom presentation</h3>
    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:12px;">
      <p>A learner is presenting a science project. The learner stands facing the class, speaks clearly, occasionally looks at different sections of the audience and uses a hand gesture to indicate a labelled part of the model.</p>
      <p>When a classmate raises a hand, the presenter pauses and invites the question.</p>
    </div>

    <p>The learner''s posture, gestures and response to the raised hand support the spoken message and help manage the interaction.</p>

    <h3>Important caution</h3>
    <p>Do not assume that one gesture always has the same meaning. A person who looks away may be thinking, reading notes, feeling uncomfortable or following a cultural norm. Interpret non-verbal behaviour alongside words and context.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Facial Expressions and Gestures', 15),
  (2007, 2007, '
    <h2>Short Vowel Sounds</h2>

    <p>Vowel letters and vowel sounds are not the same thing. English spelling does not always reveal the exact sound a vowel makes. A short vowel is a term used for a common vowel sound, especially in words such as <em>cat</em>, <em>bed</em>, <em>sit</em>, <em>hot</em> and <em>cup</em>.</p>

    <h3>Examples of common short vowel sounds</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>cat:</strong> the vowel sound in cat.</li>
      <li><strong>bed:</strong> the vowel sound in bed.</li>
      <li><strong>sit:</strong> the vowel sound in sit.</li>
      <li><strong>hot:</strong> the vowel sound in hot; its exact quality varies by accent.</li>
      <li><strong>cup:</strong> the vowel sound in cup.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Compare similar words</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>ship / sheep:</strong> the vowel sounds distinguish the two words.</li>
      <li><strong>full / fool:</strong> the vowel sounds are different in many standard accents.</li>
      <li><strong>bed / bad:</strong> the vowel sounds differ.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Practice passage</h3>
    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:12px;">
      <p>"The cat sat on a mat. Ben put his red bag beside the desk. Kim picked up a cup and set it on the shelf."</p>
    </div>

    <p>Read the passage aloud slowly. Listen for the vowel sounds in <em>cat</em>, <em>Ben</em>, <em>Kim</em>, <em>cup</em> and <em>red</em>. Then read it at a natural pace without changing the words.</p>

    <p>Pronunciation can vary between English accents. The goal is to produce understandable contrasts consistently, not to assume that every speaker uses exactly the same vowel quality.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Short Vowel Sounds', 15),
  (2008, 2008, '
    <h2>Long Vowel Sounds</h2>

    <p>The term "long vowel" is commonly used in English teaching for vowel sounds such as those heard in <em>see</em>, <em>name</em> and <em>food</em>. It does not simply mean that every vowel is held for a long time. The exact vowel quality and duration depend on the word and accent.</p>

    <h3>Common examples</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>see:</strong> the vowel sound in see.</li>
      <li><strong>name:</strong> the vowel sound in name.</li>
      <li><strong>time:</strong> the vowel sound in time.</li>
      <li><strong>home:</strong> the vowel sound in home.</li>
      <li><strong>food:</strong> the vowel sound in food.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Compare words</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>sit / seat:</strong> different vowel sounds.</li>
      <li><strong>cap / cape:</strong> different vowel sounds.</li>
      <li><strong>hop / hope:</strong> different vowel sounds.</li>
      <li><strong>full / fool:</strong> different vowel sounds in many standard accents.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Practice passage</h3>
    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:12px;">
      <p>"Steve chose a seat near the window. He wrote his name, checked the time and put his food beside his bag."</p>
    </div>

    <p>Read the passage aloud. Pay attention to the vowel contrasts in <em>seat</em>, <em>name</em>, <em>time</em> and <em>food</em>. Then compare them with the shorter vowel sounds in words such as <em>sit</em>, <em>cap</em>, <em>hop</em> and <em>full</em>.</p>

    <h3>Spelling is not a perfect guide</h3>
    <p>Different spellings can represent similar sounds, and the same spelling can represent different sounds. Learn the pronunciation of words through listening, speaking and reliable dictionary audio as well as spelling patterns.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Long Vowel Sounds', 15),
  (2009, 2009, '
    <h2>Diphthongs</h2>

    <p>A diphthong is a vowel sound that moves from one vowel position towards another within the same syllable. The tongue and mouth change position as the sound is produced.</p>

    <h3>Common English diphthongs</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>day:</strong> the vowel sound in day.</li>
      <li><strong>my:</strong> the vowel sound in my.</li>
      <li><strong>boy:</strong> the vowel sound in boy.</li>
      <li><strong>go:</strong> the vowel sound in go.</li>
      <li><strong>now:</strong> the vowel sound in now.</li>
      <li><strong>near:</strong> contains a vowel sequence commonly taught as a diphthong in some English accents.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Exact vowel sounds vary by English accent. Some accents pronounce particular vowel sequences differently, especially in words such as <em>near</em>.</p>

    <h3>Practise the movement</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Say <em>day</em> slowly and notice how the vowel changes during the sound.</li>
      <li>Repeat with <em>my</em>, <em>boy</em>, <em>go</em> and <em>now</em>.</li>
      <li>Say each word naturally without separating the vowel movement into two syllables.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Practice passage</h3>
    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:12px;">
      <p>"Today, Roy and I will go to the town fair. We may buy a toy, enjoy the music and head home before it gets too late."</p>
    </div>

    <p>Read the passage aloud. Practise the vowel sounds in <em>today</em>, <em>Roy</em>, <em>my</em>, <em>toy</em>, <em>go</em> and <em>town</em>.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Diphthongs', 15),
  (2010, 2010, '
    <h2>Voiced and Voiceless Consonants</h2>

    <p>Voiced consonants are produced with vibration of the vocal folds. Voiceless consonants are produced without vocal-fold vibration during the consonant sound.</p>

    <h3>Common consonant pairs</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>/p/ and /b/:</strong> pat / bat.</li>
      <li><strong>/t/ and /d/:</strong> ten / den.</li>
      <li><strong>/k/ and /g/:</strong> coat / goat.</li>
      <li><strong>/f/ and /v/:</strong> fan / van.</li>
      <li><strong>/s/ and /z/:</strong> sip / zip.</li>
      <li><strong>/θ/ and /ð/:</strong> thin / then.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Feel the difference</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Place your fingers lightly on the front of your throat.</li>
      <li>Say a long /s/ sound, then a long /z/ sound.</li>
      <li>You should feel vocal-fold vibration more clearly during /z/.</li>
      <li>Repeat with /f/ and /v/.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Practice passage</h3>
    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:12px;">
      <p>"Ben packed a bag before visiting his friend. They found fresh fruit near the van and shared it beside the gate."</p>
    </div>

    <p>Practise the initial consonants in <em>packed</em>, <em>bag</em>, <em>friend</em>, <em>fresh</em>, <em>fruit</em> and <em>van</em>. Listen for the difference between paired consonants.</p>

    <p>Vocal-fold vibration is one feature of consonants; tongue and lip positions also help distinguish them.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Voiced and Voiceless Consonants', 15),
  (2011, 2011, '
    <h2>Contrasting Consonant Sounds</h2>

    <p>English contains consonant sounds that differ in how or where they are produced. Confusing them can change the meaning of a word.</p>

    <h3>Useful contrasts</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>/l/ and /r/:</strong> light / right.</li>
      <li><strong>/ʃ/ and /tʃ/:</strong> ship / chip.</li>
      <li><strong>/s/ and /ʃ/:</strong> sip / ship.</li>
      <li><strong>/θ/ and /t/:</strong> thin / tin.</li>
      <li><strong>/v/ and /w/:</strong> vest / west in accents that distinguish them.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>How to practise a contrast</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Listen to a reliable pronunciation of each word.</li>
      <li>Identify the consonant that changes.</li>
      <li>Practise the two words separately.</li>
      <li>Say them in short phrases and sentences.</li>
      <li>Ask a listener whether the words sound distinct.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Practice passage</h3>
    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:12px;">
      <p>"The ship reached the shore before sunset. Chip carried a small bag to the light near the right-hand door."</p>
    </div>

    <p>Practise the words <em>ship</em>, <em>shore</em>, <em>Chip</em>, <em>light</em> and <em>right</em>. Keep the target consonants distinct.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Contrasting Consonant Sounds', 15),
  (2012, 2012, '
    <h2>Similar English Sounds</h2>

    <p>Some English sounds are difficult to distinguish because they are produced in similar ways. A minimal pair is a pair of words that differ in one sound and have different meanings.</p>

    <h3>Minimal-pair examples</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>ship / sheep</li>
      <li>bed / bad</li>
      <li>fan / van</li>
      <li>thin / tin</li>
      <li>rice / rise</li>
      <li>cap / cup</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Practice method</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Listen to the two words.</li>
      <li>Identify the sound that changes.</li>
      <li>Repeat each word carefully.</li>
      <li>Mix the words and ask a partner to identify which one you said.</li>
      <li>Use each word in a sentence to confirm its meaning.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Example sentences</h3>
    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:12px;">
      <p>"The <strong>ship</strong> left the harbour."</p>
      <p>"The sheep grazed near the fence."</p>
      <p>"Please put the <strong>cap</strong> on the bottle."</p>
      <p>"She filled the <strong>cup</strong> with water."</p>
    </div>

    <p>Do not rely on spelling alone. Listen to the sound and consider how it changes the meaning of the sentence.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Similar English Sounds', 15),
  (2013, 2013, '
    <h2>Identifying Syllables</h2>

    <p>A syllable is a unit of spoken language organised around a vowel sound. Counting syllables means counting the vowel sounds heard in a word, not simply counting vowel letters.</p>

    <h3>Examples</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>book:</strong> 1 syllable.</li>
      <li><strong>teacher:</strong> 2 syllables — teach-er.</li>
      <li><strong>computer:</strong> 3 syllables — com-pu-ter.</li>
      <li><strong>education:</strong> commonly 4 syllables — ed-u-ca-tion.</li>
      <li><strong>information:</strong> commonly 4 syllables — in-for-ma-tion.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>How to count syllables</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Say the word naturally.</li>
      <li>Listen for each vowel sound or spoken beat.</li>
      <li>Say the word slowly if necessary.</li>
      <li>Check with a reliable pronunciation guide when the word is unfamiliar.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Practice sentences</h3>
    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:12px;">
      <p>"The teacher opened the computer laboratory."</p>
      <p>"Education provides opportunities for learning."</p>
    </div>

    <p>Practise the syllables in <em>teacher</em>, <em>computer</em>, <em>laboratory</em>, <em>education</em> and <em>opportunities</em>. Syllable divisions in writing are useful guides, but actual pronunciation is what determines the spoken syllable count.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Identifying Syllables', 15),
  (2014, 2014, '
    <h2>Word Stress</h2>

    <p>Word stress is the emphasis placed on one syllable in a word. A stressed syllable is often more prominent because of a combination of loudness, length and pitch.</p>

    <h3>Examples of word stress</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>TA</strong>-ble</li>
      <li><strong>DOC</strong>-tor</li>
      <li>com-<strong>PU</strong>-ter</li>
      <li>edu-<strong>CA</strong>-tion</li>
      <li>in-for-<strong>MA</strong>-tion</li>
    </ul>

    <p>The bold syllable represents the main stress in these common pronunciations. Stress can vary in some words and accents, so check an appropriate dictionary when unsure.</p>

    <h3>Why stress matters</h3>
    <p>Incorrect stress can make speech harder to understand. It can also distinguish words or grammatical forms in English.</p>

    <h3>Practice passage</h3>
    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:12px;">
      <p>"The <strong>DOC</strong>tor explained the in-for-<strong>MA</strong>-tion about the com-<strong>PU</strong>-ter to the class."</p>
    </div>

    <p>Read the passage aloud. Make the stressed syllables prominent without shouting, and keep the other syllables clear.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Word Stress', 15),
  (2015, 2015, '
    <h2>Sentence Stress</h2>

    <p>Sentence stress is the emphasis placed on particular words in a sentence. Speakers often stress words that carry important information, such as nouns, main verbs, adjectives and adverbs. Stress can also shift to contrast one idea with another.</p>

    <h3>Example: Changing the emphasis</h3>
    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:12px;">
      <p>"I wanted the <strong>BLUE</strong> book."</p>
      <p>This can emphasise which colour of book was wanted.</p>

      <p>"<strong>I</strong> wanted the blue book."</p>
      <p>This can contrast who wanted the book.</p>

      <p>"I wanted the blue <strong>BOOK</strong>."</p>
      <p>This can contrast the book with another item.</p>
    </div>

    <p>The sentence can remain grammatically identical while the intended contrast changes with emphasis and context.</p>

    <h3>Practice dialogue</h3>
    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:12px;">
      <p><strong>Teacher:</strong> Did you say that Mary borrowed the red book?</p>
      <p><strong>Learner:</strong> No, Mary borrowed the <strong>BLUE</strong> book.</p>
      <p><strong>Teacher:</strong> So Mary borrowed the blue book, not the red one?</p>
      <p><strong>Learner:</strong> Yes, that is correct.</p>
    </div>

    <p>Practise stressing <em>blue</em> to correct the mistaken colour. Keep the sentence natural rather than making every word equally prominent.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Sentence Stress', 15),
  (2016, 2016, '
    <h2>Rhythm in Connected Speech</h2>

    <p>Speech rhythm is the pattern of prominence, timing and grouping in spoken language. In English, important information words often receive more stress, while many grammatical words are less prominent in ordinary connected speech.</p>

    <h3>Example</h3>
    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:12px;">
      <p><strong>Careful reading:</strong> "I am going to the market to buy some fruit."</p>
      <p><strong>Natural connected speech:</strong> "I''m going to the market to buy some fruit."</p>
    </div>

    <p>In ordinary speech, words may link together and some unstressed words may be shortened. The goal is to speak naturally and clearly, not to remove words so that the message becomes difficult to understand.</p>

    <h3>Practice dialogue</h3>
    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:12px;">
      <p><strong>Amina:</strong> What are you going to do after class?</p>
      <p><strong>Brian:</strong> I''m going to the library to finish my assignment.</p>
      <p><strong>Amina:</strong> Are you going to stay there for long?</p>
      <p><strong>Brian:</strong> Not too long. I''ll finish the last two questions and then go home.</p>
    </div>

    <p>Read the dialogue at a natural pace. Give prominence to key information such as <em>library</em>, <em>finish</em>, <em>assignment</em> and <em>last two questions</em>. Allow short grammatical words to connect smoothly where natural.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Rhythm in Connected Speech', 15),
  (2017, 2017, '
    <h2>Intonation in Statements and Questions</h2>

    <p>Intonation is the movement of pitch during speech. It can help communicate whether a speaker is making a statement, asking a question, expressing surprise or signalling that more information is coming.</p>

    <h3>Common patterns</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Statements:</strong> Often use falling intonation when the speaker is completing a thought.</li>
      <li><strong>Yes/no questions:</strong> Often use rising intonation.</li>
      <li><strong>Wh-questions:</strong> Often use falling intonation in neutral contexts.</li>
      <li><strong>Lists:</strong> May use rising or continuing pitch before the final item, followed by a fall.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>These are common patterns, not absolute rules. Intonation varies with context, emphasis and accent.</p>

    <h3>Model dialogue</h3>
    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:12px;">
      <p><strong>Teacher:</strong> Have you completed the assignment? (A common rising pattern in a neutral yes/no question.)</p>
      <p><strong>Learner:</strong> Yes, I completed it yesterday. (A common falling pattern in a completed statement.)</p>
      <p><strong>Teacher:</strong> When did you submit it? (A common falling pattern in a neutral wh-question.)</p>
      <p><strong>Learner:</strong> I submitted it after lunch.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Practise aloud</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Say: "You are ready." as a statement.</li>
      <li>Say: "Are you ready?" as a yes/no question.</li>
      <li>Say: "When are you ready?" as a wh-question.</li>
      <li>Notice how pitch movement helps convey the intended function.</li>
    </ol>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Intonation in Statements and Questions', 15),
  (2018, 2018, '
    <h2>Language for Different Audiences</h2>

    <p>Speakers and writers adjust their language to suit their audience, purpose and situation. A message to a close friend may be informal, while a presentation to school leaders usually requires more formal language.</p>

    <h3>Example: Asking for permission</h3>

    <p><strong>Informal message to a friend:</strong></p>
    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:12px;">
      <p>"Hey, can you send me the notes when you get a chance? Thanks!"</p>
    </div>

    <p><strong>Formal request to a teacher:</strong></p>
    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:12px;">
      <p>"Good afternoon, Madam. Could you please share the notes from today''s lesson when convenient? I would like to review the material before the next class. Thank you for your assistance."</p>
    </div>

    <h3>What changes?</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Greeting:</strong> The formal version uses a respectful greeting.</li>
      <li><strong>Request:</strong> "Could you please..." is more formal than "can you..." in this context.</li>
      <li><strong>Explanation:</strong> The formal version briefly explains why the notes are needed.</li>
      <li><strong>Closing:</strong> It acknowledges the recipient''s assistance.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Formal language should remain clear and natural. Being formal does not mean using unnecessarily complicated words.</p>
  
<hr>

    <h2>Language for Different Audiences</h2>

    <p>The audience is the person or group receiving a message. Effective communication considers what the audience needs to know, what they already understand and what tone is appropriate.</p>

    <h3>Example: Announcing a school clean-up</h3>

    <p><strong>Message to fellow learners:</strong></p>
    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:12px;">
      <p>Hi everyone,</p>
      <p>Our class clean-up is this Friday after lessons. Please bring gloves if you have them, and meet outside the classroom at 3:30 p.m. Let''s work together to leave the compound clean.</p>
      <p>Thanks!</p>
    </div>

    <p><strong>Message to the head teacher:</strong></p>
    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:12px;">
      <p>Dear Head Teacher,</p>
      <p>Our class proposes to conduct a school clean-up exercise this Friday after lessons, beginning at 3:30 p.m. We request permission to organise the activity and would appreciate guidance on the areas that should be prioritised.</p>
      <p>Yours faithfully,</p>
      <p>The Class Representative</p>
    </div>

    <h3>What changes?</h3>
    <p>Both messages communicate the same activity, but the message to learners focuses on participation and practical instructions. The message to the head teacher makes a formal proposal and requests permission.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Language for Different Audiences', 15),
  (2019, 2019, '
    <h2>Formal Language</h2>

    <p>Formal language is used in situations that require respect, professionalism or official communication. It is common when communicating with school administrators, employers, public officials and people we do not know well.</p>

    <h3>Common features</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Respectful greetings and forms of address.</li>
      <li>Complete, clear sentences.</li>
      <li>Polite requests rather than commands.</li>
      <li>Limited use of slang and casual abbreviations.</li>
      <li>A tone suited to the relationship and purpose.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Example: Requesting a meeting with a principal</h3>

    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:12px;">
      <p><strong>Learner:</strong> Good morning, Madam Principal. May I speak with you about the school science club?</p>
      <p><strong>Principal:</strong> Good morning. Certainly. What would you like to discuss?</p>
      <p><strong>Learner:</strong> Our club would like to organise a science exhibition next month. Could we arrange a short meeting to discuss the proposal?</p>
      <p><strong>Principal:</strong> Please submit a brief proposal to my office, and we can arrange a meeting after reviewing it.</p>
      <p><strong>Learner:</strong> Thank you, Madam. I will prepare the proposal and submit it this week.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Compare the wording</h3>
    <p><strong>Too casual for this situation:</strong> "Hey, we want to do a science thing. Meet us."</p>
    <p><strong>Appropriate:</strong> "Good morning, Madam. May we arrange a meeting to discuss our science exhibition proposal?"</p>

    <p>Formal language should be respectful and direct. It does not require unnecessarily difficult vocabulary.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Formal Language', 15),
  (2020, 2020, '
    <h2>Informal Language</h2>

    <p>Informal language is common when communicating with friends, relatives and people with whom we have a relaxed relationship. It can include contractions, familiar expressions and a conversational tone.</p>

    <h3>Common features</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Friendly greetings.</li>
      <li>Everyday vocabulary.</li>
      <li>Contractions such as "I''m", "we''ll" and "can''t".</li>
      <li>Short, natural sentences.</li>
      <li>Expressions suited to the relationship.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Complete example: A message to a friend</h3>

    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:12px;">
      <p><strong>Subject: Revision on Saturday</strong></p>
      <p>Hi Brian,</p>
      <p>Are you free on Saturday morning? I want us to revise algebra before the test next week. We could meet at the library at 9 a.m. and work through the questions we found difficult.</p>
      <p>Let me know if that works for you. If you''re busy, we can choose another time.</p>
      <p>See you,</p>
      <p>Amina</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Why this works</h3>
    <p>The greeting is friendly, the request is clear and the closing suits the relationship. The message is informal without being confusing or careless.</p>

    <p>Informal language is not the same as incorrect English. The message should still be understandable and appropriate for its recipient.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Informal Language', 15),
  (2021, 2021, '
    <h2>Polite Requests</h2>

    <p>A polite request asks someone to do something while respecting their choice, time and circumstances.</p>

    <h3>Useful expressions</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>"Could you please...?"</li>
      <li>"Would you mind...?"</li>
      <li>"May I ask you to...?"</li>
      <li>"Would it be possible to...?"</li>
      <li>"When you have a moment, could you...?"</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Example: Requesting help from a teacher</h3>

    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:12px;">
      <p><strong>Learner:</strong> Good afternoon, Sir. Could you please explain the last step in the equation again?</p>
      <p><strong>Teacher:</strong> Certainly. Which part is unclear?</p>
      <p><strong>Learner:</strong> I understand how you expanded the brackets, but I am not sure how you collected the like terms.</p>
      <p><strong>Teacher:</strong> Let''s write the terms with x together and the constants separately.</p>
      <p><strong>Learner:</strong> Thank you. That makes the step clearer.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Choosing the right expression</h3>
    <p>"Could you please explain this step?" is a direct, polite request. "Explain this now!" is a command and may sound demanding, especially when the listener has not agreed to help.</p>

    <p>Politeness does not require excessive apologies. State what you need clearly and respectfully.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Polite Requests', 15),
  (2022, 2022, '
    <h2>Responding to Requests</h2>

    <p>A response to a request should communicate clearly whether you can help, whether you need more information or whether you must decline.</p>

    <h3>Accepting a request</h3>
    <p>"Certainly. I can help you after lunch."</p>

    <h3>Asking for clarification</h3>
    <p>"Of course. Which section would you like me to check?"</p>

    <h3>Declining politely</h3>
    <p>"I''m sorry, but I cannot meet this afternoon because I have another commitment. Would tomorrow morning work instead?"</p>

    <h3>Complete example: A realistic exchange</h3>

    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:12px;">
      <p><strong>Grace:</strong> Peter, could you help me carry these science materials to the laboratory?</p>
      <p><strong>Peter:</strong> Certainly. Let me put my books away first, and then I''ll help you.</p>
      <p><strong>Grace:</strong> Thank you. We need to move them before the next lesson.</p>
      <p><strong>Peter:</strong> No problem. Should we carry the glass equipment separately?</p>
      <p><strong>Grace:</strong> Yes, please. The teacher asked us to handle it carefully.</p>
    </div>

    <p>A response may be positive, negative or conditional. The important thing is to make your answer understandable and respectful.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Responding to Requests', 15),
  (2023, 2023, '
    <h2>Making Offers</h2>

    <p>An offer expresses willingness to help, provide something or do an activity for another person. An effective offer is clear and suited to the situation.</p>

    <h3>Useful expressions</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>"Would you like me to help?"</li>
      <li>"Can I get you some water?"</li>
      <li>"Shall I carry that for you?"</li>
      <li>"I could help you prepare the materials if you wish."</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Complete example: Offering assistance</h3>

    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:12px;">
      <p><strong>Teacher:</strong> The class needs to arrange these books before the visitors arrive.</p>
      <p><strong>Mary:</strong> Would you like me to arrange the books by subject?</p>
      <p><strong>Teacher:</strong> Yes, please. That would be helpful.</p>
      <p><strong>Mary:</strong> I can also label the shelves after sorting them.</p>
      <p><strong>Teacher:</strong> Thank you. Please check the labels against the catalogue.</p>
      <p><strong>Mary:</strong> Certainly. I will check each label before placing the books on the shelves.</p>
    </div>

    <p>An offer should not assume that help is wanted. Give the other person an opportunity to accept or decline.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Making Offers', 15),
  (2024, 2024, '
    <h2>Responding to Offers</h2>

    <p>When someone offers help or something useful, respond in a way that clearly accepts, declines or explains what would be more helpful.</p>

    <h3>Accepting</h3>
    <p>"Yes, please. I would appreciate your help."</p>

    <h3>Declining politely</h3>
    <p>"Thank you for offering, but I can manage this myself."</p>

    <h3>Accepting part of an offer</h3>
    <p>"Thank you. I can carry the books, but could you help me with the heavier box?"</p>

    <h3>Complete example: At school</h3>

    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:12px;">
      <p><strong>Daniel:</strong> Would you like me to explain the first mathematics question?</p>
      <p><strong>Lucy:</strong> Yes, please. I understand the formula, but I am unsure how to substitute the values.</p>
      <p><strong>Daniel:</strong> Start by writing the known values beside the formula. Then substitute each value in the correct position.</p>
      <p><strong>Lucy:</strong> I see. Thank you for explaining that.</p>
      <p><strong>Daniel:</strong> You''re welcome. Would you like to try the next question yourself?</p>
      <p><strong>Lucy:</strong> Yes. I will try it first and ask if I get stuck.</p>
    </div>

    <p>A clear response helps the person offering assistance understand what is useful and whether further help is needed.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Responding to Offers', 15),
  (2025, 2025, '
    <h2>Invitations and Responses</h2>

    <p>An invitation asks someone to attend an event or take part in an activity. A good invitation identifies the event and provides the essential details, including the date, time and place.</p>

    <h3>Complete example: An invitation message</h3>

    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:12px;">
      <p>Dear Amina,</p>
      <p>I would like to invite you to our school''s science exhibition on Friday, 23 October 2026, from 10:00 a.m. to 1:00 p.m. It will be held in the school hall.</p>
      <p>Learners will present experiments and projects on renewable energy, water conservation and simple machines. I hope you will be able to attend.</p>
      <p>Please let me know if you can come.</p>
      <p>Best wishes,</p>
      <p>Brian</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Accepting the invitation</h3>
    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:12px;">
      <p>Dear Brian,</p>
      <p>Thank you for inviting me to the science exhibition. I would be delighted to attend. I look forward to seeing the projects.</p>
      <p>Best wishes,</p>
      <p>Amina</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Declining politely</h3>
    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:12px;">
      <p>Dear Brian,</p>
      <p>Thank you for the invitation. Unfortunately, I will not be able to attend because I have another commitment that day. I hope the exhibition goes well.</p>
      <p>Best wishes,</p>
      <p>Amina</p>
    </div>

    <p>An invitation response should make the decision clear. If declining, a brief explanation is often enough; there is no need to disclose private details.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Invitations and Responses', 15),
  (2026, 2026, '
    <h2>Giving and Responding to Advice</h2>

    <p>Advice suggests an action that may help someone deal with a situation. Good advice is relevant, practical and respectful. It should not pretend that one solution works for every person.</p>

    <h3>Giving advice</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>"You could try..."</li>
      <li>"It might help to..."</li>
      <li>"Have you considered...?"</li>
      <li>"One option is to..."</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Responding to advice</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>"Thank you. I will try that."</li>
      <li>"That could work. How would you suggest I begin?"</li>
      <li>"I appreciate the suggestion, but that option may not be possible for me."</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Complete example: Discussing revision</h3>

    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:12px;">
      <p><strong>Peter:</strong> I keep running out of time when I revise mathematics. I start several questions but rarely finish them.</p>
      <p><strong>Mary:</strong> Have you tried choosing one topic and completing a small set of questions before moving to another?</p>
      <p><strong>Peter:</strong> Not really. I usually switch topics whenever a question becomes difficult.</p>
      <p><strong>Mary:</strong> You could mark the difficult step, try a simpler example and return to the original question afterwards.</p>
      <p><strong>Peter:</strong> That sounds useful. I will try it with algebra tonight and see whether I can finish the questions.</p>
      <p><strong>Mary:</strong> Good idea. You can adjust the approach if it does not suit you.</p>
    </div>

    <p>The advice addresses the stated problem and offers a practical action. Peter responds by choosing a specific way to test the suggestion.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Giving and Responding to Advice', 15),
  (2027, 2027, '
    <h2>Apologising Appropriately</h2>

    <p>An apology acknowledges an action or mistake that has affected another person. A meaningful apology identifies the problem, accepts appropriate responsibility and explains what will be done to repair the situation.</p>

    <h3>Parts of an effective apology</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>State the apology clearly.</li>
      <li>Acknowledge what happened.</li>
      <li>Accept responsibility without making excuses.</li>
      <li>Offer a reasonable way to correct the problem.</li>
      <li>Take steps to prevent the same mistake where possible.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Complete example: Apologising for missing a group meeting</h3>

    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:12px;">
      <p><strong>Kevin:</strong> Amina, I am sorry I missed our group meeting yesterday without informing you.</p>
      <p><strong>Amina:</strong> We waited because we needed your research notes to finish the report.</p>
      <p><strong>Kevin:</strong> I understand that my absence delayed the group. I should have informed you earlier. I have completed the notes now and can send them this morning.</p>
      <p><strong>Amina:</strong> Thank you. Please let us know in advance if you cannot attend another meeting.</p>
      <p><strong>Kevin:</strong> I will. I am sorry for causing the delay, and I will communicate earlier next time.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Weak apology versus effective apology</h3>
    <p><strong>Weak:</strong> "Sorry if you got upset. It was not my problem."</p>
    <p><strong>Effective:</strong> "I am sorry I submitted my section late. I know it delayed the group, and I should have informed you sooner. I will send my work earlier next time."</p>

    <p>An apology should be sincere. Do not promise something you cannot reasonably do, and do not use an apology to pressure another person into forgiving you immediately.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Apologising Appropriately', 15),
  (2028, 2028, '
    <h2>Polite Complaints</h2>

    <p>A complaint explains that something is wrong, unsatisfactory or unfair and asks for an appropriate response. A polite complaint identifies the problem clearly, provides relevant details and states what action would help.</p>

    <h3>Structure of a written complaint</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Address the person or organisation responsible.</li>
      <li>State the problem and when it occurred.</li>
      <li>Give specific facts rather than insults or unsupported accusations.</li>
      <li>Explain the effect of the problem.</li>
      <li>Request a reasonable solution and close respectfully.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Complete example: Formal complaint letter</h3>

    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:14px;">
      <p>Mary Wanjiku<br>
      P.O. Box 145<br>
      Thika</p>

      <p>12 October 2026</p>

      <p>The Customer Service Manager<br>
      Brightline Stationers<br>
      Thika</p>

      <p><strong>Dear Sir/Madam,</strong></p>

      <p><strong>RE: COMPLAINT ABOUT DAMAGED EXERCISE BOOKS</strong></p>

      <p>I am writing to complain about a pack of exercise books I purchased from your shop on 9 October 2026. When I opened the pack at home, I discovered that several books had torn covers and missing pages.</p>

      <p>I have kept the receipt and the damaged books for inspection. I would appreciate it if you could replace the defective books or advise me on the procedure for obtaining a refund.</p>

      <p>I look forward to your response and hope that the matter can be resolved promptly.</p>

      <p><strong>Yours faithfully,</strong></p>

      <p>Mary Wanjiku</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Why this complaint is effective</h3>
    <p>It identifies the purchase and the defect, gives the date, mentions evidence and requests a practical solution. It does not insult the shop or make threats.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Polite Complaints', 15),
  (2029, 2029, '
    <h2>Persuading with Reasons</h2>

    <p>Persuasion attempts to influence someone''s opinion or decision. Effective persuasion presents a clear position, supports it with relevant reasons or evidence and addresses reasonable concerns.</p>

    <h3>A useful structure</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>State your position.</li>
      <li>Give a reason.</li>
      <li>Support the reason with an example or evidence where available.</li>
      <li>Address a possible concern.</li>
      <li>End with a clear recommendation.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Complete example: Persuading the school to start a reading club</h3>

    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:14px;">
      <p><strong>Brian:</strong> I think our school should establish a weekly reading club.</p>

      <p><strong>Amina:</strong> Why do you think we need one when learners already have English lessons?</p>

      <p><strong>Brian:</strong> English lessons are important, but a reading club would give learners extra time to read books they choose themselves. Members could also discuss unfamiliar words and share their interpretations of stories.</p>

      <p><strong>Amina:</strong> Wouldn''t it require a lot of money to run?</p>

      <p><strong>Brian:</strong> Not necessarily. We could begin with books already in the school library and meet for thirty minutes each Friday. If the club becomes useful, we could later request additional books.</p>

      <p><strong>Amina:</strong> That sounds manageable. How would we know whether learners benefit?</p>

      <p><strong>Brian:</strong> We could record attendance, ask members for feedback and review whether they are reading more regularly. I suggest we run a one-term trial and evaluate the results.</p>

      <p><strong>Amina:</strong> I support trying it for one term. Let''s prepare a proposal for the English department.</p>
    </div>

    <p>Brian gives reasons, responds to concerns and proposes a limited trial. He does not claim that the club will solve every reading problem.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Persuading with Reasons', 15),
  (2030, 2030, '
    <h2>Negotiating Agreement</h2>

    <p>Negotiation is a discussion in which people with different preferences try to reach an acceptable decision. It involves explaining needs, listening, proposing alternatives and agreeing on practical terms.</p>

    <h3>Complete example: Planning a group presentation</h3>

    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:14px;">
      <p><strong>Kevin:</strong> I suggest that we meet on Saturday morning to finish the presentation.</p>

      <p><strong>Lucy:</strong> Saturday morning is difficult for me because I help at home. Could we meet in the afternoon instead?</p>

      <p><strong>Kevin:</strong> I can meet in the afternoon, but I need to leave by four o''clock.</p>

      <p><strong>Brian:</strong> What if we meet at two and finish by half past three?</p>

      <p><strong>Lucy:</strong> That works for me. I can bring the research notes.</p>

      <p><strong>Kevin:</strong> I will prepare the slides before we meet.</p>

      <p><strong>Brian:</strong> I will check the references and organise the final order.</p>

      <p><strong>Lucy:</strong> Agreed. We will meet in the library at two o''clock on Saturday and aim to finish by half past three.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>What makes the negotiation successful?</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Each person explains a real constraint.</li>
      <li>The group considers an alternative time.</li>
      <li>They divide the work clearly.</li>
      <li>They confirm the final agreement.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Negotiation does not require everyone to get their first choice. The aim is to find an arrangement that participants can reasonably accept.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Negotiating Agreement', 15),
  (2031, 2031, '
    <h2>Clarification</h2>

    <p>Clarification is used when a message, instruction or explanation is unclear. Instead of guessing, ask a focused question that identifies exactly what you need to understand.</p>

    <h3>Useful expressions</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>"Could you explain what you mean by...?"</li>
      <li>"Do you mean that we should...?"</li>
      <li>"Could you give an example?"</li>
      <li>"Which section are you referring to?"</li>
      <li>"Could you repeat the last instruction, please?"</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Complete example: Clarifying an assignment</h3>

    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:14px;">
      <p><strong>Teacher:</strong> For homework, compare the two stories and discuss how the main characters change.</p>

      <p><strong>James:</strong> Excuse me, Madam. When you say "compare", should we explain both similarities and differences?</p>

      <p><strong>Teacher:</strong> Yes. Identify at least one similarity and two differences, then explain how each main character changes.</p>

      <p><strong>James:</strong> Should we write this as continuous prose or use a table?</p>

      <p><strong>Teacher:</strong> Write it in paragraphs. You may use a short planning table before you begin.</p>

      <p><strong>James:</strong> Thank you. So the final answer should be paragraphs that compare the stories and explain the character changes.</p>

      <p><strong>Teacher:</strong> Exactly.</p>
    </div>

    <p>James asks focused questions and restates the instruction to confirm his understanding. This is more reliable than silently guessing what the teacher expects.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Clarification', 15),
  (2032, 2032, '
    <h2>Correcting Misunderstandings</h2>

    <p>A misunderstanding occurs when a person interprets a message differently from what the speaker intended. Correcting it requires identifying the incorrect interpretation and explaining the intended meaning calmly.</p>

    <h3>Useful expressions</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>"I think there has been a misunderstanding."</li>
      <li>"What I meant was..."</li>
      <li>"Let me explain that more clearly."</li>
      <li>"I did not mean that you should... I meant that..."</li>
      <li>"Thank you for checking. Let me clarify."</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Complete example: A misunderstood group message</h3>

    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:14px;">
      <p><strong>Message from Njeri:</strong> Please submit your sections before Friday so I can combine them over the weekend.</p>

      <p><strong>David:</strong> I thought you meant that the whole report had to be submitted to the teacher before Friday.</p>

      <p><strong>Njeri:</strong> I think there has been a misunderstanding. I need each person''s section by Friday, but the complete report is due next Monday.</p>

      <p><strong>David:</strong> Thank you for clarifying. So I should send my section to you by Friday, not submit the final report to the teacher yet.</p>

      <p><strong>Njeri:</strong> Exactly. Please include your references with your section.</p>

      <p><strong>David:</strong> Understood. I will send both the section and its references by Friday afternoon.</p>
    </div>

    <p>The correction distinguishes the two deadlines and confirms the action David must take. Neither person needs to blame the other to resolve the confusion.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Correcting Misunderstandings', 15),
  (2033, 2033, '
    <h2>Navigating Digital Texts</h2>

    <p>Digital texts include websites, online articles, electronic documents and learning platforms. Unlike a printed page, a digital text may contain links, menus, search tools and expandable sections that help readers find information.</p>

    <h3>Common navigation features</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Menu:</strong> provides access to different sections.</li>
      <li><strong>Search box:</strong> helps locate a word, topic or page.</li>
      <li><strong>Hyperlink:</strong> opens another page, document or resource.</li>
      <li><strong>Breadcrumbs:</strong> show where a page sits within a website.</li>
      <li><strong>Table of contents:</strong> lists sections and may link directly to them.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Complete example: Finding a school revision resource</h3>

    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:14px;">
      <p><strong>Website header:</strong> School Learning Centre</p>
      <p><strong>Menu:</strong> Home | Subjects | Revision Resources | Contact</p>
      <p><strong>Search box:</strong> Search for a topic...</p>
      <p><strong>Breadcrumbs:</strong> Home &gt; Subjects &gt; English &gt; Grammar</p>
      <p><strong>Page heading:</strong> Subject–Verb Agreement</p>
      <p><strong>Page text:</strong> Learn how verbs agree with singular and plural subjects.</p>
      <p><strong>Related link:</strong> Practise Subject–Verb Agreement</p>
    </div>

    <p><strong>Reader''s task:</strong> You want to practise subject–verb agreement. You can follow the breadcrumb trail to understand where you are, then select the related practice link. If you cannot locate the topic, use the search box.</p>

    <p>Before opening a link, consider its label and destination. Avoid entering personal information into an unfamiliar website just because it appears in a search result.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Navigating Digital Texts', 15),
  (2034, 2034, '
    <h2>Links, Headings and Layout</h2>

    <p>Headings organise information into sections. Hyperlinks connect a text to another page or resource. Layout describes how text, headings, images and other elements are arranged.</p>

    <h3>How these features help readers</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Main heading:</strong> identifies the overall subject.</li>
      <li><strong>Subheading:</strong> identifies a smaller section within the subject.</li>
      <li><strong>Numbered list:</strong> presents items or steps in a sequence.</li>
      <li><strong>Bullet list:</strong> groups related points without implying a required order.</li>
      <li><strong>Descriptive hyperlink:</strong> tells readers what they can expect to open.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Complete example: An online guide</h3>

    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:14px;">
      <h3>How to Prepare for a Mathematics Test</h3>

      <p><strong>1. Plan your revision</strong></p>
      <p>List the topics covered in class and identify the ones you find difficult.</p>

      <p><strong>2. Practise questions</strong></p>
      <p>Complete questions without looking at the worked solution first.</p>

      <p><strong>3. Check your mistakes</strong></p>
      <p>Review each incorrect answer and identify the step that caused the error.</p>

      <p><strong>Related resource:</strong> <u>Practise algebra equations</u></p>
    </div>

    <p>The main heading announces the purpose of the guide. The numbered subheadings organise the process in order. The descriptive link tells readers what resource it opens.</p>

    <p>A well-organised layout makes information easier to scan, but attractive formatting alone does not prove that the information is accurate.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Links, Headings and Layout', 15),
  (2035, 2035, '
    <h2>Interpreting Charts and Graphs</h2>

    <p>Charts and graphs present information visually. To interpret them accurately, read the title, labels, units, key or legend, and the values shown before drawing a conclusion.</p>

    <h3>What to check</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Title:</strong> identifies what the chart represents.</li>
      <li><strong>Axes:</strong> show the categories or quantities being compared.</li>
      <li><strong>Scale:</strong> shows the intervals used for measurement.</li>
      <li><strong>Legend:</strong> explains the meaning of different colours or symbols where present.</li>
      <li><strong>Data:</strong> supplies the values that support the conclusion.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Complete example: A class reading survey</h3>

    <p>The English teacher asks 20 learners how many books they read during the previous month. The results are summarised below.</p>

    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:14px;">
      <p><strong>Books read by 20 learners in one month</strong></p>
      <table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;">
        <tr>
          <th style="text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #999;padding:6px;">Books read</th>
          <th style="text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #999;padding:6px;">Number of learners</th>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td style="padding:6px;">0–1</td>
          <td style="padding:6px;">4</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td style="padding:6px;">2–3</td>
          <td style="padding:6px;">9</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td style="padding:6px;">4–5</td>
          <td style="padding:6px;">5</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td style="padding:6px;">6 or more</td>
          <td style="padding:6px;">2</td>
        </tr>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h3>Worked interpretation</h3>
    <p><strong>Question:</strong> Which group was the largest?</p>
    <ol>
      <li>Compare the frequencies: 4, 9, 5 and 2.</li>
      <li>The largest frequency is 9.</li>
      <li>Therefore, the largest group was learners who read 2–3 books.</li>
    </ol>

    <p><strong>Question:</strong> How many learners were surveyed?</p>
    <p>Add the frequencies: 4 + 9 + 5 + 2 = 20 learners.</p>

    <p>Do not claim that the survey represents every learner in the school. It describes only the 20 learners surveyed unless further evidence supports a wider conclusion.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Interpreting Charts and Graphs', 15),
  (2036, 2036, '
    <h2>Interpreting Tables and Diagrams</h2>

    <p>Tables organise information into rows and columns. Diagrams show relationships, structures or processes visually. Readers should identify labels, follow the correct row or branch and distinguish what is shown from what is merely assumed.</p>

    <h3>Complete example: A school club timetable</h3>

    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:14px;">
      <p><strong>After-school club timetable</strong></p>
      <table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;">
        <tr>
          <th style="text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #999;padding:6px;">Day</th>
          <th style="text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #999;padding:6px;">Club</th>
          <th style="text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #999;padding:6px;">Venue</th>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td style="padding:6px;">Monday</td>
          <td style="padding:6px;">Science</td>
          <td style="padding:6px;">Laboratory</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td style="padding:6px;">Tuesday</td>
          <td style="padding:6px;">Debate</td>
          <td style="padding:6px;">Room 4</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td style="padding:6px;">Wednesday</td>
          <td style="padding:6px;">Reading</td>
          <td style="padding:6px;">Library</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td style="padding:6px;">Thursday</td>
          <td style="padding:6px;">Environment</td>
          <td style="padding:6px;">School garden</td>
        </tr>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h3>Worked interpretation</h3>
    <p><strong>Question:</strong> Where does the debate club meet?</p>
    <ol>
      <li>Find the row labelled Tuesday.</li>
      <li>Read across to the Club column and locate Debate.</li>
      <li>Read across to the Venue column: Room 4.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Example of a process diagram</h3>
    <p><strong>Planning a class presentation:</strong></p>
    <p>Choose a topic → Gather information → Organise ideas → Prepare slides → Present to the class</p>

    <p>The arrows indicate the intended sequence. If a diagram has labelled branches rather than arrows in a sequence, follow the labels instead of assuming every branch represents a chronological step.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Interpreting Tables and Diagrams', 15),
  (2037, 2037, '
    <h2>Images and Captions</h2>

    <p>Images can communicate information, attract attention or influence how readers interpret a story. A caption explains or adds context to an image. Readers should distinguish what an image visibly shows from what its caption claims about it.</p>

    <h3>Complete example: A school news report</h3>

    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:14px;">
      <p><strong>Headline:</strong> Learners Begin a School Tree-Planting Project</p>

      <p><strong>Image description:</strong> A photograph shows four learners placing a young tree into a prepared hole in the school compound. A teacher stands nearby holding a watering can.</p>

      <p><strong>Caption:</strong> Learners plant a young tree during the school''s environmental activity on 8 October 2026.</p>

      <p><strong>Report text:</strong> The environmental club began a tree-planting activity on Thursday. According to the club patron, members plan to care for the seedlings throughout the term. The school has not yet published a final count of the trees planted.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Interpreting the evidence</h3>
    <p>The photograph, as described, shows learners planting a tree and a teacher nearby. The caption supplies a date and context. The report says the club plans to care for the seedlings, but the image alone cannot prove that every seedling will survive or that the activity has already planted a particular total number of trees.</p>

    <h3>Questions to ask</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>What can I directly observe in the image?</li>
      <li>What extra information does the caption provide?</li>
      <li>Does the caption identify the date, location or people reliably?</li>
      <li>Does the written claim go beyond what the image can establish?</li>
    </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Images and Captions', 15),
  (2038, 2038, '
    <h2>Identifying Media Purpose and Audience</h2>

    <p>Media purpose is the reason a message is created. Common purposes include informing, persuading, entertaining, educating and warning. The audience is the group the message is intended to reach.</p>

    <h3>How to identify purpose and audience</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Read the headline or title.</li>
      <li>Identify what the message asks readers to know, believe or do.</li>
      <li>Examine the vocabulary, tone and supporting information.</li>
      <li>Consider who would benefit most from receiving the message.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Complete example: Two notices about water conservation</h3>

    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:14px;">
      <p><strong>Notice A: School Water Conservation Week</strong></p>
      <p>Our school will hold Water Conservation Week from 19 to 23 October. Learners will take part in discussions about responsible water use, and each class will inspect taps for leaks. All learners are encouraged to participate.</p>

      <p><strong>Notice B: Buy the AquaSave Water Bottle</strong></p>
      <p>Make every school day easier with the new AquaSave bottle. Its reusable design helps you carry water wherever you go. Visit our shop today to explore available sizes.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Worked interpretation</h3>
    <p><strong>Notice A:</strong> Its main purpose is to inform learners about a school activity and encourage participation. Its audience is the school community.</p>
    <p><strong>Notice B:</strong> Its main purpose is to advertise a product and persuade potential customers to consider buying it. Its audience includes people who might need a reusable bottle.</p>

    <p>A message can have more than one purpose. Identify the main purpose using the evidence in the message rather than assuming that every notice is neutral or every advertisement is dishonest.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Identifying Media Purpose and Audience', 15),
  (2039, 2039, '
    <h2>Assessing Source Credibility</h2>

    <p>Source credibility is the degree to which a source can reasonably be trusted for a particular claim. A professional-looking website or confident writer is not automatically reliable.</p>

    <h3>What to check</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Author:</strong> Is the author identified, and do they have relevant expertise?</li>
      <li><strong>Evidence:</strong> Are claims supported by verifiable information?</li>
      <li><strong>Date:</strong> Is the information current enough for the topic?</li>
      <li><strong>Purpose:</strong> Is the source informing, selling, persuading or entertaining?</li>
      <li><strong>Corroboration:</strong> Do independent, reliable sources support the claim?</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Complete example: Checking an online health claim</h3>

    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:14px;">
      <p><strong>Website A headline:</strong> "One cup of this drink guarantees perfect health!"</p>
      <p><strong>Website A article:</strong> The article has no named author, gives no supporting studies and asks readers to purchase the drink immediately.</p>

      <p><strong>Website B headline:</strong> "Understanding the evidence about hydration"</p>
      <p><strong>Website B article:</strong> The article identifies its author, provides links to relevant public-health guidance, gives a publication date and explains the limits of the evidence.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Worked judgement</h3>
    <p>Website B provides stronger reasons for confidence because readers can identify the author, inspect the evidence and check the date. Website A''s absolute promise, lack of evidence and sales pressure are warning signs.</p>

    <p>This does not mean Website B must be correct about every detail. Its claims still need to be checked against the evidence it cites.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Assessing Source Credibility', 15),
  (2040, 2040, '
    <h2>Comparing Information Sources</h2>

    <p>Comparing sources means examining how different sources report the same subject. Compare their claims, evidence, dates, methods and purposes before deciding what the information supports.</p>

    <h3>Complete example: Comparing two reports about a school garden</h3>

    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:14px;">
      <p><strong>Source A — School newsletter, 8 October</strong></p>
      <p>"Our school garden produced 120 kilograms of vegetables this term," reports the agriculture club. The newsletter states that the club recorded each harvest in a logbook.</p>

      <p><strong>Source B — Community newspaper, 10 October</strong></p>
      <p>"The school garden has increased vegetable production," reports a community journalist. The article quotes the club patron but does not provide a total harvest figure.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Worked comparison</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Agreement:</strong> Both sources report that the school garden produced vegetables.</li>
      <li><strong>Specificity:</strong> Source A provides a numerical total; Source B gives a general description.</li>
      <li><strong>Evidence to check:</strong> The harvest logbook could help verify Source A''s figure.</li>
      <li><strong>Limitation:</strong> Neither source, as presented here, provides enough information to calculate how much production increased compared with an earlier term.</li>
    </ul>

    <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> The sources agree that the garden produced vegetables, but only Source A reports a total. To verify the figure or measure growth, consult the harvest records and comparable data from an earlier period.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Comparing Information Sources', 15),
  (2041, 2041, '
    <h2>Recognising Misleading Claims</h2>

    <p>A misleading claim can cause readers to form an inaccurate impression. It may use exaggerated language, omit important context, present a correlation as causation or generalise from limited evidence.</p>

    <h3>Warning signs</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Absolute words such as "always", "never" or "guaranteed" without adequate evidence.</li>
      <li>Statistics presented without a sample size, comparison or time period.</li>
      <li>Headlines that promise more than the article demonstrates.</li>
      <li>Claims that treat one person''s experience as proof of a universal rule.</li>
      <li>Missing context that would change how the result is understood.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Complete example: A misleading school survey headline</h3>

    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:14px;">
      <p><strong>Headline:</strong> "Every learner prefers digital notes!"</p>

      <p><strong>Article:</strong> A school club asked 12 learners from one class which revision format they preferred. Eight selected digital notes, three selected printed notes and one had no preference. The club published the results on its noticeboard.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Worked analysis</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>The survey asked 12 learners, not every learner.</li>
      <li>Eight of the 12 selected digital notes; four did not select that option.</li>
      <li>The sample came from one class, so it may not represent the entire school.</li>
      <li>The headline exaggerates the evidence.</li>
    </ol>

    <p><strong>A more accurate headline:</strong> "Most learners surveyed in one class preferred digital notes."</p>

    <p>The revised headline preserves the finding while stating its scope more carefully.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Recognising Misleading Claims', 15),
  (2042, 2042, '
    <h2>Evidence vs Unsupported Claims</h2>

    <p>Evidence is information used to support or assess a claim. It may include reliable records, direct observations, measurements, documents or carefully conducted research. An unsupported claim is a statement for which adequate supporting evidence has not been provided.</p>

    <h3>Complete example: A school reading programme</h3>

    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:14px;">
      <p><strong>Claim A:</strong> "The reading programme improved learners'' reading scores by 10%."</p>

      <p><strong>Evidence supplied:</strong> The teacher provides anonymised records showing the class''s average score before and after the programme, explains how the scores were calculated and states the dates of both assessments.</p>

      <p><strong>Claim B:</strong> "The reading programme made every learner love reading."</p>

      <p><strong>Evidence supplied:</strong> No survey, interview or other evidence is provided to support this statement.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Worked evaluation</h3>
    <p>Claim A has relevant evidence that can be examined. To evaluate it properly, check whether the assessments are comparable and whether the percentage change has been calculated correctly. Even then, improvement after the programme does not by itself prove that the programme caused all of the improvement.</p>

    <p>Claim B is unsupported as presented. A score increase cannot establish that every learner enjoyed reading. That claim would require suitable evidence about learners'' attitudes, and the word "every" would require particularly strong support.</p>

    <p><strong>Remember:</strong> A claim may be true but still be unsupported by the evidence provided. Evaluate the support, not just whether the statement sounds believable.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Evidence vs Unsupported Claims', 15),
  (2043, 2043, '
    <h2>Respectful Online Communication</h2>

    <p>Respectful online communication expresses ideas clearly without personal attacks, harassment or unnecessary humiliation. Disagreement is allowed; insulting people is not necessary to explain why a claim is wrong.</p>

    <h3>Useful practices</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Address the idea rather than attacking the person.</li>
      <li>Use clear wording that reduces the risk of misunderstanding.</li>
      <li>Ask for clarification before assuming hostile intent.</li>
      <li>Disagree with reasons and relevant evidence.</li>
      <li>Do not share private messages or personal details without appropriate permission.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Complete example: A class discussion group</h3>

    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:14px;">
      <p><strong>Brian:</strong> I think our group should use printed notes because not everyone has reliable internet access.</p>

      <p><strong>Faith:</strong> That is a reasonable concern. Digital notes are easier to update, though. Could we keep a digital copy and print it for members who need one?</p>

      <p><strong>Brian:</strong> That could work. We should also make sure the digital file can be opened offline.</p>

      <p><strong>Faith:</strong> Agreed. I''ll prepare the shared file, and we can print copies for anyone who prefers them.</p>

      <p><strong>Brian:</strong> Thanks. That gives us both options without leaving anyone out.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Compare with a disrespectful response</h3>
    <p>"That is a stupid idea. You never think."</p>

    <p>The second response attacks the person and gives no useful reason. The first exchange acknowledges a concern, offers an alternative and works towards a solution.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Respectful Online Communication', 15),
  (2044, 2044, '
    <h2>Responsible Information Sharing</h2>

    <p>Responsible information sharing means checking accuracy, considering privacy and understanding the possible consequences before forwarding or publishing information.</p>

    <h3>Before sharing, check</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Accuracy:</strong> Can the claim be verified from a reliable source?</li>
      <li><strong>Context:</strong> Has the message been edited or taken out of context?</li>
      <li><strong>Privacy:</strong> Does it reveal someone''s personal information?</li>
      <li><strong>Permission:</strong> Is it appropriate to share the photo, message or document?</li>
      <li><strong>Impact:</strong> Could sharing it cause avoidable harm or spread a false rumour?</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Complete example: A message about a school closure</h3>

    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:14px;">
      <p><strong>Forwarded message:</strong> "URGENT! School is closed tomorrow. Forward this to every learner immediately!"</p>

      <p><strong>David:</strong> I have received a message saying school is closed tomorrow. Has the school confirmed this?</p>

      <p><strong>Mary:</strong> I haven''t seen an official announcement. Let''s check the school''s verified communication channel before forwarding it.</p>

      <p><strong>David:</strong> I have checked the school noticeboard online, and there is no closure notice. I will not forward the message as a fact until it is confirmed.</p>

      <p><strong>Mary:</strong> Good idea. If we need to know urgently, we can contact the class teacher.</p>
    </div>

    <p>The learners avoid spreading an unverified claim and identify appropriate ways to check it.</p>

    <p>When sharing photos or personal messages, consider whether the people involved have agreed to the sharing and whether the information is appropriate for the intended audience.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Responsible Information Sharing', 15),
  (2045, 2045, '
    <h2>Checking a Claim Across Sources</h2>

    <p>When an important claim appears online, one source may not provide enough information to establish whether it is reliable. Cross-checking means looking for independent, relevant sources and examining the evidence each one provides.</p>

    <h3>Complete example: Checking a reported examination date</h3>

    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:14px;">
      <p><strong>Post A — Unverified social media account:</strong></p>
      <p>"The national examination has been moved to 2 November. Share this immediately!"</p>

      <p><strong>Post B — School group message:</strong></p>
      <p>"Someone has said the examination date changed, but I have not seen an official notice."</p>

      <p><strong>Source C — Official examination authority notice:</strong></p>
      <p>The notice lists the examination timetable and states the applicable dates. It identifies the issuing authority and publication date.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Worked decision</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Post A makes a specific claim but gives no verifiable evidence.</li>
      <li>Post B acknowledges uncertainty; it does not confirm the change.</li>
      <li>Source C is directly relevant because it is an official notice from the responsible authority.</li>
      <li>Check the notice''s date, scope and authenticity before relying on it.</li>
    </ol>

    <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> Do not treat the examination date as changed based only on Post A. Consult and verify the official timetable. If the official notice does not confirm a change, the social media claim remains unverified.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Checking a Claim Across Sources', 15),
  (2046, 2046, '
    <h2>Responsible Use of Online Images</h2>

    <p>Images found online may be protected by copyright, contain private information or be presented with a misleading caption. Before reusing an image, check its source, permission or licence, accuracy and context.</p>

    <h3>Complete example: Preparing a school presentation</h3>

    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:14px;">
      <p><strong>Learner''s draft slide:</strong></p>
      <p><strong>Title:</strong> Effects of Plastic Pollution</p>
      <p><strong>Image:</strong> A photograph of a beach covered with plastic waste, found on a photography website.</p>
      <p><strong>Proposed caption:</strong> "This photograph proves that every beach in Kenya is covered with plastic waste."</p>

      <p><strong>Teacher''s feedback:</strong> The photograph can illustrate plastic pollution at the location shown, but it cannot prove that every beach has the same condition. Check the photographer''s licence or obtain permission before reusing it. Write a caption that describes what the image actually shows and provide the source as required.</p>

      <p><strong>Revised caption:</strong> "Plastic waste on a beach, illustrating one way discarded materials can pollute coastal environments."</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Why the revision is better</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>It avoids generalising from one photograph to every beach.</li>
      <li>It describes the image without inventing a location or date.</li>
      <li>It reminds the learner to check reuse rights.</li>
      <li>It avoids presenting an illustrative image as proof of a national statistic.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Giving credit is important, but attribution alone does not automatically grant permission to use copyrighted material. Follow the licence terms or obtain permission where needed.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Responsible Use of Online Images', 15),
  (2047, 2047, '
    <h2>Writing a Responsible Correction</h2>

    <p>When you discover that you shared inaccurate information, correct it clearly and promptly. A good correction identifies the earlier claim, explains what was wrong, supplies the verified information and avoids introducing another unsupported claim.</p>

    <h3>Complete example: Correcting a class group message</h3>

    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:14px;">
      <p><strong>Original message:</strong></p>
      <p>"The school trip is on Friday, 16 October. Everyone should arrive at 7 a.m."</p>

      <p><strong>Correction posted later:</strong></p>
      <p>Correction: I shared the wrong date for the school trip earlier. The official notice from the school states that the trip will take place on Saturday, 17 October, with learners expected to assemble at 7:00 a.m. Please use the date in the official notice rather than my earlier message. I am sorry for the confusion.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>What makes the correction responsible?</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>It clearly labels itself as a correction.</li>
      <li>It acknowledges the earlier error.</li>
      <li>It gives the updated information and identifies the official source.</li>
      <li>It asks readers to rely on the corrected information.</li>
      <li>It apologises without blaming other people.</li>
    </ol>

    <p>If the correct information has not yet been verified, do not invent a replacement date. State that the earlier claim is unconfirmed and explain where readers can check for an official update.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Writing a Responsible Correction', 15),
  (2048, 2048, '<h2>Data vs Information</h2>
<p><b>Data</b> refers to raw, unprocessed facts, while <b>Information</b> is processed data that is meaningful and useful.</p>
<h3>1. Data</h3>
<ul>
<li>Unorganized facts</li>
<li>No meaning on its own</li>
<li>Can be numbers, text, symbols</li>
</ul>
<div class="example-box">
75, 82, 90 → just numbers (data)
</div>
<h3>2. Information</h3>
<ul>
<li>Processed and organized data</li>
<li>Has meaning</li>
<li>Used for decision making</li>
</ul>
<div class="example-box">
Average score = 82% → meaningful (information)
</div>
<h3>3. Differences</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Data</th><th>Information</th></tr>
<tr><td>Meaning</td><td>Raw</td><td>Processed</td></tr>
<tr><td>Use</td><td>Input</td><td>Decision making</td></tr>
</table>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Data vs Information', 15),
  (2049, 2049, '<h2>Characteristics of Computers</h2>
<p>Computers have unique features that make them powerful tools.</p>
<h3>Key Characteristics</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Speed:</b> Performs millions of operations per second</li>
<li><b>Accuracy:</b> Produces correct results if input is correct</li>
<li><b>Diligence:</b> Does not get tired</li>
<li><b>Storage:</b> Can store large amounts of data</li>
<li><b>Automation:</b> Works automatically once programmed</li>
<li><b>Versatility:</b> Can perform many tasks</li>
</ul>
<div class="example-box">
Computer calculates results faster than humans → speed + accuracy
</div>
<div class="keyfact">
 Computers are powerful because they combine speed, accuracy, and storage.
</div>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Characteristics of Computers', 15),
  (2050, 2050, '<h2>Types of Computers</h2>
<h3> DEEP EXPLANATION</h3>
<p>
Computers are classified based on size, power, and purpose. Each type is designed for specific tasks ranging from simple personal use to highly complex scientific calculations.
</p>
<h3> CLASSIFICATION BY SIZE & POWER</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Supercomputers</b></li>
<ul>
<li>Most powerful computers</li>
<li>Used in weather forecasting, space research, simulations</li>
<li>Example tasks: climate modeling, nuclear research</li>
</ul>
<li><b>Mainframe Computers</b></li>
<ul>
<li>Handle large-scale processing</li>
<li>Used by banks, governments, large organizations</li>
<li>Support many users at once</li>
</ul>
<li><b>Minicomputers</b></li>
<ul>
<li>Mid-sized systems (less common today)</li>
<li>Used in small organizations</li>
</ul>
<li><b>Microcomputers (Personal Computers)</b></li>
<ul>
<li>Most common type</li>
<li>Examples: desktops, laptops, tablets</li>
<li>Used for everyday tasks</li>
</ul>
<li><b>Embedded Computers</b></li>
<ul>
<li>Built into devices</li>
<li>Examples: washing machines, cars, microwaves</li>
<li>Perform specific functions</li>
</ul>
</ul>
<h3> COMPARISON TABLE</h3>
<pre>
Type           | Power     | Users      | Example Use
------------------------------------------------------
Supercomputer  | Very High | Few        | Weather prediction
Mainframe      | High      | Many       | Banking systems
Microcomputer  | Moderate  | One        | Personal use
Embedded       | Low       | Single use | Appliances
</pre>
<h3> EXAM FOCUS</h3>
<ul>
<li>Differences between supercomputer and mainframe</li>
<li>Examples of embedded systems</li>
<li>Classification based on purpose</li>
</ul>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Types of Computers', 15),
  (2051, 2051, '<h2>Computer Generations (1st → 5th)</h2>
<h3> DEEP EXPLANATION</h3>
<p>
Computer generations refer to the evolution of computer technology over time.
Each generation is defined by major technological advancements.
</p>
<h3> GENERATIONS OVERVIEW</h3>
<h4>1⃣ First Generation (1940s–1950s)</h4>
<ul>
<li>Used vacuum tubes</li>
<li>Very large and expensive</li>
<li>Generated a lot of heat</li>
<li>Example: ENIAC</li>
</ul>
<h4>2⃣ Second Generation (1950s–1960s)</h4>
<ul>
<li>Used transistors</li>
<li>Smaller, faster, more reliable</li>
<li>Less heat produced</li>
</ul>
<h4>3⃣ Third Generation (1960s–1970s)</h4>
<ul>
<li>Used integrated circuits (ICs)</li>
<li>Even smaller and faster</li>
<li>Introduction of operating systems</li>
</ul>
<h4>4⃣ Fourth Generation (1970s–present)</h4>
<ul>
<li>Used microprocessors</li>
<li>Rise of personal computers</li>
<li>Very high speed and efficiency</li>
</ul>
<h4>5⃣ Fifth Generation (present & future)</h4>
<ul>
<li>Artificial Intelligence (AI)</li>
<li>Machine learning and automation</li>
<li>Focus on smart systems</li>
</ul>
<h3> SUMMARY TABLE</h3>
<pre>
Generation | Technology        | Key Feature
------------------------------------------------
1st        | Vacuum tubes      | Large, hot
2nd        | Transistors       | Smaller, faster
3rd        | ICs               | Reliable
4th        | Microprocessors   | Personal computers
5th        | AI                | Intelligent systems
</pre>
<h3> EXAM FOCUS</h3>
<ul>
<li>Key technology in each generation</li>
<li>Differences between generations</li>
<li>Examples (ENIAC, modern AI systems)</li>
</ul>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Computer Generations', 15),
  (2052, 2052, '<h2>Number Systems</h2>
<p>
  Computers use different number systems to represent and process data efficiently.
  These include decimal, binary, and hexadecimal systems, which can be converted into one another.
</p>
<h3> 1. Decimal (Base 10)</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Uses digits 0–9</li>
  <li>Used in everyday human calculations</li>
  <li>Each digit has a place value based on powers of 10</li>
</ul>
<h3> 2. Binary (Base 2)</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Uses only 0 and 1</li>
  <li>Each digit is called a bit</li>
  <li>Used internally by computers</li>
</ul>
<h3> 3. Hexadecimal (Base 16)</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Uses digits 0–9 and letters A–F</li>
  <li>A = 10, B = 11, C = 12, D = 13, E = 14, F = 15</li>
  <li>Used to simplify long binary numbers</li>
</ul>
<h3> Why Binary is Used</h3>
<ul>
  <li><b>1 = ON</b> (electric signal present)</li>
  <li><b>0 = OFF</b> (no signal)</li>
</ul>
<pre>
Computers use binary because they only understand two states:
ON  → 1
OFF → 0
</pre>
<h3> Number System Conversions</h3>
<h4> Decimal to Binary</h4>
<pre>
Example: 10₁₀ → Binary
10 ÷ 2 = 5 remainder 0
5 ÷ 2  = 2 remainder 1
2 ÷ 2  = 1 remainder 0
1 ÷ 2  = 0 remainder 1
Read from bottom to top:
10₁₀ = 1010₂
</pre>
<h4> Binary to Decimal</h4>
<pre>
Example: 1010₂ → Decimal
(1×2³) + (0×2²) + (1×2¹) + (0×2⁰)
= 8 + 0 + 2 + 0
= 10₁₀
</pre>
<h4> Binary to Hexadecimal</h4>
<pre>
Example: 1010₂ → Hexadecimal
Group into 4 bits:
1010 = A
So, 1010₂ = A₁₆
</pre>
<h4> Hexadecimal to Binary</h4>
<pre>
Example: A₁₆ → Binary
A = 10 in decimal
10 = 1010₂
So, A₁₆ = 1010₂
</pre>
<h3> Exam Focus Areas</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Convert decimal to binary</li>
  <li>Convert binary to decimal</li>
  <li>Convert binary to hexadecimal</li>
  <li>Explain why computers use binary</li>
</ul>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Number Systems', 15),
  (2053, 2053, '<h2>Data Representation</h2>
<p>
  Data in computers is stored and processed using binary digits (0s and 1s).
  These binary values form the basis of all data storage and measurement.
</p>
<h3> 1. Units of Data Storage</h3>
<ul>
  <li><b>Bit:</b> Smallest unit of data (0 or 1)</li>
  <li><b>Nibble:</b> 4 bits</li>
  <li><b>Byte:</b> 8 bits</li>
  <li><b>Kilobyte (KB):</b> 1024 Bytes</li>
  <li><b>Megabyte (MB):</b> 1024 KB</li>
  <li><b>Gigabyte (GB):</b> 1024 MB</li>
  <li><b>Terabyte (TB):</b> 1024 GB</li>
</ul>
<h3> 2. Key Formula: Bits Calculation</h3>
<div class="example-box">
  <b>Formula:</b><br><br>
  Number of Bits = Number of Bytes × 8
</div>
<h3> 3. Examples of Bit Calculation</h3>
<pre>
Example 1:
5 Bytes → 5 × 8 = 40 Bits
Example 2:
10 Bytes → 10 × 8 = 80 Bits
Example 3:
2 KB → 2 × 1024 Bytes × 8 = 16384 Bits
</pre>
<h3> 4. Character Encoding</h3>
<h4> ASCII</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Uses numbers to represent characters</li>
  <li>Example: ''A'' = 65</li>
</ul>
<h4> Unicode</h4><ul><li>Supports many languages worldwide</li></ul>
<div class="example-box">
   Example:<br>
  ''A'' = 65 (ASCII)<br>
  Each character is stored using binary inside the computer
</div>
<h3> 5. Relationship Between Units</h3>
<pre>
1 Byte  = 8 Bits
1 KB     = 1024 Bytes
1 MB     = 1024 KB
1 GB     = 1024 MB
1 TB     = 1024 GB
</pre>
<h3> 6. Why Binary is Used</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Computers use ON (1) and OFF (0) signals</li>
  <li>Binary is easy for electronic circuits</li>
  <li>All data is stored as 0s and 1s</li>
</ul>
<div class="keyfact">
 Important Fact: All data in a computer is stored in binary form, and can be measured in bits and bytes.
</div>
<h3> Simple Explanation</h3>
<p>
  Data storage works like building blocks where everything is made from bits.
  Bytes, KB, MB, and GB are just bigger groups of those bits.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Data Representation', 15),
  (2054, 2054, '<h2>CPU Components (ALU, CU, Registers)</h2>
<h3> DEEP EXPLANATION</h3>
<p>
The Central Processing Unit (CPU) is the main component of a computer system and is often called the “brain” of the computer.
It interprets and executes instructions, processes data, and controls the operations of all other components.
</p>
<h3> MAIN COMPONENTS OF THE CPU</h3>
<h4>1. Arithmetic Logic Unit (ALU)</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Performs arithmetic operations such as addition, subtraction, multiplication, and division</li>
  <li>Performs logical operations such as AND, OR, NOT, and comparisons</li>
  <li>Handles decision-making processes inside the computer</li>
</ul>
<h4>2. Control Unit (CU)</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Directs and coordinates all activities inside the CPU</li>
  <li>Fetches instructions from memory</li>
  <li>Decodes instructions into signals the CPU can understand</li>
  <li>Controls the flow of data between CPU, memory, and input/output devices</li>
</ul>
<h4>3. Registers</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Very small and fast memory locations inside the CPU</li>
  <li>Store temporary data and instructions during processing</li>
  <li>Examples include:</li>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Accumulator:</b> Stores intermediate results of calculations</li>
    <li><b>Program Counter (PC):</b> Keeps track of the next instruction</li>
    <li><b>Instruction Register (IR):</b> Holds the current instruction being executed</li>
  </ul>
</ul>
<h3> IMPORTANCE OF THE CPU</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Controls all computer operations</li>
  <li>Processes data and instructions</li>
  <li>Coordinates all hardware components</li>
  <li>Ensures programs run correctly</li>
</ul>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for CPU Components', 15),
  (2055, 2055, '<h2>Instruction Cycle (Fetch → Decode → Execute)</h2>
<h3> DEEP EXPLANATION</h3>
<p>
The instruction cycle is the repeated process the CPU follows to execute program instructions.
It runs continuously while the computer is powered on, allowing software to function step by step.
</p>

<h3> MAIN STAGES OF THE INSTRUCTION CYCLE</h3>

<h4>1. Fetch</h4>
<ul>
  <li>The CPU retrieves an instruction from main memory (RAM)</li>
  <li>The instruction is stored in the Instruction Register (IR)</li>
  <li>The Program Counter (PC) points to the next instruction</li>
</ul>

<h4>2. Decode</h4>
<ul>
  <li>The Control Unit (CU) interprets the instruction</li>
  <li>It determines what operation is required</li>
  <li>It identifies the data and components needed</li>
</ul>

<h4>3. Execute</h4>
<ul>
  <li>The ALU or other CPU components carry out the instruction</li>
  <li>Results are produced and stored in registers or memory</li>
  <li>Output may be sent to an output device if required</li>
</ul>

<h3> INSTRUCTION CYCLE FLOW</h3>
<pre>
Fetch → Decode → Execute → Fetch → Decode → Execute (repeats continuously)
</pre>

<h3> DETAILED PROCESS INSIDE CPU</h3>
<ul>
  <li>PC sends address to memory</li>
  <li>Instruction is fetched into IR</li>
  <li>CU decodes instruction</li>
  <li>ALU executes operation</li>
  <li>Result stored and PC updates</li>
</ul>

<h3> IMPORTANCE OF THE INSTRUCTION CYCLE</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Enables computers to run programs step by step</li>
  <li>Ensures systematic execution of instructions</li>
  <li>Keeps CPU working continuously and efficiently</li>
</ul>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Instruction Cycle', 15),
  (2056, 2056, '<h2>Von Neumann Architecture</h2>
<h3> DEEP EXPLANATION</h3>
<p>
The Von Neumann architecture is a computer design model where both data and instructions are stored in the same main memory.
It forms the basic structure of most modern computers and follows a sequential processing system.
</p>
<h3> MAIN COMPONENTS</h3>
<ul>
  <li><b>CPU:</b> Contains the ALU and Control Unit (executes instructions)</li>
  <li><b>Main Memory (RAM):</b> Stores both data and instructions</li>
  <li><b>Input Devices:</b> Used to enter data into the computer</li>
  <li><b>Output Devices:</b> Display processed results</li>
  <li><b>System Bus:</b> Transfers data and instructions between components</li>
</ul>
<h3> VON NEUMANN STRUCTURE</h3>
<pre>
Input Devices → Memory ↔ CPU → Output Devices
                     ↑
                 System Bus
</pre>

<h3> TYPES OF BUSES AND THEIR PURPOSES</h3>
<h4>1. Data Bus</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Transfers actual data between CPU, memory, and input/output devices</li>
  <li>Usually bidirectional (data can move both ways)</li>
  <li>Width of data bus affects performance (more bits = faster transfer)</li>
</ul>
<h4>2. Address Bus</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Transfers memory addresses from CPU to memory</li>
  <li>Usually unidirectional (CPU → Memory)</li>
  <li>Determines how much memory a system can access</li>
</ul>
<h4>3. Control Bus</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Transfers control signals between CPU and other components</li>
  <li>Includes signals like read, write, interrupt, and clock signals</li>
  <li>Ensures all parts of the system are synchronized</li>
</ul>
<h3> KEY IDEA</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Data and instructions share the same memory</li>
  <li>Instructions are processed one at a time (sequential execution)</li>
  <li>CPU follows the fetch–decode–execute cycle</li>
</ul>
<h3> HOW IT WORKS</h3>
<ol>
  <li>Input data and instructions are stored in memory</li>
  <li>CPU fetches instruction from memory</li>
  <li>Instruction is decoded by Control Unit</li>
  <li>ALU executes the instruction</li>
  <li>Result is stored back in memory or sent to output</li>
</ol>
<h3> LIMITATION: VON NEUMANN BOTTLENECK</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Data and instructions share the same bus/path</li>
  <li>CPU must wait to access memory</li>
  <li>Slows down processing speed</li>
</ul>
<pre>
Problem:
CPU ↔ SAME BUS ↔ MEMORY (data + instructions)
This creates a bottleneck (traffic jam in data flow)
</pre>
<h3> ADVANTAGES</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Simple and easy to design</li>
  <li>Cost-effective</li>
  <li>Flexible program storage (data + instructions together)</li>
</ul>
<h3> SIMPLE EXPLANATION</h3>
<p>
The Von Neumann architecture is like a single road used by both instructions and data, which makes it simple but can cause delays when traffic is heavy.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Von Neumann Architecture', 15),
  (2057, 2057, '<h2>Introduction to Computers</h2>
<p>A <strong>computer</strong> is a machine that takes in information (input), processes it, stores it, and produces results (output).</p>
<h2>1. Hardware (Physical Parts)</h2>
<p><strong>Hardware</strong> is anything you can see and touch.</p>
<h3>Main Components</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Input Devices:</strong> Used to enter data into the computer</li>
<li><strong>Processing Unit (CPU):</strong> The "brain" of the computer</li>
<li><strong>Output Devices:</strong> Show results</li>
<li><strong>Storage Devices:</strong> Save data</li>
</ul>
<h3>Examples of Hardware</h3>
<ul>
<li>Keyboard (typing)</li>
<li>Mouse (pointing)</li>
<li>Monitor (screen)</li>
<li>CPU/System Unit (processing)</li>
<li>Hard drive / SSD (storage)</li>
<li>Printer (output)</li>
</ul>
<h2>2. Software (Programs)</h2>
<p><strong>Software</strong> tells the hardware what to do.</p>

<h3>Types of Software</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>System Software:</strong> Controls the computer (Operating System)</li>
<li><strong>Application Software:</strong> Programs used by users (Word, Chrome)</li>
</ul>
<div class="example-box">
Examples:<br>
Windows (OS)<br>
Google Chrome (browser)<br>
Microsoft Word (document program)
</div>
<h2>3. Operating System (OS)</h2>
<p>The <strong>Operating System</strong> is the most important software. It connects the user to the hardware.</p>
<ul>
<li>Manages memory</li>
<li>Controls devices</li>
<li>Runs applications</li>
</ul>
<div class="example-box">
Examples:<br>
Windows<br>
Linux<br>
macOS
</div>
<h2>4. Peripheral Devices</h2>
<p>Peripheral devices are external hardware connected to the computer.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Input:</strong> keyboard, mouse, scanner</li>
<li><strong>Output:</strong> monitor, printer, speakers</li>
<li><strong>Storage:</strong> flash drive, external hard disk</li>
</ul>
<h2>5. Liveware (Human Factor)</h2>
<p><strong>Liveware</strong> refers to the human user of the computer.</p>
<ul>
<li>The person operating the computer</li>
<li>Gives instructions</li>
<li>Interprets results</li>
</ul>
<div class="example-box">
Without a user, a computer cannot function meaningfully.
</div>
<h2>How Everything Works Together</h2>
<ul>
<li>User (liveware) gives input</li>
<li>Input devices send data to CPU</li>
<li>CPU processes the data</li>
<li>Results are shown through output devices</li>
<li>Data can be stored for later use</li>
</ul>
<div class="keyfact">
 Key Fact: A computer system = Hardware + Software + Liveware working together.
</div>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Hardware vs software', 15),
  (2058, 2058, '<h2>Input & Output Devices</h2>
<p>Input devices allow users to <strong>send data to the computer</strong>. Output devices allow the computer to <strong>present results to the user</strong>.</p>
<h2>1. Input Devices</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Keyboard:</strong> Types letters, numbers, symbols. Used for typing documents, commands. <em>Advantage:</em> Fast for text input. <em>Disadvantage:</em> Requires literacy/knowledge of layout.</li>
<li><strong>Mouse:</strong> Pointing device to select items. Used for GUI navigation. <em>Advantage:</em> Accurate pointer control. <em>Disadvantage:</em> Needs flat surface.</li>
<li><strong>Touchscreen:</strong> Detects finger/stylus input directly on screen. Used in phones, tablets, ATMs. <em>Advantage:</em> Direct interaction, intuitive. <em>Disadvantage:</em> Can get dirty, less precise for long text.</li>
<li><strong>Stylus:</strong> Pen-like tool for touchscreens. Used in tablets for drawing or handwriting. <em>Advantage:</em> More precise than finger. <em>Disadvantage:</em> Can be lost, needs compatible screen.</li>
<li><strong>Joystick:</strong> Game controller, moves objects in multiple directions. Used in gaming, simulations. <em>Advantage:</em> Easy control in games. <em>Disadvantage:</em> Limited general use.</li>
<li><strong>Voice Input:</strong> Converts spoken words to digital commands. Used in smartphones, voice assistants. <em>Advantage:</em> Hands-free, fast. <em>Disadvantage:</em> Background noise may affect accuracy.</li>
<li><strong>Scanner:</strong> Converts physical documents/images to digital. Used in offices, schools. <em>Advantage:</em> Preserves original document. <em>Disadvantage:</em> Slower than direct typing.</li>
<li><strong>Pinball / Specialized Controllers:</strong> Used in entertainment or arcade systems. <em>Advantage:</em> Engaging interaction. <em>Disadvantage:</em> Limited to niche applications.</li>
</ul>
<div class="example-box">
Typing a report → keyboard input<br>
Drawing a sketch → stylus input<br>
Moving a character in a game → joystick input<br>
Voice commands to phone → voice input
</div>
<h2>2. Output Devices</h2>
<p>Output devices allow the computer to present results to the user.</p>
<h3>Soft Copy Output</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Monitor / Screen:</strong> Displays text, images, video. Advantage: Real-time, interactive. Disadvantage: Requires power, cannot be held physically.</li>
<li><strong>Speakers / Headphones:</strong> Produce sound output (music, alerts). Advantage: Hands-free audio feedback. Disadvantage: Affected by noise, not permanent.</li>
<li><strong>Voice Output:</strong> Converts text to speech (TTS). Advantage: Accessibility for visually impaired, hands-free. Disadvantage: Requires quality software, may mispronounce words.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Hard Copy Output</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Printers:</strong> Produces permanent copies on paper.</li>
<li><strong>Dot Matrix / Impact Printers:</strong> Strike ink onto paper via pins. Advantage: Cheap, multi-copy printing. Disadvantage: Noisy, low resolution.</li>
<li><strong>Laser Printers:</strong> Use laser beams to print toner. Advantage: High speed, high quality. Disadvantage: Expensive initial cost, toner cartridges.</li>
<li><strong>Inkjet Printers:</strong> Spray tiny ink droplets onto paper. Advantage: High quality, color printing. Disadvantage: Slower than laser, ink expensive.</li>
</ul>
<div class="example-box">
Printing an assignment → hard copy (paper)<br>
Watching video → soft copy (monitor)<br>
Listening to music → soft copy (speakers)
</div>
<h2>3. Advantages and Disadvantages</h2>
<table>
<tr><th>Device Type</th><th>Advantage</th><th>Disadvantage</th></tr>
<tr><td>Keyboard</td><td>Fast text input</td><td>Needs literacy</td></tr>
<tr><td>Mouse</td><td>Accurate pointer control</td><td>Needs flat surface</td></tr>
<tr><td>Touchscreen</td><td>Intuitive, direct interaction</td><td>Less precise for typing</td></tr>
<tr><td>Stylus</td><td>Precision drawing</td><td>Can be lost</td></tr>
<tr><td>Joystick</td><td>Easy control in games</td><td>Limited general use</td></tr>
<tr><td>Voice Input</td><td>Hands-free</td><td>Background noise affects accuracy</td></tr>
<tr><td>Monitor</td><td>Real-time display</td><td>Cannot hold physically</td></tr>
<tr><td>Printer</td><td>Permanent copy</td><td>Costs paper/ink</td></tr>
<tr><td>Speaker</td><td>Audio feedback</td><td>No visual info, affected by noise</td></tr>
</table>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Input & Output Devices', 15),
  (2059, 2059, '<h2>Memory & Storage</h2>
<p>Computers need memory to <strong>store data and instructions</strong>. There are two main types: <strong>Primary memory</strong> (fast, temporary) and <strong>Secondary storage</strong> (slower, permanent).</p>
<h2>1. Primary Memory (Main Memory)</h2>
<p>Used by the CPU to store data that is being actively used.</p>

<ul>
<li><strong>RAM (Random Access Memory):</strong> Volatile memory; data lost when power is off. Used for active programs and OS. Types: <em>DRAM (Dynamic), SRAM (Static)</em>. Advantage: Fast access. Disadvantage: Temporary.</li>
<li><strong>ROM (Read-Only Memory):</strong> Non-volatile; stores permanent instructions (e.g., BIOS). Cannot be modified easily. Types: PROM, EPROM, EEPROM. Advantage: Permanent. Disadvantage: Cannot change easily.</li>
<li><strong>Cache Memory:</strong> Very fast memory located close to CPU. Stores frequently used data for quick access. Types: L1 (smallest, fastest), L2 (larger, slower than L1), L3 (largest, shared in multi-core CPUs). Advantage: Improves CPU performance. Disadvantage: Expensive, limited size.</li>
<li><strong>Registers:</strong> Smallest, fastest memory inside CPU. Temporarily stores data, instructions, or addresses being processed. Advantage: Immediate access for CPU. Disadvantage: Very limited storage.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Buses in Memory</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Data Bus:</strong> Transfers data between CPU and memory.</li>
<li><strong>Address Bus:</strong> Carries memory addresses to read/write locations.</li>
<li><strong>Control Bus:</strong> Sends control signals (read/write operations).</li>
</ul>
<div class="example-box">
CPU accesses RAM → stores active program<br>
Registers hold intermediate calculation results<br>
Cache holds frequently used data to speed CPU
</div>
<h2>2. Secondary Storage</h2>
<p>Used for permanent storage of programs and data. Slower than primary memory but non-volatile.</p>
<h3>Magnetic Storage</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Hard Disk Drive (HDD):</strong> Uses magnetic disks to store data. Advantage: High capacity, cheap per GB. Disadvantage: Slower than SSD, sensitive to shocks and magnets.</li>
<li><strong>Magnetic Tape:</strong> Stores large data sequentially. Advantage: Cheap, good for backups. Disadvantage: Slow, sequential access.</li>
<li><strong>Floppy Disk:</strong> Old magnetic storage. Advantage: Portable. Disadvantage: Very low capacity, obsolete.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Solid-State Storage</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>SSD (Solid-State Drive):</strong> Uses flash memory, no moving parts. Advantage: Fast, durable. Disadvantage: More expensive per GB than HDD.</li>
<li><strong>USB Flash Drive:</strong> Portable flash memory. Advantage: Easy to carry, plug-and-play. Disadvantage: Small capacity, can be lost easily.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Optical Storage</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>CD-ROM / CD-R / CD-RW:</strong> 700 MB. CD-R: write once. CD-RW: rewriteable. Advantage: Cheap, portable. Disadvantage: Low capacity, slower than HDD/SSD.</li>
<li><strong>DVD / DVD-R / DVD-RW:</strong> 4.7–8.5 GB. Advantage: Larger capacity than CD. Disadvantage: Slower than HDD/SSD.</li>
<li><strong>Blu-Ray Disc:</strong> 25–50 GB. Advantage: High capacity, good for HD video. Disadvantage: Expensive, requires Blu-Ray drive.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Handling & Care of Storage Devices</h3>
<ul>
<li>Keep HDDs away from strong magnets → data can be erased.</li>
<li>Avoid exposing disks (HDD, CDs/DVDs/BDs) to dust, smoke, heat, or scratches.</li>
<li>Use anti-static precautions for RAM or motherboards.</li>
<li>Do not bend or drop flash drives or floppy disks.</li>
<li>Back up critical data to multiple storage types for safety.</li>
</ul>
<div class="example-box">
Saving a document → stored on HDD/SSD<br>
Watching a movie → from DVD/Blu-Ray or SSD<br>
Booting computer → BIOS instructions from ROM
</div>
<div class="keyfact"> Key Fact: Primary memory is fast but volatile; secondary storage is slower but permanent. Proper care ensures long lifespan and data safety.</div>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Memory & Storage', 15),
  (2060, 2060, '<h2>Operating System</h2>
<p>An operating system (OS) is a set of system software that manages computer hardware and software resources, serving as an interface between the user and the computer hardware. It allows applications to run, manages memory, schedules tasks, and controls peripheral devices.</p>
<h2>Key Notes and Concepts</h2>
<h3>1. Primary Roles and Goals</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Interface:</strong> Acts as an intermediary between the user and computer hardware, making the computer convenient to use.</li>
<li><strong>Resource Manager:</strong> Allocates and manages shared hardware resources such as CPU time, memory, and Input/Output (I/O) devices, ensuring efficient utilization.</li>
<li><strong>Abstraction:</strong> Conceals the complexities of hardware from applications, providing a uniform interface.</li>
<li><strong>Kernel:</strong> The core, essential part of the OS that is always running and manages critical functions.</li>
<li><strong>Shell:</strong> The component that interprets user commands and gets them executed.</li>
</ul>
<h3>2. Core Functions of an Operating System</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Process Management:</strong> Manages the execution of applications (processes), including scheduling, creation, termination, and synchronization.</li>
<li><strong>Memory Management:</strong> Tracks and allocates Main Memory (RAM). It decides which processes load into memory and manages virtual memory (using hard disk space as an extension of RAM).</li>
<li><strong>File Management:</strong> Organizes, creates, deletes, and manages files and directories on storage devices.</li>
<li><strong>Device Management:</strong> Uses device drivers to communicate with hardware peripherals (printers, disks, keyboards).</li>
<li><strong>Security and Protection:</strong> Protects data and resources from unauthorized access via authentication (passwords) and isolation of processes.</li>
<li><strong>Error Detection:</strong> Monitors system health and reports errors to prevent system failure.</li>
</ul>
<h3>3. Key OS Concepts and Techniques</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>System Calls:</strong> The interface between a running program and the OS kernel, allowing user-level programs to request services.</li>
<li><strong>Multitasking/Multiprogramming:</strong> The ability of an OS to switch between tasks quickly, allowing multiple programs to run seemingly at the same time and keeping the CPU busy.</li>
<li><strong>Interrupts:</strong> Signals sent to the CPU by hardware or software to request immediate attention, allowing the OS to react to events.</li>
<li><strong>Deadlock Handling:</strong> Manages situations where two or more processes are stuck waiting for resources held by each other.</li>
<li><strong>Spooling:</strong> Temporarily storing data in a buffer (e.g., for printers) so the CPU can continue other tasks while the slow device works.</li>
<li><strong>Booting:</strong> The process of starting a computer by loading the kernel into memory.</li>
</ul>
<h3>4. Types of Operating Systems</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Batch OS:</strong> Jobs with similar needs are grouped (batched) and processed together without user interaction (e.g., payroll).</li>
<li><strong>Time-Sharing (Multitasking) OS:</strong> Allows multiple users to share the system simultaneously by switching between tasks quickly (e.g., Unix, Windows).</li>
<li><strong>Distributed OS:</strong> Manages a group of independent computers and makes them appear as a single system.</li>
<li><strong>Real-Time OS (RTOS):</strong> Used when strict time constraints are required (e.g., robotics, airbag controllers).</li>
<li><strong>Mobile OS:</strong> Designed specifically for smartphones and tablets (e.g., Android, iOS).</li>
</ul>
<h3>5. Common Examples</h3>
<ul>
<li>Windows: Microsoft’s commercial OS for desktops and servers.</li>
<li>Linux: Open-source, Unix-based OS, popular on servers.</li>
<li>macOS: Apple’s desktop OS.</li>
<li>Android/iOS: Dominant mobile operating systems.</li>
</ul>
<h3>6. OS Structure</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Monolithic Kernel:</strong> All OS services run in kernel space, high performance but less stable (e.g., Linux).</li>
<li><strong>Microkernel:</strong> Only essential services run in kernel space; others run in user space, increasing stability (e.g., Minix).</li>
<li><strong>Hybrid Kernel:</strong> Combines aspects of both (e.g., Windows NT, macOS).</li>
</ul>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Operating systems', 15),
  (2061, 2061, '<h2>File Management</h2>
<p>File management is a fundamental concept in computing that involves how an operating system (OS) stores, names, organizes, and manipulates files. In a high school curriculum, this typically covers the hierarchy of storage, file attributes, and common operations.</p>
<h3>1. The File Hierarchy (Folders and Directories)</h3>
<p>Computers use a hierarchical structure (often called a "tree structure") to organize data.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Drive:</strong> The top level of storage (e.g., C: drive for a Hard Drive, D: for a USB).</li>
<li><strong>Folders (Directories):</strong> Containers used to group related files together.</li>
<li><strong>Sub-folders:</strong> Folders inside other folders.</li>
<li><strong>Files:</strong> The actual data units (documents, images, programs).</li>
</ul>
<div class="example-box">
Example Path:<br>
C:\Documents\School\ComputerScience\Notes.docx<br>
"School" is a folder, and "Notes.docx" is the file.
</div>
<h3>2. File Naming and Extensions</h3>
<p>A filename consists of two parts: the name and the extension, separated by a dot.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Filename:</strong> A descriptive name (e.g., History_Essay)</li>
<li><strong>Extension:</strong> Indicates the file type and program used to open it</li>
</ul>
<table>
<tr><th>Type</th><th>Extension</th><th>Program Example</th></tr>
<tr><td>Documents</td><td>.docx, .pdf, .txt</td><td>Word, Adobe Reader</td></tr>
<tr><td>Images</td><td>.jpg, .png, .gif</td><td>Photos, Photoshop</td></tr>
<tr><td>Audio</td><td>.mp3, .wav</td><td>Spotify, Media Player</td></tr>
<tr><td>Video</td><td>.mp4, .mov</td><td>VLC, QuickTime</td></tr>
<tr><td>Executables</td><td>.exe, .app</td><td>System Applications</td></tr>
</table>
<h3>3. Basic File Operations</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Create:</strong> Making a new file or folder</li>
<li><strong>Save / Save As:</strong> Writing data to storage ("Save As" changes name/type)</li>
<li><strong>Copy:</strong> Duplicate file in another location</li>
<li><strong>Move (Cut/Paste):</strong> Change file location</li>
<li><strong>Rename:</strong> Change filename</li>
<li><strong>Delete:</strong> Send file to Recycle Bin / Trash</li>
</ul>
<h3>4. File Metadata and Attributes</h3>
<p>Files have hidden information called metadata:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>File Size:</strong> KB, MB, GB</li>
<li><strong>Date Created/Modified</strong></li>
<li><strong>Permissions:</strong> Read, Write, Execute</li>
<li><strong>Status:</strong> Hidden, System, Read-only</li>
</ul>
<h3>5. Storage Best Practices</h3>
<ul>
<li>Use descriptive names (e.g., Bio_Lab_Report_V2)</li>
<li>Organize files into folders</li>
<li>Backup files regularly (Cloud or external drive)</li>
<li>Avoid deep nesting (too many folders inside folders)</li>
</ul>
<h3>6. Compression and Archiving</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Compression (.zip, .rar):</strong> Reduces file size</li>
<li><strong>Extracting:</strong> Restores compressed files to original form</li>
</ul>
<div class="example-box">
Example:<br>
Large folder → compressed to .zip → sent via email → extracted by receiver
</div>
<div class="keyfact">
 Key Fact: Good file management makes it easier to find files, saves storage space, and prevents data loss.
</div>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for File management', 15),
  (2062, 2062, '<h2>Introduction to Programming</h2>
<p><strong>Programming</strong> is the process of creating a set of instructions that tells a computer how to perform a specific task.</p>
<div class="example-box">
Think of programming like writing a recipe:<br>
Ingredients → Data<br>
Steps → Code<br>
Final dish → Output
</div>
<h2>1. Essential Programming Terminology</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Algorithm:</strong> A step-by-step procedure for solving a problem.</li>
<li><strong>Source Code:</strong> The actual text written by a programmer.</li>
<li><strong>Syntax:</strong> Rules that define how code must be written.</li>
<li><strong>Bug:</strong> An error in a program.</li>
<li><strong>Debugging:</strong> Finding and fixing errors.</li>
<li><strong>Variable:</strong> A container for storing data.</li>
<li><strong>Compiler/Interpreter:</strong> Tools that translate code into machine language (1s and 0s).</li>
</ul>
<h2>2. The Programming Process (SDLC)</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Problem Definition:</strong> Understand the problem.</li>
<li><strong>Design:</strong> Plan the solution (flowcharts, pseudocode).</li>
<li><strong>Coding:</strong> Write the program.</li>
<li><strong>Testing:</strong> Find and fix bugs.</li>
<li><strong>Maintenance:</strong> Update and improve the program.</li>
</ul>
<div class="example-box">
Example:<br>
Build a calculator → plan steps → code → test → fix errors
</div>
<h2>3. Categories of Programming Languages</h2>
<h3>Low-Level Languages</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Machine Code:</strong> Binary (0s and 1s), very hard for humans.</li>
<li><strong>Assembly Language:</strong> Uses short codes (MOV, ADD).</li>
</ul>
<h3>High-Level Languages (HLL)</h3>
<p>Designed to be easy for humans.</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Language</th><th>Use</th><th>Strength</th></tr>
<tr><td>Python</td><td>AI, Data Science</td><td>Easy for beginners</td></tr>
<tr><td>Java</td><td>Apps, Systems</td><td>Portable</td></tr>
<tr><td>C++</td><td>Games</td><td>Fast</td></tr>
<tr><td>JavaScript</td><td>Web</td><td>Runs in browsers</td></tr>
<tr><td>Swift</td><td>iOS apps</td><td>Optimized for Apple</td></tr>
</table>
<h2>4. Fundamental Logic Structures</h2>
<h3>A. Sequence</h3>
<p>Instructions executed step by step.</p>
<h3>B. Selection (Decision)</h3>
<p>Program chooses based on condition.</p>
<div class="example-box">
IF age ≥ 18 → allow voting<br>
ELSE → deny access
</div>
<h3>C. Iteration (Loop)</h3>
<p>Repeats instructions.</p>
<div class="example-box">
Send 100 emails automatically
</div>
<h2>5. Data Types</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Integer:</strong> Whole numbers (5, -42)</li>
<li><strong>Float:</strong> Decimal numbers (3.14)</li>
<li><strong>String:</strong> Text ("Hello")</li>
<li><strong>Boolean:</strong> True/False</li>
</ul>
<h2>6. Interpreted vs Compiled Languages</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Compiled:</strong> Entire program translated before running (e.g., C++) → faster</li>
<li><strong>Interpreted:</strong> Runs line-by-line (e.g., Python) → easier debugging</li>
</ul>
<div class="example-box">
C++ → compiled → fast execution<br>
Python → interpreted → easier to debug
</div>
<div class="keyfact">
 Key Fact: Every program you use — from games to mobile apps — follows these same core principles.
</div>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Introduction to Programming', 15),
  (2063, 2063, '<h2>Loops & Conditionals</h2>
<p><strong>Conditionals:</strong> decision making</p>
<pre>
if x > 5:
  print("Big")
</pre>
<p><strong>Loops:</strong> repetition</p>
<pre>
for i in range(5):
  print(i)
</pre>
<div class="example-box">
Loop prints numbers 0–4
</div>
<h2>Control Structures</h2>
<p>Control structures are the "brain" of any program. They allow the code to make decisions and repeat tasks, moving beyond simple step-by-step execution.</p>
<h2>1. Conditional Statements (Selection)</h2>
<p>Conditionals allow a program to execute specific blocks of code only if a certain condition is met.</p>
<h3>A. The if Statement</h3>
<p>The most basic decision-making statement. If the condition is true, the code runs.</p>
<pre>
age = 20
if age >= 18:
    print("You are an adult.")
</pre>
<h3>B. The if-else Statement</h3>
<p>Provides an alternative path if the condition is false.</p>
<pre>
score = 45
if score >= 50:
    print("Pass")
else:
    print("Fail")
</pre>
<h3>C. The if-elif-else Ladder</h3>
<p>Used when there are more than two possible outcomes.</p>
<pre>
grade = 85
if grade >= 90:
    print("A")
elif grade >= 80:
    print("B")
elif grade >= 70:
    print("C")
else:
    print("D or F")
</pre>
<h3>D. Nested if Statements</h3>
<p>An if statement inside another if for complex conditions.</p>
<pre>
has_ticket = True
has_id = False

if has_ticket:
    if has_id:
        print("Entry allowed.")
    else:
        print("Please show your ID.")
else:
    print("No ticket, no entry.")
</pre>
<h2>2. Loops (Iteration)</h2>
<p>Loops repeat a block of code multiple times.</p>
<h3>A. The for Loop</h3>
<p>Used when you know how many times to repeat.</p>
<pre>
for i in range(5):
    print("Loop number:", i)
</pre>
<h3>B. The while Loop</h3>
<p>Repeats while a condition is true.</p>
<pre>
count = 5
while count > 0:
    print(count)
    count = count - 1
print("Blast off!")
</pre>
<h3>C. Nested Loops</h3>
<p>A loop inside another loop.</p>
<pre>
for row in range(3):
    for col in range(3):
        print("Row", row, "Col", col)
</pre>
<h2>3. Key Loop Controls</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>break:</strong> Stops the loop immediately</li>
<li><strong>continue:</strong> Skips current iteration</li>
</ul>
<h2>Comparison: Loops vs Conditionals</h2>
<table>
<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Conditionals (if)</th><th>Loops (for/while)</th></tr>
<tr><td>Purpose</td><td>Decision making</td><td>Repetition</td></tr>
<tr><td>Execution</td><td>Runs once</td><td>Runs repeatedly</td></tr>
<tr><td>Keywords</td><td>if, else, elif</td><td>for, while</td></tr>
<tr><td>Example</td><td>Checking password</td><td>Printing multiple pages</td></tr>
</table>
<div class="keyfact">
 Conditionals decide WHAT to do, loops decide HOW MANY TIMES to do it.
</div>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Loops & conditionals', 15),
  (2064, 2064, '<h2>Functions & Methods</h2>
<p>A function is reusable code.</p>
<pre>
def add(a,b):
  return a+b
</pre>
<div class="example-box">
add(2,3) = 5
</div>
<h2>In programming, functions and methods are blocks of reusable code designed to perform a specific action.</h2>
<p>They help break down complex programs into smaller, manageable, and organized pieces.</p>
<h2>1. What is a Function?</h2>
<p>A function is a self-contained block of code that only runs when it is called. You can pass data, known as parameters, into a function, and it can return data as a result.</p>
<h3>Key Components:</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Declaration/Definition:</strong> Creating the function and giving it a name.</li>
<li><strong>Parameters:</strong> Variables listed in the function definition (the "inputs").</li>
<li><strong>Arguments:</strong> The actual values sent to the function when it is called.</li>
<li><strong>Return Statement:</strong> The "output" the function sends back to the main program.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Example in Python:</h3>
<pre>
# Defining the function
def greet_user(name): 
    return "Hello, " + name
# Calling the function
message = greet_user("Alex")
print(message)
</pre>
<h2>2. What is a Method?</h2>
<p>A method is essentially a function that "belongs" to an object or a class. While functions can be called by themselves, a method must be called on a specific object.</p>
<h3>The Difference:</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Function:</strong> calculate_area(5, 10) (Independent)</li>
<li><strong>Method:</strong> my_dog.bark() (Dependent on the "dog" object)</li>
</ul>
<h3>Example of a String Method:</h3>
<pre>
text = "hello world"
print(text.upper())
</pre>
<h2>3. Why Use Functions and Methods?</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Reusability (DRY):</strong> Write code once and reuse it.</li>
<li><strong>Abstraction:</strong> Use code without knowing internal details.</li>
<li><strong>Organization:</strong> Makes code easier to read and debug.</li>
<li><strong>Modularity:</strong> Allows teamwork in programming.</li>
</ul>
<h2>4. Function Scope (Local vs Global)</h2>
<p>Scope determines where a variable can be used.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Local Scope:</strong> Variable inside function.</li>
<li><strong>Global Scope:</strong> Variable outside function.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Example:</h3>
<pre>
x = 10 # Global variable
def my_function():
    y = 5 # Local variable
    print(x)
    print(y)

my_function()
print(y) # ERROR
</pre>
<h2>5. Built-in vs User-Defined Functions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Built-in:</strong> Provided by language (print(), len())</li>
<li><strong>User-defined:</strong> Created by programmer</li>
</ul>
<h2>Summary Table</h2>
<table>
<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Function</th><th>Method</th></tr>
<tr><td>Location</td><td>Independent</td><td>Inside class</td></tr>
<tr><td>Call</td><td>function_name()</td><td>object.method()</td></tr>
<tr><td>Relationship</td><td>No object</td><td>Linked to object</td></tr>
<tr><td>Data</td><td>Uses parameters</td><td>Uses object data</td></tr>
</table>
<div class="keyfact">
 Functions are independent blocks of code, while methods belong to objects.
</div>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Functions & methods', 15),
  (2065, 2065, '<h2>Arrays & Lists</h2>
<p>In programming, we often need to store a collection of similar items (like a list of student names or temperatures). Instead of creating many separate variables, we use <b>Arrays and Lists</b>.</p>
<h3>1. What is an Array?</h3>
<p>An <b>Array</b> is a collection of items stored at contiguous (neighboring) memory locations. It holds a fixed number of items, usually of the same data type.</p>
<ul>
<li><b>Fixed Size:</b> Cannot be changed after creation.</li>
<li><b>Homogeneous:</b> Same data type.</li>
<li><b>Index-Based:</b> Each element has a position.</li>
</ul>
<h3>2. The Concept of Indexing</h3>
<p>Indexing starts at <b>0</b>:</p>
<ul>
<li>First element → index 0</li>
<li>Second element → index 1</li>
<li>Last element → index (n-1)</li>
</ul>
<pre>
prices = [10.50, 20.00, 5.75]
prices[0] = 10.50
prices[2] = 5.75
</pre>
<h3>3. Arrays vs Lists</h3>
<table border="1">
<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Array</th><th>List</th></tr>
<tr><td>Size</td><td>Fixed</td><td>Dynamic</td></tr>
<tr><td>Data Types</td><td>Same type</td><td>Mixed allowed (Python)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Memory</td><td>Efficient</td><td>More flexible</td></tr>
<tr><td>Languages</td><td>C, C++, Java</td><td>Python, JavaScript</td></tr>
</table>
<h3>4. Common Operations</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Accessing:</b> Get value using index</li>
<li><b>Updating:</b> Change value</li>
<li><b>Appending:</b> Add item (Lists)</li>
<li><b>Traversing:</b> Loop through items</li>
</ul>
<h3>5. Code Examples</h3>
<h4>Python (Lists)</h4>
<pre>
fruits = ["Apple", "Banana", "Cherry"]
fruits.append("Orange")
fruits[1] = "Blueberry"
for x in fruits:
    print(x)
</pre>
<h4>Java (Arrays)</h4>
<pre>
int[] numbers = new int[5];

numbers[0] = 10;
numbers[1] = 20;

System.out.println(numbers[0]);
</pre>
<h3>6. Two-Dimensional (2D) Arrays</h3>
<pre>
grid = [
  [1,2,3],
  [4,5,6],
  [7,8,9]
]

print(grid[1][1])
</pre>
<h3>7. Why Use Arrays/Lists?</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Efficiency:</b> Fast access using index</li>
<li><b>Group Processing:</b> Handle many items with loops</li>
<li><b>Sorting:</b> Organize data easily</li>
</ul>
<div class="example-box">
numbers = [1,2,3]
numbers[0] = 1
</div>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Arrays & lists', 15),
  (2066, 2066, '<h2>Debugging</h2>
<p>
<b>Debugging</b> is the systematic process of identifying, analyzing, and fixing errors (bugs) in a computer program. It is a core skill in programming and software development.
</p>
<h3> DEBUGGING PROCESS</h3>
<ol>
<li><b>Reproduce the error</b>: Make the bug happen consistently</li>
<li><b>Locate the error</b>: Identify the exact part of the code causing the issue</li>
<li><b>Analyze the cause</b>: Understand why the error occurs</li>
<li><b>Fix and test</b>: Correct the code and verify the solution</li>
</ol>
<h3> TYPES OF ERRORS</h3>
<h4>1. Syntax Errors</h4>
<p>
Errors caused by breaking programming language rules. The program will not run.
</p>
<pre>
if age >= 18
    print("Allowed")  # Missing colon causes error

# Fix
if age >= 18:
    print("Allowed")
</pre>
<h4>2. Runtime Errors</h4>
<p>
Errors that occur while the program is running (often causes crash).
</p>
<pre>
x = 10
y = 0
print(x / y)  # Division by zero error

# Fix
if y != 0:
    print(x / y)
</pre>
<h4>3. Logical Errors</h4>
<p>
The program runs but produces incorrect results.
</p>
<pre>
# Wrong
average = num1 + num2 / 2

# Correct
average = (num1 + num2) / 2
</pre>
<h3> COMMON ERROR TYPES</h3>
<table border="1">
<tr><th>Error</th><th>Cause</th><th>Fix</th></tr>
<tr><td>NameError</td><td>Variable not defined</td><td>Define before use</td></tr>
<tr><td>TypeError</td><td>Wrong data type operation</td><td>Convert types (e.g. str(), int())</td></tr>
<tr><td>IndexError</td><td>Invalid list index</td><td>Check list size</td></tr>
<tr><td>IndentationError</td><td>Wrong spacing</td><td>Fix indentation</td></tr>
</table>
<h3> DEBUGGING TECHNIQUES</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Print debugging</b>: Use print() to trace values</li>
<li><b>Debugger tools</b>: Breakpoints, step-by-step execution</li>
<li><b>Rubber duck debugging</b>: Explain code to find logic errors</li>
<li><b>Code isolation</b>: Comment out sections to locate errors</li>
</ul>
<pre>
print("DEBUG:", x)
</pre>
<h3> DEBUGGING CHECKLIST</h3>
<ul>
<li>Read error messages carefully</li>
<li>Check spelling and syntax</li>
<li>Verify variable values and types</li>
<li>Simplify the problem</li>
</ul>
<div class="example-box">
Debugging = Systematic process of finding and fixing errors in code
</div>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Debugging', 15),
  (2067, 2067, '<h2>Object-Oriented Programming (OOP)</h2>
<p>
<b>Object-Oriented Programming (OOP)</b> is a programming paradigm that organizes software around <b>objects</b>, which combine data (attributes) and behavior (methods).
</p>
<h3> REAL-LIFE ANALOGY</h3>
<div class="example-box">
Car → Object<br>
Properties → color, speed<br>
Methods → drive(), brake()
</div>
<h3> CORE PRINCIPLES (PILLARS OF OOP)</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Encapsulation</b>: Hiding internal data and controlling access</li>
<li><b>Inheritance</b>: Reusing properties from another class</li>
<li><b>Polymorphism</b>: Same method behaves differently in different classes</li>
<li><b>Abstraction</b>: Hiding complex implementation and showing only essentials</li>
</ul>
<h3> BASIC STRUCTURE (PYTHON)</h3>
<pre>
class Car:
    def __init__(self, brand):
        self.brand = brand

    def drive(self):
        print(self.brand + " is moving")

my_car = Car("Toyota")
my_car.drive()
</pre>
<h3> KEY CONCEPTS</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Class</b>: Blueprint for objects</li>
<li><b>Object</b>: Instance of a class</li>
<li><b>Attributes</b>: Data inside objects</li>
<li><b>Methods</b>: Functions inside classes</li>
</ul>
<h3> WHY OOP IS IMPORTANT</h3>
<ul>
<li>Improves code reusability</li>
<li>Makes programs modular</li>
<li>Easy to maintain and scale</li>
<li>Closer to real-world modeling</li>
</ul>
<div class="keyfact">
 OOP = Objects + Data + Behavior working together
</div>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Object-Oriented Programming (OOP)', 15),
  (2068, 2068, '<h2>Classes & Objects</h2>
<p>
In Object-Oriented Programming (OOP), a <b>class</b> is a blueprint (template), while an <b>object</b> is a real instance created from that blueprint.
</p>
<h3> KEY IDEA</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Class</b>: Defines structure (attributes + methods)</li>
<li><b>Object</b>: A real entity created from a class with actual values</li>
</ul>
<h3> PYTHON EXAMPLE</h3>
<pre>
class Student:
    def __init__(self, name):
        self.name = name

student1 = Student("Alex")

print(student1.name)
</pre>
<h3> COMPONENTS</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Attributes</b>: Variables inside a class (e.g., name)</li>
<li><b>Methods</b>: Functions inside a class</li>
<li><b>Constructor (__init__)</b>: Initializes object values</li>
</ul>
<h3> REAL-WORLD ANALOGY</h3>
<ul>
<li>Class → Blueprint of a house</li>
<li>Object → Actual house built from blueprint</li>
</ul>
<div class="example-box">
Class = Template<br>
Object = Real instance created from template
</div>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Classes & Objects', 15),
  (2069, 2069, '<h2>Inheritance</h2>
<p>
<b>Inheritance</b> is an Object-Oriented Programming (OOP) concept where a new class (child class) acquires properties and methods from an existing class (parent class).
</p>
<h3> KEY IDEA</h3>
<p>
It represents a <b>parent → child relationship</b> and allows code reuse and extension.
</p>
<h3> PYTHON EXAMPLE</h3>
<pre>
class Animal:
    def speak(self):
        print("Animal sound")

class Dog(Animal):   # Dog inherits from Animal
    def bark(self):
        print("Woof!")

d = Dog()
d.speak()  # inherited method
d.bark()   # own method
</pre>
<h3> TYPES OF INHERITANCE</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Single Inheritance</b>: One parent → one child</li>
<li><b>Multiple Inheritance</b>: One child → multiple parents</li>
<li><b>Multilevel Inheritance</b>: Grandparent → Parent → Child</li>
<li><b>Hierarchical Inheritance</b>: One parent → multiple children</li>
</ul>
<h3> BENEFITS OF INHERITANCE</h3>
<ul>
<li>Code reusability</li>
<li>Reduces repetition</li>
<li>Improves maintainability</li>
<li>Supports hierarchical classification</li>
</ul>
<h3> REAL-WORLD ANALOGY</h3>
<ul>
<li>Animal → Dog, Cat, Bird</li>
<li>Vehicle → Car, Bus, Truck</li>
</ul>
<div class="keyfact">
 Inheritance = Parent class shares features with child classes
</div>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Inheritance', 15),
  (2070, 2070, '<h2>Encapsulation</h2>
<p>
<b>Encapsulation</b> is an Object-Oriented Programming (OOP) concept that involves 
<b>bundling data (variables) and methods (functions)</b> together and restricting direct access to the data.
</p>
<h3> KEY IDEA</h3>
<p>
Data is hidden from outside access and can only be modified through controlled methods.
</p>
<h3> PYTHON EXAMPLE</h3>
<pre>
class Bank:
    def __init__(self):
        self.__balance = 0   # Private variable

    def deposit(self, amount):
        self.__balance += amount

    def get_balance(self):
        return self.__balance
</pre>
<h3> ACCESS MODIFIERS (IMPORTANT)</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Public</b>: Accessible anywhere</li>
<li><b>Protected (_var)</b>: Should not be accessed directly</li>
<li><b>Private (__var)</b>: Strongly restricted access</li>
</ul>
<h3> WHY ENCAPSULATION?</h3>
<ul>
<li>Protects data from unauthorized access</li>
<li>Prevents accidental modification</li>
<li>Improves code security and reliability</li>
<li>Controls how data is used</li>
</ul>
<h3> REAL-WORLD ANALOGY</h3>
<ul>
<li>ATM Machine → You cannot access money directly</li>
<li>You use methods (deposit/withdraw) instead</li>
</ul>
<div class="example-box">
Encapsulation = Data hiding + Controlled access
</div>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Encapsulation', 15),
  (2071, 2071, '<h2>Recursion</h2>
<p>
<b>Recursion</b> is a programming technique where a function calls itself to solve a problem by breaking it into smaller sub-problems.
</p>
<h3> KEY COMPONENTS</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Base Case</b>: The stopping condition that ends recursion</li>
<li><b>Recursive Case</b>: The part where the function calls itself</li>
</ul>
<h3> HOW IT WORKS</h3>
<pre>
factorial(3)
= 3 × factorial(2)
= 3 × 2 × factorial(1)
= 3 × 2 × 1
= 6
</pre>
<h3> PYTHON EXAMPLE (FACTORIAL)</h3>
<pre>
def factorial(n):
    if n == 1:   # Base case
        return 1
    return n * factorial(n-1)  # Recursive call
</pre>
<h3> IMPORTANT RULES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Must have a base case (to avoid infinite recursion)</li>
<li>Each call should move toward the base case</li>
<li>Uses stack memory (call stack)</li>
</ul>

---

<h3> RECURSION VS ITERATION</h3>
<pre>
Recursion        | Iteration
-----------------------------
Function calls   | Loops (for/while)
Uses call stack  | Uses variables
Cleaner logic    | More memory efficient
</pre>

---

<h3> REAL-WORLD APPLICATION</h3>
<ul>
<li>Factorial and Fibonacci calculations</li>
<li>Tree and graph traversal</li>
<li>File system navigation</li>
<li>Divide-and-conquer algorithms</li>
</ul>

---

<div class="example-box">
Recursion = Function solving smaller versions of itself
</div>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Recursion', 15),
  (2072, 2072, '<h2>Stacks</h2>



<p>
A <b>stack</b> is a linear data structure that follows the <b>LIFO (Last In, First Out)</b> principle.
The last element added is the first one to be removed.
</p>

---

<h3> KEY OPERATIONS</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Push</b>: Add element to the top</li>
<li><b>Pop</b>: Remove the top element</li>
<li><b>Peek/Top</b>: View the top element without removing it</li>
<li><b>isEmpty</b>: Check if stack has no elements</li>
</ul>

---

<h3> VISUAL FLOW</h3>
<pre>
Push → [1, 2, 3] → Pop → [1, 2]
            ↑
          Top
</pre>

---

<h3> TYPES OF STACKS</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Simple Stack</b>: Basic LIFO structure</li>
<li><b>Dynamic Stack</b>: Grows/shrinks in size (using lists/linked lists)</li>
<li><b>Call Stack</b>: Used in function calls (program execution)</li>
</ul>

---

<h3> PYTHON EXAMPLE</h3>
<pre>
stack = []

# Push
stack.append(1)
stack.append(2)

# Pop
stack.pop()

# Peek
print(stack[-1])
</pre>

---

<h3> REAL-WORLD APPLICATION</h3>
<ul>
<li>Undo/Redo operations in apps</li>
<li>Function calls (call stack)</li>
<li>Expression evaluation (e.g., calculators)</li>
<li>Browser history navigation</li>
</ul>

---

<div class="example-box">
Stack = Pile of plates <br>
Last plate added → first removed
</div>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Stacks', 15),
  (2073, 2073, '<h2>Queues</h2>



<p>
A <b>queue</b> is a linear data structure that follows the <b>FIFO (First In, First Out)</b> principle.
The first element added is the first one to be removed.
</p>

---

<h3> KEY OPERATIONS</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Enqueue</b>: Add element to the rear (end)</li>
<li><b>Dequeue</b>: Remove element from the front</li>
<li><b>Peek/Front</b>: View the first element without removing it</li>
<li><b>isEmpty</b>: Check if queue has no elements</li>
</ul>

---

<h3> VISUAL FLOW</h3>
<pre>
Enqueue → [1, 2, 3] → Dequeue → [2, 3]
</pre>

---

<h3> TYPES OF QUEUES</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Simple Queue</b>: Basic FIFO structure</li>
<li><b>Circular Queue</b>: End connects to front to reuse space</li>
<li><b>Priority Queue</b>: Elements processed based on priority</li>
<li><b>Deque (Double-ended Queue)</b>: Insert/remove from both ends</li>
</ul>

---

<h3> PYTHON EXAMPLE</h3>
<pre>
from collections import deque

queue = deque()

# Enqueue
queue.append(1)
queue.append(2)

# Dequeue
queue.popleft()

# Peek
print(queue[0])
</pre>

---

<h3> REAL-WORLD APPLICATION</h3>
<ul>
<li>Printer queue (documents printed in order)</li>
<li>CPU task scheduling</li>
<li>Call center systems</li>
<li>Data buffering in streaming</li>
</ul>

---

<div class="example-box">
Queue = Line at a shop <br>
First person in line → first served
</div>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Queues', 15),
  (2074, 2074, '<h2>Advanced Databases</h2>

  <p>
    Advanced databases focus on powerful techniques used in real-world systems to manage large and complex data efficiently.
    These include JOIN operations, normalization, indexing, and performance optimization.
  </p>
  <h3> Key Idea</h3>
  <p>
    Advanced database systems are designed to organize data efficiently, reduce duplication, and speed up data retrieval.
  </p>

  <h3> 1. SQL JOINs</h3>
  <p>
    JOIN operations are used to combine data from two or more tables based on related columns.
  </p>

  <h4>INNER JOIN</h4>
  <p>Returns only records that match in both tables.</p>

  <pre>
SELECT Students.Name, Courses.CourseName
FROM Students
INNER JOIN Enrollments ON Students.ID = Enrollments.StudentID
INNER JOIN Courses ON Enrollments.CourseID = Courses.CourseID;
  </pre>

  <h4>LEFT JOIN</h4>
  <p>Returns all records from the left table and matching records from the right table.</p>

  <pre>
SELECT Students.Name, Courses.CourseName
FROM Students
LEFT JOIN Enrollments ON Students.ID = Enrollments.StudentID
LEFT JOIN Courses ON Enrollments.CourseID = Courses.CourseID;
  </pre>

  <h4>RIGHT JOIN</h4>
  <p>Returns all records from the right table and matching records from the left table.</p>

  <h3> 2. Normalization</h3>
  <p>
    Normalization is the process of organizing data in a database to reduce redundancy and improve data integrity.
  </p>

  <h4> Before Normalization</h4>
  <pre>
Student | Course | Instructor
Alex    | Math   | Mr. John
Alex    | Science| Mr. Mike
  </pre>

  <h4> After Normalization</h4>
  <pre>
Students Table
ID | Name

Courses Table
CourseID | CourseName

Enrollments Table
StudentID | CourseID
  </pre>

  <p>
    This removes duplication and improves efficiency.
  </p>

  <h3> 3. Indexing</h3>
  <p>
    An index is a database structure that improves the speed of data retrieval operations.
  </p>

  <pre>
CREATE INDEX idx_name ON Students(Name);
  </pre>

  <p>
    Without an index, the database scans every record. With an index, it quickly locates data.
  </p>

  <h3> 4. Performance Optimization</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Query Optimization:</b> Writing efficient SQL statements</li>
    <li><b>Indexing:</b> Speeds up searching</li>
    <li><b>Efficient Joins:</b> Reduce processing time</li>
    <li><b>Normalization:</b> Reduces redundancy</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    These tables are connected using relationships and JOIN queries to retrieve data efficiently.
  </p>

  <h3> Summary</h3>
  <div class="example-box">
    JOIN = Combine tables<br>
    Normalization = Remove duplication<br>
    Index = Speed up searching<br>
    Optimization = Improve performance
  </div>

  <h3> Simple Explanation</h3>
  <p>
    Advanced databases are like highly organized libraries where books are sorted, indexed, and connected for fast retrieval.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Advanced Databases', 15),
  (2075, 2075, '<h2>Tables & Records</h2>
  <p>
    A database is an organized collection of related data stored electronically and managed by a
    Database Management System (DBMS) such as MySQL or PostgreSQL.
  </p>
  <h3> Key Idea</h3>
  <p>
    Databases store information in structured formats called tables, making data easy to access, update, and manage.
  </p>

  <h3> 1. Components of a Database System</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Hardware:</b> Physical devices such as servers, storage drives, and memory</li>
    <li><b>Software:</b> DBMS like MySQL, Oracle, PostgreSQL</li>
    <li><b>Data:</b> The actual information stored in the system</li>
    <li><b>Procedures:</b> Rules and instructions for managing data</li>
    <li><b>Users:</b> Database administrators and end users</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> 2. Data Organization in Tables</h3>
  <p>
    Data in a database is stored in tables similar to spreadsheets.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><b>Table:</b> A collection of related data</li>
    <li><b>Row (Record):</b> A single complete entry (e.g., one student)</li>
    <li><b>Column (Field):</b> A specific type of data (e.g., Name, Age)</li>
  </ul>

  <h4>Example Table</h4>
  <table border="1">
    <tr><th>ID</th><th>Name</th><th>Age</th></tr>
    <tr><td>101</td><td>Alex</td><td>20</td></tr>
    <tr><td>102</td><td>Mary</td><td>22</td></tr>
  </table>

  <h3> 3. Primary Key</h3>
  <p>
    A primary key is a field that uniquely identifies each record in a table.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Must be unique</li>
    <li>Cannot be empty (NULL)</li>
    <li>Used to avoid duplicate records</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="example-box">
    Example: Student ID = Primary Key
  </div>

  <h3> 4. Importance of Databases</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Organizes large amounts of data efficiently</li>
    <li>Improves data retrieval and storage</li>
    <li>Reduces duplication of data</li>
    <li>Supports fast decision-making</li>
  </ul>
  <h3> Simple Explanation</h3>
  <p>
    A database is like a digital filing cabinet where information is stored in organized tables.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Tables & Records', 15),
  (2076, 2076, '<h2>SQL (Structured Query Language)</h2>
  <p>
    SQL is a standard programming language used to communicate with databases.
    It allows users to create, retrieve, update, and delete data in a database system.
  </p>
  <h3> Key Idea</h3>
  <p>
    SQL is the language that allows humans to communicate with databases and control stored data.
  </p>

  <h3> 1. Basic SQL Query</h3>
  <p>
    The SELECT statement is used to retrieve data from a database table.
  </p>

  <pre>
SELECT * FROM students;
  </pre>

  <p>
    This command retrieves all records from the "students" table.
  </p>

  <h3> 2. Filtering Data</h3>
  <p>
    The WHERE clause is used to filter records based on conditions.
  </p>

  <pre>
SELECT Name FROM students WHERE ID = 101;
  </pre>

  <p>
    This retrieves only the name of the student with ID 101.
  </p>

  <h3> 3. Common SQL Commands</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>SELECT:</b> Retrieves data</li>
    <li><b>INSERT:</b> Adds new data</li>
    <li><b>UPDATE:</b> Modifies existing data</li>
    <li><b>DELETE:</b> Removes data</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> 4. SQL Tools</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Direct SQL:</b> Writing commands manually in a query editor</li>
    <li><b>Form-Based Interfaces:</b> Using applications to input data</li>
    <li><b>Database Tools:</b> phpMyAdmin, MySQL Workbench, Microsoft Access</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> 5. Example Queries</h3>
  <pre>
-- Select specific columns
SELECT Name, Age FROM students;

-- Filter records
SELECT * FROM students WHERE Age > 18;

-- Insert data
INSERT INTO students (Name, Age) VALUES (''John'', 20);
  </pre>

  <h3> 6. Importance of SQL</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Used in almost all modern applications</li>
    <li>Handles large volumes of data efficiently</li>
    <li>Essential for web development and backend systems</li>
    <li>Supports data analysis and reporting</li>
  </ul>
  <h3> Simple Explanation</h3>
  <p>
    SQL is like giving instructions to a librarian so they can find, add, or remove books from a library database.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for SQL Basics', 15),
  (2077, 2077, '<h2>CRUD Operations</h2>
  <p>
    CRUD represents the four fundamental operations used to manage data in a database system.
    These operations form the basis of all database interactions and backend systems.
  </p>
  <h3> Key Idea</h3>
  <p>
    CRUD is the foundation of database management, controlling how data is created, accessed, modified, and removed.
  </p>
  <h3> 1. Meaning of CRUD</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Create:</b> Adds new records into a database</li>
    <li><b>Read:</b> Retrieves existing data</li>
    <li><b>Update:</b> Modifies existing records</li>
    <li><b>Delete:</b> Removes records from a database</li>
  </ul>
  <h3> 2. CRUD and SQL Mapping</h3>
  <h4>Create (INSERT)</h4>
  <pre>
INSERT INTO Students (ID, Name) VALUES (101, ''Alex'');
  </pre>
  <h4>Read (SELECT)</h4>
  <pre>
SELECT Name FROM Students WHERE ID = 101;
  </pre>
  <h4>Update</h4>
  <pre>
UPDATE Students SET Name = ''Alexander'' WHERE ID = 101;
  </pre>
  <h4>Delete</h4>
  <pre>
DELETE FROM Students WHERE ID = 101;
  </pre>
  <h3> 3. Database Integrity Rules</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Data Type Validation:</b> Ensures correct data formats are used</li>
    <li><b>Referential Integrity:</b> Maintains relationships between related tables</li>
    <li><b>Primary Key Constraint:</b> Ensures uniqueness of records</li>
  </ul>
  <h3> 4. Importance of CRUD</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Forms the core of all database operations</li>
    <li>Used in every application that stores data</li>
    <li>Ensures structured and controlled data management</li>
    <li>Supports backend system development</li>
  </ul>
  <h3> Simple Explanation</h3>
  <p>
    CRUD is like managing a notebook: you can write new information, read it, edit it, or erase it when no longer needed.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for CRUD Operations', 15),
  (2078, 2078, '<h2>Relational Databases</h2>
  <p>
    A relational database is a type of database that stores data in multiple tables that are linked together using relationships.
    This structure reduces duplication and improves data organization and efficiency.
  </p>
  <h3> Key Idea</h3>
  <p>
    Relational databases store data in separate tables and connect them using keys to ensure efficiency and avoid duplication.
  </p>
  <h3> 1. Why Relationships Matter</h3>
  <p>
    Instead of storing all data in one large table, relational databases divide data into smaller tables and link them.
  </p>
  <div class="example-box">
    Students Table → student details<br>
    Courses Table → course details<br>
    Enrollments Table → links students and courses
  </div>
  <h3> 2. Types of Relationships</h3>
  <h4>One-to-One (1:1)</h4>
  <p>One record in a table is linked to one record in another table.</p>
  <div class="example-box">
    Example: One student → One ID card
  </div>
  <h4>One-to-Many (1:M)</h4>
  <p>One record in a table is linked to many records in another table.</p>
  <div class="example-box">
    Example: One teacher → Many students
  </div>
  <h4>Many-to-Many (M∞M)</h4>
  <p>Many records in one table are linked to many records in another table.</p>
  <div class="example-box">
    Example: Students ↔ Courses
  </div>
  <h3> 3. Primary Key</h3>
  <p>
    A primary key is a unique identifier for each record in a table.
  </p>
  <pre>
Students Table
ID | Name
101 | Alex
102 | Jane
  </pre>

  <h3> 4. Foreign Key</h3>
  <p>
    A foreign key is a field in one table that refers to the primary key in another table.
  </p>

  <pre>
Enrollments Table
StudentID | CourseID
101       | 501
  </pre>

  <h3> 5. Example Database Structure</h3>
  <pre>
Students
ID | Name

Courses
CourseID | CourseName

Enrollments
StudentID | CourseID
  </pre>

  <h3> 6. SQL Example (Relationships)</h3>
  <pre>
CREATE TABLE Students (
  ID INT PRIMARY KEY,
  Name VARCHAR(50)
);

CREATE TABLE Courses (
  CourseID INT PRIMARY KEY,
  CourseName VARCHAR(50)
);

CREATE TABLE Enrollments (
  StudentID INT,
  CourseID INT,
  FOREIGN KEY (StudentID) REFERENCES Students(ID),
  FOREIGN KEY (CourseID) REFERENCES Courses(CourseID)
);
  </pre>

  <h3> 7. Referential Integrity</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Ensures relationships between tables remain valid</li>
    <li>Prevents invalid foreign key entries</li>
    <li>Stops deletion of linked records</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> 8. Importance of Relational Databases</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Reduces data duplication</li>
    <li>Improves data accuracy and consistency</li>
    <li>Allows complex queries across multiple tables</li>
    <li>Makes data management scalable</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> Simple Explanation</h3>
  <p>
    A relational database is like a network of linked notebooks where each notebook stores a different type of information, but all are connected.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Relational Databases', 15),
  (2079, 2079, '<h2>Transactions & ACID Properties</h2>
  <p>
    A transaction is a sequence of database operations that are executed as a single logical unit of work.
    Either all operations succeed, or none are applied.
  </p>
  <h3> Key Idea</h3>
  <p>
    Transactions ensure that database operations are reliable, consistent, and safe even when errors occur.
  </p>

  <h3> 1. Example of a Transaction</h3>
  <div class="example-box">
    Bank Transfer System:<br>
    Step 1: Deduct money from Account A<br>
    Step 2: Add money to Account B
  </div>

  <p>
    If any step fails, the entire transaction is canceled to prevent data errors.
  </p>

  <h3> 2. ACID Properties</h3>

  <h4> Atomicity</h4>
  <p>
    Ensures that all operations in a transaction succeed together or fail together.
  </p>

  <h4> Consistency</h4>
  <p>
    Ensures the database remains in a valid state before and after the transaction.
  </p>

  <h4> Isolation</h4>
  <p>
    Ensures multiple transactions do not interfere with each other when executed at the same time.
  </p>

  <h4> Durability</h4>
  <p>
    Once a transaction is committed, changes are permanent even after system failure.
  </p>

  <h3> 3. Transaction States</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Begin:</b> Transaction starts</li>
    <li><b>Commit:</b> Saves all changes permanently</li>
    <li><b>Rollback:</b> Cancels changes and restores previous state</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> 4. SQL Transaction Example</h3>

  <pre>
BEGIN;
UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - 100 WHERE id = 1;
UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance + 100 WHERE id = 2;
COMMIT;
  </pre>
  <p>
    If an error occurs before COMMIT, the system can perform a ROLLBACK.
  </p>
  <h3> 5. Importance of Transactions</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Prevents data corruption</li>
    <li>Ensures accurate financial systems</li>
    <li>Maintains data integrity</li>
    <li>Supports multi-user systems safely</li>
  </ul>
  <h3> Simple Explanation</h3>
  <p>
    A transaction is like a full mission: either everything is completed successfully, or nothing is saved.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Transactions & ACID Properties', 15),
  (2080, 2080, '<h2>NoSQL Databases (MongoDB Concept)</h2>
  <p>
    NoSQL databases are non-relational databases designed to store large volumes of unstructured or semi-structured data.
    They are commonly used in modern applications that require flexibility and high scalability.
  </p>
  <h3> Key Idea</h3>
  <p>
    NoSQL databases are designed for speed, flexibility, and scalability rather than strict table structures.
  </p>
  <h3> 1. Why NoSQL?</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Handles large-scale and big data systems</li>
    <li>Flexible schema (no fixed table structure)</li>
    <li>High performance for real-time applications</li>
    <li>Suitable for modern web and mobile apps</li>
  </ul>
  <h3> 2. Types of NoSQL Databases</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Document-based:</b> MongoDB (stores JSON-like documents)</li>
    <li><b>Key-value:</b> Redis (stores data as key-value pairs)</li>
    <li><b>Column-based:</b> Cassandra (stores data in columns)</li>
    <li><b>Graph-based:</b> Neo4j (stores relationships between data)</li>
  </ul>
  <h3> 3. MongoDB Structure</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Database:</b> Top-level container</li>
    <li><b>Collection:</b> Group of documents (like a table)</li>
    <li><b>Document:</b> Individual record stored in JSON format</li>
  </ul>
  <h3> 4. Example MongoDB Document</h3>
  <pre>
{
  "name": "Alex",
  "age": 20,
  "course": "Computer Science"
}
  </pre>
  <h3> 5. SQL vs NoSQL Comparison</h3>
  <table>
    <tr>
      <th>Feature</th>
      <th>SQL</th>
      <th>NoSQL</th>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Structure</td>
      <td>Fixed tables</td>
      <td>Flexible documents</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Schema</td>
      <td>Rigid</td>
      <td>Dynamic</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Scalability</td>
      <td>Vertical scaling</td>
      <td>Horizontal scaling</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Examples</td>
      <td>MySQL, PostgreSQL</td>
      <td>MongoDB, Redis</td>
    </tr>
  </table>
  <h3> 6. Importance of NoSQL</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Supports big data applications</li>
    <li>Used in social media platforms</li>
    <li>Handles real-time analytics</li>
    <li>Improves performance in large systems</li>
  </ul>
  <h3> Simple Explanation</h3>
  <p>
    NoSQL databases are like flexible digital storage systems that can store different types of data without strict rules.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for NoSQL Databases (MongoDB Concept)', 15),
  (2081, 2081, '<h2>Backup & Recovery Strategies</h2>

<p><b>Backup</b> is creating copies of data, while <b>recovery</b> is restoring data after loss or failure.</p>
<h3>1. Why Backup is Important</h3>
<ul>
<li>Prevents data loss</li>
<li>Protects against system failure</li>
<li>Helps recover from cyber attacks</li>
</ul>
<h3>2. Types of Backups</h3>

<h4>Full Backup</h4>
<p>Copies all data.</p>
<h4>Incremental Backup</h4>
<p>Copies only changes since last backup.</p>
<h4>Differential Backup</h4>
<p>Copies changes since last full backup.</p>
<h3>3. Recovery Methods</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Restore:</b> Recover from backup</li>
<li><b>Point-in-time recovery:</b> Restore to a specific time</li>
</ul>
<h3>4. Backup Strategies</h3>
<ul>
<li>Regular backups (daily/weekly)</li>
<li>Store backups offsite/cloud</li>
<li>Use multiple backup copies</li>
</ul>
<h3>5. The 3-2-1 Rule</h3>
<ul>
<li>3 copies of data</li>
<li>2 different storage types</li>
<li>1 offsite copy</li>
</ul>
<div class="example-box">
Laptop crash → restore from cloud backup → data recovered
</div>

<div class="keyfact">
 Backup + Recovery = Data survival strategy.
</div>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Backup & Recovery Strategies', 15),
  (2082, 2082, '<h2>Why Networks are Used</h2>
  <p>
    Computer networks are used to connect devices so that they can share resources, communicate,
    and exchange data efficiently.
  </p>
  <h3> Key Idea</h3>
  <p>
    Networks allow computers to work together, making systems faster, cheaper, and more efficient.
  </p>
  <h3> Reasons for Using Networks</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Resource Sharing:</b> Multiple users can share printers, software, and storage devices</li>
    <li><b>Data Sharing:</b> Files and databases can be accessed by many users</li>
    <li><b>Communication:</b> Enables email, video calls, and instant messaging</li>
    <li><b>Cost Efficiency:</b> Reduces cost by sharing hardware and software</li>
    <li><b>Backup & Reliability:</b> Central storage allows easy data backup and recovery</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> Importance of Networking</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Improves teamwork and collaboration</li>
    <li>Increases efficiency in organizations</li>
    <li>Reduces duplication of resources</li>
    <li>Supports global communication systems</li>
  </ul>
  <h3> Simple Explanation</h3>
  <p>
    Networks are like teamwork systems where many computers share tools and information instead of working alone.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Why Networks are Used', 15),
  (2083, 2083, '<h2>LAN, MAN, WAN, PAN Comparison</h2>
  <p>
    Computer networks are classified based on their geographical coverage and size.
    Each type serves different communication needs depending on distance and scale.
  </p>
  <h3> Key Idea</h3>
  <p>
    Networks are classified by how far devices are spread—from personal devices (PAN) to global networks (WAN).
  </p>
  <h3> Types of Networks</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>PAN (Personal Area Network):</b> Very small network for personal devices like Bluetooth connections</li>
    <li><b>LAN (Local Area Network):</b> Covers a small area like a school, office, or home</li>
    <li><b>MAN (Metropolitan Area Network):</b> Covers a city or large town</li>
    <li><b>WAN (Wide Area Network):</b> Covers large geographical areas including countries and continents</li>
  </ul>
  <h3> Comparison Summary</h3>
  <table>
    <tr>
      <th>Type</th>
      <th>Coverage</th>
      <th>Example</th>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>PAN</td>
      <td>Very small (personal)</td>
      <td>Bluetooth between phone and headset</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>LAN</td>
      <td>Small area</td>
      <td>School computer lab</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>MAN</td>
      <td>City/town</td>
      <td>City internet network</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>WAN</td>
      <td>Global</td>
      <td>Internet</td>
    </tr>
  </table>
  <h3> Importance of Network Types</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Helps organize communication systems</li>
    <li>Improves data sharing efficiency</li>
    <li>Determines network cost and design</li>
    <li>Supports different levels of connectivity</li>
  </ul>
  <h3> Simple Explanation</h3>
  <p>
    These networks are like circles of communication: PAN is your personal circle,
    LAN is your building, MAN is your city, and WAN is the whole world.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for LAN, MAN, WAN, PAN Comparison', 15),
  (2084, 2084, '<h2>Network Topologies</h2>
  <p>
    Network topology refers to the physical or logical arrangement of computers and devices in a network.
    It shows how devices are connected and how data flows between them.
  </p>
  <h3> Key Idea</h3>
  <p>
    Network topology determines how computers are arranged and how they communicate in a network.
  </p>
  <h3> Types of Network Topologies</h3>
  <h3>1. Bus Topology</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>All devices are connected to a single backbone cable</li>
    <li>Data travels in both directions along the cable</li>
    <li>Easy to install but performance reduces with many devices</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>2. Star Topology</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>All devices connect to a central device (hub or switch)</li>
    <li>Most commonly used in schools and offices</li>
    <li>If one device fails, others are not affected</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>3. Ring Topology</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Devices are connected in a circular format</li>
    <li>Data travels in one direction or both directions</li>
    <li>A failure in one device can affect the entire network</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>4. Mesh Topology</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Every device is connected to every other device</li>
    <li>Highly reliable and fault-tolerant</li>
    <li>Expensive and complex to install</li>
  </ul>
  <h3> Comparison Summary</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Bus:</b> Simple but slow with many users</li>
    <li><b>Star:</b> Reliable and most commonly used</li>
    <li><b>Ring:</b> Moderate performance but less fault-tolerant</li>
    <li><b>Mesh:</b> Most reliable but expensive</li>
  </ul>
  <h3> Advantages of Topologies</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Help organize network structure</li>
    <li>Improve communication efficiency</li>
    <li>Allow easy troubleshooting depending on type</li>
  </ul>
  <h3> Simple Explanation</h3>
  <p>
    Network topology is like the layout of a city showing how roads (connections) link buildings (computers).
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Network Topologies', 15),
  (2085, 2085, '<h2>Transmission Media</h2>
  <p>
    Transmission media refers to the physical or wireless pathways through which data is transmitted
    from one device to another in a network.
  </p>
  <h3> Key Idea</h3>
  <p>
    Transmission media are the communication channels that allow data to travel between devices either through cables or wireless signals.
  </p>
  <h3> Types of Transmission Media</h3>
  <h3>1. Guided (Wired) Media</h3>
  <p>Data is transmitted through physical cables.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Twisted Pair Cable:</b> Common in telephone and Ethernet networks</li>
    <li><b>Coaxial Cable:</b> Used in cable TV and broadband</li>
    <li><b>Fiber Optic Cable:</b> Fastest transmission, uses light signals</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>2. Unguided (Wireless) Media</h3>
  <p>Data is transmitted through air without cables.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Radio Waves:</b> Used in Wi-Fi, Bluetooth, mobile phones</li>
    <li><b>Microwaves:</b> Require line-of-sight communication</li>
    <li><b>Satellites:</b> Provide global communication coverage</li>
  </ul>
  <h3> Comparison</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Wired:</b> More stable, faster, but less flexible</li>
    <li><b>Wireless:</b> Flexible and mobile, but may suffer interference</li>
  </ul>
  <h3> Importance of Transmission Media</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Enables data communication between devices</li>
    <li>Supports internet connectivity</li>
    <li>Allows sharing of resources</li>
    <li>Enables global communication systems</li>
  </ul>
  <h3> Simple Explanation</h3>
  <p>
    Transmission media are like roads or invisible air paths that allow data to travel between computers.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Transmission Media', 15),
  (2086, 2086, '<h2>IP Addressing</h2>
  <p>
    An IP (Internet Protocol) address is a unique numerical label assigned to each device connected to a network.
    It allows devices to identify and communicate with each other.
  </p>
  <h3> Key Idea</h3>
  <p>
    An IP address is like a digital home address that allows data to reach the correct device on a network.
  </p>

  <h3> Structure of IPv4</h3>
  <p>
    IPv4 is a 32-bit address divided into four parts called octets. Each octet is separated by a dot.
  </p>

  <div class="example-box">
    Example: 192.168.1.1
  </div>

  <ul>
    <li>Each part is called an <b>octet</b></li>
    <li>Each octet ranges from 0 to 255</li>
    <li>Total = 4 octets = 32 bits</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> Binary Representation</h3>
  <p>
    Each octet is made up of 8 binary digits (bits).
  </p>

  <pre>
192 → 11000000
168 → 10101000
1   → 00000001
1   → 00000001
  </pre>

  <h3> Types of IP Addresses</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Private IP:</b> Used inside local networks (e.g., home Wi-Fi, school lab)</li>
    <li><b>Public IP:</b> Used on the internet and assigned by ISP</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> Static vs Dynamic IP</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Static IP:</b> Does not change over time</li>
    <li><b>Dynamic IP:</b> Changes automatically using DHCP</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> Importance of IP Addressing</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Identifies devices on a network</li>
    <li>Ensures correct data delivery</li>
    <li>Enables internet communication</li>
    <li>Supports network organization</li>
  </ul>
  <h3> Simple Explanation</h3>
  <p>
    An IP address is like a house number for a device so that data knows exactly where to go.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for IP Addressing', 15),
  (2087, 2087, '<h2>DNS (Domain Name System)</h2>
  <p>
    DNS (Domain Name System) is a system that translates human-readable domain names
    (like google.com) into machine-readable IP addresses that computers use to identify each other on a network.
  </p>
  <h3> Key Idea</h3>
  <p>
    DNS acts like the internet’s phonebook by converting easy-to-remember names into IP addresses that computers understand.
  </p>

  <h3> Why DNS is Needed</h3>
  <p>
    Computers communicate using numbers (IP addresses), but humans prefer names.
    DNS bridges this gap.
  </p>

  <div class="example-box">
    google.com → 142.250.190.78<br>
    facebook.com → 157.240.229.35
  </div>

  <h3> How DNS Works (Step-by-Step)</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>User types a website name in the browser</li>
    <li>The request is sent to a DNS server</li>
    <li>The DNS server looks up the matching IP address</li>
    <li>The IP address is sent back to the browser</li>
    <li>The browser connects to the website server</li>
  </ol>

  <h3> Key Components</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Domain Name:</b> Human-friendly website name (e.g., google.com)</li>
    <li><b>IP Address:</b> Numerical address used by computers</li>
    <li><b>DNS Server:</b> Stores and manages domain-to-IP mappings</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> Importance of DNS</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Makes internet easier to use</li>
    <li>Eliminates need to memorize IP addresses</li>
    <li>Speeds up website access</li>
    <li>Supports global internet communication</li>
  </ul>
  <h3> Simple Explanation</h3>
  <p>
    DNS is like a contact list in your phone: you type a name, and it finds the number automatically.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for DNS (Domain Name System)', 15),
  (2088, 2088, '<h2>Client-Server Model</h2>
  <p>
    The client-server model is a network structure where computers are divided into two roles:
    clients that request services and servers that provide those services.
  </p>
  <h3> Key Idea</h3>
  <p>
    In a client-server network, one central system (server) provides resources while other computers (clients)
    request and use those resources.
  </p>

  <h3> Components</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Client:</b> A device or program that requests services (e.g., web browser)</li>
    <li><b>Server:</b> A powerful computer that provides services, data, or resources</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> How It Works</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>The client sends a request (e.g., opening a website)</li>
    <li>The server receives and processes the request</li>
    <li>The server sends back the required response/data</li>
  </ol>

  <h3> Example</h3>
  <div class="example-box">
    • A web browser (client) requests a webpage<br>
    • The web server processes the request<br>
    • The server sends the webpage back to the browser
  </div>

  <h3> Advantages of Client-Server Model</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Centralized control of data and resources</li>
    <li>Improved security and data protection</li>
    <li>Easier backup and maintenance</li>
    <li>Efficient resource sharing</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> Disadvantages</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Expensive to set up and maintain</li>
    <li>Depends heavily on the server (if it fails, network stops)</li>
    <li>Requires skilled administration</li>
  </ul>
  <h3> Simple Explanation</h3>
  <p>
    The client-server model is like ordering food in a restaurant:
    you (client) request, and the kitchen (server) prepares and serves your order.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Client-Server Model', 15),
  (2089, 2089, '<h2>Peer-to-Peer (P2P) Networks</h2>
  <p>
    A peer-to-peer (P2P) network is a type of network where all computers are equal.
    Each computer (node) can act as both a client (requesting data) and a server (providing data).
  </p>
  <h3> Key Idea</h3>
  <p>
    In a peer-to-peer network, there is no central server; all computers share resources directly with each other.
  </p>

  <h3> Characteristics of P2P Networks</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>No central server controlling the network</li>
    <li>All computers have equal status</li>
    <li>Each device can share files and resources</li>
    <li>Simple to install and manage</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> Example of P2P Network</h3>
  <div class="example-box">
    • Sharing files directly between two computers using a USB network connection or LAN without a server
  </div>

  <h3> Advantages of P2P Networks</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Easy to set up</li>
    <li>Low installation cost</li>
    <li>No need for a dedicated server</li>
    <li>Useful for small networks</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> Disadvantages of P2P Networks</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Less secure compared to client-server networks</li>
    <li>Difficult to manage in large networks</li>
    <li>No central backup system</li>
    <li>Performance may slow down with many users</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> Comparison: P2P vs Client-Server</h3>
  <table>
    <tr>
      <th>Feature</th>
      <th>P2P Network</th>
      <th>Client-Server Network</th>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Control</td>
      <td>Decentralized</td>
      <td>Centralized</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Security</td>
      <td>Lower</td>
      <td>Higher</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Cost</td>
      <td>Low</td>
      <td>High</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Setup</td>
      <td>Simple</td>
      <td>Complex</td>
    </tr>
  </table>
  <h3> Simple Explanation</h3>
  <p>
    A peer-to-peer network is like a group of friends sharing things directly with each other
    without a boss controlling everything.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Peer-to-Peer Networks', 15),
  (2090, 2090, '<h2>Network Troubleshooting Basics</h2>
  <p>
  Troubleshooting is the systematic process of identifying, diagnosing, and fixing problems in a computer network
  to ensure it operates efficiently and reliably.
</p>
<h3> Key Idea</h3>
<p>
  Network troubleshooting helps users detect, isolate, and resolve issues so that communication,
  devices, and internet services continue to function properly.
</p>
<h3> Common Network Problems</h3>
<ul>
  <li>No internet connection</li>
  <li>Slow network performance</li>
  <li>Device failing to connect to Wi-Fi or LAN</li>
  <li>Incorrect IP configuration</li>
  <li>DNS errors (website not loading)</li>
  <li>Intermittent connection drops</li>
</ul>
<h3> Basic Troubleshooting Steps</h3>
<ol>
  <li>Check physical connections (cables, routers, switches)</li>
  <li>Restart network devices (router, modem, computer)</li>
  <li>Verify network settings and IP configuration</li>
  <li>Test connectivity using tools like ping</li>
  <li>Check firewall or antivirus settings</li>
  <li>Reconnect to the network or Wi-Fi</li>
  <li>Update or reinstall network drivers</li>
</ol>
<h3> Troubleshooting Tools</h3>
<pre>
 Ping        → Tests if a device is reachable on a network
 Traceroute  → Shows the path data takes to reach a destination
 IPConfig     → Displays IP address and network configuration
 DNS Lookup   → Checks domain name resolution
</pre>
<h3> Advanced Troubleshooting Techniques</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Checking bandwidth usage to detect congestion</li>
  <li>Using network analyzers to monitor traffic</li>
  <li>Testing with different devices to isolate faults</li>
  <li>Changing DNS servers to fix browsing issues</li>
</ul>
<h3> Importance of Troubleshooting</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Restores network connectivity quickly</li>
  <li>Reduces downtime in communication and work</li>
  <li>Helps identify faulty hardware or software</li>
  <li>Improves overall network performance</li>
  <li>Saves time and repair costs</li>
</ul>
<h3> Real-Life Example</h3>
<p>
  When a school computer lab loses internet access, technicians may restart the router, check cables,
  and use the ping command to identify where the connection is failing.
</p>
<h3> Simple Explanation</h3>
<p>
  Troubleshooting is like being a network doctor who diagnoses and fixes problems when computers,
  networks, or internet connections stop working properly.
</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Network Troubleshooting Basics', 15),
  (2091, 2091, '<h2>Data Communication Concepts</h2>
  <p>
  Data communication refers to the exchange of digital data between two or more devices
  through a transmission medium such as cables, fiber optics, or wireless signals.
</p>
<h3> Key Idea</h3>
<p>
  Data communication is the process of sending and receiving digital information between devices
  using agreed rules (protocols) and communication technologies.
</p>
<h3> Key Components of Data Communication</h3>
<pre>
 Sender          → Device that sends data
 Receiver        → Device that receives data
 Message         → The data being transmitted (text, images, video, etc.)
 Transmission Medium → Path used to carry data (cables or wireless signals)
 Protocol        → Rules that control data communication
</pre>
<h3> Key Concepts</h3>
<pre>
 Bandwidth   → Amount of data transmitted per second (speed of connection)
 Protocols   → Rules that ensure proper communication between devices (e.g., TCP/IP, HTTP)
 Modem       → Converts digital signals for transmission over communication lines
 Router      → Directs data between different networks
</pre>
<h3> Explanation of Concepts</h3>
<ul>
  <li><b>Bandwidth:</b> Determines how much data can be transferred in a given time (higher bandwidth = faster communication)</li>
  <li><b>Protocols:</b> Set rules that ensure devices understand each other during communication</li>
  <li><b>Modem:</b> Converts digital signals to analog and back to enable internet connection</li>
  <li><b>Router:</b> Directs data packets between networks such as home and the internet</li>
</ul>
<h3> Modes of Data Communication</h3>
<ul>
  <li><b>Simplex:</b> Data flows in one direction only (e.g., TV broadcast)</li>
  <li><b>Half Duplex:</b> Data flows in both directions, but one at a time (e.g., walkie-talkie)</li>
  <li><b>Full Duplex:</b> Data flows in both directions simultaneously (e.g., phone call)</li>
</ul>
<h3> Importance of Data Communication</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Enables internet communication and connectivity</li>
  <li>Allows sharing of files and information globally</li>
  <li>Supports online learning, banking, and business operations</li>
  <li>Improves speed and efficiency of communication</li>
</ul>
<h3> Real-Life Example</h3>
<p>
  When you send a WhatsApp message, your phone acts as the sender, the internet is the transmission medium,
  and the recipient’s phone is the receiver. Protocols ensure the message is delivered correctly.
</p>
<h3> Simple Explanation</h3>
<p>
  Data communication is like a digital conversation between devices where messages are sent,
  received, and understood using rules (protocols) and speed (bandwidth).
</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Data Communication Concepts', 15),
  (2092, 2092, '<h2>Network Security</h2>
  <p>
  Network security refers to the protection of computer networks, systems, and data
  from unauthorized access, damage, theft, or cyber attacks such as hacking, malware, sniffing, and spoofing.
</p>

<h3> Key Idea</h3>
<p>
  Network security ensures that only authorized users can access systems and data,
  while preventing, detecting, and responding to cyber threats.
</p>
<h3> Types of Network Security Threats</h3>
<pre>
 Malware       → Harmful software (viruses, worms, ransomware)
 Phishing      → Fake messages that trick users into revealing information
‍ Hacking       → Unauthorized access to systems or data
 Eavesdropping → Interception of network data during transmission
 Sniffing      → Capturing and monitoring network data packets secretly
 Spoofing      → Pretending to be a trusted device or user to gain access
 Denial of Service (DoS) → Overloading a system to make it unavailable
</pre>
<h3> Methods of Network Security</h3>
<pre>
 Firewall        → Blocks unauthorized access to a network
 Encryption      → Converts data into unreadable code
 Authentication  → Verifies user identity (passwords, biometrics, 2FA)
 Antivirus       → Detects and removes malicious software
 Access Control  → Restricts user permissions and privileges
 Monitoring      → Tracks network activity for suspicious behavior
</pre>
<h3> Explanation of Key Methods</h3>
<ul>
  <li><b>Firewall:</b> Filters incoming and outgoing network traffic to block threats</li>
  <li><b>Encryption:</b> Converts data into a secret code that can only be read with a key</li>
  <li><b>Authentication:</b> Confirms user identity before granting access</li>
  <li><b>Antivirus:</b> Scans and removes malicious programs from a computer</li>
  <li><b>Access Control:</b> Limits what users can access in a network system</li>
</ul>
<h3> Sniffing vs Spoofing</h3>
<ul>  <li><b>Sniffing:</b> Secretly capturing and analyzing data traveling across a network without permission</li>
  <li><b>Spoofing:</b> Pretending to be a trusted device, user, or website to trick systems into granting access</li>
</ul>
<h3> Importance of Network Security</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Protects sensitive and confidential information</li>
  <li>Prevents hacking, sniffing, and spoofing attacks</li>
  <li>Ensures privacy of users and organizations</li>
  <li>Maintains system reliability and trust</li>
  <li>Protects financial and personal data</li>
</ul>
<h3> Simple Explanation</h3>
<p>
  Network security is like a smart security system that uses locks, guards, and cameras
  to stop intruders, detect spies, and prevent people from pretending to be someone else.
</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Network Security', 15),
  (2093, 2093, '<h2>Networking Hardware</h2>
  <p>
  Networking hardware refers to physical devices used to connect computers together
  so that they can communicate and share resources such as files, printers, and internet access.
</p>
<h3> Key Idea</h3>
<p>
  Networking hardware acts as the communication bridge between computers,
  controlling how data is sent, received, and directed across a network efficiently and securely.
</p>
<h3> Types of Networking Hardware</h3>
<pre>
 NIC (Network Interface Card) → Connects a computer to a network
 Hub                         → Sends data to all connected devices
 Switch                      → Sends data only to the correct device
 Router                      → Connects different networks and provides internet access
 Bridge                      → Connects two similar networks and filters traffic
 Brouter                     → Combines the functions of a bridge and a router
 Access Point               → Allows wireless devices to connect to a network
 Modem                       → Converts signals for internet communication
</pre>
<h3> Functions of Networking Devices</h3>
<ul>
  <li><b>NIC:</b> Allows a computer to connect to a network and communicate with other devices</li>
  <li><b>Hub:</b> Broadcasts data to all connected devices (simple but inefficient)</li>
  <li><b>Switch:</b> Sends data only to the intended device using MAC addresses (efficient)</li>
  <li><b>Router:</b> Connects different networks and directs data between them (e.g., LAN to internet)</li>
  <li><b>Bridge:</b> Connects two separate but similar networks and reduces traffic by filtering data</li>
  <li><b>Brouter:</b> Combines the functions of a bridge and a router; routes some data and bridges others</li>
  <li><b>Modem:</b> Converts digital signals to allow internet communication</li>
  <li><b>Access Point:</b> Provides wireless connectivity for Wi-Fi devices</li>
</ul>
<h3> Key Differences</h3>
<ul>
  <li><b>Hub:</b> Sends data to all devices (no filtering)</li>
  <li><b>Switch:</b> Sends data only to the correct device (uses MAC address)</li>
  <li><b>Bridge:</b> Connects two LANs and filters traffic between them</li>
  <li><b>Router:</b> Connects different networks and routes data using IP addresses</li>
  <li><b>Brouter:</b> Works as both a bridge and a router depending on the data type</li>
</ul>
<h3> Advantages of Networking Hardware</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Enables sharing of files and resources</li>
  <li>Allows multiple devices to access the internet</li>
  <li>Improves communication between computers</li>
  <li>Reduces cost through shared hardware like printers</li>
</ul>
<h3> Simple Explanation</h3>
<p>
  Networking hardware is like a system of roads and traffic controllers that helps computers
  send, receive, and manage information across different networks efficiently.
</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Networking Hardware', 15),
  (2094, 2094, '<h2>Word Processing </h2>
 <p>
  Word processing refers to the use of computer software to create, edit, format, store,
  and print text-based documents such as letters, reports, CVs, essays, and official documents.
</p>
<h3> Key Idea</h3>
<p>
  Word processing software allows users to produce well-structured and professional documents quickly,
  with the ability to edit, correct, and improve content easily without rewriting everything.
</p>
<h3> Examples of Word Processing Software</h3>
<pre>
 Microsoft Word        → Most widely used word processor
 Google Docs          → Online collaborative document editor
 LibreOffice Writer   → Free offline word processing software
 WPS Writer           → Lightweight and easy-to-use word processor
</pre>
<h3> Key Features of Word Processing</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Typing, editing, and deleting text</li>
  <li>Text formatting (bold, italics, underline, font size, color)</li>
  <li>Page layout control (margins, orientation, spacing)</li>
  <li>Spell check and grammar correction tools</li>
  <li>Insertion of images, tables, shapes, and charts</li>
  <li>Find and replace text</li>
  <li>Saving and exporting files (DOCX, PDF, TXT)</li>
  <li>Printing documents professionally</li>
</ul>
<h3> Keyboard Shortcuts in Word Processing</h3>
<pre>
Ctrl + C   → Copy selected text
Ctrl + V   → Paste copied text
Ctrl + X   → Cut selected text
Ctrl + Z   → Undo last action
Ctrl + Y   → Redo last action
Ctrl + B   → Make text Bold
Ctrl + I   → Make text Italic
Ctrl + U   → Underline text
Ctrl + A   → Select all text in the document
Ctrl + S   → Save the document
Ctrl + P   → Print document
Ctrl + F   → Find a word or phrase in the document
Ctrl + H   → Find and replace text
Ctrl + N   → Create a new document
Ctrl + O   → Open an existing document
Ctrl + L   → Align text to the left
Ctrl + E   → Center align text
Ctrl + R   → Align text to the right
Ctrl + J   → Justify text (align both sides)
</pre>
<h3> Advanced Features</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Track changes for editing review</li>
  <li>Comments for collaboration</li>
  <li>Templates for CVs, letters, and reports</li>
  <li>Auto-save and cloud storage integration</li>
  <li>Mail merge for sending bulk personalized documents</li>
</ul>

<h3> Advantages of Word Processing</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Saves time compared to handwriting</li>
  <li>Allows easy editing without rewriting the whole document</li>
  <li>Produces neat, professional, and readable documents</li>
  <li>Enables easy sharing via email or cloud storage</li>
  <li>Reduces paper usage through digital storage</li>
</ul>

<h3> Disadvantages</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Requires electricity and a computer/device</li>
  <li>Can be affected by software crashes or data loss</li>
  <li>Requires basic computer skills to use effectively</li>
  <li>May lead to over-reliance on auto-correction tools</li>
</ul>

<h3> Simple Explanation</h3>
<p>
  Word processing is like a powerful digital typewriter that allows you to write, edit, format,
  and improve documents easily and professionally using a computer.
</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Word Processing', 15),
  (2095, 2095, '<h2>Spreadsheets</h2>
  <p>
  Spreadsheets are application programs used to organize, store, and analyze data
  in rows and columns. They also allow automatic calculations using formulas and functions.
</p>
<h3> Key Idea</h3>
<p>
  A spreadsheet is like a digital worksheet where data is arranged in rows and columns,
  and calculations are performed automatically using formulas.
</p>
<h3> Examples of Spreadsheet Software</h3>
<pre>
 Microsoft Excel   → Most popular spreadsheet program
 Google Sheets     → Online spreadsheet application
 LibreOffice Calc  → Free spreadsheet software
 Apple Numbers     → Spreadsheet for Apple devices
</pre>
<h3> Structure of a Spreadsheet</h3>

<div style="background: linear-gradient(145deg, #131326, #1c1c36); border: 1px solid #323254; border-radius: 14px; padding: 16px; margin: 20px 0; box-shadow: 0 8px 25px rgba(0,0,0,0.45); text-align: center;">
  <img src="/images/spreadsheet_screenshot.png" alt="Real World Spreadsheet Interface Screenshot" style="width: 100%; max-width: 680px; height: auto; border-radius: 10px; border: 1px solid #323560; box-shadow: 0 4px 20px rgba(0,0,0,0.6); display: block; margin: 0 auto 10px auto; object-fit: contain;" />
  <p style="font-size:0.85em; color:#a0a0b0; text-align:center; margin-top:10px; margin-bottom:0;"><b>Figure:</b> Real World Spreadsheet Interface Screenshot (showing Formula Bar, Rows, Columns, and Data Cells)</p>
</div>

<ul>
  <li><b>Rows:</b> Horizontal lines identified by numbers (1, 2, 3...)</li>
  <li><b>Columns:</b> Vertical lines identified by letters (A, B, C...)</li>
  <li><b>Cells:</b> Intersection of a row and column (e.g. A1, B2)</li>
</ul>
<h3> Explanation of Spreadsheet Parts</h3>
<ul>
  <li><b>Rows:</b> Horizontal sections identified by numbers</li>
  <li><b>Columns:</b> Vertical sections identified by letters</li>
  <li><b>Cells:</b> Boxes where data is entered</li>
  <li><b>Cell Address:</b> Name of a cell formed using column letter and row number (e.g., B3)</li>
  <li><b>Formula Bar:</b> Area used to enter or edit formulas</li>
  <li><b>Worksheet:</b> One page inside a spreadsheet file</li>
</ul>
<h3> Data Types in Spreadsheets</h3>
<pre>
Text     → Names or words
Numbers  → Values used in calculations
Dates    → Calendar dates
Formulas → Instructions for calculations
</pre>
<h3> Key Features</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Organizing data into tables</li>
  <li>Automatic calculations using formulas</li>
  <li>Sorting data alphabetically or numerically</li>
  <li>Filtering important information</li>
  <li>Creating charts and graphs</li>
  <li>Data analysis and reporting tools</li>
</ul>
<h3> Important Formulas</h3>
<pre>
=SUM(A1:A10)         → Adds values in a range
=AVERAGE(A1:A10)     → Calculates the mean
=MAX(A1:A10)         → Finds highest value
=MIN(A1:A10)         → Finds lowest value
=COUNT(A1:A10)       → Counts numeric entries
=COUNTA(A1:A10)      → Counts all non-empty cells
=COUNTBLANK(A1:A10)  → Counts empty cells
=IF(A1>50,"Pass","Fail") → Logical decision test
=IFS(A1>80,"A",A1>60,"B",A1>40,"C") → Multiple conditions
=SUMIF(A1:A10,">50") → Adds values greater than 50
=COUNTIF(A1:A10,">50") → Counts values greater than 50
=AVERAGEIF(A1:A10,">50") → Average of values above condition
</pre>
<h3> Formula Example</h3>

<div style="background:#131326; border:1px solid #2a2a48; border-radius:12px; padding:16px; margin:20px 0; box-shadow: 0 4px 20px rgba(0,0,0,0.4);">
  <div style="display:flex; align-items:center; background:#1c1c36; padding:8px 12px; border-radius:8px; margin-bottom:12px; border:1px solid #323254; font-family:monospace; font-size:0.9em; color:#00e5ff;">
    <span style="color:#ff007f; font-weight:bold; margin-right:12px;">fx</span>
    <span style="color:#00ffcc; font-weight:bold;">=SUM(B1:B3) &nbsp;➔ &nbsp;Result: 240</span>
  </div>
  <div style="overflow-x:auto;">
    <table style="width:100%; border-collapse:collapse; text-align:center; font-family:''Segoe UI'', Roboto, sans-serif; font-size:0.9em; color:#e0e0e0;">
      <thead>
        <tr style="background:#22223e; color:#00e5ff;">
          <th style="border:1px solid #323254; padding:8px; width:40px; background:#16162d; color:#888;">#</th>
          <th style="border:1px solid #323254; padding:8px; min-width:100px;">A</th>
          <th style="border:1px solid #323254; padding:8px; min-width:100px;">B</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td style="border:1px solid #323254; padding:8px; background:#16162d; color:#888; font-weight:bold;">1</td>
          <td style="border:1px solid #323254; padding:8px;">Math</td>
          <td style="border:1px solid #323254; padding:8px; color:#ffdd57; font-weight:bold;">80</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td style="border:1px solid #323254; padding:8px; background:#16162d; color:#888; font-weight:bold;">2</td>
          <td style="border:1px solid #323254; padding:8px;">English</td>
          <td style="border:1px solid #323254; padding:8px; color:#ffdd57; font-weight:bold;">70</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td style="border:1px solid #323254; padding:8px; background:#16162d; color:#888; font-weight:bold;">3</td>
          <td style="border:1px solid #323254; padding:8px;">Science</td>
          <td style="border:1px solid #323254; padding:8px; color:#ffdd57; font-weight:bold;">90</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="background:rgba(0, 229, 255, 0.1);">
          <td style="border:1px solid #323254; padding:8px; background:#16162d; color:#888; font-weight:bold;">4</td>
          <td style="border:1px solid #323254; padding:8px; font-weight:bold; color:#00e5ff;">Total (=SUM)</td>
          <td style="border:1px solid #323254; padding:8px; font-weight:bold; color:#00ffcc; font-size:1.05em; border:2px solid #00ffcc;">240</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p style="font-size:0.85em; color:#a0a0b0; text-align:center; margin-top:10px; margin-bottom:0;"><b>Figure:</b> Spreadsheet SUM Formula Result Grid</p>
</div>
<h3> Uses of Spreadsheets</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Keeping school marks and records</li>
  <li>Business accounting and budgeting</li>
  <li>Creating timetables and schedules</li>
  <li>Data analysis and reporting</li>
  <li>Inventory and stock management</li>
</ul>
<h3> Advantages</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Saves time by automating calculations</li>
  <li>Reduces human errors in mathematics</li>
  <li>Makes data analysis easier</li>
  <li>Helps in decision making using charts</li>
  <li>Stores large amounts of information neatly</li>
</ul>
<h3> Disadvantages</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Incorrect formulas can produce wrong results</li>
  <li>Large spreadsheets may become difficult to manage</li>
  <li>Requires computer knowledge to use advanced features</li>
  <li>Data can be lost if not saved properly</li>
</ul>
<h3> Simple Explanation</h3>
<p>
  A spreadsheet is like a smart digital table that stores information
  and performs calculations automatically when data is entered.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Spreadsheets', 15),
  (2096, 2096, '<h2>Presentations</h2>
  <p>
    Presentation software is used to display information in an organized way using slides.
    It helps users communicate ideas visually using text, images, audio, and video.
  </p>
  <h3> Key Idea</h3>
  <p>
    Presentations are used to communicate information clearly to an audience using visual slides
    instead of long written documents.
  </p>
  <h3> Examples of Presentation Software</h3>
  <div class="example-box">
    1. Microsoft PowerPoint – most popular slide software<br>
    2. Google Slides – online presentation tool<br>
    3. LibreOffice Impress – free presentation software<br>
    4. Canva – design-based presentation tool
  </div>
  <h3> Key Features of Presentation Software</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Creation of multiple slides</li>
    <li>Insertion of text, images, audio, and video</li>
    <li>Slide transitions and animations</li>
    <li>Pre-designed templates and themes</li>
    <li>Presenter notes and slideshow mode</li>
  </ul>
  <h3> Advantages</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Makes information easier to understand</li>
    <li>Improves audience engagement</li>
    <li>Helps organize ideas clearly</li>
    <li>Supports visual learning</li>
  </ul>
  <h3> Simple Explanation</h3>
  <p>
    Presentation software is like a digital flipbook where each page (slide) shows a piece of information
    to help explain a topic clearly.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Presentations', 15),
  (2097, 2097, '<h2>Email & Communication</h2>
  <p>
  Email is a method of sending digital messages over the internet.
  It is one of the most common forms of electronic communication used in education,
  business, and personal life.
</p>
<h3> Key Idea</h3>
<p>
  Email allows users to send and receive messages instantly over the internet,
  including text, images, documents, videos, and links.
</p>
<h3> Examples of Email Services</h3>
<pre>
 Gmail       → Google email service
 Outlook     → Microsoft email service
 Yahoo Mail  → Yahoo communication service
 ProtonMail  → Secure encrypted email service
</pre>
<h3> Features of Email</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Send and receive messages instantly</li>
  <li>Attach files such as documents, images, and videos</li>
  <li>Send emails to multiple recipients using CC and BCC</li>
  <li>Spam and junk mail filtering</li>
  <li>Organizing emails into folders and labels</li>
  <li>Access emails from phones, tablets, and computers</li>
</ul>
<h3> Structure of an Email Address</h3>

<div style="background:#131326; border:1px solid #2a2a48; border-radius:12px; padding:18px; margin:20px 0; text-align:center; box-shadow: 0 4px 20px rgba(0,0,0,0.4);">
  <div style="display:inline-block; background:#1c1c36; border:1px solid #00e5ff; padding:12px 24px; border-radius:30px; font-family:monospace; font-size:1.2em; letter-spacing:1px; margin-bottom:15px;">
    <span style="color:#ff007f; font-weight:bold;">student</span><span style="color:#ffdd57; font-weight:bold;">@</span><span style="color:#00e5ff; font-weight:bold;">gmail</span><span style="color:#00ffcc; font-weight:bold;">.com</span>
  </div>
  <div style="display:flex; justify-content:center; gap:15px; flex-wrap:wrap; font-size:0.85em; margin-top:10px;">
    <div style="background:#241933; border:1px solid #ff007f; padding:8px 12px; border-radius:8px; color:#ff80bf;"><b>Username:</b> student</div>
    <div style="background:#2d291e; border:1px solid #ffdd57; padding:8px 12px; border-radius:8px; color:#ffeb80;"><b>Separator:</b> @</div>
    <div style="background:#1a2838; border:1px solid #00e5ff; padding:8px 12px; border-radius:8px; color:#80f0ff;"><b>Domain Name:</b> gmail</div>
    <div style="background:#19332b; border:1px solid #00ffcc; padding:8px 12px; border-radius:8px; color:#80ffdf;"><b>Extension:</b> .com</div>
  </div>
</div>
<h3> Explanation of Parts</h3>
<ul>
  <li><b>Username:</b> The unique name of the email owner</li>
  <li><b>@ Symbol:</b> Separates the username from the mail service provider</li>
  <li><b>Domain Name:</b> The email service provider such as Gmail or Yahoo</li>
  <li><b>Domain Extension:</b> The ending of the domain such as .com, .org, or .edu</li>
</ul>
<h3> Common Domain Extensions</h3>
<pre>
.com  → Commercial organizations
.org  → Organizations
.edu  → Educational institutions
.gov  → Government institutions
.net  → Network services
</pre>
<h3> Structure of an Email</h3>
<ul>
  <li><b>To:</b> Recipient''s email address</li>
  <li><b>CC:</b> Sends a copy to another recipient</li>
  <li><b>BCC:</b> Sends a hidden copy to another recipient</li>
  <li><b>Subject:</b> The topic of the message</li>
  <li><b>Body:</b> The main message content</li>
  <li><b>Attachments:</b> Files added to the email</li>
</ul>
<h3> Advantages of Email</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Fast communication across the world</li>
  <li>Low cost compared to traditional mail</li>
  <li>Allows sending files easily</li>
  <li>Can be accessed anytime and anywhere</li>
  <li>Easy storage and searching of messages</li>
</ul>
<h3> Disadvantages of Email</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Spam or unwanted emails may occur</li>
  <li>Requires internet access</li>
  <li>Viruses can be spread through attachments</li>
  <li>Emails can be ignored or misunderstood</li>
</ul>
<h3> Simple Explanation</h3>
<p>
  Email is like a digital letter sent through the internet instead of paper and post offices.
  It allows people to communicate quickly from anywhere in the world.
</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Email & Communication', 15),
  (2098, 2098, '<h2>Collaboration Tools</h2>
  <p>
    Collaboration tools are digital software applications that allow two or more people to work together
    on tasks, projects, or communication in real time or asynchronously (not at the same time).
    They are widely used in schools, businesses, and remote teams.
  </p>
  <h3> Key Idea</h3>
  <p>
    The main purpose of collaboration tools is to improve teamwork by allowing users to share files,
    communicate instantly, edit documents together, and manage tasks efficiently.
  </p>
  <h3> Examples of Collaboration Tools</h3>
  <div class="example-box">
    1. Google Docs – real-time document editing<br>
    2. Google Drive – cloud file storage and sharing<br>
    3. Zoom – video conferencing and meetings<br>
    4. Microsoft Teams – chat, meetings, and teamwork platform<br>
    5. Slack – team messaging and communication
  </div>
  <h3> Features of Collaboration Tools</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Real-time editing of documents</li>
    <li>Video and audio conferencing</li>
    <li>File sharing and cloud storage</li>
    <li>Instant messaging and group chats</li>
    <li>Task assignment and project tracking</li>
  </ul>
  <h3> Advantages</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Improves teamwork and communication</li>
    <li>Saves time and increases productivity</li>
    <li>Allows remote work from anywhere</li>
    <li>Reduces need for physical meetings</li>
  </ul>
  <h3> Simple Explanation</h3>
  <p>
    Imagine a group of students writing one essay together at the same time even if they are in different homes.
    That is what collaboration tools make possible.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Collaboration Tools (Full Course)', 15),
  (2099, 2099, '<h2>Software Development Methodologies</h2>



<h3> DEEP EXPLANATION</h3>
<p>
Software development methodologies are structured approaches used to plan, design, develop, test, and maintain software systems.
They guide how teams organize work, manage time, and deliver reliable software.
</p>

---

<h3> 1. WATERFALL MODEL</h3>
<ul>
<li>Linear and sequential approach</li>
<li>Each phase must be completed before moving to the next</li>
<li>Clear structure and documentation</li>
</ul>

<h4> FLOW</h4>
<pre>
Requirements → Design → Implementation → Testing → Deployment → Maintenance
</pre>

<h4> ADVANTAGES</h4>
<ul>
<li>Easy to understand and manage</li>
<li>Clear stages and documentation</li>
</ul>

<h4> LIMITATIONS</h4>
<ul>
<li>Not flexible to changes</li>
<li>Errors discovered late</li>
</ul>

---

<h3> 2. AGILE MODEL</h3>
<ul>
<li>Iterative and flexible approach</li>
<li>Work divided into small cycles called <b>sprints</b></li>
<li>Continuous feedback and improvement</li>
</ul>

<h4> FLOW</h4>
<pre>
Plan → Develop → Test → Review → Improve (repeat)
</pre>

<h4> ADVANTAGES</h4>
<ul>
<li>Highly flexible</li>
<li>Early detection of issues</li>
<li>Customer involvement</li>
</ul>

<h4> LIMITATIONS</h4>
<ul>
<li>Less predictable timeline</li>
<li>Requires strong teamwork</li>
</ul>

---

<h3> 3. SPIRAL MODEL</h3>
<ul>
<li>Combines iterative development with risk analysis</li>
<li>Focuses on identifying and reducing risks early</li>
<li>Development occurs in repeated cycles (spirals)</li>
</ul>

<h4> FLOW</h4>
<pre>
Plan → Risk Analysis → Develop → Test → Repeat
</pre>

<h4> ADVANTAGES</h4>
<ul>
<li>Good for large, complex projects</li>
<li>Early risk detection</li>
</ul>

<h4> LIMITATIONS</h4>
<ul>
<li>Complex and expensive</li>
<li>Requires expertise</li>
</ul>

---

<h3> COMPARISON</h3>
<pre>
Waterfall        | Agile           | Spiral
----------------------------------------------
Linear           | Iterative       | Iterative + Risk
Rigid            | Flexible        | Flexible
Late testing     | Continuous      | Continuous
Low risk focus   | Moderate        | High risk focus
</pre>

---

<h3> REAL-WORLD USE CASES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Waterfall → Government systems (fixed requirements)</li>
<li>Agile → Mobile apps, startups</li>
<li>Spiral → Large banking or safety-critical systems</li>
</ul>

---

<div class="example-box">
Waterfall = Step-by-step <br>
Agile = Flexible cycles <br>
Spiral = Risk-focused cycles
</div>

<div class="keyfact">
 Agile is most used today because it adapts easily to changing requirements.
</div>

---

<h3> EXAM FOCUS</h3>
<ul>
<li>Differences between Agile, Waterfall, and Spiral</li>
<li>Advantages and disadvantages of each</li>
<li>When to use each model</li>
</ul>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Software Development Methodologies', 15),
  (2100, 2100, '<h2>Version Control Systems (Git & GitHub)</h2>



<h3> DEEP EXPLANATION</h3>
<p>
Version control is a system that tracks and manages changes to files (especially source code) over time.
It allows developers to collaborate, maintain history, and revert to previous versions when errors occur.
</p>

<p>
Without version control, managing large projects and teamwork becomes difficult and error-prone.
</p>

---

<h3> TYPES OF VERSION CONTROL</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Local VCS:</b> Tracks changes on a single computer</li>
<li><b>Centralized VCS:</b> Uses a central server (e.g., SVN)</li>
<li><b>Distributed VCS:</b> Every user has a full copy (e.g., Git)</li>
</ul>

---

<h3> GIT</h3>
<ul>
<li>Distributed version control system</li>
<li>Tracks file changes locally</li>
<li>Supports branching and merging</li>
<li>Works offline and online</li>
</ul>

<h4> BASIC GIT FLOW</h4>
<pre>
Write Code → Stage (git add) → Commit (git commit) → Repository
</pre>

---

<h3> GITHUB</h3>
<ul>
<li>Cloud-based hosting service for Git repositories</li>
<li>Enables collaboration among developers</li>
<li>Stores and shares code online</li>
<li>Supports features like pull requests and issue tracking</li>
</ul>

---

<h3> KEY TERMS</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Repository (Repo):</b> Project storage containing code and history</li>
<li><b>Commit:</b> Snapshot of changes saved in the repository</li>
<li><b>Branch:</b> Separate line of development</li>
<li><b>Merge:</b> Combining changes from different branches</li>
<li><b>Clone:</b> Copying a repository to a local machine</li>
<li><b>Pull:</b> Fetching updates from remote repository</li>
<li><b>Push:</b> Sending changes to remote repository</li>
</ul>

---

<h3> REAL-WORLD EXAMPLE</h3>
<ul>
<li>Developer A works on login feature (branch A)</li>
<li>Developer B works on dashboard (branch B)</li>
<li>Both changes are merged into main project</li>
</ul>

---

<h3> COMMON EXAM CONFUSION</h3>
<ul>
<li>Git = tool/software</li>
<li>GitHub = platform/service</li>
<li>Commit ≠ Save (it’s a tracked snapshot)</li>
</ul>

---

<div class="example-box">
Git = Tracks changes locally <br>
GitHub = Stores and shares code online
</div>

<div class="keyfact">
 Version control allows safe collaboration without overwriting each other''s work.
</div>

---

<h3> EXAM FOCUS</h3>
<ul>
<li>Difference between Git and GitHub</li>
<li>Meaning of key terms (commit, branch, repo)</li>
<li>Basic Git workflow</li>
<li>Advantages of version control</li>
</ul>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Version Control Systems', 15),
  (2101, 2101, '<h2>Software Testing Types</h2>

  


<h3> DEEP EXPLANATION</h3>
<p>
Software testing is the process of evaluating a software system to ensure it works correctly, meets requirements, and is free from defects (bugs).
</p>

<p>
Testing is done in stages, starting from small components to the entire system, ensuring reliability at every level.
</p>

---

<h3> TYPES OF TESTING</h3>

<ul>
<li><b>1. Unit Testing</b></li>
<ul>
<li>Tests individual components or functions</li>
<li>Performed by developers</li>
<li>Focus: smallest parts of the system</li>
</ul>

<li><b>2. Integration Testing</b></li>
<ul>
<li>Tests interaction between combined components</li>
<li>Checks data flow between modules</li>
</ul>

<li><b>3. System Testing</b></li>
<ul>
<li>Tests the complete and integrated system</li>
<li>Ensures system meets specified requirements</li>
</ul>

<li><b>4. User Acceptance Testing (UAT)</b></li>
<ul>
<li>Performed by end users or clients</li>
<li>Verifies system meets real-world needs</li>
<li>Final approval before release</li>
</ul>
</ul>

---

<h3> TESTING FLOW</h3>
<pre>
Unit → Integration → System → UAT
</pre>

---

<h3> REAL-WORLD EXAMPLE</h3>
<ul>
<li>Check login function → Unit testing</li>
<li>Login + database connection → Integration testing</li>
<li>Full application test → System testing</li>
<li>User tries app → UAT</li>
</ul>

---

<h3> COMMON EXAM TRAPS</h3>
<ul>
<li>Unit = smallest part (NOT full system)</li>
<li>UAT = done by users (NOT developers)</li>
<li>Integration = connection between modules</li>
</ul>

---

<div class="example-box">
Unit = Individual parts <br>
Integration = Parts working together <br>
System = Whole system <br>
UAT = User approval
</div>

<div class="keyfact">
 Bugs found early (unit stage) are cheaper to fix than bugs found later (system/UAT).
</div>

---

<h3> EXAM FOCUS</h3>
<ul>
<li>Order of testing stages</li>
<li>Purpose of each testing type</li>
<li>Who performs each test</li>
<li>Differences between stages</li>
</ul>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Software Testing Types', 15),
  (2102, 2102, '<h2>Documentation and Software Maintenance</h2>

  <h3>

<h3> DEEP EXPLANATION</h3>
<p>
Documentation and maintenance are critical phases of the software development lifecycle (SDLC) that occur after or alongside development.
They ensure that software remains understandable, usable, scalable, and functional over time.
</p>

<p>
Without proper documentation and maintenance, software becomes difficult to update, fix, or use effectively.
</p>

---

<h3> DOCUMENTATION</h3>
<p>
Documentation is the written or visual description of how a software system works and how it should be used or maintained.
</p>

<h4> TYPES OF DOCUMENTATION</h4>
<ul>
<li><b>User Documentation:</b> Guides end-users on how to operate the software (manuals, help guides)</li>
<li><b>Technical Documentation:</b> Describes system design, code structure, and architecture for developers</li>
<li><b>System Documentation:</b> Overall system overview including hardware, software, and processes</li>
</ul>

<h4> IMPORTANCE</h4>
<ul>
<li>Helps users understand how to use the system</li>
<li>Assists developers in maintaining and upgrading software</li>
<li>Improves collaboration in development teams</li>
</ul>

---

<h3> SOFTWARE MAINTENANCE</h3>
<p>
Software maintenance is the process of modifying and updating software after it has been deployed to fix issues, improve performance, or adapt to new requirements.
</p>

<h4> TYPES OF MAINTENANCE</h4>
<ul>
<li><b>Corrective Maintenance:</b> Fixing errors and bugs discovered after release</li>
<li><b>Adaptive Maintenance:</b> Updating software to work in new environments (e.g., OS updates)</li>
<li><b>Perfective Maintenance:</b> Enhancing features and improving performance</li>
<li><b>Preventive Maintenance:</b> Making changes to prevent future problems</li>
</ul>

---

<h3> REAL-WORLD EXAMPLES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Fixing a crash bug → Corrective</li>
<li>Updating app for new Android version → Adaptive</li>
<li>Improving app speed → Perfective</li>
<li>Refactoring code to avoid future bugs → Preventive</li>
</ul>

---

<div class="example-box">
Documentation = Understanding the system <br>
Maintenance = Improving and fixing the system
</div>

<div class="keyfact">
 Most software cost occurs after release due to maintenance, not development.
</div>

---

<h3> EXAM FOCUS</h3>
<ul>
<li>Types of documentation</li>
<li>Four types of maintenance</li>
<li>Differences between maintenance categories</li>
<li>Importance of documentation</li>
</ul>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Documentation and Maintenance', 15),
  (2103, 2103, '<h2>Malware (Malicious Software)</h2>

<h3> DEEP EXPLANATION</h3>
<p>
Malware refers to any software intentionally designed to harm, exploit, or disrupt computer systems, networks, or data.
It can steal information, damage files, spy on users, or take control of systems without permission.
</p>

<p>
Malware spreads through infected files, email attachments, downloads, or network vulnerabilities.
</p>

---

<h3> TYPES OF MALWARE</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Virus</b>: Attaches to files and spreads when the file is executed</li>
<li><b>Worm</b>: Self-replicates and spreads automatically across networks</li>
<li><b>Trojan Horse</b>: Disguised as legitimate software to trick users</li>
<li><b>Ransomware</b>: Locks or encrypts data and demands payment</li>
<li><b>Spyware</b>: Secretly monitors user activity and collects data</li>
<li><b>Adware</b>: Displays unwanted advertisements</li>
</ul>

---

<h3> REAL EXAMPLES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Opening an infected email attachment → Virus spreads</li>
<li>Network vulnerability exploited → Worm spreads automatically</li>
<li>Fake game download → Trojan installs malware</li>
<li>Files encrypted → Ransomware demands payment</li>
<li>Background tracking → Spyware steals information</li>
</ul>

---

<h3> EFFECTS OF MALWARE</h3>
<ul>
<li>Data loss or corruption</li>
<li>Unauthorized access to systems</li>
<li>Slower computer performance</li>
<li>Financial loss (ransomware, fraud)</li>
<li>Privacy breaches</li>
</ul>

---

<h3> PREVENTION METHODS</h3>
<ul>
<li>Install and update antivirus software</li>
<li>Avoid suspicious downloads and links</li>
<li>Keep operating systems updated</li>
<li>Use firewalls</li>
<li>Scan external devices (USBs)</li>
</ul>

---

<div class="example-box">
Virus = Needs user action <br>
Worm = Spreads automatically <br>
Trojan = Disguised as safe software <br>
Ransomware = Locks data for money
</div>

<div class="keyfact">
 Malware spreads mainly through user mistakes and unsecured systems.
</div>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Malware and Its Types', 15),
  (2104, 2104, '<h2>Phishing and Social Engineering</h2>

<h3> DEEP EXPLANATION</h3>
<p>
Phishing and social engineering are cyberattack techniques that exploit human psychology rather than technical weaknesses.
Instead of hacking systems directly, attackers trick users into revealing sensitive information such as passwords, bank details, or personal data.
</p>

<p>
These attacks rely on trust, urgency, fear, or curiosity to manipulate victims.
</p>

---

<h3> TYPES OF ATTACKS</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Phishing:</b> Fake emails/websites pretending to be legitimate sources</li>
<li><b>Spear Phishing:</b> Targeted phishing aimed at specific individuals</li>
<li><b>Smishing:</b> Phishing via SMS messages</li>
<li><b>Vishing:</b> Voice calls pretending to be trusted organizations</li>
<li><b>Social Engineering:</b> Psychological manipulation to gain access or data</li>
</ul>

---

<h3> REAL EXAMPLES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Email pretending to be a bank asking for login details</li>
<li>Fake website that looks identical to a real login page</li>
<li>Urgent message: "Your account will be locked—click here!"</li>
<li>Phone call pretending to be IT support requesting passwords</li>
</ul>

---

<h3> WARNING SIGNS</h3>
<ul>
<li>Urgent or threatening language</li>
<li>Suspicious links or email addresses</li>
<li>Requests for sensitive information</li>
<li>Poor grammar or unusual formatting</li>
</ul>

---

<h3> PREVENTION METHODS</h3>
<ul>
<li>Verify sender identity before responding</li>
<li>Do not click suspicious links</li>
<li>Use multi-factor authentication (MFA)</li>
<li>Check website URLs carefully</li>
<li>Never share passwords or confidential data</li>
</ul>

---

<div class="example-box">
Phishing = Fake message tricking you <br>
Social Engineering = Manipulating your thinking
</div>

<div class="keyfact">
 Most cyberattacks succeed because of human error, not system weakness.
</div>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Phishing and Social Engineering', 15),
  (2105, 2105, '<h2>Password Security</h2>
  <h3> DEEP EXPLANATION</h3>
  <p>
    Passwords are the first line of defense for protecting digital accounts. Weak passwords can be easily guessed,
    cracked, or stolen using automated hacking tools, while strong passwords significantly reduce security risks.
  </p>
  <h3> BEST PRACTICES</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Use a mix of uppercase, lowercase, numbers, and symbols</li>
    <li>Use long passwords (at least 8–12 characters or more)</li>
    <li>Avoid personal details like names or birthdates</li>
    <li>Do not reuse passwords across different accounts</li>
    <li>Enable Two-Factor Authentication (2FA)</li>
    <li>Change passwords regularly</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> EXAMPLES</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Weak:</b> 123456</li>
    <li><b>Weak:</b> password</li>
    <li><b>Strong:</b> K9!mZ@82xP</li>
    <li><b>Strong:</b> Tr7#bQ91@Lm</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> COMMON PASSWORD ATTACKS</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Brute force attacks (trying many combinations)</li>
    <li>Phishing (tricking users into revealing passwords)</li>
    <li>Dictionary attacks (using common words)</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> WHAT IS 2FA?</h3>
  <p>
    Two-Factor Authentication (2FA) adds an extra layer of security by requiring a second verification step
    such as an SMS code, email code, or authentication app.
  </p>

  <h3> REAL-WORLD APPLICATION</h3>
  <p>
    Banks, email providers, and social media platforms require strong passwords and sometimes 2FA to protect user accounts
    and prevent unauthorized access.
  </p>

  <h3> ADVANTAGES</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Protects personal data</li>
    <li>Reduces risk of hacking</li>
    <li>Improves account security</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> EXAM FOCUS</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Characteristics of strong passwords</li>
    <li>Meaning of 2FA</li>
    <li>Examples of weak vs strong passwords</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="example-box">
    Strong Password = Hard to guess combination of letters, numbers, and symbols <br>
    2FA = Extra security step beyond password
  </div>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Password Security Practices', 15),
  (2106, 2106, '<h2>Data Privacy and Backup</h2>
  <h3> DEEP EXPLANATION</h3>
  <p>
    Data privacy refers to the protection of personal and sensitive information from unauthorized access, misuse, or exposure.
    Backup strategies involve creating copies of data so it can be restored in case of loss, corruption, or cyberattacks.
  </p>
  <h3> 3-2-1 BACKUP RULE</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>3 copies of data</b> (original + 2 backups)</li>
    <li><b>2 different storage types</b> (e.g., hard drive + cloud)</li>
    <li><b>1 off-site backup</b> (stored in a different location)</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> BACKUP METHODS</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Cloud storage (Google Drive, OneDrive)</li>
    <li>External hard drives</li>
    <li>USB flash drives</li>
    <li>Network backups in organizations</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> EXAMPLES</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Saving school work on laptop + Google Drive + USB</li>
    <li>Companies backing up databases to cloud servers</li>
    <li>Photographers storing images on multiple drives</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> DATA PRIVACY MEASURES</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Using strong passwords</li>
    <li>Encryption of sensitive data</li>
    <li>Two-factor authentication (2FA)</li>
    <li>Access control permissions</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> REAL-WORLD APPLICATION</h3>
  <p>
    Organizations use backup systems and privacy controls to protect customer data, prevent data breaches, and ensure business continuity after system failures or cyberattacks.
  </p>

  <h3> ADVANTAGES</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Prevents data loss</li>
    <li>Protects sensitive information</li>
    <li>Ensures recovery after attacks or failures</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> EXAM FOCUS</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>3-2-1 backup rule</li>
    <li>Importance of data privacy</li>
    <li>Examples of backup methods</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="example-box">
    Backup = Copy of data for recovery <br>
    Privacy = Protection of sensitive data
  </div>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Data Privacy and Backup Strategies', 15),
  (2107, 2107, '<h2>Internet vs World Wide Web (WWW)</h2>
  <h3> DEEP EXPLANATION</h3>
  <p>
    The Internet and the World Wide Web are closely related but not the same. The Internet is the global
    system of interconnected computers and networks, while the World Wide Web (WWW) is a service that runs
    on top of the Internet and provides access to web pages and websites.
  </p>
  <h3> DIFFERENCE</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Internet:</b> Global network infrastructure (hardware + connections)</li>
    <li><b>WWW:</b> Collection of web pages accessed through browsers</li>
    <li><b>Internet:</b> Includes email, FTP, VoIP, websites</li>
    <li><b>WWW:</b> Only web-based content (web pages)</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> EXAMPLES</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Internet:</b> Sending emails, video calls, file sharing</li>
    <li><b>WWW:</b> Visiting websites like Google, Wikipedia, YouTube</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> RELATIONSHIP</h3>
  <p>
    The WWW is a service that operates on the Internet. Without the Internet, the Web cannot function.
  </p>

  <h3> REAL-WORLD APPLICATION</h3>
  <p>
    When you use a browser to open websites, you are using the WWW, but the data is transmitted through the Internet infrastructure.
  </p>

  <h3> ADVANTAGES</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Internet enables global communication</li>
    <li>WWW provides easy access to information</li>
    <li>Together they support education, business, and entertainment</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> EXAM FOCUS</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Difference between Internet and WWW</li>
    <li>Definition of each</li>
    <li>Examples of Internet services vs Web services</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="example-box">
    Internet = Network infrastructure <br>
    WWW = Websites and web pages
  </div>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Internet vs World Wide Web', 15),
  (2108, 2108, '<h2>Web Browsers and Search Engines</h2>
  <h3> DEEP EXPLANATION</h3>
  <p>
    A web browser is a software application used to access, retrieve, and display web pages on the Internet.
    A search engine is an online system that helps users find information by searching indexed web pages.
  </p>
  <h3> DIFFERENCE</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Web Browser:</b> Used to display web pages (e.g., Google Chrome, Mozilla Firefox, Microsoft Edge)</li>
    <li><b>Search Engine:</b> Used to search for information (e.g., Google, Bing, Yahoo)</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> EXAMPLES</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Using Google Chrome to open YouTube</li>
    <li>Using Google Search to find “photosynthesis notes”</li>
    <li>Using Firefox to browse Wikipedia</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> HOW SEARCH ENGINES WORK</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>User enters a keyword or query</li>
    <li>Search engine scans indexed websites</li>
    <li>It ranks results based on relevance</li>
    <li>Displays a list of links to the user</li>
  </ol>

  <h3> REAL-WORLD APPLICATION</h3>
  <p>
    Web browsers allow access to online platforms like e-learning systems, while search engines help students and professionals find information quickly for research and learning.
  </p>

  <h3> ADVANTAGES</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Fast access to information</li>
    <li>Easy navigation of the Internet</li>
    <li>Supports education and research</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> EXAM FOCUS</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Difference between browser and search engine</li>
    <li>Examples of each</li>
    <li>Functions of search engines</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="example-box">
    Browser = Tool for viewing websites <br>
    Search Engine = Tool for finding websites
  </div>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Web Browsers and Search Engines', 15),
  (2109, 2109, '<h2>URLs and Domain Names</h2>
  <h3> DEEP EXPLANATION</h3>
  <p>
    A URL (Uniform Resource Locator) is the complete address used to locate resources on the Internet such as web pages,
    images, or files. A domain name is the human-readable name that represents an IP address and makes websites easier to access.
  </p>
  <h3> STRUCTURE OF A URL</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Protocol:</b> http or https (communication rules)</li>
    <li><b>Domain name:</b> Website name (e.g., google.com)</li>
    <li><b>Path:</b> Specific page or resource location</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> EXAMPLES</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>https://www.google.com</li>
    <li>https://example.com/page</li>
    <li>https://www.wikipedia.org/wiki/Internet</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> BREAKDOWN EXAMPLE</h3>
  <div class="example-box">
    https://www.google.com/search<br>
    Protocol → https<br>
    Domain → google.com<br>
    Path → /search
  </div>

  <h3> REAL-WORLD APPLICATION</h3>
  <p>
    Domain names are used instead of IP addresses because they are easier for humans to remember,
    while computers still use IP addresses internally to locate servers.
  </p>

  <h3> ADVANTAGES OF DOMAIN NAMES</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Easier to remember than IP addresses</li>
    <li>More user-friendly for navigation</li>
    <li>Can represent brands and organizations</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> EXAM FOCUS</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Definition of URL</li>
    <li>Parts of a URL</li>
    <li>Meaning of domain name</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="example-box">
    URL = Full web address <br>
    Domain name = Human-friendly website name
  </div>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for URLs and Domain Names', 15),
  (2110, 2110, '<h2>HTTP vs HTTPS</h2>
  <h3> DEEP EXPLANATION</h3>
  <p>
    HTTP (HyperText Transfer Protocol) is used to transfer web data between a browser and a web server.
    HTTPS is the secure version of HTTP that uses encryption to protect data during transmission.
  </p>
  <h3> DIFFERENCES</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>HTTP:</b> Not secure, data sent in plain text</li>
    <li><b>HTTPS:</b> Secure, data is encrypted</li>
    <li><b>HTTP:</b> Uses port 80</li>
    <li><b>HTTPS:</b> Uses port 443</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> EXAMPLES</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>http://example.com → insecure connection</li>
    <li>https://bank.com → secure connection</li>
    <li>https://shopping-site.com → encrypted payment data</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> How HTTPS Works</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Browser requests a secure connection</li>
    <li>Server sends SSL/TLS certificate</li>
    <li>Data is encrypted before transmission</li>
    <li>Only intended receiver can decrypt it</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> REAL-WORLD APPLICATION</h3>
  <p>
    HTTPS is used in online banking, e-commerce, email services, and any system that handles sensitive data like passwords and payments.
  </p>

  <h3> ADVANTAGES OF HTTPS</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Protects user data</li>
    <li>Prevents hacking and interception</li>
    <li>Builds trust in websites</li>
    <li>Required for secure transactions</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> EXAM FOCUS</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Difference between HTTP and HTTPS</li>
    <li>Meaning of encryption</li>
    <li>Importance of secure communication</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="example-box">
    HTTP = Not secure communication <br>
    HTTPS = Secure encrypted communication
  </div>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for HTTP vs HTTPS', 15),
  (2111, 2111, '<h2>Cookies and Tracking</h2>
  <p>
    Cookies are small text files stored on a user''s device by websites. They are used to remember user information
    such as login details, preferences, and browsing activity.
  </p>

  <p>
    Tracking refers to the process of monitoring user activity online, often using cookies or similar technologies.
  </p>
  <h3> Types of Cookies</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Session Cookies:</b> Temporary cookies deleted when the browser is closed</li>
    <li><b>Persistent Cookies:</b> Stored on the device for a longer period</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> How Cookies Work</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>User visits a website</li>
    <li>Website sends a cookie to the browser</li>
    <li>Browser stores the cookie</li>
    <li>Website retrieves it on next visit</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> Examples</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Keeping a user logged in on a website</li>
    <li>Saving items in an online shopping cart</li>
    <li>Showing personalized ads based on browsing history</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> Real-world Application</h3>
  <p>
    E-commerce websites use cookies to remember shopping cart items, while social media platforms use them to personalize content and ads.
  </p>

  <h3> Advantages of Cookies</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Improve user experience</li>
    <li>Save login information</li>
    <li>Personalize content</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> Risks of Cookies</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Privacy concerns</li>
    <li>Tracking user behavior without consent</li>
    <li>Possible misuse of personal data</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> Simple Explanation</h3>
  <p>
    Cookies are like a website’s memory — they help it remember who you are and what you like.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Cookies and Tracking', 15),
  (2112, 2112, '<h2>Basic HTML (HyperText Markup Language)</h2>
  <p>
    HTML is the standard markup language used to create and structure web pages.
    It uses tags to define elements such as headings, paragraphs, images, and links.
  </p>

  <h3> Key Idea</h3>
  <p>
    HTML provides the structure of a web page, while other technologies like CSS and JavaScript add style and interactivity.
  </p>

  <h3> Common HTML Tags</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>&lt;html&gt;</b> – Root element of a webpage</li>
    <li><b>&lt;head&gt;</b> – Contains metadata (title, links, etc.)</li>
    <li><b>&lt;body&gt;</b> – Contains visible content</li>
    <li><b>&lt;h1&gt; to &lt;h6&gt;</b> – Headings</li>
    <li><b>&lt;p&gt;</b> – Paragraph text</li>
    <li><b>&lt;a&gt;</b> – Hyperlinks</li>
    <li><b>&lt;img&gt;</b> – Images</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> Example</h3>
  <pre>
&lt;html&gt;
  &lt;head&gt;
    &lt;title&gt;My First Page&lt;/title&gt;
  &lt;/head&gt;

  &lt;body&gt;
    &lt;h1&gt;Hello World&lt;/h1&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;This is my first webpage&lt;/p&gt;
  &lt;/body&gt;
&lt;/html&gt;
  </pre>

  <h3> Real-world Application</h3>
  <p>
    HTML is used by web developers to create websites, online forms, blogs, and web applications.
  </p>

  <h3> Exam Focus</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Definition of HTML</li>
    <li>Structure of an HTML document</li>
    <li>Functions of basic tags</li>
    <li>Difference between head and body</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> Simple Explanation</h3>
  <p>
    HTML is like the skeleton of a website — it gives structure but not style.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Basic HTML', 15),
  (2113, 2113, '<h2>Data vs Information</h2>
  <p>
    Data refers to raw, unprocessed facts and figures that have no meaning on their own.
    Information is data that has been processed, organized, and given meaning for decision-making.
  </p>

  <h3> Key Idea</h3>
  <p>
    Data becomes information when it is processed, organized, or analyzed to give meaning.
  </p>

  <h3> Differences Between Data and Information</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Data:</b> Raw facts, unorganized, meaningless on its own</li>
    <li><b>Information:</b> Processed data, meaningful and useful</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> Examples</h3>

  <h4>Data Example</h4>
  <div class="example-box">
    50, 60, 70, 80, 90
  </div>

  <h4>Information Example</h4>
  <div class="example-box">
    Average score = 70<br>
    Highest score = 90<br>
    Performance: Good
  </div>

  <h3> Conversion Process</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Collect raw data</li>
    <li>Process (calculate, sort, analyze)</li>
    <li>Organize results</li>
    <li>Present meaningful information</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> Real-world Application</h3>
  <p>
    Schools collect student marks (data) and process them into grades and reports (information) used for decision-making.
  </p>

  <h3> Importance</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Helps in decision-making</li>
    <li>Makes raw facts meaningful</li>
    <li>Improves understanding of situations</li>
    <li>Supports analysis in business and education</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> Simple Explanation</h3>
  <p>
    Data is raw material, while information is the finished product after processing.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Data vs Information', 15),
  (2114, 2114, '<h2>Types of Information Systems</h2>

  <p>
    Information systems are organized systems that collect, process, store, and distribute data to support
    operations, management, and decision-making in an organization.
  </p>
  <h3> Types of Information Systems</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>1. TPS (Transaction Processing System):</b> Handles daily routine transactions</li>
    <li><b>2. MIS (Management Information System):</b> Provides summarized reports for managers</li>
    <li><b>3. DSS (Decision Support System):</b> Helps managers make complex decisions using data analysis</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> Detailed Explanation</h3>

  <h4>1. Transaction Processing System (TPS)</h4>
  <ul>
    <li>Handles everyday business transactions</li>
    <li>Operates in real-time</li>
    <li>High speed and accuracy required</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="example-box">
    Example: Supermarket billing system, ATM withdrawals, online purchases
  </div>

  <h4>2. Management Information System (MIS)</h4>
  <ul>
    <li>Converts data into reports for managers</li>
    <li>Used for planning and monitoring performance</li>
    <li>Works on summarized data</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="example-box">
    Example: Monthly sales reports, school performance reports
  </div>

  <h4>3. Decision Support System (DSS)</h4>
  <ul>
    <li>Helps in complex decision-making</li>
    <li>Uses models, simulations, and data analysis</li>
    <li>Supports strategic planning</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="example-box">
    Example: Investment analysis systems, weather forecasting systems
  </div>

  <h3> Comparison</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>TPS:</b> Handles daily operations</li>
    <li><b>MIS:</b> Produces reports for managers</li>
    <li><b>DSS:</b> Supports decision-making</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> Real-world Application</h3>
  <p>
    Banks use TPS for transactions, MIS for financial reports, and DSS for investment and risk analysis.
  </p>

  <h3> Importance of Information Systems</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Improves efficiency in organizations</li>
    <li>Supports decision-making</li>
    <li>Reduces human error</li>
    <li>Enhances data management</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> Simple Explanation</h3>
  <p>
    Information systems are tools that help organizations collect data, process it, and use it for decisions.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Types of Information Systems', 15),
  (2115, 2115, '<h2>Data Lifecycle</h2>

  <p>
    The data lifecycle refers to the stages that data passes through from its creation to its final disposal.
    It ensures that data is properly managed, protected, and used efficiently throughout its existence.
  </p>
  <h3> Stages of Data Lifecycle</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>1. Collection:</b> Gathering raw data from sources</li>
    <li><b>2. Processing:</b> Organizing and converting data into useful information</li>
    <li><b>3. Storage:</b> Saving data in databases or storage systems</li>
    <li><b>4. Usage:</b> Using data for decision-making and analysis</li>
    <li><b>5. Archiving:</b> Long-term storage of important data</li>
    <li><b>6. Deletion:</b> Removing unnecessary or outdated data</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> Real-life Example</h3>
  <div class="example-box">
    Student marks → Collected during exams → Processed into grades → Stored in school database → Used for reports → Archived → Old records deleted
  </div>

  <h3> Importance of Data Lifecycle</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Ensures efficient data management</li>
    <li>Improves data security and organization</li>
    <li>Reduces storage costs</li>
    <li>Helps in compliance with data regulations</li>
    <li>Improves decision-making accuracy</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> Simple Explanation</h3>
  <p>
    The data lifecycle is like the life of data — it is created, used, stored, and eventually deleted when no longer needed.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Data Lifecycle', 15),
  (2116, 2116, '<h2>Big Data</h2>
  <p>
    Big Data refers to extremely large and complex datasets that cannot be easily stored, processed, or analyzed
    using traditional data processing tools.
  </p>
  <p>
    It is used in modern systems where data is generated continuously from many sources such as social media,
    sensors, and online transactions.
  </p>
  <h3> Characteristics of Big Data (3Vs)</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Volume:</b> Very large amounts of data generated daily</li>
    <li><b>Velocity:</b> High speed at which data is produced and processed</li>
    <li><b>Variety:</b> Different types of data (text, images, videos, sensor data)</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> Additional Vs (Advanced Concept)</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Veracity:</b> Accuracy and reliability of data</li>
    <li><b>Value:</b> Usefulness of data for decision-making</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> Examples of Big Data Sources</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Social media platforms (Facebook, Instagram, TikTok)</li>
    <li>E-commerce websites (Amazon, Jumia)</li>
    <li>IoT devices (smart sensors, smartwatches)</li>
    <li>Banking transactions</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> Real-world Applications</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Predicting customer behavior in marketing</li>
    <li>Fraud detection in banking systems</li>
    <li>Traffic prediction in smart cities</li>
    <li>Healthcare data analysis</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> Importance of Big Data</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Helps organizations make better decisions</li>
    <li>Improves efficiency and performance</li>
    <li>Enables personalized services</li>
    <li>Supports innovation and research</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> Simple Explanation</h3>
  <p>
    Big Data is like a massive ocean of information that needs powerful tools to analyze and understand it.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Big Data Basics', 15),
  (2117, 2117, '<h2>Computer Ethics</h2>
  <p>
    Computer ethics refers to the moral principles and guidelines that govern the responsible use of computers,
    digital systems, and information technology.
  </p>
  <p>
    It helps users decide what is right and wrong when using technology, ensuring fairness, respect, and safety in the digital world.
  </p>
  <h3> Key Principles of Computer Ethics</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Privacy:</b> Respecting personal and confidential information</li>
    <li><b>Integrity:</b> Not altering or destroying data without permission</li>
    <li><b>Responsibility:</b> Using technology in a safe and lawful way</li>
    <li><b>Respect:</b> Avoiding harassment, cyberbullying, or harmful behavior</li>
    <li><b>Legal use:</b> Using licensed software and legal resources</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> Examples of Ethical Behavior</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Protecting passwords and personal accounts</li>
    <li>Respecting others'' digital privacy</li>
    <li>Using licensed software instead of pirated versions</li>
    <li>Citing sources when using online information</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> Examples of Unethical Behavior</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Hacking into someone’s account</li>
    <li>Sharing private information without consent</li>
    <li>Cyberbullying or online harassment</li>
    <li>Using pirated software</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> Real-world Application</h3>
  <p>
    Schools, workplaces, and governments use Acceptable Use Policies (AUPs) to guide users on ethical behavior when using computer systems.
  </p>

  <h3> Importance of Computer Ethics</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Promotes safe and responsible technology use</li>
    <li>Protects users from harm and exploitation</li>
    <li>Builds trust in digital systems</li>
    <li>Reduces cybercrime and misuse of technology</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> Simple Explanation</h3>
  <p>
    Computer ethics means using technology in a way that is right, fair, and does not harm others.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Computer Ethics', 15),
  (2118, 2118, '<h2>Cyber Laws</h2>
  <p>
    Cyber laws are legal rules and regulations that govern the use of computers, digital systems, and the Internet.
    They are designed to protect users, data, and systems from cybercrime and misuse.
  </p>
  <p>
    These laws define acceptable behavior online and provide punishment for illegal digital activities.
  </p>
  <h3> Key Idea</h3>
  <p>
    Cyber laws ensure safe, legal, and responsible use of digital technologies and protect individuals and organizations from cybercrime.
  </p>

  <h3> 1. Areas Covered by Cyber Laws</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Cybercrime:</b> Illegal activities such as hacking, phishing, and fraud</li>
    <li><b>Data Protection:</b> Safeguarding personal and sensitive information</li>
    <li><b>Online Transactions:</b> Regulating e-commerce and digital payments</li>
    <li><b>Intellectual Property:</b> Protecting digital content and software</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> 2. Examples of Cyber Laws in Action</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Punishment for hacking computer systems</li>
    <li>Laws protecting online banking and mobile money transactions</li>
    <li>Regulation of social media use and online communication</li>
    <li>Protection of personal data by organizations</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> 3. Examples of Cybercrime</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Hacking into computer systems</li>
    <li>Online fraud and phishing scams</li>
    <li>Identity theft</li>
    <li>Spreading malware or viruses</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> 4. Real-world Application</h3>
  <p>
    Governments and legal authorities enforce cyber laws to prosecute cybercriminals and protect individuals,
    businesses, and national security systems.
  </p>

  <h3> 5. Importance of Cyber Laws</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Protects users from cyber threats</li>
    <li>Ensures secure online transactions</li>
    <li>Prevents misuse of technology</li>
    <li>Maintains trust in digital systems</li>
    <li>Supports law enforcement in digital investigations</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> Simple Explanation</h3>
  <p>
    Cyber laws are like traffic rules for the internet — they control behavior and punish misuse.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Cyber Laws', 15),
  (2119, 2119, '<h2>Copyright and Piracy</h2>
  <p>
    Copyright is a legal right that protects the original work of creators such as software, music, books, images, and videos.
    It gives the creator exclusive rights to use, distribute, and sell their work.
  </p>
  <p>
    Piracy is the illegal copying, downloading, or distribution of copyrighted material without permission from the owner.
  </p>
  <h3> Key Idea</h3>
  <p>
    Copyright protects original work, while piracy violates those rights by unauthorized copying or distribution.
  </p>

  <h3> 1. Copyright</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Legal protection for original creators</li>
    <li>Gives ownership rights over creative work</li>
    <li>Requires permission for use or distribution</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> 2. Piracy</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Illegal copying or sharing of protected content</li>
    <li>Includes software, music, movies, and books</li>
    <li>Violates intellectual property laws</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> 3. Examples</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Legal:</b> Buying licensed Microsoft Office software</li>
    <li><b>Illegal:</b> Downloading cracked software or pirated movies</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> 4. Real-world Application</h3>
  <p>
    Companies use digital licensing systems and legal protections to prevent unauthorized copying and protect intellectual property.
  </p>

  <h3> 5. Importance of Copyright</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Protects creators’ rights</li>
    <li>Encourages innovation and creativity</li>
    <li>Ensures fair economic reward for work</li>
    <li>Reduces illegal distribution of content</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> Simple Explanation</h3>
  <p>
    Copyright means “this is mine legally,” while piracy means “taking what is not yours without permission.”
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Copyright and Piracy', 15),
  (2120, 2120, '<h2>Plagiarism</h2>
  <p>
    Plagiarism is the act of using another person’s work, ideas, or intellectual property without giving proper credit,
    and presenting it as your own.
  </p>
  <p>
    It is considered academic dishonesty and is punishable in schools, universities, and professional environments.
  </p>
  <h3> Key Idea</h3>
  <p>
    Plagiarism occurs when someone presents another person’s work or ideas as their own without acknowledgment.
  </p>

  <h3> 1. Forms of Plagiarism</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Direct copying:</b> Copying text word-for-word without citation</li>
    <li><b>Paraphrasing without credit:</b> Rewriting someone’s ideas without acknowledgment</li>
    <li><b>Self-plagiarism:</b> Reusing your own previous work without permission</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> 2. Examples of Plagiarism</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Copying an assignment from the internet and submitting it as your own</li>
    <li>Using a friend’s project without credit</li>
    <li>Copy-pasting content from books or websites without citation</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> 3. How to Avoid Plagiarism</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Always cite sources used in your work</li>
    <li>Use quotation marks for direct quotes</li>
    <li>Paraphrase properly and acknowledge authors</li>
    <li>Use plagiarism detection tools</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> 4. Real-world Application</h3>
  <p>
    Schools and universities use plagiarism detection software (e.g., Turnitin) to check originality of student work.
  </p>

  <h3> 5. Consequences of Plagiarism</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Loss of marks or academic penalties</li>
    <li>Disqualification from exams or courses</li>
    <li>Damage to academic reputation</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> Simple Explanation</h3>
  <p>
    Plagiarism is like stealing someone’s intellectual work and pretending it is yours.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Plagiarism', 15),
  (2121, 2121, '<h2>Digital Citizenship</h2>
  <p>
    Digital citizenship refers to the responsible, safe, and appropriate use of technology and digital platforms
    by individuals in an online environment.
  </p>
  <p>
    It involves understanding how to behave ethically, communicate respectfully, and protect oneself and others online.
  </p>
  <h3> Key Idea</h3>
  <p>
    Digital citizenship is about using technology responsibly while respecting others and protecting personal information.
  </p>

  <h3> 1. Elements of Digital Citizenship</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Digital Etiquette:</b> Proper and respectful behavior online</li>
    <li><b>Online Safety:</b> Protecting personal information and privacy</li>
    <li><b>Responsible Communication:</b> Sharing accurate and respectful information</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> 2. Examples of Good Digital Citizenship</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Respecting others on social media platforms</li>
    <li>Avoiding cyberbullying or harassment</li>
    <li>Protecting passwords and personal data</li>
    <li>Verifying information before sharing</li>
    <li>Using technology for positive learning purposes</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> 3. Bad Digital Behavior (to Avoid)</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Cyberbullying or spreading hate</li>
    <li>Sharing false information (fake news)</li>
    <li>Accessing unauthorized systems</li>
    <li>Sharing private information of others</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> 4. Real-world Application</h3>
  <p>
    Schools and organizations teach digital citizenship to ensure users behave responsibly in online platforms,
    especially social media and learning environments.
  </p>

  <h3> 5. Importance of Digital Citizenship</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Promotes safe internet usage</li>
    <li>Reduces cyberbullying cases</li>
    <li>Protects personal and sensitive data</li>
    <li>Encourages respectful communication</li>
    <li>Builds a positive online community</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> Simple Explanation</h3>
  <p>
    Digital citizenship means behaving online the same way you would behave responsibly in real life.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Digital Citizenship', 15),
  (2122, 2122, '<h2>Artificial Intelligence (AI Basics)</h2>
  <p>
    Artificial Intelligence (AI) is the ability of machines or computer systems to simulate human intelligence processes
    such as learning, reasoning, problem-solving, and decision-making.
  </p>
  <p>
    AI systems analyze data, recognize patterns, and make decisions or predictions without being explicitly programmed for every situation.
  </p>
  <h3> Key Idea</h3>
  <p>
    AI enables machines to think and act like humans by learning from data and improving over time.
  </p>

  <h3> 1. Key Concepts in AI</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Machine Learning:</b> Systems learn from data</li>
    <li><b>Natural Language Processing (NLP):</b> Understanding human language</li>
    <li><b>Computer Vision:</b> Interpreting images and videos</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> 2. Types of AI</h3>

  <h4> Narrow AI</h4>
  <p>
    Designed to perform a specific task only.
  </p>
  <div class="example-box">
    Example: Chatbots, face recognition systems, recommendation engines
  </div>

  <h4> General AI</h4>
  <p>
    Hypothetical AI that can perform any intellectual task like a human.
  </p>
  <div class="example-box">
    Still under research and not yet fully developed
  </div>

  <h3> 3. Examples of AI in Action</h3>
  <pre>
Phone Face Unlock → AI recognizes facial patterns
YouTube/Netflix → AI recommends videos based on behavior
  </pre>

  <h3> 4. Real-world Applications</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Virtual assistants (Siri, Google Assistant)</li>
    <li>Self-driving cars</li>
    <li>Fraud detection in banking systems</li>
    <li>Medical diagnosis and disease prediction</li>
    <li>Smart recommendation systems</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> 5. Advantages of AI</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Automates repetitive tasks</li>
    <li>Improves decision-making</li>
    <li>Works faster than humans in data analysis</li>
    <li>Reduces human error</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> Simple Explanation</h3>
  <p>
    AI is like giving computers a “brain” that allows them to learn, think, and make decisions like humans.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Artificial Intelligence (AI Basics)', 15),
  (2123, 2123, '<h2>Machine Learning (Introduction)</h2>
  <p>
    Machine Learning (ML) is a branch of Artificial Intelligence (AI) that enables systems to learn from data,
    identify patterns, and improve performance without being explicitly programmed.
  </p>
  <p>
    Instead of following fixed rules, ML systems learn from experience and make predictions or decisions based on data.
  </p>
  <h3> Key Idea</h3>
  <p>
    Machine Learning allows computers to learn patterns from data instead of being manually programmed for every task.
  </p>

  <h3> 1. Types of Machine Learning</h3>

  <h4> Supervised Learning</h4>
  <p>
    The system is trained using labeled data (input + correct output).
  </p>
  <div class="example-box">
    Example: Email spam detection (spam / not spam)
  </div>

  <h4> Unsupervised Learning</h4>
  <p>
    The system finds patterns in unlabeled data without given answers.
  </p>
  <div class="example-box">
    Example: Customer grouping in marketing
  </div>

  <h4> Reinforcement Learning</h4>
  <p>
    The system learns through rewards and penalties based on its actions.
  </p>
  <div class="example-box">
    Example: Game-playing AI improving through trial and error
  </div>

  <h3> 2. Example of Machine Learning</h3>
  <pre>
Email System → Learns spam patterns → Automatically filters unwanted emails
  </pre>

  <h3> 3. Real-world Applications</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Recommendation systems (Netflix, YouTube, TikTok)</li>
    <li>Spam email filtering systems</li>
    <li>Stock market prediction tools</li>
    <li>Speech recognition (voice assistants)</li>
    <li>Self-driving cars</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> 4. AI vs Machine Learning</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>AI:</b> Broad field of intelligent machines</li>
    <li><b>ML:</b> Subset of AI that learns from data</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> Simple Explanation</h3>
  <p>
    Machine Learning is like teaching a computer by giving it examples instead of writing step-by-step instructions.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Machine Learning (Introduction)', 15),
  (2124, 2124, '<h2>Cloud Computing</h2>
  <p>
    Cloud computing is the delivery of computing services such as storage, servers, databases, networking,
    and software over the internet instead of using local computer hardware.
  </p>
  <p>
    It allows users to access data, applications, and services from anywhere using an internet connection.
  </p>
  <h3> Key Idea</h3>
  <p>
    Cloud computing replaces local storage and processing with internet-based services that can be accessed anytime, anywhere.
  </p>

  <h3> 1. Types of Cloud Services</h3>

  <h4> IaaS (Infrastructure as a Service)</h4>
  <p>
    Provides virtual computing resources such as servers and storage.
  </p>
  <div class="example-box">
    Example: Amazon Web Services (AWS)
  </div>

  <h4> PaaS (Platform as a Service)</h4>
  <p>
    Provides platforms for developers to build and deploy applications.
  </p>
  <div class="example-box">
    Example: Google App Engine
  </div>

  <h4> SaaS (Software as a Service)</h4>
  <p>
    Provides ready-to-use software over the internet.
  </p>
  <div class="example-box">
    Example: Google Drive, Microsoft Office 365
  </div>

  <h3> 2. Example of Cloud Computing</h3>
  <pre>
Google Drive → Stores files online
Dropbox → Allows file access from anywhere
OneDrive → Cloud-based storage system
  </pre>

  <h3> 3. Real-world Applications</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Online storage services (Google Drive, Dropbox)</li>
    <li>Video streaming (YouTube, Netflix)</li>
    <li>Online collaboration tools (Google Docs, Teams)</li>
    <li>Business data backup systems</li>
    <li>Mobile app cloud syncing</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> 4. Advantages of Cloud Computing</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Access data from anywhere</li>
    <li>Reduces cost of hardware</li>
    <li>Automatic updates and maintenance</li>
    <li>Scalable storage and services</li>
    <li>Improved collaboration</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> Simple Explanation</h3>
  <p>
    Cloud computing is like renting a powerful computer over the internet instead of owning one physically.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Cloud Computing', 15),
  (2125, 2125, '<h2>Internet of Things (IoT)</h2>
  <p>
    The Internet of Things (IoT) refers to a network of physical devices that are connected to the internet
    and can collect, share, and exchange data automatically.
  </p>
  <p>
    These devices operate with minimal or no human intervention by communicating with each other through the internet.
  </p>
  <h3> Key Idea</h3>
  <p>
    IoT connects everyday objects to the internet, allowing them to collect and exchange data automatically.
  </p>

  <h3> 1. Characteristics of IoT</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Connectivity:</b> Devices are connected to the internet</li>
    <li><b>Automation:</b> Devices perform tasks automatically</li>
    <li><b>Real-time data:</b> Information is collected and processed instantly</li>
    <li><b>Interconnectivity:</b> Devices communicate with each other</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> 2. Example of IoT System</h3>
  <pre>
Smart Home System:
Phone → Controls lights → Adjusts temperature → Manages security system
  </pre>

  <h3> 3. IoT Devices</h3>
  <div class="example-box">
    • Smart thermostats<br>
    • Smart lights<br>
    • Smart watches<br>
    • Security cameras<br>
    • Smart fridges
  </div>

  <h3> 4. Real-world Applications</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Smart homes (automated lighting and heating)</li>
    <li>Wearable devices (fitness trackers, smartwatches)</li>
    <li>Smart cities (traffic control systems)</li>
    <li>Agriculture (soil moisture sensors)</li>
    <li>Healthcare monitoring systems</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> 5. Benefits of IoT</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Improves efficiency and automation</li>
    <li>Saves time and reduces human effort</li>
    <li>Enables remote monitoring and control</li>
    <li>Improves decision-making using real-time data</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> Simple Explanation</h3>
  <p>
    IoT is like giving everyday objects the ability to “talk” to each other and work automatically using the internet.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Internet of Things (IoT)', 15),
  (2126, 2126, '<h2>Blockchain Basics</h2>
  <p>
    Blockchain is a distributed digital ledger technology used to record transactions securely across multiple computers
    in a way that makes the records difficult to alter or tamper with.
  </p>
  <p>
    Each record (called a block) is linked to the previous block, forming a continuous chain of data known as a blockchain.
  </p>
  <h3> Key Idea</h3>
  <p>
    Blockchain is a secure system where data is stored in linked blocks across a distributed network, making it nearly impossible to alter.
  </p>
  <h3> 1. How Blockchain Works</h3>
  <pre>
Transaction → Verified by network → Added to a block → Block added to chain → Stored permanently
  </pre>

  <h3> 2. Key Features of Blockchain</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Decentralization:</b> No central authority controls the system</li>
    <li><b>Security:</b> Uses cryptography to protect data</li>
    <li><b>Transparency:</b> All transactions are visible to participants</li>
    <li><b>Immutability:</b> Data cannot be changed once recorded</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> 3. Structure of Blockchain</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Block:</b> Contains transaction data</li>
    <li><b>Chain:</b> Linked sequence of blocks</li>
    <li><b>Nodes:</b> Computers that maintain copies of the blockchain</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> 4. Real-world Applications</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Cryptocurrencies (Bitcoin, Ethereum)</li>
    <li>Secure financial transactions</li>
    <li>Supply chain tracking (food, goods, medicine)</li>
    <li>Digital identity verification</li>
    <li>Smart contracts</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> 5. Advantages of Blockchain</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>High security and data integrity</li>
    <li>Reduces fraud and corruption</li>
    <li>No need for intermediaries</li>
    <li>Transparent transaction history</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> Simple Explanation</h3>
  <p>
    Blockchain is like a digital notebook shared across many computers where every entry is locked, verified, and cannot be secretly changed.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Blockchain Basics', 15);

