<h2>Displacement</h2>

<p><b>One concept:</b> Displacement is the straight-line change in position from the starting point to the final point, together with its direction.</p>

<h3>Think about the starting and final positions</h3>

<p>
Suppose a student starts at point A and walks directly to point B.
If B is 500 m east of A, the student''s displacement is:
</p>

<p><b>500 m east</b></p>

<p>
The distance describes the path travelled. Displacement describes the
change from the <b>starting position</b> to the <b>final position</b>.
</p>

<h3>Example 1: Straight movement</h3>

<p>
A car moves 80 km east from town A to town B.
Find its displacement.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify the starting position.</p>

<p>
The car starts at town A.
</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Identify the final position.</p>

<p>
The car finishes at town B.
</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> Identify the direction.</p>

<p>
Town B is 80 km east of town A.
</p>

<p><b>Answer: Displacement = 80 km east.</b></p>

<h3>Example 2: Moving west</h3>

<p>
A cyclist moves 3 km west from point P to point Q.
Find the displacement.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> The cyclist starts at P.</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> The cyclist finishes at Q.</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> Q is 3 km west of P.</p>

<p><b>Answer: Displacement = 3 km west.</b></p>

<h3>Example 3: Returning to the starting point</h3>

<p>
A student walks 2 km east from a starting point and then walks 2 km
west back to the same starting point. Find the displacement.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify the starting position.</p>

<p>
The student starts at the original point.
</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Identify the final position.</p>

<p>
The student returns to the same point.
</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> Compare the starting and final positions.</p>

<p>
There is no change in position.
</p>

<p><b>Answer: Displacement = 0 km.</b></p>

<h3>Important idea</h3>

<p>
An object can travel a large distance and still have zero displacement
if it returns to its starting position.
</p>

<h3>Common mistake</h3>

<p>
Do not give displacement as just a number when direction is required.
Displacement has both a <b>magnitude</b> and a <b>direction</b>.
</p>
', 1),
  (9, 9, '
<h2>Distance and Displacement Compared</h2>

<p><b>One concept:</b> Distance measures the total path travelled, while displacement measures the change in position from start to finish.</p>

<h3>The key difference</h3>

<p>
Distance asks:
</p>

<p><b>How much path was travelled?</b></p>

<p>
Displacement asks:
</p>

<p><b>How far and in what direction is the final position from the starting position?</b></p>

<h3>Example 1: Moving in one direction</h3>

<p>
A car travels 100 km east without changing direction.
</p>

<p>
The total path travelled is 100 km.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
Distance = <b>100 km</b>
</p>

<p>
Displacement = <b>100 km east</b>
</p>

<p>
In this case, the magnitudes are equal because the car travelled in one
straight direction.
</p>

<h3>Example 2: Moving and returning</h3>

<p>
A person walks 4 km east and then 4 km west back to the starting point.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Find the distance.</p>

<p>
Distance = 4 + 4
</p>

<p>
Distance = <b>8 km</b>
</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Find the displacement.</p>

<p>
The person finishes at the starting position.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
Displacement = <b>0 km</b>
</p>

<h3>Example 3: Different distances but same displacement direction</h3>

<p>
A student walks 3 km east and then 2 km east.
Find the distance and displacement.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Find the distance.</p>

<p>
Distance = 3 + 2
</p>

<p>
Distance = <b>5 km</b>
</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Find the displacement.</p>

<p>
Both movements are east, so the student''s final position is 5 km east
of the starting point.
</p>

<p>
Displacement = <b>5 km east</b>
</p>

<h3>What should you remember?</h3>

<ul>
<li>Distance is based on the <b>whole path</b>.</li>
<li>Displacement is based on the <b>change from start to finish</b>.</li>
<li>Distance has no direction.</li>
<li>Displacement includes direction.</li>
<li>Distance is always greater than or equal to the magnitude of displacement.</li>
</ul>
