<h2>Acceleration</h2>

<p><b>One concept:</b> Acceleration is the rate at which velocity changes with time.</p>

<h3>What does acceleration mean?</h3>

<p>
An object is accelerating when its velocity changes.
This can happen when the object:
</p>

<ul>
<li>increases its speed,</li>
<li>decreases its speed, or</li>
<li>changes direction.</li>
</ul>

<p>
For the calculations in this screen, we focus on a change in velocity
along a straight line.
</p>

<p>
The formula is:
</p>

<p><b>Acceleration = Change in velocity ÷ Time</b></p>

<p>
Using symbols:
</p>

<p><b>a = (v - u) / t</b></p>

<p>
where:
</p>

<ul>
<li><b>u</b> = initial velocity</li>
<li><b>v</b> = final velocity</li>
<li><b>t</b> = time</li>
</ul>

<h3>Example 1: Increasing velocity</h3>

<p>
A car increases its velocity from 10 m/s to 20 m/s in 5 seconds.
Find its acceleration.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify the initial velocity.</p>

<p>
u = 10 m/s
</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Identify the final velocity.</p>

<p>
v = 20 m/s
</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> Identify the time.</p>

<p>
t = 5 s
</p>

<p><b>Step 4:</b> Find the change in velocity.</p>

<p>
v - u = 20 - 10 = 10 m/s
</p>

<p><b>Step 5:</b> Divide by time.</p>

<p>
a = 10 / 5
</p>

<p>
a = <b>2 m/s²</b>
</p>

<h3>Example 2</h3>

<p>
A bicycle increases its velocity from 5 m/s to 17 m/s in 4 seconds.
Find its acceleration.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b></p>

<p>
u = 5 m/s, v = 17 m/s, t = 4 s
</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Find the change in velocity.</p>

<p>
v - u = 17 - 5 = 12 m/s
</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b></p>

<p>
a = 12 / 4
</p>

<p>
a = <b>3 m/s²</b>
</p>

<h3>Example 3: A decrease in velocity</h3>

<p>
A car decreases its velocity from 25 m/s to 10 m/s in 5 seconds.
Find its acceleration.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b></p>

<p>
u = 25 m/s, v = 10 m/s, t = 5 s
</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b></p>

<p>
v - u = 10 - 25
</p>

<p>
v - u = <b>-15 m/s</b>
</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b></p>

<p>
a = -15 / 5
</p>

<p>
a = <b>-3 m/s²</b>
</p>

<p>
The negative sign tells us that the velocity is decreasing in the
chosen positive direction.
</p>

<h3>Common mistake</h3>

<p>
Do not calculate acceleration using final velocity ÷ time.
First find the <b>change in velocity</b>:
</p>

<p><b>Change in velocity = final velocity - initial velocity</b></p>
