<h2>Distance-Speed-Time Relationship</h2>

<p><b>One concept:</b> Distance, speed and time are related by the equation d = st.</p>

<h3>Build the relationship</h3>

<p>
We know that:
</p>

<p>
<b>Speed = Distance ÷ Time</b>
</p>

<p>
To make distance the subject, multiply both sides by time:
</p>

<p>
Speed × Time = Distance
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p><b>Distance = Speed × Time</b></p>

<p>
Using symbols:
</p>

<p><b>d = st</b></p>

<h3>Example 1</h3>

<p>
A car travels at 60 km/h for 3 hours. Find the distance travelled.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Write the formula.</p>

<p>
d = st
</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Substitute the values.</p>

<p>
d = 60 × 3
</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> Calculate.</p>

<p>
d = <b>180 km</b>
</p>

<h3>Example 2</h3>

<p>
A cyclist travels at 25 km/h for 4 hours. Find the distance.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b></p>

<p>
d = st
</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b></p>

<p>
d = 25 × 4
</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b></p>

<p>
d = <b>100 km</b>
</p>

<h3>Example 3</h3>

<p>
A bus travels at 80 km/h for 2.5 hours. Find the distance.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b></p>

<p>
d = st
</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b></p>

<p>
d = 80 × 2.5
</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b></p>

<p>
d = <b>200 km</b>
</p>

<h3>Why does multiplication give distance?</h3>

<p>
Suppose a car travels 60 km every hour.
</p>

<p>
After 1 hour:
</p>

<p>
60 km
</p>

<p>
After 2 hours:
</p>

<p>
60 + 60 = 120 km
</p>

<p>
After 3 hours:
</p>

<p>
60 + 60 + 60 = 180 km
</p>

<p>
Multiplication gives the same result:
</p>

<p>
60 × 3 = 180 km
</p>

<p>
That is why:
</p>

<p><b>Distance = Speed × Time</b></p>

<h3>Common mistake</h3>

<p>
Check the units before using d = st. If speed is in km/h, time must be
in hours.
</p>
