<h2>Finding Speed from Distance and Time</h2>

<p><b>One concept:</b> When distance and time are known, speed is found using s = d/t.</p>

<h3>Deriving the formula</h3>

<p>
Start with:
</p>

<p><b>d = st</b></p>

<p>
We want speed to stand alone.
</p>

<p>
Divide both sides by time:
</p>

<p>
d / t = st / t
</p>

<p>
Time cancels from the right-hand side:
</p>

<p>
<b>s = d/t</b>
</p>

<h3>Example 1</h3>

<p>
A car travels 240 km in 4 hours. Find its speed.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b></p>

<p>
s = d/t
</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b></p>

<p>
s = 240 / 4
</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b></p>

<p>
s = <b>60 km/h</b>
</p>

<h3>Example 2</h3>

<p>
A cyclist travels 150 km in 5 hours. Find the speed.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b></p>

<p>
s = d/t
</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b></p>

<p>
s = 150 / 5
</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b></p>

<p>
s = <b>30 km/h</b>
</p>

<h3>Example 3</h3>

<p>
A bus travels 315 km in 4.5 hours. Find its speed.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b></p>

<p>
s = d/t
</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b></p>

<p>
s = 315 / 4.5
</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b></p>

<p>
s = <b>70 km/h</b>
</p>

<h3>Why do we divide?</h3>

<p>
The total distance is divided into equal time intervals.
This tells us how much distance is covered during one unit of time.
That rate is the speed.
</p>

<h3>Common mistake</h3>

<p>
Make sure the distance and time units match the speed unit you want.
For example, kilometres divided by hours gives kilometres per hour.
</p>
