<h2>Finding an Unknown Time</h2>

<p>
Sometimes distance and speed are known, but the time is unknown.
</p>

<p>
Start with:
</p>

<p>
<b>d = st</b>
</p>

<p>
To make time the subject, divide both sides by speed:
</p>

<p>
<b>t = d / s</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
A car travels 240 km at 60 km/h. Find the time taken.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b></p>
<p>d = 240 km, s = 60 km/h</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b></p>
<p>t = d / s</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b></p>
<p>t = 240 / 60</p>

<p><b>Answer = 4 hours</b></p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
A bus travels 150 km at 50 km/h.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b></p>
<p>t = d / s</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b></p>
<p>t = 150 / 50</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b></p>
<p>t = <b>3 hours</b></p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
A cyclist travels 72 km at 24 km/h.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b></p>
<p>t = 72 / 24</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b></p>
<p>t = <b>3 hours</b></p>

<h3>Check</h3>

<p>
If your answer is correct, multiplying speed by time should give the original
distance.
</p>

<p>
For Example 1:
</p>

<p>
60 × 4 = 240 km
</p>

<hr>

<h2>Finding an Unknown Time</h2>

<p>
When distance and speed are known, time is found using:
</p>

<p>
<b>t = d/s</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
A car travels 240 km at 60 km/h.
</p>

<p>
t = 240/60
</p>

<p>
<b>t = 4 hours</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
A bus travels 350 km at 70 km/h.
</p>

<p>
t = 350/70
</p>

<p>
<b>t = 5 hours</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
A cyclist travels 90 km at 30 km/h.
</p>

<p>
t = 90/30
</p>

<p>
<b>t = 3 hours</b>
</p>

<h3>Why Division?</h3>

<p>
If a vehicle covers s kilometres every hour, dividing the total distance
by s tells us how many one-hour portions are needed.
</p>
', 1),
