<h2>Finding an Unknown Speed</h2>

<p>
When distance and time are known but speed is unknown, start with:
</p>

<p>
<b>d = st</b>
</p>

<p>
Divide both sides by time:
</p>

<p>
<b>s = d / t</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
A car travels 240 km in 4 hours. Find its speed.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b></p>
<p>d = 240 km, t = 4 h</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b></p>
<p>s = d / t</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b></p>
<p>s = 240 / 4 = <b>60 km/h</b></p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
A bus covers 180 km in 3 hours.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b></p>
<p>s = d / t</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b></p>
<p>s = 180 / 3</p>

<p><b>Answer = 60 km/h</b></p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
A cyclist covers 90 km in 3 hours.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b></p>
<p>s = 90 / 3</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b></p>
<p>s = <b>30 km/h</b></p>

<h3>Check</h3>

<p>
Multiply your answer by the time:
</p>

<p>
30 × 3 = 90 km
</p>

<p>
So the answer is consistent.
</p>
', 1),
