<h2>Finding an Unknown Distance</h2>

<p>
When speed and time are given, distance can be found directly using:
</p>

<p>
<b>d = st</b>
</p>

<p>
The units must agree. For example, km/h must be multiplied by hours to give
kilometres.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
A train travels at 80 km/h for 5 hours.
</p>

<p>
d = 80 × 5
</p>

<p>
<b>d = 400 km</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
A motorcycle travels at 45 km/h for 2 hours.
</p>

<p>
d = 45 × 2
</p>

<p>
<b>d = 90 km</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
A vehicle travels at 72 km/h for 2.5 hours.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b></p>
<p>d = st</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b></p>
<p>d = 72 × 2.5</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b></p>
<p>d = 180 km</p>

<h3>Check</h3>

<p>
A speed of 72 km/h means 72 km is covered every hour.
In 2.5 hours, 180 km is reasonable because:
</p>

<p>
72 × 2 = 144 km
</p>

<p>
72 × 0.5 = 36 km
</p>

<p>
144 + 36 = 180 km
</p>
