<h2>Finding Meeting Time</h2>

<p>
When two objects start a known distance apart and move <b>towards each other</b>,
their separation decreases at their relative speed.
</p>

<p>
For objects moving towards each other:
</p>

<p>
<b>Relative speed = speed 1 + speed 2</b>
</p>

<p>
Then:
</p>

<p>
<b>Time to meet = initial distance / relative speed</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
Two towns are 180 km apart. A car leaves one town at 60 km/h while another
leaves the other town at 30 km/h towards it. Find when they meet.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b></p>
<p>Relative speed = 60 + 30 = 90 km/h</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b></p>
<p>Time = 180 / 90</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b></p>
<p>Time = <b>2 hours</b></p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
Two cyclists are 100 km apart. They travel towards each other at 30 km/h
and 20 km/h.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b></p>
<p>Relative speed = 30 + 20 = 50 km/h</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b></p>
<p>Time = 100 / 50</p>

<p><b>Answer = 2 hours</b></p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
Two buses are 240 km apart and move towards each other at 80 km/h and 40 km/h.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b></p>
<p>Relative speed = 80 + 40 = 120 km/h</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b></p>
<p>Time = 240 / 120</p>

<p><b>Answer = 2 hours</b></p>

<h3>Key Structure</h3>

<p>
<b>Meeting time = distance between them ÷ sum of their speeds.</b>
</p>
', 1),
  (31, 31, '
<h2>Finding the Meeting Point</h2>

<p>
After finding the time at which two objects meet, we can find the distance
each object travelled.
</p>

<p>
Use:
</p>

<p>
<b>Distance = Speed × Time</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
Two cars are 180 km apart. One travels at 60 km/h and the other at 30 km/h
towards each other. Find the distance travelled by the first car before they meet.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Find relative speed.</p>
<p>60 + 30 = 90 km/h</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Find meeting time.</p>
<p>180 / 90 = 2 hours</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> Find distance travelled by the first car.</p>
<p>d = 60 × 2</p>

<p><b>Answer = 120 km</b></p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
Two cyclists are 100 km apart. They travel towards each other at 30 km/h
and 20 km/h. Find the distance travelled by the faster cyclist before meeting.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b></p>
<p>Relative speed = 30 + 20 = 50 km/h</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b></p>
<p>Meeting time = 100 / 50 = 2 h</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b></p>
<p>Distance travelled by faster cyclist = 30 × 2</p>

<p><b>Answer = 60 km</b></p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
Two buses are 240 km apart and move towards each other at 80 km/h and 40 km/h.
Find the distance travelled by the slower bus before they meet.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b></p>
<p>Relative speed = 80 + 40 = 120 km/h</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b></p>
<p>Meeting time = 240 / 120 = 2 h</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b></p>
<p>Distance by slower bus = 40 × 2</p>

<p><b>Answer = 80 km</b></p>

<hr>

<h2>Finding the Meeting Point</h2>

<p>
After finding the meeting time, the meeting position can be found by using:
</p>

<p>
<b>Distance travelled = speed × time</b>
</p>

<p>
The distance travelled by either object from its own starting point is its
distance to the meeting point.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
Two cars are 180 km apart. They travel towards each other at 60 km/h and
30 km/h and meet after 2 hours.
Find the distance travelled by the first car.
</p>

<p>
Distance = speed × time
</p>

<p>
d = 60 × 2
</p>

<p>
<b>d = 120 km</b>
</p>

<p>
Therefore the meeting point is 120 km from the first town.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
Two cyclists are 100 km apart. One travels at 20 km/h and the other at
30 km/h. They meet after 2 hours.
</p>

<p>
Distance travelled by first cyclist:
</p>

<p>
20 × 2 = <b>40 km</b>
</p>

<p>
So they meet 40 km from the first cyclist''s starting point.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
Two buses are 300 km apart. The first travels at 80 km/h and they meet
after 2 hours.
</p>

<p>
Meeting distance from the first bus:
</p>

<p>
80 × 2 = <b>160 km</b>
</p>
', 1),
