<h2>Overtaking a Slower Object</h2>

<p>
An overtaking problem occurs when a faster object follows a slower object
moving in the <b>same direction</b>.
</p>

<p>
The faster object closes the gap at:
</p>

<p>
<b>Relative speed = faster speed − slower speed</b>
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>Time to overtake = distance between objects ÷ relative speed</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
A car travelling at 80 km/h is 40 km behind a bus travelling at 60 km/h.
How long will the car take to catch the bus?
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b></p>
<p>Relative speed = 80 − 60 = 20 km/h</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b></p>
<p>Time = 40 / 20</p>

<p><b>Answer = 2 hours</b></p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
A cyclist travelling at 30 km/h is 15 km behind another cyclist travelling
at 25 km/h.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b></p>
<p>Relative speed = 30 − 25 = 5 km/h</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b></p>
<p>Time = 15 / 5</p>

<p><b>Answer = 3 hours</b></p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
A motorcycle travels at 90 km/h and is 20 km behind a car travelling at
70 km/h.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b></p>
<p>Relative speed = 90 − 70 = 20 km/h</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b></p>
<p>Time = 20 / 20</p>

<p><b>Answer = 1 hour</b></p>

<h3>Key Rule</h3>

<p>
<b>Overtaking → subtract speeds because both objects move in the same direction.</b>
</p>
