<h2>Finding Total Distance in a Multi-Stage Journey</h2>

<p>
When a journey has several stages, the total distance is the sum of the
distances travelled in all stages.
</p>

<p>
<b>Total distance = distance₁ + distance₂ + distance₃ + ...</b>
</p>

<p>
First calculate each stage using:
</p>

<p>
<b>d = st</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
A car travels at 60 km/h for 2 hours and then at 40 km/h for 1 hour.
Find the total distance.
</p>

<p><b>Stage 1:</b></p>
<p>d₁ = 60 × 2 = 120 km</p>

<p><b>Stage 2:</b></p>
<p>d₂ = 40 × 1 = 40 km</p>

<p><b>Total:</b></p>
<p>120 + 40 = <b>160 km</b></p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
A cyclist travels at 20 km/h for 3 hours and then at 15 km/h for 2 hours.
</p>

<p><b>Stage 1:</b></p>
<p>d₁ = 20 × 3 = 60 km</p>

<p><b>Stage 2:</b></p>
<p>d₂ = 15 × 2 = 30 km</p>

<p><b>Total:</b></p>
<p>60 + 30 = <b>90 km</b></p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
A bus travels at 80 km/h for 1 hour, 60 km/h for 2 hours and 40 km/h for
1.5 hours.
</p>

<p><b>Stage 1:</b></p>
<p>80 × 1 = 80 km</p>

<p><b>Stage 2:</b></p>
<p>60 × 2 = 120 km</p>

<p><b>Stage 3:</b></p>
<p>40 × 1.5 = 60 km</p>

<p><b>Total:</b></p>
<p>80 + 120 + 60 = <b>260 km</b></p>
', 1),
  (43, 43, '
<h2>Finding Total Time in a Multi-Stage Journey</h2>

<p>
When a journey has several stages, the total travelling time is the sum of
the time spent in each stage.
</p>

<p>
<b>Total time = time₁ + time₂ + time₃ + ...</b>
</p>

<p>
If distance and speed are known for a stage, use:
</p>

<p>
<b>t = d / s</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
A car travels 120 km at 60 km/h and then 80 km at 40 km/h.
Find the total travelling time.
</p>

<p><b>Stage 1:</b></p>
<p>t₁ = 120 / 60 = 2 hours</p>

<p><b>Stage 2:</b></p>
<p>t₂ = 80 / 40 = 2 hours</p>

<p><b>Total:</b></p>
<p>2 + 2 = <b>4 hours</b></p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
A cyclist travels 60 km at 20 km/h and then 45 km at 15 km/h.
</p>

<p><b>Stage 1:</b></p>
<p>t₁ = 60 / 20 = 3 hours</p>

<p><b>Stage 2:</b></p>
<p>t₂ = 45 / 15 = 3 hours</p>

<p><b>Total:</b></p>
<p>3 + 3 = <b>6 hours</b></p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
A bus travels 150 km at 75 km/h and then 100 km at 50 km/h.
</p>

<p><b>Stage 1:</b></p>
<p>t₁ = 150 / 75 = 2 hours</p>

<p><b>Stage 2:</b></p>
<p>t₂ = 100 / 50 = 2 hours</p>

<p><b>Total:</b></p>
<p>2 + 2 = <b>4 hours</b></p>

<h3>Important</h3>

<p>
Only add times after calculating the time for each separate stage.
</p>

<hr>

<h2>Finding Total Time in a Multi-Stage Journey</h2>

<p>
After finding the time for each stage, the total journey time is found by
adding the stage times.
</p>

<p>
<b>Total time = time₁ + time₂ + time₃ + ...</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
A car travels 120 km at 60 km/h and then 100 km at 50 km/h.
</p>

<p><b>Stage 1:</b></p>
<p>t₁ = 120/60 = 2 h</p>

<p><b>Stage 2:</b></p>
<p>t₂ = 100/50 = 2 h</p>

<p><b>Total:</b></p>
<p>2 + 2 = <b>4 hours</b></p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
A bus travels 180 km at 60 km/h and then 120 km at 40 km/h.
</p>

<p>
t₁ = 180/60 = 3 h
</p>

<p>
t₂ = 120/40 = 3 h
</p>

<p>
Total time = 3 + 3
</p>

<p>
<b>Total time = 6 hours</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
A cyclist travels 50 km at 25 km/h and then 30 km at 15 km/h.
</p>

<p>
First stage:
</p>

<p>
t₁ = 50/25 = 2 h
</p>

<p>
Second stage:
</p>

<p>
t₂ = 30/15 = 2 h
</p>

<p>
Total time = 2 + 2
</p>

<p>
<b>Total time = 4 hours</b>
</p>

<h3>Method</h3>

<ol>
<li>Find the time of each stage.</li>
<li>Add the times.</li>
</ol>
