<h2>Understanding ≥</h2>

<h3>What does ≥ mean?</h3>

<p>
The symbol:
</p>

<p>
<b>≥</b>
</p>

<p>
means <b>greater than or equal to</b>.
</p>

<p>
It allows values that are greater than the limit and also the limit
itself.
</p>

<h3>Example</h3>

<p>
Consider:
</p>

<p>
x ≥ 20
</p>

<p>
This allows:
</p>

<p>
x = 20
</p>

<p>
and values greater than 20, such as:
</p>

<p>
x = 25
</p>

<p>
But x = 15 is not allowed.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
Check x = 25 in:
</p>

<p>
x ≥ 20
</p>

<p>
Substitute:
</p>

<p>
25 ≥ 20
</p>

<p>
This is true.
</p>

<p>
Therefore x = 25 is allowed.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
Check x = 20:
</p>

<p>
20 ≥ 20
</p>

<p>
This is true because equality is included.
</p>

<p>
Therefore x = 20 is allowed.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
Check x = 17:
</p>

<p>
17 ≥ 20
</p>

<p>
This is false.
</p>

<p>
Therefore x = 17 is not allowed.
</p>

<h3>Key idea</h3>

<p>
The symbol ≥ means:
</p>

<p>
<b>the value cannot be below the limit.</b>
</p>
