<h2>Identifying Constraints in a Word Problem</h2>

<h3>The skill</h3>

<p>
Before writing equations or inequalities, we must identify the
conditions that restrict our choices.
</p>

<p>
These conditions are the <b>constraints</b>.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
A farmer has 40 hectares of land.
Maize uses x hectares and beans use y hectares.
</p>

<p>
The important restriction is:
</p>

<p>
The total land used cannot exceed 40 hectares.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>x + y ≤ 40</b>
</p>

<p>
The statement about 40 hectares is a constraint.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
A factory has 100 hours available for two production processes.
Process A uses x hours and Process B uses y hours.
</p>

<p>
The restriction is:
</p>

<p>
The total time cannot exceed 100 hours.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>x + y ≤ 100</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
A school must have at least 500 learners in two programmes.
Let x and y be the numbers of learners.
</p>

<p>
The restriction is:
</p>

<p>
The total number of learners must be at least 500.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>x + y ≥ 500</b>
</p>

<h3>What to look for</h3>

<p>
Words such as:
</p>

<ul>
  <li>at most</li>
  <li>no more than</li>
  <li>cannot exceed</li>
  <li>at least</li>
  <li>not less than</li>
  <li>must have</li>
</ul>

<p>
often indicate a restriction.
</p>

<p>
Do not write an inequality just because you see numbers.
First ask:
</p>

<p>
<b>"What condition is limiting the choice?"</b>
</p>
