<h2>Writing the Complete Set of Constraints</h2>

<h3>Why can there be more than one constraint?</h3>

<p>
A linear programming problem can have several restrictions at the same
time.
</p>

<p>
For example, a production problem may have:
</p>

<ul>
  <li>a limit on total production;</li>
  <li>a limit on available material;</li>
  <li>a limit on available time;</li>
  <li>non-negativity conditions.</li>
</ul>

<p>
Each condition must be represented separately.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
A farmer grows maize and beans.
</p>

<p>
Let:
</p>

<p>
x = hectares of maize
</p>

<p>
y = hectares of beans
</p>

<p>
The farmer has at most 20 hectares.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>x + y ≤ 20</b>
</p>

<p>
Because land amounts cannot be negative:
</p>

<p>
<b>x ≥ 0</b>
</p>

<p>
<b>y ≥ 0</b>
</p>

<p>
The complete set of constraints is:
</p>

<p>
<b>x + y ≤ 20</b>
</p>

<p>
<b>x ≥ 0</b>
</p>

<p>
<b>y ≥ 0</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
A workshop produces products A and B.
Let x and y be the quantities produced.
</p>

<p>
The workshop can produce at most 100 products.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>x + y ≤ 100</b>
</p>

<p>
Also:
</p>

<p>
<b>x ≥ 0</b>
</p>

<p>
<b>y ≥ 0</b>
</p>

<p>
These three inequalities describe the basic restrictions.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
A school has two programmes with x and y learners.
There must be at least 500 learners altogether.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>x + y ≥ 500</b>
</p>

<p>
The numbers of learners cannot be negative:
</p>

<p>
<b>x ≥ 0</b>
</p>

<p>
<b>y ≥ 0</b>
</p>

<p>
So the complete set is:
</p>

<p>
<b>x + y ≥ 500</b>
</p>

<p>
<b>x ≥ 0</b>
</p>

<p>
<b>y ≥ 0</b>
</p>

<h3>The key habit</h3>

<p>
Do not stop after finding the main resource constraint.
Always ask:
</p>

<p>
<b>"Are there other conditions that must also be true?"</b>
</p>

<p>
For ordinary quantities, non-negativity is normally included.
</p>
