<h2>Turning an Inequality into a Boundary Equation</h2>

<h3>Why do we need a boundary line?</h3>

<p>
Suppose we have the inequality:
</p>

<p>
<b>x + y ≤ 10</b>
</p>

<p>
This describes many possible pairs of values for x and y.
To draw its boundary on a graph, we first need the line that separates
the allowed region from the disallowed region.
</p>

<p>
The boundary occurs when the two sides are exactly equal.
</p>

<p>
So we replace:
</p>

<p>
≤
</p>

<p>
with:
</p>

<p>
=
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>x + y = 10</b>
</p>

<p>
This is the boundary equation.
</p>

<h3>Why replace ≤ with =?</h3>

<p>
The inequality:
</p>

<p>
x + y ≤ 10
</p>

<p>
allows values below 10 and also values exactly equal to 10.
</p>

<p>
The equation:
</p>

<p>
x + y = 10
</p>

<p>
picks out exactly the values on the limit.
</p>

<p>
That limit becomes the boundary line.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
Given:
</p>

<p>
2x + y ≤ 12
</p>

<p>
Replace ≤ with =:
</p>

<p>
<b>2x + y = 12</b>
</p>

<p>
Therefore the boundary equation is:
</p>

<p>
<b>2x + y = 12</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
Given:
</p>

<p>
x + 3y ≥ 15
</p>

<p>
The boundary occurs when:
</p>

<p>
x + 3y = 15
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>x + 3y = 15</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
Given:
</p>

<p>
3x + 2y ≤ 18
</p>

<p>
The boundary equation is:
</p>

<p>
<b>3x + 2y = 18</b>
</p>

<h3>Important rule</h3>

<p>
When drawing the boundary of a linear inequality, first temporarily
replace the inequality symbol with an equality sign.
</p>
