<h2>Finding the x-Intercept of a Boundary Line</h2>

<h3>What is the x-intercept?</h3>

<p>
The <b>x-intercept</b> is the point where a line crosses the x-axis.
</p>

<p>
Every point on the x-axis has:
</p>

<p>
<b>y = 0</b>
</p>

<p>
Therefore, to find the x-intercept, we set y equal to zero.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
Find the x-intercept of:
</p>

<p>
x + y = 10
</p>

<p>
Because we are on the x-axis:
</p>

<p>
y = 0
</p>

<p>
Substitute:
</p>

<p>
x + 0 = 10
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
x = 10
</p>

<p>
The x-intercept is:
</p>

<p>
<b>(10, 0)</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
Find the x-intercept of:
</p>

<p>
2x + y = 12
</p>

<p>
Set:
</p>

<p>
y = 0
</p>

<p>
Then:
</p>

<p>
2x + 0 = 12
</p>

<p>
2x = 12
</p>

<p>
x = 6
</p>

<p>
Therefore the x-intercept is:
</p>

<p>
<b>(6, 0)</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
Find the x-intercept of:
</p>

<p>
3x + 2y = 18
</p>

<p>
Set y = 0:
</p>

<p>
3x + 2(0) = 18
</p>

<p>
3x = 18
</p>

<p>
x = 6
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>(6, 0)</b>
</p>

<h3>Key rule</h3>

<p>
To find the x-intercept:
</p>

<p>
<b>Set y = 0.</b>
</p>
