<h2>Testing a Point in an Inequality</h2>

<h3>Why test a point?</h3>

<p>
A boundary line divides the graph into two sides.
We need a reliable way to determine which side satisfies the
inequality.
</p>

<p>
We do this by choosing a point and substituting its coordinates
into the inequality.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
Consider:
</p>

<p>
<b>x + y ≤ 10</b>
</p>

<p>
Choose the point:
</p>

<p>
<b>(0,0)</b>
</p>

<p>
Substitute x = 0 and y = 0:
</p>

<p>
0 + 0 ≤ 10
</p>

<p>
0 ≤ 10
</p>

<p>
This is true.
</p>

<p>
Therefore (0,0) lies in the region satisfying the inequality.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
Consider:
</p>

<p>
<b>2x + y ≥ 8</b>
</p>

<p>
Test (0,0):
</p>

<p>
2(0) + 0 ≥ 8
</p>

<p>
0 ≥ 8
</p>

<p>
This is false.
</p>

<p>
Therefore (0,0) is NOT in the required region.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
Consider:
</p>

<p>
<b>3x + y ≤ 12</b>
</p>

<p>
Test the point (2,3).
</p>

<p>
3(2) + 3 ≤ 12
</p>

<p>
6 + 3 ≤ 12
</p>

<p>
9 ≤ 12
</p>

<p>
This is true.
</p>

<p>
Therefore (2,3) satisfies the inequality.
</p>

<h3>The procedure</h3>

<ol>
  <li>Choose a point.</li>
  <li>Substitute its x-coordinate.</li>
  <li>Substitute its y-coordinate.</li>
  <li>Evaluate the inequality.</li>
  <li>If true, the point is in the solution region.</li>
</ol>
