<h2>Shading the Region for ≤</h2>

<h3>The skill</h3>

<p>
After drawing the boundary of an inequality, we must identify which
side of the line represents the solution.
</p>

<p>
For a ≤ inequality, we can use a test point.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
Consider:
</p>

<p>
<b>x + y ≤ 10</b>
</p>

<p>
The boundary is:
</p>

<p>
x + y = 10
</p>

<p>
Test (0,0):
</p>

<p>
0 + 0 ≤ 10
</p>

<p>
0 ≤ 10
</p>

<p>
True.
</p>

<p>
Therefore the side containing (0,0) is the solution region.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
Consider:
</p>

<p>
<b>2x + y ≤ 8</b>
</p>

<p>
Boundary:
</p>

<p>
2x + y = 8
</p>

<p>
Test (0,0):
</p>

<p>
2(0) + 0 ≤ 8
</p>

<p>
0 ≤ 8
</p>

<p>
True.
</p>

<p>
Therefore shade the side containing (0,0).
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
Consider:
</p>

<p>
<b>3x + 2y ≤ 12</b>
</p>

<p>
Test (0,0):
</p>

<p>
3(0) + 2(0) ≤ 12
</p>

<p>
0 ≤ 12
</p>

<p>
True.
</p>

<p>
Therefore the region containing (0,0) satisfies the inequality.
</p>

<h3>Important</h3>

<p>
Do not memorise:
</p>

<p>
"Always shade below."
</p>

<p>
That shortcut can fail when the inequality is rearranged or the line
has a different form.
</p>

<p>
Instead:
</p>

<p>
<b>Test a point and let the inequality decide.</b>
</p>
