<h2>Identifying the Objective in a Word Problem</h2>

<p>
Before forming an objective function, first ask:
</p>

<p>
<b>"What quantity is the problem trying to make as large or as small as possible?"</b>
</p>

<p>
Words such as:
</p>

<ul>
<li>greatest profit</li>
<li>maximum revenue</li>
<li>largest number</li>
<li>minimum cost</li>
<li>lowest expenditure</li>
<li>least time</li>
</ul>

<p>
help identify the objective.
</p>

<h3>Example 1</h3>

<p>
A shop sells two products. The owner wants to obtain the
<b>greatest profit</b>.
</p>

<p>
The objective is therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>Profit</b>
</p>

<p>
The word <b>greatest</b> tells us that profit will be maximized.
</p>

<h3>Example 2</h3>

<p>
A factory uses two materials and wants to produce its goods at the
<b>lowest possible cost</b>.
</p>

<p>
The objective is:
</p>

<p>
<b>Cost</b>
</p>

<p>
Because the cost must be as small as possible, it will be minimized.
</p>

<h3>Example 3</h3>

<p>
A farmer produces maize and beans and wants to obtain the
<b>maximum revenue</b>.
</p>

<p>
The objective is:
</p>

<p>
<b>Revenue</b>
</p>

<p>
The word <b>maximum</b> means that revenue must be made as large
as possible.
</p>

<h3>Important distinction</h3>

<p>
The objective is not automatically "profit".
</p>

<p>
It could be:
</p>

<ul>
<li>profit</li>
<li>cost</li>
<li>revenue</li>
<li>production</li>
<li>time</li>
</ul>

<p>
The wording of the problem tells us which quantity is the objective.
</p>
