<h2>Translating Per-Unit Wording into Coefficients</h2>

<p>
A very important skill in linear programming is turning words into
numbers in an equation.
</p>

<p>
When a question says:
</p>

<p>
<b>"KSh 500 per item"</b>
</p>

<p>
the number <b>500</b> becomes the coefficient of the variable
representing the number of items.
</p>

<h3>Example 1</h3>

<p>
A company earns KSh 600 profit <b>per table</b> and KSh 400 profit
<b>per chair</b>.
</p>

<p>
Let:
</p>

<p>
x = tables<br>
y = chairs
</p>

<p>
Because each table gives KSh 600:
</p>

<p>
Table profit = 600x
</p>

<p>
Because each chair gives KSh 400:
</p>

<p>
Chair profit = 400y
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>P = 600x + 400y</b>
</p>

<h3>Example 2</h3>

<p>
A school pays KSh 50 for each notebook and KSh 200 for each textbook.
</p>

<p>
Let x = notebooks and y = textbooks.
</p>

<p>
Notebook cost = 50x
</p>

<p>
Textbook cost = 200y
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>C = 50x + 200y</b>
</p>

<h3>Example 3</h3>

<p>
A farmer sells one bag of maize for KSh 3,000 and one bag of beans
for KSh 2,500.
</p>

<p>
Let x = bags of maize and y = bags of beans.
</p>

<p>
Maize revenue = 3000x
</p>

<p>
Bean revenue = 2500y
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>R = 3000x + 2500y</b>
</p>

<h3>The key translation</h3>

<p>
<b>"per unit" → coefficient of the variable.</b>
