<h2>Evaluating an Objective Function at a Point</h2>

<p>
Once an objective function has been formed, we can calculate its value
for a particular point.
</p>

<p>
This means replacing x and y with the coordinates of the point.
</p>

<h3>Example 1</h3>

<p>
Given:
</p>

<p>
<b>P = 500x + 300y</b>
</p>

<p>
Find P at the point (4, 2).
</p>

<p>
The point (4, 2) means:
</p>

<p>
x = 4<br>
y = 2
</p>

<p>
Substitute:
</p>

<p>
P = 500(4) + 300(2)
</p>

<p>
P = 2000 + 600
</p>

<p>
<b>P = KSh 2,600</b>
</p>

<h3>Example 2</h3>

<p>
Given:
</p>

<p>
<b>C = 200x + 500y</b>
</p>

<p>
Find C at (3, 4).
</p>

<p>
x = 3 and y = 4.
</p>

<p>
C = 200(3) + 500(4)
</p>

<p>
C = 600 + 2000
</p>

<p>
<b>C = KSh 2,600</b>
</p>

<h3>Example 3</h3>

<p>
Given:
</p>

<p>
<b>R = 1000x + 1500y</b>
</p>

<p>
Find R at (5, 2).
</p>

<p>
R = 1000(5) + 1500(2)
</p>

<p>
R = 5000 + 3000
</p>

<p>
<b>R = KSh 8,000</b>
</p>

<h3>Important</h3>

<p>
A point such as (4,2) gives the values of the variables:
</p>

<p>
<b>x = 4 and y = 2</b>
</p>

<p>
Those values are then substituted into the objective function.
</p>
