  (134, 134, '
<h2>Comparing Objective Values at Two Points</h2>

<p>
Two feasible points can give different values of the same objective
function.
</p>

<p>
To compare them, calculate the objective value at each point.
</p>

<h3>Example 1</h3>

<p>
Suppose:
</p>

<p>
<b>P = 500x + 300y</b>
</p>

<p>
Compare A(4,2) and B(2,5).
</p>

<p>
For A:
</p>

<p>
P = 500(4) + 300(2)
</p>

<p>
P = 2000 + 600
</p>

<p>
<b>P = 2600</b>
</p>

<p>
For B:
</p>

<p>
P = 500(2) + 300(5)
</p>

<p>
P = 1000 + 1500
</p>

<p>
<b>P = 2500</b>
</p>

<p>
Therefore A gives the greater profit.
</p>

<h3>Example 2</h3>

<p>
C = 200x + 400y.
Compare (3,2) and (1,5).
</p>

<p>
At (3,2):
</p>

<p>
C = 200(3) + 400(2)
</p>

<p>
C = 600 + 800 = 1400
</p>

<p>
At (1,5):
</p>

<p>
C = 200(1) + 400(5)
</p>

<p>
C = 200 + 2000 = 2200
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>2200 > 1400</b>
</p>

<p>
So (1,5) gives the greater cost.
</p>

<h3>Example 3</h3>

<p>
R = 100x + 200y.
Compare (5,1) and (2,4).
</p>

<p>
At (5,1):
</p>

<p>
R = 100(5) + 200(1) = 500 + 200 = 700
</p>

<p>
At (2,4):
</p>

<p>
R = 100(2) + 200(4) = 200 + 800 = 1000
</p>

<p>
Therefore (2,4) gives the greater revenue.
</p>

<h3>Key idea</h3>

<p>
<b>Never decide which point is better just by looking at x or y.
Calculate the objective value.</b>
</p>
