<h2>Meaning of an Optimal Solution</h2>

<p>
An <b>optimal solution</b> is the feasible solution that gives the
best possible value of the objective function.
</p>

<p>
"Best" depends on the objective:
</p>

<ul>
<li>For maximum profit, the best solution gives the greatest profit.</li>
<li>For minimum cost, the best solution gives the smallest cost.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Example 1: Maximum Profit</h3>

<p>
Suppose three feasible production plans give:
</p>

<ul>
<li>A → KSh 4,000 profit</li>
<li>B → KSh 5,500 profit</li>
<li>C → KSh 4,800 profit</li>
</ul>

<p>
Because the objective is to <b>maximize profit</b>, compare the values:
</p>

<p>
4000, 5500, 4800
</p>

<p>
The greatest value is 5500.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>B is the optimal solution.</b>
</p>

<h3>Example 2: Minimum Cost</h3>

<p>
Three feasible plans have costs:
</p>

<ul>
<li>A → KSh 7,000</li>
<li>B → KSh 5,200</li>
<li>C → KSh 6,100</li>
</ul>

<p>
The objective is to minimize cost.
</p>

<p>
The smallest value is KSh 5,200.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>B is the optimal solution.</b>
</p>

<h3>Example 3</h3>

<p>
Suppose:
</p>

<p>
P = 300x + 500y
</p>

<p>
and the feasible points produce:
</p>

<p>
(2,3) → P = 2,100
</p>

<p>
(4,2) → P = 2,200
</p>

<p>
(1,4) → P = 2,300
</p>

<p>
For maximum profit, the greatest value is 2,300.
</p>

<p>
Therefore (1,4) is the optimal solution among the points considered.
</p>

<h3>Key idea</h3>

<p>
<b>Feasible</b> means the solution is allowed.
</p>

<p>
<b>Optimal</b> means it is the best among the allowed solutions
according to the objective.
</p>
