<h2>Complete Corner-Point Maximisation</h2>

<p>
We can now combine the skills learned so far.
</p>

<p>
The process is:
</p>

<ol>
<li>Identify the corner points of the feasible region.</li>
<li>Write the objective function.</li>
<li>Substitute each corner point.</li>
<li>Calculate every objective value.</li>
<li>Choose the greatest value.</li>
<li>State the optimal point and maximum value.</li>
</ol>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>
Suppose the feasible region has corner points:
</p>

<p>
(0,0), (6,0), (4,3), (0,5)
</p>

<p>
and the objective is:
</p>

<p>
<b>P = 500x + 400y</b>
</p>

<h3>Corner 1: (0,0)</h3>

<p>
P = 500(0) + 400(0)
</p>

<p>
P = 0
</p>

<h3>Corner 2: (6,0)</h3>

<p>
P = 500(6) + 400(0)
</p>

<p>
P = 3000
</p>

<h3>Corner 3: (4,3)</h3>

<p>
P = 500(4) + 400(3)
</p>

<p>
P = 2000 + 1200
</p>

<p>
P = 3200
</p>

<h3>Corner 4: (0,5)</h3>

<p>
P = 500(0) + 400(5)
</p>

<p>
P = 2000
</p>

<h3>Compare</h3>

<p>
The objective values are:
</p>

<ul>
<li>(0,0) → 0</li>
<li>(6,0) → 3000</li>
<li>(4,3) → 3200</li>
<li>(0,5) → 2000</li>
</ul>

<p>
The greatest value is:
</p>

<p>
<b>3200</b>
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>Optimal point = (4,3)</b>
</p>

<p>
<b>Maximum P = 3200</b>
</p>

<h3>What happened?</h3>

<p>
We did not guess the answer from the graph.
</p>

<p>
We calculated the objective function at every corner and then
compared the results.
</p>
