<h2>Defining Decision Variables</h2>

<p>
Before writing inequalities or an objective function, we must decide
what the variables represent.
</p>

<p>
A <b>decision variable</b> represents a quantity that we are allowed
to choose.
</p>

<h3>Example 1</h3>

<p>
A bakery makes bread and cakes.
</p>

<p>
We need to decide how many bread loaves and cakes to make.
</p>

<p>
Let:
</p>

<p>
<b>x = number of loaves of bread</b>
</p>

<p>
<b>y = number of cakes</b>
</p>

<p>
Now x and y have clear meanings.
</p>

<h3>Example 2</h3>

<p>
A farmer produces maize and beans.
</p>

<p>
Let:
</p>

<p>
<b>x = number of bags of maize</b>
</p>

<p>
<b>y = number of bags of beans</b>
</p>

<p>
These are decision variables because the farmer decides how many
bags of each crop to produce.
</p>

<h3>Example 3</h3>

<p>
A company makes tables and chairs.
</p>

<p>
Let:
</p>

<p>
<b>x = number of tables produced</b>
</p>

<p>
<b>y = number of chairs produced</b>
</p>

<p>
Every later equation must use these meanings consistently.
</p>

<h3>Why this matters</h3>

<p>
If we define x incorrectly, every inequality and the objective
function built from x will also be wrong.
</p>

<p>
So the first question in a word problem is:
</p>

<p>
<b>"What quantities are we deciding?"</b>
</p>
', 1),
