<h2>Translating Profit Wording into an Objective Function</h2>

<p>
Once the variables are defined, profit wording can be converted into
an objective function.
</p>

<p>
The important question is:
</p>

<p>
<b>How much profit does one unit of each product contribute?</b>
</p>

<h3>Example 1</h3>

<p>
A company makes tables and chairs.
</p>

<p>
Profit:
</p>

<ul>
<li>KSh 500 per table</li>
<li>KSh 300 per chair</li>
</ul>

<p>
Let:
</p>

<p>
x = tables<br>
y = chairs
</p>

<p>
Table profit = 500x
</p>

<p>
Chair profit = 300y
</p>

<p>
Total profit:
</p>

<p>
<b>P = 500x + 300y</b>
</p>

<p>
If the aim is greatest profit:
</p>

<p>
<b>Maximize P = 500x + 300y</b>
</p>

<h3>Example 2</h3>

<p>
A farmer earns KSh 1,000 per bag of maize and KSh 800 per bag
of beans.
</p>

<p>
Let x = maize bags and y = bean bags.
</p>

<p>
<b>P = 1000x + 800y</b>
</p>

<h3>Example 3</h3>

<p>
A bakery earns KSh 40 profit per loaf and KSh 100 profit per cake.
</p>

<p>
Let x = loaves and y = cakes.
</p>

<p>
<b>P = 40x + 100y</b>
</p>

<p>
The word <b>profit</b> tells us what the objective represents.
The word <b>maximum</b> tells us what to do with it.
</p>
