<h2>Building a Linear Programming Model from a Complete Problem</h2>

<p>
This screen combines the translation skills without yet solving the
model graphically.
</p>

<h3>Problem</h3>

<p>
A bakery makes bread and cakes.
</p>

<p>
Each loaf of bread requires 2 hours of labour and each cake requires
3 hours.
The bakery has at most 60 labour hours.
</p>

<p>
The bakery must make at least 10 cakes and loaves altogether.
</p>

<p>
The profit is KSh 40 per loaf and KSh 100 per cake.
The bakery wants to maximize profit.
</p>

<h3>Step 1: Define variables</h3>

<p>
Let:
</p>

<p>
<b>x = number of loaves</b>
</p>

<p>
<b>y = number of cakes</b>
</p>

<h3>Step 2: Translate labour</h3>

<p>
Bread uses 2x hours.
</p>

<p>
Cakes use 3y hours.
</p>

<p>
Total labour:
</p>

<p>
2x + 3y
</p>

<p>
At most 60:
</p>

<p>
<b>2x + 3y ≤ 60</b>
</p>

<h3>Step 3: Translate minimum production</h3>

<p>
Total products:
</p>

<p>
x + y
</p>

<p>
At least 10:
</p>

<p>
<b>x + y ≥ 10</b>
</p>

<h3>Step 4: Non-negativity</h3>

<p>
<b>x ≥ 0, y ≥ 0</b>
</p>

<h3>Step 5: Form the objective</h3>

<p>
Bread profit:
</p>

<p>
40x
</p>

<p>
Cake profit:
</p>

<p>
100y
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>Maximize P = 40x + 100y</b>
</p>

<h3>Complete model</h3>

<p>
<b>Maximize P = 40x + 100y</b>
</p>

<p>
subject to:
</p>

<p>
<b>2x + 3y ≤ 60</b>
</p>

<p>
<b>x + y ≥ 10</b>
</p>

<p>
<b>x ≥ 0, y ≥ 0</b>
</p>

<p>
The model is now ready to be solved.
