  (158, 158, '<h2>Understanding Fractions</h2>

<h3>WHAT IS A FRACTION?</h3>

<p>A fraction represents a number using two integers:</p>

<p><b>a/b</b></p>

<ul>
<li><b>a</b> is the numerator.</li>
<li><b>b</b> is the denominator.</li>
<li>The denominator must not be zero.</li>
</ul>

<p>For example:</p>

<p><b>3/5</b></p>

<ul>
<li>5 tells us the whole has been divided into 5 equal parts.</li>
<li>3 tells us that we are considering 3 of those parts.</li>
</ul>

<p><b>Important:</b> The parts must be equal. A fraction is not simply "some parts out of some parts."</p>

<h3>NUMERATOR AND DENOMINATOR</h3>

<p>In <b>7/9</b>:</p>

<ul>
<li>Numerator = 7</li>
<li>Denominator = 9</li>
</ul>

<p>The denominator describes the number of equal parts into which the whole is divided. The numerator tells how many of those parts are being counted.</p>

<h3>PROPER FRACTIONS</h3>

<p>A proper fraction has a numerator smaller than its denominator.</p>

<p>Examples:</p>

<ul>
<li>2/5</li>
<li>3/7</li>
<li>8/11</li>
</ul>

<p>Proper fractions have values between 0 and 1.</p>

<h3>IMPROPER FRACTIONS</h3>

<p>An improper fraction has a numerator greater than or equal to its denominator.</p>

<p>Examples:</p>

<ul>
<li>7/5</li>
<li>9/4</li>
<li>6/6</li>
</ul>

<p>An improper fraction can have a value greater than or equal to 1.</p>

<h3>MIXED NUMBERS</h3>

<p>A mixed number contains a whole number and a proper fraction.</p>

<p>Example:</p>

<p><b>2 1/3</b></p>

<p>This means:</p>

<p><b>2 + 1/3</b></p>

<p>To convert a mixed number to an improper fraction:</p>

<ol>
<li>Multiply the whole number by the denominator.</li>
<li>Add the numerator.</li>
<li>Keep the same denominator.</li>
</ol>

<p>Example:</p>

<p><b>2 1/3</b></p>

<p>2 × 3 = 6</p>
<p>6 + 1 = 7</p>

<p>Therefore:</p>

<p><b>2 1/3 = 7/3</b></p>

<h3>COMMON MISTAKES</h3>

<ul>
<li>Confusing numerator and denominator.</li>
<li>Forgetting that the parts represented by the denominator must be equal.</li>
<li>Thinking every fraction is less than 1.</li>
<li>Forgetting that the denominator cannot be zero.</li>
</ul>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLES</h3>

<p><b>Example 1:</b> Identify the numerator and denominator in 5/8.</p>

<p>Numerator = 5</p>
<p>Denominator = 8</p>

<p><b>Example 2:</b> Is 3/7 proper or improper?</p>

<p>3 &lt; 7, so it is a <b>proper fraction</b>.</p>

<p><b>Example 3:</b> Convert 3 2/5 to an improper fraction.</p>

<p>3 × 5 = 15</p>
<p>15 + 2 = 17</p>

<p><b>Answer: 17/5</b></p>
', 1),
  (159, 159, '<h2>Equivalent Fractions and Simplifying</h2>

<h3>WHAT ARE EQUIVALENT FRACTIONS?</h3>

<p>Equivalent fractions have <b>different numbers</b> but represent the <b>same value</b>.</p>

<p>For example:</p>

<p><b>1/2 = 2/4 = 3/6 = 4/8</b></p>

<p>The numerator and denominator can both be multiplied or divided by the same non-zero number without changing the value of the fraction.</p>

<h3>CREATING AN EQUIVALENT FRACTION</h3>

<p>Example:</p>

<p><b>3/5</b></p>

<p>Multiply both numerator and denominator by 4:</p>

<p>3 × 4 = 12</p>
<p>5 × 4 = 20</p>

<p>Therefore:</p>

<p><b>3/5 = 12/20</b></p>

<p><b>Important:</b> You must multiply or divide both parts by the same number.</p>

<h3>SIMPLIFYING A FRACTION</h3>

<p>Simplifying means writing a fraction in its simplest form without changing its value.</p>

<p>Example:</p>

<p><b>12/18</b></p>

<p>The greatest common factor of 12 and 18 is 6.</p>

<p>Divide both by 6:</p>

<p>12 ÷ 6 = 2</p>
<p>18 ÷ 6 = 3</p>

<p>Therefore:</p>

<p><b>12/18 = 2/3</b></p>

<h3>HOW TO KNOW WHEN A FRACTION IS FULLY SIMPLIFIED</h3>

<p>A fraction is in simplest form when the numerator and denominator have no common factor greater than 1.</p>

<p>Example:</p>

<p><b>4/9</b> is simplified because 4 and 9 have no common factor greater than 1.</p>

<h3>COMMON MISTAKES</h3>

<ul>
<li>Changing only the numerator.</li>
<li>Changing only the denominator.</li>
<li>Multiplying the numerator and dividing the denominator.</li>
<li>Stopping before the fraction is fully simplified.</li>
</ul>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLES</h3>

<p><b>Example 1:</b> Find an equivalent fraction to 2/3 with denominator 15.</p>

<p>3 × 5 = 15</p>

<p>Therefore multiply the numerator by 5:</p>

<p>2 × 5 = 10</p>

<p><b>Answer: 10/15</b></p>

<p><b>Example 2:</b> Simplify 20/30.</p>

<p>Both numbers are divisible by 10.</p>

<p>20 ÷ 10 = 2</p>
<p>30 ÷ 10 = 3</p>

<p><b>Answer: 2/3</b></p>
', 1),
  (160, 160, '<h2>Comparing Fractions</h2>

<h3>WHY COMPARISON CAN BE TRICKY</h3>

<p>You cannot always compare fractions by looking only at the numerator or only at the denominator.</p>

<p>The fraction''s value depends on <b>both</b>.</p>

<h3>METHOD 1: SAME DENOMINATOR</h3>

<p>If two fractions have the same denominator, compare their numerators.</p>

<p>Example:</p>

<p><b>5/8 and 3/8</b></p>

<p>Both denominators are 8.</p>

<p>Since 5 &gt; 3:</p>

<p><b>5/8 &gt; 3/8</b></p>

<h3>METHOD 2: SAME NUMERATOR</h3>

<p>If two positive fractions have the same numerator, the fraction with the smaller denominator is larger.</p>

<p>Example:</p>

<p><b>1/3 and 1/5</b></p>

<p>A third is larger than a fifth.</p>

<p>Therefore:</p>

<p><b>1/3 &gt; 1/5</b></p>

<h3>METHOD 3: COMMON DENOMINATOR</h3>

<p>Convert the fractions so that they have the same denominator.</p>

<p>Example:</p>

<p><b>2/3 and 3/4</b></p>

<p>A common denominator is 12.</p>

<p>2/3 = 8/12</p>

<p>3/4 = 9/12</p>

<p>Since 9 &gt; 8:</p>

<p><b>3/4 &gt; 2/3</b></p>

<h3>METHOD 4: CROSS-MULTIPLICATION</h3>

<p>For positive fractions:</p>

<p><b>a/b</b> and <b>c/d</b></p>

<p>Compare:</p>

<p><b>a × d</b> and <b>c × b</b></p>

<p>Example:</p>

<p>Compare 3/5 and 4/7.</p>

<p>3 × 7 = 21</p>

<p>4 × 5 = 20</p>

<p>Since 21 &gt; 20:</p>

<p><b>3/5 &gt; 4/7</b></p>

<h3>COMMON MISTAKES</h3>

<ul>
<li>Assuming a larger denominator always means a larger fraction.</li>
<li>Comparing only numerators when denominators differ.</li>
<li>Cross-multiplying but comparing the wrong products.</li>
</ul>
', 1),
  (161, 161, '<h2>Adding and Subtracting Fractions</h2>

<h3>THE MAIN RULE</h3>

<p>Fractions must have a <b>common denominator</b> before their numerators can be added or subtracted.</p>

<p>The denominator tells us the size of the parts. We cannot directly combine parts of different sizes.</p>

<h3>SAME DENOMINATOR</h3>

<p>Example:</p>

<p><b>2/7 + 3/7</b></p>

<p>The denominators are already equal.</p>

<p>Add the numerators:</p>

<p>2 + 3 = 5</p>

<p>Keep the denominator:</p>

<p><b>5/7</b></p>

<p>Notice that we do <b>not</b> add 7 + 7.</p>

<h3>DIFFERENT DENOMINATORS</h3>

<p>Example:</p>

<p><b>1/2 + 1/3</b></p>

<p>The denominators are different, so find their LCM.</p>

<p>LCM(2,3) = 6</p>

<p>Convert each fraction:</p>

<p>1/2 = 3/6</p>

<p>1/3 = 2/6</p>

<p>Now add:</p>

<p>3/6 + 2/6 = 5/6</p>

<h3>SUBTRACTION</h3>

<p>The same rule applies.</p>

<p>Example:</p>

<p><b>5/6 − 1/4</b></p>

<p>LCM of 6 and 4 = 12.</p>

<p>5/6 = 10/12</p>

<p>1/4 = 3/12</p>

<p>Therefore:</p>

<p>10/12 − 3/12 = 7/12</p>

<h3>THE GENERAL PROCEDURE</h3>

<ol>
<li>Find a common denominator.</li>
<li>Rewrite each fraction using that denominator.</li>
<li>Add or subtract the numerators.</li>
<li>Keep the common denominator.</li>
<li>Simplify the answer if possible.</li>
</ol>

<h3>COMMON MISTAKES</h3>

<ul>
<li>Adding denominators.</li>
<li>Changing the denominator without changing the numerator.</li>
<li>Using the wrong LCM.</li>
<li>Forgetting to simplify.</li>
</ul>
', 1),
  (162, 162, '<h2>Multiplying Fractions</h2>

<h3>THE RULE</h3>

<p>To multiply fractions:</p>

<p><b>Multiply numerator × numerator.</b></p>

<p><b>Multiply denominator × denominator.</b></p>

<p>Unlike addition, the denominators do <b>not</b> need to be equal.</p>

<h3>EXAMPLE 1</h3>

<p><b>2/3 × 4/5</b></p>

<p>Multiply numerators:</p>

<p>2 × 4 = 8</p>

<p>Multiply denominators:</p>

<p>3 × 5 = 15</p>

<p>Therefore:</p>

<p><b>2/3 × 4/5 = 8/15</b></p>

<h3>SIMPLIFYING</h3>

<p>Example:</p>

<p><b>2/3 × 3/4</b></p>

<p>Multiply:</p>

<p>6/12</p>

<p>Simplify:</p>

<p><b>6/12 = 1/2</b></p>

<h3>CANCELLING BEFORE MULTIPLYING</h3>

<p>You can simplify common factors before multiplying.</p>

<p>Example:</p>

<p><b>2/3 × 3/4</b></p>

<p>The 3 in the numerator and the 3 in the denominator cancel.</p>

<p>The 2 and 4 can also be simplified by dividing by 2.</p>

<p>This leaves:</p>

<p><b>1/2</b></p>

<p>This is called <b>cross-cancellation</b>.</p>

<h3>COMMON MISTAKES</h3>

<ul>
<li>Adding instead of multiplying.</li>
<li>Trying to find an LCM unnecessarily.</li>
<li>Multiplying correctly but forgetting to simplify.</li>
<li>Cancelling numbers that are not factors.</li>
</ul>
', 1),
  (163, 163, '<h2>Dividing Fractions</h2>

<h3>THE KEY RULE</h3>

<p>To divide by a fraction:</p>

<ol>
<li>Keep the first fraction.</li>
<li>Change division to multiplication.</li>
<li>Take the reciprocal of the second fraction.</li>
</ol>

<p>In short:</p>

<p><b>a/b ÷ c/d = a/b × d/c</b></p>

<h3>WHAT IS A RECIPROCAL?</h3>

<p>The reciprocal of a non-zero fraction is found by swapping the numerator and denominator.</p>

<p>Examples:</p>

<ul>
<li>2/3 → 3/2</li>
<li>5/7 → 7/5</li>
<li>4 → 1/4</li>
</ul>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE</h3>

<p><b>1/2 ÷ 1/4</b></p>

<p>Step 1: Keep 1/2.</p>

<p>Step 2: Change ÷ to ×.</p>

<p>Step 3: Flip 1/4 to 4/1.</p>

<p>Therefore:</p>

<p>1/2 × 4/1 = 4/2 = <b>2</b></p>

<h3>WHY DOES THE RECIPROCAL WORK?</h3>

<p>Division asks:</p>

<p><b>How many times does the divisor fit into the number?</b></p>

<p>For example:</p>

<p>1/2 ÷ 1/4 asks how many quarters fit into one half.</p>

<p>Two quarters fit into one half.</p>

<p>Therefore:</p>

<p><b>1/2 ÷ 1/4 = 2</b></p>

<h3>COMMON MISTAKES</h3>

<ul>
<li>Flipping the first fraction instead of the second.</li>
<li>Forgetting to change division into multiplication.</li>
<li>Flipping both fractions.</li>
<li>Forgetting to simplify.</li>
</ul>
', 1),
  (164, 164, '<h2>Decimals and Fractions</h2>

<h3>DECIMALS AS FRACTIONS</h3>

<p>A terminating decimal can be written as a fraction whose denominator is a power of 10.</p>

<p>The denominator depends on the number of digits after the decimal point.</p>

<ul>
<li>1 decimal place → denominator 10</li>
<li>2 decimal places → denominator 100</li>
<li>3 decimal places → denominator 1000</li>
</ul>

<h3>EXAMPLE 1: ONE DECIMAL PLACE</h3>

<p>Convert <b>0.6</b> to a fraction.</p>

<p>There is one digit after the decimal point:</p>

<p><b>0.6 = 6/10</b></p>

<p>Simplify:</p>

<p><b>6/10 = 3/5</b></p>

<h3>EXAMPLE 2: TWO DECIMAL PLACES</h3>

<p>Convert <b>0.25</b> to a fraction.</p>

<p>There are two digits after the decimal point:</p>

<p><b>0.25 = 25/100</b></p>

<p>Simplify by 25:</p>

<p><b>25/100 = 1/4</b></p>

<h3>FRACTION TO DECIMAL</h3>

<p>To convert a fraction to a decimal, divide the numerator by the denominator.</p>

<p>Example:</p>

<p><b>3/4</b></p>

<p>3 ÷ 4 = <b>0.75</b></p>

<p>Therefore:</p>

<p><b>3/4 = 0.75</b></p>

<h3>TERMINATING DECIMALS</h3>

<p>A terminating decimal eventually stops.</p>

<p>Examples:</p>

<ul>
<li>0.5</li>
<li>0.25</li>
<li>0.625</li>
<li>1.75</li>
</ul>

<h3>IMPORTANT DISTINCTION</h3>

<p>Not every fraction produces a terminating decimal.</p>

<p>For example:</p>

<p>1/3 = 0.333...</p>

<p>The digits continue repeating. This is a <b>recurring decimal</b>, which is studied separately.</p>

<h3>COMMON MISTAKES</h3>

<ul>
<li>Using 10 as the denominator when there are two or three decimal places.</li>
<li>Forgetting to simplify the resulting fraction.</li>
<li>Confusing terminating and recurring decimals.</li>
</ul>
', 1),
  (165, 165, '<h2>Recurring Decimals</h2>

<h3>WHAT IS A RECURRING DECIMAL?</h3>

<p>A recurring decimal is a decimal in which one or more digits repeat indefinitely.</p>

<p>Examples:</p>

<ul>
<li>0.333... = 0.3̅</li>
<li>0.666... = 0.6̅</li>
<li>0.121212... = 0.12̅</li>
</ul>

<p>The dots mean that the pattern continues forever.</p>

<h3>FRACTION TO RECURRING DECIMAL</h3>

<p>Divide the numerator by the denominator.</p>

<p>Example:</p>

<p><b>1/3</b></p>

<p>1 ÷ 3 = 0.333...</p>

<p>Therefore:</p>

<p><b>1/3 = 0.333...</b></p>

<h3>CONVERTING A RECURRING DECIMAL TO A FRACTION</h3>

<p>Use algebra to eliminate the repeating part.</p>

<h3>EXAMPLE 1: 0.333...</h3>

<p>Let:</p>

<p><b>x = 0.333...</b></p>

<p>Multiply both sides by 10:</p>

<p><b>10x = 3.333...</b></p>

<p>Subtract the original equation:</p>

<p><b>10x − x = 3.333... − 0.333...</b></p>

<p>The repeating decimals cancel:</p>

<p><b>9x = 3</b></p>

<p>Divide by 9:</p>

<p><b>x = 3/9 = 1/3</b></p>

<h3>EXAMPLE 2: 0.121212...</h3>

<p>The repeating block contains two digits: <b>12</b>.</p>

<p>Let:</p>

<p><b>x = 0.121212...</b></p>

<p>Because two digits repeat, multiply by 100:</p>

<p><b>100x = 12.121212...</b></p>

<p>Subtract x:</p>

<p><b>100x − x = 12</b></p>

<p>Therefore:</p>

<p><b>99x = 12</b></p>

<p>So:</p>

<p><b>x = 12/99 = 4/33</b></p>

<h3>THE PATTERN</h3>

<p>If one digit repeats, multiply by 10.</p>

<p>If two digits repeat, multiply by 100.</p>

<p>If three digits repeat, multiply by 1000.</p>

<p>The goal is always the same: <b>shift the decimal until the repeating parts line up, then subtract.</b></p>

<h3>COMMON MISTAKES</h3>

<ul>
<li>Using 10 when two or more digits repeat.</li>
<li>Forgetting to subtract the original equation.</li>
<li>Stopping the decimal instead of treating it as infinite.</li>
<li>Forgetting to simplify the final fraction.</li>
</ul>
', 1),
  (166, 166, '<h2>Area of Rectangles and Squares</h2>

<h3>WHAT IS AREA?</h3>

<p>Area measures the amount of <b>flat surface</b> inside a two-dimensional shape.</p>

<p>Area is measured in <b>square units</b> such as cm², m², or km².</p>

<h3>AREA OF A RECTANGLE</h3>

<p>A rectangle has a length and a width.</p>

<p>The area is:</p>

<p><b>A = l × w</b></p>

<ul>
<li><b>A</b> = area</li>
<li><b>l</b> = length</li>
<li><b>w</b> = width</li>
</ul>

<p>Example:</p>

<p>A rectangle has length 8 cm and width 3 cm.</p>

<p>A = 8 × 3</p>

<p><b>A = 24 cm²</b></p>

<h3>WHY DO WE MULTIPLY?</h3>

<p>Imagine a rectangle divided into equal 1 cm × 1 cm squares.</p>

<p>The length tells us how many squares fit along one direction. The width tells us how many rows of squares there are.</p>

<p>Multiplying the two counts gives the total number of square units.</p>

<p>That is why:</p>

<p><b>Area = length × width</b></p>

<h3>AREA OF A SQUARE</h3>

<p>A square has four equal sides.</p>

<p>Therefore its length and width are the same.</p>

<p>So:</p>

<p><b>A = side × side = side²</b></p>

<p>Example:</p>

<p>A square has side 6 cm.</p>

<p>A = 6 × 6</p>

<p><b>A = 36 cm²</b></p>

<h3>FINDING A MISSING DIMENSION</h3>

<p>If the area and one dimension are known, rearrange the formula.</p>

<p>Since:</p>

<p><b>A = l × w</b></p>

<p>Then:</p>

<p><b>l = A ÷ w</b></p>

<p>and</p>

<p><b>w = A ÷ l</b></p>

<p>Example:</p>

<p>Area = 40 cm² and width = 5 cm.</p>

<p>Length = 40 ÷ 5</p>

<p><b>Length = 8 cm</b></p>

<h3>COMMON MISTAKES</h3>

<ul>
<li>Adding length and width instead of multiplying.</li>
<li>Using the perimeter formula instead of the area formula.</li>
<li>Forgetting square units.</li>
<li>Using inconsistent units for the dimensions.</li>
</ul>
', 1),
  (167, 167, '<h2>Area of Triangles</h2>

<h3>THE FORMULA</h3>

<p>The area of a triangle is:</p>

<p><b>A = 1/2 × b × h</b></p>

<ul>
<li><b>A</b> = area</li>
<li><b>b</b> = base</li>
<li><b>h</b> = perpendicular height</li>
</ul>

<h3>WHAT DOES HEIGHT MEAN?</h3>

<p>The height of a triangle is the <b>perpendicular distance</b> from the chosen base to the opposite vertex.</p>

<p>Perpendicular means that the height meets the base at an angle of <b>90°</b>.</p>

<p><b>Important:</b> The height is not necessarily the sloping side of the triangle.</p>

<h3>WHY IS THERE A 1/2?</h3>

<p>A triangle can be paired with another identical triangle to form a parallelogram or rectangle with the same base and perpendicular height.</p>

<p>The triangle therefore has half the area of that corresponding shape.</p>

<p>That gives:</p>

<p><b>A = 1/2 × base × height</b></p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE</h3>

<p>Find the area of a triangle with base 12 cm and perpendicular height 5 cm.</p>

<p>A = 1/2 × 12 × 5</p>

<p>A = 6 × 5</p>

<p><b>A = 30 cm²</b></p>

<h3>FINDING A MISSING DIMENSION</h3>

<p>Starting with:</p>

<p><b>A = 1/2bh</b></p>

<p>Multiply both sides by 2:</p>

<p><b>2A = bh</b></p>

<p>Therefore:</p>

<p><b>h = 2A ÷ b</b></p>

<p>and:</p>

<p><b>b = 2A ÷ h</b></p>

<p>Example:</p>

<p>Area = 40 cm² and base = 10 cm.</p>

<p>h = (2 × 40) ÷ 10</p>

<p>h = 80 ÷ 10</p>

<p><b>h = 8 cm</b></p>

<h3>COMMON MISTAKES</h3>

<ul>
<li>Forgetting the factor 1/2.</li>
<li>Using the sloping side as the height.</li>
<li>Using a height that is not perpendicular to the chosen base.</li>
<li>Forgetting square units.</li>
</ul>
', 1),
  (168, 168, '<h2>Volume of Cubes and Cuboids</h2>

<h3>WHAT IS VOLUME?</h3>

<p>Volume measures the amount of <b>three-dimensional space</b> occupied by a solid.</p>

<p>Volume is measured in <b>cubic units</b>, such as cm³, m³, or km³.</p>

<h3>VOLUME OF A CUBOID</h3>

<p>A cuboid has three dimensions:</p>

<ul>
<li>length</li>
<li>width</li>
<li>height</li>
</ul>

<p>The formula is:</p>

<p><b>V = l × w × h</b></p>

<p>Example:</p>

<p>A cuboid has dimensions 5 cm, 3 cm and 2 cm.</p>

<p>V = 5 × 3 × 2</p>

<p><b>V = 30 cm³</b></p>

<h3>WHY DO WE MULTIPLY THREE DIMENSIONS?</h3>

<p>Area uses two dimensions because it measures a flat surface.</p>

<p>Volume uses three dimensions because a solid extends in three directions.</p>

<p>Therefore:</p>

<p><b>Length × width</b> gives the area of one layer.</p>

<p>Multiplying that layer by the <b>height</b> gives the total volume.</p>

<h3>VOLUME OF A CUBE</h3>

<p>A cube has all three dimensions equal.</p>

<p>If each side has length s:</p>

<p><b>V = s × s × s = s³</b></p>

<p>Example:</p>

<p>A cube has side 4 cm.</p>

<p>V = 4 × 4 × 4</p>

<p><b>V = 64 cm³</b></p>

<h3>FINDING A MISSING DIMENSION</h3>

<p>From:</p>

<p><b>V = lwh</b></p>

<p>We can rearrange:</p>

<p><b>l = V ÷ (wh)</b></p>

<p><b>w = V ÷ (lh)</b></p>

<p><b>h = V ÷ (lw)</b></p>

<h3>COMMON MISTAKES</h3>

<ul>
<li>Using only two dimensions.</li>
<li>Confusing volume with surface area.</li>
<li>Using square units instead of cubic units.</li>
<li>Forgetting that a cube has three equal dimensions.</li>
</ul>
', 1),
  (169, 169, '<h2>Surface Area of Cubes and Cuboids</h2>

<h3>WHAT IS SURFACE AREA?</h3>

<p>Surface area is the <b>total area of all the outside faces</b> of a three-dimensional object.</p>

<p>It is measured in <b>square units</b>, such as cm² or m².</p>

<p>Do not confuse surface area with volume:</p>

<ul>
<li><b>Surface area</b> measures outside faces.</li>
<li><b>Volume</b> measures three-dimensional space.</li>
</ul>

<h3>SURFACE AREA OF A CUBE</h3>

<p>A cube has 6 equal square faces.</p>

<p>Area of one face:</p>

<p><b>s²</b></p>

<p>Since there are 6 faces:</p>

<p><b>SA = 6s²</b></p>

<p>Example:</p>

<p>A cube has side 4 cm.</p>

<p>SA = 6 × 4²</p>

<p>SA = 6 × 16</p>

<p><b>SA = 96 cm²</b></p>

<h3>SURFACE AREA OF A CUBOID</h3>

<p>A cuboid has three pairs of equal faces.</p>

<p>The three different face areas are:</p>

<ul>
<li>lw</li>
<li>lh</li>
<li>wh</li>
</ul>

<p>Each occurs twice.</p>

<p>Therefore:</p>

<p><b>SA = 2(lw + lh + wh)</b></p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE</h3>

<p>Find the surface area of a cuboid with length 5 cm, width 3 cm and height 2 cm.</p>

<p>SA = 2(lw + lh + wh)</p>

<p>SA = 2[(5 × 3) + (5 × 2) + (3 × 2)]</p>

<p>SA = 2(15 + 10 + 6)</p>

<p>SA = 2 × 31</p>

<p><b>SA = 62 cm²</b></p>

<h3>COMMON MISTAKES</h3>

<ul>
<li>Calculating volume instead of surface area.</li>
<li>Forgetting that opposite faces occur in pairs.</li>
<li>Forgetting the factor 2 in the cuboid formula.</li>
<li>Using cubic units instead of square units.</li>
</ul>
', 1),
  (170, 170, '<h2>Measurement Units and Dimensions</h2>

<h3>WHY UNITS MATTER</h3>

<p>The numerical answer alone is not enough. The unit tells us <b>what kind of quantity</b> has been measured.</p>

<p>For example:</p>

<ul>
<li>5 cm measures length.</li>
<li>5 cm² measures area.</li>
<li>5 cm³ measures volume.</li>
</ul>

<p>These are different quantities even though the number is the same.</p>

<h3>LENGTH</h3>

<p>Length measures one dimension.</p>

<p>Common units include:</p>

<ul>
<li>mm</li>
<li>cm</li>
<li>m</li>
<li>km</li>
</ul>

<p>Length uses ordinary units such as <b>cm</b> or <b>m</b>.</p>

<h3>AREA</h3>

<p>Area measures two dimensions.</p>

<p>Therefore its units are squared:</p>

<ul>
<li>cm²</li>
<li>m²</li>
<li>km²</li>
</ul>

<p>For example:</p>

<p>4 cm × 3 cm = 12 cm²</p>

<h3>VOLUME</h3>

<p>Volume measures three dimensions.</p>

<p>Therefore its units are cubed:</p>

<ul>
<li>cm³</li>
<li>m³</li>
<li>km³</li>
</ul>

<p>For example:</p>

<p>4 cm × 3 cm × 2 cm = 24 cm³</p>

<h3>AREA VS VOLUME</h3>

<p>Area uses <b>two</b> dimensions:</p>

<p><b>length × width</b></p>

<p>Volume uses <b>three</b> dimensions:</p>

<p><b>length × width × height</b></p>

<h3>CONVERTING SQUARED UNITS</h3>

<p>When converting area units, the conversion factor must also be squared.</p>

<p>For example:</p>

<p>1 m = 100 cm</p>

<p>Therefore:</p>

<p><b>1 m² = 100² cm² = 10,000 cm²</b></p>

<p>It is incorrect to say 1 m² = 100 cm².</p>

<h3>CONVERTING CUBIC UNITS</h3>

<p>When converting volume units, the conversion factor must be cubed.</p>

<p>Since:</p>

<p>1 m = 100 cm</p>

<p>Then:</p>

<p><b>1 m³ = 100³ cm³ = 1,000,000 cm³</b></p>

<h3>COMMON MISTAKES</h3>

<ul>
<li>Writing cm instead of cm² for area.</li>
<li>Writing cm² instead of cm³ for volume.</li>
<li>Using the length conversion factor directly for area.</li>
<li>Using the length conversion factor directly for volume.</li>
<li>Forgetting to make units consistent before calculating.</li>
</ul>
', 1),
  (171, 171, '<h2>Coordinate Plane Basics</h2>

<p>The coordinate plane is used to locate points using numbers.</p>

<h3> NOTES (EXPLAINED)</h3>
<ul>
<li>The plane has two number lines: X-axis (horizontal) and Y-axis (vertical).</li>
<li>Points are written as (x, y).</li>
<li>X value shows left/right movement.</li>
<li>Y value shows up/down movement.</li>
<li>The center point is called the origin (0,0).</li>
</ul>

<p><b> Key idea:</b> Every point is a location made from two numbers.</p>

<h3> COMMON MISTAKES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Reversing (x, y) as (y, x)</li>
<li>Confusing axis directions</li>
<li>Forgetting origin is (0,0)</li>
<li>Mixing up horizontal and vertical axes</li>
</ul>

<h3> WORKED EXAMPLES</h3>

<ul>
<li>
<b>Example 1:</b> Identify origin<br>
Step 1: Locate center point<br>
Step 2: Coordinates are (0,0)<br>
<b>Answer: (0,0)</b>
</li>

<li>
<b>Example 2:</b> Horizontal axis<br>
Step 1: Identify left-right line<br>
Step 2: This is the X-axis<br>
<b>Answer: X-axis</b>
</li>

<li>
<b>Example 3:</b> Vertical axis<br>
Step 1: Identify up-down line<br>
Step 2: This is the Y-axis<br>
<b>Answer: Y-axis</b>
</li>
</ul>

<h3> REAL WORLD APPLICATION</h3>
<ul>
<li>GPS and map navigation systems</li>
<li>Computer graphics positioning</li>
<li>Game development movement systems</li>
<li>Engineering design layouts</li>
</ul>
', 1),
  (172, 172, '<h2>Plotting Points</h2>

<p>Plotting means marking a point on the coordinate plane using (x, y).</p>

<h3> NOTES (EXPLAINED)</h3>
<ul>
<li>Always start from origin (0,0).</li>
<li>Move along X-axis first (left/right).</li>
<li>Then move along Y-axis (up/down).</li>
<li>Positive x → right, negative x → left.</li>
<li>Positive y → up, negative y → down.</li>
</ul>

<p><b> Key idea:</b> X always comes before Y.</p>

<h3> COMMON MISTAKES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Moving Y before X</li>
<li>Mixing negative directions</li>
<li>Plotting wrong quadrant</li>
<li>Confusing (x, y) order</li>
</ul>
<h3> WORKED EXAMPLES (WITH CARTESIAN PLANE)</h3>

<ul>

<li>
<b>Example 1:</b> (2,3)<br>

Step 1: Move 2 units right<br>
Step 2: Move 3 units up<br>

<pre>
          y
          ↑
     4    |
     3    |      ● (2,3)
     2    |     /
     1    |    /
  -------O----------------→ x
     0    |  1  2  3
</pre>

<b>Answer: (2,3)</b>
</li>

<li>
<b>Example 2:</b> (-2,1)<br>

Step 1: Move 2 units left<br>
Step 2: Move 1 unit up<br>

<pre>
          y
          ↑
     3    |
     2    |
     1    |   ● (-2,1)
     0    |
  -------O----------------→ x
        -3  -2  -1
</pre>

<b>Answer: (-2,1)</b>
</li>

<li>
<b>Example 3:</b> (0,4)<br>

Step 1: Stay on Y-axis<br>
Step 2: Move 4 units up<br>

<pre>
          y
          ↑
     5    |
     4    |   ● (0,4)
     3    |
     2    |
     1    |
  -------O----------------→ x
     0
</pre>

<b>Answer: (0,4)</b>
</li>

</ul>
<h3> REAL WORLD APPLICATION</h3>
<ul>
<li>GPS location mapping</li>
<li>Computer graphics positioning</li>
<li>Game character movement</li>
<li>Engineering design layouts</li>
</ul>
', 1),
  (173, 173, '<h2>Line Graphs</h2>

<p>A line graph shows how values change over time or sequence.</p>

<h3> NOTES (EXPLAINED)</h3>
<ul>
<li>Used to show trends (increase/decrease).</li>
<li>Points are plotted first, then joined with straight lines.</li>
<li>X-axis usually shows time or order.</li>
<li>Y-axis shows values (sales, temperature, etc).</li>
</ul>

<p><b> Key idea:</b> Line graphs show change over time.</p>

<h3> COMMON MISTAKES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Not labeling axes</li>
<li>Skipping scale</li>
<li>Joining wrong points</li>
</ul>
<h3> WORKED EXAMPLES (GRAPH + VISUAL)</h3>

<ul>

<li>
<b>Example 1:</b> Steps to draw a line graph from data points<br>

Step 1: Draw x-axis (horizontal) and y-axis (vertical)<br>
Step 2: Mark equal scale on both axes<br>
Step 3: Plot each coordinate point accurately<br>
Step 4: Connect points in correct order<br>

<pre>
          y
          ↑
     5    |
     4    |        ● (3,4)
     3    |      ● (2,3)
     2    |    ● (1,2)
     1    |  ● (0,1)
  -------O------------------------→ x
     0    1    2    3
</pre>

<b>Answer: A plotted coordinate line graph showing change between points</b>
</li>

<br>

<li>
<b>Example 2:</b> What information does a line graph represent?<br>

Step 1: Observe plotted coordinates<br>
Step 2: Follow direction of connected line<br>
Step 3: Compare rise or fall in values<br>
Step 4: Identify pattern behavior over time or variable change<br>

<pre>
          y
          ↑
     5    |        ●
     4    |      ●
     3    |    ●
     2    |  ●
     1    |●
  -------O------------------------→ x
     0
</pre>

<b>Answer: It represents a pattern or trend in data (increase or decrease)</b>
</li>

<br>

<li>
<b>Example 3:</b> Why are points connected in a graph?<br>

Step 1: Identify separate data values<br>
Step 2: Observe order of values on x-axis<br>
Step 3: Connect points to show continuous change<br>
Step 4: Form a relationship model between variables<br>

<pre>
          y
          ↑
     5    |        ●──────●
     4    |      ●
     3    |    ●
     2    |  ●
     1    |●
  -------O------------------------→ x
     0
</pre>

<b>Answer: To show continuity and relationship between data points</b>
</li>

</ul>
', 1),
  (174, 174, '<h2>Gradient (Slope)</h2>

<h3> FOUNDATION EXPLANATION</h3>
<p>
Gradient measures how steep a line is on a graph.
It shows how much the vertical value changes compared to the horizontal change.
</p>
<h3> GRADIENT (SLOPE) — CALCULATION VIEW</h3>

<pre>
Gradient = rise ÷ run
m = (y₂ − y₁) / (x₂ − x₁)
</pre>

<ul>
<li><b>Rise</b> = y₂ − y₁ (vertical change)</li>
<li><b>Run</b> = x₂ − x₁ (horizontal change)</li>
<li><b>Gradient (m)</b> = rate of change of y with respect to x</li>
</ul>

<p><b>Direction rules:</b></p>
<p>
m &gt; 0 → line rises upward<br>
m &lt; 0 → line falls downward<br>
m = 0 → horizontal line<br>
Undefined → vertical line
</p>

<p><b> Key idea:</b> Larger absolute value of m = steeper line</p>

<h3> KEY FACTS (CALCULATION BASED)</h3>
<ul>
<li>Gradient compares change between two points</li>
<li>Uses subtraction of coordinates</li>
<li>Always simplifies to a number or fraction</li>
<li>Same gradient → parallel lines</li>
</ul>

<h3> COMMON MISTAKES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Using x₂ − x₁ in wrong order</li>
<li>Using y₂ − y₁ incorrectly swapped</li>
<li>Forgetting negative signs</li>
<li>Dividing run by rise instead of rise by run</li>
</ul>

<h3> WORKED EXAMPLES (STEP-BY-STEP CALCULATION)</h3>

<ul>

<li>
<b>Example 1:</b> (2,3) and (6,11)<br>

Step 1: y₂ − y₁ = 11 − 3 = 8<br>
Step 2: x₂ − x₁ = 6 − 2 = 4<br>
Step 3: m = 8 ÷ 4<br>
<b>Answer: m = 2</b>
</li>

<br>

<li>
<b>Example 2:</b> (1,5) and (4,11)<br>

Step 1: y₂ − y₁ = 11 − 5 = 6<br>
Step 2: x₂ − x₁ = 4 − 1 = 3<br>
Step 3: m = 6 ÷ 3<br>
<b>Answer: m = 2</b>
</li>

<br>

<li>
<b>Example 3:</b> (3,10) and (7,2)<br>

Step 1: y₂ − y₁ = 2 − 10 = −8<br>
Step 2: x₂ − x₁ = 7 − 3 = 4<br>
Step 3: m = −8 ÷ 4<br>
<b>Answer: m = −2</b>
</li>

</ul>
<h3> REAL WORLD APPLICATION</h3>
<ul>
<li>Road slope design (hills and ramps)</li>
<li>Building construction angles</li>
<li>Physics: speed vs time graphs</li>
<li>Economics: rate of change in profit</li>
</ul>
', 1),
  (175, 175, '<h2>Applications of Graphs</h2>

<h3> FOUNDATION EXPLANATION</h3>
<p>
Graphs help represent real-world data clearly and quickly.
They transform numbers into visual patterns that are easier to understand.
</p>

<h3> WELL EXPLAINED NOTES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Graphs simplify large sets of data</li>
<li>Used in science, business, weather, and population studies</li>
<li>Help in comparing values and identifying trends</li>
<li>Useful for prediction and decision-making</li>
</ul>

<p><b> Key idea:</b> Graphs turn data into visual meaning.</p>

<h3> COMMON MISTAKES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Ignoring axis labels</li>
<li>Misinterpreting upward/downward trends</li>
<li>Confusing bar graphs, line graphs, and pie charts</li>
</ul>

<h3> WORKED EXAMPLES</h3>

<ul>
<li>
<b>Example 1: Weather</b><br>
Step 1: Record daily temperature<br>
Step 2: Plot values over time<br>
Step 3: Observe trend<br>
<b>Answer: Weather pattern becomes visible</b>
</li>

<li>
<b>Example 2: Business</b><br>
Step 1: Collect sales data<br>
Step 2: Plot graph over months<br>
Step 3: Identify increase or decrease<br>
<b>Answer: Sales trend analysis</b>
</li>

<li>
<b>Example 3: Population</b><br>
Step 1: Record yearly population<br>
Step 2: Plot growth graph<br>
Step 3: Analyze increase rate<br>
<b>Answer: Population growth pattern</b>
</li>
</ul>

<h3> REAL WORLD APPLICATION</h3>
<ul>
<li>Weather forecasting systems</li>
<li>Business performance tracking</li>
<li>Population growth studies</li>
<li>Scientific experiment analysis</li>
</ul>
', 1),
  (176, 176, '<h2>Ratio Basics</h2>

<h3> FOUNDATION EXPLANATION</h3>
<p>
A ratio compares two or more quantities of the same type.
It shows how much of one thing exists compared to another.
</p>

<h3> WELL EXPLAINED NOTES</h3>
<ul>
<li>A ratio shows relative size, not actual total value</li>
<li>Written as a : b, where order matters</li>
<li>All quantities must be in the same unit before comparing</li>
<li>Simplify ratios using the highest common factor (HCF)</li>
</ul>

<p><b> Key idea:</b> Ratio is a comparison, not a total amount.</p>

<h3> COMMON MISTAKES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Not converting units before forming ratio</li>
<li>Reversing order of terms (a:b ≠ b:a)</li>
<li>Failing to simplify completely</li>
</ul>

<h3> WORKED EXAMPLES</h3>

<ul>
<li>
<b>Example 1:</b> 10:20<br>
Step 1: HCF = 10<br>
Step 2: 10 ÷ 10 : 20 ÷ 10<br>
<b>Answer: 1:2</b>
</li>

<li>
<b>Example 2:</b> 6 apples : 3 apples<br>
Step 1: 6:3<br>
Step 2: Divide by 3<br>
<b>Answer: 2:1</b>
</li>

<li>
<b>Example 3:</b> 15:5<br>
Step 1: HCF = 5<br>
Step 2: 15 ÷ 5 : 5 ÷ 5<br>
<b>Answer: 3:1</b>
</li>
</ul>

<h3> REAL WORLD APPLICATION</h3>
<ul>
<li>Mixing ingredients in cooking</li>
<li>Map scaling and models</li>
<li>Financial comparisons</li>
<li>Population comparisons in statistics</li>
</ul>
', 1),
  (177, 177, '<h2>Dividing in Ratio</h2>

<h3> FOUNDATION EXPLANATION</h3>
<p>
A quantity is shared into parts according to a given ratio.
Each part of the ratio represents a proportional share of the total.
</p>

<h3> STEP-BY-STEP METHOD</h3>
<ul>
  <li><b>Step 1:</b> Add all parts of the ratio</li>
  <li><b>Step 2:</b> Divide the total by the sum of parts (unit value)</li>
  <li><b>Step 3:</b> Multiply each ratio part by the unit value</li>
</ul>

<p><b> Key idea:</b> Each ratio part represents a share of the total.</p>

<h3> WELL EXPLAINED NOTES</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Ratios split quantities into proportional parts</li>
  <li>The total must always be preserved</li>
  <li>Each part is scaled using the unit value</li>
  <li>Used in sharing money, resources, and quantities</li>
</ul>

<h3> COMMON MISTAKES</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Forgetting to add ratio parts</li>
  <li>Dividing incorrectly before finding unit value</li>
  <li>Mixing up final shares</li>
</ul>

<h3> WORKED EXAMPLES</h3>

<ul>
<li>
<b>Example 1:</b> 50 in 1:1<br>
Step 1: 1 + 1 = 2<br>
Step 2: 50 ÷ 2 = 25<br>
Step 3: 1×25 = 25, 1×25 = 25<br>
<b>Answer: 25 and 25</b>
</li>

<li>
<b>Example 2:</b> 60 in 2:1<br>
Step 1: 2 + 1 = 3<br>
Step 2: 60 ÷ 3 = 20<br>
Step 3: 40 and 20<br>
<b>Answer: 40 and 20</b>
</li>

<li>
<b>Example 3:</b> 90 in 3:2<br>
Step 1: 3 + 2 = 5<br>
Step 2: 90 ÷ 5 = 18<br>
Step 3: 54 and 36<br>
<b>Answer: 54 and 36</b>
</li>
</ul>

<h3> REAL WORLD APPLICATION</h3>
<ul>
<li>Sharing profits in business partnerships</li>
<li>Dividing inheritance or property</li>
<li>Splitting resources in teamwork</li>
<li>Cooking recipe adjustments</li>
</ul>
', 1),
  (178, 178, '<h2>Proportion</h2>

<h3> FOUNDATION EXPLANATION</h3>
<p>
A proportion shows that two ratios are equal.
It helps us compare quantities and solve missing values.
</p>
<pre>a : b = c : d  →  a/b = c/
</pre>
<p>Using cross multiplication:</p>
<pre>a × d = b × c</pre>
<p><b> Key idea:</b> Proportion means two equal ratios.</p>
<h3> WELL EXPLAINED NOTES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Proportion compares two equal ratios</li>
<li>Cross multiplication is used to find unknown values</li>
<li>Always simplify final answers</li>
<li>Useful in scaling and comparison problems</li>
</ul>
<h3> COMMON MISTAKES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Mixing up numerator and denominator</li>
<li>Incorrect cross multiplication</li>
<li>Forgetting to simplify final result</li>
</ul>
<h3> WORKED EXAMPLES</h3>
<ul>
<li>
<b>Example 1:</b> 2:4 = x:8<br>
Step 1: 2 × 8 = 4x<br>
Step 2: 16 = 4x<br>
Step 3: x = 4<br>
<b>Answer: 4</b>
</li>
<li>
<b>Example 2:</b> 3:5 = x:10<br>
Step 1: 3 × 10 = 5x<br>
Step 2: 30 = 5x<br>
Step 3: x = 6<br>
<b>Answer: 6</b>
</li>
<li>
<b>Example 3:</b> 4:6 = 2:x<br>
Step 1: 4x = 12<br>
Step 2: x = 3<br>
<b>Answer: 3</b>
</li>
</ul>
', 1),
  (179, 179, '<h2>Direct Proportion</h2>

<h3> FOUNDATION EXPLANATION</h3>
<p>
In direct proportion, when one quantity increases, the other increases at the same rate.
They maintain a constant ratio.
</p>
<pre>y ∝ x  →  y = kx</pre>
<p><b> Key idea:</b> Same direction change (increase → increase, decrease → decrease)</p>
<h3> WELL EXPLAINED NOTES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Direct proportion means both variables change together</li>
<li>The ratio y/x is always constant</li>
<li>Find unit value first for easier solving</li>
<li>Used in pricing, speed, and scaling problems</li>
</ul>
<h3> WORKED EXAMPLES</h3>
<ul>
<li>
<b>Example 1:</b> 2 pens = 10<br>
Step 1: 1 pen = 10 ÷ 2 = 5<br>
Step 2: 4 pens = 5 × 4 = 20<br>
<b>Answer: 20</b>
</li>
<li>
<b>Example 2:</b> 3 kg = 30<br>
Step 1: 1 kg = 10<br>
Step 2: 6 kg = 60<br>
<b>Answer: 60</b>
</li>
<li>
<b>Example 3:</b> 5 items = 25<br>
Step 1: 1 item = 5<br>
Step 2: 10 items = 50<br>
<b>Answer: 50</b>
</li>
</ul>
<h3> REAL WORLD APPLICATION</h3>
<ul>
<li>Shopping cost calculations</li>
<li>Fuel consumption vs distance</li>
<li>Work and wage calculations</li>
<li>Recipe scaling in cooking</li>
</ul>
', 1),
  (180, 180, '<h2>Inverse Proportion</h2>

<h3> FOUNDATION EXPLANATION</h3>
<p>
In inverse proportion, when one quantity increases, the other decreases.
Their product remains constant.
</p>
<pre>x × y = k (constant)</pre>
<p><b> Key idea:</b> More workers → less time needed.</p>
<h3> WELL EXPLAINED NOTES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Inverse means opposite movement between variables</li>
<li>Used in work-rate problems, speed-time, and efficiency tasks</li>
<li>If one doubles, the other halves (if perfectly inverse)</li>
<li>Always keep the product constant</li>
</ul>
<h3> WORKED EXAMPLES</h3>
<ul>
<li>
<b>Example 1:</b> 2 workers = 10 days<br>
Step 1: 2 × 10 = 20<br>
Step 2: 20 ÷ 4 = 5<br>
<b>Answer: 5 days</b>
</li>
<li>
<b>Example 2:</b> 3 workers = 12 days<br>
Step 1: 3 × 12 = 36<br>
Step 2: 36 ÷ 6 = 6<br>
<b>Answer: 6 days</b>
</li>
<li>
<b>Example 3:</b> 4 workers = 8 days<br>
Step 1: 4 × 8 = 32<br>
Step 2: 32 ÷ 8 = 4<br>
<b>Answer: 4 days</b>
</li>
</ul>
<h3> REAL WORLD APPLICATION</h3>
<ul>
<li>Construction work scheduling</li>
<li>Machine efficiency in factories</li>
<li>Speed vs travel time in transport</li>
<li>Teamwork task distribution</li>
</ul>
', 1),
  (181, 181, '
<h2>Meaning of Data</h2>

<p>
<b>Data</b> is a collection of facts, measurements, observations, or
answers that have been recorded for a purpose.
</p>

<p>
The important idea is this:
<b>data are the actual pieces of information we collect.</b>
</p>

<h3>Example 1: Test marks</h3>

<p>
Suppose five learners obtain these marks:
</p>

<p><b>45, 62, 51, 70, 48</b></p>

<p>
These numbers are data because they are recorded observations of the
learners'' marks.
</p>

<h3>Example 2: Ages</h3>

<p>
Suppose the ages of five learners are:
</p>

<p><b>14, 15, 14, 16, 15</b></p>

<p>
These ages are also data because they are recorded information.
</p>

<h3>Example 3: Number of books</h3>

<p>
A teacher records the number of books read by four learners:
</p>

<p><b>2, 5, 1, 4</b></p>

<p>
Those numbers form a data set.
</p>

<h3>Key idea</h3>

<p>
Data does not have to be numbers only. Words, categories, measurements
and other recorded observations can also be data.
</p>
', 1),
  (182, 182, '
<h2>Meaning of Statistics</h2>

<p>
<b>Statistics</b> is the process of collecting, organising, analysing,
representing and interpreting data so that useful information can be
obtained from it.
</p>

<p>
Data is the information we collect.
Statistics is what we do with that information.
</p>

<h3>Example</h3>

<p>
Suppose five learners score:
</p>

<p><b>40, 50, 60, 70, 80</b></p>

<p>
These numbers are the <b>data</b>.
</p>

<p>
If we arrange them, calculate their mean, draw a graph, or compare the
learners'' performance, we are using <b>statistics</b>.
</p>

<h3>Think of the difference</h3>

<p>
<b>Data:</b> 40, 50, 60, 70, 80
</p>

<p>
<b>Statistical work:</b> organise the marks, calculate the mean,
represent them on a graph and interpret what they show.
</p>

<h3>Why statistics matters</h3>

<p>
A large collection of data can be difficult to understand by simply
looking at the individual values.
</p>

<p>
Statistics helps us turn those values into information that can be
compared, summarised and interpreted.
</p>
', 1),
  (183, 183, '
<h2>Primary Data</h2>

<p>
<b>Primary data</b> is data collected directly by the person or group
carrying out the investigation for the particular purpose of that
investigation.
</p>

<p>
The key question is:
<b>Did I collect the information myself for this investigation?</b>
</p>

<h3>Example 1</h3>

<p>
A learner wants to know the number of hours classmates study each day.
She asks 30 classmates and records their answers.
</p>

<p>
The answers are <b>primary data</b> because she collected them directly.
</p>

<h3>Example 2</h3>

<p>
A teacher measures the heights of learners in her class.
</p>

<p>
The measurements collected by the teacher are primary data.
</p>

<h3>Example 3</h3>

<p>
A researcher asks 100 households how many children live in each
household and records the responses.
</p>

<p>
The responses are primary data.
</p>

<h3>Important distinction</h3>

<p>
Primary data is about the <b>source and method of collection</b>, not
whether the information is numerical.
</p>
', 1),
  (184, 184, '
<h2>Secondary Data</h2>

<p>
<b>Secondary data</b> is data that has already been collected by another
person or organisation and is then used for a new investigation.
</p>

<p>
The key question is:
<b>Was the information already collected before I used it?</b>
</p>

<h3>Example 1</h3>

<p>
A learner wants to study Kenya''s population. Instead of counting the
population herself, she uses population figures from an official
published report.
</p>

<p>
The figures she uses are <b>secondary data</b>.
</p>

<h3>Example 2</h3>

<p>
A student uses rainfall records from a government report to study
rainfall patterns.
</p>

<p>
The student did not originally collect the rainfall measurements.
Therefore, the records are secondary data.
</p>

<h3>Example 3</h3>

<p>
A researcher uses results from an earlier survey published in a report.
</p>

<p>
Those results are secondary data for the new investigation.
</p>

<h3>The key difference</h3>

<p>
<b>Primary:</b> you collect the information directly.
</p>

<p>
<b>Secondary:</b> someone else has already collected it and you use it.
</p>
', 1),
  (185, 185, '
<h2>Qualitative Data</h2>

<p>
<b>Qualitative data</b> describes qualities or categories rather than
giving a numerical measurement of the characteristic.
</p>

<p>
It answers questions such as:
<b>What type?</b>
<b>Which category?</b>
<b>What kind?</b>
</p>

<h3>Example 1: Favourite subject</h3>

<p>
A survey records:
</p>

<p>
<b>Mathematics, English, Biology, Mathematics, Physics</b>
</p>

<p>
These are categories, not numerical measurements.
Therefore, the data is qualitative.
</p>

<h3>Example 2: Eye colour</h3>

<p>
A survey records:
</p>

<p>
<b>Brown, Brown, Black, Brown, Black</b>
</p>

<p>
Eye colour is a category, so this is qualitative data.
</p>

<h3>Example 3: Type of transport</h3>

<p>
A survey records:
</p>

<p>
<b>Bus, Walking, Bicycle, Bus, Motorcycle</b>
</p>

<p>
These are categories, so the data is qualitative.
</p>

<h3>Key idea</h3>

<p>
Qualitative data tells us <b>what category something belongs to</b>.
</p>
', 1),
  (186, 186, '
<h2>Quantitative Data</h2>

<p>
<b>Quantitative data</b> is data expressed as numbers representing
counts or measurements.
</p>

<p>
It answers questions such as:
<b>How many?</b>
<b>How much?</b>
<b>How long?</b>
<b>How heavy?</b>
</p>

<h3>Example 1: Number of siblings</h3>

<p>
A survey records:
</p>

<p><b>2, 1, 4, 3, 2</b></p>

<p>
These numbers represent counts, so they are quantitative data.
</p>

<h3>Example 2: Heights</h3>

<p>
A teacher records heights:
</p>

<p><b>150 cm, 162 cm, 155 cm, 170 cm</b></p>

<p>
These are numerical measurements, so they are quantitative data.
</p>

<h3>Example 3: Test marks</h3>

<p>
The marks:
</p>

<p><b>45, 61, 72, 58</b></p>

<p>
are quantitative data because they are numerical values.
</p>

<h3>Key idea</h3>

<p>
Quantitative data gives information that can be represented by
numbers as counts or measurements.
</p>
', 1),
  (187, 187, '
<h2>Discrete Data</h2>

<p>
<b>Discrete data</b> is quantitative data that consists of separate,
countable values.
</p>

<p>
The values usually come from <b>counting</b>.
</p>

<h3>Example 1: Number of learners</h3>

<p>
A classroom can have:
</p>

<p><b>30, 31, 32, 33</b> learners.</p>

<p>
We count learners. We cannot have 31.6 learners in the classroom.
Therefore, the number of learners is discrete data.
</p>

<h3>Example 2: Number of books</h3>

<p>
A learner may own:
</p>

<p><b>0, 1, 2, 3, 4...</b></p>

<p>
books.
</p>

<p>
Books are counted, so the number of books is discrete data.
</p>

<h3>Example 3: Number of goals</h3>

<p>
A football team may score:
</p>

<p><b>0, 1, 2, 3...</b></p>

<p>
goals.
</p>

<p>
Goals are counted, so the number of goals is discrete.
</p>

<h3>Key test</h3>

<p>
Ask:
<b>Am I counting separate objects or occurrences?</b>
</p>

<p>
If yes, the variable is usually discrete.
</p>
', 1),
  (188, 188, '
<h2>Continuous Data</h2>

<p>
<b>Continuous data</b> is quantitative data that can take any value
within a range because it is obtained by measurement.
</p>

<p>
A measurement can usually be made more precise by using smaller units.
</p>

<h3>Example 1: Height</h3>

<p>
A learner''s height might be:
</p>

<p><b>160 cm</b></p>

<p>
With a more precise measuring instrument it could be:
</p>

<p><b>160.4 cm</b></p>

<p>
or even:
</p>

<p><b>160.42 cm</b></p>

<p>
Therefore, height is continuous data.
</p>

<h3>Example 2: Time</h3>

<p>
A race may take:
</p>

<p><b>12 seconds</b>, <b>12.5 seconds</b>, or <b>12.53 seconds</b>.
</p>

<p>
Time is measured and can take values between whole numbers.
Therefore, time is continuous data.
</p>

<h3>Example 3: Mass</h3>

<p>
A bag may have a mass of:
</p>

<p><b>5 kg</b>, <b>5.2 kg</b>, or <b>5.27 kg</b>.
</p>

<p>
Mass is measured, so it is continuous data.
</p>

<h3>Key test</h3>

<p>
Ask:
<b>Is the value obtained by measuring something that can be more
precisely measured?</b>
</p>

<p>
If yes, it is usually continuous.
</p>
', 1),
  (189, 189, '
<h2>Population</h2>

<p>
In statistics, a <b>population</b> is the complete group of people,
objects or observations that a statistical investigation is concerned
with.
</p>

<p>
The population is the <b>whole group we want to study</b>.
</p>

<h3>Example 1</h3>

<p>
A school wants to know the average height of all learners in the school.
</p>

<p>
If the school has 1,200 learners, then the population is:
</p>

<p><b>all 1,200 learners in the school.</b></p>

<h3>Example 2</h3>

<p>
A researcher wants to study the number of hours Form 3 learners in a
school spend studying each day.
</p>

<p>
The population is:
</p>

<p><b>all Form 3 learners in that school.</b></p>

<h3>Example 3</h3>

<p>
A company wants to study the weights of all bags produced during one
day.
</p>

<p>
The population is:
</p>

<p><b>all bags produced that day.</b></p>

<h3>Important</h3>

<p>
The population is not necessarily a group of people.
It can consist of objects, measurements, events or other observations.
</p>

<h3>Key question</h3>

<p>
Ask:
<b>"What is the complete group I want to study?"</b>
</p>

<p>
That complete group is the population.
</p>
', 1),
  (190, 190, '
<h2>Sample</h2>

<p>
A <b>sample</b> is a smaller group selected from a population for the
purpose of carrying out a statistical investigation.
</p>

<p>
The key relationship is:
</p>

<p>
<b>Population = whole group</b>
</p>

<p>
<b>Sample = selected part of the population</b>
</p>

<h3>Example 1</h3>

<p>
A school has 1,000 learners. A researcher selects 100 learners to
investigate their study habits.
</p>

<p>
<b>Population:</b> all 1,000 learners.
</p>

<p>
<b>Sample:</b> the 100 selected learners.
</p>

<h3>Example 2</h3>

<p>
A factory produces 5,000 bottles in a week. A quality-control officer
checks 200 bottles.
</p>

<p>
<b>Population:</b> all 5,000 bottles.
</p>

<p>
<b>Sample:</b> the 200 bottles checked.
</p>

<h3>Example 3</h3>

<p>
A researcher wants to study all Form 3 learners in a county but cannot
ask every learner.
</p>

<p>
The researcher may select a smaller group of Form 3 learners.
That selected group is the sample.
</p>

<h3>Why use a sample?</h3>

<p>
Studying every member of a large population may require too much time,
money or effort.
</p>

<p>
A sample allows information to be collected from a smaller group.
</p>

<h3>Key question</h3>

<p>
Ask:
<b>"Is this the whole group, or only a selected part of it?"</b>
</p>

<p>
Whole group → <b>population</b>.
</p>

<p>
Selected part → <b>sample</b>.
</p>
', 1),
  (191, 191, '
<h2>Sources of Data</h2>

<p>
Before analysing data, we must first obtain it.
A <b>source of data</b> is where the information comes from.
</p>

<p>
The source may be:
</p>

<ul>
  <li>people,</li>
  <li>observations,</li>
  <li>measurements,</li>
  <li>records,</li>
  <li>reports, or</li>
  <li>other existing information.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Example 1: Asking learners</h3>

<p>
A teacher wants to know how learners travel to school.
She asks the learners directly.
</p>

<p>
The learners are the <b>source</b> of the information.
</p>

<h3>Example 2: Measuring heights</h3>

<p>
A researcher wants the heights of learners.
She measures each learner.
</p>

<p>
The learners and the measurements obtained from them provide the
source of the data.
</p>

<h3>Example 3: Existing records</h3>

<p>
A researcher wants to know the number of learners enrolled in a school
over the last five years.
The researcher uses the school''s admission records.
</p>

<p>
The school records are the source of the data.
</p>

<h3>Important distinction</h3>

<p>
A <b>source</b> tells us where the information comes from.
A <b>method</b> tells us how we obtain the information.
</p>

<p>
For example:
</p>

<p>
<b>Source:</b> learners
</p>

<p>
<b>Method:</b> questionnaire
</p>
', 1),
  (192, 192, '
<h2>Methods of Collecting Data</h2>

<p>
A <b>method of collecting data</b> is the way we obtain information
from its source.
</p>

<p>
Common methods include:
</p>

<ul>
  <li>questionnaires,</li>
  <li>interviews,</li>
  <li>observation,</li>
  <li>measurement, and</li>
  <li>experiments.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Example 1: Questionnaire</h3>

<p>
A teacher gives learners a form containing questions about the number
of hours they study each day.
</p>

<p>
The method is a <b>questionnaire</b>.
</p>

<h3>Example 2: Interview</h3>

<p>
A researcher asks farmers questions face-to-face and records their
answers.
</p>

<p>
The method is an <b>interview</b>.
</p>

<h3>Example 3: Observation</h3>

<p>
A researcher counts the number of vehicles passing a road in one hour
without asking the drivers.
</p>

<p>
The researcher is using <b>observation</b>.
</p>

<h3>Example 4: Measurement</h3>

<p>
A teacher uses a ruler to measure the heights of learners.
</p>

<p>
The method is <b>measurement</b>.
</p>

<h3>Key idea</h3>

<p>
Do not confuse the <b>source</b> with the <b>method</b>.
</p>

<p>
The source answers:
<b>"Where does the information come from?"</b>
</p>

<p>
The method answers:
<b>"How is the information obtained?"</b>
</p>
', 1),
  (193, 193, '
<h2>Tally Marks</h2>

<p>
A <b>tally mark</b> is a quick way of recording how many times an
observation occurs.
</p>

<p>
We normally record tallies in groups of five.
</p>

<p>
The first four observations are written as:
</p>

<p><b>| | | |</b></p>

<p>
The fifth observation crosses the first four:
</p>

<p><b>||||</b> with the fifth stroke crossing them.
</p>

<p>
This group represents <b>5</b>.
</p>

<h3>Example 1</h3>

<p>
Suppose the number 3 occurs seven times.
</p>

<p>
We record the first five as one group, then two more:
</p>

<p><b>||||/ ||</b></p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p><b>Frequency = 7</b></p>

<h3>Example 2</h3>

<p>
If a value occurs 10 times:
</p>

<p><b>||||/ ||||/</b></p>

<p>
There are two groups of five.
</p>

<p><b>Frequency = 10</b></p>

<h3>Example 3</h3>

<p>
If a value occurs 13 times:
</p>

<p><b>||||/ ||||/ |||</b></p>

<p>
That is:
</p>

<p>
5 + 5 + 3 = 13
</p>

<h3>Why use tally marks?</h3>

<p>
Tallies make it easier to count repeated observations without losing
track of the total.
</p>
', 1),
  (194, 194, '
<h2>Frequency</h2>

<p>
The <b>frequency</b> of a value is the number of times that value occurs
in a data set.
</p>

<p>
In simple terms:
</p>

<p>
<b>Frequency = number of occurrences</b>
</p>

<h3>Example 1</h3>

<p>
Consider:
</p>

<p><b>2, 4, 2, 5, 2, 7, 4</b></p>

<p>
The number 2 appears three times.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p><b>Frequency of 2 = 3</b></p>

<h3>Example 2</h3>

<p>
Consider:
</p>

<p><b>5, 5, 6, 7, 5, 8</b></p>

<p>
The number 5 occurs three times.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p><b>Frequency of 5 = 3</b></p>

<h3>Example 3</h3>

<p>
Consider:
</p>

<p><b>1, 3, 3, 4, 3, 6, 7, 3</b></p>

<p>
The number 3 occurs four times.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p><b>Frequency of 3 = 4</b></p>

<h3>Important</h3>

<p>
Do not confuse the <b>value</b> with its <b>frequency</b>.
</p>

<p>
For example, if 4 appears six times:
</p>

<p>
<b>Value = 4</b>
</p>

<p>
<b>Frequency = 6</b>
</p>
', 1),
  (195, 195, '
<h2>Frequency Distribution Table</h2>

<p>
A <b>frequency distribution table</b> organises data by showing each
value together with the number of times it occurs.
</p>

<p>
Instead of repeatedly searching through a long list, we can see the
frequency of each value in one place.
</p>

<h3>Example</h3>

<p>
Consider the data:
</p>

<p><b>2, 3, 2, 4, 3, 2, 5, 4, 3, 2</b></p>

<p>
First identify the different values:
</p>

<p><b>2, 3, 4, 5</b></p>

<p>
Then count how often each occurs.
</p>

<table>
<tr>
<th>Value</th>
<th>Frequency</th>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>2</td>
<td>4</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>3</td>
<td>3</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>4</td>
<td>2</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>5</td>
<td>1</td>
</tr>
</table>

<h3>Check the table</h3>

<p>
The frequencies should account for every observation.
</p>

<p>
4 + 3 + 2 + 1 = 10
</p>

<p>
There were 10 observations in the original data.
So the table accounts for all 10 observations.
</p>

<h3>Key idea</h3>

<p>
A frequency table changes a raw list into an organised summary.
</p>
', 1),
  (196, 196, '
<h2>Constructing an Ungrouped Frequency Table</h2>

<p>
An <b>ungrouped frequency table</b> lists individual data values and
the frequency of each value.
</p>

<p>
We can construct one using four steps.
</p>

<h3>Step 1: Write the raw data</h3>

<p>
Consider:
</p>

<p><b>3, 5, 3, 2, 5, 4, 3, 2, 5, 3</b></p>

<h3>Step 2: Identify each different value</h3>

<p>
The different values are:
</p>

<p><b>2, 3, 4, 5</b></p>

<h3>Step 3: Count each value</h3>

<p>
2 occurs 2 times.
</p>

<p>
3 occurs 4 times.
</p>

<p>
4 occurs 1 time.
</p>

<p>
5 occurs 3 times.
</p>

<h3>Step 4: Write the table</h3>

<table>
<tr>
<th>Value</th>
<th>Frequency</th>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>2</td>
<td>2</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>3</td>
<td>4</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>4</td>
<td>1</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>5</td>
<td>3</td>
</tr>
</table>

<h3>Final check</h3>

<p>
2 + 4 + 1 + 3 = 10
</p>

<p>
The raw data contains 10 observations, so the table is complete.
</p>

<h3>Important</h3>

<p>
Do not create a frequency table by guessing frequencies.
Every frequency must come from counting the original data.
</p>
', 1),
  (197, 197, '
<h2>Reading an Ungrouped Frequency Table</h2>

<p>
Once a frequency table has been constructed, we must be able to read
the information correctly.
</p>

<p>
Remember:
</p>

<p>
<b>Value</b> = the observation being recorded.
</p>

<p>
<b>Frequency</b> = how many times that observation occurs.
</p>

<h3>Example</h3>

<table>
<tr>
<th>Number of books</th>
<th>Frequency</th>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>0</td>
<td>2</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>1</td>
<td>5</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>2</td>
<td>7</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>3</td>
<td>4</td>
</tr>
</table>

<h3>Reading the table</h3>

<p>
The value 2 has frequency 7.
</p>

<p>
This means <b>7 learners have 2 books</b>.
</p>

<p>
The value 3 has frequency 4.
</p>

<p>
This means <b>4 learners have 3 books</b>.
</p>

<h3>Do not reverse them</h3>

<p>
If:
</p>

<p><b>2 → 7</b></p>

<p>
this does NOT mean seven books occurred twice.
</p>

<p>
It means the value <b>2 occurs 7 times</b>.
</p>
', 1),
  (198, 198, '
<h2>Total Frequency</h2>

<p>
The <b>total frequency</b> is the total number of observations in a
frequency table.
</p>

<p>
To find it, add all the frequencies.
</p>

<h3>Example 1</h3>

<table>
<tr>
<th>Value</th>
<th>Frequency</th>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>1</td>
<td>4</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>2</td>
<td>6</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>3</td>
<td>5</td>
</tr>
</table>

<p>
Total frequency:
</p>

<p>
4 + 6 + 5 = <b>15</b>
</p>

<p>
Therefore, the table represents 15 observations.
</p>

<h3>Example 2</h3>

<table>
<tr>
<th>Value</th>
<th>Frequency</th>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>5</td>
<td>3</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>6</td>
<td>7</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>7</td>
<td>2</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>8</td>
<td>4</td>
</tr>
</table>

<p>
Total frequency:
</p>

<p>
3 + 7 + 2 + 4 = <b>16</b>
</p>

<h3>Why total frequency matters</h3>

<p>
The total frequency provides a useful check.
If the original data contained 16 observations, the frequencies in the
table should add to 16.
</p>
', 1),
  (199, 199, '
<h2>Checking a Frequency Table</h2>

<p>
A frequency table should be checked before it is used for further
calculations.
</p>

<p>
One important check is:
</p>

<p>
<b>Total frequency = total number of observations.</b>
</p>

<h3>Example 1</h3>

<p>
Suppose the original data contains 12 observations.
A table gives frequencies:
</p>

<p><b>4, 3, 5</b></p>

<p>
Add them:
</p>

<p>
4 + 3 + 5 = 12
</p>

<p>
The table passes the total-frequency check.
</p>

<h3>Example 2</h3>

<p>
Suppose the original data contains 15 observations.
The table gives:
</p>

<p><b>4, 5, 3</b></p>

<p>
Add:
</p>

<p>
4 + 5 + 3 = 12
</p>

<p>
But the original data contains 15 observations.
</p>

<p>
Therefore, the table is incomplete or contains an error.
</p>

<h3>What the check catches</h3>

<p>
If the frequencies do not add to the number of observations, at least
one observation has been missed, counted incorrectly, or placed
incorrectly.
</p>

<h3>Important</h3>

<p>
A correct total does not prove that every individual frequency is
correct, but a wrong total definitely tells us that something is wrong.
</p>
', 1),
  (200, 200, '
<h2>Finding a Missing Frequency</h2>

<p>
Sometimes one frequency in a table is unknown.
If the total number of observations is known, we can find the missing
frequency.
</p>

<h3>Example 1</h3>

<table>
<tr>
<th>Value</th>
<th>Frequency</th>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>1</td>
<td>4</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>2</td>
<td>7</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>3</td>
<td>x</td>
</tr>
</table>

<p>
Suppose there are 15 observations altogether.
</p>

<p>
The known frequencies total:
</p>

<p>
4 + 7 = 11
</p>

<p>
So 11 observations have already been accounted for.
</p>

<p>
The remaining observations are:
</p>

<p>
15 - 11 = 4
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p><b>x = 4</b></p>

<h3>Example 2</h3>

<p>
A table represents 25 observations.
Three frequencies are 6, 8 and x.
</p>

<p>
First add the known frequencies:
</p>

<p>
6 + 8 = 14
</p>

<p>
The missing frequency must account for:
</p>

<p>
25 - 14 = 11
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p><b>x = 11</b></p>

<h3>The rule</h3>

<p>
<b>Missing frequency = total frequency - sum of known frequencies</b>
</p>

<p>
This works because all frequencies together must account for every
observation.
</p>
', 1),
  (201, 201, '
<h2>Meaning of Grouped Data</h2>

<p>
<b>Grouped data</b> is data that has been organised into groups called
<b>classes</b> instead of listing every individual value separately.
</p>

<p>
Grouping is useful when there are many different observations and a
long raw list would be difficult to read.
</p>

<h3>Example</h3>

<p>
Suppose the ages of 30 learners are recorded.
Instead of writing all 30 ages individually, we could group them as:
</p>

<table>
<tr>
<th>Age group</th>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>10–12</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>13–15</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>16–18</td>
</tr>
</table>

<p>
Each interval contains several possible ages.
</p>

<h3>Why group data?</h3>

<p>
Suppose a survey contains 500 measurements.
Writing every measurement separately can make the data difficult to
read.
</p>

<p>
Grouping allows us to see how many observations fall within different
ranges.
</p>

<h3>Important distinction</h3>

<p>
<b>Ungrouped:</b> individual values are shown separately.
</p>

<p>
<b>Grouped:</b> values are collected into intervals.
</p>

<h3>Example</h3>

<p>
Ungrouped:
</p>

<p><b>12, 13, 13, 14, 15, 16, 16, 17...</b></p>

<p>
Grouped:
</p>

<p><b>10–12, 13–15, 16–18...</b></p>

<p>
The second form is grouped data.
</p>
', 1),
  (202, 202, '
<h2>Class Interval</h2>

<p>
A <b>class interval</b> is a range of values used to group observations
in a frequency table.
</p>

<h3>Example</h3>

<p>
Consider these intervals:
</p>

<p><b>10–14</b></p>
<p><b>15–19</b></p>
<p><b>20–24</b></p>

<p>
Each one is a class interval.
</p>

<h3>Reading 10–14</h3>

<p>
The interval <b>10–14</b> represents values from 10 up to 14.
</p>

<p>
For whole-number data, it contains:
</p>

<p><b>10, 11, 12, 13 and 14.</b></p>

<h3>Reading 15–19</h3>

<p>
The interval <b>15–19</b> contains:
</p>

<p><b>15, 16, 17, 18 and 19.</b></p>

<h3>Why use intervals?</h3>

<p>
Instead of listing every individual value, we can tell the reader the
range into which each observation falls.
</p>

<h3>Important</h3>

<p>
A class interval is not a single value.
</p>

<p>
For example:
</p>

<p>
<b>12</b> is a value.
</p>

<p>
<b>10–14</b> is a class interval.
</p>
', 1),
  (203, 203, '
<h2>Class Limits</h2>

<p>
The <b>class limits</b> are the smallest and largest values stated in a
class interval.
</p>

<h3>Example 1</h3>

<p>
Consider:
</p>

<p><b>20–29</b></p>

<p>
The lower class limit is:
</p>

<p><b>20</b></p>

<p>
The upper class limit is:
</p>

<p><b>29</b></p>

<h3>Example 2</h3>

<p>
For:
</p>

<p><b>40–49</b></p>

<p>
Lower class limit = <b>40</b>
</p>

<p>
Upper class limit = <b>49</b>
</p>

<h3>Example 3</h3>

<p>
For:
</p>

<p><b>100–109</b></p>

<p>
Lower class limit = <b>100</b>
</p>

<p>
Upper class limit = <b>109</b>
</p>

<h3>Why are limits important?</h3>

<p>
They tell us exactly which values belong to a class.
</p>

<p>
For example, in the class <b>20–29</b>, the smallest included whole
number is 20 and the largest is 29.
</p>

<h3>Remember</h3>

<p>
<b>Lower limit = first value.</b>
</p>

<p>
<b>Upper limit = last value.</b>
</p>
', 1),
  (204, 204, '
<h2>Class Width</h2>

<p>
The <b>class width</b> tells us how wide each class interval is.
</p>

<p>
For intervals written using whole-number class limits, the class width
can be found by:
</p>

<p>
<b>Class width = upper class limit − lower class limit + 1</b>
</p>

<h3>Example 1</h3>

<p>
Consider:
</p>

<p><b>10–14</b></p>

<p>
Upper limit = 14
</p>

<p>
Lower limit = 10
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
14 − 10 + 1 = 5
</p>

<p>
So the class width is <b>5</b>.
</p>

<p>
The five values are:
</p>

<p><b>10, 11, 12, 13, 14</b></p>

<h3>Example 2</h3>

<p>
For:
</p>

<p><b>20–29</b></p>

<p>
29 − 20 + 1 = 10
</p>

<p>
Therefore, the class width is <b>10</b>.
</p>

<h3>Example 3</h3>

<p>
For:
</p>

<p><b>40–44</b></p>

<p>
44 − 40 + 1 = 5
</p>

<p>
Therefore, the class width is <b>5</b>.
</p>

<h3>Why add 1?</h3>

<p>
Because both end values are included.
</p>

<p>
For 10–14, simply doing 14 − 10 gives 4, but the interval contains
five whole numbers.
</p>
', 1),
  (205, 205, '
<h2>Non-Overlapping Class Intervals</h2>

<p>
Class intervals must be arranged so that one observation belongs to
only one class.
</p>

<p>
If two classes overlap, we cannot tell where some observations belong.
</p>

<h3>Incorrect example</h3>

<p>
Suppose we use:
</p>

<p><b>10–20</b></p>
<p><b>20–30</b></p>

<p>
Where should the value 20 go?
</p>

<p>
It could belong to the first class or the second class.
That creates ambiguity.
</p>

<h3>Better arrangement</h3>

<p>
For whole-number data, we could use:
</p>

<p><b>10–19</b></p>
<p><b>20–29</b></p>

<p>
Now every whole-number value has one clear class.
</p>

<h3>Another example</h3>

<p>
These intervals are non-overlapping:
</p>

<p><b>30–39</b></p>
<p><b>40–49</b></p>
<p><b>50–59</b></p>

<p>
The value 40 belongs only to 40–49.
</p>

<h3>Key idea</h3>

<p>
A well-constructed set of classes should assign each observation to one
and only one class.
</p>
', 1),
  (206, 206, '
<h2>Class Boundaries</h2>

<p>
<b>Class boundaries</b> are the values that separate neighbouring
classes when measured data is grouped into continuous intervals.
</p>

<p>
They remove gaps between classes.
</p>

<h3>Example</h3>

<p>
Suppose the classes are:
</p>

<p><b>10–19</b></p>
<p><b>20–29</b></p>

<p>
For continuous measurements, there is a boundary between 19 and 20.
The boundary is:
</p>

<p>
<b>19.5</b>
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
10–19 has boundaries <b>9.5 and 19.5</b>.
</p>

<p>
20–29 has boundaries <b>19.5 and 29.5</b>.
</p>

<h3>Why 19.5?</h3>

<p>
The values 19 and 20 are one unit apart.
The halfway point is:
</p>

<p>
(19 + 20) ÷ 2 = 39 ÷ 2 = <b>19.5</b>
</p>

<p>
This creates a continuous boundary.
</p>

<h3>Important</h3>

<p>
Class limits and class boundaries are not the same thing.
</p>

<p>
For 10–19:
</p>

<p>
<b>Limits:</b> 10 and 19
</p>

<p>
<b>Boundaries:</b> 9.5 and 19.5
</p>
', 1),
  (207, 207, '
<h2>Constructing Class Intervals</h2>

<p>
When grouping data, we need to create intervals that cover all relevant
values without overlapping.
</p>

<h3>Example</h3>

<p>
Suppose the smallest value is 12 and the largest value is 47.
We decide to use a class width of 10.
</p>

<p>
A suitable set of intervals is:
</p>

<p><b>10–19</b></p>
<p><b>20–29</b></p>
<p><b>30–39</b></p>
<p><b>40–49</b></p>

<h3>Why does this work?</h3>

<p>
The smallest value, 12, lies in 10–19.
</p>

<p>
The largest value, 47, lies in 40–49.
</p>

<p>
The classes do not overlap.
</p>

<p>
Each class has width 10.
</p>

<h3>Example 2</h3>

<p>
Suppose the data ranges from 23 to 61 and we want class width 10.
</p>

<p>
We can use:
</p>

<p><b>20–29</b></p>
<p><b>30–39</b></p>
<p><b>40–49</b></p>
<p><b>50–59</b></p>
<p><b>60–69</b></p>

<p>
The classes cover the entire range from 23 to 61.
</p>

<h3>Key idea</h3>

<p>
Good class intervals should:
</p>

<ul>
  <li>cover all observations,</li>
  <li>not overlap, and</li>
  <li>follow a consistent width when appropriate.</li>
</ul>
', 1),
  (208, 208, '
<h2>Class Midpoint</h2>

<p>
The <b>class midpoint</b> is the value halfway between the lower and
upper class limits of a class.
</p>

<p>
It is also called the <b>class mark</b>.
</p>

<p>
The formula is:
</p>

<p>
<b>Class midpoint = (lower limit + upper limit) ÷ 2</b>
</p>

<h3>Example 1</h3>

<p>
Find the midpoint of 10–19.
</p>

<p>
Midpoint = (10 + 19) ÷ 2
</p>

<p>
= 29 ÷ 2
</p>

<p>
= <b>14.5</b>
</p>

<h3>Example 2</h3>

<p>
Find the midpoint of 20–29.
</p>

<p>
Midpoint = (20 + 29) ÷ 2
</p>

<p>
= 49 ÷ 2
</p>

<p>
= <b>24.5</b>
</p>

<h3>Example 3</h3>

<p>
Find the midpoint of 40–49.
</p>

<p>
Midpoint = (40 + 49) ÷ 2
</p>

<p>
= 89 ÷ 2
</p>

<p>
= <b>44.5</b>
</p>

<h3>What does the midpoint mean?</h3>

<p>
It is the centre of the class interval.
It gives us one representative value for the class.
</p>
', 1),
  (209, 209, '
<h2>Cumulative Frequency</h2>

<p>
<b>Cumulative frequency</b> is the running total of frequencies as we
move through a frequency table.
</p>

<h3>Example</h3>

<table>
<tr>
<th>Value</th>
<th>Frequency</th>
<th>Cumulative Frequency</th>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>1</td>
<td>3</td>
<td>3</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>2</td>
<td>4</td>
<td>7</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>3</td>
<td>5</td>
<td>12</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>4</td>
<td>2</td>
<td>14</td>
</tr>
</table>

<h3>How the values were obtained</h3>

<p>
First cumulative frequency:
</p>

<p>
3
</p>

<p>
Second:
</p>

<p>
3 + 4 = <b>7</b>
</p>

<p>
Third:
</p>

<p>
7 + 5 = <b>12</b>
</p>

<p>
Fourth:
</p>

<p>
12 + 2 = <b>14</b>
</p>

<h3>What does 12 mean?</h3>

<p>
At the value 3, the cumulative frequency is 12.
This means there are <b>12 observations with values up to and including
3</b>.
</p>

<h3>Important</h3>

<p>
Cumulative frequency is a running total.
It never decreases as we move down the table.
</p>
', 1),
  (210, 210, '
<h2>Reading Cumulative Frequency</h2>

<p>
A cumulative frequency tells us how many observations have been
accounted for <b>up to a particular value or class</b>.
</p>

<h3>Example</h3>

<table>
<tr>
<th>Number of books</th>
<th>Frequency</th>
<th>Cumulative Frequency</th>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>0</td>
<td>3</td>
<td>3</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>1</td>
<td>5</td>
<td>8</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>2</td>
<td>4</td>
<td>12</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>3</td>
<td>3</td>
<td>15</td>
</tr>
</table>

<h3>Reading the table</h3>

<p>
At the value 1, the cumulative frequency is 8.
</p>

<p>
This means:
</p>

<p>
<b>8 learners have 1 book or fewer.</b>
</p>

<p>
At the value 2, the cumulative frequency is 12.
</p>

<p>
This means:
</p>

<p>
<b>12 learners have 2 books or fewer.</b>
</p>

<p>
At the value 3, the cumulative frequency is 15.
</p>

<p>
This means:
</p>

<p>
<b>15 learners have 3 books or fewer.</b>
</p>

<h3>Key phrase</h3>

<p>
When you see cumulative frequency, think:
</p>

<p>
<b>"How many observations are there up to this point?"</b>
</p>

<h3>Important</h3>

<p>
Cumulative frequency is not the frequency of the current value alone.
It includes all previous frequencies as well.
</p>
', 1),
  (211, 211, '
<h2>Meaning of the Mean</h2>

<p>
The <b>mean</b> is the value we get when the total of all the data values
is shared equally among all the observations.
</p>

<h3>The idea behind the mean</h3>

<p>
Imagine that four learners have these marks:
</p>

<p><b>4, 6, 8, 10</b></p>

<p>
The total is:
</p>

<p>
4 + 6 + 8 + 10 = <b>28</b>
</p>

<p>
There are 4 values.
If the total of 28 were shared equally among the 4 learners:
</p>

<p>
28 ÷ 4 = <b>7</b>
</p>

<p>
Therefore, the mean is <b>7</b>.
</p>

<p>
The mean does not have to be one of the original values.
It represents the value each observation would have if the total were
shared equally.
</p>

<h3>Mean formula</h3>

<p>
<b>Mean = Sum of all values ÷ Number of values</b>
</p>

<p>
Using symbols:
</p>

<p>
<b>Mean = Σx ÷ n</b>
</p>

<p>
where:
</p>

<ul>
<li><b>Σx</b> means the sum of all the values.</li>
<li><b>n</b> means the number of values.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
Find the mean of:
</p>

<p><b>3, 5, 7</b></p>

<p>
First add the values:
</p>

<p>
3 + 5 + 7 = <b>15</b>
</p>

<p>
There are 3 values.
</p>

<p>
Mean = 15 ÷ 3 = <b>5</b>
</p>

<p>
<b>Answer: 5</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
Find the mean of:
</p>

<p><b>6, 8, 10, 12</b></p>

<p>
Add the values:
</p>

<p>
6 + 8 + 10 + 12 = <b>36</b>
</p>

<p>
There are 4 values.
</p>

<p>
Mean = 36 ÷ 4 = <b>9</b>
</p>

<p>
<b>Answer: 9</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
Find the mean of:
</p>

<p><b>2, 4, 5, 9, 10</b></p>

<p>
Add the values:
</p>

<p>
2 + 4 + 5 + 9 + 10 = <b>30</b>
</p>

<p>
There are 5 values.
</p>

<p>
Mean = 30 ÷ 5 = <b>6</b>
</p>

<p>
<b>Answer: 6</b>
</p>

<h3>Important idea</h3>

<p>
The mean has two essential parts:
</p>

<p>
<b>Total of the values</b> and <b>number of values</b>.
</p>

<p>
If either part is wrong, the mean will also be wrong.
</p>
', 1),
  (212, 212, '
<h2>Finding the Mean from Raw Data</h2>

<p>
<b>Raw data</b> is data that has been listed without being organised
into a frequency table.
</p>

<p>
For example:
</p>

<p><b>12, 8, 15, 10, 5</b></p>

<p>
To find the mean of raw data, always follow these three steps:
</p>

<ol>
<li>Add all the values.</li>
<li>Count how many values there are.</li>
<li>Divide the total by the number of values.</li>
</ol>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
Find the mean of:
</p>

<p><b>7, 9, 4, 6</b></p>

<p>
<b>Step 1: Add the values</b></p>

<p>
7 + 9 + 4 + 6 = 26
</p>

<p>
<b>Step 2: Count the values</b></p>

<p>
There are 4 values.
</p>

<p>
<b>Step 3: Divide</b></p>

<p>
Mean = 26 ÷ 4
</p>

<p>
Mean = <b>6.5</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
Find the mean of:
</p>

<p><b>14, 11, 9, 16, 10</b></p>

<p>
Add:
</p>

<p>
14 + 11 + 9 + 16 + 10 = <b>60</b>
</p>

<p>
There are 5 values.
</p>

<p>
Mean = 60 ÷ 5 = <b>12</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
The marks of a learner in five tests are:
</p>

<p><b>12, 15, 18, 10, 20</b></p>

<p>
Find the mean mark.
</p>

<p>
First find the total:
</p>

<p>
12 + 15 + 18 + 10 + 20 = <b>75</b>
</p>

<p>
There are 5 tests.
</p>

<p>
Mean = 75 ÷ 5
</p>

<p>
Mean = <b>15</b>
</p>

<h3>Common mistake</h3>

<p>
Do not divide by the largest value.
Do not divide by the value of the mean you are trying to find.
</p>

<p>
Always divide by the <b>number of observations</b>.
</p>
', 1),
  (213, 213, '
<h2>Why Do We Divide by the Number of Values?</h2>

<p>
When calculating the mean, we divide the total by the number of
observations because the mean represents an <b>equal share</b>.
</p>

<h3>Example</h3>

<p>
Suppose three learners have:
</p>

<p><b>2, 5 and 8 books</b></p>

<p>
The total number of books is:
</p>

<p>
2 + 5 + 8 = <b>15</b>
</p>

<p>
There are 3 learners.
</p>

<p>
If the 15 books were shared equally:
</p>

<p>
15 ÷ 3 = <b>5</b>
</p>

<p>
Therefore the mean is 5.
</p>

<p>
We divide by 3 because there are <b>3 observations</b>.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
Find the mean of 4, 6 and 11.
</p>

<p>
Total:
</p>

<p>
4 + 6 + 11 = <b>21</b>
</p>

<p>
There are 3 observations.
</p>

<p>
Mean = 21 ÷ 3 = <b>7</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
Find the mean of 10, 20, 30 and 40.
</p>

<p>
Total:
</p>

<p>
10 + 20 + 30 + 40 = <b>100</b>
</p>

<p>
There are 4 observations.
</p>

<p>
Mean = 100 ÷ 4 = <b>25</b>
</p>

<p>
Notice that 25 was not in the original data.
That is perfectly acceptable.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
A set contains six values whose total is 72.
Find the mean.
</p>

<p>
We already know the total:
</p>

<p>
Σx = 72
</p>

<p>
There are 6 observations:
</p>

<p>
n = 6
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
Mean = 72 ÷ 6 = <b>12</b>
</p>

<h3>Key rule</h3>

<p>
<b>Mean = total ÷ number of observations.</b>
</p>

<p>
The denominator tells us how many equal shares the total is divided
into.
</p>
