<h2>Mean from an Ungrouped Frequency Table</h2>

<p>
A frequency table may contain the same value many times.
Instead of writing the value repeatedly, we record how many times
it occurs using its <b>frequency</b>.
</p>

<h3>Example</h3>

<table>
<tr><th>Value (x)</th><th>Frequency (f)</th></tr>
<tr><td>2</td><td>3</td></tr>
<tr><td>4</td><td>2</td></tr>
<tr><td>6</td><td>1</td></tr>
</table>

<p>
This means:
</p>

<ul>
<li>2 occurs 3 times.</li>
<li>4 occurs 2 times.</li>
<li>6 occurs 1 time.</li>
</ul>

<p>
The original data would therefore be:
</p>

<p><b>2, 2, 2, 4, 4, 6</b></p>

<p>
There are 6 observations.
</p>

<h3>Step 1: Find the total of all observations</h3>

<p>
2 + 2 + 2 + 4 + 4 + 6 = 20
</p>

<h3>Step 2: Divide by the number of observations</h3>

<p>
There are 6 observations.
</p>

<p>
Mean = 20 ÷ 6
</p>

<p>
Mean = <b>3.33...</b>
</p>

<p>
The important point is that frequency tells us how many times each
value must be counted.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<table>
<tr><th>x</th><th>f</th></tr>
<tr><td>1</td><td>2</td></tr>
<tr><td>3</td><td>3</td></tr>
</table>

<p>
The data represented is:
</p>

<p>
1, 1, 3, 3, 3
</p>

<p>
Total = 1 + 1 + 3 + 3 + 3 = <b>11</b>
</p>

<p>
Total frequency = 2 + 3 = <b>5</b>
</p>

<p>
Mean = 11 ÷ 5 = <b>2.2</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<table>
<tr><th>x</th><th>f</th></tr>
<tr><td>5</td><td>2</td></tr>
<tr><td>7</td><td>4</td></tr>
</table>

<p>
The value 5 occurs twice:
</p>

<p>
5 + 5 = 10
</p>

<p>
The value 7 occurs four times:
</p>

<p>
7 + 7 + 7 + 7 = 28
</p>

<p>
Total = 10 + 28 = <b>38</b>
</p>

<p>
Total frequency = 2 + 4 = <b>6</b>
</p>

<p>
Mean = 38 ÷ 6 = <b>6.33...</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<table>
<tr><th>x</th><th>f</th></tr>
<tr><td>10</td><td>1</td></tr>
<tr><td>20</td><td>2</td></tr>
<tr><td>30</td><td>1</td></tr>
</table>

<p>
Total:
</p>

<p>
10(1) + 20(2) + 30(1)
</p>

<p>
= 10 + 40 + 30
</p>

<p>
= <b>80</b>
</p>

<p>
Total frequency:
</p>

<p>
1 + 2 + 1 = <b>4</b>
</p>

<p>
Mean = 80 ÷ 4 = <b>20</b>
</p>
