<h2>Understanding <b>fx</b></h2>

<p>
When a value occurs many times, writing it repeatedly can become
tedious.
We use multiplication to find its total contribution.
</p>

<p>
If a value <b>x</b> occurs <b>f</b> times:
</p>

<p>
<b>fx = f × x</b>
</p>

<h3>Why do we multiply?</h3>

<p>
Suppose 7 occurs 4 times.
</p>

<p>
Instead of writing:
</p>

<p>
7 + 7 + 7 + 7
</p>

<p>
we can write:
</p>

<p>
4 × 7 = <b>28</b>
</p>

<p>
Therefore, <b>fx = 28</b>.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
A value is 5 and its frequency is 3.
</p>

<p>
x = 5
</p>

<p>
f = 3
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
fx = 3 × 5 = <b>15</b>
</p>

<p>
This means that the three 5s contribute 15 to the total.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<table>
<tr><th>x</th><th>f</th><th>fx</th></tr>
<tr><td>2</td><td>4</td><td>8</td></tr>
<tr><td>6</td><td>3</td><td>18</td></tr>
</table>

<p>
For x = 2:
</p>

<p>
fx = 4 × 2 = <b>8</b>
</p>

<p>
For x = 6:
</p>

<p>
fx = 3 × 6 = <b>18</b>
</p>

<p>
So the total contribution is:
</p>

<p>
8 + 18 = <b>26</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<table>
<tr><th>x</th><th>f</th><th>fx</th></tr>
<tr><td>3</td><td>2</td><td>6</td></tr>
<tr><td>5</td><td>4</td><td>20</td></tr>
<tr><td>8</td><td>1</td><td>8</td></tr>
</table>

<p>
Calculate each fx value:
</p>

<p>
3 × 2 = 6
</p>

<p>
5 × 4 = 20
</p>

<p>
8 × 1 = 8
</p>

<p>
Total fx = 6 + 20 + 8 = <b>34</b>
</p>

<p>
So <b>Σfx</b> means the total of all the fx values.
</p>

<h3>Important distinction</h3>

<p>
<b>f</b> tells us how many observations there are.
</p>

<p>
<b>x</b> tells us the value of each observation.
</p>

<p>
<b>fx</b> tells us the total contribution of that value.
</p>
