<p>
Middle positions:
</p>

<p>
4th and 5th.
</p>

<p>
Both lie within the cumulative frequency of 7 at x = 20.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p><b>Median = 20</b></p>
', 1),
  (229, 229, '
<h2>Comparing Median and Mean</h2>

<p>
The <b>mean</b> and <b>median</b> are both measures of central
tendency, but they are calculated differently.
</p>

<h3>Mean</h3>

<p>
The mean uses <b>every value</b> in the dataset.
</p>

<p>
<b>Mean = Total of values ÷ Number of values</b>
</p>

<h3>Median</h3>

<p>
The median depends on the <b>middle position</b> after the data has
been ordered.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
Consider:
</p>

<p><b>2, 4, 6, 8, 10</b></p>

<p>
Mean:
</p>

<p>
(2 + 4 + 6 + 8 + 10) ÷ 5
</p>

<p>
= 30 ÷ 5
</p>

<p>
= <b>6</b>
</p>

<p>
Median:
</p>

<p>
The middle value is <b>6</b>.
</p>

<p>
Here:
</p>

<p>
<b>Mean = Median = 6</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
Consider:
</p>

<p><b>2, 3, 4, 5, 26</b></p>

<p>
Mean:
</p>

<p>
(2 + 3 + 4 + 5 + 26) ÷ 5
</p>

<p>
= 40 ÷ 5
</p>

<p>
= <b>8</b>
</p>

<p>
Median:
</p>

<p>
The middle value is <b>4</b>.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
Mean = 8
</p>

<p>
Median = 4
</p>

<p>
The large value 26 increases the mean because the mean uses every
value.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
Consider:
</p>

<p><b>5, 6, 7, 8, 100</b></p>

<p>
Mean:
</p>

<p>
(5 + 6 + 7 + 8 + 100) ÷ 5
</p>

<p>
= 126 ÷ 5
</p>

<p>
= <b>25.2</b>
</p>

<p>
Median:
</p>

<p>
The middle value is <b>7</b>.
</p>

<p>
So:
</p>

<p>
Mean = 25.2
</p>

<p>
Median = 7
</p>

<h3>Important idea</h3>

<p>
A very large or very small value can pull the mean toward itself.
The median is based on position, so it is less affected by an
extreme value.
</p>
', 1),
  (230, 230, '
<h2>Choosing Between Mean and Median</h2>

<p>
Both the mean and median describe the centre of a dataset.
However, they do not always respond to data in the same way.
</p>

<h3>When the mean is useful</h3>

<p>
The mean is useful when we want to use <b>all the observations</b>
in the calculation and there are no extreme values causing a
misleading result.
</p>

<h3>When the median is useful</h3>

<p>
The median is useful when the dataset contains an unusually large
or unusually small value that could pull the mean away from the
centre of most observations.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
Consider the test marks:
</p>

<p><b>45, 48, 50, 52, 55</b></p>

<p>
Mean:
</p>

<p>
(45 + 48 + 50 + 52 + 55) ÷ 5
</p>

<p>
= 250 ÷ 5
</p>

<p>
= <b>50</b>
</p>

<p>
Median = <b>50</b>
</p>

<p>
The two measures agree.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
Consider monthly values:
</p>

<p><b>10, 11, 12, 13, 100</b></p>

<p>
Mean:
</p>

<p>
(10 + 11 + 12 + 13 + 100) ÷ 5
</p>

<p>
= 146 ÷ 5
</p>

<p>
= <b>29.2</b>
</p>

<p>
Median = <b>12</b>
</p>

<p>
Most values are near 10–13, but the mean has been pulled upward
by 100.
</p>

<p>
The median better represents the centre of the ordinary values in
this particular dataset.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
Suppose five observations are:
</p>

<p><b>20, 21, 22, 23, 24</b></p>

<p>
Mean:
</p>

<p>
110 ÷ 5 = <b>22</b>
</p>

<p>
Median = <b>22</b>
</p>

<p>
There is no extreme value pulling the centre away from the rest,
so the mean gives a good description of the centre.
</p>

<h3>Decision rule</h3>

<p>
Do not automatically say that the median is always better than the
mean.
</p>

<p>
Instead ask:
</p>

<ol>
<li>Are there extreme values?</li>
<li>Do those values strongly affect the mean?</li>
<li>Which measure better represents the centre of this particular
dataset?</li>
</ol>

<p>
The correct choice depends on the data.
</p>
', 1),
  (231, 231, '
<h2>Meaning of the Mode</h2>

<p>
The <b>mode</b> is the value that occurs <b>most frequently</b>
in a dataset.
</p>

<p>
In other words, the mode is the value with the highest frequency.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
Find the mode of:
</p>

<p><b>2, 4, 4, 5, 7</b></p>

<p>
Count how many times each value occurs:
</p>

<ul>
<li>2 occurs once.</li>
<li>4 occurs twice.</li>
<li>5 occurs once.</li>
<li>7 occurs once.</li>
</ul>

<p>
The value occurring most often is 4.
</p>

<p><b>Mode = 4</b></p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
Find the mode of:
</p>

<p><b>3, 6, 6, 8, 6, 10</b></p>

<p>
The value 6 occurs three times.
</p>

<p>
The other values occur only once.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p><b>Mode = 6</b></p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
Find the mode of:
</p>

<p><b>5, 7, 8, 7, 9, 7, 10</b></p>

<p>
Count the occurrences:
</p>

<ul>
<li>5 → 1</li>
<li>7 → 3</li>
<li>8 → 1</li>
<li>9 → 1</li>
<li>10 → 1</li>
</ul>

<p>
The highest frequency is 3.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p><b>Mode = 7</b></p>

<h3>Important distinction</h3>

<p>
The mode is not necessarily the largest value.
</p>

<p>
It is the value that occurs <b>most often</b>.
</p>

<p>
For example, in:
</p>

<p><b>2, 2, 3, 4, 10</b></p>

<p>
10 is the largest value, but 2 is the mode.
</p>
', 1),
  (232, 232, '
<h2>Finding the Mode from Raw Data</h2>

<p>
Raw data is data listed as individual observations rather than in a
frequency table.
</p>

<p>
To find the mode:
</p>

<ol>
<li>Look at the values.</li>
<li>Count how many times each repeated value occurs.</li>
<li>Identify the value with the greatest frequency.</li>
</ol>

<p>
You do <b>not</b> need to calculate a mean or arrange the data to
find the mode.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
Find the mode:
</p>

<p><b>4, 2, 7, 4, 9, 4, 6</b></p>

<p>
Count:
</p>

<ul>
<li>2 → 1</li>
<li>4 → 3</li>
<li>6 → 1</li>
<li>7 → 1</li>
<li>9 → 1</li>
</ul>

<p>
The highest frequency is 3.
</p>

<p><b>Mode = 4</b></p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
Find the mode:
</p>

<p><b>8, 3, 8, 5, 6, 3, 8</b></p>

<p>
8 occurs three times.
</p>

<p>
3 occurs twice.
</p>

<p>
The other values occur once.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p><b>Mode = 8</b></p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
Find the mode:
</p>

<p><b>10, 12, 10, 15, 12, 10, 18, 12</b></p>

<p>
Count:
</p>

<ul>
<li>10 → 3</li>
<li>12 → 3</li>
<li>15 → 1</li>
<li>18 → 1</li>
</ul>

<p>
Two values have the same highest frequency.
</p>

<p>
Therefore the dataset has <b>two modes</b>:
</p>

<p><b>10 and 12</b></p>

<h3>Important</h3>

<p>
A dataset can have:
</p>

<ul>
<li>one mode,</li>
<li>more than one mode, or</li>
<li>no mode if every value occurs equally often.</li>
</ul>
', 1),
  (233, 233, '
<h2>Mode from an Ungrouped Frequency Table</h2>

<p>
In a frequency table, the mode is the value with the
<b>highest frequency</b>.
</p>

<p>
This makes finding the mode easier because the frequencies have
already been counted.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<table>
<tr><th>Value (x)</th><th>Frequency (f)</th></tr>
<tr><td>2</td><td>3</td></tr>
<tr><td>4</td><td>5</td></tr>
<tr><td>6</td><td>2</td></tr>
</table>

<p>
The frequencies are:
</p>

<p>
3, 5 and 2.
</p>

<p>
The highest frequency is 5.
</p>

<p>
That frequency belongs to x = 4.
</p>

<p><b>Mode = 4</b></p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<table>
<tr><th>x</th><th>f</th></tr>
<tr><td>10</td><td>2</td></tr>
<tr><td>20</td><td>7</td></tr>
<tr><td>30</td><td>4</td></tr>
<tr><td>40</td><td>3</td></tr>
</table>

<p>
The largest frequency is 7.
</p>

<p>
Frequency 7 belongs to x = 20.
</p>

<p><b>Mode = 20</b></p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<table>
<tr><th>x</th><th>f</th></tr>
<tr><td>1</td><td>4</td></tr>
<tr><td>2</td><td>6</td></tr>
<tr><td>3</td><td>6</td></tr>
<tr><td>4</td><td>2</td></tr>
</table>

<p>
The highest frequency is 6.
</p>

<p>
Both x = 2 and x = 3 have frequency 6.
</p>

<p>
Therefore the dataset has two modes:
</p>

<p><b>2 and 3</b></p>

<h3>Key rule</h3>

<p>
Do not choose the largest value in the x-column.
</p>

<p>
Look for the <b>largest frequency</b>, then read the corresponding
value.
</p>
', 1),
  (234, 234, '
<h2>Identifying the Modal Class</h2>

<p>
When data is grouped into class intervals, we cannot identify the
exact mode from the grouped table because the individual values
inside each class are unknown.
</p>

<p>
Instead, we identify the <b>modal class</b>.
</p>

<p>
The modal class is the class interval with the
<b>highest frequency</b>.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<table>
<tr><th>Class</th><th>Frequency</th></tr>
<tr><td>0–9</td><td>3</td></tr>
<tr><td>10–19</td><td>8</td></tr>
<tr><td>20–29</td><td>5</td></tr>
</table>

<p>
The highest frequency is 8.
</p>

<p>
Frequency 8 belongs to class 10–19.
</p>

<p><b>Modal class = 10–19</b></p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<table>
<tr><th>Class</th><th>Frequency</th></tr>
<tr><td>5–9</td><td>4</td></tr>
<tr><td>10–14</td><td>6</td></tr>
<tr><td>15–19</td><td>9</td></tr>
<tr><td>20–24</td><td>3</td></tr>
</table>

<p>
The highest frequency is 9.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p><b>Modal class = 15–19</b></p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<table>
<tr><th>Class</th><th>Frequency</th></tr>
<tr><td>10–19</td><td>7</td></tr>
<tr><td>20–29</td><td>11</td></tr>
<tr><td>30–39</td><td>11</td></tr>
<tr><td>40–49</td><td>4</td></tr>
</table>

<p>
The highest frequency is 11.
</p>

<p>
Two classes have frequency 11:
</p>

<p>
20–29 and 30–39.
</p>

<p>
Therefore there are two modal classes.
</p>

<h3>Important distinction</h3>

<p>
<b>Ungrouped data:</b> identify the mode itself.
</p>

<p>
<b>Grouped data:</b> identify the modal class.
</p>

<p>
Do not invent an exact mode when the grouped table does not provide
the individual observations.
</p>
', 1),
  (235, 235, '
<h2>Comparing Mean, Median and Mode</h2>

<p>
The <b>mean</b>, <b>median</b> and <b>mode</b> all describe the
centre or typical behaviour of a dataset, but they answer different
questions.
</p>

<h3>Mean</h3>

<p>
The mean is the total of all values divided by the number of values.
</p>

<p>
<b>Mean = Σx ÷ n</b>
</p>

<h3>Median</h3>

<p>
The median is the middle value after the data has been arranged.
</p>

<h3>Mode</h3>

<p>
The mode is the value that occurs most frequently.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
Consider:
</p>

<p><b>2, 3, 3, 4, 8</b></p>

<p>
Mean:
</p>

<p>
(2 + 3 + 3 + 4 + 8) ÷ 5
</p>

<p>
= 20 ÷ 5
</p>

<p>
= <b>4</b>
</p>

<p>
Median:
</p>

<p>
The middle value is <b>3</b>.
</p>

<p>
Mode:
</p>

<p>
3 occurs twice, more than any other value.
</p>

<p>
<b>Mode = 3</b>
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
Mean = 4
</p>

<p>
Median = 3
</p>

<p>
Mode = 3
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
Consider:
</p>

<p><b>4, 5, 5, 5, 6</b></p>

<p>
Mean:
</p>

<p>
(4 + 5 + 5 + 5 + 6) ÷ 5
</p>

<p>
= 25 ÷ 5
</p>

<p>
= <b>5</b>
</p>

<p>
Median = <b>5</b>
</p>

<p>
Mode = <b>5</b>
</p>

<p>
All three measures are equal.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
Consider:
</p>

<p><b>1, 2, 2, 3, 12</b></p>

<p>
Mean:
</p>

<p>
(1 + 2 + 2 + 3 + 12) ÷ 5
</p>

<p>
= 20 ÷ 5
</p>

<p>
= <b>4</b>
</p>

<p>
Median = <b>2</b>
</p>

<p>
Mode = <b>2</b>
</p>

<p>
The extreme value 12 raises the mean.
</p>

<h3>What each measure asks</h3>

<ul>
<li><b>Mean:</b> What is the equal-share value?</li>
<li><b>Median:</b> What is the middle value?</li>
<li><b>Mode:</b> What value occurs most often?</li>
</ul>
', 1),
  (236, 236, '
<h2>Meaning of Range</h2>

<p>
The <b>range</b> measures how far apart the smallest and largest
values in a dataset are.
</p>

<p>
The formula is:
</p>

<p>
<b>Range = Largest value − Smallest value</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
Find the range of:
</p>

<p><b>3, 5, 8, 10, 12</b></p>

<p>
Largest value = 12
</p>

<p>
Smallest value = 3
</p>

<p>
Range = 12 − 3
</p>

<p>
= <b>9</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
Find the range of:
</p>

<p><b>18, 7, 12, 25, 10</b></p>

<p>
Largest value = 25
</p>

<p>
Smallest value = 7
</p>

<p>
Range = 25 − 7
</p>

<p>
= <b>18</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
Find the range of:
</p>

<p><b>42, 42, 42, 42</b></p>

<p>
Largest = 42
</p>

<p>
Smallest = 42
</p>

<p>
Range = 42 − 42
</p>

<p>
= <b>0</b>
</p>

<p>
A range of zero means all the values are the same.
</p>

<h3>Important</h3>

<p>
Range is a measure of <b>spread</b>, not a measure of the centre.
</p>

<p>
Mean, median and mode describe central tendency.
Range tells us how far apart the extreme values are.
</p>
', 1),
  (237, 237, '
<h2>Finding Range from Raw Data</h2>

<p>
For raw data, finding the range requires only two values:
</p>

<p>
<b>the largest value</b> and <b>the smallest value</b>.
</p>

<p>
Then subtract:
</p>

<p>
<b>Range = Largest − Smallest</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
Find the range:
</p>

<p><b>12, 5, 18, 7, 10</b></p>

<p>
Largest = 18
</p>

<p>
Smallest = 5
</p>

<p>
Range = 18 − 5
</p>

<p>
= <b>13</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
Find the range:
</p>

<p><b>30, 14, 22, 9, 41, 17</b></p>

<p>
Largest = 41
</p>

<p>
Smallest = 9
</p>

<p>
Range = 41 − 9
</p>

<p>
= <b>32</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
Find the range:
</p>

<p><b>−4, 2, −1, 7, 5</b></p>

<p>
Largest = 7
</p>

<p>
Smallest = −4
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
Range = 7 − (−4)
</p>

<p>
= 7 + 4
</p>

<p>
= <b>11</b>
</p>

<h3>Common mistake with negative values</h3>

<p>
If the smallest value is negative, subtracting it means adding its
positive amount.
</p>

<p>
For example:
</p>

<p>
8 − (−3) = 8 + 3 = 11
</p>
', 1),
  (238, 238, '
<h2>Range from a Frequency Table</h2>

<p>
In an ungrouped frequency table, the range is determined from the
smallest and largest <b>values</b>, not from their frequencies.
</p>

<p>
The formula remains:
</p>

<p>
<b>Range = Largest value − Smallest value</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<table>
<tr><th>x</th><th>f</th></tr>
<tr><td>2</td><td>4</td></tr>
<tr><td>5</td><td>3</td></tr>
<tr><td>9</td><td>2</td></tr>
</table>

<p>
Smallest value = 2
</p>

<p>
Largest value = 9
</p>

<p>
Range = 9 − 2
</p>

<p>
= <b>7</b>
</p>

<p>
Notice that the frequencies do not enter the range calculation.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<table>
<tr><th>x</th><th>f</th></tr>
<tr><td>10</td><td>2</td></tr>
<tr><td>15</td><td>6</td></tr>
<tr><td>20</td><td>4</td></tr>
<tr><td>30</td><td>1</td></tr>
</table>

<p>
Smallest = 10
</p>

<p>
Largest = 30
</p>

<p>
Range = 30 − 10
</p>

<p>
= <b>20</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<table>
<tr><th>x</th><th>f</th></tr>
<tr><td>−3</td><td>2</td></tr>
<tr><td>1</td><td>5</td></tr>
<tr><td>6</td><td>3</td></tr>
</table>

<p>
Largest = 6
</p>

<p>
Smallest = −3
</p>

<p>
Range = 6 − (−3)
</p>

<p>
= 6 + 3
</p>

<p>
= <b>9</b>
</p>

<h3>Important distinction</h3>

<p>
The frequency tells us <b>how many</b> observations have a value.
</p>

<p>
The range uses <b>which values</b> are smallest and largest.
</p>
', 1),
  (239, 239, '
<h2>Comparing Two Data Sets Using Range</h2>

<p>
The range can be used to compare the spread of two datasets.
</p>

<p>
A <b>larger range</b> means the data stretches farther between its
smallest and largest values.
</p>

<p>
A <b>smaller range</b> means the extreme values are closer together.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
Dataset A:
</p>

<p><b>4, 6, 8, 10</b></p>

<p>
Range A:
</p>

<p>
10 − 4 = <b>6</b>
</p>

<p>
Dataset B:
</p>

<p><b>2, 6, 8, 14</b></p>

<p>
Range B:
</p>

<p>
14 − 2 = <b>12</b>
</p>

<p>
Therefore B has the greater range.
</p>

<p>
B has greater spread according to the range.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
Dataset A:
</p>

<p><b>10, 12, 14, 16</b></p>

<p>
Range = 16 − 10 = <b>6</b>
</p>

<p>
Dataset B:
</p>

<p><b>20, 21, 22, 23</b></p>

<p>
Range = 23 − 20 = <b>3</b>
</p>

<p>
Dataset A has the larger range.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
Dataset A:
</p>

<p><b>5, 5, 5, 5</b></p>

<p>
Range = 0.
</p>

<p>
Dataset B:
</p>

<p><b>2, 5, 8, 11</b></p>

<p>
Range = 11 − 2 = 9.
</p>

<p>
Dataset B has greater spread.
</p>

<h3>Important</h3>

<p>
Range only compares the smallest and largest values.
It does not tell us how the observations are distributed between
those extremes.
</p>
', 1),
  (240, 240, '
<h2>Effect of an Extreme Value on Range</h2>

<p>
The range depends entirely on the smallest and largest values.
Therefore, an extreme value can change the range dramatically.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
Original data:
</p>

<p><b>4, 5, 6, 7, 8</b></p>

<p>
Largest = 8
</p>

<p>
Smallest = 4
</p>

<p>
Range = 8 − 4 = <b>4</b>
</p>

<p>
Now add an extreme value of 20:
</p>

<p><b>4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 20</b></p>

<p>
Largest = 20
</p>

<p>
Smallest = 4
</p>

<p>
New range = 20 − 4 = <b>16</b>
</p>

<p>
The range increased from 4 to 16.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
Original data:
</p>

<p><b>10, 12, 14, 16</b></p>

<p>
Range = 16 − 10 = <b>6</b>
</p>

<p>
Add −5:
</p>

<p><b>−5, 10, 12, 14, 16</b></p>

<p>
New range:
</p>

<p>
16 − (−5)
</p>

<p>
= 16 + 5
</p>

<p>
= <b>21</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
Suppose the smallest value and largest value remain unchanged.
Adding a value that lies between them does not change the range.
</p>

<p>
Original:
</p>

<p><b>5, 10, 15</b></p>

<p>
Range = 15 − 5 = <b>10</b>
</p>

<p>
Add 12:
</p>

<p><b>5, 10, 12, 15</b></p>

<p>
New range = 15 − 5 = <b>10</b>
</p>

<p>
The range did not change.
</p>

<h3>The rule</h3>

<p>
Adding a value changes the range only if it becomes the new
smallest or new largest value.
</p>

<p>
If the new value lies between the existing extremes, the range
stays the same.
</p>
', 1),
  (241, 241, '
<h2>Reading a Bar Graph</h2>

<p>
A <b>bar graph</b> represents data using separate rectangular bars.
The height of each bar shows the frequency or quantity for that category.
</p>

<p>
The categories are normally written along the horizontal axis,
while the frequency or quantity is shown on the vertical axis.
</p>

<h3>How to read a bar graph</h3>

<ol>
  <li>Identify what the horizontal axis represents.</li>
  <li>Identify what the vertical axis represents.</li>
  <li>Read the scale on the vertical axis.</li>
  <li>Find the bar for the required category.</li>
  <li>Read the height of that bar.</li>
</ol>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
Suppose a bar graph shows the number of books read by four learners:
</p>

<table border="1" cellpadding="6">
<tr><th>Learner</th><th>Books</th></tr>
<tr><td>A</td><td>4</td></tr>
<tr><td>B</td><td>7</td></tr>
<tr><td>C</td><td>5</td></tr>
<tr><td>D</td><td>3</td></tr>
</table>

<p><b>Question:</b> How many books did learner B read?</p>

<p>
The bar for B reaches 7 on the vertical scale.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
<br>
<b>Books read = 7</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
A bar graph shows the number of learners choosing four subjects:
</p>

<table border="1" cellpadding="6">
<tr><th>Subject</th><th>Learners</th></tr>
<tr><td>Math</td><td>18</td></tr>
<tr><td>English</td><td>15</td></tr>
<tr><td>Biology</td><td>12</td></tr>
<tr><td>Physics</td><td>10</td></tr>
</table>

<p><b>Question:</b> Which subject has the highest number of learners?</p>

<p>
Compare the frequencies:
</p>

<p>
18, 15, 12, 10
</p>

<p>
The greatest value is 18.
Therefore the tallest bar represents <b>Math</b>.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
A bar graph has the following values:
</p>

<table border="1" cellpadding="6">
<tr><th>Day</th><th>Visitors</th></tr>
<tr><td>Monday</td><td>20</td></tr>
<tr><td>Tuesday</td><td>35</td></tr>
<tr><td>Wednesday</td><td>25</td></tr>
</table>

<p><b>Question:</b> How many more visitors came on Tuesday than Monday?</p>

<p>
Tuesday = 35
<br>
Monday = 20
</p>

<p>
Difference:
</p>

<p>
35 − 20 = <b>15</b>
</p>

<p>
Therefore, Tuesday had <b>15 more visitors</b>.
</p>

<h3>Key idea</h3>

<p>
When reading a bar graph, do not guess the value from the appearance of the bar.
Read the scale carefully and match the top of the bar with the vertical axis.
</p>
  ', 1),
  (242, 242, '
<h2>Constructing a Bar Graph</h2>

<p>
A bar graph is constructed by turning the values in a table into bars.
Each category gets its own separate bar.
</p>

<h3>Steps</h3>

<ol>
  <li>Write the categories on the horizontal axis.</li>
  <li>Write the frequency or quantity on the vertical axis.</li>
  <li>Choose a suitable scale.</li>
  <li>Draw the axes.</li>
  <li>Draw one bar for each category.</li>
  <li>Make each bar reach the correct value.</li>
  <li>Keep equal spaces between separate bars.</li>
</ol>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<table border="1" cellpadding="6">
<tr><th>Fruit</th><th>Number sold</th></tr>
<tr><td>Apples</td><td>10</td></tr>
<tr><td>Bananas</td><td>15</td></tr>
<tr><td>Oranges</td><td>8</td></tr>
</table>

<p>
The largest value is 15.
A convenient vertical scale is:
</p>

<p>
0, 5, 10, 15
</p>

<p>
The bars therefore have heights:
</p>

<p>
Apples = 10
<br>
Bananas = 15
<br>
Oranges = 8
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<table border="1" cellpadding="6">
<tr><th>House</th><th>Learners</th></tr>
<tr><td>Red</td><td>20</td></tr>
<tr><td>Blue</td><td>30</td></tr>
<tr><td>Green</td><td>25</td></tr>
</table>

<p>
The largest value is 30.
</p>

<p>
A suitable scale is:
</p>

<p>
0, 5, 10, 15, 20, 25, 30
</p>

<p>
The Blue bar must reach 30,
the Green bar must reach 25,
and the Red bar must reach 20.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<table border="1" cellpadding="6">
<tr><th>Day</th><th>Visitors</th></tr>
<tr><td>Monday</td><td>6</td></tr>
<tr><td>Tuesday</td><td>12</td></tr>
<tr><td>Wednesday</td><td>9</td></tr>
<tr><td>Thursday</td><td>15</td></tr>
</table>

<p>
The largest frequency is 15.
</p>

<p>
A scale of 0, 5, 10, 15 is suitable.
</p>

<p>
Therefore the four bars must reach:
</p>

<p>
Monday → 6
<br>
Tuesday → 12
<br>
Wednesday → 9
<br>
Thursday → 15
</p>

<h3>Important</h3>

<p>
A bar graph must have a clear scale.
If the scale changes irregularly, the graph can give a false impression of the data.
</p>
  ', 1),
  (243, 243, '
<h2>Reading a Histogram</h2>

<p>
A <b>histogram</b> represents grouped numerical data using adjoining bars.
The classes are placed along the horizontal axis and the frequencies are shown vertically.
</p>

<p>
Unlike the separated bars of a bar graph, histogram bars <b>touch</b>
because the class intervals represent continuous or consecutive numerical ranges.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<table border="1" cellpadding="6">
<tr><th>Class</th><th>Frequency</th></tr>
<tr><td>0–9</td><td>4</td></tr>
<tr><td>10–19</td><td>7</td></tr>
<tr><td>20–29</td><td>5</td></tr>
</table>

<p>
If the histogram is drawn from this table:
</p>

<ul>
<li>The 0–9 bar has height 4.</li>
<li>The 10–19 bar has height 7.</li>
<li>The 20–29 bar has height 5.</li>
</ul>

<p>
The tallest bar is therefore the class <b>10–19</b>.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
A histogram has class frequencies:
</p>

<table border="1" cellpadding="6">
<tr><th>Class</th><th>Frequency</th></tr>
<tr><td>10–19</td><td>3</td></tr>
<tr><td>20–29</td><td>9</td></tr>
<tr><td>30–39</td><td>6</td></tr>
</table>

<p>
The question asks:
<b>How many observations lie in the class 20–29?</b>
</p>

<p>
Read the height of the 20–29 bar.
</p>

<p>
Height = 9
</p>

<p>
Therefore, <b>9 observations</b> lie in that class.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
Suppose the histogram represents ages:
</p>

<table border="1" cellpadding="6">
<tr><th>Age class</th><th>Frequency</th></tr>
<tr><td>10–14</td><td>5</td></tr>
<tr><td>15–19</td><td>8</td></tr>
<tr><td>20–24</td><td>4</td></tr>
</table>

<p>
The most frequent age class is found by locating the tallest bar.
</p>

<p>
The frequencies are:
</p>

<p>
5, 8, 4
</p>

<p>
The largest is 8.
</p>

<p>
Therefore the modal class is <b>15–19</b>.
</p>

<h3>Key idea</h3>

<p>
When reading a histogram, connect the height of a bar to the class interval directly below it.
</p>
  ', 1),
  (244, 244, '
<h2>Constructing a Histogram</h2>

<p>
A histogram is constructed from grouped numerical data.
The class intervals form the horizontal axis and the frequencies determine the heights.
</p>

<h3>Steps</h3>

<ol>
  <li>Write the class intervals on the horizontal axis.</li>
  <li>Write frequency on the vertical axis.</li>
  <li>Choose a suitable frequency scale.</li>
  <li>Convert class limits to boundaries when necessary.</li>
  <li>Draw adjoining bars.</li>
  <li>Make each bar reach the correct frequency.</li>
</ol>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<table border="1" cellpadding="6">
<tr><th>Class</th><th>Frequency</th></tr>
<tr><td>10–19</td><td>4</td></tr>
<tr><td>20–29</td><td>7</td></tr>
<tr><td>30–39</td><td>5</td></tr>
</table>

<p>
For continuous histogram boundaries:
</p>

<p>
10–19 becomes 9.5–19.5
<br>
20–29 becomes 19.5–29.5
<br>
30–39 becomes 29.5–39.5
</p>

<p>
The bars must have heights:
</p>

<p>
4, 7 and 5 respectively.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<table border="1" cellpadding="6">
<tr><th>Height (cm)</th><th>Frequency</th></tr>
<tr><td>140–149</td><td>3</td></tr>
<tr><td>150–159</td><td>8</td></tr>
<tr><td>160–169</td><td>6</td></tr>
</table>

<p>
The class 150–159 has the greatest frequency.
</p>

<p>
Therefore its bar must be the tallest and must reach frequency 8.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<table border="1" cellpadding="6">
<tr><th>Marks</th><th>Frequency</th></tr>
<tr><td>0–9</td><td>2</td></tr>
<tr><td>10–19</td><td>6</td></tr>
<tr><td>20–29</td><td>10</td></tr>
<tr><td>30–39</td><td>7</td></tr>
</table>

<p>
The largest frequency is 10.
A suitable vertical scale is:
</p>

<p>
0, 2, 4, 6, 8, 10
</p>

<p>
The bars therefore have heights:
</p>

<p>
2, 6, 10 and 7.
</p>

<h3>Important</h3>

<p>
Histogram bars touch.
There should not be spaces between adjacent class intervals.
</p>
  ', 1),
  (245, 245, '
<h2>Bar Graph versus Histogram</h2>

<p>
A bar graph and a histogram both use rectangular bars,
but they are used for different kinds of data.
</p>

<h3>Bar Graph</h3>

<p>
A bar graph is normally used for <b>categories</b>.
The categories are separate, so spaces are left between the bars.
</p>

<p>
Example:
</p>

<p>
Subjects → Mathematics, English, Biology, Physics
</p>

<p>
These are separate categories.
</p>

<h3>Histogram</h3>

<p>
A histogram is used for <b>grouped numerical data</b>.
The class intervals are consecutive, so the bars touch.
</p>

<p>
Example:
</p>

<p>
10–19, 20–29, 30–39
</p>

<p>
These intervals represent numerical ranges.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
A school records the number of learners who prefer:
</p>

<p>
Football, Basketball, Volleyball and Athletics.
</p>

<p>
These are categories.
</p>

<p>
Therefore a <b>bar graph</b> is appropriate.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
A teacher records marks in classes:
</p>

<p>
0–9, 10–19, 20–29, 30–39.
</p>

<p>
These are numerical intervals.
</p>

<p>
Therefore a <b>histogram</b> is appropriate.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
A graph contains separate bars for:
</p>

<p>
Red House = 20
<br>
Blue House = 25
<br>
Green House = 18
</p>

<p>
House names are categories.
The bars are therefore separated.
This is a <b>bar graph</b>.
</p>

<h3>Remember</h3>

<table border="1" cellpadding="6">
<tr>
<th>Bar Graph</th>
<th>Histogram</th>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Categorical data</td>
<td>Grouped numerical data</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Bars separated</td>
<td>Bars touch</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Separate categories</td>
<td>Numerical intervals</td>
</tr>
</table>
  ', 1),
  (246, 246, '
<h2>Reading a Frequency Polygon</h2>

<p>
A <b>frequency polygon</b> represents grouped data using points plotted at class midpoints.
The points are then joined with straight lines.
</p>

<p>
To read a frequency polygon, connect each plotted point to:
</p>

<p>
<b>class midpoint → frequency</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<table border="1" cellpadding="6">
<tr><th>Class</th><th>Midpoint</th><th>Frequency</th></tr>
<tr><td>0–9</td><td>4.5</td><td>3</td></tr>
<tr><td>10–19</td><td>14.5</td><td>7</td></tr>
<tr><td>20–29</td><td>24.5</td><td>5</td></tr>
</table>

<p>
The plotted points are:
</p>

<p>
(4.5, 3), (14.5, 7), (24.5, 5)
</p>

<p>
Therefore, at midpoint 14.5, the frequency is 7.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
A point on a frequency polygon is at:
</p>

<p>
(35, 9)
</p>

<p>
The first coordinate is the class midpoint.
The second coordinate is the frequency.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
Midpoint = 35
<br>
Frequency = 9
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
Three plotted points have frequencies:
</p>

<p>
4, 10 and 6.
</p>

<p>
The highest point has frequency 10.
Therefore the class represented by that midpoint has the greatest frequency.
</p>

<h3>Key idea</h3>

<p>
In a frequency polygon:
</p>

<p>
<b>Horizontal position → class midpoint</b>
<br>
<b>Vertical position → frequency</b>
</p>
  ', 1),
  (247, 247, '
<h2>Constructing a Frequency Polygon</h2>

<p>
A frequency polygon is constructed by finding the midpoint of each class,
plotting the midpoint against its frequency, and joining the points.
</p>

<h3>Steps</h3>

<ol>
  <li>Write the class intervals.</li>
  <li>Find the midpoint of every class.</li>
  <li>Use midpoint as the horizontal coordinate.</li>
  <li>Use frequency as the vertical coordinate.</li>
  <li>Plot the points.</li>
  <li>Join the points using straight lines.</li>
</ol>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<table border="1" cellpadding="6">
<tr><th>Class</th><th>Frequency</th></tr>
<tr><td>0–9</td><td>4</td></tr>
<tr><td>10–19</td><td>7</td></tr>
<tr><td>20–29</td><td>5</td></tr>
</table>

<p>
Find each midpoint:
</p>

<p>
0–9:
<br>
(0 + 9) ÷ 2 = 4.5
</p>

<p>
10–19:
<br>
(10 + 19) ÷ 2 = 14.5
</p>

<p>
20–29:
<br>
(20 + 29) ÷ 2 = 24.5
</p>

<p>
The points are therefore:
</p>

<p>
(4.5, 4), (14.5, 7), (24.5, 5)
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<table border="1" cellpadding="6">
<tr><th>Class</th><th>Frequency</th></tr>
<tr><td>20–29</td><td>3</td></tr>
<tr><td>30–39</td><td>8</td></tr>
<tr><td>40–49</td><td>6</td></tr>
</table>

<p>
Midpoints:
</p>

<p>
20–29 → (20 + 29) ÷ 2 = 24.5
<br>
30–39 → (30 + 39) ÷ 2 = 34.5
<br>
40–49 → (40 + 49) ÷ 2 = 44.5
</p>

<p>
Points:
</p>

<p>
(24.5, 3), (34.5, 8), (44.5, 6)
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<table border="1" cellpadding="6">
<tr><th>Class</th><th>Frequency</th></tr>
<tr><td>50–59</td><td>5</td></tr>
<tr><td>60–69</td><td>10</td></tr>
<tr><td>70–79</td><td>4</td></tr>
</table>

<p>
Midpoints:
</p>

<p>
50–59 → 54.5
<br>
60–69 → 64.5
<br>
70–79 → 74.5
</p>

<p>
Therefore the plotted points are:
</p>

<p>
(54.5, 5), (64.5, 10), (74.5, 4)
</p>

<h3>Important</h3>

<p>
Do not plot the upper or lower class limits as the horizontal coordinate.
The frequency polygon uses the <b>class midpoint</b>.
</p>
  ', 1),
  (248, 248, '
<h2>Interpreting a Frequency Polygon</h2>

<p>
A frequency polygon allows us to identify where frequencies are high or low
and compare the distribution across different class intervals.
</p>

<p>
The important rule is:
</p>

<p>
<b>Higher point = higher frequency.</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<table border="1" cellpadding="6">
<tr><th>Midpoint</th><th>Frequency</th></tr>
<tr><td>14.5</td><td>3</td></tr>
<tr><td>24.5</td><td>8</td></tr>
<tr><td>34.5</td><td>5</td></tr>
</table>

<p>
The highest frequency is 8.
</p>

<p>
It occurs at midpoint 24.5.
</p>

<p>
Therefore the class centred at 24.5 has the greatest frequency.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
A frequency polygon has frequencies:
</p>

<p>
2, 5, 11, 7, 3
</p>

<p>
The largest frequency is 11.
</p>

<p>
Therefore the highest point occurs at the midpoint associated with frequency 11.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
Suppose two consecutive points have frequencies 12 and 4.
</p>

<p>
The second point is much lower.
</p>

<p>
This means the number of observations in the second class is much smaller than in the first class.
</p>

<p>
Difference:
</p>

<p>
12 − 4 = <b>8</b>
</p>

<p>
Therefore the second class has 8 fewer observations.
</p>

<h3>Important</h3>

<p>
A frequency polygon does not tell us the exact individual values.
It shows the frequencies associated with class midpoints.
</p>
  ', 1),
  (249, 249, '
<h2>Comparing Two Data Representations</h2>

<p>
The same dataset can sometimes be represented in different ways.
When comparing two representations, check whether they show the same
frequencies and identify what each representation makes easier to see.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
Suppose a grouped table contains:
</p>

<table border="1" cellpadding="6">
<tr><th>Class</th><th>Frequency</th></tr>
<tr><td>0–9</td><td>4</td></tr>
<tr><td>10–19</td><td>8</td></tr>
<tr><td>20–29</td><td>5</td></tr>
</table>

<p>
A histogram for the data should have bar heights:
</p>

<p>
4, 8 and 5.
</p>

<p>
A frequency polygon for the same data should have points whose vertical
coordinates are also:
</p>

<p>
4, 8 and 5.
</p>

<p>
Therefore both representations show the same frequencies.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
Representation A shows frequencies:
</p>

<p>
6, 10, 7
</p>

<p>
Representation B shows frequencies:
</p>

<p>
6, 10, 7
</p>

<p>
The two representations agree because corresponding frequencies are equal.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
Representation A shows frequencies:
</p>

<p>
5, 8, 12
</p>

<p>
Representation B shows:
</p>

<p>
5, 7, 12
</p>

<p>
The representations do not agree.
</p>

<p>
The difference is in the middle class:
</p>

<p>
8 − 7 = <b>1</b>
</p>

<p>
Therefore one representation has one extra observation in that class.
</p>

<h3>Key idea</h3>

<p>
When comparing representations, compare corresponding classes and frequencies.
Do not compare only the visual appearance.
</p>
  ', 1),
  (250, 250, '
<h2>Choosing the Appropriate Data Representation</h2>

<p>
Different types of data require different forms of representation.
The choice should depend on what the data are and what we want the graph to show.
</p>

<h3>Bar Graph</h3>

<p>
Use a bar graph when comparing separate categories.
</p>

<p>
Example:
</p>

<p>
Favourite sports:
Football, Athletics, Basketball, Volleyball.
</p>

<p>
These are separate categories.
A bar graph is appropriate.
</p>

<h3>Histogram</h3>

<p>
Use a histogram when numerical data have been grouped into class intervals.
</p>

<p>
Example:
</p>

<p>
Marks:
0–9, 10–19, 20–29, 30–39.
</p>

<p>
A histogram is appropriate.
</p>

<h3>Frequency Polygon</h3>

<p>
Use a frequency polygon to represent grouped frequency data by plotting
frequency against class midpoint and joining the points.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
A school wants to show the number of learners in:
</p>

<p>
Form 1, Form 2, Form 3 and Form 4.
</p>

<p>
These are separate categories.
</p>

<p>
Therefore a <b>bar graph</b> is appropriate.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
A teacher groups examination marks into:
</p>

<p>
0–9, 10–19, 20–29, 30–39, 40–49.
</p>

<p>
These are numerical class intervals.
</p>

<p>
Therefore a <b>histogram</b> is appropriate.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
A teacher wants to show how the frequency changes from one class midpoint
to the next for grouped marks.
</p>

<p>
A <b>frequency polygon</b> is appropriate because it shows the frequencies
as connected points at the class midpoints.
</p>

<h3>Decision rule</h3>

<table border="1" cellpadding="6">
<tr>
<th>Data</th>
<th>Suitable representation</th>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Separate categories</td>
<td>Bar graph</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Grouped numerical intervals</td>
<td>Histogram</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Grouped frequencies using class midpoints</td>
<td>Frequency polygon</td>
</tr>
</table>

<h3>Important</h3>

<p>
Do not choose a graph because it looks attractive.
Choose it because it matches the structure of the data.
</p>
  ', 1);
INSERT INTO public.lesson_versions (id, lesson_id, content, version) VALUES
  (251, 251, '
<h2>Probability Experiment</h2>

<p>
A <b>probability experiment</b> is an action that we perform where the
outcome is not known with certainty before the action is performed.
</p>

<p>
For example, if we toss a coin, we know that the result will be either
<b>Head</b> or <b>Tail</b>, but we do not know which one will occur before
the coin is tossed.
</p>

<h3>What makes an experiment a probability experiment?</h3>

<p>
There are two important ideas:
</p>

<ol>
  <li>We know the possible outcomes.</li>
  <li>We do not know which outcome will occur before performing the experiment.</li>
</ol>

<h3>Example 1: Tossing a coin</h3>

<p>
Suppose we toss a coin once.
</p>

<p>
Before tossing it, we know that the possible outcomes are:
</p>

<p><b>Head or Tail</b></p>

<p>
However, we cannot know with certainty whether the result will be Head
or Tail before tossing the coin.
</p>

<p>
Therefore, tossing a coin is a probability experiment.
</p>

<h3>Example 2: Rolling a die</h3>

<p>
Suppose we roll a fair six-sided die once.
</p>

<p>
The possible outcomes are:
</p>

<p>
1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6
</p>

<p>
We know these possible outcomes, but we cannot know which number will
appear before rolling the die.
</p>

<p>
Therefore, rolling a die is a probability experiment.
</p>

<h3>Example 3: Drawing a ball</h3>

<p>
A bag contains red and blue balls. One ball is drawn without looking.
</p>

<p>
Before drawing, we know the possible colours.
</p>

<p>
But we do not know which colour will actually be drawn.
</p>

<p>
Therefore, drawing a ball is a probability experiment.
</p>

<h3>Important idea</h3>

<p>
A probability experiment does not mean that the result is completely
unknown. We know the possible outcomes; what we do not know beforehand
is which outcome will occur.
</p>
', 1),
  (252, 252, '
<h2>Outcomes of an Experiment</h2>

<p>
An <b>outcome</b> is one possible result of a probability experiment.
</p>

<p>
Before calculating probability, we need to know what outcomes are possible.
</p>

<h3>Example 1: One coin toss</h3>

<p>
If a coin is tossed once, there are two possible outcomes:
</p>

<p>
<b>Head (H)</b> and <b>Tail (T)</b>
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
Outcomes = {H, T}
</p>

<h3>Example 2: One die roll</h3>

<p>
A standard die has six faces.
</p>

<p>
The possible outcomes are:
</p>

<p>
{1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6}
</p>

<p>
Each number represents one possible result of the experiment.
</p>

<h3>Example 3: Drawing a coloured ball</h3>

<p>
A bag contains red, blue and green balls.
One ball is drawn.
</p>

<p>
The possible colour outcomes are:
</p>

<p>
{Red, Blue, Green}
</p>

<p>
The individual balls may be different, but if we are interested only
in colour, these are the outcomes we record.
</p>

<h3>Why identifying outcomes matters</h3>

<p>
Probability calculations depend on knowing what can happen.
If an outcome is forgotten, the probability calculation can become wrong.
</p>

<p>
Therefore, before calculating a probability, ask:
</p>

<p><b>"What are all the possible outcomes?"</b></p>
', 1),
  (253, 253, '
<h2>Recording Experimental Results</h2>

<p>
When we repeat a probability experiment, the results should be recorded
so that we can count how often each outcome occurs.
</p>

<h3>Example 1: Tossing a coin</h3>

<p>
Suppose a coin is tossed 10 times and the results are:
</p>

<p>
H, T, H, H, T, T, H, T, H, H
</p>

<p>
Count the results:
</p>

<p>
Heads = 6
</p>

<p>
Tails = 4
</p>

<p>
Total tosses:
</p>

<p>
6 + 4 = 10
</p>

<h3>Example 2: Rolling a die</h3>

<p>
Suppose a die is rolled 12 times and the results are:
</p>

<p>
2, 4, 1, 6, 2, 3, 2, 5, 4, 2, 6, 1
</p>

<p>
Count each outcome:
</p>

<p>
1 → 2 times
</p>

<p>
2 → 4 times
</p>

<p>
3 → 1 time
</p>

<p>
4 → 2 times
</p>

<p>
5 → 1 time
</p>

<p>
6 → 2 times
</p>

<p>
Check the total:
</p>

<p>
2 + 4 + 1 + 2 + 1 + 2 = 12
</p>

<p>
The total frequency must equal the total number of trials.
</p>

<h3>Example 3: Why record frequencies?</h3>

<p>
Suppose a coin is tossed 20 times and Head occurs 13 times.
</p>

<p>
The frequency of Head is therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>13</b>
</p>

<p>
The total number of trials is:
</p>

<p>
<b>20</b>
</p>

<p>
These two numbers will later allow us to calculate the experimental
probability of getting Head.
</p>
', 1),
  (254, 254, '
<h2>Calculating Experimental Probability</h2>

<p>
<b>Experimental probability</b> is the probability estimated from results
actually obtained when an experiment is performed.
</p>

<p>
The formula is:
</p>

<p>
<b>
Experimental Probability =
Number of times the outcome occurred
÷
Total number of trials
</b>
</p>

<h3>Example 1: Coin</h3>

<p>
A coin is tossed 20 times.
Heads occur 12 times.
Find the experimental probability of getting Head.
</p>

<p>
Number of Heads = 12
</p>

<p>
Total tosses = 20
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
P(Head) = 12 ÷ 20
</p>

<p>
P(Head) = 3/5
</p>

<p>
So the experimental probability is <b>3/5</b>.
</p>

<h3>Example 2: Die</h3>

<p>
A die is rolled 30 times.
The number 6 appears 4 times.
Find the experimental probability of getting 6.
</p>

<p>
P(6) = 4 ÷ 30
</p>

<p>
P(6) = 2/15
</p>

<p>
Therefore, the experimental probability is <b>2/15</b>.
</p>

<h3>Example 3: Coloured balls</h3>

<p>
A ball is selected from a bag 40 times, with the ball replaced each time.
A red ball is obtained 18 times.
Find the experimental probability of selecting red.
</p>

<p>
P(Red) = 18 ÷ 40
</p>

<p>
P(Red) = 9/20
</p>

<p>
Therefore, the experimental probability of red is <b>9/20</b>.
</p>

<h3>The key idea</h3>

<p>
Do not divide by the number of times the outcome could have occurred.
Divide by the <b>total number of trials actually performed</b>.
</p>
', 1),
  (255, 255, '
<h2>Comparing Experimental and Theoretical Probability</h2>

<p>
<b>Theoretical probability</b> is the probability we expect from the
possible outcomes of an experiment.
</p>

<p>
<b>Experimental probability</b> is the probability obtained from actually
performing the experiment.
</p>

<h3>Example 1: One coin</h3>

<p>
For a fair coin, there are two equally likely outcomes:
</p>

<p>
Head and Tail.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
Theoretical P(Head) = 1/2
</p>

<p>
Suppose we toss the coin 10 times and obtain 7 Heads.
</p>

<p>
Experimental P(Head) = 7/10
</p>

<p>
Notice:
</p>

<p>
7/10 ≠ 1/2
</p>

<p>
This does not automatically mean that the experiment is wrong.
The number of trials is small, so the observed results may differ from
the theoretical probability.
</p>

<h3>Example 2: More trials</h3>

<p>
Suppose another experiment uses 100 tosses of the same fair coin and
produces 52 Heads.
</p>

<p>
Experimental probability:
</p>

<p>
P(Head) = 52/100
</p>

<p>
P(Head) = 0.52
</p>

<p>
The theoretical probability is:
</p>

<p>
P(Head) = 0.5
</p>

<p>
The experimental result, 0.52, is now closer to 0.5.
</p>

<h3>Example 3: What happens as trials increase?</h3>

<p>
Imagine repeating the same fair-coin experiment many more times.
The experimental probability will generally tend to get closer to the
theoretical probability.
</p>

<p>
This does not mean that every experiment must produce exactly the
theoretical probability.
</p>

<p>
For example, 10 tosses do not have to produce exactly 5 Heads.
</p>

<h3>Remember</h3>

<p>
<b>Theoretical probability</b> comes from the possible outcomes.
</p>

<p>
<b>Experimental probability</b> comes from actual observations.
</p>

<p>
They can be different, especially when the number of trials is small.
With many trials, experimental results generally become more stable and
tend towards the theoretical probability.
</p>
', 1),
  (256, 256, '
<h2>Complete Probability Space</h2>

<h3>ONE CONCEPT</h3>
<p>
A <b>probability space</b> is the complete collection of all possible outcomes
of an experiment.
</p>

<p>
The important word is <b>complete</b>.
We must include every outcome that can happen and must not include an outcome
that cannot happen.
</p>

<h3>WHY DO WE NEED IT?</h3>

<p>
Before finding the probability of an event, we need to know what can happen.
If we leave out a possible outcome, our probability calculation can become wrong.
</p>

<h3>Example 1: Tossing one coin</h3>

<p>
A coin has two possible results:
</p>

<p>
<b>H</b> = Head<br>
<b>T</b> = Tail
</p>

<p>
Therefore the complete probability space is:
</p>

<p>
<b>S = {H, T}</b>
</p>

<p>
There are no other possible results in an ordinary coin toss.
</p>

<h3>Example 2: Rolling one die</h3>

<p>
A standard die has six faces numbered 1 to 6.
Therefore the possible outcomes are:
</p>

<p>
<b>S = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6}</b>
</p>

<p>
We include all six numbers because any of them can appear.
</p>

<h3>Example 3: Choosing one letter from A, B and C</h3>

<p>
The possible choices are A, B and C.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>S = {A, B, C}</b>
</p>

<p>
The set contains exactly the possible outcomes.
</p>

<h3>CHECK YOUR THINKING</h3>

<p>
Whenever you are asked to give a probability space, ask:
</p>

<ol>
<li>What experiment is being performed?</li>
<li>What can happen in that experiment?</li>
<li>Have I included every possible outcome?</li>
<li>Have I included anything impossible?</li>
</ol>
', 1),
  (257, 257, '
<h2>Listing Outcomes of a Single Experiment</h2>

<h3>ONE CONCEPT</h3>
<p>
When an experiment has several possible results, we can list the outcomes
one by one to construct its probability space.
</p>

<p>
The goal is not to guess the outcomes.
We determine what the experiment allows to happen, then list those outcomes.
</p>

<h3>Example 1: Rolling a die</h3>

<p>
A standard die has six faces.
</p>

<p>
Starting with the smallest face and moving upward:
</p>

<p>
1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>S = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6}</b>
</p>

<h3>Example 2: Selecting a vowel</h3>

<p>
Suppose one letter is selected from the set:
</p>

<p>
<b>{A, E, I, O, U}</b>
</p>

<p>
The experiment is selecting one letter.
The possible outcomes are therefore:
</p>

<p>
A, E, I, O and U.
</p>

<p>
Hence:
</p>

<p>
<b>S = {A, E, I, O, U}</b>
</p>

<h3>Example 3: Choosing a number</h3>

<p>
A number is chosen from 1, 2, 3 and 4.
</p>

<p>
We inspect the choices one at a time:
</p>

<p>
1 → possible<br>
2 → possible<br>
3 → possible<br>
4 → possible
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>S = {1, 2, 3, 4}</b>
</p>

<h3>IMPORTANT</h3>

<p>
Do not list outcomes based on what you think is likely.
A probability space contains what <b>can happen</b>, not what you expect to happen.
</p>
', 1),
  (258, 258, '
<h2>Probability Space for Two Coin Tosses</h2>

<h3>ONE CONCEPT</h3>
<p>
When an experiment is performed twice, the outcome must show the result of
<b>each trial</b> and the order matters.
</p>

<p>
For two coin tosses, we cannot simply write H and T.
We must show what happened on the first toss and what happened on the second toss.
</p>

<h3>Step 1: First toss</h3>

<p>
The first coin toss can produce:
</p>

<p>
H or T
</p>

<h3>Step 2: Second toss</h3>

<p>
The second coin toss can also produce:
</p>

<p>
H or T
</p>

<h3>Step 3: Combine them</h3>

<p>
If the first toss is H, the second toss can be H or T:
</p>

<p>
HH, HT
</p>

<p>
If the first toss is T, the second toss can be H or T:
</p>

<p>
TH, TT
</p>

<p>
Therefore the complete probability space is:
</p>

<p>
<b>S = {HH, HT, TH, TT}</b>
</p>

<h3>Why are HT and TH different?</h3>

<p>
HT means:
</p>

<p>
First toss = H<br>
Second toss = T
</p>

<p>
TH means:
</p>

<p>
First toss = T<br>
Second toss = H
</p>

<p>
The results are in different orders, so they are different outcomes.
</p>

<h3>Example 2: Three possible outcomes checked</h3>

<p>
Suppose someone writes:
</p>

<p>
{HH, HT, TT}
</p>

<p>
This is incomplete because <b>TH</b> can also occur.
</p>

<h3>Example 3: Building the list systematically</h3>

<p>
Start with H on the first toss:
</p>

<p>
HH, HT
</p>

<p>
Then start with T on the first toss:
</p>

<p>
TH, TT
</p>

<p>
So:
</p>

<p>
<b>S = {HH, HT, TH, TT}</b>
</p>

<p>
This method prevents us from accidentally leaving out an outcome.
</p>
', 1),
  (259, 259, '
<h2>Systematically Listing Two-Stage Outcomes</h2>

<h3>ONE CONCEPT</h3>
<p>
For an experiment performed in two stages, we can list outcomes systematically
by fixing the first stage and then listing every possible result of the second stage.
</p>

<p>
This is important because simply guessing combinations can cause outcomes to be missed.
</p>

<h3>Example 1: Two coin tosses</h3>

<p>
First toss = H.
</p>

<p>
The second toss can be H or T:
</p>

<p>
HH, HT
</p>

<p>
Now first toss = T.
</p>

<p>
The second toss can be H or T:
</p>

<p>
TH, TT
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>S = {HH, HT, TH, TT}</b>
</p>

<h3>Example 2: A die followed by a coin</h3>

<p>
First stage: roll a die.
</p>

<p>
Possible first results:
</p>

<p>
1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6
</p>

<p>
Second stage: toss a coin.
</p>

<p>
Possible second results:
</p>

<p>
H or T
</p>

<p>
Now attach both possible coin results to each die result:
</p>

<p>
1H, 1T<br>
2H, 2T<br>
3H, 3T<br>
4H, 4T<br>
5H, 5T<br>
6H, 6T
</p>

<p>
Therefore there are 12 possible outcomes.
</p>

<h3>Example 3: Why systematic listing matters</h3>

<p>
Suppose the first stage has A or B and the second stage has 1 or 2.
</p>

<p>
Start with A:
</p>

<p>
A1, A2
</p>

<p>
Then start with B:
</p>

<p>
B1, B2
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>S = {A1, A2, B1, B2}</b>
</p>

<p>
We did not guess the combinations. We built them from the choices available at
each stage.
</p>
', 1),
  (260, 260, '
<h2>Theoretical Probability from a Probability Space</h2>

<h3>ONE CONCEPT</h3>
<p>
Once we know the complete probability space, we can find the theoretical
probability of an event when the outcomes are <b>equally likely</b>.
</p>

<h3>The rule</h3>

<p>
<b>
Probability of an event
=
Number of favourable outcomes
÷
Total number of possible outcomes
</b>
</p>

<p>
The denominator comes from the <b>complete probability space</b>.
The numerator counts the outcomes that satisfy the event we are interested in.
</p>

<h3>Example 1: Rolling a die</h3>

<p>
Probability space:
</p>

<p>
<b>S = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6}</b>
</p>

<p>
Suppose the event is getting a 4.
</p>

<p>
There is one favourable outcome:
</p>

<p>
<b>{4}</b>
</p>

<p>
There are six possible outcomes altogether.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
P(4) = 1/6
</p>

<h3>Example 2: Getting an even number</h3>

<p>
The probability space is:
</p>

<p>
<b>S = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6}</b>
</p>

<p>
The even outcomes are:
</p>

<p>
<b>{2, 4, 6}</b>
</p>

<p>
There are 3 favourable outcomes and 6 total outcomes.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
P(even) = 3/6 = 1/2
</p>

<h3>Example 3: Two coin tosses</h3>

<p>
The probability space is:
</p>

<p>
<b>S = {HH, HT, TH, TT}</b>
</p>

<p>
Suppose we want the probability of getting two heads.
</p>

<p>
Only HH satisfies the event.
</p>

<p>
Favourable outcomes = 1.
</p>

<p>
Total outcomes = 4.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>P(two heads) = 1/4</b>
</p>

<h3>THE KEY IDEA</h3>

<p>
Do not choose the denominator randomly.
It comes directly from the complete probability space.
</p>

<p>
If there are 6 equally likely outcomes, the denominator is 6.
If there are 4 equally likely outcomes, the denominator is 4.
</p>
', 1),
  (261, 261, '
<h2>Impossible Events</h2>

<h3>ONE CONCEPT</h3>

<p>
An <b>impossible event</b> is an event that cannot happen in a particular experiment.
</p>

<p>
Its probability is:
</p>

<p>
<b>P(impossible event) = 0</b>
</p>

<h3>Why is its probability 0?</h3>

<p>
Probability compares:
</p>

<p>
<b>favourable outcomes</b> with <b>all possible outcomes</b>.
</p>

<p>
For an impossible event, there are <b>no favourable outcomes</b>.
</p>

<p>
So:
</p>

<p>
P(event) = 0 ÷ total outcomes = 0
</p>

<h3>Example 1: Rolling a standard die</h3>

<p>
A standard die has the probability space:
</p>

<p>
S = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6}
</p>

<p>
Consider the event:
</p>

<p>
<b>"Rolling a 7."</b>
</p>

<p>
There is no 7 on the die.
</p>

<p>
Therefore the number of favourable outcomes is 0.
</p>

<p>
P(7) = 0/6 = <b>0</b>
</p>

<h3>Example 2: Tossing a coin</h3>

<p>
The probability space is:
</p>

<p>
S = {H, T}
</p>

<p>
Consider the event:
</p>

<p>
<b>"Getting both Head and Tail in one toss."</b>
</p>

<p>
One coin cannot show both results at the same time.
</p>

<p>
Therefore the event is impossible.
</p>

<p>
<b>P(event) = 0</b>
</p>

<h3>Example 3: Selecting from numbered cards</h3>

<p>
Cards are labelled:
</p>

<p>
{1, 2, 3, 4, 5}
</p>

<p>
A card is selected.
</p>

<p>
The event is selecting 8.
</p>

<p>
There is no card labelled 8.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>P(8) = 0</b>
</p>

<h3>KEY TEST</h3>

<p>
If an event <b>cannot happen</b>, its probability is exactly <b>0</b>.
</p>
', 1),
  (262, 262, '
<h2>Certain Events</h2>

<h3>ONE CONCEPT</h3>

<p>
A <b>certain event</b> is an event that must happen in a particular experiment.
</p>

<p>
Its probability is:
</p>

<p>
<b>P(certain event) = 1</b>
</p>

<h3>Why is its probability 1?</h3>

<p>
A probability compares favourable outcomes with all possible outcomes.
</p>

<p>
For a certain event, <b>every possible outcome is favourable</b>.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
P(event)
=
all possible outcomes ÷ all possible outcomes
=
1
</p>

<h3>Example 1: Rolling a standard die</h3>

<p>
The possible outcomes are:
</p>

<p>
S = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6}
</p>

<p>
Consider the event:
</p>

<p>
<b>"Rolling a number less than 7."</b>
</p>

<p>
Every possible result is less than 7:
</p>

<p>
1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6
</p>

<p>
Therefore all 6 outcomes are favourable.
</p>

<p>
P(number less than 7)
=
6/6
=
<b>1</b>
</p>

<h3>Example 2: Tossing a coin</h3>

<p>
The possible outcomes are:
</p>

<p>
S = {H, T}
</p>

<p>
Every outcome is either Head or Tail.
</p>

<p>
Therefore the event:
</p>

<p>
<b>"Getting either Head or Tail."</b>
</p>

<p>
must happen.
</p>

<p>
P(event) = 1
</p>

<h3>Example 3: Selecting a card</h3>

<p>
A card is selected from:
</p>

<p>
{A, B, C}
</p>

<p>
The event is:
</p>

<p>
<b>"Selecting either A, B or C."</b>
</p>

<p>
Every available card satisfies the event.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
P(event) = 3/3 = <b>1</b>
</p>

<h3>KEY TEST</h3>

<p>
If an event <b>must happen</b>, its probability is exactly <b>1</b>.
</p>
', 1),
  (263, 263, '
<h2>Probability Between 0 and 1</h2>

<h3>ONE CONCEPT</h3>

<p>
For any event, its probability cannot be less than 0 or greater than 1.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>0 ≤ P(E) ≤ 1</b>
</p>

<p>
This is called the <b>range of probability</b>.
</p>

<h3>Why can probability not be negative?</h3>

<p>
The number of favourable outcomes cannot be negative.
</p>

<p>
We can have:
</p>

<p>
0 favourable outcomes,
</p>

<p>
1 favourable outcome,
</p>

<p>
2 favourable outcomes,
</p>

<p>
and so on.
</p>

<p>
We cannot have -1 favourable outcomes.
</p>

<p>
Therefore probability cannot be less than 0.
</p>

<h3>Why can probability not be greater than 1?</h3>

<p>
The favourable outcomes are part of the total possible outcomes.
</p>

<p>
Therefore favourable outcomes cannot be greater than the total number of outcomes.
</p>

<p>
For example:
</p>

<p>
If there are 6 possible outcomes, we cannot have 7 favourable outcomes.
</p>

<p>
The largest possible fraction is:
</p>

<p>
6/6 = 1
</p>

<h3>What do the ends mean?</h3>

<p>
<b>P(E) = 0</b> means the event is impossible.
</p>

<p>
<b>P(E) = 1</b> means the event is certain.
</p>

<p>
Values between 0 and 1 represent events that are possible but not certain.
</p>

<h3>Example 1</h3>

<p>
A die is rolled and the probability of an event is 1/2.
</p>

<p>
Convert mentally to a decimal:
</p>

<p>
1/2 = 0.5
</p>

<p>
Since:
</p>

<p>
0 ≤ 0.5 ≤ 1
</p>

<p>
the value is a possible probability.
</p>

<h3>Example 2</h3>

<p>
A probability is given as:
</p>

<p>
3/4
</p>

<p>
Since:
</p>

<p>
0 ≤ 3/4 ≤ 1
</p>

<p>
it is a possible probability.
</p>

<h3>Example 3</h3>

<p>
A student says the probability of an event is 1.4.
</p>

<p>
But:
</p>

<p>
1.4 > 1
</p>

<p>
Therefore 1.4 cannot be a probability.
</p>
', 1),
  (264, 264, '
<h2>Interpreting Probability Values</h2>

<h3>ONE CONCEPT</h3>

<p>
A probability value tells us how strongly an event is expected to occur,
from impossible to certain.
</p>

<p>
The important values are:
</p>

<p>
<b>0</b> → impossible<br>
<b>Between 0 and 1</b> → possible but not certain<br>
<b>1</b> → certain
</p>

<h3>Example 1: Probability = 0</h3>

<p>
Suppose:
</p>

<p>
P(E) = 0
</p>

<p>
This means event E cannot happen.
</p>

<p>
For example, rolling a 10 on a standard die is impossible.
</p>

<h3>Example 2: Probability = 1/2</h3>

<p>
Suppose:
</p>

<p>
P(E) = 1/2
</p>

<p>
The event is possible, but it is not guaranteed.
</p>

<p>
For a fair coin:
</p>

<p>
P(Head) = 1/2
</p>

<p>
