<h2>Testing Mutually Exclusive Events</h2>

<h3>ONE CONCEPT</h3>

<p>
When given two events, we can test them by comparing the outcomes that belong
to each event.
</p>

<p>
The decision is based on one question:
</p>

<p>
<b>Do the two events share an outcome?</b>
</p>

<p>
If they do not share an outcome, they are mutually exclusive.
</p>

<p>
If they share at least one outcome, they are not mutually exclusive.
</p>

<h3>Example 1</h3>

<p>
S = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6}
</p>

<p>
A = getting a number less than 3:
</p>

<p>
A = {1, 2}
</p>

<p>
B = getting a number greater than 4:
</p>

<p>
B = {5, 6}
</p>

<p>
There is no common outcome.
</p>

<p>
Therefore A and B are mutually exclusive.
</p>

<h3>Example 2</h3>

<p>
A = {1, 2, 3}
</p>

<p>
B = {3, 4, 5}
</p>

<p>
The common outcome is 3.
</p>

<p>
Therefore the events are not mutually exclusive.
</p>

<h3>Example 3</h3>

<p>
A = {2, 4}
</p>

<p>
B = {1, 3, 5}
</p>

<p>
There is no common outcome.
</p>

<p>
Therefore they are mutually exclusive.
</p>

<h3>FINAL RULE</h3>

<p>
<b>No shared outcome = mutually exclusive.</b>
</p>

<p>
<b>Shared outcome = not mutually exclusive.</b>
</p>

<p>
This idea must be understood before using probability laws involving mutually
exclusive events.
