<h2>Multiplication Law with Independent Events</h2>

<h3>ONE CONCEPT</h3>

<p>
The multiplication law allows us to calculate the probability that two
independent events both occur.
</p>

<p>
For independent events:
</p>

<p>
<b>P(A and B) = P(A) × P(B)</b>
</p>

<h3>Example 1: Two coin tosses</h3>

<p>
Find the probability of getting Tail on the first toss and Head on the second.
</p>

<p>
First event:
</p>

<p>
P(T) = 1/2
</p>

<p>
Second event:
</p>

<p>
P(H) = 1/2
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
P(T and H)
= 1/2 × 1/2
= <b>1/4</b>
</p>

<h3>Example 2: Two die rolls</h3>

<p>
Find the probability of getting an even number on both rolls.
</p>

<p>
For one die:
</p>

<p>
Even = {2, 4, 6}
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
P(Even) = 3/6 = 1/2
</p>

<p>
The same applies to the second roll.
</p>

<p>
Since the rolls are independent:
</p>

<p>
P(Even and Even)
= 1/2 × 1/2
= <b>1/4</b>
</p>

<h3>Example 3: Two different independent events</h3>

<p>
A coin is tossed and a die is rolled.
Find the probability of Head and an even number.
</p>

<p>
P(Head) = 1/2
</p>

<p>
P(Even) = 3/6 = 1/2
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
P(Head and Even)
= 1/2 × 1/2
= <b>1/4</b>
</p>

<h3>IMPORTANT</h3>

<p>
Do not multiply simply because the question contains the word "and".
First determine whether the events are independent.
</p>

<p>
The multiplication law being taught here applies to <b>independent events</b>.
