<h2>Choosing Addition or Multiplication</h2>

<h3>ONE CONCEPT</h3>

<p>
The operation we use depends on what the question is asking and on the
relationship between the events.
</p>

<h3>When do we add?</h3>

<p>
If the question asks for <b>A or B</b> and A and B are mutually exclusive:
</p>

<p>
<b>P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B)</b>
</p>

<h3>When do we multiply?</h3>

<p>
If the question asks for <b>A and B</b> and A and B are independent:
</p>

<p>
<b>P(A and B) = P(A) × P(B)</b>
</p>

<h3>Example 1: Addition</h3>

<p>
A fair die is rolled.
Find the probability of getting 2 or 5.
</p>

<p>
Getting 2 and getting 5 cannot happen together.
</p>

<p>
Therefore the events are mutually exclusive.
</p>

<p>
P(2 or 5)
= 1/6 + 1/6
= <b>1/3</b>
</p>

<h3>Example 2: Multiplication</h3>

<p>
A fair coin is tossed twice.
Find the probability of getting Head on both tosses.
</p>

<p>
We need both events to occur.
The tosses are independent.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
P(H and H)
= 1/2 × 1/2
= <b>1/4</b>
</p>

<h3>Example 3: Compare the wording</h3>

<p>
Question A:
</p>

<p>
"Find the probability of getting 1 <b>or</b> 6 when a die is rolled."
</p>

<p>
The outcomes are mutually exclusive, so we add.
</p>

<p>
Question B:
</p>

<p>
"Find the probability of getting 1 <b>and</b> 6 on two separate die rolls."
</p>

<p>
The rolls are independent, so we multiply.
</p>

<p>
The words <b>or</b> and <b>and</b> give us a clue, but we must still understand
the relationship between the events before choosing the rule.
