<h2>Probability Along a Complete Path</h2>

<p>
A complete path represents several events happening in sequence.
To find the probability of that particular path, we multiply the
probabilities of its branches.
</p>

<h3>The path rule</h3>

<p>
For a complete path:
</p>

<p>
<b>Path probability = first branch probability × second branch probability</b>
</p>

<p>
For more stages, continue multiplying along the path.
</p>

<h3>Example 1: Two heads</h3>

<p>
A fair coin is tossed twice.
</p>

<p>
For the path HH:
</p>

<p>
P(H) = 1/2
</p>

<p>
P(H) = 1/2
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
P(HH) = 1/2 × 1/2
</p>

<p>
= 1/4
</p>

<h3>Example 2: Head followed by Tail</h3>

<p>
For HT:
</p>

<p>
P(H) = 1/2
</p>

<p>
P(T) = 1/2
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
P(HT) = 1/2 × 1/2
</p>

<p>
= <b>1/4</b>
</p>

<h3>Example 3: Die followed by coin</h3>

<p>
A fair die is rolled and then a fair coin is tossed.
Find the probability of getting 6 followed by Head.
</p>

<p>
First event:
</p>

<p>
P(6) = 1/6
</p>

<p>
Second event:
</p>

<p>
P(H) = 1/2
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
P(6H) = 1/6 × 1/2
</p>

<p>
= 1/12
</p>

<h3>Why multiply?</h3>

<p>
The complete path requires <b>both stages</b> to occur.
We are finding the probability of reaching one particular route
through the tree.
</p>

<p>
Therefore we multiply the probabilities along that route.
</p>

<h3>Do not make this mistake</h3>

<p>
Do not add probabilities along one path.
