  (285, 285, '
<h2>Tree Diagrams With Replacement</h2>

<p>
<b>With replacement</b> means that after making the first selection,
the selected object is put back before the second selection.
</p>

<p>
This matters because putting the object back restores the original
number of objects.
</p>

<h3>Example</h3>

<p>
A bag contains:
</p>

<ul>
<li>3 red balls</li>
<li>2 blue balls</li>
</ul>

<p>
Total:
</p>

<p>
3 + 2 = <b>5</b>
</p>

<p>
First selection:
</p>

<p>
P(R) = 3/5
</p>

<p>
P(B) = 2/5
</p>

<p>
Suppose a red ball is selected.
We put it back.
</p>

<p>
The bag is again:
</p>

<p>
3 red and 2 blue.
</p>

<p>
Therefore the second selection still has:
</p>

<p>
P(R) = 3/5
</p>

<p>
P(B) = 2/5
</p>

<h3>What happens to the tree?</h3>

<pre>
First selection       Second selection

R (3/5)  ───────────→ R (3/5)
          └──────────→ B (2/5)

B (2/5)  ───────────→ R (3/5)
          └──────────→ B (2/5)
</pre>

<p>
The probabilities on the second branches are the same because
the first ball was replaced.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1: RR</h3>

<p>
P(RR) = 3/5 × 3/5
</p>

<p>
= 9/25
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2: RB</h3>

<p>
P(RB) = 3/5 × 2/5
</p>

<p>
= 6/25
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3: BB</h3>

<p>
P(BB) = 2/5 × 2/5
</p>

<p>
= 4/25
</p>

<h3>The key idea</h3>

<p>
With replacement means:
</p>

<p>
<b>The first selection does not change the contents before the
second selection.</b>
</p>

<p>
Therefore the second-stage probabilities return to their original values.
</p>
