<h2>Singular and Non-Singular Matrices</h2>

<p>
A square matrix can be classified as either <b>singular</b> or
<b>non-singular</b>.
</p>

<h3>The One Rule</h3>

<p>
First calculate the determinant.
Then use this rule:
</p>

<pre>
det(A) = 0
→ Singular

det(A) ≠ 0
→ Non-singular
</pre>

<p>
So the determinant is what decides the classification.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> Determine whether the matrix is singular or non-singular.</p>

<pre>
A = [ 2  4
      1  2 ]
</pre>

<h4>Step 1: Find the determinant</h4>

<pre>
det(A)
= (2 × 2) - (4 × 1)
= 4 - 4
= 0
</pre>

<h4>Step 2: Apply the rule</h4>

<pre>
det(A) = 0
<p> Therefore A is <b>singular</b>. </p> <h3>Worked Example 2</h3> <p><b>Question:</b> Determine whether the matrix is singular or non-singular.</p> <pre> B = [ 3 2 5 4 ] </pre> <h4>Find the determinant</h4> <pre> det(B) = (3 × 4) - (2 × 5) = 12 - 10 = 2 </pre> <p> Since: </p> <pre> 2 ≠ 0 </pre> <p> B is <b>non-singular</b>. </p> <h3>Worked Example 3</h3> <p><b>Question:</b> Find x if the matrix below is singular.</p> <pre> A = [ x 2 3 6 ] </pre> <p> A singular matrix has determinant zero. </p> <pre> det(A) = 0 </pre> <p> Calculate the determinant: </p> <pre> (x × 6) - (2 × 3) = 0 6x - 6 = 0 </pre> <p> Add 6 to both sides: </p> <pre> 6x = 6 </pre> <p> Divide both sides by 6: </p> <pre> x = 1 </pre> <h3>Worked Example 4</h3> <p><b>Question:</b> Find x if the matrix below is singular.</p> <pre> B = [ x 1 2 3 ] </pre> <p> Because the matrix is singular: </p> <pre> det(B) = 0 </pre> <p> Therefore: </p> <pre> (x × 3) - (1 × 2) = 0 3x - 2 = 0 </pre> <p> Add 2: </p> <pre> 3x = 2 </pre> <p> Divide by 3: </p> <pre> x = 2/3 </pre> <h3>Common Mistake</h3> <p> Do not decide that a matrix is singular just because its entries look similar. Always calculate its determinant. </p> ', 1),
  (309, 309, '

<h2>Inverse of a 2 × 2 Matrix</h2> <p> The inverse of a matrix A is written as: </p> <pre> A⁻¹ </pre> <p> For: </p> <pre> A = [ a b c d ] </pre> <p> the inverse is: </p> <pre> A⁻¹ = 1/(ad - bc) [ d -b -c a ] </pre> <p> The inverse exists only when: </p> <pre> ad - bc ≠ 0 </pre> <h3>The Four Actions</h3> <ol> <li>Find the determinant.</li> <li>Swap the top-left and bottom-right entries.</li> <li>Change the signs of the other two entries.</li> <li>Multiply by the reciprocal of the determinant.</li> </ol> <h3>Worked Example 1</h3> <p><b>Question:</b> Find the inverse of:</p> <pre> A = [ 3 2 1 4 ] </pre> <h4>Step 1: Find the determinant</h4> <pre> det(A) = (3 × 4) - (2 × 1) = 12 - 2 = 10 </pre> <p> The determinant is not zero, so the inverse exists. </p> <h4>Step 2: Swap the diagonal entries</h4> <pre> [ 4 2 1 3 ] </pre> <h4>Step 3: Change the signs of the other entries</h4> <pre> [ 4 -2 -1 3 ] </pre> <h4>Step 4: Multiply by 1/10</h4> <pre> A⁻¹ = 1/10 [ 4 -2 -1 3 ] </pre> <p> Therefore: </p> <pre> A⁻¹ = [ 2/5 -1/5 -1/10 3/10 ] </pre> <h3>Worked Example 2</h3> <p><b>Question:</b> Find the inverse of:</p> <pre> B = [ 2 1 5 3 ] </pre> <h4>Find the determinant</h4> <pre> det(B) = (2 × 3) - (1 × 5) = 6 - 5 = 1 </pre> <h4>Rearrange the entries</h4> <pre> [ 3 -1 -5 2 ] </pre> <p> Because the determinant is 1: </p> <pre> 1/1 = 1 </pre> <p> Therefore: </p> <pre> B⁻¹ = [ 3 -1 -5 2 ] </pre> <h3>Worked Example 3: An Inverse Does Not Exist</h3> <p><b>Question:</b> Does the following matrix have an inverse?</p> <pre> C = [ 2 4 1 2 ] </pre> <h4>Find the determinant</h4> <pre> det(C) = (2 × 2) - (4 × 1) = 4 - 4 = 0 </pre> <p> The determinant is zero. The inverse formula would require division by zero. </p> <p> Therefore C has <b>no inverse</b>. </p> <h3>Worked Example 4</h3> <p><b>Question:</b> Find the inverse of:</p> <pre> D = [ 4 1 2 1 ] </pre> <h4>Find the determinant</h4> <pre> det(D) = (4 × 1) - (1 × 2) = 4 - 2 = 2 </pre> <h4>Swap the diagonal entries</h4> <pre> [ 1 1 2 4 ] </pre> <h4>Change the signs of the other entries</h4> <pre> [ 1 -1 -2 4 ] </pre> <h4>Multiply by 1/2</h4> <pre> D⁻¹ = 1/2 [ 1 -1 -2 4 ] </pre> <p> Therefore: </p> <pre> D⁻¹ = [ 1/2 -1/2 -1 2 ] </pre> <h3>Common Mistake</h3> <p> Do not swap all four entries. Only the top-left and bottom-right entries are exchanged. The other two entries change signs. </p> ', 1),
