<h2>Direction of a Vector</h2>

<h3>ONE CONCEPT</h3>

<p>
The <b>direction of a vector</b> tells us <b>which way the vector
acts or points</b>.
</p>

<p>
For example:
</p>

<p><b>12 m east</b></p>

<p>
Here:
</p>

<p>
Magnitude = 12 m
</p>

<p>
Direction = east
</p>

<p>
The direction is essential because two vectors can have the same
magnitude but point in different directions.
</p>

<h3>SAME MAGNITUDE, DIFFERENT DIRECTION</h3>

<p>
Consider:
</p>

<p>
<b>10 m east</b>
</p>

<p>
and
</p>

<p>
<b>10 m west</b>
</p>

<p>
Both have magnitude 10 m.
However, they point in opposite directions.
Therefore, they are not the same vector.
</p>

<h3>COMMON WAYS OF DESCRIBING DIRECTION</h3>

<p>
At this stage, direction can be described using simple terms such as:
</p>

<ul>
  <li>right</li>
  <li>left</li>
  <li>up</li>
  <li>down</li>
  <li>north</li>
  <li>south</li>
  <li>east</li>
  <li>west</li>
</ul>

<p>
Later, vectors can also be represented using arrows and coordinate
directions. For now, the important idea is simply that the direction
tells us which way the vector points.
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE 1</h3>

<p>
A displacement is <b>20 m north</b>. State its direction.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify the part that tells which way the movement occurred.</p>

<p>
The word <b>north</b> tells us the direction.
</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b></p>

<p>
Direction = <b>north</b>.
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE 2</h3>

<p>
A force of <b>35 N to the right</b> acts on an object.
State its direction.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Separate magnitude from direction.</p>

<p>
35 N is the magnitude.
</p>

<p>
"To the right" tells us which way the force acts.
</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b></p>

<p>
Direction = <b>right</b>.
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE 3</h3>

<p>
Two displacements are:
</p>

<p>
A = 15 m east
</p>

<p>
B = 15 m west
</p>

<p>
Do they have the same direction?
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify the direction of A.</p>
<p>
A points east.
</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Identify the direction of B.</p>
<p>
B points west.
</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> Compare the directions.</p>
<p>
East and west are opposite directions.
</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b></p>

<p>
No. They have opposite directions.
</p>

<h3>KEY IDEA</h3>

<p>
<b>Direction tells which way a vector points.</b>
</p>
