<h2>Column Vector Notation</h2>

<h3>ONE CONCEPT</h3>

<p>
A vector can be written using a <b>column vector</b>.
A column vector records the horizontal and vertical movement
of a vector.
</p>

<p>
For example:
</p>

<p>
\[
\begin{pmatrix}
4 \\
2
\end{pmatrix}
\]
</p>

<p>
The top number represents the <b>horizontal movement</b>.
</p>

<p>
The bottom number represents the <b>vertical movement</b>.
</p>

<p>
So:
</p>

<p>
\[
\begin{pmatrix}
4 \\
2
\end{pmatrix}
\]
</p>

<p>
means:
</p>

<p>
<b>4 units horizontally and 2 units vertically.</b>
</p>

<p>
The two numbers belong together because they describe one vector.
</p>

<h3>THE ORDER MATTERS</h3>

<p>
In:
</p>

<p>
\[
\begin{pmatrix}
4 \\
2
\end{pmatrix}
\]
</p>

<p>
4 is horizontal and 2 is vertical.
</p>

<p>
We do not swap them.
</p>

<p>
\[
\begin{pmatrix}
2 \\
4
\end{pmatrix}
\]
</p>

<p>
would describe a different movement.
</p>

<h3>POSITIVE AND NEGATIVE NUMBERS</h3>

<p>
The sign of a component tells us its direction.
For the usual coordinate system:
</p>

<ul>
  <li>Positive horizontal → right</li>
  <li>Negative horizontal → left</li>
  <li>Positive vertical → up</li>
  <li>Negative vertical → down</li>
</ul>

<p>
For example:
</p>

<p>
\[
\begin{pmatrix}
-3 \\
2
\end{pmatrix}
\]
</p>

<p>
means 3 units left and 2 units up.
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE 1</h3>

<p>
Interpret:
</p>

<p>
\[
\begin{pmatrix}
5 \\
3
\end{pmatrix}
\]
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Read the top number.</p>
<p>
5 means 5 units horizontally to the right.
</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Read the bottom number.</p>
<p>
3 means 3 units vertically upward.
</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b></p>
<p>
5 units right and 3 units up.
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE 2</h3>

<p>
Interpret:
</p>

<p>
\[
\begin{pmatrix}
-4 \\
1
\end{pmatrix}
\]
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> The top number is -4.</p>
<p>
This means 4 units left.
</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> The bottom number is 1.</p>
<p>
This means 1 unit up.
</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b></p>
<p>
4 units left and 1 unit up.
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE 3</h3>

<p>
Interpret:
</p>

<p>
\[
\begin{pmatrix}
2 \\
-6
\end{pmatrix}
\]
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> The top number is 2.</p>
<p>
Move 2 units right.
</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> The bottom number is -6.</p>
<p>
Move 6 units down.
</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b></p>
<p>
2 units right and 6 units down.
</p>

<h3>KEY IDEA</h3>

<p>
In a column vector:
</p>

<p>
\[
\begin{pmatrix}
\text{horizontal} \\
\text{vertical}
\end{pmatrix}
\]
</p>

<p>
<b>Top = horizontal.</b><br>
<b>Bottom = vertical.</b>
</p>
