<h2>Adding Like Vector Terms</h2>

<h3>What is a like vector term?</h3>

<p>
Two vector terms are <b>like terms</b> when they contain the same vector.
</p>

<p>
For example:
</p>

<p>
<b>2a</b> and <b>5a</b>
</p>

<p>
are like terms because both contain vector <b>a</b>.
</p>

<p>
Just as:
</p>

<p>
<b>2x + 5x = 7x</b>
</p>

<p>
we can write:
</p>

<p>
<b>2a + 5a = 7a</b>
</p>

<h3>Why can we combine them?</h3>

<p>
If
<b>a = (3, 2)</b>,
then:
</p>

<p>
<b>2a = (6, 4)</b>
</p>

<p>
and:
</p>

<p>
<b>5a = (15, 10)</b>
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>2a + 5a = (6,4) + (15,10)</b>
</p>

<p>
<b>= (21,14)</b>
</p>

<p>
And:
</p>

<p>
<b>7a = 7(3,2) = (21,14)</b>
</p>

<p>
So:
</p>

<p>
<b>2a + 5a = 7a</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
Simplify:
</p>

<p>
<b>3a + 4a</b>
</p>

<p>
Both terms contain <b>a</b>.
</p>

<p>
Add their scalar coefficients:
</p>

<p>
<b>3 + 4 = 7</b>
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>3a + 4a = 7a</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
Simplify:
</p>

<p>
<b>6b + 2b</b>
</p>

<p>
Both terms contain <b>b</b>.
</p>

<p>
<b>6 + 2 = 8</b>
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>6b + 2b = 8b</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
Simplify:
</p>

<p>
<b>5c + 7c</b>
</p>

<p>
Add the coefficients:
</p>

<p>
<b>5 + 7 = 12</b>
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>5c + 7c = 12c</b>
</p>

<h3>Key rule</h3>

<p>
When adding like vector terms, <b>add the scalar coefficients and keep the vector</b>.
</p>
