  (387, 387, '
  <h2>Free Fall</h2>

  <p>Free fall is motion in which gravity is the only force acting on an object. Near Earth''s surface, the acceleration due to gravity is approximately 9.8 m/s² downward. Some school problems use 10 m/s² when instructed to do so.</p>

  <p>When air resistance is negligible, all objects in the same gravitational field have the same acceleration due to gravity, regardless of their masses.</p>

  <h3>Important ideas</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>An object released from rest has initial velocity u = 0.</li>
    <li>Gravity accelerates a falling object downward.</li>
    <li>If upward is chosen as positive, gravitational acceleration is negative: a = −g.</li>
    <li>If downward is chosen as positive, gravitational acceleration is positive: a = +g.</li>
    <li>Use equations of linear motion because acceleration is approximately constant near Earth''s surface.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Speed after falling</h3>
  <p>A stone is released from rest and falls for 3 s. Take g = 10 m/s² and ignore air resistance. Find its velocity after 3 s, taking downward as positive.</p>
  <p>u = 0, a = +10 m/s², t = 3 s.</p>
  <p>v = u + at.</p>
  <p>v = 0 + (10 × 3) = +30 m/s.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 30 m/s downward.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Distance fallen</h3>
  <p>A ball is released from rest and falls for 2 s. Take g = 10 m/s².</p>
  <p>s = ut + ½at².</p>
  <p>s = (0 × 2) + ½ × 10 × 2².</p>
  <p>s = 5 × 4 = 20 m.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 20 m downward.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Object thrown vertically upward</h3>
  <p>A ball is thrown vertically upward at 20 m/s. Take upward as positive and g = 10 m/s². Find the time taken to reach its highest point.</p>
  <p>At the highest point, the instantaneous velocity is v = 0 m/s.</p>
  <p>u = +20 m/s, a = −10 m/s².</p>
  <p>v = u + at.</p>
  <p>0 = 20 − 10t.</p>
  <p>10t = 20, so t = 2 s.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 2 s.</p>

  <h3>Common mistakes</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Using the wrong sign for gravitational acceleration.</li>
    <li>Assuming an object at its highest point has zero acceleration. Its velocity is momentarily zero, but gravity still acts.</li>
    <li>Claiming heavier objects fall faster in a vacuum.</li>
    <li>Forgetting that air resistance can change the motion of real falling objects.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 1),
  (388, 388, '
  <h2>Distance–Time Graphs</h2>

  <p>A distance–time graph shows how the total distance travelled changes with time. Time is normally placed on the horizontal axis, and distance on the vertical axis.</p>

  <h3>Reading the graph</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Horizontal axis:</strong> time, usually in seconds.</li>
    <li><strong>Vertical axis:</strong> distance, usually in metres.</li>
    <li><strong>Gradient:</strong> speed.</li>
    <li><strong>Horizontal line:</strong> the object is stationary because its distance from the starting point is not increasing.</li>
    <li><strong>Straight rising line:</strong> constant speed.</li>
    <li><strong>Steeper rising line:</strong> greater speed.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>The gradient is calculated using two points on the line:</p>
  <p><strong>Speed = change in distance ÷ change in time</strong></p>
  <p>In symbols: speed = (d₂ − d₁) ÷ (t₂ − t₁).</p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Finding speed from a graph</h3>
  <p>A straight section of a distance–time graph goes from (0 s, 0 m) to (5 s, 20 m). Find the speed.</p>
  <p>Change in distance = 20 − 0 = 20 m.</p>
  <p>Change in time = 5 − 0 = 5 s.</p>
  <p>Speed = 20 ÷ 5 = 4 m/s.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 4 m/s.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Recognising rest</h3>
  <p>Between 6 s and 10 s, a distance–time graph remains horizontal at 30 m.</p>
  <p>The distance does not change during this interval.</p>
  <p>Speed = (30 − 30) ÷ (10 − 6) = 0 ÷ 4 = 0 m/s.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The object is stationary for 4 seconds.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Comparing two objects</h3>
  <p>Object A travels 12 m in 3 s. Object B travels 20 m in 5 s. Compare their speeds.</p>
  <p>Speed of A = 12 ÷ 3 = 4 m/s.</p>
  <p>Speed of B = 20 ÷ 5 = 4 m/s.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> They have equal speeds, so their straight graph sections have equal gradients.</p>

  <h3>Common mistakes</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Reading the height of the graph as the speed.</li>
    <li>Calculating distance divided by time using the wrong interval.</li>
    <li>Assuming a horizontal distance–time line means constant motion rather than rest.</li>
    <li>Confusing a steeper gradient with a greater distance at that instant.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 1),
  (389, 389, '
  <h2>Displacement–Time Graphs</h2>

  <p>A displacement–time graph shows an object''s displacement from a chosen origin at different times. Unlike distance, displacement includes direction through its positive or negative sign.</p>

  <h3>What the gradient tells you</h3>
  <p><strong>Gradient of a displacement–time graph = velocity.</strong></p>
  <p>Average velocity = change in displacement ÷ change in time.</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Positive gradient:</strong> positive velocity in the chosen direction.</li>
    <li><strong>Negative gradient:</strong> motion in the opposite direction.</li>
    <li><strong>Zero gradient:</strong> the object is stationary during that interval.</li>
    <li><strong>Straight sloping line:</strong> constant velocity.</li>
    <li><strong>Changing gradient:</strong> changing velocity.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>The graph''s vertical position gives displacement, not velocity. Velocity is obtained from its gradient.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Positive velocity</h3>
  <p>An object''s displacement changes from 2 m at 1 s to 14 m at 4 s.</p>
  <p>Change in displacement = 14 − 2 = 12 m.</p>
  <p>Change in time = 4 − 1 = 3 s.</p>
  <p>Velocity = 12 ÷ 3 = +4 m/s.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> +4 m/s.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Negative velocity</h3>
  <p>An object moves from displacement +12 m at 2 s to +3 m at 5 s.</p>
  <p>Change in displacement = 3 − 12 = −9 m.</p>
  <p>Change in time = 5 − 2 = 3 s.</p>
  <p>Velocity = −9 ÷ 3 = −3 m/s.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> −3 m/s. The object moves in the negative direction.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Stationary interval</h3>
  <p>The displacement remains −4 m from 3 s to 8 s.</p>
  <p>Velocity = [−4 − (−4)] ÷ (8 − 3) = 0 ÷ 5 = 0 m/s.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The object is stationary at a displacement of −4 m during this interval.</p>

  <h3>Common mistakes</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Using the vertical displacement value as velocity.</li>
    <li>Ignoring the negative sign when displacement decreases.</li>
    <li>Confusing negative displacement with negative speed.</li>
    <li>Assuming a negative gradient means the object is slowing down. It indicates negative velocity; speed depends on the magnitude of velocity.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 1),
  (390, 390, '
  <h2>Speed–Time Graphs</h2>

  <p>A speed–time graph shows how an object''s speed changes with time. Time is placed on the horizontal axis and speed on the vertical axis.</p>

  <h3>Two important features</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Gradient:</strong> the rate of change of speed. For motion along a fixed direction without reversal, this gives the magnitude of acceleration.</li>
    <li><strong>Area under the graph:</strong> distance travelled, because speed is never negative.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>For a straight graph section, gradient = change in speed ÷ change in time.</p>
  <p>For a rectangular area, distance = base × height = time × speed.</p>
  <p>For a triangular area, distance = ½ × base × height.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Distance at constant speed</h3>
  <p>A vehicle travels at 6 m/s for 5 s. The speed–time graph is a horizontal line at 6 m/s.</p>
  <p>Area = 5 × 6 = 30 m.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 30 m travelled.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Distance while accelerating from rest</h3>
  <p>A body increases speed uniformly from 0 to 8 m/s over 4 s.</p>
  <p>The area under the graph is a triangle.</p>
  <p>Distance = ½ × base × height.</p>
  <p>Distance = ½ × 4 × 8 = 16 m.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 16 m.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Finding the rate of change of speed</h3>
  <p>Speed increases from 3 m/s to 15 m/s in 4 s.</p>
  <p>Gradient = (15 − 3) ÷ 4 = 12 ÷ 4 = 3 m/s².</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The speed increases at 3 m/s².</p>

  <h3>Common mistakes</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Using the gradient to calculate distance.</li>
    <li>Using the area under the graph to calculate acceleration.</li>
    <li>Forgetting the factor ½ for a triangular area.</li>
    <li>Using the final speed alone as the distance travelled.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 1),
  (391, 391, '
  <h2>Velocity–Time Graphs</h2>

  <p>A velocity–time graph shows how velocity changes with time. Velocity includes direction, so it can be positive, zero or negative.</p>

  <h3>Gradient gives acceleration</h3>
  <p><strong>Acceleration = change in velocity ÷ change in time.</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li>Positive gradient: positive acceleration.</li>
    <li>Negative gradient: negative acceleration.</li>
    <li>Zero gradient: constant velocity.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Signed area gives displacement</h3>
  <p>The area between the velocity–time graph and the time axis represents displacement. Area above the time axis is positive; area below it is negative. Add the signed areas to find total displacement.</p>
  <p>Total distance is different: add the magnitudes of the areas above and below the axis.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Acceleration</h3>
  <p>Velocity changes from 2 m/s to 14 m/s over 4 s.</p>
  <p>Acceleration = (14 − 2) ÷ 4 = 12 ÷ 4 = 3 m/s².</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> +3 m/s².</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Displacement during constant velocity</h3>
  <p>An object has a constant velocity of 5 m/s for 6 s.</p>
  <p>Displacement = area = 5 × 6 = 30 m.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> +30 m.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Negative displacement</h3>
  <p>An object moves at −4 m/s for 3 s.</p>
  <p>Displacement = velocity × time = −4 × 3 = −12 m.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> −12 m. The distance travelled is 12 m.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 4: Changing velocity</h3>
  <p>Velocity rises uniformly from 0 to 8 m/s over 4 s.</p>
  <p>Displacement = triangular area = ½ × 4 × 8 = 16 m.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> +16 m.</p>

  <h3>Common mistakes</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Using the graph''s height to calculate acceleration.</li>
    <li>Ignoring negative areas when calculating displacement.</li>
    <li>Confusing total distance with displacement.</li>
    <li>Assuming a negative velocity automatically means negative acceleration.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 1),
  (392, 392, '
  <h2>Interpreting Motion Graphs</h2>

  <p>Interpreting a motion graph means identifying the motion represented by each section, calculating quantities from the correct graph feature, and distinguishing what the graph shows from what it does not show.</p>

  <h3>Identify the graph first</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Distance–time:</strong> gradient gives speed; horizontal sections show rest.</li>
    <li><strong>Displacement–time:</strong> gradient gives velocity; negative gradients show motion in the negative direction.</li>
    <li><strong>Speed–time:</strong> gradient gives the rate of change of speed; area gives distance.</li>
    <li><strong>Velocity–time:</strong> gradient gives acceleration; signed area gives displacement.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Read sections in order</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Check the axis labels and units.</li>
    <li>Identify the time interval being discussed.</li>
    <li>Determine whether the graph is rising, falling, horizontal or curved.</li>
    <li>Choose the relevant feature: gradient, area or vertical value.</li>
    <li>Calculate using the correct interval and state the unit.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Distance–time interpretation</h3>
  <p>A distance–time graph rises from 0 m to 12 m during the first 3 s, then remains at 12 m until 5 s.</p>
  <p>First section: speed = 12 ÷ 3 = 4 m/s.</p>
  <p>Second section: distance is unchanged, so speed = 0 m/s.</p>
  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> The object moves at constant speed for 3 s and then rests for 2 s.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Velocity–time interpretation</h3>
  <p>A velocity–time graph shows +4 m/s from 0 to 3 s and −2 m/s from 3 to 5 s.</p>
  <p>First displacement = +4 × 3 = +12 m.</p>
  <p>Second displacement = −2 × 2 = −4 m.</p>
  <p>Total displacement = 12 − 4 = +8 m.</p>
  <p>Total distance = 12 + 4 = 16 m.</p>
  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> The object travels 16 m, but its net displacement is +8 m.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Identifying acceleration</h3>
  <p>A velocity–time graph rises in a straight line from 2 m/s at 1 s to 10 m/s at 5 s.</p>
  <p>Acceleration = (10 − 2) ÷ (5 − 1) = 8 ÷ 4 = 2 m/s².</p>
  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> The object has constant positive acceleration of 2 m/s² during this section.</p>

  <h3>Common mistakes</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Using the same calculation for every type of motion graph.</li>
    <li>Confusing a falling graph with a stationary object.</li>
    <li>Ignoring axis labels and units.</li>
    <li>Calling total distance and displacement the same quantity.</li>
    <li>Assuming a curved line always means the object is speeding up; inspect the gradient over time.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 1),
  (393, 393, '
  <h2>Newton’s First Law</h2>

  <p>Newton''s first law states that an object remains at rest or continues moving with constant velocity in a straight line unless acted on by a non-zero resultant external force.</p>

  <p>This law describes inertia: objects resist changes to their state of motion.</p>

  <h3>Resultant force and motion</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>If the resultant force is zero, acceleration is zero.</li>
    <li>An object at rest remains at rest if the resultant force stays zero.</li>
    <li>An object already moving continues at constant velocity if the resultant force stays zero.</li>
    <li>A non-zero resultant force causes acceleration, changing velocity.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Zero resultant force does not mean that no forces act. Several forces may act and balance one another.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Balanced forces</h3>
  <p>A box is pushed to the right with 20 N while friction acts to the left with 20 N.</p>
  <p>Resultant force = 20 − 20 = 0 N.</p>
  <p>Therefore, acceleration is zero. The box may remain at rest or continue at constant velocity, depending on its initial state.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: A moving vehicle</h3>
  <p>A vehicle moves in a straight line at constant velocity. Its driving force is 1,500 N forward.</p>
  <p>For the velocity to remain constant, the total opposing force must also be 1,500 N.</p>
  <p>Resultant force = 1,500 − 1,500 = 0 N.</p>
  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> Constant velocity is consistent with zero resultant force.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Why passengers move forward</h3>
  <p>A moving bus brakes suddenly. Passengers tend to move forward relative to the bus because their bodies tend to maintain their previous motion. A seat belt supplies a force that changes the passengers'' motion with the bus.</p>

  <h3>Common mistakes</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Thinking a moving object must always have a forward resultant force.</li>
    <li>Thinking zero resultant force means zero velocity.</li>
    <li>Confusing balanced forces with the absence of forces.</li>
    <li>Forgetting that a change in direction is also a change in velocity.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 1),
  (394, 394, '
  <h2>Newton’s Second Law</h2>

  <p>Newton''s second law relates the resultant force on an object to its mass and acceleration. For constant mass, the resultant force equals mass multiplied by acceleration.</p>

  <p><strong>F = ma</strong></p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>F:</strong> resultant force, in newtons (N)</li>
    <li><strong>m:</strong> mass, in kilograms (kg)</li>
    <li><strong>a:</strong> acceleration, in metres per second squared (m/s²)</li>
  </ul>

  <p>One newton is the resultant force that gives a mass of 1 kg an acceleration of 1 m/s².</p>

  <h3>Rearranging the equation</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Force: F = ma</li>
    <li>Mass: m = F ÷ a</li>
    <li>Acceleration: a = F ÷ m</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Finding force</h3>
  <p>A 4 kg trolley accelerates at 3 m/s². Find the resultant force.</p>
  <p>F = ma = 4 × 3 = 12 N.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 12 N in the direction of acceleration.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Finding acceleration</h3>
  <p>A resultant force of 20 N acts on a 5 kg object.</p>
  <p>a = F ÷ m = 20 ÷ 5 = 4 m/s².</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 4 m/s².</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Finding mass</h3>
  <p>A resultant force of 18 N gives an object an acceleration of 6 m/s².</p>
  <p>m = F ÷ a = 18 ÷ 6 = 3 kg.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 3 kg.</p>

  <h3>Common mistakes</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Using mass in grams without converting to kilograms.</li>
    <li>Using one force instead of the resultant force.</li>
    <li>Multiplying when the required rearrangement involves division.</li>
    <li>Writing the force unit as kg or m/s².</li>
  </ul>
  ', 1),
  (395, 395, '
  <h2>Newton’s Third Law</h2>

  <p>Newton''s third law states that when one object exerts a force on another object, the second object simultaneously exerts a force equal in magnitude and opposite in direction on the first object.</p>

  <p>These forces are often described as an action–reaction pair, but neither force happens first. They act on different objects.</p>

  <h3>Recognising a force pair</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>The two forces are equal in magnitude.</li>
    <li>They act in opposite directions.</li>
    <li>They act on different objects.</li>
    <li>They belong to the same interaction.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Pushing a wall</h3>
  <p>A person pushes a wall with a force of 50 N to the right.</p>
  <p>The wall exerts a force of 50 N to the left on the person.</p>
  <p><strong>Important:</strong> The forces do not cancel on one object because one acts on the wall and the other acts on the person.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Walking</h3>
  <p>A person''s foot pushes the ground backward. The ground exerts a forward frictional force on the foot. This forward force helps the person move forward.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: A swimmer</h3>
  <p>A swimmer pushes water backward. The water pushes the swimmer forward with an equal and opposite force.</p>

  <h3>Why force pairs do not cancel each other</h3>
  <p>To calculate the resultant force on an object, include only the forces acting on that object. The reaction force acts on the other object, so it is not included in the first object''s force total.</p>

  <h3>Common mistakes</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Claiming that action and reaction forces act on the same object.</li>
    <li>Claiming one force occurs before the other.</li>
    <li>Thinking the forces must always produce equal accelerations. Different masses can have different accelerations.</li>
    <li>Confusing a third-law pair with two balanced forces acting on one object.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 1),
  (396, 396, '
  <h2>Mass and Inertia</h2>

  <p>Mass is a measure of the amount of matter in an object and, in mechanics, of its resistance to acceleration. Its SI unit is the kilogram (kg).</p>

  <p><strong>Inertia</strong> is the tendency of an object to resist changes in its state of rest or uniform straight-line motion. Greater mass means greater inertia.</p>

  <h3>How mass affects motion</h3>
  <p>Newton''s second law is F = ma. Rearranging gives a = F/m.</p>
  <p>For the same resultant force, an object with greater mass has less acceleration. An object with smaller mass has greater acceleration.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Comparing acceleration</h3>
  <p>A 2 kg object and an 8 kg object each experience a resultant force of 16 N.</p>
  <p>For the 2 kg object: a = 16 ÷ 2 = 8 m/s².</p>
  <p>For the 8 kg object: a = 16 ÷ 8 = 2 m/s².</p>
  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> The 2 kg object accelerates four times as much as the 8 kg object.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Inertia in a bus</h3>
  <p>When a bus starts moving suddenly, passengers may appear to lean backward relative to the bus. Their bodies tend to remain in their previous state of rest while the bus accelerates forward.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Increasing mass</h3>
  <p>If the same net force acts on an object whose mass doubles, its acceleration halves because a = F/m.</p>

  <h3>Mass is not weight</h3>
  <p>Mass is measured in kilograms and does not depend on location. Weight is the gravitational force on an object and is measured in newtons.</p>

  <h3>Common mistakes</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Using kilograms as a unit of weight.</li>
    <li>Thinking that a large mass must always move slowly; mass alone does not specify velocity.</li>
    <li>Confusing inertia with a force.</li>
    <li>Assuming mass changes simply because an object moves to the Moon.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 1),
  (397, 397, '
  <h2>Force, Mass and Acceleration Calculations</h2>

  <p>Problems involving force, mass and acceleration are solved using Newton''s second law. The force in the equation must be the <strong>resultant force</strong>, not automatically the largest force or the applied force alone.</p>

  <p><strong>F = ma</strong></p>

  <h3>When forces act in opposite directions</h3>
  <p>Choose a positive direction. Add forces acting in that direction and subtract forces acting in the opposite direction. The result is the signed resultant force.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Opposing forces</h3>
  <p>A 5 kg trolley is pulled rightward with 30 N. Friction acts leftward with 10 N. Find the acceleration.</p>
  <p>Resultant force = 30 − 10 = 20 N rightward.</p>
  <p>a = F/m = 20/5 = 4 m/s².</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 4 m/s² rightward.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Equal opposing forces</h3>
  <p>A 10 kg box is pushed rightward with 40 N. Friction acts leftward with 40 N.</p>
  <p>Resultant force = 40 − 40 = 0 N.</p>
  <p>a = 0/10 = 0 m/s².</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Zero acceleration.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Finding the applied force</h3>
  <p>A 4 kg object accelerates rightward at 3 m/s² while friction acts leftward with 5 N. Find the applied rightward force.</p>
  <p>Required resultant force = ma = 4 × 3 = 12 N rightward.</p>
  <p>Applied force − 5 = 12.</p>
  <p>Applied force = 17 N.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 17 N rightward.</p>

  <h3>Common mistakes</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Using the applied force directly when opposing forces are present.</li>
    <li>Adding forces that act in opposite directions.</li>
    <li>Forgetting that acceleration points in the direction of the resultant force.</li>
    <li>Using grams instead of kilograms.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 1),
  (398, 398, '
  <h2>Mass and Weight</h2>

  <p>Mass is the amount of matter in an object and a measure of its resistance to acceleration. Its SI unit is the kilogram (kg).</p>

  <p>Weight is the gravitational force acting on an object. Its SI unit is the newton (N).</p>

  <p><strong>Weight = mass × gravitational field strength</strong></p>
  <p><strong>W = mg</strong></p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>W:</strong> weight in newtons (N)</li>
    <li><strong>m:</strong> mass in kilograms (kg)</li>
    <li><strong>g:</strong> gravitational field strength in N/kg</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Near Earth''s surface, g is approximately 9.8 N/kg, although some school calculations use 10 N/kg when specified.</p>

  <h3>Key differences</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Mass is measured in kilograms; weight is measured in newtons.</li>
    <li>Mass remains the same when an object moves between locations, provided no material is lost or gained.</li>
    <li>Weight changes when gravitational field strength changes.</li>
    <li>Weight acts toward the centre of the attracting body.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Finding weight</h3>
  <p>A student has a mass of 50 kg. Take g = 10 N/kg. Find the student''s weight.</p>
  <p>W = mg = 50 × 10 = 500 N.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 500 N.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Finding mass from weight</h3>
  <p>An object weighs 120 N where g = 10 N/kg. Find its mass.</p>
  <p>m = W/g = 120 ÷ 10 = 12 kg.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 12 kg.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Weight on the Moon</h3>
  <p>An object has a mass of 6 kg on Earth. If the Moon''s gravitational field strength is 1.6 N/kg, find its weight on the Moon.</p>
  <p>W = mg = 6 × 1.6 = 9.6 N.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 9.6 N. Its mass remains 6 kg.</p>

  <h3>Common mistakes</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Writing weight in kilograms.</li>
    <li>Using W = m/g instead of W = mg.</li>
    <li>Assuming mass changes on the Moon.</li>
    <li>Using a value of g without checking the question''s instructions.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 1),
  (399, 399, '
  <h2>Gravitational Field Strength</h2>

  <p>Gravitational field strength at a location is the gravitational force acting per unit mass on an object placed there.</p>

  <p><strong>g = W ÷ m</strong></p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>g:</strong> gravitational field strength, in N/kg</li>
    <li><strong>W:</strong> weight, in N</li>
    <li><strong>m:</strong> mass, in kg</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Near Earth''s surface, gravitational field strength is approximately 9.8 N/kg. Its value varies with location and is smaller at the Moon''s surface.</p>

  <p>Gravitational field strength in N/kg has the same numerical value as gravitational acceleration in m/s² at the same location, because 1 N = 1 kg·m/s².</p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Finding field strength</h3>
  <p>An object of mass 5 kg has a weight of 49 N. Calculate the gravitational field strength.</p>
  <p>g = W/m = 49 ÷ 5 = 9.8 N/kg.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 9.8 N/kg.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Comparing weight on two planets</h3>
  <p>A 10 kg object is on a planet where g = 4 N/kg.</p>
  <p>W = mg = 10 × 4 = 40 N.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 40 N.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Finding mass</h3>
  <p>An object weighs 80 N in a gravitational field of 8 N/kg.</p>
  <p>m = W/g = 80 ÷ 8 = 10 kg.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 10 kg.</p>

  <h3>Common mistakes</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Using mass divided by weight instead of weight divided by mass.</li>
    <li>Writing N/kg as the unit of mass.</li>
    <li>Assuming gravitational field strength is identical everywhere.</li>
    <li>Confusing gravitational field strength with the object''s mass.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 1),
  (400, 400, '
  <h2>Calculating Weight</h2>

  <p>Weight is the gravitational force acting on an object. Calculate it by multiplying the object''s mass by the gravitational field strength at its location.</p>

  <p><strong>W = mg</strong></p>

  <p>Before calculating:</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Identify the object''s mass in kilograms.</li>
    <li>Identify the gravitational field strength in N/kg.</li>
    <li>Multiply the values.</li>
    <li>State the answer in newtons.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Standard calculation</h3>
  <p>Find the weight of a 3.5 kg object where g = 10 N/kg.</p>
  <p>W = 3.5 × 10 = 35 N.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 35 N.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Decimal field strength</h3>
  <p>Calculate the weight of a 7.5 kg object where g = 9.8 N/kg.</p>
  <p>W = 7.5 × 9.8 = 73.5 N.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 73.5 N.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Mass given in grams</h3>
  <p>A sample has a mass of 800 g. Find its weight where g = 10 N/kg.</p>
  <p>Convert mass: 800 ÷ 1,000 = 0.8 kg.</p>
  <p>W = 0.8 × 10 = 8 N.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 8 N.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 4: Comparing locations</h3>
  <p>A 5 kg object is on Earth where g = 9.8 N/kg and then on a planet where g = 3 N/kg.</p>
  <p>Earth weight = 5 × 9.8 = 49 N.</p>
  <p>Other planet''s weight = 5 × 3 = 15 N.</p>
  <p>The mass remains 5 kg at both locations.</p>

  <h3>Common mistakes</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Using the wrong value of g.</li>
    <li>Multiplying a mass in grams directly by g in N/kg.</li>
    <li>Reporting weight in kilograms.</li>
    <li>Assuming weight stays constant at every location.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 1),
  (401, 401, '
  <h2>Density</h2>

  <p>Density is the mass per unit volume of a substance. It describes how much mass is contained in a given volume.</p>

  <p><strong>Density = mass ÷ volume</strong></p>
  <p><strong>ρ = m/V</strong></p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>ρ:</strong> density</li>
    <li><strong>m:</strong> mass</li>
    <li><strong>V:</strong> volume</li>
  </ul>

  <p>The SI unit of density is kg/m³. Another commonly used unit is g/cm³.</p>

  <p>Density is a property of the material under specified conditions. For a uniform sample of the same material, increasing the amount increases both mass and volume in the same proportion, so density remains unchanged.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Calculating density</h3>
  <p>A metal block has a mass of 600 kg and a volume of 0.2 m³. Calculate its density.</p>
  <p>ρ = m/V = 600 ÷ 0.2 = 3,000 kg/m³.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 3,000 kg/m³.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Using grams and cubic centimetres</h3>
  <p>A sample has a mass of 180 g and a volume of 60 cm³.</p>
  <p>Density = 180 ÷ 60 = 3 g/cm³.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 3 g/cm³.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Comparing equal volumes</h3>
  <p>Two blocks each have a volume of 2 m³. Block A has a mass of 10 kg, while Block B has a mass of 18 kg.</p>
  <p>Density of A = 10 ÷ 2 = 5 kg/m³.</p>
  <p>Density of B = 18 ÷ 2 = 9 kg/m³.</p>
  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> Block B has the greater density.</p>

  <h3>Common mistakes</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Multiplying mass by volume instead of dividing.</li>
    <li>Mixing units such as kg and cm³ without conversion.</li>
    <li>Writing density in kg or m³ instead of kg/m³.</li>
    <li>Assuming a larger object must have a greater density.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 1),
  (402, 402, '
  <h2>Calculating Density</h2>
  <p>Density tells us how much mass is contained in a given volume. To calculate density, divide mass by volume.</p>

  <h3>Formula</h3>
  <p><strong>Density = Mass ÷ Volume</strong></p>
  <p>\(\rho = \frac{m}{V}\)</p>
  <ul>
    <li>\(\rho\) = density</li>
    <li>\(m\) = mass</li>
    <li>\(V\) = volume</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The SI unit of density is kg/m³. Another common unit is g/cm³.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Find density</h3>
  <p>A block has a mass of 600 g and a volume of 200 cm³. Find its density.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Write the formula: density = mass ÷ volume.</li>
    <li>Substitute: density = 600 ÷ 200.</li>
    <li>Calculate: density = 3 g/cm³.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Find mass</h3>
  <p>A liquid has a density of 800 kg/m³ and a volume of 0.02 m³.</p>
  <p>Rearrange the formula: mass = density × volume.</p>
  <p>Mass = 800 × 0.02 = <strong>16 kg</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Find volume</h3>
  <p>An object has a mass of 2 kg and a density of 500 kg/m³.</p>
  <p>Volume = mass ÷ density = 2 ÷ 500 = <strong>0.004 m³</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Unit conversion</h3>
  <p>Before substituting values, make sure the units are compatible. Remember:</p>
  <p><strong>1 g/cm³ = 1000 kg/m³</strong></p>
  <p>For example, 2.7 g/cm³ = 2700 kg/m³.</p>

  <h3>Common mistake</h3>
  <p>Do not multiply mass by volume when calculating density. Divide mass by volume, and include the correct unit in your answer.</p>
  ', 1),
  (403, 403, '
  <h2>Relative Density</h2>
  <p>Relative density compares the density of a substance with the density of water. It tells us how dense the substance is compared with water.</p>

  <h3>Formula</h3>
  <p><strong>Relative density = Density of substance ÷ Density of water</strong></p>
  <p>\(\text{Relative density} = \frac{\rho_s}{\rho_w}\)</p>
  <p>For school calculations, the density of water is commonly taken as 1000 kg/m³ or 1 g/cm³.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 1</h3>
  <p>A liquid has a density of 800 kg/m³. Calculate its relative density.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Relative density = 800 ÷ 1000.</li>
    <li>Relative density = <strong>0.8</strong>.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Worked example 2</h3>
  <p>A solid has a density of 2.7 g/cm³.</p>
  <p>Relative density = 2.7 ÷ 1 = <strong>2.7</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Important properties</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Relative density has no unit because it is a ratio of two densities.</li>
    <li>Convert densities into compatible units before dividing.</li>
    <li>A substance less dense than water may float in water; one more dense than water may sink, depending on the object''s overall density and conditions.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Common mistake</h3>
  <p>Do not attach kg/m³ or g/cm³ to relative density. Those are units of density, not relative density.</p>
  ', 1),
  (404, 404, '
  <h2>Causes of Friction</h2>
  <p>Friction is a force that opposes relative sliding, or the tendency of relative sliding, between surfaces in contact.</p>

  <h3>Why friction occurs</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Surface irregularities:</strong> even surfaces that look smooth have tiny irregularities. These can interlock when pressed together.</li>
    <li><strong>Adhesion:</strong> microscopic attractions between contacting surfaces can resist sliding.</li>
    <li><strong>Force pressing surfaces together:</strong> pressing surfaces together more strongly often increases the frictional force, depending on the materials and conditions.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Worked example 1</h3>
  <p>A box is pushed across a rough floor. The floor exerts a frictional force on the box opposite to its sliding relative to the floor.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2</h3>
  <p>A heavy crate is harder to slide than a similar lighter crate on the same floor. One reason is that the heavier crate presses the surfaces together more strongly.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3</h3>
  <p>A polished surface may offer less friction than a rough surface under comparable conditions. However, friction also depends on the materials and the condition of the surfaces; roughness alone does not determine every case.</p>

  <h3>Common mistake</h3>
  <p>Friction does not always point opposite to an object''s overall motion. It opposes relative slipping at the contact surface. For example, friction between the ground and a walking foot can push the person forward.</p>
  ', 1),
  (405, 405, '
  <h2>Effects of Friction</h2>
  <p>Friction affects movement whenever contacting surfaces resist relative sliding. Its effects can be useful or unwanted.</p>

  <h3>Effect 1: It opposes sliding</h3>
  <p>A box sliding across a floor slows down if the frictional force is not balanced by another forward force.</p>

  <h3>Effect 2: It produces heating</h3>
  <p>When surfaces rub together, some mechanical energy is transferred into internal energy, warming the surfaces.</p>

  <h3>Effect 3: It causes wear</h3>
  <p>Repeated rubbing can gradually remove material from tyres, shoe soles, machine parts and other surfaces.</p>

  <h3>Effect 4: It provides grip</h3>
  <p>Friction between shoes and the ground helps walking. Friction between tyres and the road helps vehicles accelerate, turn and brake.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 1</h3>
  <p>A bicycle brake presses a pad against the wheel''s braking surface. Friction transfers kinetic energy mainly into thermal energy, slowing the bicycle.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2</h3>
  <p>Rubbing your hands together warms them because friction converts some mechanical energy into internal energy.</p>

  <h3>Common mistake</h3>
  <p>Do not conclude that friction is always harmful. Without sufficient friction, walking, gripping and effective braking would be difficult or impossible.</p>
  ', 1),
  (406, 406, '
  <h2>Advantages and Disadvantages of Friction</h2>
  <p>Friction is useful when grip or controlled slowing is needed. It is disadvantageous when it causes unwanted energy transfer, wear or resistance.</p>

  <h3>Advantages</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Walking:</strong> friction between shoes and the ground helps prevent slipping.</li>
    <li><strong>Braking:</strong> friction helps vehicles and bicycles slow down.</li>
    <li><strong>Grip:</strong> friction helps hands hold objects and tools.</li>
    <li><strong>Writing:</strong> friction between a pencil and paper allows graphite to mark the paper.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Disadvantages</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Wear:</strong> rubbing can damage surfaces and machine components.</li>
    <li><strong>Heating:</strong> some mechanical energy is transferred into internal energy.</li>
    <li><strong>Reduced efficiency:</strong> machines may require extra energy to overcome unwanted friction.</li>
    <li><strong>Resistance:</strong> friction can oppose movement where sliding is intended.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Worked example 1</h3>
  <p>For a car''s brakes, friction is an advantage because it helps the car stop.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2</h3>
  <p>Inside a poorly lubricated engine, unwanted friction can cause heating and wear. Lubrication can reduce this problem.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3</h3>
  <p>When walking on an icy surface, available friction may be too small for a secure grip, increasing the risk of slipping.</p>

  <h3>Common mistake</h3>
  <p>Whether friction is an advantage depends on the task. The same force that helps brakes work can waste energy inside a machine.</p>
  ', 1),
  (407, 407, '
  <h2>Reducing and Increasing Friction</h2>
  <p>Friction is reduced when less resistance is needed, and increased when more grip is required. The correct method depends on the task and the surfaces involved.</p>

  <h3>Methods of reducing friction</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Lubrication:</strong> oil or grease separates contacting surfaces and reduces direct rubbing.</li>
    <li><strong>Ball bearings:</strong> rolling elements allow parts to move with less sliding friction.</li>
    <li><strong>Rollers or wheels:</strong> rolling can replace sliding and often reduces resistance.</li>
    <li><strong>Streamlining:</strong> a suitable shape reduces fluid resistance when moving through air or water.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Methods of increasing friction</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Treads:</strong> tyre treads help maintain grip and channel water away under suitable conditions.</li>
    <li><strong>Rough or high-grip surfaces:</strong> suitable surface materials can improve traction.</li>
    <li><strong>Brake pads:</strong> materials are selected to provide useful friction during braking.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Worked example 1</h3>
  <p>Oil is placed between moving machine parts to reduce direct contact and unwanted friction.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2</h3>
  <p>Ball bearings in a wheel reduce resistance by allowing rolling rather than extensive sliding at the bearing surfaces.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3</h3>
  <p>Tyres are designed to provide grip on roads. Tread design is especially important when road conditions involve water or loose material.</p>

  <h3>Common mistake</h3>
  <p>Streamlining reduces fluid resistance, not every type of friction. Lubricants are also not suitable for every contact, especially where a high-grip surface is needed.</p>
  ', 1),
  (408, 408, '
  <h2>Air Resistance and Terminal Velocity</h2>
  <p>Air resistance, also called drag, is a force exerted by air on an object moving through it. It acts opposite to the object''s motion relative to the air.</p>

  <h3>How air resistance changes</h3>
  <p>For many ordinary objects, air resistance increases as speed through the air increases. The exact relationship depends on the object''s shape, size and flow conditions.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: A falling object</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>At the start of a fall, the object''s weight acts downward.</li>
    <li>If air resistance is initially small, the object accelerates downward.</li>
    <li>As its speed increases, air resistance increases upward.</li>
    <li>The resultant downward force becomes smaller, so the acceleration decreases.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Terminal velocity</h3>
  <p>Terminal velocity is reached when the upward forces of air resistance and buoyancy balance the object''s weight downward. The resultant force is then zero, so the object continues falling at a constant velocity.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2</h3>
  <p>A falling object has weight 20 N downward and air resistance 20 N upward. If buoyancy is negligible, the resultant force is zero. The object has no acceleration at that instant and, if these forces remain balanced, falls at constant velocity.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: A parachute</h3>
  <p>Opening a parachute greatly increases the area interacting with the air. Drag increases, slowing the fall until a new, lower terminal speed can be reached.</p>

  <h3>Common mistake</h3>
  <p>At terminal velocity, the object''s velocity is not zero. Its acceleration is zero because the resultant force is zero.</p>
  ', 1),
  (409, 409, '
  <h2>Elasticity and Plasticity</h2>
  <p>When a force deforms a material, the material may return to its original shape when the force is removed, or it may remain permanently deformed.</p>

  <h3>Elastic deformation</h3>
  <p>A material undergoes elastic deformation when it returns to its original shape and dimensions after the deforming force is removed, provided it has not been damaged or taken beyond its elastic limit.</p>

  <h3>Plastic deformation</h3>
  <p>A material undergoes plastic deformation when some deformation remains after the force is removed.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Spring</h3>
  <p>A spring is stretched slightly and returns to its original length when the load is removed. This is elastic behaviour.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Bent wire</h3>
  <p>A wire is bent and stays bent after the force is removed. It has undergone plastic deformation.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Elastic limit</h3>
  <p>If a spring is stretched too far, it may not return to its original length. The force has taken it beyond its elastic limit.</p>

  <h3>Common mistake</h3>
  <p>Elasticity does not mean that a material must be soft or stretch easily. It describes recovery after the deforming force is removed.</p>
  ', 1),
  (410, 410, '
  <h2>Hooke''s Law</h2>
  <p>For a suitable elastic spring, extension is directly proportional to the applied force, provided the elastic limit is not exceeded. This relationship is known as Hooke''s law.</p>

  <h3>Formula</h3>
  <p>\(F = ke\)</p>
  <ul>
    <li>\(F\) = applied force in newtons (N)</li>
    <li>\(k\) = spring constant in N/m</li>
    <li>\(e\) = extension in metres (m)</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Calculate force</h3>
  <p>A spring has a spring constant of 200 N/m and an extension of 0.05 m.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Write F = ke.</li>
    <li>Substitute F = 200 × 0.05.</li>
    <li>Force = <strong>10 N</strong>.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Calculate extension</h3>
  <p>A force of 12 N acts on a spring with k = 300 N/m.</p>
  <p>Extension = F/k = 12/300 = <strong>0.04 m</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Calculate spring constant</h3>
  <p>A force of 15 N produces an extension of 0.03 m.</p>
  <p>k = F/e = 15/0.03 = <strong>500 N/m</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Common mistake</h3>
  <p>Extension is the increase in length, not the spring''s total length. Convert centimetres to metres when using a spring constant in N/m.</p>
  
<hr>
<h2>Hooke''s Law</h2>
  <p>Hooke''s law states that, for a spring or suitable elastic material, extension is directly proportional to the applied force, provided the limit of proportionality has not been exceeded.</p>
  <p><strong>F ∝ x</strong></p>
  <p>For a spring obeying Hooke''s law:</p>
  <p><strong>F = kx</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li>F = applied force in newtons (N)</li>
    <li>k = spring constant in newtons per metre (N/m)</li>
    <li>x = extension in metres (m)</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>A spring extends by 0.04 m when a force of 8 N is applied. Calculate its spring constant.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>k = F/x.</li>
    <li>k = 8/0.04.</li>
    <li>k = 200 N/m.</li>
  </ol>
  <p><strong>Answer: 200 N/m.</strong></p>
  <p>Hooke''s law does not apply indefinitely. Beyond the limit of proportionality, extension is no longer directly proportional to force.</p>', 1),
  (411, 411, '
  <h2>Stress and Strain</h2>
  <p>Stress and strain describe how a material responds when forces deform it.</p>

  <h3>Stress</h3>
  <p>Stress is the applied force per unit cross-sectional area.</p>
  <p>\(\text{Stress} = \frac{F}{A}\)</p>
  <p>Its SI unit is the pascal (Pa), equivalent to N/m².</p>

  <h3>Strain</h3>
  <p>For a stretched wire or rod, tensile strain is extension divided by original length.</p>
  <p>\(\text{Strain} = \frac{\text{extension}}{\text{original length}}\)</p>
  <p>Strain is a ratio and has no unit.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Stress</h3>
  <p>A force of 100 N acts on a cross-sectional area of 0.002 m².</p>
  <p>Stress = 100 ÷ 0.002 = <strong>50,000 Pa</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Strain</h3>
  <p>A wire originally 2 m long extends by 0.004 m.</p>
  <p>Strain = 0.004 ÷ 2 = <strong>0.002</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Interpret strain</h3>
  <p>A strain of 0.01 means the extension is 0.01 times the original length, or 1% of that length.</p>

  <h3>Common mistake</h3>
  <p>Stress has units, but strain does not. Always use the original length in the strain formula, not the stretched length.</p>
  ', 1),
  (412, 412, '
  <h2>Young''s Modulus</h2>
  <p>Young''s modulus describes the stiffness of a material under tensile or compressive loading within its elastic range. It is the ratio of stress to strain.</p>

  <h3>Formula</h3>
  <p>\(E = \frac{\text{stress}}{\text{strain}}\)</p>
  <p>Since strain has no unit, Young''s modulus has the same unit as stress: pascals (Pa).</p>

  <h3>Worked example 1</h3>
  <p>A wire experiences a stress of 4 × 10⁷ Pa and a strain of 0.002.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Write E = stress ÷ strain.</li>
    <li>Substitute E = (4 × 10⁷) ÷ 0.002.</li>
    <li>E = <strong>2 × 10¹⁰ Pa</strong>.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Compare materials</h3>
  <p>For the same tensile stress, a material with a greater Young''s modulus has a smaller strain in the linear elastic range. It is stiffer under those conditions.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Find stress</h3>
  <p>If E = 2 × 10¹¹ Pa and strain = 0.001, stress = E × strain = 2 × 10⁸ Pa.</p>

  <h3>Common mistake</h3>
  <p>Young''s modulus is not the same as force or extension. It is a material property determined from stress and strain, and the simple relationship is used in the linear elastic range.</p>
  
<hr>
<h2>Young''s Modulus</h2>
  <p>Young''s modulus measures the stiffness of a material under tensile or compressive stress, within the range where stress is directly proportional to strain.</p>
  <p><strong>Young''s modulus = stress ÷ strain</strong></p>
  <p><strong>E = σ/ε</strong></p>
  <p>Stress is measured in pascals (Pa), while strain has no unit. Therefore, Young''s modulus is measured in pascals (Pa).</p>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>A material experiences stress of 4 × 10⁶ Pa and strain of 0.002.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>E = stress ÷ strain.</li>
    <li>E = (4 × 10⁶)/0.002.</li>
    <li>E = 2 × 10⁹ Pa.</li>
  </ol>
  <p><strong>Answer: 2 × 10⁹ Pa.</strong></p>
  <p>A material with a larger Young''s modulus is generally stiffer under comparable conditions: more stress is required to produce the same strain in the proportional region.</p>', 1),
  (413, 413, '<h2>Elasticity</h2>
  <p>Elasticity is the ability of a material to return to its original shape and dimensions after the deforming force is removed, provided the material has not been stretched beyond its elastic limit.</p>
  <p>A deforming force can stretch, compress, bend or twist an object.</p>
  <h3>Examples</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>A spring returns towards its original length after a small stretching force is removed.</li>
    <li>A rubber band returns towards its original shape when stretched within its elastic range.</li>
    <li>A metal wire can behave elastically under sufficiently small loads.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Elasticity does not mean a material cannot change shape. It means the material can recover its original shape when the force is removed, within the elastic range.</p>', 1),
  (414, 414, '<h2>Extension and Compression</h2>
  <p>Extension is the increase in length of an object when a stretching force acts on it.</p>
  <p><strong>Extension = stretched length − original length</strong></p>
  <p>Compression is a decrease in length or a reduction in size caused by a compressive force.</p>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>A spring has an original length of 12 cm. Its stretched length is 17 cm.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Extension = stretched length − original length.</li>
    <li>Extension = 17 − 12.</li>
    <li>Extension = 5 cm = 0.05 m.</li>
  </ol>
  <p><strong>Answer: 5 cm or 0.05 m.</strong></p>
  <p>When using SI units in calculations, convert centimetres to metres where required. One centimetre equals 0.01 m.</p>', 1),
  (415, 415, '<h2>Spring Constant</h2>
  <p>The spring constant, k, measures the force needed per unit extension of a spring while it obeys Hooke''s law.</p>
  <p><strong>k = F/x</strong></p>
  <p>Its SI unit is the newton per metre (N/m).</p>
  <p>A larger spring constant means a stiffer spring: more force is required to produce the same extension.</p>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>A force of 15 N produces an extension of 0.05 m.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>k = F/x.</li>
    <li>k = 15/0.05.</li>
    <li>k = 300 N/m.</li>
  </ol>
  <p><strong>Answer: 300 N/m.</strong></p>
  <p>Compare spring constants using the same units and within the range where the springs obey Hooke''s law.</p>', 1),
  (416, 416, '<h2>Force–Extension Graphs</h2>
  <p>A force–extension graph shows how the extension of a material changes as the applied force increases.</p>
  <p>For a spring obeying Hooke''s law, a graph of force F on the vertical axis against extension x on the horizontal axis is a straight line through the origin.</p>
  <p><strong>Gradient = change in force ÷ change in extension</strong></p>
  <p>For a Hookean spring, this gradient equals the spring constant k.</p>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>Force increases from 2 N to 10 N while extension increases from 0.01 m to 0.05 m.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Change in force = 10 − 2 = 8 N.</li>
    <li>Change in extension = 0.05 − 0.01 = 0.04 m.</li>
    <li>Gradient = 8/0.04 = 200 N/m.</li>
  </ol>
  <p><strong>Answer: 200 N/m.</strong></p>
  <p>Always check which quantity is plotted on each axis before calculating the gradient.</p>', 1),
  (417, 417, '<h2>Limit of Proportionality</h2>
  <p>The limit of proportionality is the point beyond which extension is no longer directly proportional to the applied force.</p>
  <p>Before this limit, a force–extension graph for a Hookean spring is a straight line through the origin. Beyond it, the graph no longer follows that straight-line relationship.</p>
  <p>The limit of proportionality is not necessarily the same as the elastic limit.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Limit of proportionality:</strong> direct proportionality between force and extension ends.</li>
    <li><strong>Elastic limit:</strong> the material can no longer fully recover its original dimensions after the force is removed.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>A material may stop obeying Hooke''s law but still recover its original shape when unloaded.</p>', 1),
  (418, 418, '<h2>Elastic and Plastic Deformation</h2>
  <p>Deformation is a change in an object''s shape or dimensions caused by a force.</p>
  <p><strong>Elastic deformation:</strong> the object returns to its original dimensions when the force is removed.</p>
  <p><strong>Plastic deformation:</strong> some change remains after the force is removed.</p>
  <h3>Examples</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>A spring stretched within its elastic range shows elastic deformation.</li>
    <li>A metal wire that remains longer after being stretched beyond its elastic limit shows plastic deformation.</li>
    <li>If a material is overloaded, it may eventually fracture.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Plastic deformation is not the same as breaking. A material can undergo permanent deformation without separating into pieces.</p>', 1),
  (419, 419, '<h2>Stress</h2>
  <p>Stress is the force acting per unit cross-sectional area inside a material under load. For a uniform tensile or compressive force acting normally on a cross-section:</p>
  <p><strong>Stress = force ÷ cross-sectional area</strong></p>
  <p><strong>σ = F/A</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li>σ = stress in pascals (Pa)</li>
    <li>F = force in newtons (N)</li>
    <li>A = cross-sectional area in square metres (m²)</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Stress describes how concentrated the force is within the material. One pascal equals one newton per square metre.</p>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>A wire carries a tensile force of 200 N and has a cross-sectional area of 0.001 m².</p>
  <ol>
    <li>σ = F/A.</li>
    <li>σ = 200/0.001.</li>
    <li>σ = 200,000 Pa.</li>
  </ol>
  <p><strong>Answer: 200,000 Pa.</strong></p>', 1),
  (420, 420, '<h2>Strain</h2>
  <p>Strain measures the fractional change in length of a material when a force deforms it.</p>
  <p><strong>Strain = extension ÷ original length</strong></p>
  <p><strong>ε = x/L</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li>ε = strain</li>
    <li>x = extension in metres</li>
    <li>L = original length in metres</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Strain has no unit because it is a ratio of two lengths measured in the same unit.</p>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>A wire of original length 2 m extends by 0.004 m.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Strain = extension ÷ original length.</li>
    <li>Strain = 0.004/2.</li>
    <li>Strain = 0.002.</li>
  </ol>
  <p><strong>Answer: 0.002, with no unit.</strong></p>', 1),
  (421, 421, '
  <h2>Moment of a Force</h2>
  <p>The moment of a force is its turning effect about a pivot or fixed point. It depends on the size of the force and the perpendicular distance from the pivot to the force''s line of action.</p>

  <h3>Formula</h3>
  <p><strong>Moment = Force × perpendicular distance</strong></p>
  <p>\(M = Fd\)</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Moment, M, is measured in newton metres (N m).</li>
    <li>Force, F, is measured in newtons (N).</li>
    <li>Perpendicular distance, d, is measured in metres (m).</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Worked example 1</h3>
  <p>A force of 20 N acts perpendicularly at a distance of 0.5 m from a pivot.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Write M = Fd.</li>
    <li>Substitute M = 20 × 0.5.</li>
    <li>Moment = <strong>10 N m</strong>.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Worked example 2</h3>
  <p>A 30 N force acts 0.2 m from a pivot.</p>
  <p>Moment = 30 × 0.2 = <strong>6 N m</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Find the force</h3>
  <p>A moment of 12 N m is produced at a perpendicular distance of 0.4 m.</p>
  <p>Force = moment ÷ distance = 12 ÷ 0.4 = <strong>30 N</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Common mistake</h3>
  <p>Use the perpendicular distance from the pivot to the force''s line of action, not necessarily the full length of the object.</p>
  ', 1),
  (422, 422, '
  <h2>Clockwise and Anticlockwise Moments</h2>
  <p>A force can turn an object clockwise or anticlockwise about a pivot. The turning direction depends on where the force acts and the direction in which it pushes or pulls.</p>

  <h3>Identifying turning direction</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Clockwise:</strong> the object tends to rotate in the direction of clock hands.</li>
    <li><strong>Anticlockwise:</strong> the object tends to rotate opposite to clock hands.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Worked example 1: A seesaw</h3>
  <p>A child sitting to the left of a central pivot pushes the left end downward. The seesaw tends to turn anticlockwise.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: A door</h3>
  <p>Pushing a door away from its hinges makes it turn about the hinge line. The hinges act as the pivot axis.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Reversing the force</h3>
  <p>At the same point on a lever, reversing the direction of the force reverses the turning tendency.</p>

  <h3>Common mistake</h3>
  <p>Do not determine the turning direction from the force''s position alone. You must consider both the position relative to the pivot and the direction of the force.</p>
  ', 1),
  (423, 423, '
  <h2>Principle of Moments</h2>
  <p>For a body in rotational equilibrium, the total clockwise moment about a pivot equals the total anticlockwise moment about that pivot.</p>

  <h3>Relationship</h3>
  <p><strong>Sum of clockwise moments = Sum of anticlockwise moments</strong></p>

  <h3>Worked example 1</h3>
  <p>A 20 N force acts 0.5 m to the left of a pivot. What force acting 0.25 m to the right would balance it, assuming the forces produce opposite turning directions?</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Left moment = 20 × 0.5 = 10 N m.</li>
    <li>For balance, the right moment must be 10 N m.</li>
    <li>Force × 0.25 = 10.</li>
    <li>Force = <strong>40 N</strong>.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Worked example 2</h3>
  <p>A clockwise moment is 12 N m and the anticlockwise moment is 12 N m. The net moment is zero, so there is no resultant turning effect.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3</h3>
  <p>If the clockwise moment is 15 N m and the anticlockwise moment is 9 N m, the resultant moment is 6 N m clockwise.</p>

  <h3>Common mistake</h3>
  <p>Equal moments establish rotational balance about the chosen pivot. Full mechanical equilibrium also requires the resultant force to be zero.</p>
  ', 1),
  (424, 424, '
  <h2>Centre of Gravity</h2>
  <p>The centre of gravity of a body is the point through which the resultant gravitational force on the body acts. In a uniform gravitational field, it coincides with the centre of mass.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: A uniform ruler</h3>
  <p>For a uniform ruler of the same material and cross-section throughout, the centre of gravity is at its midpoint when the gravitational field is uniform.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: An irregular flat object</h3>
  <p>The centre of gravity of a freely suspended irregular lamina can be located by suspending it from different points and drawing vertical lines using a plumb line. The lines intersect at the centre of gravity.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: A loaded object</h3>
  <p>Adding a load to one side of an object can shift its centre of gravity toward the added mass.</p>

  <h3>Why it matters</h3>
  <p>The position of the centre of gravity affects balance and stability. An object is less likely to topple when the vertical line through its centre of gravity remains within its base of support.</p>

  <h3>Common mistake</h3>
  <p>The centre of gravity does not have to be inside the material itself. For example, a ring''s centre of gravity can lie in its empty central space.</p>
  ', 1),
  (425, 425, '
  <h2>Stability of Objects</h2>
  <p>Stability describes how well an object resists toppling when disturbed. Two important factors are the height of its centre of gravity and the width of its base of support.</p>

  <h3>Factors that increase stability</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>A lower centre of gravity.</li>
    <li>A wider base of support.</li>
    <li>A mass distribution that keeps the vertical line through the centre of gravity within the base when slightly tilted.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Worked example 1: A wide-based vehicle</h3>
  <p>A vehicle with a wider base of support is generally more resistant to toppling than an otherwise similar vehicle with a narrow base.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Lowering a load</h3>
  <p>Carrying a heavy load lower can lower the combined centre of gravity, often improving stability.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Toppling condition</h3>
  <p>When an object tilts enough that the vertical line through its centre of gravity falls outside its base of support, its weight can create a moment that makes it topple.</p>

  <h3>Common mistake</h3>
  <p>A low centre of gravity improves stability, but it does not guarantee that an object can never topple. Base width, tilt and external forces also matter.</p>
  ', 1),
  (426, 426, '<h2>Finding the distance from a pivot</h2>
  <p>The moment of a force is calculated using:</p>
  <p><b>Moment = force × perpendicular distance from pivot</b></p>
  <p>For a balanced object, clockwise moments equal anticlockwise moments.</p>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>A 40 N force acts 0.5 m to the left of a pivot. A 20 N force acts to the right at an unknown distance d. The beam is balanced.</p>
  <p>40 × 0.5 = 20 × d</p>
  <p>20 = 20d</p>
  <p>d = 20/20 = <b>1 m</b>.</p>
  <p><b>Check:</b> both moments must be equal for rotational equilibrium.</p>', 1),
  (427, 427, '<h2>Finding a balancing force</h2>
  <p>If a beam is balanced, the total clockwise moment equals the total anticlockwise moment.</p>
  <p><b>Clockwise moment = anticlockwise moment</b></p>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>A 60 N load acts 0.4 m from a pivot. An effort acts 1.2 m from the pivot on the opposite side.</p>
  <p>60 × 0.4 = F × 1.2</p>
  <p>24 = 1.2F</p>
  <p>F = 24/1.2 = <b>20 N</b>.</p>
  <p>The effort is smaller because it acts farther from the pivot.</p>', 1),
  (428, 428, '<h2>More than one force on each side</h2>
  <p>When several forces act on a beam, calculate each moment about the same pivot.</p>
  <p>For equilibrium:</p>
  <p><b>Total clockwise moments = total anticlockwise moments</b></p>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>Two downward forces of 10 N and 20 N act on the left of a pivot at distances 1 m and 2 m respectively. A force F acts downward on the right, 2 m from the pivot.</p>
  <p>Clockwise moment = F × 2</p>
  <p>Anticlockwise moment = (10 × 1) + (20 × 2) = 50 N m</p>
  <p>For balance: 2F = 50, so <b>F = 25 N</b>.</p>
  <p>The direction assigned as clockwise or anticlockwise depends on the force''s position and direction relative to the pivot.</p>', 1),
  (429, 429, '<h2>When a beam remains balanced</h2>
  <p>For a beam to remain in static equilibrium, its forces must balance and its total turning effect must be zero.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>The resultant force must be zero.</li>
    <li>The resultant moment about a pivot must be zero.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>A beam can have zero resultant moment but still accelerate in a straight line if its forces do not balance.</p>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>A beam is at rest on a support. Its total clockwise moment is 24 N m and its total anticlockwise moment is 24 N m.</p>
  <p>Net moment = 24 − 24 = <b>0 N m</b>.</p>
  <p>If its forces also balance, it remains in static equilibrium.</p>', 1),
  (430, 430, '<h2>Where an object''s weight acts</h2>
  <p>The centre of gravity is the point through which the resultant gravitational force on an object acts.</p>
  <p>For many ordinary objects in a uniform gravitational field, the centre of gravity is at or near the centre of mass.</p>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>Consider a uniform metre rule supported at its midpoint. Its weight acts through its centre of gravity, near the 50 cm mark.</p>
  <p>When the support is directly below this point and no other turning effects act, the rule can balance.</p>
  <p><b>Remember:</b> the centre of gravity depends on how mass is distributed, not just on an object''s outer shape.</p>', 1),
  (431, 431, '<h2>Using the centre of gravity to predict balance</h2>
  <p>The line of action of weight is the vertical line passing through an object''s centre of gravity.</p>
  <p>For an object resting on a level base to remain stable, the vertical line through its centre of gravity must fall within its base of support.</p>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>A rectangular block stands on a broad, level base. Its centre of gravity is above the middle of the base.</p>
  <p>The vertical line through the centre of gravity falls within the base, so the weight does not create a toppling moment about the edge.</p>
  <p>If the block tilts so that this line falls outside the base, the weight can create a turning effect that topples it.</p>', 1),
  (432, 432, '<h2>Making objects harder to topple</h2>
  <p>Two common ways to increase an object''s stability are:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Lower its centre of gravity.</li>
    <li>Increase the width of its base of support.</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>A tall cabinet is more likely to topple than a shorter, broader cabinet with a lower centre of gravity and a wider base.</p>
  <p>Lowering the centre of gravity makes the vertical line through it less likely to move outside the base when the cabinet tilts slightly.</p>
  <p>Widening the base gives the line of action more room to move before reaching an edge.</p>
  <p><b>Application:</b> wide wheelbases and low centres of gravity help vehicles resist toppling.</p>', 1),
  (433, 433, '<h2>Why an object topples</h2>
  <p>An object may topple when it tilts far enough that the vertical line through its centre of gravity falls outside its base of support.</p>
  <p>At that point, the weight produces a moment about the edge of the base that tends to increase the tilt.</p>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>A box is slowly tilted about its lower edge.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>While the weight''s line of action falls within the base, the box can remain supported.</li>
    <li>When the line reaches the edge, the box is at the limiting condition for toppling.</li>
    <li>When the line falls outside the base, the weight creates a toppling moment.</li>
  </ul>
  <p><b>Key distinction:</b> toppling is a rotational effect; sliding is motion along the surface and involves different conditions.</p>', 1),
  (434, 434, '<h2>Turning effects in tools</h2>
  <p>Tools such as spanners, door handles and bottle openers use turning effects to make tasks easier.</p>
  <p>The moment of a force is:</p>
  <p><b>Moment = force × perpendicular distance from pivot</b></p>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>A spanner applies a turning force of 40 N at a perpendicular distance of 0.25 m from a nut.</p>
  <p>Moment = 40 × 0.25 = <b>10 N m</b>.</p>
  <p>A longer spanner can produce a larger moment for the same applied force, provided the force acts in the same effective direction.</p>
  <p><b>Important:</b> the pivot is the point or axis about which the object turns.</p>', 1),
  (435, 435, '
  <h2>Work Done by a Force</h2>
  <p>In physics, work is done when a force causes displacement. For a constant force acting in the same direction as the displacement, work done is the force multiplied by the distance moved.</p>

  <h3>Formula</h3>
  <p>\(W = Fs\)</p>
  <ul>
    <li>W = work done, measured in joules (J)</li>
    <li>F = force in newtons (N)</li>
    <li>s = displacement in metres (m), along the force''s direction</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Worked example 1</h3>
  <p>A force of 10 N moves a box 3 m in the direction of the force.</p>
  <p>Work done = 10 × 3 = <strong>30 J</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2</h3>
  <p>A force of 50 N moves an object 0.2 m in the force''s direction.</p>
  <p>Work done = 50 × 0.2 = <strong>10 J</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: No displacement</h3>
  <p>A person holds a stationary bag. Although the person applies an upward force, the bag has no displacement. The mechanical work done by that force on the stationary bag is zero.</p>

  <h3>Common mistake</h3>
  <p>In physics, feeling tired does not by itself mean that mechanical work has been done on an object. There must be displacement in the direction of the force, or a force component along the displacement.</p>
  ', 1),
  (436, 436, '
  <h2>Gravitational Potential Energy</h2>
  <p>Gravitational potential energy is energy associated with an object''s position in a gravitational field. Near Earth''s surface, the change in gravitational potential energy can be calculated using mass, gravitational field strength and vertical height change.</p>

  <h3>Formula</h3>
  <p>\(\Delta E_p = mg\Delta h\)</p>
  <ul>
    <li>m = mass in kilograms (kg)</li>
    <li>g = gravitational field strength in N/kg</li>
    <li>Δh = vertical height change in metres (m)</li>
    <li>ΔEp = change in gravitational potential energy in joules (J)</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Worked example 1</h3>
  <p>A 2 kg object is lifted vertically by 3 m. Take g = 10 N/kg.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Write ΔEp = mgΔh.</li>
    <li>Substitute ΔEp = 2 × 10 × 3.</li>
    <li>Change in potential energy = <strong>60 J</strong>.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Worked example 2</h3>
  <p>A 5 kg object is raised by 2 m where g = 9.8 N/kg.</p>
  <p>ΔEp = 5 × 9.8 × 2 = <strong>98 J</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Find height change</h3>
  <p>A 4 kg object gains 80 J of gravitational potential energy. If g = 10 N/kg, Δh = 80 ÷ (4 × 10) = <strong>2 m</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Common mistake</h3>
  <p>Use vertical height change, not the distance travelled along a sloping path. The formula gives the change in gravitational potential energy near Earth''s surface.</p>
  ', 1),
  (437, 437, '<h2>Kinetic Energy</h2>
  <p>Kinetic energy is the energy an object possesses because it is moving.</p>
  <p><strong>Formula:</strong> KE = 1/2 × m × v²</p>
  <ul>
    <li>KE = kinetic energy in joules (J)</li>
    <li>m = mass in kilograms (kg)</li>
    <li>v = speed in metres per second (m/s)</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>A 4 kg object moves at 3 m/s. Calculate its kinetic energy.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Write the formula: KE = 1/2 × m × v².</li>
    <li>Substitute: KE = 1/2 × 4 × 3².</li>
    <li>Square the speed: 3² = 9.</li>
    <li>Calculate: KE = 2 × 9 = 18 J.</li>
  </ol>
  <p><strong>Answer: 18 J.</strong></p>
  <h3>Important relationship</h3>
  <p>At constant mass, doubling speed makes kinetic energy four times greater because speed is squared. Doubling mass at constant speed doubles kinetic energy.</p>', 1),
  (438, 438, '<h2>Conservation of Energy</h2>
  <p>The principle of conservation of energy states that energy cannot be created or destroyed. It can only be transferred or transformed from one form to another.</p>
  <h3>Example: a falling object</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>At a height, the object has gravitational potential energy.</li>
    <li>As it falls, gravitational potential energy decreases.</li>
    <li>Its kinetic energy increases as its speed increases.</li>
    <li>Ignoring air resistance, the decrease in potential energy equals the increase in kinetic energy.</li>
  </ol>
  <h3>Energy in real systems</h3>
  <p>When friction or air resistance acts, some mechanical energy is transferred to the surroundings, often as thermal energy and sound. The total energy is still conserved.</p>
  <p><strong>Key idea:</strong> Energy is transferred or transformed, not destroyed.</p>', 1),
  (439, 439, '<h2>Power</h2>
  <p>Power is the rate at which work is done or energy is transferred.</p>
  <p><strong>Formula:</strong> P = W/t, where W is work done in joules and t is time in seconds.</p>
  <p>Power can also be calculated using P = E/t when E is the energy transferred.</p>
  <p><strong>Unit:</strong> watt (W). One watt equals one joule per second.</p>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>A motor does 600 J of work in 20 s.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Write P = W/t.</li>
    <li>Substitute: P = 600/20.</li>
    <li>Calculate: P = 30 W.</li>
  </ol>
  <p><strong>Answer: 30 W.</strong></p>
  <p>Doing the same amount of work in less time requires greater power.</p>', 1),
  (440, 440, '<h2>Efficiency</h2>
  <p>Efficiency measures the fraction of the input energy or power that becomes useful output.</p>
  <p><strong>Efficiency (%) = (useful output energy ÷ total input energy) × 100%</strong></p>
  <p>The same relationship applies to useful output power divided by input power.</p>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>A device receives 500 J and provides 350 J of useful energy.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Efficiency = (useful output ÷ input) × 100%.</li>
    <li>Efficiency = (350 ÷ 500) × 100%.</li>
    <li>Efficiency = 70%.</li>
  </ol>
  <p><strong>Answer: 70%.</strong></p>
  <p>For an ordinary energy-converting device, efficiency cannot exceed 100%. Some input energy is often transferred to the surroundings in less useful forms.</p>', 1),
  (441, 441, '
  <h2>Meaning of Work</h2>

  <h3>Core idea</h3>
  <p>
    In physics, work is done when a force causes displacement.
    When a constant force acts in the direction of displacement:
  </p>
  <p><b>Work done = force × displacement</b></p>
  <p><b>W = Fd</b></p>
  <p>
    Work is measured in joules (J), force in newtons (N), and
    displacement in metres (m).
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Pushing a box</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A force of 10 N moves a box 3 m in the
  direction of the force. Calculate the work done.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> W = Fd.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> W = 10 × 3.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> 30 J.</p>
  <p><b>Check:</b> N × m = J.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Pulling a trolley</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A student pulls a trolley with a constant
  force of 15 N through 4 m in the direction of the force. Find
  the work done by the pulling force.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify F = 15 N and d = 4 m.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> W = Fd = 15 × 4.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> 60 J.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: A smaller force</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A force of 6 N moves an object 5 m in the
  direction of the force. Calculate the work done.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> W = Fd.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> W = 6 × 5.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> 30 J.</p>

  <h3>Remember</h3>
  <p>Work in physics is not simply the effort someone feels they
  are making. The force must cause displacement in the relevant
  direction.</p>
  ', 1),
  (442, 442, '
  <h2>Calculating Force or Displacement from Work</h2>

  <h3>Core idea</h3>
  <p>
    When a constant force acts in the direction of displacement:
  </p>
  <p><b>W = Fd</b></p>
  <p>Rearrange the formula to find either unknown:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Force:</b> F = W/d</li>
    <li><b>Displacement:</b> d = W/F</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Finding force</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A force does 80 J of work while moving an
  object 4 m in the force''s direction. Find the force.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> W = Fd, so F = W/d.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> F = 80/4.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> 20 N.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Finding displacement</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A 12 N force does 72 J of work. Find the
  displacement in the direction of the force.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> d = W/F.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> d = 72/12.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> 6 m.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: A heavier load</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A constant force does 150 J of work while
  moving an object 5 m in its direction. Calculate the force.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> F = W/d.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> F = 150/5.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> 30 N.</p>

  <h3>Remember</h3>
  <p>Choose the rearranged formula that matches the unknown.
  Convert displacement to metres before substituting if necessary.</p>
  ', 1),
  (443, 443, '
  <h2>When Work Done Is Zero</h2>

  <h3>Core idea</h3>
  <p>
    For a constant force, work depends on the displacement in the
    direction of the force. If there is no displacement, that force
    does no work on the object.
  </p>
  <p>
    If the force is perpendicular to the displacement, the work
    done by that force is also zero.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Pushing a wall</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A student pushes a wall with a force of 100 N,
  but the wall does not move. How much work does the push do on the wall?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> The wall has zero displacement.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> W = Fd = 100 × 0.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> 0 J of work is done on the wall by the push.</p>
  <p><b>Check:</b> The student may use energy physiologically, but
  the force does no mechanical work on the wall because it does not move.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Carrying a bag horizontally</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A student carries a bag horizontally at
  constant height. The upward supporting force is 40 N. What work
  does this upward force do during a horizontal displacement of 5 m?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> The support force acts upward.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> The displacement is horizontal, so it is
  perpendicular to the support force.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> The upward supporting force does 0 J of work.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Lifting a book</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A book is lifted vertically by 2 m. Its
  weight is 10 N downward. Find the work done by gravity.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Gravity acts downward, opposite to the upward displacement.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Work done by gravity = − weight × displacement.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> W = −10 × 2.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> −20 J.</p>
  <p><b>Meaning:</b> The negative sign shows that gravity opposes
  the displacement.</p>

  <h3>Remember</h3>
  <p>Distinguish between work done by an individual force and the
  total work done by all forces. Different forces can do different
  amounts of work on the same object.</p>
  ', 1),
  (444, 444, '
  <h2>Purpose and Parts of Simple Machines</h2>

  <h3>Core idea</h3>
  <p>
    A machine helps us perform a task by changing the size or
    direction of a force, or by changing the distance over which
    the force acts.
  </p>
  <p>Common machine terms:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Load:</b> the resistance or object being moved.</li>
    <li><b>Effort:</b> the force applied to operate the machine.</li>
    <li><b>Fulcrum:</b> the pivot about which a lever turns.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    A machine does not create energy. In real machines, some energy
    is transferred to the surroundings, often through friction.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: A lever lifting a stone</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A lever is used to lift a heavy stone. Identify
  the load and the effort.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> The stone is the object being moved.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> The force applied by the person operates the lever.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> The stone is the load; the person''s applied
  force is the effort.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: A seesaw</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> In a seesaw, what is the fulcrum?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> A lever rotates around a pivot.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> The seesaw turns around its central support.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> The central support is the fulcrum.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: A ramp</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A worker uses a ramp to push a load into a
  truck. How does the ramp help?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> The load gains height over a longer distance.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> For an ideal ramp, the required effort can be
  smaller than lifting the load vertically, but it acts over a
  longer distance.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> The ramp reduces the effort needed by increasing
  the distance over which it is applied, ideally without changing
  the total work required.</p>

  <h3>Remember</h3>
  <p>Machines make tasks easier by changing force, direction or
  distance. They do not produce energy from nothing.</p>
  ', 1),
  (445, 445, '
  <h2>Mechanical Advantage</h2>

  <h3>Core idea</h3>
  <p>
    Mechanical advantage (M.A.) compares the load force produced
    by a machine with the effort force applied to it.
  </p>
  <p><b>M.A. = Load / Effort</b></p>
  <p>
    Load and effort must be expressed in the same force units.
    Mechanical advantage is a ratio and has no unit.
  </p>
  <p>
    If M.A. is greater than 1, the load force is greater than the
    effort force. If M.A. is less than 1, the effort force is
    greater than the load force.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Finding mechanical advantage</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A machine lifts a load of 200 N using an
  effort of 50 N. Calculate its mechanical advantage.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> M.A. = Load/Effort.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> M.A. = 200/50.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> M.A. = 4.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Finding effort</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A machine has a mechanical advantage of 5
  and lifts a load of 300 N. Find the effort.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> M.A. = Load/Effort.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Effort = Load/M.A.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Effort = 300/5.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> 60 N.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Finding the load</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A machine has a mechanical advantage of 3.
  An effort of 40 N is applied. Calculate the load.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Load = M.A. × Effort.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Load = 3 × 40.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> 120 N.</p>

  <h3>Remember</h3>
  <p>Mechanical advantage tells you how the load force compares
  with the applied effort. It does not by itself tell you how
  efficient the machine is.</p>
  ', 1),
  (446, 446, '
  <h2>Meaning and Unit of Power</h2>

  <h3>Core idea</h3>
  <p>
    Power is the rate at which work is done or energy is transferred.
    It tells us how quickly work is done, not simply how much work
    is done.
  </p>
  <p><b>Power = Work done / Time taken</b></p>
  <p><b>P = W/t</b></p>
  <p>
    Power is measured in watts (W). One watt is equal to one joule
    of work done per second.
  </p>
  <p><b>1 W = 1 J/s</b></p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Work done in one second</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A machine does 100 J of work in 1 s.
  What is its average power?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> P = W/t.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> P = 100/1.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> 100 W.</p>
  <p><b>Check:</b> 100 J/s = 100 W.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: A longer time</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A motor transfers 600 J of energy in 3 s.
  Calculate its average power.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> P = W/t.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> P = 600/3.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> 200 W.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Same work, different time</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> Two machines each do 400 J of work.
  Machine A takes 2 s while machine B takes 4 s. Calculate
  the average power of each machine.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> For A, P = 400/2 = 200 W.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> For B, P = 400/4 = 100 W.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> A has average power 200 W; B has average
  power 100 W.</p>
  <p><b>Conclusion:</b> For the same work, the machine that takes
  less time has greater average power.</p>

  <h3>Remember</h3>
  <p>Power measures how quickly work is done. The SI unit is
  the watt, equivalent to one joule per second.</p>
  ', 1),
  (447, 447, '
  <h2>Calculating Power from Work and Time</h2>

  <h3>Core idea</h3>
  <p>
    When the work done and the time taken are known, calculate
    average power using:
  </p>
  <p><b>P = W/t</b></p>
  <p>
    Use joules for work and seconds for time to obtain power
    in watts.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Lifting a load</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A worker does 1,200 J of work lifting a load
  in 6 s. Find the average power.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify W = 1,200 J and t = 6 s.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> P = W/t = 1,200/6.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> 200 W.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: A pump</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A pump does 9,000 J of work in 30 s.
  Calculate its average power.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> P = W/t.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> P = 9,000/30.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> 300 W.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Converting minutes to seconds</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A machine does 12,000 J of work in 2 minutes.
  Calculate its average power in watts.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Convert time to seconds: 2 × 60 = 120 s.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> P = W/t = 12,000/120.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> 100 W.</p>
  <p><b>Check:</b> Always convert minutes to seconds when using
  joules and watts.</p>

  <h3>Remember</h3>
  <p>Power is the rate of work. Convert all quantities to
  compatible SI units before calculating.</p>
  ', 1),
  (448, 448, '
  <h2>Calculating Work or Time from Power</h2>

  <h3>Core idea</h3>
  <p>
    The power formula can be rearranged to calculate work or time:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Power:</b> P = W/t</li>
    <li><b>Work:</b> W = Pt</li>
    <li><b>Time:</b> t = W/P</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Remember that 1 W = 1 J/s, so power multiplied by time
    gives work in joules.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Finding work</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A motor operates at 250 W for 8 s.
  How much work does it do at that average power?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> W = Pt.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> W = 250 × 8.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> 2,000 J.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Finding time</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A machine does 3,600 J of work at an
  average power of 600 W. Find the time taken.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> t = W/P.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> t = 3,600/600.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> 6 s.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: A stronger motor</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A motor operating at 400 W transfers energy
  for 15 s. How much work is done at that power?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> W = Pt.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> W = 400 × 15.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> 6,000 J.</p>

  <h3>Remember</h3>
  <p>Choose the version of the formula that isolates the unknown.
  Keep work in joules, power in watts and time in seconds.</p>
  ', 1),
  (449, 449, '
  <h2>Comparing Power</h2>

  <h3>Core idea</h3>
  <p>
    Power compares how quickly work is done or energy is transferred.
    When two machines do the same work, the one taking less time
    has greater average power.
  </p>
  <p>
    When two machines operate for the same time, the one doing
    more work has greater average power.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Same work, different times</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> Machine A does 600 J in 3 s. Machine B does
  the same 600 J in 6 s. Compare their average powers.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> P(A) = 600/3 = 200 W.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> P(B) = 600/6 = 100 W.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Machine A has twice the average power of B.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Same time, different work</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> Machine A does 900 J in 3 s. Machine B does
  1,500 J in the same 3 s. Which has greater average power?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> P(A) = 900/3 = 300 W.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> P(B) = 1,500/3 = 500 W.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Machine B has greater average power.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Comparing workers</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> Worker A does 2,400 J of work in 12 s.
  Worker B does 1,800 J in 6 s. Compare their average powers.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> P(A) = 2,400/12 = 200 W.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> P(B) = 1,800/6 = 300 W.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Worker B has the greater average power, even
  though worker A does more total work.</p>

  <h3>Remember</h3>
  <p>Do not compare total work alone. Calculate work per unit time
  when the question asks about power.</p>
  ', 1),
  (450, 450, '
  <h2>Power, Force and Speed</h2>

  <h3>Core idea</h3>
  <p>
    When a force acts in the direction of an object''s motion,
    the instantaneous power delivered by that force is:
  </p>
  <p><b>P = Fv</b></p>
  <p>
    Here, P is power in watts, F is force in newtons and v is
    speed in metres per second.
  </p>
  <p>
    This relation applies to the component of force in the direction
    of motion. If force and velocity are not aligned, only the
    force component along the motion contributes to the power.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: A moving vehicle</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A vehicle experiences a driving force of
  500 N while moving at 10 m/s. Calculate the power delivered
  by that force.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> P = Fv.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> P = 500 × 10.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> 5,000 W, or 5 kW.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: A faster vehicle</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A motor supplies a force of 200 N in the
  direction of motion of a vehicle travelling at 15 m/s. Find
  the power delivered by the force.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> P = Fv.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> P = 200 × 15.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> 3,000 W, or 3 kW.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Finding force</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A motor delivers 6,000 W of power to an
  object moving at 12 m/s. Assuming the force is in the direction
  of motion, calculate the force.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> P = Fv, so F = P/v.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> F = 6,000/12.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> 500 N.</p>

  <h3>Remember</h3>
  <p>Use P = Fv when the force and speed are known. Use P = W/t
  when work and time are known. Make sure the force component
  along the motion is used.</p>
  ', 1),
  (451, 451, '
  <h2>Meaning of Kinetic Energy</h2>

  <h3>1. What is kinetic energy?</h3>
  <p>
    <b>Kinetic energy</b> is the energy an object possesses because it is moving.
    A moving car, a rolling ball and a running student all have kinetic energy.
  </p>

  <p>
    An object at rest has zero kinetic energy because its speed is zero.
    The faster a moving object travels, the greater its kinetic energy,
    provided its mass remains constant.
  </p>

  <h3>2. The formula</h3>
  <p>
    <b>KE = ½mv²</b>
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>KE = kinetic energy, measured in joules (J)</li>
    <li>m = mass, measured in kilograms (kg)</li>
    <li>v = speed, measured in metres per second (m/s)</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    The speed is squared before multiplying by the mass and one-half.
    This means that doubling speed does not merely double kinetic energy.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: A moving ball</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> Explain why a moving ball possesses kinetic energy.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Kinetic energy is energy due to motion.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> The ball is moving.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> The ball possesses kinetic energy because it is in motion.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: An object at rest</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> Find the kinetic energy of a 4 kg object at rest.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Write the formula: KE = ½mv².</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Substitute m = 4 kg and v = 0 m/s.</p>
  <p>KE = ½ × 4 × 0²</p>
  <p>KE = 0 J</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> The kinetic energy is 0 J.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Identifying the quantities</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A trolley has a mass of 3 kg and moves at 2 m/s. Identify the values needed to calculate its kinetic energy.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Mass, m = 3 kg.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Speed, v = 2 m/s.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> The required formula is KE = ½mv².</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Use m = 3 kg and v = 2 m/s.</p>

  <h3>Remember</h3>
  <p>
    Kinetic energy depends on motion. Always identify the mass and speed
    before using KE = ½mv².
  </p>
  ', 1),
  (452, 452, '
  <h2>Calculating Kinetic Energy</h2>

  <h3>Formula</h3>
  <p><b>KE = ½mv²</b></p>

  <p>
    To calculate kinetic energy:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>Write down the mass and speed.</li>
    <li>Square the speed.</li>
    <li>Multiply the squared speed by the mass.</li>
    <li>Multiply the result by one-half.</li>
    <li>Give the answer in joules.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Worked example 1</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> Calculate the kinetic energy of a 2 kg object moving at 3 m/s.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> m = 2 kg, v = 3 m/s.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> KE = ½mv².</p>
  <p>KE = ½ × 2 × 3²</p>
  <p>KE = 1 × 9</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> KE = 9 J.</p>
  <p><b>Check:</b> The speed was squared before multiplication.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A 5 kg trolley moves at 4 m/s. Find its kinetic energy.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> m = 5 kg, v = 4 m/s.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> KE = ½ × 5 × 4².</p>
  <p>KE = 2.5 × 16</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> KE = 40 J.</p>
  <p><b>Check:</b> 4² = 16, not 8.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> Calculate the kinetic energy of a 1,200 kg car moving at 10 m/s.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> m = 1,200 kg, v = 10 m/s.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> KE = ½ × 1,200 × 10².</p>
  <p>KE = 600 × 100</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> KE = 60,000 J.</p>
  <p><b>Check:</b> The answer is in joules, not newtons.</p>

  <h3>Common mistake</h3>
  <p>
    Do not calculate ½ × mass × speed and stop there.
    The formula requires speed squared: v × v.
  </p>
  ', 1),
  (453, 453, '
  <h2>Mass and Speed in Kinetic Energy</h2>

  <h3>1. Effect of mass</h3>
  <p>
    From KE = ½mv², kinetic energy is directly proportional to mass
    when speed is constant.
  </p>
  <p>
    If mass doubles while speed stays the same, kinetic energy doubles.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Effect of speed</h3>
  <p>
    Kinetic energy is proportional to the square of speed.
    This makes speed especially important.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Double the speed: kinetic energy becomes 4 times greater.</li>
    <li>Triple the speed: kinetic energy becomes 9 times greater.</li>
    <li>Halve the speed: kinetic energy becomes one-quarter.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Doubling mass</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A 2 kg object moving at 4 m/s has a certain kinetic energy. What happens to its kinetic energy if its mass doubles and its speed remains unchanged?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Original KE = ½ × 2 × 4² = 16 J.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> The new mass is 4 kg.</p>
  <p>New KE = ½ × 4 × 4² = 32 J.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Kinetic energy doubles from 16 J to 32 J.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Doubling speed</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> An object''s speed increases from 3 m/s to 6 m/s while its mass remains constant. By what factor does its kinetic energy change?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Speed factor = 6 ÷ 3 = 2.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Square the factor: 2² = 4.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Its kinetic energy becomes four times greater.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Tripling speed</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> The speed of a moving object triples. Its mass does not change. Find the factor by which its kinetic energy changes.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Speed factor = 3.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Kinetic energy factor = 3².</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Kinetic energy becomes 9 times greater.</p>

  <h3>Remember</h3>
  <p>
    When comparing two situations, keep track of what changes.
    Mass affects kinetic energy directly; speed affects it through its square.
  </p>
  ', 1),
  (454, 454, '
  <h2>Meaning of Gravitational Potential Energy</h2>

  <h3>1. What is gravitational potential energy?</h3>
  <p>
    <b>Gravitational potential energy (GPE)</b> is energy an object has
    because of its position or height in a gravitational field.
  </p>
  <p>
    For example, a book held above the floor has gravitational potential
    energy relative to the floor. When it falls, this energy can be
    transferred into kinetic energy.
  </p>

  <h3>2. The formula near Earth''s surface</h3>
  <p><b>GPE = mgh</b></p>
  <ul>
    <li>GPE = gravitational potential energy in joules (J)</li>
    <li>m = mass in kilograms (kg)</li>
    <li>g = gravitational field strength in N/kg</li>
    <li>h = vertical height above the chosen reference level in metres (m)</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    In many school questions, use g = 10 N/kg when the question instructs
    you to do so. If a question gives another value, use the supplied value.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Identifying GPE</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> Why does a book resting on a high shelf have gravitational potential energy relative to the floor?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> GPE is associated with position in a gravitational field.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> The shelf is above the floor.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> The book has GPE because it is elevated above the chosen reference level.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Identifying the quantities</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A 3 kg object is held 2 m above the ground where g = 10 N/kg. Identify the quantities needed to calculate its GPE.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Mass, m = 3 kg.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Gravitational field strength, g = 10 N/kg.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Height, h = 2 m.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Use GPE = mgh with these three values.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Comparing heights</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> Two identical objects are held at different heights above the same floor. Which has more GPE?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Both objects have the same mass.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> The higher object has the greater height h.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> The higher object has more GPE relative to the floor.</p>

  <h3>Remember</h3>
  <p>
    Gravitational potential energy depends on mass, gravitational field
    strength and vertical height relative to a reference level.
  </p>
  ', 1),
  (455, 455, '
  <h2>Calculating Gravitational Potential Energy</h2>

  <h3>Formula</h3>
  <p><b>GPE = mgh</b></p>

  <p>
    To calculate GPE:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>Identify the mass, m.</li>
    <li>Identify gravitational field strength, g.</li>
    <li>Identify the vertical height, h.</li>
    <li>Multiply the three values.</li>
    <li>Give the answer in joules.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Worked example 1</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> Calculate the GPE of a 2 kg object held 5 m above the ground. Take g = 10 N/kg.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> m = 2 kg, g = 10 N/kg, h = 5 m.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> GPE = mgh.</p>
  <p>GPE = 2 × 10 × 5</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> GPE = 100 J.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A 4 kg bag is lifted to a height of 3 m. Calculate the gain in GPE using g = 10 N/kg.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> m = 4 kg, g = 10 N/kg, h = 3 m.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Gain in GPE = mgh.</p>
  <p>Gain in GPE = 4 × 10 × 3</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> The gain in GPE is 120 J.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A 6 kg object is raised vertically by 2 m where g = 9.8 N/kg. Calculate its gain in GPE.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> m = 6 kg, g = 9.8 N/kg, h = 2 m.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Gain in GPE = mgh.</p>
  <p>Gain in GPE = 6 × 9.8 × 2</p>
  <p>Gain in GPE = 117.6 J</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> 117.6 J.</p>

  <h3>Common mistakes</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Do not use speed in the GPE formula.</li>
    <li>Use mass in kilograms, not grams.</li>
    <li>Use vertical height, not the distance travelled along a slope.</li>
    <li>Use the value of g given in the question.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 1),
  (456, 456, '
  <h2>Principle of Conservation of Energy</h2>

  <h3>1. The principle</h3>
  <p>
    The <b>principle of conservation of energy</b> states that energy
    cannot be created or destroyed. It can only be transferred from
    one object or place to another, or transformed from one form to another.
  </p>

  <p>
    In a closed system, the total energy remains constant when all forms
    of energy are included.
  </p>

  <h3>2. What happens in real situations?</h3>
  <p>
    When a moving object slows down because of friction, its kinetic energy
    decreases. Energy is transferred mainly to the thermal energy of the
    object and its surroundings, with some possibly transferred as sound.
    The energy has not disappeared.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: A falling stone</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A stone falls from a height. Describe the energy change, ignoring air resistance.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Before falling, the stone has gravitational potential energy.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> As it falls, its height decreases and its speed increases.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Gravitational potential energy is transformed into kinetic energy. The total mechanical energy remains constant if air resistance is ignored.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: A braking bicycle</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A bicycle slows down when its brakes are applied. Has its kinetic energy been destroyed?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> The bicycle''s speed decreases.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Its kinetic energy decreases.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Friction in the brakes and between surfaces transfers energy to thermal energy and the surroundings.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> No. The energy is transferred and transformed; it is not destroyed.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: An electric lamp</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A lamp receives 100 J of electrical energy. The energy is eventually transferred as 15 J of light and 85 J of thermal energy. Verify conservation of energy.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Total energy supplied = 100 J.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Total energy transferred out = 15 J + 85 J.</p>
  <p>Total = 100 J.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> The energy is conserved because 100 J supplied equals 100 J transferred out.</p>

  <h3>Remember</h3>
  <p>
    Always account for all energy forms. A decrease in one form does not
    mean that energy has been destroyed.
  </p>
  ', 1),
  (457, 457, '
  <h2>Energy Transfer and Transformation</h2>

  <h3>1. Energy transfer</h3>
  <p>
    An <b>energy transfer</b> occurs when energy moves from one object
    or place to another. The energy may remain in the same form during
    the transfer.
  </p>
  <p>
    Example: thermal energy moves from hot water to a cooler metal spoon.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Energy transformation</h3>
  <p>
    An <b>energy transformation</b> occurs when energy changes from one
    form to another.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Electrical energy to light and thermal energy in a lamp.</li>
    <li>Chemical energy to kinetic energy in a moving person.</li>
    <li>Gravitational potential energy to kinetic energy in a falling object.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    A single process can involve both transfer and transformation.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: A lamp</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> State the main energy transformation in an electric lamp.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> The lamp receives electrical energy.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> It produces light and thermal energy.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Electrical energy is transformed into light and thermal energy.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: A hot spoon</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A metal spoon is placed in hot tea. Thermal energy moves from the tea into the spoon. Is this a transfer or transformation?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> The energy moves from the tea to the spoon.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> The energy remains thermal energy during this transfer.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> This is an energy transfer.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: A falling object</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> Identify the main energy transformation as a stone falls, ignoring air resistance.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> The stone initially has gravitational potential energy.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Its speed increases as it falls.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Gravitational potential energy is transformed into kinetic energy.</p>

  <h3>Remember</h3>
  <p>
    Ask two separate questions: Has energy moved between objects or places?
    Has energy changed form? Some processes involve both.
  </p>
  ', 1),
  (458, 458, '
  <h2>Energy Changes During Falling</h2>

  <h3>1. Before the object falls</h3>
  <p>
    An object held above the ground has gravitational potential energy
    relative to the ground. If it is at rest, its kinetic energy is zero.
  </p>

  <h3>2. As the object falls</h3>
  <p>
    Its height decreases, so its gravitational potential energy decreases.
    Its speed increases, so its kinetic energy increases.
  </p>

  <p>
    If air resistance is ignored, the decrease in GPE equals the increase
    in KE. If air resistance is significant, some mechanical energy is
    transferred to the surroundings, mainly as thermal energy.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Identifying the energy change</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A ball is released from rest above the ground. Describe its energy changes as it falls, ignoring air resistance.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Initially, the ball has GPE and zero KE.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> As it falls, GPE decreases and KE increases.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> GPE is transformed into KE. The total mechanical energy remains constant.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Comparing the top and bottom</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A ball falls from a height. Compare its GPE and KE at the top and just before reaching the ground. Ignore air resistance.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> At the top, the ball has maximum GPE relative to the ground and zero KE if released from rest.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Just before reaching the ground, its GPE relative to the ground is approximately zero and its KE is greatest.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> GPE decreases while KE increases. Their total remains constant.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Effect of air resistance</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A paper sheet falls through air. Why might its kinetic energy not equal the GPE it has lost?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Air resistance acts against the sheet''s motion.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Some energy is transferred to the surroundings through the interaction with air.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Some of the lost GPE is transferred to the surroundings, so not all of it becomes the sheet''s kinetic energy.</p>

  <h3>Remember</h3>
  <p>
    For a falling object, always state whether air resistance is ignored.
    This determines whether the lost GPE equals the gain in KE.
  </p>
  ', 1),
  (459, 459, '
  <h2>Calculating Speed from Lost Gravitational Potential Energy</h2>

  <h3>1. The energy relationship</h3>
  <p>
    When an object falls from rest and air resistance is ignored,
    its loss of gravitational potential energy equals its gain in
    kinetic energy.
  </p>

  <p><b>Loss in GPE = Gain in KE</b></p>
  <p><b>mgh = ½mv²</b></p>

  <p>
    Cancelling the common mass gives:
  </p>
  <p><b>v² = 2gh</b></p>
  <p><b>v = √(2gh)</b></p>

  <p>
    This simplified formula applies when the object starts from rest,
    falls vertically through height h, and air resistance is ignored.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Falling through 5 m</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> An object falls from rest through 5 m. Calculate its speed just before reaching the ground. Take g = 10 m/s² and ignore air resistance.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> v = √(2gh).</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> v = √(2 × 10 × 5).</p>
  <p>v = √100</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> v = 10 m/s.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Falling through 20 m</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A stone falls from rest through 20 m. Calculate its speed just before impact, taking g = 10 m/s².</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> v = √(2gh).</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> v = √(2 × 10 × 20).</p>
  <p>v = √400</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> v = 20 m/s.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: A smaller drop</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A ball falls from rest through 1.8 m. Calculate its speed just before reaching the ground. Take g = 10 m/s².</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> v = √(2gh).</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> v = √(2 × 10 × 1.8).</p>
  <p>v = √36</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> v = 6 m/s.</p>

  <h3>Common mistakes</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Do not forget the square root when finding v.</li>
    <li>Use height lost, not total path length along a curved route.</li>
    <li>Do not use this simplified formula if the stated assumptions do not apply.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 1),
  (460, 460, '
  <h2>Useful and Wasted Energy</h2>

  <h3>1. Useful energy</h3>
  <p>
    <b>Useful energy</b> is the energy transferred in the form needed
    to achieve a device''s intended purpose.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Wasted energy</h3>
  <p>
    <b>Wasted energy</b> is energy transferred into forms or places that
    are not useful for the intended purpose. It is often transferred
    to the surroundings as thermal energy or sound.
  </p>

  <p>
    Wasted energy has not been destroyed. It is usually less useful
    because it spreads into the surroundings and becomes harder to use.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Energy efficiency</h3>
  <p>
    Efficiency compares the useful energy output with the total energy input.
  </p>
  <p><b>Efficiency = (Useful energy output ÷ Total energy input) × 100%</b></p>

  <p>
    When all energy outputs are accounted for:
    total energy input = useful energy output + wasted energy output.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: An electric lamp</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> An electric lamp receives 100 J of electrical energy. It produces 20 J of useful light energy. Calculate its efficiency.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Useful output = 20 J; total input = 100 J.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Efficiency = (20 ÷ 100) × 100%.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Efficiency = 20%.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Finding wasted energy</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A motor receives 500 J of energy and transfers 350 J usefully into mechanical energy. Calculate the wasted energy if these are the only two output categories.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Wasted energy = total input − useful output.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Wasted energy = 500 J − 350 J.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Wasted energy = 150 J.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Comparing efficiencies</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> Device A receives 200 J and produces 100 J of useful energy. Device B receives 200 J and produces 150 J of useful energy. Calculate both efficiencies and identify the more efficient device.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Efficiency of A = (100 ÷ 200) × 100% = 50%.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Efficiency of B = (150 ÷ 200) × 100% = 75%.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Device B is more efficient because its efficiency is 75%, compared with 50% for A.</p>

  <h3>Remember</h3>
  <p>
    Efficiency is a percentage of the energy input. For ordinary devices,
    it cannot exceed 100% when all energy inputs and outputs are correctly
    accounted for.
  </p>
  ', 1),
  (461, 461, '<h2>Simple Machines</h2>
  <p>A simple machine makes a task easier by changing the size or direction of a force, or the distance over which the force acts.</p>
  <p>Common simple machines include levers, pulleys, inclined planes, and wheel-and-axle systems.</p>
  <h3>Examples</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Lever:</strong> a crowbar can help lift a heavy load.</li>
    <li><strong>Pulley:</strong> a fixed pulley can change the direction in which a person pulls.</li>
    <li><strong>Inclined plane:</strong> a ramp allows a load to be raised using a smaller force over a longer distance.</li>
    <li><strong>Wheel and axle:</strong> a handle attached to an axle can help turn a mechanism.</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Work and machines</h3>
  <p>A machine does not create energy. In an ideal machine, input work equals output work. In a real machine, the input work is greater than the useful output because of friction and other energy transfers.</p>', 1),
  (462, 462, '<h2>Mechanical Advantage</h2>
  <p>Mechanical advantage (MA) compares the load force a machine moves with the effort force applied to it.</p>
  <p><strong>MA = Load ÷ Effort</strong></p>
  <p>Load and effort must be measured in the same units, usually newtons (N). Mechanical advantage has no unit because it is a ratio.</p>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>A lever lifts a load of 600 N using an effort of 150 N.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>MA = Load ÷ Effort.</li>
    <li>MA = 600 ÷ 150.</li>
    <li>MA = 4.</li>
  </ol>
  <p><strong>Answer: MA = 4.</strong> The load force is four times the effort force.</p>
  <p>Mechanical advantage does not tell us by itself whether a machine is efficient.</p>', 1),
  (463, 463, '<h2>Velocity Ratio</h2>
  <p>Velocity ratio (VR) compares the distance moved by the effort with the distance moved by the load during the same operation.</p>
  <p><strong>VR = Distance moved by effort ÷ Distance moved by load</strong></p>
  <p>Use distances measured in the same units. Velocity ratio has no unit.</p>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>The effort moves 2 m while the load rises 0.5 m.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>VR = distance moved by effort ÷ distance moved by load.</li>
    <li>VR = 2 ÷ 0.5.</li>
    <li>VR = 4.</li>
  </ol>
  <p><strong>Answer: VR = 4.</strong></p>
  <p>A high velocity ratio means the effort moves a greater distance than the load.</p>', 1),
  (464, 464, '<h2>Mechanical Advantage and Velocity Ratio</h2>
  <p>Mechanical advantage compares forces; velocity ratio compares distances moved.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>MA = Load ÷ Effort</strong></li>
    <li><strong>VR = Distance moved by effort ÷ Distance moved by load</strong></li>
  </ul>
  <p>For an ideal machine, MA = VR because input work equals output work.</p>
  <p>For an ordinary real machine, friction and other losses mean that MA is generally less than VR.</p>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>A machine has MA = 3 and VR = 5.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Compare MA with VR.</li>
    <li>MA is smaller than VR.</li>
    <li>This is consistent with a real machine that has energy losses.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>Do not confuse the two ratios: MA uses forces; VR uses distances.</p>', 1),
  (465, 465, '<h2>Efficiency of Machines</h2>
  <p>Machine efficiency is the percentage of input work converted into useful output work.</p>
  <p><strong>Efficiency (%) = (MA ÷ VR) × 100%</strong></p>
  <p>This formula is equivalent to useful output work divided by input work, multiplied by 100%, when the relevant forces and distances are used consistently.</p>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>A machine has MA = 3 and VR = 4.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Efficiency = (MA ÷ VR) × 100%.</li>
    <li>Efficiency = (3 ÷ 4) × 100%.</li>
    <li>Efficiency = 75%.</li>
  </ol>
  <p><strong>Answer: 75%.</strong></p>
  <p>For an ordinary machine, efficiency is below 100% because some input energy is transferred to the surroundings rather than becoming useful output.</p>', 1),
  (466, 466, '<h2>Work Input and Work Output</h2>
  <p>Work input is the work done by the effort on a machine. Work output is the useful work done by the machine on the load.</p>
  <p>When force acts in the same direction as movement:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Work input = effort × distance moved by effort</strong></li>
    <li><strong>Work output = load × distance moved by load</strong></li>
  </ul>
  <p>Use force in newtons (N) and distance in metres (m). Work is measured in joules (J).</p>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>An effort of 50 N moves 3 m while lifting a load of 200 N through 0.5 m.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Input work = 50 × 3 = 150 J.</li>
    <li>Output work = 200 × 0.5 = 100 J.</li>
    <li>The difference is 50 J, transferred into other forms.</li>
  </ol>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> input work = 150 J; useful output work = 100 J.</p>
  <p>In an ideal machine, input work equals output work. In a real machine, input work is usually greater than useful output work.</p>', 1),
  (467, 467, '
  <h2>Pressure in Solids</h2>
  <p>Pressure is the normal force acting per unit area of a surface. For a force distributed uniformly over a flat area, pressure is calculated by dividing the perpendicular force by the area.</p>

  <h3>Formula</h3>
  <p><strong>Pressure = Force ÷ Area</strong></p>
  <p>\(P = \frac{F}{A}\)</p>
  <ul>
    <li>\(P\) = pressure, measured in pascals (Pa)</li>
    <li>\(F\) = force perpendicular to the surface, measured in newtons (N)</li>
    <li>\(A\) = area, measured in square metres (m²)</li>
  </ul>
  <p><strong>1 Pa = 1 N/m²</strong></p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Calculate pressure</h3>
  <p>A force of 100 N acts perpendicularly on an area of 2 m².</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Write P = F/A.</li>
    <li>Substitute P = 100/2.</li>
    <li>Pressure = <strong>50 Pa</strong>.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Smaller area</h3>
  <p>The same 100 N force acts over 0.5 m².</p>
  <p>Pressure = 100/0.5 = <strong>200 Pa</strong>.</p>
  <p>The smaller area produces a greater pressure when the force is unchanged.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Find force</h3>
  <p>If pressure is 400 Pa and area is 0.2 m², then F = PA = 400 × 0.2 = <strong>80 N</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Common mistake</h3>
  <p>Convert the area to m² before calculating pressure in pascals. Also, use the force component perpendicular to the surface.</p>
  
<hr>
<h2>Pressure in Solids</h2>
  <p>A solid object exerts pressure on a surface when it applies a force perpendicular to the surface.</p>
  <p><strong>Pressure = perpendicular force ÷ contact area.</strong></p>
  <p>For an object resting on a horizontal surface, the force may be its weight, provided no other vertical forces change the normal force.</p>
  <h3>Everyday applications</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>A sharp knife has a small cutting area, producing high pressure for a given force.</li>
    <li>Wide tyres spread a vehicle''s weight over a larger area, reducing pressure on soft ground.</li>
    <li>Wide foundations spread a building''s load over a larger area.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Changing the contact area changes pressure even if the force stays the same.</p>', 1),
  (468, 468, '
  <h2>Pressure in Liquids</h2>
  <p>A liquid exerts pressure because of the weight of the liquid above a point. In a liquid at rest, pressure increases with depth and with the liquid''s density.</p>

  <h3>Pressure due to a liquid column</h3>
  <p>For a liquid of uniform density, the gauge pressure at depth h is:</p>
  <p>\(p = \rho gh\)</p>
  <ul>
    <li>\(p\) = gauge pressure in pascals (Pa)</li>
    <li>\(\rho\) = liquid density in kg/m³</li>
    <li>\(g\) = gravitational field strength in N/kg</li>
    <li>\(h\) = depth below the liquid surface in metres</li>
  </ul>
  <p>In school calculations, use the value of g given in the question. If none is given, 10 N/kg is often used for convenient approximations.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 1</h3>
  <p>Find the gauge pressure 2 m below the surface of water. Take density = 1000 kg/m³ and g = 10 N/kg.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Write p = ρgh.</li>
    <li>Substitute p = 1000 × 10 × 2.</li>
    <li>Gauge pressure = <strong>20,000 Pa</strong>.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Greater depth</h3>
  <p>At 4 m depth in the same water, p = 1000 × 10 × 4 = 40,000 Pa. Doubling the depth doubles the gauge pressure.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Different density</h3>
  <p>At the same depth and gravitational field strength, a denser liquid produces a greater gauge pressure because p is proportional to density.</p>

  <h3>Important distinction</h3>
  <p>The formula p = ρgh gives gauge pressure due to the liquid column. Absolute pressure also includes atmospheric pressure at the surface.</p>

  <h3>Common mistake</h3>
  <p>Use depth below the liquid surface, not the container''s total height unless the point is at the bottom and the liquid fills that height.</p>
  
<hr>
<h2>Pressure in Liquids</h2>
  <p>A liquid exerts pressure because of the weight of the liquid above a point. In a stationary liquid of uniform density, pressure increases with depth.</p>
  <p><strong>Liquid pressure:</strong> p = ρgh</p>
  <ul>
    <li>ρ = liquid density in kg/m³</li>
    <li>g = gravitational field strength in N/kg</li>
    <li>h = depth below the liquid surface in metres</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The formula gives the pressure due to the liquid alone. It does not include atmospheric pressure.</p>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>Water has density 1,000 kg/m³. Take g = 10 N/kg. Find the liquid pressure at a depth of 2 m.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>p = ρgh.</li>
    <li>p = 1,000 × 10 × 2.</li>
    <li>p = 20,000 Pa.</li>
  </ol>
  <p><strong>Answer: 20,000 Pa.</strong></p>
<hr>
<h2>Pressure beneath a liquid surface</h2>
  <p>A liquid exerts pressure on the bottom and sides of its container. Liquid pressure acts in all directions and increases with depth.</p>
  <p>At the same depth in the same stationary liquid, pressure is equal at different points.</p>
  <h3>Why does pressure increase with depth?</h3>
  <p>A deeper point has a taller column of liquid above it. The weight of that liquid produces greater pressure.</p>
  <h3>Example</h3>
  <p>Water emerging from a lower hole in a container generally travels farther than water from a higher hole because the pressure is greater at the lower hole.</p>
  <p><b>Important:</b> Liquid pressure depends on depth and liquid density, not simply on the total volume of liquid in the container.</p>', 1),
  (469, 469, '
  <h2>Atmospheric Pressure</h2>
  <p>Atmospheric pressure is the pressure exerted by the air around Earth. Air has mass, and gravity gives the atmosphere weight. Air molecules also collide with surfaces, contributing to pressure.</p>

  <h3>What causes atmospheric pressure?</h3>
  <p>The air above a surface contributes to the pressure at that location. Atmospheric pressure acts in all directions, not only downward.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: A drinking straw</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>When you suck air from the straw, the pressure inside your mouth and the straw becomes lower.</li>
    <li>Atmospheric pressure on the drink''s surface is then greater than the pressure in the straw.</li>
    <li>The pressure difference pushes the liquid up the straw.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Worked example 2: A suction cup</h3>
  <p>Pressing a suction cup against a smooth surface removes some air from the space beneath it. The higher outside atmospheric pressure can then press the cup against the surface. A good seal is important.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Altitude</h3>
  <p>Atmospheric pressure generally decreases as altitude increases because less air lies above a higher location.</p>

  <h3>Measuring atmospheric pressure</h3>
  <p>A barometer is an instrument used to measure atmospheric pressure. A mercury barometer balances atmospheric pressure against the pressure due to a column of mercury.</p>

  <h3>Common mistake</h3>
  <p>Atmospheric pressure is not caused only by wind. It exists even when the air appears still, and it acts in all directions.</p>
  
<hr>
<h2>Atmospheric Pressure</h2>
  <p>Atmospheric pressure is the pressure exerted by the weight and motion of air in the atmosphere. Air pressure acts in all directions.</p>
  <p>Near sea level, standard atmospheric pressure is approximately 101,325 Pa, often rounded to 101,000 Pa or 1.01 × 10⁵ Pa.</p>
  <h3>How atmospheric pressure changes</h3>
  <p>Atmospheric pressure generally decreases with increasing altitude because less air lies above a point at higher altitude.</p>
  <h3>Everyday examples</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>A drinking straw works because sucking lowers the pressure inside the straw, allowing atmospheric pressure on the drink''s surface to push liquid up.</li>
    <li>A suction cup holds when the pressure difference presses it against a surface.</li>
    <li>A barometer is used to measure atmospheric pressure.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Atmospheric pressure is real even though we do not normally feel it as a net force, because pressures around our bodies are largely balanced.</p>
<hr>
<h2>Pressure from the atmosphere</h2>
  <p>The atmosphere is the layer of gases surrounding Earth. Air has mass, and its weight produces <b>atmospheric pressure</b>.</p>
  <p>Air molecules also collide with surfaces, exerting pressure. Atmospheric pressure acts in all directions.</p>
  <p>Near sea level, standard atmospheric pressure is approximately <b>101,325 Pa</b>, often rounded to 101,000 Pa.</p>
  <h3>Evidence for atmospheric pressure</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>A suction cup can stick to a smooth surface when pressure beneath it is reduced.</li>
    <li>A drinking straw works because pressure differences move the liquid.</li>
    <li>Atmospheric pressure generally decreases as altitude increases.</li>
  </ul>
  <p><b>Important:</b> Atmospheric pressure acts on us from all directions; we do not get crushed because internal pressures in our bodies largely balance the external pressure.</p>', 1),
  (470, 470, '
  <h2>Applications of Liquid Pressure</h2>
  <p>Liquid pressure increases with depth. This principle explains why dams are designed with thicker bases, why water can emerge forcefully from a low hole in a tank, and why divers experience greater pressure at greater depths.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Holes in a water tank</h3>
  <p>Imagine a tank with holes at different heights. Water from a lower hole generally emerges faster because the pressure due to the water above that hole is greater.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Dam walls</h3>
  <p>The bottom of a dam experiences greater water pressure than the top. A dam is therefore often made thicker near its base to withstand the greater pressure and resulting forces.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Divers</h3>
  <p>As a diver moves deeper into water, the gauge pressure increases according to p = ρgh. This is why depth matters when planning safe dives.</p>

  <h3>Key relationship</h3>
  <p>For a liquid of uniform density at rest, pressure at a point depends on the liquid''s density, gravitational field strength and depth below the surface. It does not directly depend on the shape of the container.</p>

  <h3>Common mistake</h3>
  <p>Do not confuse pressure with total force. Pressure is force per unit area. The total force on a surface depends on pressure and the area over which it acts.</p>
  ', 1),
  (471, 471, '
  <h2>Pressure Acts in All Directions</h2>
  <p>A liquid at rest exerts pressure in all directions. At a given point in a stationary liquid, the pressure has the same magnitude in every direction.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: A hole in the side of a container</h3>
  <p>Water flows sideways from a hole in a tank because the water exerts pressure on the side wall. Liquid pressure is not limited to downward forces.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: A submerged object</h3>
  <p>Water presses on the top, bottom and sides of an immersed object. The pressure is greater at the bottom than at the top when those surfaces are at different depths.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Pressure differences</h3>
  <p>Although pressure acts in all directions at a point, pressure can differ between two points at different depths. The difference can create a resultant force.</p>

  <h3>Important distinction</h3>
  <p>Pressure is a scalar quantity: it has magnitude but no single direction of its own. The force exerted by pressure on a surface acts perpendicular to that surface.</p>

  <h3>Common mistake</h3>
  <p>Do not say that liquid pressure acts only downward. The weight of the liquid contributes to the pressure, but the liquid also exerts pressure sideways and upward.</p>
  ', 1),
  (472, 472, '
  <h2>Pressure in Gases</h2>
  <p>A gas consists of particles moving randomly. Gas pressure is caused by these particles colliding with the walls of their container and exerting forces on them.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Air in a tyre</h3>
  <p>Air particles collide with the inside walls of a tyre. These collisions produce pressure on the tyre''s inner surface.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Compressing a gas</h3>
  <p>If a gas is compressed into a smaller volume while its temperature remains constant and the amount of gas stays fixed, its pressure increases. The particles have less space and collide with the walls more frequently.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Heating a gas in a sealed container</h3>
  <p>When a gas in a rigid sealed container is heated, its particles have greater average kinetic energy. Their collisions with the walls become more forceful, so the pressure increases if the volume remains constant.</p>

  <h3>Key conditions</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Compression effects depend on temperature and the amount of gas.</li>
    <li>Heating increases pressure when the gas is in a fixed-volume container.</li>
    <li>If a container can expand, its volume may change instead of pressure rising as much.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Common mistake</h3>
  <p>Gas particles do not need to be pushed continuously in one direction to produce pressure. Their random motion and collisions create pressure in all directions.</p>
  
<hr>
<h2>Pressure in Gases</h2>
  <p>A gas consists of particles moving randomly. Gas pressure arises from collisions of these particles with the walls of their container.</p>
  <p>When particles collide with a wall, they exert force on it. The combined effect of many collisions produces measurable pressure.</p>
  <h3>Changing gas pressure</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Increasing temperature in a sealed rigid container increases particle speeds and usually increases pressure.</li>
    <li>Reducing the volume of a fixed amount of gas at constant temperature increases pressure.</li>
    <li>Adding more gas particles to a fixed volume at constant temperature generally increases pressure.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>These comparisons assume the other stated conditions remain constant. Gas pressure acts in all directions.</p>
<hr>
<h2>How gases exert pressure</h2>
  <p>Gas particles move randomly and collide with the walls of their container. These collisions exert forces on the walls. The force per unit area is the gas pressure.</p>
  <p>For a gas confined in a container, pressure can change when the gas is compressed, heated or cooled, depending on which other conditions are kept constant.</p>
  <h3>Example: compressing air</h3>
  <p>If air in a sealed syringe is compressed by pushing the plunger inward, its volume decreases and its pressure generally increases, provided the gas temperature is approximately constant.</p>
  <p><b>Do not confuse:</b> pressure in a gas is caused by particle collisions with surfaces. Atmospheric pressure is the pressure exerted by the air around us, including the effect of the weight of the atmosphere.</p>', 1),
  (473, 473, '
  <h2>Pressure and Volume of a Gas</h2>
  <p>For a fixed amount of gas at constant temperature, pressure is inversely proportional to volume. This relationship is known as Boyle''s law.</p>

  <h3>Formula</h3>
  <p>\(P_1V_1 = P_2V_2\)</p>
  <p>Here, P₁ and V₁ are the initial pressure and volume, while P₂ and V₂ are the final pressure and volume.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 1</h3>
  <p>A gas occupies 600 cm³ at a pressure of 100 kPa. It is compressed to 300 cm³ at constant temperature. Find the final pressure.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Write P₁V₁ = P₂V₂.</li>
    <li>Substitute: 100 × 600 = P₂ × 300.</li>
    <li>P₂ = (100 × 600) ÷ 300 = <strong>200 kPa</strong>.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Worked example 2</h3>
  <p>A gas at 150 kPa occupies 400 cm³. Its pressure changes to 100 kPa at constant temperature.</p>
  <p>V₂ = P₁V₁ ÷ P₂ = (150 × 400) ÷ 100 = <strong>600 cm³</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Interpretation</h3>
  <p>Halving the volume doubles the pressure, provided temperature and amount of gas remain constant.</p>

  <h3>Common mistake</h3>
  <p>Do not use Boyle''s law without checking the conditions. Pressure units must be consistent, and volume units must be consistent across the two states.</p>
  ', 1),
  (474, 474, '<h2>Meaning of Pressure</h2>
  <p>Pressure is the normal force acting per unit area of a surface.</p>
  <p><strong>Formula:</strong> P = F/A</p>
  <ul>
    <li>P = pressure in pascals (Pa)</li>
    <li>F = perpendicular force in newtons (N)</li>
    <li>A = area in square metres (m²)</li>
  </ul>
  <p>One pascal is one newton per square metre: 1 Pa = 1 N/m².</p>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>A force of 100 N acts normally on an area of 5 m².</p>
  <ol>
    <li>P = F/A.</li>
    <li>P = 100/5.</li>
    <li>P = 20 Pa.</li>
  </ol>
  <p><strong>Answer: 20 Pa.</strong></p>
  <p>For the same force, a smaller contact area produces greater pressure.</p>', 1),
  (475, 475, '<h2>Factors Affecting Liquid Pressure</h2>
  <p>For a stationary liquid, the pressure due to the liquid is given by p = ρgh.</p>
  <p>Therefore, liquid pressure depends on:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Depth (h):</strong> greater depth gives greater pressure.</li>
    <li><strong>Density (ρ):</strong> a denser liquid gives greater pressure at the same depth.</li>
    <li><strong>Gravitational field strength (g):</strong> greater g gives greater pressure for the same liquid and depth.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>At the same depth in the same stationary liquid, pressure is the same regardless of the container''s shape. The formula gives gauge pressure due to the liquid, excluding atmospheric pressure.</p>', 1),
  (476, 476, '<h2>Pressure Differences</h2>
  <p>A pressure difference is the difference between pressure at two locations. A fluid tends to move from a region of higher pressure towards a region of lower pressure when a path is available.</p>
  <p><strong>Pressure difference:</strong> Δp = p₁ − p₂, where p₁ is the higher pressure and p₂ is the lower pressure.</p>
  <p>A pressure difference can produce a net force on an object or drive fluid flow.</p>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>Pressure on one side of a surface is 120,000 Pa and pressure on the other side is 100,000 Pa.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Δp = 120,000 − 100,000.</li>
    <li>Δp = 20,000 Pa.</li>
  </ol>
  <p><strong>Answer: 20,000 Pa.</strong></p>
  <p>When pressure is equal on both sides, the pressures do not produce a net force across the surface.</p>', 1),
  (477, 477, '<h2>Upthrust</h2>
  <p>Upthrust is the upward force a fluid exerts on an object partly or fully immersed in it.</p>
  <p>In a liquid, pressure increases with depth. The bottom of an immersed object is generally at a greater depth than its top, so the upward pressure force on the bottom can exceed the downward pressure force on the top. The result is an upward force.</p>
  <h3>Archimedes'' principle</h3>
  <p>The upthrust on an immersed object equals the weight of the fluid displaced by the object.</p>
  <p><strong>Upthrust = weight of displaced fluid</strong></p>
  <p>For a uniform gravitational field, the weight of displaced fluid is its mass multiplied by g.</p>
  <p>Upthrust acts upward, opposing the object''s weight, but it does not always equal the object''s weight.</p>', 1),
  (478, 478, '<h2>Floating and Sinking</h2>
  <p>Whether an object rises, sinks or floats depends on its weight and the upthrust acting on it.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Upthrust greater than weight:</strong> the object accelerates upward.</li>
    <li><strong>Weight greater than upthrust:</strong> the object accelerates downward.</li>
    <li><strong>Upthrust equal to weight:</strong> the vertical forces balance; the object can remain at rest or move at constant vertical velocity.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>A floating object at rest has upthrust equal to its weight. It displaces enough fluid for the weight of that displaced fluid to equal the object''s weight.</p>
  <p>Average density helps explain flotation: an object whose average density is less than the fluid''s density can float, depending on its shape and whether it can take in the fluid.</p>', 1),
  (479, 479, '<h2>Applications of Upthrust</h2>
  <p>Upthrust explains why ships can float, why balloons filled with a suitable gas rise in air, and how hydrometers can be used to compare liquid densities.</p>
  <h3>Ships</h3>
  <p>A ship''s hollow hull makes its overall average density lower than that of the water it displaces. It sinks until the upthrust balances its weight.</p>
  <h3>Hydrometers</h3>
  <p>A hydrometer floats at a depth determined by the balance between its weight and the upthrust. In a denser liquid, it generally needs to displace less liquid volume, so it floats higher.</p>
  <h3>Balloons</h3>
  <p>A balloon rises when the upthrust from the surrounding air exceeds the total downward weight of the balloon and its contents.</p>
  <p>In every case, the key principle is a balance between weight and upthrust, along with the density of the surrounding fluid.</p>', 1),
  (480, 480, '<h2>What is pressure?</h2>
  <p><b>Pressure</b> is the normal force acting per unit area of a surface.</p>
  <p>Formula: <b>P = F/A</b></p>
  <ul>
    <li>P = pressure in pascals (Pa)</li>
    <li>F = perpendicular force in newtons (N)</li>
    <li>A = area in square metres (m²)</li>
  </ul>
  <p>One pascal is one newton per square metre: 1 Pa = 1 N/m².</p>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>A force of 60 N acts normally on an area of 3 m².</p>
  <p>P = F/A = 60/3 = <b>20 Pa</b>.</p>
  <p><b>Key idea:</b> Pressure depends on both the force and the area over which it acts.</p>', 1),
  (481, 481, '<h2>How area affects pressure</h2>
  <p>For a solid surface, pressure is calculated using:</p>
  <p><b>P = F/A</b></p>
  <p>For the same force, a smaller contact area produces greater pressure. A larger contact area produces lower pressure.</p>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>A block exerts a force of 120 N over an area of 0.04 m².</p>
  <p>P = 120/0.04 = <b>3,000 Pa</b>.</p>
  <p><b>Watch the units:</b> area must be in m². Convert cm² to m² before substituting.</p>
  <p>Example: 20 cm² = 20 ÷ 10,000 = 0.002 m².</p>', 1),
  (482, 482, '<h2>Three factors</h2>
  <p>For a liquid at rest, the pressure due to the liquid depends on:</p>
  <ol>
    <li><b>Depth (h):</b> greater depth gives greater pressure.</li>
    <li><b>Density (ρ):</b> a denser liquid gives greater pressure at the same depth.</li>
    <li><b>Gravitational field strength (g):</b> stronger gravity gives greater pressure.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>The relationship is <b>P = ρgh</b>.</p>
  <h3>Compare two situations</h3>
  <p>If the liquid and gravity remain the same, doubling the depth doubles the liquid pressure.</p>
  <p>If the depth and gravity remain the same, doubling the liquid density doubles the liquid pressure.</p>
  <p><b>Remember:</b> this equation gives pressure due to the liquid, not the total pressure including atmospheric pressure.</p>', 1),
  (483, 483, '<h2>Using P = ρgh</h2>
  <p>Use this equation to calculate pressure due to a liquid:</p>
  <p><b>P = ρgh</b></p>
  <ul>
    <li>P = pressure in pascals (Pa)</li>
    <li>ρ = density in kg/m³</li>
    <li>g = gravitational field strength in N/kg</li>
    <li>h = depth in metres (m)</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>Calculate the pressure 2 m below the surface of water. Take ρ = 1,000 kg/m³ and g = 10 N/kg.</p>
  <p>P = 1,000 × 10 × 2</p>
  <p><b>P = 20,000 Pa</b></p>
  <p><b>Common error:</b> using centimetres instead of metres, or using density in g/cm³ without converting it to kg/m³.</p>', 1),
  (484, 484, '<h2>Pressure transmission</h2>
  <p>When pressure is applied to an enclosed fluid, the pressure change is transmitted throughout the fluid and to the walls of its container. This is the principle associated with <b>Pascal''s law</b>.</p>
  <p>Liquids are commonly used in hydraulic systems because they are difficult to compress.</p>
  <h3>Example: an enclosed liquid</h3>
  <p>Pressing a piston into a sealed liquid increases the pressure. That pressure change is transmitted through the liquid to another piston.</p>
  <p><b>Do not confuse:</b> transmission of an applied pressure change with the natural increase in liquid pressure at greater depth. They are related to pressure but describe different effects.</p>', 1),
  (485, 485, '<h2>How hydraulic machines work</h2>
  <p>Hydraulic machines use pressure transmission in an enclosed liquid. Examples include hydraulic brakes and hydraulic lifts.</p>
  <p>For ideal pistons at the same transmitted pressure:</p>
  <p><b>P = F₁/A₁ = F₂/A₂</b></p>
  <p>Therefore, <b>F₂ = F₁ × A₂/A₁</b>.</p>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>A small piston has an area of 0.01 m² and receives a force of 50 N. The larger piston has an area of 0.1 m².</p>
  <p>P = 50 ÷ 0.01 = 5,000 Pa.</p>
  <p>F₂ = 5,000 × 0.1 = <b>500 N</b>.</p>
  <p>The larger piston can produce a larger force, but in an ideal system it moves a shorter distance. Energy is not created.</p>', 1),
  (486, 486, '<h2>The barometer</h2>
  <p>A <b>barometer</b> measures atmospheric pressure. A mercury barometer uses a column of mercury supported by atmospheric pressure acting on mercury in a reservoir.</p>
  <p>At standard atmospheric pressure, the mercury column is approximately 760 mm high.</p>
  <p>The pressure represented by a liquid column can be calculated using:</p>
  <p><b>P = ρgh</b></p>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>For mercury, take ρ = 13,600 kg/m³, g = 10 N/kg and h = 0.76 m.</p>
  <p>P = 13,600 × 10 × 0.76</p>
  <p>P = <b>103,360 Pa</b>.</p>
  <p>This is close to standard atmospheric pressure; using g = 9.81 N/kg gives a value closer to 101,000 Pa.</p>
  <p><b>Remember:</b> a barometer measures atmospheric pressure. A pressure gauge commonly measures pressure relative to the surrounding atmosphere.</p>', 1),
  (487, 487, '<h2>Pressure gauges</h2>
  <p>A pressure gauge measures the pressure of a gas or liquid in a system. Many common gauges indicate pressure relative to atmospheric pressure.</p>
  <p>A Bourdon gauge uses a curved metal tube that changes shape slightly when the pressure inside it changes. A mechanism moves a pointer over a scale.</p>
  <h2>Siphons</h2>
  <p>A siphon is a tube that transfers liquid from a higher reservoir to a lower level, provided the tube is primed and the arrangement is suitable.</p>
  <p>Gravity and pressure differences maintain the flow. Atmospheric pressure can be important in ordinary siphon operation, but the explanation is not simply that air ''sucks'' the liquid through.</p>
  <p><b>Keep the devices distinct:</b> a gauge measures pressure; a siphon transfers liquid between levels.</p>', 1),
  (488, 488, '<h2>Measuring pressure difference</h2>
  <p>A U-tube manometer contains a liquid in a U-shaped tube. One side may be connected to a gas container while the other is open to the atmosphere.</p>
  <p>A difference in pressure causes the liquid levels in the two arms to differ.</p>
  <p>For a simple manometer containing the same liquid in both arms, the pressure difference is:</p>
  <p><b>ΔP = ρgh</b></p>
  <ul>
    <li>ρ = density of the manometer liquid in kg/m³</li>
    <li>g = gravitational field strength in N/kg</li>
    <li>h = vertical difference between the liquid levels in metres</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>Mercury has density 13,600 kg/m³. The level difference is 0.10 m. Take g = 10 N/kg.</p>
  <p>ΔP = 13,600 × 10 × 0.10 = <b>13,600 Pa</b>.</p>
  <p><b>Important:</b> use the vertical height difference, not the length of the tube.</p>', 1),
  (489, 489, '<h2>Which pressure is greater?</h2>
  <p>In a simple U-tube manometer containing the same liquid in both arms, the side with the lower liquid level is generally the side with the greater applied pressure.</p>
  <p>The greater pressure pushes the manometer liquid down on its side and raises the liquid on the other side.</p>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>A gas container is connected to one arm of a mercury manometer. The mercury is lower on the gas side by 0.05 m.</p>
  <p>This indicates that the gas pressure exceeds atmospheric pressure by:</p>
  <p>ΔP = ρgh = 13,600 × 10 × 0.05 = <b>6,800 Pa</b>.</p>
  <p>If atmospheric pressure is 100,000 Pa, then the gas''s absolute pressure is 100,000 + 6,800 = <b>106,800 Pa</b>.</p>
  <p><b>Distinguish:</b> gauge pressure is measured relative to atmospheric pressure; absolute pressure is measured relative to a vacuum.</p>', 1),
  (490, 490, '<h2>How hydraulic brakes work</h2>
  <p>Hydraulic brakes use pressure transmission through an enclosed liquid to apply braking force at the wheels.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>The driver presses the brake pedal.</li>
    <li>The pedal operates a master-cylinder piston.</li>
    <li>The piston increases pressure in the brake fluid.</li>
    <li>The pressure is transmitted through the fluid to the wheel mechanisms.</li>
    <li>The brake pads or shoes press against rotating parts, producing friction that slows the vehicle.</li>
  </ol>
  <h3>Properties of brake fluid</h3>
  <p>Brake fluid should transmit pressure effectively, have a suitable boiling point and be compatible with the system''s seals and materials.</p>
  <p>Air in the brake lines is undesirable because it is compressible and can make the pedal feel spongy.</p>
  <p><b>Key distinction:</b> the fluid transmits pressure; friction at the braking surfaces slows the vehicle.</p>', 1),
  (491, 491, '<h2>How a bicycle pump works</h2>
  <p>A simple hand pump compresses air and forces it into a tyre through a valve.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>When the piston is pulled back, the pump chamber expands and its pressure falls.</li>
    <li>Air enters the chamber through an inlet valve.</li>
    <li>When the piston is pushed down, the air is compressed.</li>
    <li>When the chamber pressure exceeds the tyre pressure sufficiently, the outlet valve allows air into the tyre.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>The valves control the direction of air flow.</p>
  <p><b>Important:</b> the pump raises the air pressure by compressing air and forcing more air into the tyre. The pump does not work by making the air disappear.</p>', 1),
  (492, 492, '<h2>Using pressure differences</h2>
  <p>A syringe can draw liquid in or expel liquid by changing the pressure inside its barrel.</p>
  <h3>Drawing liquid in</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Place the nozzle in the liquid.</li>
    <li>Pull the plunger back.</li>
    <li>The volume inside the syringe increases and its pressure falls.</li>
    <li>The pressure on the liquid outside is greater, so liquid enters through the nozzle.</li>
  </ol>
  <h3>Expelling liquid</h3>
  <p>Pushing the plunger decreases the volume and increases the pressure inside the syringe. If the nozzle is open, the liquid can be forced out.</p>
  <p><b>Key idea:</b> the pressure difference causes the flow. The plunger changes the pressure inside the syringe.</p>', 1),
  (493, 493, '<h2>Why liquid rises in a straw</h2>
  <p>A drinking straw works through a pressure difference.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Before drinking, the pressure on the liquid surface in the cup is approximately atmospheric pressure.</li>
    <li>When a person sucks, the pressure inside the mouth and straw is reduced.</li>
    <li>The pressure acting on the liquid surface in the cup is now greater than the pressure inside the straw.</li>
    <li>This pressure difference pushes liquid up the straw.</li>
  </ol>
  <p><b>Important:</b> sucking lowers the pressure inside the straw; atmospheric pressure helps push the liquid upwards.</p>
  <p>If the straw has a hole that allows air to enter, the pressure difference may not be maintained, making it difficult to draw up the liquid.</p>', 1),
  (494, 494, '<h2>How a force pump works</h2>
  <p>A force pump uses a piston and valves to move liquid, often water, against a pressure difference or to a higher level.</p>
  <p>In a common reciprocating force pump:</p>
  <ol>
    <li>During the intake stroke, the piston moves to draw liquid into the cylinder through an inlet valve.</li>
    <li>During the delivery stroke, the piston pushes the liquid and increases its pressure.</li>
    <li>The inlet valve closes to prevent backward flow.</li>
    <li>The outlet valve opens when the liquid pressure is sufficient to push liquid into the delivery pipe.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>The valves help maintain one-way flow.</p>
  <p><b>Remember:</b> the exact arrangement varies by pump design, but the basic principle is that a moving piston changes pressure and valves control flow direction.</p>', 1),
  (495, 495, '<h2>How a lift pump raises water</h2>
  <p>A conventional lift pump raises water from a well by creating a region of lower pressure above the water. Atmospheric pressure acting on the water surface then pushes water up the pump pipe.</p>
  <p>In a typical hand-operated lift pump:</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Moving the handle raises the piston and changes the pressure in the cylinder.</li>
    <li>The valves open or close to control the flow of water.</li>
    <li>Atmospheric pressure helps push water up the pipe into the pump.</li>
    <li>Repeated strokes move water upward and out through the spout.</li>
  </ol>
  <p><b>Important limit:</b> a simple suction lift pump cannot raise water from an arbitrarily great depth. Atmospheric pressure limits the theoretical lift, and real pumps have a lower practical limit.</p>
  <p>Do not confuse a lift pump with a force pump: the typical lift pump relies on atmospheric pressure to push water into a low-pressure region, while a force pump can deliver liquid under increased pressure.</p>', 1),
  (496, 496, '<h2>Evidence that atmospheric pressure exists</h2>
  <p>Atmospheric pressure is not visible directly, but its effects can be demonstrated.</p>
  <h3>Experiment: inverted water-filled container</h3>
  <p>A simple demonstration uses a rigid cup or container, water, and a flat sheet of stiff card.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Fill the container with water.</li>
    <li>Place the card over its opening and hold it firmly.</li>
    <li>Carefully invert the container.</li>
    <li>Release the card while keeping the setup steady.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>Under suitable conditions, the card may remain in place because the pressure difference helps support it. Surface tension and the seal at the rim also matter.</p>
  <p>Other demonstrations include suction cups and the collapse of a container when its internal pressure is reduced.</p>
  <p><b>Safety:</b> perform demonstrations over a basin and use a lightweight container. Do not use hot or fragile vessels.</p>', 1),
  (497, 497, '<h2>Temperature</h2>
  <p>Temperature is a measure of how hot or cold an object is. At the particle level, the Kelvin temperature of a substance is related to the average kinetic energy of its particles in random motion.</p>
  <p>Temperature is measured in degrees Celsius (°C) or kelvin (K).</p>
  <p><strong>Temperature conversion:</strong> T(K) = t(°C) + 273.15</p>
  <p>For many school calculations, 273 is used as an approximation.</p>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>Convert 27°C to kelvin.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>T = 27 + 273.15.</li>
    <li>T = 300.15 K.</li>
  </ol>
  <p><strong>Answer: 300.15 K, approximately 300 K.</strong></p>
  <p>Temperature is different from heat: temperature describes thermal state, while heat is energy transferred because of a temperature difference.</p>', 1),
  (498, 498, '<h2>Thermal Expansion</h2>
  <p>Thermal expansion is the increase in a material''s dimensions when its temperature rises, usually because its particles vibrate more strongly or move farther apart on average.</p>
  <p>Solids can expand in length, area and volume. Liquids and gases also expand when heated under suitable conditions.</p>
  <h3>Examples</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Bridges use expansion joints to accommodate temperature changes.</li>
    <li>Gaps are left between some railway rails or track sections to manage expansion.</li>
    <li>Overhead wires may sag more in hot weather because their length increases.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>When a material cools, it generally contracts. Different materials expand by different amounts for the same temperature rise.</p>', 1),
  (499, 499, '<h2>Linear Expansion of Solids</h2>
  <p>Linear expansion is the change in length of a solid when its temperature changes.</p>
  <p>For a solid that expands uniformly over a suitable temperature range:</p>
  <p><strong>ΔL = αL₀ΔT</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li>ΔL = change in length, in metres (m)</li>
    <li>α = coefficient of linear expansion, in K⁻¹ or °C⁻¹</li>
    <li>L₀ = original length, in metres (m)</li>
    <li>ΔT = temperature change, in K or °C</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>A 2 m rod has α = 1.2 × 10⁻⁵ K⁻¹ and is heated by 50 K.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>ΔL = αL₀ΔT.</li>
    <li>ΔL = (1.2 × 10⁻⁵) × 2 × 50.</li>
    <li>ΔL = 0.0012 m = 1.2 mm.</li>
  </ol>
  <p><strong>Answer: 1.2 mm.</strong></p>', 1),
  (500, 500, '<h2>Heat Capacity</h2>
  <p>Heat capacity is the energy required to raise the temperature of an entire object by one kelvin.</p>
  <p><strong>C = Q/ΔT</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li>C = heat capacity, in J/K</li>
    <li>Q = energy transferred as heat, in joules (J)</li>
    <li>ΔT = temperature change, in K</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Heat capacity depends on the object''s material and its mass. A large object made from a given material generally has a greater heat capacity than a smaller object made from the same material.</p>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>An object absorbs 6,000 J and its temperature rises by 20 K.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>C = Q/ΔT.</li>
    <li>C = 6,000/20.</li>
    <li>C = 300 J/K.</li>
  </ol>
  <p><strong>Answer: 300 J/K.</strong></p>', 1);
INSERT INTO public.lesson_versions (id, lesson_id, content, version) VALUES
  (501, 501, '<h2>Specific Heat Capacity</h2>
  <p>Specific heat capacity is the energy required to raise the temperature of 1 kg of a substance by 1 K, without a change of state.</p>
  <p><strong>Q = mcΔT</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li>Q = energy transferred, in J</li>
    <li>m = mass, in kg</li>
    <li>c = specific heat capacity, in J/(kg·K)</li>
    <li>ΔT = temperature change, in K or °C</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>Calculate the energy needed to heat 2 kg of water by 10 K, using c = 4,200 J/(kg·K).</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Q = mcΔT.</li>
    <li>Q = 2 × 4,200 × 10.</li>
    <li>Q = 84,000 J.</li>
  </ol>
  <p><strong>Answer: 84,000 J.</strong></p>
  <p>Specific heat capacity is a property of the substance. It is not the same as the heat capacity of an entire object.</p>', 1),
  (502, 502, '<h2>Heat Transfer by Conduction</h2>
  <p>Conduction is the transfer of thermal energy through a material without the material as a whole moving from place to place.</p>
  <p>In solids, vibrating particles transfer energy to neighbouring particles. In metals, mobile electrons also transfer energy efficiently.</p>
  <h3>Examples</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>A metal spoon becomes warm when one end is placed in hot water.</li>
    <li>A metal cooking pot transfers energy from its hot base to its contents.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Metals are generally good thermal conductors. Materials such as wood, plastic and trapped air are comparatively poor conductors and are often used as insulators.</p>', 1),
  (503, 503, '<h2>Heat Transfer by Convection</h2>
  <p>Convection is the transfer of thermal energy by the bulk movement of a fluid, such as a liquid or gas.</p>
  <p>When a region of fluid is heated, it often expands and becomes less dense than the surrounding cooler fluid. The warmer fluid rises while cooler, denser fluid moves in to replace it. This circulation can form a convection current.</p>
  <h3>Example: heating water</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Water near the heat source warms.</li>
    <li>It expands and becomes less dense than nearby cooler water.</li>
    <li>The warmer water rises and cooler water moves down towards the heat source.</li>
    <li>The circulating water transfers thermal energy around the container.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>Convection occurs in fluids, not through bulk circulation of a solid.</p>', 1),
  (504, 504, '<h2>Heat Transfer by Radiation</h2>
  <p>Thermal radiation is the transfer of energy by electromagnetic waves, mainly infrared radiation at everyday temperatures.</p>
  <p>Radiation does not require a material medium, so energy from the Sun can travel through the vacuum of space to Earth.</p>
  <h3>Surface properties</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Dull, dark surfaces are generally good absorbers and emitters of thermal radiation.</li>
    <li>Shiny, light-coloured surfaces are generally poorer absorbers and emitters, and good reflectors.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>All objects above absolute zero emit thermal radiation. The net energy transfer by radiation depends on the object''s temperature relative to its surroundings.</p>', 1),
  (505, 505, '<h2>Comparing Conduction, Convection and Radiation</h2>
  <p>Thermal energy can be transferred by conduction, convection and radiation. Different situations may involve more than one method at the same time.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Conduction:</strong> energy transfer through interactions within matter; mobile electrons also help in metals.</li>
    <li><strong>Convection:</strong> energy transfer by bulk movement of a liquid or gas.</li>
    <li><strong>Radiation:</strong> energy transfer by electromagnetic waves; a material medium is not required.</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Worked examples</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>A metal spoon warming from its immersed end: conduction.</li>
    <li>Water circulating as it heats in a pot: convection.</li>
    <li>Energy travelling from the Sun to Earth: radiation.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>In a hot cooking pot, conduction through the metal and convection in the liquid can occur together, while radiation may also transfer energy from hot surfaces.</p>', 1),
  (506, 506, '<h2>Change of State</h2>
  <p>A change of state occurs when a substance changes between solid, liquid and gas.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Melting:</strong> solid to liquid.</li>
    <li><strong>Freezing:</strong> liquid to solid.</li>
    <li><strong>Vaporisation:</strong> liquid to gas, including boiling and evaporation.</li>
    <li><strong>Condensation:</strong> gas to liquid.</li>
    <li><strong>Sublimation:</strong> solid directly to gas.</li>
    <li><strong>Deposition:</strong> gas directly to solid.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>For a pure substance undergoing a change of state at a fixed pressure, the temperature remains constant during the phase change. Energy supplied or removed changes the arrangement and separation of particles rather than increasing or decreasing the temperature during that stage.</p>
  <p>Evaporation can occur at temperatures below the boiling point; boiling occurs throughout the liquid at its boiling point for the given pressure.</p>', 1),
  (507, 507, '<h2>Latent Heat</h2>
  <p>Latent heat is the energy transferred to or from a substance during a change of state without a change in temperature, provided the change occurs at constant pressure for a pure substance.</p>
  <p>During melting, energy supplied helps particles overcome some of the attractive forces holding them in fixed positions. During boiling, energy supplied helps particles separate sufficiently to form a gas.</p>
  <p>During freezing and condensation, energy is released to the surroundings as particles form a more condensed state.</p>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>Ice at its melting point receives energy and melts while remaining at the same temperature. The energy is used for the change of state rather than raising the temperature during melting.</p>
  <p><strong>Key distinction:</strong> sensible heating changes temperature; latent heating changes state without changing temperature during the phase change.</p>', 1),
  (508, 508, '<h2>Specific Latent Heat</h2>
  <p>Specific latent heat is the energy required to change the state of 1 kg of a substance without changing its temperature.</p>
  <p><strong>Q = mL</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li>Q = energy transferred, in joules (J)</li>
    <li>m = mass, in kilograms (kg)</li>
    <li>L = specific latent heat, in J/kg</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Specific latent heat depends on the substance and the type of change of state. The specific latent heat of fusion concerns melting or freezing; the specific latent heat of vaporisation concerns boiling or condensation.</p>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>A 0.4 kg sample has a specific latent heat of 200,000 J/kg for a particular phase change.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Q = mL.</li>
    <li>Q = 0.4 × 200,000.</li>
    <li>Q = 80,000 J.</li>
  </ol>
  <p><strong>Answer: 80,000 J.</strong></p>', 1),
  (509, 509, '<h2>Specific Latent Heat of Fusion</h2>
  <p>The specific latent heat of fusion is the energy required to change 1 kg of a solid into a liquid at its melting point without a temperature change.</p>
  <p>The same magnitude of energy per kilogram is released when that substance freezes at the same pressure.</p>
  <p><strong>Q = mLᶠ</strong></p>
  <p>Here, Lᶠ represents the specific latent heat of fusion.</p>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>Use Lᶠ = 334,000 J/kg for ice near its normal melting point. Calculate the energy needed to melt 0.2 kg of ice already at 0°C.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Q = mLᶠ.</li>
    <li>Q = 0.2 × 334,000.</li>
    <li>Q = 66,800 J.</li>
  </ol>
  <p><strong>Answer: 66,800 J.</strong></p>
  <p>This calculation assumes the ice is already at its melting point. If the ice starts colder, energy is also needed to warm it to that point.</p>', 1),
  (510, 510, '<h2>Specific Latent Heat of Vaporisation</h2>
  <p>The specific latent heat of vaporisation is the energy required to change 1 kg of a liquid into gas at its boiling point without changing its temperature.</p>
  <p>For the reverse process, condensation, the same magnitude of energy per kilogram is released at the corresponding saturation temperature and pressure.</p>
  <p><strong>Q = mLᵥ</strong></p>
  <p>Here, Lᵥ is the specific latent heat of vaporisation.</p>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>Use Lᵥ = 2.26 × 10⁶ J/kg for water at its normal boiling point. Find the energy needed to vaporise 0.1 kg of water already at 100°C.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Q = mLᵥ.</li>
    <li>Q = 0.1 × 2.26 × 10⁶.</li>
    <li>Q = 226,000 J.</li>
  </ol>
  <p><strong>Answer: 226,000 J.</strong></p>
  <p>The calculation assumes the water is already at its boiling point and does not include heating it to 100°C.</p>', 1),
  (511, 511, '<h2>Heating Curves</h2>
  <p>A heating curve shows how a substance''s temperature changes as energy is supplied over time or as cumulative energy input increases.</p>
  <p>For a pure substance heated at constant pressure, a typical heating curve has:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Sloping sections:</strong> the substance remains in one state and its temperature rises.</li>
    <li><strong>Flat sections:</strong> the substance changes state at constant temperature; supplied energy provides latent heat.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>For ice initially below 0°C, a typical curve shows ice warming, melting at about 0°C, liquid water warming, boiling at about 100°C at normal atmospheric pressure, and steam warming if heating continues.</p>
  <h3>Worked interpretation</h3>
  <p>If a graph is flat while energy continues to enter a pure substance, the substance may be undergoing a phase change. The plateau''s length depends on the energy needed for that change and the rate of energy supply.</p>', 1),
  (512, 512, '<h2>Cooling Curves</h2>
  <p>A cooling curve shows how the temperature of a substance changes as it loses energy to its surroundings.</p>
  <p>For a pure substance at constant pressure, a typical curve has:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Sloping sections:</strong> the substance cools within a single state.</li>
    <li><strong>Flat sections:</strong> a phase change occurs at constant temperature, such as freezing or condensation.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>During freezing, a pure substance releases latent heat while the liquid changes into a solid. During condensation, gas releases latent heat as it changes into liquid.</p>
  <h3>Worked interpretation</h3>
  <p>If liquid water cools to its freezing point, its temperature falls. While it freezes at constant pressure, the temperature stays approximately constant until the phase change is complete. After freezing, the solid can cool further.</p>', 1),
  (513, 513, '<h2>Evaporation</h2>
  <p>Evaporation is the change from liquid to gas that occurs at the surface of a liquid. It can occur at temperatures below the boiling point.</p>
  <p>Some particles at the liquid surface have enough energy to escape into the surrounding gas. The particles that escape carry energy away, so the remaining liquid may cool if energy is not replaced from its surroundings.</p>
  <h3>Examples</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Wet clothes dry as water evaporates.</li>
    <li>Sweat evaporates from skin and can cool the body.</li>
    <li>Water in an open container gradually disappears over time.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Evaporation differs from boiling: evaporation occurs at the surface and can happen below the boiling point, while boiling occurs throughout the liquid at its boiling point for the given pressure.</p>', 1),
  (514, 514, '<h2>Factors Affecting Evaporation</h2>
  <p>The rate of evaporation depends on conditions at the liquid surface and in the surrounding air.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Temperature:</strong> a higher liquid temperature generally increases evaporation rate because more particles can escape.</li>
    <li><strong>Surface area:</strong> a larger exposed surface generally allows more particles to escape at once.</li>
    <li><strong>Air movement:</strong> moving air carries away vapour near the surface, often increasing evaporation.</li>
    <li><strong>Humidity:</strong> lower relative humidity generally increases evaporation because the air is less saturated with water vapour.</li>
    <li><strong>Nature of the liquid:</strong> different liquids evaporate at different rates because their particles experience different intermolecular attractions and have different volatilities.</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Worked comparison</h3>
  <p>Water spread over a wide tray usually evaporates faster than the same amount of water in a narrow container under the same temperature, airflow and humidity, because more surface is exposed.</p>', 1),
  (515, 515, '<h2>Boiling Point and Pressure</h2>
  <p>A liquid boils when bubbles of vapour can form and grow throughout the liquid. This occurs when the liquid''s vapour pressure reaches the external pressure acting on it.</p>
  <p>For water, the normal boiling point is approximately 100°C at standard atmospheric pressure.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Lower external pressure:</strong> the boiling point generally decreases.</li>
    <li><strong>Higher external pressure:</strong> the boiling point generally increases.</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Applications</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>At high altitude, atmospheric pressure is lower, so water boils below 100°C.</li>
    <li>A pressure cooker raises the pressure inside, allowing water and steam to reach a temperature above 100°C.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Boiling point depends on pressure, so 100°C is not the boiling point of water under every condition.</p>', 1),
  (516, 516, '<h2>Thermal Insulation</h2>
  <p>Thermal insulation reduces the rate at which thermal energy transfers between a system and its surroundings. It does not necessarily stop all energy transfer.</p>
  <p>Good insulation may reduce conduction, convection, radiation or a combination of these mechanisms.</p>
  <h3>Examples</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Trapped air in foam or fibreglass reduces conduction and suppresses convection when the air is held in small spaces.</li>
    <li>Shiny surfaces reduce radiative heat transfer by reflecting much of the incident thermal radiation.</li>
    <li>Double-glazed windows trap a layer of air or gas between panes, reducing heat transfer.</li>
    <li>Vacuum flasks use a vacuum between walls to greatly reduce conduction and convection through that gap.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Insulation can keep a hot object warm or a cold object cool by slowing energy transfer. It does not create heat or cold.</p>', 1),
  (517, 517, '<h2>Motion Calculations</h2>
<p>Speed describes how much distance an object covers per unit time.</p>
<p><strong>Speed = Distance ÷ Time</strong></p>
<p>Rearranging gives:</p>
<ul>
<li>Distance = Speed × Time</li>
<li>Time = Distance ÷ Speed</li>
</ul>
<p>Use consistent units. Convert minutes to seconds when speed is in m/s. Convert kilometres to metres when required.</p>
<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p>A cyclist travels 150 m in 30 s.</p>
<p>Speed = 150 ÷ 30 = 5 m/s.</p>
<p><strong>Check:</strong> 5 m/s × 30 s = 150 m.</p>', 1),
  (518, 518, '<h2>Distance–Time Graphs</h2>
<p>A distance–time graph shows how the distance travelled changes with time.</p>
<ul>
<li>Time is normally on the horizontal axis.</li>
<li>Distance is normally on the vertical axis.</li>
<li>A horizontal line means the object is stationary.</li>
<li>A straight rising line means constant speed.</li>
<li>A steeper rising line represents greater speed.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p>An object''s distance increases from 0 m to 60 m in 10 s.</p>
<p>Speed = gradient = change in distance ÷ change in time.</p>
<p>Speed = 60 ÷ 10 = 6 m/s.</p>
<p>Always read the axis scale before calculating a gradient.</p>', 1),
  (519, 519, '<h2>Displacement–Time Graphs</h2>
<p>A displacement–time graph shows displacement from a reference point as time passes. Displacement includes direction.</p>
<ul>
<li>The gradient gives velocity.</li>
<li>A positive gradient indicates positive velocity.</li>
<li>A negative gradient indicates motion in the negative direction.</li>
<li>A horizontal line indicates zero velocity.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p>Displacement changes from 2 m to 18 m in 4 s.</p>
<p>Velocity = change in displacement ÷ time taken.</p>
<p>Velocity = (18 − 2) ÷ 4 = 4 m/s.</p>
<p>The sign of velocity depends on the chosen positive direction.</p>', 1),
  (520, 520, '<h2>Speed–Time Graphs</h2>
<p>A speed–time graph shows how an object''s speed changes with time.</p>
<ul>
<li>The gradient gives the rate of change of speed.</li>
<li>A horizontal line means constant speed.</li>
<li>A rising line means increasing speed.</li>
<li>The area under the graph represents distance travelled.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p>Speed rises uniformly from 0 m/s to 10 m/s in 5 s.</p>
<p>Rate of change of speed = (10 − 0) ÷ 5 = 2 m/s².</p>
<p>Distance = area of triangle = ½ × 5 × 10 = 25 m.</p>', 1),
  (521, 521, '<h2>Velocity–Time Graphs</h2>
<p>A velocity–time graph shows how velocity changes with time, including direction.</p>
<ul>
<li>Gradient = acceleration.</li>
<li>Area between the graph and the time axis gives displacement, using signed area.</li>
<li>A graph above the axis represents positive velocity.</li>
<li>A graph below the axis represents negative velocity.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p>Velocity increases from 4 m/s to 16 m/s in 6 s.</p>
<p>Acceleration = (16 − 4) ÷ 6 = 2 m/s².</p>
<p>For uniform velocity of 5 m/s over 4 s, displacement = 5 × 4 = 20 m.</p>', 1),
  (522, 522, '<h2>Interpreting Motion Graphs</h2>
<p>First identify the graph type and read its axes. Then interpret its gradient or area.</p>
<ul>
<li>Distance–time gradient: speed.</li>
<li>Displacement–time gradient: velocity.</li>
<li>Velocity–time gradient: acceleration.</li>
<li>Area under a velocity–time graph: displacement.</li>
<li>Area under a speed–time graph: distance.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p>A velocity–time graph shows velocity rising uniformly from 0 to 8 m/s over 4 s.</p>
<p>Acceleration = 8 ÷ 4 = 2 m/s².</p>
<p>Displacement = ½ × 4 × 8 = 16 m.</p>
<p>Do not confuse the gradient with the area: they answer different questions.</p>', 1),
  (523, 523, '<h2>Uniform Motion</h2>
<p>Uniform motion means an object covers equal distances in equal time intervals along a straight line. Its velocity is constant.</p>
<p>For uniform motion:</p>
<ul>
<li>Acceleration is zero.</li>
<li>Distance = speed × time when speed is constant.</li>
<li>A distance–time graph is a straight line with constant gradient.</li>
<li>A velocity–time graph is horizontal.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p>A train travels at a constant 20 m/s for 12 s.</p>
<p>Distance = 20 × 12 = 240 m.</p>
<p>Because velocity is constant, acceleration is 0 m/s².</p>', 1),
  (524, 524, '<h2>Uniform Acceleration</h2>
<p>Uniform acceleration means velocity changes by equal amounts in equal time intervals.</p>
<p>For straight-line motion with constant acceleration:</p>
<p><strong>v = u + at</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>u</strong>: initial velocity (m/s)</li>
<li><strong>v</strong>: final velocity (m/s)</li>
<li><strong>a</strong>: acceleration (m/s²)</li>
<li><strong>t</strong>: time (s)</li>
</ul>
<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p>An object starts at 2 m/s and accelerates at 3 m/s² for 4 s.</p>
<p>v = u + at = 2 + (3 × 4) = 14 m/s.</p>
<p>Choose a positive direction and use signs consistently.</p>', 1),
  (525, 525, '<h2>Selecting the Correct Motion Equation</h2>
<p>For straight-line motion with constant acceleration, use the equation that contains the quantities given and the quantity required.</p>
<ul>
<li>v = u + at</li>
<li>s = ut + ½at²</li>
<li>v² = u² + 2as</li>
<li>s = ½(u + v)t</li>
</ul>
<p>Here, s is displacement, u is initial velocity, v is final velocity, a is acceleration, and t is time.</p>
<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p>A body starts from rest and accelerates at 2 m/s² for 5 s. Find displacement.</p>
<p>Known: u = 0, a = 2, t = 5. Unknown: s.</p>
<p>Choose s = ut + ½at² because it uses u, a and t.</p>
<p>s = 0 × 5 + ½ × 2 × 25 = 25 m.</p>', 1),
  (526, 526, '<h2>Solving Motion Problems</h2>
<p>Use a consistent process for motion calculations:</p>
<ol>
<li>Write down the known quantities and the unknown.</li>
<li>Convert units where necessary.</li>
<li>Choose a suitable equation for the motion.</li>
<li>Substitute values with their signs and units.</li>
<li>Calculate and check whether the answer is physically reasonable.</li>
</ol>
<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p>A car starts at 4 m/s and accelerates uniformly at 2 m/s² for 3 s. Find its displacement.</p>
<p>Known: u = 4 m/s, a = 2 m/s², t = 3 s.</p>
<p>s = ut + ½at² = (4 × 3) + (½ × 2 × 9) = 12 + 9 = 21 m.</p>
<p>Check: the average velocity is (4 + 10) ÷ 2 = 7 m/s; 7 × 3 = 21 m.</p>', 1),
  (527, 527, '<h2>Free Fall</h2>
<p>Free fall is motion in which gravity is the only force acting on an object. Air resistance is neglected.</p>
<p>Near Earth''s surface, gravitational acceleration is approximately <strong>9.8 m/s²</strong>, often approximated as <strong>10 m/s²</strong> in school calculations when instructed.</p>
<ul>
<li>An object released from rest has initial velocity u = 0.</li>
<li>When upward is positive, gravitational acceleration is negative.</li>
<li>When downward is positive, gravitational acceleration is positive.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p>A stone is released from rest and falls for 2 s. Take g = 10 m/s².</p>
<p>Using v = u + at:</p>
<p>v = 0 + (10 × 2) = 20 m/s downward.</p>
<p>Using s = ut + ½at²:</p>
<p>s = 0 + ½ × 10 × 2² = 20 m downward.</p>
<hr>
<h2>What is free fall?</h2>
  <p><b>Free fall</b> is motion in which gravity is the only force acting on an object.</p>
  <p>Near Earth''s surface, an object in free fall accelerates downward because of gravity.</p>
  <p>When air resistance is negligible, objects of different masses fall with the same acceleration from the same height.</p>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>A stone is dropped from rest. Ignore air resistance. What happens to its speed as it falls?</p>
  <p>Gravity accelerates the stone downward, so its speed increases as it falls.</p>
  <p><b>Important distinction:</b> an object moving downward is not necessarily in free fall. If air resistance or another force acts significantly, gravity is not the only force acting.</p>', 1),
  (528, 528, '<h2>Newton''s First Law of Motion</h2>
<p>An object remains at rest or continues moving with constant velocity in a straight line unless acted on by a resultant external force.</p>
<p>This is also called the law of inertia.</p>
<ul>
<li>Zero resultant force means zero acceleration.</li>
<li>An object at rest does not need a continuous resultant force to remain at rest.</li>
<li>An object moving at constant velocity does not need a resultant force to keep it moving at that velocity.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p>A trolley moves in a straight line at a constant 4 m/s. If the resultant force remains zero, its velocity stays at 4 m/s.</p>
<p>There is no acceleration because the resultant force is zero.</p>
<hr>
<h2>The law of inertia</h2>
  <p>Newton''s first law states that an object remains at rest or continues moving with constant velocity in a straight line unless acted on by a resultant external force.</p>
  <p>The key phrase is <b>resultant force</b>. Several forces can act on an object and still balance one another.</p>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>A book rests on a table. Its weight acts downward, while the table''s support force acts upward. If these forces balance, the resultant force is zero and the book remains at rest.</p>
  <p>An object does not need a continuous resultant force to maintain constant velocity. A resultant force is needed to change its velocity.</p>', 1),
  (529, 529, '<h2>Newton''s Second Law of Motion</h2>
<p>The resultant force on an object equals the rate of change of its momentum. For constant mass, this gives:</p>
<p><strong>F = ma</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>F = resultant force in newtons (N)</li>
<li>m = mass in kilograms (kg)</li>
<li>a = acceleration in m/s²</li>
</ul>
<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p>A 4 kg object accelerates at 3 m/s².</p>
<p>F = ma = 4 × 3 = 12 N.</p>
<p>Use the resultant force, not just one force acting on the object.</p>
<hr>
<h2>Force, mass and acceleration</h2>
  <p>Newton''s second law relates the resultant force on an object to its mass and acceleration.</p>
  <p>For a constant mass:</p>
  <p><b>F = ma</b></p>
  <ul>
    <li>F = resultant force in newtons (N)</li>
    <li>m = mass in kilograms (kg)</li>
    <li>a = acceleration in m/s²</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>A 4 kg trolley accelerates at 3 m/s².</p>
  <p>F = ma = 4 × 3 = <b>12 N</b>.</p>
  <p><b>Remember:</b> use the resultant force, not automatically one of the individual forces acting on the object.</p>', 1),
  (530, 530, '<h2>Newton''s Third Law of Motion</h2>
<p>When object A exerts a force on object B, object B simultaneously exerts a force of equal magnitude and opposite direction on object A.</p>
<p>The action and reaction forces:</p>
<ul>
<li>Have equal magnitudes and opposite directions.</li>
<li>Act on different objects.</li>
<li>Are the same type of force.</li>
<li>Exist simultaneously.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p>A swimmer pushes water backward. The water exerts a forward force on the swimmer.</p>
<p>The forces are equal in magnitude and opposite in direction, but act on different objects.</p>
<hr>
<h2>Action and reaction</h2>
  <p>Newton''s third law states that when one object exerts a force on another object, the second object simultaneously exerts an equal-sized force in the opposite direction on the first object.</p>
  <p>The two forces:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>have equal magnitudes;</li>
    <li>act in opposite directions;</li>
    <li>act on different objects;</li>
    <li>occur at the same time.</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>When you push a wall, you exert a force on the wall. The wall exerts an equal and opposite force on you.</p>
  <p><b>Key idea:</b> action and reaction forces do not cancel each other on a single object because they act on different objects.</p>', 1),
  (531, 531, '<h2>Mass and Inertia</h2>
<p>Inertia is the tendency of an object to resist a change in its state of rest or motion.</p>
<p>Mass is a measure of an object''s inertia.</p>
<ul>
<li>Mass is measured in kilograms (kg).</li>
<li>Greater mass means greater inertia.</li>
<li>Mass does not depend on location.</li>
<li>Weight is a gravitational force and can change with gravitational field strength.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p>A 10 kg trolley has more inertia than a 2 kg trolley. A greater resultant force is required to give the 10 kg trolley the same acceleration.</p>
<p>From F = ma, for the same acceleration, force increases with mass.</p>
<hr>
<h2>What is inertia?</h2>
  <p><b>Inertia</b> is the tendency of an object to resist a change in its state of rest or motion.</p>
  <p>Mass is a measure of an object''s inertia. An object with greater mass is harder to accelerate or decelerate by the same resultant force.</p>
  <h3>Worked comparison</h3>
  <p>A 2 kg trolley and a 10 kg trolley experience the same resultant force of 20 N.</p>
  <p>For the 2 kg trolley: a = 20/2 = 10 m/s².</p>
  <p>For the 10 kg trolley: a = 20/10 = 2 m/s².</p>
  <p>The more massive trolley has less acceleration because it has greater inertia.</p>
  <p><b>Do not confuse:</b> mass is measured in kilograms; weight is a force measured in newtons.</p>', 1),
  (532, 532, '<h2>Force, Mass and Acceleration Calculations</h2>
<p>For an object of constant mass:</p>
<p><strong>F = ma</strong></p>
<p>Rearrange depending on the unknown:</p>
<ul>
<li>Force: F = ma</li>
<li>Mass: m = F ÷ a</li>
<li>Acceleration: a = F ÷ m</li>
</ul>
<p>F must be the resultant force acting on the object.</p>
<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p>A resultant force of 18 N gives an object an acceleration of 3 m/s².</p>
<p>m = F ÷ a = 18 ÷ 3 = 6 kg.</p>
<p>Check: 6 × 3 = 18 N.</p>', 1),
  (533, 533, '<h2>Mass and Weight</h2>
<p>Mass measures inertia and is measured in kilograms (kg). Weight is the gravitational force acting on an object and is measured in newtons (N).</p>
<p><strong>Weight: W = mg</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>W = weight in N</li>
<li>m = mass in kg</li>
<li>g = gravitational field strength in N/kg (numerically equal to m/s²)</li>
</ul>
<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p>A 6 kg object is in a gravitational field of 10 N/kg.</p>
<p>W = mg = 6 × 10 = 60 N.</p>
<p>Mass stays the same when location changes, but weight changes with gravitational field strength.</p>', 1),
  (534, 534, '<h2>Gravitational Field Strength</h2>
<p>Gravitational field strength at a location is the gravitational force acting per unit mass on an object placed there.</p>
<p><strong>g = W ÷ m</strong></p>
<p>Its SI unit is N/kg. Near Earth''s surface, g is approximately 9.8 N/kg, often rounded to 10 N/kg when a question instructs you to use that value.</p>
<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p>A 4 kg object has a weight of 39.2 N.</p>
<p>g = W ÷ m = 39.2 ÷ 4 = 9.8 N/kg.</p>
<p>Do not confuse gravitational field strength with the object''s mass.</p>', 1),
  (535, 535, '<h2>Calculating Weight</h2>
<p>Weight is the gravitational force acting on an object.</p>
<p><strong>W = mg</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>W = weight in newtons (N)</li>
<li>m = mass in kilograms (kg)</li>
<li>g = gravitational field strength in N/kg</li>
</ul>
<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p>Calculate the weight of a 7 kg object where g = 10 N/kg.</p>
<p>W = 7 × 10 = 70 N.</p>
<p>Always use mass in kilograms and the gravitational field strength specified in the question.</p>', 1),
  (536, 536, '<h2>Density</h2>
<p>Density is mass per unit volume.</p>
<p><strong>Density = Mass ÷ Volume</strong></p>
<p>Common units are kg/m³ and g/cm³. The mass and volume units must be compatible.</p>
<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p>A block has mass 600 g and volume 200 cm³.</p>
<p>Density = 600 ÷ 200 = 3 g/cm³.</p>
<p>Density describes how much mass is contained in a given volume.</p>', 1),
  (537, 537, '<h2>Calculating Density</h2>
<p>Use <strong>ρ = m/V</strong>, where ρ is density, m is mass and V is volume.</p>
<p>Rearrange the formula according to the unknown:</p>
<ul>
<li>Density: ρ = m/V</li>
<li>Mass: m = ρV</li>
<li>Volume: V = m/ρ</li>
</ul>
<p>For a regularly shaped solid, volume can be calculated from its dimensions. For an irregular solid, volume can be found by liquid displacement when appropriate.</p>
<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p>A metal block has mass 540 g and volume 60 cm³.</p>
<p>ρ = 540 ÷ 60 = 9 g/cm³.</p>', 1),
  (538, 538, '<h2>Relative Density</h2>
<p>Relative density compares the density of a substance with the density of water at the same reference conditions.</p>
<p><strong>Relative density = Density of substance ÷ Density of water</strong></p>
<p>Relative density has no unit because it is a ratio of two densities expressed in compatible units.</p>
<p>For many school calculations, the density of water is taken as 1 g/cm³ or 1000 kg/m³.</p>
<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p>A liquid has density 0.8 g/cm³.</p>
<p>Relative density = 0.8 ÷ 1 = 0.8.</p>', 1),
  (539, 539, '<h2>Causes of Friction</h2>
<p>Friction is a force that opposes relative motion, or the tendency of relative motion, between surfaces in contact.</p>
<p>Friction occurs because contacting surfaces have microscopic irregularities and because the surfaces can interact at their contact points.</p>
<ul>
<li>Roughness can increase resistance to sliding.</li>
<li>The force pressing surfaces together can affect friction.</li>
<li>Lubricants can reduce direct contact between surfaces.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p>A box is pushed across a rough floor. The floor exerts a frictional force opposite the box''s sliding direction.</p>
<p>If the box is pushed to the right, kinetic friction acts to the left.</p>', 1),
  (540, 540, '<h2>Effects of Friction</h2>
<p>Friction can be useful or undesirable depending on the situation.</p>
<h3>Useful effects</h3>
<ul>
<li>It allows shoes to grip the ground when walking.</li>
<li>It helps vehicle tyres grip the road.</li>
<li>It enables brakes to slow vehicles.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Undesirable effects</h3>
<ul>
<li>It causes wear of contacting surfaces.</li>
<li>It can produce unwanted heating.</li>
<li>It wastes some useful mechanical energy through dissipation.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p>Car brakes use friction to reduce the car''s kinetic energy. Some of that energy is transferred to thermal energy in the brakes and surrounding materials.</p>', 1),
  (541, 541, '<h2>Advantages and Disadvantages of Friction</h2>
<p>Whether friction is helpful depends on the task.</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Advantages</th><th>Disadvantages</th></tr>
<tr><td>Enables walking and gripping</td><td>Causes wear</td></tr>
<tr><td>Allows braking</td><td>Produces unwanted heat</td></tr>
<tr><td>Helps writing with a pencil</td><td>Can reduce machine efficiency</td></tr>
</table>
<p>Friction should be increased where grip is needed and reduced where unwanted resistance causes problems.</p>
<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p>Tyre tread helps provide grip on the road, while lubricating oil reduces friction between moving engine parts.</p>', 1),
  (542, 542, '<h2>Reducing and Increasing Friction</h2>
<p>Friction can be controlled according to the purpose of the device.</p>
<h3>Ways to reduce friction</h3>
<ul>
<li>Lubricate surfaces with oil or grease.</li>
<li>Use rollers or ball bearings to replace sliding with rolling.</li>
<li>Smooth suitable contacting surfaces.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Ways to increase friction</h3>
<ul>
<li>Use rough or patterned surfaces where grip is needed.</li>
<li>Use tyre tread and suitable shoe soles.</li>
<li>Apply brakes to create frictional resistance.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p>Ball bearings in a wheel reduce resistance by allowing rolling between surfaces instead of direct sliding.</p>', 1),
  (543, 543, '<h2>Air Resistance and Terminal Velocity</h2>
<p>Air resistance is a force that opposes an object''s motion relative to air. It depends on factors including speed, shape and exposed area.</p>
<p>When an object falls:</p>
<ol>
<li>At first, its weight acts downward and air resistance is small.</li>
<li>As its speed increases, air resistance generally increases.</li>
<li>The resultant downward force decreases.</li>
<li>When air resistance equals weight, the resultant force becomes zero.</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>Terminal velocity</strong> is the constant velocity reached when the resultant force is zero.</p>
<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p>A falling object has weight 40 N downward and air resistance 40 N upward.</p>
<p>Resultant force = 40 − 40 = 0 N.</p>
<p>Its acceleration is zero. If it is already moving, it continues at constant velocity: terminal velocity.</p>', 1),
  (544, 544, '<h2>The value of g</h2>
  <p>Near Earth''s surface, freely falling objects accelerate downward at approximately <b>9.8 m/s²</b>. In many school calculations, use <b>g = 10 m/s²</b> when instructed.</p>
  <p>Acceleration describes the change in velocity per unit time.</p>
  <p><b>a = (v − u)/t</b></p>
  <ul>
    <li>u = initial velocity</li>
    <li>v = final velocity</li>
    <li>t = time taken</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>An object starts from rest and falls freely for 3 s. Take g = 10 m/s².</p>
  <p>v = u + gt</p>
  <p>v = 0 + 10 × 3 = <b>30 m/s downward</b>.</p>
  <p><b>Watch the units:</b> acceleration is measured in m/s², not m/s.</p>', 1),
  (545, 545, '<h2>Find the net force first</h2>
  <p>When forces act in opposite directions, subtract the smaller force from the larger force to find the resultant force.</p>
  <p>Then use <b>a = F/m</b>.</p>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>A 4 kg trolley is pulled to the right by 20 N. Friction acts to the left with a force of 8 N.</p>
  <p>Resultant force = 20 − 8 = 12 N to the right.</p>
  <p>Acceleration = 12 ÷ 4 = <b>3 m/s² to the right</b>.</p>
  <p><b>Common error:</b> using 20 N as the resultant force while ignoring friction.</p>', 1),
  (546, 546, '<h2>Rearranging Newton''s second law</h2>
  <p>From <b>F = ma</b>, two useful forms are:</p>
  <p><b>m = F/a</b></p>
  <p><b>a = F/m</b></p>
  <p>Use these relationships when the force, mass or acceleration is unknown.</p>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>A resultant force of 36 N produces an acceleration of 4 m/s².</p>
  <p>m = F/a = 36/4 = <b>9 kg</b>.</p>
  <p>Check the units: N divided by m/s² gives kg.</p>', 1),
  (547, 547, '<h2>Recognising a force pair</h2>
  <p>To identify a Newton''s third-law pair, name both interacting objects and the force each exerts on the other.</p>
  <h3>Example: a book on a table</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>The book pushes downward on the table.</li>
    <li>The table pushes upward on the book.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>These are an action-reaction pair. However, the upward support force and the book''s downward weight are <b>not</b> a third-law pair: both act on the book.</p>
  <p>The reaction to Earth''s gravitational force on the book is the book''s gravitational force on Earth.</p>
  <p><b>Diagnostic rule:</b> if both forces act on the same object, they are not a Newton''s third-law pair.</p>', 1),
  (548, 548, '<h2>Inertia in everyday life</h2>
  <p>Inertia helps explain everyday events involving sudden changes in motion.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Braking:</b> when a vehicle stops suddenly, passengers tend to continue moving forward.</li>
    <li><b>Starting:</b> when a bus accelerates forward, passengers may appear to lean backward relative to the bus.</li>
    <li><b>Seat belts:</b> they help restrain occupants during rapid deceleration.</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Worked explanation</h3>
  <p>A moving car brakes suddenly. The car''s speed decreases, but a passenger''s body tends to continue moving forward. A seat belt exerts a force that helps slow the passenger with the car.</p>
  <p><b>Key idea:</b> inertia is not an extra forward force. It is the tendency of the body to resist the change in its motion.</p>', 1),
  (549, 549, '<h2>Forces acting in the same direction</h2>
  <p>When forces act on the same object in the same direction, their magnitudes are added to find the resultant force.</p>
  <p><b>R = F₁ + F₂</b></p>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>Two people push a trolley forward with forces of 30 N and 20 N.</p>
  <p>R = 30 + 20 = <b>50 N forward</b>.</p>
  <p>If the object has mass 10 kg, its acceleration is:</p>
  <p>a = R/m = 50/10 = <b>5 m/s² forward</b>.</p>
  <p><b>Remember:</b> add forces only after confirming that they act in the same direction.</p>', 1),
  (550, 550, '<h2>Opposing forces</h2>
  <p>When two forces act in opposite directions along the same straight line, subtract the smaller magnitude from the larger.</p>
  <p><b>Resultant magnitude = larger force − smaller force</b></p>
  <p>The resultant acts in the direction of the larger force.</p>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>A rope is pulled eastward with 70 N and westward with 45 N.</p>
  <p>R = 70 − 45 = <b>25 N eastward</b>.</p>
  <p>If both forces were equal, the resultant would be zero.</p>
  <p><b>Check:</b> state both the magnitude and direction of the resultant.</p>', 1),
  (551, 551, '<h2>Support from a surface</h2>
  <p>The <b>normal reaction</b> is the contact force a surface exerts on an object. It acts perpendicular to the contact surface.</p>
  <p>For a stationary object on a horizontal surface with no other vertical forces, the normal reaction balances the object''s weight.</p>
  <p><b>R = mg</b> in that specific situation.</p>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>A 4 kg box rests on a horizontal floor. Take g = 10 N/kg.</p>
  <p>Weight = mg = 4 × 10 = 40 N downward.</p>
  <p>Since the box is vertically balanced, the normal reaction is <b>40 N upward</b>.</p>
  <p><b>Important:</b> the normal reaction is not always equal to weight. Other forces or acceleration can change its value.</p>', 1),
  (552, 552, '<h2>What is tension?</h2>
  <p><b>Tension</b> is the pulling force transmitted through a taut string, rope or cable.</p>
  <p>Tension acts along the string and pulls on the objects attached to its ends.</p>
  <h3>Worked example: a hanging mass</h3>
  <p>A 2 kg mass hangs at rest from a light vertical string. Take g = 10 N/kg.</p>
  <p>Weight = mg = 2 × 10 = 20 N downward.</p>
  <p>Because the mass is at rest, the upward tension balances the weight:</p>
  <p><b>T = 20 N</b>.</p>
  <p><b>Important:</b> tension does not always equal weight. That equality applies here because the mass is stationary and the only forces are tension and weight.</p>', 1),
  (553, 553, '<h2>Representing forces</h2>
  <p>A force diagram represents the forces acting on one chosen object using arrows.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>The arrow direction shows the force direction.</li>
    <li>The arrow length can represent the force magnitude when drawn to scale.</li>
    <li>Label each arrow with the force name or value.</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Worked example: a box on a floor</h3>
  <p>A box rests on a horizontal floor.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Weight acts downward.</li>
    <li>Normal reaction acts upward.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>If the box is stationary and no other vertical forces act, the two forces are equal in magnitude.</p>
  <p><b>Rule:</b> draw only forces acting on the chosen object. Do not draw the force it exerts on another object as if that force acted on the chosen object.</p>', 1),
  (554, 554, '<h2>Apparent weight in a lift</h2>
  <p>A person standing on a scale in an elevator experiences weight downward and a normal reaction upward. The scale reading is the normal reaction.</p>
  <p>When the elevator is stationary or moving at constant velocity, the forces balance:</p>
  <p><b>R = mg</b></p>
  <p>If the elevator accelerates upward, the normal reaction is greater than weight. If it accelerates downward, the normal reaction is less than weight.</p>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>A 50 kg person stands in an elevator accelerating upward at 2 m/s². Take g = 10 m/s².</p>
  <p>R − mg = ma</p>
  <p>R = m(g + a) = 50(10 + 2) = <b>600 N</b>.</p>
  <p>This is an application of Newton''s second law, not a change in the person''s mass.</p>', 1),
  (555, 555, '<h2>Force during braking</h2>
  <p>When brakes slow a moving vehicle, the resultant force acts opposite its direction of motion. The vehicle therefore decelerates.</p>
  <p>Using Newton''s second law, the magnitude of the resultant force is:</p>
  <p><b>F = ma</b></p>
  <p>Here, use the magnitude of the deceleration when calculating the magnitude of the braking force.</p>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>A 1,000 kg car slows at 3 m/s² on a level road. Assume the stated deceleration is caused by the resultant braking force.</p>
  <p>F = ma = 1,000 × 3 = <b>3,000 N</b>, opposite the motion.</p>
  <p><b>Distinguish:</b> deceleration is acceleration opposite the current velocity; it is not a separate kind of force.</p>', 1),
  (556, 556, '<h2>Momentum</h2>
  <p>Momentum is the product of an object''s mass and velocity.</p>
  <p><b>p = mv</b></p>
  <ul>
    <li>p = momentum in kg m/s</li>
    <li>m = mass in kg</li>
    <li>v = velocity in m/s</li>
  </ul>
  <p>A resultant force changes an object''s momentum. For constant mass and a constant force, Newton''s second law can be expressed as:</p>
  <p><b>F = (change in momentum)/(time taken)</b></p>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>A 2 kg trolley moves at 3 m/s eastward.</p>
  <p>p = mv = 2 × 3 = <b>6 kg m/s eastward</b>.</p>
  <p><b>Note:</b> velocity has direction, so momentum also has direction.</p>', 1),
  (557, 557, '<h2>What is impulse?</h2>
  <p>Impulse is the change in momentum produced by a force acting over a time interval.</p>
  <p>For a constant resultant force:</p>
  <p><b>Impulse = F × t = change in momentum</b></p>
  <p>The SI unit is N s, equivalent to kg m/s.</p>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>A resultant force of 20 N acts on a ball for 0.5 s.</p>
  <p>Impulse = 20 × 0.5 = <b>10 N s</b>.</p>
  <p>This means the ball''s momentum changes by 10 kg m/s in the direction of the resultant force.</p>
  <p><b>Safety application:</b> airbags and padded surfaces increase the time over which a person''s momentum changes, reducing the average force for the same change in momentum.</p>', 1),
  (558, 558, '<h2>Momentum in an isolated system</h2>
  <p>The principle of conservation of momentum states that the total momentum of a system remains constant when the resultant external force on the system is zero.</p>
  <p>For two objects moving along one straight line:</p>
  <p><b>Total momentum before interaction = total momentum after interaction</b></p>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>A 2 kg trolley moves at 4 m/s and collides with a stationary 2 kg trolley. They stick together.</p>
  <p>Initial momentum = (2 × 4) + (2 × 0) = 8 kg m/s.</p>
  <p>Combined mass = 2 + 2 = 4 kg.</p>
  <p>Final velocity = 8/4 = <b>2 m/s</b> in the original direction.</p>
  <p><b>Important:</b> momentum is conserved for the system when external forces have negligible resultant impulse during the interaction.</p>', 1),
  (559, 559, '<h2>Energy of a moving object</h2>
  <p>Kinetic energy is the energy an object possesses because it is moving.</p>
  <p><b>KE = ½mv²</b></p>
  <ul>
    <li>KE = kinetic energy in joules (J)</li>
    <li>m = mass in kilograms (kg)</li>
    <li>v = speed in metres per second (m/s)</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>A 4 kg object moves at 3 m/s.</p>
  <p>KE = ½ × 4 × 3²</p>
  <p>KE = 2 × 9 = <b>18 J</b>.</p>
  <p><b>Common mistake:</b> square the speed before multiplying by half the mass.</p>', 1),
  (560, 560, '<h2>Energy stored by deformation</h2>
  <p>An elastic object can store elastic potential energy when it is stretched or compressed, provided it behaves elastically.</p>
  <p>For a spring obeying Hooke''s law:</p>
  <p><b>E = ½Fx = ½kx²</b></p>
  <ul>
    <li>E = elastic potential energy in joules</li>
    <li>F = final force in newtons</li>
    <li>x = extension or compression in metres</li>
    <li>k = spring constant in N/m</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>A spring has a spring constant of 200 N/m and is extended by 0.1 m.</p>
  <p>E = ½ × 200 × 0.1²</p>
  <p>E = 100 × 0.01 = <b>1 J</b>.</p>
  <p>This formula applies while the spring follows Hooke''s law.</p>', 1),
  (561, 561, '<h2>Energy cannot be created or destroyed</h2>
  <p>The principle of conservation of energy states that energy cannot be created or destroyed; it can only be transferred or transformed from one form to another.</p>
  <p>In an isolated system, the total energy remains constant.</p>
  <h3>Worked example: a falling object</h3>
  <p>A ball is held above the ground. It has gravitational potential energy because of its position.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Before falling: most of the relevant mechanical energy is gravitational potential energy.</li>
    <li>During the fall: gravitational potential energy decreases while kinetic energy increases.</li>
    <li>If air resistance is negligible: the loss in gravitational potential energy equals the gain in kinetic energy.</li>
  </ul>
  <p><b>Important:</b> when friction or air resistance acts, some mechanical energy is transferred to thermal energy and sound. Total energy is still conserved.</p>', 1),
  (562, 562, '<h2>Changing from one form to another</h2>
  <p>An energy transformation occurs when energy changes from one form to another.</p>
  <h3>Examples</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Electric lamp: electrical energy → light and thermal energy.</li>
    <li>Electric motor: electrical energy → kinetic energy and thermal energy.</li>
    <li>Battery-powered torch: chemical energy → electrical energy → light and thermal energy.</li>
    <li>Falling object: gravitational potential energy → kinetic energy.</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>In an electric fan, electrical energy is transferred mainly into the kinetic energy of the rotating blades, with some energy becoming sound and thermal energy.</p>
  <p><b>Remember:</b> identify the input energy and the useful output, then account for other outputs where relevant.</p>', 1),
  (563, 563, '<h2>Rate of doing work</h2>
  <p>Power is the rate at which work is done or energy is transferred.</p>
  <p><b>P = W/t</b></p>
  <p>Power can also be calculated from energy transferred:</p>
  <p><b>P = E/t</b></p>
  <ul>
    <li>P = power in watts (W)</li>
    <li>W = work done in joules (J)</li>
    <li>E = energy transferred in joules (J)</li>
    <li>t = time in seconds (s)</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>A machine does 600 J of work in 20 s.</p>
  <p>P = W/t = 600/20 = <b>30 W</b>.</p>
  <p><b>Distinguish:</b> work tells you how much energy is transferred; power tells you how quickly it is transferred.</p>', 1),
  (564, 564, '<h2>Useful output compared with input</h2>
  <p>Efficiency describes what fraction of the input energy or work becomes useful output energy or work.</p>
  <p><b>Efficiency = (useful output energy ÷ input energy) × 100%</b></p>
  <p>The same ratio can be used with useful output work and input work when comparing work.</p>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>A machine receives 500 J of energy and transfers 350 J as useful output energy.</p>
  <p>Efficiency = (350/500) × 100%</p>
  <p>Efficiency = <b>70%</b>.</p>
  <p>The remaining 150 J is transferred into other forms, such as thermal energy or sound.</p>
  <p><b>Important:</b> for a real machine, efficiency is less than 100% when some input energy is not converted into the defined useful output.</p>', 1),
  (565, 565, '<h2>Force multiplication</h2>
  <p>Mechanical advantage (MA) compares the load moved by a machine with the effort applied.</p>
  <p><b>MA = load force ÷ effort force</b></p>
  <p>Mechanical advantage has no unit because it is a ratio of two forces.</p>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>A machine lifts a load of 600 N using an effort of 150 N.</p>
  <p>MA = 600/150 = <b>4</b>.</p>
  <p>The machine''s mechanical advantage is 4: the load force is four times the effort force.</p>
  <p><b>Important:</b> mechanical advantage does not by itself tell you the machine''s efficiency.</p>', 1),
  (566, 566, '<h2>Distance moved by effort and load</h2>
  <p>Velocity ratio (VR) compares the distance moved by the effort with the distance moved by the load in the same time interval.</p>
  <p><b>VR = distance moved by effort ÷ distance moved by load</b></p>
  <p>For a simple ideal machine, this ratio describes the relationship between the input and output distances. It has no unit.</p>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>The effort moves 2 m while the load rises 0.5 m.</p>
  <p>VR = 2/0.5 = <b>4</b>.</p>
  <p>The effort travels four times as far as the load.</p>
  <p><b>Do not confuse:</b> velocity ratio compares distances; mechanical advantage compares forces.</p>', 1),
  (567, 567, '<h2>Connecting mechanical advantage and velocity ratio</h2>
  <p>For a machine, efficiency can be calculated using mechanical advantage and velocity ratio:</p>
  <p><b>Efficiency = (MA/VR) × 100%</b></p>
  <p>Here:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>MA = load force ÷ effort force</li>
    <li>VR = effort distance ÷ load distance</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>A machine has MA = 3 and VR = 4.</p>
  <p>Efficiency = (3/4) × 100% = <b>75%</b>.</p>
  <p>For an ordinary real machine, MA is typically less than VR because some input energy is dissipated into unwanted forms.</p>
  <p><b>Check:</b> calculate MA and VR from the correct quantities before applying the efficiency formula.</p>', 1),
  (568, 568, '<h2>How a lever works</h2>
  <p>A lever is a rigid bar that turns about a fixed point called the fulcrum or pivot.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Fulcrum: the pivot about which the lever turns.</li>
    <li>Effort: the force applied to the lever.</li>
    <li>Load: the force or resistance the lever moves.</li>
    <li>Effort arm: perpendicular distance from the pivot to the effort''s line of action.</li>
    <li>Load arm: perpendicular distance from the pivot to the load''s line of action.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>For a lever in equilibrium, the principle of moments gives:</p>
  <p><b>Effort × effort arm = load × load arm</b></p>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>A 200 N load acts 0.2 m from the pivot. The effort acts 0.8 m from the pivot.</p>
  <p>Effort × 0.8 = 200 × 0.2</p>
  <p>Effort = 40/0.8 = <b>50 N</b>.</p>
  <p>This calculation assumes the lever is in equilibrium and the distances are measured perpendicular to the forces.</p>', 1),
  (569, 569, '<h2>Meaning of a Wave</h2>

<h3>Core idea</h3>
<p>
A <b>wave</b> is a disturbance that transfers energy
from one place to another.
</p>

<p>
When a stone falls into still water, ripples spread
out from the point where the stone enters. The
disturbance travels across the water, carrying energy
away from the point of impact.
</p>

<h3>Worked example 1</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> A ripple moves across a pond after
a stone falls into it. What is travelling across the pond?</p>
<p><b>Reasoning:</b> The ripple is the travelling
disturbance. It transfers energy away from the point
where the stone entered the water.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> A wave disturbance carrying energy.</p>

<h3>Worked example 2</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> A loudspeaker produces sound that
reaches a learner across a classroom. What does the
sound wave transfer?</p>
<p><b>Reasoning:</b> The sound wave travels through
the air and transfers energy from the vibrating
loudspeaker to the learner''s ears.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> Energy.</p>

<h3>Check your understanding</h3>
<p>A disturbance travels along a rope after one end
is moved. What is the disturbance an example of?</p>', 1),
  (570, 570, '<h2>Energy Transfer by Waves</h2>

<h3>Core idea</h3>
<p>
A wave transfers <b>energy</b> from one place to
another. In a material medium, particles generally
oscillate about their equilibrium positions rather
than travelling permanently with the wave.
</p>

<p>
Imagine a floating cork on water. As ripples pass,
the cork moves up and down, but it does not travel
all the way across the pond with each ripple.
</p>

<h3>Worked example 1</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> Ripples pass a floating cork. The
cork bobs up and down. What does this show?</p>
<p><b>Reasoning:</b> The cork responds to the passing
disturbance, but it is not carried across the pond
by the ripple.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> The wave transfers energy while the
cork oscillates near its position.</p>

<h3>Worked example 2</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> A rope pulse reaches a loose ribbon
tied to the rope. Has the ribbon travelled along the
rope with the pulse?</p>
<p><b>Reasoning:</b> The ribbon moves as the pulse
passes. The pulse travels along the rope, while the
ribbon does not travel with it from end to end.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> No. The pulse transfers energy along
the rope while the ribbon moves locally.</p>

<h3>Check your understanding</h3>
<p>A water wave reaches a floating leaf. Which
statement best describes the transfer?</p>', 1),
  (571, 571, '<h2>Transverse Waves</h2>

<h3>Core idea</h3>
<p>
In a <b>transverse wave</b>, the oscillation of the
medium is perpendicular to the direction in which
the wave travels.
</p>

<p>
Perpendicular means that the two directions are
at right angles to each other.
</p>

<p>
For example, if a pulse travels horizontally along
a rope while a point on the rope moves up and down,
the motion is transverse.
</p>

<h3>Worked example 1</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> A wave travels from left to right
along a rope. A point on the rope moves up and down.
Classify the wave.</p>
<p><b>Reasoning:</b> The wave travels horizontally
while the point moves vertically. These directions
are perpendicular.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> A transverse wave.</p>

<h3>Worked example 2</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> A learner moves one end of a rope
up and down, producing a pulse that travels along
the rope. How does the rope move relative to the
direction of wave travel?</p>
<p><b>Reasoning:</b> The rope moves vertically while
the pulse travels horizontally.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> The rope moves perpendicular to
the direction of wave travel.</p>

<h3>Check your understanding</h3>
<p>A wave travels horizontally while the medium
oscillates vertically. What type of wave is it?</p>', 1),
  (572, 572, '<h2>Longitudinal Waves</h2>

<h3>Core idea</h3>
<p>
In a <b>longitudinal wave</b>, the particles of the
medium oscillate parallel to the direction in which
the wave travels.
</p>

<p>
Parallel means that the directions lie along the
same line. The particles move backwards and forwards
as the disturbance travels through the medium.
</p>

<p>
Sound travelling through air is a common example
of a longitudinal wave.
</p>

<h3>Worked example 1</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> Sound travels through air from a
loudspeaker to a listener. The air particles vibrate
backwards and forwards along the direction of travel.
Classify the wave.</p>
<p><b>Reasoning:</b> The particle oscillation is
parallel to the direction of wave travel.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> A longitudinal wave.</p>

<h3>Worked example 2</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> A slinky is pushed and pulled
along its length, producing a travelling pulse.
What type of wave is produced?</p>
<p><b>Reasoning:</b> The coils move back and forth
along the same direction in which the pulse travels.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> A longitudinal wave.</p>

<h3>Check your understanding</h3>
<p>A disturbance travels along a spring while its
coils oscillate back and forth along the spring.
Classify the wave.</p>', 1),
  (573, 573, '<h2>Particle Motion in a Medium</h2>

<h3>Core idea</h3>
<p>
When a mechanical wave passes through a medium,
its particles oscillate about their
<b>equilibrium positions</b>. An equilibrium
position is the particle''s undisturbed position.
</p>

<p>
The particles interact with neighbouring particles,
allowing the disturbance and energy to travel through
the medium. The particles do not generally travel
all the way from the source to the receiver with
the wave.
</p>

<h3>Worked example 1</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> A sound wave travels through a
classroom. Does an air particle near the door travel
all the way to a listener''s ear?</p>
<p><b>Reasoning:</b> The air particle oscillates around
its equilibrium position as the sound disturbance
passes. The disturbance travels across the classroom.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> No. The particle oscillates locally;
the sound wave transfers energy across the room.</p>

<h3>Worked example 2</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> A pulse travels along a rope.
A marked point moves away from and then returns
towards its original position. What does this
illustrate?</p>
<p><b>Reasoning:</b> The point moves temporarily as
the pulse passes, rather than travelling permanently
along the rope.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> Local oscillation about an
equilibrium position.</p>

<h3>Check your understanding</h3>
<p>As a sound wave passes through air, what do the
air particles do?</p>', 1),
  (574, 574, '<h2>Amplitude</h2>

  <h3>Meaning</h3>
  <p>Amplitude is the <b>maximum displacement of a particle from its equilibrium position</b>.</p>

  <p>On a transverse wave, measure amplitude vertically from the equilibrium line to a crest or to a trough. Do not measure from crest to trough.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 1</h3>
  <p>A wave has a crest 4 cm above its equilibrium position. Find its amplitude.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify the maximum displacement from equilibrium.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> The displacement is 4 cm.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Amplitude = 4 cm.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2</h3>
  <p>The vertical distance from a crest to the adjacent trough is 12 cm. Find the amplitude of the wave.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Crest-to-trough distance = 2 × amplitude.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> 12 = 2A</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> A = 12 ÷ 2 = 6 cm.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Amplitude = 6 cm.</p>

  <h3>Remember</h3>
  <p>Amplitude is measured from equilibrium to a crest or trough, not from crest to trough.</p>', 1),
  (575, 575, '<h2>Wavelength</h2>

  <h3>Meaning</h3>
  <p>Wavelength is the distance between two successive points on a wave that are in the same phase.</p>

  <p>Examples include:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>One crest to the next crest.</li>
    <li>One trough to the next trough.</li>
    <li>One compression to the next compression in a longitudinal wave.</li>
    <li>One rarefaction to the next rarefaction in a longitudinal wave.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>The symbol for wavelength is <b>λ</b> (lambda). Its SI unit is the metre (m).</p>

  <h3>Worked example 1</h3>
  <p>The distance between two successive crests is 0.8 m. Find the wavelength.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Successive crests are one wavelength apart.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> λ = 0.8 m.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2</h3>
  <p>Six complete wavelengths occupy a distance of 12 m. Find the wavelength.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Total distance = number of wavelengths × wavelength.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> 12 = 6λ.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> λ = 12 ÷ 6 = 2 m.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Wavelength = 2 m.</p>

  <h3>Remember</h3>
  <p>Count complete wavelengths, not the number of crests alone. Two successive crests are separated by one wavelength.</p>', 1),
  (576, 576, '<h2>Frequency</h2>

  <h3>Meaning</h3>
  <p>Frequency is the <b>number of complete oscillations or wave cycles produced per second</b>.</p>

  <p>Its symbol is <b>f</b>. Its SI unit is the <b>hertz (Hz)</b>.</p>
  <p>1 Hz means one complete oscillation per second.</p>

  <h3>Formula</h3>
  <p><b>Frequency = Number of complete oscillations ÷ Time taken</b></p>
  <p>f = N/t</p>
  <p>Where N is the number of complete oscillations and t is the time in seconds.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 1</h3>
  <p>A source makes 20 complete oscillations in 5 seconds. Find its frequency.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> N = 20, t = 5 s.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> f = N/t.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> f = 20/5 = 4 Hz.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Frequency = 4 Hz.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2</h3>
  <p>A tuning fork vibrates at 50 Hz for 4 seconds. How many complete oscillations does it make?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> f = N/t, so N = ft.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> N = 50 × 4.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> 200 complete oscillations.</p>

  <h3>Remember</h3>
  <p>Frequency tells you how many cycles occur each second. It is not the total number of cycles unless the time is one second.</p>', 1),
  (577, 577, '<h2>Period of a Wave</h2>

  <h3>Meaning</h3>
  <p>The period is the <b>time taken to complete one full oscillation</b>.</p>

  <p>Its symbol is <b>T</b>, and its SI unit is the second (s).</p>

  <h3>Formula</h3>
  <p><b>Period = Total time ÷ Number of complete oscillations</b></p>
  <p>T = t/N</p>

  <p>Frequency and period are related by:</p>
  <p><b>T = 1/f</b></p>
  <p><b>f = 1/T</b></p>

  <h3>Worked example 1</h3>
  <p>A source makes 10 complete oscillations in 5 seconds. Find its period.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Total time = 5 s; number of oscillations = 10.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> T = t/N = 5/10.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> T = 0.5 s.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2</h3>
  <p>A wave has a frequency of 20 Hz. Calculate its period.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> T = 1/f.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> T = 1/20.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> T = 0.05 s.</p>

  <h3>Remember</h3>
  <p>Frequency and period are inversely proportional. A higher frequency means a shorter period.</p>', 1),
  (578, 578, '<h2>Wave Speed</h2>

  <h3>Meaning</h3>
  <p>Wave speed is the distance travelled by a wave per unit time.</p>

  <p>Its SI unit is metres per second (m/s).</p>

  <h3>Formula</h3>
  <p><b>Speed = Distance ÷ Time</b></p>
  <p>For a periodic wave:</p>
  <p><b>v = fλ</b></p>

  <p>Where:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>v = wave speed in m/s</li>
    <li>f = frequency in Hz</li>
    <li>λ = wavelength in m</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Worked example 1</h3>
  <p>A wave has a frequency of 5 Hz and a wavelength of 2 m. Calculate its speed.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> v = fλ.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> v = 5 × 2.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> v = 10 m/s.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2</h3>
  <p>A wave travels 24 m in 6 seconds. Calculate its speed.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> v = distance ÷ time.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> v = 24/6.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> v = 4 m/s.</p>

  <h3>Remember</h3>
  <p>Use v = fλ when frequency and wavelength are known. Use distance ÷ time when the distance travelled and time taken are known.</p>', 1),
  (579, 579, '<h2>Reflection of Waves</h2>

<h3>Core idea</h3>
<p>
Reflection occurs when a wave reaches a boundary and
returns into the medium from which it came.
</p>

<p>
For example, a pulse travelling along a rope can
reach a fixed end and return along the rope. The
returning pulse is a reflected wave.
</p>

<h3>Worked example 1</h3>
<p>
A water wave reaches a wall and travels back across
the water. Identify the phenomenon.
</p>
<p>
<b>Reasoning:</b> The wave returns after reaching
the boundary.
</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> Reflection.</p>

<h3>Worked example 2</h3>
<p>
A sound wave strikes a distant cliff and returns
to the listener. What has happened to the sound?
</p>
<p>
The sound wave has been reflected by the cliff.
The returning sound may be heard as an echo.
</p>

<h3>Remember</h3>
<p>
Reflection means a wave returns from a boundary.
It does not mean the wave has necessarily been
completely absorbed or destroyed.
</p>', 1),
  (580, 580, '<h2>Direction of a Reflected Wave</h2>

<h3>Core idea</h3>
<p>
For reflection at a plane boundary, angles are
measured from the <b>normal</b>, an imaginary line
perpendicular to the boundary at the point of
incidence.
</p>

<p>
The law of reflection states that the angle of
incidence equals the angle of reflection.
</p>

<p><b>i = r</b></p>

<p>
The angle of incidence is measured between the
incident wave''s direction and the normal. The angle
of reflection is measured between the reflected
wave''s direction and the normal.
</p>

<h3>Worked example 1</h3>
<p>
A wave strikes a reflecting boundary at an angle
of incidence of 35° to the normal. Find the angle
of reflection.
</p>
<p>
<b>Step 1:</b> Apply the law of reflection: i = r.
</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> r = 35°.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> 35°.</p>

<h3>Worked example 2</h3>
<p>
A ray makes an angle of 20° with the reflecting
surface. Find its angle of incidence.
</p>
<p>
The normal is perpendicular to the surface.
Therefore, the angle with the normal is
90° − 20° = 70°.
</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> Angle of incidence = 70°.</p>

<h3>Remember</h3>
<p>
Measure the angle from the normal, not from the
surface. This law applies to specular reflection
at a plane boundary.
</p>', 1),
  (581, 581, '<h2>Refraction of Waves</h2>

<h3>Core idea</h3>
<p>
Refraction is the change in direction of a wave
when it enters a region where its speed changes
and it meets the boundary at an angle.
</p>

<p>
When a water wave enters shallow water at an angle,
its speed changes. One part of the wavefront changes
speed before another part, causing the wavefront
to turn.
</p>

<p>
A wave entering a new region straight on may change
speed without changing direction.
</p>

<h3>Worked example 1</h3>
<p>
Water waves enter a shallow region at an angle and
change direction. Identify the phenomenon.
</p>
<p>
<b>Reasoning:</b> Their speed changes and their
direction changes at the boundary.
</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> Refraction.</p>

<h3>Worked example 2</h3>
<p>
A wave enters a new region perpendicular to the
boundary. Its speed changes, but it continues in
the same direction. Has its speed changed?
</p>
<p>
Yes. A change in speed does not always produce
a change in direction. The wave can still undergo
a change of propagation speed without bending.
</p>

<h3>Remember</h3>
<p>
Refraction does not mean reflection. During
refraction, a wave enters a different region.
For a periodic wave crossing a stationary boundary,
its frequency remains unchanged while its speed
and wavelength may change.
</p>', 1),
  (582, 582, '<h2>Diffraction of Waves</h2>

<h3>Core idea</h3>
<p>
Diffraction is the spreading of waves as they pass
through an opening or around an obstacle.
</p>

<p>
Diffraction can explain why sound may be heard
around a doorway even when the source is not
directly visible.
</p>

<h3>Worked example 1</h3>
<p>
Sound from a classroom is heard in a corridor
around an open doorway. Which wave behaviour helps
explain this?
</p>
<p>
<b>Reasoning:</b> Sound spreads through the opening
and into the region beyond it.
</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> Diffraction.</p>

<h3>Worked example 2</h3>
<p>
Water waves pass through a narrow opening in a
barrier and spread into the region beyond it.
Identify the phenomenon.
</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> Diffraction.</p>

<h3>Remember</h3>
<p>
Diffraction does not require a wave to bounce back
from a boundary. It describes the spreading of a
wave through an opening or around an obstacle.
</p>', 1),
  (583, 583, '<h2>Diffraction and Gap Size</h2>

<h3>Core idea</h3>
<p>
Diffraction is most noticeable when the width of an
opening is comparable to the wavelength.
</p>

<p>
When the gap is much wider than the wavelength,
spreading is generally less noticeable. When the
gap is similar to the wavelength, spreading is
more pronounced.
</p>

<h3>Worked example 1</h3>
<p>
Water waves have a wavelength of 5 cm and pass
through a gap 5 cm wide. Will diffraction be
noticeable?
</p>
<p>
The gap width is comparable to the wavelength,
so significant spreading is expected.
</p>

<h3>Worked example 2</h3>
<p>
Waves with a wavelength of 2 cm pass through a
gap 50 cm wide. Compare the gap width with the
wavelength.
</p>
<p>
The gap is much wider than the wavelength, so
spreading is generally less pronounced.
</p>

<h3>Remember</h3>
<p>
Compare the gap width with the wavelength. Do not
judge diffraction from gap width alone when the
wavelength is unknown.
</p>', 1),
  (584, 584, '<h2>Superposition of Waves</h2>

<h3>Core idea</h3>
<p>
When two or more waves overlap, the resultant
displacement at a point is the algebraic sum of
the individual displacements at that point.
This is the principle of superposition.
</p>

<p>
For displacements in the same direction, add their
values. For displacements in opposite directions,
use their signs when adding.
</p>

<h3>Worked example 1</h3>
<p>
Two pulses produce upward displacements of 2 cm
and 3 cm at the same point at the same time.
Find the resultant displacement.
</p>
<p>
<b>Step 1:</b> Both displacements are upward.
</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Resultant = 2 + 3 = 5 cm upward.</p>

<h3>Worked example 2</h3>
<p>
One pulse produces an upward displacement of 5 cm
and another produces a downward displacement of
2 cm at the same point. Find the resultant.
</p>
<p>
Take upward as positive and downward as negative.
Resultant = +5 + (−2) = +3 cm.
</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> 3 cm upward.</p>

<h3>Remember</h3>
<p>
Add signed displacements, not just their magnitudes.
The result depends on the direction of each
displacement at the point of overlap.
</p>', 1),
  (585, 585, '<h2>Constructive Interference</h2>

<h3>Core idea</h3>
<p>
Constructive interference occurs when overlapping
waves reinforce one another, producing a resultant
displacement greater than either individual
displacement when both are in the same direction.
</p>

<p>
For two waves of equal frequency arriving in phase,
their crests align with crests and their troughs
align with troughs. Their amplitudes add at points
of complete constructive interference.
</p>

<h3>Worked example 1</h3>
<p>
Two upward pulses each have a displacement of 3 cm
at the same point. Find the resultant displacement.
</p>
<p>
Resultant = 3 + 3 = 6 cm upward.
</p>

<h3>Worked example 2</h3>
<p>
Two equal-amplitude waves arrive in phase at a
point. What happens to the resultant amplitude?
</p>
<p>
Their displacements reinforce each other. For
equal amplitudes A, the resultant amplitude at
complete constructive interference is 2A.
</p>

<h3>Remember</h3>
<p>
Constructive interference is reinforcement caused
by overlap. It does not create energy from nothing;
energy is redistributed through the combined wave
pattern.
</p>', 1),
  (586, 586, '<h2>Destructive Interference</h2>

<h3>Core idea</h3>
<p>
Destructive interference occurs when overlapping
waves oppose one another, reducing the resultant
displacement compared with the displacement of
either wave alone.
</p>

<p>
For two equal-amplitude waves in opposite phase,
a crest of one aligns with a trough of the other.
At complete destructive interference, their
displacements cancel at that point.
</p>

<h3>Worked example 1</h3>
<p>
One pulse produces a displacement of 4 cm upward.
Another produces a displacement of 4 cm downward
at the same point. Find the resultant.
</p>
<p>
Resultant = +4 + (−4) = 0 cm.
</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> Zero resultant displacement.</p>

<h3>Worked example 2</h3>
<p>
An upward pulse of 6 cm overlaps a downward pulse
of 2 cm. Find the resultant displacement.
</p>
<p>
Resultant = +6 + (−2) = +4 cm.
</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> 4 cm upward.</p>

<h3>Remember</h3>
<p>
Destructive interference does not always mean
complete cancellation. Complete cancellation
requires equal and opposite displacements at the
point and instant considered.
</p>', 1),
  (587, 587, '<h2>Production of Sound</h2>
<p>Sound is produced when an object vibrates. A vibration is a repeated movement to and fro about a position of rest.</p>

<h3>How sound is produced</h3>
<p>When a drum is struck, its stretched skin vibrates. These vibrations disturb the surrounding air, producing a sound wave.</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> Why does a guitar string produce sound when plucked?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Plucking the string makes it vibrate.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> The vibrating string disturbs the surrounding air.</p>
<p><b>Conclusion:</b> The disturbance travels through the air as a sound wave.</p>', 1),
  (588, 588, '<h2>Sound as a Mechanical Wave</h2>
<p>Sound is a <b>mechanical wave</b> because it requires a material medium, such as air, water or a solid, to travel.</p>

<p>Sound transfers energy from a vibrating source to a listener. The particles of the medium vibrate about their equilibrium positions; they do not travel all the way from the source to the listener.</p>

<h3>Sound cannot travel through a vacuum</h3>
<p>A vacuum has no particles to transmit the sound disturbance. Therefore, sound cannot travel through a vacuum.</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> An alarm rings inside a sealed bell jar. The air is gradually removed. Why does the sound become fainter?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Sound needs particles to transmit its disturbance.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Removing air reduces the number of particles available to transmit sound.</p>
<p><b>Conclusion:</b> Less sound reaches the listener. In a perfect vacuum, sound cannot travel.</p>', 1),
  (589, 589, '<h2>Compressions in Sound Waves</h2>
<p>Sound in air normally travels as a longitudinal wave. Air particles vibrate parallel to the direction in which the sound wave travels.</p>

<p>A <b>compression</b> is a region where particles are closer together than normal. It has higher pressure and density than the surrounding undisturbed air.</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> What happens to the air particles in a compression?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> The sound source pushes nearby air particles.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Particles become closer together in that region.</p>
<p><b>Conclusion:</b> A compression is a high-pressure region.</p>', 1),
  (590, 590, '<h2>Rarefactions in Sound Waves</h2>
<p>A <b>rarefaction</b> is a region in a longitudinal sound wave where particles are farther apart than normal.</p>

<p>Rarefactions have lower pressure and density than the undisturbed medium. They alternate with compressions as the sound wave travels.</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> A region of air has particles farther apart than normal as a sound wave passes. Identify the region.</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> The particles are spread farther apart.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Such a region has lower pressure and density.</p>
<p><b>Conclusion:</b> It is a rarefaction.</p>', 1),
  (591, 591, '<h2>Transmission of Sound Through a Medium</h2>
<p>When a vibrating source produces sound, it causes nearby particles in a medium to vibrate. These particles exert forces on neighbouring particles, passing the disturbance along.</p>

<p>The particles oscillate about their equilibrium positions while the sound wave transfers energy through the medium.</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> How does sound from a loudspeaker reach a listener through air?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> The loudspeaker cone vibrates.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> It creates changing pressure regions in nearby air.</p>
<p><b>Step 3:</b> The disturbance is passed from particle to particle.</p>
<p><b>Conclusion:</b> Sound energy travels through the air to the listener.</p>', 1),
  (592, 592, '<h2>Speed of Sound</h2>
<p>The speed of sound is the distance travelled by a sound wave per unit time.</p>

<p><b>Formula:</b></p>
<p>Speed = Distance ÷ Time</p>
<p>In symbols: <b>v = d/t</b></p>
<p>Speed is measured in metres per second (m/s).</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> Sound travels 680 m in 2 s. Calculate its speed.</p>
<p><b>Step 1: Write the formula.</b></p>
<p>v = d/t</p>
<p><b>Step 2: Substitute the values.</b></p>
<p>v = 680/2</p>
<p><b>Step 3: Calculate.</b></p>
<p>v = 340 m/s</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> 340 m/s.</p>', 1),
  (593, 593, '<h2>Pitch of Sound</h2>
<p><b>Pitch</b> describes how high or low a sound seems to a listener. Pitch depends mainly on the frequency of the sound wave.</p>

<ul>
<li>Higher frequency produces a higher-pitched sound.</li>
<li>Lower frequency produces a lower-pitched sound.</li>
</ul>

<p>Frequency is the number of complete vibrations or wave cycles per second. Its unit is the hertz (Hz).</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> Two tuning forks produce sounds of 256 Hz and 512 Hz. Which produces the higher pitch?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Compare their frequencies.</p>
<p>512 Hz is greater than 256 Hz.</p>
<p><b>Conclusion:</b> The 512 Hz tuning fork produces the higher-pitched sound.</p>', 1),
  (594, 594, '<h2>Loudness of Sound</h2>
<p>Loudness describes how loud or quiet a sound seems to a listener. For the same general conditions, a sound wave with greater amplitude is usually heard as louder.</p>

<p><b>Amplitude</b> is the maximum displacement of a vibrating particle from its equilibrium position.</p>

<ul>
<li>Greater amplitude generally means a louder sound.</li>
<li>Smaller amplitude generally means a quieter sound.</li>
</ul>

<p>Do not confuse loudness with pitch: pitch is related to frequency, while loudness is related to amplitude and also depends on factors such as distance and the listener''s hearing.</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> Two sounds have the same frequency, but one has a greater amplitude. Which is generally heard as louder?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Their frequencies are equal, so their pitches are the same.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> One sound has a greater amplitude.</p>
<p><b>Conclusion:</b> The sound with greater amplitude is generally heard as louder.</p>', 1),
  (595, 595, '<h2>Echoes and Reflection of Sound</h2>
<p>An <b>echo</b> is a sound heard again after the original sound has reflected from a surface and returned to the listener.</p>

<p>Large, hard surfaces such as cliffs and walls can reflect sound. A distinct echo is heard when the reflected sound reaches the listener sufficiently later than the original sound.</p>

<h3>Calculating distance using an echo</h3>
<p>The sound travels from the listener to the reflecting surface and then back again. Therefore, the total distance travelled by sound is twice the distance to the surface.</p>

<p><b>Distance to surface:</b></p>
<p>d = vt/2</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> A person hears an echo 2 seconds after shouting. Take the speed of sound as 340 m/s. How far away is the reflecting surface?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Total distance travelled by sound = vt.</p>
<p>= 340 × 2 = 680 m</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> The sound travels to the surface and back.</p>
<p>Distance to surface = 680/2 = 340 m</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> 340 m.</p>', 1),
  (596, 596, '<h2>Nature of Light</h2>
<p>Light is a form of energy that enables us to see objects. In the wave model, light is a <b>transverse electromagnetic wave</b>.</p>

<p>Unlike sound, light does not require particles of a material medium to travel. It can travel through a vacuum.</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> Why can sunlight reach Earth through space?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Space is nearly a vacuum.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Light is an electromagnetic wave and does not require a material medium.</p>
<p><b>Conclusion:</b> Sunlight can travel through space and reach Earth.</p>', 1),
  (597, 597, '<h2>Transverse Nature of Light</h2>
<p>Light is a transverse wave. In a transverse wave, the oscillations are perpendicular to the direction in which the wave travels.</p>

<p>For electromagnetic waves, the electric and magnetic fields oscillate perpendicular to the direction of propagation. At secondary-school level, remember the defining relationship: <b>oscillation is at right angles to wave travel</b>.</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> Light travels horizontally. In a transverse wave, how are its oscillations oriented relative to its direction of travel?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify the direction of propagation: horizontal.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Transverse oscillations are perpendicular to propagation.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> At right angles to the direction of travel.</p>', 1),
  (598, 598, '<h2>Propagation of Light Through a Vacuum</h2>
<p>A vacuum is a region containing no material particles. Light can travel through a vacuum because it is an electromagnetic wave rather than a mechanical wave.</p>

<p>The speed of light in a vacuum is approximately:</p>
<p><b>c = 3.0 × 10<sup>8</sup> m/s</b></p>

<p>Light travels more slowly in transparent materials such as glass and water than it does in a vacuum.</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> Light from a distant star crosses space before reaching Earth. Does it need air to make this journey?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Space is nearly a vacuum.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Light does not need air or another material medium.</p>
<p><b>Conclusion:</b> No, light can cross space without air.</p>', 1),
  (599, 599, '<h2>Wavelength of Light</h2>
<p>The <b>wavelength</b> of light is the distance between successive points that are in the same phase of the wave. For example, it is the distance from one crest to the next crest.</p>

<p>Wavelength is represented by the Greek letter lambda (λ). Its SI unit is the metre (m), although very small wavelengths are often expressed in nanometres (nm).</p>

<p><b>1 nm = 1 × 10<sup>-9</sup> m</b></p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> A light wave has a wavelength of 600 nm. Express this wavelength in metres.</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Write the conversion.</p>
<p>1 nm = 10<sup>-9</sup> m</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Multiply by 600.</p>
<p>600 nm = 600 × 10<sup>-9</sup> m</p>
<p><b>Step 3:</b> Write in standard form.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> 6.0 × 10<sup>-7</sup> m.</p>', 1),
  (600, 600, '<h2>Frequency of Light</h2>
<p>The <b>frequency</b> of light is the number of complete wave cycles passing a fixed point each second.</p>

<p>Frequency is represented by f and measured in hertz (Hz).</p>

<p>Light with a higher frequency has a higher frequency value in hertz. In the visible spectrum, violet light has a higher frequency than red light.</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> A light wave completes 6 × 10<sup>14</sup> cycles each second. What is its frequency?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Frequency is the number of cycles per second.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> The wave completes 6 × 10<sup>14</sup> cycles per second.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> 6 × 10<sup>14</sup> Hz.</p>', 1),
  (601, 601, '<h2>Light Wave Equation</h2>
<p>Wave speed, frequency and wavelength are related by:</p>

<p><b>v = fλ</b></p>

<ul>
<li>v = wave speed in metres per second (m/s)</li>
<li>f = frequency in hertz (Hz)</li>
<li>λ = wavelength in metres (m)</li>
</ul>

<p>For light in a vacuum, use <b>c = fλ</b>, where c is approximately 3.0 × 10<sup>8</sup> m/s.</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> Light travels in a vacuum at 3.0 × 10<sup>8</sup> m/s and has a frequency of 6.0 × 10<sup>14</sup> Hz. Find its wavelength.</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Start with c = fλ.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Rearrange to make wavelength the subject.</p>
<p>λ = c/f</p>
<p><b>Step 3:</b> Substitute the values.</p>
<p>λ = (3.0 × 10<sup>8</sup>)/(6.0 × 10<sup>14</sup>)</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> λ = 5.0 × 10<sup>-7</sup> m.</p>', 1),
  (602, 602, '<h2>Visible Light Spectrum</h2>
<p>Visible light is the part of the electromagnetic spectrum that the human eye can detect.</p>

<p>The visible colours are commonly listed in this order:</p>
<p><b>Red, Orange, Yellow, Green, Blue, Indigo, Violet</b></p>

<p>From red towards violet:</p>
<ul>
<li>Wavelength decreases.</li>
<li>Frequency increases.</li>
</ul>

<p>Red light has a longer wavelength and lower frequency than violet light.</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> Which has a longer wavelength, red light or violet light?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Recall the order of the visible spectrum.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Wavelength decreases from red towards violet.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> Red light has the longer wavelength.</p>', 1),
  (603, 603, '<h2>Reflection of Light</h2>
<p><b>Reflection</b> is the bouncing back of light into the original medium after it strikes a surface.</p>

<p>When light strikes a mirror, some of the incident light is reflected. The incoming ray is called the <b>incident ray</b>, and the ray that leaves the surface is called the <b>reflected ray</b>.</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> A ray of light strikes a plane mirror and bounces back into the air. What process has occurred?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Light travels towards the mirror.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> It returns to the original medium after striking the surface.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> Reflection of light.</p>', 1),
  (604, 604, '<h2>The Normal and Angles of Reflection</h2>
<p>The <b>normal</b> is an imaginary line drawn perpendicular to a reflecting surface at the point where the incident ray strikes it.</p>

<p>The angle of incidence (i) is the angle between the incident ray and the normal. The angle of reflection (r) is the angle between the reflected ray and the normal.</p>

<p><b>Important:</b> Both angles are measured from the normal, not from the mirror surface.</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> A ray makes an angle of 30° with the mirror surface. Find its angle of incidence.</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> The normal is perpendicular to the mirror, so it makes an angle of 90° with the surface.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> The ray''s angle with the normal is 90° − 30°.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> 60°.</p>', 1),
  (605, 605, '<h2>Laws of Reflection</h2>
<p>Reflection follows two laws:</p>

<ol>
<li>The angle of incidence equals the angle of reflection: <b>i = r</b>.</li>
<li>The incident ray, the reflected ray and the normal at the point of incidence lie in the same plane.</li>
</ol>

<p>Remember that both angles are measured from the normal.</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> A ray strikes a plane mirror at an angle of incidence of 35°. Find the angle of reflection.</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Apply the first law of reflection.</p>
<p>i = r</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Substitute i = 35°.</p>
<p>r = 35°</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> 35°.</p>', 1),
  (606, 606, '<h2>Angle Between Incident and Reflected Rays</h2>
<p>For reflection at a plane mirror, the angle of incidence equals the angle of reflection.</p>

<p>If the angle of incidence is i, the angle between the incident and reflected rays on the side of the incoming light is:</p>

<p><b>Angle between rays = 2i</b></p>

<p>This relationship applies when i is measured from the normal.</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> A ray strikes a plane mirror at an angle of incidence of 30°. Find the angle between the incident and reflected rays.</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Angle of reflection = 30°.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Angle between the rays = 30° + 30°.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> 60°.</p>', 1),
  (607, 607, '<h2>Image Formation in a Plane Mirror</h2>
<p>A plane mirror forms an image when light rays from an object reflect from the mirror and appear to come from behind it.</p>

<p>The reflected rays do not actually meet behind the mirror. Their backward extensions appear to meet there, forming a <b>virtual image</b>.</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> Why can a person see an image behind a plane mirror even though light does not actually travel from the image to the eye?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Light from the person reflects from the mirror into the eye.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> The brain traces the reflected rays backwards in straight lines.</p>
<p><b>Conclusion:</b> The rays appear to originate behind the mirror, producing a virtual image.</p>', 1),
  (608, 608, '<h2>Properties of a Plane-Mirror Image</h2>
<p>The image formed by a plane mirror has these properties:</p>

<ul>
<li><b>Virtual:</b> it appears behind the mirror.</li>
<li><b>Upright:</b> it has the same vertical orientation as the object.</li>
<li><b>Same size:</b> the image height equals the object height.</li>
<li><b>Same distance:</b> the image is as far behind the mirror as the object is in front.</li>
<li><b>Laterally inverted:</b> left and right appear reversed.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> An object is 2 m in front of a plane mirror. How far behind the mirror is its image?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> A plane-mirror image is as far behind the mirror as the object is in front.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Object distance = 2 m.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> Image distance = 2 m behind the mirror.</p>', 1),
  (609, 609, '<h2>Lateral Inversion</h2>
<p><b>Lateral inversion</b> is the apparent reversal of left and right in the image formed by a plane mirror.</p>

<p>For example, a person''s right hand appears on the image''s left-hand side when the person faces a mirror. The image is not upside down; it remains upright.</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> A person raises their right hand while facing a plane mirror. Which hand appears raised on the corresponding side of the image?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> A plane mirror produces lateral inversion.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> The person''s right side appears reversed in the image.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> The image appears to raise its left hand.</p>', 1),
  (610, 610, '<h2>Refraction of Light</h2>
<p><b>Refraction</b> is the change in direction of light as it passes obliquely from one transparent medium into another because its speed changes.</p>

<p>For example, light changes speed when it passes from air into glass. If it enters at an angle to the normal, its direction also changes.</p>

<p><b>Important:</b> Refraction is caused by a change in wave speed. Light does not always bend: when it enters along the normal, its speed changes but its direction remains unchanged.</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> A ray of light passes obliquely from air into glass and changes direction. What is the phenomenon called?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> The ray crosses from one medium into another.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Its speed changes and, because it enters obliquely, its direction changes.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> Refraction.</p>', 1),
  (611, 611, '<h2>Refraction Towards the Normal</h2>
<p>When light passes obliquely from a less optically dense medium, such as air, into a more optically dense medium, such as glass, it slows down and bends <b>towards the normal</b>.</p>

<p>The angle of refraction is smaller than the angle of incidence:</p>
<p><b>r < i</b></p>

<p>Optical density describes how a medium affects light''s speed. It does not simply mean how physically dense the material is.</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> A ray enters glass obliquely from air. Its angle of incidence is 50°. Which way does it bend?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Light enters glass from air.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> It slows down in the glass.</p>
<p><b>Conclusion:</b> It bends towards the normal, so the refracted angle is less than 50°.</p>', 1),
  (612, 612, '<h2>Refraction Away from the Normal</h2>
<p>When light passes obliquely from a more optically dense medium, such as glass, into a less optically dense medium, such as air, it speeds up and bends <b>away from the normal</b>.</p>

<p>The angle of refraction is greater than the angle of incidence:</p>
<p><b>r > i</b></p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> A ray travels obliquely from glass into air with an angle of incidence of 30°. Explain how it bends.</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Light moves from glass into air.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Its speed increases.</p>
<p><b>Conclusion:</b> It bends away from the normal, so the refracted angle is greater than 30° if the ray emerges into air.</p>', 1),
  (613, 613, '<h2>Refraction at Normal Incidence</h2>
<p><b>Normal incidence</b> occurs when light strikes a boundary along the normal, so the angle of incidence is 0°.</p>

<p>At normal incidence, light changes speed when it enters a different medium, but it does not change direction. Its angle in the new medium remains 0° relative to the normal.</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> A light ray enters a glass block perpendicularly to its surface. What is its angle of refraction?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> The ray enters along the normal.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Its angle of incidence is 0°.</p>
<p><b>Step 3:</b> It continues along the normal.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> 0°.</p>', 1),
  (614, 614, '<h2>Refractive Index</h2>
<p>The absolute refractive index of a medium compares the speed of light in a vacuum with its speed in that medium.</p>

<p><b>Formula:</b></p>
<p><b>n = c/v</b></p>

<ul>
<li>n = absolute refractive index (no unit)</li>
<li>c = speed of light in a vacuum</li>
<li>v = speed of light in the medium</li>
</ul>

<p>A higher refractive index means that light travels more slowly in that medium.</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> Light travels through a material at 2.0 × 10<sup>8</sup> m/s. Calculate its absolute refractive index. Take c = 3.0 × 10<sup>8</sup> m/s.</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Use n = c/v.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Substitute the values.</p>
<p>n = (3.0 × 10<sup>8</sup>)/(2.0 × 10<sup>8</sup>)</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> n = 1.5.</p>', 1),
  (615, 615, '<h2>Snell''s Law</h2>
<p>Snell''s law relates the angles of incidence and refraction to the refractive indices of two media.</p>

<p><b>n₁ sin i = n₂ sin r</b></p>

<ul>
<li>n₁ = refractive index of the first medium</li>
<li>n₂ = refractive index of the second medium</li>
<li>i = angle of incidence</li>
<li>r = angle of refraction</li>
</ul>

<p>Angles are measured from the normal. When light travels from air into a material, the refractive index of air is often approximated as 1.0 for school calculations.</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> Light travels from air into glass. The angle of incidence is 30° and the angle of refraction is 20°. Calculate the refractive index of the glass, taking the refractive index of air as 1.0.</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Use n₁ sin i = n₂ sin r.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Rearrange: n₂ = n₁ sin i / sin r.</p>
<p><b>Step 3:</b> Substitute: n₂ = (1.0 × sin 30°) / sin 20°.</p>
<p>n₂ ≈ 0.5 / 0.342</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> n₂ ≈ 1.46.</p>', 1),
  (616, 616, '<h2>Apparent Depth</h2>
<p>When an object under water is viewed from air, light refracts as it leaves the water. The object can appear closer to the surface than it really is.</p>

<p>For viewing nearly perpendicular to a flat water surface, the apparent depth is less than the real depth.</p>

<p>For an object in water viewed from air, the approximate relationship is:</p>
<p><b>Refractive index ≈ real depth / apparent depth</b></p>

<p>This approximation assumes near-normal viewing and a flat boundary.</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> A pool has a real depth of 2.0 m and appears to be 1.5 m deep when viewed from above. Estimate the refractive index of the water.</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Use n ≈ real depth / apparent depth.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> n ≈ 2.0 / 1.5.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> n ≈ 1.33.</p>', 1),
  (617, 617, '<h2>Effect of Wavelength on Diffraction</h2>
<p><b>Diffraction</b> is the spreading of waves as they pass through an opening or around an obstacle.</p>

<p>For the same gap width, waves with longer wavelengths generally diffract more noticeably than waves with shorter wavelengths.</p>

<p>Diffraction is especially noticeable when the wavelength is comparable to the gap width.</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> Two sets of waves pass through the same opening. One has a wavelength of 2 cm and the other 8 cm. Which is expected to spread out more?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> The opening is the same for both waves.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Compare their wavelengths.</p>
<p>8 cm is the longer wavelength.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> The waves with wavelength 8 cm generally diffract more noticeably.</p>', 1),
  (618, 618, '<h2>Effect of Gap Width on Diffraction</h2>
<p>The width of an opening affects how much a wave spreads after passing through it.</p>

<ul>
<li>A narrow gap, comparable to the wavelength, produces noticeable spreading.</li>
<li>A gap much wider than the wavelength produces less noticeable spreading.</li>
</ul>

<p>When comparing two openings, keep the wavelength the same so that the effect of gap width can be identified.</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> Water waves of the same wavelength pass through two gaps. One gap is 5 cm wide and the other is 50 cm wide. Which gap produces more noticeable diffraction?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> The wavelength is unchanged.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Compare the gap widths with that wavelength.</p>
<p><b>Conclusion:</b> The narrower gap generally produces more noticeable spreading, particularly when its width is comparable to the wavelength.</p>', 1),
  (619, 619, '<h2>Diffraction of Sound Around Obstacles</h2>
<p>Sound can spread around obstacles and through doorways. This is why a person can often hear someone speaking from another room without seeing the speaker directly.</p>

<p>Diffraction is more noticeable when the wavelength is not very small compared with the size of the opening or obstacle.</p>

<p>Sound waves often have wavelengths that allow noticeable spreading around everyday obstacles. Light has much shorter wavelengths, so its diffraction around ordinary objects is usually less noticeable.</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> A student hears a conversation from around a doorway without seeing the speakers. Which wave behaviour helps explain this?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> The sound reaches the student despite the doorway blocking a direct line of sight.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Sound waves spread through openings and around obstacles.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> Diffraction of sound.</p>', 1),
  (620, 620, '<h2>Wavefronts After Diffraction</h2>
<p>A <b>wavefront</b> is a line joining points on a wave that are in the same phase.</p>

<p>When straight wavefronts pass through a narrow opening, the wavefronts beyond the opening spread out. For a small opening compared with the wavelength, the emerging wavefronts are approximately semicircular in a two-dimensional water-wave diagram.</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> Straight water-wave fronts approach a narrow gap. What shape do the wavefronts approximately take after passing through the gap?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> The waves encounter a narrow opening.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Diffraction causes the waves to spread from the opening.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> Approximately semicircular wavefronts in a two-dimensional diagram.</p>', 1),
  (621, 621, '<h2>Coherent Sources</h2>

  <h3>1. Meaning of coherent sources</h3>
  <p>Two wave sources are <b>coherent</b> when they produce waves of the same frequency and maintain a constant phase difference.</p>

  <h3>2. Why coherence matters</h3>
  <p>Coherent sources produce a stable interference pattern. The positions of reinforcement and cancellation remain fixed because the phase relationship between the waves does not continually change.</p>

  <h3>3. Same frequency is not enough</h3>
  <p>Two sources may have the same frequency but fail to maintain a constant phase difference. For a stable interference pattern, both conditions are required:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Same frequency.</li>
    <li>Constant phase difference.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>Two sources produce waves at 20 Hz and maintain a constant phase difference of 90°. Are they coherent?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Their frequencies are equal: both are 20 Hz.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Their phase difference is constant.</p>
  <p><b>Conclusion:</b> Yes, they are coherent.</p>', 1),
  (622, 622, '<h2>Path Difference</h2>

  <h3>1. Meaning of path difference</h3>
  <p><b>Path difference</b> is the difference between the distances travelled by two waves from their sources to the same point.</p>

  <p>If the two distances are d₁ and d₂, the magnitude of the path difference is:</p>
  <p><b>Path difference = |d₁ − d₂|</b></p>

  <h3>2. Why path difference matters</h3>
  <p>The path difference helps determine whether two coherent waves arrive in phase or out of phase.</p>

  <ul>
    <li>A path difference of one wavelength corresponds to waves arriving in phase, if they started in phase.</li>
    <li>A path difference of half a wavelength corresponds to waves arriving in opposite phase, if they started in phase.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>Wave A travels 8 m to a point. Wave B travels 11 m to the same point.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify the distances: d₁ = 8 m and d₂ = 11 m.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Find their difference: |11 − 8| = 3 m.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> The path difference is 3 m.</p>', 1),
  (623, 623, '<h2>Constructive Interference Condition</h2>

  <h3>1. When does reinforcement occur?</h3>
  <p>Constructive interference occurs when two waves meet in phase: crests meet crests and troughs meet troughs. Their displacements reinforce each other.</p>

  <h3>2. Path-difference condition</h3>
  <p>For two coherent sources that emit waves in phase, constructive interference occurs when:</p>
  <p><b>Path difference = nλ</b></p>

  <p>Here:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><b>n</b> is a whole number: 0, 1, 2, 3, …</li>
    <li><b>λ</b> is the wavelength.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>3. Worked example</h3>
  <p>Two in-phase sources produce waves of wavelength 0.5 m. A point has a path difference of 1.0 m. Determine whether constructive interference occurs.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Calculate the number of wavelengths: 1.0 ÷ 0.5 = 2.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> The path difference is 2λ.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Constructive interference occurs because 2 is a whole number.</p>

  <p><b>Important:</b> This path-difference rule assumes the two sources emit waves in phase.</p>', 1),
  (624, 624, '<h2>Destructive Interference Condition</h2>

  <h3>1. When does cancellation occur?</h3>
  <p>Destructive interference occurs when two waves meet in opposite phase. A crest from one wave meets a trough from the other, so their displacements oppose each other.</p>

  <h3>2. Path-difference condition</h3>
  <p>For two coherent sources that emit waves in phase, destructive interference occurs when:</p>
  <p><b>Path difference = (n + ½)λ</b></p>

  <p>Here n is a whole number: 0, 1, 2, 3, …</p>

  <p>The possible path differences include:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>½λ</li>
    <li>1½λ</li>
    <li>2½λ</li>
    <li>3½λ</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>3. Worked example</h3>
  <p>The wavelength is 0.6 m and the path difference is 0.3 m.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Calculate the path difference in wavelengths: 0.3 ÷ 0.6 = 0.5.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> The path difference is ½λ.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Destructive interference occurs.</p>

  <p><b>Important:</b> Complete cancellation occurs only when the opposing displacements have equal magnitudes.</p>', 1),
  (625, 625, '<h2>Formation of Stationary Waves</h2>

  <h3>1. How stationary waves form</h3>
  <p>A <b>stationary wave</b>, also called a standing wave, forms when two waves of the same frequency and wavelength travel in opposite directions and superpose under suitable conditions.</p>

  <p>One common example is a wave travelling along a stretched string and reflecting from a fixed end. The incoming and reflected waves travel in opposite directions.</p>

  <h3>2. Why it is called stationary</h3>
  <p>The resulting pattern has fixed positions where the displacement is always zero and fixed positions where the displacement is greatest. The pattern does not travel along the medium as a whole.</p>

  <h3>3. Stationary versus progressive waves</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Progressive wave:</b> the wave pattern travels through the medium and transfers energy along it.</li>
    <li><b>Stationary wave:</b> the pattern has fixed nodes and antinodes; there is no net energy transfer along the medium by the ideal stationary pattern.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>A wave on a string reflects from a fixed end and overlaps with the incoming wave. The two waves have the same frequency and travel in opposite directions.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify the two waves: incoming and reflected.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> They have the same frequency and travel in opposite directions.</p>
  <p><b>Conclusion:</b> Their superposition can produce a stationary-wave pattern.</p>', 1),
  (626, 626, '<h2>Nodes in Stationary Waves</h2>

  <h3>1. Meaning of a node</h3>
  <p>A <b>node</b> is a point on a stationary wave where the resultant displacement is always zero.</p>

  <p>At a node, the two overlapping waves have equal and opposite displacements at corresponding instants, so they cancel.</p>

  <h3>2. Distance between adjacent nodes</h3>
  <p>For a stationary wave formed from waves of wavelength λ:</p>
  <p><b>Distance between adjacent nodes = λ/2</b></p>

  <h3>3. Finding the wavelength</h3>
  <p>If the distance between adjacent nodes is d, then:</p>
  <p><b>λ = 2d</b></p>

  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>Adjacent nodes are separated by 0.15 m.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Use d = λ/2.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Rearrange: λ = 2d.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> λ = 2 × 0.15 = 0.30 m.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> The wavelength is 0.30 m.</p>', 1),
  (627, 627, '
  <h2>Antinodes in Stationary Waves</h2>

  <h3>Learning objective</h3>
  <p>
    By the end of this lesson, you should be able to identify an
    antinode and calculate distances involving nodes and antinodes.
  </p>

  <h3>1. What is an antinode?</h3>

  <p>
    An <b>antinode</b> is a point on a stationary wave where the
    oscillation amplitude is greatest.
  </p>

  <p>
    A <b>node</b> is a point where the resultant displacement remains
    zero. An antinode is a point where the particles oscillate with
    maximum amplitude.
  </p>

  <p>
    Remember:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><b>Node:</b> zero oscillation amplitude.</li>
    <li><b>Antinode:</b> maximum oscillation amplitude.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>2. Distance relationships</h3>

  <p>
    The positions of nodes and antinodes follow fixed wavelength
    relationships.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Node to nearest antinode:</b>
  </p>

  <p style="text-align:center;">
    <b>d = λ/4</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Distance between adjacent nodes:</b>
  </p>

  <p style="text-align:center;">
    <b>d = λ/2</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Distance between adjacent antinodes:</b>
  </p>

  <p style="text-align:center;">
    <b>d = λ/2</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    Here, <b>d</b> is the distance and <b>λ</b> (lambda) is the
    wavelength.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Worked Example 1: Distance from a node to an antinode</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Question:</b> A stationary wave has a wavelength of 0.8 m.
    Calculate the distance from a node to its nearest antinode.
  </p>

  <p><b>Step 1: Identify the relationship.</b></p>

  <p>
    A node and its nearest antinode are separated by one-quarter
    of a wavelength.
  </p>

  <p><b>d = λ/4</b></p>

  <p><b>Step 2: Substitute the wavelength.</b></p>

  <p>
    d = 0.8/4
  </p>

  <p><b>Step 3: Calculate.</b></p>

  <p>
    d = 0.2 m
  </p>

  <p><b>Answer: 0.2 m</b></p>

  <p>
    <b>Check:</b> One-quarter of 0.8 m is 0.2 m.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Worked Example 2: Finding the wavelength</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Question:</b> Two adjacent antinodes are separated by 0.35 m.
    Calculate the wavelength of the stationary wave.
  </p>

  <p><b>Step 1: Identify the relationship.</b></p>

  <p>
    Adjacent antinodes are separated by half a wavelength.
  </p>

  <p>
    d = λ/2
  </p>

  <p><b>Step 2: Rearrange the formula.</b></p>

  <p>
    Multiply both sides by 2:
  </p>

  <p>
    λ = 2d
  </p>

  <p><b>Step 3: Substitute the distance.</b></p>

  <p>
    λ = 2 × 0.35
  </p>

  <p>
    λ = 0.70 m
  </p>

  <p><b>Answer: 0.70 m</b></p>

  <p>
    <b>Check:</b> Half of 0.70 m is 0.35 m, as given in the question.
  </p>

  <h3>5. Worked Example 3: Finding the wavelength from a node-antinode distance</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Question:</b> The distance between a node and its nearest
    antinode is 0.15 m. Calculate the wavelength.
  </p>

  <p><b>Step 1: Write the relationship.</b></p>

  <p>
    d = λ/4
  </p>

  <p><b>Step 2: Make wavelength the subject.</b></p>

  <p>
    Multiply both sides by 4:
  </p>

  <p>
    λ = 4d
  </p>

  <p><b>Step 3: Substitute and calculate.</b></p>

  <p>
    λ = 4 × 0.15
  </p>

  <p>
    λ = 0.60 m
  </p>

  <p><b>Answer: 0.60 m</b></p>

  <p>
    <b>Check:</b> One-quarter of 0.60 m is 0.15 m.
  </p>

  <h3>6. Common mistake</h3>

  <p>
    Do not confuse the distance from a node to its nearest antinode
    with the distance between adjacent antinodes.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>Node to nearest antinode: λ/4.</li>
    <li>Adjacent antinodes: λ/2.</li>
    <li>Adjacent nodes: λ/2.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Before calculating, identify which two points the question
    describes.
  </p>
  ', 1),
  (628, 628, '
  <h2>Meaning of Electric Charge</h2>

  <h3>1. What is electric charge?</h3>
  <p>
    <b>Electric charge</b> is a property of matter responsible for
    electrical interactions. There are two types of charge:
    positive and negative.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>Protons have positive charge.</li>
    <li>Electrons have negative charge.</li>
    <li>Neutrons have no net electric charge.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    In a metal wire, some electrons can move through the material.
    Their movement makes electric current possible.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Unit of charge</h3>
  <p>
    Electric charge is measured in <b>coulombs (C)</b>.
    The symbol for charge is <b>Q</b>.
  </p>

  <p>
    The magnitude of the charge of one electron is approximately
    1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ C. An electron''s charge is negative, while a proton''s
    charge is positive with the same magnitude.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Identifying charge</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> What type of charge does an electron carry?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> An electron is a subatomic particle.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Electrons carry negative electric charge.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Negative charge.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Choosing the unit</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A body carries 8 C of charge. What does C represent?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> The quantity is electric charge.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Its SI unit is the coulomb.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> C represents coulombs.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Positive and negative particles</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A body gains some electrons. Explain the change in its net charge.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Electrons have negative charge.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Gaining electrons adds negative charge to the body.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> The body becomes negatively charged, assuming no other charge transfer occurs.</p>

  <h3>Remember</h3>
  <p>
    Charge is measured in coulombs. Do not confuse charge, Q,
    with current, I: charge is a quantity, while current describes
    the rate at which charge flows.
  </p>
  ', 1),
  (629, 629, '
  <h2>Meaning of Electric Current</h2>

  <h3>1. What is electric current?</h3>
  <p>
    <b>Electric current</b> is the rate of flow of electric charge
    through a conductor.
  </p>

  <p><b>I = Q/t</b></p>

  <ul>
    <li>I = current in amperes (A)</li>
    <li>Q = charge passing a point in coulombs (C)</li>
    <li>t = time in seconds (s)</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    One ampere means that one coulomb of charge passes a point
    in the circuit every second.
  </p>

  <h3>2. What makes current flow?</h3>
  <p>
    In a metal wire, electrons can move through the material.
    A potential difference across the wire can drive their movement
    around a complete circuit.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Conventional current</b> is defined as flowing from the positive
    terminal towards the negative terminal through the external circuit.
    In a metal wire, electrons drift in the opposite direction.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Interpreting one ampere</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> What does a current of 1 A mean?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Current is charge flowing per unit time.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> For 1 A, one coulomb flows each second.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> 1 A = 1 C/s.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Interpreting a larger current</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A wire carries a current of 3 A. How much charge passes a point each second?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> A = C/s.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> A current of 3 A means 3 C/s.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> 3 coulombs pass each second.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Conventional current and electrons</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> In a metal wire connected to a battery, which way do electrons move compared with conventional current?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Conventional current is defined from the positive terminal towards the negative terminal in the external circuit.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Electrons are negatively charged and drift in the opposite direction.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Electron drift is opposite to conventional current in the metal wire.</p>

  <h3>Remember</h3>
  <p>
    Current is not the same as charge. Current tells you how much charge
    passes a point per second.
  </p>
  ', 1),
  (630, 630, '
  <h2>Calculating Electric Current</h2>

  <h3>Formula</h3>
  <p><b>I = Q/t</b></p>

  <p>
    Divide the charge passing a point by the time taken.
    Charge must be in coulombs and time in seconds to obtain current in amperes.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked example 1</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A charge of 12 C passes a point in 3 s. Calculate the current.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Q = 12 C, t = 3 s.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> I = Q/t = 12/3.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> I = 4 A.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A charge of 45 C passes through a conductor in 15 s. Find the current.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Q = 45 C, t = 15 s.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> I = 45/15.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> I = 3 A.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A charge of 600 C passes through a device in 2 minutes. Calculate the current.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Convert time to seconds: 2 × 60 = 120 s.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> I = Q/t = 600/120.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> I = 5 A.</p>

  <h3>Common mistake</h3>
  <p>
    Convert minutes to seconds before calculating current.
    For example, 2 minutes is 120 seconds, not 2 seconds.
  </p>
  ', 1),
  (631, 631, '
  <h2>Calculating Electric Charge</h2>

  <h3>Formula</h3>
  <p><b>Q = It</b></p>

  <p>
    Since current is charge divided by time, multiplying current
    by time gives the total charge that passes a point.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>Q is charge in coulombs (C).</li>
    <li>I is current in amperes (A).</li>
    <li>t is time in seconds (s).</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Worked example 1</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A current of 2 A flows for 10 s. Calculate the charge transferred.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> I = 2 A, t = 10 s.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Q = It = 2 × 10.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Q = 20 C.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A current of 0.5 A flows for 40 s. Find the charge transferred.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> I = 0.5 A, t = 40 s.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Q = 0.5 × 40.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Q = 20 C.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A current of 3 A flows for 2 minutes. Calculate the charge transferred.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Convert time: 2 minutes = 120 s.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Q = It = 3 × 120.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Q = 360 C.</p>

  <h3>Common mistake</h3>
  <p>
    Do not divide current by time when calculating charge.
    Use Q = It, and convert time to seconds first.
  </p>
  ', 1),
  (632, 632, '
  <h2>Meaning and Calculation of Potential Difference</h2>

  <h3>1. What is potential difference?</h3>
  <p>
    <b>Potential difference (voltage)</b> between two points is the
    energy transferred per unit charge as charge moves between those points.
  </p>

  <p><b>V = E/Q</b></p>

  <ul>
    <li>V = potential difference in volts (V)</li>
    <li>E = energy transferred in joules (J)</li>
    <li>Q = charge in coulombs (C)</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    A potential difference of 1 V means that 1 J of energy is transferred
    per coulomb of charge.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Interpreting voltage</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> What does a potential difference of 6 V mean?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> One volt equals one joule per coulomb.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> At 6 V, each coulomb transfers 6 J of energy between the points.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> 6 J of energy is transferred per coulomb.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Calculating potential difference</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A device transfers 24 J of energy to 4 C of charge. Calculate the potential difference across it.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> E = 24 J, Q = 4 C.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> V = E/Q = 24/4.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> V = 6 V.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Finding energy transferred</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A charge of 3 C passes through a component with a potential difference of 12 V. Calculate the energy transferred.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Start with V = E/Q.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Rearrange to E = VQ.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> E = 12 × 3.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> E = 36 J.</p>

  <h3>Distinguish current from potential difference</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Current tells you the rate of charge flow.</li>
    <li>Potential difference tells you the energy transferred per unit charge.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Remember</h3>
  <p>
    Use V = E/Q when energy and charge are given.
    Use E = VQ when voltage and charge are given.
  </p>
  ', 1),
  (633, 633, '
  <h2>Meaning of Electrical Resistance</h2>

  <h3>1. What is resistance?</h3>
  <p>
    <b>Electrical resistance</b> is the opposition a material or component
    offers to the flow of electric current.
  </p>

  <p>
    Resistance is represented by <b>R</b> and measured in <b>ohms (Ω)</b>.
    A component with greater resistance carries less current than a
    component with lower resistance when the potential difference across
    each is the same and both obey Ohm''s law.
  </p>

  <h3>2. The relationship between voltage, current and resistance</h3>
  <p><b>V = IR</b></p>
  <ul>
    <li>V = potential difference in volts (V)</li>
    <li>I = current in amperes (A)</li>
    <li>R = resistance in ohms (Ω)</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Identifying the unit</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A component has a resistance of 8 Ω. What does Ω represent?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> R represents electrical resistance.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> The SI unit of resistance is the ohm.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Ω represents ohms.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Comparing two resistors</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> Two resistors have resistances of 2 Ω and 10 Ω. If the same potential difference is applied across each, which carries the smaller current, assuming both obey Ohm''s law?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Use I = V/R.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> At the same voltage, current decreases as resistance increases.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> The 10 Ω resistor carries the smaller current.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Identifying the quantities</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A resistor carries 3 A when a potential difference of 12 V is applied. Identify the quantities needed to calculate its resistance.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> V = 12 V.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> I = 3 A.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Use R = V/I.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Divide the potential difference by the current.</p>

  <h3>Remember</h3>
  <p>
    Resistance describes opposition to current. Do not confuse resistance
    in ohms with current in amperes or potential difference in volts.
  </p>
  ', 1),
  (634, 634, '
  <h2>Calculating Electrical Resistance</h2>

  <h3>Formula</h3>
  <p><b>R = V/I</b></p>

  <p>
    Resistance equals the potential difference across a component
    divided by the current through it.
  </p>

  <p>
    Use volts for potential difference and amperes for current.
    The resulting resistance is in ohms.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked example 1</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A resistor has a potential difference of 12 V across it and carries a current of 3 A. Calculate its resistance.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> V = 12 V, I = 3 A.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> R = V/I.</p>
  <p>R = 12/3</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> R = 4 Ω.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A lamp operates at 6 V and carries a current of 0.5 A. Calculate its resistance at that operating condition.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> V = 6 V, I = 0.5 A.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> R = 6/0.5.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> R = 12 Ω.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A component has 24 V across it and carries 0.2 A. Find its resistance at that operating condition.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> V = 24 V, I = 0.2 A.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> R = 24/0.2.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> R = 120 Ω.</p>

  <h3>Common mistake</h3>
  <p>
    Do not calculate I/V when finding resistance.
    The correct formula is R = V/I.
  </p>
  ', 1),
  (635, 635, '
  <h2>Calculating Current Using Ohm''s Law</h2>

  <h3>Formula</h3>
  <p><b>I = V/R</b></p>

  <p>
    Rearrange V = IR by dividing both sides by R.
    Current equals potential difference divided by resistance.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked example 1</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A 6 Ω resistor is connected across a 12 V supply. Calculate the current.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> V = 12 V, R = 6 Ω.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> I = V/R.</p>
  <p>I = 12/6</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> I = 2 A.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A 20 Ω resistor is connected across 10 V. Calculate the current.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> V = 10 V, R = 20 Ω.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> I = 10/20.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> I = 0.5 A.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A 4 Ω resistor is connected across 18 V. Find the current.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> V = 18 V, R = 4 Ω.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> I = 18/4.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> I = 4.5 A.</p>

  <h3>Check your answer</h3>
  <p>
    For a fixed resistance, increasing the potential difference increases
    the current in the same proportion, provided the component''s resistance
    remains constant.
  </p>
  ', 1),
  (636, 636, '
  <h2>Calculating Potential Difference Using Ohm''s Law</h2>

  <h3>Formula</h3>
  <p><b>V = IR</b></p>

  <p>
    Multiply current by resistance to calculate the potential difference
    across a component that follows Ohm''s law.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked example 1</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A 4 Ω resistor carries a current of 2 A. Calculate the potential difference across it.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> I = 2 A, R = 4 Ω.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> V = IR = 2 × 4.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> V = 8 V.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A 15 Ω resistor carries 0.4 A. Find the potential difference across it.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> I = 0.4 A, R = 15 Ω.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> V = 0.4 × 15.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> V = 6 V.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A 2.5 Ω resistor carries a current of 6 A. Calculate the potential difference.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> I = 6 A, R = 2.5 Ω.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> V = 6 × 2.5.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> V = 15 V.</p>

  <h3>Check your answer</h3>
  <p>
    The unit is volts. Do not divide current by resistance when calculating
    potential difference; use V = IR.
  </p>
  ', 1),
  (637, 637, '
  <h2>Ohm''s Law and Voltage-Current Graphs</h2>

  <h3>1. Statement of Ohm''s law</h3>
  <p>
    Ohm''s law states that, for a conductor at constant temperature,
    the current through it is directly proportional to the potential
    difference across it.
  </p>

  <p>
    This means that doubling the potential difference doubles the current
    if the conductor''s resistance remains constant.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Ohmic conductors</h3>
  <p>
    An <b>ohmic conductor</b> obeys Ohm''s law under the stated conditions.
    Its resistance remains constant as the potential difference changes,
    provided its temperature and other relevant conditions remain constant.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Interpreting a graph</h3>
  <p>
    On a graph with potential difference V on the vertical axis and
    current I on the horizontal axis:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>A straight line through the origin indicates constant resistance.</li>
    <li>The gradient equals V/I, which is the resistance R.</li>
    <li>A steeper line means greater resistance when the axes are V against I.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Always check which quantity is on each axis before interpreting
    the gradient.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Identifying an ohmic conductor</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A conductor''s current doubles whenever the potential difference doubles, while its temperature stays constant. Does it obey Ohm''s law?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Ohm''s law requires current to be directly proportional to potential difference at constant temperature.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> The current doubles when the voltage doubles.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Yes, this behaviour is consistent with Ohm''s law.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Calculating graph gradient</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> On a V-I graph, a straight line passes through (I = 2 A, V = 6 V). Calculate its resistance if the line passes through the origin.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Resistance = gradient = change in V ÷ change in I.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> R = 6/2.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> R = 3 Ω.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Comparing graph gradients</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> Two ohmic resistors are represented on V-I graphs with voltage on the vertical axis. Resistor A has a gradient of 2 V/A, and resistor B has a gradient of 5 V/A. Which has the greater resistance?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> For this graph, gradient = V/I = R.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> A has resistance 2 Ω and B has resistance 5 Ω.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Resistor B has the greater resistance.</p>

  <h3>Remember</h3>
  <p>
    A straight-line V-I graph through the origin indicates constant resistance.
    The gradient represents resistance only when voltage is plotted vertically
    against current horizontally.
  </p>
  ', 1),
  (638, 638, '<h2>Identifying a Series Circuit</h2>

<h3>Learning objective</h3>
<p>Identify a series circuit by recognising that its components are connected along one continuous path for electric current.</p>

<h3>1. What is a series circuit?</h3>
<p>A <b>series circuit</b> is a circuit in which components are connected one after another along a single path.</p>

<p>For example, imagine a cell connected to a switch, then a lamp, and finally back to the cell. The current has only one path to follow.</p>

<p><b>Key features:</b></p>
<ul>
<li>There is only one path for current through the components.</li>
<li>The components are connected one after another.</li>
<li>If a break occurs anywhere along the only path, the circuit becomes incomplete and current stops throughout it.</li>
</ul>

<h3>2. Worked example 1: Recognising a series circuit</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> A cell, switch and two lamps are connected one after another in one closed loop. What type of circuit is this?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify the number of paths available to the current.</p>
<p>There is only one path.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Identify how the components are connected.</p>
<p>They are connected one after another.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> It is a series circuit.</p>

<h3>3. Worked example 2: Finding the number of paths</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> Three resistors are connected in series. How many separate paths are available for current through those resistors?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Series components share one continuous route.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> No branch provides an alternative route.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> One path.</p>

<h3>4. Worked example 3: Predicting the effect of a break</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> Two lamps are connected in series. One lamp is removed, leaving a gap. What happens to the other lamp?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Removing the lamp creates a break in the only path.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> The circuit is now incomplete, so current cannot flow around the loop.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> The other lamp goes out.</p>

<h3>Remember</h3>
<p><b>Series = one path.</b> A break anywhere in that path stops the current through the entire series circuit.</p>', 1),
  (639, 639, '<h2>Current in a Series Circuit</h2>

<h3>Learning objective</h3>
<p>Use the rule that current is the same at every point in a series circuit to determine unknown current values.</p>

<h3>1. The current rule</h3>
<p>In a series circuit, the current is the same through every component.</p>

<p>If an ammeter measures 0.4 A before the first resistor, the current through the second resistor is also 0.4 A, provided the circuit is a single series path.</p>

<p>This is because charge cannot split between branches in a series circuit. The same rate of charge flow passes through every component.</p>

<p><b>Rule:</b></p>
<p>I₁ = I₂ = I₃</p>
<p>Here, I₁, I₂ and I₃ represent current at different points in the series path.</p>

<h3>2. Worked example 1: Finding current after a resistor</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> A series circuit carries a current of 0.6 A through the first resistor. Find the current through the second resistor.</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Recall that current is the same throughout a series circuit.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> The current through the second resistor equals the current through the first resistor.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> 0.6 A.</p>

<h3>3. Worked example 2: Reading an ammeter</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> An ammeter reads 250 mA at one point in a series circuit. What current should another ideal ammeter measure elsewhere in the same series path?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Current is the same at every point in a series circuit.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Keep the same unit: 250 mA.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> 250 mA.</p>

<h3>4. Worked example 3: Finding an unknown reading</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> Three ammeters are placed at different points in a series circuit. The readings are 0.8 A, I and 0.8 A. Find I.</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> In series, all current readings are equal.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Therefore, I = 0.8 A.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> 0.8 A.</p>

<h3>Remember</h3>
<p>In a series circuit, current is the same everywhere. Do not add the current readings together.</p>', 1),
  (640, 640, '<h2>Potential Difference in a Series Circuit</h2>

<h3>Learning objective</h3>
<p>Calculate the supply potential difference or an unknown voltage drop using the rule that the supply voltage equals the sum of the voltage drops across series components.</p>

<h3>1. How voltage is shared</h3>
<p>Potential difference is the energy transferred per unit charge between two points.</p>

<p>In a series circuit, the supply potential difference is shared between the components. The voltage drops across all components add up to the supply voltage.</p>

<p><b>Rule:</b></p>
<p>V<sub>supply</sub> = V₁ + V₂ + V₃</p>

<p>The voltage drops do not have to be equal. They depend on the resistance of each component and the current flowing through it.</p>

<h3>2. Worked example 1: Finding the supply voltage</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> Two series resistors have voltage drops of 3 V and 5 V. Find the supply voltage.</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Write the rule: supply voltage = sum of voltage drops.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Substitute the values: V = 3 + 5.</p>
<p><b>Step 3:</b> Calculate: V = 8 V.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> 8 V.</p>

<h3>3. Worked example 2: Finding an unknown voltage drop</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> A 12 V supply is connected to two series resistors. The first resistor has a voltage drop of 7 V. Find the voltage drop across the second resistor.</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> The two voltage drops must add to 12 V.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Let the unknown voltage be V₂.</p>
<p>12 = 7 + V₂</p>
<p><b>Step 3:</b> Rearrange: V₂ = 12 − 7 = 5 V.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> 5 V.</p>

<h3>4. Worked example 3: Three components</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> Three series components have voltage drops of 2 V, 4 V and 6 V. Find the supply voltage.</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Add the voltage drops.</p>
<p>V = 2 + 4 + 6</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Calculate: V = 12 V.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> 12 V.</p>

<h3>Remember</h3>
<p><b>Supply voltage = total of the voltage drops.</b> A component with a larger resistance generally has a larger voltage drop when the same current flows through the series components.</p>', 1),
  (641, 641, '<h2>Total Resistance in a Series Circuit</h2>

<h3>Learning objective</h3>
<p>Calculate the combined resistance of resistors connected in series.</p>

<h3>1. The series resistance rule</h3>
<p>When resistors are connected in series, the total resistance is the sum of their individual resistances.</p>

<p><b>Rule:</b></p>
<p>R<sub>T</sub> = R₁ + R₂ + R₃</p>

<p>Adding a resistor in series increases the total resistance because it adds more opposition to current along the single path.</p>

<h3>2. Worked example 1: Two resistors</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> Resistors of 2 Ω and 5 Ω are connected in series. Find the total resistance.</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Use R<sub>T</sub> = R₁ + R₂.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Substitute: R<sub>T</sub> = 2 + 5.</p>
<p><b>Step 3:</b> Calculate: R<sub>T</sub> = 7 Ω.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> 7 Ω.</p>

<h3>3. Worked example 2: Three resistors</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> Three resistors of 4 Ω, 6 Ω and 10 Ω are connected in series. Find the total resistance.</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Add all three resistances.</p>
<p>R<sub>T</sub> = 4 + 6 + 10</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Calculate: R<sub>T</sub> = 20 Ω.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> 20 Ω.</p>

<h3>4. Worked example 3: Finding an unknown resistance</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> Two resistors in series have a total resistance of 18 Ω. One resistor is 11 Ω. Find the resistance of the other resistor.</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Write: R<sub>T</sub> = R₁ + R₂.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Substitute: 18 = 11 + R₂.</p>
<p><b>Step 3:</b> Rearrange: R₂ = 18 − 11 = 7 Ω.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> 7 Ω.</p>

<h3>Remember</h3>
<p>For resistors in series, <b>add the resistances</b>. The total resistance is greater than any one positive individual resistance.</p>', 1),
  (642, 642, '<h2>Solving Series Circuit Problems</h2>

<h3>Learning objective</h3>
<p>Solve a series-circuit calculation by finding total resistance first, then current, then voltage drops where required.</p>

<h3>1. The solving sequence</h3>
<p>For a series circuit with a known supply voltage and resistances:</p>
<ol>
<li>Add the resistances to find total resistance.</li>
<li>Use Ohm''s law to calculate current: I = V/R.</li>
<li>Use V = IR to calculate the voltage drop across each resistor if required.</li>
</ol>

<p>Remember: current is the same through every resistor in series, and the voltage drops add up to the supply voltage.</p>

<h3>2. Worked example 1: Finding total resistance and current</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> A 12 V battery is connected to 2 Ω and 4 Ω resistors in series. Find the total resistance and current.</p>
<p><b>Step 1: Find total resistance.</b></p>
<p>R<sub>T</sub> = 2 + 4 = 6 Ω.</p>
<p><b>Step 2: Apply Ohm''s law.</b></p>
<p>I = V/R<sub>T</sub> = 12/6.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> Total resistance = 6 Ω; current = 2 A.</p>

<h3>3. Worked example 2: Finding voltage drops</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> A 10 V supply is connected to 2 Ω and 3 Ω resistors in series. Find the current and the voltage drop across each resistor.</p>
<p><b>Step 1: Total resistance.</b></p>
<p>R<sub>T</sub> = 2 + 3 = 5 Ω.</p>
<p><b>Step 2: Calculate current.</b></p>
<p>I = 10/5 = 2 A.</p>
<p><b>Step 3: Voltage across the 2 Ω resistor.</b></p>
<p>V₁ = IR₁ = 2 × 2 = 4 V.</p>
<p><b>Step 4: Voltage across the 3 Ω resistor.</b></p>
<p>V₂ = IR₂ = 2 × 3 = 6 V.</p>
<p><b>Check:</b> 4 + 6 = 10 V, which equals the supply.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> Current = 2 A; voltage drops = 4 V and 6 V.</p>

<h3>4. Worked example 3: Finding an unknown resistor</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> A 12 V supply drives a current of 2 A through two resistors in series. One resistor is 4 Ω. Find the other resistance.</p>
<p><b>Step 1: Find total resistance using Ohm''s law.</b></p>
<p>R<sub>T</sub> = V/I = 12/2 = 6 Ω.</p>
<p><b>Step 2: Subtract the known resistance.</b></p>
<p>R₂ = R<sub>T</sub> − R₁ = 6 − 4 = 2 Ω.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> The unknown resistor is 2 Ω.</p>

<h3>Remember</h3>
<p>Follow the order: <b>total resistance → current → individual voltage drops.</b> Check that all voltage drops add up to the supply voltage.</p>', 1),
  (643, 643, '<h2>Identifying a Parallel Circuit</h2>

<h3>Learning objective</h3>
<p>Identify a parallel circuit by recognising components connected on separate branches between the same two junctions.</p>

<h3>1. What is a parallel circuit?</h3>
<p>A <b>parallel circuit</b> has two or more branches through which electric current can flow.</p>

<p>At a junction, current can divide between the available branches. The branches join again at another junction.</p>

<p><b>Key features:</b></p>
<ul>
<li>There is more than one path for current.</li>
<li>Each branch connects across the same two junctions.</li>
<li>If one branch breaks, current may still flow through the other complete branches.</li>
</ul>

<h3>2. Worked example 1: Identifying the arrangement</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> Two lamps are connected on separate branches between the same two junctions. What type of circuit is this?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Observe whether the lamps share one path or occupy separate branches.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Each lamp has its own branch between the junctions.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> It is a parallel circuit.</p>

<h3>3. Worked example 2: Counting branches</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> Three resistors are connected on three separate branches across a battery. How many branches contain resistors?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Each resistor occupies a separate branch.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Count the branches: one, two, three.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> Three branches.</p>

<h3>4. Worked example 3: One branch breaks</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> Two lamps are connected in parallel. One lamp is removed, leaving its branch open. What happens to the other lamp, assuming its branch remains complete?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Removing one lamp breaks only its branch.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> The other lamp still has a complete path through the supply.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> The other lamp can remain lit.</p>

<h3>Remember</h3>
<p><b>Parallel = separate branches.</b> A break in one branch does not necessarily stop current in the other branches.</p>', 1),
  (644, 644, '<h2>Potential Difference in a Parallel Circuit</h2>

<h3>Learning objective</h3>
<p>Determine the potential difference across each branch of a parallel circuit.</p>

<h3>1. The voltage rule</h3>
<p>In a parallel circuit, each branch is connected across the same two junctions. Therefore, the potential difference across each branch is the same as the supply potential difference for an ideal circuit with connecting wires of negligible resistance.</p>

<p><b>Rule:</b></p>
<p>V<sub>supply</sub> = V₁ = V₂ = V₃</p>

<p>This does not mean that the current in every branch is the same. Branch currents depend on the resistance of each branch.</p>

<h3>2. Worked example 1: Finding branch voltage</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> Two resistors are connected in parallel across a 12 V supply. Find the voltage across the second resistor.</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Each branch is connected across the supply.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> The voltage across every branch equals 12 V.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> 12 V.</p>

<h3>3. Worked example 2: Three branches</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> Three lamps are connected in parallel across a 9 V battery. Find the potential difference across each lamp.</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> The three lamps occupy separate branches.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Each branch has the same potential difference as the supply.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> Each lamp has a potential difference of 9 V.</p>

<h3>4. Worked example 3: Finding an unknown supply voltage</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> A resistor in one branch of a parallel circuit has a potential difference of 6 V. The circuit is connected ideally across a battery. Find the supply voltage.</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> In parallel, branch voltage equals supply voltage.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> V<sub>supply</sub> = V<sub>branch</sub>.</p>
<p><b>Step 3:</b> V<sub>supply</sub> = 6 V.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> 6 V.</p>

<h3>Remember</h3>
<p><b>Parallel voltage is the same across each branch.</b> Do not add the branch voltages together to find the supply voltage.</p>', 1),
  (645, 645, '<h2>Total Current in a Parallel Circuit</h2>

<h3>Learning objective</h3>
<p>Calculate the total current supplied to parallel branches by adding the currents in the individual branches.</p>

<h3>1. The current rule</h3>
<p>At a junction, current divides between the available branches. When the branch currents join again, their total equals the current entering the junction.</p>

<p><b>Rule:</b></p>
<p>I<sub>total</sub> = I₁ + I₂ + I₃</p>

<p>The current in each branch depends on the branch resistance and the voltage across it. A branch with lower resistance carries more current than a branch with higher resistance when the branch voltages are equal.</p>

<h3>2. Worked example 1: Two branches</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> Two parallel branches carry currents of 2 A and 3 A. Find the total current supplied by the battery.</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Add the branch currents.</p>
<p>I<sub>total</sub> = 2 + 3.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Calculate: I<sub>total</sub> = 5 A.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> 5 A.</p>

<h3>3. Worked example 2: Three branches</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> Three branches carry currents of 0.5 A, 0.8 A and 0.7 A. Find the total current.</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Add all branch currents.</p>
<p>I<sub>total</sub> = 0.5 + 0.8 + 0.7.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Calculate: I<sub>total</sub> = 2.0 A.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> 2.0 A.</p>

<h3>4. Worked example 3: Finding an unknown branch current</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> A parallel circuit draws a total current of 4 A. One branch carries 1.5 A. Find the current in the other branch.</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Write I<sub>total</sub> = I₁ + I₂.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Substitute: 4 = 1.5 + I₂.</p>
<p><b>Step 3:</b> Rearrange: I₂ = 4 − 1.5 = 2.5 A.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> 2.5 A.</p>

<h3>Remember</h3>
<p><b>Total current = sum of branch currents.</b> Add currents to find the total; subtract a known branch current from the total to find an unknown branch current.</p>', 1),
  (646, 646, '<h2>Equivalent Resistance in a Parallel Circuit</h2>

<h3>Learning objective</h3>
<p>Calculate the equivalent resistance of two or more resistors connected in parallel.</p>

<h3>1. The parallel resistance rule</h3>
<p>For resistors in parallel, the reciprocal of the equivalent resistance equals the sum of the reciprocals of the individual resistances.</p>

<p><b>Rule:</b></p>
<p>1/R<sub>T</sub> = 1/R₁ + 1/R₂</p>

<p>For two resistors, this can also be written as:</p>
<p>R<sub>T</sub> = (R₁ × R₂)/(R₁ + R₂)</p>

<p>For positive resistances, the equivalent resistance of a parallel combination is always less than the smallest individual resistance. This happens because parallel branches provide additional paths for current.</p>

<h3>2. Worked example 1: Two equal resistors</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> Two 6 Ω resistors are connected in parallel. Find the equivalent resistance.</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Use the two-resistor formula.</p>
<p>R<sub>T</sub> = (R₁ × R₂)/(R₁ + R₂).</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Substitute the values.</p>
<p>R<sub>T</sub> = (6 × 6)/(6 + 6) = 36/12.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> 3 Ω.</p>

<h3>3. Worked example 2: Different resistances</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> Resistors of 3 Ω and 6 Ω are connected in parallel. Find the equivalent resistance.</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Apply the formula.</p>
<p>R<sub>T</sub> = (3 × 6)/(3 + 6).</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Calculate.</p>
<p>R<sub>T</sub> = 18/9 = 2 Ω.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> 2 Ω.</p>

<h3>4. Worked example 3: Three equal resistors</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> Three 6 Ω resistors are connected in parallel. Find the equivalent resistance.</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> For n equal resistors in parallel, R<sub>T</sub> = R/n.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Substitute: R<sub>T</sub> = 6/3.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> 2 Ω.</p>

<h3>Remember</h3>
<p>Do not add parallel resistances directly. Use the reciprocal rule or the two-resistor product-over-sum formula. Check that the answer is smaller than the smallest individual resistance.</p>', 1),
  (647, 647, '<h2>Solving Parallel Circuit Problems</h2>

<h3>Learning objective</h3>
<p>Use the voltage rule, Ohm''s law and the current rule to solve numerical problems involving parallel branches.</p>

<h3>1. The solving sequence</h3>
<p>When the supply voltage and branch resistances are known:</p>
<ol>
<li>Recognise that the voltage across each branch equals the supply voltage.</li>
<li>Calculate each branch current using I = V/R.</li>
<li>Add the branch currents to find the total current.</li>
</ol>

<p>If the question asks for the equivalent resistance, you can also use R<sub>T</sub> = V/I<sub>T</sub>, or calculate it using the parallel resistance formula.</p>

<h3>2. Worked example 1: Finding branch currents and total current</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> A 12 V supply is connected across 6 Ω and 3 Ω resistors in parallel. Find the current in each branch and the total current.</p>
<p><b>Step 1: Voltage across each branch.</b></p>
<p>V₁ = V₂ = 12 V.</p>
<p><b>Step 2: Current through the 6 Ω resistor.</b></p>
<p>I₁ = V/R₁ = 12/6 = 2 A.</p>
<p><b>Step 3: Current through the 3 Ω resistor.</b></p>
<p>I₂ = V/R₂ = 12/3 = 4 A.</p>
<p><b>Step 4: Total current.</b></p>
<p>I<sub>T</sub> = I₁ + I₂ = 2 + 4 = 6 A.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> Branch currents are 2 A and 4 A; total current is 6 A.</p>

<h3>3. Worked example 2: Finding total current and equivalent resistance</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> A 10 V supply is connected across 5 Ω and 10 Ω resistors in parallel. Find the total current and equivalent resistance.</p>
<p><b>Step 1: Calculate each branch current.</b></p>
<p>I₁ = 10/5 = 2 A.</p>
<p>I₂ = 10/10 = 1 A.</p>
<p><b>Step 2: Add the branch currents.</b></p>
<p>I<sub>T</sub> = 2 + 1 = 3 A.</p>
<p><b>Step 3: Find equivalent resistance.</b></p>
<p>R<sub>T</sub> = V/I<sub>T</sub> = 10/3 ≈ 3.33 Ω.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> Total current = 3 A; equivalent resistance ≈ 3.33 Ω.</p>

<h3>4. Worked example 3: Finding an unknown branch resistance</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> A 12 V supply is connected across two parallel branches. The total current is 5 A, and one branch has a resistance of 4 Ω. Find the resistance of the other branch.</p>
<p><b>Step 1: Find the current in the known branch.</b></p>
<p>I₁ = V/R₁ = 12/4 = 3 A.</p>
<p><b>Step 2: Find the current in the unknown branch.</b></p>
<p>I₂ = I<sub>T</sub> − I₁ = 5 − 3 = 2 A.</p>
<p><b>Step 3: Use Ohm''s law to find the unknown resistance.</b></p>
<p>R₂ = V/I₂ = 12/2 = 6 Ω.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> The other branch has a resistance of 6 Ω.</p>

<h3>Remember</h3>
<p>Follow the sequence: <b>branch voltage → branch currents → total current.</b> Check that the branch currents add up to the total current and that every branch has the supply voltage across it.</p>', 1),
  (648, 648, '<h2>Magnetic Field Patterns</h2>

<h3>Learning objective</h3>
<p>Interpret magnetic field lines to determine the direction and relative strength of a magnetic field.</p>

<h3>1. What is a magnetic field?</h3>
<p>A <b>magnetic field</b> is the region around a magnet or current-carrying conductor where magnetic forces can be experienced.</p>

<p>We represent a magnetic field using magnetic field lines. These lines help us show the field''s direction and relative strength.</p>

<h3>2. Direction of magnetic field lines</h3>
<p>Outside a bar magnet, magnetic field lines run from the <b>north pole to the south pole</b>. Inside the magnet, they return from south to north, forming closed loops.</p>

<p>The arrows on a field-line diagram show the direction in which the north pole of a small test magnet would point.</p>

<h3>3. Strength of a magnetic field</h3>
<p>Where field lines are close together, the field is stronger. Where they are farther apart, the field is weaker.</p>

<p>Magnetic field lines do not cross. If they crossed, the field would have two directions at the same point.</p>

<h3>4. Worked example 1: Direction outside a bar magnet</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> Outside a bar magnet, in which direction do magnetic field lines run?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify the poles: north and south.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Recall the convention for the field outside a magnet.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> From north to south.</p>

<h3>5. Worked example 2: Comparing field strength</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> A diagram shows field lines crowded near one pole and spread farther apart near the other region. Where is the field stronger?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Compare the spacing between the field lines.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> The lines are closer together near the pole.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> The field is stronger near the pole where the lines are closer together.</p>

<h3>6. Worked example 3: Understanding field lines</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> Why must magnetic field lines not cross each other?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> A field line indicates the direction of the magnetic field at a point.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Crossing lines would assign two directions to the field at the same point.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> Field lines cannot cross because the magnetic field has one direction at any given point.</p>

<h3>Remember</h3>
<ul>
<li>Outside a bar magnet: north to south.</li>
<li>Closer field lines indicate a stronger field.</li>
<li>Magnetic field lines never cross.</li>
</ul>', 1),
  (649, 649, '<h2>Magnetic Field Around a Current-Carrying Wire</h2>

<h3>Learning objective</h3>
<p>Describe the shape and direction of the magnetic field around a straight current-carrying conductor.</p>

<h3>1. Current produces a magnetic field</h3>
<p>When electric current flows through a conductor, a magnetic field forms around the conductor.</p>

<p>A straight wire produces circular magnetic field lines centred on the wire. The circles lie in planes perpendicular to the wire.</p>

<p>This effect is called the <b>magnetic effect of electric current</b>.</p>

<h3>2. Direction of the field</h3>
<p>The direction depends on the direction of the current.</p>

<p>Use the <b>right-hand grip rule</b> for a straight wire:</p>
<ul>
<li>Point your right thumb in the direction of conventional current.</li>
<li>Your curled fingers show the direction of the magnetic field around the wire.</li>
</ul>

<p>Conventional current is taken to flow from the positive terminal towards the negative terminal through the external circuit.</p>

<h3>3. Strength of the magnetic field</h3>
<p>For the same straight conductor, increasing the current increases the magnetic field strength. The field becomes weaker as you move farther from the wire.</p>

<h3>4. Worked example 1: Shape of the field</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> What is the shape of the magnetic field around a long, straight wire carrying current?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify the conductor as a straight wire.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Recall the field pattern for a straight current-carrying wire.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> Concentric circles centred on the wire.</p>

<h3>5. Worked example 2: Increasing current</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> The current through a straight wire is increased while the observation point remains the same distance from the wire. What happens to the magnetic field strength?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> A current-carrying wire produces a magnetic field.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> The field strength increases when the current increases.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> The magnetic field becomes stronger.</p>

<h3>6. Worked example 3: Reversing current</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> The current in a straight wire is reversed. What happens to the direction of the magnetic field?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> The right-hand grip rule links current direction to field direction.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Reversing the current reverses the direction of the circular field.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> The magnetic field direction reverses.</p>

<h3>Remember</h3>
<p>A straight current-carrying wire produces circular magnetic field lines. More current produces a stronger field, and reversing the current reverses the field direction.</p>', 1),
  (650, 650, '<h2>Magnetic Field of a Solenoid</h2>

<h3>Learning objective</h3>
<p>Describe the magnetic field of a solenoid and explain why a current-carrying solenoid behaves like a bar magnet.</p>

<h3>1. What is a solenoid?</h3>
<p>A <b>solenoid</b> is a long coil of insulated wire wound into many turns. When electric current flows through the coil, it produces a magnetic field.</p>

<p>The field inside a sufficiently long solenoid is approximately uniform: the field lines are nearly parallel and evenly spaced. Outside, the field resembles that of a bar magnet.</p>

<h3>2. Magnetic poles of a solenoid</h3>
<p>A current-carrying solenoid has a north pole and a south pole. The poles depend on the direction of current around the coil.</p>

<p>Reverse the current, and the north and south poles exchange positions.</p>

<h3>3. Solenoid and bar magnet comparison</h3>
<ul>
<li>Both have north and south poles.</li>
<li>Both produce magnetic fields outside them that run from north to south.</li>
<li>The solenoid''s magnetic field can be switched off by stopping the current.</li>
<li>Its field can be strengthened by increasing current or adding more turns per unit length.</li>
</ul>

<h3>4. Worked example 1: Identifying a solenoid</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> A wire is wound into many close turns in a cylindrical shape. What is this coil called?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify the many turns of wire.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> A long coil of wire is called a solenoid.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> A solenoid.</p>

<h3>5. Worked example 2: Predicting the field inside</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> Describe the magnetic field inside a long, current-carrying solenoid.</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Consider the field pattern inside the coil.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Inside a sufficiently long solenoid, the field lines are nearly parallel and evenly spaced.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> The field is approximately uniform and directed along the solenoid''s axis.</p>

<h3>6. Worked example 3: Reversing the current</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> The current through a solenoid is reversed. What happens to its magnetic poles?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> The poles depend on current direction.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Reversing the current reverses the magnetic field direction.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> The north and south poles swap positions.</p>

<h3>Remember</h3>
<p>A solenoid is a coil of wire that produces a bar-magnet-like field when current flows. Its field can be controlled by changing the current or the coil''s construction.</p>', 1),
  (651, 651, '<h2>Factors Affecting Electromagnet Strength</h2>

<h3>Learning objective</h3>
<p>Identify how current, the number of coil turns and the core material affect the strength of an electromagnet.</p>

<h3>1. What is an electromagnet?</h3>
<p>An <b>electromagnet</b> is a magnet whose magnetic field is produced mainly by electric current. It is commonly made by winding insulated wire around a soft iron core.</p>

<p>Three important factors can increase its strength:</p>
<ol>
<li><b>Current:</b> Increasing current generally strengthens the magnetic field.</li>
<li><b>Number of turns:</b> Increasing the number of turns, with other conditions unchanged, generally strengthens the field.</li>
<li><b>Core material:</b> A suitable magnetic core, such as soft iron, strengthens the field compared with an air core.</li>
</ol>

<h3>2. Worked example 1: Increasing current</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> Two otherwise identical electromagnets have the same coil and core. One carries a larger current. Which is generally stronger?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Compare the currents while keeping the other factors the same.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> The larger current produces a stronger magnetic field.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> The electromagnet carrying the larger current is generally stronger.</p>

<h3>3. Worked example 2: Increasing the number of turns</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> Two coils have the same core and current. Coil A has 100 turns, while Coil B has 200 turns wound over a similar length. Which is generally stronger?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Compare the number of turns per unit length.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Coil B has more turns over a similar length.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> Coil B is generally stronger.</p>

<h3>4. Worked example 3: Choosing a core</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> A coil is tested first with an air core and then with a suitable soft iron core. Which arrangement generally produces the stronger electromagnet?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> The coil and current are kept the same.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Soft iron concentrates and strengthens the magnetic field.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> The coil with the soft iron core generally produces the stronger electromagnet.</p>

<h3>5. A fair test</h3>
<p>To investigate one factor properly, change only that factor and keep the others constant. For example, to test the effect of current, keep the coil''s turns and core the same.</p>

<h3>Remember</h3>
<p>To strengthen an electromagnet, increase current, increase turns per unit length, or use a suitable magnetic core. A controlled experiment changes one factor at a time.</p>', 1),
  (652, 652, '<h2>Applications of Electromagnets</h2>

<h3>Learning objective</h3>
<p>Explain how electromagnets are used to control movement, attract magnetic materials and operate electrical devices.</p>

<h3>1. Why use an electromagnet?</h3>
<p>An electromagnet produces a magnetic field when current flows through its coil. This makes it useful when a magnetic force needs to be controlled electrically.</p>

<p>Its field can be switched on or off by controlling the current. Its strength can also be changed by changing the current or the coil.</p>

<h3>2. Electric bells</h3>
<p>In a typical electric bell, current through a coil magnetises its iron core. The electromagnet attracts an iron armature, causing the hammer to strike the bell. The movement interrupts the circuit, the electromagnet releases the armature, and the circuit can reconnect. This repeated action produces the ringing sound.</p>

<h3>3. Magnetic relays</h3>
<p>A relay uses an electromagnet to move a switch. A small current in the coil can control a separate circuit, allowing a low-power control circuit to operate another circuit.</p>

<h3>4. Electromagnetic cranes</h3>
<p>Industrial cranes can use powerful electromagnets to lift iron or steel objects. Switching off the current reduces the magnetic attraction, allowing the load to be released, although residual magnetism may remain.</p>

<h3>5. Worked example 1: Lifting scrap metal</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> Why can an electromagnetic crane pick up steel objects and then release them?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> When current flows, the crane''s coil produces a magnetic field.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> The field attracts suitable ferromagnetic materials such as iron and steel.</p>
<p><b>Step 3:</b> When current is switched off, the electromagnet loses most of its field.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> It can lift metal when energised and release it when the current is switched off.</p>

<h3>6. Worked example 2: Operating a relay</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> What is the purpose of the electromagnet in a relay?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Current flows through the relay coil.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> The coil attracts an armature or moves a magnetic switch mechanism.</p>
<p><b>Step 3:</b> The movement opens or closes contacts in another circuit.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> The electromagnet allows one circuit to control a switch in another circuit.</p>

<h3>7. Worked example 3: The electric bell</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> Why does the hammer in a typical electric bell move repeatedly while the bell is operating?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Current magnetises the electromagnet and attracts the armature.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> The movement interrupts the current through the coil.</p>
<p><b>Step 3:</b> The electromagnet releases the armature, allowing the circuit to reconnect.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> The make-and-break action repeatedly energises and releases the electromagnet, causing the hammer to vibrate and strike the bell.</p>

<h3>Remember</h3>
<p>Electromagnets are useful because their magnetic effect can be controlled by electric current. Common applications include electric bells, relays and electromagnetic cranes.</p>', 1),
  (653, 653, '<h2>Density</h2>

<h3>Learning objective</h3>
<p>Calculate density, mass or volume using the relationship between these three quantities.</p>

<h3>1. What is density?</h3>
<p><b>Density</b> is the mass of a substance per unit volume. It tells us how much mass is contained in a given volume.</p>

<p><b>Formula:</b></p>
<p>Density = Mass ÷ Volume</p>
<p>ρ = m/V</p>

<p>Where:</p>
<ul>
<li>ρ is density.</li>
<li>m is mass.</li>
<li>V is volume.</li>
</ul>

<p>The SI unit of density is kg/m³. Another commonly used unit is g/cm³.</p>

<p><b>Useful conversion:</b> 1 g/cm³ = 1000 kg/m³.</p>

<h3>2. Worked example 1: Calculating density</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> A metal block has a mass of 600 g and a volume of 200 cm³. Calculate its density.</p>
<p><b>Step 1: Write the formula.</b></p>
<p>ρ = m/V</p>
<p><b>Step 2: Substitute the values.</b></p>
<p>ρ = 600/200</p>
<p><b>Step 3: Calculate.</b></p>
<p>ρ = 3 g/cm³.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> 3 g/cm³.</p>

<h3>3. Worked example 2: Calculating mass</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> A liquid has a density of 800 kg/m³ and a volume of 0.5 m³. Find its mass.</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Rearrange ρ = m/V to obtain m = ρV.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Substitute: m = 800 × 0.5.</p>
<p><b>Step 3:</b> Calculate: m = 400 kg.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> 400 kg.</p>

<h3>4. Worked example 3: Calculating volume</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> An object has a mass of 2 kg and a density of 500 kg/m³. Find its volume.</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Rearrange ρ = m/V to obtain V = m/ρ.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Substitute: V = 2/500.</p>
<p><b>Step 3:</b> Calculate: V = 0.004 m³.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> 0.004 m³.</p>

<h3>Remember</h3>
<p>Use ρ = m/V. Rearrange it to m = ρV when finding mass and V = m/ρ when finding volume. Always check that the units are consistent.</p>', 1),
  (654, 654, '<h2>Pressure</h2>

<h3>Learning objective</h3>
<p>Calculate pressure, force or area using the relationship between pressure and the area over which a force acts.</p>

<h3>1. What is pressure?</h3>
<p><b>Pressure</b> is the force acting normally per unit area of a surface.</p>

<p><b>Formula:</b></p>
<p>P = F/A</p>

<p>Where:</p>
<ul>
<li>P is pressure, measured in pascals (Pa).</li>
<li>F is the force acting perpendicular to the surface, measured in newtons (N).</li>
<li>A is the area, measured in square metres (m²).</li>
</ul>

<p>One pascal is one newton per square metre:</p>
<p>1 Pa = 1 N/m².</p>

<p>For the same force, a smaller contact area produces greater pressure. A larger contact area produces lower pressure.</p>

<h3>2. Worked example 1: Calculating pressure</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> A force of 200 N acts normally on an area of 4 m². Calculate the pressure.</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Use P = F/A.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Substitute: P = 200/4.</p>
<p><b>Step 3:</b> Calculate: P = 50 Pa.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> 50 Pa.</p>

<h3>3. Worked example 2: Calculating force</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> A pressure of 3000 Pa acts over an area of 0.2 m². Find the normal force.</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Rearrange P = F/A to obtain F = PA.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Substitute: F = 3000 × 0.2.</p>
<p><b>Step 3:</b> Calculate: F = 600 N.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> 600 N.</p>

<h3>4. Worked example 3: Calculating area</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> A force of 120 N produces a pressure of 600 Pa. Calculate the area.</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Rearrange P = F/A to obtain A = F/P.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Substitute: A = 120/600.</p>
<p><b>Step 3:</b> Calculate: A = 0.2 m².</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> 0.2 m².</p>

<h3>Remember</h3>
<p>Pressure depends on both force and area. For a fixed force, reducing the area increases pressure. Convert areas to m² before calculating pressure in pascals.</p>', 1),
  (655, 655, '<h2>Pressure in Liquids</h2>

<h3>Learning objective</h3>
<p>Explain how liquid pressure changes with depth and calculate the pressure due to a column of liquid.</p>

<h3>1. Why do liquids exert pressure?</h3>
<p>A liquid has weight. The liquid above a point exerts pressure on the liquid below it and on the container walls.</p>

<p>For a liquid of uniform density, the pressure due to the liquid at depth h is:</p>

<p><b>p = ρgh</b></p>

<p>Where:</p>
<ul>
<li>p is the pressure due to the liquid, in pascals (Pa).</li>
<li>ρ is the liquid density, in kg/m³.</li>
<li>g is gravitational field strength, in N/kg.</li>
<li>h is the vertical depth below the liquid surface, in metres (m).</li>
</ul>

<p>This formula gives the <b>gauge pressure</b> due to the liquid. If the liquid surface is exposed to the atmosphere, the absolute pressure also includes atmospheric pressure.</p>

<h3>2. What affects liquid pressure?</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Depth:</b> Pressure increases with depth.</li>
<li><b>Density:</b> At the same depth, a denser liquid produces greater pressure.</li>
<li><b>Gravity:</b> Greater gravitational field strength produces greater liquid pressure.</li>
</ul>

<p>At the same depth in the same stationary liquid, the pressure is the same. The shape of the container does not by itself determine the pressure at that depth.</p>

<h3>3. Worked example 1: Calculating liquid pressure</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> Calculate the pressure due to water at a depth of 2 m. Take water density as 1000 kg/m³ and g = 10 N/kg.</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Use p = ρgh.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Substitute: p = 1000 × 10 × 2.</p>
<p><b>Step 3:</b> Calculate: p = 20,000 Pa.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> 20,000 Pa.</p>

<h3>4. Worked example 2: Comparing depths</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> The pressure due to water at 1 m depth is 10,000 Pa when g = 10 N/kg. Find the pressure at 3 m depth.</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> The liquid and gravitational field are unchanged.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Pressure is directly proportional to depth.</p>
<p><b>Step 3:</b> Tripling the depth triples the pressure: p = 3 × 10,000.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> 30,000 Pa.</p>

<h3>5. Worked example 3: Comparing two liquids</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> At a depth of 0.5 m, a liquid has a density of 800 kg/m³. Calculate its pressure due to the liquid. Take g = 10 N/kg.</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Use p = ρgh.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Substitute: p = 800 × 10 × 0.5.</p>
<p><b>Step 3:</b> Calculate: p = 4000 Pa.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> 4000 Pa.</p>

<h3>Remember</h3>
<p>Use p = ρgh for pressure due to a liquid. Measure depth vertically from the surface and use density in kg/m³, depth in metres and g in N/kg.</p>', 1),
  (656, 656, '<h2>Upthrust and Flotation</h2>

<h3>Learning objective</h3>
<p>Explain upthrust using pressure differences and apply the principle of flotation to determine whether an object sinks, floats or remains suspended.</p>

<h3>1. What is upthrust?</h3>
<p><b>Upthrust</b> is the upward force exerted by a fluid on an object that is partly or completely immersed in it.</p>

<p>In a liquid, pressure increases with depth. The bottom of an immersed object experiences greater pressure than the top, producing a resultant upward force.</p>

<h3>2. Archimedes'' principle</h3>
<p>Archimedes'' principle states that an immersed object experiences an upthrust equal to the weight of the fluid it displaces.</p>

<p><b>Upthrust = weight of displaced fluid</b></p>

<p>U = ρVg</p>

<p>Here, ρ is the fluid density and V is the volume of fluid displaced.</p>

<h3>3. When does an object float?</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Floats at rest:</b> Upthrust balances the object''s weight.</li>
<li><b>Sinks:</b> Its weight exceeds the available upthrust while it descends.</li>
<li><b>Suspended in a fluid:</b> Upthrust equals weight, with the object fully immersed and not rising or sinking.</li>
</ul>

<p>An object can float even if its material is denser than water. A hollow ship displaces a large volume of water, allowing the upthrust to balance its total weight.</p>

<h3>4. Worked example 1: Calculating upthrust</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> An object displaces 0.002 m³ of water. Calculate the upthrust. Take water density as 1000 kg/m³ and g = 10 N/kg.</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Use U = ρVg.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Substitute: U = 1000 × 0.002 × 10.</p>
<p><b>Step 3:</b> Calculate: U = 20 N.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> 20 N.</p>

<h3>5. Worked example 2: Determining whether an object floats at rest</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> An object weighs 15 N and experiences an upthrust of 15 N while floating. What is the resultant vertical force?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Upthrust acts upwards and weight acts downwards.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Resultant force = upthrust − weight.</p>
<p><b>Step 3:</b> Resultant force = 15 − 15 = 0 N.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> The resultant vertical force is zero, so the object can remain at rest.</p>

<h3>6. Worked example 3: Floating object</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> A floating object has a weight of 80 N. What is its upthrust when it is stationary?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> A stationary floating object is in vertical equilibrium.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> The upward upthrust balances the downward weight.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> Upthrust = 80 N.</p>

<h3>Remember</h3>
<p>Upthrust equals the weight of displaced fluid. A floating object at rest has upthrust equal to its weight.</p>', 1),
  (657, 657, '<h2>Viscosity</h2>

<h3>Learning objective</h3>
<p>Explain viscosity as resistance to flow and compare the flow behaviour of different fluids.</p>

<h3>1. What is viscosity?</h3>
<p><b>Viscosity</b> is a measure of a fluid''s resistance to flow. It describes how strongly a fluid resists layers of the fluid moving past one another.</p>

<p>A fluid with high viscosity flows less readily than a fluid with low viscosity under comparable conditions.</p>

<p>For example, honey generally flows more slowly than water because honey has a higher viscosity under ordinary room-temperature conditions.</p>

<h3>2. Comparing viscosity</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>High viscosity:</b> Greater resistance to flow.</li>
<li><b>Low viscosity:</b> Less resistance to flow.</li>
</ul>

<p>When comparing fluids, use similar conditions, especially temperature, because viscosity can change with temperature.</p>

<h3>3. Worked example 1: Comparing two liquids</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> Under the same conditions, honey flows more slowly than water. Which liquid has the higher viscosity?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify the liquid that resists flow more strongly.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Honey flows less readily.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> Honey has the higher viscosity.</p>

<h3>4. Worked example 2: Choosing a low-viscosity fluid</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> Two suitable liquids are available for a process that requires easy flow. Liquid A has lower viscosity than Liquid B. Which liquid should be selected if other relevant properties are equal?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Low viscosity means less resistance to flow.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Liquid A has the lower viscosity.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> Liquid A should flow more readily and is the appropriate choice.</p>

<h3>5. Worked example 3: Interpreting a flow test</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> Equal volumes of two liquids are released through the same suitable apparatus at the same temperature. Liquid X takes longer to flow through than Liquid Y. Which liquid appears more viscous in this test?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Both liquids are tested under the same conditions.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Liquid X flows less readily because it takes longer.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> Liquid X has the higher apparent viscosity in this comparison.</p>

<h3>Remember</h3>
<p>Viscosity measures resistance to flow. Higher viscosity means a fluid flows less readily under comparable conditions; lower viscosity means it flows more readily.</p>', 1),
  (658, 658, '<h2>Structure of the Atom</h2>

<h3>1. The three subatomic particles</h3>
<p>An atom contains a small central nucleus surrounded by electrons.</p>

<ul>
  <li><b>Proton:</b> has a positive charge (+1) and is found in the nucleus.</li>
  <li><b>Neutron:</b> has no charge and is found in the nucleus.</li>
  <li><b>Electron:</b> has a negative charge (-1) and occupies the space around the nucleus.</li>
</ul>

<p>Nearly all the mass of an atom is concentrated in its nucleus because protons and neutrons are much heavier than electrons.</p>

<h3>2. Atomic number</h3>
<p>The atomic number, Z, is the number of protons in the nucleus.</p>
<p>In a neutral atom, the number of electrons equals the number of protons.</p>

<h3>3. Mass number</h3>
<p>The mass number, A, is the total number of protons and neutrons.</p>

<p><b>Mass number = number of protons + number of neutrons</b></p>
<p><b>Number of neutrons = mass number - atomic number</b></p>

<p>An atom can be represented as:</p>
<p><b>ᴬZX</b></p>
<p>X is the chemical symbol, A is the mass number, and Z is the atomic number.</p>

<h3>Worked example 1: Finding the number of neutrons</h3>
<p>An atom has atomic number 11 and mass number 23. Find its number of neutrons.</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Write the relationship.</p>
<p>Neutrons = mass number - atomic number</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Substitute.</p>
<p>Neutrons = 23 - 11</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> 12 neutrons.</p>

<h3>Worked example 2: Counting particles</h3>
<p>A neutral atom has atomic number 8 and mass number 16. Find its protons, electrons and neutrons.</p>
<p>Protons = atomic number = 8.</p>
<p>Electrons = protons because the atom is neutral = 8.</p>
<p>Neutrons = 16 - 8 = 8.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> 8 protons, 8 electrons and 8 neutrons.</p>

<h3>Worked example 3: Interpreting nuclear notation</h3>
<p>An atom is written as ²⁷₁₃Al. Determine its atomic number, mass number and number of neutrons.</p>
<p>Atomic number = 13.</p>
<p>Mass number = 27.</p>
<p>Neutrons = 27 - 13 = 14.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> Atomic number 13, mass number 27, and 14 neutrons.</p>

<h3>Remember</h3>
<p>The atomic number counts protons. The mass number counts protons and neutrons together. For a neutral atom, electrons equal protons.</p>', 1),
  (659, 659, '<h2>Radioactivity and Radioactive Decay</h2>

<h3>1. What is radioactivity?</h3>
<p>Radioactivity is the spontaneous and random emission of radiation from an unstable atomic nucleus.</p>

<p>The nucleus of a radioactive atom is unstable. It changes by emitting radiation and may become a different, more stable nucleus.</p>

<h3>2. Important properties</h3>
<ul>
  <li><b>Spontaneous:</b> decay occurs without an external trigger.</li>
  <li><b>Random:</b> it is impossible to predict exactly when a particular unstable nucleus will decay.</li>
  <li><b>Nuclear:</b> the change takes place in the nucleus, not in the electron arrangement.</li>
</ul>

<p>Radioactive decay is not the same as ordinary chemical change. Chemical reactions mainly involve electrons, while radioactive decay changes the nucleus.</p>

<h3>3. Activity and count rate</h3>
<p><b>Activity</b> is the number of nuclear decays per second. Its SI unit is the becquerel (Bq).</p>
<p>1 Bq = 1 decay per second.</p>
<p><b>Count rate</b> is the number of radiation counts recorded by a detector per unit time. It may be lower than the activity because not every emitted particle or photon is detected.</p>

<h3>Worked example 1: Interpreting activity</h3>
<p>A source has an activity of 250 Bq. What does this mean?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Remember that 1 Bq means one decay per second.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Apply the definition.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> The source undergoes an average of 250 nuclear decays per second.</p>

<h3>Worked example 2: Calculating total decays</h3>
<p>A source has a constant activity of 40 Bq over a short interval of 5 seconds. Estimate the number of decays in that interval.</p>
<p>Number of decays = activity × time.</p>
<p>Number of decays = 40 × 5 = 200.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> Approximately 200 decays.</p>
<p>This estimate assumes the activity remains approximately constant during the interval.</p>

<h3>Worked example 3: Correcting a detector reading</h3>
<p>A detector records 90 counts per minute when measuring a source and 10 counts per minute from background radiation. Find the source''s net count rate.</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify the total measured count rate: 90 counts/min.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Subtract background count rate: 10 counts/min.</p>
<p>Net count rate = 90 - 10 = 80 counts/min.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> 80 counts per minute.</p>

<h3>Remember</h3>
<p>Radioactive decay is spontaneous, random and nuclear. Activity measures decays per second; a detector''s count rate measures recorded counts.</p>', 1),
  (660, 660, '<h2>Alpha, Beta and Gamma Radiation</h2>

<h3>1. Alpha radiation</h3>
<p>An alpha particle consists of two protons and two neutrons. It is the same as a helium nucleus.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Charge: +2</li>
  <li>Relative mass: 4</li>
  <li>Ionising ability: very high</li>
  <li>Penetrating ability: low; it can be stopped by paper or the outer layer of skin.</li>
</ul>
<p>Alpha radiation is particularly dangerous if an alpha-emitting substance enters the body through inhalation, ingestion or a wound.</p>

<h3>2. Beta radiation</h3>
<p>In the usual school-level example of beta-minus decay, a neutron changes into a proton and an electron is emitted. The emitted electron is called a beta-minus particle.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Charge: -1</li>
  <li>Relative mass: approximately 1/1840</li>
  <li>Ionising ability: moderate</li>
  <li>Penetrating ability: greater than alpha; it can be reduced by a thin sheet of aluminium.</li>
</ul>

<h3>3. Gamma radiation</h3>
<p>Gamma radiation is high-energy electromagnetic radiation emitted by an excited nucleus.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Charge: 0</li>
  <li>Rest mass: 0</li>
  <li>Ionising ability: lower than alpha and beta</li>
  <li>Penetrating ability: very high; thick lead or concrete can reduce its intensity.</li>
</ul>

<h3>4. Comparing the three</h3>
<p>Ionising ability: alpha &gt; beta &gt; gamma.</p>
<p>Penetrating ability: gamma &gt; beta &gt; alpha.</p>
<p>All three are ionising radiation, but they interact with matter differently.</p>

<h3>Worked example 1: Choosing a suitable absorber</h3>
<p>A school laboratory needs to reduce a beam of beta radiation. Which material is commonly suitable: paper, thin aluminium, or thick lead?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify the radiation as beta.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Recall that a thin sheet of aluminium can absorb much of the beta radiation.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> Thin aluminium is commonly used.</p>

<h3>Worked example 2: Comparing ionisation</h3>
<p>Arrange alpha, beta and gamma radiation from strongest to weakest ionising ability.</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Alpha particles are relatively massive and carry a +2 charge.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Beta particles carry a charge but have much smaller mass.</p>
<p><b>Step 3:</b> Gamma rays have no charge.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> Alpha, beta, gamma.</p>

<h3>Worked example 3: Identifying a neutral radiation</h3>
<p>Radiation from a source is unaffected by electric and magnetic fields and is highly penetrating. Which type is most likely?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Charged alpha and beta particles can be deflected by electric and magnetic fields.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Gamma radiation has no charge.</p>
<p><b>Step 3:</b> Gamma radiation is also highly penetrating.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> Gamma radiation.</p>

<h3>Remember</h3>
<p>Alpha is strongly ionising but weakly penetrating. Gamma is weakly ionising compared with alpha but highly penetrating. Beta lies between them for these properties.</p>', 1),
  (661, 661, '<h2>Half-Life</h2>

<h3>1. Meaning of half-life</h3>
<p>The half-life of a radioactive isotope is the time taken for half the radioactive nuclei in a sample to decay.</p>
<p>For a sample with a constant half-life, the number of undecayed nuclei halves after each half-life.</p>

<p>The same halving pattern applies to activity, provided the measurement is corrected for background radiation.</p>

<h3>2. The halving pattern</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Initially: 100% remains.</li>
  <li>After one half-life: 50% remains.</li>
  <li>After two half-lives: 25% remains.</li>
  <li>After three half-lives: 12.5% remains.</li>
  <li>After four half-lives: 6.25% remains.</li>
</ul>

<p><b>Number of half-lives = elapsed time ÷ half-life</b></p>

<p>For simple questions:</p>
<p><b>Remaining amount = initial amount × (1/2)<sup>number of half-lives</sup></b></p>

<h3>Worked example 1: Finding the remaining mass</h3>
<p>A radioactive sample has a mass of 80 g. Its half-life is 5 hours. Find the mass of the radioactive isotope remaining after 10 hours.</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Number of half-lives = 10 ÷ 5 = 2.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> After 5 hours: 80 ÷ 2 = 40 g.</p>
<p><b>Step 3:</b> After 10 hours: 40 ÷ 2 = 20 g.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> 20 g remains.</p>

<h3>Worked example 2: Finding elapsed time</h3>
<p>A sample has an initial activity of 1600 Bq. Its activity falls to 200 Bq. If its half-life is 3 days, find the elapsed time.</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> 1600 Bq → 800 Bq after 3 days.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> 800 Bq → 400 Bq after 6 days.</p>
<p><b>Step 3:</b> 400 Bq → 200 Bq after 9 days.</p>
<p>Three half-lives have passed.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> 9 days.</p>

<h3>Worked example 3: Finding the half-life</h3>
<p>A sample''s activity falls from 600 counts per minute to 300 counts per minute in 8 hours. Assume background has been corrected. Find its half-life.</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> The activity has halved from 600 to 300.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> One half-life has therefore passed.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> 8 hours.</p>

<h3>Common mistake</h3>
<p>After two half-lives, 25% remains, not 0%. Radioactive decay reduces the amount in stages; it does not mean the entire sample disappears after one half-life.</p>

<h3>Remember</h3>
<p>Count how many times the amount halves. Then multiply that number of half-lives by the duration of one half-life to find elapsed time.</p>', 1),
  (662, 662, '<h2>Uses and Safety of Radioactivity</h2>

<h3>1. Medical applications</h3>
<ul>
  <li><b>Medical tracers:</b> suitable radioactive isotopes help doctors investigate how organs function.</li>
  <li><b>Cancer treatment:</b> carefully controlled radiation can damage cancer cells.</li>
  <li><b>Sterilisation:</b> radiation can kill microorganisms on suitable medical equipment.</li>
</ul>

<h3>2. Industrial and scientific applications</h3>
<ul>
  <li><b>Thickness monitoring:</b> radiation detectors help monitor the thickness of paper or metal sheets.</li>
  <li><b>Industrial radiography:</b> penetrating radiation can reveal internal defects in some materials.</li>
  <li><b>Dating:</b> suitable radioactive isotopes can help estimate the age of archaeological or geological samples.</li>
</ul>

<h3>3. Radiation exposure and contamination</h3>
<p><b>Irradiation</b> means being exposed to radiation from a source. The radioactive material does not necessarily enter or remain on the person or object.</p>
<p><b>Contamination</b> means unwanted radioactive material gets onto or into a person or object. The material may continue emitting radiation until it is removed or decays.</p>

<h3>4. Basic radiation protection</h3>
<ul>
  <li><b>Reduce time:</b> spend as little time as reasonably possible near a source.</li>
  <li><b>Increase distance:</b> stay farther away where practical.</li>
  <li><b>Use shielding:</b> select shielding appropriate for the type and energy of radiation.</li>
  <li><b>Prevent contamination:</b> use proper containment, protective equipment and handling procedures.</li>
</ul>

<p>Never handle an unknown radioactive source. Laboratory sources must be controlled by trained personnel and according to safety procedures.</p>

<h3>Worked example 1: Choosing a use</h3>
<p>A factory needs to monitor whether a sheet of paper is becoming too thick during production. Which application is suitable?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> The task is to monitor material thickness.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> A suitable radiation source and detector can measure changes in the radiation reaching the detector.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> Radiation thickness monitoring.</p>

<h3>Worked example 2: Irradiation or contamination?</h3>
<p>A sealed source is used to irradiate a medical instrument. The source is removed, and no radioactive material is transferred to the instrument. Is this irradiation or contamination?</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> The instrument was exposed to radiation.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> No radioactive material was transferred onto or into it.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> Irradiation, not contamination.</p>

<h3>Worked example 3: Applying radiation protection</h3>
<p>A trained worker must take a brief reading near a properly shielded radioactive source. State three general ways to reduce exposure.</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Reduce the time spent near the source.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Increase distance where practical.</p>
<p><b>Step 3:</b> Use appropriate shielding and follow the approved procedure.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> Reduce time, increase distance and use suitable shielding.</p>

<h3>Remember</h3>
<p>Radioactivity has useful medical, industrial and scientific applications. Safe use depends on controlling exposure, preventing contamination and following trained procedures.</p>', 1),
  (663, 663, '
  <h2>Meaning of Cathode Rays</h2>

  <p>
    Cathode rays are streams of electrons moving through
    a vacuum in a discharge tube.
  </p>

  <h3>Understanding the concept</h3>

  <p>
    Electrons are tiny particles that carry negative electric
    charge. When they are emitted from the cathode and move
    through the tube, the moving electrons form cathode rays.
  </p>

  <p>
    Cathode rays are therefore not ordinary light rays.
    They consist of moving particles.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> What are cathode rays?</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify the particles involved.</p>
  <p>They are electrons.</p>

  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Describe their movement.</p>
  <p>They move through a vacuum in a discharge tube.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> Cathode rays are streams of electrons
  moving through a vacuum in a discharge tube.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Are cathode rays made of light or
  particles?</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Recall what forms cathode rays.</p>
  <p>They are streams of electrons.</p>

  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Identify the nature of electrons.</p>
  <p>Electrons are particles.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> Cathode rays consist of particles,
  specifically electrons.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> A student says cathode rays are
  streams of protons. Identify the error.</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Recall the particles forming cathode rays.</p>
  <p>They are electrons, not protons.</p>

  <p><b>Step 2:</b> State the correction.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Cathode rays are streams of electrons.</p>
  ', 1),
  (664, 664, '
  <h2>Negative Charge of Cathode Rays</h2>

  <p>
    Cathode rays carry negative electric charge because
    they consist of electrons.
  </p>

  <h3>Understanding the concept</h3>

  <p>
    Each electron carries a negative charge. A stream of
    electrons therefore carries negative charge.
  </p>

  <p>
    The charge of one electron is approximately
    -1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ C.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> What is the charge of cathode rays?</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify their constituent particles.</p>
  <p>Cathode rays consist of electrons.</p>

  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Recall the charge of an electron.</p>
  <p>An electron has a negative charge.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> Cathode rays carry negative charge.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Explain why cathode rays are negatively
  charged.</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Recall that cathode rays are electrons.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Recall that electrons carry negative charge.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> Cathode rays are negatively charged
  because they consist of negatively charged electrons.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> A particle beam is positively charged.
  Can it be a cathode ray beam as defined in this topic?</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Recall the identity of cathode rays.</p>
  <p>They are streams of electrons.</p>

  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Compare the charges.</p>
  <p>Electrons carry negative charge, not positive charge.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> No. Cathode rays consist of negatively
  charged electrons.</p>
  ', 1),
  (665, 665, '
  <h2>Straight-Line Travel of Cathode Rays</h2>

  <p>
    Cathode rays travel in straight lines when they move
    through a uniform vacuum without deflecting fields.
  </p>

  <h3>Understanding the concept</h3>

  <p>
    An object placed in the path of cathode rays can block
    the beam. The region behind the object receives no direct
    rays, producing a shadow on a suitable fluorescent screen.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> State the direction of travel of
  cathode rays in the absence of deflecting fields.</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Recall the observed path of cathode rays.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> State the path.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> They travel in straight lines.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Why can an object produce a shadow
  in the path of cathode rays?</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Cathode rays travel in straight lines.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> The object blocks the rays.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> The region behind the object receives
  no direct rays.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> The object blocks the straight-moving
  cathode rays, producing a shadow.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> A solid object is placed between a
  cathode ray source and a fluorescent screen. What observation
  supports straight-line travel?</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> The object blocks the beam.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> The screen does not glow in the blocked
  region in the same way as the exposed region.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> A shadow forms behind the object.</p>
  ', 1),
  (666, 666, '
  <h2>Cathode Rays as Particles</h2>

  <p>
    Cathode rays can transfer momentum to objects they strike.
    This provides evidence that they consist of particles.
  </p>

  <h3>Understanding the concept</h3>

  <p>
    In a traditional discharge-tube experiment, cathode rays
    can cause a light paddle wheel to move. The moving
    electrons transfer momentum when they interact with the wheel.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> What does the movement of a light
  paddle wheel in a cathode ray tube demonstrate?</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Observe that the wheel moves when struck.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Recognize that momentum is transferred.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Relate momentum transfer to particle behaviour.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> The observation provides evidence that
  cathode rays consist of particles.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> What physical quantity is transferred
  when cathode rays push a paddle wheel?</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify the interaction between the
  electron beam and the wheel.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Identify the quantity responsible for
  the wheel''s motion.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> Momentum is transferred to the wheel.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> A paddle wheel does not move when
  exposed to a cathode ray beam. Does this single observation
  prove that cathode rays are not particles?</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Recall the purpose of the experiment.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Recognize that a lack of visible motion
  does not by itself establish the nature of the beam.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> No. The result alone does not prove that
  cathode rays are not particles.</p>
  ', 1),
  (667, 667, '
  <h2>Function of the Cathode</h2>

  <p>
    The cathode is the negative electrode in a discharge
    tube. In a cathode ray tube, it provides the electrons
    that form the cathode ray beam.
  </p>

  <h3>How it works</h3>

  <p>
    When the electron source is operated under suitable
    conditions, electrons are released from the cathode.
    These electrons can then be accelerated through the tube.
  </p>

  <p>
    The cathode is therefore the source of the electrons
    used to produce cathode rays.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> State the function of the cathode
  in a cathode ray tube.</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify the particles needed to form
  cathode rays.</p>
  <p>They are electrons.</p>

  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Identify the electrode that supplies them.</p>
  <p>The cathode supplies the electrons.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> The cathode provides electrons for
  the formation of cathode rays.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Is the cathode positive or negative?</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Recall the electrode arrangement in
  a cathode ray tube.</p>

  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Identify the cathode''s polarity.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> The cathode is the negative electrode.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> A student says that the cathode
  supplies protons to form cathode rays. Identify the error.</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Recall the particles forming cathode rays.</p>
  <p>They are electrons.</p>

  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Correct the student''s statement.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> The cathode supplies electrons, not
  protons, for the cathode ray beam.</p>
  ', 1),
  (668, 668, '
  <h2>Electron Emission in a Discharge Tube</h2>

  <p>
    Electron emission is the release of electrons from
    a material. In a cathode ray tube, the electron source
    releases electrons that form the beam.
  </p>

  <h3>Understanding electron emission</h3>

  <p>
    Electrons are normally held within the material.
    Under suitable conditions, some electrons can escape
    from the electron source.
  </p>

  <p>
    In a traditional cathode ray tube, the cathode is
    commonly heated so that it releases electrons. This
    process is called thermionic emission.
  </p>

  <p>
    A potential difference then accelerates the emitted
    electrons through the tube.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> What is electron emission?</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify the particle involved.</p>
  <p>The particle is an electron.</p>

  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Describe what happens to the particle.</p>
  <p>It leaves the material from which it was held.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> Electron emission is the release of
  electrons from a material.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> What is thermionic emission?</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify the energy supplied to the cathode.</p>
  <p>The cathode is heated.</p>

  <p><b>Step 2:</b> State the result of heating.</p>
  <p>Electrons gain enough energy to escape from the material.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> Thermionic emission is the release of
  electrons from a material due to heating.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Why must electrons be released from
  the cathode before they can form a cathode ray beam?</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify what forms the beam.</p>
  <p>The beam consists of moving electrons.</p>

  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Determine where the electrons come from.</p>
  <p>They must first leave the electron source.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> Electrons must be released from the
  cathode so that they can be accelerated to form the beam.</p>
  ', 1),
  (669, 669, '
  <h2>Function of the Anode</h2>

  <p>
    The anode is the positive electrode in a cathode ray
    tube. It helps attract and accelerate electrons emitted
    by the cathode.
  </p>

  <h3>Understanding the concept</h3>

  <p>
    Electrons have negative charge. An electric field
    between the cathode and the positively charged anode
    exerts a force on the electrons.
  </p>

  <p>
    The electrons gain kinetic energy as they accelerate
    through the potential difference.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> State the function of the anode in
  a cathode ray tube.</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Recall the charge of the anode.</p>
  <p>It is positive.</p>

  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Identify the effect on electrons.</p>
  <p>The anode attracts electrons and helps accelerate them.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> The anode attracts and helps accelerate
  electrons emitted by the cathode.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Why are electrons attracted towards
  a positively charged anode?</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Electrons carry negative charge.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> The anode carries positive charge.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Opposite electric charges attract.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> Electrons are attracted towards the
  positive anode because opposite charges attract.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> What would happen to the acceleration
  of electrons if the accelerating potential difference
  were increased?</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Recall that a potential difference
  establishes an electric field.</p>

  <p><b>Step 2:</b> A larger accelerating potential
  difference provides a greater energy gain.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> The electrons gain more kinetic energy
  and leave the accelerating region at a higher speed,
  assuming the same initial conditions.</p>
  ', 1),
  (670, 670, '
  <h2>Acceleration of Electrons by Potential Difference</h2>

  <p>
    A potential difference between electrodes transfers
    electrical energy to electrons. The electrons gain
    kinetic energy as they accelerate through the electric field.
  </p>

  <h3>Energy relationship</h3>

  <p>
    For an electron accelerated from rest through a potential
    difference V, the kinetic energy gained is:
  </p>

  <p><b>KE = eV</b></p>

  <p>Where:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>KE is the kinetic energy gained, in joules.</li>
    <li>e is the magnitude of the electron charge,
    1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ C.</li>
    <li>V is the accelerating potential difference, in volts.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    If the electron starts from rest and its speed is much
    less than the speed of light, its kinetic energy is:
  </p>

  <p><b>KE = ½mv²</b></p>

  <p>
    Equating the expressions gives:
  </p>

  <p><b>eV = ½mv²</b></p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Energy gained</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Question:</b> An electron is accelerated from rest
    through a potential difference of 200 V. Calculate
    its kinetic energy gained.
  </p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Write the formula.</p>
  <p>KE = eV</p>

  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Substitute the values.</p>
  <p>KE = (1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹) × 200</p>

  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Calculate.</p>
  <p>KE = 3.2 × 10⁻¹⁷ J</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> 3.2 × 10⁻¹⁷ J</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Comparing energy gains</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Question:</b> Two electrons start from rest. One
    passes through 100 V and the other through 300 V.
    Which gains more kinetic energy?
  </p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Use KE = eV.</p>

  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Calculate the first energy gain.</p>
  <p>KE₁ = (1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹) × 100</p>
  <p>KE₁ = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁷ J</p>

  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Calculate the second energy gain.</p>
  <p>KE₂ = (1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹) × 300</p>
  <p>KE₂ = 4.8 × 10⁻¹⁷ J</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> The electron accelerated through
  300 V gains more kinetic energy.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Finding electron speed</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Question:</b> An electron starts from rest and is
    accelerated through 100 V. Calculate its final speed.
  </p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Equate the energy expressions.</p>
  <p>eV = ½mv²</p>

  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Rearrange to make v the subject.</p>
  <p>v = √(2eV/m)</p>

  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Substitute the values.</p>
  <p>
    v = √[(2 × 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ × 100) /
    (9.11 × 10⁻³¹)]
  </p>

  <p><b>Step 4:</b> Calculate.</p>
  <p>v ≈ 5.9 × 10⁶ m/s</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> Approximately 5.9 × 10⁶ m/s.</p>
  ', 1),
  (671, 671, '
  <h2>Fluorescence Caused by Cathode Rays</h2>

  <p>
    Cathode rays cause certain materials to emit visible
    light when the rays strike them. This effect is called
    fluorescence.
  </p>

  <h3>Understanding the concept</h3>

  <p>
    When fast-moving electrons strike a suitable fluorescent
    material, they transfer energy to it. The material then
    emits visible light.
  </p>

  <p>
    In a cathode ray tube, a fluorescent coating on the screen
    glows where the electron beam strikes it.
  </p>

  <p>
    Example: Zinc sulphide is a material that can fluoresce
    when struck by cathode rays.
  </p>

