  (722, 722, '
  <h2>Meaning of Matter</h2>

  <h3>Core Concept</h3>

  <p><strong>Matter is anything that has mass and occupies space.</strong></p>

  <p>Every object or substance that is matter has two essential characteristics:</p>

  <ol>
    <li><strong>Mass:</strong> Matter has mass. Mass tells us how much matter an object contains. It is commonly measured in grams (g) or kilograms (kg).</li>
    <li><strong>Occupies space:</strong> Matter takes up space. The space occupied by an object or substance is called its volume. Volume may be measured in cubic centimetres (cm³), cubic metres (m³), or litres (L), depending on the quantity and context.</li>
  </ol>

  <p>Both characteristics are necessary when identifying matter. Something that has mass but does not occupy space, or occupies space but has no mass, would not fit the scientific definition of matter.</p>

  <h3>How to Identify Matter</h3>

  <p>When deciding whether something is matter, ask two questions:</p>

  <ol>
    <li>Does it have mass?</li>
    <li>Does it occupy space?</li>
  </ol>

  <p>If both answers are yes, it satisfies the definition of matter.</p>

  <h3>Examples of Matter</h3>

  <p><strong>Water:</strong> Water has mass and occupies space in a container.</p>

  <p><strong>Air:</strong> Air is matter even though it is usually invisible. Air has mass and occupies space. An inflated balloon expands because the air inside it occupies space.</p>

  <p><strong>A stone:</strong> A stone has mass and occupies space. Its mass can be measured using a balance, and its volume can be determined using an appropriate method.</p>

  <p><strong>Cooking oil:</strong> Cooking oil has mass and occupies space in a bottle or measuring container.</p>

  <h3>Things That Are Not Matter</h3>

  <p>Light, heat and sound are forms of energy, not matter. They do not satisfy the definition of matter as something that has mass and occupies space.</p>

  <p>Be careful not to confuse an object with the energy it produces. A glowing bulb is matter, but the light it emits is energy. A ringing bell is matter, but the sound it produces is energy.</p>

  <h3>Worked Examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: Is air matter?</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Check whether air has mass. Air has mass, even though it is difficult to notice without measuring it.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Check whether air occupies space. Air fills the inside of a balloon or container.</p>

  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> Air is matter because it has mass and occupies space.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: A stone has a mass of 250 g. Does this information alone establish that it is matter?</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> The measured mass of 250 g establishes that the stone has mass.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> A stone is a physical object that occupies space.</p>

  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> The stone is matter because it has mass and occupies space. The mass measurement alone demonstrates only one of the two defining characteristics.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: A learner says that light is matter because it comes from a physical bulb. Is the learner correct?</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Separate the source from what it produces. The bulb is a physical object, while the light emitted by it is energy.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Apply the definition of matter to the light itself, not to the bulb.</p>

  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> The learner is incorrect. The bulb is matter, but the light it emits is not matter.</p>

  <h3>Common Errors</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Assuming that invisible substances cannot be matter.</li>
    <li>Thinking that only solids are matter.</li>
    <li>Confusing energy produced by an object with the object itself.</li>
    <li>Using mass alone as the complete definition instead of including occupied space.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 1),
  (723, 723, '
  <h2>Physical Properties</h2>

  <h3>Core Concept</h3>

  <p><strong>A physical property is a characteristic of a substance that can be observed or measured without changing the substance into a different substance.</strong></p>

  <p>Physical properties help us describe, compare and identify substances. Observing or measuring a physical property does not, by itself, involve the formation of a new substance.</p>

  <h3>Common Physical Properties</h3>

  <p><strong>1. Colour:</strong> The colour of a substance, such as the white colour of clean salt crystals.</p>

  <p><strong>2. State:</strong> Whether a substance is solid, liquid or gas under the stated conditions.</p>

  <p><strong>3. Density:</strong> Mass per unit volume. It describes how much mass is contained in a given volume.</p>

  <p><strong>4. Solubility:</strong> The ability of a substance to dissolve in a particular solvent under specified conditions.</p>

  <p><strong>5. Melting point:</strong> The temperature at which a substance changes from solid to liquid under specified pressure.</p>

  <p><strong>6. Boiling point:</strong> The temperature at which a liquid boils under specified pressure.</p>

  <p><strong>7. Electrical conductivity:</strong> The ability of a material to conduct electric current.</p>

  <p><strong>8. Hardness:</strong> A measure of a material''s resistance to scratching or indentation, depending on the test used.</p>

  <p><strong>9. Mass and volume:</strong> These can be measured to describe a sample of matter. They depend on the amount of sample, unlike many characteristic properties used to identify substances.</p>

  <h3>Observing a Property Versus Changing a Substance</h3>

  <p>Observing the colour of copper is a physical observation. Measuring the volume of water is a physical measurement. Dissolving sugar in water is also a physical process because the sugar can be recovered by removing the water under suitable conditions.</p>

  <p>By contrast, a property such as flammability describes how a substance can react chemically. The distinction is whether the characteristic can be observed or measured without changing the substance''s chemical identity.</p>

  <h3>Worked Examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: A learner measures 20 cm³ of water. Is volume a physical property?</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify what is measured. The measurement is the space occupied by the water.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Consider whether measuring the volume creates a new substance. It does not.</p>

  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> Volume is a physical property because it can be measured without changing water into another substance.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: A sample has a mass of 60 g and a volume of 20 cm³. Calculate its density.</strong></p>

  <p>Density = mass ÷ volume</p>

  <p>Density = 60 g ÷ 20 cm³</p>

  <p>Density = 3 g/cm³</p>

  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> The density of the sample is 3 g/cm³. Density is a physical property because it can be determined without changing the sample into a different substance.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: A learner records that a substance is blue and dissolves in water. Identify the physical properties reported.</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Blue describes the substance''s colour.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Dissolves in water describes its solubility in that solvent.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Neither observation requires the substance to become a different substance.</p>

  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> The two physical properties are colour and solubility in water.</p>

  <h3>Common Errors</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Calling a property physical merely because it can be seen. Flammability is a chemical property even though a flame can be seen.</li>
    <li>Confusing solubility with a chemical reaction. Dissolving does not always produce a new substance.</li>
    <li>Giving a numerical measurement without its unit.</li>
    <li>Confusing mass with density. Mass measures the amount of matter in a sample; density compares mass with volume.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 1),
  (724, 724, '
  <h2>Chemical Properties</h2>

  <h3>Core Concept</h3>

  <p><strong>A chemical property describes a substance''s ability to undergo a chemical change that produces one or more different substances.</strong></p>

  <p>A chemical property concerns how a substance behaves when it reacts or undergoes another chemical change. Unlike a physical property, demonstrating a chemical property through a reaction changes the chemical identity of the reacting substance.</p>

  <h3>Common Chemical Properties</h3>

  <p><strong>1. Flammability:</strong> The ability of a substance to burn in a suitable environment. Burning involves a chemical reaction and produces new substances.</p>

  <p><strong>2. Reactivity with oxygen:</strong> The tendency of a substance to react with oxygen. For example, iron can react with oxygen in the presence of water to form rust.</p>

  <p><strong>3. Reactivity with water:</strong> The ability of a substance to react chemically with water. Different substances behave differently when exposed to water.</p>

  <p><strong>4. Reactivity with acids:</strong> The ability of a substance to react with an acid. The products depend on the substances and reaction conditions involved.</p>

  <p><strong>5. Corrosiveness:</strong> The ability of a substance to chemically damage or react with materials it contacts.</p>

  <h3>How to Recognise a Chemical Property</h3>

  <p>Ask whether the property describes a substance''s ability to react and form different substances.</p>

  <p>Flammability is a chemical property because burning produces new substances. The ability of iron to rust is a chemical property because rusting forms substances different from the original iron.</p>

  <p>Colour, density and melting point are physical properties. They can be observed or measured without changing a substance into a different substance.</p>

  <h3>Property Versus Evidence of a Reaction</h3>

  <p><strong>Property:</strong> A substance is flammable. This describes its ability to burn.</p>

  <p><strong>Change:</strong> The substance burns. This is a chemical change.</p>

  <p><strong>Possible evidence:</strong> Heat or light may be released, and new substances may form. However, no single visible sign should be treated as conclusive proof of a chemical reaction in every situation.</p>

  <p>Similarly, the ability of iron to rust is a chemical property. The actual formation of rust is a chemical change.</p>

  <h3>Worked Examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: A learner says that flammability is a physical property because a flame can be observed. Is this correct?</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify what flammability describes: the ability of a substance to burn.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Consider what happens during burning. The reacting substance undergoes a chemical reaction and forms different substances.</p>

  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> Flammability is a chemical property. The visibility of a flame does not make the property physical.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: Iron can react with oxygen and form rust. Identify the chemical property and the chemical change.</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> The ability of iron to react with oxygen describes its reactivity with oxygen.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> The actual formation of rust is the chemical change.</p>

  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> Reactivity with oxygen is the chemical property; rusting is the chemical change.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: A substance has a density of 5 g/cm³ and is flammable. Classify each property and explain why.</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Density can be measured without changing the substance into a different substance.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Flammability describes the substance''s ability to undergo a burning reaction that produces new substances.</p>

  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> Density is a physical property; flammability is a chemical property.</p>

  <h3>Common Errors</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Classifying flammability as physical because a flame is visible.</li>
    <li>Confusing a chemical property with the chemical change that demonstrates it.</li>
    <li>Assuming that every chemical reaction must produce an obvious colour change or bubbles.</li>
    <li>Calling rusting a physical change. Rusting involves a chemical reaction that forms new substances.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 1),
  (725, 725, '
  <h2>Solids</h2>

  <h3>Core Concept</h3>

  <p>A <strong>solid</strong> is a state of matter that has a definite shape and a definite volume.</p>

  <p>A solid maintains its own shape and volume under ordinary conditions. It does not take the shape of its container, although it may be cut, bent, compressed slightly or broken by applying a suitable force.</p>

  <h3>Properties of Solids</h3>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Definite shape:</strong> A solid retains its own shape instead of taking the shape of its container.</li>
    <li><strong>Definite volume:</strong> A solid occupies a particular amount of space.</li>
    <li><strong>Usually difficult to compress:</strong> Applying pressure produces little change in the volume of most solids.</li>
    <li><strong>Particles are closely packed:</strong> The particles are generally close together, with little space between neighbouring particles.</li>
    <li><strong>Particles vibrate about fixed positions:</strong> The particles are not completely motionless. They vibrate around their positions rather than moving freely from place to place.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Why Solids Retain Their Shape</h3>

  <p>The particles in a solid are held in relatively fixed positions by attractive forces between them. They can vibrate, but they do not normally move freely past one another. Consequently, the solid retains its shape.</p>

  <p>The close packing of particles also explains why most solids are difficult to compress: there is little empty space available to reduce.</p>

  <h3>Examples of Solids</h3>

  <p>A stone, a metal spoon, a wooden ruler, a piece of chalk and an ice cube are examples of solids under ordinary conditions.</p>

  <p>Powdered sugar and sand are made of solid particles. A pile of these materials may change shape when poured, but the individual grains remain solid.</p>

  <h3>Worked Examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: A metal block is placed in a rectangular box. Explain why the block does not take the shape of the box.</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the state of the metal block: it is a solid.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Recall that solid particles vibrate about relatively fixed positions.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> These particles do not freely move past one another to fit the shape of the box.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The block retains its own shape because its particles remain in relatively fixed positions.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: A learner presses a solid stone with their hands, but its volume hardly changes. Explain why.</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Most solids have particles packed closely together.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> There is little empty space between the particles.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Pressing the stone cannot reduce that space very much under ordinary conditions.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The stone is difficult to compress because its particles are closely packed.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: A student says that particles in a solid do not move. Correct the statement.</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Particles in a solid occupy relatively fixed positions.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Being in fixed positions does not mean being completely motionless.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> The particles vibrate about their positions.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Particles in a solid vibrate about relatively fixed positions; they are not completely motionless.</p>

  <h3>Common Errors</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Claiming that particles in a solid do not move at all.</li>
    <li>Confusing a pile of solid grains with a liquid merely because the pile changes shape.</li>
    <li>Assuming that every solid is perfectly rigid or impossible to deform.</li>
    <li>Claiming that solids have a definite shape but no definite volume.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 1),
  (726, 726, '
  <h2>Liquids</h2>

  <h3>Core Concept</h3>

  <p>A <strong>liquid</strong> is a state of matter that has a definite volume but does not have a definite shape.</p>

  <p>A liquid takes the shape of the part of its container that it occupies. Its volume remains approximately constant under ordinary conditions, even when it is transferred between containers of different shapes.</p>

  <h3>Properties of Liquids</h3>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Definite volume:</strong> A fixed amount of liquid occupies a definite volume under specified conditions.</li>
    <li><strong>No definite shape:</strong> A liquid takes the shape of its container.</li>
    <li><strong>Ability to flow:</strong> A liquid can flow because its particles can move past one another.</li>
    <li><strong>Particles are close together:</strong> Liquid particles remain relatively close together.</li>
    <li><strong>Relatively difficult to compress:</strong> There is little empty space between the particles compared with a gas.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Why Liquids Flow</h3>

  <p>The particles in a liquid are close together but are not held in fixed positions in the same way as particles in a solid. They can move past one another.</p>

  <p>This movement allows a liquid to flow and change shape. However, the particles remain close together, so the liquid maintains a definite volume under ordinary conditions.</p>

  <h3>Examples of Liquids</h3>

  <p>Water, cooking oil, milk and liquid ethanol are examples of liquids under ordinary conditions.</p>

  <h3>Worked Examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: A learner pours 100 cm³ of water from a tall measuring cylinder into a wider container. Explain what happens to its shape and volume.</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Water is a liquid.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> A liquid takes the shape of its container, so the water changes shape.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Transferring the water does not normally change the amount of water. Its volume remains approximately 100 cm³, assuming none is lost and conditions remain unchanged.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The water takes the shape of the wider container while retaining approximately the same volume.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: Explain why cooking oil flows when a container is tilted.</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Cooking oil is a liquid.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Its particles can move past one another.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> This movement allows the oil to flow under gravity.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Cooking oil flows because its particles can move past one another.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: A learner claims that a liquid has no definite volume because it has no definite shape. Explain the error.</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Shape and volume describe different characteristics.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> A liquid changes shape to fit its container.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> The same amount of liquid retains approximately the same volume under ordinary conditions.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The claim is incorrect. A liquid has no definite shape but does have a definite volume.</p>

  <h3>Common Errors</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Claiming that liquids have neither a definite shape nor a definite volume.</li>
    <li>Assuming that liquids flow because their particles are very far apart.</li>
    <li>Confusing the volume of a liquid with the shape of its container.</li>
    <li>Assuming that transferring a liquid automatically changes its volume.</li>
  </ul>
