  (727, 727, '
  <h2>Gases</h2>

  <h3>Core Concept</h3>

  <p>A <strong>gas</strong> is a state of matter that has neither a definite shape nor a definite volume.</p>

  <p>A gas spreads to fill the available space in a container. Its volume depends on the space available and on conditions such as temperature and pressure.</p>

  <h3>Properties of Gases</h3>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>No definite shape:</strong> A gas takes the shape of its container.</li>
    <li><strong>No definite volume:</strong> A gas expands to fill the available space.</li>
    <li><strong>Compressibility:</strong> A gas can be compressed considerably because its particles are widely spaced compared with particles in solids and liquids.</li>
    <li><strong>Ability to spread:</strong> Gas particles move freely and spread throughout the available space.</li>
    <li><strong>Low density compared with many liquids and solids:</strong> For a given substance under suitable conditions, the gaseous state is generally much less dense than its liquid or solid state.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Why Gases Fill Containers</h3>

  <p>Gas particles are widely separated and move freely in different directions. They spread throughout the space available to them rather than remaining in one fixed region.</p>

  <p>Because there is substantial space between gas particles, a gas can be compressed into a smaller volume by applying pressure. Its volume is not fixed independently of the container.</p>

  <h3>Examples of Gases</h3>

  <p>Air, oxygen, nitrogen and carbon dioxide are gases under ordinary conditions.</p>

  <h3>Worked Examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: Air is released into an empty, sealed container. Explain why it spreads throughout the container.</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Air is a mixture of gases.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Gas particles move freely in different directions.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Their movement spreads the gas through the available space.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Air spreads throughout the container because its particles move freely and occupy the available space.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: A gas occupies 600 cm³ in a syringe. The nozzle is sealed, and the piston is pushed so that the gas occupies 300 cm³. Calculate the reduction in volume.</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Initial volume = 600 cm³.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Final volume = 300 cm³.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Reduction = initial volume − final volume.</p>

  <p>Reduction = 600 − 300 = 300 cm³.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The volume decreases by 300 cm³. The gas has been compressed to half its original volume.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: A learner claims that a gas has a fixed volume because it is inside a bottle. Explain the mistake.</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> The bottle limits the space available to the gas.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> The gas occupies that available space.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> If the gas is transferred to a larger suitable container, it can expand to occupy more space.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The gas does not have a definite volume of its own. Its volume depends on the available space and the conditions.</p>

  <h3>Common Errors</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Claiming that a gas has a definite volume because it is contained in a bottle.</li>
    <li>Assuming that gas particles are motionless.</li>
    <li>Thinking that compressing a gas destroys its particles.</li>
    <li>Confusing the volume of a container with the volume a gas would occupy if more space were available.</li>
  </ul>
