  <h2>Particle Arrangement</h2>

  <h3>Core Concept</h3>

  <p><strong>Particle arrangement</strong> describes how the particles of a substance are positioned relative to one another.</p>

  <p>The arrangement differs among solids, liquids and gases. These differences help explain the different properties of the three states of matter.</p>

  <h3>Arrangement in a Solid</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Particles are closely packed.</li>
    <li>They occupy relatively fixed positions.</li>
    <li>In a crystalline solid, particles often form a regular, repeating arrangement.</li>
    <li>In other solids, the arrangement may not be regular, but the particles remain closely packed.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>The close arrangement leaves little space between particles and helps a solid maintain its shape and volume.</p>

  <h3>Arrangement in a Liquid</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Particles remain relatively close together.</li>
    <li>They are not held in the same fixed positions as particles in a solid.</li>
    <li>Their arrangement can change as particles move past one another.</li>
    <li>They do not form one rigid arrangement that maintains a definite shape.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>The close spacing helps a liquid retain a definite volume, while the changeable arrangement allows it to take the shape of its container.</p>

  <h3>Arrangement in a Gas</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Particles are widely separated compared with particles in solids and liquids.</li>
    <li>There is substantial space between neighbouring particles.</li>
    <li>Particles are distributed throughout the available space.</li>
    <li>They do not form a closely packed structure.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>The widely spaced arrangement helps explain why gases can be compressed and spread throughout a container.</p>

  <h3>Comparing the Arrangements</h3>

  <p><strong>Solid:</strong> Closely packed, relatively fixed arrangement.</p>

  <p><strong>Liquid:</strong> Closely packed but changeable arrangement.</p>

  <p><strong>Gas:</strong> Widely spaced arrangement with particles distributed throughout the available space.</p>

  <p>These are general particle-model descriptions. Actual arrangements vary among substances, especially among different types of solids.</p>

  <h3>Worked Examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: A particle diagram shows particles arranged in regular rows with very little space between them. Which state is most likely represented?</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Observe that the particles are closely packed.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Observe that the arrangement is regular.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Compare these features with the particle arrangement in a solid.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> A solid is most likely represented.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: A diagram shows particles close together but arranged irregularly, with no fixed repeating pattern. Which state could it represent?</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> The particles are close together.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Their arrangement is not a rigid, repeating pattern.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> This is consistent with a liquid, whose particles remain close together but can move past one another.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The diagram could represent a liquid. Particle arrangement should be interpreted together with other evidence because not every solid has a regular arrangement.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: A diagram shows particles spread far apart throughout a box. Explain what this suggests about the state represented.</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the large spaces between the particles.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Compare this spacing with the closely packed particles in solids and liquids.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Recognise that widely spaced particles are characteristic of a gas.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The diagram represents a gas because its particles are widely separated and spread throughout the available space.</p>

  <h3>Common Errors</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Drawing particles in a solid with large gaps between them.</li>
    <li>Drawing liquid particles as widely separated as gas particles.</li>
    <li>Assuming that every solid must have perfectly regular particle rows.</li>
    <li>Confusing particle arrangement with particle movement. They are related but describe different features.</li>
  </ul>
