  <h2>Particle Movement</h2>

  <h3>Core Concept</h3>

  <p><strong>Particle movement</strong> describes how the particles of a substance move. In the particle model, particles are not generally treated as completely motionless.</p>

  <p>The type and freedom of movement differ among solids, liquids and gases. These differences help explain why the states behave differently.</p>

  <h3>Movement in Solids</h3>

  <p>Particles in a solid vibrate about relatively fixed positions. They do not normally move freely from one position to another.</p>

  <p>This restricted movement helps the solid retain its shape. The particles can vibrate even when the solid appears completely still to an observer.</p>

  <h3>Movement in Liquids</h3>

  <p>Particles in a liquid move around and can slide past one another. They remain relatively close together but are not restricted to the same fixed positions as particles in a solid.</p>

  <p>This movement allows a liquid to flow and change shape while maintaining a definite volume under ordinary conditions.</p>

  <h3>Movement in Gases</h3>

  <p>Gas particles move freely and rapidly in different directions. They travel through the available space and collide with other particles and the walls of their container.</p>

  <p>Their movement helps explain why a gas spreads throughout a container and why it exerts pressure on the container walls.</p>

  <h3>Comparing Particle Movement</h3>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Solid:</strong> Particles vibrate about relatively fixed positions.</li>
    <li><strong>Liquid:</strong> Particles move past one another while remaining relatively close together.</li>
    <li><strong>Gas:</strong> Particles move freely in different directions through the available space.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Do not confuse particle movement with particle arrangement. Arrangement describes where particles are positioned relative to one another; movement describes how they move.</p>

  <h3>Worked Examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: A metal spoon remains still on a table. Does this mean its particles have stopped moving?</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Distinguish the movement of the spoon as a whole from the movement of its particles.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> A solid''s particles vibrate about relatively fixed positions.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> The spoon can appear still even though its particles continue vibrating.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> No. The spoon can remain still as an object while its particles vibrate about relatively fixed positions.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: Explain why water flows when poured from a jug.</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Water is a liquid.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Its particles can move past one another.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> This movement allows the water to change shape and flow out of the jug.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Water flows because its particles can move past one another.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: A drop of perfume released in a room can eventually be detected farther away. Explain the particle movement involved.</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Some perfume particles enter the air as a gas.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Gas particles move freely in different directions.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> The perfume particles spread through the air, allowing the smell to reach other parts of the room.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The perfume particles spread through the air because gas particles move freely in different directions. This spreading is called diffusion.</p>

  <h3>Common Errors</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Assuming that solid particles do not move.</li>
    <li>Claiming that liquid particles remain fixed in position.</li>
    <li>Claiming that gas particles all travel in one direction continuously.</li>
    <li>Confusing the movement of an entire object with the movement of its particles.</li>
  </ul>
