  <h2>Effect of Temperature on Particles</h2>

  <h3>Core Concept</h3>

  <p><strong>Temperature is related to the average kinetic energy of the particles in a substance.</strong> Kinetic energy is the energy a particle has because of its motion.</p>

  <p>For the same substance in the same state, increasing its temperature generally increases the average kinetic energy of its particles. Decreasing its temperature generally decreases their average kinetic energy.</p>

  <h3>When Temperature Increases</h3>

  <p>When a substance is heated without undergoing a change of state, its particles generally gain average kinetic energy and move more vigorously.</p>

  <ul>
    <li>In a solid, particles vibrate more vigorously about their positions.</li>
    <li>In a liquid, particles generally move around more vigorously.</li>
    <li>In a gas, particles generally move faster on average.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Do not assume that heating always makes particles move from one place to another. Solid particles generally vibrate more vigorously while remaining around their positions.</p>

  <h3>When Temperature Decreases</h3>

  <p>When a substance is cooled without undergoing a change of state, its particles generally lose average kinetic energy and move less vigorously.</p>

  <ul>
    <li>In a solid, particles vibrate less vigorously.</li>
    <li>In a liquid, particles generally move around less vigorously.</li>
    <li>In a gas, particles generally move more slowly on average.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Temperature and Average Kinetic Energy</h3>

  <p>Temperature is related to the average kinetic energy of particles. For gases, the average translational kinetic energy is directly proportional to absolute temperature measured in kelvin.</p>

  <p>For a gas whose particles have only the usual three translational degrees of freedom, the relationship is:</p>

  <p>Average translational kinetic energy = (3/2)kT</p>

  <p>Here, k is the Boltzmann constant and T is the absolute temperature in kelvin. This formula applies to the average translational kinetic energy of an ideal-gas particle; it is not a formula for the total energy of every substance in every state.</p>

  <h3>Important Distinction</h3>

  <p>Temperature and heat are not the same thing. Temperature describes the thermal state of a substance and is related to average particle kinetic energy. Heating is a process in which energy is transferred because of a temperature difference.</p>

  <p>For this screen, focus on how temperature affects particle motion. The detailed processes in which substances change state are covered in the later changes-of-state batches.</p>

  <h3>Worked Examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: A solid is heated, but it remains a solid. Explain how its particles are affected.</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> The temperature of the solid increases.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Its particles generally gain average kinetic energy.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> The particles vibrate more vigorously about their relatively fixed positions.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The particles generally gain average kinetic energy and vibrate more vigorously.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: A gas is cooled while remaining gaseous. Explain the expected effect on its particles.</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> The temperature decreases.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> The average kinetic energy of the gas particles decreases.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> The particles move more slowly on average.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The gas particles have lower average kinetic energy and move more slowly on average.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: The absolute temperature of an ideal gas increases from 200 K to 400 K. How does its average translational kinetic energy change?</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> For an ideal gas, average translational kinetic energy is proportional to absolute temperature.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Calculate the temperature ratio: 400 K ÷ 200 K = 2.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> The absolute temperature doubles, so the average translational kinetic energy also doubles, provided the ideal-gas model applies.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The average translational kinetic energy doubles.</p>

  <h3>Common Errors</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Claiming that heating always causes particles to move freely from place to place, including in solids.</li>
    <li>Confusing temperature with the total amount of energy in a sample.</li>
    <li>Assuming that every particle has exactly the same kinetic energy.</li>
    <li>Using Celsius temperature directly in the ideal-gas kinetic-energy proportionality, which requires absolute temperature in kelvin.</li>
  </ul>
