  <h2>Melting</h2>

  <h3>Core Concept</h3>

  <p><strong>Melting is the change of state in which a solid changes into a liquid when it gains sufficient thermal energy.</strong></p>

  <p>For a pure substance at a given pressure, melting occurs at a characteristic temperature called its melting point.</p>

  <h3>What Happens During Melting?</h3>

  <ol>
    <li>The solid absorbs thermal energy from its surroundings.</li>
    <li>Before melting begins, the particles generally gain kinetic energy and vibrate more vigorously as the solid warms.</li>
    <li>At the melting point, the energy supplied helps overcome some of the forces holding the particles in their fixed arrangement.</li>
    <li>The particles become able to move past one another as the substance becomes liquid.</li>
  </ol>

  <p>During the melting of a pure crystalline substance at constant pressure, the temperature remains constant while the solid and liquid coexist, provided the substance is melting under equilibrium conditions. The energy supplied during this period is used for the change of state rather than increasing the temperature.</p>

  <h3>Melting Point</h3>

  <p>The <strong>melting point</strong> is the temperature at which a solid changes into a liquid under specified pressure.</p>

  <p>For example, pure ice melts at approximately 0°C at standard atmospheric pressure.</p>

  <p>Different substances have different melting points. This makes melting point useful when identifying and checking the purity of substances, although the measurement must be interpreted under appropriate conditions.</p>

  <h3>Worked Examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: Ice at 0°C is supplied with energy and changes into liquid water. Name the process and explain what happens.</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the initial state: ice is solid water.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Identify the final state: liquid water.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> A change from solid to liquid is melting.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Melting occurs. The ice absorbs energy and changes from a solid into liquid water.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: A pure substance melts at 80°C under a specified pressure. What does this tell us?</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the measured temperature at which the solid becomes liquid.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Compare it with the definition of melting point.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The melting point of the substance under the stated pressure is 80°C. This value may help identify the substance when compared with reliable reference data.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: A pure crystalline solid is melting at a constant pressure. Energy continues to be supplied, but its temperature remains constant. Explain why.</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Recognise that the substance is undergoing a change of state.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> The supplied energy is used to overcome forces maintaining the solid arrangement.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Under equilibrium melting conditions, the energy does not raise the temperature while both solid and liquid coexist.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The energy supplied is used for the change from solid to liquid rather than increasing the temperature during the melting stage.</p>

  <h3>Common Errors</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Confusing melting with boiling. Melting changes a solid into a liquid.</li>
    <li>Assuming every substance melts at 0°C. That temperature is the melting point of pure ice at standard atmospheric pressure.</li>
    <li>Claiming that particles stop moving during melting.</li>
    <li>Assuming the temperature of a pure crystalline substance must rise continuously throughout melting.</li>
  </ul>
