  <h2>Freezing</h2>

  <h3>Core Concept</h3>

  <p><strong>Freezing is the change of state in which a liquid changes into a solid when it loses sufficient thermal energy.</strong></p>

  <p>For a pure substance at a given pressure, freezing occurs at its characteristic freezing point. For a pure substance under the same pressure conditions, the freezing point is the same temperature as its melting point.</p>

  <h3>What Happens During Freezing?</h3>

  <ol>
    <li>The liquid transfers thermal energy to cooler surroundings.</li>
    <li>Before freezing begins, the particles generally lose kinetic energy as the liquid cools.</li>
    <li>At the freezing point, the particles form a solid arrangement as the substance changes state.</li>
    <li>The particles in the solid occupy relatively fixed positions and vibrate about those positions.</li>
  </ol>

  <p>During the freezing of a pure substance at constant pressure, the temperature remains constant while the liquid and solid coexist under equilibrium conditions. Energy is released to the surroundings during the change of state.</p>

  <h3>Freezing Point</h3>

  <p>The <strong>freezing point</strong> is the temperature at which a liquid changes into a solid under specified pressure.</p>

  <p>Pure water freezes at approximately 0°C at standard atmospheric pressure. Other substances have different freezing points.</p>

  <p>Freezing is a physical change: the substance changes state without necessarily changing its chemical identity. When liquid water freezes, it forms solid ice, and both consist of water molecules.</p>

  <h3>Worked Examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: Liquid water is cooled and becomes ice. Name the process and explain why it occurs.</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the initial state: liquid water.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Identify the final state: solid ice.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> A change from liquid to solid is freezing.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Freezing occurs because water loses thermal energy and changes from a liquid into a solid.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: A pure liquid freezes at 35°C under a specified pressure. What is its freezing point under those conditions?</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the temperature at which the liquid changes into a solid.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Apply the definition of freezing point.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Its freezing point is 35°C under the stated pressure.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: A pure substance is freezing at constant pressure. Its temperature remains constant even though it continues to lose energy. Explain why.</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Recognise that the substance is changing from liquid to solid.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> During the change of state, particles form the solid arrangement.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Energy is released to the surroundings while the substance freezes.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The substance releases energy during freezing. Under equilibrium conditions, its temperature remains constant while liquid and solid coexist.</p>

  <h3>Common Errors</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Confusing freezing with melting. Freezing changes a liquid into a solid.</li>
    <li>Assuming all substances freeze at 0°C.</li>
    <li>Claiming that particles stop moving completely when a liquid freezes.</li>
    <li>Assuming that freezing changes water into a different chemical substance.</li>
  </ul>
