  <h2>Evaporation</h2>

  <h3>Core Concept</h3>

  <p><strong>Evaporation is the process in which particles escape from the surface of a liquid and enter the gaseous state.</strong></p>

  <p>Evaporation occurs at the surface of a liquid and can take place at temperatures below the liquid''s boiling point.</p>

  <h3>How Evaporation Occurs</h3>

  <ol>
    <li>Liquid particles have a range of kinetic energies.</li>
    <li>Some particles at the surface have enough energy to overcome the attractive forces holding them in the liquid.</li>
    <li>These particles escape from the surface and become gas particles.</li>
    <li>The liquid gradually loses particles to the surrounding gas.</li>
  </ol>

  <p>Evaporation does not require the whole liquid to reach its boiling point. It can occur from an exposed liquid surface at ordinary temperatures.</p>

  <h3>Factors Affecting the Rate of Evaporation</h3>

  <p><strong>Temperature:</strong> Increasing the temperature generally increases the rate of evaporation because a greater proportion of liquid particles can escape from the surface.</p>

  <p><strong>Surface area:</strong> A larger exposed surface generally allows more particles to escape at the same time, increasing the rate of evaporation.</p>

  <p><strong>Air movement:</strong> Moving air generally increases evaporation by carrying away vapour near the liquid''s surface.</p>

  <p><strong>Humidity:</strong> Lower humidity generally favours faster evaporation because the surrounding air contains less water vapour. Higher humidity generally slows the net evaporation of water under otherwise similar conditions.</p>

  <h3>Evaporation and Cooling</h3>

  <p>When the higher-energy particles escape from a liquid, the particles left behind have lower average kinetic energy. As a result, evaporation can cool the remaining liquid and its surroundings.</p>

  <p>For example, sweat evaporating from the skin removes energy and helps cool the body.</p>

  <h3>Worked Examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: Wet clothes dry in the sun even though the water does not boil. Explain why.</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Water is present as a liquid on the clothes.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Some surface water particles gain enough energy to escape into the air.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> This process occurs below the boiling point and is called evaporation.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The clothes dry because surface water evaporates into the air. Boiling is not necessary.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: Equal amounts of water are placed in a wide, shallow dish and a narrow, deep container. Under otherwise similar conditions, which is likely to evaporate faster?</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> The wide, shallow dish exposes a larger liquid surface.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> More surface particles are exposed to the air at the same time.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> A larger exposed surface generally increases the evaporation rate.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The water in the wide, shallow dish is likely to evaporate faster, assuming other conditions are similar.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: Explain why sweat evaporating from the skin can cool the body.</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Some higher-energy water particles escape from the sweat into the air.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Energy is carried away with the escaping particles.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> The skin and remaining liquid lose thermal energy.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Evaporation removes energy from the skin and sweat, helping cool the body.</p>

  <h3>Common Errors</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Claiming that evaporation occurs only at the boiling point.</li>
    <li>Claiming that evaporation occurs throughout the entire liquid rather than at its surface.</li>
    <li>Assuming that every liquid particle has enough energy to escape at the same time.</li>
    <li>Confusing evaporation with boiling, which involves vapour formation throughout the liquid.</li>
  </ul>
  
<hr>

  <h2>Evaporation</h2>

  <h3>Meaning of Evaporation</h3>

  <p>
    <strong>Evaporation</strong> is the process by which particles escape
    from the surface of a liquid and enter the gaseous state.
  </p>

  <p>
    In separation, evaporation can be used to recover a dissolved solid
    from a solution when the liquid solvent is allowed to escape as vapour.
  </p>

  <h3>Using Evaporation to Separate a Dissolved Solid</h3>

  <p>
    Consider salt dissolved in water. The salt is distributed throughout
    the solution. When the solution is heated in a suitable evaporating
    dish, water escapes as vapour and the solution becomes more concentrated.
    If sufficient water is removed, solid salt remains.
  </p>

  <ol>
    <li>Place the solution in an evaporating dish.</li>
    <li>Heat gently when appropriate, using suitable laboratory precautions.</li>
    <li>Allow the solvent to escape as vapour.</li>
    <li>Stop heating at an appropriate point and allow the dish to cool safely.</li>
    <li>Observe the solid remaining in the dish.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>What Determines Whether Evaporation Is Suitable?</h3>

  <p>
    Evaporation is useful when the aim is to recover a dissolved solid
    and the solvent can be allowed to escape. The method is unsuitable
    when the dissolved substance would be damaged by the heating conditions
    or when recovering the solvent is also required.
  </p>

  <p>
    Evaporation does not mean that the solvent disappears. The solvent
    changes into vapour and enters the surrounding air unless it is
    collected by suitable equipment.
  </p>

  <h3>Factors Affecting the Rate of Evaporation</h3>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Temperature:</strong> higher temperature generally increases the rate of evaporation.</li>
    <li><strong>Surface area:</strong> a larger exposed surface generally increases the rate.</li>
    <li><strong>Air movement:</strong> moving air generally increases the rate by carrying vapour away.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Worked Examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: Recovering salt from salt solution</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> How can solid salt be obtained from a solution of salt in water if collecting the water is not necessary?</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Place the salt solution in an evaporating dish.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Heat gently so that water escapes as vapour.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Continue under suitable conditions until the water has been removed sufficiently to leave solid salt.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Use evaporation to remove the water and recover the salt.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: Comparing two exposed dishes</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Equal amounts of water are placed in a wide dish and a narrow dish under the same conditions. Which is expected to evaporate faster?</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> The wide dish exposes a larger surface area of water.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> More liquid particles are exposed at the surface at a given time.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> The wider dish is therefore expected to lose water faster, if the other conditions are comparable.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The wide dish is expected to evaporate faster because it has a larger exposed surface area.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: Choosing a method when only the solid is needed</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> A learner needs solid salt from a salt solution but does not need to collect the water. Explain why evaporation is suitable.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Salt is dissolved in the water, so ordinary filtration will not retain it.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Water can escape as vapour when the solution evaporates.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Removing the water leaves the dissolved salt as a solid.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Evaporation is suitable because it removes the solvent and leaves the dissolved salt behind.</p>

  <h3>Evaporation and Recovery of the Solvent</h3>

  <p>
    During ordinary evaporation, the solvent is not normally collected.
    If both the dissolved solid and the liquid solvent must be recovered,
    evaporation alone is not sufficient because the escaping vapour is
    not collected in the usual open-dish arrangement.
  </p>
