  <h2>Boiling</h2>

  <h3>Core Concept</h3>

  <p><strong>Boiling is the rapid vaporisation of a liquid that occurs throughout the liquid when its vapour pressure equals the external pressure.</strong></p>

  <p>At a given external pressure, a pure liquid has a characteristic boiling point. At the boiling point, bubbles of vapour can form within the liquid and rise to the surface.</p>

  <h3>What Happens During Boiling?</h3>

  <ol>
    <li>The liquid receives thermal energy from its surroundings.</li>
    <li>As the liquid warms, its temperature generally increases.</li>
    <li>At the boiling point for the external pressure, vapour bubbles can form throughout the liquid.</li>
    <li>The bubbles rise and escape at the surface, converting liquid into gas.</li>
  </ol>

  <p>During the boiling of a pure liquid at constant external pressure, its temperature remains constant while the liquid and vapour coexist under equilibrium boiling conditions. The energy supplied is used for vaporisation during this period.</p>

  <h3>Boiling Point</h3>

  <p>The <strong>boiling point</strong> is the temperature at which a liquid boils under a specified external pressure.</p>

  <p>Pure water boils at approximately 100°C at standard atmospheric pressure. At lower external pressure, water boils at a lower temperature; at higher external pressure, it boils at a higher temperature.</p>

  <h3>Boiling Compared with Evaporation</h3>

  <p><strong>Boiling:</strong> Vapour bubbles form throughout the liquid, and the process occurs at the boiling point for the external pressure.</p>

  <p><strong>Evaporation:</strong> Particles escape from the liquid''s surface and evaporation can occur below the boiling point.</p>

  <p>Both processes convert a liquid into gas, but they differ in where vaporisation occurs and the conditions under which it takes place.</p>

  <h3>Worked Examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: Water is heated at standard atmospheric pressure until bubbles form throughout the liquid. The temperature is approximately 100°C. Identify the process and explain the observation.</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the starting state as liquid water.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Observe that vapour bubbles form throughout the liquid.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> At standard atmospheric pressure, pure water boils at approximately 100°C.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The process is boiling. Vapour bubbles form throughout the water as it changes into gas.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: A pure liquid boils at 78°C under a specified pressure. What is its boiling point under those conditions?</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the temperature at which the liquid boils.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Apply the definition of boiling point.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Its boiling point under the stated pressure is 78°C.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: A pure liquid is boiling at constant external pressure. Energy is supplied, but its temperature remains constant. Explain why.</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Recognise that the liquid is undergoing vaporisation.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> The energy supplied is used to convert liquid into vapour.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Under equilibrium boiling conditions, the temperature remains constant while liquid and vapour coexist.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The supplied energy is used for vaporisation rather than raising the temperature during the boiling stage.</p>

  <h3>Common Errors</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Claiming that boiling occurs only at the liquid''s surface.</li>
    <li>Assuming every liquid boils at 100°C.</li>
    <li>Claiming that evaporation and boiling are identical processes.</li>
    <li>Ignoring the effect of external pressure on boiling point.</li>
  </ul>
