  <h2>Condensation</h2>

  <h3>Meaning of Condensation</h3>

  <p>
    <strong>Condensation</strong> is the change of state in which a gas
    changes into a liquid. It occurs when the gas loses thermal energy
    to its surroundings under suitable conditions.
  </p>

  <p>
    During condensation, the particles lose kinetic energy on average.
    Their movement becomes less vigorous, allowing attractive forces
    between the particles to bring them closer together in the liquid state.
  </p>

  <p>
    The substance remains the same during condensation. Only its physical
    state changes. For example, water vapour condenses to form liquid water.
  </p>

  <h3>How Condensation Occurs</h3>

  <ol>
    <li>A substance is present as a gas.</li>
    <li>The gas transfers thermal energy to its surroundings.</li>
    <li>The particles lose kinetic energy on average.</li>
    <li>Under suitable conditions, the particles come closer together.</li>
    <li>The gas changes into a liquid.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Water Droplets on a Cold Glass</h3>

  <p><strong>Observation:</strong> A cold glass of water develops droplets
  on its outside, even though no water has been poured onto the outside.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the source of the water.</p>
  <p>
    Water vapour is present in the air surrounding the glass.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Identify what happens to the water vapour.</p>
  <p>
    The water vapour transfers thermal energy to the cold glass and
    the nearby surroundings.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Identify the state change.</p>
  <p>
    The water vapour changes from a gas into liquid water droplets.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> The droplets form through condensation
  of water vapour from the air, not because water passes through the glass.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Droplets on a Bathroom Mirror</h3>

  <p><strong>Observation:</strong> A mirror becomes covered with tiny
  water droplets after a hot shower.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Warm air in the bathroom contains water vapour.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> The mirror is cooler than the warm, moist air.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Water vapour loses thermal energy near the
  cooler surface and changes into liquid water.</p>

  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> The droplets on the mirror are formed
  by condensation.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Formation of Clouds</h3>

  <p><strong>Observation:</strong> Tiny water droplets form in parts of
  the atmosphere.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Water is present in the atmosphere as water vapour.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> When moist air cools sufficiently, water vapour
  can condense.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> The vapour changes into tiny liquid water
  droplets that can form clouds.</p>

  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> Condensation changes atmospheric water
  vapour into liquid droplets. Cloud formation can involve additional
  atmospheric processes, but the state change described here is condensation.</p>

  <h3>Important Points</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Condensation is a change from gas to liquid.</li>
    <li>The gas loses thermal energy to its surroundings.</li>
    <li>The particles lose kinetic energy on average and move closer together.</li>
    <li>Condensation does not produce a new substance.</li>
    <li>A cool surface can encourage condensation when water vapour
    comes into contact with it.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Diagnostic Questions</h3>

  <p>Answer each question in your own words. Explain your reasoning,
  not just the name of the process.</p>
