  <h2>Sublimation</h2>

  <h3>Meaning of Sublimation</h3>

  <p>
    <strong>Sublimation</strong> is the change of state in which a solid
    changes directly into a gas without first becoming a liquid.
  </p>

  <p>
    During sublimation, particles in the solid gain enough energy to
    escape directly into the gas state under suitable conditions.
    The solid-to-gas change does not pass through the liquid state.
  </p>

  <p>
    Sublimation is a physical change. The substance itself does not
    become a different chemical substance merely because it changes state.
  </p>

  <h3>How Sublimation Occurs</h3>

  <ol>
    <li>The substance begins in the solid state.</li>
    <li>The solid gains thermal energy or is exposed to conditions
    that allow direct transfer into the gas state.</li>
    <li>Some particles escape from the solid directly into the gas state.</li>
    <li>The substance changes from solid to gas without becoming liquid first.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Dry Ice</h3>

  <p><strong>Observation:</strong> Solid dry ice appears to shrink while
  producing a visible mist in the surrounding air.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the substance.</p>
  <p>
    Dry ice is solid carbon dioxide.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Identify the state change.</p>
  <p>
    Under ordinary atmospheric pressure, solid carbon dioxide changes
    directly into gaseous carbon dioxide rather than melting into a liquid.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Explain the observation.</p>
  <p>
    The solid becomes smaller as carbon dioxide gas forms and disperses.
    The visible white mist is mainly tiny liquid water droplets formed
    from water vapour in the surrounding air, not the carbon dioxide gas itself.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> Dry ice undergoes sublimation under
  ordinary atmospheric pressure.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Iodine</h3>

  <p><strong>Observation:</strong> When solid iodine is heated carefully
  in a suitable laboratory setup, a purple vapour can appear.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> The starting substance is solid iodine.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Heating supplies thermal energy.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Iodine can pass directly from the solid
  state into the gas state, producing iodine vapour.</p>

  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> The direct solid-to-gas change is
  sublimation. Heating iodine requires proper laboratory safety precautions.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Naphthalene</h3>

  <p><strong>Observation:</strong> A naphthalene solid can gradually
  become smaller when left exposed to air under suitable conditions.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the initial state.</p>
  <p>
    Naphthalene begins as a solid.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Consider what leaves the solid.</p>
  <p>
    Some particles enter the gas state directly.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Check whether a liquid stage is required.</p>
  <p>
    In the process described, the solid becomes vapour without first
    forming a liquid.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> The direct solid-to-gas change is sublimation.</p>

  <h3>Important Points</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Sublimation is a change from solid directly to gas.</li>
    <li>The substance does not pass through the liquid state during this change.</li>
    <li>Thermal energy can enable particles to escape from the solid.</li>
    <li>Sublimation is a physical change, not necessarily a chemical reaction.</li>
    <li>Examples include dry ice at ordinary atmospheric pressure,
    iodine under suitable conditions, and naphthalene.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Diagnostic Questions</h3>

  <p>For every answer, identify the starting state, final state, and
  whether a liquid state occurs during the process.</p>
