  <h2>Elements</h2>

  <h3>Meaning of an Element</h3>

  <p>
    An <strong>element</strong> is a pure substance that contains only
    one type of atom. It cannot be broken down into simpler substances
    by ordinary chemical methods.
  </p>

  <p>
    Every element has a unique name and chemical symbol. For example,
    hydrogen has the symbol H, oxygen has the symbol O, and iron has
    the symbol Fe.
  </p>

  <p>
    An element may exist as individual atoms or as molecules containing
    atoms of the same element. Therefore, an element does not always
    consist of single, separate atoms.
  </p>

  <h3>Atoms and Elements</h3>

  <p>
    An atom is the smallest unit of an element that retains the chemical
    identity of that element. All atoms of a given element have the same
    number of protons in their nuclei.
  </p>

  <p>
    For example, every carbon atom has 6 protons. Every oxygen atom has
    8 protons. The number of protons identifies the element.
  </p>

  <h3>Elements That Exist as Molecules</h3>

  <p>
    Some elements exist naturally as molecules made of two or more atoms
    of the same element.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>Hydrogen gas: H₂ — each molecule contains two hydrogen atoms.</li>
    <li>Oxygen gas: O₂ — each molecule contains two oxygen atoms.</li>
    <li>Nitrogen gas: N₂ — each molecule contains two nitrogen atoms.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Hydrogen gas is still an element because the molecules contain only
    hydrogen atoms. The same reasoning applies to oxygen and nitrogen.
  </p>

  <h3>Chemical Symbols</h3>

  <p>
    A chemical symbol is the shortened representation of an element.
    It usually consists of one or two letters. The first letter is
    always capitalised, while the second letter, if present, is lowercase.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>H — hydrogen</li>
    <li>O — oxygen</li>
    <li>C — carbon</li>
    <li>N — nitrogen</li>
    <li>Na — sodium</li>
    <li>Cl — chlorine</li>
    <li>Fe — iron</li>
    <li>Cu — copper</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Capitalisation matters. For example, Co is the symbol for cobalt,
    while CO represents one carbon atom chemically combined with one
    oxygen atom in a carbon monoxide molecule.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Classifying Oxygen Gas</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Oxygen gas is represented by O₂.
  Explain why it is classified as an element.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Read the formula.</p>
  <p>
    The symbol O represents oxygen, and the subscript 2 indicates that
    each molecule contains two oxygen atoms.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Identify the types of atoms present.</p>
  <p>
    Both atoms are oxygen atoms. There is only one type of atom.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Apply the definition.</p>
  <p>
    A substance containing only one type of atom is an element.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> O₂ is an element because every molecule
  contains only oxygen atoms.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Classifying Iron</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> A sample contains only iron atoms.
  Explain why the sample is an element.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the atoms.</p>
  <p>
    The sample contains iron atoms, represented by the symbol Fe.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Count the types of atoms.</p>
  <p>
    Only one type of atom is present.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Apply the definition of an element.</p>
  <p>
    The sample is made from only one type of atom and is not a combination
    of different elements.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The sample is an element because it
  contains only iron atoms.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Interpreting a Chemical Symbol</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> What does the symbol Na represent,
  and why is sodium classified as an element?</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the symbol.</p>
  <p>
    Na is the chemical symbol for sodium.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Identify the type of substance.</p>
  <p>
    Sodium is a chemical element, so its atoms all have the same
    number of protons.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Connect this to the definition.</p>
  <p>
    Sodium is not made by chemically combining different elements.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Na represents sodium. Sodium is an
  element because it consists of one type of atom.</p>

  <h3>Important Points</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>An element contains only one type of atom.</li>
    <li>An element cannot be broken down into simpler substances by
    ordinary chemical methods.</li>
    <li>Elements are represented by chemical symbols.</li>
    <li>An element may exist as single atoms or as molecules containing
    atoms of the same element.</li>
    <li>The number of protons in an atom identifies its element.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Diagnostic Questions</h3>

  <p>
    Answer in complete sentences. When classifying a substance, explain
    your decision using the types of atoms present.
  </p>
