  <h2>Compounds</h2>

  <h3>Meaning of a Compound</h3>

  <p>
    A <strong>compound</strong> is a pure substance formed when atoms
    of two or more different elements are chemically combined in fixed
    proportions.
  </p>

  <p>
    The elements in a compound are joined by chemical bonds. A compound
    has its own chemical properties, which may differ significantly
    from the properties of the elements that form it.
  </p>

  <p>
    A compound cannot be separated into its constituent elements by
    ordinary physical methods such as filtration. Chemical reactions
    are needed to decompose it into simpler substances.
  </p>

  <h3>Fixed Proportions</h3>

  <p>
    A particular compound has a definite composition. Its formula shows
    which elements are present and the relative numbers of their atoms
    in the substance.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>Water: H₂O — two hydrogen atoms for every oxygen atom in each molecule.</li>
    <li>Carbon dioxide: CO₂ — one carbon atom for every two oxygen atoms.</li>
    <li>Sodium chloride: NaCl — sodium and chlorine in a 1:1 ratio
    in its formula unit.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    The formula H₂O does not mean that water is a mixture of hydrogen
    and oxygen gases. It represents a compound whose hydrogen and oxygen
    atoms are chemically bonded.
  </p>

  <h3>How Compounds Form</h3>

  <ol>
    <li>Atoms of different elements take part in a chemical reaction.</li>
    <li>Chemical bonds form between atoms or ions, depending on the compound.</li>
    <li>The resulting substance has a definite chemical composition.</li>
    <li>The compound has properties that are not simply identical to
    those of its constituent elements.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Water</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Explain why water, H₂O, is a compound.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Read the formula.</p>
  <p>
    H₂O contains hydrogen and oxygen.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Identify the types of elements.</p>
  <p>
    Two different elements are present: hydrogen and oxygen.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Consider how the atoms are joined.</p>
  <p>
    The hydrogen and oxygen atoms are chemically bonded in a definite
    proportion.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Water is a compound because hydrogen and
  oxygen are chemically combined in a fixed proportion.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Carbon Dioxide</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Use the formula CO₂ to explain the
  composition of carbon dioxide and why it is a compound.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the symbols.</p>
  <p>
    C represents carbon and O represents oxygen.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Interpret the subscript.</p>
  <p>
    Each carbon dioxide molecule contains one carbon atom and two oxygen atoms.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Apply the definition.</p>
  <p>
    The two different elements are chemically combined in a definite ratio.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Carbon dioxide is a compound because
  carbon and oxygen are chemically combined in a 1:2 atomic ratio.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Sodium Chloride</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Explain why sodium chloride, NaCl,
  is a compound rather than an element.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the elements.</p>
  <p>
    Na represents sodium and Cl represents chlorine.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Determine the number of elements.</p>
  <p>
    Two different elements are present.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Identify their chemical relationship.</p>
  <p>
    Sodium and chlorine form an ionic compound with a 1:1 ratio of
    sodium ions to chloride ions in the formula unit.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Sodium chloride is a compound because
  it consists of sodium and chlorine chemically combined in a fixed ratio.</p>

  <h3>Properties of Compounds</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>They contain two or more different elements chemically combined.</li>
    <li>They have a definite composition.</li>
    <li>They have their own chemical properties.</li>
    <li>They cannot be separated into their constituent elements by
    ordinary physical separation methods.</li>
    <li>Their chemical formulas represent their composition.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Diagnostic Questions</h3>

  <p>
    Use chemical formulas where appropriate. Explain how the elements
    are combined rather than merely naming the substance.
  </p>
