  <h2>Mixtures</h2>

  <h3>Meaning of a Mixture</h3>

  <p>
    A <strong>mixture</strong> is formed when two or more substances
    are combined physically without being chemically combined into
    one new compound.
  </p>

  <p>
    The substances in a mixture are called its components. These
    components can be elements, compounds, or other substances.
  </p>

  <p>
    Unlike a compound, a mixture does not have one fixed chemical
    formula that describes the whole mixture. The proportions of its
    components can vary.
  </p>

  <h3>How Mixtures Differ from Compounds</h3>

  <p>
    In a mixture, the components retain their chemical identities.
    Combining them does not necessarily produce a new substance through
    a chemical reaction.
  </p>

  <p>
    Many mixtures can be separated using physical methods. The method
    chosen depends on differences in the components'' physical properties,
    such as particle size, solubility, boiling point, or magnetic behaviour.
  </p>

  <h3>Examples of Mixtures</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Air — a mixture of gases, including nitrogen, oxygen, and argon,
    with variable amounts of water vapour and other gases.</li>
    <li>Salt water — water containing dissolved sodium chloride.</li>
    <li>Brass — an alloy made mainly of copper and zinc.</li>
    <li>Soil — a mixture of mineral particles, organic matter, water,
    air, and other components.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    These mixtures differ in composition. Some appear uniform throughout,
    while others have visibly different components.
  </p>

  <h3>Composition of Mixtures</h3>

  <p>
    The amounts of components in a mixture can change without changing
    the identity of the components themselves.
  </p>

  <p>
    For example, a small amount of salt can be dissolved in water, or
    a larger amount can be dissolved if conditions permit. Both samples
    are mixtures of salt and water, but their compositions differ.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Salt and Sand</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Salt and sand are placed together in
  a container. Explain why the resulting material is a mixture.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the components.</p>
  <p>
    The components are salt and sand.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Consider whether they have chemically
  combined into one new compound.</p>
  <p>
    Simply placing them together does not chemically combine them into
    one new compound.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Apply the definition.</p>
  <p>
    The substances are physically combined and retain their identities.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Salt and sand form a mixture because
  they are combined physically rather than chemically forming a new compound.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Salt Water</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Explain why dissolving salt in water
  produces a mixture.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the substances.</p>
  <p>
    The components are sodium chloride and water.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Consider what happens during dissolving.</p>
  <p>
    The salt separates into hydrated ions dispersed throughout the water.
    Dissolving under these conditions does not turn the salt and water
    into a single new compound.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Apply the definition.</p>
  <p>
    The salt and water form a physical combination whose composition
    can vary.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Salt water is a mixture because it contains
  dissolved sodium chloride and water without forming one new compound.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Air</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Explain why air is classified as a mixture.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the components.</p>
  <p>
    Air contains several gases, mainly nitrogen and oxygen, along with
    argon, carbon dioxide, and variable amounts of water vapour.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Consider their proportions.</p>
  <p>
    The proportions of some components, particularly water vapour,
    can vary from place to place and over time.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Apply the definition.</p>
  <p>
    The gases are physically mixed rather than chemically combined
    into one compound.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Air is a mixture because it contains
  several gases whose proportions can vary.</p>

  <h3>Separation of Mixtures</h3>

  <p>
    Physical separation methods take advantage of differences in the
    properties of the components. Examples include:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>Filtration — can separate an insoluble solid from a liquid.</li>
    <li>Evaporation — can recover a dissolved solid from a solution.</li>
    <li>Distillation — can separate substances using differences in
    volatility and boiling behaviour.</li>
    <li>Magnetic separation — can separate a magnetic component from
    non-magnetic material when their magnetic properties differ.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    The suitability of a method depends on the actual components in
    the mixture and their properties.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 4: Choosing a Separation Method</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> A mixture contains sand and water.
  Which physical method can separate the sand from the water, and why?</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the physical properties.</p>
  <p>
    Sand is insoluble in water, and its particles can be retained by
    a suitable filter.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Choose a method.</p>
  <p>
    Filtration is suitable for separating the insoluble sand from water.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Explain the result.</p>
  <p>
    The sand remains as residue on the filter while water passes through
    as filtrate.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Filtration separates the sand from the
  water because the sand is insoluble and can be retained by the filter.</p>

  <h3>Important Points</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>A mixture contains two or more substances physically combined.</li>
    <li>The components retain their chemical identities.</li>
    <li>The proportions of components can vary.</li>
    <li>A mixture does not have one fixed chemical formula describing
    the entire mixture.</li>
    <li>Many mixtures can be separated by suitable physical methods.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Diagnostic Questions</h3>

  <p>
    Explain your reasoning for every classification. Where a separation
    method is requested, connect your choice to a relevant physical property.
  </p>
