  <h2>Identifying Pure Substances</h2>

  <h3>Meaning of a Pure Substance</h3>

  <p>
    In chemistry, a <strong>pure substance</strong> is material made
    up of only one chemical substance, with a definite composition
    and characteristic properties.
  </p>

  <p>
    A pure substance may be an element or a compound. An element contains
    one type of atom, while a compound contains different elements
    chemically combined in fixed proportions.
  </p>

  <p>
    A pure substance is not necessarily an element. Pure water is a
    compound, while pure copper is an element.
  </p>

  <h3>Composition and Identity</h3>

  <p>
    A pure substance has a definite chemical identity. A sample of
    pure water consists of water, H₂O, rather than water mixed with
    dissolved salt or other substances.
  </p>

  <p>
    If two samples are both pure samples of the same substance, their
    chemical composition is the same, even if the samples have different
    masses or volumes.
  </p>

  <h3>Using Melting Point and Boiling Point</h3>

  <p>
    At a specified pressure, a pure substance generally melts and boils
    at characteristic temperatures. For a pure crystalline substance
    at a given pressure, melting commonly occurs over a very narrow
    temperature range.
  </p>

  <p>
    Many mixtures melt or boil over a range of temperatures because
    their components have different physical properties. However, this
    is evidence rather than a universal test: some mixtures have
    characteristic phase-change behaviour, and pressure must be
    considered when comparing temperatures.
  </p>

  <p>
    A melting-point or boiling-point measurement should therefore be
    interpreted alongside other evidence about the sample''s composition.
  </p>

  <h3>Purity and Appearance</h3>

  <p>
    A material that looks clear, colourless, or uniform is not
    automatically pure. Dissolved substances may be invisible, and
    some mixtures look uniform even though they contain more than one
    substance.
  </p>

  <p>
    For example, a clear salt solution is a mixture of salt and water.
    Its appearance alone does not establish that it is a pure substance.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Pure Copper</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> A sample is confirmed to contain only
  copper. Explain whether it is a pure substance and whether it is
  an element or a compound.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1: Identify its composition.</strong></p>
  <p>
    The sample contains only copper atoms.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2: Apply the definition of purity.</strong></p>
  <p>
    It contains only one chemical substance, copper.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3: Classify the substance.</strong></p>
  <p>
    Copper is an element, not a compound, because it contains only
    one type of atom.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The sample is a pure substance and an
  element because it contains only copper.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Pure Water</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> A sample contains only H₂O molecules.
  Explain why it is a pure substance but not an element.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1: Examine the chemical identity.</strong></p>
  <p>
    All the molecules in the sample are water molecules.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2: Determine whether the sample is pure.</strong></p>
  <p>
    Since only one chemical substance is present, the sample is pure water.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3: Determine whether it is an element.</strong></p>
  <p>
    Each water molecule contains hydrogen and oxygen, which are
    different elements chemically combined.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Pure water is a pure substance, but it
  is a compound rather than an element.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Interpreting Melting-Point Evidence</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> At the same specified pressure, Sample A
  melts over a very narrow temperature range, while Sample B melts
  over a broad range. What can this evidence suggest about the samples?</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1: Compare the observations.</strong></p>
  <p>
    Sample A has a narrow melting range, while Sample B has a broad range.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2: Apply the usual behaviour of pure substances.</strong></p>
  <p>
    A pure crystalline substance commonly melts at a characteristic
    temperature under specified conditions.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3: Draw a cautious conclusion.</strong></p>
  <p>
    Sample A may be purer than Sample B, while Sample B may contain
    impurities or be a mixture.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The observations suggest that Sample A
  may be purer and Sample B may contain impurities or multiple components.
  The melting data alone do not prove the identity or purity of either sample.</p>

  <h3>Important Points</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>A pure substance contains only one chemical substance.</li>
    <li>Pure substances include elements and compounds.</li>
    <li>A pure substance has a definite chemical composition.</li>
    <li>A clear or uniform appearance does not prove purity.</li>
    <li>Melting-point and boiling-point behaviour can provide evidence
    about purity when pressure and measurement conditions are controlled.</li>
    <li>Experimental evidence should be interpreted carefully rather
    than treated as an infallible test.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Diagnostic Questions</h3>

  <p>
    For each sample, explain what is present and distinguish purity
    from whether the substance is an element or a compound.
  </p>
