  <h2>Identifying Mixtures</h2>

  <h3>How to Identify a Mixture</h3>

  <p>
    A <strong>mixture</strong> contains two or more substances physically
    combined. The components retain their chemical identities and
    are not necessarily chemically combined into one new compound.
  </p>

  <p>
    To identify a mixture, investigate what substances are present,
    whether their proportions can vary, and whether they can be
    separated by suitable physical methods.
  </p>

  <p>
    No single observation is guaranteed to identify every mixture.
    A reliable conclusion should be based on the available evidence
    about the sample''s composition and behaviour.
  </p>

  <h3>Clues That a Sample May Be a Mixture</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Two or more substances are known to be present.</li>
    <li>The proportions of the components can vary from one sample
    to another.</li>
    <li>Different components can be separated by a suitable physical method.</li>
    <li>The sample contains visibly different materials or layers,
    although a mixture can also look uniform.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Physical Separation as Evidence</h3>

  <p>
    Many mixtures can be separated because their components have
    different physical properties. For example, filtration can separate
    an insoluble solid from a liquid, and a magnet can remove a magnetic
    component from non-magnetic material.
  </p>

  <p>
    Separating a mixture does not require turning its components into
    different chemical substances. The method separates substances
    that were already present.
  </p>

  <h3>Composition Can Vary</h3>

  <p>
    The component proportions in a mixture are not fixed by a single
    chemical formula. Two salt solutions can contain different
    concentrations of salt and still be mixtures of salt and water.
  </p>

  <p>
    A mixture can be homogeneous, with uniform composition, or
    heterogeneous, with non-uniform composition. Therefore, a uniform
    appearance does not rule out a mixture.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Identifying Salt Water</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Two samples of salt water contain
  different amounts of dissolved salt relative to the amount of water.
  Explain why both samples are mixtures.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1: Identify the components.</strong></p>
  <p>
    Both samples contain sodium chloride and water.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2: Compare their compositions.</strong></p>
  <p>
    The relative amounts of salt and water differ between the samples.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3: Apply the definition.</strong></p>
  <p>
    The salt and water are physically combined, and their proportions
    can vary.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Both samples are mixtures because they
  contain salt and water in variable proportions rather than one
  chemically combined substance with a fixed formula.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Identifying Iron and Sand</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> A sample contains iron filings and
  sand. A magnet removes the iron filings while the sand remains.
  Explain what this evidence tells you.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1: Identify the components.</strong></p>
  <p>
    The sample contains iron and sand.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2: Interpret the separation.</strong></p>
  <p>
    A magnet attracts the iron filings but not the sand under ordinary
    conditions.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3: Draw a conclusion.</strong></p>
  <p>
    The components were present as separate substances and could be
    separated using their different magnetic properties.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The sample is a mixture of iron and sand.
  Magnetic separation removes the iron without requiring the formation
  of new substances.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Identifying a Uniform Liquid</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> A clear liquid looks uniform. Testing
  establishes that it contains water and a dissolved substance. Explain
  whether it can be a mixture despite its uniform appearance.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1: Consider the appearance.</strong></p>
  <p>
    The liquid appears uniform, but appearance alone does not establish purity.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2: Use the composition evidence.</strong></p>
  <p>
    The sample contains water and another dissolved substance.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3: Classify the sample.</strong></p>
  <p>
    Since more than one substance is physically combined in a uniform
    composition, the liquid is a homogeneous mixture.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Yes. The sample can be a homogeneous
  mixture because it contains multiple substances even though it looks uniform.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 4: Distinguishing a Mixture from a Compound</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> A sample contains two substances
  physically combined, and each can be recovered using a suitable
  physical separation method. Explain why the evidence supports
  classifying the sample as a mixture.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the substances already present.</p>
  <p>
    Two components are present in the original sample.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Consider the separation.</p>
  <p>
    A suitable physical method separates the components without
    chemically decomposing one compound into its elements.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Apply the definition.</p>
  <p>
    The sample is a physical combination of substances.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The evidence supports classifying the
  sample as a mixture because its components were physically combined
  and can be separated using a suitable physical method.</p>

  <h3>Important Points</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>A mixture contains at least two substances physically combined.</li>
    <li>Its components retain their chemical identities.</li>
    <li>The proportions of the components can vary.</li>
    <li>Mixtures may be homogeneous or heterogeneous.</li>
    <li>Some mixtures can be separated using physical properties such
    as solubility, particle size, boiling behaviour, or magnetism.</li>
    <li>A uniform appearance does not prove that a sample is pure.</li>
    <li>Use composition and suitable experimental evidence to classify
    an unknown sample.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Diagnostic Questions</h3>

  <p>
    Use the evidence given in each question. Explain why the evidence
    supports the classification instead of relying on appearance alone.
  </p>
  ', 1),
  (745, 745, '
  <h2>Filtration</h2>

  <h3>Meaning of Filtration</h3>

  <p>
    <strong>Filtration</strong> is a method of separating an insoluble solid
    from a liquid by passing the mixture through a filter.
  </p>

  <p>
    An <strong>insoluble solid</strong> is a solid that does not dissolve
    in the liquid under the conditions used. For example, sand is insoluble
    in water.
  </p>

  <h3>How Filtration Works</h3>

  <p>
    Filter paper contains tiny pores. When a mixture of an insoluble solid
    and a liquid passes through the paper, the liquid passes through the
    pores while the solid particles are retained, provided the particles
    are larger than the effective pores.
  </p>

  <p>The two products have specific names:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Residue:</strong> the solid left on the filter paper.</li>
    <li><strong>Filtrate:</strong> the liquid that passes through the filter paper.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Apparatus and Arrangement</h3>

  <p>
    The usual apparatus includes a filter funnel, filter paper, a container
    for the mixture and a receiving container for the filtrate.
  </p>

  <ol>
    <li>Fold the filter paper and place it inside the funnel.</li>
    <li>Position the funnel above a clean receiving container.</li>
    <li>Pour the mixture carefully into the filter paper.</li>
    <li>Allow the liquid to pass through.</li>
    <li>Identify the residue and the filtrate.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>What Filtration Can and Cannot Do</h3>

  <p>
    Filtration separates an insoluble solid from a liquid. It does not
    normally separate a dissolved substance from its solution because
    dissolved particles pass through the filter paper with the liquid.
  </p>

  <p>
    For example, filtration can separate sand from water, but it cannot
    remove dissolved salt from salt solution using ordinary filter paper.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: Separating sand from water</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> A learner pours a mixture of sand and water through filter paper. Identify the residue and the filtrate.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Sand does not dissolve in water, so it remains as solid particles.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> The sand particles are retained by the filter paper.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Water passes through the filter paper into the receiving container.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Residue — sand. Filtrate — water.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: Separating chalk powder from water</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Chalk powder is mixed with water and filtered. Where is the chalk powder found?</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Chalk powder is insoluble in water.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Its solid particles are retained on the filter paper.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> The water passes through into the receiving container.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The chalk powder is the residue on the filter paper.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: Filtering a salt solution</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> A learner filters a clear salt solution. Will the filter paper collect the dissolved salt?</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> The salt has dissolved and its particles are distributed throughout the water.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> The dissolved particles pass through ordinary filter paper with the water.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> No salt residue is normally left behind if the solution contains no undissolved material.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> No. The dissolved salt passes through with the water, so filtration does not separate it from the solution.</p>

  <h3>Important Distinction</h3>

  <p>
    A mixture may look cloudy because it contains suspended solid particles.
    A dissolved substance, however, is distributed throughout the liquid
    and is not removed by ordinary filtration. The key question is whether
    the solid is insoluble or dissolved.
  </p>
  ', 1),
  (746, 746, '
  <h2>Crystallisation</h2>

  <h3>Meaning of Crystallisation</h3>

  <p>
    <strong>Crystallisation</strong> is a method of obtaining a dissolved
    solid from a solution in the form of crystals.
  </p>

  <p>
    It is useful when the aim is to obtain solid crystals rather than
    simply leave any solid residue after removing the liquid. The process
    depends on the solubility of the substance in the solvent.
  </p>

  <h3>How Crystallisation Works</h3>

  <p>
    Many solids dissolve more readily in a hot solvent than in a cold
    solvent. A hot, concentrated solution can therefore be cooled so that
    less of the dissolved substance remains dissolved. The excess substance
    forms crystals.
  </p>

  <p>A common procedure is:</p>

  <ol>
    <li>Prepare a solution containing the substance to be crystallised.</li>
    <li>Warm the solution, where appropriate, to dissolve the substance.</li>
    <li>Evaporate some solvent carefully to make the solution concentrated.</li>
    <li>Allow the concentrated solution to cool undisturbed.</li>
    <li>Allow crystals to form.</li>
    <li>Separate the crystals from the remaining liquid and dry them appropriately.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Why the Solution Is Concentrated First</h3>

  <p>
    If the solution is too dilute, cooling may not produce many crystals
    because the solvent can still keep most or all of the dissolved
    substance in solution. Removing some solvent helps make the solution
    concentrated enough for crystals to form when conditions change.
  </p>

  <h3>Crystallisation Versus Simply Evaporating to Dryness</h3>

  <p>
    In crystallisation, the solution is usually concentrated and then
    allowed to cool so that crystals form. The aim is to obtain crystals
    under controlled conditions.
  </p>

  <p>
    Evaporating a solution completely to dryness instead aims to remove
    the solvent as fully as practical and leave the solid behind. It may
    not produce crystals of the desired quality, and heating some
    substances strongly may damage them.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: Obtaining crystals from a solution</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> A learner wants to obtain crystals of a soluble solid from its aqueous solution. Describe the main steps.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Warm the solution if needed to help the solid dissolve.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Evaporate some water to produce a concentrated solution.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Allow the concentrated solution to cool undisturbed.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Collect the crystals that form and dry them appropriately.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Concentrate the solution, allow it to cool so crystals form, then collect and dry the crystals.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: Why cooling produces crystals</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> A hot concentrated solution forms crystals when it cools. Explain why.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> The solid is dissolved in the solvent while the solution is hot.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> As the solution cools, the solubility of many solids decreases.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> The solution can no longer keep as much of the solid dissolved.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Some dissolved particles form solid crystals.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Cooling can reduce solubility, causing excess dissolved substance to form crystals.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: A solution is too dilute</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> A learner cools a dilute solution but obtains very few crystals. Give a reason and a suitable adjustment.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> A dilute solution contains relatively little dissolved substance compared with the amount of solvent.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Cooling may not make enough substance come out of solution to form many crystals.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Carefully evaporating some solvent first can make the solution more concentrated.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 4:</strong> The concentrated solution can then be cooled to encourage crystal formation.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The solution may be too dilute. Evaporate some solvent carefully, then allow the concentrated solution to cool.</p>

  <h3>Conditions and Care</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Concentrate the solution carefully rather than overheating it.</li>
    <li>Allow sufficient time for crystals to form during cooling.</li>
    <li>Do not assume every dissolved solid has the same solubility behaviour.</li>
    <li>Some substances require different temperatures or procedures because their solubility changes differently.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 1),
  (747, 747, '
  <h2>Simple Distillation</h2>

  <h3>Meaning of Simple Distillation</h3>

  <p>
    <strong>Simple distillation</strong> is a method of separating a
    liquid from a solution by vaporising the liquid and then cooling
    its vapour so that it condenses back into a liquid.
  </p>

  <p>
    It is commonly used to obtain a solvent from a solution containing
    a dissolved solid, such as obtaining water from salt solution.
  </p>

  <h3>Principle of Simple Distillation</h3>

  <p>
    When a salt solution is heated, water vaporises while the dissolved
    salt remains in the distillation flask under normal conditions.
    The water vapour passes into a condenser, where cooling changes it
    back into liquid water.
  </p>

  <p>
    The liquid collected after condensation is called the <strong>distillate</strong>.
  </p>

  <h3>Main Apparatus</h3>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Distillation flask:</strong> holds the solution being heated.</li>
    <li><strong>Heat source:</strong> supplies energy to vaporise the liquid.</li>
    <li><strong>Thermometer:</strong> measures the temperature of the vapour when positioned correctly.</li>
    <li><strong>Condenser:</strong> cools the vapour so that it condenses.</li>
    <li><strong>Receiving container:</strong> collects the distillate.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Procedure</h3>

  <ol>
    <li>Place the solution in the distillation flask.</li>
    <li>Arrange the flask, thermometer and condenser correctly.</li>
    <li>Heat the solution carefully so that the more volatile liquid vaporises.</li>
    <li>Pass the vapour through the cooled condenser.</li>
    <li>Collect the condensed liquid in the receiving container.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Worked Examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: Obtaining water from salt solution</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> A learner wants to obtain water from salt solution and collect it as a liquid. Which method is suitable, and how does it work?</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Heat the salt solution in a distillation flask.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Water vaporises and passes into the condenser.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> The condenser cools the water vapour.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 4:</strong> The vapour condenses to liquid water and is collected.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Simple distillation. The collected liquid water is the distillate.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: Identifying the distillate</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> During the simple distillation of salt solution, a liquid collects in the receiving container. What is this liquid expected to be, and why?</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Water vaporises from the heated solution.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> The dissolved salt does not normally vaporise with the water under these conditions.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Water vapour condenses in the condenser and flows into the receiver.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The distillate is water because the water vapour condenses and is collected as a liquid.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: The condenser is not cooled</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Explain what problem may occur if the condenser is not cooled properly during simple distillation.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> The liquid vapour must lose heat to condense.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> If the condenser is not cooled adequately, some vapour may not condense efficiently.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Less liquid distillate may be collected, and vapour may escape from the apparatus.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Condensation may be incomplete, reducing the amount of liquid collected and allowing vapour to escape.</p>

  <h3>Why Simple Distillation Is Useful</h3>

  <p>
    Unlike ordinary evaporation in an open dish, simple distillation
    collects the vapour after it has condensed. This makes it useful
    when recovering the liquid solvent is important.
  </p>

  <p>
    Simple distillation is not the best method for separating every
    mixture of liquids. If two miscible liquids have boiling points
    that are too close for effective separation by simple distillation,
    another method may be needed.
  </p>

  <h3>Laboratory Safety</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Use suitable laboratory supervision and protective equipment.</li>
    <li>Do not seal a heated distillation system in a way that allows pressure to build up dangerously.</li>
    <li>Secure the apparatus and handle hot glassware carefully.</li>
    <li>Never heat a distillation flask to dryness unless the procedure specifically permits it.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 1),
  (748, 748, '
  <h2>Fractional Distillation</h2>

  <h3>Meaning of Fractional Distillation</h3>

  <p>
    <strong>Fractional distillation</strong> is a method of separating
    a mixture of miscible liquids that have different boiling points.
    It is particularly useful when the boiling points are relatively
    close together.
  </p>

  <p>
    Miscible liquids are liquids that mix completely to form one uniform
    liquid mixture. Fractional distillation separates them by using
    differences in volatility and boiling point.
  </p>

  <h3>Principle of Fractional Distillation</h3>

  <p>
    When a mixture of liquids is heated, the vapour formed is generally
    richer in the more volatile component. The more volatile liquid
    usually has the lower boiling point.
  </p>

  <p>
    In the fractionating column, rising vapour repeatedly cools and
    condenses while vapour below it rises again. These repeated
    vaporisation and condensation processes enrich the rising vapour
    in the more volatile component.
  </p>

  <p>
    The vapour that reaches the condenser is cooled into a liquid and
    collected. As the temperature and composition change, different
    fractions may be collected over the course of the separation.
  </p>

  <h3>Main Apparatus</h3>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Distillation flask:</strong> holds the liquid mixture.</li>
    <li><strong>Heat source:</strong> heats the mixture.</li>
    <li><strong>Fractionating column:</strong> provides a surface for repeated condensation and vaporisation.</li>
    <li><strong>Thermometer:</strong> monitors the temperature of the vapour near the column outlet.</li>
    <li><strong>Condenser:</strong> cools vapour into liquid.</li>
    <li><strong>Receiving container:</strong> collects the liquid fraction.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Procedure</h3>

  <ol>
    <li>Place the mixture of miscible liquids in the distillation flask.</li>
    <li>Fit the fractionating column between the flask and the condenser.</li>
    <li>Heat the mixture carefully.</li>
    <li>Allow repeated vaporisation and condensation to occur in the column.</li>
    <li>Pass the vapour into the condenser.</li>
    <li>Collect the condensed liquid fraction.</li>
    <li>Monitor the temperature and collect fractions as appropriate.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Worked Examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: Choosing a method for two miscible liquids</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Two miscible liquids have boiling points of 78°C and 82°C. Which distillation method is generally more suitable for separating them, and why?</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> The liquids mix completely, so they form one liquid mixture.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Their boiling points are relatively close: the difference is 82 − 78 = 4°C.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> A fractionating column improves separation through repeated vaporisation and condensation.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Fractional distillation is generally more suitable because the liquids are miscible and have relatively close boiling points.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: Identifying the more volatile component</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> A mixture contains two miscible liquids with boiling points of 65°C and 100°C. Which component is more volatile, and which is generally enriched in the early vapour?</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Compare the boiling points: 65°C is lower than 100°C.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> The liquid with the lower boiling point is generally more volatile.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> The early vapour is generally richer in that more volatile component.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The liquid with a boiling point of 65°C is more volatile and is generally enriched in the early vapour.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: Understanding the fractionating column</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Explain why a fractionating column improves the separation of two miscible liquids whose boiling points are close together.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Vapour rises through the column and encounters cooler surfaces.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Some vapour condenses, and liquid may vaporise again as heat moves through the column.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> These repeated processes enrich the rising vapour in the more volatile component.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 4:</strong> The improved vapour composition makes separation more effective.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The column provides repeated vaporisation and condensation, helping separate liquids with close boiling points.</p>

  <h3>Important Distinction</h3>

  <p>
    Simple distillation is often suitable for recovering a liquid from a
    solution containing a dissolved solid, or for separating liquids
    when their boiling points are sufficiently different. Fractional
    distillation is particularly useful for separating miscible liquids
    with relatively close boiling points.
  </p>

  <p>
    Distillation does not always produce perfectly pure fractions.
    The effectiveness depends on the substances, their boiling points,
    the apparatus and the operating conditions.
  </p>
  ', 1),
  (749, 749, '
  <h2>Chromatography</h2>

  <h3>Meaning of Chromatography</h3>

  <p>
    <strong>Chromatography</strong> is a separation technique used to
    separate the components of a mixture because the components move
    differently through a stationary material when carried by a mobile
    phase.
  </p>

  <p>
    In paper chromatography, the paper is the stationary phase and a
    suitable solvent is the mobile phase. The solvent moves through
    the paper and carries components of the mixture with it.
  </p>

  <h3>Principle of Paper Chromatography</h3>

  <p>
    Different substances interact differently with the paper and the
    solvent. Some components travel farther with the solvent, while
    others move more slowly or remain closer to the starting point.
    This causes the mixture to separate into distinct spots.
  </p>

  <h3>Procedure for Paper Chromatography</h3>

  <ol>
    <li>Draw a pencil baseline near the bottom of a strip of chromatography paper.</li>
    <li>Place a small spot of the mixture on the baseline.</li>
    <li>Allow the spot to dry if necessary.</li>
    <li>Place the lower end of the paper in a suitable solvent, keeping the baseline above the solvent level.</li>
    <li>Allow the solvent to travel up the paper.</li>
    <li>Remove the paper before the solvent reaches the top.</li>
    <li>Mark the solvent front promptly and allow the paper to dry.</li>
    <li>Observe the separated spots.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Why the Baseline Must Be Above the Solvent</h3>

  <p>
    If the sample spot is submerged in the solvent at the start, the
    sample may dissolve directly into the solvent reservoir instead of
    travelling up the paper as intended. Keeping the baseline above the
    solvent level allows the solvent to rise and carry the sample through
    the paper.
  </p>

  <h3>Interpreting a Chromatogram</h3>

  <p>
    A sample that separates into several distinct spots contains several
    components detectable under the conditions used. A sample that forms
    one spot may contain one detectable component, but one spot alone
    does not prove absolute purity because different substances can
    sometimes travel to the same position.
  </p>

  <p>
    When reference samples are run under the same conditions, the
    positions of their spots can help identify components in an unknown
    mixture. Matching positions alone are useful evidence, but may not
    always establish identity conclusively.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: Separating ink colours</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> A black ink sample produces blue, red and yellow spots on chromatography paper. What does this show?</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> The original ink appeared black.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> The chromatogram shows three distinct spots.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> The spots indicate components that travel differently through the paper under the chosen conditions.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The ink contains at least three detectable components under these conditions: those producing the blue, red and yellow spots.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: Why the baseline is drawn in pencil</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Explain why pencil is normally used to draw the baseline in paper chromatography instead of ordinary ink.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> The solvent moves up the paper and can dissolve substances that it contacts.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Ordinary ink may dissolve and separate into its own components.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Those components could create extra spots and interfere with the results.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Pencil is used because its graphite generally does not dissolve in the solvent, unlike many inks that could interfere with the chromatogram.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: Correcting an incorrect setup</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> A learner places the sample spot below the solvent level. Explain the problem and state how to correct it.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> The sample spot is submerged in the solvent reservoir.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> The sample may dissolve into the reservoir instead of being carried up the paper.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> The learner should raise the paper or lower the solvent so that the baseline is above the solvent level.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The sample may wash directly into the solvent. Keep the baseline above the solvent level before starting the separation.</p>

  <h3>What Affects the Separation?</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>The solvent selected.</li>
    <li>The type of paper or stationary material.</li>
    <li>The solubility of each component in the mobile phase.</li>
    <li>How strongly each component interacts with the stationary phase.</li>
    <li>The amount of sample placed on the baseline.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    The solvent must be suitable for the substances being separated.
    A solvent that does not carry the components effectively may produce
    little or no useful separation.
  </p>
  ', 1),
  (750, 750, '
  <h2>Decantation</h2>

  <h3>Meaning of Decantation</h3>

  <p>
    <strong>Decantation</strong> is a separation method in which a liquid
    is carefully poured away from a settled solid or from another liquid
    that does not mix with it.
  </p>

  <p>
    In solid-liquid decantation, the solid is allowed to settle at the
    bottom of the container before the liquid above it is poured away.
  </p>

  <h3>Decantation of a Solid-Liquid Mixture</h3>

  <p>
    Consider a mixture of sand and water. If the mixture is left
    undisturbed, the sand particles may settle at the bottom. The clearer
    water above the sand can then be poured carefully into another
    container without disturbing the settled solid.
  </p>

  <ol>
    <li>Allow the solid particles to settle where settling is possible.</li>
    <li>Keep the container still so the settled solid is not disturbed.</li>
    <li>Tilt the container slowly.</li>
    <li>Pour the liquid into a second container, leaving the settled solid behind.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Decantation of Immiscible Liquids</h3>

  <p>
    Decantation can also be used to separate two immiscible liquids,
    which form separate layers. For example, oil and water form distinct
    layers because they do not mix completely.
  </p>

  <p>
    The upper layer may be poured off carefully when the layers are
    clearly separated. For more controlled separation, a separating
    funnel can be used.
  </p>

  <h3>Limitations of Decantation</h3>

  <p>
    Decantation is simple, but it may not produce a completely pure
    liquid. Fine particles can remain suspended in the liquid, and
    some of the liquid may remain mixed with or trapped among the
    settled solid.
  </p>

  <p>
    If the solid does not settle well, or if a clearer separation is
    required, decantation alone may be insufficient.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: Separating sand and water</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> A learner mixes sand and water and leaves the container undisturbed until the sand settles. Explain how the learner can separate most of the water.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Allow the sand particles to settle at the bottom.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Keep the container steady so the sand remains settled.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Tilt the container carefully and pour the water into another container.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Use decantation by carefully pouring off the water after the sand has settled.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: Separating oil and water</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Oil and water form two distinct layers in a container. Explain how careful decantation can separate them.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Allow the mixture to stand until the two layers are clearly separated.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Identify the upper and lower layers.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Carefully pour off one layer without mixing it with the other.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Allow the liquids to form separate layers and carefully pour off the desired layer. A separating funnel may provide more controlled separation.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: Why decantation may leave impurities</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> After decanting water from a mixture containing fine solid particles, the water remains cloudy. Explain why.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Not all fine particles may settle during the waiting period.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Some particles remain suspended in the liquid.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> When the liquid is poured off, those suspended particles move with it.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The water remains cloudy because some fine solid particles have not settled and are carried into the receiving container.</p>

  <h3>Choosing Decantation</h3>

  <p>
    Decantation is most useful when the components can separate into
    distinct layers or when a solid settles sufficiently for the liquid
    above it to be poured away. It is less reliable when the solid
    remains suspended or when very complete separation is required.
  </p>
  ', 1);
INSERT INTO public.lesson_versions (id, lesson_id, content, version) VALUES
  (751, 751, '
  <h2>Magnetic Separation</h2>

  <h3>Meaning of Magnetic Separation</h3>

  <p>
    <strong>Magnetic separation</strong> is a method of separating
    magnetic material from non-magnetic material by using a magnet.
  </p>

  <p>
    The method works because some materials, such as iron, are attracted
    strongly to a magnet, while other materials, such as sulfur and
    ordinary sand, are not attracted in the same way.
  </p>

  <h3>Principle of Magnetic Separation</h3>

  <p>
    When a magnet is brought near a mixture containing magnetic and
    non-magnetic solids, the magnetic material is attracted to the magnet.
    The non-magnetic material remains behind, provided the arrangement
    allows the materials to be separated.
  </p>

  <h3>Procedure</h3>

  <ol>
    <li>Identify a mixture containing a magnetic material and a non-magnetic material.</li>
    <li>Bring a magnet close to the mixture.</li>
    <li>Allow the magnetic material to be attracted to the magnet.</li>
    <li>Remove the attracted material from the magnet safely.</li>
    <li>Repeat if necessary to improve the separation.</li>
  </ol>

  <p>
    Wrapping the magnet in suitable paper or a thin covering can make
    it easier to remove the collected magnetic material, provided the
    covering does not prevent effective attraction.
  </p>

  <h3>Examples of Suitable Mixtures</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Iron filings mixed with sulfur powder.</li>
    <li>Iron filings mixed with sand.</li>
    <li>Magnetic iron-containing material mixed with non-magnetic solids, where the magnetic component can be attracted effectively.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Limitations</h3>

  <p>
    Magnetic separation only works effectively when the mixture contains
    a component that responds sufficiently to the magnet. It cannot
    separate two non-magnetic substances from each other using an
    ordinary magnet alone.
  </p>

  <p>
    Not every metal is attracted strongly to an ordinary magnet. For
    example, aluminium and copper are not attracted in the same way as
    iron. Therefore, a learner must identify the magnetic behaviour of
    the materials rather than assume that every metal is magnetic.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: Separating iron filings from sulfur</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> A mixture contains iron filings and sulfur powder. Describe how to separate the iron filings.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Iron is attracted to a magnet.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Sulfur is not attracted to an ordinary magnet in the same way.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Bring a magnet close to the mixture so that the iron filings are attracted.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Remove the iron filings from the magnet using a suitable method.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Use a magnet to attract and collect the iron filings, leaving the sulfur behind.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: Separating iron from sand</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> A learner mixes iron filings with sand. Explain why a magnet can separate the two solids.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Iron is magnetic and is attracted to the magnet.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Ordinary sand is not attracted to the magnet in the same way.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> The iron can be collected while most of the sand remains behind.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Magnetic separation works because the iron is attracted to the magnet while the sand is not.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: A mixture of two non-magnetic solids</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> A mixture contains sulfur powder and ordinary sand. Explain why an ordinary magnet cannot separate these substances effectively.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Ordinary sulfur is not attracted to an ordinary magnet in the same way as iron.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Ordinary sand is also not attracted to the magnet in the same way.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> The magnet therefore does not selectively collect one of these components.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> An ordinary magnet cannot effectively separate sulfur from sand because neither component is attracted strongly enough for selective collection.</p>

  <h3>Choosing Magnetic Separation</h3>

  <p>
    Before choosing this method, ask whether one component is magnetic
    and the other is not. The method depends on that difference in
    magnetic behaviour, not simply on whether one component is a metal.
  </p>
