  <h2>Structure of the Atom</h2>

  <h3>1. What is an atom?</h3>

  <p>
    An <b>atom</b> is the smallest particle of an element that
    retains the chemical identity of that element.
    All ordinary matter is made from atoms or combinations of atoms.
  </p>

  <p>
    An atom contains a tiny, dense central region called the
    <b>nucleus</b>, surrounded by a region occupied by electrons.
    The nucleus contains protons and, in most atoms, neutrons.
    Electrons occupy specific energy levels around the nucleus.
  </p>

  <h3>2. The three main subatomic particles</h3>

  <table>
    <tr>
      <th>Particle</th>
      <th>Relative charge</th>
      <th>Relative mass</th>
      <th>Location</th>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Proton</td>
      <td>+1</td>
      <td>1</td>
      <td>Nucleus</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Neutron</td>
      <td>0</td>
      <td>1</td>
      <td>Nucleus</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Electron</td>
      <td>-1</td>
      <td>Approximately 1/1840</td>
      <td>Energy levels around the nucleus</td>
    </tr>
  </table>

  <p>
    Protons and neutrons account for almost all the mass of an atom.
    Electrons have a much smaller mass, although they are essential
    in determining how atoms form chemical bonds.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Why is an atom electrically neutral?</h3>

  <p>
    A proton carries one unit of positive relative charge.
    An electron carries one unit of negative relative charge.
    A neutron carries no charge.
  </p>

  <p>
    For a neutral atom, the number of protons equals the number
    of electrons. The positive and negative charges therefore cancel.
  </p>

  <p><b>Net relative charge = number of protons - number of electrons</b></p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Finding the overall charge</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> An atom contains 11 protons, 12 neutrons
  and 11 electrons. Calculate its overall relative charge.</p>

  <p><b>Step 1: Identify the charged particles.</b></p>
  <p>Protons contribute positive charge; electrons contribute negative charge.
  Neutrons contribute zero charge.</p>

  <p><b>Step 2: Calculate the total positive charge.</b></p>
  <p>Positive charge = 11 x (+1) = +11.</p>

  <p><b>Step 3: Calculate the total negative charge.</b></p>
  <p>Negative charge = 11 x (-1) = -11.</p>

  <p><b>Step 4: Add the charges.</b></p>
  <p>Net charge = +11 + (-11) = 0.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> The atom is electrically neutral.</p>

  <p><b>Check:</b> The 12 neutrons do not affect the charge
  because each neutron has relative charge zero.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Recognising a charged particle</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> A particle contains 13 protons and 10 electrons.
  Determine its net relative charge.</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Total positive charge = 13 x (+1) = +13.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Total negative charge = 10 x (-1) = -10.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Net charge = +13 + (-10) = +3.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> The particle has a relative charge of +3.</p>

  <p>
    It is positively charged because it has three more protons
    than electrons. If it is an aluminium particle, its symbol
    is Al3+.
  </p>

  <h3>Common mistake</h3>
  <p>
    Do not count neutrons when calculating electrical charge.
    Neutrons contribute to mass number, not net electrical charge.
  </p>
