  <h2>Protons</h2>

  <h3>Definition and properties</h3>

  <p>
    A <b>proton</b> is a positively charged subatomic particle
    found in the nucleus of an atom.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>Relative charge: +1.</li>
    <li>Relative mass: approximately 1.</li>
    <li>Location: nucleus.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    The number of protons determines the identity of an element.
    This number is called the <b>atomic number</b>, represented by Z.
  </p>

  <p>
    Hydrogen has 1 proton, carbon has 6, oxygen has 8,
    sodium has 11 and chlorine has 17.
  </p>

  <p>
    An atom cannot become a different element simply by gaining
    or losing electrons. Its proton number must change for its
    elemental identity to change.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Identifying an element</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> An atom contains 17 protons. Identify the element.</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Atomic number = number of protons.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Atomic number = 17.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> The element with atomic number 17 is chlorine.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> Chlorine (Cl).</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Comparing two particles</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Particle A contains 11 protons and particle B
  contains 12 protons. Can both particles be atoms of the same element?</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Particle A has atomic number 11, so it is sodium.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Particle B has atomic number 12, so it is magnesium.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Their proton numbers are different.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> No. They are different elements.</p>

  <p>
    Their electron or neutron numbers may vary, but their proton
    numbers establish their elemental identities.
  </p>

  <h3>Common mistake</h3>
  <p>
    Do not identify an element from its neutron or electron number
    alone. Use the proton number, or atomic number.
  </p>
