  <h2>Neutrons</h2>

  <h3>Definition and properties</h3>

  <p>
    A <b>neutron</b> is a subatomic particle found in the nucleus
    of an atom. It has no electrical charge and a relative mass
    approximately equal to 1.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>Relative charge: 0.</li>
    <li>Relative mass: approximately 1.</li>
    <li>Location: nucleus.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Neutrons contribute to the mass of an atom but do not contribute
    to its net electrical charge.
  </p>

  <h3>Calculating the number of neutrons</h3>

  <p>
    The <b>mass number</b>, A, is the total number of protons
    and neutrons in the nucleus.
  </p>

  <p><b>A = Z + n</b></p>

  <p>Rearranging gives:</p>

  <p><b>Number of neutrons, n = A - Z</b></p>

  <p>
    Here, A represents mass number and Z represents atomic number,
    which is the number of protons.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Finding neutrons</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Sodium-23 has atomic number 11.
  Calculate the number of neutrons.</p>

  <p><b>Step 1: Write the known values.</b></p>
  <p>Mass number A = 23; atomic number Z = 11.</p>

  <p><b>Step 2: Choose the correct formula.</b></p>
  <p>n = A - Z.</p>

  <p><b>Step 3: Substitute the values.</b></p>
  <p>n = 23 - 11 = 12.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> 12 neutrons.</p>

  <p><b>Verification:</b> Protons + neutrons = 11 + 12 = 23.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Finding mass number</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> An atom contains 19 protons and 20 neutrons.
  Calculate its mass number.</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> A = protons + neutrons.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> A = 19 + 20.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> A = 39.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> Mass number = 39.</p>

  <h3>Common mistake</h3>
  <p>
    Do not subtract the mass number from the atomic number.
    Neutron number = mass number - atomic number, because the
    mass number includes both protons and neutrons.
  </p>
