  <h2>Electrons</h2>

  <h3>Definition and properties</h3>

  <p>
    An <b>electron</b> is a negatively charged subatomic particle
    found in energy levels around an atom''s nucleus.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>Relative charge: -1.</li>
    <li>Relative mass: approximately 1/1840.</li>
    <li>Location: energy levels around the nucleus.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    The electron''s relative mass is much smaller than that of a proton
    or neutron. Consequently, the nucleus contains almost all the
    atom''s mass.
  </p>

  <h3>Electron number in a neutral atom</h3>

  <p>
    A neutral atom contains equal numbers of protons and electrons.
    Since the atomic number gives the number of protons:
  </p>

  <p><b>Electrons in a neutral atom = atomic number</b></p>

  <h3>What happens when electrons change?</h3>

  <p>
    Gaining or losing electrons changes the net electrical charge
    of an atom. It does not change the element''s proton number.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>Losing electrons produces a positive ion.</li>
    <li>Gaining electrons produces a negative ion.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Finding electrons</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> A neutral magnesium atom has atomic number 12.
  Calculate its number of electrons.</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Atomic number = proton number = 12.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> A neutral atom has equal numbers of protons and electrons.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Electron number = 12.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> 12 electrons.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Losing electrons</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> A neutral aluminium atom has 13 electrons.
  It loses three electrons. Calculate its new electron number and charge.</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Initial electron number = 13.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> New electron number = 13 - 3 = 10.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Aluminium still has 13 protons.</p>
  <p><b>Step 4:</b> Charge = protons - electrons = 13 - 10 = +3.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> 10 electrons; charge +3; ion Al3+.</p>

  <h3>Common mistake</h3>
  <p>
    Do not assume an ion has the same number of electrons as protons.
    That equality applies to neutral atoms, not to charged ions.
  </p>
