  <h2>Atomic Number</h2>

  <h3>Definition</h3>

  <p>
    The <b>atomic number</b>, represented by Z, is the number
    of protons in the nucleus of an atom.
  </p>

  <p><b>Z = number of protons</b></p>

  <p>
    Atomic number uniquely identifies an element. For example,
    atomic number 6 identifies carbon, 8 identifies oxygen,
    11 identifies sodium and 17 identifies chlorine.
  </p>

  <p>
    The proton number remains the same when an atom forms an ion
    or when isotopes of an element are compared.
  </p>

  <h3>Relationship to electron number</h3>

  <p>
    For a neutral atom, the number of electrons equals the atomic number.
    For an ion, the electron number differs because electrons have
    been gained or lost.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Atomic number and electron number</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> A neutral atom has atomic number 12.
  State its proton number and electron number, and identify the element.</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Proton number = Z = 12.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Because the atom is neutral, electron number = 12.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Atomic number 12 identifies magnesium.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> 12 protons, 12 electrons, magnesium (Mg).</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Atomic number of an ion</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> An ion contains 13 protons and 10 electrons.
  Determine its atomic number, elemental identity and charge.</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Atomic number = proton number = 13.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Element 13 is aluminium.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Charge = 13 - 10 = +3.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> Atomic number 13; aluminium; Al3+.</p>

  <h3>Common mistake</h3>
  <p>
    Atomic number is not the mass number. Atomic number counts protons;
    mass number counts protons plus neutrons.
  </p>
