  (757, 757, '
  <h2>Mass Number</h2>

  <h3>1. Meaning of mass number</h3>

  <p>
    The <b>mass number</b>, represented by A, is the total number
    of protons and neutrons in the nucleus of an atom.
    Protons and neutrons are called nucleons.
  </p>

  <p><b>Mass number = number of protons + number of neutrons</b></p>
  <p><b>A = Z + n</b></p>

  <p>
    In this equation, A is the mass number, Z is the atomic number
    and n is the number of neutrons.
  </p>

  <p>
    Rearranging the equation gives:
  </p>

  <p><b>Number of neutrons = A - Z</b></p>
  <p><b>Number of protons = A - n</b></p>

  <h3>2. Why are electrons excluded?</h3>

  <p>
    Protons and neutrons each have a relative mass approximately
    equal to 1. An electron has a much smaller relative mass,
    approximately 1/1840.
  </p>

  <p>
    Mass number is a count of nuclear particles, not a measurement
    of the atom''s total mass in grams. Electrons are excluded
    from the mass-number calculation.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Calculating mass number</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> An atom contains 13 protons and 14 neutrons.
  Calculate its mass number.</p>

  <p><b>Step 1: Write the relationship.</b></p>
  <p>A = Z + n</p>

  <p><b>Step 2: Substitute the values.</b></p>
  <p>A = 13 + 14</p>

  <p><b>Step 3: Calculate.</b></p>
  <p>A = 27</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> The mass number is 27.</p>

  <p><b>Verification:</b> The nucleus contains 27 nucleons:
  13 protons + 14 neutrons.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Calculating neutrons</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Aluminium-27 has atomic number 13.
  Calculate its number of neutrons.</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Mass number A = 27.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Atomic number Z = 13.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> n = A - Z = 27 - 13 = 14.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> 14 neutrons.</p>

  <p><b>Verification:</b> 13 + 14 = 27.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Nuclear notation</h3>

  <p>
    An atom can be represented as <sup>27</sup><sub>13</sub>Al.
    The upper number is the mass number, 27. The lower number
    is the atomic number, 13.
  </p>

  <p>
    Protons = 13; neutrons = 27 - 13 = 14.
    If the atom is neutral, electrons = 13.
  </p>

  <h3>Common mistake</h3>

  <p>
    Do not add electrons to the mass number. Also, do not confuse
    atomic number with mass number: atomic number counts protons;
    mass number counts protons and neutrons.
  </p>
