  <h2>Electron Arrangement</h2>

  <h3>1. Meaning of electron arrangement</h3>

  <p>
    Electrons occupy energy levels around the nucleus.
    The distribution of electrons among these energy levels
    is called the <b>electron arrangement</b>.
  </p>

  <p>
    For the first 20 elements, the introductory shell model
    commonly uses the arrangement 2, 8, 8, 2.
    This is a simplified model for these elements, not a
    universal rule for all elements.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>First shell: maximum 2 electrons.</li>
    <li>Second shell: maximum 8 electrons.</li>
    <li>Third shell: up to 8 electrons in the simplified arrangement for the first 20 elements.</li>
    <li>Fourth shell: begins to fill for potassium and calcium.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>2. Finding the number of electrons</h3>

  <p>
    For a neutral atom, the total number of electrons equals
    its atomic number. Add the numbers in the shell arrangement
    to check the total.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Sodium</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Write the electron arrangement of sodium,
  atomic number 11.</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> A neutral sodium atom has 11 electrons.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Place 2 electrons in the first shell.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Place 8 electrons in the second shell.</p>
  <p><b>Step 4:</b> Electrons placed = 2 + 8 = 10.</p>
  <p><b>Step 5:</b> Remaining electrons = 11 - 10 = 1.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> 2,8,1.</p>

  <p><b>Check:</b> 2 + 8 + 1 = 11.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Calcium</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Write the electron arrangement of calcium,
  atomic number 20.</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Calcium has 20 electrons if neutral.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> The first shell receives 2 electrons.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> The second shell receives 8 electrons.</p>
  <p><b>Step 4:</b> The third shell receives 8 electrons in the simplified arrangement.</p>
  <p><b>Step 5:</b> Electrons placed = 2 + 8 + 8 = 18.</p>
  <p><b>Step 6:</b> Remaining electrons = 20 - 18 = 2.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> 2,8,8,2.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Finding atomic number</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> A neutral atom has electron arrangement 2,8,7.
  Find its atomic number and identify the element.</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Add the electrons: 2 + 8 + 7 = 17.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> A neutral atom has 17 protons.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Atomic number = 17.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Chlorine.</p>

  <h3>Common mistake</h3>

  <p>
    Do not distribute electrons randomly. Fill the lower energy
    levels according to the model being used, then check that
    the total equals the correct electron number.
  </p>
