  <h2>Isotopes</h2>

  <h3>1. Definition</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Isotopes</b> are atoms of the same element that have the
    same number of protons but different numbers of neutrons.
  </p>

  <p>
    Since the proton number is unchanged, isotopes have the same
    atomic number and the same elemental identity. Their different
    neutron numbers give them different mass numbers.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Example: Carbon isotopes</h3>

  <p><b>Carbon-12:</b></p>
  <p>Protons = 6; neutrons = 12 - 6 = 6.</p>

  <p><b>Carbon-14:</b></p>
  <p>Protons = 6; neutrons = 14 - 6 = 8.</p>

  <p>
    Both atoms are carbon because each has six protons.
    They differ in neutron number: carbon-12 has six neutrons,
    while carbon-14 has eight.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Calculating isotope particles</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Chlorine has atomic number 17.
  Calculate the neutron numbers of chlorine-35 and chlorine-37.</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Both isotopes have 17 protons.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Chlorine-35: neutrons = 35 - 17 = 18.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Chlorine-37: neutrons = 37 - 17 = 20.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> 18 and 20 neutrons, respectively.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Comparing isotope composition</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Two atoms each contain 8 protons.
  Atom A contains 8 neutrons, while atom B contains 10 neutrons.
  Determine their mass numbers and explain their relationship.</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Atom A: mass number = 8 + 8 = 16.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Atom B: mass number = 8 + 10 = 18.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Both have atomic number 8, which is oxygen.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> Oxygen-16 and oxygen-18; they are isotopes.</p>

  <h3>Common mistake</h3>

  <p>
    Isotopes do not have different proton numbers. If their proton
    numbers differ, they are different elements.
  </p>
