  (760, 760, '
  <h2>Identifying Isotopes</h2>

  <h3>1. The identification rule</h3>

  <p>
    To determine whether two atoms are isotopes, compare their
    proton numbers first and their neutron numbers second.
  </p>

  <p><b>Same proton number + different neutron numbers = isotopes.</b></p>

  <p>
    If the proton numbers differ, the atoms are different elements
    and cannot be isotopes of one another.
  </p>

  <h3>2. A systematic comparison method</h3>

  <ol>
    <li>Identify or calculate the proton number of each atom.</li>
    <li>If proton numbers differ, they are not isotopes.</li>
    <li>If proton numbers match, compare their neutron numbers.</li>
    <li>If neutron numbers differ, they are isotopes.</li>
    <li>If both proton and neutron numbers match, they are the same nuclide, not different isotopes.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Same element, different neutrons</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Atom A has 8 protons and 8 neutrons.
  Atom B has 8 protons and 10 neutrons. Determine whether they
  are isotopes.</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Compare proton numbers: 8 and 8.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> The proton numbers are equal.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Compare neutron numbers: 8 and 10.</p>
  <p><b>Step 4:</b> The neutron numbers differ.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> They are isotopes of oxygen.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Using nuclear notation</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Determine whether ³⁵₁₇Cl and ³⁷₁₇Cl
  are isotopes.</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Both have atomic number 17.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Both contain 17 protons.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Chlorine-35 has 35 - 17 = 18 neutrons.</p>
  <p><b>Step 4:</b> Chlorine-37 has 37 - 17 = 20 neutrons.</p>
  <p><b>Step 5:</b> The proton numbers match, but neutron numbers differ.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> Yes, they are isotopes of chlorine.</p>

  <h3>Common mistake</h3>

  <p>
    Do not compare mass numbers alone. Equal mass numbers do not
    prove that atoms are isotopes.
  </p>
