  <h2>Uses of Isotopes</h2>

  <h3>1. Stable isotopes and radioisotopes</h3>

  <p>
    Isotopes of an element have the same proton number but
    different neutron numbers. Some isotopes have unstable nuclei
    and emit radiation as their nuclei change. These are called
    <b>radioisotopes</b>.
  </p>

  <p>
    Their uses depend on properties such as the radiation they emit,
    their half-life and whether the radiation can be detected safely
    for a particular application.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Medical applications</h3>

  <p>
    Some radioisotopes are used as tracers to investigate processes
    inside the body. Radiation from a tracer can be detected to
    obtain diagnostic information.
  </p>

  <p>
    Other radioisotopes are used in treatment, where suitable
    radiation can damage targeted abnormal cells.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Industrial applications</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Thickness monitoring: radiation measurements help monitor the thickness of sheet materials during manufacture.</li>
    <li>Leak detection and tracing: suitable radioactive tracers help investigate the movement of substances.</li>
    <li>Industrial measurement: radiation detection can provide information about materials and processes.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>4. Archaeology and age determination</h3>

  <p>
    Carbon-14 dating can estimate the age of suitable once-living
    materials. Living organisms exchange carbon with their
    environment. After death, this exchange stops and the carbon-14
    content decreases through radioactive decay.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked example: Radioactive decay calculation</h3>

  <p>
    The half-life of a radioisotope is the time required for half
    the radioactive nuclei in a sample to decay.
  </p>

  <p><b>Question:</b> A sample initially contains 80 mg of a
  radioisotope. Its half-life is 5 years. Calculate the mass
  of the radioactive isotope remaining after 15 years.</p>

  <p><b>Step 1: Calculate the number of half-lives.</b></p>
  <p>Number of half-lives = elapsed time / half-life.</p>
  <p>= 15 / 5 = 3.</p>

  <p><b>Step 2: Halve the mass after each half-life.</b></p>
  <p>Initially: 80 mg.</p>
  <p>After 5 years: 80 / 2 = 40 mg.</p>
  <p>After 10 years: 40 / 2 = 20 mg.</p>
  <p>After 15 years: 20 / 2 = 10 mg.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> 10 mg of the radioactive isotope remains.</p>

  <p>
    Alternatively, remaining mass = initial mass x (1/2)^n,
    where n is the number of half-lives.
  </p>

  <p>
    Remaining mass = 80 x (1/2)^3 = 80 / 8 = 10 mg.
  </p>

  <h3>5. Radiation safety</h3>

  <p>
    Ionising radiation can damage living cells. Radioactive
    materials therefore require appropriate controls, including
    reducing exposure time, maintaining distance where suitable
    and using suitable shielding.
  </p>

  <h3>Common mistake</h3>

  <p>
    Do not assume all isotopes are radioactive. Isotopes can be
    stable or radioactive, and the application depends on the
    specific isotope and its properties.
  </p>
