  <h2>Ion Formation</h2>

  <h3>1. What is an ion?</h3>

  <p>
    An <b>ion</b> is an atom or group of atoms with a net
    electrical charge. An atom forms an ion by gaining or losing
    electrons.
  </p>

  <p>
    Protons are positively charged and remain in the nucleus
    during ordinary chemical ion formation. Electrons are
    transferred, changing the balance between positive and
    negative charge.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Formation of positive ions</h3>

  <p>
    A positive ion is called a <b>cation</b>. It forms when an
    atom loses one or more electrons.
  </p>

  <p><b>Na → Na+ + e-</b></p>

  <p>
    A neutral sodium atom loses one electron to form Na+.
    The proton number remains 11, but the electron number falls
    from 11 to 10.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Formation of negative ions</h3>

  <p>
    A negative ion is called an <b>anion</b>. It forms when an
    atom gains one or more electrons.
  </p>

  <p><b>Cl + e- → Cl-</b></p>

  <p>
    A neutral chlorine atom gains one electron. It still has
    17 protons but now has 18 electrons, giving a charge of -1.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Magnesium ion</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> A magnesium atom has atomic number 12.
  It loses two electrons. Determine its ion symbol and charge.</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Proton number = 12.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Initial electron number = 12.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Final electron number = 12 - 2 = 10.</p>
  <p><b>Step 4:</b> Charge = 12 - 10 = +2.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> Mg2+.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Oxide ion</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Oxygen has atomic number 8 and gains two electrons.
  Determine the number of electrons and the charge of the ion.</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Oxygen initially has 8 electrons when neutral.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> New electron number = 8 + 2 = 10.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Charge = 8 - 10 = -2.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> O2-, containing 10 electrons.</p>

  <h3>Common mistake</h3>

  <p>
    Losing electrons produces a positive ion, not a negative one.
    Gaining electrons produces a negative ion, not a positive one.
    The protons do not disappear during ordinary ion formation.
  </p>
