  <h2>Writing Ion Charges</h2>

  <h3>1. What does an ion charge represent?</h3>

  <p>
    An ion''s charge represents the difference between the number
    of protons and electrons in the particle.
  </p>

  <p><b>Relative charge = number of protons - number of electrons</b></p>

  <p>
    A positive result means a cation; a negative result means
    an anion; zero means the particle is neutral.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Writing ion symbols</h3>

  <p>
    The charge is written as a superscript to the upper right
    of the element symbol.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>Na+ means a charge of +1.</li>
    <li>Mg2+ means a charge of +2.</li>
    <li>Al3+ means a charge of +3.</li>
    <li>Cl- means a charge of -1.</li>
    <li>O2- means a charge of -2.</li>
    <li>N3- means a charge of -3.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    For charges of magnitude one, the numeral 1 is normally omitted.
    The sign must not be omitted because it indicates whether the
    ion is positive or negative.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Charge from particle numbers</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> An ion has 12 protons and 10 electrons.
  Calculate its charge and write its symbol.</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Charge = protons - electrons.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Charge = 12 - 10 = +2.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Atomic number 12 identifies magnesium.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> Mg2+.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Charge of a negative ion</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> A particle contains 17 protons and 18 electrons.
  Determine its ion symbol.</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Charge = 17 - 18 = -1.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Proton number 17 identifies chlorine.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Write the element symbol with its charge.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> Cl-.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Finding the electron number</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> An aluminium ion has charge +3.
  Aluminium has atomic number 13. Calculate the electron number.</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Protons = 13.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Charge = protons - electrons.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> +3 = 13 - electrons.</p>
  <p><b>Step 4:</b> Electrons = 13 - 3 = 10.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> 10 electrons; Al3+.</p>

  <h3>Common mistake</h3>

  <p>
    The charge is not the total number of electrons or protons.
    It is their difference. Always preserve the sign when writing
    the ion symbol.
  </p>
