  <h2>Arrangement of the Periodic Table</h2>

  <h3>1. What is the periodic table?</h3>

  <p>
    The <strong>periodic table</strong> is a systematic arrangement
    of chemical elements in which elements are placed in order of
    increasing atomic number and elements with related chemical
    properties occur in the same groups.
  </p>

  <p>
    An <strong>element</strong> is a pure substance consisting of
    atoms with the same number of protons. Examples include hydrogen,
    oxygen, sodium and iron.
  </p>

  <p>
    Each element has a unique atomic number. The atomic number is the
    number of protons in the nucleus of an atom. For a neutral atom,
    it is also equal to the number of electrons.
  </p>

  <p>
    The periodic table is not simply a list of element names.
    Its arrangement allows us to connect an element''s position
    with its electronic structure and chemical behaviour.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Why are elements arranged by atomic number?</h3>

  <p>
    The atomic number identifies an element. Hydrogen has atomic
    number 1, helium has atomic number 2, lithium has atomic number 3,
    and so on.
  </p>

  <p>
    Each successive element has one more proton than the preceding
    element. For neutral atoms, it also has one more electron.
    This changes the electronic structure in a systematic way.
  </p>

  <p>
    As electrons occupy energy levels, recurring outer-electron
    arrangements appear. Since outer electrons strongly influence
    chemical reactions, elements with related arrangements often
    have similar properties.
  </p>

  <p>
    This recurring pattern is the basis of the periodic arrangement.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Atomic number, symbol and element name</h3>

  <p>
    Every element has a name and a chemical symbol. Symbols are
    one or two letters long in the elements considered here.
    The first letter is capitalised; the second, if present, is
    lowercase.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>H represents hydrogen.</li>
    <li>He represents helium.</li>
    <li>Na represents sodium.</li>
    <li>Cl represents chlorine.</li>
    <li>Ca represents calcium.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Notice that sodium is represented by Na, not S. The symbol
    must be learnt accurately because S represents sulfur.
  </p>

  <h3>4. The structure of the table</h3>

  <p>
    The main structure consists of horizontal rows called
    <strong>periods</strong> and vertical columns called
    <strong>groups</strong>.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Periods:</strong> horizontal rows. For the elements
      covered at this level, the period number tells us the number
      of occupied main electron energy levels.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Groups:</strong> vertical columns. Elements in the
      same main-group column generally have related outer-electron
      arrangements and similar chemical properties.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    These two directions tell us different things. A period is
    associated with the number of occupied energy levels, while
    a group helps us understand the outer electrons and chemical
    behaviour.
  </p>

  <h3>5. The first 20 elements</h3>

  <p>
    Learn to recognise the names, symbols and atomic numbers of
    the first 20 elements. Their sequence is especially useful
    when studying electronic structure and periodic position.
  </p>

  <table>
    <tr>
      <th>Atomic number</th>
      <th>Symbol</th>
      <th>Element</th>
    </tr>
    <tr><td>1</td><td>H</td><td>Hydrogen</td></tr>
    <tr><td>2</td><td>He</td><td>Helium</td></tr>
    <tr><td>3</td><td>Li</td><td>Lithium</td></tr>
    <tr><td>4</td><td>Be</td><td>Beryllium</td></tr>
    <tr><td>5</td><td>B</td><td>Boron</td></tr>
    <tr><td>6</td><td>C</td><td>Carbon</td></tr>
    <tr><td>7</td><td>N</td><td>Nitrogen</td></tr>
    <tr><td>8</td><td>O</td><td>Oxygen</td></tr>
    <tr><td>9</td><td>F</td><td>Fluorine</td></tr>
    <tr><td>10</td><td>Ne</td><td>Neon</td></tr>
    <tr><td>11</td><td>Na</td><td>Sodium</td></tr>
    <tr><td>12</td><td>Mg</td><td>Magnesium</td></tr>
    <tr><td>13</td><td>Al</td><td>Aluminium</td></tr>
    <tr><td>14</td><td>Si</td><td>Silicon</td></tr>
    <tr><td>15</td><td>P</td><td>Phosphorus</td></tr>
    <tr><td>16</td><td>S</td><td>Sulfur</td></tr>
    <tr><td>17</td><td>Cl</td><td>Chlorine</td></tr>
    <tr><td>18</td><td>Ar</td><td>Argon</td></tr>
    <tr><td>19</td><td>K</td><td>Potassium</td></tr>
    <tr><td>20</td><td>Ca</td><td>Calcium</td></tr>
  </table>

  <h3>6. How to read an element''s position</h3>

  <p>
    Suppose an element has atomic number 12. A neutral atom has
    12 protons and 12 electrons. Its electron arrangement is 2,8,2.
    This places it in Period 3 and Group 2.
  </p>

  <p>
    Its position is useful because it allows us to predict that
    magnesium has two outer electrons and commonly forms Mg²⁺ ions
    in its compounds.
  </p>

  <p>
    Therefore, the periodic table connects three important ideas:
    atomic number, electronic structure and chemical properties.
  </p>

  <h3>Common mistakes to avoid</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>
      Do not confuse atomic number with mass number. Atomic number
      counts protons; mass number counts protons plus neutrons.
    </li>
    <li>
      Do not confuse a period with a group. Periods run horizontally;
      groups run vertically.
    </li>
    <li>
      Do not assume that neighbouring elements always have similar
      chemical properties. Similarity is especially useful when
      comparing elements in the same group.
    </li>
    <li>
      Do not use the element''s mass to determine its position.
      Modern periodic order is based on atomic number.
    </li>
  </ul>
