  <h2>Groups in the Periodic Table</h2>

  <h3>1. Definition of a group</h3>

  <p>
    A <strong>group</strong> is a vertical column in the periodic
    table. Elements in the same main group generally have the same
    number of outer-shell electrons, except for special cases such
    as helium, and consequently often show similar chemical
    properties.
  </p>

  <p>
    The electrons in the outermost occupied energy level are called
    <strong>outer-shell electrons</strong> or, in this context,
    valence electrons. They are particularly important because they
    participate in many chemical reactions and chemical bonds.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Why does group membership matter?</h3>

  <p>
    Consider lithium, sodium and potassium. Their electron
    arrangements are:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>Lithium: 2,1</li>
    <li>Sodium: 2,8,1</li>
    <li>Potassium: 2,8,8,1</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    The total number of electrons differs, but each atom has one
    electron in its outermost occupied energy level.
  </p>

  <p>
    Each can lose that outer electron to form an ion with a charge
    of +1. Their similar outer-electron arrangements explain why
    they belong to the same chemical family and have related
    chemical behaviour.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Important groups and their names</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Group 1 — alkali metals:</strong> lithium, sodium
      and potassium are examples. They have one outer electron
      and commonly form +1 ions.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Group 2 — alkaline earth metals:</strong> magnesium
      and calcium are examples. They have two outer electrons and
      commonly form +2 ions.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Group 7 in the older school notation — halogens:</strong>
      fluorine and chlorine are examples. They have seven outer
      electrons and commonly form −1 ions.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Group 0 in the older school notation — noble gases:</strong>
      helium, neon and argon are examples. Their outer energy levels
      are full, so they are generally unreactive.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    <strong>Notation note:</strong> Many Kenyan school resources
    use Groups 1, 2, 7 and 0 for these familiar families. In modern
    IUPAC numbering, the halogens are Group 17 and the noble gases
    are Group 18. The chemistry is the same; the numbering system
    differs.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Finding a group from an electron arrangement</h3>

  <p>
    For the main-group elements covered in the first 20 elements,
    the number of outer electrons is a useful guide to group
    membership.
  </p>

  <p>
    For example, an atom with arrangement 2,8,6 has three occupied
    energy levels and six outer electrons. It is sulfur, a Period 3
    element in Group 6 under the older school notation.
  </p>

  <p>
    This simple rule should not be applied indiscriminately to every
    element in the periodic table. Transition elements have more
    complicated electron arrangements and require additional rules.
  </p>

  <h3>5. Groups and chemical reactivity</h3>

  <p>
    Group membership can help predict how atoms form ions and
    compounds. For example, sodium tends to lose one electron,
    while chlorine tends to gain one electron.
  </p>

  <p>
    Sodium forms Na⁺ and chlorine forms Cl⁻. Oppositely charged
    ions attract, producing the ionic compound sodium chloride,
    NaCl.
  </p>

  <p>
    The same group does not mean identical properties. Sodium and
    potassium both belong to Group 1, but potassium is more
    reactive. Related outer-electron arrangements produce
    similarities, while differences in atomic size and shielding
    affect the details.
  </p>

  <h3>Common mistakes to avoid</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Do not confuse a group with a period.</li>
    <li>
      Do not assume that elements in one group have the same total
      number of electrons.
    </li>
    <li>
      Do not confuse outer electrons with total electrons.
    </li>
    <li>
      Do not assume every group follows the simple outer-electron
      rule without exceptions.
    </li>
  </ul>
