  <h2>Periods in the Periodic Table</h2>

  <h3>1. What is a period?</h3>

  <p>
    A <strong>period</strong> is a horizontal row in the periodic
    table. Elements in the same period have the same number of
    occupied main electron energy levels.
  </p>

  <p>
    An energy level is a region associated with electrons having
    particular allowed energies around the nucleus. At this level,
    electron arrangements such as 2,8,1 provide a simplified way
    of showing how electrons are distributed among occupied levels.
  </p>

  <h3>2. The connection between energy levels and periods</h3>

  <p>
    For the first 20 elements, count the numbers in an electron
    arrangement to find the number of occupied energy levels.
    The number of occupied levels gives the period number.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      Hydrogen: 1. There is one occupied energy level, so hydrogen
      is in Period 1.
    </li>
    <li>
      Carbon: 2,4. There are two occupied energy levels, so carbon
      is in Period 2.
    </li>
    <li>
      Sodium: 2,8,1. There are three occupied energy levels, so
      sodium is in Period 3.
    </li>
    <li>
      Calcium: 2,8,8,2. There are four occupied energy levels,
      so calcium is in Period 4.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>3. Why do energy levels matter?</h3>

  <p>
    Each occupied energy level contributes to the overall size
    and electron structure of an atom. Moving down a group
    generally introduces an additional occupied energy level.
  </p>

  <p>
    The outer electrons are then, on average, farther from the
    nucleus. This affects atomic size, shielding and the attraction
    between the nucleus and electrons.
  </p>

  <p>
    These effects help explain why properties change down a group.
    The period number therefore provides more than a position
    label: it gives information about the atom''s electron structure.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Periods are not the same as groups</h3>

  <p>
    Consider magnesium, with arrangement 2,8,2, and chlorine,
    with arrangement 2,8,7. Both have three occupied energy levels,
    so both belong to Period 3.
  </p>

  <p>
    However, magnesium has two outer electrons and chlorine has
    seven. Their chemical properties differ significantly.
  </p>

  <p>
    This shows why period number alone does not tell us everything
    about an element. We also need its group and outer-electron
    arrangement.
  </p>

  <h3>5. Determining the period from a diagram or arrangement</h3>

  <p>
    If an electron arrangement is provided, count the occupied
    levels rather than adding all the electrons.
  </p>

  <p>
    For example, 2,8,5 contains 15 electrons distributed across
    three occupied energy levels. The period is 3, not 15 and
    not 5.
  </p>

  <p>
    This arrangement belongs to phosphorus. The final number
    indicates five outer electrons, but the number of occupied
    levels determines the period.
  </p>

  <h3>Common mistakes to avoid</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>
      Do not use the total number of electrons as the period number.
    </li>
    <li>
      Do not use the number of outer electrons to determine the period.
    </li>
    <li>
      Do not confuse the number of occupied levels with the maximum
      capacity of an energy level.
    </li>
    <li>
      When using simplified shell arrangements, remember that the
      method is being applied to the elements for which that
      notation is appropriate.
    </li>
  </ul>
