  <h2>Electronic Structure and Periodic Position</h2>

  <h3>1. What is electronic structure?</h3>

  <p>
    The <strong>electronic structure</strong> of an atom describes
    how its electrons are distributed among energy levels around
    the nucleus.
  </p>

  <p>
    The nucleus contains protons and neutrons. Electrons occupy
    allowed energy levels outside the nucleus. In the simplified
    shell notation used for the first 20 elements, we represent
    electron arrangements using numbers separated by commas.
  </p>

  <p>Examples:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>Hydrogen: 1</li>
    <li>Carbon: 2,4</li>
    <li>Sodium: 2,8,1</li>
    <li>Calcium: 2,8,8,2</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    The arrangement 2,8,1 means that the atom has two electrons
    in the first occupied energy level, eight in the second and
    one in the third.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Connecting atomic number to electrons</h3>

  <p>
    The <strong>atomic number</strong> is the number of protons
    in an atom''s nucleus. In a neutral atom, the number of
    electrons equals the number of protons.
  </p>

  <p>
    Therefore, the atomic number gives us the total number of
    electrons to distribute when writing the electronic structure
    of a neutral atom.
  </p>

  <p>
    For example, magnesium has atomic number 12. A neutral
    magnesium atom has 12 protons and 12 electrons. Its
    electronic structure is 2,8,2.
  </p>

  <h3>3. How to write an electron arrangement</h3>

  <p>
    For the first 20 elements, use the simplified shell capacities
    appropriate to this level: the first shell holds up to 2
    electrons, the second up to 8, and the third up to 8 for
    these elements.
  </p>

  <p>
    To write an arrangement:
  </p>

  <ol>
    <li>Find the atomic number.</li>
    <li>For a neutral atom, use that number as the total number of electrons.</li>
    <li>Place up to 2 electrons in the first shell.</li>
    <li>Place up to 8 in the second shell.</li>
    <li>For these first 20 elements, place up to 8 in the third shell before starting the fourth.</li>
    <li>Check that the numbers add up to the total number of electrons.</li>
  </ol>

  <p>
    This is a school-level shell model. Electron arrangements
    become more complicated for elements beyond the range
    covered by this simplified method.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Finding the period</h3>

  <p>
    For the elements covered here, the number of occupied
    main energy levels determines the period.
  </p>

  <p>
    An atom with arrangement 2,8,3 has three occupied levels.
    It is therefore in Period 3.
  </p>

  <h3>5. Finding the group</h3>

  <p>
    For main-group elements, the number of outer-shell electrons
    helps determine group membership.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>One outer electron: Group 1.</li>
    <li>Two outer electrons: Group 2.</li>
    <li>Six outer electrons: Group 6 in the older school notation.</li>
    <li>Seven outer electrons: Group 7 in the older school notation.</li>
    <li>A full outer shell: Group 0 in the older school notation, with helium as a special case.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    In modern IUPAC numbering, these familiar groups include
    Group 1, Group 2, Group 16 for the chalcogens, Group 17
    for the halogens and Group 18 for the noble gases.
  </p>

  <p>
    Do not apply the simple outer-electron rule to transition
    elements without considering their more complicated
    electronic structures.
  </p>

  <h3>6. Predicting chemical behaviour</h3>

  <p>
    Outer electrons are especially important in chemical reactions.
    Atoms may lose, gain or share electrons when forming ions
    or chemical bonds.
  </p>

  <p>
    Sodium has the arrangement 2,8,1. It commonly loses one
    electron to form Na⁺, giving it the electron arrangement
    2,8.
  </p>

  <p>
    Chlorine has the arrangement 2,8,7. It can gain one electron
    to form Cl⁻, giving it the arrangement 2,8,8.
  </p>

  <p>
    The resulting ions have stable outer-shell arrangements.
    Sodium chloride contains Na⁺ and Cl⁻ ions held together
    by electrostatic attraction.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Electronic structure from atomic number</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Write the electronic structure of aluminium, atomic number 13.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1: Find the total electrons.</strong></p>

  <p>
    Aluminium has atomic number 13. A neutral aluminium atom
    therefore contains 13 electrons.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2: Fill the first shell.</strong></p>

  <p>
    The first shell holds 2 electrons. Remaining electrons:
    13 − 2 = 11.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3: Fill the second shell.</strong></p>

  <p>
    The second shell holds 8 electrons. Remaining electrons:
    11 − 8 = 3.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 4: Place the remaining electrons.</strong></p>

  <p>
    The third shell receives the remaining 3 electrons.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 2,8,3.</p>

  <p>
    <strong>Check:</strong> 2 + 8 + 3 = 13. The arrangement
    accounts for every electron.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Finding group and period</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Determine the group and period of an element with arrangement 2,8,6.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1: Count the occupied levels.</strong></p>

  <p>
    There are three occupied levels, so the element is in Period 3.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2: Count the outer electrons.</strong></p>

  <p>
    The outermost level contains 6 electrons.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3: Determine the group.</strong></p>

  <p>
    Six outer electrons place this main-group element in Group 6
    using the older school notation.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Group 6, Period 3. The element is sulfur.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Inferring an ion''s electronic structure</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> A neutral sodium atom has 11 electrons. What is the electronic structure of Na⁺?</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1: Write the neutral arrangement.</strong></p>

  <p>
    Sodium has 11 electrons arranged as 2,8,1.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2: Interpret the charge.</strong></p>

  <p>
    The +1 charge means sodium has lost one electron.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3: Recalculate the number of electrons.</strong></p>

  <p>
    11 − 1 = 10 electrons remain.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 4: Distribute the electrons.</strong></p>

  <p>
    Ten electrons are arranged as 2,8.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Na⁺ has electronic structure 2,8.</p>

  <p>
    <strong>Why it matters:</strong> When forming a positive ion,
    an atom loses electrons; it does not lose protons from its
    nucleus.
  </p>

  <h3>Common mistakes</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Confusing atomic number with mass number.</li>
    <li>Adding the shell numbers incorrectly.</li>
    <li>Using total electrons instead of outer electrons to identify the main group.</li>
    <li>Forgetting that a positive ion has fewer electrons than its neutral atom.</li>
    <li>Assuming that an ion''s charge changes its atomic number.</li>
  </ul>
