  <h2>Group 1 Elements: The Alkali Metals</h2>

  <h3>1. What are Group 1 elements?</h3>

  <p>
    Group 1 elements are called the <strong>alkali metals</strong>.
    Important examples are lithium (Li), sodium (Na) and
    potassium (K).
  </p>

  <p>
    Hydrogen is positioned above Group 1 in the periodic table
    but is a non-metal and is not classified as an alkali metal.
    Its position should not lead us to treat it as chemically
    identical to lithium, sodium or potassium.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Electronic structure</h3>

  <p>
    Group 1 alkali metals have one electron in their outermost
    occupied energy level.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>Lithium: 2,1</li>
    <li>Sodium: 2,8,1</li>
    <li>Potassium: 2,8,8,1</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Their inner electron arrangements differ, but each has one
    outer electron. This explains their similar reactions.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Why do Group 1 metals form +1 ions?</h3>

  <p>
    A neutral sodium atom has 11 protons and 11 electrons.
    Its electron arrangement is 2,8,1.
  </p>

  <p>
    If sodium loses its single outer electron, it has 11 protons
    but only 10 electrons. The positive charge of its protons
    exceeds the negative charge of its electrons by one.
  </p>

  <p>
    The resulting ion is Na⁺, with arrangement 2,8.
  </p>

  <p>
    Losing one electron also produces a full outer shell in this
    example. Similar reasoning explains why lithium forms Li⁺
    and potassium forms K⁺.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Physical properties</h3>

  <p>
    Alkali metals are generally soft, silvery metals when freshly
    cut and are good conductors of heat and electricity. They
    have relatively low melting points compared with many other
    metals.
  </p>

  <p>
    They are stored carefully because they react with substances
    in their surroundings. Freshly cut surfaces quickly lose
    their shine as they react with air.
  </p>

  <h3>5. Reaction with water</h3>

  <p>
    Group 1 metals react with water to form a metal hydroxide
    and hydrogen gas.
  </p>

  <p><strong>General word equation:</strong></p>

  <p>alkali metal + water → metal hydroxide + hydrogen</p>

  <p>
    For sodium, the balanced symbol equation is:
  </p>

  <p>2Na + 2H₂O → 2NaOH + H₂</p>

  <p>
    The products are sodium hydroxide, which dissolves in water,
    and hydrogen gas.
  </p>

  <p>
    Hydrogen is flammable. Group 1 reactions can be dangerous,
    particularly for the more reactive metals. These reactions
    must only be demonstrated by trained professionals using
    appropriate safety controls; learners should not attempt
    them at home.
  </p>

  <h3>6. Why does reactivity increase down Group 1?</h3>

  <p>
    Potassium is generally more reactive than sodium, and sodium
    is generally more reactive than lithium.
  </p>

  <p>
    Moving down the group adds occupied energy levels. The
    outer electron is farther from the nucleus and is shielded
    by more inner electrons.
  </p>

  <p>
    Although the nucleus has more protons, the combined effects
    of increased distance and shielding reduce the attraction
    holding the outer electron. It can therefore be removed
    more easily.
  </p>

  <p>
    Since Group 1 reactions depend strongly on losing this
    electron, reactivity generally increases down the group.
  </p>

  <h3>7. Worked Example 1: Identifying a Group 1 element</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> An element has electron arrangement 2,8,1. Explain why it belongs to Group 1.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the outermost energy level.</p>

  <p>
    The last number in 2,8,1 is 1.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Interpret the number.</p>

  <p>
    The atom has one outer electron.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Connect the arrangement to group membership.</p>

  <p>
    One outer electron identifies Group 1 among the main-group
    elements covered here.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> It belongs to Group 1 because it has one outer electron.</p>

  <h3>8. Worked Example 2: Predicting the ion charge</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Predict the charge of the ion formed when potassium loses its outer electron.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Potassium has arrangement 2,8,8,1.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> It loses one electron.</p>

  <p>
    The number of protons is now one greater than the number
    of electrons.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Determine the net charge.</p>

  <p>
    Losing one negative charge gives a net charge of +1.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> K⁺.</p>

  <h3>9. Worked Example 3: Balancing the reaction with water</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Balance the equation for sodium reacting with water:</p>

  <p>Na + H₂O → NaOH + H₂</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Count atoms on both sides.</p>

  <p>
    Initially, the left side contains one Na, two H and one O.
    The right side contains one Na, three H and one O in total.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Balance sodium and the hydroxide product.</p>

  <p>
    Put 2 before Na and NaOH:
    2Na + H₂O → 2NaOH + H₂.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Balance oxygen and hydrogen.</p>

  <p>
    There are two oxygen atoms in 2NaOH, so put 2 before H₂O.
    The equation becomes:
  </p>

  <p>2Na + 2H₂O → 2NaOH + H₂</p>

  <p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Check the atoms.</p>

  <ul>
    <li>Na: 2 on each side.</li>
    <li>O: 2 on each side.</li>
    <li>H: 4 on each side.</li>
  </ul>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 2Na + 2H₂O → 2NaOH + H₂.</p>

  <p>
    <strong>Key principle:</strong> Balancing changes the
    coefficients in front of formulae, not the chemical formulae
    themselves.
  </p>

  <h3>Common mistakes</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Confusing the formation of a positive ion with gaining electrons.</li>
    <li>Forgetting that hydrogen is not an alkali metal.</li>
    <li>Changing subscripts to balance an equation.</li>
    <li>Explaining increased reactivity merely by saying that atoms get bigger without connecting this to electron loss.</li>
  </ul>
