  <h2>Group 2 Elements: The Alkaline Earth Metals</h2>

  <h3>1. Identity and position</h3>

  <p>
    Group 2 elements are called the <strong>alkaline earth metals</strong>.
    Examples include beryllium (Be), magnesium (Mg) and calcium (Ca).
  </p>

  <p>
    They are metals and occupy the second column of the main-group
    region of the periodic table.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Electronic structure</h3>

  <p>
    Group 2 elements have two electrons in their outermost
    occupied energy level.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>Beryllium: 2,2</li>
    <li>Magnesium: 2,8,2</li>
    <li>Calcium: 2,8,8,2</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Their inner shells differ, but the outermost shell contains
    two electrons in each case.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Formation of 2+ ions</h3>

  <p>
    Magnesium has 12 protons and 12 electrons. Its neutral
    electronic structure is 2,8,2.
  </p>

  <p>
    Magnesium can lose its two outer electrons. The resulting
    Mg²⁺ ion has 12 protons and 10 electrons, giving it a
    net charge of +2.
  </p>

  <p>
    Its electronic structure becomes 2,8.
  </p>

  <p>
    The general process can be represented as:
  </p>

  <p>Mg → Mg²⁺ + 2e⁻</p>

  <p>
    This equation shows that a magnesium atom loses two electrons.
    It does not mean that magnesium loses two protons.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Physical and chemical properties</h3>

  <p>
    Group 2 elements are metals and generally conduct heat and
    electricity. Their properties vary down the group.
  </p>

  <p>
    Magnesium burns in oxygen to produce magnesium oxide:
  </p>

  <p>2Mg + O₂ → 2MgO</p>

  <p>
    Magnesium oxide is a basic oxide. It reacts with acids to
    form a salt and water.
  </p>

  <p>
    Calcium reacts with water to produce calcium hydroxide
    and hydrogen:
  </p>

  <p>Ca + 2H₂O → Ca(OH)₂ + H₂</p>

  <p>
    This reaction is less vigorous than the reactions of
    potassium with water under comparable conditions.
    Beryllium does not react readily with water, while magnesium
    reacts only slowly with cold water.
  </p>

  <h3>5. Reactivity down Group 2</h3>

  <p>
    For the Group 2 metals, reactivity generally increases
    down the group.
  </p>

  <p>
    Moving down the group adds occupied energy levels. The
    outer electrons are farther from the nucleus and are more
    shielded by inner electrons.
  </p>

  <p>
    These effects reduce the attraction between the nucleus
    and the outer electrons. Losing two electrons becomes
    easier, so the metals generally become more reactive.
  </p>

  <p>
    The trend is related to the energy required to remove
    electrons, not simply to the number of protons in the nucleus.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Finding the group</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> An atom has arrangement 2,8,2. Explain why it belongs to Group 2.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Examine the final number.</p>

  <p>
    The final number is 2.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Interpret the arrangement.</p>

  <p>
    The atom has two outer electrons.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Determine the group.</p>

  <p>
    Two outer electrons indicate Group 2 for this main-group element.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Group 2; the element is magnesium.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Formation of magnesium ions</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Write the equation showing magnesium forming a magnesium ion.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Write the neutral atom.</p>

  <p>Mg</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Identify the electrons lost.</p>

  <p>
    Magnesium loses two electrons, represented by 2e⁻.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Write the product and balance charge.</p>

  <p>Mg → Mg²⁺ + 2e⁻</p>

  <p>
    The total charge on the right is +2 − 2 = 0, matching
    the neutral atom on the left.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Mg → Mg²⁺ + 2e⁻.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Predicting the period and group of calcium</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Calcium has atomic number 20. Determine its electronic structure, group and period.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Count its electrons.</p>

  <p>
    A neutral calcium atom has 20 electrons.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Distribute the electrons.</p>

  <p>
    Put 2 in the first shell, 8 in the second and 8 in the
    third. The remaining 2 occupy the fourth shell.
  </p>

  <p>
    Electronic structure = 2,8,8,2.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Determine the period.</p>

  <p>
    Four shells are occupied, so calcium is in Period 4.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Determine the group.</p>

  <p>
    The outermost shell contains two electrons, so calcium
    belongs to Group 2.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 2,8,8,2; Group 2, Period 4.</p>

  <h3>Common mistakes</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Writing Mg⁺ instead of Mg²⁺ when magnesium loses two electrons.</li>
    <li>Confusing a 2+ charge with two additional electrons.</li>
    <li>Assuming every Group 2 metal reacts with cold water equally vigorously.</li>
    <li>Forgetting that an ion''s proton number stays unchanged when electrons are lost.</li>
  </ul>
