  <h2>Group 7 Elements: The Halogens</h2>

  <h3>1. What are halogens?</h3>

  <p>
    The <strong>halogens</strong> are the elements in Group 17
    of the modern IUPAC periodic table. They are often called
    Group 7 elements in the older school notation.
  </p>

  <p>
    Important examples include fluorine (F), chlorine (Cl),
    bromine (Br) and iodine (I).
  </p>

  <h3>2. Electronic structure</h3>

  <p>
    Halogens have seven electrons in their outermost occupied
    energy level.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>Fluorine: 2,7</li>
    <li>Chlorine: 2,8,7</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    These arrangements show that each atom has seven outer
    electrons. An atom with seven outer electrons can gain one
    electron to reach eight in its outer shell.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Why do halogens form −1 ions?</h3>

  <p>
    A neutral chlorine atom has 17 protons and 17 electrons.
    Its arrangement is 2,8,7.
  </p>

  <p>
    If chlorine gains one electron, it has 17 protons and
    18 electrons. It now has one more electron than protons,
    so its net charge is −1.
  </p>

  <p>
    The resulting chloride ion is Cl⁻, with arrangement 2,8,8.
  </p>

  <p>Cl + e⁻ → Cl⁻</p>

  <p>
    This represents electron gain. Unlike Group 1 metals,
    halogens tend to gain rather than lose an electron when
    forming simple ions.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Physical properties</h3>

  <p>
    Halogens are non-metals and exist as diatomic molecules
    under ordinary conditions. A diatomic molecule contains
    two atoms joined together.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>Fluorine: F₂, a pale yellow gas.</li>
    <li>Chlorine: Cl₂, a greenish-yellow gas.</li>
    <li>Bromine: Br₂, a red-brown liquid.</li>
    <li>Iodine: I₂, a grey-black solid that gives purple vapour when heated.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Chlorine and other halogens can be toxic or corrosive.
    They must not be handled or inhaled outside an appropriately
    controlled laboratory.
  </p>

  <h3>5. Reactions with metals</h3>

  <p>
    Halogens react with metals to form metal halides. The metal
    generally loses electrons, while the halogen gains electrons.
  </p>

  <p>
    For example, sodium reacts with chlorine to form sodium chloride:
  </p>

  <p>2Na + Cl₂ → 2NaCl</p>

  <p>
    Each sodium atom forms Na⁺ by losing one electron. Each
    chlorine atom forms Cl⁻ by gaining one electron. Oppositely
    charged ions attract to form the ionic compound.
  </p>

  <h3>6. Displacement reactions</h3>

  <p>
    A more reactive halogen can displace a less reactive halogen
    from a solution containing its halide ions.
  </p>

  <p>
    For example, chlorine displaces bromine from potassium
    bromide solution:
  </p>

  <p>Cl₂ + 2KBr → 2KCl + Br₂</p>

  <p>
    Chlorine is more reactive than bromine, so chlorine gains
    electrons from bromide ions and produces chloride ions.
    Bromide ions lose electrons and form bromine.
  </p>

  <h3>7. Why does halogen reactivity decrease down the group?</h3>

  <p>
    Halogens react by gaining an electron. Moving down the group
    adds occupied energy levels, making the atom larger and
    increasing the shielding of the nucleus by inner electrons.
  </p>

  <p>
    An incoming electron experiences competing effects: the
    nucleus has more protons down the group, but the electron
    is entering a larger atom with greater shielding and a
    greater distance from the nucleus.
  </p>

  <p>
    The overall attraction for an incoming electron generally
    becomes less effective down the group. Therefore, halogen
    reactivity generally decreases down the group.
  </p>

  <p>
    Fluorine is more reactive than chlorine, which is more
    reactive than bromine, which is more reactive than iodine.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Formation of a chloride ion</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Explain how a chlorine atom forms Cl⁻.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Write the initial arrangement.</p>

  <p>
    Chlorine has 17 electrons arranged as 2,8,7.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Determine the electron change.</p>

  <p>
    Chlorine gains one electron.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Calculate the new number of electrons.</p>

  <p>
    17 + 1 = 18 electrons.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Write the new arrangement and charge.</p>

  <p>
    The arrangement becomes 2,8,8. One additional electron
    gives the ion a charge of −1.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Cl + e⁻ → Cl⁻.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Predicting reactivity</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Which is more reactive, chlorine or iodine? Explain why.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Locate the elements.</p>

  <p>
    Both are halogens. Chlorine is above iodine in the group.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Recall the trend.</p>

  <p>
    Halogen reactivity generally decreases down the group.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Explain the cause.</p>

  <p>
    Chlorine has fewer occupied energy levels and less shielding
    than iodine, so it attracts an incoming electron more
    effectively under comparable conditions.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Chlorine is more reactive than iodine.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Predicting a displacement reaction</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Predict what happens when chlorine is added to potassium bromide solution.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the halogens.</p>

  <p>
    Chlorine is the free halogen. Potassium bromide contains
    bromide ions.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Compare their reactivity.</p>

  <p>
    Chlorine is more reactive than bromine.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Predict the displacement.</p>

  <p>
    Chlorine displaces bromine from the bromide solution.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Write the balanced equation.</p>

  <p>Cl₂ + 2KBr → 2KCl + Br₂</p>

  <p>
    <strong>Answer:</strong> Potassium chloride and bromine form.
  </p>

  <h3>Common mistakes</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Confusing electron gain with electron loss.</li>
    <li>Writing a chloride ion as Cl⁺ rather than Cl⁻.</li>
    <li>Forgetting that elemental chlorine exists as Cl₂.</li>
    <li>Assuming a less reactive halogen can displace a more reactive one.</li>
  </ul>
