  <h2>Group 0 Elements: The Noble Gases</h2>

  <h3>1. What are noble gases?</h3>

  <p>
    The <strong>noble gases</strong> occupy Group 18 in modern
    IUPAC numbering. They are often called Group 0 elements
    in older school notation.
  </p>

  <p>
    Important examples include helium (He), neon (Ne) and
    argon (Ar).
  </p>

  <h3>2. Electronic structure</h3>

  <p>
    Noble gases have full outer electron shells. For helium,
    the first shell is full with two electrons. For neon and
    argon, the outer shell contains eight electrons.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>Helium: 2</li>
    <li>Neon: 2,8</li>
    <li>Argon: 2,8,8</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    A full outer shell makes it energetically unfavourable for
    these atoms to gain, lose or share electrons in many ordinary
    chemical reactions.
  </p>

  <p>
    This is why noble gases are generally described as
    <strong>unreactive</strong> or <strong>inert</strong>.
    Their reactivity is very low, although it is not scientifically
    correct to claim that no noble gas compound can ever exist.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Why helium is special</h3>

  <p>
    The first energy level can hold a maximum of two electrons.
    Helium has exactly two electrons, so its only occupied
    energy level is full.
  </p>

  <p>
    Helium does not need eight outer electrons. The first shell
    is complete with two. This explains why helium is a noble
    gas even though it has fewer electrons than neon or argon.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Physical properties</h3>

  <p>
    Noble gases are colourless, monatomic gases at room
    temperature. <strong>Monatomic</strong> means that each
    gas particle is a single atom rather than a molecule
    containing two or more atoms.
  </p>

  <p>
    Their atoms have weak attractions between them. As a result,
    noble gases have low boiling points. Their boiling points
    generally increase down the group as atoms become larger
    and the attractions between their electron clouds increase.
  </p>

  <h3>5. Uses of noble gases</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Helium:</strong> used in balloons and in some
      specialised cooling applications.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Neon:</strong> used in illuminated signs.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Argon:</strong> used in electric bulbs and as
      a protective atmosphere in some welding processes.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    These applications make use of the gases'' low reactivity
    or other physical properties.
  </p>

  <h3>6. Why noble gases rarely form compounds</h3>

  <p>
    Chemical bonding often involves changes in electron
    distribution. An atom with a full outer shell generally
    has little tendency to gain or lose electrons.
  </p>

  <p>
    Noble gases also have relatively high ionisation energies,
    meaning that considerable energy is required to remove
    an electron. Their full outer shells therefore help explain
    their low chemical reactivity.
  </p>

  <p>
    Heavier noble gases, especially xenon, can form compounds
    under suitable conditions. This is why "very unreactive"
    is more accurate than "absolutely incapable of reacting."
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Explaining helium''s group</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Explain why helium is a noble gas despite having only two electrons.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify its electron arrangement.</p>

  <p>
    Helium has two electrons, arranged as 2.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Consider the first shell''s capacity.</p>

  <p>
    The first shell holds a maximum of two electrons.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Determine whether the shell is full.</p>

  <p>
    Both available positions are occupied.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Helium is a noble gas because its only shell is full.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Predicting reactivity from electron structure</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Explain why neon is generally unreactive.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Write its electron arrangement.</p>

  <p>
    Neon has arrangement 2,8.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Examine its outer shell.</p>

  <p>
    The outer shell contains eight electrons and is full.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Connect this to electron transfer.</p>

  <p>
    Neon has little tendency to gain or lose electrons in
    ordinary reactions.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Neon is generally unreactive because its outer shell is full.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Identifying a noble gas</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> An atom has electronic structure 2,8,8. Identify the element and explain its low reactivity.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Count the electrons.</p>

  <p>
    2 + 8 + 8 = 18 electrons.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Identify the atomic number.</p>

  <p>
    For a neutral atom, 18 electrons correspond to 18 protons.
    Atomic number 18 identifies argon.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Examine the outer shell.</p>

  <p>
    The outermost shell contains eight electrons and is full.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Argon (Ar), a noble gas with low chemical reactivity.</p>

  <h3>Common mistakes</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Assuming every full shell must contain eight electrons.</li>
    <li>Forgetting that helium''s first shell is full with two electrons.</li>
    <li>Claiming noble gases can never form compounds.</li>
    <li>Confusing monatomic atoms with diatomic molecules.</li>
  </ul>
