  (778, 778, '
  <h2>Electron Transfer</h2>

  <h3>1. Meaning of electron transfer</h3>
  <p>
    <b>Electron transfer</b> is the movement of one or more electrons
    from one particle to another. In many introductory examples of
    ionic bonding, a metal atom loses electrons and a non-metal atom
    gains them.
  </p>

  <p>
    Losing electrons forms a positive ion. Gaining electrons forms a
    negative ion. The number of protons in the nucleus does not change
    during ordinary ion formation.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Electron loss</h3>
  <p>
    A neutral sodium atom contains 11 protons and 11 electrons.
    When it loses one electron, it still has 11 protons but only
    10 electrons. Its net charge becomes +1.
  </p>
  <p><b>Equation:</b> Na → Na⁺ + e⁻</p>

  <h3>3. Electron gain</h3>
  <p>
    A neutral chlorine atom contains 17 protons and 17 electrons.
    When it gains one electron, it has 17 protons and 18 electrons.
    Its net charge becomes −1.
  </p>
  <p><b>Equation:</b> Cl + e⁻ → Cl⁻</p>

  <h3>4. Electrons are conserved</h3>
  <p>
    In a transfer between sodium and chlorine, the electron lost by
    sodium is the electron gained by chlorine. The transfer must be
    accounted for on both sides. It does not mean that the electron
    disappears or that a new electron is created.
  </p>

  <h3>5. Predicting the number of electrons transferred</h3>
  <p>
    The common ion formed by an atom helps identify the number of
    electrons transferred. Magnesium commonly loses two electrons
    to form Mg²⁺. Oxygen commonly gains two electrons to form O²⁻.
    Their charges balance in magnesium oxide, MgO.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Write the electron-loss equation for sodium</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> Write an equation showing a sodium atom forming a sodium ion.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Sodium loses one electron.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Write Na on the left.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Write Na⁺ and the released electron e⁻ on the right.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Na → Na⁺ + e⁻.</p>
  <p><b>Check:</b> The total charge on the right is +1 − 1 = 0, matching the neutral atom on the left.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Write the electron-gain equation for oxygen</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> Write the equation for a neutral oxygen atom gaining two electrons to form an oxide ion.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Oxygen gains two electrons.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Place O and two electrons on the left.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Place O²⁻ on the right.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> O + 2e⁻ → O²⁻.</p>
  <p><b>Check:</b> The left side has total charge −2 and the right side has charge −2.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Balance electron transfer between magnesium and chlorine</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> Magnesium forms Mg²⁺ and chlorine forms Cl⁻. How many chlorine atoms are needed to accept the electrons lost by one magnesium atom?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Magnesium loses two electrons: Mg → Mg²⁺ + 2e⁻.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Each chlorine atom gains one electron: Cl + e⁻ → Cl⁻.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Two chlorine atoms are needed to accept the two electrons.</p>
  <p><b>Step 4:</b> The overall transfer is Mg + 2Cl → Mg²⁺ + 2Cl⁻.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Two chlorine atoms accept the two electrons lost by one magnesium atom. The resulting ion ratio is 1:2, giving MgCl₂.</p>

  <h3>Key idea</h3>
  <p>
    Track electrons explicitly. Electron loss produces positive ions,
    electron gain produces negative ions, and the number of electrons
    lost must equal the number gained in the transfer.
  </p>
