  (781, 781, '
  <h2>Formation of Ionic Compounds</h2>

  <h3>1. How an ionic compound forms</h3>
  <p>
    An ionic compound forms when oppositely charged ions are held
    together by electrostatic attraction. In many simple examples,
    electrons transfer from metal atoms to non-metal atoms, producing
    positive and negative ions.
  </p>

  <p>
    The electron transfer explains how the ions form. The ionic bond
    is the attraction between the resulting opposite charges.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Formation of sodium chloride</h3>
  <p>
    Sodium loses one electron to form Na⁺. Chlorine gains one electron
    to form Cl⁻. The ions attract, and the solid forms a giant ionic
    lattice with a 1:1 ratio of sodium ions to chloride ions.
  </p>
  <p><b>Sodium:</b> Na → Na⁺ + e⁻</p>
  <p><b>Chlorine:</b> Cl + e⁻ → Cl⁻</p>
  <p><b>Formula:</b> NaCl</p>

  <h3>3. Ionic formulae must be electrically neutral</h3>
  <p>
    The total positive charge in an ionic formula must balance the
    total negative charge. The formula gives the simplest whole-number
    ratio of ions.
  </p>

  <p>
    For example, magnesium forms Mg²⁺ and chlorine forms Cl⁻.
    One magnesium ion needs two chloride ions:
  </p>
  <p>+2 + 2(−1) = 0</p>
  <p>Therefore, the formula is MgCl₂.</p>

  <h3>4. Formation of magnesium oxide</h3>
  <p>
    Magnesium loses two electrons to form Mg²⁺. Oxygen gains two
    electrons to form O²⁻. The ions combine in a 1:1 ratio because
    +2 and −2 cancel.
  </p>
  <p><b>Magnesium:</b> Mg → Mg²⁺ + 2e⁻</p>
  <p><b>Oxygen:</b> O + 2e⁻ → O²⁻</p>
  <p><b>Formula:</b> MgO</p>

  <h3>5. Formation of aluminium oxide</h3>
  <p>
    Aluminium forms Al³⁺, while oxygen forms O²⁻ in aluminium oxide.
    Two Al³⁺ ions give a total charge of +6. Three O²⁻ ions give
    a total charge of −6. The charges balance in Al₂O₃.
  </p>

  <h3>6. Ionic compounds are not usually separate molecules</h3>
  <p>
    In a solid ionic compound, many positive and negative ions are
    arranged in a repeating three-dimensional lattice. A formula such
    as MgO represents the simplest ratio of ions in the structure,
    not an isolated MgO molecule.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Write the formula of sodium oxide</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> Sodium forms Na⁺ and oxygen forms O²⁻. Determine the formula of sodium oxide.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Each Na⁺ ion contributes +1.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Each O²⁻ ion contributes −2.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Two Na⁺ ions contribute +2, balancing one O²⁻ ion.</p>
  <p><b>Step 4:</b> The simplest ratio is two sodium ions to one oxide ion.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Na₂O.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Write the formula of calcium fluoride</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> Calcium forms Ca²⁺ and fluorine forms F⁻. Determine the formula of calcium fluoride.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Calcium contributes +2.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Each fluoride ion contributes −1.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Two fluoride ions contribute −2.</p>
  <p><b>Step 4:</b> The charges cancel: +2 + 2(−1) = 0.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> CaF₂.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Derive the formula of aluminium oxide</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> Aluminium forms Al³⁺ and oxygen forms O²⁻. Determine the neutral formula.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> The smallest common total charge magnitude for 3 and 2 is 6.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Two Al³⁺ ions give 2 × (+3) = +6.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Three O²⁻ ions give 3 × (−2) = −6.</p>
  <p><b>Step 4:</b> The total charge is +6 − 6 = 0.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Al₂O₃.</p>

  <h3>Key idea</h3>
  <p>
    To derive an ionic formula, identify the ions and their charges,
    choose the smallest whole-number ratio that balances the charges,
    and write the formula without the ion charges.
  </p>
