  (782, 782, '
  <h2>Properties of Ionic Compounds</h2>

  <h3>1. Structure explains properties</h3>
  <p>
    Many ionic compounds have high melting and boiling points, are
    brittle solids at room temperature, and conduct electricity when
    molten or dissolved in water if their ions are free to move.
    These properties are explained by their giant ionic lattice.
  </p>

  <h3>2. High melting and boiling points</h3>
  <p>
    In a solid ionic lattice, positive and negative ions are held
    together by strong electrostatic attractions. A large amount of
    energy is required to overcome enough of these attractions for
    the solid to melt or the liquid to boil.
  </p>

  <p>
    The melting point is the temperature at which a solid changes
    into a liquid. The boiling point is the temperature at which
    a liquid changes into a gas.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Electrical conductivity in the solid state</h3>
  <p>
    In a solid ionic compound, the ions occupy fixed positions in
    the lattice. Although the ions are charged, they cannot move
    freely through the solid. Therefore, solid sodium chloride
    does not conduct electricity under ordinary conditions.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Conductivity when molten</h3>
  <p>
    When an ionic compound melts, its lattice structure breaks down
    enough for the ions to move. The mobile charged ions carry
    electrical current through the liquid.
  </p>

  <h3>5. Conductivity in aqueous solution</h3>
  <p>
    When a soluble ionic compound dissolves in water, it separates
    into mobile ions. These ions can move towards oppositely charged
    electrodes, allowing the solution to conduct electricity.
  </p>

  <p>
    Not every ionic compound is soluble in water. Conductivity in
    aqueous solution depends on whether the substance dissolves
    sufficiently to produce mobile ions.
  </p>

  <h3>6. Why ionic compounds are brittle</h3>
  <p>
    If layers of ions shift when a force is applied, ions of the
    same charge may be brought next to one another. Their repulsion
    can cause the crystal to split. This is why ionic crystals tend
    to be brittle rather than malleable.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Explain why solid sodium chloride does not conduct electricity</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> Explain why solid sodium chloride is a poor conductor of electricity.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Sodium chloride contains positive Na⁺ and negative Cl⁻ ions.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> In the solid, the ions are held in fixed positions in the lattice.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Electrical conduction requires charged particles that can move through the material.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Solid sodium chloride does not conduct because its ions cannot move freely through the fixed lattice.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Explain why molten sodium chloride conducts electricity</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> Why does molten sodium chloride conduct electricity while solid sodium chloride does not?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> In the solid, the ions are fixed in their lattice positions.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> On melting, the lattice no longer holds the ions in fixed positions.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> The ions can move through the liquid and carry charge.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Molten sodium chloride conducts because its ions are mobile and carry electrical charge.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Explain the high melting point of an ionic compound</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> Explain why many ionic compounds have high melting points.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> The ions form a giant lattice.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Oppositely charged ions attract strongly throughout the lattice.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> A large amount of energy is needed to overcome enough of these attractions for melting to occur.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Ionic compounds often have high melting points because strong electrostatic attractions between ions require substantial energy to overcome.</p>

  <h3>Key idea</h3>
  <p>
    Always explain an ionic compound''s property using its structure:
    strong attractions explain high melting points, fixed ions explain
    poor solid conductivity, and mobile ions explain conductivity
    when molten or in solution.
  </p>
