  (783, 783, '
  <h2>Covalent Bonding</h2>

  <h3>1. What is a covalent bond?</h3>
  <p>
    A <b>covalent bond</b> is a chemical bond formed when atoms share
    one or more pairs of electrons. The shared electrons are attracted
    to the nuclei of both bonded atoms, helping hold the atoms together.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Why do atoms share electrons?</h3>
  <p>
    Sharing electrons can produce a more stable electron arrangement.
    In many simple examples, hydrogen shares electrons to fill its
    first shell with two electrons, while carbon, nitrogen, oxygen,
    and chlorine often share electrons to achieve eight outer-shell
    electrons in the introductory model.
  </p>

  <p>
    These are useful models for common covalent compounds. Not all
    covalent substances obey a strict octet rule.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Covalent bonding in hydrogen</h3>
  <p>
    Each hydrogen atom has one electron. Two hydrogen atoms share a
    pair of electrons. Each hydrogen nucleus attracts the shared pair,
    and each hydrogen atom is associated with two electrons in the
    shared arrangement.
  </p>
  <p><b>Formula:</b> H₂</p>
  <p><b>Displayed formula:</b> H—H</p>

  <h3>4. Covalent bonding in chlorine</h3>
  <p>
    Each chlorine atom has seven outer-shell electrons. Two chlorine
    atoms share one pair of electrons. Each atom contributes one
    electron to the shared pair, and each chlorine atom is associated
    with eight outer-shell electrons.
  </p>
  <p><b>Formula:</b> Cl₂</p>
  <p><b>Displayed formula:</b> Cl—Cl</p>

  <h3>5. Covalent bonding in water</h3>
  <p>
    Oxygen has six outer-shell electrons, while each hydrogen has one
    electron. Oxygen shares one pair with each of two hydrogen atoms.
    Water therefore contains two O—H single covalent bonds.
  </p>
  <p><b>Formula:</b> H₂O</p>
  <p><b>Displayed formula:</b> H—O—H</p>

  <h3>6. Shared electrons are not completely transferred</h3>
  <p>
    In ionic bonding, electrons are transferred to form ions. In
    covalent bonding, the atoms share electron pairs. The shared
    electrons are attracted to both bonded nuclei.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Explain the covalent bond in H₂</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> Explain how two hydrogen atoms form a covalent bond.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Each hydrogen atom has one electron.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Each atom contributes one electron to a shared pair.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> The shared pair is attracted to both hydrogen nuclei.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Two hydrogen atoms share one pair of electrons, forming a single covalent bond in H₂.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Predict the number of covalent bonds in H₂O</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> Oxygen has six outer-shell electrons and hydrogen has one. Explain why water contains two O—H bonds.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Oxygen has six outer-shell electrons and needs two more to reach eight in the simple shell model.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Each hydrogen can share one electron with oxygen.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Oxygen shares one pair with each of two hydrogen atoms.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Water contains two single covalent bonds, one between oxygen and each hydrogen atom.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Explain the bond in Cl₂</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> Each chlorine atom has seven outer-shell electrons. Explain how Cl₂ forms.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Each chlorine atom needs one more electron to reach eight in the simple shell model.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Each chlorine atom contributes one electron to a shared pair.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Both chlorine atoms are associated with the shared pair.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Two chlorine atoms share one pair of electrons to form Cl₂, with one single covalent bond.</p>

  <h3>Key idea</h3>
  <p>
    Covalent bonding involves shared electron pairs. A single
    covalent bond consists of one shared pair of electrons attracted
    to both bonded nuclei.
  </p>
