  (784, 784, '
  <h2>Single Covalent Bonds</h2>

  <h3>1. Definition of a single covalent bond</h3>
  <p>
    A <b>single covalent bond</b> consists of one shared pair of
    electrons between two atoms. One line in a displayed formula
    represents one shared pair.
  </p>

  <p>
    For example, H—H represents one single covalent bond between
    the two hydrogen atoms in H₂.
  </p>

  <h3>2. How a single bond forms</h3>
  <p>
    In a simple single covalent bond, each bonded atom contributes
    one electron to the shared pair. The shared electrons are attracted
    to the nuclei of both atoms. The atoms are held together by this
    attraction.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Hydrogen molecule, H₂</h3>
  <p>
    Each hydrogen atom has one electron. Sharing one pair gives each
    hydrogen access to two electrons in the shared arrangement.
  </p>
  <p><b>Displayed formula:</b> H—H</p>
  <p><b>Shared pairs between the atoms:</b> 1</p>

  <h3>4. Chlorine molecule, Cl₂</h3>
  <p>
    Each chlorine atom has seven outer-shell electrons. The atoms share
    one pair of electrons, producing one single bond. Each chlorine
    atom is then associated with eight outer-shell electrons in the
    simple shell model.
  </p>
  <p><b>Displayed formula:</b> Cl—Cl</p>

  <h3>5. Methane, CH₄</h3>
  <p>
    Carbon has four outer-shell electrons. In methane, carbon shares
    one pair of electrons with each of four hydrogen atoms. There are
    four C—H single covalent bonds in one methane molecule.
  </p>
  <p><b>Formula:</b> CH₄</p>

  <h3>6. Ammonia, NH₃</h3>
  <p>
    Nitrogen has five outer-shell electrons. In ammonia, nitrogen forms
    three single covalent bonds by sharing one pair with each of three
    hydrogen atoms. The nitrogen atom also has one lone pair that is
    not shared in bonding.
  </p>
  <p><b>Formula:</b> NH₃</p>

  <h3>7. A bond line and a shared pair</h3>
  <p>
    Each single bond line represents two electrons, not one electron.
    Lone pairs are pairs of outer-shell electrons that are not shared
    between atoms in a covalent bond.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Count bonds in methane</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> Methane has the formula CH₄. How many single covalent bonds are present in one molecule?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify the central carbon atom and the four hydrogen atoms.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Each hydrogen forms one single bond with carbon.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Count the four C—H connections.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Four single covalent bonds.</p>
  <p><b>Check:</b> Four bonds contain four shared pairs, or eight electrons in total across the four bonding pairs.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Count bonding electrons in H₂</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A hydrogen molecule contains one single covalent bond. How many electrons are in the shared pair?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> One single covalent bond represents one shared pair.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> A pair contains two electrons.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Two shared electrons.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Count the bonds and lone pairs in ammonia</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> Ammonia, NH₃, has three N—H bonds. How many shared pairs and lone pairs are around the nitrogen atom in the simple electron-pair model?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Each N—H single bond is one shared pair.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Three bonds mean three shared pairs around nitrogen.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Nitrogen has five outer-shell electrons and forms three bonds, leaving one lone pair in the simple model.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Three shared pairs and one lone pair around nitrogen.</p>

  <h3>Key idea</h3>
  <p>
    One single covalent bond equals one shared pair of electrons.
    Count bond lines to determine the number of single bonds, then
    distinguish bonding pairs from lone pairs.
  </p>
