  (785, 785, '
  <h2>Double Covalent Bonds</h2>

  <h3>1. What is a double covalent bond?</h3>
  <p>
    A <b>double covalent bond</b> consists of two shared pairs of
    electrons between the same two atoms. It contains four shared
    electrons in total.
  </p>
  <p>
    In a displayed formula, a double covalent bond is represented
    by two lines, for example O=O.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Formation of oxygen, O₂</h3>
  <p>
    Each oxygen atom has six outer-shell electrons. In the simple
    octet model, each oxygen atom needs two more electrons to reach
    eight. Two oxygen atoms share two pairs of electrons, forming
    a double bond.
  </p>
  <p><b>Displayed formula:</b> O=O</p>
  <p>
    Each oxygen atom is associated with eight outer-shell electrons
    when the two shared pairs are counted.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Formation of carbon dioxide, CO₂</h3>
  <p>
    Carbon has four outer-shell electrons, while oxygen has six.
    In carbon dioxide, carbon shares two pairs of electrons with
    each oxygen atom. Each C=O connection is a double bond.
  </p>
  <p><b>Displayed formula:</b> O=C=O</p>
  <p>
    The molecule contains two double bonds. Each double bond contains
    two shared pairs, so there are four shared pairs across the
    two C=O bonds.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Counting electrons in a double bond</h3>
  <p>
    One shared pair contains two electrons. A double bond has two
    shared pairs:
  </p>
  <p>2 pairs × 2 electrons per pair = 4 shared electrons.</p>

  <h3>5. Single and double bonds</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Single bond:</b> one shared pair, or two shared electrons.</li>
    <li><b>Double bond:</b> two shared pairs, or four shared electrons.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    In general, a double bond is stronger and shorter than a single
    bond between the same two elements, although bond properties
    depend on the atoms and their chemical environment.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Count the shared electrons in O₂</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> An oxygen molecule contains one double bond. How many electrons are shared between its two atoms?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> A double bond contains two shared pairs.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Each pair contains two electrons.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Total shared electrons = 2 × 2 = 4.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Four shared electrons.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Count the double bonds in carbon dioxide</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> Carbon dioxide has the displayed formula O=C=O. How many double bonds and shared electron pairs does it contain?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify the two C=O connections.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Each connection is a double bond, so there are two double bonds.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Each double bond contains two shared pairs.</p>
  <p><b>Step 4:</b> Total shared pairs = 2 × 2 = 4.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Two double bonds and four shared pairs, equivalent to eight shared electrons.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Compare single and double bonds</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> Calculate the number of shared electrons in one single bond and one double bond combined.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> A single bond contains two shared electrons.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> A double bond contains four shared electrons.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Total = 2 + 4 = 6 shared electrons.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Six shared electrons.</p>

  <h3>Key idea</h3>
  <p>
    A double covalent bond consists of two shared electron pairs.
    Use the displayed formula to count bonds, then multiply the
    number of shared pairs by two to find the number of shared electrons.
  </p>
