  (786, 786, '
  <h2>Triple Covalent Bonds</h2>

  <h3>1. What is a triple covalent bond?</h3>
  <p>
    A <b>triple covalent bond</b> consists of three shared pairs
    of electrons between two atoms. It contains six shared electrons.
    In a displayed formula, three lines represent the bond.
  </p>
  <p><b>Example:</b> N≡N</p>

  <h3>2. Formation of nitrogen, N₂</h3>
  <p>
    Each nitrogen atom has five outer-shell electrons. In the simple
    octet model, each nitrogen atom needs three additional electrons
    to reach eight. Two nitrogen atoms share three pairs of electrons,
    forming a triple covalent bond.
  </p>
  <p><b>Displayed formula:</b> N≡N</p>
  <p>
    Each nitrogen atom is associated with eight outer-shell electrons
    when the three shared pairs are counted.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Counting electrons in a triple bond</h3>
  <p>
    One shared pair contains two electrons. Therefore:
  </p>
  <p>3 shared pairs × 2 electrons per pair = 6 shared electrons.</p>

  <h3>4. Comparing single, double, and triple bonds</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Single bond:</b> one shared pair = two electrons.</li>
    <li><b>Double bond:</b> two shared pairs = four electrons.</li>
    <li><b>Triple bond:</b> three shared pairs = six electrons.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Between the same two elements, a triple bond is generally shorter
    and stronger than a double bond, which is generally shorter and
    stronger than a single bond. These are general trends for comparable
    bonds, not a rule that predicts every bond in every molecule.
  </p>

  <h3>5. Nitrogen and its triple bond</h3>
  <p>
    Nitrogen gas consists of N₂ molecules. The triple bond holds the
    two nitrogen atoms together strongly. Breaking this bond requires
    substantial energy, which helps explain why nitrogen gas is
    relatively unreactive under ordinary conditions.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Count electrons in N≡N</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> How many shared electrons are present in one nitrogen molecule''s triple bond?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> A triple bond contains three shared pairs.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Each pair contains two electrons.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Total shared electrons = 3 × 2 = 6.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Six shared electrons.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Compare the electron counts of three bond types</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> Calculate the total number of shared electrons represented by one single bond, one double bond, and one triple bond.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Single bond: 1 × 2 = 2 electrons.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Double bond: 2 × 2 = 4 electrons.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Triple bond: 3 × 2 = 6 electrons.</p>
  <p><b>Step 4:</b> Total = 2 + 4 + 6 = 12 electrons.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Twelve shared electrons in total.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Explain nitrogen''s triple bond</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> Explain why two nitrogen atoms form a triple bond in N₂ using the simple octet model.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Each nitrogen atom has five outer-shell electrons.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Each needs three more electrons to reach eight in the model.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> The atoms share three pairs of electrons.</p>
  <p><b>Step 4:</b> The shared pairs are attracted to both nitrogen nuclei.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> The nitrogen atoms share three pairs of electrons, forming N≡N and completing the octet for each atom in the simple model.</p>

  <h3>Key idea</h3>
  <p>
    A triple covalent bond consists of three shared pairs, or six
    electrons. Nitrogen gas is a familiar example represented by N≡N.
  </p>
