  (787, 787, '
  <h2>Dot-and-Cross Diagrams</h2>

  <h3>1. What is a dot-and-cross diagram?</h3>
  <p>
    A <b>dot-and-cross diagram</b> represents the outer-shell electrons
    of atoms using dots and crosses. The two symbols distinguish
    electrons that came from different atoms before bonding.
  </p>

  <p>
    Dots and crosses do not represent different kinds of electrons.
    They are simply a drawing convention used to show which atom
    contributed each electron.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Drawing a covalent bond</h3>
  <p>
    In a covalent bond, the shared pair is placed between the two
    bonded atoms. In a dot-and-cross diagram, one electron may be
    shown as a dot and the other as a cross.
  </p>

  <p>
    For H₂, each hydrogen contributes one electron. The shared pair
    contains one dot and one cross. Each hydrogen is associated with
    two electrons in the shared arrangement.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Drawing a chlorine molecule</h3>
  <p>
    Each chlorine atom has seven outer-shell electrons. When two
    chlorine atoms bond, each contributes one electron to the shared
    pair. Each chlorine has one shared pair and three lone pairs
    in the simple diagram.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Drawing an oxygen molecule</h3>
  <p>
    Each oxygen atom has six outer-shell electrons. In O₂, the atoms
    share two pairs of electrons. The shared region therefore contains
    four electrons: two dots from one oxygen and two crosses from
    the other, if that symbol convention is used.
  </p>

  <h3>5. Drawing an ionic compound</h3>
  <p>
    For an ionic compound such as sodium chloride, the diagram shows
    an electron transferred from sodium to chlorine. The ions are
    then placed in square brackets with their charges. The chloride
    ion is shown with eight outer-shell electrons, including the
    transferred electron.
  </p>

  <p>
    The electron symbols should show the source of the transferred
    electron, but the charge notation shows the ion''s final charge.
  </p>

  <h3>6. A reliable method for drawing the diagrams</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Write each atom''s symbol.</li>
    <li>Determine its number of outer-shell electrons.</li>
    <li>Choose dots for one atom and crosses for the other.</li>
    <li>For covalent bonding, place shared pairs between the atoms.</li>
    <li>For ionic bonding, show electron transfer, then bracket each ion and write its charge.</li>
    <li>Count the electrons around each atom or ion to check the diagram.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Draw the electron-sharing arrangement for H₂</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> Describe the dot-and-cross arrangement for a hydrogen molecule.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Each hydrogen atom has one electron.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Represent the electron from the first hydrogen as a dot and the electron from the second as a cross.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Place the dot and cross together between the two hydrogen symbols to show the shared pair.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> H atoms share one pair containing one dot and one cross; this represents one single covalent bond.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Determine the electron pairs in Cl₂</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> How many shared pairs and lone pairs are present in a dot-and-cross diagram of Cl₂?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Each chlorine has seven outer-shell electrons.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Each chlorine contributes one electron to the shared pair.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> The shared region contains one pair.</p>
  <p><b>Step 4:</b> Each chlorine has six other outer-shell electrons arranged as three lone pairs.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> One shared pair and three lone pairs on each chlorine atom.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Check the outer shell of a chloride ion</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> A chlorine atom gains one electron. How many outer-shell electrons should be shown around Cl⁻ in an ionic dot-and-cross diagram?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> A neutral chlorine atom has seven outer-shell electrons.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> The atom gains one electron.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Outer-shell electrons = 7 + 1 = 8.</p>
  <p><b>Step 4:</b> Draw the ion in square brackets with a − charge.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Show eight outer-shell electrons around [Cl]⁻, including the transferred electron.</p>

  <h3>Key idea</h3>
  <p>
    Dot-and-cross diagrams are electron-accounting diagrams. Show
    the correct outer-shell electrons, distinguish their origins,
    and check that sharing or transfer is represented accurately.
  </p>
