  (788, 788, '
  <h2>Properties of Simple Covalent Substances</h2>

  <h3>1. What is a simple covalent substance?</h3>
  <p>
    A <b>simple covalent substance</b> consists of separate molecules
    in which atoms are joined by covalent bonds. Examples include
    hydrogen, oxygen, chlorine, water, methane, and carbon dioxide.
  </p>

  <p>
    The covalent bonds within each molecule are strong. However,
    the forces between separate molecules are often much weaker than
    the covalent bonds inside the molecules.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Why many simple covalent substances have low melting points</h3>
  <p>
    When a simple molecular substance melts, the molecules can move
    past one another. The process generally involves overcoming
    intermolecular attractions rather than breaking all the covalent
    bonds within the molecules.
  </p>

  <p>
    Many simple molecular substances therefore have relatively low
    melting and boiling points. The exact values depend on the strength
    of the intermolecular forces and the molecular structure.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Why many simple covalent substances do not conduct electricity</h3>
  <p>
    Simple molecular substances generally do not contain mobile ions
    or a sea of delocalised electrons. Their electrons are involved
    in covalent bonds or remain in lone pairs, so there are usually
    no freely moving charged particles to carry current.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Solubility depends on the substance and solvent</h3>
  <p>
    Simple covalent substances do not all have the same solubility.
    Some dissolve in water, while others dissolve more readily in
    non-polar solvents. Solubility depends on the interactions between
    the solute particles and the solvent molecules.
  </p>

  <p>
    For example, ammonia is very soluble in water, while methane is
    only very slightly soluble. It is incorrect to say that all
    covalent substances are insoluble in water.
  </p>

  <h3>5. Do not confuse simple molecular and giant covalent structures</h3>
  <p>
    Diamond and silicon dioxide have giant covalent structures rather
    than separate small molecules throughout the solid. Many giant
    covalent substances have very high melting points because melting
    requires disrupting many strong covalent bonds.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Explain the low boiling point of methane</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> Methane is a simple molecular substance. Explain why it has a relatively low boiling point compared with many giant covalent substances.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Methane consists of separate CH₄ molecules.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> The molecules attract one another through weak intermolecular forces.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Boiling requires enough energy to overcome these attractions so the molecules can separate into the gas.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Methane has a relatively low boiling point because the attractions between its molecules are weak compared with the covalent bonds within molecules.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Explain why pure water does not conduct electricity well</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> Explain why pure water is a poor electrical conductor compared with a solution of sodium chloride.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Pure water contains very few ions compared with an ionic solution.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Sodium chloride dissolves to produce mobile Na⁺ and Cl⁻ ions.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> These mobile ions carry electrical current through the solution.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Pure water conducts poorly because it has very few mobile ions, while sodium chloride solution contains mobile ions that carry charge.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Compare methane and diamond</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> Explain why methane and diamond have very different melting behaviour despite both involving covalent bonds.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Methane consists of separate small molecules.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> When methane changes state, the main attractions overcome are between molecules.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Diamond has a giant three-dimensional covalent network.</p>
  <p><b>Step 4:</b> Disrupting diamond''s structure requires breaking many strong covalent bonds.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Methane is molecular and has weak intermolecular attractions, whereas diamond has a giant covalent network requiring substantial energy to disrupt.</p>

  <h3>Key idea</h3>
  <p>
    For simple covalent substances, distinguish the strong covalent
    bonds within molecules from the often weaker attractions between
    molecules. Do not apply the properties of simple molecules to
    giant covalent structures.
  </p>
