  (789, 789, '
  <h2>Giant Covalent Structures</h2>

  <h3>1. What is a giant covalent structure?</h3>
  <p>
    A <b>giant covalent structure</b> is a large continuous arrangement
    of atoms joined by covalent bonds. Instead of consisting of separate
    small molecules, the atoms form an extensive network.
  </p>

  <p>
    Examples include diamond, graphite, and silicon dioxide. Their
    properties depend on how the atoms are connected and how the
    electrons are arranged.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Why many giant covalent structures have high melting points</h3>
  <p>
    Many giant covalent structures contain numerous strong covalent
    bonds extending throughout the network. A large amount of energy
    is required to disrupt the structure because many bonds must be
    broken.
  </p>

  <p>
    This differs from melting a simple molecular substance, where
    the main attractions overcome are generally between separate
    molecules.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Diamond and graphite are different structures of carbon</h3>
  <p>
    Diamond and graphite are both forms of carbon, but their atoms
    are arranged differently.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Diamond:</b> each carbon atom forms four covalent bonds in a rigid three-dimensional network.</li>
    <li><b>Graphite:</b> each carbon atom forms three covalent bonds in layers; one electron per carbon atom is delocalised in the structure.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Diamond is very hard and does not conduct electricity under
    ordinary conditions. Graphite is soft between its layers and
    conducts electricity because it has delocalised electrons that
    can move along the layers.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Silicon dioxide</h3>
  <p>
    Silicon dioxide, SiO₂, has a giant covalent network. Each silicon
    atom is bonded to oxygen atoms, and the oxygen atoms link the
    network together. It is not made of separate SiO₂ molecules in
    the solid structure.
  </p>

  <h3>5. Why structure matters</h3>
  <p>
    The label "covalent" alone does not tell you every physical
    property. You must identify whether the substance contains
    separate molecules or a giant network, and then consider how
    its atoms and electrons are arranged.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Explain the high melting point of diamond</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> Explain why diamond has a very high melting point.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Diamond contains a giant three-dimensional network of carbon atoms.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Each carbon atom is joined to four other carbon atoms by strong covalent bonds.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Melting requires disrupting many strong covalent bonds throughout the network.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Diamond has a very high melting point because many strong covalent bonds must be broken to disrupt its giant network.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Explain why graphite conducts electricity</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> Explain why graphite conducts electricity while diamond does not conduct under ordinary conditions.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> In graphite, each carbon atom forms three covalent bonds.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> One outer electron per carbon atom is delocalised in the structure.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> These delocalised electrons can move along the layers and carry charge.</p>
  <p><b>Step 4:</b> In diamond, each carbon atom uses its four outer electrons in four covalent bonds, so there are no comparable mobile delocalised electrons.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Graphite conducts because it has mobile delocalised electrons; diamond does not conduct under ordinary conditions because its outer electrons are involved in covalent bonding.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Distinguish a giant covalent structure from a simple molecular substance</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> Explain the structural difference between silicon dioxide and carbon dioxide.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Solid silicon dioxide forms a giant covalent network.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Carbon dioxide consists of separate CO₂ molecules under ordinary conditions.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> The formula SiO₂ represents the ratio of atoms in the silicon dioxide network, whereas CO₂ represents the composition of each carbon dioxide molecule.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Silicon dioxide has a giant covalent network, while carbon dioxide consists of separate molecules. Their formulae do not indicate the same type of structure.</p>

  <h3>Key idea</h3>
  <p>
    Giant covalent structures contain extensive networks of covalent
    bonds. Their properties depend on the network''s arrangement and
    the availability of mobile electrons.
  </p>
