  (790, 790, '
  <h2>Diamond: Structure and Properties</h2>

  <h3>1. What type of structure does diamond have?</h3>

  <p>
    Diamond is a form of carbon. It has a <b>giant covalent structure</b>,
    meaning that many carbon atoms are joined together by strong covalent
    bonds in a continuous three-dimensional network.
  </p>

  <p>
    Diamond is not made of separate carbon molecules. The covalent network
    extends throughout the crystal.
  </p>

  <h3>2. How are the carbon atoms bonded?</h3>

  <p>
    Each carbon atom has four electrons in its outer shell. In diamond,
    each carbon atom shares one electron with each of four neighbouring
    carbon atoms.
  </p>

  <p>
    Each shared pair of electrons forms a covalent bond. Therefore,
    <b>each carbon atom forms four covalent bonds</b>.
  </p>

  <p>
    The carbon atoms are arranged in a three-dimensional tetrahedral
    network. The bonds extend in different directions, so the structure
    is rigid and difficult to deform.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Why is diamond extremely hard?</h3>

  <p>
    Diamond is extremely hard because each carbon atom is connected to
    four other carbon atoms by strong covalent bonds. These bonds form
    a continuous network throughout the structure.
  </p>

  <p>
    Scratching or cutting diamond requires disrupting strong covalent
    bonds. This requires considerable energy. The hardness is therefore
    a consequence of the bonding throughout the network, not simply
    because carbon is a hard element.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Why does diamond have a high melting point?</h3>

  <p>
    A large amount of energy is needed to break enough of the strong
    covalent bonds in the giant structure for the solid to melt.
    Diamond consequently has an extremely high melting temperature
    under appropriate pressure conditions.
  </p>

  <p>
    Do not confuse the energy needed to overcome attractions between
    separate molecules with the energy needed to disrupt a giant
    covalent network. Diamond does not consist of separate molecules
    held together by weak intermolecular forces.
  </p>

  <h3>5. Why does diamond not conduct electricity?</h3>

  <p>
    Each carbon atom uses all four of its outer-shell electrons in
    covalent bonding. There are no freely moving electrons available
    to carry electrical charge through the structure.
  </p>

  <p>
    Diamond is therefore an electrical insulator under ordinary
    conditions.
  </p>

  <h3>6. Uses of diamond</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <b>Cutting and drilling tools:</b> its extreme hardness makes it
      suitable for cutting or grinding hard materials.
    </li>
    <li>
      <b>Jewellery:</b> its brilliance, transparency and ability to
      withstand scratching make it valuable in jewellery.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Explain the hardness of diamond</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Explain why diamond is extremely hard.</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify its structure.</p>
  <p>
    Diamond has a giant three-dimensional covalent structure.
  </p>

  <p><b>Step 2:</b> State the bonding arrangement.</p>
  <p>
    Each carbon atom forms four strong covalent bonds with neighbouring
    carbon atoms.
  </p>

  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Connect the bonding to the property.</p>
  <p>
    The continuous network cannot be easily deformed without disrupting
    strong covalent bonds.
  </p>

  <p><b>Conclusion:</b> Diamond is extremely hard because its carbon
  atoms are joined by strong covalent bonds throughout a rigid
  three-dimensional network.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Explain the high melting point</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Why does diamond have a very high melting point?</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Diamond is a giant covalent structure, not a collection
  of separate molecules.</p>

  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Strong covalent bonds connect the carbon atoms
  throughout the structure.</p>

  <p><b>Step 3:</b> A large amount of energy is required to disrupt
  enough of these bonds for the structure to melt.</p>

  <p><b>Conclusion:</b> Diamond has a very high melting point because
  its giant covalent network contains many strong covalent bonds.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Explain electrical insulation</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Diamond and graphite are both made of carbon.
  Why does diamond not conduct electricity?</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> In diamond, each carbon atom forms four covalent bonds.</p>

  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Its four outer-shell electrons are involved in these
  bonds.</p>

  <p><b>Step 3:</b> There are no delocalised electrons free to move
  through the structure and carry charge.</p>

  <p><b>Conclusion:</b> Diamond does not conduct electricity because
  it has no freely moving charge carriers.</p>

  <h3>Common mistake</h3>

  <p>
    Do not write that diamond is hard because its atoms are closely
    packed. The essential explanation is the strong covalent bonding
    throughout its three-dimensional network.
  </p>
