  <h2>Graphite: Structure and Properties</h2>

  <h3>1. Graphite is another form of carbon</h3>

  <p>
    Graphite and diamond are both forms of the element carbon, but
    their atoms are arranged differently. This difference in structure
    gives them different physical properties.
  </p>

  <p>
    Graphite has a <b>giant covalent structure made of layers</b>.
    Each layer contains carbon atoms bonded together in a repeating
    network of hexagonal rings.
  </p>

  <h3>2. How are carbon atoms bonded in graphite?</h3>

  <p>
    Each carbon atom forms three strong covalent bonds with three
    neighbouring carbon atoms in the same layer.
  </p>

  <p>
    Carbon has four outer-shell electrons. Three are used in the
    three covalent bonds. The remaining electron contributes to a
    system of <b>delocalised electrons</b> within the layer.
  </p>

  <p>
    Delocalised electrons are not confined to one particular
    covalent bond. They can move through the graphite layers and
    carry electrical charge.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Why does graphite conduct electricity?</h3>

  <p>
    Graphite conducts electricity because it contains delocalised
    electrons that can move along its layers.
  </p>

  <p>
    When a potential difference is applied, these mobile electrons
    can carry charge through the material. This makes graphite
    different from diamond, in which all four outer-shell electrons
    of each carbon atom are used in covalent bonding.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Why is graphite soft and slippery?</h3>

  <p>
    The carbon atoms within each layer are connected by strong
    covalent bonds. However, the attractions between neighbouring
    layers are much weaker than the covalent bonds within a layer.
  </p>

  <p>
    The layers can slide over one another relatively easily.
    This makes graphite soft and allows it to act as a lubricant
    in suitable applications.
  </p>

  <p>
    Do not say that the covalent bonds inside each layer are weak.
    The weak attractions are <b>between the layers</b>, not within them.
  </p>

  <h3>5. Why does graphite have a high melting point?</h3>

  <p>
    Graphite is a giant covalent structure. Strong covalent bonds
    connect the carbon atoms throughout each layer. A very large
    amount of energy is needed to disrupt this network.
  </p>

  <p>
    Therefore, graphite has a very high melting or sublimation
    temperature under appropriate conditions. The weak attractions
    between layers help explain its softness, but they do not mean
    the entire structure melts at a low temperature.
  </p>

  <h3>6. Uses of graphite</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <b>Electrodes:</b> graphite conducts electricity and can be
      used as an electrode in suitable electrochemical processes.
    </li>
    <li>
      <b>Lubricants:</b> its layers slide over one another, reducing
      friction in suitable conditions.
    </li>
    <li>
      <b>Pencil cores:</b> layers of graphite rub off onto paper
      during writing.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Explain why graphite conducts electricity</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Explain why graphite conducts electricity but
  diamond does not.</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> In graphite, each carbon atom forms three covalent
  bonds with neighbouring atoms.</p>

  <p><b>Step 2:</b> The remaining outer-shell electron contributes to
  the delocalised electron system.</p>

  <p><b>Step 3:</b> These electrons can move along the layers and carry
  electrical charge. In diamond, each carbon uses all four outer-shell
  electrons in covalent bonds.</p>

  <p><b>Conclusion:</b> Graphite conducts electricity because it has
  mobile delocalised electrons, whereas diamond has no freely moving
  electrons under ordinary conditions.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Explain why graphite is soft</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Explain why graphite can be used as a lubricant.</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Graphite is made of layers of carbon atoms.</p>

  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Strong covalent bonds hold the atoms together
  within each layer.</p>

  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Much weaker attractions act between neighbouring
  layers, allowing them to slide over one another.</p>

  <p><b>Conclusion:</b> Graphite can act as a lubricant because its
  layers slide over one another relatively easily.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Explain the high melting point of graphite</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> If the layers in graphite slide easily, why does
  graphite still have a very high melting temperature?</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Sliding layers does not require breaking all the
  covalent bonds within those layers.</p>

  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Each layer contains a continuous network of carbon
  atoms joined by strong covalent bonds.</p>

  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Disrupting this giant covalent network requires a
  large amount of energy.</p>

  <p><b>Conclusion:</b> Graphite has a very high melting or sublimation
  temperature because of its strong covalent bonding within the layers,
  even though the layers can slide over one another.</p>

  <h3>Common mistake</h3>

  <p>
    Graphite is not soft because the bonds between carbon atoms
    within a layer are weak. Those covalent bonds are strong.
    Its softness results from the relatively weak attractions
    between the layers.
  </p>
