  <h2>Ionic and Covalent Structures</h2>

  <h3>1. Why compare ionic and covalent substances?</h3>

  <p>
    Ionic and covalent substances have different bonding arrangements.
    Understanding those arrangements helps explain their melting points,
    electrical conductivity and other physical properties.
  </p>

  <p>
    The important rule is to identify the structure first. Do not assume
    that every covalent substance has the same properties: a simple
    molecular substance behaves differently from a giant covalent
    structure.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Ionic bonding and ionic lattices</h3>

  <p>
    Ionic bonding involves electrostatic attraction between oppositely
    charged ions. A typical ionic compound contains positive ions
    (cations) and negative ions (anions).
  </p>

  <p>
    In a solid ionic compound, the ions are arranged in a regular,
    repeating three-dimensional lattice. Each ion is attracted to
    oppositely charged ions around it.
  </p>

  <p>
    The strong electrostatic attractions extend throughout the lattice.
    A large amount of energy is generally needed to separate the ions,
    so ionic compounds usually have high melting points.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Electrical conductivity of ionic compounds</h3>

  <p>
    In a solid ionic compound, the ions are held in fixed positions
    in the lattice. They cannot move freely through the solid, so the
    solid does not normally conduct electricity.
  </p>

  <p>
    When the compound is molten, the lattice has broken down and the
    ions can move. If the compound dissolves in water to produce mobile
    ions, the resulting solution can also conduct electricity.
  </p>

  <p>
    In molten ionic compounds and suitable aqueous solutions, the
    mobile ions carry electrical charge.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Simple molecular covalent substances</h3>

  <p>
    In a simple molecular substance, atoms within each molecule are
    held together by strong covalent bonds. Separate molecules are
    attracted to one another by intermolecular forces.
  </p>

  <p>
    When many simple molecular substances melt or boil, energy is
    mainly needed to overcome the intermolecular attractions between
    molecules. The covalent bonds within the molecules generally remain
    intact.
  </p>

  <p>
    Many simple molecular substances therefore have relatively low
    melting and boiling points compared with ionic compounds and giant
    covalent substances.
  </p>

  <p>
    Their low melting points should not be explained by claiming that
    the covalent bonds inside each molecule are weak. The important
    distinction is between covalent bonds within molecules and
    intermolecular forces between molecules.
  </p>

  <h3>5. Giant covalent structures</h3>

  <p>
    In a giant covalent structure, many atoms are connected by strong
    covalent bonds in a continuous network. There are no separate
    small molecules making up the network.
  </p>

  <p>
    Diamond and graphite are examples of giant covalent forms of carbon.
    Silicon dioxide is another example of a giant covalent substance.
  </p>

  <p>
    Giant covalent substances generally have high melting temperatures
    because large amounts of energy are needed to disrupt their
    extensive covalent networks.
  </p>

  <p>
    Their electrical properties depend on their structures. Diamond
    does not conduct electricity under ordinary conditions because it
    lacks mobile electrons, while graphite conducts because it has
    delocalised electrons.
  </p>

  <h3>6. Compare the structures</h3>

  <table>
    <tr>
      <th>Feature</th>
      <th>Ionic lattice</th>
      <th>Simple molecular</th>
      <th>Giant covalent</th>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Structure</td>
      <td>Positive and negative ions in a lattice</td>
      <td>Separate molecules</td>
      <td>Continuous network of atoms</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Main attraction or bond</td>
      <td>Electrostatic attraction between oppositely charged ions</td>
      <td>Covalent bonds within molecules; intermolecular forces between molecules</td>
      <td>Strong covalent bonds throughout the network</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Melting point</td>
      <td>Usually high</td>
      <td>Often relatively low</td>
      <td>Usually high</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Electrical conduction</td>
      <td>When molten or in a solution with mobile ions; not normally when solid</td>
      <td>Usually does not conduct</td>
      <td>Depends on the structure; graphite conducts, diamond does not</td>
    </tr>
  </table>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Explain the melting point of sodium chloride</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Sodium chloride has a high melting point.
  Explain this using its structure.</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Sodium chloride is an ionic compound containing
  positive sodium ions and negative chloride ions.</p>

  <p><b>Step 2:</b> The ions are arranged in a giant ionic lattice.
  Strong electrostatic attractions act between oppositely charged ions
  throughout the lattice.</p>

  <p><b>Step 3:</b> A large amount of energy is required to overcome
  enough of these attractions for the lattice to melt.</p>

  <p><b>Conclusion:</b> Sodium chloride has a high melting point
  because strong electrostatic attractions hold its oppositely
  charged ions together in a giant lattice.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Explain why solid sodium chloride does not conduct</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Solid sodium chloride does not conduct electricity,
  but molten sodium chloride does. Explain the difference.</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> In solid sodium chloride, positive and negative ions
  occupy fixed positions in the lattice.</p>

  <p><b>Step 2:</b> The ions cannot move freely through the solid to
  carry electrical charge.</p>

  <p><b>Step 3:</b> When sodium chloride melts, the lattice breaks down
  and the ions become mobile.</p>

  <p><b>Conclusion:</b> Solid sodium chloride does not normally conduct
  because its ions are fixed, while molten sodium chloride conducts
  because its mobile ions carry charge.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Compare a simple molecular substance with diamond</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Explain why a simple molecular substance may have
  a low melting point while diamond has an extremely high melting point.</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> A simple molecular substance contains separate
  molecules. When it melts, the energy supplied mainly overcomes
  intermolecular attractions between those molecules.</p>

  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Diamond is a giant covalent network. Its carbon
  atoms are joined by strong covalent bonds throughout the structure.</p>

  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Melting diamond requires disrupting the giant
  covalent network, which requires far more energy than overcoming
  the intermolecular attractions in many simple molecular substances.</p>

  <p><b>Conclusion:</b> Many simple molecular substances have relatively
  low melting points because their intermolecular forces are overcome
  during melting, whereas diamond has an extremely high melting point
  because of its giant covalent network.</p>

  <h3>Common mistakes</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>
      Do not say that solid ionic compounds conduct electricity because
      they contain ions. The ions must be mobile to carry charge.
    </li>
    <li>
      Do not say that the covalent bonds inside simple molecules are
      broken whenever the substance melts. Melting usually overcomes
      intermolecular attractions.
    </li>
    <li>
      Do not claim that all covalent substances have low melting points.
      Giant covalent structures such as diamond have very high melting
      temperatures.
    </li>
    <li>
      Do not claim that all giant covalent substances are electrical
      insulators. Graphite conducts electricity because of its
      delocalised electrons.
    </li>
  </ul>
