  <h2>Valency</h2>

  <h3>1. Meaning of Valency</h3>

  <p>
    <strong>Valency</strong> is the combining capacity of an atom or
    group of atoms. It tells us the number of bonds an atom can form
    or the number of electrons involved in combining, depending on
    the type of chemical bonding.
  </p>

  <p>
    Valency is useful when determining how atoms combine to form
    compounds. Different elements have different common valencies.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Common Valencies</h3>

  <table>
    <tr>
      <th>Element</th>
      <th>Symbol</th>
      <th>Common valency</th>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Hydrogen</td>
      <td>H</td>
      <td>1</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Sodium</td>
      <td>Na</td>
      <td>1</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Chlorine</td>
      <td>Cl</td>
      <td>1</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Magnesium</td>
      <td>Mg</td>
      <td>2</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Oxygen</td>
      <td>O</td>
      <td>2</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Aluminium</td>
      <td>Al</td>
      <td>3</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Nitrogen</td>
      <td>N</td>
      <td>3</td>
    </tr>
  </table>

  <p>
    These are common valencies used in the examples on this screen.
    Some elements can have more than one valency in different
    compounds, so a listed value should not be assumed to apply
    in every possible compound.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Using Valency to Understand Combining Ratios</h3>

  <p>
    When two elements combine, their atoms combine in a ratio that
    satisfies their combining capacities. For example, an atom with
    valency 1 can combine with an atom of valency 2 in a ratio of
    two atoms to one atom.
  </p>

  <p>
    This relationship helps us determine how many atoms of each
    element are needed when writing a chemical formula.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Identify the valency of magnesium</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Magnesium has a common valency of 2. What does this tell us?</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the given value.</p>
  <p>Valency of magnesium = 2.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Interpret the value.</p>
  <p>
    Magnesium has a combining capacity of 2 in the compounds
    considered here.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Magnesium has valency 2.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Compare sodium and oxygen</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Sodium has valency 1 and oxygen has valency 2. Which has the greater combining capacity?</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Write the two valencies.</p>
  <p>Sodium = 1; oxygen = 2.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Compare the numbers.</p>
  <p>Since 2 is greater than 1, oxygen has the greater numerical valency.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Oxygen has the greater numerical valency.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Determine a combining ratio</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> An element A has valency 2 and element B has valency 1. What is the simplest atom ratio in which they can combine?</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Write the valencies.</p>
  <p>A = 2; B = 1.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Balance the combining capacities.</p>
  <p>
    One atom of A has combining capacity 2. Each atom of B has
    combining capacity 1, so two atoms of B are needed to match
    one atom of A.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> State the ratio.</p>
  <p>A : B = 1 : 2.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The simplest atom ratio is 1 : 2.</p>

  <h3>Remember</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Valency means combining capacity.</li>
    <li>Different elements can have different common valencies.</li>
    <li>Valency helps determine the ratio of atoms in a compound.</li>
  </ul>
