  <h2>Formulae of Ionic Compounds</h2>

  <h3>1. What Is an Ionic Compound?</h3>

  <p>
    An <strong>ionic compound</strong> consists of positively charged
    ions and negatively charged ions held together by electrostatic
    attraction. Its chemical formula represents the simplest
    whole-number ratio of the ions in the compound.
  </p>

  <p>
    A positive ion is called a cation. A negative ion is called an
    anion. In an ionic compound, the total positive charge and the
    total negative charge balance, so the compound has no overall
    electrical charge.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Writing an Ionic Formula</h3>

  <ol>
    <li>Write the positive ion first and the negative ion second.</li>
    <li>Write each ion''s charge.</li>
    <li>Choose the smallest number of each ion that makes the total charge zero.</li>
    <li>Write those numbers as subscripts.</li>
    <li>Omit a subscript of 1.</li>
  </ol>

  <p>
    The charges help determine the ratio. They are not written as
    subscripts in the final formula.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Sodium chloride</h3>

  <p><strong>Given ions:</strong> Na<sup>+</sup> and Cl<sup>-</sup>.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the charges.</p>
  <p>Sodium ion = +1; chloride ion = -1.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Balance the charges.</p>
  <p>
    One positive charge balances one negative charge:
    (+1) + (-1) = 0.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Write the formula.</p>
  <p>One Na ion and one Cl ion give NaCl.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> NaCl.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Magnesium oxide</h3>

  <p><strong>Given ions:</strong> Mg<sup>2+</sup> and O<sup>2-</sup>.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the charges.</p>
  <p>Magnesium ion = +2; oxide ion = -2.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Balance the charges.</p>
  <p>
    One Mg<sup>2+</sup> ion and one O<sup>2-</sup> ion give
    (+2) + (-2) = 0.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Write the formula.</p>
  <p>MgO.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> MgO, not Mg<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub>.</p>

  <p>
    Although two of each ion would also give a total charge of zero,
    Mg<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> is not the simplest ratio. Ionic
    formulae use the lowest whole-number ratio.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Aluminium oxide</h3>

  <p><strong>Given ions:</strong> Al<sup>3+</sup> and O<sup>2-</sup>.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the charges.</p>
  <p>Aluminium ion = +3; oxide ion = -2.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Find a total charge that can balance.</p>
  <p>
    The smallest common total is 6. Two aluminium ions give
    2 × (+3) = +6. Three oxide ions give 3 × (-2) = -6.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Check the overall charge.</p>
  <p>(+6) + (-6) = 0.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Write the formula.</p>
  <p>Al<sub>2</sub>O<sub>3</sub>.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Al<sub>2</sub>O<sub>3</sub>.</p>

  <h3>3. Why Charge Balance Matters</h3>

  <p>
    An ionic formula must represent an electrically neutral compound.
    If the positive and negative charges do not add to zero, the
    proposed ratio is incorrect.
  </p>

  <p>
    Always check both the charge balance and the simplest whole-number
    ratio before accepting a formula.
  </p>

  <h3>Remember</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Write the positive ion first.</li>
    <li>Balance total positive and negative charges.</li>
    <li>Use the simplest whole-number ratio.</li>
    <li>Do not include ion charges as subscripts in the final formula.</li>
  </ul>
