  <h2>Naming Ionic Compounds</h2>

  <h3>1. The Basic Naming Rule</h3>

  <p>
    An ionic compound consists of positive ions and negative ions.
    To name a simple ionic compound, name the positive ion first,
    followed by the negative ion.
  </p>

  <p>
    For many simple compounds made from a metal and a non-metal,
    the metal keeps its element name, while the non-metal''s name
    changes to end in <strong>-ide</strong>.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>NaCl: sodium chloride</li>
    <li>MgO: magnesium oxide</li>
    <li>CaCl<sub>2</sub>: calcium chloride</li>
    <li>Al<sub>2</sub>O<sub>3</sub>: aluminium oxide</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>2. How to Name a Formula</h3>

  <ol>
    <li>Identify the positive ion from the first element or ion shown.</li>
    <li>Name the positive ion.</li>
    <li>Identify the negative ion.</li>
    <li>Use the appropriate negative-ion name, often ending in -ide for simple monatomic ions.</li>
    <li>Write the positive-ion name first, then the negative-ion name.</li>
  </ol>

  <p>
    The subscripts tell you the ratio of ions in the formula. They
    do not normally change the basic name of a simple ionic compound.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Name NaCl</h3>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the elements.</p>
  <p>Na is sodium; Cl is chlorine.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Name the positive ion.</p>
  <p>Na<sup>+</sup> is the sodium ion, so the first part is sodium.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Name the negative ion.</p>
  <p>Cl<sup>-</sup> is the chloride ion, so the second part is chloride.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Sodium chloride.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Name MgO</h3>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the elements.</p>
  <p>Mg is magnesium; O is oxygen.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Name the positive ion.</p>
  <p>Mg<sup>2+</sup> is the magnesium ion.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Name the negative ion.</p>
  <p>O<sup>2-</sup> is the oxide ion.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Magnesium oxide.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Name CaCl2</h3>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the elements.</p>
  <p>Ca is calcium; Cl is chlorine.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Name the positive ion.</p>
  <p>The positive ion is calcium.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Name the negative ion.</p>
  <p>The negative ion is chloride.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Consider the subscript.</p>
  <p>
    The subscript 2 means that the formula contains two chloride
    ions for each calcium ion. It does not require the word
    "dichloride" in the name of this simple ionic compound.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Calcium chloride.</p>

  <h3>3. Naming and Formulae</h3>

  <p>
    Naming a compound and writing its formula are related but
    different tasks. When naming, identify the ions and use their
    correct names. Do not read the subscripts as prefixes such as
    di- or tri- when naming a simple ionic compound.
  </p>

  <h3>Remember</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Name the positive ion first.</li>
    <li>For simple monatomic negative ions, the name commonly ends in -ide.</li>
    <li>Do not use covalent prefixes to name simple ionic compounds.</li>
    <li>Subscripts describe the ion ratio; they do not automatically become name prefixes.</li>
  </ul>
