  <h2>State Symbols</h2>

  <h3>1. What Are State Symbols?</h3>

  <p>
    <strong>State symbols</strong> are letters written in brackets
    after a chemical formula in an equation. They show the physical
    state of a substance under the stated conditions.
  </p>

  <p>
    State symbols do not change the identity of the substance. They
    provide extra information about whether the substance is solid,
    liquid, gas or dissolved in water.
  </p>

  <h3>2. The Four Common State Symbols</h3>

  <table>
    <tr>
      <th>Symbol</th>
      <th>Meaning</th>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>(s)</td>
      <td>Solid</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>(l)</td>
      <td>Liquid</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>(g)</td>
      <td>Gas</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>(aq)</td>
      <td>Aqueous: dissolved in water</td>
    </tr>
  </table>

  <h3>3. Understanding Each Symbol</h3>

  <p>
    <strong>(s)</strong> means the substance is in the solid state.
    For example, NaCl(s) represents solid sodium chloride.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>(l)</strong> means the substance is in the liquid state.
    For example, H2O(l) represents liquid water.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>(g)</strong> means the substance is in the gaseous state.
    For example, CO2(g) represents carbon dioxide gas.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>(aq)</strong> means the substance is dissolved in water
    to form an aqueous solution. For example, NaCl(aq) represents
    sodium chloride dissolved in water.
  </p>

  <p>
    Aqueous does not simply mean liquid. A liquid substance is itself
    in the liquid state, while an aqueous substance is a substance
    dissolved in water.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Interpret NaCl(s)</h3>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the formula.</p>
  <p>NaCl represents sodium chloride.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Read the state symbol.</p>
  <p>The symbol (s) means solid.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> NaCl(s) means solid sodium chloride.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Interpret H2O(l)</h3>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the formula.</p>
  <p>H2O represents water.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Read the state symbol.</p>
  <p>The symbol (l) means liquid.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> H2O(l) means liquid water.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Distinguish NaCl(l) from NaCl(aq)</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> What is the difference between NaCl(l) and NaCl(aq)?</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Interpret (l).</p>
  <p>NaCl(l) represents sodium chloride in the liquid state.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Interpret (aq).</p>
  <p>NaCl(aq) represents sodium chloride dissolved in water.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> State the difference.</p>
  <p>
    The first indicates the physical state of the substance itself.
    The second indicates that it is dissolved in water.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> (l) means liquid; (aq) means dissolved in water.</p>

  <h3>4. How State Symbols Are Written</h3>

  <p>
    Write a state symbol in brackets immediately after the chemical
    formula, for example H2O(l) or CO2(g). The formula identifies
    the substance, while the state symbol describes its physical
    state under the stated conditions.
  </p>

  <p>
    A substance''s state can depend on conditions such as temperature
    and pressure. Use the state given in a question or equation
    rather than assuming a state without sufficient information.
  </p>

  <h3>Remember</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>(s) = solid.</li>
    <li>(l) = liquid.</li>
    <li>(g) = gas.</li>
    <li>(aq) = dissolved in water.</li>
    <li>Aqueous and liquid do not mean the same thing.</li>
  </ul>
