  (800, 800, '<h2>Meaning of Mole</h2>

  <h3>1. Why chemists need the mole</h3>

  <p>Chemical reactions involve particles that are far too small and numerous to count individually. A tiny sample of a substance can contain billions of billions of atoms or molecules.</p>

  <p>For example, when a chemist measures 18 g of water, the sample contains an enormous number of water molecules. Counting every molecule separately would be impractical. Chemists therefore use a counting unit called the <strong>mole</strong>.</p>

  <p>The mole works in chemistry in the same way that a dozen works in ordinary counting. A dozen means 12 items, regardless of whether the items are eggs, pencils or books. Similarly, one mole means a fixed number of specified particles, regardless of the substance.</p>

  <h3>2. Definition of a mole</h3>

  <p>One mole of a substance contains exactly <strong>6.02214076 × 10²³ specified elementary entities</strong>. In many school calculations, this is rounded to <strong>6.02 × 10²³</strong>.</p>

  <p>This number is called the <strong>Avogadro constant</strong>, represented by Nₐ.</p>

  <p>Nₐ = 6.02 × 10²³ mol⁻¹ (school approximation)</p>

  <p>The symbol mol is the unit for amount of substance. The number of particles in one mole is fixed; the mass of one mole depends on which substance is being considered.</p>

  <h3>3. What counts as a particle?</h3>

  <p>The word particle must be interpreted using the substance and the question.</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Atoms:</strong> one mole of helium atoms contains 6.02 × 10²³ helium atoms.</li>
    <li><strong>Molecules:</strong> one mole of water molecules contains 6.02 × 10²³ water molecules.</li>
    <li><strong>Ions:</strong> one mole of sodium ions contains 6.02 × 10²³ sodium ions.</li>
    <li><strong>Formula units:</strong> one mole of sodium chloride contains 6.02 × 10²³ formula units of NaCl.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>For ionic compounds such as sodium chloride, it is more accurate to say <em>formula units</em> rather than molecules. Solid sodium chloride is made of an extended arrangement of Na⁺ and Cl⁻ ions, not separate NaCl molecules.</p>

  <h3>4. One mole does not mean one gram</h3>

  <p>A mole measures the <strong>amount of substance</strong>; a gram measures <strong>mass</strong>. They are different quantities.</p>

  <p>One mole of carbon atoms has a mass of approximately 12 g, while one mole of water molecules has a mass of approximately 18 g. Both samples contain the same number of specified particles, but their masses differ because their particles have different masses.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Identifying the particles</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> What does 1 mol of oxygen molecules, O₂, contain?</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1: Identify the entity.</strong> The formula O₂ represents a molecule made of two oxygen atoms. The question asks for oxygen molecules, not individual oxygen atoms.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2: Recall the definition.</strong> One mole contains 6.02 × 10²³ specified entities.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3: State the correct entity.</strong> There are 6.02 × 10²³ oxygen molecules.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 6.02 × 10²³ O₂ molecules.</p>

  <p><strong>Important distinction:</strong> Since every O₂ molecule contains two oxygen atoms, this sample contains 2 × 6.02 × 10²³ = 1.204 × 10²⁴ oxygen atoms.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Comparing two substances</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Which contains more particles: 1 mol of helium atoms or 1 mol of water molecules?</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> One mole of helium contains 6.02 × 10²³ helium atoms.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> One mole of water contains 6.02 × 10²³ water molecules.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Compare the counts. Both have the same number of specified entities.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> They contain equal numbers of specified particles, 6.02 × 10²³ each. This does not mean they have equal masses.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Distinguishing amount from mass</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> A learner says, “One mole of every substance has a mass of one gram.” Explain why this is incorrect.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> A mole is a unit of amount of substance, not a unit of mass.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> One mole of carbon atoms has a mass of approximately 12 g.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> One mole of water molecules has a mass of approximately 18 g.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 4:</strong> The masses differ because carbon atoms and water molecules have different masses.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> One mole always contains the same number of specified entities, but its mass depends on the substance.</p>

  <h3>Common mistakes to avoid</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Do not define a mole as a mass of 1 g.</li>
    <li>Do not confuse the number of molecules with the number of atoms inside those molecules.</li>
    <li>Always state whether you are counting atoms, molecules, ions or formula units.</li>
    <li>Use 6.02 × 10²³ for school calculations unless the question supplies a different value or requires greater precision.</li>
  </ul>', 1),
  (801, 801, '<h2>Avogadro Constant</h2>

  <h3>1. Meaning of the Avogadro constant</h3>

  <p>The <strong>Avogadro constant</strong>, represented by Nₐ, is the number of specified elementary entities in one mole.</p>

  <p>For school calculations, use:</p>

  <p><strong>Nₐ = 6.02 × 10²³ mol⁻¹</strong></p>

  <p>The unit mol⁻¹ means “per mole”. The constant connects the amount of substance, measured in moles, to the number of entities present.</p>

  <h3>2. Calculating particles from moles</h3>

  <p>If one mole contains 6.02 × 10²³ particles, then several moles contain the corresponding multiple of that number.</p>

  <p><strong>N = n × Nₐ</strong></p>

  <ul>
    <li>N = number of specified particles</li>
    <li>n = amount of substance in mol</li>
    <li>Nₐ = Avogadro constant</li>
  </ul>

  <p><strong>Example of the reasoning:</strong> 2 mol contains twice the number of particles in 1 mol, so the number of particles is 2 × 6.02 × 10²³ = 1.204 × 10²⁴.</p>

  <h3>3. Calculating moles from particles</h3>

  <p>If the number of particles is known, divide it by the number of particles in one mole.</p>

  <p><strong>n = N ÷ Nₐ</strong></p>

  <p>For example, 3.01 × 10²³ particles represent half a mole because this number is half of 6.02 × 10²³.</p>

  <h3>4. Handling standard form correctly</h3>

  <p>Avogadro calculations often use numbers written in standard form, such as 3.01 × 10²³.</p>

  <p>When multiplying 0.5 by 6.02 × 10²³, calculate the ordinary numbers first:</p>

  <p>0.5 × 6.02 = 3.01</p>

  <p>The power of ten remains unchanged, giving 3.01 × 10²³.</p>

  <p>When dividing numbers with the same power of ten, the powers cancel:</p>

  <p>(3.01 × 10²³) ÷ (6.02 × 10²³) = 3.01 ÷ 6.02 = 0.5 mol.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Finding particles from moles</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Calculate the number of molecules in 2.5 mol of carbon dioxide.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1: Identify what is required.</strong> The question asks for the number of CO₂ molecules, so the final unit is molecules.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2: Write the relationship.</strong></p>

  <p>N = n × Nₐ</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3: Substitute the values.</strong></p>

  <p>N = 2.5 × 6.02 × 10²³</p>

  <p><strong>Step 4: Multiply.</strong></p>

  <p>2.5 × 6.02 = 15.05</p>

  <p>N = 15.05 × 10²³</p>

  <p><strong>Step 5: Express in standard form.</strong></p>

  <p>15.05 × 10²³ = 1.505 × 10²⁴</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 1.505 × 10²⁴ CO₂ molecules.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Finding moles from particles</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Calculate the amount of substance represented by 9.03 × 10²³ atoms of an element.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Use n = N ÷ Nₐ.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Substitute the values.</p>

  <p>n = (9.03 × 10²³) ÷ (6.02 × 10²³)</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Cancel the equal powers of ten.</p>

  <p>n = 9.03 ÷ 6.02</p>

  <p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Divide.</p>

  <p>n = 1.5 mol</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 1.5 mol of atoms.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Finding particles from a small amount</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Calculate the number of molecules in 0.025 mol of a gas.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Use N = n × Nₐ.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Substitute n = 0.025 mol.</p>

  <p>N = 0.025 × 6.02 × 10²³</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Multiply.</p>

  <p>0.025 × 6.02 = 0.1505</p>

  <p>N = 0.1505 × 10²³</p>

  <p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Convert to standard form.</p>

  <p>0.1505 × 10²³ = 1.505 × 10²²</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 1.505 × 10²² molecules.</p>

  <h3>How to choose the correct formula</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>If moles are given and particles are required, multiply: N = n × Nₐ.</li>
    <li>If particles are given and moles are required, divide: n = N ÷ Nₐ.</li>
    <li>Write the particle type in the final answer.</li>
    <li>Check whether the result is reasonable: more moles must mean more particles.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Common mistakes</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Dividing when you should multiply.</li>
    <li>Multiplying when you should divide.</li>
    <li>Dropping the power of ten.</li>
    <li>Writing “particles” when the question specifically asks for molecules, atoms or ions.</li>
    <li>Giving an amount in mol when the question asks for a number of particles.</li>
  </ul>', 1),
  (802, 802, '<h2>Relative Atomic Mass</h2>

  <h3>1. Why relative atomic mass is needed</h3>

  <p>Atoms are extremely small, so their actual masses are inconvenient to use in ordinary chemical calculations. Chemists therefore compare atomic masses using a standard reference and use relative values.</p>

  <p><strong>Relative atomic mass</strong>, represented by Aᵣ, is the weighted mean mass of the atoms of an element compared with one-twelfth of the mass of a carbon-12 atom.</p>

  <p>Relative atomic mass has no unit because it is a ratio of masses.</p>

  <h3>2. Common relative atomic masses</h3>

  <p>Use the values provided by the question or the periodic table. Common school values include:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>H = 1</li>
    <li>C = 12</li>
    <li>N = 14</li>
    <li>O = 16</li>
    <li>Na = 23</li>
    <li>Mg = 24</li>
    <li>S = 32</li>
    <li>Cl = 35.5</li>
    <li>Ca = 40</li>
    <li>Fe = 56</li>
  </ul>

  <p>These are commonly rounded school values. If a question gives a value, use that value consistently rather than silently substituting another.</p>

  <h3>3. Why chlorine has a relative atomic mass of about 35.5</h3>

  <p>Many elements exist naturally as mixtures of isotopes. Isotopes are atoms of the same element with the same number of protons but different numbers of neutrons.</p>

  <p>Chlorine mainly contains chlorine-35 and chlorine-37. Its relative atomic mass is a weighted average based on the natural abundances of these isotopes. Consequently, the value is approximately 35.5 rather than a whole number.</p>

  <p>Relative atomic mass is therefore not always the mass number of one particular atom.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Selecting the correct value</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> What relative atomic mass should be used for oxygen when calculating the relative formula mass of water, H₂O?</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the element: oxygen, O.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Use the supplied periodic-table value or the common school value Aᵣ(O) = 16.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 16, with no unit.</p>

  <p><strong>Why this matters:</strong> The value will later be added to the contribution from the two hydrogen atoms when calculating the relative formula mass of water.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Using relative atomic mass in a formula</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Find the total relative mass contribution from the chlorine atoms in CaCl₂. Use Aᵣ(Cl) = 35.5.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Read the formula. CaCl₂ contains two chlorine atoms.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Each chlorine atom contributes 35.5.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Multiply the value by the number of chlorine atoms.</p>

  <p>Chlorine contribution = 2 × 35.5 = 71.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 71 relative mass units in the formula-mass sum. The complete relative formula mass of CaCl₂ also includes calcium.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Understanding a fractional value</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> A learner argues that chlorine cannot have a relative atomic mass of 35.5 because an individual atom cannot have half a neutron. Explain the mistake.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> A single chlorine atom has a particular isotope and a whole-number mass number, such as 35 or 37.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> A natural sample contains a mixture of chlorine isotopes.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> The relative atomic mass represents a weighted average of the isotopes in the sample.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 4:</strong> An average can be fractional even when the individual mass numbers are whole numbers.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Chlorine''s relative atomic mass is a weighted average of naturally occurring isotopes, not the neutron count of a single atom.</p>

  <h3>4. Relative atomic mass versus mass number</h3>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Mass number:</strong> the total number of protons and neutrons in one particular atom; it is a whole number.</li>
    <li><strong>Relative atomic mass:</strong> the weighted mean atomic mass relative to the carbon-12 standard; it may be fractional.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Common mistakes</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Do not attach grams or g mol⁻¹ to a relative atomic mass; it has no unit.</li>
    <li>Do not confuse relative atomic mass with mass number.</li>
    <li>Do not forget that a subscript tells you how many atoms contribute to a formula-mass calculation.</li>
    <li>Use the values supplied in the question when they differ from rounded reference values.</li>
  </ul>', 1),
  (803, 803, '<h2>Relative Formula Mass</h2>

  <h3>1. What is relative formula mass?</h3>

  <p>The <strong>relative formula mass</strong>, represented by Mᵣ, is the sum of the relative atomic masses of all the atoms represented in a chemical formula.</p>

  <p>A chemical formula tells us which elements are present and how many atoms of each element must be counted. The small number written after an element symbol is called a subscript. It tells us how many atoms of that element occur in the formula.</p>

  <p>For example, H₂O contains two hydrogen atoms and one oxygen atom. Its relative formula mass is therefore calculated by adding the contribution of two hydrogen atoms to the contribution of one oxygen atom.</p>

  <p><strong>General rule:</strong></p>

  <p>Mᵣ = sum of (number of atoms of each element × its Aᵣ)</p>

  <p>Relative formula mass has no unit because relative atomic masses are dimensionless.</p>

  <h3>2. Read the formula before calculating</h3>

  <p>Before doing any arithmetic, count the atoms correctly.</p>

  <ul>
    <li>NaCl contains 1 Na atom and 1 Cl atom.</li>
    <li>H₂O contains 2 H atoms and 1 O atom.</li>
    <li>CO₂ contains 1 C atom and 2 O atoms.</li>
    <li>Ca(OH)₂ contains 1 Ca atom, 2 O atoms and 2 H atoms.</li>
    <li>Al₂(SO₄)₃ contains 2 Al atoms, 3 S atoms and 12 O atoms.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>A subscript outside brackets multiplies every atom inside those brackets. This is a frequent source of mistakes in formula-mass calculations.</p>

  <h3>3. Relative atomic masses used in these examples</h3>

  <p>Unless a question gives different values, use:</p>

  <p>H = 1, C = 12, N = 14, O = 16, Na = 23, Mg = 24, Al = 27, S = 32, Cl = 35.5, Ca = 40.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Water, H₂O</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Calculate the relative formula mass of water. Use H = 1 and O = 16.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1: Count the atoms.</strong> H₂O contains two hydrogen atoms and one oxygen atom.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2: Calculate each contribution.</strong></p>

  <p>Hydrogen contribution = 2 × 1 = 2.</p>

  <p>Oxygen contribution = 1 × 16 = 16.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3: Add the contributions.</strong></p>

  <p>Mᵣ(H₂O) = 2 + 16 = 18.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Mᵣ(H₂O) = 18.</p>

  <p><strong>Check:</strong> The hydrogen contribution must be counted twice because the formula contains H₂, not H.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Calcium hydroxide, Ca(OH)₂</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Calculate Mᵣ of Ca(OH)₂. Use Ca = 40, O = 16 and H = 1.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1: Identify the bracket.</strong> The subscript 2 outside (OH) means there are two O atoms and two H atoms.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2: Calculate calcium.</strong> Calcium contribution = 1 × 40 = 40.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3: Calculate oxygen.</strong> Oxygen contribution = 2 × 16 = 32.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 4: Calculate hydrogen.</strong> Hydrogen contribution = 2 × 1 = 2.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 5: Add all contributions.</strong></p>

  <p>Mᵣ = 40 + 32 + 2 = 74.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Mᵣ[Ca(OH)₂] = 74.</p>

  <p><strong>Check:</strong> Do not calculate Ca + O + H only once. The bracket subscript doubles both O and H.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Aluminium sulfate, Al₂(SO₄)₃</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Calculate Mᵣ of Al₂(SO₄)₃. Use Al = 27, S = 32 and O = 16.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1: Count the atoms.</strong> There are 2 Al atoms, 3 S atoms and 4 × 3 = 12 O atoms.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2: Calculate aluminium.</strong></p>

  <p>2 × 27 = 54.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3: Calculate sulfur.</strong></p>

  <p>3 × 32 = 96.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 4: Calculate oxygen.</strong></p>

  <p>12 × 16 = 192.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 5: Add the contributions.</strong></p>

  <p>Mᵣ = 54 + 96 + 192 = 342.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Mᵣ[Al₂(SO₄)₃] = 342.</p>

  <h3>4. A reliable method for every formula</h3>

  <ol>
    <li>Write the formula clearly.</li>
    <li>Count each type of atom, including atoms inside brackets.</li>
    <li>Multiply each atom count by its relative atomic mass.</li>
    <li>Add the contributions.</li>
    <li>Check that no subscript or bracket multiplier was ignored.</li>
    <li>Give Mᵣ without a unit.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Common mistakes</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Ignoring a subscript such as the 2 in CO₂.</li>
    <li>Applying a bracket subscript to only the first element inside the brackets.</li>
    <li>Writing g mol⁻¹ for relative formula mass. That unit belongs to molar mass.</li>
    <li>Adding the atomic masses without multiplying them by the number of atoms.</li>
  </ul>', 1),
  (804, 804, '<h2>Molar Mass</h2>

  <h3>1. Meaning of molar mass</h3>

  <p><strong>Molar mass</strong> is the mass of one mole of a substance. It is represented by M and is commonly expressed in grams per mole, g mol⁻¹.</p>

  <p>Relative formula mass and molar mass are closely related, but they are not identical quantities:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Relative formula mass, Mᵣ:</strong> a dimensionless relative value obtained by adding relative atomic masses.</li>
    <li><strong>Molar mass, M:</strong> the mass of one mole of a substance, expressed in g mol⁻¹.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>For a substance with a formula mass of 18, its molar mass is 18 g mol⁻¹. This means one mole of that substance has a mass of 18 g.</p>

  <h3>2. From formula to molar mass</h3>

  <p>To calculate molar mass:</p>

  <ol>
    <li>Write the chemical formula.</li>
    <li>Calculate the relative formula mass using the supplied relative atomic masses.</li>
    <li>Use the same numerical value as the molar mass in g mol⁻¹.</li>
  </ol>

  <p><strong>Example:</strong> For H₂O, Mᵣ = (2 × 1) + 16 = 18. Therefore, M = 18 g mol⁻¹.</p>

  <h3>3. Why molar mass matters</h3>

  <p>A balance measures mass, but chemical equations and mole calculations use amounts in moles. Molar mass connects these two quantities.</p>

  <p>Once the molar mass is known, you can calculate the number of moles in a measured mass, or calculate the mass needed for a required amount of substance.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Molar mass of water</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Calculate the molar mass of H₂O. Use H = 1 and O = 16.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Count the atoms: two H atoms and one O atom.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Calculate the relative formula mass.</p>

  <p>Mᵣ = (2 × 1) + 16 = 18.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Express the same numerical value as molar mass.</p>

  <p>M = 18 g mol⁻¹.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 18 g mol⁻¹. One mole of water has a mass of 18 g.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Molar mass of calcium carbonate</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Calculate the molar mass of CaCO₃. Use Ca = 40, C = 12 and O = 16.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> CaCO₃ contains one Ca, one C and three O atoms.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Calculate the contributions.</p>

  <p>Ca = 1 × 40 = 40.</p>

  <p>C = 1 × 12 = 12.</p>

  <p>O = 3 × 16 = 48.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Add the contributions.</p>

  <p>Mᵣ = 40 + 12 + 48 = 100.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Give the molar mass with the correct unit.</p>

  <p>M = 100 g mol⁻¹.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 100 g mol⁻¹.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Molar mass of aluminium sulfate</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Calculate the molar mass of Al₂(SO₄)₃. Use Al = 27, S = 32 and O = 16.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Count the atoms: 2 Al, 3 S and 12 O.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Aluminium contribution = 2 × 27 = 54.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Sulfur contribution = 3 × 32 = 96.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Oxygen contribution = 12 × 16 = 192.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 5:</strong> Add all contributions.</p>

  <p>Mᵣ = 54 + 96 + 192 = 342.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 6:</strong> Add the molar-mass unit.</p>

  <p>M = 342 g mol⁻¹.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 342 g mol⁻¹.</p>

  <h3>4. Understanding the units</h3>

  <p>The unit g mol⁻¹ means grams per mole. If a substance has a molar mass of 44 g mol⁻¹, each mole of the substance has a mass of 44 g.</p>

  <p>This does not mean every sample has a mass of 44 g. A sample containing half a mole has half that mass, while a sample containing two moles has twice that mass.</p>

  <h3>Common mistakes</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Giving relative formula mass a unit.</li>
    <li>Giving molar mass without a unit.</li>
    <li>Confusing the mass of one mole with the mass of any arbitrary sample.</li>
    <li>Failing to multiply atomic masses by the subscripts in a formula.</li>
  </ul>', 1),
  (805, 805, '<h2>Calculating Moles from Mass</h2>

  <h3>1. The relationship between mass and moles</h3>

  <p>In a laboratory, we often measure a sample using a balance. However, chemical equations describe reacting amounts in moles. We therefore need a method for converting a measured mass into an amount of substance.</p>

  <p>The relationship is:</p>

  <p><strong>n = m ÷ M</strong></p>

  <ul>
    <li>n = amount of substance in mol</li>
    <li>m = mass of the sample in g</li>
    <li>M = molar mass in g mol⁻¹</li>
  </ul>

  <p><strong>In words:</strong> number of moles = mass of the sample divided by the mass of one mole.</p>

  <h3>2. Why division works</h3>

  <p>Suppose one mole of a substance has a mass of 18 g. A sample of 36 g contains two groups of 18 g. Each group represents one mole, so the sample contains 2 mol.</p>

  <p>If the sample has a mass of 9 g, it contains half of 18 g, so it represents 0.5 mol.</p>

  <p>Dividing the sample mass by the molar mass tells us how many one-mole portions are present.</p>

  <h3>3. Choose the correct molar mass first</h3>

  <p>Before using n = m ÷ M, calculate or identify the molar mass of the substance. If the substance is H₂O, use 18 g mol⁻¹. If it is CO₂, use 44 g mol⁻¹. Do not use the mass of another compound.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Moles in 36 g of water</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Calculate the amount of substance in 36 g of H₂O. Use H = 1 and O = 16.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1: Find the molar mass.</strong></p>

  <p>M(H₂O) = (2 × 1) + 16 = 18 g mol⁻¹.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2: Write the formula.</strong></p>

  <p>n = m ÷ M</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3: Substitute the values.</strong></p>

  <p>n = 36 g ÷ 18 g mol⁻¹</p>

  <p><strong>Step 4: Calculate.</strong></p>

  <p>n = 2 mol.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 2 mol of water.</p>

  <p><strong>Check:</strong> Since 36 g is twice 18 g, the sample must contain twice one mole.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Moles in 11 g of carbon dioxide</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Calculate the amount of CO₂ in 11 g. Use C = 12 and O = 16.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1: Calculate molar mass.</strong></p>

  <p>M(CO₂) = 12 + (2 × 16) = 44 g mol⁻¹.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2: Use n = m ÷ M.</strong></p>

  <p>n = 11 ÷ 44</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3: Divide.</strong></p>

  <p>n = 0.25 mol.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 0.25 mol of CO₂.</p>

  <p><strong>Check:</strong> 11 g is one-quarter of 44 g, so the amount is one-quarter of a mole.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Moles in 12.5 g of calcium carbonate</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Calculate the amount of CaCO₃ in 12.5 g. Use Ca = 40, C = 12 and O = 16.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1: Calculate the molar mass.</strong></p>

  <p>M(CaCO₃) = 40 + 12 + (3 × 16).</p>

  <p>M(CaCO₃) = 40 + 12 + 48 = 100 g mol⁻¹.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2: Use the mole formula.</strong></p>

  <p>n = m ÷ M</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3: Substitute.</strong></p>

  <p>n = 12.5 ÷ 100</p>

  <p><strong>Step 4: Calculate.</strong></p>

  <p>n = 0.125 mol.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 0.125 mol of CaCO₃.</p>

  <h3>4. Unit check</h3>

  <p>Mass is measured in grams, and molar mass is measured in grams per mole:</p>

  <p>n = g ÷ (g mol⁻¹) = mol.</p>

  <p>The grams cancel, leaving moles. This confirms that the formula produces the correct unit.</p>

  <h3>Common mistakes</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Multiplying mass by molar mass instead of dividing.</li>
    <li>Using Mᵣ without first expressing the molar mass in g mol⁻¹.</li>
    <li>Forgetting to calculate the molar mass from the chemical formula.</li>
    <li>Reporting the answer in grams when the question asks for moles.</li>
    <li>Ignoring the units or using a molar mass belonging to a different substance.</li>
  </ul>', 1),
  (806, 806, '<h2>Calculating Mass from Moles</h2>

  <h3>1. The relationship between mass, moles and molar mass</h3>

  <p>Sometimes the amount of substance is known, but the mass is required. For example, a laboratory procedure may require 0.25 mol of a substance, and the learner must calculate how many grams to weigh.</p>

  <p>The relationship is:</p>

  <p><strong>m = n × M</strong></p>

  <ul>
    <li>m = mass in g</li>
    <li>n = amount of substance in mol</li>
    <li>M = molar mass in g mol⁻¹</li>
  </ul>

  <p><strong>In words:</strong> mass = amount in moles × mass per mole.</p>

  <h3>2. Why multiplication works</h3>

  <p>If one mole of a substance has a mass of 18 g, then two moles have a mass of 2 × 18 = 36 g.</p>

  <p>Likewise, half a mole has a mass of 0.5 × 18 = 9 g.</p>

  <p>The amount in moles tells us how many one-mole portions we have. Multiplying that amount by the mass of each mole gives the total mass.</p>

  <h3>3. Choose the correct molar mass</h3>

  <p>Always identify the substance and calculate its molar mass before substituting into the equation. Do not use the molar mass of a different substance just because it appears in a previous question.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Mass of 3 mol of water</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Calculate the mass of 3 mol of H₂O. Use H = 1 and O = 16.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1: Calculate molar mass.</strong></p>

  <p>M(H₂O) = (2 × 1) + 16 = 18 g mol⁻¹.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2: Write the formula.</strong></p>

  <p>m = n × M</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3: Substitute.</strong></p>

  <p>m = 3 × 18</p>

  <p><strong>Step 4: Calculate.</strong></p>

  <p>m = 54 g.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 54 g of water.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Mass of 0.25 mol of carbon dioxide</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Calculate the mass of 0.25 mol of CO₂. Use C = 12 and O = 16.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Calculate M(CO₂) = 12 + (2 × 16) = 44 g mol⁻¹.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Use m = n × M.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Substitute m = 0.25 × 44.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Calculate m = 11 g.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 11 g of CO₂.</p>

  <p><strong>Check:</strong> One-quarter of 44 g is 11 g, so the result is reasonable.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Mass of 1.5 mol of calcium carbonate</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Calculate the mass of 1.5 mol of CaCO₃. Use Ca = 40, C = 12 and O = 16.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> M(CaCO₃) = 40 + 12 + (3 × 16) = 100 g mol⁻¹.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Use m = n × M.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Substitute m = 1.5 × 100.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Calculate m = 150 g.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 150 g of CaCO₃.</p>

  <h3>4. Checking the units</h3>

  <p>Mass = mol × g mol⁻¹.</p>

  <p>The mol units cancel, leaving grams. Therefore, the final answer must be a mass in grams when the molar mass is given in g mol⁻¹.</p>

  <h3>5. Connecting both mass–mole formulas</h3>

  <p>The two formulas describe the same relationship:</p>

  <p><strong>n = m ÷ M</strong></p>

  <p><strong>m = n × M</strong></p>

  <p>Use division when finding moles from mass. Use multiplication when finding mass from moles.</p>

  <h3>Common mistakes</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Dividing by molar mass when the mass is required.</li>
    <li>Using relative formula mass without the molar-mass unit.</li>
    <li>Using the wrong chemical formula to calculate molar mass.</li>
    <li>Reporting a mass in moles or an amount in grams.</li>
  </ul>', 1),
  (807, 807, '<h2>Moles and Particles</h2>

  <h3>1. Connecting amount of substance to particle count</h3>

  <p>A mole is a counting unit. One mole contains the Avogadro constant number of specified entities:</p>

  <p><strong>Nₐ = 6.02 × 10²³ mol⁻¹</strong></p>

  <p>This allows us to convert between the amount of substance and the number of particles.</p>

  <p><strong>Number of particles:</strong></p>

  <p>N = n × Nₐ</p>

  <p><strong>Amount in moles:</strong></p>

  <p>n = N ÷ Nₐ</p>

  <ul>
    <li>N = number of specified particles</li>
    <li>n = amount of substance in mol</li>
    <li>Nₐ = Avogadro constant</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>2. Always identify the particle type</h3>

  <p>Before calculating, read the formula and question carefully.</p>

  <ul>
    <li>For He, the entities are helium atoms.</li>
    <li>For H₂, the entities are hydrogen molecules.</li>
    <li>For H₂O, the entities are water molecules.</li>
    <li>For Na⁺, the entities are sodium ions.</li>
    <li>For NaCl, the entities are formula units in the ionic solid.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Counting molecules is not always the same as counting atoms. A molecule may contain several atoms, so the total atom count can be greater than the molecule count.</p>

  <h3>3. Atoms inside molecules</h3>

  <p>Consider one mole of O₂ molecules. It contains 6.02 × 10²³ O₂ molecules. Since every O₂ molecule contains two oxygen atoms, the total number of oxygen atoms is:</p>

  <p>2 × 6.02 × 10²³ = 1.204 × 10²⁴ oxygen atoms.</p>

  <p>For one mole of H₂O molecules, each molecule contains three atoms in total: two H atoms and one O atom. Therefore, one mole of water molecules contains 2 mol of hydrogen atoms and 1 mol of oxygen atoms.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Molecules in 2 mol of CO₂</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Calculate the number of molecules in 2 mol of carbon dioxide.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> The specified entities are CO₂ molecules.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Use N = n × Nₐ.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Substitute N = 2 × 6.02 × 10²³.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Calculate N = 12.04 × 10²³.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 5:</strong> Convert to standard form: N = 1.204 × 10²⁴.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 1.204 × 10²⁴ CO₂ molecules.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Atoms in 0.5 mol of oxygen gas</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Calculate the number of oxygen atoms in 0.5 mol of O₂ molecules.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> First calculate the number of O₂ molecules.</p>

  <p>N(molecules) = 0.5 × 6.02 × 10²³.</p>

  <p>N(molecules) = 3.01 × 10²³.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Each O₂ molecule contains two oxygen atoms.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Multiply the number of molecules by two.</p>

  <p>N(atoms) = 2 × 3.01 × 10²³.</p>

  <p>N(atoms) = 6.02 × 10²³.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 6.02 × 10²³ oxygen atoms.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Finding moles from a particle count</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> A sample contains 1.505 × 10²³ molecules of a substance. Calculate the amount in moles.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Use n = N ÷ Nₐ.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Substitute n = (1.505 × 10²³) ÷ (6.02 × 10²³).</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Cancel the equal powers of ten.</p>

  <p>n = 1.505 ÷ 6.02.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Calculate n = 0.25 mol.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 0.25 mol.</p>

  <h3>4. A reliable method</h3>

  <ol>
    <li>Identify the entity being counted.</li>
    <li>Identify whether the question gives moles or a particle count.</li>
    <li>Use N = n × Nₐ when converting moles to particles.</li>
    <li>Use n = N ÷ Nₐ when converting particles to moles.</li>
    <li>If the question asks for atoms within molecules, use the formula''s subscripts to count the atoms per molecule.</li>
    <li>Include the correct particle type in the final answer.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Common mistakes</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Reporting molecules when the question asks for atoms.</li>
    <li>Forgetting that O₂ has two oxygen atoms per molecule.</li>
    <li>Dividing by Avogadro''s constant when the number of particles is required from moles.</li>
    <li>Multiplying by Avogadro''s constant when the amount in moles is required from particles.</li>
    <li>Ignoring powers of ten when dividing or multiplying standard-form numbers.</li>
  </ul>', 1),
