  <h2>Reactions of Acids with Metals</h2>

  <h3>1. The general reaction</h3>

  <p>
    A sufficiently reactive metal reacts with a suitable dilute acid to
    form a <strong>salt and hydrogen gas</strong>.
  </p>

  <p><strong>General word equation:</strong></p>

  <p>Metal + Acid → Salt + Hydrogen</p>

  <p>
    The salt formed depends on the metal and the acid used. For example,
    hydrochloric acid forms chloride salts, while dilute sulfuric acid
    forms sulfate salts.
  </p>

  <p>
    Not every metal reacts with dilute acids in the same way. Metals
    below hydrogen in the reactivity series, such as copper, do not
    normally displace hydrogen from dilute hydrochloric acid or dilute
    sulfuric acid.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Recognising hydrogen gas</h3>

  <p>
    A reacting metal may produce bubbles of gas. To test whether the
    gas is hydrogen, collect a small sample safely and bring a lighted
    splint to the mouth of the test tube as instructed by the teacher.
    Hydrogen burns with a characteristic squeaky pop.
  </p>

  <p>
    Bubbles alone do not prove that the gas is hydrogen. The gas must
    be tested appropriately.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Worked examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: Magnesium and hydrochloric acid</strong></p>

  <p>
    Write the balanced equation for magnesium reacting with dilute
    hydrochloric acid.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1: Predict the products.</strong></p>
  <p>
    Magnesium forms magnesium chloride, and hydrogen gas is released.
  </p>

  <p>
    Mg + HCl → MgCl<sub>2</sub> + H<sub>2</sub>
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2: Balance chlorine and hydrogen.</strong></p>
  <p>
    Magnesium chloride contains two chlorine atoms, so place 2 before HCl.
    This also gives two hydrogen atoms, which form one H<sub>2</sub> molecule.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Balanced equation:</strong>
    Mg(s) + 2HCl(aq) → MgCl<sub>2</sub>(aq) + H<sub>2</sub>(g)
  </p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: Zinc and sulfuric acid</strong></p>

  <p>
    Predict the products when zinc reacts with dilute sulfuric acid.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> The salt is zinc sulfate.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Hydrogen gas is the other product.</p>

  <p>
    <strong>Balanced equation:</strong>
    Zn(s) + H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub>(aq) →
    ZnSO<sub>4</sub>(aq) + H<sub>2</sub>(g)
  </p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: Identifying the gas</strong></p>

  <p>
    Magnesium reacts with dilute hydrochloric acid. A gas is collected
    and burns with a squeaky pop when tested with a lighted splint.
    Identify the gas and explain your answer.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Recall the reaction pattern.</p>
  <p>
    A reactive metal reacting with a suitable dilute acid produces a
    salt and hydrogen.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Interpret the test.</p>
  <p>
    The squeaky-pop test is the characteristic test for hydrogen.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The gas is hydrogen.</p>

  <h3>4. Observations</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Bubbles may form as gas is released.</li>
    <li>The metal may gradually dissolve.</li>
    <li>The reaction may warm the mixture.</li>
    <li>The rate depends on the metal, acid concentration, temperature and surface area.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Carry out gas tests only with small quantities under teacher
    supervision, away from flames during gas collection.
  </p>
