  (827, 827, '
  <h2>Reactions of Acids with Hydrogen Carbonates</h2>

  <h3>1. What is a hydrogen carbonate?</h3>

  <p>
    A hydrogen carbonate contains the hydrogen carbonate ion,
    HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>−</sup>. Sodium hydrogen carbonate,
    NaHCO<sub>3</sub>, is one example.
  </p>

  <p>
    Hydrogen carbonates react with acids to form a <strong>salt, water
    and carbon dioxide</strong>.
  </p>

  <p><strong>General word equation:</strong></p>

  <p>
    Acid + Hydrogen Carbonate → Salt + Water + Carbon Dioxide
  </p>

  <p>
    The products resemble those of acid-carbonate reactions. However,
    a hydrogen carbonate contains HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>−</sup>, whereas
    a carbonate contains CO<sub>3</sub><sup>2−</sup>.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Worked examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: Sodium hydrogen carbonate and hydrochloric acid</strong></p>

  <p>
    Write the balanced equation for sodium hydrogen carbonate reacting
    with hydrochloric acid.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1: Identify the salt.</strong></p>
  <p>
    Sodium hydrogen carbonate supplies sodium, and hydrochloric acid
    supplies chloride. The salt is sodium chloride.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2: Identify the other products.</strong></p>
  <p>
    The reaction also forms water and carbon dioxide.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Balanced equation:</strong>
    NaHCO<sub>3</sub>(s) + HCl(aq) →
    NaCl(aq) + H<sub>2</sub>O(l) + CO<sub>2</sub>(g)
  </p>

  <p>
    Check: Na = 1, H = 2, C = 1, O = 3 and Cl = 1 on both sides.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: Potassium hydrogen carbonate and nitric acid</strong></p>

  <p>
    Predict the products when potassium hydrogen carbonate reacts with
    nitric acid.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> The salt is potassium nitrate, KNO<sub>3</sub>.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Water and carbon dioxide are also formed.</p>

  <p>
    <strong>Balanced equation:</strong>
    KHCO<sub>3</sub> + HNO<sub>3</sub> →
    KNO<sub>3</sub> + H<sub>2</sub>O + CO<sub>2</sub>
  </p>

  <p>
    All atoms balance with a coefficient of 1 for each substance.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: Distinguishing the gas from the salt</strong></p>

  <p>
    Sodium hydrogen carbonate reacts with dilute hydrochloric acid.
    Name the gas and the salt formed.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Use the reaction pattern to identify the gas.</p>
  <p>
    Acid-hydrogen carbonate reactions produce carbon dioxide.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Identify the salt from the reactants.</p>
  <p>
    Sodium hydrogen carbonate reacting with hydrochloric acid forms sodium chloride.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Carbon dioxide gas and sodium chloride.</p>

  <h3>3. Observations and interpretation</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Effervescence occurs as carbon dioxide is released.</li>
    <li>The hydrogen carbonate may gradually be used up.</li>
    <li>The gas turns limewater milky in the standard carbon dioxide test.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Remember the complete product pattern: the salt alone is not the
    only product. Water and carbon dioxide are also formed.
  </p>
