  <h2>Bases</h2>

  <h3>1. What is a base?</h3>

  <p>
    A <strong>base</strong> is a substance that neutralises an acid.
    Many common bases are metal oxides or metal hydroxides. Some bases
    are soluble in water, while others are insoluble.
  </p>

  <p>
    For example, copper(II) oxide is a base that reacts with sulfuric
    acid to form copper(II) sulfate and water.
  </p>

  <p>
    CuO(s) + H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub>(aq) →
    CuSO<sub>4</sub>(aq) + H<sub>2</sub>O(l)
  </p>

  <h3>2. Important properties</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Bases neutralise acids.</li>
    <li>Many bases are metal oxides or metal hydroxides.</li>
    <li>Some bases dissolve in water, while others do not.</li>
    <li>Soluble bases form alkaline solutions in water.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    A base should not automatically be called an alkali. The term
    alkali specifically refers to a base that dissolves in water and
    produces an alkaline solution.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Worked examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: Identifying a base</strong></p>

  <p>
    Copper(II) oxide reacts with hydrochloric acid to form copper(II)
    chloride and water. Explain why copper(II) oxide is classified
    as a base.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Recall the defining property of a base.</p>
  <p>A base neutralises an acid.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Examine the reaction.</p>
  <p>
    Copper(II) oxide reacts with hydrochloric acid, producing a salt
    and water.
  </p>

  <p>
    CuO + 2HCl → CuCl<sub>2</sub> + H<sub>2</sub>O
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Copper(II) oxide is a base because it
  neutralises hydrochloric acid to form a salt and water.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: Identifying the products</strong></p>

  <p>
    Predict the products when magnesium oxide reacts with sulfuric acid.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the metal in the oxide: magnesium.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Identify the acid''s salt-forming ion: sulfate.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> The products are magnesium sulfate and water.</p>

  <p>
    <strong>Equation:</strong>
    MgO + H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub> →
    MgSO<sub>4</sub> + H<sub>2</sub>O
  </p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: Base or alkali?</strong></p>

  <p>
    A student has a sample of insoluble copper(II) oxide. Should the
    student describe it as an alkali?
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Recall that an alkali is a base soluble in water.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Copper(II) oxide is insoluble in water.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> It is a base, but it is not an alkali.</p>

  <h3>4. Key distinction</h3>

  <p>
    Every alkali is a base, but not every base is an alkali.
    Solubility in water distinguishes an alkali from an insoluble base.
  </p>
