  <h2>Alkalis</h2>

  <h3>1. What is an alkali?</h3>

  <p>
    An <strong>alkali</strong> is a base that dissolves in water and
    produces an alkaline solution containing hydroxide ions,
    OH<sup>−</sup>.
  </p>

  <p>
    Examples include sodium hydroxide, potassium hydroxide and aqueous
    ammonia. Sodium hydroxide and potassium hydroxide are soluble
    metal hydroxides.
  </p>

  <p>
    Sodium hydroxide dissolves in water and separates into ions:
  </p>

  <p>
    NaOH(s) → Na<sup>+</sup>(aq) + OH<sup>−</sup>(aq)
  </p>

  <p>
    The hydroxide ions are responsible for the alkaline properties of
    the solution.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Properties of alkalis</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>They neutralise acids.</li>
    <li>Their aqueous solutions have a pH above 7 under the usual school-level conditions.</li>
    <li>They turn red litmus paper blue.</li>
    <li>Their aqueous solutions conduct electricity because they contain mobile ions.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Strong alkalis can be corrosive. Never taste chemicals to identify
    them, and avoid direct skin contact.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Worked examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: Identifying an alkali</strong></p>

  <p>
    Sodium hydroxide dissolves in water. Explain why the resulting
    solution is alkaline.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Sodium hydroxide separates into ions in water.</p>
  <p>
    NaOH → Na<sup>+</sup> + OH<sup>−</sup>
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Identify the important ion.</p>
  <p>
    The solution contains hydroxide ions, OH<sup>−</sup>.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> It is alkaline because dissolved sodium
  hydroxide produces hydroxide ions in water.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: Classifying a solution by pH</strong></p>

  <p>
    A solution has a pH of 11. Is it acidic, neutral or alkaline?
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Compare the pH with 7.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Since 11 is greater than 7, the solution is alkaline.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Alkaline.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: Electrical conductivity</strong></p>

  <p>
    Explain why aqueous potassium hydroxide conducts electricity.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Potassium hydroxide forms K<sup>+</sup> and OH<sup>−</sup> ions in water.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> These ions can move through the solution.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The mobile ions carry electric charge through the solution.</p>

  <h3>4. Remember</h3>

  <p>
    An alkali is not simply any substance that feels slippery or has
    a high pH. Its chemical classification depends on its behaviour
    in water. Never touch or taste an unknown chemical.
  </p>
