  <h2>Litmus</h2>

  <h3>1. What is litmus?</h3>

  <p>
    Litmus is an acid-base indicator. It changes colour depending on
    whether it is exposed to an acidic or alkaline solution.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Acid:</strong> blue litmus turns red.</li>
    <li><strong>Alkali:</strong> red litmus turns blue.</li>
    <li><strong>Neutral solution:</strong> litmus shows no acid-or-alkali colour change; red litmus remains red and blue litmus remains blue.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Litmus helps classify a solution as acidic or alkaline, but it does
    not provide an exact pH value.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Using litmus correctly</h3>

  <p>
    Use clean litmus paper and avoid contaminating the sample. Test a
    small amount of solution according to laboratory instructions.
    Record the original colour and the final colour before drawing
    a conclusion.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Worked examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: Blue litmus turns red</strong></p>

  <p>
    A solution changes blue litmus paper to red. Classify the solution.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Recall the colour change caused by acids.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Compare the observed change with the rule.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The solution is acidic.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: Red litmus turns blue</strong></p>

  <p>
    Red litmus paper turns blue when placed in a solution. What does
    the result indicate?
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Red litmus turns blue in an alkaline solution.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Match the observation.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The solution is alkaline.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: No colour change</strong></p>

  <p>
    Red litmus remains red and blue litmus remains blue in a sample.
    Can you conclude the exact pH?
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Recognise that litmus does not measure pH numerically.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> No colour change with both papers is consistent with a neutral solution, but it does not establish an exact pH.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> No. Litmus cannot provide an exact pH value.</p>

  <h3>4. Limitation of litmus</h3>

  <p>
    Litmus gives a broad classification, not a numerical measurement.
    For more detailed colour-based pH estimation, universal indicator
    is more useful.
  </p>
