  <h2>Universal Indicator</h2>

  <h3>1. What is universal indicator?</h3>

  <p>
    Universal indicator is a mixture of indicators that produces
    different colours across a range of pH values. It helps estimate
    how acidic or alkaline a solution is.
  </p>

  <p>
    Unlike litmus, which mainly distinguishes acidic from alkaline
    solutions, universal indicator gives a range of colours that can
    be compared with a reference colour chart.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Interpreting its colours</h3>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Strongly acidic:</strong> typically red.</li>
    <li><strong>Weakly acidic:</strong> typically orange or yellow.</li>
    <li><strong>Near neutral:</strong> typically green around pH 7.</li>
    <li><strong>Alkaline:</strong> typically blue to purple as pH increases.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    The exact colour depends on the indicator formulation and the
    reference chart. Always compare the result with the chart supplied
    for the indicator being used.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Worked examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: A red universal-indicator result</strong></p>

  <p>
    Universal indicator turns red in a sample. What does the colour
    suggest?
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Compare red with the universal-indicator chart.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Red corresponds to a strongly acidic region.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The sample is strongly acidic, subject to the chart used.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: A green result</strong></p>

  <p>
    Universal indicator turns green. What pH region does this usually
    suggest?
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Green is the typical neutral colour.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Neutral aqueous solutions are represented around pH 7 on the usual school-level chart.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The solution is approximately neutral, around pH 7.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: Comparing two solutions</strong></p>

  <p>
    Sample A produces a yellow colour, while Sample B produces a blue
    colour. Which sample is more alkaline?
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Yellow typically indicates an acidic region.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Blue typically indicates an alkaline region.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Sample B is more alkaline.</p>

  <h3>4. Limitations</h3>

  <p>
    Universal indicator estimates pH by colour comparison. It does not
    normally give the precision of a properly calibrated pH meter.
    A contaminated sample or poor lighting can make the colour harder
    to interpret.
  </p>
