  <h2>pH Scale</h2>

  <h3>1. What is pH?</h3>

  <p>
    The <strong>pH scale</strong> is used to express how acidic or
    alkaline an aqueous solution is. On the usual school-level scale,
    values range from 0 to 14, with 7 representing neutrality.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>pH below 7:</strong> acidic.</li>
    <li><strong>pH 7:</strong> neutral.</li>
    <li><strong>pH above 7:</strong> alkaline.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    These classifications apply to the usual school-level treatment
    of aqueous solutions. Values outside 0–14 can occur under some
    conditions, so the range is not an absolute limit for every
    possible solution.
  </p>

  <h3>2. What do pH numbers mean?</h3>

  <p>
    A lower pH indicates greater acidity, while a higher pH indicates
    greater alkalinity in the usual comparisons made at school.
  </p>

  <p>
    The pH scale is logarithmic. A change of one pH unit corresponds
    to a tenfold change in hydrogen-ion activity; in dilute solutions,
    this is commonly introduced as an approximately tenfold change
    in hydrogen-ion concentration.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Worked examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: Classifying pH 2</strong></p>

  <p>
    A solution has pH 2. Classify it.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Compare 2 with 7.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Since 2 is below 7, the solution is acidic.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Acidic.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: Classifying pH 7</strong></p>

  <p>
    A solution has pH 7 under the usual school-level conditions.
    Classify it.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify 7 as the neutral reference point.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Neutral.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: Comparing pH 3 and pH 5</strong></p>

  <p>
    Which solution is more acidic: one with pH 3 or one with pH 5?
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Compare the two values.</p>
  <p>
    Since 3 is lower than 5, the pH 3 solution is more acidic.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Find the difference.</p>
  <p>5 − 3 = 2 pH units.</p>

  <p>
    <strong>Answer:</strong> The pH 3 solution is more acidic. The
    difference is two pH units, corresponding to a hundredfold
    difference in hydrogen-ion activity.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Important caution</h3>

  <p>
    pH does not directly tell you the total amount of acid present.
    Concentration, volume and the identity of the acid matter when
    comparing how much acid two samples contain.
  </p>
