  <h2>Interpreting pH</h2>

  <h3>1. Reading a pH value</h3>

  <p>
    Interpreting pH means using the numerical value to classify a
    solution and compare its acidity or alkalinity.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>A lower pH indicates greater acidity.</li>
    <li>A pH near 7 is near neutral under the usual school-level conditions.</li>
    <li>A higher pH indicates greater alkalinity.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Remember that pH is logarithmic. A difference of one unit represents
    a tenfold difference in hydrogen-ion activity, not a simple increase
    or decrease by one equal-sized amount of acidity.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Worked examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: Ordering acidic solutions</strong></p>

  <p>
    Arrange solutions with pH 2, pH 4 and pH 6 from most acidic to
    least acidic.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Lower pH means greater acidity.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Order the numbers from lowest to highest.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> pH 2, pH 4, pH 6.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: Comparing pH 4 and pH 6</strong></p>

  <p>
    How many pH units separate the two solutions, and what is the
    approximate hydrogen-ion activity ratio?
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Calculate the difference.</p>
  <p>6 − 4 = 2 pH units.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Each pH unit corresponds to a tenfold change.</p>
  <p>
    Two units correspond to 10 × 10 = 100.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Answer:</strong> Two pH units; the pH 4 solution has
    approximately 100 times the hydrogen-ion activity of the pH 6
    solution.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: Interpreting a pH change</strong></p>

  <p>
    A solution changes from pH 5 to pH 3. Has its acidity increased
    or decreased?
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Compare the starting and final pH.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> The pH has fallen by two units.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> A lower pH indicates greater acidity.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Acidity has increased. The hydrogen-ion
  activity has increased by a factor of about 100.</p>

  <h3>3. Avoid these mistakes</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Do not say pH 6 is more acidic than pH 3; pH 3 is more acidic.</li>
    <li>Do not treat pH differences as ordinary linear differences in acidity.</li>
    <li>Do not infer the total amount of acid from pH alone.</li>
  </ul>
