  <h2>Neutralisation</h2>

  <h3>1. What is neutralisation?</h3>

  <p>
    <strong>Neutralisation</strong> is a reaction in which an acid
    reacts with a base. In the usual school-level reaction between
    an acid and a base such as a metal hydroxide, the products are
    a salt and water.
  </p>

  <p><strong>General word equation:</strong></p>

  <p>Acid + Base → Salt + Water</p>

  <p>
    The salt formed depends on the acid and the base used. For example,
    hydrochloric acid forms chloride salts, while sulfuric acid forms
    sulfate salts.
  </p>

  <h3>2. The ionic explanation</h3>

  <p>
    For the reaction between an aqueous acid and a soluble hydroxide,
    hydrogen ions react with hydroxide ions to form water:
  </p>

  <p>
    H<sup>+</sup>(aq) + OH<sup>−</sup>(aq) →
    H<sub>2</sub>O(l)
  </p>

  <p>
    This is the simplified net ionic equation used at school level.
    In water, hydrogen ions are associated with water molecules.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Worked examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: Hydrochloric acid and sodium hydroxide</strong></p>

  <p>
    Write the balanced equation for hydrochloric acid reacting with
    sodium hydroxide.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1: Identify the salt.</strong></p>
  <p>
    Hydrochloric acid supplies chloride, and sodium hydroxide supplies
    sodium. The salt is sodium chloride.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2: Add water as the other product.</strong></p>

  <p>
    HCl + NaOH → NaCl + H<sub>2</sub>O
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3: Check the equation.</strong></p>
  <p>
    There is one sodium, one chlorine, two hydrogen and one oxygen
    atom on each side.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong>
    HCl(aq) + NaOH(aq) → NaCl(aq) + H<sub>2</sub>O(l)
  </p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: Sulfuric acid and potassium hydroxide</strong></p>

  <p>
    Write the balanced equation for sulfuric acid reacting completely
    with potassium hydroxide.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> The salt is potassium sulfate, K<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub>.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Write the unbalanced equation.</p>

  <p>
    H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub> + KOH →
    K<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub> + H<sub>2</sub>O
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Balance potassium.</p>
  <p>
    Place 2 before KOH to supply two potassium atoms.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Balance hydrogen and oxygen.</p>
  <p>
    Two KOH units and one sulfuric acid molecule contain four hydrogen
    atoms in total, so place 2 before water.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Balanced equation:</strong></p>

  <p>
    H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub> + 2KOH →
    K<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub> + 2H<sub>2</sub>O
  </p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: Net ionic equation</strong></p>

  <p>
    Write the net ionic equation for hydrogen ions reacting with
    hydroxide ions during neutralisation.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the reacting ions: H<sup>+</sup> and OH<sup>−</sup>.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Their product is water.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong>
    H<sup>+</sup>(aq) + OH<sup>−</sup>(aq) →
    H<sub>2</sub>O(l)
  </p>

  <h3>4. Understanding neutralisation</h3>

  <p>
    Neutralisation does not mean that every mixture of acid and base
    automatically ends at pH 7. The final pH depends on the quantities
    and strengths of the substances and the reaction conditions.
  </p>

  <p>
    A neutral solution is one with the appropriate balance of acidic
    and basic character; at ordinary school-level conditions, pure
    water is represented as pH 7.
  </p>
