  <h2>Soluble Salts</h2>

  <h3>Meaning of a Salt</h3>
  <p>A <strong>salt</strong> is a compound formed when the hydrogen ions of an acid are replaced by metal ions or ammonium ions. Salts contain positive ions and negative ions.</p>

  <h3>Meaning of Solubility</h3>
  <p>A salt is described as <strong>soluble</strong> if it dissolves in water to form a solution. A salt described as insoluble dissolves only to a very small extent in water under the stated conditions.</p>
  <p>Solubility is not the same as whether a substance is a solid. A soluble salt may be a solid before it is added to water.</p>

  <h3>Common Soluble Salts</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Sodium chloride — NaCl</li>
    <li>Potassium nitrate — KNO₃</li>
    <li>Copper(II) sulfate — CuSO₄</li>
    <li>Magnesium sulfate — MgSO₄</li>
    <li>Ammonium chloride — NH₄Cl</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>How the Acid Helps Name the Salt</h3>
  <p>The acid used determines the negative ion, which helps determine the salt''s name:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Hydrochloric acid forms <strong>chloride</strong> salts.</li>
    <li>Sulfuric acid forms <strong>sulfate</strong> salts.</li>
    <li>Nitric acid forms <strong>nitrate</strong> salts.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The positive ion identifies the other part of the salt''s name. For example, copper ions combined with sulfate ions form copper(II) sulfate.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Identify a Soluble Salt</h3>
  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Name the salt NaCl.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Na represents sodium.</li>
    <li>Cl in this compound represents chloride.</li>
    <li>Combine the ion names: sodium chloride.</li>
  </ol>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Sodium chloride.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Name the Salt from Its Acid</h3>
  <p><strong>Question:</strong> What salt can be formed when sulfuric acid reacts with copper(II) oxide?</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Sulfuric acid forms sulfate salts.</li>
    <li>The copper(II) oxide supplies copper(II) ions to the salt.</li>
    <li>Combine the names copper(II) and sulfate.</li>
  </ol>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Copper(II) sulfate, CuSO₄.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Identify the Acid from the Salt</h3>
  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Which acid provides the nitrate part of potassium nitrate?</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Identify the negative ion in potassium nitrate: nitrate, NO₃⁻.</li>
    <li>Nitrate salts are associated with nitric acid.</li>
  </ol>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Nitric acid, HNO₃.</p>

  <h3>Check Your Understanding</h3>
  <p>Answer each question in writing. Explain your reasoning where requested.</p>
