  <h2>Insoluble Salts</h2>

  <h3>Meaning of an Insoluble Salt</h3>
  <p>An <strong>insoluble salt</strong> is a salt that dissolves only to a very small extent in water under the stated conditions. When it forms as a solid from aqueous solutions, the solid is called a <strong>precipitate</strong>.</p>
  <p>Insoluble does not mean that absolutely no particles dissolve. It means that the amount that dissolves is very small.</p>

  <h3>Examples of Insoluble Salts</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Silver chloride — AgCl</li>
    <li>Barium sulfate — BaSO₄</li>
    <li>Lead(II) iodide — PbI₂</li>
  </ul>
  <p>These are examples of salts that are very sparingly soluble in water. Lead compounds are toxic and must not be handled casually.</p>

  <h3>Recognising an Insoluble Salt</h3>
  <p>If solutions containing suitable ions are mixed, an insoluble salt may form as a solid. The appearance of the solid indicates that a new substance has formed, rather than the product remaining dissolved.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Name an Insoluble Salt</h3>
  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Name AgCl.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Ag represents silver.</li>
    <li>Cl represents chloride in this compound.</li>
    <li>Combine the names.</li>
  </ol>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Silver chloride.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Identify the Solid</h3>
  <p><strong>Question:</strong> A white solid of silver chloride forms when suitable aqueous solutions are mixed. What is the solid called in this reaction?</p>
  <ol>
    <li>The product is a salt.</li>
    <li>It forms as a solid from solutions.</li>
    <li>A solid formed in this way is called a precipitate.</li>
  </ol>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Silver chloride precipitate.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Interpret a Formula</h3>
  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Name BaSO₄ and identify the two ions.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Ba²⁺ is the barium ion.</li>
    <li>SO₄²⁻ is the sulfate ion.</li>
    <li>The compound is named barium sulfate.</li>
  </ol>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Barium sulfate, made from barium ions and sulfate ions.</p>

  <h3>Check Your Understanding</h3>
  <p>Answer each question in writing.</p>
