  <h2>Preparing Soluble Salts from Acids and Metals</h2>

  <h3>When This Method Is Suitable</h3>
  <p>This method is suitable when a suitable reactive metal reacts with a dilute acid to form the required soluble salt. Not every metal reacts with every dilute acid. For example, copper does not react with dilute hydrochloric acid under ordinary laboratory conditions.</p>

  <h3>General Reaction</h3>
  <p><strong>Reactive metal + dilute acid → salt + hydrogen gas</strong></p>
  <p>The acid determines the negative ion of the salt. For example, sulfuric acid forms sulfate salts, while hydrochloric acid forms chloride salts.</p>

  <h3>Laboratory Preparation</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Place a measured amount of a suitable dilute acid in a suitable container and warm it gently if instructed.</li>
    <li>Add small portions of the reactive metal carefully.</li>
    <li>Continue until the reaction stops and a little metal remains in excess. This helps ensure that the acid has been used up.</li>
    <li>Filter the mixture to remove the excess unreacted metal.</li>
    <li>Gently heat the filtrate to concentrate the salt solution until it reaches the crystallisation point.</li>
    <li>Allow the solution to cool so crystals can form.</li>
    <li>Separate the crystals and dry them appropriately.</li>
  </ol>
  <p><strong>Safety:</strong> Hydrogen is flammable. Keep flames away from the reaction, use appropriate eye protection, and follow laboratory supervision. Never seal a reacting mixture in a closed container.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Magnesium and Hydrochloric Acid</h3>
  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Write the balanced equation for magnesium reacting with dilute hydrochloric acid.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>The products are magnesium chloride and hydrogen.</li>
    <li>Write the formula equation: Mg + HCl → MgCl₂ + H₂.</li>
    <li>MgCl₂ contains two chlorine atoms, so place 2 before HCl.</li>
    <li>Two hydrogen atoms then form one H₂ molecule.</li>
  </ol>
  <p><strong>Balanced equation:</strong> Mg(s) + 2HCl(aq) → MgCl₂(aq) + H₂(g).</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Zinc and Sulfuric Acid</h3>
  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Write the balanced equation for zinc reacting with dilute sulfuric acid.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>The salt formed is zinc sulfate.</li>
    <li>Hydrogen gas is the other product.</li>
    <li>The formula equation is already balanced in a 1:1:1:1 ratio.</li>
  </ol>
  <p><strong>Balanced equation:</strong> Zn(s) + H₂SO₄(aq) → ZnSO₄(aq) + H₂(g).</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Choosing the Correct Preparation Steps</h3>
  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Why is excess magnesium filtered off before the salt solution is concentrated?</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Magnesium is added in excess to help use up the acid.</li>
    <li>Any remaining magnesium is an unreacted solid.</li>
    <li>Filtration separates that solid from the salt solution.</li>
  </ol>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> To remove the unreacted magnesium before crystallising the dissolved salt.</p>

  <h3>Check Your Understanding</h3>
  <p>Answer each question in writing.</p>
