  <h2>Preparing Soluble Salts from Insoluble Bases</h2>

  <h3>Principle</h3>
  <p>An insoluble base, such as copper(II) oxide, can react with a suitable dilute acid to form a salt and water. Because the base is insoluble, excess solid can be filtered off after the reaction.</p>

  <h3>General Reaction</h3>
  <p><strong>Insoluble base + acid → salt + water</strong></p>
  <p>This method does not produce hydrogen gas. The base neutralises the acid.</p>

  <h3>Laboratory Preparation</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Place a suitable dilute acid in a container and warm it gently if instructed.</li>
    <li>Add the insoluble base in small portions while stirring.</li>
    <li>Continue until a little unreacted base remains, indicating that the acid has been used up.</li>
    <li>Filter the mixture to remove the excess solid base.</li>
    <li>Gently heat the filtrate to concentrate the salt solution to the crystallisation point.</li>
    <li>Allow it to cool so crystals form.</li>
    <li>Separate and dry the crystals.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Copper(II) Oxide and Sulfuric Acid</h3>
  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Write the balanced equation for the reaction.</p>
