  <h2>Exothermic Reactions</h2>

  <h3>1. Meaning of an Exothermic Reaction</h3>

  <p>
    An <strong>exothermic reaction</strong> is a chemical reaction that transfers energy
    from the reacting chemicals to the surroundings. The energy transferred is commonly
    released as heat, although some reactions also release energy as light or sound.
  </p>

  <p>
    The <strong>surroundings</strong> are everything outside the reacting system.
    For example, if chemicals react inside a test tube, the chemicals are the system,
    while the test tube, thermometer and surrounding air form part of the surroundings.
  </p>

  <p>
    When an exothermic reaction transfers heat to its surroundings, the surroundings
    usually become warmer. If the reaction takes place in a solution, a thermometer
    will often show an increase in temperature, provided that the temperature change
    caused by the reaction is greater than any heat lost to the environment.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Where Does the Released Energy Come From?</h3>

  <p>
    Chemical substances contain energy associated with the arrangement of their particles
    and the bonds between their atoms. During a chemical reaction, bonds in the reactants
    are broken and new bonds form in the products.
  </p>

  <p>
    Breaking chemical bonds requires energy. Forming chemical bonds releases energy.
    In an exothermic reaction, the energy released when new bonds form is greater than
    the energy required to break the bonds in the reactants. The difference is transferred
    to the surroundings.
  </p>

  <p>
    This is why an exothermic reaction releases energy overall, even though energy must
    first be supplied to break bonds.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Energy Change of an Exothermic Reaction</h3>

  <p>
    The products of an exothermic reaction have less chemical energy than the reactants.
    The difference in energy is released to the surroundings.
  </p>

  <p>
    The energy change can be represented as:
  </p>

  <p><strong>Energy change = Energy of products − Energy of reactants</strong></p>

  <p>
    For an exothermic reaction, the energy of the products is lower than the energy of
    the reactants. Therefore, the energy change is negative.
  </p>

  <p>
    Using the symbol ΔH for enthalpy change:
    <strong>ΔH is negative (ΔH &lt; 0)</strong> for an exothermic reaction.
    The negative sign indicates that energy has been released by the reacting system.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Examples of Exothermic Reactions</h3>

  <p><strong>Combustion:</strong> Burning a fuel releases energy to the surroundings.</p>

  <p><strong>Neutralisation:</strong> When a suitable acid reacts with a suitable alkali,
  energy is released and the temperature of the mixture commonly rises.</p>

  <p><strong>Respiration:</strong> Cells release energy through chemical reactions that
  break down glucose. The released energy supports cellular activities.</p>

  <h3>5. Worked Examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: Identifying a reaction</strong></p>

  <p>
    A fuel burns and releases heat. Explain why the combustion reaction is classified
    as exothermic.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the direction of energy transfer. Heat moves from
  the reacting system to the surroundings.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Apply the definition. A reaction that transfers energy
  to the surroundings is exothermic.</p>

  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> The combustion reaction is exothermic because it
  releases energy to the surroundings.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: Interpreting a temperature change</strong></p>

  <p>
    Two solutions are mixed in an insulated container. Their temperature rises from
    22°C to 29°C. What does this observation suggest about the reaction?
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Calculate the temperature change.</p>

  <p>Temperature change = Final temperature − Initial temperature</p>

  <p>= 29°C − 22°C = 7°C</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Interpret the positive temperature change. The mixture
  became warmer, indicating that energy was transferred to the surroundings.</p>

  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> The result supports the conclusion that the reaction
  is exothermic, assuming the rise was caused by the reaction.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: Calculating an energy change</strong></p>

  <p>
    The reactants in a reaction have an energy of 250 kJ, while the products have
    an energy of 180 kJ. Calculate the energy change and classify the reaction.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Use the formula:</p>

  <p>Energy change = Energy of products − Energy of reactants</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Substitute the values.</p>

  <p>Energy change = 180 kJ − 250 kJ = −70 kJ</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Interpret the sign. The energy change is negative,
  meaning that the products have less energy than the reactants.</p>

  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> The reaction is exothermic and releases 70 kJ
  of energy per stated reaction event or amount of reaction represented by the data.</p>

  <h3>6. Common Misunderstandings</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>An exothermic reaction releases energy; it does not absorb energy overall.</li>
    <li>The reaction mixture often warms up, but heat loss to the environment can affect the measured temperature.</li>
    <li>Energy is needed to break bonds. The reaction is exothermic because bond formation releases more energy than bond breaking requires.</li>
    <li>A negative energy change indicates that the products have less energy than the reactants.</li>
  </ul>
