  <h2>Endothermic Reactions</h2>

  <h3>1. Meaning of an Endothermic Reaction</h3>

  <p>
    An <strong>endothermic reaction</strong> is a chemical reaction that absorbs energy
    from its surroundings. The absorbed energy is commonly transferred as heat.
  </p>

  <p>
    The reacting chemicals are called the system. Everything outside the reacting system
    is part of the surroundings. In an endothermic reaction, energy moves from the
    surroundings into the reacting system.
  </p>

  <p>
    When the reaction absorbs heat from a solution or its surroundings, the measured
    temperature often falls. The surroundings lose energy as the reacting system takes
    it in. However, the observed temperature change depends on the experimental setup
    and whether energy enters from other sources.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Why Does an Endothermic Reaction Absorb Energy?</h3>

  <p>
    During a chemical reaction, bonds in the reactants break and new bonds form in
    the products. Breaking bonds requires energy, while forming bonds releases energy.
  </p>

  <p>
    In an endothermic reaction, the energy required to break bonds is greater than the
    energy released when new bonds form. The difference is absorbed from the surroundings.
  </p>

  <p>
    This does not mean that no energy is released during the reaction. Some energy is
    released when product bonds form, but it is less than the energy required overall.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Energy Change in an Endothermic Reaction</h3>

  <p>
    The products of an endothermic reaction have more energy than the reactants.
    The reacting system absorbs the difference from its surroundings.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Energy change = Energy of products − Energy of reactants</strong></p>

  <p>
    Since the products have more energy, the energy change is positive.
    In enthalpy notation, <strong>ΔH &gt; 0</strong> for an endothermic reaction.
  </p>

  <p>
    A positive energy change indicates that energy has been absorbed by the reacting
    system. It does not mean that the reaction necessarily occurs quickly or slowly.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Examples of Endothermic Reactions</h3>

  <p>
    <strong>Thermal decomposition:</strong> Some compounds absorb heat as they break down
    into simpler substances. For example, calcium carbonate decomposes when heated:
  </p>

  <p>CaCO₃(s) → CaO(s) + CO₂(g)</p>

  <p>
    Heat must be supplied to maintain this decomposition under suitable conditions.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Photosynthesis:</strong> Plants absorb light energy and use it to drive
    the chemical process of making glucose from carbon dioxide and water.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Some dissolving processes:</strong> Certain substances absorb heat from their
    surroundings when they dissolve in water. This is an endothermic dissolving process;
    not every substance dissolves endothermically.
  </p>

  <h3>5. Worked Examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: Identifying energy transfer</strong></p>

  <p>
    A reaction absorbs 85 kJ of energy from its surroundings. Classify the reaction
    and explain your decision.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the direction of transfer. Energy moves from
  the surroundings into the reacting system.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Apply the definition. A reaction that absorbs energy from
  the surroundings is endothermic.</p>

  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> The reaction is endothermic because it absorbs
  85 kJ of energy.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: Interpreting a temperature decrease</strong></p>

  <p>
    A mixture cools from 27°C to 19°C during a reaction. Calculate the temperature
    change and explain what it suggests.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Use temperature change = final temperature − initial temperature.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Substitute the values.</p>

  <p>Temperature change = 19°C − 27°C = −8°C</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Interpret the result. The mixture''s temperature decreased
  by 8°C, suggesting that energy was absorbed from the surroundings.</p>

  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> The observation supports an endothermic classification,
  assuming the reaction caused the cooling.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: Calculating energy change</strong></p>

  <p>
    The reactants have an energy of 150 kJ and the products have an energy of 225 kJ.
    Calculate the energy change and classify the reaction.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Write the formula.</p>

  <p>Energy change = Energy of products − Energy of reactants</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Substitute the values.</p>

  <p>Energy change = 225 kJ − 150 kJ = +75 kJ</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Interpret the positive sign. The products contain 75 kJ
  more energy than the reactants.</p>

  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> The reaction is endothermic and absorbs 75 kJ of
  energy per stated reaction event or amount of reaction represented by the data.</p>

  <h3>6. Common Misunderstandings</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>An endothermic reaction absorbs energy from the surroundings.</li>
    <li>A temperature decrease can indicate an endothermic process, but other causes of cooling must be ruled out.</li>
    <li>Energy is still released when product bonds form. The reaction is endothermic because the energy required to break bonds is greater overall.</li>
    <li>A positive energy change means the products have more energy than the reactants.</li>
  </ul>
