  <h2>Energy Profile Diagrams</h2>

  <h3>1. What Is an Energy Profile Diagram?</h3>

  <p>
    An <strong>energy profile diagram</strong> is a graph showing how the energy of a
    reacting system changes as a reaction progresses from reactants to products.
    It helps us compare the energy levels of the reactants and products and identify
    the energy barrier that must be overcome during the reaction.
  </p>

  <p>
    The horizontal axis represents the <strong>progress of the reaction</strong>.
    It does not usually represent time. The vertical axis represents the energy of
    the reacting system.
  </p>

  <p>
    The curve usually rises from the reactants to a maximum and then falls towards
    the products. The maximum represents the high-energy state associated with the
    reaction pathway. The energy difference between the reactants and this maximum
    is the activation energy for the forward reaction.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Energy Profile for an Exothermic Reaction</h3>

  <p>
    In an exothermic reaction, the products have less energy than the reactants.
    The curve therefore ends below the starting energy level.
  </p>

  <p>
    The vertical difference between the reactants and products represents the overall
    energy change. Since the products have less energy, the energy change is negative.
  </p>

  <p>
    For example, if the reactants have an energy of 300 kJ and the products have
    an energy of 180 kJ:
  </p>

  <p>Energy change = 180 kJ − 300 kJ = −120 kJ</p>

  <p>
    The reaction releases 120 kJ per stated reaction event or amount of reaction
    represented by the data.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Energy Profile for an Endothermic Reaction</h3>

  <p>
    In an endothermic reaction, the products have more energy than the reactants.
    The curve therefore ends above the starting energy level.
  </p>

  <p>
    For example, if the reactants have an energy of 140 kJ and the products have
    an energy of 215 kJ:
  </p>

  <p>Energy change = 215 kJ − 140 kJ = +75 kJ</p>

  <p>
    The reaction absorbs 75 kJ per stated reaction event or amount of reaction
    represented by the data.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Reading the Diagram Correctly</h3>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Reactants:</strong> the starting energy level.</li>
    <li><strong>Products:</strong> the ending energy level.</li>
    <li><strong>Peak:</strong> the highest point on the reaction pathway.</li>
    <li><strong>Activation energy:</strong> the energy difference from the reactants'' level to the peak.</li>
    <li><strong>Overall energy change:</strong> the energy of the products minus the energy of the reactants.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Do not confuse the activation energy with the overall energy change. Activation
    energy measures the energy barrier from the reactants to the peak. The overall
    energy change compares the products directly with the reactants.
  </p>

  <h3>5. Worked Examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: Classifying a diagram from energy levels</strong></p>

  <p>
    An energy profile begins at 260 kJ and ends at 170 kJ. Determine the type of
    reaction and calculate the overall energy change.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the reactants'' energy: 260 kJ.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Identify the products'' energy: 170 kJ.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Calculate the change.</p>

  <p>Energy change = Products − Reactants</p>

  <p>= 170 kJ − 260 kJ = −90 kJ</p>

  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> The products are lower in energy, so the reaction
  is exothermic and the energy change is −90 kJ.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: Interpreting an endothermic diagram</strong></p>

  <p>
    A diagram begins at 125 kJ and ends at 205 kJ. Explain the type of reaction
    and calculate the energy change.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Subtract the reactants'' energy from the products'' energy.</p>

  <p>Energy change = 205 kJ − 125 kJ = +80 kJ</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Interpret the positive sign. The products have more
  energy than the reactants.</p>

  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> The reaction is endothermic and absorbs 80 kJ.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: Finding activation energy from a profile</strong></p>

  <p>
    The reactants have an energy of 90 kJ and the peak of the energy profile is
    245 kJ. Calculate the activation energy for the forward reaction.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the peak energy: 245 kJ.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Identify the reactants'' energy: 90 kJ.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Subtract the reactants'' energy from the peak energy.</p>

  <p>Activation energy = Peak energy − Reactants'' energy</p>

  <p>= 245 kJ − 90 kJ = 155 kJ</p>

  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> The activation energy for the forward reaction is
  155 kJ on the energy scale provided.</p>

  <h3>6. Common Misunderstandings</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>The horizontal axis usually shows reaction progress, not elapsed time.</li>
    <li>Exothermic profiles end below the reactants'' energy level.</li>
    <li>Endothermic profiles end above the reactants'' energy level.</li>
    <li>Activation energy is measured from the reactants'' energy level to the peak.</li>
    <li>The overall energy change is measured between the reactants and products, not between the reactants and the peak.</li>
  </ul>
