  <h2>Meaning of Rate of Reaction</h2>

  <h3>1. Definition of Rate of Reaction</h3>

  <p>
    The <strong>rate of reaction</strong> is the change in the amount of a reactant
    used up or product formed per unit time during a chemical reaction.
  </p>

  <p>
    A reaction with a high rate produces the observable change quickly. A reaction
    with a low rate produces the change more slowly. Rate describes how quickly a
    reaction proceeds, not how much product can eventually be formed.
  </p>

  <p>
    For example, magnesium reacting with dilute hydrochloric acid produces hydrogen
    gas. If the gas is produced rapidly, the reaction has a high rate. If the same
    amount of gas takes much longer to form, the reaction has a lower average rate.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Measuring the Rate of Reaction</h3>

  <p>
    The rate can be investigated by measuring a quantity that changes as the reaction
    proceeds. The measurement chosen depends on the reaction.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Volume of gas:</strong> measure the volume of gas produced over time.</li>
    <li><strong>Mass:</strong> measure the decrease in mass when a gas escapes from the reaction vessel.</li>
    <li><strong>Concentration:</strong> measure how the concentration of a reactant or product changes with time.</li>
    <li><strong>Time taken:</strong> measure how long a reaction takes to reach a clearly defined endpoint.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    For a reaction producing gas, a gas syringe can measure the volume collected.
    For a reaction that releases a gas from an open vessel, a balance can measure
    the decrease in mass.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Calculating Average Rate</h3>

  <p>
    If a measurable quantity increases as a product forms, the average rate can be
    calculated using:
  </p>

  <p><strong>Average rate = Change in quantity ÷ Time taken</strong></p>

  <p>
    For gas production:
  </p>

  <p><strong>Average rate of gas production = Volume of gas produced ÷ Time</strong></p>

  <p>
    If 60 cm³ of gas is produced in 20 seconds:
  </p>

  <p>Average rate = 60 cm³ ÷ 20 s = 3 cm³/s</p>

  <p>
    The unit depends on the quantity measured. Gas volume divided by time may be
    expressed in cm³/s. Mass divided by time may be expressed in g/s.
    Concentration divided by time may be expressed in mol dm⁻³ s⁻¹.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Average Rate and Instantaneous Rate</h3>

  <p>
    <strong>Average rate</strong> describes the change over a stated time interval.
    It is calculated by dividing the total change in the measured quantity by the
    time interval.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Instantaneous rate</strong> describes the rate at a particular moment.
    On a graph of quantity against time, it is represented by the gradient of the
    tangent to the curve at that moment.
  </p>

  <p>
    The rate often decreases as a reaction proceeds because reactants are used up.
    In many reactions, there are fewer effective collisions per second later in the
    reaction. Eventually, the measured quantity may stop changing because the reaction
    has finished or because the limiting reactant has been consumed.
  </p>

  <h3>5. Worked Examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: Calculating average gas-production rate</strong></p>

  <p>
    A reaction produces 90 cm³ of hydrogen gas in 30 seconds. Calculate its average
    rate of gas production.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Write the formula.</p>

  <p>Average rate = Volume of gas produced ÷ Time</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Substitute the values.</p>

  <p>Average rate = 90 cm³ ÷ 30 s</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Calculate.</p>

  <p>Average rate = 3 cm³/s</p>

  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> The average rate of gas production is 3 cm³/s.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: Comparing average rates</strong></p>

  <p>
    Reaction A produces 40 cm³ of gas in 10 seconds. Reaction B produces 72 cm³
    in 24 seconds. Calculate both average rates and identify which reaction has
    the higher average rate.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Calculate the rate for reaction A.</p>

  <p>Rate A = 40 cm³ ÷ 10 s = 4 cm³/s</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Calculate the rate for reaction B.</p>

  <p>Rate B = 72 cm³ ÷ 24 s = 3 cm³/s</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Compare the results.</p>

  <p>
    Reaction A produces an average of 4 cm³ each second, while reaction B produces
    an average of 3 cm³ each second.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> Reaction A has the higher average rate by 1 cm³/s.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: Calculating a rate from mass loss</strong></p>

  <p>
    A reaction vessel has a mass of 150.0 g before a reaction begins and 147.6 g
    after 40 seconds. A gas escapes during the reaction. Calculate the average
    rate of mass loss.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Calculate the decrease in mass.</p>

  <p>Mass lost = Initial mass − Final mass</p>

  <p>= 150.0 g − 147.6 g = 2.4 g</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Divide the mass lost by the time.</p>

  <p>Average rate of mass loss = 2.4 g ÷ 40 s</p>

  <p>= 0.06 g/s</p>

  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> The average rate of mass loss is 0.06 g/s.</p>

  <h3>6. Common Misunderstandings</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>A faster reaction does not necessarily produce a greater final amount of product.</li>
    <li>Always state the quantity measured and the time unit when reporting a rate.</li>
    <li>For average rate, divide the change in the measured quantity by the elapsed time.</li>
    <li>Mass loss can indicate that a gas has escaped, but it is not a direct measurement of gas volume.</li>
    <li>A rate calculated over an entire interval is an average rate, not necessarily the rate at every moment.</li>
  </ul>
