  <h2>Specific Heat Capacity</h2>

  <h3>1. Meaning of Specific Heat Capacity</h3>

  <p>
    <strong>Specific heat capacity</strong> is the amount of thermal energy required
    to raise the temperature of 1 kg of a substance by 1°C or 1 K, without a change
    of state.
  </p>

  <p>
    Different substances require different amounts of energy to produce the same
    temperature increase in equal masses. For example, water has a relatively high
    specific heat capacity, so a considerable amount of energy is required to raise
    the temperature of a given mass of water.
  </p>

  <p>
    Specific heat capacity is represented by the symbol <strong>c</strong>.
    Its SI unit is <strong>J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹</strong>. It can also be expressed as
    J kg⁻¹ °C⁻¹ when calculating temperature changes in degrees Celsius.
  </p>

  <h3>2. The Specific Heat Capacity Formula</h3>

  <p><strong>Q = mcΔT</strong></p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Q</strong> = thermal energy transferred, in joules (J).</li>
    <li><strong>m</strong> = mass of the substance, in kilograms (kg).</li>
    <li><strong>c</strong> = specific heat capacity, in J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹.</li>
    <li><strong>ΔT</strong> = temperature change, in °C or K.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    The temperature change is calculated using:
  </p>

  <p><strong>ΔT = Final temperature − Initial temperature</strong></p>

  <p>
    A temperature difference of 1°C has the same numerical size as a temperature
    difference of 1 K. Therefore, either can be used for ΔT, provided the units
    are handled consistently.
  </p>

  <h3>3. What the Formula Tells Us</h3>

  <p>
    For the same substance and the same temperature change, doubling the mass
    doubles the energy required. For the same mass and temperature change, a
    substance with a higher specific heat capacity requires more energy.
  </p>

  <p>
    If energy is supplied to a substance and no change of state occurs, its
    temperature generally rises. If thermal energy is removed, its temperature
    generally falls.
  </p>

  <p>
    The formula Q = mcΔT calculates the energy associated with the temperature
    change. It does not calculate latent heat during a change of state.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Worked Examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: Calculating thermal energy</strong></p>

  <p>
    Calculate the energy needed to raise the temperature of 2 kg of water by 5°C.
    Use c = 4,200 J kg⁻¹ °C⁻¹.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Write the formula.</p>
  <p>Q = mcΔT</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Identify the values.</p>
  <p>m = 2 kg, c = 4,200 J kg⁻¹ °C⁻¹, ΔT = 5°C.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Substitute.</p>
  <p>Q = 2 × 4,200 × 5</p>

  <p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Calculate.</p>
  <p>Q = 42,000 J = 42 kJ</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 42,000 J of thermal energy is required.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: Calculating specific heat capacity</strong></p>

  <p>
    A 0.5 kg metal block absorbs 3,000 J of energy and its temperature rises by
    20°C. Calculate its specific heat capacity.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Start with Q = mcΔT.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Rearrange to make c the subject.</p>
  <p>c = Q ÷ (mΔT)</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Substitute.</p>
  <p>c = 3,000 ÷ (0.5 × 20)</p>

  <p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Calculate.</p>
  <p>c = 3,000 ÷ 10 = 300 J kg⁻¹ °C⁻¹</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The metal''s specific heat capacity is
  300 J kg⁻¹ °C⁻¹.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: Finding the final temperature</strong></p>

  <p>
    A 2 kg substance with a specific heat capacity of 500 J kg⁻¹ °C⁻¹ absorbs
    10,000 J. Its initial temperature is 18°C. Calculate its final temperature,
    assuming no change of state or heat loss.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Rearrange Q = mcΔT.</p>
  <p>ΔT = Q ÷ (mc)</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Substitute.</p>
  <p>ΔT = 10,000 ÷ (2 × 500)</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Calculate the temperature increase.</p>
  <p>ΔT = 10,000 ÷ 1,000 = 10°C</p>

  <p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Add the temperature increase to the initial temperature.</p>
  <p>Final temperature = 18°C + 10°C = 28°C</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The final temperature is 28°C.</p>

  <h3>5. Common Errors</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Convert mass from grams to kilograms before using a specific heat capacity expressed per kilogram.</li>
    <li>Use the temperature difference, not just the final temperature.</li>
    <li>Rearrange the formula carefully when finding c, m or ΔT.</li>
    <li>Use Q = mcΔT for temperature changes without a change of state.</li>
    <li>In real experiments, some supplied energy may be lost to the surroundings or absorbed by the container.</li>
  </ul>
