  <h2>Electrolysis of Molten Compounds</h2>

  <h3>Why Molten Ionic Compounds Can Be Electrolysed</h3>

  <p>A molten ionic compound contains positive and negative ions that are free to move. When a direct electric current passes through the molten compound, the ions migrate towards oppositely charged electrodes and may gain or lose electrons.</p>

  <p>Positive ions move towards the negative cathode, where reduction occurs. Negative ions move towards the positive anode, where oxidation occurs.</p>

  <p>When a pure molten binary ionic compound is electrolysed using suitable inert electrodes, its positive ion is usually reduced to the corresponding element, while its negative ion is oxidised to the corresponding non-metal element.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Electrolysis of Molten Sodium Chloride</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> State the products and write the electrode half-equations for electrolysis of molten sodium chloride.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1: Identify the ions.</strong></p>

  <p>Molten sodium chloride contains Na⁺ and Cl⁻ ions.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2: Find the cathode product.</strong></p>

  <p>Na⁺ ions gain electrons:</p>

  <p>Na⁺ + e⁻ → Na</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3: Find the anode product.</strong></p>

  <p>Cl⁻ ions lose electrons:</p>

  <p>2Cl⁻ → Cl₂ + 2e⁻</p>

  <p><strong>Step 4: Balance the overall equation.</strong></p>

  <p>2NaCl(l) → 2Na(l) + Cl₂(g)</p>

  <p><strong>Products:</strong> Sodium metal at the cathode and chlorine gas at the anode.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Electrolysis of Molten Lead(II) Bromide</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Predict the products and write the half-equations for molten PbBr₂.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1: Identify the ions.</strong></p>

  <p>PbBr₂ contains Pb²⁺ and Br⁻ ions.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2: Reduction at the cathode.</strong></p>

  <p>Pb²⁺ ions need two electrons to form lead atoms:</p>

  <p>Pb²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Pb</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3: Oxidation at the anode.</strong></p>

  <p>Two bromide ions form one bromine molecule:</p>

  <p>2Br⁻ → Br₂ + 2e⁻</p>

  <p><strong>Step 4: Write the overall equation.</strong></p>

  <p>PbBr₂(l) → Pb(l) + Br₂(g)</p>

  <p><strong>Products:</strong> Lead at the cathode and bromine at the anode.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Electrolysis of Molten Magnesium Chloride</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Write the electrode half-equations and overall equation for molten MgCl₂.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1: Identify the ions.</strong></p>

  <p>MgCl₂ contains Mg²⁺ and Cl⁻ ions.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2: Reduction at the cathode.</strong></p>

  <p>Mg²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Mg</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3: Oxidation at the anode.</strong></p>

  <p>2Cl⁻ → Cl₂ + 2e⁻</p>

  <p><strong>Step 4: Combine the half-equations.</strong></p>

  <p>MgCl₂(l) → Mg(l) + Cl₂(g)</p>

  <p>The two half-equations involve two electrons each, so the electrons cancel when the equations are added.</p>

  <h3>Rules for Solving These Questions</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Identify the positive and negative ions in the molten compound.</li>
    <li>Send positive ions to the cathode and reduce them.</li>
    <li>Send negative ions to the anode and oxidise them.</li>
    <li>Balance both atoms and charge in each half-equation.</li>
    <li>Cancel electrons when combining the half-equations.</li>
  </ul>
