  <h2>Electrolysis of Copper(II) Sulphate</h2>

  <h3>Aqueous Copper(II) Sulphate with Inert Electrodes</h3>

  <p>Aqueous copper(II) sulphate contains Cu²⁺ and SO₄²⁻ ions, as well as species from water. When inert electrodes, such as suitable graphite electrodes, are used, the electrodes are not intended to take part in the chemical reaction.</p>

  <p>Under the usual school-laboratory conditions, copper is deposited at the cathode and oxygen is produced at the anode.</p>

  <h3>Reaction at the Cathode</h3>

  <p>Copper(II) ions move towards the negative cathode and gain two electrons:</p>

  <p><strong>Cu²⁺(aq) + 2e⁻ → Cu(s)</strong></p>

  <p>A reddish-brown deposit of copper forms on the cathode.</p>

  <h3>Reaction at the Anode</h3>

  <p>Hydroxide ions from the aqueous system can be oxidised to produce oxygen:</p>

  <p><strong>4OH⁻(aq) → O₂(g) + 2H₂O(l) + 4e⁻</strong></p>

  <p>Oxygen gas is released at the anode under these conditions. Sulphate ions generally remain in solution rather than being discharged.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Identifying the Cathode Product</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> What forms at the cathode when aqueous copper(II) sulphate is electrolysed using inert electrodes?</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Copper(II) sulphate provides Cu²⁺ ions.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Cu²⁺ ions move towards the negative cathode.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Each Cu²⁺ ion gains two electrons.</p>

  <p><strong>Half-equation:</strong></p>

  <p>Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Copper metal is deposited at the cathode.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Identifying the Anode Product</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> What gas is produced at the anode when aqueous copper(II) sulphate is electrolysed using inert electrodes?</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> The aqueous solution contains species from water.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Under the usual school-laboratory conditions, water-derived hydroxide ions are oxidised.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> The oxidation produces oxygen gas and electrons.</p>

  <p><strong>Half-equation:</strong></p>

  <p>4OH⁻ → O₂ + 2H₂O + 4e⁻</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Oxygen gas is produced at the anode.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Calculating the Change in Cathode Mass</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> During electrolysis, 0.10 mol of Cu²⁺ ions are completely reduced to copper. Calculate the mass of copper deposited. Use Cu = 63.5 g mol⁻¹.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1: Write the half-equation.</strong></p>

  <p>Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2: Find the mole ratio.</strong></p>

  <p>One mole of Cu²⁺ produces one mole of Cu.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3: Calculate the moles of copper formed.</strong></p>

  <p>Moles of Cu = 0.10 mol</p>

  <p><strong>Step 4: Calculate the mass.</strong></p>

  <p>Mass = moles × molar mass</p>

  <p>Mass = 0.10 × 63.5 = 6.35 g</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 6.35 g of copper is deposited, assuming complete reduction and no loss of the deposit.</p>

  <h3>Important Comparison</h3>

  <p>The products described above apply to aqueous copper(II) sulphate with inert electrodes. If copper electrodes are used instead, the copper anode can dissolve and supply Cu²⁺ ions. The anode reaction is then different.</p>
