  <h2>Chemical Cell Voltage</h2>

  <h3>1. Meaning of Cell Voltage</h3>

  <p>
    <strong>Cell voltage</strong> is the electrical potential difference
    between the terminals of a cell. It describes the energy transferred
    per unit charge between two points.
  </p>

  <p>
    Voltage is measured in volts (V). A volt is equivalent to one joule
    of energy transferred per coulomb of charge.
  </p>

  <p><strong>1 V = 1 J/C</strong></p>

  <h3>2. Formula Linking Voltage, Energy and Charge</h3>

  <p>
    When a quantity of charge transfers an amount of energy, the average
    energy transferred per unit charge is:
  </p>

  <p><strong>V = E / Q</strong></p>

  <p>Where:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>V = potential difference in volts (V)</li>
    <li>E = energy transferred in joules (J)</li>
    <li>Q = charge transferred in coulombs (C)</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Rearranging the formula gives:
  </p>

  <p><strong>E = V × Q</strong></p>
  <p><strong>Q = E / V</strong></p>

  <h3>3. Worked Example: Calculating Voltage</h3>

  <p>
    A cell transfers 12 J of energy when 3 C of charge passes through it.
    Calculate its voltage.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Write the formula.</p>
  <p>V = E / Q</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Substitute the values.</p>
  <p>V = 12 / 3</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Calculate.</p>
  <p>V = 4 V</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 4 V</p>

  <h3>4. Worked Example: Calculating Energy</h3>

  <p>
    A 2.0 V cell transfers 5 C of charge. Calculate the energy transferred.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Use E = V × Q.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Substitute the values.</p>
  <p>E = 2.0 × 5</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Calculate.</p>
  <p>E = 10 J</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 10 J</p>

  <h3>5. Worked Example: Calculating Charge</h3>

  <p>
    A cell transfers 18 J of energy at a potential difference of 3 V.
    Calculate the charge transferred.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Rearrange the formula.</p>
  <p>Q = E / V</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Substitute the values.</p>
  <p>Q = 18 / 3</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Calculate.</p>
  <p>Q = 6 C</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 6 C</p>

  <h3>6. Combining Cell Voltages</h3>

  <p>
    For ideal cells connected in series in the same direction, their
    voltages add. If cells are connected in opposition, their voltages
    subtract, assuming the opposing voltages are unequal.
  </p>

  <p>
    For example, two 1.5 V cells connected in series in the same direction
    provide a total voltage of:
  </p>

  <p><strong>V = 1.5 + 1.5 = 3.0 V</strong></p>

  <p>
    Two cells of 1.5 V and 1.0 V connected in opposition provide a net
    voltage of:
  </p>

  <p><strong>V = 1.5 − 1.0 = 0.5 V</strong></p>

  <p>
    These calculations describe ideal voltage combinations. The terminal
    voltage of a real cell under load can differ because of internal
    resistance and other losses.
  </p>
