  <h2>Introduction to Organic Chemistry</h2>

  <h3>1. Meaning of Organic Chemistry</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Organic chemistry</b> is the branch of chemistry that studies
    carbon compounds, especially hydrocarbons and compounds derived
    from hydrocarbons.
  </p>

  <p>
    Carbon forms a very large number of compounds because its atoms
    can form four covalent bonds and join to other carbon atoms to
    produce chains, branches and rings.
  </p>

  <h3>2. What Is an Organic Compound?</h3>

  <p>
    An organic compound is a carbon compound belonging to the broad
    group studied in organic chemistry. Many organic compounds contain
    hydrogen, and some also contain oxygen, nitrogen, sulfur or
    halogens such as chlorine.
  </p>

  <p>Examples include:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>Methane, CH<sub>4</sub> — a simple hydrocarbon.</li>
    <li>Ethanol, C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub>OH — contains carbon, hydrogen and oxygen.</li>
    <li>Ethanoic acid, CH<sub>3</sub>COOH — contains carbon, hydrogen and oxygen.</li>
    <li>Glucose, C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>12</sub>O<sub>6</sub> — contains carbon, hydrogen and oxygen.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>3. What Is a Hydrocarbon?</h3>

  <p>
    A <b>hydrocarbon</b> is a compound containing only carbon and
    hydrogen atoms. Methane, ethane and ethene are hydrocarbons.
    Ethanol is not a hydrocarbon because it also contains oxygen.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Important Exceptions at School Level</h3>

  <p>
    Although organic chemistry focuses on carbon compounds, not every
    carbon-containing substance is normally classified as organic.
    Carbon dioxide, CO<sub>2</sub>, carbon monoxide, CO, and carbonates
    such as calcium carbonate, CaCO<sub>3</sub>, are commonly studied
    as inorganic substances at this level.
  </p>

  <p>
    Therefore, the rule is not simply that every compound containing
    carbon must be organic. The type of compound matters.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Identify an Organic Compound</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Which of these is an organic compound: methane,
  CH<sub>4</sub>, or carbon dioxide, CO<sub>2</sub>?</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Recognise that methane is a simple carbon-hydrogen compound.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Recognise that carbon dioxide is conventionally classified as inorganic.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Methane is the organic compound.</p>
  <p><b>Reason:</b> Methane belongs to the hydrocarbons, while carbon dioxide is treated as inorganic at school level.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Distinguish a Hydrocarbon from an Organic Compound</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Ethanol has the formula C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub>OH.
  Is it a hydrocarbon?</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> A hydrocarbon must contain only carbon and hydrogen.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Ethanol contains oxygen, shown by the O in its formula.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Ethanol is not a hydrocarbon.</p>
  <p><b>Reason:</b> It is an organic compound, but it contains an element other than carbon and hydrogen.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Classify Glucose</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Glucose has the formula C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>12</sub>O<sub>6</sub>.
  Is it a hydrocarbon?</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Examine the elements present: carbon, hydrogen and oxygen.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Compare them with the definition of a hydrocarbon.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Glucose is not a hydrocarbon.</p>
  <p><b>Reason:</b> Its formula contains oxygen. Glucose is an organic compound containing more than two elements.</p>
