  <h2>Tetravalency of Carbon</h2>

  <h3>1. Meaning of Tetravalency</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Tetravalency</b> is the ability of an atom to form four covalent
    bonds. Carbon has four electrons in its outer shell. It commonly
    forms four covalent bonds by sharing electrons with other atoms.
  </p>

  <p>
    A covalent bond forms when atoms share a pair of electrons. In
    simple organic compounds, each shared pair forms one covalent bond.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Tetravalency in Methane</h3>

  <p>
    Methane has the molecular formula CH<sub>4</sub>. One carbon atom
    forms four single covalent bonds with four hydrogen atoms.
  </p>

  <p>
    Each hydrogen forms one covalent bond, while carbon forms four.
    This satisfies the usual bonding capacity of both elements in methane.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Tetravalency in Other Compounds</h3>

  <p>
    Carbon''s four bonds do not have to be four separate single bonds
    to four different atoms. For example, carbon dioxide contains two
    carbon-oxygen double bonds. Each double bond counts as two covalent
    bonds around carbon, giving carbon four bonds in total.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Count Bonds in Methane</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> How many covalent bonds are present in one methane molecule, CH4?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Methane contains one carbon atom and four hydrogen atoms.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Each hydrogen forms one single covalent bond with carbon.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Four C–H bonds are formed.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Four single covalent bonds.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Identify the Bonding Capacity</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> A carbon atom forms four single bonds with four hydrogen atoms. Name the property demonstrated.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Count the bonds formed by carbon: four.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> The ability to form four bonds is called tetravalency.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Tetravalency.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Count Bonds in Carbon Dioxide</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Carbon dioxide has the structure O=C=O. How many covalent bonds does carbon form?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Carbon forms one double bond with the first oxygen.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> It forms another double bond with the second oxygen.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Each double bond contains two shared pairs, so carbon forms four bonds in total.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Four covalent bonds.</p>
