  <h2>Catenation</h2>

  <h3>1. Meaning of Catenation</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Catenation</b> is the ability of atoms of the same element to
    bond to one another, forming chains, branches or rings.
  </p>

  <p>
    Carbon shows extensive catenation because carbon-carbon covalent
    bonds are sufficiently strong to allow many stable structures.
    Carbon can form long chains, branched chains and rings.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Types of Carbon Chains</h3>

  <ul>
    <li><b>Straight chain:</b> carbon atoms are connected in an unbranched sequence.</li>
    <li><b>Branched chain:</b> the main carbon chain has one or more side branches.</li>
    <li><b>Ring:</b> carbon atoms connect to form a closed chain.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>3. Why Catenation Matters</h3>

  <p>
    Carbon can form compounds containing two, three, four or many
    more carbon atoms. Different arrangements of the same number of
    carbon atoms can also produce different compounds.
  </p>

  <p>
    Catenation is not exclusive to carbon; some other elements also
    form bonds with themselves. Carbon is particularly important
    because of the extensive variety and stability of its structures.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Identify Catenation</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> A molecule contains a chain of five carbon atoms joined by C–C bonds. Which property allows this chain to form?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify the repeated atoms: carbon atoms.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> They are bonded to one another.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Catenation.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Compare a Chain and a Ring</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> A carbon structure is a closed loop rather than an open chain. Does this demonstrate catenation?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Check whether carbon atoms bond to other carbon atoms.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> In a carbon ring, the carbon atoms form a connected closed chain.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Yes. A carbon ring is an example of catenation.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Explain Carbon''s Variety</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Explain how catenation helps carbon form many different compounds.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Carbon atoms can bond to one another.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> They can form chains of different lengths, branches and rings.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> These structures can combine with different atoms and bonds.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Catenation allows carbon to form many different molecular structures, increasing the variety of carbon compounds.</p>
