  <h2>Alkanes</h2>

  <h3>1. Definition</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Alkanes</b> are saturated open-chain hydrocarbons containing
    only single bonds between carbon atoms.
  </p>

  <p>
    Because their carbon-carbon bonds are single bonds, alkanes contain
    the maximum number of hydrogen atoms possible for an open-chain
    hydrocarbon with that number of carbon atoms.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Names of the First Five Alkanes</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Methane — CH<sub>4</sub></li>
    <li>Ethane — C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>6</sub></li>
    <li>Propane — C<sub>3</sub>H<sub>8</sub></li>
    <li>Butane — C<sub>4</sub>H<sub>10</sub></li>
    <li>Pentane — C<sub>5</sub>H<sub>12</sub></li>
  </ul>

  <h3>3. Recognising an Alkane</h3>

  <p>
    To identify an alkane, check that the substance is a hydrocarbon
    and that its carbon framework is an open chain containing only
    single carbon-carbon bonds.
  </p>

  <p>
    The general formula for open-chain alkanes is
    C<sub>n</sub>H<sub>2n+2</sub>, where n is the number of carbon atoms.
    The formula is explored in detail on the dedicated formula screen.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Identify an Alkane</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Ethane contains two carbon atoms joined by a single bond. Which hydrocarbon family does it belong to?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Ethane contains carbon and hydrogen only.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Its carbon atoms are joined by a single bond.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Ethane belongs to the alkane family.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Name C3H8</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Name the alkane containing three carbon atoms.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> The prefix for three carbon atoms is prop-.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> The suffix for an alkane is -ane.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Propane, C3H8.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Compare Ethane and Ethene</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Explain why ethane is an alkane but ethene is an alkene.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Ethane has a single C–C bond.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Ethene has a C=C double bond.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Ethane is saturated and belongs to the alkanes; ethene is unsaturated and belongs to the alkenes.</p>
  ', 1),
  (876, 876, '
  <h2>Alkenes</h2>

  <h3>1. Definition</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Alkenes</b> are unsaturated hydrocarbons containing at least
    one carbon-carbon double bond, C=C. In this introductory section,
    the main examples are open-chain alkenes containing one double bond.
  </p>

  <h3>2. The Double Bond</h3>

  <p>
    In a carbon-carbon double bond, two pairs of electrons are shared
    between the carbon atoms. The double bond affects the number of
    hydrogen atoms that can be attached to the carbon framework.
  </p>

  <p>
    The simplest alkene is ethene, C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>4</sub>.
    It contains two carbon atoms joined by a double bond.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Names of Common Alkenes</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Ethene — C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>4</sub></li>
    <li>Propene — C<sub>3</sub>H<sub>6</sub></li>
    <li>Butene — C<sub>4</sub>H<sub>8</sub></li>
    <li>Pentene — C<sub>5</sub>H<sub>10</sub></li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    For compounds with four or more carbon atoms, the position of the
    double bond may need to be stated. For example, but-1-ene and
    but-2-ene have different double-bond positions.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Recognise an Alkene</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> A hydrocarbon contains a C=C bond. Which family does it belong to?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify the double bond.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> A hydrocarbon containing a carbon-carbon double bond belongs to the alkene family in this classification.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Alkene.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Name C2H4</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Name the two-carbon alkene.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Two carbon atoms use the prefix eth-.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> The suffix -ene indicates a carbon-carbon double bond.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Ethene, C2H4.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Explain Unsaturation</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Why is ethene unsaturated?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Ethene contains a C=C double bond.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> A double bond means the carbon framework does not have the maximum hydrogen count of the corresponding open-chain alkane.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Ethene is unsaturated because it contains a carbon-carbon double bond.</p>

  <p>
    <b>Scope note:</b> The formula CnH2n applies to open-chain alkenes
    with one double bond. A cyclic hydrocarbon may share that formula,
    so structure must be considered when identifying an unknown compound.
  </p>
  ', 1),
  (877, 877, '
  <h2>Alkynes</h2>

  <h3>1. Definition</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Alkynes</b> are unsaturated hydrocarbons containing at least
    one carbon-carbon triple bond, C≡C. The introductory examples
    here are open-chain alkynes containing one triple bond.
  </p>

  <h3>2. The Triple Bond</h3>

  <p>
    A carbon-carbon triple bond contains three shared pairs of electrons.
    In an alkyne, the triple bond changes how many hydrogen atoms can
    attach to the carbon framework compared with an alkane.
  </p>

  <p>
    The simplest alkyne is ethyne, C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>2</sub>.
    It contains two carbon atoms joined by a triple bond.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Names of Common Alkynes</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Ethyne — C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>2</sub></li>
    <li>Propyne — C<sub>3</sub>H<sub>4</sub></li>
    <li>Butyne — C<sub>4</sub>H<sub>6</sub></li>
    <li>Pentyne — C<sub>5</sub>H<sub>8</sub></li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    For longer chains, the triple-bond position may need to be stated,
    for example but-1-yne and but-2-yne.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Identify an Alkyne</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> A hydrocarbon contains a carbon-carbon triple bond. Identify its family.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify the C≡C bond.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Hydrocarbons with this bond belong to the alkyne family.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Alkyne.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Name C2H2</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Name the simplest alkyne.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> The molecule contains two carbon atoms, so use eth-.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> The suffix -yne identifies the triple-bond family.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Ethyne, C2H2.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Compare Ethene and Ethyne</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Distinguish between ethene and ethyne by their carbon-carbon bonds.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Ethene contains a C=C double bond.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Ethyne contains a C≡C triple bond.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Ethene is an alkene; ethyne is an alkyne.</p>

  <p>
    <b>Scope note:</b> CnH2n−2 applies to open-chain alkynes with one
    triple bond. A molecular formula alone may not identify a unique
    structure in every case.
  </p>
  ', 1),
  (878, 878, '
  <h2>Homologous Series</h2>

  <h3>1. Definition</h3>

  <p>
    A <b>homologous series</b> is a family of organic compounds that
    share the same functional group, have the same general formula
    for the family, and show a regular pattern in their molecular
    structures.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Main Characteristics</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Members have the same functional group, where applicable.</li>
    <li>Members follow the same general formula.</li>
    <li>Successive members differ by one CH<sub>2</sub> unit.</li>
    <li>Members have similar chemical properties.</li>
    <li>Physical properties generally change gradually as molecular size increases.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>3. Example: The Alkane Homologous Series</h3>

  <p>
    Methane, CH<sub>4</sub>; ethane, C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>6</sub>;
    propane, C<sub>3</sub>H<sub>8</sub>; and butane, C<sub>4</sub>H<sub>10</sub>
    belong to the alkane homologous series.
  </p>

  <p>
    Compare methane with ethane: moving from CH<sub>4</sub> to
    C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>6</sub> adds one carbon and two hydrogen atoms,
    equivalent to adding CH<sub>2</sub>. The same pattern occurs
    between successive members.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Identify Successive Members</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Are ethane, C2H6, and propane, C3H8, successive members of the alkane series?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Compare the carbon counts: 2 and 3.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Compare the hydrogen counts: 6 and 8.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> The difference is C1H2, or CH2.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Yes, they are successive members.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Find the Next Member</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> What is the next alkane after propane, C3H8?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Add one carbon atom.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Add two hydrogen atoms.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> The new formula is C4H10.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Butane, C4H10.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Explain Similar Chemical Properties</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Why do members of the alkane series have similar chemical properties?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> All members belong to the same family.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> They have the same general type of carbon-carbon and carbon-hydrogen bonding.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Their structures and bonding patterns follow the same family pattern, so their chemical properties are similar, though not necessarily identical in every reaction condition.</p>
  ', 1),
  (879, 879, '
  <h2>General Formula of Alkanes</h2>

  <h3>1. The Formula</h3>

  <p>
    The general formula for an open-chain alkane is:
  </p>

  <p><b>C<sub>n</sub>H<sub>2n+2</sub></b></p>

  <p>
    In this formula, <b>n</b> is the number of carbon atoms in the
    molecule. The expression 2n + 2 gives the number of hydrogen atoms.
  </p>

  <h3>2. How to Use the Formula</h3>

  <p>
    If the number of carbon atoms is known, substitute that value for
    n and calculate the number of hydrogen atoms.
  </p>

  <p>
    For example, if n = 3:
  </p>

  <p>H = 2n + 2</p>
  <p>H = 2(3) + 2</p>
  <p>H = 6 + 2 = 8</p>
  <p>The formula is C<sub>3</sub>H<sub>8</sub>.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Find the Formula for n = 4</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Find the molecular formula of an open-chain alkane containing four carbon atoms.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Write the general formula: CnH2n+2.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Substitute n = 4.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Calculate hydrogen: 2(4) + 2 = 10.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> C4H10, butane.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Find the Formula for n = 6</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Find the formula of an open-chain alkane containing six carbon atoms.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Substitute n = 6 into H = 2n + 2.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> H = 2(6) + 2.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> H = 12 + 2 = 14.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> C6H14, hexane.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Determine the Number of Carbon Atoms</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> An alkane has the formula C7H16. Verify that it fits the general formula.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify n = 7.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Calculate 2n + 2 = 2(7) + 2 = 16.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Compare the calculated hydrogen count with the given count.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> C7H16 fits the alkane formula because the calculated hydrogen count is 16.</p>

  <h3>3. Important Limitation</h3>

  <p>
    This formula applies to open-chain alkanes. Cyclic saturated
    hydrocarbons have a different general formula because forming a
    ring changes the hydrogen count.
  </p>
  ', 1),
  (880, 880, '
  <h2>General Formulae of Alkenes and Alkynes</h2>

  <h3>1. Formula for Open-Chain Alkenes</h3>

  <p>
    An open-chain alkene containing one carbon-carbon double bond
    follows the general formula:
  </p>

  <p><b>C<sub>n</sub>H<sub>2n</sub></b></p>

  <p>
    Here, n is the number of carbon atoms. The formula applies to
    open-chain alkenes with one double bond, not every possible
    compound with the same atom counts.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Formula for Open-Chain Alkynes</h3>

  <p>
    An open-chain alkyne containing one carbon-carbon triple bond
    follows the general formula:
  </p>

  <p><b>C<sub>n</sub>H<sub>2n-2</sub></b></p>

  <p>
    The minimum value of n for either an open-chain alkene or alkyne
    with a carbon-carbon multiple bond is 2, because the bond requires
    two carbon atoms.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Why the Formulae Differ</h3>

  <p>
    For the same number of carbon atoms, an open-chain alkane has
    formula CnH2n+2. Introducing one double bond reduces the hydrogen
    count by two, giving CnH2n. Introducing one triple bond reduces
    it by four relative to the corresponding alkane, giving CnH2n-2.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Find the Formula of an Alkene with Four Carbons</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Calculate the formula of an open-chain alkene with one double bond and four carbon atoms.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Use CnH2n.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Substitute n = 4.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> H = 2(4) = 8.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> C4H8.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Find the Formula of an Alkyne with Four Carbons</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Calculate the formula of an open-chain alkyne with one triple bond and four carbon atoms.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Use CnH2n-2.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Substitute n = 4.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> H = 2(4) − 2 = 6.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> C4H6.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Compare the Two Families</h3>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Compare the formulae of an open-chain alkene and an open-chain alkyne, each containing five carbon atoms and one multiple bond.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> For the alkene, H = 2(5) = 10.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> For the alkyne, H = 2(5) − 2 = 8.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> The alkene formula is C5H10; the alkyne formula is C5H8.</p>
  <p><b>Reason:</b> With the same number of carbon atoms, the alkyne has two fewer hydrogen atoms than the corresponding one-double-bond alkene.</p>

  <h3>4. Important Limitation</h3>

  <p>
    A general formula describes a family of structures. It does not
    always identify one unique compound. For example, C4H8 may
    represent an open-chain alkene or a cycloalkane. Structural
    information is needed to distinguish them.
  </p>
  ', 1),
  (881, 881, '<h2>Structural Formulae</h2>

  <h3>1. What is a structural formula?</h3>
  <p>A <strong>structural formula</strong> shows how atoms are connected in a molecule. It tells us which atoms are joined by covalent bonds and whether carbon atoms are connected by single, double or triple bonds.</p>

  <p>A molecular formula tells us the number of atoms of each element, but it does not necessarily show how those atoms are arranged.</p>

  <p>For example, the molecular formula C₂H₆ tells us that a molecule contains two carbon atoms and six hydrogen atoms. Its structural formula is CH₃–CH₃.</p>

  <h3>2. Applying carbon''s valency</h3>
  <p>Carbon normally forms four covalent bonds. Hydrogen forms one covalent bond. When drawing a structural formula, ensure that each carbon has four bonds in total, counting a double bond as two bonds and a triple bond as three.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Ethane</h3>
  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Write the structural formula of ethane, C₂H₆.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Place the two carbon atoms together: C–C.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Each carbon already has one bond to the other carbon.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Each carbon therefore needs three more bonds. Attach three hydrogen atoms to each carbon.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> CH₃–CH₃.</p>
  <p><strong>Check:</strong> There are two carbon atoms and six hydrogen atoms. Each carbon forms four bonds.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Propane</h3>
  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Write the structural formula of propane, C₃H₈.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Arrange three carbon atoms in a chain: C–C–C.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Each end carbon has one carbon-carbon bond, so each needs three hydrogen atoms.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> The middle carbon has two carbon-carbon bonds, so it needs two hydrogen atoms.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> CH₃–CH₂–CH₃.</p>
  <p><strong>Check:</strong> The hydrogen total is 3 + 2 + 3 = 8.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Propene</h3>
  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Write a structural formula for propene, C₃H₆, with the double bond at the end of the chain.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Arrange three carbon atoms in a chain.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Place a double bond between the first and second carbon atoms: C=C–C.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Complete each carbon''s four bonds with hydrogen atoms.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> CH₂=CH–CH₃.</p>
  <p><strong>Check:</strong> The hydrogen total is 2 + 1 + 3 = 6. The double bond must be shown because it is part of the structure.</p>

  <h3>Common mistake</h3>
  <p>Do not assume that a molecular formula alone always identifies a unique structure. Different structures can have the same molecular formula; these are called structural isomers.</p>', 1),
  (882, 882, '<h2>Condensed Structural Formulae</h2>

  <h3>1. Meaning</h3>
  <p>A <strong>condensed structural formula</strong> represents a molecule by grouping hydrogen atoms with the carbon atoms to which they are attached. It avoids drawing every individual C–H bond but keeps the order of the groups visible.</p>

  <p>For example, CH₃–CH₂–CH₂–CH₃ may be written as CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₃. Both represent straight-chain butane.</p>

  <h3>2. Understanding the groups</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>CH₃:</strong> one carbon attached to three hydrogen atoms.</li>
    <li><strong>CH₂:</strong> one carbon attached to two hydrogen atoms.</li>
    <li><strong>CH:</strong> one carbon attached to one hydrogen atom.</li>
    <li><strong>C:</strong> a carbon written without hydrogen in that group; its other bonds must be considered.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>The number of hydrogens in each group depends on the bonds that carbon makes to other atoms. Never assume that every carbon must be written as CH₃.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Propane</h3>
  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Write the condensed structural formula of propane.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Propane has three carbon atoms.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> The terminal carbons each have three hydrogen atoms.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> The middle carbon has two hydrogen atoms.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> CH₃CH₂CH₃.</p>
  <p><strong>Check:</strong> There are three carbons and eight hydrogens.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Counting atoms</h3>
  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Determine the molecular formula represented by CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₃.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Count the four carbon groups: four carbon atoms.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Add the hydrogen counts: 3 + 2 + 2 + 3 = 10.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> C₄H₁₀.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Pentane</h3>
  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Write the condensed structural formula of straight-chain pentane.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Arrange five carbon atoms in a chain.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Write CH₃ at both ends.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Write CH₂ for each of the three internal carbons.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂CH₃.</p>
  <p><strong>Check:</strong> The formula contains C₅H₁₂.</p>

  <h3>Important distinction</h3>
  <p>Brackets can show branches. For example, CH₃CH(CH₃)CH₃ represents a branched molecule: the CH₃ inside brackets is attached to the carbon immediately before the brackets.</p>', 1),
  (883, 883, '<h2>Naming Straight-Chain Alkanes</h2>

  <h3>1. What is a straight-chain alkane?</h3>
  <p>An alkane is a saturated hydrocarbon containing carbon-carbon single bonds. A straight-chain alkane has its carbon atoms connected in one continuous, unbranched chain.</p>

  <p>In basic nomenclature, its name combines a prefix indicating the number of carbon atoms with the ending <strong>-ane</strong>.</p>

  <h3>2. Carbon prefixes</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>1 carbon: meth-</li>
    <li>2 carbons: eth-</li>
    <li>3 carbons: prop-</li>
    <li>4 carbons: but-</li>
    <li>5 carbons: pent-</li>
    <li>6 carbons: hex-</li>
    <li>7 carbons: hept-</li>
    <li>8 carbons: oct-</li>
    <li>9 carbons: non-</li>
    <li>10 carbons: dec-</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Method</h3>
  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Count the carbon atoms in the continuous chain.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Select the corresponding prefix.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Add -ane because the compound is an alkane.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: CH₃CH₂CH₃</h3>
  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Count three carbon atoms.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Three carbons use the prefix prop-.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Add -ane.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Propane.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂CH₃</h3>
  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Count five carbon atoms.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Five carbons use pent-.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Add -ane.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Pentane.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂CH₂CH₃</h3>
  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Count six carbon atoms.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Six carbons use hex-.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Add -ane.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Hexane.</p>

  <h3>Check before naming</h3>
  <p>Make sure the structure is unbranched and contains only carbon-carbon single bonds. If the chain contains a double or triple bond, it belongs to a different naming pattern.</p>', 1),
  (884, 884, '<h2>Naming Branched-Chain Alkanes</h2>

  <h3>1. What is a branched-chain alkane?</h3>
  <p>A branched-chain alkane contains a main carbon chain with one or more carbon-containing groups attached to it. These attached groups are called <strong>alkyl substituents</strong>.</p>

  <p>A one-carbon substituent is methyl, –CH₃. A two-carbon substituent is ethyl, –CH₂CH₃.</p>

  <h3>2. Naming procedure</h3>
  <p><strong>Step 1: Find the longest continuous carbon chain.</strong> This determines the parent alkane name.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2: Number the parent chain.</strong> Start from the end that gives the substituent or substituents the lowest possible locants.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3: Identify each substituent.</strong> For example, –CH₃ is methyl.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 4: Record each substituent''s position.</strong> The number identifies the parent-chain carbon to which it is attached.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 5: Combine the name.</strong> Write the position number, substituent name and parent alkane name.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: CH₃CH(CH₃)CH₃</h3>
  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> The longest continuous chain has three carbons: propane.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> The additional CH₃ group is a methyl substituent.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> The methyl group is attached to carbon 2.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 2-methylpropane.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: CH₃CH(CH₃)CH₂CH₃</h3>
  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> The longest chain has four carbons: butane.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> There is one methyl substituent.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Number from the end nearest the branch. The branch is on carbon 2, not carbon 3.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 2-methylbutane.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: CH₃CH₂CH(CH₃)CH₂CH₃</h3>
  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> The longest chain contains five carbons: pentane.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> The branch is a methyl group.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Numbering from either end places the branch on carbon 3.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 3-methylpentane.</p>

  <h3>Common mistake</h3>
  <p>Do not choose the parent chain merely because it looks horizontal or straight on the page. Follow the longest continuous route through the carbon skeleton. Also, do not confuse the position of a branch with the total number of carbon atoms in the molecule.</p>', 1),
  (885, 885, '<h2>Naming Alkenes</h2>

  <h3>1. What is an alkene?</h3>
  <p>An alkene is an unsaturated hydrocarbon containing a carbon-carbon double bond. In naming an alkene, the parent chain must include the double bond.</p>

  <p>The ending <strong>-ene</strong> indicates a carbon-carbon double bond. The position number identifies the first carbon of that double bond in the numbered parent chain.</p>

  <h3>2. Naming procedure</h3>
  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Find the longest continuous carbon chain that contains the double bond.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Number the chain from the end nearest the double bond.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Identify the position where the double bond begins.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Use the carbon prefix and the ending -ene.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: CH₂=CH₂</h3>
  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> There are two carbon atoms, so use eth-.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> There is a carbon-carbon double bond, so use -ene.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Ethene. A position number is unnecessary because only one position is possible.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: CH₂=CHCH₂CH₃</h3>
  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> The chain has four carbon atoms, so use but-.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Number from the end nearest the double bond.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> The double bond begins at carbon 1.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> But-1-ene.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: CH₃CH=CHCH₃</h3>
  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> The chain contains four carbon atoms.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> The double bond lies between carbons 2 and 3.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Numbering from either end gives locant 2.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> But-2-ene.</p>

  <h3>Important check</h3>
  <p>The position number refers to the first carbon involved in the double bond. A four-carbon chain with a double bond beginning at carbon 1 is but-1-ene; one beginning at carbon 2 is but-2-ene.</p>', 1),
  (886, 886, '<h2>Naming Alkynes</h2>

  <h3>1. What is an alkyne?</h3>
  <p>An alkyne is an unsaturated hydrocarbon containing a carbon-carbon triple bond. The ending <strong>-yne</strong> indicates the presence of this bond.</p>

  <p>As with alkenes, the parent chain must contain the triple bond. The chain is numbered from the end nearest the triple bond so that the bond receives the lowest possible locant.</p>

  <h3>2. Naming procedure</h3>
  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Find the longest continuous carbon chain containing the triple bond.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Number from the end nearest the triple bond.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Identify the first carbon in the triple bond.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Use the carbon prefix and the ending -yne.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: HC≡CH</h3>
  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> The molecule has two carbon atoms, so use eth-.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> It contains a triple bond, so use -yne.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Ethyne.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: HC≡CCH₂CH₃</h3>
  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Count four carbon atoms.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Number from the end nearest the triple bond.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> The triple bond begins at carbon 1.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> But-1-yne.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: CH₃C≡CCH₃</h3>
  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> The chain contains four carbon atoms.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> The triple bond lies between carbons 2 and 3.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Its locant is 2.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> But-2-yne.</p>

  <h3>Check the bond type</h3>
  <p>Do not confuse -ene with -yne. An alkene has a C=C double bond; an alkyne has a C≡C triple bond. The locant is based on the first carbon involved in the multiple bond.</p>', 1),
  (887, 887, '<h2>Structural Isomerism</h2>

  <h3>1. Definition</h3>
  <p><strong>Structural isomers</strong> are compounds with the same molecular formula but different arrangements of bonds between their atoms. This means the atoms are connected differently, even though the total number of each type of atom is unchanged.</p>

  <h3>2. Example: C₄H₁₀</h3>
  <p>Butane has the straight-chain structure:</p>
  <p><strong>CH₃–CH₂–CH₂–CH₃</strong></p>
  <p>2-methylpropane has a branched structure:</p>
  <p><strong>CH₃–CH(CH₃)–CH₃</strong></p>
  <p>Both have the molecular formula C₄H₁₀. However, their carbon skeletons differ, so they are structural isomers.</p>

  <h3>3. How to check for structural isomerism</h3>
  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Compare the molecular formulae. They must be the same.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Compare the structures. Check whether the atoms are connected differently.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> If the formulae match and the connectivity differs, the compounds are structural isomers.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Identifying isomers</h3>
  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Are butane and 2-methylpropane structural isomers?</p>
  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Butane has formula C₄H₁₀.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> 2-methylpropane also has formula C₄H₁₀.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Butane has an unbranched chain, while 2-methylpropane has a branched carbon skeleton.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Yes, they are structural isomers.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Counting C₅H₁₂ isomers</h3>
  <p><strong>Question:</strong> How many structural isomers does C₅H₁₂ have?</p>
  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Draw the straight chain: pentane.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Draw a four-carbon chain with one methyl branch: 2-methylbutane. A branch at carbon 3 is equivalent after reversing the chain.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Draw a three-carbon chain with two methyl groups on carbon 2: 2,2-dimethylpropane.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Three structural isomers.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Rejecting a false pair</h3>
  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Are C₃H₈ and C₄H₁₀ structural isomers?</p>
  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Compare the molecular formulae.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> C₃H₈ has three carbon atoms; C₄H₁₀ has four.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> No. Their molecular formulae are different.</p>

  <h3>Important distinction</h3>
  <p>Structural isomerism is not the same as geometric isomerism. Structural isomers have different atom connectivity; geometric isomers have the same connectivity but different spatial arrangements.</p>', 1),
  (888, 888, '<h2>Geometric Isomerism</h2>

  <h3>1. Meaning</h3>
  <p><strong>Geometric isomerism</strong> occurs when compounds have the same molecular formula and the same atom connectivity but different spatial arrangements of groups around a restricted bond.</p>

  <p>In the common school-level examples involving alkenes, the carbon-carbon double bond restricts rotation. This can allow two different arrangements to exist.</p>

  <h3>2. Conditions for cis-trans isomerism</h3>
  <p>For an alkene to show the common cis-trans form of geometric isomerism:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>There must be a carbon-carbon double bond that restricts rotation.</li>
    <li>Each carbon atom in the double bond must have two different groups attached.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>If either double-bonded carbon has two identical groups attached, the usual cis-trans distinction does not apply.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: But-2-ene</h3>
  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Why can but-2-ene show geometric isomerism?</p>
  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> But-2-ene contains a C=C double bond.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Rotation around this double bond is restricted.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Each double-bonded carbon is attached to a hydrogen atom and a methyl group.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 4:</strong> The groups can be arranged with the methyl groups on the same side or on opposite sides.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> But-2-ene can exist as cis-but-2-ene and trans-but-2-ene.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Cis and trans</h3>
  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Distinguish cis-but-2-ene from trans-but-2-ene.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the C=C bond.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> In cis-but-2-ene, the two methyl groups are on the same side of the double bond.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> In trans-but-2-ene, the two methyl groups are on opposite sides.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Cis means same side; trans means opposite sides, for this pair of isomers.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Butane</h3>
  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Does butane show cis-trans geometric isomerism?</p>
  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Butane contains only carbon-carbon single bonds.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Single bonds can rotate, so butane does not have the restricted C=C bond required for the usual cis-trans alkene isomerism.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> No, butane does not show cis-trans geometric isomerism.</p>

  <h3>Structural versus geometric isomerism</h3>
  <p>Structural isomers have different atom connectivity. Geometric isomers have the same connectivity but different spatial arrangements. In both cases, the molecular formula can be the same.</p>', 1),
  (889, 889, '
<h2>Alcohols</h2>

<h3>1. Meaning of an alcohol</h3>
<p>An <strong>alcohol</strong> is an organic compound containing a hydroxyl group, <strong>–OH</strong>, bonded to a saturated carbon atom. The –OH group is called the <strong>hydroxyl functional group</strong>.</p>

<p>A functional group is an atom or group of atoms responsible for characteristic chemical reactions of an organic compound. Identifying the functional group helps us classify a compound and predict some of its properties.</p>

<h3>2. General structure</h3>
<p>A simple alcohol can be represented as <strong>R–OH</strong>. Here, R represents the carbon-containing part of the molecule, while –OH is the hydroxyl group.</p>

<p>For example, in CH₃CH₂OH, the group CH₃CH₂– is the carbon-containing part and –OH is the functional group.</p>

<h3>3. Recognising alcohols</h3>
<ul>
<li>Methanol: CH₃OH — one carbon atom.</li>
<li>Ethanol: CH₃CH₂OH — two carbon atoms.</li>
<li>Propan-1-ol: CH₃CH₂CH₂OH — three carbon atoms.</li>
</ul>

<p>These compounds belong to the same homologous series because they contain the same functional group and have related chemical properties.</p>

<h3>4. Do not confuse alcohols with other compounds</h3>
<p>Not every compound containing oxygen is an alcohol. For example, ethanoic acid, CH₃COOH, contains a –COOH group and belongs to the carboxylic acid family. Its –OH is part of the carboxyl group, not an alcohol functional group.</p>

<h3>Worked example 1: Identify the functional group</h3>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which functional group identifies CH₃CH₂OH as an alcohol?</p>
<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Examine the formula CH₃CH₂OH.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Locate the oxygen and hydrogen written together as –OH.</p>
<p><strong>Step 3:</strong> This is the hydroxyl group.</p>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> The hydroxyl group, –OH.</p>

<h3>Worked example 2: Classify a compound</h3>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> Is CH₃CH₂CH₂OH an alcohol? Explain.</p>
<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Inspect the formula for a hydroxyl group.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> The formula ends in –OH.</p>
<p><strong>Step 3:</strong> The –OH group is attached to the three-carbon chain.</p>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Yes. It is propan-1-ol, an alcohol containing three carbon atoms.</p>

<h3>Worked example 3: Distinguish an alcohol from an acid</h3>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which is an alcohol: CH₃CH₂OH or CH₃COOH?</p>
<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> CH₃CH₂OH contains the hydroxyl group –OH attached to a carbon chain.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> CH₃COOH contains the carboxyl group –COOH.</p>
<p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Compounds containing –OH as the alcohol functional group are classified as alcohols, while compounds containing –COOH are carboxylic acids.</p>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> CH₃CH₂OH is the alcohol.</p>
', 1),
  (890, 890, '
<h2>Ethanol</h2>

<h3>1. What is ethanol?</h3>
<p><strong>Ethanol</strong> is an alcohol containing two carbon atoms. The prefix <strong>eth-</strong> indicates two carbon atoms, while the ending <strong>-ol</strong> identifies the alcohol family.</p>

<p>Its molecular formula is <strong>C₂H₆O</strong>. It is also commonly written as <strong>C₂H₅OH</strong>, which makes the hydroxyl group easier to recognise.</p>

<h3>2. Understanding the formula</h3>
<p>The condensed structural formula of ethanol is <strong>CH₃CH₂OH</strong>.</p>
<ul>
<li>CH₃ represents the first carbon group.</li>
<li>CH₂ represents the second carbon group.</li>
<li>OH is the hydroxyl functional group.</li>
</ul>

<p>The two carbon atoms are joined by a single covalent bond. The atoms share electrons, and each carbon forms four covalent bonds in total.</p>

<h3>3. Counting atoms</h3>
<p>In C₂H₅OH, there are two carbon atoms, five hydrogen atoms in C₂H₅, one additional hydrogen in OH, and one oxygen atom. Therefore, the total is C₂H₆O.</p>

<h3>Worked example 1: Convert the formula</h3>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> Write the molecular formula of ethanol from C₂H₅OH.</p>
<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Count carbon atoms: C₂ means 2.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Count hydrogen atoms: H₅ plus the H in OH gives 5 + 1 = 6.</p>
<p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Count oxygen atoms: there is 1 O.</p>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> C₂H₆O.</p>

<h3>Worked example 2: Calculate relative molecular mass</h3>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> Calculate the relative molecular mass of ethanol, C₂H₆O. Use C = 12, H = 1 and O = 16.</p>
<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Carbon contribution = 2 × 12 = 24.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Hydrogen contribution = 6 × 1 = 6.</p>
<p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Oxygen contribution = 1 × 16 = 16.</p>
<p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Add the contributions: 24 + 6 + 16 = 46.</p>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Relative molecular mass = 46.</p>

<h3>Worked example 3: Find the percentage by mass of oxygen</h3>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> Calculate the percentage by mass of oxygen in ethanol, C₂H₆O.</p>
<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Relative molecular mass = 46.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Mass contributed by oxygen = 16.</p>
<p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Percentage of oxygen = (16 ÷ 46) × 100.</p>
<p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Percentage of oxygen ≈ 34.8%.</p>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Approximately 34.8% oxygen by mass.</p>
', 1),
  (891, 891, '
<h2>Properties of Ethanol</h2>

<h3>1. Physical properties</h3>
<p>Ethanol is a colourless liquid at room temperature. It has a characteristic smell, evaporates relatively readily, mixes completely with water, and dissolves many substances.</p>

<p>A <strong>physical property</strong> can be observed or measured without changing the substance into a different chemical substance. Examples include colour, state, boiling point and miscibility.</p>

<h3>2. Volatility and miscibility</h3>
<p><strong>Volatility</strong> describes how readily a liquid changes into vapour. Ethanol is volatile, so it evaporates more readily than many less volatile liquids.</p>
<p><strong>Miscible</strong> means that two liquids mix completely to form one uniform liquid. Ethanol and water are miscible.</p>

<h3>3. Flammability and combustion</h3>
<p>Ethanol is flammable. When it burns completely in sufficient oxygen, it forms carbon dioxide and water and releases energy.</p>
<p><strong>C₂H₅OH + 3O₂ → 2CO₂ + 3H₂O</strong></p>

<p>To balance the equation, balance carbon first, then hydrogen, and finally oxygen. The equation has 2 carbon atoms and 6 hydrogen atoms on each side. It also has 7 oxygen atoms on each side.</p>

<h3>Worked example 1: Balance complete combustion</h3>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> Balance C₂H₅OH + O₂ → CO₂ + H₂O.</p>
<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> There are 2 carbon atoms in ethanol, so write 2CO₂.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> There are 6 hydrogen atoms, so write 3H₂O.</p>
<p><strong>Step 3:</strong> The products contain 4 + 3 = 7 oxygen atoms.</p>
<p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Ethanol supplies 1 oxygen atom, leaving 6 to be supplied by O₂.</p>
<p><strong>Step 5:</strong> Six oxygen atoms require 3O₂.</p>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> C₂H₅OH + 3O₂ → 2CO₂ + 3H₂O.</p>

<h3>Worked example 2: Distinguish physical and chemical properties</h3>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> Classify the statements “ethanol is colourless” and “ethanol burns in oxygen”.</p>
<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Colour can be observed without changing ethanol into another substance.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Burning causes ethanol to react chemically with oxygen.</p>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Colourlessness is a physical property; flammability is a chemical property.</p>

<h3>Worked example 3: Interpret the combustion equation</h3>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> What is the mole ratio of ethanol to carbon dioxide in complete combustion?</p>
<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Read the balanced equation: C₂H₅OH + 3O₂ → 2CO₂ + 3H₂O.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> The coefficient of ethanol is 1.</p>
<p><strong>Step 3:</strong> The coefficient of carbon dioxide is 2.</p>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> 1 mole of ethanol produces 2 moles of carbon dioxide under complete combustion.</p>
', 1),
  (892, 892, '
<h2>Fermentation</h2>

<h3>1. Meaning of fermentation</h3>
<p><strong>Alcoholic fermentation</strong> is the conversion of sugars such as glucose into ethanol and carbon dioxide by yeast under anaerobic conditions. Anaerobic means that oxygen is absent or very limited.</p>

<h3>2. Reactant and products</h3>
<p>The reactant is glucose. The main products are ethanol and carbon dioxide.</p>
<p><strong>Word equation:</strong> glucose → ethanol + carbon dioxide</p>
<p><strong>Balanced symbol equation:</strong> C₆H₁₂O₆ → 2C₂H₅OH + 2CO₂</p>

<h3>3. Conditions required</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Yeast:</strong> supplies enzymes that catalyse the conversion.</li>
<li><strong>Glucose:</strong> supplies the sugar used in the process.</li>
<li><strong>Suitable temperature:</strong> allows the enzymes to work effectively. Excessive heat can denature enzymes and kill yeast.</li>
<li><strong>Little or no oxygen:</strong> provides the anaerobic conditions required for alcoholic fermentation.</li>
</ul>

<h3>4. Balancing the fermentation equation</h3>
<p>One glucose molecule contains 6 carbon atoms, 12 hydrogen atoms and 6 oxygen atoms. Two ethanol molecules contain 4 carbon atoms, while two carbon dioxide molecules contain 2. The total remains 6 carbon atoms.</p>

<h3>Worked example 1: Write the word equation</h3>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> State the word equation for alcoholic fermentation.</p>
<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the sugar: glucose.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Identify the products: ethanol and carbon dioxide.</p>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Glucose → ethanol + carbon dioxide.</p>

<h3>Worked example 2: Balance the symbol equation</h3>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> Balance C₆H₁₂O₆ → C₂H₅OH + CO₂.</p>
<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Place 2 before ethanol, giving 4 carbon atoms and 12 hydrogen atoms.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Place 2 before CO₂ to supply the remaining 2 carbon atoms.</p>
<p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Check oxygen: the products contain 2 oxygen atoms in ethanol and 4 in carbon dioxide, making 6.</p>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> C₆H₁₂O₆ → 2C₂H₅OH + 2CO₂.</p>

<h3>Worked example 3: Calculate the mole ratio</h3>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> According to the balanced equation, how many moles of ethanol can form from 3 moles of glucose if conversion is complete?</p>
<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> The equation shows 1 mole of glucose produces 2 moles of ethanol.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Multiply both sides of the ratio by 3.</p>
<p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Ethanol formed = 3 × 2 = 6 moles.</p>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> 6 moles of ethanol.</p>
', 1),
  (893, 893, '
<h2>Oxidation of Ethanol</h2>

<h3>1. Meaning of oxidation in this reaction</h3>
<p>Ethanol can be oxidised to form <strong>ethanoic acid</strong>. Oxidation can involve the addition of oxygen, removal of hydrogen, or an increase in oxidation state. At this level, the important change is the conversion of an alcohol into a carboxylic acid.</p>

<h3>2. Overall change</h3>
<p><strong>Ethanol → ethanoic acid</strong></p>
<p>When oxygen is the oxidising reactant, the overall equation is:</p>
<p><strong>C₂H₅OH + O₂ → CH₃COOH + H₂O</strong></p>

<p>Check the atoms: both sides contain 2 carbon atoms, 6 hydrogen atoms and 3 oxygen atoms. The equation is balanced as written.</p>

<h3>3. Oxidation using an oxidising agent</h3>
<p>In a laboratory, ethanol can be oxidised using a suitable oxidising agent, such as acidified potassium dichromate(VI). Under appropriate conditions, ethanol is oxidised to ethanoic acid. If oxidation is incomplete or conditions are controlled differently, other products may form, so the conditions matter.</p>

<h3>4. Oxidation by microorganisms</h3>
<p>Ethanol can also be converted into ethanoic acid by microorganisms in the presence of oxygen. This is associated with the formation of vinegar from alcoholic liquids.</p>

<h3>Worked example 1: Identify the product</h3>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> What carboxylic acid forms when ethanol is oxidised fully under suitable conditions?</p>
<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Ethanol contains two carbon atoms.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Oxidation of ethanol can produce the corresponding two-carbon carboxylic acid.</p>
<p><strong>Step 3:</strong> The two-carbon carboxylic acid is ethanoic acid.</p>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Ethanoic acid, CH₃COOH.</p>

<h3>Worked example 2: Balance the equation</h3>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> Check whether C₂H₅OH + O₂ → CH₃COOH + H₂O is balanced.</p>
<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Carbon: 2 atoms on each side.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Hydrogen: 6 on the left; 4 in CH₃COOH plus 2 in H₂O gives 6 on the right.</p>
<p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Oxygen: 1 + 2 = 3 on the left and 2 + 1 = 3 on the right.</p>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> The equation is balanced as written.</p>

<h3>Worked example 3: Recognise the product family</h3>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> Classify CH₃COOH by its functional group.</p>
<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Locate the –COOH group.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> The –COOH group is the carboxyl group.</p>
<p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Compounds containing this group are carboxylic acids.</p>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> CH₃COOH is a carboxylic acid.</p>
', 1),
  (894, 894, '
<h2>Carboxylic Acids</h2>

<h3>1. Meaning of carboxylic acids</h3>
<p><strong>Carboxylic acids</strong> are organic compounds containing the <strong>carboxyl functional group, –COOH</strong>. The carboxyl group is responsible for their characteristic acidic behaviour.</p>

<h3>2. Structure of the carboxyl group</h3>
<p>The carboxyl group contains a carbon atom double-bonded to oxygen and single-bonded to a hydroxyl group:</p>
<p><strong>–C(=O)–OH</strong></p>
<p>The complete group is written as –COOH. Although it contains an –OH part, the entire –COOH group is treated as one functional group.</p>

<h3>3. General formula</h3>
<p>A simple carboxylic acid can be represented as <strong>R–COOH</strong>, where R is the carbon-containing part attached to the carboxyl group.</p>

<h3>4. Examples and naming</h3>
<ul>
<li>Methanoic acid: HCOOH — one carbon atom.</li>
<li>Ethanoic acid: CH₃COOH — two carbon atoms.</li>
<li>Propanoic acid: CH₃CH₂COOH — three carbon atoms.</li>
</ul>

<p>When counting the carbon atoms in a carboxylic acid, the carbon in the –COOH group is included in the total carbon count.</p>

<h3>5. Why are they acidic?</h3>
<p>In water, carboxylic acids can donate a hydrogen ion, H⁺, to water molecules. For example:</p>
<p><strong>CH₃COOH + H₂O ⇌ CH₃COO⁻ + H₃O⁺</strong></p>
<p>This is an equilibrium reaction. Ethanoic acid is a weak acid because it ionises only partially in water.</p>

<h3>Worked example 1: Identify the functional group</h3>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> Identify the functional group in CH₃COOH.</p>
<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Locate the COOH part of the formula.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Write the group as –COOH.</p>
<p><strong>Step 3:</strong> This is the carboxyl group.</p>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Carboxyl group, –COOH.</p>

<h3>Worked example 2: Name a carboxylic acid</h3>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> Name HCOOH.</p>
<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Count the carbon atoms, including the carbon in –COOH: there is 1.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> The one-carbon prefix is meth-.</p>
<p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Carboxylic acid names end in -oic acid.</p>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Methanoic acid.</p>

<h3>Worked example 3: Write the formula of propanoic acid</h3>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> Write the condensed structural formula of propanoic acid.</p>
<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> The prefix prop- means the molecule has three carbon atoms in total.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> One of the three carbons belongs to the –COOH group.</p>
<p><strong>Step 3:</strong> The remaining two carbons form CH₃CH₂–.</p>
<p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Attach –COOH.</p>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> CH₃CH₂COOH.</p>
', 1),
  (895, 895, '
<h2>Ethanoic Acid</h2>

<h3>1. Formula and structure</h3>
<p><strong>Ethanoic acid</strong> is a carboxylic acid containing two carbon atoms. Its molecular formula is <strong>C₂H₄O₂</strong>, and its condensed structural formula is <strong>CH₃COOH</strong>.</p>

<p>The formula CH₃COOH shows a methyl group, CH₃–, attached to a carboxyl group, –COOH. The carbon atom inside –COOH is included in the total of two carbon atoms.</p>

<h3>2. Physical properties</h3>
<ul>
<li>Pure ethanoic acid is a colourless liquid at ordinary room temperature.</li>
<li>It has a sharp, characteristic smell.</li>
<li>It mixes with water.</li>
<li>It is acidic and reacts with suitable bases, carbonates and reactive metals.</li>
</ul>

<p>Vinegar is a dilute aqueous solution of ethanoic acid. It is not pure ethanoic acid.</p>

<h3>3. Counting atoms in the molecular formula</h3>
<p>C₂H₄O₂ contains 2 carbon atoms, 4 hydrogen atoms and 2 oxygen atoms.</p>

<h3>Worked example 1: Calculate relative molecular mass</h3>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> Calculate the relative molecular mass of C₂H₄O₂. Use C = 12, H = 1 and O = 16.</p>
<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Carbon contribution = 2 × 12 = 24.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Hydrogen contribution = 4 × 1 = 4.</p>
<p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Oxygen contribution = 2 × 16 = 32.</p>
<p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Total = 24 + 4 + 32 = 60.</p>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Relative molecular mass = 60.</p>

<h3>Worked example 2: Find the percentage by mass of hydrogen</h3>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> Calculate the percentage by mass of hydrogen in ethanoic acid, C₂H₄O₂.</p>
<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Relative molecular mass = 60.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Hydrogen contributes 4 × 1 = 4.</p>
<p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Percentage of hydrogen = (4 ÷ 60) × 100.</p>
<p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Percentage of hydrogen ≈ 6.67%.</p>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Approximately 6.67% hydrogen by mass.</p>

<h3>Worked example 3: Identify the functional group</h3>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which functional group makes CH₃COOH a carboxylic acid?</p>
<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Examine the formula and identify the COOH part.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Write it as –COOH.</p>
<p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Name the group as the carboxyl group.</p>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> The carboxyl group, –COOH.</p>
', 1),
  (896, 896, '
<h2>Reactions of Ethanoic Acid</h2>

<h3>1. General behaviour</h3>
<p>Ethanoic acid shows the characteristic reactions of an acid. It reacts with sufficiently reactive metals, carbonates and alkalis. The products depend on the substance reacting with the acid.</p>

<h3>2. Reaction with reactive metals</h3>
<p>An acid reacts with a sufficiently reactive metal to produce a salt and hydrogen gas.</p>
<p><strong>2CH₃COOH + Mg → (CH₃COO)₂Mg + H₂</strong></p>
<p>The salt is magnesium ethanoate. Hydrogen gas may be identified by bringing a lighted splint near the collected gas; hydrogen burns with a squeaky pop. The test must be carried out using suitable laboratory safety procedures.</p>

<h3>3. Reaction with carbonates</h3>
<p>An acid reacts with a carbonate to form a salt, water and carbon dioxide.</p>
<p><strong>2CH₃COOH + Na₂CO₃ → 2CH₃COONa + H₂O + CO₂</strong></p>
<p>Carbon dioxide can be tested by bubbling it through limewater. The limewater turns milky because calcium carbonate forms.</p>

<h3>4. Reaction with alkalis</h3>
<p>Ethanoic acid reacts with sodium hydroxide in a neutralisation reaction. The products are sodium ethanoate and water.</p>
<p><strong>CH₃COOH + NaOH → CH₃COONa + H₂O</strong></p>

<h3>5. How to balance these equations</h3>
<p>Write the correct formulas first, then count each type of atom on both sides. Change coefficients in front of formulas, not the subscripts inside formulas. Changing a subscript changes the identity of a compound.</p>

<h3>Worked example 1: Reaction with sodium hydroxide</h3>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> Complete and balance CH₃COOH + NaOH →</p>
<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> An acid reacts with an alkali to form a salt and water.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> The salt formed from ethanoic acid and sodium hydroxide is sodium ethanoate, CH₃COONa.</p>
<p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Add water, H₂O, as the other product.</p>
<p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Check the atoms. They are equal on both sides.</p>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> CH₃COOH + NaOH → CH₃COONa + H₂O.</p>

<h3>Worked example 2: Balance reaction with sodium carbonate</h3>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> Balance CH₃COOH + Na₂CO₃ → CH₃COONa + H₂O + CO₂.</p>
<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Na₂CO₃ contains two sodium atoms, so put 2 before CH₃COONa.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Two sodium ethanoate units require two ethanoate groups, so put 2 before CH₃COOH.</p>
<p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Keep H₂O and CO₂ with coefficients of 1.</p>
<p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Check the atoms on both sides; all are balanced.</p>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> 2CH₃COOH + Na₂CO₃ → 2CH₃COONa + H₂O + CO₂.</p>

<h3>Worked example 3: Predict the products with magnesium</h3>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> Predict the products when ethanoic acid reacts with magnesium.</p>
<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> An acid reacting with a reactive metal produces a salt and hydrogen gas.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> The metal determines the metal ion in the salt. Magnesium forms Mg²⁺.</p>
<p><strong>Step 3:</strong> The ethanoate ion is CH₃COO⁻, so two ethanoate ions are required to balance one Mg²⁺ ion.</p>
<p><strong>Step 4:</strong> The salt formula is Mg(CH₃COO)₂.</p>
<p><strong>Step 5:</strong> Balance the equation.</p>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> 2CH₃COOH + Mg → Mg(CH₃COO)₂ + H₂.</p>
', 1),
  (897, 897, '<h2>Addition Reactions of Alkenes</h2>

<h3>1. What is an addition reaction?</h3>
<p>An addition reaction is a chemical reaction in which atoms or groups of atoms combine with an unsaturated molecule to form one main product.</p>

<p>Alkenes undergo addition reactions because they contain a carbon-carbon double bond, C=C. The double bond allows atoms to add across the two carbon atoms.</p>

<p>During an addition reaction, the carbon-carbon double bond becomes a single bond. Each of the two carbon atoms forms a new bond with an added atom or group.</p>

<h3>2. Hydrogenation</h3>
<p>Hydrogenation is the addition of hydrogen to an unsaturated compound. When an alkene reacts with hydrogen, the alkene is converted into an alkane.</p>

<p>The reaction normally uses a nickel catalyst and heating.</p>

<p><strong>Ethene + hydrogen → ethane</strong></p>
<p><strong>C₂H₄ + H₂ → C₂H₆</strong></p>

<p>Before the reaction, ethene contains a C=C double bond. After hydrogenation, ethane contains a C–C single bond. The product is saturated because it no longer contains the carbon-carbon double bond.</p>

<h3>3. Addition of bromine</h3>
<p>Bromine also adds across the double bond of an alkene. Each carbon atom in the original double bond becomes bonded to one bromine atom.</p>

<p><strong>Ethene + bromine → 1,2-dibromoethane</strong></p>
<p><strong>C₂H₄ + Br₂ → CH₂BrCH₂Br</strong></p>

<p>The product is called 1,2-dibromoethane because it has two carbon atoms and bromine atoms attached to carbon numbers 1 and 2.</p>

<h3>4. Testing for unsaturation</h3>
<p>Bromine water is orange-brown. When shaken with an alkene, it is decolourised as bromine reacts with the carbon-carbon double bond. This is a test for unsaturation.</p>

<p>An alkane does not normally decolourise bromine water under these test conditions because it does not have a carbon-carbon double bond.</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1: Hydrogenation</h3>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> Write the equation for the reaction between propene and hydrogen.</p>
<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Propene is an alkene with formula C₃H₆.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Hydrogen is H₂.</p>
<p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Hydrogen adds across the double bond, producing propane, C₃H₈.</p>
<p><strong>Equation:</strong> C₃H₆ + H₂ → C₃H₈.</p>
<p><strong>Check:</strong> There are 3 carbon atoms and 8 hydrogen atoms on each side.</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2: Bromine addition</h3>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> What is formed when propene reacts with bromine?</p>
<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Propene has the structure CH₂=CHCH₃.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> The double bond is between carbon atoms 1 and 2.</p>
<p><strong>Step 3:</strong> One bromine atom adds to each of these carbon atoms.</p>
<p><strong>Product:</strong> CH₂Br–CHBr–CH₃, called 1,2-dibromopropane.</p>
<p><strong>Equation:</strong> C₃H₆ + Br₂ → C₃H₆Br₂.</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3: Identifying an alkene</h3>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> A gas turns bromine water from orange-brown to colourless. What does this observation suggest?</p>
<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Bromine reacts by addition with carbon-carbon double bonds.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> The loss of the orange-brown colour shows that bromine has reacted.</p>
<p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> The gas may contain an alkene and is therefore unsaturated.</p>
<p><strong>Important:</strong> This test is evidence of unsaturation under the test conditions; it does not by itself identify the exact compound.</p>', 1),
  (898, 898, '<h2>Ester Formation</h2>

<h3>1. What is an ester?</h3>
<p>An ester is an organic compound containing the functional group –COO– between carbon-containing groups. Many esters have distinctive, often fruity smells and are used in flavourings, fragrances and industrial products.</p>

<h3>2. What is esterification?</h3>
<p>Esterification is the reaction between an alcohol and a carboxylic acid to form an ester and water.</p>

<p><strong>Alcohol + carboxylic acid ⇌ ester + water</strong></p>

<p>The reaction is commonly carried out by heating the reactants with a few drops of concentrated sulfuric acid. The acid provides an acidic catalyst and can also help remove water.</p>

<h3>3. Naming an ester</h3>
<p>The first part of an ester name comes from the alcohol. The second part comes from the carboxylic acid.</p>

<ul>
<li>Ethanol gives the group name ethyl.</li>
<li>Ethanoic acid gives the name ethanoate.</li>
<li>The ester formed from ethanol and ethanoic acid is ethyl ethanoate.</li>
</ul>

<h3>4. Example: Ethyl ethanoate</h3>
<p><strong>Ethanoic acid + ethanol ⇌ ethyl ethanoate + water</strong></p>
<p><strong>CH₃COOH + C₂H₅OH ⇌ CH₃COOC₂H₅ + H₂O</strong></p>

<p>During the reaction, the –OH part of the carboxylic acid and a hydrogen from the alcohol combine to form water. The remaining parts join to form the ester. This is a useful simplified way to understand the overall change.</p>

<h3>5. Why is the reaction reversible?</h3>
<p>The products can react under suitable conditions to reform the alcohol and carboxylic acid. The double arrow indicates that the reaction can proceed in both directions.</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1: Predicting an ester</h3>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> What ester forms when methanol reacts with ethanoic acid?</p>
<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Methanol supplies the methyl part of the ester name.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Ethanoic acid supplies the ethanoate part.</p>
<p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Combine the names.</p>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Methyl ethanoate.</p>
<p><strong>Equation:</strong> CH₃COOH + CH₃OH ⇌ CH₃COOCH₃ + H₂O.</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2: Writing an esterification equation</h3>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> Write the equation for propanoic acid reacting with ethanol.</p>
<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Propanoic acid is CH₃CH₂COOH.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Ethanol is C₂H₅OH.</p>
<p><strong>Step 3:</strong> The ester is ethyl propanoate, CH₃CH₂COOC₂H₅.</p>
<p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Water is also formed.</p>
<p><strong>Equation:</strong> CH₃CH₂COOH + C₂H₅OH ⇌ CH₃CH₂COOC₂H₅ + H₂O.</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3: Identifying reactants</h3>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> An ester is called propyl methanoate. Which alcohol and carboxylic acid are needed to prepare it?</p>
<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> The first name, propyl, identifies the alcohol-derived group.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> The alcohol is propanol.</p>
<p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Methanoate identifies the acid-derived part.</p>
<p><strong>Step 4:</strong> The carboxylic acid is methanoic acid.</p>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Propanol and methanoic acid.</p>', 1),
  (899, 899, '<h2>Addition Polymerisation</h2>

<h3>1. Monomers and polymers</h3>
<p>A <strong>monomer</strong> is a small molecule that can join with many similar molecules to form a larger molecule.</p>
<p>A <strong>polymer</strong> is a very large molecule made from many repeating units joined by covalent bonds.</p>
<p>In addition polymerisation, many unsaturated monomers, usually alkenes, join together without eliminating a small molecule such as water.</p>

<h3>2. How addition polymerisation occurs</h3>
<p>An alkene contains a carbon-carbon double bond. During polymerisation, the double bond opens sufficiently for each monomer to form new carbon-carbon single bonds with neighbouring units.</p>
<p>Repeated joining creates a long chain. The atoms of the monomer are incorporated into the polymer chain.</p>

<h3>3. Poly(ethene)</h3>
<p>Ethene, CH₂=CH₂, is the monomer used to make poly(ethene).</p>

<p><strong>nCH₂=CH₂ → [–CH₂–CH₂–]ₙ</strong></p>

<p>The symbol <strong>n</strong> represents a large number of ethene molecules. The section [–CH₂–CH₂–] is the repeating unit.</p>

<p>Ethene is the monomer. Poly(ethene) is the polymer. The repeating unit contains two carbon atoms and four hydrogen atoms.</p>

<h3>4. Poly(propene)</h3>
<p>Propene, CH₂=CHCH₃, also undergoes addition polymerisation.</p>
<p><strong>nCH₂=CHCH₃ → [–CH₂–CH(CH₃)–]ₙ</strong></p>
<p>The methyl group, –CH₃, remains attached to every other carbon in the polymer backbone as shown by the repeating unit.</p>

<h3>5. Why addition polymerisation is important</h3>
<p>Polymers are used to make packaging, containers, pipes, household products, fibres and many other materials. Their properties depend on the monomer, the polymer structure and the manufacturing process.</p>

<p>Many common addition polymers are chemically resistant and durable. However, some plastic waste persists in the environment, so reuse, appropriate recycling and responsible disposal are important.</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1: Identifying a monomer</h3>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which monomer forms poly(ethene)?</p>
<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> The polymer name contains the name ethene.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> The monomer is ethene, CH₂=CH₂.</p>
<p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Its double bond opens during addition polymerisation.</p>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Ethene.</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2: Finding a repeating unit</h3>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> Write the repeating unit of poly(propene).</p>
<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Propene is CH₂=CHCH₃.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> The two double-bonded carbon atoms form the polymer backbone.</p>
<p><strong>Step 3:</strong> The methyl group remains attached to one of those backbone carbon atoms.</p>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> [–CH₂–CH(CH₃)–]ₙ.</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3: Counting monomer units</h3>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> A poly(ethene) chain contains 40 repeating units. How many carbon atoms are represented in those units?</p>
<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Each –CH₂–CH₂– repeating unit contains two carbon atoms.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> There are 40 repeating units.</p>
<p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Number of carbon atoms = 40 × 2 = 80.</p>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> 80 carbon atoms in the 40 repeating units.</p>', 1),
  (900, 900, '<h2>Crude Oil</h2>

<h3>1. What is crude oil?</h3>
<p>Crude oil is a naturally occurring mixture of hydrocarbons found in underground rock formations and beneath the seabed.</p>

<p>A hydrocarbon is a compound containing only carbon and hydrogen. Crude oil contains many different hydrocarbons, with molecules of different sizes and structures.</p>

<p>Crude oil is a mixture rather than a single compound because its components are not chemically joined together in one fixed formula. Its composition varies from one source to another.</p>

<h3>2. How crude oil forms</h3>
<p>Crude oil formed over millions of years from the remains of ancient organisms. Burial beneath layers of sediment, together with heat, pressure and geological conditions, transformed organic material into petroleum.</p>

<p>Crude oil is a non-renewable resource on human timescales because it takes millions of years to form and is consumed much faster than new supplies develop.</p>

<h3>3. Why crude oil is separated</h3>
<p>Crude oil contains hydrocarbons with different boiling points. Smaller molecules generally have lower boiling points than larger molecules from similar hydrocarbon series.</p>

<p>Different hydrocarbons have different uses. Refineries separate crude oil into useful groups called <strong>fractions</strong>.</p>

<h3>4. Fractional distillation</h3>
<p>Fractional distillation separates the components of a mixture according to differences in their boiling points. It is a physical separation process; it does not normally change the hydrocarbons into different substances.</p>

<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Crude oil is heated so that much of it vaporises.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> The vapours enter a fractionating column, which is hot near the bottom and cooler near the top.</p>
<p><strong>Step 3:</strong> As vapours rise and cool, different hydrocarbons condense at different heights according to their boiling-point ranges.</p>
<p><strong>Step 4:</strong> The fractions are collected separately.</p>

<p>Fractions are groups of many hydrocarbons with similar boiling-point ranges, not usually pure individual compounds.</p>

<h3>5. Properties of fractions</h3>
<p>For many fractions, moving from lighter to heavier fractions generally means:</p>
<ul>
<li>increasing average molecular size;</li>
<li>increasing boiling-point range;</li>
<li>increasing viscosity;</li>
<li>decreasing volatility;</li>
<li>decreasing ease of ignition.</li>
</ul>

<p>These are general trends. A fraction contains a range of compounds, so its properties are not described by one exact molecular formula.</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1: Classifying crude oil</h3>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> Is crude oil an element, compound or mixture?</p>
<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> An element contains one type of atom.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> A pure compound contains elements chemically combined in a fixed ratio.</p>
<p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Crude oil contains many different hydrocarbons.</p>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Crude oil is a mixture.</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2: Explaining separation</h3>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> Why can fractional distillation separate hydrocarbons in crude oil?</p>
<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> The hydrocarbons have different boiling points.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> The column has a temperature gradient.</p>
<p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Different hydrocarbons condense at different temperatures and heights.</p>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> The hydrocarbons are separated because they have different boiling-point ranges.</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3: Comparing two fractions</h3>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> Fraction A contains generally smaller hydrocarbons than fraction B. Which is likely to be more volatile?</p>
<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Smaller hydrocarbons generally have lower boiling points than larger ones in comparable series.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> A substance with a lower boiling point evaporates more readily.</p>
<p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Therefore, fraction A is generally more volatile.</p>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Fraction A.</p>', 1),
  (901, 901, '<h2>Cracking</h2>

<h3>1. What is cracking?</h3>
<p>Cracking is the process of breaking large hydrocarbon molecules into smaller molecules by heating them under suitable conditions. A catalyst may be used in catalytic cracking.</p>

<p>Cracking is useful because some heavy fractions contain large hydrocarbons that are less useful or less demanded than smaller hydrocarbons.</p>

<h3>2. Why cracking is important</h3>
<p>Cracking helps meet demand for smaller hydrocarbons used as fuels. It also produces alkenes, which are important raw materials for making polymers and other chemicals.</p>

<p>Cracking commonly produces a mixture of smaller alkanes and alkenes. The exact products depend on the starting hydrocarbon and the cracking conditions.</p>

<h3>3. Conditions for cracking</h3>
<p>Cracking requires high temperatures. Catalytic cracking uses a suitable catalyst, such as a zeolite catalyst, to help the process occur efficiently. The exact conditions depend on the industrial process.</p>

<h3>4. Understanding cracking equations</h3>
<p>A large hydrocarbon breaks into two or more smaller molecules. The total number of carbon atoms and hydrogen atoms must be the same before and after the reaction.</p>

<p><strong>Example:</strong></p>
<p><strong>C₁₀H₂₂ → C₈H₁₈ + C₂H₄</strong></p>

<p>Decane breaks into octane and ethene. Octane is an alkane and ethene is an alkene.</p>

<p><strong>Carbon check:</strong> 10 on the left; 8 + 2 = 10 on the right.</p>
<p><strong>Hydrogen check:</strong> 22 on the left; 18 + 4 = 22 on the right.</p>
<p>The equation is balanced.</p>

<h3>5. Recognising products</h3>
<p>Alkanes have the general formula CₙH₂ₙ₊₂ for open-chain molecules containing only single carbon-carbon bonds.</p>
<p>Alkenes with one carbon-carbon double bond and an open chain have the general formula CₙH₂ₙ.</p>
<p>These formulas help identify likely products, but the structural formula or reaction context may be needed to distinguish compounds.</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1: Identifying cracking</h3>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> A large hydrocarbon is heated and breaks into smaller hydrocarbons. Name the process.</p>
<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> The starting material is a large hydrocarbon.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> It breaks down into smaller molecules.</p>
<p><strong>Step 3:</strong> This process is called cracking.</p>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Cracking.</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2: Balancing a cracking equation</h3>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> Check whether C₁₂H₂₆ → C₈H₁₈ + C₄H₈ is balanced.</p>
<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Count carbon atoms on the right: 8 + 4 = 12.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Count hydrogen atoms on the right: 18 + 8 = 26.</p>
<p><strong>Step 3:</strong> The left side also has 12 carbon atoms and 26 hydrogen atoms.</p>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> The equation is balanced.</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3: Identifying an alkene product</h3>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> Cracking produces a hydrocarbon with formula C₃H₆. If it is an open-chain hydrocarbon with one double bond, identify its homologous series.</p>
<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> For an open-chain alkene with one double bond, the general formula is CₙH₂ₙ.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> For n = 3, the formula is C₃H₆.</p>
<p><strong>Step 3:</strong> The formula matches the alkene general formula.</p>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> It is an alkene, such as propene.</p>', 1),
  (902, 902, '
  <h2>Properties of Sulphur</h2>

  <h3>1. Identity of sulphur</h3>

  <p>Sulphur is a chemical element with the symbol <strong>S</strong>
  and atomic number 16. It is classified as a non-metal and occurs
  naturally in the Earth''s crust and in some minerals.</p>

  <p>At room temperature, ordinary sulphur is a yellow solid.
  Its physical properties help distinguish it from metals and
  other non-metals.</p>

  <h3>2. Physical properties</h3>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Colour:</strong> Sulphur is yellow.</li>
    <li><strong>Physical state:</strong> It is a solid at room temperature.</li>
    <li><strong>Brittleness:</strong> Solid sulphur breaks into pieces
    when struck rather than being hammered into sheets.</li>
    <li><strong>Electrical conductivity:</strong> It is a poor conductor
    of electricity.</li>
    <li><strong>Solubility:</strong> It is insoluble in water.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>These properties are physical because they can be observed
  without changing sulphur into a different chemical substance.</p>

  <h3>3. Burning sulphur in oxygen</h3>

  <p>Sulphur undergoes a chemical reaction when heated in oxygen.
  It burns to form sulphur dioxide, a colourless gas.</p>

  <p><strong>Word equation:</strong></p>
  <p>Sulphur + Oxygen → Sulphur dioxide</p>

  <p><strong>Symbol equation:</strong></p>
  <p><strong>S(s) + O₂(g) → SO₂(g)</strong></p>

  <p>The equation is balanced. There is one sulphur atom and
  two oxygen atoms on each side.</p>

  <p>Burning sulphur dioxide-producing materials can release
  harmful gases. Sulphur dioxide must not be deliberately inhaled.</p>

  <h3>4. Physical change versus chemical change</h3>

  <p>Breaking a piece of sulphur into smaller pieces is a physical
  change: the substance remains sulphur.</p>

  <p>Burning sulphur is a chemical change because sulphur reacts
  with oxygen to produce a new substance, sulphur dioxide.</p>

  <h3>Worked examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: Classifying properties</strong></p>

  <p>A student observes that sulphur is yellow, brittle and a poor
  conductor of electricity. Classify each observation.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Yellow describes its colour.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Brittle describes how easily the solid breaks.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Poor conductivity describes its ability to
  conduct electric current.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> All three are physical properties.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: Writing the combustion equation</strong></p>

  <p>Write the symbol equation for the complete reaction of sulphur
  with oxygen.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Write the reactants: S and O₂.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Identify the product: SO₂.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Check the atoms on both sides.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> S + O₂ → SO₂.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: Identifying the type of change</strong></p>

  <p>Explain why burning sulphur is a chemical change but breaking
  sulphur into powder is a physical change.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Breaking sulphur changes its size only.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> The material remains sulphur.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Burning produces sulphur dioxide, a new substance.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Breaking is a physical change; burning is
  a chemical change.</p>
