  <h2>Nitrogen Cycle</h2>

  <h3>1. Why Nitrogen Must Be Recycled</h3>

  <p>
    Nitrogen is essential for living organisms. Plants and animals
    need nitrogen to make amino acids, proteins and nucleic acids
    such as DNA.
  </p>

  <p>
    Although the atmosphere contains approximately 78% nitrogen gas,
    N<sub>2</sub>, most plants cannot use atmospheric nitrogen directly.
    The strong triple bond in nitrogen molecules makes the gas relatively
    unreactive under ordinary conditions.
  </p>

  <p>
    The nitrogen cycle describes how nitrogen moves between the
    atmosphere, soil, plants, animals and decomposing organic matter.
    Microorganisms carry out many of its essential transformations.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Nitrogen Fixation</h3>

  <p>
    Nitrogen fixation converts atmospheric nitrogen gas into compounds
    that can enter biological and soil processes.
  </p>

  <p><b>A. Biological fixation</b></p>

  <p>
    Certain bacteria convert atmospheric nitrogen into ammonia or
    related nitrogen compounds. Some live freely in the soil, while
    others live in root nodules of leguminous plants such as beans,
    peas and clover.
  </p>

  <p>
    The plants provide the bacteria with carbohydrates and a suitable
    environment. In return, the bacteria supply fixed nitrogen that
    supports plant growth.
  </p>

  <p><b>B. Atmospheric fixation</b></p>

  <p>
    Lightning provides enough energy for nitrogen and oxygen in the
    atmosphere to react and form nitrogen oxides. These eventually
    produce nitrates that rain can carry into the soil.
  </p>

  <p><b>C. Industrial fixation</b></p>

  <p>
    The Haber process converts atmospheric nitrogen and hydrogen into
    ammonia. Industrially produced ammonia is used to make nitrogen
    fertilisers.
  </p>

  <p>
    N<sub>2</sub> + 3H<sub>2</sub>
    ⇌ 2NH<sub>3</sub>
  </p>

  <h3>3. Nitrification</h3>

  <p>
    Nitrification is the microbial conversion of ammonium compounds
    into nitrites and then nitrates under suitable aerobic conditions.
  </p>

  <p>
    In simplified form:
  </p>

  <p>
    Ammonium compounds → Nitrites (NO<sub>2</sub><sup>−</sup>)
    → Nitrates (NO<sub>3</sub><sup>−</sup>)
  </p>

  <p>
    Different groups of nitrifying microorganisms carry out the stages.
    Nitrates are readily absorbed by many plants through their roots.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Assimilation</h3>

  <p>
    Assimilation occurs when plants absorb nitrate or ammonium ions
    from the soil and use the nitrogen to manufacture amino acids,
    proteins and other nitrogen-containing substances.
  </p>

  <p>
    Animals obtain nitrogen by eating plants or other animals.
    Nitrogen therefore passes through food chains from producers
    to consumers.
  </p>

  <h3>5. Ammonification</h3>

  <p>
    Ammonification is the breakdown of nitrogen-containing organic
    matter into ammonia or ammonium compounds by decomposers such
    as certain bacteria and fungi.
  </p>

  <p>
    The process occurs when organisms die or release nitrogen-containing
    waste. Decomposers break down proteins and other organic compounds,
    returning nitrogen to the soil.
  </p>

  <h3>6. Denitrification</h3>

  <p>
    Denitrification is the conversion of nitrates into gaseous nitrogen
    compounds, including nitrogen gas, by certain microorganisms.
    It commonly occurs in oxygen-poor or waterlogged soils.
  </p>

  <p>
    Denitrification returns nitrogen to the atmosphere and completes
    an important part of the cycle.
  </p>

  <p>
    Nitrification generally requires oxygen, whereas denitrification
    is favoured by low-oxygen conditions. They are different processes
    with different effects on soil nitrogen.
  </p>

  <h3>7. The Main Stages in Sequence</h3>

  <ol>
    <li><b>Fixation:</b> Atmospheric nitrogen is converted into biologically useful nitrogen compounds.</li>
    <li><b>Nitrification:</b> Ammonium compounds are converted into nitrites and nitrates.</li>
    <li><b>Assimilation:</b> Plants absorb nitrogen compounds; animals obtain nitrogen through feeding.</li>
    <li><b>Ammonification:</b> Decomposers return nitrogen from organic matter to ammonia or ammonium compounds.</li>
    <li><b>Denitrification:</b> Nitrates are converted into gaseous nitrogen compounds and returned to the atmosphere.</li>
  </ol>

  <p>
    These processes interact rather than occurring as one simple,
    uninterrupted sequence. For example, ammonium compounds released
    during decomposition may be nitrified, absorbed directly by
    plants or undergo other soil transformations.
  </p>

  <h3>8. Importance of the Nitrogen Cycle</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Recycles nitrogen between the atmosphere, soil and living organisms.</li>
    <li>Maintains a supply of usable nitrogen for plant growth.</li>
    <li>Supports the formation of proteins and nucleic acids.</li>
    <li>Connects soil microorganisms with plants and animals in ecosystems.</li>
    <li>Helps explain why nitrogen availability affects agricultural productivity.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Worked Examples</h3>

  <p><b>Example 1: Identifying a nitrogen-cycle process</b></p>

  <p><b>Question:</b> Soil bacteria convert ammonium compounds into
  nitrates. Name the process.</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify the starting substance: ammonium compounds.</p>

  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Identify the product: nitrate ions.</p>

  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Match the conversion to the correct process.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> Nitrification.</p>

  <p><b>Example 2: Nitrogen entering a food chain</b></p>

  <p><b>Question:</b> A bean plant absorbs nitrate ions and a cow eats
  the plant. Explain how nitrogen moves between them.</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> The plant absorbs nitrate ions from the soil.</p>

  <p><b>Step 2:</b> The plant uses the nitrogen to make amino acids
  and proteins.</p>

  <p><b>Step 3:</b> The cow obtains nitrogen by eating the plant''s
  nitrogen-containing tissues.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> Nitrogen is assimilated by the plant and then
  transferred to the cow through feeding.</p>

  <p><b>Example 3: Explaining nitrogen loss from soil</b></p>

  <p><b>Question:</b> A waterlogged soil loses nitrate nitrogen as
  nitrogen gas. Identify the process and explain why it matters.</p>

  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify the starting substance: nitrate ions.</p>

  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Identify the product: gaseous nitrogen compounds.</p>

  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Link the conversion to the soil conditions.</p>

  <p><b>Answer:</b> Denitrification. It returns nitrogen to the
  atmosphere and can reduce the amount of nitrate available to plants.</p>

  <h3>Key Distinctions</h3>

  <ul>
    <li><b>Fixation:</b> Makes atmospheric nitrogen available in compounds.</li>
    <li><b>Nitrification:</b> Converts ammonium compounds into nitrites and nitrates.</li>
    <li><b>Assimilation:</b> Incorporates nitrogen into living organisms.</li>
    <li><b>Ammonification:</b> Returns organic nitrogen to ammonia or ammonium compounds.</li>
    <li><b>Denitrification:</b> Returns nitrogen from nitrates to the atmosphere as gaseous compounds.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 1),
  (914, 914, '<h2>Composition of Air</h2>

  <p>Air is a mixture of gases. The major components of dry air are:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>Nitrogen — about 78%</li>
    <li>Oxygen — about 21%</li>
    <li>Argon and other noble gases — about 0.9%</li>
    <li>Carbon dioxide — about 0.04%</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Air also contains variable amounts of water vapour and small quantities of other gases.</p>

  <h3>Important point</h3>
  <p>Air is a <strong>mixture</strong>, not a compound, because its components are not chemically bonded and can be separated by physical methods.</p>

  <h3>Example</h3>
  <p>In 100 dm³ of dry air, approximately 78 dm³ is nitrogen and 21 dm³ is oxygen.</p>', 1),
  (915, 915, '<h2>Properties of Oxygen</h2>

  <p>Oxygen is a colourless, odourless and tasteless gas at room temperature.</p>

  <ul>
    <li>It is slightly soluble in water.</li>
    <li>It is slightly denser than air.</li>
    <li>It does not burn itself.</li>
    <li>It supports combustion.</li>
    <li>It is essential for respiration.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Test for oxygen</h3>
  <p>A glowing splint is inserted into the gas. If the splint relights, oxygen is present.</p>

  <h3>Combustion</h3>
  <p>Carbon burns in oxygen:</p>

  <p><strong>C + O₂ → CO₂</strong></p>', 1),
  (916, 916, '<h2>Uses of Oxygen</h2>

  <p>Oxygen is important because it supports combustion and respiration.</p>

  <h3>Main uses</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Respiration in hospitals.</li>
    <li>Welding and cutting metals.</li>
    <li>Steel manufacture.</li>
    <li>Supporting combustion in industrial processes.</li>
    <li>Life-support systems in aircraft and spacecraft.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Medical oxygen</h3>
  <p>Patients who cannot obtain enough oxygen from normal air may be supplied with oxygen.</p>

  <h3>Welding</h3>
  <p>Oxygen is used with suitable fuel gases to produce a very hot flame for cutting and welding metals.</p>', 1),
  (917, 917, '<h2>Properties of Water</h2>

  <p>Pure water is a colourless, odourless and tasteless liquid at room temperature.</p>

  <ul>
    <li>It freezes at 0°C under standard atmospheric pressure.</li>
    <li>It boils at 100°C under standard atmospheric pressure.</li>
    <li>It is a good solvent for many substances.</li>
    <li>It is essential for living organisms.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Water as a compound</h3>
  <p>Water is a compound because hydrogen and oxygen are chemically bonded in a fixed ratio.</p>

  <p><strong>Formula: H₂O</strong></p>', 1),
  (918, 918, '<h2>Water as a Solvent</h2>

  <p>A <strong>solvent</strong> is a substance that dissolves a solute to form a solution.</p>

  <p>Water dissolves many ionic and polar substances and is therefore often called a good solvent.</p>

  <h3>Examples</h3>

  <p>When sodium chloride dissolves in water:</p>

  <p><strong>NaCl(s) → Na⁺(aq) + Cl⁻(aq)</strong></p>

  <p>Water is important in living organisms because many substances are transported in aqueous solution.</p>', 1),
  (919, 919, '<h2>Hard Water</h2>

  <p>Hard water contains dissolved calcium and magnesium ions.</p>

  <p>These ions react with soap to form an insoluble substance called <strong>scum</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Effects of hard water</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Soap does not lather easily.</li>
    <li>Scum is formed.</li>
    <li>More soap is required for washing.</li>
    <li>Some types of hard water can form scale in pipes and boilers.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Important distinction</h3>
  <p>Hard water is not necessarily unsafe to drink. The main problem considered here is its behaviour with soap and heating systems.</p>', 1),
  (920, 920, '<h2>Soft Water</h2>

  <p>Soft water contains very small amounts of calcium and magnesium ions compared with hard water.</p>

  <h3>Behaviour with soap</h3>

  <p>Soft water reacts less with soap, so soap produces lather readily.</p>

  <h3>Comparison</h3>

  <p><strong>Soft water → easy lathering → little or no scum.</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Hard water → poor lathering → scum formation.</strong></p>', 1),
  (921, 921, '<h2>Water Purification</h2>

  <p>Water purification removes unwanted substances and harmful microorganisms so that water becomes suitable for its intended use.</p>

  <h3>Common purification processes</h3>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Filtration:</strong> removes insoluble solid particles.</li>
    <li><strong>Boiling:</strong> kills many microorganisms.</li>
    <li><strong>Chlorination:</strong> uses chlorine to disinfect water.</li>
    <li><strong>Distillation:</strong> separates water from many dissolved substances by evaporation followed by condensation.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>The purification method depends on the type of impurity present.</p>', 1),
  (922, 922, '<h2>Carbon Monoxide as an Air Pollutant</h2>

  <p>Carbon monoxide, CO, is a poisonous gas produced mainly by incomplete combustion of carbon-containing fuels.</p>

  <p>For example:</p>

  <p><strong>2C + O₂ → 2CO</strong></p>

  <h3>Why CO is dangerous</h3>

  <p>Carbon monoxide combines strongly with haemoglobin in blood. This reduces the ability of blood to transport oxygen around the body.</p>

  <h3>Sources</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Incomplete combustion in vehicle engines.</li>
    <li>Burning charcoal in poorly ventilated rooms.</li>
    <li>Faulty fuel-burning appliances.</li>
  </ul>', 1),
  (923, 923, '<h2>Sulphur Dioxide as an Air Pollutant</h2>

  <p>Sulphur dioxide, SO₂, is produced when sulphur-containing fuels are burned.</p>

  <p>A simplified equation is:</p>

  <p><strong>S + O₂ → SO₂</strong></p>

  <h3>Effects</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Irritates the respiratory system.</li>
    <li>Contributes to acid rain.</li>
    <li>Can damage vegetation.</li>
    <li>Can contribute to corrosion of materials.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>SO₂ can react with water in the atmosphere to form acidic substances.</p>', 1),
  (924, 924, '<h2>Nitrogen Oxides as Air Pollutants</h2>

  <p>Nitrogen oxides, commonly represented as NOₓ, include gases such as nitrogen monoxide, NO, and nitrogen dioxide, NO₂.</p>

  <p>They can form at high temperatures, especially in vehicle engines and industrial combustion processes.</p>

  <h3>Effects</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Contribute to acid rain.</li>
    <li>Can irritate the respiratory system.</li>
    <li>Contribute to photochemical smog.</li>
    <li>Can damage vegetation.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Nitrogen dioxide is a reddish-brown gas with a characteristic irritating smell.</p>', 1),
  (925, 925, '<h2>Particulate Matter</h2>

  <p>Particulate matter consists of very small solid particles or liquid droplets suspended in air.</p>

  <h3>Sources</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Vehicle exhaust.</li>
    <li>Industrial emissions.</li>
    <li>Burning biomass and waste.</li>
    <li>Dust from roads and construction.</li>
    <li>Smoke from incomplete combustion.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Effects</h3>

  <p>Very small particles can enter the respiratory system and cause or worsen respiratory problems.</p>

  <p>Reducing smoke, dust and uncontrolled combustion reduces particulate pollution.</p>', 1),
  (926, 926, '<h2>Acid Rain</h2>

  <p>Acid rain is precipitation that is more acidic than normal because acidic substances formed in the atmosphere dissolve in rainwater.</p>

  <h3>Main gases involved</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Sulphur dioxide, SO₂</li>
    <li>Nitrogen oxides, NOₓ</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Effects of acid rain</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Damages vegetation.</li>
    <li>Acidifies lakes and rivers.</li>
    <li>Can harm aquatic organisms.</li>
    <li>Corrodes metals.</li>
    <li>Can damage limestone and marble buildings.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Control</h3>
  <p>Reducing emissions of sulphur dioxide and nitrogen oxides reduces acid rain.</p>', 1),
  (927, 927, '<h2>Greenhouse Effect</h2>

  <p>The greenhouse effect is the warming of Earth''s lower atmosphere and surface caused by gases that absorb and re-emit infrared radiation.</p>

  <h3>Greenhouse gases</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Carbon dioxide, CO₂</li>
    <li>Methane, CH₄</li>
    <li>Nitrous oxide, N₂O</li>
    <li>Water vapour</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>How it works</h3>

  <p>Earth receives energy from the Sun. The Earth''s surface emits infrared radiation. Greenhouse gases absorb some of this radiation and re-emit it, reducing the rate at which heat escapes to space.</p>

  <h3>Important distinction</h3>

  <p>The natural greenhouse effect is necessary for life. The concern is the increase in greenhouse gases caused by human activities, which enhances warming.</p>', 1),
  (928, 928, '<h2>Pollution Control</h2>

  <p>Pollution control involves reducing the release of harmful substances into the environment and reducing their effects.</p>

  <h3>Air pollution control</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Use cleaner fuels.</li>
    <li>Maintain vehicle engines properly.</li>
    <li>Use emission-control systems.</li>
    <li>Reduce open burning of waste.</li>
    <li>Use renewable energy where practical.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Water pollution control</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Treat sewage before discharge.</li>
    <li>Treat industrial wastewater.</li>
    <li>Prevent harmful chemicals from entering water bodies.</li>
    <li>Control excessive use of fertilisers and pesticides.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Sustainable use of resources</h3>

  <p>Sustainable use means using natural resources in ways that meet present needs while reducing unnecessary damage and preserving resources for the future.</p>', 1),
  (929, 929, '<h2>Test for Ammonium Ions</h2>

  <p>Ammonium ions are represented by <strong>NH₄⁺</strong>.</p>

  <p>They can be identified by adding a suitable alkali, such as sodium hydroxide solution, and warming the mixture.</p>

  <h3>Test</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Add sodium hydroxide solution to the sample.</li>
    <li>Warm the mixture gently.</li>
    <li>Test any gas produced with damp red litmus paper.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Positive result</h3>
  <p>A pungent-smelling gas is produced which turns damp red litmus paper blue.</p>

  <p>The gas is <strong>ammonia, NH₃</strong>.</p>

  <p><strong>NH₄⁺ + OH⁻ → NH₃ + H₂O</strong></p>', 1),
  (930, 930, '<h2>Test for Copper(II) Ions</h2>

  <p>Copper(II) ions are represented by <strong>Cu²⁺</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Test using sodium hydroxide</h3>

  <p>Add sodium hydroxide solution to the sample.</p>

  <h3>Positive result</h3>

  <p>A <strong>blue precipitate</strong> of copper(II) hydroxide forms.</p>

  <p><strong>Cu²⁺ + 2OH⁻ → Cu(OH)₂(s)</strong></p>

  <p>The precipitate is insoluble in excess sodium hydroxide under the usual school laboratory test.</p>', 1),
  (931, 931, '<h2>Test for Iron(II) Ions</h2>

  <p>Iron(II) ions are represented by <strong>Fe²⁺</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Test</h3>

  <p>Add sodium hydroxide solution to the sample.</p>

  <h3>Positive result</h3>

  <p>A <strong>green precipitate</strong> of iron(II) hydroxide forms.</p>

  <p><strong>Fe²⁺ + 2OH⁻ → Fe(OH)₂(s)</strong></p>

  <p>The precipitate may change colour on exposure to air because iron(II) compounds can be oxidised.</p>', 1),
  (932, 932, '<h2>Test for Iron(III) Ions</h2>

  <p>Iron(III) ions are represented by <strong>Fe³⁺</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Test</h3>

  <p>Add sodium hydroxide solution to the sample.</p>

  <h3>Positive result</h3>

  <p>A <strong>red-brown precipitate</strong> of iron(III) hydroxide forms.</p>

  <p><strong>Fe³⁺ + 3OH⁻ → Fe(OH)₃(s)</strong></p>', 1),
  (933, 933, '<h2>Test for Chloride Ions</h2>

  <p>Chloride ions are represented by <strong>Cl⁻</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Test</h3>

  <ol>
    <li>Acidify the sample with dilute nitric acid.</li>
    <li>Add aqueous silver nitrate.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Positive result</h3>

  <p>A <strong>white precipitate</strong> of silver chloride forms.</p>

  <p><strong>Ag⁺ + Cl⁻ → AgCl(s)</strong></p>', 1),
  (934, 934, '<h2>Test for Sulphate Ions</h2>

  <p>Sulphate ions are represented by <strong>SO₄²⁻</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Test</h3>

  <ol>
    <li>Acidify the sample using dilute hydrochloric acid.</li>
    <li>Add barium nitrate solution or another suitable soluble barium salt.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Positive result</h3>

  <p>A <strong>white precipitate</strong> of barium sulphate forms.</p>

  <p><strong>Ba²⁺ + SO₄²⁻ → BaSO₄(s)</strong></p>', 1),
  (935, 935, '<h2>Test for Carbonate Ions</h2>

  <p>Carbonate ions are represented by <strong>CO₃²⁻</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Test</h3>

  <p>Add a dilute acid to the sample.</p>

  <h3>Positive result</h3>

  <p>Effervescence occurs because carbon dioxide gas is produced.</p>

  <p>The gas can be passed through limewater.</p>

  <p>Limewater turns <strong>milky</strong> if carbon dioxide is present.</p>

  <p><strong>CO₃²⁻ + 2H⁺ → CO₂ + H₂O</strong></p>', 1),
  (936, 936, '<h2>Test for Nitrate Ions</h2>

  <p>Nitrate ions are represented by <strong>NO₃⁻</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Test</h3>

  <p>A common school laboratory test involves adding sodium hydroxide and aluminium foil, followed by gentle warming.</p>

  <p>If nitrate ions are present, ammonia gas may be produced.</p>

  <h3>Positive result</h3>

  <p>The gas turns damp red litmus paper blue.</p>

  <p>The ammonia produced indicates nitrate ions in the original sample.</p>', 1),
  (937, 937, '<h2>Flame Test for Sodium Ions</h2>

  <p>Sodium ions are represented by <strong>Na⁺</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Procedure</h3>

  <ol>
    <li>Clean a suitable wire loop.</li>
    <li>Place a small amount of the sample on the loop.</li>
    <li>Place it in a non-luminous flame.</li>
    <li>Observe the flame colour.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Positive result</h3>

  <p>Sodium compounds produce a <strong>yellow flame</strong>.</p>', 1),
  (938, 938, '<h2>Flame Test for Potassium Ions</h2>

  <p>Potassium ions are represented by <strong>K⁺</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Flame colour</h3>

  <p>Potassium compounds produce a <strong>lilac flame</strong>.</p>

  <p>A sodium impurity can produce a strong yellow colour that may mask the lilac colour, so careful observation is important.</p>', 1),
  (939, 939, '<h2>Flame Test for Calcium Ions</h2>

  <p>Calcium ions are represented by <strong>Ca²⁺</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Flame colour</h3>

  <p>Calcium compounds produce a <strong>brick-red or orange-red flame</strong>.</p>

  <p>The exact appearance can depend on the conditions and sample purity.</p>', 1),
  (940, 940, '<h2>Flame Test for Copper(II) Ions</h2>

  <p>Copper(II) ions are represented by <strong>Cu²⁺</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Flame colour</h3>

  <p>Copper compounds can produce a <strong>blue-green or green flame</strong>.</p>

  <p>This provides another qualitative observation that can support identification of copper compounds.</p>', 1),
  (941, 941, '<h2>Test for Hydrogen</h2>

  <p>Hydrogen gas is represented by <strong>H₂</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Test</h3>

  <p>Collect a sample of the gas and bring a lighted splint close to the gas.</p>

  <h3>Positive result</h3>

  <p>Hydrogen burns with a characteristic <strong>squeaky pop</strong>.</p>

  <p>The reaction is:</p>

  <p><strong>2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O</strong></p>', 1),
  (942, 942, '<h2>Test for Oxygen</h2>

  <p>Oxygen gas is represented by <strong>O₂</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Test</h3>

  <p>Insert a glowing splint into the gas.</p>

  <h3>Positive result</h3>

  <p>The glowing splint <strong>relights</strong>.</p>

  <p>Oxygen supports combustion but does not itself burn.</p>', 1),
  (943, 943, '<h2>Test for Carbon Dioxide</h2>

  <p>Carbon dioxide is represented by <strong>CO₂</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Test</h3>

  <p>Bubble the gas through limewater.</p>

  <h3>Positive result</h3>

  <p>Limewater turns <strong>milky</strong>.</p>

  <p>The reaction is:</p>

  <p><strong>CO₂ + Ca(OH)₂ → CaCO₃ + H₂O</strong></p>

  <p>The calcium carbonate formed is insoluble and causes the milky appearance.</p>', 1),
  (944, 944, '<h2>Test for Ammonia</h2>

  <p>Ammonia is represented by <strong>NH₃</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Properties useful for identification</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>It is a colourless gas.</li>
    <li>It has a pungent smell.</li>
    <li>It is alkaline in water.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Test</h3>

  <p>Expose damp red litmus paper to the gas.</p>

  <h3>Positive result</h3>

  <p>Damp red litmus paper turns <strong>blue</strong>.</p>', 1),
  (945, 945, '<h2>Test for Chlorine</h2>

  <p>Chlorine gas is represented by <strong>Cl₂</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Properties useful for identification</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Chlorine is a greenish-yellow gas.</li>
    <li>It has a choking smell.</li>
    <li>It is toxic.</li>
    <li>It has bleaching properties when moisture is present.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Test</h3>

  <p>Expose damp blue litmus paper to the gas.</p>

  <h3>Positive result</h3>

  <p>The blue litmus paper is first turned red and then <strong>bleached</strong>.</p>

  <p>The bleaching occurs because chlorine reacts with water to form substances that can oxidise coloured materials.</p>', 1),
  (946, 946, '
  <h2>Reversible Reactions</h2>

  <h3>1. What Is a Chemical Reaction?</h3>

  <p>
    A <strong>chemical reaction</strong> is a process in which one or more
    substances change into different substances. The starting substances
    are called <strong>reactants</strong>, while the substances formed are
    called <strong>products</strong>.
  </p>

  <p>
    For example, when hydrogen reacts with oxygen, water is formed:
  </p>

  <pre>2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O</pre>

  <p>
    The arrow points from the reactants towards the products. In this
    representation, the reaction is written in one direction: hydrogen
    and oxygen form water.
  </p>

  <p>
    However, not every chemical reaction is restricted to proceeding
    in only one direction under the relevant conditions. Some reactions
    can proceed in both directions.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Meaning of a Reversible Reaction</h3>

  <p>
    A <strong>reversible reaction</strong> is a chemical reaction in which
    the products can react to reform the original reactants under suitable
    conditions.
  </p>

  <p>
    This means that the chemical change can proceed in two directions:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Forward direction:</strong> reactants form products.</li>
    <li><strong>Reverse direction:</strong> products reform reactants.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    A reversible reaction is represented by a double arrow:
  </p>

  <pre>A + B ⇌ C + D</pre>

  <p>
    The symbol <strong>⇌</strong> indicates that the reaction can proceed
    from left to right and from right to left. It does not mean that both
    directions must occur at equal rates at every moment.
  </p>

  <p>
    The substances on the left and right represent the two sides of the
    same reversible chemical change. The actual direction and extent of
    reaction depend on the substances present and the conditions.
  </p>

  <h3>3. How Can a Reaction Be Reversible?</h3>

  <p>
    In a reversible reaction, the products formed in one direction can
    undergo a chemical change that produces the original reactants.
    Therefore, the original chemical change is not necessarily permanent
    under every possible set of conditions.
  </p>

  <p>
    Some reversible changes are demonstrated by changing temperature.
    Other reversible reactions occur in a mixture containing both
    reactants and products. Reversibility is a property of the chemical
    process, not simply a matter of whether a visible change can be undone
    physically.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Recognising Reversible Reactions</h3>

  <p>
    Look for evidence that the products can chemically react to regenerate
    the original reactants. A double arrow in a correctly written chemical
    equation is the standard symbol used to represent a reversible reaction.
  </p>

  <p>
    Do not assume that every reaction is reversible merely because its
    products can be changed into something else through a different
    chemical reaction. The reverse direction must regenerate the original
    reactants.
  </p>

  <h3>WORKED EXAMPLE 1 — Understanding the Double Arrow</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> What does the equation below tell us?</p>

  <pre>A + B ⇌ C + D</pre>

  <p><strong>Step 1: Identify the left-hand side.</strong></p>

  <p>
    A and B are the reactants for the left-to-right direction. They
    chemically combine or react to form C and D.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2: Interpret the arrow.</strong></p>

  <p>
    The double arrow indicates that the chemical change can also proceed
    from right to left.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3: Identify the reverse change.</strong></p>

  <p>
    C and D can react to reform A and B under suitable conditions.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Final answer:</strong> The equation represents a reversible
  reaction because the chemical change can proceed in both directions.</p>

  <h3>WORKED EXAMPLE 2 — Recognising Reversibility in a Chemical Change</h3>

  <p>
    <strong>Question:</strong> Blue hydrated copper(II) sulfate crystals
    can be heated to form white anhydrous copper(II) sulfate. When water
    is added to the anhydrous compound under suitable conditions, the blue
    hydrated compound can form again. Explain why this demonstrates a
    reversible chemical change.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1: Identify the forward chemical change.</strong></p>

  <p>
    Heating removes water of crystallisation from hydrated copper(II)
    sulfate, producing anhydrous copper(II) sulfate.
  </p>

  <pre>CuSO₄·5H₂O ⇌ CuSO₄ + 5H₂O</pre>

  <p><strong>Step 2: Identify the reverse chemical change.</strong></p>

  <p>
    The anhydrous copper(II) sulfate can combine with water to reform
    hydrated copper(II) sulfate.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3: Connect the two directions.</strong></p>

  <p>
    The original hydrated compound is regenerated. The chemical change
    can therefore proceed in both directions.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Final answer:</strong> It is a reversible chemical change
  because dehydration and rehydration connect the same two chemical
  states in opposite directions.</p>

  <p>
    <strong>Important:</strong> The colour change helps you observe the
    process, but the evidence of reversibility is the regeneration of the
    original compound, not simply the appearance of a different colour.
  </p>

  <h3>WORKED EXAMPLE 3 — Reversible or Effectively Irreversible?</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Compare these two changes:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>Change A: A reversible reaction represented by
      <code>A + B ⇌ C + D</code>.</li>
    <li>Change B: Magnesium burns in oxygen to form magnesium oxide.</li>
  </ul>

  <p><strong>Step 1: Examine Change A.</strong></p>

  <p>
    The double arrow represents a reaction that can proceed in both
    directions. The products can reform the original reactants.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2: Examine Change B.</strong></p>

  <p>
    Magnesium burns in oxygen to form magnesium oxide:
  </p>

  <pre>2Mg + O₂ → 2MgO</pre>

  <p>
    Under ordinary conditions, magnesium oxide does not simply change
    back into magnesium and oxygen. Reversing this chemical change
    requires a different process and substantial energy.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3: Make the distinction.</strong></p>

  <p>
    Change A is represented as reversible. Change B is treated as
    effectively irreversible under ordinary classroom conditions.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Final answer:</strong> Reversibility means that the original
  reactants can be regenerated through the reverse chemical reaction
  under suitable conditions. It does not mean that every product can
  be converted back by any method whatsoever.</p>

  <h3>5. Common Misconceptions</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Misconception:</strong> A reversible reaction is one that
      can be undone by any method.
      <br/>
      <strong>Correction:</strong> Its reverse direction must regenerate
      the original reactants through the corresponding chemical change.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Misconception:</strong> The double arrow means the two
      directions are equally fast.
      <br/>
      <strong>Correction:</strong> It indicates two possible reaction
      directions, not equal reaction rates.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Misconception:</strong> Every reaction is reversible because
      products can react to form other substances.
      <br/>
      <strong>Correction:</strong> Those reactions must regenerate the
      original reactants to constitute the reverse of the original change.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Misconception:</strong> A colour change alone proves that a
      reaction is reversible.
      <br/>
      <strong>Correction:</strong> The original chemical substances must
      be regenerated.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>6. What You Should Be Able to Do</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Define a reversible reaction.</li>
    <li>Interpret the double arrow, ⇌.</li>
    <li>Identify the forward and reverse possibilities in an equation.</li>
    <li>Distinguish reversibility from simply undergoing another reaction.</li>
    <li>Explain why a chemical change can regenerate its original reactants.</li>
  </ul>
