  </ol>

  <p>The purified aluminium oxide is commonly called <strong>alumina</strong>.</p>', 1),
  (980, 980, '<h2>Purification of Bauxite</h2>

  <p>Bauxite contains aluminium compounds mixed with impurities. The aluminium compound must therefore be separated from the unwanted materials.</p>

  <p>The purified product is <strong>aluminium oxide, Al₂O₃</strong>, also called <strong>alumina</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Why Purification Is Necessary</h3>

  <p>Impurities can interfere with the extraction process and increase the amount of material that must be processed.</p>

  <p>Purification produces aluminium oxide that can be used in the electrolytic extraction stage.</p>

  <h3>Extraction Sequence</h3>

  <p><strong>Bauxite → Purification → Aluminium oxide → Electrolysis → Aluminium</strong></p>', 1),
  (981, 981, '<h2>Electrolysis of Aluminium Oxide</h2>

  <p>Aluminium is extracted from aluminium oxide by <strong>electrolysis</strong>.</p>

  <p>Aluminium oxide has a very high melting point. In the industrial process, it is dissolved in molten <strong>cryolite</strong> to lower the operating temperature and improve the conductivity of the electrolyte.</p>

  <h3>At the Cathode</h3>

  <p>Aluminium ions move towards the negative cathode and gain electrons.</p>

  <p><strong>Al³⁺ + 3e⁻ → Al</strong></p>

  <p>Aluminium metal is therefore formed at the cathode.</p>

  <h3>At the Anode</h3>

  <p>Oxide ions move towards the positive anode and lose electrons.</p>

  <p><strong>2O²⁻ → O₂ + 4e⁻</strong></p>

  <p>The oxygen produced reacts with the carbon anodes, causing them to be gradually consumed.</p>

  <h3>Important Points</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Aluminium ions are reduced at the cathode.</li>
    <li>Oxide ions are oxidised at the anode.</li>
    <li>Aluminium forms at the cathode.</li>
    <li>Carbon anodes are gradually consumed.</li>
  </ul>', 1),
  (982, 982, '<h2>Water Pollution</h2>

  <p><strong>Water pollution</strong> is the contamination of water by substances that make it harmful or unsuitable for humans, animals and other organisms.</p>

  <h3>Sources of Water Pollution</h3>

  <p>Water can be polluted by:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>untreated sewage;</li>
    <li>industrial waste;</li>
    <li>agricultural fertilisers;</li>
    <li>pesticides;</li>
    <li>oil and other chemical spills;</li>
    <li>domestic waste.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Effects of Water Pollution</h3>

  <p>Polluted water can cause disease and harm aquatic organisms.</p>

  <p>Fertilisers containing nitrates and phosphates can cause excessive growth of algae. When the algae die and decompose, microorganisms use up dissolved oxygen. This can cause fish and other aquatic organisms to die.</p>

  <p>Toxic substances can also accumulate in organisms and enter food chains.</p>

  <h3>Control of Water Pollution</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>treat sewage before discharge;</li>
    <li>treat industrial waste appropriately;</li>
    <li>reduce excessive use of fertilisers and pesticides;</li>
    <li>prevent oil and chemical spills;</li>
    <li>dispose of domestic waste properly.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Key Idea</h3>

  <p>Preventing pollutants from entering water sources is more effective than trying to remove pollution after contamination has occurred.</p>', 1),
  (983, 983, '<h2>Ozone Layer</h2>

  <p>The <strong>ozone layer</strong> is a region of the stratosphere containing a relatively high concentration of ozone, O₃.</p>

  <h3>Formation of Ozone</h3>

  <p>Ultraviolet radiation from the Sun can split oxygen molecules into oxygen atoms:</p>

  <p><strong>O₂ → 2O</strong></p>

  <p>The oxygen atoms then combine with oxygen molecules to form ozone:</p>

  <p><strong>O + O₂ → O₃</strong></p>

  <h3>Importance of the Ozone Layer</h3>

  <p>The ozone layer absorbs much of the Sun''s harmful ultraviolet radiation before it reaches Earth''s surface.</p>

  <p>This protection reduces excessive exposure of living organisms to ultraviolet radiation.</p>

  <h3>Ultraviolet Radiation</h3>

  <p>Excessive exposure to ultraviolet radiation can damage living cells and can cause problems such as skin damage and increased risk of skin cancer.</p>

  <h3>Key Idea</h3>

  <p>Ozone in the stratosphere is important because it absorbs harmful ultraviolet radiation from the Sun.</p>', 1),
  (984, 984, '<h2>Ozone Depletion</h2>

  <p><strong>Ozone depletion</strong> is the reduction in the concentration of ozone in the stratosphere.</p>

  <h3>Causes of Ozone Depletion</h3>

  <p>Certain chemicals can release reactive chlorine or bromine atoms in the stratosphere. These atoms can participate in reactions that destroy ozone molecules.</p>

  <p>One important group of ozone-depleting substances is <strong>chlorofluorocarbons (CFCs)</strong>, which were formerly used in applications such as refrigeration and aerosol propellants.</p>

  <h3>Effect on Ozone</h3>

  <p>Ultraviolet radiation can break down CFC molecules and release chlorine atoms.</p>

  <p>The chlorine can react with ozone, reducing the amount of O₃ present. A single chlorine atom can participate in repeated reactions, allowing it to destroy many ozone molecules.</p>

  <h3>Effects of Ozone Depletion</h3>

  <p>When the ozone layer becomes thinner, more ultraviolet radiation can reach Earth''s surface.</p>

  <p>Increased ultraviolet exposure can:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>damage living cells;</li>
    <li>increase the risk of skin cancer;</li>
    <li>cause eye damage such as cataracts;</li>
    <li>damage some plants and aquatic organisms.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Control of Ozone Depletion</h3>

  <p>Ozone depletion can be reduced by controlling the production and use of ozone-depleting substances and replacing them with safer alternatives.</p>

  <h3>Key Idea</h3>

  <p>Ozone depletion reduces the protection provided by stratospheric ozone and can increase the amount of harmful ultraviolet radiation reaching Earth''s surface.</p>', 1),
  (985, 985, '<h2>Sustainable Chemistry</h2>

  <p><strong>Sustainable chemistry</strong> involves designing and using chemical processes and substances in ways that reduce harm to people and the environment while making efficient use of resources.</p>

  <h3>Reducing Waste</h3>

  <p>Chemical processes should produce as little unwanted waste as possible.</p>

  <p>Reducing waste at the source is generally better than producing waste and treating it afterwards.</p>

  <h3>Efficient Use of Resources</h3>

  <p>Sustainable chemical processes aim to use raw materials, energy and water efficiently.</p>

  <p>For example, a process that requires less energy can reduce fuel consumption and associated emissions.</p>

  <h3>Safer Chemicals</h3>

  <p>Where possible, substances that are less toxic and less harmful to the environment should be used instead of more hazardous alternatives.</p>

  <h3>Renewable Resources</h3>

  <p>Renewable raw materials can be used where appropriate instead of resources that are rapidly depleted.</p>

  <h3>Recycling and Reuse</h3>

  <p>Materials can sometimes be recovered, reused or recycled instead of being discarded.</p>

  <h3>Key Idea</h3>

  <p>Sustainable chemistry aims to prevent pollution and reduce environmental harm by making chemical processes safer, more efficient and less wasteful.</p>', 1),
  (986, 986, '<h2>Waste Management</h2>

  <p><strong>Waste management</strong> is the proper handling, treatment, recovery and disposal of waste to reduce harm to people and the environment.</p>

  <h3>Types of Waste</h3>

  <p>Waste can include:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>domestic waste;</li>
    <li>industrial waste;</li>
    <li>agricultural waste;</li>
    <li>chemical waste;</li>
    <li>plastic waste;</li>
    <li>electronic waste.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Reduce</h3>

  <p><strong>Reducing</strong> waste means using fewer materials and avoiding unnecessary consumption.</p>

  <p>For example, using only the amount of a chemical required for an experiment reduces chemical waste.</p>

  <h3>Reuse</h3>

  <p><strong>Reusing</strong> means using an item again instead of throwing it away.</p>

  <p>Reuse reduces the demand for new materials and decreases the amount of waste produced.</p>

  <h3>Recycle</h3>

  <p><strong>Recycling</strong> involves collecting and processing waste materials so that they can be used to make new products.</p>

  <h3>Safe Disposal</h3>

  <p>Some wastes cannot be safely reused or recycled and require appropriate treatment and disposal.</p>

  <p>Hazardous chemical waste should not be poured into drains or released directly into the environment.</p>

  <h3>Key Idea</h3>

  <p>Good waste management aims to prevent waste where possible, recover useful materials and safely handle waste that remains.</p>', 1),
  (987, 987, '
  <h2>What Is a Cell?</h2>

  <h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

  <p>
    A <b>cell</b> is the smallest unit of a living organism that can carry out
    the processes needed for life.
  </p>

  <p>
    This means that a cell is not simply a tiny part of an organism.
    It is a living unit with structures that allow it to obtain substances,
    use energy, remove wastes, grow and respond to its surroundings.
  </p>

  <h3>Think of a Cell as a Living System</h3>

  <p>
    Imagine a cell as a very small, organised workshop.
    Different structures inside it have different jobs, but they work together
    to keep the cell alive.
  </p>

  <p>
    For example, the cell needs a boundary to control what enters and leaves,
    a region where many chemical reactions occur, and genetic material that
    contains instructions for the cell.
  </p>

  <h3>Cells Are Extremely Small</h3>

  <p>
    Most cells cannot be seen clearly with the naked eye. They are usually
    measured in <b>micrometres (µm)</b>.
  </p>

  <p>
    One micrometre is one millionth of a metre:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>1 µm = 0.000001 m</b>
  </p>

  <h3>Why Does the Cell Matter?</h3>

  <p>
    Every part of a living organism is ultimately made from cells.
    The ability of cells to carry out life processes is therefore the
    foundation of life in organisms.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Question:</b> Why is a cell described as a unit of life?
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Step 1:</b> A living organism must carry out processes such as obtaining
    substances, releasing energy and removing waste.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Step 2:</b> A cell contains structures that allow these processes to occur.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Conclusion:</b> A cell is called a unit of life because it is the smallest
    organised unit capable of carrying out the processes necessary for life.
  </p>

  <h3>KEY IDEA</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Cell = the basic living unit from which organisms are built.</b>
  </p>
  ', 1),
  (988, 988, '
  <h2>Why Cells Are the Basic Units of Life</h2>

  <h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

  <p>
    Saying that cells are the <b>basic units of life</b> means that the
    essential activities of living organisms are carried out by cells.
  </p>

  <p>
    A living organism must obtain substances, release energy, remove wastes,
    respond to changes and maintain conditions that allow life to continue.
    Cells are the units that perform these functions.
  </p>

  <h3>Cells Perform Life Processes</h3>

  <p>
    Depending on the type of cell and organism, cells are involved in processes
    such as:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>obtaining nutrients or raw materials,</li>
    <li>releasing and using energy,</li>
    <li>removing waste products,</li>
    <li>growth and repair,</li>
    <li>responding to changes in the environment, and</li>
    <li>reproduction or cell division.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Cells Do Different Jobs</h3>

  <p>
    Not every cell performs exactly the same task.
    A red blood cell is adapted for transporting oxygen, while a root hair cell
    is adapted for absorbing water and mineral ions.
  </p>

  <p>
    Their jobs are different, but both are living cells carrying out processes
    that support the organism.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Question:</b> A student says, "The heart is the basic unit of life."
    Is this correct?
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Step 1:</b> The heart is an organ.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Step 2:</b> An organ is made from different tissues, and tissues are
    made from cells.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Step 3:</b> The cells carry out the biological processes that allow the
    heart to function.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Conclusion:</b> The heart is not the basic unit of life.
    The cell is the basic unit of life.
  </p>

  <h3>KEY IDEA</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Cells are the basic units of life because living processes occur within
    cells.</b>
  </p>
  ', 1),
  (989, 989, '
  <h2>Cell Membrane</h2>

  <h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

  <p>
    The <b>cell membrane</b> is a thin boundary surrounding the cell.
    It separates the contents of the cell from its surroundings.
  </p>

  <h3>Its Main Function</h3>

  <p>
    The membrane controls the movement of substances <b>into and out of the
    cell</b>.
  </p>

  <p>
    This is important because a cell cannot allow every substance in its
    surroundings to enter freely.
  </p>

  <p>
    The cell needs some substances, such as oxygen and nutrients, but it may
    need to prevent or control the entry of other substances.
  </p>

  <h3>Selective Permeability</h3>

  <p>
    The cell membrane is described as <b>selectively permeable</b> because
    different substances cross it in different ways and at different rates.
  </p>

  <p>
    The membrane therefore helps the cell maintain suitable internal
    conditions.
  </p>

  <h3>Connection to Transport</h3>

  <p>
    Processes such as <b>diffusion, osmosis and active transport</b> involve
    movement across cell membranes.
  </p>

  <p>
    We will study these processes separately because they are different
    mechanisms.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Question:</b> Why would a cell be damaged if its membrane could no
    longer control what enters and leaves?
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Step 1:</b> Cells need particular substances in suitable amounts.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Step 2:</b> The membrane controls movement of substances.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Step 3:</b> If this control is lost, substances could enter or leave
    in inappropriate amounts.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Conclusion:</b> The cell could lose the conditions needed for normal
    functioning and may eventually be damaged.
  </p>

  <h3>KEY IDEA</h3>

  <p>
    <b>The cell membrane controls movement of substances into and out of the cell.</b>
  </p>
  ', 1),
  (990, 990, '
  <h2>Cytoplasm</h2>

  <h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

  <p>
    The <b>cytoplasm</b> is the living material inside the cell membrane,
    surrounding the cell''s internal structures.
  </p>

  <p>
    It contains a watery substance called the <b>cytosol</b> together with
    dissolved substances and structures found within the cell.
  </p>

  <h3>What Happens in the Cytoplasm?</h3>

  <p>
    Many of the chemical reactions needed by the cell occur in the cytoplasm.
  </p>

  <p>
    These reactions are part of the cell''s metabolism.
    The substances involved in these reactions must be able to move and
    interact in the cell''s internal environment.
  </p>

  <h3>Do Not Confuse Cytoplasm With the Cell Membrane</h3>

  <p>
    The <b>cell membrane</b> is the boundary around the cell.
  </p>

  <p>
    The <b>cytoplasm</b> is the internal living material within that boundary.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Question:</b> Why is the cytoplasm important even though it does not
    have the same job as an organ such as the nucleus?
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Step 1:</b> Cells need chemical reactions to remain alive.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Step 2:</b> Many of these reactions occur in the cytoplasm.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Step 3:</b> Therefore, the cytoplasm provides an important environment
    for cellular reactions.
  </p>

  <h3>KEY IDEA</h3>

  <p>
    <b>The cytoplasm is the internal living material where many cellular
    chemical reactions occur.</b>
  </p>
  ', 1),
  (991, 991, '
  <h2>Nucleus</h2>

  <h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

  <p>
    The <b>nucleus</b> is a membrane-bound structure found in many eukaryotic
    cells. It contains most of the cell''s genetic material.
  </p>

  <h3>Genetic Material</h3>

  <p>
    Genetic material contains instructions that influence how a cell develops,
    functions and reproduces.
  </p>

  <p>
    The genetic material is organised into structures called
    <b>chromosomes</b>.
  </p>

  <h3>Why Is the Nucleus Important?</h3>

  <p>
    The information in the nucleus helps regulate the activities of the cell.
    It is therefore important in processes such as growth, cell division and
    the production of proteins.
  </p>

  <h3>Important Precision</h3>

  <p>
    Do not think of the nucleus as physically performing every reaction in
    the cell.
    Its major importance is that it contains genetic information used in
    controlling cellular activities.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Question:</b> Why can damage to the nucleus seriously affect a cell?
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Step 1:</b> The nucleus contains genetic information.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Step 2:</b> Genetic information is needed to regulate many cellular
    activities.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Step 3:</b> Damage to this information or its control can interfere
    with normal cell functioning.
  </p>

  <h3>KEY IDEA</h3>

  <p>
    <b>The nucleus contains genetic material that helps control cellular
    activities.</b>
  </p>
  ', 1),
  (992, 992, '
  <h2>Mitochondria</h2>

  <h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Mitochondria</b> are structures in eukaryotic cells where important
    stages of aerobic respiration take place.
  </p>

  <p>
    Aerobic respiration releases energy from substances such as glucose
    using oxygen.
  </p>

  <h3>Why Do Cells Need Mitochondria?</h3>

  <p>
    Cells need a usable supply of energy for processes such as active
    transport, movement, growth, repair and synthesis of substances.
  </p>

  <p>
    Mitochondria are therefore particularly numerous in cells that have
    high energy demands.
  </p>

  <h3>Structure and Function</h3>

  <p>
    A mitochondrion has an inner membrane with many folds.
    These folds provide a large surface area for reactions involved in
    aerobic respiration.
  </p>

  <h3>Important Correction</h3>

  <p>
    It is common to hear the phrase
    <b>"mitochondria are the powerhouse of the cell."</b>
    This is only a rough analogy.
  </p>

  <p>
    A better scientific description is:
    <b>mitochondria are sites where important stages of aerobic respiration
    occur and where energy is transferred into usable cellular forms.</b>
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Question:</b> Why would a muscle cell contain many mitochondria?
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Step 1:</b> Muscle cells require large amounts of energy for contraction.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Step 2:</b> Aerobic respiration releases energy that can be used by
    the cell.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Step 3:</b> Mitochondria are sites of important stages of aerobic
    respiration.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Conclusion:</b> Muscle cells need many mitochondria because of their
    high energy demand.
  </p>

  <h3>KEY IDEA</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Mitochondria are sites of important stages of aerobic respiration.</b>
  </p>
  ', 1),
  (993, 993, '
  <h2>Ribosomes</h2>

  <h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Ribosomes</b> are structures where proteins are made.
  </p>

  <p>
    Proteins are essential because they perform many different roles in cells.
    They can form structures, act as enzymes, transport substances and perform
    other biological functions.
  </p>

  <h3>Why Does the Cell Need to Make Proteins?</h3>

  <p>
    Cells constantly need proteins for growth, repair, replacement and
    normal functioning.
  </p>

  <p>
    The information needed to make particular proteins ultimately comes from
    genes.
  </p>

  <p>
    The ribosome uses this information to assemble amino acids into a protein.
  </p>

  <h3>Ribosomes Are Not the Same as the Nucleus</h3>

  <p>
    The nucleus contains genetic information.
  </p>

  <p>
    Ribosomes are the structures where proteins are assembled.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Question:</b> A growing cell needs to produce many new proteins.
    Which cell structure is directly responsible for assembling those proteins?
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Step 1:</b> Proteins are built from amino acids.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Step 2:</b> Ribosomes join amino acids together in the correct sequence.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Conclusion:</b> Ribosomes are directly involved in protein synthesis.
  </p>

  <h3>KEY IDEA</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Ribosomes are the sites of protein synthesis.</b>
  </p>
  ', 1),
  (994, 994, '
  <h2>Cell Wall</h2>

  <h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

  <p>
    A <b>cell wall</b> is a strong structure found outside the cell membrane
    of plant cells.
  </p>

  <p>
    The plant cell wall is mainly made of <b>cellulose</b>.
    It is not the same structure as the cell membrane.
  </p>

  <h3>What Does the Cell Wall Do?</h3>

  <p>
    The cell wall provides <b>strength and support</b> to the plant cell.
  </p>

  <p>
    It also helps the cell maintain its shape.
    When water enters a plant cell, the cell wall resists excessive expansion
    and helps the cell become firm.
  </p>

  <h3>Cell Wall vs Cell Membrane</h3>

  <table>
    <tr>
      <th>Cell wall</th>
      <th>Cell membrane</th>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Strong outer layer of a plant cell</td>
      <td>Thin boundary around the cell</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Mainly made of cellulose</td>
      <td>Made mainly of lipids and proteins</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Provides strength and support</td>
      <td>Controls movement of substances</td>
    </tr>
  </table>

  <h3>Important Point</h3>

  <p>
    The cell wall does <b>not</b> replace the cell membrane.
    A plant cell has both.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Question:</b> Why does a plant cell need a cell wall in addition to
    its cell membrane?
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Step 1:</b> The membrane controls movement of substances.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Step 2:</b> The cell also needs mechanical strength and support.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Step 3:</b> The cell wall provides this strength while the membrane
    performs its transport-control function.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Conclusion:</b> The two structures have different functions and work
    together.
  </p>

  <h3>KEY IDEA</h3>

  <p>
    <b>The cellulose cell wall strengthens and supports plant cells.</b>
  </p>
  ', 1),
  (995, 995, '
  <h2>Large Permanent Vacuole</h2>

  <h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

  <p>
    Many mature plant cells contain a large <b>permanent vacuole</b>.
    It is surrounded by a membrane and contains a solution called
    <b>cell sap</b>.
  </p>

  <h3>What Does the Vacuole Do?</h3>

  <p>
    The vacuole stores water and dissolved substances such as sugars and
    mineral ions.
  </p>

  <p>
    It also plays an important role in maintaining the pressure inside
    plant cells.
  </p>

  <h3>Turgor</h3>

  <p>
    When a plant cell contains plenty of water, water pushes the cell contents
    against the cell wall.
    This creates <b>turgor pressure</b>.
  </p>

  <p>
    Turgor helps plant tissues remain firm.
  </p>

  <p>
    If plant cells lose too much water, turgor decreases and the plant may
    become <b>wilted</b>.
  </p>

  <h3>Why Is This Different From an Animal Cell?</h3>

  <p>
    Animal cells do not normally have one large permanent vacuole occupying
    most of the cell.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Question:</b> Why can a plant wilt when it loses too much water?
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Step 1:</b> Water is stored in the plant cell''s vacuole.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Step 2:</b> Water helps maintain turgor pressure.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Step 3:</b> When too much water is lost, turgor pressure falls.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Conclusion:</b> Cells become less firm, causing the plant tissues
    to lose their rigidity and wilt.
  </p>

  <h3>KEY IDEA</h3>

  <p>
    <b>The large permanent vacuole stores cell sap and helps maintain
    turgor in plant cells.</b>
  </p>
  ', 1),
  (996, 996, '
  <h2>Structures Shared by Plant and Animal Cells</h2>

  <h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

  <p>
    Plant and animal cells are different, but they also share several
    important structures.
  </p>

  <p>
    Both types of cells are eukaryotic cells and commonly contain:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><b>cell membrane</b></li>
    <li><b>cytoplasm</b></li>
    <li><b>nucleus</b></li>
    <li><b>mitochondria</b></li>
    <li><b>ribosomes</b></li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Why Do They Share These Structures?</h3>

  <p>
    Plant and animal cells are both living cells.
    They therefore need structures that perform fundamental cellular
    functions.
  </p>

  <p>
    For example:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Cell membrane</b>
    → controls movement of substances.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Cytoplasm</b>
    → provides an environment for many chemical reactions.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Nucleus</b>
    → contains genetic material.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Mitochondria</b>
    → carry out important stages of aerobic respiration.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Ribosomes</b>
    → make proteins.
  </p>

  <h3>Important Distinction</h3>

  <p>
    Saying that plant and animal cells share these structures does not mean
    that the cells are identical.
  </p>

  <p>
    Plant cells also have structures such as the cell wall, chloroplasts and
    a large permanent vacuole that are not characteristic of typical animal
    cells.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Question:</b> Why do both plant and animal cells need mitochondria?
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Step 1:</b> Both plant and animal cells require usable energy.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Step 2:</b> Mitochondria are sites of important stages of aerobic
    respiration.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Conclusion:</b> Both types of cells can contain mitochondria because
    both require energy for cellular processes.
  </p>

  <h3>KEY IDEA</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Plant and animal cells share structures required for fundamental
    cellular functions.</b>
  </p>
  ', 1),
  (997, 997, '
  <h2>Plant Cell vs Animal Cell</h2>

  <h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

  <p>
    Plant and animal cells share many structures, but some structures differ
    because the cells perform different functions.
  </p>

  <table>
    <tr>
      <th>Structure</th>
      <th>Typical plant cell</th>
      <th>Typical animal cell</th>
    </tr>

    <tr>
      <td>Cell membrane</td>
      <td>Present</td>
      <td>Present</td>
    </tr>

    <tr>
      <td>Cytoplasm</td>
      <td>Present</td>
      <td>Present</td>
    </tr>

    <tr>
      <td>Nucleus</td>
      <td>Present in most cells</td>
      <td>Present in most cells</td>
    </tr>

    <tr>
      <td>Mitochondria</td>
      <td>Present</td>
      <td>Present</td>
    </tr>

    <tr>
      <td>Ribosomes</td>
      <td>Present</td>
      <td>Present</td>
    </tr>

    <tr>
      <td>Cell wall</td>
      <td>Present</td>
      <td>Absent</td>
    </tr>

    <tr>
      <td>Chloroplasts</td>
      <td>Present in photosynthetic cells</td>
      <td>Absent</td>
    </tr>

    <tr>
      <td>Large permanent vacuole</td>
      <td>Common in mature plant cells</td>
      <td>Absent as a typical large permanent vacuole</td>
    </tr>
  </table>

  <h3>Do Not Memorise the Table Without Understanding It</h3>

  <p>
    The differences exist because plant cells and animal cells have different
    requirements.
  </p>

  <p>
    A photosynthetic plant cell needs chloroplasts to capture light energy.
    A plant cell also benefits from a rigid cell wall and a large vacuole that
    helps maintain turgor.
  </p>

  <p>
    Animal cells do not need these structures for the same reasons.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Question:</b> A cell has a cell wall, chloroplasts and a large permanent
    vacuole. Is it likely to be a typical plant cell or animal cell?
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Step 1:</b> Animal cells do not have a cellulose cell wall.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Step 2:</b> Chloroplasts are characteristic of photosynthetic plant cells.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Step 3:</b> A large permanent vacuole is also characteristic of many
    mature plant cells.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Conclusion:</b> The cell is a plant cell.
  </p>

  <h3>KEY IDEA</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Plant and animal cells share basic structures but differ in structures
    adapted to their different functions.</b>
  </p>
  ', 1),
  (998, 998, '
  <h2>Explaining Cell Differences</h2>

  <h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

  <p>
    Knowing that plant and animal cells are different is only the first step.
    A stronger biological explanation connects a structure to its function.
  </p>

  <h3>Example 1 — Cell Wall</h3>

  <p>
    Plant cells have a cellulose cell wall.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Structure:</b> strong cellulose wall.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Function:</b> provides mechanical support and helps the cell resist
    excessive expansion.
  </p>

  <h3>Example 2 — Chloroplast</h3>

  <p>
    Photosynthetic plant cells contain chloroplasts.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Structure:</b> chloroplast containing chlorophyll.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Function:</b> captures light energy used in photosynthesis.
  </p>

  <h3>Example 3 — Large Permanent Vacuole</h3>

  <p>
    Many mature plant cells have a large permanent vacuole.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Structure:</b> large compartment containing cell sap.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Function:</b> stores water and dissolved substances and helps maintain
    turgor pressure.
  </p>

  <h3>The Key Biological Skill</h3>

  <p>
    When explaining a biological structure, do not stop at naming it.
    Explain:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>STRUCTURE → FUNCTION → ADVANTAGE</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    This is how we explain why a cell has a particular adaptation.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Question:</b> Explain why a photosynthetic leaf cell contains
    chloroplasts.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Step 1:</b> Chloroplasts contain chlorophyll.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Step 2:</b> Chlorophyll absorbs light energy.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Step 3:</b> Light energy is required for photosynthesis.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Conclusion:</b> The chloroplast enables the cell to capture light
    energy needed for photosynthesis.
  </p>

  <h3>KEY IDEA</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Biology explanations should connect a structure to what it does and
    why that function matters.</b>
  </p>
  ', 1),
  (999, 999, '
<h2>Growth by Increase in Cell Number</h2>

<h3>What does increase in cell number mean?</h3>

<p>
When cells divide repeatedly, the total number of cells in a multicellular organism
or tissue can increase.
</p>

<p>
This is one important way multicellular organisms grow.
</p>

<h3>Following one cell division</h3>

<p>
1 cell → 2 cells
</p>

<p>
If both daughter cells divide again:
</p>

<p>
2 cells → 4 cells
</p>

<p>
If all four divide:
</p>

<p>
4 cells → 8 cells
</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> One cell undergoes three rounds of mitotic division. Assume every
daughter cell divides during each round. How many cells are produced?</p>

<p><b>Round 1:</b> 1 × 2 = 2 cells.</p>
<p><b>Round 2:</b> 2 × 2 = 4 cells.</p>
<p><b>Round 3:</b> 4 × 2 = 8 cells.</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b> 8 cells.</p>

<h3>Key idea</h3>
<p>
Repeated mitosis can cause cell number to increase rapidly.
</p>
