<h2>Egg Cell</h2>

<h3>What is an egg cell?</h3>

<p>
An <b>egg cell</b> is a female reproductive cell. It contains genetic
material and can fuse with a sperm cell during fertilisation.
</p>

<h3>How is it specialised?</h3>

<p>
The egg cell is relatively large and contains a substantial amount
of cytoplasm.
</p>

<p>
Its cytoplasm contains substances that support the early stages after
fertilisation before the embryo can obtain nutrients from its developing
environment.
</p>

<h3>Why is the nucleus important?</h3>

<p>
The nucleus contains the egg cell''s genetic material.
During fertilisation, genetic material from the sperm and egg combine.
</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> Why is the egg cell larger than a sperm cell?</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> The egg needs to contain enough cytoplasm and stored
resources to support the early stages after fertilisation.</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> A sperm cell is specialised mainly for movement and
delivery of its genetic material.</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b> The egg cell is larger because it contains substantial
cytoplasm and resources needed during the early stages following
fertilisation.</p>
