<h2>Root Hair Cell Adaptation</h2>

<h3>Why is the root hair cell specialised?</h3>

<p>
A root hair cell is specialised for absorbing water and mineral ions
from the soil.
</p>

<h3>Surface area</h3>

<p>
The long root hair provides a large surface area for contact with
water films surrounding soil particles.
</p>

<h3>Thin cell boundary</h3>

<p>
The cell has a thin surface through which water can move into the cell.
This reduces the distance over which substances need to move.
</p>

<h3>Mineral ion absorption</h3>

<p>
Mineral ions may be present in the soil at lower concentrations than
inside root cells. Plants can therefore use <b>active transport</b>
to absorb some mineral ions against a concentration gradient.
</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> Why can a plant still absorb some mineral ions when
their concentration in the soil is lower than inside the root?</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Movement from low concentration to high concentration
cannot occur by diffusion alone.</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Root cells can use active transport.</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> Active transport requires energy.</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b> Root cells can use active transport to move mineral ions
against their concentration gradient using energy.</p>
