<h2>Organ</h2>

<h3>What is an organ?</h3>

<p>
An <b>organ</b> is a structure made from different tissues working
together to perform one or more specific functions.
</p>

<h3>Why does an organ contain different tissues?</h3>

<p>
Different tissues perform different tasks. Working together allows
the organ to carry out a more complex function.
</p>

<h3>Example: the heart</h3>

<p>
The heart contains several types of tissue. Muscle tissue contracts,
while other tissues provide support and allow the heart to connect
with blood vessels and nervous control systems.
</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> Why is the heart classified as an organ rather
than a tissue?</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> A tissue is mainly a group of similar cells performing
a related function.</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> The heart contains different tissues.</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> These tissues work together to pump blood.</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b> The heart is an organ because it contains different
tissues working together to perform a specific function.</p>
