<h2>Mitosis and Replacement of Worn-Out Cells</h2>

<h3>Why must cells be replaced?</h3>

<p>
Cells do not all remain functional forever. Some become damaged, worn out or
reach the end of their normal lifespan.
</p>

<p>
In tissues where cells are continually renewed, cell division produces new cells
that replace cells that have been lost.
</p>

<h3>Example</h3>

<p>
Cells in the lining of the digestive tract are continually exposed to mechanical
and chemical conditions that can damage cells. New cells are produced to maintain
the tissue.
</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> Why is continual cell division useful in tissues where cells
are frequently lost?</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Some cells are lost or become unable to function properly.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> The tissue needs replacement cells.</p>
<p><b>Step 3:</b> Mitosis produces new cells.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> Mitosis helps maintain the required number of functioning cells.</p>

<h3>Key distinction</h3>
<p>
Mitosis does not simply happen because a cell is “old.” Cell division is controlled
by the needs and signals of the tissue.
</p>
