<h2>Homologous Chromosomes</h2>

<h3>What are homologous chromosomes?</h3>

<p>
Homologous chromosomes are a pair of chromosomes that carry the same types of
genes at corresponding positions.
</p>

<p>
One chromosome of the pair is inherited from the mother and the other from the
father.
</p>

<h3>Are they identical?</h3>

<p>
They are similar in structure and carry genes for the same characteristics, but
they may carry different versions of those genes.
</p>

<p>
These different versions are called <b>alleles</b>.
</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> A human cell has 23 pairs of chromosomes. What does each pair
represent?</p>

<p>
Each pair consists of homologous chromosomes. One chromosome was inherited from
the mother and the corresponding chromosome from the father.
</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b> The 23 pairs are homologous chromosome pairs.</p>

<h3>Why does this matter in meiosis?</h3>

<p>
Homologous chromosomes behave as pairs during the first meiotic division. Their
separation contributes to reducing the chromosome number.
</p>
