</p>

<p>
<b>Meiosis</b> produces haploid cells involved in sexual reproduction.
</p>

<h3>Number of divisions</h3>

<p>
Mitosis involves one nuclear division.
</p>

<p>
Meiosis involves two successive nuclear divisions.
</p>

<h3>Number of resulting cells</h3>

<p>
Mitosis normally produces two daughter cells.
</p>

<p>
Meiosis normally produces four cells.
</p>

<h3>Chromosome number</h3>

<p>
Mitosis normally maintains chromosome number.
</p>

<p>
Meiosis reduces chromosome number by half.
</p>

<h3>What separates?</h3>

<p>
In mitosis, sister chromatids separate during anaphase.
</p>

<p>
In Meiosis I, homologous chromosomes separate.
</p>

<p>
In Meiosis II, sister chromatids separate.
</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> A process starts with one diploid cell and produces four
haploid cells. Is it mitosis or meiosis?</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Four cells are produced.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> The cells are haploid.</p>
<p><b>Step 3:</b> The chromosome number has therefore been reduced.</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b> Meiosis.</p>

<h3>Core distinction</h3>

<p>
<b>Mitosis:</b> one division → two cells → chromosome number maintained.</p>

<p>
<b>Meiosis:</b> two divisions → four haploid cells → chromosome number reduced.
</p>
