  (1075, 1075, '
  <h2>Different Gametes Carry Different Genetic Combinations</h2>

  <h3>The problem with thinking of gametes as identical</h3>

  <p>
    It is incorrect to think that every sperm produced by a male contains
    exactly the same genetic combination.
  </p>

  <p>
    Likewise, the egg cells produced by a female can contain different
    combinations of genetic information.
  </p>

  <h3>How does this happen?</h3>

  <p>
    During meiosis, chromosome pairs are separated into different daughter
    cells.
  </p>

  <p>
    The chromosomes that enter one gamete are not necessarily the same
    combination as those entering another gamete.
  </p>

  <p>
    Therefore, meiosis can produce gametes that differ genetically from
    one another.
  </p>

  <h3>Simple example</h3>

  <p>
    Suppose a cell has two pairs of homologous chromosomes.
  </p>

  <p>
    For simplicity, represent the chromosome pairs as:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>A / a</b> and <b>B / b</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    A gamete receives one chromosome from each pair.
  </p>

  <p>
    Possible combinations include:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>AB</b>, <b>Ab</b>, <b>aB</b>, and <b>ab</b>.
  </p>

  <p>
    The important point is not the letters themselves. The important idea is
    that different gametes can receive different combinations of chromosomes.
  </p>

  <h3>Why this matters</h3>

  <p>
    If different gametes carry different genetic combinations, fertilisation
    can combine these different gametes in many ways.
  </p>

  <p>
    This contributes to genetic variation among offspring.
  </p>
