  <h2>Why Siblings Can Differ Genetically</h2>

  <h3>The question</h3>

  <p>
    If two children have the same biological parents, why are they usually
    not genetically identical?
  </p>

  <p>
    The answer is that each child results from a different combination of
    gametes.
  </p>

  <h3>What happens for the first child?</h3>

  <ol>
    <li>The father produces many sperm cells.</li>
    <li>The mother produces egg cells.</li>
    <li>One sperm and one egg take part in fertilisation.</li>
    <li>Their genetic information combines in the zygote.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>What happens for another child?</h3>

  <p>
    The same parents can produce different sperm and egg cells.
  </p>

  <p>
    A different sperm may fertilise a different egg.
  </p>

  <p>
    The second child therefore receives a different combination of genetic
    information.
  </p>

  <h3>Example</h3>

  <p>
    Imagine the first child results from:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Sperm A + Egg X</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    The second child might result from:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Sperm C + Egg Y</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    The two children have the same parents, but they did not receive exactly
    the same combination of gametes.
  </p>

  <h3>Important exception</h3>

  <p>
    Identical twins can be genetically very similar because they develop from
    the same fertilised egg that later separates into two embryos.
  </p>

  <h3>Remember</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Same parents does not mean the same combination of gametes.</b>
  </p>
