<h2>Light Intensity and Photosynthesis</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p><strong>Light intensity</strong> is the amount of light reaching a given area.</p>

<p>Because photosynthesis requires light energy, changing light intensity can change the rate of photosynthesis.</p>

<h3>At Low Light Intensity</h3>

<p>There is relatively little light energy available.</p>

<p>Increasing light intensity can increase the rate of photosynthesis.</p>

<h3>At Higher Light Intensity</h3>

<p>The rate of photosynthesis may eventually stop increasing.</p>

<p>This happens because another factor, such as carbon dioxide concentration or temperature, becomes limiting.</p>

<h3>Typical Graph</h3>

<p>When light intensity is plotted against the rate of photosynthesis:</p>

<p><strong>Low light → rate increases.</strong></p>

<p><strong>Higher light → rate approaches a plateau.</strong></p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A plant has the following results:</p>

<p>Light intensity = 10 units → photosynthesis rate = 2 units</p>

<p>Light intensity = 20 units → photosynthesis rate = 4 units</p>

<p>Light intensity = 40 units → photosynthesis rate = 6 units</p>

<p>Light intensity = 80 units → photosynthesis rate = 6 units</p>

<p><strong>What does the final result suggest?</strong></p>

<p>Increasing light from 40 to 80 units no longer increases the rate. Therefore, light is no longer the limiting factor.</p>

<h3>Exam Rule</h3>

<p>Do not say that more light always means faster photosynthesis.</p>

<p>The effect depends on whether light is currently the limiting factor.</p>
