<li>the units;</li>
<li>the overall trend;</li>
<li>any plateau;</li>
<li>any maximum or optimum;</li>
<li>unusual results.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Example: Light Intensity</h3>

<p>If the rate increases as light intensity increases, light may be limiting in that region.</p>

<p>If the graph becomes flat, increasing light no longer increases the rate.</p>

<h3>Example: Temperature</h3>

<p>If the rate rises and then falls, the graph suggests an optimum temperature.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A graph gives:</p>

<p>10 units light → 2 units rate</p>

<p>20 units light → 4 units rate</p>

<p>30 units light → 6 units rate</p>

<p>40 units light → 6 units rate</p>

<p><strong>What happens between 30 and 40 units?</strong></p>

<p>The rate remains constant.</p>

<p><strong>What does this suggest?</strong></p>

<p>Light is no longer limiting in that range.</p>

<h3>Evidence-Based Language</h3>

<p>Use phrases such as:</p>

<p><strong>"The data show..."</strong></p>

<p><strong>"The rate increases..."</strong></p>

<p><strong>"The rate reaches a plateau..."</strong></p>

<p><strong>"This suggests..."</strong></p>

<p>Avoid claiming more than the data demonstrate.</p>
