<h2>Upper Epidermis of a Leaf</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>The <strong>upper epidermis</strong> is the outer cell layer on the upper surface of many leaves.</p>

<h3>Protection</h3>

<p>The epidermis protects the internal tissues from physical damage and excessive water loss.</p>

<h3>Transparent Cells</h3>

<p>Upper epidermal cells usually contain few or no chloroplasts, allowing light to pass through towards the photosynthetic mesophyll beneath.</p>

<h3>Waxy Cuticle</h3>

<p>The outer surface is commonly covered by a waxy cuticle.</p>

<p>The cuticle reduces uncontrolled evaporation of water from the leaf surface.</p>

<h3>Why Does This Matter?</h3>

<p>The leaf needs enough water for normal cellular processes and photosynthesis while still allowing carbon dioxide to enter through stomata.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A plant has a damaged waxy cuticle.</p>

<p><strong>What is likely to increase?</strong></p>

<p>Uncontrolled water loss from the leaf surface can increase.</p>

<h3>Important Distinction</h3>

<p>The cuticle reduces water loss, but it is not the main pathway through which carbon dioxide enters the leaf.</p>

<p>Carbon dioxide mainly enters through stomata.</p>
