<h2>Spongy Mesophyll and Gas Diffusion</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>The <strong>spongy mesophyll</strong> is located below the palisade mesophyll in many leaves.</p>

<h3>Loosely Packed Cells</h3>

<p>Spongy mesophyll cells are arranged relatively loosely, leaving many internal air spaces.</p>

<h3>Air Spaces</h3>

<p>The air spaces provide pathways through which carbon dioxide can diffuse towards photosynthesising cells.</p>

<p>Oxygen produced during photosynthesis can also diffuse through these spaces towards stomata.</p>

<h3>Gas Exchange</h3>

<p>The internal air spaces increase the area of leaf cells exposed to internal air.</p>

<p>This supports efficient diffusion of gases within the leaf.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A leaf has very small internal air spaces.</p>

<p><strong>How could this affect gas movement?</strong></p>

<p>It could increase resistance to diffusion through the leaf and make movement of gases to and from photosynthetic cells less efficient.</p>

<h3>Key Structure → Function</h3>

<p><strong>Loosely arranged cells → large internal air spaces → shorter pathways for gas movement within the leaf.</strong></p>
