<h2>Leaf Veins and Photosynthesis</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Leaf veins contain vascular tissues that connect the leaf to the rest of the plant.</p>

<h3>Xylem in the Veins</h3>

<p>Xylem transports water and dissolved mineral ions into the leaf.</p>

<p>Water is needed as a raw material for photosynthesis.</p>

<h3>Phloem in the Veins</h3>

<p>Phloem transports organic assimilates such as sucrose away from or around the leaf according to source-sink relationships.</p>

<h3>Why Are Veins Spread Through the Leaf?</h3>

<p>A network of veins helps bring water relatively close to photosynthesising cells throughout the leaf.</p>

<p>It also provides pathways for distributing assimilates.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A leaf has damaged veins and begins to wilt while photosynthesis decreases.</p>

<p><strong>Give one reason photosynthesis may decrease.</strong></p>

<p>Damaged xylem can reduce water delivery to the leaf, which can limit photosynthesis.</p>

<h3>Important Distinction</h3>

<p>Xylem and phloem are both found in veins, but they transport different materials.</p>

<p><strong>Xylem → water and mineral ions.</strong></p>

<p><strong>Phloem → organic assimilates.</strong></p>
