<h2>Interpreting a Leaf Cross-Section</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>A leaf cross-section shows how different tissues are arranged to support photosynthesis and gas exchange.</p>

<h3>From Top to Bottom</h3>

<p>A typical terrestrial leaf can contain:</p>

<p><strong>Waxy cuticle → upper epidermis → palisade mesophyll → spongy mesophyll → vascular bundle → lower epidermis with stomata.</strong></p>

<h3>Upper Region</h3>

<p>The upper surface receives light.</p>

<p>The palisade mesophyll beneath it contains many chloroplasts.</p>

<h3>Middle Region</h3>

<p>The spongy mesophyll contains internal air spaces that facilitate gas diffusion.</p>

<h3>Vascular Bundle</h3>

<p>The vascular bundle contains xylem and phloem.</p>

<p>Xylem supplies water and mineral ions.</p>

<p>Phloem transports assimilates.</p>

<h3>Lower Region</h3>

<p>Stomata provide pathways for gas exchange and water vapour loss.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A student is shown a leaf cross-section and identifies a layer of tightly packed cells containing many chloroplasts.</p>

<p><strong>Which tissue is it most likely to be?</strong></p>

<p>Palisade mesophyll.</p>

<p><strong>Why?</strong></p>

<p>Palisade cells are commonly tightly packed beneath the upper epidermis and contain many chloroplasts for photosynthesis.</p>

<h3>Exam Skill</h3>

<p>When identifying a leaf tissue, use both its <strong>position</strong> and its <strong>structure</strong>.</p>
