<h2>Photosynthesis and Mineral Nutrition</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Plants need mineral ions from the soil for healthy growth and for making important substances.</p>

<p>Mineral ions are not all raw materials of the photosynthesis equation, but some are essential for the structures and molecules that allow photosynthesis to occur effectively.</p>

<h3>Magnesium</h3>

<p>Magnesium is needed for chlorophyll production.</p>

<p>Chlorophyll absorbs light energy used in photosynthesis.</p>

<p>A magnesium deficiency can therefore reduce chlorophyll formation and cause leaves to become pale or yellow.</p>

<h3>Nitrate Ions</h3>

<p>Nitrate ions provide nitrogen.</p>

<p>Nitrogen is needed to make amino acids and proteins.</p>

<h3>Why Does This Affect Photosynthesis?</h3>

<p>A plant with severe mineral deficiencies may have poor growth and reduced photosynthetic capacity.</p>

<p>For example, inadequate magnesium can reduce chlorophyll production.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>Two plants are grown under the same light intensity. One receives enough magnesium while the other receives very little.</p>

<p>The magnesium-deficient plant develops pale leaves.</p>

<p><strong>Explain why its photosynthetic performance may be reduced.</strong></p>

<p>Magnesium is needed for chlorophyll production. Less chlorophyll can reduce the plant''s ability to absorb light energy.</p>

<h3>Important Distinction</h3>

<p>Do not say magnesium is a raw material in the balanced photosynthesis equation.</p>

<p>It is a mineral nutrient required for plant processes, including chlorophyll production.</p>
