<h2>Destarching a Plant</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p><strong>Destarching</strong> is the process of removing or reducing stored starch from a plant before a photosynthesis experiment.</p>

<h3>Why Destarch a Plant?</h3>

<p>If a leaf already contains stored starch, a positive iodine test might not have resulted from photosynthesis occurring during the experiment.</p>

<p>Destarching helps make the starting conditions more controlled.</p>

<h3>How Is a Plant Destarched?</h3>

<p>The plant is kept in darkness for a sufficient period.</p>

<p>During darkness, photosynthesis stops.</p>

<p>The plant continues respiration and uses stored carbohydrates.</p>

<p>This reduces stored starch in the leaves.</p>

<h3>Then What?</h3>

<p>After destarching, the plant can be exposed to controlled experimental conditions.</p>

<p>Any starch formed during the investigation provides stronger evidence that photosynthesis occurred under those conditions.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A student wants to test whether light is necessary for starch formation.</p>

<p><strong>Why should the plant first be destarched?</strong></p>

<p>To reduce starch already present in the leaves so that the final starch result is more closely related to photosynthesis during the experiment.</p>

<h3>Exam Chain</h3>

<p><strong>Darkness → photosynthesis stops → respiration continues → stored carbohydrate is used → starch reduced.</strong></p>
