<h2>Temperature and Photosynthesis</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Photosynthesis involves enzyme-controlled reactions.</p>

<p>Temperature therefore affects the rate of photosynthesis.</p>

<h3>At Low Temperature</h3>

<p>Enzyme-controlled reactions occur more slowly.</p>

<p>As temperature increases, the rate of photosynthesis can increase.</p>

<h3>At an Optimum</h3>

<p>There is a temperature range at which photosynthesis occurs at a relatively high rate.</p>

<h3>At Excessively High Temperature</h3>

<p>Very high temperatures can damage or denature enzymes.</p>

<p>They can also cause excessive water loss and stomatal closure.</p>

<p>As a result, the rate of photosynthesis can decrease.</p>

<h3>Typical Graph</h3>

<p>Temperature against photosynthesis rate commonly produces a curve:</p>

<p><strong>Low temperature → rate increases.</strong></p>

<p><strong>Optimum temperature → highest rate.</strong></p>

<p><strong>Very high temperature → rate decreases.</strong></p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A plant has photosynthesis rates of:</p>

<p>10°C → 2 units</p>

<p>20°C → 5 units</p>

<p>30°C → 8 units</p>

<p>40°C → 3 units</p>

<p><strong>At which tested temperature is the rate highest?</strong></p>

<p>30°C.</p>

<p><strong>Why does the rate fall at 40°C?</strong></p>

<p>High temperature can damage enzymes and may also cause water stress and stomatal closure.</p>
  ', 1),
  (1122, 1122, '
<h2>Limiting Factors in Photosynthesis</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>A <strong>limiting factor</strong> is a factor that restricts the rate of a process when it is present at an insufficient level.</p>

<h3>Major Limiting Factors</h3>

<p>For photosynthesis, important limiting factors include:</p>

<ul>
<li>light intensity;</li>
<li>carbon dioxide concentration;</li>
<li>temperature;</li>
<li>water availability.</li>
</ul>

<h3>One Factor at a Time</h3>

<p>Suppose light intensity is low.</p>

<p>Increasing light can increase photosynthesis.</p>

<p>But after a certain point, increasing light further may have little effect because another factor has become limiting.</p>

<h3>Example</h3>

<p>A plant is receiving enough light but has very little carbon dioxide.</p>

<p>Increasing light will not necessarily increase photosynthesis significantly.</p>

<p>Carbon dioxide is the limiting factor.</p>

<h3>Why the Concept Matters</h3>

<p>Plants need several conditions simultaneously.</p>

<p>Improving one condition does not remove the effect of another condition that is still limiting.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A plant is tested at two light intensities:</p>

<p>Low light → rate = 3 units</p>

<p>High light → rate = 6 units</p>

<p>The carbon dioxide concentration is then increased while high light is maintained, and the rate rises to 9 units.</p>

<p><strong>What does this suggest?</strong></p>

<p>Light was limiting at the lower intensity, while carbon dioxide was limiting under the later high-light condition.</p>

<h3>Exam Rule</h3>

<p><strong>Increasing a factor only increases the rate if that factor is limiting.</strong></p>
