  (1125, 1125, '
  <h2>Solving an ABO Blood Group Inheritance Problem</h2>

  <p>
    ABO inheritance problems are solved by first converting the blood groups of
    the parents into possible genotypes.
  </p>

  <h3>Example</h3>

  <p>
    A mother has blood group A and genotype:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>I<sup>A</sup>i</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    The father has blood group B and genotype:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>I<sup>B</sup>i</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    Cross:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>I<sup>A</sup>i × I<sup>B</sup>i</b>
  </p>

  <h3>Step 1: Find the mother''s gametes</h3>

  <p>
    The mother can produce:
  </p>

  <p>
    I<sup>A</sup> or i
  </p>

  <h3>Step 2: Find the father''s gametes</h3>

  <p>
    The father can produce:
  </p>

  <p>
    I<sup>B</sup> or i
  </p>

  <h3>Step 3: Combine the alleles</h3>

  <p>
    The possible genotypes are:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>I<sup>A</sup>I<sup>B</sup></li>
    <li>I<sup>A</sup>i</li>
    <li>I<sup>B</sup>i</li>
    <li>ii</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Step 4: Convert genotype to blood group</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>I<sup>A</sup>I<sup>B</sup> → AB</li>
    <li>I<sup>A</sup>i → A</li>
    <li>I<sup>B</sup>i → B</li>
    <li>ii → O</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Therefore, in this cross, offspring can have blood group A, B, AB or O.
  </p>

  <h3>Important</h3>

  <p>
    The calculation gives possible outcomes and their probabilities. It does
    not mean that every family must produce one child of every blood group.
  </p>
  ', 1),
