  (1127, 1127, '
  <h2>Why Blood Groups Matter in Transfusion</h2>

  <p>
    A blood transfusion involves giving blood or blood components from a donor
    to a recipient.
  </p>

  <p>
    The donor''s red blood cells carry molecules called <b>antigens</b> on their
    surfaces. The recipient''s plasma contains <b>antibodies</b> that can
    recognise particular antigens.
  </p>

  <p>
    If incompatible red blood cells are given, antibodies can bind to the donor
    cells and cause them to <b>agglutinate</b> (clump).
  </p>

  <p>
    This can be dangerous because the clumped cells can interfere with normal
    blood circulation and may be destroyed.
  </p>

  <h3>Why matching matters</h3>

  <p>
    Before a transfusion, blood is tested to help ensure compatibility between
    donor and recipient.
  </p>

  <p>
    The ABO group and Rhesus status are important parts of this compatibility
    testing.
  </p>

  <h3>Important</h3>

  <p>
    Blood transfusion is not simply a matter of matching the letters. Actual
    transfusion decisions require proper blood grouping, crossmatching and
    clinical procedures.
  </p>
  ', 1),
  (1128, 1128, '
  <h2>Reading a Blood Transfusion Compatibility Problem</h2>

  <p>
    When given a blood-transfusion question, do not guess from the blood-group
    letters. Follow the antigen-antibody relationship.
  </p>

  <h3>Step 1: Identify the recipient</h3>

  <p>
    Determine the recipient''s ABO group.
  </p>

  <h3>Step 2: Identify the recipient''s antibodies</h3>

  <p>
    For example, a group A recipient has anti-B antibodies in the plasma.
  </p>

  <h3>Step 3: Identify the donor red-cell antigen</h3>

  <p>
    Group B red cells carry B antigen.
  </p>

  <h3>Step 4: Check for a matching antibody-antigen reaction</h3>

  <p>
    The group A recipient has anti-B antibodies and group B donor cells carry
    B antigen.
  </p>

  <p>
    Therefore, those donor red cells are incompatible with the recipient in the
    basic ABO red-cell model.
  </p>

  <h3>Reasoning chain</h3>

  <p>
    Recipient A → anti-B present → donor B cells carry B antigen →
    antibody-antigen reaction → incompatible.
  </p>

  <p>
    This reasoning method is more reliable than trying to memorise isolated
    donor-recipient pairs.
  </p>
