<h2>Diastole and Ventricular Filling</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
Diastole is the relaxation phase of the heart.
During this phase, the chambers can fill with blood.
</p>

<h3>What happens during ventricular filling?</h3>

<p>
Blood returning from the body enters the right atrium.
Blood returning from the lungs enters the left atrium.
</p>

<p>
As the ventricles relax, blood flows from the atria into the ventricles through the atrioventricular valves.
</p>

<h3>Why does blood flow without the ventricles contracting?</h3>

<p>
Blood moves because of pressure differences.
When the ventricles relax, ventricular pressure falls.
If atrial pressure is higher, blood flows from the atria into the ventricles.
</p>

<h3>Why is filling important?</h3>

<p>
The ventricles must contain blood before they contract.
The amount of blood entering the ventricles contributes to the amount available to be pumped during the next contraction.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p><strong>Question:</strong> Why does blood enter the ventricles during diastole?</p>

<p>
The ventricles relax, causing their pressure to fall. Blood therefore flows from the atria into the ventricles because of the pressure difference.
</p>
', 1),
  (1142, 1142, '
<h2>The Pulse</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
The pulse is the rhythmic expansion of an artery caused by pressure changes produced when the heart pumps blood.
</p>

<h3>Why can a pulse be felt?</h3>

<p>
During ventricular systole, blood is forced into the arteries.
This creates a pressure wave that travels along the artery wall.
</p>

<p>
The expansion can be detected at certain points where an artery lies close to the skin.
</p>

<h3>Common pulse points</h3>

<p>
A pulse can commonly be felt at the wrist or side of the neck.
</p>

<h3>Pulse rate</h3>

<p>
Pulse rate is normally closely related to heart rate because each ventricular contraction produces a pulse wave in the arterial system.
</p>

<h3>Pulse during exercise</h3>

<p>
Exercise increases the demand for oxygen in active muscles.
The heart usually beats faster, so the pulse rate also increases.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p><strong>Question:</strong> Why can the pulse be felt at the wrist?</p>

<p>
An artery lies close to the skin at the wrist. Each heartbeat produces a pressure wave that causes the artery to expand, which can be detected by touch.
</p>
', 1),
