<h2>Blood Pressure</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
Blood pressure is the force exerted by circulating blood against the walls of blood vessels.
</p>

<h3>Why does blood pressure change?</h3>

<p>
The pressure is not constant.
It rises when the ventricles contract and falls as the heart relaxes.
</p>

<h3>Systolic pressure</h3>

<p>
<strong>Systolic pressure</strong> is the higher pressure produced when the ventricles contract and eject blood into the arteries.
</p>

<h3>Diastolic pressure</h3>

<p>
<strong>Diastolic pressure</strong> is the lower pressure when the heart is relaxed between contractions.
</p>

<h3>How is blood pressure written?</h3>

<p>
Blood pressure is commonly recorded as:
</p>

<p>
<strong>systolic pressure / diastolic pressure</strong>
</p>

<p>
For example, a reading of 120/80 mmHg contains:
</p>

<ul>
  <li>120 — systolic pressure</li>
  <li>80 — diastolic pressure</li>
</ul>

<h3>Why are arteries exposed to higher pressure?</h3>

<p>
Arteries receive blood directly from the ventricles, so blood pressure is highest in the arterial system compared with veins.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p><strong>Question:</strong> In a blood pressure reading of 120/80 mmHg, which value represents systolic pressure?</p>

<p>
The first number, 120 mmHg, is the systolic pressure.
</p>
