<h2>Why Heart Rate Increases During Exercise</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
During exercise, muscles need more oxygen and produce more carbon dioxide.
The circulatory system responds by increasing blood flow.
</p>

<h3>HEART RATE</h3>

<p>
The heart beats faster during exercise.
This increases the rate at which blood is pumped through the circulation.
</p>

<h3>OXYGEN DELIVERY</h3>

<p>
Blood carries oxygen from the lungs to active muscles.
A faster circulation helps deliver oxygen to tissues at a greater rate.
</p>

<h3>CARBON DIOXIDE REMOVAL</h3>

<p>
Blood also carries carbon dioxide away from respiring tissues toward the lungs.
Increasing blood flow helps transport this carbon dioxide to the lungs for
removal.
</p>

<h3>COMBINING THE SYSTEMS</h3>

<p>
The respiratory and circulatory systems work together.
</p>

<p>
<strong>
Lungs → oxygen enters blood
</strong>
</p>

<p>
<strong>
Heart → pumps oxygenated blood
</strong>
</p>

<p>
<strong>
Muscles → use oxygen for respiration
</strong>
</p>

<p>
<strong>
Blood → carries carbon dioxide back to lungs
</strong>
</p>

<h3>KEY IDEA</h3>

<p>
An increased heart rate does not itself create oxygen.
It increases the rate at which blood transports oxygen and carbon dioxide.
</p>
', 1),
