<h2>The Four Chambers of the Heart</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
The human heart has four chambers:
</p>

<ul>
  <li>right atrium</li>
  <li>right ventricle</li>
  <li>left atrium</li>
  <li>left ventricle</li>
</ul>

<h3>Atria</h3>

<p>
The two atria are the upper chambers.
They receive blood returning to the heart.
</p>

<ul>
  <li>right atrium receives blood from the body</li>
  <li>left atrium receives blood from the lungs</li>
</ul>

<h3>Ventricles</h3>

<p>
The two ventricles are the lower chambers.
They pump blood out of the heart.
</p>

<ul>
  <li>right ventricle pumps blood to the lungs</li>
  <li>left ventricle pumps blood to the body</li>
</ul>

<h3>Why are there four chambers?</h3>

<p>
The four-chambered structure keeps oxygenated and deoxygenated blood largely separate and allows blood to be pumped through the lungs and body at appropriate pressures.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which chamber receives oxygenated blood from the lungs?</p>

<p>
The pulmonary veins carry oxygenated blood from the lungs to the left atrium.
Therefore, the <strong>left atrium</strong> receives oxygenated blood from the lungs.
</p>
', 1),
