<h2>The Right Side of the Heart</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
The right side of the heart handles mainly deoxygenated blood returning from the body.
</p>

<h3>Pathway</h3>

<p>
<strong>Body → vena cava → right atrium → right ventricle → pulmonary artery → lungs</strong>
</p>

<h3>Step 1: Blood returns from the body</h3>

<p>
After body tissues have removed oxygen from the blood and released carbon dioxide into it, the blood returns towards the heart.
</p>

<h3>Step 2: Blood enters the right atrium</h3>

<p>
The venae cavae carry blood from the body into the right atrium.
</p>

<h3>Step 3: Blood enters the right ventricle</h3>

<p>
The right atrium passes blood into the right ventricle.
</p>

<h3>Step 4: Blood is pumped to the lungs</h3>

<p>
The right ventricle contracts and pumps blood through the pulmonary artery to the lungs.
</p>

<h3>Why does the blood go to the lungs?</h3>

<p>
The blood needs to exchange gases.
Carbon dioxide moves out of the blood and oxygen enters it at the lungs.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p><strong>Question:</strong> Trace the pathway of deoxygenated blood from the body to the lungs.</p>

<p>
Body → vena cava → right atrium → right ventricle → pulmonary artery → lungs.
</p>
', 1),
  (1152, 1152, '
<h2>The Left Side of the Heart</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
The left side of the heart handles mainly oxygenated blood returning from the lungs.
</p>

<h3>Pathway</h3>

<p>
<strong>Lungs → pulmonary veins → left atrium → left ventricle → aorta → body</strong>
</p>

<h3>Step 1: Blood returns from the lungs</h3>

<p>
After gaseous exchange in the lungs, blood has gained oxygen and lost carbon dioxide.
</p>

<h3>Step 2: Blood enters the left atrium</h3>

<p>
The pulmonary veins carry oxygenated blood from the lungs to the left atrium.
</p>

<h3>Step 3: Blood enters the left ventricle</h3>

<p>
The left atrium passes blood into the left ventricle.
</p>

<h3>Step 4: Blood is pumped to the body</h3>

<p>
The left ventricle contracts and pumps blood into the aorta.
The aorta distributes blood to the body.
</p>

<h3>Why must the left ventricle generate high pressure?</h3>

<p>
The blood must travel throughout the entire body.
The left ventricle therefore needs to produce enough pressure to drive systemic circulation.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p><strong>Question:</strong> Trace oxygenated blood from the lungs to the body.</p>

<p>
Lungs → pulmonary veins → left atrium → left ventricle → aorta → body.
</p>
', 1),
