  <h2>ABO Antigens and Antibodies</h2>

  <p>
    To understand ABO transfusion compatibility, distinguish between
    <b>antigens</b> and <b>antibodies</b>.
  </p>

  <h3>Antigens</h3>

  <p>
    ABO antigens are found on the surface of red blood cells.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>Group A red blood cells have A antigen.</li>
    <li>Group B red blood cells have B antigen.</li>
    <li>Group AB red blood cells have both A and B antigens.</li>
    <li>Group O red blood cells have neither A nor B antigen.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Antibodies</h3>

  <p>
    ABO antibodies are found in the plasma.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>Group A plasma contains anti-B antibodies.</li>
    <li>Group B plasma contains anti-A antibodies.</li>
    <li>Group AB plasma contains neither anti-A nor anti-B antibodies.</li>
    <li>Group O plasma contains both anti-A and anti-B antibodies.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Why this matters</h3>

  <p>
    An antibody can react with the corresponding antigen.
  </p>

  <p>
    For example, anti-A antibodies can react with A antigens.
  </p>

  <p>
    This is why the ABO group of donor red blood cells matters during
    transfusion.
  </p>
  ', 1),
