<h2>Lymphocytes and Antibodies</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
Lymphocytes are white blood cells involved in specific immune responses.
One important function is the production of antibodies.
</p>

<h3>What is an antigen?</h3>

<p>
An antigen is a molecule or structure recognised by the immune system as foreign.
Pathogens can carry antigens on their surfaces.
</p>

<h3>What are antibodies?</h3>

<p>
Antibodies are proteins produced by certain lymphocytes.
They bind specifically to particular antigens.
</p>

<h3>Why is specificity important?</h3>

<p>
An antibody has a shape that allows it to bind to a matching antigen.
This means different antibodies can target different antigens.
</p>

<h3>What happens after an antibody binds?</h3>

<p>
Antibody binding can help the immune system identify pathogens and can interfere with their activity or help other immune cells destroy them.
</p>

<h3>Example</h3>

<p>
If a bacterium carries a particular antigen, lymphocytes can produce antibodies that recognise that antigen.
The antibodies bind to the matching antigen rather than randomly binding to every substance in the body.
</p>

<h3>Important distinction</h3>

<p>
<strong>Antibodies are not the same thing as white blood cells.</strong>
Lymphocytes are cells. Antibodies are proteins produced by certain lymphocytes.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p><strong>Question:</strong> Why can an antibody recognise one pathogen but not necessarily another?</p>

<p>
Antibodies have specific shapes. An antibody binds to an antigen with a complementary shape, so antibodies target particular antigens.
</p>
