<h2>Antibodies</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>An <strong>antibody</strong> is a specific protein produced by certain lymphocytes in response to a particular antigen.</p>

<p>Antibodies help the immune system recognise and deal with foreign substances.</p>

<h3>1. Specificity of Antibodies</h3>

<p>An antibody has a particular shape that allows it to bind to a specific antigen.</p>

<p>This can be compared with a lock and key:</p>

<p><strong>Antibody shape → matches a particular antigen</strong></p>

<p>Therefore, an antibody made against one antigen does not normally bind effectively to every different antigen.</p>

<h3>2. What Happens When an Antibody Binds?</h3>

<p>Binding can help the immune system deal with the pathogen.</p>

<p>For example, antibodies can:</p>

<ul>
<li>bind to antigens on pathogens;</li>
<li>neutralise some toxins or viruses;</li>
<li>help mark pathogens for destruction by other immune cells.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A pathogen has antigen X on its surface. A lymphocyte produces antibody X that is complementary to antigen X.</p>

<p><strong>What happens?</strong></p>

<p>The antibody binds to the matching antigen, helping the immune system identify and deal with the pathogen.</p>

<h3>Key Distinction</h3>

<p><strong>Antigen:</strong> recognised foreign molecule.</p>

<p><strong>Antibody:</strong> specific protein produced by the immune system that binds to an antigen.</p>
