<h2>Vaccination and Population Protection</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Vaccination can protect individuals and can also reduce the spread of some infectious diseases through a population.</p>

<h3>1. Vaccinated Individuals</h3>

<p>A vaccinated person has immune memory against the relevant antigen.</p>

<p>If exposed later, the immune response can be faster and stronger.</p>

<h3>2. Reduced Transmission</h3>

<p>If many people in a population are immune, an infectious pathogen may have fewer susceptible people through whom it can spread.</p>

<p>This can reduce transmission.</p>

<h3>3. Protection of Vulnerable People</h3>

<p>High vaccination coverage can therefore indirectly reduce exposure of people who cannot receive particular vaccines or who may respond poorly to infection.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>Suppose an infectious disease spreads easily when many susceptible people are present.</p>

<p>If vaccination greatly increases the proportion of people with immunity, what can happen to transmission?</p>

<p><strong>Answer:</strong> There may be fewer susceptible hosts available, reducing opportunities for transmission.</p>

<h3>Important Distinction</h3>

<p>Population protection does not mean that every vaccinated person can never become infected. Its effect depends on the vaccine, pathogen, coverage and transmission conditions.</p>
