<h2>Phagocytosis</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p><strong>Phagocytosis</strong> is the process by which a phagocyte surrounds, engulfs and digests a pathogen or other foreign particle.</p>

<p>Phagocytes are a type of white blood cell.</p>

<h3>Step 1: Detection</h3>

<p>The phagocyte detects a foreign particle or pathogen.</p>

<h3>Step 2: Movement Towards the Pathogen</h3>

<p>The phagocyte moves towards the pathogen.</p>

<h3>Step 3: Engulfment</h3>

<p>The cell membrane extends around the pathogen and encloses it inside the phagocyte.</p>

<h3>Step 4: Digestion</h3>

<p>Digestive enzymes break down the engulfed material.</p>

<h3>Step 5: Removal of Remains</h3>

<p>The digested material can be removed from the cell.</p>

<h3>Complete Sequence</h3>

<p><strong>Detect → move towards → engulf → digest → remove remains.</strong></p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A bacterium enters a wound and is encountered by a phagocyte.</p>

<p><strong>What happens?</strong></p>

<p>The phagocyte surrounds and engulfs the bacterium, enclosing it inside the cell. Digestive enzymes then break it down.</p>

<h3>Key Point</h3>

<p>Phagocytosis is different from antibody production. Phagocytes directly engulf and digest foreign material, while antibodies are specific proteins that bind to antigens.</p>
