<h2>Phagocytes and Lymphocytes</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Phagocytes and lymphocytes are both types of white blood cells, but they perform different roles in the immune response.</p>

<h3>Phagocytes</h3>

<p>Phagocytes defend the body by engulfing and digesting pathogens and foreign particles.</p>

<p>The main process is:</p>

<p><strong>Recognise → engulf → digest.</strong></p>

<h3>Lymphocytes</h3>

<p>Lymphocytes are involved in specific immune responses.</p>

<p>Some lymphocytes produce antibodies that bind to specific antigens.</p>

<p>Other lymphocytes contribute to immune regulation and destruction of infected or abnormal cells.</p>

<h3>Why Both Are Important</h3>

<p>The immune system uses several coordinated mechanisms rather than relying on one type of white blood cell.</p>

<p>For example:</p>

<p><strong>Pathogen → antigen recognised → antibodies bind → pathogen is targeted → phagocytes can engulf and digest it.</strong></p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A bacterium enters the bloodstream.</p>

<p>A lymphocyte produces antibodies that bind to antigens on the bacterium.</p>

<p>A phagocyte then encounters the marked bacterium.</p>

<p><strong>What can the phagocyte do?</strong></p>

<p>It can engulf and digest the bacterium.</p>

<h3>Key Distinction</h3>

