<h2>Function of Xylem</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>The main function of xylem is to transport <strong>water and dissolved mineral ions</strong> through the plant.</p>

<h3>Direction of Movement</h3>

<p>In a typical plant, water absorbed by the roots moves through the xylem towards the stem and leaves.</p>

<p>The movement is therefore mainly:</p>

<p><strong>Roots → stem → leaves.</strong></p>

<h3>Why Do Leaves Need Water?</h3>

<p>Leaves need water for several processes.</p>

<p>Water is a raw material for photosynthesis and also helps maintain cell turgidity.</p>

<h3>Mineral Ions</h3>

<p>Water entering the roots can contain dissolved mineral ions.</p>

<p>These ions are transported through the xylem to regions where they are required.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A mineral ion is absorbed from the soil by a root and later detected in a leaf.</p>

<p><strong>Which tissue transported it through the plant?</strong></p>

<p><strong>Xylem.</strong></p>

<p>The mineral ion was transported in the water moving through the xylem.</p>

<h3>Exam Trap</h3>

<p>Do not write that xylem transports only water.</p>

<p>The correct statement is:</p>

<p><strong>Xylem transports water and dissolved mineral ions.</strong></p>
