<h2>Humidity and Transpiration</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p><strong>Humidity</strong> describes the amount of water vapour present in the air.</p>

<p>Humidity affects the diffusion gradient between the air spaces inside a leaf and the surrounding atmosphere.</p>

<h3>High Humidity</h3>

<p>When surrounding air is humid, it already contains a relatively large amount of water vapour.</p>

<p>The difference in water vapour concentration between the leaf and atmosphere becomes smaller.</p>

<p>Water vapour therefore diffuses out more slowly.</p>

<p><strong>High humidity → lower transpiration rate.</strong></p>

<h3>Low Humidity</h3>

<p>Dry air contains less water vapour.</p>

<p>This creates a larger gradient between the moist air inside the leaf and the surrounding atmosphere.</p>

<p>Water vapour can diffuse out more rapidly.</p>

<p><strong>Low humidity → higher transpiration rate.</strong></p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A plant is moved from a humid greenhouse into dry outdoor air.</p>

<p><strong>What happens to the water vapour gradient?</strong></p>

<p>The gradient increases because the surrounding air contains less water vapour.</p>

<p>This can increase the rate of transpiration.</p>

<h3>Exam Chain</h3>

<p><strong>Lower humidity → larger water vapour gradient → faster diffusion → increased transpiration.</strong></p>
