<h2>Wind and Transpiration</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Wind can affect transpiration by changing the layer of air immediately surrounding the leaf.</p>

<h3>Still Air</h3>

<p>When air is still, water vapour accumulates near the leaf surface.</p>

<p>This creates a relatively humid layer around the leaf.</p>

<p>The diffusion gradient becomes smaller, so transpiration can slow.</p>

<h3>Moving Air</h3>

<p>Wind removes water vapour from around the leaf surface.</p>

<p>This prevents the formation of a thick humid boundary layer.</p>

<p>The water vapour gradient between the leaf and atmosphere remains steep.</p>

<p>Transpiration can therefore increase.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>Two identical plants are exposed to the same temperature and light.</p>

<p>Plant A is in still air.</p>

<p>Plant B is exposed to moving air.</p>

<p><strong>Which plant is likely to transpire faster?</strong></p>

<p>Plant B.</p>

<p>Moving air removes water vapour surrounding the leaf, helping maintain a steep diffusion gradient.</p>

<h3>Exam Chain</h3>

<p><strong>Wind increases → humid boundary layer removed → water vapour gradient maintained → diffusion increases → transpiration increases.</strong></p>
