<h2>Light Intensity and Transpiration</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Light can affect transpiration because it influences stomatal behaviour in many plants.</p>

<h3>Increased Light</h3>

<p>In many plants, increasing light intensity causes guard cells to become more turgid and stomata to open.</p>

<p>Open stomata allow carbon dioxide to enter for photosynthesis.</p>

<p>They also provide a route for water vapour to leave.</p>

<p>Therefore, transpiration can increase.</p>

<h3>Reduced Light</h3>

<p>In lower light conditions, stomata in many plants tend to become less open.</p>

<p>This can reduce water vapour loss.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A plant is moved from darkness into bright light.</p>

<p><strong>Why might its transpiration rate increase?</strong></p>

<p>Light can promote stomatal opening, increasing the pathway available for water vapour to diffuse out.</p>

<h3>Important Qualification</h3>

<p>Light is not the only factor controlling stomata.</p>

<p>Water availability, carbon dioxide concentration and other environmental conditions can also affect stomatal behaviour.</p>

<h3>Exam Chain</h3>

<p><strong>Higher light → stomata tend to open → greater stomatal pore area → increased water vapour loss → potentially higher transpiration.</strong></p>
