<h2>Comparing Factors Affecting Transpiration</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Several environmental factors can change transpiration rate.</p>

<h3>Temperature</h3>

<p>Higher temperature generally increases evaporation and can increase transpiration.</p>

<h3>Humidity</h3>

<p>Higher humidity reduces the water vapour gradient and generally decreases transpiration.</p>

<h3>Wind</h3>

<p>Higher wind speed removes water vapour around the leaf and can increase transpiration.</p>

<h3>Light</h3>

<p>Higher light can cause stomata to open in many plants, potentially increasing transpiration.</p>

<h3>Worked Comparison</h3>

<p>Consider four situations:</p>

<ul>
<li>A: hot, dry and windy</li>
<li>B: cool, humid and still</li>
<li>C: cool, dry and still</li>
<li>D: hot, humid and still</li>
</ul>

<p><strong>Which is most likely to have the highest transpiration rate?</strong></p>

<p><strong>A: hot, dry and windy.</strong></p>

<p>Heat increases evaporation, dry air creates a large water vapour gradient, and wind removes humid air from around the leaf.</p>

<h3>Exam Strategy</h3>

<p>When several factors change at once, explain the effect of each factor before deciding the overall outcome.</p>

<p>Do not simply memorise "hot = high" or "cold = low". Consider the complete set of conditions.</p>
