<h2>Opening and Closing of Stomata</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>The stomatal pore changes size according to the turgor of the guard cells.</p>

<h3>When Stomata Open</h3>

<p>Guard cells take up water and become more turgid.</p>

<p>Their shape changes, causing the stomatal pore to open.</p>

<p>This allows carbon dioxide to enter and water vapour to leave.</p>

<h3>When Stomata Close</h3>

<p>Guard cells lose water and become less turgid.</p>

<p>The stomatal pore closes.</p>

<p>This reduces water loss and also reduces gas exchange.</p>

<h3>Why Close Stomata?</h3>

<p>During conditions that cause excessive water loss, closing stomata can help conserve water.</p>

<h3>Trade-Off</h3>

<p>Closing stomata reduces water loss, but it also reduces the entry of carbon dioxide.</p>

<p>This can limit photosynthesis if carbon dioxide becomes insufficient.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A plant experiences severe water shortage.</p>

<p><strong>Why might its stomata close?</strong></p>

<p>Closing the stomata reduces the diffusion of water vapour out of the leaf and therefore helps reduce water loss.</p>

<h3>Exam Reasoning Chain</h3>

<p><strong>Water shortage → guard cells lose turgor → stomata close → water vapour loss decreases.</strong></p>
