<h2>Function of Phloem</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>The main function of phloem is to transport <strong>organic assimilates</strong> around the plant.</p>

<p>An important assimilate transported by phloem is <strong>sucrose</strong>.</p>

<h3>What Is an Assimilate?</h3>

<p>An assimilate is an organic substance produced by the plant as a result of metabolism, especially photosynthesis.</p>

<p>Sucrose is a major transport sugar in plants.</p>

<h3>Why Must Sucrose Be Transported?</h3>

<p>Leaves are major sites of photosynthesis, but many other parts of the plant need sugars.</p>

<p>For example, sugars may be transported to:</p>

<ul>
<li>growing roots;</li>
<li>developing fruits;</li>
<li>seeds;</li>
<li>growing shoots;</li>
<li>storage organs.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Direction of Transport</h3>

<p>Unlike the usual upward movement of water in xylem, phloem transport can occur in different directions in different sieve tubes.</p>

<p>The direction depends on where assimilates are produced and where they are needed.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A mature leaf produces sucrose by photosynthesis. A developing fruit needs sucrose for growth.</p>

<p><strong>Which tissue transports the sucrose?</strong></p>

<p><strong>Phloem.</strong></p>

<h3>Exam Trap</h3>

<p>Do not write that phloem transports only "food".</p>

<p>A stronger scientific answer is:</p>

<p><strong>Phloem transports organic assimilates, especially sucrose, between sources and sinks.</strong></p>
