<h2>What Is Fertilisation?</h2>

<h3>What is fertilisation?</h3>

<p>
Fertilisation is the fusion of the nuclei of two compatible gametes to form a
diploid cell called a <b>zygote</b>.
</p>

<p>
In humans, the sperm nucleus contributes 23 chromosomes and the egg nucleus
contributes 23 chromosomes.
</p>

<p>
23 + 23 = 46 chromosomes.
</p>

<h3>Why is fertilisation important?</h3>

<p>
It combines genetic material from two parents and restores the diploid
chromosome number.
</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> A sperm nucleus containing 23 chromosomes fuses with an egg
nucleus containing 23 chromosomes. What is the chromosome number of the zygote?</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Sperm contributes 23.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Egg contributes 23.</p>
<p><b>Step 3:</b> 23 + 23 = 46.</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b> The zygote normally has 46 chromosomes.</p>

<h3>Key idea</h3>

<p>
Haploid + haploid → diploid.
</p>
