<h2>Fertilisation Restores Diploid Chromosome Number</h2>

<h3>The problem fertilisation solves</h3>

<p>
Meiosis reduces chromosome number so that gametes contain one chromosome set.
Fertilisation then brings two chromosome sets together.
</p>

<h3>Human example</h3>

<p>
Sperm = 23 chromosomes.</p>
<p>
Egg = 23 chromosomes.</p>
<p>
Zygote = 23 + 23 = 46 chromosomes.</p>

<h3>Why does this matter?</h3>

<p>
Without chromosome-number reduction during gamete formation, combining two
diploid cells would double the chromosome number in every generation.
</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> Suppose human gametes incorrectly contained 46 chromosomes.
What would happen if two such gametes fused?</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Sperm would contribute 46.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Egg would contribute 46.</p>
<p><b>Step 3:</b> 46 + 46 = 92.</p>

<p>
<b>Answer:</b> The resulting cell would contain 92 chromosomes instead of the
normal 46.
</p>

<p>
This demonstrates why meiosis must reduce chromosome number before
fertilisation.
</p>
