<h2>From Meiosis to a New Organism</h2>

<h3>The complete connection</h3>

<p>
Meiosis and fertilisation are parts of one continuous reproductive cycle.
</p>

<p>
<b>Step 1:</b> Meiosis produces haploid reproductive cells.</p>

<p>
<b>Step 2:</b> A sperm and egg are capable of participating in fertilisation.</p>

<p>
<b>Step 3:</b> Fertilisation combines their chromosome sets.</p>

<p>
<b>Step 4:</b> A diploid zygote is formed.</p>

<p>
<b>Step 5:</b> The zygote divides repeatedly by mitosis as development begins.</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> Put these events in the correct order:
mitosis of the zygote, meiosis, fertilisation, formation of a zygote.</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Meiosis produces haploid cells.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Fertilisation occurs.</p>
<p><b>Step 3:</b> The zygote is formed.</p>
<p><b>Step 4:</b> The zygote undergoes mitosis.</p>

<p>
<b>Correct order:</b>
Meiosis → fertilisation → zygote formation → mitosis.
</p>

<h3>Core idea</h3>

<p>
Meiosis reduces chromosome number; fertilisation restores it; mitosis increases
cell number during growth and development.
</p>
