  <h2>What Is an Allele?</h2>

  <h3>Start with the gene</h3>

  <p>
    A gene is a region of DNA associated with a particular biological
    characteristic or function.
  </p>

  <p>
    A gene can exist in different forms. These different forms are called
    <b>alleles</b>.
  </p>

  <h3>Simple example</h3>

  <p>
    Suppose a gene is represented by the letter <b>A</b>.
  </p>

  <p>
    Different forms of that gene can be represented as:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>A</b> and <b>a</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    These symbols represent different alleles of the same gene.
  </p>

  <h3>Why are alleles important?</h3>

  <p>
    Alleles can contribute to differences in characteristics between
    individuals.
  </p>

  <p>
    For example, different alleles of a gene can result in different forms
    of a characteristic.
  </p>

  <h3>Gene versus allele</h3>

  <p>
    A <b>gene</b> refers to the genetic unit or DNA region associated with a
    particular characteristic or function.
  </p>

  <p>
    An <b>allele</b> is a particular version of that gene.
  </p>

  <h3>Important</h3>

  <p>
    Do not say that A and a are two completely different genes.
    In a simple inheritance model, they represent two different alleles of
    the same gene.
  </p>
