  <h2>Dominant Alleles</h2>

  <h3>What does dominant mean?</h3>

  <p>
    In a simple inheritance model, a <b>dominant allele</b> is an allele
    whose effect is expressed in the phenotype when at least one copy of the
    allele is present.
  </p>

  <p>
    A dominant allele is commonly represented using a capital letter.
  </p>

  <p>
    For example:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>A</b> = dominant allele
  </p>

  <h3>Consider two combinations</h3>

  <p>
    <b>AA</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    There are two copies of the dominant allele.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Aa</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    There is one dominant allele and one different allele.
  </p>

  <p>
    In a simple dominant-recessive model, the dominant allele determines the
    phenotype in both cases.
  </p>

  <h3>Important correction</h3>

  <p>
    Dominant does <b>not</b> mean stronger, better, healthier, or more common.
  </p>

  <p>
    It describes how an allele is expressed in a particular genetic
    combination.
  </p>

  <h3>Example</h3>

  <p>
    If <b>A</b> is dominant over <b>a</b>:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>AA</b> → dominant phenotype
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Aa</b> → dominant phenotype
  </p>
