    <b>A</b> = dominant allele
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>a</b> = recessive allele
  </p>

  <p>
    Possible genotypes include:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>AA</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Aa</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>aa</b>
  </p>

  <h3>Why are two letters used?</h3>

  <p>
    In a diploid organism, an individual normally has two copies of each
    autosomal gene, with one inherited from each parent.
  </p>

  <p>
    Therefore, a simple genotype is represented using two alleles.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked identification</h3>

  <p>
    If an organism has genotype <b>Aa</b>:
  </p>

  <p>
    First allele = <b>A</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    Second allele = <b>a</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    Therefore, the genotype is <b>Aa</b>.
  </p>

  <h3>Remember</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Genotype describes the genetic combination; phenotype describes the
    observable characteristic.</b>
  </p>
