  <h2>Carrier of a Recessive Allele</h2>

  <h3>What is a carrier?</h3>

  <p>
    In a simple dominant-recessive inheritance model, a <b>carrier</b> is an
    individual who has a recessive allele but does not show the recessive
    phenotype because a dominant allele is also present.
  </p>

  <h3>Example</h3>

  <p>
    Suppose:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>A</b> = dominant allele<br>
    <b>a</b> = recessive allele
  </p>

  <p>
    A person with genotype:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Aa</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    has one dominant allele and one recessive allele.
  </p>

  <p>
    In a simple dominant-recessive model, the dominant phenotype is expressed.
  </p>

  <p>
    However, the individual still possesses the recessive allele and can pass
    it to an offspring through a gamete.
  </p>

  <h3>Why can the allele be hidden?</h3>

  <p>
    The recessive phenotype is masked when the dominant allele is present in
    this simple model.
  </p>

  <h3>Important</h3>

  <p>
    Being a carrier does not mean that the recessive allele has disappeared.
    It is still present in the person''s genotype.
  </p>

  <h3>Key idea</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Aa → dominant phenotype, but recessive allele is carried.</b>
  </p>
