  (1266, 1266, '
  <h2>Interpreting a Test Cross</h2>

  <h3>The unknown parent</h3>

  <p>
    Suppose an organism shows the dominant phenotype.
  </p>

  <p>
    Its genotype could be:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>AA</b> or <b>Aa</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    We cross it with:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>aa</b>
  </p>

  <h3>Possible result 1: all dominant phenotype</h3>

  <p>
    Suppose all observed offspring have the dominant phenotype.
  </p>

  <p>
    If the unknown parent were AA:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>AA × aa → all Aa</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    Every offspring would show the dominant phenotype.
  </p>

  <h3>Possible result 2: recessive offspring appear</h3>

  <p>
    Suppose some offspring show the recessive phenotype.
  </p>

  <p>
    The unknown parent must have contributed a recessive allele.
  </p>

  <p>
    Therefore, the unknown parent must be:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Aa</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    Because:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Aa × aa → Aa or aa</b>
  </p>

  <h3>Key inference</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Recessive offspring in a test cross prove that the dominant-phenotype
    parent carries the recessive allele.</b>
  </p>

  <h3>Important caution</h3>

  <p>
    In a real experiment, observing only a small number of offspring does not
    make probability an absolute guarantee. Larger samples give stronger
    evidence.
  </p>
