  (1302, 1302, '
<h2>Mean, Range and Variation in Ecological Data</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
Ecological investigations often produce several measurements.
Simple statistical measures help researchers summarise the data.
</p>

<h3>MEAN</h3>

<p>
The <strong>mean</strong> is calculated by adding all values and dividing by
the number of values.
</p>

<p>
<strong>Mean = total of values ÷ number of values</strong>
</p>

<h3>RANGE</h3>

<p>
The <strong>range</strong> shows the difference between the largest and
smallest values.
</p>

<p>
<strong>Range = largest value − smallest value</strong>
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE</h3>

<p>
Plant counts from five quadrats are:
</p>

<p>
6, 8, 10, 7, 9
</p>

<p>
<strong>Mean:</strong></p>

<p>
(6 + 8 + 10 + 7 + 9) ÷ 5
</p>

<p>
= 40 ÷ 5
</p>

<p>
= <strong>8</strong>
</p>

<p>
<strong>Range:</strong></p>

<p>
10 − 6 = <strong>4</strong>
</p>

<h3>WHY THESE VALUES HELP</h3>

<p>
The mean gives a measure of the typical value.
The range gives an indication of how spread out the observations are.
</p>
