<h2>Distribution of Organisms</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
The <b>distribution</b> of a species describes where individuals of that species occur within an area.
</p>

<p>
Organisms are rarely distributed evenly.
</p>

<p>
Their distribution is affected by both biotic and abiotic factors.
</p>

<h3>Three Common Distribution Patterns</h3>

<ul>
<li><b>Clumped</b> — individuals occur in groups.</li>
<li><b>Uniform</b> — individuals are spaced relatively evenly.</li>
<li><b>Random</b> — there is no obvious regular pattern.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Worked Example 1 — Clumped</h3>

<p>
A plant may occur mainly around patches where soil moisture is high.
</p>

<p>
Individuals form groups rather than being evenly distributed.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2 — Uniform</h3>

<p>
Animals may maintain territories and keep other individuals away.
</p>

<p>
This can produce a relatively even spacing between individuals.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3 — Random</h3>

<p>
If resources are distributed fairly evenly and individuals do not strongly attract or repel one another, their positions may appear random.
</p>

<h3>Important</h3>

<p>
Distribution pattern is different from population size.
</p>

<p>
Population size asks:
</p>

<p>
<b>How many individuals are there?</b>
</p>

<p>
Distribution asks:
</p>

<p>
<b>Where are those individuals located?</b>
</p>
