<h2>Water Availability and Plant Distribution</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
Water is an essential resource for plants.
It acts as a reactant in photosynthesis, is needed for transport and helps
maintain cell turgidity.
</p>

<p>
The amount of available water therefore influences where different plants
can survive and grow.
</p>

<h3>WATER AVAILABILITY AS AN ABIOTIC FACTOR</h3>

<p>
Water availability is an <strong>abiotic factor</strong> because it is a
non-living part of the environment.
</p>

<p>
Areas with high and reliable water availability can support plant species
that require abundant water.
Areas with little available water favour plants with adaptations that reduce
water loss or improve water uptake.
</p>

<h3>EXAMPLE: DRY ENVIRONMENTS</h3>

<p>
A plant living in a dry environment may have features such as:
</p>

<ul>
  <li>reduced leaf surface area</li>
  <li>a thick waxy cuticle</li>
  <li>stomata positioned in protected locations</li>
  <li>deep or widespread roots</li>
</ul>

<p>
These features can reduce water loss or improve access to water.
</p>

<h3>KEY IDEA</h3>

<p>
Water availability is not simply a description of rainfall.
It refers to how much water is actually accessible to organisms.
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE</h3>

<p><strong>Question:</strong> Why might a plant adapted to a dry environment survive better than a plant without these adaptations?</p>

<p>
The adapted plant may lose water more slowly or obtain water more effectively.
It is therefore more likely to maintain sufficient water for cell function
during dry conditions.
</p>
