<h2>Percentage Cover</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
Counting individual plants is not always practical, especially when organisms
such as grasses grow very close together.
</p>

<p>
In such situations, ecologists can estimate <strong>percentage cover</strong>:
the proportion of the sampled area occupied by a species.
</p>

<h3>EXAMPLE</h3>

<p>
Imagine a quadrat containing grass covering approximately 60% of its surface.
The estimated percentage cover of grass is:
</p>

<p>
<strong>60%</strong>
</p>

<p>
Another quadrat might contain 20% grass cover.
The two locations can then be compared.
</p>

<h3>WHY USE PERCENTAGE COVER?</h3>

<p>
If plants form dense mats, counting each individual may be difficult because
individual boundaries cannot easily be identified.
Percentage cover provides a practical estimate of abundance.
</p>

<h3>HOW TO IMPROVE RELIABILITY</h3>

<p>
Use several quadrats and calculate a mean percentage cover.
This reduces the effect of unusually high or low individual samples.
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE</h3>

<p>
Three quadrats give grass cover values of:
</p>

<p>
40%, 60%, 50%
</p>

<p>
Mean percentage cover:
</p>

<p>
<strong>(40 + 60 + 50) ÷ 3 = 150 ÷ 3 = 50%</strong>
</p>
', 1),
