<h2>Food Web</h2>

<p>
A <b>food web</b> shows several interconnected food chains within an ecosystem.
</p>

<p>
In real ecosystems, organisms often eat more than one type of food and may be eaten by more than one type of organism.
</p>

<h3>Example</h3>

<p>
A grassland might contain:
</p>

<p>
<b>Grass → grasshopper → frog → snake</b>
</p>

<p>
and also:
</p>

<p>
<b>Grass → rabbit → hawk</b>
</p>

<p>
If the snake can also eat the frog and the hawk can eat the snake, several chains become connected.
</p>

<p>
The resulting network is a <b>food web</b>.
</p>

<h3>Why food webs are useful</h3>

<p>
They give a more realistic representation of feeding relationships than a single food chain.
</p>

<p>
A change in one population can affect several other populations because organisms are connected through multiple feeding relationships.
