<h2>Capture-Mark-Recapture</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
<b>Capture-mark-recapture</b> is a sampling method used to estimate the population size of mobile animals.
</p>

<h3>Basic Procedure</h3>

<ol>
<li>Capture a sample of animals.</li>
<li>Count and harmlessly mark them.</li>
<li>Release them back into the population.</li>
<li>Allow enough time for them to mix with the population.</li>
<li>Capture a second sample.</li>
<li>Count how many of the second sample are marked.</li>
</ol>

<p>
If many animals in the second sample are marked, the original marked group probably represented a large fraction of the population.
</p>

<p>
If very few are marked, the total population is likely to be much larger than the first sample.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
First capture:
</p>

<p>
50 fish are captured, marked and released.
</p>

<p>
Second capture:
</p>

<p>
100 fish are captured.
</p>

<p>
20 of them are marked.
</p>

<p>
The estimate is:
</p>

<p>
<b>Population ≈ (number marked first × number caught second) ÷ number marked second</b>
</p>

<p>
= (50 × 100) ÷ 20
</p>

<p>
= <b>250 fish</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
20 insects are initially marked.
</p>

<p>
A second sample contains 40 insects, of which 10 are marked.
</p>

<p>
Estimated population:
</p>

<p>
(20 × 40) ÷ 10 = <b>80 insects</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
If the second sample contains a high proportion of marked animals, the estimated population tends to be smaller.
</p>

<p>
If the second sample contains a low proportion of marked animals, the estimated population tends to be larger.
</p>

<hr>

<h2>Capture-Mark-Recapture</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
<strong>Capture-mark-recapture</strong> is a method used to estimate the size
of a population of mobile animals.
</p>

<p>
It is useful when animals move around an area and cannot easily be counted
directly.
</p>

<h3>THE BASIC METHOD</h3>

<p>
<strong>Step 1:</strong> Capture a number of animals.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 2:</strong> Mark them harmlessly.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 3:</strong> Release them back into the population.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 4:</strong> Allow enough time for them to mix with the population.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 5:</strong> Capture another sample.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 6:</strong> Count how many animals in the second sample are marked.</p>

<h3>THE ASSUMPTION</h3>

<p>
The method assumes that marked animals mix back into the population and have
a reasonable chance of being captured again.
</p>

<h3>ESTIMATION FORMULA</h3>

<p>
A commonly used estimate is:
</p>

<p>
<strong>
Population estimate =
(first sample × second sample) ÷ number of marked animals recaptured
</strong>
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE</h3>

<p>
100 animals are captured and marked.
Later, 80 animals are captured.
20 of the 80 are marked.
</p>

<p>
Estimated population:
</p>

<p>
<strong>(100 × 80) ÷ 20 = 400 animals</strong>
</p>

<h3>IMPORTANT</h3>

<p>
The result is an estimate and depends on whether the assumptions of the method
are reasonably satisfied.
</p>
', 1),
  (1403, 1403, '
<h2>Density-Dependent Factors</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
A <strong>density-dependent factor</strong> has a stronger effect on a
population as population density increases.
</p>

<h3>EXAMPLES</h3>

<ul>
  <li>Competition</li>
  <li>Spread of infectious disease</li>
  <li>Predation</li>
  <li>Food shortage</li>
</ul>

<h3>COMPETITION</h3>

<p>
When many individuals occupy the same area, they may compete more strongly
for food, water, space or mates.
</p>

<h3>DISEASE</h3>

<p>
High population density can increase the frequency of contact between
individuals.
This can make transmission of some infectious diseases easier.
</p>

<h3>PREDATION</h3>

<p>
Changes in population density can affect interactions between predators and
prey.
Predator numbers may increase when prey are abundant, while increased
predation can then reduce prey numbers.
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE</h3>

<p><strong>Question:</strong> Why can disease spread more rapidly in a dense population?</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 1:</strong> More individuals occupy the same area.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 2:</strong> Individuals are more likely to come into contact.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 3:</strong> A pathogen can therefore have more opportunities to
move between hosts.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 4:</strong> Disease transmission can increase.
</p>

<h3>KEY IDEA</h3>

<p>
Density-dependent factors help prevent populations from increasing
indefinitely.
</p>
', 1),
  (1404, 1404, '
<h2>Density-Independent Factors</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
A <strong>density-independent factor</strong> can affect a population regardless
of how densely populated the area is.
</p>

<h3>EXAMPLES</h3>

<ul>
  <li>Drought</li>
  <li>Flooding</li>
  <li>Extreme temperatures</li>
  <li>Wildfires</li>
  <li>Major storms</li>
</ul>

<h3>WHY DENSITY DOES NOT DETERMINE THE EFFECT</h3>

<p>
A severe drought can reduce water availability whether a population contains
100 individuals or 1,000 individuals.
</p>

<p>
The factor itself is not caused by the number of organisms present.
</p>

<h3>COMPARISON</h3>

<p>
<strong>Density-dependent:</strong>
effect becomes stronger as population density increases.
</p>

<p>
<strong>Density-independent:</strong>
effect does not depend primarily on population density.
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE</h3>

<p><strong>Question:</strong> Explain why drought can be density-independent.</p>

<p>
A drought is caused by a lack of rainfall and water availability.
It can affect organisms whether the population is small or large.
Therefore, its occurrence is not dependent on population density.
</p>

<h3>IMPORTANT</h3>

<p>
A factor can affect populations in different ways.
The key question is whether its effect depends on population density.
</p>
