<h2>Random Sampling</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
<strong>Random sampling</strong> means selecting sampling locations so that
each suitable location has an equal or known chance of being selected.
</p>

<p>
It helps reduce researcher bias when estimating the distribution or abundance
of organisms.
</p>

<h3>WHY RANDOM SAMPLING MATTERS</h3>

<p>
Imagine a field containing areas with many plants and areas with few plants.
If a researcher chooses only locations where plants are abundant, the estimate
will probably be too high.
</p>

<p>
Random selection reduces the temptation to choose locations based on what the
researcher expects to find.
</p>

<h3>HOW TO RANDOMLY SAMPLE AN AREA</h3>

<p>
A grid can be placed over the study area.
Each grid square can be given a number.
Random numbers can then be generated to select the squares to investigate.
</p>

<h3>STEP-BY-STEP</h3>

<p>
<strong>Step 1:</strong> Divide the study area into equal sections.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 2:</strong> Number the sections.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 3:</strong> Generate random numbers.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 4:</strong> Place the sampling equipment at the selected locations.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 5:</strong> Record the organisms found.</p>

<h3>KEY IDEA</h3>

<p>
Random sampling helps reduce selection bias.
It does not guarantee that every sample will contain the same number of
organisms.
</p>
', 1),
