<h2>Frequency of an Organism</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
In ecology, <strong>frequency</strong> describes how often a particular species
occurs within a set of sampling areas.
</p>

<p>
For example, if 10 quadrats are examined and a plant occurs in 7 of them,
its frequency is 7 out of 10 quadrats.
</p>

<h3>CALCULATING FREQUENCY</h3>

<p>
Frequency can be expressed as a percentage:
</p>

<p>
<strong>
Frequency (%) = number of quadrats containing the species ÷ total number of
quadrats × 100
</strong>
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE</h3>

<p>
A researcher examines 20 quadrats.
A particular grass species occurs in 12 quadrats.
</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 1:</strong> Number of quadrats containing the species = 12.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 2:</strong> Total quadrats = 20.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 3:</strong>
</p>

<p>
Frequency = (12 ÷ 20) × 100
</p>

<p>
= 0.6 × 100
</p>

<p>
= <strong>60%</strong>
</p>

<h3>IMPORTANT DISTINCTION</h3>

<p>
Frequency tells us how widely a species occurs across the sampled locations.
It does not tell us how many individuals occur in each quadrat.
</p>

<p>
A species can have high frequency but low abundance if it occurs in many
quadrats but only a few individuals occur in each one.
</p>
', 1),
