<h2>Combustion and the Carbon Cycle</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
<strong>Combustion</strong> is burning.
When carbon-containing fuels burn in oxygen, carbon is commonly released as
carbon dioxide.
</p>

<h3>BIOMASS COMBUSTION</h3>

<p>
Wood contains carbon in organic compounds.
When wood burns, the carbon reacts during combustion and much of it is released
into the atmosphere as carbon dioxide.
</p>

<h3>FOSSIL FUEL COMBUSTION</h3>

<p>
Coal, petroleum and natural gas contain carbon that was stored over very long
periods.
</p>

<p>
When fossil fuels are burned, this stored carbon is transferred into the
atmosphere mainly as carbon dioxide.
</p>

<h3>STEP-BY-STEP</h3>

<p><strong>Example:</strong> Burning coal to produce electricity.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 1:</strong> Coal contains stored carbon.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 2:</strong> Coal is burned in oxygen.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 3:</strong> Carbon-containing compounds react during combustion.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 4:</strong> Carbon dioxide is produced.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 5:</strong> Carbon dioxide enters the atmosphere.</p>

<h3>WHY FOSSIL FUELS MATTER</h3>

<p>
Fossil fuels contain carbon that has been stored for extremely long periods.
Burning them transfers some of that stored carbon into the atmosphere much
faster than the natural processes that originally stored it.
</p>

<h3>KEY DISTINCTION</h3>

<p>
Combustion does not create carbon from nothing.
It transfers carbon already present in the fuel into other compounds,
particularly carbon dioxide.
</p>
