<h2>Water Cycle</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
The <strong>water cycle</strong> describes the continuous movement of water
between the atmosphere, land, organisms and bodies of water.
</p>

<h3>MAJOR PROCESSES</h3>

<ul>
  <li>Evaporation</li>
  <li>Transpiration</li>
  <li>Condensation</li>
  <li>Precipitation</li>
  <li>Collection and runoff</li>
  <li>Infiltration</li>
</ul>

<h3>EVAPORATION</h3>

<p>
Liquid water at surfaces gains energy and changes into water vapour.
</p>

<h3>TRANSPIRATION</h3>

<p>
Plants lose water vapour from their aerial parts, mainly through stomata.
This process is called transpiration.
</p>

<h3>CONDENSATION</h3>

<p>
Water vapour cools and changes into tiny liquid water droplets.
</p>

<h3>PRECIPITATION</h3>

<p>
Water falls from clouds to Earth''s surface as rain, hail, sleet or snow,
depending on atmospheric conditions.
</p>

<h3>FOLLOWING ONE WATER MOLECULE</h3>

<p>
A water molecule in a lake can evaporate into the atmosphere.
It may later condense into a cloud droplet, fall as rain, enter the soil,
be absorbed by a plant and eventually leave the plant through transpiration.
</p>
