<h2>How a Dichotomous Key Works</h2>

<p>
A <b>dichotomous key</b> is a type of taxonomic key in which each stage gives <b>two contrasting choices</b>.
</p>

<p>
The word <b>dichotomous</b> means divided into two parts.
</p>

<h3>Example</h3>

<p>
A simple key for identifying four animals could begin like this:
</p>

<p>
<b>1a.</b> Has feathers → go to 2<br>
<b>1b.</b> Does not have feathers → go to 3
</p>

<p>
The two choices are opposites:
</p>

<ul>
<li>has feathers</li>
<li>does not have feathers</li>
</ul>

<p>
If the organism has feathers, choose <b>1a</b>.
If it does not have feathers, choose <b>1b</b>.
</p>

<h3>Why two choices?</h3>

<p>
Two choices make the identification process controlled and easy to follow. Each choice eliminates some possibilities.
</p>

<h3>Example</h3>

<p>
Suppose an organism has no feathers. From the first pair, you choose:
</p>

<p>
<b>1b → does not have feathers</b>
</p>

<p>
You then follow the instruction to the next numbered step.
</p>

<p>
The process continues until the organism''s identity is reached.
</p>
', 1),
