<h2>Following a Key Step by Step</h2>

<p>
A taxonomic key must be followed in the order given. Skipping a step can lead to an incorrect identification.
</p>

<h3>Example key</h3>

<p>
<b>1a.</b> Has wings → go to 2<br>
<b>1b.</b> No wings → go to 3
</p>

<p>
<b>2a.</b> Has feathers → Bird<br>
<b>2b.</b> No feathers → Insect
</p>

<p>
<b>3a.</b> Has fur → Mammal<br>
<b>3b.</b> No fur → Reptile
</p>

<h3>Identify an organism</h3>

<p>
Suppose the organism:
</p>

<ul>
<li>has wings</li>
<li>has feathers</li>
</ul>

<p>
Start at step 1.
</p>

<p>
It has wings, so choose <b>1a</b>.
</p>

<p>
The instruction says to go to step 2.
</p>

<p>
At step 2, it has feathers, so choose <b>2a</b>.
</p>

<p>
The organism is therefore identified as a <b>bird</b>.
</p>

<h3>The sequence matters</h3>

<p>
You should not jump directly to a conclusion. Every decision must be supported by the characteristics given in the key.
</p>
