<p>Plant A receives sufficient nitrate. Plant B receives very little nitrate. Both plants receive the same amount of light and water.</p>

<p>After several weeks, Plant B is smaller.</p>

<p><strong>Explain the difference.</strong></p>

<p>Plant B has insufficient nitrate to supply enough nitrogen for normal amino acid and protein production, so its growth is reduced.</p>

<h3>Important Distinction</h3>

<p>Nitrate is not used to provide the carbon atoms of glucose in photosynthesis.</p>

<p>It provides nitrogen needed for nitrogen-containing compounds such as amino acids and proteins.</p>
