<h2>Evaluating a Mineral Nutrition Experiment</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>A good mineral nutrition experiment must produce evidence that can be interpreted reliably.</p>

<h3>Use Similar Plants</h3>

<p>Plants should be similar in species, age and starting size.</p>

<p>This reduces differences caused by factors other than mineral availability.</p>

<h3>Use Replicates</h3>

<p>Several plants should be used in each treatment.</p>

<p>Repeats help identify unusual results and allow means to be calculated.</p>

<h3>Control Other Variables</h3>

<p>Important variables such as light, temperature, water supply and other mineral nutrients should be controlled.</p>

<h3>Measure Growth Quantitatively</h3>

<p>Instead of simply writing "healthy" or "unhealthy," measure something such as increase in height or biomass.</p>

<h3>Include a Control Group</h3>

<p>A control group receiving adequate mineral nutrition provides a comparison.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A student tests magnesium deficiency using one plant without magnesium and one plant with magnesium.</p>

<p>The plant without magnesium becomes yellow.</p>

<p><strong>Give one weakness of this investigation.</strong></p>

<p>Only one plant was used in each group, so individual variation could have influenced the result.</p>

<h3>Improvement</h3>

<p>Use several plants in each treatment and calculate the mean response.</p>

<h3>Key Principle</h3>

<p>A stronger experiment does not simply produce a result. It makes alternative explanations less likely.</p>
