<h2>How Mineral Deficiency Affects Plant Growth</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Plant growth depends on the production of new cells and the synthesis of substances required by those cells.</p>

<p>Mineral ions support many of these processes.</p>

<h3>Nitrate Deficiency</h3>

<p>Low nitrate availability reduces the nitrogen available for amino acid and protein production.</p>

<p>This can restrict cell growth and development.</p>

<h3>Magnesium Deficiency</h3>

<p>Low magnesium availability can reduce chlorophyll production.</p>

<p>This can reduce the plant''s capacity to absorb light energy for photosynthesis.</p>

<h3>Why Growth Can Decrease</h3>

<p>When photosynthesis is reduced, less carbohydrate may be produced.</p>

<p>When protein synthesis is restricted, cells may also have difficulty carrying out normal growth and development.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A plant receiving adequate light, water and carbon dioxide grows slowly because nitrate is severely deficient.</p>

<p><strong>Which explanation is strongest?</strong></p>

<p>Low nitrate reduces nitrogen availability. This limits amino acid and protein production, interfering with normal growth.</p>

<h3>Do Not Overgeneralise</h3>

<p>Mineral deficiency is one possible cause of poor growth. Temperature, water availability, light, disease and other factors can also affect growth.</p>

<h3>Exam Structure</h3>

<p><strong>Mineral deficiency → specific biological role affected → cellular consequence → plant-level symptom.</strong></p>
