<h2>Investigating Mineral Requirements Using Hydroponics</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p><strong>Hydroponics</strong> is a method of growing plants without ordinary soil, using a nutrient solution that supplies water and required mineral ions.</p>

<h3>Why Is This Useful?</h3>

<p>Soil contains many substances that can make it difficult to control exactly which mineral ions are available.</p>

<p>A nutrient solution allows the researcher to control the mineral composition more precisely.</p>

<h3>Testing a Mineral</h3>

<p>To investigate whether a particular mineral is required, plants can be supplied with complete nutrient solutions or solutions lacking the mineral being tested.</p>

<h3>Example</h3>

<p>One group receives a complete nutrient solution.</p>

<p>A second group receives the same solution but without magnesium.</p>

<p>If the magnesium-deficient plants develop chlorosis while the control plants remain healthy, this provides evidence that magnesium is required for normal plant development and chlorophyll production.</p>

<h3>Fair Test</h3>

<p>The groups should receive the same:</p>

<p>• plant species</p>

<p>• light conditions</p>

<p>• temperature</p>

<p>• water availability</p>

<p>• growing period</p>

<p>• other essential nutrients</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A student wants to test whether magnesium is required for healthy leaves.</p>

<p><strong>What should differ between the experimental and control nutrient solutions?</strong></p>

<p>The presence or absence of magnesium should be the deliberate difference while the other essential nutrients are kept adequate.</p>

<h3>Why a Control Is Essential</h3>

<p>Without a control group receiving adequate magnesium, it would be harder to determine whether leaf changes were caused by magnesium deficiency or another factor.</p>
