<h2>Practical: Comparing Inspired and Expired Air</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
A simple investigation can compare inspired atmospheric air with expired air
to demonstrate changes in carbon dioxide concentration.
</p>

<h3>QUESTION</h3>

<p>
<strong>
Does expired air contain more carbon dioxide than atmospheric air?
</strong>
</p>

<h3>APPARATUS</h3>

<ul>
  <li>Two containers or gas-washing arrangements</li>
  <li>Equal volumes of limewater</li>
  <li>Delivery tubes</li>
  <li>Source of atmospheric air</li>
  <li>Source of expired air</li>
</ul>

<h3>METHOD</h3>

<p>
<strong>Step 1:</strong> Place equal volumes of limewater into two identical
containers.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 2:</strong> Pass atmospheric air through one sample.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 3:</strong> Pass expired air through the other sample for the same
period.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 4:</strong> Compare the appearance of the limewater.</p>

<h3>EXPECTED OBSERVATION</h3>

<p>
The limewater exposed to expired air should turn milky more quickly or more
strongly.
</p>

<h3>CONCLUSION</h3>

<p>
Expired air contains more carbon dioxide than atmospheric air.
