<h2>Gas Exchange in Insects</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
Insects have a specialised gas-exchange system called the
<strong>tracheal system</strong>.
</p>

<h3>SPIRACLES</h3>

<p>
Air enters and leaves the insect through openings called
<strong>spiracles</strong>, usually found along the body surface.
</p>

<h3>TRACHEAE</h3>

<p>
Spiracles lead into larger tubes called <strong>tracheae</strong>.
</p>

<h3>TRACHEOLES</h3>

<p>
The tracheae branch into very fine tubes called <strong>tracheoles</strong>.
These extend close to body cells.
</p>

<h3>HOW GAS EXCHANGE OCCURS</h3>

<p>
Oxygen enters through the spiracles and travels through the tracheae and
tracheoles.
It then diffuses directly to body tissues.
</p>

<p>
Carbon dioxide produced by respiring cells diffuses in the opposite direction
and eventually leaves through the spiracles.
</p>

<h3>IMPORTANT DIFFERENCE FROM HUMANS</h3>

<p>
In insects, the tracheal system delivers oxygen directly to tissues.
The blood does not play the same central role in oxygen transport that it does
in humans.
</p>
