<h2>Plant Growth Processes: Complete Reasoning Problem</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Plant growth involves several linked cellular processes rather than one single event.</p>

<h3>Stage 1: Cell Division</h3>

<p>Meristematic cells divide by mitosis.</p>

<p>This increases cell number.</p>

<h3>Stage 2: Cell Elongation</h3>

<p>Many newly produced cells increase in size and length.</p>

<p>Water uptake and expansion of the cell and vacuole contribute to this process.</p>

<h3>Stage 3: Cell Differentiation</h3>

<p>Cells develop specialised structures and functions.</p>

<p>Examples include root hair cells, xylem cells and phloem cells.</p>

<h3>Worked Exam Problem</h3>

<p>A student observes that the root of a seedling becomes longer over several days.</p>

<p>The student asks how cells contribute to this increase in length.</p>

<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Cells in the root apical meristem divide by mitosis.</p>

<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> New cells are produced.</p>

<p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Many of these cells elongate.</p>

<p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Cells differentiate into specialised tissues.</p>

<p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> Root growth results from coordinated cell division, cell elongation and differentiation.</p>

<h3>Critical Distinction</h3>

<p>Cell division increases the number of cells.</p>

<p>Cell elongation increases cell size.</p>

<p>Differentiation produces specialised cells.</p>

<p>These processes work together to produce a functional growing plant.</p>
