<h2>Comparing Plant Growth Under Different Conditions</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Scientists can compare plant growth under different environmental conditions by changing one factor and measuring growth.</p>

<h3>Example</h3>

<p>Two groups of seedlings are grown under different light intensities.</p>

<p>Group A receives high light intensity.</p>

<p>Group B receives low light intensity.</p>

<p>Other important conditions are kept similar.</p>

<h3>What Is Measured?</h3>

<p>Possible dependent variables include:</p>

<p>• increase in height</p>

<p>• root length</p>

<p>• dry mass</p>

<p>• growth rate</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>Group A increases by 8 cm in 4 days.</p>

<p>Group B increases by 4 cm in 4 days.</p>

<p>A growth rate:</p>

<p>8 ÷ 4 = 2 cm/day</p>

<p>B growth rate:</p>

<p>4 ÷ 4 = 1 cm/day</p>

<p>Group A had the greater measured growth rate.</p>

<h3>Important</h3>

<p>A difference in growth does not automatically prove that the tested factor caused the difference unless other important variables were controlled.</p>
  
<hr>

