<h2>Evaluating a Plant Growth Investigation</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>After collecting results, an investigation can be evaluated by considering its reliability, validity and possible improvements.</p>

<h3>Reliability</h3>

<p>Use several plants and repeat measurements.</p>

<p>Calculate means where appropriate.</p>

<h3>Validity</h3>

<p>Change only the factor being investigated.</p>

<p>Keep other important conditions controlled.</p>

<h3>Accuracy</h3>

<p>Use suitable measuring equipment and read measurements consistently.</p>

<h3>Improvement Example</h3>

<p>A student investigates light intensity using one seedling in each group.</p>

<p>This is weak because natural differences between individual seedlings could affect the result.</p>

<p>A better design uses several similar seedlings per treatment and calculates mean growth.</p>

<h3>Another Improvement</h3>

<p>If temperature changes between groups while light intensity also changes, the investigation cannot confidently attribute growth differences to temperature.</p>

<p>Light intensity should therefore be controlled.</p>

<h3>Final Principle</h3>

<p>A strong investigation reduces alternative explanations for the observed result.</p>
  
<hr>

