<h2>Annual Growth Rings</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>In many woody plants, changes in growth conditions during different parts of the year can produce visible differences in the wood.</p>

<p>These differences can form growth rings.</p>

<h3>How Do Rings Form?</h3>

<p>When conditions favour rapid growth, the vascular cambium can produce wood with characteristics different from wood produced during slower growth.</p>

<p>Repeated changes in growth rate can therefore produce alternating bands in the wood.</p>

<h3>Reading a Growth Ring</h3>

<p>A sequence of rings can provide evidence about past patterns of growth.</p>

<p>However, a ring should not automatically be interpreted as proof of exactly one calendar year because growth patterns depend on the plant species and environmental conditions.</p>

<h3>Environmental Conditions</h3>

<p>Growth can be affected by factors such as:</p>

<p>• water availability</p>

<p>• temperature</p>

<p>• nutrient availability</p>

<p>• damage</p>

<h3>Exam Reasoning</h3>

<p>If one period produced a narrow ring while another produced a wider ring, the difference may indicate different growth conditions.</p>

<p>The ring width alone does not identify the exact cause.</p>
