<h2>Conditions Necessary for Seed Germination</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Germination is the process by which a seed resumes growth and the embryo develops into a seedling.</p>

<p>Three major conditions are required for successful germination of most seeds:</p>

<p><strong>Water, oxygen and a suitable temperature.</strong></p>

<h3>Water</h3>

<p>The seed absorbs water.</p>

<p>This causes the tissues of the seed to rehydrate and activates metabolic processes needed for growth.</p>

<h3>Oxygen</h3>

<p>The embryo needs energy for growth.</p>

<p>Oxygen allows aerobic respiration to release energy from stored food.</p>

<h3>Suitable Temperature</h3>

<p>Germination depends on enzyme-controlled metabolic reactions.</p>

<p>At a suitable temperature, these reactions occur at an appropriate rate.</p>

<h3>Why Too Much Water Can Be a Problem</h3>

<p>Water itself is necessary, but waterlogged conditions can reduce the availability of oxygen around the seed.</p>

<p>Without enough oxygen, aerobic respiration can be restricted.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>Three groups of seeds are placed under different conditions.</p>

<p>Group A receives water, oxygen and suitable warmth.</p>

<p>Group B receives water but is kept in oxygen-poor conditions.</p>

<p>Group C receives water and oxygen but is kept at a very low temperature.</p>

<p><strong>Which group should show the best germination?</strong></p>

<p>Group A, because all three essential conditions are provided.</p>

<h3>Exam Chain</h3>

<p><strong>Water → rehydration and metabolic activation.</strong></p>

<p><strong>Oxygen → aerobic respiration → energy.</strong></p>

<p><strong>Suitable temperature → appropriate enzyme activity.</strong></p>
