<h2>Epigeal Germination</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>In epigeal germination, the hypocotyl elongates and lifts the cotyledons above the soil surface.</p>

<p>The cotyledons therefore become exposed above the ground during early seedling development.</p>

<h3>What Happens</h3>

<p>The seed absorbs water and germination begins.</p>

<p>The radicle emerges and develops into the primary root.</p>

<p>The hypocotyl elongates and pushes the cotyledons upwards.</p>

<p>The plumule develops into the shoot.</p>

<h3>Why the Cotyledons Rise</h3>

<p>The key feature is elongation of the hypocotyl.</p>

<p>This raises the cotyledons above the soil.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A seed germinates and its cotyledons are lifted above the soil surface.</p>

<p><strong>What type of germination is occurring?</strong></p>

<p>Epigeal germination.</p>

<p>The hypocotyl has elongated sufficiently to raise the cotyledons above the soil.</p>

<h3>Identification Rule</h3>

<p><strong>Cotyledons above soil → epigeal germination.</strong></p>
  
<hr>

<h2>Epigeal Germination</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Epigeal germination is a pattern of germination in which the cotyledons are lifted above the soil surface as the seedling develops.</p>

<h3>What Causes the Movement?</h3>

<p>Growth of the embryonic stem region below the cotyledons pushes the cotyledons upward.</p>

<p>The cotyledons therefore become exposed above the soil.</p>

<h3>Example</h3>

<p>In a typical epigeal seedling, the young root develops downward while the cotyledons are carried upward above the soil.</p>

<h3>Why the Name Helps</h3>

<p>"Epigeal" refers to the cotyledons being above the ground.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A seedling emerges from the soil and its two cotyledons are clearly visible above the soil surface.</p>

<p>This is evidence of epigeal germination.</p>

<h3>Exam Rule</h3>

<p>Identify epigeal germination from the <strong>position of the cotyledons</strong>, not simply from whether the seedling has emerged.</p>
