<h2>Hypogeal Germination</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>In hypogeal germination, the cotyledons remain below the soil surface while the shoot develops above the ground.</p>

<p>The epicotyl elongates and raises the plumule above the soil.</p>

<h3>What Happens</h3>

<p>The radicle emerges and develops into the primary root.</p>

<p>The epicotyl elongates.</p>

<p>The plumule is carried upward.</p>

<p>The cotyledons remain below the soil.</p>

<h3>Key Difference</h3>

<p>In epigeal germination, the hypocotyl raises the cotyledons.</p>

<p>In hypogeal germination, the epicotyl raises the shoot while the cotyledons remain below the soil.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A germinating seed produces a shoot above the soil, but its cotyledons remain underground.</p>

<p><strong>What type of germination is this?</strong></p>

<p>Hypogeal germination.</p>

<h3>Identification Rule</h3>

<p><strong>Cotyledons below soil → hypogeal germination.</strong></p>
  
<hr>

<h2>Hypogeal Germination</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Hypogeal germination is a pattern of germination in which the cotyledons remain below the soil surface as the shoot develops.</p>

<h3>What Causes This?</h3>

<p>The epicotyl elongates and pushes the developing shoot upward while the cotyledons remain underground.</p>

<h3>Example</h3>

<p>The root grows downward.</p>

<p>The shoot grows upward.</p>

<p>The cotyledons remain below the soil surface.</p>

<h3>Why the Distinction Matters</h3>

<p>Both epigeal and hypogeal seedlings develop roots and shoots.</p>

<p>The important difference is the position of the cotyledons during development.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A seedling emerges above the soil, but the cotyledons remain underground.</p>

<p>This indicates hypogeal germination.</p>

<h3>Exam Rule</h3>

<p>Do not identify hypogeal germination simply because the shoot grows upward.</p>

<p>The key evidence is that the cotyledons remain below the soil.</p>
