<h2>Starting Seed Germination</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Germination is the process by which a viable seed resumes growth and develops into a seedling.</p>

<h3>What Happens First?</h3>

<p>When a suitable seed absorbs water, its tissues become hydrated.</p>

<p>Metabolic activity increases.</p>

<p>Enzymes become active and stored food can be mobilised to provide materials and energy for growth.</p>

<h3>Why Water Is Important</h3>

<p>Water is required for metabolic reactions and helps activate the processes needed for growth.</p>

<h3>What Happens Next?</h3>

<p>The embryo resumes growth.</p>

<p>The radicle, which develops into the primary root, emerges first in many seeds.</p>

<p>The young root can then absorb water and mineral ions.</p>

<h3>Important Distinction</h3>

<p>Water absorption by a seed is an early event in germination.</p>

<p>It does not mean that the seed has completed germination.</p>

<h3>Exam Sequence</h3>

<p><strong>Water uptake → metabolic activity increases → stored food is mobilised → embryo grows → radicle emerges.</strong></p>
