<h2>Stored Food During Seed Germination</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>A germinating seedling initially has little or no ability to make enough food by photosynthesis.</p>

<p>It therefore depends on food reserves stored in the seed.</p>

<h3>Why Stored Food Is Needed</h3>

<p>Growth requires energy and raw materials.</p>

<p>Stored carbohydrates, fats and proteins can be broken down and used by the developing embryo.</p>

<h3>Respiration</h3>

<p>Stored organic compounds can be used in respiration to release energy.</p>

<p>This energy supports processes such as cell division, active transport and growth.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A seed germinates underground before its first leaves are exposed to enough light for substantial photosynthesis.</p>

<p>The young seedling can still grow because it uses stored food reserves.</p>

<h3>Transition to Photosynthesis</h3>

<p>Once leaves develop and contain functional chlorophyll, the seedling can begin making its own organic food through photosynthesis.</p>

<p>The seed reserve is therefore particularly important during the early stages of development.</p>

<h3>Exam Trap</h3>

<p>Do not say that germinating seeds obtain their energy directly from sunlight.</p>

<p>Before substantial photosynthesis begins, energy comes mainly from respiration of stored food.</p>
