<p>20°C → 60%</p>

<p>30°C → 85%</p>

<p>40°C → 50%</p>

<h3>Step 1: Describe the Pattern</h3>

<p>Germination increases from 10°C to 30°C.</p>

<p>It then decreases at 40°C.</p>

<h3>Step 2: Identify the Highest Value</h3>

<p>The highest measured germination percentage is 85% at 30°C.</p>

<h3>Step 3: Give a Possible Explanation</h3>

<p>Increasing temperature within a suitable range can increase the rate of enzyme-controlled metabolic reactions.</p>

<p>At higher temperatures, enzyme activity can be disrupted and proteins can become damaged.</p>

<h3>Important</h3>

<p>The data support what happened in the experiment.</p>

<p>They do not automatically prove the exact molecular mechanism responsible.</p>

<h3>Exam Method</h3>

<p><strong>Describe → compare → identify maximum/minimum → explain using biology.</strong></p>
