<h2>Seed Viability</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>A seed is <strong>viable</strong> if it is alive and capable of germinating when suitable conditions are provided.</p>

<h3>Why Viability Matters</h3>

<p>Germination depends not only on external conditions such as water, oxygen and suitable temperature.</p>

<p>The seed itself must also be capable of carrying out the processes required for germination.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>Two groups of seeds receive enough water, oxygen and a suitable temperature.</p>

<p>Group A has 90% germination.</p>

<p>Group B has 20% germination.</p>

<p>If the conditions were properly controlled, one possible explanation is that the groups differed in viability.</p>

<h3>How Viability Can Be Investigated</h3>

<p>A large sample of seeds can be placed under suitable germination conditions.</p>

<p>The percentage that successfully germinates gives evidence about the viability of the sample.</p>

<h3>Important Distinction</h3>

<p>A seed that does not germinate is not automatically non-viable.</p>

<p>It may be dormant or may not have received the conditions required for germination.</p>

<h3>Reasoning Chain</h3>

<p>Viable seed + suitable conditions → germination is possible.</p>

<p>Non-viable seed → cannot successfully germinate even when suitable conditions are provided.</p>
