<h2>Water Uptake at the Start of Germination</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>The first major physical event in germination is the uptake of water by the dry seed.</p>

<h3>What Happens When Water Enters?</h3>

<p>The seed tissues become hydrated.</p>

<p>Cells that were metabolically inactive become more active.</p>

<p>Enzymes involved in mobilisation of stored food become active.</p>

<p>Respiration increases as the embryo requires more energy.</p>

<h3>Why Water Is Not Simply "Food"</h3>

<p>Water does not provide the organic food reserves needed for growth.</p>

<p>Instead, it provides the conditions needed for biochemical processes to occur and allows stored substances to be mobilised.</p>

<h3>Worked Sequence</h3>

<p>Dry seed</p>

<p>↓</p>

<p>Water enters</p>

<p>↓</p>

<p>Seed tissues hydrate</p>

<p>↓</p>

<p>Metabolic activity increases</p>

<p>↓</p>

<p>Stored food is mobilised</p>

<p>↓</p>

<p>Respiration supplies energy</p>

<p>↓</p>

<p>Embryo grows</p>

<h3>Important</h3>

<p>Water uptake begins the process, but successful germination also requires other suitable conditions.</p>
