<h2>Why a Developing Seedling Needs Light</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Light becomes essential when a young plant begins relying on photosynthesis to manufacture organic food.</p>

<h3>What Light Provides</h3>

<p>Light supplies the energy required for photosynthesis.</p>

<p>In green plant cells, chlorophyll absorbs some of this light energy.</p>

<h3>Photosynthesis</h3>

<p>Carbon dioxide and water are used to produce glucose, with oxygen released.</p>

<p><strong>6CO₂ + 6H₂O → C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂</strong></p>

<h3>Why This Matters to a Seedling</h3>

<p>Glucose produced by photosynthesis can be used:</p>

<p>• in respiration to release energy</p>

<p>• to form cellulose for cell walls</p>

<p>• to form starch for storage</p>

<p>• to form other organic substances needed for growth</p>

<h3>Important Distinction</h3>

<p>A seed can begin germination in darkness because early growth can use stored reserves.</p>

<p>However, prolonged darkness prevents normal dependence on photosynthesis because there is no light energy for photosynthesis.</p>

<h3>Reasoning Chain</h3>

<p>Light → chlorophyll absorbs light energy → photosynthesis → glucose production → continued growth.</p>
