<p>Positive tropism means growth towards the stimulus.</p>

<p>For example, a young shoot growing towards light shows positive phototropism.</p>

<h3>Negative Tropism</h3>

<p>Negative tropism means growth away from the stimulus.</p>

<p>For example, roots commonly show negative phototropism because they tend to grow away from light.</p>

<h3>Do Not Confuse Direction of Growth</h3>

<p>The words positive and negative do not mean "good" and "bad."</p>

<p>They describe the direction of growth.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A root grows away from a light source.</p>

<p><strong>What type of response is this?</strong></p>

<p>It is negative phototropism.</p>

<p>The stimulus is light, and the root grows away from it.</p>

<h3>Another Example</h3>

<p>A shoot grows towards light.</p>

<p>This is positive phototropism.</p>

<h3>Exam Rule</h3>

<p><strong>Towards stimulus → positive.</strong></p>

<p><strong>Away from stimulus → negative.</strong></p>
