<h2>Gravitropism</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Gravitropism, also called geotropism, is the directional growth response of a plant part to gravity.</p>

<h3>Roots</h3>

<p>Roots generally grow in the direction of gravity.</p>

<p>This is called positive gravitropism.</p>

<h3>Shoots</h3>

<p>Shoots generally grow against the direction of gravity.</p>

<p>This is called negative gravitropism.</p>

<h3>Why This Matters</h3>

<p>Root growth towards gravity helps roots extend into the soil.</p>

<p>Shoot growth against gravity helps shoots emerge from the soil and grow upward.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A seed germinates while lying on its side.</p>

<p>After several days, the root curves downward and the shoot curves upward.</p>

<p><strong>What explains this response?</strong></p>

<p>The root shows positive gravitropism, while the shoot shows negative gravitropism.</p>

<h3>Important Point</h3>

<p>Gravitropism is a growth response, not a rapid movement of the whole plant.</p>

<p>The curvature develops because growth is unequal on different sides of the organ.</p>
