<p>In roots, relatively high auxin concentrations can inhibit elongation.</p>

<p>This difference is important when comparing root and shoot tropisms.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A germinating seed is placed horizontally.</p>

<p>After several hours, the root curves downward.</p>

<p>The response is positive gravitropism.</p>

<h3>Key Idea</h3>

<p><strong>Root growth direction → gravity.</strong></p>

<p><strong>Unequal growth → bending.</strong></p>
