<h2>Improving a Phototropism Experiment</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>A basic experiment can be improved to produce stronger and more reliable evidence.</p>

<h3>Use More Plants</h3>

<p>Using several seedlings reduces the influence of unusual individual plants.</p>

<h3>Repeat the Investigation</h3>

<p>Repeating the experiment allows consistent patterns to be identified.</p>

<h3>Calculate a Mean</h3>

<p>If several measurements are collected, a mean can provide a useful summary.</p>

<p><strong>Mean = total of measurements ÷ number of measurements.</strong></p>

<h3>Standardise the Starting Conditions</h3>

<p>Seedlings should have similar age and starting size.</p>

<p>Otherwise, differences in growth may be caused by the starting condition rather than the treatment.</p>

<h3>Measure Quantitatively</h3>

<p>Instead of simply recording "bent" or "did not bend," measure the angle of bending.</p>

<p>Quantitative measurements provide more detailed evidence.</p>

<h3>Control Other Conditions</h3>

<p>Temperature, water, nutrients and duration should be controlled.</p>

<h3>Strong Experimental Design</h3>

<p><strong>Change one factor → control other important factors → use repeats → collect measurements → calculate a mean → compare groups.</strong></p>
