<h2>Auxin Distribution During Phototropism</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>The direction of light affects how auxin is distributed in a young shoot.</p>

<p>When light shines from one side, auxin moves towards the shaded side of the shoot.</p>

<p>The shaded side therefore has a greater concentration of auxin than the illuminated side.</p>

<h3>Effect on Shoot Cells</h3>

<p>In shoots, auxin promotes cell elongation.</p>

<p>The higher auxin concentration on the shaded side causes cells there to elongate more.</p>

<p>The shaded side becomes longer than the illuminated side.</p>

<p>This difference in length causes the shoot to curve towards the light.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>Light shines from the left side of a young shoot.</p>

<p>Auxin becomes more concentrated on the right side.</p>

<p>The right side elongates more.</p>

<p>The right side therefore becomes longer than the left side.</p>

<p>The shoot bends towards the left, where the light is coming from.</p>

<h3>Reasoning Rule</h3>

<p>Do not stop at "auxin causes bending."</p>

<p>The complete explanation is:</p>

<p><strong>Unequal auxin distribution → unequal elongation → unequal side lengths → bending.</strong></p>
