<h2>Auxin and Cell Elongation</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Auxin is a plant growth hormone that can influence the elongation of cells, especially in young shoots.</p>

<h3>How Cell Elongation Produces Growth</h3>

<p>A young plant cell can increase in length.</p>

<p>Water enters the cell, increasing cell volume and helping the cell expand.</p>

<p>Auxin helps regulate this process.</p>

<p>As many cells elongate, the plant organ becomes longer.</p>

<h3>Growth Is Not Just Cell Division</h3>

<p>Cell division increases the number of cells.</p>

<p>Cell elongation increases the size of existing cells.</p>

<p>Both processes can contribute to plant growth.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A growing shoot contains 1,000 cells. Over time, the number of cells changes only slightly, but many existing cells become much longer.</p>

<p><strong>Can the shoot still increase in length?</strong></p>

<p>Yes.</p>

<p>Cell elongation can increase the length of the shoot even when cell number does not increase greatly.</p>

<h3>Reasoning Chain</h3>

<p><strong>Auxin influence → cell elongation → cells become longer → plant organ increases in length.</strong></p>
