<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>Two root regions receive different auxin concentrations.</p>

<p>Region A receives a low concentration within its growth-promoting range.</p>

<p>Region B receives a much higher concentration.</p>

<p>Region B shows less elongation.</p>

<p><strong>Why?</strong></p>

<p>Roots are sensitive to auxin, and a high auxin concentration can inhibit root cell elongation.</p>

<h3>Important Comparison</h3>

<p><strong>Shoots:</strong> auxin generally promotes elongation within the normal physiological range.</p>

<p><strong>Roots:</strong> high auxin concentrations can inhibit elongation.</p>

<h3>Exam Trap</h3>

<p>Never write "more auxin always means more growth."</p>

<p>The effect depends on the organ and the concentration.</p>
