<h2>Auxin Concentration and Growth Response</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>The relationship between auxin concentration and growth is not simply a straight line.</p>

<p>Different concentrations can produce different growth responses.</p>

<h3>Low Concentration</h3>

<p>A low auxin concentration may promote growth in a particular plant organ.</p>

<h3>Optimum Range</h3>

<p>There may be a concentration range at which growth is greatest under the conditions tested.</p>

<h3>High Concentration</h3>

<p>Beyond an appropriate concentration, auxin can inhibit growth.</p>

<h3>Why Graphs Matter</h3>

<p>An experiment can measure growth at several auxin concentrations.</p>

<p>The results can then be plotted to determine the relationship.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A root experiment produces the following pattern:</p>

<p>0 units auxin → 4 mm growth</p>

<p>1 unit → 7 mm growth</p>

<p>2 units → 9 mm growth</p>

<p>5 units → 5 mm growth</p>

<p>10 units → 1 mm growth</p>

<p><strong>What does the data show?</strong></p>

<p>Growth increased up to 2 units, then decreased as auxin concentration increased further.</p>

<h3>Correct Conclusion</h3>

<p>Under the conditions tested, 2 units produced the greatest measured root growth.</p>

<p>Do not conclude that 2 units is universally optimal for every root or every condition.</p>

<h3>Scientific Language</h3>

<p>Use phrases such as <strong>"under the conditions tested"</strong> when the experiment has limited conditions.</p>
