<h2>Auxin Production in Plants</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Auxin is a plant growth hormone involved in regulating growth and development.</p>

<p>Auxin is produced in growing regions of plants, including shoot tips.</p>

<h3>Why the Shoot Tip Matters</h3>

<p>The shoot tip is an actively growing region.</p>

<p>Auxin produced there can move to other parts of the shoot and influence cell growth.</p>

<h3>What Auxin Does</h3>

<p>Auxin can stimulate cell elongation in shoots at suitable concentrations.</p>

<p>It therefore helps regulate the direction and pattern of shoot growth.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A shoot tip is producing auxin.</p>

<p>The auxin moves down the shoot and affects the elongation of cells below the tip.</p>

<p>The result is controlled growth of the shoot.</p>

<h3>Important Distinction</h3>

<p>Auxin is not a nutrient.</p>

<p>It is a chemical messenger that regulates growth processes.</p>

<h3>Exam Rule</h3>

<p>When explaining auxin, connect it to <strong>growth regulation</strong>, especially cell elongation and directional growth.</p>
