<h2>Primary Growth Compared with Secondary Growth</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Plants can increase in both length and thickness.</p>

<h3>Primary Growth</h3>

<p>Primary growth is an increase in the length of roots and shoots.</p>

<p>It occurs mainly at apical meristems.</p>

<p>It allows roots to extend through the soil and shoots to extend upward.</p>

<h3>Secondary Growth</h3>

<p>Secondary growth is an increase in the thickness or girth of roots and stems.</p>

<p>It occurs mainly through the activity of lateral meristems such as the vascular cambium.</p>

<h3>Direct Comparison</h3>

<p><strong>Primary growth → length.</strong></p>

<p><strong>Secondary growth → thickness.</strong></p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A tree grows from 2 m to 4 m tall while its trunk also becomes wider.</p>

<p>The increase in height is mainly primary growth.</p>

<p>The increase in trunk thickness is secondary growth.</p>

<h3>Exam Trap</h3>

<p>Do not identify growth simply from the age of the plant.</p>

<p>A young plant can undergo primary growth, while an established woody plant can undergo both primary and secondary growth.</p>
