<h2>Secondary Growth in Stems</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Secondary growth causes the stem of many woody plants to increase in diameter.</p>

<p>The vascular cambium produces new vascular tissues as the stem increases in girth.</p>

<h3>Why Woody Stems Become Thicker</h3>

<p>As the plant continues growing, additional tissues are produced around the existing stem.</p>

<p>This increases the diameter of the stem.</p>

<h3>Support</h3>

<p>A larger stem can provide structural support for a larger plant.</p>

<p>This is especially important in trees and other long-lived woody plants.</p>

<h3>Transport</h3>

<p>Secondary growth also results in the production of additional vascular tissue.</p>

<p>This supports the plant''s increasing transport requirements as the plant becomes larger.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A young tree is initially thin. Several years later, its trunk has a much larger diameter.</p>

<p><strong>What biological process explains this change?</strong></p>

<p>Secondary growth has increased the girth of the trunk.</p>

<h3>Important Point</h3>

<p>Secondary growth is not simply "the stem growing older."</p>

<p>It is a biological growth process involving lateral meristems and production of additional tissues.</p>
