<h2>Secondary Growth and Lateral Meristems</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Secondary growth is the increase in the thickness or girth of a plant stem or root.</p>

<p>It differs from primary growth, which mainly increases the length of the plant.</p>

<h3>Where Does Secondary Growth Occur?</h3>

<p>Secondary growth occurs through the activity of <strong>lateral meristems</strong>.</p>

<p>Lateral meristems contain cells that can divide by mitosis and produce new cells.</p>

<p>The main lateral meristems involved are the <strong>vascular cambium</strong> and <strong>cork cambium</strong>.</p>

<h3>Vascular Cambium</h3>

<p>The vascular cambium produces new vascular tissues.</p>

<p>It produces secondary xylem towards the inside of the stem and secondary phloem towards the outside.</p>

<h3>Why Does This Increase Girth?</h3>

<p>As new cells are produced and added to the stem, the stem becomes wider.</p>

<h3>Primary vs Secondary Growth</h3>

<p><strong>Primary growth:</strong> increase in length.</p>

<p><strong>Secondary growth:</strong> increase in thickness or girth.</p>

<h3>Exam Rule</h3>

<p>If a question asks how a woody stem becomes thicker, think of <strong>lateral meristems and secondary growth</strong>, not the shoot apical meristem.</p>
