<h2>Vascular Cambium and the Production of Vascular Tissue</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>The <strong>vascular cambium</strong> is a layer of dividing cells involved in secondary growth.</p>

<p>It produces new vascular tissue on both sides of the cambium.</p>

<h3>Towards the Inside</h3>

<p>The vascular cambium produces <strong>secondary xylem</strong> towards the centre of the stem.</p>

<p>Secondary xylem contributes to the woody part of the stem and helps transport water and mineral ions.</p>

<h3>Towards the Outside</h3>

<p>The vascular cambium produces <strong>secondary phloem</strong> towards the outside of the stem.</p>

<p>Phloem transports assimilates such as sucrose.</p>

<h3>Worked Reasoning</h3>

<p>A woody stem becomes wider as the vascular cambium continues producing new cells.</p>

<p>More secondary xylem is added inside the cambium and secondary phloem is added outside it.</p>

<p>Repeated production of these tissues contributes to an increase in stem girth.</p>

<h3>Key Direction</h3>

<p><strong>Vascular cambium → secondary xylem inward + secondary phloem outward.</strong></p>
