  (1745, 1745, '
    <h2>Common and Proper Nouns</h2>

    <p>Nouns can be classified according to whether they name
    a general category or a particular person, place, organisation
    or other named entity.</p>

    <h3>1. Common nouns</h3>

    <p>A <strong>common noun</strong> names a general class of
    people, places, animals, objects or things.</p>

    <ul>
      <li>teacher</li>
      <li>county</li>
      <li>river</li>
      <li>school</li>
      <li>mountain</li>
    </ul>

    <p>These words do not identify one particular named entity
    on their own.</p>

    <h3>2. Proper nouns</h3>

    <p>A <strong>proper noun</strong> identifies a particular
    person, place, organisation, institution or other named entity.</p>

    <ul>
      <li>Wangari Maathai</li>
      <li>Kenya</li>
      <li>Tana River</li>
      <li>Kenyatta University</li>
      <li>Mount Kenya</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Proper nouns normally begin with capital letters. The
    capitalisation of a multiword name follows the conventional
    spelling of that name.</p>

    <h3>3. Compare the general category with the specific name</h3>

    <ul>
      <li>We crossed a <strong>river</strong>. — common noun.</li>
      <li>We crossed the <strong>Tana River</strong>. — proper noun
      naming a particular river.</li>
      <li>She studies at a <strong>university</strong>. — common noun.</li>
      <li>She studies at <strong>Kenyatta University</strong>. —
      proper noun naming a particular institution.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>A word does not become a proper noun simply because it
    appears important in a sentence. Its role depends on whether
    it is part of a particular name.</p>

    <h3>4. Capitalisation in context</h3>

    <p>Compare:</p>

    <blockquote>
      The principal visited the school.
    </blockquote>

    <blockquote>
      Principal Wambui visited the school.
    </blockquote>

    <p>In the first sentence, <em>principal</em> is a common noun
    used as a job title. In the second, it forms part of a title
    attached to a person''s name and is capitalised.</p>

    <p>Capitalisation can depend on position and naming convention.
    The first word of a sentence is capitalised even when it is
    not a proper noun.</p>

    <h3>5. Avoid confusing names with categories</h3>

    <p>In “Lake Victoria is a large lake,” <em>Lake Victoria</em>
    is the proper name, while the later word <em>lake</em> is a
    common noun identifying the type of geographical feature.</p>

    <h3>Worked example</h3>

    <p><strong>Sentence:</strong> Our class visited Nairobi
    National Museum during the school holiday.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Find the general category:
    <em>class</em>, <em>museum</em> and <em>holiday</em> are common
    nouns in this sentence.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Identify the particular named
    institution: <em>Nairobi National Museum</em>.</p>

    <p><strong>Answer:</strong> <em>Nairobi National Museum</em>
    is a proper noun; the other identified nouns are common nouns.</p>
  ', 1),
  (1746, 1746, '
    <h2>Concrete and Abstract Nouns</h2>

    <p>Nouns can name things experienced through the senses or
    ideas, qualities, states and feelings that are not physical
    objects.</p>

    <h3>1. Concrete nouns</h3>

    <p>A <strong>concrete noun</strong> names something physical
    that can be experienced through one or more senses.</p>

    <ul>
      <li><em>Drum</em> — can be heard and touched.</li>
      <li><em>Perfume</em> — can be smelled.</li>
      <li><em>Ice</em> — can be seen and felt.</li>
      <li><em>Bell</em> — can be heard.</li>
      <li><em>Orange</em> — can be seen, touched, smelled and tasted.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>A concrete noun does not have to be visible at every moment.
    Something can still be physical even when it cannot currently
    be seen.</p>

    <h3>2. Abstract nouns</h3>

    <p>An <strong>abstract noun</strong> names an idea, quality,
    state, feeling or concept rather than a physical object.</p>

    <ul>
      <li><em>Honesty</em> — a quality.</li>
      <li><em>Fear</em> — a feeling.</li>
      <li><em>Childhood</em> — a stage of life.</li>
      <li><em>Freedom</em> — a concept or state.</li>
      <li><em>Knowledge</em> — what someone knows or understands.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Abstract nouns can describe real and important things.
    They are called abstract because they name concepts, qualities
    or states rather than physical objects.</p>

    <h3>3. Recognising nouns in context</h3>

    <p>Consider:</p>

    <blockquote>
      Her kindness brought comfort to the frightened child.
    </blockquote>

    <ul>
      <li><em>Kindness</em> is an abstract noun naming a quality.</li>
      <li><em>Comfort</em> is an abstract noun naming a state or feeling.</li>
      <li><em>Child</em> is a concrete noun naming a person.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>The classification depends on the meaning of the word
    in its context.</p>

    <h3>4. Abstract nouns formed from other words</h3>

    <p>Some abstract nouns are formed from adjectives or verbs.</p>

    <ul>
      <li>kind → kindness</li>
      <li>brave → bravery</li>
      <li>happy → happiness</li>
      <li>decide → decision</li>
      <li>perform → performance</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Not every noun formed this way is abstract in every possible
    use, so check the meaning of the word in the sentence.</p>

    <h3>5. Avoid classifying by appearance alone</h3>

    <p>A noun can refer to something that is physically represented
    but still name an abstract concept.</p>

    <p>For example, in “The judge valued justice,” <em>justice</em>
    names an abstract principle, even though a court may use
    physical documents and procedures to pursue it.</p>

    <h3>Worked example</h3>

    <p><strong>Sentence:</strong> The athlete''s determination
    helped her carry the trophy home.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the nouns:
    <em>athlete</em>, <em>determination</em>, <em>trophy</em> and
    <em>home</em> as used here in the expression.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> <em>Athlete</em> and <em>trophy</em>
    name physical entities.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> <em>Determination</em> names a
    quality or mental resolve.</p>

    <p><strong>Answer:</strong> <em>Athlete</em> and <em>trophy</em>
    are concrete nouns; <em>determination</em> is an abstract noun.
    Here, <em>home</em> functions as an adverbial expression of
    destination rather than a noun.</p>
