  (1747, 1747, '
    <h2>Countable and Uncountable Nouns</h2>

    <p>Nouns differ in whether they can normally be counted as
    separate units. This distinction affects singular and plural
    forms, articles and quantifiers.</p>

    <h3>1. Countable nouns</h3>

    <p><strong>Countable nouns</strong> name things that can be
    counted individually.</p>

    <ul>
      <li>one book, two books</li>
      <li>one student, five students</li>
      <li>one chair, several chairs</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Countable nouns have singular and plural forms in ordinary
    uses. A singular countable noun generally needs a determiner
    when used as a complete noun phrase.</p>

    <ul>
      <li>She borrowed <em>a book</em>.</li>
      <li>She borrowed <em>the book</em>.</li>
      <li>She borrowed <em>three books</em>.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>2. Uncountable nouns</h3>

    <p><strong>Uncountable nouns</strong> usually name substances,
    materials, activities or concepts that are not counted as
    separate units in the relevant meaning.</p>

    <ul>
      <li>water</li>
      <li>rice</li>
      <li>information</li>
      <li>furniture</li>
      <li>advice</li>
      <li>equipment</li>
    </ul>

    <p>In their usual uncountable senses, these nouns do not take
    ordinary plural forms and are not used directly with
    <em>a</em> or <em>an</em>.</p>

    <p>Say:</p>

    <ul>
      <li>some information, not normally <em>an information</em></li>
      <li>a piece of advice, not normally <em>an advice</em></li>
      <li>some furniture, not normally <em>furnitures</em></li>
    </ul>

    <h3>3. Measuring uncountable nouns</h3>

    <p>Use a suitable unit or container to count an amount of
    an uncountable substance.</p>

    <ul>
      <li>a glass of water</li>
      <li>two bags of rice</li>
      <li>three pieces of information</li>
      <li>four items of furniture</li>
    </ul>

    <p>The unit is countable even when the substance or concept
    named by the main noun is not.</p>

    <h3>4. Quantifiers with countable and uncountable nouns</h3>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>Many / few:</strong> many books, few students.</li>
      <li><strong>Much / little:</strong> much water, little time.</li>
      <li><strong>Some / enough:</strong> some books, some water;
      enough chairs, enough water.</li>
      <li><strong>A number of:</strong> a number of questions.</li>
      <li><strong>An amount of:</strong> an amount of money.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>5. Nouns that can be countable or uncountable</h3>

    <p>Some nouns change meaning depending on how they are used.</p>

    <ul>
      <li>There is <em>chicken</em> in the pot. — meat, uncountable.</li>
      <li>There are three <em>chickens</em> in the yard. — animals,
      countable.</li>
      <li>She drank <em>some coffee</em>. — a drink, uncountable.</li>
      <li>We ordered two <em>coffees</em>. — two servings, countable.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Always classify a noun according to the meaning intended
    in its sentence.</p>

    <h3>Worked example</h3>

    <p><strong>Sentence:</strong> The shopkeeper sold three bags
    of rice and some sugar.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the quantities:
    <em>three bags</em> is countable.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> <em>Rice</em> and <em>sugar</em>
    are uncountable in this context.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> The bags provide countable units
    for measuring rice; <em>some</em> quantifies sugar without
    specifying an exact number of units.</p>

    <p><strong>Answer:</strong> <em>Bags</em> is countable;
    <em>rice</em> and <em>sugar</em> are uncountable in this sentence.</p>
