    <h2>Reflexive and Emphatic Pronouns</h2>

    <p>Reflexive and emphatic pronouns have the same forms, such
    as <em>myself</em>, <em>yourself</em>, <em>herself</em>,
    <em>himself</em>, <em>itself</em>, <em>ourselves</em>,
    <em>yourselves</em> and <em>themselves</em>. Their function
    depends on how they are used.</p>

    <h3>1. Reflexive pronouns</h3>

    <p>A reflexive pronoun is used when the object refers back
    to the subject of the same clause.</p>

    <ul>
      <li>She blamed <em>herself</em>.</li>
      <li>The boy injured <em>himself</em>.</li>
      <li>We prepared <em>ourselves</em> for the examination.</li>
      <li>The cat cleaned <em>itself</em>.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>In “She blamed herself,” the person doing the blaming
    and the person receiving the blame are the same.</p>

    <h3>2. Emphatic pronouns</h3>

    <p>An emphatic pronoun adds emphasis to a noun or pronoun.
    It is not required as the object of the verb.</p>

    <ul>
      <li>The principal <em>herself</em> welcomed the visitors.</li>
      <li>I <em>myself</em> checked the figures.</li>
      <li>The students <em>themselves</em> organised the event.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>In “The principal herself welcomed the visitors,” removing
    <em>herself</em> leaves a grammatically complete sentence:
    “The principal welcomed the visitors.”</p>

    <h3>3. How to distinguish the two functions</h3>

    <p>Ask what role the pronoun plays.</p>

    <ul>
      <li><em>He taught himself to swim.</em> — reflexive; the
      pronoun is the object of <em>taught</em>.</li>
      <li><em>He himself taught the class.</em> — emphatic; it
      emphasises who taught the class.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>A useful test for emphatic use is whether the pronoun can
    be removed without changing the basic grammatical structure.
    The emphasis will disappear, but the sentence will remain complete.</p>

    <h3>4. Reflexive pronouns must fit the subject</h3>

    <ul>
      <li>I → myself</li>
      <li>you, singular → yourself</li>
      <li>he → himself</li>
      <li>she → herself</li>
      <li>it → itself</li>
      <li>we → ourselves</li>
      <li>you, plural → yourselves</li>
      <li>they → themselves</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Choose the correct form according to the subject and the
    meaning of the sentence.</p>

    <h3>5. Avoid using reflexive pronouns unnecessarily</h3>

    <p>Reflexive forms should not replace ordinary subject or
    object pronouns without a grammatical reason.</p>

    <ul>
      <li>Standard: The teacher spoke to <em>me</em>.</li>
      <li>Unnecessary in ordinary standard usage: The teacher
      spoke to <em>myself</em>.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Use <em>myself</em> when the meaning is reflexive or emphatic,
    not simply to make a sentence sound more formal.</p>

    <h3>Worked example</h3>

    <p><strong>Sentence A:</strong> Akinyi taught herself to code.</p>

    <p><strong>Sentence B:</strong> Akinyi herself designed the programme.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> In sentence A, <em>herself</em>
    receives the action of <em>taught</em> and refers back to Akinyi.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> In sentence B, <em>herself</em>
    emphasises Akinyi''s role.</p>

    <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Sentence A uses a reflexive pronoun;
    sentence B uses an emphatic pronoun.</p>
