    <h2>Demonstrative Pronouns</h2>

    <p><strong>Demonstrative pronouns</strong> point to particular
    people or things. The main forms are <em>this</em>,
    <em>that</em>, <em>these</em> and <em>those</em>.</p>

    <h3>1. This and these</h3>

    <p><em>This</em> usually refers to one person or thing near
    the speaker or closely connected to the current situation.
    <em>These</em> refers to more than one.</p>

    <ul>
      <li><em>This</em> is my notebook.</li>
      <li><em>These</em> are my notes.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>2. That and those</h3>

    <p><em>That</em> usually refers to one person or thing farther
    away or less immediate. <em>Those</em> refers to more than one.</p>

    <ul>
      <li><em>That</em> belongs to the librarian.</li>
      <li><em>Those</em> are the books we ordered.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Distance can be physical, but demonstratives can also
    refer to time, ideas or information in a conversation.</p>

    <h3>3. Pronouns and determiners</h3>

    <p>The same four words can function as pronouns or determiners.</p>

    <ul>
      <li><em>This is expensive.</em> — <em>This</em> stands alone
      and functions as a pronoun.</li>
      <li><em>This phone is expensive.</em> — <em>This</em> accompanies
      the noun <em>phone</em> and functions as a determiner.</li>
      <li><em>Those are broken.</em> — <em>Those</em> functions
      as a pronoun.</li>
      <li><em>Those chairs are broken.</em> — <em>Those</em>
      accompanies the noun <em>chairs</em>.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>4. Agreement with demonstratives</h3>

    <p>Use the singular forms with singular nouns and the plural
    forms with plural nouns.</p>

    <ul>
      <li>this chair</li>
      <li>that chair</li>
      <li>these chairs</li>
      <li>those chairs</li>
    </ul>

    <p>A common error is using <em>this</em> with a plural noun
    or <em>these</em> with a singular noun.</p>

    <h3>5. Referring to ideas and events</h3>

    <p>Demonstratives can refer to something already mentioned.</p>

    <blockquote>
      The bus arrived forty minutes late. That caused several
      learners to miss the opening announcement.
    </blockquote>

    <p>Here, <em>that</em> refers to the late arrival, not to
    a physical object.</p>

    <p>When the reference is unclear, repeat the relevant noun
    phrase instead of relying on an ambiguous demonstrative.</p>

    <h3>Worked example</h3>

    <p><strong>Sentence:</strong> These are the reports we
    discussed, but those belong to another department.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> <em>These</em> stands alone and
    points to the reports being discussed.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> <em>Those</em> also stands alone
    and points to a different group of reports.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Both words refer to plural items.</p>

    <p><strong>Answer:</strong> <em>These</em> and <em>those</em>
    are demonstrative pronouns in this sentence.</p>
