    <h2>Main and Auxiliary Verbs</h2>

    <p>A <strong>main verb</strong> expresses the central action, event or state in a clause. An <strong>auxiliary verb</strong> helps the main verb express tense, aspect, voice, questions, negatives or other meanings.</p>

    <h3>1. Main verbs</h3>
    <p>In each sentence, the main verb carries the central meaning.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Amina <strong>reads</strong> every evening.</li>
      <li>The learners <strong>completed</strong> the assignment.</li>
      <li>We <strong>understand</strong> the instructions.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>2. Primary auxiliary verbs</h3>
    <p>The primary auxiliaries are <strong>be, have</strong> and <strong>do</strong>. They can function as main verbs or as auxiliaries.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Be:</strong> The students <em>are preparing</em> for the examination. Here, <em>are</em> helps the main verb <em>preparing</em>.</li>
      <li><strong>Have:</strong> She <em>has finished</em> her work. Here, <em>has</em> helps the main verb <em>finished</em>.</li>
      <li><strong>Do:</strong> <em>Do</em> you understand? Here, <em>do</em> helps form a question.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>3. The same word can have different functions</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><em>They are happy.</em> — <strong>are</strong> is the main verb linking the subject to a description.</li>
      <li><em>They are working.</em> — <strong>are</strong> is an auxiliary; <strong>working</strong> is the main verb.</li>
      <li><em>She has a pen.</em> — <strong>has</strong> is the main verb meaning possesses.</li>
      <li><em>She has written a letter.</em> — <strong>has</strong> is an auxiliary helping <strong>written</strong>.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>4. Modal auxiliaries</h3>
    <p>Words such as <em>can, could, may, might, must, shall, should, will</em> and <em>would</em> are modal auxiliaries. They express meanings such as ability, possibility, obligation and intention.</p>
    <p>Example: <em>You must submit the assignment.</em> — <em>must</em> is the auxiliary and <em>submit</em> is the main verb.</p>

    <h3>How to identify the verbs</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Find the action, event or state being expressed.</li>
      <li>Identify the verb carrying that central meaning.</li>
      <li>Check whether another verb helps it form a tense, question, negative or passive construction.</li>
    </ol>
