  (1772, 1772, '
    <h2>Past Continuous</h2>

    <p>The past continuous describes an action that was in progress at a particular time in the past. It is formed with <strong>was/were + verb-ing</strong>.</p>

    <h3>1. Positive statements</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>I <strong>was studying</strong> at eight last night.</li>
      <li>She <strong>was cooking</strong> when the lights went out.</li>
      <li>They <strong>were playing</strong> football after school.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>2. Choosing was or were</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>I, he, she, it → <strong>was</strong></li>
      <li>You, we, they → <strong>were</strong></li>
    </ul>

    <h3>3. Negative statements and questions</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>He <strong>was not sleeping</strong>.</li>
      <li>They <strong>were not listening</strong>.</li>
      <li><strong>Was</strong> she reading?</li>
      <li>What <strong>were</strong> you doing?</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>4. An ongoing action interrupted by another event</h3>
    <p>The past continuous often describes the background action, while the past simple describes the event that happened during it.</p>
    <p><em>I was walking home when it started raining.</em></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>was walking</strong> — action already in progress.</li>
      <li><strong>started</strong> — event that occurred during that action.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>5. Two actions in progress at the same time</h3>
    <p>The past continuous can describe simultaneous activities.</p>
    <p><em>While the teacher was explaining the problem, the learners were taking notes.</em></p>

    <h3>6. Past simple or past continuous?</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><em>The phone rang at nine.</em> — reports an event.</li>
      <li><em>The phone was ringing when I entered.</em> — describes an ongoing action at that time.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Do not assume that every sentence containing <em>when</em> requires the past simple. Choose the tense according to the meaning.</p>

    <h3>How to form it correctly</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Use <em>was</em> or <em>were</em> according to the subject.</li>
      <li>Add the -ing form of the main verb.</li>
      <li>Use the past continuous for the ongoing action and the past simple when reporting a completed event, where that distinction fits the meaning.</li>
    </ol>
