  (1779, 1779, '
    <h2>Distinguishing the Subject from Nearby Nouns</h2>

    <p>A verb agrees with its grammatical subject, not necessarily the noun closest to it. Extra phrases can place a singular or plural noun near the verb and make agreement confusing.</p>

    <h3>1. Ignore nouns inside prepositional phrases</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><em>The colour of the uniforms <strong>is</strong> blue.</em></li>
      <li><em>The uniforms of the team <strong>are</strong> blue.</em></li>
      <li><em>The teacher with several learners <strong>is</strong> outside.</em></li>
    </ul>
    <p>Identify the head noun of the subject phrase before selecting the verb.</p>

    <h3>2. Do not let a nearby noun control agreement</h3>
    <p>Compare these sentences:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><em>The box of old books <strong>belongs</strong> to Amina.</em> — subject head: <strong>box</strong>.</li>
      <li><em>The old books in the box <strong>belong</strong> to Amina.</em> — subject head: <strong>books</strong>.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>3. Separate the subject from an inserted phrase</h3>
    <p>An expression between the subject and verb may add information without changing the subject''s number.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><em>The principal, along with the teachers, <strong>is</strong> attending.</em></li>
      <li><em>The learners, as well as their class teacher, <strong>are</strong> attending.</em></li>
    </ul>
    <p>Phrases such as <em>along with</em>, <em>as well as</em> and <em>together with</em> do not normally create a compound subject.</p>

    <h3>4. Find the subject before checking the verb</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Locate the verb that needs checking.</li>
      <li>Identify the clause''s subject.</li>
      <li>Find the head noun or pronoun.</li>
      <li>Ignore nearby nouns that belong to descriptive or inserted phrases.</li>
      <li>Choose the verb that agrees with the actual subject.</li>
    </ol>
