  (1780, 1780, '
    <h2>Singular Subjects in Present Simple</h2>

    <p>In the present simple, most third-person singular subjects take a main verb ending in <strong>-s</strong> or <strong>-es</strong>. The subject may be a singular noun, a singular pronoun such as <em>he</em> or <em>she</em>, or a singular noun phrase.</p>

    <h3>1. Basic agreement</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>The learner <strong>reads</strong> every evening.</li>
      <li>My sister <strong>works</strong> in town.</li>
      <li>He <strong>plays</strong> basketball.</li>
      <li>The machine <strong>makes</strong> a loud noise.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>2. Common spelling patterns</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Most verbs add -s: learn → learns.</li>
      <li>Many verbs ending in -sh, -ch, -ss, -x or -o add -es: wash → washes; watch → watches; go → goes.</li>
      <li>Consonant + y changes to -ies: carry → carries.</li>
      <li>Vowel + y takes -s: play → plays.</li>
      <li>Have → has; do → does; be → is.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>3. Singular subjects in longer phrases</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><em>The captain of the teams <strong>speaks</strong> first.</em></li>
      <li><em>The water in these bottles <strong>looks</strong> clean.</li>
      <li><em>Each learner in the class <strong>receives</strong> a booklet.</em></li>
    </ul>
    <p>Look at the head noun or pronoun. A plural noun inside the subject phrase does not automatically make the subject plural.</p>

    <h3>4. Negative statements and questions</h3>
    <p>Use <strong>does not + base verb</strong> for negatives and <strong>does + subject + base verb</strong> for questions.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>She <strong>does not understand</strong> the instruction.</li>
      <li><strong>Does</strong> she <strong>understand</strong> the instruction?</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The -s marking belongs to <em>does</em>; do not add it to the main verb in these constructions.</p>

    <h3>5. Common errors</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Incorrect: <em>He walk to school.</em></li>
      <li>Correct: <em>He walks to school.</em></li>
      <li>Incorrect: <em>Does she likes music?</em></li>
      <li>Correct: <em>Does she like music?</em></li>
    </ul>

    <h3>How to check agreement</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Confirm that the sentence is in the present simple.</li>
      <li>Identify whether the subject is third-person singular.</li>
      <li>Use the correct verb form, remembering that questions and negatives with <em>does</em> take the base form of the main verb.</li>
    </ol>
