  (1781, 1781, '
    <h2>Singular Subjects with Auxiliary Verbs</h2>

    <p>When a sentence contains an auxiliary verb, agreement depends on the auxiliary or verb form that carries it. The main verb may not take the usual third-person singular -s ending.</p>

    <h3>1. Be: am, is and are</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>I <strong>am</strong> ready.</li>
      <li>She <strong>is</strong> reading.</li>
      <li>The learner <strong>is</strong> prepared.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>For a singular third-person subject, use <em>is</em> in the present tense.</p>

    <h3>2. Have and has</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>He <strong>has</strong> completed the task.</li>
      <li>The school <strong>has</strong> introduced a new timetable.</li>
      <li>They <strong>have</strong> completed the task.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>3. Do and does</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>She <strong>does</strong> not play chess.</li>
      <li><strong>Does</strong> he understand the question?</li>
    </ul>
    <p>After <em>does</em>, the main verb uses its base form: <em>does not play</em>, not <em>does not plays</em>.</p>

    <h3>4. Modal auxiliaries</h3>
    <p>Modal auxiliaries such as <em>can, will, should, must</em> and <em>may</em> do not change form for a singular subject.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>She <strong>can solve</strong> the problem.</li>
      <li>He <strong>will arrive</strong> soon.</li>
      <li>The learner <strong>must revise</strong>.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>5. Auxiliary chains</h3>
    <p>In a longer verb group, check the first finite auxiliary for agreement.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>She <strong>has been studying</strong>.</li>
      <li>He <strong>is being interviewed</strong>.</li>
      <li>The learner <strong>will have finished</strong> by noon.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Common errors</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Incorrect: <em>She have finished.</em></li>
      <li>Correct: <em>She has finished.</em></li>
      <li>Incorrect: <em>He can solves it.</em></li>
      <li>Correct: <em>He can solve it.</em></li>
    </ul>

    <h3>How to check</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Find the subject.</li>
      <li>Identify the auxiliary or verb group.</li>
      <li>Check whether the auxiliary changes with the subject.</li>
      <li>Do not add a third-person singular ending to a main verb already following a modal or <em>does</em>.</li>
    </ol>
