  (1782, 1782, '
    <h2>Agreement with Plural Subjects</h2>

    <p>Plural subjects generally take plural verb forms. In the present simple, most main verbs use the base form without the third-person singular -s ending.</p>

    <h3>1. Basic present simple agreement</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>The learner <strong>reads</strong> daily.</li>
      <li>The learners <strong>read</strong> daily.</li>
      <li>She <strong>plays</strong> volleyball.</li>
      <li>They <strong>play</strong> volleyball.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>2. Plural subjects with be</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>The players <strong>are</strong> ready.</li>
      <li>The books <strong>are</strong> on the shelf.</li>
      <li>The teachers <strong>were</strong> present yesterday.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>3. Plural subjects with have and do</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>The learners <strong>have</strong> completed the work.</li>
      <li>They <strong>do</strong> not understand the question.</li>
      <li><strong>Do</strong> the students need help?</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>4. Do not rely on the nearest noun</h3>
    <p>The subject''s head noun determines agreement.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><em>The boxes of chalk <strong>are</strong> in the cupboard.</em></li>
      <li><em>The box of chalk <strong>is</strong> in the cupboard.</em></li>
    </ul>

    <h3>5. Plural subjects with modal auxiliaries</h3>
    <p>Modal auxiliaries keep the same form with singular and plural subjects.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>The learner <strong>will attend</strong>.</li>
      <li>The learners <strong>will attend</strong>.</li>
      <li>The learners <strong>can solve</strong> the problem.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>How to check agreement</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Identify the grammatical subject.</li>
      <li>Confirm that its head noun or pronoun is plural.</li>
      <li>Choose the matching verb form.</li>
      <li>Remember that plural subjects normally take the base form of a main verb in the present simple.</li>
    </ol>
