  (1784, 1784, '
    <h2>Compound Subjects</h2>

    <p>A compound subject contains two or more subjects linked within the same clause. The verb form depends on how the subjects are joined and whether they refer to separate things or a single combined idea.</p>

    <h3>1. Subjects joined by and</h3>
    <p>Two separate subjects joined by <strong>and</strong> usually take a plural verb.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><em>Grace and Peter <strong>are</strong> classmates.</em></li>
      <li><em>The teacher and the learners <strong>have</strong> arrived.</em></li>
      <li><em>Rice and beans <strong>are</strong> available today.</em></li>
    </ul>

    <h3>2. A single idea or unit</h3>
    <p>Some expressions joined by <em>and</em> are treated as one unit and can take a singular verb.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><em>Fish and chips <strong>is</strong> today''s special.</em> — the dish is treated as one item on the menu.</li>
      <li><em>Research and development <strong>is</strong> a major department here.</em> — the phrase names one department.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Meaning and context determine whether the expression is treated as one unit or as separate items.</p>

    <h3>3. Each subject can have its own modifiers</h3>
    <p>Do not mistake a long compound subject for a single subject merely because its words are close together.</p>
    <p><em>The head teacher of the school and the chairperson of the board <strong>are</strong> attending.</em></p>
    <p>There are two distinct people, so the compound subject is plural.</p>

    <h3>4. Subjects joined by and versus descriptive phrases</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><em>The teacher and the learner <strong>are</strong> present.</em> — two subjects joined by <em>and</em>.</li>
      <li><em>The teacher, together with the learner, <strong>is</strong> present.</em> — one subject plus an additional phrase.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>How to choose the verb</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Identify each subject in the compound.</li>
      <li>Check how the subjects are connected.</li>
      <li>For two separate subjects joined by <em>and</em>, normally use a plural verb.</li>
      <li>Check whether the expression names one recognised unit or combined idea.</li>
    </ol>
  ', 1),
  (1785, 1785, '
    <h2>Subjects Joined by And</h2>

    <p>When two or more separate subjects are joined by <strong>and</strong>, they usually form a plural subject and take a plural verb.</p>

    <h3>1. Two subjects</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><em>Mary and Joseph <strong>are</strong> studying.</em></li>
      <li><em>The dog and the cat <strong>sleep</strong> outside.</em></li>
      <li><em>Mathematics and English <strong>are</strong> compulsory subjects in this timetable.</em></li>
    </ul>

    <h3>2. More than two subjects</h3>
    <p>A list of separate subjects joined by <em>and</em> also normally takes a plural verb.</p>
    <p><em>The head teacher, the deputy and the class teachers <strong>have</strong> signed the report.</em></p>

    <h3>3. A singular verb for one combined unit</h3>
    <p>When the compound expression is understood as one unit, one dish, or one combined idea, a singular verb may be appropriate.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><em>Slow and steady <strong>wins</strong> the race.</em> — the phrase expresses a single idea.</li>
      <li><em>Peanut butter and jam <strong>is</strong> my usual sandwich filling.</em> — the expression names one combined filling.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>4. Each and every before compound subjects</h3>
    <p>When <em>each</em> or <em>every</em> is used before singular subjects joined by <em>and</em>, a singular verb is often used.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><em>Every teacher and learner <strong>is</strong> expected to attend.</em></li>
      <li><em>Each boy and girl <strong>has</strong> received a form.</em></li>
    </ul>

    <h3>5. Do not confuse and with an inserted phrase</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><em>The chairperson and the secretary <strong>are</strong> here.</em> — two subjects joined by <em>and</em>.</li>
      <li><em>The chairperson, as well as the secretary, <strong>is</strong> here.</em> — singular main subject plus an additional phrase.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>How to decide</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Find every subject joined by <em>and</em>.</li>
      <li>Determine whether they refer to separate entities or one unit.</li>
      <li>Use a plural verb for separate subjects, unless a specific construction calls for singular agreement.</li>
      <li>Check for <em>each</em> or <em>every</em>, which can affect the agreement pattern.</li>
    </ol>
