  (1787, 1787, '
    <h2>Special Subject Forms</h2>
    <p>Some subjects require extra attention because their grammatical form does not always make their number obvious.</p>

    <h3>1. Indefinite pronouns</h3>
    <p>Words such as <em>everyone, somebody, each</em> and <em>neither</em> are usually grammatically singular.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> Everyone <em>has</em> submitted the assignment.</p>

    <h3>2. Collective nouns</h3>
    <p>A collective noun names a group, such as a team, committee or family. In many formal school contexts, it takes a singular verb when the group acts as one unit.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> The committee <em>has</em> reached a decision.</p>
    <p>Plural agreement can occur in varieties of English, particularly British English, when members are considered individually. Follow the intended meaning and the variety required by the task.</p>

    <h3>3. Each, every, either and neither</h3>
    <p>When these words form the subject, they generally take singular verbs.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> Each of the candidates <em>is</em> prepared.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> Every desk and every chair <em>has</em> been cleaned.</p>

    <h3>How to check</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Find the actual subject, not a noun inside a phrase after it.</li>
      <li>Check whether the subject is an indefinite pronoun, collective noun or special expression.</li>
      <li>Choose the verb according to the subject''s grammatical number and intended meaning.</li>
    </ol>
