  (1789, 1789, '
    <h2>Collective Noun Agreement</h2>
    <p>A collective noun names a group of people, animals or things as one unit. Examples include <em>team, family, committee, class, audience</em> and <em>flock</em>.</p>

    <h3>1. The group acting as one unit</h3>
    <p>Use a singular verb when the group is considered as a single body.</p>
    <p><strong>Examples:</strong></p>
    <ul>
      <li>The class <em>is</em> taking an examination.</li>
      <li>The committee <em>has</em> approved the plan.</li>
      <li>The flock <em>moves</em> across the field.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>2. Members acting individually</h3>
    <p>In British English, a collective noun can take a plural verb when attention is on the individual members.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> The team <em>are</em> arguing among themselves.</p>
    <p>In American English, singular agreement is more common with collective nouns. In school assessments, use the variety and convention expected by the question.</p>

    <h3>3. Do not confuse a collective noun with a plural noun</h3>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> The team <em>is</em> ready. (one team)</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> The players <em>are</em> ready. (individual players, plural noun)</p>

    <h3>How to check</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Identify the collective noun.</li>
      <li>Ask whether the sentence treats the group as one unit or emphasises its members.</li>
      <li>Use the agreement convention appropriate to the sentence and required variety of English.</li>
    </ol>
  ', 1),
  (1790, 1790, '
    <h2>Each, Every, Either and Neither</h2>
    <p><em>Each, every, either</em> and <em>neither</em> commonly take singular verbs when they form the subject or determine the subject.</p>

    <h3>1. Each</h3>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> Each of the pupils <em>has</em> a textbook.</p>
    <p>The noun after <em>of</em> does not change the singular subject <em>each</em>.</p>

    <h3>2. Every</h3>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> Every participant <em>receives</em> a certificate.</p>
    <p>When two nouns are joined by <em>and</em> but each is preceded by <em>every</em>, the subject is generally treated as singular.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> Every boy and every girl <em>is</em> expected to attend.</p>

    <h3>3. Either and neither</h3>
    <p>When used alone to refer to one of two choices, these pronouns generally take singular verbs.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Either <em>is</em> acceptable.</li>
      <li>Neither <em>was</em> available.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>4. Either ... or and neither ... nor</h3>
    <p>When these expressions join two subjects, the verb generally agrees with the subject nearest to it.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> Neither the pupils nor the teacher <em>is</em> absent.</p>

    <h3>How to check</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Determine whether the word is the subject itself or part of a paired construction.</li>
      <li>Use singular agreement for each, every, either and neither when they independently form the subject.</li>
      <li>For either ... or and neither ... nor, check the nearer subject.</li>
    </ol>
  ', 1),
  (1791, 1791, '
    <h2>Agreement with Intervening Phrases</h2>
    <p>An intervening phrase comes between the subject and the verb. It may contain a noun that distracts you from the real subject.</p>

    <h3>1. Find the main subject</h3>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> The box of exercise books <em>is</em> on the table.</p>
    <p>The subject is <em>box</em>, not <em>exercise books</em>. Since <em>box</em> is singular, use <em>is</em>.</p>

    <h3>2. Ignore phrases beginning with of, with, in or along with</h3>
    <p><strong>Examples:</strong></p>
    <ul>
      <li>The captain, along with the players, <em>is</em> attending.</li>
      <li>The quality of the answers <em>is</em> improving.</li>
      <li>The books in the cupboard <em>belong</em> to the library.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The intervening phrase adds information but does not usually change the grammatical number of the main subject.</p>

    <h3>3. Check the subject, not the nearest noun</h3>
    <p><strong>Incorrect:</strong> The list of names <em>are</em> missing.</p>
    <p><strong>Correct:</strong> The list of names <em>is</em> missing.</p>

    <h3>How to solve it</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Locate the main verb.</li>
      <li>Ask who or what performs the action or is described.</li>
      <li>Remove the intervening phrase mentally.</li>
      <li>Make the verb agree with the main subject.</li>
    </ol>
  ', 1),
  (1792, 1792, '
    <h2>Correcting Subject–Verb Agreement Errors</h2>
    <p>To correct subject–verb agreement, identify the real subject, determine its grammatical number and choose the matching verb. Do not change a sentence by guessing from the nearest noun.</p>

    <h3>1. Find the subject</h3>
    <p><strong>Incorrect:</strong> The list of topics <em>are</em> on the noticeboard.</p>
    <p><strong>Correct:</strong> The list of topics <em>is</em> on the noticeboard.</p>
    <p>The subject is <em>list</em>, which is singular.</p>

    <h3>2. Check the subject''s number</h3>
    <p><strong>Incorrect:</strong> The learners <em>was</em> late.</p>
    <p><strong>Correct:</strong> The learners <em>were</em> late.</p>

    <h3>3. Check compound subjects</h3>
    <p><strong>Incorrect:</strong> A pen and a ruler <em>is</em> required.</p>
    <p><strong>Correct:</strong> A pen and a ruler <em>are</em> required.</p>
    <p>Two subjects joined by <em>and</em> generally take a plural verb.</p>

    <h3>4. Check special subjects</h3>
    <p><strong>Incorrect:</strong> Everyone <em>know</em> the rules.</p>
    <p><strong>Correct:</strong> Everyone <em>knows</em> the rules.</p>

    <h3>5. Use a repeatable correction method</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Find the main verb.</li>
      <li>Identify its subject.</li>
      <li>Ignore distracting phrases.</li>
      <li>Check whether the subject is singular or plural.</li>
      <li>Correct the verb and reread the full sentence.</li>
    </ol>
  ', 1),
  (1793, 1793, '
    <h2>Sentence Parts</h2>
    <p>A sentence can contain a subject, a predicate, objects and complements. Each part performs a different grammatical function.</p>

    <h3>1. Subject</h3>
    <p>The subject identifies who or what the sentence is about, or who or what performs the action.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> <em>The learners</em> completed the task.</p>

    <h3>2. Predicate</h3>
    <p>The predicate tells us something about the subject. It contains the verb and may include objects or other information.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> The learners <em>completed the task before lunch</em>.</p>

    <h3>3. Object</h3>
    <p>An object receives the action of a verb or identifies the person or thing affected by it.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> Amina opened <em>the window</em>.</p>

    <h3>4. Complement</h3>
    <p>A complement completes the meaning of a verb by identifying or describing the subject or object.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Subject complement: The room is <em>quiet</em>.</li>
      <li>Object complement: They elected Amina <em>chairperson</em>.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>How to analyse a sentence</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Find the verb.</li>
      <li>Identify the subject.</li>
      <li>Check whether the verb has an object.</li>
      <li>Check whether a complement identifies or describes the subject or object.</li>
    </ol>
  ', 1),
  (1794, 1794, '
    <h2>Subjects and Predicates</h2>
    <p>A complete sentence normally contains a subject and a predicate. The subject tells us who or what the sentence is about; the predicate tells us what the subject does, is or experiences.</p>

    <h3>1. Finding the subject</h3>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> <em>The new student</em> answered the question.</p>
    <p>The subject is the person who answered.</p>

    <h3>2. Finding the predicate</h3>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> The new student <em>answered the question confidently</em>.</p>
    <p>The predicate contains the verb and the information given about the subject.</p>

    <h3>3. Simple and complete subjects</h3>
    <p>The <strong>simple subject</strong> is the main noun or pronoun. The <strong>complete subject</strong> includes the words that describe or specify it.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> <em>The tall boy near the gate</em> waved.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Simple subject: <em>boy</em></li>
      <li>Complete subject: <em>The tall boy near the gate</em></li>
    </ul>

    <h3>4. Simple and complete predicates</h3>
    <p>The <strong>simple predicate</strong> is the main verb or verb phrase. The <strong>complete predicate</strong> includes the verb and the words that complete the information.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> The tall boy near the gate <em>waved to his friend</em>.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Simple predicate: <em>waved</em></li>
      <li>Complete predicate: <em>waved to his friend</em></li>
    </ul>

    <h3>How to find them</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Locate the main verb.</li>
      <li>Ask who or what the sentence is about.</li>
      <li>Mark the complete subject.</li>
      <li>Identify the predicate that tells us about the subject.</li>
    </ol>
  ', 1),
  (1795, 1795, '
    <h2>Direct and Indirect Objects</h2>
    <p>Some verbs can take two objects. The direct object identifies what is given, sent, shown or done. The indirect object identifies the person or recipient who receives it.</p>

    <h3>1. Direct object</h3>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> The teacher marked <em>the exercise</em>.</p>
    <p>Ask, “Marked what?” The answer, <em>the exercise</em>, is the direct object.</p>

    <h3>2. Indirect object</h3>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> The teacher gave <em>the learner</em> a book.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Gave what? <em>a book</em> — direct object.</li>
      <li>Gave a book to whom? <em>the learner</em> — indirect object.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>3. Two common sentence patterns</h3>
    <p><strong>Pattern A:</strong> Subject + verb + indirect object + direct object.</p>
    <p>Example: Musa sent <em>his friend</em> <em>a message</em>.</p>
    <p><strong>Pattern B:</strong> Subject + verb + direct object + to/for + recipient.</p>
    <p>Example: Musa sent <em>a message</em> to <em>his friend</em>.</p>

    <h3>4. Do not confuse an object with a subject complement</h3>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> They elected Amina captain.</p>
    <p><em>Amina</em> is the object, and <em>captain</em> is an object complement that identifies Amina''s new role.</p>

    <h3>How to identify objects</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Find the action verb.</li>
      <li>Ask what or whom the action affects to locate the direct object.</li>
      <li>If someone receives something, identify the recipient as the indirect object.</li>
      <li>Check whether the recipient is introduced by a preposition such as <em>to</em> or <em>for</em>.</li>
    </ol>
  ', 1),
  (1796, 1796, '
    <h2>Subject and Object Complements</h2>
    <p>A complement completes the meaning of a sentence by identifying or describing a subject or an object.</p>

    <h3>1. Subject complements</h3>
    <p>A subject complement follows a linking verb such as <em>be, seem, become, appear</em> or <em>feel</em>. It describes or identifies the subject.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>The classroom is <em>quiet</em>. (adjective describing the subject)</li>
      <li>Wanjiku became <em>the captain</em>. (noun phrase identifying the subject)</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>2. Object complements</h3>
    <p>An object complement follows an object and describes or identifies it.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>The class elected Wanjiku <em>captain</em>.</li>
      <li>The news made the learners <em>happy</em>.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>In the first example, <em>captain</em> identifies Wanjiku''s role. In the second, <em>happy</em> describes the learners.</p>

    <h3>3. Complements are not the same as objects</h3>
    <p>In “The learner became confident,” <em>confident</em> is a subject complement, not an object. The linking verb describes the subject rather than transferring an action to an object.</p>

    <h3>How to identify a complement</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Find the verb.</li>
      <li>Check whether it links the subject to a description or identity.</li>
      <li>If a word describes or renames the subject after a linking verb, it is a subject complement.</li>
      <li>If a word describes or renames the object, it is an object complement.</li>
    </ol>
  ', 1),
  (1797, 1797, '
    <h2>Recognising Phrases</h2>
    <p>A phrase is a group of related words that functions as a unit in a sentence. A phrase does not contain a complete subject–predicate relationship of its own.</p>

    <h3>1. Noun phrases</h3>
    <p>A noun phrase functions like a noun.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> <em>The tall girl near the window</em> answered.</p>

    <h3>2. Verb phrases</h3>
    <p>A verb phrase contains a main verb and may include auxiliary verbs.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> The learners <em>have been revising</em>.</p>

    <h3>3. Adjective phrases</h3>
    <p>An adjective phrase describes a noun or pronoun.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> The learner <em>proud of her progress</em> smiled.</p>

    <h3>4. Adverbial phrases</h3>
    <p>An adverbial phrase gives information such as when, where, how or why.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> The bus arrived <em>before sunrise</em>.</p>

    <h3>5. Prepositional phrases</h3>
    <p>A prepositional phrase begins with a preposition and includes its object.</p>
    <p><strong>Examples:</strong> <em>under the table</em>, <em>after the lesson</em>, <em>with great care</em>.</p>

    <h3>Phrase or clause?</h3>
    <p><em>After the examination</em> is a phrase. <em>After the examination ended</em> is a clause because it contains a subject and a verb.</p>

    <h3>How to identify a phrase</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Look for a group of words working together.</li>
      <li>Determine the group''s function in the sentence.</li>
      <li>Check whether it contains a subject and verb expressing a clause relationship.</li>
    </ol>
  ', 1),
  (1798, 1798, '
    <h2>Main Clauses</h2>
    <p>A main clause, also called an independent clause, contains a subject and a predicate and can stand alone as a complete sentence.</p>

    <h3>1. A complete thought</h3>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> The students completed the experiment.</p>
    <p>The clause has a subject, <em>the students</em>, and a predicate, <em>completed the experiment</em>. It can stand alone.</p>

    <h3>2. Main clauses with extra information</h3>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> The students completed the experiment before lunch.</p>
    <p>The main clause remains complete even though an additional phrase gives information about time.</p>

    <h3>3. Two main clauses in one sentence</h3>
    <p>Two main clauses can be joined with a coordinating conjunction such as <em>and, but</em> or <em>so</em>.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> The rain stopped, and the match resumed.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Main clause 1: The rain stopped.</li>
      <li>Main clause 2: The match resumed.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>4. Main clause or subordinate clause?</h3>
    <p><strong>Main clause:</strong> We returned home.</p>
    <p><strong>Subordinate clause:</strong> because the meeting ended.</p>
    <p>The subordinate clause begins with a word that makes it dependent on another clause.</p>

    <h3>How to identify a main clause</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Find the subject and verb.</li>
      <li>Check whether the clause expresses a complete thought.</li>
      <li>Ask whether it can stand alone as a sentence in the given context.</li>
    </ol>
  ', 1),
  (1799, 1799, '
    <h2>Subordinate Clauses</h2>
    <p>A subordinate clause contains a subject and a verb but depends on a main clause to complete its meaning in the sentence.</p>

    <h3>1. Adverbial clauses</h3>
    <p>These clauses explain when, where, why, how, under what condition or despite what circumstance something happens.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><em>When the lesson ended</em>, the learners left.</li>
      <li>We stayed indoors <em>because it was raining</em>.</li>
      <li><em>If you practise regularly</em>, you will improve.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>2. Relative clauses</h3>
    <p>A relative clause describes a noun and often begins with <em>who, whom, whose, which</em> or <em>that</em>.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> The learner <em>who won the competition</em> received a certificate.</p>

    <h3>3. Noun clauses</h3>
    <p>A noun clause performs a function usually performed by a noun phrase.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> <em>What she decided</em> surprised us.</p>
    <p>The clause <em>What she decided</em> functions as the subject of the sentence.</p>

    <h3>4. Punctuation with introductory subordinate clauses</h3>
    <p>When an introductory subordinate clause comes before the main clause, it is normally followed by a comma.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> Although the task was difficult, the learners completed it.</p>
    <p>When the subordinate clause comes after the main clause, a comma is often unnecessary for an ordinary adverbial clause.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> The learners completed the task although it was difficult.</p>

    <h3>How to identify one</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Find the subject and verb in the clause.</li>
      <li>Check whether it depends on another clause.</li>
      <li>Identify its function: adverbial, relative or noun clause.</li>
    </ol>
  ', 1),
  (1800, 1800, '
    <h2>Simple Sentences</h2>
    <p>A simple sentence contains one independent clause. It may have a short subject and predicate or a long subject and predicate with extra details.</p>

    <h3>1. One independent clause</h3>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> The baby slept.</p>
    <p>This sentence contains one main clause.</p>

    <h3>2. A simple sentence can be long</h3>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> The hardworking learners in the science club carefully prepared their project after school.</p>
    <p>Although the sentence contains many words, it has one independent clause and no additional subordinate clause.</p>

    <h3>3. Compound subjects and predicates</h3>
    <p>A simple sentence may have two subjects sharing a predicate or one subject performing more than one action.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Compound subject:</strong> Amina and Brian revised together.</li>
      <li><strong>Compound predicate:</strong> Amina read the notes and answered the questions.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>These can still be simple sentences because each has one independent clause.</p>

    <h3>4. Do not count verbs alone</h3>
    <p>Having two verbs does not automatically make a sentence compound. Look for the number of independent clauses.</p>
    <p><strong>Simple:</strong> The teacher entered and greeted the class.</p>
    <p><strong>Compound:</strong> The teacher entered, and the class stood up.</p>

    <h3>How to identify a simple sentence</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Locate the subject–verb relationships.</li>
      <li>Check whether the sentence contains only one independent clause.</li>
      <li>Do not classify it by length or by counting verbs alone.</li>
    </ol>
  ', 1),
  (1801, 1801, '
    <h2>Compound Sentences</h2>
    <p>A compound sentence contains at least two independent clauses. Each clause can stand alone as a sentence.</p>

    <h3>1. Joining clauses with coordinating conjunctions</h3>
    <p>Common coordinating conjunctions include <em>for, and, nor, but, or, yet</em> and <em>so</em>.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> The road was flooded, so the bus took another route.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Clause 1: The road was flooded.</li>
      <li>Clause 2: The bus took another route.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>2. Use a comma before the conjunction</h3>
    <p>When a coordinating conjunction joins two independent clauses, a comma normally comes before it.</p>
    <p><strong>Correct:</strong> The team practised hard, but it lost the match.</p>

    <h3>3. Joining clauses with a semicolon</h3>
    <p>A semicolon can join two closely related independent clauses.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> The library was closed; we studied at home.</p>

    <h3>4. Avoid comma splices</h3>
    <p><strong>Incorrect:</strong> The bell rang, the pupils left.</p>
    <p><strong>Correct:</strong> The bell rang, and the pupils left.</p>
    <p>A comma alone is not normally enough to join two independent clauses.</p>

    <h3>How to identify a compound sentence</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Identify the clauses.</li>
      <li>Check that each clause can stand alone.</li>
      <li>Look for a correct joining method, such as a coordinating conjunction or semicolon.</li>
    </ol>
  ', 1),
  (1802, 1802, '
    <h2>Complex Sentences</h2>
    <p>A complex sentence contains one independent clause and at least one subordinate clause.</p>

    <h3>1. A subordinate clause of time</h3>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> When the bell rang, the learners left.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Subordinate clause: When the bell rang.</li>
      <li>Independent clause: the learners left.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>2. A subordinate clause of reason</h3>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> We stayed indoors because it was raining.</p>
    <p>The main clause expresses the main statement. The clause beginning with <em>because</em> gives the reason.</p>

    <h3>3. A relative clause</h3>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> The book that you borrowed is on the desk.</p>
    <p>The clause <em>that you borrowed</em> describes the book.</p>

    <h3>4. Punctuation</h3>
    <p>When the subordinate clause comes first, it is normally followed by a comma.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> Although the path was muddy, we continued walking.</p>
    <p>When the main clause comes first, a comma is often unnecessary for an ordinary subordinate clause.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> We continued walking although the path was muddy.</p>

    <h3>How to identify a complex sentence</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Find the independent clause.</li>
      <li>Find the subordinate clause.</li>
      <li>Confirm that the subordinate clause depends on the independent clause.</li>
    </ol>
  ', 1),
  (1803, 1803, '
    <h2>Compound-Complex Sentences</h2>
    <p>A compound-complex sentence contains at least two independent clauses and at least one subordinate clause.</p>

    <h3>1. Identify the independent clauses</h3>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> The rain stopped, and the players returned to the field.</p>
    <p>This is a compound sentence because both clauses can stand alone.</p>

    <h3>2. Add a subordinate clause</h3>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> When the rain stopped, the players returned to the field, and the spectators cheered.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Subordinate clause: When the rain stopped.</li>
      <li>Independent clause 1: the players returned to the field.</li>
      <li>Independent clause 2: the spectators cheered.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>3. Check the clause count, not sentence length</h3>
    <p>A long sentence is not automatically compound-complex. It must contain at least two independent clauses and at least one subordinate clause.</p>

    <h3>4. Punctuation</h3>
    <p>Use punctuation to show the relationship between clauses. An introductory subordinate clause is normally followed by a comma. A comma is normally placed before a coordinating conjunction joining two independent clauses.</p>

    <h3>How to identify a compound-complex sentence</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Find at least two independent clauses.</li>
      <li>Find at least one subordinate clause.</li>
      <li>Check that the clauses are connected grammatically and punctuated clearly.</li>
    </ol>
  ', 1),
  (1804, 1804, '
    <h2>Sentence Fragments</h2>
    <p>A sentence fragment is a group of words punctuated as a sentence but lacking a required part of a complete sentence or expressing an incomplete thought in context.</p>

    <h3>1. A missing main clause</h3>
    <p><strong>Fragment:</strong> Because the road was flooded.</p>
    <p>This subordinate clause gives a reason but leaves the reader waiting for the main statement.</p>
    <p><strong>Correction:</strong> Because the road was flooded, the bus took another route.</p>

    <h3>2. A missing subject</h3>
    <p><strong>Fragment:</strong> Went to the library after class.</p>
    <p><strong>Correction:</strong> The learners went to the library after class.</p>

    <h3>3. A missing verb or incomplete verb group</h3>
    <p><strong>Fragment:</strong> The students in the computer laboratory.</p>
    <p><strong>Correction:</strong> The students are in the computer laboratory.</p>

    <h3>4. Do not confuse a fragment with a short sentence</h3>
    <p><strong>Complete:</strong> Stop!</p>
    <p>This is a complete imperative sentence because the subject <em>you</em> is understood.</p>

    <h3>How to correct a fragment</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Check whether a subject and verb are present or understood.</li>
      <li>Check whether a subordinate clause is left without a main clause.</li>
      <li>Add the missing information or connect the fragment to a suitable complete sentence.</li>
    </ol>
  ', 1),
  (1805, 1805, '
    <h2>Run-on Sentences</h2>
    <p>A run-on sentence occurs when two or more independent clauses are joined incorrectly. The problem is not necessarily sentence length; it is the missing or incorrect boundary between clauses.</p>

    <h3>1. Fused sentences</h3>
    <p>A fused sentence joins independent clauses without appropriate punctuation or a conjunction.</p>
    <p><strong>Incorrect:</strong> The bell rang the learners left.</p>

    <h3>2. Correct with a full stop</h3>
    <p><strong>Correct:</strong> The bell rang. The learners left.</p>

    <h3>3. Correct with a comma and coordinating conjunction</h3>
    <p><strong>Correct:</strong> The bell rang, and the learners left.</p>

    <h3>4. Correct with a semicolon</h3>
    <p><strong>Correct:</strong> The bell rang; the learners left.</p>
    <p>A semicolon works when the two independent clauses are closely related.</p>

    <h3>5. Do not use a comma alone</h3>
    <p><strong>Incorrect:</strong> The bell rang, the learners left.</p>
    <p>This is a comma splice, a type of run-on error. A comma alone is generally insufficient to join two independent clauses.</p>

    <h3>How to repair a run-on</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Locate the independent clauses.</li>
      <li>Separate them with a full stop, join them with a suitable conjunction, or use a semicolon where appropriate.</li>
      <li>Reread the sentence to check that the relationship between ideas is clear.</li>
    </ol>
