    <h2>Comma Splices</h2>
    <p>A comma splice occurs when two independent clauses are joined using only a comma.</p>

    <h3>1. Recognising a comma splice</h3>
    <p><strong>Incorrect:</strong> The library was closed, we studied at home.</p>
    <p>Both parts can stand alone as complete sentences. A comma alone does not correctly join them.</p>

    <h3>2. Use a full stop</h3>
    <p><strong>Correct:</strong> The library was closed. We studied at home.</p>
    <p>Use a full stop when the ideas should be separate sentences.</p>

    <h3>3. Use a comma and coordinating conjunction</h3>
    <p><strong>Correct:</strong> The library was closed, so we studied at home.</p>
    <p>Common coordinating conjunctions include <em>and, but, or, nor, for, so</em> and <em>yet</em>.</p>

    <h3>4. Use a semicolon</h3>
    <p><strong>Correct:</strong> The library was closed; we studied at home.</p>
    <p>A semicolon can join closely related independent clauses.</p>

    <h3>5. Do not remove every comma</h3>
    <p>Commas are correct when used for other purposes, such as separating an introductory subordinate clause from a main clause.</p>
    <p><strong>Correct:</strong> Although the library was closed, we studied at home.</p>

    <h3>How to correct a comma splice</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Check whether the words on both sides of the comma are independent clauses.</li>
      <li>If both can stand alone, choose a full stop, a suitable conjunction, or a semicolon.</li>
      <li>Keep the relationship between the ideas clear.</li>
    </ol>
