    <h2>Modal Verbs</h2>
    <p>Modal verbs are auxiliary verbs that express meanings such as ability, permission, obligation, advice, possibility or prediction.</p>

    <h3>1. Common modal verbs</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><em>can, could</em> — ability, permission or possibility</li>
      <li><em>may, might</em> — permission or possibility</li>
      <li><em>must</em> — strong obligation or deduction</li>
      <li><em>should, ought to</em> — advice or expectation</li>
      <li><em>will, would</em> — prediction, willingness or hypothetical meaning</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>2. Use the base verb after a modal</h3>
    <p><strong>Correct:</strong> She can solve the problem.</p>
    <p><strong>Incorrect:</strong> She can solves the problem.</p>
    <p>Modal verbs do not normally take <em>-s</em> for the third-person singular subject.</p>

    <h3>3. Forming negatives</h3>
    <p>Place <em>not</em> after the modal.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>can → cannot / can''t</li>
      <li>should → should not / shouldn''t</li>
      <li>must → must not / mustn''t</li>
    </ul>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> You should not ignore the safety instructions.</p>

    <h3>4. Forming questions</h3>
    <p>Place the modal before the subject.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> Could you explain the method?</p>

    <h3>Important distinction</h3>
    <p><em>Must not</em> expresses prohibition. <em>Do not have to</em> expresses lack of necessity.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>You must not enter. (Entering is prohibited.)</li>
      <li>You do not have to enter. (Entering is not necessary.)</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>How to choose a modal</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Identify the intended meaning: ability, permission, obligation, advice or possibility.</li>
      <li>Select a modal that expresses that meaning in context.</li>
      <li>Use the base form of the main verb after the modal.</li>
    </ol>
