  (1823, 1823, '
    <h2>Reporting Commands and Requests</h2>
    <p>Reported commands and requests communicate what someone instructed or asked another person to do. The original words are changed into an appropriate reporting structure.</p>

    <h3>1. Reporting commands</h3>
    <p>Use <strong>told + object + to + base verb</strong> to report a command.</p>
    <p><strong>Direct:</strong> The teacher said to Brian, "Close the door."</p>
    <p><strong>Reported:</strong> The teacher told Brian to close the door.</p>

    <h3>2. Reporting requests</h3>
    <p>Use verbs such as <em>asked</em> or <em>requested</em> with an object and an infinitive.</p>
    <p><strong>Direct:</strong> Amina said to me, "Please help me."</p>
    <p><strong>Reported:</strong> Amina asked me to help her.</p>

    <h3>3. Reporting negative commands</h3>
    <p>Use <strong>not to + base verb</strong> after the reporting object.</p>
    <p><strong>Direct:</strong> The officer said, "Do not enter the building."</p>
    <p><strong>Reported:</strong> The officer told us not to enter the building.</p>

    <h3>4. Choose a suitable reporting verb</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><em>ordered</em> — a strong command</li>
      <li><em>asked</em> — a request or question, depending on the structure</li>
      <li><em>advised</em> — advice</li>
      <li><em>warned</em> — a warning</li>
      <li><em>reminded</em> — a reminder</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>5. Change pronouns where necessary</h3>
    <p><strong>Direct:</strong> Jane said to Peter, "Please return my book."</p>
    <p><strong>Reported:</strong> Jane asked Peter to return her book.</p>

    <h3>How to report a command or request</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Identify the action being requested or commanded.</li>
      <li>Choose a reporting verb that matches the speaker''s intention.</li>
      <li>Include the person addressed when needed.</li>
      <li>Use to + base verb, or not to + base verb for a negative instruction.</li>
      <li>Adjust pronouns to match the reporting perspective.</li>
    </ol>
  ', 1),
  (1824, 1824, '
    <h2>Pronoun Changes in Reported Speech</h2>
    <p>When speech is reported, pronouns may need to change because the original speaker, listener and reporter may be different people.</p>

    <h3>1. First-person pronouns</h3>
    <p>First-person pronouns usually refer to the original speaker.</p>
    <p><strong>Direct:</strong> Amina said, "I am ready."</p>
    <p><strong>Reported:</strong> Amina said that she was ready.</p>
    <p>''I'' changes to ''she'' because Amina is the person being discussed.</p>

    <h3>2. Second-person pronouns</h3>
    <p>Second-person pronouns refer to the original listener. Their reported form depends on who was addressed.</p>
    <p><strong>Direct:</strong> Peter said to Mary, "You are early."</p>
    <p><strong>Reported:</strong> Peter told Mary that she was early.</p>

    <h3>3. Possessive pronouns and determiners</h3>
    <p><strong>Direct:</strong> John said, "My bag is missing."</p>
    <p><strong>Reported:</strong> John said that his bag was missing.</p>
    <p>The possessive determiner changes from ''my'' to ''his''.</p>

    <h3>4. Object pronouns</h3>
    <p><strong>Direct:</strong> Jane said to Tom, "I will help you."</p>
    <p><strong>Reported:</strong> Jane told Tom that she would help him.</p>
    <p>''I'' becomes ''she'', while ''you'' becomes ''him'' because Tom is being addressed.</p>

    <h3>5. Do not change pronouns mechanically</h3>
    <p>The correct pronoun depends on the real situation. A pronoun may remain unchanged if the reporting perspective is the same or the reference has not changed.</p>

    <h3>How to change pronouns</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Identify the original speaker.</li>
      <li>Identify the person addressed.</li>
      <li>Determine who each pronoun refers to in the reported sentence.</li>
      <li>Choose the correct subject, object or possessive form.</li>
      <li>Check that the meaning remains the same.</li>
    </ol>
  ', 1),
