    <p><strong>Example:</strong> The learner carefully checked each calculation.</p>

    <h3>2. Spelling changes</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>happy → happily</li>
      <li>easy → easily</li>
      <li>gentle → gently</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>3. Irregular forms</h3>
    <p>Some common adverbs do not follow the usual <em>-ly</em> pattern.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>good (adjective) → well (adverb)</li>
      <li>fast (adjective and adverb) → fast</li>
      <li>hard (adjective and adverb) → hard</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>4. Adjective or adverb?</h3>
    <p><strong>Adjective:</strong> The driver was careful.</p>
    <p><strong>Adverb:</strong> The driver drove carefully.</p>
    <p>Use an adjective to describe a person or thing. Use an adverb when describing how an action is carried out.</p>

    <h3>5. Position</h3>
    <p>Adverbs of manner often appear after the verb or after the verb''s object.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>The child laughed loudly.</li>
      <li>The mechanic repaired the engine carefully.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Some adverbs can appear before the verb for emphasis or style, but the sentence must remain natural.</p>
