    <h2>Adverbs of Time and Place</h2>
    <p>Adverbs of time tell us when, how long or how often something happens in a time-related sense. Adverbs of place tell us where an action happens or in which direction movement occurs.</p>

    <h3>1. Adverbs of time</h3>
    <p>Examples include <em>today, yesterday, soon, already, recently, later</em> and <em>now</em>.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>The bus arrived yesterday.</li>
      <li>We will discuss the results later.</li>
      <li>She has already completed the assignment.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>2. Duration</h3>
    <p>Some time expressions indicate how long an activity continues.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>They waited briefly.</li>
      <li>He stayed temporarily.</li>
      <li>We worked all day.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Some duration expressions are adverbial phrases rather than single adverbs.</p>

    <h3>3. Adverbs of place</h3>
    <p>Examples include <em>here, there, outside, nearby, everywhere</em> and <em>away</em>.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Please wait outside.</li>
      <li>The school is nearby.</li>
      <li>The children searched everywhere.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>4. Direction and movement</h3>
    <p>Words such as <em>upstairs, downstairs, forward</em> and <em>back</em> can indicate direction.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> The learners walked upstairs.</p>

    <h3>5. Position in a sentence</h3>
    <p>Adverbs of time often appear at the beginning or end of a clause. Adverbs of place commonly follow the verb or its object.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Tomorrow, we will visit the laboratory.</li>
      <li>We will visit the laboratory tomorrow.</li>
      <li>The students waited outside.</li>
    </ul>
