    <h2>Adverbs of Frequency</h2>
    <p>Adverbs of frequency describe how often an action or situation occurs.</p>

    <h3>1. Common frequency adverbs</h3>
    <p>From high frequency to low frequency, a useful approximate sequence is:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>always</li>
      <li>usually</li>
      <li>often</li>
      <li>sometimes</li>
      <li>rarely or seldom</li>
      <li>never</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The exact frequency depends on context; these words do not represent fixed percentages.</p>

    <h3>2. Before most main verbs</h3>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> She usually revises after class.</p>
    <p>The adverb comes before the main verb ''revises''.</p>

    <h3>3. After the verb be</h3>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> He is often late.</p>
    <p>Frequency adverbs commonly follow forms of the verb <em>be</em>.</p>

    <h3>4. With auxiliary verbs</h3>
    <p>Place the adverb after the first auxiliary verb in many ordinary sentences.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> They have never missed a lesson.</p>
