  (1850, 1850, '
    <h2>Prepositional Phrases</h2>
    <p>A <strong>prepositional phrase</strong> begins with a preposition and includes its object, together with any words describing that object.</p>

    <h3>Structure</h3>
    <p><strong>Preposition + object of the preposition</strong></p>
    <ul>
      <li><em>under the table</em> — under is the preposition; table is its object.</li>
      <li><em>after the final examination</em> — after is the preposition; examination is its object.</li>
      <li><em>beside the old classroom</em> — beside is the preposition; classroom is its object, described by old.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>What prepositional phrases do</h3>
    <p>A prepositional phrase can provide information about a noun or a verb.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Describing a noun:</strong> The books <em>on the shelf</em> belong to the teacher. The phrase identifies which books.</li>
      <li><strong>Modifying a verb:</strong> The learners revised <em>after lunch</em>. The phrase tells when they revised.</li>
      <li><strong>Describing place:</strong> The ball rolled <em>under the desk</em>. The phrase tells where it rolled.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Finding the complete phrase</h3>
    <p>Do not identify only the preposition when asked for the phrase. Include the preposition and its object, along with any modifiers that belong to the object.</p>

    <h3>Avoid confusing the phrase with the subject</h3>
    <p>In <em>The box of old books is heavy</em>, the subject is <em>The box of old books</em>. Within it, <em>of old books</em> is a prepositional phrase. The noun <em>books</em> does not become the main subject merely because it is close to the verb.</p>
