  (1851, 1851, '
    <h2>Common Verb and Adjective Preposition Patterns</h2>
    <p>Some verbs and adjectives are conventionally followed by particular prepositions. The choice often depends on the expression rather than a general rule.</p>

    <h3>Common verb patterns</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>depend on:</strong> Success depends <em>on</em> consistent effort.</li>
      <li><strong>believe in:</strong> She believes <em>in</em> honest work.</li>
      <li><strong>listen to:</strong> Please listen <em>to</em> the instructions.</li>
      <li><strong>belong to:</strong> This bag belongs <em>to</em> Amina.</li>
      <li><strong>apologise for:</strong> He apologised <em>for</em> the mistake.</li>
      <li><strong>agree with:</strong> I agree <em>with</em> your explanation.</li>
      <li><strong>apply for:</strong> She applied <em>for</em> the scholarship.</li>
      <li><strong>insist on:</strong> They insisted <em>on</em> receiving a receipt.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Common adjective patterns</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>interested in:</strong> The learners are interested <em>in</em> science.</li>
      <li><strong>good at:</strong> He is good <em>at</em> mathematics.</li>
      <li><strong>afraid of:</strong> The child is afraid <em>of</em> snakes.</li>
      <li><strong>proud of:</strong> We are proud <em>of</em> our achievement.</li>
      <li><strong>responsible for:</strong> She is responsible <em>for</em> the equipment.</li>
      <li><strong>similar to:</strong> This answer is similar <em>to</em> mine.</li>
      <li><strong>different from:</strong> Your method is different <em>from</em> mine.</li>
      <li><strong>pleased with:</strong> The teacher is pleased <em>with</em> the progress.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Meaning can change the pattern</h3>
    <p>Learn the complete expression in context. For example, <em>think of</em> can mean to imagine or consider, while <em>think about</em> often means to give something careful consideration.</p>

    <h3>How to correct an error</h3>
    <p>First identify the verb or adjective. Then recall the preposition conventionally used with that expression. Finally, read the full sentence to confirm that the meaning fits.</p>
