  (1855, 1855, '
    <h2>Cause, Time, Condition and Contrast</h2>
    <p>Conjunctions help readers understand the logical relationship between clauses. Choosing the wrong one can change the meaning of a sentence.</p>

    <h3>Cause</h3>
    <p>Cause conjunctions explain why something happens.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Because:</strong> We postponed the match because the field was wet.</li>
      <li><strong>Since:</strong> Since the shop was closed, we returned home.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Ask: <em>Why did it happen?</em></p>

    <h3>Time</h3>
    <p>Time conjunctions show when events occur or how they relate in sequence.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Before:</strong> Lock the door before you leave.</li>
      <li><strong>After:</strong> We ate after the meeting ended.</li>
      <li><strong>While:</strong> He took notes while the speaker talked.</li>
      <li><strong>Until:</strong> Stay here until the teacher returns.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Ask: <em>When did it happen?</em></p>

    <h3>Condition</h3>
    <p>Condition conjunctions state what must happen or be true for another event to occur.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>If:</strong> If you submit the form today, it can be processed tomorrow.</li>
      <li><strong>Unless:</strong> Unless you revise, you may forget the procedure.</li>
    </ul>
    <p><em>Unless</em> generally means <em>if not</em>.</p>
    <p>Example: Unless you hurry, you will miss the bus. = If you do not hurry, you will miss the bus.</p>

    <h3>Contrast</h3>
    <p>Contrast conjunctions show a difference between ideas or an unexpected outcome.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Although:</strong> Although it was raining, the game continued.</li>
      <li><strong>Whereas:</strong> Kamau prefers tea, whereas Njeri prefers coffee.</li>
      <li><strong>While:</strong> While one team defended well, the other attacked effectively.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Choose the logical relationship</h3>
    <p>Do not choose a conjunction merely because it sounds familiar. Decide whether the second clause gives a reason, a time relationship, a condition or a contrast.</p>

    <h3>Avoid double conjunction errors</h3>
    <p>Do not normally use <em>although</em> and <em>but</em> together to connect the same contrast.</p>
    <p>Incorrect: Although it was raining, but the game continued.</p>
    <p>Correct: Although it was raining, the game continued.</p>
    <p>Also correct: It was raining, but the game continued.</p>
