  (1857, 1857, '
    <h2>Correlative Pairs</h2>
    <p>Correlative conjunctions work together as pairs. Each pair connects related grammatical elements and communicates a specific relationship.</p>

    <h3>Both ... and</h3>
    <p>Use this pair to include two people, things or ideas.</p>
    <p>Example: Both the teacher and the learners agreed.</p>

    <h3>Either ... or</h3>
    <p>Use this pair to present alternatives.</p>
    <p>Example: You can either revise now or study after supper.</p>

    <h3>Neither ... nor</h3>
    <p>Use this pair to exclude both alternatives.</p>
    <p>Example: Neither the keys nor the wallet was missing.</p>

    <h3>Not only ... but also</h3>
    <p>Use this pair to add emphasis to two related points.</p>
    <p>Example: The learner was not only accurate but also thorough.</p>

    <h3>Whether ... or</h3>
    <p>Use this pair to present alternatives being considered.</p>
    <p>Example: We must decide whether to walk or take a bus.</p>

    <h3>Maintain parallel structure</h3>
    <p>The elements connected by the pair should normally have matching grammatical forms.</p>
    <p>Incorrect: She enjoys both reading and to write.</p>
    <p>Correct: She enjoys both reading and writing.</p>

    <h3>Watch the subject–verb relationship</h3>
    <p>With <em>both ... and</em>, two singular subjects generally take a plural verb.</p>
    <p>Example: Both the teacher and the learner <strong>are</strong> present.</p>
    <p>With <em>either ... or</em> and <em>neither ... nor</em>, verb agreement can depend on the subjects'' number and proximity. When the result sounds awkward, rewrite the sentence to make the subject–verb relationship clear.</p>
