    <h2>Similar Spellings</h2>
    <p>Some English words look or sound similar but have different meanings. To choose correctly, examine the meaning required by the sentence.</p>

    <h3>Commonly confused words</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Accept:</strong> receive or agree to something. Example: She accepted the invitation.</li>
      <li><strong>Except:</strong> excluding. Example: Everyone attended except Brian.</li>
      <li><strong>Affect:</strong> usually a verb meaning to influence. Example: Lack of sleep can affect concentration.</li>
      <li><strong>Effect:</strong> usually a noun meaning a result. Example: Lack of sleep had an effect on concentration.</li>
      <li><strong>Advice:</strong> a noun meaning a recommendation. Example: The teacher gave useful advice.</li>
      <li><strong>Advise:</strong> a verb meaning to recommend. Example: The teacher advised us to revise.</li>
      <li><strong>Principal:</strong> the head of a school, or most important. Example: The principal addressed the learners.</li>
      <li><strong>Principle:</strong> a rule, belief or fundamental idea. Example: Honesty is an important principle.</li>
      <li><strong>Stationary:</strong> not moving. Example: The vehicle remained stationary.</li>
      <li><strong>Stationery:</strong> writing materials. Example: We bought stationery for school.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>A reliable method</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Read the entire sentence.</li>
      <li>Identify the meaning the sentence needs.</li>
      <li>Check the grammatical role, such as noun or verb.</li>
      <li>Choose the spelling that matches both the meaning and the grammar.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Compare the words in context</h3>
    <p>Incorrect: The teacher gave me a good advise.</p>
    <p>Correct: The teacher gave me good advice.</p>
    <p>Correct: The teacher advised me to practise.</p>

    <p>Remember: Similar spelling does not mean identical meaning. The surrounding words provide clues, but you must still know what each word means.</p>
