    <h2>Similar Meanings</h2>
    <p>Some words have similar meanings but are not interchangeable in every sentence. The correct choice depends on context, intensity, grammatical patterns and tone.</p>

    <h3>Words with related meanings</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Big / large:</strong> both describe considerable size. We commonly say ''a big mistake'' and ''a large building''.</li>
      <li><strong>Begin / commence:</strong> both mean start, but ''commence'' is generally more formal.</li>
      <li><strong>Ask / enquire:</strong> both can involve seeking information; ''enquire'' often sounds more formal.</li>
      <li><strong>Help / assist:</strong> both mean provide support; ''assist'' is often more formal.</li>
      <li><strong>Childish / childlike:</strong> ''childish'' often suggests immaturity; ''childlike'' often suggests positive qualities such as innocence.</li>
      <li><strong>Thin / slim:</strong> both refer to a small body width, but ''slim'' often sounds more positive when describing a person.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Intensity matters</h3>
    <p>Words with related meanings may differ in strength.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Annoyed:</strong> slightly angry or irritated.</li>
      <li><strong>Angry:</strong> feeling strong displeasure.</li>
      <li><strong>Furious:</strong> extremely angry.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Choose according to context</h3>
    <p>In a formal report, ''The committee commenced the meeting at 9 a.m.'' may suit the tone better than ''The committee kicked off the meeting at 9 a.m.''</p>
    <p>In everyday conversation, ''Let''s begin'' may sound more natural than ''Let us commence'' in some situations.</p>

    <h3>How to choose precisely</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Identify the basic meaning required.</li>
      <li>Check the intensity or emotional tone.</li>
      <li>Consider the audience and formality.</li>
      <li>Check which words naturally occur together.</li>
    </ol>

    <p>A synonym is not automatically a perfect substitute. Choose the word that communicates the intended meaning most accurately.</p>
