  (1880, 1880, '
    <h2>Multiple Word Meanings</h2>
    <p>Many English words have more than one meaning. The surrounding words and situation determine which meaning is intended.</p>

    <h3>Examples of words with several meanings</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Bank:</strong> a financial institution — ''She deposited money at the bank.'' It can also mean land beside a river — ''They sat on the river bank.''</li>
      <li><strong>Bright:</strong> giving out much light — ''a bright lamp''; intelligent — ''a bright learner''; vivid — ''a bright colour''.</li>
      <li><strong>Run:</strong> move quickly on foot — ''He can run fast''; manage — ''She runs a business''; flow — ''The tap began to run''.</li>
      <li><strong>Head:</strong> a body part — ''He nodded his head''; a leader — ''the head of the school''; the top or front — ''the head of the table''.</li>
      <li><strong>Match:</strong> a contest — ''a football match''; something that corresponds — ''The colours match''; a small stick used to produce a flame — ''She lit a match''.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Use grammar and context together</h3>
    <p>The grammatical role may help, but it does not always settle the meaning.</p>
    <p>''They run every morning'' uses <em>run</em> to describe movement.</p>
    <p>''They run a small shop'' uses <em>run</em> to mean manage.</p>

    <h3>Distinguish literal meaning from the intended sense</h3>
    <p>In ''The news spread quickly'', the news did not physically move like a person. The verb ''spread'' means that information reached more people.</p>

    <h3>How to identify the correct meaning</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Read the complete sentence.</li>
      <li>Look at the subject, verb, object and surrounding details.</li>
      <li>List the possible meanings of the word.</li>
      <li>Select the meaning that fits the situation.</li>
    </ol>
