  (1881, 1881, '
    <h2>Literal Meaning</h2>
    <p>Literal meaning is the direct, ordinary meaning of words, interpreted without treating them as a figure of speech.</p>

    <h3>Examples</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>''The glass broke when it fell.'' The glass physically broke.</li>
      <li>''The child opened the door.'' The child physically opened a door.</li>
      <li>''The water is cold.'' The water has a low temperature.</li>
      <li>''The farmer planted maize.'' The farmer put maize seeds into the soil.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Literal and non-literal statements</h3>
    <p>Compare these sentences:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Literal:</strong> ''The boy ran out of the classroom.'' He physically ran outside.</li>
      <li><strong>Non-literal:</strong> ''The boy ran out of patience.'' He became unable to tolerate the situation any longer.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>How to recognise literal meaning</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Consider the ordinary meaning of each word.</li>
      <li>Check whether the statement describes something that can reasonably happen as stated.</li>
      <li>Look for clues that indicate exaggeration, comparison or another figure of speech.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Literal does not mean unimportant</h3>
    <p>Literal language can communicate precise information, instructions and facts. For example, ''Keep the medicine out of children''s reach'' is a direct instruction.</p>
    <p>Do not assume a sentence is figurative merely because it sounds descriptive. Interpret it according to its actual context.</p>
  ', 1),
  (1882, 1882, '
    <h2>Figurative Meaning</h2>
    <p>Figurative language uses words in a non-literal way to communicate an image, feeling, comparison or idea more vividly.</p>

    <h3>Examples</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>''The classroom was a zoo.''</strong> The classroom was noisy or disorderly, not an actual zoo.</li>
      <li><strong>''She has a heart of stone.''</strong> She seems unkind or emotionally unresponsive.</li>
      <li><strong>''Time flew.''</strong> Time seemed to pass quickly.</li>
      <li><strong>''He is drowning in work.''</strong> He has an overwhelming amount of work.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Why writers use figurative language</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>To create vivid mental pictures.</li>
      <li>To communicate emotions strongly.</li>
      <li>To make descriptions memorable.</li>
      <li>To express complex experiences in a compact way.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Interpret the expression in context</h3>
    <p>Consider: ''When the examination timetable was announced, the learners'' excitement exploded.''</p>
    <p>Their excitement did not literally explode. The expression emphasises how suddenly and strongly they reacted.</p>

    <h3>Explain both meaning and effect</h3>
    <p>When asked to explain figurative language, do more than name the expression.</p>
    <ol>
      <li>State the intended meaning.</li>
      <li>Identify the words that create the image.</li>
      <li>Explain the effect on the reader or listener.</li>
    </ol>

    <p>Example: ''The news spread like wildfire'' means the news travelled very quickly. The comparison suggests speed and rapid spread.</p>
  ', 1),
  (1883, 1883, '
    <h2>Denotation</h2>
    <p>Denotation is the direct or dictionary meaning of a word, rather than the feelings or associations it may create.</p>

    <h3>Examples</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Home:</strong> the place where someone lives.</li>
      <li><strong>Snake:</strong> a long, legless reptile.</li>
      <li><strong>Child:</strong> a young human being.</li>
      <li><strong>Rose:</strong> a flowering plant or its flower.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Denotation and connotation</h3>
    <p>Two words can refer to similar things while creating different feelings.</p>
    <p><strong>Home</strong> and <strong>house</strong> can refer to a place where someone lives. The denotation of ''house'' usually focuses on the building, while ''home'' often emphasises the place where a person lives and belongs.</p>

    <p><strong>Snake</strong> denotes a type of reptile. In some contexts, however, calling a person a snake suggests that the person is deceitful. That negative association is connotative, not the literal denotation.</p>

    <h3>Why denotation matters</h3>
    <p>Precise denotations are especially important in definitions, scientific explanations, formal instructions and factual reports.</p>

    <h3>How to identify denotation</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Identify the word being used.</li>
      <li>State its direct meaning.</li>
      <li>Separate the direct meaning from the feelings or cultural associations attached to it.</li>
    </ol>
  ', 1),
  (1884, 1884, '
    <h2>Connotation</h2>
    <p>Connotation refers to the feelings, associations or attitudes that a word suggests beyond its direct meaning.</p>

    <h3>Positive, negative and neutral associations</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Confident:</strong> generally positive; suggests belief in one''s ability.</li>
      <li><strong>Arrogant:</strong> generally negative; suggests an exaggerated sense of one''s importance.</li>
      <li><strong>Determined:</strong> often positive; suggests persistence towards a goal.</li>
      <li><strong>Stubborn:</strong> often negative; suggests unwillingness to change one''s position.</li>
      <li><strong>Economical:</strong> often positive; suggests avoiding waste.</li>
      <li><strong>Stingy:</strong> negative; suggests unwillingness to spend or share.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Words with similar denotations can create different impressions</h3>
    <p>Consider the words <strong>slim</strong> and <strong>skinny</strong>. Both can describe a person with little body fat, but ''slim'' may sound complimentary while ''skinny'' can sound critical.</p>

    <p>Compare <strong>youthful</strong> and <strong>childish</strong>. ''Youthful'' often suggests energy or freshness; ''childish'' often criticises immature behaviour.</p>

    <h3>Writers choose words for their effect</h3>
    <p>In a persuasive speech, a speaker might call a plan ''bold'' to suggest courage, or ''reckless'' to suggest dangerous carelessness. The words influence how the audience evaluates the same action.</p>

    <h3>How to explain connotation in a passage</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Identify the word.</li>
      <li>State its direct meaning if needed.</li>
      <li>Describe the feeling or association it creates in context.</li>
      <li>Explain how that association affects the reader''s view.</li>
    </ol>

    <p>Connotations can vary across cultures and situations. Use the passage as your main evidence rather than assuming every reader will react identically.</p>
