  (1885, 1885, '
    <h2>Similes</h2>
    <p>A simile is a figure of speech that compares two different things using words such as <em>like</em> or <em>as</em>.</p>

    <h3>Examples</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>As busy as a bee:</strong> very busy.</li>
      <li><strong>As light as a feather:</strong> very light.</li>
      <li><strong>He ran like the wind:</strong> he ran very quickly.</li>
      <li><strong>Her voice was like music:</strong> her voice was pleasant or harmonious.</li>
      <li><strong>The child clung to his mother like a shadow:</strong> the child stayed very close to her.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>How a simile works</h3>
    <p>A simile connects two things by highlighting a shared quality.</p>
    <p>In ''The classroom was as quiet as a library'', the comparison emphasises silence.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Subject:</strong> the classroom.</li>
      <li><strong>Comparison:</strong> a library.</li>
      <li><strong>Shared quality:</strong> quietness.</li>
      <li><strong>Effect:</strong> helps the reader imagine the degree of silence.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Identify the quality being compared</h3>
    <p>Do not stop at identifying the two things. Explain the shared quality and the effect of the comparison.</p>
    <p>Example: ''The road twisted like a snake.'' The road is compared with a snake to emphasise its winding shape.</p>

    <h3>Simile or literal comparison?</h3>
    <p>The word ''like'' does not automatically make a sentence a simile. ''She looks like her mother'' may simply state a real resemblance. A figurative simile usually creates an illustrative comparison to emphasise a quality.</p>
  ', 1),
  (1886, 1886, '
    <h2>Metaphors</h2>
    <p>A metaphor describes one thing as another to suggest a shared quality. Unlike a typical simile, it does not explicitly use ''like'' or ''as'' to make the comparison.</p>

    <h3>Examples</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>''Time is money.''</strong> Time is valuable and should not be wasted.</li>
      <li><strong>''She is the backbone of the team.''</strong> She provides essential support.</li>
      <li><strong>''The classroom was a furnace.''</strong> The classroom was extremely hot.</li>
      <li><strong>''His words were a knife.''</strong> His words were deeply hurtful.</li>
      <li><strong>''Amina is a shining star in mathematics.''</strong> Amina performs exceptionally well or stands out.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>How to interpret a metaphor</h3>
    <p>Consider: ''The rumour was a seed that grew into a crisis.''</p>
    <ol>
      <li>Identify the comparison: a rumour is described as a seed.</li>
      <li>Find the shared idea: a small beginning can develop into something much larger.</li>
      <li>Interpret the meaning: the rumour gradually caused a serious problem.</li>
      <li>Explain the effect: the image emphasises how a small event can grow into a major issue.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Metaphor and simile</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Simile:</strong> Her voice was like music.</li>
      <li><strong>Metaphor:</strong> Her voice was music.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Do not interpret a metaphor literally</h3>
    <p>When a person is called ''the backbone of a team'', the statement usually does not refer to a physical bone. It describes the person''s essential role.</p>

    <h3>Answering examination questions</h3>
    <p>When asked to explain a metaphor, state what it suggests in the passage and identify the quality or idea being emphasised. Naming it as a metaphor alone is not a complete explanation.</p>
  ', 1),
  (1887, 1887, '
    <h2>Personification</h2>
    <p>Personification gives human qualities, feelings or actions to animals, objects, ideas or forces of nature.</p>

    <h3>Examples</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>"The wind whispered through the trees."</strong> The wind is described as whispering, a human action, to suggest a soft sound.</li>
      <li><strong>"The sun smiled down on the village."</strong> The sun is given the human action of smiling, creating a warm and pleasant impression.</li>
      <li><strong>"Opportunity knocked at his door."</strong> Opportunity is presented as a person who can knock, suggesting that a chance became available.</li>
      <li><strong>"The old house groaned in the storm."</strong> The house is given a human-like action to suggest creaking sounds.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>How to explain personification</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Identify the non-human subject.</li>
      <li>Identify the human quality or action given to it.</li>
      <li>Explain the idea or image created in context.</li>
    </ol>

    <p>For example, in "The angry clouds marched across the sky", clouds are described as angry and marching. This makes the approaching storm seem powerful and threatening.</p>

    <h3>Do not confuse it with ordinary description</h3>
    <p>"The wind blew strongly" describes a natural action. "The wind angrily attacked the village" gives the wind human emotion and a human-like action.</p>

    <p>Personification helps readers imagine scenes, feel the atmosphere and connect with descriptions.</p>
  ', 1),
  (1888, 1888, '
    <h2>Hyperbole</h2>
    <p>Hyperbole is deliberate exaggeration used for emphasis, humour or emotional effect. It is not normally intended to be understood as an exact factual statement.</p>

    <h3>Examples</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>"I have told you a thousand times."</strong> The speaker exaggerates the number of times to express frustration.</li>
      <li><strong>"This bag weighs a tonne."</strong> The speaker exaggerates the weight to show that the bag feels very heavy.</li>
      <li><strong>"We waited forever."</strong> The expression emphasises a long or frustrating wait.</li>
      <li><strong>"She cried a river of tears."</strong> The exaggeration emphasises intense sadness.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Recognising hyperbole</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Identify the unusually extreme statement.</li>
      <li>Consider whether it is intended literally.</li>
      <li>Determine the real feeling or situation being emphasised.</li>
      <li>Explain the effect on the reader or listener.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Hyperbole and ordinary emphasis</h3>
    <p>"The room was very hot" may be a literal description. "The room was hot enough to melt the shoes off my feet" exaggerates the heat for effect.</p>

    <h3>Why writers use it</h3>
    <p>Hyperbole can express frustration, excitement, fear, humour or the intensity of an experience. In formal factual writing, exaggerated statements should not be mistaken for measured evidence.</p>
  ', 1),
  (1889, 1889, '
    <h2>Tone and Word Choice</h2>
    <p>Tone is the writer''s or speaker''s attitude towards a subject, audience or situation. Word choice helps communicate that attitude.</p>

    <h3>Common tones</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Optimistic:</strong> expects or emphasises positive possibilities.</li>
      <li><strong>Pessimistic:</strong> expects or emphasises negative outcomes.</li>
      <li><strong>Critical:</strong> expresses disapproval or identifies faults.</li>
      <li><strong>Appreciative:</strong> expresses approval or gratitude.</li>
      <li><strong>Sarcastic:</strong> uses cutting or mocking language, sometimes saying the opposite of what is meant.</li>
      <li><strong>Concerned:</strong> communicates worry or serious attention.</li>
      <li><strong>Humorous:</strong> aims to amuse.</li>
      <li><strong>Formal:</strong> uses language suited to official or professional situations.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Word choice reveals attitude</h3>
    <p>Compare these descriptions of the same decision:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>"The committee made a <strong>courageous</strong> decision." The word suggests approval and bravery.</li>
      <li>"The committee made a <strong>reckless</strong> decision." The word suggests criticism and lack of care.</li>
      <li>"The committee made a <strong>controversial</strong> decision." The word suggests disagreement or debate without necessarily expressing approval or disapproval.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>How to identify tone</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Read enough of the passage to understand the situation.</li>
      <li>Identify words that express feelings, judgement or attitude.</li>
      <li>Consider whether the writer praises, criticises, worries, mocks or simply informs.</li>
      <li>Support your answer with a relevant word or phrase from the passage.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Answer with evidence</h3>
