    <h2>Ambiguity and Double Meaning</h2>
    <p>A statement is ambiguous when it can reasonably be understood in more than one way. The uncertainty may arise from word meaning, sentence structure or missing context.</p>

    <h3>Ambiguity caused by word meaning</h3>
    <p>"I went to the bank."</p>
    <p>Without further context, ''bank'' could refer to a financial institution or the land beside a river.</p>

    <h3>Ambiguity caused by sentence structure</h3>
    <p>"I saw the man with the telescope."</p>
    <p>This could mean that I used a telescope to see the man, or that I saw a man who had a telescope.</p>

    <h3>Ambiguity caused by unclear reference</h3>
    <p>"Mary told Jane that she had won."</p>
    <p>The pronoun ''she'' could refer to Mary or Jane unless the surrounding context clarifies who won.</p>

    <h3>Double meaning and wordplay</h3>
    <p>Some writers deliberately create two meanings to produce humour or a clever effect. For example, a headline may use a word that refers both to a literal event and to a familiar expression.</p>

    <h3>How to resolve ambiguity</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Identify the word, phrase or sentence that allows more than one interpretation.</li>
      <li>State the possible meanings clearly.</li>
      <li>Use surrounding context to decide whether one interpretation is intended.</li>
      <li>If both meanings remain possible, explain why the statement is ambiguous.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Make writing precise</h3>
    <p>Instead of "I saw the man with the telescope", write "Using my telescope, I saw the man" if the observer used the telescope.</p>
    <p>Clear writing places words in an order that communicates the intended meaning without unnecessary confusion.</p>
