    <h2>Sentence-Initial Capital Letters</h2>
    <p>In standard written English, the first word of a sentence begins with a capital letter. A new sentence after a full stop, question mark or exclamation mark also begins with a capital letter.</p>

    <h3>Examples</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Incorrect: "the learners entered the classroom."</li>
      <li>Correct: "The learners entered the classroom."</li>
      <li>Incorrect: "Where is the library? it is near the gate."</li>
      <li>Correct: "Where is the library? It is near the gate."</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>After a colon or semicolon</h3>
    <p>A colon or semicolon does not normally require the next word to begin with a capital letter, unless the word independently requires one, such as a proper noun.</p>
    <p>Example: "Bring three items: a pen, a ruler and a notebook."</p>

    <h3>Check every sentence boundary</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Locate full stops, question marks and exclamation marks.</li>
      <li>Check the first word of each sentence that follows.</li>
      <li>Correct lowercase sentence beginnings.</li>
      <li>Keep proper nouns capitalised wherever they occur.</li>
    </ol>

    <p>Capitalisation helps readers recognise sentence boundaries and makes writing easier to follow.</p>
