    <h2>Common Spelling Patterns</h2>
    <p>English spelling follows useful patterns, although there are exceptions. Recognising common patterns helps learners spell unfamiliar words more accurately.</p>

    <h3>Changing y to i</h3>
    <p>When a word ends in a consonant followed by <strong>y</strong>, the y often changes to i before an ending such as -es or -ed.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>carry → carried</li>
      <li>try → tries</li>
      <li>But when a vowel comes before y, the y usually remains: play → played; enjoy → enjoys.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Adding -ing</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Most verbs add -ing: read → reading.</li>
      <li>Silent final e is usually dropped: make → making; write → writing.</li>
      <li>Some short verbs double the final consonant: run → running; sit → sitting.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Adding -ed</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Most regular verbs add -ed: work → worked.</li>
      <li>Verbs ending in consonant + y often change y to i: carry → carried.</li>
      <li>Some verbs double the final consonant: stop → stopped.</li>
      <li>Irregular verbs may change completely: go → went; write → wrote.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Common letter combinations</h3>
    <ul>
