      <li>"Amina studied consistently; she performed well in the examination."</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Semicolon or comma?</h3>
    <p>A comma alone cannot normally join two independent clauses.</p>
    <p>Incorrect: "The road was flooded, the bus could not pass."</p>
    <p>Correct: "The road was flooded; the bus could not pass."</p>
    <p>Another correct option: "The road was flooded, so the bus could not pass."</p>

    <h3>Separating complex list items</h3>
    <p>Semicolons help distinguish list items when the items themselves contain commas.</p>
    <p>"The visitors came from Embu, Kenya; Kampala, Uganda; and Kigali, Rwanda."</p>

    <h3>Important rule</h3>
    <p>A semicolon is not a decorative replacement for every comma or full stop. Use it when the clauses are closely related or when a complicated list needs clearer separation.</p>
