  (1913, 1913, '
    <h2>Intensive Reading</h2>
    <p>Intensive reading means reading a short text or selected section carefully to understand precise information, vocabulary, relationships and implied meaning.</p>

    <h3>What intensive reading involves</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Read the passage carefully, sentence by sentence.</li>
      <li>Identify unfamiliar words and use context to interpret them.</li>
      <li>Distinguish the main idea from supporting details.</li>
      <li>Notice linking words that show cause, contrast or sequence.</li>
      <li>Use evidence from the passage to answer questions.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Example passage</h3>
    <p>Although the village received heavy rainfall, the reservoir remained low. Most of the water flowed across the dry ground before reaching the reservoir because the surrounding slopes had lost much of their vegetation. The community therefore began planting grass and trees to reduce runoff.</p>

    <h3>Read the relationships carefully</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Contrast:</strong> ''Although'' introduces a contrast between heavy rainfall and the low reservoir level.</li>
      <li><strong>Cause:</strong> Water flowed across the ground because the slopes had lost vegetation.</li>
      <li><strong>Response:</strong> The community planted grass and trees to reduce runoff.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Support your answers</h3>
    <p>If asked why the reservoir remained low, use the passage''s explanation about runoff and reduced vegetation. Do not invent a cause that the text does not support.</p>

    <p>Intensive reading is useful for comprehension questions that require exact details, careful interpretation or evidence-based explanations.</p>
