  (1919, 1919, '
    <h2>Making Inferences</h2>
    <p>An inference is a conclusion drawn from evidence in a text and relevant background knowledge. It goes beyond what is directly stated, but it must remain supported by the evidence.</p>

    <h3>Evidence plus reasoning</h3>
    <p>Example: "The classroom windows were closed, learners were fanning themselves with exercise books, and the afternoon sun fell directly on the room."</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Evidence:</strong> Closed windows, fanning and direct sunlight.</li>
      <li><strong>Reasoning:</strong> The learners are trying to cool themselves.</li>
      <li><strong>Inference:</strong> The classroom is probably hot.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Inference is not guessing</h3>
    <p>A guess may have little evidence. An inference connects specific clues to a reasonable conclusion.</p>

    <h3>Three steps</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Collect the relevant clues.</li>
      <li>Explain what the clues suggest together.</li>
      <li>Choose the conclusion that requires the fewest unsupported assumptions.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Consider alternative explanations</h3>
    <p>A person who is quiet may be tired, worried, concentrating or simply naturally quiet. Without additional evidence, it is unsafe to claim a particular emotional state as a certainty.</p>

    <p>In comprehension questions, support an inference by referring to the words or actions that led you to it.</p>
