    <h2>Supporting Sentences</h2>
    <p>Supporting sentences develop a paragraph''s main idea. They may explain a reason, provide evidence, give an example or clarify how something works.</p>

    <h3>Example paragraph</h3>
    <p><strong>Topic sentence:</strong> "A regular revision timetable helps learners manage their study time."</p>
    <p><strong>Supporting sentence 1:</strong> "It allocates time to different subjects before examination dates approach."</p>
    <p><strong>Supporting sentence 2:</strong> "It also helps learners identify when a difficult topic needs additional practice."</p>
    <p><strong>Supporting sentence 3:</strong> "For example, a learner who struggles with algebra can reserve extra sessions for solving equations."</p>
    <p>The supporting sentences explain the benefit and provide a specific illustration.</p>

    <h3>Ways to develop a point</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Explanation:</strong> show why or how the point is true.</li>
      <li><strong>Example:</strong> provide a specific illustration.</li>
      <li><strong>Evidence:</strong> use relevant facts or reliable information.</li>
      <li><strong>Reasoning:</strong> connect the supporting detail to the main claim.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Check each sentence</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Does it develop the topic sentence?</li>
      <li>Does it add useful information rather than repeat the same point?</li>
      <li>Is the example or evidence accurate?</li>
      <li>Does it connect logically to the surrounding sentences?</li>
    </ol>

    <p>A paragraph becomes convincing when its supporting sentences explain the main point clearly instead of merely repeating it.</p>
