    <h2>Narrative Composition</h2>

    <p>A narrative composition tells a story about real or imagined events. It may describe a personal experience, an unexpected event, a difficult decision, or a lesson learnt. Strong narratives combine a clear sequence of events with believable characters, setting, conflict, and resolution.</p>

    <h3>A practical narrative structure</h3>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Opening:</strong> Establish the situation, characters, and setting.</li>
      <li><strong>Development:</strong> Show how events unfold and the problem develops.</li>
      <li><strong>Climax:</strong> Present the most tense or significant moment.</li>
      <li><strong>Resolution:</strong> Show how the problem ends and what changes.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Complete model narrative</h3>

    <p><strong>The Stranger at the Bus Stop</strong></p>

    <p>The rain had just begun when I reached the bus stop after school. Most people were hurrying home, protecting their bags beneath their jackets. Near the shelter, an elderly man sat beside a small suitcase, staring anxiously at the road.</p>

    <p>At first, I assumed he was waiting for a bus. Then I noticed that he kept looking at a folded piece of paper and asking passers-by questions. When I approached him, he explained that he had travelled to the area for a hospital appointment but could no longer remember which bus would take him home.</p>

    <p>I read the address on his paper and asked a nearby shopkeeper for directions. She recognised the neighbourhood and explained which route he needed. Unfortunately, the next bus was already full. I decided to wait with him until another one arrived, even though I knew my family would be wondering where I was.</p>

    <p>When the bus finally stopped, I helped him confirm the destination with the conductor. Before boarding, he thanked me and said that he had been afraid of getting stranded in the rain. I watched the bus disappear around the corner before calling home to explain my delay.</p>

    <p>That evening, I realised that helping someone does not always require money or special skills. Sometimes, it simply requires noticing another person''s difficulty and choosing not to walk away.</p>

    <h3>Why the narrative works</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>The rainy bus stop establishes a clear setting.</li>
      <li>The elderly man''s confusion creates a problem.</li>
      <li>The narrator takes practical action, moving the story forward.</li>
      <li>The ending resolves the problem and reflects on the experience.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Writing reminder</h3>
    <p>Keep events in a logical order. Use dialogue when it adds meaning, and choose details that develop the characters or the situation rather than filling the story with unrelated description.</p>
