    <h2>Expository Composition</h2>

    <p>An expository composition explains a topic clearly. It may describe a process, explain causes and effects, compare ideas, or provide information. Its main purpose is to help readers understand rather than simply entertain them or persuade them to accept an opinion.</p>

    <h3>How to organise an expository composition</h3>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Introduction:</strong> Introduce the topic and establish what will be explained.</li>
      <li><strong>Body paragraphs:</strong> Explain one main point at a time, using examples or relevant facts.</li>
      <li><strong>Conclusion:</strong> Bring together the explanation''s key points.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Complete model composition</h3>

    <p><strong>How Regular Revision Improves Learning</strong></p>

    <p>Regular revision helps learners strengthen their understanding of subjects they study in class. Rather than waiting until examinations approach, learners who review material consistently have more opportunities to discover what they understand and what still needs attention.</p>

    <p>One important benefit of revision is that it helps learners identify gaps in their knowledge. For example, a learner may understand how to solve a simple equation but struggle when the equation contains brackets. By attempting questions and checking each step, the learner can identify the exact difficulty instead of assuming that the entire topic is confusing.</p>

    <p>Revision also provides opportunities to practise applying knowledge. Reading notes may help a learner recognise a method, but solving a question independently shows whether the learner can use that method. When an answer is incorrect, studying the solution and attempting a similar question can help correct the misunderstanding.</p>

    <p>Another benefit is improved organisation. A realistic timetable spreads revision across several days and helps learners avoid relying entirely on last-minute study. Short, focused sessions can also make it easier to return to difficult material regularly.</p>

    <p>In conclusion, regular revision supports understanding, reveals weaknesses, and provides opportunities for independent practice. When learners combine reviewing their notes with answering questions and correcting errors, revision becomes a more purposeful part of learning.</p>

    <h3>Why this composition is expository</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>It explains a topic instead of telling a personal story.</li>
      <li>Each body paragraph develops a distinct reason or benefit.</li>
      <li>Examples clarify the explanation.</li>
      <li>The conclusion summarises the key points.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Use reliable information and relevant examples. Do not invent statistics or present an unsupported claim as an established fact.</p>
  ', 1),
  (1954, 1954, '
    <h2>Argumentative Composition</h2>

    <p>An argumentative composition presents a clear position on a debatable issue and supports it with reasons, examples, and relevant evidence. Strong arguments explain why the evidence supports the position instead of merely repeating an opinion.</p>

    <h3>Building a strong argument</h3>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Identify the issue:</strong> Understand exactly what the question asks you to debate.</li>
      <li><strong>State your position:</strong> Make your main claim clear.</li>
      <li><strong>Develop reasons:</strong> Explain the main reasons supporting your position.</li>
      <li><strong>Support your reasons:</strong> Use relevant examples, evidence, and explanations.</li>
      <li><strong>Consider an opposing view:</strong> Represent it fairly and explain your response.</li>
      <li><strong>Conclude:</strong> Reinforce your position based on the argument developed.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Complete model composition</h3>

    <p><strong>Should Schools Provide Time for Daily Reading?</strong></p>

    <p>Schools should provide a regular period for independent reading because strong reading skills support learning across many subjects. Although classroom lessons introduce important knowledge, learners also need opportunities to read independently, develop vocabulary, and understand different kinds of texts.</p>

    <p>First, regular reading gives learners repeated exposure to words and sentence structures. A learner who reads a variety of well-written texts can encounter unfamiliar vocabulary in context and observe how ideas are organised. These experiences can support comprehension and improve the learner''s own writing.</p>

    <p>Second, independent reading encourages learners to take responsibility for their learning. When learners choose suitable books and read consistently, they practise concentrating on a text, following an argument, and identifying its main ideas. These skills are useful beyond English lessons.</p>

    <p>Some people may argue that the school timetable is already crowded and that an additional reading period would reduce time for examinable subjects. This concern deserves attention. However, a carefully planned reading period need not replace essential lessons; schools can organise it within existing library or reading programmes and select materials that support the curriculum.</p>

    <p>In conclusion, a regular reading period can strengthen comprehension, expand vocabulary, and encourage independent learning. If schools organise it sensibly and provide suitable reading materials, the benefits can justify making time for this activity.</p>

    <h3>Why this argument works</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>The writer states a clear position.</li>
      <li>Each body paragraph develops a reason.</li>
      <li>The opposing view is acknowledged rather than ignored.</li>
      <li>The response addresses the concern without insulting those who disagree.</li>
      <li>The conclusion follows from the reasons already discussed.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Evidence matters</h3>
    <p>Use relevant facts, credible sources, examples, or sound reasoning. Do not invent survey results or statistics to make an argument appear stronger. Distinguish clearly between evidence, assumptions, and personal opinions.</p>
