    <h2>Report Structure</h2>

    <p>A report presents information about an event, investigation, activity or situation in a clear, organised and factual way.</p>

    <h3>Main parts of a report</h3>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Title:</strong> States what the report is about.</li>
      <li><strong>Introduction:</strong> Explains the purpose and scope of the report.</li>
      <li><strong>Findings:</strong> Presents the information discovered, preferably under clear headings.</li>
      <li><strong>Conclusion:</strong> Summarises what the findings show.</li>
      <li><strong>Recommendations:</strong> Suggests practical action where appropriate.</li>
      <li><strong>Name and date:</strong> Identifies the writer and when the report was prepared, when required.</li>
    </ol>

    <p>The structure depends on the assignment. A short report may combine sections, while a formal investigation may require all of them. Reports should distinguish observed facts from opinions and recommendations.</p>

    <h3>Model report</h3>

    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:14px;">
      <h3 style="text-align:center;">REPORT ON THE CONDITION OF THE SCHOOL LIBRARY</h3>
      <p><strong>Prepared by:</strong> Library Prefect</p>
      <p><strong>Date:</strong> 12 September 2026</p>

      <h4>1. Introduction</h4>
      <p>This report presents the findings of an inspection of the school library conducted on 10 September 2026. The inspection focused on the condition of books, furniture and reading space.</p>

      <h4>2. Findings</h4>
      <p>Several textbooks were damaged, and some books were not arranged according to subject. Two reading tables had loose legs. The room was generally clean, although the number of chairs was insufficient during busy study periods.</p>

      <h4>3. Conclusion</h4>
      <p>The library provides a useful study space, but damaged materials and inadequate seating limit its effectiveness.</p>

      <h4>4. Recommendations</h4>
      <p>The school should repair the tables, replace severely damaged books, label the shelves and provide additional chairs.</p>

      <p><strong>Signed:</strong> Library Prefect</p>
    </div>

    <h3>What makes this report effective?</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>The title identifies the subject.</li>
      <li>The introduction explains why the report was written.</li>
      <li>The findings give specific observations rather than unsupported complaints.</li>
      <li>The conclusion follows from the findings.</li>
      <li>The recommendations suggest actions related to the problems identified.</li>
    </ul>
  
<hr>

    <h2>Report Structure</h2>

    <p>A report presents information about an event, investigation or situation in an organised and objective way. The structure depends on the task, but a school report often includes a title, purpose, method or background, findings, conclusion and recommendations.</p>

    <h3>Complete example: Report on Classroom Cleanliness</h3>

    <div class="writing-example">
      <h3>REPORT ON CLASSROOM CLEANLINESS</h3>
      <p><strong>Prepared by:</strong> Kevin Mwangi, Class Representative</p>
      <p><strong>Date:</strong> 10 October 2026</p>

      <h4>1. Purpose</h4>
      <p>This report examines classroom cleanliness and identifies practical ways to improve it.</p>

      <h4>2. Method</h4>
      <p>The classroom was observed over five school days. The class representative also discussed cleaning routines with students on duty.</p>

      <h4>3. Findings</h4>
      <p>Paper was sometimes left under desks after lessons. The waste bin was occasionally full before the end of the day. The cleaning rota was displayed, but some students were unsure who was responsible for emptying the bin.</p>

      <h4>4. Conclusion</h4>
      <p>The classroom can be kept cleaner if waste is disposed of promptly and daily responsibilities are made clearer.</p>

      <h4>5. Recommendations</h4>
      <ol>
        <li>Display the cleaning rota where everyone can see it.</li>
        <li>Assign a student to check the bin at the end of each day.</li>
        <li>Remind students to collect paper and other waste before leaving.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>

    <h3>Keep findings separate from recommendations</h3>
    <p><strong>Findings</strong> describe what was observed. <strong>Recommendations</strong> propose actions. Do not present an untested assumption as a proven fact.</p>
