  (1966, 1966, '
    <h2>Investigative Reports</h2>

    <p>An investigative report explains what was examined, what evidence was collected, what the evidence shows and what conclusions can reasonably be drawn.</p>

    <h3>Typical structure</h3>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Title and purpose:</strong> Identify the issue being investigated.</li>
      <li><strong>Method:</strong> Explain how information was collected.</li>
      <li><strong>Findings:</strong> Present observations and evidence.</li>
      <li><strong>Conclusion:</strong> State what the evidence supports.</li>
      <li><strong>Recommendations:</strong> Propose appropriate action.</li>
    </ol>

    <p>Only describe methods that were actually used. Do not accuse a person of wrongdoing without sufficient evidence, and distinguish confirmed facts from possibilities.</p>

    <h3>Model investigative report</h3>

    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:14px;">
      <h3 style="text-align:center;">INVESTIGATIVE REPORT ON MISSING LIBRARY BOOKS</h3>
      <p><strong>Prepared by:</strong> Library Committee</p>
      <p><strong>Date:</strong> 20 September 2026</p>

      <h4>1. Purpose</h4>
      <p>The investigation was conducted to establish why five library books listed in the borrowing register could not be located during the weekly stock check.</p>

      <h4>2. Method</h4>
      <p>The committee checked the borrowing register, inspected the shelves and asked the librarian to verify the books'' return records.</p>

      <h4>3. Findings</h4>
      <p>Three books were recorded as borrowed and had no return entries. Two other books were listed in the register but were found on a shelf different from their assigned location. The available records did not establish who had last handled the three unreturned books.</p>

      <h4>4. Conclusion</h4>
      <p>The investigation established that two books had been misplaced. The records indicated that three books remained unaccounted for, but the available evidence did not establish why they were missing or who was responsible.</p>

      <h4>5. Recommendations</h4>
      <p>The library should follow up on the three outstanding loans, record returns immediately, and conduct regular shelf checks. A clear procedure for reporting misplaced books should also be introduced.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Important distinction</h3>
    <p>An investigation should not claim more than the evidence supports. If records show that a book was not returned, that does not automatically prove that a particular learner stole it.</p>
