    <h2>Project Reports</h2>

    <p>A project report documents a project''s purpose, activities, results, challenges and lessons. It allows readers to compare what was planned with what actually happened.</p>

    <h3>Typical structure</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Project title and reporting period</li>
      <li>Background and objectives</li>
      <li>Activities completed</li>
      <li>Results and evidence</li>
      <li>Challenges encountered</li>
      <li>Lessons learned and recommendations</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Complete model project report</h3>
    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:14px;">
      <h3 style="text-align:center;">REPORT ON THE SCHOOL TREE-PLANTING PROJECT</h3>
      <p><strong>Prepared by:</strong> Environmental Club</p>
      <p><strong>Reporting period:</strong> September–October 2026</p>
      <p><strong>Date:</strong> 30 October 2026</p>

      <h4>1. Background and Objectives</h4>
      <p>The Environmental Club organised a tree-planting project to increase shade around the school and encourage learners to care for the environment.</p>

      <h4>2. Activities Completed</h4>
      <p>Members identified planting locations, prepared the ground, planted seedlings and organised a watering rota. The club also labelled the planted areas for monitoring.</p>

      <h4>3. Results</h4>
      <p>The club planted 30 seedlings during the project. A follow-up check found that 27 were alive at the time of inspection. The remaining three required assessment to determine whether they could recover or needed replacement.</p>

      <h4>4. Challenges</h4>
      <p>Some seedlings received insufficient water during a dry period. The watering rota was not followed consistently in the first week.</p>

      <h4>5. Recommendations</h4>
      <p>The club recommends assigning named learners to each section, recording watering activities and checking seedling survival every two weeks.</p>

      <h4>6. Conclusion</h4>
      <p>The project established 30 seedlings, and the first inspection found 27 alive. Continued care and regular monitoring will be needed to improve their chances of survival.</p>
    </div>

    <p>A project report must distinguish activities from results. Planting a seedling does not prove that it survived, and an early result should not be presented as a long-term outcome.</p>
  
<hr>

    <h2>Project Reports</h2>

    <p>A project report records what a project set out to do, how it was carried out, what happened and what can be learned. Unlike a proposal, which requests approval for a plan, a project report describes work already undertaken.</p>

    <h3>Complete example: School Book Collection Project</h3>

    <div class="writing-example">
      <h3>PROJECT REPORT: SCHOOL BOOK COLLECTION</h3>
      <p><strong>Prepared by:</strong> School Reading Club</p>
      <p><strong>Reporting period:</strong> 1–30 September 2026</p>

      <h4>1. Introduction</h4>
      <p>The reading club organised a book collection project to increase the number of reading materials available to students.</p>

      <h4>2. Objectives</h4>
      <p>The project aimed to collect suitable books, encourage responsible book sharing and make additional reading materials available in the school library.</p>

      <h4>3. Activities Completed</h4>
      <p>The club announced the collection, received donated books and sorted them by subject and reading level. The teacher in charge checked the donations before the books were placed in the library.</p>

      <h4>4. Results</h4>
      <p>The club received 48 books. After inspection, 42 were accepted for the library and six were set aside because they were damaged or unsuitable. The accepted books were labelled and recorded.</p>

      <h4>5. Challenges</h4>
      <p>Some donations arrived without clear titles or authors, which increased the time needed for sorting. Storage space was also limited.</p>

      <h4>6. Lessons Learned</h4>
      <p>Future collections should provide clear donation guidelines and arrange storage before books begin arriving.</p>

      <h4>7. Recommendations</h4>
      <ol>
        <li>Provide donors with a list of acceptable books.</li>
        <li>Prepare labelled storage boxes before the next collection.</li>
        <li>Schedule regular checks of the donated books.</li>
      </ol>

      <h4>8. Conclusion</h4>
      <p>The project added 42 suitable books to the school library. Better donation guidelines and preparation could make the next collection more efficient.</p>
    </div>

    <p><strong>Key distinction:</strong> A proposal describes what is planned; a project report records what was done and the results actually obtained.</p>
