  (1982, 1982, '
    <h2>Press Releases</h2>

    <p>A press release is an announcement prepared by an organisation for journalists or the public. It presents the most important information first and provides a contact for further enquiries. It should distinguish confirmed facts from plans or expectations.</p>

    <h3>Complete example</h3>

    <div class="writing-example">
      <h3>FOR IMMEDIATE RELEASE</h3>

      <p><strong>Embu Secondary School Announces Annual Reading Day</strong></p>

      <p><strong>EMBU, 10 October 2026</strong> — Embu Secondary School has announced that its annual Reading Day will take place on 24 October 2026 at the school library.</p>

      <p>The event will bring students together for individual reading, small-group book discussions and short presentations recommending books to other learners. The school reading club is organising the programme with the support of teachers.</p>

      <p>“We want students to discover books they enjoy and feel confident sharing what they learn,” said Kevin Mwangi, secretary of the reading club.</p>

      <p>The programme is scheduled to begin at 9.00 a.m. Students interested in participating should contact the English teacher by 20 October. Attendance by visitors will be subject to school approval.</p>

      <p><strong>Media and Information Contact:</strong></p>
      <p>Amina Hassan</p>
      <p>Reading Club Secretary</p>
      <p>Email: readingclub@example.com</p>

      <p><strong>About Embu Secondary School</strong></p>
      <p>Embu Secondary School provides secondary education and supports student participation in academic and co-curricular activities.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Key conventions</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Put the main announcement near the beginning.</li>
      <li>Include a dateline and the essential event details.</li>
      <li>Use quotations only when they are real and approved.</li>
      <li>Provide a contact for follow-up questions.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>The sample quotation above is illustrative. In a real press release, do not attribute invented words to a real person.</p>
  ', 1),
  (1983, 1983, '
    <h2>Personal Statements</h2>

    <p>A personal statement explains why a person is applying for a course, programme or opportunity. It connects interests, experience, skills and future plans to the specific opportunity. It should sound personal without making unsupported claims.</p>

    <h3>Complete example: Application for a Student Coding Programme</h3>

    <div class="writing-example">
      <h3>PERSONAL STATEMENT</h3>

      <p>I am applying for the Student Coding Programme because I want to learn how software can solve practical problems in my community. My interest began when I tried to understand how educational applications help learners practise difficult topics and track their progress.</p>

      <p>At school, I have developed patience through solving Mathematics problems and persistence when a solution does not work the first time. I also enjoy working with classmates because explaining an idea often reveals whether I truly understand it.</p>

      <p>I am particularly interested in learning the fundamentals of programming, testing and user-centred design. I would like to build small projects, receive feedback and improve them through repeated practice.</p>

      <p>I understand that software development requires more than writing code. It also requires listening to users, checking assumptions and taking responsibility for errors. I am ready to learn from mistakes and work consistently with other participants.</p>

      <p>By joining the programme, I hope to strengthen my technical foundations and develop the skills needed to create useful and reliable software. I would contribute curiosity, persistence and a willingness to collaborate.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>How to strengthen a personal statement</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Explain why this particular opportunity matters to you.</li>
      <li>Use specific experiences to support claims about your skills.</li>
      <li>Connect your interests to what the programme offers.</li>
      <li>End with a realistic account of what you hope to contribute and learn.</li>
    </ul>
  ', 1),
  (1984, 1984, '
    <h2>Evaluation Reports</h2>

    <p>An evaluation report judges how well a project, event or programme met its aims. It uses evidence to identify achievements, weaknesses and improvements. It should not confuse what was planned with what actually happened.</p>

    <h3>Complete example: Evaluation of a School Reading Day</h3>

    <div class="writing-example">
      <h3>EVALUATION REPORT: SCHOOL READING DAY</h3>
      <p><strong>Prepared by:</strong> School Reading Club</p>
      <p><strong>Date:</strong> 30 October 2026</p>

      <h4>1. Purpose of the Evaluation</h4>
      <p>This report evaluates the school Reading Day held on 24 October 2026 and identifies improvements for future events.</p>

      <h4>2. Intended Outcomes</h4>
      <p>The organisers aimed to encourage reading, involve students in book discussions and give participants an opportunity to recommend books.</p>

      <h4>3. Evidence Collected</h4>
      <p>The organisers recorded attendance, reviewed the event schedule and collected feedback forms from participants. The figures below are illustrative for this model report.</p>

      <h4>4. Results</h4>
      <p>Forty students attended. Thirty-two feedback forms were returned. Twenty-six respondents said that the group discussion was useful, while six asked for more time to read before discussing their books.</p>

      <h4>5. What Worked Well</h4>
      <p>The group discussions gave students opportunities to explain their opinions. The book-recommendation session also introduced participants to titles they had not previously considered.</p>

      <h4>6. Challenges</h4>
      <p>The schedule allowed limited time for individual reading. Some groups also needed clearer instructions about how long each presentation should last.</p>

      <h4>7. Recommendations</h4>
      <ol>
        <li>Increase the time allocated to individual reading.</li>
        <li>Provide presentation guidelines before the event.</li>
        <li>Continue collecting feedback to compare future events.</li>
      </ol>

      <h4>8. Conclusion</h4>
      <p>The event supported student discussion and book sharing. More preparation time and clearer presentation guidelines could improve the next Reading Day.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Evaluation requires evidence</h3>
    <p>A good evaluation explains what evidence was collected and how it supports the judgement. If the report uses estimates or sample figures, label them clearly rather than presenting them as verified results.</p>
  ', 1),
  (1985, 1985, '
    <h2>Listening for Main Ideas</h2>

    <p>The main idea is the central message a speaker wants the audience to understand. It is broader than a single fact or example.</p>

    <h3>How to identify the main idea</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Listen for the topic being discussed.</li>
      <li>Notice ideas the speaker repeats or emphasises.</li>
      <li>Separate the central message from supporting examples.</li>
      <li>Summarise the message in one sentence.</li>
      <li>Check that the sentence represents the whole message, not just one detail.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Listening passage</h3>
    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:14px;">
      <p><strong>Teacher''s announcement:</strong></p>
      <p>Our school has introduced a reading period every Wednesday afternoon. During this time, learners may read novels, biographies, newspapers or other suitable materials. Teachers will help learners choose texts appropriate to their interests and reading levels. The purpose is to develop regular reading habits and improve learners'' ability to understand different kinds of texts. Learners should bring their reading materials and use the period quietly.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Identifying the main idea</h3>
    <p>The announcement explains a new weekly reading period intended to develop regular reading habits and reading comprehension.</p>

    <p>The types of reading materials are supporting details. They help explain the programme, but they are not the central message by themselves.</p>
  ', 1),
  (1986, 1986, '
    <h2>Listening for Specific Details</h2>

    <p>Listening for specific details means identifying exact information such as times, places, quantities, names, instructions and deadlines.</p>

    <h3>Effective strategy</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Identify what information the question asks for.</li>
      <li>Listen for key words and related expressions.</li>
      <li>Record exact figures and names carefully.</li>
      <li>Distinguish similar details, such as a meeting time and a submission deadline.</li>
      <li>Check your answer against the information actually spoken.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Listening passage</h3>
    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:14px;">
      <p><strong>Club secretary''s announcement:</strong></p>
      <p>The Environmental Club will meet on Thursday, 22 October, at 3:40 p.m. in the science laboratory. Members should bring their notebooks. The clean-up exercise will take place on Saturday, 24 October, beginning at 8:00 a.m. Anyone who cannot attend the meeting should send an apology to the club secretary before Thursday afternoon.</p>
    </div>

    <p>When answering questions, use the exact information supplied. Do not confuse the Thursday meeting with the Saturday clean-up exercise.</p>
  ', 1),
  (1987, 1987, '
    <h2>Listening for Names, Dates and Numbers</h2>

    <p>Names, dates, times, telephone numbers and quantities are easy to confuse when listening. Accurate note-taking helps preserve this information.</p>

    <h3>What to do</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Listen carefully for the complete name, not just its first part.</li>
      <li>Distinguish similar-sounding numbers.</li>
      <li>Record dates with the month and day when necessary.</li>
      <li>Write times with a.m. or p.m. when the distinction matters.</li>
      <li>Ask for repetition or clarification when it is appropriate and possible.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Listening passage</h3>
    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:14px;">
      <p><strong>School office announcement:</strong></p>
      <p>Students selected for the science exhibition should report to the laboratory on Monday, 26 October, at 9:15 a.m. The registration list will be held by Ms Njeri. Each group must submit three display labels and one project summary. The final submission deadline is Wednesday, 28 October, at 4:00 p.m.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Model listening notes</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Activity:</strong> Science exhibition</li>
      <li><strong>Report to:</strong> Laboratory</li>
      <li><strong>Meeting date and time:</strong> Monday, 26 October, 9:15 a.m.</li>
      <li><strong>Contact person:</strong> Ms Njeri</li>
      <li><strong>Materials per group:</strong> Three display labels and one project summary</li>
      <li><strong>Deadline:</strong> Wednesday, 28 October, 4:00 p.m.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Organised notes make it easier to find the correct detail later. They also reduce the risk of confusing a meeting time with a submission deadline.</p>
  ', 1),
  (1988, 1988, '
    <h2>Following Multi-step Instructions</h2>

    <p>Multi-step instructions contain two or more actions that must be completed in a particular order. Missing or rearranging a step can lead to an incorrect result.</p>

    <h3>How to follow spoken instructions</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Listen to the full instruction before beginning, unless immediate action is required for safety.</li>
      <li>Identify the order of actions.</li>
      <li>Notice sequence words such as first, next, after that and finally.</li>
      <li>Remember conditions, warnings and deadlines.</li>
      <li>Repeat or write down the steps if needed.</li>
      <li>Check that the final task has been completed.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Listening passage</h3>
    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:14px;">
      <p><strong>Teacher''s instructions:</strong></p>
      <p>Before beginning the experiment, collect a clean beaker and place it on the laboratory bench. Next, measure 50 millilitres of water and pour it into the beaker. Record the initial volume in your notebook. Then add the supplied material and observe what happens. Record your observations before cleaning the equipment and returning it to the designated storage area. Do not taste or touch any laboratory material unless your teacher has explicitly confirmed that it is safe to do so.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Correct order of actions</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Collect a clean beaker and place it on the bench.</li>
      <li>Measure 50 millilitres of water and pour it into the beaker.</li>
      <li>Record the initial volume.</li>
      <li>Add the supplied material and observe.</li>
      <li>Record observations.</li>
      <li>Clean and return the equipment as instructed.</li>
    </ol>

    <p>Do not skip a recording step simply because the next action seems more interesting. In a practical activity, the order may be necessary for accurate results and safe work.</p>
  ', 1),
  (1989, 1989, '
    <h2>Speaker Purpose</h2>

    <p>A speaker''s purpose is the main reason for speaking. A speaker may want to inform, persuade, entertain, warn, instruct or request something.</p>

    <h3>How to identify purpose</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Identify the subject of the message.</li>
      <li>Notice what the speaker wants listeners to know, believe, feel or do.</li>
      <li>Look for instructions, reasons, warnings or attempts to influence the audience.</li>
      <li>Choose the purpose that best explains the whole message.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Listening passage A: Informing</h3>
    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:12px;">
      <p><strong>School announcement:</strong></p>
      <p>The school library will open at 8:00 a.m. from Monday to Friday beginning next week. Learners may borrow two books at a time. All borrowed books must be returned by the date recorded in the borrowing register.</p>
    </div>
    <p><strong>Purpose:</strong> To inform learners about library opening hours and borrowing rules.</p>

    <h3>Listening passage B: Persuading</h3>
    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:12px;">
      <p><strong>Environmental Club representative:</strong></p>
      <p>Fellow learners, we can reduce litter around our school if each of us uses the bins provided. Instead of leaving wrappers on the ground, carry them to a bin. Let us make our school cleaner through small actions that we practise every day.</p>
    </div>
    <p><strong>Purpose:</strong> To persuade learners to dispose of waste responsibly.</p>

    <h3>Listening passage C: Warning</h3>
    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:12px;">
      <p><strong>Laboratory teacher:</strong></p>
      <p>Do not begin the experiment until I have explained the safety procedure. Some materials can cause injury if they are handled incorrectly. Keep all materials away from your mouth and follow the instructions given during the demonstration.</p>
    </div>
    <p><strong>Purpose:</strong> To warn learners about hazards and prevent unsafe behaviour.</p>

    <p>Do not identify purpose using one word alone. Consider the complete message and the action or response the speaker wants from the audience.</p>
  ', 1),
  (1990, 1990, '
    <h2>Attitude and Emotion</h2>

    <p>A speaker''s attitude is their viewpoint or feeling towards a subject. Emotion is the feeling conveyed through words, voice, pace and emphasis.</p>

    <h3>Common attitudes and emotions</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Concern:</strong> The speaker believes a problem needs attention.</li>
      <li><strong>Enthusiasm:</strong> The speaker expresses excitement or strong interest.</li>
      <li><strong>Disappointment:</strong> The speaker expresses dissatisfaction about an outcome.</li>
      <li><strong>Uncertainty:</strong> The speaker is not sure what is true or what will happen.</li>
      <li><strong>Confidence:</strong> The speaker presents a view with assurance.</li>
      <li><strong>Sarcasm:</strong> The speaker uses words to mock or criticise, often meaning the opposite of their literal meaning.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Example 1: Concern</h3>
    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:12px;">
      <p><strong>Teacher:</strong> I am concerned that several learners have missed the revision sessions. The examination is approaching, and we need to make sure everyone has access to the material.</p>
    </div>
    <p>The words "I am concerned" directly express worry about learners missing revision.</p>

    <h3>Example 2: Enthusiasm</h3>
    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:12px;">
      <p><strong>Learner:</strong> I cannot wait for the science exhibition! Our group has worked hard on the model, and I am excited to show everyone how it works.</p>
    </div>
    <p>The phrases "cannot wait" and "I am excited" signal enthusiasm.</p>

    <h3>Example 3: Disappointment</h3>
    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:12px;">
      <p><strong>Coach:</strong> We practised carefully, but we did not follow our plan during the match. I expected a more disciplined performance.</p>
    </div>
    <p>The contrast between preparation and the result, together with the final sentence, suggests disappointment.</p>

    <p>In real listening tasks, attitude may be implied rather than directly named. Consider the words and the way they are delivered; do not assume that loudness alone means anger or that quiet speech means sadness.</p>
  ', 1),
  (1991, 1991, '
    <h2>Inferring Spoken Meaning</h2>

    <p>An inference is a reasonable conclusion drawn from what a speaker says and the context in which it is said. Speakers do not always state every idea directly.</p>

    <h3>How to make a sound inference</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Identify what the speaker explicitly says.</li>
      <li>Consider the situation and relevant background details.</li>
      <li>Connect the clues logically.</li>
      <li>Choose a conclusion supported by those clues.</li>
      <li>Avoid conclusions that require facts not provided.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Listening passage</h3>
    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:12px;">
      <p><strong>Conversation:</strong></p>
      <p><strong>Brian:</strong> Have you finished the science assignment?</p>
      <p><strong>Wanjiku:</strong> I have completed the diagrams and the first two questions, but I have not understood the final calculation. I am going to ask the teacher for help before submitting it tomorrow.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>What can we infer?</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Supported inference:</strong> Wanjiku has not completed the entire assignment.</li>
      <li><strong>Evidence:</strong> She says that she has not understood the final calculation and plans to seek help.</li>
      <li><strong>Unsupported inference:</strong> Wanjiku never studies science.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>The passage gives information about one assignment. It does not establish Wanjiku''s overall ability or study habits.</p>
  ', 1),
  (1992, 1992, '
    <h2>Note-taking While Listening</h2>

    <p>Listening notes capture the most important information from a spoken message. Good notes are brief, organised and accurate enough to be understood later.</p>

    <h3>How to take useful notes</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Listen for the topic and main points first.</li>
      <li>Record important facts, names, dates and quantities.</li>
      <li>Use short phrases instead of copying every sentence.</li>
      <li>Use headings and bullets to group related ideas.</li>
      <li>Use abbreviations only when you can understand them later.</li>
      <li>Review the notes soon after listening and clarify unclear details.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Listening passage</h3>
    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:12px;">
      <p><strong>Teacher''s talk: Preparing for examinations</strong></p>
      <p>Effective examination preparation begins with a realistic timetable. Learners should divide large topics into manageable sections and practise answering questions without looking at their notes. After marking their work, they should identify repeated mistakes and revisit the exact concepts they do not understand. Regular sleep and short breaks can also help learners maintain concentration. Finally, learners should prepare their materials the night before an examination to avoid unnecessary morning stress.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Example of effective notes</h3>
    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:12px;">
      <p><strong>Topic:</strong> Examination preparation</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Create a realistic timetable.</li>
        <li>Break large topics into manageable sections.</li>
        <li>Practise questions without notes.</li>
        <li>Mark work; identify repeated errors.</li>
        <li>Revisit concepts not understood.</li>
        <li>Maintain sleep and take short breaks.</li>
        <li>Prepare examination materials the night before.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <p>The notes preserve the main actions without copying the entire talk. They should still be specific enough to support revision later.</p>
  ', 1),
  (1993, 1993, '
    <h2>Summarising Spoken Information</h2>

    <p>A spoken summary presents the central message and most important supporting points in fewer words than the original talk. It should preserve the meaning without adding personal opinions.</p>

    <h3>Steps for summarising</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Identify the main idea.</li>
      <li>Select the essential supporting points.</li>
      <li>Remove examples and repetition that are not necessary.</li>
      <li>Combine related ideas in your own words.</li>
      <li>Check that the summary preserves the speaker''s meaning.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Listening passage</h3>
    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:12px;">
      <p><strong>School counsellor:</strong></p>
      <p>Healthy study habits depend on planning and consistency. Learners should set realistic goals, study difficult topics in manageable sessions and test themselves to discover what they have not understood. When they make mistakes, they should review the steps that caused the error rather than merely memorising the correct answer. Learners should also take breaks and get adequate sleep so that they can maintain concentration. A timetable is useful only when it is realistic and followed regularly.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Model summary</h3>
    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:12px;">
      <p>Effective study requires a realistic timetable, manageable study sessions and regular self-testing. Learners should analyse mistakes, review misunderstood concepts, take breaks and get enough sleep. Consistent use of a practical plan is more useful than creating an unrealistic timetable.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Why this summary works</h3>
    <p>It preserves the central advice and its main supporting points, combines related ideas and avoids adding claims that the speaker did not make.</p>
  ', 1),
  (1994, 1994, '
    <h2>Organising Spoken Ideas</h2>

    <p>Organising spoken ideas means arranging what you say so that listeners can follow your message. A speaker should establish the topic, develop connected points and finish with a clear conclusion.</p>

    <h3>A practical structure</h3>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Opening:</strong> State the topic or purpose.</li>
      <li><strong>Point one:</strong> Present the first important idea and explain it.</li>
      <li><strong>Point two:</strong> Add another connected idea or example.</li>
      <li><strong>Point three:</strong> Develop the next relevant idea, if needed.</li>
      <li><strong>Conclusion:</strong> Summarise the message or state the next action.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Example of disorganised speaking</h3>
    <p>"We need more books. The library is quiet. Last week it rained. Some learners borrow novels. We should have a reading club."</p>

    <p>The ideas are related only loosely and are not connected into a clear message.</p>

    <h3>Improved spoken contribution</h3>
    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:12px;">
      <p>"I would like to propose that our school establish a reading club. First, the club could help learners develop a regular reading habit. Second, members could recommend books and discuss what they have read. Finally, the club could organise a shared reading session each month. I believe this would make reading more engaging and encourage learners to explore different texts."</p>
    </div>

    <p>The improved version introduces a clear proposal, develops three connected points and concludes by explaining the expected benefit.</p>
  ', 1),
  (1995, 1995, '
    <h2>Clear Explanations</h2>

    <p>A clear explanation helps another person understand how something works, why something happens or how to complete a task.</p>

    <h3>How to explain clearly</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Begin with the main idea.</li>
      <li>Use familiar words where possible.</li>
      <li>Break complicated information into manageable steps.</li>
      <li>Explain technical terms that the listener may not know.</li>
      <li>Use a relevant example or comparison.</li>
      <li>Check whether the listener understands.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Example: Explaining evaporation</h3>
    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:12px;">
      <p><strong>Unclear explanation:</strong> "Evaporation is when water does its thing and disappears because it gets hot."</p>

      <p><strong>Clear explanation:</strong> "Evaporation occurs when particles at the surface of a liquid gain enough energy to enter the air as a gas. For example, a puddle gradually becomes smaller after a sunny day because some of its water changes into water vapour. The water has not simply vanished; it has changed state and entered the air."</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Example: Explaining a mathematical procedure</h3>
    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:12px;">
      <p><strong>Question:</strong> Solve 3x + 5 = 20.</p>
      <p><strong>Explanation:</strong></p>
      <ol>
        <li>Subtract 5 from both sides: 3x = 15.</li>
        <li>Divide both sides by 3: x = 5.</li>
        <li>Check the result: 3(5) + 5 = 20, so the answer is correct.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>

    <p>A clear explanation does not skip a necessary step or use a term in a way that changes the meaning.</p>
  ', 1),
  (1996, 1996, '
    <h2>Forming Questions</h2>

    <p>Clear questions help speakers obtain information, check understanding and guide a discussion. A good question makes it clear what information is needed.</p>

    <h3>Common question types</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Yes/no questions:</strong> Often begin with an auxiliary verb, such as "Did you finish the assignment?"</li>
      <li><strong>Wh-questions:</strong> Ask for information using who, what, when, where, why or how.</li>
      <li><strong>Clarification questions:</strong> Ask someone to explain a point more clearly.</li>
      <li><strong>Follow-up questions:</strong> Ask for more information about an answer.</li>
      <li><strong>Open questions:</strong> Encourage a developed response rather than a simple yes or no.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Model conversation</h3>
    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:12px;">
      <p><strong>Brian:</strong> I did not understand the final step in the mathematics example.</p>
      <p><strong>Amina:</strong> Which part was confusing?</p>
      <p><strong>Brian:</strong> Why did the teacher divide both sides by three?</p>
      <p><strong>Amina:</strong> Because the equation had 3x = 15. Dividing both sides by three leaves x on its own and gives x = 5.</p>
      <p><strong>Brian:</strong> Could you show me how the answer is checked?</p>
      <p><strong>Amina:</strong> Substitute 5 for x in the original equation: 3(5) + 5 = 20. The left side equals the right side, so the solution is correct.</p>
    </div>

    <p>The questions identify the precise difficulty, ask for the reason behind a step and request a way to verify the answer.</p>
  ', 1),
  (1997, 1997, '
    <h2>Relevant Answers</h2>

    <p>A relevant answer addresses the question that was asked. It gives enough information to respond clearly without drifting into unrelated details.</p>

    <h3>How to answer well</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Listen to the entire question.</li>
      <li>Identify the information being requested.</li>
      <li>Answer that point directly.</li>
      <li>Add a reason or example when useful.</li>
      <li>Stop when the question has been answered, unless more detail is requested.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Model interview conversation</h3>
    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:12px;">
      <p><strong>Interviewer:</strong> Why would you like to join the Environmental Club?</p>
      <p><strong>Applicant:</strong> I would like to join because I want to help keep the school clean and learn more about environmental conservation. I can also help organise clean-up activities and encourage other learners to dispose of waste responsibly.</p>

      <p><strong>Interviewer:</strong> How would you respond if a clean-up activity had very few volunteers?</p>
      <p><strong>Applicant:</strong> I would first ask learners why participation was low. Then I would explain the purpose of the activity, agree on a convenient time with the club and invite volunteers to take manageable roles.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Compare an irrelevant answer</h3>
    <p><strong>Question:</strong> Why would you like to join the Environmental Club?</p>
    <p><strong>Irrelevant answer:</strong> My favourite subject is mathematics, and I travelled to my grandmother''s home last holiday.</p>

    <p>The answer may contain true statements, but it does not explain the applicant''s reason for joining the club.</p>
  ', 1),
  (1998, 1998, '
    <h2>Turn-taking</h2>

    <p>Turn-taking is the process of sharing speaking time in a conversation. Effective speakers recognise when to speak, when to listen and how to enter or leave a discussion politely.</p>

    <h3>Useful turn-taking expressions</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Entering:</strong> "May I add something?"</li>
      <li><strong>Giving an opinion:</strong> "I think we should consider another option."</li>
      <li><strong>Agreeing:</strong> "I agree with that point because..."</li>
      <li><strong>Disagreeing politely:</strong> "I understand your view, but I see it differently."</li>
      <li><strong>Inviting others:</strong> "What do you think about this suggestion?"</li>
      <li><strong>Clarifying:</strong> "Could you explain what you mean by that?"</li>
      <li><strong>Yielding the turn:</strong> "That is my view. I would like to hear what others think."</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Model group discussion</h3>
    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:12px;">
      <p><strong>Chairperson:</strong> We need to decide how to improve waste disposal around the school. Brian, would you like to begin?</p>

      <p><strong>Brian:</strong> I think we should place more bins near the playing field because that area often has litter.</p>

      <p><strong>Amina:</strong> I agree that bins would help. May I add that we should also label them so learners know where different kinds of waste belong?</p>

      <p><strong>Wanjiku:</strong> I understand both suggestions, but I think we should first check whether the existing bins are emptied regularly. Additional bins will not solve the problem if they overflow.</p>

      <p><strong>Chairperson:</strong> That is an important point. Could we agree to inspect the existing bins this week and then decide where additional bins are needed?</p>

      <p><strong>Brian:</strong> I support that plan. We can record which areas need more bins.</p>

      <p><strong>Chairperson:</strong> Thank you. We will inspect the bins on Friday and discuss the findings at our next meeting.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>What the discussion demonstrates</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Participants contribute one at a time.</li>
      <li>They respond to the previous speaker''s ideas.</li>
      <li>They disagree without insulting one another.</li>
      <li>The chairperson invites contributions and guides the group towards a decision.</li>
    </ul>
  ', 1),
  (1999, 1999, '
    <h2>Maintaining Conversations</h2>

    <p>Maintaining a conversation means keeping an exchange going through relevant responses, follow-up questions and contributions connected to what another person has said.</p>

    <h3>Ways to maintain a conversation</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Listen carefully before responding.</li>
      <li>Answer the question or comment directly.</li>
      <li>Ask a relevant follow-up question.</li>
      <li>Share a related experience or idea.</li>
      <li>Allow the other person an opportunity to speak.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Example: A conversation about reading</h3>
    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:12px;">
      <p><strong>Amina:</strong> I have started reading novels during the weekend.</p>
      <p><strong>Brian:</strong> That sounds interesting. What kind of novels do you enjoy?</p>
      <p><strong>Amina:</strong> I enjoy mystery stories because I like trying to solve the problem before the characters do.</p>
      <p><strong>Brian:</strong> I can understand that. Have you read any good mystery novels recently?</p>
      <p><strong>Amina:</strong> Yes. I borrowed one from the school library last week. The ending surprised me.</p>
      <p><strong>Brian:</strong> What made the ending surprising?</p>
      <p><strong>Amina:</strong> The character I suspected was innocent. The real culprit had been leaving clues throughout the story.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Why the conversation works</h3>
    <p>Each response connects to the previous contribution. Brian answers naturally, asks relevant follow-up questions and gives Amina room to develop her ideas.</p>

    <h3>Example of a conversation that stops</h3>
    <p><strong>Amina:</strong> I have started reading novels during the weekend.</p>
    <p><strong>Brian:</strong> Okay.</p>
    <p>"Okay" is not always incorrect, but by itself it gives the other speaker little opportunity to continue.</p>
  ', 1),
  (2000, 2000, '
    <h2>Checking Understanding</h2>

    <p>Checking understanding means confirming that a message has been heard and interpreted correctly. It is especially useful when instructions, unfamiliar terms or important decisions are involved.</p>

    <h3>Useful expressions</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>"Do you mean that we should submit the report on Friday?"</li>
      <li>"Could you explain the second step again?"</li>
      <li>"Let me check that I have understood correctly."</li>
      <li>"So, we should complete the experiment first and record the results afterwards. Is that right?"</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Example: Clarifying an assignment</h3>
    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:12px;">
      <p><strong>Teacher:</strong> Work in groups of four. Prepare a report on water conservation and submit it next Friday. Each learner must contribute to the research.</p>
      <p><strong>Kevin:</strong> Excuse me, teacher. Should the report be handwritten or typed?</p>
      <p><strong>Teacher:</strong> It should be typed.</p>
      <p><strong>Kevin:</strong> Let me confirm: we work in groups of four, everyone contributes, and we submit one typed report next Friday. Is that correct?</p>
      <p><strong>Teacher:</strong> Yes, that is correct.</p>
    </div>

    <p>Kevin checks the format and restates the essential instructions. This reduces the chance of completing the task incorrectly.</p>

    <h3>When you do not understand</h3>
    <p>Identify the specific point that is unclear. Instead of saying only "I don''t understand anything," ask about the step, term or instruction that needs clarification.</p>
  ', 1);
INSERT INTO public.lesson_versions (id, lesson_id, content, version) VALUES
  (2001, 2001, '
    <h2>Expressing Opinions with Reasons</h2>

    <p>An opinion expresses what a person thinks or believes. A well-supported opinion states the position clearly and gives relevant reasons or evidence to explain it.</p>

    <h3>Useful expressions</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>"In my view..."</li>
      <li>"I believe this because..."</li>
      <li>"One reason for my opinion is..."</li>
      <li>"For example..."</li>
      <li>"This suggests that..."</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Model oral contribution</h3>
    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:12px;">
      <p><strong>Topic: Should schools establish quiet study periods?</strong></p>
      <p>"In my view, schools should establish quiet study periods during the week. First, these periods would give learners time to review difficult topics without unnecessary interruptions. Second, learners could identify questions to ask their teachers before examinations. For example, a learner who struggles with algebra could use the period to practise equations and identify the exact step causing difficulty. However, schools should ensure that the periods are supervised and that learners have suitable materials. For these reasons, I believe quiet study periods would be useful."</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Opinion versus unsupported assertion</h3>
    <p><strong>Unsupported:</strong> "Quiet study periods are the best idea because I said so."</p>
    <p><strong>Supported:</strong> "Quiet study periods may help learners concentrate because they provide dedicated time for independent practice."</p>

    <p>A reason strengthens an opinion, but it does not automatically prove that the opinion is true. Evidence and careful reasoning still matter.</p>
  ', 1),
  (2002, 2002, '
    <h2>Respectful Agreement and Disagreement</h2>

    <p>Respectful agreement acknowledges another person''s view. Respectful disagreement presents a different view without insulting the person who expressed the original idea.</p>

    <h3>Expressions of agreement</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>"I agree with your point because..."</li>
      <li>"That is a good observation."</li>
      <li>"I share that view, particularly because..."</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Expressions of disagreement</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>"I understand your point, but I see it differently."</li>
      <li>"I agree with part of your argument; however..."</li>
      <li>"Could we also consider another possibility?"</li>
      <li>"The evidence may suggest a different conclusion."</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Model conversation</h3>
    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:12px;">
      <p><strong>Peter:</strong> I think our class should use all its activity funds to buy more textbooks.</p>
      <p><strong>Mary:</strong> I agree that textbooks are important. However, I think we should first check which subjects lack enough copies before deciding how to spend all the money.</p>
      <p><strong>Peter:</strong> That is reasonable. I was mainly concerned that some learners do not have access to the required books.</p>
      <p><strong>Mary:</strong> I understand. We could count the available books, identify the biggest shortages and then prioritise purchases.</p>
      <p><strong>Peter:</strong> I support that approach because it would help us direct the money where it is most needed.</p>
    </div>

    <p>The speakers challenge and refine the proposal without attacking each other. They identify common ground and develop a practical solution.</p>
  ', 1),
  (2003, 2003, '
    <h2>Discussions and Debates</h2>

    <p>A discussion allows participants to explore an issue and exchange views. A debate is more structured: speakers argue for or against a stated motion, using reasons and evidence.</p>

    <h3>Elements of a good debate</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Understand the exact motion.</li>
      <li>State whether you support or oppose it.</li>
      <li>Develop clear arguments.</li>
      <li>Support claims with examples or evidence.</li>
      <li>Address opposing arguments fairly.</li>
      <li>Conclude by explaining why your case is convincing.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Model debate excerpt</h3>
    <p><strong>Motion:</strong> This house believes that secondary schools should provide a daily supervised study period.</p>

    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:12px;">
      <p><strong>Proposition speaker:</strong></p>
      <p>"Chairperson, adjudicators and fellow learners, I support the motion that secondary schools should provide a daily supervised study period. First, it would give learners dedicated time to practise difficult topics and seek clarification. Second, it could help learners establish consistent study habits. For example, a learner who repeatedly struggles with equations could use the period to practise and identify the step causing the difficulty. I recognise that schools have limited time, so the period should be short and carefully scheduled. I therefore urge the house to support the motion."</p>

      <p><strong>Opposition speaker:</strong></p>
      <p>"Chairperson, I oppose the motion as a requirement for every school. Although supervised study can be useful, schools have different timetables and learner needs. Some learners may already have supervised preparation periods, while others may need time for clubs, sports or travel home. Instead of requiring the same arrangement everywhere, schools should be allowed to choose a suitable approach based on their circumstances. For these reasons, I oppose making a daily supervised study period compulsory for all schools."</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Evaluating the arguments</h3>
    <p>The proposition argues that regular supervised practice can support learning. The opposition argues that a compulsory arrangement may not suit every school. Both speakers address the motion and give reasons rather than relying on personal attacks.</p>

    <p>A strong debater responds to the opposing argument, not to an exaggerated version of it. Claims should be supported with evidence where available.</p>
  ', 1),
  (2004, 2004, '
    <h2>Oral Presentations</h2>

    <p>An oral presentation is a planned talk delivered to an audience. It should have a clear purpose, an organised structure and language appropriate to the audience.</p>

    <h3>Planning the presentation</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Identify the topic and audience.</li>
      <li>Choose a small number of main points.</li>
      <li>Arrange the points in a logical order.</li>
      <li>Prepare examples or evidence.</li>
      <li>Practise speaking clearly and within the available time.</li>
      <li>Prepare for likely questions.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Complete model presentation</h3>
    <p><strong>Topic: Reducing Plastic Waste at School</strong></p>

    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:12px;">
      <p><strong>Opening</strong></p>
      <p>"Good morning, teachers and fellow learners. Today, I would like to discuss three practical ways our school can reduce plastic waste."</p>

      <p><strong>First point: Reduce unnecessary plastic</strong></p>
      <p>"First, we can reduce unnecessary plastic by carrying reusable water bottles and lunch containers. This would reduce the number of disposable bottles and wrappers thrown away each day."</p>

      <p><strong>Second point: Improve waste collection</strong></p>
      <p>"Second, we should place clearly labelled waste bins in suitable locations. Learners should use them properly, and the school should ensure that collected waste is handled responsibly."</p>

      <p><strong>Third point: Build shared responsibility</strong></p>
      <p>"Third, environmental clubs and class representatives can organise awareness activities. These activities should explain practical actions rather than simply telling people not to litter."</p>

      <p><strong>Conclusion</strong></p>
      <p>"In conclusion, reducing plastic waste requires prevention, proper collection and shared responsibility. If we begin with reusable containers and use the available bins correctly, we can make our school cleaner. Thank you for listening."</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Delivery</h3>
    <p>Speak at a pace the audience can follow. Look up regularly rather than reading every word from a page. Use pauses between major points and avoid adding information that distracts from the central message.</p>
  ', 1),
  (2005, 2005, '
    <h2>Interviews</h2>

    <p>An interview is a structured conversation in which one person asks questions to obtain information, assess suitability or understand another person''s experiences and views.</p>

    <h3>How to respond in an interview</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Listen to the complete question.</li>
      <li>Answer the question directly.</li>
      <li>Give a relevant example when appropriate.</li>
      <li>Be truthful about your experience and abilities.</li>
      <li>Use polite language and an appropriate tone.</li>
      <li>Ask for clarification if a question is unclear.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Complete model interview</h3>
    <p><strong>Situation:</strong> A learner is being interviewed for a student library assistant position.</p>

    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:12px;">
      <p><strong>Interviewer:</strong> Good morning. Please introduce yourself.</p>
      <p><strong>Applicant:</strong> Good morning. My name is Daniel, and I am a learner at this school. I enjoy reading and helping classmates find useful learning materials.</p>

      <p><strong>Interviewer:</strong> Why would you like to become a library assistant?</p>
      <p><strong>Applicant:</strong> I would like to help learners access books and keep the library organised. I also want to develop my responsibility and communication skills.</p>

      <p><strong>Interviewer:</strong> What would you do if a learner returned a book late?</p>
      <p><strong>Applicant:</strong> I would follow the library rules, record the return correctly and explain the procedure politely. If I were unsure what to do, I would ask the teacher in charge.</p>

      <p><strong>Interviewer:</strong> What would you do if you did not know where a particular book was kept?</p>
      <p><strong>Applicant:</strong> I would check the catalogue or ask the librarian for help instead of giving the learner incorrect information.</p>

      <p><strong>Interviewer:</strong> Do you have any questions for us?</p>
      <p><strong>Applicant:</strong> Yes, please. What training would student library assistants receive before beginning their duties?</p>

      <p><strong>Interviewer:</strong> We provide an orientation on borrowing procedures and how to organise the books.</p>
      <p><strong>Applicant:</strong> Thank you for explaining that. I would be happy to learn the procedures and follow them carefully.</p>
    </div>

    <p>The applicant gives direct, relevant answers, describes sensible actions and asks a question connected to the role.</p>
  ', 1),
  (2006, 2006, '
    <h2>Facial Expressions and Gestures</h2>

    <p>Non-verbal communication conveys information through facial expressions, gestures, posture, eye contact and physical movement. It can support spoken words, but its meaning depends on context and culture.</p>

    <h3>Examples of non-verbal communication</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Nodding:</strong> May signal agreement or that a listener is following the message.</li>
      <li><strong>Raised hand:</strong> May signal a wish to speak or ask a question.</li>
      <li><strong>Open posture:</strong> May help communicate attentiveness.</li>
      <li><strong>Frowning:</strong> May indicate concern, confusion or displeasure, depending on the situation.</li>
      <li><strong>Eye contact:</strong> Can support engagement, but expectations vary by culture and context.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Example: A classroom presentation</h3>
    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:12px;">
      <p>A learner is presenting a science project. The learner stands facing the class, speaks clearly, occasionally looks at different sections of the audience and uses a hand gesture to indicate a labelled part of the model.</p>
      <p>When a classmate raises a hand, the presenter pauses and invites the question.</p>
    </div>

    <p>The learner''s posture, gestures and response to the raised hand support the spoken message and help manage the interaction.</p>

    <h3>Important caution</h3>
    <p>Do not assume that one gesture always has the same meaning. A person who looks away may be thinking, reading notes, feeling uncomfortable or following a cultural norm. Interpret non-verbal behaviour alongside words and context.</p>
  ', 1),
  (2007, 2007, '
    <h2>Short Vowel Sounds</h2>

    <p>Vowel letters and vowel sounds are not the same thing. English spelling does not always reveal the exact sound a vowel makes. A short vowel is a term used for a common vowel sound, especially in words such as <em>cat</em>, <em>bed</em>, <em>sit</em>, <em>hot</em> and <em>cup</em>.</p>

    <h3>Examples of common short vowel sounds</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>cat:</strong> the vowel sound in cat.</li>
      <li><strong>bed:</strong> the vowel sound in bed.</li>
      <li><strong>sit:</strong> the vowel sound in sit.</li>
      <li><strong>hot:</strong> the vowel sound in hot; its exact quality varies by accent.</li>
      <li><strong>cup:</strong> the vowel sound in cup.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Compare similar words</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>ship / sheep:</strong> the vowel sounds distinguish the two words.</li>
      <li><strong>full / fool:</strong> the vowel sounds are different in many standard accents.</li>
      <li><strong>bed / bad:</strong> the vowel sounds differ.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Practice passage</h3>
    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:12px;">
      <p>"The cat sat on a mat. Ben put his red bag beside the desk. Kim picked up a cup and set it on the shelf."</p>
    </div>

    <p>Read the passage aloud slowly. Listen for the vowel sounds in <em>cat</em>, <em>Ben</em>, <em>Kim</em>, <em>cup</em> and <em>red</em>. Then read it at a natural pace without changing the words.</p>

    <p>Pronunciation can vary between English accents. The goal is to produce understandable contrasts consistently, not to assume that every speaker uses exactly the same vowel quality.</p>
  ', 1),
  (2008, 2008, '
    <h2>Long Vowel Sounds</h2>

    <p>The term "long vowel" is commonly used in English teaching for vowel sounds such as those heard in <em>see</em>, <em>name</em> and <em>food</em>. It does not simply mean that every vowel is held for a long time. The exact vowel quality and duration depend on the word and accent.</p>

    <h3>Common examples</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>see:</strong> the vowel sound in see.</li>
      <li><strong>name:</strong> the vowel sound in name.</li>
      <li><strong>time:</strong> the vowel sound in time.</li>
      <li><strong>home:</strong> the vowel sound in home.</li>
      <li><strong>food:</strong> the vowel sound in food.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Compare words</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>sit / seat:</strong> different vowel sounds.</li>
      <li><strong>cap / cape:</strong> different vowel sounds.</li>
      <li><strong>hop / hope:</strong> different vowel sounds.</li>
      <li><strong>full / fool:</strong> different vowel sounds in many standard accents.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Practice passage</h3>
    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:12px;">
      <p>"Steve chose a seat near the window. He wrote his name, checked the time and put his food beside his bag."</p>
    </div>

    <p>Read the passage aloud. Pay attention to the vowel contrasts in <em>seat</em>, <em>name</em>, <em>time</em> and <em>food</em>. Then compare them with the shorter vowel sounds in words such as <em>sit</em>, <em>cap</em>, <em>hop</em> and <em>full</em>.</p>

    <h3>Spelling is not a perfect guide</h3>
    <p>Different spellings can represent similar sounds, and the same spelling can represent different sounds. Learn the pronunciation of words through listening, speaking and reliable dictionary audio as well as spelling patterns.</p>
  ', 1),
  (2009, 2009, '
    <h2>Diphthongs</h2>

    <p>A diphthong is a vowel sound that moves from one vowel position towards another within the same syllable. The tongue and mouth change position as the sound is produced.</p>

    <h3>Common English diphthongs</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>day:</strong> the vowel sound in day.</li>
      <li><strong>my:</strong> the vowel sound in my.</li>
      <li><strong>boy:</strong> the vowel sound in boy.</li>
      <li><strong>go:</strong> the vowel sound in go.</li>
      <li><strong>now:</strong> the vowel sound in now.</li>
      <li><strong>near:</strong> contains a vowel sequence commonly taught as a diphthong in some English accents.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Exact vowel sounds vary by English accent. Some accents pronounce particular vowel sequences differently, especially in words such as <em>near</em>.</p>

    <h3>Practise the movement</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Say <em>day</em> slowly and notice how the vowel changes during the sound.</li>
      <li>Repeat with <em>my</em>, <em>boy</em>, <em>go</em> and <em>now</em>.</li>
      <li>Say each word naturally without separating the vowel movement into two syllables.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Practice passage</h3>
    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:12px;">
      <p>"Today, Roy and I will go to the town fair. We may buy a toy, enjoy the music and head home before it gets too late."</p>
    </div>

    <p>Read the passage aloud. Practise the vowel sounds in <em>today</em>, <em>Roy</em>, <em>my</em>, <em>toy</em>, <em>go</em> and <em>town</em>.</p>
  ', 1),
  (2010, 2010, '
    <h2>Voiced and Voiceless Consonants</h2>

    <p>Voiced consonants are produced with vibration of the vocal folds. Voiceless consonants are produced without vocal-fold vibration during the consonant sound.</p>

    <h3>Common consonant pairs</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>/p/ and /b/:</strong> pat / bat.</li>
      <li><strong>/t/ and /d/:</strong> ten / den.</li>
      <li><strong>/k/ and /g/:</strong> coat / goat.</li>
      <li><strong>/f/ and /v/:</strong> fan / van.</li>
      <li><strong>/s/ and /z/:</strong> sip / zip.</li>
      <li><strong>/θ/ and /ð/:</strong> thin / then.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Feel the difference</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Place your fingers lightly on the front of your throat.</li>
      <li>Say a long /s/ sound, then a long /z/ sound.</li>
      <li>You should feel vocal-fold vibration more clearly during /z/.</li>
      <li>Repeat with /f/ and /v/.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Practice passage</h3>
    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:12px;">
      <p>"Ben packed a bag before visiting his friend. They found fresh fruit near the van and shared it beside the gate."</p>
    </div>

    <p>Practise the initial consonants in <em>packed</em>, <em>bag</em>, <em>friend</em>, <em>fresh</em>, <em>fruit</em> and <em>van</em>. Listen for the difference between paired consonants.</p>

    <p>Vocal-fold vibration is one feature of consonants; tongue and lip positions also help distinguish them.</p>
  ', 1),
  (2011, 2011, '
    <h2>Contrasting Consonant Sounds</h2>

    <p>English contains consonant sounds that differ in how or where they are produced. Confusing them can change the meaning of a word.</p>

    <h3>Useful contrasts</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>/l/ and /r/:</strong> light / right.</li>
      <li><strong>/ʃ/ and /tʃ/:</strong> ship / chip.</li>
      <li><strong>/s/ and /ʃ/:</strong> sip / ship.</li>
      <li><strong>/θ/ and /t/:</strong> thin / tin.</li>
      <li><strong>/v/ and /w/:</strong> vest / west in accents that distinguish them.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>How to practise a contrast</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Listen to a reliable pronunciation of each word.</li>
      <li>Identify the consonant that changes.</li>
      <li>Practise the two words separately.</li>
      <li>Say them in short phrases and sentences.</li>
      <li>Ask a listener whether the words sound distinct.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Practice passage</h3>
    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:12px;">
      <p>"The ship reached the shore before sunset. Chip carried a small bag to the light near the right-hand door."</p>
    </div>

    <p>Practise the words <em>ship</em>, <em>shore</em>, <em>Chip</em>, <em>light</em> and <em>right</em>. Keep the target consonants distinct.</p>
  ', 1),
  (2012, 2012, '
    <h2>Similar English Sounds</h2>

    <p>Some English sounds are difficult to distinguish because they are produced in similar ways. A minimal pair is a pair of words that differ in one sound and have different meanings.</p>

    <h3>Minimal-pair examples</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>ship / sheep</li>
      <li>bed / bad</li>
      <li>fan / van</li>
      <li>thin / tin</li>
      <li>rice / rise</li>
      <li>cap / cup</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Practice method</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Listen to the two words.</li>
      <li>Identify the sound that changes.</li>
      <li>Repeat each word carefully.</li>
      <li>Mix the words and ask a partner to identify which one you said.</li>
      <li>Use each word in a sentence to confirm its meaning.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Example sentences</h3>
    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:12px;">
      <p>"The <strong>ship</strong> left the harbour."</p>
      <p>"The sheep grazed near the fence."</p>
      <p>"Please put the <strong>cap</strong> on the bottle."</p>
      <p>"She filled the <strong>cup</strong> with water."</p>
    </div>

    <p>Do not rely on spelling alone. Listen to the sound and consider how it changes the meaning of the sentence.</p>
  ', 1),
  (2013, 2013, '
    <h2>Identifying Syllables</h2>

    <p>A syllable is a unit of spoken language organised around a vowel sound. Counting syllables means counting the vowel sounds heard in a word, not simply counting vowel letters.</p>

    <h3>Examples</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>book:</strong> 1 syllable.</li>
      <li><strong>teacher:</strong> 2 syllables — teach-er.</li>
      <li><strong>computer:</strong> 3 syllables — com-pu-ter.</li>
      <li><strong>education:</strong> commonly 4 syllables — ed-u-ca-tion.</li>
      <li><strong>information:</strong> commonly 4 syllables — in-for-ma-tion.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>How to count syllables</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Say the word naturally.</li>
      <li>Listen for each vowel sound or spoken beat.</li>
      <li>Say the word slowly if necessary.</li>
      <li>Check with a reliable pronunciation guide when the word is unfamiliar.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Practice sentences</h3>
    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:12px;">
      <p>"The teacher opened the computer laboratory."</p>
      <p>"Education provides opportunities for learning."</p>
    </div>

    <p>Practise the syllables in <em>teacher</em>, <em>computer</em>, <em>laboratory</em>, <em>education</em> and <em>opportunities</em>. Syllable divisions in writing are useful guides, but actual pronunciation is what determines the spoken syllable count.</p>
  ', 1),
  (2014, 2014, '
    <h2>Word Stress</h2>

    <p>Word stress is the emphasis placed on one syllable in a word. A stressed syllable is often more prominent because of a combination of loudness, length and pitch.</p>

    <h3>Examples of word stress</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>TA</strong>-ble</li>
      <li><strong>DOC</strong>-tor</li>
      <li>com-<strong>PU</strong>-ter</li>
      <li>edu-<strong>CA</strong>-tion</li>
      <li>in-for-<strong>MA</strong>-tion</li>
    </ul>

    <p>The bold syllable represents the main stress in these common pronunciations. Stress can vary in some words and accents, so check an appropriate dictionary when unsure.</p>

    <h3>Why stress matters</h3>
    <p>Incorrect stress can make speech harder to understand. It can also distinguish words or grammatical forms in English.</p>

    <h3>Practice passage</h3>
    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:12px;">
      <p>"The <strong>DOC</strong>tor explained the in-for-<strong>MA</strong>-tion about the com-<strong>PU</strong>-ter to the class."</p>
    </div>

    <p>Read the passage aloud. Make the stressed syllables prominent without shouting, and keep the other syllables clear.</p>
  ', 1),
  (2015, 2015, '
    <h2>Sentence Stress</h2>

    <p>Sentence stress is the emphasis placed on particular words in a sentence. Speakers often stress words that carry important information, such as nouns, main verbs, adjectives and adverbs. Stress can also shift to contrast one idea with another.</p>

    <h3>Example: Changing the emphasis</h3>
    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:12px;">
      <p>"I wanted the <strong>BLUE</strong> book."</p>
      <p>This can emphasise which colour of book was wanted.</p>

      <p>"<strong>I</strong> wanted the blue book."</p>
      <p>This can contrast who wanted the book.</p>

      <p>"I wanted the blue <strong>BOOK</strong>."</p>
      <p>This can contrast the book with another item.</p>
    </div>

    <p>The sentence can remain grammatically identical while the intended contrast changes with emphasis and context.</p>

    <h3>Practice dialogue</h3>
    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:12px;">
      <p><strong>Teacher:</strong> Did you say that Mary borrowed the red book?</p>
      <p><strong>Learner:</strong> No, Mary borrowed the <strong>BLUE</strong> book.</p>
      <p><strong>Teacher:</strong> So Mary borrowed the blue book, not the red one?</p>
      <p><strong>Learner:</strong> Yes, that is correct.</p>
    </div>

    <p>Practise stressing <em>blue</em> to correct the mistaken colour. Keep the sentence natural rather than making every word equally prominent.</p>
  ', 1),
  (2016, 2016, '
    <h2>Rhythm in Connected Speech</h2>

    <p>Speech rhythm is the pattern of prominence, timing and grouping in spoken language. In English, important information words often receive more stress, while many grammatical words are less prominent in ordinary connected speech.</p>

    <h3>Example</h3>
    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:12px;">
      <p><strong>Careful reading:</strong> "I am going to the market to buy some fruit."</p>
      <p><strong>Natural connected speech:</strong> "I''m going to the market to buy some fruit."</p>
    </div>

    <p>In ordinary speech, words may link together and some unstressed words may be shortened. The goal is to speak naturally and clearly, not to remove words so that the message becomes difficult to understand.</p>

    <h3>Practice dialogue</h3>
    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:12px;">
      <p><strong>Amina:</strong> What are you going to do after class?</p>
      <p><strong>Brian:</strong> I''m going to the library to finish my assignment.</p>
      <p><strong>Amina:</strong> Are you going to stay there for long?</p>
      <p><strong>Brian:</strong> Not too long. I''ll finish the last two questions and then go home.</p>
    </div>

    <p>Read the dialogue at a natural pace. Give prominence to key information such as <em>library</em>, <em>finish</em>, <em>assignment</em> and <em>last two questions</em>. Allow short grammatical words to connect smoothly where natural.</p>
  ', 1),
  (2017, 2017, '
    <h2>Intonation in Statements and Questions</h2>

    <p>Intonation is the movement of pitch during speech. It can help communicate whether a speaker is making a statement, asking a question, expressing surprise or signalling that more information is coming.</p>

    <h3>Common patterns</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Statements:</strong> Often use falling intonation when the speaker is completing a thought.</li>
      <li><strong>Yes/no questions:</strong> Often use rising intonation.</li>
      <li><strong>Wh-questions:</strong> Often use falling intonation in neutral contexts.</li>
      <li><strong>Lists:</strong> May use rising or continuing pitch before the final item, followed by a fall.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>These are common patterns, not absolute rules. Intonation varies with context, emphasis and accent.</p>

    <h3>Model dialogue</h3>
    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:12px;">
      <p><strong>Teacher:</strong> Have you completed the assignment? (A common rising pattern in a neutral yes/no question.)</p>
      <p><strong>Learner:</strong> Yes, I completed it yesterday. (A common falling pattern in a completed statement.)</p>
      <p><strong>Teacher:</strong> When did you submit it? (A common falling pattern in a neutral wh-question.)</p>
      <p><strong>Learner:</strong> I submitted it after lunch.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Practise aloud</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Say: "You are ready." as a statement.</li>
      <li>Say: "Are you ready?" as a yes/no question.</li>
      <li>Say: "When are you ready?" as a wh-question.</li>
      <li>Notice how pitch movement helps convey the intended function.</li>
    </ol>
  ', 1),
  (2018, 2018, '
    <h2>Language for Different Audiences</h2>

    <p>Speakers and writers adjust their language to suit their audience, purpose and situation. A message to a close friend may be informal, while a presentation to school leaders usually requires more formal language.</p>

    <h3>Example: Asking for permission</h3>

    <p><strong>Informal message to a friend:</strong></p>
    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:12px;">
      <p>"Hey, can you send me the notes when you get a chance? Thanks!"</p>
    </div>

    <p><strong>Formal request to a teacher:</strong></p>
    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:12px;">
      <p>"Good afternoon, Madam. Could you please share the notes from today''s lesson when convenient? I would like to review the material before the next class. Thank you for your assistance."</p>
    </div>

    <h3>What changes?</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Greeting:</strong> The formal version uses a respectful greeting.</li>
      <li><strong>Request:</strong> "Could you please..." is more formal than "can you..." in this context.</li>
      <li><strong>Explanation:</strong> The formal version briefly explains why the notes are needed.</li>
      <li><strong>Closing:</strong> It acknowledges the recipient''s assistance.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Formal language should remain clear and natural. Being formal does not mean using unnecessarily complicated words.</p>
  
<hr>

    <h2>Language for Different Audiences</h2>

    <p>The audience is the person or group receiving a message. Effective communication considers what the audience needs to know, what they already understand and what tone is appropriate.</p>

    <h3>Example: Announcing a school clean-up</h3>

    <p><strong>Message to fellow learners:</strong></p>
    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:12px;">
      <p>Hi everyone,</p>
      <p>Our class clean-up is this Friday after lessons. Please bring gloves if you have them, and meet outside the classroom at 3:30 p.m. Let''s work together to leave the compound clean.</p>
      <p>Thanks!</p>
    </div>

    <p><strong>Message to the head teacher:</strong></p>
    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:12px;">
      <p>Dear Head Teacher,</p>
      <p>Our class proposes to conduct a school clean-up exercise this Friday after lessons, beginning at 3:30 p.m. We request permission to organise the activity and would appreciate guidance on the areas that should be prioritised.</p>
      <p>Yours faithfully,</p>
      <p>The Class Representative</p>
    </div>

    <h3>What changes?</h3>
    <p>Both messages communicate the same activity, but the message to learners focuses on participation and practical instructions. The message to the head teacher makes a formal proposal and requests permission.</p>
  ', 1),
  (2019, 2019, '
    <h2>Formal Language</h2>

    <p>Formal language is used in situations that require respect, professionalism or official communication. It is common when communicating with school administrators, employers, public officials and people we do not know well.</p>

    <h3>Common features</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Respectful greetings and forms of address.</li>
      <li>Complete, clear sentences.</li>
      <li>Polite requests rather than commands.</li>
      <li>Limited use of slang and casual abbreviations.</li>
      <li>A tone suited to the relationship and purpose.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Example: Requesting a meeting with a principal</h3>

    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:12px;">
      <p><strong>Learner:</strong> Good morning, Madam Principal. May I speak with you about the school science club?</p>
      <p><strong>Principal:</strong> Good morning. Certainly. What would you like to discuss?</p>
      <p><strong>Learner:</strong> Our club would like to organise a science exhibition next month. Could we arrange a short meeting to discuss the proposal?</p>
      <p><strong>Principal:</strong> Please submit a brief proposal to my office, and we can arrange a meeting after reviewing it.</p>
      <p><strong>Learner:</strong> Thank you, Madam. I will prepare the proposal and submit it this week.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Compare the wording</h3>
    <p><strong>Too casual for this situation:</strong> "Hey, we want to do a science thing. Meet us."</p>
    <p><strong>Appropriate:</strong> "Good morning, Madam. May we arrange a meeting to discuss our science exhibition proposal?"</p>

    <p>Formal language should be respectful and direct. It does not require unnecessarily difficult vocabulary.</p>
  ', 1),
  (2020, 2020, '
    <h2>Informal Language</h2>

    <p>Informal language is common when communicating with friends, relatives and people with whom we have a relaxed relationship. It can include contractions, familiar expressions and a conversational tone.</p>

    <h3>Common features</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Friendly greetings.</li>
      <li>Everyday vocabulary.</li>
      <li>Contractions such as "I''m", "we''ll" and "can''t".</li>
      <li>Short, natural sentences.</li>
      <li>Expressions suited to the relationship.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Complete example: A message to a friend</h3>

    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:12px;">
      <p><strong>Subject: Revision on Saturday</strong></p>
      <p>Hi Brian,</p>
      <p>Are you free on Saturday morning? I want us to revise algebra before the test next week. We could meet at the library at 9 a.m. and work through the questions we found difficult.</p>
      <p>Let me know if that works for you. If you''re busy, we can choose another time.</p>
      <p>See you,</p>
      <p>Amina</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Why this works</h3>
    <p>The greeting is friendly, the request is clear and the closing suits the relationship. The message is informal without being confusing or careless.</p>

    <p>Informal language is not the same as incorrect English. The message should still be understandable and appropriate for its recipient.</p>
  ', 1),
  (2021, 2021, '
    <h2>Polite Requests</h2>

    <p>A polite request asks someone to do something while respecting their choice, time and circumstances.</p>

    <h3>Useful expressions</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>"Could you please...?"</li>
      <li>"Would you mind...?"</li>
      <li>"May I ask you to...?"</li>
      <li>"Would it be possible to...?"</li>
      <li>"When you have a moment, could you...?"</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Example: Requesting help from a teacher</h3>

    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:12px;">
      <p><strong>Learner:</strong> Good afternoon, Sir. Could you please explain the last step in the equation again?</p>
      <p><strong>Teacher:</strong> Certainly. Which part is unclear?</p>
      <p><strong>Learner:</strong> I understand how you expanded the brackets, but I am not sure how you collected the like terms.</p>
      <p><strong>Teacher:</strong> Let''s write the terms with x together and the constants separately.</p>
      <p><strong>Learner:</strong> Thank you. That makes the step clearer.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Choosing the right expression</h3>
    <p>"Could you please explain this step?" is a direct, polite request. "Explain this now!" is a command and may sound demanding, especially when the listener has not agreed to help.</p>

    <p>Politeness does not require excessive apologies. State what you need clearly and respectfully.</p>
  ', 1),
  (2022, 2022, '
    <h2>Responding to Requests</h2>

    <p>A response to a request should communicate clearly whether you can help, whether you need more information or whether you must decline.</p>

    <h3>Accepting a request</h3>
    <p>"Certainly. I can help you after lunch."</p>

    <h3>Asking for clarification</h3>
    <p>"Of course. Which section would you like me to check?"</p>

    <h3>Declining politely</h3>
    <p>"I''m sorry, but I cannot meet this afternoon because I have another commitment. Would tomorrow morning work instead?"</p>

    <h3>Complete example: A realistic exchange</h3>

    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:12px;">
      <p><strong>Grace:</strong> Peter, could you help me carry these science materials to the laboratory?</p>
      <p><strong>Peter:</strong> Certainly. Let me put my books away first, and then I''ll help you.</p>
      <p><strong>Grace:</strong> Thank you. We need to move them before the next lesson.</p>
      <p><strong>Peter:</strong> No problem. Should we carry the glass equipment separately?</p>
      <p><strong>Grace:</strong> Yes, please. The teacher asked us to handle it carefully.</p>
    </div>

    <p>A response may be positive, negative or conditional. The important thing is to make your answer understandable and respectful.</p>
  ', 1),
  (2023, 2023, '
    <h2>Making Offers</h2>

    <p>An offer expresses willingness to help, provide something or do an activity for another person. An effective offer is clear and suited to the situation.</p>

    <h3>Useful expressions</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>"Would you like me to help?"</li>
      <li>"Can I get you some water?"</li>
      <li>"Shall I carry that for you?"</li>
      <li>"I could help you prepare the materials if you wish."</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Complete example: Offering assistance</h3>

    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:12px;">
      <p><strong>Teacher:</strong> The class needs to arrange these books before the visitors arrive.</p>
      <p><strong>Mary:</strong> Would you like me to arrange the books by subject?</p>
      <p><strong>Teacher:</strong> Yes, please. That would be helpful.</p>
      <p><strong>Mary:</strong> I can also label the shelves after sorting them.</p>
      <p><strong>Teacher:</strong> Thank you. Please check the labels against the catalogue.</p>
      <p><strong>Mary:</strong> Certainly. I will check each label before placing the books on the shelves.</p>
    </div>

    <p>An offer should not assume that help is wanted. Give the other person an opportunity to accept or decline.</p>
  ', 1),
  (2024, 2024, '
    <h2>Responding to Offers</h2>

    <p>When someone offers help or something useful, respond in a way that clearly accepts, declines or explains what would be more helpful.</p>

    <h3>Accepting</h3>
    <p>"Yes, please. I would appreciate your help."</p>

    <h3>Declining politely</h3>
    <p>"Thank you for offering, but I can manage this myself."</p>

    <h3>Accepting part of an offer</h3>
    <p>"Thank you. I can carry the books, but could you help me with the heavier box?"</p>

    <h3>Complete example: At school</h3>

    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:12px;">
      <p><strong>Daniel:</strong> Would you like me to explain the first mathematics question?</p>
      <p><strong>Lucy:</strong> Yes, please. I understand the formula, but I am unsure how to substitute the values.</p>
      <p><strong>Daniel:</strong> Start by writing the known values beside the formula. Then substitute each value in the correct position.</p>
      <p><strong>Lucy:</strong> I see. Thank you for explaining that.</p>
      <p><strong>Daniel:</strong> You''re welcome. Would you like to try the next question yourself?</p>
      <p><strong>Lucy:</strong> Yes. I will try it first and ask if I get stuck.</p>
    </div>

    <p>A clear response helps the person offering assistance understand what is useful and whether further help is needed.</p>
  ', 1),
  (2025, 2025, '
    <h2>Invitations and Responses</h2>

    <p>An invitation asks someone to attend an event or take part in an activity. A good invitation identifies the event and provides the essential details, including the date, time and place.</p>

    <h3>Complete example: An invitation message</h3>

    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:12px;">
      <p>Dear Amina,</p>
      <p>I would like to invite you to our school''s science exhibition on Friday, 23 October 2026, from 10:00 a.m. to 1:00 p.m. It will be held in the school hall.</p>
      <p>Learners will present experiments and projects on renewable energy, water conservation and simple machines. I hope you will be able to attend.</p>
      <p>Please let me know if you can come.</p>
      <p>Best wishes,</p>
      <p>Brian</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Accepting the invitation</h3>
    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:12px;">
      <p>Dear Brian,</p>
      <p>Thank you for inviting me to the science exhibition. I would be delighted to attend. I look forward to seeing the projects.</p>
      <p>Best wishes,</p>
      <p>Amina</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Declining politely</h3>
    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:12px;">
      <p>Dear Brian,</p>
      <p>Thank you for the invitation. Unfortunately, I will not be able to attend because I have another commitment that day. I hope the exhibition goes well.</p>
      <p>Best wishes,</p>
      <p>Amina</p>
    </div>

    <p>An invitation response should make the decision clear. If declining, a brief explanation is often enough; there is no need to disclose private details.</p>
  ', 1),
  (2026, 2026, '
    <h2>Giving and Responding to Advice</h2>

    <p>Advice suggests an action that may help someone deal with a situation. Good advice is relevant, practical and respectful. It should not pretend that one solution works for every person.</p>

    <h3>Giving advice</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>"You could try..."</li>
      <li>"It might help to..."</li>
      <li>"Have you considered...?"</li>
      <li>"One option is to..."</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Responding to advice</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>"Thank you. I will try that."</li>
      <li>"That could work. How would you suggest I begin?"</li>
      <li>"I appreciate the suggestion, but that option may not be possible for me."</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Complete example: Discussing revision</h3>

    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:12px;">
      <p><strong>Peter:</strong> I keep running out of time when I revise mathematics. I start several questions but rarely finish them.</p>
      <p><strong>Mary:</strong> Have you tried choosing one topic and completing a small set of questions before moving to another?</p>
      <p><strong>Peter:</strong> Not really. I usually switch topics whenever a question becomes difficult.</p>
      <p><strong>Mary:</strong> You could mark the difficult step, try a simpler example and return to the original question afterwards.</p>
      <p><strong>Peter:</strong> That sounds useful. I will try it with algebra tonight and see whether I can finish the questions.</p>
      <p><strong>Mary:</strong> Good idea. You can adjust the approach if it does not suit you.</p>
    </div>

    <p>The advice addresses the stated problem and offers a practical action. Peter responds by choosing a specific way to test the suggestion.</p>
  ', 1),
  (2027, 2027, '
    <h2>Apologising Appropriately</h2>

    <p>An apology acknowledges an action or mistake that has affected another person. A meaningful apology identifies the problem, accepts appropriate responsibility and explains what will be done to repair the situation.</p>

    <h3>Parts of an effective apology</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>State the apology clearly.</li>
      <li>Acknowledge what happened.</li>
      <li>Accept responsibility without making excuses.</li>
      <li>Offer a reasonable way to correct the problem.</li>
      <li>Take steps to prevent the same mistake where possible.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Complete example: Apologising for missing a group meeting</h3>

    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:12px;">
      <p><strong>Kevin:</strong> Amina, I am sorry I missed our group meeting yesterday without informing you.</p>
      <p><strong>Amina:</strong> We waited because we needed your research notes to finish the report.</p>
      <p><strong>Kevin:</strong> I understand that my absence delayed the group. I should have informed you earlier. I have completed the notes now and can send them this morning.</p>
      <p><strong>Amina:</strong> Thank you. Please let us know in advance if you cannot attend another meeting.</p>
      <p><strong>Kevin:</strong> I will. I am sorry for causing the delay, and I will communicate earlier next time.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Weak apology versus effective apology</h3>
    <p><strong>Weak:</strong> "Sorry if you got upset. It was not my problem."</p>
    <p><strong>Effective:</strong> "I am sorry I submitted my section late. I know it delayed the group, and I should have informed you sooner. I will send my work earlier next time."</p>

    <p>An apology should be sincere. Do not promise something you cannot reasonably do, and do not use an apology to pressure another person into forgiving you immediately.</p>
  ', 1),
  (2028, 2028, '
    <h2>Polite Complaints</h2>

    <p>A complaint explains that something is wrong, unsatisfactory or unfair and asks for an appropriate response. A polite complaint identifies the problem clearly, provides relevant details and states what action would help.</p>

    <h3>Structure of a written complaint</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Address the person or organisation responsible.</li>
      <li>State the problem and when it occurred.</li>
      <li>Give specific facts rather than insults or unsupported accusations.</li>
      <li>Explain the effect of the problem.</li>
      <li>Request a reasonable solution and close respectfully.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Complete example: Formal complaint letter</h3>

    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:14px;">
      <p>Mary Wanjiku<br>
      P.O. Box 145<br>
      Thika</p>

      <p>12 October 2026</p>

      <p>The Customer Service Manager<br>
      Brightline Stationers<br>
      Thika</p>

      <p><strong>Dear Sir/Madam,</strong></p>

      <p><strong>RE: COMPLAINT ABOUT DAMAGED EXERCISE BOOKS</strong></p>

      <p>I am writing to complain about a pack of exercise books I purchased from your shop on 9 October 2026. When I opened the pack at home, I discovered that several books had torn covers and missing pages.</p>

      <p>I have kept the receipt and the damaged books for inspection. I would appreciate it if you could replace the defective books or advise me on the procedure for obtaining a refund.</p>

      <p>I look forward to your response and hope that the matter can be resolved promptly.</p>

      <p><strong>Yours faithfully,</strong></p>

      <p>Mary Wanjiku</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Why this complaint is effective</h3>
    <p>It identifies the purchase and the defect, gives the date, mentions evidence and requests a practical solution. It does not insult the shop or make threats.</p>
  ', 1),
  (2029, 2029, '
    <h2>Persuading with Reasons</h2>

    <p>Persuasion attempts to influence someone''s opinion or decision. Effective persuasion presents a clear position, supports it with relevant reasons or evidence and addresses reasonable concerns.</p>

    <h3>A useful structure</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>State your position.</li>
      <li>Give a reason.</li>
      <li>Support the reason with an example or evidence where available.</li>
      <li>Address a possible concern.</li>
      <li>End with a clear recommendation.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Complete example: Persuading the school to start a reading club</h3>

    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:14px;">
      <p><strong>Brian:</strong> I think our school should establish a weekly reading club.</p>

      <p><strong>Amina:</strong> Why do you think we need one when learners already have English lessons?</p>

      <p><strong>Brian:</strong> English lessons are important, but a reading club would give learners extra time to read books they choose themselves. Members could also discuss unfamiliar words and share their interpretations of stories.</p>

      <p><strong>Amina:</strong> Wouldn''t it require a lot of money to run?</p>

      <p><strong>Brian:</strong> Not necessarily. We could begin with books already in the school library and meet for thirty minutes each Friday. If the club becomes useful, we could later request additional books.</p>

      <p><strong>Amina:</strong> That sounds manageable. How would we know whether learners benefit?</p>

      <p><strong>Brian:</strong> We could record attendance, ask members for feedback and review whether they are reading more regularly. I suggest we run a one-term trial and evaluate the results.</p>

      <p><strong>Amina:</strong> I support trying it for one term. Let''s prepare a proposal for the English department.</p>
    </div>

    <p>Brian gives reasons, responds to concerns and proposes a limited trial. He does not claim that the club will solve every reading problem.</p>
  ', 1),
  (2030, 2030, '
    <h2>Negotiating Agreement</h2>

    <p>Negotiation is a discussion in which people with different preferences try to reach an acceptable decision. It involves explaining needs, listening, proposing alternatives and agreeing on practical terms.</p>

    <h3>Complete example: Planning a group presentation</h3>

    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:14px;">
      <p><strong>Kevin:</strong> I suggest that we meet on Saturday morning to finish the presentation.</p>

      <p><strong>Lucy:</strong> Saturday morning is difficult for me because I help at home. Could we meet in the afternoon instead?</p>

      <p><strong>Kevin:</strong> I can meet in the afternoon, but I need to leave by four o''clock.</p>

      <p><strong>Brian:</strong> What if we meet at two and finish by half past three?</p>

      <p><strong>Lucy:</strong> That works for me. I can bring the research notes.</p>

      <p><strong>Kevin:</strong> I will prepare the slides before we meet.</p>

      <p><strong>Brian:</strong> I will check the references and organise the final order.</p>

      <p><strong>Lucy:</strong> Agreed. We will meet in the library at two o''clock on Saturday and aim to finish by half past three.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>What makes the negotiation successful?</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Each person explains a real constraint.</li>
      <li>The group considers an alternative time.</li>
      <li>They divide the work clearly.</li>
      <li>They confirm the final agreement.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Negotiation does not require everyone to get their first choice. The aim is to find an arrangement that participants can reasonably accept.</p>
  ', 1),
  (2031, 2031, '
    <h2>Clarification</h2>

    <p>Clarification is used when a message, instruction or explanation is unclear. Instead of guessing, ask a focused question that identifies exactly what you need to understand.</p>

    <h3>Useful expressions</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>"Could you explain what you mean by...?"</li>
      <li>"Do you mean that we should...?"</li>
      <li>"Could you give an example?"</li>
      <li>"Which section are you referring to?"</li>
      <li>"Could you repeat the last instruction, please?"</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Complete example: Clarifying an assignment</h3>

    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:14px;">
      <p><strong>Teacher:</strong> For homework, compare the two stories and discuss how the main characters change.</p>

      <p><strong>James:</strong> Excuse me, Madam. When you say "compare", should we explain both similarities and differences?</p>

      <p><strong>Teacher:</strong> Yes. Identify at least one similarity and two differences, then explain how each main character changes.</p>

      <p><strong>James:</strong> Should we write this as continuous prose or use a table?</p>

      <p><strong>Teacher:</strong> Write it in paragraphs. You may use a short planning table before you begin.</p>

      <p><strong>James:</strong> Thank you. So the final answer should be paragraphs that compare the stories and explain the character changes.</p>

      <p><strong>Teacher:</strong> Exactly.</p>
    </div>

    <p>James asks focused questions and restates the instruction to confirm his understanding. This is more reliable than silently guessing what the teacher expects.</p>
  ', 1),
  (2032, 2032, '
    <h2>Correcting Misunderstandings</h2>

    <p>A misunderstanding occurs when a person interprets a message differently from what the speaker intended. Correcting it requires identifying the incorrect interpretation and explaining the intended meaning calmly.</p>

    <h3>Useful expressions</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>"I think there has been a misunderstanding."</li>
      <li>"What I meant was..."</li>
      <li>"Let me explain that more clearly."</li>
      <li>"I did not mean that you should... I meant that..."</li>
      <li>"Thank you for checking. Let me clarify."</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Complete example: A misunderstood group message</h3>

    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:14px;">
      <p><strong>Message from Njeri:</strong> Please submit your sections before Friday so I can combine them over the weekend.</p>

      <p><strong>David:</strong> I thought you meant that the whole report had to be submitted to the teacher before Friday.</p>

      <p><strong>Njeri:</strong> I think there has been a misunderstanding. I need each person''s section by Friday, but the complete report is due next Monday.</p>

      <p><strong>David:</strong> Thank you for clarifying. So I should send my section to you by Friday, not submit the final report to the teacher yet.</p>

      <p><strong>Njeri:</strong> Exactly. Please include your references with your section.</p>

      <p><strong>David:</strong> Understood. I will send both the section and its references by Friday afternoon.</p>
    </div>

    <p>The correction distinguishes the two deadlines and confirms the action David must take. Neither person needs to blame the other to resolve the confusion.</p>
  ', 1),
  (2033, 2033, '
    <h2>Navigating Digital Texts</h2>

    <p>Digital texts include websites, online articles, electronic documents and learning platforms. Unlike a printed page, a digital text may contain links, menus, search tools and expandable sections that help readers find information.</p>

    <h3>Common navigation features</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Menu:</strong> provides access to different sections.</li>
      <li><strong>Search box:</strong> helps locate a word, topic or page.</li>
      <li><strong>Hyperlink:</strong> opens another page, document or resource.</li>
      <li><strong>Breadcrumbs:</strong> show where a page sits within a website.</li>
      <li><strong>Table of contents:</strong> lists sections and may link directly to them.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Complete example: Finding a school revision resource</h3>

    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:14px;">
      <p><strong>Website header:</strong> School Learning Centre</p>
      <p><strong>Menu:</strong> Home | Subjects | Revision Resources | Contact</p>
      <p><strong>Search box:</strong> Search for a topic...</p>
      <p><strong>Breadcrumbs:</strong> Home &gt; Subjects &gt; English &gt; Grammar</p>
      <p><strong>Page heading:</strong> Subject–Verb Agreement</p>
      <p><strong>Page text:</strong> Learn how verbs agree with singular and plural subjects.</p>
      <p><strong>Related link:</strong> Practise Subject–Verb Agreement</p>
    </div>

    <p><strong>Reader''s task:</strong> You want to practise subject–verb agreement. You can follow the breadcrumb trail to understand where you are, then select the related practice link. If you cannot locate the topic, use the search box.</p>

    <p>Before opening a link, consider its label and destination. Avoid entering personal information into an unfamiliar website just because it appears in a search result.</p>
  ', 1),
  (2034, 2034, '
    <h2>Links, Headings and Layout</h2>

    <p>Headings organise information into sections. Hyperlinks connect a text to another page or resource. Layout describes how text, headings, images and other elements are arranged.</p>

    <h3>How these features help readers</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Main heading:</strong> identifies the overall subject.</li>
      <li><strong>Subheading:</strong> identifies a smaller section within the subject.</li>
      <li><strong>Numbered list:</strong> presents items or steps in a sequence.</li>
      <li><strong>Bullet list:</strong> groups related points without implying a required order.</li>
      <li><strong>Descriptive hyperlink:</strong> tells readers what they can expect to open.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Complete example: An online guide</h3>

    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:14px;">
      <h3>How to Prepare for a Mathematics Test</h3>

      <p><strong>1. Plan your revision</strong></p>
      <p>List the topics covered in class and identify the ones you find difficult.</p>

      <p><strong>2. Practise questions</strong></p>
      <p>Complete questions without looking at the worked solution first.</p>

      <p><strong>3. Check your mistakes</strong></p>
      <p>Review each incorrect answer and identify the step that caused the error.</p>

      <p><strong>Related resource:</strong> <u>Practise algebra equations</u></p>
    </div>

    <p>The main heading announces the purpose of the guide. The numbered subheadings organise the process in order. The descriptive link tells readers what resource it opens.</p>

    <p>A well-organised layout makes information easier to scan, but attractive formatting alone does not prove that the information is accurate.</p>
  ', 1),
  (2035, 2035, '
    <h2>Interpreting Charts and Graphs</h2>

    <p>Charts and graphs present information visually. To interpret them accurately, read the title, labels, units, key or legend, and the values shown before drawing a conclusion.</p>

    <h3>What to check</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Title:</strong> identifies what the chart represents.</li>
      <li><strong>Axes:</strong> show the categories or quantities being compared.</li>
      <li><strong>Scale:</strong> shows the intervals used for measurement.</li>
      <li><strong>Legend:</strong> explains the meaning of different colours or symbols where present.</li>
      <li><strong>Data:</strong> supplies the values that support the conclusion.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Complete example: A class reading survey</h3>

    <p>The English teacher asks 20 learners how many books they read during the previous month. The results are summarised below.</p>

    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:14px;">
      <p><strong>Books read by 20 learners in one month</strong></p>
      <table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;">
        <tr>
          <th style="text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #999;padding:6px;">Books read</th>
          <th style="text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #999;padding:6px;">Number of learners</th>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td style="padding:6px;">0–1</td>
          <td style="padding:6px;">4</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td style="padding:6px;">2–3</td>
          <td style="padding:6px;">9</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td style="padding:6px;">4–5</td>
          <td style="padding:6px;">5</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td style="padding:6px;">6 or more</td>
          <td style="padding:6px;">2</td>
        </tr>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h3>Worked interpretation</h3>
    <p><strong>Question:</strong> Which group was the largest?</p>
    <ol>
      <li>Compare the frequencies: 4, 9, 5 and 2.</li>
      <li>The largest frequency is 9.</li>
      <li>Therefore, the largest group was learners who read 2–3 books.</li>
    </ol>

    <p><strong>Question:</strong> How many learners were surveyed?</p>
    <p>Add the frequencies: 4 + 9 + 5 + 2 = 20 learners.</p>

    <p>Do not claim that the survey represents every learner in the school. It describes only the 20 learners surveyed unless further evidence supports a wider conclusion.</p>
  ', 1),
  (2036, 2036, '
    <h2>Interpreting Tables and Diagrams</h2>

    <p>Tables organise information into rows and columns. Diagrams show relationships, structures or processes visually. Readers should identify labels, follow the correct row or branch and distinguish what is shown from what is merely assumed.</p>

    <h3>Complete example: A school club timetable</h3>

    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:14px;">
      <p><strong>After-school club timetable</strong></p>
      <table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;">
        <tr>
          <th style="text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #999;padding:6px;">Day</th>
          <th style="text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #999;padding:6px;">Club</th>
          <th style="text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #999;padding:6px;">Venue</th>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td style="padding:6px;">Monday</td>
          <td style="padding:6px;">Science</td>
          <td style="padding:6px;">Laboratory</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td style="padding:6px;">Tuesday</td>
          <td style="padding:6px;">Debate</td>
          <td style="padding:6px;">Room 4</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td style="padding:6px;">Wednesday</td>
          <td style="padding:6px;">Reading</td>
          <td style="padding:6px;">Library</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td style="padding:6px;">Thursday</td>
          <td style="padding:6px;">Environment</td>
          <td style="padding:6px;">School garden</td>
        </tr>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h3>Worked interpretation</h3>
    <p><strong>Question:</strong> Where does the debate club meet?</p>
    <ol>
      <li>Find the row labelled Tuesday.</li>
      <li>Read across to the Club column and locate Debate.</li>
      <li>Read across to the Venue column: Room 4.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Example of a process diagram</h3>
    <p><strong>Planning a class presentation:</strong></p>
    <p>Choose a topic → Gather information → Organise ideas → Prepare slides → Present to the class</p>

    <p>The arrows indicate the intended sequence. If a diagram has labelled branches rather than arrows in a sequence, follow the labels instead of assuming every branch represents a chronological step.</p>
  ', 1),
  (2037, 2037, '
    <h2>Images and Captions</h2>

    <p>Images can communicate information, attract attention or influence how readers interpret a story. A caption explains or adds context to an image. Readers should distinguish what an image visibly shows from what its caption claims about it.</p>

    <h3>Complete example: A school news report</h3>

    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:14px;">
      <p><strong>Headline:</strong> Learners Begin a School Tree-Planting Project</p>

      <p><strong>Image description:</strong> A photograph shows four learners placing a young tree into a prepared hole in the school compound. A teacher stands nearby holding a watering can.</p>

      <p><strong>Caption:</strong> Learners plant a young tree during the school''s environmental activity on 8 October 2026.</p>

      <p><strong>Report text:</strong> The environmental club began a tree-planting activity on Thursday. According to the club patron, members plan to care for the seedlings throughout the term. The school has not yet published a final count of the trees planted.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Interpreting the evidence</h3>
    <p>The photograph, as described, shows learners planting a tree and a teacher nearby. The caption supplies a date and context. The report says the club plans to care for the seedlings, but the image alone cannot prove that every seedling will survive or that the activity has already planted a particular total number of trees.</p>

    <h3>Questions to ask</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>What can I directly observe in the image?</li>
      <li>What extra information does the caption provide?</li>
      <li>Does the caption identify the date, location or people reliably?</li>
      <li>Does the written claim go beyond what the image can establish?</li>
    </ul>
  ', 1),
  (2038, 2038, '
    <h2>Identifying Media Purpose and Audience</h2>

    <p>Media purpose is the reason a message is created. Common purposes include informing, persuading, entertaining, educating and warning. The audience is the group the message is intended to reach.</p>

    <h3>How to identify purpose and audience</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Read the headline or title.</li>
      <li>Identify what the message asks readers to know, believe or do.</li>
      <li>Examine the vocabulary, tone and supporting information.</li>
      <li>Consider who would benefit most from receiving the message.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Complete example: Two notices about water conservation</h3>

    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:14px;">
      <p><strong>Notice A: School Water Conservation Week</strong></p>
      <p>Our school will hold Water Conservation Week from 19 to 23 October. Learners will take part in discussions about responsible water use, and each class will inspect taps for leaks. All learners are encouraged to participate.</p>

      <p><strong>Notice B: Buy the AquaSave Water Bottle</strong></p>
      <p>Make every school day easier with the new AquaSave bottle. Its reusable design helps you carry water wherever you go. Visit our shop today to explore available sizes.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Worked interpretation</h3>
    <p><strong>Notice A:</strong> Its main purpose is to inform learners about a school activity and encourage participation. Its audience is the school community.</p>
    <p><strong>Notice B:</strong> Its main purpose is to advertise a product and persuade potential customers to consider buying it. Its audience includes people who might need a reusable bottle.</p>

    <p>A message can have more than one purpose. Identify the main purpose using the evidence in the message rather than assuming that every notice is neutral or every advertisement is dishonest.</p>
  ', 1),
  (2039, 2039, '
    <h2>Assessing Source Credibility</h2>

    <p>Source credibility is the degree to which a source can reasonably be trusted for a particular claim. A professional-looking website or confident writer is not automatically reliable.</p>

    <h3>What to check</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Author:</strong> Is the author identified, and do they have relevant expertise?</li>
      <li><strong>Evidence:</strong> Are claims supported by verifiable information?</li>
      <li><strong>Date:</strong> Is the information current enough for the topic?</li>
      <li><strong>Purpose:</strong> Is the source informing, selling, persuading or entertaining?</li>
      <li><strong>Corroboration:</strong> Do independent, reliable sources support the claim?</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Complete example: Checking an online health claim</h3>

    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:14px;">
      <p><strong>Website A headline:</strong> "One cup of this drink guarantees perfect health!"</p>
      <p><strong>Website A article:</strong> The article has no named author, gives no supporting studies and asks readers to purchase the drink immediately.</p>

      <p><strong>Website B headline:</strong> "Understanding the evidence about hydration"</p>
      <p><strong>Website B article:</strong> The article identifies its author, provides links to relevant public-health guidance, gives a publication date and explains the limits of the evidence.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Worked judgement</h3>
    <p>Website B provides stronger reasons for confidence because readers can identify the author, inspect the evidence and check the date. Website A''s absolute promise, lack of evidence and sales pressure are warning signs.</p>

    <p>This does not mean Website B must be correct about every detail. Its claims still need to be checked against the evidence it cites.</p>
  ', 1),
  (2040, 2040, '
    <h2>Comparing Information Sources</h2>

    <p>Comparing sources means examining how different sources report the same subject. Compare their claims, evidence, dates, methods and purposes before deciding what the information supports.</p>

    <h3>Complete example: Comparing two reports about a school garden</h3>

    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:14px;">
      <p><strong>Source A — School newsletter, 8 October</strong></p>
      <p>"Our school garden produced 120 kilograms of vegetables this term," reports the agriculture club. The newsletter states that the club recorded each harvest in a logbook.</p>

      <p><strong>Source B — Community newspaper, 10 October</strong></p>
      <p>"The school garden has increased vegetable production," reports a community journalist. The article quotes the club patron but does not provide a total harvest figure.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Worked comparison</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Agreement:</strong> Both sources report that the school garden produced vegetables.</li>
      <li><strong>Specificity:</strong> Source A provides a numerical total; Source B gives a general description.</li>
      <li><strong>Evidence to check:</strong> The harvest logbook could help verify Source A''s figure.</li>
      <li><strong>Limitation:</strong> Neither source, as presented here, provides enough information to calculate how much production increased compared with an earlier term.</li>
    </ul>

    <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> The sources agree that the garden produced vegetables, but only Source A reports a total. To verify the figure or measure growth, consult the harvest records and comparable data from an earlier period.</p>
  ', 1),
  (2041, 2041, '
    <h2>Recognising Misleading Claims</h2>

    <p>A misleading claim can cause readers to form an inaccurate impression. It may use exaggerated language, omit important context, present a correlation as causation or generalise from limited evidence.</p>

    <h3>Warning signs</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Absolute words such as "always", "never" or "guaranteed" without adequate evidence.</li>
      <li>Statistics presented without a sample size, comparison or time period.</li>
      <li>Headlines that promise more than the article demonstrates.</li>
      <li>Claims that treat one person''s experience as proof of a universal rule.</li>
      <li>Missing context that would change how the result is understood.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Complete example: A misleading school survey headline</h3>

    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:14px;">
      <p><strong>Headline:</strong> "Every learner prefers digital notes!"</p>

      <p><strong>Article:</strong> A school club asked 12 learners from one class which revision format they preferred. Eight selected digital notes, three selected printed notes and one had no preference. The club published the results on its noticeboard.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Worked analysis</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>The survey asked 12 learners, not every learner.</li>
      <li>Eight of the 12 selected digital notes; four did not select that option.</li>
      <li>The sample came from one class, so it may not represent the entire school.</li>
      <li>The headline exaggerates the evidence.</li>
    </ol>

    <p><strong>A more accurate headline:</strong> "Most learners surveyed in one class preferred digital notes."</p>

    <p>The revised headline preserves the finding while stating its scope more carefully.</p>
  ', 1),
  (2042, 2042, '
    <h2>Evidence vs Unsupported Claims</h2>

    <p>Evidence is information used to support or assess a claim. It may include reliable records, direct observations, measurements, documents or carefully conducted research. An unsupported claim is a statement for which adequate supporting evidence has not been provided.</p>

    <h3>Complete example: A school reading programme</h3>

    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:14px;">
      <p><strong>Claim A:</strong> "The reading programme improved learners'' reading scores by 10%."</p>

      <p><strong>Evidence supplied:</strong> The teacher provides anonymised records showing the class''s average score before and after the programme, explains how the scores were calculated and states the dates of both assessments.</p>

      <p><strong>Claim B:</strong> "The reading programme made every learner love reading."</p>

      <p><strong>Evidence supplied:</strong> No survey, interview or other evidence is provided to support this statement.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Worked evaluation</h3>
    <p>Claim A has relevant evidence that can be examined. To evaluate it properly, check whether the assessments are comparable and whether the percentage change has been calculated correctly. Even then, improvement after the programme does not by itself prove that the programme caused all of the improvement.</p>

    <p>Claim B is unsupported as presented. A score increase cannot establish that every learner enjoyed reading. That claim would require suitable evidence about learners'' attitudes, and the word "every" would require particularly strong support.</p>

    <p><strong>Remember:</strong> A claim may be true but still be unsupported by the evidence provided. Evaluate the support, not just whether the statement sounds believable.</p>
  ', 1),
  (2043, 2043, '
    <h2>Respectful Online Communication</h2>

    <p>Respectful online communication expresses ideas clearly without personal attacks, harassment or unnecessary humiliation. Disagreement is allowed; insulting people is not necessary to explain why a claim is wrong.</p>

    <h3>Useful practices</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Address the idea rather than attacking the person.</li>
      <li>Use clear wording that reduces the risk of misunderstanding.</li>
      <li>Ask for clarification before assuming hostile intent.</li>
      <li>Disagree with reasons and relevant evidence.</li>
      <li>Do not share private messages or personal details without appropriate permission.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Complete example: A class discussion group</h3>

    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:14px;">
      <p><strong>Brian:</strong> I think our group should use printed notes because not everyone has reliable internet access.</p>

      <p><strong>Faith:</strong> That is a reasonable concern. Digital notes are easier to update, though. Could we keep a digital copy and print it for members who need one?</p>

      <p><strong>Brian:</strong> That could work. We should also make sure the digital file can be opened offline.</p>

      <p><strong>Faith:</strong> Agreed. I''ll prepare the shared file, and we can print copies for anyone who prefers them.</p>

      <p><strong>Brian:</strong> Thanks. That gives us both options without leaving anyone out.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Compare with a disrespectful response</h3>
    <p>"That is a stupid idea. You never think."</p>

    <p>The second response attacks the person and gives no useful reason. The first exchange acknowledges a concern, offers an alternative and works towards a solution.</p>
  ', 1),
  (2044, 2044, '
    <h2>Responsible Information Sharing</h2>

    <p>Responsible information sharing means checking accuracy, considering privacy and understanding the possible consequences before forwarding or publishing information.</p>

    <h3>Before sharing, check</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Accuracy:</strong> Can the claim be verified from a reliable source?</li>
      <li><strong>Context:</strong> Has the message been edited or taken out of context?</li>
      <li><strong>Privacy:</strong> Does it reveal someone''s personal information?</li>
      <li><strong>Permission:</strong> Is it appropriate to share the photo, message or document?</li>
      <li><strong>Impact:</strong> Could sharing it cause avoidable harm or spread a false rumour?</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Complete example: A message about a school closure</h3>

    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:14px;">
      <p><strong>Forwarded message:</strong> "URGENT! School is closed tomorrow. Forward this to every learner immediately!"</p>

      <p><strong>David:</strong> I have received a message saying school is closed tomorrow. Has the school confirmed this?</p>

      <p><strong>Mary:</strong> I haven''t seen an official announcement. Let''s check the school''s verified communication channel before forwarding it.</p>

      <p><strong>David:</strong> I have checked the school noticeboard online, and there is no closure notice. I will not forward the message as a fact until it is confirmed.</p>

      <p><strong>Mary:</strong> Good idea. If we need to know urgently, we can contact the class teacher.</p>
    </div>

    <p>The learners avoid spreading an unverified claim and identify appropriate ways to check it.</p>

    <p>When sharing photos or personal messages, consider whether the people involved have agreed to the sharing and whether the information is appropriate for the intended audience.</p>
  ', 1),
  (2045, 2045, '
    <h2>Checking a Claim Across Sources</h2>

    <p>When an important claim appears online, one source may not provide enough information to establish whether it is reliable. Cross-checking means looking for independent, relevant sources and examining the evidence each one provides.</p>

    <h3>Complete example: Checking a reported examination date</h3>

    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:14px;">
      <p><strong>Post A — Unverified social media account:</strong></p>
      <p>"The national examination has been moved to 2 November. Share this immediately!"</p>

      <p><strong>Post B — School group message:</strong></p>
      <p>"Someone has said the examination date changed, but I have not seen an official notice."</p>

      <p><strong>Source C — Official examination authority notice:</strong></p>
      <p>The notice lists the examination timetable and states the applicable dates. It identifies the issuing authority and publication date.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Worked decision</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Post A makes a specific claim but gives no verifiable evidence.</li>
      <li>Post B acknowledges uncertainty; it does not confirm the change.</li>
      <li>Source C is directly relevant because it is an official notice from the responsible authority.</li>
      <li>Check the notice''s date, scope and authenticity before relying on it.</li>
    </ol>

    <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> Do not treat the examination date as changed based only on Post A. Consult and verify the official timetable. If the official notice does not confirm a change, the social media claim remains unverified.</p>
  ', 1),
  (2046, 2046, '
    <h2>Responsible Use of Online Images</h2>

    <p>Images found online may be protected by copyright, contain private information or be presented with a misleading caption. Before reusing an image, check its source, permission or licence, accuracy and context.</p>

    <h3>Complete example: Preparing a school presentation</h3>

    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:14px;">
      <p><strong>Learner''s draft slide:</strong></p>
      <p><strong>Title:</strong> Effects of Plastic Pollution</p>
      <p><strong>Image:</strong> A photograph of a beach covered with plastic waste, found on a photography website.</p>
      <p><strong>Proposed caption:</strong> "This photograph proves that every beach in Kenya is covered with plastic waste."</p>

      <p><strong>Teacher''s feedback:</strong> The photograph can illustrate plastic pollution at the location shown, but it cannot prove that every beach has the same condition. Check the photographer''s licence or obtain permission before reusing it. Write a caption that describes what the image actually shows and provide the source as required.</p>

      <p><strong>Revised caption:</strong> "Plastic waste on a beach, illustrating one way discarded materials can pollute coastal environments."</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Why the revision is better</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>It avoids generalising from one photograph to every beach.</li>
      <li>It describes the image without inventing a location or date.</li>
      <li>It reminds the learner to check reuse rights.</li>
      <li>It avoids presenting an illustrative image as proof of a national statistic.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Giving credit is important, but attribution alone does not automatically grant permission to use copyrighted material. Follow the licence terms or obtain permission where needed.</p>
  ', 1),
  (2047, 2047, '
    <h2>Writing a Responsible Correction</h2>

    <p>When you discover that you shared inaccurate information, correct it clearly and promptly. A good correction identifies the earlier claim, explains what was wrong, supplies the verified information and avoids introducing another unsupported claim.</p>

    <h3>Complete example: Correcting a class group message</h3>

    <div style="border:1px solid #999;padding:14px;">
      <p><strong>Original message:</strong></p>
      <p>"The school trip is on Friday, 16 October. Everyone should arrive at 7 a.m."</p>

      <p><strong>Correction posted later:</strong></p>
      <p>Correction: I shared the wrong date for the school trip earlier. The official notice from the school states that the trip will take place on Saturday, 17 October, with learners expected to assemble at 7:00 a.m. Please use the date in the official notice rather than my earlier message. I am sorry for the confusion.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>What makes the correction responsible?</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>It clearly labels itself as a correction.</li>
      <li>It acknowledges the earlier error.</li>
      <li>It gives the updated information and identifies the official source.</li>
      <li>It asks readers to rely on the corrected information.</li>
      <li>It apologises without blaming other people.</li>
    </ol>

    <p>If the correct information has not yet been verified, do not invent a replacement date. State that the earlier claim is unconfirmed and explain where readers can check for an official update.</p>
  ', 1),
  (2048, 2048, '<h2>Data vs Information</h2>
<p><b>Data</b> refers to raw, unprocessed facts, while <b>Information</b> is processed data that is meaningful and useful.</p>
<h3>1. Data</h3>
<ul>
<li>Unorganized facts</li>
<li>No meaning on its own</li>
<li>Can be numbers, text, symbols</li>
</ul>
<div class="example-box">
75, 82, 90 → just numbers (data)
</div>
<h3>2. Information</h3>
<ul>
<li>Processed and organized data</li>
<li>Has meaning</li>
<li>Used for decision making</li>
</ul>
<div class="example-box">
Average score = 82% → meaningful (information)
</div>
<h3>3. Differences</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Data</th><th>Information</th></tr>
<tr><td>Meaning</td><td>Raw</td><td>Processed</td></tr>
<tr><td>Use</td><td>Input</td><td>Decision making</td></tr>
</table>
', 1),
  (2049, 2049, '<h2>Characteristics of Computers</h2>
<p>Computers have unique features that make them powerful tools.</p>
<h3>Key Characteristics</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Speed:</b> Performs millions of operations per second</li>
<li><b>Accuracy:</b> Produces correct results if input is correct</li>
<li><b>Diligence:</b> Does not get tired</li>
<li><b>Storage:</b> Can store large amounts of data</li>
<li><b>Automation:</b> Works automatically once programmed</li>
<li><b>Versatility:</b> Can perform many tasks</li>
</ul>
<div class="example-box">
Computer calculates results faster than humans → speed + accuracy
</div>
<div class="keyfact">
 Computers are powerful because they combine speed, accuracy, and storage.
</div>
', 1),
  (2050, 2050, '<h2>Types of Computers</h2>
<h3> DEEP EXPLANATION</h3>
<p>
Computers are classified based on size, power, and purpose. Each type is designed for specific tasks ranging from simple personal use to highly complex scientific calculations.
</p>
<h3> CLASSIFICATION BY SIZE & POWER</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Supercomputers</b></li>
<ul>
<li>Most powerful computers</li>
<li>Used in weather forecasting, space research, simulations</li>
<li>Example tasks: climate modeling, nuclear research</li>
</ul>
<li><b>Mainframe Computers</b></li>
<ul>
<li>Handle large-scale processing</li>
<li>Used by banks, governments, large organizations</li>
<li>Support many users at once</li>
</ul>
<li><b>Minicomputers</b></li>
<ul>
<li>Mid-sized systems (less common today)</li>
<li>Used in small organizations</li>
</ul>
<li><b>Microcomputers (Personal Computers)</b></li>
<ul>
<li>Most common type</li>
<li>Examples: desktops, laptops, tablets</li>
<li>Used for everyday tasks</li>
</ul>
<li><b>Embedded Computers</b></li>
<ul>
<li>Built into devices</li>
<li>Examples: washing machines, cars, microwaves</li>
<li>Perform specific functions</li>
</ul>
</ul>
<h3> COMPARISON TABLE</h3>
<pre>
Type           | Power     | Users      | Example Use
------------------------------------------------------
Supercomputer  | Very High | Few        | Weather prediction
Mainframe      | High      | Many       | Banking systems
Microcomputer  | Moderate  | One        | Personal use
Embedded       | Low       | Single use | Appliances
</pre>
<h3> EXAM FOCUS</h3>
<ul>
<li>Differences between supercomputer and mainframe</li>
<li>Examples of embedded systems</li>
<li>Classification based on purpose</li>
</ul>
', 1),
  (2051, 2051, '<h2>Computer Generations (1st → 5th)</h2>
<h3> DEEP EXPLANATION</h3>
<p>
Computer generations refer to the evolution of computer technology over time.
Each generation is defined by major technological advancements.
</p>
<h3> GENERATIONS OVERVIEW</h3>
<h4>1⃣ First Generation (1940s–1950s)</h4>
<ul>
<li>Used vacuum tubes</li>
<li>Very large and expensive</li>
<li>Generated a lot of heat</li>
<li>Example: ENIAC</li>
</ul>
<h4>2⃣ Second Generation (1950s–1960s)</h4>
<ul>
<li>Used transistors</li>
<li>Smaller, faster, more reliable</li>
<li>Less heat produced</li>
</ul>
<h4>3⃣ Third Generation (1960s–1970s)</h4>
<ul>
<li>Used integrated circuits (ICs)</li>
<li>Even smaller and faster</li>
<li>Introduction of operating systems</li>
</ul>
<h4>4⃣ Fourth Generation (1970s–present)</h4>
<ul>
<li>Used microprocessors</li>
<li>Rise of personal computers</li>
<li>Very high speed and efficiency</li>
</ul>
<h4>5⃣ Fifth Generation (present & future)</h4>
<ul>
<li>Artificial Intelligence (AI)</li>
<li>Machine learning and automation</li>
<li>Focus on smart systems</li>
</ul>
<h3> SUMMARY TABLE</h3>
<pre>
Generation | Technology        | Key Feature
------------------------------------------------
1st        | Vacuum tubes      | Large, hot
2nd        | Transistors       | Smaller, faster
3rd        | ICs               | Reliable
4th        | Microprocessors   | Personal computers
5th        | AI                | Intelligent systems
</pre>
<h3> EXAM FOCUS</h3>
<ul>
<li>Key technology in each generation</li>
<li>Differences between generations</li>
<li>Examples (ENIAC, modern AI systems)</li>
</ul>
', 1),
  (2052, 2052, '<h2>Number Systems</h2>
<p>
  Computers use different number systems to represent and process data efficiently.
  These include decimal, binary, and hexadecimal systems, which can be converted into one another.
</p>
<h3> 1. Decimal (Base 10)</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Uses digits 0–9</li>
  <li>Used in everyday human calculations</li>
  <li>Each digit has a place value based on powers of 10</li>
</ul>
<h3> 2. Binary (Base 2)</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Uses only 0 and 1</li>
  <li>Each digit is called a bit</li>
  <li>Used internally by computers</li>
</ul>
<h3> 3. Hexadecimal (Base 16)</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Uses digits 0–9 and letters A–F</li>
  <li>A = 10, B = 11, C = 12, D = 13, E = 14, F = 15</li>
  <li>Used to simplify long binary numbers</li>
</ul>
<h3> Why Binary is Used</h3>
<ul>
  <li><b>1 = ON</b> (electric signal present)</li>
  <li><b>0 = OFF</b> (no signal)</li>
</ul>
<pre>
Computers use binary because they only understand two states:
ON  → 1
OFF → 0
</pre>
<h3> Number System Conversions</h3>
<h4> Decimal to Binary</h4>
<pre>
Example: 10₁₀ → Binary
10 ÷ 2 = 5 remainder 0
5 ÷ 2  = 2 remainder 1
2 ÷ 2  = 1 remainder 0
1 ÷ 2  = 0 remainder 1
Read from bottom to top:
10₁₀ = 1010₂
</pre>
<h4> Binary to Decimal</h4>
<pre>
Example: 1010₂ → Decimal
(1×2³) + (0×2²) + (1×2¹) + (0×2⁰)
= 8 + 0 + 2 + 0
= 10₁₀
</pre>
<h4> Binary to Hexadecimal</h4>
<pre>
Example: 1010₂ → Hexadecimal
Group into 4 bits:
1010 = A
So, 1010₂ = A₁₆
</pre>
<h4> Hexadecimal to Binary</h4>
<pre>
Example: A₁₆ → Binary
A = 10 in decimal
10 = 1010₂
So, A₁₆ = 1010₂
</pre>
<h3> Exam Focus Areas</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Convert decimal to binary</li>
  <li>Convert binary to decimal</li>
  <li>Convert binary to hexadecimal</li>
  <li>Explain why computers use binary</li>
</ul>
', 1),
  (2053, 2053, '<h2>Data Representation</h2>
<p>
  Data in computers is stored and processed using binary digits (0s and 1s).
  These binary values form the basis of all data storage and measurement.
</p>
<h3> 1. Units of Data Storage</h3>
<ul>
  <li><b>Bit:</b> Smallest unit of data (0 or 1)</li>
  <li><b>Nibble:</b> 4 bits</li>
  <li><b>Byte:</b> 8 bits</li>
  <li><b>Kilobyte (KB):</b> 1024 Bytes</li>
  <li><b>Megabyte (MB):</b> 1024 KB</li>
  <li><b>Gigabyte (GB):</b> 1024 MB</li>
  <li><b>Terabyte (TB):</b> 1024 GB</li>
</ul>
<h3> 2. Key Formula: Bits Calculation</h3>
<div class="example-box">
  <b>Formula:</b><br><br>
  Number of Bits = Number of Bytes × 8
</div>
<h3> 3. Examples of Bit Calculation</h3>
<pre>
Example 1:
5 Bytes → 5 × 8 = 40 Bits
Example 2:
10 Bytes → 10 × 8 = 80 Bits
Example 3:
2 KB → 2 × 1024 Bytes × 8 = 16384 Bits
</pre>
<h3> 4. Character Encoding</h3>
<h4> ASCII</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Uses numbers to represent characters</li>
  <li>Example: ''A'' = 65</li>
</ul>
<h4> Unicode</h4><ul><li>Supports many languages worldwide</li></ul>
<div class="example-box">
   Example:<br>
  ''A'' = 65 (ASCII)<br>
  Each character is stored using binary inside the computer
</div>
<h3> 5. Relationship Between Units</h3>
<pre>
1 Byte  = 8 Bits
1 KB     = 1024 Bytes
1 MB     = 1024 KB
1 GB     = 1024 MB
1 TB     = 1024 GB
</pre>
<h3> 6. Why Binary is Used</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Computers use ON (1) and OFF (0) signals</li>
  <li>Binary is easy for electronic circuits</li>
  <li>All data is stored as 0s and 1s</li>
</ul>
<div class="keyfact">
 Important Fact: All data in a computer is stored in binary form, and can be measured in bits and bytes.
</div>
<h3> Simple Explanation</h3>
<p>
  Data storage works like building blocks where everything is made from bits.
  Bytes, KB, MB, and GB are just bigger groups of those bits.
</p>
', 1),
  (2054, 2054, '<h2>CPU Components (ALU, CU, Registers)</h2>
<h3> DEEP EXPLANATION</h3>
<p>
The Central Processing Unit (CPU) is the main component of a computer system and is often called the “brain” of the computer.
It interprets and executes instructions, processes data, and controls the operations of all other components.
</p>
<h3> MAIN COMPONENTS OF THE CPU</h3>
<h4>1. Arithmetic Logic Unit (ALU)</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Performs arithmetic operations such as addition, subtraction, multiplication, and division</li>
  <li>Performs logical operations such as AND, OR, NOT, and comparisons</li>
  <li>Handles decision-making processes inside the computer</li>
</ul>
<h4>2. Control Unit (CU)</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Directs and coordinates all activities inside the CPU</li>
  <li>Fetches instructions from memory</li>
  <li>Decodes instructions into signals the CPU can understand</li>
  <li>Controls the flow of data between CPU, memory, and input/output devices</li>
</ul>
<h4>3. Registers</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Very small and fast memory locations inside the CPU</li>
  <li>Store temporary data and instructions during processing</li>
  <li>Examples include:</li>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Accumulator:</b> Stores intermediate results of calculations</li>
    <li><b>Program Counter (PC):</b> Keeps track of the next instruction</li>
    <li><b>Instruction Register (IR):</b> Holds the current instruction being executed</li>
  </ul>
</ul>
<h3> IMPORTANCE OF THE CPU</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Controls all computer operations</li>
  <li>Processes data and instructions</li>
  <li>Coordinates all hardware components</li>
  <li>Ensures programs run correctly</li>
</ul>
', 1),
  (2055, 2055, '<h2>Instruction Cycle (Fetch → Decode → Execute)</h2>
<h3> DEEP EXPLANATION</h3>
<p>
The instruction cycle is the repeated process the CPU follows to execute program instructions.
It runs continuously while the computer is powered on, allowing software to function step by step.
</p>

<h3> MAIN STAGES OF THE INSTRUCTION CYCLE</h3>

<h4>1. Fetch</h4>
<ul>
  <li>The CPU retrieves an instruction from main memory (RAM)</li>
  <li>The instruction is stored in the Instruction Register (IR)</li>
  <li>The Program Counter (PC) points to the next instruction</li>
</ul>

<h4>2. Decode</h4>
<ul>
  <li>The Control Unit (CU) interprets the instruction</li>
  <li>It determines what operation is required</li>
  <li>It identifies the data and components needed</li>
</ul>

<h4>3. Execute</h4>
<ul>
  <li>The ALU or other CPU components carry out the instruction</li>
  <li>Results are produced and stored in registers or memory</li>
  <li>Output may be sent to an output device if required</li>
</ul>

<h3> INSTRUCTION CYCLE FLOW</h3>
<pre>
Fetch → Decode → Execute → Fetch → Decode → Execute (repeats continuously)
</pre>

<h3> DETAILED PROCESS INSIDE CPU</h3>
<ul>
  <li>PC sends address to memory</li>
  <li>Instruction is fetched into IR</li>
  <li>CU decodes instruction</li>
  <li>ALU executes operation</li>
  <li>Result stored and PC updates</li>
</ul>

<h3> IMPORTANCE OF THE INSTRUCTION CYCLE</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Enables computers to run programs step by step</li>
  <li>Ensures systematic execution of instructions</li>
  <li>Keeps CPU working continuously and efficiently</li>
</ul>
', 1),
  (2056, 2056, '<h2>Von Neumann Architecture</h2>
<h3> DEEP EXPLANATION</h3>
<p>
The Von Neumann architecture is a computer design model where both data and instructions are stored in the same main memory.
It forms the basic structure of most modern computers and follows a sequential processing system.
</p>
<h3> MAIN COMPONENTS</h3>
<ul>
  <li><b>CPU:</b> Contains the ALU and Control Unit (executes instructions)</li>
  <li><b>Main Memory (RAM):</b> Stores both data and instructions</li>
  <li><b>Input Devices:</b> Used to enter data into the computer</li>
  <li><b>Output Devices:</b> Display processed results</li>
  <li><b>System Bus:</b> Transfers data and instructions between components</li>
</ul>
<h3> VON NEUMANN STRUCTURE</h3>
<pre>
Input Devices → Memory ↔ CPU → Output Devices
                     ↑
                 System Bus
</pre>

<h3> TYPES OF BUSES AND THEIR PURPOSES</h3>
<h4>1. Data Bus</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Transfers actual data between CPU, memory, and input/output devices</li>
  <li>Usually bidirectional (data can move both ways)</li>
  <li>Width of data bus affects performance (more bits = faster transfer)</li>
</ul>
<h4>2. Address Bus</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Transfers memory addresses from CPU to memory</li>
  <li>Usually unidirectional (CPU → Memory)</li>
  <li>Determines how much memory a system can access</li>
</ul>
<h4>3. Control Bus</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Transfers control signals between CPU and other components</li>
  <li>Includes signals like read, write, interrupt, and clock signals</li>
  <li>Ensures all parts of the system are synchronized</li>
</ul>
<h3> KEY IDEA</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Data and instructions share the same memory</li>
  <li>Instructions are processed one at a time (sequential execution)</li>
  <li>CPU follows the fetch–decode–execute cycle</li>
</ul>
<h3> HOW IT WORKS</h3>
<ol>
  <li>Input data and instructions are stored in memory</li>
  <li>CPU fetches instruction from memory</li>
  <li>Instruction is decoded by Control Unit</li>
  <li>ALU executes the instruction</li>
  <li>Result is stored back in memory or sent to output</li>
</ol>
<h3> LIMITATION: VON NEUMANN BOTTLENECK</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Data and instructions share the same bus/path</li>
  <li>CPU must wait to access memory</li>
  <li>Slows down processing speed</li>
</ul>
<pre>
Problem:
CPU ↔ SAME BUS ↔ MEMORY (data + instructions)
This creates a bottleneck (traffic jam in data flow)
</pre>
<h3> ADVANTAGES</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Simple and easy to design</li>
  <li>Cost-effective</li>
  <li>Flexible program storage (data + instructions together)</li>
</ul>
<h3> SIMPLE EXPLANATION</h3>
<p>
The Von Neumann architecture is like a single road used by both instructions and data, which makes it simple but can cause delays when traffic is heavy.
</p>
', 1),
  (2057, 2057, '<h2>Introduction to Computers</h2>
<p>A <strong>computer</strong> is a machine that takes in information (input), processes it, stores it, and produces results (output).</p>
<h2>1. Hardware (Physical Parts)</h2>
<p><strong>Hardware</strong> is anything you can see and touch.</p>
<h3>Main Components</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Input Devices:</strong> Used to enter data into the computer</li>
<li><strong>Processing Unit (CPU):</strong> The "brain" of the computer</li>
<li><strong>Output Devices:</strong> Show results</li>
<li><strong>Storage Devices:</strong> Save data</li>
</ul>
<h3>Examples of Hardware</h3>
<ul>
<li>Keyboard (typing)</li>
<li>Mouse (pointing)</li>
<li>Monitor (screen)</li>
<li>CPU/System Unit (processing)</li>
<li>Hard drive / SSD (storage)</li>
<li>Printer (output)</li>
</ul>
<h2>2. Software (Programs)</h2>
<p><strong>Software</strong> tells the hardware what to do.</p>

<h3>Types of Software</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>System Software:</strong> Controls the computer (Operating System)</li>
<li><strong>Application Software:</strong> Programs used by users (Word, Chrome)</li>
</ul>
<div class="example-box">
Examples:<br>
Windows (OS)<br>
Google Chrome (browser)<br>
Microsoft Word (document program)
</div>
<h2>3. Operating System (OS)</h2>
<p>The <strong>Operating System</strong> is the most important software. It connects the user to the hardware.</p>
<ul>
<li>Manages memory</li>
<li>Controls devices</li>
<li>Runs applications</li>
</ul>
<div class="example-box">
Examples:<br>
Windows<br>
Linux<br>
macOS
</div>
<h2>4. Peripheral Devices</h2>
<p>Peripheral devices are external hardware connected to the computer.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Input:</strong> keyboard, mouse, scanner</li>
<li><strong>Output:</strong> monitor, printer, speakers</li>
<li><strong>Storage:</strong> flash drive, external hard disk</li>
</ul>
<h2>5. Liveware (Human Factor)</h2>
<p><strong>Liveware</strong> refers to the human user of the computer.</p>
<ul>
<li>The person operating the computer</li>
<li>Gives instructions</li>
<li>Interprets results</li>
</ul>
<div class="example-box">
Without a user, a computer cannot function meaningfully.
</div>
<h2>How Everything Works Together</h2>
<ul>
<li>User (liveware) gives input</li>
<li>Input devices send data to CPU</li>
<li>CPU processes the data</li>
<li>Results are shown through output devices</li>
<li>Data can be stored for later use</li>
</ul>
<div class="keyfact">
 Key Fact: A computer system = Hardware + Software + Liveware working together.
</div>
', 1),
  (2058, 2058, '<h2>Input & Output Devices</h2>
<p>Input devices allow users to <strong>send data to the computer</strong>. Output devices allow the computer to <strong>present results to the user</strong>.</p>
<h2>1. Input Devices</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Keyboard:</strong> Types letters, numbers, symbols. Used for typing documents, commands. <em>Advantage:</em> Fast for text input. <em>Disadvantage:</em> Requires literacy/knowledge of layout.</li>
<li><strong>Mouse:</strong> Pointing device to select items. Used for GUI navigation. <em>Advantage:</em> Accurate pointer control. <em>Disadvantage:</em> Needs flat surface.</li>
<li><strong>Touchscreen:</strong> Detects finger/stylus input directly on screen. Used in phones, tablets, ATMs. <em>Advantage:</em> Direct interaction, intuitive. <em>Disadvantage:</em> Can get dirty, less precise for long text.</li>
<li><strong>Stylus:</strong> Pen-like tool for touchscreens. Used in tablets for drawing or handwriting. <em>Advantage:</em> More precise than finger. <em>Disadvantage:</em> Can be lost, needs compatible screen.</li>
<li><strong>Joystick:</strong> Game controller, moves objects in multiple directions. Used in gaming, simulations. <em>Advantage:</em> Easy control in games. <em>Disadvantage:</em> Limited general use.</li>
<li><strong>Voice Input:</strong> Converts spoken words to digital commands. Used in smartphones, voice assistants. <em>Advantage:</em> Hands-free, fast. <em>Disadvantage:</em> Background noise may affect accuracy.</li>
<li><strong>Scanner:</strong> Converts physical documents/images to digital. Used in offices, schools. <em>Advantage:</em> Preserves original document. <em>Disadvantage:</em> Slower than direct typing.</li>
<li><strong>Pinball / Specialized Controllers:</strong> Used in entertainment or arcade systems. <em>Advantage:</em> Engaging interaction. <em>Disadvantage:</em> Limited to niche applications.</li>
</ul>
<div class="example-box">
Typing a report → keyboard input<br>
Drawing a sketch → stylus input<br>
Moving a character in a game → joystick input<br>
Voice commands to phone → voice input
</div>
<h2>2. Output Devices</h2>
<p>Output devices allow the computer to present results to the user.</p>
<h3>Soft Copy Output</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Monitor / Screen:</strong> Displays text, images, video. Advantage: Real-time, interactive. Disadvantage: Requires power, cannot be held physically.</li>
<li><strong>Speakers / Headphones:</strong> Produce sound output (music, alerts). Advantage: Hands-free audio feedback. Disadvantage: Affected by noise, not permanent.</li>
<li><strong>Voice Output:</strong> Converts text to speech (TTS). Advantage: Accessibility for visually impaired, hands-free. Disadvantage: Requires quality software, may mispronounce words.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Hard Copy Output</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Printers:</strong> Produces permanent copies on paper.</li>
<li><strong>Dot Matrix / Impact Printers:</strong> Strike ink onto paper via pins. Advantage: Cheap, multi-copy printing. Disadvantage: Noisy, low resolution.</li>
<li><strong>Laser Printers:</strong> Use laser beams to print toner. Advantage: High speed, high quality. Disadvantage: Expensive initial cost, toner cartridges.</li>
<li><strong>Inkjet Printers:</strong> Spray tiny ink droplets onto paper. Advantage: High quality, color printing. Disadvantage: Slower than laser, ink expensive.</li>
</ul>
<div class="example-box">
Printing an assignment → hard copy (paper)<br>
Watching video → soft copy (monitor)<br>
Listening to music → soft copy (speakers)
</div>
<h2>3. Advantages and Disadvantages</h2>
<table>
<tr><th>Device Type</th><th>Advantage</th><th>Disadvantage</th></tr>
<tr><td>Keyboard</td><td>Fast text input</td><td>Needs literacy</td></tr>
<tr><td>Mouse</td><td>Accurate pointer control</td><td>Needs flat surface</td></tr>
<tr><td>Touchscreen</td><td>Intuitive, direct interaction</td><td>Less precise for typing</td></tr>
<tr><td>Stylus</td><td>Precision drawing</td><td>Can be lost</td></tr>
<tr><td>Joystick</td><td>Easy control in games</td><td>Limited general use</td></tr>
<tr><td>Voice Input</td><td>Hands-free</td><td>Background noise affects accuracy</td></tr>
<tr><td>Monitor</td><td>Real-time display</td><td>Cannot hold physically</td></tr>
<tr><td>Printer</td><td>Permanent copy</td><td>Costs paper/ink</td></tr>
<tr><td>Speaker</td><td>Audio feedback</td><td>No visual info, affected by noise</td></tr>
</table>
', 1),
  (2059, 2059, '<h2>Memory & Storage</h2>
<p>Computers need memory to <strong>store data and instructions</strong>. There are two main types: <strong>Primary memory</strong> (fast, temporary) and <strong>Secondary storage</strong> (slower, permanent).</p>
<h2>1. Primary Memory (Main Memory)</h2>
<p>Used by the CPU to store data that is being actively used.</p>

<ul>
<li><strong>RAM (Random Access Memory):</strong> Volatile memory; data lost when power is off. Used for active programs and OS. Types: <em>DRAM (Dynamic), SRAM (Static)</em>. Advantage: Fast access. Disadvantage: Temporary.</li>
<li><strong>ROM (Read-Only Memory):</strong> Non-volatile; stores permanent instructions (e.g., BIOS). Cannot be modified easily. Types: PROM, EPROM, EEPROM. Advantage: Permanent. Disadvantage: Cannot change easily.</li>
<li><strong>Cache Memory:</strong> Very fast memory located close to CPU. Stores frequently used data for quick access. Types: L1 (smallest, fastest), L2 (larger, slower than L1), L3 (largest, shared in multi-core CPUs). Advantage: Improves CPU performance. Disadvantage: Expensive, limited size.</li>
<li><strong>Registers:</strong> Smallest, fastest memory inside CPU. Temporarily stores data, instructions, or addresses being processed. Advantage: Immediate access for CPU. Disadvantage: Very limited storage.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Buses in Memory</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Data Bus:</strong> Transfers data between CPU and memory.</li>
<li><strong>Address Bus:</strong> Carries memory addresses to read/write locations.</li>
<li><strong>Control Bus:</strong> Sends control signals (read/write operations).</li>
</ul>
<div class="example-box">
CPU accesses RAM → stores active program<br>
Registers hold intermediate calculation results<br>
Cache holds frequently used data to speed CPU
</div>
<h2>2. Secondary Storage</h2>
<p>Used for permanent storage of programs and data. Slower than primary memory but non-volatile.</p>
<h3>Magnetic Storage</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Hard Disk Drive (HDD):</strong> Uses magnetic disks to store data. Advantage: High capacity, cheap per GB. Disadvantage: Slower than SSD, sensitive to shocks and magnets.</li>
<li><strong>Magnetic Tape:</strong> Stores large data sequentially. Advantage: Cheap, good for backups. Disadvantage: Slow, sequential access.</li>
<li><strong>Floppy Disk:</strong> Old magnetic storage. Advantage: Portable. Disadvantage: Very low capacity, obsolete.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Solid-State Storage</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>SSD (Solid-State Drive):</strong> Uses flash memory, no moving parts. Advantage: Fast, durable. Disadvantage: More expensive per GB than HDD.</li>
<li><strong>USB Flash Drive:</strong> Portable flash memory. Advantage: Easy to carry, plug-and-play. Disadvantage: Small capacity, can be lost easily.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Optical Storage</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>CD-ROM / CD-R / CD-RW:</strong> 700 MB. CD-R: write once. CD-RW: rewriteable. Advantage: Cheap, portable. Disadvantage: Low capacity, slower than HDD/SSD.</li>
<li><strong>DVD / DVD-R / DVD-RW:</strong> 4.7–8.5 GB. Advantage: Larger capacity than CD. Disadvantage: Slower than HDD/SSD.</li>
<li><strong>Blu-Ray Disc:</strong> 25–50 GB. Advantage: High capacity, good for HD video. Disadvantage: Expensive, requires Blu-Ray drive.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Handling & Care of Storage Devices</h3>
<ul>
<li>Keep HDDs away from strong magnets → data can be erased.</li>
<li>Avoid exposing disks (HDD, CDs/DVDs/BDs) to dust, smoke, heat, or scratches.</li>
<li>Use anti-static precautions for RAM or motherboards.</li>
<li>Do not bend or drop flash drives or floppy disks.</li>
<li>Back up critical data to multiple storage types for safety.</li>
</ul>
<div class="example-box">
Saving a document → stored on HDD/SSD<br>
Watching a movie → from DVD/Blu-Ray or SSD<br>
Booting computer → BIOS instructions from ROM
</div>
<div class="keyfact"> Key Fact: Primary memory is fast but volatile; secondary storage is slower but permanent. Proper care ensures long lifespan and data safety.</div>
', 1),
  (2060, 2060, '<h2>Operating System</h2>
<p>An operating system (OS) is a set of system software that manages computer hardware and software resources, serving as an interface between the user and the computer hardware. It allows applications to run, manages memory, schedules tasks, and controls peripheral devices.</p>
<h2>Key Notes and Concepts</h2>
<h3>1. Primary Roles and Goals</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Interface:</strong> Acts as an intermediary between the user and computer hardware, making the computer convenient to use.</li>
<li><strong>Resource Manager:</strong> Allocates and manages shared hardware resources such as CPU time, memory, and Input/Output (I/O) devices, ensuring efficient utilization.</li>
<li><strong>Abstraction:</strong> Conceals the complexities of hardware from applications, providing a uniform interface.</li>
<li><strong>Kernel:</strong> The core, essential part of the OS that is always running and manages critical functions.</li>
<li><strong>Shell:</strong> The component that interprets user commands and gets them executed.</li>
</ul>
<h3>2. Core Functions of an Operating System</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Process Management:</strong> Manages the execution of applications (processes), including scheduling, creation, termination, and synchronization.</li>
<li><strong>Memory Management:</strong> Tracks and allocates Main Memory (RAM). It decides which processes load into memory and manages virtual memory (using hard disk space as an extension of RAM).</li>
<li><strong>File Management:</strong> Organizes, creates, deletes, and manages files and directories on storage devices.</li>
<li><strong>Device Management:</strong> Uses device drivers to communicate with hardware peripherals (printers, disks, keyboards).</li>
<li><strong>Security and Protection:</strong> Protects data and resources from unauthorized access via authentication (passwords) and isolation of processes.</li>
<li><strong>Error Detection:</strong> Monitors system health and reports errors to prevent system failure.</li>
</ul>
<h3>3. Key OS Concepts and Techniques</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>System Calls:</strong> The interface between a running program and the OS kernel, allowing user-level programs to request services.</li>
<li><strong>Multitasking/Multiprogramming:</strong> The ability of an OS to switch between tasks quickly, allowing multiple programs to run seemingly at the same time and keeping the CPU busy.</li>
<li><strong>Interrupts:</strong> Signals sent to the CPU by hardware or software to request immediate attention, allowing the OS to react to events.</li>
<li><strong>Deadlock Handling:</strong> Manages situations where two or more processes are stuck waiting for resources held by each other.</li>
<li><strong>Spooling:</strong> Temporarily storing data in a buffer (e.g., for printers) so the CPU can continue other tasks while the slow device works.</li>
<li><strong>Booting:</strong> The process of starting a computer by loading the kernel into memory.</li>
</ul>
<h3>4. Types of Operating Systems</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Batch OS:</strong> Jobs with similar needs are grouped (batched) and processed together without user interaction (e.g., payroll).</li>
<li><strong>Time-Sharing (Multitasking) OS:</strong> Allows multiple users to share the system simultaneously by switching between tasks quickly (e.g., Unix, Windows).</li>
<li><strong>Distributed OS:</strong> Manages a group of independent computers and makes them appear as a single system.</li>
<li><strong>Real-Time OS (RTOS):</strong> Used when strict time constraints are required (e.g., robotics, airbag controllers).</li>
<li><strong>Mobile OS:</strong> Designed specifically for smartphones and tablets (e.g., Android, iOS).</li>
</ul>
<h3>5. Common Examples</h3>
<ul>
<li>Windows: Microsoft’s commercial OS for desktops and servers.</li>
<li>Linux: Open-source, Unix-based OS, popular on servers.</li>
<li>macOS: Apple’s desktop OS.</li>
<li>Android/iOS: Dominant mobile operating systems.</li>
</ul>
<h3>6. OS Structure</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Monolithic Kernel:</strong> All OS services run in kernel space, high performance but less stable (e.g., Linux).</li>
<li><strong>Microkernel:</strong> Only essential services run in kernel space; others run in user space, increasing stability (e.g., Minix).</li>
<li><strong>Hybrid Kernel:</strong> Combines aspects of both (e.g., Windows NT, macOS).</li>
</ul>
', 1),
  (2061, 2061, '<h2>File Management</h2>
<p>File management is a fundamental concept in computing that involves how an operating system (OS) stores, names, organizes, and manipulates files. In a high school curriculum, this typically covers the hierarchy of storage, file attributes, and common operations.</p>
<h3>1. The File Hierarchy (Folders and Directories)</h3>
<p>Computers use a hierarchical structure (often called a "tree structure") to organize data.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Drive:</strong> The top level of storage (e.g., C: drive for a Hard Drive, D: for a USB).</li>
<li><strong>Folders (Directories):</strong> Containers used to group related files together.</li>
<li><strong>Sub-folders:</strong> Folders inside other folders.</li>
<li><strong>Files:</strong> The actual data units (documents, images, programs).</li>
</ul>
<div class="example-box">
Example Path:<br>
C:\Documents\School\ComputerScience\Notes.docx<br>
"School" is a folder, and "Notes.docx" is the file.
</div>
<h3>2. File Naming and Extensions</h3>
<p>A filename consists of two parts: the name and the extension, separated by a dot.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Filename:</strong> A descriptive name (e.g., History_Essay)</li>
<li><strong>Extension:</strong> Indicates the file type and program used to open it</li>
</ul>
<table>
<tr><th>Type</th><th>Extension</th><th>Program Example</th></tr>
<tr><td>Documents</td><td>.docx, .pdf, .txt</td><td>Word, Adobe Reader</td></tr>
<tr><td>Images</td><td>.jpg, .png, .gif</td><td>Photos, Photoshop</td></tr>
<tr><td>Audio</td><td>.mp3, .wav</td><td>Spotify, Media Player</td></tr>
<tr><td>Video</td><td>.mp4, .mov</td><td>VLC, QuickTime</td></tr>
<tr><td>Executables</td><td>.exe, .app</td><td>System Applications</td></tr>
</table>
<h3>3. Basic File Operations</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Create:</strong> Making a new file or folder</li>
<li><strong>Save / Save As:</strong> Writing data to storage ("Save As" changes name/type)</li>
<li><strong>Copy:</strong> Duplicate file in another location</li>
<li><strong>Move (Cut/Paste):</strong> Change file location</li>
<li><strong>Rename:</strong> Change filename</li>
<li><strong>Delete:</strong> Send file to Recycle Bin / Trash</li>
</ul>
<h3>4. File Metadata and Attributes</h3>
<p>Files have hidden information called metadata:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>File Size:</strong> KB, MB, GB</li>
<li><strong>Date Created/Modified</strong></li>
<li><strong>Permissions:</strong> Read, Write, Execute</li>
<li><strong>Status:</strong> Hidden, System, Read-only</li>
</ul>
<h3>5. Storage Best Practices</h3>
<ul>
<li>Use descriptive names (e.g., Bio_Lab_Report_V2)</li>
<li>Organize files into folders</li>
<li>Backup files regularly (Cloud or external drive)</li>
<li>Avoid deep nesting (too many folders inside folders)</li>
</ul>
<h3>6. Compression and Archiving</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Compression (.zip, .rar):</strong> Reduces file size</li>
<li><strong>Extracting:</strong> Restores compressed files to original form</li>
</ul>
<div class="example-box">
Example:<br>
Large folder → compressed to .zip → sent via email → extracted by receiver
</div>
<div class="keyfact">
 Key Fact: Good file management makes it easier to find files, saves storage space, and prevents data loss.
</div>
', 1),
  (2062, 2062, '<h2>Introduction to Programming</h2>
<p><strong>Programming</strong> is the process of creating a set of instructions that tells a computer how to perform a specific task.</p>
<div class="example-box">
Think of programming like writing a recipe:<br>
Ingredients → Data<br>
Steps → Code<br>
Final dish → Output
</div>
<h2>1. Essential Programming Terminology</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Algorithm:</strong> A step-by-step procedure for solving a problem.</li>
<li><strong>Source Code:</strong> The actual text written by a programmer.</li>
<li><strong>Syntax:</strong> Rules that define how code must be written.</li>
<li><strong>Bug:</strong> An error in a program.</li>
<li><strong>Debugging:</strong> Finding and fixing errors.</li>
<li><strong>Variable:</strong> A container for storing data.</li>
<li><strong>Compiler/Interpreter:</strong> Tools that translate code into machine language (1s and 0s).</li>
</ul>
<h2>2. The Programming Process (SDLC)</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Problem Definition:</strong> Understand the problem.</li>
<li><strong>Design:</strong> Plan the solution (flowcharts, pseudocode).</li>
<li><strong>Coding:</strong> Write the program.</li>
<li><strong>Testing:</strong> Find and fix bugs.</li>
<li><strong>Maintenance:</strong> Update and improve the program.</li>
</ul>
<div class="example-box">
Example:<br>
Build a calculator → plan steps → code → test → fix errors
</div>
<h2>3. Categories of Programming Languages</h2>
<h3>Low-Level Languages</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Machine Code:</strong> Binary (0s and 1s), very hard for humans.</li>
<li><strong>Assembly Language:</strong> Uses short codes (MOV, ADD).</li>
</ul>
<h3>High-Level Languages (HLL)</h3>
<p>Designed to be easy for humans.</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Language</th><th>Use</th><th>Strength</th></tr>
<tr><td>Python</td><td>AI, Data Science</td><td>Easy for beginners</td></tr>
<tr><td>Java</td><td>Apps, Systems</td><td>Portable</td></tr>
<tr><td>C++</td><td>Games</td><td>Fast</td></tr>
<tr><td>JavaScript</td><td>Web</td><td>Runs in browsers</td></tr>
<tr><td>Swift</td><td>iOS apps</td><td>Optimized for Apple</td></tr>
</table>
<h2>4. Fundamental Logic Structures</h2>
<h3>A. Sequence</h3>
<p>Instructions executed step by step.</p>
<h3>B. Selection (Decision)</h3>
<p>Program chooses based on condition.</p>
<div class="example-box">
IF age ≥ 18 → allow voting<br>
ELSE → deny access
</div>
<h3>C. Iteration (Loop)</h3>
<p>Repeats instructions.</p>
<div class="example-box">
Send 100 emails automatically
</div>
<h2>5. Data Types</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Integer:</strong> Whole numbers (5, -42)</li>
<li><strong>Float:</strong> Decimal numbers (3.14)</li>
<li><strong>String:</strong> Text ("Hello")</li>
<li><strong>Boolean:</strong> True/False</li>
</ul>
<h2>6. Interpreted vs Compiled Languages</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Compiled:</strong> Entire program translated before running (e.g., C++) → faster</li>
<li><strong>Interpreted:</strong> Runs line-by-line (e.g., Python) → easier debugging</li>
</ul>
<div class="example-box">
C++ → compiled → fast execution<br>
Python → interpreted → easier to debug
</div>
<div class="keyfact">
 Key Fact: Every program you use — from games to mobile apps — follows these same core principles.
</div>
', 1),
  (2063, 2063, '<h2>Loops & Conditionals</h2>
<p><strong>Conditionals:</strong> decision making</p>
<pre>
if x > 5:
  print("Big")
</pre>
<p><strong>Loops:</strong> repetition</p>
<pre>
for i in range(5):
  print(i)
</pre>
<div class="example-box">
Loop prints numbers 0–4
</div>
<h2>Control Structures</h2>
<p>Control structures are the "brain" of any program. They allow the code to make decisions and repeat tasks, moving beyond simple step-by-step execution.</p>
<h2>1. Conditional Statements (Selection)</h2>
<p>Conditionals allow a program to execute specific blocks of code only if a certain condition is met.</p>
<h3>A. The if Statement</h3>
<p>The most basic decision-making statement. If the condition is true, the code runs.</p>
<pre>
age = 20
if age >= 18:
    print("You are an adult.")
</pre>
<h3>B. The if-else Statement</h3>
<p>Provides an alternative path if the condition is false.</p>
<pre>
score = 45
if score >= 50:
    print("Pass")
else:
    print("Fail")
</pre>
<h3>C. The if-elif-else Ladder</h3>
<p>Used when there are more than two possible outcomes.</p>
<pre>
grade = 85
if grade >= 90:
    print("A")
elif grade >= 80:
    print("B")
elif grade >= 70:
    print("C")
else:
    print("D or F")
</pre>
<h3>D. Nested if Statements</h3>
<p>An if statement inside another if for complex conditions.</p>
<pre>
has_ticket = True
has_id = False

if has_ticket:
    if has_id:
        print("Entry allowed.")
    else:
        print("Please show your ID.")
else:
    print("No ticket, no entry.")
</pre>
<h2>2. Loops (Iteration)</h2>
<p>Loops repeat a block of code multiple times.</p>
<h3>A. The for Loop</h3>
<p>Used when you know how many times to repeat.</p>
<pre>
for i in range(5):
    print("Loop number:", i)
</pre>
<h3>B. The while Loop</h3>
<p>Repeats while a condition is true.</p>
<pre>
count = 5
while count > 0:
    print(count)
    count = count - 1
print("Blast off!")
</pre>
<h3>C. Nested Loops</h3>
<p>A loop inside another loop.</p>
<pre>
for row in range(3):
    for col in range(3):
        print("Row", row, "Col", col)
</pre>
<h2>3. Key Loop Controls</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>break:</strong> Stops the loop immediately</li>
<li><strong>continue:</strong> Skips current iteration</li>
</ul>
<h2>Comparison: Loops vs Conditionals</h2>
<table>
<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Conditionals (if)</th><th>Loops (for/while)</th></tr>
<tr><td>Purpose</td><td>Decision making</td><td>Repetition</td></tr>
<tr><td>Execution</td><td>Runs once</td><td>Runs repeatedly</td></tr>
<tr><td>Keywords</td><td>if, else, elif</td><td>for, while</td></tr>
<tr><td>Example</td><td>Checking password</td><td>Printing multiple pages</td></tr>
</table>
<div class="keyfact">
 Conditionals decide WHAT to do, loops decide HOW MANY TIMES to do it.
</div>
', 1),
  (2064, 2064, '<h2>Functions & Methods</h2>
<p>A function is reusable code.</p>
<pre>
def add(a,b):
  return a+b
</pre>
<div class="example-box">
add(2,3) = 5
</div>
<h2>In programming, functions and methods are blocks of reusable code designed to perform a specific action.</h2>
<p>They help break down complex programs into smaller, manageable, and organized pieces.</p>
<h2>1. What is a Function?</h2>
<p>A function is a self-contained block of code that only runs when it is called. You can pass data, known as parameters, into a function, and it can return data as a result.</p>
<h3>Key Components:</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Declaration/Definition:</strong> Creating the function and giving it a name.</li>
<li><strong>Parameters:</strong> Variables listed in the function definition (the "inputs").</li>
<li><strong>Arguments:</strong> The actual values sent to the function when it is called.</li>
<li><strong>Return Statement:</strong> The "output" the function sends back to the main program.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Example in Python:</h3>
<pre>
# Defining the function
def greet_user(name): 
    return "Hello, " + name
# Calling the function
message = greet_user("Alex")
print(message)
</pre>
<h2>2. What is a Method?</h2>
<p>A method is essentially a function that "belongs" to an object or a class. While functions can be called by themselves, a method must be called on a specific object.</p>
<h3>The Difference:</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Function:</strong> calculate_area(5, 10) (Independent)</li>
<li><strong>Method:</strong> my_dog.bark() (Dependent on the "dog" object)</li>
</ul>
<h3>Example of a String Method:</h3>
<pre>
text = "hello world"
print(text.upper())
</pre>
<h2>3. Why Use Functions and Methods?</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Reusability (DRY):</strong> Write code once and reuse it.</li>
<li><strong>Abstraction:</strong> Use code without knowing internal details.</li>
<li><strong>Organization:</strong> Makes code easier to read and debug.</li>
<li><strong>Modularity:</strong> Allows teamwork in programming.</li>
</ul>
<h2>4. Function Scope (Local vs Global)</h2>
<p>Scope determines where a variable can be used.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Local Scope:</strong> Variable inside function.</li>
<li><strong>Global Scope:</strong> Variable outside function.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Example:</h3>
<pre>
x = 10 # Global variable
def my_function():
    y = 5 # Local variable
    print(x)
    print(y)

my_function()
print(y) # ERROR
</pre>
<h2>5. Built-in vs User-Defined Functions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Built-in:</strong> Provided by language (print(), len())</li>
<li><strong>User-defined:</strong> Created by programmer</li>
</ul>
<h2>Summary Table</h2>
<table>
<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Function</th><th>Method</th></tr>
<tr><td>Location</td><td>Independent</td><td>Inside class</td></tr>
<tr><td>Call</td><td>function_name()</td><td>object.method()</td></tr>
<tr><td>Relationship</td><td>No object</td><td>Linked to object</td></tr>
<tr><td>Data</td><td>Uses parameters</td><td>Uses object data</td></tr>
</table>
<div class="keyfact">
 Functions are independent blocks of code, while methods belong to objects.
</div>
', 1),
  (2065, 2065, '<h2>Arrays & Lists</h2>
<p>In programming, we often need to store a collection of similar items (like a list of student names or temperatures). Instead of creating many separate variables, we use <b>Arrays and Lists</b>.</p>
<h3>1. What is an Array?</h3>
<p>An <b>Array</b> is a collection of items stored at contiguous (neighboring) memory locations. It holds a fixed number of items, usually of the same data type.</p>
<ul>
<li><b>Fixed Size:</b> Cannot be changed after creation.</li>
<li><b>Homogeneous:</b> Same data type.</li>
<li><b>Index-Based:</b> Each element has a position.</li>
</ul>
<h3>2. The Concept of Indexing</h3>
<p>Indexing starts at <b>0</b>:</p>
<ul>
<li>First element → index 0</li>
<li>Second element → index 1</li>
<li>Last element → index (n-1)</li>
</ul>
<pre>
prices = [10.50, 20.00, 5.75]
prices[0] = 10.50
prices[2] = 5.75
</pre>
<h3>3. Arrays vs Lists</h3>
<table border="1">
<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Array</th><th>List</th></tr>
<tr><td>Size</td><td>Fixed</td><td>Dynamic</td></tr>
<tr><td>Data Types</td><td>Same type</td><td>Mixed allowed (Python)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Memory</td><td>Efficient</td><td>More flexible</td></tr>
<tr><td>Languages</td><td>C, C++, Java</td><td>Python, JavaScript</td></tr>
</table>
<h3>4. Common Operations</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Accessing:</b> Get value using index</li>
<li><b>Updating:</b> Change value</li>
<li><b>Appending:</b> Add item (Lists)</li>
<li><b>Traversing:</b> Loop through items</li>
</ul>
<h3>5. Code Examples</h3>
<h4>Python (Lists)</h4>
<pre>
fruits = ["Apple", "Banana", "Cherry"]
fruits.append("Orange")
fruits[1] = "Blueberry"
for x in fruits:
    print(x)
</pre>
<h4>Java (Arrays)</h4>
<pre>
int[] numbers = new int[5];

numbers[0] = 10;
numbers[1] = 20;

System.out.println(numbers[0]);
</pre>
<h3>6. Two-Dimensional (2D) Arrays</h3>
<pre>
grid = [
  [1,2,3],
  [4,5,6],
  [7,8,9]
]

print(grid[1][1])
</pre>
<h3>7. Why Use Arrays/Lists?</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Efficiency:</b> Fast access using index</li>
<li><b>Group Processing:</b> Handle many items with loops</li>
<li><b>Sorting:</b> Organize data easily</li>
</ul>
<div class="example-box">
numbers = [1,2,3]
numbers[0] = 1
</div>
', 1),
  (2066, 2066, '<h2>Debugging</h2>
<p>
<b>Debugging</b> is the systematic process of identifying, analyzing, and fixing errors (bugs) in a computer program. It is a core skill in programming and software development.
</p>
<h3> DEBUGGING PROCESS</h3>
<ol>
<li><b>Reproduce the error</b>: Make the bug happen consistently</li>
<li><b>Locate the error</b>: Identify the exact part of the code causing the issue</li>
<li><b>Analyze the cause</b>: Understand why the error occurs</li>
<li><b>Fix and test</b>: Correct the code and verify the solution</li>
</ol>
<h3> TYPES OF ERRORS</h3>
<h4>1. Syntax Errors</h4>
<p>
Errors caused by breaking programming language rules. The program will not run.
</p>
<pre>
if age >= 18
    print("Allowed")  # Missing colon causes error

# Fix
if age >= 18:
    print("Allowed")
</pre>
<h4>2. Runtime Errors</h4>
<p>
Errors that occur while the program is running (often causes crash).
</p>
<pre>
x = 10
y = 0
print(x / y)  # Division by zero error

# Fix
if y != 0:
    print(x / y)
</pre>
<h4>3. Logical Errors</h4>
<p>
The program runs but produces incorrect results.
</p>
<pre>
# Wrong
average = num1 + num2 / 2

# Correct
average = (num1 + num2) / 2
</pre>
<h3> COMMON ERROR TYPES</h3>
<table border="1">
<tr><th>Error</th><th>Cause</th><th>Fix</th></tr>
<tr><td>NameError</td><td>Variable not defined</td><td>Define before use</td></tr>
<tr><td>TypeError</td><td>Wrong data type operation</td><td>Convert types (e.g. str(), int())</td></tr>
<tr><td>IndexError</td><td>Invalid list index</td><td>Check list size</td></tr>
<tr><td>IndentationError</td><td>Wrong spacing</td><td>Fix indentation</td></tr>
</table>
<h3> DEBUGGING TECHNIQUES</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Print debugging</b>: Use print() to trace values</li>
<li><b>Debugger tools</b>: Breakpoints, step-by-step execution</li>
<li><b>Rubber duck debugging</b>: Explain code to find logic errors</li>
<li><b>Code isolation</b>: Comment out sections to locate errors</li>
</ul>
<pre>
print("DEBUG:", x)
</pre>
<h3> DEBUGGING CHECKLIST</h3>
<ul>
<li>Read error messages carefully</li>
<li>Check spelling and syntax</li>
<li>Verify variable values and types</li>
<li>Simplify the problem</li>
</ul>
<div class="example-box">
Debugging = Systematic process of finding and fixing errors in code
</div>
', 1),
  (2067, 2067, '<h2>Object-Oriented Programming (OOP)</h2>
<p>
<b>Object-Oriented Programming (OOP)</b> is a programming paradigm that organizes software around <b>objects</b>, which combine data (attributes) and behavior (methods).
</p>
<h3> REAL-LIFE ANALOGY</h3>
<div class="example-box">
Car → Object<br>
Properties → color, speed<br>
Methods → drive(), brake()
</div>
<h3> CORE PRINCIPLES (PILLARS OF OOP)</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Encapsulation</b>: Hiding internal data and controlling access</li>
<li><b>Inheritance</b>: Reusing properties from another class</li>
<li><b>Polymorphism</b>: Same method behaves differently in different classes</li>
<li><b>Abstraction</b>: Hiding complex implementation and showing only essentials</li>
</ul>
<h3> BASIC STRUCTURE (PYTHON)</h3>
<pre>
class Car:
    def __init__(self, brand):
        self.brand = brand

    def drive(self):
        print(self.brand + " is moving")

my_car = Car("Toyota")
my_car.drive()
</pre>
<h3> KEY CONCEPTS</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Class</b>: Blueprint for objects</li>
<li><b>Object</b>: Instance of a class</li>
<li><b>Attributes</b>: Data inside objects</li>
<li><b>Methods</b>: Functions inside classes</li>
</ul>
<h3> WHY OOP IS IMPORTANT</h3>
<ul>
<li>Improves code reusability</li>
<li>Makes programs modular</li>
<li>Easy to maintain and scale</li>
<li>Closer to real-world modeling</li>
</ul>
<div class="keyfact">
 OOP = Objects + Data + Behavior working together
</div>
', 1),
  (2068, 2068, '<h2>Classes & Objects</h2>
<p>
In Object-Oriented Programming (OOP), a <b>class</b> is a blueprint (template), while an <b>object</b> is a real instance created from that blueprint.
</p>
<h3> KEY IDEA</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Class</b>: Defines structure (attributes + methods)</li>
<li><b>Object</b>: A real entity created from a class with actual values</li>
</ul>
<h3> PYTHON EXAMPLE</h3>
<pre>
class Student:
    def __init__(self, name):
        self.name = name

student1 = Student("Alex")

print(student1.name)
</pre>
<h3> COMPONENTS</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Attributes</b>: Variables inside a class (e.g., name)</li>
<li><b>Methods</b>: Functions inside a class</li>
<li><b>Constructor (__init__)</b>: Initializes object values</li>
</ul>
<h3> REAL-WORLD ANALOGY</h3>
<ul>
<li>Class → Blueprint of a house</li>
<li>Object → Actual house built from blueprint</li>
</ul>
<div class="example-box">
Class = Template<br>
Object = Real instance created from template
</div>
', 1),
  (2069, 2069, '<h2>Inheritance</h2>
<p>
<b>Inheritance</b> is an Object-Oriented Programming (OOP) concept where a new class (child class) acquires properties and methods from an existing class (parent class).
</p>
<h3> KEY IDEA</h3>
<p>
It represents a <b>parent → child relationship</b> and allows code reuse and extension.
</p>
<h3> PYTHON EXAMPLE</h3>
<pre>
class Animal:
    def speak(self):
        print("Animal sound")

class Dog(Animal):   # Dog inherits from Animal
    def bark(self):
        print("Woof!")

d = Dog()
d.speak()  # inherited method
d.bark()   # own method
</pre>
<h3> TYPES OF INHERITANCE</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Single Inheritance</b>: One parent → one child</li>
<li><b>Multiple Inheritance</b>: One child → multiple parents</li>
<li><b>Multilevel Inheritance</b>: Grandparent → Parent → Child</li>
<li><b>Hierarchical Inheritance</b>: One parent → multiple children</li>
</ul>
<h3> BENEFITS OF INHERITANCE</h3>
<ul>
<li>Code reusability</li>
<li>Reduces repetition</li>
<li>Improves maintainability</li>
<li>Supports hierarchical classification</li>
</ul>
<h3> REAL-WORLD ANALOGY</h3>
<ul>
<li>Animal → Dog, Cat, Bird</li>
<li>Vehicle → Car, Bus, Truck</li>
</ul>
<div class="keyfact">
 Inheritance = Parent class shares features with child classes
</div>
', 1),
  (2070, 2070, '<h2>Encapsulation</h2>
<p>
<b>Encapsulation</b> is an Object-Oriented Programming (OOP) concept that involves 
<b>bundling data (variables) and methods (functions)</b> together and restricting direct access to the data.
</p>
<h3> KEY IDEA</h3>
<p>
Data is hidden from outside access and can only be modified through controlled methods.
</p>
<h3> PYTHON EXAMPLE</h3>
<pre>
class Bank:
    def __init__(self):
        self.__balance = 0   # Private variable

    def deposit(self, amount):
        self.__balance += amount

    def get_balance(self):
        return self.__balance
</pre>
<h3> ACCESS MODIFIERS (IMPORTANT)</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Public</b>: Accessible anywhere</li>
<li><b>Protected (_var)</b>: Should not be accessed directly</li>
<li><b>Private (__var)</b>: Strongly restricted access</li>
</ul>
<h3> WHY ENCAPSULATION?</h3>
<ul>
<li>Protects data from unauthorized access</li>
<li>Prevents accidental modification</li>
<li>Improves code security and reliability</li>
<li>Controls how data is used</li>
</ul>
<h3> REAL-WORLD ANALOGY</h3>
<ul>
<li>ATM Machine → You cannot access money directly</li>
<li>You use methods (deposit/withdraw) instead</li>
</ul>
<div class="example-box">
Encapsulation = Data hiding + Controlled access
</div>
', 1),
  (2071, 2071, '<h2>Recursion</h2>
<p>
<b>Recursion</b> is a programming technique where a function calls itself to solve a problem by breaking it into smaller sub-problems.
</p>
<h3> KEY COMPONENTS</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Base Case</b>: The stopping condition that ends recursion</li>
<li><b>Recursive Case</b>: The part where the function calls itself</li>
</ul>
<h3> HOW IT WORKS</h3>
<pre>
factorial(3)
= 3 × factorial(2)
= 3 × 2 × factorial(1)
= 3 × 2 × 1
= 6
</pre>
<h3> PYTHON EXAMPLE (FACTORIAL)</h3>
<pre>
def factorial(n):
    if n == 1:   # Base case
        return 1
    return n * factorial(n-1)  # Recursive call
</pre>
<h3> IMPORTANT RULES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Must have a base case (to avoid infinite recursion)</li>
<li>Each call should move toward the base case</li>
<li>Uses stack memory (call stack)</li>
</ul>

---

<h3> RECURSION VS ITERATION</h3>
<pre>
Recursion        | Iteration
-----------------------------
Function calls   | Loops (for/while)
Uses call stack  | Uses variables
Cleaner logic    | More memory efficient
</pre>

---

<h3> REAL-WORLD APPLICATION</h3>
<ul>
<li>Factorial and Fibonacci calculations</li>
<li>Tree and graph traversal</li>
<li>File system navigation</li>
<li>Divide-and-conquer algorithms</li>
</ul>

---

<div class="example-box">
Recursion = Function solving smaller versions of itself
</div>
', 1),
  (2072, 2072, '<h2>Stacks</h2>



<p>
A <b>stack</b> is a linear data structure that follows the <b>LIFO (Last In, First Out)</b> principle.
The last element added is the first one to be removed.
</p>

---

<h3> KEY OPERATIONS</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Push</b>: Add element to the top</li>
<li><b>Pop</b>: Remove the top element</li>
<li><b>Peek/Top</b>: View the top element without removing it</li>
<li><b>isEmpty</b>: Check if stack has no elements</li>
</ul>

---

<h3> VISUAL FLOW</h3>
<pre>
Push → [1, 2, 3] → Pop → [1, 2]
            ↑
          Top
</pre>

---

<h3> TYPES OF STACKS</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Simple Stack</b>: Basic LIFO structure</li>
<li><b>Dynamic Stack</b>: Grows/shrinks in size (using lists/linked lists)</li>
<li><b>Call Stack</b>: Used in function calls (program execution)</li>
</ul>

---

<h3> PYTHON EXAMPLE</h3>
<pre>
stack = []

# Push
stack.append(1)
stack.append(2)

# Pop
stack.pop()

# Peek
print(stack[-1])
</pre>

---

<h3> REAL-WORLD APPLICATION</h3>
<ul>
<li>Undo/Redo operations in apps</li>
<li>Function calls (call stack)</li>
<li>Expression evaluation (e.g., calculators)</li>
<li>Browser history navigation</li>
</ul>

---

<div class="example-box">
Stack = Pile of plates <br>
Last plate added → first removed
</div>
', 1),
  (2073, 2073, '<h2>Queues</h2>



<p>
A <b>queue</b> is a linear data structure that follows the <b>FIFO (First In, First Out)</b> principle.
The first element added is the first one to be removed.
</p>

---

<h3> KEY OPERATIONS</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Enqueue</b>: Add element to the rear (end)</li>
<li><b>Dequeue</b>: Remove element from the front</li>
<li><b>Peek/Front</b>: View the first element without removing it</li>
<li><b>isEmpty</b>: Check if queue has no elements</li>
</ul>

---

<h3> VISUAL FLOW</h3>
<pre>
Enqueue → [1, 2, 3] → Dequeue → [2, 3]
</pre>

---

<h3> TYPES OF QUEUES</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Simple Queue</b>: Basic FIFO structure</li>
<li><b>Circular Queue</b>: End connects to front to reuse space</li>
<li><b>Priority Queue</b>: Elements processed based on priority</li>
<li><b>Deque (Double-ended Queue)</b>: Insert/remove from both ends</li>
</ul>

---

<h3> PYTHON EXAMPLE</h3>
<pre>
from collections import deque

queue = deque()

# Enqueue
queue.append(1)
queue.append(2)

# Dequeue
queue.popleft()

# Peek
print(queue[0])
</pre>

---

<h3> REAL-WORLD APPLICATION</h3>
<ul>
<li>Printer queue (documents printed in order)</li>
<li>CPU task scheduling</li>
<li>Call center systems</li>
<li>Data buffering in streaming</li>
</ul>

---

<div class="example-box">
Queue = Line at a shop <br>
First person in line → first served
</div>
', 1),
  (2074, 2074, '<h2>Advanced Databases</h2>

  <p>
    Advanced databases focus on powerful techniques used in real-world systems to manage large and complex data efficiently.
    These include JOIN operations, normalization, indexing, and performance optimization.
  </p>
  <h3> Key Idea</h3>
  <p>
    Advanced database systems are designed to organize data efficiently, reduce duplication, and speed up data retrieval.
  </p>

  <h3> 1. SQL JOINs</h3>
  <p>
    JOIN operations are used to combine data from two or more tables based on related columns.
  </p>

  <h4>INNER JOIN</h4>
  <p>Returns only records that match in both tables.</p>

  <pre>
SELECT Students.Name, Courses.CourseName
FROM Students
INNER JOIN Enrollments ON Students.ID = Enrollments.StudentID
INNER JOIN Courses ON Enrollments.CourseID = Courses.CourseID;
  </pre>

  <h4>LEFT JOIN</h4>
  <p>Returns all records from the left table and matching records from the right table.</p>

  <pre>
SELECT Students.Name, Courses.CourseName
FROM Students
LEFT JOIN Enrollments ON Students.ID = Enrollments.StudentID
LEFT JOIN Courses ON Enrollments.CourseID = Courses.CourseID;
  </pre>

  <h4>RIGHT JOIN</h4>
  <p>Returns all records from the right table and matching records from the left table.</p>

  <h3> 2. Normalization</h3>
  <p>
    Normalization is the process of organizing data in a database to reduce redundancy and improve data integrity.
  </p>

  <h4> Before Normalization</h4>
  <pre>
Student | Course | Instructor
Alex    | Math   | Mr. John
Alex    | Science| Mr. Mike
  </pre>

  <h4> After Normalization</h4>
  <pre>
Students Table
ID | Name

Courses Table
CourseID | CourseName

Enrollments Table
StudentID | CourseID
  </pre>

  <p>
    This removes duplication and improves efficiency.
  </p>

  <h3> 3. Indexing</h3>
  <p>
    An index is a database structure that improves the speed of data retrieval operations.
  </p>

  <pre>
CREATE INDEX idx_name ON Students(Name);
  </pre>

  <p>
    Without an index, the database scans every record. With an index, it quickly locates data.
  </p>

  <h3> 4. Performance Optimization</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Query Optimization:</b> Writing efficient SQL statements</li>
    <li><b>Indexing:</b> Speeds up searching</li>
    <li><b>Efficient Joins:</b> Reduce processing time</li>
    <li><b>Normalization:</b> Reduces redundancy</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    These tables are connected using relationships and JOIN queries to retrieve data efficiently.
  </p>

  <h3> Summary</h3>
  <div class="example-box">
    JOIN = Combine tables<br>
    Normalization = Remove duplication<br>
    Index = Speed up searching<br>
    Optimization = Improve performance
  </div>

  <h3> Simple Explanation</h3>
  <p>
    Advanced databases are like highly organized libraries where books are sorted, indexed, and connected for fast retrieval.
  </p>
  ', 1),
  (2075, 2075, '<h2>Tables & Records</h2>
  <p>
    A database is an organized collection of related data stored electronically and managed by a
    Database Management System (DBMS) such as MySQL or PostgreSQL.
  </p>
  <h3> Key Idea</h3>
  <p>
    Databases store information in structured formats called tables, making data easy to access, update, and manage.
  </p>

  <h3> 1. Components of a Database System</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Hardware:</b> Physical devices such as servers, storage drives, and memory</li>
    <li><b>Software:</b> DBMS like MySQL, Oracle, PostgreSQL</li>
    <li><b>Data:</b> The actual information stored in the system</li>
    <li><b>Procedures:</b> Rules and instructions for managing data</li>
    <li><b>Users:</b> Database administrators and end users</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> 2. Data Organization in Tables</h3>
  <p>
    Data in a database is stored in tables similar to spreadsheets.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><b>Table:</b> A collection of related data</li>
    <li><b>Row (Record):</b> A single complete entry (e.g., one student)</li>
    <li><b>Column (Field):</b> A specific type of data (e.g., Name, Age)</li>
  </ul>

  <h4>Example Table</h4>
  <table border="1">
    <tr><th>ID</th><th>Name</th><th>Age</th></tr>
    <tr><td>101</td><td>Alex</td><td>20</td></tr>
    <tr><td>102</td><td>Mary</td><td>22</td></tr>
  </table>

  <h3> 3. Primary Key</h3>
  <p>
    A primary key is a field that uniquely identifies each record in a table.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Must be unique</li>
    <li>Cannot be empty (NULL)</li>
    <li>Used to avoid duplicate records</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="example-box">
    Example: Student ID = Primary Key
  </div>

  <h3> 4. Importance of Databases</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Organizes large amounts of data efficiently</li>
    <li>Improves data retrieval and storage</li>
    <li>Reduces duplication of data</li>
    <li>Supports fast decision-making</li>
  </ul>
  <h3> Simple Explanation</h3>
  <p>
    A database is like a digital filing cabinet where information is stored in organized tables.
  </p>
  ', 1),
  (2076, 2076, '<h2>SQL (Structured Query Language)</h2>
  <p>
    SQL is a standard programming language used to communicate with databases.
    It allows users to create, retrieve, update, and delete data in a database system.
  </p>
  <h3> Key Idea</h3>
  <p>
    SQL is the language that allows humans to communicate with databases and control stored data.
  </p>

  <h3> 1. Basic SQL Query</h3>
  <p>
    The SELECT statement is used to retrieve data from a database table.
  </p>

  <pre>
SELECT * FROM students;
  </pre>

  <p>
    This command retrieves all records from the "students" table.
  </p>

  <h3> 2. Filtering Data</h3>
  <p>
    The WHERE clause is used to filter records based on conditions.
  </p>

  <pre>
SELECT Name FROM students WHERE ID = 101;
  </pre>

  <p>
    This retrieves only the name of the student with ID 101.
  </p>

  <h3> 3. Common SQL Commands</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>SELECT:</b> Retrieves data</li>
    <li><b>INSERT:</b> Adds new data</li>
    <li><b>UPDATE:</b> Modifies existing data</li>
    <li><b>DELETE:</b> Removes data</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> 4. SQL Tools</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Direct SQL:</b> Writing commands manually in a query editor</li>
    <li><b>Form-Based Interfaces:</b> Using applications to input data</li>
    <li><b>Database Tools:</b> phpMyAdmin, MySQL Workbench, Microsoft Access</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> 5. Example Queries</h3>
  <pre>
-- Select specific columns
SELECT Name, Age FROM students;

-- Filter records
SELECT * FROM students WHERE Age > 18;

-- Insert data
INSERT INTO students (Name, Age) VALUES (''John'', 20);
  </pre>

  <h3> 6. Importance of SQL</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Used in almost all modern applications</li>
    <li>Handles large volumes of data efficiently</li>
    <li>Essential for web development and backend systems</li>
    <li>Supports data analysis and reporting</li>
  </ul>
  <h3> Simple Explanation</h3>
  <p>
    SQL is like giving instructions to a librarian so they can find, add, or remove books from a library database.
  </p>
  ', 1),
  (2077, 2077, '<h2>CRUD Operations</h2>
  <p>
    CRUD represents the four fundamental operations used to manage data in a database system.
    These operations form the basis of all database interactions and backend systems.
  </p>
  <h3> Key Idea</h3>
  <p>
    CRUD is the foundation of database management, controlling how data is created, accessed, modified, and removed.
  </p>
  <h3> 1. Meaning of CRUD</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Create:</b> Adds new records into a database</li>
    <li><b>Read:</b> Retrieves existing data</li>
    <li><b>Update:</b> Modifies existing records</li>
    <li><b>Delete:</b> Removes records from a database</li>
  </ul>
  <h3> 2. CRUD and SQL Mapping</h3>
  <h4>Create (INSERT)</h4>
  <pre>
INSERT INTO Students (ID, Name) VALUES (101, ''Alex'');
  </pre>
  <h4>Read (SELECT)</h4>
  <pre>
SELECT Name FROM Students WHERE ID = 101;
  </pre>
  <h4>Update</h4>
  <pre>
UPDATE Students SET Name = ''Alexander'' WHERE ID = 101;
  </pre>
  <h4>Delete</h4>
  <pre>
DELETE FROM Students WHERE ID = 101;
  </pre>
  <h3> 3. Database Integrity Rules</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Data Type Validation:</b> Ensures correct data formats are used</li>
    <li><b>Referential Integrity:</b> Maintains relationships between related tables</li>
    <li><b>Primary Key Constraint:</b> Ensures uniqueness of records</li>
  </ul>
  <h3> 4. Importance of CRUD</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Forms the core of all database operations</li>
    <li>Used in every application that stores data</li>
    <li>Ensures structured and controlled data management</li>
    <li>Supports backend system development</li>
  </ul>
  <h3> Simple Explanation</h3>
  <p>
    CRUD is like managing a notebook: you can write new information, read it, edit it, or erase it when no longer needed.
  </p>
  ', 1),
  (2078, 2078, '<h2>Relational Databases</h2>
  <p>
    A relational database is a type of database that stores data in multiple tables that are linked together using relationships.
    This structure reduces duplication and improves data organization and efficiency.
  </p>
  <h3> Key Idea</h3>
  <p>
    Relational databases store data in separate tables and connect them using keys to ensure efficiency and avoid duplication.
  </p>
  <h3> 1. Why Relationships Matter</h3>
  <p>
    Instead of storing all data in one large table, relational databases divide data into smaller tables and link them.
  </p>
  <div class="example-box">
    Students Table → student details<br>
    Courses Table → course details<br>
    Enrollments Table → links students and courses
  </div>
  <h3> 2. Types of Relationships</h3>
  <h4>One-to-One (1:1)</h4>
  <p>One record in a table is linked to one record in another table.</p>
  <div class="example-box">
    Example: One student → One ID card
  </div>
  <h4>One-to-Many (1:M)</h4>
  <p>One record in a table is linked to many records in another table.</p>
  <div class="example-box">
    Example: One teacher → Many students
  </div>
  <h4>Many-to-Many (M∞M)</h4>
  <p>Many records in one table are linked to many records in another table.</p>
  <div class="example-box">
    Example: Students ↔ Courses
  </div>
  <h3> 3. Primary Key</h3>
  <p>
    A primary key is a unique identifier for each record in a table.
  </p>
  <pre>
Students Table
ID | Name
101 | Alex
102 | Jane
  </pre>

  <h3> 4. Foreign Key</h3>
  <p>
    A foreign key is a field in one table that refers to the primary key in another table.
  </p>

  <pre>
Enrollments Table
StudentID | CourseID
101       | 501
  </pre>

  <h3> 5. Example Database Structure</h3>
  <pre>
Students
ID | Name

Courses
CourseID | CourseName

Enrollments
StudentID | CourseID
  </pre>

  <h3> 6. SQL Example (Relationships)</h3>
  <pre>
CREATE TABLE Students (
  ID INT PRIMARY KEY,
  Name VARCHAR(50)
);

CREATE TABLE Courses (
  CourseID INT PRIMARY KEY,
  CourseName VARCHAR(50)
);

CREATE TABLE Enrollments (
  StudentID INT,
  CourseID INT,
  FOREIGN KEY (StudentID) REFERENCES Students(ID),
  FOREIGN KEY (CourseID) REFERENCES Courses(CourseID)
);
  </pre>

  <h3> 7. Referential Integrity</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Ensures relationships between tables remain valid</li>
    <li>Prevents invalid foreign key entries</li>
    <li>Stops deletion of linked records</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> 8. Importance of Relational Databases</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Reduces data duplication</li>
    <li>Improves data accuracy and consistency</li>
    <li>Allows complex queries across multiple tables</li>
    <li>Makes data management scalable</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> Simple Explanation</h3>
  <p>
    A relational database is like a network of linked notebooks where each notebook stores a different type of information, but all are connected.
  </p>
  ', 1),
  (2079, 2079, '<h2>Transactions & ACID Properties</h2>
  <p>
    A transaction is a sequence of database operations that are executed as a single logical unit of work.
    Either all operations succeed, or none are applied.
  </p>
  <h3> Key Idea</h3>
  <p>
    Transactions ensure that database operations are reliable, consistent, and safe even when errors occur.
  </p>

  <h3> 1. Example of a Transaction</h3>
  <div class="example-box">
    Bank Transfer System:<br>
    Step 1: Deduct money from Account A<br>
    Step 2: Add money to Account B
  </div>

  <p>
    If any step fails, the entire transaction is canceled to prevent data errors.
  </p>

  <h3> 2. ACID Properties</h3>

  <h4> Atomicity</h4>
  <p>
    Ensures that all operations in a transaction succeed together or fail together.
  </p>

  <h4> Consistency</h4>
  <p>
    Ensures the database remains in a valid state before and after the transaction.
  </p>

  <h4> Isolation</h4>
  <p>
    Ensures multiple transactions do not interfere with each other when executed at the same time.
  </p>

  <h4> Durability</h4>
  <p>
    Once a transaction is committed, changes are permanent even after system failure.
  </p>

  <h3> 3. Transaction States</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Begin:</b> Transaction starts</li>
    <li><b>Commit:</b> Saves all changes permanently</li>
    <li><b>Rollback:</b> Cancels changes and restores previous state</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> 4. SQL Transaction Example</h3>

  <pre>
BEGIN;
UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - 100 WHERE id = 1;
UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance + 100 WHERE id = 2;
COMMIT;
  </pre>
  <p>
    If an error occurs before COMMIT, the system can perform a ROLLBACK.
  </p>
  <h3> 5. Importance of Transactions</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Prevents data corruption</li>
    <li>Ensures accurate financial systems</li>
    <li>Maintains data integrity</li>
    <li>Supports multi-user systems safely</li>
  </ul>
  <h3> Simple Explanation</h3>
  <p>
    A transaction is like a full mission: either everything is completed successfully, or nothing is saved.
  </p>
  ', 1),
  (2080, 2080, '<h2>NoSQL Databases (MongoDB Concept)</h2>
  <p>
    NoSQL databases are non-relational databases designed to store large volumes of unstructured or semi-structured data.
    They are commonly used in modern applications that require flexibility and high scalability.
  </p>
  <h3> Key Idea</h3>
  <p>
    NoSQL databases are designed for speed, flexibility, and scalability rather than strict table structures.
  </p>
  <h3> 1. Why NoSQL?</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Handles large-scale and big data systems</li>
    <li>Flexible schema (no fixed table structure)</li>
    <li>High performance for real-time applications</li>
    <li>Suitable for modern web and mobile apps</li>
  </ul>
  <h3> 2. Types of NoSQL Databases</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Document-based:</b> MongoDB (stores JSON-like documents)</li>
    <li><b>Key-value:</b> Redis (stores data as key-value pairs)</li>
    <li><b>Column-based:</b> Cassandra (stores data in columns)</li>
    <li><b>Graph-based:</b> Neo4j (stores relationships between data)</li>
  </ul>
  <h3> 3. MongoDB Structure</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Database:</b> Top-level container</li>
    <li><b>Collection:</b> Group of documents (like a table)</li>
    <li><b>Document:</b> Individual record stored in JSON format</li>
  </ul>
  <h3> 4. Example MongoDB Document</h3>
  <pre>
{
  "name": "Alex",
  "age": 20,
  "course": "Computer Science"
}
  </pre>
  <h3> 5. SQL vs NoSQL Comparison</h3>
  <table>
    <tr>
      <th>Feature</th>
      <th>SQL</th>
      <th>NoSQL</th>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Structure</td>
      <td>Fixed tables</td>
      <td>Flexible documents</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Schema</td>
      <td>Rigid</td>
      <td>Dynamic</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Scalability</td>
      <td>Vertical scaling</td>
      <td>Horizontal scaling</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Examples</td>
      <td>MySQL, PostgreSQL</td>
      <td>MongoDB, Redis</td>
    </tr>
  </table>
  <h3> 6. Importance of NoSQL</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Supports big data applications</li>
    <li>Used in social media platforms</li>
    <li>Handles real-time analytics</li>
    <li>Improves performance in large systems</li>
  </ul>
  <h3> Simple Explanation</h3>
  <p>
    NoSQL databases are like flexible digital storage systems that can store different types of data without strict rules.
  </p>
  ', 1),
  (2081, 2081, '<h2>Backup & Recovery Strategies</h2>

<p><b>Backup</b> is creating copies of data, while <b>recovery</b> is restoring data after loss or failure.</p>
<h3>1. Why Backup is Important</h3>
<ul>
<li>Prevents data loss</li>
<li>Protects against system failure</li>
<li>Helps recover from cyber attacks</li>
</ul>
<h3>2. Types of Backups</h3>

<h4>Full Backup</h4>
<p>Copies all data.</p>
<h4>Incremental Backup</h4>
<p>Copies only changes since last backup.</p>
<h4>Differential Backup</h4>
<p>Copies changes since last full backup.</p>
<h3>3. Recovery Methods</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Restore:</b> Recover from backup</li>
<li><b>Point-in-time recovery:</b> Restore to a specific time</li>
</ul>
<h3>4. Backup Strategies</h3>
<ul>
<li>Regular backups (daily/weekly)</li>
<li>Store backups offsite/cloud</li>
<li>Use multiple backup copies</li>
</ul>
<h3>5. The 3-2-1 Rule</h3>
<ul>
<li>3 copies of data</li>
<li>2 different storage types</li>
<li>1 offsite copy</li>
</ul>
<div class="example-box">
Laptop crash → restore from cloud backup → data recovered
</div>

<div class="keyfact">
 Backup + Recovery = Data survival strategy.
</div>
', 1),
  (2082, 2082, '<h2>Why Networks are Used</h2>
  <p>
    Computer networks are used to connect devices so that they can share resources, communicate,
    and exchange data efficiently.
  </p>
  <h3> Key Idea</h3>
  <p>
    Networks allow computers to work together, making systems faster, cheaper, and more efficient.
  </p>
  <h3> Reasons for Using Networks</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Resource Sharing:</b> Multiple users can share printers, software, and storage devices</li>
    <li><b>Data Sharing:</b> Files and databases can be accessed by many users</li>
    <li><b>Communication:</b> Enables email, video calls, and instant messaging</li>
    <li><b>Cost Efficiency:</b> Reduces cost by sharing hardware and software</li>
    <li><b>Backup & Reliability:</b> Central storage allows easy data backup and recovery</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> Importance of Networking</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Improves teamwork and collaboration</li>
    <li>Increases efficiency in organizations</li>
    <li>Reduces duplication of resources</li>
    <li>Supports global communication systems</li>
  </ul>
  <h3> Simple Explanation</h3>
  <p>
    Networks are like teamwork systems where many computers share tools and information instead of working alone.
  </p>
  ', 1),
  (2083, 2083, '<h2>LAN, MAN, WAN, PAN Comparison</h2>
  <p>
    Computer networks are classified based on their geographical coverage and size.
    Each type serves different communication needs depending on distance and scale.
  </p>
  <h3> Key Idea</h3>
  <p>
    Networks are classified by how far devices are spread—from personal devices (PAN) to global networks (WAN).
  </p>
  <h3> Types of Networks</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>PAN (Personal Area Network):</b> Very small network for personal devices like Bluetooth connections</li>
    <li><b>LAN (Local Area Network):</b> Covers a small area like a school, office, or home</li>
    <li><b>MAN (Metropolitan Area Network):</b> Covers a city or large town</li>
    <li><b>WAN (Wide Area Network):</b> Covers large geographical areas including countries and continents</li>
  </ul>
  <h3> Comparison Summary</h3>
  <table>
    <tr>
      <th>Type</th>
      <th>Coverage</th>
      <th>Example</th>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>PAN</td>
      <td>Very small (personal)</td>
      <td>Bluetooth between phone and headset</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>LAN</td>
      <td>Small area</td>
      <td>School computer lab</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>MAN</td>
      <td>City/town</td>
      <td>City internet network</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>WAN</td>
      <td>Global</td>
      <td>Internet</td>
    </tr>
  </table>
  <h3> Importance of Network Types</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Helps organize communication systems</li>
    <li>Improves data sharing efficiency</li>
    <li>Determines network cost and design</li>
    <li>Supports different levels of connectivity</li>
  </ul>
  <h3> Simple Explanation</h3>
  <p>
    These networks are like circles of communication: PAN is your personal circle,
    LAN is your building, MAN is your city, and WAN is the whole world.
  </p>
  ', 1),
  (2084, 2084, '<h2>Network Topologies</h2>
  <p>
    Network topology refers to the physical or logical arrangement of computers and devices in a network.
    It shows how devices are connected and how data flows between them.
  </p>
  <h3> Key Idea</h3>
  <p>
    Network topology determines how computers are arranged and how they communicate in a network.
  </p>
  <h3> Types of Network Topologies</h3>
  <h3>1. Bus Topology</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>All devices are connected to a single backbone cable</li>
    <li>Data travels in both directions along the cable</li>
    <li>Easy to install but performance reduces with many devices</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>2. Star Topology</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>All devices connect to a central device (hub or switch)</li>
    <li>Most commonly used in schools and offices</li>
    <li>If one device fails, others are not affected</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>3. Ring Topology</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Devices are connected in a circular format</li>
    <li>Data travels in one direction or both directions</li>
    <li>A failure in one device can affect the entire network</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>4. Mesh Topology</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Every device is connected to every other device</li>
    <li>Highly reliable and fault-tolerant</li>
    <li>Expensive and complex to install</li>
  </ul>
  <h3> Comparison Summary</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Bus:</b> Simple but slow with many users</li>
    <li><b>Star:</b> Reliable and most commonly used</li>
    <li><b>Ring:</b> Moderate performance but less fault-tolerant</li>
    <li><b>Mesh:</b> Most reliable but expensive</li>
  </ul>
  <h3> Advantages of Topologies</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Help organize network structure</li>
    <li>Improve communication efficiency</li>
    <li>Allow easy troubleshooting depending on type</li>
  </ul>
  <h3> Simple Explanation</h3>
  <p>
    Network topology is like the layout of a city showing how roads (connections) link buildings (computers).
  </p>
  ', 1),
  (2085, 2085, '<h2>Transmission Media</h2>
  <p>
    Transmission media refers to the physical or wireless pathways through which data is transmitted
    from one device to another in a network.
  </p>
  <h3> Key Idea</h3>
  <p>
    Transmission media are the communication channels that allow data to travel between devices either through cables or wireless signals.
  </p>
  <h3> Types of Transmission Media</h3>
  <h3>1. Guided (Wired) Media</h3>
  <p>Data is transmitted through physical cables.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Twisted Pair Cable:</b> Common in telephone and Ethernet networks</li>
    <li><b>Coaxial Cable:</b> Used in cable TV and broadband</li>
    <li><b>Fiber Optic Cable:</b> Fastest transmission, uses light signals</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>2. Unguided (Wireless) Media</h3>
  <p>Data is transmitted through air without cables.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Radio Waves:</b> Used in Wi-Fi, Bluetooth, mobile phones</li>
    <li><b>Microwaves:</b> Require line-of-sight communication</li>
    <li><b>Satellites:</b> Provide global communication coverage</li>
  </ul>
  <h3> Comparison</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Wired:</b> More stable, faster, but less flexible</li>
    <li><b>Wireless:</b> Flexible and mobile, but may suffer interference</li>
  </ul>
  <h3> Importance of Transmission Media</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Enables data communication between devices</li>
    <li>Supports internet connectivity</li>
    <li>Allows sharing of resources</li>
    <li>Enables global communication systems</li>
  </ul>
  <h3> Simple Explanation</h3>
  <p>
    Transmission media are like roads or invisible air paths that allow data to travel between computers.
  </p>
  ', 1),
  (2086, 2086, '<h2>IP Addressing</h2>
  <p>
    An IP (Internet Protocol) address is a unique numerical label assigned to each device connected to a network.
    It allows devices to identify and communicate with each other.
  </p>
  <h3> Key Idea</h3>
  <p>
    An IP address is like a digital home address that allows data to reach the correct device on a network.
  </p>

  <h3> Structure of IPv4</h3>
  <p>
    IPv4 is a 32-bit address divided into four parts called octets. Each octet is separated by a dot.
  </p>

  <div class="example-box">
    Example: 192.168.1.1
  </div>

  <ul>
    <li>Each part is called an <b>octet</b></li>
    <li>Each octet ranges from 0 to 255</li>
    <li>Total = 4 octets = 32 bits</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> Binary Representation</h3>
  <p>
    Each octet is made up of 8 binary digits (bits).
  </p>

  <pre>
192 → 11000000
168 → 10101000
1   → 00000001
1   → 00000001
  </pre>

  <h3> Types of IP Addresses</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Private IP:</b> Used inside local networks (e.g., home Wi-Fi, school lab)</li>
    <li><b>Public IP:</b> Used on the internet and assigned by ISP</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> Static vs Dynamic IP</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Static IP:</b> Does not change over time</li>
    <li><b>Dynamic IP:</b> Changes automatically using DHCP</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> Importance of IP Addressing</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Identifies devices on a network</li>
    <li>Ensures correct data delivery</li>
    <li>Enables internet communication</li>
    <li>Supports network organization</li>
  </ul>
  <h3> Simple Explanation</h3>
  <p>
    An IP address is like a house number for a device so that data knows exactly where to go.
  </p>
  ', 1),
  (2087, 2087, '<h2>DNS (Domain Name System)</h2>
  <p>
    DNS (Domain Name System) is a system that translates human-readable domain names
    (like google.com) into machine-readable IP addresses that computers use to identify each other on a network.
  </p>
  <h3> Key Idea</h3>
  <p>
    DNS acts like the internet’s phonebook by converting easy-to-remember names into IP addresses that computers understand.
  </p>

  <h3> Why DNS is Needed</h3>
  <p>
    Computers communicate using numbers (IP addresses), but humans prefer names.
    DNS bridges this gap.
  </p>

  <div class="example-box">
    google.com → 142.250.190.78<br>
    facebook.com → 157.240.229.35
  </div>

  <h3> How DNS Works (Step-by-Step)</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>User types a website name in the browser</li>
    <li>The request is sent to a DNS server</li>
    <li>The DNS server looks up the matching IP address</li>
    <li>The IP address is sent back to the browser</li>
    <li>The browser connects to the website server</li>
  </ol>

  <h3> Key Components</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Domain Name:</b> Human-friendly website name (e.g., google.com)</li>
    <li><b>IP Address:</b> Numerical address used by computers</li>
    <li><b>DNS Server:</b> Stores and manages domain-to-IP mappings</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> Importance of DNS</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Makes internet easier to use</li>
    <li>Eliminates need to memorize IP addresses</li>
    <li>Speeds up website access</li>
    <li>Supports global internet communication</li>
  </ul>
  <h3> Simple Explanation</h3>
  <p>
    DNS is like a contact list in your phone: you type a name, and it finds the number automatically.
  </p>
  ', 1),
  (2088, 2088, '<h2>Client-Server Model</h2>
  <p>
    The client-server model is a network structure where computers are divided into two roles:
    clients that request services and servers that provide those services.
  </p>
  <h3> Key Idea</h3>
  <p>
    In a client-server network, one central system (server) provides resources while other computers (clients)
    request and use those resources.
  </p>

  <h3> Components</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Client:</b> A device or program that requests services (e.g., web browser)</li>
    <li><b>Server:</b> A powerful computer that provides services, data, or resources</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> How It Works</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>The client sends a request (e.g., opening a website)</li>
    <li>The server receives and processes the request</li>
    <li>The server sends back the required response/data</li>
  </ol>

  <h3> Example</h3>
  <div class="example-box">
    • A web browser (client) requests a webpage<br>
    • The web server processes the request<br>
    • The server sends the webpage back to the browser
  </div>

  <h3> Advantages of Client-Server Model</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Centralized control of data and resources</li>
    <li>Improved security and data protection</li>
    <li>Easier backup and maintenance</li>
    <li>Efficient resource sharing</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> Disadvantages</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Expensive to set up and maintain</li>
    <li>Depends heavily on the server (if it fails, network stops)</li>
    <li>Requires skilled administration</li>
  </ul>
  <h3> Simple Explanation</h3>
  <p>
    The client-server model is like ordering food in a restaurant:
    you (client) request, and the kitchen (server) prepares and serves your order.
  </p>
  ', 1),
  (2089, 2089, '<h2>Peer-to-Peer (P2P) Networks</h2>
  <p>
    A peer-to-peer (P2P) network is a type of network where all computers are equal.
    Each computer (node) can act as both a client (requesting data) and a server (providing data).
  </p>
  <h3> Key Idea</h3>
  <p>
    In a peer-to-peer network, there is no central server; all computers share resources directly with each other.
  </p>

  <h3> Characteristics of P2P Networks</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>No central server controlling the network</li>
    <li>All computers have equal status</li>
    <li>Each device can share files and resources</li>
    <li>Simple to install and manage</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> Example of P2P Network</h3>
  <div class="example-box">
    • Sharing files directly between two computers using a USB network connection or LAN without a server
  </div>

  <h3> Advantages of P2P Networks</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Easy to set up</li>
    <li>Low installation cost</li>
    <li>No need for a dedicated server</li>
    <li>Useful for small networks</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> Disadvantages of P2P Networks</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Less secure compared to client-server networks</li>
    <li>Difficult to manage in large networks</li>
    <li>No central backup system</li>
    <li>Performance may slow down with many users</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> Comparison: P2P vs Client-Server</h3>
  <table>
    <tr>
      <th>Feature</th>
      <th>P2P Network</th>
      <th>Client-Server Network</th>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Control</td>
      <td>Decentralized</td>
      <td>Centralized</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Security</td>
      <td>Lower</td>
      <td>Higher</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Cost</td>
      <td>Low</td>
      <td>High</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Setup</td>
      <td>Simple</td>
      <td>Complex</td>
    </tr>
  </table>
  <h3> Simple Explanation</h3>
  <p>
    A peer-to-peer network is like a group of friends sharing things directly with each other
    without a boss controlling everything.
  </p>
  ', 1),
  (2090, 2090, '<h2>Network Troubleshooting Basics</h2>
  <p>
  Troubleshooting is the systematic process of identifying, diagnosing, and fixing problems in a computer network
  to ensure it operates efficiently and reliably.
</p>
<h3> Key Idea</h3>
<p>
  Network troubleshooting helps users detect, isolate, and resolve issues so that communication,
  devices, and internet services continue to function properly.
</p>
<h3> Common Network Problems</h3>
<ul>
  <li>No internet connection</li>
  <li>Slow network performance</li>
  <li>Device failing to connect to Wi-Fi or LAN</li>
  <li>Incorrect IP configuration</li>
  <li>DNS errors (website not loading)</li>
  <li>Intermittent connection drops</li>
</ul>
<h3> Basic Troubleshooting Steps</h3>
<ol>
  <li>Check physical connections (cables, routers, switches)</li>
  <li>Restart network devices (router, modem, computer)</li>
  <li>Verify network settings and IP configuration</li>
  <li>Test connectivity using tools like ping</li>
  <li>Check firewall or antivirus settings</li>
  <li>Reconnect to the network or Wi-Fi</li>
  <li>Update or reinstall network drivers</li>
</ol>
<h3> Troubleshooting Tools</h3>
<pre>
 Ping        → Tests if a device is reachable on a network
 Traceroute  → Shows the path data takes to reach a destination
 IPConfig     → Displays IP address and network configuration
 DNS Lookup   → Checks domain name resolution
</pre>
<h3> Advanced Troubleshooting Techniques</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Checking bandwidth usage to detect congestion</li>
  <li>Using network analyzers to monitor traffic</li>
  <li>Testing with different devices to isolate faults</li>
  <li>Changing DNS servers to fix browsing issues</li>
</ul>
<h3> Importance of Troubleshooting</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Restores network connectivity quickly</li>
  <li>Reduces downtime in communication and work</li>
  <li>Helps identify faulty hardware or software</li>
  <li>Improves overall network performance</li>
  <li>Saves time and repair costs</li>
</ul>
<h3> Real-Life Example</h3>
<p>
  When a school computer lab loses internet access, technicians may restart the router, check cables,
  and use the ping command to identify where the connection is failing.
</p>
<h3> Simple Explanation</h3>
<p>
  Troubleshooting is like being a network doctor who diagnoses and fixes problems when computers,
  networks, or internet connections stop working properly.
</p>
  ', 1),
  (2091, 2091, '<h2>Data Communication Concepts</h2>
  <p>
  Data communication refers to the exchange of digital data between two or more devices
  through a transmission medium such as cables, fiber optics, or wireless signals.
</p>
<h3> Key Idea</h3>
<p>
  Data communication is the process of sending and receiving digital information between devices
  using agreed rules (protocols) and communication technologies.
</p>
<h3> Key Components of Data Communication</h3>
<pre>
 Sender          → Device that sends data
 Receiver        → Device that receives data
 Message         → The data being transmitted (text, images, video, etc.)
 Transmission Medium → Path used to carry data (cables or wireless signals)
 Protocol        → Rules that control data communication
</pre>
<h3> Key Concepts</h3>
<pre>
 Bandwidth   → Amount of data transmitted per second (speed of connection)
 Protocols   → Rules that ensure proper communication between devices (e.g., TCP/IP, HTTP)
 Modem       → Converts digital signals for transmission over communication lines
 Router      → Directs data between different networks
</pre>
<h3> Explanation of Concepts</h3>
<ul>
  <li><b>Bandwidth:</b> Determines how much data can be transferred in a given time (higher bandwidth = faster communication)</li>
  <li><b>Protocols:</b> Set rules that ensure devices understand each other during communication</li>
  <li><b>Modem:</b> Converts digital signals to analog and back to enable internet connection</li>
  <li><b>Router:</b> Directs data packets between networks such as home and the internet</li>
</ul>
<h3> Modes of Data Communication</h3>
<ul>
  <li><b>Simplex:</b> Data flows in one direction only (e.g., TV broadcast)</li>
  <li><b>Half Duplex:</b> Data flows in both directions, but one at a time (e.g., walkie-talkie)</li>
  <li><b>Full Duplex:</b> Data flows in both directions simultaneously (e.g., phone call)</li>
</ul>
<h3> Importance of Data Communication</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Enables internet communication and connectivity</li>
  <li>Allows sharing of files and information globally</li>
  <li>Supports online learning, banking, and business operations</li>
  <li>Improves speed and efficiency of communication</li>
</ul>
<h3> Real-Life Example</h3>
<p>
  When you send a WhatsApp message, your phone acts as the sender, the internet is the transmission medium,
  and the recipient’s phone is the receiver. Protocols ensure the message is delivered correctly.
</p>
<h3> Simple Explanation</h3>
<p>
  Data communication is like a digital conversation between devices where messages are sent,
  received, and understood using rules (protocols) and speed (bandwidth).
</p>
  ', 1),
  (2092, 2092, '<h2>Network Security</h2>
  <p>
  Network security refers to the protection of computer networks, systems, and data
  from unauthorized access, damage, theft, or cyber attacks such as hacking, malware, sniffing, and spoofing.
</p>

<h3> Key Idea</h3>
<p>
  Network security ensures that only authorized users can access systems and data,
  while preventing, detecting, and responding to cyber threats.
</p>
<h3> Types of Network Security Threats</h3>
<pre>
 Malware       → Harmful software (viruses, worms, ransomware)
 Phishing      → Fake messages that trick users into revealing information
‍ Hacking       → Unauthorized access to systems or data
 Eavesdropping → Interception of network data during transmission
 Sniffing      → Capturing and monitoring network data packets secretly
 Spoofing      → Pretending to be a trusted device or user to gain access
 Denial of Service (DoS) → Overloading a system to make it unavailable
</pre>
<h3> Methods of Network Security</h3>
<pre>
 Firewall        → Blocks unauthorized access to a network
 Encryption      → Converts data into unreadable code
 Authentication  → Verifies user identity (passwords, biometrics, 2FA)
 Antivirus       → Detects and removes malicious software
 Access Control  → Restricts user permissions and privileges
 Monitoring      → Tracks network activity for suspicious behavior
</pre>
<h3> Explanation of Key Methods</h3>
<ul>
  <li><b>Firewall:</b> Filters incoming and outgoing network traffic to block threats</li>
  <li><b>Encryption:</b> Converts data into a secret code that can only be read with a key</li>
  <li><b>Authentication:</b> Confirms user identity before granting access</li>
  <li><b>Antivirus:</b> Scans and removes malicious programs from a computer</li>
  <li><b>Access Control:</b> Limits what users can access in a network system</li>
</ul>
<h3> Sniffing vs Spoofing</h3>
<ul>  <li><b>Sniffing:</b> Secretly capturing and analyzing data traveling across a network without permission</li>
  <li><b>Spoofing:</b> Pretending to be a trusted device, user, or website to trick systems into granting access</li>
</ul>
<h3> Importance of Network Security</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Protects sensitive and confidential information</li>
  <li>Prevents hacking, sniffing, and spoofing attacks</li>
  <li>Ensures privacy of users and organizations</li>
  <li>Maintains system reliability and trust</li>
  <li>Protects financial and personal data</li>
</ul>
<h3> Simple Explanation</h3>
<p>
  Network security is like a smart security system that uses locks, guards, and cameras
  to stop intruders, detect spies, and prevent people from pretending to be someone else.
</p>
  ', 1),
  (2093, 2093, '<h2>Networking Hardware</h2>
  <p>
  Networking hardware refers to physical devices used to connect computers together
  so that they can communicate and share resources such as files, printers, and internet access.
</p>
<h3> Key Idea</h3>
<p>
  Networking hardware acts as the communication bridge between computers,
  controlling how data is sent, received, and directed across a network efficiently and securely.
</p>
<h3> Types of Networking Hardware</h3>
<pre>
 NIC (Network Interface Card) → Connects a computer to a network
 Hub                         → Sends data to all connected devices
 Switch                      → Sends data only to the correct device
 Router                      → Connects different networks and provides internet access
 Bridge                      → Connects two similar networks and filters traffic
 Brouter                     → Combines the functions of a bridge and a router
 Access Point               → Allows wireless devices to connect to a network
 Modem                       → Converts signals for internet communication
</pre>
<h3> Functions of Networking Devices</h3>
<ul>
  <li><b>NIC:</b> Allows a computer to connect to a network and communicate with other devices</li>
  <li><b>Hub:</b> Broadcasts data to all connected devices (simple but inefficient)</li>
  <li><b>Switch:</b> Sends data only to the intended device using MAC addresses (efficient)</li>
  <li><b>Router:</b> Connects different networks and directs data between them (e.g., LAN to internet)</li>
  <li><b>Bridge:</b> Connects two separate but similar networks and reduces traffic by filtering data</li>
  <li><b>Brouter:</b> Combines the functions of a bridge and a router; routes some data and bridges others</li>
  <li><b>Modem:</b> Converts digital signals to allow internet communication</li>
  <li><b>Access Point:</b> Provides wireless connectivity for Wi-Fi devices</li>
</ul>
<h3> Key Differences</h3>
<ul>
  <li><b>Hub:</b> Sends data to all devices (no filtering)</li>
  <li><b>Switch:</b> Sends data only to the correct device (uses MAC address)</li>
  <li><b>Bridge:</b> Connects two LANs and filters traffic between them</li>
  <li><b>Router:</b> Connects different networks and routes data using IP addresses</li>
  <li><b>Brouter:</b> Works as both a bridge and a router depending on the data type</li>
</ul>
<h3> Advantages of Networking Hardware</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Enables sharing of files and resources</li>
  <li>Allows multiple devices to access the internet</li>
  <li>Improves communication between computers</li>
  <li>Reduces cost through shared hardware like printers</li>
</ul>
<h3> Simple Explanation</h3>
<p>
  Networking hardware is like a system of roads and traffic controllers that helps computers
  send, receive, and manage information across different networks efficiently.
</p>
  ', 1),
  (2094, 2094, '<h2>Word Processing </h2>
 <p>
  Word processing refers to the use of computer software to create, edit, format, store,
  and print text-based documents such as letters, reports, CVs, essays, and official documents.
</p>
<h3> Key Idea</h3>
<p>
  Word processing software allows users to produce well-structured and professional documents quickly,
  with the ability to edit, correct, and improve content easily without rewriting everything.
</p>
<h3> Examples of Word Processing Software</h3>
<pre>
 Microsoft Word        → Most widely used word processor
 Google Docs          → Online collaborative document editor
 LibreOffice Writer   → Free offline word processing software
 WPS Writer           → Lightweight and easy-to-use word processor
</pre>
<h3> Key Features of Word Processing</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Typing, editing, and deleting text</li>
  <li>Text formatting (bold, italics, underline, font size, color)</li>
  <li>Page layout control (margins, orientation, spacing)</li>
  <li>Spell check and grammar correction tools</li>
  <li>Insertion of images, tables, shapes, and charts</li>
  <li>Find and replace text</li>
  <li>Saving and exporting files (DOCX, PDF, TXT)</li>
  <li>Printing documents professionally</li>
</ul>
<h3> Keyboard Shortcuts in Word Processing</h3>
<pre>
Ctrl + C   → Copy selected text
Ctrl + V   → Paste copied text
Ctrl + X   → Cut selected text
Ctrl + Z   → Undo last action
Ctrl + Y   → Redo last action
Ctrl + B   → Make text Bold
Ctrl + I   → Make text Italic
Ctrl + U   → Underline text
Ctrl + A   → Select all text in the document
Ctrl + S   → Save the document
Ctrl + P   → Print document
Ctrl + F   → Find a word or phrase in the document
Ctrl + H   → Find and replace text
Ctrl + N   → Create a new document
Ctrl + O   → Open an existing document
Ctrl + L   → Align text to the left
Ctrl + E   → Center align text
Ctrl + R   → Align text to the right
Ctrl + J   → Justify text (align both sides)
</pre>
<h3> Advanced Features</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Track changes for editing review</li>
  <li>Comments for collaboration</li>
  <li>Templates for CVs, letters, and reports</li>
  <li>Auto-save and cloud storage integration</li>
  <li>Mail merge for sending bulk personalized documents</li>
</ul>

<h3> Advantages of Word Processing</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Saves time compared to handwriting</li>
  <li>Allows easy editing without rewriting the whole document</li>
  <li>Produces neat, professional, and readable documents</li>
  <li>Enables easy sharing via email or cloud storage</li>
  <li>Reduces paper usage through digital storage</li>
</ul>

<h3> Disadvantages</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Requires electricity and a computer/device</li>
  <li>Can be affected by software crashes or data loss</li>
  <li>Requires basic computer skills to use effectively</li>
  <li>May lead to over-reliance on auto-correction tools</li>
</ul>

<h3> Simple Explanation</h3>
<p>
  Word processing is like a powerful digital typewriter that allows you to write, edit, format,
  and improve documents easily and professionally using a computer.
</p>
  ', 1),
  (2095, 2095, '<h2>Spreadsheets</h2>
  <p>
  Spreadsheets are application programs used to organize, store, and analyze data
  in rows and columns. They also allow automatic calculations using formulas and functions.
</p>
<h3> Key Idea</h3>
<p>
  A spreadsheet is like a digital worksheet where data is arranged in rows and columns,
  and calculations are performed automatically using formulas.
</p>
<h3> Examples of Spreadsheet Software</h3>
<pre>
 Microsoft Excel   → Most popular spreadsheet program
 Google Sheets     → Online spreadsheet application
 LibreOffice Calc  → Free spreadsheet software
 Apple Numbers     → Spreadsheet for Apple devices
</pre>
<h3> Structure of a Spreadsheet</h3>

<div style="background: linear-gradient(145deg, #131326, #1c1c36); border: 1px solid #323254; border-radius: 14px; padding: 16px; margin: 20px 0; box-shadow: 0 8px 25px rgba(0,0,0,0.45); text-align: center;">
  <img src="/images/spreadsheet_screenshot.png" alt="Real World Spreadsheet Interface Screenshot" style="width: 100%; max-width: 680px; height: auto; border-radius: 10px; border: 1px solid #323560; box-shadow: 0 4px 20px rgba(0,0,0,0.6); display: block; margin: 0 auto 10px auto; object-fit: contain;" />
  <p style="font-size:0.85em; color:#a0a0b0; text-align:center; margin-top:10px; margin-bottom:0;"><b>Figure:</b> Real World Spreadsheet Interface Screenshot (showing Formula Bar, Rows, Columns, and Data Cells)</p>
</div>

<ul>
  <li><b>Rows:</b> Horizontal lines identified by numbers (1, 2, 3...)</li>
  <li><b>Columns:</b> Vertical lines identified by letters (A, B, C...)</li>
  <li><b>Cells:</b> Intersection of a row and column (e.g. A1, B2)</li>
</ul>
<h3> Explanation of Spreadsheet Parts</h3>
<ul>
  <li><b>Rows:</b> Horizontal sections identified by numbers</li>
  <li><b>Columns:</b> Vertical sections identified by letters</li>
  <li><b>Cells:</b> Boxes where data is entered</li>
  <li><b>Cell Address:</b> Name of a cell formed using column letter and row number (e.g., B3)</li>
  <li><b>Formula Bar:</b> Area used to enter or edit formulas</li>
  <li><b>Worksheet:</b> One page inside a spreadsheet file</li>
</ul>
<h3> Data Types in Spreadsheets</h3>
<pre>
Text     → Names or words
Numbers  → Values used in calculations
Dates    → Calendar dates
Formulas → Instructions for calculations
</pre>
<h3> Key Features</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Organizing data into tables</li>
  <li>Automatic calculations using formulas</li>
  <li>Sorting data alphabetically or numerically</li>
  <li>Filtering important information</li>
  <li>Creating charts and graphs</li>
  <li>Data analysis and reporting tools</li>
</ul>
<h3> Important Formulas</h3>
<pre>
=SUM(A1:A10)         → Adds values in a range
=AVERAGE(A1:A10)     → Calculates the mean
=MAX(A1:A10)         → Finds highest value
=MIN(A1:A10)         → Finds lowest value
=COUNT(A1:A10)       → Counts numeric entries
=COUNTA(A1:A10)      → Counts all non-empty cells
=COUNTBLANK(A1:A10)  → Counts empty cells
=IF(A1>50,"Pass","Fail") → Logical decision test
=IFS(A1>80,"A",A1>60,"B",A1>40,"C") → Multiple conditions
=SUMIF(A1:A10,">50") → Adds values greater than 50
=COUNTIF(A1:A10,">50") → Counts values greater than 50
=AVERAGEIF(A1:A10,">50") → Average of values above condition
</pre>
<h3> Formula Example</h3>

<div style="background:#131326; border:1px solid #2a2a48; border-radius:12px; padding:16px; margin:20px 0; box-shadow: 0 4px 20px rgba(0,0,0,0.4);">
  <div style="display:flex; align-items:center; background:#1c1c36; padding:8px 12px; border-radius:8px; margin-bottom:12px; border:1px solid #323254; font-family:monospace; font-size:0.9em; color:#00e5ff;">
    <span style="color:#ff007f; font-weight:bold; margin-right:12px;">fx</span>
    <span style="color:#00ffcc; font-weight:bold;">=SUM(B1:B3) &nbsp;➔ &nbsp;Result: 240</span>
  </div>
  <div style="overflow-x:auto;">
    <table style="width:100%; border-collapse:collapse; text-align:center; font-family:''Segoe UI'', Roboto, sans-serif; font-size:0.9em; color:#e0e0e0;">
      <thead>
        <tr style="background:#22223e; color:#00e5ff;">
          <th style="border:1px solid #323254; padding:8px; width:40px; background:#16162d; color:#888;">#</th>
          <th style="border:1px solid #323254; padding:8px; min-width:100px;">A</th>
          <th style="border:1px solid #323254; padding:8px; min-width:100px;">B</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td style="border:1px solid #323254; padding:8px; background:#16162d; color:#888; font-weight:bold;">1</td>
          <td style="border:1px solid #323254; padding:8px;">Math</td>
          <td style="border:1px solid #323254; padding:8px; color:#ffdd57; font-weight:bold;">80</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td style="border:1px solid #323254; padding:8px; background:#16162d; color:#888; font-weight:bold;">2</td>
          <td style="border:1px solid #323254; padding:8px;">English</td>
          <td style="border:1px solid #323254; padding:8px; color:#ffdd57; font-weight:bold;">70</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td style="border:1px solid #323254; padding:8px; background:#16162d; color:#888; font-weight:bold;">3</td>
          <td style="border:1px solid #323254; padding:8px;">Science</td>
          <td style="border:1px solid #323254; padding:8px; color:#ffdd57; font-weight:bold;">90</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="background:rgba(0, 229, 255, 0.1);">
          <td style="border:1px solid #323254; padding:8px; background:#16162d; color:#888; font-weight:bold;">4</td>
          <td style="border:1px solid #323254; padding:8px; font-weight:bold; color:#00e5ff;">Total (=SUM)</td>
          <td style="border:1px solid #323254; padding:8px; font-weight:bold; color:#00ffcc; font-size:1.05em; border:2px solid #00ffcc;">240</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p style="font-size:0.85em; color:#a0a0b0; text-align:center; margin-top:10px; margin-bottom:0;"><b>Figure:</b> Spreadsheet SUM Formula Result Grid</p>
</div>
<h3> Uses of Spreadsheets</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Keeping school marks and records</li>
  <li>Business accounting and budgeting</li>
  <li>Creating timetables and schedules</li>
  <li>Data analysis and reporting</li>
  <li>Inventory and stock management</li>
</ul>
<h3> Advantages</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Saves time by automating calculations</li>
  <li>Reduces human errors in mathematics</li>
  <li>Makes data analysis easier</li>
  <li>Helps in decision making using charts</li>
  <li>Stores large amounts of information neatly</li>
</ul>
<h3> Disadvantages</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Incorrect formulas can produce wrong results</li>
  <li>Large spreadsheets may become difficult to manage</li>
  <li>Requires computer knowledge to use advanced features</li>
  <li>Data can be lost if not saved properly</li>
</ul>
<h3> Simple Explanation</h3>
<p>
  A spreadsheet is like a smart digital table that stores information
  and performs calculations automatically when data is entered.
</p>
', 1),
  (2096, 2096, '<h2>Presentations</h2>
  <p>
