  (3953, 2903, 'A liquid can flow because its particles move past one another. A solid normally retains its shape because its particles vibrate about relatively fixed positions.', TRUE),
  (3954, 2904, 'Cooking oil has a definite volume but no definite shape. It spreads because its particles can move past one another, not because it lacks a definite volume.', TRUE),
  (3955, 2905, 'A liquid flows because its particles can move past one another. It has a definite volume because its particles remain relatively close together.', TRUE),
  (3956, 2906, 'A gas is a state of matter with neither a definite shape nor a definite volume because its particles move freely and spread to fill the available space.', TRUE),
  (3957, 2907, 'The decrease in volume is 300 cm³.', TRUE),
  (3958, 2908, 'A gas is more compressible because its particles are widely separated, leaving substantial space that can be reduced by applying pressure. Solid particles are closely packed.', TRUE),
  (3959, 2909, 'The gas can occupy the larger space because its particles move freely and spread throughout the available volume.', TRUE),
  (3960, 2910, 'A gas occupies the available space and can expand if more space is provided. A fixed amount of liquid retains approximately the same volume under ordinary conditions while changing shape to fit its container.', TRUE),
  (3961, 2911, 'Particle arrangement is how particles are positioned relative to one another. In a typical solid, particles are closely packed and occupy relatively fixed positions.', TRUE),
  (3962, 2912, 'Liquid particles are relatively close together in a changeable arrangement, while gas particles are widely separated and distributed throughout the available space.', TRUE),
  (3963, 2913, 'The likely state is a gas because its particles are widely spaced and spread throughout the available space.', TRUE),
  (3964, 2914, 'Both solids and liquids have closely packed particles, but solid particles occupy relatively fixed positions while liquid particles can move past one another. Therefore, a solid retains its shape, whereas a liquid takes the shape of its container.', TRUE),
  (3965, 2915, 'The model is too restrictive because not all solids have a perfectly regular particle arrangement. Some solids have irregular arrangements, although their particles remain closely packed.', TRUE),
  (3966, 2916, 'Particles in a solid vibrate about relatively fixed positions. A solid can appear motionless because it is not moving as a whole, even though its particles continue vibrating.', TRUE),
  (3967, 2917, 'Liquid particles can move past one another, allowing the liquid to flow and take the shape of its container.', TRUE),
  (3968, 2918, 'The molecules responsible for the smell move and spread through the air by diffusion, eventually reaching other parts of the room.', TRUE),
  (3969, 2919, 'Solid particles vibrate about relatively fixed positions. Liquid particles move past one another while remaining relatively close together. Gas particles move freely in different directions throughout the available space.', TRUE),
  (3970, 2920, 'Particle arrangement describes how particles are positioned relative to one another. Particle movement describes how they move. In a solid, particles are closely packed in relatively fixed positions, but they still vibrate about those positions.', TRUE),
  (3971, 2921, 'The particles generally gain average kinetic energy and vibrate more vigorously about their relatively fixed positions.', TRUE),
  (3972, 2922, 'The average kinetic energy decreases, and the particles move more slowly on average.', TRUE),
  (3973, 2923, 'The statement is incorrect because temperature is related to average kinetic energy, not to every particle having exactly the same kinetic energy. Individual particles can have different speeds and kinetic energies.', TRUE),
  (3974, 2924, 'The average translational kinetic energy doubles, a factor of 2.', TRUE),
  (3975, 2925, 'Temperature describes the thermal state of a substance and is related to average particle kinetic energy. Heating is energy transferred because of a temperature difference. When heating raises the temperature without a change of state, particles generally move more vigorously: solid particles vibrate more, liquid particles move around more vigorously, and gas particles move faster on average.', TRUE),
  (3976, 2926, 'Melting is the change of state in which a solid changes into a liquid after absorbing sufficient thermal energy.', TRUE),
  (3977, 2927, 'Its melting point is approximately 0°C at standard atmospheric pressure: the temperature at which solid ice changes into liquid water.', TRUE),
  (3978, 2928, 'The supplied energy is used to change the solid into a liquid by overcoming forces maintaining the solid arrangement. Therefore, the temperature remains constant during equilibrium melting.', TRUE),
  (3979, 2929, 'The statement is incorrect because different substances have different melting points. Pure ice melts at approximately 0°C at standard atmospheric pressure, whereas iron melts at a much higher temperature.', TRUE),
  (3980, 2930, 'The particles initially vibrate more vigorously as the solid warms. During melting, supplied energy helps overcome forces holding them in their fixed arrangement, allowing them to move past one another in the liquid.', TRUE),
  (3981, 2931, 'Freezing is the change of state in which a liquid loses thermal energy and changes into a solid.', TRUE),
  (3982, 2932, 'The freezing point of pure water is approximately 0°C at standard atmospheric pressure. It is the temperature at which liquid water changes into solid ice under those conditions.', TRUE),
  (3983, 2933, 'As the liquid cools, its particles generally lose kinetic energy. During freezing, they form a solid arrangement and thereafter vibrate about relatively fixed positions.', TRUE),
  (3984, 2934, 'The learner is incorrect because different substances have different freezing points. A pure substance can freeze at 65°C under the stated pressure conditions even though pure water freezes at approximately 0°C at standard atmospheric pressure.', TRUE),
  (3985, 2935, 'Freezing water is a physical change because liquid water becomes solid ice without changing into a different chemical substance. Both are H₂O.', TRUE),
  (3986, 2936, 'Evaporation is the process in which particles escape from the surface of a liquid and enter the gaseous state.', TRUE),
  (3987, 2937, 'Water evaporates because some surface particles have enough energy to escape into the air. Evaporation can occur below the boiling point, so boiling is unnecessary.', TRUE),
  (3988, 2938, 'Dish A is likely to lose water faster because its larger exposed surface allows more particles to escape at the same time.', TRUE),
  (3989, 2939, 'Increasing temperature generally increases the rate of evaporation because more surface particles have sufficient energy to escape into the gaseous state.', TRUE),
  (3990, 2940, 'Evaporation can cause cooling because escaping particles carry energy away from the liquid or skin, reducing the thermal energy remaining there.', TRUE),
  (3991, 2941, 'Heat the salt solution in an evaporating dish so that the water escapes as vapour, leaving solid salt behind.', TRUE),
  (3992, 2942, 'The volume decreases because water escapes as vapour. If enough water is removed, solid salt remains.', TRUE),
  (3993, 2943, 'The water in the wide dish is expected to evaporate faster because it has a larger exposed surface area.', TRUE),
  (3994, 2944, 'Ordinary evaporation can leave the salt behind, but the water escapes as vapour and is not collected.', TRUE),
  (3995, 2945, 'Increasing the temperature generally increases the rate of evaporation because more water particles have enough energy to escape from the surface.', TRUE),
  (3996, 2946, 'Boiling is the rapid vaporisation of a liquid that occurs throughout the liquid when its vapour pressure equals the external pressure.', TRUE),
  (3997, 2947, 'The boiling point of pure water is approximately 100°C at standard atmospheric pressure. At this temperature, vapour bubbles form throughout the liquid as water boils.', TRUE),
  (3998, 2948, 'The energy supplied is used for vaporisation rather than increasing the temperature. Therefore, the temperature remains constant while the pure liquid boils at constant external pressure under equilibrium conditions.', TRUE),
  (3999, 2949, 'Boiling occurs throughout a liquid, with vapour bubbles forming at the boiling point for the external pressure. Evaporation occurs at the surface and can happen below the boiling point.', TRUE),
  (4000, 2950, 'At high altitude, the external atmospheric pressure is lower. Water therefore reaches the condition for boiling at a lower temperature than it does at standard atmospheric pressure.', TRUE);
INSERT INTO public.answers (id, question_id, answer_text, is_correct) VALUES
  (4001, 2951, 'Condensation is the change of state from gas to liquid.', TRUE),
  (4002, 2952, 'The droplets form when water vapour in the surrounding air loses thermal energy near the cold can and changes into liquid water.', TRUE),
  (4003, 2953, 'The particles lose kinetic energy on average, and they come closer together as the gas changes into a liquid.', TRUE),
  (4004, 2954, 'Water vapour in the air loses thermal energy at the cold outside surface and condenses into liquid droplets.', TRUE),
  (4005, 2955, 'When moist air cools sufficiently, water vapour loses thermal energy and can change into liquid water droplets.', TRUE),
  (4006, 2956, 'Sublimation is the direct change of a solid into a gas without first becoming a liquid.', TRUE),
  (4007, 2957, 'Solid carbon dioxide changes directly into gaseous carbon dioxide. This is sublimation because it does not first become liquid under ordinary atmospheric pressure.', TRUE),
  (4008, 2958, 'The solid iodine changes directly into gaseous iodine vapour. This is sublimation because the change occurs without first forming liquid iodine.', TRUE),
  (4009, 2959, 'Sublimation is occurring. One example is naphthalene gradually passing from solid to vapour under suitable conditions.', TRUE),
  (4010, 2960, 'The conclusion is incorrect because sublimation is a physical change. A substance changes directly from solid to gas without necessarily forming a new substance.', TRUE),
  (4011, 2961, 'Deposition is the direct change of a gas into a solid without first becoming a liquid.', TRUE),
  (4012, 2962, 'Water vapour loses thermal energy at the cold surface and changes directly into solid ice crystals. This process is deposition.', TRUE),
  (4013, 2963, 'It must be established that the gas changed directly into a solid without first becoming a liquid.', TRUE),
  (4014, 2964, 'The gas transfers thermal energy to its surroundings. Its particles lose kinetic energy on average, and the substance changes directly into a solid under suitable conditions.', TRUE),
  (4015, 2965, 'The explanation is not always correct because water vapour can change directly into solid ice without first becoming liquid. This direct change is deposition.', TRUE),
  (4016, 2966, 'An element is a pure substance containing only one type of atom. It cannot be broken down into simpler substances by ordinary chemical methods because it is already a single chemical element.', TRUE),
  (4017, 2967, 'O₂ is an element because each molecule contains two oxygen atoms and no atoms of another element.', TRUE),
  (4018, 2968, 'Hydrogen is H, oxygen is O, sodium is Na, and iron is Fe.', TRUE),
  (4019, 2969, 'The sample is an element because it contains only copper atoms. The chemical symbol for copper is Cu.', TRUE),
  (4020, 2970, 'Co is the symbol for cobalt. CO is a chemical formula containing carbon and oxygen, representing carbon monoxide.', TRUE),
  (4021, 2971, 'A compound is a pure substance formed when atoms of two or more different elements are chemically combined in fixed proportions.', TRUE),
  (4022, 2972, 'H₂O represents water containing hydrogen and oxygen in a 2:1 atomic ratio in each molecule. Water is a compound because these different elements are chemically bonded.', TRUE),
  (4023, 2973, 'There is 1 carbon atom and 2 oxygen atoms, so the carbon-to-oxygen atomic ratio is 1:2. Carbon dioxide is a compound because carbon and oxygen are chemically combined.', TRUE),
  (4024, 2974, 'Filtration cannot separate water into hydrogen and oxygen because they are chemically combined in water molecules. A suitable chemical process is required to decompose water.', TRUE),
  (4025, 2975, 'Sodium and chlorine chemically combine to form sodium chloride, a new substance with its own chemical properties that differ from those of sodium and chlorine.', TRUE),
  (4026, 2976, 'A mixture contains two or more substances physically combined without being chemically combined into one new compound. Its component proportions can vary because the substances can be mixed in different amounts.', TRUE),
  (4027, 2977, 'Salt water is a mixture because sodium chloride dissolves in water without the two substances becoming one new compound. The amount of salt dissolved can vary.', TRUE),
  (4028, 2978, 'Air is a mixture because it contains several different gases that retain their chemical identities, and the proportions of some components, such as water vapour, can vary.', TRUE),
  (4029, 2979, 'The material is a mixture because iron and sand are physically combined and retain their identities. A magnet can separate the iron filings from the sand.', TRUE),
  (4030, 2980, 'Use filtration. Sand remains on the filter as the residue, while water passes through as the filtrate.', TRUE),
  (4031, 2981, 'A homogeneous mixture is a mixture with uniform composition throughout the sample. Equal portions taken from different places have the same composition, within ordinary measurement limits.', TRUE),
  (4032, 2982, 'Sodium chloride is the solute, and water is the solvent. The solution is homogeneous because the dissolved salt is distributed uniformly throughout the water.', TRUE),
  (4033, 2983, 'The two portions should have the same sugar concentration, within ordinary measurement limits, because the sugar is uniformly distributed throughout the solution.', TRUE),
  (4034, 2984, 'The concentration decreases because the same amount of dissolved salt is distributed through a larger amount of solution. The solution can remain homogeneous if the salt stays dissolved and is uniformly distributed.', TRUE),
  (4035, 2985, 'Air contains several gases, such as nitrogen and oxygen, distributed uniformly in a well-mixed sample. It is therefore a homogeneous mixture at the scale being considered.', TRUE),
  (4036, 2986, 'A heterogeneous mixture is a mixture whose composition is not uniform throughout the sample. Different portions can contain different proportions of its components.', TRUE),
  (4037, 2987, 'The mixture is heterogeneous because the upper portion is mainly water while the bottom contains accumulated sand. The components are not distributed uniformly throughout the sample.', TRUE),
  (4038, 2988, 'One layer is mainly oil and the other is mainly water. Since the layers have different compositions, the sample is a heterogeneous mixture.', TRUE),
  (4039, 2989, 'Different regions may contain different types and proportions of mineral grains. Granite is therefore a heterogeneous mixture because its composition is not uniform throughout.', TRUE),
  (4040, 2990, 'No. A heterogeneous mixture has a non-uniform composition, but its different components or regions do not always have to be distinguishable with the naked eye.', TRUE),
  (4041, 2991, 'A pure substance consists of only one chemical substance with a definite composition. It can be an element, which contains one type of atom, or a compound, which contains different elements chemically combined in fixed proportions.', TRUE),
  (4042, 2992, 'The sample is pure because it contains only water. It is not an element because each H₂O molecule contains hydrogen and oxygen chemically combined.', TRUE),
  (4043, 2993, 'No. The liquid is a mixture of water and dissolved salt, even if it looks clear and colourless.', TRUE),
  (4044, 2994, 'A broad melting range may suggest that the sample contains impurities or is a mixture. However, the observation alone does not prove its composition or identity.', TRUE),
  (4045, 2995, 'The statement is incorrect. Pure water is a pure substance because it contains only H₂O, but it is a compound because each molecule contains hydrogen and oxygen.', TRUE),
  (4046, 2996, 'Evidence includes finding two or more substances in the sample, observing that their proportions can vary, and showing that the components can be separated by a suitable physical method.', TRUE),
  (4047, 2997, 'Both solutions contain sodium chloride and water. Their different concentrations show that the proportions can vary, so both are mixtures.', TRUE),
  (4048, 2998, 'The original sample is a mixture because it contains iron and sand as separate substances. Their different magnetic properties allow the iron to be removed without chemically decomposing a compound.', TRUE),
  (4049, 2999, 'The liquid is a mixture because it contains both water and dissolved salt. Its uniform, clear appearance does not make it a pure substance.', TRUE),
  (4050, 3000, 'A homogeneous mixture has uniform composition throughout the sample, while a heterogeneous mixture has non-uniform composition. Both are mixtures because each contains two or more substances physically combined.', TRUE),
  (4051, 3001, 'Residue: sand. Filtrate: water.', TRUE),
  (4052, 3002, 'The chalk powder remains as the residue, while the water forms the filtrate.', TRUE),
  (4053, 3003, 'Filtration cannot separate dissolved salt from the solution because the dissolved salt passes through the filter paper with the water.', TRUE),
  (4054, 3004, 'The solid is the residue, and the collected liquid is the filtrate.', TRUE),
  (4055, 3005, 'Place filter paper in a funnel above a receiving container, then pour the mixture through it. The insoluble solid remains as the residue, while the water collects as the filtrate.', TRUE),
  (4056, 3006, 'Concentrate the solution by evaporating some solvent, allow it to cool so crystals form, then collect and dry the crystals.', TRUE),
  (4057, 3007, 'Cooling may decrease the solubility of the solid, causing excess dissolved substance to form crystals.', TRUE),
  (4058, 3008, 'The solution may be too dilute. Carefully evaporate some solvent to concentrate it, then allow it to cool.', TRUE),
  (4059, 3009, 'Crystallisation obtains crystals by creating suitable conditions for crystal formation; evaporation to dryness removes nearly all the solvent and leaves the solid behind.', TRUE),
  (4060, 3010, 'Heating to dryness may prevent controlled crystal formation and may damage a heat-sensitive substance. The solution should be concentrated carefully and then cooled to form crystals.', TRUE),
  (4061, 3011, 'Heat the salt solution, pass the water vapour through a cooled condenser, and collect the condensed water as the distillate.', TRUE),
  (4062, 3012, 'The distillate is water. The salt remains in the distillation flask because it does not normally vaporise with the water under these conditions.', TRUE),
  (4063, 3013, 'The condenser cools vapour and changes it into liquid. If it is not cooled adequately, condensation may be incomplete and less liquid will be collected.', TRUE),
  (4064, 3014, 'Evaporation in an open dish leaves salt behind but does not normally collect the water. Simple distillation condenses the water vapour and collects the water as a liquid.', TRUE),
  (4065, 3015, 'Distillation flask — holds the solution; heat source — vaporises the liquid; condenser — cools vapour and condenses it; receiving container — collects the distillate.', TRUE),
  (4066, 3016, 'Fractional distillation is generally suitable because the liquids are miscible and their boiling points are close together.', TRUE),
  (4067, 3017, 'The liquid with a boiling point of 60°C is more volatile and is generally enriched in the early vapour.', TRUE),
  (4068, 3018, 'The column provides surfaces for condensation and allows repeated vaporisation and condensation, improving separation of the components.', TRUE),
  (4069, 3019, 'The boiling points may be very close, the column may not provide sufficient separation, or the apparatus may be operated under unsuitable conditions.', TRUE),
  (4070, 3020, 'The condenser cools vapour into liquid, and the receiving container collects the condensed fraction.', TRUE),
  (4071, 3021, 'The ink contains at least four detectable components under the conditions used, but the spots alone do not identify every substance or prove the sample contains exactly four substances.', TRUE),
  (4072, 3022, 'A pencil baseline is used because graphite generally does not dissolve and form extra spots, whereas ordinary ink may interfere with the chromatogram.', TRUE),
  (4073, 3023, 'The sample may wash into the solvent reservoir. The baseline must be above the solvent level before the experiment begins.', TRUE),
  (4074, 3024, 'The matching positions suggest the components may be the same substance, but the match is not conclusive because different substances can sometimes behave similarly under the same conditions.', TRUE),
  (4075, 3025, 'Draw a pencil baseline, apply the dye spot, place the paper in a suitable solvent with the baseline above the solvent, allow separation to occur, then remove the paper and examine the spots.', TRUE),
  (4076, 3026, 'Allow the sand to settle, then carefully pour the water into another container while leaving the sand behind.', TRUE),
  (4077, 3027, 'Allow the layers to separate and carefully pour off the desired layer without disturbing the boundary between them.', TRUE),
  (4078, 3028, 'The separation was incomplete because some fine mud particles remained suspended and were poured off with the water.', TRUE),
  (4079, 3029, 'Advantage: it is simple and requires little apparatus. Limitation: fine suspended particles may remain in the collected liquid, making separation incomplete.', TRUE),
  (4080, 3030, 'The mixture is left undisturbed to allow sand particles to settle, making it easier to pour away the water without carrying away much sand.', TRUE),
  (4081, 3031, 'Bring a magnet close to the mixture to attract the iron filings, then remove the iron from the magnet, leaving the sulfur behind.', TRUE),
  (4082, 3032, 'The method works because the iron filings are attracted to the magnet while the sand is not.', TRUE),
  (4083, 3033, 'Neither sulfur nor ordinary sand is attracted strongly enough for an ordinary magnet to separate them selectively.', TRUE),
  (4084, 3034, 'The assumption is incorrect because not every metal is attracted strongly to an ordinary magnet. Aluminium and copper, for example, are not attracted in the same way as iron.', TRUE),
  (4085, 3035, 'Wrap the magnet in suitable thin paper before collecting the iron filings, then remove the paper to release the filings.', TRUE),
  (4086, 3036, 'Proton: +1, nucleus. Neutron: 0, nucleus. Electron: -1, energy levels around the nucleus.', TRUE),
  (4087, 3037, 'Net relative charge = 0; the atom is neutral.', TRUE),
  (4088, 3038, '+2.', TRUE),
  (4089, 3039, '+2.', TRUE),
  (4090, 3040, 'The atom remains neutral because its 19 protons and 19 electrons balance, regardless of its neutron number.', TRUE),
  (4091, 3041, 'The particle has charge +1, so it is not neutral.', TRUE),
  (4092, 3042, '20 electrons.', TRUE),
  (4093, 3043, '13 electrons.', TRUE),
  (4094, 3044, 'The electron number increases by one and the charge becomes -1 if the original atom was neutral. The proton number, neutron number and element identity remain unchanged.', TRUE),
  (4095, 3045, 'A proton is a positively charged subatomic particle in the nucleus, with relative charge +1 and relative mass approximately 1.', TRUE),
  (4096, 3046, 'Atomic number = 12; element = magnesium (Mg).', TRUE),
  (4097, 3047, 'It cannot be carbon because it has 8 protons; it is oxygen.', TRUE),
  (4098, 3048, 'Aluminium; 13 electrons.', TRUE),
  (4099, 3049, 'X is sulfur; Y is chlorine. They are different elements because their proton numbers differ.', TRUE),
