  (4635, 3585, 'CuO + H2SO4 → CuSO4 + H2O', TRUE),
  (4636, 3586, 'MgO + 2HCl → MgCl2 + H2O', TRUE),
  (4637, 3587, 'The salt is zinc sulfate. ZnO + H2SO4 → ZnSO4 + H2O.', TRUE),
  (4638, 3588, 'A basic metal oxide reacts with an acid to form a salt and water. A metal carbonate reacts with an acid to form a salt, water and carbon dioxide. Carbon dioxide is the additional product in the carbonate reaction.', TRUE),
  (4639, 3589, 'A base is a substance that neutralises an acid.', TRUE),
  (4640, 3590, 'CuO + 2HCl → CuCl2 + H2O. Copper(II) oxide is a base because it neutralises the acid, forming a salt and water.', TRUE),
  (4641, 3591, 'MgO + H2SO4 → MgSO4 + H2O', TRUE),
  (4642, 3592, 'No. It can be a base, but an alkali must dissolve in water and produce an alkaline solution.', TRUE),
  (4643, 3593, 'A base neutralises an acid. An alkali is a base that dissolves in water to produce an alkaline solution.', TRUE),
  (4644, 3594, 'An alkali is a base that dissolves in water to produce an alkaline solution containing hydroxide ions, OH−.', TRUE),
  (4645, 3595, 'NaOH(s) → Na+(aq) + OH−(aq)', TRUE),
  (4646, 3596, 'The solution is alkaline because its pH is above 7.', TRUE),
  (4647, 3597, 'Aqueous potassium hydroxide conducts electricity because it contains mobile K+ and OH− ions that carry charge.', TRUE),
  (4648, 3598, 'An aqueous alkali turns red litmus blue because it is alkaline. It conducts electricity because it contains mobile ions. Its pH is above 7 under the usual school-level conditions.', TRUE),
  (4649, 3599, 'Litmus is an acid-base indicator used to distinguish acidic and alkaline solutions.', TRUE),
  (4650, 3600, 'The solution is acidic.', TRUE),
  (4651, 3601, 'The sample is alkaline because alkalis turn red litmus blue.', TRUE),
  (4652, 3602, 'The result is consistent with a neutral solution, but litmus cannot determine the exact pH.', TRUE),
  (4653, 3603, 'Litmus indicates whether a solution is acidic or alkaline but does not give a numerical pH. Therefore, it cannot reliably compare the acidity of two acidic solutions.', TRUE),
  (4654, 3604, 'Universal indicator is a mixture of indicators that changes colour across a range of pH values, helping estimate how acidic or alkaline a solution is.', TRUE),
  (4655, 3605, 'The sample is strongly acidic, according to the usual universal-indicator colour chart.', TRUE),
  (4656, 3606, 'The sample is approximately neutral, around pH 7.', TRUE),
  (4657, 3607, 'Sample B is more alkaline. Yellow typically indicates an acidic region, while blue indicates an alkaline region.', TRUE),
  (4658, 3608, 'Universal indicator estimates pH by colour comparison, so the result may be imprecise if the colours are difficult to distinguish or the sample is contaminated.', TRUE),
  (4659, 3609, 'Below 7 is acidic, 7 is neutral, and above 7 is alkaline.', TRUE),
  (4660, 3610, 'The solution is acidic because its pH is below 7.', TRUE),
  (4661, 3611, 'The pH 3 solution is more acidic. The difference is 5 − 3 = 2 pH units.', TRUE),
  (4662, 3612, 'A change of one pH unit corresponds to a tenfold change in hydrogen-ion activity. In dilute solutions, this is commonly approximated as a tenfold change in hydrogen-ion concentration.', TRUE),
  (4663, 3613, 'pH describes the acidity of a solution, not the total quantity of acid present. The total amount also depends on factors such as volume and the identity and concentration of the acid.', TRUE),
  (4664, 3614, 'pH 2, pH 4, pH 6.', TRUE),
  (4665, 3615, 'The difference is 2 pH units. The pH 4 solution has approximately 100 times the hydrogen-ion activity of the pH 6 solution.', TRUE),
  (4666, 3616, 'The pH has fallen by two units, so the solution has become more acidic. Its hydrogen-ion activity has increased by a factor of about 100.', TRUE),
  (4667, 3617, 'Solution B is more alkaline by 3 pH units.', TRUE),
  (4668, 3618, 'A one-unit decrease from pH 5 to pH 4 corresponds to about ten times the hydrogen-ion activity, not twice as much. pH alone also does not establish the total amount of acid present.', TRUE),
  (4669, 3619, 'A strong acid ionises essentially completely in water.', TRUE),
  (4670, 3620, 'Ethanoic acid is weak because it ionises only partially in water and establishes an equilibrium between molecules and ions.', TRUE),
  (4671, 3621, 'HCl(aq) → H+(aq) + Cl−(aq)', TRUE),
  (4672, 3622, 'CH3COOH(aq) ⇌ H+(aq) + CH3COO−(aq). The double arrow shows reversible partial ionisation and equilibrium.', TRUE),
  (4673, 3623, 'A strong acid ionises essentially completely in water. A concentrated acid contains a relatively large amount of dissolved acid per unit volume. These describe different properties.', TRUE),
  (4674, 3624, 'Sodium hydroxide is a strong base because it dissociates essentially completely in water to form Na+ and OH− ions: NaOH → Na+ + OH−.', TRUE),
  (4675, 3625, 'NH3(aq) + H2O(l) ⇌ NH4+(aq) + OH−(aq). The double arrow indicates a reversible reaction that establishes equilibrium.', TRUE),
  (4676, 3626, 'Sodium hydroxide is a strong base; aqueous ammonia is a weak base.', TRUE),
  (4677, 3627, 'Dilute describes the small amount of sodium hydroxide per unit volume. Sodium hydroxide remains a strong base because the dissolved compound dissociates essentially completely in water.', TRUE),
  (4678, 3628, 'Strength describes the extent of ion formation or reaction in water, while concentration describes the amount of dissolved substance per unit volume. A strong base can be dilute.', TRUE),
  (4679, 3629, 'Neutralisation is a reaction in which an acid reacts with a base. An acid reacting with a metal hydroxide usually forms a salt and water.', TRUE),
  (4680, 3630, 'HCl + NaOH → NaCl + H2O', TRUE),
  (4681, 3631, 'H2SO4 + 2KOH → K2SO4 + 2H2O', TRUE),
  (4682, 3632, 'H+(aq) + OH−(aq) → H2O(l)', TRUE),
  (4683, 3633, 'The final pH depends on the amounts and strengths of the acid and base and the reaction conditions. Excess acid can leave the mixture acidic, while excess base can leave it alkaline.', TRUE),
  (4684, 3634, 'A salt is an ionic compound made of positive and negative ions. A soluble salt dissolves in water to form a solution.', TRUE),
  (4685, 3635, 'Potassium nitrate; its ions are K⁺ and NO₃⁻.', TRUE),
  (4686, 3636, 'Sulfuric acid, H₂SO₄, because sulfuric acid forms sulfate salts.', TRUE),
  (4687, 3637, 'The correct name is sodium chloride because Na represents sodium.', TRUE),
  (4688, 3638, 'The tested salt is soluble under those conditions. It does not mean that all salts are soluble in water.', TRUE),
  (4689, 3639, 'An insoluble salt dissolves only to a very small extent in water under the stated conditions.', TRUE),
  (4690, 3640, 'Lead(II) iodide; it contains lead(II) ions and iodide ions.', TRUE),
  (4691, 3641, 'A precipitate is a solid that forms from a solution during a reaction.', TRUE),
  (4692, 3642, 'The statement is incorrect. For example, silver chloride (AgCl) is very sparingly soluble in water.', TRUE),
  (4693, 3643, 'Barium sulfate: Ba represents barium, and SO₄ represents sulfate.', TRUE),
  (4694, 3644, 'Reactive metal + dilute acid → salt + hydrogen gas.', TRUE),
  (4695, 3645, 'Mg + 2HCl → MgCl₂ + H₂.', TRUE),
  (4696, 3646, 'Excess metal helps ensure the acid is used up. The unreacted metal is then removed by filtration.', TRUE),
  (4697, 3647, 'Zinc sulfate; Zn + H₂SO₄ → ZnSO₄ + H₂.', TRUE),
  (4698, 3648, 'Gently concentrate the filtrate to the crystallisation point, cool it to form crystals, then separate and dry the crystals.', TRUE),
  (4699, 3649, 'Insoluble base + acid → salt + water.', TRUE),
  (4700, 3650, 'CuO + H₂SO₄ → CuSO₄ + H₂O.', TRUE),
  (4701, 3651, 'Excess base helps ensure that all the acid has reacted. The remaining base can then be filtered off.', TRUE),
  (4702, 3652, 'Magnesium chloride; MgO + 2HCl → MgCl₂ + H₂O.', TRUE),
  (4703, 3653, 'Concentrate the filtrate gently to the crystallisation point, cool it, then collect and dry the crystals.', TRUE),
  (4704, 3654, 'Carbonate + acid → salt + water + carbon dioxide.', TRUE),
  (4705, 3655, 'CuCO₃ + H₂SO₄ → CuSO₄ + H₂O + CO₂.', TRUE),
  (4706, 3656, 'Fizzing occurs because carbon dioxide gas is released.', TRUE),
  (4707, 3657, 'Zinc chloride; ZnCO₃ + 2HCl → ZnCl₂ + H₂O + CO₂.', TRUE),
  (4708, 3658, 'The excess carbonate is an undissolved solid, so filtration removes it while the dissolved salt remains in the filtrate.', TRUE),
  (4709, 3659, 'Titration is a laboratory method in which measured solutions are reacted to determine the volume of one solution required to react with a measured amount of another.', TRUE),
  (4710, 3660, 'Titre = 23.40 − 0.85 = 22.55 cm³.', TRUE),
  (4711, 3661, 'A pipette measures and transfers a fixed volume, such as 25.00 cm³, accurately into the conical flask.', TRUE),
  (4712, 3662, 'Mean titre = 21.70 cm³.', TRUE),
  (4713, 3663, 'The endpoint is the observed indicator colour change. The equivalence point is where the acid and base have reacted in the stoichiometric proportions required by the balanced equation. A suitable indicator helps the observed endpoint approximate the equivalence point.', TRUE),
  (4714, 3664, 'An exothermic reaction is a reaction that transfers energy from the reacting system to the surroundings.', TRUE),
  (4715, 3665, 'Temperature change = 31°C − 24°C = 7°C. The rise suggests that the reaction transferred energy to the surroundings and was exothermic, assuming the reaction caused the rise.', TRUE),
  (4716, 3666, 'Energy change = 305 kJ − 420 kJ = −115 kJ. The reaction is exothermic.', TRUE),
  (4717, 3667, 'Breaking bonds requires energy, so energy may be needed to initiate the reaction. Forming new bonds releases energy. In an exothermic reaction, bond formation releases more energy than bond breaking requires, so energy is released overall.', TRUE),
  (4718, 3668, 'The statement is incorrect. The products of an exothermic reaction have less energy than the reactants because the difference is released to the surroundings.', TRUE),
  (4719, 3669, 'Record the initial temperature, mix the reactants in a suitable container, and measure the temperature during and after the reaction. A rise in temperature supports the conclusion that the reaction is exothermic, provided other sources of heating are excluded.', TRUE),
  (4720, 3670, 'An endothermic reaction absorbs energy from the surroundings into the reacting system.', TRUE),
  (4721, 3671, 'Temperature change = 21°C − 30°C = −9°C. The mixture cooled by 9°C, which supports an endothermic classification if the reaction caused the cooling.', TRUE),
  (4722, 3672, 'Energy change = 415 kJ − 360 kJ = +55 kJ. The reaction is endothermic.', TRUE),
  (4723, 3673, 'Bond formation releases energy, but in an endothermic reaction the energy required to break bonds is greater than the energy released when new bonds form. The difference is absorbed from the surroundings.', TRUE),
  (4724, 3674, 'CaCO₃(s) → CaO(s) + CO₂(g). Heat must be supplied because the decomposition is endothermic under these conditions.', TRUE),
  (4725, 3675, 'Cooling can indicate an endothermic reaction, but it is not sufficient by itself to prove one. The temperature change should be measured and linked to the reaction, with other sources of cooling controlled or ruled out.', TRUE),
  (4726, 3676, 'The horizontal axis represents the progress of the reaction, while the vertical axis represents the energy of the reacting system. Reaction progress is not the same as elapsed time.', TRUE),
  (4727, 3677, 'Energy change = 215 kJ − 340 kJ = −125 kJ. The reaction is exothermic.', TRUE),
  (4728, 3678, 'Energy change = 235 kJ − 160 kJ = +75 kJ. The reaction is endothermic.', TRUE),
  (4729, 3679, 'Activation energy = 290 kJ − 115 kJ = 175 kJ.', TRUE),
  (4730, 3680, 'An exothermic reaction has products below the reactants in energy. An endothermic reaction has products above the reactants in energy.', TRUE),
  (4731, 3681, 'The learner has confused activation energy with the overall energy change. Activation energy is measured from the reactants'' energy level to the peak of the profile. The overall energy change is measured from the reactants'' level to the products'' level.', TRUE),
  (4732, 3682, 'Activation energy is the minimum energy that reacting particles must have for a successful reaction to occur through a particular reaction pathway.', TRUE),
  (4733, 3683, 'Activation energy = 225 kJ − 80 kJ = 145 kJ.', TRUE),
  (4734, 3684, 'The reacting particles must overcome the activation-energy barrier before the reaction can proceed. If the products have less energy than the reactants, the reaction releases energy overall even though the barrier must first be overcome.', TRUE),
  (4735, 3685, 'Difference = 120 kJ − 75 kJ = 45 kJ. Pathway B has the lower activation energy by 45 kJ.', TRUE),
  (4736, 3686, 'A catalyst provides an alternative pathway with lower activation energy. Under the same conditions, a greater proportion of particles can overcome the lower barrier, so the reaction can proceed faster.', TRUE),
  (4737, 3687, 'Activation energy = 265 kJ − 180 kJ = 85 kJ. Overall energy change = 130 kJ − 180 kJ = −50 kJ. The reaction is exothermic.', TRUE),
  (4738, 3688, 'Rate of reaction is the change in the amount of a reactant used up or product formed per unit time. A high rate means the measured chemical change occurs quickly.', TRUE),
  (4739, 3689, 'Average rate = 126 cm³ ÷ 42 s = 3 cm³/s.', TRUE),
  (4740, 3690, 'Rate A = 0.06 g/s. Rate B = 0.08 g/s. Reaction B has the higher average rate of mass loss.', TRUE),
  (4741, 3691, 'A gas syringe can measure the volume of gas collected at regular time intervals. Alternatively, a reaction vessel on a balance can be monitored as gas escapes; the decrease in mass is recorded at regular intervals.', TRUE),
  (4742, 3692, 'The method is incorrect because it does not divide a change in gas volume by a time interval. Average gas-production rate = Change in gas volume ÷ Time taken.', TRUE),
  (4743, 3693, 'First interval rate = 4 cm³/s. Second interval rate = 2 cm³/s. The average rate decreased in the second interval.', TRUE),
  (4744, 3694, 'Increasing temperature raises the average kinetic energy of particles. They move faster, and a greater proportion have enough energy to overcome the activation-energy barrier. More successful collisions occur per second, so the reaction rate generally increases.', TRUE),
  (4745, 3695, 'Rate at 25°C = 0.8 cm³/s. Rate at 45°C = 1.6 cm³/s. The second rate is twice the first.', TRUE),
  (4746, 3696, 'Use the same volume and concentration of acid and the same mass and surface area of magnesium. Set the acid to a chosen temperature, add the magnesium, and measure hydrogen volume at regular intervals. Repeat at another temperature under otherwise identical conditions and compare the average rates.', TRUE),
  (4747, 3697, 'Particles have a range of energies. At a higher temperature, the distribution shifts so that a greater proportion of particles have energy equal to or greater than the activation energy. Therefore, more collisions can lead to successful reactions.', TRUE),
  (4748, 3698, 'Temperature, acid concentration and magnesium surface area all affect reaction rate. Because three variables changed, the results cannot show the effect of temperature alone. Keep concentration and magnesium surface area constant and change only temperature.', TRUE),
  (4749, 3699, 'Rate ratio = (Q ÷ 40) ÷ (Q ÷ 80) = 80 ÷ 40 = 2. The average rate at the higher temperature is twice the lower-temperature rate.', TRUE),
  (4750, 3700, 'Concentration describes the amount of dissolved substance per unit volume of solution. A more concentrated solution contains more reactant particles per unit volume, so collisions between reacting particles generally occur more frequently and the rate increases.', TRUE);
INSERT INTO public.answers (id, question_id, answer_text, is_correct) VALUES
  (4751, 3701, 'C₂ = (1.5 × 40) ÷ 120 = 60 ÷ 120 = 0.50 mol dm⁻³.', TRUE),
  (4752, 3702, 'Dilute-acid rate = 1.4 cm³/s. Concentrated-acid rate = 2.1 cm³/s. Rate ratio = 2.1 ÷ 1.4 = 1.5.', TRUE),
  (4753, 3703, 'React equal volumes of hydrochloric acid at different known concentrations with identical masses and surface areas of magnesium at the same temperature. Measure the hydrogen volume at regular intervals and calculate or compare the rates.', TRUE),
  (4754, 3704, 'Doubling concentration increases the number of reactant particles per unit volume and generally increases the rate. However, the exact relationship depends on the reaction mechanism and its rate law, so the rate does not necessarily double.', TRUE),
  (4755, 3705, 'C₂ = (3.0 × 50) ÷ 150 = 1.0 mol dm⁻³. The amount of solute remains constant during dilution, but its concentration decreases because the solution volume increases.', TRUE),
  (4756, 3706, 'Powdered calcium carbonate has a greater total exposed surface area than the same mass in large chips. More of the solid is available to contact the acid, so more effective collisions can occur per second and the reaction generally proceeds faster.', TRUE),
  (4757, 3707, 'Chip rate = 0.6 cm³/s. Powder rate = 0.9 cm³/s. Rate ratio = 0.9 ÷ 0.6 = 1.5.', TRUE),
  (4758, 3708, 'React equal masses of calcium carbonate in different particle sizes with equal volumes of hydrochloric acid at the same concentration and temperature. Measure carbon dioxide volume at regular intervals and compare the average rates.', TRUE),
  (4759, 3709, 'Powdering the solid increases exposed surface area, but doubling the mass also increases the amount of reactant available. Since two variables changed, the effect cannot be attributed to surface area alone. Use equal masses and change only particle size.', TRUE),
  (4760, 3710, 'Powdering increases the exposed surface area and allows the reaction to proceed faster. However, the same mass of calcium carbonate contains the same number of moles. Since one mole of CaCO₃ produces one mole of CO₂, the theoretical amount of carbon dioxide remains the same when sufficient acid is available.', TRUE),
  (4761, 3711, 'First interval rate = 1.2 cm³/s. Second interval rate = 1.8 cm³/s. The rate was higher in the second interval in the stated measurements. These measurements alone do not prove a surface-area effect because the surface area was not compared with a controlled alternative, and other conditions or measurement effects may explain the difference.', TRUE),
  (4762, 3712, 'A catalyst is a substance that increases the rate of a reaction without being permanently consumed by the overall reaction. It provides an alternative pathway with lower activation energy, allowing a greater proportion of particles to react successfully per second.', TRUE),
  (4763, 3713, 'Reduction = 175 kJ − 105 kJ = 70 kJ.', TRUE),
  (4764, 3714, 'Manganese(IV) oxide catalyses the decomposition of hydrogen peroxide by providing an alternative pathway with lower activation energy. It increases the reaction rate but does not change the overall equation: 2H₂O₂ → 2H₂O + O₂.', TRUE),
  (4765, 3715, 'Energy change = 165 kJ − 240 kJ = −75 kJ. The reaction is exothermic. The overall energy change remains −75 kJ because the catalyst changes the reaction pathway and activation energy, not the energy levels of the reactants and products.', TRUE),
  (4766, 3716, 'Use equal volumes and concentrations of hydrogen peroxide at the same temperature. Add a measured amount of manganese(IV) oxide to one sample and no catalyst to the other. Measure oxygen production at regular intervals and compare the rates.', TRUE),
  (4767, 3717, 'A catalyst speeds up both the forward and reverse reactions by providing lower-activation-energy pathways. It helps the system reach equilibrium sooner, but it does not change the equilibrium position or the equilibrium amounts of reactants and products.', TRUE),
  (4768, 3718, 'Specific heat capacity is the energy required to raise the temperature of 1 kg of a substance by 1 K without a change of state. Its SI unit is J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹.', TRUE),
  (4769, 3719, 'Q = 3 × 4,200 × 8 = 100,800 J.', TRUE),
  (4770, 3720, 'ΔT = 15°C. c = 4,800 ÷ 12 = 400 J kg⁻¹ °C⁻¹.', TRUE),
  (4771, 3721, 'Q = 2 × 600 × 25 = 30,000 J.', TRUE),
  (4772, 3722, 'Different substances have different specific heat capacities. For equal masses and the same temperature change, Q = mcΔT shows that the substance with the higher specific heat capacity requires more energy.', TRUE),
  (4773, 3723, 'ΔT = 24,000 ÷ 1,200 = 20°C. Final temperature = 12°C + 20°C = 32°C.', TRUE),
  (4774, 3724, 'Q = mcΔT, where Q is thermal energy transferred in joules, m is mass in kilograms, c is specific heat capacity in J kg⁻¹ °C⁻¹ or J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹, and ΔT is the final temperature minus the initial temperature.', TRUE),
  (4775, 3725, 'Q = 0.400 × 4,200 × 30 = 50,400 J.', TRUE),
  (4776, 3726, 'ΔT = 18,000 ÷ 900 = 20°C. Final temperature = 20°C + 20°C = 40°C.', TRUE),
  (4777, 3727, 'c = 90,000 ÷ 200 = 450 J kg⁻¹ °C⁻¹.', TRUE),
  (4778, 3728, 'Q = mcΔT calculates the thermal energy associated with the substance''s temperature change. Some electrical energy may heat the container or be lost to the surroundings, so the total energy supplied by the heater can be greater than the energy absorbed by the substance.', TRUE),
  (4779, 3729, 'Q = 0.75 × 400 × 60 = 18,000 J. In kilojoules, Q = 18 kJ.', TRUE),
  (4780, 3730, 'Specific latent heat of fusion is the energy required to change 1 kg of a substance from solid to liquid at its melting point without changing its temperature. Its SI unit is J kg⁻¹.', TRUE),
  (4781, 3731, 'Q = 0.75 × 334,000 = 250,500 J = 250.5 kJ.', TRUE),
  (4782, 3732, 'm = 100,200 ÷ 334,000 = 0.30 kg.', TRUE),
  (4783, 3733, 'Lf = 120,000 ÷ 0.4 = 300,000 J kg⁻¹.', TRUE),
  (4784, 3734, 'During melting, the absorbed energy is used to overcome interactions holding the particles in the solid arrangement and allow them to form a liquid. It does not raise the temperature while the phase change is occurring. Once all the ice has melted, further energy can raise the water''s temperature.', TRUE),
  (4785, 3735, 'The temperature change is 0°C, so Q = mcΔT would incorrectly give zero energy for the melting process. The energy is used for the change of state. Use Q = mLf; for 1 kg of ice with Lf = 334,000 J kg⁻¹, Q = 334,000 J.', TRUE),
  (4786, 3736, 'Specific latent heat of vaporisation is the energy required to change 1 kg of a liquid into gas at its boiling point without changing its temperature. Its SI unit is J kg⁻¹.', TRUE),
  (4787, 3737, 'Q = 0.8 × 2,260,000 = 1,808,000 J = 1,808 kJ.', TRUE),
  (4788, 3738, 'm = 565,000 ÷ 2,260,000 = 0.25 kg.', TRUE),
  (4789, 3739, 'Lv = 920,000 ÷ 0.4 = 2,300,000 J kg⁻¹.', TRUE),
  (4790, 3740, 'During boiling, the supplied energy is used to separate particles sufficiently for the liquid to become gas rather than raising the temperature. The temperature remains constant while the liquid changes state at constant pressure. After all the liquid has vaporised, further heating can raise the steam''s temperature.', TRUE),
  (4791, 3741, 'The temperature difference is 0°C, so Q = mcΔT does not calculate the energy required for the state change. Use Q = mLv = 0.5 × 2,260,000 = 1,130,000 J.', TRUE),
  (4792, 3742, 'An electrolyte is a substance that conducts electricity when molten or dissolved in water because it contains mobile ions.', TRUE),
  (4793, 3743, 'Its Na⁺ and Cl⁻ ions are held in fixed positions in the lattice and cannot move freely.', TRUE),
  (4794, 3744, 'Its conductivity increases because melting allows Na⁺ and Cl⁻ ions to move freely.', TRUE),
  (4795, 3745, 'Sodium chloride solution conducts because its dissolved Na⁺ and Cl⁻ ions are mobile and carry electrical charge.', TRUE),
  (4796, 3746, 'The claim is incorrect because ions must be mobile. Solid sodium chloride contains ions, but they cannot move freely.', TRUE),
  (4797, 3747, 'Sample Y conducts electricity effectively because its ions are mobile. Sample X does not conduct effectively because its ions are fixed in the solid lattice.', TRUE),
  (4798, 3748, 'Electrolysis is the chemical decomposition of an ionic substance in the molten state or in aqueous solution by passing an electric current through it.', TRUE),
  (4799, 3749, 'The electrolyte must contain mobile ions, and an external power supply must pass an electric current through it.', TRUE),
  (4800, 3750, '2NaCl(l) → 2Na(l) + Cl₂(g).', TRUE),
  (4801, 3751, 'Positive ions move towards the negative electrode, while negative ions move towards the positive electrode. They may gain or lose electrons at the electrodes.', TRUE),
  (4802, 3752, 'The experiment is unsuitable because the ions in solid sodium chloride are fixed in the lattice and cannot move freely. The compound should be molten or dissolved in water for its ions to be mobile.', TRUE),
  (4803, 3753, 'Electrolysis uses electrical energy to drive a chemical change, whereas a simple chemical cell converts chemical energy into electrical energy.', TRUE),
  (4804, 3754, 'An electrode is an electrical conductor through which current enters or leaves an electrolyte. It provides a surface where ions may gain or lose electrons.', TRUE),
  (4805, 3755, 'The cathode is negative and the anode is positive during electrolysis.', TRUE),
  (4806, 3756, 'Mg²⁺ ions move towards the cathode because they are positively charged and are attracted to the negative electrode.', TRUE),
  (4807, 3757, 'S²⁻ ions move towards the anode because they are negatively charged and are attracted to the positive electrode.', TRUE),
  (4808, 3758, 'Ions carry charge through the electrolyte, while electrons carry charge through the external conducting circuit.', TRUE),
  (4809, 3759, 'The statement is incorrect. During electrolysis, the positive anode attracts negative ions, while positive ions move towards the negative cathode.', TRUE),
  (4810, 3760, 'The anode is the electrode at which oxidation occurs.', TRUE),
  (4811, 3761, 'The anode is positive during electrolysis. Negative ions move towards it because opposite charges attract.', TRUE),
  (4812, 3762, '2Cl⁻ → Cl₂ + 2e⁻.', TRUE),
  (4813, 3763, '2Br⁻ → Br₂ + 2e⁻.', TRUE),
  (4814, 3764, 'It is oxidation because iodide ions lose two electrons to form an iodine molecule.', TRUE),
  (4815, 3765, 'The student''s equation represents reduction, not oxidation. The correct anode equation is 2Cl⁻ → Cl₂ + 2e⁻.', TRUE),
  (4816, 3766, 'The cathode is the electrode at which reduction occurs.', TRUE),
  (4817, 3767, 'The cathode is negative during electrolysis. Positive ions move towards it because opposite charges attract.', TRUE),
  (4818, 3768, 'Na⁺ + e⁻ → Na.', TRUE),
  (4819, 3769, 'Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu.', TRUE),
  (4820, 3770, 'It is reduction because the silver ion gains one electron to form a neutral silver atom.', TRUE),
  (4821, 3771, 'The student''s equation represents oxidation, not reduction. The correct cathode equation is Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu.', TRUE),
  (4822, 3772, 'Cathode: sodium, Na⁺ + e⁻ → Na. Anode: chlorine, 2Cl⁻ → Cl₂ + 2e⁻.', TRUE),
  (4823, 3773, 'PbBr₂(l) → Pb(l) + Br₂(g). The products are lead and bromine; the lead is molten under the hot operating conditions and bromine is gaseous.', TRUE),
  (4824, 3774, 'Mg²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Mg. Two electrons are required because the magnesium ion has a charge of +2.', TRUE),
  (4825, 3775, '2Cl⁻ → Cl₂ + 2e⁻. Electrons appear on the right because chloride ions lose electrons during oxidation.', TRUE),
  (4826, 3776, 'The correct equation is MgCl₂(l) → Mg(l) + Cl₂(g). Chlorine should be written as Cl₂.', TRUE),
  (4827, 3777, '2NaCl(l) → 2Na(l) + Cl₂(g).', TRUE),
  (4828, 3778, 'An aqueous solution contains species from water as well as ions from the dissolved compound. These species compete for discharge, so its products may differ from those obtained from the molten compound.', TRUE),
  (4829, 3779, 'Na⁺, Cl⁻, H⁺ and OH⁻.', TRUE),
  (4830, 3780, 'Sodium metal is not normally produced because water is reduced preferentially under ordinary aqueous conditions, producing hydrogen gas at the cathode.', TRUE),
  (4831, 3781, 'Concentrated chloride solutions generally favour chlorine production, while sufficiently dilute solutions may favour oxygen production. The result also depends on electrode material and operating conditions.', TRUE),
  (4832, 3782, 'The method is incomplete because water contributes additional species that may compete with the ions from the dissolved salt for discharge at the electrodes.', TRUE),
  (4833, 3783, 'Consider the identity of the electrolyte and its ions, the aqueous state, the nature of the electrodes, and the concentration or relevant discharge conditions.', TRUE),
  (4834, 3784, 'Copper metal is deposited at the cathode, and oxygen gas is produced at the anode under the usual school-laboratory conditions.', TRUE),
  (4835, 3785, 'Cu²⁺(aq) + 2e⁻ → Cu(s).', TRUE),
  (4836, 3786, '4OH⁻(aq) → O₂(g) + 2H₂O(l) + 4e⁻.', TRUE),
  (4837, 3787, 'Copper forms at the cathode because Cu²⁺ ions are attracted to the negative electrode and gain electrons there.', TRUE),
  (4838, 3788, '12.7 g of copper.', TRUE),
  (4839, 3789, 'With copper electrodes, the anode can dissolve to form Cu²⁺ ions: Cu(s) → Cu²⁺(aq) + 2e⁻. With inert electrodes, water-derived species are oxidised to produce oxygen under the usual school-laboratory conditions.', TRUE),
  (4840, 3790, 'The electrodes provide surfaces for chemical reactions and electron transfer. The electrolyte conducts charge through mobile ions. The wire connects the electrodes and allows electrons to flow through the external circuit.', TRUE),
  (4841, 3791, 'Zn(s) → Zn²⁺(aq) + 2e⁻', TRUE),
  (4842, 3792, 'Cu²⁺(aq) + 2e⁻ → Cu(s)', TRUE),
  (4843, 3793, 'Electrons flow from zinc to copper because zinc releases electrons during oxidation.', TRUE),
  (4844, 3794, 'Electrons flow from zinc to copper, while conventional current flows in the opposite direction, from copper towards zinc through the external circuit.', TRUE),
  (4845, 3795, 'The voltmeter may indicate a potential difference between the electrodes. However, an open circuit prevents a continuous current from flowing through an external load.', TRUE),
  (4846, 3796, 'V = 12 / 3 = 4 V', TRUE),
  (4847, 3797, 'E = 2.0 × 5 = 10 J', TRUE),
  (4848, 3798, 'Q = 18 / 3 = 6 C', TRUE),
  (4849, 3799, 'V = 24 / 4 = 6 V. The cell transfers 6 J of energy per coulomb of charge.', TRUE),
  (4850, 3800, 'V = 1.5 + 1.5 = 3.0 V', TRUE),
  (4851, 3801, 'Net voltage = 1.5 − 1.0 = 0.5 V. The 1.5 V cell determines the direction of the net potential difference.', TRUE),
  (4852, 3802, 'A primary battery is generally designed for single use and is not intended to be recharged. A secondary battery is designed to be recharged and used through repeated charge-discharge cycles.', TRUE),
  (4853, 3803, 'V = 1.5 + 1.5 + 1.5 = 4.5 V', TRUE),
  (4854, 3804, 'Connect them in series in the same direction. Total voltage = 1.5 + 1.5 = 3.0 V.', TRUE),
  (4855, 3805, 'The arrangement provides approximately 1.5 V. Depending on the cells and circuit, parallel connection can increase available capacity or operating time.', TRUE),
  (4856, 3806, 'Rechargeable batteries have a limited useful life. Repeated cycling, ageing, heat and unsuitable charging can reduce their capacity and performance.', TRUE),
  (4857, 3807, 'Connecting compatible cells in series in the same direction adds their voltages. The cells should have suitable, compatible ratings and condition, and the arrangement must meet the device''s requirements.', TRUE),
  (4858, 3808, 'NaCl(s) → Na⁺(aq) + Cl⁻(aq). The mobile sodium and chloride ions carry charge through the solution when a potential difference is applied.', TRUE),
  (4859, 3809, 'NaCl(s) → Na⁺(aq) + Cl⁻(aq). Both atoms are balanced, and the product charges +1 and −1 sum to zero.', TRUE),
  (4860, 3810, 'HCl(aq) → H⁺(aq) + Cl⁻(aq)', TRUE),
  (4861, 3811, 'Strength describes the extent to which a substance forms ions in solution. Concentration describes the amount of dissolved substance per unit volume.', TRUE),
  (4862, 3812, 'The electrolyte may still form ions extensively, but the extremely dilute solution contains relatively few ions per unit volume, reducing its conductivity.', TRUE),
  (4863, 3813, 'NaOH(s) → Na⁺(aq) + OH⁻(aq). Mobile sodium ions and hydroxide ions carry electrical charge through the solution.', TRUE),
  (4864, 3814, 'A weak electrolyte forms ions only partially in water. At equilibrium, the solution contains ions as well as un-ionised molecules because ion formation and the reverse process both occur.', TRUE),
  (4865, 3815, 'CH₃COOH(aq) ⇌ H⁺(aq) + CH₃COO⁻(aq)', TRUE),
  (4866, 3816, 'NH₃(aq) + H₂O(l) ⇌ NH₄⁺(aq) + OH⁻(aq)', TRUE),
  (4867, 3817, 'A weak electrolyte produces some mobile ions. These ions move through the solution and carry electrical charge when a potential difference is applied.', TRUE),
  (4868, 3818, 'The strong electrolyte would generally have the higher conductivity because it forms ions more extensively, providing more mobile charge carriers under comparable conditions.', TRUE),
  (4869, 3819, 'Weak means that ethanoic acid ionises only partially. Dilute means that relatively little ethanoic acid is dissolved per unit volume. A solution can be weak but concentrated, or strong but dilute.', TRUE),
  (4870, 3820, 'A non-electrolyte does not produce a significant concentration of mobile ions in water. Its particles remain largely neutral molecules, so the solution conducts electricity very poorly through ions.', TRUE),
  (4871, 3821, 'Sucrose dissolves largely as neutral molecules rather than separating into ions. Because the solution contains very few ions from the sugar, it conducts electricity very poorly.', TRUE),
  (4872, 3822, 'C₁₂H₂₂O₁₁(s) → C₁₂H₂₂O₁₁(aq). This represents dissolution because the sucrose remains molecular and no ions are shown as products.', TRUE),
  (4873, 3823, 'Dissolving distributes a substance through a solvent. Ionisation forms ions from neutral particles. Sucrose dissolves in water but remains largely as neutral molecules, so dissolving does not cause significant ion formation.', TRUE),
  (4874, 3824, 'Ethanol is a non-electrolyte under ordinary aqueous conditions because it dissolves without producing a significant concentration of mobile ions.', TRUE),
  (4875, 3825, 'A strong electrolyte forms ions extensively and generally conducts well. A weak electrolyte forms ions partially and generally conducts less effectively at comparable concentration. A non-electrolyte forms no significant concentration of ions and conducts very poorly through ions.', TRUE),
  (4876, 3826, 'Organic chemistry is the branch of chemistry that studies carbon compounds, especially hydrocarbons and their derivatives.', TRUE),
  (4877, 3827, 'Ethanol is not a hydrocarbon because it contains oxygen in addition to carbon and hydrogen.', TRUE),
  (4878, 3828, 'Methane, CH4, and ethane, C2H6.', TRUE),
  (4879, 3829, 'The statement is incorrect because some carbon compounds are classified as inorganic. Examples include carbon dioxide, CO2, and calcium carbonate, CaCO3.', TRUE),
  (4880, 3830, 'CH4 is a hydrocarbon. C2H5OH and CO2 are not hydrocarbons.', TRUE),
  (4881, 3831, 'Yes. Ethanol, C2H5OH, is an organic compound containing carbon, hydrogen and oxygen.', TRUE),
  (4882, 3832, 'A hydrocarbon contains only carbon and hydrogen. An organic compound may also contain elements such as oxygen or nitrogen. Ethanol is organic but is not a hydrocarbon.', TRUE),
  (4883, 3833, 'Glucose is an organic compound, but it is not a hydrocarbon because it contains oxygen as well as carbon and hydrogen.', TRUE),
  (4884, 3834, 'Tetravalency is the ability of an atom, such as carbon, to form four covalent bonds.', TRUE),
  (4885, 3835, 'Methane is CH4 because one carbon atom forms four single covalent bonds with four hydrogen atoms.', TRUE),
  (4886, 3836, 'Four C–H covalent bonds.', TRUE),
  (4887, 3837, 'Carbon forms four single covalent bonds with four hydrogen atoms in methane, demonstrating its tetravalency.', TRUE),
  (4888, 3838, 'The structure is incomplete because methane requires four C–H bonds. Its correct formula is CH4, not CH3.', TRUE),
  (4889, 3839, 'Carbon forms two double bonds, each counting as two covalent bonds, giving a total of four.', TRUE),
  (4890, 3840, 'Carbon normally forms four covalent bonds. This allows it to bond with different elements and with other carbon atoms, producing a wide variety of compounds.', TRUE),
  (4891, 3841, 'Carbon forms four covalent bonds in total, so it satisfies tetravalency.', TRUE),
  (4892, 3842, 'Catenation is the ability of atoms of the same element to bond to one another, forming chains, branches or rings.', TRUE),
  (4893, 3843, 'Straight chains, branched chains and rings.', TRUE),
  (4894, 3844, 'Catenation allows carbon to form chains, branches and rings of different sizes, contributing to the large number of organic compounds.', TRUE),
  (4895, 3845, 'The six carbon atoms are joined by C–C bonds, demonstrating the ability of carbon atoms to bond to one another.', TRUE),
  (4896, 3846, 'It demonstrates catenation because carbon atoms bond to one another to form a closed ring.', TRUE),
  (4897, 3847, 'Carbon can form chains of different lengths and arrangements. Straight and branched structures can differ even when they contain the same number of carbon atoms, increasing the variety of compounds.', TRUE),
  (4898, 3848, 'No. Carbon shows extensive catenation, but some other elements can also bond to themselves.', TRUE),
  (4899, 3849, 'Catenation is the ability of carbon atoms to bond to other carbon atoms. Tetravalency is the ability of carbon to form four covalent bonds.', TRUE),
  (4900, 3850, 'A hydrocarbon is a compound containing carbon and hydrogen only.', TRUE),
  (4901, 3851, 'Saturated.', TRUE),
  (4902, 3852, 'Alkynes.', TRUE),
  (4903, 3853, 'C3H8 is a hydrocarbon because it contains carbon and hydrogen only.', TRUE),
  (4904, 3854, 'The classification is incorrect because ethanol contains oxygen as well as carbon and hydrogen.', TRUE),
  (4905, 3855, 'A saturated hydrocarbon has only C–C single bonds, for example ethane. An unsaturated hydrocarbon contains a C=C or C≡C bond, for example ethene.', TRUE),
  (4906, 3856, 'It belongs to the alkene family and is unsaturated because it contains a carbon-carbon double bond.', TRUE),
  (4907, 3857, 'C4H8 could represent an open-chain alkene or a cycloalkane. The molecular formula alone does not establish which structure is present.', TRUE),
  (4908, 3858, 'An alkane is a saturated open-chain hydrocarbon containing only single bonds between carbon atoms.', TRUE),
  (4909, 3859, 'Butane, C4H10.', TRUE),
  (4910, 3860, 'Ethane is saturated because its carbon atoms are joined by a single bond and it has no carbon-carbon multiple bond.', TRUE),
  (4911, 3861, 'It is an alkane called propane, C3H8.', TRUE),
  (4912, 3862, 'Methane, CH4; ethane, C2H6; propane, C3H8.', TRUE),
  (4913, 3863, 'Ethene is an alkene, not an alkane, because it contains a carbon-carbon double bond.', TRUE),
  (4914, 3864, 'An alkane is a saturated open-chain hydrocarbon with only single carbon-carbon bonds. An alkene is unsaturated and contains a carbon-carbon double bond.', TRUE),
  (4915, 3865, 'Pentane, C5H12. Its name uses pent- for five carbon atoms and -ane for the alkane family.', TRUE),
  (4916, 3866, 'An alkene is an unsaturated hydrocarbon containing at least one carbon-carbon double bond.', TRUE),
  (4917, 3867, 'Ethene, C2H4.', TRUE),
  (4918, 3868, 'Propene is unsaturated because it contains a carbon-carbon double bond.', TRUE),
  (4919, 3869, 'Ethane contains a C–C single bond, while ethene contains a C=C double bond. Ethane is saturated and ethene is unsaturated.', TRUE),
  (4920, 3870, 'Ethene, C2H4; propene, C3H6; butene, C4H8.', TRUE),
  (4921, 3871, 'It is a butene. A more precise name, such as but-1-ene or but-2-ene, may be needed to state the double-bond position.', TRUE),
  (4922, 3872, 'C4H8 may represent an open-chain alkene or a cycloalkane, so the molecular formula alone is insufficient to identify the structure.', TRUE),
  (4923, 3873, 'The statement is incorrect. The suffix -ene indicates a carbon-carbon double bond, while -yne indicates a carbon-carbon triple bond.', TRUE),
  (4924, 3874, 'An alkyne is an unsaturated hydrocarbon containing at least one carbon-carbon triple bond.', TRUE),
  (4925, 3875, 'Ethyne, C2H2.', TRUE),
  (4926, 3876, 'Ethyne is unsaturated because it contains a carbon-carbon triple bond.', TRUE),
  (4927, 3877, 'Ethene has a carbon-carbon double bond, whereas ethyne has a carbon-carbon triple bond. They belong to different hydrocarbon families.', TRUE),
  (4928, 3878, 'Ethyne, C2H2; propyne, C3H4; butyne, C4H6.', TRUE),
  (4929, 3879, 'It is a butyne. A more precise name, such as but-1-yne or but-2-yne, may be required to indicate the triple-bond position.', TRUE),
  (4930, 3880, 'Ethyne uses -yne because it contains a carbon-carbon triple bond. Ethene uses -ene because it contains a carbon-carbon double bond.', TRUE),
  (4931, 3881, 'C3H4 fits the formula for a three-carbon open-chain alkyne with one triple bond, such as propyne. However, a molecular formula alone does not fully specify the structure.', TRUE),
  (4932, 3882, 'A homologous series is a family of organic compounds with a common general formula and shared structural features, in which successive members differ by CH2.', TRUE),
  (4933, 3883, 'Successive members differ by one CH2 unit.', TRUE),
  (4934, 3884, 'C3H8 − C2H6 gives CH2 as the difference in atom counts.', TRUE),
  (4935, 3885, 'They belong to the alkane homologous series because they share the same family and general formula, and successive members differ by CH2.', TRUE),
  (4936, 3886, 'Pentane, C5H12.', TRUE),
  (4937, 3887, 'Members have similar chemical properties because they share the same family characteristics and bonding pattern, although their properties are not necessarily identical in every respect.', TRUE),
  (4938, 3888, 'The next member has one more carbon and two more hydrogen atoms, so successive members differ by CH2.', TRUE),
  (4939, 3889, 'Any two: members follow the same general formula; have similar chemical properties; share a functional group where applicable; or show gradual changes in physical properties.', TRUE),
  (4940, 3890, 'CnH2n+2.', TRUE),
  (4941, 3891, 'C5H12, pentane.', TRUE),
  (4942, 3892, 'Yes. C3H8 fits the general formula because 2(3) + 2 = 8.', TRUE),
  (4943, 3893, 'C8H18, octane.', TRUE),
  (4944, 3894, 'It has five carbon atoms and the formula C5H12, pentane.', TRUE),
  (4945, 3895, 'The claim is incorrect. An open-chain alkane with six carbon atoms has formula C6H14.', TRUE),
  (4946, 3896, 'Yes. C9H20 fits the formula because the general formula predicts twenty hydrogen atoms.', TRUE),
  (4947, 3897, 'The formula applies to open-chain alkanes. Saturated cyclic hydrocarbons generally follow CnH2n because forming a ring reduces the hydrogen count by two.', TRUE),
  (4948, 3898, 'Alkenes: CnH2n. Alkynes: CnH2n−2.', TRUE),
  (4949, 3899, 'C6H12.', TRUE),
  (4950, 3900, 'C3H4, propyne for the three-carbon alkyne example.', TRUE),
  (4951, 3901, 'C7H14.', TRUE),
  (4952, 3902, 'C6H10.', TRUE),
  (4953, 3903, 'It fits the alkene general formula because CnH2n gives C5H10 when n = 5.', TRUE),
