  (5203, 4153, 'Ammonium nitrate is NH4NO3, a salt; nitric acid is HNO3, an acid.', TRUE),
  (5204, 4154, 'Atmospheric nitrogen has a strong triple bond and must first be converted into usable nitrogen compounds.', TRUE),
  (5205, 4155, 'Nitrification.', TRUE),
  (5206, 4156, 'It is the microbial conversion of nitrates into gaseous nitrogen compounds, commonly in oxygen-poor or waterlogged soils.', TRUE),
  (5207, 4157, 'Atmospheric nitrogen makes up about 78% of air, but its strong triple bond makes it relatively unreactive. Most plants absorb nitrogen mainly as nitrate or ammonium ions, so nitrogen must first be converted into usable compounds.', TRUE),
  (5208, 4158, 'Nitrogen-fixing bacteria convert atmospheric nitrogen into ammonia or related compounds. Lightning forms nitrogen oxides that eventually contribute nitrates to the soil. Industrially, the Haber process converts nitrogen and hydrogen into ammonia.', TRUE),
  (5209, 4159, 'Nitrification converts ammonium compounds into nitrites and nitrates, generally under aerobic conditions. Denitrification converts nitrates into gaseous nitrogen compounds, commonly under oxygen-poor conditions.', TRUE),
  (5210, 4160, 'The plant absorbs nitrate or ammonium ions through its roots and assimilates the nitrogen into amino acids and proteins. An animal obtains that nitrogen by eating the plant.', TRUE),
  (5211, 4161, 'Decomposer bacteria and fungi break down nitrogen-containing organic matter and release ammonia or ammonium compounds. This process is called ammonification.', TRUE),
  (5212, 4162, 'Fixation converts atmospheric nitrogen into usable compounds; plants assimilate nitrogen, and animals obtain it through feeding. Ammonification returns nitrogen from organic matter to the soil. Denitrification converts nitrates into gaseous compounds, reducing nitrate availability to crops.', TRUE),
