  (5341, 4291, 'A chemical reaction in which the products can react to reform the original reactants under suitable conditions.', TRUE),
  (5342, 4292, 'It indicates that the reaction can proceed in both the forward and reverse directions.', TRUE),
  (5343, 4293, 'It demonstrates a reversible chemical change because the original hydrated compound can be regenerated.', TRUE),
  (5344, 4294, 'The learner has ignored the reverse direction. C and D can react to reform A and B under suitable conditions.', TRUE),
  (5345, 4295, 'Equation (ii), P + Q ⇌ R, explicitly represents a reversible reaction.', TRUE),
  (5346, 4296, 'No. The original reactants are not regenerated, so the separate reaction does not by itself prove that the first reaction is reversible.', TRUE),
  (5347, 4297, 'Heating removes water of crystallisation to form anhydrous copper(II) sulfate, while adding water can regenerate hydrated copper(II) sulfate. The original compound is restored.', TRUE),
  (5348, 4298, 'No. The symbol indicates that the reaction can proceed in both directions; it does not establish that their rates are equal.', TRUE),
  (5349, 4299, 'Because magnesium oxide does not readily regenerate magnesium and oxygen under ordinary classroom conditions, the reaction is treated as effectively irreversible.', TRUE),
  (5350, 4300, 'A reacts with B to form C and D.', TRUE),
  (5351, 4301, '2HI → H₂ + I₂', TRUE),
  (5352, 4302, 'C and D.', TRUE),
  (5353, 4303, 'Forward: X reacts with Y to form Z. Reverse: Z reacts to reform X and Y.', TRUE),
  (5354, 4304, 'The coefficient 2 was omitted. The correct reverse equation is 2HI → H₂ + I₂.', TRUE),
  (5355, 4305, 'The reverse reaction uses 2NH₃ as the reactant and forms N₂ and 3H₂.', TRUE),
  (5356, 4306, 'No. C and D are reactants in the reverse direction because they react to form A and B.', TRUE),
  (5357, 4307, 'Forward describes the direction from reactants to products; it does not mean faster. The forward and reverse rates depend on the reaction conditions and composition.', TRUE),
  (5358, 4308, 'R → P + 2Q. The coefficient 2 must be retained to preserve the original atom ratio.', TRUE),
  (5359, 4309, 'A state in a closed system where the forward and reverse reactions continue at equal rates and the concentrations of reactants and products remain constant at a given temperature.', TRUE),
  (5360, 4310, '15 units per second.', TRUE),
  (5361, 4311, 'Because equilibrium requires equal forward and reverse reaction rates, not equal concentrations.', TRUE),
  (5362, 4312, 'No. The forward rate is 6 mol/dm³/s and the reverse rate is 4 mol/dm³/s, so the rates are unequal.', TRUE),
  (5363, 4313, 'At equilibrium, the forward reaction continues, but the reverse reaction occurs at the same rate. Their effects balance, so concentrations remain constant.', TRUE),
  (5364, 4314, 'No. Unequal concentrations do not rule out equilibrium, provided the closed system has equal forward and reverse rates and constant concentrations.', TRUE),
  (5365, 4315, 'A closed system prevents substances from freely entering or escaping, allowing the reacting mixture to establish and maintain equilibrium under constant conditions.', TRUE),
  (5366, 4316, 'Constant concentrations show that the overall composition is not changing, but do not alone prove that both reactions are continuing at equal rates. The closed-system and equal-rate conditions must also be satisfied.', TRUE),
  (5367, 4317, 'The concentrations remain constant because the forward and reverse reactions occur at equal rates of 20 units per second, so neither direction produces a net change.', TRUE),
  (5368, 4318, 'The initial reverse reaction rate is zero, assuming the reverse reaction requires the products that are initially absent.', TRUE),
  (5369, 4319, 'The forward rate commonly decreases as reactants are consumed, while the reverse rate increases as products accumulate.', TRUE),
  (5370, 4320, 'The reaction rates satisfy the condition for dynamic equilibrium.', TRUE),
  (5371, 4321, 'The reverse rate is initially zero because the substances required for the reverse reaction are absent.', TRUE),
  (5372, 4322, 'The system has reached dynamic equilibrium, provided the system is closed and the conditions remain constant.', TRUE),
  (5373, 4323, 'The forward rate commonly decreases as reactants are consumed and their concentrations fall. The reverse rate commonly increases as products accumulate. The reverse reaction starting is not, by itself, the complete explanation for the falling forward rate.', TRUE),
  (5374, 4324, 'Reactants do not have to be completely consumed. Equilibrium occurs when the forward and reverse rates become equal, and reactants and products can both remain present.', TRUE),
  (5375, 4325, 'No. The forward rate exceeds the reverse rate by 6 units/s, so the rates are not equal and the system is not at dynamic equilibrium.', TRUE),
  (5376, 4326, 'Net change = 25 − 25 = 0 units per minute. The reactions have not stopped; both continue at 25 units per minute.', TRUE),
  (5377, 4327, 'The equilibrium shifts to the right, favouring the formation of HI.', TRUE),
  (5378, 4328, 'The equilibrium shifts to the right, towards the formation of ammonia.', TRUE),
  (5379, 4329, 'No. It can change the equilibrium position, but the equilibrium constant remains unchanged at the same temperature.', TRUE),
  (5380, 4330, 'The equilibrium shifts to the right, towards C and D, because the forward reaction consumes some of the added A.', TRUE),
  (5381, 4331, 'The equilibrium shifts to the left. The reverse reaction, 2NH₃(g) → N₂(g) + 3H₂(g), produces nitrogen.', TRUE),
  (5382, 4332, 'The equilibrium shifts to the right because the forward reaction produces HI, tending to replace some of the removed product.', TRUE),
  (5383, 4333, 'The equilibrium shifts to the left, towards A and B, because the reverse reaction consumes some of the added C.', TRUE),
  (5384, 4334, 'The equilibrium shifts towards products and consumes some of the added reactant, but it does not necessarily consume all of it. A new equilibrium is reached when the forward and reverse rates become equal.', TRUE),
  (5385, 4335, 'Adding the reactant can shift equilibrium towards products, but the equilibrium constant does not change because the temperature remains constant.', TRUE),
  (5386, 4336, 'The equilibrium shifts to the left, towards A and B.', TRUE),
  (5387, 4337, 'The equilibrium shifts to the left, towards A and B.', TRUE),
  (5388, 4338, 'It shifts equilibrium towards nitrogen and hydrogen and reduces the equilibrium yield of ammonia.', TRUE),
  (5389, 4339, 'The equilibrium shifts to the right, towards C and D, because the forward reaction releases heat.', TRUE),
  (5390, 4340, 'The equilibrium shifts to the right, towards C and D.', TRUE),
  (5391, 4341, 'Increasing temperature generally speeds up reaction rates, but it favours the endothermic direction at equilibrium. If the forward reaction is exothermic, the reverse reaction is endothermic, so equilibrium shifts backwards.', TRUE),
  (5392, 4342, 'Cooling favours the exothermic reverse reaction, so equilibrium shifts to the left, towards reactants.', TRUE),
  (5393, 4343, 'Increasing temperature shifts equilibrium to the left. Decreasing temperature shifts equilibrium to the right.', TRUE),
  (5394, 4344, 'Increasing temperature generally speeds up reactions, but it favours the endothermic direction. If product formation is exothermic, heating shifts equilibrium towards reactants and reduces the product''s equilibrium yield.', TRUE),
  (5395, 4345, 'Equilibrium shifts to the right, favouring ammonia formation.', TRUE),
  (5396, 4346, 'There is no shift in the equilibrium position.', TRUE),
  (5397, 4347, 'Equilibrium shifts to the left, favouring CaCO₃.', TRUE),
  (5398, 4348, 'The claim is incorrect as a general rule. In this particular equation, increasing pressure shifts equilibrium to the right.', TRUE),
  (5399, 4349, 'Left = 3 moles; right = 2 moles. Equilibrium shifts to the right, favouring SO₃.', TRUE),
  (5400, 4350, 'The student counted atoms instead of gaseous moles. The correct totals are 2 on the left and 2 on the right.', TRUE),
  (5401, 4351, 'Equilibrium shifts to the right, favouring CaO and CO₂. The solids are excluded because the pressure rule compares gaseous particles.', TRUE),
  (5402, 4352, 'Equilibrium shifts to the right.', TRUE),
  (5403, 4353, 'The claim is incorrect. Pressure increases, but if the gaseous mole totals are equal on both sides, the equilibrium position does not shift because of that pressure change.', TRUE),
  (5404, 4354, 'N₂(g) + 3H₂(g) ⇌ 2NH₃(g). Increasing pressure shifts equilibrium to the right because the product side has fewer gaseous moles.', TRUE),
  (5405, 4355, 'A temperature around 450°C is a compromise: it gives a useful reaction rate while retaining a reasonable equilibrium yield of ammonia.', TRUE),
  (5406, 4356, 'Iron speeds up both forward and reverse reactions, allowing equilibrium to be reached faster. It does not change the equilibrium position or equilibrium yield at a fixed temperature.', TRUE),
  (5407, 4357, 'Lower temperature favours ammonia formation at equilibrium, but it slows the reaction. Industry therefore uses a compromise temperature that provides a practical rate and yield.', TRUE),
  (5408, 4358, 'The totals are 3 on the left and 2 on the right. Increasing pressure shifts equilibrium to the right, favouring SO₃.', TRUE),
  (5409, 4359, 'V₂O₅ speeds up the forward and reverse reactions so equilibrium is reached faster. It does not increase the equilibrium yield of SO₃ at a fixed temperature.', TRUE),
  (5410, 4360, 'Lower temperature improves the equilibrium tendency towards SO₃ but slows production. A compromise temperature, commonly taught as around 450°C, balances yield and rate.', TRUE),
  (5411, 4361, 'Increasing pressure favours ammonia in the Haber process and sulfur trioxide in the Contact process because each product side has fewer gaseous moles than its reactant side.', TRUE),
  (5412, 4362, 'Removing ammonia shifts equilibrium towards the products, encouraging further ammonia formation. A catalyst speeds up both reaction directions and helps equilibrium be reached faster but does not shift the equilibrium position.', TRUE),
  (5413, 4363, 'Particles must collide with sufficient energy and suitable orientation for a chemical reaction to occur.', TRUE),
  (5414, 4364, 'Because the particles may not have enough energy or may collide in an unsuitable orientation.', TRUE),
  (5415, 4365, 'The rate of reaction increases.', TRUE),
  (5416, 4366, 'The particles may have insufficient energy, or they may collide in an unsuitable orientation.', TRUE),
  (5417, 4367, 'Activation energy is the minimum energy that reacting particles must have for a successful reaction to occur.', TRUE),
  (5418, 4368, 'No. The particle does not have enough energy to overcome the activation energy.', TRUE),
  (5419, 4369, 'Yes. The particle has enough energy to overcome the activation energy.', TRUE),
  (5420, 4370, '5 kJ.', TRUE),
  (5421, 4371, 'An effective collision is a collision between reacting particles that results in a chemical reaction.', TRUE),
  (5422, 4372, 'Increasing temperature increases the number of effective collisions per second.', TRUE),
  (5423, 4373, 'A catalyst increases the rate by providing an alternative pathway with lower activation energy.', TRUE),
  (5424, 4374, 'Add a suitable catalyst.', TRUE),
  (5425, 4375, 'Breaking a covalent bond absorbs energy because energy must be supplied to separate the bonded atoms.', TRUE),
  (5426, 4376, 'Approximately 436 kJ is required to break one mole of H–H bonds in gaseous hydrogen molecules.', TRUE),
  (5427, 4377, 'Bond B requires 250 kJ more energy per mole of bonds.', TRUE),
  (5428, 4378, '820 kJ is absorbed.', TRUE),
  (5429, 4379, '520 kJ is required to break one mole of the specified bonds, not one individual bond.', TRUE),
  (5430, 4380, '300 kJ is absorbed.', TRUE),
  (5431, 4381, '360 kJ/mol.', TRUE),
  (5432, 4382, 'The claim is incorrect. Breaking a bond absorbs energy; a higher bond energy means more energy must be supplied to break the bond.', TRUE),
  (5433, 4383, '1100 kJ is absorbed.', TRUE),
  (5434, 4384, 'Energy is released when the H–H bond forms.', TRUE),
  (5435, 4385, 'Approximately 500 kJ is released.', TRUE),
  (5436, 4386, 'Bond breaking absorbs energy, while the corresponding bond formation releases energy.', TRUE),
  (5437, 4387, '1200 kJ is released.', TRUE),
  (5438, 4388, 'Forming one mole of the specified bonds releases approximately 420 kJ; it does not absorb that energy.', TRUE),
  (5439, 4389, 'Bond B releases approximately 250 kJ/mol more energy when formed.', TRUE),
  (5440, 4390, '360 kJ is released to the surroundings.', TRUE),
  (5441, 4391, 'The conclusion is not justified. The reaction is exothermic only if bond formation releases more energy than bond breaking absorbs; otherwise, it is endothermic.', TRUE),
  (5442, 4392, 'Forming 0.5 mol releases 360 kJ. Breaking the same amount of corresponding bonds absorbs approximately 360 kJ.', TRUE),
  (5443, 4393, 'ΔH ≈ ΣBE(bonds broken) − ΣBE(bonds formed).', TRUE),
  (5444, 4394, 'ΔH ≈ −200 kJ; the reaction is exothermic.', TRUE),
  (5445, 4395, 'ΔH ≈ −183 kJ/mol of reaction.', TRUE),
  (5446, 4396, 'ΔH ≈ −250 kJ; exothermic.', TRUE),
  (5447, 4397, 'ΔH ≈ +400 kJ; the reaction is endothermic.', TRUE),
  (5448, 4398, 'ΔH ≈ −183 kJ/mol of reaction.', TRUE),
  (5449, 4399, 'ΔH ≈ −93 kJ/mol of reaction; exothermic.', TRUE),
  (5450, 4400, 'The subtraction order is reversed. The correct formula is ΔH ≈ ΣBE(bonds broken) − ΣBE(bonds formed).', TRUE),
  (5451, 4401, 'ΔH ≈ +200 kJ; the reaction is endothermic.', TRUE),
  (5452, 4402, 'Complete combustion is the burning of a hydrocarbon in sufficient oxygen to produce carbon dioxide and water.', TRUE),
